e public awareness, conductors and emergency response.',1,NULL,NULL),(10991,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','वर्णन करा','descriptive','Storm Maharashtra?',20,0,NULL,NULL,'Storms in Maharashtra can cause flooding, landslides and crop damage.',1,NULL,NULL),(10992,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','वर्णन करा','descriptive','Cloudburst',21,0,NULL,NULL,'A cloudburst is an intense, localized rainfall event.',1,NULL,NULL),(10993,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','वर्णन करा','descriptive','With reference to disaster management',22,0,NULL,NULL,'Disaster management involves preparedness, response and recovery strategies.',1,NULL,NULL),(10994,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','सत्य किंवा असत्य','true_false','True or false? Give reasons for your answer. Information about a forthcoming Project storm is to be kept secret.',23,0,NULL,NULL,'false',1,NULL,NULL),(10995,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','क्रिया','activity','Information about a forthcoming Project storm is to be kept secret',24,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(10996,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','वर्णन करा','descriptive','You should not swim when there is',25,0,NULL,NULL,'lightning in the sky.',1,NULL,NULL),(10997,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','क्रिया','activity','With the help of the internet collect information about the lightning in the sky.',26,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(10998,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','वर्णन करा','descriptive','It is possible to prevent the eruption of a volcano?',27,0,NULL,NULL,'No, it is not possible to prevent a volcanic eruption.',1,NULL,NULL),(10999,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','क्रिया','activity','Collect information from the internet about how cyclonic storms are named',28,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(11000,46,17152,7,'Science','en',80,'','वर्णन करा','descriptive','Heavy rains result in famine',29,0,NULL,NULL,'Heavy rains can cause flooding and disrupt agriculture, leading to famine.',1,NULL,NULL),(11001,46,17153,7,'Science','en',84,'','Answer the following','define','What is meant by micro‑organisms?',1,0,NULL,NULL,'Micro‑organisms are tiny living organisms that can only be seen under a microscope, such as bacteria, fungi, protozoa and viruses.',1,NULL,NULL),(11002,46,17153,7,'Science','en',84,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Categorise the following organisms into two groups according to size – amoeba, paramoecium, euglena, snail, elephant, pigeon, worms.',2,0,NULL,'[\"A: amoeba, paramoecium, euglena, worms\\nB: snail, elephant, pigeon\"]','A: amoeba, paramoecium, euglena, worms\nB: snail, elephant, pigeon',1,NULL,NULL),(11003,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','define','(a) What is a ‘cell’?',3,0,NULL,NULL,'A cell is the smallest unit of life that makes up all living organisms and contains organelles that carry out life processes.',1,NULL,NULL),(11004,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','one_word','(b) Name the different organelles in a cell.',4,0,NULL,NULL,'Nucleus, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, mitochondria, lysosomes, vacuoles, ribosomes, chloroplasts (in plants).',1,NULL,NULL),(11005,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','define','(c) Which are the different types of poisoning if we eat stale food?',5,0,NULL,NULL,'Food poisoning can be caused by bacterial toxins, fungal toxins, or viral contamination.',1,NULL,NULL),(11006,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','define','(d) Fungus grows quickly in moist or humid conditions.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'Fungi thrive in damp environments and can cause spoilage and disease.',1,NULL,NULL),(11007,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','define','(e) A refrigerator is used in almost every home.',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Refrigerators keep food cold to slow down microbial growth.',1,NULL,NULL),(11008,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','define','(f) Fodder is soaked in water before offering to cattle.',8,0,NULL,NULL,'Soaking fodder softens it and reduces dust, making it easier for cattle to eat.',1,NULL,NULL),(11009,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','The organelle called the ______ is present in plant cells only.',9,0,NULL,NULL,'cell wall',1,NULL,NULL),(11010,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Solve','solve','In the cell, photosynthesis is carried out with the help of wo______',10,0,NULL,NULL,'chloroplasts',1,NULL,NULL),(11011,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Solve','solve','An electron microscope is necessary and when, a compound microscope?',11,0,NULL,NULL,'Use a compound microscope for general cell observation; use an electron microscope for detailed ultrastructure and very small organelles.',1,NULL,NULL),(11012,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','difference','Plant cell and animal cell.',12,0,NULL,NULL,'Plant cells have a cell wall, large central vacuole and chloroplasts; animal cells lack a cell wall and have many small vacuoles.',1,NULL,NULL),(11013,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','difference','Prokaryotic cell and eukaryotic cell.',13,0,NULL,NULL,'Prokaryotic cells lack a nucleus and membrane‑bound organelles; eukaryotic cells have a nucleus and many organelles.',1,NULL,NULL),(11014,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','difference','Sketch and describe in your own words the plant cell and animal cell.',14,1,NULL,NULL,'Plant cell: has cell wall, large vacuole, chloroplasts; Animal cell: lacks cell wall, has many small vacuoles and lysosomes.',1,NULL,NULL),(11015,46,17153,7,'Science','en',90,'','Answer the following','difference','Explain the uses and the harmful effects of micro‑organisms.',15,0,NULL,NULL,'Uses: fermentation, antibiotics, waste decomposition. Harmful effects: food poisoning, diseases such as tuberculosis, typhoid, and infections caused by bacteria and viruses.',1,NULL,NULL),(11016,46,17154,7,'Science','en',93,'','Describe','descriptive','The cardiac muscles do not move.',5,0,NULL,NULL,'The heart keeps beating continuously; if cardiac muscles stopped moving, the heart would cease to pump blood, leading to loss of circulation and death.',1,NULL,NULL),(11017,46,17154,7,'Science','en',93,'','Describe','descriptive','Food enters the stomach and ______ the stomach muscles do not move.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'if the stomach muscles do not contract, food would not be churned and digestion would be severely impaired.',1,NULL,NULL),(11018,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','The process of digestion starts from the ______ (stomach / mouth).',1,0,NULL,NULL,'mouth',1,NULL,NULL),(11019,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','Eyelids have ______ muscles that perform an involuntary role (voluntary / involuntary).',2,0,NULL,NULL,'involuntary',1,NULL,NULL),(11020,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Explain','reason','Muscles of the heart are ______ muscles (ordinary muscles / cardiac muscles).',3,0,NULL,NULL,'cardiac',1,NULL,NULL),(11021,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','Pushing forward the food that has been chewed is the function of the ______ (stomach / oesophagus).',4,0,NULL,NULL,'oesophagus',1,NULL,NULL),(11022,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Cardiac muscles always function. Which are those types?',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Cardiac, skeletal, and smooth muscles.',1,NULL,NULL),(11023,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Answer the following','descriptive','What causes the problem of acidity?',8,0,NULL,NULL,'Excess stomach acid or reflux of acid into the esophagus.',1,NULL,NULL),(11024,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Answer the following','descriptive','What is its effect on the body?',9,0,NULL,NULL,'Acidity can damage the lining of the stomach and esophagus, causing pain and ulcers.',1,NULL,NULL),(11025,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Answer the following','descriptive','We never feel about by muscles ______ tired.',10,0,NULL,NULL,'when muscles are tired, we feel fatigue and reduced strength.',1,NULL,NULL),(11026,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Explain','explain','Pepsin',11,1,'46/096.jpg',NULL,'Pepsin is a digestive enzyme that breaks down proteins into smaller peptides in the stomach.',1,'46/096_3.jpg',NULL),(11027,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Activity','activity','Cramps',12,1,'46/096.jpg',NULL,NULL,1,'46/096_3.jpg',NULL),(11028,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Skeletal muscles',13,0,NULL,NULL,'Skeletal muscles are voluntary muscles attached to bones.',1,NULL,NULL),(11029,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Who is telling a lie?',14,0,NULL,NULL,'The statement that only a sweet taste can be detected by the tongue is false; the tongue can detect sweet, salty, sour, bitter, and umami.',1,NULL,NULL),(11030,46,17154,7,'Science','en',97,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Liver',15,0,NULL,NULL,'The liver is the largest gland in the body and processes nutrients, detoxifies blood, and stores bile.',1,NULL,NULL),(11032,46,17155,7,'Science','en',100,'','Answer the following','short_answer','From 6.00 am to 6.00 pm, how many times does the hour hand of a clock show a periodic change?',1,0,NULL,NULL,'12 times',1,NULL,NULL),(11033,46,17155,7,'Science','en',100,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Which of the changes shown in the picture are permanent?',2,1,'46/099.jpg',NULL,'(Answer depends on the picture; e.g., rusting of iron is permanent.)',1,NULL,NULL),(11034,46,17155,7,'Science','en',100,'','Answer the following','short_answer','In which of the changes did the original matter undergo a change? In which did the original matter remain unchanged? In which of the changes was a new substance with a new property formed?',3,1,'46/099.jpg',NULL,'(Answer depends on the picture; e.g., burning wood – matter changes; melting ice – matter unchanged; rusting iron – new substance formed.)',1,NULL,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ep_book_questions` VALUES (11036,46,17155,7,'Science','en',100,'','Answer the following','short_answer','What processes occur during the change of state of matter?',4,1,'46/099.jpg',NULL,'Melting, boiling, evaporation, condensation, sublimation',1,NULL,NULL),(11037,46,17155,7,'Science','en',100,'','Answer the following','short_answer','What happens when water is taken in a bowl and heated?',5,1,'46/099.jpg',NULL,'It boils and turns into vapor (evaporation).',1,NULL,NULL),(11040,46,17155,7,'Science','en',101,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Under which different types can the following changes be classified? (a) Conversion of milk into yoghurt. (b) Bursting of a cracker. (c) Occurrence of an earthquake. (d) Revolution of the earth around the sun. (e) Stretching of a spring.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'(a) Natural, useful, slow. (b) Natural, useful, fast. (c) Natural, harmful, non‑periodic. (d) Natural, periodic. (e) Natural, useful, reversible.',1,NULL,NULL),(11046,46,17155,7,'Science','en',101,'','Answer the following','reason','Why should the expiry date of canned food be checked before purchasing?',7,0,NULL,NULL,'To ensure the food is still safe to eat and has not spoiled.',1,NULL,NULL),(11047,46,17155,7,'Science','en',101,'','Answer the following','reason','Why should an iron article be given a coat of paint?',8,0,NULL,NULL,'To prevent corrosion and extend its life.',1,NULL,NULL),(11048,46,17155,7,'Science','en',101,'','Answer the following','reason','Why should a wooden article be polished?',9,0,NULL,NULL,'To protect it from moisture and wear.',1,NULL,NULL),(11049,46,17155,7,'Science','en',101,'','Answer the following','explain','Explain why copper and brass utensils should be tinned.',10,0,NULL,NULL,'Tinning coats the metal with tin, preventing corrosion and keeping the utensils safe for cooking.',1,NULL,NULL),(11050,46,17155,7,'Science','en',101,'','Answer the following','explain','Explain why a dry handkerchief gets wet instantly when dipped in water, but a wet handkerchief takes long to dry.',11,0,NULL,NULL,'Dry cloth absorbs water quickly due to capillary action; wet cloth dries slowly because water must evaporate from a saturated surface.',1,NULL,NULL),(11051,46,17156,7,'Science','en',103,'','Answer the following','mcq','Which pan goes down when a small stone is placed in one pan and a big stone in the other?',1,0,NULL,'[\"The pan with the small stone\",\"The pan with the big stone\",\"Both pans go down equally\",\"Neither pan goes down\"]','1',1,NULL,NULL),(11052,46,17156,7,'Science','en',103,'','Answer the following','fill_blank','When water is heated in a teapot, the water droplets that appear on the inside of the lid are formed by ______ of the vapour.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'condensation',1,NULL,NULL),(11062,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Fill appropriate terms in the blanks','fill_blank','(a) The substance that helps water to _______ the surface of materials is called surface activity.',1,0,NULL,NULL,'spread',1,NULL,NULL),(11063,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Fill appropriate terms in the blanks','fill_blank','(b) Soap and synthetic detergent remove dirt from the surface of _______ materials.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'clothes',1,NULL,NULL),(11064,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Fill appropriate terms in the blanks','fill_blank','(c) Bath soap and soap for washing clothes are called _______ soap.',3,0,NULL,NULL,'hard',1,NULL,NULL),(11065,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Fill appropriate terms in the blanks','fill_blank','(d) Fluoride is used in toothpaste to _______ tooth decay.',4,0,NULL,NULL,'prevent',1,NULL,NULL),(11066,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Explain','reason','(e) Soap is a salt of sodium hydroxide.',5,0,NULL,NULL,'Because the reaction of fats with sodium hydroxide produces sodium salts of fatty acids.',1,NULL,NULL),(11067,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Explain','reason','(f) Synthetic detergents can be used in hard water as well.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'Because they are not affected by calcium and magnesium ions and do not form scum.',1,NULL,NULL),(11068,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Explain','reason','(g) Soap cannot be used in hard water.',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Because it reacts with calcium and magnesium ions to form insoluble scum, reducing lather.',1,NULL,NULL),(11069,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Answer the following','descriptive','(a) How does the use of a detergent help to clean soiled clothes?',8,0,NULL,NULL,'Detergents have surfactants that attach to both oil and water, allowing oil and dirt to be washed away with water.',1,NULL,NULL),(11070,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Answer the following','descriptive','(b) How will you check with the help of soap powder whether water is hard?',9,0,NULL,NULL,'Add soap powder to water; if it forms a scum or does not lather, the water is hard.',1,NULL,NULL),(11071,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Answer the following','descriptive','(c) What are the important ingredients of a toothpaste, and what is the function of each?',10,0,NULL,NULL,'Abrasives (calcium carbonate) clean teeth; fluoride strengthens enamel and prevents decay; humectants keep toothpaste moist.',1,NULL,NULL),(11072,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Activity','activity','Visit a cement factory. See how cement is prepared and discuss the process. Write a conversation based on cement houses, mud‑houses and wattle‑and‑daub houses.',11,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(11073,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Answer the following','descriptive','(e) What will happen if cement is not used in making concrete?',12,0,NULL,NULL,'Concrete would lack strength and durability, leading to weak structures.',1,NULL,NULL),(11074,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Answer the following','descriptive','(g) What should be expected from a detergent for delicate garments?',13,0,NULL,NULL,'It should be mild, free from harsh chemicals, and not leave residue or cause color fading.',1,NULL,NULL),(11075,46,17157,7,'Science','en',113,'','Answer the following','activity','(h) What is meant by ‘surface activity’? Name three chemicals responsible for the surface activity of various detergents.',14,0,NULL,NULL,'Surface activity is the ability of a substance to spread on a surface and reduce surface tension. Three chemicals are sodium lauryl sulfate, cocamidopropyl betaine, and alkylbenzene sulfonate.',1,NULL,NULL),(11076,46,17158,7,'Science','en',120,'','Describe','short_answer','Young trees should not be cut.',1,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(11077,46,17158,7,'Science','en',120,'','Describe','short_answer','Many more trees than are cut down should be planted and looked after.',2,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(11078,46,17158,7,'Science','en',120,'','Describe','short_answer','The stringent restrictions/laws/regulations regarding use of forests should be strictly followed.',3,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(11079,46,17158,7,'Science','en',120,'','Name','one_word','Name the oceans of the earth.',4,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(11080,46,17158,7,'Science','en',120,'','Describe','short_answer','How is seawater useful to us, even though it is salty?',5,1,'46/119.jpg',NULL,'Sea water contains dissolved minerals and salts that are useful for industrial processes, agriculture (fertilizers), and marine life. It also provides energy from waves and currents and is a source of marine resources such as fish, oil, and gas.',1,NULL,NULL),(11081,46,17158,7,'Science','en',122,'','Describe','short_answer','What is meant by fossil fuel?',6,0,NULL,NULL,'A fossil fuel is a natural energy source formed from the remains of ancient plants and animals, such as coal, oil, and natural gas.',1,NULL,NULL),(11082,46,17158,7,'Science','en',122,'','Describe','short_answer','What do we get from forests?',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Forests provide timber, fruits, medicinal plants, oxygen, and habitat for wildlife.',1,NULL,NULL),(11083,46,17158,7,'Science','en',122,'','Describe','short_answer','What are the items included in bituminous coal resources? What are their uses?',8,0,NULL,NULL,'Bituminous coal is a type of coal used mainly for electricity generation, industrial heating, and as a raw material in steel production.',1,NULL,NULL),(11084,46,17158,7,'Science','en',122,'','Explain','reason','Why should we prevent the wastage of fuel used for vehicles?',9,0,NULL,NULL,'Preventing fuel wastage reduces pollution, conserves limited fossil fuel reserves, and saves money.',1,NULL,NULL),(11085,46,17158,7,'Science','en',122,'','Explain','reason','How does the economic condition of a nation depend on its natural resources?',10,0,NULL,NULL,'A nation\'s economy can grow with abundant natural resources through exports, job creation, and industrial development, but overreliance can lead to resource depletion and economic instability.',1,NULL,NULL),(11086,46,17158,7,'Science','en',122,'','Name','one_word','Name the two important stages in the process of obtaining metals from various minerals.',11,0,NULL,NULL,'Mining and refining',1,NULL,NULL),(11087,46,17158,7,'Science','en',122,'','Activity','activity','Collect conches and shells of the process of obtaining metals from various minerals? Make a decorative article. What steps are taken for protection and conservation of natural resources?',12,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(11090,46,17159,7,'Science','en',127,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','When the beams from the headlights of a car fall on an object in the night, the shadows called ______ and (b) Does the shadow really vanish in the zero shadow condition?',1,0,NULL,NULL,'umbra',1,NULL,NULL),(11091,46,17159,7,'Science','en',127,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','During a lunar eclipse the shadow of the earth falls on the moon and (c) Will the laser beam be seen if it passes through a glass box which contains a lighted incense stick ______.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'yes',1,NULL,NULL),(11092,46,17159,7,'Science','en',127,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','During a solar eclipse the shadow of the earth falls on the moon and contains a lighted incense stick ______.',3,0,NULL,NULL,'no',1,NULL,NULL),(11093,46,17159,7,'Science','en',127,'','Write a paragraph','descriptive','Write a science based paragraph on the sky at sunrise and sunset due to “What if the sun did not rise?”',4,0,NULL,NULL,'The sky would be dark all day; we would not see sunrise or sunset because the sun’s light is what creates these colours.',1,NULL,NULL),(11095,46,17159,7,'Science','en',127,'','Explain the difference','difference','We should not observe the solar eclipse with naked eyes. (b) Umbra and penumbra.',5,0,NULL,NULL,'The sun’s rays are intense and can damage the eyes, whereas the umbra is the fully shaded part of the shadow and the penumbra is the partially shaded part.',1,NULL,NULL),(11096,46,17159,7,'Science','en',127,'','Give examples','reason','Give some examples of scattering of light that we come across in day‑to‑day life.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'Seeing a beam of headlights in fog, the blue colour of the sky, the red glow of a sunset, and the white glare from a dusty window.',1,NULL,NULL),(11097,46,17159,7,'Science','en',127,'','Explain','reason','Why is the shadow of a bird flying high not seen on the earth?',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Because the bird is too high and the light from the sun is scattered by the atmosphere, the shadow is very faint and often invisible.',1,NULL,NULL),(11098,46,17159,7,'Science','en',127,'','Explain','reason','Why is a penumbra not obtained from a solar eclipse of a point source?',8,0,NULL,NULL,'A point source produces a sharp umbra with no penumbra because all rays come from a single point.',1,NULL,NULL),(11099,46,17160,7,'Science','en',133,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Which of the sounds, the roar of a lion or the hum of a mosquito has the higher pitch?',1,0,NULL,NULL,'The hum of a mosquito has the higher pitch.',1,NULL,NULL),(11100,46,17160,7,'Science','en',133,'','Answer the following','descriptive','What structures in the sitar help to produce higher or lower pitched sounds?',2,0,NULL,NULL,'The sitar uses the length, thickness and tension of its strings, the placement of the bridge, the tuning pegs, and the sympathetic strings to produce higher or lower pitches.',1,NULL,NULL),(11102,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','Sound is generated by the rhythmic ______ your friend will not be able to hear of any object.',3,0,NULL,NULL,'vibration',1,NULL,NULL),(11103,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','The frequency of sound is measured ______.',4,0,NULL,NULL,'Hz',1,NULL,NULL),(11104,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','When the ______ of sound is decreased, the loudness also decreases.',5,0,NULL,NULL,'intensity',1,NULL,NULL),(11107,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Frequency less depends upon?',6,0,NULL,NULL,'Amplitude of vibration',1,NULL,NULL),(11108,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','Frequency',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Frequency is the number of vibrations or oscillations that occur in one second. It is measured in hertz (Hz). The higher the frequency, the higher the pitch of the sound.',1,NULL,NULL),(11109,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','Frequency more oscillation is related to the length than 20000 Hz of a pendulum and the amplitude',8,0,NULL,NULL,'The frequency of a vibrating object depends mainly on its length and tension; for a pendulum, a shorter length gives a higher frequency. Amplitude does not affect frequency but changes the loudness.',1,NULL,NULL),(11110,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','Sound level',9,0,NULL,NULL,'Sound level, or intensity, is the perceived loudness of a sound. It is measured in decibels (dB) and is proportional to the square of the amplitude of vibration.',1,NULL,NULL),(11111,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','Vibrations in of its oscillation',10,0,NULL,NULL,'Vibrations in a sound-producing object are oscillatory motions that transfer energy to the surrounding medium, creating pressure waves that our ears interpret as sound.',1,NULL,NULL),(11112,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','Ultrasonic',11,0,NULL,NULL,'Ultrasonic sound has a frequency higher than 20,000 Hz, beyond human hearing. Animals like dogs and bats can detect and use these high‑frequency sounds for communication and navigation.',1,NULL,NULL),(11113,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','Measured in Hz pitch of the sound generated by a sound stretched string can be changed',12,0,NULL,NULL,'The pitch of a stretched string is measured in hertz (Hz). Changing the tension or length of the string changes its frequency, thereby altering the pitch.',1,NULL,NULL),(11114,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','Infrasonic',13,0,NULL,NULL,'Infrasonic sound has a frequency below 20 Hz, which is inaudible to humans but can be felt. It is produced by large natural events like earthquakes or by animals such as elephants.',1,NULL,NULL),(11115,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','Decibel sound Project',14,0,NULL,NULL,'The decibel (dB) is a logarithmic unit used to express sound intensity. It allows comparison of very loud and very soft sounds on a manageable scale.',1,NULL,NULL),(11116,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','Give scientific reasons. The bat, a mammal, flies during',15,0,NULL,NULL,'Bats use echolocation, emitting ultrasonic clicks and listening to the echoes that bounce back from objects. This allows them to navigate and hunt in complete darkness.',1,NULL,NULL),(11117,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','In earlier times, people used to the night manoeuvering with the listen for the arrival of a distant help of the ultrasonic sounds it train by putting their ear to the produces. Find out more about rail.',16,0,NULL,NULL,'Historically, people used low‑frequency sounds (infrasonic) to communicate over long distances, such as in railways or for signaling in remote areas, before electronic communication.',1,NULL,NULL),(11118,46,17160,7,'Science','en',135,'','Explain','explain','The sounds generated by a tabla and a sitar are different',17,0,NULL,NULL,'The tabla produces percussive sounds from struck membranes, while the sitar produces melodic tones from vibrating strings. Their timbres differ due to the materials, construction, and playing techniques.',1,NULL,NULL),(11119,46,17161,7,'Science','en',136,'','Answer the following','mcq','Which metals are used for making magnets?',1,0,NULL,'[\"Iron, cobalt and nickel\",\"Gold, silver and copper\",\"Lead, tin and zinc\",\"None of the above\"]','Iron, cobalt and nickel',1,NULL,NULL),(11120,46,17161,7,'Science','en',136,'','Answer the following','fill_blank','Magnets are made from alloys of ______, ______ and ______.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'iron, cobalt, nickel',1,NULL,NULL),(11121,46,17161,7,'Science','en',136,'','Answer the following','fill_blank','1. Magnetising a steel bar ______ aeaeyr',5,0,NULL,NULL,'(question incomplete – original text corrupted)',1,NULL,NULL),(11122,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','fill_blank','A magnetic field can pass through ______.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'non‑magnetic materials such as cardboard, water and plastic',1,NULL,NULL),(11123,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','fill_blank','The real test of a magnet is ______ a compass.',7,1,'46/139.jpg',NULL,'to see if it repels or attracts a compass needle',1,NULL,NULL),(11124,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Describe the function of a magnetic needle in a compass.',9,0,NULL,NULL,'It aligns with the Earth’s magnetic field, pointing north‑south, allowing direction to be read.',1,NULL,NULL),(11125,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Explain how the intensity of a magnetic field can be determined.',10,0,NULL,NULL,'By counting the number of magnetic lines of force per unit area perpendicular to the area.',1,NULL,NULL),(11126,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','short_answer','What is the highest point of a magnetic field?',11,0,NULL,NULL,'The point where the density of magnetic lines is greatest, usually near the poles.',1,NULL,NULL),(11127,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','short_answer','What happens when two like poles of magnets are brought close to each other?',12,0,NULL,NULL,'They repel each other.',1,NULL,NULL),(11128,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Describe how merchants of olden times used a cupboard magnet while travelling.',13,0,NULL,NULL,'They used it to attract iron objects and keep them in place during travel.',1,NULL,NULL),(11129,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','short_answer','What is repulsion in magnetism?',14,0,NULL,NULL,'The force that pushes like poles apart.',1,NULL,NULL),(11130,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','activity','A magnet Project',15,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(11131,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','short_answer','What is a magnetic?',16,0,NULL,NULL,'(question incomplete – original text corrupted)',1,NULL,NULL),(11132,46,17161,7,'Science','en',140,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Obtain information about the pole needle function of metal detectors.',17,0,NULL,NULL,'Metal detectors use electromagnets to detect metallic objects by sensing changes in magnetic field.',1,NULL,NULL),(11133,46,17162,7,'Science','en',146,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','The ______ is used while defining celestial coordinates.',1,0,NULL,NULL,'celestial sphere',1,NULL,NULL),(11134,46,17162,7,'Science','en',146,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','A sea (c) Is it wrong to say that the planets, ______?',2,0,NULL,NULL,'are moving in a straight line',1,NULL,NULL),(11135,46,17162,7,'Science','en',146,'','Answer the following','reason','Classified according to seasons, one season will have ______ nakshatras. Why?',3,0,NULL,NULL,'nine',1,NULL,NULL),(11136,46,17162,7,'Science','en',146,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','The rising of the sun in the east and its setting in the west is the ______ motion of the sun.',4,0,NULL,NULL,'apparent',1,NULL,NULL),(11137,46,17162,7,'Science','en',146,'','Answer the following','activity','What points should be considered before a sky watch? Write a paragraph on the birth and lifecycle of stars using figure 20.1. Project: Visit a planetarium, collect information and present it in your school on Science day.',5,1,'46/145.jpg',NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(11138,46,17162,7,'Science','en',146,'','Answer the following','reason','A star rises at 8 pm tonight. At what time will it rise after a month? Why?',6,0,NULL,NULL,'About 4 minutes later, because the Earth rotates 360° in 24 hours, so each day the star rises 4 minutes later.',1,NULL,NULL),(11163,47,17163,7,'Science','mr',14,'','उत्तर द्या','one_sentence','पायांचा त्यांना कशासाठी उपयोग होतो?',1,0,NULL,NULL,'पायांचा उपयोग चालणे, उडी मारणे, पोहणे आणि अन्न शोधण्यासाठी होतो.',1,NULL,NULL),(11164,47,17163,7,'Science','mr',14,'','उत्तर द्या','one_sentence','बेडूक पाण्यात असताना कशाद्वारे श्वसन करतो?',2,0,NULL,NULL,'बेडूक पाण्यात त्वचेच्या माध्यमातून श्वसन करतो आणि पाण्याखाली फुप्फुसाद्वारेही श्वसन करतो.',1,NULL,NULL),(11165,47,17163,7,'Science','mr',14,'','उत्तर द्या','one_sentence','बेडकाच्या मागच्या लांब पायांचा कशासाठी उपयोग होतो?',3,0,NULL,NULL,'मागच्या लांब पायांचा उपयोग उडी मारण्यासाठी आणि जलद पोहण्यासाठी होतो.',1,NULL,NULL),(11166,47,17163,7,'Science','mr',14,'','उत्तर द्या','one_sentence','बदक पाण्यात असताना ओले का होत नाही?',4,0,NULL,NULL,'बदकाच्या पायांवरील पडदे आणि पिसांच्या तेलकटपणामुळे पाणी ओघळून जात नाही.',1,NULL,NULL),(11168,47,17163,7,'Science','mr',19,'','उत्तर द्या','one_sentence','कमळाची फुले व पाने कीटकांना कसे आकर्षित करतात?',5,0,NULL,NULL,'कमळाच्या फुलांमध्ये आणि पानांमध्ये गोड सुगंध व रंग असतो ज्यामुळे कीटक आकर्षित होतात.',1,NULL,NULL),(11169,47,17163,7,'Science','mr',19,'','उत्