3,0,NULL,NULL,'15',1,NULL,NULL),(14704,56,17316,8,'Maths','mr',129,'','विभाजित स्तंभालेख दाखवा.','solve','ही माहिती विभाजित स्तंभालेखाने दाखवा.',4,0,NULL,NULL,'उपलब्ध नाही',1,NULL,NULL),(14705,56,17316,8,'Maths','mr',129,'','शतमान स्तंभालेख दाखवा.','solve','ही माहिती शतमान स्तंभालेखाने दाखवा.',5,0,NULL,NULL,'उपलब्ध नाही',1,NULL,NULL),(14706,56,17316,8,'Maths','mr',130,'','समीकरणे सोडवा.','solve','17x² – 8x + 1746 = 11(x + 5) + 15(x + 3)',6,0,NULL,NULL,'x = 16',1,NULL,NULL),(14707,56,17316,8,'Maths','mr',130,'','समीकरणे सोडवा.','solve','5(1 – 2x) = 9(1 – x)',7,0,NULL,NULL,'x = 7',1,NULL,NULL),(14708,56,17316,8,'Maths','mr',130,'','समभुज त्रिकोणाचे कर्ण काढा.','solve','समभुज त्रिकोणाची बाजू 13 से. कर्ण किती?',8,0,NULL,NULL,'13√3 से',1,NULL,NULL),(14714,56,17316,8,'Maths','mr',130,'','शेतजमिनीचे क्षेत्रफळ काढा.','solve','एका शेतजमिनीचा आकार चौकोनी आहे. त्याच्या चार कोपऱ्यांना 1”, 2, 3, 4 ही नावे देऊन घेतलेली मोजमापे: 170 मी, 250 मी, 100 मी, 260 मी. या शेतजमिनीचे क्षेत्रफळ हेक्टरमध्ये काढा.',9,0,NULL,NULL,'4 हेक्टर',1,NULL,NULL),(14715,56,17316,8,'Maths','mr',130,'','ग्रंथालयातील पुस्तकांची एकूण संख्या काढा.','solve','एका ग्रंथालयातील एकूण पुस्तकांच्या 50% मराठीची आहेत. मराठीच्या पुस्तकांच्या 25% इंग्रजीची आहेत. इंग्रजीच्या पुस्तकांच्या 25% गणिताची आहेत. उरलेली 560 पुस्तके इतर विषयांची आहेत. तर त्या ग्रंथालयात एकूण किती पुस्तके आहेत?',10,0,NULL,NULL,'1920',1,NULL,NULL),(14736,57,17317,8,'Science','en',15,'','True/False','true_false','Lactobacilli are harmful bacteria.',1,0,NULL,NULL,'false',1,NULL,NULL),(14737,57,17317,8,'Science','en',15,'','True/False','true_false','Cell wall of fungi is made up of chitin.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'true',1,NULL,NULL),(14738,57,17317,8,'Science','en',15,'','True/False','true_false','Organ of locomotion in amoeba is pseudopodia.',3,0,NULL,NULL,'true',1,NULL,NULL),(14739,57,17317,8,'Science','en',15,'','True/False','true_false','Tomato wilt is a viral disease.',4,0,NULL,NULL,'true',1,NULL,NULL),(14740,57,17317,8,'Science','en',15,'','Short answer','short_answer','State the merits of Whittaker’s method of classification.',5,0,NULL,NULL,'It is simple, based on cell type and nutrition, and easy to remember.',1,NULL,NULL),(14741,57,17318,8,'Science','en',16,'','Answer the following','define','What do we mean by pathogen?',1,0,NULL,NULL,'A microorganism that can cause disease.',1,NULL,NULL),(14742,57,17318,8,'Science','en',16,'','Answer the following','define','What do we mean by infectious diseases?',2,0,NULL,NULL,'Diseases that spread through contaminated air, water, food or vectors.',1,NULL,NULL),(14743,57,17318,8,'Science','en',16,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Infection through sputum',3,0,NULL,NULL,'Infection that spreads by coughing or spitting, especially in tuberculosis.',1,NULL,NULL),(14744,57,17318,8,'Science','en',17,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Pulse polio drive, b. WHO',4,0,NULL,NULL,'Information about polio vaccination campaigns and WHO guidelines.',1,NULL,NULL),(14745,57,17318,8,'Science','en',17,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Where do you see the water‑logged materials shown in picture?',5,1,'57/016.jpg',NULL,'In stagnant water bodies such as ponds, drains or puddles.',1,NULL,NULL),(14746,57,17318,8,'Science','en',17,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Which hazards do you anticipate with the help of picture?',6,1,'57/016.jpg',NULL,'Risk of mosquito breeding, waterborne diseases, contamination of drinking water.',1,NULL,NULL),(14748,57,17318,8,'Science','en',18,'','Answer the following','one_word','Acute fever and headache, vomiting',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Symptoms',1,NULL,NULL),(14749,57,17318,8,'Science','en',18,'','Answer the following','one_word','Pains in eye socket is very prominent symptom',8,0,NULL,NULL,'Symptom',1,NULL,NULL),(14750,57,17318,8,'Science','en',18,'','Answer the following','one_word','Decrease in platelet count that may lead to internal hemorrhage',9,0,NULL,NULL,'Complication',1,NULL,NULL),(14751,57,17318,8,'Science','en',18,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Observe the pictures shown in following and write description in the boxes. Which measures are taken for mosquito control by grampanchayat, municipal council, municipality of your area?',10,1,'57/017.jpg',NULL,'Measures include eliminating standing water, using insecticide nets, spraying insecticides, community clean‑up drives.',1,NULL,NULL),(14752,57,17318,8,'Science','en',20,'','Answer the following','one_word','Incurable scar and inflammation',11,0,NULL,NULL,'Complication',1,NULL,NULL),(14753,57,17318,8,'Science','en',20,'','Answer the following','one_word','Lumps in breast',12,0,NULL,NULL,'Symptom',1,NULL,NULL),(14754,57,17318,8,'Science','en',20,'','Answer the following','one_word','Unexplained weight‑loss',13,0,NULL,NULL,'Symptom',1,NULL,NULL),(14755,57,17318,8,'Science','en',20,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Diabetes: The hormone‑insulin produced. Symptoms which cannot be ignored: Frequent urination at night, increased glucose in blood. Reasons for diabetes: Heredity, obesity, lack of physical exercise, mental stress. Preventive measures: Following the proper diet, medicines and exercise under the supervision of doctor.',14,0,NULL,NULL,'Diabetes is a metabolic disorder caused by insufficient insulin.',1,NULL,NULL),(14768,57,17319,8,'Science','en',26,'','Difference','difference','If two forces are applied on one object in directions opposite to each other, a force equal to their ______ acts on the object',1,0,NULL,NULL,'difference',1,NULL,NULL),(14769,57,17319,8,'Science','en',26,'','Solve','solve','A force is expressed in magnitude and direction. Force is a vector quantity. When a force is applied on a stationary object it moves, its speed and direction change. Similarly, a force is required to stop an object in motion. An object can change its shape due to force. While kneading a dough made from flour, the dough changes its shape when a force is applied. A potter applies a force in a specific direction while shaping the pot. Rubber band, when stretched, expands.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'A force is a vector quantity that changes speed, direction and shape of objects.',1,NULL,NULL),(14775,57,17319,8,'Science','en',32,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','The SI unit of force is ______',3,1,'57/031.jpg',NULL,'Newton (N)',1,'57/031_2.jpg',NULL),(14778,57,17319,8,'Science','en',32,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','The SI unit of pressure is ______',4,1,'57/031.jpg',NULL,'Pascal (Pa)',1,'57/031_2.jpg',NULL),(14786,57,17319,8,'Science','en',32,'','Short answer','short_answer','A plastic cube is released in water. Will it sink or come to the surface of water?',5,0,NULL,NULL,'It will float to the surface if its density is less than that of water.',1,NULL,NULL),(14787,57,17319,8,'Science','en',32,'','Reason','reason','Why do load‑carrying heavy vehicles have a large number of wheels?',6,0,NULL,NULL,'More wheels distribute the weight over a larger area, reducing pressure on the road and preventing damage.',1,NULL,NULL),(14788,57,17319,8,'Science','en',32,'','Descriptive','descriptive','Explain how the volume of water displaced by a submerged box relates to its buoyancy.',7,0,NULL,NULL,'The displaced water’s weight equals the buoyant force; if this exceeds the box’s weight, the box floats; otherwise it sinks.',1,NULL,NULL),(14789,57,17319,8,'Science','en',32,'','Descriptive','descriptive','A plastic sealed box of volume 350 cm³ and mass 500 g is placed in water. Will it float or sink? What will be the mass of water displaced?',8,0,NULL,NULL,'It will sink because its density (≈1.43 g/cm³) is greater than water’s. The displaced water mass equals the box’s mass, 500 g.',1,NULL,NULL),(14792,57,17319,8,'Science','en',32,'','Descriptive','descriptive','If a stationary bus suddenly speeds up, passengers are thrown in the backward direction. Explain the cause.',9,0,NULL,NULL,'Inertia causes passengers to resist the change in motion, so they lag behind as the bus accelerates forward.',1,NULL,NULL),(14793,57,17320,8,'Science','en',37,'','Write proper words from the following group of words in the blanks.','fill_blank','Electric cells having ___ potential difference of 2V are connected in the form of a battery. What will be the total potential difference of the battery in both cases?',1,1,'57/036.jpg',NULL,'5 V',1,'57/036_2.jpg',NULL),(14794,57,17320,8,'Science','en',37,'','In an electric circuit, electrons flow from a point of potential to the point of lower potential. The potential difference of the battery will be ___','fill_blank','In an electric circuit, electrons flow from a point of potential to the point of lower potential. The potential difference of the battery will be ___',2,0,NULL,NULL,'V',1,NULL,NULL),(14795,57,17320,8,'Science','en',37,'','Describe the difference between the electrostatic potential of the positive end and the negative end.','short_answer','Describe the difference between the electrostatic potential of the positive end and the negative end.',3,0,NULL,NULL,'The positive end has higher potential energy for a positive charge, while the negative end has lower potential energy.',1,NULL,NULL),(14796,57,17320,8,'Science','en',37,'','Present all the activities that you performed in this chapter in Science exhibition.','short_answer','Present all the activities that you performed in this chapter in Science exhibition.',4,0,NULL,NULL,'(Answer should list the activities performed, e.g., magnetic needle experiment, electromagnet experiment, electric bell demonstration, etc.)',1,NULL,NULL),(14797,57,17320,8,'Science','en',37,'','Show how you will connect the wires by drawing a diagram.','draw','A battery is to be formed by joining 3 dry cells with connecting wires. Show how you will connect the wires by drawing a diagram.',5,1,'57/036.jpg',NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(14798,57,17320,8,'Science','en',37,'','If the bulb is not glowing, which tests will you perform to find out the reason?','short_answer','In an electric circuit, a battery and a bulb have been connected and the battery consists of two cells of equal potential difference. If the bulb is not glowing, which tests will you perform to find out the reason?',6,0,NULL,NULL,'Check the connections, test the battery with a multimeter, replace the bulb, and ensure the plug key is closed.',1,NULL,NULL),(14799,57,17321,8,'Science','en',46,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Write down the number of electrons present in the outermost shell of each of the following elements: H, Na, Cl, N.',1,0,NULL,NULL,'H:1, Na:1, Cl:1, N:1',1,NULL,NULL),(14800,57,17321,8,'Science','en',46,'','Answer the following','short_answer','For Li, C, Mg, O write the electronic configuration, number of valence electrons and valency.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'Li: 1s²2s¹ – 1 valence electron – valency 1; C: 1s²2s²2p² – 4 valence electrons – valency 4; Mg: 1s²2s²2p⁶3s² – 2 valence electrons – valency 2; O: 1s²2s²2p⁴ – 2 valence electrons – valency 2',1,NULL,NULL),(14801,57,17321,8,'Science','en',46,'','Answer the following','explain','Why are the atomic numbers and atomic mass numbers always in whole numbers?',3,0,NULL,NULL,'Because they count discrete particles (protons, neutrons) which are indivisible.',1,NULL,NULL),(14802,57,17321,8,'Science','en',46,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Sulphur contains 16 protons and 16 neutrons. What would be its atomic number and mass number?',4,0,NULL,NULL,'Atomic number = 16, Mass number = 32',1,NULL,NULL),(14803,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Answer the following','difference','What is the difference between the atomic models of Thomson and Rutherford?',5,0,NULL,NULL,'Thomson’s plum‑pudding model has a diffuse positive charge with electrons embedded; Rutherford’s model has a small, dense positively charged nucleus with electrons orbiting around it.',1,NULL,NULL),(14804,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Answer the following','fill_blank','Magnesium is ______0.0+',6,0,NULL,NULL,'2',1,NULL,NULL),(14805,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Answer the following','explain','Explain how the atomic mass of carbon is 12 and 13 and 14 for its isotopes C‑12, C‑13, C‑14.',7,0,NULL,NULL,'The mass number equals the sum of protons (6) and neutrons (6,7,8 respectively).',1,NULL,NULL),(14806,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Give scientific reasons','match','Match the subatomic particles with their properties.',8,0,NULL,'[\"Proton | positively charged, mass ≈1 u, in nucleus\",\"Electron | negatively charged, mass ≈1\\/1800 u, in orbit\",\"Neutron | neutral, mass ≈1 u, in nucleus\"]','Proton – positively charged, mass ≈1 u, in nucleus; Electron – negatively charged, mass ≈1/1800 u, in orbit; Neutron – neutral, mass ≈1 u, in nucleus',1,NULL,'[[\"Proton\",\"positively charged, mass ≈1 u, in nucleus\"],[\"Electron\",\"negatively charged, mass ≈1\\/1800 u, in orbit\"],[\"Neutron\",\"neutral, mass ≈1 u, in nucleus\"]]'),(14807,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Give scientific reasons','short_answer','All the mass of an atom is concentrated in the nucleus.',9,0,NULL,'[\"True\",\"False\"]','True',1,NULL,NULL),(14808,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Give scientific reasons','match','Match the pairs.',10,0,NULL,'[\"Proton | Positively Charged\",\"Electron | Negatively charged\",\"Neutron | Neutral\"]','Proton – Positively Charged; Electron – Negatively charged; Neutron – Neutral',1,NULL,'[[\"Proton\",\"Positively Charged\"],[\"Electron\",\"Negatively charged\"],[\"Neutron\",\"Neutral\"]]'),(14809,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Give scientific reasons','short_answer','Atomic mass number is a whole number.',11,0,NULL,'[\"True\",\"False\"]','True',1,NULL,NULL),(14810,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Give scientific reasons','match','Match the pairs.',12,1,'57/047.jpg','[\"Proton | Positively Charged\",\"Electron | Negatively charged electrical charge, mass and location\",\"Neutron | Neutral\"]','Proton – Positively Charged; Electron – Negatively charged electrical charge, mass and location; Neutron – Neutral',1,NULL,'[[\"Proton\",\"Positively Charged\"],[\"Electron\",\"Negatively charged electrical charge, mass and location\"],[\"Neutron\",\"Neutral\"]]'),(14811,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Define the following terms','define','Atom, Isotope, Atomic number, Atomic mass number, Moderator',13,0,NULL,NULL,'Atom – basic unit of matter; Isotope – atoms of same element with different mass numbers; Atomic number – number of protons; Atomic mass number – total of protons and neutrons; Moderator – material that slows neutrons in a reactor.',1,NULL,NULL),(14813,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Draw a neat labelled diagram','draw','Rutherford’s scattering experiment',14,1,'57/047.jpg',NULL,NULL,1,'57/047_1.jpg',NULL),(14814,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Explain','explain','Diagrammatic sketch of electronic configuration of Magnesium (Atomic number 12).',15,1,'57/047.jpg',NULL,'K‑shell: 2 electrons, L‑shell: 8 electrons, M‑shell: 2 electrons.',1,NULL,NULL),(14815,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Explain','explain','Diagrammatic sketch of electronic configuration of Argon (Atomic number 18).',16,1,'57/047.jpg',NULL,'K‑shell: 2 electrons, L‑shell: 8 electrons, M‑shell: 8 electrons.',1,NULL,NULL),(14816,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','Electron, proton, neutron are the types of ______ in an atom.',17,0,NULL,NULL,'subatomic particles',1,NULL,NULL),(14817,57,17321,8,'Science','en',48,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','An electron carries a ______ charge.',18,0,NULL,NULL,'negative',1,NULL,NULL),(14855,57,17323,8,'Science','en',59,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','What are the metals and nonmetals that we use in everyday life?',1,0,NULL,NULL,'Metals such as gold, silver, iron, copper, aluminium, magnesium, calcium, sodium, platinum; non‑metals such as carbon, sulphur, phosphorus, chlorine, iodine, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, bromine.',1,NULL,NULL),(14856,57,17323,8,'Science','en',59,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','Explain the property of ductility in metals.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'Ductility is the ability of a metal to be drawn into a wire; it is shown when a metal is pulled through a hole and a thin wire is formed.',1,NULL,NULL),(14857,57,17323,8,'Science','en',59,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','Why are metals good conductors of heat?',3,0,NULL,NULL,'Because they have free electrons that can transfer thermal energy efficiently.',1,NULL,NULL),(14858,57,17323,8,'Science','en',59,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','Which metal is used to make electrical wires and why?',4,0,NULL,NULL,'Copper, because it is a good conductor of electricity and is inexpensive.',1,NULL,NULL),(14859,57,17323,8,'Science','en',59,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','What is lustre and how does it change after cleaning a metal surface?',5,0,NULL,NULL,'Lustre is the shine of a metal surface; cleaning removes tarnish and restores the shiny appearance.',1,NULL,NULL),(14860,57,17323,8,'Science','en',59,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','Explain why mercury and gallium are exceptions to the rule that metals are solid at room temperature.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'Mercury and gallium are liquid at room temperature because their atomic structure allows them to stay in liquid form.',1,NULL,NULL),(14861,57,17323,8,'Science','en',59,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','Why are most metals hard?',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Because of strong metallic bonding that holds atoms tightly together.',1,NULL,NULL),(14862,57,17323,8,'Science','en',59,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','What is sonority in metals and give an example of a metal used to make a bell.',8,0,NULL,NULL,'Sonority is the ringing sound produced when a metal is struck; iron is commonly used to make bells.',1,NULL,NULL),(14866,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Fill in the blanks.','fill_blank','Ductility, brittleness, sonority, ________ are properties of metals.',9,0,NULL,NULL,'rust',1,NULL,NULL),(14867,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Fill in the blanks.','fill_blank','Carbon, bromine, sulphur, phosphorus are ________.',10,0,NULL,NULL,'non‑metals',1,NULL,NULL),(14868,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Answer the following.','reason','Why is the nail in test tube 2 not rusted?',11,0,NULL,NULL,'Because the test tube was dry air, preventing moisture needed for rust formation.',1,NULL,NULL),(14869,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Answer the following.','reason','Why would the nail in test tube 3 get rusted?',12,0,NULL,NULL,'Because test tube 3 contained water, providing moisture for rust formation.',1,NULL,NULL),(14870,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Answer the following.','reason','Why are copper and brass vessels cleaned with lemon?',13,0,NULL,NULL,'Lemon juice is acidic and removes tarnish from copper and brass.',1,NULL,NULL),(14871,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Answer the following.','reason','Why is sodium metal kept in kerosene?',14,0,NULL,NULL,'Kerosene prevents sodium from reacting with air and moisture.',1,NULL,NULL),(14872,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Answer the following.','activity','How is the \'Varkha\' or silver foil used in sweets made?',15,0,NULL,NULL,'Varkha is spread on sweets to give a shiny, metallic finish and to preserve them.',1,NULL,NULL),(14873,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','What is done to prevent corrosion of metals?',16,0,NULL,NULL,'Coatings such as oil, varnish, paint or plating with non‑corroding metals are applied to protect the surface.',1,NULL,NULL),(14874,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','What are the metals that make the alloys brass and bronze?',17,0,NULL,NULL,'Brass is made of copper and zinc; bronze is made of copper and tin.',1,NULL,NULL),(14875,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','What are the adverse effects of corrosion?',18,0,NULL,NULL,'Corrosion weakens metals, causes rust, reduces strength, and can lead to structural failure.',1,NULL,NULL),(14876,57,17323,8,'Science','en',63,'','Answer the following.','descriptive','What are uses of noble metals?',19,0,NULL,NULL,'Noble metals are used in jewelry, medicine, electronics, and as catalysts.',1,NULL,NULL),(14877,57,17324,8,'Science','en',65,'','Answer the following','reason','Why is it said that air is a homogeneous mixture of different gases?',1,1,'57/064.jpg',NULL,'Because the gases are uniformly mixed throughout the atmosphere, giving the same composition at any point.',1,'57/064_1.jpg',NULL),(14878,57,17324,8,'Science','en',65,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Which different hazardous gases are released through fuel combustion?',2,1,'57/064.jpg',NULL,'Sulphur dioxide, nitrogen oxides, carbon monoxide, particulate matter, and methane.',1,'57/064_1.jpg',NULL),(14886,57,17324,8,'Science','en',65,'','Answer the following','short_answer','What are reasons other than the above mentioned responsible for air pollution?',3,1,'57/064.jpg',NULL,'Industrial processes, vehicular emissions, biomass burning, and waste incineration.',1,NULL,NULL),(14887,57,17324,8,'Science','en',65,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Do vehicles with two‑stroke engines cause more pollution than four‑stroke engines?',4,1,'57/064.jpg',NULL,'Yes, because two‑stroke engines have incomplete combustion and emit more pollutants.',1,NULL,NULL),(14888,57,17324,8,'Science','en',68,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Why is water referred to as ‘molecule Water Pollutants of life’?',5,1,'57/067.jpg',NULL,'Because water contains molecules that can carry pollutants, making it a medium for pollution.',1,NULL,NULL),(14889,57,17324,8,'Science','en',71,'','Answer the following','define','What is pollution?',6,0,NULL,NULL,'The presence of substances that harm living organisms and the environment.',1,NULL,NULL),(14890,57,17324,8,'Science','en',71,'','Answer the following','define','What is acid rain?',8,0,NULL,NULL,'Rain that contains acidic compounds formed from sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides.',1,NULL,NULL),(14891,57,17324,8,'Science','en',71,'','Answer the following','short_answer','What are visible pollutants known to occur after eating ‘pani puri’?',9,0,NULL,NULL,'Foodborne bacteria and parasites.',1,NULL,NULL),(14892,57,17324,8,'Science','en',71,'','Answer the following','short_answer','What are invisible pollutants?',10,0,NULL,NULL,'Gases like CO, NOx, SO₂, and ozone that cannot be seen.',1,NULL,NULL),(14894,57,17324,8,'Science','en',71,'','Answer the following','short_answer','Give two examples of each of water, soil, and air pollution.',11,0,NULL,NULL,'Water: sewage, pesticides; Soil: heavy metals, fertilizers; Air: CO₂, particulate matter.',1,NULL,NULL),(14895,57,17324,8,'Science','en',71,'','Answer the following','short_answer','People living in busy squares face which respiratory problems?',12,0,NULL,NULL,'Shortness of breath, asthma, and irritation of the eyes.',1,NULL,NULL),(14896,57,17324,8,'Science','en',71,'','Answer the following','short_answer','How does pollution occur due to vehicles?',13,0,NULL,NULL,'By emitting exhaust gases and particulates from engines.',1,NULL,NULL),(14904,57,17324,8,'Science','en',71,'','True or false','true_false','Water does not get polluted by washing the cloths in running water of river.',15,0,NULL,NULL,'false',1,NULL,NULL),(14905,57,17324,8,'Science','en',71,'','True or false','true_false','More the use of electric appliances, more will be the pollution.',16,0,NULL,NULL,'true',1,NULL,NULL),(14906,57,17325,8,'Science','en',75,'','activity','activity','Rivers get flooded and change their paths. classroom and ______— suggest',1,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(14907,57,17325,8,'Science','en',75,'','activity','activity','Displacement of waterfalls, formation of artificial preventive measures water reservoirs',2,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(14908,57,17325,8,'Science','en',75,'','activity','activity','There is live and financial loss on a large scale, as trees, buildings, rocks on sloppy area collapse on low-lying land',3,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(14909,57,17