,8,'Science','en',81,'','Explain','explain','During starvation, lysosomes digest stored proteins, fats.',8,0,NULL,NULL,'It recycles nutrients when food is scarce.',1,NULL,NULL),(14935,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Describe','descriptive','RBCs had mitochondria',9,0,NULL,NULL,'RBCs would consume oxygen for energy production, reducing oxygen transport.',1,NULL,NULL),(14936,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Describe','difference','There had been no difference between mitochondria and plastids',10,0,NULL,NULL,'Both would perform similar functions, losing energy production or photosynthesis specialization.',1,NULL,NULL),(14937,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Describe','descriptive','Genes had been absent on the chromosomes',11,0,NULL,NULL,'Cells would lack hereditary information and cannot reproduce.',1,NULL,NULL),(14938,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Describe','descriptive','Plasma membrane had not been selectively permeable',12,0,NULL,NULL,'Cells would lose homeostasis and could burst or shrink.',1,NULL,NULL),(14939,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Describe','descriptive','Plants lacked anthocyanin',13,0,NULL,NULL,'Flowers and fruits would not show red/purple colours.',1,NULL,NULL),(14940,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Choose the odd one','odd_one','Nucleolus, mitochondria, plastids, and endoplasmic reticulum',14,0,NULL,'[\"Nucleolus\",\"Mitochondria\",\"Plastids\",\"Endoplasmic reticulum\"]','Nucleolus',1,NULL,NULL),(14941,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Choose the odd one','odd_one','DNA, Ribosomes, Chlorophyll',15,0,NULL,'[\"DNA\",\"Ribosomes\",\"Chlorophyll\"]','Chlorophyll',1,NULL,NULL),(14942,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Choose the odd one','odd_one','Plasma membrane',16,0,NULL,'[\"Plasma membrane\"]','Plasma membrane',1,NULL,NULL),(14943,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Choose the odd one','odd_one','Cytoplasm',17,0,NULL,'[\"Cytoplasm\"]','Cytoplasm',1,NULL,NULL),(14944,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Choose the odd one','odd_one','Lysosome',18,0,NULL,'[\"Lysosome\"]','Lysosome',1,NULL,NULL),(14945,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Choose the odd one','odd_one','Vacuole',19,0,NULL,'[\"Vacuole\"]','Vacuole',1,NULL,NULL),(14946,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Choose the odd one','odd_one','Nucleus',20,0,NULL,'[\"Nucleus\"]','Nucleus',1,NULL,NULL),(14948,57,17326,8,'Science','en',84,'','Choose the correct option','mcq','Who gives me the colour? (Select the correct option)',21,0,NULL,'[\"Red tomato – Chlorophyll\",\"Green leaf – Carotene\",\"Carrot – Anthocyanin\",\"Violet – Lycopene\"]','0',1,NULL,NULL),(14965,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','Heart beats ______ ml',1,0,NULL,NULL,'350',1,NULL,NULL),(14966,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','RBCs contain ______ an',2,0,NULL,NULL,'hemoglobin',1,NULL,NULL),(14967,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','WBCs are ______',3,0,NULL,NULL,'nucleated',1,NULL,NULL),(14968,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','Blood donation is ______',4,0,NULL,NULL,'voluntary',1,NULL,NULL),(14969,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','Normal body temperature is ______°C',5,0,NULL,NULL,'37',1,NULL,NULL),(14970,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','pH of oxygenated blood is ______',6,0,NULL,NULL,'7.4',1,NULL,NULL),(14971,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Complete the table','short_answer','Organ systems – Organs – Functions',7,1,'57/091.jpg','[\"A: Respiratory system | B: Trachea, alveoli, diaphragm | C: Air exchange\",\"D: Circulatory system | E: Heart, arteries, veins | F: Blood transport\"]','A-B-C, D-E-F',1,'57/091_3.jpg',NULL),(14972,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Draw','draw','Respiratory system – Trachea, alveoli, diaphragm, capillaries',8,1,'57/091.jpg',NULL,NULL,1,'57/091_3.jpg',NULL),(14973,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Draw','draw','Internal structure of heart – Neutrophils, globulins, albumins',9,1,'57/091.jpg',NULL,NULL,1,'57/091_3.jpg',NULL),(14974,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Short answer','short_answer','Explain why a person with blood group \'O\' is called a universal donor.',10,0,NULL,NULL,'Because type O has no A or B antigens on red cells, so it can be transfused to any recipient.',1,NULL,NULL),(14975,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Descriptive','descriptive','Food must have limited amount of salts. Why is this important?',11,0,NULL,NULL,'High salt intake raises blood pressure and strains the heart and kidneys.',1,NULL,NULL),(14976,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Descriptive','descriptive','Your neighboring uncle has been diagnosed with hypertension. What should he do to keep his blood pressure within normal range?',12,0,NULL,NULL,'Maintain a healthy weight, exercise regularly, reduce salt intake, avoid smoking, and take prescribed medication.',1,NULL,NULL),(14977,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Explain','explain','Explain the importance of blood donation.',13,0,NULL,NULL,'Blood donation saves lives, maintains blood supply for emergencies, and promotes community health.',1,NULL,NULL),(14978,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Difference','difference','Explain the difference between arteries and veins.',14,0,NULL,NULL,'Arteries carry oxygenated blood away from the heart (except pulmonary artery) and have thick walls; veins carry deoxygenated blood to the heart (except pulmonary vein) and have valves.',1,NULL,NULL),(14979,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Difference','difference','Explain the difference between external and internal respiration.',15,0,NULL,NULL,'External respiration is gas exchange between air and blood in lungs; internal respiration is gas exchange between blood and tissues.',1,NULL,NULL),(14980,57,17327,8,'Science','en',92,'','Difference','difference','Which health parameters of a blood donor should be checked?',16,0,NULL,NULL,'Blood pressure, hemoglobin level, weight, age, and overall health status.',1,NULL,NULL),(14981,57,17328,8,'Science','en',93,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Do all food items taste similar?',1,0,NULL,NULL,'No, food items taste different: lemon is sour, sugar is sweet, salt is salty, etc.',1,NULL,NULL),(14982,57,17328,8,'Science','en',95,'','Answer the following','reason','Collect soil samples from your surroundings and find out whether it is acidic, alkaline or neutral.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'Use a litmus test or pH paper to determine the soil\'s acidity.',1,NULL,NULL),(14983,57,17328,8,'Science','en',95,'','Answer the following','reason','Which substances are used to clean organic acids? They are mild in nature and greenish stains on copper vessels and to hence are not harmful like mineral acids shine blackish silver utensils?',3,0,NULL,NULL,'Mild organic acids such as acetic acid are used; they do not corrode metals like strong mineral acids.',1,NULL,NULL),(14984,57,17328,8,'Science','en',95,'','Answer the following','reason','Why is tooth‑paste used for brushing teeth?',4,0,NULL,NULL,'Tooth‑paste contains mild acids and bases that clean teeth and neutralise acids produced by bacteria.',1,NULL,NULL),(14985,57,17328,8,'Science','en',95,'','Answer the following','reason','List some natural acids.',5,0,NULL,NULL,'Citric acid (lemon), acetic acid (vinegar), tartaric acid (grape), oxalic acid (spinach).',1,NULL,NULL),(14986,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','solve','Explain why acids are used in the production of chemical fertilizers.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'Acids react with metal oxides to form metal salts (fertilizers) and release heat, aiding production.',1,NULL,NULL),(14987,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','solve','List some industrial uses of acids.',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Acids are used in oil purification, medicine, dyes, paints, and explosives.',1,NULL,NULL),(14988,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','solve','Explain the role of hydrochloric acid in the preparation of potassium chloride.',8,0,NULL,NULL,'HCl reacts with KOH to form KCl and water, producing the salt.',1,NULL,NULL),(14989,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','solve','Why is dilute sulphuric acid used in batteries?',9,0,NULL,NULL,'It provides ions for the electrochemical reaction without being too corrosive.',1,NULL,NULL),(14990,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','solve','Why is dilute hydrochloric acid used for sterilising water?',10,0,NULL,NULL,'It kills bacteria by acidifying the water, making it safe to drink.',1,NULL,NULL),(14991,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','reason','Why does an iron knife shine better after cutting sour fruits like lemon or raw mango?',11,0,NULL,NULL,'The acid removes rust and restores the metal surface, making it shine.',1,NULL,NULL),(14992,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','solve','Explain the role of DNA as an acid in the body.',12,0,NULL,NULL,'DNA contains phosphate groups that give it acidic properties and store genetic information.',1,NULL,NULL),(14993,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','solve','How are proteins related to acids?',13,0,NULL,NULL,'Proteins are made of amino acids, which contain both acidic and basic groups.',1,NULL,NULL),(14994,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','solve','What is a base? Give an example.',14,0,NULL,NULL,'A base is a substance that releases OH⁻ ions in water; e.g., NaOH (sodium hydroxide).',1,NULL,NULL),(14995,57,17328,8,'Science','en',96,'','Answer the following','solve','List five examples of bases.',15,0,NULL,NULL,'Sodium hydroxide, potassium hydroxide, calcium hydroxide, magnesium hydroxide, ammonium hydroxide.',1,NULL,NULL),(14996,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','reason','Why are sulphuric acids important in the chemical industry?',16,0,NULL,NULL,'They are strong, inexpensive, and react with many materials to produce useful salts.',1,NULL,NULL),(14997,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Which acid is used for producing chloride salts?',17,1,'57/097.jpg',NULL,'Hydrochloric acid is used to produce chloride salts like NaCl.',1,'57/097_1.jpg',NULL),(14998,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','descriptive','By squishing lemon on a piece of rock, what happens?',18,0,NULL,NULL,'The acid reacts with metal oxides in the rock, producing a milky solution of calcium carbonate.',1,NULL,NULL),(14999,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','descriptive','Classify the following substances into acidic, basic, and neutral groups: HCl, NaCl, MgO, KCl, CaO, H₂SO₄.',19,0,NULL,NULL,'Acidic: HCl, H₂SO₄; Basic: MgO, CaO; Neutral: NaCl, KCl.',1,NULL,NULL),(15000,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','solve','How will you find whether HNO₃, H₂O, NaCO₃ is acidic or not?',20,0,NULL,NULL,'Use litmus paper or pH paper; acids turn blue litmus red.',1,NULL,NULL),(15001,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','reason','Which substances are produced by the neutralisation process?',21,0,NULL,NULL,'Salts and water are produced when an acid reacts with a base.',1,NULL,NULL),(15002,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','reason','What are the industrial uses of acids?',22,0,NULL,NULL,'Acids are used in manufacturing fertilizers, detergents, dyes, paints, and in metal processing.',1,NULL,NULL),(15003,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','reason','Select the proper word given in brackets and fill in the blanks.',23,0,NULL,NULL,'Main constituent of acid is hydrogen ion; main constituent of base is hydroxide ion; tartaric acid is an organic acid.',1,NULL,NULL),(15004,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','fill_blank','Main constituent of acid is ________',24,0,NULL,'[\"hydrogen ion\",\"hydroxide ion\",\"oxygen atom\",\"carbon atom\"]','hydrogen ion',1,NULL,NULL),(15005,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','fill_blank','Main constituent of base is ________',25,0,NULL,'[\"hydroxide ion\",\"hydrogen ion\",\"oxygen atom\",\"carbon atom\"]','hydroxide ion',1,NULL,NULL),(15006,57,17328,8,'Science','en',98,'','Answer the following','fill_blank','Tartaric acid is a ________ acid.',26,0,NULL,'[\"organic\",\"inorganic\",\"strong\",\"weak\"]','organic',1,NULL,NULL),(15007,57,17329,8,'Science','en',99,'','Classify the following changes into physical and chemical change','short_answer','Ripening of mango, melting of ice, boiling of water, dissolution of salt in water, ripening of banana, fragrance on ripening fruit, darkening of a cut potato, bursting of an inflated balloon, sound of bursting firecracker, foul smell from a spoiled food',1,0,NULL,'[\"Physical change\",\"Chemical change\"]',NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(15008,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Identify the type of change for each item','short_answer','Rusting of iron, Fluorine, Physical change, Chemical change, Magnesium, Tendency to',2,0,NULL,'[\"Physical change\",\"Chemical change\"]',NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(15009,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','The spoiling of food is a chemical change which is recognized from the generation of certain ______ due to it','fill_blank','The spoiling of food is a chemical change which is recognized from the generation of certain ______ due to it',3,0,NULL,NULL,'smell',1,NULL,NULL),(15010,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','A colourless solution of calcium hydroxide in a test tube turns ______ on blowing in it through a blow tube for some time','fill_blank','A colourless solution of calcium hydroxide in a test tube turns ______ on blowing in it through a blow tube for some time',4,0,NULL,NULL,'milky',1,NULL,NULL),(15011,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','The white particles of baking soda disappear when put in lemon juice','fill_blank','The white particles of baking soda disappear when put in lemon juice ______.',5,0,NULL,NULL,'disappear',1,NULL,NULL),(15012,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Show with the help of diagram of electronic configuration how the following compound are formed from the constituent atoms','draw','Show how the following compounds are formed: a) Sodium chloride b) Potassium fluoride c) Water d) Hydrogen chloride',6,1,'57/103.jpg',NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(15013,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Describe the formation of the following compounds','descriptive','Sodium chloride',7,0,NULL,NULL,'Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions combine by ionic bonding to form NaCl',1,NULL,NULL),(15014,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Describe the formation of the following compounds','descriptive','Water',8,0,NULL,NULL,'Two H atoms share two electrons with an O atom to form H₂O via covalent bonding',1,NULL,NULL),(15015,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Oxygen is ______ in respiration. List the chemical changes that occur in your house and surroundings','one_sentence','Oxygen is ______ in respiration. List the chemical changes that occur in your house and surroundings',9,0,NULL,NULL,'used',1,NULL,NULL),(15016,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Chlorine (Cl₂) molecule is formed by ______ of electrons between two chlorine atoms','one_word','Chlorine (Cl₂) molecule is formed by ______ of electrons between two chlorine atoms',10,0,NULL,NULL,'sharing',1,NULL,NULL),(15017,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Explain why respiration is a chemical change','explain','Explain why respiration is a chemical change',11,0,NULL,NULL,'Glucose reacts with oxygen to produce CO₂, H₂O and energy, changing the composition of the reactants.',1,NULL,NULL),(15018,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Explain why hard water gets softened on mixing with a solution of washing soda','explain','Explain why hard water gets softened on mixing with a solution of washing soda',12,0,NULL,NULL,'Sodium carbonate reacts with Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions to form insoluble carbonates, removing hardness.',1,NULL,NULL),(15019,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Explain why limestone powder disappears on adding to dilute hydrochloric acids','explain','Explain why limestone powder disappears on adding to dilute hydrochloric acids',13,0,NULL,NULL,'CaCO₃ reacts with HCl to form CaCl₂, CO₂ gas, and H₂O, dissolving the limestone.',1,NULL,NULL),(15020,57,17329,8,'Science','en',104,'','Explain why bubbles are seen on adding lemon juice to baking soda','explain','Explain why bubbles are seen on adding lemon juice to baking soda',14,0,NULL,NULL,'Citric acid reacts with NaHCO₃ to produce CO₂ gas, forming bubbles.',1,NULL,NULL),(15021,57,17330,8,'Science','en',106,'','Solve','one_sentence','Take three similar vessels. Let us call them ‘A’, ‘B’ and ‘C’.',1,1,'57/105.jpg',NULL,NULL,1,'57/105_1.jpg',NULL),(15022,57,17330,8,'Science','en',106,'','Solve','one_sentence','Fill vessel A with hot water and vessel B with cold water. Put some water from A and B into vessel C.',2,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(15023,57,17330,8,'Science','en',106,'','Solve','one_sentence','Dip your right hand in vessel A and your left hand in vessel B, and keep them immersed for 2 to 3 minutes.',3,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(15024,57,17330,8,'Science','en',106,'','Solve','fill_blank','Now dip both hands in vessel C. What do you feel? Even though both hands are dipped in water at the same temperature, your right hand will find the water ______ while the left hand will find it ______. What is the reason for this?',4,0,NULL,NULL,'cold; hot',1,NULL,NULL),(15029,57,17330,8,'Science','en',110,'','Solve','short_answer','Suppose the masses of the calorimeter, the water in it and the hot copper object are the same. The initial temperature of the calorimeter and water is 30 °C and that of the hot object is 60 °C. The specific heats of copper and water are 0.09 cal/(gm °C) and 1 cal/(gm °C) respectively. What will be the final temperature of the water?',5,0,NULL,NULL,'32.29 °C',1,NULL,NULL),(15030,57,17330,8,'Science','en',110,'','Solve','short_answer','Using the same data as above, calculate the final temperature of the water.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'32.29 °C',1,NULL,NULL),(15031,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Solve','one_sentence','A. Whom should I pair with?',7,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,1,NULL,NULL),(15032,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Solve','short_answer','Temperature of a substance is ______?',8,0,NULL,NULL,'the measure of the average kinetic energy of its atoms',1,NULL,NULL),(15033,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','Boiling point of water',9,0,NULL,NULL,'100 °C (212 °F)',1,NULL,NULL),(15034,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','6 °F (Ans. 320 °F)',10,0,NULL,NULL,'6 °F is approximately 320 K',1,NULL,NULL),(15035,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','Room temperature',11,0,NULL,NULL,'around 25 °C (77 °F)',1,NULL,NULL),(15036,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','Freezing point of water',12,0,NULL,NULL,'0 °C (32 °F)',1,NULL,NULL),(15037,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','Heat flows from an object at higher temperature to an object at lower temperature.',13,0,NULL,NULL,'True',1,NULL,NULL),(15038,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','Joule is the unit of heat.',14,0,NULL,NULL,'True',1,NULL,NULL),(15039,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','Objects contract on heating.',15,0,NULL,NULL,'False',1,NULL,NULL),(15040,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','The apparatus used to measure heat is called a calorimeter.',16,0,NULL,NULL,'True',1,NULL,NULL),(15041,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','The heat contained in a substance is the heat of the substance?',17,0,NULL,NULL,'It is the heat energy stored within the substance.',1,NULL,NULL),(15042,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','Explain why rails have gaps at specific distances.',18,0,NULL,NULL,'To accommodate thermal expansion and prevent buckling.',1,NULL,NULL),(15043,57,17330,8,'Science','en',113,'','Descriptive','one_sentence','Explain with the help of formulae the relationship between Celsius and Kelvin.',19,0,NULL,NULL,'K = C + 273.15',1,NULL,NULL),(15053,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','The region in a sound wave with higher pressure and density is called ______ and that with low pressure and density is called ______.',1,0,NULL,NULL,'compression; rarefaction',1,NULL,NULL),(15054,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','Medium is ______ for generation of sound.',2,0,NULL,NULL,'air',1,NULL,NULL),(15055,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','The total number of compressions and rarefactions produced per second in a sound wave is 1000. The frequency of the sound wave is ______.',3,0,NULL,NULL,'1000 Hz',1,NULL,NULL),(15056,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','Different sound notes have different ______.',4,0,NULL,NULL,'frequencies',1,NULL,NULL),(15057,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','In a loudspeaker, ______ energy is converted into sound energy.',5,0,NULL,NULL,'electrical',1,NULL,NULL),(15058,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','Take two plastic glasses and tie a thread between them to make a toy phone. Does the voice of your friend reach you through the thread ______.',6,0,NULL,NULL,'Yes, the vibration travels through the thread',1,NULL,NULL),(15059,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','Take a plastic or tin can or a plastic glass, remove its bottom, stretch a piece of rubber balloon across one end and fix it with a rubber band. Put a few grains of ragi or bajra on the stretched rubber and observe the motion ______.',7,0,NULL,NULL,'The grains vibrate and produce sound when the can is struck',1,NULL,NULL),(15060,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Give scientific reasons','short_answer','Give scientific reasons for the following statements: (a) It is essential to change the tension on the vocal cords to produce different sound notes. (b) Astronauts on the moon cannot hear each other directly. (c) As the sound wave propagates from one place to another in air, the air itself is not required to move from one place to another.',8,0,NULL,NULL,'(a) Changing tension changes the vibration frequency of the vocal cords, altering pitch. (b) In vacuum of space there is no medium, so sound cannot travel. (c) Sound propagates as pressure waves; the medium’s molecules oscillate locally, not move across space.',1,NULL,NULL),(15061,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','It is essential to change the tension on the vocal cords to produce different sound notes from our larynx ______.',9,0,NULL,NULL,'True',1,NULL,NULL),(15062,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','Astronauts on the moon cannot hear each other directly ______.',10,0,NULL,NULL,'True',1,NULL,NULL),(15063,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','As the sound wave propagates from one place to another in air, the air itself is not required to move from one place to another ______.',11,0,NULL,NULL,'True',1,NULL,NULL),(15064,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','How are different sound notes generated in musical instruments like guitar, flute, and vocal cords ______.',12,0,NULL,NULL,'By changing the vibrating length, tension, or air column length, which alters the frequency of vibration.',1,NULL,NULL),(15065,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Fill in the blank','fill_blank','How is sound produced in a human larynx and a loudspeaker ______.',13,0,NULL,NULL,'In the larynx, vocal cords vibrate as air passes; in a loudspeaker, a coil moves the diaphragm, creating vibrations in air.',1,NULL,NULL),(15066,57,17331,8,'Science','en',119,'','Explain the experiment','explain','Explain the experiment, with a neat diagram, to prove that sound needs a material medium for propagation.',14,1,'57/118.jpg',NULL,'Place a bell in a sealed jar, remove air with a vacuum pump, and observe that the bell’s sound cannot be heard outside the jar when the air is removed. This shows sound requires a medium.',1,NULL,NULL),(15071,57,17332,8,'Science','en',120,'','What do you observe?','short_answer','What do you observe when a mirror is placed in the path of a light ray?',1,1,'57/119.jpg',NULL,'The light ray is reflected and changes direction.',1,'57/119_2.jpg',NULL),(15074,57,17332,8,'Science','en',121,'','Draw a figure','draw','Draw a line PQ showing the mirror, the incident ray AO, the reflected ray OB, and the normal ON at point O.',2,1,'57/120.jpg',NULL,NULL,1,'57/120_1.jpg',NULL),(15092,57,17332,8,'Science','en',122,'','Regular reflection of light','define','Define regular reflection of light.',3,1,'57/121.jpg',NULL,'Reflection from a smooth surface where all incident and reflected angles are equal and reflected rays are parallel.',1,'57/121_3.jpg',NULL),(15093,57,17332,8,'Science','en',122,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','If the angles of incidence for parallel rays are i₁, i₂, …, iₙ and the angles of reflection are r₁, r₂, …, rₙ, then i₁ = i₂ = … = iₙ and r₁ = r₂ = … = rₙ ______.',4,1,'57/121.jpg',NULL,'i₁ = i₂ = … = iₙ and r₁ = r₂ = … = rₙ',1,'57/121_2.jpg',NULL),(15094,57,17332,8,'Science','en',122,'','Fill in the blanks','fill_blank','In irregular reflection, the angles of incidence for parallel rays are not equal, so the angles of reflection are also not equal ______.',5,1,'57/121.jpg',NULL,'not equal',1,'57/121_3.jpg',NULL),(15095,57,17332,8,'Science','en',122,'','True or False','true_false','Laws of reflection are followed in both regular and irregular reflection.',6,1,'57/121.jpg',NULL,'true',1,NULL,NULL),(15096,57,17332,8,'Science','en',122,'','True or False','true_false','In irregular reflection, the laws of reflection are not followed.',7,1,'57/121.jpg',NULL,'false',1,NULL,NULL),(15097,57,17332,8,'Science','en',122,'','True or False','true_false','At any point on a rough surface, the angle of inci