INSERT INTO `ep_questions` VALUES (3208,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Volvox, Paramecium, Euglena, Staphylococcus aureus.','','','Staphylococcus aureus : It belongs to Kingdom Monera while others belong to Kingdom Protista.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3209,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Columella, Rhizoids, Sporangiophore, Spirillum ','','','Spirillum : It is a type of bacteria while others are parts of fungi','',0,0,0,NULL),(3210,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Bacteria, Protista, Fungi, Algae.','','','Bacteria : It is a Prokaryote while others are Eukaryotes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3211,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Baker’s yeast, Penicillium, Bacillus, Aspergillus.','','','Bacillus : It is a type of Bacteria while others are Fungi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3212,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Chlorella, Chlamydomonas, Candida, Spirogyra.','','','Candida : It is a fungus while others are algae.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3213,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Influenza, Rinderpest, AIDS, Polio. ','','','Rinderpest : it is a disease of cattle while others are diseases of humans.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3214,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Carl Linnaeus : 2 kingdoms : : ------ : 3 kingdoms ','','','Haeckel','',0,0,0,NULL),(3215,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy:  Whittaker : -------- : : Kopland : 4 kingdom.','','','5 Kingdom','',0,0,0,NULL),(3217,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: ------- : Heterotroph : : Volvox : Autotrophic. ','','','Amoeba','',0,0,0,NULL),(3219,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy:  Bacteria : ------ : : Protozoa : Cell division','','','Binary fission','',0,0,0,NULL),(3223,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Algae : Autotrophic : : ------ : Heterotrophic','','','Fungi','',0,0,0,NULL),(3224,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: ----- : Tomato Vilt virus : : Cattle : Picorna ','','','Plants','',0,0,0,NULL),(3250,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Arrange the following order of size Bacteria, Fungi, Viruses, Algae.','','','Viruses, Fungi, Algae, Bacteria','',0,0,0,NULL),(3251,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is the hierarchy of classification of living organisms?','','','i) Carl Linnaeus developed hierarchy of groups for classification\nii) The taxa in hierarchical order is kingdom, Phylum, Class Order, Family, Genus and Species.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3252,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Who invented ‘binomial system’ of nomenclature?','','','i) Binomial nomenclature is a formal system of naming species of living things by giving each a name composed of two parts\nii) The first part is the genus and second part is species.\niii) This type of nomenclature was invented by scientist Carl Linnaeus.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3253,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which living organisms are included in the kingdom Monera?  ','','','All type of bacteria and blue green algae are included in the Kingdom Monera.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3254,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: i) Biological classification             ii) Microbiology\n\n','','','i) The process of dividing living organisms into groups and sub groups is called biological classification.\nii)  The science that deals with the study of microorganisms is called microbiology.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3256,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Saprophyte','','','These are organisms which obtain their nutrition from dead and decaying matter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3257,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Algae','','','These are plant like organisms whose cell contain chlorophyll. Algae is mostly aquatic.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3258,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Rhizoids','','','Root like structures which try to derive its nutrition is bread mould, are called Rhizoids.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3259,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Food poisoning ','','','The toxic substances producing by the microbes make the food harmful and later cause serious health problems, is called food poisoning. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(3260,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Bacteriophage','','','Viruses that infect bacteria are known as Bacteriophage.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3261,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Need for classification','','','i) According to 2011 census around, 87 million species of living organisms are found on the earth.\nii) This includes the organisms from land and sea\niii) All the living organisms on the earth have adopted according to geographic regions, food ingestion, defense, etc.\niv) While adapting, many differences are observed in the organisms of same species too.\nv) To study such a vast number, it was essential to divide them into groups.\nvi) This process of dividing living organisms into groups and subgroups is called biological classification.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3262,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Criteria of Whittaker’s five kingdom','','','Criteria of Whittaker’s five Kingdom are follows :\ni) Complexity of cell structure : Whether the organism is Prokaryotic or Eukaryotic.\nii) Complexity of organization : Weather organisms is insular or multicellular\niii) Mode of nutrition : whether the organisms is autotrophic, heterotrophic or saprophytic.\niv) Life style : whether the organisms are producer, consumer or decomposer\nv) Phylogenetic relationship : It is the evolutionary history of organisms.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3263,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: General characteristics of Monera ','','','The characteristics of Kingdom Monera are follows : \ni) They are mostly in cellular autotrophic or heterotrophic\nii) They are usually non motile, but some may have flagella\niii) These are prokaryotic cells without distinct nucleus or cell organelles.\niv) All types of bacteria and blue green algae are included in the Kingdom Monera.\nv) Moving small road like lactobacilli can be seen on a high power of compound microscope, by observing a drop of curd or buttermilk on a glass slide.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3264,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Importance of Microorganisms ','','','i) The microorganisms like bacteria and fungi are very important components of our ecosystem.\nii) The decompose the dead bodies of plants and animals and act as scavengers of nature.\niii) They help in the formation of humus that increases the fertility of soil and aid in healthy growth of plants.\niv) They play an important role in converting the complete organic compounds into simpler forms and continue with biochemical cycle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3265,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason: All microorganisms are not pathogens.','','','i) Microorganisms which cause diseases in plants and animals are called as pathogens. E.g. Plasmodium vivax causes malaria, Entamoeba histolytica causes amoebiasis.\nii) However, some microorganisms are useful to humans in various ways. They are used in industries for preparation of products like cheese, bread, etc. e.g. Lactobacilli\niii) They are also used in productions of certain antibiotics. E.g. Penicillin from a fungus Penicillium.\niv) Microorganisms are inhabitant on human body and are present on skin, nose, gut, etc. Which are beneficial. E.g. E-coli, Enterococcus faecalis are present in the gut of humans.\nHence, all microorganisms are not pathogens.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3267,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason: All types of bacteria are prokaryotes.','','','i) Bacteria are unicellular organisms.\nii) Their cells are smaller in size ranging from 1 μm to 10 μm\niii) Bacterial cells are with cell wall, but distinct nucleus or cell organelles are absent.\niv) Therefore, all types of bacteria are considered as prokaryotes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3268,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason: Algae are eukaryotic, autotrophic organisms. ','','','i) Algal cells are comparatively large in size ranging from 10 – 100 μm\nii) They have a well defined nucleus and membrane bound organelles. Hence, they are eukaryotic organism.\niii) Photosynthesis is carried out with the help of chloroplast present in the cell. iv) So, they can prepare their own food by photosynthesis, hence, they are autotrophic organisms.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3269,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why were groups and subgroups created among the living organisms?','','','i) According to the 2011 census, around 87 million species of living organisms we are found on the earth inclusive of land and sea.\nii) It was essential to divide them in groups hence groups and subgroups were created considering the similarities and differences among the living organisms.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3271,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the diseases caused by Protozoa in humans.','','','i) Plasmodium vivax causes malaria while Entamoeba histolytica causes Amoebiasis.\nii) They also cause sleeping sickness and Kala azar in humans.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3272,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which diseases can humans and plants suffer due to fungal attack?','','','i) Human beings to suffer from Ringworm which is a skin disease. Also food poisoning, due to consumption of food items even after their expiry date.\nii) Plants do suffer from Rust of wheat, wilting of chick pea is a fungal diseases and white Rust in case of mustard.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3273,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain the nutrition in fungi.','','','i) Fungi are non- green, eukaryotic and heterotrophic organisms.\nii) Most of fungi are saprophytic in nature, that is they de depend on dead or decaying matters for their food.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3274,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which living organisms are included in kingdom Monera?','','','All types of bacteria and blue- green algae are included in Kingdom Monera. Examples : Streptococcus pneumoniae, Clostridium tetani, Clostridium botulinum, Treponema pallidum, Vibrio cholerae, Staphylococcus aureus, Legionella pneumoniae, Salmonella typhi ','',0,0,0,NULL),(3276,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Prokaryotes and Eukaryotes','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3277,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Kingdom Monera and Kingdom Protista.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3278,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Algae and Fungi.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3280,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give any four examples of bacteria.','','','Streptococcus pneumoniae, Clostridium tetani, Clostridium botulinum, Treponema pallidum, Vibrio cholerae, Staphylococcus aureus, Legionella pneumoniae, Salmonella typhi etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3281,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What criteria did Whittaker take to classify organisms?','','','Criteria of Whittaker’s five Kingdom are follows :\ni) Complexity of cell structure : Whether the organism is Prokaryotic or Eukaryotic.\nii) Complexity of organization : Weather organisms is insular or multicellular\niii) Mode of nutrition : whether the organisms is autotrophic, heterotrophic or saprophytic.\niv) Life style : whether the organisms are producer, consumer or decomposer\nv) Phylogenetic relationship : It is the evolutionary history of organisms.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3282,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the characteristics of kingdom Monera, with examples.','','','The characteristics of Kingdom Monera are follows : \ni) They are mostly in cellular autotrophic or heterotrophic\nii) They are usually non motile, but some may have flagella\niii) These are prokaryotic cells without distinct nucleus or cell organelles.\niv) All types of bacteria and blue green algae are included in the Kingdom Monera.\nv) Moving small road like lactobacilli can be seen on a high power of compound microscope, by observing a drop of curd or buttermilk on a glass slide.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3283,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the characteristics of kingdom Protista.','','','The characteristics of Kingdom Protista are as follows :\ni) These are Eukaryotic,  unicellular organisms.\nii) They are aquatic, found in oceans, ponds, lakes and dams.\niii) They have a well defined the nucleus enclosed in a nuclear membrane\niv) They are autotrophic like Euglena, Volvox etc. and heterotrophic like Amoeba and Paramoecium.\nv) Locomotion is by pseudopodia, flagella or cilla. \nvi) These organisms can be seen on low power and high power of compound microscope on a glass slide with the help of a drop of pond water.\nvii) These are motile microbes, irregular in shape.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3284,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on Protista.','','','The characteristics of Kingdom Protista are as follows :\ni) These are Eukaryotic,  unicellular organisms.\nii) They are aquatic, found in oceans, ponds, lakes and dams.\niii) They have a well defined the nucleus enclosed in a nuclear membrane\niv) They are autotrophic like Euglena, Volvox etc. and heterotrophic like Amoeba and Paramoecium.\nv) Locomotion is by pseudopodia, flagella or cilla. \nvi) These organisms can be seen on low power and high power of compound microscope on a glass slide with the help of a drop of pond water.\nvii) These are motile microbes, irregular in shape.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3285,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the characteristics of kingdom fungi.','','','The characteristics of the organisms in Kingdom Fungi are as follows :\ni) These organisms can be observed on a piece of bread when it is kept for 2 to 3 days.\nii) A ball is grayish, greenish, yellowish, cottony mass is observed on it.\niii) These are non- green plant like organisms, eukaryotic, heterotrophic organisms.\niv) Most of them are saprotrophs. They feed on feed on dead decaying organic matter.\nv) Their cell wall is made up of tough and complex sugar called ‘chitin’\nvi) Plant body is called mycelium, made up of interwoven thread- like structures called hyphae. Examples : Baker’s yeast, Aspergillus, Penicillium, Yeast, Rhizopus, Mushrooms, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3286,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain merits and demerits of Whittaker’s classification.','','','Merits\ni) The system has been widely accepted and recommended by the Institute of Biology.\nii) This grouping is useful for practical reasons because the technique used is simple.\nDemerits\ni) This system of classification is not natural grouping as smallest organisms are called as microorganisms.\nii) This scheme did not consider, one group of organisms called viruses into any Kingdom neatly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3287,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are bacteria divided on basis of their shape?','','','On the basis of shape, bacteria are divided into the following groups :\ni) Coccus : These are spherical bacteria occurring in singles or in groups. E.g. Streptococcus pneumonia\nii) Bacilli : These are rod shaped bacteria, occurring in singles or in chains. E.g. Bacillus anthracis.\niii) Spirilla : These are spiral shaped bacteria. E.g. Treponemas pallidum\niv) Comma shaped (Vibrio’s) : These are short and curve ones. They appear like a comma, when incomplete. E.g. Vibrio cholera\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3288,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the structure of a bacteria.','','','i) These are single celled organisms.	ii) They do not have a definite nucleus	iii) Cell wall is present, but cell organelles are absent.\niv) Coccus : These are spherical bacteria occurring in singles or in groups. E.g. Streptococcus pneumonia\nv) Bacilli : These are rod shaped bacteria, occurring in singles or in chains. E.g. Bacillus anthracis.\nvi) Spirilla : These are spiral shaped bacteria. E.g. Treponemas pallidum\nvii) Comma shaped (Vibrio’s) : These are short and curve ones. They appear like a comma, when incomplete. E.g. Vibrio cholera\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3289,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','List the characteristics features of Protozoa.','','','i) They are unicellular organisms found in soil, fresh water and sea water.\nii) They may be free living or parasitic, solitary are found in loose colonies.\niii) There is a great variation in cell structure, Locomotory organs and modes of nutrition among protozoans.\niv) They are generally motile. Locomotion is by flagella, cilia or pseudopodia.\nv) They are sensitive to various stimuli like touch, temperature, vacuoles etc.\nvi) These organisms reproduce by simple cell division. E.g. Amoeba, Paramoecium, Euglena.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3290,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the characteristics of Chlorella.','','','i) It is a green spherical shaped algae. \nii) It is found in fresh water.\niii) It contain a cup shaped chloroplast that contains the nucleus.\niv) It prepares its own food (autotroph) and reproduces asexually. \nv) Photosynthesis is carried with the help of chloroplast hence, used in scientific studies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3291,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What extra ordinary characteristics do viruses possess?','','','i) Generally, viruses are not considered as living organisms as they are on the border line of living and nonliving.\nii) They reproduce hence they are living but they can be crystallized, hence, they are non- livings.\niii) They survive only in living plants or animal cells and produce their own proteins with the help of host cells and create their numerous replica.\niv) They destroy the host cell and become free to infect the new cells.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3292,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain the structure of viruses or write the characteristics of viruses.','','','i) Their size is extremely minute (approx. 10nm to 100nm)\nii) They can be seen only under electron microscope.\niii) They contain DNA aur RNA in the form of genetic material covered by a protein coat.\niv) They do not have cytoplasm, nucleus or cell membrane.\nv) They survive only in living plants or animal cells and produce their own proteins with help of host cells and create their numerous replica.\nvi) They destroy the host cell and become free to infect the new cells.\nvii) Examples, Polio Virus, Influenza Virus, HIV-AIDS virus, Picorna virus, Tomato Wilt virus, etc. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3293,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the flow chart:','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(3295,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the five kingdom, their characteristics, types and examples.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3296,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a neat and labelled diagram: Organisms from kingdom Monera','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3297,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a neat and labelled diagram: Amoeba','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3298,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a neat and labelled diagram: Fungi','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3299,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw a neat and labelled diagram: Different types of bacteria.','','','Diagram','',0,0,1,NULL),(3300,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw a neat and labelled diagram: Paramecium.','','','Diagram','',0,0,1,NULL),(3301,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw a neat and labelled diagram: Bacteriophage','','','Diagram :','',0,0,1,NULL),(3302,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw a neat and labelled diagram: Euglena \n','','','Diagram :','',0,0,1,NULL),(3310,25,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the biological classification? Why was the need to classify?','','','i) The process of dividing living organisms into groups and subgroups is called biological classification.\nii) There were around 87 million species of living organisms found on earth, inclusive of land and sea. To study such a vast number, it was essential to divide them into groups.\niii) So groups and subgroups were created considering the similarities and differences among the living organisms.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3314,25,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the structure of Fungi with a neat labelled diagram.','','','i) Most fungi grow as filaments (thread -like structures).\nii) These filaments are called hypha.\niii) A mass of hypha make up the body of fungus which is called mycelium.\niv) Sporangiophore are ariel hyphae and sporangium form on them\nv) Columella is a sterile dome shaped structure found at the tip of Sporangiophore \nvi) A sporangium in fungi is simply a cell containing spores.\nvii) Sporangiophore are spores that are produced in sporangium.\nviii) Apophasis in fungi  is the swelling shaped like a cup at the end of Sporangiophore below the sporangium.\nix) Rhizoids are root- like structures, small branching hyphae that grow downwards that anchor the fungus.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(3315,25,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are microbes classified? State the characteristics of each in short.','','','i) Microorganisms are classified as Prokaryotes and Eukaryotes. Further Prokaryotes are classified as a bacteria.\nBacteria : They are different types. They are unicellular Prokaryotic microorganisms. They reproduced by simple binary fission.\nii) Eukaryotes are further divided into Protista, Fungi and Algae.\na) Protista are unicellular, aquatic organisms. Some are pathogens. They reproduce by simple cell division. They cause diseases in humans.\nb) Fungi are saprophytic unicellular organisms. They produce sexually and asexually by budding or cell division.\nc) Algae are autotrophic, aquatic, unicellular, eukaryotic organism. They carry on photosynthesis with the help of chloroplast.\niii) Viruses are not added into the list as they are not considered to be living or nonliving they produce their own proteins with the help of coast sales and create their numerous replica\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3316,25,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain characteristics of Bacteria.','','','i) The characteristics of bacteria are as follows \ni) These organisms are unicellular and sometimes free- living, parasitic or may exist in some other forms. Many bacteria together form colonies.\nii) Bacteria are prokaryotic with Cell wall. They lack distinct nucleus or other membrane bound cell organelles.\niii) Bacteria grow vigorously in their suitable conditions. Bacteria like E- coli can divide every 20 minutes.\niv) Bacteria can occur in different shapes : Spherical (coccus), Rod shaped (bacillus), Spiral (spirilium), Conical shaped (Vibrio), Intermediate between spherical and rod shaped (Cocco- bacillus)\nv) The size of bacterial cell varies from 1nm to 10 nm.\nvi) Bacteria reproduces by simple binary fission.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3360,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out: Bacteria, virus, shigella, Mycobacterium tuberculae.','','','Mycobacterium tuberculae : It causes Tuberculosis while other causes Dysentery.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3361,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out: Hepatitis virus – A, B, D, E, F','','','F : It is not a type of Hepatitis virus while others are types of Hepatitis virus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3364,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out: CT scan, MRI scan, Mammography, Robotic surgery','','','Robotic surgery : It is a treatment method for cancer while others are diagnostic techniques for cancer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3366,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the odd one out: Malaria, Hepatitis, Elephantiasis, Dengue','','','Hepatitis : It is a water borne disease while others are vector borne diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3367,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the odd one out: Plague, AIDS, Cholera, TB','','','AIDS : It is cause by a virus while others are caused by bacteria.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3386,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of the given: Viral diseases','','','Chicken pox, Measles, Mumps, Influenza, Rabies, Chikungunya, Hepatitis, Herpes, Polio, Encephalitis, Dengue, AIDS','',0,0,0,NULL),(3387,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of the given: Bacterial diseases','','','Typhoid, Gonorrhoea, Tetanus, Food poisoning, Anthrax, Diarrhoea, Plague, Diphtheria, Meningitis, Woophing cough, Cholera, Syphilis.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3388,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of the given: Diseases spread through insects','','','Malaria, Filaria, Elephantiasis, Dengue, Kala azar, Chikungunya','',0,0,0,NULL),(3389,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of the given: Hereditary diseases','','','Heart diseases, Cancer, Dwarfism, Sickle cell anaemia, Huntington’s disease, Gigantism, Cystic fibrosis, Phenyl ketonuria, Down’s syndrome','',0,0,0,NULL),(3390,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make a list of the given: Fungal diseases','','','Mycosis – Histoplasmosis, Aspeigillosis, Eukaryotic Microbar, Dermatophytes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3391,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make a list of the given: Protozoan diseases','','','Malaria, Amoebiasis','',0,0,0,NULL),(3392,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Techniques for detection of cancer','','','CT scan, MRI scan, biopsy, mammography','',0,0,0,NULL),(3394,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Therapy for tuberculosis','','','DOTS','',0,0,0,NULL),(3396,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: These insects breed in water logged places.','','','Mosquitosis','',0,0,0,NULL),(3397,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Swine flu spreads by this animal.','','','Pig and human','',0,0,0,NULL),(3398,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: First case of swine flu reported ','','','Mexico in 2009','',0,0,0,NULL),(3399,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Test required for diagnosis of AIDS','','','ELISA','',0,0,0,NULL),(3401,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Uncontrolled and abnormal growth of cells.','','','Cancer','',0,0,0,NULL),(3402,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Any two Non-infectious disease.','','','Diabetes, heart disease','',0,0,0,NULL),(3403,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: A case where glucose level cannot be controlled.','','','Diabetes','',0,0,0,NULL),(3406,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Have you ever taken leave from school due to sickness?','','','Yes, I have taken leave from school due to sickness.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3407,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What happens exactly when we become sick?','','','Our immune system is affected when we become sick.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3409,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Define: Health','','','It is a state of complete physical, mental and social well- beingness of a person and not merely the absence of any disease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3410,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Define: Disease','','','Condition of disturbance is psychological or psychological processes of body is called as disease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3411,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Infectious disease ','','','The disease that spread through contaminated air, water, food or vectors are called as infectious diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3413,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Non- Infectious Disease ','','','Diseases which do not occur through infections and body contacts are called as non- infectious diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3414,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Cancer ','','','Uncontrolled and abnormal cell growth is called cancer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3415,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Malignant tumor ','','','Group or lump of cancerous cells is called as malignant tumor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3416,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Generic medicines ','','','General medicines, that are manufactured and distributed without any patent are known as generic medicines.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3417,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is health?','','','It is a state of complete physical, mental and social well- beingness and not merely the absence of any disease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3418,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give names of 5 non-infectious disease other than given in the lesson.','','','Cataract, obesity, chronic kidney diseases, osteoporosis and Alzheimer’s disease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3419,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the symptoms of Tuberculosis?','','','Symptoms of Tuberculosis are chronic cough, blood spitting, emaciation and difficult breathing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3420,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why rabies called as hydrophobia?','','','The person infected with rabies virus is afraid of water so it is commonly called as hydrophobia.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3421,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the full form of AIDS?','','','Acquired Immune Deficiency syndrome is the full form of AIDS.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3422,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What causes Dengue?','','','Dengue is caused by DEN- 1, 2 virus belonging to the type flavivirus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3423,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where do mosquitoes lay eggs?','','','Mosquito lay eggs in water logged places increasing their population.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3425,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the reasons of infection of swine flu?','','','Infection for swine flu occurs through pigs and humans. It is also spreads through secretions of nose, throat and saliva.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3426,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the symptoms of swine flu?','','','The symptoms are difficulty in breathing with sore throat and body pain.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3427,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where are the diagnostic facilities of swine flu available?','','','Diagnostic facilities are available in National institute of Virology (NIV), Pune and National institute of Communicable Diseases (NICD), Delhi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3428,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What must we remember when we come across an AIDS infected person?','','','Our behaviour with HIV infected person must be normal as AIDS does not occur due to touching and sharing food with HIV impacted person.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3429,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the symptoms of Rabies?','','','There is a fever for 2 to 12 weeks accompanied with exaggerations in behaviour and hydrophobia.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3430,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can we prevent Rabies?','','','It can be prevented by timely vaccination before the onset of symptoms that start to appear within 90 to 170 days of dog bite.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3431,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the various techniques to detect cancer?','','','Various techniques like CT scan, MRI scan, mammography, biopsy are used in detection of cancer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3432,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What can be the reasons for acquiring cancer?','','','It can be consumption of tobacco, gutkha, smoking, alcoholism, lack of fibre content in food, excessive consumption of junk food, heredity and many other factors.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3433,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which organs are generally attacked by cancer?','','','Cancer can attack organ like mouth, lungs, tongue, stomach, pancreas, breast, uterus, skin and tissues like blood.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3434,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What could be the reasons for diabetes?','','','It could be heredity, obesity, lack of physical exercise, mental stress, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3435,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What causes diabetes?','','','If the hormone- insulin produced in pancreas is secreted in low quantities, then glucose level cannot be controlled and it causes diabetes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3436,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can the frequency of becoming sick lowered?','','','If people follow right lifestyle like right food, sufficient sleep, yogasana, Pranayam and physical exercise, then one can remain healthy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3438,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which are various media of spreading the infectious diseases?','','','Infectious diseases can spread from one infected person to other, by contaminated food or water or by transfusion of blood or by sharing of needle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3439,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Depending upon which symptoms, rabies can be identified?','','','There is fever for 2 to 12 weeks accompanied with exaggerations in behaviour and hydrophobia.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3440,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between: Infectious diseases and Non-Infectious diseases.','','','Difference :','',0,0,1,NULL),(3441,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Tuberculosis and AIDS','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3442,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Cholera and Typhoid','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3443,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Hereditary diseases and Acquired diseases ','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3445,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give the preventive measures of the given diseases: Dengue ','','','i) We must see to that water should not be kept open\nii) Water logged areas should be fumigated or sprayed by D. D. T.\niii) Plants having dishes below it should be checked for water. Water can get collected into it.\niv) Boiled water before consumption.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3446,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the preventive measures of the given diseases: Diabetes ','','','If one follows proper diet, take medicines and exercise regularly under the supervision of doctor then diabetes can be controlled.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3447,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give the preventive measures of the given diseases: Cancer','','','i) Controlled diet helps to prevent some types of cancers.\nii) Physical exercise along with modern treatment is more beneficial to cure the cancer.\niii) Avoiding addiction like tobacco chewing, smoking, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3448,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give the preventive measures of the given diseases: AIDS','','','i) To avoid sexual contact with the infected person.\nii) People should be educated about transmission of AIDS.\niii) Before transfusion one must be sure that it has been screened for HIV.\niv) There is no cure but prevention is the only way to stop it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3449,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are droplet nuclei? Give names of 5 infectious diseases.','','','Infectious microbes spread through air. The infected person throws out little droplets on coughing, sneezing. These are termed as droplet nuclei.\nTuberculosis; common cold, dengue, flu, Diarrhoea are the examples of infectious diseases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3450,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it essential to take vaccines?','','','i) The resistance to the onset of a disease after infection by harmful germs is termed as vaccination.\nii) It increases the ability to resist diseases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3451,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is Rabies a fatal disease?','','','i) Rabies occurs due to the bite of the infected dog, rabbit, monkey, cat, etc.\nii) It enters the brain via neurons and can cause death.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3452,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are generic medicines?','','','i) These are manufactured and distributed without any patent.\nii) The cost is low \niii) The medicines are at par in quality with branded medicines.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3453,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which are main reasons of diabetes and heart diseases?','','','Reasons of diabetes : Heredity, obesity, lack of physical exercise and mental stress.\nReasons of heart diseases : Smoking, alcoholism, diabetes, hypertension, obesity, lack of physical exercise, heredity, mental stress, anger, anxiety.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3458,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain importance of: Balanced diet ','','','i) A diet which contains all the constituents in the proper proportion, i.e. Carbohydrates, Proteins, Vitamins, Fats, Vitamins and Minerals is termed as balanced diet.\nii) Balanced diet depends on age, kind of work and gender.\niii) In childhood, children grow fast, they need more proteins than the senior people. Every food group is essential for our body.\niv) Each food group has its own special nutrients. By taking equal proportions, we can have a balanced diet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3459,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain importance of: Physical exercise/Yogasanas ','','','i) A strong physique and an alert mind developed through regular exercise, participation in games, yogabhyas keeps one free from diseases.\nii) Physical exercise helps to inculcate self-discipline.\niii) It also aims to maintain a good quality life.\niv) It makes the students vigilant, develops team spirit and attains leadership qualities.\nv) It develops a stress-free environment, sportsmanship and mental strength.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3460,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain importance of: Generic medicines ','','','i) Generic medicines are also known as general medicines \nii) These are manufactured and distributed without any patent\niii) These medicines are at par in quality with branded medicines\niv) Production cost of these medicines is very low due to fewer expenses on research as their formula is readily available.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3464,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What do you mean by pathogen?','','','i) Pathogens are defined as disease causing microorganisms.\nii) E.g. Tuberculosis is caused by a pathogen named Mycobacterium tuberculae.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3465,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What do you mean by infectious disease?','','','i) Infectious disease; also known as transmissible disease or communicable disease, is illness resulting from an infection.\nii) Infections are caused by infectious agents including virus, bacteria and parasites etc.\niii) E.g. Tuberculosis, Influenza, Cholera, HIV/ AIDS, Malaria.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3470,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How can we observe personal hygiene?','','','We can observe personal hygiene in following ways : \ni) By washing hands and legs before eating and after visiting the toilet.\nii) By keeping genitals clean.\niii) By brushing teeth regularly twice a day to keep away from bad breath\niv) By taking bath regularly to keep away from body odour.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3471,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why should we drink boiled water in rainy season?','','','We should drink boiled water in rainy season for the following reasons :\ni) It helps in releasing congestion caused due to cold and flu.\nii) Boiled water dissolves cough and helps to remove it from respiratory tract iii) Boiled water helps in detoxing our body from toxins.\niv) It helps in digestion of food\nv) It helps in proper bowel movement \nvi) It also helps in blood circulation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3472,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why the ‘clean hands’ drive is run in school?','','','Clean hands drive is run in school to create awareness of the usefulness and positive impact that it has on healthy living.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3473,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which measures are taken at your home to control the rats?','','','Following measures can be taken at home to control rats : \ni) Using rat traps 		ii) Covering all gaps and holes properly 	iii) Cleaning home regularly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3474,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is it necessary to care about health of domestic dogs, cats and birds?','','','i) Dogs, cats and birds being domestic revolve around human being. \nii) Therefore, to protect humans from all sort of infections it is necessary to take care of them and vaccinate them regularly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3475,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Is there any relation between pigeons, stray animals and human health?','','','i) Yes, there is a relation between pigeons, stray animals and human health\nii) Pigeons dropping can cause a disease called Histoplasmosis to humans and similarly stray animals can also cause various diseases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3476,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are effects of rats, cockroaches on human health?','','','i) Rats and cockroaches are some of the most common pests that can impact human health.\nii) Rats can even spread epidemics such as the black plague. Most common illness caused by rats is hantavirus which is spread by breathing in debris contaminated with rat faces and urine.\niii) Cockroaches unknowingly touch contaminated areas and in turn touch food and water or any kitchen utensils. Since the regularly shed their shells they can be a great risk to human health.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3482,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Observe the pictures shown in following and write description in boxes:','','','a) Since open containers with water are place for mosquitoes to lay their eggs, keep such type of containers always closed.\nb) An open container with drinking water is a place for mosquito breeding so this should be kept covered all the time.\nc)   Like open containers, air cooler is also a mosquito to breeding place. Cleaning air cooler properly and regularly can avoid this.\nd) Small babies should wear proper dresses to prevent diseases spread by mosquitos.\ne) Fish tanks kept at homes are also place for mosquito breeding. Changing water and cleaning tank regularly can avoid this.\nf) Mosquito breeding can be prevented by spraying insecticides.\ng) Mosquito repellants and useful in indoors and outdoors.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(3484,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Enlist the names and composition of the medicines present at your home.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3486,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following chart:','','','a) Angioplasty		b) Bypass surgery		c) Open heart surgery		d) Installation of pacemaker 	e) Heart transplant  	f) Installation of stents','',1,0,0,NULL),(3490,26,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is Dengue caused? State the symptoms and preventive measures?','','','i) Dengue is caused by DEN -  1, 2 virus belonging to the type flavivirus.\nii) The symptoms are as follows : a) Acute fever, headache and vomiting	b) Pains in eye socket		c) Decrease in platelet count that may lead to internal hemorrhage.\niii) Preventive measures are as follows : a) Keep breeding places of mosquitoes sprayed with pesticides	b) Make use of mosquito nets while sleeping 	c) Keep the containers filled with water closed by lid 		d) Dispose all the junk away		e) Make use of mosquito coils and repellents.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3494,26,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Text Book','','Write the information on modern diagnostics and treatment of cancer?','','','Modern diagnostics and Treatment of cancer are as follows :\ni) Various techniques like CT scan, MRI scan, mammography, biopsy are used in diagnosis.\nii) Along with conventional methods like chemotherapy, radiation therapy and surgery, modern techniques like robotic and laparoscopic surgery are also followed.\niii) It also include Pat scan which helps to detect how far has the disease spread.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3495,26,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the given diagram in detail.','','','i) The diagram depicts the spread of infectious air borne diseases.\nii) These diseases are usually spread through secretions of the nose and throat while coughing and sneezing.\niii) The small droplets secreted during sneezing or coughing are generally vaporized into fine mist particles (aerosols), and remain in the air for several minutes to a few hours.\niv) The larger droplets however settle on the ground within a few seconds.\nv) The mist containing infectious agents usually facilities transmission of the diseases to a person who come in contact with it.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(3496,26,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the web diagram: ','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(3530,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is force? State its SI unit.','','','A force is a physical quantity which is described as push or pull on an object. Its SI unit is Newton.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3532,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Force : Vector quantity : : Pressure : ------','','','Scalar quantity','',0,0,0,NULL),(3534,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Pressure : Barometer : : Archimedes principle : ----','','','Lactometer','',0,0,0,NULL),(3535,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Force/Area : Pressure : : Mass/Volume : ------','','','density','',0,0,0,NULL),(3536,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: 101 × 10³ pa : 10³ mbr : : 10² pa : ------','','','1 mbar','',0,0,0,NULL),(3538,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd one out: N/m², kg/m³, bar, pascal ','','','kg/m³ : kg/m³ is the unit of density while rest are the units of pressure','',0,0,0,NULL),(3539,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd one out:    Density, relative density, pressure, volume','','','Relative density : Relative density has no unit whereas density, pressure and volume have units.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3540,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd one out: Push, Pull, Pressure','','','Pressure : Push or pull is a force and pressure is per unit area','',0,0,0,NULL),(3541,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd one out: Rocket, racing car, boat, rickshaw','','','Rocket : Rocket moves against the gravitational pull of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3566,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Friction','','','The force created due to relative moment between two surfaces is called friction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3567,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Inertia ','','','The tendency of an object to remain in its existing state is called its inertia.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3568,27,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Contact force','','','If a force is run to act through a direct contact of the objects or via one or more objects, such a force is called a contact force.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3569,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Non – contact force ','','','When a force is applied between two objects even if the two objects are not in contact such a force is called a non- contact force.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3570,27,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Pressure','','','The force exerted perpendicularly on a unit surface area is called pressure.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3571,27,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Atmospheric pressure','','','The force exerted on unit surface area by the weight of air above it is called atmospheric pressure.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3572,27,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Balanced forces ','','','Two or more forces exerted on an object are balanced forces if their effects cancel each other and they do not cause a change in the objects motion. Here the net force is zero.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3573,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Unbalanced forces ','','','Two or more process exerted on an object are unbalanced if their effects do not cancel each other, net course is not zero which may result in change in state of motion of the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3575,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What force can do?','','','When a force is applied on an object, it\ni) Can move an object from rest		ii) Can change the speed of an object if it is moving	iii) Can bring about a change in the shape of an object.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3576,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it easier to push a nail in a wooden plank through its pointed end?','','','It is easier to push a nail into a wooden plank through its pointed end than through its blunt end because small area of the pointed ends helps in creating more pressure and it becomes easier to insert the nail.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3577,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the instrument used to measure atmospheric pressure.','','','Barometer is the instrument used to measure atmospheric pressure.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3578,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do fluids exert pressure?','','','Fluids exert pressure in all directions. Fluid exerts pressure on all bodies immersed in them and also on the walls of the container that holds them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3579,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Difference between: Contact force and Non-Contact force ','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3580,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Difference between: Balanced force and Unbalanced force ','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3581,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Difference between: Density and Relative Density','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3582,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Difference between: Inertia of motion and Inertia of rest','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3585,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give reason: Fruits can easily be cut with sharp knife.','','','i) Effect of force increases when the area in small. While cutting fruits force applied on the handle is transformed to the edge.\nii) Since the edge is sharp, tremendous pressure is exerted on a small area on the fruits duo to which they get cut easily.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3586,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give reason: The wall of a dam is broad at its base.','','','i) The wall of a dam is broad at its base than at the top because the force exerted are strongest close to the ground.\nii) Water pressure increases with the depth, so the lower portion of the dam has greater water pressure exerted upon it.\niii) The increasing thickness of the lower portion of the dam also helps it to supports its own weight.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3588,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: Inflated balloons have uniform shape.','','','i) Inflated balloons are filled with air.\nii) Air is a fluid and fluids exerts equal pressure in all directions inside the balloon causing the inflated balloons to have uniform shape.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3589,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: School bags have broad shoulder straps. ','','','The broad shoulder straps help to spread the weight of the bag over the wider area. This reduces the pressure acting on the shoulder.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3590,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: Our ear aches when we travel in an aeroplane.','','','i) While travelling by air, the pressure of the air surrounding us changes\nii) As the plane lands, it loses height rapidly and air pressure increases.\niii) The increased pressure on the eardrum causes the ear to ache.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3591,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: Eskimos wear ski-like shoes.','','','Eskimos wear ski-like wear shoes because wider area of the footwear reduces pressure and the prevent the Eskimos feet from sinking in ice.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3592,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: Sportsman use shoes with spike.','','','i) Shoes with spikes offer more friction because of more irregularities in the surface.\nii) This gives better grip to sportsman while running.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3593,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: It is difficult to drive on a wet road.','','','i) It is very difficult to drive on a wet road because of less friction\nii) A thin film of water is made over the surface of road.\niii) This thin film of water reduces friction\niv) Due to this, driver finds it difficult to control the vehicle while driving on a wet road.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3594,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How much pressure do we carry on our heads? Why don’t we feel it?','','','i) Pressure exerted by atmosphere on our head is nearly 1000 kg or 1 ton\nii) We constantly bear this tremendous pressure in our head and never feel it because air exerts pressure equally from all directions from our body.\niii) The cavities in our body are filled with air and arteries and veins are filled with blood. Therefore, we do not get crushed due to atmospheric pressure has our body pressure the atmospheric pressure.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3596,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Two tyres of a tractor are very wide and huge.','','','i) A wide tyre means larger area. We know that pressure reduces when area increases. The wide tyres reduce the pressure affected by tractor.\nii) As a result, it becomes easier to drive attractor on a muddy surface or on a fleshly ploughed surface.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3601,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do liquids and gases exert pressure?','','','i) Pressure at the bottom of container depends on the height of column of gas or liquid.\nii) A fluid exerts pressure on walls of the container. Containers of gases and liquids are usually cylindrical in shape to equally distribute the pressure on all portions of the wall.\niii) A fluid exerts equal pressure at some depth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3604,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How atmospheric pressure decreases with height from the sea level?','','','i) Atmospheric pressure decrease as the height increase from the surface of the area.\nii) At the sea level the atmospheric pressure has a magnitude of 101 × 10³ Pa.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3605,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can you increase the buoyant force acting on a body?','','','The buoyant force acting on a body can be increased by choosing a liquid of higher density or by choosing object of greater volume.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3607,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why do the load carrying heavy vehicles have large number of wheels?','','','i) The load carrying heavy vehicles have large number of wheels to distribute the weight over a greater area and lesser the fatigue on the street.\nii) Greater the area lesser is the pressure.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3608,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why will a sheet of paper fall slower than one that is crumpled into a ball?','','','i) A sheet of paper will fall slower than one that is crumpled into a ball because air offers resistance to the following objects.\nii) The paper in sheet form has larger surface area than that of crumpled paper ball.\niii) So, air exerts less pressure on sheet, which makes it falls slower.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3612,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','For skiing on ice, why are long flat ski used?','','','i) Effect of force decreases as area increases.\nii) Duo to long, flat broad board, pressure applied by the board on ice decreases and his sliding on ice becomes easier.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3622,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following chart:','','','A – Solids,   B – Gases,    C – Density of liquid,   D – Collisions ','',1,0,0,NULL),(3624,27,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','How atmospheric pressure is created?','','','i) The pressure created due to air is called the atmospheric pressure. This pressure at a point in air is equal from all sides.\nii) Imagine that a very long hollow cylinder of unit cross sectional area is standing on the surface of earth and it contains air. Weight of this air is the force applied in the direction of the centre of the earth.\niii) This means that atmospheric pressure is the ratio of this weight decided by the area of the surface.\niv) If air exists in a closed container, the air molecules in random motion continuously hit the walls of the container. In this interaction of force is exerted on the walls of the container pressure is created due to this force.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3626,27,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe an experiment to study balanced and unbalanced forces.','','','i) Take a cardboard box, tie thick string to its two sides and keep it on a smooth table as shown in figure.\nii) Take the strings on both sides of the table. Tie weighing pans to the two ends. Keep equal masses in both the pans. The box does not move on the table.\niii) If more mass is kept in one of the pans then in the other, the box starts moving in the direction of that pan. Equal gravitational force acts on both the pans in equal masses are kept in them.\niv) This means balanced forces act on the box, with effective force equal to zero as these are acting in opposite directions.\nv) On the contrary, if more mass is kept in one pan than in the other, the box starts moving in the direction of the pan with more mass.\nvi) When unequal forces are applied to the box on the two sides, an unbalanced force acts on the box resulting in imparting motion to the box.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(3627,27,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prove Archimedes Principle with the help of an experiment.','','','i) Take a long rubber band and cut it at one point. At one of its ends tie a clean water stone or 50 g weight as shown in the figure.\nii) Now hold the other end of the rubber band and make a mark there. Keep the stone hanging in air and measure the length of the rubber band from the stone to the mark made earlier.\niii) Now take water in a pot and hold the rubber band at such a height that the stone sinks in it. Again, measure the length of the rubber band up to the mark.\niv) This length in shorter than the earlier length. While dipping the stone in water, length of the stretched rubber gets slowly reduced and is minimum when it sinks completely.\nv) This is because when the stone sunk in water, a buoyant force acts on it in the upper direction. The weight of the stone acts downwards. Therefore, the force which acts on it in the downward direction is effectively reduced.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(3628,27,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain different types of inertia.','','','Different types of inertia are inertia of the state of rest, inertia of motion and directional inertia.\ni) Inertia of the state of rest : An object in the state of rest cannot change in its state of rest due to its inherent property. This property is called inertia of state of rest. For e.g. Fruits fall down due to inertia of rest when the branches of a tree are shaken.\nii) Inertia of motion : The inherent property of an object due to which its state of motion cannot change is called inertia of motion. For e.g. passengers sitting in the running bus get a jerk in the forward direction if the bus suddenly stops\niii) Directional inertia : The inherent property of an object due to which the object cannot change the direction of its motion, is called directional inertia. For e.g. if a vehicle in motion along a straight line suddenly turns, the passenger sitting in it are thrown opposite to the direction of turning.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3663,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Dry cell, Hydrochloric Acid, Ni-Cd cell, Lead Acid cell','','','Hydrochloric cell. It is not type of cell','',0,0,0,NULL),(3664,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Smartphone, Car battery, Solar cell, Torch','','','Solar cell. In rest all, chemical energy is used as a electrical energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3665,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Electrons, Protons, Molecule, Neutrons','','','Molecule. It is not a basic constituent of an atom.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3667,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Lithium, Nickel, Zinc, Wood','','','Wood. Wood is not a constituent of nay cell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3668,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: ZnCl₂, NH₄Cl, H₂SO₄, PbO₂.','','','PbO₂','',0,0,0,NULL),(3669,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: electric bulb, Electric bell, Electric iron, Electric oven','','','Electric bell','',0,0,0,NULL),(3671,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Dry cell : ZnCl₂ and NH₄Cl : : Dilute H₂SO₄ : ------','','','Lead acid cell : Electrolyte in dry cell is a wet pulp of zinc chloride (ZnCl₂) and ammonium chloride (NH₄Cl) whereas dilute Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄) is used as electrolyte in lead-acid cell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3672,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Wall clocks : Dry cell : : Electric bell : ------','','','Electromagnet','',0,0,0,NULL),(3673,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Dry cell : Carbon electrode : : Lead acid cell : -----','','','Lead electrode','',0,0,0,NULL),(3674,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Current : ampere : : Charge : ------','','','coulomb','',0,0,0,NULL),(3677,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Lead acid cell : 2 V : : Ni-Cd cell : -------','','','1.2 V','',0,0,0,NULL),(3678,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Torch : Light energy : : Bell : -----','','','Sound energy','',0,0,0,NULL),(3692,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: The three sources of electricity. ','','','Electric generators, Solar cell, Dry cell','',0,0,0,NULL),(3693,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Three main effect of electric current.','','','Heating effect, magnetic effect, chemical effect','',0,0,0,NULL),(3698,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the function of electric cell?','','','The main function of electric cells is to maintain a constant potential difference between its two terminals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3699,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why a large electric current cannot be drawn from a dry cell?','','','Due to the wet pulp used in dry cell, the chemical reaction proceeds very slowly. Hence, a large electric current cannot be obtained from this.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3700,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are uses of lead acid cells?','','','Lead acid electric cells have a capacity to deliver large current. Hence lead acid cells are used in cars, trucks, motorcycles and uninterrupted power supplies (UPS).','',0,0,0,NULL),(3701,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If each cell has potential 1 V, how can we create total potential of 3 V?','','','If each cell has a potential difference of  1 V the total potential difference of three cells will be 3 V, when they are connected in series.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3702,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the use of electromagnets?','','','Electromagnets are used to produce strong magnetic field useful in scientific research.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3703,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What acts as an electromagnet in an electric bell?','','','A Copper wire wound around an iron piece act as an electromagnet in an electric bell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3704,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Charge ','','','Charge is a physical property that cause matter to flow a current through it.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3705,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Electric current','','','Electric current is due to flow of electrons in a conductor or amount of charge flowing through wire in unit time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3706,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Electrostatic potential','','','There is a tendency of the positive charge to flow from a point of higher electric level to a point of lower electric level. This electric level deciding the direction of flow of electric charge is called electrostatic potential.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3707,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Potential difference','','','The difference between the electrostatic potential of two points is called potential difference.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3708,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Volt','','','The SI unit of potential difference is called volt.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3709,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: 1 ampere ','','','When one Coulomb of charge is passing through a conductor in one second, then the amount of current passing through it is called as one ampere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3710,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Electric cell','','','A device that allows uniform flow of charges in a circuit, is called as electric cell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3712,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Electric circuit','','','An electrical device that provides a path for electrical current to flow, is called an electric circuit which is a connection of various electrical components.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3713,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Electromagnetism','','','An interaction between electricity and magnetism, when an electric current or a charging electric field generates a magnetic field or when a changing magnetic field generates an electric field.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3714,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Battery of cells','','','When more than one cells are connected in a circuit, the set of cells is called a battery of cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3715,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Volt and Ampere','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3716,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Dry cell and Lead-Acid cell','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3717,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Lithium cell and Ni-Cd cell','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3718,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can the strength of an electromagnet be increased?','','','i) The strength of an electromagnet can be increased by increasing the strength of the current.\nii) Greater the strength of the current, greater is the strength of the electromagnet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3720,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was the dry cell given this name?','','','The chemicals used inside the dry cell are dry powders or thick moist pastes. Hence, the dry cell was given this name.','',0,0,0,NULL),(3721,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason: Free electrons are charged carriers. ','','','i) Each atom in a metallic conductor has one or more loosely bound electrons called free electrons.\nii) These electrons can move from one part of a conductor to another part.\niii) Since the free electrons move throughout the conductor they are called the charge carriers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3725,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason: Current flows through a wire in a circuit.','','','i) There is a tendency of the positive charge to flow from a point of higher electric level to a point of lower electric level.\nii) This electric level deciding the direction of flow of electric charges is called electrostatic potential.\niii) The difference between the potential of two points is called as potential difference.\niv) When potential difference is generated across two ends of a wire, current flows through it.\nv) There is no current as there is no potential difference in the absence of any cell.\nvi) Current starts flowing in the circuit as soon as the potential difference is applied.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3728,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why the power supplier in a car is referred as battery and not cell?','','','i) Cell refers to single cell whereas battery refers to combination of cells in series.\nii) Combination of cell produces more potential and more current.\niii) In a car, more power source is required hence we prefer combination of cells over single cell.\niv) Hence, the power supplier is called as battery and not cell in case of cars.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3730,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following chart :','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(3731,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the figures below and state their uses:','','','i) Cell holder : It is used to hold dry cells which fit in it.\nii) Battery of cells : A battery is used to obtain potential difference higher than potential difference obtained the help of single cell.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(3739,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Represent a simple electric circuit with a neat labelled diagram.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3740,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw and neat labelled diagram of electric bell.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(3742,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How electrical circuit is formed?','','','i) When a cell holder, an electric bulb and a plug key are connected by connecting wires, as shown in the figure and a dry cell is fitted in the holder, then the bulb lights a by closing the plug key.\nii) This means that a current flows through the circuit and bulb lights up.\niii) On the removal of the cell, the electric current flowing through the circuit stops as indicated by the bulb which ceases to glow.\niv) This type of connection of electrical components is called an electrical circuit.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(3743,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain about electromagnet?','','','i) Take a meter long flexible copper wire having resistive coating and wind tightly on a long iron screw.\nii) Connect the two ends of the wire in a circuit as shown in the figure aside.\niii) Now start the current in the circuit by plugging the key.\niv) It will be noticed that the pins/ nails have stuck to the tip of the screw.\nv) When the electric current flows in the wire, magnetic effect is produced in the coil around the screw and because of that the screw also attains magnetism.\nvi) As soon as the current is stopped, this magnetism vanishes.\nvii) The system of coil and the screw is called an electromagnet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(3745,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Correct and complete the following circuit diagram.','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(3751,28,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on Lead-Acid cell.','','','i) The Lead - Acid cell contains a lead electrode and a lead oxide electrode and both the dipped in dilute sulphuric acid.\nii) Lead oxide (PbO₂) carries a positive charge, while lead (Pb) electrode carries a negative charge.\niii) Because of the chemical reaction between the substances in the cell, electric charge is produced on both the electrodes and electric current flows through the load in the circuit.\niv) These cells have a capacity to deliver large amount of current. Hence, lead acid cells are used in cars, trucks, motorcycles and an interpreted power supplies (UPS).\nv) These cells can be recharged after getting electrically discharged.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(3752,28,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain with an activity the property of electromagnetism.','','','i) Take a meter long flexible copper wire having resistive coating and wind tightly on a long iron screw.\nii) Connect the two ends of the wire in a circuit.\niii) Connect and electric cell and a plug key in the circuit.\niv) Keep 2-4 iron pins/ small nails near the screw.\nv) Now, start the current in the circuit by plugging the key.\nvi) It will be noticed that the pins/ nails have stuck to the tip of the screw.\nvii) When the electric current flows in the wire, magnetism is produced in the coil around the screw and because of that the screw also attains magnetism.\nviii) As soon as the current is stopped, this magnetism vanishes. The system of coil and the screw is called and electromagnet.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(4414,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following numbers on number line : 3/5  ,6/5\n','','','Student can draw a number line by using above numbers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4415,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following numbers on number line : 4/9  ,(-7)/9  ,(-5)/9\n','','','Student can draw a number line by using above numbers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4416,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : -7, -2 \n','','','-7 < -2    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4417,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : 0,   (-9)/5 \n','','','0 > -9/5','',0,0,0,NULL),(4418,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : 8/7, 0\n','','','8/7 > 0','',0,0,0,NULL),(4419,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : (-5)/4  ,1/4\n','','','-5/4 < 1/4','',0,0,0,NULL),(4420,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : 40/29  ,   141/29\n','','','40/29 < 141/29','',0,0,0,NULL),(4421,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : (-17)/20  ,   (-13)/20\n','','','-17/20 < -13/20','',0,0,0,NULL),(4422,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : 7/9  ,    11/13\n','','','7 × 13 = 91  and 9 × 11 = 99\n∴ 91 < 99\n∴ 7/9 < 11/13\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4423,33,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : (-9)/13  ,   (-5)/9\n','','','-9 × 9 = -81  and 13 × (-5) = -65\n∴ -81 < -65\n∴ -9/13 < -5/9 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4424,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following numbers on number line : 3/2, 5/2, (-3)/2\n','','','Student can draw a number line by using above numbers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4425,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following numbers on number line : 7/5  ,(-2)/5,  (-4)/5    \n','','','Student can draw a number line by using above numbers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4426,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following numbers on number line : (-5)/8  ,11/8\n','','','Student can draw a number line by using above numbers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4427,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following numbers on number line : 13/10  ,(-17)/10\n','','','Student can draw a number line by using above numbers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4428,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : 15/12  ,7/16\n','','','15 × 16 = 240  and 12 × 7 = 84\n∴  240 > 84\n ∴ 15/12 > 7/16\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4429,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : i) (-25)/8  ,(-9)/4 		ii) 12/15  ,3/5\n','','','i) -25 × 4 = -100 and -9 × 8 = -72\n∴ -100 < -72\n∴ -25/8 < -9/4\nii) 12 × 5 = 60  and 15 × 3 = 45\n∴ 60 > 45\n∴ 12/15 > 3/5 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4430,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : (-7)/11  ,(-3)/4\n','','','-7 × 4 = -28 and -3 × 11 = -33\n∴ -28 > -33\n∴ -7/11 > -3/4\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4431,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : 25/16  ,71/48\n','','','25 × 48 = 1200 and 16 × 71 = 1136\n∴ 1200 > 1136\n∴ 25/16  > 71/48\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4432,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the numbers : 4/7  ,16/28\n','','','4 × 28 = 112  and 7 × 16 = 112\n∴ 112 = 112\n∴ 4/7 = 16/28\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4433,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following numbers on a number line : 2/3  ,(-5)/3,5/3\n','','','Student can draw a number line by using above numbers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4447,33,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following rational numbers in decimal form : 9/37\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(4448,33,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following rational numbers in decimal form : 18/42\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(4449,33,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following rational numbers in decimal form : 9/14\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(4450,33,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following rational numbers in decimal form : (-103)/5\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(4451,33,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following numbers on a number line : 1/6  ,(-7)/6,11/6    \n','','','Student can draw a number line by using above numbers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4452,33,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following numbers in decimal form : i) 7/4     ii) (-29)/5 \n','','','i) 7/4 = 1.75\nii) -29/5 = -5.8\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4453,33,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following numbers in decimal form : i) 35/8     ii) 2/11 ','','','i) 35/8 = 4.375\nii) 2/11 = 0.181818-----\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4454,33,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following rational numbers in decimal form : (-11)/13\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(4456,33,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the number √5 on the number line','','','Construction on the number line\nOQ = 2, OQ ꓕ OP. Take PQ = 1\nBy Pythagoras theorem, OP² = OQ² + QP²\n= 2² + 1² = 4 + 1 = 5\n∴ OP = √5 \n','',0,0,1,NULL),(4457,33,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the number √7 on the number line\n','','','Construction on the number line\nOA = 1, AP = 1,   OP = √2      ∴ OB = √2\nOB = √2, BQ = 1  ∴ OQ = √3     ∴ OC = √3\nOC = √3, CR = 2     ∴ OR = √7\nBy Pythagoras theorem, OC² + CR² = OR²\n(√3)² + 2² = 3 + 4 = 7\n∴ OR = √7\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(4460,33,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following numbers in decimal form : i) 9/13     ii) (-15)/7 \n\n       ','','','i) 9/13 = 0.692307692307-----\nii) -15/7 = -2.142857142857------\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4461,33,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the number √10  and √17 on the same number line.\n','','','Student can draw number line','',0,0,0,NULL),(4487,34,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If lines l∥ m and ∠x and ∠y are interior angles if ∠x=100⁰  then ∠y?','','','∠x + ∠y = 180⁰ ----- [interior angles are supplementary]\n∴ ∠y = 180 – 100 = 80⁰\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4488,34,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the adjoining figure, ∠P=?   ','','','∠P = 90⁰ ----- [corresponding angles]','',1,0,0,NULL),(4492,34,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the relationship between the angles shown in the given figure','','','line l ‖ line m and line p is transversal\n∴ ∠a = ∠b ------ [Alternate angles]','',1,0,0,NULL),(4502,34,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the value of x if line a ∥ line b ','','','∠y + 120 = 180⁰ ----- [linear pair]\n∴ ∠y = 180 – 120 = 60⁰\nLine a ‖ line b \n∴ ∠x = ∠y = 60⁰ ----- [corresponding angles]\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(4504,34,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a line k parallel to the line m at a distance of 5 cm from it','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4512,34,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find a, b and c in the following figure, if line x ∥ line y ∥line z  ','','','a = 110⁰ ---- [Opposite angles]\nLine x ‖ line y \n∴ ∠a + ∠b = 180⁰ ----- [Interior angles]\n∴ ∠b = 180 – 110 = 70⁰ \nLine y ‖ line z\n∴ ∠b = ∠c = 70⁰ ----- [corresponding angles]\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(4515,34,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','line l ∥line m and line p ∥ line q. If ∠ABC=130⁰ then ∠BCD =----','','','line l ‖ line m and line p is transversal\n∴ ∠ABC + ∠BCD = 180⁰ ----- [Interior angles]\n∴ ∠BCD = 180 – 130 = 50⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(4518,34,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Line l ∥ line m ∥ line n , line p is a transversal. Find the value of x','','','line l ‖ line n and line p is transversal\n∴ y = 2x ----- [corresponding angles]\n∠x + ∠y = 180⁰ ----- [linear pair]\n∴ x + 2x = 180⁰      ⟹  3x = 180   \n∴ x = 60⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(4526,34,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find x if line l ∥ line m ∥ line n  ','','','line m ‖ line n \n∴ ∠x + ∠y = 180⁰ ----- [interior angles] ---- (i)\nLine l ‖ line n\n∴ 30 + ∠y = 105⁰ ----- [Alternate angles]\n∴ ∠y = 105 – 30 = 75⁰ \nPut in equation (i), we get,\n∠x + 75 = 180  \n∴ ∠x = 180 – 75 = 105⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(4527,34,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find a from the figure if line x ∥ line y ∥ line z  ','','','line y ‖ line z\n∴ ∠b = 126⁰ ----- [corresponding angles]\nLine x ‖ line y\n∴ ∠a = ∠b = 126⁰ ----- [Alternate angles]','',1,0,0,NULL),(4529,34,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Line AB ∥ line CD, ∠BQP = 56⁰,∠PRD = 44⁰. Find ∠RPQ ','','','Draw line EF passing through point P such that, AB ‖ EF ‖ CD\n∴ ∠RPQ = ∠QPE + ∠EPR ----- (i)\nLine AB ‖ line EF and line QP is transversal\n∴ ∠QPE = 56⁰ ----- [Alternate angles]\nLine EF ‖ line CD and line PR is transversal\n∴ ∠EPR = 44⁰ ----- [Alternate angles]\n∴ ∠RPQ = 56 + 44 = 100⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(4549,35,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Orthocenter','','','The point of concurrence of the three altitudes of the triangle is called the orthocenter and is usually denoted by O.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4550,35,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Centroid','','','The point of concurrence of the three medians of the triangle is called the centroid and is usually denoted by G.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4551,35,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Altitude','','','The perpendicular segment drawn from a vertex of a triangle on the side opposite to it is called an altitude of the triangle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4554,35,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where does the orthocenter of obtuse angled triangle lies?','','','The orthocenter of obtuse angled triangle lies in exterior of triangle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4561,35,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw perpendicular bisector of seg AB, l(AB)=7 cm','','','Students can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4562,35,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where does the orthocenter of acute angled triangle and right angled triangle lies?','','','The orthocenter of acute angled triangles lies in the interior of triangle and the orthocenter of right angled triangle lies in the vertex containing the right angle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4568,35,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw an obtuse angled ∆MAN. Draw its medians and name its centroid G','','','Students can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4569,35,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw an acute angled ∆LUV. Draw its altitude. Note your observation.','','','Students can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4570,35,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a right angled ∆PQR. Draw its altitude. Note your observation.','','','Students can draw the figure by given information.\nObservation : Point of concurrence of altitudes of triangles is the vertex containing the right angle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(4614,36,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Express the given number in index form : seventh root of 7','','','(7)⅟₇','',0,0,0,NULL),(4615,36,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Express the given number in index form : square root of cube of 4','','','(4)³⁄₂','',0,0,0,NULL),(4616,36,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Express the given number in index form : Sixth root of 11','','','(11)⅙','',0,0,0,NULL),(4617,36,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in the form of ‘nth root of a’ :  27⅓','','','Cube root of 27','',0,0,0,NULL),(4618,36,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in the form of ‘nth root of a’ :  49¹/₇','','','Seventh root of 49','',0,0,0,NULL),(4619,36,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in the form of ‘nth root of a’ : 10¹/₁₀','','','Tenth root of 10','',0,0,0,NULL),(4624,36,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The cube of 0.03 ','','','0.03 × 0.03 × 0.03 = 0.000027','',0,0,0,NULL),(4627,36,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the cube root of the following numbers : i) 512 		ii) -1728','','','i) 512 = 8 × 8 × 8\n∴ ∛512 = 8\nii) -1728 = -12 × -12 × -12\n∴ ∛(-1728) = -12\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4628,36,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the cube root of the following numbers : i) 42875       	ii) -13824','','','i) 42875 = 35 × 35 × 35\n∴ ∛42875 = 35\nii) -13824 = -24 × -24 × -24\n∴ ∛(-13824)  = -24\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4629,36,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Express power of root and root of the power :  (225)³/₂','','','Power of the root : Cube of square root of 225\nRoot of the power : Square root of cube of 225\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4630,36,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Express power of root and root of the power :  (21)³/₇','','','Power of the root : Cube of seventh root of 21\nRoot of the power : Seventh root of cube of 21\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4631,36,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the cube root of the following numbers : i) 8000          ii) 729','','','i) 8000 = 20 × 20 × 20\n∴ ∛8000  = 20\nii) 729 = 9 × 9 × 9\n∴ ∛729  = 9\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4633,36,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the cube root of 0.003375','','','0.003375 = 0.15 × 0.15 × 0.15\n∴ ∛0.003375 = 0.15\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4634,36,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Evaluate : i)  (3/8)¯² × (4/5)¯³	          ii)    (4¯¹ × 3¯¹)²','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(4637,36,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the cube root of the following numbers : i) 343             ii) -512','','','i) 343 = 7 × 7 × 7\n∴ ∛343 = 7\nii) -512 = -8 × -8 × -8\n∴ ∛(-512) = -8\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4638,36,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the cube root of the following numbers : i) -2744           ii) 32768','','','i) -2744 = -14 × -14 × -14\n∴ ∛(-2744) = -14\nii) 32768 = 32 × 32 × 32\n∴ ∛32768 = 32\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4644,36,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x = (3/2)² × (2/3)¯⁴  find the value of x²','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(4673,37,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The expansions of (x – 10) (x + 12) is','','','(x – 10)(x + 12) = x² + (-10 + 12)x + 12 × (-10)\n             = x² + 2x – 120 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4674,37,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The expansion of (m – 3) (m – 7) is','','','(m – 3)(m – 7) = m² + (-3 – 7)m + (-3) × (-7)\n              = m² - 10m + 21\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4675,37,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The expansion of  (x + 1/3) (x – 7/3) is ','','','(x + 1/3)(x – 7/3) = x² + (1/3 – 7/3) x + 1/3 × (-7/3)\n           = x² - 2x – 7/9 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4676,37,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fill in the blanks : (x + 4) (x – 3) = x² + (-----)x + 4 × (-----) ','','','(x + 4) (x – 3) = x² + (4 – 3) x + 4 × (-3) = x² + x – 12 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4677,37,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fill in the blanks : (2x + 5y) (2x – 5y) = (2x)² + (-----) × 2x + (5y)²','','','(2x + 5y)(2x – 5y) = (2x)² - (5y)²  = 4x² - 25y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(4678,37,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand : (a + 2) (a – 1)','','','(a + 2)(a – 1) = a² + (2 – 1)a + 2 × (-1) = a² + a – 2 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4679,37,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand : (m – 4) (m + 6)','','','(m – 4)(m + 6) = m² + (6 – 4)m + (6) × (-4) = m² + 2m – 24 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4680,37,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :    (p + 8) (p – 3)','','','(p + 8)(p – 3) = p² + (8 – 3)p + 8 × (-3) = p² + 5p – 24 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4681,37,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (13 + x) (13 – x)','','','(13 + x)(13 – x) = (13)² - x² = 169 – x² ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4684,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :   (x – 2) (x + 4)','','','(x – 2)(x + 4) = x² + 2x – 8 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4685,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :    (p + 5) (p + 2)','','','(p + 5)(p + 2) = p² + 7p + 10','',0,0,0,NULL),(4686,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :   (n – 3) (n – 7)','','','(n – 3)(n – 7) = n² - 10n + 21','',0,0,0,NULL),(4687,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :   (3x + 4y) (3x + 5y)','','','(3x + 4y)(3x + 5y) = (3x)² + (4y + 5y) × 3x + (4y) × 5y\n         = 9x² + 27xy + 20y²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4688,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :   (9x – 5t) (9x + 5t)','','','(9x – 5t)(9x + 5t) = (9x)² - (5t)² = 81x² - 25t²','',0,0,0,NULL),(4689,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (m + 2/3) (m – 7/3)','','','(m + 2/3)(m – 7/3) = m² + (2/3 – 7/3)m + (2/3) × (-7/3)\n          = m² - 5/3 m – 14/9 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4691,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (k + 4)³','','','(k + 4)³ = k³ + 3k² × 4 + 3k × 4² + 4³ = k³ + 12k² + 48k + 64','',0,0,0,NULL),(4692,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (7x + 8y)³','','','(7x  +8y)³ = (7x)³ + 3(7x)²(8y) + 3(7x)(8y)² + (8y)³\n                       = 343x³ + 1176x²y + 1344xy² + 512y³','',0,0,0,NULL),(4693,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (7 + m)³','','','(7 + m)³ = 7³ + 3 (7)² × m + 3(7) × m² + m³\n                     = 343 + 147m + 21m² + m³','',0,0,0,NULL),(4694,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (52)³','','','(52)³ = (50 + 2)³ = (50)³ + 3(50)²(2) + 3(50)(2)² + 2³\n                = 125000 + 15000 + 600 + 8 = 140608\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4695,37,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (101)³','','','(101)³ = (100 + 1)³ = (100)³ + 3(100)²(1) + 3(100)(1)² + 1³\n                = 1000000 + 30000 + 300 + 1 = 1030301\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4701,37,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (2m – 5)³','','','(2m – 5)³ = (2m)³ - 3(2m)²(5) + 3(2m)(5)² - 5³ \n                      = 8m³ - 60m² + 150m – 125 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4702,37,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (4 – p)³','','','(4 – p)³ = 4³ - 3(4)²(p) + 3(4)(p)² - p³ = 64 – 48p + 12p² - p³','',0,0,0,NULL),(4703,37,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand : (7x – 9y)³','','','(7x – 9y)³ = (7x)³ - 3(7x)²(9y) + 3(7x)(9y)² - (9y)³\n                     = 343x³- 1323x²y + 1701xy² - 729y³\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4704,37,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (58)³','','','(58)³ = (60 – 2)³ = (60)³ - 3(60)²(2) + 3(60)(2)² - 2³ \n               = 216000 – 21600 + 720 – 8 = 195112\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4705,37,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (198)³','','','(198)³ = (200 – 2)³ = (200)³ - 3(200)²(2) + 3(200)(2)² - 2³ \n               = 8000000 – 240000 + 2400 – 8 = 7762392 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4708,37,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (2p + q + 5)²','','','(2p + q + 5)² = (2p)² + q² + 5² + 2(2p)(q) + 2(q)(5) + 2(2p)(5)\n                   = 4p² + q² + 25 + 4pq + 10q + 20p\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4709,37,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (m + 2n + 3r)²','','','(m + 2n + 3r)² = m² + (2n)² + (3r)² + 2(m)(2n) + 2(2n)(3r) + 2(m)(3r)\n           = m² + 4n² + 9r² + 4mn + 12nr + 6mr\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4710,37,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (3x + 4y – 5y)²','','','(3x + 4y – 5p)² = (3x)² + (4y)² + (-5p)² + 2(3x)(4y) + 2(4y)(-5p) + 2(3x)(-5p)\n            = 9x² + 16y² + 25p² + 24xy – 40yp – 30px ','',0,0,0,NULL),(4711,37,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :  (7m – 3n – 4k)²','','','(7m – 3n – 4k)² \n         = (7m)² + (-3n)² + (-4k)² + 2(7m)(-3n) + 2(-3n)(-4k) + 2(7m)(-4k)\n            = 49m² + 9n² + 16k² - 42mn + 24nk – 56mk \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4713,37,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify :  (2a + b)³ - (2a – b)³','','','(a + b)³ - (a – b)³ = a³ + 3a²b + 3ab² + b³- a³ + 3a²b - 3ab² + b³\n                        = 6a²b + 2b³\n∴ (2a + b)³ - (2a – b)³ = 6(2a)² + 2b³ = 24a²b + 2b³\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4714,37,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify :  (3r – 2k)³ + (3r + 2k)³','','','(a - b)³ + (a + b)³ = a³ - 3a²b + 3ab² - b³+ a³ + 3a²b + 3ab² + b³\n               = 2a³ + 6ab²\n∴ (3r – 2k)³ + (3r + 2k)³ = 2(3r)³ + 6(3r)(2k)² = 54r³ + 72rk²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4715,37,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify :  (4a – 3)³ - (4a + 3)³','','','(a - b)³ - (a + b)³ = a³ - 3a²b + 3ab² - b³- a³ - 3a²b - 3ab² - b³\n                  = -6a²b – 2b³\n∴ (4a – 3)³ - (4a + 3)³ = -6(4a)²(3) – 2(3)³ = -288a² - 54 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4716,37,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify :  (5x – 7y)³ + (5x + 7y)³','','','(a - b)³ + (a + b)³ = a³ - 3a²b + 3ab² - b³+ a³ + 3a²b + 3ab² + b³\n               = 2a³ + 6ab²\n∴ (5x – 7y)³ + (5x + 7y)³ = 2(5x)³ + 6(5x)(7y)² = 250x³ + 1470xy²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4717,37,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (x – 2y + 3)² + (x + 2y – 3)²','','','(x – 2y + 3)² + (x + 2y – 3)²\n= x² + 4y² + 9 – 4xy – 12y + 6x + x² + 4y² + 9 + 4xy – 12y – 6x \n= 2x² + 8y² + 18 – 24y \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4718,37,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify :  (3k – 4r – 2m)² - (3k + 4r – 2m)²','','','(3k – 4r – 2m)² - (3k + 4r – 2m)²\n= (9k² + 16r² + 4m² - 24kr + 16rm – 12km) – (9k² + 16r² + 4m² + 24kr – 16rm – 12km)\n= 9k² + 16r² + 4m² - 24kr + 16rm – 12km - 9k² - 16r² - 4m² - 24kr + 16rm + 12km\n= -48kr + 32rm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(4719,37,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify :  (7a – 6b + 5c)² + (7a + 6b – 5c)²','','','(7a – 6b + 5c)² + (7a + 6b – 5c)²\n= 49a² + 36b² + 25c² - 84ab – 60bc + 70ac + 49a² + 36b² + 25c² + 84ab – 60bc – 70ac \n= 98a² + 72b² + 50c² - 120bc \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7176,68,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities. ----- 10M\nA1) State whether the statements are true or false.\n        i) Androcles master was a kind and gentle person.\n        ii) The masters were forced to work for their slaves\n        iii) Androcles master often whipped his slaves.\n         iv) According to Androcles, death was better than serving such a master.\n                 Long, long ago, there lived a kind, gentle and courteous man called Androcles.  But he had a very cruel master. In those days, kings, nobles and others will be men used to poor people as their slaves and replace was a slave. The slaves were forced to work for the masters. They had to do what their masters told them. They had no freedom, no rights, even when they were unhappy with their master they could not leave him. They were bound by law to obey their masters, however wicked he might be.\n                Androcles did not like his heartless, wicked master. His master started his starved his slaves and often whipped them. Androcles felt that even death was better than serving such a master. He kept looking out for a good opportunity. At the first chance he got, he escaped from the clutches of his cruel master.\n                Now, although, Androcles had thus run away, he was not coward. He took shelter in a forest. He was not afraid of the wild beasts that room the forest. He did not mind that he had no roof over his head. He was happy that he was free as a bird to go wherever he wished.\nA2) Complex factual question\n      What type of man was Androcles?\nA3) Match the following words with their meaning.\n       Column A                               Column B \ni) courteous                                        a) opportunity\nii) chance                                           b) clutches\niii) wandered                                     c) polite\niv) tight bond                                    d) roamed\nA4) Grammar\n      i) Androcles did not like his cruel master. (Add question tag)\n      ii) Long, long ago there lived a king. (Identify the tense) \nA5) Open ended question \n        What do you think about the people who treat others disrespectfully?\n','','','A1] i) False       ii) False      iii) True         iv) True\nA2] Androcles was a kind gentle and courteous man. he obeyed and respected his master.\nA3] i - c, ii - a, iii - d, iv - b \nA4] i) did he?       ii) simple past tense\nA5] I think that the people who treat disrespectfully to others are selfish people. They only care for themselves. Such people are wicked and heartless. They never think about others. They live in their own world. They think that they are more superior than others. We should avoid such people.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7177,68,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','One day, as he was wondering in the forest. He suddenly came face to face with a lion. his first reaction was to turn and flee. but then he saw that the lion had not mood on seeing him. It neither gowned nor roared. in fact, it looked at him pitiously as if it was in great pain.\n                   Androcles paused. Then he moved very cautiously towards the lion, step by step, the lion moaned and whimpered.\" Please help me, \" it\'s eyes seemed to say.\n                    When Androcles dree near. The lion lowered its head and began to lick its paw. Then Androcles saw that the lion paw was wounded, sore, and Swollen\n             On a closer look, Androcles noticed that a big thorn had pierced one of the lions toes. So this is the cause of the lines distress\' , thought Androcles.\n             Summoning up of all the courage,  Androcles streched his hand slowly towards the injured paw. He was afraid that if the lion would attack him if he touched his swollen paw. But the lion seemed to understand his good intention.\n             Androcles took the paw in his left hand. Deftly he pulled out the thorn. Then tearing a piece of his own clothing the kind man bound of the lions injured power to his surprise and wonder the lion then lit his harm as if to show gratitude and brock Les remained with the lion till it could walk again you would hunt in the forest and share his food with the helpless beast everyday he would clean and dress the lions wound soon the lion was able to move about many times it follows wherever he went.','<b>A1) Fill in the blanks by choosing the correct alternative</b>\n      i) When Androcles came face to face with the lion, his first reaction was to turn and.......\n       a) attack                              b) tease                       c) flee\n      ii) He moved  very ................towards the lion.\n       a)  hurriedly                          b) slowly                     c) cautiously\n      iii) The lion seemed to understand his good............\n       a)  intention                          b) habit                        c) mood\n      iv) Androcles pulled out the throne..............\n       a) smoothly                           b) deftly                        c) slowly','<b>A2) Complex factual question</b>\nWhy was Androcles happy? \n<b>A3) Find the words from the passage that mean</b>\n       i) hurting                ii) cried painful                 iii) feeling of thankfulness             iv) without help\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       1) Everyday he would clean and dress the lions wound. \n            (Pick out the auxiliary verb)\n       2) The kind man bounded up the lions injured paw. \n            (Pick out the adjective)\n<b>A5] Open ended questions</b>\nWhat do you learn about Androcles from the passage?','','A1] i) d) flee       ii) c) cautiously       iii) a)  intention       iv) b) deftly     \nA2] Androcles was happy because he was free as bird. He could also go where he wished\nA3] i) sore        ii) whimpered           iii) gratitude         iv) helpless\nA4] 1) would        2) adjective - kind, injured\nA5] From the passage one learn that Androcles was a kind, gentle and courteous man. he saved the lion which was in great pain. This shows that he was a courageous and sensitive man. He nursed the lion\'s wound and offered him food. It shows his caring and co-operative nature. he was brave as well as a person with great presence of mind.','',0,1,0,NULL),(7178,68,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities. ----- 10M\nA1) State whether the statements are true or false\n        i) Androcles moved carelessly towards lion.\n        ii) Androcles stretched his hands slowly.\n        iii) The lion lowered its head.\n         iv) Androcles came face to face worth tiger.\n                       \n                  One day, as he was wondering in the forest. He suddenly came face to face with a lion. his first reaction was to turn and flee. but then he saw that the lion had not mood on seeing him. It neither gowned nor roared. in fact, it looked at him pitiously as if it was in great pain.\n                   Androcles paused. Then he moved very cautiously towards the lion, step by step, the lion moaned and whimpered.\" Please help me, \" it\'s eyes seemed to say.\n                    When Androcles dree near. The lion lowered its head and began to lick its paw. Then Androcles saw that the lion paw was wounded, sore, and Swollen\n             On a closer look, Androcles noticed that a big thorn had pierced one of the lions toes. So this is the cause of the lines distress\' , thought Androcles.\n             Summoning up of all the courage,  Androcles streched his hand slowly towards the injured paw. He was afraid that if the lion would attack him if he touched his swollen paw. But the lion seemed to understand his good intention.\n             Androcles took the paw in his left hand. Deftly he pulled out the thorn. Then tearing a piece of his own clothing the kind man bound of the lions injured power to his surprise and wonder the lion then lit his harm as if to show gratitude and brock Les remained with the lion till it could walk again you would hunt in the forest and share his food with the helpless beast everyday he would clean and dress the lions wound soon the lion was able to move about many times it follows wherever he went.\nA2) Complex factual question\nWhy was the lion in pain?\nA3) Write down \'ly\' words for the following.\n        i) sudden               ii) piteous                  iii) cautious         iv) deft\nA4] Grammar\n       1) Androcles took the paw in his left hand.( Identify the tense)\n       2) He was wondering in the forest. (Add question tag)\nA5) Open ended question\nWhich is your favourite animal? Why?\n','','','A1] i) True       ii) True      iii) True           iv) False\nA2] The lion was in pain because a big thorn had pierced one of its toes and that paw was wounded, sore and swollen.\nA3] i) suddenly       ii) piteously                  iii) cautiously         iv) deftly\nA4] i) Simple past tense        ii) wasn\'t he?\nA5] My favourite animal is dog. Because, dog is faithful animal. He protects our home from robbers.      \n       ','',0,0,0,NULL),(7179,68,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','In the meanwhile, Androcles\'s master had complained to the authorities that his slave and replace had a run away. Soldiers were Sent in all the directions to capture Androcles. They searched for him high and low but couldn\'t find him. At last, they came to the forest where Androcles leaved.\n                It is so happened that Androcles had gone out alone that day while the lion rested near the cave. Both of them fill into the hands of the soldiers. The soldiers took them back to the city They put Androcles in chain in a prison. The lion was caged.\n                In those days, people who were found to be guilty of a serious crime were fed to wild beast as punishment running away from Once master was a very serious crime at that time there for Androcles 2 was to be thrown to the wild beast\n               On the appointed day the emperor arrived at the circus and took his place. The crowds cheered loudly. The trumpet blew the trumpet and Androcles was brought into the arena. A chain was unbound. He filled with and dismayed he was certain that this was the last day of his life.\n                At the opposite end, a gate was raised and ferocious lion was released into the arena. It had been kept hungry to make it more ferocious and replace was filled with fear but he put up a brave front.\n               The lion saw Androcles and rushed towards him, bounding and roaring. But suddenly, it stopped in its tracks they need to move it slowly forward. All its a Rafe seem to have melted. Androcles saw this too. Each friend recognized the other.\n              To everyone\'s amazement, the lion bounded towards Androcles again, but when it reached him, it began to purr and rub its nose against him. Androcles hugged the lion. He wept with relief and joy.\n              The audience, including the emperor, watched this tender sight in great wonder. People began to cheer Androcles and asked for his released the emperor asked and broke Les to explain the lines strange behavior when he heard the whole story the emperor was also moved by the gratitude and friendship between man and beast, he ordered that endocasts should be set free he would no longer slave when the emperor asked and broke Les what other reward he would like Androcles replied that he did not want anything for himself. But he wanted the lion to be set free too. The two friends went back to the forest to you a free life.','<b>A1) State whether the statements are true or false</b>\n      i) Androcles alone fill into the hands of the soldiers.\n      ii) Running away from Once master was a common crime at the time.\n      iii) The lion killed Androcles.\n      iv) The emperor set free Androcles as well as the lion.\n\n','A2) What did Androcles feel after he was a brought to the arena?\nA3) Find out the antonyms of the following words from the passage.\n      a) bound                      b) enemy                      c) catch               d) ingratitude\n<b>A4) Grammar</b>\ni) Running away from the ones master was a very serious crime at that time.  \n      (Identify subject and predicate)\n      ii) He felt weak and dismayed. \n       (Change into past continuous tense)\n<b>A5) Open ended questions</b>\nDo you forgive others? How?\n','','A1] i) False           ii) False       iii) False       iv) True\nA2] When Androcles was brought to the arena he felt weak and sad. He knew that it was the last day of his life. At first he was filled with fear then showed that he was not afraid.\nA3] a) unbound     b) friend        c) release       d) gratitude\nA4] i) Subject : Running away from one\'s master\n         Predicate : was a very serious crime at that time\n       ii) He was feeling weak and dismayed.\nA5] Yes, I forgive others. When my friends behave rudely with me, tease me, I forgive them, because it is a sign of large heartedness. It is a quality of a powerful mind.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7180,68,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities. ----- 10M\nA1) Write one word for the many words.\n       i) the male ruler of an Empire.\n       ii) a ground surrounded by seats on all sides.\n       iii) gathering of spectators at a performance.\n       iv) brass musical instrument with a brilliant tone.\n                In the meanwhile, Androcles\'s master had complained to the authorities that his slave and replace had a run away. Soldiers were Sent in all the directions to capture Androcles. They searched for him high and low but couldn\'t find him. At last, they came to the forest where Androcles leaved.\n                It is so happened that Androcles had gone out alone that day while the lion rested near the cave. Both of them fill into the hands of the soldiers. The soldiers took them back to the city They put Androcles in chain in a prison. The lion was caged.\n                In those days, people who were found to be guilty of a serious crime were fed to wild beast as punishment running away from Once master was a very serious crime at that time there for Androcles 2 was to be thrown to the wild beast\n               On the appointed day the emperor arrived at the circus and took his place. The crowds cheered loudly. The trumpet blew the trumpet and Androcles was brought into the arena. A chain was unbound. He filled with and dismayed he was certain that this was the last day of his life.\n                At the opposite end, a gate was raised and ferocious lion was released into the arena. It had been kept hungry to make it more ferocious and replace was filled with fear but he put up a brave front.\n               The lion saw Androcles and rushed towards him, bounding and roaring. But suddenly, it stopped in its tracks they need to move it slowly forward. All its a Rafe seem to have melted. Androcles saw this too. Each friend recognized the other.\n              To everyone\'s amazement, the lion bounded towards Androcles again, but when it reached him, it began to purr and rub its nose against him. Androcles hugged the lion. He wept with relief and joy.\n              The audience, including the emperor, watched this tender sight in great wonder. People began to cheer Androcles and asked for his released the emperor asked and broke Les to explain the lines strange behavior when he heard the whole story the emperor was also moved by the gratitude and friendship between man and beast, he ordered that endocasts should be set free he would no longer slave when the emperor asked and broke Les what other reward he would like Androcles replied that he did not want anything for himself. But he wanted the lion to be set free too. The two friends went back to the forest to you a free life.\nA2) How did the emperor reward Androcles?\nA3) Write opposite words from the passage for the following\n      a) kind        b) poor       c) slave       d) high\nA4) Grammar\n      i) Androcles saw lion and rushed towards him. (Identify the tense)\n      a) Simple past tense         b) simple present tense         c) Perfect past tense\n       ii) The emperor arrived at the circus. (Add question tag)\nA5) Open ended question\nDo you say sorry when you commit a mistake? How do you feel after saying sorry?\n','','','A1] i) emperor      ii) arena       iii)  audience     iv) trumpet    \nA2] The emperor rewarded Androcles by setting him as well as his friend, the lion free\nA3] a) wicked/cruel      b) wealthy      c) master          d) low   \nA4] i) Simple past tense        ii) didn\'t he?\nA5] Yes. I say sorry when I commit a mistake. I feel so much relax after saying sorry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7185,70,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','I got ready and went to school. In the first period, our class teacher, who taught us English, told us to take out our composition notebooks. I took out the mathematics notebook by mistake. I replaced it quickly but the teacher, perhaps, has gaze fixed on me. She shouted, \" come here with your book, Sayli!\" I Obaid her but her angry looks made in my legs symbol and when I reached her chair, that notebook fill on her feet. \n     Some girls laughed loudly. The teacher thought that I had drop the notebook on purpose. She stared at me with an all devouring look. \" Go and stand near your seat,\" she yelled. I kept standing, punished for the whole period. \n     The next History class and my favourite subject. The teacher had asked us to memorize the causes and effects of the Battle of Panipat. I was will - prepared. She began testing all of us, one by one. When my turn came, I went blank. The teacher gives me a stern stare, while I stood there stiff, still, with my head hanging down in shame','<b>A1) State whether the statements are true or false</b>\n      i) Class teacher taught Math’s subject\n     ii) The notebook fill on the class teachers’ feet.\n     iii) Sayali\'s favorite subject was English.\n     iv) The teacher had asked the students to memorize the causes and effects of the battle of Plasi.','<b>A2) Complex factual question</b>\n          Why could the notebook have fallen from Sayali\'s hand? \n<b>A3] Find out the words from the passage that mean...............</b>\n       i) Shiver .............          ii) Could not remember anything ...............\n      iii) exchanged..........   iv) not moving.........\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\ni) I had dropped the notebook. (Name the tense)\nii) Choose the imperative sentences.\na) I obeyed her.           b) The notebook fell on her feet.  \nc) Come here with your book.\n<b>A5] Open ended questions</b>\n       Which is your favourite subject? Why?','','A1] i) False          ii) True         iii) False        iv) False\nA2] Sayali took out mathematics notebook instead of English composition notebook by mistake. her teacher got angry for that. When teacher called her, her legs started trembling. So, due to fear, the \n       notebook could have fallen from Sayali\'s hand.\nA3] i) Shiver - tremble         ii)  Could not remember anything - went blank            iii)  exchanged - replaced            iv) not moving - stiff\nA4] i) Past perfect tense          ii) c) Come here with your book\nA5] My favourite subject is English. I like English because it is a simple and easy language. it is the language of computers and knowledge. And I have a very good English teacher. He created interest in me for this subject.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7186,70,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities.\nA1) Complete the remaining part from the passage.\n       i) Class teacher told us ..........................\n       ii) when I reached her chair, ..............\n      iii) The teacher thought that ..............\n       iv) Sayli was punished for ................\n     I got ready and went to school. In the first period, our class teacher, who taught us English, told us to take out our composition notebooks. I took out the mathematics notebook by mistake. I replaced it quickly but the teacher, perhaps, has gaze fixed on me. She shouted, \" come here with your book, Sayli!\" I Obaid her but her angry looks made in my legs symbol and when I reached her chair, that notebook fill on her feet. \n     Some girls laughed loudly. The teacher thought that I had drop the notebook on purpose. She stared at me with an all devouring look. \" Go and stand near your seat,\" she yelled. I kept standing, punished for the whole period. \n     The next History class and my favourite subject. The teacher had asked us to memorize the causes and effects of the Battle of Panipat. I was will - prepared. She began testing all of us, one by one. When my turn came, I went blank. The teacher gives me a stern stare, while I stood there stiff, still, with my head hanging down in shame\nA2) Complex factual question\n         What happened when it was Sayali\'s turn to answer?\nA3) Match the pairs of opposite words (antonyms)\n        Column A                       Column B\n        a) reward                        i) memorize\n        b) disobey                      ii) quickly\n        c) Forget                        iii) obey\n        d) slowly                       iv) punishment\nA4] Grammar\n1) She shouted come here with your book Sayali. (Punctuate the sentence)\n2) I obeyed her (make negative)\nA5) Open ended question\n       Do you laugh at your classmate in the class? When?\n','','','A1] i) to take out our composition notebooks.            ii) the notebook fell on her feet.\n      iii) I had dropped the notebook                            iv) the whole period\nA2] Actually History was Sayali\'s favourite subject. She had prepared the causes and effects of the Battle of Panipat. Well but when her turn came she could not remember anything. So she could not \n       answer.  \nA3] a) - iv,      b) - iii, c) - i, d - ii\nA4] i) She shouted, \"Come here with your book, Sayali. \"\n      ii) I did not disobey her.\nA5] Yes, sometimes I laugh at my classmates in the class. there are some funny incidents in the class, when we cannot stop ourselves from laughing. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(7187,70,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Mama and papa continued their never ending tiffs. Papa used every opportunity to express his annoyance. Mama on her part, did not allow any such occasion to slip without turning it into a vociferous quarrel. The quarrel was between the two, that is me at school. Wherever and whenever I heard raised voices I filled as if mama and papa were quarreling. Sometimes, even in the midst of silence, Mama\'s screams came through my ears. And, whenever I heard Mama\'s high pitched yells my legs quivered.\n       One day, I was coming down the school stairs. Somebody from the top give a frantic scream. I fell down and came tumbling down about seven steps. My elbows and knees were badly bruised. My new frock was torn at the seams. When I reached home, Mama shouted, \"I am sick of you. When will you stop making me pay through my nose for such extravagant loses?\" Mother seized me by the arm, gave me a good jerk and seated me on the bed. She fetched an antiseptic lotion and applied it to my elbow and knees but did not hug me. Within me lingered yearning that she would who told me and console me! Even if she had given me just a sympathetic pat, my smarting limbs would have been soothed.\n     My younger brother was closed by as mama walks away to the kitchen he came and sat beside me he placed is little hand on my arm and whispered are you in a great pain I hug Rahim tight and started crying again.\n     I used to walk quite cautiously, yet I would often stumble. One day, the class monitress announced, \"We\'ll call her Slippery. Others sleep on wet ground Sayli manages to sleep even on dry land\". Soon a verse was composed in my owner. \n      \"Slippery! Slippery! \n     Slipping without muck!\n    Ask her the simplest question\n     No answer is your luck!\"\n   I live in perpetual fear of everyone the girls, the teachers, the young and the old. When I watch in class, I dared not move out and when I was outside, I was afraid of re- entering the class. Nor could mustard the courage to speak to anyone.','<b>A1) Simple factual questions</b>\nChoose the correct options\n   i) The quarrel was between the two, but the punishment inevitable        \n      descended on a third one that is............\n       a) Sayali\'s younger brother             b) Sayali\'s father             c) Sayali\n   ii) Sayli hugged her............... tight and started crying again.\n       a) mother                                           b) younger brother        c) sister\n   iii) ..........gave Sayli a new title.\n         a) The teacher                      b) some girls                      c) class monitors\n  iv) Sayli could not ................the courage to speak to anyone\n        a) muster                                b) perpetual                              c) musk','A2] What did Sayali long for, from her mother?\nA3] Find the synonyms of the following words from the passage \n       i) certainly      ii) extreme fear      iii) very expensive     iv) hurting\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\ni) I was coming down the school stairs. \n     (Change into past perfect continuous tense)\nii) Are you in great pain? (Identify the type of sentence)\n<b>A5] Open ended questions</b>\n     Does your mother shout at you? When?','','A1] i) c) Sayali           ii) b) younger brother          iii) c) The class moniterss           iv) a) muster\nA2] Sayali longed for affectionate words from her mother, when she reached home with torn new frock. She also hoped that her mother would console and hug her. In that condition, she wanted a \n        sympathetic pat. \nA3] i) inevitably          ii) frantic            iii) extravagant               iv) smarting \nA4] i) I had been coming down the school stairs \n      ii) Interrogative sentence \nA5] Yes, sometimes my mother shouts at me. Whenever I lose my belonging in school, she shouts at me.\n    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(7188,70,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities.\nA1] Complete the following : Effects of a frantic scream on Sayali\ni) ---------------------   ii) --------------------------\niii) ---------------          iv) -------------------------\n\n      One day, I was coming down the school stairs. Somebody from the top give a frantic scream. I fell down and came tumbling down about seven steps. My elbows and knees were badly bruised. My new frock was torn at the seams. When I reached home, Mama shouted, \"I am sick of you. When will you stop making me pay through my nose for such extravagant loses?\" Mother seized me by the arm, gave me a good jerk and seated me on the bed. She fetched an antiseptic lotion and applied it to my elbow and knees but did not hug me. Within me lingered yearning that she would who told me and console me! Even if she had given me just a sympathetic pat, my smarting limbs would have been soothed.\n     My younger brother was closed by as mama walks away to the kitchen he came and sat beside me he placed is little hand on my arm and whispered are you in a great pain I hug Rahim tight and started crying again.\n     I used to walk quite cautiously, yet I would often stumble. One day, the class monitress announced, \"We\'ll call her Slippery. Others sleep on wet ground Sayli manages to sleep even on dry land\". Soon a verse was composed in my owner. \n      \"Slippery! Slippery! \n     Slipping without muck!\n    Ask her the simplest question\n     No answer is your luck!\"\n   I live in perpetual fear of everyone the girls, the teachers, the young and the old. When I watch in class, I dared not move out and when I was outside, I was afraid of re- entering the class. Nor could mustard the courage to speak to anyone.\nA2) Complex factual question\n       What did Sayli long for from her mother?\nA3] Find adverbs of \n        a) invitable            ii) cautious \nA4] Grammar\n1) I was coming down the school stairs. (change into past continuous tense)\n2) Are you in great pain? (Identify the type of sentence)\n      (Underline the gerund form of the verb)\nA5] Open ended question\n      Does your mother shout at you? When?\n','','','A1] i) Sayali fell down           ii) She came tumbling down             iii) Her elbows and knees were badly bruised                 iv) Her new frock was torn   \nA2] Sayali longed for affectionate words from her mother, when she reached home with torn new frock. She also hoped that her mother would console and hug her. In that condition, she wanted a \n      sympathetic pat.\nA3] a) inevitably              b) cautiously \nA4] 1) I had been coming down the school stairs. \n       2) Interrogative sentence \nA5] Yes, sometimes my mother shouts at me. Whenever I lose belonging in school, she shouts at me.              ','',0,0,0,NULL),(7189,70,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The bell had already run when I reached the school. The teacher was in the classroom. I sought permission to enter. The teacher wore a frown of her face, but she let me enter to see her in the teacher\'s room, after the class. Her tone was not of annoyance or anger. Yet an unknown fear of made me nervous. Reassuring myself that she was a kind hearted teacher and would not punish me, I reached the teacher\'s room with hesitant steps. \n          The teacher greeted me with a smile and asked me to sit by her side. Her tender tone turned my sights into sobs. She pulled her chair close to mine and patting me on the head, said, \" What makes you cry, my child? I haven\'t said a word to you.\"  After a brief pause, she continued, \"Tell me what your trouble is.\" Confide in me, for am I not like a sister to you?\" Rare thrill through my entire frame. For a moment I feel I should cling to her and cry and cry. Of course, I did not do so but I keep giving at her with your full eyes. \n     Tenderly, teacher continued, \" Do you know the meaning of Didi? It means elder sister. Won\'t you tell your sister your trouble?\" Amid sobs I said, \" The girls keep teasing me.\" She laughed,\" Why are you scared of them? If you cry like this, they will pester you all the more. So, ignore them. Who is your friend?\" \n          I shook my head and teacher smiled. It seemed the teacher had a old her smile into my eyes. \"Like to be my friend? she said. \nThe bell rang. She rose. With a tender pet on my back, she said, \"listen You were a sister alright! Now you are a friend too. But I like little pal. Now Rise and hop off to your class.\"\n        Excitement made my face flush. Red - hot to the ear- tips. Never before had anyone lavished love on me thus and here was my teacher who had spontaneously accepted me as a sister, a friend!  I was beyond myself with joy. I wanted to chuckle to burst into near hysterical laughter. \n       On winged feet I went to my class. Sun I was lost in a rare reverie. I forgot that I was sitting in a class of wild girls whose Prime pastime watch teasing me.','<b>A1] Simple factual questions</b>\n       State whether the statements are true or false.\ni) The teacher did not allow sailyi to enter in the class.\nii) The teacher told Sayli that she was her friend and elder sister.\niii) Never before had anyone lavished love on Sayali.\niv) the girls\' prime first time was helping Sayali.','A2] Why did Sayali feel like rejoicing a lot?\nA3] Find describing words for the following nouns.\n         a) .............. teacher           b)........... tone               \n         c) ............eyes.                  d) .......... pal\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n      i) I haven\'t said a word to you. (Pick out auxiliary form)\n      ii) Rise and hop off to your class. ( Name the type of sentence)\n<b>A5] Open ended questions</b>\n        Have you ever come to late to school? What was your teacher’s reaction?\n','','A1] i) False            ii) True          iii) True             iv) False\nA2] Sayali felt like rejoicing a lot because her teacher accepted her as a sister and a close friend. Before that no one had lavished love on her. She felt that there was someone who understood her.\nA3] a) kind-hearted teacher           b) tender            c) tearful              d) little\nA4] i) haven\'t                   ii) Imperative sentences \nA5] Yes, I had come late to school last month. I was fifteen minutes late for the first period. At that time my class-teacher was teaching. When I sought permission to enter, he did not allow me. He was very angry. he asked me to stay outside for the whole period as a punishment.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(7190,70,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities.\nA1] Complete the sentences.\n  i) The teacher greeted me with a .........\n ii) A rare thrill ran through my .............\niii) I reached the Teacher\'s room with ..........\niv) I was beyond myself with.......\n       The bell had already run when I reached the school. The teacher was in the classroom. I sought permission to enter. The teacher wore a frown of her face, but she let me enter to see her in the teacher\'s room, after the class. Her tone was not of annoyance or anger. Yet an unknown fear of made me nervous. Reassuring myself that she was a kind hearted teacher and would not punish me, I reached the teacher\'s room with hesitant steps. \n          The teacher greeted me with a smile and asked me to sit by her side. Her tender tone turned my sights into sobs. She pulled her chair close to mine and patting me on the head, said, \" What makes you cry, my child? I haven\'t said a word to you.\"  After a brief pause, she continued, \"Tell me what your trouble is.\" Confide in me, for am I not like a sister to you?\" Rare thrill through my entire frame. For a moment I feel I should cling to her and cry and cry. Of course, I did not do so but I keep giving at her with your full eyes. \n     Tenderly, teacher continued, \" Do you know the meaning of Didi? It means elder sister. Won\'t you tell your sister your trouble?\" Amid sobs I said, \" The girls keep teasing me.\" She laughed,\" Why are you scared of them? If you cry like this, they will pester you all the more. So, ignore them. Who is your friend?\" \n          I shook my head and teacher smiled. It seemed the teacher had a old her smile into my eyes. \"Like to be my friend? she said. \nThe bell rang. She rose. With a tender pet on my back, she said, \"listen You were a sister alright! Now you are a friend too. But I like little pal. Now Rise and hop off to your class.\"\n        Excitement made my face flush. Red - hot to the ear- tips. Never before had anyone lavished love on me thus and here was my teacher who had spontaneously accepted me as a sister, a friend!  I was beyond myself with joy. I wanted to chuckle to burst into near hysterical laughter. \n       On winged feet I went to my class. Sun I was lost in a rare reverie. I forgot that I was sitting in a class of wild girls whose Prime pastime watch teasing me.\nA2] Complex factual question\n         What was Sayli told to do by teacher?\nA3] Find the words from the passage that mean.\n         i) great joy  ............         ii) share a secret ............  \n       iii) dreamy sleep ............ iv) uncontrolled ........\nA4] Grammar\n      1) Excitement made my face flush. (Identify the tense)\n     2) Teacher greeted me with a smile. (Add question tag)\nA5] Open ended question\n       Do you like to bunk (miss) class? Why?\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(7191,70,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','During the break, the water bottle that I was drinking from fell on me and I got fully drenched. Right in front stood Maya and Chanda, laughing away to their heart\'s content. Chanda teased, \" From the slippery hands of Slippery, the slippery bottle went slip, slip, slip!\" And now, let\' watch the fun,\" said Veena. \n     I was squeezing the water out of my wet frock. Remembering the teacher\'s words, I turned my back to them and ignored them completely. They were non plussed and went away. O sensed a feeling of victory. \n   Before the school closed for the day, the teacher came to the classroom again and said to me, \" From today, you are going to be responsible for bringing the register, chalk and duster every morning. Come, take this key, carry these notebooks to the teacher\'s room. Count them and keep them in my drawer. Ask the peon to show you, my drawer. \n    My favourite trembling legs were now filled with some miraculous strength. I rushed enthusiastically and completed the assigned job in a jiffy. When I placed the key on the teacher\'s table, she looked at me and smiled. I smiled back..\n     I was thrilled from top to toe. I felt my little heart inflate with pride. I found no awkwardness in moving about in my still- wet frock. Nor was there any trace of fear, while returning home.','<b>A1] Name the person</b>\ni) The person who was eager to watch the fun.\nii) The girl who teased Sayli as a slippery.\niii) The person who keeps school clean and tidy.\niv) The girl who was appointed as a class monitor.','A2] What responsibilities did the teacher give Sayali?\nA3] Find antonyms for the following words from the passage \n        i) dry        ii) forget       iii) defeat       iv) irresponsible\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n1) I sensed a feeling of victory. (Add question tag)\n2) I was squeezing the water out of my wet. (Identify the tense)\n<b>A5] Personal response question</b>\n        Were you a class monitor? How did you feel?','','A1] i) Veena         ii) Chanda           iii) Peon            iv) Sayali\nA2] The teacher appointed Sayali as the class monitor. She was made responsible for bringing the register, chalk and carry the notebooks to the teachers\' room.\nA3] i) drenched, wet           ii) remember        iii) victory                iv) responsible\nA4] 1) I sensed a feeling of victory, didn\'t I?\n       2) Continuous tense (Past)\nA5] Yes, I was a class monitor when I was in fifth standard. i felt very happy. I also felt like I had got some suoerpower and supreme authority. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(7192,70,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities.\nA1] State whether the statements are true or false.\ni) Tiffin fell down on narrator\'s feet.\nii) Narrator was squeezing water from her wet frock.\niii) Narrator\'s legs were filled with some miraculous strength.\niv) She became nervous at the end of the day.\n\n     During the break, the water bottle that I was drinking from fell on me and I got fully drenched. Right in front stood Maya and Chanda, laughing away to their heart\'s content. Chanda teased, \" From the slippery hands of Slippery, the slippery bottle went slip, slip, slip!\" And now, let\' watch the fun,\" said Veena. \n     I was squeezing the water out of my wet frock. Remembering the teacher\'s words, I turned my back to them and ignored them completely. They were non plussed and went away. O sensed a feeling of victory. \n   Before the school closed for the day, the teacher came to the classroom again and said to me, \" From today, you are going to be responsible for bringing the register, chalk and duster every morning. Come, take this key, carry these notebooks to the teacher\'s room. Count them and keep them in my drawer. Ask the peon to show you, my drawer. \n    My favourite trembling legs were now filled with some miraculous strength. I rushed enthusiastically and completed the assigned job in a jiffy. When I placed the key on the teacher\'s table, she looked at me and smiled. I smiled back..\n     I was thrilled from top to toe. I felt my little heart inflate with pride. I found no awkwardness in moving about in my still- wet frock. Nor was there any trace of fear, while returning home.\nA2] What responsibilities did the teacher give Sayli?\nA3] Find out and write words end with \'ly\'\nA4] Grammar\n1) I was squeezing water from my frock.  (Underline the helping verb)\n2) The teacher came to the classroom again. \n      (Convert it into continuous present tense)\nA5] Open ended question\n       What are the different tasks of a class monitor?\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(7235,76,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve: ','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(7236,76,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fill in the blanks:  x² - 10x + 24 = (----) (-------)','','','x² - 10x + 24 = x² - 6x – 4x + 24 = x(x – 6) – 4(x – 6)\n              = (x – 6)(x – 4)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7238,76,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fill in the blanks : 6x² - ------- - 10 = (2x – 5) (3x + 2)','','','6x² - 18x - 10 = (2x – 5) (3x + 2)','',0,0,0,NULL),(7239,76,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : 64xy + 8xy²','','','64xy + 8xy² = 8xy (8 + y)','',0,0,0,NULL),(7240,76,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : p² - 9q²','','','p² - 9q² = p² - (3q)² = (p – 3q)(p + 3q)','',0,0,0,NULL),(7241,76,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : m² - 1/4m²','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(7242,76,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : 9a²bc + 12abc','','','9a²bc + 12abc = 3abc (3a + 4)','',0,0,0,NULL),(7243,76,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise: 4a² - 25b²','','','4a² - 25b² = (2a)² - (5b)² = (2a – 5b)(2a + 5b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(7244,76,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : x² + 9x + 18','','','x² + 6x + 3x + 18     =  x(x + 6) + 3(x + 6)\n= (x + 6)(x + 3)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7245,76,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : x² - 10x + 9 ','','','x² - 9x – 1x + 9 \n= x (x – 9) – 1(x – 9) = (x – 9)(x – 1)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7246,76,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : y² + 24y + 144','','','y² + 12y + 12y + 144\n= y(y + 12) + 12(y + 12) = (y + 12)(y + 12)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7247,76,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : p² - 7p – 44 ','','','p² - 11p + 4p – 44 \n= p(p – 11) + 4(p – 11)  = (p – 11)(p + 4)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7248,76,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : p² - 2p – 35 ','','','p² - 7p + 5p – 35 \n= p(p – 7) + 5(p – 7)  = (p – 7)(p + 5)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7249,76,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : m² - 23m + 120','','','m² - 15m – 8m + 120\n= m(m – 15) – 8(m – 15) = (m – 15)(m – 8)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7250,76,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : m² - 25m + 100','','','m² - 20m – 5m + 100\n= m(m – 20) – 5(m – 20) = (m – 20)(m – 5)','',0,0,0,NULL),(7260,76,'descriptive','3',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : 5y² + 5y – 10 ','','','5y² + 5y – 10 = 5[y² + y – 2] = 5[y² + 2y – y – 2]\n= 5[y(y + 2) – 1(y + 2)] = 5[(y + 2)(y – 1)]\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7261,76,'descriptive','3',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : 3x² + 14x + 15','','','3x² + 14x + 15 = 3x² + 9x + 5x + 15 \n= 3x(x + 3) + 5(x + 3) = (x + 3)(3x + 5)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7262,76,'descriptive','3',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : 2x² + x – 45 ','','','2x² + x – 45 = 2x² + 10x – 9x – 45\n= 2x(x + 5) – 9 (x + 5) = (x + 5)(2x – 9)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7263,76,'descriptive','3',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : 20x² - 26x + 8','','','20x² - 26x + 8 = 20x² - 16x – 10x + 8\n= 4x(5x – 4) – 2(5x – 4) = (5x – 4)(4x – 2)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7264,76,'descriptive','3',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factorise : 44x² - x – 3 ','','','44x² - x – 3 = 44x² - 12x + 11x – 3 \n= 4x(11x – 3) + 1(11x – 3) = (11x – 3)(4x + 1)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7277,76,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify:','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(7278,76,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify:','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(7279,76,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify:','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(7280,76,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify:','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(7281,76,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify:','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(7282,76,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify:','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(7283,76,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify:','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(7284,76,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify:','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(7323,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','x varies inversely as y. When x = 15, y = 10. If x = 20, then y =?','','','x α 1/y ----- [given]\n∴ x = k/y   ⟹ k = x × y = 15 × 10 = 150\n∴ If x = 20 and k = 150 then y = k/x = 150/20 = 15/2 = 7.5\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(7325,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','n is directly proportional to m. Complete the following table','','','n α m ----- [given]\n∴ n = km     ⟹ k = n/m = 21/6 = 7/2 = 3.5\ni) m = 10 then n = km = 3.5 × 10 = 35\nii) If n = 14 then m = n/k = 14/3.5 = 4\niii) If m = 8 then n = km = 8 × 3.5 = 28','',1,0,0,NULL),(7326,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','s α t and s = 50 then t = 2. Find the value of s when t = 5.','','','s α t ----- [Given]\n∴ s = kt     ⟹ k = s/t = 50/2 = 25\nIf t = 5 and k = 25 then s = kt = 25 × 5 = 125','',0,0,0,NULL),(7328,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','f α 1/d² and d = 5 when f = 16. Find the value of f when d = 10','','','f α 1/d² ------ [given]\n∴ f × d² = k     ⟹ k = 16 × 5² = 16 × 25 = 400\nIf d = 10 and k = 400 then f = k/d² = 400/100 = 4','',0,0,0,NULL),(7333,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If n varies directly as m, complete the following table:','','','n α m ----- [given]\n∴ n = km     ⟹ k = n/m = 12/3 = 4\ni) If m = 6.5 and k = 4 then n = 6.5 × 4 = 26\nii) If n = 28 and k = 4 then m = n/k = 28/4 = 7\niii) If m = 1.25 and k = 4 then n = 1.25 × 4 = 5','',1,0,0,NULL),(7336,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x α 1/√y and when x = 40, y = 16. If x = 10, find y.','','','x α 1/√y      ∴ x × √y = k      ⟹ k = 40 × √16 = 40 × 4 = 160\nIf x = 10 and k = 160 then √y = k/x = 160/10 = 16\n∴ y = 256','',0,0,0,NULL),(7381,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','□ABCD is a parallelogram if ∠A = 50⁰ then find ∠C.','','','∠A = ∠C = 50⁰ ---- [Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal]','',0,0,0,NULL),(7382,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','□ABCD is a parallelogram if ∠C = 75⁰ then find ∠B.','','','□ABCD is a parallelogram.\n∴ ∠B + ∠C = 180⁰ ----- [Adjacent angles of a parallelogram are supplementary]\n ∴ ∠B = 180 – 75 = 105⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(7383,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the quadrilaterals whose : diagonals bisect each other','','','Rectangle and parallelogram.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7385,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the quadrilaterals whose : Diagonals bisect the opposite angles','','','Diagonals of rhombus and square bisect the opposite angles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7386,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the perimeter of square of side 10 cm. Justify your answer.','','','Perimeter of square = 4 × side = 4 × 10 = 40 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(7388,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Adjacent angles of parallelogram are in the ratio 4 : 5 then find the angles.','','','Adjacent angles of a parallelogram are supplementary \n∴ 4x + 5x = 180⁰      ⟹ 9x = 180     ⟹  x = 20\n∴ Angles are 4x = 4 × 20 = 80⁰ and 5x = 5 × 20 = 100⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(7391,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','□ABCD is a rhombus. Diagonals AC and BD intersects at O. Then ∠AOB.','','','Diagonal of rhombus perpendicular bisect each other.\n∴ ∠AOB = 90⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(7396,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a rectangle ABCD such that, l(AB) = 6 cm, l(BC) = 4.5 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7397,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a square WXYZ with side 5.2 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7398,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the length of diagonal of a square with side 8 cm','','','Diagonal of square = √2 × side\n∴ diagonal of square = 8√2 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(7399,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State diagonals property of rectangle and rhombus.','','','a) i) Diagonals of rhombus bisect each other and they are perpendicular to each other. \nii) Diagonals of rhombus bisect opposite angles.\nb) i) Diagonals of a rectangle are congruent.\nii) Diagonals of rectangle bisect each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7400,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct square of side 4 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7406,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a rhombus KLMN such that its side is 4 cm and ∠K = 75⁰.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7415,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a rhombus KLMN such that its side is 5 cm and ∠K = 60⁰.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7416,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a rectangle PQRS such that, l(PQ) = 5.3 cm and l(QR) = 6.8 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(7450,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If MP = Rs. 1400, SP = Rs. 1120, then percentage of discount = ','','','Discount = MP – SP = 1400 – 1120 = 280\n% of discount = (discount/MP) × 100 = (280/1400) × 100 = 20%','',0,0,0,NULL),(7452,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If Marked price is Rs.2570, Discount is Rs. 280, then SP = Rs. -----','','','SP = MP – Discount = 2570 – 280 = Rs. 2290','',0,0,0,NULL),(7453,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After giving discount of 20%, a chair was sold at Rs. 1120, then its MP?','','','Let Its MP be Rs. x\n∴ Discount = 20% of x\n∴ SP = MP – Discount \n∴ 1120 = x – 20x/100 = 80x/100   \n∴ x = (1120 × 100)/80 = 1400\n∴ MP = Rs. 1400','',0,0,0,NULL),(7455,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If MP = RS. 1200, discount = 12%, then find amount of discount?','','','Amount of discount = (12/100)× 1200 = Rs. 144','',0,0,0,NULL),(7456,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','4% of ----- = 12','','','4% of x = 12\n∴ x = (12 × 100)/4  = 300','',0,0,0,NULL),(7457,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the discount at 15% on MP is Rs. 500?','','','Amount of discount = (15/100) × 500 = Rs. 75','',0,0,0,NULL),(7460,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the values of : 2% of 3000','','','(2/100) × 3000 = 60','',0,0,0,NULL),(7461,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the values of : 60% of 1610.','','','(60/100) × 1610 = 966','',0,0,0,NULL),(7462,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the values of 12.5% of 400.','','','(12.5/100) × 400 = 50','',0,0,0,NULL),(7463,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the values of 6.5% of 11400.','','','(6.5/100) × 11400 = 741','',0,0,0,NULL),(7472,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If MP = Rs. 1700, SP = Rs. 1540, then find discount.','','','Discount = MP – SP = Rs. 160','',0,0,0,NULL),(7474,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the selling price = Rs. 900, discount 20%, then find the marked price.','','','Let MP be Rs. x\n∴ Discount = 20x/100\nSP = MP – Discount           ⟹  900 = x – 20x/100 = 80x/100\n   ∴ x = (900 × 100)/80 = Rs. 1125\n∴ MP = Rs. 1125','',0,0,0,NULL),(7978,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table:','','','a) Density = mass / Volume = 350/175 = 2 kg/m³\nb) Density = Mass/Volume \n4 = m/190        ∴ m = 190 × 4 = 760 kg\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(7979,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table:','','','a) Given : Area = 0.04 m², Pressure = 20000 N/m²\nPressure = Weight/Area \n20000 = Wt/0.04      Weight = 800 N\nb) Pressure = Weight/Area = 1500/500 = 3 N/m²\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(7980,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table:','','','a) Relative density = Density of substance/ Density of water\n5 = Density of metal / 10³ \n∴ Density of metal = 5 × 10³ kg/m³\nb) Relative density = Density of substance/Density of water  = (8.5 × 10³) / 10³ = 8.5\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(8376,33,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The decimal form of the number 19/15  is -----','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(8377,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following decimal fractions as terminating and non terminating recurring decimals: \n','','','i) The given decimal fraction is non-terminating and recurring\nii) The given decimal fraction is non-terminating and recurring.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(8378,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following decimal fractions as terminating and non terminating recurring decimals:\n','','','i) The given decimal fraction is non-terminating and recurring.\nii) The given decimal fraction is terminating.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(8379,33,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following decimal fractions as terminating and non terminating recurring decimals:\n','','','i) The given decimal fraction is non-terminating and recurring.\nii) The given decimal fraction is non-terminating and non-recurring.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(8380,35,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the pairs','','','1 – ii, 2 – iii, 3 – iv, 4 – i','',1,0,0,NULL),(8381,35,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns (A) and (B)','','','1 – c, 2 – d, 3 – a, 4 – b','',1,0,0,NULL),(8382,36,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If','','','b) 0.012','',1,0,0,NULL),(8383,36,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find','','','d) 3/4','',1,0,0,NULL),(8385,36,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find','','','c) 1','',1