 	            	c) 8 		           d) 20','','','a) 18 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12839,77,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a certain work is done in 10 days by 4 workers then 2 workers can complete the work in how many days? \na) 20 	          b) 10 	         	c) 5 		       d) 40','','','a) 20 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12840,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following statements using symbol of variation : Circumference (c) of a circle is directly proportional to its radius (r).','','','c α r','',0,0,0,NULL),(12841,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following statements using symbol of variation : Consumption of petrol (l) in a car and distance travelled by that car (d) are in direct variation. ','','','l α d','',0,0,0,NULL),(12842,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following statements using symbol of variation : The wavelength of sound (l) and its frequency (f) are in inverse variation','','','l α 1/f','',0,0,0,NULL),(12843,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the following statements using symbol of variation : The intensity (I) of light varies inversely with the square of the distance (d) of a screen from the lamp.','','','I α 1/d²','',0,0,0,NULL),(12844,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the following statements is inversely variation or not : Number of workers on a job and time taken by them to complete the job.','','','Since if number of workers increases then time taken to complete the job decreases it is in inverse variation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12845,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the following statements is inversely variation or not : Number of pipes of same size to fill a tank and the time taken by them to fill the tank.','','','If number of pipes are increase, time taken to fill the tank decrease. It is inverse variation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12846,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the following statements is inversely variation or not : Petrol filled in the tank of a vehicle and its cost','','','As petrol filled in tank increases, it cost also increases. Hence it is not in inverse variation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12847,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the following statements is inversely variation or not : Area of circle and its radius.','','','As area of circle increases, its radius also increases, hence it is not inverse variation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12848,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the given statements using symbol of variation : The area (A) of the circle is directly proportional to square of its radius (r)','','','A α r²','',0,0,0,NULL),(12849,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the given statements using symbol of variation : The mass (m) of the iron block has direct variation with its volume (v)','','','m α v','',0,0,0,NULL),(12850,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the given statements using symbol of variation. : The cost (c) of diamond is directly proportional to square of its weight (w) ','','','c α w²','',0,0,0,NULL),(12851,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the given statements using symbol of variation : The force (f) of attraction between two unlike magnetic poles varies inversely as the square of distance (d) between them','','','f α 1/d²','',0,0,0,NULL),(12852,77,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the given statements using symbol of variation : The volume (v) of a gas inversely proportional to pressure (p) on it.','','','v α 1/p','',0,0,0,NULL),(12853,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','y varies directly as the square root of x. When x = 16, y = 24. Find the constant of variation and the equation of variation.','','','y α √x ----- [Given]\n∴ y = k√x     ⟹ y = k × √16      \n∴ 24 = 4k\n∴ k = 24/4 = 6','',0,0,0,NULL),(12854,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the constant of variation and write the equation of variation : p α 1/q. If p = 15 then q = 4','','','p α 1/q ----- [given]\n∴ p = k/q   ⟹ k = p × q = 15 × 4 = 60','',0,0,0,NULL),(12855,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the constant of variation and write the equation of variation : z α 1/w. If p = 2.5 then w = 24','','','z α 1/w ----- [given]\n∴ z = k/w   ⟹ k = z × w = 2.5 × 24 = 60','',0,0,0,NULL),(12856,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the constant of variation and write the equation of variation : s α 1/t². If s = 4 then t = 5','','','s α 1/t² ----- [given]\n∴ s = k/t²   ⟹ k = s × t² = 4 × 5² = 4 × 25 = 100','',0,0,0,NULL),(12857,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the constant of variation and write the equation of variation : x α 1/√y. If x = 15 then y = 9','','','x α 1/√y ----- [given]\n∴ x = k/√y   ⟹ k = x × √y = 15 × √9 = 15 × 3 = 45','',0,0,0,NULL),(12858,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If y is in direct variation with x and x = 18 when y = 15 then find the value of y when x = 4.5.','','','y α x ---- [given]\n∴ y = kx     ⟹   k = y/x = 15/18 = 5/6 \n∴ If x = 4.5 and k = 5/6 then y = kx = 5/6  × 4.5 = 3.75','',0,0,0,NULL),(12859,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','y varies inversely as x and y = 12 when x = 5. Find the value of y when x = 10','','','y α 1/x ----- [given]\n∴ xy = k       ⟹ k = 12 × 5 = 60\nIf x = 10 and k = 60 then y = k/x = 60/10 = 6','',0,0,0,NULL),(12860,77,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 15 workers can build a wall in 48 hours, how many workers will be required to do the same work in 30 hours?','','','Let x = no. of workers and y = time taken to complete work\n∴ x α 1/y          ∴  xy = k ------ (i)\nIf x = 15 and y = 48 then k = 15 × 48 = 720\nIf y = 30 and k = 720 then x = 720/30 = 24\n∴ 24 workers are needed to complete the job in 30 hours.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12861,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table considering that the cost of apples and their numbers are in direct variation.','','','x α y ----- [given]\n∴ x = ky      ⟹ k = x/y = 1/8\ni) If y = 56 and k = 1/8 then x = ky = 56/8 = 7\nii) If x = 12 and k = 1/8 then y = 12 × 8 = 96\niii) If y = 160 and k = 1/8 then x = 160/8 = 20','',1,0,0,NULL),(12862,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The total remuneration paid to laborers, employed to harvest soyabean, is in direct variation with the number of laborers. If the remuneration of 4 laborer’s is Rs. 1000, find the remuneration of 17 laborers.  ','','','Let, total remuneration paid = x and no. of labors = y\n∴ x α y       ∴ x = ky     ⟹ k = x/y = 4/1000 = 1/250\nIf x = 17 and k = 1/250 then y = k/x = 250 × 17 = 4250','',0,0,0,NULL),(12863,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The information about the numbers of workers and the number of days to complete a work is given in the following table. Complete the table.','','','Let  x = no. of workers and y = no. of days\n∴ x α 1/y      ∴ xy = k       ⟹ k = 30 × 6 = 180\ni) If y = 12 and k = 180 then x = k/y = 180/12 = 15\nii) If x = 10 and k = 180 then y = k/x = 180/10 = 18\niii) If y = 36 and k = 180 then x = k/y = 180/36 = 5','',1,0,0,NULL),(12864,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If m α n, n = 15 when m = 25. Hence      i) Find m when n = 87 	ii) Find n when m = 155','','','m α n     ∴ m = kn      ⟹ k = m/n = 25/15 = 5/3 \ni) If n = 87 and k = 5/3 then m = 5/3   × 87 = 5 × 29 = 145\nii) If m = 155 and k = 5/3 then n = m/k = 155  × 3/5 = 31 × 3 = 93','',0,0,0,NULL),(12865,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','y varies inversely with x. If y = 30 when x = 12,  Find i) y when x = 15          	ii) x when y = 18','','','y α 1/x       ∴ y = k/x    ⟹ k = xy = 30 × 12 = 360\ni) If x = 15 and k = 360 then y = k/x = 360/15 = 24\nii) If y = 18 and k = 360 then x = k/y = 360/18 = 20','',0,0,0,NULL),(12866,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The circumference of a circle varies directly as its diameter. The circumference of a circle is 33 cm when diameter is 10.5 cm. Find the diameter of circle whose circumference is 88 cm.','','','c α d      ∴ c = kd      ⟹ k = c/d = 33/10.5 = 3.14\nIf c = 88 and k = 3.14 then d = c/k = 28','',0,0,0,NULL),(12867,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The cost of sugar is directly proportional to its weight. If the cost of 12 kg sugar is Rs. 360 then find the cost of 5 kg of sugar.','','','x = cost of sugar and y = weight of sugar\n∴ x α y     ∴ x = ky       ⟹ k = x/y  = 360/12 = 30\nIf y = 5 and k = 30 then x = ky = 30 × 5 = 150 \n∴ cost of 5 kg sugar is Rs. 150','',0,0,0,NULL),(12868,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','y varies inversely as the square root of x and y = 5 when x = 36. Find the value of x when y = 10.','','','y α 1/√x       ∴ y × √x = k     ⟹ 5 × √36 = 5 × 6 = 30\nIf y = 10 and k = 30 then √x = k/y = 30/10 = 3\n∴ x = 9','',0,0,0,NULL),(12869,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A train is moving at a uniform speed of 75 km/hr. Find the time required to cover distance of 250 km.','','','Speed = distance /Time\n∴ Time = Distance/Speed = 250/75 = 3.33 hours = 3 hours and 20 minutes','',0,0,0,NULL),(12870,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','6 pipes are required to fill a tank in 1 hour 20 minutes. How long will it take if only 5 pipes of the same type are used?','','','Let x = no. of pipes and y = time taken\n1 hours 20 min = 80 minutes\n∴ x α 1/y       ∴ xy = k    ⟹ k = 6 × 80 = 480\nIf x = 5 and k = 480 then y = k/x = 480/5 = 96 minutes\n∴ 5 pipes time taken = 1 hours and 36 minutes','',0,0,0,NULL),(12871,77,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','15 women finish the work of harvesting a crop in 8 days. Find the number of women to be employed if the same job is to be completed in 6 days.','','','Let the number of days be x and number of women be y\n∴ x α 1/y        ∴ xy = k     ⟹ k = 15 × 8 = 120\nIf x = 6 and k = 120 then y = k/x = 120/6 = 20\n∴ 20 women should be employed to finish the work in 6 days.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12872,77,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The boxes are to be filled with apples. If 24 apples are put in a box, then 27 boxes are needed. If 36 apples are filled in a box, how many boxes will be needed?','','','Let no. of apples per box = x and no. of boxes needed = y\n∴ x α 1/y     ∴ xy = k    ⟹ k = 24 × 27 = 648\nIf x = 36 and k = 648 then y = k/x = 648/36 = 18\n∴ If 36 apples are filled in a box then 18 boxes will be needed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12873,77,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','120 bags of half liter milk can be filled by a machine within 3 minutes. Find the time to fill such 1800 bags.\n','','','Let x = no. of bags and y = time taken to fill the bags \n∴ x α y       ∴ x = ky       ⟹ k = x/y = 120/3 = 40\nIf x = 1800 and k = 40 then y = x/k = 1800/40 = 45\n∴ 45 minutes are required to fill 1800 milk bags.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12874,77,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A car with speed 60 km/hr take 8 hours to travel some distance. What should be the increase in the speed, if the same distance is to be covered in 7 1/2 hours?','','','Let speed of car = x and time taken = y\n∴ x α 1/y     ∴ xy = k     ⟹ k = 60 × 8 = 480\nIf y = 7.5 and k = 480 then x = k/y = 480/7.5 = 64 km/hrs\n∴ Increase in speed = 64 – 60 = 4 km/hrs\n∴ The increase in speed is 4 km/hr','',0,0,0,NULL),(12875,77,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the variation and solve. It takes 5 hours to travel from one town to the other if speed of the bus is 48 km/hr. If the speed of bus is reduced by 8 km/hr, how much time will take for the same travel? ','','','Let x = time taken and y = speed of bus\n∴ x α 1/y      ∴ xy = k     ⟹ k = 48 × 5 = 240\nIf speed is reduced by 8 km/hr then new speed = 48 – 8 = 40 km/hr\n∴ y = 40 and k = 240 then x = k/y = 240/40 = 6\n∴ Time taken to travel the same distance at the speed 40 km/hr is 6 hours.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12876,77,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The number of sheets of paper and their weights are directly proportional to each other. If the weight of 12 sheets of paper is 40 grams, how many sheets of the same paper would weight 2.5 kilograms?','','','Let x = No. of sheet and y = weight of sheet\n∴ x α y       ∴ x = ky      ⟹ k = x/y = 12/40 = 3/10 = 0.3\nIf y = 2.5 kg = 2500 gm and k = 0.3 then x = x = 0.3 × 2500 = 750 \n∴ 750 sheets of same paper would weight 2.5 kg.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12877,77,'descriptive','4',4,3,'Out of Text Book','','Monthly expenditure of a person is directly proportion to his monthly income. When the expenditure is Rs. 6300 his income is Rs. 9000. Find the monthly expenditure when his income is Rs. 15000.','','','Let x = Monthly expenditure and y = monthly income\n∴ x α y      ∴ x = ky      ⟹  k = x/y  = 6300/9000 = 0.7\nIf y = 15000 and k = 0.7 then x = ky = 0.7 × 15000 = 10500\n∴ The monthly expenditure is Rs. 10500','',0,0,0,NULL),(12878,77,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The number of days required to read a book is inversely proportional to the number of pages read daily. When 15 pages are read daily, the book can be finished in 32 days, find the number of days required to finish the reading of the book when 30 pages are read daily.','','','Let x = No. of days and y = no. of pages\n∴ x α 1/y     ∴ xy = k     ⟹ k = 15 × 32 = 480\nIf y = 30 and k = 480 then x = k/y = 480/30 = 16\n∴ 16 days are required to finish the 30 pages read daily.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12879,77,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','There are 100 students in a hostel. Food provision for them is for 20 days. How long will these provisions last, if 25 more students join the group?','','','Let x = No. of students and y = no. of days\n∴ x α 1/y     ∴  xy = k    ⟹ k = 100 × 20 = 2000\nIf 25 students are more then no. of students = 100 + 25 = 125\n∴ If x = 125 and k = 2000 then y = k/x = 2000/125 = 16\n∴ 16 days are food provision id 25 students are added.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12880,77,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A factory requires 42 machines to produce a given number of articles in 63 days. How many machines would be required to produces the same number of articles in 54 days?','','','Let x = No. of machine and y = no. of days\n∴ x α 1/y      ∴ xy = k      ⟹ k = xy = 42 × 63 = 2646\nIf y = 54 and k = 2646 then x = k/y = 2646/54 = 49\n∴ 49 machines are required to produce same articles in 54 days.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12882,77,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A loaded truck travels 14 km in 25 minutes. If the speed remains the same, how far can it travel in 5 hours?','','','Let x = distance and y = time taken\n∴ x α y       ∴  x = ky      ⟹ k = x/y = 14/25 = 0.56\nIf y = 5 hours = 5 × 60 = 300 minutes and k = 0.56 then x = 0.56 × 300 = 168\n∴ 168 km travel in 5 hours.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12884,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Diagonals bisects each other and they are perpendicular to each other in a \na) rectangle 	      b) parallelogram           	c) trapezium            	d) rhombus ','','','d) rhombus','',0,0,0,NULL),(12885,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','□ABCD is a square. What is ∠DCA?\na) 45⁰		b) 60⁰		c) 30⁰		d) 90⁰','','','d) 90⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(12886,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a rhombus ABCD if ∠ACB = 40⁰ then ∠ADB = --------\na) 70⁰		b) 45⁰		c) 50⁰		d) 60⁰','','','c) 50⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(12887,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Diagonals necessarily bisect opposite angles in a -----\na) rectangle 	        b) parallelogram             	c) trapezium            	d) square','','','d) square','',0,0,0,NULL),(12888,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If adjacent sides of rectangle are 5 cm and 12 cm then its diagonals = \na) 13 cm            	b) 6.5 cm 		c) 26 cm 	               	d) 17 cm','','','a) 13 cm  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12889,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Side of a square is 4 cm. Length of its diagonal is -----\na) 4 cm 		     b) 8 cm        		c) 4√2 cm		d) 8√2 cm','','','c) 4√2 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(12890,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','□PQRS is a rhombus with ∠PQR = 56⁰ and ∠PRS = ---------\na)  28⁰		b) 56⁰		c) 62⁰		d) 124⁰','','','c) 62⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(12891,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Measures of opposite angles of a parallelogram are (60 – x)⁰ and (3x – 4)⁰ then value of x = -----\na) 16 		b) 32 			c) 28 			d) 17','','','a) 16 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12892,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Diagonals of quadrilateral bisect each other. If ∠A = 45⁰ and ∠B = --------\na) 115⁰	        	b) 120⁰	         	c) 125⁰            		d) 135⁰','','','b) 120⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(12893,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a parallelogram ABCD, if adjacent sides are 3 cm and 5 cm then what will be its perimeter?\na) 13 cm 	        	b) 26 cm 	         	c) 3 cm 	           	d) 52 cm','','','b) 26 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(12894,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Diagonals of a ------ are congruent\na) rhombus 		b) parallelogram 	          c) trapezium 	           d) rectangle','','','d) rectangle','',0,0,0,NULL),(12895,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If all sides and all angles of a quadrilateral are congruent, it is called a ---\na) rhombus 		b) square 	      c) rectangle 		d) parallelogram','','','b) square ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12896,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','□ABCD is a parallelogram. If ∠A = 75⁰ and ∠D = -------\na) 75⁰		    b) 95⁰	          	c) 105⁰	        	d) 85⁰','','','c) 105⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(12897,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Diagonals of a rectangle ------- each other\na) perpendicular to             b) perpendicular bisector of          c) bisect          	d) none','','','c) bisect    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12898,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of a diagonal of a square of side 6 cm is ------ cm\na) √12		  b) 6 		c) √72	           	d) √6','','','c) √72	','',0,0,0,NULL),(12899,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diagonals of rhombus ---- is opposite angles\na) bisects 	       b) trisects          	c) divides in 4 equals parts           	d) none ','','','a) bisect','',0,0,0,NULL),(12900,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If □BEST is a parallelogram and ∠B = 100⁰ find ∠S = --------\na) 70⁰		b) 100⁰	        	c) 80⁰		d) 180⁰','','','b) 100⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(12901,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In rhombus HELP, ∠H = 115⁰ then ∠E\na) 75⁰	        	b) 65⁰		c) 55⁰		d) 155⁰','','','b) 65⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(12902,78,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following properties is incorrect :  i) Diagonals of parallelogram bisect each other               ii) Diagonals of rectangle are congruent \n iii) Diagonals of rhombus are perpendicular to each other           iv) Diagonals of kite are perpendicular bisectors to each other\na) iii 		 b) ii 		c) i 	        	d) iv','','','d) iv','',0,0,0,NULL),(12903,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If diagonal of rectangle is 37 cm and one side is 12 cm, then find the other side of a rectangle.','','','(diagonal)² = (length)² + (breadth)²\n∴ 37² = 12² + (length)²          ⟹ 1369 – 144 = (length)²\n∴ (length)² = 1225    ⟹ length =  35 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(12904,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In □ABCD, ∠A + ∠D = 180⁰ but ∠A + ∠B ≠ 180⁰. Then □ABCD is what type of quadrilateral?','','','□ABCD is a trapezium.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12905,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If adjacent sides of a parallelogram are of 8 cm and 10 cm then find its perimeter.','','','Perimeter of parallelogram = 2[l + b] = 2[8 + 10] = 2 × 18 = 36 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(12906,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, □ABCD is a rhombus. l(BM) = 3 and l(MC) = 4 then perimeter of □ABCD? ','','','Diagonal of rhombus are perpendicular bisect each other.\n  ∴ In ΔBMC, ∠M = 90⁰\nBC² = BM² + CM² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25\n∴ BC = 5 cm\nPerimeter of rhombus = 4 × side = 4 × 5 = 20 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(12907,78,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the quadrilaterals whose : Diagonals are perpendicular bisectors of each other','','','Rhombus and square','',0,0,0,NULL),(12908,78,'descriptive','2',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The measures of adjacent angles of a parallelogram are (5x – 7)⁰ and (4x + 25)⁰ then find the measures of these angles?\n','','','(5x – 7) + (4x + 25) = 180⁰ ----- [adjacent angles of a parallelogram are supplementary]\n∴ 9x = 180 – 18 = 162     ⟹  x = 18\n∴ 5x – 7 = 5(18) – 7 = 90 – 7 = 83⁰\n4x + 25 = 4(18) + 25 = 97⁰\n∴ Angles of a parallelogram are 83⁰, 97⁰, 83⁰, 97⁰ respectively.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12910,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Measure of one angle of a rhombus is 100⁰ find the measure of remaining three angles.','','','Let □ABCD is a rhombus\n∴ ∠A = ∠C = 100⁰ ---- [Opposite angles of rhombus are equal]\nAnd ∠B = ∠D = x⁰\n∴ ∠A + ∠B + ∠C  + ∠D = 360⁰\n∴ x + x = 360 – 200 = 160     ⟹  x = 80⁰\n∴ ∠B = ∠D = 80⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(12911,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If adjacent sides of a rectangle are equal of length 12cm then identify the type of rectangle and hence find its perimeter.','','','If adjacent sides of rectangle are equal then it is formed a square.\n∴ Perimeter of square = 4 × side = 4 × 12 = 48 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(12912,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If diagonals of rectangle is 26 cm and one side is 24 cm, find the other side of rectangle.','','','(diagonal)² = (length)² + (breadth)²\n∴ 26² = 24² + (breadth)²          ⟹ 676 – 576 = (breadth)²\n∴ (breadth)² = 100    ⟹ breadth =  10 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(12913,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □PQRS such that, l(PQ) = 5.5 cm, l(QR) = 5 cm, l(PS) = 4.2 cm, l(QS) = 6.1 cm and l(RS) = 7 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12914,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □WXYZ such that, l(YZ) = 3.9 cm, l(ZX) = 6 cm, l(WX) = 4.5 cm,  l(ZW) = 5 cm and l(YW) = 6.4 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12915,78,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the adjacent sides of rectangle are 60 cm and 11 cm then find the length of its diagonals.','','','(diagonal)² = (length)² + (breadth)²\n∴ (diagonal)² = (60)² + (11)² = 3600 + 121 = 3721 \n∴ diagonal = 61 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(12916,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □ABCD such that, l(AB) = 4.4cm, l(BC) = 5.5cm, ∠A = 65⁰, ∠B = 100⁰ and ∠C = 110⁰.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12917,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □PQRS such that, l(QR) = 4.9 cm, l(RS) = 6.2 cm, l(SP) = 3.9 cm,  ∠R = 60⁰ and ∠S = 75⁰','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12918,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □MORE such that, l(MO) = 5.8 cm, l(OR) = 4.4 cm, ∠M = 58⁰, ∠O = 105⁰ and ∠R = 90⁰','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12919,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □DEFG such that, l(DE) = 4.5 cm, l(EF) = 6.5 cm, l(DG) = 5.5 cm, l(DF) = 7.2 cm and l(EG) = 7.8 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12920,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Lengths of diagonals of a rhombus ABCD are 16 cm and 12 cm. Find the side and perimeter of the rhombus.','','','Diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular bisect each other.\nLet AC and BD are the diagonals of a rhombus ABCD intersect at point O.\n∴ AO = 1/2 AB = 1/2 × 16 = 8  \n ∴ BO = 1/2 BD = 1/2 × 12 = 6\n∠AOB = 90⁰\n∴ AB² = AO² + BO² = 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100\n∴ AB = 10 cm\n∴ Perimeter of rhombus = 4 × side = 4 × 10 = 40 cm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12921,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Measure of one angle of a rhombus is 50⁰, find the measures of remaining three angles.','','','Let □ABCD is a rhombus\n∴ ∠A = ∠C = 50⁰ ---- [Opposite angles of rhombus are equal]\nAnd ∠B = ∠D = x⁰\n∴ ∠A + ∠B + ∠C  + ∠D = 360⁰\n∴ x + x = 360 – 100 = 260     ⟹  x = 130⁰\n∴ ∠B = ∠D = 130⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(12922,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Measures of opposite angles of a parallelogram are (3x – 2)⁰ and (50 – x)⁰. Find the measure of its each angle.','','','Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal\n∴ 3x – 2 = 50 – x       ⟹   3x + x = 50 + 2     ⟹  4x = 52  \n∴ x = 13\n∴ 3x – 2 = 3(13) – 2 = 39 – 2 = 37⁰\nAdjacent angles of a parallelogram are supplementary.\n∴ adjacent angles = 180 – 37 = 143⁰\n∴ Angles of a parallelogram are 37⁰, 143⁰, 37⁰, 143⁰ respectively.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12923,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct a parallelogram ABCD such that, l(BC) = 7 cm, ∠ABC = 40⁰, l(AB) = 3cm','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12924,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □BARC such that, l(BA) = l(BC) = 4.2 cm, l(AR) = l(CR) = 5.6 cm, l(AC) = 6 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12925,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □PQRS such that, l(PQ) = 5.8 cm, l(QR) = 5.7 cm, l(SP) = 4 cm, l(RS) = 7.1 cm, l(QS) = 6.3 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12926,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □MNOP such that, l(MN) = 4 cm, l(NO) = 5.5 cm, l(OP) = 5 cm, l(NP) = 6.3 cm, l(MO) = 5 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12927,78,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □ABCD such that, l(AB) = 5.5 cm, ∠A = 50⁰, ∠B = 120⁰ and ∠C = 90⁰.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12928,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □ABCD such that, l(AB) = 6.4 cm, l(BC) = 4.8 cm, ∠A = 70⁰, ∠B = 50⁰ and ∠C = 140⁰.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12929,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □LMNO such that, l(LM) = l(LO) = 6 cm, l(ON) = l(NM) = 4.5 cm, l(OM) = 7.5 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12930,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Referring the adjacent figure of a parallelogram, write the answers of questions given below :  i) If l(WZ) = 4.5 cm then l(XY) = ? 		ii) If l(YZ) = 8.2 cm then l(XW) = ?    \niii) If l(OX) = 2.5 cm then l(OZ) = ? 		iv) If l(WO) = 3.3 cm then l(WY) = ?       v) If ∠WZY = 120⁰ then ∠WXY =? And ∠XWZ =?','','','i) WZ = XY = 4.5 cm ---- [opposite sides of a parallelogram]\nii) YZ = XW = 8.2 cm ----- [Opposite sides of a parallelogram]\niii) OX = OZ = 2.5 cm ---- [diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other]\niv) WO = 3.3 cm then WY = 2WO = 2 × 3.3 = 6.6 cm\nv)  ∠WXY = ∠WZY = 120⁰ ---- [opposite angles of a parallelogram]\n∠XWZ = 180 - ∠WZY ------- [adjacent angles of a parallelogram]\n∠XWZ = 180 – 120 = 60⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(12931,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ratio of consecutive angles of quadrilateral is 1 : 2 : 3 : 4. Find the measure of its each angle. Write with reason, what type of a quadrilateral it is.','','','Let □ABCD, \n∴ x + 2x + 3x + 4x = 360 ------ [sum of angles of a quadrilateral]\n∴ 10x = 360      ⟹  x = 36\n∴ Angles are 1x = 36⁰, 2x = 2 × 36 = 72⁰, 3x = 3 × 36 = 108⁰, 4x = 4 × 36 = 144⁰\n∠A + ∠D = 36 + 144 = 180⁰  and ∠B + ∠D = 72 + 108 = 180⁰\n∴ side AB ‖ side CD and ∠A + ∠B = 36 + 72 = 118⁰\n∠A ≠ ∠B\n∴ AD is not parallel to BC.\n∴ only one pair of side is parallel\nSo, □ABCD is a trapezium.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12932,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □PQRS such that, l(PQ) = 3.5, l(QR) = 5.6 cm, l(RS) = 3.5 cm, ∠Q = 110⁰, ∠R = 70⁰. If it is given that □PQRS is a parallelogram, which of the given information is unnecessary? ','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12933,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ratio of the measures of angles of a quadrilateral in order is 7 : 9 : 3 : 5 then find the measures of its angles and write the type of quadrilateral.','','','Let □ABCD, \n∴ 7x + 9x + 3x + 5x = 360 ------ [sum of angles of a quadrilateral]\n∴ 24x = 360      ⟹  x = 15\n∴ Angles are 7x = 105⁰, 9x = 9 × 15 = 135⁰, 3x = 3 × 15 = 45⁰, 5x = 5 × 15 = 75⁰\n∠A + ∠D = 105 + 75 = 180⁰  \n∴ side AB ‖ side CD and ∠A + ∠B = 105 + 135 = 240⁰\n∠A ≠ ∠B\n∴ AD is not parallel to BC.\n∴ only one pair of side is parallel\nSo, □ABCD is a trapezium.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12934,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Measures of adjacent angles of a parallelogram are (3x + 30)⁰ and (10x – 45)⁰ then find the measures of the angles of parallelogram.','','','Adjacent angles of a parallelogram are supplementary.\n∴ 10x – 45 + 3x + 30 = 180⁰\n∴ 13x = 180 + 15 = 195  \n∴ x = 15\n∴ Angles are 10x – 45 = 10(15) – 45 = 150 – 45 = 105⁰\n3x + 30 = 3(15) + 30 = 45 + 30 = 75⁰\n∴ Angles of a parallelogram are 105⁰, 75⁰, 105⁰, 75⁰ respectively.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12935,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, all the sides of □PQRS are equal : i) Mention the type of quadrilateral        ii) If l(QS) = 24 and l(QM) = 2x then find x.          \niii) If l(PR) = 18 and l(MR) = 3y then find y.          iv) Find perimeter of □PQRS','','','i) In □PQRS, all sides are equal\n∴ PQRS is a rhombus.\nii) QM = 1/2 QS ------ [diagonals of rhombus bisect each other]\n∴ 2x = 1/2 × 24      ∴ 2x = 12   ⟹ x = 6 \niii) MR = 1/2 PR ----- [diagonals of rhombus bisect each other]\n∴ 3y = 1/2 × 18     ⟹ 3y = 9     ⟹ y = 3\niv) In ΔQMR, ∠QMR = 90⁰\n∴ QR² = QM² + MR² = (2x)² + (3y)² = (12)² + 9² = 144 + 81 = 225\n∴ QR = 15 cm\n∴ Perimeter of PQRS = 4 × side = 4 × 15 = 60 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(12936,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a parallelogram ABCD such that, l(DC) = 5.5 cm, ∠D = 45⁰, l(AD) = 4 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12937,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct rhombus BEND such that, l(BN) = 5.6 cm, l(DE) = 6.5 cm.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12938,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct □PLAN such that, l(PL) = 6 cm, l(LA) = 7.5 cm, ∠P = 55⁰, ∠L = 130⁰ and ∠N = 75⁰.','','','Student can draw the figure by given information.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12939,78,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ratio of consecutive angles of a quadrilateral is 3 : 4 : 5 : 6. Find the measure of its each angle. Write with reason what type of a quadrilateral it is.','','','Let □ABCD, \n∴ 3x + 4x + 5x + 6x = 360 ------ [sum of angles of a quadrilateral]\n∴ 18x = 360      ⟹  x = 20\n∴ Angles are 3x = 60⁰, 4x = 4 × 20 = 80⁰, 5x = 5 × 20 = 100⁰, 6x = 6 × 20 = 120⁰\n∠A + ∠D = 60 + 120 = 180⁰\n∴ side AB ‖ side CD and ∠A + ∠B = 60 + 80 = 140⁰\n∠A ≠ ∠B\n∴ AD is not parallel to BC.\n∴ only one pair of side is parallel\nSo, □ABCD is a trapezium.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12940,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','5% of 400 = ----\na) 10 		b) 40 		c) 20 		d) 50','','','c) 20','',0,0,0,NULL),(12941,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Marked Price – Selling Price = ---\na) commission 	b) Brokerage        	c) Rebate                	d) Discount','','','d) Discount','',0,0,0,NULL),(12942,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Marked Price – Selling Price = ---\na) commission         	b) Brokerage           	c) Rebate          	d) Discount','','','c) Rebate     ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12943,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','MP of table Rs. 1800, Discount = 10%, ؞ Discount = -----\na) Rs. 200 		b) Rs. 180 		c) Rs. 18           	d) Rs. 10','','','b) Rs. 180 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12944,79,'mcq','1a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','Shopkeeper marks the price of item by increasing the decided price by 30% means if decided price = Rs. 100       ؞ MP = Rs. -----\na) 30 			b) 130 		 c) 70 		       d) 230','','','b) 130 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12945,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Selling Price = Rs. 1200, Discount = 25%,  ؞ MP = Rs. -----\na) 300 			b) 900 		 c) 1500 	           d) 250','','','b) 900 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12946,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Malhar sold stationary toa school worth Rs. 10000. He received 12% commission. His commission is Rs. ------- \na) 1200 		b) 120 		c) 100               	d) 240','','','a) 1200','',0,0,0,NULL),(12947,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Price of land Rs. 300000, Brokerage = Rs. 6000,  ؞ Land seller will get amount = Rs.  -----\na) 306000		b) 300000		c) 294000             	d) 6000','','','c) 294000 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12948,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mr. Vivek sold food grains through commission agent worth Rs. 25000, commission paid was Rs. 500. ؞ Mr. Vivek got amount = Rs. -----\na) 25000 		       b) 25500		c) 24500	                  d) 500','','','c) 24500	','',0,0,0,NULL),(12949,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','To purchases price of sarees Rs. 1800, Rebate = Rs. 180   ؞ Paid amount = Rs. -----\na) 1980 	         	b) 1620 		c) 1720                	d) 180','','','b) 1620 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12950,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find SP, if MP = Rs. 1300 and discount = 10%\na) 1200 		b) 1270 		c) 1170              	d) 1100','','','c) 1170         ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12951,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find discount percent when MP = Rs. 900 and SP = Rs. 873\na) 3% 			b) 5% 			c) 7% 		d) 8%','','','a) 3%','',0,0,0,NULL),(12952,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find MP if SP = Rs. 1222 and discount = 6% \na) 1350 	        	b) 1300 	            	c) 1310              	d) 1320','','','b) 1300 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12953,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An article is sold for Rs. 555 after getting 7.5% discount. Find the discount percent = -----\na) 15% 		      b) 10% 		c) 20% 	             	d) 12%','','','d) 12%','',0,0,0,NULL),(12954,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A car is bought through an agent for Rs. 360000 at a commission of 3%  find total commission ------\na) 10800 		    b) 10500 		c) 10600 	            	d) 10000','','','a) 10800 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(12955,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A handloom shop offers a rebate of 20%. Find the selling price of carpet. Whose MP is Rs. 2000 \na) 1800		    b) 1600		c) 1850 	          	d) 1750','','','b) 1600','',0,0,0,NULL),(12956,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A pair of shoes are sold for Rs. 1530 whose MP is Rs. 1800. Find its discount percent \na) 10% 	        	b) 12% 	         	c) 15% 	            	d) 20%','','','c) 15%','',0,0,0,NULL),(12957,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A flat 45% discount in monsoon sale is offered on Levi’s jeans. Find the selling price of jeans whose marked price is Rs.1999\na) 1200	          	b) 1199.45		        c) 1100           		d) 1099.45','','','d) 1099.45','',0,0,0,NULL),(12958,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On what is the discount on an article offered?\na) Purchase price 	      b) Printed price 	c) selling price     	d) cost price','','','b) Printed price ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12959,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ----- , the amount of discount is compensated by the government\na) Commission          	b) discount 		c) rebate		        d) income tax','','','c) rebate	','',0,0,0,NULL),(12960,79,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The individuals having income up to certain limit, also receive some discount on their income tax. This discount is also known as -----\na) commission         	b) brokerage	        c) rebate	               d) compensation  ','','','c) rebate	 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(12961,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Farmer sold vegetables worth Rs. 18000 through an agent. The commission was paid at the rate of 5%. Find the amount received by farmer ','','','Amount of commission = (5/100) × 18000 = 900\n∴ Amount received by farmer = 18000 – 900 = Rs. 17100','',0,0,0,NULL),(12962,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','20% discount means if the marked price of an article is Rs.100 then find its selling price.','','','Amount of discount = (20/100)  × 100 = 20\n∴ SP = MP – discount = 100 – 20 = Rs. 80','',0,0,0,NULL),(12963,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the selling price of an article, if 8% discount is given on the MP is Rs. 1200?','','','Amount of discount = (8/100) × 1200 = Rs. 96\nSP = MP – discount = 1200 – 96 = Rs. 1104\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12964,79,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The MP of an article is Rs. 1400. If it is sold for Rs. 1120, what is the amount of discount?','','','Discount = MP – SP = 1400 – 1120 = Rs. 280','',0,0,0,NULL),(12965,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Deepak sold a land for Rs. 250000 through an agent. An agent received 2.5% of brokerage. Find the amount received by Deepak after selling a land? ','','','Amount of brokerage = (2.5/100) × 250000 = Rs. 6250\nAmount received by Deepak = 250000 – 6250 = Rs. 243750','',0,0,0,NULL),(12966,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','From a Khadi store, Kishore purchased 3 towels for Rs. 225 each and 2 shirts for Rs. 525 each. He received 10% rebate on it. Find the amount paid by Kishor to shopkeeper.','','','Total amount : 3 towels = 3 × 225 = Rs. 675\n2 shirts = 2 × 525 = 1050\n∴ Total Amount = 675 + 1050 = 1725\nRebate = 10%\n∴ Amount of rebate = (10/100) × 1725 = 172.50\n∴ Amount paid by Kishor = 1725 – 172.50 = Rs. 1552.50','',0,0,0,NULL),(12967,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mrs. Pushpa bought 4 sarees for Rs. 600 each of a rebate of 12%. Find the amount given by Mrs. Pushpa ','','','Total amount = 4 × 600 = Rs. 2400\nAmount of rebate = (12/100) × 2400 = Rs. 288\nAmount given by Mrs. Pushpa = 2400 – 288 = Rs. 2112','',0,0,0,NULL),(12968,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','On purchase of Khadi cloth worth Rs. 800, 12.5% rebate is given. What is the amount of rebate?','','','Amount of rebate = (12.5/100) × 800 = Rs. 100','',0,0,0,NULL),(12969,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A shopkeeper sold a TV set for Rs. 11500 after allowing 8% discount. What is the marked price of TV set?','','','Let the marked price be Rs. x\n∴ Discount = 8x/100\n∴ SP = MP – discount       ⟹ 11500 = x – 8x/100 = 92x/100\n∴ x = (11500 × 100)/92 = Rs. 12500\n∴ MP = Rs. 12500','',0,0,0,NULL),(12970,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A farmer sold a wheat for Rs. 40600 through an agent. The commission was paid at the rate of 1.5%. What commission did the agent get?','','','Amount of commission = (1.5/100) × 40600 = Rs. 609','',0,0,0,NULL),(12971,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sudhatai purchased articles from Khadi Gram Udyog Bhandar costing Rs. 725. She received a rebate of 12%. How much amount did Sudhatai pay?','','','Amount of rebate = (12/100) × 725 = Rs. 87\nSudhatai paid amount = 725 – 87 = Rs. 638\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12972,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The marked price of bicycle is Rs. 5000. The shopkeeper gave 5% discount. Find the discount. How much a customer has to pay to buy the bicycle?','','','Amount of discount = (5/100) × 5000 = 250\nCustomer pay to buy bicycle = 5000 – 250 = Rs. 4750\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12973,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the marked price = Rs. 990 and the percentage of discount is 10, then find the selling price.','','','Amount of discount = (10/100) × 990 = Rs. 99\nSP = MP – Discount = 990 – 99 = Rs. 891\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12974,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The marked price of fan is Rs. 3000. Shopkeeper gave 12% discount on it. Find the discount and the selling price of the fan.','','','Amount of discount = (12/100) × 3000 = Rs. 360\nSP = MP – Discount = 3000 – 360 = Rs. 2640\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12975,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','John sold books worth Rs. 4500 for a publisher. For this he received 15% commission. Complete the following activity to find the total commission Jhon obtained.\nSelling price of book = ⎕, Rate of commission = ⎕         Commission obtained =  ⎕/⎕ × ⎕ = ⎕','','','Selling price of books = Rs. 4500, Rate of commission = 15%\nCommission obtained = (15/100) × 4500 = Rs. 675\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12976,79,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A farmer sold food grains for Rs. 9200 through an agent. The rate of commission was 2%. How much amount did the agent get?','','','Amount of commission = (2/100) × 9200 = Rs. 184','',0,0,0,NULL),(12977,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ravindra bought a used motorcycle through an agent. The motorcycle was priced at Rs. 35000 and the commission was paid at 2.5%. what did the motorcycle cost him?','','','Amount of commission = (2.5/100) × 35000 = Rs. 875\nPrice of bicycle = 35000 + 875 = Rs. 35875\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12978,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The marked price of fan is Rs. 2000. During off season a discount is given and it is sold for Rs. 1750. Find the rate of discount?','','','Discount = MP – SP = 2000 – 1750 = Rs. 250\nRate of discount = (Discount/MP) × 100 = (250/2000) × 100\n                          = 12.5%','',0,0,0,NULL),(12979,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sheshrao purchased 2 bed sheets for Rs. 350 each and 6 handkerchiefs for Rs. 25 each and paid Rs. 748. Find the amount of rebate and the percentage of rebate?','','','Total Amount : 2 bed sheets = 2 × 350 = 700\n6 handkerchiefs = 6 × 25 = 150\nTotal amount = 700 + 150 = Rs. 850\nRebate  = 850 – 748 = Rs. 102\nRate of rebate = (Rebate/Total Amount) × 100 = (102/850) × 100\n                           = 12%','',0,0,0,NULL),(12980,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Marked price of book is Rs. 75. It is sold at a discount of 12%. Find the discount given on the book and its selling price.','','','Amount of discount = (12/100) × 75 = Rs. 9\n∴ SP = MP – Discount = 75 – 9 = Rs. 66\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12981,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A shopkeeper marks his goods 25% above the cost price. He then allows 16% discount for selling the goods. What percent does he really make?','','','Let MP be Rs. 100 and increasing cost of goods 25%\n∴ MP = 100 + 25 = Rs. 125\nDiscount = (16/100) × 125 = 20\nSP = 125 – 20 = 105\nNow, Profit = 105 – 100 = 5\nProfit percent = (5/100) × 100 = 5%','',0,0,0,NULL),(12982,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The marked price of mixer is Rs. 2300. A customer purchased it for Rs. 1955. Find the percentage of discount offered to the customer.','','','Discount = MP – SP = 2300 – 1955 = Rs. 345\n% of discount = (discount/MP) × 100 = (345/2300) × 100 = 15%\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12983,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A shopkeeper gives 11% discount on a television set, hence a customer gets it for Rs. 22250. Find the marked price of the television set.','','','Let The MP be Rs. x\n∴ Amount of discount = 11x/100\nSP = MP – Discount         ⟹ 22250 = x – 11x/100 = 89x/100\n∴ x = (22250 × 100)/89 = Rs. 25000\n∴ MP = Rs. 25000','',0,0,0,NULL),(12984,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rafique sold flowers worth Rs. 15000 by giving 4% commission to the agent. Find the commission he paid. Find the amount received by Rafique.','','','Amount of commission = (4/100) × 15000 = Rs. 600\nAmount received by Rafique = 15000 – 600 = Rs. 14400\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12985,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Umatai purchased the following items from a Khadi Bhandar. i) 3 sarees for Rs. 560 each 	      ii) 6 bottles of honey for Rs. 90 each on the purchase, she received a rebate of 12%. How much total amount did Umatai pay?','','','Total amount of purchase : 3 sarees = 3 × 560 = 1680\n6 bottles = 6 × 90 = 540\n∴ Total amount = 1680 + 540 = Rs. 2220\nAmount of rebate = (12/100) × 2220 = 266.40\nAmount paid by Umatai = 2220 – 266.40 = Rs. 1953.60','',0,0,0,NULL),(12986,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The marked price of TV set is Rs. 5000. The shopkeeper sold it at 15% discount. Find the price of it for the customer.','','','Amount of discount = (15/100) × 5000 = Rs. 750\nSP = MP – discount = 5000 – 750 = Rs. 4250\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12987,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Jatin sold vegetables worth Rs. 24000 by giving 3% commission to the agent. Find the amount received by Jatin.','','','Amount of commission = (3/100) × 24000 = Rs. 720\nAmount received by Jatin = 24000 – 720 = Rs. 23280\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12988,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','An agent earns 6% commission on the total sale of Rs. 12000. Find the commission of the agent?','','','Amount of commission = (6/100) × 12000 = Rs. 720','',0,0,0,NULL),(12989,79,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The marked price of school uniform is Rs. 2000. The shopkeeper sold it at a discount of 10%. Find the discount given on the uniform and its selling price.','','','Amount of discount = (10/100) × 2000 = Rs. 200\nSP = MP – discount = 2000 – 200 = Rs. 1800\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12990,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','After offering a discount of 10% on marked price, a customer gets total discount of Rs.17. to find the cost price for the customer, fill in the following boxes with appropriate numbers and complete the activity ','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(12991,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A shopkeeper decides to sell a certain item at a certain price. He tags the price on the item by increasing the decided price by 25%. While selling the item, he offers 20% discount. Find how many more or less percent he gets on the decided price. ','','','Let the original price = Rs. 100 ------ (i)\n∴ When shopkeeper tags new price by increasing 25% of original price\n          = 100 + 25 = Rs. 125\nNow, he gives 20% discount on Rs. 125\n    ∴ Discount = (125 × 20)/100 = Rs. 25\n∴ SP = MP – Discount = 125 – 25 = Rs. 100\nThus, original marked price = Selling price\nHence shopkeeper will not get any more or less percent on the decided price. i.e. he gets 0% more or less on decided price.','',0,0,0,NULL),(12992,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use the given information and fill in the boxes with suitable numbers. Smt Deepanjali purchased a house for Rs. 750000 from Smt Leelaben through an agent. Agent charged 2% brokerage from both of them.\nSolution : i) Smt. Deepanjali paid = ⎕ × ⎕/⎕ = Rs. ⎕        As brokerage for purchasing the house\nii) Smt. Leelaben paid brokerage of Rs. ⎕                         iii) Total brokerage received by the agent is Rs. ⎕\niv) The cost of house Smt. Deepanjali paid is Rs. ⎕           v) The selling price of house for Smt. Leelaben is Rs. ⎕','','','Solution : i) Smt. Deepanjali paid = (2/100) × 750000 = Rs. 15000\n                                As brokerage for purchasing the house\n           ii) Smt. Leelaben paid brokerage of Rs. 15000\n           iii) Total brokerage received by the agent is Rs. 30000\n           iv) The cost of house Smt. Deepanjali paid is Rs. 765000\n           v) The selling price of house for Smt. Leelaben is Rs. 735000','',0,0,0,NULL),(12993,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A shirt was sold for Rs. 425 after allowing 15% discount on marked price. Find the marked price of the shirt.Complete the following activity by filling the boxes with suitable numbers\nSP of the shirt = Rs. ⎕, Rate of discount = Rs. ⎕           Let the MP of the shirt be Rs. 100        ؞ discount = Rs. ⎕        SP = MP - ⎕ = Rs. ⎕ = Rs. 85          \nWhen SP is Rs. 85, MP is Rs. 100       When SP is Rs. ⎕, MP is Rs. X          85/⎕ = 100/⎕	         ؞ x= ⎕              The marked price of the shirt is Rs. 500','','','SP of the shirt = Rs. 100, Rate of discount = 15%\n          Let the MP of the shirt be Rs. 100\n           ∴ discount = Rs. 15        SP = MP - discount = Rs. 100 – 15  = Rs. 85\n              When SP is Rs. 85, MP is Rs. 100       When SP is Rs. 425, MP is Rs. X\n              85/425 = 100/x	 ∴ x= 500\n            The marked price of the shirt is Rs. 500','',0,0,0,NULL),(12994,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mr. Pant purchased the following articles from Handicraft Emporium. Find the total amount of rebate and the amount he paid. The percentage of rebate is given in the brackets  2 flower pots for Rs. 140 each [15%]; 5 idols for Rs. 220 each [12%]','','','i) Total amount of flower pot : 2 × 140 = 280\nAmount of rebate = (15/100) × 280 = Rs. 42\nii) Total amount of Idols : 5 × 220 = 1100\nAmount of rebate = (12/100) × 1100 = 132\nTotal amount of purchasing = 280 + 1100 = Rs. 1380\nTotal amount of rebate = 42 + 132 = Rs. 174\n ∴ Amount paid = 1380 – 174 = Rs. 1206','',0,0,0,NULL),(12995,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rajiv sold 2.5 ton groundnut (export quality) through an agent. The commission was paid at the rate of 1%. Rajiv received Rs. 237600. Find the selling price of groundnut per ton.','','','Let the MP be Rs. x\n∴ Discount = 1x/100\n∴ SP = MP – Discount      ⟹ 237600 = x – x/100 = 99x/100\n∴ x = (237600 × 100)/99 = 240000\n∴ MP of 2.5 ton groundnut be Rs. 240000\n∴ MP of 1 ton groundnut be Rs. 96000','',0,0,0,NULL),(12996,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rajabhau sold his flat to Vasantrao for Rs. 8800000 through an agent. The agent charged 2% commission from both of them. Find how much commission the agent got. ','','','Amount of commission for one side = (2/100) × 8800000 = Rs. 176000\nAmount of commission for both side = 2 × 176000 = Rs. 352000\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12997,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Raj purchased following articles from Sevagram Khadi Bhandar. Bhandar declares rebate of 20% on purchase articles. : a) 3 honey bottles for Rs. 150 each 	\nb) 4 sarees for Rs. 425 each        c) 12 handkerchiefs for Rs. 15 each. Find the total rebate and the amount to be paid by Raj. ','','','Total purchasing amount : \n3 honey bottles = 3 × 150 = 450\n4 sarees = 4 × 425 = Rs. 1700\n12 handkerchiefs = 12 × 15 = 180\nTotal amount = 450 + 1700 + 180 = Rs. 2330\nAmount of rebate = (20/100) × 2330 = Rs. 466\nAmount paid by Raj = 2330 – 466 = Rs. 1864','',0,0,0,NULL),(12998,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mery sold his flat to Hazal for Rs. 7500000 through an agent. The agent charged 2% commission from both of them. Find how much commission the agent got.','','','Amount of commission for one side = (2/100) × 7500000 = Rs. 150000\nAmount of commission for both side = 2 × 150000 = Rs. 300000\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(12999,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rajguru sold 40 quintals of wheat at Rs. 4550 per quintal through an agent. The commission was paid at the rate of 2%. Find the amount Rajguru received after selling the wheat.  ','','','Amount of commission = (2/100) × 4550 = Rs. 91\nAmount received by Rajguru = 4550 – 91 = Rs. 4459\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(13001,79,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','On certain item, a shopkeeper gives 8% discount to the customer and still he gets 15% profit. If marked price of the item is Rs. 1750, then, what is the cost price of the item for the shopkeeper?','','','Discount = (8/100) × 1750 = Rs. 140\n∴ SP = MP – discount = 1750 – 140 = Rs. 1610\nProfit is 15%. Hence if the cost price is Rs. 100 then selling price is Rs. 115. That is, when selling price is Rs. 115, cost price is Rs. 100. SO when the selling price is Rs. 1610, let the cost price be Rs. x\n∴ x/100  = 1610/115          ∴ x = (1610 × 100)/115 = 1400\n∴ Cost price of the item = Rs. 1400','',0,0,0,NULL),(15106,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by matter?','','','Matter is anything around us that we can perceive through our sense. It is made up of particles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15107,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is an atom?','','','An atom is the smallest unit of matter. It is the smallest particle of an element which retains its chemical identity in all the physical and chemical changes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15108,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is the smallest unit of matter?','','','An atom is the smallest unit of matter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15109,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If the striker hit the coin, in which direction would it go? Straight forward to a side or in the reverse direction?\n','','','The striker would go in the reverse direction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15110,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Ar, Ne, O₂, C.','','','O₂. It is a molecule while, the rest are atoms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15111,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: CO₂, NH₃, CH₄, Cl₂ ','','','Cl₂. It is an element while, the rest are compounds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15112,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Boron, Graphite, Cadmium, Beryllium ','','','Graphite. It is used as a moderator in nuclear reactors while, the rest are used as controllers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15113,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Nucleus, Protons, Neutrons, Electrons','','','Nucleus. It is not a subatomic particle while the rest are subatomic particles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15114,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: He, Ne, H, Ar','','','H. It is not an inert gas while the rest are inert gases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15115,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Hydrogen, magnesium, chlorine, sodium','','','Magnesium. The valency of magnesium is two while the valency of all other elements is one.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15116,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out: Boron, cadmium, beryllium, heavy water','','','Heavy water. Heavy water is used as moderator while others are used as controllers in nuclear chain reaction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15117,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: ¹⁶₆C : 6 neutrons : : ¹⁶₈O : ------','','','8 neutrons','',0,0,0,NULL),(15118,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: K shell : 2 electrons : : M shell : -------','','','18 electrons','',0,0,0,NULL),(15119,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Sodium : 2, 8, 1 : : Chlorine : ------ ','','','(2, 8, 7)','',0,0,0,NULL),(15120,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Fluorine : 1 : : Aluminum : ------','','','3','',0,0,0,NULL),(15121,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy:  ‘M’ shell : 18 electrons : : ‘N’ shell : ----','','','32 electrons','',0,0,0,NULL),(15122,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Protons : ------ : : Electrons : Shells','','','Nucleus','',0,0,0,NULL),(15123,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Florine : 2, 7 : : Argon : ------ ','','','2, 8, 8','',0,0,0,NULL),(15124,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: H₂ : Element : : NH₃ : ------','','','Compound','',0,0,0,NULL),(15129,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The atomic mass number is equal to the number of neutrons in an atom.\n','','','False. The atomic mass number is equal to the number of protons and neutrons in an atom.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15130,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The atomic number of an element is equal to the number of protons in each atom.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15131,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Electrons present in the first shell is called valence electrons.\n','','','False. Electrons present in the last shell is called valence electrons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15132,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Atoms are divisible.\n','','','False. Atoms are indivisible','',0,0,0,NULL),(15133,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : β-particles are bombarded with gold foil.\n','','','False. α – Particles are bombarded with gold foil.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15134,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Neutron is electrically neutral.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15135,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The maximum number of electrons a shell can contain is obtained by the formula 2n². \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15136,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Mass of electrons is 800 times less than that of a hydrogen atom.\n','','','False. Mass of an electron is 1800 times less than that of a hydrogen atom.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15137,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Mass of an atom is distributed evenly.\n','','','False. Mass of an atom is concentrated is its nucleus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15138,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The ‘K’ shell has maximum energy.\n','','','False. The ‘K’ shell has minimum energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15139,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Isotopes have same atomic number but different atomic mass number.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15140,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Define: Atom','','','An atom is the smallest particles of an element which retains its chemical identity in all the physical and chemical changes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15141,129,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Text Book','','Define: Isotopes ','','','The atomic number is the fundamental property of an element and its chemical identity. Some elements in nature have atoms with same atomic number but different mass number, such atoms of the same element having different mass number are called Isotopes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15142,129,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Text Book','','Define: Atomic number','','','Atomic number is the number of electrons or protons present in the nucleus. It is denoted by letter ‘Z’.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15143,129,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Text Book','','Define: Atomic mass number','','','Atomic mass number is defined as the sum of the number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus. It is denoted by letter ‘A’.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15144,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Define: Moderator in nuclear reactor ','','','In nuclear reactor, a moderator is a medium that reduces the speed of the fast neutrons and thereby turning them into thermal neutrons, capable of a nuclear chain reaction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15145,129,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Nucleus ','','','Total number of protons and neutrons present in nucleus of an atom is known as nucleons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15146,129,'descriptive','2',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Valence electrons','','','The outermost shell of any item is known as valence shell and the electrons present in this shell are known as valence electrons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15147,129,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Valency','','','The number of electrons gained, lost or shared by the atom of an element to complete its valence shell with 8 electrons is called the valency of that element.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15148,129,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Electronic configuration of elements','','','The systematic at arrangement of electrons in different orbits or shells of atom of an element is known as electronic configuration of element.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15149,129,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: Orbit','','','An orbit is the path of the electron around the nucleus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15150,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason: All the mass of an atom is concentrated in the nucleus.\n','','','i) The nucleus consists of protons and neutrons.\nii) The mass of a neutrons is approximately equal to that of a proton.\niii) The electrons revolve around the nucleus in specified orbits and their mass is negligible as compared to that of a protons or neutron.\niv) Hence, the mass of an atom is concentrated in the nucleus.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15151,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason: Atom is electrically neutral.','','','i) Positively charged protons are present in the nucleus of an atom. The negatively charged electrons revolve around the nucleus in orbits.\nii) The number of positive charges in the nucleus is equal to the number of negative charges around the nucleus.\niii) The neutrons do not carry any charge.\niv) Hence, the atom is electrically neutral.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15152,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason: Atomic mass number is a whole number.','','','i) Atomic mass number is the sum of protons and neutrons present in the atom.\nii) As the number of protons and neutrons are always whole numbers, hence the atomic mass number is also a whole number.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15153,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Atoms are stable though negatively charged electrons are revolving within it.\n','','','i) Electrostatic forces act on the electrons present in the atoms duo  to which they revolve around the nucleus in specific orbits.\nii) Hence, atoms are stable though negatively charged electrons are revolving within it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15154,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason: In Rutherford’s experiment, some alpha particles collided with the gold sheet and turned back.\n','','','i) The alpha particles are positively charged a particles.\nii) These alpha particles which collided with protons present in the atoms of the gold sheet turned back.\niii) This is because protons are also positively charged and like charges repel each other.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15155,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason: Many elements have isotopes.','','','i) Isotopes are atoms of same element having same atomic number but different mass number.\nii) Mass number of an atom of an element is dependent upon the number of neutrons present in them.\niii) In nature it is found that in atoms of many elements the number of neutrons vary. E.g. most of the hydrogen atom have no neutrons.\niv) But, there are some which have one neutron and some which have two neutrons. Therefore, it is found that many elements have isotopes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15156,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between: Thomson’s atomic model and Rutherford’s atomic Model\n','','','Difference :','',0,0,1,NULL),(15157,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Atomic number and Atomic mass number','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15158,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Neutrons and Protons','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15159,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between: Electrons and Protons','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15160,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is the difference between the solid atom in Dalton’s atomic theory and Thomson’s atomic model?\n','','','According to Dalton\'s atomic theory, atom is a hard, ball with no internal structure, whereas according to Thomson’s atomic model, atom consists of smaller particles called electrons and protons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15161,129,'descriptive','2',2,1,'Text Book','','Explain the difference between the distribution of positive charge in Thomson’s atomic model and Rutherford’s atomic model.\n','','','i) In Thomson\'s atomic model, Positive charge was uniformly distributed, whereas in Rutherford’s atomic model, the positive charge is not uniformly spread throughs out the atom.\nii) It has to be concentrated in a very small volume to deflect the positively charged a particle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15162,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is the thing which is present in Rutherford’s atomic model and not present in Dalton’s and Thomson’s atomic models?\n','','','Nucleus at the centre of the atom is present in Rutherford’s atomic model but it is absent in Dalton\'s and Thomson’s atomic models.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15163,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How many types of subatomic particles are found in atom?','','','There are three types of sub atomic particles found in an atom namely – Electron, Proton and Neutron.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15164,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which subatomic particles are electrically charged?','','','The subatomic particles- Electrons with negative charge and Protons with positive charge.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15165,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which subatomic particles are present in the nucleus?','','','Subatomic particles Neutrons and Protons are present in the nucleus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15166,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Where are electrons revolving around the nucleus placed?','','','Electrons revolving around the nucleus are placed in the discrete orbits.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15167,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the symbols used for the shells which accommodate the electrons in various atom?\n','','','The symbols used for the shells K, L, M, N.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15168,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is the symbol and ordinal number of the innermost shell?','','','Symbol for innermost shell is ‘K’ and ordinal number is ‘1’.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15169,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which is the outermost shell of Fluorine atom?','','','The outermost shell of Fluorine atom is ‘L’ shell with 7 electrons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15170,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which is the outermost shell of sodium atom?','','','Atomic number of sodium is 11. The outermost shell of sodium atom with 1 electron.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15171,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which is the outermost shell of hydrogen atom?','','','Atomic number of Hydrogen is 1. K- shell is the outermost shell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15172,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by the atomic number (Z) of an element?','','','The atomic number (Z) is defined as the number of protons or number of electrons present in an atom.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15173,129,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Why are atomic numbers and atomic mass numbers always in whole numbers? \n','','','Atomic numbers and atomic mass numbers gives us respectively; the number of protons and the sum of protons and neutrons together. Hence, they are always in whole numbers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15175,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Deduce from the datum provided:','','','i) Sodium has a mass number 23. Since, sodium has 11 protons, the number of neutrons must be -23 – 11 = 12.\n∴  Sodium has 12 Neutrons.\nii) Carbon has atomic number 6, that means it has 6 protons. The number of Neutrons must be 14 – 6 = 8 Neutrons.\nAtomic mass number is the sum of number of protons and number of neutrons.\niii) Atomic number of chlorine is 17. By the definition of atomic number, it is the number of protons or electrons of an atom.\n∴ Number of protons = Atomic number\n∴ Number of protons is 17.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15176,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw a neat labelled diagram of Rutherford’s scattering experiment.','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(15177,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw a neat labelled diagram of Thomson’s atomic model.','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(15178,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table : Write the maximum number of electron in a shell using the table.\n','','','Table :','',1,0,1,NULL),(15179,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Atomic numbers (Z) of some elements are given. Write down the number of electrons present in the outermost shell of each of them.\n','','','Table :','',1,0,1,NULL),(15180,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The number of electrons of some elements is given. By using it write the electronic configuration, number of valence electron and valency of the respective elements.\n','','','Table :','',1,0,1,NULL),(15181,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Complete the table:','','','Table :','',1,0,1,NULL),(15182,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How will you think about atomic mass distribution according to Thomson’s model? Whether this distribution is uniform or non-uniform as per Dalton’s atomic theory?\n','','','i) According to Thomson’s atomic model, the positive charge is distributed throughout the atom and the negatively charged electrons are embedded in it.\nii) The positive charges were spread uniformly all over the atom and therefore the mass of the atom was spread uniformly.\niii) As per the Dalton’s atomic theory, atom is a hard, solid ball like structure with no internal structure.\niv) So, the distribution of mass is not defined by this theory. Atomic mass distribution according to Thomson’s model is uniform.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15183,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the striker flicked by you misses the coin that you aimed at, where would the strikers go?\n','','','When the striker misses the coin that we aimed at, the striker will hit the opposite side of the carrom and reverse back or bounces back.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15184,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which discovery did point out that an atom has internal structure?','','','J. J. Thomson’s discovery of negatively charged particles i.e. electrons point out that an atom has internal structure.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15185,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','When the number of valence electrons in an element ‘x’ is 4 or less than 4, does ‘x’ match with the valency of that element?\n','','','Yes','',0,0,0,NULL),(15186,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','When the number of the valence electrons in an element ‘x’ is 4 or more than 4, does ‘(8 – x)’ match with the valency of that element? How many electrons are used to complete the octet?\n','','','No, it does not match with the valency of the element. The number of electrons are used to complete the octet, i.e. number of electrons required to complete the octet. For example, in case of chlorine valence electrons are 7. So, to complete its octet, 1 electron is required.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15187,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The symbol used for oxygen is ‘O’. There are 8 protons and 8 neutrons in its nucleus. From this determine the atomic number (Z) and mass number (A) of oxygen and arrange these in a conventional symbol. \n','','','In Oxygen atom, there are 8 protons and 8 neutrons. Therefore its atomic number is 8 and mass number is 8 + 8 = 16. (Number of protons + Number of neutrons)\nTherefore, conventional symbol of oxygen ¹⁶₈O.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15188,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Atomic number of carbon is 6. How many electrons are there in a carbon atom?\n','','','Atomic number is the number of protons or number of electrons of an atom. As atomic number of carbon is 6, the number of electrons is also 6.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15189,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A sodium atom contains 11 electrons. What is the atomic number of sodium?\n','','','As sodium contains 11 electrons, its atomic number is also 11.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15190,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The atomic number and mass number of Magnesium are 12 and 24 respectively. How will you show this by the convention symbol? \n','','','Mass number (A) and Atomic number (Z) of Magnesium is 24 and 12 respectively. Hence, it is represented as, ²⁴₁₂Mg','',0,0,0,NULL),(15191,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The atomic number and mass number of Calcium are 20 and 40 respectively. Deduce the number of neutron present in the Calcium nucleus.\n','','','The atomic number and mass number of Calcium are 20 and 40.\nAtomic number = 20, Number of protons = 20\nAtomic mass = Sum of protons + Neutrons \n40 = 20 + Neutrons\n∴ Number of neutrons = 40 – 20 = 20\n∴ 20 Neutrons are present in the Calcium nucleus\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15192,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write symbol of electron distribution in shell of fluorine atom.','','','Atomic number of Fluorine is 9. Its electronic configuration is, \nK      L\n2      7\n∴ Electronic configuration in the numerical form is 2, 7.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15193,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Sulphur contains 16 protons and 16 neutrons. What would be its atomic number and mass number?\n','','','Atomic number = Number of protons = 16\n∴ Atomic number = 16\nAtomic mass number = No. of protons + No. of neutrons = 16 + 16 = 32\n∴ Atomic mass number is 32.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15194,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the diagram and answer the question given below : a) Identify the element represented in figure (a) and (b).        b) State the number of valence electrons in figure (b).         c) Write the valency of atom of the element represented in figure (a).\n','','','a) The element represented in fig. (a) is neon (Ne) and the element represented in fig. (b) is carbon.\nb) The number of valence electrons in fig. (b) is 4.\nc) The valency of atom of the element represented in fig. (a) i.e. valency of neon is zero.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15195,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the diagram and answer the questions based on it : a) Total how many electrons are present in the element shown in the diagram?        b) The given diagram represents electron distribution in which element?        c) What is the valency of this element?\n','','','a) There are total 11 electrons in the element represented in the above diagram.\nb) The given diagram represents electron distribution in sodium.\nc) Valency of sodium is one since there is one electron in its outermost (valence) shell.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15196,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the diagram given below:','','','i) The diagram represents the disintegration of uranium - 235 taking place in a nuclear reactor.\nii) In a nuclear reactor, the nucleus of the isotope uranium – 235, on bombardment with slow speed of neutrons, undergoes nuclear fission to form nuclei of two different elements, krypton – 92 and barium – 141 and 2 to 3 neutrons.\niii) On decreasing the speed and number, these neutrons bring about fission of more uranium – 235 nuclei and thus, a chain reaction of nuclear fission takes place.\niv) A large amount of nuclear energy in the form of heat is released during a chain reaction of fission.\nv) The chain reaction is kept under control to prevent any probable explosion.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15197,129,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the following diagram and answer the questions given below it : a) What does the above figure represent?         b) Which metal was used by the scientist for bombardment in the experiment?       c) Very few α – particles were deflected through large angles. What does it indicate?\n','','','a) The above figure represents Rutherford’s -particle scattering experiment. \nb) A thin foil of gold was used by Rutherford for bombardment in the -particle scattering experiment. \nc) Very few -particles were deflected through large angles in the scattering experiment which indicated that all the positive charge was concentrated in a small volume inside an atom.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15198,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Use the following molecular formulae to determine the valences of H, Cl, O, S, N, C, Br, I, Na. Molecular formula : H₂, HCl, H₂O, H₂S, NH₃, CH₄, HBr, HI, NaH.\n','','','Valency of Hydrogen is 1. In H₂, two hydrogen atoms shared 1 electron each and form H₂ molecule.\nH₂ → H	    H                 HO →  H 		O\n        ↘1           1↙                               ↘2           1↙          ∴ H₂O\nValency of Hydrogen is 1 and Valency of oxygen is 2.\nH₂S  →  H 	      S\n              ↘2       1↙			∴ H₂S\nValency of hydrogen is 1 and sulphur is 2\nNH₃ →  N	         H\n               ↘1	       3↙                     ∴ NH₃\nSimilarly, we can show that, valency of CH₄, HBr, HI, NaH\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15199,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by valency of an element? What is the relationship between the number of valence electrons and valency? \n','','','i) Valency is the ability of an atom to lose or gain electrons to complete its balance shell.\nii) Valance electrons is the number of electrons in the outermost shell.\niii) Valency of an element depends on the Valence electrons. The valency of an elements is same as the number of valence electrons, electrons; if this number is 4 or less than 4.\niv) However, when an element has four or more valence electrons, the number of electrons by which the octet is a short of completion is the valency of that element.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15200,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by atomic mass number? Explain how the atomic number and mass number of carbon are 6 and 12 respectively.\n','','','i) Atomic mass number is the sum of the number of protons and the number of neutrons.\nii) Atomic number is the number of protons or electrons.\niii) Carbon’s atomic number is 6 means carbon has 6 protons and 6 neutrons in its nucleus.\niv) Carbon’s atomic mass is 12 means carbon has 6 protons and 6 neutrons in its nucleus.\nv) We know from the definition of atomic mass, it is the sum of the number of protons and neutrons, therefore, 6 protons + 6 neutrons = 12 is the atomic mass of carbon atom.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15201,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by subatomic particles? Give brief information of three subatomic particles with reference to electrical charge, mass and location.\n','','','i) The subatomic particles are particles smaller than an atom. It is a unit of matter or energy that is the fundamental make up of all matter.\nii) There are three types of subatomic particles in an atom : Proton (p), Neutron (n) and Electrons (e⁻)\nNucleus : The atomic nucleus is positively charged. Entire mass of the atom is concentrated in the nucleus. The nucleus contains two types of subatomic particles together called nucleons.\n Protons and neutrons are the two types of nucleons.\na) Proton (p) : i) Proton is a positively charged subatomic particle in the atomic nucleus located inside the nucleus.\nb) Neutron : Neutron is an electrically neutral subatomic particle and is denoted by the symbol ‘n’. Neutron is also located inside the nucleus.\nc) Electron : Electron is a negatively charged subatomic particle and is denoted by the symbol (e⁻).\nElectron is the extra nuclear part revolving in the discrete orbits around the nucleus.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15202,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the fundamental properties of an atom.','','','i) An atom is the smallest particle of an element which may or may not be able to exist freely. \nii) It is the smallest particle that takes part in a chemical reaction.\niii) Atoms are so small that they cannot be seen even under a microscope.\niv) Atoms are electrically neutral.\nv) The atoms are composes of subatomic particles -  protons, neutrons and electrons.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15203,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the postulates of Dalton’s Atomic theory.','','','i) British scientist John Dalton in the year 1803 AD proposed his atomic theory.\nii) According to this theory, matter is made of atoms and atoms are individual and indestructible.\niii) Atoms of the same element are alike but they are different from atoms of other elements.\niv) According to John Dalton, atom is hard, solid sphere with no internal structure.\nv) Atoms combine to form molecules.\nvi) Molecule is the smallest unit of compound \nvii) According to this theory, the mass is distributed uniformly in an atom.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15204,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain Rutherford’s atomic model.','','','Ernest Rutherford studied in the inside of atom by scattering experiment and put forth nuclear model of atom in the year 1911. The main postulates of his model are :\ni) There is a positively charged in nucleus at centre of an atom.\nii) Almost to the entire mass of the atom is concentrated in the nucleus.\niii) Negatively charged particles called electrons revolve around the nucleus.\niv) The total negative charge on all the electrons is equal to the positive charge on the nucleus. As the opposite charges are balanced, the atom is electrically neutral.\nv) There is an empty space between the revolving electron and the atomic nucleus.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15206,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the postulates of Bohr’s atomic model.','','','In the year 1913, Danish scientist : Niels Bohr explained the stability of atom by putting stable orbit atomic model. The important postulates of Bohr’s atomic model are as follows :\ni) The electrons revolving around the atomic nucleus lie in the concentric circular orbits at certain distance from the nucleus.\nii) Energy of an electron is constant while it is in a particular orbit.\niii) When an electron jumps from an inner orbit to an outer orbit, it absorbs energy equal to the difference of its energy level and when it jumps from outer orbit to an inner orbit, it emits energy equal to the difference of its energy level.\niv) The stationary orbits are numbered as 1, 2, 3, 4, …. or by letters K, L, M, N respectively.\nv) The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in nth orbit is 2n².\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15207,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is Rutherford’s model not stable? Is Neil Bohr’s model stable? Why?','','','i) Rutherford’s model could not explain the stability of an atom.\nii) According to him, an atom considered of a heavy positively charged nucleus at the centre and the electrons where revolving around it.\niii) But, whenever a charged particle like electron revolve around a central force, like nucleus, it loses its energy continuously in the form of radiations.\niv) Thus, the orbit will becomes smaller and smaller following a spiral path and finally the electron will fall into the nucleus.\nv) According to Bohr\'s model, electrons cannot radiate energy while revolving in discrete orbits.\nvi) Therefore, it explains the stability of the atom. Neil Bohr’s model was considered more stable than Rutherford’s atomic model.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15208,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe Thomson’s atomic model.','','','i) Thomson\'s atomic model, is also known as Plum Pudding model of atom.\nii) J. J. Thomson in the year 1904 gave the first model of atomic structure.\niii) According to this model, the positive charge is distributed throughout the atom and the negativity charged particles are embedded in it.\niv) Positively charged particles are protons and negatively charged particles are electrons.\nv) The distributed positive charge is balanced by the negative charge and therefore, the atom becomes electrically neutral.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15209,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a note on Isotopes. ','','','i) Atoms of same element exists in different forms having same atomic number but different mass number. Such atoms of the same element are called isotopes.\nii) The chemical properties of isotopes are same but physical properties may differ.\niii) For example, carbon has three isotopes namely C-12, C- 13, C – 14. The mass number of isotopes is also represented by another method as ¹²C, ¹³C, ¹⁴C.\niv) The isotopes have same proton number but different neutron number.\nv) The most common isotopes of Hydrogen has no neutrons in it. Another isotope of Hydrogen is called Deuterium with the neutron and third isotope Tritium exist with two neutrons in it.\nUses of isotopes\nvi) Isotopes are used in various field such as industry, agriculture, medicine, research field.\nvii) Uranium- 235 is used for nuclear fission and production of electricity.\nviii) The radioactive isotopes are used for food preservation from microbes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15210,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on Nuclear Reactor.','','','a) A nuclear reactor is a machine that generates electricity on a large scale by using atomic energy. \nb) Working of nuclear reactor: i) In a nuclear reactor, the nucleus of the isotope uranium-235, on bombardment with slow speed of neutrons, undergoes nuclear fission to form nuclei of two different elements, krypton-92 and barium-141 and 2 to 3 neutrons. \nii) On decreasing the speed and number, these neutrons bring about fission of more uranium-235 nuclei and thus, a chain reaction of nuclear fission takes place. \niii) A large amount of nuclear energy in the form of heat isreleased during a chain reaction of fission. \niv) The chain reaction is kept under control to prevent any probable explosion. c) To control the chain reaction in the nuclear reactor, it is necessary to decrease the speed and number of neutrons, used for the bombardment on U-235 isotope. The following provisions are made in a nuclear reactor in order to avoid explosion: \n1) Moderator: Graphite or heavy water is used as moderator for reducing the speed of neutrons. \n2) Controller: Rods of boron, cadmium, beryllium, etc. are used as controller for reducing the number of neutrons by absorbing them. \n3) Coolant: The nuclear fission process produces large amount of heat which is taken out by using water as coolant.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15211,129,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is the atomic structure of an atom of an element ¹⁶₈Y :          a) What is wrong with the structure of the given atom of element ‘Y’?        b) Draw a correct representation of this atom.        c) Identify the element.         d) Calculate the number of neutrons from the correct atomic structure.          e) How many electrons are present in the valence shell of the correct atomic structure of ‘Y’?\n\n','','','a) The element Y has atomic number, Z = 8 which means that it has 2 shells, K and L, with completely filled K shell. K-shell should have 2 electrons and L-shell should have the remaining 6 electrons. \n∴ The electron distribution in Y can be shown as K L 2, 6 . \nb) The correct representation of atomic structure of element ‘Y’ is as follows:\nc) The element ‘Y’ with mass number 16 and atomic number 8 is oxygen ¹⁶₈O). \nd) Atomic number = 8 \nNumber of protons = Atomic number = 8 \nMass number = 16 \nMass number = Number of protons + Number of neutrons \n∴ Number of neutrons = Mass number - Number of protons = 16 - 8 = 8 \ne) The number of electrons in valence shell of ‘Y’ is six\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15273,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Kingdom Monera, kingdom Fungi, kingdom Plantae, kingdom Animalia. ','','','Kingdom Monera – They are unicellular microorganisms while others are multicellular','',0,0,0,NULL),(15274,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Salmonella typhi, legionella pneumonia, Clostridium, Aspergillus. ','','','Aspergillus : It is a fungus while others are bacteria','',0,0,0,NULL),(15275,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Kingdom Monera : Treponema palladium : : ------ : Plasmodium. ','','','Kingdom Protista','',0,0,0,NULL),(15276,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Entamoeba histolytica : ------ : : Plasmodium vivax : Malaria ','','','Amoebiasis','',0,0,0,NULL),(15277,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out the difference between the two components in one sentences : Unicellular and Multicellular ','','','Unicellular means organism is made of a single cell and Multicellular means organisms is made up of many cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15278,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : 10 µm to 100 µm : ----- : : approximately 200 µm : Protozoa','','','Algae','',0,0,0,NULL),(15279,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out the difference between the two components in one sentences : Autotrophic and Heterotrophic ','','','Autotrophic organisms can prepare their own food and Heterotrophic organisms depend upon others for food','',0,0,0,NULL),(15280,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out the difference between the two components in one sentences : Prokaryotic and Eukaryotic ','','','In Prokaryotic, nucleus is not well defined. In Eukaryotes, nucleus is well defined with nuclear envelope and nucleus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15281,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out the difference between the two components in one sentences : Protista and Fungi','','','Protista are single celled organisms, whereas Fungi are multicellular organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15282,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out the difference between the two components in one sentences : Volvox and Paramecium','','','Volvox is an autotroph whereas, Paramecium is a heterotroph.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15283,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out the difference between the two components in one sentences : Algae and Fungi ','','','Algae is green, autotrophic in nature, containing only one nucleus, whereas Fungi is non-green, heterotrophic in nature, containing many nuclei.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15284,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following sentence is True or False. Correct the false statements : Haeckel in 1735 divided living world in two kingdoms.','','','False. Carl Linnaeus in 1735 divided living world into two kingdoms, Vegetablia and Animalia.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15285,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following sentence is True or False. Correct the false statements : Organisms belonging to kingdom Monera include all types of bacteria and blue green algae.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15286,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the following sentence is True or False. Correct the false statements : Lactobacilli are harmful bacteria.','','','False. These are useful bacteria as they convert milk into curds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15287,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the following sentence is True or False. Correct the false statements : Cell wall of fungi is made up of chitin.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15288,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the following sentence is True or False. Correct the false statements : Organ of locomotion in Amoeba is pseudopodia. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15289,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the following sentence is True or False. Correct the false statements : Tomato wilt is a viral disease. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15290,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following sentence is True or False. Correct the false statements : Humans are affected by Picorna virus.','','','False. Cattles are affected by picorna virus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15291,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following sentence is True or False. Correct the false statements : Viruses are the organisms that live on the borderline of living and non-living.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15292,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following sentence is True or False. Correct the false statements : In Bacteriophage, bacteria attacks viruses. ','','','False. In Bacteriophage, viruses attack bacteria.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15293,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following/who am I? : I don’t have true nucleus, cell organelles or plasma membrane.','','','Kingdom from Kingdom Monera','',0,0,0,NULL),(15294,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following/who am I? : I have nucleus and membrane bound cell organelle.','','','Kingdom Protista/Eukaryote','',0,0,0,NULL),(15295,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following/who am I? : I reproduce mainly by cell division.','','','Protozoa','',0,0,0,NULL),(15296,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following/who am I? : I live on decaying organic matter.','','','Fungi/Bacteria','',0,0,0,NULL),(15297,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following/who am I? : I reproduce sexually by cell division and asexually by budding.','','','Fungi','',0,0,0,NULL),(15298,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following/who am I? : I am green but don’t have organs.','','','Algae','',0,0,0,NULL),(15299,25,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following/who am I? : I can produce my replica.','','','Virus','',0,0,0,NULL),(15300,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Viruses are said to be organisms on the edge of living and non-living.','','','i) Size of viruses are approximately 10 nm to 100 nm\nii) They can be seen only with an electron microscope\niii) They are independent particles made up of a long molecule of DNA or RNA covered by a protein coat.\niv) Viruses survive only in living plant or animal cells where they produce their own proteins with the help of host cells and creating numerous replica.\nv) Generally, viruses are not considered as living organisms. Hence, viruses are said to be organisms on the age of living and non-living.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15301,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are microorganisms classified being the largest in nuclear membrane?  ','','','i) Microorganisms are broadly classified into prokaryotes and Eukaryotes on the basis of the presence of nuclear membrane.\nii) The Prokaryotes include all bacteria. The Eukaryotes are broadly classified into Protista, Fungi and Algae.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15302,25,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Use Whittaker method to classify bacteria, protozoa, fungi, algae, prokaryotic and Eukaryotic microbes. \nAns. : \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram :','',0,0,1,NULL),(15303,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Take a small drop of curd or buttermilk on clean glass side. Dilute with a little water. Carefully keep a cover slip. Observe it under high power of compound microscope. What did you see? ','','','When a small drop of curd or buttermilk is taken on a glass slide we observed rod shaped bacteria called Lactobacillus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15304,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Prepare a temporary mount of one drop of pond water on a glass slide. Observe it under low power and high power of microscope. ','','','i) When a drop of pond water is taken on a glass slide, motile irregular shaped Amoebae are observed.\nii) Other organisms like Paramecium, Euglena may be observed.\niii) Moreover we can observe organisms like algae, protozoa and some fungi.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15305,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Take a moist piece of bread or bhakri and keep it in a container with lid for 2-3 days. After 2-3 days a fine cotton thread like tuft is found growing on the surface of the bread. Prepare a temporary mount of few threads from this culture and observe it under the microscope.','','','i) The fine tuft grown on the surface of the bread is a bread mold fungus. E.g. Rhizopus\nii) The thread- like structures formed are called hyphae and the network of hyphae is called mycelium.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15306,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the figure given below and answer the questions : i) The given organisms is commonly known as?         ii) Is it unicellular or multicellular?        iii) It belongs to which kingdom?','','','i) The given organism is commonly known as Baker’s yeast\nii) It is unicellular\niii) It belongs to kingdom fungi\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15307,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the figure and answer the questions : i) Identify the organisms.       ii) Is it unicellular or multicellular?       iii) It belongs to which kingdom?','','','i) Volvox	ii) Unicellular	iii) Protista','',1,0,0,NULL),(15308,25,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the figure given below and answer the questions : i) Identify the organisms given above.         ii) It belongs to which kingdom?         iii) What is the mode of nutrition in this organisms? ','','','i) The organism given above is Amoeba\nii) It belongs to kingdom Protista\niii) The mode of nutrition in Amoeba is heterotrophic\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15309,25,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the history how different scientists worked on biological classification.','','','i) It was Carl Linnaeus in 1735 divided the living world into 2 kingdoms – Vegetabilia and Animalia.\nii) Haeckel in 1866, considered 3 kingdoms- Protista, Plants and animals.\niii) In 1925, Chatton made 2 groups. Prokaryotes and Eukaryotes.\niv) In 1938, Kopland divided living organisms into 4 Kingdom Monera, Protista, Plants and animals.\nv) In 1969, Robert Whittaker divided living organisms into 5 groups- Monera, Protista, Fungi and Animalia.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15310,25,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Whittaker classify organisms? State and explain the different stages. ','','','i) Complexity of cell structure : Prokaryotic and Eukaryotic\nii) Complexity of organisms : Unicellular or multicellular\niii) Mode of nutrition : Plants- Autotrophic- photosynthetic\niv) Fungi – Saprophytic- Absorption from dead organisms.\n Animals - Heterotrophic and ingestive\nv) Life style : Plants – Producers, Animals – Consumers, Fungi - Decomposers\nv) Phylogenetic relationship : Prokaryotic to Eukaryotic, unicellular to multicellular.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15311,25,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','With neat labelled diagram of organisms in kingdom Monera and explain its characteristics.','','','i) They are mostly unicellular, autotrophic or heterotrophic.\nii) They are usually non motile but some may have flagella.\niii) These are prokaryotic cells without distinct nucleus or cell organelles\niv) All types of bacteria and blue green algae are included in the Kingdom Monera.\nv) Moving, small rod like Lactobacilli can be seen on high power of compound microscope, by observing a drop of curd or buttermilk on a glass slide.\nvi) Examples : Streptococcus pneumoniae, Clostridium titan, Clostridium botulinum, Treponema pallidum, Vibrio cholera, Staphylococcus aureus, Legionella pneumoniae, Salmonella typhi\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(15319,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Bite of dog, bite of rabbit, bite of monkey, bite of  parrot.','','','Bite of parrot : It is not a cause of Rabies while others cause Rabies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15331,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Hydrophobia, Exaggerations in behavior, Prolonged fear, stomach ache.','','','Stomach ache : It is not the symptom of Rabies while others are symptoms of Rabies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15332,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Angioplasty, By-pass surgery, Open heart surgery, Sonography.','','','Sonography : It is a diagnostic technique while others are treatment methods of heart problems.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15333,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Disease effects the mental health of a person.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15334,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : BCG vaccine is given to a child at birth.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15335,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Different species of mosquitoes cause different diseases. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15336,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Increase in platelet count is a symptom of Dengue.','','','False. Decrease in platelet count is a symptom of dengue.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15337,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Typhoid spreads through (Aedes aegypti).','','','False. Typhoid spread through contaminated food and water and Dengue is spread by Aedes aegypti.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15338,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : First case of swine flu was reported in Germany. ','','','False. First case of swine flu was reported in Mexico.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15339,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : HIV was first reported in an African species of Monkey.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15340,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Hydrophobia is one of the symptoms of cholera.','','','False. Hydrophobia is one of the symptoms of Rabies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15341,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Rabies is a bacterial disease. ','','','False. Rabies is a viral disease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15342,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Uncontrolled diet helps to prevent some cancer.','','','False. Controlled diet helps to prevent some cancers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15343,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Africa has the largest number of diabetes patient.','','','False. India has largest number of diabetes patients in the world.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15344,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Overdose of antibiotics may lead to nausea, dysentery, etc.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15345,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Generic medicines are also known as general medicines.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15346,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Vaccination is important to prevent the infectious diseases. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15347,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : One can save the life of twenty persons by donating the blood for four times a year.','','','False. One can save the life of twelve persons by donating blood for four times a year.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15348,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Disturbances in physiological and psychological processes.','','','Diseases','',0,0,0,NULL),(15349,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Diseases where regular medication like DOTS needs to be taken','','','Tuberclosis','',0,0,0,NULL),(15350,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Disease that occurs due to the bite of infected dog or Monkey.','','','Rabies','',0,0,0,NULL),(15351,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Medicines without patent and with low production cost.','','','Generic medicines','',0,0,0,NULL),(15352,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Scheme of government to make available medicines at low prices.','','','Pradhan Mantri Jan Aushidhi Yojna','',0,0,0,NULL),(15353,26,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Sometimes, we feel good even without taking any medicines and sometimes we need to consult doctor and take regular medicines in sickness. Why is it so?','','','i) When the intensity of disease or sickness is very low, we don’t consult a doctor and feel better without any medication.\nii) But when we suffer from heavy infection related to any part of body; it is extremely important to consult a doctor who will prescribe proper medication.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15354,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What measures are taken by the BMC to control the population of mosquitoes?','','','Spraying DDT, spraying and fuming pesticides and throws awareness campaigns.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15355,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What scheme did the government of India declare about Pradhan Mantri Jan Aushadi Yojana?','','','In this scheme, best quality medicines are made available in affordable prices to the citizens.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15356,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What can be achieved/can be prevented? : Drinking boiled and filtered water','','','i) One can achieve good health by drinking boiled and filtered water.\nii) One can also keep away from diseases like Hepatitis, Dysentery, Cholera and Typhoid, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15357,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What can be achieved/can be prevented? : Avoiding smoking and alcoholism ','','','One will be free from cancer and heart attacks.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15358,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What can be achieved/can be prevented? : Regular balanced diet and Exercise ','','','i) One will remain fit and healthy; and lead a good healthy life.\nii) He/ She will be free from diseases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15359,26,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What can be achieved/can be prevented? : Proper checking of blood before blood donation.','','','One can prevent from the fatal diseases AIDS, if blood is screened before transfusion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15360,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the following questions : Master ‘X’ is a 3 years old child. He is living with his family in a slum. His father is drunkard. His mother does not know the importance of balanced diet.\ni) Master ‘X’ may suffer from which possible diseases in above conditions.         ii) How will you help him and his family in this situation?            iii) Which disease can occur to the father of master ‘X’','','','i) He may suffer from malnutrition, anaemia, weakness, stunted growth.\nii) a) We can direct them to medical institutions, give them training about clean healthy habits.\nb) An awareness program highlighting the importance of healthy nutritious food can be arranged.\niii) The father of master ‘X’ can suffer from Cancer, Heart attack, Liver failure.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15361,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How do the following disease spread? : i) Hepatitis 	ii) Malaria 		iii) Scabies 		iv) T. B.','','','i) Hepatitis : spreads through consumption of contaminated food and water\nii) Malaria : spreads whose female Anopheles mosquito\niii) Scabies : spreads whose skin- skin contact of an infected individual.\niv) T. B. :  spreads from one infected individual to other by air, ','',0,0,0,NULL),(15362,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How do the following disease spread? : i) Dengue 	         ii) Dysentery          	iii) Ringworm 	            iv) Swine flu','','','i) Dengue : virus spreads through DEN -  1, 2 virus.\nii) Dysentery : spreads through consumption of contaminated food and water.\niii) Ringworm : spreads through direct contact with infected person.\niv) Swine flu : spreads through infected pigs and humans.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15363,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write Pathogens, Mode of infection, Symptoms, Prevention and treatment of the given disease: Enteritis ','','','i) Pathogens : Serratia    \nii) Mode of infection : Contaminated food and water \niii) Symptoms : Abdominal pain, cramping, diarrhoea, dehydration and fever \niv) Prevention and treatment : Oral rehydration solution (ORS) can be made with proper diet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15364,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write Pathogens, Mode of infection, Symptoms, Prevention and treatment of the given disease : Malaria ','','','i) Pathogens : Plasmodium vivax \nii) Mode of infection : Female and Anopheles mosquito \niii) Symptoms : High fever, vomiting and headache \niv) Prevention and treatment : Use of mosquito nets or repellants followed by proper medication.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15365,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write Pathogens, Mode of infection, Symptoms, Prevention and treatment of the given disease :  Plague ','','','i) Pathogen : Yersinia Pestis\nii) Mode of infection : Rodents like rats or infected flea\niii) Symptom : Fever, chills, fatigue, headache \niv) Prevention and treatment : Proper dosage of antibiotics.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15366,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write Pathogens, Mode of infection, Symptoms, Prevention and treatment of the given disease : AIDS','','','i) Pathogens : HIV - virus \nii) Mode of infection : Sharing of needles, transfusion of blood, sexual contact ii) Symptoms : Large Lymph nodes, weight loss, etc.\niii) Prevention and treatment : Safe sex, no sharing of needles, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15367,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why the animal cages and their living places should not be near the kitchen?','','','i) Animal cages and their living places should not be near the kitchen because it make contaminate food.\nii) The hair or feather from animals or birds may enter food thus making it unfit for consumption.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15368,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Do you remember any person drinking the sugar free tea or avoiding consumption of sweets? What may be the possible reason for this?','','','Person drinking sugar free tea or avoiding consumption of sweets may probably be suffering from diabetes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15369,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Poor people do not afford the costly medicines. Is there any alternative for this? Which one?','','','i) Poor people do not afford the costly medicines\nii) So, they can choose for generic medicines\niii) Also, Government of India declared the Pradhan Mantri Jan Aushadi Yojana on 1st July 2015\niv) In this scheme, best quality medicines are made available in affordable prices to the citizens.\nv) ‘Jan Aushadi Stores’ are launched for this purpose.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15370,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which measures are taken for mosquito control by Grampanchyat, Municipal council, Municipality of your area.','','','The following measures can be taken for mosquito central by Grampanchayat, Municipal Council/ Municipality.\ni) Creating awareness in public to avoid pounding of water around their homes.                   ii) Fogging.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15371,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Observe the diagram and answer the following questions : i) Where do you see the water-logged materials?          ii) Which hazards do you anticipate with the help of picture?','','','i) a) Bucket	b) Tyre 	c) Container\nii) It may spread various diseases like Malaria, Dengue, Dysentry, Typhoid, Hepatitis, etc.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15373,26,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Collect the information from your grandparents about use of aloe vera, turmeric, ginger, garlic as medicines. ','','','i) Aloe vera  : It is the best natural medicine for healing of wounds, burns, etc. if we include this in our food, it will provide healthy immune and digestive system.\nii) Turmeric : It is also used for wound healing. Also, it helps to reduce cholesterol level and to manage diabetes.\niii) Ginger : It is good for digestion and for reducing acidity problems.\niv) Garlic :  For preventing heart diseases, including garlic in our food is the best method.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15374,26,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is disease? How are diseases classified? Explain with the help of flowchart. Name few diseases.','','','i) Condition of disturbances in physiological or psychological processes of body is called as disease.\nii) The names of various diseases are diabetes, common cold, asthma, Down’s syndrome, Cardiac problems.\niii) Diseases are classified as according to duration and according to reasons.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(15375,26,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are infectious disease? Give the pathogen of mode of infection, symptom and prevention and treatment of diseases like Tuberculosis, Hepatitis, Dysentery, cholera, Typhoid. ','','','Infectious diseases which spread through contaminated air, water, food or vectors are called as infectious diseases.','',0,0,1,NULL),(15376,26,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does swine flu spread? What are the symptoms of swine flu? Where is the diagnosis of swine flu done.','','','i) Swine Flu spread through pigs and humans \nii) The virus spreads through the sweat of infected person. Similarly, it spreads through secretions of nose, throat and saliva.\niii) The symptoms are difficulty in breathing, sore throat and body pain\niv) The diagnosis is carried on a National institute of Virology (NIV), Pune and National Institute of Communicable Diseases (NICD), Delhi.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15377,26,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does AIDS spread? State its signs, symptoms and the preventive measures?','','','i) AIDS is a medical condition caused by a virus Human Immune Deficiency Virus i.e. HIV. This virus weakens the human system.\nii) It spreads through : a) Unprotected sex with an infected person	b) Direct contact with infected blood	c) Sharing needles, especially drug abusers do suffer		d) An AIDS infected mother can transfer the disease to the baby 	e) Blood transfusion.\niii) Symptoms are :	a) Reddish spots on skin		b) fever and weakness	c) weakened immune system\niv) Preventive measures : a) To avoid sexual contact with the infected person	b) People should be educated about transmission of AIDS	c) Before blood transfusion it should be screened for HIV	d) There is no cure but prevention is the only way to stop it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15378,26,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is cancer? State the reasons, why one acquires it? What are the symptoms?','','','i) Cancer is an uncontrolled and abnormal cell growth. Group of lump of cancerous cells is called an malignant tumor.\nii) Cancer can occur in various organs like lungs, mouth, tongue, breast, uterus, skin and tissues like blood.\niii) Reasons : People consuming tobacco, gutkha, smoking, alcoholism, lack of fibre contains in food, excessive consumption of junk food and heredity are the main reasons.\niv) Symptoms are : a) Chronic cough, hoarse voice, difficulty in swallowing	b) incurable scar and inflammation	c) lumps in breast		d) unexplained weight loss.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15390,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Muscular force : Contact force : : Electrostatic force : ------','','','Non-contact force','',0,0,0,NULL),(15391,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Altitude increases : ------ : : Depth increases : Pressure increases','','','Pressure decreases','',0,0,0,NULL),(15392,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd one out : Electrostatic force, frictional force, gravitational force, magnetic force.','','','Frictional force : Frictional force is a contact force while others are non-contact force','',0,0,0,NULL),(15393,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Pressure is force per unit volume.','','','False : Pressure is force per unit area','',0,0,0,NULL),(15394,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The pressure inside an inflated balloon is equal to atmospheric pressure.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15395,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Fluids always flow from lower pressure to higher pressure region.','','','False. Fluids always flow from higher pressure to lower pressure region.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15396,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : If area is reduced pressure is reduced.','','','False. If area is reduced pressure is increased.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15397,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : As we go up higher and higher over the mountains, air pressure decreases. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15398,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : When diver jumps in water it is the force of friction which slows his motion.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15399,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Atmospheric pressure at the poles is greater than atmospheric pressure at the equator.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15400,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The SI unit of pressure is Newton.','','','False. The SI unit of pressure is Pascal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15401,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : By making a knife sharp, we decrease the area of contact and hence, the pressure increases.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15402,27,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The force exerted by a magnet is an example of contact force.','','','False. The force exerted by a magnet is an example of non-contact force.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15403,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The density of metal is 10.8 × 10³ kg/m³. Find the relative density of the metal.','','','Given : Density of a metal = 10.8 × 10³ kg/m³\nFind : Relative density of the metal\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(15404,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Volume of an object is 20 cm³ and the mass is 50 g. Density of water is 2 gm cm¯³. Will the object float on water or sink in water.','','','Given : Volume of an object = 20 cm³ , Mass = 50 gm\nFind : Density\nDensity = Mass/Volume = 50/20 = 2.5 gm/cm³\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15405,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The volume of a plastic covered sealed box is 350 cm³ and the box has a mass 500 gm. Will the box float on water? What will be the mass of water displaced by the box.','','','Given : Volume of the box = 350 cm³ , Mass of the box = 500 gm\nDensity = Mass/Volume = 500/350 = 1.42 gm/cm³\nAs Density of the box is more than that of water. So, the box will sink in the water.\n∴ Mass of water displayed by the box is equal to the volume of the box.\nThe mass of water displayed by the box is 350 g.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15406,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A stone of mass 6000 kg has a volume of 3 m³. Find its  i) density in kgm¯³ 	ii) density in gm¯³              	iii) Relative density ','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15407,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A solid weights 80 N. When placed on a surface, the area of contact is found to be 1.6 m². Find the pressure exerted by the solid on the surface. ','','','Given : Force = weight = 80 N, Area = 1.6 m² ; Find : Pressure\nPressure = Force/Area = 80/1.6 = 50 Pa\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15408,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of the bottom of a tiffin box is 0.25 m² and weight is 50 N, Calculate the pressure exerted by the box on the shelf.','','','Given : Area = 0.25 m², weight of the box = 50 N\nPressure = Force/Area = 50/0.25 = 200 N/m²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15409,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Calculate the relative density of iron if the density of water is 10³ kg/m³ and the density of iron is 7.85 × 10³ kg/m³.','','','Given : density of water = 10³ kg/m³, density of iron = 7.85 × 10³ kg/m³','',0,0,1,NULL),(15410,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of the tip of a screw is 0.5 mm² and its weight in 0.5 N. Calculate the pressure (in Pa) exerted by the screw on a wooden plank.','','','Given : Area = 0.5 × 10⁻⁶ m², Weight of the screw = 0.5 N','',0,0,1,NULL),(15411,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A piece of marble tile weighs 100 g in air. If its density is 2.5 g/cc, what will be its weighs in water?','','','Given : Weight in air = 100 g, Density = 2.5 g/cc\n∴ Volume = 100 g / (2.5 g/cc) = 40 cc\n∴ According to Archemedes principle, when the piece is dipped in water, water of volume equal to the volume of the piece i.e. 40 cc will be displaced. The loss in the weight of the piece will be equal to the weight of displaced water 40 g.\n∴ Weight in water = 100 g – 40 g = 60 g\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15412,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples each of situation in which you push or pull to change the state of motion of objects.','','','i) Goalkeeper saving a goal 		ii) Hockey player flicking a ball.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15413,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','An empty bottle floats on the surface of water while a bottle filled with water floats inside the liquid.','','','i) An empty water floats on the surface of water because its weight is negligible as compared with the weight of the water inside.\nii) The bottle full of water floats inside water but does not go to the bottom\niii) It means that the force due to gravity acting downwards (fg) is balanced by an opposing force in the upper direction (fb) on the bottle with water.\niv) This force is because of the water surrounding the bottle. These upward force acting on the object in water is called the Buoyant force (fb).\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15414,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','It is easy to cut vegetable, fruits with a sharp knife. A blunt knife does not work here. Why does this happen?','','','i) Effect of force increases when the area is small. While cutting fruits, vegetables, force is applied on handle of the knife.\nii) The force is transferred to the edge.\niii) Since the edge is sharp, tremendous pressure is exerted on a small area on the fruits and vegetables due to which they cut easily.\niv) In case of blunt knife, area is bigger; so, pressure will be less.\nv) Hence, it is difficult to cut vegetables or fruits by a blunt knife.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15415,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : A ship dips to a larger depth in fresh water as compared to marine water.','','','i) The density of marine water is more than the density of fresh water.\nii) Marine water exerts upward force because of high density, due to which the object sink to a shorter depth.\niii) Hence, ship dips to larger depth in fresh water as compared to marine water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15416,27,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : If a stationary bus suddenly speeds up passengers are thrown in the backward direction.','','','i) When bus suddenly starts the passengers fall back.\nii) This is because our body is initially at rest but when bus starts moving our feet which are in contact with the bus also tend to move forward, but our body shows inability to move duo to inertia and as a result it falls back.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15418,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mass of a block of metal is 10 kg and its dimensions are length 50 cm, breadth 10 cm, height 20 cm. If the metal block is placed on the following surface on the table, find out on which of the surface ABCD, CDEF and BCFG will the pressure on the table be maximum.','','','Weight of the block = mg = 10 × 9.8 N = 98 N, For the surface ABCD, length = 50 cm, height = 20 cm\nArea = 50 cm × 20 cm = 1000 cm² = 0.1 m² \nPressure = weight /area = 98/(0.1) = 980 Pa\nFor the surface BCFG , height = 20 cm, breadth = 10 cm\nArea = 20 cm × 10 cm = 200 cm² = 0.02 m² \nPressure = weight/area = 98 N/ 0.02 m² = 4900 Pa\n∴  Maximum pressure. Hence, smaller the area of contact, larger is the pressure.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15419,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the forces acting on a plastic bucket containing water held above ground level in your hand. Discuss why the forces acting on the bucket do not bring a change in its state of motion.','','','i) In this case, muscular forces acting in upward direction and the force of gravity is acting in downward direction.\nii) Both forces are acting in opposite direction to each other and hence nullify the effect of each other.\niii) Due to this, there is no change in the state of motion of the bucket.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15420,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define Buoyant force. State the factors on which the buoyant force depends?','','','i) Whenever a body is immersed partially or completely inside a fluid, it experiences an upward force. This upward force is called the Buoyant force or upthrust and the property of the fluids to applied upward force on the body immersed in it is called buoyancy.\nii) The buoyant force depends on two factors \niii) The volume of the object immersed \niv) The density of the fluid.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15421,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','State Archimedes Principle and based on their principle, write its two applications. ','','','i) When a body is partially or fully immersed in a fluid, a force of buoyancy acts on it in the upward direction. This force is equal to the weight of the fluid displayed by the object.\nii) Applications of Archimedes principle : a) Designing the ships and submarines	b) Lactometers which are used to determine the purity of a sample of milk		c) Hydrometers used for determining the density of liquids.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15422,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A plastic cube is released in water. Will it sink or come to the surface of water.','','','i) When a plastic cube is released in water. It will float on the surface of water.\nii) Density of plastic cube is less than the water\niii) More buoyant force is exerted on the plastic cube which lifts the cube upwards and allows it to plot on the surface.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15423,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','An 10 cm nail sinks in water, but why does the massive steel ship float on it?','','','i) Iron nail sinks in water because buoyant force acting on it is smaller than its weight.\nii) Buoyant force depends on the volume of dipping object Buoyant force is more if the volume of the dipping object is more.\niii) For steel ship, its volume is higher so more buoyant force is acting on it and thus it floats on the surface of the water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15424,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','You must have seen a vegetable vendor carrying a basket on her head. She keeps a twisted piece of cloth on the head below the basket. How does it help?','','','i) We know that, as the area is larger, lesser is the pressure.\nii) By keeping twisted piece of cloth, area increases so less pressure on head.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15425,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','We can not stand at one place for a long time. How then can we sleep on a place for 8 and odd hours?','','','i) We cannot stand at one place for a long time however we can sleep on a place for 8 and odd hours.\nii) Because while standing area is less and pressure is more, but while sleeping area increases and pressure becomes less.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15426,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','At the sea level the atmospheric pressure 101 × 103 Pa is acting on table top of size 1 m²? Under such a heavy pressure. Why doesn’t the table top crumble down?','','','i) Air exerts pressure equally from all directions on the table top.\nii) So, the table doesn\'t crumble down under heavy pressure since the effect of forces on table from all directions are balanced.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15427,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','While pulling a bucket from a well, the bucket full of water immersed fully in water appears to weigh less than when it has been pulled out of water. Why?','','','The bucket full of water immersed fully in water appears to weigh less as it experiences the upward force acting on the bucket or due to buoyant force it appears to weigh less rather than it has been pulled out of water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15428,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of some examples in which contact and non-contact forces are applied. Write the types of force.','','','i) Contact force is a force between two objects that is in contact with each other e.g. muscular force - exercises in gym, frictional force - rolling of ball on road, cycling - mechanical force.\nii) Non- contact force -  A non- contact force is any force applied to an object by another body that is not in direct contact with it.\nE.g. gravitational force- ball or any object when thrown upward.\nElectrostatic force - paper bits to the comb.\nMagnetic force - all the electronic devices, microphones, speaker.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15429,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','You have learned about static electricity. Electrostatic force is a non-contact force. To verify this, which experiment will you perform?','','','i) To verify electrostatic force, following experiment will be performed :\nii) Take a glass rod and a piece of silk cloth. Hold them close to small pieces of paper. None of them will attract the pieces of paper.\nii) Now rub the glass rod with the silk cloth. Bring the glass rod and the cloth slightly above the bits and silk cloth are attracting the pieces of paper.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15430,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain the observations in the experiment given according to the Archimedes Principle.','','','i) In the experiment given, length of the rubber band is shorter when immersed in water as compared to the length of rubber band hanging in air.\nii) The stone is sunk in water, a buoyant force acts in the upper direction\niii) The weight of the stone acts downwards\niv) Therefore, the force which acts on it in the downward direction is effectively reduced.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15431,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Answer the following questions : a) How is it decided that an object left in liquid will get in the liquid, will float on the surface, or will float inside the liquid?       b) Which forces are balanced in the above cases?\n','','','a) The object floats if buoyant force is larger than its weight.\nii) The object sinks if the buoyant force is smaller than its weight\niii) The object floats inside the liquid if buoyant force is equal to its weight.\nb) The forces in the first two cases (i) and (ii) are unbalanced.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15435,27,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given diagram, explain at which point pressure due to liquid will be minimum and maximum?\n','','','i) Pressure increases as the depth of the liquid increases from its surface\nii) As the depth of point 3 is minimum from the surface of the liquid the liquid will exert minimum pressure.\niii) Similarly, the depth of the point 1 being maximum from the surface of the liquid, the liquid will exert maximum pressure.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15436,27,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain with an experiment that pressure increases as the depth of the liquid increases.','','','i) Take a plastic bottle. Pierce it with a thick needle at the points 1, 2, 3 as shown in the figure. Fill water in the bottle up to full height.\nii) As shown in the figure, water jets will be seen emerging and projecting out.\niii) The water Jet emerging from the hole at the top will fall closest to the bottle.\niv) The jet from the lowest hole falls from the bottle.\nv) Also, jets coming out from the two holes at the same level fall at the same distance from the bottle.\nvi) That is at any one level, the liquid pressure is the same and the pressure increases as the depth of the liquid increases.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(15439,27,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the given figure and answer the following questions : i) What are the forces ‘A’ and ‘B’?          ii) Explain the reason behind floating of objects P and R.         iii) On which objects are the unbalanced forces acting? Which are they?','','','i) A denotes the buoyant force while B denote the gravitational force (weight of the bottle). \nii) The objects P and R are floating on the surface of the liquid because the buoyant force acting on them is larger than their weight. \niii)  The buoyant forces and the gravitational forces acting on objects P and R are unbalanced. \n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15441,27,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Answer the following questions : a) Define Buoyant force. A plastic cube is released in water will it sink or come to the surface of water.          b) If density of water is 10³ kg/m³ and density of copper is 7.5 × 10³ kg/m³. calculate relative density of copper.','','','a) i) The upward force exerted by a fluid (liquid or gas) on an object completely or partially immersed in it is called the buoyant force. \nii) An object sinks if the buoyant force is smaller than its weight and floats if the buoyant force is larger than its weight. \niii) The buoyant force acting on an object is large if the density of the liquid it is dipped in is large. \niv) Density of plastic is less than that of water. Hence, the buoyant force exerted by water on the plastic cube will be more than the weight of the plastic cube causing the plastic cube to come to the surface. Hence, a plastic cube released in water will come to the surface of water. \nb) Given: Density of copper = 7.52  103 kg/m³ , Density of water = 103 kg/m³ , To find: Relative density \nFormula: Relative density = Density of copper/ Density of water \nCalculation: From formula, Relative density = (7.52 ×10³)/ 10³  = 7.52 \nAns: The relative density of copper is 7.52\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15455,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Electric current : ------ : : Potential difference : Volt (V)','','','Ampere (A) : SI unit of potential difference is volt (V) and that of electric current is ampere (A).','',0,0,0,NULL),(15456,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Heat flow : temperature difference : : current flow : ------','','','Potential difference','',0,0,0,NULL),(15457,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Flow of protons constitutes an electric current.','','','False. Flow of electrons constitutes an electric current.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15458,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Bulb is the source of electricity in a torch.','','','False. Cell is the source of electricity in a torch','',0,0,0,NULL),(15459,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Silk threads are very good conductors.','','','False. Silk-threads are very bad conductors','',0,0,0,NULL),(15460,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : SI unit of current is Ampere.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15461,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The direction of flow of conventional current is from negative terminal to positive terminal. ','','','False. The direction of flow of conventional current is from positive terminal to the negative terminal','',0,0,0,NULL),(15462,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Electrons flow from negative terminal to positive terminal of the cell.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15463,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Electric current is a vector quantity.','','','False. Electric current is a scalar quantity','',0,0,0,NULL),(15464,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Ni-Cd cells store more electrical energy as compared to Lithium-ion cells.','','','False. Lithium ion cells store more electrical energy as compared to Ni-Cd cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15465,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Electromagnets are used to produce strong magnetic field','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15466,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Lead-Acid cells are used in cars, trucks, motorcycles.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15467,28,'descriptive','1b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The electric level, which decides the direction of flow of electric charges.','','','Electrostatic potential','',0,0,0,NULL),(15468,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : A source required to produce a uniform flow of charges in a circuit.','','','Electric cell','',0,0,0,NULL),(15469,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : It is used to obtain higher potential difference as compared to potential difference obtained with the help of single cell.','','','Battery','',0,0,0,NULL),(15470,28,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : It is formed by wounding a conductor (a copper wire) around an iron piece/screw.','','','Electromagnet','',0,0,0,NULL),(15471,28,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When electric current flows, what is the direction of motion of electrons in a dry cell?','','','In a dry cell, the electrons flow from the Zinc electrode towards the carbon rod, i.e. from negative terminal to the positive terminal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15472,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : The temperature of metallic conductor rises when current flows through it.','','','i) When current flows through a metallic conductor, the free electrons in the metal starts moving.\nii) When these free electrons move, they collide with the atoms of the matter.\niii) At each collision, a part of kinetic energy of the electron converts into heat and slowly the temperature of the conductor rises.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15473,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : An electromagnet is used instead of a permanent magnet in a big crane used to lift heavy loads.','','','i) An important advantage of an electromagnet is that if stronger currents are used in it, the strength of the magnet also goes on increasing.\nii) There are limitations to the strength of the permanent magnet.\niii) It is because of this property that electromagnets are used in big crains for moving heavy loads.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15474,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : In an electric bell, the electric current stops again and again.','','','i) Electric bell consists of an electromagnet, a flexible iron strip, gong, striker and contact screw.\nii) The electromagnet is placed in front of the flexible iron strip and the iron strip is in contact with the screw.\niii) When the iron strip is in contact with the screw, the circuit is complete and current flows.\niv) As a result, the electromagnet is magnetized and the iron strip is attached and the striker attached to it hits the gong.\nv) The circuit is now broken and hence no current flows and the strip comes back to its initial position.\nvi) Again, the current starts flowing and the entire process is again repeated. Hence, current stops again and again in an electric bell.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15475,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Magnetic needle changes its position if it is kept closed to electrical circuit. ','','','i) A magnetic needle is a magnet.\nii) The magnetic needle changes its direction when a bar magnet is taken near it.\niii) A magnetic field is created when an electric current flows in a wire.\niv) When an electric current passes through a wire, a magnetic field is produced around that wire.\nv) Hence, the magnetic needle changes its direction when a current starts flowing in the circuit.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15476,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Lithium cells are used instead of Ni-Cd cell in smartphones.','','','i) Lithium cell uses lithium metal ions which makes the cell lighter duo to low molecular weight.\nii) These cells can be recharged \niii) More electrical energy can be stored in these cells as compared to that in Ni-Cd cells.\niv) Hence, Lithium ion cells are used in modern equipment’s for example smartphone, laptop, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15477,28,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : In transistor radio 2-3 dry cells are connected in series.','','','i) The purpose of doing this is to obtain more potential difference than that of single cell.\nii) Therefore, it is possible to obtain higher current.\niii) If the cells are connected in series the connection of cells is known as a battery.\niv) Hence, in the transistor radio 2 - 3 dry cells are seen to be connected in series.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15478,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Electric cells having 2V potential difference each have been connected in the form of a battery. What will be the total potential difference of the battery in both cases?','','','i) 2 V + 2 V + 2 V = 6 V                  ii) 2 V + 2 V + 2 V + 2 V = 8 V\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15479,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Answer the following questions : a) What will happen if a glass rod is rubbed on a silk cloth?         b) How do objects get charge?           c) What are static and moving charges?','','','a) When a glass rod is rubbed on a silk cloth, the moving charges are transferred from the glass rod to the silk road.\nb) Depending on the pair of object, one way to get charge is to rub the objects against each other.\nc) The charges which are at rest are static charges while the charges which get transferred from one object to another are moving charges.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15480,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Answer the following questions : a) Moving negatively charged particles are electrons. Can this negative charge be made to flow?          b) Can electricity be made to flow like water flowing from higher to lower level?','','','a) Yes, negatively charged particles can be made to flow.\nb) Yes, electricity can be made to flow like water from higher potential to lower level.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15481,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when the two ends of a wire are connected to the terminal of a cell or a battery?','','','When two ends of a wire are connected to the terminals of a cell or a battery, an electric force acts on the free electrons present in the wire and the electron start moving from negative to the positive end of the cell or battery.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15482,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','You must have seen the car battery available in the market. It is called a battery and not a cell. Why?','','','i) In cars, a group of cells is used and this group of cells is called a battery.\nii) Its single cell is used then it is simply called a cell and if a group of cells is used then it is called as battery.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15483,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Take the inside tray of a used up match box and place a small magnetic needle inside the tray. Now take a long connecting wire and wind it around the tray. Complete the electric circuit by connecting in it, this wire, electric cell, plug and a bulb as shown in the figure below. Mark the position of the magnetic needle. Take a bar magnet near the magnetic needle.        \na) What do you observe?         b) Keep looking at the needle and close the plug key. The bulb will light up, and will realize that the current has started flowing. Does the magnetic needle changes its position?         c) Now open the plug key. Does the magnetic needle come back to the original position?             d) What will you conclude from this experiment?','','','a) When a bar magnet is taken near the magnetic needle, the direction of the magnetic needle is changed.\nb) Yes, the magnetic needle changes its position.\nc) Yes, the magnetic needle comes back to the original position.\nd) From this experiment it can be concluded that when an electric current passes through a wire, a magnetic field is produced around it.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15484,28,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A battery is to be formed by joining 3 dry cells with connecting wires. Show how will you connect the wires by drawing a diagram.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15488,28,'descriptive','4',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the proper word from the following group of words in the blanks : [magnetism, 4.5 V, 3.0 V, gravitational attraction, potential difference, potential, higher, lower, 0 V]\ni) Water in the waterfall flows from a higher level to the lower level because of --------.         ii) In an electric circuit, electrons flow from a point of ---- potential to the point of ------ potential.         iii) The difference between the electrostatic potential of the positive end and the negative end of an electric cells is the ------ of the cell.          iv) Three electric cells of potential difference 1.5 V each have been connected as a battery. The potential difference of the battery will be  ---- V.            v) An electric current flowing in a wire creates ----- around the wire.\n','','','i) Gravitational attraction	ii) higher, lower	iii) potential difference		iv) 4.5 	v) magnetism','',0,0,0,NULL),(15489,28,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Text Book','','In an electric circuit. A battery and a bulb have been connected and the battery consists of two cells of equal potential difference. If the bulb is not glowing, then which tests will you perform in order to find out the reason for the bulb not glowing? ','','','i) For any circuit to work, it should be closed. If the bulb is not glowing, then we should check whether circuit is closed or open.\nii) If Bulb is connected to positive terminal of one battery and negative terminal of another, then there is a closed a path for flow of electrons or current. Electrons always flow from negative to positive terminal of battery through circuit.\niii) The connections of in between the cells is also need to be checked. The positive terminal of one cell should be connected to the negative of the other.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15490,28,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Text Book','','Describe the construction, working and usefulness of a dry cell, with the help of a diagram.','','','Construction\ni) A dry cell consist of a thick zinc (Zn) case, which acts as a negative all of the cell.\nii) Inside the zinc case in the centre, there is a carbon rod which is the positive pole of the cell.\niii) Around carbon rod, there is a mixture of finely powdered manganese dioxide (MnO₂) and graphite held in a bag of thin material.\niv) The space between this bag and the outer cylindrical zinc cover is filled with a moist paste of Zinc chloride (ZnCl₂) and ammonium chloride (NH₄Cl)\nv) Then, the carbon rod is filled with a metal cap and cell is sealed.\nWorking\ni) Due to the chemical reactions of all chemical present in a dry cell, electrical charge is produced on the two terminals (graphite rod and zinc layer) and an electric current flow in the circuit.\nii) Because of the wet pulp used in the cell, the chemical reaction proceeds very slowly. Hence, a large electric current cannot be obtained from this.\niii) Compared to the electric cells using liquids, the life of dry cells is longer.\nUses\ni) Dry cells are very convenient to use as these can be held in any direction with respect to ground.\nii) It is used in a devices like electric toys, torches, transistor radios, any mobile instruments.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15491,28,'descriptive','4',5,2,'Text Book','','Describe the construction and working of an electric bell with the help of a diagram.','','','Construction \ni) An Electric bell consists of an insulated copper wire, which is coiled around the horse shoe shaped iron core.\nii) One end of the coil of wire is connected to one end of an electric cell. \niii) When electric current flows through the coil, it behaves like a magnet.\niv) A flexible iron strip is placed in front of this magnet.\nv) A metal striker is attached to this strip. This striker can strike a metal gong.\nvi) The other pole of the cell is connected to a contact screw through a switch. vii) The flexible metal strip is in contact with the screw.\nWorking\ni) When we press the switch, circuit is completed. When screw is in contact with the strip electric current flows through the coil making the core and electromagnet.\nii) At once, it attracts the flexible iron strip and the striker hits the gong and sound is produced.\niii) However, in this position the contact of the strip with the screw breaks and current stops.\niv) At this time, the coil loses its magnetism and flexible strip moves back and makes contact with the screw.\nv) This causes the current to flow again and again and striker hits the gong.\nvi) This make and break of current keeps the bell ringing till the switch is pressed.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15492,28,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Answer the following questions based on the given diagram : i) Name the cell shown in the given figure.      ii) Which metal acts as positive terminal of the cell?          iii) Which metal layer acts as negative terminal of the cell?           iv) What does label A represent?         v) What does the electrolyte in the given cell consist of?','','','i) The cell shown in the given diagram is dry cell. \nii) Graphite rod acts as positive terminal of the cell. \niii) Zinc metal layer acts as negative terminal of the cell. \niv) Label A represents paste of manganese dioxide (MnO₂). \nv) The electrolyte in dry cell consists of zinc chloride (ZnCl₂) and ammonium chloride (NH₄Cl).   \n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15501,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many types of subatomic particles are found in atom?\n','','','Three types of subatomic particles namely protons, neutrons and electrons are found in atom.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15502,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which discovery pointed out that an atom has internal models?','','','Thomson’s Theory point out that an atom has internal models.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15503,129,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by electronic configuration of an element?  ','','','The shell wise distribution of the electrons in an atom of an element is called the electronic configuration of that element.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15537,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Gold, Silver, Copper, Brass','','','Brass. It is an alloy while rest are elements.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15538,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Hydrogen, Hydrogen peroxide, Carbon dioxide, Water vapor.\n','','','Hydrogen. It is an element while the rest are compounds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15539,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Milk, Lemon juice, Carbon, Steel','','','Carbon. It is element while rest are the mixture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15540,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Water, Mercury, Bromine, Petrol','','','Petrol. As it is a mixture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15541,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Sugar, Salt, Baking soda, Blue vitriol.','','','Sugar. It is organic compound while the rest are inorganic compound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15542,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Hydrogen, Sodium, Potassium, Carbon','','','Carbon. It is tetravalent element while the rest are monovalent element.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15543,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Seawater, Blue Vitriol, Vinegar, Sugar syrup','','','Vinegar. It is a liquid in a liquid type of solution while rest are solid in liquid type of solution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15544,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Chlorophyll, Hemoglobin, Baking soda, Cyanocobalamin','','','Baking soda. It is a compound while the rest are complex compound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15545,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Chlorophyll : Magnesium : : Hemoglobin : ------','','','Iron','',0,0,0,NULL),(15546,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Cyanocobalamin : ------ : : Chlorophyll : Magnesium','','','Cobalt','',0,0,0,NULL),(15547,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy :  Limestone : Inorganic compounds : : Urea : -----','','','Organic compound','',0,0,0,NULL),(15548,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy :  Cyanocobalamin : ------ : : Glucose : Organic compound  \n','','','Complex compound','',0,0,0,NULL),(15549,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Copper + zinc : Brass : : ----- : Bronze','','','Copper + Tin','',0,0,0,NULL),(15550,130,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: NaHCO₃ : Compound : : Sn : -------','','','Element','',0,0,0,NULL),(15551,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Copper : Element : : Brass : ------','','','Mixture','',0,0,0,NULL),(15555,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : A compound contains only one type of atoms.\n','','','False. A compound is formed when two or more elements combine. Hence, in a compound there can two or more types of atoms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15556,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Two different elements may have similar atoms.\n','','','False. Two different elements have dissimilar atoms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15557,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : A solution is a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15558,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Ink is a compound.\n','','','False. Ink is a colloid.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15559,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Carbon gives harness to steel.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15560,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : In a mixture, the constituents are in a fixed proportion. \n','','','False. In a mixture, the constituents are not in fixed proportions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15561,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : In a solution, the proportion of solute is more than that of solvent.\n','','','False. In a solution, the proportion of solute is less than that of solvent.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15562,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Water is a mixture.\n','','','False. Water is a compound of hydrogen and oxygen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15563,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Heterogeneous mixture of a liquid and a solid is called a suspension.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15564,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Molecular formula indicates the number of atom of each of the constituent elements present in the compound. \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15565,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Liquid element.','','','Bromine, Mercury','',0,0,0,NULL),(15566,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Gaseous element ','','','Oxygen, Chlorine','',0,0,0,NULL),(15567,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Solid element','','','Carbon, Iron','',0,0,0,NULL),(15568,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Homogeneous mixture','','','Plastic, Pure water','',0,0,0,NULL),(15569,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Colloid ','','','Milk, ink, blood','',0,0,0,NULL),(15570,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Organic compound','','','Urea, glucose','',0,0,0,NULL),(15571,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Complex compound','','','Chlorophyll, Hemoglobin','',0,0,0,NULL),(15572,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Inorganic compound','','','Baking soda, Blue vitriol','',0,0,0,NULL),(15573,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Metalloid ','','','Arsenic, Silicon','',0,0,0,NULL),(15574,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Elements with valency 1','','','Sodium, Potassium','',0,0,0,NULL),(15575,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two example each : Elements with valency 2','','','Magnesium, Calcium','',0,0,0,NULL),(15576,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the various states of matter?','','','Solid, Liquid, Gas, Plasma, Bose-Einstein condensate.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15577,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the difference in ice, water and steam?','','','Ice exists in Solid state; Water exist in Liquid state and Steam exist in vapour (gaseous) state.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15578,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the smallest particle of matter called?','','','The smallest particles of matter are called atom.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15579,130,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the types of matter?','','','The three types of matter are element, compound and mixture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15580,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Element','','','An element is a type of matter composed of only one kind of substance where each smallest individual part of it has the same proportion. An element is a substance which cannot be further subdivided into simpler substances by any physical or chemical means.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15581,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Compound','','','A compound is a substance formed when two or more elements are combine chemically in a fixed proportion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15582,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Mixture ','','','A mixture is a substance formed in two or more elements of a compounds which do not react chemically mixed with each other in any proportion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15583,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Organic compounds','','','The compounds containing carbon that are typically found in living system. For example : Sugar, camphor, glucose. In these compounds carbon remain behind as residue.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15584,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Inorganic compounds','','','An inorganic compound is a compound that locks a carbon atom. The compounds that decompose on strong heating to leave a residue behind are called inorganic compounds. For example, common salt, soda, rust, blue vitriol, limestone.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15585,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Homogeneous mixture','','','A homogeneous mixture is a solid, liquid or gases mixture that has the same proportions of its components throughout any given sample. For example, Air, solution of sugar in water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15586,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Heterogeneous mixture','','','A mixture is a combination of two or more pure substances in which the original substances retain their chemical properties. For example, solution of oil and water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15587,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Solution ','','','A homogeneous mixture of two or more substances is called solution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15588,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Solvent','','','The component of a solution which is present in the largest proportion is called solvent.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15589,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Solute','','','The component of a solution which is present in less proportion that the solvent are called solute.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15590,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Suspension','','','Homogeneous mixture of a solid and a liquid is called a suspension.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15591,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Valency of the atom','','','The ability of joining to another atom with a chemical bond is a chemical property of each atom. This ability is indicated by a number and this number is called valency of that atom.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15592,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Compound and Mixture ','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15593,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Element and Compound','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15594,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Homogeneous mixture and Heterogeneous mixture','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15595,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between :  Solution and Colloid','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15596,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between :  Metal and Non-metals ','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15597,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Hydrogen is combustible, oxygen helps combustion, but water helps to extinguish fire.\n','','','i) Water is a compound of hydrogen and oxygen. Hydrogen burns with a mild explosion and oxygen supports burning.\nii) But water neither burns nor supports burning, as the properties of compound is different from its constituents element.\niii) Hence, hydrogen is combustible, oxygen helps combustion, but water helps to extinguish fire.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15598,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Constituent substances of a colloid cannot be separated by ordinary filtration. \n','','','i) A collide is a heterogeneous mixture where the size of the particles are very small.\nii) The diameter of particles is very small around 10⁻⁵m.\niii) They appear homogeneous when placed in solution.\niv) The ordinary filter paper have larger pores than that of the collide.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15599,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Lemon sharbat has sweet , sour and salty taste and it can be poured in a glass. ','','','i) Lemon sharbat is a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances like salt, sugar, water and lemon juice.\nii) The particles of sugar and salt is completely soluble in lemon juice and water and are spread uniformly in it.\niii) Taste is also same throughout, thus indicating the homogeneity of the particles. Lemon sharbat is a solution. Hence, can be poured in a glass.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15600,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : A solid matter has the properties of definite shape and volume.','','','i) The molecules of solid are held together so tightly as they are very close to each other, their movement is limited.\nii) The attraction between these molecules i.e. intermolecular force is very strong which gives them definite shape and volume.\niii) Hence, a solid matter has the properties of definite shape and volume.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15601,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Air is a mixture.','','','i) Air consist of nitrogen, Oxygen, carbon dioxide, water vapour, etc. Nitrogen is the solvent and others are solute.\nii) These gases retain their original properties. They do not chemically combine with each other on their own. The properties of the constituents vary.\niii) Hence, air is a mixture.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15602,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : An alloy is a mixture.','','','i) An alloy is obtained when two metals or a metal and non-metal are fused.\nii) The components of an alloy do not react chemically and they retain the properties of its constituents and can have variable composition.\niii) Thus, alloys are homogeneous mixtures of two or more metals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15603,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following matter into three groups. Cold drink, air, sharbat, soil, wood, water, cement.\n','','','Compound – Cement, water; Mixture – Cold drink, air, sherbat, soil : Element – Wood','',0,0,0,NULL),(15604,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the states of matter that you used as criteria for the above classification?\n','','','Compound, mixture and element was the criteria used.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15605,130,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following chart :','','','i) Liquid in liquid	ii) Gas in gas		iii) Solid in solid	iv) Gas in liquid','',1,0,0,NULL),(15606,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the composition of the following materials by mean of chemical formulae and classify them accordingly :\n','','','i) Compound	ii) Element	iii) Element 		iv) Mixture 	v) element		vi) Mixture 		vii) Compound','',1,0,0,NULL),(15607,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Molecular formulae of some compound are given in the following table. Use these to fill in the gaps in the table :\n','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(15608,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the following diagram which shows the submicroscopic picture of the physical states of matter :   i) Arrange the physical states of matter : solid, liquid and gas in decreasing order of the interparticle distance.         ii) In which physical state of matter, the intermolecular force is strongest?        iii) Explain why particles of gas move freely and occupy all the available space.\n','','','i) The decreasing order of the interparticle distance is : Gas > Liquid > Solid\nii) The intermolecular force is strongest in the solid state.\niii) The intermolecular force of attraction between the particles of gas is very weak. Hence, particles of gas move freely and occupy all the available space.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15610,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the diagrams given below which represents different samples of substances and answer the following questions :  a) Which figure represents elements?         b) Which figures represent compounds?          c) Which figures represent mixtures?         d) Write any two characteristics of the sample represented by figure (E).        e) Name the type of matter formed when sample (F) is added to sample (D)','','','a) Figures (A) and (C) represent elements as all the atoms in a molecule of these samples are alike.\nii) Figures (D) and (F) represent compounds as the atoms in a molecule of these samples are different.\niii) Figures (B) and (E) represent mixtures as the molecules of these samples are different from each other.\niv) The sample represented by figure (E) is a mixture. Its characteristics are : a) The proportion of constituent substances in a mixture can change.	b) The properties of constituent substances are retained in a mixture.\nv) When sample (F) is added to sample (D), a mixture is formed.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15611,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Select the appropriate options and complete the following paragraph. : [cloud, translucent, scattered, heterogeneous, Milk, Smoke, filtration, homogeneous, transparent] \nThe mixture of water and milk is ------ means that when incident light passes through this mixture, it is partly transmitted and partly -------. Such a ----- mixture is called a colloid. This mixture cannot be separated by ------- using ordinary filter paper. ------- itself is a colloid, in which solid and liquid particles of proteins, fats etc., are dispersed in the aqueous medium. ------- is another example of colloid which is of the type ‘solid in gas’.\n','','','translucent, scattered, heterogeneous, filtration, milk, smoke','',0,0,0,NULL),(15612,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table by writing the molecular formula of the compounds formed from the given pairs of elements and their valences :\n','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(15615,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain why milk is colloid.','','','i) In the milk solid and liquid particles of proteins, fats, etc. having a diameter around 10⁻⁵ m. are dispersed in the aqueous medium.\nii) It is a heterogeneous mixture and the components can not be separated by filtration using ordinary filter paper.\niii) When incident light passes the colloid, it is partly transmitted and partly scattered.\niv) Therefore, milk is a colloid.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15616,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the term :  Molecular formula  ','','','i) A definite proportion of the constituent element in a compound.\nii) Certain number of atom of the constituent elements are joined to each other in a molecule of a compound.\niii) Molecular formula indicates the number of atom of each of the constituent element present in a molecule of a compound.\niv) A molecular formula includes the information regarding the symbols of the all the constituent element and their respective number as subscripts.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15617,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Plants synthesize glucose in sunlight with the help of chlorophyll from carbon dioxide and water and give away oxygen. Identify the four compounds in this process and name their types.\n','','','For compounds in photosynthesis are chlorophyll, carbon dioxide, glucose and water.\n chlorophyll type : Complex compounds ; Carbon dioxide type : Molecular compound ;  Glucose type : Organic compounds ; Water type : Molecular compound.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15618,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In one sample of brass, the following ingredients were found. Copper (70%) and zinc (30%). Identify the solvent, solute and solution from these. \n','','','In one sample of brass – copper (70%) and zinc (30%) were found. In this copper is solvent as it is present in largest proportion and Zinc is solute as it is present in lesser proportion. The solution is brass as it is a mixture of two metals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15619,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sea water tastes salty due to the dissolved salt. The salinity (The proportion of salt in water) of some water bodies. Lonar lake – 7.9%, Pacific ocean – 3.8%, Dead sea – 33%. Explain two characteristics of mixture from the above information.  \n','','','Seawater tastes salty due to dissolved salt. The salinity is different for different water bodies as given in the information. Sea water is a mixture as it contains many salts. In mixture, the proportion of the constituents is not fixed and the constituents retain their individual properties.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15620,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the term solvent with an example.','','','i) The component of a solution, which is present in the largest proportion is called solvent.\nii) For examples, when lot of common salt is dissolved in 100 ml water, a salt solution is formed. As water is present in the largest proportion, it is the solvent.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15621,130,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is suspension. ','','',') i) A Heterogeneous mixture of a liquid and a solid is called a suspension.\nii) The diameter of a solid particle in a suspension is larger than 10⁻⁴m.\niii) The particles of suspension do not pass through ordinary filter paper. Hence, its components can be separated by filtration.\niv) Light cannot transmit through suspension.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15622,130,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Deduce the molecular formulae of compound obtained from the following pairs of elements by the cross multiplication method. : i) C (Valency 4) and Cl (valency 1) 	       ii) N (valency 3) and H (valency 1)         iii) C (valency 4) and O (valency 2)	           iv) Ca (Valency 2) and O (valency 2)\n','','','i) C (Valency 4) and Cl (valency 1)\nC	Cl\n↘1    4↙           ∴ Molecular formula - CCl₄\nii) N(Valency 3) and H(valency 1)\nN    	    H\n ↘1       3↙         ∴ Molecular formula - NH₃\niii) C (valency 4) and O (valency 2)\nC	  O\n↘2	4↙		∴ Molecular formula - CO₂\niv) Ca (Valency 2) and O (Valency 2)\nCa               O\n↘2          2↙		∴ Molecular formula - Ca₂O₂\nDividing by 2 we get, CaO\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15624,130,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the following giving examples : a) Colloid 	b) Suspension','','','a) Colloid :\ni) Collide is a heterogeneous mixture where the particle size is less than the size in a suspension.\nii) The two components of a colloid are in the dispersed phase and dispersion medium.\niii) For example, in milk solids is the dispersed phase and water is the dispersion medium. In smoke, carbon is the dispersed phase and air is the dispersion medium.\niv) The particles of the colloid cannot be seen with the naked eye \nv) However, they can easily scatter the beam of light.\nb) Suspension :\ni) Suspension are heterogeneous mixtures.\nii) The solute and solvent exist in a distinct phases.\niii) The solute is not soluble in the solvent and they remains suspended.\niv) Their particle size is greater than 100 nm.\nv) It can be seen with the naked eyes. They scatter a beam of light passing through it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15625,130,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Deduce the molecular formulae of the compound obtained from the following pairs of elements by the cross multiplication method. : i) H (Valency 1) and O (valency 2) 	          ii) N (valency 3) and H (Valency 1)         iii) Fe (Valency 2) and S (Valency 2)\n','','','i) H (valency 1) and O (Valency 2)\nH		O\n↘2	         1↙ 	∴ Molecular formula - H₂O\nii) N (Valency 3) and H (valency 1)\nN		H\n↘1            3↙	∴ Molecular formula - NH₃\niii) Fe (Valency 2) and S (valency 2)\nFe		S\n↘2       2↙              	∴ Molecular formula - Fe₂S₂\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15626,130,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The valences of the atom H, O and N are 2 and 3 respectively. The molecular formulae of these gaseous elements are H₂, O₂ and N₂ respectively. how many chemical bonds are there in each of these molecules?\n','','','i) The valency of H is 1. Hence, the number of chemical bonds between two atoms of hydrogen in H₂ molecule is 1.\nii) The valency of O is 2. Hence, the number of chemical bonds between two items of oxygen in O₂ molecule is 2.\niii) The valency of N is 3. Hence, the number of chemical bonds between two atoms of nitrogen in N₂ molecule is 3.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15627,130,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the names and symbols of the constituent elements and identify their valences from the molecular formulae given below : KCl, HBr, MgBr₂, K₂O, NaH, CaCl₂, CCl₄, HI, H₂S, Na₂S, FeS, BaCl₂\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15628,130,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(15661,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the three types in which the elements are generally classified?','','','Elements are generally classified into three types : metal, non – metals and metalloids.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15662,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','You might have seen a doctor’s pressure gauge to measure blood pressure, during your visit to a dispensary, accompanying your relative. In that, you might have seen a grey coloured liquid in a glass tube. What is that metal? \n','','','The grey coloured liquid in a doctor’s pressure gauge to measure blood pressure is Mercury (Hg).','',0,0,0,NULL),(15663,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the metals and non-metals that we use in everyday life?','','','Metals : Copper, iron, gold, silver, aluminum, magnesium, calcium, platinum\nNon-metals : oxygen, Nitrogen, Carbon, Diamond\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15664,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain while studying chemical properties of metals it is formed that gold or silver do not react readily. \n','','','i) Noble metals do not take part in chemical reactions because they are less reactive metals.\nii) So they are resistant to corrosion and oxidation in moist air.\niii) Gold and silver being noble metals, do not react readily.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15665,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name two metals which have low density.','','','Potassium (K) and lithium (Li) have low density.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15666,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the electronic configuration of the following metals : a) ₁₂Mg                 	b) ₁₉K\n','','','a) (2, 8, 2)		b) (2, 8, 8, 1)','',0,0,0,NULL),(15667,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is done to prevent corrosion of metals?','','','To prevent corrosion layers of oil, grease, varnish and paint are applied on metals. Also, plating with another non-corroding metal is done.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15668,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name a metal which is soft and a non-metal which is hard.','','','Soft metal – Sodium, Hard metal – Diamond','',0,0,0,NULL),(15669,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name a non-metal which is a good conductor of electricity. ','','','Graphite is a non-metal which is a good conductor of electricity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15670,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the metals that make the alloys bronze and brass?','','','The alloy bronze is found from copper and tin, alloy brass is formed from copper and zinc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15671,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Gold, Silver, iron, diamond','','','Diamond. It is a non-metal while, the rest are metals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15672,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Ductility, brittleness, sonority, malleability ','','','Brittleness. It is the property of non-metal while, rest are the properties of non-metals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15673,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Carbon, bromine, Sulphur, phosphorous ','','','Bromine. It exists in liquid state while, the other element exist in solid state.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15674,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Brass, bronze, iron, steel','','','Iron. It is a metal, while, the rest are alloys.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15675,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Iron, copper, sodium, calcium','','','Sodium. Sodium is a soft metal while others are hard metal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15676,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Copper, aluminum, iron, mercury','','','Mercury. Mercury is in liquid state at room temperature while others are in solid state at room temperature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15677,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Sodium, potassium, Lithium, magnesium ','','','Magnesium. Magnesium has high density while others have low density than water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15678,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : H₂, N₂, O, Br₂','','','Br₂ : Br₂ is in the liquid state while others are in gaseous state.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15679,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Arsenic, Silicon, Gallium, Germanium','','','Gallium. It is a metal while, the rest are metalloids.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15680,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Platinum, Palladium, Rhodium, Aluminum','','','Aluminium. It is a less malleable metal while, the rest are highly malleable metals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15681,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Magnesium : Metal : : Silicon : ------','','','Metalloids','',0,0,0,NULL),(15682,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Na : 2, 8, 1 : : N : ------','','','2, 5','',0,0,0,NULL),(15683,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Metals form : ------ : : Non-metal form : Anions ','','','cation','',0,0,0,NULL),(15684,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Sulphur : Yellow : : Bromine : ------','','','brown','',0,0,0,NULL),(15685,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy :  Diamond : ----- : : Graphite : brittle ','','','hardest','',0,0,0,NULL),(15691,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Lead metal is a good conductor of heat and electricity.','','','False. Lead metal is a neither a good conductor of heat nor a good conductor of electricity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15692,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Generally metals have high melting points and boiling points.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15693,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Non-metals do not react with dilute acids.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15694,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Graphite is a good conductor of heat.','','','False. Graphite is a good conductor of electricity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15695,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Metal oxides are acidic in nature.','','','False. Metal oxides are basic in nature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15696,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Most of the movements have 4 to 7 electrons in their valence shell.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15697,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Oxides of non-metals are acidic in nature. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15698,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Pure gold is very hard.','','','False. Pure gold is soft.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15699,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Silver has antibacterial property.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15700,131,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Metals produce sound on striking.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15701,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Metalloid ','','','An element which shows properly of both metals as well as those of non- metals is called a metalloid. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(15702,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Ductility ','','','The property due to which a substance can be drawn into a thin wire is called ductility.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15703,131,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Alloy','','','An alloy is a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals or a homogeneous mixture of metal with non-metals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15704,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Corrosion ','','','Gases in the air react with metals in presence of moisture to form metal compounds, this gradually destroys the metal and is called corrosion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15705,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Malleability ','','','The property due to which a substance can be drawn into a thin sheet is called malleability.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15706,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Corrosion ','','','i) Gases in the air react with metals in presence of moisture to form metal compounds.\nii) The metals get affected by this reaction and undergo a process called corrosion.\niii) For example : A reddish coloured deposit of iron oxide is formed on iron by reaction with oxygen present in the air.\niv) To prevent corrosion of metals layers of oil, grease, varnish and paint are applied on them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15707,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Metalloids','','','i) Some elements such as arsenic (As), Silicon (Si), Germanium (Ge), Antimony (Sb) have properties which are intermediate between metals and nonmetals. \nii) Such elements are called metalloids.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15708,131,'descriptive','2',2,1,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Alloy ','','','i) A homogeneous mixture of two or more metals or a homogeneous mixture of metal with non- metals is called alloy.\nii) Alloys are made by mixing the constituent elements in as per the requirement.\niii) For example, the stainless steel utensils used at home are made of an alloy of iron with carbon, chromium and nickel.\niv) The alloy bronze is formed from copper and tin.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15709,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is done to prevent corrosion of metals?','','','i) To prevent corrosion of metals, layers of oils, grease, varnish and paint are applied on metals.\nii) Also electroplating with another noncorroding metal is done on some metals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15710,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the metals that make the alloys brass and bronze?','','','i) Brass is an alloy of metals copper and zinc \nii) The alloy bronze is formed from copper and tin.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15711,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the metal that your school bell made of? How does a bell function?\n','','','i) School bells are made up of bronze which is an alloy of copper and tin. \nii) Since metals are Sonorous, they produce sound on striking.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15712,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which metals are used to make electric wires?','','','Copper and Aluminium are the metals used to make electrical wires.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15713,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the colour of carbon in the form of coal? ','','','Colour of carbon in the form of coal is black.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15714,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : The stainless steel vessels in kitchen have cooper coating on the bottom. \n','','','i) Copper is a better conductor of heat than the stainless steel.\nii) The stainless-steel racers in kitchen have copper coating at the bottom to get heated a faster.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15715,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Copper and brass vessels are cleaned with lemon.','','','i) Copper reacts with moist carbon dioxide in air to form copper carbonate, as a result of copper and brass vessels lose their shiny surface.\nii) The citric acid present in lemon neutralizes the copper carbonate.\niii) This is the reason why copper and brass vessels are cleaned with lemon.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15716,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Sodium is kept in kerosene.','','','i) Sodium is a highly reactive metal and reacts with water vapour present in air to form sodium hydroxide and hydrogen.\nii) The hydrogen released catches fire in the presence of oxygen.\niii) Therefore, sodium is kept immersed in kerosene to prevent their contact with air and moisture. Also, sodium does not react with kerosene.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15717,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Vertical cracks are seen in stainless steel vessels. ','','','i) A cheap variety of stainless steel is made sometimes by using copper instead of costly nickel.\nii) Hence, vertical cracks are seen in stainless steel vessels.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15718,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : The iron pillar of Kutubminar in Delhi is lustrous even after so many years.\n','','','i) The iron pillar of Kutubminar in Delhi is made from an alloy of iron. \nii) It contains small proportion of carbon silicon and phosphorus mixed in iron. Iii) Hence, the pillar is lustrous even after so many years.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15719,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : The statue of Liberty in New York City looks green.','','','The Statue of Liberty in New York City was made of copper and now it looks green because green colour copper carbonate has been formed by the reaction of copper with carbon dioxide and moisture in the air.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15720,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Metal and Non-metals (based on physical properties)\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15721,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Metal and Non-metal (based on chemical properties)\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15722,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when : Iron comes in contact with air.','','','A reddish coloured deposit of iron oxide is formed on iron by the reaction with oxygen present in the air.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15723,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when : Copper comes in contact with air.','','','A greenish coloured deposit of copper carbonate is formed on copper by the reaction with carbon dioxide gas present in the air.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15724,131,'descriptive','2',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when : Silver comes in contact with air.','','','A blackish coloured deposit of silver sulphate is formed on silver by the reaction with hydrogen sulphide gas present in the air.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15725,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the reaction with balanced equation: Sodium reacts with water.','','','When sodium reacts with water, sodium hydroxide and hydrogen gas is formed.\n2Na  + 2H₂O   → 2NaOH  + H₂↑\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15726,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of metals that have low melting point and boiling point.\n','','','Mercury (Hg), Gallium (Ga), Sodium (Na), Potassium (K)','',0,0,0,NULL),(15727,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How would you distinguish between samples of metals and nonmetals using hammer?\n','','','i) If a sample breaks into pieces on hammering, that means it is brittle and hence, it is a nonmetal.\nii)  If a sample flattens to form a sheet, that means it is malleable and hence, it is a metal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15728,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of metalloids.','','','Arsenic (As), Silicon (Si), Germanium (Ge), Antimony (Sb)','',0,0,0,NULL),(15729,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of noble metals?','','','Gold, Silver, Platinum, Palladium','',0,0,0,NULL),(15730,131,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is an alloy? How is it prepared? ','','','i) A homogeneous mixture of two or more metals or a homogeneous mixture of metal with nonmetals is called an alloy.\nii) Alloys are prepared by mixing the constituent elements as per the requirement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15731,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the table :','','','1) Electrical wires of copper and aluminum\n2) Gold and silver ornaments, aluminum foil\n3) Copper coating on the bottom of stainless steel utensils\n4) Electrical wires of cooper and aluminium \n5) School bells\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15732,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the adverse effects of corrosion?','','','Gases present in the air react with metals in the presence of moisture to metal compounds. The metal get affected by this reaction and undergo a process called corrosion.\ni) Loss of efficiency of the metallic equipment’s. \nii) Contamination of products.\niii) Inability to use metallic materials.\niv) Loss of valuable materials by blockage of pipes and mechanical damage of underground water pipes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15733,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the uses of noble metals?','','','Uses of noble metals : i) Gold, silver and platinum are used to prepare ornaments.\nii) Silver used in medicines\niii) Gold and silver also used to make medals\niv) Gold and silver also used to make few electronic devices\niv) Platinum, palladium metals are used as catalyst in various chemical reaction.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15734,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the statement : To prepare ornaments 22 carat gold is used.','','','i) Gold is a noble metal in nature it occurs in elemental form.\nii) 100% pure gold is of 24 carat\niii) Pure gold is soft, therefore, ornaments prepared by pure gold may break or bend due to pressure.\niv) Hence, goldsmith add specific amount of copper or silver in pure gold\nv) 22 carat gold means 22 parts of the ornaments is gold and the remaining 2 parts are other metals \nvi) This makes the ornament harder and durable.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15735,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Have you ever gone to goldsmith’s shop? Have you seen the goldsmith making a wire of gold or silver?\n','','','Yes, when gold or silver is pulled through hole its wire is formed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15737,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do scrap dealers do with the scrap? Why is this needed?','','','i) Scrap dealers collect scrap and sell it an profit.\nii) They have an important role in the recycling of metals\niii) The collected scrap is recycled to produce new products\niv) Metal recycling reduces solid waste and pollution in the environment. It conserves natural resources also.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15738,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Take copper vessels at your home. Scrub them with lemon and rinse with water. Observe the luster before and after cleaning. \n','','','i) The copper vessels appear dull before cleaning.\nii) On scrubbing these vessels with lemon and rinsing with water, they shine as well as reflect light.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15739,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Take coal and hammer it. See what happens to it.','','','When the coal is hammered, it breaks into pieces coal is brittle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15740,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Take dilute hydrochloric acid in a test tube. Then add zinc dust to it. Take a glowing splinter near the mouth of the tube. Observe the glowing splinter.\n','','','i) On bringing the glowing splinter near the mouth of the test tube of a pop sound comes out from it.\nii) Zinc reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to form salt and hydrogen gas.\niii) Word equation : Metal + dilute HCl → Salt + Hydrogen gas ↑\niv) When glowing splinter is brought near to it, hydrogen gas burns with pop sound.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15741,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a list of uses of metals and nonmetals in our everyday life.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15742,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a list of examples of corrosion in your daily life.','','','i) Rusted Nails : This is due to rusting of iron. By reaction with Oxygen gas a reddish colour deposits is formed on iron.\nii) Blackened Silver Articles : A blackish coloured deposit is formed on silver by reaction with hydrogen sulphide gas in air.\niii) Green coloured copper vessels : A greenish coloured deposit of copper carbonate is formed on copper by reaction with CO₂ gas in air.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15743,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of balanced chemical equations. Explain the following chemical reactions : a) Magnesium combines with oxygen.          b) Magnesium oxide reacts with dilute HCl\n','','','a) Magnesium combines with oxygen to form magnesium oxide.\n2Mg   + O₂  →  2 MgO\nb) Magnesium oxide reacts with dilute HCl magnesium chloride (salt) and water are formed.\nMgO   +   2HCl  →   MgCl₂  + H₂O\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15744,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of balanced chemical equations. Explain the following chemical reactions : a) Magnesium reacts with dilute HCl            b) Carbon burns in air\n','','','a) When magnesium reacts with dilute HCl, magnesium chloride is formed and hydrogen gas is evolved.\nMg₍ₛ₎  + 2HCl₍ₐ₎  →  MgCl₂₍ₐ₎  + H₂₍g₎ \nb) When carbon burns in air, carbon dioxide is formed.\nC + O₂  → CO₂\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15745,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of balanced chemical equations. Explain the following chemical reactions : a) Carbon dioxide reacts with sodium hydroxide.           b) Carbon dioxide reacts with water.\n','','','a) When carbon dioxide reacts with sodium hydroxide, soluble salt sodium carbonate and water are formed.\nCO₂  + 2NaOH  → Na₂CO₃  + H₂O\nb) When carbon dioxide reacts with water, carbonic acid is formed.\nCO₂  +  H₂O  →  H₂CO₃\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15746,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of balanced chemical equations. Explain the following chemical reactions : a) Sulphur dioxide reacts with water           b) Sulphur trioxide reacts with water.\n','','','a) When sulphur dioxide reacts with water, sulphurous acid is formed.\nSO₂₍g₎ + H₂O → H₂SO₃\nb) When sulphur trioxide reacts with water, Sulphuric acid is formed.\nSO₃₍g₎  + H₂O   → H₂SO₄\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15747,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','State physical properties of nonmetals.','','',') i) At room temperature, non- metals exist as solids and gases (except bromine)\nii) Non- metals are not lustrous (except iodine and diamond)\niii) Solid non- metals are brittle (except diamond)\niv) Non- metals are neither malleable nor ductile\nv) Non- metals are poor conductors of heat and electricity (except graphite) \nvi) Non-metals have low density\nvii) Non- metals have low melting and boiling points (except carbon and boron)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15748,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write atomic symbol, atomic number and electronic configuration of each of the following : i) Nitrogen         	ii) Oxygen         	iii) Chlorine \n','','','i) Nitrogen : Atomic symbol : N ; Atomic number : 7; Electronic configuration : (2 , 5)\nii) Oxygen : Atomic symbol : O ; Atomic number : 8; Electronic configuration : (2 , 6)\niii) Chlorine : Atomic symbol : Cl ; Atomic number : 17; Electronic configuration : (2 , 8, 7)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15749,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(15750,131,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Select the appropriate options and complete the following paragraph : [element, compound, gold, platinum, copper, 22, 24, 20, noble metals, nonmetals] \nThe ---- such as silver and gold occur in ----- form in nature. Pure ---- is soft and therefore, ornaments made from it may bend or break due to pressure. Hence, jewellers add some amount of ---- or silver to gold. 100 percent pure gold is ---- carat while the one that used to prepare ornaments is ---- carat or smaller than that. \n','','','noble metals, elemental, gold, copper, 24, 22.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15751,131,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Three experiments to study the process of rusting is given below. Observe the three test tubes and answer the following questions : a) Why the nail in the test tube 2 is not rusted?        b) Why is the nail in the test tube 1 is rusted highly?        c) Would the nail in the test tube 3 get rusted?\n \n','','','a) i) Iron and steel rust when they come in contact with water and oxygen. Both water and oxygen are needed for resting to occur.\nii) In test tube 2 due to the oil layer the nail is not in contact with air at all thus it is not rusted.\nb) i) The nail in the test tube 1 is in contact with water as well as air \nii) Both water and oxygen in air is needed for rusting to occur \niii) Hence, it is rusted highly\nc) i) Calcium chloride is drying agent. It will absorb all the moisture present in the air of test tube\nii) Thus, the nail present in test to 3 does not rust at all.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15753,131,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the formation of cations from metals with examples.','','','i) Most of the metals have up to three electrons in their outermost valence shells. \nii) Metals have a tendency to lose valence electrons from their outermost shells to form positively charged ions called cations.\niii) Sodium has electronic configuration (2, 8, 1). It loses one electron form sodium cation, Na⁺. The electronic configuration of Na⁺ cation is (2, 8).\nNa   → Na⁺ + 1e⁻\niv) Magnesium has electronic configuration (2, 8, 2). It loses two electrons to form magnesium cation, Mg⁺⁺. The electronic configuration of Mg⁺⁺ cation is (2, 8).\nMg  →  Mg⁺⁺  + 2e⁻\nv) Aluminium has electronic configuration (2, 8, 3). It loses three electrons to form aluminium cation, Al⁺⁺⁺. The electronic configuration of Al⁺⁺⁺ cation is (2, 8)\nAl  →  Al⁺⁺⁺ + 3e⁻\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15754,131,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the formation of anions from non metals with examples.','','','i) Most of the non- metals have 4 to 7 electrons in their outermost valence shells.\nii) Non- metals have a tendency to gain electrons in their outermost valence shells to form negatively charged ions called anions.\niii) Chlorine has electronic configuration (2, 8, 7). It gains one electron to form chloride ion, Cl⁻. The electronic configuration of Cl⁻ anion is (2, 8, 8).\nCl + 1e⁻ → Cl⁻\niv) Oxygen has electronic configuration (2, 6). It gains two electrons to form oxide ion, O⁻⁻. The electronic configuration of O⁻⁻ anion is (2, 8).\nO + 2e⁻ → O⁻⁻\nv) Nitrogen has electronic configuration (2, 5). It gains three electrons to form nitride ion, N⁻⁻⁻. The electronic configuration of N⁻⁻⁻ anion is (2, 8)\nN + 3e⁻ → N⁻⁻⁻\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15755,131,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Place each item to appropriate bin :','','','Bin 1 : Metals – Sodium, Iron, Copper\nBin 2 : Non-metals – Carbon, Oxygen, Bromine, Sulphur\nBin 3 : Metalloids – Silicon, Germanium, Arsenic\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15756,131,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','a) State two properties of sodium by which it differs from the general physical properties of most metals.        b) Give four examples of metalloids          c) Write four uses of silver.\n','','','a) Two properties of sodium by which it differs from the general physical properties of most metals are: i) Sodium is so soft that it can be easily cut with a knife. 	ii) It has low melting and boiling points. \nb) Four metalloids are: i) Arsenic (As) 	ii) Silicon (Si) 	iii) Germanium (Ge) 	iv) Antimony (Sb) \nc) Four uses of silver are: i) It is used to make ornaments. 	ii) It is used in medicines as it has an antibacterial property. 	iii) It is used to make silver medals. 	iv) It is used in few electronic devices.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15791,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which different hazardous gases are released through fuel combustion?','','','Hazardous gases like carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, nitrogen dioxide, Sulphur dioxide, are released through fuel combustion.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15792,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the importance of ozone layer?','','','i) Ozone layer protects everything living on Earth from harmful ultraviolet rays (UV - B) radiating from the sun.\nii) It would be very difficult for anything to survive on the surface in absence of ozone layer.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15793,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are reasons for depletion in ozone layer?','','','i) Ozone depletion occurs when chlorofluorocarbons (CFC) found in aerosol spray cans and refrigerators are released into atmosphere.\nii) CFC cause chemical reactions that breaks down ozone molecules, reducing ozone’s ultraviolet radiation absorbing capacity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15794,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Air is homogeneous mixture of different gases.','','','i) A homogeneous mixture is a mixture which contains a definite proportion of substances every instance.\nii) Hence air is a homogeneous mixture of gases as gases present in it are in a definite proportion by volume.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15796,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by soil erosion? What are the reasons for depletion of soil fertility?\n','','','i) Topsoil is the layer of soil that is the most fertile because it contains the most organic nutrient rich materials.\nii) Soil erosion is defined as wearing away of top soil.\niii) The reasons for depletion of soil fertility are as follows : a) Soil erosion	b) Depletion of nutrients due to salinization or water logging 	c) Deforestation	d) Improper cultivation\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15797,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which factors are responsible for pollution shown in the following pictures? \n','','','a) Forest fire	b) Volcanic eruption	c) Hazardous gases released through fuel combustion.','',1,0,0,NULL),(15798,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is air pollution caused?','','','Air pollution is by contamination of air by harmful substances like poisonous gases, smoke, particulate matter, microbes, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15799,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When does soil pollution occur?','','','Soil pollution occurs when there are changes in its physical, biological and chemical properties of the soil due to which its fertility decreases either by natural or manmade reasons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15800,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : LPG, Kerosene, Biogas, Petrol','','','Biogas. All others cause pollutants excepts biogas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15801,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Respiratory disease, Silicosis, Asthma, Typhoid','','','Typhoid. Other diseases are caused due to air pollution, but typhoid is caused due to water pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15802,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Mercury, Lead, Cadmium, Endrine','','','Endrine. All others are inorganic pollutants that pollute water, but endrine is a pesticide.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15803,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Acid rain, Algal bloom, global warming, green house effect.\n','','','Algal bloom. Others are effects of air pollution, but algal bloom is caused due to rapid growth of algae on surface of water bodies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15804,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Volcanic eruption, earthquake, industrialization, Forest Fire\n','','','Burning of solid waste. Burning of solid waste is a manmade reason for air pollution, whereas the rest are natural reasons for air pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15805,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Smoke, dust, SO₂, domestic sewage','','','Domestic sewage. It causes water pollution while others cause air pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15806,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : CO₂, Nitrogen, Methane, Nitrous oxide, CFC','','','Nitrogen. It is a constituent of air while others are green house gases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15807,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Erosion of buildings : Acid rain : : Global warming : ------- \n','','','Greenhouse effect','',0,0,0,NULL),(15808,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Mercury : Inorganic pollutants : : Parasites : -----','','','Biological pollutant','',0,0,0,NULL),(15809,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Respiratory disorders : Air pollution : : Typhoid : ---','','','Water pollution','',0,0,0,NULL),(15810,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Nitrous oxide : Air pollutant : : Plastics : -----','','','Soil pollutant','',0,0,0,NULL),(15811,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Sewage : organic water pollutant : : Cadmium : -----','','','Inorganic water pollutant','',0,0,0,NULL),(15812,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Carbon monoxide : Air pollution : : Excessive algal growth : -----\n','','','water pollution','',0,0,0,NULL),(15813,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The main cause of silicosis','','','Dust','',0,0,0,NULL),(15814,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Pollution controlling machinery which prevents air Pollution.\n','','','Arresters','',0,0,0,NULL),(15815,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Worst ever industrial accident that occurred during 2nd December 1984.\n','','','Bhopal tragedy','',0,0,0,NULL),(15816,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Compound which decreases the oxygen carrying capacity of the blood.\n','','','Carbon monoxide','',0,0,0,NULL),(15817,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The pollution caused by excessive use of chemical fertilizers. \n','','','Water pollution','',0,0,0,NULL),(15818,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Air pollutants released by volcanic eruptions.','','','Hydrogen Sulphide, sulphur dioxide, carbon dioxide, hydrogen, water vapour, dust, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15819,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Fuels causing pollution on burning.','','','Coal, timber, LPG, kerosene, diesel, petrol','',0,0,0,NULL),(15824,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Water does not get polluted by washing the clothes in running water of river.\n','','','False. Water gets polluted by detergents and other chemicals while washing clothes in running water or river.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15825,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : More the use of electric appliances, more will be the pollution.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15826,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Forest fire is considered as the man made cause of air pollution.\n','','','False. Forest fire is natural cause of air pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15827,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : One of the major effects of air pollution on plants is that stomata get closed.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15828,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Energy emitted by the sun is absorbed by the atmosphere and earth’s surface.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15829,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Microbes like fungi and bacteria grow on organic matter decaying in water.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15830,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Diseases like hepatitis and typhoid are caused due to air pollution.\n','','','False. Diseases like hepatitis and typhoid are caused due to water pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15831,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Radioactive and other pollutants enter and pass through food chain like soil, crop, water and human body.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15832,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Soil erosion results in the depletion of oxygen level of water bodies.\n','','','False. Aquatic weeds reduce the oxygen level of water bodies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15833,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Heavy metals like mercury and cadmium are absorbed by plants and thereby enter the food chain indirectly due to acid rain. \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15834,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Acid rain causes erosion of building and historical monuments. \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15835,132,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Direct exposure of ultra-violet rays prevents skin cancer.\n','','','False. Exposure of ultra-violet rays causes skin cancer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15836,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Following are some statements about pollution. Which type of pollution do those express?     a) Fog seems to be appearing in Delhi during day hours.         b) Many times vomiting and dysentery occurs after eating panipuri.\n','','','a) Air pollution		b) Water pollution','',0,0,0,NULL),(15837,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is pollution?','','','Contamination of natural environment that will be harmful to ecosystems is called as pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15838,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are pollutants?','','','Factors affecting natural functions of ecosystem and causing harmful effects on abiotic and biotic factors are called pollutants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15839,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are visible pollutants known to you?','','','Particulate emissions (smog) is example of visible pollutant.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15840,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are invisible pollutants?','','','Nitrogen oxide, sulphur oxides, carbon monoxide are some of the invisible pollutants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15841,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','From which water sources do we get the water suitable to use?','','','Surface water like river, lakes, streams, groundwater, wells and rain water are the sources from which we get water suitable to use.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15842,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much percent of the earth’s area occupied by water?','','','About 71% of the earth area is occupied by water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15843,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by soil erosion? ','','','Soil erosion is defined as the wearing away of topsoil. Topsoil is the top layer of soil and is the most fertile layer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15844,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Various problems in environment have been arrived','','','i) Industrialization, increasing population, mining, transportation, indiscriminate use of pesticides and fertilizers are causing pollution on the earth.\nii) It is affecting human beings to a great extent.\niii) The various problems in environment may have been arrived due to human interference with nature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15845,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Water is referred ‘as molecule of life’.','','','i) Water is needed by all known forms of life.\nii) Due to the difference in the distribution of charge, water is a polar molecule.\niii) Hydrogen bonds hold adjacent water molecules together.\niv) Water is involved in many biochemical reactions. As result, water is referred as molecule of life.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15846,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Palpoisoning, skin cancers referred as Puzzar (Gutter river)\n','','','i) Large number of tanning centres are present in Tamil Nadu.\nii) Wastewater from those centres is released into Palar river, and it is polluted.\niii) Hence, that river is referred as Puzzar (Gutter river).\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15847,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','1) Carbon monoxide		2) Vehicular and industrial smoke                3) Production and use of pesticides	                     4) Methane\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15848,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following pollutants into natural and manmade categories. Sewage, dust, pollen grains, chemical fertilizers, vehicular smoke, algae, pesticides, waste of animals and birds\n','','','Natural pollutants : Sewage dust, pollengrains, algae, waste of animals and birds\nMan made pollutants : Chemical fertilizers, vehicular smoke, pesticides\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15849,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the importance of Ozone layer?','','','i) Ozone layer is present in stratosphere region of the earth atmosphere.\nii) The ozone layer absorbed the UV - B rays radiated from the sun.\niii) If these radiations reach the surface of the earth directly. These can cause severe harm to plants, aquatic species animals and human being.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15850,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State any two natural reasons of water pollution?','','','Aquatic weeds, decomposing matter, mud/sludge, soil erosion, Microbes like fungi and bacteria, algae, Nematodes','',0,0,0,NULL),(15851,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Effects of acid rain','','','The acid rain causes the following effects :\ni) Acidity of soil and water increases due to acid rain and it harms the aquatic organisms, plants and entire forest life.\nii) Erosion of buildings, historical monuments, bridges, metal idols, wire fences, etc. occurs due to acid rain.\niii) Heavy metals like mercury and cadmium are absolved up by plants and thereby enter the food chain indirectly due to acid rain.\niv) Due to acidification of water in water bodies and pipes, leaching of metal and plastic material occurs in water and thereby serious health problems arise.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15852,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Effects of water pollution','','','i) Water pollution mainly affect human beings and ecosystem.\nii) In human beings, diseases like Hepatitis, typhoid, diseases of skin and alimentary canal are caused. Aliments in liver, kidney, brain, deformities in bones, hypertension, etc. is also caused.\niii) The main effect of water pollution are on ecosystem includes retarded plant growth, loss of plant species, increase in Salt content of water, decreases dissolved oxygen level, disturbance is in aquatic ecosystem, death of aquatic animals and adverse effects on sea birds.\niv) other effects include changes in physical and chemical properties in water, changes in natural colour and taste, aquatic fauna gets destroyed, soil fertility gets effected and toxic materials gets added to crops.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15853,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Prevention and control of pollution','','','i) Government of India has enacted some laws for control, regulation and prevention of pollution, which includes- Water protection and prevention act 1974, Air pollution and prevention act, 1981, Environment protection act 1986.\nii) Various laws and rules are in force in relation to biomedical waste, harmful effluents, solid waste and sound pollution.\niii) Government statutory bodies such as Maharashtra Pollution Control Board and Central Pollution Control Board supervise whether the industries, industrial areas, local governing bodies like Municipalities, District council, Gram Panchayat, etc. about pollution control.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15854,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on :  Effects of soil pollution','','','Soil pollution causes several effects which are as follows :\ni) Soil fertility decreases due to mixing of salty and acidic water from industry\nii) Radioactive and other pollutants enter and fast food chain like the soil, crop, water and human body.\niii) Problems of water pollution increases due to soil pollution. Toxic substances leach into water. Similarly, diseases spreads through various pathogens.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15855,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on :  Types of water pollutants. ','','','Water pollutants are classified into organic, inorganic and biological pollutants.\ni) Organic pollutants includes weedicides, insecticides, fertilizers, sewage, industrial effluents, etc.\nii) Biological pollutants include algae, bacteria, viruses and parasites. Diseases are spread due to biological pollution.\niii) Inorganic pollutants include suspended particles like fine sand, dust, soil, precipitates of salt, compounds of arsenic, cadmium, lead, Mercury and traces of radioactive material.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15856,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Air pollution and water pollution','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15857,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Air pollution and soil pollution','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15858,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Whether the vehicles with two stroke engines cause more pollution than four stroke engines?\n','','','The vehicles with two stroke engines cause more pollution than vehicles with four stroke engine because two stroke engines produce lots of particulate matter because the fuel is not completely combusted in them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15859,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it said that air is homogeneous mixture of different gases?','','','i) Air contains various gases such as nitrogen, Oxygen, carbon dioxide and smaller amount of other gases.\nii) It contains of 78% of nitrogen, 21% of Oxygen and 1% of other gases in small composition.\niii) Therefore, it is said that air is homogeneous mixture of different gases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15860,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the reasons of water pollution?','','','i) Water pollution may be caused either by natural or man- made causes.\nii) Natural reasons include aquatic weeds, decomposing matter, mud/ sledge, soil erosion, algae, nematodes and microbes like fungi and bacteria.\niii) Man made causes include domestic sewage, oil spillage, industrial effluent, uses of fertilizers and pesticides, water from thermal power plant, disposal of ashes, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15861,132,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it said that soil pollution is related to air and water pollution?','','','i) Soil pollution is related to air and water pollution.\nii) If wet waste is dumped at wrong places instead of composting, pathogens grow upon it, which gets then mixed with water causing water pollution.\niii) Excessively sprayed insecticides and weedicides gets mixed with air causing air pollution.\niv) Similarly, excessive use of chemical fertilizers causes water pollution.\nv) Soil pollution occurs due to mixing of human waste, birds and animal waste. This releases various stinking gases causing air pollution. Same waste causes water pollution if mixed with water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15863,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Following are some statements about pollution. Which type of pollution do those express? : a) Problems of sneezing occurs sometimes during visit to garden.         b) Crop does not grow up in some areas.        c) People living in the busy squares face the problems like short breathing and other respiratory problems.\n','','','a) Air Pollution 		b) Soil pollution 		c) Air pollution','',0,0,0,NULL),(15864,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','For which different purpose do we use the water?','','','Water is used mainly for the following :\ni) Domestic purposes : Cooking, drinking, bathing, washing\nii) Agriculture purposes : Farming, gardening, fisheries\niii) Industrial purposes\niv) Recreation – Swimming, rafting, boating\nv) Hydro power generation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15865,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is acid rain?','','','i) Oxides of Sulphur and nitrogen are released into atmosphere through burning of coal, timber and fuel oils.\nii) These oxides mix with rain water and form acids like sulphuric acid, nitric acid, nitrous, etc.\niii) These acids mix with raindrops and snowflakes and come down as rain called as acid rain.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15866,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is green house effect?','','','i) CO₂ plays very important role of absorption of solar heat.\nii) Effect of CO₂ on earth’s temperature is nothing but greenhouse effect.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15867,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Suggest four preventive measures for air pollution.','','','i) Prevention measures of air pollution :\ni) Smoke emerging from factories contains harmful particles. Hence, pollution controlling machinery should be used. E.g. Arresters, filters\nii) Proper disposal of stinking waste generated in cities.\niii) Control of atomic tests, chemical weapons, etc.\niv) Control/ ban over CFC production.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15868,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the reasons of water pollution?','','','Water pollution is caused due to several reasons. Some of the major causes of water pollution are as follows :\ni) Sewage and waste water : Sewage, garbage and liquid waste of household, agriculture and industries contain harmful chemicals and toxins which make the water poisonous.\nii) Industrial waste : Industrial waste contains pollutants which are extremely harmful.\niii) Acid rain\niv)  Oil spillage.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15869,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the labelling of the given figure.','','','i) Energy emitted by Sun is absorbed in atmosphere.           ii) Infrared radiation         	iii) Atmosphere	iv) Energy emitted by fun is absorbed by earth surface\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15870,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the labelling of the given figure.','','','i) Burning fuels		ii) Acid rains		iii) Damage of animals and plants	                 iv) Oxides','',1,0,0,NULL),(15871,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Construct two slogans each on air, water and soil pollution.','','','a) Air pollution\ni) Everything is fair, if it doesn’t pollute the air”!\nii) Air pollution does not need visa to travel across the borders.\nb) Water Pollution\ni) “Stop the pollution quick, don’t make the water sick.’\nii) Humans should have one true devotion, don’t throw waste.\nc) Soil Pollution\ni) “Stop littering, Earth is not your garbage can”!\nii) “Free the Earth from this bondage, stop dumping garbage.”\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15873,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where do you find the pollution around you?','','','i) In urban areas, air pollution gets worse in the morning rush hour and in some cases extremely worse at the traffic signals. It is also caused by smoke released from the factories.\nii) Domestic sewage and disposal of waste in the canals cause water pollutions.\niii) These are the regular sites of a person living in urban area.\niv) In rural areas, use of pesticides and fertilizers by farmers pollute the nearby water bodies.\nv) Also, in certain localities disposal of ashes, floral offerings to god and washing of clothes pollutes the local water bodies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15874,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of pollutants is observed? Explain with examples.','','','i) Pollutants maybe natural or manmade.\nii) Natural pollutants are destroyed in due course of time by nature’s rule. Example of natural pollutants includes aquatic weeds, pollens, microbes, spores, domestic sewage, etc.\niii) However, manmade pollutants are not destroyed and it is remained in the environment. Examples of manmade pollutants include CFC, plastic insecticides, pesticides, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15875,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Plot a graph showing the proportion of various gases in Earth’s atmosphere?\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15877,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the reasons for depletion in ozone layer?','','','i) Mainly ozone layer is getting depleted due to greenhouse effect and global warming.\nii) Depletion of ozone layer occurs due to gases such as carbon dioxide, nitrous oxide, CFC and methane are considered as greenhouse gases.\niii) In the presence of UV light, these gases dissociate, releasing chlorine atoms, which then lead to ozone destruction.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15878,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','For which different purposes do we use the water?','','','i) Mainly water is used for household purposes such as cooking, bathing, washing clothes, washing dishes, watering and yard, etc.\nii) Water is used for industrial purposes (manufacturing) and agriculture purposes (farming, fisheries).\niii) Also, water is used by for recreation purposes including water theme parks, rafting, boating and swimming.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15879,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','From which water sources do we get the water suitable to use?','','','i) Though 71% Earth is covered by water, only few percentage of water is potable.\nii) Water present in the ocean and sea are not used for daily needs as it is salty.\niii) Thus, for generations, water for daily uses are obtained from river, lakes, streams, etc.\niv) Only water from these sources is considered potable for humans and animals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15880,132,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','See the picture and answer the following questions : a) Why these problems in environment may have been arised?        b) What should be done to overcome these problems?\n','','','a) Many problems have arisen on Earth due to human interference in the nature. Industrialization, increasing population, mining, transportation, indiscriminate use of pesticides and fertilizers are causing pollution on earth.\nb) The best way to overcome these problems is to plant more trees and implement strict rules against illegal action of the human being. Though there are several laws, most of these are not implemented by the authorities. Thus, proper implementation of the laws reduces dominance of human beings on the nature.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15882,132,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each of water, soil and air pollution from your residential area.\n','','','a) Water pollution : i) Improper disposal of sewage water	ii) Detergents from washing our cars	iii) Improper disposal of paints\nb) Examples of soil pollution : i) Drainage of contaminated surface water into the soil	ii) Discharge of sewage          	iii) Improper disposal of electronic waste\nc) Examples of air pollution : i) Vehicle exhaust release toxic gases like CO₂ etc.                 	ii) Burning waste in the open area.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15883,132,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the pollution occur due to vehicles? Give the names of vehicles causing least pollution.\n','','','i) Vehicles burn fuel, that is petrol or diesel to power their engines and move. \nii) This process leads to emission of harmful gases in the environment.\niii) Vehicles that use diesel are more harmful as compared to those that use petrol\niv)  Poor quality of fuel will also lead to more pollutants into atmosphere.\nv) Noise from moving vehicles lead to noise pollution\nvi) Bicycle, CNG vehicles, electric vehicles are some of the vehicles that cause the least pollution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15884,132,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the relation between green house effect and global warming.','','','i) Over the last 100 years, proportion of CO₂ has been increased due to industrialization.\nii) Effect of this CO₂ on the Earth\'s temperature is nothing but greenhouse effect.\niii) Similar to CO₂, nitrous oxide, methane and CFC also trap the heat. These are called greenhouse gases.\niv) Thereby this leads to a raise in the temperature of the Earth.\nv) This increase in temperature of the Earth is referred as global warming.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15885,132,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the effects of air pollution on plants, animals and the environment.\n','','','a) Plants : i) Stomata get closed		ii) Slowing down of rate of photosynthesis	iii) Growth is retarded. Leaves fall off or become yellow.\nb) Animals : i)  Respiration is adversely affected	ii) Irritation of eyes, lowered O₂ carrying capacity of blood	iii) Poisoning, skin cancer, asthma, silicosis, death due to prolonged exposure, mental weakness.\nc) Environment : i) Greenhouse effect and global warming	ii) Ozone layer depletion	iii) Acid rain\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15886,132,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and identify the sentence expressing types of pollution.\nNilesh is a student of std. VIII and lives in urban area. It takes about an hour to go to the school by bus. He faces the heavy traffic of two wheelers, four wheelers, rickshaws, buses while going to school. He is facing the problem of asthma since last few days. Doctors recommended him to stay away from urban area. Since then, his mother sent him to the village of his maternal uncle. Nilesh saw the heaps of garbage at many places in village. Foul smell of human and animal wastes was present at many places. Blackish water with foul smell was flowing in a stream. He developed some abdominal disease within few days. \n a) He faces the heavy traffic ----- going to school           b) He is facing the problem ----- last few days.           c) Nilesh saw heaps of garbage ----- at many places.         d) He developed ----- within few days.\n','','','a) Air and noise pollution 	b) Air pollution 	c) Soil pollution 	d) Water pollution','',0,0,0,NULL),(15888,132,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Whether the pollutants are degradable or non- degradable? Explain with examples?\n','','','i) Pollutants can be both degradable and non-degradable.\nii) Biodegradable pollutants are degraded by microbes. In this case, degradation process will be faster.\nii) Biodegradable pollutants are not accumulated and gets used up in short time.\niii) These can be used to produce manure, compost and biogas. E.g. sewage, paper, vegetables waste, etc.\niv) Non- degradable pollutants cannot be degraded by the microbes. Here, degradation process is slow and it often accumulates.\nv) Certain methods are developed for degradation of this waste, but it is considered expensive. E.g. DDT, Mercury, silver foils, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15889,132,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the reasons responsible for air pollution?','','','Some other reasons for air pollution include :\ni) Mining activities : During the process of mining, dust and other toxic chemicals are released into the air that are harmful for the miners and people exposed to these pollutants.\nii) Household products : Household cleaning products, painting supplies and electrical appliances like refrigerators and their conditioners emit toxic chemicals into the air.\niii) Rocket engine emissions : In space stations, launching rockets can cause major air pollution and release pollutants like carbon dioxide, water, black carbon and alumina particles into the air.\niv) Marine vessels and aircrafts : They are responsible for Major emission of air pollutants by burning of fuels.\nv) Construction sites : Lot of dust and particles are mixed in the air by construction activities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15890,132,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','See the picture and answer the following questions : a) Mention any two causes of water pollution?         b) Give the names of two pesticides that may cause water pollution?       c) What is the health problems associated with contamination of water?\n','','','a) Water pollution can be caused by the domestic sewage and dumping of industrial effluents into the nearby water bodies.\nb) Pesticides such as enrine, chlorine and carbonate cause water pollution.\nc) Diseases like hepatitis, typhoid, diseases of skin, aliments of liver, kidney, brain, hypertension, etc. are caused due to contamination of water.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15892,132,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the concept map and explain : [Sulphur dioxide, air pollutants, dust, methane, skin cancer, irritation of eyes, pesticides, silicosis and mental weakness]','','','Explanations : i) There are several air pollutants which include Sulphur dioxide, dust, methane and pesticides.\nii) Sulphur dioxide released from factories causes eye irritation and excess mucous.\niii) Dust from vehicles cause silicosis and methane from industrial leakage causes skin cancer.\niv) Pesticides used in agricultural farming causes mental weakness.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(15927,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do you mean by disaster?','','','A disaster is a sudden, calamitous event that seriously disrupts the functioning of a community or society and causes human, material and economic or environmental loses that exceeds the ability to cope using one’s own resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15928,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different types of disaster?','','','Natural disaster and Man-made disaster are two different types of disaster.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15929,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is an earthquake?','','','Sudden vibrations in the earth and shaking of the Earth’s surface/ ground is called an earthquake.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15930,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the effects of earthquake?','','','i) Loss of life of humans, wild animals and pets.\nii) Destruction of electric pole, pipelines, houses, buildings, roads and railway tracks causing huge economic loss.\niii) Destruction of ecosystem due to loss of biodiversity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15931,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the possible causes of earthquakes?','','','Mining, underground atomic test, volcanic eruptions, stress of big dams on the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15932,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are earthquakes resistant buildings?','','','Earthquake resistant buildings are the constructions which do not get damaged due to Earth movement up to a certain limit.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15933,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Candles, lantern, matchbox, battery','','','Battery. It is inflammable while others are flammable.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15934,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Varnish, cooking oil, petrol, water.','','','Water. it is incombustible while others are inflammable.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15935,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Wood, clothes, coal, solvents','','','Solvents. They are liquids while others are solids.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15936,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Tsunami, Nuclear disaster, Heavy rains, storms.','','','Nuclear disaster. It is man-made disaster while others are natural disasters.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15937,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Geiger counter, creep meter, spectrometer, strain meter.\n','','','Spectrometer. It is an apparatus used for measuring and recording spectra. Other devices are used for intimating earthquake.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15938,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Malin mishap, Bhopal tragedy, 9/11 attack, Three Mile Island.\n','','','Malin mishap. It is the landslide (natural calamity) which occurred in Pune district (Maharashtra), whereas others are man-made disasters.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15939,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Earthquake, cyclone, terrorism, landslide.','','','Terrorism. All others are natural calamities, but terrorism is considered as man-made disaster.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15940,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Class A fire : Solid substances : : Class B fire : ----','','','Liquid substances','',0,0,0,NULL),(15941,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Barometer : Atmospheric pressure : : Seismometer : ------\n','','','Earthquake','',0,0,0,NULL),(15942,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Suppress fire : Froth : : Cooling out : ------','','','Water','',0,0,0,NULL),(15943,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Earthquake : Direction of river changes : : ----- : River gets flooded.\n','','','Landslide','',0,0,0,NULL),(15944,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Displacement of waterfalls : Landslide : : Change in direction of flow of rivers : -------\n','','','Earthquake','',0,0,0,NULL),(15945,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Coal : Class A Fire : : Petrol : -------','','','Class B fire','',0,0,0,NULL),(15947,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The shocks and waves formed in the exterior of the earth spreads to the interior of the surface.  \n','','','False. The shocks and waves formed in the interior of the spread on the surface in all direction','',0,0,0,NULL),(15948,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Shocks occurred due to an earthquakes can be mild or acute\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15949,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Everyday, earthquakes are noticed at some or the other places on the earth. \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15950,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The accentuation (intensity) of an earthquake is measured on a graph paper. \n','','','False. The accentuation (intensity) of an earthquake is measured in ‘Richter Scale’','',0,0,0,NULL),(15951,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The earthquake resistant buildings are very strong.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15952,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Fire caused due to electrical components can be put off by sand or soil.  \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15953,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Stir up pumps are used to put off small fires.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15954,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Natural disasters like floods and earthquakes cause landslides.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15955,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Every month a mock drill must be conducted in the school.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(15956,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Sudden vibrations on the earth.','','','Earthquake','',0,0,0,NULL),(15957,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Mild earthquakes occurring on larger numbers.','','','Runners','',0,0,0,NULL),(15958,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Instrument used to measure Earthquakes.','','','Seismograph or Seismometer','',0,0,0,NULL),(15959,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Intensity of Earthquakes is measured in.','','','Richter scale','',0,0,0,NULL),(15960,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Earthquakes occurring at the bottom of the sea.','','','Tsunami','',0,0,0,NULL),(15961,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Buildings that do not get damaged due to earth movements. \n','','','Earthquake resistant buildings','',0,0,0,NULL),(15962,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Class C fire is caused due to','','','Gaseous substances','',0,0,0,NULL),(15963,133,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Substance that cuts off the fire from contact with O₂','','','Froth like substance','',0,0,0,NULL),(15964,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Disaster','','','Disaster is a result of differential behaviour of nature due to many conditions. It can be man- made or natural.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15965,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Earthquake ','','','Sudden vibrations on the earth and shaking of the Earth surface/ ground is called an earthquake.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15966,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Richter scale','','','The accentuation or intensity of the earthquake is measured in Richter scale.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15967,133,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Seismograph','','','An instrument which records the earthquakes is called seismometer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15968,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Landslide','','','Landslide is a sudden uncontrollable movement of Rock, debris or earth down a slope due to heavy rainfall.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15969,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Disaster','','','i) A disaster is a sudden, calamitous event that seriously disrupts the functioning of a community or society and causes human, material and economic or environmental losses that exceeds the ability to cope using one’s on resources.\nii) It is many a times caused by nature, disasters can have human origins.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15970,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Earthquake','','','i) An earthquake is the shaking of the ground caused by the sudden breaking and movement of large sections (tectonic plates)\nii) The damage caused by earthquakes also depends on their depth and fault type.\niii) Scientists use the different speeds of seismic waves to locate the epicentre. Seismometers are used to measure the magnitude of the earthquake.\niv) Earthquakes kill nearly 8000 people every year.\nv) They are caused by geological faults but they can also be caused by landslides, nuclear testing, mine test and volcanic activities.\nvi) An earthquake under the ocean can cause a Tsunami.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15971,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on :  Measuring Earthquakes','','','i) Earthquake is measured in ‘Richter scale’ and its intensity is a measured. A network of sensors, called seismometer, is used to record earthquakes.\nii) An earthquake’s magnitude is calculated based on the ground motion recorded by each seismometer from the earthquake.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15972,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Precaution to be taken at the time of earthquake','','','i) One has to stand still at one place	ii) One should sit on the floor or below the bed	iii) If there is no table or bed then one must cover the head by folding your hands around it		iv) If you are in a vehicle, avoid parking your car under a tree or electric pole.\nv) Avoid using lift in the multistoried building\nvi) As there is always a possibility of fire during earthquake, one must switch off the main electric supply.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15973,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : It is safer to find shelter under things like a bed, table at the time of earthquake.\n','','','i) If one is at home, then you need not get panicked and instead must stand still at one place or sit on the floor or below the bed until the movement of the earth stops.\nii) If there is no table for any shelter then one must sit in any corner of the room and cover your head by folding your hands around it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15974,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : In monsoon, don’t take shelter neat a hillside.','','','i) During monsoon, landslide chances are found to be the maximum. (for e.g. Malin village).\nii) There is life and financial loss on a large scale as a trees, buildings, rocks on slopes collapse on low lying land. Hence, we need to more careful.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15975,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Don’t use lifts at the time of earthquake.','','','i) After the earthquake, there is a possibility of fire, hence one must avoid using lift in a multistoried building, as it may catch fire.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15976,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fire is natural calamity or a man-made calamity?','','','i) Fires may take place due to natural sources like lightening, etc. that set trees on fire. Also, atmospheric factors like high temperatures and dryness make trees more prone to catching fire.\nii) Fires may also be caused by human, when humans deliberately or accidentally provide a source of fire.\n Therefore, fire can be a natural as well as man- made calamity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15977,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Methods of fire extinction','','','There are three main methods to stop the spreads of fire or to control the fire :\na) Cool out (use of water) : To extinguish fire, water is most common and effective solution which is easily available everywhere. If you spray water on the fire or around the fire it creates cooling effects and helps to control the fire.\nb) Suppress the fire : To control the fire which is caused due to electricity or oil, sand or soil can be used. If we spread froth like substance on the fire it cuts of the contact between air and fire. This method is more useful to extinguish the fire spread due to oil.\nc) Keep away flammable substances : In this method, all flammable substances are kept away from the fire. Any wooden article or flammable substances must be kept away so that no sources are left for the fire to spread. Stir up pumps is the best device to put off small fires. It sprays water in all directions around the fire.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15978,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happen when water percolate through the cracks of earth?','','','When water percolates through the cracks of earth, due to tremendous heat water gets converted into steam. The steam formed tries to come out through the loosened earth surface causing earthquakes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15979,133,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happen when earthquake occurs at the bottom of the ocean? ','','','When earthquake occurs at the bottom of the sea or ocean, large Tsunami waves are resulted. These are considered as large seismic sea waves raised due to large displacement of water. Tsunami results in large scale destruction of coastal region.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15980,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a crowd gathers at the place of earthquake, what would be the difficulties in relief work?\n','','','i) One should adopt sensibility and not crowd at the place of relief site making it difficult for those people who are helping.\nii) We must take the initiative of keeping the crowd away from site and request only a few good Samaritans to go and offer their services.\niii) Some of them can look out for those people who are injured and first aid can be given to them, at a little far-off place from site.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15981,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the relation between continuous rains and landslides. Give reasons.\n','','','i) When it rains incessantly it can cause flooding even on day soil. Landslides refers to several forms of mass wasting.\nii) It was in Malin village that continuous rains led to land sliding because of which the entire Malin village was razed to the ground.\niii) Thus, continuous rains and landslides are related together.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15982,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a chart showing ‘Does’ and Don’ts at the time of earthquake.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(15983,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of the following pictures explain your role in the disaster management.\n','','','i) The basic role of a student is awareness of what to do during and after disasters.\nii) There would be lesser panicking, paranoid and uncontrollable people running around.\niii) Also, what to do when disaster strikes will also lessen the death toll. \niv) Knowing what to do after disaster and at least basic first aid, will enable students help the authorities in saving lives.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(15984,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make a list of the Institutes who provide help for disaster management. Collect more information about their work.\n','','','National Institute of Disaster Management, Emergency Management Institute, Federal Emergency Management, National Disaster Response, International Association of Emergency, National Disaster Management Plan, National Civil Defense, National Crisis Management Committee, Joint Assistance Centre, Centre for Disaster Management Maharashtra, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(15985,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Are there any possible places of landslide in your area? Collect information about the work.\n','','','i) The area where I stay is a plane surface far away from any mountainous region.\nii) There are concrete constructional sites and buildings and have no possible place of landslides.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15987,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the figure below :','','','A) Epicenter	B) Plate movement             	C) Focal point             	D) Seismic waves','',1,0,0,NULL),(15988,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the figure below :\n          \n','','','A) Vertical seismometer	B) Horizontal seismometer','',1,0,0,NULL),(15989,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the guidelines provided by Bureau of Indian standards for construction of building?\n','','','i) Bureau of Indian standards has established a code of conduct to construct tall buildings.\nii) Buildings are constructed as per IS 456 which provides the code of practice for general structural use of plane and rain forced concrete.\niii) IS 1893 specifies criteria for earthquake resistant design of structure.\niv) IS 13920 provides ductile detailing of rain forced concrete structure subjected to seismic forces.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15990,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does the picture represent? Explain?','','','i) The picture represents the precautions to be taken during an earthquake.                ii) A person who is indoors during an earthquake must sit below the table or bed or any other furniture covering head by folding hands around it until the movements of earth stops.','',1,0,0,NULL),(15991,133,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does the picture represent? Explain? ','','','The picture represents precautions that need to be taken to avoid gas leak during earthquake. Turn off the gas stove knob immediately to prevent leakage or explosion.','',1,0,0,NULL),(15992,133,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is earthquake? What are the effects of earthquake?','','','Sudden vibrations on the earth and shaking of the Earth surface/ ground is called an earthquake.\nEffects of an earthquake :\ni) Loss of human beings, wild animals and pets\nii) Economic loss at largest scale (Electric pole, pipelines, houses, buildings, roads, railway tracks are destroyed).\niii) Ecosystem destruction due to the loss of biodiversity \niv) Direction of flow of rivers, streams changes\nv) Possibility of fire in cities increases\nvi) If there is earthquake at the bottom of ocean, it may create Tsunami waves and thereby large scale destruction of coastal region\nvii) Level of water table changes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15993,133,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fire is a natural calamity or man made calamity?','','','Fire is a natural calamity in the following cases :\ni) Forest fire - Due to soaring temperature and dryness trees catch fire.\nii) Lightning strikes on a tree and set it on fire.\niii) Fire due to volcanic eruptions. Fire is a man made calamity when there is fire due to oil spills, fuel leakage, accidental or deliberate fire in industries and houses or due to nuclear meltdown, etc.\nFire can be both natural as well as man made calamity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15994,133,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the reasons for ‘Malin’ mishap in Pune district? What are its effects?\n','','','On 31st July 2014, a landslide occurred in the village of Malin in the Ambegaon, taluka of Pune district.\na) Causes of the landslide are : i) Heavy rainfall	ii) Neglecting the geological facts before any development and process, like construction of Dimbhe dam	iii) Deforestation 	iv) Changing agriculture practices\nb) Effects : i) Entire village was wiped out	ii) More than 100 people died.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15995,133,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the causes and effects of an earthquake?','','','a) Causes of an earthquake are : i) Volcanic eruption	ii) stress of big dams on earth	iii) Mining 	iv) Underground atomic test\nv) The water percolate through cracks. Due to tremendous heat, it gets converted into steam. The steam rises up from the loosened earth surface causing earthquakes.\nb) Effects of an earthquake :\ni) Loss of human beings, wild animals and pets\nii) Economic loss at largest scale (Electric pole, pipelines, houses, buildings, roads, railway tracks are destroyed).\niii) Ecosystem destruction due to the loss of biodiversity \niv) Direction of flow of rivers, streams changes\nv) Possibility of fire in cities increases\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15996,133,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the different types of fire and ways to control it?','','','Types of fire :\ni) Class A Fire (Solid substances) : Commonly flammable materials are their fuel source (wood, clothes, coal, paper, etc.). Thus fire can be put out with water.\nii) Class B Fire (Liquid substances ) : Fires caused due to flammable liquid substances (petrol, oil, varnish, solvents, cooking oil, paints, etc.). All these substances are lighter than water, therefore, fire extinguishers can be used to extinguish fire.\niii) Class C Fire (Gaseous substances : Fires caused due to acetylene, household gas (LPG)\niv) Class D Fire (Chemical substances) : Fires caused due to combustile metal like potassium, sodium and calcium, which react with water at normal room temperature.\niv) Class E Fire (Electrical : A fire caused due to electrical components is class E fire. To extinguish such fire, power is cut off and nonconductive fire extinguishers like carbon dioxide are used.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15997,133,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the methods of Fire extinctions and safety measure?','','','a) Methods of ire extinction : There are three main methods to stop the spreads of fire or to control the fire.\ni) Cool Out (Use of water : To extinguish firewater is most common and effective solution which is easily available everywhere. If you spray water on the fire or around the fire it creates cooling effect and helps to control the fire\nii) Suppress the Fire : To control the fire which is caused due to electricity or oil, sand or soil can be used. If we spread a froth like substance on the fire, it cuts off the contact between air and fire. This method is more useful to extinguish the fire due to oil.\niii) Keep away Flammable Substances : In this method, all flammable substances are kept away from the fire. Any wooden article or flammable substances must be kept away so that no source is left for the fire to spread. Stirr up pump is the best device to put off small fires. It sprays water in all directions around to fire.\nb) Safety measures :\ni) Develop the habit of switching off the gas regulator when not in use specially during night. Switch off the electrical appliances when you are going out of the house.\nii) To make others alert and call them for help shout loudly.\niii) Call fire brigade		iv) Provide information how to use fire extinguishers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(15998,133,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the causes and effects of landslide? ','','','a) Causes of landslide :\ni) After effects of natural disasters like earthquake, Tsunami, heavy rains, storms, floods cause landslide.\nii) Unlimited cutting of trees causes soil erosion \niii) While building roads in mountains, there is a lot of digging, that makes the mountain week.\nb) Effects of landslide :\ni) Rivers get flooded and change their paths.\nii) Displacement of waterfalls, formation of artificial water reservoirs.\niii) There is a loss of life and financial loss on a large scale, as a trees, buildings, rocks on sloppy area collapse on low-lying land.\niv) Landslide affects the traffic \nv) Landslide destroys plant life on it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16000,133,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the specifications of an earthquake- proof building?','','','i) Earthquake resistant structures are structures designed to protect buildings from earthquakes. Currently there are several design philosophies in earthquake engineering.\nii) Modern equipment’s like laser ranging, long baseline, Geiger counter, creep meter, strain meter, tide gauge, tilt meter and volumetric strain gauge are used.\niii) To construct tall buildings ‘Indian Standard Institute’ has set some ‘code of conduct’ i.e. IS 456, IS13920 and IS1893.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16001,133,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Is there any relation between dam and earthquake? Explain.','','','i) The most widely accepted explanation is dams to cause earthquakes.\nii) It is related to the pressure created in the micro cracks and fissures in the ground near reservoirs.\niii) When the pressure of water in the rocks increases it acts to lubricate the faults which are already under tectonic strain, but are prevented from sleeping by the friction of the rock surfaces.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16036,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Which instrument had you used to observe cell?','','','We used a compound microscope to observe cell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16039,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Enlist the four main parts of a cell?','','','Main parts of a cell are : 1) Cell wall          2) Plasma membrane         3) Cytoplasm             4) Cell organelles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16040,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does nucleus consist of?','','','Nucleus consists of one round nucleolus and a network of chromatin fibres.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16041,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is endoplasmic reticulum?','','','Endoplasmic reticulum is a cell organelle which is the intranuclear transport system of a cell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16042,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who are ‘organs of a cell’?','','','Cell organelles are the \'organs of a cell\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16043,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Bacteria, Fungi, animal cell, plant cells','','','Bacteria. All others are eukaryotes except bacteria.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16044,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the odd one out : DNA, Ribosomes, Chlorophyll. ','','','Chlorophyll. In this, only chlorophyll is a pigment which gives plants green colour.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16045,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Nucleolus, Mitochondria, Plastids, Endoplasmic Reticulum.\n','','','Nucleolus. All  others are cell organelles, whereas nucleolus is the small spherical structure inside nucleus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16046,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Nucleoplasm, nucleolus, chromatin fibers, Chromoplasts\n','','','Chromoplasts: It is an organelle present in plant cell while others are parts of nucleus of the cell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16047,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Demolition squads, digestive bags, suicide bags, cristae.\n','','','Cristae: It is the inner fold of mitochondria while others are functions of Lysosomes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16048,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Centrioles, xanthophyll, carotenoids, chlorophyll','','','Centrioles: These are present in the nucleus of animal cell while others are pigments of the plants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16049,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Bacteria : Prokaryote : : ---- : Eukaryote ','','','Fungi ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16050,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Green : Chlorophyll : : Orange : ------','','','Carotene','',0,0,0,NULL),(16051,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Colourless plastids : Leucoplast : : ---- : Chromoplast\n','','','Coloured plastids ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16052,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Who am I? : I am single layered, but maintain cellular osmotic pressure.','','','Vacuole','',0,0,0,NULL),(16053,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Who am I? : I support the cell, but I am not the cell wall. I have a body resembling net.\n','','','Endoplasmic reticulum','',0,0,0,NULL),(16054,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : Name the scientist who put forward the concept of cell.','','','Robert Hooke','',0,0,0,NULL),(16055,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am an organelle which controls all the activities of the cell.','','','Nucleus','',0,0,0,NULL),(16056,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am responsible for the transmission of hereditary characters of the successive generation.\n','','','Chromosomes','',0,0,0,NULL),(16057,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Who am I? : I am ATP producing factory.','','','Mitochondria','',0,0,0,NULL),(16058,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the chemical factory of the cell.','','','Mitochondria','',0,0,0,NULL),(16059,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the power house of the cell.','','','Mitochondria','',0,0,0,NULL),(16060,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am colourless, green or variously coloured. ','','','Plastids','',0,0,0,NULL),(16061,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Who am I? : Leaves are green because of me.','','','Chloroplast ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16062,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am bound by single membrane and filled with water and various substances.\n','','','Vacuoles','',0,0,0,NULL),(16063,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I have a large sized single vacuole in me.','','','Plant cell','',0,0,0,NULL),(16064,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the secretory organ of the cell.','','','Golgi complex','',0,0,0,NULL),(16065,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Chromosomes and genes can be viewed with the help of light microscope. \n','','','False. Chromosomes and genes can be viewed with the help of elctron microscope.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16066,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Proteins are conducted inside the cell with the help of endoplasmic reticulum.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16067,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Endocytosis is the process of gulp food or other substances from outer environment.  \n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16068,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : If raisins are kept in water, it swells and become turgid after sometimes.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16069,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Nucleus is the medium for cellular chemical reactions.\n','','','False. Cytoplasm is the medium for cellular chemical reaction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16070,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Nucleus has small pores, which enables materials to pass in and out of the nucleus.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16071,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Nucleus and chloroplast are two organelles that can be viewed by electron microscope. \n','','','False. Except nucleus and chloroplast, all other organelles are viewed by electron microscope.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16072,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Sieve tubes of the plant phloem lacks nucleus. \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16073,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Vacuoles pack materials synthesized inside the cell.\n','','','False. Golgi complex packs materials synthesized inside the cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16074,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Mitochondria oxides fats and carbohydrates with the help of enzymes.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16075,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The inner membrane of mitochondria is folded and these folds are known as cisternae. \n','','','False. The inner membrane of mitochondria is folded and those folds are known as cristae.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16080,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give any two functions of the following : Cell wall','','','i) To protect the cell from external shock.\nii) To support the cellular structure.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16081,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Give any two functions of the following : Plasma membrane ','','','i) Separates cell component from outer environment.\nii) Allows selected components to enter the cell. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16082,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Give any two functions of the following : Cytoplasm ','','','i) Cytoplasm is the medium for cellular chemical reactions.\nii) It protects the cell organelles.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16083,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Give any two functions of the following : Nucleus ','','','i) Controls all metabolic activities.\nii) Involves in transmission of heredity characters \'from parents to offspring.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16084,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give any two functions of the following : Endoplasmic Reticulum','','','i) It is a framework that support cells.\nii) Helps in conduction of protein.\n ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16085,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Give any two functions of the following : Lysosomes','','','i) Destroys and digests the viruses and bacteria.\nii) They digest the stored proteins and fats.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16086,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give any two functions of the following : Plastids ','','','They impart colour to flowers and fruit.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16087,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Give any two functions of the following : Vacuole ','','','i) Maintains the osmotic pressure of cell.\nii) It stores metabolic byproduct and end products. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16089,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Cell            ii) Endocytosis','','','i) It is the structural and functional unit of all forms of life.\nii) It is a process to gulp food and other substances from outer environment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16091,134,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Exocytosis          ii) Osmosis ','','','i) It is a process to excrete waste materials out of the cell.\nii) The travelling of water from a part with more water to a part with less water through a selectively permeable membrane is termed as Osmosis.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16093,134,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Organelle            ii) Genes ','','','i) It is a specialized subunit having specific function within the cell.\nii) Functional segments on chromosomes are called genes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16095,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Autolysis                ii) Plastids ','','','i) It is a process when Lysosomes destroy worn out cellular organelles and organic debris.\nii) The organelle which imparts colour to the plant cells is called as plastids.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16097,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Homeostasis ','','','The tendency of the cell which keeps the cellular environment constant inspite of the changes in the outer environment is called homeostasis.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16099,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Plasma membrane','','','i) Plasma Membrane is composed of lipids and proteins.\nii) It is a highly sensitive membrane.\niii) It allows only certain molecules to pass through while restricts the entry of other substances. Therefore, it is termed as selectively permeable membrane.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16101,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Types of Endoplasmic Reticulum','','','The Endoplasmic Reticulum is of two types:\ni) Rough ER [RER], which has ribosomes attached to its surface. It facilitates protein synthesis.\nii) Smooth ER [SER], It is devoid of ribosomes on its surface. It helps in lipid synthesis and various biochemical reactions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16103,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Functions of Mitochondria ','','','The functions of mitochondria are:\ni) They are known as the power house of the cell as they provide energy for various cellular activities by producing energy rich compound- ATP.\nii) They store the energy as ATP which is known as the energy currency of the cell.\niii) Mitochondria are also regarded as biochemical machines of the cell as they help in synthesis of proteins, carbohydrates, lipids, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16104,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Chloroplast ','','','i) Chloroplasts are important for photosynthesis that takes place in leaves.\nii) Chlorophyll in chloroplast traps solar energy and converts it to chemical energy.\niii) Stroma in the chloroplast contains enzymes, DNA, ribosomes and carbohydrates that are necessary for photosynthesis.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16106,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Camillo Golgi ','','','i) Golgi apparatus was first observed by Camillo Golgi in 1898.\nii) He developed the staining technique called \'Black reaction\' and with the help of it, he studied the nervous system.\niii) He won the Nobel Prize of 1906 along with Santiago Cajal for the study of structure of nervous system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16107,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Prokaryotic Cell and Eukaryotic Cell','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16108,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between :  Plant Cell and Animal Cell','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16109,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Cell wall and Cell Membrane ','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16110,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What would have happened? If red blood cells had Mitochondria.','','','i) Cellular aerobic respiration is carried out by Mitochondria.\nii) RBC\'s do not require much of energy because they lack almost every piece of cell machinery.\niii) They carry out energy production through fermentation.\niv) In mammals, Mitochondria is lost from RBCs during erythropoiesis.\nv) If RBCs had Mitochondria, then they would have used the oxygen for their use.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16111,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What would have happened? If there had been no difference between mitochondria and plastids. \n','','','i) Mitochondria is termed as the power house of the cell where as plastids are known as the kitchen of the cell.\nii) Plastids occur only in plant and algae cells but mitochondria are found in all eukaryotic cells.\niii) If there had been no difference between mitochondria and plastids, animals cell would also have varied colours just like the plant cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16112,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What would have happened? If genes had been absent on the chromosomes.\n','','','i) Each gene occupies a specific portion on a chromosome.\nii) Because genes provide instructions for making proteins, and proteins determine the structure and function of each cell in the body, it follows that they are responsible for all the characteristics one inherits.\niii) If genes had been absent on the chromosomes then there will be no genetic information of an organism from one generation to another.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16113,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What would have happened? If plasma membrane had not been selectively permeable.\n','','','i) Plasma membrane allows only certain substances to pass through and blocks others substances from entering the cell.\nii) If it had not been selectively permeable, then viruses could have also pass through it easily.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16114,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What would have happened? If plants lack anthocyanins.','','','i) Anthocyanins are naturally occurring compounds, involved in defense against the damaging effects of UV irradiation in plants.\nii) They are responsible for the attractive colours, because of which it attracts insects for pollination.\niii) Had this not been there the plants would be only appearing dull, with UV radiation in plenty of the plants.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16115,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Chloroplasts are important for photosynthesis.','','','i) Chlorophyll in the chloroplast traps solar energy.\nii) This trapped solar energy is converted into chemical energy.\niii) The stroma in chloroplast contains DNA, ribosomes and carbohydrates which are needed for photosynthesis.\niv) Hence, chloroplast photosynthesis. are important for photosynthesis.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16116,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Lysosomes are also called as ‘suicide bags’.','','','i) Lysosomes are single, membrane bound sacs.\nii) They are filled with digestive enzymes.\niii) When cell becomes old or is damaged, lysosomes burst and digest their own cells.\niv) Hence, lysosomes are also called as \'suicide bags\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16117,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Cell is called the structural and functional unit of life.\n','','','i) All living organisms are made up of cells and they start their life as single cells.\nii) Due to the organization of its membrane and organelles, cell performs basic functions such as respiration, obtaining nutrition, removal of waste material, photosynthesis, etc. \niii) Therefore, cell is called the structural and functional unit of life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16118,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Lysosomes are called demolition squads.','','','i) Lysosomes are cell\'s waste disposal system.\nii) They remove worn out and poorly working cellular organelles and all the organic debris from the cell.\niii) Therefore, lysosomes are called demolition squads.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16119,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the given figure of nucleus and answer the questions given below : a) Identify the part labelled as ‘X’ in the given figure and write its function.       b) Write the functions of nucleus.\n\n','','','a) The part labelled as \'X\' in the given figure is nuclear pore. Nuclear pores allow the passage of material in and out of the nucleus.\nb) The functions of the nucleus are as follows:\ni) It controls cell division and all the metabolic activities of the cell.\nii) Genes on the chromosomes present in the nucleus are responsible for transfer of hereditary characters from parents to offsprings.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16120,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give any four examples of single membrane bound organelle. ','','','Lysosome, vacuole, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi complex','',0,0,0,NULL),(16121,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How many types of pipelines are there in your building/home? What are their function? What will happen if they weren’t there?\n','','','i) There are two different pipelines in building.\nii) Their main function is to transfer all the waste material from building to the main drainage pipeline.\niii) If such system was absent all the waste would get accumulated in homes generating a foul smell followed by various diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16123,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is the function of Packaging Department of any factory?','','','The functions of packaging department are as follows:\ni) Presentation of product\nii) Protection of product\niii) Preservation of product and\niv) Convenience to customer    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16127,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How do substances travel in the cell?','','','i) Plasma membrane allows some substances to enter the cell, and prevents other substances.\nii) Due to this property, useful molecules like water, salt and oxygen enter the cell and CO₂ exits the cell.\niii) The food and other substances from outer environment is a process of endocytosis and waste materials are excreted through the process of exocytosis.    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16129,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Observe the following figures, write the names and complete the chart :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16130,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the cell organelle and answer the following questions : a) Name the cell organelle and label its parts.    b) Is it present in the plant cell or animal cell?       c) Give two important functions of the organelle.\n','','','a) The cell organelle is Endoplasmic Reticulum.\nb) It is present in both cells.\nc) i) It is the framework that support cells.\nii) Helps in conduction of protein.  ','',1,0,0,NULL),(16131,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the cell organelle and answer the following questions : a) Identify the organelle         b) Describe about the outer membrane and inner membrane.         c) What is it generally called as? Why?      d) State the functions of it.\n','','','a) Mitochondria\nb) Outer membrane is porous and inner membrane is deeply folded.\nc) It is called power house of cell because it supplies energy for various cell processes.\nd) i) It provides energy for cellular activities.\nii) Stores energy as ATP.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16132,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the following diagram and answer the following questions : a) What does it show?          b) What will happen if the water level is the same inside and outside the cell. What is the condition termed?           c) What will happen if the cytoplasm contracts? What is it called?\n','','','a) It shows movement of water.\nb) There will be no flow of water in either direction. Such solution is isotonic solution.\nc) When cytoplasm contracts, water moves out of cell and this process is called plasmolysis.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16133,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Keep 4-5 raisins in water and observe after an hour. Afterwards, keep the same raisins in sugar solution and observe after an hour. Note down the observations and discuss in the classroom.\n','','','i) When the raisins are placed in water, they gain water and swell up due to endosmosis.\nii) Since the composition of raisins is sugar, the concentration of water inside the cells is lower than that of the surrounding when placed in pure water. Thus, the water is taken up by the cells due to endosmosis.\niii) When the raisins are placed in sugar solution, they tend to lose water and shrink.\niv) This happens because the concentration of water outside the cell is lower than the concentration of water inside the cells. Hence, the raisins loses the water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16134,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Take a drop of water on a clear glass slide. Using an ice-cream spoon, gently scrape the inner surface of your cheek. With a needle, transfer a little material from spoon to the water drop on the slide and spread it evenly. Put a drop of methylene blue stain on the smear. Put a coverslip and observe under microscope.\n','','','i) After performing this activity, cheek cells are observed on the slide.\nii) Cheek cells are flat, irregular in shape and lack cell wall.\niii) When the cells are stained with methylene blue and observed under microscope, a distinct blue nucleus is observed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16135,134,'descriptive','3',3,1,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the given figure and answer the questions given below : a) Identify the structure shown in the diagram.        b) Identify the structure labelled as ‘X’.       c) The cell wall is mainly composed of which carbohydrate? \n','','','a) The diagram depicts the structure of the cell wall.\nb) The structure labelled as ‘X’ is cellulose microfibril.\nc) The cell wall is mainly composed of carbohydrates like cellulose, hemicellulose and pectin.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16136,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the given figure and answer the questions given below : a) Which instrument is shown in the given figure?        b) What is the purpose of this instrument?\n','','','a) The instrument in the given figure is electron microscope.\nb) i) It is used for visualization of cells with a better resolution than the compound microscope.\nii) All cell organelles apart from the nucleus and chloroplast can be observed only under the electron microscope.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16137,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain osmosis with examples.','','','Osmosis is the physical process by which water travels from a part or region having higher water concentration to a part with lesser water concentration through a selectively permeable membrane. With reference to a cell, osmosis can occur in the following ways : \ni) Isotonic solution: When the medium outside and inside the cell has the same water concentration, there is no movement of water molecules in either direction. \nii) Hypotonic solution (Endosmosis): When the cell has a lesser water concentration than the outside medium, water enters the cell by endosmosis. e.g. Raisins kept in water swell and become turgid after sometime. \niii) Hypertonic solution (Exosmosis): When the cell has a higher water concentration than the outside medium, water moves out of the cell by exosmosis. e.g. Contraction of cytoplasm and loss of water from an animal cell kept in a hypertonic solution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16138,134,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the figure of a plant and animal cell and answer the following questions : a) Which part controls the metabolic activities of a cell?         b) Which organelle is responsible for transmission of hereditary characteristics?       c) Which organelle provides energy for carrying out functions?         d) Name one organelle that is absent in the animal cell?\n\n','','','a) Nucleus\nb) Nucleus\nc) Mitochondria\nd) Cell wall','',1,0,0,NULL),(16139,134,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the structure and function of the nucleus with a labelled diagram.','','','Answer : ','Functions of Nucleus:\ni) It is the controlling centre of the cell.\nii) It controls the enzyme synthesis.\niii) It also controls the metabolic activities of the cell.\niv) It controls the inheritance of characters from parents to the offspring.\nv) It plays an important role in cell division. ',0,0,1,NULL),(16140,134,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the functions of Golgi complex and explain its structure with the help of a neat labelled diagram.\n','','','Answer : ','i) It is made up of 5-8 hollow and flat sacs placed parallel to each other.\nii) Those sacs are called \'cisternae\' and are filled with different enzymes.\niii) The proteins are enclosed in vesicles.\niv) Golgi complex is the secretory organ of the cell.\nv) It modifies, sorts, concentrates and transports the synthesized substances.\nvi) It helps in the formation of cell wall, plasma membrane and lysosomes.',0,0,1,NULL),(16141,134,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are cristae in Mitochondria? What do they contain? State the functions of mitochondria?\n','','','Answer : ','i) Outer membrane is porous and the inner one is deeply folded.\nii) These folds are called cristae.\niii) The inner cavity is filled with a proteinaceous gel like matrix that contains ribosomes, phosphate granules and DNA.\nFunctions of Mitochondria\ni) They are known as the power house of the cell as they provide energy for various cellular activities by producing energy rich compound- ATP.\nii) They store the energy as ATP which is known as the energy currency of the cell.\niii) Mitochondria are also regarded as biochemical machines of the cell as they help in synthesis of proteins, carbohydrates, lipids, etc.',0,0,1,NULL),(16142,134,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain with a neat labelled diagram structure and functions of Endoplasmic Reticulum.\n','','','Answer : ','i) The organelle which conducts various substances inside the cell is called endoplasmic reticulum.\nii) ER has a net like structure consisting of interconnected miniature tubes and sheets filled with fluid.\niii) ER is connected to nucleus from inner side and to plasma membrane from outer side. Rough ER has ribosome granules on its outer surface. \nTypes of Endoplasmic Reticulum:\ni) Rough ER [RER], which has ribosomes attached to its surface. It facilitates protein synthesis.\nii) Smooth ER [SER], is devoid of ribosomes on its surface. It helps in lipid synthesis and various biochemical reactions. \nFunctions of ER:\ni) It is the framework that supports cell.\nii) Conduction of proteins.\niii) Toxins that have entered the body through food, air and water are made water soluble by ER and then flushed out of the body.',0,0,1,NULL),(16144,134,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the structure and functions for vacuoles.','','','Vacuoles are storage sacks for solid or liquid contents. They do not have any basic shape or size. The structure of the vacuole changes according to the need of the cell. There are few small sized and temporary vacuoles in animal cells, while plant cells have one or more large vacuoles. Vacuole is bound by a single membrane i.e. it is one layered.\nFunctions:\ni) Vacuoles help to maintain osmotic pressure of the cell.\nii) They store metabolic by-products and end products e.g. Glycogen, proteins and water.\niii) They store waste products and food in animal cell. e.g. In amoeba, it stores food so it is called as food vacuole.\niv) In plant cells, vacuoles are full of cell sap and provide turgidity and rigidity to them.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16145,134,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe in detail the structure of the mitochondria with a neat labelled diagrams.\n','','','i) The mitochondria is a double membranous structure.\nii) The outer membrane is porous and the inner membrane is deeply folded.\niii) The folds on the inner membrane are known as cristae. These folds increase the surface area of the membrane and are the site of many chemical reactions occurring in the cell.\niv) The inner cavity of the mitochondria is filled with proteinaceous gel (matrix) containing ribosomes and DNA.\nv) There are comparatively fewer number of mitochondria in plant cells than animal cells.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16146,134,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a neat and labelled diagram of the chloroplast and explain its structure and function in plant cell.\n','','','i) Chloroplasts are green coloured plastids present in plant leaves which contain the pigment chlorophyll that is important for the process of photosynthesis in plants.\nii) Green coloured structures called grana, consisting of disc-like thylakoids are present in the stroma of the chloroplast.\niii) The chlorophyll present in chloroplasts trap and convert solar energy into chemical energy.\niv) Important molecules like enzymes, DNA and ribosomes that are essential for photosynthesis are present in the stroma of the chloroplast.\nv) Since, chloroplasts contain their own DNA and ribosomes they are capable of producing their own proteins and replicating by themselves.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16147,134,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain in detail the structure of the plasma membrane.','','','i) The plasma membrane is a thin, fragile and elastic covering that separates the components of a cell from the outer environment. \nii)  It is a double layered phospholipid structure in which proteins are embedded. \niii) The structure of the plasma membrane allows the passage of some substances into the cell while preventing the entry of others, hence it is called a selectively permeable membrane. \niv) This property of the cell membrane allows the entry of useful molecules like water, salt, oxygen and also the removal/exit of carbon dioxide and other waste materials from the cell. \nv) Homeostasis is established by the plasma membrane due to which changes in the external environment of the cell does not change the cellular environment.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16181,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which organ systems are present in Human Body?','','','Human body consist of various organ system like Respiratory system, Digestive system, Nervous system, Circulatory system, Excretory system, Reproductive system, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16183,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by blood circulation?','','','The process of pumping the blood towards various parts of the body and bringing it back towards the heart is called blood circulation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16184,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Which organs are present in blood circulatory system? ','','','The major components of the blood circulatory system are heart and blood vessels arteries, veins and capillaries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16185,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find odd one out : Trachea, alveoli, diaphragm, capillaries','','','Capillaries: Capillaries are a part of circulatory system, others are parts of respiratory system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16186,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Basophils, eosinophils, neutrophils, globulins','','','Globulins: Globulins are plasma proteins, others are types of WBCs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16187,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Heart, blood vessels, alveoli, capillaries','','','Alveoli: Alveoli is involved in respiratory system, others are involved in circulatory system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16188,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find odd one out : A. O, K, AB, B','','','K : A, O, AB, B are four main groups of human blood.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16189,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find odd one out : Blood plasma, platelets, blood transformation, blood corpuscles.\n','','','Blood transfusion: Blood plasma, platelets, blood corpuscles are components of the blood.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16190,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find odd one out : Neutrophils, globulins, albumins, prothrombin.','','','Neutrophils: It is a type of WBC while others are proteins present is plasma.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16191,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Universal donor : O : : Universal recipient : -----','','','AB','',0,0,0,NULL),(16192,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Arteries : Pure blood : : Veins : ------','','','Impure blood','',0,0,0,NULL),(16193,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Globulins : Protection : : ---- : Clotting ','','','Fibrinogen','',0,0,0,NULL),(16194,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy :  Normal systolic B. P. : 90-119 mm Hg : : Normal diastolic B. P. : ------\n','','','60 – 79 mm Hg','',0,0,0,NULL),(16199,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Cells need the supply of soluble nutrients and oxygen for energy production.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16200,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Energy production occur within the cells.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16201,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Each lung has a single layered covering called pleura.\n','','','False. Each lungs has a double layered covering called pleura.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16202,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Rich network of capillaries are present around each alveolus.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16203,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Carbon dioxide and water vapor is taken into the blood and oxygen is removed from the blood during exhalation.  \n','','','False. CO₂ from various parts of body is carried by blood towards the lungs and is expelled out driving exhalation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16204,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Digestive system performs the function of transport of various substances like water and waste materials through different organs.\n','','','False. Circulatory system performs the function of transport of various substances like water, and waste materials through different organs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16205,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The heart contracts and relaxes with a definite rhythm. This is called as beating of heart.\n','','',' True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16206,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Heart pumps about 75 ml of blood during each beat.\n','','',' True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16207,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Blood groups are heredity and depend upon the genes inherited from parents.\n','','',' True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16208,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Blood group ‘O’ was discovered by De Castello and Sturli in 1902.\n','','','False. Blood group ‘AB’ was discovered by Decastello and Sturli in 1902.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16209,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Healthy person of age more than 18 years can donate blood for 3-4 times a year.  \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16210,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The process where group of organs works together to perform a specific function.\n','','','Organ system ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16211,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The small compartments present in the lungs.','','','Alveoli','',0,0,0,NULL),(16212,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Iron containing protein in RBC’s','','','Hemoglobin ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16213,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : A muscular partition at the base of the thoracic cage','','','Diaphragm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16214,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Blood vessels that carry blood away from heart.','','','Arteries','',0,0,0,NULL),(16215,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Healthy person’s rate of heart beat per minute.','','','72 beat ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16216,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Consecutive single contraction and relaxation of heart. \n','','','Heart beat ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16217,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The pH of blood.','','','7.4','',0,0,0,NULL),(16218,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Two components of blood.','','','Plasma and blood cells','',0,0,0,NULL),(16219,135,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The instrument to measure blood group.','','','Sphygmomanometer','',0,0,0,NULL),(16220,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: i) Life processes          ii) Internal respiration','','','i) The vital processes essential for maintaining life are called life processes.\nii) Exchange of gases between cells and tissue fluid is called as Internal Respiration.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16222,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Diaphragm           ii) Blood circulation','','','i) A muscular partition is present at the base of thoracic cage. This partition is called as diaphragm.\nii) Process of pumping the blood towards the various parts of the body and bringing it back, towards the heart is called as blood circulation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16224,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Veins          ii) Arteries','','','i) Vessels carrying blood towards the heart from various parts of the body are called as veins.\nii) Blood vessels which carry the blood away from heart are called as arteries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16226,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Blood recipient           ii) Universal donor ','','','i) Person who receives the blood is referred as recipient.\nii) Person of the blood group \'O\' can donate the blood to the person having any other blood group. Hence, person of blood group \'O\' is called as universal donor.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16228,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Universal recipient         ii) Hematology ','','','i) Person of the blood group \'AB\' can receive the blood from the person having any other blood group. Here, the person is called universal recipient.\nii) The branch of medical science dealing with the study of blood is called Hematology.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16230,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is cellular respiration?','','','i) Dissolved nutrients like glucose gets slowly burnt (oxidised) with the help of oxygen and energy is released in the form of ATP.\nii) The waste materials like CO₂ and water vapour A are produced during this process.\niii) It can be summarised as follows :\n        C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + Energy','',0,0,0,NULL),(16231,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does exchange of gases take place lungs?','','',') i) Gaseous exchange occurs continuously.\nii) At the same time blood circulates around the alveoli.\niii) An iron containing protein - haemoglobin absorbs the oxygen from air within alveoli. At the same time CO₂ and H₂O move from blood into the alveoli.\niv) The oxygen is taken, absorbed into the blood and CO₂ and water vapour are removed from the blood and given out by exhalation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16232,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the importance of arteries, veins and capillaries.','','','i) Arteries carry blood away from the heart. They are deeply located and they do not have valves.\nii) Veins carry blood towards the heart from various parts of the body. They are superficially located and are provided with valves.\niii) Capillaries are extremely thin and made of a single layer of cells. Capillaries unite to form veins of larger diameter. Capillary network is present in each organ. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16233,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which life processes occur continuously in our body?','','','Respiratory process and circulation occur continuously in our body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16234,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which processes occur in our body during sound sleep? ','','','i) Nearly all significant physiological processes occur during sound sleep.\nii) Breathing pattern may change during sleep.\niii) Certain physiological activities are slowed down.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16235,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which organs are present in the respiratory system?','','','Human respiratory system consists of nose, pharynx, wind wipe, lungs and diaphragm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16236,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What flows out when we have an injury?','','','Blood flows out when we have an injury.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16237,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between : Internal respiration and External respiration ','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16238,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Aerobic Respiration and Anaerobic respiration','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16239,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between : Arteries and Veins','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16240,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Red blood cells and White blood cells','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16241,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the functions of the circulatory system.','','','Circulatory system transports various substances like soluble nutrients, hormones, oxygen, carbon dioxide, water and other waste products through different organs in the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16242,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define life processes. Give any two examples.','','','The vital processes essential for maintaining life are called life processes. e.g. Nutrition, respiration, digestion, excretion, circulation, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16243,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define organ system and what are the different organ systems present in our body? \n','','','i) An organ system is made up of group of organs collectively performing a specific function.\nii) The different organ systems present in human body are circulatory, respiratory, digestive, excretory, nervous, skeletal, muscular, reproductive, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16244,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Upward and downward movement of diaphragm occurs consecutively.\n','','','i) The diaphragm is a muscular partition present at the base of the thoracic cage.\nii) It is present between the thoracic cavity and abdominal cavity.\niii) Rising of ribs and lowering of diaphragm causes the decrease in pressure on lungs. Due to this, air moves into the lungs through nose. When ribs return to their original position, diaphragm rises, pressure on lungs increases.\niv) Due to this, air moves out from it, through nose.\nv) Thus, upward and downward movement of diaphragm is necessary to bring about the breathing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16245,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Human blood is red coloured.','','','i) Human blood have iron containing red coloured cells.\nii) The cells appear red due red coloured pigment called haemoglobin.\niii) Oxygen binds to the iron molecules, giving it a bright red colour.\niv) Hence, human blood is red coloured.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16246,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Blood donation is considered to be superior of all donation.\n','','','i) Blood donation without any expectation is always life saving.\nii) Person who donates the blood may be recipient in future.\niii) Blood is required in various situations like accidents, bleeding, parturition, surgical operation.\niv) Blood donated by a healthy person is used to save the life of needful person.\nv) Hence, blood donation is considered as the best donation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16247,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Person with blood group ‘O’ is considered as universal donor.\n','','','i) Person who donates blood is a blood donor.\nii) Person with blood group \'O\' can donate blood to the person having any other blood group.\niii) Hence, person of blood group \'O\' is called as a universal donor.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16248,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Food must have limited amount of salts.','','','i) Salts like NaCl controls the function of muscles and nerves.\nii) If the balance of minerals like Na (Sodium) and K (Potassium) are not maintained, it gives rise to hypertension leading the heart to perform more function than the normal condition.\niii) Therefore, it is advised to have limited salts in diet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16250,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How do the organisms like Amoeba, earthworm, cockroach, plants, various aquatic animals, birds respire?\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16251,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','One should not talk while eating. Why is it so?','','','i) Food pipe and wind pipe originate in pharynx.\nii) Wind pipe is present in front of the food pipe. There is a lid at the beginning of wind pipe.\niii) This lid closes the wind pipe while eating.\niv) So, if we talk while eating the food may enter the wind pipe and may cause choking and sometimes death.\nv) Therefore, it is recommended not to talk while eating. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16252,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why the veins provided with valves? Why the arteries have thick wall?','','','Veins have valves to ensure the blood flows in one direction and helps the blood to travel back to heart against gravity. Arteries have thick walls to withstand and absorb pressure which begins at heart.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16253,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','We can feel the pulse behind ears and near the heel of foot. How the pulses occur?\n','','','i) Pressure waves generated by heart in systole move the arterial walls.\nii) Forward movement of blood occur when the boundaries are compliant.\niii) The heart rate is greater or lesser depending on physiologic demand.\niv) Therefore, we feel pulse.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16254,135,'descriptive','3',3,1,'Text Book','','Find correlation between heart beats and pulses at wrist.','','','i) Heart rate is the number of times the heart beats in a minute.\nii) The pulse rate is exactly equal to the heart beat, as the contractions of the heart causes blood pressure in arteries to increase. This causes a noticeable pulse.\niii) Hence, the direct way to check heart rate is through pulse which can be easily felt at wrist.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16255,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Your neighboring uncle has been diagnosed with hypertension. What should he do to keep his blood pressure within normal range? \n','','','Here is the life styles that will help lower your blood pressure and keep it down.\ni) Lose extra fats and watch the waistline.\nii) One must exercise regularly.\niii) Eat a healthy diet.\niv) Reduce the intake of salt in one\'s food.\nv) Quit smoking or drinking alcohol if you are habituated.\nvi) Cut out on caffeine products.\nvii) Reduce your stress levels.\nviii) One must avoid fats, red meat and excess sugar. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16256,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Read the paragraph and identify the disease : Today her child has become one and half year old. However, the child does not seem to be healthy and happy. It was continuously crying and gradually becoming weak. It has shortness of breath. Its nails have become blue. \n','','','The child suffers from a serious heart problem. It may be hypoxia.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16257,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Observe the movements in the region below the thoracic cage, occurring during breathing and discuss in class.\n','','','i) When we inhale, ribs rise up, air is filled in lungs and diaphragm lowers.\nii) When we exhale, ribs comes to its position, lungs shrink and diaphragm rises.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(16258,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw neat labelled diagram : Respiratory system','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16259,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw neat labelled diagram : Blood vessels ','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16260,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw neat labelled diagram : Structure of heart','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16261,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart :','','','Diagram : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16262,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16263,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the process shown in the given figure :','','','i) The deoxygenated blood from the right atrium enters the right ventricle. Tricuspid valve prevents the backflow of blood.\nii) The oxygenated blood from the left atrium enters the left ventricle. The backflow of blood is prevented by bicuspid valve.\niii) The deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle then enters the pulmonary artery, which carries blood to lungs for oxygenation.\niv) The oxygenated blood from the left ventricle enters the aorta, which carries blood to all parts of the body. The backflow of blood is prevented by semilunar valves.\nv) The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from all parts of the body through superior vena cava, inferior vena cava and coronary sinus.\nvi) The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs through two pairs of pulmonary veins.\nvii) These events occur within one heart beat.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16264,135,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the given diagram and answer the given questions : a) Identify ‘X’ in the given diagram.         b) Describe the given diagram.\n','','','a) In the given diagram ‘X’ represents capillaries.\nb) i) Arteries gradually branch out and decrease in diameter as they spread in the body and form fine hair-like vessels called as capillaries.\nii) Walls of capillaries are extremely thin and made up of a single layer of cells, this aids in exchange of materials like oxygen, nutrients, hormones, vitamins, etc.\niii) These substances are sent towards the cells and waste materials from the cells move into the blood.\niv) Capillaries unite to form venules. An extensive network of capillaries is present in each organ.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16266,135,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16267,135,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain with the help of neat labelled diagram the structure of heart.','','','i) Heart is present at the centre of the thoracic cavity.\nii) Size of our heart is equal to one’s one fist and is about 360 grams.\niii) It is covered by a, double layered pericardial membrane.\niv) Human heart is a muscular organ, made up of involuntary vascular muscles.\nv) A fluid is present between two membranes and the heart is protected from friction and mechanical shock.\nvi) The heart is divided into left and right compartment by a vertical partition. Each is again divided into two chambers.\nvii) Thus, heart consists four chambers - Upper chambers are called atria and lower chambers are termed as ventricles. ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16268,135,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the structure and function of human blood.','','','i) Blood is a red coloured fluid material.\nii) It is a fluid connective tissue.\niii) Oxygenated blood is red coloured, salty in taste with a pH of 7.4.\niv) It is mainly composed of two components Plasma and blood cells.\na) Plasma :\ni) It is a pale yellow alkaline fluid that contains 90- 92% water, 6-8% proteins, and 1 - 2% inorganic salts.\nii) Albumin distributes water all over the body.\niii) Globulins are concerned with protection.\niv) Fibrinogen and Prothrombin helps in blood clotting process.\nv) Inorganic ions - Ca, Na, K control the function muscles and nerves. \nb) Blood cells :\ni) RBC : Small, circular, enucleated, red due to haemoglobin, produced in bone marrow and their life is for 100 - 127 days.\nii) WBC : Large, nucleated and colourless. Produced in red bone marrow and act as soldiers of our body. There are 5 types of WBC - basophils, eosinophils, neutrophils, monocytes and lymphocytes. They protect us from microbial diseases.\niii) Platelets : They are extremely small and disc shaped. They participate in blood clotting process. Functions of blood are:\na) Transport of gases: Oxygen is supplied to the tissues and CO₂ from tissues to lungs.\nb) Transport of Nutrients: Nutrients like glucose, amino acids, fatty acids are transported to each cell.\nc) Transport of waste materials: Wastes like ammonia, urea, creatinine are released by tissues into blood.\nd) Protection: Antibodies protect the body from microbes and other harmful particles.\ne) Transport of enzymes and hormones: Transport to the production to the site of their action.\nf) It helps in thermoregulation.\ng) It maintains balance of minerals.\nh) A clot is formed with the protein called fibrinogen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16269,135,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the importance and need of blood donation.','','','i) Blood is required in various situations like accidents, bleeding, parturition, surgical operations, etc.\nii) Blood donated by a healthy person is used to save a life of a needy person.\niii) Blood cannot be produced by artificial ways.\niv) Blood production occurs continuously in our body.\nv) About 350 ml of blood can be collected from a person during donation and our body can restore it within 24 hours.\nvi) Healthy people of age more than 18 can donate blood for 3 - 4 times a year.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16270,135,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the functional correlation of circulatory system with respiratory digestive and excretory system.\n','','','i) Various substances like water, hormones, oxygen, soluble nutrients and waste materials from different organs are transported by the circulatory system.\nii) The inhaled oxygen during respiration reaches alveoli and is diffused into the blood. This diffused oxygen is then transported to the cells in various parts of the body for cellular respiration.\niii) Carbon dioxide being a waste product of cellular respiration is diffused in the alveoli, which is transported into the bronchioles and reaches trachea through bronchi. From there it is exhaled out of the body through nose.\niv) The circulatory system needs the organs of the digestive system to transport nutrients to the body cells. Simple nutrients like glucose, fatty acids, amino acids, etc., are carried by the blood from the wall of alimentary canal to each cell of the body.\nv) Ammonia, urea, creatinine, etc. are nitrogenous wastes, which are released by the tissues for excretion, carried by blood.\nvi) In this way, different body systems like circulatory, respiratory, digestive and excretory systems are functionally correlated.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16271,135,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the diagram and describe it briefly.','','','i) The given diagram represents the structure of artery and vein.\nii) Artery carries oxygenated blood, whereas vein carries deoxygenated blood.\niii) Arteries branch out forming hair-like vessels with decreased diameter, called as capillaries. Capillaries unite to form venules, which further unite to form larger veins.\niv) Tunica intima is the innermost layer present in the artery and vein.\nv) Arteries do not have valves and veins possess valves.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16272,135,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Master ‘X’ met with an accident and suffered major injuries. He was severely bleeding and was immediately taken to the hospital. Doctor advised for blood transfusion as ‘X’ had suffered from heavy blood loss. Unaware of the procedure his family panicked and raised some questions to the doctor. \nFollowing are the questions raised by the parents, what would be the possible answers : \na) Why is blood transfusion done?          \nb) From where will the family donate blood to Master ‘X’?           \nc) Can any person from the family donate blood to Master ‘X’?           \nd) What do you mean by blood groups?\n','','','a) Blood transfusion is done to compensate shortage of blood in the body that has occurred due to heavy blood loss.\nb) The blood required for the transfusion would be obtained from blood banks.\nc) No. The donor\'s blood group needs to match the patient\'s blood group. If transfusion is done without matching the blood group it may prove fatal for the patient.\nd) i) Blood groups depend upon genes inherited from the parents (hereditary).\nii) Depending upon the presence or absence of antigens and antibodies, four main blood groups are formed. They are A, B, AB and O.\niii) Also, depending on the presence of Rh antigen on red blood cells, they are further classified as \'Rh\' positive and \'Rh\' negative. For e.g. \'A\' positive and \'A\' negative.\niv) A person with \'O\' blood group can donate blood to a person with any other blood group (A, B, AB, O) and is known as universal donor. A person with \'AB\' blood group can receive blood from a person with any other blood group (A, B, AB, O) and is known as universal recipient.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16306,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Hydrochloric acid, Carbonic acid, Sulphuric acid, Nitric Acid\n','','','Carbonic acid: It is a weak acid while others are strong acids.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16307,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Citric acid, Tartaric acid, Sulphuric acid, Ascorbic acid','','','Sulphuric acid: It is a strong acid while others are weak acids.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16308,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Sodium hydroxide, Calcium hydroxide, Ammonium hydroxide, Potassium hydroxide.\n','','','Ammonium hydroxide: It is a strong base while others are weak bases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16309,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Phenolphthalein, Methyl orange, Turmeric, salt','','','Salt : Others are indicators.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16310,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Plaster of Paris, Chalk, Lactic acid, baking soda','','','Lactic acid: It is an acid while others are bases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16311,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Curd, lemon, soap, orange','','','Soap is a basic substance while the rest are acidic substances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16312,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Methyl orange, hibiscus, rose, turmeric','','','Methyl orange is a synthetic indicator while rest are natural indicators.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16317,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Vinegar : Acetic acid : : Tomato : ------','','','oxalic acid','',0,0,0,NULL),(16318,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Lemon : ----- : : Tamarind : Tartaric acid ','','','citric acid','',0,0,0,NULL),(16319,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Oxalic acid : Organic acid : : nitric acid : ------','','','mineral acid','',0,0,0,NULL),(16320,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Ammonium hydroxide : ----- : : Calcium hydroxide : Ca(OH₂)\n','','','NH₄OH','',0,0,0,NULL),(16321,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Hydrogen ions : Acid : : Hydroxide ions : ------','','','Alkali','',0,0,0,NULL),(16322,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Reaction of acid with carbonates : CO₂ : : Reaction of acid with metal : -------\n','','','H₂','',0,0,0,NULL),(16323,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Carbonic acid is a weak acid.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16324,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Phenolphthalein becomes pink in acid.\n','','','False. Phenolphthalein becomes pink in basic.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16325,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Acids are bitter in taste.\n','','','False. Acid are sour in taste.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16326,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Oxides of metals are alkalis in nature.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16327,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Salt is acidic.\n','','','False. Salt is neutral','',0,0,0,NULL),(16328,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Metal corrodes due to slats.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16329,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Salts are neutral.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16330,136,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Acids react with metal to form CO₂ gas.\n','','','False. Acids reacts with metal to form hydrogen gas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16331,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Antacids are used to control blood pressure.\n','','','False. Antacids are used to control hyper acidity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16332,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Blue litmus turns red in acid.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16333,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Bases change red litmus paper to blue.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16334,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the chemical names from given formula : H₂SO₄','','','Sulphuric acid','',0,0,0,NULL),(16335,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the chemical names from given formula : Ca(OH₂)','','','Calcium hydroxide','',0,0,0,NULL),(16336,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the chemical names from given formula : HCl','','','Hydrochloric acid','',0,0,0,NULL),(16337,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the chemical names from given formula : NaOH','','','Sodium hydroxide','',0,0,0,NULL),(16338,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the chemical names from given formula : KOH','','','Potassium hydroxide','',0,0,0,NULL),(16339,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the chemical names from given formula : NH₄OH','','','Ammonium hydroxide','',0,0,0,NULL),(16340,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the chemical names from given formula : Mg(OH)₂','','','Magnesium hydroxide','',0,0,0,NULL),(16341,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the chemical names from given formula : HNO₃','','','Nitric acid','',0,0,0,NULL),(16342,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the chemical formula : Milk of Magnesia ','','','Mg(OH)₂','',0,0,0,NULL),(16343,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the chemical formula : Nitric acid','','','HNO₃','',0,0,0,NULL),(16344,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the chemical formula : Baking soda','','','NaHCO₃','',0,0,0,NULL),(16345,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the chemical formula : Carbonic acid','','','H₂CO₃','',0,0,0,NULL),(16346,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : Laboratory indicators','','','Methyl orange, litmus, phenolphthalein, methyl red','',0,0,0,NULL),(16347,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : Natural indicators ','','','Turmeric, juice of red cabbage, radish, tomato','',0,0,0,NULL),(16348,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : Carbonic or natural acids','','','Citric acid, lactic acid, tartaric acid','',0,0,0,NULL),(16349,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : Mineral acids','','','Hydrochloric acid, Sulphuric acid, nitric acid','',0,0,0,NULL),(16350,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : Bases','','','Baking soda, caustic soda, lime water','',0,0,0,NULL),(16351,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Indicator ','','','The substance which changes its colour in the presence of acid or base is called an indicator.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16352,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define: i) Acid            ii) Base','','','i) Acid is such a substance which gives hydrogen (H⁺) ions in solution state.\nii) Alkali is a substance whose solution in water gives hydroxide (OH⁻) ion.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16354,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What happen when and why? Sour substances like lime juice, tamarind water falls on a Shahabad stones or kitchen platform.\n','','','i) When lime juice or tamarind water falls on a shahabad stone which contains calcium carbonate, the acid in lime juice reacts with it.\n\nCalcium Carbonate + citric acid (lemon juice) → Calcium citrate + CO₂↑ \nii) A white mark of calcium citrate is left on the tile which can be washed with water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16355,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happen when and why? Concentrated solution of acid falls on skin.\n','','','i) The skin gets burnt when the concentrated solution of acid falls on skin.\nii) Even their vapours if inhaled can be harmful to health.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16356,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happen when and why?  Yellow food stain turns red when washing with soap.\n','','','i) The cloth which has a yellow food stain is soaked in soap water.\nii) The yellow food stain turns red when soaked in soap water.\niii) The soap water is alkaline in nature. The yellow colour of the stain is due to turmeric which is an indicator.\niv) Turmeric turns red in alkaline solutions. Hence yellow stain turns red in soap water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16357,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Classify the following substances into acidic, basic and neutral group : HCl, NaCl, MgO, KCl, CaO, H₂SO₄, HNO₃, H₂O, Na₂CO₃.\n','','','Acidic group : HCl, H₂SO₄, HNO₃\nBasic group : MgO, CaO, Na₂CO₃\nNeutral group : NaCl, KCl, H₂O','',0,0,0,NULL),(16358,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Identify the following solutions, whether they are acidic or alkali : Vinegar, lemon, potash, curd, caustic soda, lime water, orange, tomato, milk of magnesia.\n','','','Acid : Vinegar, lemon, curd, orange, tomato\nAlkali : potash, caustic soda, lime water, milk of magnesia\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16359,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Typical indicators are used to recognize acid or base','','','i) Acids taste sour and bases taste astringent/ bitter.\nii) Touching or tasting of acid or base is very harmful.\niii) Hence typical indicators are used to recognize them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16360,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Toothpaste is used for brushing teeth.','','','i) Toothpaste is a base.\nii) It neutralises acidic nature of our mouth which otherwise will lead to tooth decay.\niii) Hence to protect us from tooth decay, tooth paste is used for brushing teeth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16361,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : The iron knife shines better after cutting the sour fruits. \n','','','i) Iron knife gets a coating of Iron oxide (rust) by the action of oxygen and moisture in the air.\nii) The acids present in the sour fruits like lemon and raw mangoes react with the rust.\niii) Lemon contains citric acid. Raw mangoes contain citric, malic and tartaric acid.\niv) These acids act as a cleansing agent.\nv) Hence, iron knife shines after cutting lemon or raw mangoes.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16362,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Concentrated solution of alkalis is harmful to skin.','','','i) Alkalies such as sodium hydroxide and potassium hydroxide are strong bases and are caustic in nature.\nii) Concentrated solution of such alkalis burns skin because it decomposes the proteins present in skin.\nHence, concentrated solution of alkalis is harmful to skin.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16363,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason :  Antacids are used to control hyperacidity in stomach.  \n','','','i) Hydrochloric acid is produced in our stomach.\nii) This acid helps in the digestion process.\niii) However, an excess of hydrochloric acid causes indigestion.\niv) Antacids such as milk of magnesia are alkalies and they neutralize the excess of acid present in stomach.\nHence, antacids are used to control hyperacidity in stomach.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16364,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Acid and Alkali','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16366,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Domestic or Natural indicators ','','','i) On non-availability of laboratory indicator, \'natural indicators\' can be made by using several domestic substances.\nii) Turmeric acts as an indicator. Yellow colour of turmeric turns red when it comes in contact with base/ alkali.\niii) Natural indication can also be prepared from red cabbage, radish, tomato and similarly from flowers like hibiscus and rose.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16367,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why indicator does not get affected by salt?','','','i) The substances which undergo change in their colours in presence of acid or base are called indicators.\nii) Salt is neutral substance and does not have H⁺ and OH⁻ ions.\niii) Salts do not have any effect on indicators.\niv) Hence indicator does not get affected by salts.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16368,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which substances are produced by neutralization process?','','','Salt and water are formed by combination of acid and alkali in neutralization process.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16369,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which are the industrial uses of acids?','','','i) Acids are used in the production of chemical fertilizers.\nii) Acids are used in the production of explosives, oil purification, medicines, dyes and paint.\niii) Hydrochloric acid is used for the preparation of different types of chloride salts.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16371,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give properties of alkali/bases.','','','i) Alkali has astringent/ bitter taste.\nii) They are slippery to touch.\niii) Alkali contain hydroxide ion (OH) as the main constituent.\niv) Metal oxides are generally alkaline in nature','',0,0,0,NULL),(16375,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','List the properties of base.','','','i) Alkalies have bitter taste.\nii) They turn red litmus paper to blue.\niii) They are slippery to touch.\niv) They give hydroxide (OH⁻) ions in water.\nv)  Metal oxides are generally alkaline in nature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16376,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain neutralization reaction.','','','i) Acids contain hydrogen ions (H⁺) while bases contain hydroxide (OH⁻) ion.\nii) By combination of acid and alkali, salt and water are formed.\nThis chemical reaction is called neutralization reaction.\n\nE.g. Acid + Base → Salt + Water\n\nHCl          +            NaOH        →        NaCl + H₂O\nHydrochloric        sodium                  Sodium\n Acid                    hydroxide             chloride','',0,0,0,NULL),(16378,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of bases (Alkali).','','','i) Lime water [Ca(OH)₂]\nii) Baking soda (NaHCO₃)\niii) Caustic soda (NaOH)\niv) Potash (KOH)','',0,0,0,NULL),(16379,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on domestic or natural indicators.','','','i) Domestic or natural indicators are often used on non availability of laboratory indicators.\nii) These indicators are made from natural substances such as hibiscus, rose, turmeric, red cabbage leaves, red tomato, etc.\nE.g. Yellow turmeric stain turns red upon washing with soap. This is because turmeric reacts with alkaline material of soap. Hence, turmeric acts as an indicator.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16380,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of mineral acids.','','','i) Hydrochloric acid (HCl)\nii) Nitric acid (HΝΟ₃)\niii) Sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄) \niv) Carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16381,136,'descriptive','2',0,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify mineral and organic acids from the following : Carbonic acid, acetic acid, oxalic acid, nitric acid. \n','','','a) Mineral acids : carbonic acid, nitric acid \nb) Organic acids : acetic acid, oxalic acid\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16382,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Select the appropriate options and complete the following paragraph : [strong, mild, organic, acetic acid, citric acid, tartaric acid, lactic acid, oxalic acid]\nVarious foodstuff that we use daily contain some ----- acids. These are ----- in nature as compared to minerals acids. Lemon and orange contain ---- while tomato contains ------. Tamarind is a common ingredient in our foods. It contains ------. Vinegar, which is used for salad dressing, contains -----.\n','','','organic, mild, citric acid, oxalic acid, tartaric acid, acetic acid','',0,0,0,NULL),(16383,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the nature of toothpaste? How do they prevent tooth decay?','','','i) Toothpastes are alkaline in nature. \nii) They neutralize the excess acid produced by bacteria present in the mouth and thus prevent tooth decay.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16384,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can phenolphthalein help in the identification of acid or base? Explain.\n','','','i) Phenolphthalein is an indicator which can help to identify whether the solution is acidic or basic. \nii) Identification can be done by observing the colour change of the solution. \niii) In acidic solution, phenolphthalein does not show any colour change. \niv) In basic solution, phenolphthalein gives pink colour.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16385,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16386,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16387,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16388,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the following diagram, identify the nature of solution in each test tube and the colour of the solution after adding indicator in each test tube. \n','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16389,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','We consume many food items in our daily life e.g. lemon, tamarind, tomato, sugar, vinegar, salt etc. Do all items taste similar? \n','','','No, all items do not taste similar, some of these items taste bitter, some taste sweet and some taste salty.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16390,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write the taste of lemon, sugar, curd, lime, baking soda, amla, tamarind, raw mango, pomegranate, water (Sour, astringent, sweet, bitter, tasteless)\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16393,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','By squeezing lemon on a piece of rock the gas liberated turned lime water milky. Which compound is present in rock?\n','','','i) The compound in rock is calcium carbonate.\nThe following reaction takes place : \n   Calcium    +  citric acid       →      calcium    +  carbon\nCarbonate       (lime juice)              citrate      dioxide gas\nii) Liberated CO₂ gas turns lime water milky.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16394,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The label on the bottle of chemical is spoiled. How will you find whether the chemical is acidic or not? \n','','','i) If I am in a laboratory, I will use laboratory indicators like litmus paper to find whether the given chemical is acidic or not.\nii) At home I can use natural indicators like turmeric to do the same. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16395,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Take baking soda. Add little water to it. Add this solution on to lime juice, vinegar, orange juice, apple juice, etc. and note the findings. What do you observe on addition of baking soda solution in the fruit juice? Whether bubbles formed or effervescence came out of fruit juice?\n','','','When baking soda solution is added in the fruit juice or vinegar, either bubbles are formed or effervescence is produced. This is basically because the fruit juice is acidic.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16397,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What happens when lime juice falls on kitchen platform?','','','i) Shahabad stones or kitchen platform contains calcium carbonate.\nii) Calcium carbonate reacts with the acids present in substances like lime juice and tamarind water.\niii) As a result, white deposit on the surface of the shahabad stones or kitchen platform are formed which can be cleaned-using water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16398,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on indicators used in laboratory.','','','i) In laboratory, generally litmus paper is used to test acid or alkali.\nii) Acid turns blue litmus paper to red while base turns red litmus paper to blue.\niii) Other indicators such as methyl orange, methyl red and phenolphthalein are often used in liquid state (solution form) to test acid or base.\niv) Methyl orange turns pink in acid and yellow in alkali while methyl red remains red in acid and turns yellow in alkali.\nv) Phenolphthalein remains colourless in acid while turns pink in alkali.\nvi) Universal indicators in liquid state change colour in acid or alkali.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16399,136,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain neutralization with two examples in our day to day life.','','','i) Acid contains (H⁺) hydrogen ions and alkali contain (OH⁻) hydroxide ions.\nii) Salt and water are formed by combination of acid and base.\niii) Acid + Base → salt + water\n      HCI     +      NaOH    →     NaCl         +      H₂O\n  Hydrochloric  Sodium        Sodium                 water \n   acid                    hydroxide   chloride\niv) This chemical reaction is called neutralization reaction.\nv) Hydrochloric acid is present in our stomach, to ease digestion process.\nvi) Antacids are used to control hyper acidity.\nvii) Antacids contain milk of magnesia (Mg(OH)₂), an alkaline medicine which will neutralize excess acid present in stomach.\nviii) The acidity of soil increases due to excessive use of chemical fertilizers. Lime stone or lime water is mixed in the acidic soil. Such alkaline substances neutralize excess acid present in the roll.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16400,136,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Sulphuric acid has highest importance in chemical industry? Why? ','','','i) Sulphuric acid is a strong colourless mineral acid that is soluble in water at all concentrations.\nii) Sulphuric acid is used for ore producing, oil refining, fertilizer manufacturing and waste water processing.\niii) It is also used in the production of dyes, plastics, glue, film and explosives.\niv) Thus, sulphuric acid has highest importance in chemical industry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16401,136,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give properties of acid and bases.','','','Properties of acid\ni) Acids have sour taste.\nii) Acids are either mineral acids or natural (organic) acid.\niii) Acids give hydrogen (H⁺) ions in water.\niv) Acids react with metals and liberate hydrogen (H₂) gas.\nv) Acids react with carbonates and liberate carbon dioxide (CO₂) gas.\nvi) Acids turn blue litmus paper to red. \nProperties of base :\ni) Alkalies have bitter taste.\nii) They turn red litmus paper to blue.\niii) They are slippery to touch.\niv) They give hydroxide (OH⁻) ions in water.\nv) Metal oxides are generally alkaline in nature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16402,136,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16403,136,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16404,136,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16405,136,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Acids present in food stuffs are called natural or organic acids. Such acids are weak acids and less harmful as compared to strong acids. Strong acids are minerals acids. Concentrated solution of strong acid is harmful to health. Direct contact of such acids causes severe burns. Hence, strong acids are converted to dilute acid by slowly adding them to water. \nBased on the above information, answer the following questions : a) Name the organic acids present in tomato and lemon.         b) Give chemical formulae of four mineral acids.          c) How will you prepare dilute H₂SO₄ from its concentrated solution?\n\n','','','a) The organic acid present in tomato is oxalic acid while the organic acid present in lemon is citric acid.\nb) Chemical formulae of four mineral acids:\n      i) H₂SO₄              ii) HCl                 iii) HNO₃            iv) H₂CO₃\nc) i) When concentrated acid is added to water, a large amount of heat is generated which may cause explosion. Therefore, one should never add water to concentrated acid.\nii) To prepare dilute solution from concentrated H₂SO₄, small amount of concentrated H₂SO₄ is taken and added it slowly to a large amount of water with continuous stirring of mixture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16434,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the methods of classification of changes?','','','Changes are classified as physical change and chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16435,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is the difference between physical change and chemical change?','','','In physical change, the composition of the original substance does not change whereas in chemical change, the composition of the original substance changes and new substances are formed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16436,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is an ionic or electrovalent bond?','','','The chemical bond formed due to an electrostatic force of attraction between the oppositely charged cation and anion is called and ionic bond or an electrovalent bond.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16437,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is ionic compound?','','','The compound formed by means of one or more ionic bonds is called ionic compound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16438,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the difference between word equation and chemical equation?','','','A word equation is written using the names of the reactants and products whereas a chemical equation is written using chemical formulae of reactants and products.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16439,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : A metal wire gets red hot, Quick lime mixed in water, rusting of iron, darkening of a cut potato.\n','','','A metal wire gets red hot : It is a physical change while others are chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16440,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Respiration, precipitation of calcium carbonate, softening of hard water, photosynthesis \n','','','Softening of hard water : It is a man-made chemical change while others are natural chemical change. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16441,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : NaCl, MgCl₂, KF, H₂O','','','H₂O : It is formed by covalent bond, while others are formed by ionic bond.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16442,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Na, Mg, Cl, K.','','','Cl. It forms anion while the rest form cations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16443,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Melting of ice, rusting of iron, Ripening of fruits, spoiling of food. \n','','','Melting of ice. It is an example of physical change while the rest are chemical changes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16444,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd one out : Combustion of fuels, softening of hard water, photosynthesis, bursting of hard water.\n','','','Photosynthesis : It is a natural chemical change while the rest are man-made chemical changes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16445,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Respiration : Natural chemical change : : Combustion of fuels : ------\n','','','man-made chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16446,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Sodium carbonate : Na₂CO₃ : : Calcium carbonate : -------\n','','','CaCO₃','',0,0,0,NULL),(16447,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : MgCl₂ : Ionic bond : : HCl : ------','','','covalent bond','',0,0,0,NULL),(16448,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Na : 2, 8, 1 : : Cl : ------','','','2, 8, 7','',0,0,0,NULL),(16452,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : In chemical change the chemical composition of original substance remains same.\n','','','False. In chemical change the chemical composition of original substance is changed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16453,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Respiration is a continuously occurring biological process.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16454,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Noble gases do not form any chemical bond.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16455,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The valency of hydrogen is two.\n','','','False. The valency of hydrogen is one.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16456,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : In H₂O molecule, the oxygen atom completes its octet by forming two ionic bonds.\n','','','False. In H₂O molecule, the oxygen atom complete its octet by forming two covalent bonds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16457,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Combustion is a reversible change.\n','','','False. Combustion is an irreversible change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16458,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Softening of hard water is possible.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16459,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Oxidation of glucose is a physical change.\n','','','False. Oxidation if glucose is chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16460,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Wood, Coal, petrol, or cooking gas are burnt for getting energy.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16461,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Green plants perform respiration in sunlight.\n','','','False. Green plants perform photosynthesis in sunlight.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16462,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of : Natural chemical change','','','Respiration and photosynthesis ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16463,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of : Man-made Chemical change','','','Combustion of fuels and cleaning Shahabad tile with dilute hydrochloric acid.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16464,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Covalent bond','','','The chemical bond formed by sharing of valence electrons of two atoms with each other is called a covalent bond.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16465,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Ionic or electrovalent bond','','','The chemical bond formed due to an electrostatic force of attraction between the oppositely charged cation and anion is called an ionic bond or electrovalent bond.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16466,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Reactant          ii) Products','','','i) Original substances written on the left side of the arrow in chemical equation are called reactants.\nii) Newly formed substances written on the right side of the arrow in chemical equation are called products.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16469,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens during combustion of carbon?','','','i) During the combustion process, carbon combines with oxygen in air and the product carbon dioxide is formed.\nii) Word equation : Carbon + oxygen → Carbon dioxide\niii) Chemical equation : C + O₂ → CO₂\niv) Combustion of carbon in the fuels is a fast and irreversible chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16470,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How to soften hard water?','','','To soften hard water, a solution of washing soda is added to it.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16471,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why valency of hydrogen is one?','','','i) A hydrogen atom contains one electron on its outermost shell.\nii) Its duplet is short of one electron.\niii) It requires one electron to complete its duplet.\niv) Therefore, the valency of hydrogen is one.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16472,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Physical change and Chemical Change','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16473,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Covalent bond and Ionic bond','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16474,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Respiration ','','','i) Respiration is a continuously occurring biological process.\nii) In this process, we inhale the air and exhale carbon dioxide and water vapour.\niii) Glucose in the cells reacts with oxygen in the inhaled air to form carbon dioxide and water.\niv) Word equation :\n      Glucose + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + water\nv) Chemical equation :\n       C₆H₁₂O₆ +6O₂  → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O','',0,0,0,NULL),(16475,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on :  Photosynthesis','','','i) Green plants perform photosynthesis in sunlight.\nii) Photosynthesis is chemical change in a which plants use sunlight in the presence of chlorophyll to make their own food.\niii) Word equation :','',0,0,1,NULL),(16478,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are different ways of representing a chemical reaction? Give one example of each?\n','','','A chemical reaction can be represented in two different ways; word equation and chemical equation.\ni) Word equation:\n    E.g. The word equation for the reaction between glucose and oxygen is as follows:  Glucose + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + Water\nii) Chemical equation:\n    E.g. The chemical equation (unbalanced) for the reaction between glucose and oxygen is as follows: C₆H₁₂O₆ + O₂  → CO₂ + H₂O','',0,0,0,NULL),(16479,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Noble gases do not form chemical bond under normal condition.\n','','','i) To form a chemical bond, an atom uses (exchanges or shares) its valence electrons to attain electronic configuration of complete octet or duplet.\nii) The number of chemical bonds formed by an atom is equal to its valency. Noble gases have electronic configurations of complete octet or duplet. It means that the valency of noble gases is zero.\nHence, noble gases do not form chemical bond under normal condition. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16480,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The number of ionic bonds in magnesium chloride molecule is two.\n','','','i) The electronic configuration of magnesium is (2, 8, 2) and that of chlorine is (2, 8, 7).\nii) The valency of magnesium is 2 as it has two electrons in its valence shell. The valency of chlorine is 1 as it has 7 electrons in its valence shell and it just needs one electron to complete the octet. Hence, the charge on magnesium is +2 and the charge on chlorine is -1.\niii) The valency of an ion is equal to its magnitude of positive or negative charge. \niv) The number of chemical bonds formed by an atom is equal to its valency.\nHence, the number of ionic bonds in magnesium chloride molecule is two and its chemical formula is MgCl₂.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16481,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show diagrammatically the formation of Sodium chloride.','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16482,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show diagrammatically the formation of Magnesium chloride.','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16483,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write word equation and chemical equation (unbalance) for the removal of chloride salt of calcium from hard water using washing soda.\n','','','i) The word equation is,\n Calcium  + Sodium carbonate  Calcium  + Sodium \nChloride     (washing soda)                  carbonate    chloride\n ii) The chemical equation is, \n      CaCl₂ + Na₂CO₃  CaCO₃ + NaCl','',0,0,0,NULL),(16484,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show diagrammatic representation for the formation of Ca²⁺O²⁻ from ₂₀Ca and ₈O\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16486,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when? Wood, coal, petrol or cooking gas are burnt for getting energy.\n','','','i) During the combustion process of wood, coal, petrol or cooking gas, carbon combines with oxygen in air and the product carbon dioxide is formed.\nii) Carbon + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide \n     2Ca + O₂ → 2CaO\niii) Combustion of fuel is a fast and irreversible chemical reaction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16487,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when?  Shahabad tile cleared with hydrochloric acid.\n','','','i) During the cleaning of Shahabad tile with hydrochloric acid, the upper layer of the tile reacts with hydrochloric acid and three products are formed as follows.\n     Calcium        +  Hydrochloric       →    calcium    +   carbon    +   water\n     Carbonate       acid                              chloride      dioxide\n\n        CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + CO₂ + H₂O\nii) Carbon dioxide bubbles are seen.\niii) Calcium chloride, which is soluble in water gets washed away with water.\niv) Carbon dioxide mixes with air.\nv) Water mixes with water.\nvi) It is a chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16488,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happen when? Washing soda is added to soften the hard water.\n','','','i) Hard water contains chloride and sulphate salt of calcium and magnesium.\nii) When washing soda is added to the hard water, a chemical reaction takes place.\niii) It forms a precipitate of insoluble carbonate salts of calcium and magnesium.\niv) As dissolved salt of calcium and magnesium go out in the form of precipitate of the carbonate salts, the water is softened.\nv) Word equation : \n     Calcium    +   Sodium       →   calcium     +   Sodium\n      Chloride      carbonate          carbonate       chloride\nvi) Chemical equation : CaCl₂ + Na₂CO₃ → CaCO₃ + 2NaCl ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16489,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain by writing a word equation : Respiration is a chemical change ','','','i) Respiration is a continuously occurring biological process.\nii) In this process we inhale the air and exhale carbon dioxide and water vapour.\niii) Glucose in the cells reacts with oxygen in the inhaled air to form carbon dioxide and water.\niv) Word equation : Glucose + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide + water\nv) Chemical equation: C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16490,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain by writing a word equation : Hard water gets softened on mixing with solution of washing soda.\n','','','i) Hard water contains the chloride and sulphate Q salts of calcium and magnesium in dissolved state.\nii) To soften hard water, a solution of washing soda is added to it.\niii) This results in a chemical reaction to form a precipitate of insoluble salts of calcium and magnesium.\niv) As the dissolved salts of calcium and magnesium go out in the form of precipitate of the carbonate salts, the water is softened.  \nv) Word equation is : \nMagnesium   + Sodium         →  Magnesium   + Sodium \n  Chloride          carbonate           carbonate         chloride\nvi) Chemical equation is :\n   MgCl₂  +  Na₂CO₃   →   MgCO₃   +   2NaCl','',0,0,0,NULL),(16491,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain by writing a word equation : Lime stone powder disappears on adding dilute hydrochloric acid.\n','','','i) Lime stone is made of calcium carbonate.\nii) Calcium carbonate vigorously reacts with hydrochloric acid to form calcium chloride, carbon dioxide and water.\niii) Word equation:\nCalcium         + Hydrochloric    →   Calcium  + carbon    +  water\nCarbonate        acid                        chloride      dioxide\niv) Chemical equation:\n      CaCO₃ + 2HCl → CaCl₂ + CO₂ + H₂O  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16492,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain by writing a word equation : Bubbles are seen on adding lemon juice to baking soda.\n','','','i) The sodium bicarbonate of the baking soda reacts with the citric acid in lemon juice to form bubbles of carbon dioxide gas.\nii) Word equation is:\n\nCitric acid  +   Sodium bicarbonate  → Carbon + water + sodium citrate  \n(lemon juice)   (Baking soda)                  dioxide','',0,0,0,NULL),(16493,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Take lemon juice in a clean glass. Take two drops of the lemon juice in a spoon and taste. Add a pinch of baking soda in the glass of lemon juice. \na) Did you notice bubbling around the particles of soda?        b) Did you hear a sound on taking your ear near the glass?         c) Now taste again it. Did it taste as sour as it was in the beginning?\n','','','a) Yes, a gas released in the process produced bubbles around the particles of baking soda.\nb) Yes, we could hear a low sound when the ear was taken close to glass. This happens because the gas released with bubbles produces sound.\nc) No, the original sour taste of lemon juice became mild.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16494,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Take some freshly prepared lime water (Solution of calcium hydroxide) in a test tube. Keep on blowing in it with a blow tube. What is seen after some time? Did the colourless lime water turn milky?\n','','','i) On blowing in the test tube, lime water turns milky after sometime.\nii) After sometime, white solid settles at the bottom of the tube.\niii) This shows that the blown gas mixed in test tube containing lime water is carbon dioxide.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16495,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain how to write a word equation for a chemical change.','','','i) When a chemical change takes place, the chemical composition of original matter changes and new substances with different chemical properties and different chemical composition are formed.\nii) If the exact chemical compositions of original substance and newly generated substances are known, a chemical equation can be written for any chemical change.\niii) While writing word equation, names of original substances and newly generated substances are used.\nEg. When baking soda is added to lemon juice, a chemical change takes place in which citric acid present in the lemon juice reacts with baking soda to form carbon dioxide gas and sodium citrate. This reaction is called neutralization reaction. The word equation is as follows: \n   Citric acid + Sodium bicarbonate → Carbon dioxide + Sodium citrate','',0,0,0,NULL),(16496,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention the steps involved in writing chemical equation.','','','i) Identify chemical formula of each of the substances involved in chemical reaction.\nii) Write formulae of original substances on the left side and formulae of newly formed substances on right side.\niii) Draw an arrow in between original substances and newly formed substances with arrow head pointing towards newly formed substances.\niv) Arrow head indicates the direction of the reaction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16497,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on a chemical change occurring during the combustion of fuels.\n','','','i) In combustion, fuels such as wood, coal, petrol, cooking gas, etc. are burnt to get energy.\nii) Carbon is the common substance that burns in all of these fuels.\niii) During combustion, carbon reacts with oxygen to form carbon dioxide gas as product.\niv) Hence, during the combustion process, a chemical change occurs.\nv) The word equation for a chemical change occurring during the combustion of fuel is, Carbon + Oxygen → Carbon dioxide\nvi) The chemical equation for a chemical change occurring during the combustion of fuel is, C + O₂ → CO₂','',0,0,0,NULL),(16498,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show diagrammatic representation of electronic configuration of the following elements : a) Sodium        	b) Oxygen           	c) Calcium \n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16500,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the given table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16501,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use the word ‘ionic’ or ‘covalent’ to complete the following table.','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16502,137,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following into physical and chemical change : Ripening of mango, melting of ice, boiling of water, dissolution of salt in water, ripening of banana, fragrance of an ripening fruit, darkening of a cut potato, bursting of an inflated balloon, sound of fire cracker, foul smell from a spoiled food.\n','','','a) The physical changes are as follows:\n1) Melting of ice           2) Boiling of water          3) Dissolution of salt in water\n4) Bursting of an inflated balloon\n\nb) The chemical changes are as follows:\n1) Ripening of mango             2) Ripening of banana       3) Fragrance on ripening fruit        4) Darkening of a cut potato        5) Sound of bursting fire cracker      6) Foul smell from a spoiled food','',0,0,0,NULL),(16504,137,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Show with the help of diagram of electronic configuration how the following compounds are found from the constituent atoms : Sodium chloride\n','','','i) The atomic number of sodium is 11 and electronic configuration is (2, 8, 1). Sodium has only 1 electron in its outermost shell. So the sodium atom donates 1 electron and forms sodium cation Na⁺.\nii) The atomic number of chlorine is 17 and electronic configuration is (2, 8, 7). Chlorine has 7 electrons in its outermost shell and needs more electron to achieve the stable inert gas configuration.\niii) So a chlorine atom takes 1 electron forms a negatively charged chloride ion Cl⁻.\niv) When sodium reacts with chlorine, it transfers its 1 outermost electron to the chlorine atom by losing 1 electron and forming sodium ion Na⁺ and by gaining 1 electron, the chlorine atom forms a chloride ion Cl⁻.\nv) Sodium ions have positive charge and chloride ions have negative charge. Due to opposite charges, sodium and chloride ions are held together by the electrostatic force of attraction to form sodium chloride-NaCl.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16505,137,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Show with the help of diagram of electronic configuration how the following compounds are found from the constituent atoms : Potassium fluoride\n','','','i) Atomic number of potassium is 19 and electronic configuration is (2, 8, 8, 1). Potassium has one electron in its outermost orbit. Potassium forms a positive charge by donating one electron.\nii) Atomic number of Fluorine is 9 and electronic configuration is (2, 7). Fluorine has 7 electrons in its outermost orbit and needs only one electron to complete its octet. So, fluorine forms a negative charge by accepting one electron.\niii) These two oppositely charged ions have strong electrostatic force of attraction between each other so they form an ionic compound KF. ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16506,137,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Show with the help of diagram of electronic configuration how the following compounds are found from the constituent atoms : Water \n','','','i) Water molecule is a covalent compound. It is formed from hydrogen and oxygen atoms.\nii) There are six electrons in the valence shell of an oxygen atom. It means that the electron octet in oxygen is short of two electrons and the valency of oxygen is 2.\niii) In the H₂O molecule, the oxygen atom completes its octet by forming two covalent bonds, one each with the two hydrogen atoms.\niv) The duplets of the two hydrogen atoms are also completed by sharing. ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16507,137,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Show with the help of diagram of electronic configuration how the following compounds are found from the constituent atoms : Hydrogen chloride \n','','','i) A hydrogen atom has 1 electron in its outermost orbit. So it can share only one electron.\nii) Chlorine atom has 7 electrons in its outermost orbit and needs to share one electron to complete its orbit.\niii) Hydrogen atom will share its one electron with chlorine to form one covalent bond and make a hydrogen chloride molecule.  ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16508,137,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Show with the help of diagram of electronic configuration how the following compounds are found from the constituent atoms : Formation of Ionic bond in CaO molecule\n','','','i) Calcium (2, 8, 8, 2) losses its two valence electrons to attain the octet state but the electron number is less by 2, so calcium gains 2 positive charges as Ca²⁺\nii) On the other hand, oxygen has 2 electrons less to the octet state. On accepting 2 electrons from calcium, octet of oxygen is completed. However, addition of two electrons results in the formation of an anion O²⁻ carrying net negative charge -2.\niii) Due to electrostatic force of attraction, Ca²⁺ ion and O²⁻ ion get attracted to each other and a chemical bond is formed. The product formed is calcium oxide.\nCalcium + Oxygen → Calcium oxide \n2Ca + O₂       → 2CaO','',0,0,1,NULL),(16509,137,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Some wells or tube have hard water? Why?','','','i) Some wells or tube wells have hard water because it contains chloride and sulphate salts of calcium and magnesium in dissolved state.\nii) This results in a chemical reaction to form a precipitate of insoluble carbonate salts of calcium and magnesium.\niii) The dissolved salts of calcium and magnesium go out as precipitate and water is softened.\niv) Word equation:\n    Calcium   + Sodium          →  calcium       +  Sodium\n     Chloride     carbonate          carbonate        chloride\nv) Chemical equation: CaCl₂ + Na₂CO₃ → CaCO₃ + 2NaCl\nvi) Magnesium      + Sodium         →  Magnesium   +  Sodium\n      chloride                carbonate        carbonate           chloride\nvii) MgCl₂ + Na₂CO₃ → MgCO₃ + 2NaCl','',0,0,0,NULL),(16512,137,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe numerical method for the formation of ionic bond is sodium chloride.\n','','','i) Sodium chloride is formed from its constituent elements, sodium ( 11 Na ) and chlorine ( 17 Cl )\nii) The electronic configuration of sodium is (2, 8, 1) and that of chlorine is (2, 8, 7).\niii) The valency of sodium is 1 as it has one electron in valence shell (M-shell). The valency of chlorine is 1 as it has 7 electrons in its valence shell (M-shell) and it just needs one electron to complete the octet.\niv) Sodium loses its valence electron from M-shell and hence, the penultimate L-shell becomes the outermost shell which has 8 electrons in it. Thus, sodium attains a complete electron octet.\nv) On loss of one electron, the number of electrons in sodium becomes 10. Therefore, the total positive charge of +11 on sodium nucleus is imbalanced. Thus, a Na⁺ cation which carries net positive charge +1 is formed.\nvi) Similarly, chlorine gains one electron to complete its octet in M-shell. However, the charge is imbalanced on chlorine nucleus. Thus, a CI⁻ anion which carries net negative charge -1 is formed.\nvii) When elements of sodium and chlorine combine, a sodium atom gives its valence electron to a chlorine atom. In the process, cation and CI⁻ anion are formed. Na⁺\nviii) These oppositely charged cations and anions are attracted by electrostatic force of attraction resulting in the formation of a chemical bond which is also called ionic bond or electrovalent bond. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16547,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','How is sound produced? ','','','Sound is produced when something vibrates. The vibrating body causes the medium (water, air, etc.) around it to vibrate.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16548,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does sound from a sound producing body travel through air to reach our ears?\n','','','Sound from a sound producing body travels through air to reach our ears by making regular compression and rarefaction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16549,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the necessary condition for the propagation of sound.','','','The propagation of sound requires a material medium.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16550,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do astronauts talk to each other on the surface of the moon?','','','Astronauts communicate with each other with the help of radio waves (The waves used in cell phone technology) on the moon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16551,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On what factors is the frequency of a tuning fork decided?','','','The frequency of a tuning fork is decided by the dimensions of the prongs (length, thickness) and the material used for making the fork.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16552,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Ni, Sa, Dha, Ya.','','','Ya. Since \'Ya\' is is not musical note.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16553,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out :  Sitar, Violin, Flute, Guitar ','','','Flute. Because it is the only wind/pipe instrument.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16554,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out :  Vacuum, Liquid, Gas, Solid','','','Vacuum. Since sound can travel through liquid, gas and solid but it cannot travel through vacuum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16555,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out :  Tuning fork, Torch, Loudspeaker, Alarm','','','Torch. Others produce sound but Torch produces light not sound. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16556,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out :  Ear drum, Vocal cord, Larynx, Epiglottis.','','','Ear drum. It is associated with hearing of the sound, rest all are required to produce sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16558,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Vocal cord in man : 20 cm : : Vocal cord in women : ---','','','15 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(16559,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Bulb : Light energy : : Loudspeaker : ------','','','Sound energy','',0,0,0,NULL),(16560,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Compression : High density : : ----- : Low density','','','Rarefaction','',0,0,0,NULL),(16561,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Heart the sound : Ear drum : : Produce the sound : ----\n','','','Larynx','',0,0,0,NULL),(16562,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : 312 Hz : Ga : : 280 Hz : -------','','','Re','',0,0,0,NULL),(16565,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : In humans, sound is produced in the lungs.\n','','','False. In humans, sound is produced in a larynx.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16566,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Frequency is the number of vibration of sound per second.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16567,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Rarefaction is the region with high density and low pressure\n','','','False. Rarefaction is the region with low density and low pressure.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16568,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Loudspeaker converts sound energy into electrical energy.\n','','','False. Loudspeaker converts electrical energy into sound energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16569,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : In flute, different frequency can be generated by opening and closing of holes.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16570,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Sound can travel through vacuum. \n','','','False. Sound cannot travel through vacuum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16571,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : A tuning fork is made of two prongs and a stem.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16572,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The frequency is measured in decibel.\n','','','False. The frequency is measured as Hertz.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16573,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Vibration         ii) Compression ','','','i) The rapid rhythmic back and forth motion of an object is called vibration.\nii) High density and pressure regions where particles of a medium are created together is called as compression.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16575,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Rarefaction          ii) Frequency','','','i) Low density and pressure regions where particles of a medium are spread apart is called as rarefaction.\nii) Number of cycles of one compression and one rarefaction that are produced in the air (or other medium) per second is called as the frequency of the sound wave.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16577,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Vacuum           ii) Larynx ','','','i) Vacuum can be defined as a free space where no molecules are present. It is a place with zero density and zero air pressure.\nii) It is the hollow muscular organ that forms an air passage to the lungs and holds cords in human beings (and other mammal).\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16579,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Loudspeaker','','','Loudspeaker is an instrument that converts electrical energy into sound energy to be heard at long distance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16582,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Compression and Rarefaction ','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16583,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : String Instrument and Pipe Instrument','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16584,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : It is essential to change the tension on the vocal cords, as we produce different sound notes from our larynx.\n','','','i) In humans, sound is produced in the larynx.\nii) When the lungs force air through the slit, the vocal chords in larynx vibrate, producing sound.\niii) Muscles attached to the vocal chords can make the chords tight or loose.\niv) Hence, it is essential to change the tension on the vocal chords, as we produce different sound notes from our larynx.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16585,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Astronauts on the moon cannot hear each other directly. \n','','','i) The moon does not have atmosphere.\nii) Since there is no medium which is necessary for (A) generation and propagation of sound between the astronauts, direct sound propagation between them is not possible.\niii) Thus, two astronauts on the moon will be unable to listen to each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16586,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : As the sound wave propagates from one place to the other in air, the air itself is not required to move from one place to other place.\n','','','i) The air molecules in the region, which were in compressed state earlier transfer their energy to the air molecules in the next region.\nii) Such a periodic motion creates compression and rarefaction in the air and forms sound waves.\niii) Hence, as the sound wave propagates from one place to the other in the air, the air itself is not required to move from one place to the other.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16587,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : The voice of male, female and children are different.\n','','','i) Vocal cords are 20 cm in length in males, about 15 cm in females and even smaller in children.\nii) Therefore, the voice of male, female and children are different.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16588,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : There must be limit on the loudness of loudspeaker','','','i) Very loud sound can be produced by using a loudspeaker.\nii) If sound level is around 100 decibels, the sound can be harmful for us.\niii) Therefore, although the loudspeaker can generate very loud sound, there must be a limit on its loudness. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16589,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find ways to avoid sound pollution.','','','i) Sound pollution is a major cause affecting the environment and social health.\nii) Hence, we should find ways to avoid sound pollution to protect the environment and social health.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16591,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain, how sound of different frequencies can be produced using a flute.','','','i) When air is blown through the mouthpiece of a flute, the air column inside start vibrating and sound is produced.\nii) By opening and closing the holes of the flute, the length of vibrating air column in the flute is changed.\niii) The change in length of vibrating air column produces waves of different frequencies.\niv) Sound of different frequencies can also be produced by changing the way air is blown in flute.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16592,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is sound produced by humans?','','','i) In humans, sound is produced in the larynx (voice box) situated at the upper end of the windpipe.\nii) Two vocal cords are stretched across the larynx leaving a narrow slit between them for the passage of air.\niii) When the lungs force air through the slit, the vocal cords vibrate, producing sound.\niv) Muscles attached to the vocal cords can make the cords tight or loose. When the vocal cords are tight and thin, the type or quality of sound produced is different than the sound produced when vocal cords are loose and thick.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16593,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following chart :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16594,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the given diagram : The diagram below represents a tuning fork','','','A – Prong, B – Stem','',1,0,0,NULL),(16595,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the diagram : The pressure variations during the propagation of sound in air in the form of waves.\n','','','A – Increased pressure (compression), B – Reduced pressure (rarefaction), C – Atmospheric pressure','',1,0,0,NULL),(16596,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can we hear the sound?','','','i) Mediums are necessary for the propagation of sound.\nii) The sound waves produced with the compression and rarefaction in air reach our ear.\niii)  Ear-Drums are present in our ear.\niv) When the sound waves reaches to the ear- drums, they get vibrate.\nv) They gives signal to the brain and as a result we get a sense of hearing a sound.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16598,139,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the diagram :','','','a) Epiglottis                b) Vocal cords            c) Trachea          d) Esophagus','',1,0,0,NULL),(16599,139,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the diagram :','','','a) Permanent magnet             b) coil                   c) Screen            d) Sound waves            e) Electrical signal from microphone','',1,0,0,NULL),(16600,139,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Identify the instrument diagrams and discuss sound production in them.','','','i) Sitar           ii) Flute             iii) Santoor              iv) Bugle               v) Clarinet         vi) Mandolin                 vii) Saxophone                viii) Tabla\ni) a) Sitar, Santoor and Mandolin are string based instruments.\n    b) The vibrations in the metal wires produce sound.\n    c) The frequency of vibration of the string is changed by changing the tension on the string and or by changing the vibrating length of the string using fingers.\n    d) This results in generation of different notes.\nii) a) In Tabla, sound is produced by the vibration of the membrane caused by a variety of different finger and hand strokes.\n    b) In Clarinet, Bugle, Flute and Saxophone, sound is produced by blowing air into these instruments. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(16607,139,'descriptive','3',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is sound produced in a loudspeaker? Why should loudness of loudspeaker be controlled?\n','','','i) Generation of sound in loudspeaker : \na) Loudspeaker consists of a permanent magnet with a coil wound around it. \nb) When current flows through the coil, magnetic field is generated around the coil. \nc) Due to this magnetic field, the coil moves back and forth. \nd) The frequency and amplitude of the movement of the coil depends on the variation in the current flowing through the coil. \ne) Due to the motion of the coil, the conical screen attached to the coil also starts moving back and forth. \nf) This back and forth movement of loudspeaker screen gives rise to sound waves in air. \nReason to enforce limit on loudness of loudspeaker : \na) When sound level is around 100 decibels or more, it is harmful to humans. \nb) As the loudspeaker can generate very loud sound, it may harm us. Hence, there must be limit on loudness of loudspeaker. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16608,139,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is frequency of a tuning fork? How can decided?','','','i) The number of the cycles of compression and rarefactions that are produced in the air (or other medium) per second is called as the frequency of the second waves.\nii) If one cycle is completed in one second, the frequency is said to be 1 Hz. \niii) A tuning fork vibrates 512 times per second. These vibrations will set 512\ncycles of compression and rarefaction in the air, per second. \niv) Thus, the sound generated by the fork will have a frequency of 512 Hz.\nv) The frequency of a tuning fork is decided by the dimensions of the prongs (length, thickness) and the material used for making the fork. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16609,139,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How are different sound notes generated in musical instruments like guitar, which uses strings for sound generation and flute which uses blown air for sound generation.\n','','','a) String based instruments:\ni) The frequency of vibration of the string is changed (A) by changing the tension on the string or (B) by changing the vibrating length of the string using fingers.\nii) This results in generation of different notes.\nb) Musical instruments like flute:\ni) The holes on the flute are opened or closed to change the length of vibrating air column in the flute.\nii) The frequency of waves, therefore changes and it results in the production of different notes.\niii) In flute, different notes can be generated by changing the way of air blowing also.     ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16610,139,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the cycles of compression and rarefaction in a sound wave and change in air pressure with the help of diagram. \n','','','i) If any object vibrates in the air, sound waves are produced in the air.\nii) The vibrations result in generating compressions and rarefactions in air. This figure shows the actual variation in the air density and pressure.\niii) One compression and one rarefaction together form one cycle of the wave.\niv) The number of cycles formed in the air in one second will be decided by the number of times the vibrating object vibrates back and forth i.e, the number of vibration in one second.\nv) Number of such cycles that are produced in the air (or other medium) per second is called as the frequency of the sound wave. The frequency is measured in Hertz.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16611,139,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How is sound produced in a human larynx and a loudspeaker?','','','Human Larynx :\ni) In the humans, sound is produced in the larynx. It is at the upper end of the wind pipe.\nii) Two vocal cords are stretched across the voice box or larynx in such a way that it leaves a narrow slit between them for the passage of air.\niii) When the lungs force air through the slit, the vocal cords vibrate, producing sound.\niv) Muscles attached to the vocal cords can make the cords tight or loose.\nv) When the vocal cords are tight and thin, the type or quality of voice is different.\nvi) Vocal cords are 20 cm in length in males, about 15 cm in females and even smaller in children. \nvii) Therefore, the voices of men, women and children are different.','Loudspeaker :\ni) The internal arrangement of a loudspeaker consists of a magnet. A coil is wound around it and if a current flows through thin coil, it generates magnetic field.\nii) Depending on the magnetic field created due to the coil, the coil moves back and forth.\niii) As the coil moves, the conical screen of the loudspeaker which is attached to the coil, also moves back and forth.\niv) Due to the back and forth motion of the loudspeaker screen, sound waves are produced in air, just like the back and forth motion of the prongs of a tuning fork produce sound waves.   ',0,0,1,NULL),(16612,139,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the experiment with neat diagram, to prove the following : ‘Sound needs material medium for propagation.’\n','','','i) The experimental arrangement is as shown in the above figure.\nii) In the experiment, a vacuum tight bell jar is placed on a smooth horizontal surface.\niii) The bell jar is connected to a vacuum pump via a tube.\niv) We can remove air from inside the bell jar using the vacuum pump.\nv) The bell jar contains an electric bell, which is connected to the power supply through the lid of the bell jar.\nvi) At the start of experiment, the vacuum pump is off and the bell jar contains air.\nvii) If the key to electric bell is now switched on, the bell starts ringing and the ringing can be heard from outside the bell jar.\nviii) Now if the vacuum pump is switched on, it starts removing the air from the bell jar.\nix) As the quantity of air inside the bell jar decreases, the level of ringing sound heard outside also decreases.\nx) If the pump is operated for sufficiently long period, the quantity of air inside the bell jar will be low.\nxi) At this point the level of ringing sound will also be very low.\nxii) This experiment demonstrates that sound generation and propagation needs a medium like air.\nxiii) If the air inside the bell jar is totally removed we will not be able to hear the sound of ringing bell at all.  ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16613,139,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain how sound is generated from a vibrating object like a tuning fork.','','','i) A tuning fork is made of two prongs and a stem. Keeping the stem steady, if the prongs are stuck, they start vibrating.\nii) It means that they are set into periodic motion in forward and backward direction.\niii) If during the vibration, the prongs of the tuning fork go away from each other, the air in the prongs is compressed and the pressure there increases. This region A in which air is at high pressure and high density is called compression. \niv) In the next step of vibration, the prongs of the fork come close to each other. The air molecule near the prongs get more space to move away from each other. Such region \'A\' in which air is at low pressure and low density is called rarefaction.\nv) The air molecules which were in compressed state earlier, transfer their energy to the air molecules in the next region \'B\'.\nvi) Such a periodic motion of the prongs create compression and rarefaction in the air, they are sound waves.\nvii) When these waves reach our ear, the ear drum vibrates, specific signals reach the brain and we get a sense of hearing a sound.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16615,139,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe in detail about Jaltarang.','','','i) Jaltarang is an Indian melodious percussion as well as non percussion instrument. It consists of china bowls filled with water and struck by two cane sticks.\nii) The instrument includes a series of bowls made of china clay of descending sizes arranged in a semicircular manner. The player sits in the middle of the circle softly striking the cups on the edge to create a sound.\niii) The cups producing the notes of the \'Mandra Saptak\' are large in size, the ones used for \'Madhya Saptak\' are medium in size and the ones used to produce \'Taar Saptak\' are small sized cups made of porcelain.\niv) Each bowl can be tuned to the desired frequency by varying the quantity of water in it.\nv) When a cup is struck, waves are set up in the air column above the water level in the cup. The frequency of the generated waves depends on the height of the air column inside the cup.\nvi) The water level in each cup is different, hence the height of air columns are also different.\nvii) Therefore, the frequency of sound generated by each cup will also be different. So the sound generated is different.\nviii) The cups made of steel, glass, ceramic, bronze of different sizes could also be used.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16616,139,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the generation of sound in a loudspeaker with the help of neat diagram. Why should there be limit on the loudness of loudspeaker?  \n','','','Generation of sound in loudspeaker :\ni) Loudspeaker consists of a permanent magnet with a coil wound around it as shown in figure. \nii) When current flows through the coil, magnetic field is generated around the coil.\niii) Due to this magnetic field, the coil moves back and forth.\niv) The frequency and amplitude of the movement of the coil depends on the variation in the current flowing through the coil.\nv) Due to the motion of the coil, the conical screen attached to the coil also starts moving back and forth.\nvi) This back and forth movement of loudspeaker screen gives rise to sound waves in air.\n\nReason to enforce limit on loudness of loudspeaker :\ni) When sound level is around 100 decibels or more, it is harmful to humans.\nii) As the loudspeaker can generate very loud sound, it may harm us.\nHence, there must be limit on loudness of loudspeaker.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16647,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Mirror in salon, Image of moon in water, Periscope, Transparent window. \n','','','Transparent window. Reflection of light does not occur in transparent window, whereas remaining works on the phenomenon of reflection of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16648,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Periscope, Lens, Mirror, Kaleidoscope.','','','Lens : Lens does not work on principal of reflection while others can work on principle of reflection.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16649,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Incident ray, Normal, reflecting surface, reflected ray.','','','Reflecting surface. It is not necessarily required to be in the same plane, but the rest are required to be in the same plane.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16650,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Sun, Stars, Fireflies, Moon.','','','Moon. The moon is non-luminous body while rest are all luminous bodies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16651,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy: Angle of incidence : 60⁰ : : Angle of reflection : ----','','','60⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(16652,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy :  Smooth surface : regular reflection of light : : Rough surface : ------  \n','','','Irregular reflection of light','',0,0,0,NULL),(16653,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Submarines : Periscope : : Designing of cloth : ----','','','Kaleidoscope ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16654,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Lens : Refraction : : Mirror : ----- ','','','Reflection','',0,0,0,NULL),(16655,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Sun : Self Luminous : : Moon : -----','','','Non-self luminous','',0,0,0,NULL),(16659,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The rays going away from the surface after reflection are called incident rays.\n','','','False. The rays going away from the surface after reflection are called reflected rays.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16660,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The angle of reflection is equal to the angle of incidence.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16661,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Only smooth polished surfaces reflect the light.\n','','','False. Smooth as well as rough surface reflect light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16662,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Light can be reflected several times to the angle of reflection\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16663,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Angle of incidence is sometimes equal to the angle of reflection.\n','','','False. Angle of incidence is always equal to the angle of reflection.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16664,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Laws of reflection are not followed in both regular and irregular reflection.\n','','','False. Laws of reflection are followed in both regular and irregular reflection.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16665,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : In irregular reflection the angle of incidence at different points are different.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16666,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Kaleidoscope is used to making wallpapers.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16667,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Submarine uses periscope to observe what is in front of top window. \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16668,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : In Kaleidoscope, every time we get the same design.\n','','','False. In Kaleidoscope, every time we get different designs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16669,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do we see objects?','','','We can see object due to reflection of light from the surface of the objects.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16670,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which apparatus do the submarines use?','','','Submarines use periscope for the out of sight visions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16671,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Is moon self-luminous? Why can we see it?','','','Moon is non self-Luminous. We can see the moon since light from the sun is reflected from its surface.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16672,140,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is Double (Multiple) reflection? Where do we use it? ','','','When reflected light is again reflected from two (or more) surfaces, we call it as double (multiple) reflection. We use it in saloon to observe the backside hair.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16673,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many images obtained in two parallel mirrors?','','','When mirrors are parallel multiple reflections take place and we can see infinite number of images.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16674,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Are the laws of reflection followed when irregular reflection takes place? ','','','Yes, they are followed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16675,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the surface of glass made smooth and shiny while making of mirror.\n','','','All incident light is reflected from a shiny, smooth surface and we get a clear image.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16676,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the function of Periscope in a submarine?','','','Periscope is used in submarine to see the objects above the surface of water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16677,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the uses of Kaleidoscope?','','','Kaleidoscopes are used for making wall papers designs and cloth designs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16678,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Reflection          ii) Incident ray','','','i) When light rays fall on an object their direction changes and they turn back. This is called reflection of light.\nii) A ray of light falling on any surface is called incident ray.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16680,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Reflected ray           ii) Normal ','','','i) The ray of light that turns back after being incident on a surface is called reflected ray.\nii) The perpendicular line at the point of incidence of light ray is called the normal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16682,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Law of reflection          ii) Angle of incidence ','','','i) The direction of the reflected rays is decided by some rules which are called laws of reflection.\nii) The angle formed by the incident ray with the normal is called angle of incidence.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16684,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Angle of reflection        ii) Regular reflection ','','','i) The angle formed by the reflected ray with the normal is called angle of reflection.\nii) Reflection of light from a plane and smooth surface is called regular reflection.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16686,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Irregular reflection','','','Reflection of light from a rough surface is called irregular reflection.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16687,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : A mirror reflects sunlight to give a bright patch of light on the wall, while the lid of a tin gives a less bright patch.\n','','','A mirror reflects sunlight to give bright patch of light on the wall because mirror is a polished surface and gives regular reflection while the lid of a tin being uneven and rough gives a less bright patch due to irregular reflection.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16688,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Numerous images are seen in the palace of mirrors.','','','i) In the palace of mirrors\', the mirrors are placed parallel or the mirrors are placed at an angle.\nii) Due to multiple reflections taking place infinite number of images are formed.\niii) Therefore, numerous images are seen in the \'Palace of mirrors\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16689,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : The image formed in still water is of the same size as the object.\n','','','i) Still water behaves like a plane mirror.\nii) Hence, when objects are placed in front of water, the nature of the images formed is similar to the image formed in a plane mirror.\niii) Hence, the image formed in still water is of the same size as the object.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16690,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : We do not see an image in transparent glass.','','','i) An image is seen by us only when the reflected rays reach our eyes.\nii) When light rays fall on a transparent glass, they pass through the glass instead of getting reflected.\niii) Hence, no reflected rays reach our eyes.\niv) Hence, we do not see an image in transparent glass.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16691,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : We cannot see the image of the moon clearly in running water.\n','','','i) Running water behaves like a rough surface.\nii) Hence, light rays falling on it undergo irregular reflection, and are scattered.\niii) Hence, all the reflected rays do not reach our eyes.\niv) Therefore, we cannot see the image of the moon clearly in running water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16692,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How will you explain the statement “we cannot see the objects in a dark room?”\n','','','i) We can see any object, when the light reflected by that object reaches our eyes.\nii) But in the dark form, no light is reflected by the object.\niii) Hence, we are unable to see the objects in dark room.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16693,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are two mirrors in a Periscope placed parallel to each other?','','','i) The object which is above the sea forms an image in the upper mirror of the periscope.\nii) The reflected rays from this upper mirror then strike the lower mirror and another image of the object is formed, and then we are able to see the object.\niii) Multiple reflections take place only if the mirrors are parallel to each other.\niv) Hence, the two mirrors are placed parallel to each other in a periscope.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16694,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do rays in a parallel beam of incident light not remain parallel to each other after reflection from a rough surface?\n','','','i) Since the surface is rough the normal drawn at every point of incidence do not remain parallel to each other.\nii) Due to the angle of incidences formed by all incident rays which are parallel to each other are different.\niii) Hence, after reflection the angle of reflection for each reflected ray will also be different.\niv) Therefore, the rays get scattered and not parallel to each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16695,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between : Regular reflection and Irregular reflection.','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16696,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart :','','','Answer :','',1,0,1,NULL),(16698,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State some uses of Kaleidoscope.','','','i) Kaleidoscope is used to see different designs formed due to multiple reflections.\nii) Wallpapers which are used to decorate walls are designed using Kaleidoscope.\niii) Kaleidoscope is also used by cloth designers for making new designs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16699,140,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the angle of incidence is 40⁰ then what must be the angle of reflection?','','','Given : Angle of incidence (∠i) = 40⁰\nTo find : Angle of reflection (∠r)\nFormula : ∠i = ∠r\n            ∴ Angle of reflection = ∠r = 40⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(16700,140,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the reflected ray makes an angle of 60⁰ with the normal, what angle must the incident ray make with normal? \n','','','Given : Angle of incidence (∠i) = 60⁰\nTo find : Angle of reflection (∠r)\nFormula : ∠i = ∠r\n                 ∴ Angle of reflection = ∠r = 60⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(16701,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the angle between the reflected ray and the incident ray is 100⁰, then what will be the value of angle of reflection?\n','','','Given : Angle between the incident ray and reflected ray is 100⁰\n                     ⟹   ∠i + ∠r = 100⁰\nTo find : Angle of reflection (∠r)\nFormula : ∠i = ∠r\n                  Given that, ∠i + ∠r = 100⁰\n          ∴  ∠r + ∠r = 100⁰       ⟹  2 ∠r = 100⁰\n           ∴ ∠r  = 100/2 = 50⁰\n           The angle of reflection will be 50⁰.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16702,140,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the angle between the incident ray and the reflected ray is 90⁰, what are the values of the angle of incidence and angle of reflection?\n','','','Given : Angle between the incident ray and reflected ray is 100⁰\n                     ⟹   ∠i + ∠r = 90⁰\nTo find : Angle of reflection (∠r) and angle of incidence (∠i)\nFormula : ∠i = ∠r\n                  Given that, ∠i + ∠r = 90⁰\n          ∴  ∠i + ∠i = 90⁰       ⟹  2 ∠i = 90⁰\n           ∴ ∠i  = 90/2 = 45⁰\n           The angle of incidence is 45⁰ and angle of reflection is 45⁰.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16703,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the angle between plane mirror and the incident ray is 40⁰. What are the angles of incidence and reflection.\n','','','Given : ∠AOP = 40⁰ ---------- (i)\nFrom figure, ∠AOP + ∠PON = 90⁰\n∠PON = 90⁰ - ∠AOP = 90 – 40 = 50⁰\n∴ ∠PON = ∠i = 50⁰\nAccording to law of reflection, ∠i = ∠r\n∴ ∠r = 50⁰\nThe angle of incidence is 50⁰ and angle of reflection is 50⁰','',0,0,1,NULL),(16704,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the angle between the mirror and reflected ray is 23⁰. What is the angle of incidence of the incident ray.\n','','','Given : ∠BOR = 23⁰ ---------- (i)\nFrom figure, ∠BOR + ∠RON = 90⁰\n∠RON = 90⁰ - ∠BOR = 90 – 23 = 67⁰\n∴ ∠PON = ∠i = 67⁰\nAccording to law of reflection, ∠i = ∠r\n∴ ∠i = 67⁰\nThe angle of incidence is 67⁰','',0,0,1,NULL),(16705,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the reflected ray makes an angle of 75⁰ with the normal, what angle will the incident ray make with the normal?\n','','','Given : Angle of reflection (∠r) = 75⁰\nTo find : Angle of incidence (∠i)\nFormula : ∠i = ∠r\n                 ∴ Angle of incidence = ∠i = 75⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(16706,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw a figure describing the following. The reflecting surface of two mirrors make an angle of 90⁰ with each other. If a ray incident of one mirror has angle of incidence of 30⁰, draw the ray reflected from the second mirror. What will be its angle of reflection.\n','','','From the figure, ∠MSN = 30⁰ \n∠TSN = ∠MSN = 30⁰ (∠i = ∠r)\nSince LT is normal to mirror B.\n∠QTL = 90⁰\nSince SN ∥ QR, ∠QTS = ∠TSN = 30⁰ (Alternate angles)\nAlso, ∠QTL = ∠QTS + ∠STL \n90⁰ = 30⁰ + ∠STL\n∴ ∠STL = 90 – 30 = 60⁰\n∠STL = ∠LTK \n∴ ∠LTK = 60⁰','',0,0,1,NULL),(16707,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the angle of incidence of ray if the reflected ray is at angle of 90⁰ to the incident ray?\n','','','According to laws of reflection, angle of incidence = angle of reflection\nAs given, ∠i = ∠r = 90⁰\n∴ ∠i = ∠r = 90/2 = 45⁰\n∴ Angle of incidence = 45⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(16708,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do we see the image of the moon in water?','','','i) As moon is not self luminous, the light coming from sun falls on the surface of the moon and gets reflected.\nii) This reflected light coming from moon is again reflected by water to give the image of the moon.\niii) In this way, we can see the image of the moon in water due to multiple reflections.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16709,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How do you see if the barber in a salon has cut the hair on your neck properly or not? \n','','','i) In saloon, there are mirrors kept at our front and at our back.\nii) The image of the back of our neck is formed in the mirror which is placed at our back.\niii) The image of this image is formed in the mirror which is kept in front of us.\niv) In this way, we can see if the barber in a saloon has cut the hair on our neck properly or not. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16710,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What type of image do we see in mirror?','','','i) We see virtual, upright image of the some shape and size as the object in front of the mirror.\nii) Left and right sides are interchange in mirror.   \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16711,140,'descriptive','3',1,2,'Text Book','','Draw a figure showing a following : a) incident ray 	b) Normal           c) Angle of incident 		d) Angle of reflection 	e) Point of incidence         f) Reflected ray\n','','','Image ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16712,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw net and labelled diagrams for the following : a) Regular reflection 	b) Irregular reflection\n','','','Image ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16713,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the laws of reflection with the help of a diagram.','','','The laws of reflection are as under:\ni) The incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal lie in the same plane.\nii) The incident ray and the reflected ray lie on the opposite side of the normal.\niii) The angle of incidence is equal in measure to the angle of reflection, i.e. ∠i = ∠r','',0,0,1,NULL),(16714,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify ‘Y’ and state its significance.','','','i) Y - Periscope on a submarine.\nii) It is used to view objects above the surface of sea water.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(16715,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In figure show three parallel rays, incident on smooth and rough surfaces. The reflected rays are drawn using laws of reflection. : a) Rays reflected from which surface are parallel to one another?              b) What conclusion can you draw from the figure?\n','','','a) Rays reflected from smooth surface are parallel to one another.\nb) For smooth surface, the reflected rays are parallel to one another, whereas for rough surface, the reflected rays are not parallel to one another.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(16716,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Switch off the light in your room at night for sometime and then turn it on again. Could you see objects in the room clearly when the light was switched off? What did you feel when it was turned on again?\n','','','i) No, we could not see the objects in the room clearly when the light was switched off.\nii) Again when it is turned on we could not see immediately any object, it takes fraction of second for the clear vision.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16717,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What will happen when a light ray is indent perpendicular to the mirror?','','','When a light ray falls perpendicular to the surface of mirror, the angle of incidence is zero. As the angle of reflection is also zero, there will be no reflection.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16719,140,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Study the following incident : a) Is there a relation between the reflection of light and the blurring of the images?         b) Which types of reflection of light can you notice from this?         c) Are laws of reflection followed in these types of reflection?','','','a) i) Yes, there is a relation between the reflection of light and the blurring of the images.\nii) When reflection occurs through smooth and shining surface we get clear images.\niii) When Yash threw a stone in the water surface of water become rough due to ripples formed by stone, and so the images become blur.\nb) i) We can see two types of reflection from this incident.\nii) In still water reflection is regular and after throwing stone reflection is irregular.\nc) Yes, laws of reflection are followed in these types of reflection.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16721,140,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the construction, working and use of a periscope.','','','a) Construction:\ni) Take a cardboard box. Make slits in the top and bottom sides of the box and place the two mirrors so that they make an angle of 45 with the sides of the box and are parallel to each other. Fix them with sticking tape.\nii) Make two windows of 1 inch each near the two mirrors.\n\nb) Working:\ni) Now look through the lower window. You can see the image formed in the upper mirror.\nii) It gets reflected into the lower mirror which can be seen through the window at the bottom.    \nThis is a Periscope. It uses the properties of reflection of light.\n\nc) Use:\ni) A Periscope is useful on a submarine to see objects above the surface of water.\nii) It is also used to observe and keep a watch on the objects or persons on the ground from an underground bunker.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16722,140,'descriptive','4',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the construction, working and uses of Kaleidoscope.','','','Construction of kaleidoscope:\ni) Three rectangular mirrors of equal size are stuck with tape to gather in such a way that they form a triangle.\nii) The reflecting surfaces are kept facing each other inwards.\niii) After closing one end with triangular shaped white paper, few coloured glass pieces are put inside.\niv) The other end is also closed with a paper and a hole is made in it.\n\nWorking:\ni) When we look through the hole, we will see innumerable images of the glass pieces.\nii) These images are formed due to multiple reflection by the three mirrors.\niii) When we rotate the kaleidoscope, we see different designs every time.\niv) These designs do not easily repeat themselves.\n\nUse:\ni) Kaleidoscope is used to design wall papers which decorate the walls.\nii) Cloth designers use kaleidoscope for making new designs.','',0,0,1,NULL),(16723,140,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe an experiment to verify the ‘Laws of Reflection.’','','','i) Fix a white sheet on a drawing board tightly.\nii) Draw a line AB to indicate the position by the mirror.\niii) Draw a perpendicular OM to AB at O.\niv) Draw a ray PO making an angle of 30° with OM.\nv) Fix two pins P, Q along PO.\nvi) Place the mirror with support along AB, and perpendicular to the drawing board.\nvii) Fix two pins R and S along the line joining the bottom of the reflected images of the pins at P and Q.\nviii) Remove the mirror and join the points R and S and extends it up to O.\nix) Measure ∠SOM = ---------\n x) Repeat the same steps for angle of incidence 45° and 50°.\nObservation : \nAngle of incidence = Angle of reflection\n\nConclusion: It is verified that,\ni) The angle of incidence = angle of reflection\nii) The normal incident ray and the reflected ray are in the same plane.\niii) The normal incident ray and the reflected ray are the opposite side of the normal. ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16724,140,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain regular and irregular reflection of light with the help of neat diagrams.\n','','','a) Regular reflection of light: \ni) The reflection of light from a plane and smooth surface is called regular reflection of light. \nii) The angles of incidence and the angles of reflection are same for all parallel rays falling on the surface. \niii) Hence, the reflected rays are also parallel to incident rays. \niv) If i₁, i₂, i₃ … in represent the angles of incidence and r₁, r₂, r₃ ……. rₙ represent the angles of reflection, then i₁ = r₁, i₂ = r₂, …… iₙ = rₙ and i₁ = i₂ = i₃ …… = iₙ = r₁ = r₂ = r₃ ……. rₙ\nb) Irregular reflection of light: \ni) Reflection of light from a rough surface is called irregular reflection of light. \nii) The angles of incidence for all parallel incident rays are not equal in irregular reflection. \niii) Hence, the angles of reflection are also not equal. Thus, the reflected rays do not travel parallel to one another but they spread over a larger area. \niv) If i₁, i₂, i₃ …… iₙ represent the angles of incidence and r₁, r₂, r₃…… rₙ represent the angles of reflection, then iᵢ = rᵢ, i₂ = r₂ … iₙ = rₙ but iᵢ  i₂  i₃… iₙ and r₁  r₂  r₃… rₙ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16725,140,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Answer the following question based on the given diagram : a) Which rays do AO and OB denote?            b) What are angles ∠AON and ∠BON called?           c) What do point ‘O’ and line ‘ON’ denote?          d) What will be the values of ∠PON and ∠QON?          e) What is the relation between ∠AON and ∠BON?\n','','','a) Ray AO denotes incident ray and ray OB denotes reflected ray. \nb) AON is called angle of incidence (i) and BON is called angle of reflection (r). \nc) Point ‘O’ denotes the point of incidence and line ON denotes normal. \nd) Since ON is perpendicular to PQ, therefore PON = QON = 90. e. According to laws of reflection, the angle of incidence (i) is equal to angle of reflection (r), \n AON = BON','',1,0,0,NULL),(16726,140,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the following incident : Swara and Yash were looking in a water filled vessel. They could see their images clearly in the still water. At that instant, Yash threw a stone in the  water. Now their images were blurred. Swara could not understand the reason for the blurring of the images. \nExplain the reason for blurring of the images to Swara by answering the following question : a) Is there a relation between the reflection of light and the blurring of the images?           b) Which types of reflection of light can you notice from this?          c) Are laws of reflection followed in these types of reflection?\n','','','i) Yes, there is a relation between the reflection of light and blurring of the images.\nii) When water was still, regular reflection of light was noticed as the surface of water was smooth. As soon as Yash threw a stone in the water, the surface of the water became irregular. Hence, irregular reflection of light was noticed.\niii) Yes, laws of reflection are followed by both; regular and irregular types of reflection.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16727,140,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rajive heard about mirror maze in an amusement park and visited the park. Initially it was dark inside. He didn’t observe any image. But when he switched on the light, he saw many images of him selves in the mirror. Rajiv got confused and many questions were raised in his mind. \nHelp him to solve these questions. : a) Why initially no image was formed?          b) Why Rajiv saw so many images once switch was turned on?          c) Is it possible for a single mirror to show multiple images of Rajiv?         d) What would be the nature of multiple images formed in a single mirror?\n \n','','','a) Since the room was dark and no light was in that room, no light got reflected from any mirror. Hence, no image was formed.\nb) When switch was turned on, light started reflecting from every mirror. Every time light fell on a mirror, an image was formed. Hence, he saw many images of himself after turning on the switch.  \nc) As all the mirrors are facing one another, every mirror\'s image will get reflected in the mirror in front of it. And hence, multiple images of Rajiv in a single mirror are possible.\nd) Images formed in a single mirror would give reflection from front as well as back i.e. Rajiv can see his face and back in alternate images formed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16766,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Wood, rock, clay, plastic','','','Plastic: Plastic is a manmade material while others are natural materials.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16767,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Glass, plastic, thermocol, water','','','Water : Water is a natural material while others are manmade materials.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16768,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Toys, combs, plates, electric switches.','','','Combs : Comb is a thermoplastic while others are thermosetting plastics.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16769,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Polyvinyl chloride, polystyrene, polyester, polypropylene.\n','','','Polyester : Polyester is thermosetting plastic while others are thermoplastics.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16770,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Bakelite, polystyrene, polyurethane, polyester','','','Polystyrene : Polystyrene is thermoplastic while others are thermosetting plastics.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16771,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Vegetable, cotton cloth, plastic, wood','','','Plastic : Plastic is a manmade material while others are natural materials.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16772,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Reduce, reproduce, reuse, recover','','','Reproduce : It is not in 4R principle while others are points of 4R principle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16773,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Silica glass, borosilicate glass, lens glass, alkali silicate glass  \n','','','Lens glass : It is not a type of glass while others are type of glass.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16774,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Optical glass, fibre glass, plain glass, translucent glass','','','Optical glass : It is a type of glass while others are not.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16775,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Cotton : degradable : : Plastic bag : ------','','','Non-degradable','',0,0,0,NULL),(16776,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Cars : ----- : : Artificial teeth : Polyacrylic.','','','Teflon','',0,0,0,NULL),(16777,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : PVC : Thermoplastic : : Bakelite : ------','','','Thermosetting plastic','',0,0,0,NULL),(16778,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Mineral : natural material : : Thermocol : ------','','','Manmade material','',0,0,0,NULL),(16779,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Light bulbs : Lead glass : : Microscopic lenses : -----','','','Optical glass','',0,0,0,NULL),(16780,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Barium oxide : Optical glass : : Boric acid : ------','','','Borosilicate glass','',0,0,0,NULL),(16784,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The plastic that can be molded as per our wish is called thermoplastic. \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16785,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Plastic is not used in health care sectors.\n','','','False. Plastic is used in healthcare sectors.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16786,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Polyethylene is a type of plastic used for manufacturing lenses and artificial teeth.\n','','','False. Polyacrylic is used for manufacturing lenses and artificial teeth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16787,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Plastic is non-degradable and so an environment pollutant.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16788,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Plates used in mass production are made of glass.\n','','','False. Plates used in mass production are made of Thermocol.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16789,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Styrene contains carcinogenic ingredients.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16790,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Glass is non-crystalline, hard but a brittle solid.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16791,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Sand melts at 1700⁰C, but to melt at low temperature, vanadium is added to it.\n','','','False. To melt sand at low temperature pieces of discarded glass are added','',0,0,0,NULL),(16792,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : As glass is a good conductor of electricity, it is used as an insulator in electric appliances.\n','','','False. Being bad conductor of electricity glass is used as insulator in electric appliances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16793,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Drainage may get blocked due to glass.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(16794,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Plastic that can be molded','','','Thermoplastic','',0,0,0,NULL),(16795,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Plastic, the shape of whom cannot be changed on heating. \n','','','Thermosetting plastic','',0,0,0,NULL),(16796,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Protective covering like covers of CD and DVD are made from\n','','','Polystyrene','',0,0,0,NULL),(16797,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Surfing boards, small boats made from','','','Polyurethane','',0,0,0,NULL),(16798,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Vehicles are coated with it to protect from scratches.','','','Teflon','',0,0,0,NULL),(16799,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : This plastic is used for manufacturing lenses and artificial teeth.\n','','','Polyacrylic','',0,0,0,NULL),(16800,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : A new easily breakable item usually packed in a box prevents the items from breaking.','','','Thermocol','',0,0,0,NULL),(16801,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Thermocol is used in packing of delicate items as it is bears this quality.','','','Shock – absorber ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16802,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Persons staying in contact with thermocol develop problems of eyes, skin while liquid styrene causes.','','','Skin burns','',0,0,0,NULL),(16803,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : If oxides of these are added large amount of light is absorbed in glass.','','','Chromium, vanadium or iron','',0,0,0,NULL),(16804,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : This glass is soluble in water.','','','Water glass','',0,0,0,NULL),(16805,141,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The temperature at which the mixture of glass gets heated.','','','1500⁰C','',0,0,0,NULL),(16806,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Man made materials      ii) Natural materials ','','','i) Materials manufactured in the factories by processing naturally available materials are called manmade materials.\nii) Materials available in nature are called natural materials.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16808,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Plastic           ii) Thermoplastic ','','','i) A manmade material made up of organic polymer and which shows the property of plasticity is plastic.\nii) The plastic that can be molded, as per wish is called thermoplastic.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16810,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Thermosetting plastic           ii) Degradable materials ','','','i) The plastic that cannot be given a shape, once a specific shape is given with the help of a mould is called thermosetting plastic.\nii) Some materials are naturally degraded, they are called as degradable materials.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16812,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Non- degradable materials       ii) Thermocol ','','','i) Some materials do not degrade naturally, degrade, they are termed as non-degradable materials.\nii) Thermocol is a form of a complex material called polystyrene and it transforms into liquid state on heating at more than 100°C and returns to solid state on cooling.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16814,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Glass','','','Glass is a non-crystalline, hard but brittle solid material formed from mixture of silica and silicate.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16815,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between : Natural materials and Man-made materials','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16816,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between :  Thermoplastic and Thermosetting plastic','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16817,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between :  Degradable and Non-degradable','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16818,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is plastic an environment pollutant?','','','Plastic is non-degradable and hence an environment pollutant.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16819,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many different types of plastic are there?','','','There are more than 2000 different types of plastic.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16820,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is thermocol made up of?','','','Thermocol is a form of complex material called polystyrene.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16821,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which is the most hazardous method to destroy thermocol?','','','Burning is the hazardous method to destroy thermocol as it releases poisonous gases in the atmosphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16822,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is glass used in electric gases?','','','Since glass is a bad conductor of electricity, it is used as an insulator in electric appliances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16823,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name some green house gases?','','','They are sulphur dioxide, nitrogen dioxide, carbon dioxide released through burning of fuel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16824,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is Boro silicate glass used in pharmaceutical industry?','','','Borosilicate glass does not show any effect on medicines, so it is used in pharmaceutical industries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16825,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is added to soda lime glass to attain colours?','','','Ferrous oxide is mixed to get bluish green glass and copper oxide to get red glass.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16826,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the main ingredients of glass?','','','The main ingredients of glass are mixture of sand, soda, lime and small quantity of magnesium oxide.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16827,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Write the uses of PVC?','','','PVC is used for manufacturing the toys, bottles, raincoat, pipes, handbags, shoes, ropes, electric cable insulation and furniture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16828,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill effects of using thermocol for a long duration?','','','The person in contact with thermocol for a long duration may suffer from blood cancer like leukemia and lymphoma.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16829,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cups made from thermocol should be avoided, why?','','','The styrene may dissolve in the tea that is present in thermocole cups, leading to health problems.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16830,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the effects on health of people working on thermocol factory?','','','People staying in contact with thermocol for a long term may develop problems of eye, respiratory system, skin, digestive system, skin burns and also pregnant women may suffer from miscarriage.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16831,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are discarded glass pieces added to the mixture during glass production?','','','i) Sand, i.e. silicon dioxide melts at 1700°C.\nii) So as to melt the mixture at low temperature, pieces of discarded glass are added to it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16832,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the properties of silica glass?','','','i) Items made up of silica glass show minimum expansion on heating.\nii) It is not affected by acid and alkali.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16833,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Thermocol is produced from which material? ','','','i) Thermocol can be produced from a complex material called polystyrene.\nii) It can be given a desired shape after heating it more 100°C and returning to solid state on cooling.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16834,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why should we pot materials made of degradable substances?','','','i) Cotton cloth, woods, etc. are degradable materials.\nii) Cotton cloth takes 1 year to degrade, whereas wood takes 10 to 15 years to degrade.\niii) On the other hand, plastic takes thousand years to degrade, hence we should opt for materials made of degradable substances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16835,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the physical properties of plastic.','','','i) Plastic does not corrode or decompose. It is not easily affected by humidity, heat, rain etc.\nii) It can be molded into any shape and is a bad conductor of heat and electricity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16836,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain the effect of following materials on environment and health : Plastic','','','i) Plastic is a non-degradable substance and hence it is an environment pollutant.\nii) It emits harmful greenhouse gases. Plastic debris kills 1,00,000 marine animals annually\niii) Plastic packing material contains toxic chemicals that cause cancers, reproductive problems and metabolic diseases such as obesity.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16837,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain the effect of following materials on environment and health : Glass','','','i) The major environmental impact of glass production is due to atmosphere emissions from melting activities leading to the emission of CO₂, SO₂, and NO₂.\nii) As glass is non-degradable, pieces of waste glass material flows into water body harming the ecosystem.\niii) It may also block the drainage due to these pieces.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16840,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Write a short note on : Optic glass','','','i) Optic glass is produced from the mixture of sand, soda, limestone, barium oxide and boron.\nii) This type of pure glass is useful in production of spectacles, lenses, microscopic lenses etc.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16841,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Write a short note on : Uses of plastic','','','i) Plastic is versatile, hygienic, lightweight, flexible and highly durable substance.\nii) It is used for numerous packaging applications including containers, bottles, drums, trays, boxes, cup and vending packaging, baby products and protection packaging.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16842,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Plastic is non-degradable and environment pollutant.','','','i) Plastic materials can neither be degraded naturally nor are they environment friendly.\nii) It is not easily affected by humidity, heat and rain.\niii) It is not degraded by any microorganisms and hence it is a menace.\niv) Also, it cannot be broken down easily.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16843,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Thermocol is used in packing of delicate items.','','','i) Thermocol is a good shock-absorber.\nii) Hence the delicate items can be transported easily.\niii) Hence it is used in packing of delicate items.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16844,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Pieces of discarded glass are added while making glass. ','','','i) For glass production, mixture of sand, soda, lime and small quantity of magnesium oxide is heated in furnace.\nii) Silicon dioxide melts at 1700°C.\niii) So, as to melt the mixture at low temperature, pieces of discarded glass are added to it.\niv) Due to this, mixture, melts at 850°C. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16845,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Borosilicate glass is used in pharmaceutical industry','','','i) Borosilicate glass does not show any effect on medicines.\nii) Hence, bottles made of borosilicate glass are used in pharmaceutical industry to store the medicines. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16846,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Plastic is used everywhere and everything, its has caused harm to environment. Plastic bags are very harmful to the environment. It doesn’t decompose and becomes a big part of landfill. Is it right to make use of plastic on a larger scale?\n','','','i) The use of plastic and thermocol has increased significantly in the recent past.\nii) Firms consider plastic easier and cheaper to manufacture.\niii) Consumers also consider plastic lighter and beneficial to use.\niv) But we need to shift from plastic to natural materials, as plastics is non-biodegradable substance.\nv) Plastic doesn\'t corrode nor decompose.\nvi) Plastics clog waterways and float on reservoir, polluting them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16847,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which is better to use -glass or plastic?','','','i) Consumers believe glass is better for the environment.\nii) When glass is recycled it gets turned to more glass.\niii) It can be recycled over and over and never lose its integrity.\niv) Glass can be used safely at high temperature.\nv) They use 40% less energy and 80% of all recycled glass can be reclaimed.\nvi) Not all plastic can be recycled.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16848,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why are the plastic tanks used for storage chemical?','','','i) Chemicals are highly corrosive by nature.\nii) They are stored in plastic tanks as plastic does not corrode on coming in contact with chemicals.\niii) If chemicals are put in metal tanks, the tanks will be corroded due to the reaction between the chemicals and metals.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16852,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How many plastic carry bags are brought in your home in a day? What happens to those later on?\n','','','i) Previously, when there were no restriction, it would be minimum 5 to 6 plastic bags in a day.\nii) On market days, it would exceed more than a dozen also.\niii) These are either used for throwing wet waste or storing green vegetables in the refrigerators.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16853,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write the natural or man made raw material of the following items – mattress, beaker, bangle, chair, gunny bag, broom, knife, pen.\n','','','i) Mattress - Latex, polyrethane, national cotton\nii) Beaker - Glass (borosilicate glass)\niii) Bangles - Metal, wood, plastic, glass\niv) Chair - Wood, plastic, cane, fabric\nv) Gunny bag - Jute, hemp, polypropylene\nvi) Broom - Plastic, hair, corn husks, grass\nvii) Knife - Steel, iron, plastic, carbon steel, titanium alloys, cobalt\nviii) Pen - Brass, plastic, steel ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16854,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How are the used up and thrown away carry bags, water bottles, milk bags recycled? \n','','','i) The plastic grocery bags may have one more life after they are placed in recycling bin.\nii) It is then taken to a recycling center. These are then processed and used to create new types of plastic bags.\niii) Some plastic bags can be used as landfill.\niv) Plastic water bottles can also be recycled and can be used in many applications.\nv) It is used in Textiles, building materials, car industry, toys and non-food grade containers.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16855,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','To prevent the degradation due to sunlight, some materials are stored in which type of bottles?\n','','','i) The degradation of materials due to sunlight is called photo degradation.\nii) Amber glass has the ability to absorb UV radiation from sunlight.\niii) Beer, skin products, medicines, etc are stored in amber glass to prevent the degradation.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16856,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list about use of thermocol in your everyday life.','','','i) For wrapping easily breakable items packed in a box thermocol is used.\nii) Plates and cups used in mass feasts are also made up of thermocol.\niii) It is also used for packing fruits, vegetables, medicines and fish because of its properties like tasteless, fungi resistant and insulation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16857,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of 20 different man made materials present in your home, school and places around and discuss.\n','','','i) At school: chairs, benches, educom, chalk, duster, bags, pens, erasers, water bottles, plastic, tiffin’s, plastic cover for books, fans, regulators, air conditioners, cupboards, etc.\nii) At home: sofa, bed, chairs, telephone, table, flower vase, utensils, soap, shampoo, plastic bags, refrigerator, oven, hair dryer, television mobiles, etc.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16858,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Classify and make a chart of the materials used in various items in house. Make addition to the chart with reference to various materials.\n','','','Answern;','',0,0,1,NULL),(16859,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of items in your house those can be made up of a degradable material instead of plastic?\n','','','i) Storage bags - canvas or cloth bags\nii) Carpet - natural wool carpet\niii) Cups - glass or stainless steel\niv) Toothbrush - wooden toothbrushes\nv) Toys - soft toys with cotton and cloth\nvi) Yoga mat - Jute, hemp, bamboo  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16860,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of glass items of daily use. Glass of which different colours is used in these items.\n','','','Answer :','',0,0,1,NULL),(16861,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How plastic is produced?','','','i) Ingredients of plastic are cellulose, coal, natural gas, salt, crude oil.\nii) The process included in the production of plastic are polymerisation and polycondensation.\niii) In these processes, long polymer chains are formed from monomers.\niv) Each polymer has its own properties, structure and size depending on the various types of basic monomers used.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16862,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which measures will you arrange to minimize the environmental problems arising due to non-degradable plastic?\n','','','i) Avoiding throwing packaging materials like disposable plates, cutlery, cups, glasses etc.\nii) Using a reusable bag for shopping.\niii) Ban plastic bags and carry cloth or paper bags.\niv) Ignoring microbeads:\na) These are small plastic particles used in cosmetics, soap and personal care products.\nb) We should use microbead free products.\nv) Recycling - Plastic items that can\'t be reused should always be recycled to make sure that they do not end up in polluting the environment.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16863,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In what ways can we say the environment from pollution? ','','','i) Each responsible citizen should follow the 4R principle.\n          Reduce - Minimal use\n          Reuse - Use again\n          Recycle - Use again after processing\n          Recover - Reclaiming\nii) Then only we can save the environment from pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16864,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the different properties of plastics?','','','Properties of plastic are as follows:\ni) It does not corrode and decompose.\nii) It is not easily affected by humidity rain, heat etc.\niii) Items of any colour can be made from it.\niv) It can be molded into any shape due to the property of plasticity.\nv) It is a bad conductor of heat and electricity.\nvi) Being light in weight, it is easy to carry.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16865,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16867,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of thermosetting plastics.','','','Thermosetting plastics include the following examples:\ni) Bakelite         ii) Melamine         iii) Polyurethane   iv) Polyester\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16868,141,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is plastic? State the different types of plastics. Where are they used?','','','i) Plastic is a versatile substance, a man made material, made up of organic polymers.\nii) Plastic is classified into two types: the plastic that can be molded as per our wish is called thermoplastic.\niii) Another plastic is thermosetting plastic. If a specific shape is given with the help of a mould then the shape cannot be changed on heating.\niv) Different types and uses of thermoplastic and thermosetting plastics are as follows :\nThermoplastic :\na) Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) – Bottles, raincoat, pipes, handbags, shoes, cables, ropes, toys\nb) Polystyrene (PS) – used in toys, CD cover and DVD, thermos insulating parts\nc) Polyethylene (PE) – Milk bags, packing bags, flexible garden pipes\nd) Polypropylene (PP) – Parts of loudspeaker and vehicles, ropes, mattresses, laboratory appliances.\n\nThermosetting plastic\na) Bakelite – Cabinets of radio, TV, telephones, electric switches, toys, coverings over handles of cookers\nb) Melamine – useful items like cup – saucers, plates, tray, some spare parts of airplane engines, insulating covering\nc) Polyurethane – Surfing boards, small boats, furniture, vehicle seats\nd) Polyester  - Fiber glass, toners of laser printers, textile industry','',0,0,0,NULL),(16869,141,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the adverse effects of excessive use of thermocol on environment and humans? \n','','','The adverse effects of environment and humans are: thermocol on\ni) It is non-biodegradable substance. Hence many people opt for destroying it by burning which is more hazardous as it releases poisonous gases in the atmosphere.\nii) If food kept in it is heated into it, then styrene may dissolve in food, leading to health problems.\niii) Styrene is carcinogenic. The person in contact for long duration may suffer from blood cancer like leukemia and lymphoma.\niv) Persons staying in contact with thermocol for long term may develop the problems of eyes, respiratory system, skin, digestive system, etc.\nv) Pregnant women may face miscarriage. Liquid styrene may cause skin burns.    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16870,141,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is glass produced and state the properties of glass?','','','a) Glass making :\ni) For glass production, a mixture of sand, soda, lime and small quantity of magnesium is heated in a furnace.\nii) Sand i.e. silicon dioxide melts at 1700⁰C.\niii) So as to melt the mixture at low temperature, pieces of discarded glass is added to it.\niv) Due to this, the mixture melts a 850⁰C.\nv) When all the ingredients liquefied, it is heated upto 1500⁰C.\nvi) Due to sudden cooling mixture becomes homogeneous, amorphous and transparent instead of becoming crystalline.\n\nb) Properties of glass :\ni) On heating glass, it becomes soft and can be molded into any shape.\nii) Density depends upon the ingredients.\niii) Glass is a slow conductor of heat.\niv) It is a bad conductor of electricity, so used as an insulator in electric appliances.\nv) It is transparent, hence most light passes through glass. ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16871,141,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the different types of glass and their uses.','','','Different types of glass and their uses are:\ni) Silica glass: Produced by using silica. Not effected by acids or alkali, used to produced laboratory glass-wares.\nii) Borosilicate glass Made up of sand soda, boric acid and aluminium oxide. Used to store medicines.\niii) Alkali silicate glass: Made up of sand and soda. This glass is soluble in water as called as water glass.\niv) Lead glass: Made with sand, soda, limestone and leadoxide. Used in manufacturing light bulbs and tubes.\nv) Optical glass Made up of sand, soda, limestone, barium oxide and boron. Used in making spectacles, lenses.\nvi) Coloured glass: Oxide of different metals is mixed with glass. To get bluish green glass, ferrous oxide is added to it and copper oxide to get red glass.\nvii) Processed glass: To improve the quality and utility, processes are performed on glass to form reinforced glass, plain glass, fiber glass, fen glass, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16872,141,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the following diagram and answer the questions given below : a) What does the above figure represent?              b) Which raw material are used in the above process?          c) Enlist any two properties of the substance produced in the above process.\n','','','a) The given figure represents the glass manufacturing process. \nb) The raw materials used in glass manufacturing process includes sand, soda, limestone, magnesium oxide and other metal oxides (to obtain coloured glass, if any) depending on the type of required glass.\nc) The properties of glass are: \ni) Glass can be moulded into any shape as it turns soft on heating. \nii) Glass is a slow conductor of heat. \niii)  Glass is a bad conductor of electricity, hence, it is used as an insulator in electric appliances. \niv) Density of glass depends upon its ingredients. \nv) As glass is transparent, most of the light passes through the glass. On the other hand, if glass contains oxides of chromium, vanadium or iron, large amount of light is absorbed in glass.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16873,141,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','a) How will you define glass?        b) What led to the development of glass production technique?            c) Write a short note on : i) Borosilicate glass          	ii) Coloured glass\n','','','a) Glass is a non-crystalline, transparent, hard but brittle solid material formed from a mixture of silica and silicate. \nb) 1) Glass was discovered by chance. \n2) Some Phoenician traders while cooking in desert placed their cooking vessels on limestones. \n3) When the hot cooking vessel was kept off the limestone, a transparent material had been formed. \n4) It was thought that this transparent material was formed due to heating of sand and limestone. This led to the development of glass production technique. \nc) 1) Borosilicate glass:\n i) Borosilicate glass is produced by melting the mixture of sand, soda, boric acid and aluminium oxide. \nii) This glass does not show any effect on medicines. Hence, the bottles used in pharmaceutical industry are made up of borosilicate glass to store the medicines. \n2) Coloured glass : \ni) Soda lime glass is colourless. \nii) So as to impart a desired colour, oxide of specific metal is mixed during manufacturing process. E.g. Ferrous oxide is mixed with the raw materials to get bluish green glass.    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16875,142,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the interaction among the factors of an ecosystem.','','','i) Each and every abiotic factor (air, water, soil, sunlight, temperature, humidity) affects the biotic factors in the ecosystem\nii) The abiotic factors in an ecosystem decide which biotic factors will survive in it and what will be their number.\niii) The proportion of abiotic factors in an ecosystem is always changing as biotic factors use or excrete abiotic factors.\niv) Every biotic factor affects abiotic factors as well as other biotic factors around it.\nv) Every living organism in an ecosystem plays a role while living, moving in that ecosystem.\nvi) Most of the ecosystems are complex and there is tremendous variety of species in them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16876,142,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Diminishment of ecosystem due to human interference.','','','i) Many human activities have side effects on functions of ecosystems and cause their diminishment.\nii) For example, mining and excessive cutting of trees changes the use of land, so interactions between biotic and abiotic factors are also affected.\niii) Different human activities have different effects on ecosystems. These can be transformation of an ecosystem from one to another or extinction of a species.\niv) Human activities responsible for diminishment of ecosystems are:\na) Increasing use of resources due to increased population. Humans are consumers in an ecosystem and Ecosystems can provide basic needs in normal conditions. But the increased population and changing life style demands more than necessary which make stress on ecosystem.\nb) Urbanization: Due to continuous process of urbanization more and more forests, wetlands and grasslands are destroyed which causes the ecosystems either change or get completely destroyed.\nc) Industrialization and traffic: Raw materials for industrialization are obtained by destroying forests. To provide amenities for increased traffic, many times roads and railways are built through forests and wetlands.\nd) Tourism: A lot of amenities are created for tourists. This causes the destruction of local ecosystems.\ne) Large Dams: Since dams cover vast lands, forests or grasslands in that area get converted into aquatic ecosystem.\nf) Wars: Heavy bombing and mine explosions during war are not only life threatening but also destroy natural ecosystem.\ng) Natural disasters like earthquake, volcano, floods, etc. also result in destruction of ecosystem.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16877,142,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Describe features of Asian Cheetah.','','','i) Light fawn-coloured fur that becomes paler on the sides, on the front of the muzzle below the eyes and is one of the features of Asian Cheetah.\nii) Black coloured spots are arranged on the head and nape, but irregularly scattered on body, legs, paws and tail.\niii) The tail has black stripe. The coat and the mane are short.\niv) The head and body measures about 112 - 135 cms with a 66 - 84 cm long tail.\nv) It weighs about 55 kg.\nvi) Males are slightly larger than the females.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16878,142,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain effect of tourism on the ecosystem.','','','i) Since tourism has very much importance in human life, increased tourist flow has reduced quality of environment.\nii) Excessive development in all fields of life has led to overcrowding which causes environmental disturbances.\niii) Noise pollution causes disturbance to the biotic factors in surrounding.\niv) Uncontrolled waste disposable has led to degradation of natural ecosystem.\nv) Green house gases from increased number of vehicles can cause soil and vegetal degradation.\nvi) Improper disposal of solid waste and littering also affects natural ecosystem.\nvii) Sand degradation causes soil erosion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16879,142,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain some national parks/sanctuaries in India.','','','i) Many ecosystems are conserved in around 520 sanctuaries and national parks in India.\nii) The Great Himalayan National Park is the largest sanctuary where white panther is conserved.\niii) Elephants, deers, tigers are conserved at Kaziranga.\niv) The sanctuary Bharatpur is famous for aquatic birds.\nv) The Ranthambore Sanctuary is famous for tigers.\nvi) Geer forest in Gujarat is the only habitat for Asiatic lion.    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16880,142,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is an ecosystem? Explain the effects of increased population on the ecosystem in detail.\n','','','a) Biotic and abiotic factors and their interactions form an ecosystem. \n                   OR \nThe structure formed due to reciprocal relationships between living organisms, their habitat and environment is called as ecosystem. \nb) Following are the effects of increased population on ecosystems: \n1) Depletion of natural resources: Increase in population has led to an increase in the demand for natural resources. Ecosystems can fulfil basic requirements of humans but large scale increase in population and change in lifestyle of humans has lead to over- exploitation of natural resources. This has created stress on the ecosystems and has generated large amount of wastes. Burden of waste disposal has also created stress on ecosystems. \n2) Threat to biodiversity / Loss of biodiversity: Due to rapid urbanization, industrialization, etc., more and more forest areas are being cleared. Destruction of agricultural lands, marshlands, wetlands, forests and grasslands for construction of buildings, roads, dams, etc., have either changed or destroyed ecosystems. Loss of habitats of many organisms has ultimately led to loss of biodiversity. \n3) Increase in pollution levels: Population growth has had a negative impact on the quality of the ecosystem. Increase in population has led to excessive use of fossil fuels, chemical fertilizers and pesticides, resulting in a rapid increase in waste generation. This has increased the levels of air, water and soil pollution. \n4) Global warming: Increased level of CO₂ emissions due to burning of fossil fuels, deforestation, etc., has caused a rise in the global temperature. It has tremendous impacts on plants, animals as well as humans.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16882,142,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Many human activities have side effects on function of ecosystem and cause their diminishment. Example-mining and excessive cutting of tress changes the use of land, so interactions between biotic and abiotic factors are also affected. Different human activities have different effects on ecosystems. There can be transformation of an ecosystem from one to another or extinction of a species. \na) State any two human activities which affects ecosystem.           b) How does industrialization affect the ecosystem?             c) Name some natural diseases that affect ecosystem.  \n','','','a) Mining and deforestation are the two activities which affect the ecosystem.\nb) Raw materials required for industrialization are obtained by destroying forests. This results in destruction of forests thereby affecting the ecosystem.\nc) Floods, volcanoes, earthquakes and droughts.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16883,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the possible threats to grasslands?','','','i) Global warming causes increase in atmospheric temperature and affects rainfall pattern. This adversely affect the growth of grass.\nii) Increased population causes grasslands to be converted into residential and commercial areas.\niii) Uncontrolled grazing of cattle can also cause reduction in grasslands.   \niv) Occasional wild fire may also lead in damaging of grassland areas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16884,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which biotic factors get affected due to a dam?','','','i) Dams cover vast lands. Forests or grasslands in that area get converted into aquatic ecosystem.\nii) Dams affect biotic factors in a number of ways.\niii) The wall of the dam stops migration of fish.\niv) It may lead to extinction of some fish, aquatic animals and birds.   \nv) Number of forests, wetlands and farmlands may get destroyed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16885,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the causes for extinction of wild life?','','','i) Natural disasters like fire, floods, drought, storm, etc are the some reasons for extinction of wild life.\nii) Increased population causes deforestation that has brought loss of living space for wild life.\niii) Hunting is another major reason for extinction of wild life.\niv) Increased population causes pollution, which results in death of wild animals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16886,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the steps that we can take to conserve wild life forms?','','','i) Some rules should be introduced against hunting wild life.\nii) Living space should be provided for wild life animals.\niii) Strict actions and laws should be taken against deforestation.\niv) Take some steps for increasing the population of wild life species which are going to extinct. It can be done by capturing and breeding them and releasing them into their natural habitats.\nv) Strict control to be exercised to prevent commercial exploitation of the species and their products. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16887,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Describe the following pictures :','','','The first picture looks like a deserted region, with few trees and less water, with many cactus plants growing here and there. You can see many camels in this region. It is a place with no rainfall, the days are very hot and nights are very cold.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16888,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Describe the following pictures :','','','The second picture looks like a forested area with plenty of trees around, like a leafy paradise and a woody heaven for the animals living in it. The birds are moving at their own space. The water body provides with plenty of water to these biotic life forms.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16889,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the picture given below and explain in it detail. ','','','i) The given picture represents a forest ecosystem. \nii) A forest ecosystem is a community of organisms that lives within a forest. High diversity of plants and animals is seen. \niii) Forest ecosystem consists of biotic and abiotic factors. Biotic factors include producers, consumers and decomposers. Abiotic factors include air, soil, temperature, pH, etc. \niv) Other examples of producers, consumers and decomposers of forest ecosystem are as follows: Producers: Dipterocarpus, teak, pine, sandalwood, conifer (cedar, Sequoia), ferns, mosses, oak, Eucalyptus, etc. Primary consumers: Ant, grasshopper, butterfly, squirrel, Iguana, fruit bat, etc. Secondary consumers: Spider, snake, bird lizard, jackal, chameleon, frog, owls, etc. Decomposers: Aspergillus, Agaricus, Amanita, Fusarium, etc.    ','',1,0,0,NULL),(16890,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the figure given below and describe it briefly :','','','i) The given picture represents the interactions among the different factors of an ecosystem.\nii) Ecosystem consists of biotic and abiotic factors. Both these factors continuously interact with each other. The biotic and abiotic factors and the interactions between them form an ecosystem.\niii) Biotic (living) factors include producers, consumers and decomposers, whereas abiotic (non-living) factors include chemical factors like organic and inorganic substances and physical factors like sunlight, water, etc.\niv) In an ecosystem, the abiotic factors determine the survival of biotic factors.\nv) Producers (plants) are autotrophic, they depend on abiotic factors like sunlight, water, soil, minerals, etc. for photosynthesis. Energy produced during this process enters the ecosystem through the producers.\nvi) Primary consumers (herbivores) depend on producers for their food and the secondary consumers depend on the primary consumers. The tertiary consumers depend on secondary consumers. The primary consumers play a major role in transfer of energy from plants to other biotic factors in an ecosystem.\nvii) Decomposers breakdown the dead remains of plants and animals and make nutrients available in the ecosystem.\nviii) The sequence of transfer of energy from producers to decomposers forms a food chain and removal any of these factors can disrupt the entire food chain.\nix) Thus, biotic and abiotic factors interact and work together in an ecosystem. They play a major role in the maintenance of the ecosystem.    ','',1,0,0,NULL),(16891,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the picture given below. Identify the type of ecosystem and mention its characteristics :\n','','','i) The given picture represents grassland ecosystem.\nii) Grasslands are the areas with extensive growth of grass. Very few trees and shrubs are found in this ecosystem.\niii) These ecosystems develop in areas where the rainfall is insufficient to support the growth of big trees.\niv) Dwarf plants grow in these areas due to long summers and limited rainfall.\nv) Animals like goat, sheep, giraffe, zebra, elephant, deer, chital, tiger, lion, etc. are found in this ecosystem. Also, various birds, insects and microbes are present.','',1,0,0,NULL),(16892,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Complete the chart :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16893,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following chart :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16894,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Complete the table :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(16895,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Select the appropriate options and complete the following paragraph : [71, 29, fresh-water, creek ecosystem, marine ecosystem, producers, decomposers, 40, 50]\n  ------ % of the Earth’s surface is covered with water and only ---- % is land. According biomes are widespread. These aquatic biomes contains different ecosystems. The types of aquatic ecosystem are ---- , ------ and fresh-water ecosystem. Ponds, lakes and rivers are --- ecosystems. ----- present at the dead remains of aquatic plants and animals into abiotic factors.\n','','','71%, 29%, marine ecosystem, creek ecosystem, fresh water, Decomposers','',0,0,0,NULL),(16896,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Biotic factors     ii) Abiotic factors','','','i) The world comprises of living organisms, these are known as the biotic factors.\nii) The world comprises of non-living factors called as the abiotic factors.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16898,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Ecosystem         ii) Niche','','','i) The interaction of the biotic factors with the abiotic factors is termed as the Ecosystem.\nii) The position of any living organism in context to the other living organisms and role it is playing is termed as the \'Niche\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16900,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Decomposers','','','The organic substances from dead bodies of plants and animals are converted into inorganic substances by microorganisms. These microorganisms are called decomposers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16901,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Biomes ','','','In some regions on earth, a large area has same climate and abiotic factors. The living organisms in these area are also similar. So a specific ecosystem develops in a vast area. Such large ecosystems are called Biomes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16902,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Urbanization ','','','Destruction of more and more agricultural lands, marsh lands, wetlands, forests for buildings and other facilities is called urbanization.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16903,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the effects of increased population on ecosystems?','','','Due to increased population, man kept on snatching natural resources on a large scale. Changing life style demands \'more\' than \'necessary\'. This has increased stress on the ecosystems.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16904,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How is urbanization responsible for destruction of ecosystems?','','','i) More and more agricultural lands, marsh lands, wetlands, forests and grasslands are being destroyed for buildings and other basic facilities around.\nii) As a result of this human interference, ecosystems either change or get completely destroyed.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16905,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the reasons for war?','','','Differences and competition over land, water, mineral resources or some economic and political reasons lead to war among human races.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16906,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What destroys the natural ecosystems?','','','Heavy explosions during war and mine explosions destroy the natural ecosystems.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16907,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Why did Asian Cheetah become extinct in last century?','','','i) Loss of living space is the one reason behind the extinction of Asian Cheetah. Due to increased population, they lose their habitat.\nii) Lack of availability of food, hunting for pleasure as well as skin are another reasons for extinction of Asian Cheetah.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16908,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Can we call a tree an independent ecosystem?','','','i) An ecosystem refers to the continuous interaction between living and non-living factors.\nii) Most of the ecosystems are complex and they cannot exist independently.\niii) So we could not call a tree an independent ecosystem.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16909,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Are the local ecosystems like river, ponds, lake safe?','','','i) Harmful effluents like sewage, acid rain, oil or industrial discharge when released into these water bodies, causes immense harm.\nii) This leads to a wild range of effects on these aquatic ecosystems like the population of fishes are destroyed.\niii) As a result, these water bodies like river, ponds, lakes are not safe.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16910,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What will be the effects on biotic factors in the running water of a river?','','','i) Sometimes flow of water is so fast that it cannot support plants.\nii) If there are not plants, then animals living in the water body cannot dwell there.\niii) Plant cannot take roots. They will be drifted and uprooted.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16911,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Classify the following into living and non-living factor : sunlight, sunflower, elephant, lotus, algae, stone, grass, water, ant, soil, cat, tern, air, lion.\n','','','Living things – Sunflower, Elephant, Lotus, Algae, Grass, Ant, Cat, Fern, Lion\nNon-living things – Sunlight, Stone, Water, Soil, Air\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(16917,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Plants in an ecosystem are producers ','','','i) Chlorophyll allows the plants to trap solar energy and convert it into chemical energy.\nii) Food produced by plants is taken up by primary consumers.\niii) Secondary consumers derive nutrition from primary consumers, and so on.\niv) Thus, all the consumer organisms in an ecosystem get energy directly/indirectly from the trapped solar energy stored in plants.\nv) Hence, plants are called producers in an ecosystem.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16918,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Large dams destroy ecosystems.','','','i) Dams cover vast land.\nii) Hence, forests or grasslands in that area get converted into aquatic ecosystem.  \niii) Dams also lessen the water current in the lower area.\niv) Therefore, the previous ecosystems in that running water are destroyed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16919,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Rhinos were restored in Dudhwa forest.','','','i) About 150 years ago, Dudhwa forest was the habitat for single horned Rhinos.\nii) But in the 20th century the species faced extinction due to unrelenting hunting.\niii) On 1st April 1984, rhino was restored there and were bred in captivity and then released in their habitat.\niv) For this, 27 sq. km grassland and forest were made available with facilities of water supply for the rhinos.\nv) Later 2 observatories were established and these efforts are successful.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16920,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between : Evergreen forests and Grass lands.','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16921,142,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Producers and Consumers ','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(16956,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Fusarium, Aspergillus, Leptocorsia, Polycarpus','','','Leptocorsia. All others are decomposers, but Leptocorsia is a consumer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16957,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Earthquake, Mining, Tourism, War','','','Earthquake. All others are man made causes of ecosystem destruction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16958,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Ulothix, Hydrilla, Azolla, Fungi','','','Fungi. It is a decomposer while the rest are producers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16959,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Fresh water ecosystem, Creek ecosystem, Grass land ecosystem, Marine ecosystem.\n','','','Grassland ecosystem. It is a land biomes while, the rest are types of aquatic ecosystem.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16960,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Elephants, wild boars, aquatic birds, deer.','','','Aquatic birds. It is famous at the sanctuary of Bharatpur while, the rest are famous at Kaziranga.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16961,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Potassium, proteins, carbohydrates, lipids','','','Potassium. Potassium is an inorganic substance while, rest are the organic substance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16962,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Air, water, calcium, sunlight','','','Calcium. Calcium is a inorganic substance while, rest are the physical abiotic factors.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16963,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Lion, deer, rabbit, Vulture','','','Deer : deer is a secondary consumers while, rest are the territory consumers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16964,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Cow, deer, rabbit, lion','','','Lion. Lion is a territory consumer while, rest are all the primary consumers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16965,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Nitrogen, minerals, water, plants','','','Plants. Plants are biotic factors while, rest are abiotic factors.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16966,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Ranthambore : Rajasthan : : Gir forest : -----','','','Gujrat','',0,0,0,NULL),(16967,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Grasshopper : Consumer : : Teak : -----','','','Producer ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16968,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Plants : Producer : : Bacteria : ------','','','Decomposers','',0,0,0,NULL),(16969,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Earthquake : Natural disaster : : War : -----','','','Man-made disaster ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16970,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Zebra : Grassland : : Frog : ------','','','aquatic ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16971,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Annelids : Primary consumers : : Herons : -----','','','Territory consumers','',0,0,0,NULL),(16972,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : 71% of the Earth : Water : : 29 % of earth : ----','','','land ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16977,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The world around us is made up of only living things.\n','','','False. The world around us is made up of living and non-living things.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16978,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Biotic and abiotic factors and their interactions form an ecosystem.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16979,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The proportion of abiotic factors in an ecosystems is always changing as biotic factors use or excrete abiotic factors.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16980,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Biotic factor does not affect on abiotic factors in an ecosystem\n','','','False. Biotic factors affects on abiotic factors in an ecosystem.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16981,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The biomes which exist only on land are called land biomes.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16982,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Rabbit is a secondary consumer.\n','','','False. Rabbit is a primary consumer','',0,0,0,NULL),(16983,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : All microbes survive due to oxygen.\n','','','False. Some microbes survive due to oxygen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16984,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Human interference result in destruction of ecosystem.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16985,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Microbes are considered as the secondary consumers in the food chain.  \n','','','False. Microbes are considered as decomposer in the food chain.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16986,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Large ecosystems which contain many small ecosystem.\n','','','Biomes ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16987,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Place where rainfall is not enough to grow big trees.','','','Grassland ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16988,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : A forest where single horned rhino survives with the other animals.\n','','','Dudhwa forest ','',0,0,0,NULL),(16989,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Human activities that cause diminished of ecosystem','','','Mining, Deforestation, industrialization etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16990,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Substances like carbohydrates, proteins and fats are termed as\n','','','Organic substances','',0,0,0,NULL),(16991,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : A natural ecosystem which consists of variety of plants, animals and abiotic factors.\n','','','Evergreen forest','',0,0,0,NULL),(16992,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The largest sanctuary where a white panther is a rare species. \n','','','Great Himalayan National park','',0,0,0,NULL),(16993,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the types of aquatic ecosystems?','','','There are three types of aquatic ecosystems namely; Fresh water ecosystems, marine ecosystem and creek ecosystem.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16994,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What leads to the destruction of forests?','','','Raw materials required for industrialization are obtained by destroying forests. This results in the destruction of forests.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16995,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are land biomes?','','','The biomes which exist only on land are called land biomes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(16996,142,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a ‘niche’?','','','The position of any living organism in context to other living organisms and the role it is playing is called \'Niche\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17030,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Mercury, Venus, Alpha Centauri, Neptune','','','Alpha Centauri. All others are planets in the solar system where as Alpha Centauri is a star.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17031,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Black hole, White dwarf, Neutron star, Red giant star','','','Red giant star. All others are the end stage of the evolution of the star.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17032,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Hubble, Nebula, Spitzer, James Webb.','','','Nebula. All others are space telescope launched in the outer space, whereas Nebula is interstellar cloud of dust and helium.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17033,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Spiral galaxy, elliptical galaxy, irregular galaxy, planetary galaxy.\n','','','Planetary galaxy. It is not a type of galaxy while others are types of galaxy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17034,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Initial mass of the star- Between 8 to 35 Msun, Mstar < 8Msun, between 8 to 25 Msun, Mstar > 8 Msun.\n','','','Between 8 to 35 Mₛᵤₙ - It is not the initial mass in division of stars in evolution while others are initial mass for division of stars in evolution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17035,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Less than 8 M sun : White dwarf : : Between 8 to 25 M sun : ------\n','','','Neutron star','',0,0,0,NULL),(17036,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : High mass star : Super giant : : Low mass star : -----','','','Red giant','',0,0,0,NULL),(17037,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : ---- : Mars : : Moon : Earth.','','','Phobos','',0,0,0,NULL),(17038,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Radius of Sun : 695700 km : : Mass of sun : ------','','','2 × 10³⁰ kg','',0,0,0,NULL),(17039,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Msun : unit of mass : : Light year : -----','','','Unit of length','',0,0,0,NULL),(17040,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : 1 Msun : 2 × 10³⁰ kg : : 1 light year : -------','','','9.5 × 10¹² km','',0,0,0,NULL),(17043,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Light year is used to measure time.\n','','','False. Light year is used to measure large distances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17044,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : End stage of a star depends on its initial mass.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17045,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Among the billions of stars, Sun is an ordinary star.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17046,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : We can divide our galaxy into three types mainly spiral, elliptical and irregular.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17047,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Helium at the center of the star acts as a fuel.\n','','','False. Hydrogen at the centre of the star act as a fuel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17048,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Light takes 1 sec to reach us from the moon while it takes 8 minutes to reach us from the Sun.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17049,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Sun remained unchanged over the last 4.5 billion years.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17050,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Evolution of a star means no change in its properties with time resulting in its passing through different stages.\n','','','False. Evolution of a star means change in its properties with time resulting in its passing through different stages.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17051,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : A star in our galaxy exploded 7500 years back.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17052,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Depending on mass, there are three paths of evolution and three end stages of stars.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17053,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : A star ends its life as a neutron star when the pressure of its electrons balances its gravity. \n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17054,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Only light can emit from black hole.\n','','','False. Light does not get reflected from black hole but absorbed.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17055,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The sun will pass through the supergiant stage during its evolution.\n','','','False. The sun will pass through red giant stage during its evolution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17056,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The sun will end its life as a white dwarf.\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17057,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : A collection of billions of stars.','','','Galaxy','',0,0,0,NULL),(17058,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Our galaxy is also called as ','','','Milky way','',0,0,0,NULL),(17059,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : An object through which we can see the planets and stars.\n','','','Telescope','',0,0,0,NULL),(17060,143,'descriptive','1b',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : These are gigantic spheres of hot gas.','','','Stars ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17061,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : 72% of the mass of the Sun is made up of this gas.','','','Hydrogen','',0,0,0,NULL),(17062,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The mass of the Sun is about this many times that of earth.\n','','','3.3 lakh ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17063,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The distance travelled by light in one year.','','','Light year ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17064,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Huge clouds of gas and dust are known as ----','','','Interstellar Clouds','',0,0,0,NULL),(17065,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The evolution of stars depends on the ','','','Initial mass','',0,0,0,NULL),(17066,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : When the sun becomes a red giant, the diameter','','','increases ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17067,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is galaxy?','','','A collection of billion of stars, their planetary systems and interstellar clouds, which are present in the empty spaces between stars is termed as a galaxy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17068,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the different constituents of Solar system?','','','Solar system consist of Milky Way, Stars and planets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17069,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is a satellite?','','','An artificial body placed in orbit around the earth or any other planet in order to collect information is called as satellite.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17071,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Which is the star nearest to us?','','','Sun is the only star nearest to us.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17072,143,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by balanced and unbalanced forces? ','','','Balanced forces are those forces with net force equal to zero and unbalanced forces are those with an affective net force.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17074,143,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What has helped study the universe?','','','i) If we observe the sky, we can see only planets and stars.\nii) But the other components were far from reach of the human eye.\niii) The telescopes have helped to view the heavenly objects more effectively.\niv) These gave a detailed information about the universe.\nv) Some telescopes are placed on the surface of the earth while some others are kept aboard manmade satellites, which are orbiting the earth in fixed orbits.\nvi) As these telescopes are situated above the earth\'s atmosphere, they can observe astronomical objects more effectively.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17075,143,'descriptive','4',4,1,'Text Book','','How do star form?','','','i) Huge clouds of gas and dust present in the empty spaces between stars in a galaxy are called interstellar clouds.\nii) Scientist use the unit of light year for measuring large distances.\niii) A light year is the distance travelled by light in a year.\niv) The size of interstellar cloud is about a few light years.\nv) Due to some disturbance the clouds start contracting.\nvi) The density increases and the temperature also increases with a dense sphere of hot gas formed from the cloud.\nvii) When the temperature and the density increases, the nuclear energy generation starts there.\nviii) Because of this energy generation the gas sphere becomes self-luminous and a star is formed.\nix) In the Sun, the energy is generated by the fusion of hydrogen nuclei to form helium nuclei. The hydrogen at the center of the star acts as a fuel.  \nx) Thus, energy is generated by burning the fuel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17076,143,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain in detail about the evolution of stars.','','','i) It means a change in its properties with time resulting in its passing through different stages.\nii) The reason for evolution of stars is the burning of and therefore the decrease in the amount of fuel in the center.\niii) When the fuel finishes the energy generation stops, due to which the temperature of the star decreases.\niv) Due to the decrease in temperature, the gas pressure decrease and the balance, between gas pressure and gravitational force cannot be maintained.\nv) The star starts contracting and causes another fuel to start burning.\nvi) Higher the mass of the star, higher is the number of fuels used thus a lot of changes occur in the star.\nvii) When the possible fuels are exhausted, the energy generation finally stops and the temperature of the star starts decreasing.\nviii) The balance between the gravitational force and gas pressure cannot be maintained.\nix) The star starts contracting and density increases to such high value that the new pressures generated are no longer depend temperature.\nx) The stability of star remains intact forever and this is the end stage of the star.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17078,143,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the end stages of star.','','','Stars can be classified based on their mass and end stages : \n1) Stars having initial mass less than 8 times the mass of the sun (M star\n< 8 M sun): Stars in this group undergo huge expansion and their radius increases by a factor of 100 to 200. This is called as red giant stars. At the end of the evolution, these stars explode and their outer gas envelope is thrown out. In this stage, star looks white and due to its small size it is called white dwarf. \n2) Stars having initial mass between 8 and 25 time the mass of the sun: These stars also go through red giant stage and later through supergiant stage during which their size may increase 1000 times. Huge explosion called supernova explosion is very powerful. Finally, the central portion of star which is left behind is made of neutrons and is called neutron stars.\nNeutron star is the end stage of this star.\n3) Stars having mass larger than 25 times the mass of sun: These stars evolve like the stars in the second group but after the supernova explosion, no pressure is capable of balancing their huge gravitational force and they continue contracting for ever. Their gravitational force increases tremendously. Thus, we cannot see the star at all but can probably see a minute black hole. The end stage of this star is therefore called a black hole. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17079,143,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','a) If you are the Sun, write about your properties in your own words.              b) Describe White dwarf. \n','','','a) The properties of sun:\ni) I am the star at the centre of our solar system.\nii) I am the source of energy for all life on earth.\niii) I have four layers namely Photosphere, Chromosphere, Transition zone and Corona.\niv) Total energy emitted by me is 4 x 10²⁶ watts.\nv) My mass is 2 x 10³⁰ kg which is about 3.3 lakh times that of the earth.\nvi) My radius is 695700 km which is 100 times that of earth.\nvii) My surface temperature is 5800 K, and a temperature at the centre is 1.5 × 10⁷ K.\nviii) I am 4.5 x 10⁹ years old and have remained unchanged over the past 4.5 billion years.\n\nb) White dwarfs:\ni) Stars having initial mass less than 8 times the mass of Sun undergo huge expansion and their radius increases and such stars are called Red giant stars.\nii) At the end of the evolution, stars explode and outer gas envelope is thrown out.\niii) The inner part contracts and size of star becomes same as that of earth. The density also becomes very high.\niv) The pressure due to electrons thus become independent of temperature and is able to balance gravitational force forever.\nv) In this state, the star looks white due to its small size it is called a white dwarf.\nvi) White dwarf is the end stage of stars in this range.    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17080,143,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between : White dwarf, Neutron star and Black hole. ','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(17082,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the end stages of stars having initial mass less than 8 times the mass of the Sun (Mstar < 8 Msun).\n','','','Stars in this group undergo huge expansion and their radius increases by a factor 100 to 200. They are called as red giant stars because of their large size and low temperature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17083,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the end stages of stars having mass between 8 and 25 times the mass of the Sun (8 Msun < Mstar < 25 Msun).\n','','','i) These stars go through the red giant stage and the supergiant stage during which their size may increase 1000 times.\nii) The huge explosion is called as supernova explosion, which occurs at last is very powerful and a large amount of energy released.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(17084,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the net stages of stars having mass larger than 25 times the mass of the Sun (Mstar > 25 Msun).\n','','','i) These stars evolve like the stars in the second group but after the supernova explosion, no pressure is capable of balancing their huge gravitational force and they continue contracting for ever.\nii) As the size becomes smaller, their density and gravitational force increase tremendously.\niii) All the nearby objects get attracted towards these stars and nothing can come out of them not even light.\niv) Any light falling on these stars does not get reflected and gets absorbed inside the star.\nv) Thus, we cannot see the star at all but can see a black hole at its place.\nvi) This end stage of the star is called as black hole.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17085,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why was the name black hole given?','','','i) During evolution of stars whose initial mass is greater than 25 times the mass of the Sun, they go through the supernova explosion stage.\nii) These stars continue to contract forever.\niii) As their size becomes smaller, their density and their gravitational force increases tremendously.\niv) As all the nearby objects get attracted towards the stars, nothing comes out of them, not even light.\nv) Any light falling on these stars does not get reflected and gets absorbed inside the star.\nvi) Thus, we cannot see the star but a minute black hole at its place. Hence, these stars are given the name blackhole.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17086,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which type of stars end their life as a neutron star?','','','i) The stars having a mass between 8 and 25 times the mass of the Sun go through the red giant stage and later through the super giant stage during which size may increase 1000 times.\nii) The central portion which is left behind after the explosion, contracts and its size becomes as small as about 10 km.\niii) In this state the stars are completely made up of neutrons and are called as neutron stars.    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17087,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which celestial bodies/events are seen here?','','','a) Spiral galaxy            b) Interstellar clouds            c) A large cluster of stars          d) White dwarf              e) The supernova explosion','',1,0,0,NULL),(17088,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Based on the life cycle of stars given below, answer the following questions :  a) What are the three end stages of stars?         b) Name the factor which evolution of star is based on.         c) How long does it take for light to reach the earth from Alpha Century? \n\n','','','a) The three end stages of stars are:\ni) Initial mass less than 8 times the mass of the Sun. (Mstar < 8 Msun).\nii) Initial mass between 8 and 25 times the mass of the Sun. (8 Msun < Mstar < 25 Msun).\niii) Initial mass larger than 25 times the mass of the Sun (Mstar > 25 Msun).\nb) The evolution of star is based on their initial mass.\nc) It takes about 4.2 years for light to reach the earth from Alpha Centauri.','',1,0,0,NULL),(17089,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Derive the relation: 1 light year ≈ 9.5 × 10¹² km','','','A light year is distance travelled by light in one year.\nAs speed of light is 3 × 10⁵ km/s, light covers distance of 3 × 10⁵ km in 1 second\n∴ Distance covered by light in 1 day = 3 × 10⁵ × (60 × 60 × 24) km\n∴ Distance covered by light in 1 year = 3 × 10⁵ × (60 × 60 × 24) × 365\n               = 94608000 × 10⁵ km\n               = 9.4608 × 10¹² km ≈ 9.5 × 10¹² km\nThus, 1 light year ≈ 9.5 × 10¹² km','',0,0,0,NULL),(17090,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the evolution of star finally stop?','','','i) When all the possible fuels at the centre of a star are exhausted, energy generation stops and temperature decreases.\nii) This causes gas pressure to decrease disturbing the balance between gas pressure and gravitational force of star.\niii) The star contracts under the influence of its gravitational force and its density increases.\niv) When density becomes very high, pressure, independent of temperature of gas is generated.\nv) This gas pressure remains constant even after energy generation stops completely and temperature of gas goes on decreasing.\nvi) At this stage, the stability of star can remain intact forever and thus is considered as end stage of a star.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17091,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will happens in case of a star : a) When its gravitational force equal pressure due to gases?            b) When its gravitational force is more than pressure due to gases?           c) When its gravitational force is lesser than pressure due to gases?\n','','','a) The star will be stable. \nb) The star will undergo contraction. \nc) The star will undergo expansion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17092,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the life cycle of a star having mass twice as that of the Sun.','','','i) Stars having initial mass less than 8 times mass of the Sun evolve to undergo huge expansion. \nii) Their radius increases by a factor of 100 to 200. They become large in size and look reddish due to their lower temperature. Hence, they are called red giant stars. \niii) At the end of its evolution, red giant stars explode and their outer gas envelope is thrown out. Inner part of stars contract reducing it to a size similar to that of the earth. \niv) As mass of the star is much higher than that of the earth while its size is similar to that of the earth, density of the star becomes very high. \nv) At this stage, pressure due to electrons in the star becomes independent of temperature and balances its gravitational force. \nvi) In this stage, star looks white and due to its small size, it is called as white dwarf. \nvii) After this its temperature keeps decreasing but its size and mass no longer change. Hence, white dwarf is the end stage of stars having initial mass less than 8 times mass of the Sun.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17093,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The figure below shows forces acting on a star : a) What does an outward arrow indicate?             b) What does an inward arrow indicate?              c) What will happen if quantity represented by outward arrow is more than quantity represented by inward arrow?\n','','','a) Outward arrow indicates pressure of hot gas in the star.\nb) Inward arrow indicates gravitational force between the gas particles of the star.\nc) When pressure of hot gas in star is more than its gravitational force, star will expand.','',1,0,0,NULL),(17094,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why was the name black hole given?','','','i) When supergiant stars undergo supernova explosion, their size becomes smaller and gravitational force increases tremendously.\nii) The pull of gravitational force is so strong that any light falling on these stars cannot escape out.\niii) Hence, light is not reflected from these stars but is absorbed inside the stars.\niv) As a result, star is not seen at all. Instead, probably a minute black hole is observed at its place. Hence, the name of black hole was given to this stage of the star. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17095,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when star contracts and its density increases?','','','i) When energy generation stops inside the star, the temperature decreases causing the gas pressure to decrease. \nii) The star contracts and its density increases.\niii) When the density becomes high some new type of pressure are generated which do not depend on the temperature of gas.\niv) In such cases, gas pressure remains constant even after the energy generation stops completely and temperature of the gas goes on decreasing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17096,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Telescope       ii) Interstellar clouds','','','i) It is a device to observe astronomical objects more effectively. It is placed on the surface of the earth, while some are kept aboard manmade satellites.\nii) Huge clouds of gas and dust are present in the empty spaces between stars in a galaxy, these are called interstellar clouds.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(17098,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Evolution of a star','','','A change in the properties with time resulting in its passing through different shapes of the Sun is the Evolution of a star.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17099,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Red giant stars','','','Stars in the group having initial mass less than 8 times the mass of the sun undergo huge expansion and their radius increases. These are called as red giant stars.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17100,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Supernova Explosion ','','','The huge and powerful explosion that occurs at the end of supergiant stage and which gives off so much energy that the star can be seen during the day in the sky, is called as supernova explosion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17101,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Why do stars evolve?','','','The reason for the evolution of stars is the burning of fuel and therefore the decrease in the amount of fuel in their centre.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17102,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the source of nuclear energy in our sun?','','','Fusion reaction of hydrogen nuclei to form helium nuclei is the source of energy in our sun.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17103,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the sun an ordinary star in the universe?','','','There are innumerable stars having higher or lower mass, size and temperature than that of sun. Therefore, sun is an ordinary star in the universe.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17104,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Properties of stars','','','i) At night we see only 4000 stars with our naked eyes.\nii) Sun is an ordinary star among them. It appears to be larger than all other stars in the sky because it is the only star nearest to us.\niii) There are billions of stars which have higher or lower mass size and temperature than those of the Sun.\niv) Stars are gigantic spheres of hot gas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17106,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Stability of stars','','','i) The properties of the Sun remained unchanged for the last 4.5 billion years because of the gravitational force.\nii) The gravitational force between the gas particles of the star which keeps these particles together and the pressure of the hot gas which disperses the gas are balanced and the star remains stable.\niii) The gravitational force is acting inwards, towards the centre of the star and the gas pressure act outwards.','',0,0,1,NULL),(17107,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are different types of galaxies? On what basis these types are classified?\n','','','There are three different types of galaxies, viz., spiral, elliptical and irregular. These types are classified on the basis of their structure and shape.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17108,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can scientists conclude that properties of the Sun have remained unchanged since its birth?\n','','','i) If the properties of the Sun had changed in its life time, it would have caused changes in the properties of the earth and in the life on the earth. But detailed studies carried out by scientists\nii) about properties of earth and the life on the earth exhibit no such changes.\nAs a result, scientists concluded that properties of the Sun have remained unchanged since its birth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17109,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Why doesn’t the hot gas in the stars disperse in space?','','','The hot gas in stars does not disperse in space due to gravitational force acting between the gas particles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17110,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Why have the properties of the Sun remained unchanged over the last 4.5 billion years?\n','','','In case of the Sun, gravitational force between gas particles balances the pressure of hot gas. As a result, the Sun is stable. Hence, its properties have remained unchanged over the last 4.5 billion years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17111,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : White dwarf and neutron star.','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(17112,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : White dwarf and Black hole','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(17113,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(17114,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(17115,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State any four properties of the Sun.','','','i) Sun is made up of hydrogen (72%), helium (26%) and elements heavier than helium (2%). \nii) Mass of the Sun is 2 × 10³⁰ kg which is about 3.3 lakh times that of the earth. \niii) Radius of the Sun is 695700 km which is about 100 times that of the earth. \niv) Surface temperature of the Sun is 5800 K and temperature at its centre is 1.5 × 10⁷ K. \nv) Age of the Sun is 4.5 × 10⁹ years. \nvi) The properties of the Sun have remained unchanged since its birth and will slowly change in future after 4.5 billion years. Around this time the Sun will turn into a red giant eventually ending into a white dwarf.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17143,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Which effects of heat do you know?','','','When we heat an object then it has following effects :\ni) It becomes hot, there is rise in temperature.\nii) Expansion and contraction.\niii) Change of state.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17144,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Which sources do we get heat from?','','','The sun, the earth, air, burning fuels and electricity are the sources of heat energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17145,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','How is heat transferred?','','','Heat is transferred from one place to another by conduction, convection and radiation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17148,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by expansion co-efficient of solid? What is its unit?','','','Expansion co-efficient is the change in length of a rod of unit length when its temperature is increased by 1 degree. Unit of expansion constant is the inverse of the unit of temperature, i.e. 1/⁰C.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17149,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Celsius, Calorie, Fahrenheit, Kelvin','','','Calories. It is a unit of heat while the rest are units of temperature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17150,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Expansion, contraction, conduction, change of state','','','Conduction. It is a mode of transmission of heat while the rest are effects of heat.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17151,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Sun, Earth, Air, Moon light.','','','Moon light. It is not a source of heat, while the rest are sources of heat.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17152,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Geysers, hot springs, volcano, waterfall','','','Waterfall. All others reservoirs of geothermal energy while waterfall is not a reservoir of geothermal energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17153,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Clinical thermometer, maximum-minimum thermometer, digital thermometer, laboratory thermometer.\n','','','Digital thermometer. Digital thermometer does not use the property of expansion of liquid due to heat but it uses a sensor which measures heat coming out from the body. While, all other thermometers work on the property of expansion of liquid.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17154,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd one out : Water, alcohol, mercury, chloroform','','','Water. Water shows anomalous behaviour on heating while, other liquids simply expand on heating and do not show anomalous behaviour.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17155,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Heat content : total kinetic energy of atoms : : Temperature : ----- \n','','','Average kinetic energy of atoms','',0,0,0,NULL),(17156,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy :  Clinical thermometer : 35⁰C to 42⁰C: : Laboratory thermometer : ------\n','','','40⁰C to 110⁰C','',0,0,0,NULL),(17157,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy :  Heat : Joule : : Specific heat : -----','','','J/kg⁰C','',0,0,0,NULL),(17158,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Thermometer : Temperature : : calorimeter : -----','','','Heat content ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17159,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Liquids : Volumetric expansion coefficient : : Gases : ------  \n','','','Constant pressure expansion coefficient','',0,0,0,NULL),(17160,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Sun : Nuclear fusion : : Radioactive elements : ----','','','Nuclear fission','',0,0,0,NULL),(17161,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Electricity : Electrical energy : : Fuels : -----','','','Chemical energy','',0,0,0,NULL),(17162,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the analogy : Joule : ---- : : Kelvin : Temperature ','','','Heat','',0,0,0,NULL),(17166,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The temperature of a substance is measured in Joules.\n','','','False. The temperature of the substance is measured in ⁰C.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17167,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Heat flows from an object at higher temperature to an object at lower temperature.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(17168,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Joule is the unit of heat.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(17169,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Objects contract on heating.\n','','','False. Objects expands on heating.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17170,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Atoms of a solid are free.\n','','','False. Atoms of a solid are not free, they are lightly packed to each other.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17171,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The average kinetic energy of atoms in a hot object is less than the average kinetic energy of atoms in a cold object.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(17172,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : When heat energy is supplied to a substance its intermolecular space decreases. \n','','','False. When heat energy is supplied to a substance its intermolecular spaces increases.\n  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(17173,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : The more than temperature of a hot body, the more slowly the heat flows from it to the cold body.\n','','','False. The more the temperature of a hot body the more rapidly the heat flows from it into the cord body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17174,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Solids expand more than liquids and gases.\n','','','False. Solid expands less than liquid and gases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17175,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : Calorimeter is used to measure the heat content of an object\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(17176,138,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is True or False. Correct the false statement : In expansion of gas, volume can increase but pressure cannot. \n','','','False. In expansion of gases, volume cannot increase, but pressure can increase.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17177,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Heat           ii) Temperature','','','i) Heat is a form of energy which flows from an object at high temperature to an object at low temperature. In SI system, unit of heat is Joule while CGS unit is calorie.\nii) The degree of hotness or coldness is called temperature. It is measured in degree Celsius (°C), Fahrenheit (°F) and Kelvin (K).\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(17179,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) One calorie           ii) Specific heat ','','','i) One calorie is defined as the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of 1 gm of water through 1°C.\nii) The specific heat of an object is defined as the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of unit mass of that substance through one degree. S.I. unit of specific heat is Joule/ (kg °C) and CGS is Cal / (gm °C).','',0,0,0,NULL),(17181,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Thermometer         ii) Calorimeter ','','','i) Thermometer is a device which is used to measure the temperature of a body or environment.\nii) Calorimeter is a device used to measure the heat content of an object.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(17183,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Linear expansion         ii) Areal expansion of solids','','','i) The linear expansion of a solid is the increase in length of a wire or a rod of a solid due to increase in its temperature.\nii) The area of a sheet of a solid material increases on heating. This is called the areal expansion of solids.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(17185,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Volumetric expansion of solids','','','A three dimensional piece of solid expands on all sides when heated and its volume increases. This is called the volumetric expansion of a solid.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17186,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What must be the temperature in Fahrenheit so that it will be twice its value in Celsius? \n','','','Let the temperature in Celsius = x\n∴ Temperature in Fahrenheit = 2x\n∴ (F – 32)/9   = C/5\n∴ (2x – 32)/9 = x/5     ⟹  5(2x – 32) = 9x \n∴  x = 160 \n∴ Temperature in Fahrenheit 2x = 2 × 160 = 320⁰F','',0,0,0,NULL),(17187,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','When a substance having mass 3 kg receives 600 cal. of heat, its temperature increases by 10⁰C. What is the specific heat of the substance?  \n','','','Mass of substance = 3 kg (m) = 3000 g\nAmount of heat received by the substance (Q) = 600 cal\nTemperature increase = ΔT = 10⁰C\nSpecific heat of the substance = c = ?\nQ = m × c × ΔT         ⟹   600 = 3000 × c × 10\nc = 600/(3000 × 10) = 0.02 cal','',0,0,0,NULL),(17188,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Clinical thermometer and Laboratory thermometer','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(17189,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Heat and Temperature ','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(17190,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Linear expansion and Areal expansion','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(17191,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the similarities between laboratory thermometer and the clinical thermometer? \n','','','i) Construction and working principle are same.\nii) Both are used to measure temperature.\niii) Both have thick walled glass tube enclosing a fine uniform bore capillary tube.\niv) Mercury is used in both thermometers to measure temperature.\nv) Both have Celsius and Fahrenheit markings  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17192,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the relation between the three units of temperature?','','','The relation between the three units of temperature is \n(F - 32)/9 = C/5 and K = C + 273.15 \nWhere C = °Celsius, F = °Fahrenheit, K = °Kelvin','',0,0,0,NULL),(17193,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Derive the formula for coefficient of linear expansion.','','','When a rod of length l₁ is heated from temperature T₁ to temperature T₂ this shows the expansion of length.\nIf the length after expansion is l₂ then, change in length α (original length) × (Change in temperature) \n∴ l₂ - l₁ α l₁ × ΔT\n∴ l₂ - l₁ = λ (l₁ × ΔT)      ⟹  l₂ = l₁ (1 + λΔT) --------- (i)\nHere, λ = constant of proportionality, coefficient of linear expansion. This coefficient of linear expansion is different for different materials. From (i) we get, \nλ = (l₂ - l₁)/l₁ΔT','',0,0,0,NULL),(17194,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Mercury is chosen as thermometric liquid.','','','i) Mercury being liquid metal is a good conductor of heat. It has uniform rate of thermal expansion.\nii) It does not wet the glass. Its appearance is like shining silver, hence it can be seen easily through glass tube.\niii) It quickly measures the temperature of an object absorbing very less amount of heat.\niv) It can measure good range of temperature because it has high boiling point (357 ⁰C) and low melting point (- 39⁰C)\nv) Hence, mercury is chosen as thermometric liquid.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17195,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : We feel when we rub our palms and touch our cheeks.\n','','','i) While rubbing the hands due to friction between them heat energy is produced.\nii) In this case mechanical energy is converted into heat energy.\niii) Hence, we feel warm when we rub our palms and touch our cheeks.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17196,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : A thick glass tumbler cracks when hot water is poured in it.\n','','','i) Substances expand on heating. When we pour hot water to glass tumbler, heat is transferred from hot water to glass tumbler, then there is unequal expansion of inner and outer surface of glass.\nii) Due to this uneven expansion a strain builds up in the glass. It is this strain which cracks the glass.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(17197,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : The diameter of an iron tyre is kept slightly smaller than the wooden wheel on which this tyre is to be mounted.\n','','','The diameter of an iron tyre is kept slightly smaller than the wooden wheels, because on heating it expands and fits, over the wooden wheels.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17198,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : The sun heats up earth, but the air in the atmosphere does not get heated up.','','','Heat is transferred from the sun to earth without heating air in between. This process of transfer of heat is called radiation where the medium in which the heat travels does not play any role.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17199,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain why rails have gaps at specific distance.','','','i) All metals expand when heated.\nii) If two railway tracks are laid together without any gap between them they will push against each other when they expand summer time because of the heat of the sun.\niii) This situation will result in the derailment of the trains causing major accidents and loss of lives.\niv) This gas is kept to accommodate the change in the length of the rails which change with temperature. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17202,138,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Some specific temperatures are given in the three scales in the following table. Verify that they satisfy the above relations :\n','','','a) Boiling point of water = 100⁰C, 212⁰F and 373.5 K\n(F – 32)/9 = C/5 (relation between F and C)\nLHS = (F – 32)/9 = (212 – 32)/9 = 20\nRHS = C/5 = 100/5 = 20\nLHS = RHS \nK = C + 273.15 \nLHS = 373.15\nLHS = 100 + 273.15 = 373.15  = RHS\nb) Freezing point of water = 0⁰C, 32⁰F and 273 K\n(F – 32)/9 = C/5 \nLHS = (F – 32)/9 = (32 – 32)/9 = 0\nRHS = C/5 = 0/5 = 0\nLHS = RHS\nK = C + 273.15 \nLHS = 273\nRHS = 0 + 273.15 = 273.15\nLHS ≠ RHS\nc) Room temperature = 23⁰C, 72⁰F and 296K\n(F – 32)/9 = C/5 \nLHS = (F – 32)/9 = (72 – 32)/9 = 40/9 = 4.44 = 4.4\nRHS = C/5 = 23/5 = 4.6 \nLHS ≠ RHS \nK = C + 273.15 \nLHS = K = 296\nRHS = C + 273.15 = 23 + 273.15 = 296.15\nLHS ≠ RHS\nThe values do not satisfy the relations between K, C and F.','',1,0,0,NULL),(17203,138,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the different sources of heat.','','','The different sources of heat are:\ni) Sun: The Sun is the biggest source of heat received by the earth. A large amount of heat is generated due to the nuclear fusion taking place in its centre. In this process hydrogen nuclei fuse together to form helium nuclei generating heat in the process. Some of it reaches the earth in the form of light and heat.\nii) Earth: As the temperature at the centre of the earth is high, the earth is also a source of heat. This heat is called geothermal energy.\niii) Chemical Energy: When fuels like wood, coal, petrol etc. burn, there is chemical reaction between the fuel and oxygen. Heat is generated in these reactions.\niv) Electrical Energy: Heat is produced through electrical gadgets like electric press, electric heater etc. thus electricity is a source of heat.\nv) Atomic Energy: A huge amount of heat is produced in a very short time when the nuclei of some elements like uranium, thorium etc, undergo fission. This is used in atomic energy projects.\nvi) Air: A large amount of heat is present in the air around us.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17204,138,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Describe a clinical thermometer. How does it differ from the thermometer used in laboratory? \n','','','i) Thermometer that is used to measure the body temperature or when someone has fever is called clinical thermometer.\nii) As the body temperature of a healthy person is 37⁰C clinical thermometers are designed to measure temperature between 35⁰C and 42⁰C or from 94⁰F to 108⁰F\niii) The earlier clinical thermometer were mercury based consisting of a small bulb filled with mercury and connected to a thin glass neck.\niv) As the temperature rises the mercury expands causing it to be forced up the neck.\nv) Due to toxicity of mercury, it has been replaced by alcohol.\nvi) The laboratory thermometer differs in the range of temperature with clinical thermometer\nvii) In laboratory thermometer range is 40⁰C to 110⁰C or even large. Generally it scaled from - 10⁰C to 110⁰C  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17205,138,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the construction of a calorimeter. Draw the necessary figure.','','','Answer : ','A calorimeter is a device used to measure the heat content of an object. By using calorimeter, we can measure the heat produced or absorbed in a physical or chemical process.\na) Construction:\ni) A calorimeter has two vessels, an inner and an outer one. No heat can be transferred from the inner to the outer vessel or from the outer to the inner vessel, as in a thermo flask.\nii) The inner vessel is thermally isolated from the surroundings.\niii) Inner vessel is made of copper.\niv) A thermometer for measuring the temperature and a stirrer for stirring the liquid in the calorimeter are fitted in it.\nb) Working:\ni) Water at a fixed temperature is placed inside the calorimeter. This means that the temperature Measurement and Effects of Heat of the inner vessel and that of the water in it are the same.\nii)When a hot object is placed in water, heat is exchanged between the hot object, water and the calorimeter and all three reach the same temperature.\niii) The total heat lost by the hot object is equal to the total heat absorbed by the calorimeter and water inside it.\niv) Similarly, if we put a cold object in the calorimeter the cold object will receive heat from the water and its temperature will increase while water and calorimeter will lose heat and their temperature will decrease. ',0,0,1,NULL),(17206,138,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain with the help of formulae the expansion coefficients of liquid and gas.\n','','','i) Expansion of liquids: A liquid does not have a definite shape but it has a definite volume. The volumetric expansion coefficient for a liquid is as follows. V₂ = V₁ (1 + βΔT)\nii) Here ΔT is the change in temperature and V₁ and V, are the initial and final volumes of the liquid. β is the volumetric expansion coefficient of the liquid.\niii) The effect of heat on water is somewhat different from that for other liquids. This is called anomalous behavior of water.\niv) Expansion of gases: A gas does not have a fixed volume. Gas expands on heating but if the gas is kept in a closed box, its volume cannot increase but its pressure increases.\nv) The expansion of gas is measured by keeping its pressure constant. It is given by the formula. V₂ = V₁(1 + βΔT)\nvi) ΔT is the change in temperature and V₁ and V₂ are two initial and final volumes of the gas at constant pressure. beta is the constant pressure expansion coefficient of the gas. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17207,138,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the effect of temperature on atoms of gases and solids? Explain with the help of diagrams.\n','','','Answer : ','Substances are made of atoms. The temperature of substance is related to the average kinetic energy.\na) It shows the velocity of atoms in a gas at higher temperature.\nb) It shows the velocity of atoms in a gas at lower/ cooler temperature.\ni) The direction and the length of the arrows show the direction and magnitude of the velocity of atoms.\nii) The velocity of atoms in the gas at higher temperature in larger than the velocity of atoms in the gas at lower temperature.\nc) It shows the velocities of atoms in a solid object.\ni) The atoms in a solid object are tied to one another by the forces acting upon them.\nii) They cannot be displaced from their positions.\niii) On heating, they oscillate around the fixed position.\niv) Higher the temperature of the solid, faster will be the velocity oscillate.',0,0,1,NULL),(17208,138,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain Expansion of solids.\n','','','i) When heat is given to any substance, its temperature increases and it expands. Its expansion depends on the increase in its temperature.\nii) Expansion of solids:\na) Linear Expansion: The linear expansion of a solid is the increase in length of a wire or a rod of a solid due to increase in its temperature. When we increase the temperature of a rod of length l₁ from T₁ to T₂ its length becomes l₂ The change in length is proportional to the original length and the increase in temperature (ΔT = T₂ - T₁)\n∴ Change in the length α original length x Change in temperature \n∴ l₂ - l₁ α l₁ × ΔT\n∴ l₂ - l₁ = λ × l₁ × ΔT\n∴ l₂ = l₁ (1 + λΔT)\nHere, λ is the constant of proportionality and it is called the coefficient of linear expansion of the solid substance.\nb) Areal Expansion of solids: The area of a sheet of a solid material also increases on heating. This is called areal expansion of solids. This is given by the formula: \nA₂ = A₁(l + σΔT)\n Here, ΔT is the change in the temperature and A₁ and A₂ are the initial and the final areas of the sheet, σ is the coefficient of areal expansion of the solid.\nc) Volumetric expansion of solids: A three dimensional piece of solid expands on all sides when heated and its volume increases this is called the volumetric expansion of solids. This is given by the formula: V₂ = V₁(1 + βΔT) \nHere, ΔT is the change in the temperature and V₁ and V₂ are the initial and final volumes of the solid. ẞ is the volumetric expansion coefficient of the solid.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17210,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Two substances A and B have specific heats c and 2c respectively. if A and B are given Q and 4Q amounts of heat respectively. the change in their temperature in the same. If the mass of A is m, what is the mass of B?\n','','','Given : For substance A : Specific heat = c, Amount of heat = Q, Mass = m\nChange in temperature = t\nFor substance B : Specific heat = 2c, Amount of heat = 4Q, temperature = t\nTo find : mass of substance B\nQ = m × c × (Tf – Ti)\nFor substance A : Q = m × c × t --------- (i)\nFor substance B [let the mass of substance B be x]\n4Q = x × 2c × t ----------- (ii)\n∴     Divide (i) by (ii), \n∴ Q/4Q = mct/x × 2c × t \n∴ 1/4 = m/2x     \n∴ x = 2m','',0,0,0,NULL),(17211,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A bridge is made from 20 m long iron rods. At temperature 18⁰C, the distance between two rods is 0.4 cm. Up to what temperature will the bridge be in good shape?\n','','','Length of the rod = 20 m (l₁)\nLength of the rod after expansion = 20.004 m(l₂)\nInitial temperature = 18⁰C (T₁) , Final temperature = T₂ \nT₂ - T₁ = ΔT\nCoefficient of Linear expansion of Iron = 11.5 × 10⁻⁶ l/⁰C\nl₂ = l₁ (1 + λΔT)\n20.004 = 20[1 + (11.5 × 10⁻⁶)ΔT]\n1 + 11.5 × 10⁻⁶ ΔT = 20.004/20\n1 +11.5 × 10⁻⁶ ΔT = 1.0002\nΔT = 0.0002/(11.5 × 10⁻⁶)\n      = T₂ - T₁ = 17.4\n      = T₂ - 18 = 17.4 \nΔT = T₂ = 17.4 + 18 = 35.4 \nThe bridge will be in good shape upto the temperature 35.4⁰C.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17212,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','At 15⁰C the height of Eifel tower is 32.4 m. If it is made of iron, what will be the increase in length in cm at 30⁰C?\n','','','Height of Eifel tower = 32.4 m(l₁)\nHeight of Eifel tower after expansion = (l₂)\nInitial temperature = 15⁰C (T₁) , Final temperature = 30⁰C(T₂)\nLinear expansion of iron = 11.5 × 10⁻⁶ l/⁰C (λ)\nl₂ = l₁(1 + λΔT) = 324 × [1 + 11.5 × 10⁻⁶ (30 – 15)]\n    = 324 × (1 + 0.0001725) = 324 × 1.0001725 = 324.056\nIncrease in length = l₂ - l₁ = 324.056 – 32.4 = 0.056 m = 5.6 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(17213,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Some specific temperatures are given in the three scales in the following table. Verify that they satisfy the above relations and fill appropriate numbers in the blanks : \n\n','','','a) Boiling point of mercury = 356.7⁰C, find in ⁰F and ⁰C\ni) (F – 32)/9  = C/5 \nF – 32 = [9(356.7)]/5 = 642.06\n⁰F = 642.06 + 32 = 674.06 \nK = C + 273.15 = 356.7 + 273.15 = 629.85\nb) (F – 32)/9 = C/5 \nF – 32 = 9(-38.8)/5 = -69.84\nK = C + 273.15 = -38.8 + 273.15 = 234.35','',1,0,0,NULL),(17214,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the following thermometer ','','','a) A thermometer             b) Clinical thermometer \nc) Digital thermometer         d) Maximum minimum thermometer\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(17215,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The density of a gas decreases on heating. Which of the pictures given below makes use of this.\n','','','i) The hot air balloon makes use of the fact that the density of a gas decreases on heating.\nii) As the density decreases, the volume increases and this inflates the balloon and it rises higher and higher.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(17216,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the figure and find out the answers : \na) Using the formula density = mass/volume. Explain what will be the effect of heat on the gas kept in a closed bottle.          \nb) If the bottle is not closed but has a movable piston attached to its open end, what will be the effects of heating the gas in the bottle?\n','','','a) i) Gases do not have a fixed volume.\nii) Gases expand on heating but if the gas is kept in a closed box, its volume cannot increase but its pressure increases.\nb) i) As the bottle is fitted with a movable piston the volume can changes, it does not remain constant.\nii) When the gas is heated, it expands in all direction and pushes the piston upwards.\niii) As more volume is available the pressure remains the same.','',1,0,0,NULL),(17217,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Nishigandha kept a vessel containing all the ingredient for making tea in a solar cooker. Shivani kept a similar vessel on a stove whose tea will be ready first and why?\n','','','i) The solar cooker focuses the available sunlight at a certain point. The vessel kept at that point for cooking.\nii) It warms up slowly as the amount of sunlight (heat) reaching the earth is less. It consumes lot of time.\niii) Whereas, the stove uses fuels which burn instantaneously and provide large amount of heat.\niv) Therefore, Shivani\'s tea will be ready first.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17218,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why does your mother put folded cloth strips soaked in cold water on your forehead when you have high fever?\n','','','i) When we have fever, the temperature of the body is higher than 37 °C.\nii) It is advised to put folded cloth strips soaked in cold water on our forehead to reduce the body temperature.\niii) As the water evaporates from the cloth, it absorbs necessary heat from our body.\niv) This lowers our body temperature.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17219,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is the calorimeter made of copper?','','','i) Copper is a good conductor of heat.\nii) Copper has low specific heat capacity which helps in absorbing small amount of heat also.\niii) A very thin sheet of copper can be made because of its high malleability.\niv) This helps in decreasing the heat capacity of the container.\nv) Lower heat capacity ensures that calorimeter absorbs or liberates even a small amount of heat to reach the final temperature.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(17222,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the table for specific heat capacity of the following : i) gold 		ii) Mercury 	iii) Water 	             iv) Alcohol \n','','','i) gold – 0.03 cal/g⁰C                 ii) Mecury – 0.03 cal/g⁰C\niii) Water – 1.0 cal/g⁰C              iv) Alcohol – 0.5 cal/g⁰C\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(17223,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Take three similar vessels. Let us call them ‘A’, ‘B’ and ‘C’. Fill A with hot water and B with cold water. Put some water from A and B in C. dip your right hand in A and left hand in B, and keep them immersed for 2 to 3 minutes. Now dip both the hands in C.\n','','','a) When both the hands are dipped in vessel C, the right hand finds the water to be cold while at the same time the left hand finds the water to be hot.\nb) i) When the right hand is dipped in hot water, heat is transferred from water to hand which increases the heat content of the hand.\nii) Similarly, when left hand is dipped in cold water, heat is transferred from hand to water. Hence, the heat content of the left hand decreases.\niii) When both the hands are dipped in water vessel C, the heat content of right hand is more, and the heat is lost in water vessel C which makes the right hand feel the water to be cold.  \niv) The heat content of left hand is less. So it gains heat from the water in the vessel and so the left hand finds the water to be hot.\nv) This all happens as heat flows from a body at higher temperature to a body at lower temperature. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(17224,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Take two steel vessels A and B of the same size. Fill some water in A and double that amount in B. Make sure that the water in both vessels are at the same temperature. Raise the temperatures of water in both vessels by 10⁰C using a spirit lamp. Did it take the same time to increase the temperature in the two vessels? \n','','','No, the time taken to increase the temperature of water in vessel B is more than that by vessel A.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17226,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much heat will be needed to raise the temperature of 1.5 kg of water from 15⁰C to 45⁰C. Give the answer in calorie as well as Joule. \n','','','Given : mass of water = 1.5 kg = 1500 gm\n         Change in temp. = 45 – 15 = 30⁰C\nTo find : Heat required for temperature change of water.\nAmount of heat (Cal) = mass of water (gm) × change in temp.\n                    = 1500 × 30 = 45000 cal\n                    = 45000 × 4.18 J = 188100 J\nAmount of heat needed to raise temperature of water will be 45000 cal or 188100 J.','',0,0,0,NULL),(17227,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on specific heat of a substance.','','','Specific heat:\ni) The specific heat of a substance is the amount of heat required to increase the temperature of unit mass of the substance through one degree.\nii) It is denoted by letter c.\niii) The S.I. unit of specific heat is J/kg⁰C and C.G.S. unit is cal/g⁰C.\niv) The amount of heat (Q) required to raise the temperature of object of mass m and specific heat c from initial temperature Ti to temperature Tf is given by, Q = m × c × (Tf - Ti)\nv) Examples: Specific heat of water is 1cal /g⁰C while that of gold is 0.03cal /g⁰C','',0,0,0,NULL),(17228,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the following figure and answer the questions : a) Which effect of heat does the above instrument work on?             b) Considering the reading shown on the instrument in ⁰F, determine its value in ⁰C using the relation between Fahrenheit and Celsius scale.\n','','','a) The figure shows a thermometer which works on the expansion of liquids due to heat.\nb) Given : Temperature in Fahrenheit (F) = 100⁰F\nTo find : Temperature in Celsius (C)\n                  (F – 32)/9 = C/5 \n                (100 – 32)/9   = C/5\n           ∴  C = (68 × 5)/9 = 37.78⁰C\n           The value of the reading in ⁰C is 37.78⁰C.','',1,0,0,NULL),(17229,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','20 g of water given 2090 J of heat. Determine the change in the temperature of water.\n','','','Given : Mass of water (m) = 20 g, amount of heat given to water = 2090 J\nTo find : Change in temperature (ΔT)\n                Amount of heat (J) = 4.18 × amount of heat (cal)\n               Amount of heat (cal) = Amount of heat (J)/4.18\n                             = 2090/4.18 = 500 cal\n               From formula, 500 = 20 × ΔT\n          ∴ ΔT = 500/20 = 25⁰C','',0,0,0,NULL),(17230,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the boiling point of oxygen is considered to be 90.15 K, what will be its value if Fahrenheit? \n','','','Given : Boiling point of oxygen in Kelvin (K₀) = 90.15 K\nTo find : Boiling point of oxygen in Fahrenheit (F₀)\nFormula : i) (F – 32)/9  = C/5 \n                  ii) K = C + 273.15\nCalculation : From formula (ii), C = K – 273.15 \nSubstituting in formula (i), ','',0,0,1,NULL),(17231,138,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','Given : Melting point of iron in Kelvin (K) = 1811.15 K\nTo find : Melting point of iron in ⁰C, and ⁰F\nFormulae : i) K = C + 273.15\n                    ii) (F – 32)/9 = C/5 \nCalculation : From formula (i), \n     C = K – 273.15 = 1811.15 – 273.15  = 1538⁰C\nFrom formula (ii),\n    (F – 32)/9  = 1538/5\n∴  F – 32 = 2768.4 \n∴  F = 2768.4 + 32 = 2800.4⁰F','',1,0,0,NULL),(18646,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The decimal form of 35/16 is -------\na) 2.1875 		b) 3.1875 		c) 2.2875 		d) 2.2275\n','','','a) 2.1875','',0,0,0,NULL),(18647,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','18/5 × (-5)/9 = --------\na) 2 			b) 1 			c) -2 			d) -1\n','','','c) -2 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(18648,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The multiplicative inverse of -6/7 is ------\na) 6/7 		b) -7/6 		c) 7/6 		d) -6/7\n','','','b) -7/6 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(18649,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The group of integers is not sufficient for ------\na) addition 		b) subtraction 		   c) Multiplication 		    d) division\n','','','d) division','',0,0,0,NULL),(18650,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is correct?','','','d) 10/3 = 3.333----','',1,0,0,NULL),(18651,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','0.4 × 0.4 × 0.04 is equal to \na) 6.4 			b) 0.64 		c) 0.064 		d) 0.0064\n','','','d) 0.0064','',0,0,0,NULL),(18652,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a reducible fraction?\na) 105/112 		b) 104/121 		c) 77/72 		d) 46/63\n','','','a) 105/112 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(18653,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following statements is true?','','','c) 9/16  > 13/24','',1,0,0,NULL),(18654,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Reciprocal of','','','d) none of these','',1,0,0,NULL),(18655,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','0.06 = ?\na) 3/5 		b) 3/50 		c) 3/500		d) none of these\n','','','b) 3/50 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(18656,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','1.04= ?','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(18657,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','52/25 = ?\na) 2.8 		b) 2.08 		c) 0.28 		d) 2.008\n','','','b) 2.08','',0,0,0,NULL),(18658,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','1.007 – 0.7 = ?\na) 1 		b) 0.37 		c) 0.307 		d) none of these\n','','','c) 0.307 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(18659,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What should be added to 3.07 to get 3.5?\na) 0.57 		b) 0.34 		c) 0.43 		d) 0.02\n','','','c) 0.43 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(18660,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','0.23 × 0.3 = ?\na) 0.69 		b) 6.9 			c) 0.069 		d) none of these\n','','','c) 0.069 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(18661,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','1.1 × 0.1 × 0.01 = ?\na) 0.011		b) 0.0011 		c) 0.11 		d) none of these\n','','','b) 0.0011','',0,0,0,NULL),(18662,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Reciprocal of','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(18663,154,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 537 × 2 = 1074, then 537 × 0.02 = \na) 0.1074 		b) 1.074 		c) 10.74 		d) 107.4\n','','','c) 10.74','',0,0,0,NULL),(18664,154,'mcq','1a