'Solution : ','Quotient = x² - 2x + 2 ; remainder = -7x + 3',0,0,1,NULL),(25503,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y are in direct proportion and x = 10 when y = 2. Then find the value of x, when y = 4.\na) 16 		b) 20 		c) 24 			d) -20\n','','','b) 20','',0,0,0,NULL),(25504,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y are in inverse proportion such that x = 7 when y = 6 then find y when x = 21\na) 2 		b) 3 		c) 9 		d) -2\n','','','a) 2 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(25505,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is an example of direct proportion?\na) No. of workers and their wages 		                                b) No. of workers and no. of days to complete a work	\nc) No. of students and no. of laddus distributed them       	d) None of these\n','','','a) No. of workers and their wages','',0,0,0,NULL),(25506,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is an example of inverse proportion?\na) No. of workers and their wages          	b) No. of students and no. of notebooks required 		\nc) Speed of vehicle and time taken to cover a distance 		d) none of these\n','','','c) Speed of vehicle and time taken to cover a distance ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25507,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','8 men can do a piece of work in 10 days. How long will 10 men take?\na) 12 days 		b) 8 days 		c) 7 days 		d) 6 days\n','','','b) 8 days ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25508,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rohit bought 12 registers for Rs. 156. Find the cost of 7 registers?\na) Rs. 110		b) Rs. 84 		c) Rs. 91 		d) Rs. 267\n','','','c) ₹ 91','',0,0,0,NULL),(25509,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 14 kg of pulses cost Rs. 441, what is the cost of 22 kg of pulses?\na) Rs. 627 		b) Rs. 649 		c) Rs. 671 		d) Rs. 693\n','','','d) Rs. 693','',0,0,0,NULL),(25510,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 36 men can do a piece of work in 25 days, in how many days will 15 men do it?\na) 50 		b) 56 		c) 60 		d) 72\n','','','c) 60 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(25511,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','6 men can do a piece of work in 8 days. How long will 8 men take?\na) 6 days 		b) 8 days 		c) 7 days 		d) 5 days\n','','','a) 6 days ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25512,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Raju types 270 words in 15 minutes. The number of words he will type in 3 minutes is -----\na) 44 			b) 64 			c) 74 			d) 54\n','','','d) 54','',0,0,0,NULL),(25513,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y vary directly, which of the following is not true?\na) If x increases y also increases 		b) If x decreases y also decreases 	      c) If x decreases y increases 		d) none of these\n','','','c) If x decreases y increases','',0,0,0,NULL),(25514,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','“The water consumed and water bill” is a case of -----\na) direct proportion 		b) inverse proportion 		c) not in proportion 		d) none of these\n','','','a) direct proportion ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25515,276,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','x and y are said to vary directly with each other if for some positive number k, then ------ = k.\na) y/x²		b) x/y 		c) xy 		d) none of these\n','','','b) x/y ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25516,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 10 pens cost 60 rupees, what is the cost of 13 such pens?','','','Given that, cost of 10 pen is ₹ 60\n∴ cost of 1 pen = 60/10 = ₹ 6\n∴ cost of 13 pens = 13 × 6 = ₹ 78 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25517,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the cost of 6 toys is 240, then find the cost of 21 toys.','','','Given that, cost of 6 toys is ₹ 240\n∴ cost of 1 toy = 240/6 = ₹ 40\n∴ Cost of 21 toys = 21 × 40 = ₹ 840 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25518,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the cost of 8 kg sugar is Rs. 280, find the cost of 15 kg of sugar.','','','Given that, cost of 8 kg sugar is ₹ 280\n∴ cost of 1 kg sugar = 280/8 = ₹ 35\n∴ cost of 15 kg sugar = 15 × 35 = ₹ 525','',0,0,0,NULL),(25519,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y varies directly, complete the following tables:','','','x and y varies directly\n∴ 2.5/5 = x/8 \n∴ x × 5 = 2.5 × 8 \n∴ x = 20/5 = 4 ','',1,0,0,NULL),(25520,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y varies directly, complete the following tables:','','','x and y varies directly\n∴ 9/12 = x/48 \n∴ x × 12 = 9 × 48 \n∴ x = 432/12 = 36 ','',1,0,0,NULL),(25521,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y varies inversely, complete the following tables:','','','x and y varies inversely\n∴ 16 × 4 = 32 × y\n∴ y = (16 × 4)/32 = 2','',1,0,0,NULL),(25522,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y varies inversely, complete the following tables:','','','x and y varies inversely\n∴ 9 × 27 = x × 9\n∴ x = (9 × 27)/9 = 27','',1,0,0,NULL),(25523,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 100 students take 20 days to complete the project, then how much time will be taken by 125 students?','','','Given that, \nx             100             125\ny              20               -----\nGiven example is in inverse variation.\nx and y varies inversely\n∴ 100 × 20 = 125 × y\n∴ y = (100 × 20)/125 = 16','',0,0,0,NULL),(25524,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x varies inversely to y and x = 4, when y = 6. The value of y, when x = 3.','','','Given that, \nx            4           3\ny            6           -----\nx and y varies inversely\n∴ 4 × 6 = 3 × y\n∴ y = (6 × 4)/3 = 8','',0,0,0,NULL),(25525,276,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For a group of 32 students food lasts for 45 days. For how many days will the same food last for 72 students?','','','Given that, \nx            32            72\ny            45            -----\nx and y varies inversely\n∴ 45 × 32 = 72 × y\n∴ y = (45 × 32)/72 = 20','',0,0,0,NULL),(25526,276,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If 7 kg onions cost 140 rupees, how much must we pay for 12 kg onions?','','','Given that, \nx            7           12\ny           140       ------\nGiven example is in direct variation\n ∴ 7/140  = 12/y \n∴ y × 7 = 12 × 140 \n∴ y = (12 × 140)/7 = 240\n∴ 12 kg onion price is ₹ 240.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25527,276,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If 600 rupees buys 15 bunches of feed, how many will 1280 rupees buy?','','','Given that, \nx            600           1280\ny            15               ------\nGiven example is in direct variation \n∴ 600/15 = 1280/y \n∴ y × 600 = 1280 × 15\n∴ y = (1280 × 15)/600 = 32\nHence, 32 bunches of feed will be bought by ₹ 1280.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25528,276,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','For 9 cows, 13 kg 500 gm of food supplement are required every day. In the same proportion, how much will be needed for 12 cows?','','','Given that, \nx        9           12\ny       13.50     -----\nGiven example is in direct variation\n∴ 9/13.5  = 12/y \n∴ y × 9 = 12 × 13.5 \n∴ y = (12 × 13.5)/9 = 18\nHence, 12 cows 18 kg of feed is required.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25529,276,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Fifteen workers take 8 hours to build a wall. How many hours will 12 workers need to build the same wall?','','','Given that, \nx   15       12\ny    8        -----\nGiven example is in inverse proportion.\n∴ 15 × 8 = 12 × y \n∴ y = 120/12 = 10\n∴ 12 workers will take 10 hours to build the wall.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25530,276,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','A 40 - page class magazine is to be written. If one student would require 80 days to write it, how many would 4 student require? ','','','Given that,\nx     1          4 \ny      80      -----\nGiven example is in inverse proportion.\n∴ 1 × 80 = 4 × y \n∴ y = 80/4 = 20\n∴ 4 students require 20 days.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25531,276,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Five workers take 12 days to weed a field. How many days would 6 workers take? How many would 15 take?','','','Let 6 workers take x days to weed the field.\nSince number of workers and number of days required to weed the field are in inverse proportion.\n∴ x       5          6          15\n    y      12        ----        -----\ni) 5 × 12 = 6 × y \n    ∴ y = (5 × 12)/6 = 10\n    Hence, 6 workers take 10 days to weed a field\nii) 5 × 12 = 15 × y\n    ∴ y = 60/15 = 4\n     Hence, 15 workers take 4 days to weed a filed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25532,276,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','60 cows can graze in a field for 12 days then how many cows will graze in the same field in 5 days.','','','Given that, \nx    60      ----\ny    12      5 \ngiven example is in inverse proportion.\n∴ 60 × 12 = 5 × x\n∴ x = (60 × 12)/5 = 12 × 12 = 144 \nHence, 144 cows are required to graze the same field in 5 days.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25533,276,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y are in direct variation. Complete the following table:','','','Given that, x and y varies directly proportion.\ni)  x         4               5\n     y       28             ----\n   ∴ 4/28 = 5/y \n   ∴ 4 × y = 5 × 28 \n    ∴ y = (5 × 28)/4 = 5 × 7 = 35\nii)  x      4          ----\n     y      28         56\n    ∴  4/28 = x/56\n    ∴ x × 28 = 4 × 56\n    ∴ x = (4 × 56)/28 = 8 ','',1,0,0,NULL),(25534,276,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table of inverse proportion :','','','Given that, a and b varies inversely proportion.\ni) a     2      ----\n   b     10    10\n    ∴ 2 × 10 = a × 10\n    ∴  a = 2\nii) a      2           20\n    b      10          -----\n   ∴ 2 × 10 = 20 × b\n   ∴ b = 20/20 = 1','',1,0,0,NULL),(25535,276,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The cost of 12 quintals of Soyabeen is 3600 rupees. How much will 8 quintals cost?','','','Let the cost of 8 quintals of soyabean  is ₹ x\nSince the cost of soyabean and its weight is in direct proportion, we get\n3600/12  = x/8 \n ∴ 3000 = x/8\n  ∴ x = 24000\n  ∴ 8 quintals of soyabean costs ₹ 24000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25536,276,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Two mobiles cost 16000 rupees. How much money will be required to buy 13 such mobiles?','','','Let the cost of 13 mobiles is ₹ x\nSince the cost of mobiles and number of mobiles are in direct proportion.\n∴  16000/2  = x/13\n∴ 8000 = x/13\n∴ x = 8000 × 13\n∴ x = 104000\nHence, the cost of 13 mobiles is ₹ 1,04,000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25537,276,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Mohanrao took 10 days to finish a book, reading 40 pages every day. How many pages must he read in a day to finish it in 8 days?','','','Let the Mohanrao reads x pages every day to finish the book in 8 days.\nSince number of pages read per day and number of days required to finish the book are in inverse variation, we get,\n40 × 10 = x × 8\n∴ x = 400/8 \n∴ x = 50\nHence, Mohanrao must read 50 pages per day to finish the book is 8 days.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25538,276,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Mary cycles at 6 km per hour. How long will she take to reach her Aunt’s house which is 12 km away? If she cycles at a speed of 4km/hr, how long would she take?','','','Here speed and times are in inverse proportion.\nHence, Speed × time = distance (constant)\nNow, ','We have, speed × time = 12\ni) 6 × x = 12\n    ∴ x = 2\nii) 4 × y = 12\n    ∴ y = 3\n    ∴ Mary takes 2 hours to reach Aunt’s house at the speed 6 km/hr. and Mary takes 3 hours to reach her Aunts house at the speed 4 km/hr.',0,0,1,NULL),(25540,276,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Jhelum and Athrava invested 2100 and 2800 rupees respectively and started a business. They made a profit of 3500 rupees. How should it be shared?','','','Let us find out the proportion of investments.\n2100 : 2800 = 2100/2800 = 3/4 = 3 : 4\n∴ Proportion of investments is 3 : 4\nThe profit must also be shared in the same proportion.\nLet Jhelum’s profit be 3x and that of Atharva 4x then,\n3x + 4x = 3500\n∴ 7x = 3500\n∴ x = 500\nJhelum’s share = 3x = 3 × 500 = 1500\nAthrava’s share = 4x = 4 × 500 = 2000 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25541,276,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Suresh and Ramesh together invested 144000 rupees in the ratio 4 : 5 and bought a plot of land. After some years they sold it at a profit of 20%. What is the profit each of them got?','','','Here investment = ₹ 144000 and profit = 20%\n∴ Total profit = 144000 × 20/100 = ₹ 28800\nSince the investment of Suresh and Ramesh is in the ratio 4 : 5. The profit will also distributed in the ratio  4 : 5.\nIf Suresh gets 4x and Ramesh gets 5x as profit, then\n4x + 5x = 28800\n∴ 9x = 28800\n∴ x = 3200\nHence, Suresh gets a profit of 4x = 4 × 3200 = ₹ 12800\nAnd Ramesh gets a profit of 5x = 5 × 3200 = ₹ 16000','',0,0,0,NULL),(25542,276,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Virat and Samrat together invested 50000 and 120000 rupees to start a business. They suffered a loss of 20%. How much loss did each of them incur?','','','The ratio of investment of Virat and Samrat is \n      50000/120000 = 5/12 = 5 : 12\nAlso, Total investment = 50,000 + 1,20,000 = 1,70,000\nSince their loss of 20%\nTotal loss = 170000 × 20/100 = 34000\nLet Virat incurred loss of 5x and Samrat incurred loss of 12x then\n5x + 12x = 34000\n∴ 17x = 34000\n∴ x = 2000\n∴ 5x = 5 × 2000 = 10,000 and 12x = 12 × 2000 = 24,000\nHence, Virat incurred loss of ₹ 10,000 and Samrat incurred loss of ₹ 24000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25543,276,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A car covers a distance of 125 km in 24 liter of petrol then how far can it travel in 10 liter petrol? ','','','Given that, \n      x         125     ------\n      y           24        10\n    Here, x and y varies directly \n∴  125/24 = x/10\n∴ x × 24 = 125 × 10\n∴ x = (125 × 10)/24 = 52 km','',0,0,0,NULL),(25544,276,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Chinmaya and Sam invested a total of 130000 rupees in a business in the proportion 3 : 2 respectively. what amount did each of them invest? If their total profit was 36000 rupees, what is the share of each?','','','The proportion of Chinmaya’s and Sam’s investment is 3 : 2.\nThe profit is shared in the same proportion as the investment, hence, proportion of profit is 3 : 2\nLet Chinmaya’s investment be 3y and Sam’s 2y.\n3y + 2y = 130000\n∴ 5y = 130000\n∴ y = 26000\n∴ Chinmaya’s investment = 3y = 3 × 26000 = 78,000\nSam’s investment = 2y = 2 × 26000 = 52000\nLet Chinmaya’s profit be 3x and Sam’s be 2x\n∴ 3x + 2x = 36000\n∴ 5x = 36000\n∴ x = 7200\n∴ Chinmaya’s profit = 3x = 3 × 7200 = 21600\nSam’s profit = 2x = 2 × 7200 = 14400 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25545,276,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Abdul, Sejal and Soham each gave Sayali 30 rupees, 70 rupees and 50 rupees respectively. Sayali put in 150 rupees and bought a paper, colors, etc. together they made greeting cards and sold them all. If they made a total profit of 420 rupees. What was each one’s share in the profit?','','','The capital invested by all four was ₹ 300. Of this Sayali had invested 150 rupees, that is half of the capital. \nThe total profit was 420 rupees. So, Sayali’s profit was half of the capital. i.e. 210 rupees.\nThe remaining 210 was shared by Abdul, Sejal and Soham\nAbdul, Sejal and Soham’s investment is 30, 70 and 50 rupees.\nThe proportion is 30 : 70 : 50. i.e. 3 : 7 : 5\nTheir share of the profit is altogether 210 rupees.\nLet their individual profit be 3k, 7k, 5k.\nThen, 3k + 7k + 5k = 210\n∴ 15k = 210\n∴ k = 14\nAbdul’s profit = 3k = 3 × 14 = 42 rupees\nSejal’s profit = 7k = 7 × 14 = 98 rupees\nSoham’s profit = 5k = 5 × 14 = 70 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25546,276,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Saritaben, Ayesha and Meenakshi started a business by investing 2400, 5200 and 3400 rupees. They made a profit of 50%. How should they share it? If they reinvest all their profit by adding it to the capital, what will each one’s share be in the following year?','','','Total capital = 2400 + 5200 + 3400 = 11000 rupees\nProfit 50%\n∴ Total profit = (11000 × 50)/100 = 5500\nProfit will be shared in the same proportion as the investment.\nWe simplify the ratio of two numbers by dividing by a common factor.\nThe same can be done for 3 or more numbers.\nProportion of shares = 2400 : 5200 : 3400 = 24 : 52 : 34 = 12 : 26 : 17\nAssume that, Saritaben’s profit = 12p, Ayesha’s profit = 26p and Meenakshi’s profit = 17p\n∴ 12p + 26p + 17p = 5500\n∴ 55p = 5500\n∴ p = 100\n∴ Saritaben’s profit = 12 × 100 = 1200\nAyesha’s profit = 26 × 100 = 2600 and \nMeenakshi’s profit = 17 × 100 = 1700\nIf they add their profit to the capital, their further investments will be : \nSaritaben’s capital = 2400 + 1200 = ₹ 3600\nAyesha’s capital = 5200 + 2600 = ₹ 7800\nMeenakshi’s capital = 3400 + 1700 = ₹ 5100','',0,0,0,NULL),(25547,276,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Shweta, Piyush and Nachiket together invested 80000 rupees and started a business of selling sheets and towels from Solapur. Shweta’s share of the capital was 30000 rupees and Piyush’s 12000. At the end of the year they had made a profit of 24%. What was Nachiket’s investment and what was his share of the profit?','','','Let Nachiket investment is ₹ x\n∴ Total investment = 30000 + 12000 + x\n∴ 80,000 = 42000 + x\n∴ x = 38000\nNow, profit is of 24%\n∴ Nachiket profit = 24% of his investment = 38000 × 24/100 = 9120\nHence, Nachiket invested ₹ 38000 and got a profit of ₹ 9120 in the business.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25548,276,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A and B shared a profit of 24500 rupees in the proportion 3 : 7. Each of them gave 2% of his share of the profit to the Soldiers Welfare Fund. What was the actual amount given to the Fund by each of them?','','','Let A got a profit of ₹ 3x and B got a profit ₹ 7x\n∴ Total profit = A + B \n∴ 3x + 7x = 24500\n∴ 10x = 24500\n∴ x = 2450\n∴ 3x = 3 × 2450 = 7350 and 7x = 7 × 2450 = 17150\nThus, profit of A = ₹ 73350 and B = ₹ 17150\nNow each of them gave 2% of amount to the soldier’s welfare fund\n∴ A gave 2% of 73350 = 73350 × 2/100 = ₹ 147\nB gave 2% of ₹ 17150 = 17150 × 2/100 = ₹ 343','',0,0,0,NULL),(25549,276,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Jaya, Seema, Nikhil and Neelesh put in altogether 360000 rupees to form a partnership, with their investments being in the proportion 3 : 4 : 7 : 6. What was Jaya’s actual share in the capital? They made a profit of 12%. How much profit did Nikhil make?','','','Since the investment is in proportion of 3 : 4 : 7 : 6\nLet the investments of Jaya, Seema, Nikhil and Neelesh are 3x, 4x, 7x and 6x respectively.\nTotal capital = 360000\n∴ 3x + 4x + 7x + 6x = 360000\n∴ 20x = 360000\n∴ x = 18000\n∴ Jaya’s investment = 3x = 3 × 18000 = 54000\nNikhil’s investment = 7x = 7 × 18000 = 126000\nNow, profit percent is 12%\n∴ Nikhil’s profit = 126000 × 12/100 = ₹ 15120\nHence, Jaya’s investment is ₹ 54000 and Nikhil’s profit is ₹ 15120.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25550,276,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ram and Shyam together invested Rs. 50000 and 120000 rupees to start a business. They suffered a loss of 20%. Find their individual loss.','','','Loss of Ram be 20% of 50,000\n∴ Loss of Ram = 50,000 × 20/100 = ₹ 10,000\nLoss of Shyam be 20% of 120,000\n∴ Loss of Shyam = 120000 × 20/100 = ₹ 24000','',0,0,0,NULL),(25551,276,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Suresh and Ramesh together invested Rs. 144000 in the ratio 4 : 5 for a business. They have 20% profit. Then find their individual profit.','','','Given that, ratio of investment of Suresh and Ramesh be 4 : 5\n∴ Suresh invested 4x and Ramesh invested 5x respectively.\n∴ 4x + 5x = 144000\n∴ 9x = 144000\n∴ x = 16000\n∴ Suresh invested = 4x = 4 × 16000 = ₹ 64,000\nRamesh invested = 5x = 5 × 16000 = ₹ 80,000\nThey have 20% profit.\n∴ Suresh profit = 64000 × 20/100 = ₹ 12,800\nRamesh profit = 80000 × 20/100 = ₹ 16000. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25728,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mean (X ̅) = ------/N\na) Σfᵢ		b) Σfᵢ xᵢ		c) fᵢ xᵢ			d) xᵢ\n','','','b) Σfᵢ xᵢ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25729,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Chest size of 10 persons in cm are 60, 40, 50, 50, 40, 40, 60, 50, 60, 40. Find the mean chest size = ------ cm\na) 50 			b) 49 			c) 51 			d) 52\n','','','b) 49 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25730,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mean of 1st 5 whole numbers is ------ cm.\na) 1 			b) 2 			c) 3 			d) 4\n','','','b) 2 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(25731,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mean of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7?\na) 3 	        	b) 4 		         c) 5 			d) 6\n','','','b) 4 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(25732,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mean of 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13?\na) 3 		b) 5 		c) 7 			d) 9\n','','','c) 7 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(25733,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mean of the first four prime numbers?\na) 3.5 		b) 7 		c) 5 			d) 4.25\n','','','d) 4.25','',0,0,0,NULL),(25734,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 10 observations is 12, what is their sum?\na) 120			b) 1.2 			c) 12 		d) 60\n','','','a) 120	','',0,0,0,NULL),(25735,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 3, 7, 11 and x is 9, what is the value of x?\na) 9 			b) 11 			c) 13 			d) 15\n','','','d) 15','',0,0,0,NULL),(25736,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The sides of two squares are 2 cm and 5 cm respectively. what is their mean perimeter?\na) 7 cm 		b) 14 cm 		c) 8 cm 		d) 11 cm\n','','','b) 14 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(25737,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mean is also referred as -----\na) average 		b) difference 		c) total 		d) none of these\n','','','a) average 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(25738,279,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Choose the right statement ------\na) Percentage (%) = Data/Total × 100		b) Percentage bar graph can have multiple data		c) both (a) and (b) 		d) none of these\n','','','a) Percentage (%) = Data/Total × 100','',0,0,0,NULL),(25739,279,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of 4, 6, 7, 9 and 4.','','','Mean = (Sum of observation)/Total number of observations\n                = (4 + 6 + 7 + 9 + 4)/5 = 30/5 = 6\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(25740,279,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of first five odd numbers.','','','First five odd numbers are 1, 3, 5, 7, 9\nMean = (1 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 9)/5 = 25/5 = 5\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(25741,279,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 4, x and y is 6, then find the mean of x, y and 10.','','','Given : mean of 4, x and y is 6\n∴ 6 = (4 + x + y)/3\n∴ 4 + x + y = 18\n∴ x + y = 14  ----------- (i)\nMean of x, y and 10 is\nMean = (x + y + 10)/3 = (14 + 10)/3 = 24/3 = 8 -------- [from (i)]','',0,0,0,NULL),(25742,279,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 2, 3, x, 7, 8 is x, then find the value of x.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total number of observations\n∴  x = (2 + 3 + x + 7 + 8)/5\n∴ 5x = 20 + x \n∴ 5x – x = 20\n∴ 4x = 20 \n∴ x = 20/4 = 5 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25743,279,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of ‘n’ observations is 5 and sum of observations is 25, then find n.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total no. of observations\n∴ 5 = 25/n \n∴ 5n = 25\n∴ n = 5  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25744,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of observations 14, 5, 4, 8, 2, 3, x is x, then find the value of x.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total no. of observations\n∴ x = (14 + 5 + 4 + 8 + 2 + 3 + x)/7\n∴ 7x = 36 + x\n∴ 7x – x = 36\n∴ 6x = 36\n∴ x = 5','',0,0,0,NULL),(25745,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of the given data : 8, 2, 3, 4, 1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 4, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9.','','','Mean = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observations\n                = (8 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 1 + 3 + 2 + 4 + 5 + 4 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 6 + 9)/15\n∴ Mean = 60/15 = 4','',0,0,0,NULL),(25746,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The enrolment in a school during six consecutive years was as follows : 1555, 1670, 1750, 2013, 2540, 2820. Find the mean.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total no. of observations\n∴ Mean = (1555 + 1670 + 1750 + 2013 + 2540 + 2820)/6\n               = 12348/6 = 2058','',0,0,0,NULL),(25747,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The heights of 7 players in a group are : 175 cm, 158 cm, 180 cm, 164 cm, 182 cm, 160 cm and 171 cm. Find their mean height.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total no. of observations\n∴ Mean = (175 + 158 + 180 + 164 + 182 + 160 + 171)/7\n               = 1190/7 = 170 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(25748,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Pocket money received by 7 students is given below : 24, 22, 30, 28, 32, 26, 34. Find the mean.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total no. of observations\n∴ Mean = (24 + 22 + 30 + 28 + 32 + 26 + 34)/7\n               = 196/7 = 28','',0,0,0,NULL),(25749,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of the following observations is 39, find the value of x, 29, 48, x, 51, x – 3.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total no. of observations\n∴ 39 = (29 + 48 + x + 51 + x – 3)/5\n∴ 195 = (125 + 2x)\n∴ 2x = 195 – 125 = 70\n∴ x = 70/2 = 35','',0,0,0,NULL),(25750,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of the given data : 40, 50, 65, 70, 75','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total no. of observations\n∴ Mean = (40 + 50 + 65 + 70 + 75)/5\n               = 300/5 = 60','',0,0,0,NULL),(25751,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of the given data : 2, 3, 5, 4, 2, 1, 3, 3, 4, 3.','','','Mean = (2 + 3 + 5 + 4 + 2 + 1 + 3 + 3 + 4 + 3)/10 \n                = 30/10 = 3\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(25752,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The mean of 12, 10, x, 20 and 18 is 17. Find x.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total number of observations\n  ∴ 17 = (12 + 10 + x + 20 + 18)/5\n∴ 17 × 5 = 60 + x\n∴ x = 85 – 60 = 25','',0,0,0,NULL),(25754,279,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw percentage bar graph of the following data.','','','Student can draw the percentage bar graph by using the information.','',1,0,0,NULL),(25755,279,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The number of members in the 40 families in Bihar are as follows : 1, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 7, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 4, 6, 2, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7, 3, 4, 5, 2, 4, 3, 2, 3, 5, 5, 4, 6, 2, 3, 5, 6, 4, 2. Prepare a frequency table and find the mean of members of 40 families.','','','Solution : ','Mean = (Σfᵢ xᵢ)/N = 156/40 = 3.9 ',0,0,1,NULL),(25756,279,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The number of Science and Mathematics projects submitted by Model high school, Nandpur in last 20 years at the state level science exhibition is : 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 3, 1, 5, 4, 2, 3, 1, 3, 5, 4, 3, 2, 2, 3, 2. Prepare a frequency table and find the mean of the data.','','','Solution : ','Mean = (Σfᵢ × xᵢ)/N = 55/20 = 2.75',0,0,1,NULL),(25757,279,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In the following number of trees planted in the year 2016 and 2017 in four towns is given. Show the data with the help of subdivided bar graph.','','','Student can draw the graph by using given information.','',1,0,0,NULL),(25758,279,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Show the following information by a percentage bar graph.','','','Student can draw the graph by using given information.','',1,0,0,NULL),(25759,279,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A survey of 20 schools were done to find out number of students failed in 10th board exam. The number were as follows : 5, 15, 3, 5, 1, 2, 2, 6, 1, 7, 8, 8, 15, 9, 1, 7, 8, 1, 2, 13. Evaluate mean of failed students.','','','Solution : ','Mean = (Σfᵢ × xᵢ)/N = 119/20 = 5.95',0,0,1,NULL),(25760,279,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The data shows marks scored by students of 4 schools A, B, C, D in maths and science. Prepare sub divided bar graph.','','','Student can draw the graph by using given information.','',1,0,0,NULL),(25761,279,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The data shows usage of public and private transport by 4 office employees. Prepare percentage bar graph. Also state who contributed to less pollution.','','','Student can draw the graph by using given information.','',1,0,0,NULL),(25762,279,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The following table shows the electricity (in units) used by 25 families of Eklara village in a month of May. Complete the table and answer the following question.\ni) How many families used 45 units electricity? 		ii) State the score, the frequency of which is 5.		iii) Find N, and Σfᵢxᵢ		iv) Find the mean of electricity used by each family in the month of May.\n','','','Solution : ','i) 2 families \nii) The score is 75\niii) N = 25 ; Σ fᵢ xᵢ = 1425\niv) Mean = (Σfᵢ xᵢ)/N = 1425/25 = 57',1,0,1,NULL),(25763,279,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Observe the following graph and answer the following questions :\ni) State the type of the graph. 		\nii) How much is the saving of Vaishali in the month of April?		\niii) How much is the total saving of Saroj in the months of March and April?		\niv) How much more is the saving of Savita than the total savings of Megha?	\nv) Whose savings in the month of April is the least?\n','','','i) The given graph is subdivided bar graph.\nii) The savings of Vaishali in the month of April is ₹ 600.\niii) The total of savings of Saroj in the months of March and April is ₹ 800.\niv) The total savings by Savita is ₹ 1000 and that by Megha is ₹ 500.\n       ₹ (1000 – 500) = ₹ 500.\nv) The savings of Megha is the least in the month of April.','',1,0,0,NULL),(25764,279,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The number of boys and girls, in std. 5 to 8 in a Z. P. school is given in the table. Draw a subdivided bar graph to show the data. (Scale : On Y axis, 1 cm = 10 students)','','','Student can draw the graph by using given information.','',1,0,0,NULL),(25765,279,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In the following table, data of the transport means used by students in 8th standard for commutation between home and school is given. Draw a subdivided bar graph to show the data. (Scale : On Y axis: 1 cm = 500 students)','','','Student can draw the graph by using given information.','',1,0,0,NULL),(25766,279,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Observe the following graph and answer the questions.\ni) State the type of graph. 		\nii) How much percent is the Tur production to total production in Ajita’s farm?	\niii) Compare the production of Gram in the farms of Yash and Ravi and state whose percentage of production is more and by how much?	    \niv) Whose percentage production of Tur is the least?		\nv) State production percentages of Tur and gram in Sudha’s farm.\n','','','i) It is a percentage bar graph.\nii) The Tur production is 60%\niii) Gram Production : Yash’s farm : 50% ; Ravi’s farm : 30% \n      Gram production in Yash’s farm is more by 20%\niv) The percentage production of Tur is least in Sudha’s farm.\nv) The percentage production of Tur in Sudha’s farm : 40% and the percentage production of gram : 60%.','',1,0,0,NULL),(25767,279,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The following data is collected in a survey of some students of 10th standard from some schools. Draw the percentage bar graph of the data.','','','Student can draw the graph by using given information. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(25768,280,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','If the interest on 1700 rupees is 340 rupees for 2 years. The rate of interest must be -----\na) 12% 		b) 15% 		c) 4% 		d) 10%\n','','','d) 10% ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25769,280,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','If the interest on 3000 rupees is 600 rupees at a certain rate for a certain number of years, what would the interest be on 1500 rupees under the same conditions?\na) Rs. 300	        	b) Rs. 1000		c) Rs. 700       		d) Rs. 500\n','','','a) Rs. 300	        ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25770,280,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of account holder can deposit or withdraw money at any number of times in a day.\na) saving 		b) current 		c) fixed deposit		d) recuring deposit\n','','','b) current ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25771,280,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The transactions of an account holder are recorded in -----\na) cheque book 		b) pass book 	           c) balance book 		d) slip\n','','','b) pass book 	 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25772,280,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fill in the blanks : A = ----- + I\na) P 	         	b) R 	           	c) T 	             	d) 100\n','','','a) P 	   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(25773,280,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fill in the blanks : Interest (I) = (P × ---- × T)/100\na) P 	          	b) T 	          	c) R           		d) A\n','','','c) R','',0,0,0,NULL),(25774,280,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The full form of ATM is -----\na) Automatic tailor money 		b) Any time money 	c) Automatic tailor machine		d) None of these \n','','','c) Automatic tailor machine		','',0,0,0,NULL),(25775,280,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If Rs. 120000 are borrowed for 4 years at 10 p. c. p. a. find its interest.','','','Here, P = 1,20,000, T = 4 years, R = 10%\n∴ S. I. = (P × R × T)/100 = (1,20,000 × 4 × 10)/100 = ₹ 48,000 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(25776,280,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If P = 10000, R = 8%, T = 2 years. Find A.','','','Here, P = 10,000, T = 2 years, R = 8 %\n∴ S. I. = (P × R × T)/100 = (10,000 × 2 × 8)/100 = ₹ 1600\n ∴ A = P + I = 10,000 + 1600 = ₹ 11600','',0,0,0,NULL),(25777,280,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any four names of nationalized banks.','','','State Bank of India, Bank of India, Bank of Maharashtra, Bank of Baroda, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25778,280,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If I = 1200, T = 5 years, R = 10%, find P.','','','Here, I = 1200, T = 5, R = 10\n∴ S. I. = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 1200 = (P × 5 × 10)/100\n∴ 1200 = P/2\n∴ P = 2400','',0,0,0,NULL),(25779,280,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If A = 5082, P = 4200, R = 7%, find T.','','','Here, A = 5082, P = 4200, R = 7 %\n∴ A = P + I \n∴ 5082 = 4200 + I \n∴ I = 5082 – 4200 = 882\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 882 = (4200 × 7 × T)/100\n∴ T = 882/294\n∴ T = 3 years','',0,0,0,NULL),(25780,280,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If P = Rs. 5000, T = 4 years, I = Rs. 1200, find R.','','','Here, P = 5000, T = 4, I = 1200\n∴  I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 1200 = (5000 × 4 × R)/100\n∴ R = 1200/200 = 6 %','',0,0,0,NULL),(25781,280,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Neha took a loan of 50000 rupees at 12 p. c. p. a. to a buy a two-wheeler. What amount will she return to the bank at the end of the one year?','','','Here, P = 50,000, R = 12%, T = 1 year\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ I = (50,000 × 12 × 1)/100\n∴ I = 6000\n∴ A = P + I = 50,000 + 6000 = ₹ 56,000\n∴ Amount to be returned to the bank = ₹ 56,000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25782,280,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Aakash deposited 25000 rupees in a bank at a rate of 8 p. c. p. a. for 3 years. How much interest does he get every year? How much, altogether?','','','Here, P = 25,000, R = 8%, T = 3 year\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ I = (25,000 × 8 × 3)/100\n∴ I = 6000','',0,0,0,NULL),(25783,280,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If Rihanna deposits 1500 rupees in the school fund at 9 p. c. p. a. for 2 years, what is the total amount she will get?','','','Here, P = 1500, R = 9%, T = 2 year\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ I = (1500 × 9 × 2)/100\n∴ I = 270\n∴ A = P + I = 1500 + 270 = ₹ 1770\n∴ Rihana will get total amount of ₹ 1770.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25784,280,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If Pankaj deposits 150000 rupees in a bank at 10 p. c. p. a. for two years, what is the total amount he will get from the bank?','','','Here, P = 150000, R = 10 %, T = 2 year\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ I = (150000 × 10 × 2)/100\n∴ I = 30,000\n∴ A = P + I = 150000 + 30000 = ₹ 180000\n Hence, Pankaj get ₹ 180000 from the bank.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25785,280,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If P = 25000 rupees, Amount = 31000 rupees, Time = 4 years, what is the rate of interest? ','','','Here, P = 25000, A = 31000, T = 4 years, R = ?\nA = P + I \n31000 = 25000 + I\nI = 31000 – 25000 = 6000\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 6000 = (25000 × 4 × R)/100\n∴ R = 6000/1000 = 6%','',0,0,0,NULL),(25786,280,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Sandeepbhau borrowed 120000 rupees from a bank for 4 years at the rate of 8.5 p. c. p. a. for his son’s education. What is the total amount he returned to the bank at the end of that period?','','','Here, P = 120000, R = 8.5, T = 4\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100 \n     = (120000 × 8.5 × 4)/100 = 40800\nThe total amount returned to the bank = 120000 + 40800\n         = ₹ 160800.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25787,280,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Shrikant deposited 85000 rupees for 2.5 years at 7 p. c. p. a. in a saving bank account. What is the total interest he received at the end of the period?','','','Here, P = 85000, R = 7 %, T = 2.5 years\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100 \n      = (85000 × 7 × 2.5)/100 = 14875\nHence, Shrikant received total interest of ₹ 14875 at the end of the period.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25788,280,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Unmesh borrowed some money for 5 years at simple interest. The rate of interest is 9 p. c. p. a. If he returned 17400 rupees altogether at the end of 5 years, how much had he borrowed?','','','I = (P × R × T)/100\nThis formula cannot be used directly to solve the problem because we do not know either interest or principle. \nHowever, the interest on principal of 100 rupees for 5 years is 45 rupees. \nHence, the amount is 100 + 45 = 145 rupees.\nNow we can express the ratio of principal and amount in two ways and obtain an equation.\nIf Unmesh’s principal is P then P/17400 = 100/145\n∴ P = (100 × 17400)/145 = 12000\n∴ The principal that Unmesh borrowed was 12000 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25789,280,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If Principal (P) = 4200, R = 7%, T = 3 years, then find Interest (I) and Amount (A).','','','I = (P × R × T)/100\n       = (4200 × 7 × 3)/100 = ₹ 882\nA = P + I = 4200 + 882 = ₹ 5082','',0,0,0,NULL),(25790,280,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If R = 6%, T = 4 years, Interest (I) = 1200, then find P and A?','','','I = (P × R × T)/100\n     1200  = (P × 6 × 4)/100 \n∴ P = (1200 × 100)/24\n      = 5000\nA = P + I = 5000 + 1200 = ₹ 6200','',0,0,0,NULL),(25791,280,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If Sharayu deposited Rs. 150000 in a bank for 2 years at 10 p. c. p. a. What amount will she get after 2 years.','','','Here, P = 150000, R = 10%, T = 2 years\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n      = (150000 × 10 × 2)/100\n      = 30,000\n∴ A = P + I = 150000 + 30000 = ₹ 1,80,000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25792,280,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Asha borrowed Rs. 21000 for 4 years at certain rate of interest. If she returned Rs. 27300 at the end of 4 years, find the rate of interest she paid. ','','','P = 21000, T = 4 years, A = 27300, R = ?\n∴ I = A – P = 27300 – 21000 = 6300\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 6300 = (21000 × 4 × R)/100\n∴ R = 6300/840 = 7.5 %','',0,0,0,NULL),(25793,280,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Jethalal took a housing loan of 250000 rupees from a bank at 10 p. c. p. a. for 5 years. What is the yearly interest he must pay and the total amount he returns to the bank?','','','Here, P = 2,50,000, R = 10%, T = 5 years\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n     = (250000 × 10 × 1)/100 = 25000\n∴ Jethalal has to pay an interest of ₹ 25000 yearly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25794,280,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','At a certain rate of interest, the interest after 4 years on 5000 rupees principal is 1200 rupees. What would be the interest on 15000 rupees at the same rate of interest for the same period?','','','Here, P = 5000, T = 4 years, I = 1200\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 1200 = (5000 × 4 × R)/100\n∴ R = 1200/200 = 6\nNow, to find interest on 15000 rupees at same time period and same rate.\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n      = (15000 × 6 × 4)/100 = 3600\n∴ The interest on ₹ 15000 for 4 years at 6 % is ₹ 3600.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25795,280,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Javed deposited 12000 rupees at 9 p. c. p. a. in a bank for some years and withdrew his interest every year. At the end of the period, he had received altogether 17400 rupees. For how many years had he deposited his money?','','','Here, P = 12000, R = 9 %, A = 17400\nA = P + I \n∴ I = 17400 – 12000 = 5400\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 5400 = (12000 × 9 × T)/100\n∴ T = 5400/1080 = 5\n∴ Javed deposited his money in the bank for 5 years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25796,280,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Lataben borrowed some money form a bank at a rate of 10 p. c. p. a. interest for 2.5 years to start a cottage industry. If she paid 10250 rupees as total interest, how much money had she borrowed?','','','Here, R = 10%, T = 2.5 years, I = 10250\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 10250 = (P × 10 × 2.5)/100\n∴ P = 10250 × 4 = 41000\n∴ Lataben borrowed ₹ 41000 from the bank.','',0,0,0,NULL),(25797,280,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If Interest (I) = 6000, Amount (A) = 18000, R = 5%, then find P and T?','','','Here, R = 5, I = 6000, A = 18000\nA = P + I \n∴ P = 18000 – 6000 = 12000\nI = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 6000 = (12000 × 5 × T)/100\n∴ T = 6000/600 = 10 years','',0,0,0,NULL),(25798,280,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If Interest (I) = 2400, R = 2.5%, T = 5 years, then find P and A?','','','Here, R = 2.5 %, T = 5 years, I = 2400\nI = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 2400 = (P × 2.5 × 5)/100\n∴ P = (2400 × 100)/12.5 = 19200\nA = P + I = 19200 + 2400 = 21600\n∴ A = ₹ 21600. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26012,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The value of the variable which satisfies the given equation is called the ----- of the equation.\na) real number 		b) whole number 		c) solution 		d) remainder \n','','','c) solution','',0,0,0,NULL),(26013,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','We can divide both sides of an equation by the same non ----- number.\na) positive 		b) zero 		c) equal 		d) negative\n','','','b) zero 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26014,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 9x – 4 = 6x + 29 then x = ------\na) 12 			b) 44 			c) 33 			d) 11\n','','','d) 11','',0,0,0,NULL),(26015,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>2</mn>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math> + 5a = 4 then a = ----\na) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>2</mn>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math> 		b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n    <mn>2</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math>		c) 4 			d) 5\n','','','a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>2</mn>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math>	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26016,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>x</mn>\n    <mn>2</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math> – <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>x</mn>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math> = 8 then x = -----\na) 48 			b) 16 			c) 24 			d) 32\n','','','a) 48 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26017,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 2x – 3 = 5 then x = -------\na) 1 			b) -1 			c) 4 			d) -4\n','','','c) 4','',0,0,0,NULL),(26019,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ages of Sonu and Monu are in the ratio 7 : 5. Ten years, hence, the ratio of their ages will be 9 : 7. Find the present age of Monu.\na) 30 			b) 35 			c) 25 			d) 33\n','','','c) 25 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26020,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','I am currently 5 times as old as my son. After 6 years I will be three times as old as my son, find my son’s age -----\na) 10 			b) 6 			c) 5 			d) 7\n','','','b) 6 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26022,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 0.16 (5x – 2) = 0.4x + 7 then x = ------\na) 18.3 		b) 18 			c) 18.1 		d) 19.3\n','','','a) 18.3','',0,0,0,NULL),(26023,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If sum of ages of Ram and Shyam is 35 years and the difference of ages of Ram and Shyam is 1 year, find other ages.\na) 13, 14		b) 15, 16 		c) 18, 17 		d) 17, 16\n','','','c) 18, 17 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26024,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x – 4 = 3, then x = ----\na) -1 			b) 7 			c) -7 			d) 1\n','','','b) 7 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26025,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 17p – 2 = 49, then p = -----\na) 2 			b) 3 			c) 4 			d) -2\n','','','b) 3 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26026,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>3x</mn>\n    <mn>5</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math> = 18','','','3x/5 = 18\n∴  3x = 5 × 18 = 90\n∴ x = 90/3 = 30','',0,0,0,NULL),(26027,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Is x = 4, the solution of the equation 7(x – 9) = 5(x – 5)?','','','LHS = 7x – 63 = 7 × 4 – 63 = 28 – 63 = -35\nRHS = 5x – 25 = 5 × 4 – 25 = 20 – 25 = -5 \nLHS ≠ RHS \n∴ x = 4 is not solution of the given equation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26028,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : x + 4 = 9','','','x = 9 – 4 = 5 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26029,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : x – 2 = 7','','','x = 7 + 2 = 9','',0,0,0,NULL),(26030,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>x</mn>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math>= 4','','','x = 3 × 4 = 12','',0,0,0,NULL),(26031,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : 4x = 24','','','x = 24/4 = 6 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26032,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : 2m + 7 = 9','','','2m = 9 – 7 = 2\n∴ m = 2/2 = 1\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26033,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : 3x + 12 = 2x – 4 ','','','3x – 2x = -4 – 12 \n∴ x = -16\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26034,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x = 3 is the solution of 3k – x² = 0, find k.','','','Put x = 3 then the equation is, 3k – (3)² = 0\n∴ 3k = 9 \n∴ k = 9/3 = 3','',0,0,0,NULL),(26035,284,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : 3a – 9 = 2(a – 8)','','','3a – 9 = 2a – 16 \n∴ 3a – 2a = -16 + 9\n∴ a = -7 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26036,284,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Solve : 5(x – 3) = 3(x + 2)','','','5x – 15 = 3x + 6\n∴ 5x – 3x = 6 + 15\n∴ 2x = 21\n∴ x = 21/2 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26037,284,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Solve :<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>9x</mn>\n    <mn>8</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math>+ 1 = 10','','','9x/8 = 10 – 1 = 9 \n∴ 9x = 9 × 8 \n∴ x = 8','',0,0,0,NULL),(26039,284,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Solve : 13x – 5 = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n    <mn>2</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math>\n','','','2(13x – 5) = 3\n∴ 26x – 10 = 3 \n∴ 26x = 10 + 3 = 13\n∴ x = 13/26 = 1/2','',0,0,0,NULL),(26040,284,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Solve : 3(y + 8) = 10(y – 4) + 8','','','3y + 24 = 10y – 40 + 8\n∴ 3y – 10y = -32 – 24 \n∴ -7y = -56 \n∴ y = 8','',0,0,0,NULL),(26045,284,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : 5x – 3 = 3x + 7','','','5x – 3x = 7 + 3 \n∴ 8x = 10 \n∴ x = 10/8 = 5/4 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26046,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Each equation is followed by the values of the variable. Decide whether these values are the solutions of that equation or not : x – 4 = 3, x = -1, 7, -7','','','Given equation is, x – 4 = 3 \nPut x = -1 \nLHS = -1 – 4 = -5 ≠ RHS\n∴ x = -1 is not solution\nPut x = 7 \nLHS = 7 – 4 = 3 = RHS\n∴ x = 7 is a solution\nPut x = -7\nLHS = -7 – 4 = -11 ≠ RHS\n∴ x = -7 is not solution. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26047,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Each equation is followed by the values of the variable. Decide whether these values are the solutions of that equation or not : 9m = 81, m = 3, 9, -3','','','Given equation is, 9m = 81\nPut m = 3 \nLHS = 9 × 3 = 27 ≠ RHS\n∴ m = 3 is not solution\nPut m = 9\nLHS = 9 × 9 = 81 = RHS\n∴ m = 9 is a solution.\nPut m = -3 \nLHS = 9 × (-3) = -27 ≠ RHS\n∴ m = -3 is not solution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26048,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Each equation is followed by the values of the variable. Decide whether these values are the solutions of that equation or not : 2a + 4 = 0, a = 2, -2, 1','','','Given equation is 2a + 4 = 0\nPut a = 2 \nLHS = 2(2) + 4 = 4 + 4 = 8 ≠ RHS\n∴ a = 2 is not solution \nPut a = -2 \nLHS = 2(-2) + 4 = -4 + 4 = 0 = RHS\n∴ a = -2 is a solution\nPut a = 1 \nLHS = 2(1) + 4 = 2 + 4 = 6 ≠ RHS\n∴ a = 1 is not solution','',0,0,0,NULL),(26049,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Each equation is followed by the values of the variable. Decide whether these values are the solutions of that equation or not : 3 – y = 4, y = -1, 1, 2','','','Given equation is, 3 – y = 4 \nPut y = -1 \nLHS = 3 – (-1) = 3 + 1 = 4 = RHS\n∴ y = -1 is a solution \nPut y = 1\nLHS = 3 – 1 = 2 ≠ RHS \n∴ y = 1 is not a solution \nPut y = 2\nLHS = 3 – 2 = 1 ≠ RHS\n∴ y = 2 is not a solution ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26050,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Joseph’s weight is two times the weight of his younger brother. Find Joseph’s weight if sum of their weights is 63 kg.','','','Let the weight of Joseph’s younger brother be x kg\n∴ Joseph’s weight is two times the weight of his younger brother = 2x\n∴ From the given information, x + 2x = 63\n∴ 3x = 63\n∴ x = 21\n∴ Joseph’s weight = 2x = 2 × 21 = 42 kg','',0,0,0,NULL),(26051,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Mother is 25 years older than her son. Find son’s age if after 8 years ratio of son’s age to mother’s age will be 4/9.','','','Let son’s age = x years and mother’s age = x  + 25 years\nAfter 8 years son’s age = x + 8 years and mother’s age = x + 25 + 8 = x + 33 years\nNow, by given information, x + 8/x + 33 = 4/9\n∴ 9(x + 8) = 4 (x + 33)\n∴ 9x + 72 = 4x + 132 \n∴ 9x – 4x = 132 – 72 \n∴ 5x = 60\n∴ x = 12\n∴ Son’s age = 12 years. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26052,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The ratio of weights of copper and zinc in brass is 13 : 7. Find the weight of zinc in a brass utensil weighing 700 gm.','','','Let weight of copper = 13x and weight of zinc in brass = 7x \nNow, Total weight of brass = 700 gm\n∴ 13x + 7x = 700\n∴ 20x = 700 \n∴ x = 700/35 = 35 \n∴ Weight of copper in brass = 13x = 13 × 35 = 455 gm\n∴ weight of zinc in brass = 7x = 7 × 35 = 245 gm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26053,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Of the three consecutive natural numbers, five times the smallest number is 9 more than four times the greatest number, find the numbers.','','','Let the three consecutive natural numbers are x, x + 1, x + 2\nBy given condition, five times the smallest number is 9 more than four times the greatest number.\n∴ 5x = 4(x + 2) + 9 \n∴ 5x = 4x + 8 + 9 \n∴ 5x – 4x = 17\n∴ x = 17\n∴ The required numbers are x = 17, x + 1 = 17 + 1 = 18, x + 2 = 17 + 2 = 19.\n∴ The required numbers are 17, 18, 19.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26054,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A cricket player scored 180 runs in the first match and 257 runs in the second match. Find the number of runs he should score in the third match so that the average of runs in the three matches be 230.','','','Let the player scored ₹ x in the third match.\nIt is given that, 180, 257, x has average of ₹ 230\n∴ 230 = (180 + 257 + x)/3 \n∴ 230 × 3 = 437 + x\n∴ x = 690 – 437 = 253\n∴ The player scored 253 runs in the third match.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26056,284,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The difference between squares of two consecutive terms is 31. Find the numbers.','','','Let the two consecutive terms are x, x + 1\nFrom given condition, (x + 1)² - x² = 31\n∴ x² + 2x + 1 - x² = 31\n∴ 2x + 1 = 31\n∴ 2x = 31 – 1 = 30\n∴ x = 15 \n∴ The numbers are x = 15 and x + 1 = 15 + 1 = 16.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26057,284,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Numerator of a fraction is 5 more than its denominator. If 4 is added to numerator and denominator, the fraction obtained is 6/5. Find the fraction.','','','Let the denominator of the fraction be x.\n∴ Numerator of the fraction is 5 more than denominator means x + 5.\n∴ The fraction is (x + 5)/x\nIf 4 is added to both numerator and denominator, fraction obtained is 6/5.\n∴ (x + 5 + 4)/(x + 4) = 6/5\n∴ (x + 9)/(x + 4) = 6/5\n∴ 5(x + 9) = 6(x + 4)\n∴ 5x + 45 = 6x + 24\n∴ 5x – 6x = 24 – 45 \n∴ x = 21\n∴ denominator of fraction = 21 and numerator = 21 + 5 = 26\n∴ The fraction is = 26/21','',0,0,0,NULL),(26059,284,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The denominator of a fraction is greater than its numerator by 12. If the numerator is decreased by 2 and the denominator is increased by 7, the new fraction is equivalent with 1/2. Find the fraction.','','','Numerator of fraction be x and denominator = x  +12\nNow, by given condition, \n(x – 2)/(x + 12 + 7)  = 1/2 \n∴ 2(x – 2) = x + 19 \n∴ 2x – 4 = x + 19\n∴ 2x – x = 19 + 4 \n∴ x = 23\n∴ Numerator = 23 and denominator = x + 12 = 23 + 12 = 35\n∴ The fraction = 23/35','',0,0,0,NULL),(26060,284,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Find three consecutive whole numbers whose sum is more than 45 but less than 54.','','','Let the three consecutive whole numbers one x, x + 1 and x + 2\n∴ Sum of these number = x + x + 1 + x + 2 = 3x + 3\nIt is given that the sum is more than 45 and less than 54.\n∴ When the sum is 45\n3x + 3 = 45 \n∴ x = 14 and when sum is 54.\n3x + 3 = 54\n∴ x = 17\nThus x is greater than 14 and less than 17 i.e. x = 15 or x = 16\nCase I : For x = 15 \nThe numbers are x = 15, x + 1 = 15 + 1 = 16, x + 2 = 15 + 2 = 17\nCase II : For x = 16 \nThe numbers are x = 16, x + 1 = 16 + 1 = 17, x + 2 = 16 + 2 = 18\n∴ The three consecutive whole numbers are 15, 16, 17 or 16, 17, 18.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26061,284,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In a two digit number, digit at the ten’s place is twice the digit at unit’s place. If the number obtained by interchanging the digits is added to the original number, the sum is 66. Find the number.','','','Let the digit at units place = x\n∴ digit at tens place = 2x\n∴ The two digit number is 10(2x) + x = 20x + x = 21x\nNow, if digits are interchanged the number will be, 10x + 2x = 12x\nNow by given condition, 21x + 12x = 66\n∴ 33x = 66\n∴ x = 66/33 \n∴ x = 2\n∴ The digit at units place = 2 and The digit at tens place = 2x = 2 × 2 = 4\n∴ The two digit number is = 10(4) + 2 = 40 + 2 = 42','',0,0,0,NULL),(26062,284,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Some tickets of Rs. 200 and some of Rs. 100, of a drama in a theater were sold. The number of tickets of Rs. 200 sold was 20 more than the number of tickets of Rs. 100. The total amount received by the theatre by sale of tickets was Rs. 37000. Find the number of Rs. 100 tickets sold.','','','Let the number of tickets sold for ₹ 100 is x\n∴ The number of tickets sold for ₹ 200 is x + 20\nNow by given condition, \nTotal amount received by selling tickets = 37000\n∴ (100 × x) + [200 × (x + 20)] = 37000\n∴ 300x = 37000 – 4000 \n∴ x = 110\n∴ 110 tickets of ₹ 100 are sold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26063,284,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Raju sold a bicycle to Amit at 8% profit. Amit repaired it spending Rs. 54. Then he sold the bicycle to Nikhil for Rs. 1134 with no loss and no profit. Find the cost price of the bicycle for which Raju purchased it.','','','Let Raju purchased the bicycle at ₹ x\nHe sold the bicycle at 8% profit.\n∴ Profit = 8% of x = 8x/100\n∴ Selling price of bicycle = x + 8x/100 = 108x/100\nNow Amit repaired the bicycle spending ₹ 54 and sold to Nikhil for ₹ 1134 on no profit or no loss\n∴ 108/100 + 54 = 1134\n∴ 108x/100 = 1134 – 54 \n∴ 108x = 1080 × 100 \n∴ x = ₹ 1000\nThus, Raju purchased the bicycle at ₹ 1000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26067,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When the two triangles are congruent, we get ------ congruences.\na) Four 		b) Three 		c) Six 			d) Two\n','','','c) Six','',0,0,0,NULL),(26068,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------- test for congruence does not exist.\na) SSA 		b) SAS			c) ASA 		d) Hypotenuse – side test\n','','','a) SSA 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26069,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If STU ↔ XZY then ------- of the following is not true.\na) TU ↔ ZY 		b) ZX ↔ TS 		c) S ↔ Y 		d) YX ↔ US\n','','','c) S ↔ Y ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26070,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','ΔABC ≅ ΔPQR. If ∠Q = 60⁰, ∠R = 40⁰ then ∠A = --------\na) 80⁰			b) 40⁰			c) 60⁰			d) None of these\n','','','a) 80⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26071,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','ΔABD ≅ ΔACD by ------- Test.\na) Hypotenuse – side test 		b) SAS Test 		c) ASA Test 		d) SSS Test\n','','','b) SAS Test ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26072,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ΔADB ≅ ΔCDB by ------- test.\na) SSS			b) SAS 			c) AAS 		d) ASA\n','','','d) ASA Test','',1,0,0,NULL),(26073,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','ΔWXZ ≅ ΔWYZ by ------ test.\na) Hypotenuse – side 		b) ASA 		c) SAS 		d) AAS\n','','','c) SAS 	','',1,0,0,NULL),(26074,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The triangles given in the figure are congruent by ------ test.\na) SSS			b) SAA 		c) ASA 		d) SAS\n','','','c) ASA ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26075,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ test is used only right angled triangles.\na) SSS Test 		b) Hypotenuse – side test 		c) ASA Test 		d) SAS Test\n','','','b) Hypotenuse – side test ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26076,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In figure which is required to prove that, ΔABC ≅ ΔPQR by SAS test.\na) BC ≅ QR 		b) AC ≅ PR 		c) ∠A ≅ ∠P 		d) ∠C ≅ ∠R\n','','','a) BC ≅ QR ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26077,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In figure, if AB = 5 cm then AC = -----\na) 7 cm 		b) 10 cm 		c) 4 cm 		d) 5 cm\n','','','d) 5 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26078,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In figure, ΔABC is ------ triangle.\na) Isosceles triangle 		b) equilateral 		c) Right angled 		d) obtuse angled\n','','','a) Isosceles triangle ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26079,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If ΔEFG ≅ ΔPQR and GE is the hypotenuse in ΔEFG, then right angle in ΔPQR is -----\na) ∠P			b) ∠Q			c) ∠R 			d) can’t be determined\n','','','b) ∠Q	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26080,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','ΔABC ≅ ΔPRQ, then ∠B and PQ respectively equal to ------\na) ∠P and AC 		b) ∠R and BC 		c) ∠R and AC 		d) ∠Q and AB\n','','','c) ∠R and AC 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26081,285,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If for ΔABC and ΔDEF, the correspondence CAB ↔ EDF gives a congruence, then which of the following is not true?\na) AC = DE 		b) AB = EF 		c) ∠A = ∠D 		d) ∠C = ∠ E\n','','','b) AB = EF 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26082,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, ΔABC is congruent with ΔPQC by ----- test.','','','In ΔABC and ΔPQC\nSide AB ≅ side PQ\n∠ABC = ∠PQC \nSide BC ≅ side QC\n∴ ΔABC ≅ ΔPQC ----------- [SAS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26083,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the corresponding sides of ΔABC and ΔPQR under the correspondence ABC ↔ RQP.','','','side AB ≅ side RQ\nSide BC ≅ side QP\nSide AC ≅ side RP','',0,0,0,NULL),(26084,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Two line segments are congruent if ------','','','Having lengths are equal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26085,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Among two congruent angles, one has measure of 70⁰; the measure of the other angle is ------','','','70⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26086,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give any two real-life examples for congruent shapes.','','','Shape of window, Shape of wall','',0,0,0,NULL),(26087,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the hypotenuse and a side of a right -angled triangle are equal to the hypotenuse and a side of another right-angled triangle, then by which congruency criteria the two triangles are congruent?','','','Hypotenuse – side test','',0,0,0,NULL),(26088,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If two angles and the included side of one triangle are respectively equal to two angles and the included side of another triangle, then by which congruency rule the two triangles are congruent?','','','ASA Test','',0,0,0,NULL),(26089,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If three sides of a triangle are equal to the three sides of another triangle, then the two triangles are congruent by which congruence criteria?','','','SSS Test','',0,0,0,NULL),(26090,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which angle is included between the sides DE and EF of ΔDEF?','','','∠E','',0,0,0,NULL),(26091,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which congruence test do you use in the following? Given : AC = DF, AB = DE, BC = EF. So, ΔABC ≅ ΔDEF.','','','SSS Test','',0,0,0,NULL),(26092,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','ΔABC ≅ ΔXYZ, ∠A = 50⁰, ∠B = 60⁰ then measure of ∠Z = -----','','','Given that : ΔABC, ∠A = 50⁰, ∠B = 60⁰\n∴ ∠A + ∠B + ∠C = 180⁰\n∴ ∠C = 180 – 50 – 60 = 70⁰\nΔABC ≅ ΔXYZ\n∴ ∠C = ∠Z = 70⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26093,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','By applying SAS congruence rule, you want to establish that ΔPQR ≅ ΔFED. It is given that PQ = EF and RP = DF. What additional information is needed to establish the congruence?','','','∠P = ∠F is additional information is needed to establish the congruence.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26094,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','You want to establish ΔDEF ≅ ΔMNP, using the ASA congruence rule. You are given that ∠D = ∠M and ∠F = ∠P. What information is needed to establish the congruence?','','','side DF ≅ side MP is additional information is needed to establish the congruence. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26095,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which congruence criterion do you use in the following? Given :  EB = BD, AE = CB, ∠A = ∠C = 90⁰. So, ΔABE ≅ ΔCDB.','','','Hypotenuse – side test','',0,0,0,NULL),(26096,285,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','To which triangles can hypotenuse side test be applied?','','','Right angled triangle ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26097,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔXWZ and ΔYWZ\nBy XWZ ↔ YWZ\nSide XW ≅ side YW\n∠XWZ ≅ ∠YWZ\nSide WZ ≅ side WZ ---------- [common side]\n∴ ΔXWZ ≅ ΔYWZ --------- [SAS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26098,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔKJI and ΔLJI\nBy KJI ↔ LJI\nHypo. KI ≅ Hypo. LI\nSide JI ≅ side JI ---------- [common side]\n∴ ΔKJI ≅ ΔLJI --------- [Hypo. – side Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26099,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔHEG and ΔFGE\nBy HEG ↔ FGE\nSide HE ≅ side FG\nSide EG ≅ side GE ---------- [common side]\nSide HG ≅ side FE\n∴ ΔHEG ≅ ΔFGE --------- [SSS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26100,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔSMA and ΔOPT\nBy SMA ↔ OPT\n∠S ≅ ∠O\nSide SM ≅ side OP\n∠M ≅ ∠P\n∴ ΔSMA ≅ ΔOPT --------- [ASA Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26101,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔMNT and ΔSNT\nBy MNT ↔ SNT\n∠MNT ≅ ∠SNT\n∠NTM ≅ ∠NTS\nSide MT ≅ side ST\n∴ ΔMNT ≅ ΔSNT --------- [AAS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26102,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔABC and ΔRPQ\nBy ABC ↔ RPQ\nSide AB ≅ side RP\n∠ABC ≅ ∠RPQ\n∠BCA ≅ ∠RQP\n∴ ΔXWZ ≅ ΔYWZ --------- [SAA Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26103,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔABC and ΔDEC\nBy ABC ↔ DCE\nSide AB ≅ side DC\n∠ABC ≅ ∠DCE\nSide BC ≅ side CE \n∴ ΔABC ≅ ΔDCE --------- [SAS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26104,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔPRQ and ΔTRS\nBy PRQ ↔ TRS\nSide PQ ≅ side TS\n∠PQR ≅ ∠TSR\n∠PRQ ≅ ∠SRT ---- [opposite angles]\n∴ ΔPRQ ≅ ΔTRS --------- [SAA Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26105,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔABC and ΔDCB\nBy ABC ↔ DCB\nSide AB ≅ side DC\n∠ABC ≅ ∠DCB = 90⁰\nSide BC ≅ side BC ---------- [common side]\n∴ ΔABC ≅ ΔDCB --------- [SAS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26106,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In each pair of triangles in the following figures, parts bearing identical marks are congruent. State the test and correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent.','','','In ΔXWZ and ΔZXY\nBy XWZ ↔ ZXY\nSide WZ ≅ side XY\n∠WZX ≅ ∠XYZ\nSide XZ ≅ side XZ ---------- [common side]\n∴ ΔXWZ ≅ ΔZXY --------- [SAS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26107,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, AB = CD and AD = CB. Prove that, ΔABD ≅ ΔCDB.','','','In ΔABD and ΔCDB\nSide AB ≅ side CD\nSide AD ≅ side CB\nSide BD ≅ side BD ---------- [common side]\n∴ ΔABD ≅ ΔCDB --------- [SSS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26108,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, ∠SPR = ∠QRP and ∠RSP = ∠PQR. Prove that, PQ = RS','','','In ΔSPR and ΔQRP\n∠SPR ≅ ∠QRP\n∠RSP ≅ ∠PQR\nSide PR ≅ side PR ---------- [common side]\n∴ ΔSPR ≅ ΔQRP --------- [AAS Test]\n∴ side PQ ≅ side RS ------ [c. s. c. t.]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26109,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, we have AO = BO and CO = DO. Prove that, ΔAOC ≅ ΔBOD.','','','In ΔAOC and ΔBOD\nSide AO ≅ side BO\n∠AOC ≅ ∠BOD ------- [opposite angles]\nSide CO ≅ side DO \n∴ ΔAOC ≅ ΔBOC --------- [SAS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26110,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, we have PQ = SR and PR = SQ. Prove that, ΔPQR ≅ ΔSRQ.','','','In ΔPQR and ΔSRQ\nSide PQ ≅ side SR\nSide QR ≅ side QR ---------- [common side]\nSide PR ≅ side SQ\n∴ ΔPQR ≅ ΔSRQ --------- [SSS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26111,285,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, we have AC Ʇ CD, BC Ʇ CD and DA = DB. Prove that, CA = CB.','','','In ΔDAC and ΔDBC\nSide DA ≅ side DB\n∠DCA ≅ ∠DCB = 90⁰\nSide DC ≅ side DC ---------- [common side]\n∴ ΔDAC ≅ ΔDBC --------- [Hypo. – side Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26112,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles given below, parts shown by identical marks are congruent. State the test and the one to one correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent and write remaining congruent parts.','','','In ΔMST and ΔTBM\nBy MST ↔ TBM\n∠S ≅ ∠B = 90⁰\nHypo MT ≅ Hypo MT --------- [common side]\nSide MS ≅ side TB\n∴ ΔMST ≅ ΔTBM --------- [Hypo. – side test]\nThus, i) side ST ≅ side BM\nii) ∠SMT ≅ ∠BTM\niii) ∠STM ≅ ∠BMT','',1,0,0,NULL),(26113,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles given below, parts shown by identical marks are congruent. State the test and the one to one correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent and write remaining congruent parts.','','','In ΔPRQ and ΔTRS\nBy PRQ ↔ TRS\nSide PR ≅ Side TR \n∠PRQ ≅ ∠TRS ------ [opposite angles]\nSide RQ ≅ side RS\n∴ ΔPRQ ≅ ΔTRS --------- [SAS test]\nThus, i) side PQ ≅ side TS\nii) ∠RPQ ≅ ∠RTS\niii) ∠RQP ≅ ∠RST','',1,0,0,NULL),(26114,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','ΔDEF and ΔLMN are congruent in the correspondence EDF ↔ LMN. Write the pairs of congruent sides and congruent angles in the correspondence.','','','ΔDEF ≅ ΔLMN -------- [given]\nEDF ↔ LMN\nSide ED ≅  side LM\nSide DF ≅ side MN\nSide EF ≅ side LN\n∠E ≅ ∠L ; ∠D ≅ ∠M ; ∠F ≅ ∠N','',0,0,0,NULL),(26115,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In the adjacent figure, congruent sides of ▯ABCD are shown by identical marks. state if there are any pairs of congruent angles in the figure.','','','In ΔABD and ΔCBD\nSide AB ≅ side CB\nSide AD ≅ side CD\nSide BD ≅ side BD -------- [common side]\n∴ ΔABD ≅ ΔCBD ---------- [SSS test]\n∠ABD ≅ ∠CBD --------- [c. a. c. t.]\n∠ADB ≅ ∠CDB\n∠BAD ≅ ∠BCD','',1,0,0,NULL),(26116,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles given below, parts shown by identical marks are congruent. State the test and the one to one correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent and write remaining congruent parts.','','','In ΔPQR and ΔUTS\nPQR ↔ UTS\nSide PQ ≅ side UT\nSide QR ≅ side TS\nSide PR ≅ side US \n∴ ΔPQR ≅ ΔUTS ----------- [SSS Test]\n∠PQR ≅ ∠UTS \n∠PRQ ≅ ∠UST \n∠QPR ≅ ∠TUS ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26117,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles given below, parts shown by identical marks are congruent. State the test and the one to one correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent and write remaining congruent parts.','','','In ΔDBA and ΔDBC\nDBA ↔ DBC\n∠ADB ≅ ∠CDB\nSide BD ≅ side BD ----------- [common side]\n∠ABD ≅ ∠CBD\n∴ ΔDBA ≅ ΔDBC ---------- [ASA Test]\nSide AD ≅ side CD\nSide AB ≅ side CB\n∠BAD ≅ ∠BCD','',1,0,0,NULL),(26118,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the adjoining figure, ΔABC is an isosceles triangle in which AB = AC. BM Ʇ AC and CN Ʇ AB. If AN = AM, then prove that ΔBMA ≅ ΔCNA.','','','In ΔBMA and ΔCNA\nSide AB ≅ side AC\nSide AM ≅ side AN\n∠BMA ≅ ∠CNA = 90⁰\n∴ ΔBMA ≅ ΔCNA ----------- [Hypo. – side Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26119,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, AB = CD and ∠ABC = ∠CDB = 90⁰. Prove that, AC = DB.','','','In ΔABC and ΔCDB\nSide AB ≅ side CD\n∠ABC ≅ ∠DCB \nSide BC ≅ side BC -------- [common side]\n∴ ΔABC ≅ ΔCDB --------- [SAS Test]\nSide AC ≅ side DB --------- [c. s. c. t.]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26120,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, AB Ʇ BD, CD Ʇ BD and AB = CD. Prove that, ΔABD ≅ ΔCDB.','','','In ΔABD and ΔCDB\nSide AB ≅ side CD\n∠ABD ≅ ∠CDB \nSide BD ≅ side BD --------- [common side]\n∴ ΔABD ≅ ΔCDB ----------- [SAS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26121,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, PL Ʇ OA and PM Ʇ OB such that OL = OM. Prove that ΔOLP ≅ ΔOMP.','','','In ΔOLP and ΔOMP\nSide OL ≅  side OM \n∠PLO ≅ ∠PMO = 90⁰\nHypo PO ≅ Hypo PO ----------- [common side]\n∴ ΔOLP ≅ ΔOMP ----------- [Hypo – side Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26122,285,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prove that, ΔABD ≅ ΔCDB','','','In ΔABD and ΔCDB\nSide AD ≅ side BC\n∠ADB ≅ ∠CBD \nSide BD ≅ side BD --------- [common side]\n∴ ΔABD ≅ ΔCDB ----------- [SAS Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26123,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In each pair of triangles given below, parts shown by identical marks are congruent. State the test and the one to one correspondence of vertices by which triangles in each pair are congruent and write remaining congruent parts.','','','In ΔDCH and ΔDCF\nBy DCH ↔ DCF\nSide DC ≅ side DC ----------- [common side]\n∠DCH ≅ ∠DCF\n∠DHC ≅ ∠DFC\n∴ ΔDCH ≅ ΔDCF ----------- [SAA Test]\nThus, i) ∠CDH ≅ ∠CDF\nii) side CH ≅ side CF\niii) side DH ≅ side DF','',1,0,0,NULL),(26124,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In the adjoining figure, seg AD ≅ seg EC. Which additional information is needed to show that ΔABD and ΔCBE will be congruent by AAS Test.','','','To show that ΔABD ≅ ΔEBC by AAS Test\nIt is given that, side AD ≅ side EC\nIt is more need to give that,\n∠ADB ≅ ∠CEB and ∠ABD ≅ ∠CBE\n                OR\n∠DAB ≅ ∠ECB and ∠ABD ≅ ∠CBE\nBut in figure we observe that,\n∠ABD ≅ ∠CBE --------- [opposite angles]\nThus, it is not necessary to give it.','',1,0,0,NULL),(26125,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Complete the activity according to the given steps :  \ni) Draw rhombus ABCD. Draw diagonal AC. 		\nii) Show the congruent parts in the figure by identical marks. 		\niii) State by which test and in which correspondence ΔADC and ΔABC are congruent. 		\niv) Give reason to show ∠DAC ≅ ∠BAC.	\nv) State which property of a rhombus is revealed from the above parts.','','','Solution : ','Side AD ≅ side AB\nSide DC ≅ side BC\nSide AC ≅ side AC --------- [common side]\nΔADC ≅ ΔABC ---------- [SSS Test]\nΔADC ≅ ΔABC --------- [SAS Test]\niv) ∠DCA ≅ ∠BCA\n     ∠DAC ≅ ∠BAC -------- [c. a. c. t.]\nv) The diagonals of a rhombus bisect the opposite angles.',0,0,1,NULL),(26126,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','PQR is a triangle in which PM is the bisector of ∠P and PM Ʇ QR. Prove that : i) ΔPMQ ≅ Δ PMR 		ii) QM = RM','','','In ΔPMQ and ΔPMR,\n∠QPM ≅ ∠RPM --------- [ray PM is bisector]\nSide PM ≅ side PM ---------- [common side]\n∠PMQ ≅ ∠PMR = 90⁰\n∴ ΔPMQ ≅ ΔPMR ----------- [ASA Test]\nSide QM ≅ side RM -------- [c. s. c. t.]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26127,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, ∠BAC = ∠CDB and ∠ACB = ∠DBC. Prove that, AC = DB.','','','In ΔBAC and ΔCDB\n∠BAC ≅ ∠CDB\n∠ACB ≅ ∠DBC\nSide BC ≅ side BC -------- [common side]\n∴ ΔBAC ≅ ΔCDB --------- [AAS Test]\nSide AC ≅ side DB --------- [c. s. c. t.]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26128,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ΔABC, BD and CE are perpendiculars to the sides AC and AB respectively and BD = CE. Prove that, ΔBCD ≅ ΔCBE','','','In ΔBCD and ΔCBE\nSide BD ≅ side CE\n∠D ≅ ∠E = 90⁰ \nHypo BC ≅ Hypo BC ---------- [common side]\n∴ ΔBCD ≅ ΔCBE ----------- [Hypo – side Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26129,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, AB = AC, D is the mid-point of the side BC. Prove that : i) AD Ʇ BC		ii) AD bisects ∠BAC','','','In ΔADB and ΔADC\nSide AB ≅ side AC\nSide AD ≅ side AD ----------- [common side]\nSide BD ≅ side CD ---------- [Point D is midpoint of side BC]\n∴ ΔADB ≅ ΔADC ---------- [SSS Test]\ni) ∠ADB ≅ ∠ADC --------- [c. a. c. t.]\nBut these are linear pair\n∴ ∠ADB + ∠ADC = 180⁰\n∠ADB + ∠ADB = 180⁰ --------- [from (i)]\n2∠ADB = 180⁰\n∠ADB = ∠ADC = 90⁰\n∴ AD Ʇ BC\nii) ∠BAD ≅ ∠CAD \n∴ ray AD is bisector of ∠BAC. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26130,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Two triangles ABC and CDE are such that AC = EC, BC = DC, ∠E = 60⁰, ∠DCE = 30⁰ and ∠B = 90⁰. Show that the triangles are congruent.','','','In ΔEDC,\n∠E + ∠D + ∠ECD = 180⁰ --------- [Sum of angles of triangle]\n∴ ∠EDC = 180 – 60 – 30 = 90⁰\nIn ΔEDC and ΔABC, \nHypo EC ≅ Hypo AC\n∠EDC ≅ ∠ABC = 90⁰\nSide DC ≅ side BC\n∴ ΔEDC ≅ ΔABC --------- [Hypo – side Test]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26131,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','AB = DC and ∠ABC = ∠DCB. Prove that : i) AC = DB 	ii) BE = CE','','','In ΔABC and ΔDCB\nSide AB ≅ side DC\n∠ABC ≅ ∠DCB\nSide BC ≅ side BC ---------- [common side]\n∴ ΔABC ≅ ΔDCB ---------- [SAS Test]\nSide AC ≅ side DB ------------- [c. s. c. t.]\n∠BAC ≅ ∠CDB -------- [c. a. c. t.]\nii) In ΔAEB and ΔDEC\n∠BAE ≅ ∠CDE ------------ [proved]\nSide AB ≅ side DC\n∠AEB ≅ ∠CED ----------- [opposite angles]\n∴ ΔAEB ≅ ΔDEC ------------ [SAA Test]\nSide BE ≅ side CE ----------- [c. s. c. t.]','',1,0,0,NULL),(26132,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If P = 3000, R = 5, N = 2, find compound interest.\na) 315 		b) 310.50		         c) 307.50	             	d) 317.5\n','','','c) 307.50','',0,0,0,NULL),(26133,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If P = 5000, A = 650, R = 10, find N. (Compound interest)\na) 2 years 		b) 3 years 		c) 1 year 		d) 4 years\n','','','a) 2 years 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26134,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For P = 160000, R = 10, N = 2, find amount calculated by compound interest.\na) Rs. 184481 		b) Rs. 194481		c) Rs. 194000           	d) Rs. 174481\n','','','b) Rs. 194481	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26135,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If A = 2662, P = 2000, N = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n    <mn>2</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math>years, find R.\na) 15% 		b) 5% 			c) 20% 		d) 10%\n','','','d) 10%','',0,0,0,NULL),(26137,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A certain amount to Rs. 756.25 at 10% per years in 2 years. Compounded annually. Find the principal.\na) Rs. 625 		b) Rs. 650 		c) Rs. 725 		d) Rs. 710\n','','','a) Rs. 625 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26139,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The interest charged every year on the amount of last year is ------\na) Simple interest 		b) Principal 		c) Compound interest 		d) Installment \n','','','c) Compound interest ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26140,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The difference between the simple interest and the compound interest of a certain sum at the same rate for the first year is ------\na) different 			b) same 		c) zero 		d) can’t determined \n','','','b) same 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26141,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The C. I. on Rs. 1000 for 3 years at 10% p.a. is ------\na) 1331 		b) 100		c) 331 		d) 131\n','','','c) 331 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26142,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When C. I. is calculated monthly then the interest rate is taken as ------\na) R 			b) 12R 		c) R/12 		d) 2R','','','c) R/12 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26143,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The difference between C. I. and S. I. on a sum of Rs. 11000 for first year at 11% annually is -------\na) 210 		b) 1000		c) 0 			d) 100\n','','','c) 0 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26144,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When interest is calculated at an interval of six months for the duration of N years at the rate of R p. c. p. a. then the six monthly rate and stages of six months are taken as ------\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mrow>\n    <mtext>(a)</mtext>\n    <mo>&nbsp;</mo>\n    <mn>2</mn><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo>\n    <mfrac><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac>\n    <mo>&emsp;</mo>\n    <mtext>(b)</mtext>\n    <mo>&nbsp;</mo>\n    <mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo>\n    <mi>N</mi>\n    <mo>&emsp;</mo>\n    <mtext>(c)</mtext>\n    <mo>&nbsp;</mo>\n    <mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo>\n    <mi>N</mi>\n    <mo>&emsp;</mo>\n    <mtext>(d)</mtext>\n    <mo>&nbsp;</mo>\n    <mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo>\n    <mn>2</mn><mi>N</mi>\n  </mrow>\n</math>','','','d) R/2, 2N ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26145,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A man borrowed Rs. 8500 from a bank for three years at the rate of 8% p. a.; interest being compounded annually. If he returns Rs. 13600, the C. I. paid by him is -------\na) Rs. 1700		b) Rs. 5100		c) Rs. 22100		d) Rs. 2100\n','','','b) Rs. 5100	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26146,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If N = 1 then SI ▯ CI.\na) > 			b) < 			c) = 			d) can not say\n','','','c) = 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26147,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If N > 1, then SI ▯ CI.\na) > 			b) < 			c) = 			d) can not say\n','','','b) < ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26148,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If compound interest is calculated monthly then interest rate is taken R/▯ monthly.\na) 12 			b) 2 			c) 6 			d) 1\n','','','a) 12 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26149,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If compound interest is calculated monthly then duration is taken ▯× N.\na) 1 			b) 6 			c) 2 			d) 12\n','','','d) 12','',0,0,0,NULL),(26150,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If value of the article decreases, so the rate of depreciation R is taken as ▯\na) Zero 		b) Positive 		c) Negative 		d) Same as N\n','','','c) Negative 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26151,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If in 2 years a principal of Rs. 100 amounts to Rs. 144, then the rate of interest is ----- p. c. p. a., if compound annually.\na) 10 			b) 20 			c) 14 			d) 15\n','','','b) 20 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26152,286,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In how many years Rs. 600 are amount to Rs. 726 at a compound interest of 10 p. c. p. a.\na) 4 			b) 2 			c) 3 			d) 1 \n','','','b) 2 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26153,286,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the compound interest if Rs. 1000 are invested for 2 years at the rate of 10 p. c. p. a.','','','A = P (1 + R/100)ᴺ\nHere, P = 1000, R = 10, N = 2 years\n∴ A = 1000(1 + 10/100)² = 1000(1 + 1/10)²\n        = 1000(11/10)² = (1000 × 11 × 11)/100 = 121 × 10 = 1210 \n∴ C. I. = A – P = 1210 – 1000 = 210','',0,0,0,NULL),(26154,286,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If P = 2000, R = 5 p. c. p. a. and N = 2 years find simple interest.','','','A = P (1 + R/100)ᴺ\nHere, P = 2000, R = 5, N = 2 years\n∴ A = 2000(1 + 5/100)² = 2000(1 + 1/20)²\n        = 2000(21/20)² = (2000 × 21 × 21)/400 = 2205 \n∴ C. I. = A – P = 2205 – 2000  = 205','',0,0,0,NULL),(26155,286,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the principal Rs. 10000 amounts to Rs. 13924 in 2 years what will be the compound interest?','','','Here, P = 10,000 ; A = 13924\nC. I. = A – P = 13924 – 10000 = ₹ 3924 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26156,286,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the compound interest on Rs. 9000 invested for 2 years at the rate of 10 p. c. p. a.','','','A = P (1 + R/100)ᴺ\nHere, P = 9000, R = 10, N = 2 years\n∴ A = 9000(1 + 10/100)² = 9000(1 + 1/10)²\n        = 9000(11/10)² = (9000 × 11 × 11)/100 = 121 × 90 = 10,890 \n∴ C. I. = A – P = 10890 – 9000 = ₹ 1890','',0,0,0,NULL),(26157,286,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the amount after 3 years on a principal of Rs. 64000 at the rate of compound interest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mrow>\n    <mn>12</mn>\n    <mfrac>\n      <mn>1</mn>\n      <mn>2</mn>\n    </mfrac>\n  </mrow>\n</math>% p. c. p. a.','','','A = P (1 + R/100)ᴺ\nHere, P = 64,000, R = 12.5, N = 3 years\n∴ A = 64,000(1 + 12.5/100)³ = 64000(1 + 125/1000)³\n        = 64000(1 + 5/40)³ = 64000 (1 + 1/8)³ \n         = 64,000 (9/8)³ = (64,000 × 9 × 9 × 9)/8 × 8 × 8\n          = 125 × 729 = 91125 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26158,286,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the simple interest on Rs. 9000 invested at the rate of 10 p. c. p. a. for 3 years.','','','Simple Interest = (P × R × N)/100\n    Here, P = 9000, R = 10, N = 3\n∴ I = (9000 × 10 × 3)/100 = 90 × 10 × 3 = 900 × 3 = 2700','',0,0,0,NULL),(26159,286,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Neha invested Rs. 10000 at the rate 10 p. c. p. a. in a scheme for 3 years. Find the amount she gets after 3 years, if the interest is compounded annually. ','','','A = P (1 + R/100)ᴺ\nHere, P = 10,000, R = 10, N = 3 years\n∴ A = 10,000(1 + 10/100)³ = 10,000(1 + 1/10)³\n        = 10,000(11/10)³ \n         = (10,000 × 11 × 11 × 11)/10 × 10 × 10\n          = 10 × 121 × 11 = 13310','',0,0,0,NULL),(26160,286,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If P = Rs. 3000, R = 5%, N = 3 years. Find compound interest.','','','Here, P = 3000, R = 5%, N = 3 years\nA = P [1 + R/100]ᴺ\n    = 3000 [1 + 5/100]³ = 3000 [1 + 1/20]³\n          = 3000 [21/20]³ = (3000 × 21 × 21 × 21)/20 × 20 × 20\n           = (3 × 21 × 21 × 21)/8 = 3472.87\nC. I. = A – P = 3472.87 – 3000 = 472.87 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26162,286,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If P = Rs. 5000, R = 8, N = 3 years. Find compound interest.','','','If P = Rs. 5000, R = 8, N = 3 years. Find compound interest.\n10) Here, P = 5000, R = 8%, N = 3 years\nA = P [1 + R/100]ᴺ\n    = 5000 [1 + 8/100]³ = 3000 [108/100]³\n     = (5000 × 108 × 108 × 108)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 6298.56\nC. I. = A – P = 6298.56 – 5000 = ₹ 1298.56 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26163,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If P = Rs. 4000, R = 7.5%, N = 2 years. Find compound interest.','','','Here, P = 4000, R = 7.5%, N = 2 years\nA = P [1 + R/100]ᴺ\n    = 4000 [1 + 7.5/100]² = 4000 [1 + 75/1000]²\n    = (4000 × 175 × 175)/1000 × 1000 \n     = 4622.50\nC. I. = A – P =4622.50 – 4000 = ₹ 622.50','',0,0,0,NULL),(26164,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the compound interest if Rs. 4000 are invested for 3 years at the rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mrow>\n    <mn>12</mn>\n    <mfrac>\n      <mn>1</mn>\n      <mn>2</mn>\n    </mfrac>\n  </mrow>\n</math>% p. c. p. a.','','','Here, P = 4000, R = 12.5, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 4000[1 + 12.5/100]³\n    = 4000[1 + 125/1000]³ \n     = 4000 [1 + 1/8]³ = (4000 × 9 × 9 × 9)/8 × 8 × 8\n      = 7.8125 × 729 = 5695.31\nC. I. = A – P = 5695.31 – 4000 = 1695.31','',0,0,0,NULL),(26165,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rohit deposited Rs. 8000 in a bank for 3 years at compound interest rate of 15%. Find the amount received after 3 years.','','','Here, P = 8000, R = 15, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 8000[1 + 15/100]³\n    = 8000[115/100]³ \n     = (8000 × 115 × 115 × 115)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 12167\nC. I. = A – P = 12167 – 8000  = 4167','',0,0,0,NULL),(26166,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vinay takes a loan of Rs. 12000 at compound interest for 3 years at 10%. What amount should he return after 3 years.','','','Here, P = 12,000, R = 10, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 12,000[1 + 10/100]³\n    = 12,000[1 + 1/10]³ \n     = 12,000 [11/10]³ = (12000 × 11 × 11 × 11)/10 × 10 × 10\n      = 15972\nC. I. = A – P = 15972 – 12000  = 3972','',0,0,0,NULL),(26167,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the amount if P = 5000, R = 8 p. c. p. a. and N = 3 years.','','','Here, P = 5000, R = 8, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 5000[1 + 8/100]³\n    = 5000[108/100]³ \n    = (5000 × 108 × 108 × 108)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 6298.56\nC. I. = A – P = 6298.56 – 5000 = 1298.56','',0,0,0,NULL),(26168,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rohit invested Rs. 50000 in a bank for 2 years at the rate of 9 p. c. p. a. compound interest. What amount will he receive at the end of 2 years.','','','Here, P = 50,000, R = 9, N = 2\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 50,000[1 + 9/100]²\n    = 50,000[109/100]² \n    = (50,000 × 109 × 109)/100 × 100 \n      = 59,405','',0,0,0,NULL),(26169,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will be the compound interest on Rs. 4000 in 2 years when rate of interest is 5 p. c. p. a.','','','Here, P = 4000, R = 5, N = 2\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 4000[1 + 5/100]²\n    = 4000[105/100]² \n    = (4000 × 105 × 105)/100 × 100 \n      = 4410\nC. I. = A – P = 4410 – 4000 = 410','',0,0,0,NULL),(26170,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The population of a city was Rs. 30000 in the year 1998. If it is increased at the rate of 5 p. c. p. a., find the population at the end of the year 2001.','','','Here, P = 30,000, R = 5, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 30,000[1 + 5/100]³\n    = 30,000[105/100]³ \n    = (30,000 × 105 × 105 × 105)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 34,728.75\nC. I. = A – P = 34,728.75 – 30,000 = 4728.75','',0,0,0,NULL),(26171,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In how many years will sum of money double it self at 12.5% p. a. at simple interest.','','','Suppose P = 100 then I = P = 100, R = 12.5%\n∴ I = (P × R × N)/100\n∴ P = (P × R × N)/100 \n∴ N = 100/R = 100/12.5 = 8 years ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26172,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rita lent Rs. 15625 to Lata at the rate of 8 p. c. p. a. compound interest. Find the amount payable to Rita after three years.','','','Here, P = 15625, R = 8, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 15625[1 + 8/100]³\n    = 15625[108/100]³ \n    = (15625 × 108 × 108 × 108)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 19683','',0,0,0,NULL),(26173,286,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Calculate the compound interest if P = Rs. 7000, R = 10 p. c. p. a., N = 3 years','','','Here, P = 7000, R = 10, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 7000[1 + 10/100]³\n    = 7000[110/100]³ \n    = (7000 × 110 × 110 × 110)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 9317\nC. I. = A – P = 9317 – 7000 = 2317','',0,0,0,NULL),(26174,286,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Sameerrao has taken a loan of Rs. 12500 at a rate of 12 p. c. p. a. for 3 years. If the interest is years. If the interest is compounded annually then how many rupees should he pay to clear his loan?','','','Here, P = 12500, R = 12, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 12,500[1 + 12/100]³\n    = 12,500[112/100]³ \n    = (12,500 × 112 × 112 × 112)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 17561.6','',0,0,0,NULL),(26175,286,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Text Book','','On the construction work of a flyover bridge there were 320 workers initially. The number of workers were increased by 25% every year. Find the number of workers after 2 years.','','','Here, P = 320, R = 25, N = 2\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 320[1 + 25/100]²\n    = 320[125/100]² \n    = (320 × 125 × 125)/100 × 100 \n      = 500','',0,0,0,NULL),(26176,286,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A shepherd has 200 sheep with him. Find the number of sheep’s with him after 3 years if the increase in number of sheep’s is 8% every year.','','','Here, P = 200, R = 8, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 200[1 + 8/100]³\n    = 200[108/100]³ \n    = (200 × 108 × 108 × 108)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 251.9424\nNo. of sheep cannot be infraction A = 252 sheep','',0,0,0,NULL),(26177,286,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In a forest there are 40000 trees. Find the expected number of trees after 3 years if the number at the rate 5% per year.','','','Here, P = 40,000, R = 5, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 40,000[1 + 5/100]³\n    = 40,000[105/100]³ \n    = (40,000 × 105 × 105 × 105)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 46305','',0,0,0,NULL),(26178,286,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The cost price of a machine is 250000. If the rate of depreciation is 10% per year. Find the depreciation in price of the machine after two years.','','','Here, P = 2,50,000, R = -10, N = 2\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 2,50,000[1 + (-10)/100]²\n    = 2,50,000[90/100]² \n    = (2,50,000 × 90 × 90)/100 × 100 \n      = 202500\nDepreciation = Original cost – New depreciated cost \n     = P – A = 2,50,000 – 202500 = ₹ 47500','',0,0,0,NULL),(26179,286,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Find the compound interest if the amount of a certain principal after two years is Rs. 4036.80 at the rate of 16 p. c. p. a.','','','Here, A = 4036.80, R = 16%, N = 2 years\nA = P(1 + R/100)ᴺ \n∴ 4036.80 = P × (1 + 16/100)² \n∴ P = (4036.80 × 100 × 100)/116 ×116\n       = 40368000/13456 = 3000\nC. I. = A – P = 4036.80 – 3000 = 1036.80','',0,0,0,NULL),(26180,286,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the difference between compound interest and simple interest if P = Rs. 3000, R = 5 p. c. p. a. and N = 2 years.','','','i) To find simple interest : \nHere, P = 3000, R = 5 %, N = 2\nI = (P × R × N)/100 = (3000 × 5 × 2)/100 = ₹ 300\nii) For compound interest : \nA = P(1 + R/100)ᴺ = 3000 (1 + 5/100)²\n    = 3000 (105/100)² = (3000 ×105 × 105)/100 × 100\n    = 3307.50\nC. I. = A – P = 3307.50 – 3000 = 307.50\nDifference between compound interest and simple interest \n= 307.50 – 300 = ₹ 7.50','',0,0,0,NULL),(26181,286,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The population of a village is 120000. If the population increase by 5% every year, what will be it after 3 years.','','','Here, P = 120000, R = 5, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 120000[1 + 5/100]³\n    = 120000[105/100]³ \n    = (120000 × 105 × 105 × 105)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 138915\nPopulation increases after 3 years be 138915.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26182,286,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Two years ago Khushi purchase a machine for Rs. 75000. If its value falls by 4% every year, what is the value of the machine today?','','','Here, P = 75000, R = -4%, N = 2 years\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 75,000[1 + (-4)/100]²\n    = 75000[96/100]² \n    = (75000 × 96 × 96)/100 × 100\n      = 69120\nValue of machine = 75000 – 69120 = 5880','',0,0,0,NULL),(26183,286,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If Rs. 4000 amount to Rs. 5290 in 2 years. Find the rate of compound interest.','','','Here, P = 4000, A = 5290 \nA = P(1 + R/100)ᴺ \n∴ 5290 = 4000 (1 + R/100)² \n∴ 5290/4000 = (100 + R)²/100 × 100\n∴ (529 × 100)/4 = (100 + R)²\n∴ (529 × 25) = (100 + R)²\nTaking square root, \n23 × 5 = 100 + R \n∴  115 – 100 = R\n∴ R = 15% ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26184,286,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','To start a business Shalaka has taken a loan of Rs. 8000 at a rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mrow>\n    <mn>10</mn>\n    <mfrac>\n      <mn>1</mn>\n      <mn>2</mn>\n    </mfrac>\n  </mrow>\n</math>%p. c. p. a. After two years how much compound interest will she have to pay?','','','Here, P = ₹ 8000, R = 10.5%, N = 2 Years\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ\n    = 8000[1 + 10.5/100]²\n    = 8000[110.5/100]² \n     = (8000 × 110.5 × 110.5)²/100 × 100\n      = 9768.20\nC. I. = A – P = 9768.20 – 8000 = 1768.20\nThus, Shalaka will have to pay ₹ 1768.20 as compound interest for 2 years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26185,286,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A loan of Rs. 15000 was taken on compound interest. If the rate of compound interest is 12 p. c. p. a. Find the amount to settle the loan after 3 years.','','','Here, P = 15000, R = 12, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 15000[1 + 12/100]³\n    = 15000[112/100]³ \n    = (15000 × 112 × 112 × 112)/100 × 100 × 100\n      = 21073.92\nThus, the amount to settle the loan after 3 years is ₹ 21073.92.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26186,286,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A principal amount to Rs. 13924 in 2 years by compound interest at 18 p. c. p. a. Find the principal.','','','Here, A = ₹ 13924, R = 18%, N = 2 Years\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ\n   13924 = P[1 + 18/100]²\n    13924 = P[118/100]² \n∴ P = (13924 × 100 × 100)/118 × 118\n       = ₹ 10,000','',0,0,0,NULL),(26187,286,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The population of a suburb is 16000. Find the rate of increase in the population if the population after 2 years is 17640.','','','Here we take, P = 16000, A = 17640, N = 2\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ\n∴ 17640 = 16000[1 + R/100]²\n∴ 17640/160000 = [1 + R/100]²\nTaking square roots, \n420/400 = 1 + R/100\n∴ 21/20 = 1 + R/100\n∴ R/100 = (21 – 20)/20 = 1/20\n∴ R = 100 × 1/20 = 5\nThus, rate of increase of population in the suburb is 5 p. c. p. a.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26188,286,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In how many years Rs. 7000 will amount of Rs. 847 at a compound interest rate of 10 p. c. p. a.','','','Here, A = 847, P = 700, R = 10\n∴ A = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ\n847 = 700[1 + 10/100]ᴺ\n847/700 = [1 + 1/10]ᴺ\n121/100 = [11/10]ᴺ\nWe observe that, (11/10)² = (11/10)ᴺ\n∴ N = 2 years\n∴ After 2 years the amount will be ₹ 847.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26189,286,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Find the difference between simple interest and compound interest on Rs. 20000 at 8 p. c. p. a. ','','','i) For simple interest :\nS. I. = (P × N × R)/100 = (20000 × 2 × 8)/100 = ₹ 3200\nii) For compound interest :\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ \n    = 20000[1 + 8/100]²\n     = 20000[108/100]²\n     = (20000 × 108 × 108)/100 × 100\n      = 23328\nThus, C. I. = A – P = 23328 – 20,000 = ₹ 3328\nHence, the difference between compound interest and simple interest = C. I. – S. I. = 3328 – 3200 = ₹ 128 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26190,286,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The cost of machine is Rs. 250000. It depreciates at the rate of 4% per annum. Find the cost of machine after three years.','','','Here, P = 2,50,000, R = -4, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 2,50,000[1 + (-4)/100]³\n    = 2,50,000[96/100]³ \n    = (2,50,000 × 96 × 96 × 96)/100 × 100 × 100 \n      = 221184\nDepreciation = Original cost – New depreciated cost \n     = P – A = 2,50,000 – 221184 = ₹ 28816','',0,0,0,NULL),(26191,286,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A wholesaler trader sold Rs. 20 crore worth of cloth this year. If the sale of cloth increases at a rate of 2% per year, how much will the sale be after three years?','','','Here, P = 20 crore, R = 2%, N = 3\nHere, P = 20, R = 2, N = 3\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ = 20[1 + 2/100]³\n    = 20[102/100]³ \n    = (20 × 102 × 102 × 102)/100 × 100 × 100 \n      = 21.22\nThus, the sale after 3 years 21.22 crore.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26192,286,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Reet invested Rs. 12000 at 5% per year. After how many years her investment will amount to Rs. 13230 if interest compounded annually?','','','Here, P = 12000, A = 13230, R = 5%, find N = ?\nA = P[1 + R/100]ᴺ\n∴ 13230 = 12000[1 + 5/100]ᴺ\n∴ 13230/12000 = [105/100]ᴺ\n∴ 1323/1200 = [21/20]ᴺ\n∴ 441/400 = [21/20]ᴺ\nWe observe that, (21/20)² = [21/20]ᴺ\n∴ N = 2 years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26193,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a rectangle is formed from the parallelogram, then area of both the figures are ------\na) unequal 		b) equal 		c) increased 			d) can’t say\n','','','b) equal','',0,0,0,NULL),(26194,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Area of rectangle is equal to area of a square with side 12 cm. If length of rectangle is 24 cm, then its breadth is ------ cm.\na) 6 cm 		b) 24 cm 		c) 18 cm 		d) 12 cm\n','','','a) 6 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26195,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If in a triangle its sides are 60 cm, 60 cm and 96 cm then s = ------ cm.\na) 1728 		b) 216 		c) 108 		d) 60\n','','','c) 108 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26196,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If radius of a circle is 21 cm, then its area is ----- cm².\na) 1386 		b) 42 		c) 132 		d) 441\n','','','c) 132 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26197,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the adjoining figure, A(ΔABC) = 1/2 × BC × AB = 1/2 × BM × ▯\na) AM 		b) CM 		c) AC 		d) AM × CM\n','','','a) AM ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26198,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of rhombus is ---- when the lengths of the diagonals are 15 cm and 24 cm.\na) 280 sq. cm. 		b) 200 sq. cm. 		c) 360 sq. cm. 		d) 180 sq. cm.\n','','','d) 180 sq. cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26199,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the lengths of diagonals of rhombus are 11.2 and 7.5 cm then its area is ---\na) 42 sq. cm 			b) 42 cm 		c) 84 sq. cm		d) 84 cm\n','','','a) 42 sq. cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26200,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the area of parallelogram is 112 sq. cm and base is 10 cm then the height of parallelogram is -----\na) 10 cm 		b) 11.2 cm 		c) 12.5 cm 		d) 12 cm\n','','','b) 11.2 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26201,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','1 acre is nearly -----\na) 0.5 hectare 		b) 0.4 hectare 		c) 0.2 hectare 		d) 1 hectare\n','','','b) 0.4 hectare 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26202,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the area of the circle of radius 10 cm is equal to the area of parallelogram of base 31.4 cm, then its height is ------\na) 31.4 cm 		b) 12 cm 		c) 10 cm 		d) can’t be determined\n','','','c) 10 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26203,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of triangle with base 9 cm and height 8 cm is -----\na) 36 cm²			b) 72 cm²		c) 18 cm²		d) 17 cm²\n','','','a) 36 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26204,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of a parallelogram whose base is 25 cm is 500 sq. cm. find its height\na) 25 cm 		b) 50 cm 		c) 20 cm 		d) 15 cm\n','','','c) 20 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26205,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of rhombus = 1/2 × Product of lengths of ------\na) sides 		b) diagonal 		c) hypotenuse 		d) medians\n','','','b) diagonal ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26206,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If length of parallel sides of a trapezium are 18 cm and 12 cm and its height is 8 cm find its area\na) 60 cm²		b) 80 cm²		c) 110 cm²		d) 120 cm²\n','','','d) 120 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26207,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The circumference of a circle whose radius is 21 cm = ------\na) 110 cm 		b) 132 cm 		c) 112 cm 			d) 122 cm\n','','','b) 132 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26208,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of a circle whose circumference is 440 cm is -----\na) 15400 cm²		b) 17600 cm²		c) 11000 cm²	                 d) 15500 cm²\n','','','a) 15400 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26209,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If base of a parallelogram is 20 cm and height is 15 cm, find its area.','','','Area of parallelogram = base × height = 20 × 15 = 300 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26210,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the radius of the circle is 22 cm, find the circumference.','','','Circumference of circle = 2πr = 2 × π × 22 = 44π sq cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26211,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of the right-angled triangle whose sides are 25 cm, 24 cm and 7 cm.','','','Longest side (Hypotenuse) = 25 cm\n∴ Area of right angled triangle = 1/2 × product of perpendicular sides\n               = 1/2 × 24 × 7 = 12 × 7 = 84 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26212,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The sides of triangle are 13 cm, 14 cm, 15 cm find its area.','','','s = (13 + 14 + 15)/2 = 42/2 = 21\n∴ Area of triangle = √s(s – a)(s – b) (s – c)\n                = √21(21 – 13)(21 – 14)(21 – 15)\n                = √21 × 8 × 7 × 6 = √21 × 8 × 7 × 2 × 3\n                = 21 × 4 = 84 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26213,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The lengths of diagonals of a rhombus are 24 cm and 9 cm find its area.','','','Area of rhombus = 1/2 × product of diagonals \n          = 1/2 × 24 × 9 = 12 × 9 = 108 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26214,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of parallelogram.','','','Area of parallelogram = base × height','',0,0,0,NULL),(26215,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of rectangle.','','','Area of rectangle = length × breadth','',0,0,0,NULL),(26216,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of square.','','','Area of square = (side)²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26217,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of rhombus.','','','Area of rhombus = 1/2 × product of diagonals','',0,0,0,NULL),(26218,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of triangle.','','','Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height','',0,0,0,NULL),(26219,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of right-angled triangle.','','','Area of right angled triangle = 1/2 × product of perpendicular sides','',0,0,0,NULL),(26220,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of the trapezium.','','','Area of trapezium = 1/2 × [sum of parallel sides] × height','',0,0,0,NULL),(26221,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of circle.','','','Area of circle = πr²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26222,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for circumference of circle.','','','Circumference of circle = 2πr','',0,0,0,NULL),(26223,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If base of parallelogram is 8 cm and height is 5 cm then find its area.','','','Area of parallelogram = base × height = 8 × 15 = 120 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26224,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If area of a parallelogram is 112 sq cm and base of it is 10 cm then find its height.','','','Area of parallelogram = base × height\n∴ 112 = 10 × height\n∴ height = 112/10 = 11.2 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26225,287,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If radius of circle is 21 cm, then find its area.','','','Area of circle = πr² = 22/7 × 21 × 21 = 1386 sq cm. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26226,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If base of parallelogram is 18 cm and its height is 11 cm, find its area.','','','Area of parallelogram = base × height = 18 × 11 = 198 sq cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26227,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If area of a parallelogram is 29.6 sq cm and its base is 8 cm, find its height.','','','Area of parallelogram = base × height \n∴ 29.6 = 8 × height\n∴ height = 29.6/8 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26228,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Area of a parallelogram is 83.2 sq cm. If its height is 6.4 cm, find the length of its base.','','','Area of parallelogram = base × height \n∴ 83.2 = 6.4 × base\n∴ base = 83.2/6.4 = 13 cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26229,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Lengths of the diagonals of a rhombus are 11.2 cm and 7.5 cm respectively. find the area of rhombus.','','','Area of rhombus = 1/2 × product of diagonals\n∴ Area of rhombus = 1/2 × 11.2 × 7.5 = 42 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26230,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Lengths of diagonals of a rhombus are 15 cm and 24 cm, find its area.','','','Area of rhombus = 1/2 × product of diagonals\n∴ Area of rhombus = 1/2 × 15 × 24 = 180 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26231,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In ▯ABCD, l(AB) = 13 cm, l(DC) = 9 cm, l(AD) = 8 cm, find the area of ▯ABCD.','','','▭ABCD is a trapezium\n∴ A(▭ABCD) = 1/2 × [AB + DC] × AD\n                        = 1/2 × [13 × 9] × 8 = 22 × 4 = 88 sq cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26232,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Area of a circular ground is 3850 sq m. Find the radius of the circular ground.','','','Area of circular ground = πr²\n∴ 3850 = 22/7 × r²\n∴ r² = (3850 × 7)/22 = 1225 \n∴ r = 35 m.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26233,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If r = 28 cm find area of circle.','','','Area of circle = πr² = 22/7 × 28 × 28 \n                              = 88 × 28 = 2464 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26234,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If r = 10.5 cm find area of circle.','','','Area of circle = πr² = 22/7 × 10.5 × 10.5 \n                              = 22 × 1.5 × 10.5 = 346.5 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26235,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If r = 17.5 cm find area of circle.','','','Area of circle = πr² = 22/7 × 17.5 × 17.5 \n                              = 22 × 2.5 × 17.5 = 962.5 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26236,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Area of circle is 176 sq cm find diameter of circle.','','','Area of circle = πr²  \n      ∴ 176 = 22/7 × r²\n∴ r² = (176 × 7)/22 = 8 × 7 = 56\n∴ r = 4√14 cm\n∴ d = 8√14 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26237,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Area of circle is 394.24 sq cm find diameter of circle.','','','Area of circle = πr² = 22/7 \n      ∴ 394.24 = 22/7 × r²\n∴ r² = (394.24 × 7)/22 = 17.92 × 7 = 125.44\n∴ r = 11.2 cm\n∴ d = 22.4 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26238,287,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Area of circle is 12474 sq cm find diameter of circle.','','','Area of circle = πr² = 22/7 \n      ∴ 12474 = 22/7 × r²\n∴ r² = (12474 × 7)/22 = 567 × 7 = 3969\n∴ r = 63 cm\n∴ d = 126 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26239,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Area of a rhombus is 96 sq cm. One of the diagonals is 12 cm find the length of its side.','','','Here, Area of rhombus = 96 sq cm, d₁ = 12 cm, d₂ = ?\nArea of rhombus = 1/2 × d₁ × d₂\n∴ 96 = 1/2 × 12 × d₂\n∴ d₂ = 96/6 = 16 cm','In figure, E be the point of intersection of diagonals of a rhombus. diagonals of rhombus are perpendicular bisectors of each other.\n∴ In ΔADE, ∠E = 90⁰\nDE = 1/2 DB = 1/2 × 12 = 6 and AE = 1/2 AC = 1/2 × 16 = 8 cm\nAD² = AE² + DE² = 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100\n∴ l(AD) = 10 cm\n∴ Side of the rhombus is 10 cm.',0,0,1,NULL),(26240,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Lengths of the diagonals of rhombus are 16.5 cm and 14.2 cm, find its area.','','','Here, d₁ = 16.5 cm , d₂ = 14.2 cm\nArea of rhombus = 1/2 × d₁ × d₂ = 1/2 × 16.5 × 14.2 = 117.15 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26241,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If length of diagonal of a rhombus is 30 cm and its area is 240 sq cm, find its perimeter.','','','Given that, d₁ = 30 cm, area of rhombus = 240 sq cm, \n∴ Area of rhombus = 1/2 × d₁ × d₂ \n∴ 240 = 1/2 × 30 × d₂\n∴ d₂ = 16 cm','Now, diagonals of rhombus are perpendicular bisectors of each other.\n∴ AO = OC = 1/2 AC = 1/2 × 30 = 15 cm\nAnd BO = OD = 1/2 × BD = 1/2 × 16 = 8 cm\nIn ΔAOB, ∠AOB = 90⁰\nAB² = AO² + BO² = 15² + 8² = 225 + 64 = 289\n∴ AB = 17 cm\nHence, perimeter of rhombus = 4 × side = 4 × 17 = 68 cm ',0,0,1,NULL),(26242,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In a trapezium, if distance between parallel sides is 6 cm and lengths of the parallel sides are 7 cm and 8 cm respectively then find the area of the trapezium.','','','Area of trapezium = 1/2 × (sum of parallel side) × height\n            = 1/2 × [7 + 8] × 6 = 45 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26243,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Length of the two parallel sides of a trapezium are 8.5 cm and 11.5 cm respectively and its height is 4.2 cm, find its area.','','','Area of trapezium = 1/2 × (sum of parallel side) × height\n            = 1/2 × [8.5 + 11.5] × 4.2 = 42 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26244,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the sides of a triangle are 17 cm, 25 cm, and 26 cm, find the area of the triangle.','','','s = (a + b + c)/2 = (17 + 25 + 26)/2 = 68/2 = 34\nArea of triangle = √s(s – a)(s – b)(s – c)\n = √34(34 – 17)(34 – 25)(34 - 26)\n = 204 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26245,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Sides of a triangle are 45 cm, 39 cm and 42 cm, find its area.','','','s = (a + b + c)/2 = (45 + 39 + 42)/2 = 126/2 = 63\nArea of triangle = √s(s – a)(s – b)(s – c)\n = √63(63 – 45)(63 – 39)(63 - 42)\n = 756 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26246,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Some measures are given in the adjacent figure, find the area of ▯ABCD.','','','A(▭ABCD) = A(ΔBAD) + A(ΔBDC) ------------ (i)\nA(ΔBAD) = 1/2 × 40 × 9 = 20 × 9 = 180 sq cm\nA(ΔBDC) = 1/2 × DC × BT = 1/2 × 60 × 13 \n                 = 30 × 13 = 390 sq cm\n∴ A(▭ABCD) = 180 + 390 = 570 sq cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26247,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Find the area of the circle if its circumference is 88 cm.','','','Circumference of circle = 88 cm\n ∴ 88 = 2πr\n∴ 88 = 2 × 22/7 × r\n∴ r = (88 × 7)/2 × 22 = 14 cm\n∴ Area of circle = πr² = 22/7 × 14 × 14 = 616 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26248,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If area of a trapezium shaped plot is 480 m², the height is 15 m and one of its parallel sides of 20 m. Find the other side.','','','Area of trapezium = 1/2 × [sum of parallel sides] ×  height\n∴ 480 = 1/2 × [20 + other side] × 15\n∴ (480 × 2)/15 = 20 + other side\n∴ 64 = 20 + other side\n∴ other side = 64 – 20 = 44 m ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26249,287,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of the plot. All lengths are in meters.','','','Area of plot ABCDE = A(ΔDCM) + A(▭CMNB) + A(ΔBNA) + A(ΔEAD) ----------- (i)\nA(ΔDCM) = 1/2 × DM × CM = 1/2 × 75 × 110 = 4125 sq m\nA(▭CMNB) = 1/2 × [CM + BN] × MN\n                      = 1/2 × [110 + 70] × 95 = 8550 sq m\nA(ΔBNA) = 1/2 × BN × NA = 1/2 × 70 × 80 = 2800 sq m\nA(ΔEAD) = 1/2 × EN × AD = 1/2 × 120 × [80 + 95 + 75]\n                = 60 × 250 = 15000 sq m\n∴ Area of plot = 4125 + 8550 + 2800 + 15000 = 30475 sq m ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26250,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If perimeter of a rhombus is 100 cm and length of one diagonal is 48 cm, what is the area of the quadrilateral?','','','Given that, Perimeter of rhombus = 100 cm\n∴ side of rhombus = 100/4 = 25 cm\nAlso, d₁ = 48 cm\nLet ▭ABCD is the given rhombus with AB = BC = CD = AD = 25 cm\nAnd diagonal AC = 48 cm\nWe know that the diagonals of a rhombus are perpendicular bisectors of each other.\n∴ AO = OC = 1/2 AC = 1/2 × 48 = 24 cm','In ΔAOB, AB² = AO² + BO²\n∴ 25² = 24² + BO²\n∴ BO² = 625 – 576 = 49 \n∴ BO 7 cm\nThus, BD = 2BO = 2 × 7 = 14 cm\nArea of rhombus = 1/2 × d₁ × d₂ = 1/2 × 48 × 14 = 336 sq cm.',0,0,1,NULL),(26251,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','▯PQRS is an isosceles trapezium l(PQ) = 7 cm. seg PM Ʇ seg SR, l(SM) = 3 cm. Distance between two parallel sides is 4 cm, find the area of ▯PQRS.','','','It is given that, PQ = 7 cm, SM = 3 cm, PM = 4 cm\nDraw QN ꓕ side SR\nNow distance between two parallel sides is same\n∴ QN = 4 cm\nIn ΔPMS, PS² = PM² + SM² = 4² + 3² = 16 + 9 = 25\n∴ PS = 5 cm\nSince ▭PQRS is an isosceles trapezium\n∴ PS = QR = 5 cm\nSimilarly, we can find NR = 3 cm\n∴ SR = SM + MN + NR = 3 + 7 + 3 = 13 cm\nA(▭PQRS) = 1/2 × (PQ + SR) × PM \n                    = 1/2 × (7 + 13) × 4 = 40 sq cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26252,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The figure of a plot and its measures are given : l(LM) = 60 m, l(MN) = 60 m, l(LN) = 96 m, l(OP) = 70 m. Find the area of the plot.','','','Given that, LM = 60 m, MN = 60 m, LN = 96 m, OP = 70 m\nArea of plot = A(ΔLMN) + A(ΔLNO) ----------- (i)\nA(ΔLMN),\n∴ s = (60 + 60 + 96)/2 = 216/2 = 108 \nA(ΔLMN) = √s(s – a)(s – b) (s – c)\n                  = √108(108 – 60)(108 – 60)(108 – 96)\n                  = 1728 sq m\nA(ΔLNO) = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 96 × 70 \n                 = 3360 sq m\nA(▭LMNO) = A(ΔLMN) + A(ΔLNO) = 1728 + 3360 = 5088 sq m','',1,0,0,NULL),(26253,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Look at the measures shown in the adjacent figure and find the area of ▯PQRS.','','','In ΔPSR by Pythagoras theorem,\nPR² = PS² + SR² = 36² + 15² = 1296 + 225 = 1521\n∴ PR = 39 cm\nNow lets find area of ΔPSR\nA(ΔPSR) = 1/2 × PS × SR = 1/2 × 36 × 15 = 270 sq m\nA(ΔPQR), s = (25 + 56 + 39)/2 = 120/2 = 60\n∴ By Heron’s formula,\nA(ΔPQR) = √s(s – a)(s – b)(s – c)\n                 = √60(60 – 25)(60 – 56)(60 – 39)\n                  = 420 sq m\nFrom figure,\nA(▭PQRS) = A(ΔPQR) + A(ΔPSR) = 420 + 270 = 690 sq m','',1,0,0,NULL),(26254,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Adjacent figure is a polygon ABCDE. All given measures are in meter. Find the area of the given figure.','','','A(ΔAQB) = 1/2 × BQ × AQ = 1/2 × 10 × 13 = 65 sq m\nA(ΔERD) = 1/2 × RD × ER = 1/2 × 16 × 17 = 136 sq m\nA(▭AQRE) = 1/2 × [AQ + ER] × QR = 1/2 × [13 + 17] × (12 + 15)\n                     = 1/2 × 30 × 27 = 405 sq m\nBD = BP + PD = 10 + 12 + 15 + 16 = 53 m\nA(ΔBCD) = 1/2 × BD × PC = 1/2 × 53 × 10 = 265 sq m\n∴ Area of polygon ABCDE = A(ΔAQB) + A(▭AQRE) + A(ΔERD) + A(ΔBCD)\n= 65 + 405 + 136 + 265 = 871 sq m','',1,0,0,NULL),(26255,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Find the area of given plots (All measures in meters)','','','i) A(ΔPAQ) = 1/2 × PA × QA = 1/2 × 30 × 50 = 750 sq m\nii) A(ΔPBT) = 1/2 × PB × BT = 1/2 × (30 + 30) × 30 = 900 sq cm\niii) A(ΔBST) = 1/2  × BS × BT = 1/2 × (30 + 60) × 30 = 1650 sq m\niv) A(ΔRCS) = 1/2 × CS × RC = 1/2 × 25 × 60 = 750 sq m\nv) A(▭AQRC) = 1/2 × (AQ + CR) × AC = 1/2 × (50 + 25) × (30 + 30)\n                         = 1/2 × 75 × 60 = 2250 sq m\nFrom figure, \nArea of plot = A(ΔPAQ) + A(ΔPBT) + A(ΔBST) + A(ΔRCS) + A(▭ACRQ)\n= 750 + 900 + 1350 + 750 + 2250 = 6000 sq m','',1,0,0,NULL),(26256,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Find the area of given plots (All measures in meters)','','','In figure, ΔABE, BE² = AB² + AE²\n∴ 30² = 24² + AE² \n∴ AE² = 900 – 576 = 324 \n∴ AE = 18 m\ni) A(ΔABE) = 1/2 × AB × AE = 1/2 × 24 × 18 = 216 sq m\nii) For ΔBCE, s = (30 + 28 + 26)/2 = 84/2 = 42 m\n∴ A(ΔBCE) = √s(s – a)(s – b)(s – c)\n        = √42(42 – 30)(42 – 28)(42 – 26) = 336 sq m\nNow, A(ΔEDC) = 1/2 × CE × DF = 1/2 × 28 × 16 = 224 sq m\n∴ Area of plot = A(ΔABE) + A(ΔBCE) + A(ΔEDC)\n= 216 + 336 + 224 = 776 sq m','',1,0,0,NULL),(26257,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Diameter of the circular garden is 42 m. There is a 3.5 m wide road around the garden. Find the area of the road.','','','diameter = 42 m\n∴ radius of garden r₁ = 42/2 = 21 m\nNow, outer radius of road = r₂ = 21 + 3.5 = 24.5 m\n∴ Area of road = Area of circle with outer radius – Area of circle with inner radius = πr₂² - πr₁² = π [(24.5)² - (21)²] = 500.5 sq m\nThus, the area of the road is 500.5 sq m.','',1,0,0,NULL),(26258,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of the given figure, from measures given in the figure.','','','A(poly ABCDE) = A(ΔABF) + A(▭BFGC) + A(ΔCGD) + A(ΔEAD) ---------- (i)\ni) A(ΔABF) = 1/2 × AF × BF = 1/2 × 5 × 5 = 25/2 = 12.5 sq cm\nAD = AF + FH + HG + GD \n∴ AG = AF + FG\n8 = 5 + FG \n∴ FG = 8 – 5 = 3 \nii) A(▭BFGC) = 1/2 × (BF + CG) × FG = 1/2 × (5 + 7) × 3 = 6 × 3 = 18 sq cm\nGD = AD – AG = 10 – 8 = 2\niii) A(ΔCGD) = 1/2 × CG × GD = 1/2 × 7 × 2 = 7 sq cm\niv) A(ΔEAD) = 1/2 × AD × EH = 1/2 × 10 × 3 = 15 sq cm\nNow, from (i)\nA(poly ABCDE) = 12.5 + 18 + 7 + 15 = 52.5 sq cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26259,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Radius of inner circle is 7 cm. Thickness of boundary around is 3.5 cm. Find the area between two circles.','','','Area between two circles = Area of outer circle – Area of inner circle ------- (i)\n∴ Area of outer circle = πr₂² = π[7 + 3.5]² = π(10.5)²\nArea of inner circle = πr₁² = π(7)²\nNow, from (i), we get, \nArea between two circles = π(10.5)² - π(7)² \n= π [(10.5)² - 7²] = 22/7 × 61.25 = 192.5 sq cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26260,287,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The shape of a farm is a quadrilateral. Measurement taken of the farm, by naming its corners as P, Q, R, S in order are as follows : l(PQ) = 170 m, l(QR) = 250 m, l(RS) = 100 m, l(PS) = 240 m, l(PR) = 260 m. Find the area of the field in hectare. (1 hectare = 10000 sq. m) ','','','Solution : ','In ΔPQR, s = (170 + 250 + 260)/2 = 680/2 = 340 m\nA(ΔPQR) = √s(s – a)(s – b)(s – c)\n= √340(340 – 170)(340 – 250)(340 – 260)\n= 20,400 sq m\nIn ΔPSR, s = (100 + 240 + 260)/2 = 600/2 = 300 m\nA(ΔPSR) = √s(s – a)(s – b)(s – c) \n= √300(300 – 100)(300 – 240)(300 – 260)\n= 12000 sq m\nArea of field = A(ΔPQR) + A(ΔPSR) = 20,400 + 12,000 = 32,400 sq m',0,0,1,NULL),(26320,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If side of cube is 1.2 cm, then its volume is ------\na) 1.444 cc		b) 1.728 cc		c) 1648 cc		d) 1244 cc\n','','','b) 1.728 cc','',0,0,0,NULL),(26321,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The volume of a tank whose dimensions are 2 m × 1.5 m × 25 cm is -----\na) 0.75 m³		b) 75 cm³		c) 0.075 m³		d) 7.5 m³\n','','','a) 0.75 m³','',0,0,0,NULL),(26322,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much earth is dug out by digging a tank measuring 3 m × 2 m × 5 m \na) 300 m³		b) 30 m³		c) 13 m³		d) 3 m³\n','','','b) 30 m³	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26323,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A water tank is 3 m long, 2 m broad and 3 m deep, find its capacity in liters\na) 180000 ltr. 		b) 18 ltr. 		c) 1800 ltr. 		d) 18000 ltr. \n','','','d) 18000 ltr. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26325,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diameter of base of a cylinder is 42 cm and height is 10 cm, find its curved surface area.\na) 4092 cm²		b) 1320 cm²		c) 1340 cm²		d) 968 cm²\n','','','b) 1320 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26326,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the height of a cylinder whose radius is 7 cm and total surface area is 968 cm².\na) 10 cm 		b) 12 cm 		c) 15 cm 		d) 13 cm\n','','','c) 15 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26327,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If r = 7 cm and h = 15 cm, then the volume of cylinders is -----\na) 2310 cc 		b) 2308 cc		c) 2312 cc		d) 2300 cc\n','','','a) 2310 cc ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26328,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Two cylinders with equal volumes have their heights in the ratio 1 : 2, find the ratio of their radii\na) 2 : 1 		b) 1 : √2 		c) √2 : 1 		d) √3 : 1\n','','','c) √2 : 1 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26329,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Volume of a cube with side 6 cm is ------\na) 216 cm³		b) 216 cm²		c) 36 cm²		d) 36 cm³\n','','','a) 216 cm³	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26330,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of base of cuboid with length 50 cm, breadth 40 cm and height 10 cm.\na) 500 cm²		b) 400 cm²		c) 2000 cm²		d) 200 cm²\n','','','c) 2000 cm²	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26331,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the radius of cylinder is doubled and the height halved then the curved surface area will be --------\na) Triple 		b) double 		c) the same 			d) half\n','','','c) the same','',0,0,0,NULL),(26332,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Volume of a cylinder whose height is 10 cm and radius of a base is 5 cm = ---- (π = 3.14)\na) 587 cm³		b) 758 cm³		c) 785 cm³		d) 100 × 3.14 cm³\n','','','c) 785 cm³','',0,0,0,NULL),(26333,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of metal sheet required to make a pipe with radius 0.07 m and length 100 m.\na) 44 cm³		b) 154 cm³		c) 44 cm²		d) none of these\n','','','c) 44 cm²		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26334,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A ---- is a solid bounded by six rectangular plane rigorous.\na) cube 		b) cylinder 		c) cone 		d) cuboid\n','','','d) cuboid','',0,0,0,NULL),(26335,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A solid cube is the part of the space enclosed by the ----- face of a cube.\na) 4 		b) 6 			c) 8 		d) 10 \n','','','b) 6 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26336,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Curved surface area of cylinder ------\na) 2πrh 		b) πrh 		c) 2πr²		d) r²h\n','','','a) 2πrh','',0,0,0,NULL),(26337,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The surface area of a sphere of radius 7 cm is ------\na) 49 cm²		b) 343 cm²		c) 616 cm²		d) 2401 cm²\n','','','c) 616 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26338,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Euler’s formula is F + V = E + -----\na) 2 			b) 3 			c) 1 			d) 0\n','','','a) 2 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26339,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the volume of cylinder if h = 7 m and r = 2.5 m\na) 137.5 m³		b) 2772 cm³		c) 27072 m³		d) 137.5 cm³\n','','','a) 137.5 m³	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26340,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For a prism, number of faces are 7, number of edges are fifteen, then number of vertices are ------\na) 10 			b) 12 			c) 9 			d) 17\n','','','a) 10 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26341,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The space occupied by solids is called its\na) Area 		b) surface area 		c) volume 		d) density\n','','','c) volume','',0,0,0,NULL),(26342,290,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The side of a cube is 4 m. If it is doubled, how many times will be the volume of the new cube, as compared with the original cube?\na) Two times 		b) Three times 		c) Four times 		d) Eight times\n','','','d) Eight times','',0,0,0,NULL),(26343,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the side of cube is 5 cm, then find its surface area.','','','Surface area of cube = 6l² = 6 × 5² = 6 × 25 = 150 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26344,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For a solid E = 10, F = 6, then find the number of vertices.','','','Euler’s formula, F + V = E + 2 \n∴ 6 + V = 10 + 2 \n∴ V = 12 – 6 = 6','',0,0,0,NULL),(26345,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for volume of cuboid.','','','Volume of cuboid = l × b × h','',0,0,0,NULL),(26346,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for total surface area of cuboid.','','','Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26347,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for vertical surface area of cuboid.','','','Vertical surface area of cuboid = 2(bh + lh)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26348,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for volume of cube.','','','Volume of cube = l³ = (side)³','',0,0,0,NULL),(26349,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for total surface area of cube.','','','Total surface area of cube = 6l²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26350,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for vertical surface area of cube.','','','Vertical surface area of cube = 4l²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26351,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for volume of cylinder.','','','Volume of cylinder = πr²h','',0,0,0,NULL),(26352,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for total surface area of cylinder.','','','Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26353,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for curved surface area of cylinder.','','','Curved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh','',0,0,0,NULL),(26354,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of base of cylinder.','','','Area of base of cylinder = πr²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26355,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Volume of cube is 216 cm³. Find its total surface area.','','','Given that, volume of cube = 216 cm³\n∴ l³ = 216 \n∴ l = 6 cm\nTotal surface area of cube = 6l² = 6 × 6² = 216 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26356,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the volume of cuboid with length 7 m, breadth 4.5 m and height 5 m.','','','Here, l = 7 m, b = 4.5 m, h = 5 m\nVolume of cuboid = l × b × h = 7 × 4.5 × 5 = 157.5 m³\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26357,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If r = 70 cm, h = 1.4 cm, find curved surface area of cylinder.','','','Here, r = 70 cm, h = 1.4 cm\nCurved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh\n= 2 × 22/7 × 70 × 1.4 = 616 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26358,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If r = 70 cm, h = 1.4 cm, total surface area of cylinder.','','','Here, r = 70 cm, h = 1.4 cm\nTotal surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h)\n= 2 × 22/7 × 70 × (70 + 1.4) = 31416 sq cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26359,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If r = 4.2 cm, h = 14 cm, total surface area of cylinder.','','','Here, r = 4.2 cm, h = 14 cm\nTotal surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h)\n= 2 × 22/7 × 4.2 × (4.2 + 14) = 480.48 sq cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26360,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If r = 4.2 cm, h = 14 cm, find curved surface area of cylinder.','','','Here, r = 4.2 cm, h = 14 cm\nCurved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh\n= 2 × 22/7 × 4.2 × 14 = 369.6 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26361,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If r = 4.2 cm, h = 5 cm, find volume of the cylinder.','','','Here, r = 4.2 cm, h = 5 cm\nVolume of cylinder = πr²h = 22/7 × 4.2 × 4.2 × 5 \n= 277.2 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26362,290,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If r = 5.6 cm, h = 5 cm, find volume of the cylinder.','','','Here, r = 5.6 cm, h = 5 cm\nVolume of cylinder = πr²h = 22/7 × 5.6 × 5.6 × 5 \n= 492.8 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26363,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the volume of a box, if its length, breadth and height are 20 cm, 10.5 cm and 8 cm respectively.','','','Given that, l = 20 cm, b = 10.5 cm, h = 8 cm\n∴ Volume of box = volume of cuboid = l × b × h \n= 20 × 10.5 × 8 = 1680 cc','',0,0,0,NULL),(26364,290,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Text Book','','A cuboid shape soap bar has volume 150 cc. Find its thickness if its length is 10 cm and breadth is 5 cm.','','','Given that, l = 10 cm, b = 5 cm, volume = 150 cc\nVolume of cuboid = l × b × h\n∴ 150 = 10 × 5 × h \n∴ h = 3 cm\nThus, thickness of soap bar is 3 cm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26365,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The total surface area of a cube is 294 cm². Find its volume.','','','Total surface area of cube = 6l²\n∴ 294 = 6 × l²\n∴ l² = 294/6 = 49\n∴ side (l) = 7\nVolume of cube = (side)³ = 7³ = 343 cm³','',0,0,0,NULL),(26366,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If r = 7 cm, h = 10 cm find curved surface area and total surface area of cylinder.','','','Here, r = 7 cm, h = 10 cm\ni) Curved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh = 2 × 22/7 × 7 × 10\n                             = 440 sq cm\nii) Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h) \n            = 2 × 22/7 × 7 × (7 + 10) = 748 sq cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26367,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If r = 1.4 cm, h = 2.1 cm find curved surface area and total surface area of cylinder.','','','Here, r = 1.4 cm, h = 2.1 cm\ni) Curved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh = 2 × 22/7 × 1.4 × 2.1\n                             = 18.48 sq cm\nii) Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h) \n            = 2 × 22/7 × 1.4 × (1.4 + 2.1) = 30.8 sq cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26368,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If r = 2.5 cm, h = 7 cm find curved surface area and total surface area of cylinder.','','','Here, r = 2.5 cm, h = 7 cm\ni) Curved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh = 2 × 22/7 × 2.5 × 7\n                             = 110 sq cm\nii) Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h) \n            = 2 × 22/7 × 2.5 × (2.5 + 7) = 149.29 sq cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26369,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If r = 10.5 cm, h = 8 cm, find volume of the cylinder.','','','Here, r = 10.5 cm, h = 8 cm\nVolume of cylinder = πr²h = 22/7 × 10.5 × 10.5 × 8 \n            = 2772 cc cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26370,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If r = 2.5 m, h = 7 m, find volume of the cylinder.','','','Here, r = 2.5 cm, h = 7 cm\nVolume of cylinder = πr²h = 22/7 × 2.5 × 2.5 × 7 \n            = 137.5 cc cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26371,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find how many liters of water will a cuboidal fish tank contain if its length, breadth and height are 1 m, 40 cm and 50 cm respectively.','','','The water contained in the tank is equal to volume of the tank\nLength of tank = 1 m = 100 cm, breadth = 40 cm, height = 50 cm\n∴ Volume of tank = l × b × h = 100 × 40 × 50 = 200000 cc\nVolume of water in the tank = 200000/1000 = 200 litre  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26372,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The length, breadth and height of cuboidal warehouse is 6 m, 4 m and 4 m respectively. How many cube shaped of side 40 cm will fill the warehouse completely?','','','Length of warehouse = 6 m = 600 cm, breadth = height = 4 m = 400 cm\nVolume of box = l³ = 40³ = 40 × 40 × 40 cc\nNumber of boxes = (volume of warehouse)/volume of a box\n= (600 × 400 × 400)/40 × 40 × 40 = 1500 \n ∴ 1500 boxes will fill the warehouse completely.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26373,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the volume of cylinder whose height is 10 cm and radius of base is 5 cm. (π = 3.14)','','','Radius of base of cylinder (r) = 5 cm and height (h) = 10 cm\nVolume of cylinder = πr²h = 3.14 × 5 × 5 × 10 = 785 cc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26374,290,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Height of a cylindrical drum is 56 cm. Find the radius of the drum if the capacity of that drum is 70.4 liter. (π = 22/7)','','','Let the radius of cylindrical drum be (r)\nCapacity of drum = volume of drum = 70.4 × 1000 cc\n1 lit = 1000 ml\n∴ 70.4 lit = 70400 ml\n∴ volume of water = πr²h = 70400\n∴ r² = (70400)/πh = (70400 × 7)/(22 × 56)\n       = 400\n∴ r = 20','',0,0,0,NULL),(26375,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How many bricks of length 25 cm, breadth 15 cm and height 10 cm are required to build a wall of length 6 m, height 2.5 m and breadth 0.5 m.','','','Given that for a brick, l = 25 cm, b = 15 cm, h = 10 cm\n∴ Volume of a brick (V₁) = l × b × h = 25 × 15 × 10 ----------- (i)\nFor the wall, L = 6 m = 600 cm, H = 2.5 m = 250 cm, B = 0.5 m = 50 cm\n∴ Volume of wall (V₂) = L × B × H = 600 × 250 × 50\nNo. of bricks required to build a wall = volume of wall/volume of brick\n= V₂/V₁ = (600 × 250 × 50)/(25 × 15 × 10) \n= 2000  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26376,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of a closed cylindrical drum if its diameter is 50 cm and height is 45 cm. (π = 3.14)','','','Given, diameter = 50 cm, radius = 25 cm and h = 45 cm\nTotal surface area of cylindrical drum = 2πr(h + r)\n= 2 × 3.14 × 25 × (25 + 45) = 10990 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26377,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Find the area of base and radius of a cylinder if its curved surface area is 660 sq. cm and height is 21 cm.','','','Given, curved surface area of cylinder = 660\n∴ 2πrh = 660\n∴ 2 × 22/7 × r × 21 = 660\n∴ r = 5 cm\n∴ Area of base = πr² = 22/7 × 5 × 5 = 78.57 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26378,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How much iron is needed to make a rod of length 90 cm and diameter 1.4 cm?','','','Given : length of rod = height = 90 cm\nDiameter = 1.4 cm ; radius = 0.7 cm\n∴ Iron needed to make a rod = volume of rod = πr²h \n= 22/7 × 0.7 × 0.7 × 90 = 138.6 cc cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26379,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How much water will a tank hold if the interior diameter of the tank is 1.6 m and its depth is 0.7m?','','','Given : diameter = 1.6 m, radius = 0.8 m and depth = height = 0.7 m\n∴ Capacity of tank = volume of tank = πr²h = 22/7 × 0.8 × 0.8 × 0.7\n= 1.408 cc m\n= 1.408 × 1000 = 1408 litre\n∴ Capacity of tank is 1408 liters.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26380,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If r = 3.5 cm, h = 20 cm, find curved surface area and total surface area of cylinders.','','','Here, r = 3.5, h = 20 cm\ni) curved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh = 2 × 22/7 × 3.5 × 20 \n               = 440 cm²\nii) Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h)\n= 2 × 22/7 × 3.5 × (3.5 + 20) = 517 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26381,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If r = 2.8 cm, h = 12 cm, find curved surface area and total surface area of cylinders.','','','Here, r = 2.8, h = 12 cm\ni) curved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh = 2 × 22/7 × 2.8 × 12  \n               = 211.2 cm²\nii) Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h)\n= 2 × 22/7 × 2.8 × (2.8 + 12) = 260.48 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26382,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If r = 4.2 cm, h = 7 cm, find curved surface area and total surface area of cylinders.','','','Here, r = 4.2, h = 7 cm\ni) curved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh = 2 × 22/7 × 4.2 × 7 \n               = 184.8 cm²\nii) Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h)\n= 2 × 22/7 × 4.2 × (4.2 + 7) = 295.68 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26383,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If r = 10.5 cm, h = 10 cm, find curved surface area and total surface area of cylinders.','','','Here, r = 10.5, h = 10 cm\ni) curved surface area of cylinder = 2πrh = 2 × 22/7 × 10.5 × 10 \n               = 660 cm²\nii) Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(r + h)\n= 2 × 22/7 × 10.5 × (10.5 + 10) = 1353 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26384,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The volume of a cylinder is 2512 cc and its height is 12.5 cm. Find radius of its base. (π = 3.14)','','','Given that, volume of cylinder = 2512 cc, h = 12.5 cm\nVolume of cylinder = πr²h\n∴ 3.14 × r² × 12.5 = 2512\n∴ r² = 2512/(3.14 × 12.5) = 2512/39.25 \n∴ r² = 64\n∴ r = 8 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26385,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If 5 liter molten mixture of khao and sugar is poured in a tray it fills to its full capacity. Find the length of the tray if its breadth is 40 cm and height is 2.5 cm.','','','Capacity of tray = 5 litre = 5000 cc\nVolume of mixture = 5000 cc\nVolume of rectangular tray = volume of mixture\n∴ l × b × h = 5000\n∴ l × 40 × 2.5 = 5000\n∴ length = 5000/100 = 50 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26386,290,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A water tank of cylindrical shape has diameter 1 m and height 2 m. Tank is closed with lid. The tank is to be painted internally and externally including the lid. Find the expenditure if the rate of painting is Rs. 80 per sq. m. (π = 3.14)','','','The tank is to be painted internally and externally. It means that the area to be painted is twice the total surface area.\nThe diameter of base of cylinder is 1 m\n∴ radius is 0.5 m and height is 2 m\n∴ Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(h + r)\n= 2 × 3.14 × 0.5 (2.0 + 0.5) = 7.85 sq m\n∴ The area of the surface to be painted = 2 × 7.85 = 15. 70 sq m \n∴ Total expenditure of painting the tank = 15.70 × 80 = ₹ 1256','',0,0,0,NULL),(26387,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','For rain water harvesting a tank of length 10 m, breadth 6 m and depth 3 m is built. What is the capacity of the tank? How many liters of water can it hold?','','','Given that, for the tank\nl = 10 m, b = 6 m, h = 3 m\n∴ Capacity of tank = volume of tank = l × b × h\n= 10 × 6 × 3 = 180 cc\n= 180 × 1000 liter = 1,80,000 litre\n∴ Thus, the capacity of tank is 1,80,000 liters.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26388,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Find the area of the sheet required to make a cylindrical container which is open at one side and whose diameter is 28 cm and height is 20 cm. Find the approximate area of the sheet required to make a lid of height 2 cm for this container.','','','Given, diameter = 28 cm, r = 14 cm and h = 20 cm\n∴ Area of tin required to make the container = curved surface area of cylinder + Area of base\n= 2πrh + πr² = πr (2h + r) = 22/7 × 14 × (2(20) + 14)\n= 44 × 54 = 2376 sq cm\n∴ 2376 sq cm tin is required to make the container.\nNow, for the lid, r = 14 cm, h₁ = 2 cm\n∴ Area of tin required to make the lid = Area of base + curved surface area of lid\n= πr² + 2πrh₁ = πr (r + 2h₁) = 22/7 × 14 × (14 + 2(2))\n= 44 × 18 = 792 sq cm\n∴ 792 sq cm tin is required to make the lid of the container.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26389,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Find the volume of the cylinder if the circumference of the cylinder is 132 cm and height is 25 cm.','','','Given, circumference of cylinder = 132\n∴ 2πr = 132\n∴ r = (132 × 7)/2 × 22 = 21 cm and h = 25 cm\n∴ volume of cylinder = πr²h = 22/7 × 21 × 21 × 25 \n= 66 × 525 = 34650 cu cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26390,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The diameter and height of a cylindrical tank is 1.75 m and 3.2 m respectively. How much is the capacity of tank in liter? (π = 22/7)','','','Given, for cylinder \nd = 1.75 m and h = 3.2 m\n∴ r = 1.75/2 = 0.875 m\n∴ Capacity of tank = volume of cylinder = πr²h \n= 22/7 × 0.875 × 0.875 × 3.2 = 22 × 0.125 × 0.875 × 3.2\n= 7.7 cu m\n= 7.7 × 1000 litre = 7700 liters. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26391,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A wall of length, breadth and height as 3 m × 2 m × 4 m is built using bricks measuring 50 cm × 20 cm × 10 cm. Find number of bricks required.','','','For brick : l = 50 cm, b = 20 cm, h = 10 cm\n∴ volume of brick = l × b × h = 50 × 20 × 10\nFor wall : L = 3 m = 300 cm, B = 2 m = 200 cm, H = 4 m = 400 cm\n∴ Volume of wall = L × B × H = 300 × 200 × 400\nNo. of bricks = Volume of wall/ volume of bricks\n= (300 × 200 × 400)/(50 × 20 × 10) = 2400','',0,0,0,NULL),(26392,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much metal sheet is sq. m will be required to make a closed cylinder of height 1 m and diameter, 140 cm?','','','Given that, height = length = 1 m \nDiameter = 140 cm = 1.4 m ; radius = 0.7 m\n∴ Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr(h + r)\n= 2 × 22/7 × 0.7 × (1 + 0.7) = 2 × 22 × 0.1 × 1.7 = 7.48 m²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26393,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The total surface area of a cylinder is 8448 sq. cm. If radius of its base is 28 cm, what is its height?','','','Given that, total surface area of cylinder = 8448 sq cm, r = 28 cm\n∴ Total surface area of cylinder = 2πr (r + h)\n∴ 8448 = 2 × 22/7 × 28 (28 + h)\n∴ 8448 = 2 × 22 × 4 × (28 + h)\n∴ 28 + h = 48\n∴ h = 48 – 28 = 20 cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26394,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A cylinder tank has a height of 40 cm and diameter of 70 cm. How many liters of water can it hold?','','','Given that, h = 40 cm, d = 70 cm; r = 35 cm\nVolume of cylinder = πr²h = 22/7 × 35 × 35 × 40\n= 22 × 5 × 35 × 40 = 154000\n∴ Capacity of tank = 154000/1000 = 154 liters.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26395,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A zinc sheet is of length 3.3 m and breadth 3 m. How many pipes of length 30 cm and radius 3.5 cm can be made from this sheet?','','','Area of rectangular sheet = length × breadth \n= 3.3 × 3 sq m = 330 × 300 sq cm\nLength of pipe is same as the height of cylinder = h = 30 cm\nRadius of pipe = Radius of base of cylinder = r = 3.5 cm,\nThe sheet required for a pipe = curved surface area of one pipe \n= 2πrh = 2 × 22/7 × 35/10 × 30/1 = 660 sq cm\nNumber of pipes made from the sheet\n= Area of sheet/Curved surface area of pipe\n= (330 × 300)/660 = 150\n∴ From the zinc sheet 150 pipes can be made.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26396,290,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Radius of a solid copper cylinder is 4.2 cm and its height is 16 cm. How many discs of diameter 1.4 cm and thickness 0.2 cm can be made from thus cylinder melting it.','','','Radius of the base of cylinder = R = 4.2 cm\nHeight of the cylinder = H = 16 cm\nVolume of cylinder = πR²H = π × 4.2 × 4.2 × 16 \nRadius of the base of the disc = 1.4 ÷ 2 = 0.7 cm\nThickness of disc = height of disc = 0.2 cm\nVolume of disc = πr²h = π × 0.7 × 0.7 × 0.2\nLet n discs be made from the molten cylinder\n∴ n × volume of one disc = volume of cylinder\n∴ n = (volume of cylinder)/(volume of one disc)\n       = (4.2 × 4.2 × 16)/(0.7 × 0.7 × 0.2)\n        = 2880\n∴ 2880 discs will be made from the cylinder.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26397,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ratio of radius and diameter of a circle is -------\na) 1 : 1 		b) 1 : 2 		c) 2 : 1 		d) 0\n','','','b) 1 : 2','',0,0,0,NULL),(26398,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---- is the longest chord of the circle.\na) radius 		b) tangent 		c) secant 		d) diameter\n','','','d) diameter','',0,0,0,NULL),(26399,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Measure of a minor arc is ------\na) 180⁰		b) less than 90⁰		c) less than 180⁰		d) 90⁰\n','','','c) less than 180⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26400,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Measure of circle is -------\na) can not be found 		b) 180⁰		c) 360⁰		d) 90⁰\n','','','c) 360⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26401,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A diameter of a circle divides circle into two -------\na) semicircles 		b) minor arcs 		c) major arcs            	d) unequal parts\n','','','a) semicircles 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26402,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of a chord of circle is 48 cm and radius 25 cm. Then its distance from the center is ----- cm.\na) 23 			b) 24 			c) 7 			d) 9\n','','','c) 7','',0,0,0,NULL),(26403,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Two chords AB and CD are congruent. m(arc AB) = 110⁰ then the central angle of chord CD is -------\na) 250⁰		b) 70⁰			c) 110⁰		d) 120⁰\n','','','c) 110⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26404,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The perpendicular drawn from the center of circle on a chord ----- the chord.\na) double of 		b) equals 		c) trisects 		d) bisects\n','','','d) bisects','',0,0,0,NULL),(26405,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The chords of a circle are congruent if they \na) makes equal angles at the center 		b) makes angles of 90⁰ at the center 		\nc) are parallel to each other 		                d) are perpendicular to each other.\n','','','a) makes equal angles at the center','',0,0,0,NULL),(26406,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A circle has radius 10 cm and a chord is at 6 cm from the center. Then length of the chord is -----\na) 8 cm 		b) 16 cm 		c) 12 cm 		d) 6 cm\n','','','b) 16 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26407,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the length of perpendicular drawn from center of a circle with radius ‘r’ on the chord of length ‘2m’\na) r² + m²			b) r² - m² 		c) m² - r²		d) r² - 4m²\n','','','b) r² - m²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26408,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The segment joining the center of a circle and midpoint of its chord is \na) parallel to the chord 		b) perpendicular to the chord 		c) bisects the chord		d) Intersects the chord\n','','','b) perpendicular to the chord 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26409,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' If the C is the center of the circle and ∠ACB = 60⁰ then its corresponding arc AXB = -------\na) 120⁰		b) 30⁰			c) 60⁰			d) 90⁰\n','','','c) 60⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26410,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If O is the center of the circle and ∠AOB and ∠BOC are central angles. If m∠AOB = 30⁰ and m∠BOC = 45⁰, then chord AB and chord BC are ------\na) congruent 		b) parallel 		c) perpendicular 		d) not congruent\n','','','d) not congruent','',0,0,0,NULL),(26411,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Measures of an arc is equal to the measure of -----\na) inscribed angle 			b) semicircle 		c) central angle 		d) circle\n','','','c) central angle 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26412,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the circle, chord AB is equal to the radius of circle the m(arc AB) = ----\na) 60⁰			b) 30⁰			c) 120⁰		d) 90⁰\n','','','a) 60⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26413,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The chords corresponding to congruent arcs are ------\na) parallel 		b) perpendicular 		c) intersecting 		d) congruent \n','','','d) congruent ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26414,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If O is the center of circle and ∠AOB is central angle with measure 35⁰, then its corresponding arc AB is of measure ------\na) 90⁰			b) 45⁰			c) 175⁰		d) 35⁰ 	\n','','','d) 35⁰ 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26415,291,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In figure, m(arc DAB) = -----','','','m(arc DAB) = 360⁰ - m(arc AXD) \n                 = 360 – 120 = 240⁰\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(26416,291,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a circle with center O, seg PQ is a chord by length 7 cm. seg OA Ʇ chord PQ, then find l(AP)?','','','Perpendicular drawn from the center of circle to chord, bisect the chord.\n∴ l(AP) = 1/2 × PQ = 1/2 × 7 = 3.5 cm\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(26417,291,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the corresponding chord of arc AXB and arc AYB.','','','chord AB','',1,0,0,NULL),(26418,291,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, if l(PM) = 4 cm then l(PQ) = -----','','','l(PQ) = 2 × l(PM) = 2 × 4 = 8 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26419,291,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, O is the center of the circle, radius OT divides arc MTN equally then m(arc MNT) = -----','','','radius OT divides are MTN equally \nBut arc MTN is semicircle\n∴ m(arc NT) = 90⁰\n∴ m(arc MNT) = 360 – 90 = 270⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(26420,291,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, m(arc AXM) = 60⁰ then ∠BOM = -------','','','Given that, m(arc AXM) = 60⁰\n∴ central angle ∠AOM = 60⁰\n∴ ∠BOM + ∠AOM = 180⁰ ---------- (linear pair)\n∴ ∠BOM = 180 – 60 = 120⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(26421,291,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Radius of circle with center O is 10 cm. find the length of the chord if the chord is at a distance of 6 cm from the center.','','','Let O be the centre of the circle and AB be the chord of the circle\nOM ꓕ AB\nIn ΔOMA, ∠OMA = 90⁰\n∴ OA² = OM² + AM²\n∴ AM² = OA² - OM² = 10² - 6² = 100 – 36 = 64\n∴ AM = 8 cm\n∴ Length of chord AB = 2AM = 2 × 8 = 16 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26422,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In a circle with center P, chord AB is drawn of length 13 cm, seg PQ Ʇ chord AB, then find l(QB).','','','The perpendicular drawn on a chord of circle from its center bisects the chord.\n∴ l(QB) = 1/2 × l(AB) = 1/2 × 13 = 6.5 cm\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(26423,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Radius of a circle with center O is 10 cm. Find the length of the chord if the chord is at a distance of 6 cm from the center.','','','Distance of the chord from the center of the circle is the length of perpendicular drawn from the center of the circle to the chord.\nAB is the chord of the circle with center O.\nSeg OP ꓕ chord AB\nRadius of circle = l(OB) = 10 cm, l(OP) = 6 cm','Now, ΔOPB is a right angled triangle,\nOP² + PB² = OB² \n∴ PB² = OB² - OP² = 10² - 6² = 100 – 36 = 64 \n∴ PB = 8 cm\nWe know that, the perpendicular drawn from the centre of the circle to the chord bisects the chord.\n∴ AB = 2 × PB = 2 × 8 = 16 cm',0,0,1,NULL),(26424,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The radius of circle is 5 cm. The distance of a chord from the center is 4 cm. Find the length of the chord.','','','Solution :','Given that, r = 5 cm, PQ = 4 cm, PQ ꓕ AB\nIn ΔPQA, AP² = PQ² + AQ²\n∴ AQ² = AP² - PQ² = 5² - 4² = 25 – 16 = 9\n∴ AQ = 3 cm\n∴ AB = 2 × AQ = 2 × 3 = 6 cm',0,0,1,NULL),(26425,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A chord of a circle is 30 cm long. Its distance from the center is 8 cm. Find the radius of the circle.','','','Given that, AB = 30 cm, PQ = 8 cm, PQ ꓕ AB\nPerpendicular drawn from the center of circle to chord, bisects the chord.\nAQ = 1/2 × AB = 1/2 × 30 = 15 cm','In ΔPQA, AP² = PQ² + AQ²\n∴ AP² = 8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 17\n∴ AP = 17 cm',0,0,1,NULL),(26426,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The radius of the circle is 13 cm. The length of chord 10 cm. Find the distance of the chord from the center of the circle.','','','Given that, AP = 13 cm, AB = 10 cm, PQ ꓕ AB\nPerpendicular drawn from the center of circle to chord, bisects the chord.\nAQ = 1/2 × AB = 1/2 × 10 = 5 cm','In ΔPQA, AP² = PQ² + AQ²\n∴ PQ² = 13² - 5² = 169 - 25 = 144\n∴ PQ = 12 cm',0,0,1,NULL),(26427,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, O is the center of the circle. Seg AB is the chord and seg OP Ʇ chord AB. If l(AB) = 3x and l(PB) = 27, then find the value of x.','','','We know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of the circle to chord, bisects the chord.\n∴ PB = 1/2 × AB \n∴ 27 = 3x \n∴ x = 27/3 = 9 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26428,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, P is the midpoint of chord AB. O is the center of the circle. m∠AOB = 60⁰, then find m∠OAP. Justify your steps.','','','In ΔOAB, OA = OB -------- [radii of same circle]\n∴ ΔOAB is isosceles triangle. \n∴ ∠OAP = ∠OBP  ---------- (i)\n∴ ∠AOB + ∠OAB + ∠OBA = 180⁰\n∴ ∠OAP + ∠OAP = 180 – 60 = 120⁰ ------- (from (i))\n∴ 2 ∠OAP = 120⁰\n∴ ∠OAP = 120/2 = 60⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(26429,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, arc AB ≅ arc CD and m∠AOB = 62⁰, then find m∠COD.','','','Given that, arc AB ≅ arc CD \n∴ ∠AOB = ∠COD = 62⁰ \nCongruent arcs made by congruent central angle.  ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26430,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Radius of a circle is 61 cm. Find the length of a chord which is at a distance of 11 cm from the center.','','','Given that, AP = 61 cm, PQ = 11 cm, PQ ꓕ AB\nIn ΔPQA, AP² = PQ² + AQ²\n','∴ AQ² = 61² - 11² = 3721 - 121 = 3600\n∴ AQ = 60 cm\n∴ AB = 2AQ = 2 × 60 = 120 cm',0,0,1,NULL),(26431,291,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A chord of a circle is at a distance of 15 cm from the center whose length is 16 cm. Then find radius of the circle.','','','Given that, PQ = 15 cm, AB = 16 cm, PQ ꓕ AB\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of the circle to chord, bisects the chord.\n∴ AQ = 1/2 × AB = 1/2 × 16 = 8 cm','In ΔPQA, AP² = PQ² + AQ²\n∴ AP² = 15² + 8² = 225 + 64 = 289\n∴ AP = 17 cm',0,0,1,NULL),(26432,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Radius of a circle with center O is 25 cm. Find the distance of a chord from the center if length of the chord is 48 cm.','','','The perpendicular drawn on a chord of a circle from its center bisects the chord.\n∴ PD = 1/2 CD = 1/2 × 48 = 24 cm\nIn ΔOPD, by Pythagoras theorem, \nOD² = OP² + PD²\n∴ OP² = OD² - PD² = 25² - 24² = 625 – 576 = 49\n∴ OP = 7 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26433,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','O is the center of the circle. Find the length of radius, if the chord length 24 cm is at a distance of 9 cm from the center of the circle.','','','The perpendicular drawn on a chord of a circle from its center bisects the chord.\n∴ AP = 1/2 AB = 1/2 × 24 = 12 cm\nIn ΔOPA, OA² = OP² + AP²\n∴ OA² = 9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225\n∴ OA = 15 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26434,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The diameters PQ and RS of the circle with center C are perpendicular to each other at C. State why arc PS and arc SQ are congruent? Write the other arcs which are congruent to arc PS.','','','Given that, Diameter PQ ꓕ diameter RS\n∴ ∠PCS = ∠SCQ = 90⁰\n∴ ∠PCS ≅ ∠SCQ\nAs arcs corresponding to congruent angles are congruent.\n∴ arc PS ≅ arc SQ\nSimilarly, we can prove that, arc PS ≅ arc SQ ≅ arc QR ≅ arc PR','',1,0,0,NULL),(26435,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the measure of a central angle is 120⁰, find the measure of corresponding minor arc and that of the major arc corresponding to this arc.','','','Central angle = corresponding minor arc = 120⁰\nMajor arc = 360 – corresponding minor arc \n                   = 360 – 120 = 240⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26436,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, diameters AD and BC intersect at point O, the center of the circle if m(arc AB) = 60⁰ then find m(arc CD) and m(arc CBA).','','','Central angle ∠AOB = m(arc AB) = 60⁰\nWe know that, ∠AOB = ∠COD ------------ [opposite angles]\n∴ ∠COD = 60⁰\n∴ m(arc CD) = ∠COD = 60⁰ \n∴ m(arc CBA) = m(arc CDB) + m(arc BA)        [m(arc CDB) = 180⁰  semicircle]\n∴ m(arc CBA) = 180 + 60 = 240⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(26437,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, O is the center of the circle. l(OM) = 11 cm, l(AB) = 120 cm, then find l(AD).','','','Given that, OM ꓕ AB\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of circle to chord bisects the chord.\n∴ AM = 1/2 × AB = 1/2 × 120 = 60 cm\nIn ΔOMA, OA² = OM² + AM² = 11² + 60² = 121 + 3600 = 3721\n∴ OA = 61\n∴ radius of circle (OA) = 61 cm\n∴ diameter of circle (AD) = 2r = 2 × 61 = 122 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26438,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the length of the chord of a circle which is at 10 cm from the center and whose radius is 26 cm.','','','Given that, PQ = 10 cm, PA = 26 cm, PQ ꓕ AB','In ΔPQA, AP² = PQ² + AQ²\n∴ AQ² = 26² - 10² = 676 - 100 = 576\n∴ AQ = 24 cm\n∴ AB = 2AQ = 2 × 24 = 48 cm',0,0,1,NULL),(26439,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a circle with center O, chord AB ≅ chord CD and m∠AOB = 70⁰ then find m∠COD.','','','Given that, chord AB ≅ chord CD\nCongruent chords made congruent arcs of a circle.\n∴ arc AB ≅ arc CD\nWe have, measure of central angle = corresponding minor arc\n∴ ∠AOB ≅ ∠COD\n∴ ∠COD = 70⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26440,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','For a Lazim Kawayat for girls of standard VIII are arranged in two circular rings as shown in the figure. If PA = 15, PR = 25, QR = 10, find RS, PQ, QT.','','','Given that, PA ꓕ RS\ni) In ΔPAR, PR² = PA² + RA²\n∴ RA² = PR² - PA² = 25² - 15² = 625 – 225 = 400\n∴ RA = 20 cm\n∴ RS = 2 × RA = 2 × 20 = 40 cm\nii) QA = QR + RA = 10 + 20 = 30 cm\nIn ΔPQA, PQ² = QA² + PA² \n∴ PQ² = 30² + 15² = 900 + 225 = 1125\n∴ PQ = √1125 cm\niii) QT = 2QA = 2 × 30 = 60 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26441,291,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','P is the center of the circle whose radius is 10 cm. Find the distance of the chord from the center if the length of the chord is 12 cm. ','','','Given that, PA = 10 cm, AB = 12 cm, PQ ꓕ AB\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of the circle to chord, bisects the chord.\n∴ AQ = 1/2 × 12 = 1/2 × 12 = 6 cm','In ΔPQA, AP² = PQ² + AQ²\n∴ PQ² = 10² - 6² = 100 - 36 = 64\n∴ PQ = 8 cm',0,0,1,NULL),(26442,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','C is the center of the circle whose radius is 10 cm. Find the distance of the chord from the center if the length of the chord is 12 cm.','','','Given that, CA = 10 cm, AB = 12 cm, CM ꓕ AB\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of the circle to chord, bisects the chord.\n∴ AM = 1/2 × 12 = 1/2 × 12 = 6 cm\nIn ΔCMA, CA² = AM² + CM²\n∴ CM² = 10² - 6² = 100 - 36 = 64\n∴ CM = 8 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26443,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In the adjoining figure, O is the center of the circle whose diameter is MN. Measure of some central angles are given in the figure. Hence find : i) m∠AOB and m∠COD 		ii) Show that arc AB ≅ arc CD		iii) Show that chord AB ≅ chord CD.','','','i) Given that, MN is diameter of circle\n∴ ∠MOA + ∠AOB + ∠BON = 180⁰ --------- [angles in linear pair]\n∴ 100 + ∠AOB + 35 = 180\n∴ ∠AOB = 180 – 135 = 45⁰\nSimilarly, ∠MOD + ∠COD + ∠CON = 180⁰\n∴ 100 + ∠COD + 35 = 180⁰\n∴ ∠COD = 180 – 135 = 45⁰\nii) ∠AOB = ∠COD = 45⁰\nThus, arc AB ≅ arc CD -------- [arcs corresponding to congruent angles]\niii) Chord AB is corresponding chord of arc AB and chord CD is corresponding chord of arc CD.\nBut arc AB ≅ arc CD\nThus, their corresponding chords are also congruent.\n∴  chord AB ≅ chord CD.','',1,0,0,NULL),(26444,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of a chord of a circle of 16.8 cm, radius is 9.1 cm. Find its distance from the center.','','','Given that, AB = 16.8 cm, PM ꓕ AB\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from center of circle to chord bisect the chord.\n∴ AM = 1/2 × AB = 1/2 × 16.8 = 8.4 cm\nIn ΔPMA, PA² = AM² + PM²\n∴ PM² = PA² - AM² = (9.1)² - (8.4)² = 82.81 – 70.56 = 12.25\n∴ PM = 3.5 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26445,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, O is the center of the circle, M is the midpoint of chord AB. l(AM) = 2x, l(MB) = 8, l(OM) = 6 and l(OA) = y, then find the values of x and y. also find the length of the longest chord of the circle.','','','Given that, OM ꓕ AB\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of circle to chord bisects the chord.\n∴ AM = MB \n∴ 2x = 8 \n∴ x = 4 \nIn ΔOMA, OA² = AM² + OM²\n∴ y² = 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100\n∴ y = 10 cm\n∴ radius of circle (y) = 10 cm\nLongest chord of circle is a diameter of circle \n∴ diameter = 2 × 10 = 20 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26446,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the figure, O is the center of the circle, m∠AOB = 28⁰, m∠COD = 58⁰, then find : i) m(arc CYD) 		ii) m(arc AMB) 		iii) m(arc ADC) 		iv) m(arc BC)','','','i) m(arc CYD) = ∠COD  = 58⁰ ------- [central angle = corresponding minor arc]\nii) m(arc AMB) = ∠AOB = 28⁰ \niii) m(arc ADC) = m(arc AXD) + m(arc DYC)\n                           = 180 + 58 = 238⁰ ---------- [m(arc AXD) is a semicircle]\niv) m(arc BC) = 180 – m(arc AMB) – m(arc CYD) \n                        = 180 – 28 – 58 = 94⁰','',1,0,0,NULL),(26447,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Using the information given in figure, find the pairs of congruent chords.','','','Given that, ∠COD = 65⁰  and ∠AOE = 65⁰\nWe know that, central angle = corresponding minor arcs\n∴   ∠COD = arc CD       and ∠AOE = arc AE\n∴ arc CD ≅ arc AE\nWe know that, in a circle, congruent arc made by congruent chords.\n∴ chord CD ≅ chord AE\nSimilarly, ∠COB = ∠AOB = 90⁰\n∴ arc AB ≅ arc CB\n∴ chord AB ≅ chord CB.','',1,0,0,NULL),(26448,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the adjoining figure, O is the center of a circle AB = 16 cm, CD = 14 cm, seg OM Ʇ seg AB, seg ON Ʇ seg CD. If OM = 6 cm then find seg ON.','','','Given that, OM ꓕ AB\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of circle to chord bisects the chord.\n∴ AM = 1/2 × AB = 1/2 × 16 = 8 cm  and CN = 1/2 × CD = 1/2 × 14 = 7 cm\nIn ΔOMA, OA² = OM² + AM² = 6² + 8² = 36 + 84 = 100\n∴ OA = 10 cm\n∴ radius of circle = 10 cm\n∴ In ΔONC, OC² = ON² + CN²\n∴ ON² = OC² - CN² = 10² - 7² = 100 – 49 = 51\n∴ ON = √51 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26449,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If chord PQ of a circle have length equal to the radius, then find the distance of chord from center in terms of radius.','','','Solution : ','Given that, length of chord = radius of circle = r\nOA ꓕ PQ\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of circle to chord bisects the chord.\n∴ PA = 1/2 × PQ = r/2\nIn ΔOAP, OP² = OA² + PA²\n∴ OA² = OP² - PA² = r² - (r/2)² = r² - r²/4 \n∴ OA² = (4r² - r²)/4 = 3r²/4 \n∴ OA = √3 r/2',0,0,1,NULL),(26450,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a circle with center P, seg AB is smaller than the sum of the side PA and side PB by 4 cm. If perimeter of ΔPAB is 144 cm then find the length of side PM.\n','','','Given that, length of AB is smaller than sum of PA and PB by 4 cm\n∴ from figure, AB = 2x – 4 \nPM ꓕ AB\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of circle to chord bisects the chord.\n∴ AM = 1/2 × AB = 1/2 × (2x – 4) = (x – 2) cm\nIn ΔPMA, PA² = PM² + AM²\n∴ PM² = PA² - AM² = x² - (x – 2)²\n            = x² - (x² - 4x + 4) = x² - x² + 4x + 4 = 4x + 4\n∴  PM² = 4 (x + 1)\n∴ PM = 2√(x + 1) cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26451,291,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The radius of a circle with center O is 41. The length of the chord PQ of same circle is 80. Determine the distance of chord PQ from center O.','','','Solution : ','Given that, OA ꓕ PQ\nWe know that, perpendicular drawn from the center of circle to chord bisect the chord.\n∴ PA = 1/2 × PQ = 1/2 × 80 = 40 cm\nIn ΔOPA, OP² = OA² + PA²\n∴ OA² = OP² - PA² = 41² - 40² = 1681 – 1600 = 81 \n∴ OA = 9 cm',0,0,1,NULL),(26452,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If arc AXB and arc AYB are the corresponding arcs and m(arc AXB) = 120⁰, then m(arc AYB) = ?\na) 140⁰		b) 60⁰			c) 240⁰		d) 160⁰\n','','','c) 240⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26453,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The value of π in fractional form is taken as -----\na) 22/7 		b) 23/7 		c) 20/7 		d) 40/7\n','','','a) 22/7 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26454,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ratio of circumference of a circle to its radius is -----\na) 2π 			b) 4π/3 	           	c) π			d) π/2\n','','','c) π		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26455,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If radius of a circle is 7 cm, then its circumference is -----\na) 22 cm 		b) 44 cm 		c) 21 cm 		d) 88 cm\n','','','b) 44 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26456,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The measure of a semicircular arc is ------\na) 0⁰			b) 90⁰			c) 360⁰		d) 180⁰\n','','','d) 180⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26457,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the measure of minor arc is 135⁰ then find the measure of its corresponding major arc.\na) 135⁰		b) 235⁰		c) 45⁰			d) 225⁰\n','','','d) 225⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26458,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A chord that passes through the center of a circle is -----\na) diameter 		b) segment 		c) radius 		d) center\n','','','a) diameter ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26459,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The number of arcs made by a chord on a circle is ------\na) 3 			b) 2 			c) 1 			d) 4\n','','','b) 2 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26460,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diameter of a circle passes through -----\na) The center 		b) The circumference 		c) Any where in the interior 		d) Always two points\n','','','a) The center 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26461,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The shortest path between any two points is called -----\na) line segment 		b) angle 		c) curve 		d) chord\n','','','d) chord','',0,0,0,NULL),(26462,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The relation between radius (r) and diameter (d) of a circle is -----\na) d = 2 × r			b) d = r/2 		c) d = r/3 		d) d = 3r\n','','','a) d = 2 × r','',0,0,0,NULL),(26463,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The chord of a circle which passes through the center is ----- the radius.\na) thrice		b) double		c) half 		d) equal to\n','','','c) half 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26464,292,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The distance around the circle is known as ------\na) diameter 			b) radius 		c) circumference		d) chord\n','','','c) circumference	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26465,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the circumference of a circle with diameter 7 cm?','','','Circumference = πd = 22/7 × 7 = 22 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26466,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of the longest chord of a circle is 10 cm. What is the length of its radius?','','','Longest chord of circle is the diameter of circle.\n∴ d = 10 cm\n∴ r = d/2 = 10/2 = 5 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26467,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the measure of a semicircle?','','','Measure of semicircle is 180⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26468,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the ratio of the circumference of a circle to its diameter?','','','Circumference/diameter = πd/d = π','',0,0,0,NULL),(26469,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The measure of a major arc is 270⁰. What is the measure of the corresponding minor arc?','','','Minor arc = 360 – 270 = 90⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26470,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If r = 7 cm then find diameter and circumference of circle.','','','Circumference of circle = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 7 = 44 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26471,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a circle the measure of major arc is 230⁰, find the measure of its corresponding minor arc.','','','Minor are = 360 – major arc = 360 – 230 = 130⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26472,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If measure of central angle of a circle is 100⁰, then find the measure of corresponding minor arc.','','','Measure of minor arc = central angle = 100⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26473,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If measure of central angle of circle is 70⁰, then find the measure of corresponding major arc.','','','Measure of major arc = 360 – minor arc \n           = 360 – 70 = 290⁰        [central angle = minor arc]\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26474,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the relation between radius and diameter of circle?','','','radius of circle = diameter/2 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26475,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the measure of one complete circle?','','','Measure of complete circle = 360⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26476,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If OA = 30 cm, then find the radius OB (in cm), where O is the center of circle.','','','Radius OA = OB = 30 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26477,292,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If OA = 12 cm, then find the length of PQ (in cm), where O is the center of circle.','','','OA = 12 cm ; radius of circle\n∴ PQ is a diameter of circle\n∴ PQ = 2 × 12 = 24 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26478,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If d = 28 cm then find radius and circumference of circle.','','','Circumference of circle = πd = 22/7 × 28 = 88 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26479,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If circumference of circle is 616 cm, then find radius and diameter.','','','Circumference of circle = 616 cm\n∴ 2πr = 616\n∴  r = (616 × 7)/22 × 2 = 98 cm\n∴ d = 2r = 2 × 98 = 196 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26480,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If circumference of a circle is 176 cm. Find its radius.','','','Circumference of circle = 616 cm\n∴ 2πr = 176\n∴  r = (176 × 7)/22 × 2 = 28 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26481,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The diameter of a circle is 14 cm. Find its circumference.','','','Given that, d = 14 cm\nCircumference of circle = πd = 22/7 × 14 = 44 cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26482,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The radius of a circle is 35 cm. Find its circumference.','','','Given that, r = 35 cm\n∴ circumference = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 35 = 220 cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26483,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The circumference of a circle is 198 cm. Find its radius and diameter.','','','Circumference = 198\n∴ 2πr = 198\n∴ r = (198 × 7)/22 × 2 = 31.5 cm\n∴ d = 2r = 2 × 31.5 = 63 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26484,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If m(arc PQR) = 140⁰, find the measure of its corresponding arc PYR.','','','m(arc PYR) = 360 – m(arc PQR)\n                           = 360 – 140 = 220⁰\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26485,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the circumference of a circle is 308 cm, find its diameter.','','','Circumference = 308 cm\n∴ πd = 308\n∴ d = (308 × 7)/22 = 98 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26486,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diameter of circle is 5.6 cm. Find its circumference.','','','Circumference = πd = 22/7 × 5.6 = 17.6 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26487,292,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, O is the center of the circle. i) Name the all chords of the circle	ii) Name all radii of the circle   	iii) Name a chord, which is not the diameter of the circle','','','i) chord CP, chord AB\nii) OA, OB, OC, OP\niii) chord CP','',1,0,0,NULL),(26488,292,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If circumference of circle is 72.6 cm, then find radius and diameter of circle.','','','Circumference = 72.6 cm\n∴ 2πr = 72.6 \n∴ r = (72.6 × 7)/2 × 22 = 11.55 cm\n∴ d = 2r = 2 × 11.55 = 23.10 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26489,292,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The radius of a circular garden is 56 m. What is the cost of four layered fence around this garden at the rate of Rs. 40 per meter?','','','Here, r = 56 m\nWe know that, length of wire required to put 1 round of fence = circumference\n∴ c = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 56 = 352 m\nThus, length of wire required to put 4 round of fence = 4 × 352 = 1408 m\nHence, cost to put 4 round fence = length of wire × rate \n          = 1408 × 40 = ₹ 56320','',0,0,0,NULL),(26490,292,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The circumference of a circle is 62.80 cm. Taking π = 3.14, find its diameter.','','','Circumference = 62.80 cm\n∴ πd = 62.80 \n∴ d = (62.80)/3.14 = 20\n∴ diameter = 20 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26491,292,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The radius of circular plot is 7.7 meters. How much will it cost to fence the plot with 3 rounds of wire at the rate of 50 rupees per meter?','','','Circumference of circular plot = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 7.7 = 48.4\nLength of wire required for one round of fencing = 48.4 m.\nCost of one round of fence = length of wire × cost per meter\n            = 48.4 × 50 = 2420 rupees.\nCost of 3 rounds of fencing = 3 × 2420 = 7260 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26492,292,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In a circle with center O, the measure of a minor arc is 110⁰, what is the measure of the major arc PYQ? ','','','Major arc PYQ = 360 – measure of minor arc = 360 – 110 = 250⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26493,292,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If diameter of circle is 28 cm, then find its radius and circumference.','','','Here, d = 28 cm\n∴ r = d/2 = 28/2 = 14 cm\nCircumference of circle = πd = 22/7 × 28 = 22 × 4 = 88 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26494,292,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the circumference of circle is 176 cm, find its radius.','','','Circumference of circle = 176 cm\n∴ 2πr = 176\n∴  r = (176 × 7)/(2 × 22) = 28 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26495,292,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, name the following : i) center of the circle 	ii) radii of the circle 		iii) diameters of the circle 	iv) chords of the circle	v) Two minor and major arcs','','','i) O be the center of circle\nii) Radii of circle AO, OC, OB, OE\niii) diameters of circle : AC, BE\niv) Chords of circle : AB, BD\nv) Minor arcs : CD, ED, AE, AB, BC\n    Major arcs : ABD, CEB','',1,0,0,NULL),(26496,292,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The diameter of the wheel of a bullock cart is 1.4 m. How many rotations will the wheel complete as the cart travels 1.1 km?','','','Here, d = 1.4 m\nWe have, c = πd\n∴ c = 22/7 × 1.4 = 22 × 0.2 = 4.4 m\nNow, the distance covered in one rotation of bullock cart = Circumference of its wheel = 4.4 m\nHence, No. of rotations = (Distance covered)/circumference\n         = (1.1 km)/4.4m = (1.1 × 1000)/4.4 = 250\nHence, the wheel of bullock cart completes 250 rotations to travel a distance of 1.1 km.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26497,292,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The radius of the wheel of a bus is 0.7 m. How many rotations will a wheel complete while travelling a distance of 22 km?','','','Circumference = πd\n                                = 22/7 × 0.7 = 2.2 m\nWhen the wheel completes one rotation it crosses a distance of 2.2 m\nTotal no. of rotations = distance/circumference\n       = 22000/2.2 = 220000/22 = 10000\nA wheel completes 10000 rotations to cover the distance of 22 km.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26498,292,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Some arcs are shown in the circle with center ‘O’. Write the names of the minor arcs, major arcs and semicircular arcs from among them.','','','i) Minor arcs : arc PXQ, arc PR, arc RY, arc QY, arc PX, arc QX\nii) Major arcs : are PQR, arc XQR, arc XRQ, arc QRP, arc PQX\niii) Semicircular arcs : arc QPR, arc QYR','',1,0,0,NULL),(26499,292,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diameter of a circular garden is 175 m, find the cost of 5 round of fence around it at the rate of Rs. 12 per meter?','','','Given that, d= 175 m, \n∴ c = πd = 22/7 × 175 = 550 m\n∴ Cost of 5 rounds = 5 × 12 × 550 = 60 × 550 = ₹ 33000','',0,0,0,NULL),(26500,292,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the circumference of a circle is 34.54 cm, find its radius. (π = 3.14)','','','Circumference of circle = 34.54 cm\n∴ c = 2πr\n∴ 34.54 = 2 × 3.14 × r\n∴ r = 34.54/6.28 = 5.5 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26501,292,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A wheel of bus has diameter 42 cm. Find the distance covered by the bus in 2500 rotations of the wheel.','','','Given that, d = 42 cm\nCircumference = πd = 22/7 × 42 = 132 cm\n∴ Distance covered = No. of rotations × diameter = 2500 × 132 = 330,000 cm\n∴ Distance = 330,000/1000 × 100 = 3.3 km','',0,0,0,NULL),(26502,292,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the circumference of a circle is 251.2, find the diameter of the circle. (π = 3.14)','','','Circumference of circle = 251.2\n∴ c = πd\n∴ d = 251.2/3.14 = 80','',0,0,0,NULL),(26503,292,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diameter of the wheel of a cart is 1.05 m. How much distance will the cart cover in 1000 rotations of the wheel?','','','Given that, d = 1.05 m\nCircumference = πd = 22/7 × 1.05 = 3.3 m\n∴ Distance covered = No. of rotations × diameter = 1000 × 3.3 = 3300 m\n∴ Distance = 3300/1000  = 3.3 km','',0,0,0,NULL),(26504,292,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given circle, find the following : i) three radii 		ii) three chords	\niii) a diameter 	          iv) a triangle that has the center of the circle as vertex.','','','i) Radii of circle : OA, OB, OC\nii) Chords : AC, CB, AB\niii) diameter : AB\niv) In ΔAOC and ΔOCB; O is the center of circle.','',1,0,0,NULL),(26505,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the length and breadth of a rectangle both are doubled, its perimeter ----\na) remains the same 		b) become double 		c) becomes triple 		d) becomes 4 times\n','','','b) become double','',0,0,0,NULL),(26506,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The sum of all sides of a closed figure is its -----\na) perimeter 		b) Area 		c) surface area 		d) volume\n','','','a) perimeter 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26507,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The perimeter of a square whose side is of length 8 cm is -----\na) 16 cm 		b) 64 cm 		c) 32 cm 		d) 24 cm\n','','','c) 32 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26508,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The total surface area of a cube whose side is 6 cm is ------\na) 216 cm²		b) 36 cm²		c) 256 cm²		d) 244 cm²\n','','','a) 216 cm²	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26509,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The total surface area of cuboid is given by ------\na) (lb + bh + lh) 		b) 3(lb + bh + lh) 		c) 2(lb + bh) 		d) 2(lb + bh + lh)\n','','','d) 2(lb + bh + lh)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26510,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the altitude of triangle whose base is 40 cm and area is 240 cm²?\na) 8 cm 		b) 12 cm 		c) 14 cm 		d) 10 cm\n','','','b) 12 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26511,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the cost of painting a wall with dimension 25 m by 9 m at the rate of Rs. 120 per square meter.\na) Rs. 25000		b) Rs. 24500			c) Rs. 29450 		d) Rs. 27000\n','','','d) Rs. 27000','',0,0,0,NULL),(26512,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the length and the width of a rectangle are each doubled, by what percent is the area increased?\na) 75% 		b) 100% 		c) 300% 		d) 400%\n','','','b) 100% 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26513,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of square of side 12 cm is -----\na) 144 cm 		b) 144 sq cm		c) 48 cm 		d) 48 sq cm\n','','','b) 144 sq cm		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26514,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Each side of a square measures 5.6 cm. The perimeter of the square is -----\na) 23.5 cm 		b) 22 cm 		c) 22.4 cm 		d) 22.4 cm²\n','','','c) 22.4 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26515,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many cm² are there in 1 m²?\na) 100			b) 1000		c) 10			d) 10000\n','','','d) 10,000','',0,0,0,NULL),(26516,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The distance around the closed figure is called as -----\na) volume 		b) area 		c) Perimeter 		d) none of these\n','','','c) Perimeter 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26517,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the perimeter of an isosceles triangle with equal sides 9 cm each and the third side 5 cm?\na) 40 cm 		b) 23 cm 		c) 22 cm 		d) 69 cm\n','','','b) 23 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26518,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of square with perimeter 40 cm is -----\na) 1600 cm²		b) 100 cm²		c) 10 cm²		d) 250 cm²\n','','','b) 100 cm²	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26519,293,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The cost of fencing a 15 m × 7 m rectangular park at Rs. 45 per meter is -----\na) Rs. 4275 		b) Rs. 4725 		c) Rs. 1980 		d) Rs. 5427 \n','','','b) Rs. 4725 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26520,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for perimeter of square.','','','Perimeter of square = 4 × side','',0,0,0,NULL),(26521,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for perimeter of rectangle.','','','Perimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]','',0,0,0,NULL),(26522,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The perimeter of rectangle is 64 cm. If its length is 17 cm, what is its breadth?','','','Perimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]\n∴  64 = 2[17 + breadth]\n∴  breadth + 17 = 32\n∴ breadth = 32 – 17 = 15 cm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26523,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of square.','','','Area of square = (side)²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26524,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of rectangle.','','','Area of rectangle = length × breadth','',0,0,0,NULL),(26525,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If the side of a square is 12 cm, find its area.','','','Area of square = (side)² = (12)² = 144 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26526,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If the length of a rectangle is 15 cm and breadth is 5 cm, find its area.','','','Area of rectangle = length × breadth = 15 × 5 = 75 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26527,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of right-angled triangle.','','','Area of right angled triangle = 1/2 × Product of perpendicular sides','',0,0,0,NULL),(26528,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of triangle.','','','Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height','',0,0,0,NULL),(26529,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for surface area of cuboid.','','','Surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26530,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for surface area of cube.','','','Surface area of cube = 6l² = 6 × (side)²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26531,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 3 cm.','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × 3² = 54 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26532,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 5 cm.','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × 5² = 150 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26533,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 7.2 m.','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × (7.2)² = 311.04 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26534,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 6.8 m.','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × (6.8)² = 277.44 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26535,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 5.5 m.','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × (5.5)² = 181.5 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26536,293,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the base of a triangle is 28 cm and height is 14 cm, find its area.','','','Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 28 × 14 = 196 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26537,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The perimeter of a rectangle of length 28 cm and breadth 20 cm is equal to the perimeter of a square. What is the length of the side of that square?','','','Perimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]\n              = 2(28 + 20) = 96\nIf the side of that square is a then 4a = 96\n∴ 4a = 96\n ∴ a = 96/4 = 24\n∴ Side of square is 24 cm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26538,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the length and breadth of a rectangle are doubled, how many times the perimeter of the old rectangle will that of the new rectangle be?','','','Let the original length and breadth of the rectangle are x and y respectively\n∴ Perimeter of original rectangle = 2x + 2y\nNow when length and breadth are doubled\ni.e. 2x and 2y then\nPerimeter of new rectangle = 2(2x + 2y) = 2 × 2 (x + y)\n        = 2 × perimeter of original rectangle','',0,0,0,NULL),(26539,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the side of a square is tripled, how many times the perimeter of the first square will that of the new square be?','','','Let the side of old square is x\n∴ Perimeter of old square = 4x\nNow the side of new square is 3x i.e. triple of old square\n∴ Perimeter of new square = 4 × 3x = 3 × 4x\n           = 3 × Perimeter of old square\nHence, the perimeter of new square is 3 times the perimeter of old square.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26540,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The area of a rectangle is 102 sq cm. If its length is 17 cm, what is its perimeter?','','','Here, area of rectangle = 102 sq cm, l = 17 cm\nWe have, Area of rectangle = l × b\n∴ 102 = 17 × b\n∴ b = 102/17 = 6 cm\nHence, perimeter of rectangle = 2l + 2b = 2(17) + 2(6) = 34 + 12 = 46 cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26541,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the side of a square is tripled, how many times will its area be as compared to the area of the original square?','','','Let side of original square is x\n∴ Area of original square = (side)² = x²\nNow, when side is tripled\nSide of new square = 3x\n∴ Area of new square = (side)² = (3x)² = 9x² = 9 × area of original square\nThus, when side of a square is tripled its area will be 9 times the area of original square.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26542,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the sides that form the right angle of a triangle are 3.5 cm and 4.2 cm long, find the area of the triangle.','','','Area of right angles triangle = 1/2 × product of perpendicular sides\n       = 1/2 × 3.5 × 4.2 = 7.35 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26543,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the base of the triangle is 5.6 cm and height is 4.5 cm, what is its area?','','','Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 5.6 × 4.5 = 12.6 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26544,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','A page of calendar is 45 cm long and 26 cm wide. What is its area?','','','The shape of page of a calendar is rectangular.\n∴ l = 45 cm, b = 26 cm\n∴ Area of page = Area of rectangle = l × b = 45 × 26 = 1170 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26545,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is the area of a triangle with base 4.8 cm and height 3.6 cm?','','','Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height \n                     = 1/2 × 4.8 × 3.6 = 8.64 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26546,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much sheet metal is required to make a closed rectangular box of length 1.5 m, breadth 1.2 m and height 1.3 m?','','','A closed rectangular box is a cuboid shape.\n∴ Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(1.5 × 1.2 + 1.2 × 1.3 + 1.5 × 1.3) = 2(1.8 + 1.56 + 1.95)\n        = 2(5.31) = 10.62 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(26547,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cuboids of length, breadth and height as given : 12 cm, 10 cm, 5 cm','','','Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(12 × 10 + 10 × 5 + 12 × 5) = 2(120 + 50 + 60)\n        = 2(230) = 460 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26548,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cuboids of length, breadth and height as given : 5 cm, 3.5 cm, 1.4 cm','','','Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(5 × 3.5 + 3.5 × 1.4 + 1.4 × 5) = 2(17.5 + 4.9 + 7)\n        = 2(29.4) = 58.8 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26549,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cuboids of length, breadth and height as given : 2.5 m, 2 m, 2.4 m','','','Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(2.5 × 2 + 2 × 2.4 + 2.4 × 2.5) = 2(5 + 4.8 + 6)\n        = 2(15.8) = 31.6 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(26550,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cuboids of length, breadth and height as given : 8 m, 5 m, 3.5 m','','','Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(8 × 5 + 3.5 × 5 + 8 × 3.5) = 2(40 + 17.5 + 28)\n        = 2(85.5) = 171 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(26551,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of right-angled triangle whose sides making right angles are 8 cm and 10 cm.','','','Area of right angled triangle = 1/2 × product of perpendicular sides\n               = 1/2 × 10 × 8 = 40 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26552,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the length of cube is doubled, how many times its surface area becomes?','','','If the length of side be x\nSurface area of cube = 6(side)² = 6x²\nNow, length of cube is double then new length = 2x\n∴ Area of new cube = 6 × (2x)² = 6 × 4 × x² \n               = 4 × 6x² = 4 × surface area of old cube','',0,0,0,NULL),(26553,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the area of rectangle is 102 sq cm and its breadth is 6 cm, find its perimeter.','','','Area of rectangle = 102\n∴ Area of rectangle = length × breadth \n∴ 102 = 6 × length \n∴ length = 17 cm\n∴ Perimeter of rectangle = 2[l + b]\n                 = 2[17 + 6] = 2 × 23 = 46 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26554,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cube whose side is 5.5 cm.','','','Total surface area of cube = 6l² = 6 × (5.5)² = 6 × 30.25 = 181.5 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26555,293,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of sheet 50 cm long and 25 cm wide.','','','Area of sheet = area of rectangle = length × breadth\n           = 50 ×  25 = 1250 cm²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26556,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Given alongside is the diagram of a playground. It shows the length of its sides. Find the perimeter of the playground.','','','Solution : ','From figure, AF = GE = GD + DE \n∴ AF = BC + DE ---------- (BC = GD)\n∴ AF = 15 + 15 = 30 m and EF = AG = AB + BG \n∴ EF = 10 + 5 = 15 m\nNow, perimeter of playground = AB + BC + CD + DE + EF + AF \n           = 10 + 15 + 5 + 15 + 15 + 30 = 90 m\n∴ Perimeter of playground is 90 m.',1,0,1,NULL),(26557,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The length and the width of a mobile phone are 13 cm and 7 cm respectively. It has a screen PQRS as shown in the figure. What is the area of screen?','','','ABCD is the rectangular formed by the edges of the mobile. PQRS is the rectangle formed by leaving a 1.5 cm wide edge alongside AB, BC and DC and a 2 cm edge alongside DA.\nLength of rectangle PQRS = 9.5 cm and breadth = 4 cm\nArea of screen = Area of rectangle PQRS = 9.5 × 4 = 38 sq m','',1,0,0,NULL),(26558,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is the value of rectangular plot of land 75.5 m long and 30.5 m broad at the rate of 1000 rupees per square meter?','','','Here, l = 75.5 m, b = 30.5 m\n∴ Area of plot = l × b = 75.5 × 30.5 = 2302.75 sq m\nAs the rate of plot is ₹ 1000 per sq m\nCost of the plot = Area × rate = 2302.75 × 1000 = ₹ 2302750\nThus, the value of plot is ₹ 2302750','',0,0,0,NULL),(26559,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Find the perimeter and area of garden with measures as shown in the figure alongside.','','','Name the figure of garden as shown','Now, Perimeter of garden = sum of lengths of all sides\n        = AB + BC + CD + DE + EF + FG + GH + HI + IJ + JK + KL + AL \n        = 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13\n         = 156 m\nAnd from figure, \nArea of the garden = 5 × area of square with side 13 \n          = 5 × (13)² = 5 × 169 = 845 sq m\nThus, perimeter of garden is 156 m and its area is 845 sq m ',1,0,1,NULL),(26560,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','One side of a cubic box is 0.4 m. How much will it cost to paint the outer surface of the box at the rate of 50 rupees per sqm?','','','side = l = 0.4 m\nTotal surface area of a cube = 6 l² = 6 × (0.4)² = 6 × 0.16 = 0.96 sq m\nCost of painting 1 sq m is 50 rupees.\n∴ Cost of painting 0.96 sq m will be = 0.96 × 50 = 48 rupees\nIt will cost 48 rupees to paint the outer surface of the box. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26561,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A matchbox is 4 cm long, 2.5 cm broad and 1.5 cm is height. Its outer sides are to be covered exactly with craft paper. How much paper will be required to do so?','','','Here, l = 4 cm, b = 2.5 cm, h = 1.5 cm\nTotal surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n         = 2(4 × 2.5 + 2.5 × 1.5 + 4 × 1.5) \n          2 [10 + 3.75 + 6] = 2 × 19.75 = 39.50 sq cm\nThus, the area of paper needed to cover the match box is 39.5 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26562,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The measurement of bricks is 10 cm long, 5 cm wide and 4 cm thick, find its total surface area.','','','Here, l = 10 cm, b = 5 cm, h = 4 cm\nTotal surface area of brick = total surface area of cuboid \n        = 2[lb + bh + lh] = 2[10 × 5 + 5 × 4 + 10 × 4]\n        = 2[50 + 20 + 40] = 2 × 110 = 220 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26563,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A rectangular plot is 40 m long and 30 m wide. Find its cost at the rate of Rs. 150 per sq m.','','','Area of rectangular plot = length × breadth = 40 × 30 = 1200 sq m\nRate of plot ₹ 150 per sq cm\n∴ Cost of plot = 150 × 1200 = ₹ 180000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26564,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of shaded region.','','','i) Area of inner rectangular = length × breadth \n             = 18 × 9 = 162 sq m \nii) Here, length of outer rectangle = 18 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 18 + 3 = 21 m\nbreadth = 9 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 9 + 3 = 12 m\n∴ Area of outer rectangle = 21 × 12 = 252 sq m\n∴ Area of shaded region = Area of outer rectangle – Area of inner rectangle \n          = 252 – 162 = 90 sq m','',1,0,0,NULL),(26565,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A rectangular field, 90 m long and 70 m wide has a 3 m wide path all around its inside. Find the area of the path and the cost of its construction at the rate of Rs. 20 per 10 m²?','','','i) Area of inner rectangle = length × breadth\n               = HG × FG             [HG = 90 – 3 – 3 = 84 and FG = 70 – 3 – 3 = 64]\n               = 84 × 64 = 5376 sq m\nii) Area of outer rectangle = length × breadth = DC × AD = 90 × 70 = 6300 sq m\niii) Area of path = Area of outer rectangle – Area of inner rectangle\n                = 6300 – 5376 = 924 sq m\niv) Cost of construction of path ₹ 20 per 10 sq m \n∴ Cost of construction of path = 20 × 924/10 = 20 × 92.4 = ₹ 1848','',1,0,0,NULL),(26566,293,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the edge of cube is 8 cm long, find its total surface area.','','','Total surface area of cube = 6l² = 6 × 8² = 64 × 6 = 384 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26567,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','As shown is the figure, four napkins all of the same size were made from a square piece of cloth of length 1 m. What length of lace will be required to trim all four sides of all the napkins?','','','The napkins are made as shown in figure from a cloth.\nThus, length of each square shaped napkin = 1/2 m\n∴ Perimeter of each napkin = 4 × 1/2 = 2 m\nHence, perimeter of all 4 napkins = 4 × 2 = 8 m\nSince the lace required to trim all four sides of each napkin is equal to its perimeter, the length of lace required is 8 m.','',1,0,0,NULL),(26568,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A rectangular garden is 40 m long and 30 m wide. A two-meter wide path is to be paved inside the garden along its boundary, using tiles 25 cm × 20 cm in size. How many such tiles will be required?','','','Let us find the area to be paved\nArea of garden = 40 × 30 = 1200 sq m\nArea not to be paved = 36 × 26 = 936 sq m\n∴ Area to be paved = 1200 – 936 = 264 sqm\nArea of each tile = (25/100) × (20/100) × 1/20\n             = 1/20 sqm\nHence, let us find the number of tiles required to tile 264 sqm\n∴ No. of tiles = Total area/Area of one paver\n             = 264 ÷ 1/20 = 264 × 20 = 5280\n∴ 5280 tiles will be required.','',1,0,0,NULL),(26569,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A rectangular playground is 65 m long and 30 m wide. A pathway of 1.5 m width goes all around the ground, outside it. Find the area of the pathway.','','','The playground is rectangular','▭ABCD is the playground. Around it is a pathway 1.5 m wide.\nAround ▭ABCD we get the rectangle ▭PQRS\nLength of new rectangle PQRS = 65 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 68 m\nBreadth = 30 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 33 m\nArea of path = Area of rectangle PQRS – Area of rectangle ABCD \n        = 68 × 33 – 65 × 30 = 2244 – 1950 = 294 sqm',0,0,1,NULL),(26570,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A rectangular hall is 12 m long and 6 m broad. Its flooring is to be made of square tiles of side 30 cm. How many tiles will fit in the entire hall? How many would be required if tiles of side 15 cm were used?','','','Length of hall, l = 12 m = 1200 cm, b= 6 m = 600 cm\n∴ Area of the flooring of hall = l × b = 1200 × 600 = 720000 sq cm\ni) If tiles of square shape with side 30 cm are used then\nArea of tile = (side)² = 30² = 900 sq cm\n∴ No. of tiles required = Area of flooring/Area of a tile\n        = 720000/900 = 800 tiles.\nii) If tile of side 15 cm is used then Area of a tile = (side)² = (15)² = 225 sq cm\n∴ No. of tiles used = Area of flooring/Area of tile = 720000/225\n            = 3200 \nHence, if tile of 30 cm side is used, 800 tiles are required and if tile of 15 cm side are used 3200 tiles are required for the flooring of the hall.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26571,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','An open box of length 1.5 m, breadth 1 m and height 1 m is to be made for use a trolley for carrying garden waste. How much sheet metal will be required to make the box? The inside and outside surface of the box is to be painted with rust proof paint. At a rate of 150 rupees per sq m, how much will it cost to paint the box?','','','Here, l = 1.5 m, b = 1 m, h = 1 m\nSheet metal used to make the open box = (lb + 2bh + 2lh)\n     = (1.5 × 1) + 2(1 × 1) + 2(1.5 × 1) = (1.5 + 2 + 3) = 6.5 sq m\nThe cost of painting the box from both sides is = 2(Area of cuboid) × Rate\n      = 2 × (6.5) × 150 = 13 × 150 = ₹ 1950\nHence, 6.5 sq m metal sheet is required to make the box and it costs ₹ 1950 to paint it.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26572,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The outer sides of the box which is 12 cm long, 8 cm broad and 6 cm high to be covered by a colour paper, find the area of paper needed.','','','Here, l = 12 cm, b = 8 cm, h = 6 cm\nTotal surface area of box = Total surface area of cuboid\n     = 1/2[lb + bh + lh] = 1/2[12 × 8 + 8 × 6 + 12 × 60]\n     = 1/2[96 + 48 + 720] = 1/2 [864] = 432 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26573,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A 3 m wide path runs outside and around a rectangular park of length 125 m and breadth 65 m. Find the area if the path.','','','i) Area of outer side of rectangle = length × breadth \n           [length = 125 + 3 + 3 = 131 ; breadth = 65 + 3 + 3 = 71]\n∴ Area of outer side of rectangle = 131 × 71 = 9301 sq m\nii) Area of inner side rectangle = length × breadth \n          = 125 × 65 = 8125 sq m\niii) Area of path = Area of outer side rectangle – Area of inner side rectangle\n          = 9301 – 8125 = 1176 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(26574,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the length and breadth of a rectangle are in the ratio 13 : 8 and its perimeter is 420 m, then find the area of the rectangle.','','','Given that, ratio of length and breadth of rectangle is 13 : 8.\n∴ length = 13x and breadth = 8x\n∴ Perimeter of rectangle = 2[l + b]\n∴ 420 = 13x + 8x \n∴ 21x = 210\n∴ x = 210/21 = 10  \n∴ length = 13x = 13 × 10 = 130 m and breadth = 8x = 8 × 10 = 80 m\n∴ Area of rectangle = length × breadth = 130 × 80 = 10400 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(26575,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the ratio between the length and the perimeter of a rectangular plot is 1 : 3, then find the ratio between the length and breadth of the plot.','','','Ratio of length and the perimeter of rectangular plot is 1 : 3.\n∴ length = 1x and perimeter = 3x \n∴ Perimeter = 2[l + b]\n∴ 3x = 2[x + breadth]\n∴ 3x – 2x = 2 × breadth\n∴ breadth = x/2\n∴ Ratio of length and breadth = x/(x/2) = 2/1 = 2 : 1','',0,0,0,NULL),(26576,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of a rectangular plot of land is twice its breadth. If the perimeter of the plot be 420 m, find its area. ','','','Let breadth of rectangle = x then length of rectangle = 2x\n∴ Perimeter of rectangle = 2[l + b]\n∴ 420 = 2[2x + x]\n∴ 210 = 3x\n∴ x = 70\n∴ length = 2x = 2 × 70 = 140 m and breadth = x = 70 m\n∴ Area of rectangle = length × breadth = 140 × 70 = 9800 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(26577,293,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The area of the rectangular garden of length 40 m is 1000 m². Find \ni) The breadth of the garden 		\nii) The perimeter of the garden. The garden is to be enclosed by 3 rounds of fencing, leaving an entrance of 4 m. Find the cost of fencing the garden at a rate of Rs. 250 per meter. ','','','i) Area of rectangle = l × b\n∴ 1000 = 40 × b\n∴ b = 25 m\nii) The perimeter of the rectangular garden = 2l + 2b \n        = 2 × 40 + 2 × 25 = 80 + 50 = 130 m\nThe perimeter for fencing the garden = (130 – 4) = 126 m\n     ------- (The entrance is 4 m wide)\nThree rounds of fencing = 3 × 126 = 378 m\nCost of fencing = rate × length of fencing = 250 × 378\n        = ₹ 94500','',0,0,0,NULL),(26578,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find a Pythagoras triplet among the following\na) 20, 22, 30 		b) 12, 13, 25 		c) 6, 8, 10 		d) 25, 50, 75\n','','','c) 6, 8, 10','',0,0,0,NULL),(26579,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the missing number in the Pythagorean triplet (28, ---, 53)\na) 45 			b) 45			c) 43 			d) 41\n','','','a) 45','',0,0,0,NULL),(26580,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If sides making right angles of a right- angled triangle are 8 cm and 15 cm. Find its hypotenuse.\na) 16 cm 		b) 17 cm 		b) 19 cm 		d) 21 cm\n','','','b) 17 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26581,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is -----\na) side opposite to shortest angle		b) side opposite to right angle 		c) any side of the triangle		d) one of the sides making right angle\n','','','b) side opposite to right angle 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26582,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the Pythagorean triplet (30, 40, ----)\na) 45 			b) 55 			c) 52 			d) 50\n','','','d) 50','',0,0,0,NULL),(26583,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a ΔABC, if ∠A = ∠B + ∠C, then find ∠A.\na) 60⁰			b) 45⁰			c) 90⁰		c) 120⁰\n','','','c) 90⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(26584,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A man goes 24 m due east and then 10 m due north. How far is he away from his starting position?\na) 18 m		b) 26 m		c) 24 m		d) 28 m\n','','','c) 24 m		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26585,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a right isosceles triangle, the measure of one of the angles other than the right angle is ----\na) 90⁰			b) 60⁰  		c) 45⁰			d) can’t be determined\n','','','c) 45⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26586,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 6, x, 10 is a Pythagorean triplet, then value of x is ----\na) 2 		b) 4		c) 8 		d) 12\n','','','c) 8 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26587,294,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a triangle, one angle is of 90⁰. Then in the given statements which are true?\ni) The other two angles are of 45⁰ each 		ii) In remaining two angles, one angle is 90⁰ and other is 45⁰		iii) Reaming two angles are complementary\na) (i) only 		b) (ii) only 		c) (iii) only 		d) (i) and (ii)\n','','','c) (iii) only ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26588,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','In the figure given below, find the value of x.','','','In ΔLMN, ∠LMN = 90⁰\nLN² = LM² + MN²  ----------- (by Pythagoras theorem)\n∴ x² = 7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625\n∴ x = 25','',1,0,0,NULL),(26589,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','In the figure given below, find the value of x.','','','In ΔPQR, ∠PQR = 90⁰\n∴ By Pythagoras theorem, PR² = PQ² + QR²\n∴ QR² = PR² - PQ² \n∴ x² = 41² - 9² = 1681 – 81 = 1600\n∴ x = 40   ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26590,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 7, 24, 25','','','7² = 49, 24² = 576, 25² = 625\n∴ 49 + 576 = 625\n∴ 7² + 24² = 25²\n∴ 7, 24 and 25 is Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26591,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 3, 4, 5','','','3² = 9, 4² = 16, 5² = 25\n∴ 9 + 16 = 25\n∴ 3² + 4² = 5²\n∴ 3, 4 and 5 is Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26592,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 2, 4, 5','','','2² = 4, 4² = 16, 5² = 25\n∴ 4 + 16 ≠ 25\n∴ 2² + 4² ≠ 5²\n∴ 2, 4 and 5 is not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26593,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 4, 5, 6','','','4² = 16, 5² = 25, 6² = 36\n∴ 16 + 24 ≠ 36\n∴ 4² + 5² ≠ 6²\n∴ 4, 5 and 6 is not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26594,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 2, 6, 7','','','2² = 4, 6² = 36, 7² = 49\n∴ 4 + 36 ≠ 49\n∴ 2² + 6² ≠ 7²\n∴ 2, 6 and 7 is not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26595,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 9, 40, 41','','','9² = 81, 40² = 1600, 41² = 1681\n∴ 81 + 1600 = 1681\n∴ 9² + 40² = 41²\n∴ 9, 40 and 41 is Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26596,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 4, 7, 8','','','4² = 16, 7² = 49, 8² = 64\n∴ 16 + 49 ≠ 64\n∴ 4² + 7² ≠ 8²\n∴ 4, 7 and 8 is not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26597,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle whose base is 12 cm and height is 5 cm?','','','We know that, Pythagoras theorem,\n(Hypo)² = (base)² + (height)² = 12² + 5² = 144 + 25 = 169\n∴ Hypotenuse = 13 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26598,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ΔPQR, seg PR is the hypotenuse. Which angle of ΔPQR is a right angle?','','','In ΔPQR, seg PR is the Hypotenuse.\n∴ ∠Q = 90⁰\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26599,294,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ΔABC, ∠A = 90⁰. Name the sides forming the right angle.','','','In ΔABC, ∠A = 90⁰\n∴ side AC and AB are the sides forming the right angle. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26600,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In ΔABC, ∠C = 90⁰, l(AC) = 5 cm and l(BC) = 12 cm. What is the length of seg AB?','','','In ΔABC, ∠C = 90⁰\n∴ seg AB is the Hypotenuse\n∴ (AB)² = AC² + BC² = 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169\n∴ AB = 13 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26601,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In the figure given below, find the value of x.','','','In ΔEDF, ∠EDF = 90⁰\nBy using Pythagoras theorem, EF² = ED² + DF²\n∴ ED² = EF² - DF²\n∴ x² = 17² - 8² = 289 – 64 = 225\n∴ x = 15 unit','',1,0,0,NULL),(26602,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 8, 15, 17','','','Here, 17² = 289\n8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289 \n∴ 17² = 8² + 15² \n∴ sides of the triangle are in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26603,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 11, 12, 15','','','Here, 15² = 225\n11² + 12² = 121 + 144 = 265 \n∴ 15² ≠ 11² + 12² \n∴ sides of the triangle are not in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26604,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 11, 60, 61','','','Here, 61² = 3721\n11² + 60² = 121 + 3600 = 3721 \n∴ 61² = 11² + 60² \n∴ sides of the triangle are in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26605,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 1.5, 1.6, 1.7','','','Here, 1.7² = 2.89\n1.6² + 1.5² = 2.56 + 2.25 = 4.81 \n∴ 1.7² ≠ 1.6² + 1.5² \n∴ sides of the triangle are not in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26606,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 40, 20, 30','','','Here, 40² = 1600\n20² + 30² = 400 + 900 = 1300 \n∴ 40² ≠ 20² + 30² \n∴ sides of the triangle are not in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26607,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the remaining side of right-angled ΔLMN.','','','In ΔLMN, ∠LMN = 90⁰\nBy using Pythagoras theorem,\nLN² = LM² + MN²\n∴ LM² = LN² - MN² = 37² - 35² = 1369 – 1225 = 144\n∴ LM = 12 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26608,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the length of wire tied to a window on a wall at height 8 m and which is tied to the ground at 15 m from the wall.','','','Window is perpendicular to ground.\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(Lenth of wire)² = 8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289\n∴ Length of wire = 17 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(26609,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','PQR is a triangle, right-angled at P. If PQ = 10 cm and PR = 24 cm, find QR.','','','In ΔPQR, ∠P = 90⁰\n∴ seg QR is hypotenuse\n∴ QR² = PQ² + PR² = 10² + 24² = 100 + 576 = 676 \n∴ QR = 26 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26610,294,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','ABC is a triangle, right-angled at C. If AB = 25 cm and AC = 7 cm, find BC.','','','In ΔABC, ∠C = 90⁰\n∴ seg AB is a hypotenuse.\n∴ AB² = AC² + BC²\n∴ BC² = AB² - AC² = 25² - 7² = 625 – 49 = 576 \n∴ BC = 24 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26611,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In the right-angled ΔPQR, ∠P = 90⁰, if l(PQ) = 24 cm and l(PR) = 10 cm, find the length of seg QR.','','','In ΔPQR, ∠P = 90⁰\n∴ Seg QR is a hypotenuse\n∴ QR² = PQ² + PR² = 24² + 10² = 576 + 100 = 676\n∴ QR = 26 cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26612,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In the right-angled ΔLMN, ∠M = 90⁰. If l(LM) = 12 cm and l(LN) = 20 cm, find the length of seg MN.','','','In ΔLMN, ∠M = 90⁰\n∴ Seg LN is hypotenuse\n∴ By Pythagoras theorem, LN² = LM² + MN²\n∴ MN² = LN² - LM² = 20² - 12² = 400 – 144 = 256\n∴ MN = 16 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26613,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the sides of triangle are 14, 48 and 50. Check whether it is a right-angled triangle or not.','','','Highest length = 50\n∴ 50² = 2500\n14² + 48² = 196 + 2304 = 2500\n∴ 50² = 14² + 48²\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, given sides formed right angled triangle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26614,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Verify whether the numbers 35, 65, 100 are in Pythagorean triplet.','','','Highest length = 100\n∴ 100² = 10000\n35² + 65² = 1225 + 3025 = 4250\n∴ 100² ≠ 35² + 65²\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, given sides 35, 65 and 100 are not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26615,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the value of y.','','','In ΔXYZ, ∠Y = 90⁰\nBy using Pythagoras theorem, XZ² = XY² + YZ²\n ∴ 45² = 27² + y²\n∴ y² = 45² - 27² = 2025 – 729 = 1296\n∴ y = 36','',1,0,0,NULL),(26616,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ΔLMN, ∠M = 90⁰, l(LM) = 9 cm and l(MN) = 12 cm, find the length of LN.','','','In ΔLMN, ∠M = 90⁰\n∴ Seg LN is a hypotenuse.\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, LN² = LM² + MN²\n∴ LN² = 9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225\n∴ LN = 15 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(26617,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A 15 m long ladder reached a window 12 m high from the ground on placing it against a wall at a distance ‘a’. Find the distance of the foot of the ladder from the wall.','','','Let, length of ladder = Hypotenuse = 15 m\nHeight of window = 12 m and distance between wall and ladder = a m\n∴ Wall is perpendicular to ground\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n∴ 15² = 12² + a²\n∴ a² = 225 – 144 = 81\n∴ a = 9 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(26618,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A tree is broken at a height of 8 m from the ground and its top touches the ground at a distance of 15 m from the base of the tree. Find the original height of the tree.','','','Solution : ','In figure, let AB be the height of tree, BC be the distance between tree top and tree. Tree is broken at point A’ \n∴ Here,  A’B = 8 m, BC = 15 m and AA’ = A’C \nAB ꓕ BC \n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(A’C)² = (A’B)² + (BC)² = 8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289\n∴ A’C = 17 m\n∴ AA’ = A’C = 17 m\n∴ Original height of tree = AA’ + A’B = 8 + 17 = 23 m',0,0,1,NULL),(26619,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A ladder is placed in such a way that its foot is 5 m away from the wall and its top reaches a window 12 m above the ground. Find the length of the ladder.','','','Distance between ladder and wall = 5 m and height of window from ground is 12 m\nWall of window is perpendicular to ground.\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem,\nLength of ladder = Hypotenuse\n∴ (Hypo.)² = 12² + 5² = 144 + 25 = 169\n∴ Hypo. = 13 m\n∴ Length of ladder is 13 m. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26620,294,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the value of x.','','','In ΔADB, ∠ADB = 90⁰\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \nAB² = AD² + BD²\n∴ BD² = AB² - AD² = (6.5)² - 6² = 42.25 – 36 = 6.25\n∴ BD = 2.5 cm\nIn ΔADC, ∠ADC = 90⁰\n∴ AC² = AD² + DC²\n∴ DC² = AC² - AD² = 10² - 6² = 100 – 36 = 64 \n∴ DC = 8 cm\n∴ BC = x = BD + DC = 2.5 + 8 = 10.5 cm ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26621,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The top of a ladder of length of 15 m reaches a window 9 m above the ground. What is the distance between the base of the wall and that of the ladder?','','','In figure, Let AC is the ladder','AB is the wall, the window is at point A, such that AC = 15 m, AB = 9 m\n∠B = 90⁰        ∴ AC is the hypotenuse\n∴ By Pythagoras theorem,\nAC² = AB² + BC² \n∴ BC² = AC² - AB² = 15² - 9² = 225 – 81 = 144\n∴ BC = 12 m\nThus, the distance between the base of the wall and foot of ladder is 12 m.',0,0,1,NULL),(26622,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','From the given figure, find the length of AC and the perimeter of ΔABC.','','','In ΔABC, ∠ABC = 90⁰\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, AC² = AB² + BC²\n∴ AC² = 20² + 21² = 400 + 441 = 841\n∴ AC = 29 m\n∴ Perimeter of ΔABC = AB + BC + AC = 20 + 21 + 29 = 70 m','',1,0,0,NULL),(26623,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the numbers (13, 84, x) are in Pythagorean triplet, where x is largest number among them, find x.','','','From given information, x is the longest side\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n∴ x² = 13² + 84² = 169 + 7056 = 7225\n∴ x = 85 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26624,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Check whether (3.9, 1.5, 3.6) is a Pythagorean triplet or not.','','','Here, (3.9)² = 15.21 \n(1.5)² + (3.6)² = 2.25 + 12.96 = 15.21 \n∴ (3.9)² = (1.5)² + (3.6)²\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, given numbers are 3.9, 1.5, 3.6 are Pythagorean triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26625,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any four Pythagorean triplet.','','','Pythagorean triplets are : (3, 4, 5), (13, 84, 85), (20, 21, 29), (15, 9, 12) etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(26626,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A tree is broken at a height of 5 m from the ground and its top touches the ground at a distance of 12 m from the base of the tree. Find the original height of the tree.','','','Solution : ','In figure, let AB be the height of tree, BC be the distance between tree top and tree. Tree is broken at point A’ \n∴ Here,  A’B = 5 m, BC = 12 m and AA’ = A’C \nAB ꓕ BC \n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(A’C)² = (A’B)² + (BC)² = 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169\n∴ A’C = 13 m\n∴ AA’ = A’C = 13 m\n∴ Original height of tree = AA’ + A’B = 5 + 13 = 18 m',0,0,1,NULL),(26627,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, find x.','','','In ΔACD, ∠ACD = 90⁰\n∴ By using Pythagoras triplet, AD² = AC² + CD²\n∴ AC² = AD² - CD² = 25² - 7² = 625 – 49 = 576\n∴ AC = 24 cm\nIn ΔACB, ∠ACB = 90⁰\nAB² = BC² + AC²\n∴ BC² = AB² - AC²\n∴ x² = 30² - 24² = 900 – 576 = 324\n∴ x = 18 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26628,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A tree is broken at a height of 6 m from the ground and its top touches the ground at a distance of 8 m from the base of the tree. Find the original height of the tree.','','','Solution : ','In figure, let AB be the height of tree, BC be the distance between tree top and tree. Tree is broken at point A’ \n∴ Here,  A’B = 6 m, BC = 8 m and AA’ = A’C \nAB ꓕ BC \n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(A’C)² = (A’B)² + (BC)² = 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100\n∴ A’C = 10 m\n∴ AA’ = A’C = 10 m\n∴ Original height of tree = AA’ + A’B = 6 + 10 = 16 m',0,0,1,NULL),(26629,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A tree is broken at a height of 15 m from the ground and its top touches the ground at a distance of 20 m from the base of tree. If the original height of tree is k m, then find k.','','','Solution : ','In figure, let AB be the height of tree, BC be the distance between tree top and tree. Tree is broken at point A’ \n∴ Here,  A’B = 15 m, BC = 20 m and AA’ = A’C \nAB ꓕ BC \n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(A’C)² = (A’B)² + (BC)² = 15² + 20² = 225 + 400 = 625\n∴ A’C = 25 m\n∴ AA’ = A’C = 25 m\n∴ Original height of tree = AA’ + A’B = 15 + 25 = 40 m',0,0,1,NULL),(26630,294,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the perimeter of rectangle?','','','▭ABCD is a rectangle\n∴ ∠B = 90⁰\nIn ΔABC,\nBy using Pythagoras theorem, AC² = AB² + BC²\n∴ AB² = AC² - BC² = 26² - 10² = 676 – 100 = 576\n∴ AB = 24 cm\n∴ Length of rectangle = 24 cm and breadth = 10 cm\n∴ Perimeter of rectangle = 2(length + breadth) \n             = 2(24 + 10) = 2 × 34 = 68 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(26631,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Of which of the binomials given below is m²n² + 14mnpq + 49p²q² the expansion?\na) (m + n) (p + q) 		b) (mn – pq) 	        c) (7mn + pq)	                d) (mn + 7pq)\n','','','d) (mn + 7pq)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26632,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Which of the options given below is the square of the binomial ','','','c) 64 – (16/x) + 1/x²','',1,0,0,NULL),(26633,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(a + b)² = -------\na) a² + b²		b) a² + 2ab + b²		c) a² - 2ab + b²		d) a² - b²\n','','','b) a² + 2ab + b²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26634,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a wrong sum.\na) (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²		b) (a – b)² = a² - 2ab + b²		c) (a + b) (a – b) = a² + b²		d) none of these\n','','','c) (a + b) (a – b) = a² + b²	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26635,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The factors of 2x² - 128y²\na) (x – 8y) (x + 8y) 		b) 2(x – 8y) (x + 8y) 	c) (2x – 8y) (2x + 8y) 	d) (2x – 4y) (2x + 4y)\n','','','b) 2(x – 8y) (x + 8y) ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26636,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If ab = 6 and a + b = 5 then a² + b² = -----\na) 11			b) 12 			c) 13 		d) 16\n','','','c) 13','',0,0,0,NULL),(26637,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a + b = 11 and ab = 30 then a² + b² = -----\na) 61 			b) 21 			c) 24 			d) 81\n','','','a) 61 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26638,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the value of (10.2 × 9.8) = -----\na) 98.96 		b) 99.6 		c) 99.92 		d) 99.96\n','','','d) 99.96','',0,0,0,NULL),(26640,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Evaluate :','','','c) 12','',1,0,0,NULL),(26641,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the number of terms in product of (2x + 3y) and (4x + 5y)\na) 5 			b) 2 			c) 3 			d) 4\n','','','c) 3','',0,0,0,NULL),(26642,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x – y = 5, xy = 24 then the value of x² + y² will be -----\na) 23 			b) 73 			c) 65 			d) 74\n','','','b) 73','',0,0,0,NULL),(26643,295,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x + y = 8, xy = 24 then the value of x² + y² will be ------\na) 32 			b) 34 			c) 36 			d) 38\n','','','b) 34 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26644,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(a + b)² = -------','','','(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26645,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(a – b)² = ------','','','(a - b)² = a² - 2ab + b²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26646,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','a² - b² = -------','','','a² - b² = (a – b) (a + b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26647,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If a = 5, b = 3 then verify the formula, (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²	','','','Here, a = 5, b = 3\nLHS = (a + b)² = (5 + 3)² = 8² = 64\nRHS = a² + 2ab + b² = 5² + 2 × 5 × 3 + 3² = 25 + 30 + 9 = 64\n∴ LHS = RHS ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26648,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If a = 11, b = 3, then verify the formula, (a – b)² = a² - 2ab + b²','','','Here, a = 11, b = 3\nLHS = (a - b)² = (11 - 3)² = 8² = 64\nRHS = a² - 2ab + b² = 11² - 2 × 11 × 3 + 3² = 121 - 66 + 9 = 64\n∴ LHS = RHS 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26649,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (2x + 3y)²','','','(2x + 3y)² = (2x)² + 2(2x)(3y) + (3y)² = 4x² + 12xy + 9y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26650,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (5x – 4)²','','','(5x – 4)² = (5x)² - 2 × 5x × 4 + 4² = 25x² - 40x + 16','',0,0,0,NULL),(26651,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (51)²','','','(51)² = (50 + 1)² = (50)² + 2 × 50 × 1 + 1² = 2500 + 100 + 1 = 2601','',0,0,0,NULL),(26652,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (98)²','','','(98)² = (100 – 2)² = (100)² - 2 × 100 × 2 + 2² = 10000 – 400 + 4 = 9604','',0,0,0,NULL),(26653,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : (3x + 4y) (3x – 4y)','','','(3x + 4y) (3x – 4y) = (3x)² - (4y)² = 9x² - 16y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26654,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : (x + y) (x – y)','','','(x + y)(x – y) = x² - y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26655,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : (3x – 5) (3x + 5)','','','(3x – 5)(3x + 5) = (3x)² - 5² = 9x² - 25 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26656,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : (a + 6) (a – 6)','','','(a + 6)(a - 6) = a² - 6² = a² - 36 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26657,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 201a³b²','','','201a³b² = 3 × 67 × a × a × a × b × b','',0,0,0,NULL),(26658,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 91xyt²','','','91xyt² = 7 × 13 × x × y × t × t','',0,0,0,NULL),(26659,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 24a²b²','','','24a²b² = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × a × a × b × b','',0,0,0,NULL),(26660,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : tr²s³','','','tr²s³ = t × r × r × s × s × s','',0,0,0,NULL),(26661,295,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : p² - q²','','','p² - q² = (p – q)(p + q)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26662,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (5a + 6b)²','','','(5a + 6b)² = (5a)² + 2 × 5a × 6b + (6b)²\n                 = 25a² + 60ab + 36b²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26663,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand :    ','','','(a/2  + b/3)² = (a/2)² + 2 × a/2 × b/3 + (b/3)²\n                   = a²/4 + ab/3 + b²/9\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(26664,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (2p – 3q)²','','','(2p – 3q)² = (2p)² - 2 × 2p × 3q + (3q)² = 4p² - 12pq + 9q²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26665,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand :    ','','','(x – 2/x)² = x² - 2 × x × 2/x + (2/x)² = x² - 4 + 4/x²','',1,0,0,NULL),(26666,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (ax + by)²','','','(ax + by)² = (ax)² + 2 × ax × by + (by)² = a²x² + 2abxy + b²y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26667,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (7m – 4)²','','','(7m – 4)² = (7m)² - 2 × 7m × 4 + (4)² = 49m² - 56m + 16','',0,0,0,NULL),(26668,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand :   ','','','(x + 1/2)² = x² + 2 × x × 1/2 + (1/2)² = x² + x + 1/4','',1,0,0,NULL),(26669,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand :   ','','','(a – 1/a)² = a² - 2 × a × 1/a + (1/a)² = a² - 2 + 1/a²','',1,0,0,NULL),(26670,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Use an expansion formula to find the values : (997)²','','','(997)² = (1000 – 3)² = (1000)² - 2 × 1000 × 3 + 3² \n          = 1000000 – 6000 + 9 = 994,009 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26671,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 4xy + 8xy²','','','4xy + 8xy² = 4xy (1 + 2y)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26672,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 9a²bc + 12abc²','','','9a²bc + 12abc² = 3abc (3a + 4c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26673,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories : a² - 4b²','','','a² - 4b² = a² - (2b)² = (a – 2b)(a + 2b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(26674,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 3a² - 27b²','','','3a² - 27b² = 3(a² - 9b²) = 3[a² - (3b)²]\n          = 3[(a – 3b)(a + 3b)]\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26675,295,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories :     ','','','x² - (1/x)² = (x – 1/x)(x + 1/x)','',1,0,0,NULL),(26676,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Use an expansion formula to find the values : (102)²','','','(102)² = (100 + 2)² = (100)² + 2 × 100 × 2 + 2²\n                = 10000 + 400 + 4 = 10,404\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26677,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Use an expansion formula to find the values : (97)²','','','(97)² = (100 – 3)² = (100)² - 2 × 100 × 3 + 3²\n              = 10000 – 600 + 9 = 9409\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26678,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Use the formula to find the values : 502 × 498','','','502 × 498 = (500 + 2)(500 – 2) = (500)² - 2²\n             = 250000 – 4 = 249996\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26679,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Use the formula to find the values : 97 × 103','','','97 × 103 = (100 – 3) (100 + 3) = (100)² - 3²\n         = 10000 – 9 = 9991\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26680,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Factories : i) 4x² - 25y²		ii) y² - 4 ','','','i) 4x² - 25y² = (2x)² - (5y)² = (2x – 5y)(2x + 5y)\nii) y² - 4 = y² - 2² = (y – 2)(y + 2)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26681,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Factories :      ','','','i) p² - 1/25 = p² - (1/5)² = (p – 1/5)(p + 1/5)\nii) 9x² - y²/16 = (3x)² - (y/4)² = (3x – y/4) (3x + y/4)\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(26682,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factories : 365y⁴z³ - 146y²z⁴','','','365y⁴z³ - 146y²z⁴ = 5 × 73 × y⁴ × z³ - 2 × 73 × y² × z⁴\n          = 73y²z² (5y²z – 2z²)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26683,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use formula to multiply : i) (x – 5) (x + 5) 		ii) (2a – 13) (2a + 13)','','','i) (x – 5)(x + 5) = x² - (5)² = x² - 25 \nii) (2a – 13)(2a + 13) = (2a)² - (13)² = 4a² - 169 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26684,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use formula to multiply : i) (4z – 5y) (4z + 5y)	ii) (2t – 5) (2t + 5)','','','i) (4z – 5y)(4z + 5y) = (4z)² - (5y)² = 16z² - 25y²\nii) (2t – 5)(2t + 5) = (2t)² - (5)² = 4t² - 25 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26685,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Expand : i) (2a – 3b)²		ii) (10 + y)²','','','i) (2a – 3b)² = (2a)² - 2 × 2a × 3b + (3b)² = 4a² - 12ab + 9b²\nii) (10 + y)² = 10² + 2 × 10 × y + y² = 100 + 20y + y²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26686,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand : i) (b – 7)²		ii) (xy + 3z)²','','','i) (b – 7)² = b² - 2 × b × 7 + 7² = b² - 14b + 49\nii) (xy + 3z)² = (xy)² + 2 × xy × 3z + (3z)² = x²y² + 6xyz + 9z²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26687,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (a² - b²)²','','','(a² - b²)² = (a²)² - 2 × a² × b² + (b²)² = a⁴ - 2a²b² + b⁴','',0,0,0,NULL),(26688,295,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (2x + 5)² - (2x – 5)²','','','(2x + 5)² - (2x – 5)² = (2x)² + 2 × 2x × 5 + 5² - [(2x)² - 2 × 2x × 5 + 5²]\n         = (2x)² + 20x + 25 – (2x)² + 20x – 25 = 40x \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26689,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Use an expansion formula to find the values : (1005)²','','','(1005)² = (1000 + 5)² = (1000)² + 2 × 1000 × 5 + 5²\n                 = 1000000 + 10000 + 25 = 1010025\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26690,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Use the formula to find the values : 54 × 46','','','54 × 46 = (50 + 4)(50 – 4) = (50)² - 4² = 2500 – 16 = 2484','',0,0,0,NULL),(26691,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Use the formula to find the values : 98 × 102','','','98 × 102 = (100 – 2)(100 + 2) = (100)² - 2² = 10000 – 4 = 996','',0,0,0,NULL),(26692,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factories : i) a²b – ab 		ii) 4x²y – 6x²','','','i) a²b – ab = ab(a – 1)\nii) 4x²y – 6x² = 2x²(2y – 3)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26693,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factories : i) 1/2 y² - 8z²		ii) 2x² - 8y²','','','i) 1/2 (y² - 16z²) = 1/2 [y² - (4z)²] = 1/2 [(y – 4z)(y + 4z)]\nii) 2x² - 8y² = 2(x² - 4y²) = 2[x² - (2y)²] = 2[(x – 2y)(x + 2y)]\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26694,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Expand : ','','','i) (p/3 + q/4)² = (p/3)² + 2 × p/3 × q/4 + (q/4)²\n           = p²/9 + pq/6 + q²/16\nii) (y – 3/y)² = y² - 2 × y × 3/y + (3/y)²\n                   = y² - 6 + 9/y² ','',1,0,0,NULL),(26695,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve :     ','','','(4x²/3  + 3)² = (4x²/3)² + 2 × 4x²/3 × 3 + 3²\n              = 16x⁴/9 + 8x² + 9','',1,0,0,NULL),(26696,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If','','','Given that, x – 1/x = 9 \nWe have, (x – 1/x)² = x² - 2 × x × 1/x + 1/x²\n∴ 9² = x² - 2 + 1/x²\n∴ 81 + 2 = x² + 1/x²\n∴ x² + 1/x² = 83','',1,0,0,NULL),(26697,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :    ','','','(2m/3 + 3n/2)² = (2m/3)² + 2 × 2m/3  × 3n/2  + (3n/2)²\n            = 4m²/9 + 2mn + 9n²/4\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(26698,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand : (6x² - 5y)²','','','(6x² - 5y)² = (6x²)² - 2 × 6x² × 5y + (5y)² = 36x⁴ - 60x²y + 25y²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26699,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand : (0.4p – 0.5q)²','','','(0.4p – 0.5q)² = (0.4p)² - 2 × 0.4p × 0.5q + (0.5q)²\n                      = 0.16p² - 0.40pq + 0.25q²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26700,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (7m – 8n)² + (7m + 8n)²','','','(7m – 8n)² + (7m + 8n)²\n= (7m)² - 2 × 7m × 8n + (8n)²+ (7m)² + 2 × 7m × 8n + (8n)²\n= 2(7m)² + 2(8n)² = 98m² + 128n²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26701,295,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (4m + 5n)² + (5m + 4n)²','','','(4m + 5n)² + (5m + 4n)²\n= (4m)² + 2 × 4m × 5n + (5n)² + (5m)² + 2 × 5m × 4n + (4n)²\n= 16m² + 40mn + 25n² + 25m² + 40mn + 16n²\n= 41m² + 80mn + 41n²','',0,0,0,NULL),(26702,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The average of 33, 34, 35, x, 37, 38, 39 is x. The value of x is ----\na) 40 			b) 32 		c) 42 			d) 36\n','','','d) 36','',0,0,0,NULL),(26703,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The mean of the first ten natural numbers is -----\na) 5 			b) 5.5 		c) 6 			d) 6.5\n','','','b) 5.5 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26704,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The mean of 12, 10, x, 20 and 18 is 17. Find x.\na) 12 			b) 25 		c) 18 			d) 15\n','','','b) 25 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26705,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of integers lying between -5 and 5.\na) 0.6 			b) 0 		c) 0.57 		d) 0.5\n','','','b) 0 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26706,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mean of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7?\na) 3 		b) 4 		c) 5 			d) 6\n','','','b) 4 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26707,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mean of 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13?\na) 3 		b) 5 		c) 7 			d) 9\n','','','c) 7','',0,0,0,NULL),(26708,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mean of the first four prime numbers?\na) 3.5 		b) 7 		c) 5 			d) 4.25\n','','','d) 4.25','',0,0,0,NULL),(26709,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 10 observations is 12, what is their sum?\na) 120			b) 1.2 			c) 12 		d) 60\n','','','a) 120	','',0,0,0,NULL),(26710,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 3, 7, 11 and x is 9, what is the value of x?\na) 9 			b) 11 			c) 13 			d) 15\n','','','d) 15','',0,0,0,NULL),(26711,296,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mean is also referred as -----\na) average 		b) difference 		c) total 		d) none of these\n','','','a) average 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(26712,296,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of 4, 6, 7, 9 and 4.','','','Mean = (Sum of observation)/Total number of observations\n                = (4 + 6 + 7 + 9 + 4)/5 = 30/5 = 6\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26713,296,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of first five odd numbers.','','','First five odd numbers are 1, 3, 5, 7, 9\nMean = (1 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 9)/5 = 25/5 = 5\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26714,296,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 4, x and y is 6, then find the mean of x, y and 10.','','','Given : mean of 4, x and y is 6\n∴ 6 = (4 + x + y)/3\n∴ 4 + x + y = 18\n∴ x + y = 14  ----------- (i)\nMean of x, y and 10 is\nMean = (x + y + 10)/3 = (14 + 10)/3 = 24/3 = 8 -------- [from (i)]','',0,0,0,NULL),(26715,296,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 2, 3, x, 7, 8 is x, then find the value of x.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total number of observations\n∴  x = (2 + 3 + x + 7 + 8)/5\n∴ 5x = 20 + x \n∴ 5x – x = 20\n∴ 4x = 20 \n∴ x = 20/4 = 5 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26716,296,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of ‘n’ observations is 5 and sum of observations is 25, then find n.','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total no. of observations\n∴ 5 = 25/n \n∴ 5n = 25\n∴ n = 5  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(26717,296,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the average of the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5?','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5)/5 = 15/5 = 3\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26718,296,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the average of the numbers 101, 103, 105, 107, 109?','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (101 + 103 + 105 + 107 + 109)/5 = 525/5 = 105\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26719,296,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the average of the numbers 110, 114, 118, 122, 126, 130?','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (110 + 114 + 118 + 122 + 126 + 130)/6 = 720/6 = 120\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26720,296,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for mean (average).','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation','',0,0,0,NULL),(26721,296,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the rainfall for each day of a week in a certain city is given in millimeters. Find the average rainfall during the week, 9, 11, 8, 20, 10, 16, 12.','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (9 + 11 + 8 + 20 + 10 + 16 + 12)/7 = 86/7 = 12.285\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26722,296,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The annual rainfall in Vidarbha in five years is given below. What is the average rainfall for those 5 years? 900 mm, 650 mm, 733 mm, 400 mm.','','','Average rainfall = Total rainfall in 5 years/5\n         = (900 + 650 + 450 + 733 + 400)/2 = 3133/5 = 626.6\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(26723,296,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','A school conducted a survey to find out how far their students live from the school. Given below is the distance of the houses of six of the students from the school. 950 m, 800 m, 700 m, 1.5 km, 1 km, 750 m. Let us find their average distance from the school.','','','Here, 1 km = 1000 m\nAverage = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (950 + 800 + 700 + 1500 + 1000 + 750)/6 \n                     = 5700/6 = 950','',0,0,0,NULL),(26724,296,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Rutuja practiced skipping with a rope all seven days of a week. The number of times she jumped the rope in one minute every day is given below. 60, 62, 61, 60, 59, 63, 58. Find average.','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (60 + 62 + 61 + 60 + 59 + 63 + 58)/7 \n                     = 423/7 = 60.42','',0,0,0,NULL),(26725,296,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The distance at which some children live from their school is given below in kilometers. To make frequency table of this data. 1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 4, 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 4, 6, 4, 6.','','','Solution : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(26726,296,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the average of the numbers 98, 99, 97, 92, 91, 93, 95.','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (98 + 99 + 97 + 92 + 91 + 93 + 95)/7 \n                     = 665/7 = 95','',0,0,0,NULL),(26727,296,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the average of 42, 43, x, 47, 44 is 45. Find the value of x.','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n∴ 45 = (42 + 43 + x + 47 + 44)/5\n∴ 45 × 5 = 176 + x\n∴ x = 225 – 176 = 49','',0,0,0,NULL),(26728,296,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sachin scored runs in 6 innings as follows, find the average score. 49, 50, 51, 53, 55, 56.','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (49 + 50 + 51 + 53 + 55 + 56)/6 \n                     = 314/6 = 52.33','',0,0,0,NULL),(26729,296,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the average of 97, 99, 98, 97, 96, 95, 94, 92, 98, 99.','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (97 + 99 + 98 + 97 + 96 + 95 + 94 + 92 + 98 + 99)/10 \n                     = 965/10 = 96.5','',0,0,0,NULL),(26730,296,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','During the annual function of a school, a Women’s Self-Help Group had set up a snacks stall. Their sales every hour were worth Rs. 960, Rs. 830, Rs. 945, Rs. 800, Rs. 847, Rs. 970 respectively. What was the average of the hourly sales?','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (960 + 830 + 945 + 800 + 847 + 970)/6 \n                     = 5352/6 = 892','',0,0,0,NULL),(26732,296,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The height (in cm) of 30 children in a class is given in centimeters. Draw up a frequency table of this data. 131, 135, 140, 138, 132, 133, 135, 133, 134, 135, 132, 133, 140, 139, 132, 131, 134, 133, 140, 140, 139, 136, 137, 136, 139, 137, 133, 134, 131, 140.','','','Solution : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(26733,296,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw frequency table for this given data : 4, 3, 2, 4, 3, 4, 3, 3, 2, 8, 2, 3, 3, 4, 3, 4, 4, 5, 2, 8, 8, 2, 5, 3, 4, 4, 3, 6, 2, 3, 4, 4, 3, 3, 2, 6, 4, 4, 7, 2, 3, 6, 3, 6, 6, 6, 7, 6, 7, 3.','','','Solution : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(26734,296,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a class test of maths, each of the 30 students scored marks as follows. Prepare a frequency distribution table. 25, 20, 19, 17, 11, 20, 21, 27, 28, 30, 25, 30, 29, 24, 24, 27, 21, 19, 18, 25, 24, 22, 23, 25, 27, 28, 27, 25, 24, 23.','','','Solution : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(26735,296,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In a certain colony, there are 50 families. The number of people in every family is given below. draw up the frequency table. 5, 4, 5, 4, 5, 3, 3, 3, 4, 3, 4, 2, 3, 4, 2, 2, 2, 2, 4, 5, 1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 3, 3, 2, 4, 4, 2, 3, 4, 3, 4, 2, 3, 4, 5, 3, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 5, 3, 2, 3, 2.','','','Solution : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(26736,296,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A dice was thrown 40 times and the number on the upper face noted is given below. draw up a frequency table for this data. 3, 2, 5, 6, 4, 2, 3, 1, 6, 6, 2, 3, 5, 3, 5, 3, 4, 2, 4, 5, 4, 2, 6, 3, 3, 2, 4, 3, 3, 4, 1, 4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 5, 3, 3, 4.','','','Solution : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(26737,296,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The number of chapatis that 30 children in a hostel need at every meal is given below. make a frequency table for these scores. 3, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4, 3, 4, 5, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2, 5, 4, 4, 4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2, 3, 2.','','','Solution : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(26738,296,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The number of peas in each pod are recorded as follows prepare a frequency distribution table. 4, 3, 5, 3, 4, 2, 1, 3, 3, 5, 4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2, 4, 4, 3, 3, 2, 5, 1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 5, 3, 3, 3, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 2, 2.','','','Solution : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(26739,296,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The blood groups of the donor’s in a blood donation camp are given below. prepare a frequency distribution table. O, B, A, AB, O, O, O, B, A, A, AB, AB, O, O, O, B, A, AB, O, AB, O, AB, B, A, A, B, O, O, O, AB, B, B, B, A.','','','Solution : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(27131,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many factors does 36 have?\na) 6 		b) 8 			c) 9 			d) 10\n','','','c) 9 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27132,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following numbers is not a factor of 32?\na) 64 		b) 32 			c) 16 			d) 8\n','','','a) 64 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27133,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the sum of all divisors of 24?\na) 59 		b) 36 			c) 60 			d) 66\n','','','c) 60 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27134,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following numbers is not divisible by 3?\na) 124 		b) 126 		c) 132 		d) 138\n','','','a) 124 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27135,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','By which of the following digits should * be replaced in the number 5*234, so that the number is divisible by 6?\na) 2 		b) 6 			c) 4 			d) 6\n','','','c) 4 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27136,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which is the digit at the place * in the number 6*54*, so that it is divisible by 9?\na) 6 	        	b) 7 			c) 8 			d) 9\n','','','a) 6','',0,0,0,NULL),(27137,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following numbers is divisible both by 3 and 5?\na) 25955 		b) 26955		c) 27955		d) 28955\n','','','c) 27955	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27138,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which minimum number should be added to the smallest five-digit number so that the number formed is divisible by 3?\na) 1 	          	b) 2 	         	c) 5 			d) 8\n','','','b) 2 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27139,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the sum of all the divisors of the number obtained by adding the highest divisor of 31 and the lowest divisor of 30.\na) 16 			b) 61 		c) 63 		d) 36\n','','','c) 63 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27140,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many times is the greatest divisor of 52 greater than that of its smallest divisor?\na) 26 			b) 13 			c) 4 			d) 52\n','','','d) 52','',0,0,0,NULL),(27141,305,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','By which of the following digits should * be replaced in the number 7*48, so that the number is divisible by 3 as well as 4?\na) 8 			b) 7 			c) 4 			d) 3 \n','','','b) 7 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(27142,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the factors of the number 8','','','8 = 1, 2, 4, 8','',0,0,0,NULL),(27143,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the factors of the number 5','','','5 = 1, 5','',0,0,0,NULL),(27144,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the factors of the number 14','','','14 = 1, 2, 7, 14','',0,0,0,NULL),(27145,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the factors of the number 7','','','7 = 1, 7','',0,0,0,NULL),(27146,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the factors of the number 22','','','22 = 1, 2, 11, 22','',0,0,0,NULL),(27147,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the factors of the number 25','','','25 = 1, 5, 25','',0,0,0,NULL),(27148,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the factors of the number 32','','','32 = 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32','',0,0,0,NULL),(27149,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the factors of the number 33','','','33 = 1, 3, 11, 33','',0,0,0,NULL),(27150,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Which of the prime numbers are even numbers?','','','2 is the even prime number','',0,0,0,NULL),(27151,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Determine whether the pairs of numbers given below are coprime numbers or not : 22, 24','','','The number 22 and 24 having more than 1 common factor.\n∴ 22 and 24 are not co-prime number.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27152,305,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write test of divisibility by 2','','','If there is 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8 in the units place, the number is a multiple of 2, or is exactly divisible by 2.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27153,305,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write test of divisibility by 3','','','If the sum of the digits of a number is divisible by 3 then the number is also divisible by 3.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27154,305,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write test of divisibility by 5','','','If the unit place of a number is 0 or 5 then this number is divisible by 5.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27155,305,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write test of divisibility by 10','','','If the unit place of the digits is zero then the number is divisible by 10','',0,0,0,NULL),(27156,305,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Write five three-digit numbers that are multiples of 2.','','','i) 284            ii) 468             iii) 238            iv) 254           v) 548 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27157,305,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Write all the prime numbers between 1 and 20.','','','Prime numbers between 1 to 20 are : 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27158,305,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Circle the prime numbers in the list given below : 22, 37, 43, 48, 53, 60, 91, 57, 59, 77, 79, 97, 100.','','','Prime numbers in the list : 37, 43, 53, 59, 79, 97','',0,0,0,NULL),(27159,305,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Determine whether the pairs of numbers given below are coprime numbers or not : i) 14, 21		ii) 10, 33','','','i) The number 14 and 21 having more than 1 common factor.\n∴ 14 and 21 are not co-prime number.\nii) The number 10 and 33 having only one 1 common factor.\n∴ 10 and 33 are co-prime number.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27160,305,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Determine whether the pairs of numbers given below are coprime numbers or not : i) 50, 52		ii) 17, 18','','','i) The number 50 and 52 having more than 1 common factor.\n∴ 50 and 52 are not co-prime number.\nii) The number 17 and 18 having only one 1 common factor.\n∴ 17 and 18 are co-prime number.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27161,305,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write five three-digit numbers that are multiple of 5','','','i) 125           ii) 525          iii) 450           iv) 785           v) 990','',0,0,0,NULL),(27162,305,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write five three-digit numbers that are multiple of 10','','','i) 100            ii) 150            iii) 570            iv) 650           v) 990','',0,0,0,NULL),(27163,305,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A ribbon is 3 meters long. Can we cut it into 50 cm piece and have nothing left over? Write the reason why or why not?','','','We can cut it into 50 cm pieces and nothing left over. Because 3 m is 300 cm and can be divide easily by 50 cm.\n50 cm is multiple of 300 cm.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27164,305,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the number given in the table is divisible by the given divisor, put √ in the box. If it is not divisible by the divisor, put × in the box :','','','Solution : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(27165,305,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write all the composite numbers from 21 to 50.','','','21, 22, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 30, 32, 33, 34, 35, 36, 38, 39, 40, 42, 44, 45, 46, 48 49.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27166,305,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Determine whether the pairs of numbers given below are coprime numbers or not : i) 11, 30		ii) 5, 7 		iii) 15, 16','','','i) The number 11 and 30 having only one 1 common factor.\n∴ 11 and 30 are co-prime number.\nii) The number 5 and 7 having only one 1 common factor.\n∴ 5 and 7 are co-prime number.\niii) The number 15 and 16 having only one 1 common factor.\n∴ 15 and 16 are co-prime number.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27167,305,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write five numbers that multiples of 2 as well as 3.','','','6, 12, 24, 36, 48','',0,0,0,NULL),(27168,305,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A ribbon is 3 meters long. I need 8 pieces of ribbon each 40 cm long. How many centimeters shorter is the given ribbon than the length I need?','','','3 m = 300 cm\nTotal needed = 8 × 40 = 320 cm\nIt is shorter ribbon = 320 – 300 = 20 cm\nIt is 20 cm shorter than the length they need.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27169,305,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If the number given in the table is divisible by the given divisor, put √ in the box. If it is not divisible by the divisor, put × in the box :','','','Solution : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(27170,305,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Can a 5 meters long ribbon be cut into 50 cm long pieces? If yes, how many?','','','5m long ribbon mean 5m = 500 cm\n500 is a multiple of 50.\n∴ 50 cm  long pieces cut into 50 cm each.\nTotal number of pieces made = 500/50 = 10 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27171,305,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write all the twin prime numbers pairs from 1 to 100.','','','(3, 5), (5, 7), (11, 13), (17, 19), (29, 31), (41, 43), (59, 61), (71, 73)','',0,0,0,NULL),(27172,305,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Prime numbers 		ii) Composite numbers','','','i) Prime number : A number which has only two factors, 1 and the number itself, is called as prime numbers.\nii) Composite numbers : A number which has more than two factors is called a composite numbers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27173,305,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : i) Twin prime numbers 		ii) Co-prime numbers','','','i) Twin prime numbers : Prime numbers with a difference of two are called as twin prime numbers.\nii) Co – prime numbers : Numbers which have only 1 as a common factor are called co-prime numbers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27174,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How will you write ‘ninety-two point four eight’ in the decimal form?\na) 92.85 		b) 92.84 		c) 92.48 		d) 94.28\n','','','c) 92.48','',0,0,0,NULL),(27175,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which of the following numbers, the place value of 3 is 3 × 1/100?\na) 43.52 		b) 42.53 		c) 42.35 		d) 45.32\n','','','b) 42.53 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27176,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the place value of the digit 8 in the number ‘Seventeen point zero eight’?\na) 8 			b) 0.8 			c) 0.08 		d) 0.008\n','','','c) 0.08 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27177,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','3/4  = ----\na) 3.25 		b) 0.25		c) 0.75 		d) 0.075\n','','','c) 0.75 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27178,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find','','','a) 1.125','',1,0,0,NULL),(27179,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','7/16 = ?\na) 0.375 		b) 0.4375 		c) 0.475 		d) 0.4735\n','','','b) 0.4375','',0,0,0,NULL),(27180,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sixty-nine seconds equal to how many minutes?\na) 6.9 			b) 1.9 			c) 1.25 		d) 1.15\n','','','d) 1.15','',0,0,0,NULL),(27181,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in decimal form :','','','d) 7.9','',1,0,0,NULL),(27182,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','3/8 = ?\na) 0.375 		b) 0.125 		c) 3.75 		d) 0.425\n','','','a) 0.375 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27183,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the place value of the circular digit in the number 97.05⑦?\na) 7 			b) 0.7 			c) 0.07 		d) 0.007\n\n','','','d) 0.007','',0,0,0,NULL),(27184,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','5 thousand + 3 ten + 7 tenth + 8 hundredth = ?\na) 530.078 		b) 530.78 		c) 53.78 		d) 5030.78\n','','','d) 5030.78','',0,0,0,NULL),(27185,306,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the place value of the digit 9 in the number twelve point zero nine?\na) 9 			b) 0.9 			c) 0.09 		d) 0.009\n','','','c) 0.09 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27186,306,'mcq',