65\ni. e.  9             05\n9 hrs 05 minutes ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27265,307,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subtract : 6 hours 20 min – 4 hours 45 min','','','     Hrs            Min\n      5                60 + 20\n -   4                45\n----------------------\n        1             35\ni. e.  1 hrs 35 minutes ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27266,307,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If a bus that leaves Nashik at 5 o’clock in the morning reaches Pune that same day at ten thirty in the morning, how long does the journey take?','','','The bus leaves at 5 o’clock in the morning. The bus reaches at 10.30 in the morning.\n    10.30\n-  05.00\n-------------\n    05.30\nIt takes 5.30 time to reach the Pune.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27267,307,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A play, that was to start at nine fifteen at night, was delayed by half an hour because of a power failure. What time did the play start?','','','9.15 PM a play start but by problem a power outage it starts by half an hour. i.e. 30 min late\n     9.15\n +  0.30\n------------\n    9.45\nThe play starts at 9.45 PM.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27268,307,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A government office opens at 7 in the morning and closes at 3 in the afternoon. how long is this office open?','','','Opens at 7 Am\nCloses at 3 PM\n     15.00\n  -  07.00\n  ------------\n     8.00\nThe office opens for 8 hours.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27269,307,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A movie starts at 45 minutes past 3 in the afternoon and finishes two and a half hour later. At what time does the movie end?','','','Movie starts at 3 : 45 in the afternoon ends at 2 : 50 hour later.\n      Hrs            Min\n      3                45\n +   2                30\n----------------------\n        5             75\ni. e.  6 hrs 15 minutes \nThe movie ends at 6 : 15 hours','',0,0,0,NULL),(27270,307,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Sakharam ploughed the field from 8 in the morning till 12 : 30 in the afternoon. he stopped and started for home. He reached home at 1 : 30 for lunch. How long did he plough the field? How long did it take him to reach home from the field?','','','The ploughing started at 8 in the morning stopped at 12 : 30 in the afternoon\nReached at 1 : 30 = 13 : 30\n     12 : 30\n-    08 : 00\n-------------------\n     4 : 30\nHe ploughed for 4 hours and 30 minutes\n      13 : 30\n   -  12 : 30\n   ---------------\n       01 : 00\nIt takes one hour to reach home from the field. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27271,307,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Rambhau started the water pump at ten thirty at night and put it off the same night at a quarter to twelve. How long did he keep the water pump on?','','','Started the water pump at 10 : 30 at night 22 : 30 at a quarter twelve\n        = 11.45 = 23.45\n       Hrs            Min\n       23                45\n -    22                30\n----------------------\n        1             15\ni. e.  1 hrs 15 minutes \nThe water give for 1 : 15 hours ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27273,307,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If a train leaves Mumbai at ten fifteen at night and reaches Nagpur at one forty the next afternoon, how long does the journey take?','','','The time from ten fifteen at night to ten fifteen in the morning next day = 12 hours\nThe time from ten fifteen in the morning to one fifteen in the afternoon = 3 hours\nThe time from one fifteen to one forty = (40 – 15) min = 25 min\nThe total time = 12 hours + 3 hours + 25 minutes\n               = 15 hours 25 minutes\nThe journey takes 15 hours 25 minutes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27274,307,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(i – d), (ii – c), (iii – e), (iv – a), (v – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(27275,307,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Geeta taught in the classroom for 2 hours and 25 minutes in the morning and 1 hour and 45 minutes in the afternoon. How long did she teach in all?','','','Add to find the total time\n      Hrs            Min\n      2                25\n +   1                45\n----------------------\n        3             70\ni. e.  4 hrs 10 minutes\nGeeta taught for 4 hours 10 minutes. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27276,307,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If a bank is open for business from 10 in the morning till 4 : 30 in the evening, how long is it open?','','','According to 24-hour clock, 4 : 30 in the evening means 16 : 30\nHere, subtract 10 : 00 from 16 : 30.\n     Hrs            Min\n      16              30\n -    10               00\n----------------------\n        6             30\ni. e.  6 hrs 30 minutes  \nThe bank remains open for 6 hours 30 minutes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27277,307,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If a shop is open from 9 : 30 am to 10 pm, how long is it open?','','','According to 24-hour clock, 10 pm means 22 : 00\nHere, we subtract 9 : 30 from 22 : 00\n         Hrs            Min\n          21               60\n -        9                30\n----------------------\n        12             30\ni. e.  12 hrs 30 minutes \nthe shop is open for 12 hours 30 minutes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27278,307,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If the Maharashtra Express leaving Kolhapur at 15 : 30 arrives at Gondiya the next day at 20 : 15, how long is the journey from Kolhapur to Gondiya?','','','If leaves Kolhapur at 15 : 30 arrives at Gondia at 20 : 15 the next day\n        Hrs      Min               Hrs          Min           Hrs           Min\n         24        00                 8              30              20             15\n   -     15        30            +  20             15          -   00            00\n    -----------------               ------------------           ------------------\n          8         30                   28          45                 20           15\nIt requires 35 hours 45 min to reach from Kolhapur to Gondia.     ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27279,307,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Geeta travelled by train for 2 hours 20 minutes and by rickshaw for 4 hours 40 minutes. How long did she spend travelling?','','','Total time spend to Geeta by train and by rikshaw is \n          Hrs            Min\n           2               20\n +       4                40 \n----------------------\n          6               60\ni. e.  7 hrs 00 minutes   	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27280,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How will you write, 12 m 3 mm in decimal form?\na) 12.03 m		b) 12.003 m		c) 12.3 m 		d) 12.0003 m\n','','','b) 12.003 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27281,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A rikshaw covers 30 km in 1 hour. How far will it travel in three-quarters of an hour at the same speed?\na) 1500 m 		b) 15000 m 		c) 22500 m 		d) 2500 m\n','','','c) 22500 m 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27282,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If there are in all 1002 trees on both the sides of a road at a distance of 12 m each, what is the length of the road?\na) 500 m 		b) 6 km 		c) 60 m 		d) 12 km \n','','','b) 6 km 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27283,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sam travelled 25 km on bicycle, 220 km by train and 175 km by bus. How many more km did he travel by train than on bicycle and bus together?\na) 20 km 		b) 70 km 		c) 25 km 		d) 45 km\n','','','a) 20 km 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27284,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','42000 millimeters long wire is cut equally at 6 places. Find the length of each piece of wire.\na) 700 mm 		b) 600 mm 		c) 7 m 		d) 6 m\n','','','d) 6 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27285,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','30 m long rope cut at 5 places. What will be the length of 2 pieces?\na) 6 m 		b) 10 m 		c) 5 m 		d) 18 m\n','','','b) 10 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27286,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','24 cm 2 mm – 8 cm 8 mm = ?\na) 16 cm 		b) 164 mm 		c) 15 cm 		d) 154 mm\n','','','d) 154 mm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27287,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sayali covered a distance of 4 km in 10 minutes. How much distance will she cover in one minute?\na) 4/10 m 		b) 800 m 		c) 400 m 		d) 40 m\n','','','c) 400 m 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27288,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','10 kg 10 g = how many kg?\na) 10.10 		b) 10.01 		c) 10.001 		d) 10.100\n','','','b) 10.01 		','',0,0,0,NULL),(27289,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Out of 17 kg of sugar, 50 persons each were given 300 g of sugar. How much sugar remained?\na) 300 g 		b) 100 g 		c) 2 kg 		d) 2.3 kg\n','','','c) 2 kg 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27290,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','40 kg – 750 g = how many kg?\na) 390250 		b) 39.250 		c) 39.50 		d) 40.75\n','','','b) 39.250','',0,0,0,NULL),(27291,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','360 grams make how many kilograms?\na) 360 		b) 36 	c) 0.360 		d) 0.036\n','','','c) 0.360 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27292,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A sack contains 48 kg of rice. How many such sacks are required to fill 11040 kg of rice?\na) 230 			b) 460 		c) 320 		d) 640\n','','','a) 230 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27293,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','0.09 kg + 0.5 g – 120 mg = ?\na) 93.38 mg 			b) 93.38 g 		c) 90.38 mg 		d) 90.38 g\n','','','d) 90.38 g','',0,0,0,NULL),(27294,308,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','One marble weigh 75 grams. What is the total weight of 600 such marbles?\na) 4.5 kg 		b) 45 kg 		c) 0.45 kg 		d) 48 kg\n','','','b) 45 kg 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27295,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : 13 km 590 m + 28 km 725 m','','','       Km             m\n        13              590\n   +   28              725\n  ----------------------------\n       41                1315\n    i.e. 42 km 315 m  [1000 m = 1 km]','',0,0,0,NULL),(27296,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subtract : 37 liter 500 ml – 15 liter 750 ml','','','        lit                ml\n        36              1000 + 500\n   -   15                750\n     --------------------------\n      21              750\ni.e. 21 lit 750 ml','',0,0,0,NULL),(27297,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Add : Rs. 9 p 50 + Rs. 14 p 60','','','        Rs           P\n         9            50\n    +  14           60\n   -------------------\n       23           110\n  i.e. Rs. 24 paise 10  [100 paise = 1 Rs.]','',0,0,0,NULL),(27298,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Add : 6 cm 5 mm + 7 cm 9 mm','','','      Cm            mm\n        6                 5\n  +   7             9\n  ---------------------\n      13          14\ni.e. 14 cm 4 mm     [10 mm = 1 cm]','',0,0,0,NULL),(27299,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Subtract : Rs. 19 p 50 – Rs. 12 p 60','','','        Rs              P\n        18               100 + 50\n    -    12                 60\n    ----------------------------\n         6                  90\ni.e. Rs. 6 P 90','',0,0,0,NULL),(27300,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Subtract : 24 cm 2 mm – 3 cm 8 mm','','','         Cm             mm\n          23             10 + 2\n      -   3                8\n -------------------------------\n        20                4\ni.e. 20 cm 4 mm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27301,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Subtract : 20 m 30 cm – 17 m 60 cm','','','        M               cm\n        19               100 + 30\n   -    17                60\n    ------------------------------\n        2                  70\ni.e. 2 m 70 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27302,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The rate of toor dal is Rs. 90 per kg. What is the cost of 3 kg of toor dal?','','','Rate of 1 kg toor dal is ₹ 90\n∴ Cost of 3 kg toor dal = 3 × 90 = ₹ 270\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27303,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many rupees in all are Rs. 11 p 50 and Rs. 8 p 50?','','','        Rs             P\n       11           50\n   +   8             50\n  ------------------------\n       19            100\ni.e. 20 Rupees     [100 paise = 1 Rs.]','',0,0,0,NULL),(27304,308,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','3 m 25 cm of cloth is needed to make 1 dress. How much cloth is needed for 3 such dresses?','','','3 m 25 cm of cloth is needed to make 1 dress.\n∴ Cloth need to 3 such dresses = 3 × 3.25 = 9.75 = 9 m 75 cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27305,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Add : 22 m 50 cm + 25 m 75 cm','','','       M             cm\n       22             50\n  +   25             75\n  ------------------------\n      47             125\ni.e. 48 m 25 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27306,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Add : 15 km 740 m + 13 km 950 m','','','       Km           m\n        15           740\n   +   13            950\n   -------------------------\n       28           1690\ni.e. 29 Km 690 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27307,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Add : 25 kg 650 g + 29 kg 770 g','','','      Kg               g\n       25             650\n   +  29             770\n  -------------------------\n       54             1420\ni.e. 55 kg 420 g','',0,0,0,NULL),(27308,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Add : 19 liter 840 ml + 25 liter 250 ml','','','       lit           ml\n       19           840\n   +  25           250\n   -----------------------\n       44           1090\ni.e. 45 lit 90 ml','',0,0,0,NULL),(27309,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Subtract : 40 km 255 m – 17 km 960 m','','','       Km           m\n       39           1000 + 255\n  -    17            960\n    ------------------------\n       22           265\ni.e. 22 km 265 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27310,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Subtract : 35 kg 150 g – 26 kg 470 g','','','      Kg            g\n      34          1000 + 150\n  -  26        470\n----------------------\n      8          680\ni.e. 8 kg 680 g','',0,0,0,NULL),(27311,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Subtract : 46 liter 200 ml – 38 liter 750 ml','','','       lit          ml\n       45         1000 + 200\n   -   38          750\n    --------------------------\n       7            450\ni.e. 7 lit 450 ml','',0,0,0,NULL),(27312,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If half a liter of milk costs Rs. 22, how much will 7 liters cost?','','','Half a liter of milk costs Rs. 22\n∴ 1 liter milk cost = ₹ 44\n∴ 7 liters milk costs = 7 × 44 = ₹ 308','',0,0,0,NULL),(27313,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much distance does a bus cover in 4 hours at the speed of 50 km/h?','','','Speed of bus is 50 km/hr\n∴ Distance covered by bus in 4 hours = 4 × 50 = 200 km/hrs\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27314,308,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the cost of half a liter of milk is Rs. 25, what will 1 liter of milk cost?','','','Cost of half liter milk = ₹ 25\n∴ Cost of 1 liter milk = 2 × 25 = ₹ 50\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27315,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','On his birthday, Ajay gave 20 liter 450 ml of milk to the children in an Ashramshala and 28 liter 800 ml of milk to the children in an orphanage. How much milk did Ajay distribute?','','','      lit     ml\n     20     450\n +  28     800\n  --------------\n     48      1250\ni.e. 19 lit 250 ml\nAjay donated 49 lit 250 ml milk to Ashramshala.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27316,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Under the Rural Cleanliness Mission, college students cleaned 1 km 750 m of village road that is 2 km 575 m long. How much road remained to be cleaned?','','','         Km          m\n         1            1000 + 575\n    -    1              750\n      ------------------------\n           0            825\ni.e. 0 km 825 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27317,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Babulgaon used 21250 liters of treated waste water in the fields. Samvatsar village used 31350 liters of similar water. How much treated waste water was used in all in the fields?','','','Babhulgaon used waste water = 21,250 lit\nSamvatsar used waste water = 31,350 lit\nTotal water used = 21250 + 31350 = 52,600 lit\n∴ Total waste water used = 52,600 lit','',0,0,0,NULL),(27318,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the speed of a motorcycle is 40 km per hour, how far will it travel in an hour and a quarter?','','','An hour and a quarter = 1 + 1/4 hrs\nFor one hour 40 km and 1/4 h = 10 Km\n∴ Total travel = 40 + 10 = 50 Km\nThe motorcycle travels 46 km in an hour and a quarter. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27319,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If a man walks at a speed of 4 km/h, how long will it take him to walk 3 km?','','','The speed is 4 km/h\n∴ It takes 1 hour to walk 4 Km\n∴ To walk 3 Km, it takes = 3/4 hours = 45 min\n∴ The man takes 45 min.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27320,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If a rickshaw travels at a speed of 30 kmph, how far will it travel in three quarters of an hour?','','','Three quarter = 3/4 hours\n∴ 30 × 3/4 = 90/4 = 22 Km 500 m\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27321,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If one shirts needs 2 m 50 cm of cloth, how much cloth do 5 shirts need?','','','One shirt need 2 m 50 cm of cloth\n∴ 5 shirts needs = 2.50 × 5 = 12.50\n∴ 5 shirts needs 12 m 50 cm cloths. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27322,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If one gold bangle is made from 12 grams 250 milligrams of gold, how much gold will be needed to make 8 such bangles?','','','        Gram     milligrams\n          12              250\n           ×               8\n      -------------------------\n            98           000\nSuch 8 bangles needs 98 gm of gold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27323,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How many pouches each of 20 gm of cloves can be made from 1 kg 240 g of cloves.','','','1 cloves made by 1 kg 240 g = 1240 gm \n∴ 20 cloves made by = 1240/20 = 62 Pouches \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27324,308,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Seema’s mother bought 2 m 70 cm of cloth for a kurta and 2 m 40 cm for a shirt. How much cloth did she buy in all?','','','           m       cm\n            2         70\n      +   2        40\n        --------------------\n              5         10\nShe bought 5 m 10 cm cloth.        ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27325,308,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','During a cleanliness week, children cleaned the public park in their town. They collected three-quarter kilogram of plastic bags and five and a half kilograms of other garbage. How much garbage did they collect in all?','','','Total garbage collection = 0.750 gm plastic bags + 5 kg 500 gm other garbage \n∴ Total garbage = 6 kg 250 gm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27326,308,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If a car travels 60 km in an hour, how far will it travel in \na) 2 hours		         b) 15 minutes 		\nc) half an hour 		d) three and a half hours?','','','a) Speed of car is 60 km per hour\n∴ distance covered by car in 2 hours = 2 × 60 = 120 km\nb) 15 min = 1/4 hours \n∴ Distance covered by car in 15 min = 60 × 1/4 = 15 km\nc) half an hour = 30 min\n∴ Distance covered by a car in 30 min = 60/2 = 30 km\nd) three and half hour = 3.50\n∴ Distance covered by a car in 3 hrs 30 min = 60 × 3.50 = 210 km','',0,0,0,NULL),(27327,308,'descriptive','4',3,2,'Text Book','','A water tank holds 125 liter of water. If 97 liter 500 ml of water is used, how much water remains in the tank?','','','         lit           ml\n       124         1000\n   -    97            500\n    --------------------------\n        27           500\n∴ 27 lit 500 ml water remains in the tank.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27328,308,'descriptive','4',3,2,'Text Book','','Harminder bought 57 kg 500 g of wheat from one shop and 36 kg 800 g of wheat from another shop. How much wheat did he buy altogether?','','','        Kg             gm\n        57             500\n  +    36              800\n     -------------------------\n        94               300\nHe bought 94 kg 300 g wheat altogether. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27329,308,'descriptive','4',3,2,'Text Book','','Renu took part in a 100 m race. She tripped and fell down after running 80 m 50 cm. How much distance did she have left to run?','','','         m          cm\n        99          100\n    -   80           50\n     --------------------\n         19         50\nShe left 19 m 50 cm to run','',0,0,0,NULL),(27330,308,'descriptive','4',3,2,'Text Book','','A sack had 40 kg 300 grams of vegetables. There were 17 kg 700 gram of potatoes, 13 kg 400 gram of cabbage and the rest were onions. What was the weight of onions?','','','Weight of potatoes + cabbage = 17 kg 700 gm + 13 kg 400 gm \n                  = 31 kg 100 gm\nTotal weight of sack = 40 kg 300 gm\n∴ Weight of onion \n           Kg                gm\n           40               300\n      -     31               100\n       ---------------------------\n             9              200\n∴ The weight of the onion is 9 kg 200 gm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27331,308,'descriptive','4',3,2,'Text Book','','One day, Gurminder Singh walked 3 km 750 m and Parmindar Singh walked 2 km 825 m. Who walked farther and by how much?','','','        Km           m\n        2              1000 + 750\n    -   2                825\n     --------------------------\n          0              925\nGurminder Singh walked more distance by 925 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27332,308,'descriptive','4',3,2,'Text Book','','Suresh bought 3 kg 250 gram of tomatoes, 2 kg 500 gram of peas and 1 kg 750 g of cauliflower. How much was the total weight of the vegetables he bought? ','','','        kg               gm\n         3                250\n   +    2                500\n   +    1                750\n  --------------------------\n       7                500\nHe bought total weight of the vegetables are 7 kg 500 gm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27333,308,'descriptive','4',3,2,'Text Book','','The distance of some places from Akola are given below. use them to make word problems and solve the problems : Amravati – 95 km, Bhusawal – 154 km, Nagpur – 249 km, Jalgaon – 181 km.','','','1) Which distance is more? Akola to Bhusawal or Akola to Nagpur? How much more?\n2) Which distance is less? Bhusawal to Akola or Amravati to Akola? How much less?\nSolution : 1) Suppose distance from Akola is 0 Km\nThe distance between Akola to Bhusawal = 95 – 0 = 95 km\nThe distance between Akola and Nagpur = 249 – 0 = 249 km\nThe difference between the distance = 249 – 95 = 154 km\n2) The distance between Bhusawal and Akola = 154 – 0 = 154 km\nThe distance between Amravati and Akola = 95 – 0 = 95 \nThe distance between Amravati and Akola is less than the distance between Bhusawal and Akola = 154 – 95 = 59 km','',0,0,0,NULL),(27334,308,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table and prepare the total bill :','','','Solution : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(27335,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The sides of a triangle are 5 cm, 6 cm and 7 cm. What is its perimeter?\na) 18 cm 		b) 13 cm 		c) 11 cm 		d) 12 cm\n','','','a) 18 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27336,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the perimeter of a square having length of the side 6 cm?\na) 6 cm 		b) 12 cm 		c) 24 cm 		d) 30 cm\n','','','c) 24 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27337,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The perimeter of a square and a rectangle are equal. If the perimeter of the rectangle is 80 cm, what is the length of the side of the square?\na) 80 cm 		b) 40 cm 		c) 20 cm 		d) 10 cm\n','','','c) 20 cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27338,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The perimeter of a rectangle is 60 cm. If its breadth is 10 cm, then what will be its length?\na) 10 cm 		b) 20 cm 		c) 30 cm 		d) 50 cm\n','','','b) 20 cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27339,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If two rectangles having length 13 cm and breadth 5 cm are joined at the breadth, what is the perimeter of the new figure formed?\na) 67 cm 		b) 130 cm 		c) 72 cm 		d) 62 cm\n','','','d) 62 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27340,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A rectangle having length 12 cm and breadth 5 cm is joined to a square of sides 5 cm at the breadth. What is the perimeter of the new figure formed?\na) 22 cm 		b) 44 cm 		c) 17 cm 		d) 54 cm\n','','','b) 44 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27341,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bhushan runs for practice daily 5 times around a square ground and completes 2 km. What is the length of one side of the ground?\na) 100 m 		b) 10 m 		c) 400 m 		d) 40 m\n','','','a) 100 m 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27342,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of a rectangle is 24 cm and its perimeter is 68 cm. Find its area.\na) 92 cm²		b) 120 cm²		c) 240 cm²		d) 1632 cm²\n','','','c) 240 cm²	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27343,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of a rectangle having breadth 20 cm is 1200 cm². What is the perimeter of the rectangle?\na) 160 cm 		b) 120 cm 		c) 100 cm 		d) 80 cm\n','','','a) 160 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27344,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A hall with length 48 m and breadth 12 m requires 64 square shaped tiles. What will be the perimeter of each tile?\na) 9 m 		b) 81 m 		c) 8 m 		d) 12 m\n','','','d) 12 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27345,309,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length and breadth of a rectangular room are 4 m and 3 m respectively. If the square tiles of side 40 cm are to be fitted on the floor of the room, how many tiles will be required?\na) 50 		b) 75 		c) 100 		d) 400\n','','','b) 75 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27346,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the perimeter of given figure','','','Perimeter of given figure = Sum of all sides \n          = 7 + 16 + 20 + 14 = 57 cm\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27347,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the perimeter of given figure','','','Perimeter of given figure = Sum of all sides \n          = 8 + 8 + 10 + 6 + 6 + 10 = 48 cm\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27348,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the perimeter of given figure','','','Perimeter of given figure = Sum of all sides \n          = 8 + 8 + 18 + 12 + 8 = 64 cm\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27349,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Look at the figures on the sheet of a graph paper. Measure their sides with the help of the lines on the graph paper. Write the perimeter of given figure.','','','The perimeter of rectangle ABCD = (2 × 3.5 + 2 × 2.5)\n            = 7 + 5 = 12\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27350,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Look at the figures on the sheet of a graph paper. Measure their sides with the help of the lines on the graph paper. Write the perimeter of given figure.','','','The perimeter of rectangle PQRS = (4 × 2.4 ) = 9.6 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(27351,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Look at the figures on the sheet of a graph paper. Measure their sides with the help of the lines on the graph paper. Write the perimeter of given figure.','','','The perimeter of rectangle EFGH = (2 × 3.8 + 2 × 1.3)\n            = 7.6 + 2.6 = 10.2\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27352,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Look at the figures on the sheet of a graph paper. Measure their sides with the help of the lines on the graph paper. Write the perimeter of given figure.','','','The perimeter of rectangle SVUT = (2 × 3 + 2 × 2)\n            = 6 + 4 = 10\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27353,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of rectangle.','','','Area of rectangle = length × breadth','',0,0,0,NULL),(27354,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of square.','','','Area of square = (side)²','',0,0,0,NULL),(27355,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for perimeter of rectangle.','','','Perimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]','',0,0,0,NULL),(27356,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for perimeter of square.','','','Perimeter of square = 4 × side','',0,0,0,NULL),(27357,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the perimeter of each figure :','','','Perimeter of given figure = Sum of all sides \n          = 2 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 3 = 14 cm\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27358,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the perimeter of each figure :','','','Perimeter of given figure = Sum of all sides \n          = 3 + 2 + 3 + 2  = 10 cm\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27359,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the perimeter of each figure :','','','Perimeter of given figure = Sum of all sides \n          = 5 + 3 + 2 + 5 + 2 + 3 = 20 cm\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27360,309,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the perimeter of each figure :','','','Perimeter of given figure = Sum of all sides \n          = 2 + 8 + 7 + 10  = 27 cm\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(27361,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How much wire will be needed to make a rectangle 7 cm long and 4 cm wide?','','','Length = 7 cm, breadth = 4 cm\nPerimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]\n                   = 2[7 + 4] = 2 × 11 = 22 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27362,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the length of a rectangle is 20 m and its width is 12 m, what is its perimeter?','','','Length = 20 m, breadth = 12 m\nPerimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]\n                   = 2[20 + 12] = 2 × 32 = 64 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27363,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Each side of a square is 9 m long. Find its perimeter.','','','Side of square = 9 m\nPerimeter of square = 4 × side = 4 × 9 = 36 m\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27364,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the length of the side of square is 12 m, find its area.','','','Side of square = 12 m\nArea of square = (side)² = (12)² = 144 m²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27365,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the length of the side of square is 6 cm, find its area.','','','Side of square = 6 cm\nArea of square = (side)² = (6)² = 36 cm²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27366,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the length of the side of square is 25 m, find its area.','','','Side of square = 25 m\nArea of square = (side)² = (25)² = 625 m²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27367,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the length of the side of square is 18 cm, find its area.','','','Side of square = 18 cm\nArea of square = (side)² = (18)² = 324 cm²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27368,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of the base of a square-shaped box is 5 cm. What is the perimeter of the base?','','','Side of square based box = 5 cm\nPerimeter of base = 4 × 5 = 20 cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27369,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the area of the square with side 5 cm?','','','Side of square = 5 cm\nArea of square = (side)² = (5)² = 25 cm²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27370,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much wire will be needed to make a rectangle 8 cm long and 5 cm wide?','','','Here, length = 8 cm, breadth = 5 cm\nPerimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]\n                  = 2 [8 + 5] = 2 × 13 = 26 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27371,309,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Each side of a square is 7 cm long, find its perimeter.','','','Side of square = 7 cm\nPerimeter of square = 4 × side = 4 × 7 = 28 cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27372,309,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If a square of a side 1 cm is cut off from one corner of a square with side 3 cm. What will be the perimeter of the remaining figure?','','','The perimeter of 3 cm square = 4 × side = 4 × 3 = 12 cm\nThe perimeter of 1 cm square = 4 × 1 = 4 cm\nThe perimeter of remaining shape = 8 + 4 = 12 cm both have same Perimeter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27373,309,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If we take 4 rounds around a field that is 160 m long and 90 m wide, what is the distance we walk in kilometers?','','','length = 160 m, breadth = 90 m\nPerimeter of rectangle = 2 × [length + breadth] \n                = 2 × [160 + 90] = 2 × 250 = 500\nThe distance we walk = 500 × 4 = 2000 m or 2 km','',0,0,0,NULL),(27374,309,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Sanju completes 12 rounds around a square part every day. If the side of the park is 120 m, find out in kilometers and meters the distance that Sanju covers daily.','','','One side of the park = 120 m\nPerimeter of the square = 4 × length of one side\n                = 4 × 120 = 480 m\n12 rounds around a square park = 480 × 12 = 5760 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27375,309,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Four rounds of wire fence have to be put around a field. If the filed is 60 m long and 40 m wide, how much wire will be needed?','','','Length = 60 m, breadth = 40 m\nPerimeter of rectangle = 2 × [length + breadth] \n                = 2 × [60 + 40] = 2 × 100 = 200 m\nThe fence have four rounds = 200 × 4 = 800 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(27376,309,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The sides of a triangle are 24.7 cm, 20.4 cm and 10.5 cm respectively. what is the perimeter of the triangle?','','','Perimeter of triangle = sum of all sides \n                = 24.7 + 20.4 + 10.5 = 55.6 cm\nThe perimeter of the triangle = 55.6 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(27377,309,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the cost of 1 sq m of a plot of land is Rs. 900, find the cost of a plot of land that is 25 m long and 20 m broad.','','','Area of rectangle = length × breadth = 25 × 20 = 500 m²\nThe total cost of a plot = 500 × 900 = 4,50,000 rupees.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27378,309,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What will be the labour cost of lying the floor of an assembly hall that is 16 m long and 12 m wide, if the cost of lying 1 sq m is Rs. 80?','','','Length = 16 m, breadth = 12 m\nArea of rectangle = length × breadth = 16 × 12 = 192 m²\n∴ The cost of lying the floor of an assembly hall is 1 sq m is 80 rupees \n         = 192 × 80 = 15,360 rupees \nThe cost is 15,360 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27379,309,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A plot which has some part is square in shape. While some part is rectangle shape. If the square has side 6 cm and the rectangle has 14 cm long and 7 cm wide, which perimeter is more?','','','a) Perimeter of square : Here, side = 6 cm\nPerimeter of square = 4 × side = 4 × 6 = 24 cm\nb) Perimeter of rectangle : length = 14 cm, breadth = 7 cm\nPerimeter of rectangle = 2 [length + breadth]\n                 = 2 [14 + 7] = 2 × 21 = 42 cm\n∴ Perimeter of rectangle is more than perimeter of square.             ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27380,309,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The length of a rectangular plot of land is 50 m and its width is 30 m. A triple fence has to be put along its edges. If the wire costs Rs. 60 per meter, what will be the total costs of the wire needed for the fence?','','','Length = 50 m, breadth = 30 m\nPerimeter of rectangle = 2 × [length + breadth]\n            = 2 × [50 + 30] = 2 × 80 = 160 m\nThe plot has triple fence = 160 × 3 = 480 m\nThe cost of the wire needed for the fence = 480 × 60 = 28800 \n∴ The cost of wire fences 28 rupees 800 paise ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27381,309,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A game requires its players to run around a square playground. Each side of the playground is 20 m long. One player took 5 rounds around the playground. How many meters did he run altogether? ','','','Side of playground = 20 m\nPerimeter of (playground) square = 4 × side\n            = 4 × 20 = 80 m\nOne player took 5 rounds which = 80 × 5 = 400 m\n∴ He ran 400 m altogether.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27382,309,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The side of a square is 4 cm. The length of a rectangle is 8 cm and its width is 2 cm . Find the perimeter and area of both the figures.','','','The area of the square = side × side = 4 × 4 = 16 cm²\nThe area of rectangle = length × breadth = 8 × 2 = 16 cm²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27386,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the given alternatives will complete the following pattern?  : 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, ?\na) 11 			b) 12 			c) 13 		d) 14\n','','','c) 13','',0,0,0,NULL),(27387,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many square numbers are there in the two-digit natural numbers?\na) 10 			b) 8 			c) 7 		d) 6\n','','','d) 6','',0,0,0,NULL),(27388,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many triangular numbers are there in the given list? : 6, 9, 10, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 36\na) 5 		b) 6 			c) 7 			d) 8\n','','','a) 5 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27389,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','1   3    6    10    ?\na) 16 			b) 15 		c) 14 			d) 13\n','','','b) 15 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27390,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which number will come in the place of? In the given pattern?  : 1, 4, 9, 16, ?, 36\na) 22 			b) 25 			c) 28 			d) 31\n','','','b) 25 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27391,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which will be the next letters in the given pattern? AB, BBC, CCCD, ?\na) DDDE 		b) DDDDEF 		c) DDDDE 		d) EEEEF\n','','','c) DDDDE','',0,0,0,NULL),(27392,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which number should be in the box to make the following equality true? : (66 ÷ 6) = (4 + ▯)\na) 4 			b) 7 			c) 6 			d) 5\n','','','b) 7 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27393,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which number should replace the box to make the following inequality true? : (8 × 12) < (70 + ▯)\na) 26 			b) 24 			c) 27 			d) 7\n','','','c) 27 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27394,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the simplification of 6 ÷ 6 + 6 × 6 – 6?\na) 31 			b) 36 			c) 1 			d) 7\n','','','a) 31 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27395,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a = 4 and b = 5, then 5a × 4b = ?\na) 40 			b) 41 			c) 20 			d) 400\n','','','d) 400','',0,0,0,NULL),(27396,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which numbers should be in the boxes to make the following statement true? : (54 ÷ 6) = (▯ + ▯)\na) 3, 7 		b) 7, 4 		c) 4, 5 		d) 5, 6\n','','','c) 4, 5','',0,0,0,NULL),(27397,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Put the appropriate sign in the box : 18 × 4 – 6 ▯ 70 × 2 ÷ 2 – 4 \na) Ʌ			b) > 			c) < 			d) =\n','','','d) =','',0,0,0,NULL),(27398,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following equality is false?\na) 18 + 4 = 88 ÷ 4 		b) 21 × 3 = 9 × 7 		c) 22 ÷ 2 = 9 + 3 		d) 25 – 7 = 12 + 6\n','','','c) 22 ÷ 2 = 9 + 3 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27399,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following expressions has the same answer as the expression : 33 + 22 ÷ 11?\na) 33 ÷ 11 + 22		b) 11 × 5 		c) 33 + 2 × 1 		d) 50 ÷ 10\n','','','c) 33 + 2 × 1 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27401,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If b × 1/8 = 8 then b = ?\na) 1 			b) 16 			c) 64 			d) 72\n','','','c) 64','',0,0,0,NULL),(27402,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','9999 + 9999 = x × 18. For what value of x is the given equality true?\na) 1001 		b) 1010		c) 1111		d) 1100\n','','','c) 1111','',0,0,0,NULL),(27403,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a = 15 and b = 4, then 8a ÷ b = ?\na) 20 			b) 30 			c) 15 			d) 10\n','','','b) 30 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27404,311,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If p = 3, t = 2 then p × p + 2 × p × t + t × t = ?\na) 21 			b) 25 			c) 24 			d) 17\n','','','b) 25 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(27405,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name a number which is square as well as triangular.','','','‘36’ is the number which is square as well as triangular.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27406,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If 4 is the first square number, which is the tenth one?','','','Yes, 121 is the tenth square number.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27407,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : (23 + 4)  = (4 + 23)','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27408,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : (9 + 4) > 12','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(27409,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : (9 + 4) < 12','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27410,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : 138 > 138','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27411,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : 138 < 138','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27412,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : 138 = 138','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27413,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : (4 × 7) = 30 – 2 ','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27414,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : 25/5 > 5','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27415,311,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Fill in the blanks : (12 × 2) ▯ (25 + 10)','','','(12 × 2) < (25 + 10)','',0,0,0,NULL),(27416,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the square numbers from the list given below : 5, 9, 12, 16, 50, 60, 64, 72, 80, 81','','','The square numbers are = 9, 16, 64, 81','',0,0,0,NULL),(27417,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which are the triangular numbers in the given list: 3, 6, 8, 9, 12, 15, 16, 20, 21, 42.','','','The triangular numbers are = 3, 6, 15, 21','',0,0,0,NULL),(27418,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If 3 is the first triangular number, which is the tenth one?','','','Yes, ‘66’ is the tenth triangular number.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27419,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give any two pairs of numbers whose sum is 20.','','','i) (12 + 8) = (22 – 2)\nii) (7 + 13) = (17 + 3)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27420,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say whether the equality 25 + 7 = 8 × 4 is right or wrong.','','','25 + 7 = 32 and 8 × 4 = 32 \nThe given equality is right.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27421,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','9 + 9 = x + 8. For what value of x is the given equality true?','','','9 + 9 = x + 8 \n∴ 18 = x + 8 \n∴ 18 – 8 = x + 8 – 8 ------- (subtract 8 from both sides)\n∴ x = 10','',0,0,0,NULL),(27422,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','For which value of x, will the statement x × 3 = 4 × 6 becomes true?','','','x × 3 = 4 × 6 = 24\n∴ (x × 3)/3 = 24/3 ------- (divide 3 both sides)\n∴ x = 8 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27423,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : i) (5 × 8) = (8 × 5) 		ii) (16 + 0) = 0','','','i) Right        ii) Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27424,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : i) (16 + 0) = 16 		ii) (9 + 4) = 12','','','i) Right       ii) wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27425,311,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Fill in the blanks : i) (45 ÷ 9) ▯ (9 – 4)  		ii) (6 + 1)  ▯  (3 × 2)','','','i) (45 ÷ 9) = (9 – 4)\nii) (6 + 1) > (3 × 2) \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27426,311,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Using brackets, write three pairs of numbers whose sum is 13. Write three equalities using these numbers.','','','i) (6 + 7) = (8 + 5)\nii) (9 + 4) = (11 + 2)\niii) (7 + 6) = (5 + 8)','',0,0,0,NULL),(27427,311,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write the given properties in words : m – 0 = m','','','The subtraction of nay number and zero is the number itself.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27428,311,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write the given properties in words : n ÷ 1 = n','','','The division of any number and one is the number itself.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27429,311,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Use a letter for ‘any number’ and write the following properties in short : The product of any number and zero is zero','','','a × 0 = 0','',0,0,0,NULL),(27430,311,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','a + b = b + a. State this property in words.','','','Sum of any two numbers is equal to the sum of two numbers interchange the digits.\nEx. 1 + 2 = 3 and 2 + 1 = 3\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27431,311,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Fill in the blanks in the expression with the proper numbers : i) (1 × 7)  = (▯× 1)		ii) (5 × 4) > (7 × ▯)','','','i) (1 × 7) = (7 × 1)\nii) (5 × 4) > (7 × 2)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27432,311,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Fill in the blanks in the expression with the proper numbers : i) (48 ÷ 3) < (▯ × 5)		ii) (0 + 1) > (▯ × 5)','','','i) (48 ÷ 3) < (5 × 5)\nii) (0 + 1) > (0 × 5)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27433,311,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Say whether right or wrong : i) 25 – 2 = 3 + 20		ii) 188 < 192		iii) 3 × 6 = 2 × 9','','','i) Right \nii) Right \niii) Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27434,311,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Find four pairs of numbers, one for each of the operations addition, subtraction, multiplication and division that make the number 18. Write the equality for each of them.','','','(9 + 9) = (21 – 3) = (6 × 3) = (36 ÷ 2)','',0,0,0,NULL),(27435,311,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Use a letter for ‘any number’ and write the following properties in short :\ni) The sum of any number and zero is the number itself 		\nii) The product of any two numbers and the product obtained after changing the order of those numbers is the same.','','','i) (x + 0) = x\nii) (a × b) = (b × a)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27436,311,'descriptive','4',4,3,'Out of Text Book','','Fill in the blanks in the expression with the proper numbers : i) (35 ÷ 7) = (▯ + ▯) 		ii) (6 – ▯) < (2 + 3)','','','i) (35 ÷ 7) = (3 + 2)\nii) (6 – 3) < (2 + 3)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27437,311,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use proper symbol >, < or = in the box : i) 18 ▯ 17 		ii) 9 + 7 ▯ 4 + 12		iii) 27 ÷ 3 ▯ 9 + 4		iv) 5 + 5 ▯ 2 × 7','','',' i) 18 > 17\nii) 9 + 4 = 13 and 4 + 12 = 16\n      ∴ 13 < 16\niii) 27  ÷ 3 = 9 and 9 + 4 = 13\n      ∴ 9 < 13\niv) 5 + 5 = 10 and 2 × 7 = 14\n     ∴ 10 < 14','',0,0,0,NULL),(27440,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a cube is made by folding the given net, which number will not be adjacent to the number 2?\na) 3 		b) 6 		c) 4 		d) 5\n','','','d) 5','',1,0,0,NULL),(27441,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following nets will fold into a cube?','','','1) ','',1,0,0,NULL),(27442,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following nets fold into a cuboid?','','','3) ','',1,0,0,NULL),(27443,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following figures is not a net pentomino (five square net)?','','','4) ','',1,0,0,NULL),(27444,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How will the three-dimensional figure given alongside appear as viewed from above?','','','2) ','',1,0,0,NULL),(27445,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','From which of the nets given below can an open box be formed?','','','3) ','',1,0,0,NULL),(27446,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following objects will appear the same when viewed from the front, from a side or from above?','','','1) ','',1,0,0,NULL),(27447,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a cube is formed from the given net which letter will be on the opposite side of the letter A?\n1) A		2) C			3) D 			4) E\n','','','2) C','',1,0,0,NULL),(27448,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following figures is of pentomino?','','','2) ','',1,0,0,NULL),(27450,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The net of a cube-shaped number die is given below. which number will not come on the adjacent surface which has number 5 on it?\n1) 4 			2) 1 		3) 2 			4) 3\n','','','2) 1','',1,0,0,NULL),(27451,310,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A paper is folded according to the figure and a cubical box is made. Which letter will there be opposite to the surface with letter R?\n1) L 		2) A 		3) I 		4) U\n','','','1) L','',1,0,0,NULL),(27452,310,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The given figure shows a structure made of blocks. Say whether each view is from the front, from a side or from above.','','','i) front          ii) side          iii) above','',1,0,0,NULL),(27453,310,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The given figure shows a structure made of blocks. Say whether each view is from the front, from a side or from above.','','','i) above             ii) side             iii) front ','',1,0,0,NULL),(27454,310,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The given figure shows a structure made of blocks. Say whether each view is from the front, from a side or from above.','','','i) side            ii) front              iii) above','',1,0,0,NULL),(27455,310,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','All the surfaces are square, then this shape is called as ------','','','Cube','',0,0,0,NULL),(27456,310,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The arrangement of five square net is called a ------.','','','pentomino ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27457,310,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','All the surfaces are in rectangular in shape, then this shape is called as -----','','','cuboid','',0,0,0,NULL),(27458,310,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define two dimensional objects.','','','The objects which have only length and breadth and no thickness or negligible thickness are called two dimensional objects.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27459,310,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','Define three dimensional objects.','','','The objects which have length, breadth (width) and height are called three dimensional objects.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27460,310,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define net. ','','','The two dimensional shape, from which three dimensional object can ne made by folding it, is called a net of that object.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27461,310,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Draw a picture of a table, which is three dimensional objects as viewed from the front, from a side and from above.','','','Diagram : Infront and from above','',1,0,1,NULL),(27462,310,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Draw a picture of a chair, which is three dimensional objects as viewed from the front, from a side and from above.','','','Diagram : Infront and from above','',1,0,1,NULL),(27463,310,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Draw a picture of a water bottle, which is three dimensional objects as viewed from the front, from a side and from above.','','','Diagram : Infront and from above','',1,0,1,NULL),(27464,310,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Stocks of various types of grains in a warehouse are as given below. Make a pictographs based on the given information :','','','Here, the given numbers are multiple of 8.\nSo, take the scale as 1 picture = 8 sacks \nFor 40 sacks, 40 ÷ 8 = 5 pictures, for 56 sacks, 56 ÷ 8 = 7 pictures, for 8 sacks, 8 ÷ 8 = 1 picture and for 32 sacks, 32 ÷ 8 = 4 pictures \n(Scale : 1 picture = 8 sack)','',1,0,1,NULL),(27465,310,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the scales is appropriate to represent the following information as a pictograph?','','','Here, the given numbers are multiple of 25.\nSo, take the scale as 1 picture = 25 tree \nFor 75 tree, 75 ÷ 25 = 3 pictures, for 50 tree, 50 ÷ 25 = 2 pictures, for 100 tree, 100 ÷ 25 = 4 picture and for 125 tree, 125 ÷ 25 = 5 pictures \n(Scale : 1 picture = 25 tree)','',1,0,0,NULL),(27466,310,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Information about the various types of vehicles in Wadgaon is given below. make a pictograph for this data :','','','Here, the given numbers are divisible by 12.\nSo, take the scale as 1 picture = 12 vehicles \n','',1,0,1,NULL),(27467,310,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The numbers of the various grains kept in a warehouse are as given below. Make a pictograph based on the information given.','','','Here, the given numbers are multiple of 8.\nSo, take the scale as 1 picture = 8 sacks \nFor 16 sacks, 16 ÷ 8 = 2 pictures, for 24 sacks, 24 ÷ 8 = 3 pictures, for 32 sacks, 32 ÷ 8 = 4 picture and for 8 sacks, 8 ÷ 8 = 1 pictures \n(Scale : 1 picture = 8 sack)','',1,0,1,NULL),(27468,310,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make a pictograph showing the information given :','','','Here, the given numbers are multiple of 25.\nSo, take the scale as 1 picture = 25 tree \nFor 75 tree, 75 ÷ 25 = 3 pictures, for 50 tree, 50 ÷ 25 = 2 pictures, for 100 tree, 100 ÷ 25 = 4 picture and for 125 tree, 125 ÷ 25 = 5 pictures \n(Scale : 1 picture = 25 tree)','',1,0,1,NULL),(27469,310,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Answer the given questions (Scale : 1 Δ = 35 students) : \ni) What is the total number of students?	\nii) By how much is the number of students who like Mathematics more than the number of students who like English? ','','','i) Total number of students = 175 for maths + 105 for Marathi + 70 for English + 140 for science = 490 students\nii) Number of students in mathematics = 175 \nnumber of students for English = 70\n∴ 175 – 70 = 105 students like mathematics more than English subjects. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(27470,310,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The number of books on different subjects kept in a cupboard in the school library are given below. Make a pictograph showing the information given : ','','','Here, the given number of books is divisible by 7.\nSo, take the scale as 1 picture = 7 books\n','',1,0,1,NULL),(27471,310,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Stocks of various types of books stored in a bookstall are given below. Make a pictograph based on the information given.','','','Here, the given number of books is divisible by 5\nSo, take the scale as 1 picture = 5 books\n','',1,0,1,NULL),(27472,310,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Answer the following questions from the given information (scale : 1 @ = 6 students): \ni) What is the number of students about whom the information is given in pictorial diagram? 	\nii) By how much is the number of students liking Maths is more than that of the number of students liking Science?\n','','','i) Total number of students = 24 students for maths + 18 students for Marathi + 12 students for Science + 24 students for drawing \n    = 78 students\nii) Number of students in Maths = 24 and number of students in Science = 12\n∴ 24 – 12 = 12 students like maths more than science subjects.','',1,0,0,NULL),(27473,310,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Answer the following questions from the given information (Scale : 1 Δ = 50 students) : \ni) Information about how many students is given in the pictograph?	\nii) How many students coming by Rikshaw are more than the students coming by cycle?','','','i) Total number of students = 150 students for walk + 200 students for Cycle + 300 students by rikshaw + 100 students with parents \n= 750 students\nii) Students coming in rikshaw = 300 and students coming in cycle = 200\n∴ (300 – 200) = 100 students coming in rikshaw more than by cycle.','',1,0,0,NULL),(27904,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : We can see the cells with the naked eye.','','','Wrong','',0,0,0,NULL),(27905,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Plant cells have a definite shape due to the presence of the cell wall around them.','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27906,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : The nucleus controls all functions of the cell.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(27907,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Micro-organisms are present only in water.','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27908,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Antibiotics are produced with the help of specific microbes.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(27909,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Mitochondrion, Vacuoles, Cell wall, Nucleus','','','Cell wall (Cell wall present in only plant cell rest are all present in both plant and animal cell)','',0,0,0,NULL),(27910,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Cholera, Malaria, Typhoid, Hepatitis','','','Malaria (Malaria is infected by mosquito rest are all spread through contaminated water)','',0,0,0,NULL),(27911,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Dengue, Yellow fever, Zika fever, Pneumonia','','','Pneumonia (Pneumonia infected by contaminated air, rest are all spread by mosquitoes.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(27912,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Common cold, Elephantiasis, Cough, Diphtheria','','','Elephantiasis (Elephantiasis is spread by female mosquito)','',0,0,0,NULL),(27913,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What happens to the sweet meal or bread forgotten in a lunch-box for three or four days?','','','If sweetmeat is not consumed in time it becomes rotten and hence cannot be eaten The microbes make the sweetmeats bad. The bread usually shown growth of fungus over it, if kept just like that in a lunch box.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27914,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What happens if clothes remain damp in the rainy season?','','','Due to humid atmosphere, the clothes do not dry completely during rainy season. Such damp clothes emit foul odour. The flies and other insects hover around such clothes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27915,318,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the black or white spot sometime seen on gunny bags?','','','The black or white spots seen on gunny bags are due to the fungal growth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27916,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – d), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(27917,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(27918,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which units are used to measure the size of the cells?','','','Micrometre and nanometre are the units used to measure the size of the cell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27919,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What kind of microscope is used for observing cell?','','','The compound microscope is used for observing cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27920,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where does the micro-organisms present?','','','Micro-organisms are present in the air, water, soil, food, sewage, garbage as well as in the bodies of plant and animals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27921,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who was stated that all cells are formed from pre-existing cells?','','','Rudolph Virchow was stated that all cells are formed from pre-existing cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27922,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Micro-organisms','','','The organisms which cannot be seen with our eyes but only be observed under a microscope are called micro-organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27923,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Fermentation','','','The process in which the carbon compound is converted into another type of carbon compound by the action of micro-organisms is called fermentation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27924,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Pathogens','','','The pathogens are the biological agent causes disease to the host are called pathogens.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27925,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the roots of the pea, bean and fenugreek plants. What could be the function of the nodules on their roots.','','','The root nodules converts the atmospheric nitrogen into its compounds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27926,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does your mother do the make yoghurt from milk?','','','My mother adds few drops of previous yoghurt to make yoghurt from milk.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27927,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who discovered the process of fermentation? ','','','The French chemist Louis Pasteur is discovered the process of fermentation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27928,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are infants vaccinated according to a fixed times schedule?','','','i) Vaccines helps to fight against the serious diseases.\nii) Vaccination is not possible to give all time, so doctor draws a fixed times schedule.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27929,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are lather articles like purses wallets, belts, footwear always polished before storing away.','','','To avoid the fungal growth lather artificial like purses, wallets, belts, footwear always polished before storing away.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27930,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Where does the life process of unicellular organisms take place?','','','Life process of unicellular organisms take place within the cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27931,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How do the cells acquire definite shape?','','','The cells acquire definite shape due to their cell wall and plasma membrane.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27932,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are cells protected?','','','The cell wall of the plant cell protect the cell. Some protective proteins helps to fight against the harmful microbes. Plasma membrane does not allow any other substance in the cell. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27933,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Plant cell and Animal cell','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(27934,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Prokaryotic Cell and Eukaryotic cell','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(27935,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is a ‘cell’?','','','Cell is the fundamental, structural and functional unit of living organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27936,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the different organelles in a cell?','','','Nucleus, endoplasmic reticulum, ribosome, Golgi body, lysosomes, plastids, vacuoles, mitochondria, chloroplast, are some organelles present in the cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27937,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are micro-organisms?','','','The organisms which can not be seen with our eyes but can only be observed under a micro scope are called micro-organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27938,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which are the different types of micro-organisms?','','','Bacteria, viruses, fungi, algae, and protozoa are all the different types of micro- organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27939,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : There is possibility of food poisoning if we eat stale food.','','','i) Stale food has numbers of microbes which produce the toxic material in the food.\nii) Toxic material causes food poisoning.\niii) Therefore, there is possibility of food poisoning if we eat stale food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27940,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Fungus grow quickly in moist or humid condition.','','','i) The fungus needs the moist and damp area for the proper growth and reproduction.\nii) It does not grow in dry temperature.\niii) Hence, the fungus needs the moist or humid condition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27941,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Fodder is soaked in water before offering to cattle.','','','i) Soaked folder has a essential nutrients.\nii) Soaking fooder provide the energy to the cattle.\niii) Due to soaking it becomes easier for digestion.\niv) Hence, fodder is soaked in water be offering to cattle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27942,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by micro-organisms?','','','The organisms which not be seen with our naked eyes but is can be observed under a microscope are called micro-organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27943,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Categories the following organisms in two groups, according to size : Amoeba, worms, snail, Paramecium, pigeon, elephant, euglena.','','','i) Small (micro-organisms) – amoeba, paramecium, euglena\nii) Large – Worms, snail, pigeon, elephant\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27944,318,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why should dry and wet waste be collected separately? ','','','i) The wet waste has organic matter which decomposed easily by microbes.\nii) Dry waste is not biodegradable it remains as it is for a long time.\niii) The dry waste like plastic, fibre, paper are harmful for the health. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27945,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Sketch and describe in your own words the plant cell and animal cell.','','','a) Plant Cell:\ni) Plant cells have a definite shape due to the presence of the cell wall around them.\nii) Cell wall is present which is the outermost covering of the cell.\niii) There is plasma membrane inside the cell wall that encloses cytoplasm.\niv) Plastids is an important cell organelles that present only in the plant cell only.\nv) Chloroplast in the cell helps in the photosynthesis process.','b) Animal Cell:\ni) Plasma membrane is the outermost covering of animal cells.\nii) Vacuoles in animal cell are smaller than plant cells.\niii) Chloroplast is absent in the animal cells.\niv) Due to absence of chloroplast animal cells does not perform the photosynthesis. ',0,0,1,NULL),(27946,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the uses and the harmful effects of micro-organisms.','','','a) Uses of micro-organisms :-\ni) Micro-organisms present in the soil decompose the garbage and converts it into excellent manure.\nii) The microbes residing in the soil and in the root noduels of leguminous plant helps in the biological nitrogen fixation.\niii) Micro-organisms helps in the formation of curd.\niv) Micro-organisms makes the Vital role the fermentation.\nv) By the process of fermentation antibiotics, vaccine are produced.\nb) Harmful-effects of micro-organisms:-\ni) Some microbes releases toxic material and spoils the foodstuffs.\nii) Harmful micro-organisms causes different types of diseases to the animals and plants.\niii) Microbes that causes diseases like malaria, dengue, elephantiasis, yellow fever, chikungunya, zika fever etc.\niv) Food and water get contaminate due to harmful micro-organisms.\nv) Diseases like common cold, cough diphtheria, pneumonia, tuberculosis caused by harmful microbes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27947,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','When will you use a simple microscope and when a compound microscope? Explain in detail how you will use them.','','','i) The simple microscope is used for the study of insects part or parts of the flower.\nii) It is used to observe the smaller objects which can not be seen with our naked eyes.\niii) Compound microscope is used for observing microbes, cells and tissue.\niv) The object is placed over the glass slide and covered over with cover slip.\nv) The mirror of microscope is adjusted for optimum light.\nvi) The coarse focus knob is adjusted first. In this way simple microscope and compound microscope are used for the study of microbes as well as insects.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27948,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Diseases spread on a large-scale during periods of heavy rainfall and floods.','','','i) Heavy rainfall and flood causes the growth of microorganisms is increased due to the favourable atmosphere.\nii) Contaminated water spreads the pathogens rapidly.\niii) Floods and stagnant water is favorable for the growth of mosquitoes that can spread various diseases.\niv) The humid atmosphere in such a condition helps to the micro-organisms and spread of diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27949,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Soil is turned over during tilling.','','','i) Tilling helps to grow the fertility of soil.\nii) Soil contains are the useful bacteria increase the nutritional value in the coil.\niii) Some microbes helps in the nitrogen fixation.\niv) Therefore, for the better yield of crop, the soil is turned over during tilling.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27950,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : A refrigerator is used in almost every home.','','','i) Refrigerator has a very low temperature.\nii) Under this low temperature microbes do not grow and food remains safe.\niii) The spoilage of food is prevented with the help of refrigerators.\niv) Therefore, a refrigerator is used in almost every home.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27951,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Bread ‘rises’ during baking.','','','i) While preparing the bread yeast is added in it.\nii) These yeast carry out the fermentation.\niii) In this process carbon dioxide and some other gases are released.\niv) These gases causes the bread increase in volume.\nv) Therefore, bread rises during baking.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27952,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why is yoghurt mixed in the batter or dough for making rava-idli, bhature naan?','','','i) Yoghurt contains useful bacteria causes the better fermentation.\nii) The heat, carbon dioxide and other gases helps to rise the flour.\niii) It increases the nutrition in the food and make the tasty.\niv) Therefore, the yoghurt is mixed in the batter, or dough for making rava idli, bhature naan.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27953,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How do preparation like yoghurt, idli, dosa, becomes easy to digest.','','','Idli-dosa are made from the batter is fermented. Yoghurt as prepared with the help of useful bacteria. The process of fermentation increases the nutrient and taste and also it make light and more nourishing vitamin content.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27954,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is correlation between the normal body temperature of human which is 37⁰C and the optimum temperature for the growth of micro-organisms 15⁰C to 35⁰C?','','','i) Micro-organisms needs the favorable condition for their growth.\nii) They needs temperature between 15°C to 35°C.\niii) The normal body temperature of human is 37°C at this temperature.\niv) Resulting micro-organisms can be killed above the temperature 37°C.\nv) Thus, man protected by bacteria attack.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27955,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the well labelled diagrams :  Plant cell','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(27956,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the well labelled diagrams : Animal cell','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(27957,318,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the well labelled diagrams : Prokaryotic cell','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(27973,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : All types of changes are the useful changes.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(27974,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Bursting of a balloon is a quick change.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27975,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Metals become strong due to corrosion ','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(27976,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Applying colours on the metal prevent the corrosion.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27977,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Evaporation is a physical change.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(27978,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Baking bread is a harmful change.','','','False. Baking bread is a useful change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27979,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Rusting of iron is a physical change.','','','False. Rusting of iron is a chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27980,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : The objects become durable due to corrosion.','','','False. The objects become weak due to corrosion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27981,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : A number of different criteria can be applied to the same change.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(27982,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Which type of change is the change of season from summer to rain to winter?','','','After summer, comes the rainy season. After rainy season follows the winter. This cycle of seasons goes continuously on the earth. This occurs in specific time. Therefore change of seasons is a periodic change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27983,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of chemical change.','','','Rusting of iron, conversion of milk into curd, ripening of fruits etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27984,320,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by periodic change?','','','Changes that occurs again and again after a definite intervals of time. Such changes are called periodic changes.\nExamples : Sunset and sunrise, changes of seasons\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27985,320,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(27986,320,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by harmful changes?','','','Changes that do us harm are called harmful changes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27987,320,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is evaporation?','','','The process of formation of vapour from a liquid is called evaporation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27988,320,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by physical changes?','','','The changes in which the original properties remains same, no new substance is formed such changes are called physical changes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27989,320,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by galvanization?','','','A thin coat of zinc is given to prevent the corrosion of iron article is called galvanization.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27990,320,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified : Conversion of milk into yoghurt.','','','Useful change, man-made change, chemical change, irreversible change, slow change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27991,320,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified : Bursting of a cracker.','','','Harmful change, man-made change, chemical change, irreversible change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27992,320,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified : Occurrence of an earthquake ','','','Natural change, quick change, harmful change, irreversible change, non-periodic change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27993,320,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified : Revolution of the earth around the Sun.','','','Periodic change, natural change, reversible change, slow change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27994,320,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified :  Stretching of a spring.','','','Reversible change, man-made change, slow change','',0,0,0,NULL),(27995,320,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the different types of changes?','','','Various types of changes occurs in our surrounding :\ni) natural and man-made change\nii) Useful and harmful change\niii) Reversible and irreversible change\niv) Periodic and non-periodic change\nv) Physical and chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(27996,320,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are natural changes?','','','Changes that occurs naturally in the surrounding are called natural changes.\nExamples : Ripening of fruits, spoiling of milk, falling of leaves of trees, changes of seasons etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(27997,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write the difference between : Physical change and Chemical change','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(27998,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Periodic change and non-periodic change','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(27999,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Natural change and Man-made change','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28000,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : While purchasing canned food, its expiry date should be checked.','','','i) All canned food items has specific life span during the life span every food items remains safe for eating.\nii) The expiry date is mentioned on the can. But after the expiry date is over food may not remains in good condition.\niii) Due to consumption of such food items effect on our health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28001,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : A wooden articles should be polished.','','','It is an organic material which be affected by fungus or termites. Due to them wood get damaged polishing wooden prevent the damage. Hence, a wooden article should be polished.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28002,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What will you take into account while identifying the following : A physical change in a substance.','','','If the properties and composition of original substance does not change after processing, then it is a physical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28003,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What will you take into account while identifying the following : A chemical change in substance.','','','When the properties of original substance are changed, then we said to be it is a chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28004,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the causes of the changes occurring in our surrounding?','','','The causes of the changes occurring are as follows.\ni) Natural events.\nii) Human activities.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28005,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What happen when water is taken in a bowl and heated?','','','When water is taken in bowl and heated, it becomes warm first and then a certain temperature it will start boiling and converts in vapours.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28006,320,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which of the following physical changes and which are chemical changes. Making table from wood, burning of a wood, breaking of glass object, ripening of tomato, rusting of iron.','','','Physical change : \ni) Making a table from a wood\nii) Breaking of a glass object\nChemical change : \ni) Burning wood\nii) Ripening of tomato\niii) Rusting of iron','',0,0,0,NULL),(28007,320,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : An iron article should be given a coat of paint.','','','i) An iron articles rust due to change weather which is called corrosion.\nii) If the iron comes in the contact of moisture, oxygen, vapours causes the corrosion.\niii) This corrosion makes article weak and unusable.\niv) Therefore, to keep it in a stronger condition it should be given a coat of paint.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28008,320,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Copper and brass utensils should be tipped.','','','i) When the metal like copper and brass come in contact of moisture oxygen or vapour a greenish layer is formed on these metals.\nii) Due to this corrosion is take place.\niii) To prevent this corrosion, copper and brass utensils are tinned.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28009,320,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : A dry handkerchief get wet at one on dipping in water, but it takes along for a wet handkerchief to dry.','','','i) Dipping the dry clothes absorb the water. Once it is dipped in water. It is quick change.\nii) But drying such wet handkerchief is a slow process which occurs due to evaporation.\niii) Evaporation is a physical change which depends upon the temperature and humidity.\niv) Therefore, it takes long time for a wet handkerchief to dry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28010,320,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Read the paragraph and note down the various types of changes it mentions: It was nearing six o’clock in the evening. The sun was setting. A breeze was blowing. Leaves on the tree were shaking. Sahil was sitting in the courtyard, rolling balls of wet soil and shaping them into various toys. Then he felt hungry and went into the house. Mother made a dough from wheat flour and fried purees. While eating hot purees, his attention was drawn outside the window. It had started raining. There was lighting, too. Sahil was enjoying his dinner in the dim light.','','','1) It was nearing six o\'clock in the evening: (Time-reversible, periodic)\n2) The sun was setting: (Sunset- Periodic, reversible)\n3) A breeze was blowing: (Blowing of wind-Physical, non-periodic)\n4) Leaves on the tree were shaking: (Movements due to wind- Physical, periodic) \n5) Sahil was sitting in the courtyard, rolling balls of wet soil and shaping them into various toys. (Making toys of soil-Physical) \n6) Then he felt hungry and went into the house. (Hunger-Periodic)\n7) Mother made a dough from wheat flour and fried purees. (Frying-Physical, irreversible)\n8) While eating hot purees, his attention was drawn outside the window. (Seeing randomly-Non-periodic)\n9) It had started raining. (Raining-Physical, Periodic if raining in specific season) \n10) There was lightning. (Lightning-Physical, Chemical, Fast)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28011,320,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by man-made changes? Which are these changes?','','','The changes occurs due to human activities are called man-made changes.\ni) Baking bread                                  ii) Making furniture.\niii) Bursting of fire crackers             iv) Pollution of water.\nv) Cooking food. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28012,320,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What processes occurs during the change of state of matter.','','','The following processes occurs during the change of state of matter.\ni) Boiling              ii) Freezing             iii) Melting\niv) Evaporation      v) Sublimation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28013,320,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give some examples of fast and slow changes that occurs in your surroundings.','','','Examples: \na) Fast Changes :\ni) Bursting of balloon           ii) Bursting of fire crackers.\niii) Switching on an electric bulb.\nb) Slow Changes : \ni) Rusting of iron         ii) Growth of baby\niii) Curdling of milk','',0,0,0,NULL),(28014,320,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by irreversible change? Give some examples.','','','Changes which are permanent and cannot be reversed is called irreversible change.\nExamples: Rusting of iron, burning of wood, curdling of milk, ripening of mango. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28038,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : All elements are metals','','','Wrong','',0,0,0,NULL),(28039,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : The smallest particle of elements are made of only one type of atom','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28040,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : There are 92 elements occurs in the nature','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28041,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Sulphur is a metalloid.','','','Wrong','',0,0,0,NULL),(28042,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : All compounds have the same property.','','','Wrong','',0,0,0,NULL),(28043,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Aluminum, Sodium, Argon, Carbon ','','','Aluminium (Rest are all non-metals)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28044,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Sulphur, Carbon, Nitrogen, Argentum','','','Argentum (Rest are all non-metals)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28045,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Air, water, milk, acetic acid','','','Acetic acid (Rest are all mixtures)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28046,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Stainless steel, copper, silver, gold','','','Stainless steel (Rest are all element)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28047,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Element','','','Aluminium, Sodium','',0,0,0,NULL),(28048,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Elements in molecular form','','','Oxygen, Hydrogen, Fluorine, Chlorine ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28049,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Metals','','','Gold, Silver, Copper, Mercury, Sodium','',0,0,0,NULL),(28050,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Non-metals','','','Carbon, Sulphur, Oxygen, Hydrogen','',0,0,0,NULL),(28051,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Compound','','','Water, common salt, sugar, washing soda','',0,0,0,NULL),(28052,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Alloy','','','Brass, Steel, Stainless steel','',0,0,0,NULL),(28053,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which element helps combustions?','','','Oxygen helps in combustion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28054,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Does water help combustion?','','','No. In fact water stops the combustion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28055,321,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Are all mixtures useful to us?','','','No, some mixtures are harmful for us. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28056,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – e), (2 – f), (3 – c), (4 – b), (5 – a), (6 – d)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28057,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – c), (2 – b), (3 – d), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28058,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of each : Metals','','','Iron, Copper, Gold, Silver.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28059,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of each : Non-metals','','','Sulphur, Chlorine, Phosphorus, Nitrogen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28060,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of each : Compounds','','','H₂O, H₂SO₄, NaCl, MgCl₂.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28061,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of each : Mixtures','','','Air, Mixture of oil and water, Juice.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28062,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Element','','','A substance whose molecules are made of one or more atoms which are exactly alike, is called an element.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28063,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define :  Atom','','','Atom is the smallest particle of an element.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28064,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Compound','','','The substance formed by a chemical combination of two or more elements is called a compound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28065,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Molecule ','','','Molecule is the smallest particle of a compound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28066,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Mixture','','','When two or more different elements or compounds are mixed with each other, it is called a mixture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28067,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Alloy','','','When some metals are difficult to use in pure form they are mixed in particular proportion with each other. By such mixing their properties are modified it. Such a mixture of metals is called an alloy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28068,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Symbol','','','Symbols are short forms used to refer an element.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28069,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How many different states of matter are there? Name them.','','','There are three states of matter they are solid, liquid and gaseous. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28070,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What brings about a change of state of matter?','','','Change in temperature brings about a change of state of matter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28071,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are objects made of?','','','Objects are made up of matter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28072,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the mixtures used in everyday life?','','','Air, food, cold drinks, cement, juice are some mixtures used in day-to-day life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28073,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Is carbon dioxide is an element?','','','No, it is compound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28074,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Are the atoms of different elements similar or dissimilar?','','','The atoms of different elements are dissimilar. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28075,321,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which properties of a liquid are seen in the distillation method?','','','Vaporization and condensation of liquid are the properties seen in distillation method. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28076,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write the names of elements from the following symbols : Zn, Cd, Xe, Ti, Cu, Fe, Si, Ir, Pt.','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28077,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Metals and Non-metals','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28078,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Mixtures and Compounds','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28079,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Atoms and Molecules','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28080,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Separating by distillation and separating by funnel','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28081,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Buttermilk is churned to get butter.','','','i) Churning is a good technique which helps to separate butter from buttermilk.\nii) Due to the centrifugal force all particles of butter collected together.\niii) Therefore, buttermilk is churned to get butter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28082,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : In chromatography, the ingredients of a mixture rise up to a limited height when water rises up to the upper end of paper.','','','In this method two opposite properties are used.\ni) The solubility of a substance in the solvent that moves up.\nii) The ability of the substance to stick to the stationary filter paper. Hence, water is a solvent, it rises rapidly across the chromatography filter paper.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28083,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : A wet cloth wrapped around a water storage container in summer.','','','i) There is high temperature in summer.\nii) Water in the container can be evaporate due to this temperature.\niii) To prevent the evaporation of water and to keep the water cool in the container, wet cloth is wrapped around a container.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28084,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the articles of every day use made of electric wire, kitchen utensils, nails, tables and chairs, window panes, soil, salt, sugar?','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28085,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which metals do we use in day-to-day life?','','','The following metals we use in day-to-day life : Iron, Gold, Silver, Brass, Copper','',0,0,0,NULL),(28086,321,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which elements are present in air?','','','Nitrogen, Oxygen, Argon and minute percentage of Helium, Neon, Krypton, Xenon and radon are the elements present in the air. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28087,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the names of elements and name in use of following symbols : Au, Ag, Ne, Ni, He, Hg, W','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28088,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the molecular formulae of the following compounds : Hydrochloric acid, Sulphuric acid, Sodium chloride, Glucose, Methane','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28089,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How are the components of mixtures separated by simple method?','','','There are various simple methods are used for mixture. These methods are as follows. Straining, Sifting, Picking, Winnowing.\n1) Straining : This method is used for separating insoluble substances from the solution.\n2) Sifting :\ni) It is used for separating stones, or large particles from the grains.\nii) The smaller and larger particle from the mixture get separated.\n3) Picking : By this method unnecessary substances are separated.\n4) Winnowing : Substances which are lighter in weight or unwanted are separated by this methods.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28090,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which elements (metal and non-metals) compound and mixture do we use in our day-to-day life?','','','1) Elements used in a day to - day life : Metals: Iron, Copper, Gold, Silver, etc. 2) Non-metals : Phosphorus, Oxygen, Carbon, Nitrogen, etc.\n3) Compounds used in day-to-day life : Water, salt (sodium chloride), toothpaste (sodium flouride), glucose, etc. \n4) Mixtures used in day-to-day life : Air, milk, food, juice, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28091,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In every day life, where and for what purpose do we use centrifugations?','','','By centrifugation method the following task can be done.\ni) Separation of cream from milk.\nii) Drying wet clothes in a washing machines.\niii) Particles of butter are separated from buttermilk.\niv) Solid components are separated from blood.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28092,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Where are the methods of separation by distillation and by separating funnel used? Why?','','','Distillation:\n i) It is used to separate impurities or dissolved salts from the solution.\nii) Solute and solvent can be separated by this method.\niii) For removing salt from sea water this help making potable water.\nUsing Separating Funnel :\ni) Immiscible mixture can be separated by this method.\nii) It is used to separate kerosene and water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28093,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which precaution will you take while using the method of distillation and separation by separating funnel?','','','The following precautions to be taken while using the method of distillation and separation by separating funnel are:\ni) The apparatus will be connecting properly.\nii) Gloves will be used while handling the chemicals.\niii) Area must be well verticillated to prevent accumulation of vapour.\niv) The condenser tube should be properly cooled.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28094,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the properties of matter?','','','Properties of matters are:\ni. Mass           ii. Brittleness           iii. Hardness            iv. Solubility\nv. Transparency          vi. Fluidity         vii. Elasticity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28095,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which of the element are metals, which are non-metals and which are metalloids?','','','1) Metals: Aluminium, Copper, Gold, Silver. \n2) Non-metals : Sulphur, Phosphorus, Nitrogen, Carbon.\n3) Metalloids : Arsenic, Silicon, Platinum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28096,321,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','For what purpose is distilled water used?','','','i) It is used for manufacturing cold drinks.\nii) It is used in laboratories.\niii) Distilled water is used in car batteries.\niv) It is used for health care.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28107,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Fluoride in the toothpaste helps to prevent tooth decay.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(28108,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Soap nut is a chemical detergent.','','','Wrong','',0,0,0,NULL),(28109,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Hard soap is used for washing clothes.','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28110,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Synthetic detergent doe not effects on the skin.','','','Wrong','',0,0,0,NULL),(28111,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  Many years ago toothpaste was made by mixing the powder of bones and shells.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28112,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  The molecules of a detergent are short.','','','False. The molecules of a detergent are long.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28113,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  One effect of surface activity is no lather formation.','','','False. One effect of surface activity is lather formation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28114,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : About 2000 years ago. In those days, soap was prepared using animal fat and wood ash.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28115,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : In 1756, the Romans developed the method of making aqueous cement.','','','False. In 1756, the British engineer, John Smeaton developed the method of making aqueous cement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28116,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the similarities between Natural detergents and man-made detergents.  ','','','Both are used for cleaning.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28117,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the similarities between Soap and synthetic detergents.','','','Both are used cleaning.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28118,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the similarities between Bath soap and soap for washing clothes.','','','Both the soaps contains fatty acids and both are used for cleansing. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28119,322,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the similarities between Modern cement and ancient cement.','','','Both are used for construction work. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28120,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What should be expected from a detergent for delicate garments?','','','Soap nut and soap pod are the best detergent for the delicate garments, as they do not have any harmful effect on delicate silk and woollen threads and cloth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28121,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How will you check with the help of soap powder whether water is hard?','','','The soap does not produce foam in hard water, due to the dissolved salts in the hard water. Thus, by using soap powder we can understand if water is hard.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28122,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Nowadays, why are the roads made of concrete?','','','These days there is a very heavy traffic on the road. Therefore, there is need of strong roads that can support speedy transport. Therefore, nowadays, roads are made of concrete.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28123,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What causes the hardness of water?','','','If the salts of calcium and magnesium such as bicarbonates, chlorides and sulphates are dissolved in the water, then there is hardness caused to the water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28124,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are important ingredients of the toothpaste?','','','Calcium Carbonate and Calcium Hydrogen Sulphate are the important ingredients of the toothpaste.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28125,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by natural detergents?','','','Detergents obtained from the nature are called natural resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28126,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is prepared the concrete?','','','Concrete is prepared by mixing cement, water, sand and gravel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28127,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of soap is used for bathing.','','','Soft soap are used for bathing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28128,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the ingredients in cement?','','','The ingredients of simple cements are silica (sand), alumina (aluminum oxide), lime, iron oxide and magnesia (magnesium oxide)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28129,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of detergent that you use.','','','Soap, washing soda, shampoo, toothpaste are some examples of detergents.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28130,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What do you use today to clean our teeth?','','','A variety of toothpaste and tooth powder are used today.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28131,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the source of the fluoride in a toothpaste or tooth powder?','','','Sodium fluoride is the source of fluoride in toothpaste or tooth powder.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28132,322,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do we use for cleaning body?','','','Soap, handwash, toothbrush, water, shampoo, toothpaste, facewash, tooth powder are the substances we used for cleaning our body. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28133,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Natural detergents and man-made detergents','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28134,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Soap and synthetic detergent','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28135,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Bath soap and soap for washing clothes.','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28136,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Modern cement and ancient cement','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28137,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the important ingredients of toothpaste and what is the function of each.','','','i) Calcium carbonate and calcium hydrogen phosphate are the important ingredients of the toothpaste.\nii) These ingredients remove the dirt and polish the teeth.\niii) Fluoride prevents the tooth decay and makes the enamel of teeth strong.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28138,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain soap can not be used in hard water.','','','i) Hard water is mixture of different salts, therefore soap does not give lather but form a scum.\nii) Due to this soap lose its cleaning properties.\niii) Hence soap cannot be used in hard water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28139,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain synthetic detergents are superior to soap.','','','i) Because, in synthetic detergent varieties of chemicals which helps to remove the dust or to help in cleaning.\nii) Therefore, synthetic detergent are superior to soap.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28140,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain tobacco masher should not be used for cleaning teeth.','','','i) Tobacco has various bad effects on body.\nii) It may cause oral cancer, hence it is not used.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28141,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by natural and man-made materials?','','','i) The materials which are obtained from the nature are called natural materials.\nii) Materials those made by man, they are called man-made materials.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28142,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make a list of natural and man-made substances you see around you.','','','i) Natural Substances : Water, milk, cotton, soil, wood.\nii)  Man-made Substances: Toothpaste, Oil, yoghurts, ghee, clothes, nail paint, soap.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28143,322,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the materials used for construction?','','','Cement, sand, water, gravel, iron, wood concrete are the different materials used for construction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28144,322,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How does the use of a detergent help to clean soiled clothes?','','','i) The molecules of detergent are long in their structure.\nii) It holds on to a water molecule on one side and the oil molecule onto the other.\niii) As a result, the molecules of oil mix with water.\niv) Hence, it come out of the cloth along with dirt. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28145,322,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What will happen if cement is not used in making concrete?','','','i) Concrete is prepared by mixing cement, water, sand and gravel.\nii) Cement hold the other material strongly to make it hard.\niii) If cement is not used in concrete the other constituent will not mixed properly. \niv) To make hard and strong slab, blocks, pillars, constructions. Cement is mixed in the concrete.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28146,322,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by surface activity?','','','i) The property of a substance of spreading on a surface is called surface activity.\nii) The substance that spread known as surfactant.\niii) Detergents are surface active.\niv) One effect of surface activity is lather formation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28147,322,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain oil does not mix in water, however oil and water become homogeneous if a sufficient quantity of detergent is added.','','','i) Detergent has a long molecule in its two end.\nii) It holds the oil at one end and of detergent in water other end water.\niii) Hence, it makes mixture of water and oil.\niv) Thus, oil and water will not mixed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28148,322,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain often coloured spot are formed cloths during washing.','','','i) Addictive chemical in the detergent remains as it is on the clothes.\nii) Therefore coloured spot are formed cloths during washing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28149,322,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which substances were used earlier for cleaning teeth?','','','The following substances were used to cleaning teeth.\nAcacia bark, neem twinges, coal powder, ash, tooth powder, salt pomegranate etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28150,322,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What causes of the hardness of water?','','','Due to the presence of magnesium salt and calcium salt in the water causes the hardness of water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28151,322,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nowadays why are the roads made of concrete?','','','i) Concrete is made by mixing cement, sand, water and gravel.\nii) Due to concrete become strong, hard and durable.\niii) Therefore, for longer life of the roads, roads are made by concrete.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28172,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : All metals occur in free state in nature','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28173,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Metals are obtained from their ores','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28174,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Bauxite is the most important ore of aluminum.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28175,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sulphate is used in making ornaments.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28176,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sea cucumbers are used as medicines for treating cancer and tumors. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28177,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Mica, gold, Sulphur, potash','','','Gold - Gold is metal mineral while other all are non-metal minerals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28178,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Silver, bauxite, Iron, graphite','','','Graphite - Graphite is a non-metal mineral while all other are metal mineral.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28179,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Coal, mineral oil, gypsum, natural gas','','','Gypsum - Gypsum is a non-metal mineral while other all are energy minerals. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28180,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Fishes, thorium, shells, fungi','','','Thorium - Thorium is a mineral resource while other all are bio-resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28181,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Methane, Ethane, Propane, Butane','','','Methane (All others are constituents of natural gas that are in very small proportions).','',0,0,0,NULL),(28182,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Magnetite, Jade, Zircon, Emerald','','','Magnetite (All others are gem minerals)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28183,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Magnetite, Hematite, Limonite, Graphite','','','Graphite (All others are ores of iron)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28184,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Diamond, Halite, Ruby, Sapphire','','','Halite (All others are gems)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28185,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Mica, Sulphur, Gypsum, Bauxite','','','Bauxite (All others are non-metallic minerals)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28186,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by ores?','','','Minerals that contain a high proportion of metal are called ores.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28187,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which minerals founds in free state in the nature?','','','Gold, Silver, Copper, Platinum and Bismuth occur in the free state in the nature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28188,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the different types of coal?','','','Peat, lignite, bituminous coal and anthracite are the various types of coals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28189,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the medicinal plants that you know.','','','Adulsa, Bel, Neem, Periwinkle, Cinnamon, Cinchona etc. are the medicinal plants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28190,323,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ocean resources?','','','Oil and gas, fish and marine organisms, iodine etc. are the ocean resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28191,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – c), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28192,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – d), (2 – c), (3 – a), (4 – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28193,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by fossil fuel? What are their types?','','','i) Fuels which are formed from the remains of plant and animals that burried underground millions of years ago is called fuel.\nii) Coal, mineral oil and natural gas are the types of fossil fuels. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28194,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Name the two important stages in the process of obtaining metals from ores.','','','Extraction and purification are the two important stages in the process of obtaining metals from their ores.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28195,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by natural resources?','','','The resources that obtained from nature and fulfil our needs are called natural resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28196,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give some examples of natural resources.','','','Air, water, soil, minerals, plants and animals are some example of natural resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28197,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by fuels?','','','Substances which are used for obtaining energy are called fuels.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28198,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by lithosphere?','','','The land portion which occupies by stones, rock and soil is called lithosphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28199,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by forest?','','','An extensive area of land covered by a variety of plants is called a forest.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28200,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by metal minerals and non-metal minerals?','','','i) Metal Minerals: The mineral that contain metallic element is called a metal mineral.\nii) Non-metal Mineral: The mineral that does not contain metallic element is called a non-metal minerals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28201,323,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What would be happen if underground mineral resources are exhausted?','','','i) If mineral resources are exhausted, there would be energy crisis in the World.\nii) All the things that dependent upon mineral get stopped.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28202,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of the components we obtain from mineral oil.','','','The components obtained from mineral oil are as follows:\ni. Aviation petrol                 ii. Gasoline              iii. Diesel\niv. Kerosene                         v. Naphtha              vi. Lubricating oil\nvii. Tar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28203,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is the diversity of plants and animals in the forest declining?','','','i) People are cutting the trees for various purposes such as farming, industries, building houses, making roads etc. Which effect of diversity of plants.\nii) The diversity in animals decline due to hunting of animals polluting, habitat destruction etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28204,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What steps are taken for protection and conservation of natural resources.','','','i) Prevent the excessive use of natural resources.\nii) Use of alternative sources.\niii) Renewable resources should be used for the protection and conservation of natural resources.\niv) Reforestation programmes have been launched on a large scale.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28205,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How does the economic condition of a nation depend on its natural resources?','','','i) The raw materials are obtained from the natural resources for manufacturing of goods, articles and appliances.\na) Wood from the forest.\nb) Shells and conches from the seas or oceans.\nii) These natural resources provides income, employments, occupations for the citizens.\niii) A country can earn a great amount of exchange of foreign by exporting natural resources.\niv) Thus, the economic condition of a nation depend on its natural resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28206,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which natural resources do we use as fuels?','','','Natural resources used as fuels are:\ni) Petroleum             ii) Diesel          iii) Coal\niv) Kerosene             v) LPG               vi) CNG etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28207,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the uses of forest?','','','i) Forest prevent the soil erosion and control floods.\nii) Forest helps in percolation of water and protecting wildlife and maintain the environment.\niii) Medicinal plants, wood, fruits, gum, rubber, honey are useful things provided by the forest.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28208,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the oceans of the earth.','','','The oceans of the earth are :\ni) Arctic ocean         ii) Indian ocean          iii) Pacific ocean\niv) Atlantic ocean    v) Antarctic ocean','',0,0,0,NULL),(28209,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How is sea water useful to us even though it is salty?','','','For the following purpose sea water is useful for us even though it is salty.\ni) It provides us sea foods.\nii) Sea water gives habitat to the marine ecosystem.\niii) It helps to maintain water cycle.\niv) It helps for the transportation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28210,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why are all minerals not ores?','','','i) Mineral occurs naturally in the earth\'s crust.\nii) Ore has high proportion of metal.\niii) Each mineral is not suitable for profitable extraction of a particular metal.\niv) Hence, all ores are minerals but all minerals are not ores.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28211,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is mineral oil called ‘liquid gold’?','','',') i) It is valuable and non-renewable natural resource.\nii) It\'s reserves are limited.\niii) It is very expensive and essential to all countries in the World.\niv) Hence, mineral oil called the liquid gold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28212,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is coal called ‘black gold’?','','','i) Coal is a natural resource.\nii) It is costly and have a great demand and used for various purposes.\niii) It is black in colour.\niv) Hence, coal called black gold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28213,323,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is natural gas an eco-friendly fuel?','','','i) On burning or combustion it does not produce any solid waste.\nii) As compare to other fossil fuels it produces 50% less carbon dioxide.\niii) Natural gas do not make the air pollution.\niv) Therefore, natural gas is an eco-friendly fuel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28214,323,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What do we get from forest?','','','The following product or substances we get from the forest.\ni) Medicinal plant            ii. Wood             iii. Fruits              iv. Paper\nv. Rubber.                          vi. Honey          vii. Aromatic Substances\nviii. Gum                            ix. Catechu       x. Dyes','',0,0,0,NULL),(28215,323,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the items included in ocean resources? What are their uses?','','','The items included in ocean resources and their uses are mentioned in the following :\na) Mineral Resources :\ni) Thorium - In production of atomic energy.\nii) Magnesium - In flash bulb of a camera.\niii) Potassium - In production of soap glass and fertilizers.\niv) Sodium - In production of cloths and paper.\nv) Sulphate - For making artificial silk, rubber.\nb) Bio – Resources : \ni) Fishes - Used as sea food.\nii) Dried shrimp Bombay duck powder - Used as poultry feed.\niii) Shells - Used in preparation of medicines, ornaments and decorative articles.\niv) Fungi - Used for the production of antibiotics.\nv) Shark & codfish - Used for producing edible oil rich in vitamin A, D and E.\nvi) Sea cucumber - Used medicine for treating cancer and tumors.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28216,323,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why should we prevent the wastage of fuel used for vehicles?','','','i) Fuels like petrol and diesel are used in vehicles.\nii) These fuels obtained by processing through the earth\'s crust.\niii) Their reserves are limited may exhaust in this century.\niv) On combustion in vehicle, give out harmful gases causes air pollution.\nv) Therefore, we should prevent the wastage of fuels used in vehicles. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28217,323,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Write the names of five minerals and the useful substances obtained from them.','','','The five important minerals are iron, ore, manganese, bauxite, copper and mica.\n1) Iron Ore : Iron ore are used for making farming implements, rails of railway tracks, heavy machinery etc.\n2) Manganese : Manganese occurs in the forms of its carbonate, silicate, and oxide.\n3) These are used in preparation of medicines and giving pink tinge to the glass.\n4) Bauxite : Bauxite is most important ore of aluminium. Aluminium is a good conductor of electricity and heat. Therefore, it is used in aeroplanes, transport vehicles and to make electric wires.\n5) Copper - Copper is a very good conductor of electricity. Therefore it is used to make electric wires, as well as in telephones, radios, vehicles, and making for utensils and statues.\n6) Mica-\ni) Mica is a bad conductor of electricity.\nii) It is used in ayurvedic medicines, dyes, electric machines and equipment, wireless communication equipment etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28218,323,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which medical plants will you grow on your school premises and near your house? Why?','','','The following medicinal plants can be grown in the premises and near the house.\ni) Tulsi - Tulsi helps in the treatment of cough, cold, fever and malaria.\nii) Aloe Vera - It is used as cosmatics. It helps to recover the wound and cut.\niii) Adulsa - Useful for cough and cold.\niv) Cinnamon - Useful for diarrhoea, nausea.\nv) Cinchona - Helps in treatment of malaria.\nvi) Turmeric Useful in the foodstuffs and as preservatives.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28219,323,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe natural resources with references to the given type : Mineral resources','','','Mineral Resources :\ni) Mineral resources are found in the earth\'s crust. Minerals like copper, platinum, gold, silver and bismuth are found in free states.\nii) Minerals like iron, aluminium, mica are found in mixed state.\niii) From these metal and non-metal mineral made the articles, objects, for various purposes.\niv) Mineral like diamond, ruby, sapphire, emerald, jade, zircon are used as gems.\nv) Petroleum, coal and natural gas are used as a fuel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28220,323,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe natural resources with references to the given type : Forest resources','','','Forest Resources :\ni) A forest is a natural habitat of plants, animals and microbes.\nii) Forest performs certain specific protective and productive functions.\niii) The forest provides as medicinal plants, wood, oxygen, honey, fruits and flowers as a useful things.\niv) Forest prevent the soil erosion and helps in the percolation of rain water.\nv) Forest resources maintain the environment balance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28221,323,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe natural resources with references to the given type : Ocean resources','','','Ocean Resources :\ni) We get both mineral and bio-resources. from the ocean.\nii) The mineral resources includes Thorium, magnesium, potassium, sodium and sulphate.\niii) The bio-resources provides us fishes, dried shrimps, Bobay duck powder, shells, fungi, shark and cod-fish.\niv) We get salt, fishes, as a food from the ocean resources.\nv) Fuels like petrol, diesel are obtained from the seabed by processing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28222,323,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the flow chart :','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(28243,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Red light is scattered more than blue light.','','','False. Red light is scattered less than blue light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28244,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The sun is an example of a point source of light.','','','False. The sun is an example of an extended source of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28245,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : When the sun, the moon, the earth come in a straight line, an occultation takes place.','','','False. When the sun, the moon, the earth come in a straight line, an eclipse takes place.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28246,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :When the solar disc is completely covered by the moon, a partial solar eclipse occurs.','','','False. When the solar disc is completely covered by the moon, a total solar eclipse occurs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28247,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : A light beam can be seen in plain water.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28248,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Light is composed of several colours.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28249,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The blue colour in sunlight is scattered the most of which all.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28250,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The beam of light coming through a fine hole is a extended source of light.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28251,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by occultation?','','','The state in which a planet or a star hides behind the moon is called a occultation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28252,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the umbra appear?','','','The umbra appears dark.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28253,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The earth appears to be bluish, what is the cause of this effect?','','','The earth appears to be bluish due to the scattering of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28254,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where does the event of ‘zero shadow day’ occur?','','','The event of \'zero shadow day\' occurs in the region between the Tropic of Cancer (23.5°N) and the Tropic of Capricorn (23.5°S).','',0,0,0,NULL),(28255,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is seen in a beam of light?','','','Tiny dust particles are seen floating in the beam of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28256,324,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When does an eclipse occur?','','','When the sun, the moon and the earth all revolving in different orbits come in a straight line, an eclipse occurs.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28257,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28258,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following : ','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28259,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Point source of light','','','When light emerges through very fine hole. This is called point source of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28260,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Eclipse','','','When the sun, the moon and the earth come in a straight line an eclipse is said to have taken place.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28261,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Solar Eclipse ','','','When the moon comes between the sun and the earth, a shadow of the moon cast on the earth and they are in a straight line. This is called solar eclipse.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28262,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Lunar Eclipse','','','When the earth comes between the sun and the moon a shadow of the earth cast on the moon and the part of the moon is covered. This called Lunar Eclipse.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28263,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by scattering of light?','','','i) When the light rays falls on the tiny dust particles, molecules and other particles present in the air. It get reflected in different direction. This phenomenon is called scattering of light.\nii) Scattered ray enter our eye due to which we see the beam of light.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28265,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The sky appears blue during the day.','','','The molecules of gases like nitrogen, oxygen present in the atmosphere, scatter the sunlight. At this time, the blue colour in the sunlight scatter the most. Hence, the sky appears blue during the day.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28266,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','When sunrays pass through a glass prism. What are the colours in the band of light seen on the other side of the prism?','','','We see the following colours in the band of light on the other side of the prisms while sunrays passes through a glass prism. Violet, Indigo, Blue, Green, Yellow, Orange, Red respectively. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28267,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the parts in the given diagram appropriately :','','','Diagram : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(28268,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Shadow formed by a point source of light','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28269,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Shadow formed by an extended source','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28270,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Does the shadow really vanish in the zero-shadow condition.','','','i) On the zero shadow day at noon. The sun reaches completely over our head.\nii) Hence, the shadow of our body formed below the feet.\niii) Therefore, the shadow vanish in the zero shadow condition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28271,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give some examples of scattering of light that we come across in day-to-day life.','','','i) During a sunrise or sunset, the sky appear yellowish.\nii) Beam of light emerging from the car headlight is visible in fog.\niii) The sky appear blue during a day time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28272,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is the shadow of a bird flying high not seen on the earth?','','','i) The sun is the extended sources of light.\nii) When the bird flies near the ground form the umbra.\niii) As the bird fly away from the ground, the shadow of the bird becomes fainter and fainter and finally the shadow of a bird disappears.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28273,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Point source of light and extended source of light','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28274,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Umbra and Penumbra','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28275,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Solar eclipse and Lunar Eclipse','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28276,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Space beyond the earth’s atmosphere appears dark.','','','i) In the earth\'s atmosphere enter the sunlight, it hits the tiny particles.\nii) Light get scattered due to hitting the tiny particles and we perceive the rays of light.\niii) Beyond the earth\'s space there is no particles. Thus the scattering of light does not take place in space.\niv) Therefore, the space beyond the earth\'s atmosphere appear dark.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28277,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : We are able to read while sitting in the shade.','','','i) The scattering of light occurs in the shade and enter our eyes.\nii) The sunlight get scattered is spread even in shade. Hence we are able to read sitting in the shade.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28278,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : We should not observe the solar eclipse with naked eyes.','','','i) The light emitted by the sun is harmful for our eyes.\nii) The ultra violet ray that comes from the sun enter the eyes causes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28279,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : When the sun rises, surroundings appear illuminated.','','','When the sun rises, the sunlight gets scattered due to the molecules, dust particles and other tiny particles present in the atmosphere. because of this scattered sunlight, surroundings appears illuminated.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28280,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Solar eclipse','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28281,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Lunar Eclipse ','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28282,324,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Will the laser beam be seen if it passes through a glass box which contains a lighted incense stick?','','','i) When the light rays scattered, the ray of light to be visible.\nii) Light rays get scattered on falling the tiny particles in this path.\niii) The particles of the incense stick scattered in the glass box.\niv) These rays enter our eyes and the laser beam is seen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28283,324,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Write the science-based paragraph on what if the sun did not rise?','','','The earth revolving around the sun, if it stop the part of the earth never face to the Sun. So, sun never rise at that part. The sun is the largest source of light on the earth. Due to this we see an objects around us plants on the earth make their own food with the help of sunlight. If there is no sunlight on the earth, photosynthesis get stopped. Due to absence of sunlight all plants can die and animals also die. Water in the seas and ocean would not evaporates, due to which there would be no rainfall. As a result the fresh water available on the earth would not increases. The amount of solar energy is generated with the help of sunlight would be stopped. The sun is also make a vital role in the maintaining temperature on the earth. In this way if the sun did not rise, the biotic and abiotic factors will be affected.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28284,324,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What efforts will you make to remove the misconceptions about eclipse?','','','i) Many superstition or misconceptions connected to eclipse are prevalent in our society. We will make people to understand and accept the fact that an eclipse is the natural phenomenon.\nii) We will aware to the people who are not educate about an eclipse.\niii) We will give information about eclipse through various media.\niv) We can arrange the programme to observe such a event in school and colleges.\nv) We should provide scientific information to the people to aware about misconceptions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28285,324,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Various eclipses and the conditions during that period.','','','i) Solar Eclipse: The moon comes between the sun and the earth and they are in a straight line, the moon\'s shadow falls on the earth.\nii) Partial Solar Eclipse: It visible us where penumbra of the moon falls on the earth. It occurs on new moon day.\niii) Total Lunar Eclipse: The earth comes between the sun and the moon and they are in a straight line. It occurs on a full moon day.\niv) Partial Lunar Eclipse: The moon comes in the shadow of the earth at this events. It occurs on a full moon day.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28286,324,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is shadow? How shadow is formed?','','','i) Shadow is a dark part that formed by an object on otherside due to the source of light.\nii) When the opaque object comes in the way of light, light does not passes through it, as a result the shadow is formed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28287,324,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : A beam of light is not seen in plain water, but it is seen in turbid water.','','','i) There are no tiny particles in plain water required for scattering of light, due to which the beam of light is not seen in plain water. \nii) Whereas, in turbid water, there are tiny particles. \niii) On hitting these particles, the light rays get scattered. When these scattered rays enter our eyes, we see the beam of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28288,324,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The sun appears reddish early in the morning.','','','i) During sunrise, when the sun is very close to the horizon, sunlight travels a longer path through the atmosphere before reaching the observer. \nii) The blue light and violet light are scattered much more by earth’s atmosphere than red light.\niii) The blue light and violet light are scattered away from the path of light due to the greater thickness of the atmosphere.\niv) Hence, the light reaching the observer on the earth is mostly red and yellow.\nv) Therefore, the sun appears reddish early in the morning.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28289,324,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If a few more drops of milk are added, the reddish colour seen from the front becomes an intense red. However, if many more drops are added, the reddish colour is not seen. Why is this so?','','','i) When a few more drops of milk are added in water, more light is scattered. The reddish light contains red, yellow and orange colours.\nii) Gradually yellow and orange colours are also scattered in the water.\niii) If we add many more drops of milk the red light emerging from the front also gets scattered and thus it is not seen from the front.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28314,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Sound can travel without any medium','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28315,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Human ear can hear the sound with the frequency 20 Hz and 20000 Hz.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28316,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : All kind of sound have the same frequency.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28317,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Ultrasonic sound is used in RADAR systems.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28318,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Sound is produced due to vibrations of an object','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28319,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : The oscillations completed by an oscillator in one second is called the time period of the oscillator.','','','False. The number of oscillations completed by an oscillator is called the frequency of oscillation. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28320,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : The pitch of men’s voice is higher than women’s voice','','','False. The pitch of men’s voice is lower than that of women’s voice. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28321,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : As the tension in the stretched wire is decreased, the pitch of the sound increases.','','','False. As the tension in the stretched wire is increased the pitch of the sound increases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28322,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am produced by the rhythmic vibrations of an object.','','','Sound','',0,0,0,NULL),(28323,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the object moving from one end to the other end again and again in fixed intervals of time.','','','Pendulum ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28324,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the maximum distance on any side up to which the bob of the pendulum can move.','','','Amplitude','',0,0,0,NULL),(28325,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the one due to which the stretched rubber comes back to original state on releasing.','','','Elasticiry ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28326,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the measure of the number of oscillations completed in one second by an object performing oscillations.','','','Frequency ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28327,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the time required to complete one oscillation.','','','Time period of oscillation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28328,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Tell any four uses of ultrasonic sound.','','','Ultrasonic sound is used as  :\ni) To observe internal organs of the body\nii) To detect tumours in the brain.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28329,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by amplitude?','','','The maximum distance covered by an object on any one side of its central position while undergoing vibrations, is called amplitude of vibration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28330,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by oscillatory motion?','','','The back and forth motion of an oscillator on either side of a central position is called oscillatory motion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28331,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the unit of measuring frequency of sound?','','','Hertz ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28332,325,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which unit sound level is measured?','','','decibel','',0,0,0,NULL),(28333,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Match the following : ','','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – e), (4 – b), (5 – a) ','',1,0,0,NULL),(28334,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following : ','','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28335,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Simple pendulum','','','The object moving from one end to the other end of its central position, again and again in fixed intervals of time is called a simple pendulum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28336,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Oscillatory motion','','','The back and forth motion of an oscillator on either side of a central position is called oscillatory motion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28337,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Oscillation','','','When an oscillator moves from one end to the other and returns to its starting point, it is said to have completed one oscillation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28338,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Amplitude','','','The maximum distance covered by an object on any one side of its central position while undergoing vibrations, is called amplitude of vibration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28339,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Time period of oscillation of the pendulum','','','The time taken by an oscillator to complete one oscillation is called the time period of the oscillation of the pendulum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28340,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define :  Frequency of a pendulum','','','The number of oscillations completed by an oscillator in one second is called the frequency of a pendulum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28341,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Elasticity','','','A stretched rubber comes back to its original state on releasing. This property is called elasticity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28342,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name few animals which can hear infrasonic sound.','','','Whales, elephants and rhinoceros can hear infrasonic sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28343,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does the intensity of sound depend upon?','','','The intensity of sound is depend upon frequency of vibration, amplitude of vibration and distance from the source of sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28344,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by time period oscillator?','','','The time required by an oscillator to complete one oscillation is called the time period of the oscillator.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28345,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What things is necessary to be sound produced?','','','A vibration is necessary to be sound produced.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28346,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Audible sound','','','The sound with the frequency between 20Hz to 20,000 Hz is called audible sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28347,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Infrasonic sound','','','A sound with frequency less than 20Hz is called infrasonic sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28348,325,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Ultrasonic sound','','','A sound with a frequency higher than 20,000Hz. Such sound is called ultrasonic sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28349,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How is sound produced?','','','i) A vibration source is necessary to be sound produced.\nii) When an object structed, it get vibrate and sound is produced.\niii) The rhythmic vibration causes sound is produced by objects.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28350,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What does the intensity of sound depends upon?','','','The intensity of sound depends upon frequency of vibration, amplitude of vibration and distance of the sound source from the observer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28351,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain how the frequency of oscillation is related to the length of a pendulum and the amplitude of its oscillation.','','','i) As the length of pendulum increases, its period of oscillation is also increases.\nii) Means that the length of pendulum increases, the number of oscillation in one second is decreases.\niii) Frequency of oscillation of pendulum is dependent upon its amplitude of oscillation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28352,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','When a singer tunes the musical instruments before he starts singing. What exactly does he do?','','','i) Singers increases tension of a string with the help of tuner. \nii) A tabla player tunes the tabla by hammering its pegs to adjust the tension in the diphragm of tabla.\niii) A harmonium accompanist also adjust its harmonium to obtain the tunes properly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28353,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Will a sound the generated no matter? How the ruler is kept on the table?','','','No, sound will be generated if the ruler on the table kept properly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28354,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Is there any correlation between the length of the free part of the ruler and the sound generated?','','','Yes, if there is length of ruler is more than frequency become less and the length of the ruler on the table i.e. free part of ruler is less the sound will be generated with high tunes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28355,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the ruler is plucked while it is held with 25 cm of it off the table. Does it makes any sound? If there is no sound look for the reason why it is so.','','','i) No, if the ruler held with 2cm of its length on the table. It does not make any sound.\nii) Because, the vibration in ruler will take place very less.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28356,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the intensity of sound affected if the amplitude of sound is increased three times the original?','','','i) The intensity of sound is proportional to the square of the amplitude of sound.\nii) Thus, if the amplitude of sound is increased three times the original then the intensity of sound would become nine times the original.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28357,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What structure in the sitar help to produce higher or lower pitched sounds?','','','i) Sitar has two structures, one is a gourd-shaped and other is long neck. \nii) The free end at the upper side of the long neck has many nails to roll the string. Seven strings are attached from the nails to the gourd. \niii) There are membranes below these strings. The tension in the strings can be increased or decreased by adjusting the nails. \niv) With the help of these structures, the higher and lower pitched sounds can be produced from the sitar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28358,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vibrating object completes 450 oscillations in 15 seconds. What is the frequency of vibrations of that object?','','','450 oscillations are completed in 15 seconds.\n∴  In one second, 450/15 = 30 oscillations will be completed. \n(The number of oscillations completed in one second is the frequency of oscillations) OR The frequency is the number of oscillations completed in one second.\nThe frequency of oscillations of that object = 30 Hz.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28359,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If an oscillating object completes 180 oscillations in (one) minute, what is the time period of oscillations?','','','180 oscillations are completed in 1 minute. \nThat means, 180 oscillations are completed in 60 seconds.\n∴ In one second, 180/60 = 3 oscillations will be completed \n∴ Frequency = 3 Hz. \nNow, frequency = 1/T ----- (Formula) \n∴ T= 1 /Frequency = 1/3  second\nThe time period of oscillations = 1/3 second.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28360,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A pendulum having a length of 30 cm takes 30 seconds to complete 20 oscillations. What is the time period of that pendulum?','','','The time taken by pendulum to complete 20 oscillations = 30 seconds.\n∴ The time required for 1 oscillation = 30/20 = 1.5 second \nThe time period of that pendulum = 1.5 second.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28361,325,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the time period of a vibrating object is 0.002 second, what will be frequency of that object? ','','','Frequency = 1/T ------ (Formula) \n                          = 1/0.002 \n                           = 1/(2/1000) = 1000/2 \n                           = 500Hz \n Frequency of that object = 500 Hz','',0,0,0,NULL),(28362,325,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the two ways by which the pitch of the sound generated by a stretched string can be changed.','','','The pitch of sound generated by a stretched string can be changed by the following two ways.\na) By Changing Length of String:\ni) When the length of string increases, the frequency of vibration decreases.\nii) When the length of the string decreases, the frequency of vibration increases.\nb) By Changing Tension of String:\ni) When the tension of the string is increased, frequency of vibration also increases causes high pitch shrill sound.\nii) When the tension of the strill decreases the frequency of vibration decreases due to low pitch of sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28363,325,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : In earlier times, people used listen for the arrival of a distant train by putting their ear to the rail.','','','i) A medium is necessary to travel the sound and vibration for the production.\nii) Sound travel fastest through the solid medium.\niii) A moving train produces sound which propagate through the rail tracks.\niv) Sound level and the intensity of sound enable listener to gauge how near the train is.\nv) Therefore, in earlier time people used to listen for the arrival of a distant train by putting their ear to the rail.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28364,325,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : The sounds generated by a tabla and a sitar different.','','','i) The sound generated by different instrument can be different.\nii) The diaphragm of tabla get vibrate, it generated the sound of different frequency than sound generated by plucking of a string of sitar.\niii) There may difference between the vibration of frequency of tabla and a plucked string of sitar.\niv) Hence, the sound generated by a tabla and a sitar are different.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28365,325,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : If you were on the moon your friend will not be able to hear you call.','','','i) A medium is necessary for the propagation of sound.\nii) There is no atmosphere on the moon. Thus due to the absence of medium on the moon. Sound cannot propagate on the moon.\niii) Hence, sound can not heard if my friend and I were both on the moon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28366,325,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : We can hear the movement of a mosquito’s wings but we can not hear the movement of our hands.','','','i) A human being can hear sound with frequency of 20Hz to 20,000Hz.\nii) The mosquito\'s buzz has the frequency between 20Hz to 20,000Hz.\niii) One cannot move his hands fast enough to perform 20Hz oscillation in one second, the frequency of oscillation of hand is less than 20 Hz.\niv) Hence, the movement of our hands produces infrasonic sound. This sound we cannot hear clearly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28367,325,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What would be the difference perceived between hearing only two pupils in the class talking to each other and all the children talking to one another at the same time?','','','a) When two pupil talk to each other.\ni) When two pupile talk in the class each other the producing sound will be less.\nii) Sound will be heard clearly while talking one person at a time.\nb) When all children talk to one another at the same time.\ni) When all children talk one another the sound produced with different frequency cannot be heard clearly.\nii) The sound level also increase as a result noise will be created.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28368,325,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain what is meant by a pendulum and draw its neat diagram. In the diagram, show the central position of the pendulum and amplitude of oscillation.','','','Pendulum: An oscillator which is made up of a small iron or wooden ball tied to a half a meter long strong thread and the other end of the thread is tied to a fixed support such that the ball is freely suspended in the air. Such an oscillator is called a pendulum.','',0,0,1,NULL),(28369,325,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the high and low pitch of the sound and explain how is the pitch of sound of women’s voice and men’s voice.','','','i) When the tension in the vibrating wire is increased, the frequency increases and thus the sound produced is shriller. \nii) When the tension in the vibrating wire is decreased, the frequency decreases and thus the sound produced is less shrill. \niii) This is called the high and low pitch of sound. Generally, the pitch of men\'s voice is low and the pitch of women\'s and children\'s voice is high.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28370,325,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the types of sound based on frequency of sound or how it is heard (or whether it is heard or not)?','','','Following are the types of sound on the basis of frequency of sound :\ni) Infrasonic sound: A sound of frequency less than 20 Hz.\nii) Audible sound: A sound of frequency between 20 Hz to 20000 Hz.\niii) Ultrasonic sound: A sound of frequency higher than 20000 Hz.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28390,326,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : A suspended bar magnet always settle in east-west direction.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28391,326,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Magnetic lines of force repel each other.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28392,326,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Magnetic lines of force are limited.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28393,326,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The real test of magnet is repulsion.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28394,326,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Magnetic lines of force intersect each other.','','','False. Magnetic lines of force repel each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28395,326,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Magnetic lines of force are invisible.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28396,326,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the magnetic substances.','','','Iron, Cobalt and Nickel are the magnetic substances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28397,326,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which magnetic poles attracts each other?','','','South and north poles of a magnet attracts each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28398,326,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by magnetic filed?','','','The magnetic field around a magnet where magnetic force acts on an object is called a magnetic field. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28399,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','With whom should I pair up?','','','(1 – d), (2 – c), (3 – b), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28400,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any two properties of magnetic lines of force?','','','i) A magnetic lines of force repel each other.\nii) Magnetic lines of force always run from the north pole to the south pole.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28401,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between the two methods of making artificial magnet.','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28402,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which substances are used for making electromagnets?','','','A iron nail, copper wire, A battery and pins area the substances used for making electromagnet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28403,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In which direction does a freely suspended magnet settle?','','','A freely suspended magnet always settles in north-south direction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28405,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which magnetic poles attract each other?','','','Opposite pole of magnet attract each other i.e. north pole and south pole.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28406,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which metals are used for a making magnet?','','','Metals like Iron, Cobalt and Nickel are used to making magnets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28407,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which pole of the spherical magnet will get attracted towards the south pole of the bar magnet?','','','South pole of the spherical magnet will get attract towards the south pole of a bar magnet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28408,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Is magnetic force a vector or scalar quantity?','','','i) Magnetic force is a vector quantity.\nii) Because it has both magnitude and direction.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28409,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Earth’s magnetism','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28410,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Magnetic field','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28411,326,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Properties of magnetic lines of force','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28412,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write a short note on magnetic field.','','','i) The region around a magnet where the magnetic force acts on an object is called magnetic field\nii) The intensity of a magnetic field is low where the lines of force are sparse\niii) The intensity is high where the lines of force are concentrated.\niv) Michael Faraday, imagined that there might be invisible lines of force going from one pole of a magnet to the other pole.\nv) The magnetic field can be shown around the magnet with magnetic lines of force.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28413,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why magnetic needle used in a compass?','','','i) The earth has its magnetic north pole near geographical south and magnetic south pole near geographical north pole.\nii) When the freely suspended magnetic needle get affected by this magnetic field on the earth and settles in the north-south direction.\niii) North pole of the needle points in the direction of geographical south pole of the earth.\niv) Therefore, the magnetic needle is used in a compass.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28414,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the names given to the two ends of a magnet? Why are they named thus?','','','i) The two ends of magnet are named as north pole and south pole.\nii) The north pole of a magnet shows the di rection of geographical north pole of the earth and the south pole of magnet shows geographically south pole of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28415,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why does a freely suspended magnet always settle in the north-south direction?','','','i) The earth has a magnetic poles.\nii) The north pole of a freely suspended mag net settles in the direction of the geographic north pole of the earth.\niii) The south pole of magnet is near the geographic north pole of the earth and the north pole of the magnet, near the geographic south pole of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28416,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which direction will a magnetic needle show on the geographic north pole?','','','A magnetic needle shows the direction towards the geographic south pole of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28417,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by magnetic force?','','','The magnet get attracts the magnetic sub- stances by exerting the force, such force is called magnetic force.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28418,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is the difference between gravitational force and magnetic force.','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28419,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any four properties of magnetic lines of force.','','','i) Magnetic lines of force always start from the north pole and end on the south pole. \nii) Magnetic lines of force are in a state of tension like a stretched string. \niii) Magnetic lines of force repel each other. \niv) Magnetic lines of force never intersect each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28420,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write two uses of metal detectors. ','','','i) The metal detectors are used for inspection of persons entering very important places. e.g. airports, bus stand, certain temples and buildings. \nii) In geology, the metal detectors are used to detect the presence and quantity of metals such iron in the minerals found in the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28421,326,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is electromagnet? ','','','i) A magnet is prepared by passing an electric current through a copper wire wound around a piece of soft iron. \nii) The magnetism is produced in a piece of iron till the current flows through the copper wire. \niii) The magnet produced in this way, is called an electromagnet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28422,326,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain with the help of diagram how the intensity and direction of the magnetic filed of a bar magnet can be determined.','','','i) The region around a magnet where the magnetic force acts on an object is called a magnetic field.','ii) The intensity of a magnetic field at a place can be gauged by the number of lines of force that passes through a unit area at that place perpendicular to that area.\niii) The intensity of the magnetic field can be obtained from the number of lines of force.\niv) The intensity of a magnetic field is low where the lines of force are concentrated.\nv) The direction of the magnetic field is determined by the direction of magnetic lines of force. As magnetic lines. Lines of force always run from the north pole to the south pole.\nvi) Hence, the magnetic field of bar magnet is always directed from the north pole to the south pole.',0,0,1,NULL),(28423,326,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give detailed information about how the merchant of olden times. Use a magnet while travelling.','','','i) While travelling the merchant in olden times use the magnet to find the direction of its way.\nii) The freely suspended magnet always settles in the north-south direction.\niii) This helps them in determining the direction of their way.\niv) They were suspended such magnet by a thread, they used to settle in north-south direction.\nv) In this way magnets helped the merchant in their transportation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28424,326,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the properties of magnetic lines of force?','','','The properties of magnetic lines of force are:\ni) Magnetic lines of force always run from the north pole to the south pole.\nii) Magnetic lines of force are in a state of tension like a stretched spring.\niii) Magnetic lines of force repel each other.\niv) Magnetic lines of force do not intersect each other.\nv) The number of magnetic lines force at a particular point determines the strength of the magnetic field there.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28425,326,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Where and how are magnet used in our houses and our surroundings?','','','i) Magnet is used in maglev train on the basis of repulsion proportion and electromagnetisms.\nii) Permanent magnets are used in pin holder and in door of the cupboard.\niii) Electromagnets are used in door bells.\niv) Magnetic materials are in many electrical devices.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28426,326,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the characteristics of magnets?','','','The characteristics of magnet are:\ni) A magnet always settles in north-south direction.\nii) Opposite poles of magnet attract each other.\niii) The strength of magnet is in its both poles. \niv) Magnet attracts only magnetic substances.\nv) Two poles of magnet cannot be separated.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28427,326,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How does a magnetic force act without direct contact?','','','i) The magnetic lines of force spreads around magnet near a certain distance.\nii) The magnetic force is developed around the magnet,\niii) Thus, a magnetic force act without direct contact.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28428,326,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why is repulsion the real test for identifying a magnet?','','','i) There is attraction between two unlike poles of a magnet.\nii) However, the magnet attracts the magnetic substances.\niii) The magnet attracts the other magnet with unlike poles.\niv) There is repulsion only between two like poles of a magnet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28429,326,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How will you find a magnet from among the various articles given to you?','','','i) Magnet has repulsion and attraction properties.\nii) We will take a bar magnet first and it brings near the various articles that we have taken.\niii) If there is no attraction between both the end then we comes to know it is a non- magnetic substance.\niv) If there is attraction at one of the end and at one is repulsion then the other object is a magnet.\nv) Because, repulsion is a real test of a magnet.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28430,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','When seen from a great distance, the sky seems to be touching the ground along a circle. this circle is called the -----','','','horizon ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28431,327,'fillinblanks','1a',2,2,'Text Book','','The ---- is used while defining the Zodiac Signs.','','','ecliptic ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28432,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Classified according to seasons, one season will have ----- Nakshatras.','','','nine','',0,0,0,NULL),(28433,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The rising of the sun in the east and its setting in the west is the ----- motion of the sun.','','','apparent ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28434,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Nebulae are clouds made up mainly of ----- gas.','','','hydrogen ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28435,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The portion transversed by the moon in one day is called ------','','','nakshtra ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28436,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Stars are born out of ------','','','nebulae ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28437,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The circular horizon divides celestial sphere into ----- equal parts.','','','two','',0,0,0,NULL),(28438,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A straight line passing through the three middle stars of Mruga nakashtra (Orion constellation) reaches a bright star called ------','','','Vyodh or Sirius ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28439,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Pole star has ----- constellation on one side and ---- constellation on the other side.','','','The Great Bear (Saptarshi) and Cassiopeia (Sharmishta)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28440,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nebulae are made up of ----- gas and ------.\na) hydrogen, dust particles 		b) nitrogen, water droplets 		c) carbon dioxide, air molecules   		d) helium, ice\n','','','a) hydrogen, dust particles','',0,0,0,NULL),(28441,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Stars are born out of -----\na) Supernova 		b) Galaxy 		c) Nebulae 		d) Black hole\n','','','c) Nebulae 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(28442,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- or telescope are used for sky watching.\na) microscope 		b) binoculars	          c) speedometer 		d) stethoscope\n','','','b) binoculars	 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28443,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The moon completes one revolution around the earth in approximately ---- days.\na) 30 			b) 26.3 		c) 27.3 		d) 27.5\n','','','c) 27.3 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(28444,327,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The star, brighter and larger than the red giant stars are ------\na) Pole stars 			b) Twin star 		c) Supernova 		d) Sunlike star  	 \n','','','c) Supernova 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(28445,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Nebulae are clouds made up of only dust particles.','','','Wrong','',0,0,0,NULL),(28446,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The ecliptic are divided into 12 parts.','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28447,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The constellation can be seen in bright sunlight.','','','Wrong','',0,0,0,NULL),(28448,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Different stars have the different characteristics.','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28449,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : On a sky map, south is shown at bottom and north is shown at the top of the map.','','','False. On a sky map, south is shown at top and north is shown at the bottom of the map.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28450,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : In December, ‘The great Bear’ constellation rises on the horizon at 8 pm in northeast direction.','','','False. In February Great bear constellation rises on the horizon at 8 pm in northwest direction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28451,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : In February, Orion rises at 8 pm on the eastern horizon.','','','False. In December, Orion rises at 8 pm on the eastern horizon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28452,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : As Cassiopeia constellation sets, Great Bear constellation rises.','','','False. As Cassiopeia constellation sets, \'The Great Bear\' constellation rises.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28453,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Orion is easily seen on summer nights.','','','False. Orion is easily seen on winter nights.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28454,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Constellation seen on the meridian in February.','','','Orian or Mrug nakshtra ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28455,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Brightest star in the Scorpio constellation.','','','Jyeshtha or Antares ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28456,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Apparent motion of sun','','','Apparent path ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28457,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Institute located at Pune that carries out fundamental research in astronomy.','','','IUCAA – Inter University Centre for Astronomy and Astrophysics.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28458,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Clouds made up of hydrogen gas and dust particles.','','','Nebula ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28459,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Continuous emptiness between stars and planes.','','','Space ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28460,327,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Brightest star that helps in identifying a constellation.','','','Yogtara ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28461,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28462,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – e), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a), (5 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28463,327,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Zenith','','','While standing on a ground the point on the celestial sphere exactly above our head is called zenith.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28464,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Nadir','','','As we stand under the sky, the point on the celestial sphere exactly below our feet is called the Nadir.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28465,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Celestial poles','','','If the axis of rotation of the earth is extended in the north and south direction it penetrates celestial sphere at points called north pole and south poles respectively.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28466,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Meridian ','','','The great astronomical circle which passes through both the celestial poles and the observes zenith and nadir called a meridian. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28467,327,'descriptive','2',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Celestial equator','','','When the earth’s equator is extended identify in all direction it will penetrates the celestial sphere along a circle. This circle is known as the celestial equator.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28468,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Ecliptic','','','The earth moves around the sun, but seen from the earth, the sun appears to move along a circle on the celestial sphere. The circle which shows the apparent motion of the sun around the earth is called the ecliptic.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28469,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Zodiac sign','','','When ecliptic is divided into 12 equal parts of 30 degrees from the centre of the celestial sphere each is called zodiac sign.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28470,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Nakshtra','','','The portion of 27.3 days that revolution around the earth transversed by moon in one day is called nakshatra.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28471,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name any four stars.','','','Massive Star, Red Super Giant Star, Red Giant Star and Neutron Star.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28472,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the zodiac signs?','','','Aries, Taurus, Gemini, Cancer, Leo, Virgo, Libra, Scorpio, Sagittarius, Capricorn, Aquarias and Pisces are the zodiac sign.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28473,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','For what purposes the IUCAA have been established in India?','','','IUCAA have been established for the astronomy research and to present a virtual projection of various stars and constellations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28474,327,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the constituents of the solar system.','','','Planets, satellites and comets are some constituents of the solar system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28475,327,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is a constellation?','','','i) A group of the stars occupying a small portion of the celestial sphere is called a constellation.\nii) These stars appear to form a certain figures animals, humans or object.\niii) These figures have been named after a certain events or beliefs of the times when the constellation were identified.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28476,327,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Text Book','','Is it wrong to say that the planets, stars and nakshatras affect human life? Why?','','','i) Yes, because any planets, stars and nakshatras do not affect on the human life.\nii) Due to the technological progress man has stepped over the moon.\niii) Therefore, we can not say that planets, stars and any celestial body impact on human life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28477,327,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A star rises at 8 pm tonight. At what time will it rise after a month? Why?','','','i) In the sky a stars rises 4 minutes early every next day.\nii) Hence after a month it will be 120 minutes or 2 hours earlier than tonight. In this way the star will rise at 6pm after a month.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28478,327,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why pole star is important in sky observation?','','','i) Since Pole star occupies a position on the earth\'s axis passing through the north pole. \nii) Therefore, it always points the north in a sky map. \niii) The position of Pole Star can be also indicated by constellation of Saptarshi i.e. The Great Bear on one side and Sharmishtha i.e. Cassiopeia on the other side.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28479,327,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a relation between The Great Bear, Cassiopeia and Pole star?','','','i) The constellations of The Great Bear (Saptarshi) and Cassiopeia (Sharmishtha) are useful in locating the Pole Star. \nii) Cassiopeia is made up of five bright stars which are distributed along the figure of the letter M. \niii) The perpendicular bisector of the line joining the third and fourth stars in Cassiopeia goes towards the Pole Star. \niv) The Great Bear is a group of seven bright stars. It is in the shape of a quadrangle with a tail made up of three stars. \nv) It resembles a kite and can be easily recognized. If we extend one side of the quadrangle, it reaches the Pole Star.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28480,327,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What points should be considered before a sky watch?','','','The following points should be considered before sky watching. sudo (S\ni) It should be a new moon night\nii) The telescope should be used for sky watching.\niii) The place which is selected should be away from the city.\niv) The pole star should be used as a preference point for sky watching.\nv) The device which is taken for sky watching should be adjusted properly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28481,327,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by ‘The Sun Enters a Nakshtra’? It is said that in the rainy season the sun enters the Mrug Nakshatra. What does it mean?','','','i) When the observer looks from the earth, then observer sees many nakshatras behind the sun.\nii) But clear that a particular Raashi is entered there.\niii) In the rainy season, the sun enters the Mrug nakshatra means behind the sun.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28482,327,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a paragraph on the birth and lifecycle of an ordinary stars.','','','Life cycle of an ordinary stars :\ni) Ordinary Stars : These types of stars born out of nebulae, which are very big and balls of hot gases.\nii) Red giant stars : If the hydrogen gas at the centre of a star get exhausted, star begins to expand and cool and it becomes bigger 400 times than its original shape.\niii) Planetary Nebulae : In the lifecycle of the star ordinary stars turns planetary nebula.\niv) White Drawff : It has a brightly shining core, and it is very hot. Hence it called a white dwarf. It has low mass and very dense.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28483,327,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a paragraph on the birth and lifecycle of a massive stars.','','','Life Cycle of Massive Stars :\ni) Massive Star : It is bigger than ordinary stars.\nii) Massive Star : It is many time massive than red giant star.\niii) Supernova :\na) It is explosion of massive stars. \nb) It is very bright.\niv) Neutron Star : These are very compact star which remains after the end of super nova phase.\nv) Black Hole : \na) These black holes formed when a massive star ends after supernova phase. \nb) Here is gravity is very strong. 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(28495,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : In sago and potato there is starch/protein.','','','Starch ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28496,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : When a piece of bread is slowly chewed, it tastes sour/sweet.','','','sweet','',0,0,0,NULL),(28497,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Fibre is also called roughage/stool.','','','roughage','',0,0,0,NULL),(28498,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : The white part/egg yolk of the egg is a fatty substance.','','','egg yolk','',0,0,0,NULL),(28499,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Oil makes the paper opaque/translucent. ','','','translucent ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28500,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Carbohydrates','','','Sugar, starch, fibres','',0,0,0,NULL),(28501,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Vitamins','','','A, B, C, D','',0,0,0,NULL),(28502,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Sugars ','','','Sucrose, glucose','',0,0,0,NULL),(28503,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Minerals ','','','calcium, iron ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28504,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name any four cereals','','','maize, rice, wheat, barley, sorghum, millet, oat, rye and triticale etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28505,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which food items have sugars in them?','','','Jowar roti, bread, chickoo, butter, Jaggery etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28506,328,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which food constituents keeps the body warm?','','','eggs, fish, ghee, butter etc. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28507,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give two sources of each of the following food constituents : Minerals','','','Leafy green vegetables, fruits, sprouted pulses.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28508,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give two sources of each of the following food constituents : Proteins','','','Paneer, eggs, fish','',0,0,0,NULL),(28509,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give two sources of each of the following food constituents : Starch','','','Potato, sago, rice, bread','',0,0,0,NULL),(28510,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Match the following : ','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – c), (4 – e), (5 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28511,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are carbohydrates?','','','The energy providing food constituents which consists of starch, sugars and fibres are called carbohydrates.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28512,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by malnutrition?','','','If a person\'s diet lacks proper constituents of food for a long time, then this person does not get proper nourishment. Such condition is called malnutrition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28513,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is balanced diet?','','','The diet that constitutes the proteins, fats, carbohydrates, vitamins and minerals in right quantities is called a balanced diet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28514,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of fatty substances.','','','Cream, butter, ghee, oil, nuts, meat, egg yolk, are the examples of fatty sub- stances. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28515,328,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Of what use are the sugars that we get from digestion of starch?','','','The sugars formed from digestion of starch are burned slowly. While burning they release energy. The sugars thus act as fuels. This energy is used for different kinds of work. It also keeps the body warm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28516,328,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the sources of fibre in our diet.','','','Bran in the flour of grains, whole grains and pulses, fruits, vegetables and especially their skin, sprouted pulses with their skins, green leafy vegetables are all sources of fibres in our diet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28517,328,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is fat stored in the body? When is it reused?','','','i) The excess of fats in the diet are stored in the body under the skin. \nii) When the fats are more in diet than required for the body they are stored at different places in the body.\niii) If food is not available for some time, then the body can get energy from the stored fats.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28518,328,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Sugar cane is sweet to taste.','','','Sugar cane has sugar called sucrose. Therefore, sugar cane is sweet to taste.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28519,328,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Boiled egg is one of the best nourishing foods.','','','i) In a boiled egg there are proteins and fats. It also has vitamins A, B and D. \nii) There are many minerals such as calcium, phosphorus and potassium in it. \niii) Therefore, an egg provides good nourishment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28520,328,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Thread gets stuck between the teeth when we eat certain types of mangoes. Which kind of carbohydrates are they?','','','The threads that get stuck between the teeth while eating certain types of mangoes are fibres.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28521,328,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why are children told to drink milk every day?','','','i) Milk has almost all the food constituents. \nii) It has carbohydrates, sugars, fats and proteins. It is rich in vitamins A, B and D. \niii) The calcium content of milk is also quite high. Milk is easy to digest. It gives energy. \niv) The growth and development of the body is faster when milk is taken in our every day diet. \nv) Therefore, children are told to drink milk every day.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28522,328,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The flour of any grains should not be sifted.','','','i) The flour contains particles of bran. When the flour is sifted, this bran is left behind on the sieve. \nii) The bran particles are larger and hence they are removed from flour when the flour is sifted. \niii) This bran contains vitamins and minerals and it also gives roughage. This bran is fibre which helps in movement of the food in the alimentary canal. \niv) Fibres in the bran helps to form stools from the undigested food. Because of such benefits, bran should not be removed, thus, the flour should not be sifted.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28523,328,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : An injury from the outside of the body does not at once cause damage to internal organs.','','','There is layer of fat on the internal organs. This layer protects the delicate in- ternal organs. Therefore, even if there is an injury from the outside the body, it does not at once cause damage to the internal organs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28524,328,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why do we feel hungrier in winter than we do in summer?','','','i) In winter, the weather is cool. We need more calories to keep our body warm. \nii) Therefore, we need more energy. To get more energy, there should be enough nutrients in the body. \niii) When we feel hungrier, we take in more food. More starch, sugars and fats can give us more energy to keep our body warm. \niv) In summer season, such need is not there. Thus we feel hungrier in winter than we do in summer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28525,328,'descriptive','4',4,1,'Text Book','','Why do we use a padding of cloth under a mortar when we place it on the floor and pound something in it?','','','i) While pounding the substance in a mortar, there could be jerks to the floor. \nii) The tiles of the floor can break. \niii) Therefore, a padding of cloth has to be placed between the mortar and the floor to absorb these shocks or jerks. \niv) This offers protection to the floor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28526,328,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Do we get all the different constituents of food from a meal that consists of a green veg thalipeeth eaten with yoghurt?','','','i) In the flour of thalipeeth there is mixed flour of rice, daals and pulses. \nii) This flour gives carbohydrates and proteins. While roasting the thalipeeth, oil or butter is applied to the fry pan. \niii) Thus, even fats are present in it. If greens are added to the flour, there would be vitamins, minerals like iron and roughage added to the flour. \niv) Curds also provide proteins, carbohydrates, fats and vitamins. \nv) Hence meals consisting of green veg thalipeeth eaten with yoghurt is a complete meal with all the food constituents.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28527,328,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which food constituents do we get from the ingredients used to make bhel?','','','i) The kurmura in the bhel is a carbohydrate. The grams and peanuts are rich in proteins. \nii) The shev is made of gram flour and hence it also contains proteins. Since it is fried, there is good content of fats too in it. \niii) Chutney made from dates have sugars and minerals like iron. Pudina used in bhel is rich in vitamins, minerals and high in fibre content. \niv) If lemon or raw mango is used, the vitamin C content is also high. \nv) However, if bhel is purchased from a roadside shop, it may have microorganisms and contaminants and it can cause illness.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28528,328,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? The body requires an adequate quantity of proteins.','','','i) Body requires lots of proteins for body building. There is constant wear and tear of the body. \nii) That is also taken care of by proteins. Therefore, large amount of proteins should be in diet.\niii) For vegetarian people, different daals, pulses, groundnuts, grams, paneer, tofu, soyabean have to be included in diet. \niv) For non-vegetarian people, fish, meat, eggs are excellent sources of proteins.\nv) Everyday diet should include sprouted pulses. The milk and milk products are also rich in protein, caesin.\nvi) Such diet would give ample proteins along with vitamins.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28544,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : We get oxygen from the air.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28545,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Saliva helps in the digestion of food.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28546,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Drinking alcohol does not effects on the body.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28547,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : The blood is continuously forward through the blood vessels.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28548,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Choose appropriate word in the bracket : Respiration takes place because of its up and down movement. (circulation, Trachea, Diaphragm)','','','Diaphragm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28549,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Choose appropriate word in the bracket : The process of keeping the blood flowing continuously throughout the body. (circulation, Trachea, Diaphragm)','','','circulation','',0,0,0,NULL),(28550,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Choose appropriate word in the bracket : Air that enters through the nose passes into this tube. (circulation, Trachea, Diaphragm)','','','Trachea ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28551,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Nose, Trachea, Stomach, diaphragm','','','Stomach','',0,0,0,NULL),(28552,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Mouth, Lungs, Stomach, Small intestine.','','','Lungs ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28553,329,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by breathing?','','','The inhalation and exhalation together called breathing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28554,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(28555,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the parts of respiratory system.','','','Nose, trachea, lungs, bronchi and diaphragm are the parts of respiratory system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28556,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where does the process of digestion begins?','','','The process of digestion begins in the mouth. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28557,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which system co-ordinates all the functions of the body?','','','The nervous system co-ordinates all the functions of the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28558,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Respiration ','','','The process of taking oxygen from air and give out carbon dioxide through the nose is together called respiration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28559,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Circulation of blood','','','The process of keeping the blood flowing through all parts of the body is called blood circulation. The blood vessels carry blood away from the heart.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28560,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is digestive juice?','','','Digestive juice is the secretion that brings about digestive processes after eating food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28561,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a gland? Which are the digestive glands?','','','An organ that secretes certain substances is called a gland. Salivary glands, liver and pancreas are the digestive glands.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28562,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by a system in a body?','','','A group of organs that work together to carry out a particular function of the body is called a system in a body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28563,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by circulation of blood?','','','The process of keeping blood flowing through all the parts of the body is known as circulation of blood.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28564,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the function of the excretory system?','','','The excretory system expels the waste substances that are formed in the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28565,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the nervous system made up of?','','','Nervous system is made up of brain and the network of nerves that run through- out the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28566,329,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the function of the skeletal system?','','','The skeletal system gives shape and support to the body and also gives protection to the vital organs of the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28567,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the systems that carry out the different functions of the body.','','','Respiratory system, digestive system, circulatory system, nervous system, skeletal system and excretory system are some of the systems that carry out different functions of the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28568,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do the actions of inhalation and exhalation take place?','','','There is diaphragm in between the thoracic cavity and abdominal cavity. When the diaphragm moves downward, we inhale automatically. When the diaphragm moves upwards, air in the lungs is pushed out.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28569,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill-effects of smoking on health?','','','i) Smoking leads to different diseases of respiratory organs. Lung cancer can also be caused due to smoking. \nii) The solid particles of tobacco smoke from cigarette or bidi forms a sticky layer inside the alveoli. \niii) This reduces the amount of oxygen supplied to the body which causes ill-effects on health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28570,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the functions of stomach?','','','i) Stomach is like a bag in which the food is churned. \nii) Some digestive actions take place in the stomach due to the digestive juices secreted here. \niii) Some disease producing germs are also destroyed here. \niv) Food is changed into slurry and then it is passed on to the small intestine.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28571,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which internal organs are vital and work ceaselessly?','','','The brain, the heart, the lungs, the digestive system, the diaphragm are some of the organs which are vital and they work ceaselessly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28572,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which organ maintains coordination of the body? How is it maintained?','','','The brain maintains the coordination between all the body parts. There is a network of nerves that connects the brain with all the different body parts. The brain helps in maintenance of the body functions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28573,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why do we call saliva a digestive juice?','','','Saliva is the juice secreted by the salivary glands. Saliva helps to make bolus from the food that we eat. Saliva also contains a starch digesting enzyme. This enzyme turns starch into sugar. Therefore, saliva is called a digestive juice.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28574,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the organs that about respiration.','','','Organs that carry out respiration are the nose, the pharynx (throat), the diaphragm, the trachea, the branches and sub-branches of the trachea, the lungs and the alveoli.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28575,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the organs of the digestive system.','','','i) The alimentary canal and associated digestive glands together form the digestive system. \nii) The alimentary canal consists of the oesophagus, the stomach, the small intestine and the large intestine. \niii) The digestive glands are salivary glands present in the mouth, the liver and the pancreas. \niv) The teeth and tongue in the mouth help in food intake.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28576,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which systems work together to provide the body with energy?','','','The lungs, the heart, the stomach, the intestines, the brain all of these internal organs work together to provide energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28577,329,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a well labelled diagram of the respiratory system. ','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(28578,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Describe how the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide gases take place in the lungs.','','','i) At the time of respiration, the external air reaches up to the alveoli in the lungs.\nii) The oxygen in the air enters the blood vessels which surround the alveoli. \niii) Through the network of blood vessels oxygen is supplied to the entire body. At the same time, carbon dioxide is brought by the circulating blood into the alveoli. \niv) From here carbon dioxide is given out of the body through exhalation. In this way exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide gases take place in the alveoli of the lungs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28579,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which digestive organs are included in the digestive system? Which digestive glands are associated with the alimentary canal?','','','i) Alimentary canal is a long and flexible tube present in the body. \nii) Alimentary canal begins with the mouth and ends in anus. In mouth, the teeth, saliva and tongue help in forming a bolus which is easier for swallowing. The anus helps in throwing faces out of the body. \niii) Alimentary canal is of varying diameter in different parts. Oesophagus, stomach, small intestine and large intestine are the main parts of the alimentary canal. All the above are called digestive organs. \niv) Salivary glands, liver and pancreas are the digestive glands associated with the alimentary canal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28580,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the difference between small intestine and large intestine.','','','i) The small intestine is 7 metres long whereas the large intestine is one and half metre long. \nii) In the small intestine, the food is completely digested and absorbed in the blood. \niii) In the large intestine, the water from the digested food is reabsorbed. The remaining undigested matter is called faces and is stored for some time. \niv) In the small intestine, many digestive juices are mixed with the food. While in the large intestine, no secretions are mixed with the food. \nv) From the small intestine, the undigested food enters the large intestine. From the large intestine, the undigested faucal matter is thrown out of the body through the anus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28581,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is circulation of blood necessary?','','','i) Through blood circulation, the oxygen is sent to the entire body. Similarly, the absorbed food nutrients are also sent to all the body parts. \nii) Carbon dioxide produced in the body is carried by the blood to the lungs for expelling it out. \niii) For producing energy by burning nutrient fuels, circulation of blood is continuously needed. \niv) Thus. for survival of life, blood circulation is necessary.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28582,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why do we sometimes choke suddenly while eating?','','','i) The upper ends of both the oesophagus and the trachea open in the throat next to each other, there is a flap that covers the trachea. \nii) When the food is swallowed, the trachea remains closed. But if we eat in a hurry, the food enters the trachea. \niii) This causes choking. But due to coordination of the brain, we immediately start coughing and the food particles are expelled out of the trachea.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28583,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How is the air that we inhale purified in our body?','','','i) There are tiny, fine hair-like outgrowths called cilia inside the nose from where the air passes inside. \nii) Larger dust particles get caught in these hairs. The air going inside the trachea thus gets filtered. \niii) There is also a layer of a sticky substance called mucous. \niv) The dust and smoke particles stick to the mucous. \nv) Thus, the air that we inhale gets purified before it reaches the lungs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28584,329,'descriptive','4',4,1,'Text Book','','How do we know that we are now hungry and that we should now eat?','','','i) When the level of energy giving substances present in the blood falls down, our brain can understand it. \nii) The brain tells us that \'we are hungry and we should eat something to restore this level. \niii) Thus, the coordinating brain gives us order to eat.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28585,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How do the salivary glands come to know that there is food in the mouth and it is time to secrete saliva?','','','i) Through the nerves present in the mouth, the brain comes to know that there is food in the mouth. \nii) The brain then gives orders to the salivary glands to secret the saliva. \niii) Thus, when there is food in the mouth, automatic secretion of saliva takes place.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28586,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What makes respiration and blood circulation go on all the time, and the processes of digestion happen at the right time?','','','All the systems and their functioning is under the control and coordination of the brain. The brain keeps all the processes working in normally functioning body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28587,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? A person fainted and a crowd of people has surrounded him.','','','When a person faints, he should be immediately given clean and fresh air. Therefore, ask the crowd to move away. Then splash cold water on the face of this man. If he is not reviving, take him immediately to the doctor with the help of others. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28588,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the diagram of the digestive system.','','','Diagram : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(28600,330,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : A baby gradually learns to perform newly learnt tasks without making mistake.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28601,330,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : At birth, we already have all skills.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28602,330,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : We cannot manage all our tasks entirely by ourselves. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28603,330,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Our height increases from birth till we grow.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28604,330,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Ravi likes geometry he takes an extra effort to learn new things in geometry.','','','Good ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28605,330,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Ganu has started consuming tobacco.','','','Bad','',0,0,0,NULL),(28606,330,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Ahmed helps his father in repairing the bicycle.','','','Good','',0,0,0,NULL),(28607,330,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Manisha shouts at everybody.','','','Bad','',0,0,0,NULL),(28608,330,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Kailas has developed an expertise in drawing','','','Good','',0,0,0,NULL),(28609,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What changes does take place during the growth?','','','During the growth height and weight increases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28610,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are skills?','','','The ability to do something well is called skills.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28611,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by learning of skills?','','','Achieving control of one\'s movements to learn to do somethings new is called learning a skills.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28612,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by a diet?','','','All that we eat during a day is together called a diet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28613,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','From where do we learn the skills?','','','Most of the skills we learn from parents, teachers and others.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28614,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','After what age is it called adulthood?','','','18 years old','',0,0,0,NULL),(28619,330,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by heredity?','','','Heredity means the characteristic traits and features of parents being passed to their children is called heredity. Due to heredity, family members also show similarities.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28620,330,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name any three skills you have acquired.','','','We have acquired the following skills.\nskills we have acquired such as skipping on a rope, combing our own hair, learning a song, swimming, cycling, giving a speech, playing a field game, climbing a tree etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28621,330,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by physical growth?','','','Growth in height and weight of a child is called physical growth. The efficiency and strength of the body also rises due to physical growth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28622,330,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are necessary things for good developing  good personality?','','','i) Nutritious food \nii) Adequate exercise \niii) To avoid addictions and their ill- effects \niv) Developing good hobbies \nv) To develop new skills. These are all the things which cause development of good personality.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28623,330,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe good diet.','','','During growing years, one should always have a good diet so that all the abilities and characteristics develop to fullest extent. Inadequate nutrition during growing years cause malnutrition and stunted growth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28624,330,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the factors of supporting growth?','','','Following are the necessary things for perfect and good development.\ni) Nutritious food                          ii) Proper exercise \niii) Habit of studying well            iv) Avoiding addictions \nv) Developing hobbies \nvi) Participation in sports and other new skills.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28625,330,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How many years does it take for the young ones of the tiger/elephant to start living independently?','','','Tiger\'s cubs are independent at the age of one and half years old. But they stay with their mother. But by the end of two and half years of age, they separate from the mother and start an independent life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28626,330,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What difference do we see between children in pre-primary classes and children in Std. V?','','','i) Children studying in the pre-primary classes are 3 to 4 years old. \nii) Their height and physical strength is not yet fully developed. They cannot go to school alone, on their own. \niii) Their understanding and maturity is also less. If they run, they may fall.\niv) Children from Standard V are 9 to 10 years old. They are a little grown-up. \nv) Their physical and mental capacity is increased. Their height has also increased. \nvi) They can commute alone to the school and back home. They play in the playground in the open.\nvii) They can make calculations and can handle money. They have many more skills as compared to the children in the pre-primary class.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28627,330,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What changes takes place in us from birth till adulthood?','','','i) At birth our weight is 2 to 3 kg. By the time we reach adulthood, it becomes 50 to 60 kg for men and 45 to 50 kg for women. \nii) Our height also increases four to five times. \niii) There is no strength in arms and legs during infancy, but by young age arms and legs become strong and can perform many actions. \niv) The brain develops from birth till the age of 6. Later the structure of the brain becomes very complex. \nv) At the age of 12 to 14 many other major changes take place in the body. In order to become a father or a mother in the future, the body undergoes corresponding changes in the structure and function.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28628,330,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does our personality grow?','','','i) Every person is different and unique. The physical and mental makeup of every person is different than the others. \nii) This largely depends upon heredity. By constant interests and efforts, our personality develops. \niii) During the growing years, we also learn to understand about what is good and what is bad. \niv) When we put all the good thoughts into practice then our personality grows.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28629,330,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What skill do we already have before we learn to ride a bicycle?','','','i) We should have enough strength in legs to paddle the bicycle. \nii) For balancing the cycle, the entire body should be agile, strong and fit. \niii) We should be able to turn the handle of the bicycle whenever we are riding it. We should be cautious of traffic moving in both the directions. \niv) We therefore turn the neck quickly to look around. \nv) Thus, complete control over legs, hands, neck and eyes is an appropriate skill required to ride a bicycle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28630,330,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Suman wishes to run a restaurant of her own in the future. Which of the skills she is learning today will be of use in her future occupation?','','','i) Suman will need many people to run a restaurant. She should have the ability to interact and work together with the staff. \nii) She should also have information about different recipes, market rates of different foodstuffs, liking of customers, responsibility to keep the restaurant clean and hygienic. \niii) Right from now, she should acquire skills of buying groceries and other provisions, cooking different dishes of food, etc. \niv) Even now when she visits the hotels, she should keenly observe the working and organizing of the hotels.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28631,330,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Kabir wants to be a professor of zoology. What can he do today to prepare for that? ','','','i) In order to be a professor of zoology, Kabir should have interest in science subjects. \nii) Kabir should gather information from different magazines, books and pictorial encyclopaedia. \niii) He should develop the habit of observing different animals and birds. Zoology is taught in the science stream, so he needs to concentrate on studies in order to get admission in science stream. \niv) For becoming a professor, Kabir will have to acquire degree of M.Sc. and then Ph.D. For this, he should be able to study for long hours and with all the devotion. \nv) Since childhood, Kabir needs to be inclined towards studies in order to become a professor in the future.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28647,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : Intestinal diseases spread through air.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28648,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : Some diseases are caused by the anger of the gods.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28649,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : All micro-organisms cause the diseases.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28650,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Diarrhea spread through air. ','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28651,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Tuberculosis is an airborne disease.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28652,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Primary Health centers have been established at the village level.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28653,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Cholera spread through contaminated water.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28654,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Micro-organisms are present at everywhere.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28655,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False :  Some micro-organisms are useful.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28656,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : You should not cover your mouth while sneezing or coughing, it makes handkerchief dirty.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28657,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Always keep food items covered.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28658,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Lice and fleas do not spread diseases.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28659,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : The spittle of a person who has tuberculosis is collected in a vessel.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28660,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : During epidemic many people from a particular area get infected at the same time.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28661,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Ringworm, typhoid, cholera, diarrhea','','','Ringworm','',0,0,0,NULL),(28662,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Tuberculosis, diphtheria, jaundice, flu','','','jaundice','',0,0,0,NULL),(28663,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Cold, scabies, diarrhea, chickenpox','','','diarrhea','',0,0,0,NULL),(28664,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Diarrhea, Malaria, Jaundice, cholera','','','Diarrhea ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28665,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of infectious diseases','','','Infectious diseases : Flu, cold, ringworm, scabies, chickenpox','',0,0,0,NULL),(28666,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of diseases that spread through air','','','Tuberculosis diphtheria, cold and cough, swine flu','',0,0,0,NULL),(28667,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of diseases that spread a water','','','Cholera, diarrheas, typhoid ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28668,331,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of vaccines','','','Polio ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28669,331,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the medium through which spread the diseases. ','','','Air, water, food and insects are the medium through which diseases spread.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28670,331,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does scabies and ringworm spread?','','','Scabies and ringworm are spread through direct contact.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28671,331,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which programmes has been undertaken at the national level to prevent infectious diseases?','','','Community welfare programme has taken to prevent infectious diseases and epidemics.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28672,331,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the functions of micro-organisms?','','','The micro-organisms converts the milk into yoghurt and the fermentation of the batter for idlies, dosas etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28673,331,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does given the polio vaccine?','','','The polio vaccine is given orally.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28674,331,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is an infectious disease?','','','The disease caused by specific microorganism is an infectious disease. It is transmitted from one person to another through infections.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28675,331,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What happens when there is an epidemic of a diseases?','','','When the epidemic of a disease is spread, many people from a particular area become ill with the same disease at the same time.','',0,0,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ep_questions` VALUES (28676,331,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which preventive measures are done against epidemic malaria?','','','Malaria is spread through mosquito bites. Hence to prevent epidemic of malaria, mosquitos are eradicated. For we ensure that there is no stagnant water. If it is there then this water is sprayed with insecticides.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28677,331,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? You are very hungry but the food has been left uncovered.','','','If the food is left uncovered, it will be contaminated with dirt, dust and flies. Many microorganisms may be present in them. Such food can cause infections. Therefore, such food should not be eaten. If we are hungry we can eat packed biscuits or fruits like, banana which can be peeled and eaten. If food is boiling hot, we can consume otherwise, even if we are very hungry, we should not eat uncovered food. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28678,331,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Classify the diseases given below as diseases that spread through food, through water and through air : Malaria, typhoid, cholera, tuberculosis, jaundice, gastro, diarrhea, diphtheria.','','','a) Through air – Tuberculosis, diphtheria\nb) Through food – Typhoid, cholera, jaundice, gastro, diarrhoea\nc) Through water – Typhoid, cholera, jaundice, diarrhoea\nd) Malaria spreads through mosquito bite','',0,0,0,NULL),(28679,331,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the mediums of the spread of diseases?','','','Contaminated air, water, food are mediums of the spread of disease. Lice, flea and mosquito also spread certain diseases. In case of skin diseases, direct contact through touch and contact with contaminated clothes or utensils can also cause disease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28680,331,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is a vaccination?','','','i) Vaccination is the process in which the vaccine is given to the person or child through injection or as an oral dose. \nii) This vaccine builds resistance against a certain disease. When the disease-causing germs enter our body, we already have the ability to fight against these germs. So we do not get that disease. \niii) Due to vaccination, the out- break of an epidemic can be controlled and disease prevention can be done. \niv) Children are given vaccinations for many diseases as their resistance power is weak. \nv) Community programmes for vaccination are also done to control epidemics.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28681,331,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of the vaccinations that are given to a new born baby.','','','i) BCG or anti tuberculosis vaccine. \nii) Triple vaccine: Given against whooping cough, diphtheria and tetanus. \niii) Polio oral vaccine is given to build resistance against polio.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28682,331,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What care is taken in case of tuberculosis patient?','','','i) Patient suffering from tuberculosis is kept in the separate ward in the hospital. This is called quarantine. \nii) Clothes and utensils used by these patient are disinfected. \niii) Spittle of a person is collected in a vessel and covered with phenyl which is a germicide. \niv) Patients are given suitable and proper medicines and injections.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28683,331,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the benefit of covering food?','','','i) When food is kept covered, it remains safe.\nii) Dust and dirt does not contaminate it. The houseflies do not contaminate the food by sitting on it. \niii) The germs of different diseases are not mixed with the food. \niv) By covering the food, we can prevent spreading of diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28684,331,'descriptive','3',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which method of preventing the breeding of mosquitoes. Will you recommend for your surrounds. Not allowing water to stagnate or spraying insecticides on the water? Why?','','','i) In the stagnant water mosquitoes breed. \nii) Mosquitoes spread diseases like malaria, dengue and elephantiasis. \niii) Therefore, stagnant water should not be allowed to remain in our surroundings. \niv) One can also use insecticides to destroy mosquitoes. But this would also create insecticide pollution. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28685,331,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the epidemic of jaundice spread?','','','i) Epidemic of jaundice spreads through contaminated water and food. \nii) The microorganisms causing jaundice are present in the faecal matter of the patient. \niii) If such faeces gets mixed with river or lake water, the water gets contaminated. \niv) If such water is used by other people for drinking, the germs cause infection to all of them. In this way if the water source gets contaminated, many people get jaundice at a time. \nv) Thus, the epidemic of jaundice is spread.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28686,331,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : When there is an epidemic of cholera, we should drink boiled water.','','','i) Germs of cholera spread through faeces of a patient. They pass into water or food. \nii) If such contaminated water is used for drinking, others may get the infection of cholera. \niii) Therefore, during epidemic of cholera, water should be boiled. Boiling destroys the germs in the water and therefore we are protected against the germs. \niv) In order to keep our health safe, we should drink boiled water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28687,331,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : We should not allow puddles of water to stand in our surroundings.','','','i) Stagnant water of puddles is a breeding ground for mosquitoes. Germs causing malaria are transmitted through mosquitoes bite from a patient to other people. \nii) Epidemic of such diseases will spread if mosquitoes are abundant. \niii) Therefore, to control infections of malaria and dengue we should eradicate mosquitoes. \niv) Hence, we should not allow puddles of water to stand in our surroundings.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28688,331,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Vaccination is done at the time of pilgrimage.','','','i) During pilgrimage, a large number of people come together. \nii) Some of them may be infected or are carriers of certain diseases. \niii) Many a times there is uncovered and un- hygienic food at such events.\niv) People take holy bath together in contaminated water. All these facts can cause the spread of germs. \nv) In order to control spread of diseases and to protect pilgrims and local people, vaccination is given to all during such pilgrimage.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28689,331,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : To spit in a public place is forbidden by law.','','','i) By spitting, the surroundings become dirty. Many germs of diseases also spread through spittle.\nii) To prevent spread of such disease and to protect the health of people, spitting in a public place is forbidden by law.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(28690,331,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Mouth and nose is to be covered while coughing or sneezing.','','','i) When we cough or sneeze millions of germs may find their way into the air. \nii) Different diseases can be spread through such acts. \niii) By covering the mouth and nose while coughing or sneezing, we protect the health of ours as well as that of others.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28691,331,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Small babies are given vaccination.','','','i) By vaccination, the resistance against different diseases can be built up. \nii) Babies are protected against hazardous and lethal diseases. \niii) Even if the germs of the disease enter the body, baby can defend itself. \niv) Therefore, small babies are given vaccination.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28705,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : We can see fine particles of the substances with our naked eyes.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28706,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Objects are made from substances.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28707,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sunlight, wind and water are exhaustible sources of energy.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28708,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sound is a type of energy.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28709,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Different substances have a different property.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28710,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : petrol, diesel, CNG, kerosene','','','CNG','',0,0,0,NULL),(28711,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : sunlight, wind, water, coal','','','Coal ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28712,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Nitrogen, kerosene, oxygen, carbon dioxide','','','kerosene ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28713,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Hamida rides a bicycle to come to school.','','','Muscular energy','',0,0,0,NULL),(28714,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Aunty boils the curry on the gas stove.','','','Heat energy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28715,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : We watch TV in the evening.','','','Electricity ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28716,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Saplings of vegetables are in the sunlight.','','','Solar energy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28717,332,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : It takes time for the food to get cooked in the solar cooker.','','','Wind energy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28718,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by peelav?','','','The minute particles of an object is called peelav.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28719,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the states of substances?','','','solid, liquid and gases are the states of substances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28720,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by kinetic energy?','','','Energy in the form of motion is called kinetic energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28721,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the forms of energy?','','','Electricity, sound, heat and light are the forms of energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28722,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are solar batteries?','','','Batteries in which the electricity is produced by using sunlight are called solar batteries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28723,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the non-exhaustible sources of energy.','','','Sunlight, wind and water are non- exhaustible sources of energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28724,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In which states is water found in nature?','','','Solid, liquid and gaseous are the three states in which water is found in nature. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28725,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by energy?','','','The capacity to do the work is called energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28726,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What uses of heat energy do we see in our daily life?','','','We use heat energy for cooking, keeping the place warm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28727,332,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the small particles that can be seen in a beam of light that enters a dark room?','','','In the beam of light the small particles of dust are seen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28728,332,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following substances as per their states, into solids, liquid and gas : Chalk, water, rangoli, smoke, oxygen, nitrogen, petrol, milk, aluminium, wood, coal, kerosene, ice, water vapour.','','','a) Solid – Chalk, Rangoli, Aluminium, Wood, Coal, Ice\nb) Liquid – Water, Petrol, Milk, Kerosene \nc) Gas – Smoke, Oxygen, Nitrogen, Water vapour  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28729,332,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different properties in the substances?','','','Hardness, transparency, colour, smell, solubility in water, etc. are the different properties seen in the substances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28730,332,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which substances give energy in the form of heat? What are these substances known as?','','','Substances like coal, diesel, CNG, LPG, petrol, kerosene, etc. are the substances that give energy in the form of heat. These substances are known as fuels.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28731,332,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write some of the activities that can be done due to wind energy.','','','The windmills turn, the boats sail, clouds move from one place to the other are the activities that take place due to kinetic energy of wind.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28732,332,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? We need to make a sherbet quickly for some guests. But we only have sugar candy in the house.','','','We can pound sugar candy into fine powder. When the powder is fine, it will quickly dissolve in water. Therefore we can make sherbet quickly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28733,332,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? We need to rub salt on a corncob but only salt crystals are available. ','','','We can pound salt crystals. Either we can use mortar pestle or we can also crush the crystals of salt in a grinder. Such powdered salt can be easily applied on the corncob.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28734,332,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Naphthalene balls are also placed into toilets. Why do they become smaller after a few days?','','','Due to the property of naphthalene to turn into a gaseous state, the naphthalene balls kept in the toilet also become smaller and smaller after few days.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28735,332,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','When and why do clothes smell of naphthalene?','','','i) The naphthalene balls are continuously converted into small particles in the gaseous state. \nii) These particles settle down on the clothes in which they are kept. \niii) Therefore, if naphthalene balls are kept for a long time in the clothes, they smell of naphthalene due to these particles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28736,332,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is the difference between solid, liquid and gaseous states of a substance?','','','i) The arrangements of the particles in different states of a substance are different. \nii) In solid state the particles are closely packed. \niii) In liquid state, the particles are slightly away from each other \niv) While in gaseous state they are not in contact with each other and are freely placed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28737,332,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of all the everyday substances that we use in the form of a powder.','','','Chilli powder, turmeric powder, masalas, pepper powder, different types of flour, face powder, soap powder, rangoli, kumkum, ash, etc. are the substances used in powder form.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28738,332,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Naphthalene balls are placed in warm clothes. After some days, why do the clothes also smell of naphthalene?','','','The chemical naphthalene in the naphthalene balls continuously converts into small particles in the gaseous state. These particles settle down on the clothes in which they are kept. So after some days, the clothes start smelling of naphthalene.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28739,332,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why do tablets of camphor decrease in size day by day?','','','i) Tablets of Camphor are in solid state. \nii) Camphor has a property to turn into gaseous state directly from a solid state. \niii) Therefore, camphor decreases in size gradually. \niv) One can also notice the fragrance of camphor as it turns into vapour.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28740,332,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How do we save fuel by using public transport?','','','i) Each automobile vehicle needs fuel either in the form of petrol, diesel or CNG.\nii) If all the people use a separate vehicle, the amount of fuel consumed would be much more. \niii) If many people start using a public transport, the fuel consumption would be reduced. \niv) A single bus or a train can carry many people and thus it can save lots of fuel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28741,332,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','You do not have rangoli powder. What things can you use as an alternative?','','','We can use flour, face powder, chalk powder or any other particulate powdered matter to replace rangoli. We can use coloured powders like turmeric, kumkum, etc. for filling colours in the rangoli.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28752,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : Pollution, squalor, epidemics, addictions and insect-borne diseases are all beneficial to community health.','','','False (Pollution, squalor, epidemics, addictions and insect-borne diseases all threats to the community)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28753,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : There is a ban on spitting in public places.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28754,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : A nutritious diet, personal hygiene, exercise and pursuit of hobbies lead to excellent health.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(28755,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : We cannot live a wholesome life if we take care of our health.','','','False (We can live a wholesome life if we take care of our health)','',0,0,0,NULL),(28756,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : We should take care of our own health.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28757,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : Spitting anywhere on the road is good for health.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28758,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : Chewing tobacco leads to a cancer of mouth.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28759,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : Drinking alcohol does not effects on the body.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(28760,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them : It is unnecessary to be alert about health and hygiene.','','','It is necessary to be alert about health and hygiene.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28761,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them : Everyone should lead a tensed life.','','','Everyone should lead a tension-free life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28762,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them :  Tobacco consumption is harmless to health.','','','Tobacco consumption is harmful to health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28763,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them :  Alcohol consumption causes alertness.','','','Alcohol consumption causes drowsiness.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28764,333,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them : Malnutrition causes fall in infant deaths. ','','','Malnutrition causes rise in infant deaths','',0,0,0,NULL),(28765,333,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How can you achieve excellent health?','','','Nutritious food, personal cleanliness, exercise and pursuit of hobbies leads to achievement of excellent health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28766,333,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What factors are a threat to community health?','','','Pollution, squalor, epidemics of infectious diseases, addiction, insect-borne diseases, malnutrition are the factors that cause a threat to community health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28767,333,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is spitting in a public place banned?','','','Spitting can spread different diseases and also makes surrounding dirty, there- fore, spitting in a public place is banned.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28768,333,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which is the tobacco containing products?','','','Gutka, snuff, masheri, mawa, pan masala, cigarettes, bidis, chillum, hookah, cigar, pipe, etc. are tobacco containing products.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28769,333,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is done for becoming addiction-free?','','','The addict should take medical help of counsellor and doctor, he should develop hobbies and sports in order to become addiction-free.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28770,333,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which addiction can totally devastate one’s life?','','','Getting addicted to drugs like cocaine, brown sugar, etc. can devastate one\'s life. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28771,333,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the ill-effects of alcoholism? ','','','i) Alcohol acts on the brain of a person. Person becomes drowsy and con- fused. \nii) ) Alcohol makes a person lose control over one\'s actions. \niii) Alcohol causes diseases of liver, intestines and kidney and urinary bladder.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28772,333,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do we nurture community health?','','','To nurture community health following things are done: \ni) general public from infectious diseases. \nii) Observing personal and community hygiene.\niii) Controlling pollution, squalor, insect-borne diseases, addiction, etc.\niv) Making people happy and healthy in all respects.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28773,333,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the benefit of wholesome life?','','','i) By leading wholesome life, we become happy. We can take interest in many things. \nii) Feelings of hatred and envy are ended. People in the society lead healthy and happy life. \niii) Social tensions decrease and feelings of friendship are nurtured.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28774,333,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','When is it said that a person is addicted to tobacco?','','','When a person gets restless when he does not get tobacco and he is unable to pay attention to anything else, he is said to be addicted to tobacco.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28775,333,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : When we take care of our health, entire community also becomes wholesome.','','','i) When everybody takes care of their health and hygiene, the entire community health is also achieved. \nii) Through the habits of cleanliness the entire surroundings also become clean. In this way all can lead a wholesome life. \niii) The entire community then becomes wholesome.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28776,333,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Pollution and epidemics of infectious diseases are threats to community health.','','','i) When there is an outbreak of epidemic, many people from the same area are affected at the same time. \nii) Similarly, pollution also causes harm to the life of the people. \niii) Many people can die too. Therefore, pollution and epidemics of infectious diseases are threats to community health. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(28777,333,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the ill – effects of chewing tobacco?','','','i) If tobacco is consumed, there are ulcers in the mouth. Ulcers develop into\nbigger wounds and they are later turn into tumours. \nii) Oral cancer, i.e. cancer of the mouth may be formed. \niii) Tobacco entering into digestive system cause cancer of any of the digestive organs. It also leads to various complaints of digestive system. \niv) A person with cancer has to undergo painful treatment. It can lead to death.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28778,333,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the effect of addiction on the family of an addict?','','','i) When the condition of an addict becomes pathetic, his family members also suffer. \nii) Lot of money is required for treatment. Everyone is put into great trouble and lot of time and efforts are wasted. \niii) The family gets deprived of health and happiness.\niv) The family gets ruined due to insecurity of all kind. \nv) Addict destroys the entire family.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28779,333,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is to be done for good health?','','','i) For good health, nutritious diet, safe and clean drinking water should be used. \nii) Habits of personal and community cleanliness should be followed. Never get hooked on to any addiction. \niii) Always stay away from tobacco, alcohol and drugs.\niv) Feeling of hatred and envy should be avoided. \nv) Proper exercise should be done. Hobbies and sports should be taken up. \nvi) Along with personal health, community health should also be nurtured.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28780,333,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is to be done to stay away from addictions?','','','i) We should have good habits, remain happy and enthusiastic. \nii) There should be complete control over mind. \niii) We should remain firm in avoiding addictions.\niv) We should not be lured by people who can force someone in addictions. \nv) Sports and hobbies also keep the addictions away. \nvi) We should be completely aware of dangers of addictions. All these measures can help us to stay away from addictions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(28781,333,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Always stay away addictions.','','','i) Addictions spoils health of a person. Tobacco addiction can cause cancers of different organs. \nii) By keeping tobacco constantly in the mouth, there are chances of oral cancer. \niii) Alcoholism cause severe problems of liver, kidney and intestines. The addict completely destroys the family. \niv) The family is deprived of health and happiness and is ruined. Due to tobacco spitting the surroundings get dirty. \nv) By drug addiction, life is totally devastated. \nvi) Therefore, always stay away from addictions. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(29629,340,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','4x² × 2x = -----\na) 8x³			b) 6x³		c) 6x 		d) 6x²\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(29630,340,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','35x + 1x = --------\na) 35x² 		b) 36x 		c) 36x²		d) 35x\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(29631,340,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','एका चलातील बैजिक राशीच्या प्रत्येक पदातील चलाचा घातांक हा ------- संख्या असेल तर, ती राशी एका चलातील बहुपदी असते.\na) वास्तव		b)  नैसर्गिक 		c) पूर्ण		d)  पूर्णांक\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(29632,340,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' -5m² ÷ (-m) = ------\na) -5m 		b) 5m 		c) -5m³ 		d) 5m²\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(29633,340,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','दिलेल्या बहुपदीतील सर्वांत ------  चलाच्या घातांकास, त्या बहुपदीची कोटी म्हणतात.\na) लहान 		b) मोठ्या 		c) यापैकी नाही		d)  सर्वात मोठ्या\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(29634,340,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','4a³ - 5a² + 13a +5 या बहुपदीतील चलाचा सर्वांत मोठा घातांक ------\na) 2 		b) 3 		c) 1 		d) 4\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(29635,340,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(4x³ + 3x² - 4x + 5) ÷ 2x यांचा भागाकारा खालीलपैकी कोणता येईल?\na) 2x² + 3/2 x – 2 		b) 2x² + 3