e new words.\n             (i) Report 	                                  (ii)  Act \n	                             OR\n  Make a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words.\n      (i) Report                                    	(ii)  Act\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(36192,439,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words.\n       (i) Magic 	                             (ii)  Different \n	                       OR\n Make a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words.\n       (i)  Magic                                  	(ii)  Different\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(36194,440,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words.\n        (i)  Note                                         	(ii)  Bright \n	                      OR\n   Make a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words.\n        (i) Note                                 	(ii)  Bright\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(36195,440,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words.\n         (i) Concept                              	(ii)  Power \n	                     OR\n Make a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words.\n     (i) Concept                                	(ii)  Power \n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(36196,441,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words.\n     (i)  Friend                              	(ii)  Report \n	                         OR \n Make a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words.\n              (i) friend 	                               (ii)  Report\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(36197,441,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words.\n         (i) Able 	                           (ii)  Active \n	                OR\n   Make a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words.\n             (i)  Able 	                            (ii)  Active\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(36198,442,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words.\n               (i) Agree                                     	(ii)  Amaze \n	                                  OR\n    Make a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words.\n           (i)  Agree                                	(ii)  Amaze\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(36199,442,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words.\n      (i)  Attract                           	(ii)  Beauty \n	                     OR\n    Make a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words.\n             (i) Attract 	                                    (ii)  Beauty\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(36213,68,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities. \nA1) Fill in the blanks by choosing the correct alternative\n      i) When Androcles came face to face with the lion, his first reaction was to turn and.......\n       a) attack                              b) tease                       c) flee\n      ii) He moved  very ................towards the lion.\n       a)  hurriedly                          b) slowly                     c) cautiously\n      iii) The lion seemed to understand his good............\n       a)  intention                          b) habit                        c) mood\n      iv) Androcles pulled out the throne..............\n       a) smoothly                           b) deftly                        c) slowly\nOne day, as he was wondering in the forest. He suddenly came face to face with a lion. his first reaction was to turn and flee. but then he saw that the lion had not mood on seeing him. It neither gowned nor roared. in fact, it looked at him pitiously as if it was in great pain.\n Androcles paused. Then he moved very cautiously towards the lion, step by step, the lion moaned and whimpered.\" Please help me, \" it\'s eyes seemed to say.\nWhen Androcles dree near. The lion lowered its head and began to lick its paw. Then Androcles saw that the lion paw was wounded, sore, and Swollen\nOn a closer look, Androcles noticed that a big thorn had pierced one of the lions toes. So this is the cause of the lines distress\' , thought Androcles.\nSummoning up of all the courage,  Androcles stretched his hand slowly towards the injured paw. He was afraid that if the lion would attack him if he touched his swollen paw. But the lion seemed to understand his good intention.\nAndrocles took the paw in his left hand. Deftly he pulled out the thorn. Then tearing a piece of his own clothing the kind man bound of the lions injured power to his surprise and wonder the lion then lit his harm as if to show gratitude and brock Les remained with the lion till it could walk again you would hunt in the forest and share his food with the helpless beast everyday he would clean and dress the lions wound soon the lion was able to move about many times it follows wherever he went.\nA2) Complex factual question\n         Why was Androcles happy? \nA3) Find the words from the passage that mean:\n       i) hurting                ii) cried painful                 iii) feeling of thankfulness             iv) without help\nA4] Grammar\n       1) Everyday he would clean and dress the lions wound. \n            (Pick out the auxiliary verb)\n       2) The kind man bounded up the lions injured paw. \n            (Pick out the adjective)\nA5] Open ended questions.\nWhat do you learn about Androcles from the passage?\n','','','Answer\nA1] i) d) flee       ii) c) cautiously       iii) a)  intention       iv) b) deftly     \nA2] Androcles was happy because he was free as bird. He could also go where he wished\nA3] i) sore        ii) whimpered           iii) gratitude         iv) helpless\nA4] 1) would        2) adjective - kind, injured\nA5] From the passage one learn that Androcles was a kind, gentle and courteous man. he saved the lion which was in great pain. This shows that he was a courageous and sensitive man. He nursed the lion\'s wound and offered him food. It shows his caring and co-operative nature. he was brave as well as a person with great presence of mind.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36214,68,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities. ----- 10M\nA1) State whether the statements are true or false\n      i) Androcles alone fill into the hands of the soldiers.\n      ii) Running away from Once master was a common crime at the time.\n      iii) The lion killed Androcles.\n      iv) The emperor set free Androcles as well as the lion.\nIn the meanwhile, Androcles\'s master had complained to the authorities that his slave and replace had a run away. Soldiers were Sent in all the directions to capture Androcles. They searched for him high and low but couldn\'t find him. At last, they came to the forest where Androcles leaved.\nIt is so happened that Androcles had gone out alone that day while the lion rested near the cave. Both of them fill into the hands of the soldiers. The soldiers took them back to the city They put Androcles in chain in a prison. The lion was caged.\nIn those days, people who were found to be guilty of a serious crime were fed to wild beast as punishment running away from Once master was a very serious crime at that time there for Androcles 2 was to be thrown to the wild beast\nOn the appointed day the emperor arrived at the circus and took his place. The crowds cheered loudly. The trumpet blew the trumpet and Androcles was brought into the arena. A chain was unbound. He filled with and dismayed he was certain that this was the last day of his life.\nAt the opposite end, a gate was raised and ferocious lion was released into the arena. It had been kept hungry to make it more ferocious and replace was filled with fear but he put up a brave front.\nThe lion saw Androcles and rushed towards him, bounding and roaring. But suddenly, it stopped in its tracks they need to move it slowly forward. All its a Rafe seem to have melted. Androcles saw this too. Each friend recognized the other.\nTo everyone\'s amazement, the lion bounded towards Androcles again, but when it reached him, it began to purr and rub its nose against him. Androcles hugged the lion. He wept with relief and joy.\nThe audience, including the emperor, watched this tender sight in great wonder. People began to cheer Androcles and asked for his released the emperor asked and broke Les to explain the lines strange behavior when he heard the whole story the emperor was also moved by the gratitude and friendship between man and beast, he ordered that endocasts should be set free he would no longer slave when the emperor asked and broke Les what other reward he would like Androcles replied that he did not want anything for himself. But he wanted the lion to be set free too. The two friends went back to the forest to you a free life.\nA2) What did Androcles feel after he was a brought to the arena?\nA3) Find out the antonyms of the following words from the passage.\n      a) bound                      b) enemy                      c) catch               d) ingratitude\nA4) Grammar\n       i) Running away from the ones master was a very serious crime at that time.  \n              (Identify subject and predicate)\n         ii) He felt weak and dismayed. \n       (Change into past continuous tense)\nA5) Open ended questions\n         Do you forgive others? How?\n ','','','Answer\nA1] i) False           ii) False       iii) False       iv) True\nA2] When Androcles was brought to the arena he felt weak and sad. He knew that it was the last day of his life. At first he was filled with fear then showed that he was not afraid.\nA3] a) unbound     b) friend        c) release       d) gratitude\nA4] i) Subject : Running away from one\'s master\n         Predicate : was a very serious crime at that time\n       ii) He was feeling weak and dismayed.\nA5] Yes, I forgive others. When my friends behave rudely with me, tease me, I forgive them, because it is a sign of large heartedness. It is a quality of a powerful mind.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36217,70,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities.\nA1) State whether the statements are true or false.\n      i) Class teacher taught Math’s subject\n     ii) The notebook fill on the class teachers’ feet.\n     iii) Sayali\'s favorite subject was English.\n     iv) The teacher had asked the students to memorize the causes and effects \n           of the battle of Plasi.\nI got ready and went to school. In the first period, our class teacher, who taught us English, told us to take out our composition notebooks. I took out the mathematics notebook by mistake. I replaced it quickly but the teacher, perhaps, has gaze fixed on me. She shouted, \" come here with your book, Sayli!\" I Obaid her but her angry looks made in my legs symbol and when I reached her chair, that notebook fill on her feet. \n     Some girls laughed loudly. The teacher thought that I had drop the notebook on purpose. She stared at me with an all devouring look. \" Go and stand near your seat,\" she yelled. I kept standing, punished for the whole period. \n     The next History class and my favourite subject. The teacher had asked us to memorize the causes and effects of the Battle of Panipat. I was will - prepared. She began testing all of us, one by one. When my turn came, I went blank. The teacher gives me a stern stare, while I stood there stiff, still, with my head hanging down in shame\nA2) Complex factual question\n          Why could the notebook have fallen from Sayali\'s hand? \nA3] Find out the words from the passage that mean...............\n       i) Shiver .............          ii) Could not remember anything ...............\n      iii) exchanged..........   iv) not moving.........\nA4] Grammar\ni) I had dropped the notebook. (Name the tense)\nii) Choose the imperative sentences.\na) I obeyed her.           b) The notebook fell on her feet.  \nc) Come here with your book.\nA5] Open ended questions\n       Which is your favourite subject? Why?\n','','','Answer\nA1] i) False          ii) True         iii) False        iv) False\nA2] Sayali took out mathematics notebook instead of English composition notebook by mistake. her teacher got angry for that. When teacher called her, her legs started trembling. So, due to fear, the \n       notebook could have fallen from Sayali\'s hand.\nA3] i) Shiver - tremble         ii)  Could not remember anything - went blank            iii)  exchanged - replaced            iv) not moving - stiff\nA4] i) Past perfect tense          ii) c) Come here with your book\nA5] My favourite subject is English. I like English because it is a simple and easy language. it is the language of computers and knowledge. And I have a very good English teacher. He created interest in me for this subject.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36218,70,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities.\nA1) Simple factual questions\nChoose the correct options\n   i) The quarrel was between the two, but the punishment inevitable        \n      descended on a third one that is............\n       a) Sayali\'s younger brother             b) Sayali\'s father             c) Sayali\n   ii) Sayli hugged her............... tight and started crying again.\n       a) mother                                           b) younger brother        c) sister\n   iii) ..........gave Sayli a new title.\n         a) The teacher                      b) some girls                      c) class monitors\n  iv) Sayli could not ................the courage to speak to anyone\n        a) muster                                b) perpetual                              c) musk\nMama and papa continued their never ending tiffs. Papa used every opportunity to express his annoyance. Mama on her part, did not allow any such occasion to slip without turning it into a vociferous quarrel. The quarrel was between the two, that is me at school. Wherever and whenever I heard raised voices I filled as if mama and papa were quarreling. Sometimes, even in the midst of silence, Mama\'s screams came through my ears. And, whenever I heard Mama\'s high pitched yells my legs quivered.\n       One day, I was coming down the school stairs. Somebody from the top give a frantic scream. I fell down and came tumbling down about seven steps. My elbows and knees were badly bruised. My new frock was torn at the seams. When I reached home, Mama shouted, \"I am sick of you. When will you stop making me pay through my nose for such extravagant loses?\" Mother seized me by the arm, gave me a good jerk and seated me on the bed. She fetched an antiseptic lotion and applied it to my elbow and knees but did not hug me. Within me lingered yearning that she would who told me and console me! Even if she had given me just a sympathetic pat, my smarting limbs would have been soothed.\n     My younger brother was closed by as mama walks away to the kitchen he came and sat beside me he placed is little hand on my arm and whispered are you in a great pain I hug Rahim tight and started crying again.\n     I used to walk quite cautiously, yet I would often stumble. One day, the class monitress announced, \"We\'ll call her Slippery. Others sleep on wet ground Sayli manages to sleep even on dry land\". Soon a verse was composed in my owner. \n      \"Slippery! Slippery! \n     Slipping without muck!\n    Ask her the simplest question\n     No answer is your luck!\"\n   I live in perpetual fear of everyone the girls, the teachers, the young and the old. When I watch in class, I dared not move out and when I was outside, I was afraid of re- entering the class. Nor could mustard the courage to speak to anyone.\nA2] What did Sayali long for, from her mother?\nA3] Find the synonyms of the following words from the passage \n       i) certainly      ii) extreme fear      iii) very expensive     iv) hurting\nA4] Grammar\ni) I was coming down the school stairs. \n     (Change into past perfect continuous tense)\nii) Are you in great pain? (Identify the type of sentence)\nA5] Open ended questions\n     Does your mother shout at you? When?\n','','','Answer\nA1] i) c) Sayali           ii) b) younger brother          iii) c) The class moniterss           iv) a) muster\nA2] Sayali longed for affectionate words from her mother, when she reached home with torn new frock. She also hoped that her mother would console and hug her. In that condition, she wanted a \n        sympathetic pat. \nA3] i) inevitably          ii) frantic            iii) extravagant               iv) smarting \nA4] i) I had been coming down the school stairs \n      ii) Interrogative sentence \nA5] Yes, sometimes my mother shouts at me. Whenever I lose my belonging in school, she shouts at me.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36219,70,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities.\nA1] Simple factual questions\n       State whether the statements are true or false.\ni) The teacher did not allow sailyi to enter in the class.\nii) The teacher told Sayli that she was her friend and elder sister.\niii) Never before had anyone lavished love on Sayali.\niv) the girls\' prime first time was helping Sayali.\nThe bell had already run when I reached the school. The teacher was in the classroom. I sought permission to enter. The teacher wore a frown of her face, but she let me enter to see her in the teacher\'s room, after the class. Her tone was not of annoyance or anger. Yet an unknown fear of made me nervous. Reassuring myself that she was a kind hearted teacher and would not punish me, I reached the teacher\'s room with hesitant steps. \n          The teacher greeted me with a smile and asked me to sit by her side. Her tender tone turned my sights into sobs. She pulled her chair close to mine and patting me on the head, said, \" What makes you cry, my child? I haven\'t said a word to you.\"  After a brief pause, she continued, \"Tell me what your trouble is.\" Confide in me, for am I not like a sister to you?\" Rare thrill through my entire frame. For a moment I feel I should cling to her and cry and cry. Of course, I did not do so but I keep giving at her with your full eyes. \n     Tenderly, teacher continued, \" Do you know the meaning of Didi? It means elder sister. Won\'t you tell your sister your trouble?\" Amid sobs I said, \" The girls keep teasing me.\" She laughed,\" Why are you scared of them? If you cry like this, they will pester you all the more. So, ignore them. Who is your friend?\" \n          I shook my head and teacher smiled. It seemed the teacher had a old her smile into my eyes. \"Like to be my friend? she said. \nThe bell rang. She rose. With a tender pet on my back, she said, \"listen You were a sister alright! Now you are a friend too. But I like little pal. Now Rise and hop off to your class.\"\n        Excitement made my face flush. Red - hot to the ear- tips. Never before had anyone lavished love on me thus and here was my teacher who had spontaneously accepted me as a sister, a friend!  I was beyond myself with joy. I wanted to chuckle to burst into near hysterical laughter. \n       On winged feet I went to my class. Sun I was lost in a rare reverie. I forgot that I was sitting in a class of wild girls whose Prime pastime watch teasing me.\nA2] Why did Sayali feel like rejoicing a lot?\nA3] Find describing words for the following nouns.\n         a) .............. teacher           b)........... tone               \n         c) ............eyes.                  d) .......... pal\nA4] Grammar\n      i) I haven\'t said a word to you. (Pick out auxiliary form)\n      ii) Rise and hop off to your class. ( Name the type of sentence)\nA5] Open ended questions\n        Have you ever come to late to school? What was your teacher’s reaction?\n','','','Answer\nA1] i) False            ii) True          iii) True             iv) False\nA2] Sayali felt like rejoicing a lot because her teacher accepted her as a sister and a close friend. Before that no one had lavished love on her. She felt that there was someone who understood her.\nA3] a) kind-hearted teacher           b) tender            c) tearful              d) little\nA4] i) haven\'t                   ii) Imperative sentences \nA5] Yes, I had come late to school last month. I was fifteen minutes late for the first period. At that time my class-teacher was teaching. When I sought permission to enter, he did not allow me. He was very angry. he asked me to stay outside for the whole period as a punishment. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(36220,70,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities.\nA1] Name the person\ni) The person who was eager to watch the fun.\nii) The girl who teased Sayli as a slippery.\niii) The person who keeps school clean and tidy.\niv) The girl who was appointed as a class monitor.\nDuring the break, the water bottle that I was drinking from fell on me and I got fully drenched. Right in front stood Maya and Chanda, laughing away to their heart\'s content. Chanda teased, \" From the slippery hands of Slippery, the slippery bottle went slip, slip, slip!\" And now, let\' watch the fun,\" said Veena. \n     I was squeezing the water out of my wet frock. Remembering the teacher\'s words, I turned my back to them and ignored them completely. They were non plussed and went away. O sensed a feeling of victory. \n   Before the school closed for the day, the teacher came to the classroom again and said to me, \" From today, you are going to be responsible for bringing the register, chalk and duster every morning. Come, take this key, carry these notebooks to the teacher\'s room. Count them and keep them in my drawer. Ask the peon to show you, my drawer. \n    My favourite trembling legs were now filled with some miraculous strength. I rushed enthusiastically and completed the assigned job in a jiffy. When I placed the key on the teacher\'s table, she looked at me and smiled. I smiled back..\n     I was thrilled from top to toe. I felt my little heart inflate with pride. I found no awkwardness in moving about in my still- wet frock. Nor was there any trace of fear, while returning home.\nA2] What responsibilities did the teacher give Sayali?\nA3] Find antonyms for the following words from the passage \n        i) dry        ii) forget       iii) defeat       iv) irresponsible\nA4] Grammar\n1) I sensed a feeling of victory. (Add question tag)\n2) I was squeezing the water out of my wet. (Identify the tense)\nA5] Personal response question\n        Were you a class monitor? How did you feel?\n','','','Answer\nA1] i) Veena         ii) Chanda           iii) Peon            iv) Sayali\nA2] The teacher appointed Sayali as the class monitor. She was made responsible for bringing the register, chalk and carry the notebooks to the teachers\' room.\nA3] i) drenched, wet           ii) remember        iii) victory                iv) responsible\nA4] 1) I sensed a feeling of victory, didn\'t I?\n       2) Continuous tense (Past)\nA5] Yes, I was a class monitor when I was in fifth standard. i felt very happy. I also felt like I had got some suoerpower and supreme authority. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36292,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The part that grows from inside the seed towards the soil is called ------\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','radicle','',0,0,0,NULL),(36293,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Plants have tap roots while ----- plants have fibers. \n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Dicotyledonous','',0,0,0,NULL),(36294,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Leaves come out at the -------\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Node','',0,0,0,NULL),(36295,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The apical end of the stem is called ---------.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Bud','',0,0,0,NULL),(36296,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The broad spread out part of the leaf is called -------.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Leaf blade','',0,0,0,NULL),(36297,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- and ----- are the main two types of leaves.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Simple leaf, compound leaf','',0,0,0,NULL),(36298,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The peepal leaf blade has ----- venation.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Reticulate','',0,0,0,NULL),(36299,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- is the male reproductive part of the flower.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Androecium','',0,0,0,NULL),(36300,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ is the female reproductive part of the flower.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n\n','','','Gynoecium','',0,0,0,NULL),(36301,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Seeds which get divided into two equal parts are called ------. \n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Dicotyledonous','',0,0,0,NULL),(36302,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ are the hair-like processes near the root tips.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Root hairs','',0,0,0,NULL),(36303,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Monocotyledonous plants have ----- roots.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','fibrous','',0,0,0,NULL),(36304,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The part of the steam between two nodes is called an ------\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','internode','',0,0,0,NULL),(36305,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The broad, spread-out part of the leaf is called ----- while its tip is called the -----.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','leaf-blade, leaf apex','',0,0,0,NULL),(36306,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the bud condition the petals are covered by leaf-like whorl called ------\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n\n','','','calyx','',0,0,0,NULL),(36307,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The part grow from inside the seed towards the soil is called -----\na) radical 		b) plumule 		c) root cap 		d) root hair\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n\n','','','a) radical','',0,0,0,NULL),(36308,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Onion has ------ type of root.\na) tap root 		b) fibrous roots 		c) lateral roots 		d) adventurous root\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','c) lateral roots ','',0,0,0,NULL),(36309,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The growing end of the stem is called-----\na) Internode 		b) Node 		c) bud 		d) none\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','b) Node','',0,0,0,NULL),(36310,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is not a part of stem?\na) Bud 		b) node 		c) Internode 		d) Pedicel\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','d) Pedicel','',0,0,0,NULL),(36311,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The male reproductive part of a flower is called -----\na) androecium     b) gynoecium 	c) anthers 		d) filament\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','a) androecium ','',0,0,0,NULL),(36312,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is not a part of flower?\na) flower 		b) calyx       	c) ovary 		d) petiole\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','d) petiole','',0,0,0,NULL),(36313,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',') ------ is a female reproductive part of the flower.\na) androecium    b) gynoecium 	c) stigma 		d) style \n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','c) stigma','',0,0,0,NULL),(36314,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a dicotyledon seed?\na) Jowar 		b) wheat 		c) moong 		d) maize\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','b) wheat','',0,0,0,NULL),(36315,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a single seeded fruit?\na) apple 		b) mango 		c) jack fruit 		d) water melon\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','c) jack fruit ','',0,0,0,NULL),(36316,482,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The part grow from inside the seed towards the soil is called -----\na) radical 		b) plumule 		c) root cap 		d) root hair \n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','a) radical','',0,0,0,NULL),(36317,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns: ','','','(1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(36318,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement are true or false: Roots are tapers to a pointed end.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(36319,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement are true or false: The tip of root is strong and hard part.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(36320,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement are true or false: Node is the part of leaf.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(36321,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement are true or false: The peepal leaf is divided into two equal parts.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(36322,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement are true or false: All seeds are monocotyledonous.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(36323,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statement are true or false: Stamen is made up of anther and filament. \n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(36324,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Root, stem, corolla, leaf\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Corolla','',0,0,0,NULL),(36325,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Bud, root cap, node, internode\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Root cap','',0,0,0,NULL),(36326,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Calyx, corolla, gynoecium, petiole\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Petiole','',0,0,0,NULL),(36327,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Moong, Jowar, wheat, corn\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Moong','',0,0,0,NULL),(36328,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Watermelon, jackfruit, apple, Mango\n\nAns. : ----------------------------\n','','','Mango','',0,0,0,NULL),(36329,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the examples of 3 plants that have: Spiny fruits\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Datura, Jack fruit, Castor','',0,0,0,NULL),(36330,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the examples of 3 plants that have: Spiny stem\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Silk cotton tree, Cactus, Silk floss tree','',0,0,0,NULL),(36331,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the examples of 3 plants that have: Red flowers\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Sunrose, Hibiscus, Canna','',0,0,0,NULL),(36332,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the examples of 3 plants that have: Yellow flowers\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Sunflowers, Golden shower tree, Golden creeping jenny','',0,0,0,NULL),(36333,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the examples of 3 plants that have: Leaves which close at night\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Peace lily, Gulmohar, amla','',0,0,0,NULL),(36334,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the examples of 3 plants that have: Single seeded fruit\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Mango, Ber (ziziphus), Cashew','',0,0,0,NULL),(36335,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give the examples of 3 plants that have: Many seeds fruits\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Jackfruit, Watermelon, Guava','',0,0,0,NULL),(36336,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Part of the leaf \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Leaf base, petiole, leaf blade, leaf margin, leaf apex','',0,0,0,NULL),(36337,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: part of the steam \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Node, internode, bud','',0,0,0,NULL),(36338,482,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Shape of the leaves\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Palmate, rounded, lanceolate linear','',0,0,0,NULL),(36339,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the similarities and differences between: Jowar and Moong\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Similarities : Both are food stuffs.','',0,0,1,NULL),(36340,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the similarities and differences between: Onion and Coriander\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Similarities : Both are consumed in the form of salads. They are used as supplementary food stuffs in cooking and also for garnishing.','',0,0,1,NULL),(36341,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the similarities and differences between: Leaves of banana and mango\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Similarities : Both the leaves have religious, importance and sacred.','',0,0,1,NULL),(36342,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the similarities and differences between: Coconut tree and jowar stalk plant.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Similarities : Both are monocotyledonous plants that yield food stuffs.','',0,0,1,NULL),(36343,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the various parts of plants?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n','','','The root, stem, leaves, flowers, fruits etc. are the different parts of the plant.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36344,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different types of roots?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n          \n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n','','','i) Tap roots and fibrous roots are the two main types of roots.\nii) Dicotyledonous plants have tap roots while monocotyledonous plants have fibrous roots\niii) In some plants the roots are modified to perform additional functions. These roots are aerial roots, stilt-roots, runners, breathing roots (pneumatophores) \niv) Some roots called adventitious roots grow from the stem just above the soil.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36345,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different parts of stem?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n          \n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) There are nodes on the stem from where the leaves come out.\nii) The part of the stem between two successive nodes is called an internode\niii) The tip that is the apical end of the stem is called a bud\niv) There is also a bud in the axils of the stem from where the branches arise.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36346,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Of what use a plant are the insects flitting about around its flowers?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n          \n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Insect help in pollination of the flowers. They transfer the pollen grains from one flower to another.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36347,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw neat labelled diagram of different parts of leaf.\n\nAns. : \n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram','',0,0,1,NULL),(36348,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw neat labelled diagram of fibrous root\n\nAns. : \n\n\n\n','','','Diagram','',0,0,1,NULL),(36349,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw neat labelled diagram of the stem\n\nAns. : \n\n\n\n','','','Diagram','',0,0,1,NULL),(36350,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which stems are used as vegetables?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n','','','Potatoes, Suran, Tubers, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36351,482,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which dicotyledonous seeds are used for cooking purpose?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n','','','Moong, Rajma, Chick peas, Green peas, Gram, Moth beans, Lentils, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36352,482,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the following images in your own words:\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n','','','i) Fig. A is of monocotyledonous seed. It is a grain of Maize figure B is of Dicotyledonous seed. It us a bean seed.  \nii) Each figure show entire seed/grain and a longitudinal section of the same.\niii) In figure A : The radicle and plumule is seen inside the covering surrounded by starchy substance. There is a single cotyledon.\niv) In figure B : The radicle and plumule are seen on the upper side of cotyledon. There are two cotyledons.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(36353,482,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the plant parts','','','Answer :','',1,0,1,NULL),(36354,482,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What helps us to easily identify the plants around us?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Different characteristics of plants such as their height, life span, shapes of leaves, colour of flower, fruits etc. help us to identify the plants around us.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36355,482,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the different types of leaf.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Leaves are generally thin, flat and green in colour.\nii) The leaf blade or lamina is the broad, spread- out part of the leaf. The edge is called a leaf- margin which can be different types search as entire, dentate or lobed.\niii) The leaf apex is the tip of the leaf. It may be tapering, pointed or rounded.\niv) Some leaves have a stalk called a petiole whereas some leaves do not have it.\nv) The leaf base is the portion of the leaf attached to the stem.\nvi) Stipules which are small leaf- like structures may be present near the leaf base or they may not.\nvii) Leaves of some plants are called simple leaves while some have compound leaves. Simple leaves have a single undivided leaf blade and a single mid-rib. Compound leaves have the leaf-blade divided into many small parts called leaflets.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36356,482,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different types of leaves according to their arrangement of leaves on the stem and their shapes?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','According to arrangement of leaves on the stem they are alternate, opposite, whorled, spiral, etc. According to their shape, leaves are rounded (obovate), palmate, lanceolate, linear, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36357,482,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What would have happened if plants like tamarind, banyan and mango had fibrous roots?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Tamarind and mango are huge trees with a grand canopy of leaves. The roots of these plants should be strong enough to support these trees. If they had fibrous roots, the trees will be uprooted and fallen down.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36358,482,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will happen if the root-tip is inured?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','If the root tip is injured, the growth of the plant will be stunted. Initially the tree will try to survive but injured root may not absorb water and minerals from the soil. This will make the tree weak. The support of the tree will also become insufficient and the tree will eventually die.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36359,482,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which types of roots do the fenugreek, spinach and onion plants have?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Fenugreek has tap root but it does not traverse deep down in the soil. Spinach horizontally in the soil. Onion has fibrous roots. They emerge from disc shaped structure which is a modified stem.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36360,482,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw neat labelled diagram: Tap root\n\nAns. : \n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram','',0,0,1,NULL),(36361,482,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe any one flower and its various parts and describe it in your own word.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Flower chosen in Hibiscus. \ni) Hibiscus flower has a pedicel.\nii) All the whorls are arranged over the thalamus.\niii) Four important floral whorls are seen clearly. These are :\na) Calyx : Calyx has five sepals. This part is green.\nb) Corolla : Corolla is made up of five petals which are big and bright red in colour. The petals are free from each other.\nc) Androecium : This part is a male part of the flower. The stamen looks like a long tube. At the end of this tube are seen anthers. Anthers are attached to this tube by tiny filaments. Pollen grains are seen on the anthers. Some of the pollen grains stuck up to the hands.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36362,482,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describes the functions of various of plants: i) Functions of the root	ii) Functions of steam\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','a) Functions of the root :\ni) Anchoring and supporting the plant. 	ii) Absorption of water and minerals	iii) Some roots show modifications for performing additional functions. E.g. Aerial roots absorb moisture from air, stilt-roots-give additional support, runners-help in vegetative propagation, breathing roots (pneumatophores)- breathe for plants, some underground roots store food materials etc.\nb) Functions of stems :\ni) To hold all the branches of the plant\nii) To perform function of photosynthesis as in cactus\niii) To transport water absorbed by the roots to all the parts of a plant and to transport food prepared by the leaves to various plant parts.\niv) Modified stems perform functions such as food storage, reproduction, support, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36363,482,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describes the functions of various of plants: i) Functions of the leaves	ii) Functions of the flower		iii) Functions of the fruits. \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n','','','a) Functions of the leaves : \ni) Production of food by performing photosynthesis\nii) Transpiration, i.e. giving out excess of water\niii) Modified leaves perform additional functions such as reproduction, food storage, support etc.\nb) Functions of flowers : Reproduction\nc) Functions of fruits : Protection of seeds and storage of food materials.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36364,482,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Certain properties are mentioned below. Find a leaf corresponding to each properties and describe those plants: i) Leave with smooth surface 	ii) Leaves with rough surface.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Leave with smooth surface : Leaves : Mango leaves\nDescription : When we touch the leaf lamina, it appears smooth.\nii) Leaves with rough surface : Fig leaf\nDescription : When we touch the lamina of fig, leaf it feels rough.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36365,482,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Certain properties are mentioned below. Find a leaf corresponding to each properties and describe those plants: i) Fleshy leaf		ii) Spines on leaf\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','a) Fleshy leaf : Aloe vera leaf\nDescription : Aloe vera leaf contain the water and it modified for storing food. This help them to survive in place where little water is available.\nb) Spines on leaf : Pandanus (Kewada)\nDescription : This leaf posses prickly spines along the edges of mid-rib.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36366,482,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is venation? Describe the types of venation with suitable examples.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) The arrangement of the veins in the leaf is called venation. The main two types of venations are reticulate venation and parallel venation.\nii) When the leaf is divided into two equal parts by a single mid- vein lying in the mid- line of the leaf blade, it is called reticulate venation.\niii) In this type of secondary veins arise from the mid-vein forming a branched the network or reticulum.\niv) When all the veins run parallel to each other from the leaf-base to the leaf apex it is called parallel venation. Examples : Reticulate venation, Dicotyledonous plants like Peepul and Hibiscus. Parallel venation : monocotyledonous plants like Maize.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(36367,482,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw neat labelled diagram of the vertical section of hibiscus flower.\n\nAns. : \n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram','',0,0,1,NULL),(36495,386,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Text Book','','1) Choose the correct words given in the bracket and complete the following conversations. ----------------------- 3M\n         (fine, good morning, you)\n       \n         Neha : Hello Radha, --------------\n        \n       Radha : Good morning, Neha. How are ------?\n     \n        Neha : I am --------. Thank you.\n\n2) Write the meaning of the following words : --------------------------- 2M\n     i) Good morning         ii) Best\n\nAns. i) -------------- ----------------\n     \n      ii) --------------- ---------------','','','Answer\nA. Neha : Hello Radha, good morning\n    Radha : Good morning, Neha. How are you?\nNeha : I am fine. Thank you.','',0,0,0,NULL),(36496,386,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Choose the correct words given in the bracket and complete the following conversations. : ----------------------- 3M\n   (Hello, going, bye)\n   Ram : Hi\n\n   Imran : -----------\n\n   Ram : Where are  you ------------- ?\n\n   Imran : I am -------- to school.\n   Ram : ok, bye.\n\n   Imran : ----------------\n    \n2) Write the meaning of the following words : ------------------------ 2M\n       i) Hope          ii) Feeling\n\nAns. : i) ----------------------------------------\n         \n         ii) ----------------------------------------','','','Answer\nRam : Hi\nImran : Hello\nRam : Where are you going?\nImran : I am going to school.\nRam : Ok, bye.\nImran : Bye','',0,0,0,NULL),(36497,387,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write all the small letters on single line. --------------------------------- 2M\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Write all the small letters in different sizes - very big and very tiny. ----------------------- 3M\n\nAns. a) --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        b) --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(36498,387,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write all the capital letters in fancy shapes. ---------------------- 3M\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Write all the capital letters on a single line. --------------------- 2M\n\nAns. : -----------