,'Out of Text Book','','1) Complete the following sentences.\n      i. One of them was good at --------------\n      ii. The other friend had heard that.\nTwo friends were travelling together through a wood, when suddenly they came across a bear. The bear saw the travellers and rushed out upon them.\nOne of the travellers was good at climbing trees. Without waiting for his friend, he caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree, and hid himself among the leaves.\nThe other did not know what to do. He had heard that bears do not bother with dead animals. So he threw himself flat down upon the ground, with his face in the dust.\nThe bear, coming up to him, put its muzzle close to his ear, and sniffed and sniffed. But the man lay very quietly on the ground and did not even breathe. At last with a growl it shook its head and slouched off.\nThen the fellow in the tree came down to his comrade, and laughing, said, \"What was it that Master Bruin whispered to you?\"\n\"He told me,\" said the other, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\n2) Complete the following chart about what the two friends did when they saw a bear.\n    First friend = --------------------------                   \n    Second friend = --------------------------\n3) Write the advice of the Bear   ','','','1) i) climbing trees.\n    ii) that bears do not bother with dead animals.\n2) First Friend - At the sight of a bear the first friend caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree and hid himself among the leaves.\n     Second Friend - The second friend threw himself flat down upon the ground with his face in the dust.\n3) Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39272,570,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Complete the following web : ','Two friends were travelling together through a wood, when suddenly they came across a bear. The bear saw the travellers and rushed out upon them.\nOne of the travellers was good at climbing trees. Without waiting for his friend, he caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree, and hid himself among the leaves.\nThe other did not know what to do. He had heard that bears do not bother with dead animals. So he threw himself flat down upon the ground, with his face in the dust.\nThe bear, coming up to him, put its muzzle close to his ear, and sniffed and sniffed. But the man lay very quietly on the ground and did not even breathe. At last with a growl it shook its head and slouched off.\nThen the fellow in the tree came down to his comrade, and laughing, said, \"What was it that Master Bruin whispered to you?\"\n\"He told me,\" said the other, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\n2) Find out the words that are used for :\n     a) friend               b) Bear                 c) Man \n3) From the passage find out he opposites of\n      a) bad x   -----           b) living x  --------','','1) i) The bear came near the second friend. \n    ii) He put his muzzle close to his ear and sniffed. \n    iii) At last he growled and walked away.\n2) a) comrade            b) Master Bruin        c) traveller, fellow\n3) a) good              b) dead','',1,0,0,NULL),(39273,570,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Fill in the blanks with appropriate phrase given in the bracket.\n      (was good at, caught hold of, at last, at a pinch)\n     a) The policeman ran fast and --------- the thief.\n     b) Arjun -------- archery.\n     c) --------- we reached our home.\n     d) I am grateful to you because you helped me -------------\nTwo friends were travelling together through a wood, when suddenly they came across a bear. The bear saw the travellers and rushed out upon them.\nOne of the travellers was good at climbing trees. Without waiting for his friend, he caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree, and hid himself among the leaves.\nThe other did not know what to do. He had heard that bears do not bother with dead animals. So he threw himself flat down upon the ground, with his face in the dust.\nThe bear, coming up to him, put its muzzle close to his ear, and sniffed and sniffed. But the man lay very quietly on the ground and did not even breathe. At last with a growl it shook its head and slouched off.\nThen the fellow in the tree came down to his comrade, and laughing, said, \"What was it that Master Bruin whispered to you?\"\n\"He told me,\" said the other, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\n2) Identify the tense of the following sentences.\n     i) He deserts you at a pinch.\n    ii) He hid himself among the trees.\n3) Who is your true friend? why?','','','1) a) caught hold of          b) is good at            c) At last         d) at a pinch\n2) i) Simple present tense           ii) Simple past tense\n3) Keshav is my true friend. He helps me all the time. He is very cheerful and clever. We live in the neighbouring houses.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39274,570,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Two friends were travelling together through a wood, when suddenly they came across a bear. The bear saw the travellers and rushed out upon them.\nOne of the travellers was good at climbing trees. Without waiting for his friend, he caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree, and hid himself among the leaves.\nThe other did not know what to do. He had heard that bears do not bother with dead animals. So he threw himself flat down upon the ground, with his face in the dust.\nThe bear, coming up to him, put its muzzle close to his ear, and sniffed and sniffed. But the man lay very quietly on the ground and did not even breathe. At last with a growl it shook its head and slouched off.\nThen the fellow in the tree came down to his comrade, and laughing, said, \"What was it that Master Bruin whispered to you?\"\n\"He told me,\" said the other, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\n1) Who rushed out upon the friends? Where were they at that time\n2) Why did the other traveller lie with his face in the dust? \n3) Did the bear really whisper to the traveller? \n4) What did the other traveller tell his friend? \n5) Use the phrase ‘be good at something’ in your own sentence.','','','1) The bear rushed out upon the friends. At that time they were in a wood.\n2) The bear rushed out upon the friends while travelling through a wood. The other traveller didn\'t know what to do. He had heard that bears did not bother with dead animals. So he lay down upon the ground with his face in the dust.\n3) No, the bear didn\'t whisper to the traveller.\n4) The other traveller told his friend that the bear whispered in his ear, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\"\n5) I am good at swimming.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39275,570,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false :\n     i. The Town Mouse liked the country food very much.\n     ii. The Country Mouse loved his town friend.\n     iii. The Town Mouse invited his country friend.\n     iv. The Country Mouse offered every thing freely.\nOnce upon a time, a Town Mouse went on a visit to his cousin in the country. The Country Mouse was a simple, rustic fellow, he loved his town friend. He gave him a hearty welcome. Beans, cheese and bread, were all he had to offer,\nbut he offered them freely. The Town Mouse rather turned up his long nose at this simple country fare, and said;\n\"I cannot understand, Cousin, how you can put up with this poor food! But of course you cannot expect anything better in the country. Come with me and I will show you how to live. When you see town life, you will wonder how you could ever live in the country.\"\n2) Give the plurals of : \n     a) Mouse = -----------    b) Country = ------------\n3) Do as directed \n     i. I can not understand (Make affirmative)\n     ii. I will show you (Identify the tense)','','','1) i) False            ii) True           iii) True            iv) True\n2) a) mice           b) countries \n3) i) I am unable to understand.\n    ii) Simple future tense.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39276,570,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Complete the following web : \n','Once upon a time, a Town Mouse went on a visit to his cousin in the country. The Country Mouse was a simple, rustic fellow, he loved his town friend. He gave him a hearty welcome. Beans, cheese and bread, were all he had to offer,\nbut he offered them freely. The Town Mouse rather turned up his long nose at this simple country fare, and said;\n\"I cannot understand, Cousin, how you can put up with this poor food! But of course you cannot expect anything better in the country. Come with me and I will show you how to live. When you see town life, you will wonder how you could ever live in the country.\"\n2) Give the plurals : \n    a) life = ----------    b) child = ------------\n3) Do as directed \n     i. the town mouse said I cannot understand cousin. (Punctuate)\n     ii. He will live in a hole. (Identify tense)','','1)  The country food includes beans cheese and bread. It was offered freely. But the Town mouse didn\'t like that simple poor food.\n2) a) lives         b) children\n3) i) The Town Mouse said, “I can not understand, Cousin”\n     ii) Future tense','',1,0,0,NULL),(39277,570,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The Town mouse took his friend......\n     ii. The two mice ate -------------\n      iii. Suddenly the mice heard --------------\n      iv. At the sight of two huge mastiffs, two mice --------------\n\"You will want some refreshment after our long journey,\" said the polite Town Mouse, and took his friend into a grand dining room. There they found the remains of a fine feast. Soon the two mice were eating up jellies and cakes and all that was nice.\nSuddenly they heard growling and barking.\n\"What is that?\" said the Country Mouse.\n\"It is only the dogs of the house, answered the other.\n\"Only!\" said the Country Mouse. \"I do not like that music at my dinner!\"\nJust at that moment the door flew open, in came two huge mastiffs, and the two mice had to scamper down and run off.\n\"Good-bye, Cousin,\" said the Country Mouse.\n\"What! Going so soon?\" said the other.\n\"Yes,\" he replied; \"Better beans and bread in peace than cakes and jellies in fear.\"\n2) Write the following nouns in appropriate column. \n      (Cakes, bread, beans, water)\n   i) Countable Noun = -------------------------\n  ii) Uncountable Noun = --------------------\n3) Find the past tense form of :\n     a) hear = ----------------   b) fly = -------------','','','1) i) into a grand dining room.          ii) jellies and cakes.\n     iii) growling and barking.              iv) scampered down and ran off.\n2) i) Countable Noun = cakes, beans\n    ii) Uncountable Noun – bread, water\n3) a) heard         b) flew','',0,0,0,NULL),(39278,570,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Read the passage and complete the table :\n','\"You will want some refreshment after our long journey,\" said the polite Town Mouse, and took his friend into a grand dining room. There they found the remains of a fine feast. Soon the two mice were eating up jellies and cakes and all that was nice.\nSuddenly they heard growling and barking.\n\"What is that?\" said the Country Mouse.\n\"It is only the dogs of the house, answered the other.\n\"Only!\" said the Country Mouse. \"I do not like that music at my dinner!\"\nJust at that moment the door flew open, in came two huge mastiffs, and the two mice had to scamper down and run off.\n\"Good-bye, Cousin,\" said the Country Mouse.\n\"What! Going so soon?\" said the other.\n\"Yes,\" he replied; \"Better beans and bread in peace than cakes and jellies in fear.\"\n2) Find the past tense forms of :\n     a) say - -------------      b) take - -----------\n3) Which life do you like more, country life or town life?','','1) Country food – beans, cheese and bread, is eaten in peace \n     Town food – jellies and cakes, should be eaten in fear\n2) a) said             b) took\n3) I like country life more. It is not crowded. It is free from air pollution, noise pollution, food pollution and water pollution. We can get fresh air, fresh water, fresh fruits and vegetables in villages. It is free from traffic. It is calm and quiet in villages.','',1,0,0,NULL),(39279,187,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities :\n1) Complete the following sentences.\n\n      i. One of them was good at -------------------------\n\n      ii. The other friend had heard that -----------------\nTwo friends were travelling together through a wood, when suddenly they came across a bear. The bear saw the travellers and rushed out upon them.\nOne of the travellers was good at climbing trees. Without waiting for his friend, he caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree, and hid himself among the leaves.\nThe other did not know what to do. He had heard that bears do not bother with dead animals. So he threw himself flat down upon the ground, with his face in the dust.\nThe bear, coming up to him, put its muzzle close to his ear, and sniffed and sniffed. But the man lay very quietly on the ground and did not even breathe. At last with a growl it shook its head and slouched off.\nThen the fellow in the tree came down to his comrade, and laughing, said, \"What was it that Master Bruin whispered to you?\"\n\"He told me,\" said the other, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\n2) Complete the following chart about what the two friends did when they saw a bear.\n\n    First friend = ----------------------------------------------------                   \n    \n    Second friend = ------------------------------------------------\n3) Write the advice of the Bear   \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n           \n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','1) i) climbing trees.\n    ii) that bears do not bother with dead animals.\n2) First Friend - At the sight of a bear the first friend caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree and hid himself among the leaves.\n     Second Friend - The second friend threw himself flat down upon the ground with his face in the dust.\n3) Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39280,187,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities :\n1) Complete the following web : \n','\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo friends were travelling together through a wood, when suddenly they came across a bear. The bear saw the travellers and rushed out upon them.\nOne of the travellers was good at climbing trees. Without waiting for his friend, he caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree, and hid himself among the leaves.\nThe other did not know what to do. He had heard that bears do not bother with dead animals. So he threw himself flat down upon the ground, with his face in the dust.\nThe bear, coming up to him, put its muzzle close to his ear, and sniffed and sniffed. But the man lay very quietly on the ground and did not even breathe. At last with a growl it shook its head and slouched off.\nThen the fellow in the tree came down to his comrade, and laughing, said, \"What was it that Master Bruin whispered to you?\"\n\"He told me,\" said the other, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\n2) Find out the words that are used for :\n     a) friend = ------------------------              \n    \n      b) Bear = -----------------------------                 \n\n       c) Man  = --------------------------------------\n3) From the passage find out he opposites of\n      a) bad x   ---------------  --------------------         \n\n      b) living x  -------- --------------------','','1) i) The bear came near the second friend. \n    ii) He put his muzzle close to his ear and sniffed. \n    iii) At last he growled and walked away.\n2) a) comrade            b) Master Bruin        c) traveller, fellow\n3) a) good              b) dead','',1,0,0,NULL),(39282,187,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities :\n1) Fill in the blanks with appropriate phrase given in the bracket.\n      (was good at, caught hold of, at last, at a pinch)\n     a) The policeman ran fast and --------------------- the thief.\n    \n      b) Arjun ------------------------ archery.\n\n      c) --------------------- we reached our home.\n     \n      d) I am grateful to you because you helped me --------------------------\n\nTwo friends were travelling together through a wood, when suddenly they came across a bear. The bear saw the travellers and rushed out upon them.\nOne of the travellers was good at climbing trees. Without waiting for his friend, he caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree, and hid himself among the leaves.\nThe other did not know what to do. He had heard that bears do not bother with dead animals. So he threw himself flat down upon the ground, with his face in the dust.\nThe bear, coming up to him, put its muzzle close to his ear, and sniffed and sniffed. But the man lay very quietly on the ground and did not even breathe. At last with a growl it shook its head and slouched off.\nThen the fellow in the tree came down to his comrade, and laughing, said, \"What was it that Master Bruin whispered to you?\"\n\"He told me,\" said the other, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\n2) Identify the tense of the following sentences.\n     i) He deserts you at a pinch.\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------\n    ii) He hid himself among the trees.\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Who is your true friend? why?\n\n    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','1) a) caught hold of          b) is good at            c) At last         d) at a pinch\n2) i) Simple present tense           ii) Simple past tense\n3) Keshav is my true friend. He helps me all the time. He is very cheerful and clever. We live in the neighbouring houses.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39283,187,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities :\nTwo friends were travelling together through a wood, when suddenly they came across a bear. The bear saw the travellers and rushed out upon them.\nOne of the travellers was good at climbing trees. Without waiting for his friend, he caught hold of the branch of a nearby tree, and hid himself among the leaves.\nThe other did not know what to do. He had heard that bears do not bother with dead animals. So he threw himself flat down upon the ground, with his face in the dust.\nThe bear, coming up to him, put its muzzle close to his ear, and sniffed and sniffed. But the man lay very quietly on the ground and did not even breathe. At last with a growl it shook its head and slouched off.\nThen the fellow in the tree came down to his comrade, and laughing, said, \"What was it that Master Bruin whispered to you?\"\n\"He told me,\" said the other, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\n1) Who rushed out upon the friends? Where were they at that time\n\n     -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Why did the other traveller lie with his face in the dust? \n\n     -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Did the bear really whisper to the traveller? \n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4) What did the other traveller tell his friend? \n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Use the phrase ‘be good at something’ in your own sentence.\n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','1) The bear rushed out upon the friends. At that time they were in a wood.\n2) The bear rushed out upon the friends while travelling through a wood. The other traveller didn\'t know what to do. He had heard that bears did not bother with dead animals. So he lay down upon the ground with his face in the dust.\n3) No, the bear didn\'t whisper to the traveller.\n4) The other traveller told his friend that the bear whispered in his ear, \"Never trust a friend who deserts you at a pinch.\"\n5) I am good at swimming.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39284,187,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities :\n1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false :\n     i. The Town Mouse liked the country food very much. --------------------\n\n     ii. The Country Mouse loved his town friend. ----------------------------\n\n     iii. The Town Mouse invited his country friend. ----------------------------\n\n     iv. The Country Mouse offered every thing freely. ------------------------------\nOnce upon a time, a Town Mouse went on a visit to his cousin in the country. The Country Mouse was a simple, rustic fellow, he loved his town friend. He gave him a hearty welcome. Beans, cheese and bread, were all he had to offer,\nbut he offered them freely. The Town Mouse rather turned up his long nose at this simple country fare, and said;\n\"I cannot understand, Cousin, how you can put up with this poor food! But of course you cannot expect anything better in the country. Come with me and I will show you how to live. When you see town life, you will wonder how you could ever live in the country.\"\n2) Give the plurals of : \n     \n        a) Mouse = -----------                b) Country = ------------\n3) Do as directed \n     i. I can not understand (Make affirmative)\n       \n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. I will show you (Identify the tense)\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','1) i) False            ii) True           iii) True            iv) True\n2) a) mice           b) countries \n3) i) I am unable to understand.\n    ii) Simple future tense.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39285,187,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities :\n1) Complete the following web : \n','\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOnce upon a time, a Town Mouse went on a visit to his cousin in the country. The Country Mouse was a simple, rustic fellow, he loved his town friend. He gave him a hearty welcome. Beans, cheese and bread, were all he had to offer,\nbut he offered them freely. The Town Mouse rather turned up his long nose at this simple country fare, and said;\n\"I cannot understand, Cousin, how you can put up with this poor food! But of course you cannot expect anything better in the country. Come with me and I will show you how to live. When you see town life, you will wonder how you could ever live in the country.\"\n2) Give the plurals : \n\n    a) life = ----------                      b) child = ------------\n3) Do as directed \n     i. the town mouse said I cannot understand cousin. (Punctuate)\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. He will live in a hole. (Identify tense)\n\n           -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','1)  The country food includes beans cheese and bread. It was offered freely. But the Town mouse didn\'t like that simple poor food.\n2) a) lives         b) children\n3) i) The Town Mouse said, “I can not understand, Cousin”\n     ii) Future tense','',1,0,0,NULL),(39286,187,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities :\n1) Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The Town mouse took his friend ------------------------------------------\n\n     ii. The two mice ate ----------------------------------------------------------\n\n      iii. Suddenly the mice heard -------------------------------------------------\n\n      iv. At the sight of two huge mastiffs, two mice -----------------------------------\n\"You will want some refreshment after our long journey,\" said the polite Town Mouse, and took his friend into a grand dining room. There they found the remains of a fine feast. Soon the two mice were eating up jellies and cakes and all that was nice.\nSuddenly they heard growling and barking.\n\"What is that?\" said the Country Mouse.\n\"It is only the dogs of the house, answered the other.\n\"Only!\" said the Country Mouse. \"I do not like that music at my dinner!\"\nJust at that moment the door flew open, in came two huge mastiffs, and the two mice had to scamper down and run off.\n\"Good-bye, Cousin,\" said the Country Mouse.\n\"What! Going so soon?\" said the other.\n\"Yes,\" he replied; \"Better beans and bread in peace than cakes and jellies in fear.\"\n2) Write the following nouns in appropriate column. \n      (Cakes, bread, beans, water)\n\n   i) Countable Noun = -------------------------\n\n  ii) Uncountable Noun = --------------------\n3) Find the past tense form of :\n\n     a) hear = ----------------        b) fly = -------------','','','1) i) into a grand dining room.          ii) jellies and cakes.\n     iii) growling and barking.              iv) scampered down and ran off.\n2) i) Countable Noun = cakes, beans\n    ii) Uncountable Noun – bread, water\n3) a) heard         b) flew','',0,0,0,NULL),(39287,187,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities :\n1) Read the passage and complete the table :\n','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\"You will want some refreshment after our long journey,\" said the polite Town Mouse, and took his friend into a grand dining room. There they found the remains of a fine feast. Soon the two mice were eating up jellies and cakes and all that was nice.\nSuddenly they heard growling and barking.\n\"What is that?\" said the Country Mouse.\n\"It is only the dogs of the house, answered the other.\n\"Only!\" said the Country Mouse. \"I do not like that music at my dinner!\"\nJust at that moment the door flew open, in came two huge mastiffs, and the two mice had to scamper down and run off.\n\"Good-bye, Cousin,\" said the Country Mouse.\n\"What! Going so soon?\" said the other.\n\"Yes,\" he replied; \"Better beans and bread in peace than cakes and jellies in fear.\"\n2) Find the past tense forms of :\n\n     a) say - -------------               b) take - -----------\n3) Which life do you like more, country life or town life?\n \n      ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','1) Country food – beans, cheese and bread, is eaten in peace \n     Town food – jellies and cakes, should be eaten in fear\n2) a) said             b) took\n3) I like country life more. It is not crowded. It is free from air pollution, noise pollution, food pollution and water pollution. We can get fresh air, fresh water, fresh fruits and vegetables in villages. It is free from traffic. It is calm and quiet i