i) Will ---------------?\n iii) May ---------?                                iv) Could --------------?\nv) Did ----------?','','','i) Can you sing?                ii) Will you stand up, please?\niii) May I come in?            iv) Could you please send me your mobile number?  \nv) Did they go home?','',0,0,0,NULL),(39844,563,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following sentences using the \'to be\', \'to have\' \ni) I am ------                         ii) I have ----------- \niii) You have ---------           iv) They have ---------- \nv) She has ---------              vi) He is ----------','','','i) I am a teacher               ii) I have played.\niii) You have driven.         iv) They have listened.\nv) She has spoken            vi) He is a doctor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39845,563,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following sentence using the \'to be\', \'to have\' \ni) It is ----------                         ii) We have ------\n iii) They are ---------               iv) He has ---------- \nv) You has ---------- ','','','i) It is a bird                            ii) We have played.\niii) They are naughty             iv) He has dawn.\nv) You has driven.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39846,563,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Adding \'s\' to verbs. \ni) I like -----------                    ii) We like ---------- \niii) You like -------                 iv) He likes ---------- \nv) She likes ----------','','','i) I like tomatoes              ii) We like tomatoes.\niii) You like tomatoes      iv) He likes tomatoes.\nv) She likes tomatoes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39847,563,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use of \'do\' \ni) I do/don’t ---------                     ii) You do/don’t ------------\niii) We do/don’t ----------            iv) We did/didn’t ------------\nv) You did/didn’t ------------        vi) They did/didn’t ----------------','','','i) I don’t go                      ii) You don’t think.\niii) We don’t rest             iv) We didn’t rest.\nv) You didn’t think           v) They didn’t go.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39848,563,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the following table and answer the questions: ','1) Write down three letter words from the given table.\n2) Write down the words which begin with ‘a\'\n3) Write down the words which end with ‘t’.\n4) Write down four letter words.','','1) the, and, for, not, you, but, his, her, she, one, all, out, who, can\n2) and, as, at, an, all, about\n3) that, it, not, at, but, what, out, about\n4) that, have, with, this, from, they, will, what, when','',1,0,0,NULL),(39849,563,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Answer the following questions : \n1) Why is Tara excited?\n2) Why did Bittu become nervous?\n3) Why is Kuku happy?\n4) Why did Cat think that they are lucky?\n5) What did Parrot say to the Cat?','','','1) Tara is excited about starting studies again.\n2) Bittu became nervous because holidays were over.\n3) Kuku is happy because now she can play more on the school ground.\n4) The cat thought that they were lucky because they didn\'t have school.\n5) Parrot said to the Cat that it would never learn.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39850,181,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write one meaningful question each beginning with the following with the following words : \ni) Who ----------?                                             ii) What -------------? \n\nii) Where -------?                                             iv) When ----------? \n\nv) Why ---------?                                               vi) How ------------?','','','i) Who is that boy?                     ii) What are you doing? \niii) Where do you live?               iv) When is English period? \nv) Why are you laughing?           vi) How do you go to school? ','',0,0,0,NULL),(39851,181,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write one meaningful question each beginning with the following with the following words \n\n i) Are ----------?                                       ii) Is -------------?\n\n ii) Have -------?                                        iv) Has ----------? \n\nv) Do ---------?                                          vi) Does ------------?','','','i) Are you ready?                              ii) Is she is good girl?\niii) Have you finished your work?     iv) Has she finished her lunch? \nv) Do you like cold drink?                  vi) Does she know everything? ','',0,0,0,NULL),(39852,181,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write one meaningful question each beginning with the following with the following words : \n i) Aren\'t they ----------?                             ii) Isn\'t he -------------? \n\nii) Don\'t you -------?                                    iv) Doesn\'t she ----------? \n\nv) Can\'t we ---------?                                   vi) Won\'t you ------------?','','','i) Aren\'t they coming?                        ii) Isn\'t he precious?\niii) Don\'t you have any money?         iv) Doesn\'t she like to play? \nv) Can\'t we swim today?                      vi) Won\'t you play? ','',0,0,0,NULL),(39853,181,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write one meaningful question each beginning with the following with the following words : \n i) Can ----------?                                 ii) Will ---------------?\n\n iii) May ---------?                                iv) Could --------------?\n\n  v) Did ----------?','','','i) Can you sing?                ii) Will you stand up, please?\niii) May I come in?            iv) Could you please send me your mobile number?  \nv) Did they go home?','',0,0,0,NULL),(39854,181,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following sentences using the \'to be\', \'to have\' \ni) I am ------                         ii) I have ----------- \n\niii) You have ---------           iv) They have ----------\n \nv) She has ---------              vi) He is ----------','','','i) I am a teacher               ii) I have played.\niii) You have driven.         iv) They have listened.\nv) She has spoken            vi) He is a doctor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39855,181,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following sentence using the \'to be\', \'to have\' \n\ni) It is ----------                         ii) We have ------\n\n iii) They are ---------               iv) He has ---------- \n\nv) You has ---------- ','','','i) It is a bird                            ii) We have played.\niii) They are naughty             iv) He has dawn.\nv) You has driven.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(39856,181,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Adding \'s\' to verbs. \n\ni) I like -----------                    ii) We like ---------- \n\niii) You like -------                 iv) He likes ---------- \n\nv) She likes ----------','','','i) I like tomatoes              ii) We like tomatoes.\niii) You like tomatoes      iv) He likes tomatoes.\nv) She likes tomatoes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39857,181,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use of \'do\' \n\ni) I do/don’t ---------                     ii) You do/don’t ------------\n\niii) We do/don’t ----------            iv) We did/didn’t ------------\n\nv) You did/didn’t ------------        vi) They did/didn’t ----------------','','','i) I don’t go                      ii) You don’t think.\niii) We don’t rest             iv) We didn’t rest.\nv) You didn’t think           v) They didn’t go.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39858,182,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','It was an early Easter. In the yards lay snow, and rills ran down the village. A large puddle had run down from a manure pile into a lane between two farms. And at this puddle two girls, one older than the other, had met. Both of them had been dressed by their mothers in new clothes. The little girl had a blue dress, and the elder a yellow one with a design. Both had their heads wrapped in red kerchiefs. After prayers in the Church, the two girls went to the puddle, where they showed their new garments to each other, and began to play. They wanted to plash in the water. The little girl started to go into the puddle with her shoes on, but the older girl said to her: \n\"Don\'t go, Malasha, your mother will scold you. I will take off my shoes, and you do the same.” ','1) Name the following : \na) Name of the festival : ------- \n\nAns. : ------------------ \nb) Place of worship for the christians : ------------ \n\nAns. : ---------------- ','2) Complete the following : Two girls  \n\nAge : -----------                                    Dress :  ------------------\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------- ------------------------------------------------------------ \n3) Write the following words in appropriate Coloumn : \nSnow, village, water, farms \n\ni) Countable noun : ---------------------------------------------------- \n\nii) Uncountable noun : ------------------------------------------------ ','','1) a) Easter               b) Church \n2) Age : elder, younger Dress : yellow, blue \n3) i) Countable Noun : Village, farms \n    ii) Uncountable noun : Snow, water ','',0,0,0,NULL),(39859,182,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','It was an early Easter. In the yards lay snow, and rills ran down the village. A large puddle had run down from a manure pile into a lane between two farms. And at this puddle two girls, one older than the other, had met. Both of them had been dressed by their mothers in new clothes. The little girl had a blue dress, and the elder a yellow one with a design. Both had their heads wrapped in red kerchiefs. After prayers in the Church, the two girls went to the puddle, where they showed their new garments to each other, and began to play. They wanted to plash in the water. The little girl started to go into the puddle with her shoes on, but the older girl said to her: \n\"Don\'t go, Malasha, your mother will scold you. I will take off my shoes, and you do the same.” ','1) Complete the following sentences : \na) The two girls went to the puddle (time) ---------- \nAns. : ---------------------------- \nb) The large puddle ran into (place) -------- \nAns. : -------------------- ','2) Where did the two girls go after prayers in the church?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Write the past tense of given verbs : \n\ni) grasp - --------------------------        ii) pick - ----------------------- \n\niii) gather - ---------------------           iv) laugh -----------------------','','1) a) After prayers in the church           b) a lane between two farms \n2) After prayers in the church, the two girls went to the puddle.\n3) 3) i) grasped ii) picked iii) gathered iv) laughed ','',0,0,0,NULL),(39860,564,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','It was an early Easter. In the yards lay snow, and rills ran down the village. A large puddle had run down from a manure pile into a lane between two farms. And at this puddle two girls, one older than the other, had met. Both of them had been dressed by their mothers in new clothes. The little girl had a blue dress, and the elder a yellow one with a design. Both had their heads wrapped in red kerchiefs. After prayers in the Church, the two girls went to the puddle, where they showed their new garments to each other, and began to play. They wanted to plash in the water. The little girl started to go into the puddle with her shoes on, but the older girl said to her: \n\"Don\'t go, Malasha, your mother will scold you. I will take off my shoes, and you do the same.” ','1) Name the following : \na) Name of the festival : ------- \nb) Place of worship for the christians : ------------ \n\n','2) Complete the following : Two girls  \nAge : -----------                                    Dress :  ------------------\n3) Write the following words in appropriate Coloumn : \nSnow, village, water, farms \ni) Countable noun : ---------------------------------------------------- \nii) Uncountable noun : ------------------------------------------------- ','','1) a) Easter               b) Church \n2) Age : elder, younger Dress : yellow, blue \n3) 3) i) Countable Noun : Village, farms \n    ii) Uncountable noun : Snow, water ','',0,0,0,NULL),(39861,564,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','It was an early Easter. In the yards lay snow, and rills ran down the village. A large puddle had run down from a manure pile into a lane between two farms. And at this puddle two girls, one older than the other, had met. Both of them had been dressed by their mothers in new clothes. The little girl had a blue dress, and the elder a yellow one with a design. Both had their heads wrapped in red kerchiefs. After prayers in the Church, the two girls went to the puddle, where they showed their new garments to each other, and began to play. They wanted to plash in the water. The little girl started to go into the puddle with her shoes on, but the older girl said to her: \n\"Don\'t go, Malasha, your mother will scold you. I will take off my shoes, and you do the same.” ','1) Complete the following sentences : \na) The two girls went to the puddle (time) ---------- \nb) The large puddle ran into (place) -------- \n\n','2) Where did the two girls go after prayers in the church?\n3) Write the past tense of given verbs : \ni) grasp - --------------------------          ii) pick - ----------------------- \niii) gather - ---------------------            iv) laugh -----------------------','','1) a) After prayers in the church           b) a lane between two farms \n2) After prayers in the church, the two girls went to the puddle.\n3) 3) i) grasped        ii) picked            iii) gathered            iv) laughed ','',0,0,0,NULL),(39862,564,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','After prayers in the Church, the two girls went to the puddle, where they showed their new garments to each other, and began to play. They wanted to plash in the water. The little girl started to go into the puddle with her shoes on, but the older girl said to her:\n\"Don\'t go, Malasha, your mother will scold you. I will take off my shoes, and you do the same.\"\nThe girls took off their shoes, raised their skirts, and walked through the puddle toward each other. Malasha stepped in up to her ankles, and said:\n\"It is deep, Akulka, I am afraid.\"\n\"Never mind,\" she replied, \"it will not be any deeper. Come straight toward me!\"\nThey came closer to each other. Akulka said: \"Malasha, look out, and do not splash it up, but walk softly.\"\nShe had barely said that when Malasha plumped her foot into the water and bespattered Akulka\'s new dress, and not only her dress, but also her nose and eyes. When Akulka saw the spots on her dress, she grew angry at Malasha, and scolded her, and ran after her, and wanted to strike her. Malasha was frightened and, seeing what trouble she had caused, jumped out of the puddle and ran home.\nAkulka\'s mother passed by; she saw her daughter\'s dress bespattered and soiled.\n\"Where, accursed one, did you get yourself so dirty?\"\n\"Malasha has purposely splashed it on me.\"\nAkulka\'s mother grasped Malasha and gave her a knock on the nape of her neck. Malasha began to howl, and her mother ran out of the house.','\nRead the following passage and do the activities : ','Q.1) Which mistakes did Malasha commit?\n2) a) Why did Akulka want to strike Malasha?\n    b) Why did Malasha run home?\n3) What did Akulka tell her mother?\n4) How did-Akulka\'s mother react at that time?\n5) Spot the names of body parts from the story. Add three other names of your own.','','1) Malasha plumped her foot into the water and bespattered Akulka’s new dress.\n2) a) Malasha bespattered Akulka\'s new dress, her nose and eyes. That\'s why Akulka grew angry with Malasha and wanted to strike her.\nb) Akulka grew angry with Malasha after seeing the spots on her new dress. She scolded Malasha and ran after her. Malasha was frightened by this and ran home.\n3) Akulka told her mother that Malasha had purposely splashed the spots on her dress.\n4) Akulka\'s mother grasped Malasha and gave her a knock on the nape of her neck.\n5) head, foot, eyes, nose, neck, back, legs, hands, ears, shoulders, mouth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39863,564,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','One word brought back another, and the women began to quarrel. The men, too, ran out, and a big crowd gathered in the street. All were crying, and nobody could hear his neighbour. They scolded and cursed each other; one man gave another man a push, and a fight had begun, when Akulka\'s grandmother came out. She stepped in the midst of the peasants, and began to talk to them:\n\"What are you doing, dear ones? Consider the holiday. This is a time for rejoicing. And see what sin you are doing!\"\nThey paid no attention to the old woman, and almost knocked her off her feet. She would never have stopped them, if it had not been for Akulka and Malasha.\nWhile the women exchanged words, Akulka wiped off her dress, and went back to the puddle in the lane. She picked up a pebble and began to scratch the ground so as to let the water off into the street. While she was scratching, Malasha came up and began to help her. She picked up a chip and widened the rill.','Read the following passage and do the activities ','1) How did the quarrel start?\n2) Did the people stop fighting?\n3) What did Akulka do?\n4) What did Malasha do?\n5) Change the words in the boxes to make other meaningful sentences :\n     i) I’ll take off may shoes and you do the same.\n     ii) This is a time for rejoicing.','','1) Malasha bespattered Alkuka’s new dress, her nose and eyes. Akulka told her mother that Malasha had purposely did it. So Akulka’s mother grasped Malasha and gave her a knock. Malasha began to howl and her mother ran out of the house. When Malasha’s mother saw that she began to scold her neighbor and the quarrel began.\n2) People didn\'t stop their fighting eventhough the old woman was telling them to stop.\n3) Akulka wiped off her dress and went back to the puddle in the lane. She picked up a pebble and began to scratch the ground to let the water off into the street.\n4) While Akulka was scratching, Malasha came up and began to help her. She picked up a chip and widened the rill.\n5) i) I’ll put on my new dress and you do the same.\nii) This is a time for studying.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39864,564,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','The peasants had begun to fight, just as the water went down the rill toward the place where the old woman was trying to separate the men. The girls ran, one from one side of the rill, the other from the other side.\n\"Look out, Malasha, look out!\" shouted Akulka.\nMalasha wanted to say something herself, but could not speak for laughter. The girls were running and laughing at a chip which was bobbing up and down the rill. They ran straight into the crowd of the peasants. The old woman saw them and said to the peasants:\n\"Shame on you before God, men! You have started fighting on account of these two girls, and they have long ago forgotten it: the dear children have been playing nicely together. They are wiser than you.\"\nThe men looked at the girls, and they felt ashamed. Then they laughed at themselves, and scattered to their farms.','\nRead the following passage and do the activities ','1) What was the old woman trying to do?\n2) Did the girls forget their quarrel?\n3) How can we say that?\n4) Why did the men feel ashamed?\n5) List the characters in the story and write their approximate ages.','','1) The old woman was trying to stop the fighting among the peasants. She was trying to tell them that it was Easter, time for rejoicing and not for making sin.\n2) Yes, the girls forgot their quarrel after some time.\n3) After sometime when the men were fighting, Akulka and Malasha began to talk and play with each other.\n4) When the old woman told peasants that they had started fighting on account of those two girls and they had long ago forgotton it and they had started playing nicely together. At that time the men looked at the girls and they felt ashamed.\n5) i) Akulka – 10, Malasha – 8, Akulaka’s Mother – 35, Malasha’s Mother – 32, Akulaka’s Grand-mother – 60','',0,0,0,NULL),(39866,565,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','This particular journey that we are going to read about was actually a journey from China to our own country! It was made by the famous seventh century traveller Yuan Chwang.\nIn those days, travelling from China to India was not an easy task - one had to travel thousands of miles on foot or horseback, crossing deserts, crossing snow-clad mountains, crossing strange, unfamiliar regions, using roads where bandits and robbers roamed - who would do it? But Yuan Chwang had a dream. He wanted to take Buddhist scriptures from India to his homeland in China. It was like a pilgrimage to him.','1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false :\n     i. The novel ‘Journey to the West’ is about a journey to western countries like England and America.\n    ii. In the days of Yuan Chwang travelling from China to India was not an easy task.\n','2) Answer the following questions.\n     i. What was the Yuan Chwang’s dream?\n     ii. What is the novel about?\n3) Find out the words that mean.\n    a) really               b) thieves','','1) i) False              ii) True\n2) i) Yuan Chwang wanted to take Buddhist Scriptures from India to his homeland in China.\n    ii) The novel is about the famous pilgrimage undertaken by Yuan Chwang.\n3) a) actually             b) robbers','',0,0,0,NULL),(39868,565,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','The most powerful among the three disciples is Sun Wukong. Everything about him is extra- ordinary. He was born when winds blew over a huge stone egg on the Flower Fruit Mountain. He is able to transform himself in 72 different ways. Not only that but he can also transform each of his hair strands into any living or non-living thing of his choice. His \'As-you-wish-gold-banded cudgel\' is also magical. It can grow as big as a pillar supporting the sky, but at other times, it becomes as tiny as a needle. Then the Monkey King just tucks it away behind his ear. Sun Wukong can travel thousands of kilometres in just one somersault. He can walk through fire, travel under water and fly to any place at will. His body is as hard as a diamond. So no one and nothing can injure him. His \'golden-gaze-fiery eyes\' have x-ray vision. No one can deceive him.','1) Say whether the following sentences are True or False\n     i. The three disciples were three saints\n     ii. The three disciples have supernatural powers.\n\n','2) Who was the most powerful among the three disciples?\n3) Write the past tense forms of \n     a) choose               b) fly','','1) i) False              ii) True\n2) The most powerful among the three disciples was Sun Wukong.\n3) a) chose          b) flew','',0,0,0,NULL),(39870,183,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','This particular journey that we are going to read about was actually a journey from China to our own country! It was made by the famous seventh century traveller Yuan Chwang.\nIn those days, travelling from China to India was not an easy task - one had to travel thousands of miles on foot or horseback, crossing deserts, crossing snow-clad mountains, crossing strange, unfamiliar regions, using roads where bandits and robbers roamed - who would do it? But Yuan Chwang had a dream. He wanted to take Buddhist scriptures from India to his homeland in China. It was like a pilgrimage to him.','1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false :\n     i. The novel ‘Journey to the West’ is about a journey to western countries like England and America. -------------------------\n\n    ii. In the days of Yuan Chwang travelling from China to India was not an easy task. ------------------------------','2) Answer the following questions.\n     i. What was the Yuan Chwang’s dream?\n\n           -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. What is the novel about?\n\n      -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Find out the words that mean.\n\n    a) really × -----------             b) thieves × ---------------','','1) i) False              ii) True\n2) i) Yuan Chwang wanted to take Buddhist Scriptures from India to his homeland in China.\n    ii) The novel is about the famous pilgrimage undertaken by Yuan Chwang.\n3) a) actually             b) robbers','',0,0,0,NULL),(39872,183,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','The most powerful among the three disciples is Sun Wukong. Everything about him is extra- ordinary. He was born when winds blew over a huge stone egg on the Flower Fruit Mountain. He is able to transform himself in 72 different ways. Not only that but he can also transform each of his hair strands into any living or non-living thing of his choice. His \'As-you-wish-gold-banded cudgel\' is also magical. It can grow as big as a pillar supporting the sky, but at other times, it becomes as tiny as a needle. Then the Monkey King just tucks it away behind his ear. Sun Wukong can travel thousands of kilometres in just one somersault. He can walk through fire, travel under water and fly to any place at will. His body is as hard as a diamond. So no one and nothing can injure him. His \'golden-gaze-fiery eyes\' have x-ray vision. No one can deceive him.','1) Say whether the following sentences are True or False\n\n     i. The three disciples were three saints. ------------------\n\n     ii. The three disciples have supernatural powers. --------------------','2) Who was the most powerful among the three disciples?\n \n       ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Write the past tense forms of \n\n     a) choose  = --------------              b) fly = ------------------------------','','1) i) False              ii) True\n2) The most powerful among the three disciples was Sun Wukong.\n3) a) chose          b) flew','',0,0,0,NULL),(39874,567,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The sun is dressed up in ---------------\n     ii. The children are -------------------\nThe Sun, after his morning dip in the river \ndressed up in his golden muslin turban \nwaits and smiles on one side of the road, \nFor children are going to school.\nThrough the green-topped branches, \nBreezes sing their songs of blessings.\nFragrant Flowers awaken the sleepy roads \nwith their melodies. \nAt the corner of the lane, the Peepal in his lush deep foliage \nwaves his hands\n2) Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.','3) From the extract find the words that rhyme with\n     i. code                ii. pool\n','','1) i) his golden muslin turban\n    ii) going to school\n2) (1 – c), (2 – a)\n3) i) road             ii) school','',1,0,0,NULL),(39876,567,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The trees have ----------------\n     ii. The Peepal tree is at -------------\nThe Sun, after his morning dip in the river \ndressed up in his golden muslin turban \nwaits and smiles on one side of the road, \nFor children are going to school.\nThrough the green-topped branches, \nBreezes sing their songs of blessings.\nFragrant Flowers awaken the sleepy roads \nwith their melodies. \nAt the corner of the lane, the Peepal in his lush deep foliage \nwaves his hands\n2) Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.','3) From the extract find the words that rhyme with\n     i. power                 ii. cart\n','','1) i) green topped branches \n     ii) at the corner of the lane\n2) (1 – c), (2 – a)\n3) i) hour            ii) heart','',1,0,0,NULL),(39878,184,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Complete the following sentences.\n\n     i. The sun is dressed up in ------------------------------------\n\n     ii. The children are -----------------------------------------------\nThe Sun, after his morning dip in the river \ndressed up in his golden muslin turban \nwaits and smiles on one side of the road, \nFor children are going to school.\nThrough the green-topped branches, \nBreezes sing their songs of blessings.\nFragrant Flowers awaken the sleepy roads \nwith their melodies. \nAt the corner of the lane, the Peepal in his lush deep foliage \nwaves his hands\n2) Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.','\n3) From the extract find the words that rhyme with\n\n     i. code = ----------               ii. pool = ----------------\n','','1) i) his golden muslin turban\n    ii) going to school\n2) (1 – c), (2 – a)\n3) i) road             ii) school','',1,0,0,NULL),(39880,184,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Complete the following sentences.\n\n     i. The trees have ------------------------------\n\n     ii. The Peepal tree is at ------------------------------\nThe Sun, after his morning dip in the river \ndressed up in his golden muslin turban \nwaits and smiles on one side of the road, \nFor children are going to school.\nThrough the green-topped branches, \nBreezes sing their songs of blessings.\nFragrant Flowers awaken the sleepy roads \nwith their melodies. \nAt the corner of the lane, the Peepal in his lush deep foliage \nwaves his hands\n2) Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.','\n3) From the extract find the words that rhyme with\n\n     i. power = ---------                ii. cart = --------------------\n','','1) i) green topped branches \n     ii) at the corner of the lane\n2) (1 – c), (2 – a)\n3) i) hour            ii) heart','',1,0,0,NULL),(39882,568,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','We were used to teachers shouting at us for poor marks, shabby work, incomplete homework, not attending school regularly and all the other things that teachers worry about. But we didn\'t mind it. It made no difference to us. We all liked our class the way it was. We were, in fact, quite proud of our class.\n- And so was our new Class Teacher, Mrs Desai. She said so in the very first period. She said, \"I have heard about you people from your last year\'s teachers, and I am proud of you. Unity is a very important quality and you must always maintain it. I am very happy to be your Class Teacher.\"','1) Fill in the blanks with appropriate phrase given in the bracket.\n     [i) used to, ii) shout at, iii) to be proud of, iv) in fact]\n    a) I ------ my country.\n   b) Keep quiet Don\'t ---------- me.\n   c) The Punekars are --------- the heavy traffic. \n   d) -----------, it is the earth that moves round the sun.\n\n','2) Make the following sentences exclamatory.\n     i. Unity is a very important quality.\n     ii. I am very happy.\n3) When do your parents shout at you?','','1) a) iii             b) ii             c) i                  d) iv\n2) i) What an important quality unity is!\n    ii) How happy I am!\n3) My mother shouts at me when I am late to home. If I don\'t do my homework, my father shouts at me.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39883,568,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','However, there is one last thing which I must tell. We had hoped that we would all do well. Many of us did do well, but alas, there were still some who got poor marks, and some had even failed. So then we all began to feel crushed. Naina, who had got a red mark in her card couldn\'t bear it.\n\"I have let the whole class down,\" she sobbed.\nBut our dear dear Class Teacher went to her and hugged her.\n\"No, Naina, you haven\'t let anyone down. Look at your card. Last time you had got only two out of fifty in Science and this time, it\'s sixteen. That\'s quite a leap. You have started off in the right direction and I know you will keep it up. I am proud of you, each one of you.\"\nCan you guess what happened in the next test ?','1) Give reasons ---------\n     i. We all began to feel crushed because ----------------.\n     ii. Naina started crying because --------------------.\n\n','2) From the passage, find out the opposites of :\n      i) Succeeded                          ii) First\n      iii) Wrong                               iv) up\n3) Punctuate the following.\n     I have let the whole class down she sobbed.','','1) i) Some of us got poor marks and some had even failed.\n    ii) She had got a red mark in her card.\n2) i) failed           ii) last           iii) right         iv) down\n3) \"I have let the whole class down\" she sobbed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39884,568,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','However, there is one last thing which I must tell. We had hoped that we would all do well. Many of us did do well, but alas, there were still some who got poor marks, and some had even failed. So then we all began to feel crushed. Naina, who had got a red mark in her card couldn\'t bear it.\n\"I have let the whole class down,\" she sobbed.\nBut our dear dear Class Teacher went to her and hugged her.\n\"No, Naina, you haven\'t let anyone down. Look at your card. Last time you had got only two out of fifty in Science and this time, it\'s sixteen. That\'s quite a leap. You have started off in the right direction and I know you will keep it up. I am proud of you, each one of you.\"\nCan you guess what happened in the next test ?','1) Complete the following web :','2) Do as directed\n     i. Some had even failed (Make negative)\n     ii. Naina could\'nt bear it (Make Affirmative)\n3) Can you guess, what happened in the next test?','','1) i) You haven\'t let any one down. \n    ii) Last time you had got only two marks but this time you got fifteen. \n    iii) It is quite a progress \n    iv) I am sure that you will keep it\n2) i) Some had not even succeeded.\n    ii) Naina was unable to bear it.\n3) In the next test number of failures decreased. Naina made progress and she did not get red mark in any subject.','',1,0,0,NULL),(39886,185,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','We were used to teachers shouting at us for poor marks, shabby work, incomplete homework, not attending school regularly and all the other things that teachers worry about. But we didn\'t mind it. It made no difference to us. We all liked our class the way it was. We were, in fact, quite proud of our class.\n- And so was our new Class Teacher, Mrs Desai. She said so in the very first period. She said, \"I have heard about you people from your last year\'s teachers, and I am proud of you. Unity is a very important quality and you must always maintain it. I am very happy to be your Class Teacher.\"','1) Fill in the blanks with appropriate phrase given in the bracket.\n     [i) used to, ii) shout at, iii) to be proud of, iv) in fact]\n\n    a) I ---------------- my country.\n\n   b) Keep quiet Don\'t --------------------- me.\n\n   c) The Punekars are --------------------- the heavy traffic. \n\n   d) ----------------------, it is the earth that moves round the sun.\n','2) Make the following sentences exclamatory.\n     i. Unity is a very important quality.\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. I am very happy.\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) When do your parents shout at you?\n\n    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','1) a) iii             b) ii             c) i                  d) iv\n2) i) What an important quality unity is!\n    ii) How happy I am!\n3) My mother shouts at me when I am late to home. If I don\'t do my homework, my father shouts at me.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39887,185,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','However, there is one last thing which I must tell. We had hoped that we would all do well. Many of us did do well, but alas, there were still some who got poor marks, and some had even failed. So then we all began to feel crushed. Naina, who had got a red mark in her card couldn\'t bear it.\n\"I have let the whole class down,\" she sobbed.\nBut our dear dear Class Teacher went to her and hugged her.\n\"No, Naina, you haven\'t let anyone down. Look at your card. Last time you had got only two out of fifty in Science and this time, it\'s sixteen. That\'s quite a leap. You have started off in the right direction and I know you will keep it up. I am proud of you, each one of you.\"\nCan you guess what happened in the next test ?','1) Give reasons ---------\n     i. We all began to feel crushed because ----------------.\n     ii. Naina started crying because --------------------.\n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','2) From the passage, find out the opposites of :\n\n      i) Succeeded × ----------                         ii) First × --------------\n\n      iii) Wrong × ----------                              iv) up × ----------------\n3) Punctuate the following.\n     I have let the whole class down she sobbed.\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','1) i) Some of us got poor marks and some had even failed.\n    ii) She had got a red mark in her card.\n2) i) failed           ii) last           iii) right         iv) down\n3) \"I have let the whole class down\" she sobbed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39888,185,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','However, there is one last thing which I must tell. We had hoped that we would all do well. Many of us did do well, but alas, there were still some who got poor marks, and some had even failed. So then we all began to feel crushed. Naina, who had got a red mark in her card couldn\'t bear it.\n\"I have let the whole class down,\" she sobbed.\nBut our dear dear Class Teacher went to her and hugged her.\n\"No, Naina, you haven\'t let anyone down. Look at your card. Last time you had got only two out of fifty in Science and this time, it\'s sixteen. That\'s quite a leap. You have started off in the right direction and I know you will keep it up. I am proud of you, each one of you.\"\nCan you guess what happened in the next test ?','1) Complete the following web :','\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n2) Do as directed\n     i. Some had even failed (Make negative)\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. Naina could\'nt bear it (Make Affirmative)\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Can you guess, what happened in the next test?\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','1) i) You haven\'t let any one down. \n    ii) Last time you had got only two marks but this time you got fifteen. \n    iii) It is quite a progress \n    iv) I am sure that you will keep it\n2) i) Some had not even succeeded.\n    ii) Naina was unable to bear it.\n3) In the next test number of failures decreased. Naina made progress and she did not get red mark in any subject.','',1,0,0,NULL),(39889,604,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and answer the following questions \nCuckoo, cuckoo\nWhat do you do?\nIn April\nI open my bill;\nIn May \nI sing all day;\nIn June\nI change my tune;\nIn July\nAway I fly;\nIn August\nGo, go I must.\n1) Write the months in the poem.\n2) Write the names of months.\n3) Find the rhyming words in the poem.','','','1) April, May, June, July, August\n2) January, February, March, April, May, June, July, August, September, October, November, December\n3) April – bill, May – day, June – tune ','',0,0,0,NULL),(39890,604,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Write what does the cuckoo do in the given months. Complete the table','Cuckoo, cuckoo\nWhat do you do?\nIn April\nI open my bill;\nIn May \nI sing all day;\nIn June\nI change my tune;\nIn July\nAway I fly;\nIn August\nGo, go I must.\n2) Complete the series of months.\n     i. -----------, July, ---------------, September\n     ii. November, --------------, January, -----------------\n3) Write the names of months in Marathi.','','1) a) opens the bill                 b) sings all day\n     c) changes the tune           d) flies away\n2) i) June, July, August, September\n     ii) November, December, January, February\n3) जानेवारी, फेब्रुवारी, मार्च, एप्रिल, मे, जून, जुलै, ऑगस्ट, सप्टेंबर, ऑक्टोबर, नोव्हेंबर, डिसेंबर ','',1,0,0,NULL),(39891,493,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and answer the following questions \nCuckoo, cuckoo\nWhat do you do?\nIn April\nI open my bill;\nIn May \nI sing all day;\nIn June\nI change my tune;\nIn July\nAway I fly;\nIn August\nGo, go I must.\n1) Write the months in the poem.\n\n     ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Write the names of months.\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Find the rhyming words in the poem.\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','1) April, May, June, July, August\n2) January, February, March, April, May, June, July, August, September, October, November, December\n3) April – bill, May – day, June – tune ','',0,0,0,NULL),(39892,493,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1) Write what does the cuckoo do in the given months. Complete the table','\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCuckoo, cuckoo\nWhat do you do?\nIn April\nI open my bill;\nIn May \nI sing all day;\nIn June\nI change my tune;\nIn July\nAway I fly;\nIn August\nGo, go I must.\n2) Complete the series of months.\n\n     i. ----------------, July, ---------------, September\n\n     ii. November, ----------------------, January, -----------------\n3) Write the names of months in Marathi.\n\n       ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','1) a) opens the bill                 b) sings all day\n     c) changes the tune           d) flies away\n2) i) June, July, August, September\n     ii) November, December, January, February\n3) जानेवारी, फेब्रुवारी, मार्च, एप्रिल, मे, जून, जुलै, ऑगस्ट, सप्टेंबर, ऑक्टोबर, नोव्हेंबर, डिसेंबर ','',1,0,0,NULL),(39893,605,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and answer the following questions\nA little red hen lived on a farm with \na cat, a dog and a duck.\nOne day, she found a few \ngrains of wheat.\nThen she said, \"Who will eat the bread?\"\n\"Oh! I will,\" said the duck.\n\"Oh! I will,\" said the cat.\n\"Oh! I will,\" said the dog.\n\"Oh, no, you won\'t!\"\nsaid the Little Red Hen. \"I will.\"\nAnd she called her chicks and they all ate up the lovely loaf of bread.\n1) Where did a little red hen live?\n2) Who ate up the loaf of bread?\n3) Birds and animals mentioned in the story.\n4) Write the names of grains.\n5) Write past tense forms of the given verbs.\n      a) find                 b) cut','','','1) A little red hen lived on a farm.\n2) The little red hen and her chicks ate up the loaf of bread.\n3) hen, chicks, cat, dog, duck\n4) wheat, jawar, bajari, rice\n5)  a) found                b) cut','',0,0,0,NULL),(39895,605,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and answer the following questions.\n\"Who will sow the wheat?\" she said.\n\"Not I,\" said the duck.\n\"Not I,\" said the cat.\n\"Not I,\" said the dog.\n\"Very well then,\" said the Little Red Hen, \"I will.\" So she sowed the wheat.\nAfter some time, the wheat grew tall with ears full of grain.\n\"Who will cut the wheat?\" asked the Little Red Hen.\n\"Not I,\" said the duck.\n\"Not I,\" said the cat.\n\"Not I,\" said the dog.\n\"Very well then,\" said the Little Red Hen, \"I will.\" So she cut the wheat.\n\"Now,\" she said, \"who will thresh the wheat?\"\n\"Not I,\" said the duck.\n\"Not I,\" said the cat.\n\"Not I,\" said the dog.\n\"Very well then,\" said the Little Red Hen,\n\"I will.\" So she threshed the wheat.\n1) Actions done the by little red hen with wheat\n2) Match the pairs ','3) Write past tens form of the given verbs \n       a) grind                 b) eat\n4) List the food items made from wheat.','','1) sowed, threshed, cut\n2) (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – a)\n3) a) ground            b) ate\n4) paratha, puri, chapati, roti','',1,0,0,NULL),(39896,494,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and answer the following questions\nA little red hen lived on a farm with \na cat, a dog and a duck.\nOne day, she found a few \ngrains of wheat.\nThen she said, \"Who will eat the bread?\"\n\"Oh! I will,\" said the duck.\n\"Oh! I will,\" said the cat.\n\"Oh! I will,\" said the dog.\n\"Oh, no, you won\'t!\"\nsaid the Little Red Hen. \"I will.\"\nAnd she called her chicks and they all ate up the lovely loaf of bread.\n1) Where did a little red hen live?\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Who ate up the loaf of bread?\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Birds and animals mentioned in the story.\n\n      --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4) Write the names of grains.\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Write past tense forms of the given verbs.\n\n      a) find = -------------                b) cut = --------------------------','','','1) A little red hen lived on a farm.\n2) The little red hen and her chicks ate up the loaf of bread.\n3) hen, chicks, cat, dog, duck\n4) wheat, jawar, bajari, rice\n5)  a) found                b) cut','',0,0,0,NULL),(39897,494,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and answer the following questions.\n\"Who will sow the wheat?\" she said.\n\"Not I,\" said the duck.\n\"Not I,\" said the cat.\n\"Not I,\" said the dog.\n\"Very well then,\" said the Little Red Hen, \"I will.\" So she sowed the wheat.\nAfter some time, the wheat grew tall with ears full of grain.\n\"Who will cut the wheat?\" asked the Little Red Hen.\n\"Not I,\" said the duck.\n\"Not I,\" said the cat.\n\"Not I,\" said the dog.\n\"Very well then,\" said the Little Red Hen, \"I will.\" So she cut the wheat.\n\"Now,\" she said, \"who will thresh the wheat?\"\n\"Not I,\" said the duck.\n\"Not I,\" said the cat.\n\"Not I,\" said the dog.\n\"Very well then,\" said the Little Red Hen,\n\"I will.\" So she threshed the wheat.\n1) Actions done the by little red hen with wheat\n\n      --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Match the pairs ','\n3) Write past tens form of the given verbs \n\n       a) grind = ---------------                b) eat = --------------------------\n4) List the food items made from wheat.\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','1) sowed, threshed, cut\n2) (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – a)\n3) a) ground            b) ate\n4) paratha, puri, chapati, roti','',1,0,0,NULL),(39904,615,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say what’s wrong with these people. Write down your answer.','','','1) a stomach                2) a headache               \n3) cough and cold           4) fever','',1,0,0,NULL),(39905,615,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the pairs :','','','(1 – d), (2 – c), (3 – e), (4 – d), (5 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(39906,495,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say what’s wrong with these people. Write down your answer.','\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','1) a stomach                2) a headache               \n3) cough and cold           4) fever','',1,0,0,NULL),(39907,495,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the pairs :','\n','','(1 – d), (2 – c), (3 – e), (4 – d), (5 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(39908,616,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the story carefully and answer the questions.\n1) Who was Amna’s best friend?\n2) Who fell ill?\n3) Where was Aman working?\n4) When did Aman work in the shop?\n5) Where was the shop?','','','1) Ronak was Aman’s best friend.\n2) Aman fell ill.\n3) Aman was working in a shop.\n4) Aman worked in a shop in the evening.\n5) The shop was near Aman’s house.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39909,616,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the story carefully and answer the questions.\n1) What did shopkeeper say to Aman?\n2) Write true or false \n     i. Ronak was poor.\n     ii. Aman fell ill.\n     iii. Aman and Ronak were true friends.\n3) Find the names of characters from the story.','','','1) The shopkeeper said that if he did not come to work, he would give the job to some other boy.\n2) i) False           ii) True           iii) True\n3) Aman, Shopkeeper, Ronak, Munna','',0,0,0,NULL),(39910,616,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the story carefully and answer the following questions.\n1) How do you help your friend?\n2) Write opposite words.\n      a) many ×                         b) friend ×\n      c) same ×                          d) poor ×\n3) What is the moral of the story?','','','1) We study together. if we have any difficulties we discuss with each other. if my friend is absent in the school, I feel bad. I give my notebooks to complete the home work.\n2) a) few          b) enemy             c) different            d) rich\n3) A friend is need is a friend indeed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39911,616,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following using any suitable options from those given below.\n[know, watch TV, work here, want it, grow tall, fall ill, eat vegetables, jump high, get angry, have a sister]\n1) I don’t ------------                            2) She doesn’t ----------------\n3) He doesn’t ----------                        4) It doesn’t -----------','','','1) I don’t watch TV/know/jump high.\n2) She doesn’t work hard/have a sister.\n3) He doesn’t want it.\n4) It doesn’t grow tall/jump high.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39912,616,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following using any suitable options from those given below.\n[know, watch TV, work here, want it, grow tall, fall ill, eat vegetables, jump high, get angry, have a sister]\n1) We don’t -------------                  2) You don’t ----------------\n3) They don’t -----------                   4) I don’t ---------------','','','1) We don’t fall ill/want it.\n2) You don’t eat vegetables.\n3) They don’t get angry.\n4) I don’t know.','',0,0,0,NULL),(39913,616,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out 