 तुम्ही तुमच्या वृद्ध आजीआजोबांची काळजी घेतली पाहिजे.\n    ii) इच्छा असेल तर मार्ग दिसेल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40349,700,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n      i. know              ii. clap\nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. He promised me to look into the matter.\n     ii. Service to man is service to God.    ','','','A] i) माहित असणे               ii) टाळ्या वाजवणे\nB] i) या बाबीत लक्ष घालण्याचे त्यांनी मला आश्वासन दिले.\n     ii) जनसेवा हीच ईश्वरसेवा.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40350,700,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n      i. house              ii. happy\nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. We are looking forward to Diwali holidays.\n     ii. Shallow water makes much noise.','','','A] i) घर                ii) आनंदी असणे\nB] i) आम्ही दिवाळी सुट्टीची उत्कंठेने वाट पाहात आहोत.\n     ii) उथळ पाण्याला खळखळाट फार.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40351,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Choose the correct letter and complete the words :\n     [s, t, o, a, n, l, m, k]\n    i. _at             ii. s_ar              iii. re_d           iv. bana_a\n2) Write the opposite words of the following :\n     i. small          ii. ugly           iii. young              iv. take\n3) Write the opposite gender of the following words :\n     i. horse          ii. dog          iii. bull             iv. son \n4) Write the words in contracted forms :\n     i. am not            ii. is not            iii. are not           iv. have not\n5) Make a negative sentences\n     i. I am happy                ii. You are clever\n6) Write the rhyming words of :\n     i. wheat            ii. cat            iii. duck            iv. dog','','','i) mat           ii) star           iii) read              iv) banana\n2)  i) big           ii) beautiful          iii) old          iv) give\n3) i) mare         ii) bitch           iii) cow          iv) daughter\n4) i) amn’t         ii) isn’t            iii) aren’t           iv) haven’t \n5) i) I am not happy             ii) You are not clever\n6) i) seat            ii) mat           iii) luck             iv) fog','',0,0,0,NULL),(40352,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Choose the correct letter and complete the words :\n     [s, t, o, a, n, l, m, k]\n     i. penci_                ii. bas_et           iii. b_y               iv. _ky\n2) Write the opposite gender of the following words :\n     i. nephew           ii. husband          iii. uncle          iv. brother\n3) Write the opposite words of :\n     i. tall               ii. high            iii. happy            iv. black \n4) Make a negative sentences :\n     i. We are students               ii. She is a singer\n5) Underline the odd words :\n     i. Rose, Chama, Tiger, Marigold\n     ii. White, Blue, Black, Tree\n6) Write the rhyming words of :\n     i. bake           ii. hen            iii. red            iv. chick','','',') i)  pencil               ii) basket          iii) boy             iv) sky\n2) i) niece           ii) wife            iii) aunt             iv) sister\n3) i) short           ii) low           iii) sad               iv) white\n4) i) We are not students             ii) She is not a singer\n5) i) Tiger            ii) Tree\n6) i) cake             ii) men          iii) bed         iv) tick','',0,0,0,NULL),(40353,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the opposite gender of the following words :\n     i. father             ii. boy               iii. man            iv. King\n2) Write the opposite words of :\n     i. clean              ii. long            iii. hot               iv. thick\n3) Underline the odd words :\n     i. Fruits, Flower, Buds, Leaves, Eyes\n     ii. Apple, Cabbage, Carrot, Spinach\n4) Write the rhyming words of :\n     i. loaf                 ii. cut             iii. plains            iv. mail\n5) Write the plural forms of following words :\n     i. friend              ii. day            iii. college          iv. class\n6) Write the words formed by pair : \n     For example : bread and butter','','','i) mother           ii) girl           iii) women           iv) Queen\n2) i) dirty              ii) short          iii) cold               iv) thin\n3) i) Eyes             ii) Apple\n4) i) moaf            ii) hut            iii) trains            iv) fail\n5) i) friends            ii) days           iii) colleges            iv) classes\n6) i) boys and girls            ii) come and go         iii) cup and saucer       \n     iv) give and take          v) good and evil        vi) young and old             ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40354,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the opposite words of :\n     i. many           ii. friend           iii. same             iv. poor\n2) Write the plural forms of following words of :\n     i. house              ii. shop           iii. job             iv. boy\n3) Write the rhyming words of :\n     i. on                ii. rusing              iii. milk            iv. river\n4) Write the three words in which ‘C’ sounds as ‘S’\n     For example : cycle\n5) Write the nouns in alphabetical order :\n     Eye, fish, arm. Lamp, nose, hare\n6) Make negative sentences :\n     i. This is a tree                  ii. That is an orange.','','','i) few            ii) enemy           iii) different          iv) rich\n2) i) houses         ii) shops          iii) jobs                  iv) boys\n3) i) dawn           ii) gushing          iii) silk                iv) quiver\n4) circus, circle, city\n5) arm, eye, fish, hare, lamp, nose\n6) i) This is not a tree             ii) That is not an orange.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40355,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the opposite words of :\n     i. ill              ii. day               iii. come               iv. next\n2) Write the rhyming words of :\n     i. thought            ii. edge           iii. wood            iv. flown\n3) Separate the common nouns and the proper nouns in the following list :\n     India, house, city, book, Kalidas, lion\n4) Write the plurals of the following :\n     i. foot            ii. tooth              iii. cow             iv. lotus \n5) Make a negative sentences :\n     i. These are ships             ii. Those are stars\n6) Read the following sentences and underline the adjectives :\n     i. Once a rich person was scolding his lazy son.\n     ii. That’s good payment for this simple fare.','','','i) well           ii) night            iii) go             iv) previous \n2) i) fought        ii) wedge         iii) good          iv) glown \n3) Proper noun : India, Kalidas, lion\n     Common nouns : house, city, book\n4) i) feet              ii) teeth             iii) cows            iv) lotus \n5) i) These are not ships           ii) Those are not stars\n6) i) rich, lazy           ii) good, simple','',0,0,0,NULL),(40356,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write opposite words of :\n     i. Yesterday             ii. best            iii. work             iv. good\n2) Write the rhyming words of :\n    i. down            ii. play           iii. might              iv. well\n3) Separate the common nouns and the proper nouns in the following list :\n     Gangabai, boy, mother, Kedar, school, Dadar\n4) Write the plural forms of the following words :\n     i. dish              ii. branch             iii. lady            iv. patch\n5) Read the following sentences and underline the adjectives :\n     i. We are moving the invisible bench.\n     ii. The naughty jackal slipped away but the poor camel could not do so.\n6) Fill in the blanks with correct word given in the brackets :\n     i. I go to school --------- (happy/happily)\n     ii. It was a ---------- place. (quietly/quiet)      ','','','i) today              ii) worst            iii) rest           iv) bad\n2) i) town            ii) way            iii) right             iv) tell \n3) Proper nouns : Gangabai, Kedar, Dadar \n     Common nouns : boy, mother, school\n4) i) dishes        ii) branches         iii) ladies          iv) patches\n5) i) invisible          ii) naughty, poor\n6) i) happily             ii) quiet','',0,0,0,NULL),(40357,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write opposite words of : \n     i. right                ii. morning              iii. back           iv. true\n2) Write the rhyming words of :\n     i. you                 ii. one                  iii. should             iv. happy\n3) Write the nouns in alphabetical order :\n     Uncle, van, zoo, goat, pot, trains, wool\n4) Choose two available adjectives for each of the nouns given below :\n     i. sky                 ii. king\n5) Write the plural forms of the following words :\n      i. key           ii. bench       iii. lens           iv. orange\n6) Fill in the blanks choosing the correct word from the brackets :\n     i. Suresh painted the picture ---------. (nice, nicely)\n     ii. This is a ---------- place. (safe, safely) ','','','i) wrong/left         ii) evening            iii) front          iv) false\n2) i) few              ii) none                  iii) could                iv) tappy\n3) goat, pot, trains, uncle, van, wool, zoo\n4) i) clear, blue           ii) brave, kind\n5) i) keys       ii) benches         iii) lenses          iv) oranges\n6) i) nicely             ii) safe','',0,0,0,NULL),(40358,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write opposite words of :\n     i. With               ii. fail             iii. dusk             iv. load\n2) Write the rhyming words of :\n     i. high               ii. pass               iii. long            iv. did\n3) Choose two suitable adjectives for each of the nouns given below :\n      i. woman                 ii. bridge\n4) Write the plural forms of the following :\n      i. jeep           ii. story        iii. life            iv. loss\n5) Fill in the blanks choosing the correct word from the bracket :\n     i. They live ---------- in the village. (happy, happily)\n     ii. The children were ------------ (cheerful, cheerfully)\n6) Fill in the blanks using on or in :\n     i. Gopal is --------- the room.\n     ii. The monkey is sitting -------- the roof.','','','i) without             ii) pass            iii) dawn            iv) unload\n2) i) sky                    ii) grass           iii) song             iv) hid\n3) i) intelligent, simple             ii) tall, long\n4) i) jeeps           ii) stories           iii) lives          iv) losses\n5) i) happily             ii) cheerful\n6) i) in              ii) on','',0,0,0,NULL),(40359,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write opposite words of :\n     i. dawn               ii. brother             iii. fast               iv. wild\n2) Write the rhyming words of :\n       i. sand                ii. foam                iii. mill                iv. more\n3) Put a, an or the in the blanks :\n     i. ----- earth and ---- moon          ii. ----- iron box\n     iii. ------ mango and ------ oranges.\n4) Write the plural forms of the following :\n     i. tree            ii. flower         iii. name           iv. cup\n5) Fill in the blanks using the correct adverb forms of the words given in the bracket.\n     i. Somadatta offered them the gram and water -----------. (polite)\n     ii. Hercules was standing ------------- with a dead lion at his feet. (proud)\n6) Fill in the blanks using on or in :\n     i. The letters are ------- the postman’s hand.\n     ii. The mouse is sitting ------ the hole.','','','i) up            ii) sister            iii) slow            iv) domestic\n2) i) hand            ii) home           iii) chill            iv) pour\n3) i) the, the           ii) an          iii) a, an\n4) i) Trees       ii) flowers         iii) names        iv) cups\n5) i) politely               ii) proudly \n6) i) in            ii) in','',0,0,0,NULL),(40360,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the opposite words of :\n     i. high               ii. long              iii. strong             iv. old\n2) Write the rhyming words of :\n     i. stars           ii. sky              iii. shell            iv. shore\n3) Fill in the blanks correctly, using and, but, or, because whereas necessary :\n     i. Raman ate a lot -------------- he was hungry.\n     ii. There were many table ---------- chairs in the hall.\n4) Write the plural forms of the following words :\n      i. face            ii. match         iii. glass          iv. watch\n5) Fill in the blanks with correct word given in the brackets :\n     i. Rajani painted the picture -------. (nice/nicely)\n      ii. He is a --------- person. (slow/slowly)\n6) Fill in the blanks using the correct adverb forms of the words given in the brackets \n      i. He trims his nails ------------. (regular)\n      ii. The fox began to act as if he was ----------- scared. (real)','','','i) low               ii) short            iii) weak             iv) young\n2) i) cars            ii) by               iii) well            iv) core\n3) i) because              ii) and \n4) i) faces          ii) matches         iii) glasses       iv) watches\n5) i) nice           ii) slow\n6)  i) regularly                  ii) really','',0,0,0,NULL),(40361,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Arrange the following letters in proper order to make meaningful words.\n     i. iglth                  ii. ialt\n2) Correct the spelling of the following words.\n     i. goverment                   ii. shoud\n3) Find the past tense of the following :\n    i. tell               ii. look\n4) Correct the following sentences :\n     i. The sun rise to the east.\n     ii. Cows gives us milk.\n5) Write the rhyming words of the following :\n     i. hole               ii. long\n6) Name the type of sentences :\n    i. Are you ready to help?\n    ii. Wait till I catch you again.','','','i) light                ii) tail\n2) i) Government             ii) should\n3) i) told            ii) looked\n4) i) The sun rises to the east.\n    ii) Cows give us milk.\n5) i) coal            ii) song\n6) i) verbal question           ii) command','',0,0,0,NULL),(40362,701,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Arrange the following letters in proper order to make meaningful words.\n     i. yeht                ii. mite\n2) Correct the spelling of the following words.\n     i. peice                   ii. pleit \n3) Find the past tense of the following :\n    i. keep                ii. pass\n4) Write the rhyming words of the following :\n     i. ball                 ii. ring\n5) Name the type of sentences :\n     i. How foolish we are!\n     ii. Please prepare a simple food for me.\n6) Write the short forms of the following :\n     i. have not              ii. they are','','','i) they             ii) time\n2) i) piece             ii) plate\n3) i) kept              ii) passed\n4) i) all               ii) king\n5) i) exclamation           ii) command\n6) i) haven’t            ii) they’re ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40375,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on ‘My friend’ with the help of given questions.\n i. Who is your best friend?\n ii. How old is she?\n iii. What are her good qualities?\n iv. Write about her Favourite things?\n v. Does he/she help you in your study?\n vi. Do you like your friend? Why?','','','I have many friends but Neha is my best friend. She is 10 years old. We study together in the same class. She is very kind and helpful. Her favourite dish is pav-bhaji. She likes to read story books and playing games. She helps me in my study. We study together. I like my friend very much because she is very polite and always helps me in my difficult times.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40376,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the sentences and use proper pronoun and complete the sentences.\ni. Neha is a girl. ---------- is very clever. (He/she)\nii. This is a cow. ----------- is black. (It/she)\niii. I am a girl. --------- name is Asma. (My/Me)\niv. This is -------- classroom. (our/us)','','','i) she            ii) it            iii) my             iv) our','',0,0,0,NULL),(40377,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Choose the correct words given in the bracket and complete the essay :\n     [grains, loud, high, national, green, graceful, peahen]\n                                                   * The Peacock *\n     The Peacock is our ------------- bird. Its feathers are deep blue and -----------. It has wings but cannot fly ----------. The female bird is called as ----------. The peacock eats ----------- and worms. The peacock is famous for its ---------- dance and -------- cry.','','','The Peacock is our national bird. Its feathers are deep blue and green. It has wings but cannot fly high. The female bird is called as peahen. The peacock eats grains and worms. The peacock is famous for its graceful dance and loud cry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40378,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Choose the correct words given in the bracket and complete the essay :\n     [dead, clean, black, loud]\n                                                   * The crow *\n     The crow is a ----------- bird. Everyone hears its --------- sound caw – caw. The crow eats worms and ------- bodies of animals. It keeps surroundings ----------.','','','The crow is a black bird. Everyone hears its loud sound caw – caw. The crow eats worms and dead bodies of animals. It keeps surroundings clean.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40379,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make the meaningful sentences by the given diagram :','','','1) I always eat vegetables.\n2) I never tease animals.\n3) He always quarrels with my friends.\n4) She never wastes food.\n5) I always drink enough water.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40380,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the picture and answer the following questions :\n     Shubham is taking guavas to the market.\n','i. Who is grazing?\nii. What are in the basket?\niii. Where is the parrot?\niv. What colours are the tree?','','i) The buffalo is grazing                      ii) There are guavas in the basket\niii) The parrot is flying in the air.         iv) The trees are green.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(40381,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the picture and answer the following questions :\n     Champa is selling flowers at the bus stop.\n','i. Where are the people standing?\nii. Where are the flowers?\niii. What colour are the flowers?\niv. How many people are there?','','i) The people are standing on the bus – stop.\nii) The flowers are in the basket.\niii) The flowers are white.\niv) There are five people.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40382,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the picture and answer the following questions :\n     Sonu and Monu are busy watching TV\n','i. Where is the cat sitting?\nii. Where is the TV?\niii. How many animals are there?','','i) The cat is sitting to the left of sofa.\nii) The TV is infront of the sofa.\niii) There are two animals.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40383,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the essay by using the words in the brackets :\n       [35 years, simple, kitchen, Sidhu, school, dress, homework, ill, morning, drink]\n                                                       * My Mother *\nMy mother\'s name is ---------. She is ---------- old. She is very kind and ---------. She wears a simple ---------. She wakes up early in the ----------. She works in the ---------. She bathes me, dresses me and makes me ready to ----------.\nIn the evening she gives me milk to --------. Then she teaches me. She helps me in my ---------. She tells me stories. She looks after me when I am --------, So I like my mother very much.','','','                                                             My Mother\nMy mother\'s name is Sindhu. She is 35 years old. She is very kind and simple. She wears a smiple dress. She wakes up early in the morning. She works in the kitchen. She bathes me, dresses me and makes me ready to school.\nIn the evening she gives me milk to drink. Then she teaches me. She helps me in my homework. She tells me stories. She looks after me when I am ill, So I like my mother very much.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40384,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the essay by using the words in the brackets :\n        [most, faithful, barks, curved, black, rice, meat, house]\n                                                      * My Pet Animal*\nCows, bulls, dog, cats, sheep’s all these are pet animals. But I like dog -------.\nDog is a very --------- animal. He guards our ---------. He guards our field. He doesn\'t allow anyone to enter the house. He --------- loudly.\nA Dog eats bread and ---------. He drinks milk. He likes bones and ------ most. Dogs are black, brown and ----------. They have a curved tail.\nDog helps a man in his work. So, I like dog ------------.','','','My Pet Animal\nCows, bulls, dog, cats, sheep’s all these are pet animals. But I like dog most.\nDog is a very faithful animal. He guards our house. He guards our field. He doesn\'t allow anyone to enter the house. He barks loudly.\nA Dog eats bread and rice. He drinks milk. He likes bones and meat most. Dogs are black, brown and black. They have a curved tail.\nDog helps a man in his work. So, I like dog most.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40385,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the essay by using the words in the brackets :\n        [15 teachers, mathematics, 500, well, school, flowers, garden, much, Ghari]\n                                                  * My School*\nThe name of my school is Nagnath High school, ---------. It is just outside of the village. There are ---------- in my school. There are ---------- boys and girls in my school. The teachers teach us -----------.\nMr. Chandrakant Ghawate is the Headmaster of our ----------. He teaches ------------. But he is a very strict man.\nThere is a big playground in front of our school. We play many games there. There is a ----------- at the back of our school. It is full of ----------------.\nI love my school very ------------.','','','My School\nThe name of my school is Nagnath High school, Ghari. It is just outside of the village. There are 15 teachers in my school. There are 500 boys and girls in my school. The teachers teach us well.\nMr. Chandrakant Ghawate is the Headmaster of our school. He teaches Mathematics. But he is a very strict man.\nThere is a big playground in front of our school. We play many games there. There is a garden at the back of our school. It is full of flowers.\nI love my school very much.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40386,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a note to your friend and invite him/her on your birthday. Write the note with the help of the following words:\n[friends, birthday, little, to invite, to come, little, evening, sending, small, invited)\nDate : --------------------,\nDear ----------------------,\nI cannot come ---------- you personally. So, I am --------- this note with Raghukaka. My -------- is on 10th October. We have a -------- birthday party in the ------------- at about 7 p.m. at my home. I have ------------ only my close ---------------. I want you ----------. Please do come.\nPlease bring ------------ Pinki with you.\nYours,','','','Date : ---------------\nDear Sakshi,\nI cannot come to invite you personally. So, I am sending this note with Raghukaka. My birthday is on 10th October. We have a small birthday party in the evening at about 7 p.m. at my home. I have invited only my close friends. I want you to come. Please do come.\nPlease bring little Pinki with you.\nYours,\nReshma.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40387,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mohan\'s mother has gone to visit his sick aunty. Mohan comes from school. He gets his mother\'s note. Write the mother\'s note with the help of the following words:\n[home, little, kitchen, return, Mohan, forget, mummy, going]\nDate : -------------------------\nDear --------------,\nI am to ---------- Radhakaku. There is upma for you in the ----------. There are bananas, too. Look after --------- Sonu. Don’t ----------- to give her milk. Stay at --------- till your father comes. I shall return at night.\nYours,\n--------------','','','Dear Mohan,\nI am going to Radhakaku. There is upma for you in the kitchen. There are bananas, too. Look after little Sonu. Don\'t forget to give her milk. Stay at home till your father comes. I shall return at night.\nYours,\nMummy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40388,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Informal Letter\nYou have recently celebrated your birthday. Your uncle couldn\'t attend it. However, he sent you a nice wrist watch. Write a letter to him using the words given below:\n[Thank, nephew, birthday, useful, studies, wrist watch, come, preparing, disappointed, a lot, friends, great, over, beautiful]\nJeevandhara,\n3/304, Mangal Road,\nLatur-413 512.\n11th January, 2025.\nDear ----------,\nI was ---------- that you couldn’t be present here for my birthday. I missed you -----------. However, when I received the ----------, you sent with Kaki, I was thrilled. -------------- you very much. Kaka. I’ve always wanted one. Most of my --------- have watches, and now I, too, have one. Thanks to you! It will be very --------- to me.\nMy --------- went very well. All my friends had -----------. We had a --------- time. Our Unit Tests are ---------- and I am now ------------- for the annual examination. My ----------- are going on very well.\nThank you again for your ----------- gift.\nLove to all.\nYour loving, \nSumit.','','','Jeevandhara,\n3/304, Mangal Road,\nLatur-413 512.\n11th January, 2025.\nDear Suresh Kaka,\nI was disappointed that you couldn\'t be present here for my birthday. I missed you a lot. However, when I received the wrist watch, you sent with Kaki, I was thrilled. Thank you very much. Kaka. I\'ve always wanted one.\nMost of my friends have watches, and now I, too, have one. Thanks to you! It will be very useful to me.\nMy birthday went of very well. All my friends had come. We had a great time. Our Unit Tests are over and I am now preparing for the annual examination. My studies are going on very well.\nThank you again for your beautiful gift.\nLove to all.\nYour loving nephew,\nSumit.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40389,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Informal Letter\nYou need a mobile to attend online lectures. Write a letter to your father and ask him to buy a new mobile for the same. Promise him that you would use it very carefully. Use the following words and complete the letter:\n[mother, agreed, to conduct, use, renovation, assure, completing, Android, attend, Waiting, health, fine, lectures, everything]\nRadha Sheth,\n10, Dnyandeep,\nM. G. Road,\nJalgaon - 412 005\n15th August, 2024.\nDear ------------,\nOur school is undergoing some ---------- work. So, they have decided ------ classes online through Google Meet for this full month. Parents have ------- it in the parents meeting. The mobile which --------- has been using is not an android mobile so I can’t ----------- it for the same.\nSo requesting you to buy and send a new ----------- mobile so that I can attend the online lectures. I ------------------ you that I won’t misuse it at any cost and hand it over to mother after ---------- my lectures.\nHope ---------- is fine there. ----------- for the mobile before 1st of September so that I would be able to attend online ----------- from the first day. Everything is -------- here. Take care of your -------------.\nYours lovingly,\nRadha Sheth','','','Radha Sheth, \n10, Dnyandeep, \nM. G. Road, Jalgaon 412 005 \n15th August, 2024.\nDear father,\nOur school is undergoing some renovation work. So, they have decided to conduct classes online through Google Meet for this full month. Parents have agreed for it in the parents meeting. The mobile which mother has been using is not an android mobile so I can\'t use it for the same.\nSo, requesting you to buy and send a new Android mobile so that I can attend the online lectures. I assure you that I won\'t misuse it at any cost and hand it over to mother after completing my lectures.\nHope everything is fine there. Waiting for the mobile before 1st of September so that I would be able to attend online lectures from the first day. Everything is fine here. Take care of your health.\nYours lovingly,\nRadha Sheth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40390,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Formal Letters\nYou have been suffering from Typhoid for the last two days and doctor has advised you to take bed rest for 4 days. You are unable to attend the school for 4-5 days. Write a letter to the class teacher by using the following words:\n[school, leave, advised, recovery, infection, obedient, assure, Thanking, Typhoid, studying, illness, needful]\nAnish Shirke, \nShivneri, A2/69, \nTulja Bhavani Nagar, \nKharadi, \nPune-411 014. \n6th July, 2024.\nTo\nThe Class teacher, \nMahatma Gandhi High School, \nKharadi,\nPune - 411 014.\nSub. : About 5 days leave.\nRespected Madam,\nI am the student of your class --------- in Standard VII A. I have been suffering from ----------- from ----------- for the last two days. Our family doctor has --------- me to take complete bed rest. It is good for speedy -----------. So I am not able to attend the ---------- from 7th July to 11Th July.\nIt will also avoid the --------- to spread to the other students in my class. So, kindly sanction me 5 days -------- so that I can take complete bed rest. I ---------- that I will complete my studies once I recover from this -----------.\nKindly do the --------------. ------------------- you in anticipation. \nYours ---------- student,\nAnish Shirke.','','','Anish Shirke, \nShivneri, A2/69, \nTulja Bhavani Nagar, \nKharadi, \nPune - 411 014. \n6th July, 2024.\nTo\nThe Class teacher, \nMahatma Gandhi High School, \nKharadi, \nPune - 411 014.\nSub. : About 5 days leave\nRespected madam,\nI am the student of your class studying in Standard VII A. I have been suffering from Typhoid for the last two days. Our family doctor has advised me to take complete bed rest. It is good for speedy recovery. So I am not able to attend the class from 7th July to 11th July.\nIt will also avoid the infection to spread to the other students in my class. So kindly sanction me 5 days leave so that I can take complete bed rest. I assure you that I will complete my studies once I recover from this illness. Kindly do the needful. Thanking you in anticipation.\nYours obedient student,\nAnish Shirke.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40391,703,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Formal Letter\nYour school library has got good number of books from one of the NGOs. The Librarian has taken a lot of efforts for it. Write a letter to him/her by using the words given in the brackets:\n[books, reader, library, student, faithfully, efforts, Recently, Librarian, guide, teachers)\n\n-----------------------,\nA/24, Swapnapurti, \nV. P. Road, \nSawantwadi, \nSindhudurg - 416 510. \n26th August 2024.\nTo\nThe ----------, \nNew English High School, \nSawantwadi, \nSindhudurg - 416 510. \nSub. Thanks for the books in the -----------\nRespected Sir,\nI am a ----------- of New English High School, studying in Standard VII (A). I am a regular ----------- of different books. You always ----------- me to read different books. ---------------- our school library has got a good store of books from one of the NGOs from Mumbai. I know you have a lion’s share to get these ----------- for the library.\nNow students and ------------ will be able to read different books on different subjects. Thanks a lot for your ---------- to get these books.\nThank you.\nYours\nPratik Dalvi.','','','Pratik Dalvi, \nA/24, Swapnapurti,\nV. P. Road, \nSawantwadi, \nSindhudurg - 416 510. \n26th August, 2024.\nTo \nThe Librarian, \nNew English High School, \nSawantwadi, \nSindhudurg - 416 510.\nSub. : Thanks for the books in the Library.\nRespected Sir,\nI am the student of New English High School, studying in Standard VII (A). I am a regular reader of different books. You always guide me to read different books. Recently our library has got a good store of books from one of the NGOs from Mumbai. I know you have a lion\'s share to get these books for the library.\nNow students and teachers will be able to read different books on different subjects. Thanks a lot for your efforts to get these books for the students. Thank you.\nYours faithfully, \nPratik Dalvi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40409,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use the given phrases and make meaningful sentence : (any 3)\n     i. Cold water                             ii. Fresh air           \n     iii. Colourful flowers                iv. bright sun','','','     i) I never drink cold water.                ii) I like to walk in the fresh air.\n    iii) There are colourful flowers on the tree.\n    iv) We cannot see bright sun without goggles. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40410,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the sentences with am, is, are [any 3]\n     i. I ------------ a girl                ii. she ----- playing \n     iii. He ---------- clever             iv. The mangoes ---------- sweet.','','','i) I am a girl                ii)  She  is playing            \niii) He is clever            iv) The mangoes are sweet\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40411,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the sentences with am, is, are [any 3]\n     i. Rani ---------- clever                  ii. You ------------ happy\n     iii. They ------------ happy.            iv. It ------ a cage','','','i) Rani is clever                ii) You are happy          \niii) They are happy         iv) It is a cage\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40412,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following interview :\n     You : What’s your full name?\n    Friend : ------------------------\n  You    : Where do you live?\n  Friend : -------------------\n You : What is your favourite subject?\n Friend : --------------------','','','You : What’s your full name?\n    Friend My name is Mangal Mohan Rane\nYou    : Where do you live?\nFriend : I live in Pune.\nYou : What is your favourite subject?\nFriend : I like Marathi and English.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40413,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the pair \n    Write how animals help us.\n','','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(40414,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','See the advertisement and answer the following questions :','i. What is the picture?\nii. What is the show of?','','i) It is a ticket for a show.              ii) It is a show of dance festival.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40415,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','See the advertisement and answer the following questions :','i. What is an advertisement?\nii. What is the time duration of exhibition?\niii. How much discount offer?','','i) It is an advertisement of Vastra Emporium.\nii) The duration of exhibition is from 26th October to 8 November 2015\niii) 10 to 50% discount is offered.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40416,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame questions :\n     i. How do --------------?              ii. What does -------------?\n     iii. Why do ------------?  ','','','i) How do you go to school?                \nii) What does that mean?\niii) Why do you go there?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40417,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame questions :\n     i. How does --------------?                ii. What do --------------?\n     iii. Where do ------------?','','','i) How does the rain fall?              ii) What do you do?             iii) Where do you live?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40418,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Choose the correct words given in the bracket and complete the following conversations :\n       [fine, good morning, you]\nNeha : Hello Radha. ------------------\nRadha : ----------------, Neha. How are ---------?\nNeha : I am ----------. Thank ---------.','','','Neha : Hello Radha, good morning\nRadha : Good morning, Neha. How are you?\nNeha : I am fine. Thank you.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40419,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following conversations :\n     A : Do you like potatoes?\n     B : ----------------------------\n     A : What other vegetables do you like?\n     B : -----------------------------------------','','','A : Do you like potatoes?\n     B : Yes, I like.\n     A : What other vegetables do you like?\n     B : I like spinach.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40420,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following conversations :\n     A : Do you have a green pencil?\n     B: ----------------------------------\n     A : Can you give me it please?\n     B : ---------------------------------','','','    A : Do you have a green pencil?\n     B: Yes, I have.\n     A : Can you give me it please?\n     B : Yes, Sure.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40421,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare the meaningful dialogue from jumbled sentences.\n* I like to drink milk.\n* No, I don\'t.\n* Do you like to drink tea ?\n* What do you like to drink then?','','','* Do you like to drink tea?\n* No, I don\'t.\n* What do you like to drink then.\n* I like to drink milk.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40422,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare the meaningful dialogue from jumbled sentences.\n* We should wear mask and keep social distancing.\n* It is the result of our negligence.\n* How\'s that?\n* Corona is again spreading rapidly.','','','* Corona is again spreading rapidly.\n* It is the result of our negligence.\n* How is that?\n* We should wear masks and keep social distancing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40423,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare  the meaningful dialogue from jumbled sentences.\n* Don\'t mention it.\n* Thanks a lot.\n* With great pleasure. Please take it.\n* Will you please give me our pencil?  ','','','* Will you please give me your pencil?\n* With great pleasure. Please take it.\n* Thanks a lot.\n* Don\'t mention it.	\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40424,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following dialogue :\nA : What is your father?\nB : -----------------------------------------------\nA : And your mother ?\nB : ---------------------------------------------------','','','A : What is your father ?\nB : My father is a teacher.\nA : And your mother ?\nB : She is also a teacher.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40425,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following dialogue.\nKuldeep : What is your hobby?\nSumit : ---------------------------------------------\nKuldeep : What kind of books do you like to read?\nSumit : -----------------------------------------------','','','Kuldeep : What is your hobby? \nSumit : Reading is my hobby.\nKuldeep : What type of books do you like to read?\nSumit : I like adventurous stories to read.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40426,705,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following dialogue :\nShlok : What is social distancing, Mamma?\nAishwarya : ----------------------------------------\nShlok : But why should we keep social distance \nAishwarya : -----------------------------------------------','','','Shlok: What is social distancing, Mamma?\nAishwarya : keeping safe distance between two.\nShlok: But why should we keep social distance? \nAishwarya: Because Covid-19 is a highly infectious disease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40445,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A) Translate the following words in Marathi ---------- 1M\n      i. purposely           ii. peasants \nB) Translate the following sentences in Marathi --------- 2M\n      i. Our day starts with a prayer.\n      ii. We all liked her very much.','','','A] i) मुद्दामहून                ii) शेतकरी\nB] i) आमचा दिवस प्रार्थनेने सुरू होतो\n    ii) आम्हा सर्वांना ती फारच आवडते','',0,0,0,NULL),(40446,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi ------------- 1M\n     i. Good Morning                 ii. Best             \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. Wake – up early in the morning.\n     ii. Brush teeth everyday.','','','A] i) i) सुप्रभात         ii) सर्वोत्कृष्ट      \nB] i) दररोज लवकर उठा             ii) दररोज दात घासा.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40447,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi  ----------------- 1M\n           i. Hope                         ii. Fine\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi -------------- 2M\n      i. Take a bath everyday.\n      ii. Don’t wear dirty clothes. ','','','A] i) अपेक्षा           ii) चांगला\nB] i) दररोज नियमितपणे स्नान करा            ii) अस्वच्छ कपडे घालू नका.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40448,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n      i. Stand up                         ii. Sit down  \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. Keep your house and surrounding clean.\n     ii. Cleanliness is very important for healthy life.','','','A] i) उभे रहा          ii) खाली बसा          \nB] i) तुमचे घर व परिसर स्वच्छ ठेवा.\n     ii)  निरोगी जीवनाकरिता स्वच्छता फार महत्त्वाची आहे\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40449,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n             i. Commands                       ii. fast   \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. I don’t watch TV.\n     ii. She doesn’t work here.','','','A] i) आज्ञा                            ii) जलद गतीने    \nB] i) मी टीव्ही पाहत नाही.             ii) ती येथे काम करीत नाही.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40450,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n           i. Slowly                     ii. quickly           \nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. as sly as a fox\n      ii. as quiet as a mouse','','','A] i) सावकाशपणे            ii) वेगाने         \nB] i) कोल्ह्यासारखा लबाड              ii) उंदरासारखा स्थिर','',0,0,0,NULL),(40451,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n          i. Infront                            ii. Behind\nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. as brave as a lion\n     ii. as strong as ox','','','A] i) च्या समोर                                   ii) पाठीमागे\nB] i) सिंहासारखा शूर           ii) बैलासारखा मजबूत\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40452,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n     i. just now            ii. found              \nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. as free as a bird\n     ii. as busy as a bee','','','A] i) आत्ताच           ii) सापडले          \nB] i) पक्षासारखा मुक्त             ii) मधमाशी सारखा व्यस्त\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40453,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n               i. peanut          ii. cracked\nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. as slow as a snail.\n     ii. as proud as a peacock.','','','A] i) शेंगदाणा                                   ii) फोडली\nB] i) गोगलगायी सारखा मंद                ii) मोरासारखा गर्विष्ठ\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40454,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Write English words for given words  -------------- 1M\n      i. मैत्री            ii. दुकानदार          \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. Do exercise regularly.\n     ii. Do not eat junk food. ','','','A] i) friendship               ii) shopkeeper\nB] i) नियमित व्यायाम करा.          ii)  बाहेरचे पदार्थ खाऊ नका.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40455,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Write English words for given words  -------------- 1M\n                i. काम                               ii. वर्ग\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi -------------- 2M\n       i. Complete your homework in time.\n       ii. Help your mother in kitchen. ','','','A] i) job                   ii) class\nB] i) वेळेत गृहपाठ पूर्ण करा.         ii)  स्वयंपाकात आईला कामात मदत करा.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40456,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Write the given name of body parts into Marathi  -------------- 1M\n              i. Brain                        ii. eyes            \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. Speak politely with elders.\n     ii. Never tell a lie.','','','A] i) मेंदू             ii) डोळे            \nB] i) वडीलधाऱ्या व्यक्तींशी नम्रपणे बोलावे.          ii) खोटे बोलू नये.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40457,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Write the given name of body parts into Marathi  -------------- 1M\n              i. Nose                                    ii. bones\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ------------- 2M\n     i. Complete your work in time.\n     ii. Don’t watch T. V. \n','','','A] i) नाक                               ii) हाडे\nB] i) अभ्यास वेळेत पूर्ण करा            ii) टीव्ही पाहू नका\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40458,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n               i. laboratory                            ii. demand           \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ------------ 2M\n     i. Keep social distancing\n     ii. I am 14 years old.','','','A] i) प्रयोगशाळा                 ii) मागणी          \nB] i) सुरक्षित अंतर ठेवा.               ii) मी 14 वर्षाचा आहे.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40459,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n               i. museum                          ii. collect\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ------------ 2M\n     i. The sky is blue.\n     ii. Corona is a highly infectious disease.','','','A] i) संग्रहालय                                 ii) गोळा करणे\nB] i) आकाश निळे असते.             ii) कोरोना हा अत्यंत संपर्कजन्य रोग आहे.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40460,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n      i. stain             ii. college            \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ------------ 2M\n     i. Please switch on the light.\n     ii. It is 15 kms. away from here.','','','A] i) डाग           ii) महाविद्यालय           \nB] i) कृपया लाईट लावा. (दिवा लावा) \n     ii) ते इथून 15 किमी अंतरावर आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40461,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n                i. expert           ii. famous \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ------------ 2M\n     i. Cover your nose and mouth with mask.\n     ii. He left for Pune yesterday.','','','A] i) तज्ञ                                       ii) प्रसिद्ध\nB] i) तुमचे नाक व तोंडावर मास्क बांधा.\n    ii) काल तो पुण्याला गेला.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40462,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n       i. sing                ii. close\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ------------ 2M\n     i. My Father is a tailor.\n     ii. She is Raja’s Mother.','','','A] i) गाणे म्हणणे             ii) बंद करणे\nB] i) माझे वडील शिंपी आहेत.\n    ii) ती राजाची आई आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40463,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n      i. shadow                ii. bright\nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. You should look after your old grandparents.\n     ii. Where there is a will, there is a way.','','','A] i) सावली              ii) चमकणे\nB] i) तुम्ही तुमच्या वृद्ध आजीआजोबांची काळजी घेतली पाहिजे.\n    ii) इच्छा असेल तर मार्ग दिसेल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40464,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n      i. know              ii. clap\nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. He promised me to look into the matter.\n     ii. Service to man is service to God.    ','','','A] i) माहित असणे               ii) टाळ्या वाजवणे\nB] i) या बाबीत लक्ष घालण्याचे त्यांनी मला आश्वासन दिले.\n     ii) जनसेवा हीच ईश्वरसेवा.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40465,707,'descriptive','5',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi -------------- 1M\n      i. house              ii. happy\nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi -------------- 2M\n     i. We are looking forward to Diwali holidays.\n     ii. Shallow water makes much noise.','','','A] i) घर                ii) आनंदी असणे\nB] i) आम्ही दिवाळी सुट्टीची उत्कंठेने वाट पाहात आहोत.\n     ii) उथळ पाण्याला खळखळाट फार.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40612,709,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Be The Best \nIf you can’t be a pine on the top of the hill, \nBe a scrub in the valley – but be \nThe best little scrub by the side of the rill; \nBe a bush if you can’t be a tree. \nIf you can’t be a bush be a bit of the grass, \nAnd some highway happier make; \nIf you can\'t be a muskie then just be a bass – \nBut the liveliest bass in the lake!  <br>- Dauglas Malloch<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\ni. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)\n','','','i) Title : \'Be The Best\'\nii) The poem is written by Douglas Malloch\niii) The poem has four stanzas. Each stanza has four lines.\niv) The lines that rhyme in each stanza are 1 = 3; 2 = 4\nv) The poet compares big things with small things.\nvi) \'If you can\'t be ...... be........\' is a repeated ex pression in the poem.\nvii) The line I shall always remember is\' Be the best of whatever you are!\'','',0,0,0,NULL),(40613,709,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Trees are the Kindest\nTrees are the Kindest Things I Know\nTrees are the kindest things I know, \nThey do not harm, they simply grow.\nAnd spread a shade for sleepy cows, \nAnd gather birds among their boughs.\nThey give us fruit, in leaves above, \nAnd wood to make our houses of, \nAnd leaves to burn on Hallowe\'en, \nAnd in the Spring new buds of green. <br> - Harry Behn<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\ni. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)\n','','','i) Name of the poem: Trees are the kindest things I know.\nii) Name of the poet: Harry Behn.\niii) Rhyme scheme: aa, bb, cc, dd, ee, ff, gg, hh\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) Alliteration and spread a shade for sleepy cows.\n      b) Personification They hum a drowsy lullaby.\nv) Theme / central idea: Here in this poem the poet explains the usefulness of trees. Each and every part of trees is useful to human beings, animals and birds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40614,709,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Try Again\n\'Tis a lesson you should heed,\nTry again;\nIf at first you don\'t succeed,\nTry again;\nThen your courage should appear, \nFor if you will persevere, \nYou will conquer, never fear,\nTry again.\nOnce or twice, though you should fail,\nTry again;\nIf you would at last prevail,\nTry again; <br>- W.E. Hickson<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\ni. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)\n','','','i) Title of the poem: Try Again\nii) Name of the poet: W. E. Hickson\niii) Rhyme scheme: abab, cdcb, ebeb, fffb, gbgb, hhhb\niv) Figure of speech:\n      a) What should we do in that case? Interrogation\nv) Theme / Central idea: Here the poet tells us the secrets of success. Qualities like hard work, patience perseverance are ncessary to succeed. Even if we fail for the first attempt we should not lose heart. On the contrary we should try again.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40615,709,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">The Little River\nOur little river, it meanders along; \nIn summer, the water is only knee-deep, \nAnd cows and carts can cross it with ease, \nFor the banks, though high, are not too steep.\nNo sign of slime, the sands shine bright, \nOn one shore, kash fields blossom white. \nChirping mynahs through that site,\nThe jackal\'s howl is heard there at night. <br> - Rabindranath Tagore<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\ni. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)\n','','','i) Title of the poem: The Little River\nii) Name of the poet: Rabindranath Tagore\niii) Rhyme scheme: abcb, dddd\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) Girls and boys bathe close to the bank - Alliteration\n      b) As the muddy torrent swirls along onomatopoeia\nv) Theme: Here the poet describes two different pictures of the river in summer and in rainy season.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40616,709,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">WHY?\nI know a curious little boy \nWho is always asking, \"why?\"\nWhy this, why that, why then, why now, \nWhy not, why by-and-by?\nHe wants to know why wood should swim, \nWhy lead and marbles sink; \nWhy stars should shine and winds should blow, \nAnd why we eat and drink.\nHe wants to know what makes the clouds, \nAnd why they cross the sky; \nWhy sinks the sun behind the hills, \nAnd why the flowers die.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\ni. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)\n','','','i) Title of the poem: Why\nii) Name of the poet: Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme: aaba, cded, faga ………\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) why this, why that, why then, why now Repetition.\nv) Theme / central idea: The poem deals with a curious boy who wants to know the reasons behind some of the phenomena in the nature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40617,709,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Truth\nSticks and stones may break my bones, \nBut words can also hurt me. \nStones and sticks break only skin,\nWhile words are ghosts that haunt me.\nSlant and curved the fall word-swords \nTo pierce and stick inside me.\nBats and bricks may ache through bones.\nBut words can mortify me.\nPain from words has left its scar \nOn mind and heart that\'s tender \nCuts and bruises now have healed;\nIt\'s words that I remember. <br>- Barrie Wade<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\ni. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)\n','','','i) Title of the poem : Truth\nii) The poet of the poem: Barrie Wade\niii) Theme of the poem: Stones and sticks hurt us. Simi- unt larly the insulting words also hurt us. But the injuries of stones and sticks can be healed. But the word - injuries and, leave a permanent mark on our minds.\niv) Number of Stanzas : 3\n     Rhyme scheme: abcb, abab, efgf\nv) Figure of speech:\n     a) Alliteration : i) Stones and sticks break only skin.\n           ii) Sticks and stones may break my bones.\n     b) Repetition :  i) Sticks and stones\n                                ii) break my bones / skins\n      c) Metaphor  : Words are ghosts that haunt me. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40618,709,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">The Vet\no be a successful and competent vet, \nNeeds knowledge exceedingly wide, \nFor each of the patients he\'s likely to get \nPossesses a different inside. \nHe must know why the cat is refusing her milk,\nWhy the dog is not eating his bone,\nWhy the coat of the horse is not shining like silk,\nWhy the parrot does nothing but groan;\nWhy the ducks and the chickens are failing to lay,\nWhy so faint the canary bird sings, \nAnd if he is called to the zoo he must say \nAn incredible number of things.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\ni. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)\n','','','i) Title of the poem - The vet \nii) Name of the poet Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme abab, cdcd, efef\niv) Figure of speech : If the lion\'s caught a cold Hyperbole.\nv) Theme / central Idea :- This is a humourous poem. Here the poet says that a veterinarian needs exceedingly wide knowledge as his patients possess a different inside.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40619,709,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Golden Chain\nFriendship is a Golden chain, \nThe links our friends so dear, \nAnd like a rare and precious jewel \nIt\'s treasured more each year...\nIt\'s clasped together firmly \nWith a love that\'s deep and true, \nAnd it\'s rich with happy memories \nAnd fond recollections, too...\nTime can\'t destroy its beauty \nFor as long as memory lives, \nYears can\'t erase the pleasure \nThat the joy of friendship gives... <br>- Helen Steiner Rice<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\ni. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)\n','','','i) Title of the poem :- Golden chain\nii) Name of the poetess :- Helen Steiner Rice\niii) Rhyme scheme: abcb, defe, …….\niv) Figure of speech :- Friendship is a golden chain - Metaphor.\nv) Theme / central idea: Here, in this poem, the poet tells us the very distinctive features of Friendship. It binds two kindred hearts together for years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40621,67,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','If you can\'t be a pine on the top of the hill, \nBe a scrub in the valley but be \nThe best little scrub by the side of the rill; \nBe a bush if you can\'t be a tree.\nIf you can\'t be a bush be a bit of the grass, \nAnd some highway happier make; \nIf you can\'t be a muskie then just be a bass \nBut the liveliest bass in the lake!','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether the following statements are True or False \n       i. A bit of grass can make the highway happier.\n       ii. A scrub is found at the top of the hill.\n       iii. The liveliest bass is found in the valley.\n        iv. If we start from big, we would be the best.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following sentences with appropriate words/phrases from the stanza :\n        i. --------------, be a scrub.\n        ii.  If you can be a bass, -------------------.\n        iii. If you can\'t be a tree, ---------.\n         iv. --------------------, be a bass.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        What is the main difference between a pine tree and a scrub?\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ------------ 5M\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) True        ii) False        iii) False          iv) False\nA2] i) If you can\'t be a pine         ii) be the liveliest bass in the lake\n       iii) be a bush                            iv) If you are a muskie\nA3] A pine tree is tall whereas a scrub is a small short plant. \nB] i) Title : \'Be The Best\'\nii) The poem is written by Douglas Malloch\niii) The poem has four stanzas. Each stanza has four lines.\niv) The lines that rhyme in each stanza are 1 = 3; 2 = 4\nv) The poet compares big things with small things.\nvi) \'If you can\'t be ...... be........\' is a repeated ex pression in the poem.\nvii) The line I shall always remember is\' Be the best of whatever you are!\'','',0,0,0,NULL),(40622,67,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Complete the following sentences :\n      i. If you can\'t be a captain --------------\n      ii. ------------ be a star.\n      iii. Success or failure are not determined by -----------------\n      iv. Whatever we are, we should try to be -------------------\nWe can\'t all be captains, we\'ve got to be crew,\nThere\'s something for all of us here, \nThere\'s big work to do, and there\'s lesser, to do,\nAnd the task you must do is the near.\nIf you can\'t be a highway then just be a trail, \nIf you can\'t be the sun be a star; \nIt isn\'t by size that you win or you fail- \nBe the best of whatever you are!\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       What do you learn from the poem?\nA3] Poetic Device\n        Find out two pairs of opposite words from the poem.\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ------------ 5M\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1]  i) be crew       ii) If you can\'t be the sun        iii) by size       iv) the best\nA2] i) All works are important no big, no small.\n      ii) Secondly success and failure are not determined by the size. So whatever we do, we should try to be the best in it.\nA3] i) big × lesser               ii) win × fail\nB] i) Title : \'Be The Best\'\nii) The poem is written by Douglas Malloch\niii) The poem has four stanzas. Each stanza has four lines.\niv) The lines that rhyme in each stanza are 1 = 3; 2 = 4\nv) The poet compares big things with small things.\nvi) \'If you can\'t be ...... be........\' is a repeated ex pression in the poem.\nvii) The line I shall always remember is\' Be the best of whatever you are!\'','',0,0,0,NULL),(40623,67,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       State whether the following statements are True or False.\n       i. All must be the captains of the teams.\n       ii. We win or fail depending upon size or position.\n       iii. If your work is not important, don\'t do it.\n       iv. Whatever job you have, do your best.\nWe can\'t all be captains, we\'ve got to be crew,\nThere\'s something for all of us here, \nThere\'s big work to do, and there\'s lesser, to do,\nAnd the task you must do is the near.\nIf you can\'t be a highway then just be a trail, \nIf you can\'t be the sun be a star; \nIt isn\'t by size that you win or you fail- \nBe the best of whatever you are!\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        a) What do people like to see along the highway? \n        b) What is the message given at the end?\nA3] Poetic Device\n        Find and write the pairs of rhyming words from the stanzas.\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ------------ 5M\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1] i) False          ii) False           iii) False           iv) True\nA2] a) Along the highway people like to see narrow paths.\n       b) The message given at the end is Be the best of whatever you are!\nA3] i) crew – do            ii) here -  near      iii) trail – fail           iv) star – are \nB] i) Title : \'Be The Best\'\nii) The poem is written by Douglas Malloch\niii) The poem has four stanzas. Each stanza has four lines.\niv) The lines that rhyme in each stanza are 1 = 3; 2 = 4\nv) The poet compares big things with small things.\nvi) \'If you can\'t be ...... be........\' is a repeated ex pression in the poem.\nvii) The line I shall always remember is\' Be the best of whatever you are!\'','',0,0,0,NULL),(40624,67,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','If you can\'t be a pine on the top of the hill, \nBe a scrub in the valley but be \nThe best little scrub by the side of the rill; \nBe a bush if you can\'t be a tree.\nIf you can\'t be a bush be a bit of the grass, \nAnd some highway happier make; \nIf you can\'t be a muskie then just be a bass \nBut the liveliest bass in the lake!','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following web diagram.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Match the following :\n         <table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Vegetation/Animals</th>\n    <th>Spot</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. A pine</td>\n    <td>a) in the lakes</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. A scrub</td>\n    <td>b) by the highway</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Grass</td>\n    <td>c) on the top of the hill</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Bass</td>\n    <td>d) in the valley</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write down pairs of rhyming words from the extract.','','A1] Pine, bush, tree, grass\nA2] (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\nA3] hill – rill, be – tree, grass – bass, make – lake ','',1,0,0,NULL),(40625,67,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','If you can\'t be a pine on the top of the hill, \nBe a scrub in the valley but be \nThe best little scrub by the side of the rill; \nBe a bush if you can\'t be a tree.\nIf you can\'t be a bush be a bit of the grass, \nAnd some highway happier make; \nIf you can\'t be a muskie then just be a bass \nBut the liveliest bass in the lake!','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether the following statements are True or False \n       i. A bit of grass can make the highway happier.\n       ii. A scrub is found at the top of the hill.\n       iii. The liveliest bass is found in the valley.\n        iv. If we start from big, we would be the best.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following sentences with appropriate words/phrases from the stanza :\n        i. --------------, be a scrub.\n        ii.  If you can be a bass, -------------------.\n        iii. If you can\'t be a tree, ---------.\n         iv. --------------------, be a bass.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        What is the main difference between a pine tree and a scrub?','','A1] i) True        ii) False        iii) False          iv) False\nA2] i) If you can\'t be a pine         ii) be the liveliest bass in the lake\n       iii) be a bush                            iv) If you are a muskie\nA3] A pine tree is tall whereas a scrub is a small short plant. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40626,67,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','We can\'t all be captains, we\'ve got to be crew,\nThere\'s something for all of us here, \nThere\'s big work to do, and there\'s lesser, to do,\nAnd the task you must do is the near.\nIf you can\'t be a highway then just be a trail, \nIf you can\'t be the sun be a star; \nIt isn\'t by size that you win or you fail- \nBe the best of whatever you are!','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following sentences :\n      i. If you can\'t be a captain --------------\n      ii. ------------ be a star.\n      iii. Success or failure are not determined by -----------------\n      iv. Whatever we are, we should try to be -------------------','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       What do you learn from the poem?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        Find out two pairs of opposite words from the poem.','','A1]  i) be crew       ii) If you can\'t be the sun        iii) by size       iv) the best\nA2] i) All works are important no big, no small.\n      ii) Secondly success and failure are not determined by the size. So whatever we do, we should try to be the best in it.\nA3] i) big × lesser               ii) win × fail','',0,0,0,NULL),(40627,67,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','We can\'t all be captains, we\'ve got to be crew,\nThere\'s something for all of us here, \nThere\'s big work to do, and there\'s lesser, to do,\nAnd the task you must do is the near.\nIf you can\'t be a highway then just be a trail, \nIf you can\'t be the sun be a star; \nIt isn\'t by size that you win or you fail- \nBe the best of whatever you are!','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether the following statements are True or False.\n       i. All must be the captains of the teams.\n       ii. We win or fail depending upon size or position.\n       iii. If your work is not important, don\'t do it.\n       iv. Whatever job you have, do your best.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        a) What do people like to see along the highway? \n        b) What is the message given at the end?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        Find and write the pairs of rhyming words from the stanzas.','','A1] i) False          ii) False           iii) False           iv) True\nA2] a) Along the highway people like to see narrow paths.\n       b) The message given at the end is Be the best of whatever you are!\nA3] i) crew – do            ii) here -  near      iii) trail – fail           iv) star – are ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40628,68,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities.  \nA1] Simple Factual Question\n         Fill in the blanks. \n        i. Androcles was kind, gentle and ........... man. \n        ii. The ..............were forced to work for their masters. \n        iii. They had no .......... , no rights. \n        iv. He escaped from the ......of his cruel master. \nLong, long ago, there lived a kind, gentle and courteous man called Androcles. But he had a very cruel master. In those days, kings, nobles and others will be men used to poor people as their slaves and replace was a slave. The slaves were forced to work for the masters. They had to do what their masters told them. They had no freedom, no rights, even when they were unhappy with their master they could not leave him. They were bound by law to obey their masters, however wicked he might be. \nAndrocles did not like his heartless, wicked master. His master started his starved his slaves and often whipped them. Androcles felt that even death was better than serving such a master. He kept looking out for a good opportunity. At the first chance he got, he escaped from the clutches of his cruel master. \nNow, although, Androcles had thus run away, he was not coward. He took shelter in a forest. He was not afraid of the wild beasts that room the forest. He did not mind that he had no roof over his head. He was happy that he was free as a bird to go wherever he wished. \nA2] Complete the following web diagram','A3] Find the antonyms from the passage for: \n        i. birth                 ii. brave                iii. never           iv. happy \nA4] Grammar \n        i. He took a shelter in a forest. (Name the tense) \n        ii. His master starved his slaves. (Choose the correct sentence type for the given sentence) \n              a) assertive sentence                         b) imperative sentence \n              c) interrogative sentence                   d) exclamatory sentence \nA5] Open ended questions \n        Do you like to read stories? Name your favourite story.','','A1] i) courteous             ii) slaves               iii) freedom              iv) clutches \nA2] Forced to work for their masters, bound law to obey, could not leave their masters, had no freedom no rights \nA3] i) death             ii) coward               iii) often               iv) unhappy \nA4] i) Simple past tense              ii) Assertive sentence \nA5] Yes, I like to read stories. I read moral stories. My favourite story is \'Alyonushka.\'','',1,0,0,NULL),(40629,68,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities. \nA1) State whether the statements are true or false \n       i. Androcles moved carelessly towards lion. \n       ii. Androcles stretched his hands slowly. \n       iii. The lion lowered its head. \n       iv. Androcles came face to face worth tiger. \nOne day, as he was wondering in the forest. He suddenly came face to face with a lion. his first reaction was to turn and flee. but then he saw that the lion had not mood on seeing him. It neither gowned nor roared. in fact, it looked at him piteously as if it was in great pain. \nAndrocles paused. Then he moved very cautiously towards the lion, step by step, the lion moaned and whimpered.\" Please help me, \" it\'s eyes seemed to say. \nWhen Androcles dree near. The lion lowered its head and began to lick its paw. Then Androcles saw that the lion paw was wounded, sore, and Swollen.\nOn a closer look, Androcles noticed that a big thorn had pierced one of the lions toes. So this is the cause of the lines distress\' , thought Androcles. \nSummoning up of all the courage, Androcles stretched his hand slowly towards the injured paw. He was afraid that if the lion would attack him if he touched his swollen paw. But the lion seemed to understand his good intention. \nAndrocles took the paw in his left hand. Deftly he pulled out the thorn. Then tearing a piece of his own clothing the kind man bound of the lions injured power to his surprise and wonder the lion then lit his harm as if to show gratitude.\n Androcles remained with the lion till it could walk again you would hunt in the forest and share his food with the helpless beast everyday he would clean and dress the lions wound soon the lion was able to move about many times it follows wherever he went. \nA2] Complex factual question \n         Why was the lion in pain? \nA3] Write down \'ly\' words for the following. \n        i. sudden               ii. piteous             iii. cautious              iv. deft \nA4] Grammar \n        1) Androcles took the paw in his left hand.( Identify the tense) \n        2) He was wondering in the forest. (Add question tag) \nA5] Open ended question \n        Which is your favourite animal? Why? ','','','A1] i) True              ii) True               iii) True              iv) False \nA2] The lion was in pain because a big thorn had pierced one of its toes and that paw was wounded, sore and swollen. \nA3] i) suddenly                 ii) piteously                   iii) cautiously                  iv) deftly \nA4] i) Simple past tense                ii) wasn\'t he? \nA5] My favourite animal is dog. Because, dog is faithful animal. He protects our home from robbers. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40632,69,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Complete the following sentences.\n      i. Trees are the first to --------------\n      ii. Trees are the last to ----------------\nThey are the first when day\'s begun \nTo touch the beams of morning sun.\nThey are the last to hold the light, \nWhen evening changes into night. \nAnd when a moon floats on the sky \nThey hum a drowsy lullaby \nOf sleepy children long ago... \nTrees are the kindest things I know.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        a) Which part of the trees glow first at sunrise?\n        b) What do trees do for small kids?\nA3] Poetic Device\n       Pick from the poem words that rhyme with the following words :\n       i. Fun            ii. sight             iii. fly\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : -------------- 5M\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1] i) Trees are the first to touch the beams of morning sun.\n       ii) Trees are the last to hold the light of setting sun.\nA2] a) Green buds glow first at sunrise.\n      b) Trees hum a drowsy lullaby for small kids.\nA3] i) sun              ii) night             iii) sky\nB] i) Name of the poem: Trees are the kindest things I know.\nii) Name of the poet: Harry Behn.\niii) Rhyme scheme: aa, bb, cc, dd, ee, ff, gg, hh\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) Alliteration and spread a shade for sleepy cows.\n      b) Personification They hum a drowsy lullaby.\nv) Theme / central idea: Here in this poem the poet explains the usefulness of trees. Each and every part of trees is useful to human beings, animals and birds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40633,69,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Complete the following sentences : \n      i. Trees sing ---------- for small kids.\n      ii. Trees hold the light -------------------------.\nThey are the first when day\'s begun \nTo touch the beams of morning sun.\nThey are the last to hold the light, \nWhen evening changes into night. \nAnd when a moon floats on the sky \nThey hum a drowsy lullaby \nOf sleepy children long ago... \nTrees are the kindest things I know.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        Pick out lines that mean the following:\n       i. Kids love to listen to songs from trees ----------\n       ii. Trees are very kind.\nA3] Poetic Device\n       Find the examples of Personification from the extract.\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : -------------- 5M\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1] i) a lullaby\n       ii) when evening changes into night.\nA2] i) They hum a drowsy lullaby of sleepy children long ago.\n       ii) Trees are the kindest things, I know.\nA3] i) Holding light                ii) Humming lullaby\n       iii) Moon floats                iv) Touching the beams \nB] i) Name of the poem: Trees are the kindest things I know.\nii) Name of the poet: Harry Behn.\niii) Rhyme scheme: aa, bb, cc, dd, ee, ff, gg, hh\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) Alliteration and spread a shade for sleepy cows.\n      b) Personification They hum a drowsy lullaby.\nv) Theme / central idea: Here in this poem the poet explains the usefulness of trees. Each and every part of trees is useful to human beings, animals and birds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40634,69,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Trees are the kindest things I know, \nThey do not harm, they simply grow. \nAnd spread a shade for sleepy cows, \nAnd gather birds among their boughs. \nThey give us fruit, in leaves above, \nAnd wood to make our houses of, \nAnd leaves to burn on Hallowe\'en, \nAnd in the Spring new buds of green.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b> \n       Complete the following web diagram :','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Pick out lines that mean following :\n        i. Trees provide shelter for animals : --------------------\n        ii. Trees provide food for all : ------------------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        Pick from the extract words that rhyme with the following words :\n         i. no                  ii. now             iii. fun','','A1] shade, fruit, wood, leaves, buds\nA2] i) And spread a shade for sleepy cows.\n       ii) They give us fruit, in leaves above.\nA3] i) know               ii) cow             iii) burn','',1,0,0,NULL),(40635,69,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Trees are the kindest things I know, \nThey do not harm, they simply grow. \nAnd spread a shade for sleepy cows, \nAnd gather birds among their boughs. \nThey give us fruit, in leaves above, \nAnd wood to make our houses of, \nAnd leaves to burn on Hallowe\'en, \nAnd in the Spring new buds of green.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following web diagram.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Pick out lines that mean following :\n        i. Trees are most lovable : --------------------------\n         ii. Trees live a simple life : ----------------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n          Find words that describe the following\n         i. ----------- cows                  ii.  ---------- new buds','','A1] i) For cows : spread a shade\n       ii) For birds : give shelter among boughs\n       iii) For human being : give fruits, wood, leaves, buds\nA2] i) Trees are the kindest things, I know\n       ii) They do not harm, they simply grow\nA3] i) sleepy                 ii) green','',1,0,0,NULL),(40636,69,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','They are the first when day\'s begun \nTo touch the beams of morning sun.\nThey are the last to hold the light, \nWhen evening changes into night. \nAnd when a moon floats on the sky \nThey hum a drowsy lullaby \nOf sleepy children long ago... \nTrees are the kindest things I know.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following sentences.\n      i. Trees are the first to --------------\n      ii. Trees are the last to ----------------','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        a) Which part of the trees glow first at sunrise?\n        b) What do trees do for small kids?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Pick from the poem words that rhyme with the following words :\n       i. Fun            ii. sight             iii. fly','','A1] i) Trees are the first to touch the beams of morning sun.\n       ii) Trees are the last to hold the light of setting sun.\nA2] a) Green buds glow first at sunrise.\n      b) Trees hum a drowsy lullaby for small kids.\nA3] i) sun              ii) night             iii) sky','',0,0,0,NULL),(40637,69,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','They are the first when day\'s begun \nTo touch the beams of morning sun.\nThey are the last to hold the light, \nWhen evening changes into night. \nAnd when a moon floats on the sky \nThey hum a drowsy lullaby \nOf sleepy children long ago... \nTrees are the kindest things I know.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following sentences : \n      i. Trees sing ---------- for small kids.\n      ii. Trees hold the light -------------------------.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Pick out lines that mean the following:\n       i. Kids love to listen to songs from trees ----------\n       ii. Trees are very kind.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Find the examples of Personification from the extract.','','A1] i) a lullaby\n       ii) when evening changes into night.\nA2] i) They hum a drowsy lullaby of sleepy children long ago.\n       ii) Trees are the kindest things, I know.\nA3] i) Holding light                ii) Humming lullaby\n       iii) Moon floats                iv) Touching the beams ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40638,71,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','\"T is a lesson you should heed, \nTry again;\nIf at first you don\'t succeed, \nTry again; \nThen your courage should appear, \nFor if you will persevere, \nYou will conquer, never fear, \nTry again.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the lines with proper words from the extract:\n       i. If you don\'t ----------- in the beginning, you should try again.\n       ii. Show your ----------- to win without fear.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b> \n       What lesson should be kept always in mind?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write the rhyming words for the following from the extract :\n        i. succeed                    ii. fear\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ---------------- 5M\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) succeed            ii) courage\nA2] If at first you don\'t succeed, you should try again to get success. This lesson should be always kept in mind.\nA3] i) heed            ii) appear\nB] i) Title of the poem: Try Again\nii) Name of the poet: W. E. Hickson\niii) Rhyme scheme: abab, cdcb, ebeb, fffb, gbgb, hhhb\niv) Figure of speech:\n      a) What should we do in that case? Interrogation\nv) Theme / Central idea: Here the poet tells us the secrets of success. Qualities like hard work, patience perseverance are necessary to succeed. Even if we fail for the first attempt we should not lose heart. On the contrary we should try again.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40639,71,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','\"Tis a lesson you should heed, \nTry again;\nIf at first you don\'t succeed, \nTry again; \nThen your courage should appear, \nFor if you will persevere, \nYou will conquer, never fear, \nTry again.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Pick out lines that convey the following from the extract :\n       i. If you keep on trying, without a doubt, you shall win.\n       ii. When you fail in your first attempt, make another one.\n\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b> \n        How will you conquer the fear of failure?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write the antonyms from the extract :\n        i. courage x                    ii. stop/quit x \nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ---------------- 5M\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) For if you will persevere, you will conquer, never fear.\n       ii) If at first you don\'t succeed, Try again.\nA2] You will conquer the fear of failure by showing your great courage and with the help of your perseverance. Trying again without fear is the only way to face your failure.\nA3] i) fear              ii) persevere\nB] i) Title of the poem: Try Again\nii) Name of the poet: W. E. Hickson\niii) Rhyme scheme: abab, cdcb, ebeb, fffb, gbgb, hhhb\niv) Figure of speech:\n      a) What should we do in that case? Interrogation\nv) Theme / Central idea: Here the poet tells us the secrets of success. Qualities like hard work, patience perseverance are necessary to succeed. Even if we fail for the first attempt we should not lose heart. On the contrary we should try again.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40640,71,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Pick out the lines that convey the following from the extract : \n      i. It is not shameful to struggle hard.\n      ii. If you wish to get success at last, you should try again.\nOnce or twice, though you should fail, \nTry again;\nIf you would at last prevail, \nTry again;\nIf we strive \'tis no disgrace\nThough we do not win the race;\nWhat should we do in that case?\nTry again.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       When do we generally feel ashamed?\nA3] Poetic Device\n       Find the antonyms of the following from the passage.\n       i. success                 ii. lose\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ---------------- 5M\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1] i) If we strive \'tis no disgrace Though we do not win the race;.\n       ii) If you would at last prevail, Try again.\nA2] We generally feel ashamed when we fail to achieve success in the work we have undertaken. We also sometimes feel ashamed when we think that people will laugh at us for our failure.\nA3] i) fail                ii) win\nB] i) Title of the poem: Try Again\nii) Name of the poet: W. E. Hickson\niii) Rhyme scheme: abab, cdcb, ebeb, fffb, gbgb, hhhb\niv) Figure of speech:\n      a) What should we do in that case? Interrogation\nv) Theme / Central idea: Here the poet tells us the secrets of success. Qualities like hard work, patience perseverance are necessary to succeed. Even if we fail for the first attempt we should not lose heart. On the contrary we should try again.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40641,71,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n        Complete the lines with proper words from the extract\n       i. You will get your -------- at the right time.\n       ii. The important quality required when we are struggling for success is ---------.\nIf you find your task is hard, \nTry again; \nTime will bring you, your reward, \nTry again;\nAll that other folk can do,\nWhy, with patience, may not you? \nOnly keep this rule in view,\nTry again.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        What rules must be always kept in mind?\nA3] Poetic Device\n       Find the antonyms of the following from the passage.\n       i. punishment                 ii. impatience\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ---------------- 5M\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1] i) reward              ii) patience\nA2] When other people can get success with their patience, you also can do it likewise (in the same way). But don\'t forget to try again and again till you succeed. These are the rules that must be kept in mind.\nA3] i) reward                ii) patience\nB] i) Title of the poem: Try Again\nii) Name of the poet: W. E. Hickson\niii) Rhyme scheme: abab, cdcb, ebeb, fffb, gbgb, hhhb\niv) Figure of speech:\n      a) What should we do in that case? Interrogation\nv) Theme / Central idea: Here the poet tells us the secrets of success. Qualities like hard work, patience perseverance are necessary to succeed. Even if we fail for the first attempt we should not lose heart. On the contrary we should try again.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40642,71,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','\"Tis a lesson you should heed, \nTry again;\nIf at first you don\'t succeed, \nTry again; \nThen your courage should appear, \nFor if you will persevere, \nYou will conquer, never fear, \nTry again.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the lines with proper words from the extract:\n       i. If you don\'t ----------- in the beginning, you should try again.\n       ii. Show your ----------- to win without fear.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b> \n       What lesson should be kept always in mind?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write the rhyming words for the following from the extract :\n        i. succeed                    ii. fear','','A1] i) succeed            ii) courage\nA2] If at first you don\'t succeed, you should try again to get success. This lesson should be always kept in mind.\nA3] i) heed            ii) appear','',0,0,0,NULL),(40643,71,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','\"Tis a lesson you should heed, \nTry again;\nIf at first you don\'t succeed, \nTry again; \nThen your courage should appear, \nFor if you will persevere, \nYou will conquer, never fear, \nTry again.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Pick out lines that convey the following from the extract :\n       i. If you keep on trying, without a doubt, you shall win.\n       ii. When you fail in your first attempt, make another one.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b> \n        How will you conquer the fear of failure?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write the antonyms from the extract :\n        i. courage x                    ii. stop/quit x ','','A1] i) For if you will persevere, you will conquer, never fear.\n       ii) If at first you don\'t succeed, Try again.\nA2] You will conquer the fear of failure by showing your great courage and with the help of your perseverance. Trying again without fear is the only way to face your failure.\nA3] i) fear              ii) persevere','',0,0,0,NULL),(40644,71,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Once or twice, though you should fail, \nTry again;\nIf you would at last prevail, \nTry again;\nIf we strive \'tis no disgrace\nThough we do not win the race;\nWhat should we do in that case?\nTry again.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Pick out the lines that convey the following from the extract : \n      i. It is not shameful to struggle hard.\n      ii. If you wish to get success at last, you should try again.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       When do we generally feel ashamed?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Find the antonyms of the following from the passage.\n       i. success                 ii. lose','','A1] i) If we strive \'tis no disgrace Though we do not win the race;.\n       ii) If you would at last prevail, Try again.\nA2] We generally feel ashamed when we fail to achieve success in the work we have undertaken. We also sometimes feel ashamed when we think that people will laugh at us for our failure.\nA3] i) fail                ii) win','',0,0,0,NULL),(40645,71,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','If you find your task is hard, \nTry again; \nTime will bring you, your reward, \nTry again;\nAll that other folk can do,\nWhy, with patience, may not you? \nOnly keep this rule in view,\nTry again.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n        Complete the lines with proper words from the extract\n       i. You will get your -------- at the right time.\n       ii. The important quality required when we are struggling for success is ---------.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        What rules must be always kept in mind?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Find the antonyms of the following from the passage.\n       i. punishment                 ii. impatience','','A1] i) reward              ii) patience\nA2] When other people can get success with their patience, you also can do it likewise (in the same way). But don\'t forget to try again and again till you succeed. These are the rules that must be kept in mind.\nA3] i) reward                ii) patience','',0,0,0,NULL),(40646,73,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question \n       Name the following from the poem : \n     i. Two trees: --------------\n     ii. Two animals: ------------\nOur little river, it meanders along: \nIn summer, the water is only knee-deep, \nAnd cows and carts can cross it with ease, \nFor the banks, though high, are not too steep.\nNo sign of slime, the sands shine bright, \nOn one shore, kash fields blossom white. \nChirping mynahs through that site, \nThe jackal\'s howl is heard there at night. \nAcross lie groves of mango and palm; \nThe village priests dwell in their cool shade, \nGirls and boys bathe close to the bank, \nSplashing with their gamchhas as they wade.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       How do we know that the river is just knee deep?\nA3] Poetic Device\n       Pick from the extract words that describe sounds :\n       i. ...... of mynahs             ii.  jackal\'s -------------\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ----------------- 5M\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1] i) mango, palms            ii) Cows, jackals\nA2] The cows and carts can cross the river with ease. They can move and walk only in shallow water. This shows that the river is just knee-deep.\nA3] i) chirping            ii) howl\nB] i) Title of the poem: The Little River\nii) Name of the poet: Rabindranath Tagore\niii) Rhyme scheme: abcb, dddd\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) Girls and boys bathe close to the bank - Alliteration\n      b) As the muddy torrent swirls along onomatopoeia\nv) Theme: Here the poet describes two different pictures of the river in summer and in rainy season.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40647,73,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Complete the following activities. \n       i. Kash fields blossom white. (Where)\n       ii. The jackal\'s howl is heard there (When)\n       iii. The sands shine (How)\n       iv. Across the river lie (what)\nOur little river, it meanders along: \nIn summer, the water is only knee-deep, \nAnd cows and carts can cross it with ease, \nFor the banks, though high, are not too steep.\nNo sign of slime, the sands shine bright, \nOn one shore, kash fields blossom white. \nChirping mynahs through that site, \nThe jackal\'s howl is heard there at night. \nAcross lie groves of mango and palm; \nThe village priests dwell in their cool shade, \nGirls and boys bathe close to the bank, \nSplashing with their gamchhas as they wade.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       What does one see along the banks of the river?\nA3] Pick out words that describe the flow of the river-water.\n B] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ----------------- 5M\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1] i) on one shore of the river                  ii) at night\n        iii) bright                iv) groves of mango and palm.\nA2] One can see the sands shining brightly on the banks. Kash fields are blossomed with white flowers. Mynahs are chirping there and the jackals are howling at night. There are groves of mango and palm on the banks. The village priests live there in the cool shades of the groves. Girls and boys of the village are seen bathing and splashing water close to the bank.\nA3] meanders.\nB] i) Title of the poem: The Little River\nii) Name of the poet: Rabindranath Tagore\niii) Rhyme scheme: abcb, dddd\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) Girls and boys bathe close to the bank - Alliteration\n      b) As the muddy torrent swirls along onomatopoeia\nv) Theme: Here the poet describes two different pictures of the river in summer and in rainy season.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40648,73,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Name the following:\n      i. Two seasons : -------------------\n      ii. Two non-English words : ------------------ \n      iii. Two different times of the day : -----------------\nGirls and boys bathe close to the bank, \nSplashing with their gamchhas as they wade.\nAt dusk and dawn, once their bath is done, \nThey dip washcloths to trap small fish.\nTo their household tasks the wives return, \nHaving used river-sand to scour each dish.\nIn Ashadh, clouds gather, the waters rise; \nThe river\'s in spate, the current grows strong.\nThe air is rife with babbling sounds, \nAs the muddy torrent swirls along.\nThe woods onshore stir to life again, \nAnd our village wakens to celebrate the rain.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\nWhen do women return to do their household work? \nA3] Poetic Device\n       Pick from the extract words that describe sounds:\n      i. ----------- sounds                     ii. torrent -----------.\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ----------------- 5M\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1] i) Summer and rainy season            ii) Ashadh and gamchha\n       iii) Dusk and dawn.\nA2] The women return to do their household work after scrubbing their dishes with river-sand and washing them clean with river-water.\nA3] i) babbling                  ii) swirls\nB] i) Title of the poem: The Little River\nii) Name of the poet: Rabindranath Tagore\niii) Rhyme scheme: abcb, dddd\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) Girls and boys bathe close to the bank - Alliteration\n      b) As the muddy torrent swirls along onomatopoeia\nv) Theme: Here the poet describes two different pictures of the river in summer and in rainy season.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40649,73,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Complete the following activities.\n     i. They bathe close to the bank. (who)\n     ii. The wives use it to scour each dish. (what)\n     iii. The water rises in the river. (when)\n      iv. They dip wash clothes. (why)\nGirls and boys bathe close to the bank, \nSplashing with their gamchhas as they wade.\nAt dusk and dawn, once their bath is done, \nThey dip washcloths to trap small fish.\nTo their household tasks the wives return, \nHaving used river-sand to scour each dish.\nIn Ashadh, clouds gather, the waters rise; \nThe river\'s in spate, the current grows strong.\nThe air is rife with babbling sounds, \nAs the muddy torrent swirls along.\nThe woods onshore stir to life again, \nAnd our village wakens to celebrate the rain.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       How does the river change in \'Ashadh\'?\nA3] Poetic Device\n        Pick out words from the extract that describe the flow of the river-water.\nB] Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points : ----------------- 5M\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','','A1] i) Girls and boys              ii) river - sand\n      iii) in Ashadh                    iv) to trap small fish.\nA2] In Ashadh, black clouds of rain gather and it starts raining heavily. Waters in the river rise. The river is flooded and the river\'s current grows strong and forceful. The air is full of babbling sounds and the muddy torrent swirls along the river. Everything around the land becomes alive and people celebrate to welcome the rain.\nA3] i) strong current             ii) in spate         iii) muddy torrent.\nB] i) Title of the poem: The Little River\nii) Name of the poet: Rabindranath Tagore\niii) Rhyme scheme: abcb, dddd\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) Girls and boys bathe close to the bank - Alliteration\n      b) As the muddy torrent swirls along onomatopoeia\nv) Theme: Here the poet describes two different pictures of the river in summer and in rainy season.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40650,73,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Our little river, it meanders along: \nIn summer, the water is only knee-deep, \nAnd cows and carts can cross it with ease, \nFor the banks, though high, are not too steep.\nNo sign of slime, the sands shine bright, \nOn one shore, kash fields blossom white. \nChirping mynahs through that site, \nThe jackal\'s howl is heard there at night. \nAcross lie groves of mango and palm; \nThe village priests dwell in their cool shade, \nGirls and boys bathe close to the bank, \nSplashing with their gamchhas as they wade.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b> \n       Name the following from the poem : \n     i. Two trees: --------------\n     ii. Two animals: ------------','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       How do we know that the river is just knee deep?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Pick from the extract words that describe sounds :\n       i. ...... of mynahs             ii.  jackal\'s -------------','','A1] i) mango, palms            ii) Cows, jackals\nA2] The cows and carts can cross the river with ease. They can move and walk only in shallow water. This shows that the river is just knee-deep.\nA3] i) chirping            ii) howl','',0,0,0,NULL),(40651,73,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Our little river, it meanders along: \nIn summer, the water is only knee-deep, \nAnd cows and carts can cross it with ease, \nFor the banks, though high, are not too steep.\nNo sign of slime, the sands shine bright, \nOn one shore, kash fields blossom white. \nChirping mynahs through that site, \nThe jackal\'s howl is heard there at night. \nAcross lie groves of mango and palm; \nThe village priests dwell in their cool shade, \nGirls and boys bathe close to the bank, \nSplashing with their gamchhas as they wade.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following activities. \n       i. Kash fields blossom white. (Where)\n       ii. The jackal\'s howl is heard there (When)\n       iii. The sands shine (How)\n       iv. Across the river lie (what)','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       What does one see along the banks of the river?\n<b>A3] Pick out words that describe the flow of the river-water.</b>','','A1] i) on one shore of the river                  ii) at night\n        iii) bright                iv) groves of mango and palm.\nA2] One can see the sands shining brightly on the banks. Kash fields are blossomed with white flowers. Mynahs are chirping there and the jackals are howling at night. There are groves of mango and palm on the banks. The village priests live there in the cool shades of the groves. Girls and boys of the village are seen bathing and splashing water close to the bank.\nA3] meanders.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40652,73,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Girls and boys bathe close to the bank, \nSplashing with their gamchhas as they wade.\nAt dusk and dawn, once their bath is done, \nThey dip washcloths to trap small fish.\nTo their household tasks the wives return, \nHaving used river-sand to scour each dish.\nIn Ashadh, clouds gather, the waters rise; \nThe river\'s in spate, the current grows strong.\nThe air is rife with babbling sounds, \nAs the muddy torrent swirls along.\nThe woods onshore stir to life again, \nAnd our village wakens to celebrate the rain.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Name the following:\n      i. Two seasons : -------------------\n      ii. Two non-English words : ------------------ \n      iii. Two different times of the day : -----------------','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\nWhen do women return to do their household work? \n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Pick from the extract words that describe sounds:\n      i. ----------- sounds                     ii. torrent -----------.','','A1] i) Summer and rainy season            ii) Ashadh and gamchha\n       iii) Dusk and dawn.\nA2] The women return to do their household work after scrubbing their dishes with river-sand and washing them clean with river-water.\nA3] i) babbling                  ii) swirls','',0,0,0,NULL),(40653,73,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Girls and boys bathe close to the bank, \nSplashing with their gamchhas as they wade.\nAt dusk and dawn, once their bath is done, \nThey dip washcloths to trap small fish.\nTo their household tasks the wives return, \nHaving used river-sand to scour each dish.\nIn Ashadh, clouds gather, the waters rise; \nThe river\'s in spate, the current grows strong.\nThe air is rife with babbling sounds, \nAs the muddy torrent swirls along.\nThe woods onshore stir to life again, \nAnd our village wakens to celebrate the rain.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following activities.\n     i. They bathe close to the bank. (who)\n     ii. The wives use it to scour each dish. (what)\n     iii. The water rises in the river. (when)\n      iv. They dip wash clothes. (why)','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       How does the river change in \'Ashadh\'?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        Pick out words from the extract that describe the flow of the river-water.','','A1] i) Girls and boys              ii) river - sand\n      iii) in Ashadh                    iv) to trap small fish.\nA2] In Ashadh, black clouds of rain gather and it starts raining heavily. Waters in the river rise. The river is flooded and the river\'s current grows strong and forceful. The air is full of babbling sounds and the muddy torrent swirls along the river. Everything around the land becomes alive and people celebrate to welcome the rain.\nA3] i) strong current             ii) in spate         iii) muddy torrent.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40654,72,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question \n       Choose the correct alternative.\n     i. This is a story of a ----------------------\n         a) architect          b) engineer           c) mason        d) building contractor\n    ii. -----------  is a person who plans and designs\n       a) mason          b) architect         c) engineer          d) interior decorator\n    iii. The contractor recompensed the mason ---------------\n        a) attractively        b) slowly       c) subsequently       d) handsomely\n    v. The mason decided to retire when he became a -----------------\n        a) father           b) grandfather       c) great grandfather        d) uncle\nThis is a story of a mason, who worked for a building contractor. The mason always strove to do his work to perfection.\nAs soon as a house had been designed by the architect and details finalized by the engineer, the contractor would send for the mason and leave the responsibility of building the house to him. The mason would then, without wasting any time start his work. He would take great interest in his work of building and do it with full dedication. He made sure that he used the best construction material, of high quality, the measurements of the walls, doorways, arches etc. were perfect and he gave an excellent and appealing finish to whatever he built. The owners of the new houses built, would be more than satisfied with the construction.\nBecause of the zeal, diligence, loyalty and persistence that he displayed in his work, the mason was in great demand and fetched good business to his contractor. Subsequently the contractor, recompensed the mason, handsomely, with a very good salary. He could meet the needs of his family sufficiently, without any scarcity.\nThe mason thus continued working with great devotion for many years, till he became a grandfather. Then he decided that it was time to retire and spend time playing with his grandchildren. Being a firm- minded person, he informed his contractor of his decision to retire from house-building. The contractor was very disheartened to lose such an excellent workman and urged the mason to continue his job, but to no avail. The mason had made up his mind and refused to carry on.\nA2] Complex Factual Question \n       Complete the following web diagram','A3] Vocabulary\n       From the passage, find out the opposites of:\n       i. bad x             ii. worst x          iii. low x              iv. old x\nA4] Grammar\n       i. The contractor recompensed the mason handsomely.\n            (Frame a wh-question with How...?)\n      ii. He could meet the needs of his family sufficiently.\n            (Pick out the modal auxiliary verb)\nA5] Personal Response\n        Write four sentences about your house.','','A1] i) c) mason             ii) b) architect              iii) d) handsomely \n       iv) b) grandfather\nA2] loyalty, zeal for work, dedication, devotion, persistence, diligence\nA3] i) good           ii) best           iii) high           iv) new\nA4] i) How did the contractor recompense the mason?\n       ii) Could\nA5] My house is on the bank of a small stream. It is a simple cottage with mud walls and grass roof. We do not need fans, coolers and refrigerator there. It is the place where love and satisfaction dwell with us.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40655,72,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question \n       Complete the following web diagram.','This is a story of a mason, who worked for a building contractor. The mason always strove to do his work to perfection.\nAs soon as a house had been designed by the architect and details finalized by the engineer, the contractor would send for the mason and leave the responsibility of building the house to him. The mason would then, without wasting any time start his work. He would take great interest in his work of building and do it with full dedication. He made sure that he used the best construction material, of high quality, the measurements of the walls, doorways, arches etc. were perfect and he gave an excellent and appealing finish to whatever he built. The owners of the new houses built, would be more than satisfied with the construction.\nBecause of the zeal, diligence, loyalty and persistence that he displayed in his work, the mason was in great demand and fetched good business to his contractor. Subsequently the contractor, recompensed the mason, handsomely, with a very good salary. He could meet the needs of his family sufficiently, without any scarcity.\nThe mason thus continued working with great devotion for many years, till he became a grandfather. Then he decided that it was time to retire and spend time playing with his grandchildren. Being a firm- minded person, he informed his contractor of his decision to retire from house-building. The contractor was very disheartened to lose such an excellent workman and urged the mason to continue his job, but to no avail. The mason had made up his mind and refused to carry on.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        The mason made a firm decision to retire. WHEN?\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Find the noun forms of the following from the passage :\n        i. dedicate        ii. measure           iii. decide              iv. perfect\nA4] Grammar\n       i. As soon as the house had been designed, the contractor would send for the mason.\n            (Use \'No sooner...... than\')\n      ii. He would take great interest in his work.\n             (Use \' Used to)\nA5] Personal Response\n        Have you met any retired persons? What do they generally do after their retirement?','','A1] i) mason              ii) contractor             iii) architect        iv) engineer\nA2] The mason was working hard and sincerely for many years with the contractor. He had settled his family nicely and without any scarcity of any kind. When he became a grandfather, he thought that it was the right time for him to retire. He informed his contractor about his retirement and he was firm about his decision.\nA3] i) dedication            ii) measurement          iii) decision          iv) perfection\nA4] i) No sooner had the house been designed than the contractor would send for the mason.\n      ii) He used to take great interest in his work.\nA5] Yes, I meet some retired person in our building every day. They have formed a senior citizen\'s club. They come together in the morning and evening on our terrace or the space provided for them on the ground floor. They enjoy the activities that suit to their age and health. They also enjoy their family life happily.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40656,72,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n        Say whether the following sentences are true and false.\n       i. The contractor was a firm minded person.\n       ii. The mason agreed to build one last house willingly.\n       iii. The contractor urged the mason to continue his job.\n       iv. The mason wished to play with his grandchildren.\n Finally, the contractor convinced the mason to build one last house on a beautiful and convenient site, as a special favour. The mason agreed to do so with great reluctance, not too happily either.\nThe construction started. It was a most beautifully designed house to be built at a sought-after location. However, the mason could not put his mind and heart in his work. He did his work very roughly and carelessly, without interest or dedication. Even the building material used was of low quality; but he did not care. He simply wanted to complete the job in haste and finally retire.\nThe house was ready earlier than expected very roughly made structures, weak walls, poor finish and did not look as good at all, as designed. Yet the mason did not care. He informed the contractor that the house was ready and he was waiting to hand over its keys to him.\nThe contractor arrived at the site. He had a file of legal documents in his hand. The mason handed over the new house\'s keys to him. But the contractor did not step forward to enter the house, to see it. Instead, with a broad smile, he handed over the file along with the house-keys to the mason and said beaming happy tone, \"These are the ownership documents of this new house in your name. The house is yours. I had planned it as a gift for you.\"\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        Complete the following sentences.\n       i. The mason decided to retire when ---------------\n       ii. The contractor was disheartened to ----------------------\n       iii. The site of the last house was -----------------\n       iv. The mason had made up his mind to -----------------\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Match the words with their meanings.','A4] Grammar\n        i. The contractor was very disheartened.  (Make exclamatory)\n        ii. The mason agreed to do so. (Make negative)\nA5] Personal Response\n       Was the mason\'s decision to retire right or wrong? What do you think?','','A1] i) False         ii) False           iii) True           iv) True\nA2] i) he became a grandfather.              \n       ii) lose an excellent workman like the mason.\n       iii) beautiful and convenient.\n        iv) retire from house - building.  \nA3] (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\nA4] i) How disheartened the contractor was!\n      ii) The mason did not disagree to do so.\nA5] I think that the mason\'s decision to retire was right. The young will get an opportunity to work only when the old retire. Moreover it is good of the mason that he decided to spend time playing with his grandchildren.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40657,72,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       State whether the following statements are true or false :\n       i. The mason agreed happily to build one last house for the contractor.\n       ii. The mason just wanted to complete the last work in hurry.\n       iii. The contractor had given the new house to the mason as a gift.\n       iv. The mason put a lot of effort doing his construction work.\nFinally, the contractor convinced the mason to build one last house on a beautiful and convenient site, as a special favour. The mason agreed to do so with great reluctance, not too happily either.\nThe construction started. It was a most beautifully designed house to be built at a sought-after location. However, the mason could not put his mind and heart in his work. He did his work very roughly and carelessly, without interest or dedication. Even the building material used was of low quality; but he did not care. He simply wanted to complete the job in haste and finally retire.\nThe house was ready earlier than expected very roughly made structures, weak walls, poor finish and did not look as good at all, as designed. Yet the mason did not care. He informed the contractor that the house was ready and he was waiting to hand over its keys to him.\nThe contractor arrived at the site. He had a file of legal documents in his hand. The mason handed over the new house\'s keys to him. But the contractor did not step forward to enter the house, to see it. Instead, with a broad smile, he handed over the file along with the house-keys to the mason and said beaming happy tone, \"These are the ownership documents of this new house in your name. The house is yours. I had planned it as a gift for you.\"\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        What shock awaited the mason?\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Pick out from the passage Homophones of the following words :\n        i. won          ii. sight          iii. week           iv. would\nA4] Grammar\n        i. He did his work very roughly. (Change the voice. Begin with-His work...)\n        ii. Yet the mason did not care. \n            (Rewrite the sentence using \'Past Perfect Tense\'.)\nA5] Personal Response\n       Do you think that the mason was happy for the house gifted to him?','','','A1] i) False          ii) True         iii) True           iv) False\nA2] The mason was waiting for the contractor to hand over the keys to the house to him. He gave the keys to him but the contractor instead, handed over the legal documents and the keys of the new house to the mason. The contractor had given the new house to the mason as a gift for his good work done before. That was the great shock that awaited the mason. The new house with his bad workmanship now belonged to him as a gift from the contractor. You reap what you sow!\nA3] i) one             ii) site           iii) weak             iv) wood\nA4] i) His work had been done roughly.\n       ii) Yet the mason had not cared.\nA5] No. The mason was not happy for the house gifted by the contractor because the mason had built that house with the least care and without any interest. The construction was bad. He must have repented for his negligence. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40660,72,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n        Say whether the following sentences are true and false.\n       i. The contractor was a firm minded person.\n       ii. The mason agreed to build one last house willingly.\n       iii. The contractor urged the mason to continue his job.\n       iv. The mason wished to play with his grandchildren.\n Finally, the contractor convinced the mason to build one last house on a beautiful and convenient site, as a special favour. The mason agreed to do so with great reluctance, not too happily either.\nThe construction started. It was a most beautifully designed house to be built at a sought-after location. However, the mason could not put his mind and heart in his work. He did his work very roughly and carelessly, without interest or dedication. Even the building material used was of low quality; but he did not care. He simply wanted to complete the job in haste and finally retire.\nThe house was ready earlier than expected very roughly made structures, weak walls, poor finish and did not look as good at all, as designed. Yet the mason did not care. He informed the contractor that the house was ready and he was waiting to hand over its keys to him.\nThe contractor arrived at the site. He had a file of legal documents in his hand. The mason handed over the new house\'s keys to him. But the contractor did not step forward to enter the house, to see it. Instead, with a broad smile, he handed over the file along with the house-keys to the mason and said beaming happy tone, \"These are the ownership documents of this new house in your name. The house is yours. I had planned it as a gift for you.\"\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        Complete the following sentences.\n       i. The mason decided to retire when ---------------\n       ii. The contractor was disheartened to ----------------------\n       iii. The site of the last house was -----------------\n       iv. The mason had made up his mind to -----------------\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Match the words with their meanings.','A4] Grammar\n        i. The contractor was very disheartened.  (Make exclamatory)\n        ii. The mason agreed to do so. (Make negative)\nA5] Personal Response\n       Was the mason\'s decision to retire right or wrong? What do you think?','','A1] i) False         ii) False           iii) True           iv) True\nA2] i) he became a grandfather.              \n       ii) lose an excellent workman like the mason.\n       iii) beautiful and convenient.\n        iv) retire from house - building.  \nA3] (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\nA4] i) How disheartened the contractor was!\n      ii) The mason did not disagree to do so.\nA5] I think that the mason\'s decision to retire was right. The young will get an opportunity to work only when the old retire. Moreover it is good of the mason that he decided to spend time playing with his grandchildren.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40661,72,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       State whether the following statements are true or false :\n       i. The mason agreed happily to build one last house for the contractor.\n       ii. The mason just wanted to complete the last work in hurry.\n       iii. The contractor had given the new house to the mason as a gift.\n       iv. The mason put a lot of effort doing his construction work.\nFinally, the contractor convinced the mason to build one last house on a beautiful and convenient site, as a special favour. The mason agreed to do so with great reluctance, not too happily either.\nThe construction started. It was a most beautifully designed house to be built at a sought-after location. However, the mason could not put his mind and heart in his work. He did his work very roughly and carelessly, without interest or dedication. Even the building material used was of low quality; but he did not care. He simply wanted to complete the job in haste and finally retire.\nThe house was ready earlier than expected very roughly made structures, weak walls, poor finish and did not look as good at all, as designed. Yet the mason did not care. He informed the contractor that the house was ready and he was waiting to hand over its keys to him.\nThe contractor arrived at the site. He had a file of legal documents in his hand. The mason handed over the new house\'s keys to him. But the contractor did not step forward to enter the house, to see it. Instead, with a broad smile, he handed over the file along with the house-keys to the mason and said beaming happy tone, \"These are the ownership documents of this new house in your name. The house is yours. I had planned it as a gift for you.\"\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        What shock awaited the mason?\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Pick out from the passage Homophones of the following words :\n        i. won          ii. sight          iii. week           iv. would\nA4] Grammar\n        i. He did his work very roughly. (Change the voice. Begin with-His work...)\n        ii. Yet the mason did not care. \n            (Rewrite the sentence using \'Past Perfect Tense\'.)\nA5] Personal Response\n       Do you think that the mason was happy for the house gifted to him?','','','A1] i) False          ii) True         iii) True           iv) False\nA2] The mason was waiting for the contractor to hand over the keys to the house to him. He gave the keys to him but the contractor instead, handed over the legal documents and the keys of the new house to the mason. The contractor had given the new house to the mason as a gift for his good work done before. That was the great shock that awaited the mason. The new house with his bad workmanship now belonged to him as a gift from the contractor. You reap what you sow!\nA3] i) one             ii) site           iii) weak             iv) wood\nA4] i) His work had been done roughly.\n       ii) Yet the mason had not cared.\nA5] No. The mason was not happy for the house gifted by the contractor because the mason had built that house with the least care and without any interest. The construction was bad. He must have repented for his negligence. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40662,74,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n     i. Ritesh heard a scream from a narrow lane.\n     ii. Ritesh took the man to Dr. Jadhav\n     iii. Ritesh ran after the thieves.\n     iv. The man near the pavement also ran with the thief.\nRitesh : May I come in, Madam?\nMrs Sen: Now? Don\'t you think you are early?\nRitesh : Pardon, Ma\'am?\nMrs Sen: I mean for the second period. You are too early for the second period.\nRitesh : I am so sorry! Ma\'am, I\'m really sorry. I started well in time from home, you won\'t believe what happened.\nMrs Sen: Hmm, We\'ll see. Let\'s hear the story first. What happened? Nothing short of an earthquake, I \'m sure.\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, really, Ma\'am. Honestly, Ma\'am.\nMrs Sen: Go ahead. Go ahead.\nRitesh : I was walking to school as usual, I was half way down the narrow lane that leads to the market. Suddenly, I heard a scream from the main road.\nMrs Sen: And of course, you had to investigate!\nRitesh : Yes, Madam, I had to find out if someone needed help. And sure enough, there was this person lying near the pavement. he was groaning with pain.\nMrs Sen: So, you took him to Dr Jadhav.\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, I didn\'t get the chance. Just as I reached that man, another man came running out of the shop there, with a packet in his hand. And the shopkeeper also ran out shouting, \"Chor! Chor! catch him!\"\nMrs Sen: Amazing! Quite a handful for you to deal with and all at once! What did you do then?\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am, but the most amazing thing happened then. That man near the pavement- he, too, got up and began to run with the thief. Without losing a moment, I ran after them.\nMrs Sen: And then you caught them and took them to the police station !\nRitesh : We couldn\'t do that, Ma\'am. They just vanished around the corner. All this happened so fast.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        Complete the following sentences.\n        i. The person was lying ---------------------\n        ii. Another man had ------------ in the hand.\n        iii. The most amazing thing was that -------------------\n        iv. The narrow lane leads to ----------------.\nA3] Vocabulary\n       From the passage, find out the opposites of:\n       i) broad x              ii) far x          iii) before x         iv) up x\nA4] Grammar\n       i. I was walking to school as usual. (Name the tense)\n       ii. As soon as I was half way down the narrow lane, I heard a scream \n             (Use \'No sooner - than\')\nA5] Personal Response\n       Do you believe Ritesh\'s stories ? Why?','','','A1] i) True            ii) False              iii) True             iv) True\nA2] i) near the pavement.                     ii) a packet\n      iii) the man near the pavement also ran with the thief.\n      iv) the market.\nA3] i) narrow         ii) near         iii) after         iv) down \nA4] i) Continuous past tense. \n       ii) No sooner was I half way down the narrow lane than I heard a scream.\nA5] No, I don\'t believe Ritesh\'s stories. Ritesh heard a scream from the main road man lying near the pavement-groaning with pain and the same man running after the thief all these are unbelievable.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40663,74,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question \n       Who said to whom? \n      i. \"Nothing short of an earthquake.\"\n      ii. \"I had to find out if someone needed help.\"\n      iii. \"They just vanished around the corner.\"\n      iv. \"Chor! Chor! catch him.\"\nRitesh : May I come in, Madam?\nMrs Sen: Now? Don\'t you think you are early?\nRitesh : Pardon, Ma\'am?\nMrs Sen: I mean for the second period. You are too early for the second period.\nRitesh : I am so sorry! Ma\'am, I\'m really sorry. I started well in time from home, you won\'t believe what happened.\nMrs Sen: Hmm, We\'ll see. Let\'s hear the story first. What happened? Nothing short of an earthquake, I \'m sure.\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, really, Ma\'am. Honestly, Ma\'am.\nMrs Sen: Go ahead. Go ahead.\nRitesh : I was walking to school as usual, I was half way down the narrow lane that leads to the market. Suddenly, I heard a scream from the main road.\nMrs Sen: And of course, you had to investigate!\nRitesh : Yes, Madam, I had to find out if someone needed help. And sure enough, there was this person lying near the pavement. he was groaning with pain.\nMrs Sen: So, you took him to Dr Jadhav.\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, I didn\'t get the chance. Just as I reached that man, another man came running out of the shop there, with a packet in his hand. And the shopkeeper also ran out shouting, \"Chor! Chor! catch him!\"\nMrs Sen: Amazing! Quite a handful for you to deal with and all at once! What did you do then?\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am, but the most amazing thing happened then. That man near the pavement- he, too, got up and began to run with the thief. Without losing a moment, I ran after them.\nMrs Sen: And then you caught them and took them to the police station !\nRitesh : We couldn\'t do that, Ma\'am. They just vanished around the corner. All this happened so fast.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       Rearrange the order of the following events as per the narrator :\n       i. The groaning man joined the thief and ran away.\n       ii. Ritesh heard a scream from the main road.\n       iii. The thief came out of the shop.\n       iv. A man, groaning with pain was lying near the pavement.\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Complete inserting the correct following words the skit :\n       i. The police arrived to ----------- the crime.\n       ii. The injured child kept ----------- with pain.\nA4] Grammar\n        Report the following in Indirect Speech :\n       i. Ritesh: May I come in, Madam?\n       ii. Mrs Sen: Ritesh, you are too early for the second period.\nA5] Personal Response\n       What really are the students\' reasons of coming late to school? ','','','A1] i) Teacher said to Ritesh         ii) Ritesh said to teacher\n      iii) Ritesh said to teacher        iv) The shopkeeper said to people.\nA2] ii, iv, iii, i\nA3] i) deal with                ii) groaning\nA4] i) Ritesh requested the teacher if he could come in.\n       ii) Mrs Sen told Ritesh that he was too early for the second period.\nA5] They do not keep themselves thoroughly ready for coming to school. They start late from home for coming to school. They waste time on the way unnecessarily.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40664,74,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Complete the following sentences with the help of the passage:\n      i. Ritesh went back to the shop to -------------------.\n      ii. The dog finally left the school bag when Ritesh --------------.\n      iii. Ritesh left the dog only when he found ------------.\n      iv. Ritesh was good at ----------------. \nMrs Sen : Then why are you late?\nRitesh : That\'s not all. I realized that I had left my bag at the shop in the mad rush. So I ran back to get it.\nMrs Sen: And it wasn\'t there!\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, it was there all right, but there was a huge, hairy, brown dog sitting on it. I didn\'t know what to do.\nMrs Sen: Interesting! A dog on a bag in a shop!\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am. Even the shopkeeper was surprised. He didn\'t know where the dog had come from. How that dog growled ! We tried all kinds of things to make it move whistling and clapping, and showing him the stick but he wouldn\'t budge, and I couldn\'t leave without my school-bag.\nMrs Sen: But I see that you got your bag finally.\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am. Finally the shopkeeper gave me some biscuits. I offered the biscuits to the dog and lured him away from the bag.\nMrs Sen: OK. So let\'s say that accounts for fifteen minutes.\nRitesh : But there\'s more, Ma\'am. The poor dog began to follow me to school. It looked so poor and lonely and small...\nMrs Sen: Small? Did you say small?\nRitesh : I mean young, Ma\'am. It was big in size but it was only a puppy, really. And it looked lost. Maybe it was looking for its mother or owner and I thought it was asking for my help. So I began a enquire with the other shopkeepers and passers by, but nobody could tell and couldn\'t leave it behind.\nMrs Sen: And then you found the owner? Who was it?\nRitesh : That\'s the funny part, Ma\'am. The puppy itself found the owner. It saw someone across the road and just bounded towards the person. I was so relieved. Then I turned and ran straight to school.\nMrs Sen: Good boy! Now that your good deeds are done, there\'s something else you can do. There\'s a sto- ry-writing competition coming up. Would you like to send an entry?\nRitesh : Who Ma\'am? Me, Ma\'am? Now..... I wouldn\'t know what to write. I\'m not good at making up stories.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n     Rearrange the order of the following events as per the narrator :\n     i. The dog began to follow Ritesh to school.\n     ii. A huge dog was sitting on Ritesh\'s school-bag.\n     iii. The dog refused to move.\n      iv. The dog itself found its owner.\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Complete the following by inserting the correct words from the skit:\n       i. The heavy load would not ------------.\n       ii. My friend ---------- me to eat chocolates in the classroom.\nA4] Grammar\n        Report the following in Indirect speech:\n       i. Ritesh: I had left my bag at the shop.\n       ii. Mrs. Sen: There\'s a story-writing competition.\nA5] Personal Response\n       Why, do you think, did Ritesh refuse to take part in the story-writing competition?','','','A1] i) get back his bag.                 ii) offered him biscuits\n       iii) its owner                            iv) making up stories\nA2] ii, iv, i, iii\nA3] i) move                 ii) offered \nA4] i) Ritesh said that he had left his bag at the shop.\n       ii) Mrs. Sen said that there was a story- writing competition.\nA5] I think, if he had agreed to take part in the story-writing competition, he was afraid that his teacher and his classmates would know that the excuses he had made were the stories he had made up. He wanted to hide the falsehood of his excuses.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40665,74,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Complete the following web diagram.','Mrs Sen : Then why are you late?\nRitesh : That\'s not all. I realized that I had left my bag at the shop in the mad rush. So I ran back to get it.\nMrs Sen: And it wasn\'t there!\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, it was there all right, but there was a huge, hairy, brown dog sitting on it. I didn\'t know what to do.\nMrs Sen: Interesting! A dog on a bag in a shop!\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am. Even the shopkeeper was surprised. He didn\'t know where the dog had come from. How that dog growled ! We tried all kinds of things to make it move whistling and clapping, and showing him the stick but he wouldn\'t budge, and I couldn\'t leave without my school-bag.\nMrs Sen: But I see that you got your bag finally.\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am. Finally the shopkeeper gave me some biscuits. I offered the biscuits to the dog and lured him away from the bag.\nMrs Sen: OK. So let\'s say that accounts for fifteen minutes.\nRitesh : But there\'s more, Ma\'am. The poor dog began to follow me to school. It looked so poor and lonely and small...\nMrs Sen: Small? Did you say small?\nRitesh : I mean young, Ma\'am. It was big in size but it was only a puppy, really. And it looked lost. Maybe it was looking for its mother or owner and I thought it was asking for my help. So I began a enquire with the other shopkeepers and passers by, but nobody could tell and couldn\'t leave it behind.\nMrs Sen: And then you found the owner? Who was it?\nRitesh : That\'s the funny part, Ma\'am. The puppy itself found the owner. It saw someone across the road and just bounded towards the person. I was so relieved. Then I turned and ran straight to school.\nMrs Sen: Good boy! Now that your good deeds are done, there\'s something else you can do. There\'s a sto- ry-writing competition coming up. Would you like to send an entry?\nRitesh : Who Ma\'am? Me, Ma\'am? Now..... I wouldn\'t know what to write. I\'m not good at making up stories.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       How did Ritesh describe the same dog, the second time?\nA3] Vocabulary\n       Match the words with their meanings\n              Words                            Meanings\n        1. huge                           a) move\n        2. budge                        b) give\n        3. finally                         c) very big\n        4. offer                            d) in the end\nA4] Grammar\n       i. I couldn\'t leave without my school bag. (Make Affirmative)\n       ii. I offered the biscuits to The dog.\n            (Begin the sentence with the biscuits........)\nA5] Personal Response\n        Do you believe Ritesh\'s stories ? Why?','','A1] i) whistling                ii) clapping            \n       iii) showing him the stick         iv) offering biscuits.\nA2] The dog which Ritesh described as a huge, dog before, is described the second time as a small puppy. Ritesh said that it was a puppy but was big in size.\nA3] (1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – b)\nA4] i) I was unable to leave without my school bag. \n       ii) The biscuits were offered to the dog. (by me) \nA5] No, I don\'t believe Ritesh\'s stories. Dog sitting quietly in the shop, growling of dog at the shopkeeper and finally luring him away from the bag. All these things are unbelievable.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40666,74,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n     i. Ritesh heard a scream from a narrow lane.\n     ii. Ritesh took the man to Dr. Jadhav\n     iii. Ritesh ran after the thieves.\n     iv. The man near the pavement also ran with the thief.\nRitesh : May I come in, Madam?\nMrs Sen: Now? Don\'t you think you are early?\nRitesh : Pardon, Ma\'am?\nMrs Sen: I mean for the second period. You are too early for the second period.\nRitesh : I am so sorry! Ma\'am, I\'m really sorry. I started well in time from home, you won\'t believe what happened.\nMrs Sen: Hmm, We\'ll see. Let\'s hear the story first. What happened? Nothing short of an earthquake, I \'m sure.\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, really, Ma\'am. Honestly, Ma\'am.\nMrs Sen: Go ahead. Go ahead.\nRitesh : I was walking to school as usual, I was half way down the narrow lane that leads to the market. Suddenly, I heard a scream from the main road.\nMrs Sen: And of course, you had to investigate!\nRitesh : Yes, Madam, I had to find out if someone needed help. And sure enough, there was this person lying near the pavement. he was groaning with pain.\nMrs Sen: So, you took him to Dr Jadhav.\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, I didn\'t get the chance. Just as I reached that man, another man came running out of the shop there, with a packet in his hand. And the shopkeeper also ran out shouting, \"Chor! Chor! catch him!\"\nMrs Sen: Amazing! Quite a handful for you to deal with and all at once! What did you do then?\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am, but the most amazing thing happened then. That man near the pavement- he, too, got up and began to run with the thief. Without losing a moment, I ran after them.\nMrs Sen: And then you caught them and took them to the police station !\nRitesh : We couldn\'t do that, Ma\'am. They just vanished around the corner. All this happened so fast.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        Complete the following sentences.\n        i. The person was lying ---------------------\n        ii. Another man had ------------ in the hand.\n        iii. The most amazing thing was that -------------------\n        iv. The narrow lane leads to ----------------.\nA3] Vocabulary\n       From the passage, find out the opposites of:\n       i) broad x              ii) far x          iii) before x         iv) up x\nA4] Grammar\n       i. I was walking to school as usual. (Name the tense)\n       ii. As soon as I was half way down the narrow lane, I heard a scream \n             (Use \'No sooner - than\')\nA5] Personal Response\n       Do you believe Ritesh\'s stories ? Why?','','','A1] i) True            ii) False              iii) True             iv) True\nA2] i) near the pavement.                     ii) a packet\n      iii) the man near the pavement also ran with the thief.\n      iv) the market.\nA3] i) narrow         ii) near         iii) after         iv) down \nA4] i) Continuous past tense. \n       ii) No sooner was I half way down the narrow lane than I heard a scream.\nA5] No, I don\'t believe Ritesh\'s stories. Ritesh heard a scream from the main road man lying near the pavement-groaning with pain and the same man running after the thief all these are unbelievable.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40667,74,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question \n       Who said to whom? \n      i. \"Nothing short of an earthquake.\"\n      ii. \"I had to find out if someone needed help.\"\n      iii. \"They just vanished around the corner.\"\n      iv. \"Chor! Chor! catch him.\"\nRitesh : May I come in, Madam?\nMrs Sen: Now? Don\'t you think you are early?\nRitesh : Pardon, Ma\'am?\nMrs Sen: I mean for the second period. You are too early for the second period.\nRitesh : I am so sorry! Ma\'am, I\'m really sorry. I started well in time from home, you won\'t believe what happened.\nMrs Sen: Hmm, We\'ll see. Let\'s hear the story first. What happened? Nothing short of an earthquake, I \'m sure.\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, really, Ma\'am. Honestly, Ma\'am.\nMrs Sen: Go ahead. Go ahead.\nRitesh : I was walking to school as usual, I was half way down the narrow lane that leads to the market. Suddenly, I heard a scream from the main road.\nMrs Sen: And of course, you had to investigate!\nRitesh : Yes, Madam, I had to find out if someone needed help. And sure enough, there was this person lying near the pavement. he was groaning with pain.\nMrs Sen: So, you took him to Dr Jadhav.\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, I didn\'t get the chance. Just as I reached that man, another man came running out of the shop there, with a packet in his hand. And the shopkeeper also ran out shouting, \"Chor! Chor! catch him!\"\nMrs Sen: Amazing! Quite a handful for you to deal with and all at once! What did you do then?\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am, but the most amazing thing happened then. That man near the pavement- he, too, got up and began to run with the thief. Without losing a moment, I ran after them.\nMrs Sen: And then you caught them and took them to the police station !\nRitesh : We couldn\'t do that, Ma\'am. They just vanished around the corner. All this happened so fast.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       Rearrange the order of the following events as per the narrator :\n       i. The groaning man joined the thief and ran away.\n       ii. Ritesh heard a scream from the main road.\n       iii. The thief came out of the shop.\n       iv. A man, groaning with pain was lying near the pavement.\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Complete inserting the correct following words the skit :\n       i. The police arrived to ----------- the crime.\n       ii. The injured child kept ----------- with pain.\nA4] Grammar\n        Report the following in Indirect Speech :\n       i. Ritesh: May I come in, Madam?\n       ii. Mrs Sen: Ritesh, you are too early for the second period.\nA5] Personal Response\n       What really are the students\' reasons of coming late to school? ','','','A1] i) Teacher said to Ritesh         ii) Ritesh said to teacher\n      iii) Ritesh said to teacher        iv) The shopkeeper said to people.\nA2] ii, iv, iii, i\nA3] i) deal with                ii) groaning\nA4] i) Ritesh requested the teacher if he could come in.\n       ii) Mrs Sen told Ritesh that he was too early for the second period.\nA5] They do not keep themselves thoroughly ready for coming to school. They start late from home for coming to school. They waste time on the way unnecessarily.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40668,74,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Complete the following sentences with the help of the passage:\n      i. Ritesh went back to the shop to -------------------.\n      ii. The dog finally left the school bag when Ritesh --------------.\n      iii. Ritesh left the dog only when he found ------------.\n      iv. Ritesh was good at ----------------. \nMrs Sen : Then why are you late?\nRitesh : That\'s not all. I realized that I had left my bag at the shop in the mad rush. So I ran back to get it.\nMrs Sen: And it wasn\'t there!\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, it was there all right, but there was a huge, hairy, brown dog sitting on it. I didn\'t know what to do.\nMrs Sen: Interesting! A dog on a bag in a shop!\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am. Even the shopkeeper was surprised. He didn\'t know where the dog had come from. How that dog growled ! We tried all kinds of things to make it move whistling and clapping, and showing him the stick but he wouldn\'t budge, and I couldn\'t leave without my school-bag.\nMrs Sen: But I see that you got your bag finally.\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am. Finally the shopkeeper gave me some biscuits. I offered the biscuits to the dog and lured him away from the bag.\nMrs Sen: OK. So let\'s say that accounts for fifteen minutes.\nRitesh : But there\'s more, Ma\'am. The poor dog began to follow me to school. It looked so poor and lonely and small...\nMrs Sen: Small? Did you say small?\nRitesh : I mean young, Ma\'am. It was big in size but it was only a puppy, really. And it looked lost. Maybe it was looking for its mother or owner and I thought it was asking for my help. So I began a enquire with the other shopkeepers and passers by, but nobody could tell and couldn\'t leave it behind.\nMrs Sen: And then you found the owner? Who was it?\nRitesh : That\'s the funny part, Ma\'am. The puppy itself found the owner. It saw someone across the road and just bounded towards the person. I was so relieved. Then I turned and ran straight to school.\nMrs Sen: Good boy! Now that your good deeds are done, there\'s something else you can do. There\'s a sto- ry-writing competition coming up. Would you like to send an entry?\nRitesh : Who Ma\'am? Me, Ma\'am? Now..... I wouldn\'t know what to write. I\'m not good at making up stories.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n     Rearrange the order of the following events as per the narrator :\n     i. The dog began to follow Ritesh to school.\n     ii. A huge dog was sitting on Ritesh\'s school-bag.\n     iii. The dog refused to move.\n      iv. The dog itself found its owner.\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Complete the following by inserting the correct words from the skit:\n       i. The heavy load would not ------------.\n       ii. My friend ---------- me to eat chocolates in the classroom.\nA4] Grammar\n        Report the following in Indirect speech:\n       i. Ritesh: I had left my bag at the shop.\n       ii. Mrs. Sen: There\'s a story-writing competition.\nA5] Personal Response\n       Why, do you think, did Ritesh refuse to take part in the story-writing competition?','','','A1] i) get back his bag.                 ii) offered him biscuits\n       iii) its owner                            iv) making up stories\nA2] ii, iv, i, iii\nA3] i) move                 ii) offered \nA4] i) Ritesh said that he had left his bag at the shop.\n       ii) Mrs. Sen said that there was a story- writing competition.\nA5] I think, if he had agreed to take part in the story-writing competition, he was afraid that his teacher and his classmates would know that the excuses he had made were the stories he had made up. He wanted to hide the falsehood of his excuses.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40669,74,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Complete the following web diagram.','Mrs Sen : Then why are you late?\nRitesh : That\'s not all. I realized that I had left my bag at the shop in the mad rush. So I ran back to get it.\nMrs Sen: And it wasn\'t there!\nRitesh : No, Ma\'am, it was there all right, but there was a huge, hairy, brown dog sitting on it. I didn\'t know what to do.\nMrs Sen: Interesting! A dog on a bag in a shop!\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am. Even the shopkeeper was surprised. He didn\'t know where the dog had come from. How that dog growled ! We tried all kinds of things to make it move whistling and clapping, and showing him the stick but he wouldn\'t budge, and I couldn\'t leave without my school-bag.\nMrs Sen: But I see that you got your bag finally.\nRitesh : Yes, Ma\'am. Finally the shopkeeper gave me some biscuits. I offered the biscuits to the dog and lured him away from the bag.\nMrs Sen: OK. So let\'s say that accounts for fifteen minutes.\nRitesh : But there\'s more, Ma\'am. The poor dog began to follow me to school. It looked so poor and lonely and small...\nMrs Sen: Small? Did you say small?\nRitesh : I mean young, Ma\'am. It was big in size but it was only a puppy, really. And it looked lost. Maybe it was looking for its mother or owner and I thought it was asking for my help. So I began a enquire with the other shopkeepers and passers by, but nobody could tell and couldn\'t leave it behind.\nMrs Sen: And then you found the owner? Who was it?\nRitesh : That\'s the funny part, Ma\'am. The puppy itself found the owner. It saw someone across the road and just bounded towards the person. I was so relieved. Then I turned and ran straight to school.\nMrs Sen: Good boy! Now that your good deeds are done, there\'s something else you can do. There\'s a sto- ry-writing competition coming up. Would you like to send an entry?\nRitesh : Who Ma\'am? Me, Ma\'am? Now..... I wouldn\'t know what to write. I\'m not good at making up stories.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       How did Ritesh describe the same dog, the second time?\nA3] Vocabulary\n       Match the words with their meanings\n              Words                            Meanings\n        1. huge                           a) move\n        2. budge                        b) give\n        3. finally                         c) very big\n        4. offer                            d) in the end\nA4] Grammar\n       i. I couldn\'t leave without my school bag. (Make Affirmative)\n       ii. I offered the biscuits to The dog.\n            (Begin the sentence with the biscuits........)\nA5] Personal Response\n        Do you believe Ritesh\'s stories ? Why?','','A1] i) whistling                ii) clapping            \n       iii) showing him the stick         iv) offering biscuits.\nA2] The dog which Ritesh described as a huge, dog before, is described the second time as a small puppy. Ritesh said that it was a puppy but was big in size.\nA3] (1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – b)\nA4] i) I was unable to leave without my school bag. \n       ii) The biscuits were offered to the dog. (by me) \nA5] No, I don\'t believe Ritesh\'s stories. Dog sitting quietly in the shop, growling of dog at the shopkeeper and finally luring him away from the bag. All these things are unbelievable.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40670,75,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Write appropriate words in the blanks.\n     i. Height of Grace\'s father : --------------------\n     ii. Name of the little woman : -----------------\n     iii. Name of the light house : --------------------\n     iv. Name of Grace\'s country : -------------------\nAround the rocky coast of England stand many lonely lighthouses. Their lights, shining across the sea, warn sailors at night of dangerous rocks. When they see the lights sailors take their ships far out to sea. But sometimes fierce storms drive ships on to the rocks. This is the story of a brave girl and of a shipwreck on the rocks one night in 1838.\nGrace Darling was the daughter of a lighthouse keeper. She had lived all her life in lonely lighthouses, far away from towns and cities. There was not even a village near their home. Their nearest neighbours were many miles away. But Grace\'s father was a wise and educated man who brought up his children very carefully. Grace and her brothers and sisters were all taught to read and write. Their father also taught them to be honest and brave and unselfish.\nWhen Grace was ten years old the family went to live in the Longstone lighthouse. Their new home was on some wild islands, near the east coast of England. The Darling children grew up here. When they were older they left their lonely home, one by one, and found work in the cities. At last, all of them had left the lighthouse, except Grace, who stayed at home to help her mother and father. She had not grown very tall. She was only a little woman, not at all like her father, who was more than six feet tall.\nOn the night of September 6th 1838, a terrible storm was blowing. Grace was used to storms. She herself was warm and safe in the big lighthouse. But she was always unhappy about the poor sailors who were at sea in the storm.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        Complete the following web diagram.','A3] Vocabulary\n        From the passage find out the words that are related to relations.\nA4] Grammar\n        i. He brought up his children very carefully. (Make exclamatory)\n        ii. Their father taught them to be honest and un selfish. (Use \'as well as\')\nA5] Personal Response\n       Write four sentences about your father.','','A1] i) six feet                ii) Grace            iii) Longstone          iv) England \nA2] Grace\'s father was a lighthouse keeper. He was a wise and educated man. He brought up his children very carefully. He taught them to be honest, brave and unselfish. He was more than six feet tall. \nA3] i) daughter         ii) neighbours          iii) father       iv) brothers     v) sisters\nA4] i) How carefully he brought up his children!\n      ii) Their father taught them to be honest as well as unselfish.\nA5] Dattatray Eknath Kakade is my father. He is a teacher in a primary school. He is 54 years old. He is very simple and caring.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40671,75,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n        Answer in short in your own word :\n      i. She stayed back home, with her parents WHO?\n     ii. A ship was wrecked that night. WHY? \n     iii. Nine men and women did not drown. WHY NOT? \nAround the rocky coast of England stand many lonely lighthouses. Their lights, shining across the sea, warn sailors at night of dangerous rocks. When they see the lights sailors take their ships far out to sea. But sometimes fierce storms drive ships on to the rocks. This is the story of a brave girl and of a shipwreck on the rocks one night in 1838.\nGrace Darling was the daughter of a lighthouse keeper. She had lived all her life in lonely lighthouses, far away from towns and cities. There was not even a village near their home. Their nearest neighbours were many miles away. But Grace\'s father was a wise and educated man who brought up his children very carefully. Grace and her brothers and sisters were all taught to read and write. Their father also taught them to be honest and brave and unselfish.\nWhen Grace was ten years old the family went to live in the Longstone lighthouse. Their new home was on some wild islands, near the east coast of England. The Darling children grew up here. When they were older they left their lonely home, one by one, and found work in the cities. At last, all of them had left the lighthouse, except Grace, who stayed at home to help her mother and father. She had not grown very tall. She was only a little woman, not at all like her father, who was more than six feet tall.\nOn the night of September 6th 1838, a terrible storm was blowing. Grace was used to storms. She herself was warm and safe in the big lighthouse. But she was always unhappy about the poor sailors who were at sea in the storm.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        What role does the lighthouse play?\nA3] Vocabulary \n        Make a list of words related to sea-travel.\nA4] Grammar\n        Choose the proper Modal to fill in the gap: \n       i. They ---------- hear anything else.\n           (does not/could not/have not)\n     ii. Nine men and women --------- climb on the rock. \n            (has/does/should/could)\nA5] Personal Response\n        Do you think, that the sailors who are at sea in the storm, are in danger? Why?','','','A1] i) Grace stayed with her parents.\n       ii) Because of a terrible storm the ship wrecked upon the rocks.\n       iii) Because they could climb on to a rock and clung to it.\nA2] The bright light of the lighthouse shines across the sea. It warns sailors at night of dangerous rocks. When the sailors see the signs of lighthouse lights, they take their ships safely, far out to sea.\nA3] Words related to sea-travel: ship, sailors, lighthouse, rocks; storms, shipwreck, islands, wind, waves.\nA4] i) could not            ii) could\nA5] Yes, the sailors who are at sea in the storm are always in danger. Terrible things happen during the storm in the sea. Noise of the wind and waves frighten them to death.\n If the ship wrecks or sinks, they are sure to drown. There is rarely any shelter around. There lives are at stake. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40672,75,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n        Answer in short in your own words:\n      i. Grace said she would help her father to rescue. HOW? \n      ii. Mrs. Darling helped them to get the boat out. HOW? \nNext morning at six o\'clock Grace was dressing. She looked out of her window and saw the storm was still blowing. Suddenly she stopped! Wasn\'t there\nsomething on the distant rocks? Calling her father, she looked again. Yes, there were certainly people clinging on to the rocks. But they were half under the sea!\n\'We must rescue them before they are drowned!\' cried Grace. We must do something! Will you ever sleep again, Father, if they die?\nWilliam Darling was a brave old man, but he shook his head. \'It is hopeless,\' he said. \"We can do nothing. How can I row a boat by myself through these waves? If only I had another man here to help me!\"\n\'I can help you, Father, cried Grace. \'I can row a boat as well as any man, can\'t I? I am small but I am strong. You and I have often rowed together. We can reach those unfortunate people!\'\nMrs Darling was with her husband and daughter at the window. \'No, no!\' she exclaimed. \'How will it help those poor people if you are drowned? You cannot row in this storm. Do not try! A girl like you, Grace, cannot do a man\'s job. Let us pray for them, husband. God will help them, perhaps, but we cannot!\'\nGrace was determined, however. She argued until at last her father agreed. Poor Mrs Darling, with a heavy heart, helped them to get the boat out. She watched and prayed as they set off.\nAnyone who has seen a storm at sea can imagine that journey. The boat went up and down over waves as high as hills. Sometimes it stood on one end, sometimes on the other end. It went up, up to the top of a wave and then down, down into a great valley between the waves. Every time they went down the boat nearly went under the water.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        What did Grace notice from the window ?\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Find from the story the antonyms of the following :\n      i. disagreed ×                ii. young ×             iii. weak ×         iv. low × \nA4] Grammar\n        Choose the proper Modal to fill in the gap :\n       i. ---------  you ever sleep again?  (must/will/may)\n       ii. We -------- do something.       (must/need/would)\nA5] Personal Response\n        What is your father? What do you think about him?','','','A1] i) She would row with her father and reach the sailors to rescue them.\n        ii) with a heavy heart.\nA2] Grace saw from the window that the storm was still blowing. Suddenly, she saw something on the distant rocks. She noticed that some people were clinging on to the rocks and they were half under the sea.\nA3] i) agreed                ii) old            iii) strong             iv) high\nA4] i) will               ii) must\nA5] My father is a farmer. He is very kind. He works very hard. He provides me all facilities for leaning. He teaches me to be honest and unselfish. He tells me to do good deeds. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40673,75,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n        Complete the following sentences.\n       i. Grace died at the age of --------------------\n       ii. Grace helped her mother to -------------------\n       iii. Money was raised to ----------------------\n       iv. Grace is remembered for ----------------\nGrace did not have any time to rest now. All that day she was busy helping her mother to warm and feed the rescued passengers. After several days these passengers were able to return to their homes in England. They told the story of the brave girl and her father to their families and friends. Suddenly Grace Darling and her father were famous. The story of their heroism was told in the newspapers. Money was raised to help all the lighthouse keepers in their dangerous, lonely lives. Grace and her father were given a special reward for their heroism. Poor Grace did not live long after the rescue. She died at the age of twenty seven. But she is still remembered for her unselfish courage. She risked her own life for others.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       Arrange the following sentences in correct order.\n      i. The story of their heroism was told in the newspapers.\n      ii. They told about Grace and her father in their families. \n      iii. The passengers returned to their homes. \n      iv. They were given a special reward.\nA3] Vocabulary\n        From the passage find out four adjectives.\nA4] Grammar\n        i. She risked her own life for others. (Add a question tag)\n        ii. These passengers were able to return to their homes. \n              (Remove \'able to\' from the sentence)\nA5] Personal Response\n        How do you help your father in his work?','','','A1]  i) twenty seven         ii) warm and feed the rescued passengers.\n        iii) help all the lighthouse keepers.        iv) her unselfish courage.\nA2] iii, ii, i, iv\nA3] several, brave, famous, dangerous, lonely.\nA4] i) She risked her own life for others, didn\'t she?\n       ii) These passengers could return to their homes. \nA5] I always help my father in his work. My father is a farmer. He goes to the field early in the morning. I carry his breakfast to the field. On the Sundays I help him in farming. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40674,75,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n       Write appropriate words in the blanks.\n     i. Height of Grace\'s father : --------------------\n     ii. Name of the little woman : -----------------\n     iii. Name of the light house : --------------------\n     iv. Name of Grace\'s country : -------------------\nAround the rocky coast of England stand many lonely lighthouses. Their lights, shining across the sea, warn sailors at night of dangerous rocks. When they see the lights sailors take their ships far out to sea. But sometimes fierce storms drive ships on to the rocks. This is the story of a brave girl and of a shipwreck on the rocks one night in 1838.\nGrace Darling was the daughter of a lighthouse keeper. She had lived all her life in lonely lighthouses, far away from towns and cities. There was not even a village near their home. Their nearest neighbours were many miles away. But Grace\'s father was a wise and educated man who brought up his children very carefully. Grace and her brothers and sisters were all taught to read and write. Their father also taught them to be honest and brave and unselfish.\nWhen Grace was ten years old the family went to live in the Longstone lighthouse. Their new home was on some wild islands, near the east coast of England. The Darling children grew up here. When they were older they left their lonely home, one by one, and found work in the cities. At last, all of them had left the lighthouse, except Grace, who stayed at home to help her mother and father. She had not grown very tall. She was only a little woman, not at all like her father, who was more than six feet tall.\nOn the night of September 6th 1838, a terrible storm was blowing. Grace was used to storms. She herself was warm and safe in the big lighthouse. But she was always unhappy about the poor sailors who were at sea in the storm.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        Complete the following web diagram.','A3] Vocabulary\n        From the passage find out the words that are related to relations.\nA4] Grammar\n        i. He brought up his children very carefully. (Make exclamatory)\n        ii. Their father taught them to be honest and un selfish. (Use \'as well as\')\nA5] Personal Response\n       Write four sentences about your father.','','A1] i) six feet                ii) Grace            iii) Longstone          iv) England \nA2] Grace\'s father was a lighthouse keeper. He was a wise and educated man. He brought up his children very carefully. He taught them to be honest, brave and unselfish. He was more than six feet tall. \nA3] i) daughter         ii) neighbours          iii) father       iv) brothers     v) sisters\nA4] i) How carefully he brought up his children!\n      ii) Their father taught them to be honest as well as unselfish.\nA5] Dattatray Eknath Kakade is my father. He is a teacher in a primary school. He is 54 years old. He is very simple and caring.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40675,75,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n        Answer in short in your own word :\n      i. She stayed back home, with her parents WHO?\n     ii. A ship was wrecked that night. WHY? \n     iii. Nine men and women did not drown. WHY NOT? \nAround the rocky coast of England stand many lonely lighthouses. Their lights, shining across the sea, warn sailors at night of dangerous rocks. When they see the lights sailors take their ships far out to sea. But sometimes fierce storms drive ships on to the rocks. This is the story of a brave girl and of a shipwreck on the rocks one night in 1838.\nGrace Darling was the daughter of a lighthouse keeper. She had lived all her life in lonely lighthouses, far away from towns and cities. There was not even a village near their home. Their nearest neighbours were many miles away. But Grace\'s father was a wise and educated man who brought up his children very carefully. Grace and her brothers and sisters were all taught to read and write. Their father also taught them to be honest and brave and unselfish.\nWhen Grace was ten years old the family went to live in the Longstone lighthouse. Their new home was on some wild islands, near the east coast of England. The Darling children grew up here. When they were older they left their lonely home, one by one, and found work in the cities. At last, all of them had left the lighthouse, except Grace, who stayed at home to help her mother and father. She had not grown very tall. She was only a little woman, not at all like her father, who was more than six feet tall.\nOn the night of September 6th 1838, a terrible storm was blowing. Grace was used to storms. She herself was warm and safe in the big lighthouse. But she was always unhappy about the poor sailors who were at sea in the storm.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        What role does the lighthouse play?\nA3] Vocabulary \n        Make a list of words related to sea-travel.\nA4] Grammar\n        Choose the proper Modal to fill in the gap: \n       i. They ---------- hear anything else.\n           (does not/could not/have not)\n     ii. Nine men and women --------- climb on the rock. \n            (has/does/should/could)\nA5] Personal Response\n        Do you think, that the sailors who are at sea in the storm, are in danger? Why?','','','A1] i) Grace stayed with her parents.\n       ii) Because of a terrible storm the ship wrecked upon the rocks.\n       iii) Because they could climb on to a rock and clung to it.\nA2] The bright light of the lighthouse shines across the sea. It warns sailors at night of dangerous rocks. When the sailors see the signs of lighthouse lights, they take their ships safely, far out to sea.\nA3] Words related to sea-travel: ship, sailors, lighthouse, rocks; storms, shipwreck, islands, wind, waves.\nA4] i) could not            ii) could\nA5] Yes, the sailors who are at sea in the storm are always in danger. Terrible things happen during the storm in the sea. Noise of the wind and waves frighten them to death.\n If the ship wrecks or sinks, they are sure to drown. There is rarely any shelter around. There lives are at stake. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40676,75,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n        Answer in short in your own words:\n      i. Grace said she would help her father to rescue. HOW? \n      ii. Mrs. Darling helped them to get the boat out. HOW? \nNext morning at six o\'clock Grace was dressing. She looked out of her window and saw the storm was still blowing. Suddenly she stopped! Wasn\'t there\nsomething on the distant rocks? Calling her father, she looked again. Yes, there were certainly people clinging on to the rocks. But they were half under the sea!\n\'We must rescue them before they are drowned!\' cried Grace. We must do something! Will you ever sleep again, Father, if they die?\nWilliam Darling was a brave old man, but he shook his head. \'It is hopeless,\' he said. \"We can do nothing. How can I row a boat by myself through these waves? If only I had another man here to help me!\"\n\'I can help you, Father, cried Grace. \'I can row a boat as well as any man, can\'t I? I am small but I am strong. You and I have often rowed together. We can reach those unfortunate people!\'\nMrs Darling was with her husband and daughter at the window. \'No, no!\' she exclaimed. \'How will it help those poor people if you are drowned? You cannot row in this storm. Do not try! A girl like you, Grace, cannot do a man\'s job. Let us pray for them, husband. God will help them, perhaps, but we cannot!\'\nGrace was determined, however. She argued until at last her father agreed. Poor Mrs Darling, with a heavy heart, helped them to get the boat out. She watched and prayed as they set off.\nAnyone who has seen a storm at sea can imagine that journey. The boat went up and down over waves as high as hills. Sometimes it stood on one end, sometimes on the other end. It went up, up to the top of a wave and then down, down into a great valley between the waves. Every time they went down the boat nearly went under the water.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n        What did Grace notice from the window ?\nA3] Vocabulary\n        Find from the story the antonyms of the following :\n      i. disagreed ×                ii. young ×             iii. weak ×         iv. low × \nA4] Grammar\n        Choose the proper Modal to fill in the gap :\n       i. ---------  you ever sleep again?  (must/will/may)\n       ii. We -------- do something.       (must/need/would)\nA5] Personal Response\n        What is your father? What do you think about him?','','','A1] i) She would row with her father and reach the sailors to rescue them.\n        ii) with a heavy heart.\nA2] Grace saw from the window that the storm was still blowing. Suddenly, she saw something on the distant rocks. She noticed that some people were clinging on to the rocks and they were half under the sea.\nA3] i) agreed                ii) old            iii) strong             iv) high\nA4] i) will               ii) must\nA5] My father is a farmer. He is very kind. He works very hard. He provides me all facilities for leaning. He teaches me to be honest and unselfish. He tells me to do good deeds. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40677,75,'descriptive','2b',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Simple Factual Question\n        Complete the following sentences.\n       i. Grace died at the age of --------------------\n       ii. Grace helped her mother to -------------------\n       iii. Money was raised to ----------------------\n       iv. Grace is remembered for ----------------\nGrace did not have any time to rest now. All that day she was busy helping her mother to warm and feed the rescued passengers. After several days these passengers were able to return to their homes in England. They told the story of the brave girl and her father to their families and friends. Suddenly Grace Darling and her father were famous. The story of their heroism was told in the newspapers. Money was raised to help all the lighthouse keepers in their dangerous, lonely lives. Grace and her father were given a special reward for their heroism. Poor Grace did not live long after the rescue. She died at the age of twenty seven. But she is still remembered for her unselfish courage. She risked her own life for others.\nA2] Complex Factual Question\n       Arrange the following sentences in correct order.\n      i. The story of their heroism was told in the newspapers.\n      ii. They told about Grace and her father in their families. \n      iii. The passengers returned to their homes. \n      iv. They were given a special reward.\nA3] Vocabulary\n        From the passage find out four adjectives.\nA4] Grammar\n        i. She risked her own life for others. (Add a question tag)\n        ii. These passengers were able to return to their homes. \n              (Remove \'able to\' from the sentence)\nA5] Personal Response\n        How do you help your father in his work?','','','A1]  i) twenty seven         ii) warm and feed the rescued passengers.\n        iii) help all the lighthouse keepers.        iv) her unselfish courage.\nA2] iii, ii, i, iv\nA3] several, brave, famous, dangerous, lonely.\nA4] i) She risked her own life for others, didn\'t she?\n       ii) These passengers could return to their homes. \nA5] I always help my father in his work. My father is a farmer. He goes to the field early in the morning. I carry his breakfast to the field. On the Sundays I help him in farming. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40678,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Write the noun forms of the following :\n          i. measure                      ii. give\n   2) Underline the adverbs in the sentences given below.\n         He did his work very roughly and carelessly.\n   3) Fill in the blanks using the correct propositions from those given in the brackets :\n         i. Haridas took the tanpura ------- Tansen’s hand. (by/from)\n         ii. My legs were now filled ------- some miraculous strength. (with/for)\nB] Translate the following words into Marathi ---------------- 2M \n      i. valley           ii. highway       iii. liveliest           iv. lake','','','A] 1) i) measurement            ii) gift\n     2) roughly, carelessly\n     3) i) from              ii) with\nB] i) दरी           ii) महामार्ग           iii) सर्वात तजेलदार           iv) तळे   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40679,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Write the noun forms of the following :\n          i. dedicate                      ii. contract\n    2) Underline the adverbs in the sentences given below.\n         He simply wanted to finish the job. \n     3) Fill in the blanks using the correct propositions from those given in the brackets :\n         i. The lion looked ----------- him piteously. (to/at)\n         ii. The slaves were forced to work --------- their masters. (for/by)\nB] Translate the following words into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n     i. crew          ii. virus           iii. traffic           iv. size','','','A] 1) i) dedication               ii) contractor \n     2) simply\n     3) i) at                 ii) for\nB] i) खलासी            ii) विषाणू          iii) रहदारी           iv) आकार ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40680,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Write the noun forms of the following :\n          i. encourage                      ii. agree\n   2) Underline the adverbs in the sentences given below.\n         Never before had anyone lavished love on me. \n    3) Fill in the blanks using the correct propositions from those given in the brackets :\n         i. He wept -------------  relief and joy. (with/for)\n         ii. Both of them fell ----------- the hands of the soldiers. (at/into)\nB] Translate the following words into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n     i. slave           ii. obey         iii. starve            iv. coward','','','A] 1) i) encouragement               ii) agreement \n     2) Never, before\n     3) i) with              ii) into\nB] i) गुलाम             ii) आज्ञा पाळणे            iii) उपाशी ठेवणे           iv)  भित्रा ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40681,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Write the noun forms of the following :\n          i. realize                      ii. perfect\n    2) Write adverb forms of the following words :\n         i. speedy                      ii. recent \n    3) Fill in the blanks using the correct propositions from those given in the brackets :\n         i. Mother seized me --------- the arm and gave me a good jerk and seated me. (by/in)\n         ii. Soon those passengers were able to return ------- their homes. (to/on)\nB] Translate the following words into Marathi ---------------- 2M  \n     i. crime            ii. intension           iii. roam          iv. freedom ','','','A] 1) i) realization               ii) perfection\n     2) i) speedily                  ii) recently \n     3) i) by             ii) to\nB] i) गुन्हा            ii) हेतू             iii) भटकणे              iv) स्वातंत्र्य ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40682,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Write the correct articles ‘a’, ‘an’ or ‘the’ where necessary. \n          i. ---------- apple                     ii. ------------ earth\n   2) Write adverb forms of the following words :\n         i. entire                      ii. gentle \n    3) Spot the error and rewrite the correct sentence :\n        At last they came close on the rocks.\nB] Translate the following words into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n     i. beam              ii. float          iii. memorize          iv. confide','','','A] 1) i) an              ii) The earth\n     2) i) entirely             ii) gently \n     3) At last they came close to the rocks.\nB] i) किरण             ii) तरंगणे             iii) पाठ करणे           iv) विश्वास ठेवणे                ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40683,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) 1) Write the correct articles ‘a’, ‘an’ or ‘the’ where necessary. \n          i. ---------- internet                     ii. ------------ World\n   2) Use the adverbs in the brackets in the correct position and rewrite the following sentences :\n        He helps the needy people. (often) \n  3) Spot the error and rewrite the correct sentence :\n       She died to the age of twenty-seven.\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n     i. Keep social distancing\n     ii. I am 14 years old.','','','A] 1) i) an                      ii) The world\n     2) He often helps the needy people.\n     3) She died at the age of twenty-seven. \nB] i) सुरक्षित अंतर ठेवा.           ii) मी 14 वर्षाचा आहे.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40684,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Fill in the blanks with ‘a’, ‘an’ or ‘the’ where necessary.\n         i. Recently I read --------- interesting story\n         ii. --------- boys are very useful to ------- narrator.\n2) Use the adverbs in the brackets in the correct position and rewrite the following sentences :\n        That man is without a job. (sometimes) \n3) Spot the error and rewrite the correct sentence :\n     The puppy was looking on its mother or owner.\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n     i. The sky is blue.\n     ii. Corona is a highly infectious disease.','','','A] 1) i) an             ii) The, a\n     2) Sometimes, that man is without job.\n     3) The puppy was looking for its mother or owner.\nB] i) आकाश निळे असते                ii) कोरोना हा अत्यंत संपर्कजन्य रोग आहे. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40685,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Fill in the blanks with ‘a’, ‘an’ or ‘the’ where necessary.\n          i. The story begins with -------- narrator driving down the foothills of ------ Alps.\n         ii. ------ constructor was -------- honest man.\n     2) Use the adverbs in the brackets in the correct position and rewrite the following sentences :\n        I will do the wrong thing. (never)\n     3) Say whether each of the following sentences is a statement, a question, a command or an exclamation :\n          i. Did the teacher believe Ritesh’s story?\n          ii. The dog refused to move.\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M   \n      i. Please switch on the light.\n      ii. It is 15 kms away from here.','','','A] 1) i) The, the            ii) The, an\n     2) I will never do the wrong thing.\n     3) i) a question         ii) a statement\nB] i) कृपया लाईट लावा                  ii) ते इथून १५ किमी अंतरावर आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40686,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Choose two suitable adjectives for each of the following nouns.\n          i. sky                     ii. woman\n    2) Spot the error and rewrite the sentence.\n         Our principal is very strictly. \n     3) Say whether each of the following sentences is a statement, a question, a command or an exclamation :\n          i. The teacher greeted me with a smile.\n          ii. When will we stop these losses?\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n      i. Cover your nose and mouth with mask.\n      ii. He left for Pune yesterday.','','','A] 1) i) blue, cloudy             ii) beautiful, graceful\n     2) Our principal is very strict.\n     3) i) a statement                ii) a question\nB] i) तुमचे नाक व तोंडावर मास्क बांधा               ii) काल तो पुण्याला गेला. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40687,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Choose two suitable adjectives for each of the following nouns.\n          i. coat                     ii. smell\n    2) Spot the error and rewrite the sentence.\n         You are speaking fastly.\n     3) Say whether each of the following sentences is a statement, a question, a command or an exclamation :\n           i. Now rise and hop off to your class.\n           ii. How kind and gentle Androcles was!\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M \n      i. Stand in a queue.\n      ii. Enter one after another in bus.','','','A] 1) i) black, fashionable               ii) fragrant, sweet\n     2) You are speaking fast.\n     3) i) a command                 ii) an exclamation \nB] i) रांगेत उभे रहा                ii) बस मध्ये एकापाठोपाठ एकाने प्रवेश करा. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40688,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Choose two suitable adjectives for each of the following nouns.\n          i. appearance                     ii. taste\n    2) Spot the error and rewrite the sentence.\n         He completed his homework and than he took his food. \n      3) Complete the following questions using the proper question words from those given in the brackets :\n           i. --------------- makes you cry? (Who/What/Which)\n         ii. --------------- did you return from Mumbai? (Where/When/What)\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n      i. We should talk politely.\n      ii. A stitch in time, saves nine.','','','A] 1) i) gentle, attractive                  ii) bitter, salty\n     2) He completed his homework and then he took his food.\n     3) i) What             ii) When\nB] i) आपण नम्रतेने बोलले पाहिजे \n     ii) वेळेवर केलेल्या उपायाने संभाव्य मोठी हानी ठरते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40689,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Spot the error and correct the sentences\n          This boy is the tallest than the others. \n     2) Complete the following questions using the proper question words from those given in the brackets :\n          i. ----------- have you decided? (Where/What/Who)\n          ii. ------------ did you come late? (Where/What/Why)\n     3) Add suffix to convert the following words to adjective forms :\n          i. laugh                 ii. thought\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n      i. Keep your surroundings clean.\n      ii. Do not spit in public place.','','','A] 1) This boy is taller than the others.\n     2) i) What           ii) Why\n     3) i) laughable                 ii) thoughtful\nB] i) आपला परिसर स्वच्छ ठेवा             ii) सार्वजनिक ठिकाणी थुंकू नका.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40690,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Spot the error and correct the sentences\n          It was a red, big apple. \n    2) Complete the following questions using the proper question words from those given in the brackets :\n          i. --------------- subject is easy? (Which/Who/When)\n          ii. --------------- did you buy this book? (Where/What/Who)\n     3) Add suffix to convert the following words to adjective forms :\n          i. forget                 ii. care\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M \n      i. The teacher greeted me with a smile.\n      ii. Do not make friendship with a stranger. ','','','A] 1) It was a big, red apple.\n     2) i) Which             ii) Where\n     3) i) forgettable               ii) careful\nB] i) शिक्षकेने माझे स्मित हास्य आणि स्वागत केले.\n     ii)  अनोळखी व्यक्तीशी मैत्री करू नका.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40691,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Spot the error and correct the sentences\n          It was a brown, huge, hairy dog. \n    2) Insert the proper question words to complete the questions to get the excepted answer given in the brackets :\n        i. ------------------ is the capital of Maharashtra? (Mumbai)\n        ii. ------------------- is your school located? (On S. B. Road)\n    3) Add suffix to convert the following words to adjective forms :\n          i. count                 ii. fear\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M  \n      i. Do not believe in rumours.\n      ii. Experience is the best teacher. ','','','A] 1) It was a huge, hairy, brown dog.\n     2) i) What              ii) Where\n     3) i) countable           ii) fearful\nB] i) अफवांवर विश्वास ठेवू नका            ii) अनुभव हा सर्वश्रेष्ठ गुरु होय.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40692,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Spot the error and correct the sentences\n          Chhagan is as stronger as his brother. \n    2) Insert the proper question words to complete the questions to get the excepted answer given in the brackets :\n         i. ------------- is your examination going to begin? (Next Monday)\n         ii. -------------- does the old man walk? (slowly)\n    3) Make anagrams of the following words :\n         i. state               ii. dusty\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------------- 2M \n     i. Follow the rules of the society.\n     ii. Follow the advice of elders.','','','A] 1) Chhagan is as strong as his brother.\n     2) i) When             ii) How\n     3) i) taste              ii) study\nB] i) समाजाचे नियम पाळा              ii) ज्येष्ठांचा सल्ला पाळा. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40693,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Underline the adverbs in the sentences given below.\n          Grace Darling was always unhappy about the sailors caught in the storm.\n   2) Insert the proper question words to complete the questions to get the excepted answer given in the brackets :\n       i. -------------- colour is your favourite? (Blue)\n       ii. --------------- teaches you English? (Mrs Ashwini Patil)\n   3) Make anagrams of the following words :\n         i. ten               ii. allergy\nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n      i. An empty vessel makes much noise.\n      ii. Experience is the best teacher.','','','A] 1) always\n     2) i) Which               ii) Who\n     3) i) net             ii) gallery \nB] i) उथळ पाण्याला खळखळाट फार\n     ii) अनुभव हा सर्वश्रेष्ठ गुरु होय.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40694,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Underline the adverbs in the sentences given below.\n          Sometimes the boat stood on one end, sometimes on the other end.\n    2) Write the synonyms of the following words : \n          i. alike                   ii. begin\n    3) Underline the Auxiliary Verbs in the following sentences.\n         i. We have a holiday tomorrow.\n         ii. Reena is injured badly  \nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n      i. No pain, No gain\n      ii. Use public service.','','','A] 1) sometimes\n     2) i) same               ii) start\n     3) i) have           ii) is\nB] i) कष्टाशिवाय फळ नाही              ii) सार्वजनिक वाहनांचा वापर करा.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40695,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Underline the adverbs in the sentences given below.\n          You and I often rowed together.\n    2) Write the synonyms of the following words : \n          i. happy                 ii. want\n     3) Underline the Auxiliary Verbs in the following sentences.\n          i. You must wear clean clothes.\n          ii. She should talk politely.\n     B] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n           i. Service to man is service to God.\n           ii. Where there is will, there is a way.','','','A] 1) often\n     2) i) glad              ii) need\n     3) i) must             ii) should\nB] i) जनसेवा हीच खरी ईश्वरसेवा           ii) इच्छा असेल तर मार्ग दिसेल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40696,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Write adverb forms of the following words :\n          i. easy                   ii. merry\n    2) Write the synonyms of the following words : \n          i. unhappy            ii. damp\n     3) Add the proper prefix to make the following words opposite in meaning\n         i. obey                  ii. sympathetic \nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi ---------------- 2M \n      i. A friend is need is a friend indeed.\n      ii. Necessity is the mother of invention.','','','A] 1) i) easily              ii) merrily \n     2) i) sad                ii) moist\n     3) i) disobey                ii) unsympathetic\nB] i) संकट समयी मदत करतो तोच खरा मित्र \n     ii) गरज ही शोधाची जननी आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40697,710,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Do as directed ------------------ 3M\n     1) Write adverb forms of the following words :\n          i. faithful                   ii. quick\n    2) Write the synonyms of the following words : \n          i. ill                         ii. hot\n     3) Add the proper prefix to make the following words opposite in meaning\n          i. honour               ii. afraid\nB] Translate the following proverbs into Marathi ---------------- 2M\n     i. Practice makes man perfect.\n     ii. Laughter is the best medicine. ','','','A] 1) i) faithfully                 ii) quickly \n     2) i) sick                ii) warm\n     3) i) dishonor             ii) unafraid \nB] i) सरावाने मनुष्य परिपूर्ण होतो\n     ii) हास्य हे सर्वोत्तम औषध आहे.      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40698,711,'descriptive','4a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Pongal is a South Indian festival celebrated in Tamil Nadu. It is really the harvest festival held in the month of January every year. It generally coincides with the festival of Makar Sankranti. The houses are decorated with banana and mango leaves and the floors are embellished with decorative patterns drawn using rice flour. Pongal in Tamil generally refers to festivity; more specifically Pongal means \'boiling over\' or \'overflow\', especially of milk or rice.\nThe first day is Thanksgiving day. On the second day, married women have an early bath and cook newly harvested rice in the open. While the rice is being cooked shouts of \'pongal, pongal\' are heard all over. Rice cooked with milk and jaggery (this is also called Pongal) is prepared. The third and last day is devoted to cattle. They are decorated with garlands and fed with love and affection. They are taken out in processions.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether the following statements are True or False:\n      i. Pongal is a harvest festival.\n      ii. The second day of Pongal is Thanksgiving day.\n      iii. Pongal is celebrated at the end of the harvest in September/October.\n      iv. Rice cooked with milk and jaggery is called Pongal.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        How is Pongal celebrated? \n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        Write down the root words of the following:\n        i. generally           ii. celebrated       iii. decorative          iv. taken\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. The houses are decorated with banana and mango leaves.\n             (Rewrite the sentence by using \'as well as.\')\n        ii. Pongal means \'boiling over\'.\n              (Frame a Wh-type question to get the ‘boiling over’ answer)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n        Describe your favourite festival.','','A1] i) True          ii) False          iii) False           iv) True.\nA2] The first day of Pongal is a Thanksgiving day. On the second day married women take bath early in the morning and then cook newly harvested rice in the open. People shout \'Pongal Pongal\' while the rice is being cooked. The third day is devoted to cattle. On that day cattle are decorated with garlands and fed with love and affection and then taken out in processions. Thus Pongal is celebrated joyfully for three days.\nA3] i) generally – general                  ii) celebrated - celebrate\n       iii) decorative – decorate            iv) taken - take.\nA4] i) The houses are decorated with banana as well as mango leaves.\n        ii) What does Pongal mean?\nA5] Holi is my favourite festival. It is called the festival of colours. It is celebrated with lots of joy. It is one of the happiest religious festivals of Hindus. People enjoy delicious foods and sweets on this day. The festival indicates the victory of good over evil.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40704,711,'descriptive','4a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Pahalgam is about one hundred kilometres from Srinagar in Kashmir. It is one of the most beautiful hill stations in India. It is located at a height of more than 2,100 metres in the Lidder valley of the Himalayas. It is here that in July and August pil- grims begin their Amarnath Yatra to visit the famous caves of Lord Shiva.\nThe Lidder river flows near Pahalgam and fishing is a major attraction for tourists. In November beautiful saffron plants grow all around the region. This is indeed a breathtaking sight. Tourists are seen everywhere travelling horseback or trekking up the hills. All around one can see tall pine trees and snow-capped peaks. Many tourists compare this place to beautiful Scotland in the UK. ','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following sentences by choosing the correct alternatives :\n     i. Pilgrims begin their Amarnath Yatra in the month of ------------\n        a) June and July                      b) August and September\n        c) July and August                  d) May and June\n    ii. Pahalgam is located at a height of more than ----------\n       a) 120 metres               b) 1,200 metres      c) 2,100 metres        d) 210 metres\n    iii. Pilgrims visit Amarnath to worship Lord ------------ \n         a) Ganesh           b) Vaishno Devi         c) Krishna       d) Shiva\n     iv. Tourists compare Pahalgam to beautiful ------- in the UK\n           a) Switzerland            b) Vaishno Devi         c) Scotland         d) Ireland\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Describe the beauty of ‘Pahalgam’.\n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n       Find from the passage the describing words used for the following nouns :   \n      i. -------------- hill station         ii. ------------ caves\n      iii. --------------- attraction       iv. ----------- peaks \n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       i. The Lidder river flows near Pahalgam. The sentence is in ----------\n          a) Simple present tense          b) Simple past tense\n          c) Present perfect tense.\n      ii. Tourists are seen everywhere. The sentence is in --------------\n          a) Active voice          b) Passive voice          c) Neither active nor passive.\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Write 4-5 sentences about any other hill station you have visited. \n<b>B]</b> Write a Summary of the passage given for the comprehension :','','A1] i) c) July and August          ii) c) 2100 meters \n      iii) d) Shiva                          iv) c) Scotland \nA2] Pahalgam near Srinagar in Kashmir is one of the most beautiful hill stations in India. Beautiful saffron plants grow around and that is breathtaking sight. We can see tall pine trees and snow-capped peaks all around. So it is compared to beautiful Scotland in the UK.\nA3] i) beautiful            ii) famous           iii) major        iv) snow-capped\nA4] i) a) Simple present tense          ii) Passive voice\nA5] We have recently visited Mahabaleshwar. It is situated in the Sahyadri ranges. It is cool throughout the year. It has pleasant atmosphere and natural beauty. There are number of points from where we can watch the scenery around. It is really a good tourist attraction.\nB] Heaven on the Earth Pahalgam\nPahalgam in Srinagar, Kashmir is one of the most beautiful hill stations which is located around 2,100 m in the Lidder valley of the Himalaya. The Lidder river increases the beauty of the region in which fishing is a major attraction for tourists. The surrounding is covered with beautiful plants of saffron. Tourists go on horseback rides or trekking up the hills. They can see the pine trees, snow-covered peaks and also go for Amarnath Yatra to visit the famous caves of Lord Shiva. Tourists compare Pahalgam to Scotland in UK.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40705,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Randhir/Rutuja Sane from 3/303, Ram Nagar, Khetwadi, 7th Lane, Mumbai - 400 004. You have joined a school in Mumbai. Write a letter to your friend Sadanand/Sadicha in your village, telling him about your new class and your different activities :\nYou can use the following points :\nYou like the new school – learning new and interesting things - missing fun and games of new school - read newspapers, magazines - GK books\n                                                OR\nChoose the correct word from the brackets and complete the story given below.\nPravin was paid back in his own coin\nPravin was a twelve-year-old boy. He lived on a farm with ----- (his/her) parents. ---------(One/Won) day his mother told him to ---------(fed/ feed) the animals on  the --------(farm/form). He didn\'t ---------(like/liked) the work. So, he decided to play some mischief.\nHe gave a bowl of cotton -------- (seed/sid) to the cow. The cat got --------- (sum/some) grass. The hens ------- (were/wear) given milk. The dog got grains and -------- (seeds/sides). The goats were given a bunch of bananas. He ------- (made/maid) a mess of it all and -------------- (then/than) ran away to play. \nPravin\'s mother saw this. She wanted to teach him a --------- (lesson/lesson). When Pravin returned home, he was ----------- (hungry/angry). He asked for his lunch. His mother put before him a --------- (plait/plate) of green and dried --------- (leaves/leafs) of different trees and said nothing. She didn\'t give him any food that day.\nPravin ----------- (knew/new) that he was unkind to the animals. He never played such a mischief -------- (against/again). Pravin was paid back in his own coin.','','','Randhir Sane\n3/303, Ram Nagar, \nKhetwadi, \n7th Lane, Mumbai 400 004.\n20th June, 2019\nMy dear Sadanand.\nI hope you are doing well in your school. I have joined a new school here in Mumbai. Its name is Meera High School. I am enjoying my new class. I have made some new friends here. I am learning new and interesting things. But we get a lot of homework to do. And there is no way to escape doing it. It is checked every day. We have very good teachers here.\nBut in spite of my studies and homework. I make sure not to miss on the fun and games. I take exercise regularly in the morning. I play games every evening. I also read newspapers and magazines to enrich my general knowledge. I am going to start some good hobbies. I also find time to watch some good educative programmes on TV.\nLet me know how you enjoy your new class. Give my regards to your parents.\nYour loving friend.\nRandhir\n2) Pravin was paid back in his own coin\nPravin was a twelve-year-old boy. He lived on a farm with his parents. One day his mother told him to feed the animals on the farm. He didn\'t like the work. So he decided to play some mischief.\nHe gave a bowl of cotton seed to the cow. The cat got some grass. The hens were given milk. The dog got grains and seeds. The goats were given a bunch of bananas. He made a mess of it all and then ran away to play. \nPravin\'s mother saw this. She wanted to teach him a lesson. When Pravin returned home, he was hungry. He asked for his lunch. His mother put before him a plate of green and dried leaves of different trees and said nothing. She didn\'t give him any food that day.\nPravin knew that he was unkind to the animals. He never played such a mischief again. Pravin was paid back in his own coin.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40706,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Madhu/Madhavi Surve from 5 - Visava, Kandarpada\nDahisar (West), Mumbai - 400068. Write a letter of thanks to your grandfather You can use the following points :\nYou liked the visit - liked village and village life - liked interesting stories of village and villagers - learnt many things like farming plants - Thank grandfather\n                            OR\nComplete the following story with the help of the words given in the brackets. Give a suitable title to the story.\n[piece of meat, a tree, voice, meat, nothing, laughed, compliments, to eat, wanted, do you!]\nA crow was sitting on a branch of ------ and was eating a piece of ------- with its beak. A hungry fox who was passing by wanted that ------------.\nThe fox praised the crow\'s ---------. He requested the crow to sing. The crow was happy but he knew the old story of the foolish crow. He --------- to himself and said ------------. He did not sing, of course. He continued ------ the piece of meat. Slowly he finished the meat. And then he said to the fox, \"Dear friend, thank you very much for your ----------. And now I hope you don\'t want me to sing for you, -----------? I knew what you really -------------.\" ','','','Madhu Surve\n     5, Visava, Kandarpada,\n     Dahisar (West),\nMumbai 400 068.\n20th February, 2020\nDear Aajoba,\nHow I remember you! I am writing this letter to thank you for what you have done for me during my visit to our native place in vacation. I know you will not like it.\nBut Aajoba, we really enjoyed our vacation in your loving company. We had a stroll in the village and you explained to us the ways of the village life. You told us different interesting stories of the villagers. You enlightened us, in your humorous way, on their social life The of all was how you taught us the plucking of sweet juicy mangoes. We learnt many things about local farming, plants and animals.\nWe returned to Mumbai with a lot of knowledge and many village gifts that you gave us. Thank you Aajoba.\nPlease take care of your health.\nYour loving grandson, \nMadhu\n2) A clever crow\n  A crow was sitting on a branch of a tree and was eating a piece of meat with its beak. A hungry fox who was passing by wanted that piece of meat.\nThe fox praised the crow\'s voice. He requested the crow to sing. The crow was happy but he knew the old story of the foolish crow. He laughed to himself and said nothing. He did not sing, of course. He continued eat the piece of meat. Slowly he finished the meat. And then he said to the fox, \"Dear friend, thank you very much for your compliments. And now I hope you don\'t want me to sing for you, do you? I knew what you really wanted.\" ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40707,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','You are Raman/Ragini Palande from Naupada, Thane. You have been to a social service camp at Dahanu, Palghar District, with your classmates. Write a letter to your mother with the help of the points given below and describe your activities at the camp:\nPoints: i) Time and day you reached the camp,         ii) Your daily programme,  \n    iii) Number of campers, their meals            iv) Your social service in the village \n        v) Other activities               vi) Your feelings\n\n                                       OR \nNarrate your own story with the help of the given beginning. Suggest a suitable title for it:\n[It was Friday. I was getting ready for school. Suddenly my next-door neighbour, Ramakaku came shouting for help....]','','','Raman Palande\nNaupada, Thane\n4th October, 2019\nMy dear Mother,\nI am quite fine here and hope you are all well there. We reached Dahanu at 9.00 o\'clock in the morning yesterday, and we have already started our programme here. We begin the day with prayers and then a parade. After breakfast our teachers instruct us about the day\'s programme. Though life here is hard and rules are tough, I enjoy all the activities and learn a lot from them.\nWe are 200 campers in all. But we live together like a family. We prepare our meals ourselves. Every group has to do this work by turns. Here we realize how important it is to know cooking. We dig ponds for cattle and help the villagers to build small roads in the village.\nWe are also conducting a survey to find out the lifestyle of the Adivasis living here.\nWe play games and visit interesting places in the evening. After supper we have discussions and cultural programmes. I am quite all right. Don\'t worry about me. We shall return home on 10th October, 2019. Give my regards to dear father.\nYour loving son,\nRaman\n2) How I Helped my Neighbour\nIt was Friday. I was getting ready for school. Suddenly my next-door neighbour, Ramakaku, came shouting for help. She was frightened. Her seven-year-old son, Raja, had slipped in the bathroom. He had an injury. Nobody else was there at home. So I hurried to their room.\nRaja was lying in the bathroom. He had dashed against the water-tap in the bathroom. His forehead was bleeding. He was uneasy because he was much frightened. Ramakaku did not know what to do.\nI asked Ramakaku for some cotton and a piece of clean cloth. I made Raja sit properly and then took the cotton and the clean cloth and folded it to make a pad. Then I bandaged it firmly over the wound. The wound was small but Raja was in a state of shock. I remembered the first-aid lessons. I asked Ramakaku to make tea. I gave Raja warm tea. Soon he was feeling better.\nThen Ramakaku and I took him to their family doctor in a rickshaw. The doctor examined Raja and dressed his wound. We brought him home and soon he felt better. Raja and his mother thanked me for the timely help. I missed my school but I was happy to help my neighbour.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40708,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Bajrang/Beena Patil from 4 - 12, Anant Niwas, Gokhale Road, Dadar (West), Mumbai - 400 028. Write a letter to your father with the help of the following points :\nPoints: - You received Jr. Sports Championship of your school.\n                You won important sports events.\n                Your feelings.\n                You are paying attention to your studies, too.\n                                OR\nNarrate an experience with the help of the given beginning. Suggest a suitable title for it : \nIt was Saturday. I was going to school in the morning. I was already late I was in a hurry. Just then I heard a loud noise.....','','','Bajrang Patil \n4-12, Anant Niwas, \nGokhale Road, \nDadar (West), \nMumbai - 400 028.\n24th December, 2019\nMy dear Father,\nYou will be glad to know that I have won the Jr Sports Championship of our school. There were several sports events on our Annual Sports Day. I won the first place in the 400 metres and 100 metres running race. I also got the second prize in long jump, discus throw and sack-race.\nI was thrilled when I received a beautiful trophy from our Principal. All my friends were very happy. Don\'t worry about my studies. I am taking great interest in studies, too.\nI study very hard. I hope you are all well there. Give my regards to dear Mother and love to little Guddi.\nYour loving son,\nBajrang\n2) The Accident I saw\nIt was Saturday. I was going to school in the morning. I was already late for school. So I was in a hurry. Just then I heard a loud noise. I looked back. I saw a huge crowd standing around a taxi, at the crossing. Out of curiosity, I rushed to the spot. It was an accident.\nA small girl of about eight, was knocked down by the taxi. She was injured and bleeding a little. I learnt that the poor girl was going with a pot of milk in her hand. The taxi was coming fast. The girl was crossing the road. When she saw the taxi coming, she was confused. The taxi driver could not stop the taxi in time. The taxi just brushed her a little and knocked her down.\nSoon a big crowd gathered. Parents of the girl came running from the nearby  building. A policeman also came there. We helped the injured girl to sit up. We asked the taxi-driver to take her to hospital. We helped him. The policeman also helped.\nAt the hospital a doctor examined the girl. He assured us that the wound was not serious. A nurse dressed her wound. The doctor gave her some injection. Soon she felt comfortable. The girl\'s parents thanked us. We were happy to help the poor little girl.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40709,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Manohar/Mini Patil from 103, Visava, Jawahar Nagar, Goregaon (W), Mumbai - 400 062. You had been to your friend\'s village during the summer vacation. Write a letter of thanks for what your friend and his family did for you : You can use the following points:\nthanks for hospitality - love and care given by parents - memorable visit - moments you have enjoyed.\n                     OR\nWrite a story in about 90-100 words with the help of given ending. Suggest a suitable title for it.\n..... Thus, Ganesh\'s honesty paid him rich dividends.','','','Manohar Patil,\n103, Visava, \nJawahar Nagar, \nGoregaon (W). \nMumbai - 400 062.\n14th June, 2019\nMy dear Rajan,\nI reached home safely. There was no trouble on the way.\nI write this letter to thank you for your kind hospitality during my stay at your place in the summer vacation. Everyone of you treated me as one of your family members.\nI shall never forget those sweet memories of your company. I remember how your parents were worried and cared for me when I had a slight pain in my stomach. The memory of the tasty dishes prepared by your mother still makes my mouth water.\nI remember how we wandered in the woods, climbed the hills and swam in the river. How I enjoyed the beauty of nature !\nWill you please come to stay with us in Mumbai during the Diwali vacation ? Do come, if possible with your parents.\nWith best regards to your parents,\nYour loving friend,\nManohar\n2) Honesty Rewarded\nGanesh was a young boy of 19. He could not continue his education after S.S.C. examination because he was too poor to afford to pay the college fees. He had no parents to look after him. He found a job as a peon in a jeweller\'s shop. The manager of the shop decided to test Ganesh. So one day he left a 500-rupee note on the floor in such a part of the shop that Ganesh was sure to notice it. Then the manager went out from there for some other work. \nGanesh saw the note, picked it up and as soon as the manager arrived, he returned it to him. The manager was much pleased to see his honesty. Soon he made Ganesh a clerk. With his sincerity, honesty and hard work, he reached a very high position in the shop. A few years later the owner of the shop made him a partner in his business. Thus, Ganesh\'s honesty paid him rich dividends.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40710,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Ravi/Neeta Vyas from Matruchhaya, Grant Road, Mumbai - 400 007. Your younger brother always likes to eat fast food instead of eating home-made food. Write a letter insisting him to eat home-made food. You may use the following points :\nPoints: Eating fast food - a bad habit - not good for body and health - pleasant taste but harmful effects on body contains additives, preservatives, excess oil causes ailments like obesity, kidney failure, acidity, blood pressure, etc. - eating dal-chaval and home-made food good for health - natural ingredients and made with grace and love - nourishing - eating in moderation - good eating habit.\n                                           OR\nWrite a story with the help of given ending. Suggest a suitable title for it :\n... He decided to appear for his examination again. He studied hard and passed the examination with good marks.','','','Neeta Vyas \nMatruchhaya, \nGrant Road, \nMumbai - 400 007.\n10th January 2019\nDear Raju,\nI am shocked to hear from mother that you have been always insisting on eating fast food instead of home-made food. Try to understand that it is a very bad and dangerous food habit.\nDo you know that ready-to-eat foods are loaded with additives, preservatives and excessive oil ? Chemical substances added to them are a threat to our body and health. This type of food might be pleasing your taste-buds and fill your stomach, but they create many health problems like obesity, lethargy, renal failure, acidity, heart attack and many more.\nDear Raju, eating dal-chaval is a healthier option. Home-made food is always good. nourishing and healthy. It is made with natural ingredients and is fresh. It is also made with great care, grace and love and our mother cooks delicious food.\nRemember, we eat to live. So eat in moderation. The habit of excessive eating brings disaster of diseases in your life. I expect you to start good eating habits. I know you will understand.\nLove to all at home.\nYour loving sister, \nNeeta\n2) Never give up Hope\nThere was a boy. His name was Ulhas. He failed in his S.S.C. examination. He lost all his hopes of passing the examination. He decided to give up studying any more. His parents asked him to appear for the examination again; but it was all in vain. He was so sad and disappointed that he did not wish to take the examination again.\nOnce he was sitting alone, disappointed. Suddenly, he saw a spider, trying to climb to its cobweb in the corner of the wall of his room. The spider climbed up but fell down on the floor. It tried again. It fell once and twice and then it fell again and again. But the spider did not give up hope. It tried again and again. At last it succeeded in climbing up to its cobweb. Ulhas was observing all this. He was fascinated by the efforts of the small creature. He decided to appear for his examination again. He studied hard and passed the examination with good marks.\nWe must never give up hope. We must try again and again till we succeed in our work.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40711,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Sahil/Shamita Bhosale from 55, Green Apartments, Blue Street, Akola. Some people celebrate their birthdays differently. Suppose you were inspired by someone\'s birthday celebration and celebrated your birthday in a different way. Write a letter to your friend Anil/Amita about how you did it. The following points will help you :\nPoints: Example before you - Different thinking - Different activities - Any resolution - Value/s you mentioned – Message - Your birthday celebration as a precedent to your friend/s.\n                                          OR\nWrite imaginary story ending with :\n.... It was memorable dream for me. It was a fascinating land I had ever dreamt.\nGive suitable title to the story.','','','Sahil Bhosale.\n55, Green Apartments, \nBlue Street, Akola - 444 002.\n6th October 2019\nDear Anil,\nI attended the birthday celebrations of the Mayor of our city. It was a different kind of celebration. Instead of the usual snacks or cake or lunch, the Mayor distributed food to the poor. All the people who attended the birthday celebrations were invited to help out. Later in a speech, he said that this made his birthday very meaningful.\nI was quite impressed by this and so decided to celebrate my birthday with some act of service. I spoke about my resolution to daddy and he quite liked the idea. So I sent a letter to all my friends telling them of my plans. On that day, all my friends came with different dishes. Mummy prepared yummy vegetable biryani. Then we went to the nearest orphanage. Daddy had already informed them of my intention and the authorities were waiting for us.\nWe spent some time with the orphans and heard them sing and watched them dance. Then we served lunch to them on plantain leaves. We were eight friends in all, so there were eight different dishes. The joy on the faces of the orphans was more than the joy I had felt for all my birthdays put together.\nWe went home quite excited and my friends resolved that they would do the same for their birthdays. Whatever money we spend on giving each other presents, we will use to prepare some food to serve someone who is very much in need of it. What better birthday celebration can there be than to be of service to others?\nI hope this sets a precedent for you too.\nYour loving friend,\nSahil Bhosale\n2) In the World of Aliens\nIt was month of May. I enjoyed the movie \"Koi Mil Gaya\". I liked the movie very much. It was based on the imaginary world of aliens. Thinking about the movie I went to sleep. As I was sleeping peacefully, I noticed that a UFO had landed at our backyard. As soon as I saw it, I was eager to see it from inside. In a minute, I was surprised to see three aliens coming out. They were looking strange. They were using extraordinary language. I was not only thrilled but also frightened to see them. \nTo my own surprise, they started walking towards our house. I was very much scared to see them. They entered inside the house and took me out. They took me inside the UFO. It started flying. I was quite happy when I found them harmless. After some time the UFO landed at an unknown place. It was full of aliens. It was based on the high tech world. Everything was automatic and based on electronics. The moment I landed on this land, I was pretty surprised to see the unknown world. I could see all aliens working everywhere in that high tech world. But when I found that there was neither food nor water I was scared. It was a scary place.\nWatching everything scary around, I started shouting loudly. As soon as I heard my own voice, I got up from deep sleep. I was surprised to see myself sitting on the bed completely sweating. I realized where I am! I started laughing loudly when I realized about the reality and the great dream. It was a memorable dream for me. It was a fascinating land I had ever dreamt.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40712,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Suraj/Swara Madhavi from 11, Sapna Apartments, S. B. Marg, Akola - 444 001. Write a letter of apology to your friend Sakshi for having misbehaved with him or her. You may use the points given below :\nPoints: a) argued with your friend        b) said hurtful things, called a lot of names       c) admit you behaved badly         d) sincerely sorry, apology    e) friendship too valuable to be broken\n                                           OR\nWrite a story about a situation when you became the laughing stock of your friends. Suggest a suitable title to it :','','','Swara Madhavi,\n11, Sapna Apartments,\nS.B. Marg, Akola - 444 001.\n16th October 2019\nDear Sakshi,\nThe last time we met it was not in so friendly a spirit. We argued about many things and said many hurtful things to each other. The matter went up to the teacher and class was disturbed. I have been thinking about the incident, and I must admit I behaved very badly. I called you a lot of names, which I had no right to do.\nI am writing this letter, because I cannot face you. I sincerely feel sorry for my behaviour, and would like you to forgive me. Our friendship is too valuable to be broken by an incident so silly as this. Please say that you have accepted my apology and let us be friends again. I shall take care, in future, to see that such a thing does not happen.\nWe have had good times together and have shared many wonderful experiences. I don\'t think we should throw it all away over such a petty issue. If you are willing, I could come over to your house when you want or you could come over. You are welcome anytime, any day!\nDo tell me that you have forgiven me.\nYour truly apologetic friend, \nSwara Madhavi\n2) Just for Fun!\nIt was the first of April and I was late for school. In my hurry, I hadn\'t realized the significance of the date. My teacher looked at me sternly as I entered the class, but didn\'t say anything. I knew that she was angry with me because of what had happened the previous day in class. Teacher had gone out of class for a while and Shubha had pinched me, just for fun. I retaliated by tickling her. She started laughing loudly and begged me to stop. Just then the teacher walked in and gave both of us a good piece of her mind. She even threatened to report us to the headmaster.\nDuring lunch break, the head girl of the class came up to me and said, \"Sumita, the headmaster wants to see you in the office right away.\"\nI was panic-stricken. Would he ask me to call for my parents? Would he suspend me from school? I was terrified.\nTrembling with fear, I went to the office and knocked.\n\"Come in,\" I heard the sharp voice of the headmaster.\nI walked in diffidently. \"Yes, Sir,\" I said, my voice shaking nervously, \"You called for me.\"\n\"You\'re Sumita, aren\'t you?\" I nodded my head. \"No, I didn\'t call you. Whoever gave you that idea ?\"\n\"But-but- Aarti, the head girl told me-\"\n\"Child,\" his voice rose in anger, \"Don\'t you know that today is April Fool\'s Day ?\nYou\'ve been made a fool of. I have good mind to call in Aarti and tell her to keep her April Fool jokes out of my office.\"\n\"Sorry, Sir,\" I said and walked out, embarrassed and angry. All my friends were laughing uncontrollably.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40713,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Saurabh/Surabhi Sawant from 10, Aashiyana, M. G. Road, Shivaji Nagar, Pune - 411 005. You are going to participate in an inter. school football match. You want to submit bona fide certificate of your school. Write a letter to the Headmaster of your school and request him/her to issue a bona fide certificate.\n                                                OR\nNarrate how did you enjoy your school picnic. Suggest a catering title :\n[Points: Saturday - Matheran - beautiful - tourist resort - hilly region - birds - trees - wild animals - enjoy beauty of the nature and journey - stay - visit to interesting places - weather - horse riding - refresh - return happily.]','','','Saurabh Sawant, \n10, Aashiyana, \nM. G. Road, \nShivaji Nagar, \nPune - 411 005.\n21st July, 2019.\nTo\nThe Headmaster, \nModern High school, \nShivaji Nagar,\nPune.\nSub.: Request for bona fide certificate\nRespected Sir/Madam,\nI am Saurabh Sawant studying in Std. VIII/B in your school. My role number is 28. You know that I have been playing football from last two years in our school\'s junior football team.\nNow interschool football competitions are being held by district sports authority. They need bona fide certificate of each and every player who is participating in the competition. I am also representing our team, so I too need the bona fide certificate including date of birth on it.\nI hope you will kindly issue the certificate as early as possible.\nThanking you, \nYours obediently, \nSaurabh Sawant Std. VIII/B\nRoll No. 28.\n2) Our school picnic\nLast Saturday our school arranged a picnic to Matheran. About a hundred students joined in the picnic. Four of our teachers and two peons were there with us. We went by train.\nMatheran is a beautiful tourists resort. The journey to Matheran itself is very interesting. From Neral there is a mini train journey to Matheran. But instead of going by the train, we walked the distance to enjoy the hilly region. On the way to Matheran we saw various kinds of birds, trees, some wild animals and enjoyed the beauty of nature. It is two hours journey from Neral.\nWe stayed in a bungalow at Matheran. We had rented it for two days. We started hiking all over Matheran from the very next day. We saw many interesting places such as the Echo Point, the Charlotte Lake, the Monkey Point, the Sunset Point, etc. We saw monkeys on trees and even on roads. The birds sang beautiful tunes.\nMatheran is a cool, hilly place. The weather is charming. We visited beautiful parks there. Many tourists and children from other schools were there. Rickshaws and horses are the means used for transport. I rode on a horse. It was a thrilling experience. We had a lot of fun during our stay at Matheran. \nWe left the picnic spot by the train to Neral and from there to Mumbai. The trip to Matheran refreshed us all. We returned home happily. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40714,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','You Amit/Amita Shirke, (Roll No. 30) are down with typhoid. Your family doctor has advised you to take complete bedrest. Write a letter to your class teacher of Std. VIII A, Saraswati Vidya Mandir, Bhandup East, Mumbai – 400 078 requesting him/her to grant you leave of absence for some days.\n                                          OR\nDescribe your friend with the help of the points given below :\n[Points: Name - age - description - nature - qualities - your relations him/her - why is he/she your good friend - your opinion about him/her.]','','','Amit Shirke,\nRoll No. 30, VIII A,\nSaraswati Vidya Mandir,\nBhandup (East), Mumbai 400 078\n22nd July, 2019.\nTo\nThe Class teacher,\nStd. VIII A,\nSaraswati Vidya Mandir,\nBhandup East,\nMumbai - 400 078.\nSub.: About 8 days leave of absence\nRespected Madam,\nI am Amit Shirke (Roll No. 30) studying in your class Std. VIII A. I have been suffering from typhoid from the last 4 - 5 days, so I could not attend school.\nMy medical tests detected Typhoid which has made me bedridden. My family doctor Mr. Joshi has advised me to take complete bedrest, so that I would recover as early as possible. So now I would not be able to attend school at least for a week or so.\nI assure you that I will surely try my best to make up the loss of my studies after my recovery. I request you to grant me a leave of absence from 23rd July 2019 to the date of my full recovery so that I could take complete rest and attend school regularly. I have also enclosed medical certificate along with this letter.\nThanking you,\nYours obediently,\nAmit Shirke,\nStd. VIII A,\nRoll No. 30.\nEnclosure Medical - certificate.\n2) My Friend\nThe name of my best friend is Heena. She is studying in my class so she is of my age. She is a smart girl having fair complexion and dimpled cheeks. She is one of the ideal students of my class. She is very punctual and comes to school at right time. She always completes her homework regularly as well as helps me too. She keeps her books and copies very clean. Her writing is very nice and she inspires and guides me to write well too.\nShe is my good friend and cares for me a lot. She behaves well and helps me always. She is my true friend because she understands me very well and cares for my every need. Our parents respect us very much and like our friendship. She is so precious to me and so I never want to lose her. Whenever I remain absent in the classroom, she helps me in completing my homework and classwork. She is much similar to me in many aspects. She never quarrels with me. She is very amusing by nature and makes me laugh by cracking jokes and talks.\nShe encourages me always to do better in the classroom and exams. We are good in sports and academic activities. She takes my opinions in her every tough situation. We always perform better in the class tests and main exams but never behave as competitors. She is very valuable to me and I value her friendship a lot. Really, she is my best and true friend. I love her very much.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40715,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Sameer/Seema Dighe, the class representative of Std. VIII A. Your class is visiting the TV Centre at Worli. Write a letter for permission to the Director, Doordarshan Kendra, Worli. Give following details in the letter:\nPoints: Why would you like to visit the TV centre? When are you going to visit?\nHow many students are there with you? What do you hope to learn? How will you maintain the discipline at the place?\n                                                 OR\nWrite a description of \'a farmer\' in about 90 - 100 words with the help of the points given below :\n[Points: Farmer - son of the soil - works hard - backward - and illiterate - rears animals use technology in agriculture - nowadays backbone of Indian economy.]','','','Seema Dighe,\nClass representative,\nStd. VIII A,\nMamta High School,\nDahisar East, Mumbai - 400 068.\n17th August 2019\nTo\nThe Director,\nDoordarshan Kendra, \nWorli, \nMumbai - 400 018.\n17th August, 2019.\nSub.: Permission to visit Doordarshan Kendra.\nRespected Sir,\nOur class would like to visit the Doordarshan Kendra at Worli in order to observe the working of the Doordarshan Kendra in general and preparation of programmes in particular. TV programmes play an important role in modern education, so we are all eager to know how the TV programmes are made and relayed.\nWe are in all 40 students of Std. VIII and would like to visit the Doordarshan Kendra on 15th of November, 2019, if it is convenient to you. Our NCC teachers Mr Patil and Mrs Jadhav will accompany us.\nI assure you on behalf of my class that your routine work will not be disturbed by our visit and we shall observe discipline and silence during our visit. This visit will give us a great deal of knowledge about the functioning of Doordarshan Kendra.\nKindly grant us the necessary permission and inform us about the suitable time when we could visit the place.\nThanking you, \nYours faithfully, \nSeema Dighe \nClass Representative \nStd. VIII-A.\n2) A Farmer\nFarming is the main occupation in India. A farmer is truly called the son of the soil as he toils in his field from morning till evening and in all seasons. He ploughs his field and keeps it ready for sowing seeds before the onset of the rainy season. Formerly he used plough and bullocks for ploughing his field but nowadays he uses modern ploughing machines and tractors. He is no longer backward and illiterate. He has changed himself with the changing times. He now uses hybrid seeds, fertilizers, insecticides and grows cereals like rice, wheat, maize and also cash crops like sugar cane and cotton. He has developed his skill in plantation.\nHe rears animals like cows, goats and sheep for additional income. He can afford to have a radio, TV, scooter and other facilities of the modern times. He uses his skill and modern agricultural know-how to improve his crops and after a lot of hard work looks forward to reap a good harvest. He is the backbone of the Indian economy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40716,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Amol/Amita Kadam, Prabhadevi, Mumbai - 400 025. Write a letter to the Branch Manager of Maharashtra State Electricity Board about the power shortage in the evenings when you want to study :\nYou can use the following points :\nPoints : Name of the area - problem the people face - what are your expectations from the Electricity Board.\n                               OR\nDescribe your hobby with the help of the questions given below :\n[Points: Have you any hobbies? What are they? Which one is your favourite hobby? Reading? When did you start reading? What benefits do you get from reading? What is its importance? When do you read books? What kind of books do you like to read? How has the hobby helped you in your life? What do you feel about your hobby?]','','','Amol Kadam,\n7, Ujala Nagar, \nPrabhadevi, \nMumbai - 400 025. \n9th September, 2019\nTo\nThe Branch Manager, \nMaharashtra State Electricity Board, \nMumbai.\nSub.: Power shortage in Prabhadevi area.\nRespected Sir,\nI am sorry to bring to your kind notice that the power shortage in Prabhadevi area has adversely affected our routine life for the last six consecutive days.\nThis power failure, especially in the evenings, disturbs the students most. We cannot study in the evening. Our examinations are fast approaching and we are helpless without electricity. We have to resort to candlelight for studies. Besides, we cannot use the fans and have to endure unbearable heat and sweating during these hot summer days. We students totally depend upon electricity during the examination period.\nKindly look into the matter urgently and put an end to this shortage of electricity, once and for all.\nThanking you, \nYours faithfully, \nAmol Kadam\n2) My Hobby - Reading\nI have some hobbies like painting, photography and trekking. But my favourite hobby is reading. My father gave me eight story books on my eighth birthday. I read them and since then I have never looked back. I went on reading. \nReading is the best hobby because it brings variety and charm in my routine life. I derive many benefits from this hobby. When I feel bored or tired, books refresh me. They are my never-failing friends. Reading gives me a lot of information, knowledge and recreation.\nOn school days I read for an hour or so, but on Sundays and holidays I read a lot. I enjoy reading in our school library. I have also joined the Marathi Granth Sangrahalaya. I read there and sometimes bring books for reading at home.\nI like to read historical stories, biographies, novels, humorous books, magazines, newspapers and all sorts of good literature. I read some serious types of books, too. Such books provide food for thought. These books are my friends, guides and teachers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40717,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Suppose you are Vijay/Vidya Saravate from Std. VIII - C, B. H. Vidyalaya, Tardeo. Write a letter to your English teacher and thank him for whatever he\nYou can use the following points:\nPoints: i) You were not good in English  ii) He/She helps to boost your moral and confidence.   iii) Create interest in English.        iv) The activities he/she has done for you          v) Thank from the bottom of your heart.\n                                              OR\nWrite a report based on the headline given below :\n        28 students from Nandadeep \n     High School bagged Pre-secondary scholarship','','','Vidya Saravate,\nVIII - C,\nB. H. Vidyalaya,\nTardeo,\nMumbai - 400 034.\n20th March, 2020\nTo\nMr. Arun Patil,\nThe English Teacher,\nVIII \'C\'\nB.H. Vidyalaya, \nTardeo,\nMumbai - 400 034.\nSub.: A letter to thank you\nDear Sir,\nI do not know how to express my deep sense of gratitude for everything you have done for us and for me in particular. For the whole year you have been working hard to see to it that we all do well in English.\nIn the beginning of the academic year our class was very weak in English. But it was you who inspired us and boosted our morale and confidence. Slowly and steadily with your remarkable skill of teaching, we improved our English. Soon we realized that English is not that difficult as we had thought it to be.\nHow painstaking you were! You created and developed our interest in English. You kept us busy in the \'English Club\' activities. You made us prepare posters, wallpapers, projects and what not. I could give a speech in English only because of your guidance and encouragement. We realised the importance of English only because of your efforts. You have always been our friend, philosopher and guide.\nOn behalf of my class VIII \'C\' and on my own behalf, I once again express my sincere thanks to you. Thank you very much, Sir. \nYour obedient student,\nVidya Saravate\n2) Khed - 28th June (By Staff Reporter) : 28 students from Nandadeep Vidyalaya, bagged pre-secondary scholarship. The examination was conducted by Maharashtra State Council of Examination, Pune on 23rd February 2019. 40 students from the school appeared for the exam and 28 out of them were selected for the scholarship. \nThe Headmaster Mr. Bhausaheb Ghadge congratulated all the successful students with a bouquet and sweets. It is the highest result in the school\'s history. The Education Inspector of the District Mr. Prabhakar Chavan congratulated the Headmaster and the teachers for this great achievement. Out of 28 students 18 are girls and 10 are boys.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40718,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Arjun/Arpita Kusumbe from Ideal English High School, Osmanabad 413 501. You require School Leaving Certificate. Write a letter to your Headmaster requesting him to issue it to you.\nTake the help of the following points :\nPoints: i) State the reason that your father is transferred.     ii) You want to take admission in new school         iii) Give details about yourself, i.e. your standard, division, year of leaving school, etc.       iv) Express your gratitude          v) Make request to do the needful.\n                                           OR\nWrite a newspaper report based on the following headline :\n        Two youngsters died in bike accident.','','','Arjun Kusumbe,\nRoll No. 38,\nStd. VIII - B\nIdeal English High School, \nDharashiv - 413 501.\n6th August 2019.\nTo\nThe Headmaster,\nIdeal English High School, \nDharashiv - 413 501.\nSub.: Application for School Leaving Certificate\nRespected Sir,\nSince my father has been transferred to Gondia, I shall be leaving this school. As I have to seek admission in another school, I request you to issue me the School Leaving Certificate at the earliest.\nDetails about me are as under:\nName: Arjun Kusumbe\nClass: VIII В\nRoll No.: 38\nThanking you, \nYour obedient student, \nArjun Kusumbe\n2) Pune: (By Staff Reporter) – Yesterday evening on 26th February two youngsters died on the spot in a bike accident near Vishrant Wadi.\nThe boy was riding bike over 120 speed on crowded road near Vishrant Wadi dashed into an overloaded truck. He could not control the speed and both of them died on the spot. The people took them to hospital but were declared brought dead. They were about 19 - 20 years old. Such incidents are taking place everywhere. The local corporator, Mr. Manohar Parab visited the hospital and expressed his concern and consoled the family. Both of them were college students studying in Std. XII.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40719,712,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are Dinkar/Divya Pandit from Saikripa Chawl, Samta Nagar, Rambaug, Gangapur - 431 109. There have been many incidents of robberies in your village/ city. People in the locality are quite scared. Write a letter to the Police Inspector. You may use the following points:\nPoints : i) What do the people talk about the robbers?      ii) What do the people do to protect themselves from the robbers?      iii) Request the Inspector to keep a night watch         iv) Add any other suitable ideas of your own.\n                                                  OR\nWrite a report on the First Aid Course organised by \'Manav Seva Kendra\' at Dahisar. You may use the following points:\n[Points : date – place participants - time and duration - practical training - lectures by nurses and doctors - discussions - certificates - free First Aid kits.]','','','Divya Pandit,\nSaikripa Chawl,\nSamta Nagar,\nRambaug,\nGangapur - 431 109.\n17th November 2019\nTo\nThe Police Inspector, \nRambaug, \nGangapur - 431 109.\nSub.: Complaint regarding robberies in Rambaug\nDear Sir,\nHope you are aware of the robberies taking place in the Rambaug area of this town. In two out of the five cases, residents of the houses that were robbed were attacked by the robbers. One is in a serious condition at the Sanjeevani Hospital. The people in this locality are really scared. The robbers seem to be quite emboldened by the inaction on the part of the authorities. They would not be so daring, unless they were sure they could get away with their crimes.\nIf a night watch was kept in our locality, it would help a great deal. Please treat the matter urgently as our very lives are in danger. Thanking you,\nYours truly,\nDivya Pandit\n2) A Report on the First Aid Course at Dahisar\nBy Staff Reporter \n15th August 2019, \nDahisar, Mumbai.\n\'Manav Seva Kendra\' had organized a First Aid Course for the students of Dahisar area. 50 students from different local schools attended the course at \'Manav Kalyan Kendra Hall\', Dahisar. The spacious hall was suitable for all the activities during the course. The course was conducted for a week. It was a busy schedule from morning to evening. There were lectures by famous doctors and nurses of the hospitals in Mumbai. The students were made aware of the importance of the First Aid. They had a lot of practical training in the First Aid. There were discussions and film shows on the subject. At the end of the course all the participants were awarded certificates and free First Aid kits.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40720,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a meaningful dialogue from the jumbled sentences:\n    a) Why not? What are friends for?\n    b) You are welcome.\n    c) Can you help me in my studies?\n    d) Thank you very much.\n                       OR\n   Information Transfer – Verbal to Non – Verbal\nRead the following passage and complete the table given below it.\nThere are two true friends of a farmer. One is a bull and the other is a dog. Both help the farmer in his work. A bull is a very strong animal. It helps a farmer in ploughing, sowing and reaping.\nA dog is also a friend of a farmer. It is a faithful ani- mal. It guards his field and crop. It guards his house too. ','','','    a) Can you help me in my studies?\n    b) Why not? What are friends for?\n    c) Thank you very much.\n    d) You are welcome.\n2) ','',1,0,1,NULL),(40721,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a meaningful dialogue from the jumbled sentences:\n    a) Oh! Thanks a lot.\n     b) Take this pen.\n     c) It\'s your birthday gift.\n     d) What is it for?\n                               OR\nInformation Transfer – Non – Verbal to Verbal\nRead the information given in the table and write two paragraphs.','','','    a) Take this pen.\n    b) What is it for?\n    c) It\'s your birthday gift.\n    d) Oh! Thanks a lot.  \n2) Two Brothers\nRam and Shyam are two brothers. Their father\'s name is Keshav and Mother\'s name is Sindhu.\nRam is 15 years old. He is an elder brother. On the other hand Shyam is 10 years old and he is a younger brother. Ram is in Std. IX and Shyam is in Std. IV. Ram is 140 cms tall whereas Shyam is 100 cms tall. Ram is taller than Shyam. Reading story-books is Ram\'s hobby. On the other hand Shyam likes playing cricket. Ram is a very calm boy, On the contrary Shyam is a very naughty boy. Though they are the sons of the same parents, they are two poles apart.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40722,714,'descriptive','6',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi ------------ 1M\n      i. valley           ii. highway       \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------- 2M\n     i. Keep social distancing\n     ii. I am 14 years old.','','','A] i) दरी           ii) महामार्ग           \nB] i) सुरक्षित अंतर ठेवा.           ii) मी 14 वर्षाचा आहे.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40723,714,'descriptive','6',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi ------------ 1M\n      i. liveliest                      ii. lake\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------- 2M\n     i. The sky is blue.\n     ii. Corona is a highly infectious disease.','','','A] i) सर्वात तजेलदार                      ii) तळे   \nB] i) आकाश निळे असते                ii) कोरोना हा अत्यंत संपर्कजन्य रोग आहे. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40724,714,'descriptive','6',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi ------------ 1M\n      i. crew          ii. virus           \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------- 2M\n     i. Please switch on the light.\n      ii. It is 15 kms away from here.','','','A] i) खलासी            ii) विषाणू          \nB] i) कृपया लाईट लावा                  ii) ते इथून १५ किमी अंतरावर आहे. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40725,714,'descriptive','6',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi ------------ 1M\n      i. traffic                            ii. size\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------- 2M\n     i. Cover your nose and mouth with mask.\n      ii. He left for Pune yesterday.','','','A] i) रहदारी                               ii) आकार\nB] i) तुमचे नाक व तोंडावर मास्क बांधा               ii) काल तो पुण्याला गेला.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40726,714,'descriptive','6',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi ------------ 1M\n      i. slave           ii. obey         \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------- 2M\n    i. Stand in a queue.\n    ii. Enter one after another in bus.','','','A] i) गुलाम             ii) आज्ञा पाळणे            \nB] i) रांगेत उभे रहा                ii) बस मध्ये एकापाठोपाठ एकाने प्रवेश करा.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(40727,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a meaningful dialogue from the jumbled sentences :\n    a) Yes. I do.\n    b) Why do you like poems?\n    c) because they given us joy and pleasure.\n    d) Do you like poems?\n                                            OR\nInformation Transfer – Non – Verbal to Verbal \nRead the following diagram and write 10 – 12 sentences on it.','','','a) Do you like poems?\n    b) Yes. I do.\n    c) Why do you like poems?\n    d) Because they give us joy and pleasure.\n2) Keshav is the family holder of the kakade family. The name of his wife is Sindhu. They have two sons and no daugh- ter. The elder son is Kuldeep. He is a teacher in a primary school. His wife Aishwarya is also a teacher. They have a son. His name is Shlok. He is 4 years old.\nOn the other hand Sumit is the younger son. He is an engineer. He is a bachelor. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(40728,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a meaningful dialogue from the jumbled sentences :\n    * I like to drink milk.\n    * No, I don\'t.\n    * Do you like to drink tea ?\n    * What do you like to drink then? \n                           OR\nInformation Transfer – Non – Verbal to Verbal\nWrite a paragraph on ‘The Use of Mobile Phones’ with the help of the following diagram :','','','* Do you like to drink tea ?\n* No, I don\'t.\n* What do you like to drink then.\n* I like to drink milk.\n2) The Use of Mobile Phones\nThe mobile phone is a wonderful modern invention. It has many uses. Its most important use is as a phone that you can use wherever you go. It also has a voice mail facility which helps you record phone calls. Besides it has the facility of a camera whereby you can click pictures. It also has the facility of FM radio and downloading music. You can send short SMS messages by the mobile phone. Social media nowadays let us be in touch with our friends and relatives. A recent facility is that mobile phones can be connected to a television and so you can see videos, movies of your choice on mobile phones directly and so you can also avail of the Internet facility. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(40729,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a meaningful dialogue from the jumbled sentences :\n    * We should wear mask and keep social distancing.\n    * It is the result of our negligence.\n    * How\'s that?\n    * Corona is again spreading rapidly.\n                                 OR\nInformation Transfer – Non – Verbal to Verbal \nThe table given below gives the information about the awards that the students have received. Write a short paragraph using the information.','','','* Corona is again spreading rapidly.\n* It is the result of our negligence.\n* How is that?\n* We should wear masks and keep social distancing.\n2) Two gems - Abhiman and Sanjana\nThe table shows the information about two award winners. They have received these awards on 26th January, 2013. Abhiman is 18 years old. He is studying in higher secondary. He has received the bravery award at the hands of the district collector. Sanjana who is a secondary school student and the recipient of Bal Puraskar on the same day. She has got this award at the hands of Tahasildar at Taluka level. This is a very prestigious award for her at this secondary level. Both of them are talented students.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40730,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a meaningful dialogue from the jumbled sentences :\n      * Don\'t mention it.\n      * Thanks a lot.\n      * With great pleasure. Please take it.\n      * Will you please give me your pencil?\n                           OR\nInformation Transfer – None – Verbal to Verbal \nThe pie – chart below shows the percentage of Smartphone market share in India. Write a short paragraph using the information :','','','* Will you please give me your pencil?\n* With great pleasure. Please take it.\n* Thanks a lot.\n* Don\'t mention it. \n2) The Percentage of Smartphone Market\nThe pie-chart gives us the information about the Smartphone market in India. It shows the percentages of 5 main competitors and the others in the Smartphones market share.\nSamsung has 26% of the total market share which is the highest among all other Smartphone companies. Next to Samsung, Micromax holds 22% of the market share. Karbon Smartphones has the share of 13% which is higher than Sony and Nokia which hold 5% each of the market share. Remaining 29% of share is owned by other companies. It looks that nearly 50 per cent of the Smartphone market has been captured by the products of Samsung and Micromax Smartphones. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(40731,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following dialogue : \n    A : Has anyone ever helped you in your need?\n    B : ------------------------------------------------------\n    A : How did you feel at that time?\n    B : ------------------------------------------------\n                                        OR\nInformation Transfer – None – Verbal to Verbal \nYou have visited a tourist place. you liked it very much. use the points given in the table and write a paragraph.','','','A: Has anyone ever helped you in your need?\nB : Yes, my friend Ajeet helped me to complete my homework when I was absent in the school for a week.\nA : How did you feel at that time?\nB : I really felt happy and satisfied.\n2) Kolhapur - The place of Tourist attraction\nKolhapur is one of the most attractive places in Maharashtra. It is famous for Mahalaxmi temple. People from all over Maharashtra and neighbouring states visit this religious place. It is convenient to travel by bus or train. Dharmashalas and affordable hotels are available for stay. Many tourists visit Rajwada, Rankala Lake, Mahalaxmi temple. Panhala and Jyotiba are other close places to visit. They are very popular in Maharashtra. Panhala and Jyotiba are situated on the top of the mountain. Kolhapur is one of the best market places. People can buy Kolhapuri chappals and traditional ornaments. People also enjoy non-veg food. Being historical and religious, Kolhapur has got recognition therefore so many people visit the place. It has become the place of great attraction for the tourists all over India.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40732,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following dialogue : \n    A : What is the best gift that you have ever received?\n    B : ------------------------------------------------------------\n    A : Why is it special for you?\n    B : ---------------------------------------------------------------\n                                  OR\nInformation Transfer – Non – Verbal to Verbal \nWrite a short paragraph using the information given in the following web :','','','A : What is the best gift that you have ever received?\nB : My life given by my parents.\nA : Why is it special for you?\nB : Because I could see this beautiful world.\n2) Success - The fruit of hardwork\nEveryone is hungry for success. But it cannot be achieved without any efforts. The success of any student depends upon many factors. The most important factor is his regularity in school. If he/she respects the teachers and seeks proper guidance from them, it is quite easy for him/her to prepare any subject. His/Her family background, good friends\' circle are also considered to be very important in his/her success. It affects his growth if both the factors are not good. Students should know how to overcome any kind of difficulties. He/She should eat good and balanced diet to keep himself/herself physically and mentally fit. He/She should develop the reading habit to gain information and knowledge. He/She should be regular in his/her work and his/her studies and follow a regular timetable. If a student follows the above guidelines in his/ her life, definitely he/she would be successful in his/her academics and in his/her life as well.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40733,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following dialogue :\n     A : Do you think poem can inspire us?\n     B : ----------------------------------------------\n     A : Do you remember any such inspiring poem?\n     B : -------------------------------------------------------\n                       OR\nInformation Transfer : Non – Verbal to Verbal \nThe pie- chart below shows the percentage of the persons in India watching TV programmes. Write a short paragraph using the information :','','','A : Do you think poem can inspire us?\nB : Absolutely yes.\nA : Do you remember any such inspiring poem?\nB : Yes, it is \"You can.\"\n2) Percentage of viewers watching TV Programmes\nThe pie-chart shows the information about persons watching TV programmes in India. It shows the percentage of the persons in India watching different TV serials and programmes. 50% of total TV viewers are women. They mainly watch family serials. 30% students watch different programmes. Students love to watch cricket matches, Discovery channel and films. There are 10% men who watch news and quiz. Men hardly get time to watch other serials. Some religious serials are watched by senior citizens. The number of men who watch TV serials are comparatively less than women. Watching family serials is the best pastime among the masses in India. Watching TV is a good source of entertainment.','',1,0,0,NULL),(40734,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following dialogue :\n       A : What is your father?\n       B : ----------------------------------------\n       A : And your mother?\n       B : ---------------------------------------------\n                                  OR\nInformation Transfer : Verbal to non-Verbal \nRead the following paragraph and complete the tree – diagram putting the correct information.\nThere are different kinds of fuels. Broadly they can be divided as solid, liquid and gaseous fuels. Solid fuels include substances such as wood, coal and coke. Most liquid fuels are obtained from petroleum which is obtained from oil wells sunk deep into the earth. Petrol, kerosene and diesel are all separated from petroleum. The most important gaseous fuels are natural gas coal and water gas. Natural gas is a fuel that is widely used for cooking and heating.','','','A : What is your father?\nB : My father is a teacher.\nA : And your mother ?\nB : She is also a teacher.\n2) ','',1,0,1,NULL),(40735,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following dialogue :\n       Kuldeep : What is your hobby ?\n       Sumit : ------------------------------------\n       Kuldeep : What kind of books do you like to read?\n        Sumit : ---------------------------------------------\n                             OR\nInformation Transfer : Verbal  to non – Verbal\nComplete the given table with the information from the passage :\nThere are four different types of soils. They are laterite soil, sandy desert soil, black cotton soil and alluvial soil. The formation of laterite soil takes place due to the washing of silica, salt, organic matter and \'accumulation of sesquioxides\'. Sandy desert soil, on the other hand, is formed due to the action of winds. Mechanical weathering of igneous rocks leads to the formation of black cotton soil. Last but not the least, is the rich alluvial soil that is obtained through the depositing process of rivers.','','','Kuldeep : What is your hobby?\nSumit : Reading is my hobby.\nKuldeep : What type of books do you like to read?\nSumit :  I like adventurous stories to read.\n2) ','',1,0,1,NULL),(40736,713,'descriptive','5b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following dialogue :\n       Shlok : What is social distancing, Mother?\n       Aishwarya : -------------------------------------------\n       Shlok : But why should we keep social distance?\n       Aishwarya : -----------------------------------------------\n                                     OR\nInformation Transfer : Verbal to non – Verbal\nUse the information given below and prepare a table comparing child labourer and normal child :\nA child labourer has to work hard to earn his living, parents send him to work. He cannot go to school. He has no proper home. Some live in slum area. Parents cannot pay attention to them. A child labourer has no dreams of happy life. He cannot pay attention to studies. He cannot hope for it. He has no hobbies as he has no spare time. His future is not bright.\nA normal child is lucky enough to enjoy childhood. He has toys and friends. He gets nice clothes. He goes to school. He is safe and sound at home. Parents guide him and look after his health. He can spend money on hobby. He has high hopes. He can dream of a happy time.','','','Shlok : What is social distancing, Mother ?\nAishwarya : keeping safe distance between two.\nShlok : But why should we keep social distance ?\nAishwarya : Because Covid-19 is a highly infectious disease.\n2) ','',0,0,1,NULL),(40737,714,'descriptive','6',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi ------------ 1M\n      i. starve            ii. coward\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------- 2M\n     i. We should talk politely.\n     ii. A stitch in time, saves nine.','','','A] i) उपाशी ठेवणे                             ii)  भित्रा\nB] i) आपण नम्रतेने बोलले पाहिजे \n     ii) वेळेवर केलेल्या उपायाने संभाव्य मोठी हानी ठरते. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40738,714,'descriptive','6',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi ------------ 1M\n      i. crime            ii. intension           \nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------- 2M\n     i. Keep your surroundings clean.\n      ii. Do not spit in public place.','','','A] i) गुन्हा            ii) हेतू             \nB] i) आपला परिसर स्वच्छ ठेवा             ii) सार्वजनिक ठिकाणी थुंकू नका.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(40739,714,'descriptive','6',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A] Translate the following words into Marathi ------------ 1M\n      i. roam          ii. freedom\nB] Translate the following sentences into Marathi ---------- 2M\n     i. The teacher greeted me with a smile.\n      ii. Do not make friendship with a stranger. ','','','A] i) भटकणे              ii) स्वातंत्र्य\nB] i) शिक्षकेने माझे स्मित हास्य आणि स्वागत केले.\n     ii)  अनोळखी व्यक्तीशी मैत्री करू नका.','',0,0,0,NULL),(40740,714,'descriptive','6',3,2,'Out of Text Book','',