2,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The solution of y + 3 = 8/3 is -----\na) 1/3 		b) -1/3 		c) 17/3 		d) -17/3\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) -1/3','',0,0,0,NULL),(41793,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : 4k – 2 + 3k² - 5k + 6\na) k² + k + 4 		b) 3k² - k + 4 	          c) 3k² - 9k + 4 	d) 3k² - 9k – 4 \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) 3k² - k + 4 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41794,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the value of -25 + 4c²/6 when c = 12\na) 24 			b) 71 			c) 29 			d) 35\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) 71 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41795,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The product of 2x and y is equal to -----\na) 2xy 		b) -2xy 		c) 4xy 			d) -4xy\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) 2xy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41796,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the sides of a triangle are 3p, 4p – 2 and 5p + 1. Find the perimeter of the triangle.\na) 9p – 3 		b) 7p – 1 		c) 12p – 1 		d) 8p – 3 \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) 12p – 1 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41797,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the perimeter of a triangle is 8x – 7 and two sides are 3x + 5 and 2x – 3, find the third side.\na) 7x – 9 		b) 3x – 6 		c) 6x – 7 		d) 3x – 9 \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','d) 3x – 9 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41798,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','John covers x cm in one step. How many centimeters does he cover in 9 steps?\na) x/9 			b) 9x 			c) 18x 		d) x + 9\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) 9x 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41799,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The coefficient of y² in -63x³y²z is -----\na) -63 		b) -63z 		c) -63x³z 		d) -63y²\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) -63x³z ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41800,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of a rectangle is 7x – 3y and its breadth is 3x + 4y. The perimeter of the rectangle is ------\na) 13x + 5y 		b) 17x – 2y 		c) 20x + 2y 		d) 10x + y\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) 20x + 2y 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41801,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The HCF of 14x³y² and 21y³z² is -----\na) 7xyz 		b) 7x²y 		c) 7y² 		d) xyz\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) 7y² 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41802,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 7x + 3 = 17, then what is the value of 7x – 3?\na) 14 		b) 11 		c) 0 		d) -3\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) 11 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41803,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 5x – 3/4 = 2x – 2/3, then the value of x is ------\na) 1/12 		b) 1/4 		c) 1/36 		d) 36\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) 1/36 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41804,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If (2n + 5) = 3(3n – 10), then the value of n is ------\na) 5 			b) 3 			c) 2/5 		d) 2/3\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) 5 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41805,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve for x : 18 – (2x – 12) = 8x \na) 15 			b) 26 		c) 3 			d) 13/5\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) 3 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41806,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve for m : 3m + 2(m + 2) = 20 – (2m – 5) \na) 2 			b) 3 		c) 5 			d) 6\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) 3 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41807,731,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The statement “Thrice a number x decreased by 5 is equal to 27” is expressed in equation as ----\na) 5 – 3x = 27 		b) 3x – 5 = 27 	               c) x/3 – 5 = 27           	d) 3x – 27 + 5 = 0  \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) 3x – 5 = 27 	  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41808,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : -3x – 8x + 5x \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','-6x','',0,0,0,NULL),(41809,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : 2/3 ab – 5/7 ab\n \nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','-1/21 ab','',0,0,0,NULL),(41810,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : -2p² + 7p² \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','5p²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41811,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : 9p + 16q ; 13p + 2q\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','9p + 16q + 13p + 2q = 22p + 18q','',0,0,0,NULL),(41812,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : 2a + 6b + 8c ; 16a + 13c + 18b\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','2a + 6b + 8c + 16a + 13c + 18b = 18a + 24b + 21c','',0,0,0,NULL),(41813,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subtract the second expression from the first : (4xy – 9z) ; (3xy – 16z)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','(4xy – 9z) - (3xy – 16z) = 4xy – 9z – 3xy + 16z = xy + 7z','',0,0,0,NULL),(41814,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiply: 3x × 12y \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','36xy','',0,0,0,NULL),(41815,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiply : (-12x) × (3y²)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','-36xy²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41816,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiply : 2a² × 3ab²\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','6a³b²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41817,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiply : (-3x²) × (-4xy)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','12x³y','',0,0,0,NULL),(41818,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiply : x (x + y)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','x² + xy','',0,0,0,NULL),(41819,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiply : (7x – 6y) × 3z\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','21xz – 18yz','',0,0,0,NULL),(41820,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : x + 7 = 4\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','x = 4 – 7 = -3 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41821,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : 4p = 12\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','p = 3','',0,0,0,NULL),(41822,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : m – 5 = 4\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','m = 4 + 5 = 9','',0,0,0,NULL),(41823,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : t/3 = 6\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','t = 6 × 3 = 18','',0,0,0,NULL),(41824,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : 10 = 2y + 5\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','2y = 10 – 5 = 5     ∴ y = 5/2 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41825,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : (6a – 5b – 8c) + (15b + 2a – 5c)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','8a + 10b – 13c','',0,0,0,NULL),(41826,731,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve :  4(x + 12) = 8\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','x + 12 = 2     ∴ x = 2 – 12 = -10','',0,0,0,NULL),(41827,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : 13x² - 12y² ; 6x² - 8y²\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','13x² - 12y² + 6x² - 8y² = 19x² - 20y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41828,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : 17a²b² + 16c ; 28c – 28a²b²\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','17a²b² + 16c + 28c – 28a²b² = -11a²b² + 44c','',0,0,0,NULL),(41829,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : 3y² - 10y + 16 ; 2y – 7 \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','3y² - 10y + 16 + 2y – 7 = 3y² - 8y + 9','',0,0,0,NULL),(41830,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add : -3y² + 10y – 16 ; 7y² + 8\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','-3y² + 10y – 16 + 7y² + 8 = 4y² + 10y – 8 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41831,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subtract the second expression from the first : 16x + 23y + 12z ; 9x – 27 y + 14z\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','16x + 23y + 12z – (9x – 27 y + 14z)\n= 16x + 23y + 12z – 9x + 27y – 14z = 7x + 50y – 2z \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41832,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subtract the second expression from the first : (5xy + 4y + 7z) ; (x + 2y + 3z)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','(5xy + 4y + 7z) - (x + 2y + 3z)\n= 5xy + 4y + 7z – x – 2y – 3z = 5xy + 2y + 4z\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41833,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subtract the second expression from the first : (14x² + 8xy + 3y²) ; (26x² - 8xy – 17y²)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(14x² + 8xy + 3y²) - (26x² - 8xy – 17y²)\n= 14x² + 8xy + 3y² - 26x² + 8xy + 17y²\n= -12x² + 16xy + 20y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41834,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiply : (3x + 4y) × (5x + 7y)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','(3x + 4y) × (5x + 7y) = 3x(5x + 7y) + 4y(3x + 5y)\n= 15x² + 21xy + 12xy + 20y²\n= 15x² + 33xy + 20y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41835,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of a rectangular field whose length is (2x + 7) m and breadth is (x + 2) m.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of rectangle = length × breadth\n∴ Area of rectangle = (2x + 7)(x + 2) \n= 2x(x + 2) + 7(x + 2) = 2x² + 4x + 7x + 14\n= 2x² + 11x + 14','',0,0,0,NULL),(41836,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiply : i) 16xy × 18xy 		ii) 23xy² × 4yz²\n\nAns. : -------------------------------              -------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------              ---------------------------------\n\n            ------------------------------             ----------------------------------','','','i) 16xy × 18xy = 288x²y²\nii) 23xy² × 4yz² = 92xy³z²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41837,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : i) 2x + 2 = 8 		ii) 3x – 5 = x – 17 \n\nAns. : -------------------------------              -------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------              ---------------------------------\n\n            ------------------------------             ----------------------------------','','','i) 2x + 2 = 8      ⟹ 2x = 8 – 2 = 6    ⟹ x = 3\nii) 3x – 5 = x – 17   ⟹ 3x – x = -17 + 5   ⟹ 2x = -12\n∴ x = -6','',0,0,0,NULL),(41838,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of a rectangle is 1 cm more than twice its breadth. If the perimeter of the rectangle is 50 cm, find its length.','','','Let breadth of rectangle be x cm then length = 2x + 1\nPerimeter of rectangle = 2[l + b]\n∴ 50 = 2[(2x + 1) + x]    ⟹ 3x + 1 = 25     ⟹ 3x = 24\n∴ x = 8\n∴ length = 2x + 1 = 2(8) + 1 = 17 cm and breadth = x = 8 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(41840,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : i) 6x – 1 = 3x + 8 		ii) 2(x – 4) = 4x + 2\n\nAns. : -------------------------------              -------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------              ---------------------------------\n\n            ------------------------------             ----------------------------------','','','i) 6x – 3x = 8 + 1    ∴ 3x = 9     ∴   x = 3\nii) 2(x – 4) = 4x + 2      ⟹ 2x – 8 = 4x + 2 \n∴ 2x – 4x = 2 + 8     ⟹ -2x = 10     ⟹ x = -5','',0,0,0,NULL),(41841,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : (3x + 2y) (7x – 8y)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','3x(7x – 8y) + 2y(7x – 8y)\n= 21x² - 24xy + 14xy – 16y² \n= 21x² - 10xy – 16y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41842,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : (7m – 5n) – (-4n – 11m)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','7m – 5n + 4n + 11m = 18m – n ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41843,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : (11m – 12n + 3p) – (9m + 7n – 8p)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','11m – 12n + 3p – 9m – 7n + 8p \n= 2m – 19n + 11p\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41844,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : 4(x + 12) = 8\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','x + 12 = 2       ∴   x = 2 – 12 = -10','',0,0,0,NULL),(41845,731,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : 3y + 4 = 5y – 6 \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ------------------------------  ----------------------------------','','','3y – 5y = -6 – 4 \n-2y = -10       ∴ y = 5\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41846,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subtract the second expression from the first : (6x² + 7xy + 16y²) ; (16x² - 17xy)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','(6x² + 7xy + 16y²) - (16x² - 17xy) \n= 6x² + 7xy + 16y² - 16x² + 17xy = -10x² + 24xy +16y²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41847,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subtract the second expression from the first : (4x + 16z) – (19y – 14z + 16x)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','(4x + 16z) – (19y – 14z + 16x)\n= 4x + 16z – 19y + 14z – 16x = -12x + 30z – 19y \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41848,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiply : i) (12a + 17b) × 4c 		ii) (4x + 5y) × (9x + 7y)\n\nAns. : -----------------------------               ---------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------               ----------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------                ----------------------------------','','','i) (12a + 17b) × 4c = 48ac + 68bc\nii) 4x(9x + 7y) + 5y(9x + 7y)\n= 36x² + 28xy + 45xy + 35y² = 36x² + 73xy + 35y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41849,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A rectangle is (8x + 5) cm long and (5x + 3) cm broad. Find its area.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of rectangle = l × b = (8x + 5) × (5x + 3)\n= 8x(5x + 3) + 5(5x + 3) = 40x² + 24x + 25x + 15\n= 40x² + 49x + 15','',0,0,0,NULL),(41850,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The sum of two consecutive natural numbers is 69. Find the numbers.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let two consecutive natural number be x and x+ 1\n∴ x + x + 1 = 69      ⟹  2x = 69 – 1 = 68\n∴ x = 34\n∴ The numbers are x = 34 and x + 1 = 34 + 1 = 35','',0,0,0,NULL),(41851,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : i) 5(x + 1) = 74 		ii) 3 (2m – 1) = 12\n\nAns. : -----------------------------               ---------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------               ----------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------                ----------------------------------','','','i) 5x + 5 = 74      ⟹  5x = 74 – 5 = 69\n∴ x = 69/5\nii) 2m – 1 = 4     ⟹  2m = 4 + 1 = 5\n∴ m = 5/2 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41852,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following algebraic expressions as monomials, binomials, trinomials or polynomials : x²y - y², -x³, 3x² - 3y² + 2xy, 8y, x² - y², m² - mn + n² - m²n²\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Monomial : -x³, 8y\nBinomial : x²y – y², x² - y²\nTrinomial : 3x² - 3y² + 2xy\nPolynomial : m² - mn + n² - m²n²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41853,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (5p² + 3q² - pq) – (4pq – 5q² - 3p²) + (p² + 3q²)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','(5p² + 3q² - pq) – (4pq – 5q² - 3p²) + (p² + 3q²)\n= 5p² + 3q² - pq – 4pq + 5q² + 3p² + p² + 3q²\n= 9p² + 11q² - 5pq ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41854,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What should be added to (x² + xy + y²) to get (2x² + 3xy)\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let p(x) be added, we get, \np(x) = 2x² + 3xy – (x² + xy + y²) = 2x² + 3xy - x² - xy - y²\n= x² + 2xy - y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41855,731,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : i) (21b – 32) + (7b – 20) 		ii) (4m² - 3mn + 8) - (-m² + 5mn) \n\nAns. : -----------------------------               ---------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------               ----------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------                ----------------------------------','','','i) 21b – 32 + 7b – 20 = 20b – 52 \nii) 4m² - 3mn + 8 + m² - 5mn = 5m² - 8mn + 8\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41856,731,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following algebraic expressions as monomials, binomials, trinomials or polynomials : 7x, 5y – 7z, 3x³ - 5x² - 11, 1 – 8a – 7a² - 7a³, 5m – 3, a, 4, 3y² - 7y + 5.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Monomials : 7x, a, 4\nBinomial : 5y – 7z, 5m – 3\nTrinomial : 3x³ - 5x² - 11, 3y² - 7y + 5\nPolynomial : 1 – 8a – 7a² - 7a³','',0,0,0,NULL),(41857,731,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rakesh’s age is less than Sanika’s age by 5 years. The sum of their ages is 27 years. How old are they?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let Sanika age be x years then Rakesh age be x – 5 years\nAccording to given condition, x + x – 5 = 27\n∴ 2x = 32      ⟹ x = 16\n∴ Sanika’s age be 16 years and Rakesh age be x – 5 = 16 – 5 = 11 years','',0,0,0,NULL),(41858,731,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','When planting a forest, the number of jambhul trees planted was greater than the number of Ashoka trees by 60. If there are altogether 200 trees of these two types, how many jambhul trees were planted?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let number of Ashoka trees planted be x, then number of Jambhul trees be (x + 60)\nAccording to their condition, x + x + 60 = 200\n∴ 2x = 200 – 60 = 140         ⟹ x = 70\n∴ Number of Ashoka tree planted 70 and Jambhul tree plated x + 60 = 70 + 60 = 130.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41859,731,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shubhangi has twice as many 20 rupees notes as she has 50 rupees notes. Altogether, she has 2700 rupees. How many notes does she have?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let Shubhangi have Rs. 50 notes be x, then Rs. 20 notes be 2x\nAccording to given condition, 50x + 20(2x) = 2700\n∴ 50x + 40x = 2700      ⟹ 90x = 2700\n∴ x = 30\n∴ Rs. 50 notes be 30 and Rs. 20 notes be 2x = 2 × 30 = 60','',0,0,0,NULL),(41860,731,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Virat made twice as many runs as Rohit. The total of their scores is 2 less than a double century. How many runs did each of them make?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let Rohit made by run be x, then Virat made runs be 2x\nTotal score = 2(100) – 2 = 198\nAccording to the given condition, x + 2x = 198\n∴ 3x = 198       ⟹ x = 66\n∴ Rohit make runs be 66 and Virat makes runs be 2x = 2 × 66 = 132','',0,0,0,NULL),(41861,731,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A natural number is 4 more than twice the other number. If their sum is 31, find the numbers.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let 1st natural number be x then other natural number be 2x + 4\n∴ x + 2x + 4 = 31      ⟹ 3x = 31 – 4 = 27   \n∴ x = 9\n∴ Natural numbers be x = 9 and 2x + 4 = 2(9) + 4 = 22','',0,0,0,NULL),(41862,731,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The base of a triangle is (2x + 4) cm and its height is (3y + 5) cm, find its area.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of triangles = 1/2 × base × height\n= 1/2 × (2x + 4) (3y + 5) = 1/2 × [6xy + 10x + 12y + 20]\n= 1/2 × 2[3xy + 5x + 6y + 10] \n= 3xy + 5x + 6y + 10','',0,0,0,NULL),(41863,731,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If side of a square is of (x + 4) cm, find its perimeter and area.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Perimeter of square = 4 × side \n= 4 × (x + 4) = 4x + 16\nArea of square = (side)² = (x + 4)(x + 4)\n= x² + 8x + 16','',0,0,0,NULL),(41864,731,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve : i) (3m + 5) × (2m – 8) 			ii) 5(8 + y) = 3y + 13\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) 3m(2m – 8) + 5(2m – 8)\n= 6m² - 24m + 10m – 40 \n= 6m² - 14m – 40 \nii) 40 + 5y = 3y + 13\n∴ 5y – 3y = 13 – 40 \n∴ 2y = -27\n∴ y = -27/2','',0,0,0,NULL),(41865,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------- is the qualitative measure of the inertia of an object.','','','mass','',0,0,0,NULL),(41866,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Weight is a ----------------- quantity.','','','vector','',0,0,0,NULL),(41867,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- measures are required for measuring things.','','','standardized ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41868,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The unit of measuring speed is --------------------------','','','m/s','',0,0,0,NULL),(41869,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The continuous displacement of an object is called -------------------------','','','velocity','',0,0,0,NULL),(41870,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The gravitational force that acts on the mass of an object is called ------------------------','','','weight','',0,0,0,NULL),(41871,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','System international is also called ------------------------','','','metric system','',0,0,0,NULL),(41872,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The time required for one revolution of the earth is taken as ------------------ hours.','','','24','',0,0,0,NULL),(41873,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A --------------- and a -------------------- are used to express the magnitude of a physical quantity.','','','value, unit','',0,0,0,NULL),(41874,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ------------------------ that acts on this mass is called its weight.','','','gravitational force','',0,0,0,NULL),(41875,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The quantity ‘speed’ is the ratio of --------------- and ------------------- quantities.','','','distance, time','',0,0,0,NULL),(41876,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Standard ---------------------- unit must not be variable. ','','','fundamental','',0,0,0,NULL),(41877,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An international system of units based on seven fundamental units is called -----------------------','','','System International','',0,0,0,NULL),(41878,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mass is the ----- measure of the inertia of an object.\na) Quantitative 	b) Qualitative  	c) Valuable 		d) None of these\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','b) Qualitative  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41879,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a vector quantity?\na) Weight 		b) Mass 		c) Temperature 		d) Area\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','a) Weight ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41880,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An international system of unit based on ----- fundamental units.\na) Four 		b) Two 		c) Seven 		d) Five\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','c) Seven 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41881,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A value and a ----- are used to express the magnitude of a physical quantity.\na) Ratio 		b) Purity 		c) Proportion 		d) Unit\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','d) Unit','',0,0,0,NULL),(41882,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gram and kilogram are the unit of -------\na) Force 		b) Mass 		c) Weight 		d) Density\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','b) Mass ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41883,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In C.G.S. system distance is measured in --------.\na) Centimeter		b) Meter 		c) Kilometer 		d) Miles\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','a) Centimeter	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41884,732,'fillinblanks','1a',0,0,'Out of Text Book','','The earth needs ----- hours to complete one revolution.\na) 12 		b) 24 		c) 48 		d) 36\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','b) 24','',0,0,0,NULL),(41885,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is not a physical quantity?\na) Weight 		b) Mass 		c) Velocity 		d) Direction\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','d) Direction','',0,0,0,NULL),(41886,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' ---- is a vector quantity.\na) Weight 		b) Mass 		c) Time 		d) Length\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','a) Weight ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41887,732,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- TMC means one thousand million cubic feet.\na) 1 			b) 10 		c) 100		d) 5 \n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','a) 1 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41888,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is true or false : All fundamental quantities are vector quantities.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(41889,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is true or false : Distance is measured in centimeter in the CGS system.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41890,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is true or false : Standard measures are required for measuring things.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41891,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is true or false : Density is a fundamental quantity.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(41892,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether given statement is true or false : The unit of time is a same in MKS and CGS systems.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41893,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Length, mass, displacement, temperature.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','displacement','',0,0,0,NULL),(41894,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Velocity, area, force, weight.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','Area','',0,0,0,NULL),(41895,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Weight, distance, speed, density.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','density','',0,0,0,NULL),(41896,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Meter, Kilogram, velocity, second\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','velocity','',0,0,0,NULL),(41897,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who is my companion?','','','(1 – c), (2 – e), (3 – a), (4 – b), (5 – d)','',1,0,0,NULL),(41898,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the unit of mass in MKS system?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Kilograms is the unit used for measuring mass in MKS system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41899,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which unit the temperature is measured?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Temperature is measured in degree Celsius (⁰C) unit. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41900,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by inertia?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The tendency of an object to stay at a rest is known as inertia.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41901,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the physical quantities?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Mass, Distance, time, Speed','',0,0,0,NULL),(41902,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of vector quantity.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Displacement, velocity, force, weight.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41903,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by weight?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The gravitational force is act on the mass of an object is called weight.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41904,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the standard devices that we used in day to day life for measurement.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Time – Clock, watch; Height/length – Measuring tape/ruler; Speed of vehicle – Speedo meter, weight – Weighing machine','',0,0,0,NULL),(41905,732,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which unit the length of a class is measured?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Measuring tape/ruler','',0,0,0,NULL),(41906,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name any two scalar quantities.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Mass and temperature are the two types of scalar quantities.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41907,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which device was used for measuring time in olden times?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','An hour glass was used for measuring time in olden times.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41908,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reasons : It is not proper to measure quantities by using body parts as units.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The measurement of body parts for every person will be different.\nii) There is no standardization of any kind.\niii) Therefore, it is not proper to measure quantities by using body parts.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41909,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : It is necessary to get the weights and measures standardized at regular intervals.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Due to constant use, the weights and the weighing apparatus may lose their standardization. \nii) using such devices may result into mal practice.\niii) Therefore, it is necessary to get the weights and measures standardized at regular intervals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41910,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Our mass is same on both the moon and the earth.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The amount of matter present in a substance is called mass.\nii) mass does not increase or decrease in any part of the universe.\niii) Therefore, it will remain the same on moon as well as on the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41911,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the difference between Scalar quantity and vector quantity.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer :','',0,0,1,NULL),(41912,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is ‘second’ standardized?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The time required for one revolution of the earth is measured by means of an accurate device.\nii) This time is taken as 24 hours\niii) This is then standardized\niv) One hour is considered to have 60 minutes and one minute to have 60 seconds\nv) By appropriate methods the item period of 1 second is then standardized.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41913,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is measurements done at the petrol pump?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Petrol pump measured fuel by using petrol flowmeter which PD flowmeter type.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41914,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is used while measuring the cloth?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Tape meter and rods are the devices used to measure cloth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41915,732,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where is atomic clock kept in India?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Atomic clock is a device that works with utmost accuracy. Its work dependent on the vibrations of atom and molecules. \nii) The functioning is due to molecular systems of Caesium or ammonia.\niii) The atomic clock in Mumbai is in National Physical Laboratory in New Delhi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41916,732,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who is my companion?','','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – e), (4 – a), (5 – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(41917,732,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :','','','Answer :','',1,0,1,NULL),(41918,732,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the weight of the same object different on different planets?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The gravitational force is act on the mass of an object is called weight.\nii) The gravitational force exerted by the earth is not constant at everywhere.\niii) It is different at different places on the earth\niv) Weight of an object is depends upon the distance of the planets\nv) Hence, the weight of the same object is a different on the different planets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41919,732,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What precautions will you take make accurate measurement in day-to-day affairs?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following precautions need to be taken for accurate measurement.\ni) While buying grocery or any other food stuffs balance and weight should be checked.\nii) While, filling petrol that the digital display that indicates litres of petrol should be seen carefully.\niii) Check the balance while buying vegetables that is stable with its pointer upright, if weights are made up of metals and pans of the balance are proper.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41920,732,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the difference between mass and weight?\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer :','',0,0,1,NULL),(41921,732,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : Scalar quantity\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A quantity that can be completely expressed by its magnitude alone is called a scalar quantity. Example : length, mass, work, volume, breadth, temperature.\nii) It is subtracted by using simple mathematical addition or subtraction.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41922,732,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : Vector quantity\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The quantity that expressed completely only when magnitude and direction are both given is called a factor quantities. Example : displacement, velocity, force, weight.\nii) It cannot subtracted or added by using simple mathematical method.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41923,732,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : The errors that occur while making measurement.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','While making measurements the following errors may occur.\ni) Not using the appropriate device : An accuracy of measurement depends upon the device that are we used. Example : If we used the hand span to measure the length of a table instead of scale or tape error may occur.\nii) Not using the device properly : If the device is not handled while measuring length of the segment, an error may occur. Hence, to avoid the error it is essential to check whether the zero mark of scale coincides, with one of the end points of the line segment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41924,732,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : The need for accurate measurement and the devices to be used for that.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Accurate measurement is depend upon its purpose.\nii) Measurement of precious substances such as gold, diamond must be done meticulously and accurately.\niii) Accuracy of measurement also depends upon the devices.\niv) An appropriate device should be chosen and always should be checked.\nv) Devices should be handle appropriately while measuring the things. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41925,732,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why would the weight of an object be maximum at the poles and minimum at the equator? \n\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The earth is flattened at the poles and bulges out at the equator i.e. its poles are at shorter distance from the centre than the equator.\nii) Due to this gravitational force exerted by the Earth on the object at poles is more than the gravitational force exerted on the same object when it is placed at equator.\niii) As a result, an object weighs more at poles than at equator.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41926,732,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the weight of an object at a height altitude less than its weight at the sea level? \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The gravitational force by which the earth attracts towards its centre is the weight of the object.\nii) The distance of an object at an altitude from centre of the Earth is greater than that at sea level.\niii) More gravitational force exerted by the Earth on the object at sea level than at a high altitude.\niv) Therefore, an object weighs less at high altitude than at a sea level.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41927,732,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Will it possible to use one and the same unit to measure physical quantities such as mass, weight, distance, velocity, temperature?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) No, it will not be possible to use one and the same unit to measure physical quantities.\nii) Because of physical quantities are different from one another.\niii) Hence, they require different units of measurement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41928,732,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which quantities are taken into account to calculate the area of the classroom?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following quantities are taken into account for calculate the area of the classroom.\ni) Length and breadth are the quantities taken into account for calculate the area of the classroom.\nii) We can measure them with the help of standardized measuring tape and we can calculate the area.\niii) Formula : Area of classroom =  length × breadth','',0,0,0,NULL),(41929,732,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is an atomic clock? where is it kept?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A clock is needed to measure any time intervals.\nii) In atomic clock to measure time periodic vibrations of cesium atom are used as a standard.\niii) One second is the time required for 9,192,631,770 vibrations of cesium atomic clock.\niv) The cesium atomic clock is very accurate.\nv) It is kept in National Physical Laboratory (NPL) in Delhi. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41930,732,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the velocity of light used for determining the standard meter?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Light travel in a vacuum with a speed of 299, 792, 458 m/s.\nii) Using this data, 1 meter is defined as length of path travelled by light in vacuum in 1/299,792,458 part of a second.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41931,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a body traverse a distance in direct proportion to the time, the speed of the body is --------------------------','','','constant','',0,0,0,NULL),(41932,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a body is moving with a constant velocity its acceleration is ----------------------------','','','zero','',0,0,0,NULL),(41933,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------------------- is a scalar quantity.','','','speed','',0,0,0,NULL),(41934,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','--------------------- is the distance traversed by a body in a particular direction in unit time.','','','velocity','',0,0,0,NULL),(41935,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','Displacement is a --------------------- quantity.','','','vector','',0,0,0,NULL),(41936,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The unit of speed or velocity written as --------------------------','','','meter/second','',0,0,0,NULL),(41937,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Work = force × ---------------------','','','displacement','',0,0,0,NULL),(41938,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------------------- is measured by acceleration produced due to it.','','','force','',0,0,0,NULL),(41939,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When an object comes back to the point of origin, its displacement is -----------------------','','','zero','',0,0,0,NULL),(41940,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Velocity has magnitude and --------------------------.','','','direction','',0,0,0,NULL),(41941,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Acceleration is the rate of change in the -------------------- with respect to time.','','','velocity','',0,0,0,NULL),(41942,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a vector quantity?\na) Distance 		b) Work 		c) Mass 		d) Velocity\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','d) Velocity','',0,0,0,NULL),(41943,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The unit of velocity is written as :\na) meter/second	b) cubic meter/s²		c) second²/meter		d) m²/s²\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','a) meter/second','',0,0,0,NULL),(41944,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The unit of work in SI system is -------\na) Dyne 		b) Erg 		c) Joule 		d) None\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','c) Joule 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41945,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Work = force × ------\na) Distance 		b) Displacement 		c) Velocity 		d) Time\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','b) Displacement 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41946,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The unit of acceleration is ------\na) Kilometer/s 		b) m/s²       		c) cm²/s		d) N/S\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','b) m/s²      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41947,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ability to do work is called ------\na) Force 		b) Work 		c) Motion 		d) Energy\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','d) Energy','',0,0,0,NULL),(41948,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Velocity = -------','\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','a) Displacement/Total time','',1,0,0,NULL),(41949,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The unit of force in CGS system is ------\na) Joule 		b) Dyne 		c) Erg 		d) cm\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','b) Dyne 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(41950,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Force is measured by the ----- that it produces.\na) Energy 		b) Displacement 		c) Work 		d) Acceleration\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','d) Acceleration\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41951,733,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The interaction that brings about the acceleration is called ----\na) Work 		b) Energy 		c) Force 		d) Displacement\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','c) Force ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41952,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns :','','','(1 – c – p), (2 – a – r), (3 – b – q)','',1,0,0,NULL),(41953,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Every moving object have the same speed.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(41954,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : If no force is acting on a body its velocity does not change.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41955,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The rate of change of velocity is called displacement. \n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41956,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The instantaneous velocity can be different at different times.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41957,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : When a body performs uniform circular motion, its acceleration remains constant.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(41958,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Displacement of a body and distance covered by the body in a given time interval have always the same magnitude.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(41959,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The direction of acceleration can be opposite to that of velocity.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41960,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Acceleration can be perpendicular to velocity.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41961,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Deceleration has magnitude and direction.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41962,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The average velocity of a body can be zero.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(41963,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Acceleration, force, velocity, speed.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------','','','Speed','',0,0,0,NULL),(41964,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Distance\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The actual length of the route traversed by a moving object irrespective of the direction, is called distance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41965,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Displacement\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The minimum distance traversed by a moving object in a particular direction along a straight line is called displacement. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41966,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Velocity\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The distance traversed by moving a body in a specific direction in unit time is called velocity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41967,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Acceleration\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The rate of change of velocity is called acceleration. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41968,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the quantities that must be specified to describe force.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','To describe force, its magnitude as well as direction must be specified.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41969,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when a force is applied in the direction of motion of a body?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When a force is applied in the direction of motion of a body, the speed of the body increases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41970,733,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when a force is applied in the direction opposite to that of motion of a body?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When a force is applied in the direction opposite to that of motion of a body, the speed of the body decreases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41971,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the difference between Distance and displacement.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer :','',0,0,1,NULL),(41972,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the difference between Speed and Velocity.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer :','',0,0,1,NULL),(41973,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by speed?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Speed is the distance covered by a body in unit time.\nii) The SI unit of speed is the meter per second.\niii) The CGS unit of speed is the centimeter per second.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41974,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the formula for calculating speed?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Speed = Distance/Time','',0,0,0,NULL),(41975,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by motion? What causes a change in motion?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Displacement of an object from one place to another in a specific time is called the motion of that object.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41976,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Verify : The unit of acceleration is cm/s²\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Acceleration = change in the velocity/Time taken of change.\nThe unit of change of velocity = m/s, Unit of period = s\n∴ The unit of acceleration = m/s × 1/s = m/s²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41977,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Acceleration is vector quantity. Is force a vector quantity, too?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) According to Newton’s first law of motion when a force is applied on an object, acceleration takes place.\nii) The effect of force depends on both, magnitude and direction of the force.\niii) The force can increase or decrease the velocity. Sometimes, the magnitude of velocity may remain constant but the direction of the velocity can change.\niv) This shows that acceleration is a vector quantity.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41978,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula and unit of Speed.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Speed = Distance/Time\nThe SI unit of speed is the meter/second (m/s) and CGS unit is the centimeter/second (cm/s).\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41979,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula and unit of Velocity.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Velocity = Displacement/Time\nThe SI unit of velocity is the meter per second (m/s) and CGS unit is the centimeter per second (cm/s).\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41980,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reasons : Distance and displacement are different concepts.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Distance is the length of the actual path followed by a body between the points under consideration. It can be greater than the length of the straight line joining the two points, i.e. the initial and the final points. It does not involve the concept of direction.\nii) Displacement is the shortest distance from the initial point to the final point of movement of a body. It involves the concept of direction. It, therefore, follows that distance and displacement are different concepts.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41981,733,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reasons : When a body falls freely to the ground, its motion has a uniform acceleration.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When a body falls freely, the only force acting on the body is that due to the earth’s gravity.\nii) Near the earth’s surface, this force on a given body is practically uniform.\nHence, a body falling freely to the ground has almost a uniform acceleration. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(41982,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A bird sitting on a wire flies circle around and comes back to its perch. Explain the total distance it traversed during its fights and its eventual displacement.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A bird takes a circular turn and come back. The distance traversed by the birds is equal to the circular path that it has taken.\nii) When a bird comes back again to its initial place the displacement will be zero.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41983,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the given concept in your own words, with everyday examples :  Force.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The force is the push or pull on an object with mass that causes it to change velocity.\nii) Force is a vector quantity.\niii) The force is measured in SI system in the unit Newton (N) and in CGS system dyne.\niv) Applying force causes displacement, change of shape and size of an object. v) Force causes object can be change its direction and motion.\nvi) Examples : Hitting a ball with a bat, pushing the doors, rolling the chapati, throwing a ball, lifting a school bag, pulling a bucket of water from a well, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41984,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the given concept in your own words, with everyday examples : Work\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the force is applied on the body, body will be displaced in the direction of the force then we said to be work is done.\ni.e. W = F × S\nii) It is a scalar quantity\niii) The unit of work joule in SI system whereas erg in CGS system.\niv) The more forces applied more work is done.\nv) When a force of 1 Newton is applied on the body placed caused displacement of 1 m in the body along the direction of force then, work done on the body by force is said to be 1 joule.\nvi) Example : Bicycle moving from one place to another, displacement of Swing, a player running on the ground, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41985,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the given concept in your own words, with everyday examples : Displacement\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The minimum distance traversed by a moving object in a particular direction along a straight line is called displacement.\nii) It is a vector quantity\niii) It is measured with SI unit Meter.\niv) Magnitude of displacement is equal to distance covered by the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41986,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the given concept in your own words, with everyday examples : Velocity\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The distance traversed by moving a body in a specific direction in unit time is called velocity.\nii) It is a vector quantity with SI unit (m/s).\niii) It can be changed by changing speed or an object.\nVelocity = Displacement/Time\niv) Velocity has magnitude and direction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41987,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the given concept in your own words, with everyday examples : Acceleration\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The rate of change velocity is called acceleration.\nii) It is a vector quantity.\niii) Acceleration take place on changing the velocity an object.\niv) It has both magnitude and direction.\nv) Acceleration = change in velocity/time taken for change = v – u/t\nvi) The SI unit of is m/s²','',0,0,0,NULL),(41988,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the given concept in your own words, with everyday examples : Distance\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The actual length of the route traversed by a moving object irrespective of the direction, is called distance.\nii) Distance is a scalar quantity.\niii) The SI unit is the metre and CGS unit is the centimetre.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41989,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A person travels a distance of 72 km in 4 hours. Calculate the speed in m/s.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : s = 72 km = 72 × 1000 = 72000 m, t = 4 hours = 4 × 60 × 60 = 14400 s, speed = ?\nv = s/t = 72000/14400 = 720/144 = 10/2 = 5 m/s\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41990,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A body covers 10 m in the first 5 sec and 20 m in the next 5 sec. Find its average speed.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total time taken = 5 + 5 = 10 sec\nSpeed = distance/time = 30/10 = 3 m/s\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41991,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A car takes 20 minutes to cover a distance of 24 km. Find its speed in m/s.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : Distance = 24 km = 24 × 1000 = 24000 m, time = 20 min = 20 × 60 = 1200 sec\nSpeed = distance /time = 24000/1200 = 20 m/s \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41992,733,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Calculate the force required to produce an acceleration of 0.2 m/s² in a body of mass 10 kg.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : mass = 10 kg, acceleration = 0.2 m/s²\nF = ma = 10 × 0.2 = 2 N\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41993,733,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the figures and answer the questions : Sachin and Sameer started on a motorbike from place A, took the turn at B, did a task at C, travelled by the route CD to D and then went to W. Altogether, they took an hour for this journey. Find out the actual distance traversed by them and the displacement from A to E. From this deduce their speed. What was their velocity from A to E in the direction AE? Can this velocity be called average velocity? ','\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','The actual distance traversed by Sachin and Sameer :\n= d(A,B) + d(BC) + d(CD) + d(DE) = 3 + 4 + 5 + 3 = 15 km\nTotal displacement from A to E = d(AB) + d(BD) + d(DE) = 3 + 3 + 3  = 9 km\nSpeed = Distance/Time = 15/1 = 15 km/hr        Velocity = Displacement /Time = 9/1 = 9 km/hr\nThe velocity from A to E is 15 km/hr which can be called the average velocity.','',1,0,0,NULL),(41994,733,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A ball is rolling from A to D on a flat and smooth surface. Its speed is 2 cm/s. On reaching B, it was pushed continuously up to C. On reaching D from C, its speed had become 4 cm/s. It took 2 seconds for it to go from B to C. What is the acceleration of the ball as it goes from B to C?','\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','The ball moves up to B point from the A point with velocity 1 cm/s. From point B to C the ball is accelerating hence, the speed is 2 cm/s\n∴ Acceleration of the ball as it goes from B to C\nAcceleration = change in velocity/time taken for change = 4 – 2 /2 = 1 cm/s²\n∴  Acceleration the ball as it goes from B to C = 1 cm/s²','',1,0,0,NULL),(41995,733,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A force of 1000 N was applied to stop a car that was moving with a constant velocity. The car stopped after moving through 10 m. How much is the work done?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : F = 1000 N, S = 10 m, W = ?\n∴ W = F × S = 1000 × 10 = 10000 J\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41996,733,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A cart with mass 20 kg went 10 m. in a straight line on a plain and smooth road when a force of 2 N was applied to it. How much work was done by the force?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : mass (m) = 20 kg, Displacement (s) = 50 m\nF = 2N \n∴ W = F × S = 2 N × 50 m = 100 J','',0,0,0,NULL),(41997,733,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the direction of velocity of a particle performing uniform circular motion?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The direction of velocity of a particle performing uniform circular motion is along the tangent to the circle at the position of the particle, in the sense of motion of the particle.\nii) In uniform circular motion, speed is uniform, velocity is not uniform.\niii) The magnitude of velocity remains constant, but the direction of velocity changes continuously with time.\niv) It follows that the motion is accelerated, i.e. the acceleration is not zero.\nv) The magnitude of acceleration remains constant, but the direction of acceleration changes continuously with time.\nvi) Acceleration is always directed towards the center of the circle. It is called centripetal force.','',0,0,0,NULL),(41998,733,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The velocity of a moving body increases 10m/s to 20m/s in 5 seconds. Find its acceleration.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : u = 10 m/s, v = 20 m/s, t = 5 s, a = ?\nA = v – u /t = 20 – 10 /5 = 10/5 = 2 m/s²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(41999,733,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A body moves with a velocity of 10m/s for 10 sec and then with a velocity of 20 m/s in the same direction for 40 sec. Find its average velocity.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : total distance = 10 × 10 + 40 × 20 = 100 + 800 = 900 m\nTotal time = 40 + 10 = 50 s\nAverage velocity = distance/Time = 900/50 = 18 m/s','',0,0,0,NULL),(42000,733,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A certain force acts on a body at rest. If after 10 seconds the body moves with a velocity of 20 m/s and if its mass is 3 kg, find the magnitude of the force acting on the body.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : m = 3 kg v = 20 m/s, u = 0 m/s, t = 10 sec\na = v – u /t = 20 – 0 / 10 = 20/10 = 2\nF = ma = 3 × 2 = 6 N','',0,0,0,NULL),(42001,733,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A body of mass 1 kg moves with an initial velocity of 15m/s. If its velocity changes to 25 m/s in 5 seconds, find the force acting on the body.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : mass (m) = 1 kg, Initial velocity (u) = 15 m/s, final velocity (v) = 25 m/s, t = 5 sec\na = v – u /t = 25 – 15 /5 = 10/5 = 2\nF = ma = 1 × 2 = 2 N','',0,0,0,NULL),(42002,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There is ----------------- between like charges.','','','always repulsion','',0,0,0,NULL),(42003,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------------------------- is responsible for generation of electric charge in an object.','','','displacement of negative charge','',0,0,0,NULL),(42004,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A lighting conductor is made of a ----- strip.','','','Copper','',0,0,0,NULL),(42005,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- does not get electricity charged easily by rubbing.','','','Plastic','',0,0,0,NULL),(42006,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There is ----- when opposite electric chargers come near each other.','','','always attraction ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42007,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A ---- can be detected with an electroscope.','','','charged object','',0,0,0,NULL),(42008,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the Greek amber is called -------','','','electron','',0,0,0,NULL),(42009,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Electrically charged objects attracts ---- objects.','','','Uncharged','',0,0,0,NULL),(42010,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','During, chemical reaction between nitrogen and oxygen in the air ----- gas is formed.','','','nitrogen oxide','',0,0,0,NULL),(42011,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- is used to identity each organisms.','','','key','',0,0,0,NULL),(42012,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many types are there of electric charge are there?\na) One 		b) Two 		c) Three 		d) Four\n','','','b) Two ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42013,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ is the smallest particle of an element.\na) Atom 		b) Molecule 		c) Structure 		d) Nucleus\n','','','a) Atom ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42014,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The electric charge generated by friction is called ----- electricity.\na) Motivational 		b) Static 		c) Frictional 		d) Positive\n','','','c) Frictional ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42015,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There is attraction between ------ electric charges.\na) Unlike 		b) Like 		c) Both a and b 		d) None of these\n','','','a) Unlike 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(42016,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The electric charge generated by ----- stays only as long as the charged object is near to it.\na) Conduction 		b) Convection 		c) Induction 		d) Transmission\n','','','c) Induction','',0,0,0,NULL),(42017,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following device is used to detect the electric charge?\na) Microscope 		b) Telescope 		c) Stethoscope 		d) Electroscope\n','','','d) Electroscope','',0,0,0,NULL),(42018,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Lighting causes the ------ in the air is converted into ozone?\na) Nitrogen 		b) Oxygen 		c) Neon 		d) Carbon\n','','','b) Oxygen','',0,0,0,NULL),(42019,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following metal is used to make the lighting conductor?\na) Copper 		b) Gold 		c) Silver 		d) Sodium\n','','','a) Copper ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42020,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Each atom is electrically -----\na) Acidic 		b) Basic 		c) Neutral 		d) None of these\n','','','c) Neutral 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(42021,734,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a good conductor of electricity?\na) Iron 		b) Wood 		c) Cloth 		d) Copper\n','','','a) Iron ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42022,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns : ','','','(1 – b), (2 – a), (3 – c), (4 – d)','',1,0,0,NULL),(42023,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : There is only one type of charge on every object.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42024,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : All objects are electrically neutral.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42025,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The charges of static electricity are absorbed in moist air.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42026,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The lighting conductor is made up of iron strip.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42027,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Attraction is used as a test for identifying an electrically charged object.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42028,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : If a glass rod is rubbed on the silk cloth, it becomes positively charged.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42029,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by atom?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The smallest particle of an element is called atom.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42030,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the types of charge.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Positive and negative charge are the two types of charge.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42031,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by frictional electricity?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The electric charge generated by friction is called frictional electricity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42032,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by gold leaf electroscope?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The device which is used to detect the electric charge on an object is known as gold leaf electroscope.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42033,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is lighting conductor?','','','A device that used for protection from a lighting strike is known as lighting conductor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42034,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens if we perform experiments of frictional electricity or static electricity during rainy season?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Moist air is a good conductor of electricity so during rainy season the charge produced on a body due to friction is conducted away by the surrounding moist air. Hence experiments on static electricity are not successful.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42035,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are plants benefited by lighting?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The energy of lighting, converts the oxygen in the air to ozone.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42036,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the meaning of electron in Greek language?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Amber','',0,0,0,NULL),(42037,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was discovered by Thales.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Greek philosopher Thales performed some experiments with the aid of amber. Greek word for amber is ‘elektron’. So, the energy produced from amber was called ‘electricity’.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42038,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who names the electric charges as positive charge (+) and negative charge (-)?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Benjamin Franklin','',0,0,0,NULL),(42039,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When is it said that a particular object is electricity neutral?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','If positive charge and  negative charge are perfectly balanced, therefore an atom is electrically neutral. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42040,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the word electricity derived?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Greek philosopher Thales performed some experiments with the aid of amber. Greek word for amber is ‘elektron’. So, the energy produced from amber was called ‘electricity’.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42041,734,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name five things on which electric charge is produced by friction.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Glass, Ebonite, Plastic, Silk, Wool are the five things on which electric charge is produced by friction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42042,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How will you protect yourself from lighting?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In order to protect oneself, the shelter should be taken in a house that has lighting conductor. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42043,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are charges generated?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When two objects are rubbed against each other, the negative charge on one of the objects is transferred to other object. Making the second object to acquire negative charge. The first object becomes positively charged due to loss of negative charge. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42044,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the lighting conductor, what provision is made for spreading the electricity into the ground?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In the lighting conductor a pit is dug under the ground.\nii) The pit is filled with coal and salt and iron plate is placed upright in the pit.\niii) There is provision for pouring water into the pit which help to spread the electric charge quickly into the ground and prevent damage.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42045,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do farmers stick an iron staff into the ground while working in the field in rainy conditions?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) An iron staff works as lighting conductor.\nii) While lighting charges may flow to the ground through farmer’s body.\niii) When an iron staff in to the ground, the charges get attracted toward the staff instead of farmer’s body.\niv) Therefore, to prevent at lighting strike farmer’s stick an iron staff in the ground.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42046,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a glass rod is rubbed against a silk cloth, what kind of electric charge is developed on the cloth?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When a glass rod is rubbed against a silk cloth, the negative electric charge is developed on the silk cloth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42047,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a plastic rod is rubbed against a woolen cloth, what kind of electric charge is developed on the plastic rod?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When a plastic rod is rubbed against a woolen cloth, the negative charge is developed on the plastic rod.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42048,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The diverged leaves of a positively charged gold leaf electroscope collapse if its disc is touched with a charged ebonite rod.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A charged ebonite rod carries a negative charge.\nii) When the disc of an electroscope is touched with the charged ebonite rod, the former acquires a negative charge due to conduction.\niii) The negative charge given by the ebonite rod neutralizes almost all the positive charge on the positively charged gold leaf electroscope and the diverged leaves collapse. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42049,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Leaves of a gold leaf electroscope diverge if a charged body is brought near its disc, but they collapse if the body is taken away.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When a charged body is brought near the disc of a gold leaf electroscope, it induces a charge on the leaves of the electroscope.\nii) As both the leaves possesses the similar charge, they repel each other and diverge.\niii) As the induced charge is only temporary, the leaves become chargeless and collapse when the charged body is taken away from the electroscope.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42050,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Small bits of paper are attracted by a plastic comb which has been move vigorously through dry air.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When a plastic comb is moved vigorously through dry hair.\nii) It acquires electric charge.\niii) When this charged comb is brought near small bits of paper, they are attracted by the comb.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42051,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Dust and other small particles are attracted by a plastic comb which has been removed vigorously through dry air.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Static electricity charge produced only at the place of friction in an object. \nii) Charge developed after rubbing two unlike object.\niii) It is remain on the object for short duration.\niv) It absorbed in the moist region.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42052,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : When dry hair is combed vigorously, a crackling sound is produced.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When dry hair is combed vigorously, opposite electric charges are produced on the comb and the hair.\nii) When the charged comb is brought near the hair having opposite charge minute sparks are produced and they give rise to a crackling sound.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42053,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Experiments on static electricity are not successful during rainy season.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Normally dry air is an insulator of electricity.\nii) In the rainy season, it becomes moist due to the presence of excess of water vapour.\niii) Moist air is a conductor of electricity. So, during rainy season the charge produced on a body due to friction is conducted away by the surrounding moist air.\niv) Hence, experiments on static electricity are not successful during rainy season.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42054,734,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : A metal rod which is to be charged by friction is held by an insulating handle.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Metals are good conductors of electricity.\nii) If we try to charge them by holding them in our hands, the charge produced on them will pass to the earth through our body.\niii) If, however, an insulating handle is fitted on them, the charge produced on the road remains on it and the rod is charged.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42055,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is lighting not seen every day during the rainy season?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Lightning is produced due to the charged clouds.\nii) When the charge is produced in the large scale on the clouds, there may see lightening in the rainy season.\niii) If small amount of charges are generated on the clouds, lightning is not seen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42056,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the characteristics of a static electric charge?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Characteristic of static electric charge :\ni) Static electricity charge produced only at the place of friction in an object. ii) Charge developed after rubbing two unlike object.\niii) It is remained on the object for short duration.\niv) It absorbed in the moist region.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42057,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Do all object get charged by rubbing?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Yes, most of the object get charged by rubbing. (that will depend upon how you the atmosphere).','',0,0,0,NULL),(42058,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why does a charged balloon stick to a wall?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A wall is electrically neutral.\nii) When the charged a balloon is taken near the wall, attraction is take place. iii) Charged  balloon has excess of either positive or negative charges.\niv) On the wall there is equal number of charges.\nv) Hence, the charged the balloon stick to a wall.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42059,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Can we use leaves of some other metal instead of gold, in the electroscope?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Yes, we can use leaves of other metals like aluminium instead of gold in the electroscope.\nii) Metal should be good conductor of electricity and having the malleability or ductility property.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42060,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What kind of damage is caused by a lighting strike?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following damage caused by lightning :\ni) It damage loss of life, trees and property.\nii) The electrical devices like TV, mobile phone, radio, get damaged.\niii) If it strike on flammable materials, it may get fire.\niv) It leads to forest fire and destroy the wildlife also.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42061,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : Frictional electricity and static electricity.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The electric charge generated by friction is called frictional electricity. \nii) When certain objects are rubbed against each other, the negatively charged particles from one object to go to the other object.\niii) Due to this other objects becomes negatively charged whereas the first object becomes positively charged.\niv) Such charge is called frictional electricity.\nv) The charge is produced only at the place of friction. The transferred charge remains there in the object for some duration, therefore, it is known as static electricity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42062,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Suppose there are two objects A and B. Initially both are electrically neutral. If both are rubbed against each other, the object A becomes positively charged and object B becomes negatively charged. What is the reason for this change?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Initially, the electric charge on object A and B is zero.\nii) When A and B are rubbed against each other, particles from A which have acquired negative charge are transferred to object B.\niii) This results in to decrease in number of negatively charged particles as compared to the positively charged particles on object A.\niv) This makes object A to become positively charged.\nv) At the same time, negatively charged a particles in object B increase and thus object B becomes negatively charged.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42063,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain, why the repulsion is used as a test for identifying an electrically charged object?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There is attraction between unlike electric charges.\nii) Similarly, any electrically charged body and electrically neutral body will also display attraction.\niii) So, attraction cannot be used as the test for identifying an electrically charged object.\niv) Hence, repulsion is used as a test for identifying an electrically charged object.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42064,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Will you given a negatively charged ebonite rod and a conductor fixed on a glass stand how till you make it (A) positively charged	(B) negatively charged\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A) When charged ebonite rod is touched on the conductor, the negative charge will be transferred to conductor and it will become negatively charged.\nB) i) To make conductor positively charged, method of induction has to be followed. \nii) For this purpose the charged tip of rod is brought near one end and the other tip is touched to the ground (or earthing is done).\niii) After the earthing wire is removed, take the ebonite rod away. The positive charge developed due to induction will spread on conductor and it will also become positively charged.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42065,734,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','When a glass rod is rubbed with a silk cloth, what type of charge is produced on the silk cloth? Why?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When a glass rod is rubbed with a silk cloth, the silk cloth is negatively charged.\nii) When a glass rod is rubbed with a silk cloth, the loosely bound electrons of the glass rod are detached from it and attach themselves to the silk cloth.\niii) As there are excess of electrons on the silk cloth, the silk cloth is negatively charged .','',0,0,0,NULL),(42066,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the damage caused by lighting? How will you create awareness to prevent it?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) It damage to the loss of life, trees and property.\nii) The electrical devices become damage in the house.\niii) If it strikes on flammable material, there may catch fire quickly.\niv) It leads to forest fire and destroy the wild life.\nAwareness to prevent damage :\ni) People should not stay out of the house or near the electrical poles.\nii) One should not stand under the tree.\niii) Workshop or seminar should be conduct to aware the people from lightning.\niv) Lightning conductor should be fitted in the buildings.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42067,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain why it is not safe to go out with an umbrella when there is heavy rain, lighting or thunder?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The rod of an umbrella made up metal.\nii) If there is heavy rain along with lightning and thunder, the lightning strike can be attracted towards an umbrella.\niii) The charges that developed by the clouds can be transferred to the umbrella.\niv) These charges can flow through our body, causes death.\n Hence, it is not safe to go out with an umbrella, when there is heavy rain lightning or thunder.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42068,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is that actually happen when there is lighting strikes the earth?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the clouds are rubbed against the air, the positive charges are generated on the upper part of the clouds and negative charges on the lower part of the clouds.\nii) When this negative charge on the bottom of the cloud becomes larger than the charge on the ground. It flows towards the ground quickly.\niii) Lightning strikes on the earth, produces heat, light and sound energy along with electric current.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42069,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What measure will you take to prevent the damage will be taken to prevent the damage.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following measures will be taken to prevent the damage :\ni) Avoid standing near the electrical poles.\nii) We should avoid to use electrical appliances like, mobile phones or telephones.\niii) We should not swim in water or climb on the tree.\niv) Lightening conductor should be fitted on the buildings.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42070,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the upper and of the lighting conductor pointed?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The pointed end of the lightning conductor collects the large number of electric charges.\nii) The pointed lightning conductor pull down these charges with great force of attraction.\niii) Hence, to increase the efficiency the upper end of the lightning conductor is pointed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42071,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are coal and salt added to the pit in the ground.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Adding coal and salt in the pit helps to spread the electric charge quickly into the ground.\nii) Due to this damaging surrounding will be prevented.\niii) This helps damaging surrounding will be prevent the rustling of iron plate.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42072,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain in brief the flash of lighting \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Air and clouds rub against each other in the sky, the upper part of clouds generate the positively charged and lower side becomes generate negatively charged.\nii) When this negative charge on the bottom of the clouds becomes much larger than the charge on the ground.\niii) The charges starts flowing very fast towards the ground.\niv) In this way heat, light and sound energy are produced along with the electric current.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42073,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain in brief lighting strike.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When electrically charged clouds comes over the tall building or tree electricity is attracted towards a tall building or tree if lightning strike there.\nii) An opposite electric charge is generated due to induction.\niii) due to the attraction between the opposite charges on the cloud and the building, the charge on the cloud flows towards the building. This is called a lightning strike.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42074,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the method of charging a gold leaf electroscope with a negative charge.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A simple device that used detect the electric charge on an object is known as electroscope.\nii) A gold leaf electroscope has two leaves at the end of the rod and a metal disc at other end.\niii) When an uncharged object is taken near the disc the leaves remain closed.\niv) When a charged object is taken near the disc both leaves are charged by the same electric charge and repel each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42075,734,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Answer the given questions with neat and well-labelled diagram : Explain briefly the construction and working of lighting conductor.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A device which is used for protection from a lightning strike is known as lightning conductor.\nii) It consist of a long copper strip with one and forked at the highest part of the building and other end of the strip is connected to a plate of cast iron.\niii) This strip flowing the charges from a clouds taken to the ground.\niv) A lightning conductor prevents the damage or buildings and the surrounding area.\nv) A pit is to be dug in the ground which contains coal and salt are filled.\nvi) In this pit iron plate is placed upright.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42076,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maximum heat is absorbed by a ------------------- coloured objects.','','','Black','',0,0,0,NULL),(42077,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------ of heat does not require a medium.','','','radiation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42078,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Conduction of heat takes place through a --------------------- substance.','','','good conductor','',0,0,0,NULL),(42079,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The shining surface in a thermos flask decreases the outgoing heat by --------------------','','','reflection ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42080,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cooking utensils are made from metals due to their property of -------------------------','','','conduction ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42081,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The earth receives heat from the sun by ------------------------','','','radiation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42082,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------- required for the conduction of heat.','','','medium ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42083,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sir James Dewar was made the first thermos flask in --------------------------','','','1892','',0,0,0,NULL),(42084,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Metals --------------------- on heating.','','','expands','',0,0,0,NULL),(42085,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dark clothes are used in winter because they ----------------------- heat.','','','absorbs','',0,0,0,NULL),(42086,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Some heat is lost from a thermos flask by ------------------- from the lid.','','','conduction ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42087,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a metal?\na) Plastic 		b) Wood 		c) Iron 		d) Rubber\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','c) Iron ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42088,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The transfer of heat from the hot part of an object to the cold part is called ------\na) Conductor 		b) Radiation 		c) Convection 		d) Attraction\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','a) Conductor','',0,0,0,NULL),(42089,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ can occur in liquid and gaseous substance.\na) Radiation 		b) Convection 		c) Conduction 		d) Propagation \n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','b) Convection ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42090,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The density of water ----- due to heating.\na) Increases 		b) Remains constant 		c) Decreases 	       d) None of these\n.\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','c) Decreases ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42091,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The absorption of heat by radiation is depends upon the ----- of an object.\na) Colour 		b) Size 		c) Mass 		d) Weight\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','a) Colour ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42092,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a bad conductor of heat?\na) Iron 		b) Copper 		c) Aluminum 	d) Plastic\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','d) Plastic','',0,0,0,NULL),(42093,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ---- of the gas increases on heating.\na) Mass 		b) Volume 		c) Weight 		d) All above\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','b) Volume ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42094,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following metal is used in thermometer?\na) Mercury 		b) Gold 		c) Silver 		d) Sodium\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','a) Mercury 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(42095,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who made the first thermos flask in 1892?\na) Sir Issac Newton 	            b) Sir James Dewar 	c) C. V. Raman 	d) Benjamin Franklin\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','b) Sir James Dewar ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42096,735,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The freezing point of mercury is ------\na) 37⁰C		b) 100⁰C		c) -39⁰C		d) 0⁰C \n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','c) -39⁰C	','',0,0,0,NULL),(42098,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns : ','','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(42099,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The sun is the largest source of heat energy.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42100,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Convection can be occur only in liquids.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42101,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Wood is good conductor of heat.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42102,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : When a liquid is heated the distance between its particle increases.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42103,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Plastic is a good conductor of heat.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42104,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Mica is a good conductor of heat.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42105,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Density of hot water is more than that of cold water.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42106,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Heat flows from cold place to hot place.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42107,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Enclosed air is a good conductor of heat.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42108,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Copper, silver, iron, wood\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','Wood','',0,0,0,NULL),(42109,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Tea cup, plastic tumbler, silver vase, glass tumbler\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','silver vase','',0,0,0,NULL),(42110,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Conduction, convection, expansion, radiation\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','Expansion ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42111,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Plastic, cloths, wood, copper\n\nAns. : ------------------------------','','','Copper ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42112,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by transfer of heat?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The flow of heat from one place to another is called transfer of heat.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42113,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are bad conductors of heat? Give two examples.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Substances through which heat is not transferred easily are called bad conductor of heat. E.g. wood, plastic','',0,0,0,NULL),(42114,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the modes of heat transfer?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Conduction, convection and radiation are the modes of heat transfer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42115,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why solar cookers are painted black from outside?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Solar cooker uses radiant heat energy from the sunlight for cooking the enclosed food. Since black objects absorbs maximum heat, they are painted dark from outside.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42116,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why cycle tube tyres are pumped in with less air in summer?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In summer air in cycle tyres heats up due to high temperature and friction which expands and creates higher pressure on the tubes resulting in bursting of tyres. Therefore, tyres are filled in with less air in summer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42117,735,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are thermos flask (Dewar flask) used?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Thermos flasks are used for keeping hot drinks like tea and coffee hot; and chilled liquids and ice in cold condition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42118,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does a fever get lowered by putting a cold compress on the forehead of a patient?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A cold compress absorbs some heat from the patient’s forehead as water slowly evaporates from the cloth and lowers the body temperature. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42119,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are the houses in Rajasthan painted white?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In Rajasthan, temperature is very high in summer.\nii) White colour reflect more heat that radiant by the sun.\niii) White colour absorbs also a little of heat.\niv) Therefore, to keep the low temperature inside the house, the house is painted white. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42120,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : An ordinary glass bottle cracks when boiling water is poured into it, but a borosil glass bottle does not.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When boiling water is poured in the glass soft the surface of glass become hot and expand. Glass is a bad conductor of heat.\nii) The heat is not conducted easily through the bad conductor of heat.\niii) The heat is not conducted in equal to the pair of glass bottle.\niv) This unequal heating causes cracking of glass bottle.\nv) On the other hand borocil glass is made up of constituents than that of ordinary glass.\nvi) Hence, the borosil does not crack due to hot water.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42121,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The telephone wires which sag in summer becomes straight in winter.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In the summer, temperature around us is high.\nii) Due to this telephone wires gets expand and start sagging.\niii) There is low temperature in winter cause wire gets contract.\niv) Due to this contraction wire become straight. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42122,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Dew drops form on the grass, in winter.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In water vapour is present in the air.\nii) The water holding capacity of air is depends upon the temperature.\niii) In winter temperature of air falls down.\niv) As result vapour convert into droplets.\nv) These droplets from the air falls on the grass.\nvi) Such a way dew drops from on the glass in winter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42123,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : In winter why does an Iron pillar feel colder than a wooden pole.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) An Iron is a good conductor of heat.\nii) Transfer heat to the surrounding during winter and becomes cold.\niii) Wood is a bad conductor of heat, no heat transfer is take place between wood and surrounding. Therefore, wooden pole does hot become cold in winter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42124,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : An iron tyre is heated before it is fitted on a wheel.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When this expanded tyre is heated it expands and become bigger than the wooden wheel.\nii) When this expanded tyre is placed on the wheel, it fits over it.\niii) After cooling the tyre, it gets contracts and fit over the wheel.\niv) Therefore, the iron tyre is heated before it is fitted on wheel. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42125,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : We can feel the heat of a table lamp, under it.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the table lamp turn on, it emits heat in the surrounding.\nii) This heat is radiated absorbed by us.\niii) Therefore, we can feel heat of a table lamp, under it.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42126,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The upper layer of water in a deep well are cool but those in a pond are warm.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A deep well has a smaller surface.\nii) This smaller surface absorbs less heat which is radiated from the sun.\niii) The surface of pond is large causes absorbs more heat.\niv) Hence, the upper layer of deep well are cool but those in a pond are warm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42127,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by radiation?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The transfer of heat take place without any medium, this is called radiation. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42128,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give any four examples of conductors or heat.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Copper, Iron, silver and gold','',0,0,0,NULL),(42129,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','For what purpose of thermometer is used.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Thermos flask are used for keeping hot drinks like tea and coffee hot; and chilled liquids and ice in cold condition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42130,735,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why concrete roads have gaps filled with tar?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Concrete roads expand in summer and contract in winter due to heat. \nii) Tar on the other hand melts in hot summer and solidifies at normal temperatures.\niii)  This property of allows expansion and contraction of concrete roads and keeps the road in good condition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42131,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will absorb heat?  [Steel, spoon, wooden board, glass vessel, iron griddle (tava), glass, wooden spoon, plastic plate]\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) From the above substances like steel spoon, iron griddle, water and wax are the good conductors of heat.\nii) hence, they will become hot.\niii) Rest of the substances like wooden board, glass vessel, glass, wooden spoon, plastic plate, soil are the bad conductors of heat.\niv) They will not absorbs the heat.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42132,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why does the halwai wrap up cloth around the end of his slotted spoon while stirring milk in his large kalhai?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the slotted spoon comes the contact of boiled milk, heat is transferred to the spoon.\nii) The halwai may get burn if he touches the hot spoon.\niii) To avoid this he wrap the cloth around the end of the spoon.\niv) Wrapping cloth prevent the transfer of heat to the hands of halwai.\nv) Hence, the halwai wrap up cloth around the end of his slotted spoon while stirring the boiling milk in his large kalhai. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42133,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by thermoware?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A device that made by placing two vessels, one in side the other.\nii) The inner is made of shiny metal and outer is made from plastic.\niii) The heat is not lost by conduction and convection.\niv) The inner surface is shiny, this can not be lost heat by radiation.\nv) Therefore, hot food-stuff remains hot for a long time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42134,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do you wear woolen clothes in winter?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) wool id bad conductor of heat.\nii) The woolen clothes prevents the transfer of heat from the body.\niii) The woolen clothes keep us secure from the cold in the winter.\niv) Hence, we wear woolen clothes in winter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42135,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do we use white clothes in summer and dark or black clothes in winter?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Absorption and reflection of heat is depend upon the colour of an object.\nii) White colour reflect most of the heat that falling from sun.\niii) Dark or black clothes absorbs more heat hence we feel warm.\niv) Therefore, to maintain our normal body temperature we use white clothes in summer and dark or black in winter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42136,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is mercury or alcohol used in a thermometer?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There is a much difference between freezing and boiling point of a Mercury.\nii) The freezing point is -39⁰C and the boiling point is 357⁰C.\niii) Mercury is shiny and does not stick to the glass.\niv) The alcohol’s freezing point is -115⁰C.\nv) Therefore, the lower temperature can be measured only by alcohol thermometer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42137,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the neat and well-labelled diagram of : Conduction of heat. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram :','',0,0,1,NULL),(42138,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the neat and well-labelled diagram of : Convection of heat.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram :','',0,0,1,NULL),(42139,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the neat and well-labelled diagram of : Thermos flask\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(42140,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The side of a lantern is provided with holes on the lower side.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Lantern requires fresh air with oxygen for burning.\nii) If holes are not provided near the wick, the same oxygen deficient air will circulate in the lantern glass by convection current said by the hot air.\niii) Therefore, fresh supply of oxygen comes from the bottom and uses up hot air that goes out from the top vent.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42141,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do we feel warm near the born-fire?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The heat transferred near bonfire is by the process of radiation.\nii) Every warm object continuously emits heat in all the directions around itself. iii) Similarly, bonfire also radiates heat.\niv) Some of this heat is absorbed by our body while some of this heat is reflected.\niv) Therefore, by sitting near a bonfire, we feel warm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42142,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are thin bottom copper vessels applied with a layer of mud from outside while cooking?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Copper is a good conductor of heat which passes on heat from the stove to the raw food very easily. \nii) But when large amount of heat is applied suddenly, the food inside the vessel may get burnt.\niii) Mud is a bad conductor of heat therefore heat coming from the stove is slowly conducted by the copper vessel to the food.\niv) This allows steady and uniform cooking of the food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42143,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is an infra-red camera? What is it used for?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In the absence of light at night usual camera with light images cannot be used.\nii) But most objects like trees, mountains and stones radiate heat on cool night. iii) This radiator heat is captured by the infra - red camera to make surroundings visible.\niv) The IR camera is used for watching movements of enemy at night.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42144,735,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happens to the radiated heat falling on an object?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Generally, radiated heat is reflected, absorbed and transferred by the object.\nii) If the object is bright and glossy the incident radiant heat is mostly reflected and some of it absorbed.\niii) But, when the object is dark and rough, it will absorb most of the heat and become warm and some heat will be transferred.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42145,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain which mode of heat transfer causes sea breezes and land breezes.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) During a day time, air near the surface of land gets heated by the heat.\nii) The hot air has the low density than the cool air above the sea.\niii) Air above the land flows by the convection currents.\niv) Similarly, during night time air above the land is cooler than air above the sea, the air above the sea become hotter moves upwards and replaced by comparatively cooler air moving from the land forming convection current. In this way land breeze is set up.\nv) In this way, sea and land breeze are formed due to the convection of heat. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42146,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the outer coat of the penguins of Antarctica black?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There is very low temperature in Antarctica.\nii) Black colour absorb the heat that radiant from the sun.\niii) Animals in Antarctica struggle to keep their body warm.\niv) To survive convenient in cold weather, the outer court of Penguins in Antarctica is black.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42147,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are heaters fitted near the floor and air conditioners, near the ceiling or room?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Heaters are used to keep the room warm.\nii) The warm air that coming from the heater near the floor rises up easily duo to its lower density and the cold air near ceiling comes down and replaces it to higher density.\niii) The density difference of air at upper and lower heights sets in convection currents by transfer of heat.\niv) Air conditioner are used to keep the room cool, the air coming out of the conditioner is heavier and sink.\nv) Warm air being lighter flows upwards and replaces the cool air.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42148,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the construction and function of thermos flask.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Construction :\ni) It consists of two glass tubes one inside the other.\nii) The surfaces of both tubes are made shiny by silver coating.\niii) There is vacuum between two tubes.\niv) There is protective Jar of metal or plastic outside the tubes.\nv) For the protection of the flask, pieces of sponge or rubber are fixed between the outer jar and the flask.\nFunction of the thermos flask :\ni) In the thermos flask hot substance remains hot and cold substance remains cold.\nii) Because, duo to the vacuum neither conduction nor convection is occurred.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42149,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Conduction of heat.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Transfer of heat from the hot part of an object to the cold part, is called conduction of heat.\nii) Conduction of heat is takes place through the solid substance.\niii) A medium is required for the conduction of heat.\niv) It means heat cannot be conduct through vacuum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42150,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Convection of heat\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In liquid heat is transferred by means of convection currents is called convection of heat.\nii) In this process water at the bottom in container become warm water has lower density as a result it moves upwards.\niii) It is replaced by cold water.\niv) Convection needs a medium to transfer heat.\nv) It occurs in liquid medium and gases medium also.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42151,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Radiation\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The transfer of heat is take place without any medium this is known as radiation.\nii) In vacuum or space heat is transferred by radiation.\niii) It does not require solid or liquid medium for radiation.\niv) Most of the substances transfer the heat by the process of radiation.\nv) The capacity of absorbing heat of the substance depends upon the colour and its intrinsic property.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42152,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : The upper layers of water in a deep well are cool but those in a pond are warm.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The deep well has a smaller surface.\nii) Therefore, this smaller surface absorbs less heat which is radiated from the sun.\niii) On the other hand, the pond surface being large absorbs more heat due to a larger surface area.\niv) Therefore, the upper layers of water in a deep well are cool but those in a pond are warm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42153,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : Ice is kept in sawdust to keep it from melting for a longer time.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Sawdust is a bad conductor of heat.\nii) Through sawdust heat is not transferred rapidly. Therefore, this prevents heat from reaching the ice.\niii) Ice, thus does not melt if kept in sawdust.\niv) Therefore, ice is kept in shortest to keep it from melting for a longer time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42154,735,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : Two steel bowls stuck in each other can be separated by heating.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n            ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Metals expand on heating.\nii) Stainless steel too expands when heated up.\niii) If the bowls are stuck, the heat given would help the outer bowl to expand. iv) The outer bowl gets heated both the inner bowl does not get much heat. \nv) Because of this, inner bowl can be separated out easily.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42155,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Government of India established the National flood commission in -------------------------','','','1976','',0,0,0,NULL),(42156,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Tsunami is a Japanese word which means ------------------- wave.','','','harbor','',0,0,0,NULL),(42157,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- Day is observed as International day for Disaster Reduction.','','','13th October ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42158,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- is a natural disaster.','','','famine ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42159,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There is the possibility of loss of life and damage to property in -----------------------','','','natural calamities','',0,0,0,NULL),(42160,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------- is the main cause of famine.','','','Drought ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42161,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Asia has turned out to be the most ------------------- continent of the world. ','','','famine affected ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42162,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------ take place on a large scale due to tsunami.','','','Landslide ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42163,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','It rains often as a result of a --------------------------','','','volcanic eruption','',0,0,0,NULL),(42164,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There should be a --------------------- fitted at the height on your house.','','','lightening conductor','',0,0,0,NULL),(42165,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- is the most famine affected continent of the world.\na) Asia 		b) Nort America 		c) Australia 		d) Antarctica\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','a) Asia ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42166,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a natural calamity?\na) Accident 	b) Bomb explosion 		c) Terrorism	d) Flood\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','d) Flood','',0,0,0,NULL),(42167,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is man made disaster?\na) Famine 		b) Railway accident 	c) Cloudburst		d) Lighting\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','b) Railway accident ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42168,736,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The cloudburst that occurred in Mumbai on 26th August in ------\na) 2005 		b) 2009 		c) 2010 		d) 2008\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','a) 2005 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(42169,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Information about a forthcoming storm is to be kept secret.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42170,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : You should not swim when there is a lighting in the sky.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42171,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : It is possible to prevent the eruption of a volcano.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42172,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Heavy rains result in famine.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42173,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Small dams should be constructed in mountains region.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42174,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Water can be used abundantly.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42175,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Construction of small dams, not standing under a tree, construction of percolation tanks, cultivation of new forests.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','not standing under a tree','',0,0,0,NULL),(42176,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Famine, earthquake, cloudburst, railway accident.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','railway accident','',0,0,0,NULL),(42177,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Drought, heavy rains, storm, tsunami\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','drought ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42178,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Lava, hot mud, ash, locusts.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','locusts','',0,0,0,NULL),(42179,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Washing away of crops, attack of pests on crops, volcano, singeing of crops.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','Volcano ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42180,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give three example of : Natural disasters \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','Famine, earthquake, Tsunami','',0,0,0,NULL),(42181,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give three example of : Man made disaster\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','War, terrorism and bomb explosion are the examples of man-made disaster.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42182,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Have you seen a flash of lighting in the sky? When?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Yes, rainy season','',0,0,0,NULL),(42183,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is an earthquake?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The vibrations and the cracking up of the earth’s crust is called an earthquake.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42184,736,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will happen if an earthquake and volcanic eruption occur at the bottom of the sea?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  ','','','Tremendous energy comes out of the earth’s crust at the time of earthquake. If such movements occur at the bottom of the sea, the huge waves are formed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42185,736,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the types of disasters?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  ','','','Natural and man-made disaster are the two types of disasters.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42186,736,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any two measures to avoid the floods?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  ','','','i) Construction of small dams in mountainous region.\nii) Connecting rivers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42187,736,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write two destructive effects of tsunami?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  ','','','i) Buildings and other constructions are destroyed.\nii) Trees get uprooted and landslides take place on a large scale.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42188,736,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by man made disaster?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  ','','','i) The disaster that being happen by the people purposefully is known as man-made disaster.\nii) Ex. Terrorisms, bomb explosion.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42189,736,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Man is responsible for famines.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Man has done a lot of development but has not thought of conservation of natural resources.\nii) The human population rose tremendously causing water and food shortage.\niii) Due to Green Revolution, there was steep rise in food grain production, but at the same time excessive use of fertilizers and pesticides have created problems like pollution.\niv) The entire environmental balance is lost due to unplanned activities of man. Therefore, it is rightly said that man is responsible for famines.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42190,736,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Lives of people can be saved from the disaster of Tsunami.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Due to advances in science and technology, newer equipment’s are discovered.\nii) The artificial geostationary satellite is one such device that gives the warning of tsunami.\niii) In such cases people staying in coastal area are moved to other place for safety.\niv) Thus, lives of people can be saved from the disaster of Tsunami.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42191,736,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why shouldn’t we wait at the foothill while it is raining heavily?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) During cloud bursting, severe rainfall or floods there is possibility of landslides.\nii) The houses located on hills may come down due to such changes.\niii) The excessive rainfall can create strong water currents which flow down from the heights to the lower areas.\niv) Therefore, to save our lives, we should not wait at the foot hills.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42192,736,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What gives rise to a storm? What are their effects?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There is formation of high and low air pressure belts in the atmosphere.\nii) This causes strong winds to blow.\niii) The weather suddenly changes due to this resulting into storms.\nEffects of storm :\ni) there is a large scale damage to the property and life.\nii) The storm affected are greatly damaged.\niii) The electric supply and transportation is disrupted.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42193,736,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the remedial measures for the given calamities : Famine\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Proper planning of water and reuse it.\nii) Proper planning of water conservation and prevention at the local level. \niii) Large scale plantation of trees and precention of deforestation.\niv) Making appropriate changes in plans, taking into account the weathers forecasts.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42194,736,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the remedial measures for the given calamities : Lighting \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) One should stay at home when there is thundering and lightning.\nii) One should not take shelter under a tree, on the heights, or in open ground as there is higher chance of lightning sticking here.\niii) One should not stand near an electric pole, a telephone pole, a tower, etc. iv) Not to get into water for swimming.\nv) Install a lightening conductor on the top of one’s building.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42195,736,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the remedial measures for the given calamities : Storm\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) One should take a shelter in safe place.\nii) To avoid any damage the electrical supply should be stopped.\niii) Listening the radio or use the media to know the information.\niv) Do not exit out of the home during storm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42196,736,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the remedial measures for the given calamities : Cloudburst \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Making a river bank flat and keeping them clean.\nii) To inform to the disaster management team or department.\niii) Do not take shelter near the river bank\niv) Avoid to go out of the home\nv) Rivers should be connect to prevent the floods.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42197,736,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What should be done to prevent the bunds on a farm from getting washed away in the rainy season?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The bunds on the farm prevents the soil from getting eroded.\nii) During the rains the flow of water increases and can wash these bunds off.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42198,736,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What gives rise to a storm?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  ','','','The formation of low and high air pressure belts in the atmosphere causes changes in weather resulting a storms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42199,736,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the causes of famine?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Heavy rain and floods.\nii) Drought is the important cause of famine.\niii) Environmental changes such as storms, fog and global warming.\niv) Natural calamity like earthquake or cold.\nv) Damage of crops due to attack of paste, and mice, rats.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42200,736,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the measures taken to reduce the impacts of famine?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Proper planning of water and reuse it.\nii) Proper planning of water conservation and prevention at the local level. \niii) Large scale plantation of trees and precention of deforestation.\niv) Making appropriate changes in plans, taking into account the weathers forecasts.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42201,736,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a tsunami? What gives rise to a tsunami?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) ‘Tsunami’ means huge harbour wave which breaks at the coastal regions causing tremendous damage.\nii) These waves are formed due to volcanic eruption on the ocean floor or due to earthquake at the bottom of the ocean.\niii) The energy released because of the earthquake pushes the water upwards. This results in the formation of waves.\niv) These waves are not very high near the source, but they spread rapidly to long distance.\nv) The velocity of these waves is 800 to 900 km per hour.\nvi) Upon reaching the coastal area, their velocity is reduced, but their height increases tremendously to about 30 m. This causes a great damage.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42202,736,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a cloudburst?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The cloud bursting means the excessive rainfall suddenly pouring in a short span of time in a specific area.\nii) Due to very high temperature near the land, the water coming down from rain clouds does not reach the land in the form of rain. But vaporizes and goes back into the same clouds.\niii) This causes large amount of vapour in these clouds.\niv) When rapid condensation occurs in such water-laden clouds, it rains suddenly at a rate of 100 mm per hour or more in a specific area.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42203,736,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the effects of a volcano.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Whenever there is volcanic eruption, the chemical substances such as lava, vapour, hot mud, sulphur, etc. is thrown out.\nii) These substances are usually present in the interior of the earth’s crust. When they are thrown out they form mountains and hills and change the land form.\niii) There is atmospheric pollution due to the ash and gases ejected. \niv) Temperature of the surrounding area rises due to hot gases by the volcano.\nv) It starts raining after volcanic eruption.\nvi) Nearby forests and settlements gate buried under the hot mud.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42204,736,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the measures to prevent loss of life due to lighting?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) One should stay at home when there is thundering and lightning.\nii) One should not take shelter under a tree, on the heights, or in open ground as there is higher chance of lightning sticking here.\niii) One should not stand near an electric pole, a telephone pole, a tower, etc. iv) Not to get into water for swimming.\nv) Install a lightening conductor on the top of one’s building.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42205,736,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What measures have been taken to deal with calamities such as floods and landslides under the disaster management programme in Maharashtra?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following measures have been taken.\ni) Disaster management has been established the National Flood commission in 1976.\nii) Disaster management cell has been established by the Government of Maharashtra.\niii) Using this cell, people are given warning about the storm, floods or earthquake.\niv) Government makes efforts to prevent the large scale of damage of property and loss of life.\nv) People those suffered by the disaster, the shelter is provided by the government.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42206,736,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','With reference to disaster management what are the things in your house that you will check?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following things I have at my house.\ni) First aid kit	\nii) Torch light or batteries		\niii) Emergency contact numbers	\niv) Extra clothes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42207,736,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the disastrous effects of tsunami?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------  \n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There is a large scale loss of life and property.\nii) Buildings and other construction get destroyed.\niii) Changes take place in the original land near the coast giving rise to swamps. iv) Trees get uprooted Landslides take place.\nv) Traffic obstruction arise.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42422,740,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------- and ---------------- factors are dependent on each other.','','','Biotic, abiotic','',0,0,0,NULL),(42423,740,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Every living thing gets its food from the --------------------','','','environment ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42424,740,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Plants make their own food in the presence of -------------------','','','sunlight','',0,0,0,NULL),(42425,740,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Living things use -------------- from the air for breathing.','','','oxygen ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42426,740,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The variety we see in all the living things that belong to a particular area is called the ------------------- of that place.','','','biodiversity ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42427,740,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The surrounding and the conditions there in which affect the life of the organisms is called an -----------------','','','environment ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42428,740,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Environmental science studies ----------------------- between living and non-living things.','','','interactions','',0,0,0,NULL),(42429,740,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------------- are the main support for every food chain.','','','Plants ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42430,740,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For the existence of living things, it is important that ---------------------- is maintained.','','','environmental ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42431,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Microorganisms form a part of the environment.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42432,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: It is necessary to maintain biodiversity.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42433,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: A grasshopper eats birds.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42434,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Sunlight is an important factor for every environment.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42435,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Scientists make observations about biodiversity for just one week.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42436,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Plants use oxygen to prepare their food.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42437,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Plants use carbon dioxide to make their own food.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42438,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Microorganisms does not help in the decomposition of plant residue or excreta.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42439,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: The interaction between living and non-living things maintain the food chain in the environment. \n\nAns. : --------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42440,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Factors in the environment\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Living things, air, water, sunlight, soil','',0,0,0,NULL),(42441,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: The interaction among the living things in the environment.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Food chain, food web','',0,0,0,NULL),(42442,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: The main support of all food chains.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Plants','',0,0,0,NULL),(42443,740,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: The cycles in the environment. \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Water cycle, carbon dioxide – oxygen cycle, decomposition cycle carried out by micro-organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42444,740,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which components are included in the environment?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sunlight, air, water, soil, plants and animals are the components include in the environment. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42445,740,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','From where does the living things get its food.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Living things get its food from the environment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42446,740,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the abiotic components in the environment.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Air, soil, water, sunlight are abiotic components in the environment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42447,740,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What eats a grasshopper?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A grasshopper eats the worms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42448,740,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cross out the wrong word: The living things that are seen in the field and near the water source are of the same/different type.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','same','',0,0,0,NULL),(42449,740,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cross out the wrong word: Air, water, soil and sunlight are living/non-living factors in the environment.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','living ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42450,740,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cross out the wrong word: If all the sparrows from the surroundings disappear, then number insects/owls will rise.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','owls ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42451,740,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cross out the wrong word: Plants/Microorganisms decompose the dead and decaying materials in the environment. \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Plants ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42452,740,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cross out the wrong word: Environment balance should be maintained for the happiness/existence of the living things.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','happiness ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42453,740,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is food chain? Give an example of it.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Biotic components in the environment are dependent on each other for their food. This is called food chain.\nEx. A worm eats grass and leaves. A grasshopper eats worms, birds eat grasshopper.\nSunlight → plants → caterpillar → grasshopper → bird','',0,0,0,NULL),(42454,740,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the solution? We have to remove insect from the grain without using insecticides.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Grains should be dry in the sun		\nii) It helps to remove the insects from the grain	\niii) Neem leaves should be added','',0,0,0,NULL),(42455,740,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who study the biodiversity in a particular area? How do they do this?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n       ','','','i) The scientists make the observations about the biodiversity\nii) They make these observations in different conditions, in day and night and also in different seasons\niii) They use special devices to make the observations of living things found at great depths or heights\niv) They make large number of observations for a long time they keep on taking the observations and note down all the data.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42456,740,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it said that plants are the main support for all the type of food chains?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Plants prepare their own food with the help of sunlight\nii) For this process they need water and minerals from the soil and carbon dioxide from the air\niii) Many animals are dependent on plants for their food\niv) Such herbivorous (plant eating) animals are eaten by carnivores (flesh eating) animals\nv) Any food chain will not start without plants. Therefore, plants are said to be the main support for all types of food chains.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42457,740,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How the microorganisms maintain the environmental balance?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There are different types of microorganisms in the soil\nii) They help the process of decomposition. Plant residues, dead animals, excreta and other such material is decomposed by them\niii) This decomposition forms that substances which are useful for the plants for their growth\niv) When the plants die, once again micro- organisms release these useful substances back into the soil by decomposition. In this way microorganisms maintain the environmental balance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42458,740,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the deer’s food?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Deer is an herbivorous animal. It eats grass. In search of green grass, they more about in the jungle. It also feeds on leaves and smaller herbs and shrubs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42459,740,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the food of the tiger?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Tiger is a carnivorous animal. it eats by hunting on small prey. Flesh of these animals is food for the tiger.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42460,740,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who eats birds?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Smaller birds are killed and eaten by bigger birds. Cats and snakes may also hunt the smaller birds. Man also kills birds for food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42461,740,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the food of plants?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Water and minerals present in the soil is food for plants. \nii) Plants take up water and minerals from soil with the help of their roots.\niii) With the help of the sunlight they use carbon dioxide from the air and prepare their own food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42462,740,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the balance in the environment maintained?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The interaction amongst living things and between living and non-living things is taking place continuously.\nii) This helps to maintain the food chain in the environment \niii) The various cycles in the environment are maintained the environmental balance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42463,740,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What substances in the soil are useful for the growth of plants?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Micro-organisms in the soil helps in the process of decomposition of plant residue, dead animals, excreta etc.\nii) By this process ab excellent manure is formed in the soil.\niii) It helps for the growth of plants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42464,740,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make up the food chain: Frog, Kite, Worm, Snake, Grass.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Grass → worm → frog → snake → kite','',0,0,0,NULL),(42465,740,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is water cycle? How does it help living things?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Due to sun’s heat the water from all the water bodies on the earth evaporates.\nii) This water vapour goes up in the cooler heights and condenses forming clouds.\niii) The clouds bring rains back to the earth. This is called a water cycle.\niv) Due to the water cycle all the living things get water. Water is needed for survival.\nv) In this way, water cycle helps all the living things on the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42466,740,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the cycle of oxygen-carbon dioxide function in an environment?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) All the living things need oxygen for respiration. As they respire they produce carbon dioxide.\nii) This carbon dioxide is given out in the air. This carbon dioxide is used by the plans to prepare their food.\niii) In this process, plants give out oxygen in the air.\niv) In this way, plants and other living things balance the natural cycle of carbon dioxide- oxygen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42467,740,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','We have to remove insects from the given without using insecticides.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Let the grain dry in the sunlight. The harsh sunlight does not allow eggs and caterpillars to grow in the grain.\nii) Keep dried Neem leaves in the container in which the grains are stored. The Neem acts as a repellent for insects.\niii) Grains like beans are smeared with mud. The mud particles are keeping the weevils away from infesting the grains.\niv) The place where grain should be cool and dry. It should be airy. The chances of insect infestations are less in such storage places.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42468,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','About -------------- of the land of our country is in use for agriculture.','','','60%','',0,0,0,NULL),(42469,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','--------------- and ---------------- are two types of modern methods.','','','drip irrigation, sprinkle irrigation','',0,0,0,NULL),(42470,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Natural Fertilizers are called --------------------','','','Manures','',0,0,0,NULL),(42471,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------------- and --------------------- are sprayed on the crops.','','','insecticides, pesticides','',0,0,0,NULL),(42472,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The surplus food grain is stored in huge --------------------------','','','warehouses','',0,0,0,NULL),(42473,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The seasons from June to October is called ---------------- season.','','','Kharif ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42474,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In farming it is necessary to carry out the tasks out the tasks of ------------------- of the land.','','','tilling ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42475,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Overuse of fertilizers lowers the ------------------- of the soil.','','','fertility ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42476,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----------------- leaves are mixed with the grains.','','','Neem ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42477,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Squirrels store --------------------','','','seeds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42478,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','People are displaced due to disasters like -----------------------','','','earthquake ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42479,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The credit for research that led to Green Revolution goes to --------------------------','','','Dr. M. S. Swaminathan ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42480,741,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The laws made to ensure that person gets sufficient food are called ---------------------- laws.','','','security laws','',0,0,0,NULL),(42481,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns :','','','(1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(42482,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns :','','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – d), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(42483,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: There is only one way of farming.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42484,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: India is an agricultural country.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42485,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Use of improved seeds does not lead to higher yields.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42486,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Storing grains is an important task.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42487,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: The season from October to March is called season of summer crops.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42488,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Storing of harvested grains is not as important as the farming operations.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42489,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Crops are traditionally watered by canals.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42490,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false:  By obstructing the flow of river water downstream, the ground water level can decreases.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42491,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false:  Drip irrigation is a traditional method of agriculture.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42492,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Over watering makes the land saline and fallow.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42493,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: The harvested crop does not store properly. \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42494,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false:  Food is basic need.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42495,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Drip irrigation, traditional, irrigation method, sprinkle irrigation\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Traditional irrigation method','',0,0,0,NULL),(42496,741,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Garlic, Radishes, sweet potatoes \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','garlic ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42497,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the two modern methods of irrigation?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Drip and sprinkle irrigation are the two types of irrigation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42498,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the kinds of fertilizers.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Natural and chemical are two kinds of fertilizers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42499,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the plants which stored food in their stems.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Plants link onions, potatoes, ginger and garlic stored food in their stems.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42500,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','To whom is the credit of Green revolution in India given?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The credit of Green revolution is given to Dr. M. S. Swaminathan ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42501,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by organic farming?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Farming that relies on natural materials is called organic farming.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42502,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','From where does the farmer get information about modern agricultural technology?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Farmers get information about modern agriculture technology from Agricultural Assistance programmers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42503,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out the names of two varieties of improved seeds of Jowar.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','CSH4 and SSV-84, Phule Yashoda, Phule Mauli, Swati are the names of improved seed varieties of Jowar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42504,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out who helps the farmer to drive the ‘mot’?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A pair of oxen help the farmer to drive the mot’.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42505,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out how is groundwater lifted these days?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The groundwater was taken out with the help of hand pumps and by digging bore wells. Even now at many places hand pumps are used. However, in majority cases, now electric motor is fitted to draw water from the underground level.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42506,741,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which vegetables do we get around the time of Diwali? Which fruits? Which grains?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a) vegetables : Spiny gourd (Kantoli), Ridge gourd (Shiroli), Ghosali, Kohala\nb) Fruits : Sweet lime, Oranges, Apples, Musk melons\nc) Grains : Ragi, Rice ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42507,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the advantages of using improved seeds?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Improved seeds are essential for a good harvest.\nii) These seeds are developed through research\niii) These have a higher yield\niv) They can resist pest attacks.\nv) Such improved seeds lead to an increase in production.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42508,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the modern methods of irrigation? What are their advantages? \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Drip irrigation and sprinkler irrigation are two modern methods of the irrigation.\nAdvantages\ni) By these methods we can provide enough water to the plants\nii) These methods helps to save a lot of water\niii) In the drip irrigation the required amount of water. Drips only near the plants. Where as in the sprinkle spray water directly on plants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42509,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the ‘drip irrigation’ method.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Drip irrigation also known as trickle irrigation.\nii) In this method water drips slowly to the roots of plants. Through a network of valves, pipes are tubing.\niii) By using this method, we can save lot of water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42510,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the solution? The plant in the pot is not growing well.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Because, plants need a plenty of water and sunlight for growing well. But these content does not get in the right proportion to the potted plants.\nii) Roots of the plants do not grow well in the pot.\niii) Plants do not have a support\niv) Use the fertilizers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42511,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the purpose of storing foodgrain in our house?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) We need food grain throughout the year.\nii) For availability of food grains in the house we stored it.\niii) To avoid from mice, rats and insects’ food grains must be stored.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42512,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the advantage of storing grain in wattle and daub baskets?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Wattle and daub baskets have holes on their surface. This keeps air circulating around the stored grains.\nii) The grains do not become moist and thus do not catch fungus.\niii) Moreover, there is less chance of insect attack due to dry and airy conditions.\niv) The grains stored like this can remain in better condition for a longer period of time. Therefore, grains are stored in wattle and daub baskets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42513,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it necessary to store food grains?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In some years there is a surplus food grains production. Therefore, excess grains can be stored.\nii) In some years there are natural disasters and there is reduced crop yield. People need food grains during such times.\niii) To provide food grains research difficult times, it is necessary to store food grains.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42514,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which law reacted to food is enacted in India? What is the purpose of this law?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Food Security Law is enacted in India from 2013.\nii) Due to this law every person in India, gets sufficient food according to their needs.\niii) This law also fight against the ills like malnutrition, starvation and deaths due to hunger.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42515,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the activities done through Agricultural assistance programme? \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Farmers are given proper information and guidance about the latest technology, irrigation facilities, improved seeds, use of fertilizers and pesticides. ii) Farmers also gives information about weather forecast and other agriculture related information.\niii) Agricultural schools have started to teach modern technology to farmers and their family members.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42516,741,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the seasons for jowar, bajra, rice, mangoes, oranges and jack fruit?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a) Jowar and Bajra : During winter		\nb) Mango and Jackfruit : In summer	\nc) Rice : In monsoon/rainy season	\nd) Oranges : Beginning of winter ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42517,741,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the reasons due to which growing crops are damaged?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The condition of growing crops depends upon a quality of soil and cultivation.\nii) Crops can be damaged due to diseases and pest attacks.\niii) If the fertility of the soil is reduced it can affects on the crops.\niv) To prevent the damaging crops insecticides or pesticides must be sprayed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42518,741,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is done to avoid the damage to crops?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) To avoid the damage of crops as a remedy insecticides and pesticides must be sprayed.\nii) The certain chemicals are rubbed on the seeds before sowing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42519,741,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What causes the fertility of soil to diminish?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The overuse of chemical fertilizers is reduced the fertility of the soil.\nii) If the same crops are grown frequently in the field, the fertility of soil will be decreases.\niii) Over watering also makes the land saline and fallow.\niv) This lowers the fertility of the soil and reduces its productivity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42520,741,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What changes has modern technology brought about in the methods of farming?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Through the modern technology farmers get all information about agriculture.\nii) The farmers knows about irrigation facilities, improved seeds, use of fertilizer, latest technology and pesticides through Agriculture Assistance Programme \niii) The agriculture department spread of modern method of agriculture through agriculture universities, television, newspaper and various periodicals work.\niv) This helps to increase production.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42521,741,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What methods are used to preserve foodgrain?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Preserving food grain safely is as important as increasing methods are used for this purposes.\ni) The grain is dried well in the sun and filled in the sacks.\nii) The food grain is protected in houses or warehouses from mice, rats and insects to avoid spoil.\niii) Some chemical pests are sprayed at the place of storage.\niv) Some protective chemicals are mixed in the food grain.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42522,741,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is water made available for farming?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In traditional method of agriculture a ‘mot’ is used to drawn water from a well with the help of oxen.\nii) Water is made available by building dams and bunds on rivers to abstract the flow of water downstream.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42523,741,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Organic farming\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Traditional farming methods using natural and organic materials is known as organic farming.\nii) In this method, organic fertilizers and pesticides are used. They do not cause pollution of the soil.\niii) Manures consisting of animal and plant products increase the nutritive substances in the soil.\niv) The grains grown by this method are nutritious and tasty.\nv) More and more farmers are opting for organic methods of farming.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42524,741,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Green Revolution.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) India has become self-reliant as far as food grain production is concerned. ii) This abundant supply of grains in India due to revolution in agriculture is known as Green Revolution.\niii) The agricultural scientist Dr M. S. Swaminathan is responsible for this Green revolution.\niv) Due to research, there was improvement in the seeds of rice and wheat. Such seeds helped us to grow surplus crops.\nv) After green revolution, we are now exporting surplus grains to other countries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42525,741,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which parts of plants do we use as food?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Roots : Carrot, Beet root, Radish, Sweet potato\nii) Steam : Potato, Elephant’s foot (Suran), Ginger, Onion\niii) Leaves : Arum, Colocasia, Cabbage, all leafy green vegetables\niv) Fruits : Pumpkin, Tamarind, Bitter gourd, Snake gourd\nv) Flower : Cauliflower, Banana flower\nvi) Seeds : Beans, Lentil, Cow pea, Gram.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42526,742,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- are dried in the Sun.','','','Grains ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42527,742,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A fungus is a type of -----------------------','','','Micro-organisms','',0,0,0,NULL),(42528,742,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The foodstuffs should be preserved in ----------------------- containers.','','','Airtight ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42529,742,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- are obtained from the specific parts of the plants.','','','Spices ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42530,742,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: In summer/winter foodstuffs remain unspoiled for a longer period.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','winter ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42531,742,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: Grains are dried in the sun/shade before storing.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','Sun ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42532,742,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: Spores/seeds of fungus are present in air.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','Spores ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42533,742,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: Food in which plants/microorganisms grow is bad for health.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','microorganisms ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42534,742,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: Different spices are obtained from specific parts of the plants/animals.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','plants ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42535,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Food stuffs gets spoiled due to microorganisms.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(42536,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Milk does not spoil quickly.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42537,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: A fungus can grow at everywhere.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42538,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Spices has a different taste.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42539,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: When something is boiled, the microorganisms in it are destroyed.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42540,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Our food does not get spoiled when microorganisms begin to grow in it.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42541,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Foodstuffs dries in summer cannot be used for the rest of the year.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42542,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Foodstuffs get warmth when put in a fridge.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42543,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: One season’s crop must be stored and protected till the next season.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42544,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Fruits and vegetables do not taste their best in the season in which they are grown.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42545,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Mango pulp and milk are not spoiled quickly.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42546,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Cotton wool like fibers growing on chapatti are cotton wool fibers only.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42547,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Wheat, Rice, Tomato, Pulses.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','Tomato ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42548,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Papads, Jams Pickles, Jowar\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','Jowar ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42549,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Potato, Chilli, Milk, garlic\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','Milk ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42550,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Mango, Apple, Grape, Methi\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','Methi ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42551,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Sugar, Salt, Jams and Pickles, Vinegar\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------','','','Jams and Pickles','',0,0,0,NULL),(42552,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of grains\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wheat, Jowar, Rice, Pulses','',0,0,0,NULL),(42553,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of vegetables.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Cabbage, brinjal, Carrot, spinach','',0,0,0,NULL),(42554,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of Fruits.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Apple, banana, Mango, Grapes','',0,0,0,NULL),(42555,742,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of Spices \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Clove, Pepper, Garlic, Cinnamon','',0,0,0,NULL),(42556,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Preservative substances.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sugar, salt, asafetida, mustard, oil, vinegar','',0,0,0,NULL),(42557,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Foodstuffs prepared after drying in the Sun.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Papad, Kurdai, Sandage','',0,0,0,NULL),(42558,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Favourable factors for growth of microorganisms.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Air, moisture, warmth','',0,0,0,NULL),(42559,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Matter printed on the boxes or sealed bags foodstuffs.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Expiry date ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42560,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Place for the storage of onions and potatoes.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Airy and  dry place','',0,0,0,NULL),(42561,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following: Cotton-wool like fibers growth on chapatti.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Fungus ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42562,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which foods get spoil quickly?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Foods like milk and mango pulp gets spoil quickly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42563,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why does we boil the milk?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We boil the milk to preserve it for a long time','',0,0,0,NULL),(42564,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name any two methods that used for preserving foodstuffs?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Methods like cooking and boiling are used to preserve foodstuffs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42565,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the preservatives? \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sugar, salt, asafetida (hing), mustard oil and vinegar are the preservatives. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42566,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the foodstuffs get spoiled?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The food stuffs gets spoiled due to Microorganisms or germs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42567,742,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which foodstuffs spoil most quickly?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Milk and milk products, fruits, vegetables, fish, meat, etc. are the foodstuffs that spoil most quickly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42568,742,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the solution? The Papads have become soft and moist.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------           ','','','i) When the papads are come in the contact with the water or in the moist place, papads becomes soft and moist.\nii) We should be dried properly in the sun and preserve in the dry place.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42569,742,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the solution? Fruits like mangoes, amlas, guavas and vegetables like peas, onions, tomatoes, fenugreek are needed all year round. They are available in plenty only in certain seasons.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------    ','','','i) To preserve the fruits and vegetable for a long time, we should use the refrigerator or airtight cans to avoid the microorganisms.\nii) Vegetables like onion, potatoes, must be dried in the sun before storing. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42570,742,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Semolina (shevaya) do not get spoiled for a very long time. But kheer made from them spoils easily. Why is this so?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------    \n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Because, semolina do not have the water contain.\nii) It is dry foodstuff whereas the kheer has the water contain.\niii) Kheer is made by adding the milk and milk spoils very quickly. Therefore, Kheer spoils quickly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42571,742,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: Leftover food prepared for one meal is stored in a refrigerator.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------    \n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The leftover food prepared for one meal may become spoilt after sometime in order to keep it safe and proper.\nii) It is stored in a refrigerator in cooler temperature.\niii) In cooler temperature, the spoiling of food is not rapid and therefore food is well preserved in a refrigerator for later use.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42572,742,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: Milk and eggs are carefully stored and then sent to their users. \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------    \n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Milk and eggs are produced in faraway places from the cities where dairy, and poultry is present.\nii) Both the food products can be spoiled easily.\niii) Therefore, they are carefully stored and then sent to their users.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42573,742,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: We store foodstuffs that we need for several days in our house.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------    \n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When we cook food we need various provisions which have to be brought from market.\nii) We have to go again and again in the market to get these provisions.\niii) In order to avoid this we store foodstuffs that we need for several days in our house.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42574,742,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What measures are taken to keep the foodstuffs in the house in good condition, for as long as possible?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------    ','','','We keep certain foodstuffs in the refrigerator. Some foodstuffs are dried and then stored. Some are processed and kept for a longer time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42575,742,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What precaution are taken before sealing the food in airtight containers?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Make sure your food storage containers are clean and in good condition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42576,742,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is date of expiry? Where is it written?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The date printed on the container of an item for sale, especially food or medicine, after which the item can no longer be sold and should not be used.\nExpiry date printed on the package of the product. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42577,742,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different methods of preserving food?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following methods are used for preserving food : Drying, cooling, boiling, salting, placing in airtight cans and pasteurization are the methods of preserving food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42578,742,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do we avoid eating that has got spoiled?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Most of the food stuffs got spoiled due to microorganisms.\nii) If we ate such food can causes a stomachache, diarrhea as well as vomiting. iii) The nutrition value of such food is also reduced and such food is not a fit for our health. Therefore, we should avoid eating food that has got spoiled.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42579,742,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do we make jams from fruits?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Some fruits has a acid contain with a sour taste.\nii) Jams which made up of fruits is preserved for a long time by adding preservatives.\niii) Jams does not get spoil quickly. Therefore, jams is made from fruits.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42580,742,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are preservatives?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Certain substances are added to jams and pickles to preserve them for a long time. They are used for preserve the food for a long time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42581,742,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out the names of the different spices. Also find out which part of their plants they are.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------\n \n          --------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Answer :','',0,0,1,NULL),(42582,742,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Is the time in which foodstuffs get spoilt different in different seasons?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Spoiling of foodstuffs is dependent on the temperature and humidity of the surrounding.\nii) Food spoilage is rapid when the surrounding temperature and humidity is more.\niii) If the surrounding air is dry and cool, the food spoilage is not immediate. \niv) Thus, the food stuffs take different time for spoilage in different seasons.\nv) In summer season, food may get spoilt rapidly but not so during winter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42583,742,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','When do we say that the foodstuffs is spoiled? What changes are seen in it?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The foodstuffs that is smelling foul and with unpleasant appearance is a spoilt food.\nii) Sometimes there is growth of fungus on the foodstuff.\niii) The spoilt food undergoes chemical changes.\niv) The colour and taste of such food is changed.\nv) There is growth of fungus and microorganisms in such food. Such foodstuffs develop hazardous substances in it. This food is bad for health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42584,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Stars have their own ---------------------- ','','','light ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42585,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Movement of the earth around the sun is called its ----------------','','','revolution ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42586,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The moon is called the -------------------- of the earth.','','','satellite ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42587,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Pluto is a -------------------------- ','','','dwarf planet','',0,0,0,NULL),(42588,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Due to the force of earth’s -----------------, all things on the earth remain on it.','','','gravity ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42589,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: On a clear night sky, we can see many stars/lamps.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','stars ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42590,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: Planets have/do not have their own light.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','do not have ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42591,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: Heavenly bodies generally have a square/round shape.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','round ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42592,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word:  Between the planets, Mars and Jupiter, there are numerous dwarf planet /asteroids.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','asteroids','',0,0,0,NULL),(42593,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: The moon/sun is closest to the earth as compared to other heavenly bodies.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','Moon ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42594,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: Scientists who travel in the spacecraft are called pilots/astronauts.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','astronauts','',0,0,0,NULL),(42595,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: Very powerful rockets/crackers are used to send a spacecraft into space.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','rockets','',0,0,0,NULL),(42596,743,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word: All the bodies in the solar system get light from the sun/moon.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','Sun ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42597,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Sky watching activity can be organized during the daytime.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(42598,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: There are nine planets in our solar system.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42599,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Diwali firecrackers are packed with explosives\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42600,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Man-made satellites can remain in the space for only one year.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42601,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: The degradation of earth’s environment for any reason will be a threat to the existence of life itself.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42602,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? You can see me from the earth but the lighted part of me that you see changes every day.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','Moon ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42603,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? I have my own light. It is only from me that the planets get light and heat.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','Sun ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42604,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? I turn around myself, around a planet and also around a star.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','Satellite ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42605,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? I turn around myself and revolve around the sun.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','Planet','',0,0,0,NULL),(42606,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? No other planet has a living world like mine.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','Earth ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42607,743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? I am the nearest star to the earth.\n\nAns. : --------------------------','','','Sun ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42608,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Stars and Planets.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer :','',0,0,1,NULL),(42609,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the meaning of the Planets?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Heavenly bodies that cannot twinkle but get light from the sun and those that revolve around the sun are called planets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42610,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the meaning of the Solar system?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The sun, the planets revolving around the sun along with satellites, asteroids and dwarf planets together form the solar system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42611,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the meaning of the Stars?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The heavenly bodies having light of their own are called stars.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42612,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the meaning of the Asteroids? \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A band of numerous small heavenly bodies between Mars and Jupiter is called the belt of asteroids. Each asteroids also revolves around the sun.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42613,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the meaning of the Dwarf planets?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Small heavenly bodies revolving around the sun independently in their own orbit are called dwarf planets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42614,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which is the star nearest to the Earth?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The sun is the stars nearest the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42615,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why can’t we see stars during the daytime?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','During daytime, there is bright between and beyond the stars and planets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42616,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is space?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Space is the emptiness between and beyond the stars and planets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42617,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do the heavenly bodies exert a force of attraction on each other?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Heavenly bodies exert a force of attraction due to the force of gravity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42618,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is an orbit?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','An orbit is the specific path on which the planets move around the sun.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42619,743,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write long form of ISRO.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Indian Space Research Organization','',0,0,0,NULL),(42620,743,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: The moon is the satellite of the earth.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Our Earth is a planet		\nii) Satellites revolve around the planets	\niii) The moon revolves around the earth		\niv) Both the earth and moon receive a light from the sun.	\niv) Therefore, the moon is the satellite of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42621,743,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: The sun appears prominent and bright.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The sun is a star having its own light. As compared to other stars in space, the sun is closest to the earth. Therefore, the sun appears prominent and bright.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42622,743,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: All the things on the earth remain with the earth only.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Earth exerts gravitational force	\nii) Due to this force of gravity, even if something is thrown in the upward direction, it comes back towards the ground	\niii) Therefore, all the things on the earth remain with the Earth only.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42623,743,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason: Diwali rocket firecrackers are packed with explosive substances. \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When explosive in the firecracker burn, they produce a lot of energy	\nii) With this energy, the rocket firecracker is pushed in a certain direction with a speed		\niii) The design of the rocket is also favourable for speedy movement 		\niv) For this speed, explosive substances are packed in the rocket firecracker.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42624,743,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which direction do these things fall? a) Leaves, flowers, fruit from a tree	b) Rocks that come loose from a hillside 	c) Rain falling from the sky.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Due to the force of gravity, all the things fall towards the earth’s surface.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42625,743,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on Man-made satellites.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Man- made satellite are put around orbit of the earth. They can remain in space for many years.\nii) Man- made satellite can provide important information for agriculture, environment and weather forecasting, maps, searching for water and mineral wealth and telecommunication.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42626,743,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will happen to our solar system if the sun was to suddenly disappear? \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There would be darkness everywhere	\nii) All the plants would die as they will not be able to perform photosynthesis		\niii) All the planets would stop revolving 	\niv) The cycle of day and night would come to a halt		\nv) There will not be weeks or months anymore		\nvi) There will be uniformly only one season, seasonal changes will come to an end.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42627,743,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Suppose you want to give your address to a friend you have on the planet Mars. How will you write your address if you want them to understand exactly where you live? \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Mr. --------\nGanesh khind, Sadashive Peth, Pune – 411023\nState of Maharashtra, India, Continent of Aisa, Earth, (3rd planet) \nSolar system, Milky way. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42628,743,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which points are to be considered while observing the sky?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','While observing the sky the following points are to be considered : \ni) The brightness of the heavenly bodies	\nii) Whether the heavenly bodies are twinkling	\niii) The colour and size of these heavenly bodies	\niv) The changes in the position of these heavenly bodies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42629,743,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','When and where is the sky watching activity arranged?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Sky watching activity is arranged only at night		\nii) Large open space is chosen for sky watching		\niii) The sky should be clear and dark for observing heavenly bodies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42630,743,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write down the names of all the planets in correct order as they are present in our solar system.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune are the planets present in our solar system	\nii) Mercury is closest to the sun while the remaining occur in the above order.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42631,743,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which heavenly bodies form our solar system?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The sun and the eight planets revolving around the sun along with their satellites. dwarf planets and asteroids together from the solar system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42632,743,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','For what purpose are rockets used in space travel?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Space launch technology is used to send the spacecraft in outer space against the force of gravity	\nii) It requires tremendous amount of energy to launch the spacecraft which thousands of tons in weight		\niii) This energy is obtained through burning of ample quantity of fuel present in the rocket		\niv) Thus, rockets are used to launch the spacecraft in space travel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42633,743,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What information do man-made satellites provide?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Man- made satellites provide useful information on agriculture, environment, weather forecasting, searching for water and minerals from the Earth		\nii) Such information can be used to make maps	\niii) Man- made satellites are also used for telecommunications.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42634,744,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The motion of the earth around itself is called ----------------------','','','rotation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42635,744,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The motion of the earth around the sun is called -------------------------','','','revolution ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42636,744,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The rotation of the earth gives rise to -------------------------','','','day and night ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42637,744,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The axis of the earth is ---------------------------','','','inclined ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42638,744,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The earth takes ---------------- days and --------------- hours to complete one revolution around the sun.','','','365 days and 6 hours','',0,0,0,NULL),(42639,744,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the -------------------- calendar, the year is taken to have ---------------- days.','','','Gregorian, 365','',0,0,0,NULL),(42640,744,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','From the new moon to the full moon the moon is seen to become ----------------------','','','bigger and bigger ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42641,744,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ------------------- of the axis of earth gives rise to the cycle of the seasons.','','','inclination ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42642,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The earth rotates from east to west.\n\nAns. : -------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42643,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false :  In a leap year, there are 28 days in the month of February.\n\nAns. : -------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42644,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : We do not see the moon in the sky on a new moon day.\n\nAns. : -------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42645,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The lighted part of the moon decreases in size and seems smaller and smaller in Shukla paksha.\n\nAns. : -------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42646,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : There is a waxing phase from the full moon day to the new moon day.\n\nAns. : -------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42647,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by full moon?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the complete and round moon is seen in the sky, it is known as full moon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42648,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by new moon?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the moon is not visible in the sky, it is known as new moon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42649,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by Lunar month?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The period of 28 to 30 days from one new moon day to the next new moon day is called a lunar month.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42650,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by Tithi?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Every day in the lunar month is called Tithi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42651,744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by a leap year?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The year in which there are 366 days with February having 29 days, is called a leap year. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42652,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by rotation?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The turning of an object or planet around its axis is called rotation. The earth takes 24 hours to complete one rotation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42653,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by revolution?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The movement of a planet around the star is called revolution. The Earth takes one year to revolve completely around the sun. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42654,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by Krishna paksha?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The period of fortnight in which the illuminated portion of the moon appears to reduce and become smaller and smaller is called Krishna Paksha or phase of waning moon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42655,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by Shukla paksha?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The period of fortnight in which the illuminated portion of the moon appears to increase every day is called Shukla Paksha or phase of waxing moon. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42656,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by the phase of the moon?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The different shapes of the moon which are visible from the earth, from the full moon day to the new moon day and vice-versa are called phases of the moon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42657,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which was the recently observed leap year?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A leap year in which there are 29 days in the month of February or 366 days in that year.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42658,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Are there any other types of calendars in addition to the Gregorian calendar?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Indian, Chinese, Islamic, Julian calendars.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42659,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the months of winter in the Northern hemisphere and summer in the Southern hemisphere.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','December, January and February in the Northern hemisphere and June, July and August in Southern hemisphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42660,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the name given to the changing shapes of the moon that we see?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The changing shapes of the moon that we see are called the phases of the moon. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42661,744,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the names of the days on which we see a round moon and on which we see no moon at all?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The day on which we see a complete and round moon is called the full moon day. The day on which we cannot see the moon from the earth is called the new moon day.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42662,744,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the equator?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','An imaginary circle present between the North and the South pole is called the Equator.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42663,744,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the two parts of the earth made by the equator?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The Northern hemisphere and Southern hemisphere are the two parts of the earth made by the equator.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42664,744,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are poles of the earth?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The earth’s axis passes through the centre of the earth. The two ends of the axis are called the north pole and the South pole. The line connecting the two poles is called NS line. NS line thus connects the two poles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42665,744,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the months of more daylight in the Northern hemisphere?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','It occurs on June 20 or 21 in the Northern Hemisphere','',0,0,0,NULL),(42666,744,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the summer months in the Southern hemisphere?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','December, January, February','',0,0,0,NULL),(42667,744,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the sub-seasons in India.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The sub-seasons in India are Vasant, Grishma, Varsha, Sharad, Hemant and Shishir.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42668,744,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many rotations does the earth complete during one revolution around the sun?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The Earth takes one complete rotation in one day. It takes complete 365 days and one, 1/4th day for one complete revolution. This means that in one revolution, the earth shows 365 and 1/4th rotations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42669,744,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','It is the sunrise at Itanagar in Arunachal Pradesh. Write the names of the following cities in the order in which the sun will rise there : Mumbai (Maharashtra), Kolkata (West Bengal), Bhopal (Madhya Pradesh), Nagpur (Maharashtra).\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The Earth rotates from West to East. Therefore, there would be sunrise in the eastern states earlier. Later gradually the sunrise can be seen from the Western states of India. The order in which the sunrise would be seen is as follows : a) Itanagar in Arunachal Pradesh  b) Kolkata (West Bengal)	c) Bhopal (Madhya Pradesh)		d) Nagpur (Maharashtra)		e) Mumbai (Maharashtra)','',0,0,0,NULL),(42670,744,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the solution? Amit wants to take granny to Australia which is in the Southern hemisphere. But she cannot bear very cold weather. When should they make this trip?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Australia is located in Southern hemisphere. During the time period of 23rd September to 22 March, there is a summer. So, Amit’s granny could be taken to Australia during this period. During the period of 22nd March to 23rd September there is winter so she should avoid going to Australia as she cannot bear cold weather.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42671,744,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In India various festivals are celebrated as per the tithi of the lunar month. Some religions observe the phases of the moon for the celebrations. Tell ten names of such festivals which are celebrated according to tithi.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Ramnavami, Gokulashtami, Dussehra or Vijayadashmi, Nagpanchmi, Ashadhi Ekadashi, Bahubijm (Yam Dwitiya), Mahaveer Jayanti, Narak Chaturdashi, Dhantrayodashi, Ganesh Chaturthi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42672,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Substances that have got mixed with the water are ------------------- for living things.','','','harmful ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42673,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Waste water contains many -----------------------.','','','impurities ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42674,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sewage water can carry many ------------------- that cause disease.','','','microorganism','',0,0,0,NULL),(42675,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Before water is supplied to a town or city, it is ------------------------','','','purified ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42676,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','It is ------------------------ for a factory owner to treat water begore letting it out.\n\n','','',' compulsory','',0,0,0,NULL),(42677,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There is a threat to the ------------------- living things if the proportion of oxygen in water is reduced.','','','aquatic ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42678,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A famine is a ---------------------- disaster.','','','natural ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42679,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------------------- are set up to take care of domestic animals.','','','Fodder camps','',0,0,0,NULL),(42680,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- are presents in sewage water that spread disease.','','','Micro-organisms','',0,0,0,NULL),(42681,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The proportion of ---------------------- dissolved in the water is reduced.','','','Oxygen ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42682,745,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- is mixed in the water to kill the germs in it.','','','Chlorine ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42683,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : We get rainwater throughout the year.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42684,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : During a famine, the government moves people and animals temporarily to safe places.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42685,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The sewage water does not have the impurities.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42686,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Grain and fodder become scare during a famine.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42687,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Large dams can be built in all places.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42688,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Bunds should be built for storing water.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42689,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Flowing water of rivers get purified completely by natural processes.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42690,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Water evaporates continuously.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42691,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Water pollution is not harmful.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42692,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of Sources of water.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wells, tube-wells, rivers, lakes, ponds, streams','',0,0,0,NULL),(42693,745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of Methods of purification of water.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Settling, Oxygenation, Filtration and Chlorination ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42694,745,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is achieved by stopping the water from flowing away and making it percolate in the soil?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Water management ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42695,745,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who undertakes several works of making water available even after the rainy season?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The Government and the local people','',0,0,0,NULL),(42696,745,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is done in water-works?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Purification of water ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42697,745,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the three states of water?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Solid, liquid and gaseous are the three states of water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42698,745,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which method will you use to kills the germs in water?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Chlorination method will be used to kill germs in water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42699,745,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by scarcity of water?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Places at which falls the water level, there is no water foe animals and agriculture. This is called scarcity of water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42700,745,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the different methods that used for purification of water.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Settling, oxygenation, filtration and chlorination are the methods used for purification of water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42701,745,'descriptive','2',2,1,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when a spoonful sugar, sawdust and soil are added to a glass of water?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When sugar is added to the water, it dissolves completely and disappears in water. When sawdust is added, it will float on the surface of the water. when soil is added it will settle at the bottom of the water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42702,745,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is done to make drinking water clean and safe?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Methods of settling and filtering will make the water clean. For making it safe it is boiled for some time. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42703,745,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What conditions prevail during a famine?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The following conditions prevail during a famine :\ni) The water level falls in river, wells, lakes, bunds as well as in dams.\nii) Humans and animals suffered from scarcity of water.\niii) Grain and fodder became scared during a famine\niv) There is no water for agriculture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42704,745,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What works are undertaken to make water available even after the rainy season?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Works such as building smaller, reservoirs, contour bunding, stopping the water of the smaller stream by constructing bunds.\nii) The large dams can be built to make availability of water after the rainy season.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42705,745,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it necessary to stop rainwater?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) We get rain for only four months but we needs of water throughout the year.\nii) To meet the whole year’s need for water, rainwater must be stopped.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42706,745,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by water management?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Water management is the process of stopping or storing water into the ground to make it available even after the rainy season.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42707,745,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does water pollution take place?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) the waste water from households is collected and let out in larger water bodies in cities and villages.\nii) This water contains many impurities. The sewage water contains many diseases-causing micro-organisms.\niii) Poisonous substances are present in the water let out by industries and factories.\niv) Some of the impurities are dissolved in water while some are floating in water. All these impurities cause water pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42708,745,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the methods of storing rainwater?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Wells are dug in the river beds and water is allowed to collect in these wells\nii) Rainwater falling of the rooftops of houses is allowed to flow through pipes in the tanks placed beside the buildings\niii) Bunds or bandharas are constructed on the smaller streams so that the water stops and percolates in the soil. Smaller reservoirs are also made to store rainwater.\niv) Stopping the rainwater and allowing it to percolate in the ground, helps to raise the ground water level. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42709,745,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can we reduce the proportion of water pollution?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Sewage and waste water of towns and cities must be purified before letting into a water body.\nii) Water that released from factories should be treat before letting out.\niii) Trench latrines, septic tanks and soak pits should be built to drain away the waste water from toilets and latrines.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42710,745,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','While it is raining, collect some of the rainwater directly in a clean container placed in an open. Also collect some of the rainwater that is flowing over the ground. Observe and compare the two. What difference do you see? What is the reason for this?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Water collected directly from the falling rain will appear to be clean. The water that flows over the ground will appear turbid. Since water flowing over the ground carries a lot of contamination, it will look turbid and dirty.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42711,745,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution : Soil in a garden gets washed away with the water due to the slope of the land.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The lateral trenches should be dug at regular intervals across the slope of the land\nii) These trenches prevents flowing soil with the water.\niii) Bunds should be built at slope of the land.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42712,745,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How should roads and footpaths be built to make rainwater seep into the ground?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Trenches should be built at both sides to allow the flowing rainwater\nii) Bunds should be built at the sides of the footpaths\niii) Roads should be built with the slopes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42713,745,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution : Your class will be visiting a lake which is far away from the city. Arrangements have to be made for the day’s drinking water.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When will go for a trip we always carry water bottles with us.\nii) We will be visiting a lake so potable water is also available there. However, it may not be clean and safe.\niii) So, we need to carry a clean cloth for filtering the water, a piece of alum for settling the impurities, a purifying chemical for making water safe.\niv) If we go prepared like this, then there will not be a problem of a day’s safe drinking water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42714,745,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : Water management is being used in your surroundings. \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The flowing rainwater is collected into the tanks and reused. This is called water management.\nii) In this method rainwater is stored by the digging a well. \niii) Rainwater falling on roofs of houses is drainage into large tanks.\niv) Flowing rainwater makes seep into the ground.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42715,745,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','List the substances that are mixed in the waste water drained out of the kitchen and bathroom in your house.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Various substances are mixed with waste water that drains out of the kitchen and bathroom of our house.\nii) This drained water contains excreta, urine, bathing water, liquid soap, detergents, water containing leftover food particles, dirty water formed due to washing utensils and clothes and various other contaminants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42716,745,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If, at some place, it did not rain for a very long period of time, what would be its effect on the life of the people there?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) If a particular place does not get enough rains for a long period of time then there would be scarcity of water.\nii) If water is not available, farming cannot be done.\niii) Crops will not grow. People might have to migrate in search of food and water.\niv) Cattle would also die due to lack of food and water. This complete area will be under drought. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42717,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The population of the world is now close to -------------- crore.','','','six hundred ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42718,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Marshy or low-lying areas are filled to -------------- the land.','','','reclaim ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42719,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Due to pollution, number of living things reduce and some become ------------------','','','extinct ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42720,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In industries and factories, ------------ is used in great quantities.','','','fuel ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42721,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Tremendous quantities of -------------- enter the air due to burning of fuels.','','','carbon dioxide ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42722,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Due to human -------------- in nature, large scale changes are taking place in the air, water and land.','','','intervention ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42723,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','We should make all possible efforts for ---------------- of the living world.','','','conservation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42724,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Forest areas reserved for the protection and conservation of a particular animal or a plant is called a -----------------','','','sanctuary ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42725,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Flamingo from --------------- region in Northern Asia migrate to Mayani.','','','Siberia','',0,0,0,NULL),(42726,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----------- or -------------- can be called the sanctuaries of ancient times.','','','Devrais or Sharanavanas ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42727,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The environment provides --------------- to different kinds of living things.','','','shelter ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42728,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The proportion of ----------------- in the air leads to rise the temperature.','','','carbon dioxide ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42729,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Food, clothes and ------------------ are our basic needs.','','','shelter ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42730,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the plants on the earth are destroyed the number of ----------------- also fall.','','','animals ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42731,746,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Indian culture gives importance to the protection of ----------------------','','','forest ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42732,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Dead plants and animals are abiotic components.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42733,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : It is necessary to conserve biodiversity.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42734,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Man is not using up more and more land and water resources.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42735,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Soil becomes fertile, if polluted water seeps into it.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42736,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The temperature of the earth rises when the proportion of carbon dioxide rises.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42737,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The process of living things becoming extinct is at a very slow rate.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42738,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : People visiting biodiversity park enjoy being close to nature.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42739,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Deer are found in the grasslands of Nannaj.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(42740,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns : ','','','(1 – b – r), (2 – a – p), (3 – c – q)','',1,0,0,NULL),(42741,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was Mayani Lake formed? Which birds come to this lake?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','By building a dam on the Chand river in Satara district, Mayani lake was formed. Flemingo from Siberia migrate to build nests and lay eggs on this lake.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42742,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it said that Maldhok is a friend of farmers?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','These birds of grasslands feed on insects and thus protect crops. Therefore, they said that friends of farmer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42743,746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which village in Maharashtra gives security of peafowls? \n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Morachi Chincholi near Pune','',0,0,0,NULL),(42744,746,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by migration?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The movement of animals from one area to other is known as migration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42745,746,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the lifecycle of birds.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Birds are the important biotic factor	\nii) They insects which have on the plants		\niii) Birds lives in the forest, lay eggs, give birth to young one	\niv) If the number of trees are decreased they are also extinct.	','',0,0,0,NULL),(42746,746,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two causes of air pollution.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Unlimited growth in the number of factories and vehicles		\nii) Cutting down the trees		\niii) Burning of plastic or rubber		\niv) An increasing the proportion of carbon dioxide in the air these are all the causes of air pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42747,746,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','For what purpose do we use land obtained by clearing forests?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) We used the land for agriculture		\nii) Land is used for building houses	\niii) Land is used to making roads as well as factories 	\niv) For the railway land is used','',0,0,0,NULL),(42748,746,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Many aquatic animals are becoming extinct.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Soil and water is polluted to a great extent due to human intervention.\nii) The chemical fertilizers and insecticides used in farming practices also find their way into water.\niii) Waste water from industries, factories and household also find their way into water.\niv) All these may have poisonous and harmful substances in them. Therefore, many aquatic animals living in such water are becoming extinct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42749,746,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What could the reasons of air pollution?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Some natural events may also cause air pollution. E.g. Cyclones, volcanic eruptions, strong winds.\nii) But air pollution is caused to a greater extent due to human activities. E.g.  burning of fossil fuel, the toxic gases and particles, etc. emitted from industries and during transport.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42750,746,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different purposes for which fuels such as petrol, diesel, kerosene, natural gas, coal and wood are used?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Petrol and diesel are used as a fuel in vehicles used for transport.\nii) Two wheelers, cars, trucks and aeroplanes run on these fuels.\niii) Kerosene, cooking gas (LPG) and wood are used as fuels for cooking and other domestic use.\niv) Coal is used for electricity generation and also in industrial process.\nv) Diesel is also used to run engines and machines in factories.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42751,746,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What makes the loss of biodiversity?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Growth in human population : The human population of the world is continuously increasing. Now it is close to six hundred crore.\nii) Deforestation : Due to increasing human population, the needs of man are ever rising. For agriculture, housing, industry, building roads and railways, forest are cut down.\niii) All such activities are resulting into reduction of forests. Due to this Shelters of animals are destroyed resulting in loss of biodiversity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42752,746,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following as natural or man made : Soil, horse, stone, water hyacinth, book, sunlight, dolphin, pen, chair, water, cotton wool, table, trees, brick.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a) Natural : Soil, horse, stone, water hyacinth, sunlight, dolphin, water, cotton wool, trees\nb) Man-made : Book, pen, chair, table, brick\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42753,746,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution : Rivers and lakes are filled with water hyacinth.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) If the water hyacinth grow excessively it causes problems for other aquatic organism.\nii) In order to save environment of such a water body, water hyacinth should be removed.\niii) Care should be taken not to use organic pollutants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42754,746,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What would happen if no kites (birds) are left in a particular region. Which living things would increase in number? Which would decrease?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) If the birds are left some kinds of insects will be increased in that region. \nii) The cycles are very important because all animals are dependent on one another for their food.\niii) If no birds are left in the region large number of insects are increased which can be affects on the plants also.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42755,746,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What should all of us resolve about environment?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) We should resolve that no action should be done that will cause pollution or cause harm to living things.\nii) All the possible efforts should be done to conserve nature and living world.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42756,746,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of question below, explain what would happen if forests are cleared on a large scale. Will the space occupies by the plants and animals increase or decrease? Why?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Due to deforestation, the plants and Shelters of animals will be destroyed completely. The barren space would increase but the space occupied by living things would decrease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42757,746,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of question below, explain what would happen if forests are cleared on a large scale. Will the number of living things increase or decrease? Why?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','After deforestation, the number of living things would definitely decrease. Due to loss of forests, there is a loss of habitat. There would be loss of food and shelter and thus the number of living things will dwindle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42758,746,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : It is important to conserve the living components of the environment.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Living components are very important factors of the environment.\nii) Living components includes plans and animals. They are dependent on each other.\niii) We get foods from this living component.\niv) If the living component do not exist then the food chain and food web will not function.\nv) Some species may become exist\nvi) Therefore, it is important to conserve living component of environment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42759,746,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : The numbers of wild animals are falling day-by-day.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Man has cutting down the trees for building houses, roads, factories and for agriculture.\nii) Due to loss of forest, the animals are displaced.\niii) Hunting of animals is increased day by day. Therefore, the number of wild animals are falling day by day.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42760,746,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write some information about the Great Indian Bustard or Maldhok.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The Great Indian Bustards are huge weighty birds which are famous for their graceful gait.\nii) These birds live in the open grasslands. \niii) They feed on insects from the grasses and farms. Thus, they provide protection to the crops. Therefore, they are called friends of farmers.\niv) These birds were hunted for their meet and eggs.\nv) Their number was therefore dwindling.\nvi) But now Maharashtra State Government has declared Nannaj Solapur district as Maldhok Sanctuary.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42761,746,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it said that village “Morachi Chincholi” is the best example of conservation of environment?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Morachi chincholi is the village present on the Pune- Ahmednagar Highway.\nii) This village is famous for peafowl population. The local people of this village give protection to the peacocks and peahens. \niii) There are many tamarind trees. These old and well looked after trees give favourable environment to the peafowl.\niv) Nobody disturbs the peafowl population and therefore village Morachi Chicholi is said to be the best example of conservation of the environment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42762,746,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a dam is built at a particular place, what changes will be seen in the environment?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When a large dam is constructed, we get ample water.\nii) In some parts there will be growth of vegetation. But the natural environment which was there previously will be completely lost.\niii) The plant cover, trees, etc. which were there will be totally lost.\niv) Some animals would be completely displaced. Some will be extinct.\nv) Human being’s residents of those areas will be displaced and they will have to migrate to some other place.\nvi) The local environment will be changed forever.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42763,746,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of question below, explain what would happen if forests are cleared on a large scale. Will water and food sources for living things grow or dwindle? Why?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) If deforestation is done on a largest scale, there will be a decrease in the water and food sources for resident living organisms.\nii) Food chains will be disturbed. Animals which are dependent on each other for food will die due to want of food. \niii) When trees are cut down the sources of water will also deplete. The roots of trees hold the soil particles and water.\niv) When forest are cut down there will be soil erosion. There will be decrease in the rainfall resulting in loses of sources of water.\nv) Ground water level will also decrease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(42764,746,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of question below, explain what would happen if forests are cleared on a large scale. Will the living things look foe shelters elsewhere or stay on? Why?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Animals staying in forests with migrate elsewhere in search of food and water.\nii) Because their Shelters are lost, they might also encroach in the surrounding areas in search of shelter.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(42817,751,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','19 ही संख्या रोमन अंकामध्ये खालीलपैकी कोणती येईल?\n         a) XVIIII			b) IXX 		c) XIX 			d) XXI\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(42818,751,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','XVII ही रोमन संख्या आंतरराष्ट्रीय संख्या मध्ये खालीलपैकी कोणती येईल?\n         a) 12 			b) 17 			c) 12 		d) 21\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(42819,751,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','D अक्षराने खालीलपैकी कोणती रोमन संख्या दाखविली जाते?\n         a) 500			b) 1000		c) 50 		d) 10\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(42820,751,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','10 ही संख्या रोमन संख्या ने खालीलपैकी कोणत्या अक्षराने दाखविली जाते?\n         a) V 			b) X 			c) L 		d) M\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(42821,751,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','C अक्षर खालीलपैकी कोणता रोमन अंक दाखविते?\n         a) 50 			b) 10 			c) 1