\n  \n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a) i) boxer                   ii) awareness \nb) i) box - The box is full of chocolates. \nii) aware - I have been made acutely aware of that fact since I was a child. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45340,575,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n          i. beautify                  ii. courage \n                                     OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n         i. beautify                     ii. courage ','','','a) i) beautiful                    ii) courageous \n b) i) beautify - You can beautify your home or office without spending a lot of money \nii) courage - He was very courageous to do anything for his family. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45341,575,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n            i. manage                 ii. assess \n                                   OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n                 i. manage                   ii. assess ','','','a) i) management                  ii) assessment \nb) i) manage - I can manage it. \nii) assess - A social worker will assess you for the support you need. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45342,576,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n                i. colored                      ii. possible \n                                      OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n               i. colored                      ii. possible ','','','a) i) discolored                   ii) impossible \nb) i) colored - She selected an off-the-shoulder blue colored dress. \nii) possible - It is possible to learn any skill if you spend enough time studying and practicing. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45343,576,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n           i. correct                     ii. complete \n                                   OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n          i. correct                    ii. complete ','','','a) i) incorrect                   ii) incomplete \nb) i) correct - I gave correct answer to my teacher. \nii) complete - It\'s a nice house, a clean house, complete with a front porch and swing. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45344,577,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n              i. interview                   ii. condition \n                                     OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n           i. interview                       ii. condition ','','','a) i) interviewer                       ii) conditioning \nb) i) interview - I got the opportunity to interview a famous cricketer. \nii) condition - The condition of the stray dog was depleting. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45345,577,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n          i. Farsightedness                  ii. unshaven \n                                           OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n          i. Farsightedness                   ii. unshaven ','','','a) i) sight                        ii) shave \nb) i) Farsightedness - He has a good eye sight. \nii) unshaven - His clothing was ripped and his face was unshaven. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45346,578,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n           i. organized                 ii. impressive \n                                 OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n              i. organized                 ii. impressive ','','','a) i) disorganized                     ii) unimpressive \nb) i) organized - She\'s not a very organized person and she always arrives late at meetings. \nii) impressive - The show was not impressive. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45347,578,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n          i. success                ii. meters \n                               OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n          i. success                 ii. meters ','','','a) i) successful                    ii) parameters \nb) i) success - Do not think of today\'s failures, but of the success that may come tomorrow. \nii) meters - The river was several hundred meters away. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45348,579,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n          i. east                    ii. courage \n                            OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n           i. east                      ii. courage ','','','a) i) eastern                 ii) courageous \nb) i) east - They live in Malad east. \n    ii) courage - On one of the occasions he and his company were in danger of perishing from want, and the courage of even he gave way. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45349,579,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n               i. trouble                      ii. haste \n                                       OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n               i. trouble                      ii. haste ','','','a) i) troublesome                    ii) hastily \nb) i) trouble - It is not a trouble at all to have you as a guest. \nii) haste - The messengers made due haste to carry the golden prize to Athens. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45350,580,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n                 i. believe                      ii. clean \n                                           OR\nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n                i. believe                        ii. clean ','','','a) i) believable                  ii) unclean \nb) i) believe - Everyone has to believe there are rules and that they apply to everyone. \nii) clean - We should clean our hands properly. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45351,580,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n                i. present                    ii. nutrition \n                                       OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n               i. present                       ii. nutrition ','','','a) i) represent                     ii) malnutrition \nb) i) present - He was present for the lecture today. \nii) nutrition - Giving the right nutrition to kids is important for healthy growth in children. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45352,581,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n               i. care                     ii. dust \n                                OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n               i. care                     ii. dust ','','','a) i) careful, careless                       ii) dustbin \nb) i) care - Please take care of yourself. \nii) dust - The floor was covered with dust. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45353,581,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n           i. disable/unable                 ii. easily \n                                            OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n          i. disable/unable                  ii. easily ','','','a) i) able                     ii) easy \nb) i) disable/unable - The connection of the network has been disabled. \nii) easily - Our house is at central, so we can easily get to theaters and cinemas. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45354,582,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n                i. successful                 ii. champion \n                                          OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n              i. successful                     ii. champion ','','','a) i) unsuccessful                   ii) championship \nb) i) successful - My parents want me to be successful in everything I take up. \nii) champion - He was Queen Catherine\'s confessor and her only champion and advocate. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45355,582,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n             i. exploit                     ii. ignore \n                                   OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n              i. exploit                    ii. ignore ','','','a) i) exploitation                ii) ignorance \nb) i) exploit - Mobile phones are exploiting the young generation. \nii) ignore - Don\'t ignore the advice of elders. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45356,583,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n            i. complete                  ii. lucky \n                                     OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n            i. complete                    ii. lucky ','','','a) i) completely                       ii) unlucky \nb) i) complete - His medical theories rest upon a complete theory of the universe. \nii) lucky - I was very lucky to learn the new coursebook. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45357,583,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n                 i. disconnect                  ii. invisibility \n                                           OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n                 i. disconnect                    ii. invisibility ','','','a) i) connect                 ii) visible \nb) i) disconnect - For better understanding we must connect to our teachers. \nii) invisibility - Reviewers also mentioned its comfort level and invisibility factor. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45358,584,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n              i. connect                     ii. agree \n                                    OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n               i. connect                         ii. agree ','','','a) i) disconnect                    ii) disagree \nb) i) connect - Please connect the pen drive to PC. \nii) agree - She did not agree with the commander\'s orders to kill anyone who stepped within range. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45359,584,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Add a prefix or suffix to make new words. \n              i. box                   ii. aware \n                                OR \nMake a meaningful sentence using any one of the following words. \n             i. box                      ii. aware ','','','a) i) boxer                   ii) awareness \nb) i) box - The box is full of chocolates. \nii) aware - I have been made acutely aware of that fact since I was a child. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45717,274,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(18x⁴ + 21x³) ÷ 3x² = ?\na) 6x²             b) 7x               c) 6x² + 7x             d) 6x + 7\n','','','c) 6x² + 7x    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45718,274,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(64y² - 4) ÷ (4y – 1)\na) 16y – 4            b) 16y + 4             c) 16y + 1             d) 16y – 1 \n','','','b) 16y + 4   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45719,274,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(5x³ - 17x² + 14x) ÷ (x – 2) = ?\na) 5x² - 17x + 7               b) 5x² + 17x – 7            c) 5x² + 7x             d) 5x² - 7x \n','','','d) 5x² - 7x ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45720,274,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The quotient when (x² - x – 42) ÷ (x + 6) is -----------\na) (x – 7)            b) (x + 7)            c) (x – 8)            d) (x + 8)\n','','','a) (x – 7)  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45721,274,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The remainder when (3x² + 4x + 5) ÷ (x – 2) is -----------\na) 20           b) 5             c) 25                d) 21\n','','','c) 25  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45722,274,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The remainder in (4x² - 13x – 12) ÷ (4x – 1) is ---------\na) 1             b) 0           c) 4                d) -15\n','','',' d) -15','',0,0,0,NULL),(45723,279,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the average of the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5?','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5)/5 = 15/5 = 3\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(45724,279,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the average of the numbers 101, 103, 105, 107, 109?','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (101 + 103 + 105 + 107 + 109)/5 = 525/5 = 105\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(45725,279,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the average of the numbers 110, 114, 118, 122, 126, 130?','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (110 + 114 + 118 + 122 + 126 + 130)/6 = 720/6 = 120\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(45726,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If A, B, C, D are non-zero expressions such that A : B = C : D, the multiplying both sides by B × D, we get the equation ----------.\na) AD = BC             b) AC = BD           c) AB = CD         d) none of these\n','','','a) AD = BC    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45727,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','One number is thrice the other number. Their sum is 24. What is the equation?\na) x + x/3  = 8           b) x + 3x = 24          c) 3x – x = 24        d) 2x+ 6x = 24\n','','','b) x + 3x = 24 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45728,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','I have (5x – 1) coins of 2 rupees each. What is its value in 5 rupee coins?\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mrow>\n    <mtext>a)</mtext>\n    <mo>&nbsp;</mo>\n    <mfrac>\n      <mrow>\n        <mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn>\n      </mrow>\n      <mn>5</mn>\n    </mfrac>\n    <mo>&emsp;</mo>\n    <mtext>b)</mtext>\n    <mo>&nbsp;</mo>\n    <mfrac>\n      <mrow>\n        <mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn>\n      </mrow>\n      <mn>10</mn>\n    </mfrac>\n    <mo>&emsp;</mo>\n    <mtext>c)</mtext>\n    <mo>&nbsp;</mo>\n    <mfrac>\n      <mrow>\n        <mn>5</mn>\n        <mrow>\n          <mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn>\n        </mrow>\n      </mrow>\n      <mn>2</mn>\n    </mfrac>\n    <mo>&emsp;</mo>\n    <mtext>d)</mtext>\n    <mo>&nbsp;</mo>\n    <mfrac>\n      <mrow>\n        <mn>2</mn>\n        <mrow>\n          <mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn>\n        </mrow>\n      </mrow>\n      <mn>5</mn>\n    </mfrac>\n  </mrow>\n</math>\n','','','d) 2(5x – 1)/5','',0,0,0,NULL),(45729,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of a rectangle is twice its breadth. What is the length, if the perimeter is 90 cm?\na) 30 cm           b) 15 cm            c) 45 cm            d) 10 cm\n','','','a) 30 cm  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45730,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','3 is subtracted from twice a number. If the difference is multiplied by 4, we get 52. Form an equation.\na) 4(2x – 3) = 52       b) 4(3x – 2) = 52         c) 4(x – 3) = 52        d) 4(3 – x) = 52\n','','','a) 4(2x – 3) = 52','',0,0,0,NULL),(45731,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find x if x + 5 = 8\na) 2            b) -8             c) 3                d) 1\n','','','c) 3  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45732,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will be the value of a if 2a + 16 = 32\na) -2             b) 8              c) -8                 d) 16\n','','','b) 8   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45733,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>3x</mn>\n    <mn>2</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math> = 5 then x = ?\na) 0            b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>15</mn>\n    <mn>2</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math>             c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>10</mn>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math>        d) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\">\n  <mfrac>\n    <mn>3</mn>\n    <mn>10</mn>\n  </mfrac>\n</math>\n\n','','','c) 10/3      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45734,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the value of x in equation 15x = 60.\na) 2               b) 4                c) 3                d) 1\n','','',' b) 4     ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45735,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the age of father is five times the age of his son. What is the age of son if the father’s current age is 35?\na) 6                b) 8                 c) 7                   d) 10\n','','',' c) 7  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45736,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Two years ago my age was x years then what was my age 5 years ago.\na) x – 2             b) x – 2 + 5           c) x – 5               d) x – 3 \n','','','c) x – 5    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45737,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x is an even number, which is the next odd number.\na) x – 2              b) x + 2             c) x – 1             d) x + 1\n','','','d) x + 1','',0,0,0,NULL),(45738,284,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the difference between two consecutive numbers is 12 and greater number is x then the smaller number.\na) x + 12             b) x – 12             c) 12               d) none of these\n','','',' b) x – 12   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45739,285,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','ΔDEF and ΔLMN are congruent in the correspondence EDF ↔ LMN. Write the pairs of congruent sides and congruent angles in the correspondence.','','','Solution : ','Congruent sides :\nSide ED ≅ side LM\nSide DF ≅ side MN\nSide EF ≅ side LN\nCongruent angles : \n∠EDF ≅ ∠LMN\n∠DFE ≅ ∠MNL\n∠DEF ≅ ∠MLN',0,0,1,NULL),(45740,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the radius of circle is 21 cm then the area of the circle is ----------.\na) 1386 sq. cm           b) 1368 sq. cm            c) 441 sq cm          d) 1441 sq cm\n','','','a) 1386 sq. cm  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45741,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If sides of a triangle are 13 cm, 14 cm, 15 cm find its area.\na) 75 cm²            b) 110 cm²           c) 76 cm²            d) 84 cm²\n','','','d) 84 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(45742,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The lengths of diagonals of a rhombus are 24 cm and 9 cm find its area.\na) 216 cm²           b) 108 cm²           c) 133 cm²           d) 112 cm²\n','','','b) 108 cm²  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45743,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of right angled ΔABC is 6 sq cm, lengths of one perpendicular side is 4 cm. ∴ Length of other perpendicular side is ------------\na) 3 cm              b) 5 cm              c) 4 cm                   d) 2/3 cm\n','','','a) 3 cm  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45744,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Side of parallelogram is in the ratio 2 : 3 area is 150 sq cm. ∴ sides are -------\na) 2 cm, 3 cm           b) 10 cm, 12 cm           c) 10 cm, 15 cm          d) 18 cm, 15 cm\n','','','  c) 10 cm, 15 cm   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45745,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Radius of circle is 1 cm. ∴ Ratio of area to its circumference is ---------\na) 2 : 1             b) 4 : 1             c) 1 : 2                d) 1 : 4\n','','','c) 1 : 2     ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45746,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Area of rhombus is 12 sq cm, length of one diagonal is 6 cm. ∴ length of other diagonal is ------------.\na) 4 cm              b) 12 cm                c) 8 cm                d) 3 cm\n','','','a) 4 cm   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45747,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the area of trapezium whose parallel sides are of length 15 cm and 23 cm and height is 8 cm is ----------\na) 152 cm²            b) 125 cm²            c) 142 cm²            d) 120 cm²\n','','','a) 152 cm²  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45748,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The semi perimeter of triangle whose sides are 17 cm, 25 cm and 26 cm is --------\na) 68 cm            b) 34 cm              c) 136 cm               d) none of these\n','','','c) 136 cm   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45749,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Area of trapezium with height 6 cm is 45 cm²  ∴ Sum of the length of its parallel side is -----------\na) 7 cm             b) 8 cm                c) 75 cm               d) 10 cm\n','','','c) 75 cm    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45750,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Area of a circular ground is 3850 sq cm, then radius is ----------.\na) 70 m               b) 17.5 m             c) 35 m              d) 140 m\n','','',' c) 35 m   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45751,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Length of the diagonals of a rhombus are 11.2 cm and 7.5 cm respectively then area of rhombus is -----------\na) 84 sq cm              b) 42 sq cm               c) 21 sq cm               d) none of these\n','','','b) 42 sq cm       ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45752,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of a circle is numerically equal to its circumference then its diameter is -----------.\na) 2 unit                b) 4 unit                c) 2π unit               d) 4π unit\n','','',' b) 4 unit         ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45753,287,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the wheel of the bus with 90 cm diameter then its circumference is --------\na) 45π cm               b) 90π cm                c) 180π cm               d) 45 cm\n','','',' b) 90π cm    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45754,276,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The stock of grain in a government warehouse lasts 30 days for 4000 people. How many days will it last for 6000 people?','','','Let the stock of grain lasts for x days for 6000 people.\nSince number of people and number of days are inverse proportion, we get\n4000 × 30 = 6000 × x\n∴ x =120000/6000 = 20\nHence, the stock of grain will last in 20 days for 6000 people.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45755,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the radius of circle in which a chord of length 2x is at a distance y from the center \na) x² + y²           b) x² - y²             c) 4x² + y²              d) 4x² - y²\n','','','a) x² + y²  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45756,291,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a circle with center O, seg PQ is a chord of length 7 cm, seg OA ꓕ chord PQ, then l(AP) = -------------.\na) 7 cm                b) 3.5 cm                c) 14 cm               d) 49 cm\n','','','b) 3.5 cm   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45780,305,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the factors of the number 10','','','10 = 1, 2, 5, 10','',0,0,0,NULL),(45781,308,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A sack has capacity to collect the vegetables is 75 kg 250 g, if it contains 15 kg 500 g potatoes, 10 kg 250 g tomatoes and 20 kg 750 g chillis in it. What quantity of other vegetables if has?','','','Total vegetables : 15.50 kg + 10.250 kg + 20.750 kg = 46.500 \n       Total weight of vegetables = 46 kg 500 gm\n      Quantity of other vegetables in the sack = 75.250 – 46.500 = 28.750\n                     = 28 kg 750 gm','',0,0,0,NULL),(45782,308,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The capacity of one petrol tank is 50 lit 250 ml and other tank is 24 lit 750 ml. What is total capacity of the tank?','','','Total Capacity of tank : \n           Lit          ml\n          50           250\n    +    24          750\n    ---------------------------\n         74           1000\ni.e. 75 lit 00 ml          [1000 ml = 1 lit]','',0,0,0,NULL),(45783,434,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">I know a curious little boy\nWho is always asking, \"why?\"\nWhy this, why that, why then, why now,\nWhy not, why by-and-by?\nHe wants to know why wood should swim, \nWhy lead and marbles sink; \nWhy stars should shine and winds should blow,\nAnd why we eat and drink.\nHe wants to know what makes the clouds,\nAnd why they cross the sky; \nWhy sinks the sun behind the hills, \nAnd why the flowers die.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether the following statements are True or False:\n      i. The little boy is curious because he never asks questions\n      ii. The sun sinks behind the hills\n      iii. The flowers always remain alive\n      iv. Lead and marbles sink in water\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following sentences :\n      i. I know that the little boy is really curious because ------------\n      ii. The boy wants to know ----------------------\n      iii. The boy wants to know --------------\n   iv. The boy always asks Why this, --------------------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Complete the following with proper action words from the extract:\n      i. wood                   ii. sun                  iii. marbles \n      iv. flowers               v. stars                vi. winds ','','A1] i) False         ii) True          iii) False            iv) True\nA2] i) he always asked question \"why?\"\n       ii) what makes the clouds and why they cross the sky.\n       iii) why stars shine and winds blow.\n       iv) why that then, why now, why not, why by and by.\nA3] i) swim            ii) sink              iii) sink\n       iv) die              v) shine            vi) blow ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45785,434,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">He wants to know why wind should come \nFrom out the bellows\' nose;\nWhy pop-guns should go pop and why\nThe ocean ebbs and flows.\nHe wants to know why fish have gills, \nAnd why boys cannot fly;\nWhy steam comes from the kettle\'s spout,\nAnd rain falls from the sky. \nHe wants to know why coal should burn, \nAnd not a bit of stone; \nHow seeds get in the apple-core, \nAnd marrow in the bone.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether you Agree or Disagree with the following statements. :\n       i. Wind blows out of bellows \n       ii. Pop guns ebb and flow\n       iii. Steam comes out from the kettle\'s spout \n        iv. A bit of stone never burns \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following with proper action words from the extract:\n      i. ocean -------------\n      ii. rain ----------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        Find out the lines from the poem which show Repetition.','','A1] i) agree                ii) Disagree            iii) Agree            iv) Agree\nA2] i) ebbs and flows             ii) falls\nA3] Why pop-guns should go pop and why the ocean ebbs and flows.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45787,434,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">He wants to know why fish have gills,\nAnd why boys cannot fly;\nWhy steam comes from the kettle\'s spout, \nAnd rain falls from the sky.\nHe wants to know why coal should burn, \nAnd not a bit of stone; \nHow seeds get in the apple-core, \nAnd marrow in the bone.\nHe wants to know why ice should melt; \nWhy spiders eat the flies; \nWhy bees should sting and why the yeast \nShould make the dough to rise.\nSome of his whys are not too hard \nTo answer, if you\'ll try: \nBut other no one ever yet \nHas found the reason why.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n        Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n       i. Some of the boy\'s questions can\'t be answered.\n       ii. The yeast makes the dough to rise.\n       iii. A bit of stone burns.\n       iv. Some questions are not very hard.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Fill in the blanks with appropriate word / phrase from the extract.\n      i. We find seeds in --------------\n      ii. There is --------- in the bone.\n      iii. coal -------- and ice -------------.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write two pairs of rhyming words from the extract.','','A1] i) True       ii) True         iii) False         iv) True\nA2] i) in the apple - core          ii) marrow            iii) burns, melts.\nA3] i) stone – bone             ii) files – rise ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45788,434,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">WHY?\nI know a curious little boy\nWho is always asking, \"why?\"\nWhy this, why that, why then, why now,\nWhy not, why by-and-by?\nHe wants to know why wood should swim, \nWhy lead and marbles sink; \nWhy stars should shine and winds should blow,\nAnd why we eat and drink.\nHe wants to know what makes the clouds,\nAnd why they cross the sky; \nWhy sinks the sun behind the hills, \nAnd why the flowers die.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether the following statements are True or False:\n      i. The little boy is curious because he never asks questions\n      ii. The sun sinks behind the hills\n      iii. The flowers always remain alive\n      iv. Lead and marbles sink in water\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following sentences :\n      i. I know that the little boy is really curious because ------------\n      ii. The boy wants to know ----------------------\n      iii. The boy wants to know --------------\n   iv. The boy always asks Why this, --------------------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Complete the following with proper action words from the extract:\n      i. wood                   ii. sun                  iii. marbles \n      iv. flowers               v. stars                vi. winds \n<b>B] </b>Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) False         ii) True          iii) False            iv) True\nA2] i) he always asked question \"why?\"\n       ii) what makes the clouds and why they cross the sky.\n       iii) why stars shine and winds blow.\n       iv) why that then, why now, why not, why by and by.\nA3] i) swim            ii) sink              iii) sink\n       iv) die              v) shine            vi) blow \nB] i) Title of the poem: Why\nii) Name of the poet: Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme: aaba, cded, faga ………\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) why this, why that, why then, why now Repetition.\nv) Theme / central idea: The poem deals with a curious boy who wants to know the reasons behind some of the phenomena in the nature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45790,434,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">WHY?\nHe wants to know why wind should come \nFrom out the bellows\' nose;\nWhy pop-guns should go pop and why\nThe ocean ebbs and flows.\nHe wants to know why fish have gills, \nAnd why boys cannot fly;\nWhy steam comes from the kettle\'s spout,\nAnd rain falls from the sky. \nHe wants to know why coal should burn, \nAnd not a bit of stone; \nHow seeds get in the apple-core, \nAnd marrow in the bone.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether you Agree or Disagree with the following statements. :\n       i. Wind blows out of bellows \n       ii. Pop guns ebb and flow\n       iii. Steam comes out from the kettle\'s spout \n        iv. A bit of stone never burns \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following with proper action words from the extract:\n      i. ocean -------------\n      ii. rain ----------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device </b>\n        Find out the lines from the poem which show Repetition.\n<b>B] </b>Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) agree                ii) Disagree            iii) Agree            iv) Agree\nA2] i) ebbs and flows             ii) falls\nA3] Why pop-guns should go pop and why the ocean ebbs and flows.\nB] i) Title of the poem: Why\nii) Name of the poet: Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme: aaba, cded, faga ………\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) why this, why that, why then, why now Repetition.\nv) Theme / central idea: The poem deals with a curious boy who wants to know the reasons behind some of the phenomena in the nature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45792,434,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">WHY?\nHe wants to know why fish have gills,\nAnd why boys cannot fly;\nWhy steam comes from the kettle\'s spout, \nAnd rain falls from the sky.\nHe wants to know why coal should burn, \nAnd not a bit of stone; \nHow seeds get in the apple-core, \nAnd marrow in the bone.\nHe wants to know why ice should melt; \nWhy spiders eat the flies; \nWhy bees should sting and why the yeast \nShould make the dough to rise.\nSome of his whys are not too hard \nTo answer, if you\'ll try: \nBut other no one ever yet \nHas found the reason why.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n        Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n       i. Some of the boy\'s questions can\'t be answered.\n       ii. The yeast makes the dough to rise.\n       iii. A bit of stone burns.\n       iv. Some questions are not very hard.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Fill in the blanks with appropriate word / phrase from the extract.\n      i. We find seeds in --------------\n      ii. There is --------- in the bone.\n      iii. coal -------- and ice -------------.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write two pairs of rhyming words from the extract.\n<b>B] </b>Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) True       ii) True         iii) False         iv) True\nA2] i) in the apple - core          ii) marrow            iii) burns, melts.\nA3] i) stone – bone             ii) files – rise \nB] i) Title of the poem: Why\nii) Name of the poet: Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme: aaba, cded, faga ………\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) why this, why that, why then, why now Repetition.\nv) Theme / central idea: The poem deals with a curious boy who wants to know the reasons behind some of the phenomena in the nature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45794,435,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','But Tansen knew how to get round him. He offered to sing before his guru. And he made a deliberate mistake. \"That\'s not the right note, Tansen,\" cried his teacher, amazed. \"What has happened to you?\"\nTansen did not seem to understand his teacher and he made the same mistake again. Exasperated. Sant Haridas took the tanpura from Tansen\'s hands and sang the right note. Then he went on to the next and the next!\nThe music spread across the forest, like the first glimmer of dawn or the fragrance of jasmine. Both Akbar and Tansen listened to him as if they were hypnotised and the emperor realized that Tansen had spoken the truth. He had not known that music could be like this! He had certainly never heard anything like it before.\nAs they walked back, the emperor suddenly broke the silence to ask Tansen. \"Why can\'t you sing like him, Ustadji?\"\nTansen smiled. \"Shahenshah, I sing at your command, the command of the Emperor of Hindustan. But Guruji sings for one who is Master of even kings! GOD! His music springs from the depths of his soul, free and unasked for. How can I expect my music to be so beautiful and divine?\"','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Write T for True, NT for Not True and CNS for Can Not Say :\n       i. In spite of being a great singer, Tansen continued to make deliberate mistakes.\n       ii. Any effort dedicated to God turns to be the best.\n       iii. Sant Haridas sings at the Emperor\'s command.\n       iv. Both Akbar and Tansen listened to him as if they were hypnotized.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b> \n        Why did Tansen make deliberate mistake? \n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n       Frame your own sentences by using words/phrase :\n      i. So beautiful and divine.\n      ii. to get round somebody/something.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       i. Make it affirmative : That is not the right note.\n       ii. Make it Assertive : How beautiful his music was!\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       What will you do if you find a blind person singing songs melodiously for begging?','','A1] i) True          ii) True          iii) Not true          iv) True\nA2] Tansen made deliberate mistake because when they reached him, his guru was not ready to sing. Tansen tried to sing and made mistakes so that they could hear his Guru singing.\nA3] i) The music is so beautiful and divine.\n       ii) The trust is looking for ways to get round someone to solve the funding problem.\nA4] i) That is the wrong note.\n       ii) His music was very beautiful.\nA5] If I find a blind person singing songs melodiously for begging, I would ask him about his educational qualifications in music. If he is well qualified, I will take him to my school. And I will request my school authorities to depute him as a music teacher so that we would enjoy his melodious voice and he would earn his bread and butter instead of begging.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45795,435,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Slowly, gently, the exhausted musician laid his tanpura on the carpet. The exquisite strains of Raag Darbari still vibrated within the hall. Emperor Akbar looked up, his eyes lit with admiration. \"Wonderful!\" he said. \"Superb! I listen to you every day, but I can\'t seem to have enough!\"\nA Tansen bowed in silent gratitude.\n\"I think you have the most wonderful voice in the world!\" said Akbar.\n\"But I don\'t, Shahenshah!\" said Tansen with a smile. \"There is someone who sings far better than I do.\"\n\"Really?\" cried the disbelieving Akbar. \"Then I must have him sing in my court. Can you arrange it?\nTansen shook his head. \"I\'m afraid he will not come. Sire.\"\n\"What! Not even if he hears that the emperor himself has summoned him?\"\n\"No, not even then.\"\nThis reply would have enraged any other emperor.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Name the following.\n      i. The emperor                ii. Musical instrument\n      iii. The musician              iv. Raag   \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Write down the lines from the passage that show that Akbar was pleased with Tansen\'s music.\n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        Write the following words in right column.\n        (Slowly, admiration, listen, he, wonderful)\n        <table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: center;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Noun</th>\n    <th>Pronoun</th>\n    <th>Adjective</th>\n    <th>Adverb</th>\n    <th>Verb</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>------</td>\n    <td>---------</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n    <td>-------</td>\n    <td>--------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       Change into positive and comparative degrees. \n        You have the most wonderful voice in the world. \n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Do you like music? why?','','A1] i) Akbar        ii) Tanpura         iii) Tansen           iv) Darbari\nA2] i) Emperor\'s eyes lit with admiration.       ii) \'Wonderful\' he said.\n       iii) Superb.        iv) I listen to you everyday but I can\'t seem to have enough. \nA3] i) slowly-Adverb      ii) admiration – noun         iii) listen - verb \n       iv) he - pronoun        v) wonderful - adjective. \nA4] Positive - No one in the world has as wonderful voice as you do.\n       Comparative - You have more wonderful voice than any other person in the world has.\nA5] Yes, I like music because music pacifies our un- satisfied minds. It brings relief and contentment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45796,435,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Tansen did not seem to understand his teacher and he made the same mistake again. Exasperated. Sant Haridas took the tanpura from Tansen\'s hands and sang the right note. Then he went on to the next and the next!\nThe music spread across the forest, like the first glimmer of dawn or the fragrance of jasmine. Both Akbar and Tansen listened to him as if they were hypnotised and the emperor realized that Tansen had spoken the truth. He had not known that music could be like this! He had certainly never heard anything like it before.\nAs they walked back, the emperor suddenly broke the silence to ask Tansen, \"Why can\'t you sing like him, Ustadji?\"\nTansen smiled. \"Shahenshah, I sing at your command, the command of the Emperor of Hindustan. But Guruji sings for one who is Master of even kings! GOD! His music springs from the depths of his soul, free and unasked for. How can I expect my music to be so beautiful and divine?\"','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following sentences.\n       i. Sant Haridas was exasperated because ------------\n       ii. Sant Haridas music is compared to ------------\n            a) ---------------                b) ----------------\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Write the differences between Tansen\'s and Haridas\' music.\n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n       From the passage find out the words that mean :-\n        i. order         ii. sweet smell         iii. angry          iv. ray\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. Tansen did not understand his teacher (Make Affirmative)\n        ii. He had never heard anything like it before (Add a question tag)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n        Why, do you think, was Sant Haridas\'s music so beautiful?','','A1] i) Tansen made the same mistake again.\n       ii) a) The first glimmer of dawn.        b) The fragrance of jasmine.\nA2]  Tansen Music                                      Haridas Music\n      i) The emperor was pleased         a) The emperor was hypnotized \n          by the music                                     by the music\n      ii) Tansen sang for Emperor          b) Sant Haridas sang for God.\n           Akbar.\n       iii) Tansen’s music was not free     c) Sant Haridas music was free and \n                                                                          unasked for\nA3] i) command        ii) fragrance        iii) exasperated        iv) glimmer. \nA4] i) Tansen failed to understand his teacher.\n       ii) He had never heard anything like it before, had he?\nA5]  Sant Haridas\' music was spontaneous. He was free to sing whatever and however he likes. And the last but not least, Sant Haridas sang for god and not for any human being.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45797,436,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Sticks and stones may break my bones, \nBut words can also hurt me. \nStones and sticks break only skin. \nWhile words are ghosts that haunt me. \nSlant and curved the fall word-swords \nTo pierce and stick inside me. \nBats and bricks may ache through bones, \nBut words can mortify me.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether you agree or disagree with the following statements : \n     i. Angry and harsh words injure and hurt like swords.\n     ii. The poet speaks about the pain caused by weapons and the hurt caused by unkind languages.\n     iii. The wounds caused to our body never heal fast. \n     iv. The insult caused by words disturbs our mind for a short period of time. \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Why are harsh words called ‘ghosts’?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Find from the poem 2 lines, each that contain the following figures of speech.\n        a) Alliteration                    b) Repetition-','','A1] i) Agree           ii) Agree         iii) Disagree         iv) Disagree\nA2] Harsh words are called \'ghosts\' because they hurt our emotions. And the insult caused by words disturbs our mind for a long time.\nA3] a) i) stick and stones may break my bones.\n            ii) Stones and sticks break only skin.\nb) Sticks and stones may break my bones. Stones and sticks break only skin.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45798,436,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Sticks and stones may break my bones, \nBut words can also hurt me. \nStones and sticks break only skin. \nWhile words are ghosts that haunt me. \nSlant and curved the fall word-swords \nTo pierce and stick inside me. \nBats and bricks may ache through bones, \nBut words can mortify me.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following sentences :\n      i. The objects that can cause injury to our body are ------------\n      ii. ------------- our mind for a long period of time.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        What can heal faster - body or mind and heart (emotions)? Explain your choice.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Find from the extract opposites of the following:\n      i. make         ii. rise               iii. outside.','','A1] i) sticks, stones            ii)  The insult caused by words disturbs\nA2] Body can heal from any scar faster than our mind and heart because when our mind and heart get hurt it disturbs us for a long time. It is quiet difficult to come out of that trauma.\nA3] i) break               ii) fall              iii) inside','',0,0,0,NULL),(45799,436,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Slant and curved the fall word - swords \nTo pierce and stick inside me.\nBats and bricks may ache through bones, \nBut words can mortify me. \nPain from words has left its scar \nOn mind and heart that\'s tender \nCuts and bruises now have healed; \nIt\'s words that I remember.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether the following statements are True or False:\n      i. The poet says that objects like swords injure our mind deeply.\n      ii. The poet says that the damage caused by harsh language is more than the damages caused by physical injury.\n     iii. The scars on the mind and heart do not let us forget the experience.\n     iv. Harsh language and words pacify our heart.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       What does the poet want you to keep in mind, when you speak?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Pick out from the poem the synonyms of :\n        i. injury              ii. delicate            iii. ache             iv. embarrass','','A1] i) False         ii) True            iii) True            iv) False\nA2] The poet says that harsh language hurts our mind and heart which last for a long time. So the poet wants us to keep in mind to use very kind and gentle words when you we speak.\nA3] i) bruise             ii) tender             iii) scar            iv) mortify ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45801,436,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Truth\nSticks and stones may break my bones, \nBut words can also hurt me. \nStones and sticks break only skin. \nWhile words are ghosts that haunt me. \nSlant and curved the fall word-swords \nTo pierce and stick inside me. \nBats and bricks may ache through bones, \nBut words can mortify me. <br>- Barrie Wade<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether you agree or disagree with the following statements : \n     i. Angry and harsh words injure and hurt like swords.\n     ii. The poet speaks about the pain caused by weapons and the hurt caused by unkind languages.\n     iii. The wounds caused to our body never heal fast. \n     iv. The insult caused by words disturbs our mind for a short period of time. \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Why are harsh words called ‘ghosts’?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Find from the poem 2 lines, each that contain the following figures of speech.\n    a) Alliteration                    b) Repetition-\n<b>B]</b> Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) Agree           ii) Agree         iii) Disagree         iv) Disagree\nA2] Harsh words are called \'ghosts\' because they hurt our emotions. And the insult caused by words disturbs our mind for a long time.\nA3] a) i) stick and stones may break my bones.\n            ii) Stones and sticks break only skin.\nb) Sticks and stones may break my bones. Stones and sticks break only skin.\nB] i) Title of the poem : Truth\nii) The poet of the poem: Barrie Wade\niii) Theme of the poem: Stones and sticks hurt us. Simi- unt larly the insulting words also hurt us. But the injuries of stones and sticks can be healed. But the word - injuries and, leave a permanent mark on our minds.\niv) Number of Stanzas : 3\n     Rhyme scheme: abcb, abab, efgf\nv) Figure of speech:\n     a) Alliteration : i) Stones and sticks break only skin.\n           ii) Sticks and stones may break my bones.\n     b) Repetition :  i) Sticks and stones\n                                ii) break my bones / skins\n      c) Metaphor  : Words are ghosts that haunt me. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45802,436,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Truth\nSticks and stones may break my bones, \nBut words can also hurt me. \nStones and sticks break only skin. \nWhile words are ghosts that haunt me. \nSlant and curved the fall word-swords \nTo pierce and stick inside me. \nBats and bricks may ache through bones, \nBut words can mortify me. <br> - Barrie Wade<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following sentences :\n      i. The objects that can cause injury to our body are ------------\n      ii. ------------- our mind for a long period of time.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        What can heal faster - body or mind and heart (emotions)? Explain your choice.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Find from the extract opposites of the following:\n      i. make         ii. rise               iii. outside.\n<b>B] </b>Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) sticks, stones            ii)  The insult caused by words disturbs\nA2] Body can heal from any scar faster than our mind and heart because when our mind and heart get hurt it disturbs us for a long time. It is quiet difficult to come out of that trauma.\nA3] i) break               ii) fall              iii) inside\nB] i) Title of the poem : Truth\nii) The poet of the poem: Barrie Wade\niii) Theme of the poem: Stones and sticks hurt us. Simi- unt larly the insulting words also hurt us. But the injuries of stones and sticks can be healed. But the word - injuries and, leave a permanent mark on our minds.\niv) Number of Stanzas : 3\n     Rhyme scheme: abcb, abab, efgf\nv) Figure of speech:\n     a) Alliteration : i) Stones and sticks break only skin.\n           ii) Sticks and stones may break my bones.\n     b) Repetition :  i) Sticks and stones\n                                ii) break my bones / skins\n      c) Metaphor  : Words are ghosts that haunt me. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45803,436,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Truth\nSlant and curved the fall word - swords \nTo pierce and stick inside me.\nBats and bricks may ache through bones, \nBut words can mortify me. \nPain from words has left its scar \nOn mind and heart that\'s tender \nCuts and bruises now have healed; \nIt\'s words that I remember. <br> - Barrie Wade<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether the following statements are True or False:\n      i. The poet says that objects like swords injure our mind deeply.\n      ii. The poet says that the damage caused by harsh language is more than the damages caused by physical injury.\n     iii. The scars on the mind and heart do not let us forget the experience.\n     iv. Harsh language and words pacify our heart.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       What does the poet want you to keep in mind, when you speak?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Pick out from the poem the synonyms of :\n     i. injury              ii. delicate            iii. ache             iv. embarrass\n<b>B] </b>Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) False         ii) True            iii) True            iv) False\nA2] The poet says that harsh language hurts our mind and heart which last for a long time. So the poet wants us to keep in mind to use very kind and gentle words when you we speak.\nA3] i) bruise             ii) tender             iii) scar            iv) mortify \nB] i) Title of the poem : Truth\nii) The poet of the poem: Barrie Wade\niii) Theme of the poem: Stones and sticks hurt us. Simi- unt larly the insulting words also hurt us. But the injuries of stones and sticks can be healed. But the word - injuries and, leave a permanent mark on our minds.\niv) Number of Stanzas : 3\n     Rhyme scheme: abcb, abab, efgf\nv) Figure of speech:\n     a) Alliteration : i) Stones and sticks break only skin.\n           ii) Sticks and stones may break my bones.\n     b) Repetition :  i) Sticks and stones\n                                ii) break my bones / skins\n      c) Metaphor  : Words are ghosts that haunt me. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45805,437,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','A.J. Cronin has very well carved the idea of selfless help and explained the importance of dedication towards relationships in the story, The two Gentlemen of Verona. The story revolves around the two boys named Nicola and Jacopo who do numerous things to earn money only to pay for their sister\'s treatment who suffers from tuberculosis of the spine.\nThe story begins with the narrator driving down the foothills of the Alps. Here is when he first encounters the two brothers selling wild strawberries. Even after the driver forbids the narrator to buy the wild fruits, he buys the biggest basket and drives towards the town. This scene emphasizes on the first step of growing intimacy between the narrator and the two boys.\nNext day, the boys are found shining shoes in the public square. This amazes the narrator. This is when they reveal the fact that they do numerous kinds of things to earn money. Here, the narrator is shown to develop a soft corner for them in his heart. He finds them to be innocent, serious, pleasant and earnest.\nThe boys are very useful to the narrator. They are very willing to satisfy all that the narrator requires. They are also found selling newspapers, by the narrator, one stormy night. One day, when the narrator asks if he can help them in any, they say they will be very grateful if they get a lift to the nearby village Poleta. Although it doesn\'t come in the narrator\'s way he takes them there. The boys get down in a building and ask the narrator to wait in a nearby cafe, until they return within an hour.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        Name the following :\n      i. Names of the boys around whom the story revolves - \n      ii. Name the city which the boys belong to ------\n      iii. Name of the writer \'The Two Gentlemen of Verona\' - \n      iv. Name of the village nearby \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following :\n      i. Cronin\'s story focuses on ---------------\n      ii. The narrator -------------- to encourage the two brothers.\n      iii. The narrator develops a liking for the two brothers because they are  ------------.\n       iv. The boys wish to visit the village Poeta to --------------\n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Find out similar words for the following:\n        i. disallow                                    ii. make known\n        iii. unexpected meeting            iv. sincere\n<b>A4] Grammar </b>\n        Find the Noun forms of the following words used in the passage.\n        i. important           ii. eager          iii. dedicate         iv. appear\n<b>A5] Personal Response Question</b>\n       What do you think about the two boys?','','A1] i) Nicola and Jacopo            ii) Verona        \n        iii) A. J. Cronin                      iv) Poleta \nA2] i) selfless help and the importance of dedication towards relationship.\n       ii) bought the biggest basket of the wild fruits\n       iii) innocent, serious, pleasant and earnest.\n       iv) sell newspapers.\nA3] i) forbid         ii) reveal         iii) encounter         iv) innocent \nA4] i) importance           ii) eagerness           iii) dedication         iv) appearance \nA5] I think the two boys are simply great. They are caring by nature. So they do numerous jobs for the treatment of their ill sister. They are hard working, sincere and sensible. They are ready to do anything for their sister. They are emotional so they work hard for their beloved sister.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45809,438,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">If the lion\'s caught a cold,\nIf the zebra\'s getting old.\nIf the centipede has trouble with his feet,\nIf the hippo\'s feeling ill,\nIf the bison\'s got a chill,\nIf the Arctic fox is suffering from heat,\nIf some virulent disease\nHas attacked the chimpanzees,\nIf the tortoise hasn\'t stirred for several years,\nIf the bear\'s too full of buns, \nIf the cobra eats her sons, \nIf the panther has a wife who chews his ears;<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        What are the opposite illnesses of the lion and the Arctic fox? \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n      Write the illnesses in the extract, that you find most amusing/laughable :\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write rhyming pairs from the extract.','','A1] The lion has caught the cold and the Arctic fox who lives in cold weather is suffering from heat. These are the opposite illnesses of the lion and the Arctic fox.\nA2] i) The lion has caught a cold.          ii) The Arctic fox is suffering from heat.\n       iii) Some virulent disease has attacked the chimpanzees.\n       iv) The panther has a wife who chews his ears.\nA3] old - old, feet - heat, ill - chill, disease - chimpanzees, years - ears, buns - sons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45810,438,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">If giraffes have had a tiff \nAnd their necks are feeling stiff \nIf hyenas will not laugh at keepers\' jokes.\nIf the elephant eats paper-bags and chokes,\nAnd expect him to put everything to rights.\nIf the monkey\'s pinched his tail, \nIf the rhino\'s looking pale, \nIf the camel-hurts his hump, \nIf the kangaroo won\'t jump, \nIf the crocodile turns cannibal and bites, \nThey run away and get \nThat omniscient, the vet\nAnd expect him to put everything to rights.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following information from the extract :\n      i. Necks of the giraffes are feeling stiff because --------------\n      ii. The elephant has choked because it has eaten ---------------\n      iii. The crocodile bites because it has turned ------------------\n      iv. The kangaroo\'s illness is that it ---------------\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Why does the poet call the vet \'Omniscient\'? \n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write the rhyme scheme of these two stanzas.','','A1] i) they had a tiff                ii) paper-bags\n       iii) a cannibal                     iv) won\'t jump\nA2] he poet calls the vet \'omniscient\' because he always has to deal with every kind of disease of the animals and everything about the animals including their inside and their behavioral pattern and their suitable treatments.\nA3] i) aabccb           ii) aabccb','',0,0,0,NULL),(45811,438,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Profoundly I pity the vet, who must learn \nSuch a very great deal for his pay;\nMy son, I advise you most strongly to earn \nYour living an easier way.\nDon\'t attempt to attend the zoological crowd;\na far more advisable plan\nIs to call yourself \'Doctor\', and so be allowed\nTo treat the illnesses of man.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following sentences :\n       i. The poet deeply ----------- the vet.\n       ii. He advises you to earn your living in an ---------------.\n       ii. The vet has to learn ---------------.\n       iv. The vet does not get his --------- if compared to his hard work.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        What is the poet\'s advice to you? Do you think he means it?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write the rhyming pairs from the extract.','','A1] i) feels pity for            ii) easier way\n       iii) a great deal           iv) pay\nA2] The poet\'s advice to you that you should not become a vet and don\'t try to treat the wild, dangerous animals. Find some easy way for living and that can be you should be allowed to treat the illnesses of man. The poet does not really mean to say so, but wants to point out the importance of the vet\'s work.\nA3] learn earn, pay - way, crowd allowed, plan – man.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45813,438,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">“The Vet”\nIf the lion\'s caught a cold,\nIf the zebra\'s getting old.\nIf the centipede has trouble with his feet,\nIf the hippo\'s feeling ill,\nIf the bison\'s got a chill,\nIf the Arctic fox is suffering from heat,\nIf some virulent disease\nHas attacked the chimpanzees,\nIf the tortoise hasn\'t stirred for several years,\nIf the bear\'s too full of buns, \nIf the cobra eats her sons, \nIf the panther has a wife who chews his ears;<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        What are the opposite illnesses of the lion and the Arctic fox? \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n      Write the illnesses in the extract, that you find most amusing/laughable :\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write rhyming pairs from the extract.\n<b>B] </b>Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] The lion has caught the cold and the Arctic fox who lives in cold weather is suffering from heat. These are the opposite illnesses of the lion and the Arctic fox.\nA2] i) The lion has caught a cold.          ii) The Arctic fox is suffering from heat.\n       iii) Some virulent disease has attacked the chimpanzees.\n       iv) The panther has a wife who chews his ears.\nA3] old - old, feet - heat, ill - chill, disease - chimpanzees, years - ears, buns - sons.\nB] i) Title of the poem - The vet \nii) Name of the poet Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme abab, cdcd, efef\niv) Figure of speech : If the lion\'s caught a cold Hyperbole.\nv) Theme / central Idea :- This is a humourous poem. Here the poet says that a veterinarian needs exceedingly wide knowledge as his patients possess a different inside.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45814,438,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">“The Vet”\nIf giraffes have had a tiff \nAnd their necks are feeling stiff \nIf hyenas will not laugh at keepers\' jokes.\nIf the elephant eats paper-bags and chokes,\nAnd expect him to put everything to rights.\nIf the monkey\'s pinched his tail, \nIf the rhino\'s looking pale, \nIf the camel-hurts his hump, \nIf the kangaroo won\'t jump, \nIf the crocodile turns cannibal and bites, \nThey run away and get \nThat omniscient, the vet\nAnd expect him to put everything to rights.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following information from the extract :\n      i. Necks of the giraffes are feeling stiff because --------------\n      ii. The elephant has choked because it has eaten ---------------\n      iii. The crocodile bites because it has turned ------------------\n      iv. The kangaroo\'s illness is that it ---------------\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Why does the poet call the vet \'Omniscient\'? \n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write the rhyme scheme of these two stanzas.\n<b>B] </b>Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) they had a tiff                ii) paper-bags\n       iii) a cannibal                     iv) won\'t jump\nA2] he poet calls the vet \'omniscient\' because he always has to deal with every kind of disease of the animals and everything about the animals including their inside and their behavioral pattern and their suitable treatments.\nA3] i) aabccb           ii) aabccb\nB] i) Title of the poem - The vet \nii) Name of the poet Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme abab, cdcd, efef\niv) Figure of speech : If the lion\'s caught a cold Hyperbole.\nv) Theme / central Idea :- This is a humourous poem. Here the poet says that a veterinarian needs exceedingly wide knowledge as his patients possess a different inside.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45815,438,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">“The Vet”\nProfoundly I pity the vet, who must learn \nSuch a very great deal for his pay;\nMy son, I advise you most strongly to earn \nYour living an easier way.\nDon\'t attempt to attend the zoological crowd;\na far more advisable plan\nIs to call yourself \'Doctor\', and so be allowed\nTo treat the illnesses of man.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following sentences :\n       i. The poet deeply ----------- the vet.\n       ii. He advises you to earn your living in an ---------------.\n       ii. The vet has to learn ---------------.\n       iv. The vet does not get his --------- if compared to his hard work.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        What is the poet\'s advice to you? Do you think he means it?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write the rhyming pairs from the extract.\n<b>B]</b> Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) feels pity for            ii) easier way\n       iii) a great deal           iv) pay\nA2] The poet\'s advice to you that you should not become a vet and don\'t try to treat the wild, dangerous animals. Find some easy way for living and that can be you should be allowed to treat the illnesses of man. The poet does not really mean to say so, but wants to point out the importance of the vet\'s work.\nA3] learn earn, pay - way, crowd allowed, plan – man.\n B] i) Title of the poem - The vet \nii) Name of the poet Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme abab, cdcd, efef\niv) Figure of speech : If the lion\'s caught a cold Hyperbole.\nv) Theme / central Idea :- This is a humourous poem. Here the poet says that a veterinarian needs exceedingly wide knowledge as his patients possess a different inside.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45816,439,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','P. V. Sindhu or Pusarla Venkata Sindhu is an ace shuttler and silver medalist at 2016 Rio Summer Olympics. This young badminton player from Andhra Pradesh was born to volleyball players P. V. Ramana and P. Vijaya in the year 1995. Her father was honoured with Arjuna Award for having displayed excellence in his sport. One of the youngest and talented badminton players of the contemporary times, Sindhu has featured in top 10 in the world badminton rankings for the most part of 2014.\nP. V. Sindhu started playing badminton from the age of eight, after she drew inspiration from Pullela Gopichand\'s victory in All England Open Badminton Championship in 2001. That\'s the reason why she chose badminton over volleyball although her parents were professional volleyball players. Her initial apprenticeship started with Mehboob Ali at the badminton courts of Indian Railway Institute of Signal Engineering and Telecommunications in Secunderabad. Later, she joined Pullela Gopichand\'s badminton academy to further enhance her sporting skills.\nEarly Success\nSindhu\'s first major recognition came in the form of the 5th Servo All India ranking championship for under-10 category. She also won the singles title at the Ambuja Cement All India ranking.\nIn the under-13 category, Sindhu won doubles titles at the IOC All India Ranking, Krishna Khaitan All India Tournament, the Sub-Junior Nationals and the All India Ranking in Pune.\nAt the 51st National School Games in India, Sindhu won the gold medal in under-14 category.\nSindhu\'s Debut and Performance at International level\nP. V. Sindhu didn\'t take much time to launch and establish herself as a promising player in the global arena. She won a bronze medal at the 2009 Sub-Junior Asian Badminton Championships held in Colombo.\nThe star player bagged silver in the women\'s singles at Iran Fair International Badminton Challenge in 2010 and found a place in the national team at the 2010 Uber Cup.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the sentences using the information from the passage:\n       i. ----------  was Sindhu\'s first win at international level.\n       ii. Sindhu started her achievements in badminton around to age of ------ .\n       iii. Sindhu won her first International medal in the year ----------.\n       iv. P. V. Sindhu won -------- at 2016 Summer Olympics.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        What made Sindhu take up badminton at the age of eight? \n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Complete the words by filling in the missing letters:\n       i. e_hance          ii. fe_tures          iii. l_unch         iv. p_ayer.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       i. Sindhu won doubles titles.  (Begin with \'Double titles-and change the voice.)\n      ii. She won a bronze medal.  (Add a Question Tag.)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Who is your favourite player? Why do you like him/her?','','A1] i) Bronze medal             ii) 10       iii) 2009          iv) Silver medal\nA2] Pullela Gopichand won the All-England Open Badminton Championship in 2001. Sindhu was just eight years old then. She used to play volleyball. Gopichand\'s victory in England inspired her to play badminton instead of volleyball. So at the age of eight she started playing badminton.\nA3] i) enhance          ii) features          iii) launch         iv) player.\nA4] i) Doubles titles were won by Sindhu.\n      ii) She won a bronze medal, didn\'t she?\nA5] My favourite player is Mahendra Singh Dhoni. I like him as a powerful batsman, very smart, dynamic and quick wicketkeeper and cool captain. His fitness as a cricketer at this age is commendable.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45817,439,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','P. V. Sindhu or Pusarla Venkata Sindhu is an ace shuttler and silver medalist at 2016 Rio Summer Olympics. This young badminton player from Andhra Pradesh was born to volleyball players P. V. Ramana and P. Vijaya in the year 1995. Her father was honoured with Arjuna Award for having displayed excellence in his sport. One of the youngest and talented badminton players of the contemporary times, Sindhu has featured in top 10 in the world badminton rankings for the most part of 2014.\nP. V. Sindhu started playing badminton from the age of eight, after she drew inspiration from Pullela Gopichand\'s victory in All England Open Badminton Championship in 2001. That\'s the reason why she chose badminton over volleyball although her parents were professional volleyball players. Her initial apprenticeship started with Mehboob Ali at the badminton courts of Indian Railway Institute of Signal Engineering and Telecommunications in Secunderabad. Later, she joined Pullela Gopichand\'s badminton academy to further enhance her sporting skills.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b> \n       Complete the following chart.\n        P. V. Sindhu\n       i. Full Name : ------------------\n       ii. Father\'s Name : -----------------------\n       iii. Mother\'s Name : -------------------\n       iv. Birth-Date : ---------------------\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following table.\n       P.V. Sindhu - Sports Career\n       i. Initial apprenticeship\n          a) Trainer : ------------------         b) Institute : ---------------\n      ii. Later apprenticeship\n          a) Trainer : ---------------            b) Institute : ---------------\n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Write down four words related to sports.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       i. Her father was honoured with Arjuna Award. (Change the voice)\n       ii. She chose badminton over volleyball although her par- ents were professional volleyball players. (use \'but)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Why are sports necessary to us?','','A1] i) Pusarla Venkata Sindhu           ii) P. V. Ramana\n       iii) P. Vijaya                                    iv) 1995 \nA2] i) a) Mehboob Ali                      b) Indian Railway Institute.\n       ii) a) Pullela Gopichand            b) Badminton Academy. \nA3] Olympic, badminton, player, volleyball.\nA4] i) i) Government honoured her father with Arjuna Award.\n       ii) Her parents were professional volleyball players but she chose badminton over volleyball.\nA5] Sports are very necessary to us because they make us strong and cheerful. It is a good exercise. The qualities like sportsmanship, leadership, co-operation, team- work are also inculcated through sports.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45818,439,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','P. V. Sindhu\'s Recent Recognitions\nOn 7 July 2012, Sindhu won Asia Youth Under-19 Championship. Sindhu\'s performance at Malaysian open 2013 made her win the maiden Grand Prix Gold title.\nThe best moment in her career perhaps came in the same year when she became India\'s first medalist in women\'s singles at the Badminton World Championships.\nP. V. Sindhu ended 2013 on a happy note by winning Macau Open Grand Prix Gold title, and also won the Arjun Award, one of the highest honours for a sportsperson in the country. In 2014, she won back-to-back medals in the World Badminton Championship and became the first Indian to do that. In November, 2015, she bagged successive women\'s singles title at the Macau Open Grand Prix Gold.\nThe year 2016 brought her another achievement of winning the Malaysia Masters Grand Prix Gold women\'s singles title, where she defeated Scotland\'s Kirsty Gilmour in the final. In August, she reached the semi-final of the women\'s singles event at the 2016 Summer Olympics, after defeating World No.2 Wang Yihan.\nSindhu created history by reaching the finals after beating Japan\'s Nozomi Okuhara in the women\'s singles semi-final in the Rio Olympics. She won a silver medal in the finals, while the gold was taken by Spain\'s Carolina Marin.\nLater, she defeated Carolina Marin and won 2017 India Open Superseries title.\nNoteworthy Honours\nAmong the other noteworthy honours that Sindhu has received are the Padma Shri in 2015, which is the fourth highest civilian award of India. In 2016, she received the Rajiv Gandhi Khel Ratna Award.\nThe best endorsement of Sindhu has been Gopichand\'s words about her dedication and commitment to the sport: \"The most striking feature in Sindhu\'s game is her attitude and the never-say- die spirit.\"','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether following statements are true or false :\n      i. Sindhu won Arjuna Award in 2013\n      ii. Sindhu defeated Spain\'s Carolina Marin in the Rio Olympics, 2016.\n      iii. Sindhu created history by reaching the finals in women\'s singles in the Rio Olympics, 2016.\n      iv. Carolina was defeated by Sindhu in the year 2017.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        What history was created by P.V. Sindhu in 2016? What was her victory in the following year?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        Make sentences of your own using the following phrases/idioms :\n       i. to create history          ii. never-say-die spirit.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. Arjuna Award is one of the highest honours for a sportsperson in the country.\n           (Change into comparative degree.)\n       ii. She won a silver medal in the finals of Rio Olympics. \n        (Rewrite beginning with \'A silver medal...)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       What is your favourite game? Why do you like it?','','A1] i) True           ii) False            iii) True               iv) True\nA2] In 2016, P.V. Sindhu created history by reaching the finals after beating Japan\'s Nozomi Okuhara in the women\'s singles semi-final in the Rio Olympics. Later in 2017, she defeated Carolina Marin and won India Open Superseries title.\nA3] i) Sachin Tendulkar has created history in the world of cricket by scoring more than 15,000 runs in his career.\n      ii) His never-say-die spirit has made him reach the highest position in the organization. \nA4] i) Arjuna Award is higher than most other honours for a sportsperson in the country.\n       ii) A silver medal was won by her in the finals of Rio Olympics.\nA5] My favourite game is cricket. I like it because almost all my friends play it. It is a world famous game. The game gives us a lot of exercise, teaches us discipline, team spirit and keeps us fit. I like its different formats-Test match, One Day match and T-20 match.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45819,439,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','P. V. Sindhu ended 2013 on a happy note by winning Macau Open Grand Prix Gold title, and also won the Arjun Award, one of the highest honours for a sportsperson in the country. In 2014, she won back-to-back medals in the World Badminton Championship and became the first Indian to do that. In November, 2015, she bagged successive women\'s singles title at the Macau Open Grand Prix Gold.\nThe year 2016 brought her another achievement of winning the Malaysia Masters Grand Prix Gold women\'s singles title, where she defeated Scotland\'s Kirsty Gilmour in the final. In August, she reached the semi-final of the women\'s singles event at the 2016 Summer Olympics, after defeating World No.2 Wang Yihan.\nSindhu created history by reaching the finals after beating Japan\'s Nozomi Okuhara in the women\'s singles semi-final in the Rio Olympics. She won a silver medal in the finals, while the gold was taken by Spain\'s Carolina Marin.\nLater, she defeated Carolina Marin and won 2017 India Open Super series title.\nNoteworthy Honours\nAmong the other noteworthy honours that Sindhu has received are the Padma Shri in 2015, which is the fourth highest civilian award of India. In 2016, she received the Rajiv Gandhi Khel Ratna Award.\nThe best endorsement of Sindhu has been Gopichand\'s words about her dedication and commitment to the sport: \"The most striking feature in Sindhu\'s game is her attitude and the never-say- die spirit.\"','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Write remarkable events in Sindhu\'s career against the years given below.\n       i. June 2016 :-            ii. August 2016 :-            iii. 2017 :- \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following.\n      i. Noteworthy honours that Sindhu received :-\n          a) 2015 : ---------------        b) 2016 :-----------------------\n     ii. The most striking features in Sindhu\'s game.\n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        i. Write the names of two metals.\n        ii. Give the opposites of :- a) lost             b) doubles.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        Change into positive and comparative degrees.\n       The Padma Shri is one of the highest civilian awards of India.\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Which game do you like to play? why?','','A1] i) winning the Malaysia M.G. women\'s single title.\n       ii) semi-final in Olympics.\n      iii) won 2017 India open super series title.\nA2] i) a) 2015:- Padm shri         b) 2016:- Rajiv Gandhi khel Ratna Award.\n       ii) a) her attitude                 b) the never-say- die-spirit.\nA3] i) Silver, gold\n          ii) a) won                b) singles\nA4] Comparative - The Padma Shri is higher than most other civilian awards of India. \n      Positive -Very few awards of India are as high as the Padma Shri. \nA5] I am fond of cricket. I have been playing cricket from my childhood. It is popular all over the world. We can see cricket matches on the T.V. So I developed liking for Cricket.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45820,439,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','P.V. Sindhu has been pushing the envelope like no young badminton player had done before in the history of women\'s badminton, in India.\nAt an interview, Sindhu was asked how much mentoring/guidance her father and mother had been able to give her and how involved they were in her sports career. In her own words,\n\"Guidance wise, every time I go for national level tournaments, they accompany me there and when I practise and play, they tell me what I\'m doing. My dad gives me a summary of how I play and it is very helpful, because as outsiders those who look at it, know more about the game. As badminton players, we obviously, know a few things, but someone from the outside can pinpoint stuff, that we may not have noticed easily. So, both as counsellors and parents, it is really helpful.\"\nIn the same interview, a practical question was asked it\'s not possible for a badminton player to win week in and week out. As a top player, how did she manage to just go to a tournament and psychologically deal with an early loss? Did it hurt when she didn\'t win?\nTo this she replied, \"If you don\'t play well and lose in the first round, second round, you feel bad, especially if you\'re one of the top players. But you need to come out of it and practice harder and identify and learn from your mistakes. I think, to come out of it, it takes time. But ups and downs are always there, and\nyou need to cope with it.\" Hats off, to you, Sindhu, and wish you even greater success!','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Write whether you Agree or Disagree with the following statements : Ans.\n      i. Mentoring/guidance is always helpful to badminton players.\n      ii. If a player loses an important match he/she loses all hopes.\n      iii. It\'s impossible for a top badminton player to win every match.\n       iv. Outsiders cannot pinpoint the faults in the game of badminton players. D\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        What role do Sindhu\'s parents play during her important matches?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        Make sentences of your own using the following phrases/idioms.\n        i. push the envelope                 ii. week in and week out\n        iii. to come out of                     iv. ups and downs.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. My dad gives me a summary.\n            (Rewrite the sentence using Present Perfect Tense.)\n        ii. It is really helpful. \n            (Rewrite the sentence as an exclamatory sentence.)\n<b>A5] Personal Response </b>\n       Why, do you think, does every team of players need a coach/trainer?','','A1] i) Agree          ii) Disagree              iii) Agree            iv) Disagree\nA2] When Sindhu\'s parents attend her tournaments they tell her what she is doing. Her father gives her a summary of how she plays and that is very helpful to her, because as outsiders who look at her game, know more about her errors or faults. Her parents can show or describe exactly where she goes wrong in the game; which she may not notice easily.\nA3] i) Virat Kohli has been pushing the envelope like no batsman had done before in the history of Indian cricket.\n      ii) It\'s impossible for any man to be successful week in and week out.\n      iii) It\'s not easy to come out of the natural calamity like earthquake.\n      iv) All men have ups and downs in their life. \nA4] i) i) My dad has given me a summary.\n        ii) How helpful it is!\nA5] I think, every team of players need a coach/trainer because coach/trainer only can guide them properly when they go wrong with their game. The coach helps them to maintain discipline and gives them practical training of the game on the ground, under his, supervision. He tells their mistakes, flaws and corrects them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45821,440,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Friendship is a Golden chain, \nThe links our friends so dear, \nAnd like a rare and precious jewel \nIt\'s treasured more each year...\nIt\'s clasped together firmly \nWith a love that\'s deep and true, \nAnd it\'s rich with happy memories \nAnd fond recollections, too... \nTime can\'t destroy its beauty \nFor as long as memory lives, \nYears can\'t erase the pleasure \nThat the joy of friendship gives...<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the sentences using the information in the extract:\n      i. The two things that are compared to friendship are --------------\n     ii. ------------ holds friends closely together.\n     iii. The true friends are last a long as -------------.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Why friendship is compared to :\n         a) a golden chain                    b) a precious jewel?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        Write the words that go with the following: (Answers are given directly.)\n       i. -------- jewel                      ii. ---------- memories     \n       iii. ---------- chain                  iv. ------------------ love.','','A1] i) golden chain and precious jewel            ii) Deep and true love\n       iii) memory lives\nA2] Friendship is compared to \'golden chain\' because it links our dear friends together. It is compared to \'precious jewel\', because it grows more precious as each year passes by.\nA3] i) precious jewel              ii) happy memories\n       iii) golden chain               iv) deep and true love. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45823,440,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">For friendship is a priceless gift \nThat can\'t be bought or sold, \nBut to have an understanding friend \nIs worth far more than gold...\nAnd the golden chain of Friendship \nIs a strong and blessed tie \nBinding kindred hearts together \nAs the years go passing by.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false :\n         i. Friendship is precious (has more value) than gold.\n         ii. Friendship can be bought or sold.\n         iii. Friendship is a strong, blessed chain.\n         iv. As the years pass the similar hearts are separated.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Say why friendship is compared to-\n        1) a priceless gift                  2) a golden chain.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        Identify the figures of speech :\n       i. That can\'t be bought or sold.\n       ii. For friendship is a priceless gift.','','A1] i) True           ii) False          iii) True             iv) False\nA2] 1) because it cannot be bought or sold.\n        2) because it is a strong, blessed tie that binds similar hearts together. \nA3] i) Antithesis                        ii) Metaphor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45824,440,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">“Golden Chain”\nFriendship is a Golden chain, \nThe links our friends so dear, \nAnd like a rare and precious jewel \nIt\'s treasured more each year...\nIt\'s clasped together firmly \nWith a love that\'s deep and true, \nAnd it\'s rich with happy memories \nAnd fond recollections, too... \nTime can\'t destroy its beauty \nFor as long as memory lives, \nYears can\'t erase the pleasure \nThat the joy of friendship gives... <br>- Helen Steiner Rice<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the sentences using the information in the extract:\n      i. The two things that are compared to friendship are --------------\n     ii. ------------ holds friends closely together.\n     iii. The true friends are last a long as -------------.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Why friendship is compared to :\n         a) a golden chain                    b) a precious jewel?\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        Write the words that go with the following: (Answers are given directly.)\n       i. -------- jewel                      ii. ---------- memories     \n      iii. ---------- chain                  iv. ------------------ love.\n<b>B] </b>Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) golden chain and precious jewel            ii) Deep and true love\n       iii) memory lives\nA2] Friendship is compared to \'golden chain\' because it links our dear friends together. It is compared to \'precious jewel\', because it grows more precious as each year passes by.\nA3] i) precious jewel              ii) happy memories\n       iii) golden chain               iv) deep and true love. \nB] i) Title of the poem :- Golden chain\nii) Name of the poetess :- Helen Steiner Rice\niii) Rhyme scheme: abcb, defe, …….\niv) Figure of speech :- Friendship is a golden chain - Metaphor.\nv) Theme / central idea: Here, in this poem, the poet tells us the very distinctive features of Friendship. It binds two kindred hearts together for years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45826,440,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">“Golden Chain”\nFor friendship is a priceless gift \nThat can\'t be bought or sold, \nBut to have an understanding friend \nIs worth far more than gold...\nAnd the golden chain of Friendship \nIs a strong and blessed tie \nBinding kindred hearts together \nAs the years go passing by <br> - Helen Steiner Rice<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false :\n         i. Friendship is precious (has more value) than gold.\n         ii. Friendship can be bought or sold.\n         iii. Friendship is a strong, blessed chain.\n         iv. As the years pass the similar hearts are separated.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Say why friendship is compared to-\n        1) a priceless gift                  2) a golden chain.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n        Identify the figures of speech :\n       i. That can\'t be bought or sold.\n       ii. For friendship is a priceless gift.\n<b>B]</b> Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) True           ii) False          iii) True             iv) False\nA2] 1) because it cannot be bought or sold.\n        2) because it is a strong, blessed tie that binds similar hearts together. \nA3] i) Antithesis                        ii) Metaphor.\nB] i) Title of the poem :- Golden chain\nii) Name of the poetess :- Helen Steiner Rice\niii) Rhyme scheme: abcb, defe, …….\niv) Figure of speech :- Friendship is a golden chain - Metaphor.\nv) Theme / central idea: Here, in this poem, the poet tells us the very distinctive features of Friendship. It binds two kindred hearts together for years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45827,441,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','On 10 April 1912, the largest luxury ship in the world set sail from Southampton in England to New York. She was called the Titanic. The word \'titanic\' means \'huge\'. This was her maiden voyage, that is her first voyage. She set out to cross the Atlantic Ocean and she carried more than two thousand passengers. The Titanic was like a floating town.\nEveryone called the Titanic the finest ship in the world. Everyone was proud of her. She was as tall as a building with fifteen storeys. Inside, she was like a large hotel with grand staircases, dining-rooms, shops and swimming pools.\nEveryone thought that the Titanic could never sink. They called her the unsinkable ship. The ship\'s builders were so sure of this, that they put only a few life-boats on the ship.\nThe journey was very comfortable. The passengers were proud because they were travelling in the best ship in the world. They felt safe.\nThen came the night of 14 April. The sky was clear and the sea was calm. Many of the passengers were asleep. Captain Smith knew that there were icebergs nearby, so he told a man to keep a watch from the top of the ship. Suddenly at 11.40 p.m. the man saw something. He grew cold with fear. A huge iceberg stood right in the path of the Titanic. ICEBERGE! He yelled.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Answer the following questions in short :\n      i. What does the word \'Titanic\' mean?\n      ii. How many passengers did Titanic carry? \n      iii. Why were passengers on Titanic, proud?\n      iv. Why did people call \'Titanic\' the unsinkable ship?  \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Describe Titanic in short.\n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        Write the compound words from the passage.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       State whether the following are Simple, Complex or Compound sentences :\n       i. They felt safe.\n       ii. Captain Smith knew that there were icebergs nearby. \n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       What is the safest transportation system road, sea or air?','','A1] i) Titanic means \'huge\'.\n       ii) More than two thousand passengers.\n       iii) because they were travelling in the best ship in the world\n       iv) because they thought that it could never sink\nA2] Titanic was the largest ship in the world. It was going on its first voyage. It was as tall as a fifteen-storeyed building. It was like a large hotel with grand staircases, dining rooms, shops and swimming pools. There were more than two thousand passengers on it. It was like a floating town.\nA3] i) dining-room : (dining + room)        ii) life-boat : (life + boat)\n       iii) iceberg : (ice + berg)                      iv) something : (some + thing)\n        v) everyone : (every + one). \nA4] i) Simple sentence.                  ii) Complex sentence.\nA5] I think that air is the safest transportation system. It is also the fastest way to travel from one place to another. Most accidents occur on road. Air has least accidents. There is no tension of heavy traffic. Journey by air is comfortable. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45828,441,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','On 10 April 1912, the largest luxury ship in the world set sail from Southampton in England to New York. She was called the Titanic. The word \'titanic\' means \'huge\'. This was her maiden voyage, that is her first voyage. She set out to cross the Atlantic Ocean and she carried more than two thousand passengers. The Titanic was like a floating town.\nEveryone called the Titanic the finest ship in the world. Everyone was proud of her. She was as tall as a building with fifteen storeys. Inside, she was like a large hotel with grand staircases, dining-rooms, shops and swimming pools.\nEveryone thought that the Titanic could never sink. They called her the unsinkable ship. The ship\'s builders were so sure of this, that they put only a few life-boats on the ship.\nThe journey was very comfortable. The passengers were proud because they were travelling in the best ship in the world. They felt safe.\nThen came the night of 14 April. The sky was clear and the sea was calm. Many of the passengers were asleep. Captain Smith knew that there were icebergs nearby, so he told a man to keep a watch from the top of the ship. Suddenly at 11.40 p.m. the man saw something. He grew cold with fear. A huge iceberg stood right in the path of the Titanic. ICEBERGE! He yelled.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following sentences with the help of the passage:\n     i. Titanic began its journey on -------------.\n     ii. The ship\'s builders were confident that it would ------------ .\n     iii. Facilities of Titanic were ---------------------.\n     iv. The captain of the Titanic was --------.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       What made the man turn cold with fright? \n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Write the words from the passage that have suffixes. Underline the suffix in each.\n      i.  unsinkable          ii. comfortable       iii. suddenly            iv. Titanic.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       State whether the following are Simple, Complex or Compound sentences :\n       i. He fought hard.\n       ii. The sky was clear and the sea was calm.\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n        What kind of journey do you prefer bus, train or ship? Why?','','A1] i) 10 April 1912                ii) never sink\n       iii) grand staircases, dining-rooms, shops and swimming pools\n       iv) Smith\nA2] The man was asked to keep watch on icebergs from the top of the ship. Suddenly at 11.40 pm he saw something. He grew cold with fear because he had seen a huge iceberg stood right in the path of the Titanic.\nA3] i) able             ii) able              iii) ly               iv) ic\nA4] i) Simple sentence.          ii) Compound sentence.\nA5] I prefer a journey by train instead of bus or ship. It is a safer journey and more comfortable, too. We can stand, move/walk to any place we want in the train. We can reach to our destination fast and earlier than any other vehicles running on the road.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45829,441,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Captain Smith fought hard to steer the Titanic away from the iceberg, but the ship changed direction slowly and her side scraped the mountain of ice. A shower of ice fell on to the decks and the iceberg cut holes along the ship\'s side. Water began to pour into the lower decks. Most passengers heard nothing and slept on.\nCaptain Smith and his crew could not stop the water flooding through the ship. They knew the Titanic was in great danger. The unsinkable ship was sinking. At 12.15 a.m., Captain Smith sent urgent messages on the ship\'s radio asking for help. \'HELP!\' TITANIC SINKING! COME QUICKLY! Then he ordered the crew to wake all the passengers up.\nA ship called \'The California\' was only twenty miles away, but she did not receive Titanic\'s messages. Her radio was turned off and the radio operator was asleep.\nAnother ship called the \'Carpathia\' heard the Titanic\'s calls and sailed at full speed towards her. Unfortunately, the Carpathia was far away and she took over three hours to reach the Titanic.\nMeanwhile, the Titanic was sinking rapidly. The crew lowered the life-boats into the sea, but there was only enough space in the boat for a few hundred passengers. The Titanic had more than two thousand people on board. The ship began to tilt into the sea. People began to jump from the decks into the ice-cold water.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether the statements are True or False :\n      i. Captain Smith steered the Titanic away from the iceberg.\n      ii. The ship \'Carpathia\' reached Titanic too late to save the people on Titanic.\n     iii. The ship \'California\' reached Titanic a little late.\n        iv. The space in the life-boats was not enough for the two thousand people boarded on Titanic.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Find the reasons for sinking of the Titanic. \n                  OR\n       What was the damage caused to the ship? \n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n       Find the words from the passage that have suffixes. Underline the suffix in each of them.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        Frame two sentences of your own using the construction as ... as\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       What will you do while facing a grave danger?','','A1] i) False           ii) True         iii) False          iv) True\nA2] Captain Smith tried hard to steer the Titanic away from the iceberg but the side of the ship scratched deep against the mountain of ice. A shower of ice fell on the decks and the iceberg cut holes along the ship\'s side. Water began to pour into the lower decks of the ship. So the Titanic began to sink.\nA3] 1) direction          2) slowly           3) unsinkable             4) quickly\n        5) operator          6) unfortunately         7) rapidly.   \nA4] i) The policeman was as watchful as a hawk.\n       ii) The hero\'s gait was as graceful as a swan. \nA5] I will not get into panic in such a situation. I shall gather myself together and try to know how serious is the danger. Then, what may come, with the presence of mind, I will face the danger bravely.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45830,441,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','With its lights still shining, the Titanic stood upright for a few minutes. Then, at 2.20 a.m., on 15 April, she vanished into the sea. About fifteen hundred people drowned. Captain Smith and most of his crew went down with the ship.\nThe Carpathia arrived at 3.40 a.m. The ship\'s crew found the life-boats and saved seven hundred people in them. They also found chairs and pieces of wood floating on the water. But almost all of the people in the water had drowned or died of cold. The Carpathia was too late to save them.\nThe sinking of the Titanic made people think harder about the safety of passengers on ships. They asked why ships carry such few life-boats and why they turn off their radios. In 1913, it was decided that all ships must have enough room in their life-boats to carry everyone on board. It was also decided that ships must keep their radios working every hour of the day and night.\nIn 1985, scientists sent a submarine to look for the wreck of the Titanic. They found the ship lying in two pieces on the ocean floor, at a depth of around 4000 metres (13,000 feet). Later, they took photographs of the wreck and brought small parts of it to the surface of the water.\nThere are many stories of bravery on that terrible night in April 1912. In 1998, the director James Cameron made a famous film of the Titanic\'s maiden voyage. It is a very sad film. Almost everyone who sees the film leaves the cinema in tears.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following sentences using the information from the passage:\n      i. Captain Smith and most -------- went down with the ship.\n      ii. Scientists found the ship lying in --------- on the ocean floor.\n      iii. The famous film on the Titanic\'s maiden voyage was made by the director ----------- .\n      iv. Titanic had sunk at a depth of around ------------------.\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Why could \'Carpathia\' not do much to save more lives?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Use the following phrases in the sentences of your own :\n        i. to look for              ii. almost all.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. They took photographs of the wreck.\n            (Begin with the word \'photographs\' and rewrite the sentence.)\n       ii. It was decid that all ships must have enough room in their life-boats. \n            (Find the еггог and rewrite the sentences.)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Give your suggestions to avoid accidents in the waters.','','A1] i) of his crew            ii) two pieces\n       iii) James Cameron              iv) 4000 metres (13,000 feet)\nA2] The ship \'Carpathia\' arrived at 3.40 a.m. The Titanic had already vanished into the sea before that. Carpathia\'s crew found the life-boats and they could save only seven hundred people. But almost all of the people in the water had drowned or died of cold. The ship \'Carpathia\' was so late that its crew could not save more lives.\nA3] i) Their house has been dilapidated, so they are looking for a new house.\n       ii) Almost all the students of our class are going for the picnic. \nA4] i) Photographs of the wreck were taken by them.\n       ii) It was decided that all ships must have enough room in their life-boats.\nA5] 1) Entering the unknown river/lake, etc. may be avoided.\n       2) Entering the waters may be avoided unless you know swimming.\n       3) The warning signals put up on signboards should be taken in notice.\n       4) Travelling on overcrowded boat/launch may be avoided.\n       5) Instructions of the life-guards should be followed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45833,442,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Read about how different festivals are celebrated in the north-eastern States of India.\nThe North east region of India, consisting of seven States, is a place of diverse cultures. The different communities and tribes celebrate their unique festivals with great enthusiasm and joy. Many of their festivals are based on agriculture and no celebration is complete without the traditional music and dance.\nBlessed with lush greenery and the mighty river Brahmaputra, the people of Assam have a lot to celebrate. So Bihu is the chief festival of this State. It is celebrated by people of all religions, castes or tribes. The three different sets of Bihu mark the beginning of the harvesting season, the completion of sowing and the end of the harvest season.\nThe Bihu dance is a joyous one performed by both young men and women and is noted for its brisk steps and hand movement. Unusual instruments provide traditional music for the dance the dhol which is similar to a drum, the pepa, a wind instrument made from a buffalo horn, cymbals and a bamboo clapper. The songs have been handed down through many generations. Bihu competitions held all over Assam attract visitors and locals alike in large numbers.\nLiving further north in the mountainous region of the Himalayas, Arunachal Pradesh finds a mention in the ancient literature of the Puranas and the Mahabharata. Nature has provided the people of this region with a deep feeling for beauty which can be seen in their festivities, songs and dances.\nThe new year festival, called Losar is perhaps the most important festival in certain areas of Arunachal. On this occasion, people clean out their homes to bring in the new year and to discard the old.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Choose the appropriate alternatives to complete the following:\n      i. Every festival of North-East India is incomplete without ------------.\n          a) food and sweets         b) dance and music         c) lights and crackers\n      ii. People of -------- are true lovers of nature and its beauty.\n           a) Assam            b) Mizoram           c) Arunachal Pradesh\n      iii. The ------- festival is celebrated three times a year to mark the stages of agriculture.\n           a) Bihu            b) Chapchar Kut              c) Wangala\n      iv.  Arunachal Pradesh finds a mention in an ancient literature of the  ------- and Mahabharat.\n             a) Ramayan             b) Vedas              c) Puranas\n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       On what occasions are the three sets of Bihu festival celebrated?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        Pick out the noun forms of the following words from the passage :\n        i. complete         ii. move         iii. green         iv. celebrate.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. The Bihu dance is performed by both young men and women.\n             (Rewrite using \'as well as\'.)\n       ii. ii. The Bihu dance is a very joyous one.\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Which festival do you like most? Why?','','A1] i) b) dance and music            ii) c) Arunachal Pradesh\n      iii) a) Bihu                                 iv) c) Puranas\nA2] The three different sets of Bihu festival are celebrated according to different agricultural activities. These three sets are the beginning of the harvesting season, the completion of sowing and the end of the harvest season.\nA3] i) completion         ii) movement          iii) greenery     iv) celebration.  \nA4] i) The Bihu dance is performed both by young men as well as women.\n       ii) What a joyous the Bihu dance is!\nA5] I like Navaratri festival the most. I like it most because I can enjoy Garba dancing a lot. I join different groups of Garba and enjoy dancing. I also get to meet my friends and relatives during Navaratri.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45834,442,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','A charming festival of Manipur-Ningol Chakouba- binds and revives the relationship between married girls and their parents. The women and their children are given a sumptuous feast and gifts. During Cheiraoba, the Manipur new year festival in April, people clean and decorate their houses and prepare special festive dishes. As part of the ritual, the villagers climb the nearest hilltops for they believe that this will help them rise to a higher plane in this life.\nThe Spring Festival or Chapchar Kut is an important occasion, mostly celebrated in Mizoram. Men and women of all ages, wearing traditional dresses and headgear, perform folk dances and sing traditional songs on this occasion. Drums, gongs and cymbals add to the festive air!\nWangala or the hundred-drum festival is the most important festival celebrated by the Garos in the villages of Meghalaya. This harvest festival is celebrated to mark the end of a period of hard work, which naturally brings a good harvest. The dance performed during the festival has two parallel lines one of men and the other of women, clad in their festive costumes. While the men beat the drums, the lines of dancers move forward together in a rhythmic manner.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following sentences using the information in the passage:\n       i. Losar is the most important festival in -----------.\n       ii. The hundred-drum festival is celebrated by ---------- in the villages of Meghalaya.\n       iii. The festival Ningol Chakouba binds and revives the relation between ----------- .\n       iv. A typical dance is performed in -------- by two parallel lines-one of men and the other of  \n            women. \n','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        How do the villagers dance during the Wangala festival?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Find the adjective forms of the following from the passage:\n         i. rite          ii.  importance       iii. festival          iv. rhythm.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. The Spring Festival is an important occasion, (Add a Question Tag.)\n        ii. Losar is the most important festival in certain areas of Arunachal. \n               (Rewrite the sentence using Positive Degree.)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n        Why, do you think, should we know about the different festivals of other states?','','A1] i) Arunachal               ii) Garos\n       iii) married girls and their parents           iv) Meghalaya\nA2] The dance performed by the villagers during Wangala festival has two parallel lines. One is of men and the other is of women. Women wear their festive costumes and the men beat the drums. The lines of dancers move forward together in a rhythmic manner.\nA3] i) ritual                 ii) important           iii) festive          iv) rhythmic.   \nA4] i) The Spring Festival is an important occasion. isn\'t it?\n       ii) No other festival in certain areas of Arunachal is as (so) important as Losar.\nA5] We must know about the different festivals of other states because we have to understand their customs, culture and their rituals during festivals. Many people from other states stay in Maharashtra and we join them in their festivals as friends. To make our participation in their festivals meaningful, we must know everything about them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45887,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How many types of cells are found in living organisms?','','','Living organisms have different types of cells which are adapted to particular function. They are of following types\ni) Skin cells            ii) Muscle cells           iii) Neuron             iv) Blood cells            v) Stem cells','',0,0,0,NULL),(45888,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Have you seen fully turgid, rectangular cells of onion peelings?','','','Yes, the cells are rectangular with nucleus at the periphery of each cell.','',0,0,1,NULL),(45889,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Characteristics of a cell','','','i) A cell is the structural and functional unit of an organism.\nii) Cells are generally small in size. They cannot be seen by naked eye.\niii) Every cell is independent and has its metabolic machinery\niv) The cell can divide, multiply, grow and die.\nv) The dead cells are replaced by new ones.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45890,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Structure of Nucleus ','','','i) It is a double layered membrane with small pores.\nii) These pores allow the passage of material in and out of the nucleus.\niii) It has one round nucleolus and a network of chromatin fibres.\niv) They are thin thread like structures, which condense to form short thick chromosomes at the time of cell division.\nv) Functional segments on chromosomes are called genes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45891,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Functions of Golgi Complex','','','The functions of Golgi Complex are:\ni) It is associated with the secretions of the cell, such as enzymes and hormones.\nii) It modifies, sorts and packs materials synthesized in the cell and dispatches them to various targets like plasma membrane and lysosomes.\niii) It produces vacuoles and secretory vesicles.\niv) It helps in the formation of cell wall, plasma membrane and protein synthesis.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45892,134,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Lysosomes – Functions ','','','The functions of Lysosomes are:\ni) They play an important role in the immune system of the body as they destroy viruses and bacteria that attack the cell.\nii) They act as demolition squads which destroy worn out cellular organelles and organic debris by cellular digestion.\niii) They are also known as suicide bags of the cell because they burst and enzymatically digest old and damaged cells by autolysis.\niv) They also digest stored food content like proteins and fats which provides required energy to the cell during starvation.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45893,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What materials are used to pack your favorite biscuits and chocolates?','','','Tin plate and aluminum are used to pack biscuits and chocolates.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45894,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What happens to the agricultural waste after a few days when it is dumped in a compost pit?\n','','','Agricultural waste starts to decompose forming manure.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45895,134,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What is the benefit of folding of inner membrane in mitochondria?','','','The folding\'s of inner membrane increases the surface area inside the organelle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45896,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which type of energy is required to the fans, computers and electric bulb? Where is this energy produced? ','','','i) Electrical energy is required to run fans computers and electric bulb.\nii) Electrical energy is produced at Electric Power stations.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(45897,134,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Wooden doors fit very tightly in rainy season. Why does this happen?','','','i) There is a lot of moisture in the surroundings in rainy season, which gets absorbed by the wood.\nii) This causes in swelling of the cells which increases the overall size of the wooden door.    \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(45941,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','From what the organs and organ systems are made up of?','','','i) Human body is made up of different types of cells.\nii) Different cells combine to form tissue.\niii) Many tissue together form a organ.\niv) And many organs form a organ system.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45942,135,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which different organs perform the function of breathing in the body animals? \n','','','i) Different animals have different organs for breathing.\nii) Animals have cuticles, gills, lungs, network of tubes, etc. whereas humans may have organs like nose, pharynx, wind pipe, lungs and diaphragm for breathing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45943,135,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which health parameter of blood donor should be checked?','','','i) A donor must be healthy and free of disease.\nii) He/she must have completed 18 years of age.\niii) He/she must be atleast 50 kg in weight.\niv) A parental permission is required for those youngsters donating blood.\nv) Females must have a minimum of 12.5 g/dL of haemoglobin and males with a minimum level of 13.0 g/dL.\nvi) He/She should not have donated blood in the last 56 days.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45944,508,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and answer the following questions :\nThe lion walks on padded paws,\nThe squirrel leaps from limb to limb,\nWhile flies can crawl straight up a wall, \nAnd seals can dive and swim.\nThe worm he wiggles all around,\nThe monkey swings by his tail, \nAnd birds may hop upon the ground\nOr spread their wings and sail.\nBut boys and girls have much more fun:\nThey leap and dance and walk and run.\n1] Write down any five pairs of ‘one – many’ from the poem.\n     Example : lion – lions \n\n     ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2] List all action words from poem.\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) lion – lions             ii) tiger – tigers          iii) plant – plants \n    iv) tree – trees         v) boy – boys              vi) girl – girls \n    vii) hill – hills \n2] walk, leap, crawl, swim, dive, swing, hop, sail, dance, run','',0,0,0,NULL),(45945,508,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the following table and make meaningful sentences.',' -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------','','Rains falls from the sky\n2) Rain falls on the ground\n3) Rain runs in the river\n4) Rain falls on the hill etc.','',1,0,0,NULL),(45946,508,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and answer the following questions :\nThe lion walks on padded paws,\nThe squirrel leaps from limb to limb,\nWhile flies can crawl straight up a wall, \nAnd seals can dive and swim.\nThe worm he wiggles all around,\nThe monkey swings by his tail, \nAnd birds may hop upon the ground\nOr spread their wings and sail.\nBut boys and girls have much more fun:\nThey leap and dance and walk and run.\n1] Write down any five pairs of ‘one – many’ from the poem.\n     Example : lion – lions \n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2] List all action words from poem.\n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) lion – lions             ii) tiger – tigers          iii) plant – plants \n    iv) tree – trees         v) boy – boys              vi) girl – girls \n    vii) hill – hills \n2] walk, leap, crawl, swim, dive, swing, hop, sail, dance, run','',0,0,0,NULL),(45947,508,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the following table and make meaningful sentences.','\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------','','Rains falls from the sky\n2) Rain falls on the ground\n3) Rain runs in the river\n4) Rain falls on the hill etc.','',1,0,0,NULL),(45948,510,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the questions\nThe Clever Turtle\nOnce, some villagers caught a turtle in the field and took him to their chief. \"You must punish this thief!\" they said.\n\"Oh, no!\" said the turtle. \"Please don\'t throw me back in the river. You may hang me, whip me, burn me, or push me from a cliff. But please, please, whatever you do, don\'t throw me back in the river!\"\n\"Throw him back in the river!\" The chief ordered. The villagers threw the turtle in the river. The turtle laughed and laughed and swam away merrily.\n1] Complete the following.\n     a) The villagers caught the turtle --------------------------\n\n     b) The chief ordered to ------------------------------------\n2] Complete the table :','','','a) in the field\n    b) throw the tertial in the river.\n2) caught, took, said, threw, swam','',1,0,0,NULL),(45949,510,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the questions\nThe Clever Turtle\nOnce, some villagers caught a turtle in the field and took him to their chief. \"You must punish this thief!\" they said.\n\"Oh, no!\" said the turtle. \"Please don\'t throw me back in the river. You may hang me, whip me, burn me, or push me from a cliff. But please, please, whatever you do, don\'t throw me back in the river!\"\n\"Throw him back in the river!\" The chief ordered. The villagers threw the turtle in the river. The turtle laughed and laughed and swam away merrily.\n1] Complete the following.\n     a) The villagers caught the turtle ---------------------------\n\n     b) The chief ordered to --------------------------------------\n2] Complete the table :','','','a) in the field\n    b) throw the tertile in the river.\n2) caught, took, said, threw, swam','',1,0,0,NULL),(45950,511,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following shapes :','\n------------------------------   -------------------------------\n\n-----------------------------     ------------------------------\n\n----------------------------       ------------------------------','','Arrow, square, triangle, line, cross, point, rectangle, star, circle, temple','\n',1,0,0,NULL),(45951,511,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','See the following map and answer the following questions :','1) Where is the fountain? \n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------\n2) How many slides are there?\n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Where is the banyan tree.\n\n     --------------------------------------------------------\n4) How many swings are there?\n\n     ----------------------------------------------------------\n5) Where is the entrance?\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------','','In the center of the garden\n2) Four\n3) in the north – east corner\n4) five\n5) Left of north – east side','',1,0,0,NULL),(45952,511,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following shapes :','\n------------------------------   -------------------------------\n\n-----------------------------     ------------------------------\n\n----------------------------       ------------------------------','','Arrow, square, triangle, line, cross, point, rectangle, star, circle, temple','',1,0,0,NULL),(45953,511,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','See the following map and answer the following questions :','1) Where is the fountain? \n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------\n2) How many slides are there?\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Where is the banyan tree.\n\n     ----------------------------------------------------------\n\n4) How many swings are there?\n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Where is the entrance?\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------------','','In the center of the garden\n2) Four\n3) in the north – east corner\n4) five\n5) Left of north – east side','',1,0,0,NULL),(45954,512,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the questions :\nLong, long ago, in the faraway land of Korea, there was a minister. On the right of his house, there lived a blacksmith. On the left, there lived a carpenter.\nThe blacksmith and the carpenter began their work early in the morning. Tong, tong, tong the blacksmith beat the iron with his big hammer. Tock, tock, tock, the carpenter worked with his small hammer. Krrr. krrr krrr he used his saw to cut the wood. They worked day and night and made a lot of noise, All that noise disturbed the minister.\n1) Who lived between the blacksmith and the carpenter?\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Who used a big hammer?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Who used a small hammer?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------\n4) Which time they worked?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Who disturbed noise pollution?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------','','','A minister\n2) The blacksmith\n3) The carpenter\n4) day and night\n5) A minister','',0,0,0,NULL),(45955,512,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the following questions :\nBut the next morning, the minister woke up with the sound of tong, tong, tong, tock, tock, tock, krrr, krır, krrr again. He got annoyed. He called his servants. \"Go and see who is making all that noise!\"\nAfter some time, the servants came back. They had some news for the minister. The carpenter and the blacksmith had moved their houses the blacksmith had moved to the carpenter\'s house and the carpenter had moved to the blacksmith\'s house!\nThe blacksmith and the carpenter carried on their work in their houses day and night! \n1) Did the blacksmith shift his house?\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Did the carpenter shift his house?\n\n ------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Was the minister happy at the end?\n\n ------------------------------------------------------------\n4) Who moved to the blacksmith’s house?\n\n       ------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Who moved to the carpenter’s house?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------','','','Yes\n2) Yes\n3) No\n4) The carpenter\n5) The blacksmith ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45956,512,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the questions :\nLong, long ago, in the faraway land of Korea, there was a minister. On the right of his house, there lived a blacksmith. On the left, there lived a carpenter.\nThe blacksmith and the carpenter began their work early in the morning. Tong, tong, tong the blacksmith beat the iron with his big hammer. Tock, tock, tock, the carpenter worked with his small hammer. Krrr. krrr krrr he used his saw to cut the wood. They worked day and night and made a lot of noise, All that noise disturbed the minister.\n1) Who lived between the blacksmith and the carpenter?\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Who used a big hammer?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Who used a small hammer?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------\n4) Which time they worked?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Who disturbed noise pollution?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------','','','A minister\n2) The blacksmith\n3) The carpenter\n4) day and night\n5) A minister','',0,0,0,NULL),(45957,512,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the following questions :\nBut the next morning, the minister woke up with the sound of tong, tong, tong, tock, tock, tock, krrr, krır, krrr again. He got annoyed. He called his servants. \"Go and see who is making all that noise!\"\nAfter some time, the servants came back. They had some news for the minister. The carpenter and the blacksmith had moved their houses the blacksmith had moved to the carpenter\'s house and the carpenter had moved to the blacksmith\'s house!\nThe blacksmith and the carpenter carried on their work in their houses day and night! \n1) Did the blacksmith shift his house?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Did the carpenter shift his house?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Was the minister happy at the end?\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------\n4) Who moved to the blacksmith’s house?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Who moved to the carpenter’s house?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------','','','Yes\n2) Yes\n3) No\n4) The carpenter\n5) The blacksmith ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45958,514,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following poem and answer the questions :\nCats purr. Lions roar. Owls hoot. Bears snore.\nCrickets creak. Mice squeak. Sheep baa. But I speak!\nMonkeys chatter. Cows moo. Ducks quack. Pigeons coo.\nPigs squeal. Horses neigh. Chickens cluck. But I speak!\nRewrite the lines as shown :\n1) First stanza  : cats purr – A cat purrs\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Second stanza : Moneys chatter – Monkeys are chattering\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Lions roar – A lion roars.\n    Owls hoot – AN owl hoots\n2) Cows moo – Cows are mooing.\n    Ducks quack – Ducks are quacking. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45959,514,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following poem and answer the questions :\nCats purr. Lions roar. Owls hoot. Bears snore.\nCrickets creak. Mice squeak. Sheep baa. But I speak!\nMonkeys chatter. Cows moo. Ducks quack. Pigeons coo.\nPigs squeal. Horses neigh. Chickens cluck. But I speak!\nRewrite the lines as shown :\n1) First stanza  : cats purr – A cat purrs\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Second stanza : Moneys chatter – Monkeys are chattering\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Lions roar – A lion roars.\n    Owls hoot – AN owl hoots\n2) Cows moo – Cows are mooing.\n    Ducks quack – Ducks are quacking. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(45960,516,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the sentences.\n\n     i. The child is speaking to ------------------------------\n\n     ii. The wind passes like ----------------------------------\nI saw you toss the kites on high \nAnd blow the birds about the sky; \nAnd all around I heard you pass, \nLike ladies\' skirts across the grass \nO wind, a-blowing all day long, \nO wind, that sings so loud a song!\n2] Complete the following  \n     The thing that the wind does ---------------------\n\n     i. ---------------------          ii. ---------------  iii. --------------------\n3] Write down the pairs of rhyming words.\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) the wind\n     ii) like ladies skirts across the grass.\n2] i) tosses the kites on high\n     ii) blows the birds.\n     iii) sings a loud song.\n3] i) high = sky               ii) pass = grass.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45961,516,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the web \n','\n  -------------------------------         --------------------------------------\nO you that are so strong and cold, \nO blower, are you young or old? \nAre you a beast of field and tree, \nOr just a stronger child than me? \nO wind, a-blowing all day long, \nO wind, that sings so loud a song!\n2] a) Can we see the wind?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) Can we hear the wind?\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write down the pair of rhyming words.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) strong                  ii) cold\n2] a) No, we cannot see the wind.\n     b) Yes, we can hear the wind.\n3] i) cold – old,           ii) tree – me        iii) long – song ','',1,0,0,NULL),(45962,516,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the sentences.\n\n     i. The child is speaking to -----------------------------\n\n     ii. The wind passes like -------------------------------\nI saw you toss the kites on high \nAnd blow the birds about the sky; \nAnd all around I heard you pass, \nLike ladies\' skirts across the grass \nO wind, a-blowing all day long, \nO wind, that sings so loud a song!\n2] Complete the following \n     The thing that the wind does -----------------------\n\n     i. -------------          ii. ---------------        iii. --------------------\n3] Write down the pairs of rhyming words.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) the wind\n     ii) like ladies skirts across the grass.\n2] i) tosses the kites on high\n     ii) blows the birds.\n     iii) sings a loud song.\n3] i) high = sky               ii) pass = grass.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45963,516,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the web \n','\n   -----------------------------        ----------------------------------------\nO you that are so strong and cold, \nO blower, are you young or old? \nAre you a beast of field and tree, \nOr just a stronger child than me? \nO wind, a-blowing all day long, \nO wind, that sings so loud a song!\n2] a) Can we see the wind?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) Can we hear the wind?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write down the pair of rhyming words.\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) strong                  ii) cold\n2] a) No, we cannot see the wind.\n     b) Yes, we can hear the wind.\n3] i) cold – old,           ii) tree – me        iii) long – song ','',1,0,0,NULL),(45964,517,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities : \n1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n    i. The little boy lived in ---------------------------------------\n\n    ii. The boy’s uncle believed in ------------------------------\n\n    iii. The lovely garden was ----------------------------------\n\n    iv. The boy was playing -----------------------------------------\nOnce there was a little boy. He lived in a pretty little cottage. There was a lovely garden around the cottage. Different kinds of plants grew in the garden. So, there was a lot of work to do their water the plants every day, give manure from time to time, cut and prune the plants at the right time, weed the garden, clean the garden. Everybody in the little boy\'s family helped to take care of the garden.\nThe boy had a very strict uncle. He believed in hard work and discipline. One day the boy was playing around in the house.\n2] Use the appropriate phrase in the sentences.\n      [a lot of, take care of, believe in]\n\n      i. I don’t ----------------- ghosts.\n\n      ii. Mother ................... her child.\n\n      iii. There is ------------------- water in the river.\n3] Find different action words. \n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) a pretty little cottage            ii) hard work and discipline\n    iii) around the cottage              iv) in the house  \n2] i) believe in          ii) take care of          iii) a lot of\n3] work, clean, help, play','',0,0,0,NULL),(45965,517,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web \n','\n ------------------------------          ---------------------------------------\n\n -------------------------------         --------------------------------------\nOnce there was a little boy. He lived in a pretty little cottage. There was a lovely garden around the cottage. Different kinds of plants grew in the garden. So, there was a lot of work to do their water the plants every day, give manure from time to time, cut and prune the plants at the right time, weed the garden, clean the garden. Everybody in the little boy\'s family helped to take care of the garden.\nThe boy had a very strict uncle. He believed in hard work and discipline. One day the boy was playing around in the house.\n2] a) How many people are there in the passage?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) Who are they?\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] From the passage find out opposites of :\n\n     i. dirty : --------------               ii. soft : -------------------------','','i) Water the plants                ii) Give manure to plants\n     iii) Weed the garden             iv) Clean the garden\n2] a) There are two people in the passage.\n     b) boy and uncle\n3] i) clean          ii. hard','',1,0,0,NULL),(45966,517,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Who said to whom.\n\n      i. \'Uncle is so unfair.\'\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n      ii. \'Why do you look so unhappy?\'\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe little boy felt sad. \'Uncle is so unfair,\' he thought. His chin began to wobble, he had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up. Just then, his mother walked in.\n\"What\'s the matter, little boy?\" she asked, \"Why do you look so unhappy?\"\n\"Uncle told me to weed the garden,\" said the little boy.\n\"Oh!\" said his mother, \"What fun that will be! I love to weed, and it is such a fine day! Can I come and help you to weed?\"\n\"Why, yes!\" said the boy.\nSo, the boy and his mother went outside. They weeded the garden. They had a very good time together, working and chatting and laughing.\n2] From the passage find out the opposites of :\n\n     i. happy x ……...........                  ii. bad x ……….............\n\n     iii. hate x ……...........                    iv. fair x ………...............\n3] Punctuate the following.\n     \"What\'s the matter, little boy ?\" she asked.\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The little boy to himself                   ii) The boy\'s mother to him.\n2] i) unhappy, sad          ii) good          iii) love         iv) unfair\n3] \"What\'s the matter, little boy she asked.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45967,517,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web \n','\n     ------------------------------              ---------------------------------------\n\n     -------------------------------              --------------------------------------\nThe little boy felt sad. \'Uncle is so unfair,\' he thought. His chin began to wobble, he had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up. Just then, his mother walked in.\n\"What\'s the matter, little boy?\" she asked, \"Why do you look so unhappy?\"\n\"Uncle told me to weed the garden,\" said the little boy.\n\"Oh!\" said his mother, \"What fun that will be! I love to weed, and it is such a fine day! Can I come and help you to weed?\"\n\"Why, yes!\" said the boy.\nSo, the boy and his mother went outside. They weeded the garden. They had a very good time together, working and chatting and laughing.\n2] a) How many people are there in the passage?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) Who are they?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] What body parts are mentioned in the passage?\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','The little boy felt sad. His chin began to wobble He had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up.\n2] a) There are three people in the story.\n    b) Boy, his mother and his uncle.\n3] Chin, hands, eyes, throat','',1,0,0,NULL),(45968,517,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities : \n1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n    i. The little boy lived in ------------------------------\n\n    ii. The boy’s uncle believed in -------------------------\n\n    iii. The lovely garden was -------------------------------\n\n    iv. The boy was playing -----------------------------------\nOnce there was a little boy. He lived in a pretty little cottage. There was a lovely garden around the cottage. Different kinds of plants grew in the garden. So, there was a lot of work to do their water the plants every day, give manure from time to time, cut and prune the plants at the right time, weed the garden, clean the garden. Everybody in the little boy\'s family helped to take care of the garden.\nThe boy had a very strict uncle. He believed in hard work and discipline. One day the boy was playing around in the house.\n2] Use the appropriate phrase in the sentences.\n      [a lot of, take care of, believe in]\n\n      i. I don’t ------------------- ghosts.\n\n      ii. Mother ............................ her child.\n\n      iii. There is -------------------- water in the river.\n3] Find different action words. \n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) a pretty little cottage            ii) hard work and discipline\n    iii) around the cottage              iv) in the house  \n2] i) believe in          ii) take care of          iii) a lot of\n3] work, clean, help, play','',0,0,0,NULL),(45969,517,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web \n','\n    ------------------------------               ---------------------------------------\n\n    -------------------------------              --------------------------------------\nOnce there was a little boy. He lived in a pretty little cottage. There was a lovely garden around the cottage. Different kinds of plants grew in the garden. So, there was a lot of work to do their water the plants every day, give manure from time to time, cut and prune the plants at the right time, weed the garden, clean the garden. Everybody in the little boy\'s family helped to take care of the garden.\nThe boy had a very strict uncle. He believed in hard work and discipline. One day the boy was playing around in the house.\n2] a) How many people are there in the passage?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) Who are they?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] From the passage find out opposites of :\n\n     i. dirty : ----------------               ii. soft : -----------------------','','i) Water the plants                ii) Give manure to plants\n     iii) Weed the garden             iv) Clean the garden\n2] a) There are two people in the passage.\n     b) boy and uncle\n3] i) clean          ii. hard','',1,0,0,NULL),(45970,517,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Who said to whom.\n      i. \'Uncle is so unfair.\'\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n      ii. \'Why do you look so unhappy?\'\n\n           ---------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe little boy felt sad. \'Uncle is so unfair,\' he thought. His chin began to wobble, he had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up. Just then, his mother walked in.\n\"What\'s the matter, little boy?\" she asked, \"Why do you look so unhappy?\"\n\"Uncle told me to weed the garden,\" said the little boy.\n\"Oh!\" said his mother, \"What fun that will be! I love to weed, and it is such a fine day! Can I come and help you to weed?\"\n\"Why, yes!\" said the boy.\nSo, the boy and his mother went outside. They weeded the garden. They had a very good time together, working and chatting and laughing.\n2] From the passage find out the opposites of :\n\n     i. happy x ……............                  ii. bad x ………..............\n\n     iii. hate x …….................                    iv. fair x ………................\n3] Punctuate the following.\n     \"What\'s the matter, little boy ?\" she asked.\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The little boy to himself                   ii) The boy\'s mother to him.\n2] i) unhappy, sad          ii) good          iii) love         iv) unfair\n3] \"What\'s the matter, little boy she asked.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45971,517,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web \n','\n---------------------------             ------------------------------------------\n\n----------------------------             -----------------------------------------\nThe little boy felt sad. \'Uncle is so unfair,\' he thought. His chin began to wobble, he had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up. Just then, his mother walked in.\n\"What\'s the matter, little boy?\" she asked, \"Why do you look so unhappy?\"\n\"Uncle told me to weed the garden,\" said the little boy.\n\"Oh!\" said his mother, \"What fun that will be! I love to weed, and it is such a fine day! Can I come and help you to weed?\"\n\"Why, yes!\" said the boy.\nSo, the boy and his mother went outside. They weeded the garden. They had a very good time together, working and chatting and laughing.\n2] a) How many people are there in the passage?\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) Who are they?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] What body parts are mentioned in the passage?\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','The little boy felt sad. His chin began to wobble He had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up.\n2] a) There are three people in the story.\n    b) Boy, his mother and his uncle.\n3] Chin, hands, eyes, throat','',1,0,0,NULL),(45972,518,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n\n     i. Everyone loves gold. : --------------------------\n\n     ii. Flowers and Sunshine are more beautiful than gold. : -----------------------\n\n     iii. The king has a strange wish. : -------------------------------\n\n     iv. The king did not thank the stranger. : ---------------------------\nKing : Why, everyone loves gold. It makes you rich. I love to sit in this dark cellar and look at all my gold.\nStranger : In your garden are flowers and golden sunshine. They are far more beautiful\nKing : Not to me. I like to be here, where everything is made of real gold. I wish that everything I touched would turn to gold.\nStranger: That is a strange wish. Are you sure you want to turn everything you touch to gold?\nKing : Yes, yes. I\'m sure. Everything made of gold! I would be so happy!\nStranger : Then you shall have your wish. Tomorrow morning, at sunrise, you shall have the Golden Touch.\nKing : Thank you so much, O Stranger!\nStranger: Do not thank me. Perhaps you will be sorry.\nKing : Oh, no! It\'s a dream come true!\n2] Use the correct word and complete the sentences, \n       [of, at, to]\n\n     i. Don\'t look .................. the sun with naked eye.\n\n     ii. You shall have the Golden Touch .................... sunrise.\n\n     iii. Everything here is made.......................... gold.\n\n     iv. Do you want to turn everything .......................... gold.\n3] Find out two describing words from the passage.\n \n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) True          ii) True            iii) True           iv) False.\n2] i) at            ii) at               iii) of              iv) to\n3] i) rich           ii) dark','',0,0,0,NULL),(45973,518,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n     i. The king loves to ----------------------------------\n\n     ii. He wishes that -------------------------------------------\n\n     iii. Next morning at sunrise the king has --------------------------------------\nKing : Why, everyone loves gold. It makes you rich. I love to sit in this dark cellar and look at all my gold.\nStranger : In your garden are flowers and golden sunshine. They are far more beautiful\nKing : Not to me. I like to be here, where everything is made of real gold. I wish that everything I touched would turn to gold.\nStranger: That is a strange wish. Are you sure you want to turn everything you touch to gold?\nKing : Yes, yes. I\'m sure. Everything made of gold! I would be so happy!\nStranger : Then you shall have your wish. Tomorrow morning, at sunrise, you shall have the Golden Touch.\nKing : Thank you so much, O Stranger!\nStranger: Do not thank me. Perhaps you will be sorry.\nKing : Oh, no! It\'s a dream come true!\n2] a) Name the characters in the play.\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    b) Who loved gold?\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out four describing words from the passage.\n\n    -------------------------------         --------------------------------------\n\n    ---------------------------------       ------------------------------------','','','i) sit in the dark cellar and look at his gold.\n     ii) everything he touches will turn to gold.\n     iii) the Golden Touch.\n2] a) King Midas, a stranger \n     b) King Midas loved gold.\n3] i) golden         ii) beautiful','',0,0,0,NULL),(45974,518,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Make a list of things that are turned to gold.\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------\nKing : Good morning, Marygold. See what I have brought for you.\nMarygold : A fine yellow rose. (Smells it.) Oh, it has no smell, and the hard petals have pricked my nose. (Starts crying.)\nKing : What are you crying for? It is a golden rose. All roses in my garden are golden!\nMarygold : Are they all like this one?\nKing : Come on, now. Let\'s have our breakfast. ……. oh!\nMarygold : What\'s the matter? Have you burnt your mouth?\nKing : This fish has turned to gold. I must try an egg.... oh, that has turned to gold, too. Oh, no! What shall I do now?\nMarygold : Tell me, what\'s the matter?\nKing : Everything I touch turns to gold. I shall never be able to eat anything.\nMarygold : My poor father! Can I help you?\n                     Marygold rushes forward.\nKing : No, don\'t touch me. Keep away!\n           Oh, Marygold, what have you done?\n           Marygold has turned into a statue of gold.\nKing : My daughter! My dear, dear Marygold! So full of life! Like a ray of sunshine! She is only a golden doll, now!\n2] a) Who was Marygold?\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    b) What did the king wish?\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] What happened to Marygold? \n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) a rose          ii) fish           iii) an egg          iv) Marygold\n2] a) Marygold was daughter of King Midas\n    b) The King wished that everything he touched could turn to gold.\n3] Marygold turned into a statue of gold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45975,518,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following table.\n','King : Good morning, Marygold. See what I have brought for you.\nMarygold : A fine yellow rose. (Smells it.) Oh, it has no smell, and the hard petals have pricked my nose. (Starts crying.)\nKing : What are you crying for? It is a golden rose. All roses in my garden are golden!\nMarygold : Are they all like this one?\nKing : Come on, now. Let\'s have our breakfast. ……. oh!\nMarygold : What\'s the matter? Have you burnt your mouth?\nKing : This fish has turned to gold. I must try an egg.... oh, that has turned to gold, too. Oh, no! What shall I do now?\nMarygold : Tell me, what\'s the matter?\nKing : Everything I touch turns to gold. I shall never be able to eat anything.\nMarygold : My poor father! Can I help you?\n                     Marygold rushes forward.\nKing : No, don\'t touch me. Keep away!\n           Oh, Marygold, what have you done?\n           Marygold has turned into a statue of gold.\nKing : My daughter! My dear, dear Marygold! So full of life! Like a ray of sunshine! She is only a golden doll, now!\n2] a) Find out two colours from the passage.\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    b) Find out two body organs from the passage\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Why was Marygold crying?\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) Before the Golden Touch, Marygold was so full of life, She was like a ray of sunshine.\n    ii) After the Golden Touch she turned into a statue of gold. She was only a golden doll.\n2] a) golden, yellow                b) nose, mouth.\n3] Because all the roses in her garden turned to gold.','',1,0,0,NULL),(45976,518,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n\n     i. Everyone loves gold. : --------------------------------\n\n     ii. Flowers and Sunshine are more beautiful than gold. : -------------------------\n\n     iii. The king has a strange wish. : ---------------------------------\n\n     iv. The king did not thank the stranger. : --------------------------\nKing : Why, everyone loves gold. It makes you rich. I love to sit in this dark cellar and look at all my gold.\nStranger : In your garden are flowers and golden sunshine. They are far more beautiful\nKing : Not to me. I like to be here, where everything is made of real gold. I wish that everything I touched would turn to gold.\nStranger: That is a strange wish. Are you sure you want to turn everything you touch to gold?\nKing : Yes, yes. I\'m sure. Everything made of gold! I would be so happy!\nStranger : Then you shall have your wish. Tomorrow morning, at sunrise, you shall have the Golden Touch.\nKing : Thank you so much, O Stranger!\nStranger: Do not thank me. Perhaps you will be sorry.\nKing : Oh, no! It\'s a dream come true!\n2] Use the correct word and complete the sentences, \n       [of, at, to]\n\n     i. Don\'t look ................... the sun with naked eye.\n\n     ii. You shall have the Golden Touch ....................... sunrise.\n\n     iii. Everything here is made..................... gold.\n\n     iv. Do you want to turn everything ......................... gold.\n3] Find out two describing words from the passage.\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) True          ii) True            iii) True           iv) False.\n2] i) at            ii) at               iii) of              iv) to\n3] i) rich           ii) dark','',0,0,0,NULL),(45977,518,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n     i. The king loves to ---------------------------------------\n\n     ii. He wishes that -------------------------------------------\n\n     iii. Next morning at sunrise the king has ----------------------------------------\nKing : Why, everyone loves gold. It makes you rich. I love to sit in this dark cellar and look at all my gold.\nStranger : In your garden are flowers and golden sunshine. They are far more beautiful\nKing : Not to me. I like to be here, where everything is made of real gold. I wish that everything I touched would turn to gold.\nStranger: That is a strange wish. Are you sure you want to turn everything you touch to gold?\nKing : Yes, yes. I\'m sure. Everything made of gold! I would be so happy!\nStranger : Then you shall have your wish. Tomorrow morning, at sunrise, you shall have the Golden Touch.\nKing : Thank you so much, O Stranger!\nStranger: Do not thank me. Perhaps you will be sorry.\nKing : Oh, no! It\'s a dream come true!\n2] a) Name the characters in the play.\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    b) Who loved gold?\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out four describing words from the passage.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) sit in the dark cellar and look at his gold.\n     ii) everything he touches will turn to gold.\n     iii) the Golden Touch.\n2] a) King Midas, a stranger \n     b) King Midas loved gold.\n3] i) golden         ii) beautiful','',0,0,0,NULL),(45978,518,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Make a list of things that are turned to gold.\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\nKing : Good morning, Marygold. See what I have brought for you.\nMarygold : A fine yellow rose. (Smells it.) Oh, it has no smell, and the hard petals have pricked my nose. (Starts crying.)\nKing : What are you crying for? It is a golden rose. All roses in my garden are golden!\nMarygold : Are they all like this one?\nKing : Come on, now. Let\'s have our breakfast. ……. oh!\nMarygold : What\'s the matter? Have you burnt your mouth?\nKing : This fish has turned to gold. I must try an egg.... oh, that has turned to gold, too. Oh, no! What shall I do now?\nMarygold : Tell me, what\'s the matter?\nKing : Everything I touch turns to gold. I shall never be able to eat anything.\nMarygold : My poor father! Can I help you?\n                     Marygold rushes forward.\nKing : No, don\'t touch me. Keep away!\n           Oh, Marygold, what have you done?\n           Marygold has turned into a statue of gold.\nKing : My daughter! My dear, dear Marygold! So full of life! Like a ray of sunshine! She is only a golden doll, now!\n2] a) Who was Marygold?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    b) What did the king wish?\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] What happened to Marygold? \n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) a rose          ii) fish           iii) an egg          iv) Marygold\n2] a) Marygold was daughter of King Midas\n    b) The King wished that everything he touched could turn to gold.\n3] Marygold turned into a statue of gold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45979,518,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following table.\n','King : Good morning, Marygold. See what I have brought for you.\nMarygold : A fine yellow rose. (Smells it.) Oh, it has no smell, and the hard petals have pricked my nose. (Starts crying.)\nKing : What are you crying for? It is a golden rose. All roses in my garden are golden!\nMarygold : Are they all like this one?\nKing : Come on, now. Let\'s have our breakfast. ……. oh!\nMarygold : What\'s the matter? Have you burnt your mouth?\nKing : This fish has turned to gold. I must try an egg.... oh, that has turned to gold, too. Oh, no! What shall I do now?\nMarygold : Tell me, what\'s the matter?\nKing : Everything I touch turns to gold. I shall never be able to eat anything.\nMarygold : My poor father! Can I help you?\n                     Marygold rushes forward.\nKing : No, don\'t touch me. Keep away!\n           Oh, Marygold, what have you done?\n           Marygold has turned into a statue of gold.\nKing : My daughter! My dear, dear Marygold! So full of life! Like a ray of sunshine! She is only a golden doll, now!\n2] a) Find out two colours from the passage.\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    b) Find out two body organs from the passage\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Why was Marygold crying?\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) Before the Golden Touch, Marygold was so full of life, She was like a ray of sunshine.\n    ii) After the Golden Touch she turned into a statue of gold. She was only a golden doll.\n2] a) golden, yellow                b) nose, mouth.\n3] Because all the roses in her garden turned to gold.','',1,0,0,NULL),(45980,520,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Match the following :\n','Dark brown is the river, \nGolden is the sand.\nIt flows along for ever, \nWith trees on either hand.\nGreen leaves a-floating, \nCastles of the foam, \nBoats of mine a-boating \nWhere will all come home?\n2] Find out the lines that mean the following.\n     i. Leaves are floating on the water.\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. The river flows down continuously.\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     iii. There is foam on the surface of water.\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     iv. There are trees on the bank of the river.\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.\n\n     ----------------------------         -----------------------------------------','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)\n2] i) Green leaves a – floating         ii) It flows along for ever.\n     iii) Castles of the foam.               iv) With trees on either hand.\n3] i) Sand = hand           ii) Foam = home. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(45981,520,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Match the following :\n','Dark brown is the river, \nGolden is the sand.\nIt flows along for ever, \nWith trees on either hand.\nGreen leaves a-floating, \nCastles of the foam, \nBoats of mine a-boating \nWhere will all come home?\n2] Find out the lines that mean the following.\n     i. Leaves are floating on the water.\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. The river flows down continuously.\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     iii. There is foam on the surface of water.\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     iv. There are trees on the bank of the river.\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.\n\n     ----------------------------        -----------------------------------------','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)\n2] i) Green leaves a – floating         ii) It flows along for ever.\n     iii) Castles of the foam.               iv) With trees on either hand.\n3] i) Sand = hand           ii) Foam = home. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(45982,521,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are true or false :\n\n    i. The sun rises in the west. : -----------------------\n\n    ii. The sun is a star. : ----------------------------\n\n    iii. The sun goes round the earth. : ----------------------------\n\n    iv. We see the stars at night. : ----------------------------\nHello, friends.\nYou see me in the sky every day.\nYou say that I rise in the east and set in the west.\nDid you guess who I am?\nYes, that\'s right! I am the sun.\nActually, I am a star like other stars you see at night.\nBut I am close to the earth.\nThat is why, I look so big and bright.\nI give the earth heat and light.\nAnd remember, I don\'t go round the earth.\nThe earth goes round me.\n2] Complete the following sentences.\n\n      i. The sun looks --------------------- and -------------------------\n\n      ii. The sun give the earth ------------------- and -----------------------\n\n      iii. The sun looks big and bright because -----------------------------\n\n      iv. The earth goes round ------------------------------------\n3] From the passage find the opposites of :\n\n     i. forget x ………...........              ii. dim x …………..................\n\n      iii. open x ………….............         iv. rise x ……….....................','','','i) False             ii) True              iii) False                iv) True\n2] i) big and bright                            ii) heat and light\n     iii) it is close to the earth           iv) the sun\n3] i) remember             ii) bright\n    iii) close                   iv) set','',0,0,0,NULL),(45983,521,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Name the following\n\n     i. The third planet from the sun -----------------------------\n\n     ii. The earth moves round ------------------------------------\n\n     iii. An envelope around the earth - ----------------------------\n\n     iv. Time taken by the earth to go round the sun ------------------------\nHello, everybody!\nI am the earth.\nI am a planet in the solar system.\nI am the third planet from the sun.\nI spin around myself all the time.\nAt the same time, I go round the sun.\nI take one year to go round the sun.\nMuch of my surface is covered with water.\nIf you look at me from space,\nI appear blue in colour.\nThat is why, some people call me the blue planet.\nThere is an envelope of air around me.\nToday, I am the only home of plants, animals and human beings.\n\n2] The earth is called the blue planet because ------\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] From the passage, find out the words that mean :- \n     i. go round  : ------------------                ii. look like : ------------------\n\n     iii. cover : ------------------                     iv. men and women. : ------------------','','','i) the earth             ii) the sun\n     iii) air                       iv) one year\n2] much of its surface is covered with water.\n3] i) spine           ii) appear            iii) envelope         iv) human beings.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45984,521,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web\n','\n-----------------------------            ----------------------------------------\n\n------------------------------           ---------------------------------------\nHello, everybody!\nI am the earth.\nI am a planet in the solar system.\nI am the third planet from the sun.\nI spin around myself all the time.\nAt the same time, I go round the sun.\nI take one year to go round the sun.\nMuch of my surface is covered with water.\nIf you look at me from space,\nI appear blue in colour.\nThat is why, some people call me the blue planet.\nThere is an envelope of air around me.\nToday, I am the only home of plants, animals and human beings.\n2] Find the English names of other two plantes.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Use the correct word from the bracket and complete the sentences. \n       [at, from round, with]\n\n     i. The ground was covered .................…….. green grass.\n\n     ii. The earth goes …….................…. the sun.\n\n     iii. The king looked …......................…… the beggar.\n\n     iv. The earth is the third plant........................... the sun.','','Plants, animals and human beings.\n2] Saturn, Mars, Jupiter, Mercury, Venus, Uranus, Neptune\n3] i) with          ii) round           iii) at           iv) from ','',1,0,0,NULL),(45985,521,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Name the following\n\n     i. Our closest neighbour ……………........................\n\n     ii. The number of astronauts landed on the moon ………...................\n\n     iii. The moon moves round the …………...........................\n\n     iv. …....................…….. wrote stories and poems about the moon.\nHello, people on earth!\nI am your closest neighbour.\nI go round and round the earth. \nFrom the earth, you always see only one side of me.\nYou never see the other side or the other half of me.\nPeople on earth love me.\nThey write stories and poems about me.\nThey send spacecrafts to study me from close. \nDo you know, two astronauts from earth landed on me once. \nMaybe, some day, other people on earth will also come to visit me. \nI am the moon.\n2] Write four sentences about the moon.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out numbers from the passage.\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) the moon             ii) two            iii) the earth          iv) people on the earth\n2] The moon is our closest neighbour. It moves around the earth. We can see only one side of the moon. We love the moon.\n3] One, half, two, once','',0,0,0,NULL),(45986,521,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are true or false :\n\n    i. The sun rises in the west. : ---------------------------\n\n    ii. The sun is a star. : ------------------------------\n\n    iii. The sun goes round the earth. : -------------------------\n\n    iv. We see the stars at night. : -------------------------------\nHello, friends.\nYou see me in the sky every day.\nYou say that I rise in the east and set in the west.\nDid you guess who I am?\nYes, that\'s right! I am the sun.\nActually, I am a star like other stars you see at night.\nBut I am close to the earth.\nThat is why, I look so big and bright.\nI give the earth heat and light.\nAnd remember, I don\'t go round the earth.\nThe earth goes round me.\n2] Complete the following sentences.\n\n      i. The sun looks --------------------- and --------------------------\n\n      ii. The sun give the earth ------------------------- and -----------------------\n\n      iii. The sun looks big and bright because -------------------------\n\n      iv. The earth goes round --------------------------------\n3] From the passage find the opposites of :\n\n     i. forget x ……….................            ii. dim x …………..................\n\n      iii. open x …………................         iv. rise x ……….....................','','','i) False             ii) True              iii) False                iv) True\n2] i) big and bright                            ii) heat and light\n     iii) it is close to the earth           iv) the sun\n3] i) remember              ii) bright\n     iii) close                   iv) set','',0,0,0,NULL),(45987,521,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Name the following\n\n     i. The third planet from the sun ------------------------------\n\n     ii. The earth moves round ----------------------------------\n\n     iii. An envelope around the earth - ----------------------------------\n\n     iv. Time taken by the earth to go round the sun --------