ind the English names of other two plantes.\n \n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Use the correct word from the bracket and complete the sentences. \n       [at, from round, with]\n\n     i. The ground was covered ……....................... green grass.\n\n     ii. The earth goes …….........................…. the sun.\n\n     iii. The king looked …..........................…… the beggar.\n\n     iv. The earth is the third plant........................... the sun.','','Plants, animals and human beings.\n2] Saturn, Mars, Jupiter, Mercury, Venus, Uranus, Neptune\n3] i) with          ii) round           iii) at           iv) from ','',1,0,0,NULL),(45989,521,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Name the following\n\n     i. Our closest neighbour …………….....................\n\n     ii. The number of astronauts landed on the moon ……….............................\n\n     iii. The moon moves round the …………....................................\n\n     iv. ……....................….. wrote stories and poems about the moon.\nHello, people on earth!\nI am your closest neighbour.\nI go round and round the earth. \nFrom the earth, you always see only one side of me.\nYou never see the other side or the other half of me.\nPeople on earth love me.\nThey write stories and poems about me.\nThey send spacecrafts to study me from close. \nDo you know, two astronauts from earth landed on me once. \nMaybe, some day, other people on earth will also come to visit me. \nI am the moon.\n2] Write four sentences about the moon.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out numbers from the passage.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) the moon             ii) two            iii) the earth          iv) people on the earth\n2] The moon is our closest neighbour. It moves around the earth. We can see only one side of the moon. We love the moon.\n3] One, half, two, once','',0,0,0,NULL),(45990,522,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make meaningful sentences by the given diagram','\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------','','I always eat vegetables.\n2) I never tease animals.\n3) He always quarrels with my friends.\n4) She never wastes food.\n5) I always drink enough water.','',1,0,0,NULL),(45991,522,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make meaningful sentences by the given diagram','\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------','','I always wash my hands.\n2) She always tells lies.\n3) He never wastes food.\n4) They never tease animals.\n5) I never use plastics bags.','',1,0,0,NULL),(45992,523,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n     i. The speaker in the poem is …………........................…\n\n     ii. If we clean the book, it will look …………................................\n\n     iii. A book will be our friend if …………..........................................\n\n     iv. A book won\'t talk to us if ……………........................................\nWhen you drop me on the floor \nI get stepped on - my sides are sore;\nTorn-out pages make me groan; \nI feel dizzy if I\'m thrown;\nEvery mark and every stain \nOn my covers gives me pain;\nPlease don\'t bend me, if you do \nI don\'t want to talk to you;\nBut we will both be friends together, \nIf you protect me from the weather\nAnd keep me clean so that I look \nA tidy, neat and happy book.\n2] Say what happens when :-\n     a) You drop a book on the floor –\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) You tear out pages from a book -\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write down two pairs of the rhyming words.\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) a book,         ii) tidy, neat and happy\n     iii) we protect it from the weather              iv) we bend it\n2] a) The boo gets stepped on, and its sides are sore.\n     b) It makes the book again.\n3] a) floor = sore          b) groan = thrown.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45993,523,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.\n','When you drop me on the floor \nI get stepped on - my sides are sore;\nTorn-out pages make me groan; \nI feel dizzy if I\'m thrown;\nEvery mark and every stain \nOn my covers gives me pain;\nPlease don\'t bend me, if you do \nI don\'t want to talk to you;\nBut we will both be friends together, \nIf you protect me from the weather\nAnd keep me clean so that I look \nA tidy, neat and happy book.\n2] Say what happens when :-\n     a) You put marks and stains on the book’s cover – \n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) You throw a book – \n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write full forms of :\n\n     i. I’m : ---------------               ii. don’t  : ---------------------','','] (i – c), (ii – a), (iii – d), (iv – b)\n2] a) It gives the book pain\n     b) A book feels dizzy\n3] i) I am            ii) do not','',1,0,0,NULL),(45994,523,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n     i. The speaker in the poem is ……………...........................\n\n     ii. If we clean the book, it will look …………..................................\n\n     iii. A book will be our friend if …………...........................................\n\n     iv. A book won\'t talk to us if ……………........................................\nWhen you drop me on the floor \nI get stepped on - my sides are sore;\nTorn-out pages make me groan; \nI feel dizzy if I\'m thrown;\nEvery mark and every stain \nOn my covers gives me pain;\nPlease don\'t bend me, if you do \nI don\'t want to talk to you;\nBut we will both be friends together, \nIf you protect me from the weather\nAnd keep me clean so that I look \nA tidy, neat and happy book.\n2] Say what happens when :-\n     a) You drop a book on the floor –\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) You tear out pages from a book -\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write down two pairs of the rhyming words.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) a book,         ii) tidy, neat and happy\n     iii) we protect it from the weather              iv) we bend it\n2] a) The boo gets stepped on, and its sides are sore.\n     b) It makes the book again.\n3] a) floor = sore          b) groan = thrown.','',0,0,0,NULL),(45995,523,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.\n','When you drop me on the floor \nI get stepped on - my sides are sore;\nTorn-out pages make me groan; \nI feel dizzy if I\'m thrown;\nEvery mark and every stain \nOn my covers gives me pain;\nPlease don\'t bend me, if you do \nI don\'t want to talk to you;\nBut we will both be friends together, \nIf you protect me from the weather\nAnd keep me clean so that I look \nA tidy, neat and happy book.\n2] Say what happens when :-\n     a) You put marks and stains on the book’s cover – \n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) You throw a book – \n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write full forms of :\n\n     i. I’m : ----------------               ii. don’t  : -----------------------------','','(i – c), (ii – a), (iii – d), (iv – b)\n2] a) It gives the book pain\n     b) A book feels dizzy\n3] i) I am            ii) do not','',1,0,0,NULL),(45996,524,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the sentences\n\n    i. Carver lost his parents when --------------------------\n\n    ii. Carver was born in ----------------------------------\n\n    iii. George Washington Carver was ------------------------------\n\n    iv. Carver did not get admission in schools because -----------------------------\nGeorge Washington Carver was born in 1861. His parents were slaves. His family worked on a farm in America. He lost both his parents when he was still a baby.\nCarver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted, what was wrong with them. Soon, people around the farm began to call the young boy \'plant doctor\'.\nYoung Carver wanted to go to school and then college. But he was a Black boy. Many schools and colleges in those days did not take Black students. Carver did not lose heart. He went from place to place. He found a college where he could study. He studied hard and became a scientist.\n2] From the passage find out the words that mean.\n\n     i. Father and mother : -----------------          ii. liked : -------------------------\n\n     iii. become nervous : -------------            iv. field : -----------------------------\n3] Find out the past tense forms of :-\n\n      i. lose : ----------------               ii. go : ------------------------','','','i) When he was still a boy             ii) 1861\n     iii) a scientist                                   iv) he was a black boy.\n2] i) parents,           ii) loved,             iii) lose heart,                iv) farm.\n3] i) lost               ii) went','',0,0,0,NULL),(45997,524,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities \n1] Why did people begin to call Carver a plant doctor?\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeorge Washington Carver was born in 1861. His parents were slaves. His family worked on a farm in America. He lost both his parents when he was still a baby.\nCarver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted, what was wrong with them. Soon, people around the farm began to call the young boy \'plant doctor\'.\nYoung Carver wanted to go to school and then college. But he was a Black boy. Many schools and colleges in those days did not take Black students. Carver did not lose heart. He went from place to place. He found a college where he could study. He studied hard and became a scientist.\n2] a) Who was George Washington Carver?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) When was George Washington Carver born?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out the past tense forms of :-\n\n      i. understand : ---------------             ii. begin : ---------------------','','','Carver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted and what in was wrong with them. So people began to call him a plant doctor.\n2] a) A black American scientists \n     b) He was born in 1861\n3] i) understood          ii) began','',0,0,0,NULL),(45998,524,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities\n1] Complete the following Web.\n','\n      --------------------------------              -------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------             ------------------------------------\nCarver wanted to help the poor farmers who had small farms in Alabama. He showed them how to get good crops. These farmers grew plants like cotton because there was profit in it. But cotton made the soil poor. Carver told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made the soil rich again. The farmers got very good crops. In fact, the crops were so good that the farmers did not know what to do with so much of them!\nAgain, Carver went to work. He began to look for new uses for these plants. Can you imagine how many uses he found? From peanuts, he made sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, paper, tiles, butter, plastic, milk, as many as 300 things! From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things. Soon, these crops were in great demand and the farmers were happy.\nThis great scientist died in 1943. The place where he worked is now turned into a \'museum\'.\n2] From the passage, find the opposites of :\n\n     i. bitter : ---------------             ii. loss : -------------------\n\n     iii. old : -------------               iv. rich : -------------------------\n3] Find out the words related to agriculture.\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, milk\n2] i) sweet,             ii) profit,          iii) new         iv) poor\n3] farmer, crops, plants, soil, peanut, bean','',1,0,0,NULL),(45999,524,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities\n1] How did Carver help farmers? \n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------\nCarver wanted to help the poor farmers who had small farms in Alabama. He showed them how to get good crops. These farmers grew plants like cotton because there was profit in it. But cotton made the soil poor. Carver told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made the soil rich again. The farmers got very good crops. In fact, the crops were so good that the farmers did not know what to do with so much of them!\nAgain, Carver went to work. He began to look for new uses for these plants. Can you imagine how many uses he found? From peanuts, he made sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, paper, tiles, butter, plastic, milk, as many as 300 things! From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things. Soon, these crops were in great demand and the farmers were happy.\nThis great scientist died in 1943. The place where he worked is now turned into a \'museum\'.\n2] a) What did Carver love?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) What did Carver find? \n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] How many things he made sweet potato?\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Carver showed the farmers how to get good crops. He told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made soil rich again and the farmers get very good crops.\n2] a) Carver loved plants.\n     b) He found new uses of the ‘useless things’\n3] From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46000,524,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the sentences\n\n    i. Carver lost his parents when ----------------------\n\n    ii. Carver was born in -------------------------------\n\n    iii. George Washington Carver was ---------------------------------\n\n    iv. Carver did not get admission in schools because -----------------------------\nGeorge Washington Carver was born in 1861. His parents were slaves. His family worked on a farm in America. He lost both his parents when he was still a baby.\nCarver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted, what was wrong with them. Soon, people around the farm began to call the young boy \'plant doctor\'.\nYoung Carver wanted to go to school and then college. But he was a Black boy. Many schools and colleges in those days did not take Black students. Carver did not lose heart. He went from place to place. He found a college where he could study. He studied hard and became a scientist.\n2] From the passage find out the words that mean.\n\n     i. Father and mother : -----------------          ii. liked : ---------------------\n\n     iii. become nervous  : -------------------           iv. field : ----------------------------\n3] Find out the past tense forms of :-\n\n      i. lose : ------------------               ii. go : ------------------------','','','i) When he was still a boy             ii) 1861\n     iii) a scientist                                   iv) he was a black boy.\n2] i) parents,           ii) loved,             iii) lose heart,                iv) farm.\n3] i) lost               ii) went','',0,0,0,NULL),(46001,524,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities \n1] Why did people begin to call Carver a plant doctor?\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeorge Washington Carver was born in 1861. His parents were slaves. His family worked on a farm in America. He lost both his parents when he was still a baby.\nCarver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted, what was wrong with them. Soon, people around the farm began to call the young boy \'plant doctor\'.\nYoung Carver wanted to go to school and then college. But he was a Black boy. Many schools and colleges in those days did not take Black students. Carver did not lose heart. He went from place to place. He found a college where he could study. He studied hard and became a scientist.\n2] a) Who was George Washington Carver?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) When was George Washington Carver born?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out the past tense forms of :-\n\n      i. understand : --------------------             ii. begin : -------------------','','','Carver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted and what in was wrong with them. So people began to call him a plant doctor.\n2] a) A black American scientists \n     b) He was born in 1861\n3] i) understood          ii) began','',0,0,0,NULL),(46002,524,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities\n1] Complete the following Web.\n','\n   -----------------------------               ----------------------------------------\n\n    -----------------------------               ----------------------------------------\nCarver wanted to help the poor farmers who had small farms in Alabama. He showed them how to get good crops. These farmers grew plants like cotton because there was profit in it. But cotton made the soil poor. Carver told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made the soil rich again. The farmers got very good crops. In fact, the crops were so good that the farmers did not know what to do with so much of them!\nAgain, Carver went to work. He began to look for new uses for these plants. Can you imagine how many uses he found? From peanuts, he made sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, paper, tiles, butter, plastic, milk, as many as 300 things! From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things. Soon, these crops were in great demand and the farmers were happy.\nThis great scientist died in 1943. The place where he worked is now turned into a \'museum\'.\n2] From the passage, find the opposites of :\n\n     i. bitter : -----------------              ii. loss : --------------------\n\n     iii. old : ---------------               iv. rich : --------------------------\n3] Find out the words related to agriculture.\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, milk\n2] i) sweet,             ii) profit,          iii) new         iv) poor\n3] farmer, crops, plants, soil, peanut, bean','',1,0,0,NULL),(46003,524,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities\n1] How did Carver help farmers? \n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------\nCarver wanted to help the poor farmers who had small farms in Alabama. He showed them how to get good crops. These farmers grew plants like cotton because there was profit in it. But cotton made the soil poor. Carver told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made the soil rich again. The farmers got very good crops. In fact, the crops were so good that the farmers did not know what to do with so much of them!\nAgain, Carver went to work. He began to look for new uses for these plants. Can you imagine how many uses he found? From peanuts, he made sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, paper, tiles, butter, plastic, milk, as many as 300 things! From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things. Soon, these crops were in great demand and the farmers were happy.\nThis great scientist died in 1943. The place where he worked is now turned into a \'museum\'.\n2] a) What did Carver love?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) What did Carver find? \n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] How many things he made sweet potato?\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Carver showed the farmers how to get good crops. He told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made soil rich again and the farmers get very good crops.\n2] a) Carver loved plants.\n     b) He found new uses of the ‘useless things’\n3] From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46004,525,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame at least 5 to 6 questions.\n        How does / do...........?             What do / does ……….?\n        When do/does …….?               Who ……..?\n        Why do / does / did ……. ?      Where do / does ………?  \n\n  --------------------------------                    -------------------------------------\n\n   --------------------------------                    -------------------------------------\n\n   ----------------------------------                  -----------------------------------','','','How does the rain fall?\n2) How do you go to school?\n3) What do you do?\n4) What does that mean?\n5) When do you get up?\n6) When does your vacation start?','',0,0,0,NULL),(46005,525,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame at least 5 to 6 questions.\n        How does / do...........?             What do / does ……….?\n        When do/does …….?               Who ……..?\n        Why do / does / did ……. ?      Where do / does ………?  \n\n --------------------------------                    -------------------------------------\n\n   --------------------------------                    -------------------------------------\n\n   ----------------------------------                  -----------------------------------','','','Who are you?\n2) Why do you go there?\n3) Why does the sun shine? \n4) Why did she come?\n5) Where do you live?\n6) Where does he come from?','',0,0,0,NULL),(46006,525,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame at least 5 to 6 questions.\n        How does / do...........?             What do / does ……….?\n        When do/does …….?               Who ……..?\n        Why do / does / did ……. ?      Where do / does ………?  \n\n      --------------------------------                    -------------------------------------\n\n   --------------------------------                    -------------------------------------\n\n   ----------------------------------                  -----------------------------------','','','How does the rain fall?\n2) How do you go to school?\n3) What do you do?\n4) What does that mean?\n5) When do you get up?\n6) When does your vacation start?','',0,0,0,NULL),(46007,525,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame at least 5 to 6 questions.\n        How does / do...........?             What do / does ……….?\n        When do/does …….?               Who ……..?\n        Why do / does / did ……. ?      Where do / does ………?  \n\n      --------------------------------                    -------------------------------------\n\n   --------------------------------                    -------------------------------------\n\n   ----------------------------------                  -----------------------------------','','','Who are you?\n2) Why do you go there?\n3) Why does the sun shine? \n4) Why did she come?\n5) Where do you live?\n6) Where does he come from?','',0,0,0,NULL),(46008,527,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web\n','\n     ---------------------------------             --------------------------------------------\nWe all need money to buy things and to pay people who work for us.\nBut what is \'money\'?\nWho invented \'money\'?\nToday, we see money in the form of coins and notes.\nThese are the coins and notes we use in India.\nBut different countries use different coins and notes.\nTheir money is known by different names.\nIn the United States, they use $ dollars. \nIn England, they use £ pounds.\n2] Match the following \n      Sign              Currency\n      i) ₹                  a) Pound\n      ii) $                 b) Rupee\n      iii) £                c) dollar \n3] Complete the following :\n\n     We need money to -----------------------\n\n     i. ------------------------             ii. ---------------------------------','','coins, notes\n2) (i – b), (ii – c), (iii – a)\n3) i) to buy things          ii) to pay people who work for us.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46009,527,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nBut long, long ago, people did not use coins and notes. They exchanged one thing for another. This was known as \'barter\'.\nIt may seem unbelievable, but long ago people even used special stones as money! Imagine how difficult it must have been to carry this money!\nPeople also used bricks of salt, shells and cowries as money in the past.\nThey gave grain, farm produce, cattle, goats, chickens, etc. in return for things like clothes, tools and implements or as wages.\nThen they began to use bits of precious metals like gold, silver and copper as money. It was easier to carry this money around.\nPaper money was first used in China about 1000 years ago.\n1) In olden days which system was used?\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) What is a barter system?\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) What things were used as money?\n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------\n4) Who first invented paper money?\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Which three metals were used as money?\n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Barter system was used.\n2) A barter system means exchanging one things for another.\n3) Stones, salt, shells, couries, metal.\n4) China first invented paper money.\n5) Gold, Silver and Copper.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46010,527,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web\n','\n   -----------------------------              --------------------------------------\nWe all need money to buy things and to pay people who work for us.\nBut what is \'money\'?\nWho invented \'money\'?\nToday, we see money in the form of coins and notes.\nThese are the coins and notes we use in India.\nBut different countries use different coins and notes.\nTheir money is known by different names.\nIn the United States, they use $ dollars. \nIn England, they use £ pounds.\n2] Match the following \n      Sign              Currency\n      i) ₹                  a) Pound\n      ii) $                 b) Rupee\n      iii) £                c) dollar \n3] Complete the following :\n\n     We need money to -----------------------------\n\n     i. ----------------------------             ii. -----------------------------------','','coins, notes\n2) (i – b), (ii – c), (iii – a)\n3) i) to buy things          ii) to pay people who work for us.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46011,527,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nBut long, long ago, people did not use coins and notes. They exchanged one thing for another. This was known as \'barter\'.\nIt may seem unbelievable, but long ago people even used special stones as money! Imagine how difficult it must have been to carry this money!\nPeople also used bricks of salt, shells and cowries as money in the past.\nThey gave grain, farm produce, cattle, goats, chickens, etc. in return for things like clothes, tools and implements or as wages.\nThen they began to use bits of precious metals like gold, silver and copper as money. It was easier to carry this money around.\nPaper money was first used in China about 1000 years ago.\n1) In olden days which system was used?\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) What is a barter system?\n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) What things were used as money?\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------\n4) Who first invented paper money?\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Which three metals were used as money?\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Barter system was used.\n2) A barter system means exchanging one things for another.\n3) Stones, salt, shells, couries, metal.\n4) China first invented paper money.\n5) Gold, Silver and Copper.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46012,528,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n     i. There are hundreds of butterflies ……….......................\n\n     ii. Hundreds of birds …………....................................\n\n     iii. There are hundreds of bees ………........................................\n\n     iv. There are hundreds of lambs ……..................................\nHundreds of stars in the pretty sky,\nHundreds of shells on the shore together,\nHundreds of birds that go singing by,\nHundreds of lambs in the sunny weather.\nHundreds of dewdrops to greet the dawn,\nHundreds of bees in the purple clover,\nHundreds of butterflies on the lawn,\nBut only one mother the wide world over.\n2] Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.','3] From the extract find out the pairs of rhyming words.  \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','i) on the lawn                           ii) go singing by.\n     iii) in the purple clover           iv) in the sunny weather.\n2) (i – d), (ii – c), (iii – b), (iv – a)\n3) i) sky – by                      ii) bees – butterflies \n     iii) lawn – dawn           iv) clover – over  ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46013,528,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n     i. There are hundreds of butterflies ………..........................\n\n     ii. Hundreds of birds …………..........................................\n\n     iii. There are hundreds of bees ………...........................................\n\n     iv. There are hundreds of lambs ……..................................\n\nHundreds of stars in the pretty sky,\nHundreds of shells on the shore together,\nHundreds of birds that go singing by,\nHundreds of lambs in the sunny weather.\nHundreds of dewdrops to greet the dawn,\nHundreds of bees in the purple clover,\nHundreds of butterflies on the lawn,\nBut only one mother the wide world over.\n2] Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.','3] From the extract find out the pairs of rhyming words.  \n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------','','      i) on the lawn                           ii) go singing by.\n     iii) in the purple clover              iv) in the sunny weather.\n2) (i – d), (ii – c), (iii – b), (iv – a)\n3) i) sky – by                      ii) bees – butterflies \n     iii) lawn – dawn           iv) clover – over  ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46014,530,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences :\n\n     i. A new temple was being but built …………….......................................\n\n     ii. They tried to make the temple …………............................................\n\n     iii. Jaikishan was ………………...........................................\n\n      iv. Jaikishan carved ……...............................….. in stone.\nAt the time of King Ashoka, a new temple was being built in the capital city. All the people in the capital helped to build the temple. They tried their best to make it strong and beautiful.\nRich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones, and cut the timber. Everyone helped to build the great temple.\nOne day, an old man came to offer his help. He was Jaikishan, the stone carver. He carved beautiful figures and designs in stone. Now he was old, and his hands shook a little. Yet, he was a good workman. He took great care with his work.\n2] a) Which King’s name is there in this passage?\n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) What did Ashoka want to build?\n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Correct the following sentence. \n       Reach people gave there money.\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) in the capital city                  ii) strong and beautiful.\n     iii) the stone carver                 iv) beautiful figures and designs.\n2) a) Ashoka                b) A temple\n3) Rich people gave their money.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46015,530,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] How did the people in the capital help to build the temple?\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------\nAt the time of King Ashoka, a new temple was being built in the capital city. All the people in the capital helped to build the temple. They tried their best to make it strong and beautiful.\nRich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones, and cut the timber. Everyone helped to build the great temple.\nOne day, an old man came to offer his help. He was Jaikishan, the stone carver. He carved beautiful figures and designs in stone. Now he was old, and his hands shook a little. Yet, he was a good workman. He took great care with his work.\n2] a) What did the rich people give?\n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) What did the workmen give?\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] From the passage find out the words that describe the temple.\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Everyone in the capital helped to build the great temple. Rich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones and cut the timber.\n2) a) Money               b) Best work\n3] new, strong beautiful, great.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46016,530,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n\n      i. Jaikishan was told to carve a figure of devadoota. : ---------------------\n\n      ii. People could see the devadoota clearly. : --------------------------\n\n      iii. Jaikishan carved only the face of the devadoota. : ------------------------------\n\n       iv. The man smiled to see Jaikishan working. : ----------------------------\nJaikishan was told to carve the figure of a devadoota in a corner. This corner of the temple was nearly always dark. Here, no one would ever see the devadoota very clearly. People would only see the face of the devadoota clearly.\nJaikishan made a devadoota with a beautiful face. \"But this is not enough,\" he said. He went on carving. He carved the entire devadoota carefully. He carved very well. The garments of the devadoota were carved in stone, but they looked thin and soft.\nOne day a man came to that Scorner. He saw Jaikishan carving the/ devadoota. He saw how beautiful the carving looked. He saw how carefully and lovingly Jaikishan worked. The man smiled. He asked Jaikishan, \"Why do you take such trouble with the garments? No one will see your work in this dark corner. No one will know who did it.\"\n\"That may be,\" said old Jaikishan, \"But God can see my work even in - this dark corner and he knows the workman, too.\"\n2] How did Jaikishan answer the man\'s questions?\n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] What did Jaikishan carve?\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) True             ii) False            iii) False              iv) True\n2) Jaikishan answered that God could see his work even in the dark corner. God knew the workman too.\n3) Devadoota','',0,0,0,NULL),(46017,530,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Match the following \n','Jaikishan was told to carve the figure of a devadoota in a corner. This corner of the temple was nearly always dark. Here, no one would ever see the devadoota very clearly. People would only see the face of the devadoota clearly.\nJaikishan made a devadoota with a beautiful face. \"But this is not enough,\" he said. He went on carving. He carved the entire devadoota carefully. He carved very well. The garments of the devadoota were carved in stone, but they looked thin and soft.\nOne day a man came to that Scorner. He saw Jaikishan carving the/ devadoota. He saw how beautiful the carving looked. He saw how carefully and lovingly Jaikishan worked. The man smiled. He asked Jaikishan, \"Why do you take such trouble with the garments? No one will see your work in this dark corner. No one will know who did it.\"\n\"That may be,\" said old Jaikishan, \"But God can see my work even in - this dark corner and he knows the workman, too.\"\n2] a) Who is the carver in this passage?\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------\n    b) Who can see Jaikishan’s work?\n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out the words that end with - ly -\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------','','(i – c), (ii – d), (iii – b), (iv – a)\n2) a) Jaikishan                b) God\n3) nearly, clearly, carefully lovingly.  ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46018,530,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences :\n\n     i. A new temple was being but built …………….....................\n\n     ii. They tried to make the temple …………..................................\n\n     iii. Jaikishan was ……………….......................................\n\n      iv. Jaikishan carved ……..........................….. in stone.\nAt the time of King Ashoka, a new temple was being built in the capital city. All the people in the capital helped to build the temple. They tried their best to make it strong and beautiful.\nRich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones, and cut the timber. Everyone helped to build the great temple.\nOne day, an old man came to offer his help. He was Jaikishan, the stone carver. He carved beautiful figures and designs in stone. Now he was old, and his hands shook a little. Yet, he was a good workman. He took great care with his work.\n2] a) Which King’s name is there in this passage?\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) What did Ashoka want to build?\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Correct the following sentence. \n       Reach people gave there money.\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) in the capital city                  ii) strong and beautiful.\n     iii) the stone carver                 iv) beautiful figures and designs.\n2) a) Ashoka                b) A temple\n3) Rich people gave their money.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46019,530,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] How did the people in the capital help to build the temple?\n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------\nAt the time of King Ashoka, a new temple was being built in the capital city. All the people in the capital helped to build the temple. They tried their best to make it strong and beautiful.\nRich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones, and cut the timber. Everyone helped to build the great temple.\nOne day, an old man came to offer his help. He was Jaikishan, the stone carver. He carved beautiful figures and designs in stone. Now he was old, and his hands shook a little. Yet, he was a good workman. He took great care with his work.\n2] a) What did the rich people give?\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------\n     b) What did the workmen give?\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] From the passage find out the words that describe the temple.\n\n     -------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Everyone in the capital helped to build the great temple. Rich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones and cut the timber.\n2) a) Money               b) Best work\n3] new, strong beautiful, great.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46020,530,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are True or False :\n\n      i. Jaikishan was told to carve a figure of devadoota. : -------------------------\n\n      ii. People could see the devadoota clearly. : --------------------------\n\n      iii. Jaikishan carved only the face of the devadoota. : --------------------------\n\n       iv. The man smiled to see Jaikishan working. : -----------------------------\nJaikishan was told to carve the figure of a devadoota in a corner. This corner of the temple was nearly always dark. Here, no one would ever see the devadoota very clearly. People would only see the face of the devadoota clearly.\nJaikishan made a devadoota with a beautiful face. \"But this is not enough,\" he said. He went on carving. He carved the entire devadoota carefully. He carved very well. The garments of the devadoota were carved in stone, but they looked thin and soft.\nOne day a man came to that Scorner. He saw Jaikishan carving the/ devadoota. He saw how beautiful the carving looked. He saw how carefully and lovingly Jaikishan worked. The man smiled. He asked Jaikishan, \"Why do you take such trouble with the garments? No one will see your work in this dark corner. No one will know who did it.\"\n\"That may be,\" said old Jaikishan, \"But God can see my work even in - this dark corner and he knows the workman, too.\"\n2] How did Jaikishan answer the man\'s questions?\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] What did Jaikishan carve?\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) True             ii) False            iii) False              iv) True\n2) Jaikishan answered that God could see his work even in the dark corner. God knew the workman too.\n3) Devadoota','',0,0,0,NULL),(46021,530,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Match the following \n','Jaikishan was told to carve the figure of a devadoota in a corner. This corner of the temple was nearly always dark. Here, no one would ever see the devadoota very clearly. People would only see the face of the devadoota clearly.\nJaikishan made a devadoota with a beautiful face. \"But this is not enough,\" he said. He went on carving. He carved the entire devadoota carefully. He carved very well. The garments of the devadoota were carved in stone, but they looked thin and soft.\nOne day a man came to that Scorner. He saw Jaikishan carving the/ devadoota. He saw how beautiful the carving looked. He saw how carefully and lovingly Jaikishan worked. The man smiled. He asked Jaikishan, \"Why do you take such trouble with the garments? No one will see your work in this dark corner. No one will know who did it.\"\n\"That may be,\" said old Jaikishan, \"But God can see my work even in - this dark corner and he knows the workman, too.\"\n2] a) Who is the carver in this passage?\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------\n    b) Who can see Jaikishan’s work?\n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out the words that end with - ly -\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------','','(i – c), (ii – d), (iii – b), (iv – a)\n2) a) Jaikishan                b) God\n3) nearly, clearly, carefully lovingly.  ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46059,446,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities.\n1) Complete the following sentences.\n     i. Mother asks her baby to ----------------------------\n\n     ii. Pretty dreams fall from --------------------------------\n\n     iii. The stars are ---------------------------------------------\n\n     iv. The moon is ---------------------------------------------\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nYour father herds his sheep: \nYour mother shakes the little tree \nFrom which fall pretty dreams on thee; \nSleep, baby, sleep !\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nThe heavens are white with sheep : \nFor they are lambs- those stars so bright: \nAnd the moon\'s shepherd of the night\n2) Complete the following table :','i) --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ii) ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Find out four adjectives from the extract.\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) sleep           ii) the little tree         iii) very bright         iv) the shepherd of the night\n2) i) Father : herds his sleep\n     ii) Mother – shakes the little tree\n3) little, pretty, white, bright','',1,0,0,NULL),(46060,446,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities.\n1) A) Complete the sentences. ------------ 1M\n\n     i. If the baby doesn’t sleep --------------------------------------------\n   B] Have you noticed the changed mood of the mother in the stanza?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \nSleep, baby, sleep! \nAnd bleat not like a sheep, \nOr else the shepherd\'s angry dog\nWill come and bite my naughty rogue; \nSleep, baby, sleep!\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nGo out and herd, the sheep,\nGo out, you barking black dog, go\nAnd waken not my baby so;\nSleep, baby, sleep!\n2) Complete the following table.','i) -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nii) --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Write down to pairs of rhyming words.\n \n     -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A] the shepherds angry dog will bite it.\n     B] Yes, the mother’s changed mood is clearly visible. Earlier the mother warns the baby that the dog will bite it. But here she orders the dog to go out and let the baby sleep quietly.\n2) i) Mother asks the baby to go out and herd the sheep.\n    ii) She asks the dog not to waken her baby.\n3) i) sleep – sheep          ii) go – so ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46061,446,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities.\n1) Complete the following sentences.\n     i. Mother asks her baby to ----------------------------\n\n     ii. Pretty dreams fall from --------------------------------\n\n     iii. The stars are ---------------------------------------------\n\n     iv. The moon is ---------------------------------------------\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nYour father herds his sheep: \nYour mother shakes the little tree \nFrom which fall pretty dreams on thee; \nSleep, baby, sleep !\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nThe heavens are white with sheep : \nFor they are lambs- those stars so bright: \nAnd the moon\'s shepherd of the night\n2) Complete the following table :','i) --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ii) ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Find out four adjectives from the extract.\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) sleep           ii) the little tree         iii) very bright         iv) the shepherd of the night\n2) i) Father : herds his sleep\n     ii) Mother – shakes the little tree\n3) little, pretty, white, bright','',1,0,0,NULL),(46062,446,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities.\n1) A) Complete the sentences. ------------ 1M\n\n     i. If the baby doesn’t sleep --------------------------------------------\n   B] Have you noticed the changed mood of the mother in the stanza?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \nSleep, baby, sleep! \nAnd bleat not like a sheep, \nOr else the shepherd\'s angry dog\nWill come and bite my naughty rogue; \nSleep, baby, sleep!\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nGo out and herd, the sheep,\nGo out, you barking black dog, go\nAnd waken not my baby so;\nSleep, baby, sleep!\n2) Complete the following table.','i) -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nii) --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Write down to pairs of rhyming words.\n \n     -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A] the shepherds angry dog will bite it.\n     B] Yes, the mother’s changed mood is clearly visible. Earlier the mother warns the baby that the dog will bite it. But here she orders the dog to go out and let the baby sleep quietly.\n2) i) Mother asks the baby to go out and herd the sheep.\n    ii) She asks the dog not to waken her baby.\n3) i) sleep – sheep          ii) go – so ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46063,447,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n       i. Chaturbhuj is talking about -----------------------------------------------------\n\n       ii. Nilratan is talking about -------------------------------------------------------\n(A village road. Chaturbhuj Babu has come back to the village after passing his MA examinations, hoping that everyone will make a great fuss over him. There is a plump Afghan cat with him. Enter Nilratan.) \nNilratan : : Hello there, Chatur Babu. When did you arrive?\nChaturbhuj : Directly after the MA exams were over. I - \nNilratan : Ah, you\'ve got a fine cat there! \nChaturbhuj : This year the exams were very – \nNilratan : Tell me, where did you find that cat?\nChaturbhuj : Bought it. The subjects I\'d offered \nNilratan : How much did you pay for it?\nChaturbhuj : I don\'t remember. Nilratan Babu, has anybody passed any exams in our village?\nNilratan : Oh, lots of them. But you won\'t see a cat like that in these parts.  \nChaturbhuj : (to himself) Confound him, he can\'t talk about anything but the cat! It doesn\'t seem to matter that I\'ve just passed my MA!\nA2] Why is Chaturbhuj angry with Nilratan?\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA3] a) Find out the words that mean : \n\n       i) come : ------------------------------------        \n\n       ii) many : --------------------------------------\n       b) Find out opposites of : \n\n        i) forget : ----------------------        ii) failed : -----------------------\nA4] Do as directed\n        i. I don\'t remember. (Make it Affirmative)\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n        ii. He can\'t talk about anything but the cat (Make Affirmative)\n\n           ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] Write 3-4-sentences about what you think about the temper of Nilratan - the villager?\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A1] i) his M.A. examination          ii) the car\nA2] Chatubhuj is angry with Nilratan because he doesn\'t talk about anything but the cat. He doesn\'t seem to matter that Chatubhuj have passed his M.A. examination.\nA3] a) i) arrive           ii) lots of\n       b) i) remember         ii) passed\nA4] i) I forget\n    ii) He can talk only about the cat\nA5] I think that Nilratan is illiterate. He is not interested in the exam that Chaturbhuj has passed. On the contrary he is interested in the fine cat that Chaturbhuj has.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46064,447,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Say whether the following sentences are true or false : \n\n     i. Chaturbhuj was angry with all. : ------------------------------\n\n     ii. Chaturbhuj decided to leave at once. : ---------------------------------------\n\n     iii. Mother cared more for the cat than for Chaturbhuj : ------------------------------\n \n      iv. Mother asked Chaturbhuj to bathe first. : ----------------------------\n(Inside Chaturbhuj\'s house.)\nMaid : Mother, Dada Babu has come home in a blazing temper.\nMother : Why, what\'s the Why, what\'s the matter \nMaid : I don\'t know (Enter Chaturbhuj.)\nLittle Boy : Dada, can I have this cat?\nChaturbhuj : (slaps him hard.) Cat, cat, cat the whole day long, is it?\nMother : No wonder the poor boy\'s angry! He comes home after such a long time and these brats start annoying him at once. (To Chaturbhuj) Let me have the cat, son. I\'ll give it some rice and milk that I\'ve put by.\nChaturbhuj  : (furiously) Here, mother, you can have the cat and feed it all\nyou want. I shan\'t stop to eat - I\'m leaving at once.\nMother : (plaintively) What makes you say such a thing? - Your meal\'s ready and waiting, dear. You can sit down to it as soon as you\'ve had your bath!\nA2] Was Chaturbhuj was angry with mother too why?\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA3] Find out words that mean :\n\n       i. naughty kids : -------------------             ii. making angry : ------------------------\n\n       iii. angrily : -------------------                     iv. in a sad way : --------------------------\nA4] Punctuate the following.\n     mother said what makes you say such a thing.\n\n       -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] Do you think that Chaturbhuj\'s mother cared more for the cat than for her son? \n\n       --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A1] i) True         ii) Ture            iii) False          iv) True\nA2] Yes, Chaturbhuj was angry with mother too. Because she cared more for the cat and not for Chaturbhuj. She offered some rice and milk to cat. But she did not ask Chaturbhuj about his meal.\nA3] i) brats            ii) annoying           iii) furiously         iv) plaintively \nA4] Mother said, \" What makes you say such a thing?\"\nA5] No, I don\'t think so. Mother cared for the cat as well as for Chaturbhuj. She offered some rice and milk to the cat. But before that she had made Chaturbhuj\'s meal ready. And she was waiting for Chaturbhuj to have bath and take his meal. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46065,448,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Complete the following web \n','\n----------------------------------------   --------------------------------------\n\n---------------------------------------    --------------------------------------\nThe famous physics professor Stephen Hawking has warned that mankind is facing the most dangerous time in its history today. He said that the world today is facing huge challenges like climate change, overpopulation, epidemic diseases. \'We have the technology to destroy the planet on which we live and have not yet developed the ability to escape it. Perhaps in a few hundred years from now, we will have built colonies amid the stars, but right now, we have only one planet and we need to work together to protect it.\' Hawking wrote recently in the Guardian newspaper.\nA2] Write a note on Stephen Hawking’s Warning\n\n        i. -------------------------------------------------------------     \n\n        ii. ------------------------------------------------------------   \n\n        iii. -----------------------------------------------------------\nA3] From the passage find out the words that are related to astronomy.\n\n        -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA4] a) Find out the adjective forms of : \n\n         i) danger : -----------------------        ii) fame : ----------------------------\n       b) Find out the past tense of : \n\n         i) write  : --------------------            ii) build : -------------------------------\nA5] What should we do to save our planet?\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A1] i) Climate change          ii) overpopulation        iii) epidemic diseases\nA2] Stephen Hawking has warned us that we are e facing the most dangerous time today. Our technology will destroy our earth And we have not the ability to escape it.\nA3]  i) world         ii) climate         iii) planet          iv) stars\nA4] a) i) dangerous             ii) famous\n       b) i) wrote                      ii) built\nA5] To save our planet we should :\n     i) plant more and more trees.\n     ii) avoid excessive use of natural resources.\n     iii) ban plastic','',1,0,0,NULL),(46066,448,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Name the following\n\n     i. A tiny settlement : -------------------     ii. A quack : -------------------------\n\n     iii. Mountain : --------------------------    iv. Hospital : -------------------------\nHundreds of people flock every hour to Ralewadi, a tiny settlement near Ambegaon. They wait in long winding queues in the hot sun to get a \'special\' powder from Miribaba who claims that it cures all sorts of diseases. According to him, he has developed this powder from a special herb in the Himalayas. \nUnwilling to give the exact name and source of the herb, he only says that it was revealed to him. He offers it at Rs 25 a packet. Each packet is specially \'blessed\' by him. Some of his followers claim that they have seen him euring thousands of people with his powder. However, Dr Karnik of Ambegaon Civil Hospital says that the number of patients in their hospital has in fact increased in the last few weeks.\nA2] Complete the following web ','\n       ----------------------------------------------       -------------------------------------\n\n       ---------------------------------------------         ------------------------------------         \nA3] From the passage find out the opposites of :\n\n     i. cold x ----------------------       ii. decreased x --------------------------\n\n     iii. huge x ---------------------     iv. cursed x -----------------------------\nA4] Frame \'wh questions\' to get ‘Ralewadi’ words by answers.\n     i. Hundered of people flock every hour to Ralewadi\n\n        --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. It cures all sorts of diseases.\n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] What shows that Miribaba\'s claim is unreliable?\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A1] i) Ralewadi              ii) Miribaba\n     iii) Himalaya            iv) Ambegaon Civil Hospital\nA2] Special powder: Miribaba claims that his special powder cures all sort of diseases. He has developed it from a special herb in the Himalayas. He offers it at Rs. 25 a packet.\nA3] i) hot                       ii) increased\n     iii) tiny                     iv) blessed\nA4] i) Where do hunderds of people flock every hour?\n       ii) What does it cure?\nA5] Miribaba claims that his special powder cures all sorts of diseases. As a result of his claim, the number of patients should have decreased. But Dr. Karnik points out the that number of patients has increased. It shows that Miribaba\'s claim is unreliable.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46067,450,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities.\nA1] Say whether the following sentences are true or false.\n\n        i. Sherlock Holmes is narrating the story - ------------------\n\n        ii. Mr. Jabez Wilson was a pawn-broker. - ------------------\n\n        iii. The office of the Red -headed League is on Fleet street. --------------------\n\n        iv. Mr. Wilson liked his assistant. ---------------------\nWhen I called upon my friend, Mr Sherlock Holmes, he was conversing with a visitor, who was an elderly man with fiery red hair.\nHolmes introduced me to the visitor, Mr Jabez Wilson. He was a pawn-broker. Holmes asked him to repeat his story for me. He began by showing us an advertisement in a newspaper. It read as follows-\nTO THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE\nThere is a vacancy for a member of the League, and the salary is four pounds a week for nominal services. Red-headed men may apply in person on Monday, at eleven o\'clock. to Duncan Ross, at the office of the League, 7 Fleet Street.\nThe advertisement had appeared in newspaper two months ago. Mr Wilson\'s assistant, Vincent Spaulding, had shown it to him. Mr Wilson liked his assistant Spaulding. He was smart, efficient and worked for only half the normal wages! But, the assistant also had his faults. Every now and then, he left work and went down into the cellar to develop photographs. Photography was his hobby.\nA2] Complete the following web ','\n       -------------------------------      ----------------------------------------\n\n       -------------------------------      ----------------------------------------\nA3] Match the following words with their meanings \n         Words                              Meanings\n        i. Converse                      a) wages\n        ii. fiery                              b) speak\n        iii. repeat                          c) like fire\n        iv. salary                           d) say again\nA4] Identify the tense of the following sentences,\n     i. He was conversing with a visitor.\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. Vincent Spaulding had shown it to him.\n\n        --------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] There is a vacancy for a teacher in your school Make an advertisement for it.\n\n       --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A1] i) i) False           ii) True            iii) True            iv) True\nA2] Vincent Spaulding was Mr. Wilson\'s assistant. He was smart, efficient. He worked for only half the normal wages. Photography was his hobby.\nA3] (i – b), (ii – c), (iii – d), (iv – a)\nA4] i) Continuous present tense           ii) perfect past tense\nA5] New English school, Pune \n     Wanted Teacher\n      Sub - English\n      Interested candidates may apply to \n       Headmaster, New English School, Pune.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46068,450,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nA1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n     i. The revolver fell down on the floor because -----------------------------\n\n     ii. The man\'s companion had -------------------------------------------\nWe waited silently for more than an hour. It was pitch dark in the cellar. Then suddenly, a point of bright light appeared in the floor of the cellar, then a line, and a gash seemed to open, and a hand appeared. A broad stone turned over upon its side, and left a square hele. A boyish face emerged. \nThe man looked about and came out of the hole. He had a companion with him, a man with very red hair. The pair was none other than Spaulding the assistant alias the criminal Clay and the red-headed Mr Duncan Ross! As soon as they climbed out of the hole, Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. \nThe other dived down the hole and disappeared. Clay took out a revolver. But Holmes hit him on his wrist, and the revolver fell on the floor.\nA2] Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.\n     i. Holmess seized him by the collar\n     ii. A broad stone turned over upon its side.\n     iii. A point of bright light appeared in the floor.\n     iv. A man came out of the hole.\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA3] From the passage find out two adjectives and two adverbs.\n\n      ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ----------------------------------------------------------------------\nA4] Change the following sentence into a question and negative sentence.\n      There was pitch dark in the cellar.\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] Find out the sentences from the passage that show that Holmes was a very brave man. \n\n      --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A1] i) Holmes hit him on his wrist.           ii) very red hair.\nA2] iii – ii – iv – i\nA3] i) Adjectives = dark, bright         ii) Adverbs = silently, suddenly\nA4] i) Question - Was there pitch dark in the cellar?\n    ii) Negative - There was not pitch dark in the cellar.\nA5] Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. Holmes hit him on his wrist and the revolver fell on the floor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46069,451,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following statements are true or false :\n\n     i. A child is called lazy if he wakes up late. : --------------------------------\n\n     ii. When grown – ups say ‘no’ to something, they are called stubborn. : ----------------------------\n\n     iii. The grown-ups wake late because the alarm get crazy. : -----------------------------------\n\n     iv. A child is always right. : -----------------------------\nWhen I wake up late, \nI\'m getting very lazy. \nWhen you wake up late, lazy.\nit\'s the alarm that\'s crazy.\nWhen I say \'No\' to something, \nI\'m being stubborn. \nWhen you say \'No\' to something, \nyou are being firm.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\n2] Write down the double standards about-raising voice.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.\n\n     -------------------------------------   -------------------------------------------','','','i) True            ii) False         iii) True        iv) False\n2) When a child raises his voice, it is considered shocking and rude. On the other hand when a grown-up raises his voice, it is only a teaching to be good.\n3) i) lazy crazy        ii) rude = good','',0,0,0,NULL),(46070,451,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following table.\n','\n --------------------------- -------        ----------------------------------\n\n -----------------------------------       -----------------------------------\nWhen I quarrel with a friend, \nI am wild and naughty. \nWhen you quarrel with a neighbour, \nyou\'re only doing your duty.\nSo, I am always wrong \nand you are always right. \nOh, why do people grow up \nand spoil a child\'s delight?\n2] What blame does the child put on the grow-ups?\n\n    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] From the extract, find out a pair of opposite words.\n\n    ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) a friend                   ii) a neighbour \n2] The child blames that the grown-ups grow up only to spoil a child\'s delight.\n3] wrong x right','',1,0,0,NULL),(46071,451,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] List all the words that the grown – ups in the poem use for the child.\n\n    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\nWhen I break something, \nI am clumsy or careless. \nWhen you break something, \nit slipped; you were helpless.\nWhen I do something wrong, \nI am such a terror \nWhen you do something wrong, \nit\'s a forgivable error.\n2] List all the situations described in the poem. For example, waking up late.\n\n    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Translate the following into your mother tongue.\n\n      i. careless : ----------------------           ii. helpless : -------------------             \n\n      iii. clumsy  : ----------------------            ','','','rude, shocking, clumsy, careless, terror\n2] raising voice, breaking something, do something wrong\n3] i) careless – निष्काळजी              ii) helpless - असहाय्य\n     iii) clumsy - वेंधळा','',0,0,0,NULL),(46072,451,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following statements are true or false :\n     i. A child is called lazy if he wakes up late.\n     ii. When grown – ups say ‘no’ to something, they are called stubborn. \n     iii. The grown-ups wake late because the alarm get crazy.\n     iv. A child is always right.\nWhen I wake up late, \nI\'m getting very lazy. \nWhen you wake up late, lazy.\nit\'s the alarm that\'s crazy.\nWhen I say \'No\' to something, \nI\'m being stubborn. \nWhen you say \'No\' to something, \nyou are being firm.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\n2] Write down the double standards about-raising voice.\n3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.','','','i) True            ii) False         iii) True        iv) False\n2) When a child raises his voice, it is considered shocking and rude. On the other hand when a grown-up raises his voice, it is only a teaching to be good.\n3) i) lazy crazy        ii) rude = good','',0,0,0,NULL),(46073,451,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following table.\n','When I quarrel with a friend, \nI am wild and naughty. \nWhen you quarrel with a neighbour, \nyou\'re only doing your duty.\nSo, I am always wrong \nand you are always right. \nOh, why do people grow up \nand spoil a child\'s delight?\n2] What blame does the child put on the grow-ups?\n3] From the extract, find out a pair of opposite words.','','i) a friend                   ii) a neighbour \n2] The child blames that the grown-ups grow up only to spoil a child\'s delight.\n3] wrong x right','',1,0,0,NULL),(46074,451,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] List all the words that the grown – ups in the poem use for the child.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\nWhen I break something, \nI am clumsy or careless. \nWhen you break something, \nit slipped; you were helpless.\nWhen I do something wrong, \nI am such a terror \nWhen you do something wrong, \nit\'s a forgivable error.\n2] List all the situations described in the poem. For example, waking up late.\n3] Translate the following into your mother tongue.\n      i. careless            ii. helpless              iii. clumsy              ','','','rude, shocking, clumsy, careless, terror\n2] raising voice, breaking something, do something wrong\n3] i) careless – निष्काळजी              ii) helpless - असहाय्य\n     iii) clumsy - वेंधळा','',0,0,0,NULL),(46075,451,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following statements are true or false :\n     i. A child is called lazy if he wakes up late.\n     ii. When grown – ups say ‘no’ to something, they are called stubborn. \n     iii. The grown-ups wake late because the alarm get crazy.\n     iv. A child is always right.\nWhen I wake up late, \nI\'m getting very lazy. \nWhen you wake up late, lazy.\nit\'s the alarm that\'s crazy.\nWhen I say \'No\' to something, \nI\'m being stubborn. \nWhen you say \'No\' to something, \nyou are being firm.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\n2] Write down the double standards about-raising voice.\n3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.','','','i) True            ii) False         iii) True        iv) False\n2) When a child raises his voice, it is considered shocking and rude. On the other hand when a grown-up raises his voice, it is only a teaching to be good.\n3) i) lazy crazy        ii) rude = good','',0,0,0,NULL),(46076,451,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following table.\n','When I quarrel with a friend, \nI am wild and naughty. \nWhen you quarrel with a neighbour, \nyou\'re only doing your duty.\nSo, I am always wrong \nand you are always right. \nOh, why do people grow up \nand spoil a child\'s delight?\n2] What blame does the child put on the grow-ups?\n3] From the extract, find out a pair of opposite words.','','i) a friend                   ii) a neighbour \n2] The child blames that the grown-ups grow up only to spoil a child\'s delight.\n3] wrong x right','',1,0,0,NULL),(46077,451,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] List all the words that the grown – ups in the poem use for the child.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\nWhen I break something, \nI am clumsy or careless. \nWhen you break something, \nit slipped; you were helpless.\nWhen I do something wrong, \nI am such a terror \nWhen you do something wrong, \nit\'s a forgivable error.\n2] List all the situations described in the poem. For example, waking up late.\n3] Translate the following into your mother tongue.\n      i. careless            ii. helpless              iii. clumsy              ','','','rude, shocking, clumsy, careless, terror\n2] raising voice, breaking something, do something wrong\n3] i) careless – निष्काळजी              ii) helpless - असहाय्य\n     iii) clumsy - वेंधळा','',0,0,0,NULL),(46078,451,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following statements are true or false :\n     i. A child is called lazy if he wakes up late.\n     ii. When grown – ups say ‘no’ to something, they are called stubborn. \n     iii. The grown-ups wake late because the alarm get crazy.\n     iv. A child is always right.\nWhen I wake up late, \nI\'m getting very lazy. \nWhen you wake up late, lazy.\nit\'s the alarm that\'s crazy.\nWhen I say \'No\' to something, \nI\'m being stubborn. \nWhen you say \'No\' to something, \nyou are being firm.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\n2] Write down the double standards about-raising voice.\n3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.','','','i) True            ii) False         iii) True        iv) False\n2) When a child raises his voice, it is considered shocking and rude. On the other hand when a grown-up raises his voice, it is only a teaching to be good.\n3) i) lazy crazy        ii) rude = good','',0,0,0,NULL),(46079,451,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following table.\n','When I quarrel with a friend, \nI am wild and naughty. \nWhen you quarrel with a neighbour, \nyou\'re only doing your duty.\nSo, I am always wrong \nand you are always right. \nOh, why do people grow up \nand spoil a child\'s delight?\n2] What blame does the child put on the grow-ups?\n3] From the extract, find out a pair of opposite words.','','i) a friend                   ii) a neighbour \n2] The child blames that the grown-ups grow up only to spoil a child\'s delight.\n3] wrong x right','',1,0,0,NULL),(46080,451,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] List all the words that the grown – ups in the poem use for the child.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\nWhen I break something, \nI am clumsy or careless. \nWhen you break something, \nit slipped; you were helpless.\nWhen I do something wrong, \nI am such a terror \nWhen you do something wrong, \nit\'s a forgivable error.\n2] List all the situations described in the poem. For example, waking up late.\n3] Translate the following into your mother tongue.\n      i. careless            ii. helpless              iii. clumsy              ','','','rude, shocking, clumsy, careless, terror\n2] raising voice, breaking something, do something wrong\n3] i) careless – निष्काळजी              ii) helpless - असहाय्य\n     iii) clumsy - वेंधळा','',0,0,0,NULL),(46081,452,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Who said to whom.\n    i. \"I don\'t want to walk all the way\"\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------\n    ii. \"Now get up, get ready\"\n\n      --------------------------------------------------------------\n\"Come, Baby, Wake up! We will go ant- eating today!\" Mama Pangolin gave a gentle loving push to her one-month old baby, with her long tapering mouth. Baby was still drowsy after a day\'s sleep. Mother tried to persuade him, \"You keep asking me to take you out; you say you are tired of drinking milk all the time. Now get up, get ready.\"\n\"No! Won\'t!\" said Baby, stretching his long tail lazily, \"I don\'t want to walk all the way!\"\n\"Don\'t worry,\" said Mama, \"I\'ll carry you on my tail. Good boy, come on, it\'s time!\"\nMama set him on her tail and finally brought him out of the home. Their home was a snug hole in the earth, nine inches wide and more than a metre long. Mama cautioned Baby to sit tight and hold on to her scales with his claws. She began to walk steadily and cautiously. Just then, a porcupine went past them.\n2] From the passage find out the words related to time\n\n     -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Complete the following table :','','','i) Baby Panglin to his mother.\n    ii) Mother Pangolin to her baby.\n2) today, day, month\n3) i) gave       ii) tried           iii) drank            iv) took','',1,0,0,NULL),(46083,452,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Say whether the following statements are true or false :\n    i. Mama Pangolin used her claws to eat ants and termites.\n    ii. Baby Pangolin did not like termites\nBy now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.\n2] Complete the following sentences.\n      i. Pangolin\'s claws are ------------------\n      ii. Pangolin\'s tongue is -----------------\n3] Find out the opposites of:\n     i. weak            ii. short          iii. sad             iv. fast','','','i) False                ii) False\n2] i) strong, sharp and curved.         ii) half foot long, sticky. \n3] i) strong       ii) long         iii) happy        iv) slowly','',0,0,0,NULL),(46084,452,'descriptive','2b',0,0,'Out of Text Book','','1] How do Pangolins get their food?\nBy now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.\n2] Do as directed.\n      i. in and out went his tiny tongue. \n          (Arrange the words in correct order)\n     ii. Baby could do it (Use \'able to\')\n3] How did Mother Pangolin teach her baby to find its food?','','','Pangolins are ant eating animals. They wander for food at night. They demolish the anthill with their claws. When the termits begin to run here and there they begin to devour them with their sticky tongue.\n2] i) His tiny tongue went in and out.\n     ii) Baby was able to do it.\n3] When the baby Pangolin was one month old, Mother Pangolin took it out for ant-eating. She demolish- ed the anthill and began to devour the termites Baby watched mother and tried to follow her.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46085,453,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web \n','\n -------------------------------------------------             ------------------------------------------------\n\n--------------------------------------------------             ------------------------------------------------\nOur frigate wanted to go back, but the unearthly animal came at us with a speed double our own. We gasped. More stunned than afraid, we stood mute and motionless. The animal caught up with us, played with us. It made a full circle around the frigate and wrapped us in sheets of electricity that were like luminous dust. At any instant it could have dashed against our ship.\nMeanwhile I was surprised to see that our warship was fleeing, not fighting. I commented on this to Commander Farragut. His face, ordinarily so emotionless, showed great astonishment.\n\"Professor Aronnax,\" he answered me, \"I don\'t know what kind of fearsome creature I\'m up against, and I don\'t want my frigate running foolish risks in all this darkness. Besides, how should we attack this unknown creature, how should we defend ourselves against it? Let\'s wait for daylight and then we\'ll play a different role.\"\n2] Why was the warship fleeing and not fighting?\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out the verb in the following sentence and say whether it is transitive or intransitive. We will play a different role.\n\n    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) The unearthly animal caught up with them\n     ii) Played with them                 iii) It made a full circle round the frigate. \n     iv) It wrapped the frigate in sheets of electricity.\n2] The commander of the frigate did not want to run foolish risks in the darkness. He decided to wait for daylight. So, the frigate was not fighting but fleeing.\n3] Verb phrase = will play; Transitive verb','',1,0,0,NULL),(46086,453,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] i) What is the name of narrator?\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    ii) At what time does this event take place?\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOur frigate wanted to go back, but the unearthly animal came at us with a speed double our own. We gasped. More stunned than afraid, we stood mute and motionless. The animal caught up with us, played with us. It made a full circle around the frigate and wrapped us in sheets of electricity that were like luminous dust. At any instant it could have dashed against our ship.\nMeanwhile I was surprised to see that our warship was fleeing, not fighting. I commented on this to Commander Farragut. His face, ordinarily so emotionless, showed great astonishment.\n\"Professor Aronnax,\" he answered me, \"I don\'t know what kind of fearsome creature I\'m up against, and I don\'t want my frigate running foolish risks in all this darkness. Besides, how should we attack this unknown creature, how should we defend ourselves against it? Let\'s wait for daylight and then we\'ll play a different role.\"\n2] Match the words with their meanings.','3] Why was the narrator surprised to see that their warship was fleeing?\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) Professor Aronnax               ii) At night\n2] (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\n3] The narrator\'s ship was a warship. It was meant to fight and not to flee. But when the narrator noticed that the warship was not fighting against the unearthly animal, he was surprised.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46087,453,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences.\n      i. The animal\'s electric glow disappeared when........\n      ii. They were disappointed and angry because\nAt six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.\n2] Find out four words related to time.\n3] Write the following words in correct order.\n     midday, night, evening, morning, dawn, midnight.','','','i) day began to break.\n     ii) a very dense morning mist spread around them.\n2] day, dawn, morning, o’clock\n3] dawn → morning midday → evening → night → midnight','',0,0,0,NULL),(46088,453,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.\n     i. The horizon grew wider and clearer.\n     ii. The day began to break.\n     iii. A very dense morning mist spread around us.\n     iv. We were disappointed and angry.\nAt six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.\n2] How long does the whole event described in this passage take?\n3] Find out the subject and object from the sentence.\n     Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it.','','','Chronological order : ii → iii → iv → i\n2] All night long\n3] Subject - our best spyglasses; object - it','',0,0,0,NULL),(46089,454,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Match the following :\n','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n2] Answer the following questions :\n     i. Why is the bee called busy?\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n\n        -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.\n\n     -------------------------------------------     ---------------------------------------','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – a), (4 – d)\n2] i) The bee is called busy because she gathers honey all the day.\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46090,454,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Match the parts to make meaningful sentences ','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. what is the bee’s cell stored with?\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n\n      -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.\n\n    ----------------------------------             -------------------------------------------------','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\n2] i) The bee’s cell is stored with sweet food (honey)\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46091,454,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Name the following\n     i. The river        ii. Sea-animals             iii. Colour\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n3] Write down pairs of rhyming words.','','','i) Nile        ii) Crocodile, Fishes       iii) golden\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] i) crocodile – Nile           ii) claws – jaws ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46092,454,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences\n      i. The crocodile pours water on.......\n      ii. With smiling jaws she welcomes in ----------\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n3] Write down the organs of crocodiles body.','','','i) every golden scale              ii) little fishes.\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] tail, scale, claws, jaws','',0,0,0,NULL),(46093,454,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Match the following :\n','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n2] Answer the following questions :\n     i. Why is the bee called busy?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – a), (4 – d)\n2] i) The bee is called busy because she gathers honey all the day.\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46094,454,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Match the parts to make meaningful sentences \n','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. what is the bee’s cell stored with?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\n2] i) The bee’s cell is stored with sweet food (honey)\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46095,454,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Name the following\n     i. The river        ii. Sea-animals             iii. Colour\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n3] Write down pairs of rhyming words.','','','i) Nile        ii) Crocodile, Fishes       iii) golden\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] i) crocodile – Nile           ii) claws – jaws ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46096,454,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences\n      i. The crocodile pours water on.......\n      ii. With smiling jaws she welcomes in ----------\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n3] Write down the organs of crocodiles body.','','','i) every golden scale              ii) little fishes.\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] tail, scale, claws, jaws','',0,0,0,NULL),(46097,455,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences\n\n     i. The children used to go and play ……...............................................\n\n     ii. The children used to stop their games………........................................\n\n     iii. When the Giant arrived back he saw…………......................................\n\n     iv. The giant had stayed with his friend………….......................................\nEvery afternoon, as they were coming from school, the children used to go and play in the giant\'s garden.\nIt was a large lovely garden with soft green grass. Here and there over the grass stood beautiful flowers like stars, and there were twelve peach trees that in the springtime broke out into delicate blossoms of pink and pearl, and in the autumn bore rich fruit. The birds sat on the trees and sang so sweetly that the children used to stop their games in order to listen to them. \"How happy we are!\" they cried to each other.\nOne day the giant came back. He had been to visit his friend the Cornish Ogre, and had stayed with him for seven years. After the seven years he determined to return to his own castle. When he arrived he saw the children playing in the garden.\n2] From the extract, find out the opposites of :\n\n    i. hard : ----------------------         ii. ugly : -------------------------------\n3] Do as directed\n     i. The children used to go and play in the garden\n          (Use \'would\' in the sentence)\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. Birds sing very sweetly [Make it Exclamatory]\n\n        --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) in the giant’s garden \n    ii) in order to listen the sweet song of the birds\n    iii) the children playing in his garden\n    iv) for seven years. \n2] i) soft           ii) beautiful \n3] i) The children would go and play in the garden.\n     ii) How sweetly birds sing!','',0,0,0,NULL),(46098,455,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following web ','\n -----------------------------------              ---------------------------------------------\n\n ------------------------------------             --------------------------------------------\nEvery afternoon, as they were coming from school, the children used to go and play in the giant\'s garden.\nIt was a large lovely garden with soft green grass. Here and there over the grass stood beautiful flowers like stars, and there were twelve peach trees that in the springtime broke out into delicate blossoms of pink and pearl, and in the autumn bore rich fruit. The birds sat on the trees and sang so sweetly that the children used to stop their games in order to listen to them. \"How happy we are!\" they cried to each other.\nOne day the giant came back. He had been to visit his friend the Cornish Ogre, and had stayed with him for seven years. After the seven years he determined to return to his own castle. When he arrived he saw the children playing in the garden.\n2] From the extract, find out the opposites of :\n\n     i. poor : --------------------            ii. unhappy : ------------------------\n3] Where do you play? Which games?\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','The giant\'s garden was very large and lovely. There was green grass everywhere. There were peach trees with rich fruit. Birds sing sweet songs.\n2] i) rich             ii) happy\n3] We play on the playground of our school. we play the games like kho – kho, kabbadi and cricket.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46099,455,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences\n     i. All the children ran away when………..\n     ii. The little boy did not run away because……….\nSo, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.\n2] Write down four adverbs from the passage.\n3] What change do you notice in the behaviour of the giant?','','','i) ........they saw the giant\n     ii)...........his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming.\n2] softly, gently, away, only\n3] At first the giant drove the children away from his garden. He built a high wall around his garden. But after- wards he realised his mistake. And he invited children to play in his garden. He koncked down the wall. Moreover the giant started playing with the children.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46100,455,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web\n','So, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.\n2] The giant knocked down the wall. [Find out the subject and object]\n3] What do your prefer summer or winter? Why?','','The trees broke into blossom. The birds came and sing on the tree. All the children came running back. And with them, came the spring.\n2] Subject = the giant object - the wall.\n3] I like winter because winter is wonder season. It is a season of enthusiasm and energy. It is a harvest season in Maharashtra.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46101,452,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Who said to whom.\n    i. \"I don\'t want to walk all the way\"\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n    ii. \"Now get up, get ready\"\n\n        --------------------------------------------------------------------\n\"Come, Baby, Wake up! We will go ant- eating today!\" Mama Pangolin gave a gentle loving push to her one-month old baby, with her long tapering mouth. Baby was still drowsy after a day\'s sleep. Mother tried to persuade him, \"You keep asking me to take you out; you say you are tired of drinking milk all the time. Now get up, get ready.\"\n\"No! Won\'t!\" said Baby, stretching his long tail lazily, \"I don\'t want to walk all the way!\"\n\"Don\'t worry,\" said Mama, \"I\'ll carry you on my tail. Good boy, come on, it\'s time!\"\nMama set him on her tail and finally brought him out of the home. Their home was a snug hole in the earth, nine inches wide and more than a metre long. Mama cautioned Baby to sit tight and hold on to her scales with his claws. She began to walk steadily and cautiously. Just then, a porcupine went past them.\nA2] Complete the following sentence.\n     Baby Pangolin was not willing to go out because....\n\n     i. -----------------------------------------------------        \n\n    ii. ----------------------------------------------------\nA3] From the passage find out the words related to time\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA4] Complete the following table','A5] Why did Mother Pangolin decide to take baby out?\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A1] i) Baby Panglin to his mother.\n    ii) Mother Pangolin to her baby.\nA2] i) Baby was still drowsy after a day\'s sleep.\n     ii) It did not want to walk all the way long.\nA3] today, day, month\nA4] i) gave       ii) tried           iii) drank            iv) took \nA5] Baby Pangolin kept asking her Mother to take him out. Moreover he was tired of drinking milk all the time. So Mother Pangolin decided to take the baby out for ant- eating.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46102,452,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n      i. Pangolin\'s claws are -----------------------------------------------\n\n      ii. Pangolin\'s tongue is -----------------------------------------------------\nBy now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.\nA2] How do Pangolins get their food?\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA3] Find out the opposites of :\n\n      i. weak : --------------------    ii. short : ----------------------            \n\n      iii. sad : --------------------     iv. fast : -----------------------\nA4] Do as directed.\n      i. in and out went his tiny tongue. \n          (Arrange the words in correct order)\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. Baby could do it (Use \'able to\')\n\n        -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] How did Mother Pangolin teach her baby to find its food?\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A1] i) i) strong, sharp and curved.         ii) half foot long, sticky. \nA2] Pangolins are ant eating animals. They wander for food at night. They demolish the anthill with their claws. When the termits begin to run here and there they begin to devour them with their sticky tongue.\nA3] i) strong              ii) long              iii) happy             iv) slowly \nA4] i) i) His tiny tongue went in and out.\n     ii) Baby was able to do it.\nA5] When the baby Pangolin was one month old, Mother Pangolin took it out for ant-eating. She demolish- ed the anthill and began to devour the termites Baby watched mother and tried to follow her.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46103,453,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following web ','\n -----------------------------------------     ------------------------------------------\n\n-----------------------------------------      ------------------------------------------\nOur frigate wanted to go back, but the unearthly animal came at us with a speed double our own. We gasped. More stunned than afraid, we stood mute and motionless. The animal caught up with us, played with us. It made a full circle around the frigate and wrapped us in sheets of electricity that were like luminous dust. At any instant it could have dashed against our ship.\nMeanwhile I was surprised to see that our warship was fleeing, not fighting. I commented on this to Commander Farragut. His face, ordinarily so emotionless, showed great astonishment.\n\"Professor Aronnax,\" he answered me, \"I don\'t know what kind of fearsome creature I\'m up against, and I don\'t want my frigate running foolish risks in all this darkness. Besides, how should we attack this unknown creature, how should we defend ourselves against it? Let\'s wait for daylight and then we\'ll play a different role.\"\nA2] Why was the warship fleeing and not fighting?\n\n       ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA3] Match the words with their meanings.\n               Words                   Meanings\n        1. mute                      a) run away\n        2. motionless            b) cover\n        3. wrap                       c) silent\n        4. flee                         d) not moving\nA4] Find out the verb in the following sentence and say whether it is transitive or intransitive. We will play a different role.\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] Why was the narrator surprised to see that their warship was fleeing? \n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A1] i) i) The unearthly animal caught up with them\n     ii) Played with them                 iii) It made a full circle round the frigate. \n     iv) It wrapped the frigate in sheets of electricity.\nA2] The commander of the frigate did not want to run foolish risks in the darkness. He decided to wait for daylight. So, the frigate was not fighting but fleeing.\n  A3] (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\nA4] Verb phrase = will play; Transitive verb\nA5] The narrator\'s ship was a warship. It was meant to fight and not to flee. But when the narrator noticed that the warship was not fighting against the unearthly animal, he was surprised.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46104,453,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n      i. The animal\'s electric glow disappeared when................................................................\n\n      ii. They were disappointed and angry because ................................................................\nAt six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.\nA2] Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.\n     i. The horizon grew wider and clearer.\n     ii. The day began to break.\n     iii. A very dense morning mist spread around us.\n     iv. We were disappointed and angry.\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA3] Find out four words related to time.\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA4] Find out the subject and object from the sentence.\n        Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it.\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] Write the following words in correct order.\n     midday, night, evening, morning, dawn, midnight.\n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A1] i) day began to break.\n     ii) a very dense morning mist spread around them.\nA2] ii – iii – iv – i\nA3] day, dawn, morning, o’clock\nA4] Subject - our best spyglasses; object - it\nA5] dawn → morning midday → evening → night → midnight ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46105,455,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences\n\n     i. The children used to go and play ……........................................................\n\n     ii. The children used to stop their games………..........................................\n\n     iii. When the Giant arrived back he saw………….......................................\n\n     iv. The giant had stayed with his friend…………......................................\nEvery afternoon, as they were coming from school, the children used to go and play in the giant\'s garden.\nIt was a large lovely garden with soft green grass. Here and there over the grass stood beautiful flowers like stars, and there were twelve peach trees that in the springtime broke out into delicate blossoms of pink and pearl, and in the autumn bore rich fruit. The birds sat on the trees and sang so sweetly that the children used to stop their games in order to listen to them. \"How happy we are!\" they cried to each other.\nOne day the giant came back. He had been to visit his friend the Cornish Ogre, and had stayed with him for seven years. After the seven years he determined to return to his own castle. When he arrived he saw the children playing in the garden.\nA2] Complete the following web ','\n    ----------------------------------------    -------------------------------------\n\n    ---------------------------------------     -------------------------------------\nA3] From the extract, find out the opposites of :\n\n        i. hard : ---------------------     ii. ugly : ----------------------           \n\n        iii. poor : -------------------      iv. unhappy : ------------------\nA4] Do as directed\n     i. The children used to go and play in the garden\n          (Use \'would\' in the sentence)\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. Birds sing very sweetly [Make it Exclamatory]\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] Where do you play? Which games?  \n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A1] i) in the giant’s garden \n    ii) in order to listen the sweet song of the birds\n    iii) the children playing in his garden\n    iv) for seven years. \nA2] The giant\'s garden was very large and lovely. There was green grass everywhere. There were peach trees with rich fruit. Birds sing sweet songs.\nA3] i) soft            ii) beautiful             iii) rich            iv) happy\nA4] i) The children would go and play in the garden.\n     ii) How sweetly birds sing!\nA5] We play on the playground of our school. we play the games like kho – kho, kabbadi and cricket.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46106,455,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following sentences\n\n     i. All the children ran away when……….....................................................\n\n     ii. The little boy did not run away because………............................................\nSo, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.\nA2] Complete the following web ','\n      ------------------------------------------     ---------------------------------------\n\n      ------------------------------------------     ---------------------------------------\nA3] Write down four adverbs from the passage.\n\n       ---------------------------------------       ----------------------------------------\n\n       ---------------------------------------       ----------------------------------------\nA4]  The giant knocked down the wall. [Find out the subject and object]\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA5] What change do you notice in the behaviour of the giant?\n\n        -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A1] i) ........they saw the giant\n     ii)...........his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming.\nA2] The trees broke into blossom. The birds came and sing on the tree. All the children came running back. And with them, came the spring.\nA3] softly, gently, away, only\nA4] Subject = the giant object - the wall.\nA5] At first the giant drove the children away from his garden. He built a high wall around his garden. But after- wards he realised his mistake. And he invited children to play in his garden. He koncked down the wall. Moreover the giant started playing with the children.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46107,576,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following sentences.\n       i. Chaturbhuj is talking about --------------------------\n       ii. Nilratan is talking about ----------------------------------\n(A village road. Chaturbhuj Babu has come back to the village after passing his MA examinations, hoping that everyone will make a great fuss over him. There is a plump Afghan cat with him. Enter Nilratan.) \nNilratan : : Hello there, Chatur Babu. When did you arrive?\nChaturbhuj : Directly after the MA exams were over. I - \nNilratan : Ah, you\'ve got a fine cat there! \nChaturbhuj : This year the exams were very – \nNilratan : Tell me, where did you find that cat?\nChaturbhuj : Bought it. The subjects I\'d offered \nNilratan : How much did you pay for it?\nChaturbhuj : I don\'t remember. Nilratan Babu, has anybody passed any exams in our village?\nNilratan : Oh, lots of them. But you won\'t see a cat like that in these parts.  \nChaturbhuj : (to himself) Confound him, he can\'t talk about anything but the cat! It doesn\'t seem to matter that I\'ve just passed my MA!\nA2] Why is Chaturbhuj angry with Nilratan?\nA3] a) Find out the words that mean : i) come         ii) many\n       b) Find out opposites of : i) forget         ii) failed\nA4] Do as directed\n        i. I don\'t remember. (Make it Affirmative)\n        ii. He can\'t talk about anything but the cat (Make Affirmative)\nA5] Write 3-4-sentences about what you think about the temper of Nilratan - the villager?','','','A1] i) his M.A. examination          ii) the car\nA2] Chatubhuj is angry with Nilratan because he doesn\'t talk about anything but the cat. He doesn\'t seem to matter that Chatubhuj have passed his M.A. examination.\nA3] a) i) arrive           ii) lots of\n       b) i) remember         ii) passed\nA4] i) I forget\n    ii) He can talk only about the cat\nA5] I think that Nilratan is illiterate. He is not interested in the exam that Chaturbhuj has passed. On the contrary he is interested in the fine cat that Chaturbhuj has.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46108,576,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Say whether the following sentences are true or false\n     i. Chaturbhuj was angry with all.\n     ii. Chaturbhuj decided to leave at once.\n     iii. Mother cared more for the cat than for Chaturbhuj:\n      iv. Mother asked Chaturbhuj to bathe first.\n(Inside Chaturbhuj\'s house.)\nMaid : Mother, Dada Babu has come home in a blazing temper.\nMother : Why, what\'s the Why, what\'s the matter \nMaid : I don\'t know (Enter Chaturbhuj.)\nLittle Boy : Dada, can I have this cat?\nChaturbhuj : (slaps him hard.) Cat, cat, cat the whole day long, is it?\nMother : No wonder the poor boy\'s angry! He comes home after such a long time and these brats start annoying him at once. (To Chaturbhuj) Let me have the cat, son. I\'ll give it some rice and milk that I\'ve put by.\nChaturbhuj  : (furiously) Here, mother, you can have the cat and feed it all\nyou want. I shan\'t stop to eat - I\'m leaving at once.\nMother : (plaintively) What makes you say such a thing? - Your meal\'s ready and waiting, dear. You can sit down to it as soon as you\'ve had your bath!\nA2] Was Chaturbhuj was angry with mother too why?\nA3] Find out words that mean :\n       i. naughty kids              ii. making angry\n       iii. angrily                      iv. in a sad way\nA4] Punctuate the following.\n     mother said what makes you say such a thing.\nA5] Do you think that Chaturbhuj\'s mother cared more for the cat than for her son? ','','','A1] i) True         ii) Ture            iii) False          iv) True\nA2] Yes, Chaturbhuj was angry with mother too. Because she cared more for the cat and not for Chaturbhuj. She offered some rice and milk to cat. But she did not ask Chaturbhuj about his meal.\nA3] i) brats            ii) annoying           iii) furiously         iv) plaintively \nA4] Mother said, \" What makes you say such a thing?\"\nA5] No, I don\'t think so. Mother cared for the cat as well as for Chaturbhuj. She offered some rice and milk to the cat. But before that she had made Chaturbhuj\'s meal ready. And she was waiting for Chaturbhuj to have bath and take his meal. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46109,577,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following web ','The famous physics professor Stephen Hawking has warned that mankind is facing the most dangerous time in its history today. He said that the world today is facing huge challenges like climate change, overpopulation, epidemic diseases. \'We have the technology to destroy the planet on which we live and have not yet developed the ability to escape it. Perhaps in a few hundred years from now, we will have built colonies amid the stars, but right now, we have only one planet and we need to work together to protect it.\' Hawking wrote recently in the Guardian newspaper.\nA2] Write a note on Stephen Hawking’s Warning\n        i. ---------------------     ii. ---------------   iii. -------------\nA3] From the passage find out the words that are related to astronomy.\nA4] a) Find out the adjective forms of : i) danger         ii) fame\n       b) Find out the past tense of : i) write              ii) build\nA5] What should we do to save our planet?','','A1] i) Climate change          ii) overpopulation        iii) epidemic diseases\nA2] Stephen Hawking has warned us that we are e facing the most dangerous time today. Our technology will destroy our earth And we have not the ability to escape it.\nA3]  i) world         ii) climate         iii) planet          iv) stars\nA4] a) i) dangerous             ii) famous\n       b) i) wrote                      ii) built\nA5] To save our planet we should :\n     i) plant more and more trees.\n     ii) avoid excessive use of natural resources.\n     iii) ban plastic','',1,0,0,NULL),(46110,577,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Name the following\n     i. A tiny settlement                   ii. A quack\n     iii. Mountain                              iv. Hospital\nHundreds of people flock every hour to Ralewadi, a tiny settlement near Ambegaon. They wait in long winding queues in the hot sun to get a \'special\' powder from Miribaba who claims that it cures all sorts of diseases. According to him, he has developed this powder from a special herb in the Himalayas. \nUnwilling to give the exact name and source of the herb, he only says that it was revealed to him. He offers it at Rs 25 a packet. Each packet is specially \'blessed\' by him. Some of his followers claim that they have seen him euring thousands of people with his powder. However, Dr Karnik of Ambegaon Civil Hospital says that the number of patients in their hospital has in fact increased in the last few weeks.\nA2] Complete the following web ','A3] From the passage find out the opposites of:\n     i. cold x                          ii. decreased x\n     iii. huge x                       iv. cursed x\nA4] Frame \'wh questions\' to get ‘Ralewadi’ words by answers.\n     i. Hundered of people flock every hour to Ralewadi\n     ii. It cures all sorts of diseases.\nA5] What shows that Miribaba\'s claim is unreliable?','','A1] i) Ralewadi              ii) Miribaba\n     iii) Himalaya            iv) Ambegaon Civil Hospital\nA2] Special powder: Miribaba claims that his special powder cures all sort of diseases. He has developed it from a special herb in the Himalayas. He offers it at Rs. 25 a packet.\nA3] i) hot                       ii) increased\n     iii) tiny                     iv) blessed\nA4] i) Where do hunderds of people flock every hour?\n       ii) What does it cure?\nA5] Miribaba claims that his special powder cures all sorts of diseases. As a result of his claim, the number of patients should have decreased. But Dr. Karnik points out the that number of patients has increased. It shows that Miribaba\'s claim is unreliable.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46111,579,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Say whether the following sentences are true or false.\n        i. Sherlock Holmes is narrating the story - ------------------\n        ii. Mr. Jabez Wilson was a pawn-broker. - ------------------\n        iii. The office of the Red -headed League is on Fleet street. ---------\n        iv. Mr. Wilson liked his assistant. ---------------------\nWhen I called upon my friend, Mr Sherlock Holmes, he was conversing with a visitor, who was an elderly man with fiery red hair.\nHolmes introduced me to the visitor, Mr Jabez Wilson. He was a pawn-broker. Holmes asked him to repeat his story for me. He began by showing us an advertisement in a newspaper. It read as follows-\nTO THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE\nThere is a vacancy for a member of the League, and the salary is four pounds a week for nominal services. Red-headed men may apply in person on Monday, at eleven o\'clock. to Duncan Ross, at the office of the League, 7 Fleet Street.\nThe advertisement had appeared in newspaper two months ago. Mr Wilson\'s assistant, Vincent Spaulding, had shown it to him. Mr Wilson liked his assistant Spaulding. He was smart, efficient and worked for only half the normal wages! But, the assistant also had his faults. Every now and then, he left work and went down into the cellar to develop photographs. Photography was his hobby.\nA2] Complete the following web ','A3] Match the following words with their meanings \n         Words                              Meanings\n        i. Converse                      a) wages\n        ii. fiery                              b) speak\n        iii. repeat                          c) like fire\n        iv. salary                           d) say again\nA4] Identify the tense of the following sentences,\n     i. He was conversing with a visitor.\n     ii. Vincent Spaulding had shown it to him.\nA5] There is a vacancy for a teacher in your school Make an advertisement for it.','','A1] i) i) False           ii) True            iii) True            iv) True\nA2] Vincent Spaulding was Mr. Wilson\'s assistant. He was smart, efficient. He worked for only half the normal wages. Photography was his hobby.\nA3] (i – b), (ii – c), (iii – d), (iv – a)\nA4] i) Continuous present tense           ii) perfect past tense\nA5] New English school, Pune \n     Wanted Teacher\n      Sub - English\n      Interested candidates may apply to \n       Headmaster, New English School, Pune.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46112,579,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The revolver fell down on the floor because ----------------\n     ii. The man\'s companion had ----------------------------\nWe waited silently for more than an hour. It was pitch dark in the cellar. Then suddenly, a point of bright light appeared in the floor of the cellar, then a line, and a gash seemed to open, and a hand appeared. A broad stone turned over upon its side, and left a square hele. A boyish face emerged. \nThe man looked about and came out of the hole. He had a companion with him, a man with very red hair. The pair was none other than Spaulding the assistant alias the criminal Clay and the red-headed Mr Duncan Ross! As soon as they climbed out of the hole, Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. \nThe other dived down the hole and disappeared. Clay took out a revolver. But Holmes hit him on his wrist, and the revolver fell on the floor.\nA2] Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.\n     i. Holmess seized him by the collar\n     ii. A broad stone turned over upon its side.\n     iii. A point of bright light appeared in the floor.\n      iv. A man came out of the hole.\nA3] From the passage find out two adjectives and two adverbs.\nA4] Change the following sentence into a question and negative sentence.\n      There was pitch dark in the cellar.\nA5] Find out the sentences from the passage that show that Holmes was a very brave man. ','','','A1] i) Holmes hit him on his wrist.           ii) very red hair.\nA2] iii – ii – iv – i\nA3] i) Adjectives = dark, bright         ii) Adverbs = silently, suddenly\nA4] i) Question - Was there pitch dark in the cellar?\n    ii) Negative - There was not pitch dark in the cellar.\nA5] Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. Holmes hit him on his wrist and the revolver fell on the floor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46113,580,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">When I wake up late, \nI\'m getting very lazy. \nWhen you wake up late, lazy.\nit\'s the alarm that\'s crazy.\nWhen I say \'No\' to something, \nI\'m being stubborn. \nWhen you say \'No\' to something, \nyou are being firm.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Say whether the following statements are true or false </b>\n     i. A child is called lazy if he wakes up late.\n     ii. When grown – ups say ‘no’ to something, they are called stubborn. \n     iii. The grown-ups wake late because the alarm get crazy.\n     iv. A child is always right.\n','<b>2] Write down the double standards about-raising voice.</b>\n<b>3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','1) i) True            ii) False         iii) True        iv) False\n2) When a child raises his voice, it is considered shocking and rude. On the other hand when a grown-up raises his voice, it is only a teaching to be good.\n3) i) lazy crazy        ii) rude = good','',0,0,0,NULL),(46115,580,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">When I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\nWhen I break something, \nI am clumsy or careless. \nWhen you break something, \nit slipped; you were helpless.\nWhen I do something wrong, \nI am such a terror \nWhen you do something wrong, \nit\'s a forgivable error.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] List all the words that the grown – ups in the poem use for the child.</b>\n        ','<b>2] List all the situations described in the poem. For example, waking up late.</b>\n<b>3] Translate the following into your mother tongue.</b>\n      i. careless            ii. helpless              iii. clumsy      ','','1] rude, shocking, clumsy, careless, terror\n2] raising voice, breaking something, do something wrong\n3] i) careless – निष्काळजी              ii) helpless - असहाय्य\n     iii) clumsy - वेंधळा','',0,0,0,NULL),(46116,580,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Say whether the following statements are true or false :\n     i. A child is called lazy if he wakes up late.\n     ii. When grown – ups say ‘no’ to something, they are called stubborn. \n     iii. The grown-ups wake late because the alarm get crazy.\n     iv. A child is always right.\nWhen I wake up late, \nI\'m getting very lazy. \nWhen you wake up late, lazy.\nit\'s the alarm that\'s crazy.\nWhen I say \'No\' to something, \nI\'m being stubborn. \nWhen you say \'No\' to something, \nyou are being firm.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\n2] Write down the double standards about-raising voice.\n3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.','','','i) True            ii) False         iii) True        iv) False\n2) When a child raises his voice, it is considered shocking and rude. On the other hand when a grown-up raises his voice, it is only a teaching to be good.\n3) i) lazy crazy        ii) rude = good','',0,0,0,NULL),(46117,580,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following table.','When I quarrel with a friend, \nI am wild and naughty. \nWhen you quarrel with a neighbour, \nyou\'re only doing your duty.\nSo, I am always wrong \nand you are always right. \nOh, why do people grow up \nand spoil a child\'s delight?\n2] What blame does the child put on the grow-ups?\n3] From the extract, find out a pair of opposite words.','','i) a friend                   ii) a neighbour \n2] The child blames that the grown-ups grow up only to spoil a child\'s delight.\n3] wrong x right','',1,0,0,NULL),(46118,580,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] List all the words that the grown – ups in the poem use for the child.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\nWhen I break something, \nI am clumsy or careless. \nWhen you break something, \nit slipped; you were helpless.\nWhen I do something wrong, \nI am such a terror \nWhen you do something wrong, \nit\'s a forgivable error.\n2] List all the situations described in the poem. For example, waking up late.\n3] Translate the following into your mother tongue.\n      i. careless            ii. helpless              iii. clumsy              ','','','rude, shocking, clumsy, careless, terror\n2] raising voice, breaking something, do something wrong\n3] i) careless – निष्काळजी              ii) helpless - असहाय्य\n     iii) clumsy - वेंधळा','',0,0,0,NULL),(46119,580,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">When I wake up late, \nI\'m getting very lazy. \nWhen you wake up late, lazy.\nit\'s the alarm that\'s crazy.\nWhen I say \'No\' to something, \nI\'m being stubborn. \nWhen you say \'No\' to something, \nyou are being firm.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Say whether the following statements are true or false </b>\n     i. A child is called lazy if he wakes up late.\n     ii. When grown – ups say ‘no’ to something, they are called stubborn. \n     iii. The grown-ups wake late because the alarm get crazy.\n     iv. A child is always right.\n','<b>2] Write down the double standards about-raising voice.</b>\n<b>3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','i) True            ii) False         iii) True        iv) False\n2) When a child raises his voice, it is considered shocking and rude. On the other hand when a grown-up raises his voice, it is only a teaching to be good.\n3) i) lazy crazy        ii) rude = good','',0,0,0,NULL),(46121,580,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">When I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\nWhen I break something, \nI am clumsy or careless. \nWhen you break something, \nit slipped; you were helpless.\nWhen I do something wrong, \nI am such a terror \nWhen you do something wrong, \nit\'s a forgivable error.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] List all the words that the grown – ups in the poem use for the child.</b>\n          ','<b>2] List all the situations described in the poem. For example, waking up late.</b>\n<b>3] Translate the following into your mother tongue.</b>\n      i. careless            ii. helpless              iii. clumsy    ','','1] rude, shocking, clumsy, careless, terror\n2] raising voice, breaking something, do something wrong\n3] i) careless – निष्काळजी              ii) helpless - असहाय्य\n     iii) clumsy - वेंधळा','',0,0,0,NULL),(46122,580,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">When I wake up late, \nI\'m getting very lazy. \nWhen you wake up late, lazy.\nit\'s the alarm that\'s crazy.\nWhen I say \'No\' to something, \nI\'m being stubborn. \nWhen you say \'No\' to something, \nyou are being firm.\nWhen I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Say whether the following statements are true or false </b>\n     i. A child is called lazy if he wakes up late.\n     ii. When grown – ups say ‘no’ to something, they are called stubborn. \n     iii. The grown-ups wake late because the alarm get crazy.\n     iv. A child is always right.\n','<b>2] Write down the double standards about-raising voice.</b>\n<b>3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','1) i) True            ii) False         iii) True        iv) False\n2) When a child raises his voice, it is considered shocking and rude. On the other hand when a grown-up raises his voice, it is only a teaching to be good.\n3) i) lazy crazy        ii) rude = good','',0,0,0,NULL),(46124,580,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">When I raise my voice, \nit is shocking and rude. \nWhen you raise your voice, \nyou\'re teaching me to be good.\nWhen I break something, \nI am clumsy or careless. \nWhen you break something, \nit slipped; you were helpless.\nWhen I do something wrong, \nI am such a terror \nWhen you do something wrong, \nit\'s a forgivable error.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] List all the words that the grown – ups in the poem use for the child.</b>\n','<b>2] List all the situations described in the poem. For example, waking up late.</b>\n<b>3] Translate the following into your mother tongue.</b>\n      i. careless            ii. helpless              iii. clumsy              ','','1] rude, shocking, clumsy, careless, terror\n2] raising voice, breaking something, do something wrong\n3] i) careless – निष्काळजी              ii) helpless - असहाय्य\n     iii) clumsy - वेंधळा','',0,0,0,NULL),(46126,581,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','\"Come, Baby, Wake up! We will go ant- eating today!\" Mama Pangolin gave a gentle loving push to her one-month old baby, with her long tapering mouth. Baby was still drowsy after a day\'s sleep. Mother tried to persuade him, \"You keep asking me to take you out; you say you are tired of drinking milk all the time. Now get up, get ready.\"\n\"No! Won\'t!\" said Baby, stretching his long tail lazily, \"I don\'t want to walk all the way!\"\n\"Don\'t worry,\" said Mama, \"I\'ll carry you on my tail. Good boy, come on, it\'s time!\"\nMama set him on her tail and finally brought him out of the home. Their home was a snug hole in the earth, nine inches wide and more than a metre long. Mama cautioned Baby to sit tight and hold on to her scales with his claws. She began to walk steadily and cautiously. Just then, a porcupine went past them.','<b>1] Complete the following sentence.</b>\n     Baby Pangolin was not willing to go out because....\n     i. ----------------------        ii. ------------------------------\n','<b>2] i) What did Mama Pangolin do? Why?</b>\n     ii) Guess where they live.\n<b>3] Why did Mother Pangolin decide to take baby out?</b>','','i) Baby was still drowsy after a day\'s sleep.\n     ii) It did not want to walk all the way long.\n2] i) Mama Pangolin gave a gentle loving push to her baby.\n      ii) In a snug hole in the earth\n3] Baby Pangolin kept asking her Mother to take him out. Moreover he was tired of drinking milk all the time. So Mother Pangolin decided to take the baby out for ant- eating.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46127,581,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Say whether the following statements are true or false :\n    i. Mama Pangolin used her claws to eat ants and termites.\n    ii. Baby Pangolin did not like termites\nBy now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.\n2] Complete the following sentences.\n      i. Pangolin\'s claws are ------------------\n      ii. Pangolin\'s tongue is -----------------\n3] Find out the opposites of:\n     i. weak            ii. short          iii. sad             iv. fast','','','i) False                ii) False\n2] i) strong, sharp and curved.         ii) half foot long, sticky. \n3] i) strong       ii) long         iii) happy        iv) slowly','',0,0,0,NULL),(46128,581,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] How do Pangolins get their food?\nBy now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.\n2] Do as directed.\n      i. in and out went his tiny tongue. \n          (Arrange the words in correct order)\n     ii. Baby could do it (Use \'able to\')\n3] How did Mother Pangolin teach her baby to find its food?','','','1] Pangolins are ant eating animals. They wander for food at night. They demolish the anthill with their claws. When the termits begin to run here and there they begin to devour them with their sticky tongue.\n2] i) His tiny tongue went in and out.\n     ii) Baby was able to do it.\n3] When the baby Pangolin was one month old, Mother Pangolin took it out for ant-eating. She demolish- ed the anthill and began to devour the termites Baby watched mother and tried to follow her.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46131,582,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences.\n      i. The animal\'s electric glow disappeared when........\n      ii. They were disappointed and angry because\nAt six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.\n2] Find out four words related to time.\n3] Write the following words in correct order.\n     midday, night, evening, morning, dawn, midnight.','','','1] i) day began to break.\n     ii) a very dense morning mist spread around them.\n2] day, dawn, morning, o’clock\n3] dawn → morning midday → evening → night → midnight','',0,0,0,NULL),(46132,582,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.\n     i. The horizon grew wider and clearer.\n     ii. The day began to break.\n     iii. A very dense morning mist spread around us.\n     iv. We were disappointed and angry.\nAt six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.\n2] How long does the whole event described in this passage take?\n3] Find out the subject and object from the sentence.\n     Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it.','','','Chronological order : ii → iii → iv → i\n2] All night long\n3] Subject - our best spyglasses; object - it','',0,0,0,NULL),(46135,583,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">How doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Name the following</b>\n     i. The river        ii. Sea-animals             iii. Colour\n','<b>2] Answer the following questions</b>\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n<b>3] Write down pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','i) Nile        ii) Crocodile, Fishes       iii) golden\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] i) crocodile – Nile           ii) claws – jaws ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46136,583,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">How doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Complete the following sentences</b>\n      i. The crocodile pours water on.......\n      ii. With smiling jaws she welcomes in ----------\n','<b>2] Answer the following questions</b>\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n<b>3] Write down the organs of crocodiles body.</b>','','i) every golden scale              ii) little fishes.\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] tail, scale, claws, jaws','',0,0,0,NULL),(46137,583,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Match the following :\n','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n2] Answer the following questions :\n     i. Why is the bee called busy?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – a), (4 – d)\n2] i) The bee is called busy because she gathers honey all the day.\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46138,583,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Match the parts to make meaningful sentences ','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. what is the bee’s cell stored with?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\n2] i) The bee’s cell is stored with sweet food (honey)\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46139,583,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Name the following\n     i. The river        ii. Sea-animals             iii. Colour\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n3] Write down pairs of rhyming words.','','','1] i) Nile        ii) Crocodile, Fishes       iii) golden\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] i) crocodile – Nile           ii) claws – jaws ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46140,583,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences\n      i. The crocodile pours water on.......\n      ii. With smiling jaws she welcomes in ----------\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n3] Write down the organs of crocodiles body.','','','1] i) every golden scale              ii) little fishes.\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] tail, scale, claws, jaws','',0,0,0,NULL),(46143,583,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">How doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Name the following</b>\n     i. The river        ii. Sea-animals             iii. Colour\n','<b>2] Answer the following questions</b>\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n<b>3] Write down pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','1] i) Nile        ii) Crocodile, Fishes       iii) golden\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] i) crocodile – Nile           ii) claws – jaws ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46144,583,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">How doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Complete the following sentences</b>\n      i. The crocodile pours water on.......\n      ii. With smiling jaws she welcomes in ----------\n','<b>2] Answer the following questions</b>\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n<b>3] Write down the organs of crocodiles body.</b>','','1] i) every golden scale              ii) little fishes.\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] tail, scale, claws, jaws','',0,0,0,NULL),(46145,583,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Match the following :\n','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n2] Answer the following questions :\n     i. Why is the bee called busy?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – a), (4 – d)\n2] i) The bee is called busy because she gathers honey all the day.\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46146,583,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Match the parts to make meaningful sentences ','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. what is the bee’s cell stored with?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.','','1] (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\n2] i) The bee’s cell is stored with sweet food (honey)\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',1,0,0,NULL),(46147,583,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Name the following\n     i. The river        ii. Sea-animals             iii. Colour\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n3] Write down pairs of rhyming words.','','','1] i) Nile        ii) Crocodile, Fishes       iii) golden\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] i) crocodile – Nile           ii) claws – jaws ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46148,583,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences\n      i. The crocodile pours water on.......\n      ii. With smiling jaws she welcomes in ----------\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n3] Write down the organs of crocodiles body.','','','1] i) every golden scale              ii) little fishes.\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] tail, scale, claws, jaws','',0,0,0,NULL),(46149,584,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Every afternoon, as they were coming from school, the children used to go and play in the giant\'s garden.\nIt was a large lovely garden with soft green grass. Here and there over the grass stood beautiful flowers like stars, and there were twelve peach trees that in the springtime broke out into delicate blossoms of pink and pearl, and in the autumn bore rich fruit. The birds sat on the trees and sang so sweetly that the children used to stop their games in order to listen to them. \"How happy we are!\" they cried to each other.\nOne day the giant came back. He had been to visit his friend the Cornish Ogre, and had stayed with him for seven years. After the seven years he determined to return to his own castle. When he arrived he saw the children playing in the garden.','<b>1] Complete the following sentences</b>\n     i. The children used to go and play ……..\n     ii. The children used to stop their games……….\n     iii. When the Giant arrived back he saw…………..\n     iv. The giant had stayed with his friend…………\n','<b>2] From the extract, find out the opposites of:</b>\n    i. hard          ii. ugly\n<b>3] Do as directed</b>\n     i. The children used to go and play in the garden\n          (Use \'would\' in the sentence)\n     ii. Birds sing very sweetly [Make it Exclamatory]','','1] i) in the giant’s garden \n    ii) in order to listen the sweet song of the birds\n    iii) the children playing in his garden\n    iv) for seven years. \n2] i) soft           ii) beautiful \n3] i) The children would go and play in the garden.\n     ii) How sweetly birds sing!','',0,0,0,NULL),(46151,584,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','So, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.','<b>1] Complete the following sentences</b>\n     i. All the children ran away when………..\n     ii. The little boy did not run away because……….\n','<b>2] Write down four adverbs from the passage.</b>\n<b>3] What change do you notice in the behaviour of the giant?</b>','','1] i) ........they saw the giant\n     ii)...........his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming.\n2] softly, gently, away, only\n3] At first the giant drove the children away from his garden. He built a high wall around his garden. But after- wards he realised his mistake. And he invited children to play in his garden. He koncked down the wall. Moreover the giant started playing with the children.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46153,584,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences\n     i. The children used to go and play ……..\n     ii. The children used to stop their games……….\n     iii. When the Giant arrived back he saw…………..\n     iv. The giant had stayed with his friend…………\nEvery afternoon, as they were coming from school, the children used to go and play in the giant\'s garden.\nIt was a large lovely garden with soft green grass. Here and there over the grass stood beautiful flowers like stars, and there were twelve peach trees that in the springtime broke out into delicate blossoms of pink and pearl, and in the autumn bore rich fruit. The birds sat on the trees and sang so sweetly that the children used to stop their games in order to listen to them. \"How happy we are!\" they cried to each other.\nOne day the giant came back. He had been to visit his friend the Cornish Ogre, and had stayed with him for seven years. After the seven years he determined to return to his own castle. When he arrived he saw the children playing in the garden.\n2] From the extract, find out the opposites of:\n    i. hard          ii. ugly\n3] Do as directed\n     i. The children used to go and play in the garden\n          (Use \'would\' in the sentence)\n     ii. Birds sing very sweetly [Make it Exclamatory]','','','1] i) in the giant’s garden \n    ii) in order to listen the sweet song of the birds\n    iii) the children playing in his garden\n    iv) for seven years. \n2] i) soft           ii) beautiful \n3] i) The children would go and play in the garden.\n     ii) How sweetly birds sing!','',0,0,0,NULL),(46154,584,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following web ','Every afternoon, as they were coming from school, the children used to go and play in the giant\'s garden.\nIt was a large lovely garden with soft green grass. Here and there over the grass stood beautiful flowers like stars, and there were twelve peach trees that in the springtime broke out into delicate blossoms of pink and pearl, and in the autumn bore rich fruit. The birds sat on the trees and sang so sweetly that the children used to stop their games in order to listen to them. \"How happy we are!\" they cried to each other.\nOne day the giant came back. He had been to visit his friend the Cornish Ogre, and had stayed with him for seven years. After the seven years he determined to return to his own castle. When he arrived he saw the children playing in the garden.\n2] From the extract, find out the opposites of:\n     i. poor             ii. unhappy\n3] Where do you play? Which games?','','1] The giant\'s garden was very large and lovely. There was green grass everywhere. There were peach trees with rich fruit. Birds sing sweet songs.\n2] i) rich             ii) happy\n3] We play on the playground of our school. we play the games like kho – kho, kabbadi and cricket.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46155,584,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences\n     i. All the children ran away when………..\n     ii. The little boy did not run away because……….\nSo, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.\n2] Write down four adverbs from the passage.\n3] What change do you notice in the behaviour of the giant?','','','1] i) ........they saw the giant\n     ii)...........his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming.\n2] softly, gently, away, only\n3] At first the giant drove the children away from his garden. He built a high wall around his garden. But after- wards he realised his mistake. And he invited children to play in his garden. He koncked down the wall. Moreover the giant started playing with the children.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46156,584,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following web','So, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.\n2] The giant knocked down the wall. [Find out the subject and object]\n3] What do your prefer summer or winter? Why?','','1] The trees broke into blossom. The birds came and sing on the tree. All the children came running back. And with them, came the spring.\n2] Subject = the giant object - the wall.\n3] I like winter because winter is wonder season. It is a season of enthusiasm and energy. It is a harvest season in Maharashtra.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46157,581,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Who said to whom.\n    i. \"I don\'t want to walk all the way\"\n    ii. \"Now get up, get ready\"\n\"Come, Baby, Wake up! We will go ant- eating today!\" Mama Pangolin gave a gentle loving push to her one-month old baby, with her long tapering mouth. Baby was still drowsy after a day\'s sleep. Mother tried to persuade him, \"You keep asking me to take you out; you say you are tired of drinking milk all the time. Now get up, get ready.\"\n\"No! Won\'t!\" said Baby, stretching his long tail lazily, \"I don\'t want to walk all the way!\"\n\"Don\'t worry,\" said Mama, \"I\'ll carry you on my tail. Good boy, come on, it\'s time!\"\nMama set him on her tail and finally brought him out of the home. Their home was a snug hole in the earth, nine inches wide and more than a metre long. Mama cautioned Baby to sit tight and hold on to her scales with his claws. She began to walk steadily and cautiously. Just then, a porcupine went past them.\nA2] Complete the following sentence.\n     Baby Pangolin was not willing to go out because....\n     i. ----------------------        ii. ------------------------------\nA3] From the passage find out the words related to time\nA4] Complete the following table','A5] Why did Mother Pangolin decide to take baby out?','','A1] i) Baby Panglin to his mother.\n    ii) Mother Pangolin to her baby.\nA2] i) Baby was still drowsy after a day\'s sleep.\n     ii) It did not want to walk all the way long.\nA3] today, day, month\nA4] i) gave       ii) tried           iii) drank            iv) took \nA5] Baby Pangolin kept asking her Mother to take him out. Moreover he was tired of drinking milk all the time. So Mother Pangolin decided to take the baby out for ant- eating.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46158,581,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following sentences.\n      i. Pangolin\'s claws are ------------------\n      ii. Pangolin\'s tongue is -----------------\nBy now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.\nA2] How do Pangolins get their food?\nA3] Find out the opposites of :\n      i. weak             ii. short             iii. sad               iv. fast\nA4] Do as directed.\n      i. in and out went his tiny tongue. \n          (Arrange the words in correct order)\n     ii. Baby could do it (Use \'able to\')\nA5] How did Mother Pangolin teach her baby to find its food?','','','A1] i) i) strong, sharp and curved.         ii) half foot long, sticky. \nA2] Pangolins are ant eating animals. They wander for food at night. They demolish the anthill with their claws. When the termits begin to run here and there they begin to devour them with their sticky tongue.\nA3] i) strong              ii) long              iii) happy             iv) slowly \nA4] i) i) His tiny tongue went in and out.\n     ii) Baby was able to do it.\nA5] When the baby Pangolin was one month old, Mother Pangolin took it out for ant-eating. She demolish- ed the anthill and began to devour the termites Baby watched mother and tried to follow her.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46159,582,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following web ','Our frigate wanted to go back, but the unearthly animal came at us with a speed double our own. We gasped. More stunned than afraid, we stood mute and motionless. The animal caught up with us, played with us. It made a full circle around the frigate and wrapped us in sheets of electricity that were like luminous dust. At any instant it could have dashed against our ship.\nMeanwhile I was surprised to see that our warship was fleeing, not fighting. I commented on this to Commander Farragut. His face, ordinarily so emotionless, showed great astonishment.\n\"Professor Aronnax,\" he answered me, \"I don\'t know what kind of fearsome creature I\'m up against, and I don\'t want my frigate running foolish risks in all this darkness. Besides, how should we attack this unknown creature, how should we defend ourselves against it? Let\'s wait for daylight and then we\'ll play a different role.\"\nA2] Why was the warship fleeing and not fighting?\nA3] Match the words with their meanings.\n               Words                   Meanings\n        1. mute                      a) run away\n        2. motionless            b) cover\n        3. wrap                       c) silent\n        4. flee                         d) not moving\nA4] Find out the verb in the following sentence and say whether it is transitive or intransitive. We will play a different role.\nA5] Why was the narrator surprised to see that their warship was fleeing? ','','A1] i) i) The unearthly animal caught up with them\n     ii) Played with them                 iii) It made a full circle round the frigate. \n     iv) It wrapped the frigate in sheets of electricity.\nA2] The commander of the frigate did not want to run foolish risks in the darkness. He decided to wait for daylight. So, the frigate was not fighting but fleeing.\n  A3] (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\nA4] Verb phrase = will play; Transitive verb\nA5] The narrator\'s ship was a warship. It was meant to fight and not to flee. But when the narrator noticed that the warship was not fighting against the unearthly animal, he was surprised.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46160,582,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following sentences.\n      i. The animal\'s electric glow disappeared when........\n      ii. They were disappointed and angry because\nAt six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.\nA2] Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.\n     i. The horizon grew wider and clearer.\n     ii. The day began to break.\n     iii. A very dense morning mist spread around us.\n     iv. We were disappointed and angry.\nA3] Find out four words related to time.\nA4] Find out the subject and object from the sentence.\n        Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it.\nA5] Write the following words in correct order.\n     midday, night, evening, morning, dawn, midnight.','','','A1] i) day began to break.\n     ii) a very dense morning mist spread around them.\nA2] ii – iii – iv – i\nA3] day, dawn, morning, o’clock\nA4] Subject - our best spyglasses; object - it\nA5] dawn → morning midday → evening → night → midnight ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46161,584,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','1] Complete the following sentences\n     i. The children used to go and play ……..\n     ii. The children used to stop their games……….\n     iii. When the Giant arrived back he saw…………..\n     iv. The giant had stayed with his friend…………\nEvery afternoon, as they were coming from school, the children used to go and play in the giant\'s garden.\nIt was a large lovely garden with soft green grass. Here and there over the grass stood beautiful flowers like stars, and there were twelve peach trees that in the springtime broke out into delicate blossoms of pink and pearl, and in the autumn bore rich fruit. The birds sat on the trees and sang so sweetly that the children used to stop their games in order to listen to them. \"How happy we are!\" they cried to each other.\nOne day the giant came back. He had been to visit his friend the Cornish Ogre, and had stayed with him for seven years. After the seven years he determined to return to his own castle. When he arrived he saw the children playing in the garden.\nA2] Complete the following web ','A3] From the extract, find out the opposites of :\n        i. hard            ii. ugly           iii. poor              iv. unhappy\nA4] Do as directed\n     i. The children used to go and play in the garden\n          (Use \'would\' in the sentence)\n     ii. Birds sing very sweetly [Make it Exclamatory]\nA5] Where do you play? Which games?  ','','A1] i) in the giant’s garden \n    ii) in order to listen the sweet song of the birds\n    iii) the children playing in his garden\n    iv) for seven years. \nA2] The giant\'s garden was very large and lovely. There was green grass everywhere. There were peach trees with rich fruit. Birds sing sweet songs.\nA3] i) soft            ii) beautiful             iii) rich            iv) happy\nA4] i) The children would go and play in the garden.\n     ii) How sweetly birds sing!\nA5] We play on the playground of our school. we play the games like kho – kho, kabbadi and cricket.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46162,584,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','','A1] Complete the following sentences\n     i. All the children ran away when………..\n     ii. The little boy did not run away because……….\nSo, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.\nA2] Complete the following web ','A3] Write down four adverbs from the passage.\nA4]  The giant knocked down the wall. [Find out the subject and object]\nA5] What change do you notice in the behaviour of the giant?','','A1] i) ........they saw the giant\n     ii)...........his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming.\nA2] The trees broke into blossom. The birds came and sing on the tree. All the children came running back. And with them, came the spring.\nA3] softly, gently, away, only\nA4] Subject = the giant object - the wall.\nA5] At first the giant drove the children away from his garden. He built a high wall around his garden. But after- wards he realised his mistake. And he invited children to play in his garden. He koncked down the wall. Moreover the giant started playing with the children.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46179,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is vaccine?','','','Vaccine is the process in which killed microorganisms are used to increase the immunity against the specific diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46180,318,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the black or white spot sometime seen on gunny bags?','','','The black or white spot are seen gunny bags are the fungi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46202,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The action of muscles is necessary for all kinds of movement.','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46203,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  The functions of the voluntary muscles do not depend upon our will.','','','Wrong – The functions of the voluntary muscles are depends upon our will.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46204,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  Muscles in our body does not works in groups.','','','Wrong – Muscles in our body works in groups.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46205,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The function of liver is the storage of glucose.','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46206,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  Pancreatic juice contains hydrochloric acid.','','','Wrong – Pancreatic juice contains various enzymes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46207,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  Ptyalin converts starch into a sugar.','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46208,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Stomach, intestine, arm, uterus','','','Arm - Because arm is a skeletal muscle and rest are all smooth muscles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46209,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Hydrochloric acid, ptyalin, pepsin, mucus.','','','Ptyalin Hydrochloric acid, pepsin, mucus are secreted by gastric juice while Ptyalin.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46210,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Bile, trypsin, lipase, amylase','','','Bile- secreted by gastric wall.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46211,319,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Deltoid, Biceps, flexors, canine ','','','Canine- it is a part of tooth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46212,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following : ','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – e), (4 – c), (5 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(46213,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How many muscles are there in human body?','','','There are more than 600 muscles in the human body. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46214,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What kind of movement is seen in the heart?','','','Involuntary movement is seen in the heart.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46215,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by myology?','','','The study of muscles is called myology.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46216,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the parts of alimentary canal?','','','The mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine are the parts of the alimentary canal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46217,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the enzymes present in the saliva?','','','Ptyalin or salivary amylase are the enzymes present in the saliva.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46218,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are the bones in our body joined to each other?','','','Bones in our body joined at the both end by ligaments.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46219,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mutual relationship between muscles and bones?','','','i) The muscles of our body are attached to the bones by means of tendons.\nii) Muscles protect the bones from the minor injuries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46220,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happened to the food we eat inside our body?','','','The food that we eat inside the body get digest due to mixing different juices.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46221,319,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which enzymes help in the digestion of proteins?','','','The protein digesting enzymes are as follows : \ni) Pepsin from stomach and\nii) Trypsin from pancreas. There are some additional enzymes in the small intestinal juice too. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46222,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Who is telling a lie?','','','i) Tongue is lying.\nReason: Taste had of tongue can tell different types of tastes like, sweet, bitter, sour etc.\nii) Large intestine is lying.\nReason: Large intestine is about 1.5 meter long.\niv) Appendix is lying.\nReason: It is first part of a large intestine. It does not make any function.','',1,0,0,NULL),(46223,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How many types of muscles are there? Which are those types?','','','i) There are three types of muscles.\nii) Those muscles are skeletal muscles, heart or cardiac muscles, and smooth muscles.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46224,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What causes the problem of acidity? What is its effects on the body?','','','i) Acid (HCl) is produced in the stomach for digestion of food.\nii) When the amount of acid is produced than what we required to digest food. It causes the acidity.\niii) Due to this stomach may suffer by ulcer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46225,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the different types of teeth. What is the function of each type?','','','i) There are four types of the teeth namely incisor, canine, premolar and molar.\nii) Incisors cut the food and convert it into small pieces. \niii) Canine teeth is to tear and shred food apart.\niv) Pre-molar are helpful and piercing food and grinds food into small particles.\nv) The function of molar is to chew and grinds into very small pieces.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46226,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Food becomes acidic in the stomach.','','','i) The gastric juice is secreted by the gastric glands in the stomach.\nii) The gastric juice contains the hydrochloric acid which mixed with the food.\niii) Therefore, food becomes acidic in the stomach.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46227,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Cardiac muscles are said to be involuntary muscles.','','','i) These muscles brings about the contraction and relaxation (beating) of the heart.\nii) The function of the cardiac muscles are not depend on our will. \niii) Therefore, cardiac muscles are said to be involuntary muscles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46228,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by organ system?','','','A group of organs which perform the different functions is called as an organ system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46229,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do muscles perform their functions?','','','i) Muscles in our body always perform their functions in groups.\nii) During the function some muscles get contract whereas some relax.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46230,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which part of our body are made up only of muscles?','','','Liver, stomach, heart, small intestine, large intestine are the part of our body made up of muscles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46231,319,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do we affect our health adversely?','','','Improper diet, lack of exercise and bad habits affect our physical health. If we resort to addictive habits like smoking, chewing of tobacco, drinking alcohol, it affects our health adversely.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46232,319,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Intoxicating substances should not be consumed.','','','i) Intoxicating substance like tobacco or tobacco products effects on the physical health.\nii) Consuming tobacco cause vomiting, nausea and headache.\niii) Mouth, pharynx, and alimentary canal and other organs of the digestive system cannot function properly due to consumption of intoxicating substances.\niv) Drinking alcohol, smoking effects on the nervous system.\nv) The particles of tobacco stick to the teeth, gums and skin of the mouth cavity and slowly causes injury.\nvi) Hence, intoxicating substances should not be consumed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46233,319,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Your muscles should be strong and efficient.','','','i) Different part of the body carry different function with the help of muscles.\nii) Activities like, running, walking, jumping, pulling can be done involving muscles.\niii) Lifting heavy loads or chopping wood with axe needs more strength.\niv) Therefore, our muscles should be strong and efficient.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46234,319,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sketch and label a diagram of the digestive system and describe it in your own words.','','','Answer : ','i) The digestive system consists alimentary canal and the digestive glands.\nii) Mouth, pharynx, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, rectum and anus are the parts of alimentary canal.\n1) Mouth :\ni) The process of digestion of food begins from mouth.\nii) Food is broken down by teeth into the mouth,\niii) The saliva is mixed with food which secreted\niv) The morsel is pushed by tongue into the pharynx.\n2) Pharynx : In the pharynx the oesophagus and trachea open.\n3) Oesophagus : Oesophagus pushes the food forward to the stomach.\n4) Stomach : It is bag like part of the alimentary canal. There are gastric gland in the stomach which secretes gastric juice. It has mild hydrochloric acid, pepsin and mucus. Due to churning food becomes semi-solid and slowly pushed into small intestine.\n5) Small Intestine : Small intestine is long and narrow tube like part of alimentary canal. It is about 6 meter long. In the small intestine digestion and absorption of nutrients is take place. The intestinal juice from wall of small intestine mixed with the food here.\n6) Large Intestine : It is about 1.5 metre long water is absorbed in the large intestine. There is appendix attached to the first part of the large intestine. The undigested food is pushed down into the anus which is thrown out the body.',0,0,1,NULL),(46235,319,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What would be happen if the cardiac muscles do not move.','','','i) Contraction and relaxation of the heart would stop.\nii) The function of the heart may stop causes to death.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46236,319,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram of digestive system of man.','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(46237,319,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What would be happen if : Food enters the stomach and the stomach muscles do not move.','','','i) Food in the stomach will not mix with gastric juice.\nii) Due to this food remains undigested in the stomach.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46238,319,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What soluble nutrients are formed after the digestion of food?','','','The proteins are broken down into amino acids. The complex carbohydrates are converted into simple sugars. The fats are converted into fatty acids and glycerol. Thus, amino acids, sugars and fatty acids-glycerol are the nutrients formed after the digestion of food.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46306,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is an eclipse?','','','When the sun, the moon and the earth comes in a straight the eclipse has taken place.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46307,324,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','When sunrays pass through a glass prism, what are the colours in the band of light seen on the other side of the prism?','','','Sunrays after passing through the prism show bands of seven colours, violet, indigo, blue, grey, yellow, orange and red. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46308,324,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is a shadow? How is a shadow formed?','','','The light emerging from a source of light illuminates the screen placed in front of it. But, if an object is placed between the source of light and the screen, the light rays get obstructed by the object. As that part of the screen does not get illuminated, it remains dark. This dark part is known as shadow.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46309,324,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why is a penumbra not obtained from a point source?','','','i) The faint part of shadow called penumbra.\nii) It is obtained by only small part of light source is covered by an object. \niii) If the size of the object is bigger as compare to point source of light. The object completely block the point source forming umbra on the screen.\niv) Extended source is essential to form the penumbra of the object.\nv) Hence, penumbra is not obtained from the point source. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46333,179,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Text Book','','Where do you lay the foundation of buildings?','','','The foundation of buildings is laid on land.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46334,179,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Text Book','','What need do we meet through breathing? How is this need met?','','','The need of oxygen to survive is met through breathing. For this purpose we breathe in air through our nose.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46335,179,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Text Book','','From where does the earth get light and heat?','','','The earth gets light and heat from the sun.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46336,179,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Where do you get water from?','','','Water is obtained from different sources at different places. In cities we get it from taps and pipes. In villages we get it from wells or rivers, lakes, ponds, rains, dams, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46337,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by diet?','','','The food containing different constituents that we eat for our survival is called diet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46338,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','For what purpose do we need food?','','','We need food for growth, development of the body and also for proper functioning of the body. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46339,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What substances do we use to give our food a sweet taste?','','','For giving sweet taste to our food, we can use sugar, jaggery, honey, fruit juice, dates, ripe bananas, sugarcane juice, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46340,328,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Of the food that we eat raw, which ones are sweet?','','','Ripe fruits, honey, milk, sugarcane juice, jaggery are the food items which are eaten raw and are sweet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46341,328,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the different tastes that foodstuffs have? How do we experience these tastes?','','','Sweet, sour, salty and bitter are the main tastes. \'Hot\' is not a taste, it is just the burning sensation of the tongue. We experience these different tastes due to taste buds present on the tongue.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46342,329,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a well labelled diagram for digestive system ','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(46343,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Do the clothes and shoes you wore as a baby fit you now?','','','No, as we grow we cannot fit into clothes or shoes that we used to wear when we were babies. These remain the same but we grow in size, height and weight.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46344,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Can an infant feed itself?','','','No, an infant cannot feed itself. It has to be fed by someone else.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46345,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Can a two-year old child fold clothes neatly?','','','No.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46346,330,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','When do they learn to do these tasks?','','','They can learn to do these tasks when they are 7 to 8 years old.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46367,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Concentrated acid and causticity of alkalis ','','','i) Dissolution of concentrated H,SO, in water generates large amount of heat.\nii) Therefore, for dilution of concentrated acid, acid is slowly added to water.\niii) If water is added to the concentrated sulphuric acid, it will produce enormous heat and cause explosion.\niv) The skin gets burnt when concentrated acid falls on skin, similarly their vapors if inhaled can be harmful to health.\nv) Alkalis like sodium hydroxide or potassium hydroxide are strong and caustic in nature.\nvi) Their concentrated solution burns skin as it decomposes the proteins in skin.\nvii) These are inflammatory to skin like acids.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46368,136,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give properties of acid.','','','i) Acids are sour in taste.\nii) Acid molecules contain hydrogen ion (H) as the main constituent.\niii) Acid react with metal to form hydrogen gas.\niv) Acid reacts with carbonates and liberates CO₂ gas.\nv) Blue litmus turns red in acid.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46369,136,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which substances are used to clean greenish stains on copper vessels and to shine blackish silver utensils?','','','Lemon, tamarind, vinegar, baking soda are used to clean greenish stains on copper vessels and to shine blackish silver utensils.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46370,136,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Which acid is used for getting chloride salts?','','','Hydrochloric acid (HCl) is used for getting chloride salts.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46405,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The change in which composition of matter does not change.','','','Physical change ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46406,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The gas we inhale in respiration.','','','Oxygen ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46407,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following :  The change in which the composition of matter is changed.','','','Chemical change ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46408,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following :  New substance formed as a result of the reaction.','','','Products ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46409,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following :  The main composition of shahabad ','','','Calcium carbonate ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46410,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following :  The gas involved in photosynthesis process.','','','Carbon dioxide ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46411,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following :  A fast and irreversible manmade chemical change.','','','Combustion of fuels','',0,0,0,NULL),(46412,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : A compound formed by means of one or more ionic bonds.','','','Ionic compound ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46413,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which change the chemical composition of original matter is changed?','','','The chemical composition of original matter in change in chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46414,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by reactants?','','','Substances written on the left side of the arrow are original substances that fake part in the reaction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46415,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by products?','','','New substances are formed as a result of the reaction are called products.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46416,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is ionic bond?','','','The chemical bond formed due to an electrostatic force of attraction between the oppositely charged cation and anion is called an ionic bond.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46417,137,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which atom duplet is completed by sharing of an electron?','','','In hydrogen atom, duplet is completed by sharing of an electrons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46418,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on :  Ionic bond','','','i) The chemical bond formed due to an electrostatic force of attraction between the oppositely charged cation and anion is called an ionic bond or an electrovalent bond.\nii) The compounds formed by means of one or more ionic bonds is called ionic compound.\niii) One ionic bond is formed due to the electrical charge +1 or -1 on an ion.\niv) An ion forms the same number of ionic bonds as its valency.\nv) The valency of an ion is equal to the magnitude of positive or negative charge on it.\nvi) The formation of an ionic compound sodium chloride from the elements sodium and chlorine is shown in the diagram.','',0,0,1,NULL),(46419,137,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Covalent bond','','','i) The chemical bond formed by sharing of valence electrons of two atoms with each other is called a covalent bond.\nii) A covalent bond is formed when atoms of two elements having similar properties combine.\niii) One covalent bond formed by sharing of valence electron each of two atoms with each other is called a covalent bond.\niv) The shared electrons become a common property of both the atoms.\nv) For example, formation of covalent bond in H₂ molecule.','',0,0,1,NULL),(46420,137,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Cleaning Shahabad tile with dilute hydrochloric acid is a chemical change.','','','i) Shahabad tile mainly contains calcium carbonate (lime stone).\nii) During the cleaning of Shahabad tile with dilute hydrochloric acid, the upper layer of the tile reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to form three products i.e., calcium chloride, carbon dioxide and water.\nHence, cleaning of Shahabad tile with dilute hydrochloric acid is a chemical change.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46444,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are potential and kinetic energies?','','','i) Kinetic energy:\n(a) Kinetic energy is a from of energy that results from an object\'s motion.\nb) It is calculated based on the objects mass and velocity.\nii) Potential energy:\na) Potential energy is a form of energy that results from an object\'s position or arrangement of its parts.\nb) It is a stored energy. It is calculated based on the object\'s mass and its height or distance.    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46446,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The specific heat capacity of Hydrogen is 3.42 cal/g⁰C and that of water is 1 cal/g⁰C. What do you understand by this?','','','Hydrogen requires 3.42 calories of heat to increase 1 gram of hydrogen through one degree whereas water requires 1 calorie of heat to increase 1 gram of water through one degree.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46447,138,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which use of the expansion of liquids in daily life you know?','','','i) In thermometers, the thermal expansion of mercury is used in temperature measurements.\nii) In recent times, alcohol is used in thermometer in place of mercury.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46474,139,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Frequency of sound waves','','','i) One compression and one rarefaction together form one cycle of the wave.\nii) The number of cycles formed in the air in one second will be decided by the number of times the prongs of the tuning fork (or any other vibrating body) vibrates back and forth i.e. the number of vibrations of the tuning fork in one second. \niii) Number of such cycles that are produced in the air (or other medium) per second is called as the frequency of the sound wave.\niv) Frequency is measured in Hertz. If one cycle is completed in one second, the frequency is said to be 1 Hz. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46475,139,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Sound production due to tuning fork ','','','i) A tuning fork is made of two prongs and a stem. Keeping the stem steady, if the prongs are struck, they start vibrating. \nii) If the stem is kept steady and the prongs are struck, the prongs are set into vibrations.\niii)  It means that they are set into periodic motion in forward and backward direction.\niv) If during the vibration, the prongs of the tuning fork go away from each other, the air outside the prongs is compressed and the pressure there increases.\nv) Such a state of high pressure is created in region A.\nvi)  The region in which air is at high pressure and high density is called compression.\nvii) In the next step of vibration, the prongs of the fork come close to (a) each other.\nviii)  In this case, the air molecules near the prongs get more space to move\naway from each other. \nix) As a result, the air pressure in this region (Region A) decreases.\nx)  Such region in which air is at low pressure and low density is called rarefaction. \nxi) Such a periodic motion of creates compression prongs and rarefaction and these are propagated away from the prongs. These are the sound waves.\nxii) When these waves reach our ear, the ear - drum vibrates; accordingly, specific signals reach our brain and sound is heard.','',0,0,1,NULL),(46476,139,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is sound generated in loudspeaker? ','','','i) Loudspeaker consists of a permanent magnet with a coil wound around it.\nii) When current flows through the coil, magnetic field is generated around the coil moves back and forth.\niii) Due to this magnetic field, the coil moves back and forth\niv) The frequency and the amplitude of the movement of the current flowing through the coil.\nv) As the coil moves, the conical screen of the loudspeaker, which is attached to the coil, also moves back and forth.\nvi) Due to back and forth motion of the loudspeaker screen, sound waves are produced, in air.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46477,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do you feel when you touch a sound producing school bell?','','','When we touch a sound producing school bell, we feel the vibrations of the bell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46478,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If sound waves are generated in air, what moves away from the source? Is it the air itself or the state of compression and rarefaction created in the air? \n','','','The compressions and rarefactions created in the air move away from the source.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46479,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do astronauts talk to each other when they are on the moon surface? \n','','','When astronauts are on the moon, they communicate with each other with the help of radio waves (the waves used in cell-phone technology).','',0,0,0,NULL),(46480,139,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Either speak a little loudly or sing a song or produce a humming sound like a bee and put your fingers on your throat. Do you feel some vibrations?','','','Yes, some vibrations can be felt.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46481,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Produce a sound ‘bho --- bhoo ---‘just like a dog-barking and ‘meow – meow ---’ just like a mewing cat. Carefully notice the tension on the vocal cords, when you produce these sounds. Do you feel that the tension on the vocal cords changes when you produce these two different sounds?','','','Yes, when these two different sounds are produced, I can feel the tension on the vocal cords and the difference in the tension in respect of two different sounds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46482,139,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','A ‘app’ may be available on cellphones to measure the loudness of sound in decibel. With the help of your teacher, use the app to measure the sound level of a sound from a loudspeaker at some public place. Measure the sound level at different distances from the loudspeaker. Do you observe some relation between the distance from the loudspeaker and the sound level?','','','Yes, as the distance from the loudspeaker increases the sound level decreases. This states that the loudness of the sound depends upon the distance of the listener from the source.\n   \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46483,139,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does Jal tarang produce sound?','','','i) Jal tarang is a musical instrument which consisting several glass cups filled with water in different level.\nii) The height of the air column in each glass is also different.\niii) When a cup is struck, waves are produced due to vibrations in the air column above the water level in the cup.\niv) The frequency of the generated wave depends on the height of the air column inside the glass cup.\nv) Therefore, the frequency of sound generated by each glass cup will also be different.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46532,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain the effect of following materials on environment and health : Thermocol','','','i) t takes a long time for natural degradation.\nii) Contact with thermocol for a long duration may lead to blood cancer like cancer like leukaemia, and lymphoma.\niii) If food cooked in thermocol is heated, styrene may dissolve in that food and will lead to health problems like problems of eyes, respiratory system, skin, digestive system\niv) Pregnant women may face miscarriage\nv) Liquid styrene may cause skin burns.\nvi) Many people opt for destroying plastic by burning which is more hazardous as it releases poisonous gases in the atmosphere.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46533,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write a short note on : Glass production','','','i) For glass production, mixture of sand, soda lime and small quantity of magnesium oxide is heated in furnace.\nii) Sand i.e. silicon dioxide melts at 1700°C. So pieces of discarded glass are added to lower the temperature to 850°C.\niii) When the mixture liquefies, it is heated up to 1500°C and immediately cooled.\niv) Due to sudden cooling, mixture becomes homogenous, amorphous and transparent instead of being crystalline.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46534,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give scientific reason : Burning thermocol is a hazardous method.','','','i) It takes a long time for natural degradation of thermocol.\nii) Hence people opt for destroying it by burning.\niii) However, it is still more hazardous as it releases poisonous gases in atmosphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46535,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Why most domestically useful items are replaced by plastic?','','','Plastic does not corrode and since light in weight, easy to carry. So useful items are replaced by plastic.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46536,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What material is wrapped around the items of glass during transportation to prevent from breaking?','','','Thermocol is wrapped around the items of glass during transportation as it is a shock absorbent material.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46537,141,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which type of glass is used in vehicles to avoid injuries in accidents?','','','i) Modern windshields are made from laminated safety glass for safety.\nii) Side and rear windows are made up of tempered glass. The tempering process makes the glass stronger.    \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46538,141,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the properties of glass.','','','The properties of glass are:\ni) Glass can be moulded into any shape as it turns soft on heating.\nii) Glass is a slow conductor of heat.\niii) Glass is a bad conductor of electricity, hence, it is used as an insulator in electric appliances.\niv) Density of glass depends upon its ingredients.\nv) As glass is transparent, most of the light passes through the glass. On the other hand, if glass contains oxides of chromium, vanadium or iron, large amount of light is absorbed in glass.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46560,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Ecosystem','','','i) The world around us is made of living and non-living factors termed as biotic and abiotic factors.\nii) There is a continuous interaction between these factors.\niii) The living organisms and their habitat and environment are correlated with each other.\niv) The structure so formed due to the reciprocal relationships is called an ecosystem.\nv) The biotic and abiotic factors and their interactions forms an ecosystem.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46561,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Biomes','','','i) In some regions on the earth, a large area has same climate and abiotic factors.\nii) The living organisms in these area are also similar.\niii) So a specific ecosystem develops in a vast area. Such large ecosystems are called Biomes.\niv) These biomes contain many small ecosystems.\nv) The earth itself is a vast ecosystem.\nvi) There are two types of Biomes present on the earth namely: Acquatic biomes and Land Biomes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46562,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Food web','','','i) An ecosystem that are interconnected at various levels. This is called food web.\nii) An organisms may be the prey for many other organisms.\niii) For example, an insect feeds upon leaves of various plants but the same insect is the prey for different animals like wall lizards, birds, etc.\niv) This forms an intricate web instead of a linear food chain. Such an intricate network is called as food web.\nv) Generally food webs are formed everywhere in nature.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46563,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Structure of ecosystem ','','','i) Living organisms need different types of abiotic factors and they have different capacities to adapt with those abiotic factors.\nii) Some microbes need oxygen while others do not require.\niii) Some plants thrive with sunlight, others grow well in shade.\niv) Each and every abiotic factor (air, water, soil, temperature, humidity) affects the biotic factors in an ecosystem.\nv) The proportion of abiotic factors always changes as biotic factors use or give out abiotic factors.\nvi) Every living organism in an ecosystem plays a particular role while living, moving in that ecosystem.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46564,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Characteristics of marine ecosystem','','','i) Marine plants grow in this ecosystem.\nii) Shallow water contains small fishes, prawns, feeding on algae.\niii) The central part of sea has less number of aquatic living organisms.\niv) Large fishes are secondary consumers.\nv) Ocean has a large amount of nutrients.\nvi) The bottom of oceans has more number of Decomposers. Dead bodies of plants, animals, waste materials are decomposed by bacteria.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46565,142,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart:','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(46583,143,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the major differences between a star and a planet?','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(46584,143,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Properties of Sun','','','i) Hydrogen makes up for 72% of the mass of the Sun.\nii) Helium makes up for 26% of the mass of the Sun.\niii) The rest 2% is made up of elements heavier than helium\niv) Mass of the Sun is 2 x 10³⁰ kg and the radius is 695700 km\nv) The surface temperature is 5800 K and the temperature at the center is 1.5 × 10⁷ K and the age is 4.5 x 10⁹ years.\nvi) The mass of the Sun is about 3.3 lakh times that of the earth and its radius is 100 times that of the earth.\nvii) Detailed study of the properties of the earth has led scientists to conclude that the properties of the Sun have remained unchanged over the past 4.5 billion years and the properties will slowly change after 4.5 billion years only.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46585,780,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ------------------ extends under the water also.','','','Hydrosphere ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46586,780,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------- are the smallest and ------------------ are the biggest fresh water sources.','','','Rills, Rivers','',0,0,0,NULL),(46587,780,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Huge blocks of ice floating in the sea are called -----------------------','','','icebergs ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46588,780,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Almost all the water vapour in the atmosphere is contained in the -------------------------','','','troposphere ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46589,780,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The layer of atmosphere which is next to troposphere is called ---------------------------','','','stratosphere ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46590,780,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Many lakes and wells get water from ----------------------------','','','springs ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46591,780,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Kangaroos are found only in -----------------------------','','','Australia ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46592,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The sun is not the cause of many natural processes on the earth.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46593,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : We can see rocks split by the tree roots.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46594,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Rills, brooks and streams join each other to form oceans.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46595,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The huge mass of ice slipping down the slope forms a glacier.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46596,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : One can see elephants and lions only in polar region.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(46597,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : A water body formed by water collecting naturally is called a lake.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46598,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Anil lives at the foot of a hill.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','hill ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46599,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Ria lives in a plateau region.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','plateau','',0,0,0,NULL),(46600,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : We can see many valleys near Lonavala.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','valleys ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46601,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Mount Everest is situated in the Himalaya mountain ranges.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','mountain','',0,0,0,NULL),(46602,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Ratan can easily climb the hill of Saptashrungi.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','hill','',0,0,0,NULL),(46603,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Give two examples of : Weather related events\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Formation of clouds                 ii) Rainfall            iii) Fog\niv) Winds                v) Storms\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46604,780,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Give two examples of : Sources of water.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Rills and brooks                 ii) Streams            iii) Wells and rivers\niv) Lakes and ponds            v) Glaciers \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46605,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Five oceans\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Atlantic, Pacific, Arctic, Indian Ocean, Southern Ocean.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46606,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Seven continents\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Africa, South America, North America, Antarctica, Asia, Australia, Europe.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46607,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Coastal water bodies\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sea, bay, gulf, strait, creek','',0,0,0,NULL),(46608,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Animal seen in the snowbound polar regions.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Polar bear ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46609,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the names of the envelopes of the earth.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Lithosphere, hydrosphere, atmosphere and biosphere are the envelopes of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46610,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a continents? How many continents are there on earth?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A vast continuous stretch of land on the earth is called a continent and there are seven continents on the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46611,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are landforms?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The unevenness of the land that gives different shapes to the land in the different places are called landforms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46612,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is waterfall formed?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When in some places, a river cascades down a sudden drop, it forms a water- fall.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46613,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a glacier?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The huge mass of ice that slips gradually over the slope at a very slow speed is called a glacier.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46614,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What makes the hydrosphere of the earth?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The water or ice that occupies the earth\'s surface, ground water and the water vapour present in the atmosphere together form the hydrosphere of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46615,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the main constituents of the atmosphere?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Nitrogen, oxygen, carbon dioxide and other gases present in small quantity. water vapour and dust particles are the main constituents of the atmosphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46616,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does ozone gas protect living beings?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The ozone gas present in the stratosphere absorbs the harmful ultraviolet ray present in the sunlight, due to which living beings are protected from those radiations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46617,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the source of water in the well?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Water is stored in the underground layers of rock. Bore wells and dug wells are used to extract this water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46618,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Where do you lay the foundation of buildings?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The foundation of buildings is laid on land.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46619,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What need do we meet through breathing? How is this need met?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The need of oxygen to survive is met through breathing. For this purpose we breathe in air through our nose.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46620,780,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','From where does the earth get light and heat?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The earth gets light and heat from the sun.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46621,780,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are clouds made up of?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Clouds are made up of fine droplets of water. \nii) The sun\'s heat evaporates the water to form vapours from the earth\'s surface. \niii) This water vapour rises high up in the atmosphere. At cool heights, it condenses forming fine droplets of water which in turn make the clouds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46622,780,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by ‘biosphere’?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Living things are seen in all the envelopes of the earth such as lithosphere, hydrosphere and atmosphere. \nii) All the living things together in these three envelopes form a biosphere. \niii) Thus, the plants, animals and microorganisms together make a biosphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46623,780,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where do all the weather-related phenomena take place?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) All the weather-related phenomena take place in the troposphere. \nii) Almost all the water vapour found in the atmosphere is in the troposphere. \niii) Therefore, formation of clouds, rains, storms and winds and all other weather-related phenomena take place here.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46624,780,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why have special arrangements to be made to ensure that passengers in airplane get enough air for breathing?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Aeroplanes fly at the higher levels of the troposphere. \nii) Here the air is rare. There can be less oxygen present which is necessary for breathing. \niii) Therefore, passengers who are in the aeroplanes need some arrangements to ensure that they get enough air for breathing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46625,780,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In which layer of the atmosphere do we see the rainbow?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We see rainbow in the layer of troposphere of the atmosphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46626,780,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Mountaineers carry oxygen in cylinders when they climb mountains that are more than 5000 m high. What could be the reason for that?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) As we climb up the heights, the air at the top becomes rare. \nii) At the height of 5000 m or more the air is very rare. \niii) The oxygen may be limited here. Therefore the mountaineers carry oxygen in cylinders when they climb mountains that are more than 5000 m high.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46627,780,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why are microorganisms important?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In every envelope of the earth there are microorganisms. \nii) These microorganisms perform important function of degradation. They also occupy important place in the food chain. \niii) The dead and decaying plant and animal matter is converted into soil and minerals by these microorganisms. \niv) If there were no microorganisms, huge piles of debris could have piled up everywhere on the earth. \nv) Therefore, microorganisms are very important organisms on the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46628,780,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of landforms you see in your surroundings and give description of any two of them.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In my surroundings there is small hillock and a playground. I can see mountain ranges at a far distance. Between these mountain ranges there are valleys and passes. Therefore the list of landforms: Hillock, plain, mountain, valleys, passes.\n1) Hillock : Hillock is a small sized mountain. The land on the hillock is sloping. It is easy to climb a hillock. We can plant trees on the slope of a hill to stop soil erosion.\n2) Plain : Playground is a plain. Plain is a flat part of the land. There is no slope or elevations on the plain. Since the entire surface is flat, we can use it for playing and other activities. We can plant trees around the plain. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46629,780,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Where do you get water from?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Water is obtained from different sources at different places. In cities we get it from taps and pipes. In villages we get it from wells or rivers, lakes, ponds, rains, dams, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46630,780,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Think about all the food stuffs obtained from the sea. Find more information and write ten lines about them.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Many marine organisms inhabit seas. Some of these form our food. \nii) Various edible varieties of fish are caught from the seas. \niii) There are good quality proteins and fatty acids in the fish. Therefore, it is a nutritive food. \niv) Shell fish varieties such as crabs, prawns, lobsters are favourite food items worldwide. Prawns are abundantly available in the Indian waters, therefore we get lots of foreign exchange through export of prawns. \nv) Bivalves, squids, oysters are also eaten as delicacies. \nvi) Algae from the seas are also eaten in the Asian countries, though not in India. \nvii) Agar is the substance made from marine algae which is used in jellies and puddings. \nviii) Salt is made from the sea water. \nix) In some countries potable water is made from the sea water by special processes. \nx) Young ones of prawns and fishes are used for the aquaculture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46631,780,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Write short note on : Evaporation \n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The heat changes water into vapour. This action is called evaporation. \nii) The sun\'s heat converts the water present on the earth\'s surface into water vapour. \niii) The water that has percolated into the soil also evaporates due to heat. All such vapour enters the atmosphere. \niv) This vapour condenses and comes in the form of rain back to the surface of the earth. \nv) Thus, evaporation is necessary for rainfall. The water cycle keeps on function due to evaporation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46632,780,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Write short note on : Condensation\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Condensation is the process in which vapour is changed into water. \nii) In nature, due to condensation, there is rainfall. \niii) The water vapour that reaches the upper layers of atmosphere, gets condensed and forms fine droplets of water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46633,780,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Write short note on : Water cycle\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The movement of water is continuously taking place from earth\'s surface to earth\'s atmosphere. \nii) The process of evaporation, condensation and rainfall is a continuous process. \niii) Due to the sun\'s heat, the water on the earth\'s surface is continuously evaporated. Since the water vapour is lighter than air, it rises high in the atmosphere. \niv) Due to cool temperatures at the heights, the water vapour condenses forming fine droplets of water. \nv) These droplets later form clouds. When small droplets of water become heavier and bigger, they descend down on the earth in the form of rain. Rainwater mixes with different water bodies. \nvi) The snowbound regions also show melting of ice and glaciers. This water also eventually mixes with ocean waters. \nvii) In this way water moves in the cyclic manner, which is known as water cycle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46634,780,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a labelled diagram showing water cycle.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(46635,780,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is the solution? Dark patches appear on the skin after exposure to the sun.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The harmful radiation of the sun can cause burning sensation to our skin. In order to avoid this we should not roam in the harsh sunlight. \nii) If it is necessary to leave the house, we should use an umbrella or cap. Wear cotton clothes that would cover the skin of hands and legs. \niii) Use of sun-screen cream is advisable. Covering the face with handkerchief can protect the delicate skin. \niv) After returning from outside. splash cold water on the face. \nv) This will help to reduce the temperature of the skin.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46636,780,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is the solution? Glaciers are melting on the entire earth.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Due to the increasing proportion of carbon dioxide, there is a danger of cli mate change. \nii) The entire earth is getting heated up. This phenomena is called global warming. \niii) Due to increased temperature, the glaciers are melting. This results in the discharge of water which flows down to seas and oceans. \niv) This is resulting in increase of sea level. Therefore, all the low-lying areas and the coastal regions are under the threat of submergence. \nv) Therefore, all of us should plant more and more trees and try to reduce the dangers of global warming. \nvi) We should also consume less energy in order to save the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46637,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','------------------ in our food give us the ability to resist diseases.','','','Vitamins ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46638,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Calcium makes our bones -------------------------','','','Strong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46639,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Foodstuffs that taste sweet contain various kinds of ---------------------------','','','Sugars ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46640,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','A diet that provides all the constituents of the food in the right proportion is called a ---------------------- diet.','','','Balanced ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46641,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sugar cane juice contains sugar called ------------------------','','','sucrose ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46642,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The condition is called -------------------- when there is a deficiency of iron in the body.','','','anaemia ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46643,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If food does not contain enough fibre, it can lead to -------------------------  ','','','constipation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46644,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Substances like starch, sugar and fibre are together called ----------------------------','','','Carbohydrates ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46645,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------------------------- makes our bones strong.','','','Calcium ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46646,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cereals like jowar, bajra, wheat, rice contain a lot of --------------------------','','','Starch ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46647,781,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','We get --------------- and -------------------- from leafy vegetables.','','','Vitamins and minerals  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46648,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : In sago and potato there is starch/protein.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Starch ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46649,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : When a piece of bread is slowly chewed, it tastes sour/sweet.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','sweet ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46650,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Fibre is also called roughage/stool.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','roughage','',0,0,0,NULL),(46651,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : The white part/egg yolk of the egg is a fatty substance.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','egg yolk ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46652,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Oil makes the paper opaque/translucent. \n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','translucent','',0,0,0,NULL),(46653,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Carbohydrates\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sugar, starch, fibres','',0,0,0,NULL),(46654,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Vitamins\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A, B, C, D','',0,0,0,NULL),(46655,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Sugars \n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sucrose, glucose','',0,0,0,NULL),(46656,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Minerals \n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','calcium, iron ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46657,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name any four cereals\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','maize, rice, wheat, barley, sorghum, millet, oat, rye and triticale etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46658,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which food items have sugars in them?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Jowar roti, bread, chickoo, butter, Jaggery etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46659,781,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which food constituents keeps the body warm?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','eggs, fish, ghee, butter etc. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46660,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give two sources of each of the following food constituents : Minerals\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Leafy green vegetables, fruits, sprouted pulses.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46661,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give two sources of each of the following food constituents : Proteins\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Paneer, eggs, fish','',0,0,0,NULL),(46662,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give two sources of each of the following food constituents : Starch\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Potato, sago, rice, bread','',0,0,0,NULL),(46663,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Match the following : ','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – c), (4 – e), (5 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(46664,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are carbohydrates?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The energy providing food constituents which consists of starch, sugars and fibres are called carbohydrates.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46665,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by malnutrition?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','If a person\'s diet lacks proper constituents of food for a long time, then this person does not get proper nourishment. Such condition is called malnutrition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46666,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is balanced diet?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The diet that constitutes the proteins, fats, carbohydrates, vitamins and minerals in right quantities is called a balanced diet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46667,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of fatty substances.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Cream, butter, ghee, oil, nuts, meat, egg yolk, are the examples of fatty sub- stances. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46668,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by diet?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The food containing different constituents that we eat for our survival is called diet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46669,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','For what purpose do we need food ?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We need food for growth, development of the body and also for proper functioning of the body. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46670,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What substances do we use to give our food a sweet taste?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','For giving sweet taste to our food, we can use sugar, jaggery, honey, fruit juice, dates, ripe bananas, sugarcane juice, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46671,781,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Of the food that we eat raw, which ones are sweet?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Ripe fruits, honey, milk, sugarcane juice, jaggery are the food items which are eaten raw and are sweet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46672,781,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Of what use are the sugars that we get from digestion of starch?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The sugars formed from digestion of starch are burned slowly. While burning they release energy. The sugars thus act as fuels. This energy is used for different kinds of work. It also keeps the body warm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46673,781,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the sources of fibre in our diet.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Bran in the flour of grains, whole grains and pulses, fruits, vegetables and especially their skin, sprouted pulses with their skins, green leafy vegetables are all sources of fibres in our diet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46674,781,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is fat stored in the body? When is it reused?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The excess of fats in the diet are stored in the body under the skin. \nii) When the fats are more in diet than required for the body they are stored at different places in the body.\niii) If food is not available for some time, then the body can get energy from the stored fats.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46675,781,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Sugar cane is sweet to taste.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sugar cane has sugar called sucrose. Therefore, sugar cane is sweet to taste.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46676,781,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Boiled egg is one of the best nourishing foods.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In a boiled egg there are proteins and fats. It also has vitamins A, B and D. \nii) There are many minerals such as calcium, phosphorus and potassium in it. \niii) Therefore, an egg provides good nourishment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46677,781,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Thread gets stuck between the teeth when we eat certain types of mangoes. Which kind of carbohydrates are they?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The threads that get stuck between the teeth while eating certain types of mangoes are fibres.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46678,781,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why are children told to drink milk every day?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Milk has almost all the food constituents. \nii) It has carbohydrates, sugars, fats and proteins. It is rich in vitamins A, B and D. \niii) The calcium content of milk is also quite high. Milk is easy to digest. It gives energy. \niv) The growth and development of the body is faster when milk is taken in our every day diet. \nv) Therefore, children are told to drink milk every day.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46679,781,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the different tastes that foodstuffs have? How do we experience these tastes?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sweet, sour, salty and bitter are the main tastes. \'Hot\' is not a taste, it is just the burning sensation of the tongue. We experience these different tastes due to taste buds present on the tongue.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46680,781,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The flour of any grains should not be sifted.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------','\n','','i) The flour contains particles of bran. When the flour is sifted, this bran is left behind on the sieve. \nii) The bran particles are larger and hence they are removed from flour when the flour is sifted. \niii) This bran contains vitamins and minerals and it also gives roughage. This bran is fibre which helps in movement of the food in the alimentary canal. \niv) Fibres in the bran helps to form stools from the undigested food. Because of such benefits, bran should not be removed, thus, the flour should not be sifted.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46681,781,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : An injury from the outside of the body does not at once cause damage to internal organs.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','There is layer of fat on the internal organs. This layer protects the delicate in- ternal organs. Therefore, even if there is an injury from the outside the body, it does not at once cause damage to the internal organs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46682,781,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why do we feel hungrier in winter than we do in summer?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In winter, the weather is cool. We need more calories to keep our body warm. \nii) Therefore, we need more energy. To get more energy, there should be enough nutrients in the body. \niii) When we feel hungrier, we take in more food. More starch, sugars and fats can give us more energy to keep our body warm. \niv) In summer season, such need is not there. Thus we feel hungrier in winter than we do in summer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46683,781,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why do we use a padding of cloth under a mortar when we place it on the floor and pound something in it?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) While pounding the substance in a mortar, there could be jerks to the floor. \nii) The tiles of the floor can break. \niii) Therefore, a padding of cloth has to be placed between the mortar and the floor to absorb these shocks or jerks. \niv) This offers protection to the floor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46684,781,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Do we get all the different constituents of food from a meal that consists of a green veg thalipeeth eaten with yoghurt?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In the flour of thalipeeth there is mixed flour of rice, daals and pulses. \nii) This flour gives carbohydrates and proteins. While roasting the thalipeeth, oil or butter is applied to the fry pan. \niii) Thus, even fats are present in it. If greens are added to the flour, there would be vitamins, minerals like iron and roughage added to the flour. \niv) Curds also provide proteins, carbohydrates, fats and vitamins. \nv) Hence meals consisting of green veg thalipeeth eaten with yoghurt is a complete meal with all the food constituents.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46685,781,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which food constituents do we get from the ingredients used to make bhel?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The kurmura in the bhel is a carbohydrate. The grams and peanuts are rich in proteins. \nii) The shev is made of gram flour and hence it also contains proteins. Since it is fried, there is good content of fats too in it. \niii) Chutney made from dates have sugars and minerals like iron. Pudina used in bhel is rich in vitamins, minerals and high in fibre content. \niv) If lemon or raw mango is used, the vitamin C content is also high. \nv) However, if bhel is purchased from a roadside shop, it may have microorganisms and contaminants and it can cause illness.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46686,781,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? The body requires an adequate quantity of proteins.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Body requires lots of proteins for body building. There is constant wear and tear of the body. \nii) That is also taken care of by proteins. Therefore, large amount of proteins should be in diet.\niii) For vegetarian people, different daals, pulses, groundnuts, grams, paneer, tofu, soyabean have to be included in diet. \niv) For non-vegetarian people, fish, meat, eggs are excellent sources of proteins.\nv) Everyday diet should include sprouted pulses. The milk and milk products are also rich in protein, caesin.\nvi) Such diet would give ample proteins along with vitamins.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46687,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When we inhale the air from outside it goes into -------------------','','','trachea ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46688,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The small air sacs in the lungs are called ------------------------','','','alveoli ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46689,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','-------------------- gas is transported to all parts of the body.','','','Oxygen ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46690,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Smoking can cause life-threatening disease like ------------------------','','','lung cancer ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46691,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The lower end of the alimentary canal is ---------------------------','','','anus ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46692,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the stomach food is -----------------------------','','','churned ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46693,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The faces are collected in the ---------------------- for some time.','','','rectum ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46694,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Some substances act as ------------------ and yield ------------------ to carry out all the tasks.','','','fuels and energy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46695,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maintaining the ------------------- is the function of the brain.','','','co - ordination ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46696,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The stomach is like a -----------------------','','','bag ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46697,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','--------------------- is necessary for the breathing.','','','oxygen ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46699,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The bolus passes through the ------------------------','','','oesophagus ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46700,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ---------------------- and the network of blood vessels together form the circulatory system.','','','heart ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46701,782,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ------------------- system expels the waste substances that are formed in the body.','','','excretory ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46702,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : We get oxygen from the air.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46703,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Saliva helps in the digestion of food.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46704,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Drinking alcohol does not effects on the body.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46705,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : The blood is continuously forward through the blood vessels.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46706,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Choose appropriate word in the bracket : Respiration takes place because of its up and down movement. (circulation, Trachea, Diaphragm)\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','Diaphragm','',0,0,0,NULL),(46707,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Choose appropriate word in the bracket : The process of keeping the blood flowing continuously throughout the body. (circulation, Trachea, Diaphragm)\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','circulation','',0,0,0,NULL),(46708,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Choose appropriate word in the bracket : Air that enters through the nose passes into this tube. (circulation, Trachea, Diaphragm)\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','Trachea','',0,0,0,NULL),(46709,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Nose, Trachea, Stomach, diaphragm\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','Stomach ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46710,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Mouth, Lungs, Stomach, Small intestine.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','Lungs ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46711,782,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by breathing?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','The inhalation and exhalation together called breathing  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46712,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(46713,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the parts of respiratory system.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Nose, trachea, lungs, bronchi and diaphragm are the parts of respiratory system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46714,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where does the process of digestion begins?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The process of digestion begins in the mouth. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46715,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which system co-ordinates all the functions of the body?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The nervous system co-ordinates all the functions of the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46716,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Respiration \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The process of taking oxygen from air and give out carbon dioxide through the nose is together called respiration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46717,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Circulation of blood\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The process of keeping the blood flowing through all parts of the body is called blood circulation. The blood vessels carry blood away from the heart.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46718,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is digestive juice?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Digestive juice is the secretion that brings about digestive processes after eating food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46719,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a gland? Which are the digestive glands?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','An organ that secretes certain substances is called a gland. Salivary glands, liver and pancreas are the digestive glands.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46720,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by a system in a body?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A group of organs that work together to carry out a particular function of the body is called a system in a body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46721,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by circulation of blood?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The process of keeping blood flowing through all the parts of the body is known as circulation of blood.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46722,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the function of the excretory system?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The excretory system expels the waste substances that are formed in the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46723,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the nervous system made up of?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Nervous system is made up of brain and the network of nerves that run through- out the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46724,782,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the function of the skeletal system?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The skeletal system gives shape and support to the body and also gives protection to the vital organs of the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46725,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the systems that carry out the different functions of the body.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Respiratory system, digestive system, circulatory system, nervous system, skeletal system and excretory system are some of the systems that carry out different functions of the body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46726,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do the actions of inhalation and exhalation take place?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','There is diaphragm in between the thoracic cavity and abdominal cavity. When the diaphragm moves downward, we inhale automatically. When the diaphragm moves upwards, air in the lungs is pushed out.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46727,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill-effects of smoking on health?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Smoking leads to different diseases of respiratory organs. Lung cancer can also be caused due to smoking. \nii) The solid particles of tobacco smoke from cigarette or bidi forms a sticky layer inside the alveoli. \niii) This reduces the amount of oxygen supplied to the body which causes ill-effects on health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46728,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the functions of stomach?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Stomach is like a bag in which the food is churned. \nii) Some digestive actions take place in the stomach due to the digestive juices secreted here. \niii) Some disease producing germs are also destroyed here. \niv) Food is changed into slurry and then it is passed on to the small intestine.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46729,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which internal organs are vital and work ceaselessly?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The brain, the heart, the lungs, the digestive system, the diaphragm are some of the organs which are vital and they work ceaselessly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46730,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which organ maintains coordination of the body? How is it maintained?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The brain maintains the coordination between all the body parts. There is a network of nerves that connects the brain with all the different body parts. The brain helps in maintenance of the body functions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46731,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why do we call saliva a digestive juice?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Saliva is the juice secreted by the salivary glands. Saliva helps to make bolus from the food that we eat. Saliva also contains a starch digesting enzyme. This enzyme turns starch into sugar. Therefore, saliva is called a digestive juice.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46732,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the organs that about respiration.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Organs that carry out respiration are the nose, the pharynx (throat), the diaphragm, the trachea, the branches and sub-branches of the trachea, the lungs and the alveoli.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46733,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the organs of the digestive system.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The alimentary canal and associated digestive glands together form the digestive system. \nii) The alimentary canal consists of the oesophagus, the stomach, the small intestine and the large intestine. \niii) The digestive glands are salivary glands present in the mouth, the liver and the pancreas. \niv) The teeth and tongue in the mouth help in food intake.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46734,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which systems work together to provide the body with energy?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The lungs, the heart, the stomach, the intestines, the brain all of these internal organs work together to provide energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46735,782,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a well labelled diagram of the respiratory system. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(46736,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Describe how the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide gases take place in the lungs.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) At the time of respiration, the external air reaches up to the alveoli in the lungs.\nii) The oxygen in the air enters the blood vessels which surround the alveoli. \niii) Through the network of blood vessels oxygen is supplied to the entire body. At the same time, carbon dioxide is brought by the circulating blood into the alveoli. \niv) From here carbon dioxide is given out of the body through exhalation. In this way exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide gases take place in the alveoli of the lungs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46737,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which digestive organs are included in the digestive system? Which digestive glands are associated with the alimentary canal?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Alimentary canal is a long and flexible tube present in the body. \nii) Alimentary canal begins with the mouth and ends in anus. In mouth, the teeth, saliva and tongue help in forming a bolus which is easier for swallowing. The anus helps in throwing faces out of the body. \niii) Alimentary canal is of varying diameter in different parts. Oesophagus, stomach, small intestine and large intestine are the main parts of the alimentary canal. All the above are called digestive organs. \niv) Salivary glands, liver and pancreas are the digestive glands associated with the alimentary canal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46738,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the difference between small intestine and large intestine.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The small intestine is 7 metres long whereas the large intestine is one and half metre long. \nii) In the small intestine, the food is completely digested and absorbed in the blood. \niii) In the large intestine, the water from the digested food is reabsorbed. The remaining undigested matter is called faces and is stored for some time. \niv) In the small intestine, many digestive juices are mixed with the food. While in the large intestine, no secretions are mixed with the food. \nv) From the small intestine, the undigested food enters the large intestine. From the large intestine, the undigested faucal matter is thrown out of the body through the anus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46739,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is circulation of blood necessary?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Through blood circulation, the oxygen is sent to the entire body. Similarly, the absorbed food nutrients are also sent to all the body parts. \nii) Carbon dioxide produced in the body is carried by the blood to the lungs for expelling it out. \niii) For producing energy by burning nutrient fuels, circulation of blood is continuously needed. \niv) Thus. for survival of life, blood circulation is necessary.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46740,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why do we sometimes choke suddenly while eating?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The upper ends of both the oesophagus and the trachea open in the throat next to each other, there is a flap that covers the trachea. \nii) When the food is swallowed, the trachea remains closed. But if we eat in a hurry, the food enters the trachea. \niii) This causes choking. But due to coordination of the brain, we immediately start coughing and the food particles are expelled out of the trachea.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46741,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How is the air that we inhale purified in our body?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There are tiny, fine hair-like outgrowths called cilia inside the nose from where the air passes inside. \nii) Larger dust particles get caught in these hairs. The air going inside the trachea thus gets filtered. \niii) There is also a layer of a sticky substance called mucous. \niv) The dust and smoke particles stick to the mucous. \nv) Thus, the air that we inhale gets purified before it reaches the lungs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46742,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How do we know that we are now hungry and that we should now eat?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the level of energy giving substances present in the blood falls down, our brain can understand it. \nii) The brain tells us that \'we are hungry and we should eat something to restore this level. \niii) Thus, the coordinating brain gives us order to eat.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46743,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How do the salivary glands come to know that there is food in the mouth and it is time to secrete saliva?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Through the nerves present in the mouth, the brain comes to know that there is food in the mouth. \nii) The brain then gives orders to the salivary glands to secret the saliva. \niii) Thus, when there is food in the mouth, automatic secretion of saliva takes place.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46744,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What makes respiration and blood circulation go on all the time, and the processes of digestion happen at the right time?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','All the systems and their functioning is under the control and coordination of the brain. The brain keeps all the processes working in normally functioning body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46745,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? A person fainted and a crowd of people has surrounded him.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When a person faints, he should be immediately given clean and fresh air. Therefore, ask the crowd to move away. Then splash cold water on the face of this man. If he is not reviving, take him immediately to the doctor with the help of others. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46746,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the diagram of the digestive system.','','','Diagram : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(46747,782,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a well labelled diagram for digestive system \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(46748,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Achieving control of one’s movements to learn to do something new is called learning a skill/lesson.','','','learning a skill','',0,0,0,NULL),(46749,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Doing tasks more easily and without making mistakes is called efficiency/knowledge.','','','efficiency','',0,0,0,NULL),(46750,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : With age our physical/financial strength also increases.','','','physical','',0,0,0,NULL),(46751,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Young ones of animals learn their skills from their father/mother.','','','mother','',0,0,0,NULL),(46752,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Our height grows till we are 18/25 years old.','','','18 years old ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46753,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : The growing age/old age is the time to recognize one’s abilities and to develop the skills.','','','growing age','',0,0,0,NULL),(46754,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Our look/personality develops through our efforts and interests.','','','personality','',0,0,0,NULL),(46755,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ability to do something well is called ------------------------','','','skills ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46756,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','During our growth height and -------------------- increases.','','','weight ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46757,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Lack of food constituents in the diet leads to ----------------------','','','Malnutrition ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46758,783,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','We should avoid the ------------------- and their ill effects.','','','addictions   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46759,783,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : A baby gradually learns to perform newly learnt tasks without making mistake.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46760,783,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : At birth, we already have all skills.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46761,783,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : We cannot manage all our tasks entirely by ourselves. \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46762,783,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : Our height increases from birth till we grow.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46763,783,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Ravi likes geometry he takes an extra effort to learn new things in geometry.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Good ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46764,783,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Ganu has started consuming tobacco.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Bad ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46765,783,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Ahmed helps his father in repairing the bicycle.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Good ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46766,783,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Manisha shouts at everybody.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Bad ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46767,783,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Kailas has developed an expertise in drawing.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Good ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46768,783,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What changes does take place during the growth?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','During the growth height and weight increases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46769,783,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are skills?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The ability to do something well is called skills.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46770,783,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by learning of skills?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Achieving control of one\'s movements to learn to do somethings new is called learning a skills.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46771,783,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by a diet?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','All that we eat during a day is together called a diet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46772,783,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','From where do we learn the skills?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Most of the skills we learn from parents, teachers and others.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46773,783,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Do the clothes and shoes you wore as a baby fit you now?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','No, as we grow we cannot fit into clothes or shoes that we used to wear when we were babies. These remain the same but we grow in size, height and weight.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46774,783,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Can an infant feed itself?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','No, an infant cannot feed itself. It has to be fed by someone else.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46775,783,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Can a two-year old child fold clothes neatly?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','No ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46776,783,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','When do they learn to do these tasks?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','They can learn to do these tasks when they are 7 to 8 years old.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46777,783,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by heredity?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Heredity means the characteristic traits and features of parents being passed to their children is called heredity. Due to heredity, family members also show similarities.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46778,783,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name any three skills you have acquired.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We have acquired the following skills.\nskills we have acquired such as skipping on a rope, combing our own hair, learning a song, swimming, cycling, giving a speech, playing a field game, climbing a tree etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46779,783,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by physical growth?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Growth in height and weight of a child is called physical growth. The efficiency and strength of the body also rises due to physical growth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46780,783,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are necessary things for good developing  good personality?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Nutritious food \nii) Adequate exercise \niii) To avoid addictions and their ill- effects \niv) Developing good hobbies \nv) To develop new skills. These are all the things which cause development of good personality.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46781,783,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe good diet.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','During growing years, one should always have a good diet so that all the abilities and characteristics develop to fullest extent. Inadequate nutrition during growing years cause malnutrition and stunted growth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46782,783,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the factors of supporting growth?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Following are the necessary things for perfect and good development.\ni) Nutritious food                          ii) Proper exercise \niii) Habit of studying well            iv) Avoiding addictions \nv) Developing hobbies \nvi) Participation in sports and other new skills.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46783,783,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How many years does it take for the young ones of the tiger/elephant to start living independently?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Tiger\'s cubs are independent at the age of one and half years old. But they stay with their mother. But by the end of two and half years of age, they separate from the mother and start an independent life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46784,783,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What difference do we see between children in pre-primary classes and children in Std. V?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Children studying in the pre-primary classes are 3 to 4 years old. \nii) Their height and physical strength is not yet fully developed. They cannot go to school alone, on their own. \niii) Their understanding and maturity is also less. If they run, they may fall.\niv) Children from Standard V are 9 to 10 years old. They are a little grown-up. \nv) Their physical and mental capacity is increased. Their height has also increased. \nvi) They can commute alone to the school and back home. They play in the playground in the open.\nvii) They can make calculations and can handle money. They have many more skills as compared to the children in the pre-primary class.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46785,783,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What changes takes place in us from birth till adulthood?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) At birth our weight is 2 to 3 kg. By the time we reach adulthood, it becomes 50 to 60 kg for men and 45 to 50 kg for women. \nii) Our height also increases four to five times. \niii) There is no strength in arms and legs during infancy, but by young age arms and legs become strong and can perform many actions. \niv) The brain develops from birth till the age of 6. Later the structure of the brain becomes very complex. \nv) At the age of 12 to 14 many other major changes take place in the body. In order to become a father or a mother in the future, the body undergoes corresponding changes in the structure and function.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46786,783,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does our personality grow?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Every person is different and unique. The physical and mental makeup of every person is different than the others. \nii) This largely depends upon heredity. By constant interests and efforts, our personality develops. \niii) During the growing years, we also learn to understand about what is good and what is bad. \niv) When we put all the good thoughts into practice then our personality grows.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46787,783,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What skill do we already have before we learn to ride a bicycle?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) We should have enough strength in legs to paddle the bicycle. \nii) For balancing the cycle, the entire body should be agile, strong and fit. \niii) We should be able to turn the handle of the bicycle whenever we are riding it. We should be cautious of traffic moving in both the directions. \niv) We therefore turn the neck quickly to look around. \nv) Thus, complete control over legs, hands, neck and eyes is an appropriate skill required to ride a bicycle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46788,783,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Suman wishes to run a restaurant of her own in the future. Which of the skills she is learning today will be of use in her future occupation?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Suman will need many people to run a restaurant. She should have the ability to interact and work together with the staff. \nii) She should also have information about different recipes, market rates of different foodstuffs, liking of customers, responsibility to keep the restaurant clean and hygienic. \niii) Right from now, she should acquire skills of buying groceries and other provisions, cooking different dishes of food, etc. \niv) Even now when she visits the hotels, she should keenly observe the working and organizing of the hotels.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46789,783,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Kabir wants to be a professor of zoology. What can he do today to prepare for that? \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In order to be a professor of zoology, Kabir should have interest in science subjects. \nii) Kabir should gather information from different magazines, books and pictorial encyclopaedia. \niii) He should develop the habit of observing different animals and birds. Zoology is taught in the science stream, so he needs to concentrate on studies in order to get admission in science stream. \niv) For becoming a professor, Kabir will have to acquire degree of M.Sc. and then Ph.D. For this, he should be able to study for long hours and with all the devotion. \nv) Since childhood, Kabir needs to be inclined towards studies in order to become a professor in the future.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46790,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Diseases are caused by -----------------------','','','microorganisms ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46791,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The germs of influenza are present  in the ------------------- or saliva of the patient.','','','spit ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46792,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maintaining cleanliness everywhere is ----------------------','','','beneficial ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46793,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- is spread through the bite of mosquito.','','','Malaria ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46794,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Clothes and utensils of a patient are washed with ---------------------.','','','germicides ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46795,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----------------- is added to community sources of water to kill the germs.','','','Bleaching powder ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46796,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------ are spread on stagnant water to prevent mosquito breeding.','','','insecticides ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46797,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Our body develops resistance to a particular disease due to ---------------------','','','vaccination ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46798,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Soon after a baby is born, she is given the ------------------ vaccine.','','','tuberculosis ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46799,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mass media is used for raising --------------------- about health and hygiene issues.','','','public awareness ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46800,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Diseases if the throat and the chest spread through -------------------------','','','Air ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46801,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----------------------- spread malaria.','','','Mosquitoes','',0,0,0,NULL),(46802,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Diseases that spread from one person to other called --------------------- diseases.','','','Infectious ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46803,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- is an excellent method of preventing disease.','','','Vaccination ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46804,784,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','--------------------- are very-very small in the size.','','','Micro - organism','',0,0,0,NULL),(46805,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : Intestinal diseases spread through air.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46806,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : Some diseases are caused by the anger of the gods.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46807,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say True or False : All micro-organisms cause the diseases.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46808,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Diarrhea spread through air. \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46809,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Tuberculosis is an airborne disease.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46810,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Primary Health centers have been established at the village level.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46811,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Cholera spread through contaminated water.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46812,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Micro-organisms are present at everywhere.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46813,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False :  Some micro-organisms are useful.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46814,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : You should not cover your mouth while sneezing or coughing, it makes handkerchief dirty.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46815,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Always keep food items covered.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46816,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Lice and fleas do not spread diseases.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46817,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : The spittle of a person who has tuberculosis is collected in a vessel.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46818,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : During epidemic many people from a particular area get infected at the same time.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46819,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Ringworm, typhoid, cholera, diarrhea\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','Ringworm','',0,0,0,NULL),(46820,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Tuberculosis, diphtheria, jaundice, flu\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','jaundice','',0,0,0,NULL),(46821,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Diarrhea, Malaria, Jaundice, cholera\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','Diarrhea','',0,0,0,NULL),(46822,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of infectious diseases\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Infectious diseases : Flu, cold, ringworm, scabies, chickenpox','',0,0,0,NULL),(46823,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of diseases that spread through air\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Tuberculosis diphtheria, cold and cough, swine flu','',0,0,0,NULL),(46824,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of diseases that spread a water\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Cholera, diarrheas, typhoid ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46825,784,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of vaccines\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------','','','polio ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46826,784,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the medium through which spread the diseases. \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Air, water, food and insects are the medium through which diseases spread.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46827,784,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does scabies and ringworm spread?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Scabies and ringworm are spread through direct contact.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46828,784,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which programmes has been undertaken at the national level to prevent infectious diseases?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Community welfare programme has taken to prevent infectious diseases and epidemics.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46829,784,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the functions of micro-organisms?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The micro-organisms converts the milk into yoghurt and the fermentation of the batter for idlies, dosas etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46830,784,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does given the polio vaccine?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The polio vaccine is given orally.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46831,784,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is an infectious disease?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The disease caused by specific microorganism is an infectious disease. It is transmitted from one person to another through infections.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46832,784,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What happens when there is an epidemic of a diseases?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the epidemic of a disease is spread, many people from a particular area become ill with the same disease at the same time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46833,784,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which preventive measures are done against epidemic malaria?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Malaria is spread through mosquito bites. Hence to prevent epidemic of malaria, mosquitos are eradicated. For we ensure that there is no stagnant water. If it is there then this water is sprayed with insecticides.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46834,784,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? You are very hungry but the food has been left uncovered.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','If the food is left uncovered, it will be contaminated with dirt, dust and flies. Many microorganisms may be present in them. Such food can cause infections. Therefore, such food should not be eaten. If we are hungry we can eat packed biscuits or fruits like, banana which can be peeled and eaten. If food is boiling hot, we can consume otherwise, even if we are very hungry, we should not eat uncovered food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46835,784,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Classify the diseases given below as diseases that spread through food, through water and through air : Malaria, typhoid, cholera, tuberculosis, jaundice, gastro, diarrhea, diphtheria.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a) Through air – Tuberculosis, diphtheria\nb) Through food – Typhoid, cholera, jaundice, gastro, diarrhoea\nc) Through water – Typhoid, cholera, jaundice, diarrhoea\nd) Malaria spreads through mosquito bite','',0,0,0,NULL),(46836,784,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the mediums of the spread of diseases?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Contaminated air, water, food are mediums of the spread of disease. Lice, flea and mosquito also spread certain diseases. In case of skin diseases, direct contact through touch and contact with contaminated clothes or utensils can also cause disease.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46837,784,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is a vaccination?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Vaccination is the process in which the vaccine is given to the person or child through injection or as an oral dose. \nii) This vaccine builds resistance against a certain disease. When the disease-causing germs enter our body, we already have the ability to fight against these germs. So we do not get that disease. \niii) Due to vaccination, the out- break of an epidemic can be controlled and disease prevention can be done. \niv) Children are given vaccinations for many diseases as their resistance power is weak. \nv) Community programmes for vaccination are also done to control epidemics.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46838,784,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of the vaccinations that are given to a new born baby.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) BCG or anti tuberculosis vaccine. \nii) Triple vaccine: Given against whooping cough, diphtheria and tetanus. \niii) Polio oral vaccine is given to build resistance against polio.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46839,784,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What care is taken in case of tuberculosis patient?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Patient suffering from tuberculosis is kept in the separate ward in the hospital. This is called quarantine. \nii) Clothes and utensils used by these patient are disinfected. \niii) Spittle of a person is collected in a vessel and covered with phenyl which is a germicide. \niv) Patients are given suitable and proper medicines and injections.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46840,784,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the benefit of covering food?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When food is kept covered, it remains safe.\nii) Dust and dirt does not contaminate it. The houseflies do not contaminate the food by sitting on it. \niii) The germs of different diseases are not mixed with the food. \niv) By covering the food, we can prevent spreading of diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46841,784,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which method of preventing the breeding of mosquitoes. Will you recommend for your surrounds. Not allowing water to stagnate or spraying insecticides on the water? Why?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In the stagnant water mosquitoes breed. \nii) Mosquitoes spread diseases like malaria, dengue and elephantiasis. \niii) Therefore, stagnant water should not be allowed to remain in our surroundings. \niv) One can also use insecticides to destroy mosquitoes. But this would also create insecticide pollution. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46842,784,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the epidemic of jaundice spread?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Epidemic of jaundice spreads through contaminated water and food. \nii) The microorganisms causing jaundice are present in the faecal matter of the patient. \niii) If such faeces gets mixed with river or lake water, the water gets contaminated. \niv) If such water is used by other people for drinking, the germs cause infection to all of them. In this way if the water source gets contaminated, many people get jaundice at a time. \nv) Thus, the epidemic of jaundice is spread.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46843,784,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : When there is an epidemic of cholera, we should drink boiled water.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Germs of cholera spread through faeces of a patient. They pass into water or food. \nii) If such contaminated water is used for drinking, others may get the infection of cholera. \niii) Therefore, during epidemic of cholera, water should be boiled. Boiling destroys the germs in the water and therefore we are protected against the germs. \niv) In order to keep our health safe, we should drink boiled water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46844,784,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : We should not allow puddles of water to stand in our surroundings.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Stagnant water of puddles is a breeding ground for mosquitoes. Germs causing malaria are transmitted through mosquitoes bite from a patient to other people. \nii) Epidemic of such diseases will spread if mosquitoes are abundant. \niii) Therefore, to control infections of malaria and dengue we should eradicate mosquitoes. \niv) Hence, we should not allow puddles of water to stand in our surroundings.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46845,784,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Vaccination is done at the time of pilgrimage.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) During pilgrimage, a large number of people come together. \nii) Some of them may be infected or are carriers of certain diseases. \niii) Many a times there is uncovered and un- hygienic food at such events.\niv) People take holy bath together in contaminated water. All these facts can cause the spread of germs. \nv) In order to control spread of diseases and to protect pilgrims and local people, vaccination is given to all during such pilgrimage.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46846,784,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : To spit in a public place is forbidden by law.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) By spitting, the surroundings become dirty. Many germs of diseases also spread through spittle.\nii) To prevent spread of such disease and to protect the health of people, spitting in a public place is forbidden by law.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(46847,784,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Mouth and nose is to be covered while coughing or sneezing.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When we cough or sneeze millions of germs may find their way into the air. \nii) Different diseases can be spread through such acts. \niii) By covering the mouth and nose while coughing or sneezing, we protect the health of ours as well as that of others.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46848,784,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Small babies are given vaccination.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) By vaccination, the resistance against different diseases can be built up. \nii) Babies are protected against hazardous and lethal diseases. \niii) Even if the germs of the disease enter the body, baby can defend itself. \niv) Therefore, small babies are given vaccination.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46849,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ---------------------- of particles in each of the substances is different.','','','arrangement ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46850,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','All substances around us are made up of tiny -------------------------','','','particles ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46851,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In our body, energy is obtained by the ----------------------- of certain substances.','','','burning ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46852,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Energy in the form of motion is called -------------------- energy.','','','kinetic energy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46853,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Energy from ---------------------- is stored in the food substances which the plants prepare.','','','sunlight ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46854,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In today’s world the main source of energy are ----------------- and ---------------------.','','','fuels and electricity ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46855,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Many power stations use fuels to produce ------------------.','','','electricity ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46856,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When we burn fuels the stored energy in them is converted into -------------------- energy.','','','heat ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46857,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','All substances are made up of ------------------------','','','tiny particles ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46858,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- is required to do work.','','','energy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46859,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','All moving things have --------------------- energy.','','','kinetic ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46860,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Plants used ------------------- to prepare their own food.','','','sunlight ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46861,785,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Water is found in nature in ------------------ states.','','','three ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46862,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : We can see fine particles of the substances with our naked eyes.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46863,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Objects are made from substances.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46864,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sunlight, wind and water are exhaustible sources of energy.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46865,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sound is a type of energy.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46866,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Different substances have a different property.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46867,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : petrol, diesel, CNG, kerosene\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','CNG ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46868,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : sunlight, wind, water, coal\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','coal ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46869,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Nitrogen, kerosene, oxygen, carbon dioxide\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','kerosene','',0,0,0,NULL),(46870,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Hamida rides a bicycle to come to school.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Muscular energy','',0,0,0,NULL),(46871,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Aunty boils the curry on the gas stove.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Heat energy','',0,0,0,NULL),(46872,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : We watch TV in the evening.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Electricity ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46873,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Saplings of vegetables are in the sunlight.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Solar energy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46874,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : It takes time for the food to get cooked in the solar cooker.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Solar energy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46875,785,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Twings of the tree move due to wind.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wind energy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46876,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by peelav?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The minute particles of an object is called peelav.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46877,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the states of substances?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','solid, liquid and gases are the states of substances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46878,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by kinetic energy?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Energy in the form of motion is called kinetic energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46879,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the forms of energy?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Electricity, sound, heat and light are the forms of energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46880,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are solar batteries?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Batteries in which the electricity is produced by using sunlight are called solar batteries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46881,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the non-exhaustible sources of energy.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sunlight, wind and water are non- exhaustible sources of energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46882,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In which states is water found in nature?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Solid, liquid and gaseous are the three states in which water is found in nature. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46883,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by energy?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The capacity to do the work is called energy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46884,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What uses of heat energy do we see in our daily life?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We use heat energy for cooking, keeping the place warm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46885,785,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the small particles that can be seen in a beam of light that enters a dark room?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In the beam of light the small particles of dust are seen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46886,785,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following substances as per their states, into solids, liquid and gas : Chalk, water, rangoli, smoke, oxygen, nitrogen, petrol, milk, aluminium, wood, coal, kerosene, ice, water vapour.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a) Solid – Chalk, Rangoli, Aluminium, Wood, Coal, Ice\nb) Liquid – Water, Petrol, Milk, Kerosene \nc) Gas – Smoke, Oxygen, Nitrogen, Water vapour  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46887,785,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different properties in the substances?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Hardness, transparency, colour, smell, solubility in water, etc. are the different properties seen in the substances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46888,785,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which substances give energy in the form of heat? What are these substances known as?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Substances like coal, diesel, CNG, LPG, petrol, kerosene, etc. are the substances that give energy in the form of heat. These substances are known as fuels.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46889,785,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write some of the activities that can be done due to wind energy.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The windmills turn, the boats sail, clouds move from one place to the other are the activities that take place due to kinetic energy of wind.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46890,785,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? We need to make a sherbet quickly for some guests. But we only have sugar candy in the house.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We can pound sugar candy into fine powder. When the powder is fine, it will quickly dissolve in water. Therefore, we can make sherbet quickly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46891,785,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What’s the solution? We need to rub salt on a corncob but only salt crystals are available. \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We can pound salt crystals. Either we can use mortar pestle or we can also crush the crystals of salt in a grinder. Such powdered salt can be easily applied on the corncob.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46892,785,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Naphthalene balls are also placed into toilets. Why do they become smaller after a few days?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Due to the property of naphthalene to turn into a gaseous state, the naphthalene balls kept in the toilet also become smaller and smaller after few days.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46893,785,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','When and why do clothes smell of naphthalene?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The naphthalene balls are continuously converted into small particles in the gaseous state. \nii) These particles settle down on the clothes in which they are kept. \niii) Therefore, if naphthalene balls are kept for a long time in the clothes, they smell of naphthalene due to these particles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46894,785,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is the difference between solid, liquid and gaseous states of a substance?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The arrangements of the particles in different states of a substance are different. \nii) In solid state the particles are closely packed. \niii) In liquid state, the particles are slightly away from each other \niv) While in gaseous state they are not in contact with each other and are freely placed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46895,785,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of all the everyday substances that we use in the form of a powder.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Chilli powder, turmeric powder, masalas, pepper powder, different types of flour, face powder, soap powder, rangoli, kumkum, ash, etc. are the substances used in powder form.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46896,785,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Naphthalene balls are placed in warm clothes. After some days, why do the clothes also smell of naphthalene?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The chemical naphthalene in the naphthalene balls continuously converts into small particles in the gaseous state. These particles settle down on the clothes in which they are kept. So after some days, the clothes start smelling of naphthalene.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46897,785,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why do tablets of camphor decrease in size day by day?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Tablets of Camphor are in solid state. \nii) Camphor has a property to turn into gaseous state directly from a solid state. \niii) Therefore, camphor decreases in size gradually. \niv) One can also notice the fragrance of camphor as it turns into vapour.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46898,785,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How do we save fuel by using public transport?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Each automobile vehicle needs fuel either in the form of petrol, diesel or CNG.\nii) If all the people use a separate vehicle, the amount of fuel consumed would be much more. \niii) If many people start using a public transport, the fuel consumption would be reduced. \niv) A single bus or a train can carry many people and thus it can save lots of fuel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46899,785,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','You do not have rangoli powder. What things can you use as an alternative?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We can use flour, face powder, chalk powder or any other particulate powdered matter to replace rangoli. We can use coloured powders like turmeric, kumkum, etc. for filling colours in the rangoli.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46900,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','A healthy and wholesome life leads to a feeling of -------------------------------','','','friendship ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46901,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Constant presence of tobacco in the digestive organs can lead to a cancer of (any) ----------------------------','','','digestive organs ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46902,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','----------------------- leads to diseases of the liver, intestines and urinary bladder.','','','Alcoholism ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46903,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The most important factor in the country’s progress and development are its -----------------------------','','','people ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46904,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Good community ------------------------- can be achieved through habits of hygiene and good health.','','','health and hygiene ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46905,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','It is ------------------------ to spit in a public place.','','','legal offence ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46906,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Alcohol makes a person -------------------- and ------------------------.','','','drowsy and confused','',0,0,0,NULL),(46907,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Take up some sports or ---------------------- to stay away from addictions.','','','hobbies ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46908,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Never be --------------------- about health.','','','negligent ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46909,786,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Each person should put in efforts to maintain the ---------------------- health. ','','','community’s ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46910,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : Pollution, squalor, epidemics, addictions and insect-borne diseases are all beneficial to community health.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False (Pollution, squalor, epidemics, addictions and insect-borne diseases all threats to the community)','',0,0,0,NULL),(46911,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : There is a ban on spitting in public places.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46912,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : A nutritious diet, personal hygiene, exercise and pursuit of hobbies lead to excellent health.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46913,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : We cannot live a wholesome life if we take care of our health.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False (We can live a wholesome life if we take care of our health)','',0,0,0,NULL),(46914,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : We should take care of our own health.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46915,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : Spitting anywhere on the road is good for health.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46916,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : Chewing tobacco leads to a cancer of mouth.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46917,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write whether the given statement is true or false. Rewrite the false statement after correcting them : Drinking alcohol does not effects on the body.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46918,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them : It is unnecessary to be alert about health and hygiene.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','It is necessary to be alert about health and hygiene.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46919,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them : Everyone should lead a tensed life.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Everyone should lead a tension-free life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46920,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them :  Tobacco consumption is harmless to health.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Tobacco consumption is harmful to health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46921,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them :  Alcohol consumption causes alertness.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Alcohol consumption causes drowsiness.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46922,786,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Given below is some wrong statement. Change a word in these and correct them : Malnutrition causes fall in infant deaths. \n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Malnutrition causes rise in infant deaths','',0,0,0,NULL),(46923,786,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How can you achieve excellent health?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Nutritious food, personal cleanliness, exercise and pursuit of hobbies leads to achievement of excellent health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46924,786,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What factors are a threat to community health?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Pollution, squalor, epidemics of infectious diseases, addiction, insect-borne diseases, malnutrition are the factors that cause a threat to community health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46925,786,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is spitting in a public place banned?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Spitting can spread different diseases and also makes surrounding dirty, there- fore, spitting in a public place is banned.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46926,786,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which is the tobacco containing products?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Gutka, snuff, masheri, mawa, pan masala, cigarettes, bidis, chillum, hookah, cigar, pipe, etc. are tobacco containing products.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46927,786,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is done for becoming addiction-free?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The addict should take medical help of counsellor and doctor, he should develop hobbies and sports in order to become addiction-free.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46928,786,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which addiction can totally devastate one’s life?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Getting addicted to drugs like cocaine, brown sugar, etc. can devastate one\'s life. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46929,786,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the ill-effects of alcoholism? \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Alcohol acts on the brain of a person. Person becomes drowsy and con- fused. \nii) ) Alcohol makes a person lose control over one\'s actions. \niii) Alcohol causes diseases of liver, intestines and kidney and urinary bladder.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46930,786,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do we nurture community health?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','To nurture community health following things are done: \ni) general public from infectious diseases. \nii) Observing personal and community hygiene.\niii) Controlling pollution, squalor, insect-borne diseases, addiction, etc.\niv) Making people happy and healthy in all respects.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46931,786,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the benefit of wholesome life?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) By leading wholesome life, we become happy. We can take interest in many things. \nii) Feelings of hatred and envy are ended. People in the society lead healthy and happy life. \niii) Social tensions decrease and feelings of friendship are nurtured.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46932,786,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','When is it said that a person is addicted to tobacco?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When a person gets restless when he does not get tobacco and he is unable to pay attention to anything else, he is said to be addicted to tobacco.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46933,786,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : When we take care of our health, entire community also becomes wholesome.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When everybody takes care of their health and hygiene, the entire community health is also achieved. \nii) Through the habits of cleanliness the entire surroundings also become clean. In this way all can lead a wholesome life. \niii) The entire community then becomes wholesome.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46934,786,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Pollution and epidemics of infectious diseases are threats to community health.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When there is an outbreak of epidemic, many people from the same area are affected at the same time. \nii) Similarly, pollution also causes harm to the life of the people. \niii) Many people can die too. Therefore, pollution and epidemics of infectious diseases are threats to community health. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(46935,786,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the ill – effects of chewing tobacco?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) If tobacco is consumed, there are ulcers in the mouth. Ulcers develop into\nbigger wounds and they are later turn into tumours. \nii) Oral cancer, i.e. cancer of the mouth may be formed. \niii) Tobacco entering into digestive system cause cancer of any of the digestive organs. It also leads to various complaints of digestive system. \niv) A person with cancer has to undergo painful treatment. It can lead to death.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46936,786,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the effect of addiction on the family of an addict?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the condition of an addict becomes pathetic, his family members also suffer. \nii) Lot of money is required for treatment. Everyone is put into great trouble and lot of time and efforts are wasted. \niii) The family gets deprived of health and happiness.\niv) The family gets ruined due to insecurity of all kind. \nv) Addict destroys the entire family.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46937,786,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is to be done for good health?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) For good health, nutritious diet, safe and clean drinking water should be used. \nii) Habits of personal and community cleanliness should be followed. Never get hooked on to any addiction. \niii) Always stay away from tobacco, alcohol and drugs.\niv) Feeling of hatred and envy should be avoided. \nv) Proper exercise should be done. Hobbies and sports should be taken up. \nvi) Along with personal health, community health should also be nurtured.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46938,786,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is to be done to stay away from addictions?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) We should have good habits, remain happy and enthusiastic. \nii) There should be complete control over mind. \niii) We should remain firm in avoiding addictions.\niv) We should not be lured by people who can force someone in addictions. \nv) Sports and hobbies also keep the addictions away. \nvi) We should be completely aware of dangers of addictions. All these measures can help us to stay away from addictions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(46939,786,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Always stay away addictions.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Addictions spoils health of a person. Tobacco addiction can cause cancers of different organs. \nii) By keeping tobacco constantly in the mouth, there are chances of oral cancer. \niii) Alcoholism cause severe problems of liver, kidney and intestines. The addict completely destroys the family. \niv) The family is deprived of health and happiness and is ruined. Due to tobacco spitting the surroundings get dirty. \nv) By drug addiction, life is totally devastated. \nvi) Therefore, always stay away from addictions. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47427,628,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and answer the following questions :\nThe lion walks on padded paws,\nThe squirrel leaps from limb to limb,\nWhile flies can crawl straight up a wall, \nAnd seals can dive and swim.\nThe worm he wiggles all around,\nThe monkey swings by his tail, \nAnd birds may hop upon the ground\nOr spread their wings and sail.\nBut boys and girls have much more fun:\nThey leap and dance and walk and run.\n1] Write down any five pairs of ‘one – many’ from the poem.\n     Example : lion – lions \n2] List all action words from poem.','','','1] i) lion – lions             ii) tiger – tigers          iii) plant – plants \n    iv) tree – trees         v) boy – boys              vi) girl – girls \n    vii) hill – hills \n2] walk, leap, crawl, swim, dive, swing, hop, sail, dance, run','',0,0,0,NULL),(47428,628,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the following table and make meaningful sentences.','','','Rains falls from the sky\n2) Rain falls on the ground\n3) Rain runs in the river\n4) Rain falls on the hill etc.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47429,628,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and answer the following questions :\nThe lion walks on padded paws,\nThe squirrel leaps from limb to limb,\nWhile flies can crawl straight up a wall, \nAnd seals can dive and swim.\nThe worm he wiggles all around,\nThe monkey swings by his tail, \nAnd birds may hop upon the ground\nOr spread their wings and sail.\nBut boys and girls have much more fun:\nThey leap and dance and walk and run.\n1] Write down any five pairs of ‘one – many’ from the poem.\n     Example : lion – lions \n2] List all action words from poem.','','','i) lion – lions             ii) tiger – tigers          iii) plant – plants \n    iv) tree – trees         v) boy – boys              vi) girl – girls \n    vii) hill – hills \n2] walk, leap, crawl, swim, dive, swing, hop, sail, dance, run','',0,0,0,NULL),(47430,628,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the following table and make meaningful sentences.','','','Rains falls from the sky\n2) Rain falls on the ground\n3) Rain runs in the river\n4) Rain falls on the hill etc.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47431,630,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the questions\nThe Clever Turtle\nOnce, some villagers caught a turtle in the field and took him to their chief. \"You must punish this thief!\" they said.\n\"Oh, no!\" said the turtle. \"Please don\'t throw me back in the river. You may hang me, whip me, burn me, or push me from a cliff. But please, please, whatever you do, don\'t throw me back in the river!\"\n\"Throw him back in the river!\" The chief ordered. The villagers threw the turtle in the river. The turtle laughed and laughed and swam away merrily.\n1] Complete the following.\n     a) The villagers caught the turtle -----------\n     b) The chief ordered to -----------------\n2] Complete the table :','','','a) in the field\n    b) throw the tertile in the river.\n2) caught, took, said, threw, swam','',1,0,0,NULL),(47432,630,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the questions\nThe Clever Turtle\nOnce, some villagers caught a turtle in the field and took him to their chief. \"You must punish this thief!\" they said.\n\"Oh, no!\" said the turtle. \"Please don\'t throw me back in the river. You may hang me, whip me, burn me, or push me from a cliff. But please, please, whatever you do, don\'t throw me back in the river!\"\n\"Throw him back in the river!\" The chief ordered. The villagers threw the turtle in the river. The turtle laughed and laughed and swam away merrily.\n1] Complete the following.\n     a) The villagers caught the turtle -----------\n     b) The chief ordered to -----------------\n2] Complete the table :','','','a) in the field\n    b) throw the tertile in the river.\n2) caught, took, said, threw, swam','',1,0,0,NULL),(47433,631,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following shapes :','','','Arrow, square, triangle, line, cross, point, rectangle, star, circle, temple','',1,0,0,NULL),(47434,631,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','See the following map and answer the following questions :','1) Where is the fountain? \n2) How many slides are there?\n3) Where is the banyan tree.\n4) How many swings are there?\n5) Where is the entrance?','','In the center of the garden\n2) Four\n3) in the north – east corner\n4) five\n5) Left of north – east side','',1,0,0,NULL),(47435,631,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following shapes :','','','Arrow, square, triangle, line, cross, point, rectangle, star, circle, temple','',1,0,0,NULL),(47436,631,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','See the following map and answer the following questions :','1) Where is the fountain? \n2) How many slides are there?\n3) Where is the banyan tree.\n4) How many swings are there?\n5) Where is the entrance?','','In the center of the garden\n2) Four\n3) in the north – east corner\n4) five\n5) Left of north – east side','',1,0,0,NULL),(47437,632,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the questions :\nLong, long ago, in the faraway land of Korea, there was a minister. On the right of his house, there lived a blacksmith. On the left, there lived a carpenter.\nThe blacksmith and the carpenter began their work early in the morning. Tong, tong, tong the blacksmith beat the iron with his big hammer. Tock, tock, tock, the carpenter worked with his small hammer. Krrr. krrr krrr he used his saw to cut the wood. They worked day and night and made a lot of noise, All that noise disturbed the minister.\n1) Who lived between the blacksmith and the carpenter?\n2) Who used a big hammer?\n3) Who used a small hammer?\n4) Which time they worked?\n5) Who disturbed noise pollution?','','','A minister\n2) The blacksmith\n3) The carpenter\n4) day and night\n5) A minister','',0,0,0,NULL),(47438,632,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the following questions :\nBut the next morning, the minister woke up with the sound of tong, tong, tong, tock, tock, tock, krrr, krır, krrr again. He got annoyed. He called his servants. \"Go and see who is making all that noise!\"\nAfter some time, the servants came back. They had some news for the minister. The carpenter and the blacksmith had moved their houses the blacksmith had moved to the carpenter\'s house and the carpenter had moved to the blacksmith\'s house!\nThe blacksmith and the carpenter carried on their work in their houses day and night! \n1) Did the blacksmith shift his house?\n2) Did the carpenter shift his house?\n3) Was the minister happy at the end?\n4) Who moved to the blacksmith’s house?\n5) Who moved to the carpenter’s house?','','','Yes\n2) Yes\n3) No\n4) The carpenter\n5) The blacksmith ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47439,632,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the questions :\nLong, long ago, in the faraway land of Korea, there was a minister. On the right of his house, there lived a blacksmith. On the left, there lived a carpenter.\nThe blacksmith and the carpenter began their work early in the morning. Tong, tong, tong the blacksmith beat the iron with his big hammer. Tock, tock, tock, the carpenter worked with his small hammer. Krrr. krrr krrr he used his saw to cut the wood. They worked day and night and made a lot of noise, All that noise disturbed the minister.\n1) Who lived between the blacksmith and the carpenter?\n2) Who used a big hammer?\n3) Who used a small hammer?\n4) Which time they worked?\n5) Who disturbed noise pollution?','','','A minister\n2) The blacksmith\n3) The carpenter\n4) day and night\n5) A minister','',0,0,0,NULL),(47440,632,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and answer the following questions :\nBut the next morning, the minister woke up with the sound of tong, tong, tong, tock, tock, tock, krrr, krır, krrr again. He got annoyed. He called his servants. \"Go and see who is making all that noise!\"\nAfter some time, the servants came back. They had some news for the minister. The carpenter and the blacksmith had moved their houses the blacksmith had moved to the carpenter\'s house and the carpenter had moved to the blacksmith\'s house!\nThe blacksmith and the carpenter carried on their work in their houses day and night! \n1) Did the blacksmith shift his house?\n2) Did the carpenter shift his house?\n3) Was the minister happy at the end?\n4) Who moved to the blacksmith’s house?\n5) Who moved to the carpenter’s house?','','','Yes\n2) Yes\n3) No\n4) The carpenter\n5) The blacksmith ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47441,634,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following poem and answer the questions :\nCats purr. Lions roar. Owls hoot. Bears snore.\nCrickets creak. Mice squeak. Sheep baa. But I speak!\nMonkeys chatter. Cows moo. Ducks quack. Pigeons coo.\nPigs squeal. Horses neigh. Chickens cluck. But I speak!\nRewrite the lines as shown :\n1) First stanza  : cats purr – A cat purrs\n2) Second stanza : Moneys chatter – Monkeys are chattering','','','Lions roar – A lion roars.\n    Owls hoot – AN owl hoots\n2) Cows moo – Cows are mooing.\n    Ducks quack – Ducks are quacking. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47442,634,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following poem and answer the questions :\nCats purr. Lions roar. Owls hoot. Bears snore.\nCrickets creak. Mice squeak. Sheep baa. But I speak!\nMonkeys chatter. Cows moo. Ducks quack. Pigeons coo.\nPigs squeal. Horses neigh. Chickens cluck. But I speak!\nRewrite the lines as shown :\n1) First stanza  : cats purr – A cat purrs\n2) Second stanza : Moneys chatter – Monkeys are chattering','','','Lions roar – A lion roars.\n    Owls hoot – AN owl hoots\n2) Cows moo – Cows are mooing.\n    Ducks quack – Ducks are quacking. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47443,636,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the sentences.\n     i. The child is speaking to -------------\n     ii. The wind passes like --------------\nI saw you toss the kites on high \nAnd blow the birds about the sky; \nAnd all around I heard you pass, \nLike ladies\' skirts across the grass \nO wind, a-blowing all day long, \nO wind, that sings so loud a song!\n2] Complete the following \n     The thing that the wind does ----------\n     i. -------------          ii. ---------------  iii. --------------------\n3] Write down the pairs of rhyming words.','','','i) the wind\n     ii) like ladies skirts across the grass.\n2] i) tosses the kites on high\n     ii) blows the birds.\n     iii) sings a loud song.\n3] i) high = sky               ii) pass = grass','',0,0,0,NULL),(47444,636,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the web \n','O you that are so strong and cold, \nO blower, are you young or old? \nAre you a beast of field and tree, \nOr just a stronger child than me? \nO wind, a-blowing all day long, \nO wind, that sings so loud a song!\n2] a) Can we see the wind?\n     b) Can we hear the wind?\n3] Write down the pair of rhyming words.','','i) strong                  ii) cold\n2] a) No, we cannot see the wind.\n     b) Yes, we can hear the wind.\n3] i) cold – old,           ii) tree – me        iii) long – song ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47445,636,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the sentences.\n     i. The child is speaking to -------------\n     ii. The wind passes like --------------\nI saw you toss the kites on high \nAnd blow the birds about the sky; \nAnd all around I heard you pass, \nLike ladies\' skirts across the grass \nO wind, a-blowing all day long, \nO wind, that sings so loud a song!\n2] Complete the following \n     The thing that the wind does ----------\n     i. -------------          ii. ---------------  iii. --------------------\n3] Write down the pairs of rhyming words.','','','i) the wind\n     ii) like ladies skirts across the grass.\n2] i) tosses the kites on high\n     ii) blows the birds.\n     iii) sings a loud song.\n3] i) high = sky               ii) pass = grass','',0,0,0,NULL),(47446,636,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the web \n','O you that are so strong and cold, \nO blower, are you young or old? \nAre you a beast of field and tree, \nOr just a stronger child than me? \nO wind, a-blowing all day long, \nO wind, that sings so loud a song!\n2] a) Can we see the wind?\n     b) Can we hear the wind?\n3] Write down the pair of rhyming words.','','i) strong                  ii) cold\n2] a) No, we cannot see the wind.\n     b) Yes, we can hear the wind.\n3] i) cold – old,           ii) tree – me        iii) long – song ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47447,638,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities : \n1] Complete the following sentences.\n    i. The little boy lived in ------------\n    ii. The boy’s uncle believed in ------------\n    iii. The lovely garden was ------------\n    iv. The boy was playing ---------------\nOnce there was a little boy. He lived in a pretty little cottage. There was a lovely garden around the cottage. Different kinds of plants grew in the garden. So, there was a lot of work to do their water the plants every day, give manure from time to time, cut and prune the plants at the right time, weed the garden, clean the garden. Everybody in the little boy\'s family helped to take care of the garden.\nThe boy had a very strict uncle. He believed in hard work and discipline. One day the boy was playing around in the house.\n2] Use the appropriate phrase in the sentences.\n      [a lot of, take care of, believe in]\n      i. I don’t ------- ghosts.\n      ii. Mother .... her child.\n      iii. There is --------- water in the river.\n3] Find different action words. ','','','i) a pretty little cottage            ii) hard work and discipline\n    iii) around the cottage              iv) in the house  \n2] i) believe in          ii) take care of          iii) a lot of\n3] work, clean, help, play','',0,0,0,NULL),(47448,638,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web \n','Once there was a little boy. He lived in a pretty little cottage. There was a lovely garden around the cottage. Different kinds of plants grew in the garden. So, there was a lot of work to do their water the plants every day, give manure from time to time, cut and prune the plants at the right time, weed the garden, clean the garden. Everybody in the little boy\'s family helped to take care of the garden.\nThe boy had a very strict uncle. He believed in hard work and discipline. One day the boy was playing around in the house.\n2] a) How many people are there in the passage?\n     b) Who are they?\n3] From the passage find out opposites of :\n     i. dirty                ii. soft','','i) Water the plants                ii) Give manure to plants\n     iii) Weed the garden             iv) Clean the garden\n2] a) There are two people in the passage.\n     b) boy and uncle\n3] i) clean          ii. hard','',1,0,0,NULL),(47449,638,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Who said to whom.\n      i. \'Uncle is so unfair.\'\n      ii. \'Why do you look so unhappy?\'\nThe little boy felt sad. \'Uncle is so unfair,\' he thought. His chin began to wobble, he had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up. Just then, his mother walked in.\n\"What\'s the matter, little boy?\" she asked, \"Why do you look so unhappy?\"\n\"Uncle told me to weed the garden,\" said the little boy.\n\"Oh!\" said his mother, \"What fun that will be! I love to weed, and it is such a fine day! Can I come and help you to weed?\"\n\"Why, yes!\" said the boy.\nSo, the boy and his mother went outside. They weeded the garden. They had a very good time together, working and chatting and laughing.\n2] From the passage find out the opposites of de\n     i. happy x …….                  ii. bad x ………\n     iii. hate x ……                    iv. fair x ……….\n3] Punctuate the following.\n     \"What\'s the matter, little boy ?\" she asked.','','','i) The little boy to himself                   ii) The boy\'s mother to him.\n2] i) unhappy, sad          ii) good          iii) love         iv) unfair\n3] \"What\'s the matter, little boy she asked.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47450,638,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web \n','The little boy felt sad. \'Uncle is so unfair,\' he thought. His chin began to wobble, he had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up. Just then, his mother walked in.\n\"What\'s the matter, little boy?\" she asked, \"Why do you look so unhappy?\"\n\"Uncle told me to weed the garden,\" said the little boy.\n\"Oh!\" said his mother, \"What fun that will be! I love to weed, and it is such a fine day! Can I come and help you to weed?\"\n\"Why, yes!\" said the boy.\nSo, the boy and his mother went outside. They weeded the garden. They had a very good time together, working and chatting and laughing.\n2] a) How many people are there in the passage?\n     b) Who are they?\n3] What body parts are mentioned in the passage?','','The little boy felt sad. His chin began to wobble He had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up.\n2] a) There are three people in the story.\n    b) Boy, his mother and his uncle.\n3] Chin, hands, eyes, throat','',1,0,0,NULL),(47451,638,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities : \n1] Complete the following sentences.\n    i. The little boy lived in ------------\n    ii. The boy’s uncle believed in ------------\n    iii. The lovely garden was ------------\n    iv. The boy was playing ---------------\nOnce there was a little boy. He lived in a pretty little cottage. There was a lovely garden around the cottage. Different kinds of plants grew in the garden. So, there was a lot of work to do their water the plants every day, give manure from time to time, cut and prune the plants at the right time, weed the garden, clean the garden. Everybody in the little boy\'s family helped to take care of the garden.\nThe boy had a very strict uncle. He believed in hard work and discipline. One day the boy was playing around in the house.\n2] Use the appropriate phrase in the sentences.\n      [a lot of, take care of, believe in]\n      i. I don’t ------- ghosts.\n      ii. Mother .... her child.\n      iii. There is --------- water in the river.\n3] Find different action words. ','','','i) a pretty little cottage            ii) hard work and discipline\n    iii) around the cottage              iv) in the house  \n2] i) believe in          ii) take care of          iii) a lot of\n3] work, clean, help, play','',0,0,0,NULL),(47452,638,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web \n','Once there was a little boy. He lived in a pretty little cottage. There was a lovely garden around the cottage. Different kinds of plants grew in the garden. So, there was a lot of work to do their water the plants every day, give manure from time to time, cut and prune the plants at the right time, weed the garden, clean the garden. Everybody in the little boy\'s family helped to take care of the garden.\nThe boy had a very strict uncle. He believed in hard work and discipline. One day the boy was playing around in the house.\n2] a) How many people are there in the passage?\n     b) Who are they?\n3] From the passage find out opposites of :\n     i. dirty                ii. soft','','i) Water the plants                ii) Give manure to plants\n     iii) Weed the garden             iv) Clean the garden\n2] a) There are two people in the passage.\n     b) boy and uncle\n3] i) clean          ii. hard','',1,0,0,NULL),(47453,638,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Who said to whom.\n      i. \'Uncle is so unfair.\'\n      ii. \'Why do you look so unhappy?\'\nThe little boy felt sad. \'Uncle is so unfair,\' he thought. His chin began to wobble, he had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up. Just then, his mother walked in.\n\"What\'s the matter, little boy?\" she asked, \"Why do you look so unhappy?\"\n\"Uncle told me to weed the garden,\" said the little boy.\n\"Oh!\" said his mother, \"What fun that will be! I love to weed, and it is such a fine day! Can I come and help you to weed?\"\n\"Why, yes!\" said the boy.\nSo, the boy and his mother went outside. They weeded the garden. They had a very good time together, working and chatting and laughing.\n2] From the passage find out the opposites of de\n     i. happy x …….                  ii. bad x ………\n     iii. hate x ……                    iv. fair x ……….\n3] Punctuate the following.\n     \"What\'s the matter, little boy ?\" she asked.','','','i) The little boy to himself                   ii) The boy\'s mother to him.\n2] i) unhappy, sad          ii) good          iii) love         iv) unfair\n3] \"What\'s the matter, little boy she asked.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47454,638,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web \n','The little boy felt sad. \'Uncle is so unfair,\' he thought. His chin began to wobble, he had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up. Just then, his mother walked in.\n\"What\'s the matter, little boy?\" she asked, \"Why do you look so unhappy?\"\n\"Uncle told me to weed the garden,\" said the little boy.\n\"Oh!\" said his mother, \"What fun that will be! I love to weed, and it is such a fine day! Can I come and help you to weed?\"\n\"Why, yes!\" said the boy.\nSo, the boy and his mother went outside. They weeded the garden. They had a very good time together, working and chatting and laughing.\n2] a) How many people are there in the passage?\n     b) Who are they?\n3] What body parts are mentioned in the passage?','','The little boy felt sad. His chin began to wobble He had a lump in his throat. His eyes welled up.\n2] a) There are three people in the story.\n    b) Boy, his mother and his uncle.\n3] Chin, hands, eyes, throat','',1,0,0,NULL),(47455,639,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n     i. Everyone loves gold.\n     ii. Flowers and Sunshine are more beautiful than gold.\n     iii. The king has a strange wish.\n     iv. The king did not thank the stranger.\nKing : Why, everyone loves gold. It makes you rich. I love to sit in this dark cellar and look at all my gold.\nStranger : In your garden are flowers and golden sunshine. They are far more beautiful\nKing : Not to me. I like to be here, where everything is made of real gold. I wish that everything I touched would turn to gold.\nStranger: That is a strange wish. Are you sure you want to turn everything you touch to gold?\nKing : Yes, yes. I\'m sure. Everything made of gold! I would be so happy!\nStranger : Then you shall have your wish. Tomorrow morning, at sunrise, you shall have the Golden Touch.\nKing : Thank you so much, O Stranger!\nStranger: Do not thank me. Perhaps you will be sorry.\nKing : Oh, no! It\'s a dream come true!\n2] Use the correct word and complete the sentences, \n       [of, at, to]\n     i. Don\'t look ...... the sun with naked eye.\n     ii. You shall have the Golden Touch .... sunrise.\n     iii. Everything here is made... gold.\n     iv. Do you want to turn everything .... gold.\n3] Find out two describing words from the passage.','','','i) True          ii) True            iii) True           iv) False.\n2] i) at            ii) at               iii) of              iv) to\n3] i) rich           ii) dark','',0,0,0,NULL),(47456,639,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The king loves to ----------\n     ii. He wishes that ----------\n     iii. Next morning at sunrise the king has -----------\nKing : Why, everyone loves gold. It makes you rich. I love to sit in this dark cellar and look at all my gold.\nStranger : In your garden are flowers and golden sunshine. They are far more beautiful\nKing : Not to me. I like to be here, where everything is made of real gold. I wish that everything I touched would turn to gold.\nStranger: That is a strange wish. Are you sure you want to turn everything you touch to gold?\nKing : Yes, yes. I\'m sure. Everything made of gold! I would be so happy!\nStranger : Then you shall have your wish. Tomorrow morning, at sunrise, you shall have the Golden Touch.\nKing : Thank you so much, O Stranger!\nStranger: Do not thank me. Perhaps you will be sorry.\nKing : Oh, no! It\'s a dream come true!\n2] a) Name the characters in the play.\n    b) Who loved gold?\n3] Find out four describing words from the passage.','','','i) sit in the dark cellar and look at his gold.\n     ii) everything he touches will turn to gold.\n     iii) the Golden Touch.\n2] a) King Midas, a stranger \n     b) King Midas loved gold.\n3] i) golden         ii) beautiful','',0,0,0,NULL),(47457,639,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Make a list of things that are turned to gold.\nKing : Good morning, Marygold. See what I have brought for you.\nMarygold : A fine yellow rose. (Smells it.) Oh, it has no smell, and the hard petals have pricked my nose. (Starts crying.)\nKing : What are you crying for? It is a golden rose. All roses in my garden are golden!\nMarygold : Are they all like this one?\nKing : Come on, now. Let\'s have our breakfast. ……. oh!\nMarygold : What\'s the matter? Have you burnt your mouth?\nKing : This fish has turned to gold. I must try an egg.... oh, that has turned to gold, too. Oh, no! What shall I do now?\nMarygold : Tell me, what\'s the matter?\nKing : Everything I touch turns to gold. I shall never be able to eat anything.\nMarygold : My poor father! Can I help you?\n                     Marygold rushes forward.\nKing : No, don\'t touch me. Keep away!\n           Oh, Marygold, what have you done?\n           Marygold has turned into a statue of gold.\nKing : My daughter! My dear, dear Marygold! So full of life! Like a ray of sunshine! She is only a golden doll, now!\n2] a) Who was Marygold?\n    b) What did the king wish?\n3] What happened to Marygold? ','','','i) a rose          ii) fish           iii) an egg          iv) Marygold\n2] a) Marygold was daughter of King Midas\n    b) The King wished that everything he touched could turn to gold.\n3] Marygold turned into a statue of gold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47458,639,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following table.\n','King : Good morning, Marygold. See what I have brought for you.\nMarygold : A fine yellow rose. (Smells it.) Oh, it has no smell, and the hard petals have pricked my nose. (Starts crying.)\nKing : What are you crying for? It is a golden rose. All roses in my garden are golden!\nMarygold : Are they all like this one?\nKing : Come on, now. Let\'s have our breakfast. ……. oh!\nMarygold : What\'s the matter? Have you burnt your mouth?\nKing : This fish has turned to gold. I must try an egg.... oh, that has turned to gold, too. Oh, no! What shall I do now?\nMarygold : Tell me, what\'s the matter?\nKing : Everything I touch turns to gold. I shall never be able to eat anything.\nMarygold : My poor father! Can I help you?\n                     Marygold rushes forward.\nKing : No, don\'t touch me. Keep away!\n           Oh, Marygold, what have you done?\n           Marygold has turned into a statue of gold.\nKing : My daughter! My dear, dear Marygold! So full of life! Like a ray of sunshine! She is only a golden doll, now!\n2] a) Find out two colours from the passage.\n    b) Find out two body organs from the passage\n3] Why was Marygold crying?','','i) Before the Golden Touch, Marygold was so full of life, She was like a ray of sunshine.\n    ii) After the Golden Touch she turned into a statue of gold. She was only a golden doll.\n2] a) golden, yellow                b) nose, mouth.\n3] Because all the roses in her garden turned to gold.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47459,639,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the f