d.\nKing : My daughter! My dear, dear Marygold! So full of life! Like a ray of sunshine! She is only a golden doll, now!\n2] a) Who was Marygold?\n    b) What did the king wish?\n3] What happened to Marygold? ','','','i) a rose          ii) fish           iii) an egg          iv) Marygold\n2] a) Marygold was daughter of King Midas\n    b) The King wished that everything he touched could turn to gold.\n3] Marygold turned into a statue of gold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47462,639,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following table.\n','King : Good morning, Marygold. See what I have brought for you.\nMarygold : A fine yellow rose. (Smells it.) Oh, it has no smell, and the hard petals have pricked my nose. (Starts crying.)\nKing : What are you crying for? It is a golden rose. All roses in my garden are golden!\nMarygold : Are they all like this one?\nKing : Come on, now. Let\'s have our breakfast. ……. oh!\nMarygold : What\'s the matter? Have you burnt your mouth?\nKing : This fish has turned to gold. I must try an egg.... oh, that has turned to gold, too. Oh, no! What shall I do now?\nMarygold : Tell me, what\'s the matter?\nKing : Everything I touch turns to gold. I shall never be able to eat anything.\nMarygold : My poor father! Can I help you?\n                     Marygold rushes forward.\nKing : No, don\'t touch me. Keep away!\n           Oh, Marygold, what have you done?\n           Marygold has turned into a statue of gold.\nKing : My daughter! My dear, dear Marygold! So full of life! Like a ray of sunshine! She is only a golden doll, now!\n2] a) Find out two colours from the passage.\n    b) Find out two body organs from the passage\n3] Why was Marygold crying?','','i) Before the Golden Touch, Marygold was so full of life, She was like a ray of sunshine.\n    ii) After the Golden Touch she turned into a statue of gold. She was only a golden doll.\n2] a) golden, yellow                b) nose, mouth.\n3] Because all the roses in her garden turned to gold.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47463,640,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Match the following :\n','Dark brown is the river, \nGolden is the sand.\nIt flows along for ever, \nWith trees on either hand.\nGreen leaves a-floating, \nCastles of the foam, \nBoats of mine a-boating \nWhere will all come home?\n2] Find out the lines that mean the following.\n     i. Leaves are floating on the water.\n     ii. The river flows down continuously.\n     iii. There is foam on the surface of water.\n     iv. There are trees on the bank of the river.\n3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)\n2] i) Green leaves a – floating         ii) It flows along for ever.\n     iii) Castles of the foam.               iv) With trees on either hand.\n3] i) Sand = hand           ii) Foam = home. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47464,640,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Match the following :\n','Dark brown is the river, \nGolden is the sand.\nIt flows along for ever, \nWith trees on either hand.\nGreen leaves a-floating, \nCastles of the foam, \nBoats of mine a-boating \nWhere will all come home?\n2] Find out the lines that mean the following.\n     i. Leaves are floating on the water.\n     ii. The river flows down continuously.\n     iii. There is foam on the surface of water.\n     iv. There are trees on the bank of the river.\n3] Write down two pairs of rhyming words.','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)\n2] i) Green leaves a – floating         ii) It flows along for ever.\n     iii) Castles of the foam.               iv) With trees on either hand.\n3] i) Sand = hand           ii) Foam = home. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47465,641,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are true or false :\n    i. The sun rises in the west.\n    ii. The sun is a star.\n    iii. The sun goes round the earth.\n    iv. We see the stars at night.\nHello, friends.\nYou see me in the sky every day.\nYou say that I rise in the east and set in the west.\nDid you guess who I am?\nYes, that\'s right! I am the sun.\nActually, I am a star like other stars you see at night.\nBut I am close to the earth.\nThat is why, I look so big and bright.\nI give the earth heat and light.\nAnd remember, I don\'t go round the earth.\nThe earth goes round me.\n2] Complete the following sentences.\n      i. The sun looks ------- and ------------\n      ii. The sun give the earth ----------- and -----------\n      iii. The sun looks big and bright because ------------\n      iv. The earth goes round -------------\n3] From the passage find the opposites of :\n     i. forget x ……….            ii. dim x …………..\n      iii. open x …………..         iv. rise x ……….','','','i) False             ii) True              iii) False                iv) True\n2] i) big and bright                            ii) heat and light\n     iii) it is close to the earth           iv) the sun\n3] i) remember          ii) bright\n     iii) close                   iv) set','',0,0,0,NULL),(47466,641,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Name the following\n     i. The third planet from the sun -----------\n     ii. The earth moves round -------------\n     iii. An envelope around the earth - -------\n     iv. Time taken by the earth to go round the sun --------\nHello, everybody!\nI am the earth.\nI am a planet in the solar system.\nI am the third planet from the sun.\nI spin around myself all the time.\nAt the same time, I go round the sun.\nI take one year to go round the sun.\nMuch of my surface is covered with water.\nIf you look at me from space,\nI appear blue in colour.\nThat is why, some people call me the blue planet.\nThere is an envelope of air around me.\nToday, I am the only home of plants, animals and human beings.\n2] The earth is called the blue planet because ------\n3] From the passage, find out the words that mean :- a\n     i. go round                  ii. look like\n     iii. cover                      iv. men and women.','','','i) the earth             ii) the sun\n     iii) air                       iv) one year\n2] much of its surface is covered with water.\n3] i) spine           ii) appear            iii) envelope         iv) human beings.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47467,641,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web\n','Hello, everybody!\nI am the earth.\nI am a planet in the solar system.\nI am the third planet from the sun.\nI spin around myself all the time.\nAt the same time, I go round the sun.\nI take one year to go round the sun.\nMuch of my surface is covered with water.\nIf you look at me from space,\nI appear blue in colour.\nThat is why, some people call me the blue planet.\nThere is an envelope of air around me.\nToday, I am the only home of plants, animals and human beings.\n2] Find the English names of other two plantes.\n3] Use the correct word from the bracket and complete the sentences. \n       [at, from round, with]\n     i. The ground was covered …….. green grass.\n     ii. The earth goes ………. the sun.\n     iii. The king looked ……… the beggar.\n     iv. The earth is the third plant... the sun.','','Plants, animals and human beings.\n2] Saturn, Mars, Jupiter, Mercury, Venus, Uranus, Neptune\n3] i) with          ii) round           iii) at           iv) from ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47468,641,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Name the following\n     i. Our closest neighbour …………….\n     ii. The number of astronauts landed on the moon ………..\n     iii. The moon moves round the ………….\n     iv. ……….. wrote stories and poems about the moon.\nHello, people on earth!\nI am your closest neighbour.\nI go round and round the earth. \nFrom the earth, you always see only one side of me.\nYou never see the other side or the other half of me.\nPeople on earth love me.\nThey write stories and poems about me.\nThey send spacecrafts to study me from close. \nDo you know, two astronauts from earth landed on me once. \nMaybe, some day, other people on earth will also come to visit me. \nI am the moon.\n2] Write four sentences about the moon.\n3] Find out numbers from the passage.','','','i) the moon             ii) two            iii) the earth          iv) people on the earth\n2] The moon is our closest neighbour. It moves around the earth. We can see only one side of the moon. We love the moon.\n3] One, half, two, once','',0,0,0,NULL),(47469,641,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are true or false :\n    i. The sun rises in the west.\n    ii. The sun is a star.\n    iii. The sun goes round the earth.\n    iv. We see the stars at night.\nHello, friends.\nYou see me in the sky every day.\nYou say that I rise in the east and set in the west.\nDid you guess who I am?\nYes, that\'s right! I am the sun.\nActually, I am a star like other stars you see at night.\nBut I am close to the earth.\nThat is why, I look so big and bright.\nI give the earth heat and light.\nAnd remember, I don\'t go round the earth.\nThe earth goes round me.\n2] Complete the following sentences.\n      i. The sun looks ------- and ------------\n      ii. The sun give the earth ----------- and -----------\n      iii. The sun looks big and bright because ------------\n      iv. The earth goes round -------------\n3] From the passage find the opposites of :\n     i. forget x ……….            ii. dim x …………..\n      iii. open x …………..         iv. rise x ……….','','','i) False             ii) True              iii) False                iv) True\n2] i) big and bright                            ii) heat and light\n     iii) it is close to the earth           iv) the sun\n3] i) remember          ii) bright\n     iii) close                   iv) set','',0,0,0,NULL),(47470,641,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Name the following\n     i. The third planet from the sun -----------\n     ii. The earth moves round -------------\n     iii. An envelope around the earth - -------\n     iv. Time taken by the earth to go round the sun --------\nHello, everybody!\nI am the earth.\nI am a planet in the solar system.\nI am the third planet from the sun.\nI spin around myself all the time.\nAt the same time, I go round the sun.\nI take one year to go round the sun.\nMuch of my surface is covered with water.\nIf you look at me from space,\nI appear blue in colour.\nThat is why, some people call me the blue planet.\nThere is an envelope of air around me.\nToday, I am the only home of plants, animals and human beings.\n2] The earth is called the blue planet because ------\n3] From the passage, find out the words that mean :- a\n     i. go round                  ii. look like\n     iii. cover                      iv. men and women.','','','i) the earth             ii) the sun\n     iii) air                       iv) one year\n2] much of its surface is covered with water.\n3] i) spine           ii) appear            iii) envelope         iv) human beings.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47471,641,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web\n','Hello, everybody!\nI am the earth.\nI am a planet in the solar system.\nI am the third planet from the sun.\nI spin around myself all the time.\nAt the same time, I go round the sun.\nI take one year to go round the sun.\nMuch of my surface is covered with water.\nIf you look at me from space,\nI appear blue in colour.\nThat is why, some people call me the blue planet.\nThere is an envelope of air around me.\nToday, I am the only home of plants, animals and human beings.\n2] Find the English names of other two plantes.\n3] Use the correct word from the bracket and complete the sentences. \n       [at, from round, with]\n     i. The ground was covered …….. green grass.\n     ii. The earth goes ………. the sun.\n     iii. The king looked ……… the beggar.\n     iv. The earth is the third plant... the sun.','','Plants, animals and human beings.\n2] Saturn, Mars, Jupiter, Mercury, Venus, Uranus, Neptune\n3] i) with          ii) round           iii) at           iv) from ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47472,641,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Name the following\n     i. Our closest neighbour …………….\n     ii. The number of astronauts landed on the moon ………..\n     iii. The moon moves round the ………….\n     iv. ……….. wrote stories and poems about the moon.\nHello, people on earth!\nI am your closest neighbour.\nI go round and round the earth. \nFrom the earth, you always see only one side of me.\nYou never see the other side or the other half of me.\nPeople on earth love me.\nThey write stories and poems about me.\nThey send spacecrafts to study me from close. \nDo you know, two astronauts from earth landed on me once. \nMaybe, some day, other people on earth will also come to visit me. \nI am the moon.\n2] Write four sentences about the moon.\n3] Find out numbers from the passage.','','','i) the moon             ii) two            iii) the earth          iv) people on the earth\n2] The moon is our closest neighbour. It moves around the earth. We can see only one side of the moon. We love the moon.\n3] One, half, two, once','',0,0,0,NULL),(47473,642,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make meaningful sentences by the given diagram','','','I always eat vegetables.\n2) I never tease animals.\n3) He always quarrels with my friends.\n4) She never wastes food.\n5) I always drink enough water.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47475,642,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make meaningful sentences by the given diagram','','','I always wash my hands.\n2) She always tells lies.\n3) He never wastes food.\n4) They never tease animals.\n5) I never use plastics bags.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47476,642,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make meaningful sentences by the given diagram','','','I always eat vegetables.\n2) I never tease animals.\n3) He always quarrels with my friends.\n4) She never wastes food.\n5) I always drink enough water.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47477,642,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make meaningful sentences by the given diagram','','','I always wash my hands.\n2) She always tells lies.\n3) He never wastes food.\n4) They never tease animals.\n5) I never use plastics bags.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47478,643,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The speaker in the poem is ……………\n     ii. If we clean the book, it will look ………….\n     iii. A book will be our friend if …………..\n     iv. A book won\'t talk to us if ……………\nWhen you drop me on the floor \nI get stepped on - my sides are sore;\nTorn-out pages make me groan; \nI feel dizzy if I\'m thrown;\nEvery mark and every stain \nOn my covers gives me pain;\nPlease don\'t bend me, if you do \nI don\'t want to talk to you;\nBut we will both be friends together, \nIf you protect me from the weather\nAnd keep me clean so that I look \nA tidy, neat and happy book.\n2] Say what happens when :-\n     a) You drop a book on the floor –\n     b) You tear out pages from a book -\n3] Write down two pairs of the rhyming words.','','','i) a book,         ii) tidy, neat and happy\n     iii) we protect it from the weather              iv) we bend it\n2] a) The boo gets stepped on, and its sides are sore.\n     b) It makes the book again.\n3] a) floor = sore          b) groan = thrown.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47479,643,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.\n','When you drop me on the floor \nI get stepped on - my sides are sore;\nTorn-out pages make me groan; \nI feel dizzy if I\'m thrown;\nEvery mark and every stain \nOn my covers gives me pain;\nPlease don\'t bend me, if you do \nI don\'t want to talk to you;\nBut we will both be friends together, \nIf you protect me from the weather\nAnd keep me clean so that I look \nA tidy, neat and happy book.\n2] Say what happens when :-\n     a) You put marks and stains on the book’s cover – \n     b) You throw a book – \n3] Write full forms of :\n     i. I’m                ii. don’t  ','','(i – c), (ii – a), (iii – d), (iv – b)\n2] a) It gives the book pain\n     b) A book feels dizzy\n3] i) I am            ii) do not','',1,0,0,NULL),(47480,643,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The speaker in the poem is ……………\n     ii. If we clean the book, it will look ………….\n     iii. A book will be our friend if …………..\n     iv. A book won\'t talk to us if ……………\nWhen you drop me on the floor \nI get stepped on - my sides are sore;\nTorn-out pages make me groan; \nI feel dizzy if I\'m thrown;\nEvery mark and every stain \nOn my covers gives me pain;\nPlease don\'t bend me, if you do \nI don\'t want to talk to you;\nBut we will both be friends together, \nIf you protect me from the weather\nAnd keep me clean so that I look \nA tidy, neat and happy book.\n2] Say what happens when :-\n     a) You drop a book on the floor –\n     b) You tear out pages from a book -\n3] Write down two pairs of the rhyming words.','','','i) a book,         ii) tidy, neat and happy\n     iii) we protect it from the weather              iv) we bend it\n2] a) The boo gets stepped on, and its sides are sore.\n     b) It makes the book again.\n3] a) floor = sore          b) groan = thrown.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47481,643,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the poem and do the activities :\n1] Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.\n','When you drop me on the floor \nI get stepped on - my sides are sore;\nTorn-out pages make me groan; \nI feel dizzy if I\'m thrown;\nEvery mark and every stain \nOn my covers gives me pain;\nPlease don\'t bend me, if you do \nI don\'t want to talk to you;\nBut we will both be friends together, \nIf you protect me from the weather\nAnd keep me clean so that I look \nA tidy, neat and happy book.\n2] Say what happens when :-\n     a) You put marks and stains on the book’s cover – \n     b) You throw a book – \n3] Write full forms of :\n     i. I’m                ii. don’t  ','','(i – c), (ii – a), (iii – d), (iv – b)\n2] a) It gives the book pain\n     b) A book feels dizzy\n3] i) I am            ii) do not','',1,0,0,NULL),(47482,644,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the sentences\n    i. Carver lost his parents when ---------\n    ii. Carver was born in --------\n    iii. George Washington Carver was ---------\n    iv. Carver did not get admission in schools because ----------\nGeorge Washington Carver was born in 1861. His parents were slaves. His family worked on a farm in America. He lost both his parents when he was still a baby.\nCarver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted, what was wrong with them. Soon, people around the farm began to call the young boy \'plant doctor\'.\nYoung Carver wanted to go to school and then college. But he was a Black boy. Many schools and colleges in those days did not take Black students. Carver did not lose heart. He went from place to place. He found a college where he could study. He studied hard and became a scientist.\n2] From the passage find out the words that mean.\n     i. Father and mother           ii. liked\n     iii. become nervous             iv. field.\n3] Find out the past tense forms of :-\n      i. lose                ii. go','','','i) When he was still a boy             ii) 1861\n     iii) a scientist                                   iv) he was a black boy.\n2] i) parents,           ii) loved,             iii) lose heart,                iv) farm.\n3] i) lost               ii) went','',0,0,0,NULL),(47483,644,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities \n1] Why did people begin to call Carver a plant doctor?\nGeorge Washington Carver was born in 1861. His parents were slaves. His family worked on a farm in America. He lost both his parents when he was still a baby.\nCarver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted, what was wrong with them. Soon, people around the farm began to call the young boy \'plant doctor\'.\nYoung Carver wanted to go to school and then college. But he was a Black boy. Many schools and colleges in those days did not take Black students. Carver did not lose heart. He went from place to place. He found a college where he could study. He studied hard and became a scientist.\n2] a) Who was George Washington Carver?\n     b) When was George Washington Carver born?\n3] Find out the past tense forms of :-\n      i. understand              ii. begin','','','Carver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted and what in was wrong with them. So people began to call him a plant doctor.\n2] a) A black American scientists \n     b) He was born in 1861\n3] i) understood          ii) began','',0,0,0,NULL),(47484,644,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities\n1] Complete the following Web.\n','Carver wanted to help the poor farmers who had small farms in Alabama. He showed them how to get good crops. These farmers grew plants like cotton because there was profit in it. But cotton made the soil poor. Carver told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made the soil rich again. The farmers got very good crops. In fact, the crops were so good that the farmers did not know what to do with so much of them!\nAgain, Carver went to work. He began to look for new uses for these plants. Can you imagine how many uses he found? From peanuts, he made sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, paper, tiles, butter, plastic, milk, as many as 300 things! From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things. Soon, these crops were in great demand and the farmers were happy.\nThis great scientist died in 1943. The place where he worked is now turned into a \'museum\'.\n2] From the passage, find the opposites of:\n     i. bitter              ii. loss\n     iii. old                iv. rich\n3] Find out the words related to agriculture.','','i) sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, milk\n2] i) sweet,             ii) profit,          iii) new         iv) poor\n3] farmer, crops, plants, soil, peanut, bean','',1,0,0,NULL),(47485,644,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities\n1] How did Carver help farmers? \nCarver wanted to help the poor farmers who had small farms in Alabama. He showed them how to get good crops. These farmers grew plants like cotton because there was profit in it. But cotton made the soil poor. Carver told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made the soil rich again. The farmers got very good crops. In fact, the crops were so good that the farmers did not know what to do with so much of them!\nAgain, Carver went to work. He began to look for new uses for these plants. Can you imagine how many uses he found? From peanuts, he made sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, paper, tiles, butter, plastic, milk, as many as 300 things! From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things. Soon, these crops were in great demand and the farmers were happy.\nThis great scientist died in 1943. The place where he worked is now turned into a \'museum\'.\n2] a) What did Carver love?\n     b) What did Carver find? \n3] How many things he made sweet potato?','','','Carver showed the farmers how to get good crops. He told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made soil rich again and the farmers get very good crops.\n2] a) Carver loved plants.\n     b) He found new uses of the ‘useless things’\n3] From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47486,644,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the sentences\n    i. Carver lost his parents when ---------\n    ii. Carver was born in --------\n    iii. George Washington Carver was ---------\n    iv. Carver did not get admission in schools because ----------\nGeorge Washington Carver was born in 1861. His parents were slaves. His family worked on a farm in America. He lost both his parents when he was still a baby.\nCarver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted, what was wrong with them. Soon, people around the farm began to call the young boy \'plant doctor\'.\nYoung Carver wanted to go to school and then college. But he was a Black boy. Many schools and colleges in those days did not take Black students. Carver did not lose heart. He went from place to place. He found a college where he could study. He studied hard and became a scientist.\n2] From the passage find out the words that mean.\n     i. Father and mother           ii. liked\n     iii. become nervous             iv. field.\n3] Find out the past tense forms of :-\n      i. lose                ii. go','','','i) When he was still a boy             ii) 1861\n     iii) a scientist                                   iv) he was a black boy.\n2] i) parents,           ii) loved,             iii) lose heart,                iv) farm.\n3] i) lost               ii) went','',0,0,0,NULL),(47487,644,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities \n1] Why did people begin to call Carver a plant doctor?\nGeorge Washington Carver was born in 1861. His parents were slaves. His family worked on a farm in America. He lost both his parents when he was still a baby.\nCarver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted, what was wrong with them. Soon, people around the farm began to call the young boy \'plant doctor\'.\nYoung Carver wanted to go to school and then college. But he was a Black boy. Many schools and colleges in those days did not take Black students. Carver did not lose heart. He went from place to place. He found a college where he could study. He studied hard and became a scientist.\n2] a) Who was George Washington Carver?\n     b) When was George Washington Carver born?\n3] Find out the past tense forms of :-\n      i. understand              ii. begin','','','Carver loved plants even as a child. He cared for them. He understood what the plants wanted and what in was wrong with them. So people began to call him a plant doctor.\n2] a) A black American scientists \n     b) He was born in 1861\n3] i) understood          ii) began','',0,0,0,NULL),(47488,644,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities\n1] Complete the following Web.\n','Carver wanted to help the poor farmers who had small farms in Alabama. He showed them how to get good crops. These farmers grew plants like cotton because there was profit in it. But cotton made the soil poor. Carver told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made the soil rich again. The farmers got very good crops. In fact, the crops were so good that the farmers did not know what to do with so much of them!\nAgain, Carver went to work. He began to look for new uses for these plants. Can you imagine how many uses he found? From peanuts, he made sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, paper, tiles, butter, plastic, milk, as many as 300 things! From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things. Soon, these crops were in great demand and the farmers were happy.\nThis great scientist died in 1943. The place where he worked is now turned into a \'museum\'.\n2] From the passage, find the opposites of:\n     i. bitter              ii. loss\n     iii. old                iv. rich\n3] Find out the words related to agriculture.','','i) sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, milk\n2] i) sweet,             ii) profit,          iii) new         iv) poor\n3] farmer, crops, plants, soil, peanut, bean','',1,0,0,NULL),(47489,644,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities\n1] How did Carver help farmers? \nCarver wanted to help the poor farmers who had small farms in Alabama. He showed them how to get good crops. These farmers grew plants like cotton because there was profit in it. But cotton made the soil poor. Carver told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made the soil rich again. The farmers got very good crops. In fact, the crops were so good that the farmers did not know what to do with so much of them!\nAgain, Carver went to work. He began to look for new uses for these plants. Can you imagine how many uses he found? From peanuts, he made sugar, ink, boot-polish, colours, soap, paper, tiles, butter, plastic, milk, as many as 300 things! From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things. Soon, these crops were in great demand and the farmers were happy.\nThis great scientist died in 1943. The place where he worked is now turned into a \'museum\'.\n2] a) What did Carver love?\n     b) What did Carver find? \n3] How many things he made sweet potato?','','','Carver showed the farmers how to get good crops. He told them to grow crops like peanuts and beans. These crops made soil rich again and the farmers get very good crops.\n2] a) Carver loved plants.\n     b) He found new uses of the ‘useless things’\n3] From sweet potato, he made as many as 118 things.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47490,645,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame at least 5 to 6 questions.\n        How does / do...........?             What do / does ……….?\n        When do/does …….?               Who ……..?\n        Why do / does / did ……. ?      Where do / does ………?  ','','','How does the rain fall?\n2) How do you go to school?\n3) What do you do?\n4) What does that mean?\n5) When do you get up?\n6) When does your vacation start?','',0,0,0,NULL),(47491,645,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame at least 5 to 6 questions.\n        How does / do...........?             What do / does ……….?\n        When do/does …….?               Who ……..?\n        Why do / does / did ……. ?      Where do / does ………?  ','','','Who are you?\n2) Why do you go there?\n3) Why does the sun shine? \n4) Why did she come?\n5) Where do you live?\n6) Where does he come from?','',0,0,0,NULL),(47492,645,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame at least 5 to 6 questions.\n        How does / do...........?             What do / does ……….?\n        When do/does …….?               Who ……..?\n        Why do / does / did ……. ?      Where do / does ………?  ','','','How does the rain fall?\n2) How do you go to school?\n3) What do you do?\n4) What does that mean?\n5) When do you get up?\n6) When does your vacation start?','',0,0,0,NULL),(47493,645,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Look around, think and frame at least 5 to 6 questions.\n        How does / do...........?             What do / does ……….?\n        When do/does …….?               Who ……..?\n        Why do / does / did ……. ?      Where do / does ………?  ','','','Who are you?\n2) Why do you go there?\n3) Why does the sun shine? \n4) Why did she come?\n5) Where do you live?\n6) Where does he come from?','',0,0,0,NULL),(47494,647,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web\n','We all need money to buy things and to pay people who work for us.\nBut what is \'money\'?\nWho invented \'money\'?\nToday, we see money in the form of coins and notes.\nThese are the coins and notes we use in India.\nBut different countries use different coins and notes.\nTheir money is known by different names.\nIn the United States, they use $ dollars. \nIn England, they use £ pounds.\n2] Match the following \n         Sign              Currency\n      i) ₹                  a) Pound\n      ii) $                 b) Rupee\n      iii) £                c) dollar \n3] Complete the following :\n     We need money to ----------\n     i. ------------             ii. ----------------','','1) coins, notes\n2) (i – b), (ii – c), (iii – a)\n3) i) to buy things          ii) to pay people who work for us.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47495,647,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nBut long, long ago, people did not use coins and notes. They exchanged one thing for another. This was known as \'barter\'.\nIt may seem unbelievable, but long ago people even used special stones as money! Imagine how difficult it must have been to carry this money!\nPeople also used bricks of salt, shells and cowries as money in the past.\nThey gave grain, farm produce, cattle, goats, chickens, etc. in return for things like clothes, tools and implements or as wages.\nThen they began to use bits of precious metals like gold, silver and copper as money. It was easier to carry this money around.\nPaper money was first used in China about 1000 years ago.\n1) In olden days which system was used?\n2) what is a barter system?\n3) What things were used as money?\n4) Who first invented paper money?\n5) Which three metals were used as money?','','','Barter system was used.\n2) A barter system means exchanging one things for another.\n3) Stones, salt, shells, couries, metal.\n4) China first invented paper money.\n5) Gold, Silver and Copper.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47496,647,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web\n','We all need money to buy things and to pay people who work for us.\nBut what is \'money\'?\nWho invented \'money\'?\nToday, we see money in the form of coins and notes.\nThese are the coins and notes we use in India.\nBut different countries use different coins and notes.\nTheir money is known by different names.\nIn the United States, they use $ dollars. \nIn England, they use £ pounds.\n2] Match the following \n         Sign              Currency\n      i) ₹                  a) Pound\n      ii) $                 b) Rupee\n      iii) £                c) dollar \n3] Complete the following :\n     We need money to ----------\n     i. ------------             ii. ----------------','','coins, notes\n2) (i – b), (ii – c), (iii – a)\n3) i) to buy things          ii) to pay people who work for us.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47497,647,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\nBut long, long ago, people did not use coins and notes. They exchanged one thing for another. This was known as \'barter\'.\nIt may seem unbelievable, but long ago people even used special stones as money! Imagine how difficult it must have been to carry this money!\nPeople also used bricks of salt, shells and cowries as money in the past.\nThey gave grain, farm produce, cattle, goats, chickens, etc. in return for things like clothes, tools and implements or as wages.\nThen they began to use bits of precious metals like gold, silver and copper as money. It was easier to carry this money around.\nPaper money was first used in China about 1000 years ago.\n1) In olden days which system was used?\n2) what is a barter system?\n3) What things were used as money?\n4) Who first invented paper money?\n5) Which three metals were used as money?','','','Barter system was used.\n2) A barter system means exchanging one things for another.\n3) Stones, salt, shells, couries, metal.\n4) China first invented paper money.\n5) Gold, Silver and Copper.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47498,648,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n     i. There are hundreds of butterflies ……….\n     ii. Hundreds of birds ………….\n     iii. There are hundreds of bees ………..\n     iv. There are hundreds of lambs ……..\nHundreds of stars in the pretty sky,\nHundreds of shells on the shore together,\nHundreds of birds that go singing by,\nHundreds of lambs in the sunny weather.\nHundreds of dewdrops to greet the dawn,\nHundreds of bees in the purple clover,\nHundreds of butterflies on the lawn,\nBut only one mother the wide world over.\n2] Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.','3] From the extract find out the pairs of rhyming words.  ','','i) on the lawn                           ii) go singing by.\n     iii) in the purple clover           iv) in the sunny weather.\n2) (i – d), (ii – c), (iii – b), (iv – a)\n3) i) sky – by                      ii) bees – butterflies \n     iii) lawn – dawn           iv) clover – over  ','',1,0,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ep_questions` VALUES (47499,648,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n     i. There are hundreds of butterflies ……….\n     ii. Hundreds of birds ………….\n     iii. There are hundreds of bees ………..\n     iv. There are hundreds of lambs ……..\nHundreds of stars in the pretty sky,\nHundreds of shells on the shore together,\nHundreds of birds that go singing by,\nHundreds of lambs in the sunny weather.\nHundreds of dewdrops to greet the dawn,\nHundreds of bees in the purple clover,\nHundreds of butterflies on the lawn,\nBut only one mother the wide world over.\n2] Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.','3] From the extract find out the pairs of rhyming words.  ','','i) on the lawn                           ii) go singing by.\n     iii) in the purple clover           iv) in the sunny weather.\n2) (i – d), (ii – c), (iii – b), (iv – a)\n3) i) sky – by                      ii) bees – butterflies \n     iii) lawn – dawn           iv) clover – over  ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47500,650,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences:\n     i. A new temple was being but built ……………..\n     ii. They tried to make the temple …………..\n     iii. Jaikishan was ……………….\n      iv. Jaikishan carved ……….. in stone.\nAt the time of King Ashoka, a new temple was being built in the capital city. All the people in the capital helped to build the temple. They tried their best to make it strong and beautiful.\nRich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones, and cut the timber. Everyone helped to build the great temple.\nOne day, an old man came to offer his help. He was Jaikishan, the stone carver. He carved beautiful figures and designs in stone. Now he was old, and his hands shook a little. Yet, he was a good workman. He took great care with his work.\n2] a) Which King’s name is there in this passage?\n     b) What did Ashoka want to build?\n3] Correct the following sentence. \n       Reach people gave there money.','','','i) in the capital city                  ii) strong and beautiful.\n     iii) the stone carver                 iv) beautiful figures and designs.\n2) a) Ashoka                b) A temple\n3) Rich people gave their money.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47501,650,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] How did the people in the capital help to build the temple?\nAt the time of King Ashoka, a new temple was being built in the capital city. All the people in the capital helped to build the temple. They tried their best to make it strong and beautiful.\nRich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones, and cut the timber. Everyone helped to build the great temple.\nOne day, an old man came to offer his help. He was Jaikishan, the stone carver. He carved beautiful figures and designs in stone. Now he was old, and his hands shook a little. Yet, he was a good workman. He took great care with his work.\n2] a) What did the rich people give?\n     b) What did the workmen give?\n3] From the passage find out the words that describe the temple.','','','Everyone in the capital helped to build the great temple. Rich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones and cut the timber.\n2) a) Money               b) Best work\n3] new, strong beautiful, great.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47502,650,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n      i. Jaikishan was told to carve a figure of devadoota.\n      ii. People could see the devadoota clearly.\n      iii. Jaikishan carved only the face of the devadoota.\n       iv. The man smiled to see Jaikishan working.\nJaikishan was told to carve the figure of a devadoota in a corner. This corner of the temple was nearly always dark. Here, no one would ever see the devadoota very clearly. People would only see the face of the devadoota clearly.\nJaikishan made a devadoota with a beautiful face. \"But this is not enough,\" he said. He went on carving. He carved the entire devadoota carefully. He carved very well. The garments of the devadoota were carved in stone, but they looked thin and soft.\nOne day a man came to that Scorner. He saw Jaikishan carving the/ devadoota. He saw how beautiful the carving looked. He saw how carefully and lovingly Jaikishan worked. The man smiled. He asked Jaikishan, \"Why do you take such trouble with the garments? No one will see your work in this dark corner. No one will know who did it.\"\n\"That may be,\" said old Jaikishan, \"But God can see my work even in - this dark corner and he knows the workman, too.\"\n2] How did Jaikishan answer the man\'s questions?\n3] What did Jaikishan carve?','','','i) True             ii) False            iii) False              iv) True\n2) Jaikishan answered that God could see his work even in the dark corner. God knew the workman too.\n3) Devadoota','',0,0,0,NULL),(47503,650,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Match the following \n','Jaikishan was told to carve the figure of a devadoota in a corner. This corner of the temple was nearly always dark. Here, no one would ever see the devadoota very clearly. People would only see the face of the devadoota clearly.\nJaikishan made a devadoota with a beautiful face. \"But this is not enough,\" he said. He went on carving. He carved the entire devadoota carefully. He carved very well. The garments of the devadoota were carved in stone, but they looked thin and soft.\nOne day a man came to that Scorner. He saw Jaikishan carving the/ devadoota. He saw how beautiful the carving looked. He saw how carefully and lovingly Jaikishan worked. The man smiled. He asked Jaikishan, \"Why do you take such trouble with the garments? No one will see your work in this dark corner. No one will know who did it.\"\n\"That may be,\" said old Jaikishan, \"But God can see my work even in - this dark corner and he knows the workman, too.\"\n2] a) Who is the carver in this passage?\n    b) Who can see Jaikishan’s work?\n3] Find out the words that end with - ly -','','(i – c), (ii – d), (iii – b), (iv – a)\n2) a) Jaikishan                b) God\n3) nearly, clearly, carefully lovingly.  ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47504,650,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences:\n     i. A new temple was being but built ……………..\n     ii. They tried to make the temple …………..\n     iii. Jaikishan was ……………….\n      iv. Jaikishan carved ……….. in stone.\nAt the time of King Ashoka, a new temple was being built in the capital city. All the people in the capital helped to build the temple. They tried their best to make it strong and beautiful.\nRich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones, and cut the timber. Everyone helped to build the great temple.\nOne day, an old man came to offer his help. He was Jaikishan, the stone carver. He carved beautiful figures and designs in stone. Now he was old, and his hands shook a little. Yet, he was a good workman. He took great care with his work.\n2] a) Which King’s name is there in this passage?\n     b) What did Ashoka want to build?\n3] Correct the following sentence. \n       Reach people gave there money.','','','i) in the capital city                  ii) strong and beautiful.\n     iii) the stone carver                 iv) beautiful figures and designs.\n2) a) Ashoka                b) A temple\n3) Rich people gave their money.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47505,650,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] How did the people in the capital help to build the temple?\nAt the time of King Ashoka, a new temple was being built in the capital city. All the people in the capital helped to build the temple. They tried their best to make it strong and beautiful.\nRich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones, and cut the timber. Everyone helped to build the great temple.\nOne day, an old man came to offer his help. He was Jaikishan, the stone carver. He carved beautiful figures and designs in stone. Now he was old, and his hands shook a little. Yet, he was a good workman. He took great care with his work.\n2] a) What did the rich people give?\n     b) What did the workmen give?\n3] From the passage find out the words that describe the temple.','','','Everyone in the capital helped to build the great temple. Rich people gave their money. Workmen put in their best work. Others helped to prepare the stones and cut the timber.\n2) a) Money               b) Best work\n3] new, strong beautiful, great.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47506,650,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following sentences are True or False.\n      i. Jaikishan was told to carve a figure of devadoota.\n      ii. People could see the devadoota clearly.\n      iii. Jaikishan carved only the face of the devadoota.\n       iv. The man smiled to see Jaikishan working.\nJaikishan was told to carve the figure of a devadoota in a corner. This corner of the temple was nearly always dark. Here, no one would ever see the devadoota very clearly. People would only see the face of the devadoota clearly.\nJaikishan made a devadoota with a beautiful face. \"But this is not enough,\" he said. He went on carving. He carved the entire devadoota carefully. He carved very well. The garments of the devadoota were carved in stone, but they looked thin and soft.\nOne day a man came to that Scorner. He saw Jaikishan carving the/ devadoota. He saw how beautiful the carving looked. He saw how carefully and lovingly Jaikishan worked. The man smiled. He asked Jaikishan, \"Why do you take such trouble with the garments? No one will see your work in this dark corner. No one will know who did it.\"\n\"That may be,\" said old Jaikishan, \"But God can see my work even in - this dark corner and he knows the workman, too.\"\n2] How did Jaikishan answer the man\'s questions?\n3] What did Jaikishan carve?','','','i) True             ii) False            iii) False              iv) True\n2) Jaikishan answered that God could see his work even in the dark corner. God knew the workman too.\n3) Devadoota','',0,0,0,NULL),(47507,650,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Match the following \n','Jaikishan was told to carve the figure of a devadoota in a corner. This corner of the temple was nearly always dark. Here, no one would ever see the devadoota very clearly. People would only see the face of the devadoota clearly.\nJaikishan made a devadoota with a beautiful face. \"But this is not enough,\" he said. He went on carving. He carved the entire devadoota carefully. He carved very well. The garments of the devadoota were carved in stone, but they looked thin and soft.\nOne day a man came to that Scorner. He saw Jaikishan carving the/ devadoota. He saw how beautiful the carving looked. He saw how carefully and lovingly Jaikishan worked. The man smiled. He asked Jaikishan, \"Why do you take such trouble with the garments? No one will see your work in this dark corner. No one will know who did it.\"\n\"That may be,\" said old Jaikishan, \"But God can see my work even in - this dark corner and he knows the workman, too.\"\n2] a) Who is the carver in this passage?\n    b) Who can see Jaikishan’s work?\n3] Find out the words that end with - ly -','','(i – c), (ii – d), (iii – b), (iv – a)\n2) a) Jaikishan                b) God\n3) nearly, clearly, carefully lovingly.  ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47540,446,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities.\n1) A) Complete the sentences. ------------ 1M\n\n     i. If the baby doesn’t sleep --------------------------------------------\n   B] Have you noticed the changed mood of the mother in the stanza?\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \nSleep, baby, sleep! \nAnd bleat not like a sheep, \nOr else the shepherd\'s angry dog\nWill come and bite my naughty rogue; \nSleep, baby, sleep!\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nGo out and herd, the sheep,\nGo out, you barking black dog, go\nAnd waken not my baby so;\nSleep, baby, sleep!\n2) Complete the following table.','i) -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nii) --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Write down to pairs of rhyming words.\n \n     -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','A] the shepherds angry dog will bite it.\n     B] Yes, the mother’s changed mood is clearly visible. Earlier the mother warns the baby that the dog will bite it. But here she orders the dog to go out and let the baby sleep quietly.\n2) i) Mother asks the baby to go out and herd the sheep.\n    ii) She asks the dog not to waken her baby.\n3) i) sleep – sheep          ii) go – so ','',1,0,0,NULL),(47541,447,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities :\n1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false\n     i. Chaturbhuj was angry with all.\n     ii. Chaturbhuj decided to leave at once.\n     iii. Mother cared more for the cat than for Chaturbhuj:\n      iv. Mother asked Chaturbhuj to bathe first.\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(Inside Chaturbhuj\'s house.)\nMaid : Mother, Dada Babu has come home in a blazing temper.\nMother : Why, what\'s the Why, what\'s the matter \nMaid : I don\'t know (Enter Chaturbhuj.)\nLittle Boy : Dada, can I have this cat?\nChaturbhuj : (slaps him hard.) Cat, cat, cat the whole day long, is it?\nMother : No wonder the poor boy\'s angry! He comes home after such a long time and these brats start annoying him at once. (To Chaturbhuj) Let me have the cat, son. I\'ll give it some rice and milk that I\'ve put by.\nChaturbhuj  : (furiously) Here, mother, you can have the cat and feed it all\nyou want. I shan\'t stop to eat - I\'m leaving at once.\nMother : (plaintively) What makes you say such a thing? - Your meal\'s ready and waiting, dear. You can sit down to it as soon as you\'ve had your bath!\n2) Find out the words that mean\n    i. naughty kids               ii. making angry.\n    iii. angrily                        iv. in a sad way. \n\n    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Punctuate the following.\n     mother said what makes you say such a thing.\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) True         ii) Ture            iii) False          iv) True\n2) i) brats         ii) annoying        iii) furiously        iv) plaintively\n3) Mother said, \" What makes you say such a thing?\"','',0,0,0,NULL),(47542,447,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities .\n1) Was Chaturbhuj was angry with mother too why?\n\n      ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(Inside Chaturbhuj\'s house.)\nMaid : Mother, Dada Babu has come home in a blazing temper.\nMother : Why, what\'s the Why, what\'s the matter \nMaid : I don\'t know (Enter Chaturbhuj.)\nLittle Boy : Dada, can I have this cat?\nChaturbhuj : (slaps him hard.) Cat, cat, cat the whole day long, is it?\nMother : No wonder the poor boy\'s angry! He comes home after such a long time and these brats start annoying him at once. (To Chaturbhuj) Let me have the cat, son. I\'ll give it some rice and milk that I\'ve put by.\nChaturbhuj  : (furiously) Here, mother, you can have the cat and feed it all\nyou want. I shan\'t stop to eat - I\'m leaving at once.\nMother : (plaintively) What makes you say such a thing? - Your meal\'s ready and waiting, dear. You can sit down to it as soon as you\'ve had your bath!\n2) Name the tense of the following sentences \n    i. We had completed the work\n    ii. She has written a story.\n\n      i)  ------------------------------------        ii) ----------------------------------------------\n3) Do you think that Chaturbhuj\'s mother cared more for the cat than for her son?\n\n        -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Yes, Chaturbhuj was angry with mother too. Because she cared more for the cat and not for Chaturbhuj. She offered some rice and milk to cat. But she did not ask Chaturbhuj about his meal.\n2) i) Perfect Past tense           ii) Perfect Present Tense\n3) No, I don\'t think so. Mother cared for the cat as well as for Chaturbhuj. She offered some rice and milk to the cat. But before that she had made Chaturbhuj\'s meal ready. And she was waiting for Chaturbhuj to have bath and take his meal. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47543,448,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities.\n1) Name the following\n\n     i. A tiny settlement : --------------------                  ii. A quack : -----------------------\n\n     iii. Mountain : -------------------                             iv. Hospital : ------------------------\nHundreds of people flock every hour to Ralewadi, a tiny settlement near Ambegaon. They wait in long winding queues in the hot sun to get a \'special\' powder from Miribaba who claims that it cures all sorts of diseases. According to him, he has developed this powder from a special herb in the Himalayas. \nUnwilling to give the exact name and source of the herb, he only says that it was revealed to him. He offers it at Rs 25 a packet. Each packet is specially \'blessed\' by him. Some of his followers claim that they have seen him euring thousands of people with his powder. However, Dr Karnik of Ambegaon Civil Hospital says that the number of patients in their hospital has in fact increased in the last few weeks.\n2) From the passage find out the opposites of :\n\n     i. cold x ---------------                         ii. decreased x ----------------------\n\n     iii. huge x -----------------                      iv. cursed x -------------------------\n3) Frame \'wh questions\' to get ‘Ralewadi’ words by answers.\n     Hundered of people flock every hour to Ralewadi\n\n     -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Ralewadi              ii) Miribaba\n     iii) Himalaya            iv) Ambegaon Civil Hospital\n2) i) hot                       ii) increased\n     iii) tiny                     iv) blessed\n3) Where do hunderds of people flock every hour?','',0,0,0,NULL),(47544,448,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities.\n1) Complete the following web :\n','\n   ---------------------------------------         ----------------------------------\n\n   ---------------------------------------         ----------------------------------\nHundreds of people flock every hour to Ralewadi, a tiny settlement near Ambegaon. They wait in long winding queues in the hot sun to get a \'special\' powder from Miribaba who claims that it cures all sorts of diseases. According to him, he has developed this powder from a special herb in the Himalayas. \nUnwilling to give the exact name and source of the herb, he only says that it was revealed to him. He offers it at Rs 25 a packet. Each packet is specially \'blessed\' by him. Some of his followers claim that they have seen him euring thousands of people with his powder. However, Dr Karnik of Ambegaon Civil Hospital says that the number of patients in their hospital has in fact increased in the last few weeks.\n2) Frame \'wh questions\' to get ‘sorts of diseases’ words by answers.\n     It cures all sorts of diseases.\n\n     ----------------------------------------------------------------\n3) What shows that Miribaba\'s claim is unreliable?\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','Special powder: Miribaba claims that his special powder cures all sort of diseases. He has developed it from a special herb in the Himalayas. He offers it at Rs. 25 a packet.\n2) What does it cure?\n3) Miribaba claims that his special powder cures all sorts of diseases. As a result of his claim, the number of patients should have decreased. But Dr. Karnik points out the that number of patients has increased. It shows that Miribaba\'s claim is unreliable.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47545,449,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the extract and do the activities.\nYour friends will all forget you. \nYou won\'t be even missed. \nYour family, too, will likely \nforget that you exist.\nAnd all because you read this \ninstead of having fun. \nIt\'s too late now, amigo;\nthe poem\'s nearly done.\nThere\'s only one solution.\nHere\'s what you\'ll have to do : \nTell all your friends and family \nthey shouldn\'t read it too.\nAnswer the following questions :\n1) The poet is telling someone not to read the poem. Who is that someone?\n\n      --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Does the poet really want you to stop reading the poem? \n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) What does the poet mean when he says, \"Tell all your friends & family they should not read it too\".\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4) Find all contracted forms used in the poem. \n\n      --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Find the pairs of rhyming words used in each stanza. \n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','It must be the poet\'s friend.\n2) No, he does not want so.\n3) He means that all friends & family should read it.\n4) don\'t, it\'s, that\'s, there\'s, isn\'t, won\'t, Here\'s, shouldn\'t.\n5) fun – done, do – too, family – likely ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47546,450,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities.\n1) Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The revolver fell down on the floor because -----------------------------\n\n     ii. The man\'s companion had ---------------------------------------------\nWe waited silently for more than an hour. It was pitch dark in the cellar. Then suddenly, a point of bright light appeared in the floor of the cellar, then a line, and a gash seemed to open, and a hand appeared. A broad stone turned over upon its side, and left a square hele. A boyish face emerged. \nThe man looked about and came out of the hole. He had a companion with him, a man with very red hair. The pair was none other than Spaulding the assistant alias the criminal Clay and the red-headed Mr Duncan Ross! As soon as they climbed out of the hole, Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. \nThe other dived down the hole and disappeared. Clay took out a revolver. But Holmes hit him on his wrist, and the revolver fell on the floor.\n2) From the passage find out two adjectives and two adverbs.\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Change the following sentence into a question and negative sentence.\n      There was pitch dark in the cellar.\n\n       -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Holmes hit him on his wrist.           ii) very red hair.\n2) i) Adjectives = dark, bright         ii) Adverbs = silently, suddenly\n3) i) Question - Was there pitch dark in the cellar?\n    ii) Negative - There was not pitch dark in the cellar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47547,450,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the passage and do the activities.\n1) Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.\n     i. Holmess seized him by the collar\n     ii. A broad stone turned over upon its side.\n     iii. A point of bright light appeared in the floor.\n      iv. A man came out of the hole.\n\n      -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe waited silently for more than an hour. It was pitch dark in the cellar. Then suddenly, a point of bright light appeared in the floor of the cellar, then a line, and a gash seemed to open, and a hand appeared. A broad stone turned over upon its side, and left a square hele. A boyish face emerged. \nThe man looked about and came out of the hole. He had a companion with him, a man with very red hair. The pair was none other than Spaulding the assistant alias the criminal Clay and the red-headed Mr Duncan Ross! As soon as they climbed out of the hole, Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. \nThe other dived down the hole and disappeared. Clay took out a revolver. But Holmes hit him on his wrist, and the revolver fell on the floor.\n2) Who were the two criminals?\n\n       ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Find out the sentences from the passage that show that Holmes was a very brave man.\n\n       ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','iii, ii, iv, i\n2) Spaulding the assistant alias Clay and red-headed Mr. Duncan Ross.\n3) Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. Holmes hit him on his wrist and the revolver fell on the floor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47548,452,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Say whether the following statements are true or false :\n\n    i. Mama Pangolin used her claws to eat ants and termites. : --------------------------------\n\n    ii. Baby Pangolin did not like termites : ---------------------------------\nBy now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.\n2] Complete the following sentences.\n\n      i. Pangolin\'s claws are -----------------------------------------\n\n      ii. Pangolin\'s tongue is -------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out the opposites of :\n\n     i. weak : ---------------           ii. short : ------------------         \n   \n      iii. sad : ---------------            iv. fast  : ------------------','','','i) False                ii) False\n2] i) strong, sharp and curved.         ii) half foot long, sticky. \n3] i) strong       ii) long         iii) happy        iv) slowly','',0,0,0,NULL),(47549,452,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] How do Pangolins get their food?\n\n     -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     -----------------------------------------------------------------------\nBy now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.\n2] Do as directed.\n      i. in and out went his tiny tongue. \n          (Arrange the words in correct order)\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. Baby could do it (Use \'able to\')\n\n        -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] How did Mother Pangolin teach her baby to find its food?\n\n    -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Pangolins are ant eating animals. They wander for food at night. They demolish the anthill with their claws. When the termits begin to run here and there they begin to devour them with their sticky tongue.\n2] i) His tiny tongue went in and out.\n     ii) Baby was able to do it.\n3] When the baby Pangolin was one month old, Mother Pangolin took it out for ant-eating. She demolish- ed the anthill and began to devour the termites Baby watched mother and tried to follow her.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47550,453,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences.\n\n      i. The animal\'s electric glow disappeared when..................................\n\n      ii. They were disappointed and angry because ....................................................\nAt six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.\n2] Find out four words related to time.\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write the following words in correct order.\n     midday, night, evening, morning, dawn, midnight.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) day began to break.\n     ii) a very dense morning mist spread around them.\n2] day, dawn, morning, o’clock\n3] dawn → morning midday → evening → night → midnight','',0,0,0,NULL),(47551,453,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.\n     i. The horizon grew wider and clearer.\n     ii. The day began to break.\n     iii. A very dense morning mist spread around us.\n     iv. We were disappointed and angry.\n\n   ----------------------------------------             -----------------------------------------------\n\n   ------------------------------------------           ---------------------------------------------\nAt six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.\n2] How long does the whole event described in this passage take?\n\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Find out the subject and object from the sentence.\n     Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it.\n\n     ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Chronological order : ii → iii → iv → i\n2] All night long\n3] Subject - our best spyglasses; object - it','',0,0,0,NULL),(47552,454,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities \n1] Name the following\n\n     i. The river : ---------------------------------       \n     \n      ii. Sea-animals : ---------------------------            \n\n       iii. Colour : ---------------------------------\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write down pairs of rhyming words.\n\n     -----------------------------------             ------------------------------------------------','','','i) Nile        ii) Crocodile, Fishes       iii) golden\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] i) crocodile – Nile           ii) claws – jaws ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47553,454,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following extract and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences\n\n      i. The crocodile pours water on.................................................................\n\n      ii. With smiling jaws she welcomes in ----------------------------------------\nHow doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the Nile \nOn every golden scale!\nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n2] Answer the following questions\n     i. What does the little crocodile pour the waters on?\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n     ii. Whom does the crocodile welcome?\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] Write down the organs of crocodiles body.\n\n     -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n      -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) every golden scale              ii) little fishes.\n2] i) The little crocodile pours the waters on every golden scale.\n    ii) The crocodile welcomes little fishes.\n3] tail, scale, claws, jaws','',0,0,0,NULL),(47554,455,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following sentences\n\n     i. All the children ran away when……….............................................\n\n     ii. The little boy did not run away because……….........................................\nSo, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.\n2] Write down four adverbs from the passage.\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] What change do you notice in the behaviour of the giant?\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) ........they saw the giant\nii)...........his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming.\n2] softly, gently, away, only\n3] At first the giant drove the children away from his garden. He built a high wall around his garden. But after- wards he realised his mistake. And he invited children to play in his garden. He koncked down the wall. Moreover the giant started playing with the children.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47555,455,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','Read the following passage and do the activities :\n1] Complete the following web\n','\n----------------------------------                ----------------------------------------------\n\n-----------------------------------               ---------------------------------------------\nSo, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.\n2] The giant knocked down the wall. [Find out the subject and object]\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3] What do your prefer summer or winter? Why?\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','The trees broke into blossom. The birds came and sing on the tree. All the children came running back. And with them, came the spring.\n2] Subject = the giant object - the wall.\n3] I like winter because winter is wonder season. It is a season of enthusiasm and energy. It is a harvest season in Maharashtra.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47557,576,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','(Inside Chaturbhuj\'s house.)\nMaid : Mother, Dada Babu has come home in a blazing temper.\nMother : Why, what\'s the Why, what\'s the matter \nMaid : I don\'t know (Enter Chaturbhuj.)\nLittle Boy : Dada, can I have this cat?\nChaturbhuj : (slaps him hard.) Cat, cat, cat the whole day long, is it?\nMother : No wonder the poor boy\'s angry! He comes home after such a long time and these brats start annoying him at once. (To Chaturbhuj) Let me have the cat, son. I\'ll give it some rice and milk that I\'ve put by.\nChaturbhuj  : (furiously) Here, mother, you can have the cat and feed it all\nyou want. I shan\'t stop to eat - I\'m leaving at once.\nMother : (plaintively) What makes you say such a thing? - Your meal\'s ready and waiting, dear. You can sit down to it as soon as you\'ve had your bath!','<b>1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false</b>\n     i. Chaturbhuj was angry with all.\n     ii. Chaturbhuj decided to leave at once.\n     iii. Mother cared more for the cat than for Chaturbhuj:\n      iv. Mother asked Chaturbhuj to bathe first.\n','<b>2) Find out the words that mean</b>\n    i. naughty kids               ii. making angry.\n    iii. angrily                        iv. in a sad way. \n<b>3) Punctuate the following.</b>\n     mother said what makes you say such a thing.','','i) True         ii) Ture            iii) False          iv) True\n2) i) brats         ii) annoying        iii) furiously        iv) plaintively\n3) Mother said, \" What makes you say such a thing?\"','',0,0,0,NULL),(47558,576,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','(Inside Chaturbhuj\'s house.)\nMaid : Mother, Dada Babu has come home in a blazing temper.\nMother : Why, what\'s the Why, what\'s the matter \nMaid : I don\'t know (Enter Chaturbhuj.)\nLittle Boy : Dada, can I have this cat?\nChaturbhuj : (slaps him hard.) Cat, cat, cat the whole day long, is it?\nMother : No wonder the poor boy\'s angry! He comes home after such a long time and these brats start annoying him at once. (To Chaturbhuj) Let me have the cat, son. I\'ll give it some rice and milk that I\'ve put by.\nChaturbhuj  : (furiously) Here, mother, you can have the cat and feed it all\nyou want. I shan\'t stop to eat - I\'m leaving at once.\nMother : (plaintively) What makes you say such a thing? - Your meal\'s ready and waiting, dear. You can sit down to it as soon as you\'ve had your bath!','<b>1) Was Chaturbhuj was angry with mother too why?</b>\n','<b>2) Name the tense of the following sentences </b>\n    i. We had completed the work\n    ii. She has written a story.\n<b>3) Do you think that Chaturbhuj\'s mother cared more for the cat than for her son?</b>','','Yes, Chaturbhuj was angry with mother too. Because she cared more for the cat and not for Chaturbhuj. She offered some rice and milk to cat. But she did not ask Chaturbhuj about his meal.\n2) i) Perfect Past tense           ii) Perfect Present Tense\n3) No, I don\'t think so. Mother cared for the cat as well as for Chaturbhuj. She offered some rice and milk to the cat. But before that she had made Chaturbhuj\'s meal ready. And she was waiting for Chaturbhuj to have bath and take his meal. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47559,577,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Hundreds of people flock every hour to Ralewadi, a tiny settlement near Ambegaon. They wait in long winding queues in the hot sun to get a \'special\' powder from Miribaba who claims that it cures all sorts of diseases. According to him, he has developed this powder from a special herb in the Himalayas. \nUnwilling to give the exact name and source of the herb, he only says that it was revealed to him. He offers it at Rs 25 a packet. Each packet is specially \'blessed\' by him. Some of his followers claim that they have seen him euring thousands of people with his powder. However, Dr Karnik of Ambegaon Civil Hospital says that the number of patients in their hospital has in fact increased in the last few weeks.','<b>1) Name the following</b>\n     i. A tiny settlement                   ii. A quack\n     iii. Mountain                              iv. Hospital\n','<b>2) From the passage find out the opposites of:</b>\n     i. cold x                          ii. decreased x\n     iii. huge x                       iv. cursed x\n<b>3) Frame \'wh questions\' to get ‘Ralewadi’ words by answers.</b>\n     Hundered of people flock every hour to Ralewadi','','i) Ralewadi              ii) Miribaba\n     iii) Himalaya            iv) Ambegaon Civil Hospital\n2) i) hot                       ii) increased\n     iii) tiny                     iv) blessed\n3) Where do hunderds of people flock every hour?','',0,0,0,NULL),(47561,578,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Your friends will all forget you. \nYou won\'t be even missed. \nYour family, too, will likely \nforget that you exist.\nAnd all because you read this \ninstead of having fun. \nIt\'s too late now, amigo;\nthe poem\'s nearly done.\nThere\'s only one solution.\nHere\'s what you\'ll have to do : \nTell all your friends and family \nthey shouldn\'t read it too.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>Answer the following questions </b>\n1) The poet is telling someone not to read the poem. Who is that someone?\n2) Does the poet really want you to stop reading the poem? \n3) What does the poet mean when he says, \"Tell all your friends & family they should not read it too\".\n4) Find all contracted forms used in the poem. \n5) Find the pairs of rhyming words used in each stanza.','','','It must be the poet\'s friend.\n2) No, he does not want so.\n3) He means that all friends & family should read it.\n4) don\'t, it\'s, that\'s, there\'s, isn\'t, won\'t, Here\'s, shouldn\'t.\n5) fun – done, do – too, family – likely ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47562,579,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','We waited silently for more than an hour. It was pitch dark in the cellar. Then suddenly, a point of bright light appeared in the floor of the cellar, then a line, and a gash seemed to open, and a hand appeared. A broad stone turned over upon its side, and left a square hele. A boyish face emerged. \nThe man looked about and came out of the hole. He had a companion with him, a man with very red hair. The pair was none other than Spaulding the assistant alias the criminal Clay and the red-headed Mr Duncan Ross! As soon as they climbed out of the hole, Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. \nThe other dived down the hole and disappeared. Clay took out a revolver. But Holmes hit him on his wrist, and the revolver fell on the floor.','<b>1) Complete the following sentences.</b>\n     i. The revolver fell down on the floor because -----------\n     ii. The man\'s companion had ------------\n','<b>2) From the passage find out two adjectives and two adverbs.</b>\n<b>3) Change the following sentence into a question and negative sentence.</b>\n      There was pitch dark in the cellar.','','i) Holmes hit him on his wrist.           ii) very red hair.\n2) i) Adjectives = dark, bright         ii) Adverbs = silently, suddenly\n3) i) Question - Was there pitch dark in the cellar?\n    ii) Negative - There was not pitch dark in the cellar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47563,579,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','We waited silently for more than an hour. It was pitch dark in the cellar. Then suddenly, a point of bright light appeared in the floor of the cellar, then a line, and a gash seemed to open, and a hand appeared. A broad stone turned over upon its side, and left a square hele. A boyish face emerged. \nThe man looked about and came out of the hole. He had a companion with him, a man with very red hair. The pair was none other than Spaulding the assistant alias the criminal Clay and the red-headed Mr Duncan Ross! As soon as they climbed out of the hole, Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. \nThe other dived down the hole and disappeared. Clay took out a revolver. But Holmes hit him on his wrist, and the revolver fell on the floor.','<b>1) Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.</b>\n     i. Holmess seized him by the collar\n     ii. A broad stone turned over upon its side.\n     iii. A point of bright light appeared in the floor.\n      iv. A man came out of the hole.\n','<b>2) Who were the two criminals?</b>\n<b>3) Find out the sentences from the passage that show that Holmes was a very brave man.</b>','','iii, ii, iv, i\n2) Spaulding the assistant alias Clay and red-headed Mr. Duncan Ross.\n3) Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar. Holmes hit him on his wrist and the revolver fell on the floor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47564,581,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','By now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.','<b>1] Say whether the following statements are true or false</b>\n    i. Mama Pangolin used her claws to eat ants and termites.\n    ii. Baby Pangolin did not like termites\n','<b>2] Complete the following sentences.</b>\n      i. Pangolin\'s claws are ------------------\n      ii. Pangolin\'s tongue is -----------------\n<b>3] Find out the opposites of </b>\n     i. weak            ii. short          iii. sad             iv. fast','','i) False                ii) False\n2] i) strong, sharp and curved.         ii) half foot long, sticky. \n3] i) strong       ii) long         iii) happy        iv) slowly','',0,0,0,NULL),(47565,581,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','By now, Mama and Baby had reached the anthill. Mama used her strong, sharp and curved front claws to quickly demolish the anthill. The termites inside began to run helter-skelter. In and out, in and out went Mother\'s one and a half foot long sticky tongue. She began to devour the termites.\n\"Look, Baby, they\'re delicious! Eat them up! Quickly! This is how we pangolins gobble up termites!\"\nBaby watched Mother and tried to follow her example. Slowly, slowly, Yes! Baby could do it too! In and out, in and out went his tiny tongue. This was his first solid meal after being nursed on mother\'s milk for a month. And he found the termites truly delicious! Just like Mother had said they would be! She, too, was happy.','<b>1] How do Pangolins get their food?</b>\n','<b>2] Do as directed.</b>\n      i. in and out went his tiny tongue. \n          (Arrange the words in correct order)\n     ii. Baby could do it (Use \'able to\')\n<b>3] How did Mother Pangolin teach her baby to find its food?</b>','','Pangolins are ant eating animals. They wander for food at night. They demolish the anthill with their claws. When the termits begin to run here and there they begin to devour them with their sticky tongue.\n2] i) His tiny tongue went in and out.\n     ii) Baby was able to do it.\n3] When the baby Pangolin was one month old, Mother Pangolin took it out for ant-eating. She demolish- ed the anthill and began to devour the termites Baby watched mother and tried to follow her.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47566,582,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','At six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.','<b>1] Complete the following sentences.</b>\n      i. The animal\'s electric glow disappeared when........\n      ii. They were disappointed and angry because\n','<b>2] Find out four words related to time.</b>\n<b>3] Write the following words in correct order.</b>\n     midday, night, evening, morning, dawn, midnight.','','i) day began to break.\n     ii) a very dense morning mist spread around them.\n2] day, dawn, morning, o’clock\n3] dawn → morning midday → evening → night → midnight','',0,0,0,NULL),(47567,582,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','At six o\'clock day began to break, and with the dawn\'s early light, the animal\'s electric glow disappeared. At seven o\'clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger.\nAt eight o\'clock the mist rolled away, and the of: horizon grew wider and clearer.\nSuddenly, Ned Land\'s voice could be heard. \"There\'s the thing in question, astern to port!\" the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated.','<b>1] Arrange the following sentences in chronological order.</b>\n     i. The horizon grew wider and clearer.\n     ii. The day began to break.\n     iii. A very dense morning mist spread around us.\n     iv. We were disappointed and angry.\n','<b>2] How long does the whole event described in this passage take?</b>\n<b>3] Find out the subject and object from the sentence.</b>\n     Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it.','','Chronological order : ii → iii → iv → i\n2] All night long\n3] Subject - our best spyglasses; object - it','',0,0,0,NULL),(47568,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The organelle called the --------------- is present in plant cells only.','','','Plastids ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47569,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Garbage is converted into ---------------- by micro-organisms.','','','manure ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47570,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','In the cell, photosynthesis is carried out with the help of ---------------------','','','chloroplast ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47571,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','An electron microscope is necessary for the study of ---------------------','','','microbes ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47572,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----------------- is the powerhouse of the cell.','','','mitochondria','',0,0,0,NULL),(47573,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The -------------------- in plant cell is lesser.','','','cytoplasm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47574,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The process of -------------------- is used for making yoghurts.','','','fermentation','',0,0,0,NULL),(47575,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The -------------------- compounds help to increase soil fertility.','','','nitrogenous','',0,0,0,NULL),(47576,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','--------------------------- assembled various lenses to construct a microscope.','','','Anton van Leeuwenhoek','',0,0,0,NULL),(47577,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ------------------- is the most important organelle of the cell.','','','nucleus','',0,0,0,NULL),(47578,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------------- are called the powerhouses of the cell.','','','Mitochondria ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47579,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The process of -------------------------- is used for making yoghurt from milk.','','','fermentation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47580,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Some microbes release toxic materials called ----------------------- into the food.','','','enterotoxins','',0,0,0,NULL),(47581,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Pathogens are released in the air when a person having an infection of the ---------------------- sneezes or coughs.','','','respiratory tract  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47582,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The smallest unit of a body is -----\na) Cell		b) tissue 		c) Organ 		d) Stomach \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','a) Cell','',0,0,0,NULL),(47583,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------- is used for observing cells.\na) telescope 		b) microscope 	c) electroscope 	d) none of these\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','b) microscope ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47584,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ---- is the most important organelle of the cell.\na) Cytoplasm 		b) Cell wall 		c) Nucleolus		d) Chloroplast\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','c) Nucleolus	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47585,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ is the powerhouse of the cell.\na) Golgi bodies 	b) Vacuoles 	c) Lysosomes 		d) Mitochondria\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','d) Mitochondria','',0,0,0,NULL),(47586,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a unicellular organisms -----\na) Paramecium 		b) Ant 		c) Butterfly 		d) Rat\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','a) Paramecium 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47587,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The body temperature of a healthy human being is about ----\na) 20⁰C			b) 35⁰C		c) 37⁰C		d) 27⁰C\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','c) 37⁰C','',0,0,0,NULL),(47588,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ----in plant cells carry out the function of photosynthesis? \na) Cell wall 		b) Cytoplasm		c) Chloroplasts 		d) Nucleus\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','c) Chloroplasts ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47589,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The size of paramecium is about -----\na) 1000 micrometers 	b) 28 micrometers   	c) 100 micrometers 	d) 28 nanometers\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','c) 100 micrometers ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47590,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mitochondria and ----- are organelle with double outer covering.\na) Plastids 		b) A cell membrane 		c) Nucleolus 		d) Vacuole \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','a) Plastids ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47591,795,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The largest gland in the human body is ------\na) Salivary gland 		b) Liver 		c) Endocrine 		d) None\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','b) Liver ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47592,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : We can see the cells with the naked eye.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47593,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Plant cells have a definite shape due to the presence of the cell wall around them.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47594,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : The nucleus controls all functions of the cell.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47595,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Micro-organisms are present only in water.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47596,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Antibiotics are produced with the help of specific microbes.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47597,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Mitochondrion, Vacuoles, Cell wall, Nucleus\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Cell wall (Cell wall present in only plant cell rest are all present in both plant and animal cell)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47598,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Cholera, Malaria, Typhoid, Hepatitis\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Malaria (Malaria is infected by mosquito rest are all spread through contaminated water)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47599,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Dengue, Yellow fever, Zika fever, Pneumonia\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Pneumonia (Pneumonia infected by contaminated air, rest are all spread by mosquitoes.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47600,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Common cold, Elephantiasis, Cough, Diphtheria\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Elephantiasis (Elephantiasis is spread by female mosquito)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47601,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What happens to the sweet meal or bread forgotten in a lunch-box for three or four days?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','If sweetmeat is not consumed in time it becomes rotten and hence cannot be eaten The microbes make the sweetmeats bad. The bread usually shown growth of fungus over it, if kept just like that in a lunch box.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47602,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What happens if clothes remain damp in the rainy season?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Due to humid atmosphere, the clothes do not dry completely during rainy season. Such damp clothes emit foul odour. The flies and other insects hover around such clothes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47603,795,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the black or white spot sometime seen on gunny bags?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The black or white spots seen on gunny bags are due to the fungal growth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47604,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – d), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(47605,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(47606,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which units are used to measure the size of the cells?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Micrometre and nanometre are the units used to measure the size of the cell.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47607,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What kind of microscope is used for observing cell?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The compound microscope is used for observing cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47608,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where does the micro-organisms present?.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Micro-organisms are present in the air, water, soil, food, sewage, garbage as well as in the bodies of plant and animals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47609,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who was stated that all cells are formed from pre-existing cells?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Rudolph Virchow was stated that all cells are formed from pre-existing cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47610,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Micro-organisms\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The organisms which cannot be seen with our eyes but only be observed under a microscope are called micro-organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47611,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Fermentation\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The process in which the carbon compound is converted into another type of carbon compound by the action of micro-organisms is called fermentation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47612,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Pathogens\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The pathogens are the biological agent causes disease to the host are called pathogenes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47613,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the roots of the pea, bean and fenugreek plants. what could be the function of the nodules on their roots.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The root nodules converts the atmospheric nitrogen into its compounds.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47614,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does your mother do the make yoghurt from milk?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','My mother adds few drops of previous yoghurt to make yoghurt from milk.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47615,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who discovered the process of fermentation? \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The French chemist Louis Pasteur is discovered the process of fermentation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47616,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are infants vaccinated according to a fixed times schedule?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Vaccins helps to fight against the serious diseases.\nii) Vaccination is not possible to give all time, so doctor draws a fixed times schedule.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47617,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are lather articles like purses wallets, belts, footwear always polished before storing away.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','To avoid the fungal growth lather artificial like purses, wallets, belts, footwear always polished before storing away.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47618,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Where does the life process of unicellular organisms take place?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Life process of unicellular organisms take place within the cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47619,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How do the cells acquire definite shape?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The cells acquire definite shape due to their cell wall and plasma membrane.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47620,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How are cells protected?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The cell wall of the plant cell protect the cell. Some protective proteins helps to fight against the harmful microbes. Plasma membrane does not allow any other substance in the cell. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47621,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is vaccine?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Vaccine is the process in which killed microorganisms are used to increase the immunity against the specific diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47622,795,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the black or white spot sometime seen on gunny bags?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The black or white spot are seen gunny bags are the fungi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47623,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Plant cell and Animal cell\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47624,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Prokaryotic Cell and Eukaryotic cell\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47625,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is a ‘cell’?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Cell is the fundamental, structural and functional unit of living organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47626,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the different organelles in a cell?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Nucleus, endoplasmic reticulum, ribosome, Golgi body, lysosomes, plastids, vacuoles, mitochondria, chloroplast, are some organelles present in the cells.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47627,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are micro-organisms?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The organisms which can not be seen with our eyes but can only be observed under a micro scope are called micro-organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47628,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which are the different types of micro-organisms?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Bacteria, viruses, fungi, algae, and protozoa are all the different types of micro- organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47629,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : There is possibility of food poisoning if we eat stale food.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Stale food has numbers of microbes which produce the toxic material in the food.\nii) Toxic material causes food poisoning.\niii) Therefore, there is possibility of food poisoning if we eat stale food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47630,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Fungus grow quickly in moist or humid condition.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The fungus needs the moist and damp area for the proper growth and reproduction.\nii) It does not grow in dry temperature.\niii) Hence, the fungus needs the moist or humid condition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47631,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Fodder is soaked in water before offering to cattle.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Soaked folder has a essential nutrients.\nii) Soaking fooder provide the energy to the cattle.\niii) Due to soaking it becomes easier for digestion.\niv) Hence, fodder is soaked in water be offering to cattle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47632,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by micro-organisms?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The organisms which not be seen with our naked eyes but is can be observed under a microscope are called micro-organisms.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47633,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Categories the following organisms in two groups, according to size : Amoeba, worms, snail, Paramecium, pigeon, elephant, euglena.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Small (micro-organisms) – amoeba, paramecium, euglena\nii) Large – Worms, snail, pigeon, elephant\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47634,795,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why should dry and wet waste be collected separately? \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The wet waste has organic matter which decomposed easily by microbes.\nii) Dry waste is not biodegradable it remains as it is for a long time.\niii) The dry waste like plastic, fibre, paper are harmful for the health. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47635,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Sketch and describe in your own words the plant cell and animal cell.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','a) Plant Cell:\ni) Plant cells have a definite shape due to the presence of the cell wall around them.\nii) Cell wall is present which is the outermost covering of the cell.\niii) There is plasma membrane inside the cell wall that encloses cytoplasm.\niv) Plastids is an important cell organelles that present only in the plant cell only.\nv) Chloroplast in the cell helps in the photosynthesis process.\nb) Animal Cell:\ni) Plasma membrane is the outermost covering of animal cells.\nii) Vacuoles in animal cell are smaller than plant cells.\niii) Chloroplast is absent in the animal cells.\niv) Due to absence of chloroplast animal cells does not perform the photosynthesis. ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47636,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the uses and the harmful effects of micro-organisms.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a) Uses of micro-organisms :-\ni) Micro-organisms present in the soil decompose the garbage and converts it into excellent manure.\nii) The microbes residing in the soil and in the root noduels of leguminous plant helps in the biological nitrogen fixation.\niii) Micro-organisms helps in the formation of curd.\niv) Micro-organisms makes the Vital role the fermentation.\nv) By the process of fermentation antibiotics, vaccine are produced.\nb) Harmful-effects of micro-organisms:-\ni) Some microbes releases toxic material and spoils the foodstuffs.\nii) Harmful micro-organisms causes different types of diseases to the animals and plants.\niii) Microbes that causes diseases like malaria, dengue, elephantiasis, yellow fever, chikungunya, zika fever etc.\niv) Food and water get contaminate due to harmful micro-organisms.\nv) Diseases like common cold, cough diphtheria, pneumonia, tuberculosis caused by harmful microbes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47637,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','When will you use a simple microscope and when a compound microscope? Explain in detail how you will use them.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The simple microscope is used for the study of insects part or parts of the flower.\nii) It is used to observe the smaller objects which can not be seen with our naked eyes.\niii) Compound microscope is used for observing microbes, cells and tissue.\niv) The object is placed over the glass slide and covered over with cover slip.\nv) The mirror of microscope is adjusted for optimum light.\nvi) The coarse focus knob is adjusted first. In this way simple microscope and compound microscope are used for the study of microbes as well as insects.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47638,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Diseases spread on a large-scale during periods of heavy rainfall and floods.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Heavy rainfall and flood causes the growth of microorganisms is increased due to the favourable atmosphere.\nii) Contaminated water spreads the pathogens rapidly.\niii) Floods and stagnant water is favorable for the growth of mosquitoes that can spread various diseases.\niv) The humid atmosphere in such a condition helps to the micro-organisms and spread of diseases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47639,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Soil is turned over during tilling.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Tilling helps to grow the fertility of soil.\nii) Soil contains are the useful bacteria increase the nutritional value in the coil.\niii) Some microbes helps in the nitrogen fixation.\niv) Therefore, for the better yield of crop, the soil is turned over during tilling.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47640,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : A refrigerator is used in almost every home.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Refrigerator has a very low temperature.\nii) Under this low temperature microbes do not grow and food remains safe.\niii) The spoilage of food is prevented with the help of refrigerators.\niv) Therefore, a refrigerator is used in almost every home.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47641,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Bread ‘rises’ during baking.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) While preparing the bread yeast is added in it.\nii) These yeast carry out the fermentation.\niii) In this process carbon dioxide and some other gases are released.\niv) These gases causes the bread increase in volume.\nv) Therefore, bread rises during baking.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47642,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why is yoghurt mixed in the batter or dough for making rava-idli, bhature naan?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Yoghurt contains useful bacteria causes the better fermentation.\nii) The heat, carbon dioxide and other gases helps to rise the flour.\niii) It increases the nutrition in the food and make the tasty.\niv) Therefore, the yoghurt is mixed in the batter, or dough for making rava idli, bhature naan.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47643,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How do preparation like yoghurt, idli, dosa, becomes easy to digest?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Idli-dosa are made from the batter is fermented. Yoghurt as prepared with the help of useful bacteria. The process of fermentation increases the nutrient and taste and also it make light and more nourishing vitamin content.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47644,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is correlation between the normal body temperature of human which is 37⁰C and the optimum temperature for the growth of micro-organisms 15⁰C to 35⁰C?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Micro-organisms needs the favorable condition for their growth.\nii) They needs temperature between 15°C to 35°C.\niii) The normal body temperature of human is 37°C at this temperature.\niv) Resulting micro-organisms can be killed above the temperature 37°C.\nv) Thus, man protected by bacteria attack.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47645,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the well labelled diagrams :  Plant cell\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47646,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the well labelled diagrams : Animal cell\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47647,795,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the well labelled diagrams : Prokaryotic cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47648,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The process of digestion starts from the -------------------- (stomach/mouth)','','','Mouth ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47649,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Eyelids have -------------------- muscles. (voluntary/involuntary)','','','involuntary','',0,0,0,NULL),(47650,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','-------------------- is not a function of the muscular system. (production of blood cell/performing movements)','','','production of blood cell','',0,0,0,NULL),(47651,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Muscles of the heart are ----------------------- (ordinary muscles/cardiac muscles)','','','cardiac muscles','',0,0,0,NULL),(47652,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Pushing forward the food that has been chewed is the function of the -------------------------- (Stomach/esophagus)','','','esophagus','',0,0,0,NULL),(47653,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The muscle on the front of the bone in our upper arm is called ------------------------ (biceps/triceps)','','','biceps','',0,0,0,NULL),(47654,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The total length of alimentary canal is about --------------------- (8 meters/9 meters)','','','9 meters','',0,0,0,NULL),(47655,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ptyalin converts starch into a sugar called --------------------- (fructose/maltose)','','','maltose','',0,0,0,NULL),(47656,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ----------------- is the largest gland in the body. (Pancreas/liver)','','','liver ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47657,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Enamel is made up of a ---------------------- (calcium salt/sea salt)','','','calcium salt ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47658,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Muscles are bundles of --------------------------- that can contract and relax as required. (cells/fibres)','','','fibres ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47659,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In blood vessels and uterus there are ------------------------- muscles. (skeletal/smooth)','','','smooth ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47660,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is voluntary muscles?\na) Arms 		b) Stomach 		c) Intestine 		d) Eyelid\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','a) Arms 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47661,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The study of muscles is called -----\na) morphology 		b) myology 		c) cytology 		d) psychology\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','b) myology ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47662,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is present in the stomach?\na) Saliva 		b) Pepsin		c) HCl 			d) Mucus\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','c) HCl ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47663,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- is made of a calcium salt.\na) Dentine 		b) Gum 		c) Bone 		d) Enamel\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','d) Enamel','',0,0,0,NULL),(47664,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Saliva contains an enzyme called ------\na) Ptyalin		b) Bile 		c) Lipase 		d) Mucus \n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','a) Ptyalin	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47665,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Eyelids have ------ muscles.\na) voluntary 		b) involuntary 		c) Skeletal 		d) none of these\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','b) involuntary 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47666,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of the large intestine is about ----- meter.\na) 6 			b) 1.5 			c) 2.5 			d) 1\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','b) 1.5 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47667,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which day is observed as World Health Day?\na) 7th April 		b) 14th April 		c) 31st May 		d) 22nd June\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','a) 7th April ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47668,796,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Liver secretes -------\na) Bile 		b) Lipase 		c) Pepsin 		d) ptyalin\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','a) Bile ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47669,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The action of muscles is necessary for all kinds of movement.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47670,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  The functions of the voluntary muscles do not depend upon our will.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong – The functions of the voluntary muscles are depends upon our will.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47671,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  Muscles in our body does not works in groups.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong – Muscles in our body works in groups.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47672,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The function of liver is the storage of glucose.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47673,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  Pancreatic juice contains hydrochloric acid.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong – Pancreatic juice contains various enzymes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47674,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  Ptyalin converts starch into a sugar.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47675,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Stomach, intestine, arm, uterus\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Arm - Because arm is a skeletal muscle and rest are all smooth muscles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47676,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Hydrochloric acid, ptyalin, pepsin, mucus.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Ptyalin : Hydrochloric acid, pepsin, mucus are secreted by gastric juice ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47677,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Bile, trypsin, lipase, amylase\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Bile- secreted by gastric wall.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47678,796,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Deltoid, Biceps, flexors, canine \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Canine- it is a part of tooth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47679,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following : ','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – e), (4 – c), (5 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(47680,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How many muscles are there in human body?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','There are more than 600 muscles in the human body. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47681,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What kind of movement is seen in the heart?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Involuntary movement is seen in the heart.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47682,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by myology?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The study of muscles is called myology.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47683,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the parts of alimentary canal?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The mouth, pharynx, esophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine are the parts of the alimentary canal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47684,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the enzymes present in the saliva?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Ptyalin or salivary amylase are the enzymes present in the saliva.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47685,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are the bones in our body joined to each other?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Bones in our body joined at the both end by ligaments.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47686,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mutual relationship between muscles and bones?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The muscles of our body are attached to the bones by means of tendons.\nii) Muscles protect the bones from the minor injuries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47687,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happened to the food we eat inside our body?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The food that we eat inside the body get digest due to mixing different juices.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47688,796,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which enzymes help in the digestion of proteins?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The protein digesting enzymes are as follows : \ni) Pepsin from stomach and\nii) Trypsin from pancreas. There are some additional enzymes in the small intestinal juice too. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47689,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Who is telling a lie?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Tongue is lying.\nReason: Taste had of tongue can tell different types of tastes like, sweet, bitter, sour etc.\nii) Large intestine is lying.\nReason: Large intestine is about 1.5 meter long.\niv) Appendix is lying.\nReason: It is first part of a large intestine. It does not make any function.','',1,0,0,NULL),(47690,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How many types of muscles are there? Which are those types?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There are three types of muscles.\nii) Those muscles are skeletal muscles, heart or cardiac muscles, and smooth muscles.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47691,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What causes the problem of acidity? What is its effects on the body?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Acid (HCl) is produced in the stomach for digestion of food.\nii) When the amount of acid is produced than what we required to digest food. It causes the acidity.\niii) Due to this stomach may suffer by ulcer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47692,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the different types of teeth. What is the function of each type?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There are four types of the teeth namely incisor, canine, premolar and molar.\nii) Incisors cut the food and convert it into small pieces. \niii) Canine teeth is to tear and shred food apart.\niv) Pre-molar are helpful and piercing food and grinds food into small particles.\nv) The function of molar is to chew and grinds into very small pieces.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47693,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Food becomes acidic in the stomach.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The gastric juice is secreted by the gastric glands in the stomach.\nii) The gastric juice contains the hydrochloric acid which mixed with the food.\niii) Therefore, food becomes acidic in the stomach.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47694,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Cardiac muscles are said to be involuntary muscles.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) These muscles brings about the contraction and relaxation (beating) of the heart.\nii) The function of the cardiac muscles are not depend on our will. \niii) Therefore, cardiac muscles are said to be involuntary muscles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47695,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by organ system?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A group of organs which perform the different functions is called as an organ system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47696,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do muscles perform their functions?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Muscles in our body always perform their functions in groups.\nii) During the function some muscles get contract whereas some relax.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47697,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which part of our body are made up only of muscles?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Liver, stomach, heart, small intestine, large intestine are the part of our body made up of muscles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47698,796,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do we affect our health adversely?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Improper diet, lack of exercise and bad habits affect our physical health. If we resort to addictive habits like smoking, chewing of tobacco, drinking alcohol, it affects our health adversely.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47699,796,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Intoxicating substances should not be consumed.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Intoxicating substance like tobacco or tobacco products effects on the physical health.\nii) Consuming tobacco cause vomiting, nausea and headache.\niii) Mouth, pharynx, and alimentary canal and other organs of the digestive system cannot function properly due to consumption of intoxicating substances.\niv) Drinking alcohol, smoking effects on the nervous system.\nv) The particles of tobacco stick to the teeth, gums and skin of the mouth cavity and slowly causes injury.\nvi) Hence, intoxicating substances should not be consumed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47700,796,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Your muscles should be strong and efficient.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Different part of the body carry different function with the help of muscles.\nii) Activities like, running, walking, jumping, pulling can be done involving muscles.\niii) Lifting heavy loads or chopping wood with axe needs more strength.\niv) Therefore, our muscles should be strong and efficient.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47701,796,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sketch and label a diagram of the digestive system and describe it in your own words.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','i) The digestive system consists alimentary canal and the digestive glands.\nii) Mouth, pharynx, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, rectum and anus are the parts of alimentary canal.\n1) Mouth :\ni) The process of digestion of food begins from mouth.\nii) Food is broken down by teeth into the mouth,\niii) The saliva is mixed with food which secreted\niv) The morsel is pushed by tongue into the pharynx.\n2) Pharynx : In the pharynx the oesophagus and trachea open.\n3) Oesophagus : Oesophagus pushes the food forward to the stomach.\n4) Stomach : It is bag like part of the alimentary canal. There are gastric gland in the stomach which secretes gastric juice. It has mild hydrochloric acid, pepsin and mucus. Due to churning food becomes semi-solid and slowly pushed into small intestine.\n5) Small Intestine : Small intestine is long and narrow tube like part of alimentary canal. It is about 6 meter long. In the small intestine digestion and absorption of nutrients is take place. The intestinal juice from wall of small intestine mixed with the food here.\n6) Large Intestine : It is about 1.5 metre long water is absorbed in the large intestine. There is appendix attached to the first part of the large intestine. The undigested food is pushed down into the anus which is thrown out the body.','',0,0,1,NULL),(47702,796,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What would be happen if the cardiac muscles do not move.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Contraction and relaxation of the heart would stop.\nii) The function of the heart may stop causes to death.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47703,796,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram of digestive system of man.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47704,796,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What would be happen if : Food enters the stomach and the stomach muscles do not move.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Food in the stomach will not mix with gastric juice.\nii) Due to this food remains undigested in the stomach.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47705,796,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What soluble nutrients are formed after the digestion of food?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The proteins are broken down into amino acids. The complex carbohydrates are converted into simple sugars. The fats are converted into fatty acids and glycerol. Thus, amino acids, sugars and fatty acids-glycerol are the nutrients formed after the digestion of food.   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47706,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Melting of ice is the example --------------------- changes.','','','Physical ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47707,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The process of formation of vapour from a liquid is called ----------------------------','','','Evaporation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47708,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Things become weak due to ------------------------------','','','Corrosion ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47709,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Conversion of milk into curd is a -------------------------- type of change.','','','Chemical ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47710,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Change like spoiling of the milk is a ------------------------ change.','','','natural ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47711,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ripening of the fruit is a ------------------------- change.','','','slow ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47712,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the properties of the original substances remain the same, then this type of change is called ----------------------- change.','','','physical ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47713,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The change that can occur in a forward and reverse directions again and again is ---------------------- change.','','','periodic ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47714,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The changes in which the time interval is not fixed are ----------------------- changes.','','','non - periodic ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47715,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the process of ---------------------- iron articles are given a thin coat of zinc to prevent corrosion.','','','galvanization','',0,0,0,NULL),(47716,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the chemical change from the following.\na) Rusting of iron 		b) Melting of ice 		c) Breaking of glass 	d) Evaporation of water\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) Rusting of iron 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47717,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What kind of change is the conversion of milk into yoghurt?\na) Physical 		b) Chemical 		c) Fast 		d) Harmful\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) Chemical ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47718,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is an example of harmful change?\na) Sharpening a pencil  		b) Baking bread 		c) Cooking food 		d) Bursting of fire crackers\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','d) Bursting of fire crackers','',0,0,0,NULL),(47719,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the evaporation process ----- turns into vapour.\na) Liquid 		b) Solid 		c) Gas 		d) all above\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) Liquid 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47720,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which one of the periodic change?\na) Lightening 		b) Storm 		c) Earthquake 	           d) Rotation of the earth\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','d) Rotation of the earth','',0,0,0,NULL),(47721,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is not a reversible change?\na) Heating of water to steam           b) Cooking of eggs\nc) Heating of wax to molten wax     d) None of these\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) Cooking of eggs','',0,0,0,NULL),(47722,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Properties of a substance before and after a change remained same. What might be the nature of the change?\na) Physical change         b) Chemical change       c) Fast change     d) Periodic change\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) Physical change    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47723,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The new substances formed during a chemical reaction are called\na) reactants          b) products         c) molecules          d) atoms.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) products ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47724,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Formation of steam from water on boiling is\na) reversible change          b) irreversible change\nc) chemical change            d) undesirable change.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) reversible change ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47725,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the chemical change.\na) Breaking of brick by hammer                   b) Ironing of clothes\nc) Combustion of petrol in automobiles      d) Melting of an ice cream\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) Combustion of petrol in automobiles  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47726,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A seed growing into tree is an example of\na) slow physical change              b) fast periodic change\nc) slow natural change                d) man-made reversible change\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) slow natural change ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47727,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The growth of a seedling into a plant is ----------\na) undesirable change               b) desirable change\nc) irreversible change                d) physical change\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) irreversible change       ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47728,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Burning of coal in air is a --------------\na) physical change           b) chemical change      \nc) reversible change        d) endothermic change\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) chemical change  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47729,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following does not take place during tearing of a paper?\na) Change of shape         b) Change of size        c) Change of colour     d) All of these.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','',' c) Change of colour','',0,0,0,NULL),(47730,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If two substances interact to produce a change they will do so\na) under any condition      \nb) no matter what kind of interaction takes place\nc) only if they are in close contact    \nd) only if the interaction is under the favourable conditions.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','d) only if the interaction is under the favourable conditions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47731,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Photosynthesis by green plants is a\na) physical change          b) chemical change       c) reversible change   d) undesirable change.\n','','','b) chemical change','',0,0,0,NULL),(47732,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following processes involve change in state?\ni) Evaporation       ii) Condensation       iii) Sublimation\na) (i) and (ii) only          b) (i) and (iii) only       c) (i), (ii) and (iii)     d) (ii) and (iii) only','','','c) (i), (ii) and (iii) ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47733,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is not a periodic change?\na) Formation of a rainbow          b) Occurrence of seasons\nc) Formation of full moon           d) Occurrence of day and night','','','a) Formation of a rainbow   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47734,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following statements is correct?\na) All chemical changes are reversible.      \nb) The ripening of fruits is a fast change.\nc) Rusting of iron is a fast change.      \nd) In a chemical change either energy is absorbed or released','','','d) In a chemical change either energy is absorbed or released','',0,0,0,NULL),(47735,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Stretching of a rubber band is a/an\na) permanent change        b) chemical change       c) reversible change     d) irreversible change.\n','','','c) reversible change ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47736,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following properties can change in case of a physical change?\na) Shape and size           b) Colour         c) Temperature            d) All of these\n','','','d) All of these','',0,0,0,NULL),(47737,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the liquid which evaporates at the fastest rate?\na) Water              b) Oil               c) Petrol             d) Kerosene\n','','',' c) Petrol     ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47738,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When a wooden stick is burnt, what new products are formed?\na) Coal             b) Ash               c) Heat energy            d) All of these\n','','','  d) All of these','',0,0,0,NULL),(47739,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Plaster of Paris hardness when applied over a bandage. This process is \na) reversible            b) irreversible             c) physical            d) natural\n','','','b) irreversible ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47740,797,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : All types of changes are the useful changes.','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47741,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : All types of changes are the useful changes.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47742,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Bursting of a balloon is a quick change.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47743,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Metals become strong due to corrosion \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47744,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Applying colours on the metal prevent the corrosion.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47745,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Evaporation is a physical change.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47746,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Baking bread is a harmful change.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------','','','False. Baking bread is a useful change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47747,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Rusting of iron is a physical change.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------','','','False. Rusting of iron is a chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47748,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : The objects become durable due to corrosion.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------','','','False. The objects become weak due to corrosion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47749,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : A number of different criteria can be applied to the same change.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47750,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Which type of change is the change of season from summer to rain to winter?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','After summer, comes the rainy season. After rainy season follows the winter. This cycle of seasons goes continuously on the earth. This occurs in specific time. Therefore change of seasons is a periodic change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47751,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of chemical change.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Rusting of iron, conversion of milk into curd, ripening of fruits etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47752,797,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by periodic change?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Changes that occurs again and again after a definite intervals of time. Such changes are called periodic changes.\nExamples : Sunset and sunrise, changes of seasons\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47753,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(47754,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by harmful changes?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Changes that do us harm are called harmful changes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47755,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is evaporation?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The process of formation of vapour from a liquid is called evaporation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47756,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by physical changes?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The changes in which the original properties remains same, no new substance is formed such changes are called physical changes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47757,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by galvanization?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A thin coat of zinc is given to prevent the corrosion of iron artical is called galvanization.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47758,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified : Conversion of milk into yoghurt.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Useful change, man-made change, chemical change, irreversible change, slow change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47759,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified : Bursting of a cracker.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Harmful change, man-made change, chemical change, irreversible change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47760,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified : Occurrence of an earthquake \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Natural change, quick change, harmful change, irreversible change, non-periodic change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47761,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified : Revolution of the earth around the Sun.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Periodic change, natural change, reversible change, slow change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47762,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Under which different types can be the following changes be classified :  Stretching of a spring.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Reversible change, man-made change, slow change','',0,0,0,NULL),(47763,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the different types of changes?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Various types of changes occurs in our surrounding :\ni) natural and man-made change\nii) Useful and harmful change\niii) Reversible and irreversible change\niv) Periodic and non-periodic change\nv) Physical and chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47764,797,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are natural changes?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Changes that occurs naturally in the surrounding are called natural changes.\nExamples : Ripening of fruits, spoiling of milk, falling of leaves of trees, changes of seasons etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47765,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write the difference between : Physical change and Chemical change\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47766,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Periodic change and non-periodic change\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47767,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Natural change and Man-made change\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47768,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : While purchasing canned food, its expiry date should be checked.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) All canned food items has specific life span during the life span every food items remains safe for eating.\nii) The expiry date is mentioned on the can. But after the expiry date is over food may not remains in good condition.\niii) Due to consumption of such food items effect on our health.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47769,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : A wooden articles should be polished.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) It is an organic material which be affected by fungus or termites. \nii) Due to them wood get damaged polishing wooden prevent the damage. \niii) Hence, a wooden article should be polished.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47770,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What will you take into account while identifying the following : A physical change in a substance.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','If the properties and composition of original substance does not change after processing, then it is a physical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47771,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What will you take into account while identifying the following : A chemical change in substance.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the properties of original substance are changed, then we said to be it is a chemical change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47772,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the causes of the changes occurring in our surrounding?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The causes of the changes occurring are as follows.\ni) Natural events.\nii) Human activities.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47773,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What happen when water is taken in a bowl and heated?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When water is taken in bowl and heated, it becomes warm first and then a certain temperature it will start boiling and converts in vapours.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47774,797,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which of the following physical changes and which are chemical changes. Making table from wood, burning of a wood, breaking of glass object, ripening of tomato, rusting of iron.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Physical change : \ni) Making a table from a wood\nii) Breaking of a glass object\nChemical change : \ni) Burning wood\nii) Ripening of tomato\niii) Rusting of iron','',0,0,0,NULL),(47775,797,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : An iron article should be given a coat of paint.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) An iron articles rust due to change weather which is called corrosion.\nii) If the iron comes in the contact of moisture, oxygen, vapours causes the corrosion.\niii) This corrosion makes article weak and unusable.\niv) Therefore, to keep it in a stronger condition it should be given a coat of paint.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47776,797,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Copper and brass utensils should be tipped.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the metal like copper and brass come in contact of moisture oxygen or vapour a greenish layer is formed on these metals.\nii) Due to this corrosion is take place.\niii) To prevent this corrosion, copper and brass utensils are tinned.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47777,797,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : A dry handkerchief get wet at one on dipping in water, but it takes along for a wet handkerchief to dry.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Dipping the dry clothes absorb the water. Once it is dipped in water. It is quick change.\nii) But drying such wet handkerchief is a slow process which occurs due to evaporation.\niii) Evaporation is a physical change which depends upon the temperature and humidity.\niv) Therefore, it takes long time for a wet handkerchief to dry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47778,797,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Read the paragraph and note down the various types of changes it mentions: It was nearing six o’clock in the evening. The sun was setting. A breeze was blowing. Leaves on the tree were shaking. Sahil was sitting in the courtyard, rolling balls of wet soil and shaping them into various toys. Then he felt hungry and went into the house. Mother made a dough from wheat flour and fried purees. While eating hot purees, his attention was drawn outside the window. It had started raining. There was lighting, too. Sahil was enjoying his dinner in the dim light.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','1) It was nearing six o\'clock in the evening: (Time-reversible, periodic)\n2) The sun was setting: (Sunset- Periodic, reversible)\n3) A breeze was blowing: (Blowing of wind-Physical, non-periodic)\n4) Leaves on the tree were shaking: (Movements due to wind- Physical, periodic) \n5) Sahil was sitting in the courtyard, rolling balls of wet soil and shaping them into various toys. (Making toys of soil-Physical) \n6) Then he felt hungry and went into the house. (Hunger-Periodic)\n7) Mother made a dough from wheat flour and fried purees. (Frying-Physical, irreversible)\n8) While eating hot purees, his attention was drawn outside the window. (Seeing randomly-Non-periodic)\n9) It had started raining. (Raining-Physical, Periodic if raining in specific season) \n10) There was lightning. (Lightning-Physical, Chemical, Fast)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47779,797,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by man-made changes? Which are these changes?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The changes occurs due to human activities are called man-made changes.\ni) Baking bread                                  ii) Making furniture.\niii) Bursting of fire crackers             iv) Pollution of water.\nv) Cooking food. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47780,797,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What processes occurs during the change of state of matter.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following processes occurs during the change of state of matter.\ni) Boiling              ii) Freezing             iii) Melting\niv) Evaporation      v) Sublimation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47781,797,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give some examples of fast and slow changes that occurs in your surroundings.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Examples: \na) Fast Changes :\ni) Bursting of balloon           ii) Bursting of fire crackers.\niii) Switching on an electric bulb.\nb) Slow Changes : \ni) Rusting of iron         ii) Growth of baby\niii) Curdling of milk','',0,0,0,NULL),(47782,797,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by irreversible change? Give some examples.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Changes which are permanent and cannot be reversed is called irreversible change.\nExamples: Rusting of iron, burning of wood, curdling of milk, ripening of mango. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47783,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The --------------------- are the smallest particles of a substance.','','','molecules ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47784,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','About --------------------- elements are discovered by the scientists.','','','118 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47785,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The element that show some properties of both metals and non-metals are called -------------------------','','','Metalloids ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47786,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The symbol of Wolfram is ----------------','','','W','',0,0,0,NULL),(47787,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Water is a ----------------------------','','','Compound ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47788,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A molecular formula is used to represented ---------------------------','','','H Compound ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47789,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Molecular formula of water is -------------------------------','','','H₂O','',0,0,0,NULL),(47790,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The smallest particle of an element is called ----------------------------------','','','Atom ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47791,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An object is made of --------------------------------','','','matter ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47792,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','--------------------- and ------------------------ are two important properties of matter.','','','Mass, volume ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47793,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Matter made of only one constituent is called -----------------------------','','','Substance ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47794,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A substance whose molecules are made of exactly alike atoms is called ----------------------------','','','element ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47795,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Scientists have discovered ------------------- elements of these, ---------------------- elements occur in nature.','','','118, 92 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47796,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Brass, steel, twenty-two carat gold are a few examples of --------------------------','','','alloys ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47797,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------------- is also used for purification of impure liquids.','','','Distillation','',0,0,0,NULL),(47798,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The property of solubility of the substance in the solvent that moves up is used in the technique of ---------------------------.','','','chromatography','',0,0,0,NULL),(47799,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many elements are there found in nature?\na) 80 		b) 92 			c) 118 		d) 108\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','b) 92 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47800,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The symbol of sodium is ------\na) S 		b) SO 		c) Na 		d) None of these\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','c) Na ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47801,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Molecular formula of water is ------\na) H₂S		b) H₂SO₄		c) HNO₃		d) H₂O\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','d) H₂O','',0,0,0,NULL),(47802,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following element helps combustion?\na) Oxygen 		b) Carbon 		c) Sodium 		d) Argon\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','a) Oxygen 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47803,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following element is a metalloid?\na) Aluminum		b) Silicon 		c) Tungsten 		d) Boron\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','b) Silicon ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47804,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Molecular formula of Sulphuric acid is -------\na) HCl 		b) H₂SO₄		c) HNO₃		d) Argon\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','b) H₂SO₄	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47805,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The smallest particle of an element is called ------\na) Compound 		b) Mixture 		c) Matter 	d) atom\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','d) atom','',0,0,0,NULL),(47806,798,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the symbol of element potassium?\na) O                 b) K              c) Na                d) C\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','',' b) K  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47807,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : All elements are metals\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47808,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : The smallest particle of elements are made of only one type of atom\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47809,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : There are 92 elements occurs in the nature\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47810,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Sulphur is a metalloid.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47811,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : All compounds have the same property.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47812,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Aluminum, Sodium, Argon, Carbon \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Aluminium (Rest are all non-metals)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47813,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Sulphur, Carbon, Nitrogen, Argentum\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Argentum (Rest are all non-metals)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47814,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Air, water, milk, acetic acid\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Acetic acid (Rest are all mixtures)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47815,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Stainless steel, copper, silver, gold\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Stainless steel (Rest are all element)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47816,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Element\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Aluminium, Sodium','',0,0,0,NULL),(47817,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Elements in molecular form\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Oxygen, Hydrogen, Fluorine, Chlorine ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47818,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Metals\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Gold, Silver, Copper, Mercury, Sodium','',0,0,0,NULL),(47819,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Non-metals\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Carbon, Sulphur, Oxygen, Hydrogen','',0,0,0,NULL),(47820,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Compound\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Water, common salt, sugar, washing soda','',0,0,0,NULL),(47821,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of Alloy\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Brass, Steel, Stainless steel','',0,0,0,NULL),(47822,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which element helps combustions?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Oxygen helps in combustion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47823,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Does water help combustion?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','No. In fact water stops the combustion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47824,798,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Are all mixtures useful to us?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','No, some mixtures are harmful for us. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47825,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – e), (2 – f), (3 – c), (4 – b), (5 – a), (6 – d)','',1,0,0,NULL),(47826,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – c), (2 – b), (3 – d), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(47827,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of each : Metals\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Iron, Copper, Gold, Silver.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47828,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of each : Non-metals\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sulphur, Chlorine, Phosphorus, Nitrogen','',0,0,0,NULL),(47829,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of each : Compounds\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','H₂O, H₂SO₄, NaCl, MgCl₂','',0,0,0,NULL),(47830,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give four examples of each : Mixtures\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Air, Mixture of oil and water, Juice','',0,0,0,NULL),(47831,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Element\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A substance whose molecules are made of one or more atoms which are exactly alike, is called an element.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47832,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define :  Atom\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Atom is the smallest particle of an element','',0,0,0,NULL),(47833,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Compound\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The substance formed by a chemical combination of two or more elements is called a compound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47834,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Molecule \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Molecule is the smallest particle of a compound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47835,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Mixture\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When two or more different elements or compounds are mixed with each other, it is called a mixture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47836,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Alloy\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When some metals are difficult to use in pure form they are mixed in particular proportion with each other. By such mixing their properties are modified it. Such a mixture of metals is called an alloy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47837,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Symbol\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Symbols are short forms used to refer an element.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47838,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How many different states of matter are there? Name them.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','There are three states of matter they are solid, liquid and gaseous. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47839,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What brings about a change of state of matter?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Change in temperature brings about a change of state of matter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47840,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are objects made of?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Objects are made up of matter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47841,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the mixtures used in everyday life?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Air, food, cold drinks, cement, juice are some mixtures used in day-to-day life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47842,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Is carbon dioxide is an element?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','No, it is compound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47843,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Are the atoms of different elements similar or dissimilar?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The atoms of different elements are dissimilar. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47844,798,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which properties of a liquid are seen in the distillation method?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Vaporization and condensation of liquid are the properties seen in distillation method. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47845,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write the names of elements from the following symbols : Zn, Cd, Xe, Ti, Cu, Fe, Si, Ir, Pt.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47846,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Metals and Non-metals\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47847,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Mixtures and Compounds\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47848,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Atoms and Molecules\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47849,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Separating by distillation and separating by funnel\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47851,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Buttermilk is churned to get butter.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Churning is a good technique which helps to separate butter from buttermilk.\nii) Due to the centrifugal force all particles of butter collected together.\niii) Therefore, buttermilk is churned to get butter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47852,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : In chromatography, the ingredients of a mixture rise up to a limited height when water rises up to the upper end of paper.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In this method two opposite properties are used.\ni) The solubility of a substance in the solvent that moves up.\nii) The ability of the substance to stick to the stationary filter paper. Hence, water is a solvent, it rises rapidly across the chromatography filter paper.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47853,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : A wet cloth wrapped around a water storage container in summer.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There is high temperature in summer.\nii) Water in the container can be evaporate due to this temperature.\niii) To prevent the evaporation of water and to keep the water cool in the container, wet cloth is wrapped around a container.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47854,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the articles of every day use made of electric wire, kitchen utensils, nails, tables and chairs, window panes, soil, salt, sugar?\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47855,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which metals do we use in day-to-day life?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following metals we use in day-to-day life : Iron, Gold, Silver, Brass, Copper','',0,0,0,NULL),(47856,798,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which elements are present in air?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Nitrogen, Oxygen, Argon and minute percentage of Helium, Neon, Krypton, Xenon and radon are the elements present in the air. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47857,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the names of elements and name in use of following symbols : Au, Ag, Ne, Ni, He, Hg, W\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47858,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the molecular formulae of the following compounds : Hydrochloric acid, Sulphuric acid, Sodium chloride, Glucose, Methane\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47859,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How are the components of mixtures separated by simple method?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','There are various simple methods are used for mixture. These methods are as follows. Straining, Sifting, Picking, Winnowing.\n1) Straining : This method is used for separating insoluble substances from the solution.\n2) Sifting :\ni) It is used for separating stones, or large particles from the grains.\nii) The smaller and larger particle from the mixture get separated.\n3) Picking : By this method unnecessary substances are separated.\n4) Winnowing : Substances which are lighter in weight or unwanted are separated by this methods.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47860,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which elements (metal and non-metals) compound and mixture do we use in our day-to-day life?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','1) Elements used in a day to - day life : Metals: Iron, Copper, Gold, Silver, etc. \n2) Non-metals : Phosphorus, Oxygen, Carbon, Nitrogen, etc.\n3) Compounds used in day-to-day life : Water, salt (sodium chloride), toothpaste (sodium flouride), glucose, etc. \n4) Mixtures used in day-to-day life : Air, milk, food, juice, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47861,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In every day life, where and for what purpose do we use centrifugations?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','By centrifugation method the following task can be done.\ni) Separation of cream from milk.\nii) Drying wet clothes in a washing machines.\niii) Particles of butter are separated from buttermilk.\niv) Solid components are separated from blood.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47862,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Where are the methods of separation by distillation and by separating funnel used? Why?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Distillation:\n i) It is used to separate impurities or dissolved salts from the solution.\nii) Solute and solvent can be separated by this method.\niii) For removing salt from sea water this help making potable water.\nUsing Separating Funnel :\ni) Immiscible mixture can be separated by this method.\nii) It is used to separate kerosene and water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47863,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which precaution will you take while using the method of distillation and separation by separating funnel?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following precautions to be taken while using the method of distillation and separation by separating funnel are:\ni) The apparatus will be connecting properly.\nii) Gloves will be used while handling the chemicals.\niii) Area must be well verticillated to prevent accumulation of vapour.\niv) The condenser tube should be properly cooled.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47864,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the properties of matter?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Properties of matters are:\ni. Mass           ii. Brittleness           iii. Hardness            iv. Solubility\nv. Transparency          vi. Fluidity         vii. Elasticity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47865,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which of the element are metals, which are non-metals and which are metalloids?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','1) Metals: Aluminium, Copper, Gold, Silver. \n2) Non-metals : Sulphur, Phosphorus, Nitrogen, Carbon.\n3) Metalloids : Arsenic, Silicon, Platinum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47866,798,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','For what purpose is distilled water used?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) It is used for manufacturing cold drinks.\nii) It is used in laboratories.\niii) Distilled water is used in car batteries.\niv) It is used for health care.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47867,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','A substance that help water to remove dirt from the surface of material is called ----------------------','','','detergent ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47868,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Fluoride is used in toothpaste to prevent ---------------------------','','','tooth decay ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47869,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Soap is a salt of -------------------- and sodium hydroxide.','','','Fatty acids ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47870,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Synthetic detergent can be used in ----------------------- water as well.','','','Hard ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47871,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','For construction purposes --------------------- cement is the most commonly used cement.','','','Portland ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47872,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Any liquid containing some dissolved matter is called a -----------------------------','','','solution ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47873,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The space occupied by any matter is called its ------------------------------','','','volume ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47874,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Kitchen utensils have wooden handles since wood is a --------------------------- of heat.','','','poor conductor ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47875,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Materials which do not conduct electricity are called ---------------------------','','','insulators ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47876,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Grouping of things is done for -----------------------------','','','Convenience ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47877,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An object can be made of different -----------------------------.','','','Materials ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47878,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The objects through which we can see are known as -----------------------.','','','Transparent ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47879,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Different objects can be grouped on the basis of their ----------------- and -----------------------.','','','Similarities and disimilarities','',0,0,0,NULL),(47880,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The material through which objects can be seen but not clearly are called -----------------------.','','','Translucent','',0,0,0,NULL),(47881,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sugar and salt are ------------------------ in water.','','','soluble '