Sand\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','','a) Sugar  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47898,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the object which is made up of a magnetic material.\na) Eraser          b) Pencil         c) Safety pin       d) Paper\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','','c) Safety pin  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47899,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the substances which are highly inflammable.\na) LPG            b) Petrol           c) Spirit        d) All of them\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','',' d) All of them','',0,0,0,NULL),(47900,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A rod can be bent if it is ----------\na) light            b) hard              c) rigid          d) flexible.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','','d) flexible','',0,0,0,NULL),(47901,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A substance which can be broken into small pieces or powdered easily is called --------\na) brittle            b) hard           c) malleable           d) ductile.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','','a) brittle     ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47902,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A substance which is not easy to be stretched or compressed is called ------\na) flexible           b) rigid           c) magnetic          d) sonorous\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','','b) rigid  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47903,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Oil and paint when mixed with water ------------\na) sink in water          b) float on water        c) dissolve in water     d) make a layer in the centre of water.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','','b) float on water ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47904,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','To make a cooking pan we need a material that is light, strong and --------\na) flexible                                    b) good conductor of heat\nc) bad conductor of heat           d) transparent\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','','b) good conductor of heat','',0,0,0,NULL),(47905,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An oiled paper is -------------\na) opaque           b) transparent          c) translucent         d) none of these\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','','c) translucent  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47906,799,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Air is -----------\na) transparent             b) opaque            c) translucent          d) none of these\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------','','','a) transparent ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47907,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Fluoride in the toothpaste helps to prevent tooth decay.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47908,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Soap nut is a chemical detergent.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47909,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Hard soap is used for washing clothes.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47910,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Synthetic detergent doe not effects on the skin.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47911,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  Many years ago toothpaste was made by mixing the powder of bones and shells.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47912,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  The molecules of a detergent are short.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. The molecules of a detergent are long.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47913,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong :  One effect of surface activity is no lather formation.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. One effect of surface activity is lather formation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47914,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : About 2000 years ago. In those days, soap was prepared using animal fat and wood ash.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47915,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : In 1756, the Romans developed the method of making aqueous cement.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. In 1756, the British engineer, John Smeaton developed the method of making aqueous cement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47916,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the similarities between Natural detergents and man-made detergents.  \n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Both are used for cleaning.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47917,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the similarities between Soap and synthetic detergents.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Both are used cleaning.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47918,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the similarities between Bath soap and soap for washing clothes.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Both the soaps contains fatty acids and both are used for cleansing. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47919,799,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the similarities between Modern cement and ancient cement.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Both are used for construction work. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47920,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What should be expected from a detergent for delicate garments?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Soap nut and soap pod are the best detergent for the delicate garments, as they do not have any harmful effect on delicate silk and woollen threads and cloth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47921,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How will you check with the help of soap powder whether water is hard?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The soap does not produce foam in hard water, due to the dissolved salts in the hard water. Thus by using soap powder we can understand if water is hard.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47922,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Nowadays, why are the roads made of concrete?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','These days there is a very heavy traffic on the road. Therefore, there is need of strong roads that can support speedy transport. Therefore, nowadays, roads are made of concrete.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47923,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What causes the hardness of water?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','If the salts of calcium and magnesium such as bicarbonates, chlorides and sulphates are dissolved in the water, then there is hardness caused to the water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47924,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are important ingredients of the toothpaste?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Calcium Carbonate and Calcium Hydrogen Sulphate are the important ingredients of the toothpaste.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47925,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by natural detergents?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Detergents obtained from the nature are called natural resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47926,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is prepared the concrete?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Concrete is prepared by mixing cement, water, sand and gravel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47927,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of soap is used for bathing.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Soft soap are used for bathing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47928,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the ingredients in cement?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The ingredients of simple cements are silica (sand), alumina (aluminum oxide), lime, iron oxide and magnesia (magnesium oxide)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47929,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of detergent that you use.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Soap, washing soda, shampoo, toothpaste are some examples of detergents.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47930,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What do you use today to clean our teeth?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A variety of toothpaste and tooth powder are used today.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47931,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the source of the fluoride in a toothpaste or tooth powder?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sodium fluoride is the source of fluoride in toothpaste or tooth powder.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47932,799,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do we use for cleaning body?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Soap, handwash, toothbrush, water, shampoo, toothpaste, facewash, tooth powder are the substances we used for cleaning our body. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47933,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Natural detergents and man-made detergents\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47934,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Soap and synthetic detergent\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer :  ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47935,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Bath soap and soap for washing clothes.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47936,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Modern cement and ancient cement\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(47937,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the important ingredients of toothpaste and what is the function of each.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Calcium carbonate and calcium hydrogen phosphate are the important ingredients of the toothpaste.\nii) These ingredients remove the dirt and polish the teeth.\niii) Fluoride prevents the tooth decay and makes the enamel of teeth strong.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47938,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain soap can not be used in hard water.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Hard water is mixture of different salts, therefore soap does not give lather but form a scum.\nii) Due to this soap lose its cleaning properties.\niii) Hence soap cannot be used in hard water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47939,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain synthetic detergents are superior to soap.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Because, in synthetic detergent varieties of chemicals which helps to remove the dust or to help in cleaning.\nii) Therefore, synthetic detergent are superior to soap.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47940,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain tobacco masher should not be used for cleaning teeth.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Tobacco has various bad effects on body.\nii) It may cause oral cancer, hence it is not used.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47941,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by natural and man-made materials?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The materials which are obtained from the nature are called natural materials.\nii) Materials those made by man, they are called man-made materials.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47942,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Make a list of natural and man-made substances you see around you.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Natural Substances : Water, milk, cotton, soil, wood.\nii)  Man-made Substances: Toothpaste, Oil, yoghurts, ghee, clothes, nail paint, soap.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47943,799,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the materials used for construction?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Cement, sand, water, gravel, iron, wood concrete are the different materials used for construction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47944,799,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How does the use of a detergent help to clean soiled clothes?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The molecules of detergent are long in their structure.\nii) It holds on to a water molecule on one side and the oil molecule onto the other.\niii) As a result, the molecules of oil mix with water.\niv) Hence, it come out of the cloth along with dirt. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47945,799,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What will happen if cement is not used in making concrete?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Concrete is prepared by mixing cement, water, sand and gravel.\nii) Cement hold the other material strongly to make it hard.\niii) If cement is not used in concrete the other constituent will not mixed properly. \niv) To make hard and strong slab, blocks, pillars, constructions. Cement is mixed in the concrete.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47946,799,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by surface activity?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The property of a substance of spreading on a surface is called surface activity.\nii) The substance that spread known as surfactant.\niii) Detergents are surface active.\niv) One effect of surface activity is lather formation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47947,799,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain oil does not mix in water, however oil and water become homogeneous if a sufficient quantity of detergent is added.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Detergent has a long molecule in its two end.\nii) It holds the oil at one end and of detergent in water other end water.\niii) Hence, it makes mixture of water and oil.\niv) Thus, oil and water will not mixed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47948,799,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain often coloured spot are formed cloths during washing.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Addictive chemical in the detergent remains as it is on the clothes.\nii) Therefore coloured spot are formed cloths during washing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47949,799,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which substances were used earlier for cleaning teeth?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following substances were used to cleaning teeth.\nAcacia bark, neem twinges, coal powder, ash, tooth powder, salt pomegranate etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47950,799,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What causes of the hardness of water?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Due to the presence of magnesium salt and calcium salt in the water causes the hardness of water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47951,799,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nowadays why are the roads made of concrete?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Concrete is made by mixing cement, sand, water and gravel.\nii) Due to concrete become strong, hard and durable.\niii) Therefore, for longer life of the roads, roads are made by concrete.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47952,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Minerals that contain a high proportion of metal are called ------------------------','','','Ores ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47953,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bauxite contains -------------- aluminum.','','','55%','',0,0,0,NULL),(47954,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mica is ------------------- of electricity.','','','Bad conductor ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47955,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------------- is the main component of natural gas.','','','Methane ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47956,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','About -------------------- of the total land of the world is covered by forest.','','','30% ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47957,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------- is the coal of the highest grade.','','','Anthracite ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47958,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------------------- is the most important ore of aluminum.','','','Bauxite ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47959,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- is a very good conductor of electricity.','','','Copper ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47960,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------------------- value depends on the thickness of its layers.','','','Mica\'s ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47961,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------------------- is said to be a fossil fuel.','','','Coal ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47962,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following minerals is a non-metal?\na) Diamond		b) Copper 		c) Silicon 		d) Gold\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) Diamond','',0,0,0,NULL),(47963,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following mineral is a metal?\na) Sulphur 		b) Platinum 		c) Graphite 		d) Mica\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) Platinum','',0,0,0,NULL),(47964,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- is a good conductor of electricity.\na) Coal 		b) Diamond 		c) Aluminum 		d) Gypsum \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) Aluminum ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47965,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The chemical formula of propane is ------\na) C₃H₈		b) CH₄			c) C₂H₆		d) C₄H₁₀\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) C₃H₈	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47966,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following mineral is used in the production of atomic energy?\na) Uranium 		b) Magnesium 		c) Sulphate 		d) Potassium\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) Uranium ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47967,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a bio-resources?\na) Sodium 		b) Magnesium 	c) Shells 		d) Sulphate\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) Shells ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47968,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is not a type of coal?\na) Anthracite 		b) Lignite 		c) Bituminous 		d) Bauxite\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','d) Bauxite','',0,0,0,NULL),(47969,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The oil and natural gas corporation was established on ------\na) 14th August 1956 	b) 14 September 1946 		c) 20 August 1956		d) 2 April 1956\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','a) 14th August 1956 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47970,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where is the Indian Institute of Technology?\na) Bengaluru 		b)  Delhi 		c) Dhanbad 		d) Mumbai\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','c) Dhanbad 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47971,800,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following mineral is found in the free state in the nature.\na) Iron 		b) Platinum 		c) Aluminum 		d) Coal\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------','','','b) Platinum 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(47972,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : All metals occur in free state in nature\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47973,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Metals are obtained from their ores\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47974,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Bauxite is the most important ore of aluminum.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47975,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sulphate is used in making ornaments.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47976,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sea cucumbers are used as medicines for treating cancer and tumors. \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47977,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Mica, gold, Sulphur, potash\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Gold - Gold is metal mineral while other all are non-metal minerals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47978,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Silver, bauxite, Iron, graphite\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Graphite - Graphite is a non-metal mineral while all other are metal mineral.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47979,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Coal, mineral oil, gypsum, natural gas\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Gypsum - Gypsum is a non-metal mineral while other all are energy minerals. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(47980,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Fishes, thorium, shells, fungi\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Thorium - Thorium is a mineral resource while other all are bio-resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47981,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Methane, Ethane, Propane, Butane\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Methane (All others are constituents of natural gas that are in very small proportions).','',0,0,0,NULL),(47982,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Magnetite, Jade, Zircon, Emerald\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Magnetite (All others are gem minerals)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47983,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Magnetite, Hematite, Limonite, Graphite\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Graphite (All others are ores of iron)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47984,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Diamond, Halite, Ruby, Sapphire\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Halite (All others are gems)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47985,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Mica, Sulphur, Gypsum, Bauxite\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Bauxite (All others are non-metallic minerals)','',0,0,0,NULL),(47986,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by ores?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Minerals that contain a high proportion of metal are called ores.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47987,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which minerals founds in free state in the nature?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Gold, Silver, Copper, Platinum and Bismuth occur in the free state in the nature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47988,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the different types of coal?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Peat, lignite, bituminous coal and anthracite are the various types of coals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47989,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the medicinal plants that you know.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Adulsa, Bel, Neem, Periwinkle, Cinnamon, Cinchona etc. are the medicinal plants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47990,800,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ocean resources?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Oil and gas, fish and marine organisms, iodine etc. are the ocean resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47991,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – c), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(47992,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – d), (2 – c), (3 – a), (4 – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(47993,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by fossil fuel? What are their types?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Fuels which are formed from the remains of plant and animals that burried underground millions of years ago is called fuel.\nii) Coal, mineral oil and natural gas are the types of fossil fuels. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(47994,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Name the two important stages in the process of obtaining metals from ores.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Extraction and purification are the two important stages in the process of obtaining metals from their ores.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47995,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by natural resources?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The resources that obtained from nature and fulfil our needs are called natural resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47996,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Give some examples of natural resources.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Air, water, soil, minerals, plants and animals are some example of natural resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47997,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by fuels?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Substances which are used for obtaining energy are called fuels.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47998,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by lithosphere?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The land portion which occupies by stones, rock and soil is called lithosphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(47999,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by forest?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','An extensive area of land covered by a variety of plants is called a forest.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48000,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by metal minerals and non-metal minerals?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Metal Minerals: The mineral that contain metallic element is called a metal mineral.\nii) Non-metal Mineral: The mineral that does not contain metallic element is called a non-metal minerals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48001,800,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What would be happen if underground mineral resources are exhausted?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) If mineral resources are exhausted, there would be energy crisis in the World.\nii) All the things that dependent upon mineral get stopped.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48002,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a list of the components we obtain from mineral oil.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The components obtained from mineral oil are as follows:\ni. Aviation petrol                 ii. Gasoline              iii. Diesel\niv. Kerosene                         v. Naphtha              vi. Lubricating oil\nvii. Tar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48003,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is the diversity of plants and animals in the forest declining?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) People are cutting the trees for various purposes such as farming, industries, building houses, making roads etc. Which effect of diversity of plants.\nii) The diversity in animals decline due to hunting of animals polluting, habitat destruction etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48004,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What steps are taken for protection and conservation of natural resources.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Prevent the excessive use of natural resources.\nii) Use of alternative sources.\niii) Renewable resources should be used for the protection and conservation of natural resources.\niv) Reforestation programmes have been launched on a large scale.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48005,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How does the economic condition of a nation depend on its natural resources?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The raw materials are obtained from the natural resources for manufacturing of goods, articles and appliances.\na) Wood from the forest.\nb) Shells and conches from the seas or oceans.\nii) These natural resources provides income, employments, occupations for the citizens.\niii) A country can earn a great amount of exchange of foreign by exporting natural resources.\niv) Thus, the economic condition of a nation depend on its natural resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48006,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which natural resources do we use as fuels?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Natural resources used as fuels are:\ni) Petroleum             ii) Diesel          iii) Coal\niv) Kerosene             v) LPG               vi) CNG etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48007,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the uses of forest?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Forest prevent the soil erosion and control floods.\nii) Forest helps in percolation of water and protecting wildlife and maintain the environment.\niii) Medicinal plants, wood, fruits, gum, rubber, honey are useful things provided by the forest.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48008,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Name the oceans of the earth.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The oceans of the earth are :\ni) Arctic ocean         ii) Indian ocean          iii) Pacific ocean\niv) Atlantic ocean    v) Antarctic ocean','',0,0,0,NULL),(48009,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How is sea water useful to us even though it is salty?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','For the following purpose sea water is useful for us even though it is salty.\ni) It provides us sea foods.\nii) Sea water gives habitat to the marine ecosystem.\niii) It helps to maintain water cycle.\niv) It helps for the transportation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48010,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why are all minerals not ores?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Mineral occurs naturally in the earth\'s crust.\nii) Ore has high proportion of metal.\niii) Each mineral is not suitable for profitable extraction of a particular metal.\niv) Hence, all ores are minerals but all minerals are not ores.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48011,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is mineral oil called ‘liquid gold’?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) It is valuable and non-renewable natural resource.\nii) It\'s reserves are limited.\niii) It is very expensive and essential to all countries in the World.\niv) Hence, mineral oil called the liquid gold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48012,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is coal called ‘black gold’?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Coal is a natural resource.\nii) It is costly and have a great demand and used for various purposes.\niii) It is black in colour.\niv) Hence, coal called black gold.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48013,800,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is natural gas an eco-friendly fuel?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) On burning or combustion it does not produce any solid waste.\nii) As compare to other fossil fuels it produces 50% less carbon dioxide.\niii) Natural gas do not make the air pollution.\niv) Therefore, natural gas is an eco-friendly fuel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48014,800,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What do we get from forest?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following product or substances we get from the forest.\ni) Medicinal plant            ii. Wood             iii. Fruits              iv. Paper\nv. Rubber.                          vi. Honey          vii. Aromatic Substances\nviii. Gum                            ix. Catechu       x. Dyes','',0,0,0,NULL),(48015,800,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the items included in ocean resources? What are their uses?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The items included in ocean resources and their uses are mentioned in the following :\na) Mineral Resources :\ni) Thorium - In production of atomic energy.\nii) Magnesium - In flash bulb of a camera.\niii) Potassium - In production of soap glass and fertilizers.\niv) Sodium - In production of cloths and paper.\nv) Sulphate - For making artificial silk, rubber.\nb) Bio – Resources : \ni) Fishes - Used as sea food.\nii) Dried shrimp Bombay duck powder - Used as poultry feed.\niii) Shells - Used in preparation of medicines, ornaments and decorative articles.\niv) Fungi - Used for the production of antibiotics.\nv) Shark & codfish - Used for producing edible oil rich in vitamin A, D and E.\nvi) Sea cucumber - Used medicine for treating cancer and tumors.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48016,800,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why should we prevent the wastage of fuel used for vehicles?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Fuels like petrol and diesel are used in vehicles.\nii) These fuels obtained by processing through the earth\'s crust.\niii) Their reserves are limited may exhaust in this century.\niv) On combustion in vehicle, give out harmful gases causes air pollution.\nv) Therefore, we should prevent the wastage of fuels used in vehicles. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48017,800,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Write the names of five minerals and the useful substances obtained from them.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The five important minerals are iron, ore, manganese, bauxite, copper and mica.\n1) Iron Ore : Iron ore are used for making farming implements, rails of railway tracks, heavy machinery etc.\n2) Manganese : Manganese occurs in the forms of its carbonate, silicate, and oxide.\n3) These are used in preparation of medicines and giving pink tinge to the glass.\n4) Bauxite : Bauxite is most important ore of aluminium. Aluminium is a good conductor of electricity and heat. Therefore, it is used in aeroplanes, transport vehicles and to make electric wires.\n5) Copper - Copper is a very good conductor of electricity. Therefore it is used to make electric wires, as well as in telephones, radios, vehicles, and making for utensils and statues.\n6) Mica-\ni) Mica is a bad conductor of electricity.\nii) It is used in ayurvedic medicines, dyes, electric machines and equipment, wireless communication equipment etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48018,800,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Which medical plants will you grow on your school premises and near your house? Why?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following medicinal plants can be grown in the premises and near the house.\ni) Tulsi - Tulsi helps in the treatment of cough, cold, fever and malaria.\nii) Aloe Vera - It is used as cosmatics. It helps to recover the wound and cut.\niii) Adulsa - Useful for cough and cold.\niv) Cinnamon - Useful for diarrhoea, nausea.\nv) Cinchona - Helps in treatment of malaria.\nvi) Turmeric Useful in the foodstuffs and as preservatives.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48019,800,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe natural resources with references to the given type : Mineral resources\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Mineral Resources :\ni) Mineral resources are found in the earth\'s crust. Minerals like copper, platinum, gold, silver and bismuth are found in free states.\nii) Minerals like iron, aluminium, mica are found in mixed state.\niii) From these metal and non-metal mineral made the articles, objects, for various purposes.\niv) Mineral like diamond, ruby, sapphire, emerald, jade, zircon are used as gems.\nv) Petroleum, coal and natural gas are used as a fuel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48020,800,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe natural resources with references to the given type : Forest resources\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Forest Resources :\ni) A forest is a natural habitat of plants, animals and microbes.\nii) Forest performs certain specific protective and productive functions.\niii) The forest provides as medicinal plants, wood, oxygen, honey, fruits and flowers as a useful things.\niv) Forest prevent the soil erosion and helps in the percolation of rain water.\nv) Forest resources maintain the environment balance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48021,800,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe natural resources with references to the given type : Ocean resources\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Ocean Resources :\ni) We get both mineral and bio-resources. from the ocean.\nii) The mineral resources includes Thorium, magnesium, potassium, sodium and sulphate.\niii) The bio-resources provides us fishes, dried shrimps, Bobay duck powder, shells, fungi, shark and cod-fish.\niv) We get salt, fishes, as a food from the ocean resources.\nv) Fuels like petrol, diesel are obtained from the seabed by processing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48022,800,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the flow chart :','\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(48023,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','When the beams from the headlights of a car fall on an object in the night, the shadows called ----------------- and -------------------- can be seen.','','','umbra, penumbra','',0,0,0,NULL),(48024,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','During a lunar eclipse the shadow of the ----------------- falls on the ---------------------','','','earth, moon ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48025,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','During a solar eclipse the shadow of the ----------------- falls on the --------------------','','','moon, earth ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48026,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Various shades of colour are seen in the sky at sunrise and sunset due to --------------------','','','scattering of light','',0,0,0,NULL),(48027,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------ is scattered by the molecules of gases.','','','Sunlight ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48028,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A ------------------ eclipse is seen only on a new moon day.','','','Solar ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48029,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A faint shadow of an object called ----------------- is obtained from an extended source of light.','','','penumbra ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48030,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------ is celebrated as the World Anti Superstition Day.','','','21st September','',0,0,0,NULL),(48031,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Tiny --------------------- are seen floating in the beam of light.','','','dust particles ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48032,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the absence of atmosphere, the sky would have appeared ---------------- during the day.','','','dark ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48033,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ---------------- colour in the sunlight is scattered the most, due to the molecules of gases present in the atmosphere.','','','blue ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48034,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A --------------- eclipse should never be seen/watched with the naked eye.','','','Solar ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48035,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many colours are there in sunlight?\na) 4 			b) 6 			c) 7 			d) 10\n\nAns. : ---------------------------','','','c) 7 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48036,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A light beam is seen in ----- water.\na) Turbid 		b) plain 		c) Filtered water 		d) None of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) Turbid 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48037,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When a planet or a star passes behind the moon, that state is called a -----\na) Transmission		b) Occultation		c) Alternation 		d) Transformation\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) Occultation	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48038,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A ---- eclipse is seen only on a new moon day.\na) Solar 		b) Lunar 		c) Both a and b		d) None of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) Solar 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48039,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- rays are harmful to us which reach the earth.\na) Light 		b) violet 		c) Ultra violet 		d) all above\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','c) Ultra violet ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48040,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much time is required to come the light rays from the sun to the earth?\na) 8 min, 20 sec 	b) 10 min, 30 sec 	c) 7 min, 10 sec 	d) 8 hours, 20 sec\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) 8 min, 20 sec ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48041,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of colour is scattered most in sunlight?\na) Violet 		b) yellow 		c) Blue 		d) Green\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','c) Blue 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48042,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How an object visible to us in the surrounding?\na) Due to refraction of light 		b) due to propagation of light 		\nc) Due to source of light 		d) Due to reflection of light\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','d) Due to reflection of light\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48043,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which among the following is an artificial source of light?\na) Star             b) Tube light            c) Sun               d) Firefly\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) Tube light   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48044,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is/are the natural source(s) of light?\na) Earth             b) Moon             c) Sun                d) Both b and c\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','c) Sun  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48045,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Substances which allow light to pass through them are called ----------\na) translucent             b) opaque            c) transparent          d) all of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','c) transparent ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48046,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When the moon’s shadow falls on the earth, we have a ------------\na) lunar eclipse              b) solar eclipse             c) umbra         d) penumbra \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) solar eclipse ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48047,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The image produced in a pinhole camera is ---------\na) inverted           b) of the same size of object          c) both a and b        d) neither a nor b\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','c) both a and b','',0,0,0,NULL),(48048,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A mirror is an example of -----------\na) luminous object             b) reflecting surface           c) translucent object           d) none of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) reflecting surface  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48049,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An example of translucent object is ----------\na) butter paper             b) wood             c) glass             d) mirror\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) butter paper ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48050,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is non-luminous object?\na) Sun                b) Candle             c) Oil lamp              d) Water\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','d) Water','',0,0,0,NULL),(48051,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shadow formed by sunlight is longest \na) at noon              b) at night              c) in the morning            d) none of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','',' c) in the morning      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48052,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solar eclipse occurs on a ------------\na) new moon day             b) half moon day             c) full moon day           d) first quarter moon day\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) new moon day    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48053,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which one is odd among them?\na) Oil lamp               b) Candle            c) Sun              d) Moon\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','d) Moon','',0,0,0,NULL),(48054,801,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of these is not source of light?\na) Moon            b) Lighted candle           c) Stars                d) Sun\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) Moon   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48055,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Red light is scattered more than blue light.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. Red light is scattered less than blue light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48056,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The sun is an example of a point source of light.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. The sun is an example of an extended source of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48057,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : When the sun, the moon, the earth come in a straight line, an occultation takes place.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. When the sun, the moon, the earth come in a straight line, an eclipse takes place.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48058,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : When the solar disc is completely covered by the moon, a partial solar eclipse occurs.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. When the solar disc is completely covered by the moon, a total solar eclipse occurs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48059,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : A light beam can be seen in plain water.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48060,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Light is composed of several colours.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48061,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The blue colour in sunlight is scattered the most of which all.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48062,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The beam of light coming through a fine hole is a extended source of light.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48063,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by occultation?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The state in which a planet or a star hides behind the moon is called a occultation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48064,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the umbra appear?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The umbra appears dark.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48065,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The earth appears to be bluish, what is the cause of this effect?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The earth appears to be bluish due to the scattering of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48066,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where does the event of ‘zero shadow day’ occur?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The event of \'zero shadow day\' occurs in the region between the Tropic of Cancer (23.5°N) and the Tropic of Capricorn (23.5°S).','',0,0,0,NULL),(48067,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is seen in a beam of light?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Tiny dust particles are seen floating in the beam of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48068,801,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When does an eclipse occur?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the sun, the moon and the earth all revolving in different orbits come in a straight line, an eclipse occurs.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48069,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(48070,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(48071,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Point source of light\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When light emerges through very fine hole. This is called point source of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48072,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Eclipse\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the sun, the moon and the earth come in a straight line an eclipse is said to have taken place.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48073,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Solar Eclipse \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the moon comes between the sun and the earth, a shadow of the moon cast on the earth and they are in a straight line. This is called solar eclipse.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48075,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by scattering of light?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the light rays falls on the tiny dust particles, molecules and other particles present in the air. It get reflected in different direction. This phenomenon is called scattering of light.\nii) Scattered ray enter our eye due to which we see the beam of light.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48076,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The sky appears blue during the day.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The molecules of gases like nitrogen, oxygen present in the atmosphere, scatter the sunlight. At this time, the blue colour in the sunlight scatter the most. Hence, the sky appears blue during the day.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48077,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','When sunrays pass through a glass prism. What are the colours in the band of light seen on the other side of the prism?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We see the following colours in the band of light on the other side of the prisms while sunrays passes through a glass prism. Violet, Indigo, Blue, Green, Yellow, Orange, Red respectively. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48078,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Label the parts in the given diagram appropriately :\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',1,0,1,NULL),(48079,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Shadow formed by a point source of light\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48080,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Shadow formed by an extended source\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48081,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is an eclipse?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the sun, the moon and the earth comes in a straight the eclipse has taken place.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48082,801,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','When sunrays pass through a glass prism, what are the colours in the band of light seen on the other side of the prism?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sunrays after passing through the prism show bands of seven colours, violet, indigo, blue, grey, yellow, orange and red. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48083,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Does the shadow really vanish in the zero-shadow condition.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) On the zero shadow day at noon. The sun reaches completely over our head.\nii) Hence, the shadow of our body formed below the feet.\niii) Therefore, the shadow vanish in the zero shadow condition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48084,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give some examples of scattering of light that we come across in day-to-day life.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) During a sunrise or sunset, the sky appear yellowish.\nii) Beam of light emerging from the car headlight is visible in fog.\niii) The sky appear blue during a day time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48085,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is the shadow of a bird flying high not seen on the earth?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The sun is the extended sources of light.\nii) When the bird flies near the ground form the umbra.\niii) As the bird fly away from the ground, the shadow of the bird becomes fainter and fainter and finally the shadow of a bird disappears.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48086,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Point source of light and extended source of light\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48087,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Umbra and Penumbra\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48088,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Difference between : Solar eclipse and Lunar Eclipse\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48089,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : Space beyond the earth’s atmosphere appears dark.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In the earth\'s atmosphere enter the sunlight, it hits the tiny particles.\nii) Light get scattered due to hitting the tiny particles and we perceive the rays of light.\niii) Beyond the earth\'s space there is no particles. Thus the scattering of light does not take place in space.\niv) Therefore, the space beyond the earth\'s atmosphere appear dark.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48090,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : We are able to read while sitting in the shade.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The scattering of light occurs in the shade and enter our eyes.\nii) The sunlight get scattered is spread even in shade. Hence we are able to read sitting in the shade.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48091,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : We should not observe the solar eclipse with naked eyes.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The light emitted by the sun is harmful for our eyes.\nii) The ultra violet ray that comes from the sun enter the eyes causes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48092,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : When the sun rises, surroundings appear illuminated.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the sun rises, the sunlight gets scattered due to the molecules, dust particles and other tiny particles present in the atmosphere. because of this scattered sunlight, surroundings appears illuminated.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48093,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Solar eclipse\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48094,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Lunar Eclipse \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48095,801,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is a shadow? How is a shadow formed?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The light emerging from a source of light illuminates the screen placed in front of it. But, if an object is placed between the source of light and the screen, the light rays get obstructed by the object. As that part of the screen does not get illuminated, it remains dark. This dark part is known as shadow.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48096,801,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Will the laser beam be seen if it passes through a glass box which contains a lighted incense stick?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the light rays scattered, the ray of light to be visible.\nii) Light rays get scattered on falling the tiny particles in this path.\niii) The particles of the incense stick scattered in the glass box.\niv) These rays enter our eyes and the laser beam is seen.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48097,801,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Write the science-based paragraph on what if the sun did not rise?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The earth revolving around the sun, if it stop the part of the earth never face to the Sun. So, sun never rise at that part. The sun is the largest source of light on the earth. Due to this we see an objects around us plants on the earth make their own food with the help of sunlight. If there is no sunlight on the earth, photosynthesis get stopped. Due to absence of sunlight all plants can die and animals also die. Water in the seas and ocean would not evaporates, due to which there would be no rainfall. As a result the fresh water available on the earth would not increases. The amount of solar energy is generated with the help of sunlight would be stopped. The sun is also make a vital role in the maintaining temperature on the earth. In this way if the sun did not rise, the biotic and abiotic factors will be affected.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48098,801,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What efforts will you make to remove the misconceptions about eclipse?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Many superstition or misconceptions connected to eclipse are prevalent in our society. We will make people to understand and accept the fact that an eclipse is the natural phenomenon.\nii) We will aware to the people who are not educate about an eclipse.\niii) We will give information about eclipse through various media.\niv) We can arrange the programme to observe such a event in school and colleges.\nv) We should provide scientific information to the people to aware about misconceptions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48099,801,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Various eclipses and the conditions during that period.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Solar Eclipse: The moon comes between the sun and the earth and they are in a straight line, the moon\'s shadow falls on the earth.\nii) Partial Solar Eclipse: It visible us where penumbra of the moon falls on the earth. It occurs on new moon day.\niii) Total Lunar Eclipse: The earth comes between the sun and the moon and they are in a straight line. It occurs on a full moon day.\niv) Partial Lunar Eclipse: The moon comes in the shadow of the earth at this events. It occurs on a full moon day.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48100,801,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is shadow? How shadow is formed?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Shadow is a dark part that formed by an object on otherside due to the source of light.\nii) When the opaque object comes in the way of light, light does not passes through it, as a result the shadow is formed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48101,801,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : A beam of light is not seen in plain water, but it is seen in turbid water.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There are no tiny particles in plain water required for scattering of light, due to which the beam of light is not seen in plain water. \nii) Whereas, in turbid water, there are tiny particles. \niii) On hitting these particles, the light rays get scattered. When these scattered rays enter our eyes, we see the beam of light.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48102,801,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The sun appears reddish early in the morning.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) During sunrise, when the sun is very close to the horizon, sunlight travels a longer path through the atmosphere before reaching the observer. \nii) The blue light and violet light are scattered much more by earth’s atmosphere than red light.\niii) The blue light and violet light are scattered away from the path of light due to the greater thickness of the atmosphere.\niv) Hence, the light reaching the observer on the earth is mostly red and yellow.\nv) Therefore, the sun appears reddish early in the morning.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48103,801,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If a few more drops of milk are added, the reddish colour seen from the front becomes an intense red. However, if many more drops are added, the reddish colour is not seen. Why is this so?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When a few more drops of milk are added in water, more light is scattered. The reddish light contains red, yellow and orange colours.\nii) Gradually yellow and orange colours are also scattered in the water.\niii) If we add many more drops of milk the red light emerging from the front also gets scattered and thus it is not seen from the front.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48104,801,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why is a penumbra not obtained from a point source?\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The faint part of shadow called penumbra.\nii) It is obtained by only small part of light source is covered by an object. \niii) If the size of the object is bigger as compare to point source of light. The object completely block the point source forming umbra on the screen.\niv) Extended source is essential to form the penumbra of the object.\nv) Hence, penumbra is not obtained from the point source. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48105,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Sound is generated by the rhythmic ------------------- of an object.','','','vibrations ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48106,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The frequency of sound is measured in --------------------------','','','Hertz ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48107,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','If ------------------ of sound is decreased, its loudness also decreases.','','','frequency ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48108,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','A medium is necessary for the ------------------------ of sound.','','','Propagation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48109,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The unit of frequency is ----------------------------','','','Hertz ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48110,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The time period of oscillation is depending on the ---------------------- of the pendulum.','','','length ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48111,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The pitch of sound is directly related to the ---------------------- of sound.','','','Frequency ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48112,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A sound with frequency higher than --------------------- Hz is called ultrasonic sound.','','','20000 Hz','',0,0,0,NULL),(48113,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When a stretched rubber band vibrates, ---------------------- works on the stretched rubber band.','','','elasticity ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48114,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','While the pendulum oscillates, earth’s -------------------- works on it. ','','','gravitation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48115,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','As the length of the pendulum increases, the time period of oscillation ----------------------------','','','increases ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48116,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Frequency ------------------------- on increasing the length of the pendulum.','','','decreases ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48117,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The intensity of sound is proportional to the ------------------------- of the amplitude to vibration.','','','square ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48118,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the amplitude of vibrations is doubled, the intensity of sound becomes -------------------------------','','','four times ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48119,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A medium is necessary for the ---- of sound.\na) propagation	b) production 		c) measure 	d) none of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) propagation	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48120,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The velocity of sound is highest is ---- state of a substance.\na) gaseous		b) solids		c) liquids 		d) all above\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) solids	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48121,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following animal produces the ultra-sonic sound?\na) dog 		b) Fox 		c) Bat 		d) Fish\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','c) Bat 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48122,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The frequency of sound is measured ----- unit.\na) Decibel 	b) Intensity 		c) Minutes 	d) Hertz (Hz)\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','d) Hertz (Hz)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48123,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which sound is used in RADAR system?\na) Ultrasonic sound 	b) Infrasonic sound 	c) Audible sound 	d) All above\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) Ultrasonic sound ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48124,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following animal can produce the infrasonic sound.\na) Whales 		b) deer 		c) bat 		d) cat\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) Whales 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48125,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ultrasonic sound has a frequency -------\na) Lower than 20000 Hz 		                b) Higher than 20000 Hz 		\nc) Between 20 Hz to 20000 Hz		d) None of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) Higher than 20000 Hz 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48126,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which kind of sound is used to detect the tumors in the brains.\na) Infrasonic sound 	b) audible sound 	c) Ultrasonic sound	          d) Both a and c\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','c) Ultrasonic sound	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48127,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Frequency of sound = -----\na) 1/T 		b) T/1 		c) V/F 		d) D/V\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) 1/T 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48128,802,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Velocity of sound = ------\na) Frequency × wavelength 	b) Distance × speed 	c) Distance × speed 	d) both a and b  	\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) Frequency × wavelength ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48129,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Sound can travel without any medium\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48130,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Human ear can hear the sound with the frequency 20 Hz and 20000 Hz.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48131,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : All kind of sound have the same frequency.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48132,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Ultrasonic sound is used in RADAR systems.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48133,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : Sound is produced due to vibrations of an object.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48134,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : The oscillations completed by an oscillator in one second is called the time period of the oscillator.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. The number of oscillations completed by an oscillator is called the frequency of oscillation. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48135,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : The pitch of men’s voice is higher than women’s voice.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. The pitch of men’s voice is lower than that of women’s voice. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48136,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say right or wrong : As the tension in the stretched wire is decreased, the pitch of the sound increases.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. As the tension in the stretched wire is increased the pitch of the sound increases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48137,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am produced by the rhythmic vibrations of an object.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Sound ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48138,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the object moving from one end to the other end again and again in fixed intervals of time.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Pendulum ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48139,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the maximum distance on any side up to which the bob of the pendulum can move.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Amplitude ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48140,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the one due to which the stretched rubber comes back to original state on releasing.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Elasticity ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48141,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the measure of the number of oscillations completed in one second by an object performing oscillations.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Frequency ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48142,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who am I? : I am the time required to complete one oscillation.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Time period of oscillation ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48143,802,'descriptive','1b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Tell any two uses of ultrasonic sound.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Ultrasonic sound is used as  :\ni) To observe internal organs of the body\nii) To detect tumours in the brain.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48144,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by amplitude?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The maximum distance covered by an object on any one side of its central position while undergoing vibrations, is called amplitude of vibration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48145,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by oscillatory motion?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The back and forth motion of an oscillator on either side of a central position is called oscillatory motion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48146,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the unit of measuring frequency of sound?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Hertz ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48147,802,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which unit sound level is measured?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','decibel ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48148,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Match the following : ','','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – e), (4 – b), (5 – a) ','',1,0,0,NULL),(48149,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following : ','','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(48150,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Simple pendulum\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The object moving from one end to the other end of its central position, again and again in fixed intervals of time is called a simple pendulum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48151,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Oscillatory motion\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The back and forth motion of an oscillator on either side of a central position is called oscillatory motion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48152,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Oscillation\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When an oscillator moves from one end to the other and returns to its starting point, it is said to have completed one oscillation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48153,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Amplitude\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The maximum distance covered by an object on any one side of its central position while undergoing vibrations, is called amplitude of vibration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48154,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Time period of oscillation of the pendulum\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The time taken by an oscillator to complete one oscillation is called the time period of the oscillation of the pendulum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48155,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define :  Frequency of a pendulum\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The number of oscillations completed by an oscillator in one second is called the frequency of a pendulum.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48156,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Elasticity\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A stretched rubber comes back to its original state on releasing. This property is called elasticity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48157,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name few animals which can hear infrasonic sound.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Whales, elephants and rhinoceros can hear infrasonic sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48158,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does the intensity of sound depend upon?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The intensity of sound is depend upon frequency of vibration, amplitude of vibration and distance from the source of sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48159,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by time period oscillator?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The time required by an oscillator to complete one oscillation is called the time period of the oscillator.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48160,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What things is necessary to be sound produced?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A vibration is necessary to be sound produced.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48161,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Audible sound\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The sound with the frequency between 20Hz to 20,000 Hz is called audible sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48162,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Infrasonic sound\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A sound with frequency less than 20Hz is called infrasonic sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48163,802,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Ultrasonic sound\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A sound with a frequency higher than 20,000Hz. Such sound is called ultrasonic sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48164,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','How is sound produced?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A vibration source is necessary to be sound produced.\nii) When an object structed, it get vibrate and sound is produced.\niii) The rhythmic vibration causes sound is produced by objects.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48165,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What does the intensity of sound depends upon?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The intensity of sound depends upon frequency of vibration, amplitude of vibration and distance of the sound source from the observer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48166,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Explain how the frequency of oscillation is related to the length of a pendulum and the amplitude of its oscillation.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) As the length of pendulum increases, its period of oscillation is also increases.\nii) Means that the length of pendulum increases, the number of oscillation in one second is decreases.\niii) Frequency of oscillation of pendulum is dependent upon its amplitude of oscillation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48167,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','When a singer tunes the musical instruments before he starts singing. What exactly does he do?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Singers increases tension of a string with the help of tuner. \nii) A tabla player tunes the tabla by hammering its pegs to adjust the tension in the diphragm of tabla.\niii) A harmonium accompanist also adjust its harmonium to obtain the tunes properly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48168,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Will a sound the generated no matter? How the ruler is kept on the table?\n\nns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','No, sound will be generated if the ruler on the table kept properly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48169,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Is there any correlation between the length of the free part of the ruler and the sound generated?\n\nns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Yes, if there is length of ruler is more than frequency become less and the length of the ruler on the table i.e. free part of rular is less the sound will be generated with high tunes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48170,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the ruler is plucked while it is held with 25 cm of it off the table. Does it makes any sound? If there is no sound look for the reason why it is so.\n\nns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) No, if the ruler held with 2cm of its length on the table. It does not make any sound.\nii) Because, the vibration in ruler will take place very less.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48171,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the intensity of sound affected if the amplitude of sound is increased three times the original?\n\nns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The intensity of sound is proportional to the square of the amplitude of sound.\nii) Thus, if the amplitude of sound is increased three times the original then the intensity of sound would become nine times the original.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48172,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What structure in the sitar help to produce higher or lower pitched sounds?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Sitar has two structures, one is a gourd-shaped and other is long neck. \nii) The free end at the upper side of the long neck has many nails to roll the string. Seven strings are attached from the nails to the gourd. \niii) There are membranes below these strings. The tension in the strings can be increased or decreased by adjusting the nails. \niv) With the help of these structures, the higher and lower pitched sounds can be produced from the sitar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48173,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vibrating object completes 450 oscillations in 15 seconds. What is the frequency of vibrations of that object?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','450 oscillations are completed in 15 seconds.\n∴  In one second, 450/15 = 30 oscillations will be completed. \n(The number of oscillations completed in one second is the frequency of oscillations) OR The frequency is the number of oscillations completed in one second.\nThe frequency of oscillations of that object = 30 Hz.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48174,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If an oscillating object completes 180 oscillations in (one) minute, what is the time period of oscillations?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','180 oscillations are completed in 1 minute. \nThat means, 180 oscillations are completed in 60 seconds.\n∴ In one second, 180/60 = 3 oscillations will be completed \n∴ Frequency = 3 Hz. \nNow, frequency = 1/T ----- (Formula) \n∴ T= 1 /Frequency = 1/3  second\nThe time period of oscillations = 1/3 second.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48175,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A pendulum having a length of 30 cm takes 30 seconds to complete 20 oscillations. What is the time period of that pendulum?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The time taken by pendulum to complete 20 oscillations = 30 seconds.\n\n∴ The time required for 1 oscillation = 30/20 = 1.5 second \nThe time period of that pendulum = 1.5 second.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48176,802,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the time period of a vibrating object is 0.002 second, what will be frequency of that object? \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Frequency = 1/T ------ (Formula) \n                          = 1/0.002 \n                           = 1/(2/1000) = 1000/2 \n                           = 500Hz \n Frequency of that object = 500 Hz','',0,0,0,NULL),(48177,802,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the two ways by which the pitch of the sound generated by a stretched string can be changed.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The pitch of sound generated by a stretched string can be changed by the following two ways.\na) By Changing Length of String:\ni) When the length of string increases, the frequency of vibration decreases.\nii) When the length of the string decreases, the frequency of vibration increases.\nb) By Changing Tension of String:\ni) When the tension of the string is increased, frequency of vibration also increases causes high pitch shrill sound.\nii) When the tension of the strill decreases the frequency of vibration decreases due to low pitch of sound.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48178,802,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : In earlier times, people used listen for the arrival of a distant train by putting their ear to the rail.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A medium is necessary to travel the sound and vibration for the production.\nii) Sound travel fastest through the solid medium.\niii) A moving train produces sound which propagate through the rail tracks.\niv) Sound level and the intensity of sound enable listener to gauge how near the train is.\nv) Therefore, in earlier time people used to listen for the arrival of a distant train by putting their ear to the rail.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48179,802,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : The sounds generated by a tabla and a sitar different.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The sound generated by different instrument can be different.\nii) The diaphragm of tabla get vibrate, it generated the sound of different frequency than sound generated by plucking of a string of sitar.\niii) There may difference between the vibration of frequency of tabla and a plucked string of sitar.\niv) Hence, the sound generated by a tabla and a sitar are different.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48180,802,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : If you were on the moon your friend will not be able to hear you call.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A medium is necessary for the propagation of sound.\nii) There is no atmosphere on the moon. Thus due to the absence of medium on the moon. Sound cannot propagate on the moon.\niii) Hence, sound can not heard if my friend and I were both on the moon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48181,802,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Give reason : We can hear the movement of a mosquito’s wings but we can not hear the movement of our hands.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A human being can hear sound with frequency of 20Hz to 20,000Hz.\nii) The mosquito\'s buzz has the frequency between 20Hz to 20,000Hz.\niii) One cannot move his hands fast enough to perform 20Hz oscillation in one second, the frequency of oscillation of hand is less than 20 Hz.\niv) Hence, the movement of our hands produces infrasonic sound. This sound we cannot hear clearly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48182,802,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What would be the difference perceived between hearing only two pupils in the class talking to each other and all the children talking to one another at the same time?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a) When two pupil talk to each other.\ni) When two pupile talk in the class each other the producing sound will be less.\nii) Sound will be heard clearly while talking one person at a time.\nb) When all children talk to one another at the same time.\ni) When all children talk one another the sound produced with different frequency cannot be heard clearly.\nii) The sound level also increase as a result noise will be created.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48183,802,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain what is meant by a pendulum and draw its neat diagram. In the diagram, show the central position of the pendulum and amplitude of oscillation.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        \n\n\n\n','','','Pendulum: An oscillator which is made up of a small iron or wooden ball tied to a half a meter long strong thread and the other end of the thread is tied to a fixed support such that the ball is freely suspended in the air. Such an oscillator is called a pendulum.','',0,0,1,NULL),(48184,802,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the high and low pitch of the sound and explain how is the pitch of sound of women’s voice and men’s voice.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the tension in the vibrating wire is increased, the frequency increases and thus the sound produced is shriller. \nii) When the tension in the vibrating wire is decreased, the frequency decreases and thus the sound produced is less shrill. \niii) This is called the high and low pitch of sound. Generally, the pitch of men\'s voice is low and the pitch of women\'s and children\'s voice is high.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48185,802,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the types of sound based on frequency of sound or how it is heard (or whether it is heard or not)?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Following are the types of sound on the basis of frequency of sound :\ni) Infrasonic sound: A sound of frequency less than 20 Hz.\nii) Audible sound: A sound of frequency between 20 Hz to 20000 Hz.\niii) Ultrasonic sound: A sound of frequency higher than 20000 Hz.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48186,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The alloys called --------------- and -------------------- are used for making industrial magnet.','','','Nipermag, alnico ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48187,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','A magnetic field can pass through ---------------- and ------------------------','','','cardboard and water ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48188,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The intensity of a magnetic field is indicated by the lines of ----------------------------','','','Force ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48189,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The real test of a magnet is ----------------------------','','','repulsion ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48190,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Magnetic lines of force ------------------------ each other.','','','Repel ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48191,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A suspended magnet always settle in ------------------------- direction.','','','North - South ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48192,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------------------------ is use in metal detectors.','','','Electromagnet ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48193,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Earth’s magnetic ----------------------- pole is near the geographic south pole of the earth.','','','North ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48194,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The function of metal detectors is based on the properties of ----------------------------','','','electromagnet ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48195,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a magnetic substance.\na) Cobalt 		b) Copper 		c) Aluminum 		d) Plastic \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','a) Cobalt 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48196,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Magnet has ----- poles.\na) 3 			b) 2 			c) 4 			d) 6\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) 2 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48197,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which part of a bar magnet the magnetization is zero?\na) At the south pole 	b) At the north pole 	c) At the center 	d) none of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','c) At the center ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48198,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There is a ----- between like poles.\na) Contraction 	b) Attraction 		c) Refraction 		d) repulsion \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','d) repulsion ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48199,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An electromagnet is a ---- magnet.\na) Permanent 		b) Temporary 		c) Soft 		d) All above\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) Temporary 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48200,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The north pole of a freely suspended magnet settles in the direction of the geographic ----- pole of the earth.\na) North 		b) South 		c) West 		d) East\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','d) East','',0,0,0,NULL),(48201,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is not a magnetic substance?\na) Iron 		b) Nickel 		c) Cobalt 		d) Gold\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','d) Gold','',0,0,0,NULL),(48202,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Magnetic poles always exist as ------\na) Monopole 		b) Dipole		c) Tripole 		d) None of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) Dipole	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48203,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A bar magnet always settle in ----- direction.\na) South-North 	b) North-South	c) East-West 	         d) West-East\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','b) North-South','',0,0,0,NULL),(48204,803,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','To which material, magnet can be attracted towards itself?\na) Plastic 		b) Wood 		c) Cloth 		d) Iron nail \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------','','','d) Iron nail ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48205,803,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : A suspended bar magnet always settle in east-west direction.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48206,803,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Magnetic lines of force repel each other.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48207,803,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Magnetic lines of force are limited.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48208,803,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The real test of magnet is repulsion.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48209,803,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Magnetic lines of force intersect each other.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. Magnetic lines of force repel each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48210,803,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Magnetic lines of force are invisible.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48211,803,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the magnetic substances.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Iron, Cobalt and Nickel are the magnetic substances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48212,803,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which magnetic poles attracts each other?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','South and north poles of a magnet attracts each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48213,803,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by magnetic filed?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The magnetic field around a magnet where magnetic force acts on an object is called a magnetic field. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48214,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','With whom should I pair up?','','','(1 – d), (2 – c), (3 – b), (4 – a)','',1,0,0,NULL),(48215,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any two properties of magnetic lines of force?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A magnetic lines of force repel each other.\nii) Magnetic lines of force always run from the north pole to the south pole.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48216,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between the two methods of making artificial magnet.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48217,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which substances are used for making electromagnets?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','An iron nail, copper wire, A battery and pins area the substances used for making electromagnet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48218,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In which direction does a freely suspended magnet settle?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A freely suspended magnet always settles in north-south direction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48219,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which metals are used for a making magnet?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Metals like Iron, Cobalt and Nickel are used to making magnets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48220,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which magnetic poles attract each other?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Opposite pole of magnet attract each other i.e. north pole and south pole.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48221,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Which pole of the spherical magnet will get attracted towards the south pole of the bar magnet?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','South pole of the spherical magnet will get attract towards the south pole of a bar magnet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48222,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Is magnetic force a vector or scalar quantity?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Magnetic force is a vector quantity.\nii) Because it has both magnitude and direction.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48223,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Earth’s magnetism\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48224,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Magnetic field\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48225,803,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw the diagram : Properties of magnetic lines of force\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Diagram : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48226,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write a short note on magnetic field.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The region around a magnet where the magnetic force acts on an object is called magnetic field\nii) The intensity of a magnetic field is low where the lines of force are sparse\niii) The intensity is high where the lines of force are concentrated.\niv) Michael Faraday, imagined that there might be invisible lines of force going from one pole of a magnet to the other pole.\nv) The magnetic field can be shown around the magnet with magnetic lines of force.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48227,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why magnetic needle used in a compass?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The earth has its magnetic north pole near geographical south and magnetic south pole near geographical north pole.\nii) When the freely suspended magnetic needle get affected by this magnetic field on the earth and settles in the north-south direction.\niii) North pole of the needle points in the direction of geographical south pole of the earth.\niv) Therefore, the magnetic needle is used in a compass.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48228,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What are the names given to the two ends of a magnet? Why are they named thus?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The two ends of magnet are named as north pole and south pole.\nii) The north pole of a magnet shows the di rection of geographical north pole of the earth and the south pole of magnet shows geographically south pole of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48229,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Why does a freely suspended magnet always settle in the north-south direction?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The earth has a magnetic poles.\nii) The north pole of a freely suspended mag net settles in the direction of the geographic north pole of the earth.\niii) The south pole of magnet is near the geographic north pole of the earth and the north pole of the magnet, near the geographic south pole of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48230,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Which direction will a magnetic needle show on the geographic north pole?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A magnetic needle shows the direction towards the geographic south pole of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48231,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by magnetic force?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The magnet get attracts the magnetic sub- stances by exerting the force, such force is called magnetic force.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48232,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is the difference between gravitational force and magnetic force.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48233,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any four properties of magnetic lines of force.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Magnetic lines of force always start from the north pole and end on the south pole. \nii) Magnetic lines of force are in a state of tension like a stretched string. \niii) Magnetic lines of force repel each other. \niv) Magnetic lines of force never intersect each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48234,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write two uses of metal detectors. \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The metal detectors are used for inspection of persons entering very important places. e.g. airports, bus stand, certain temples and buildings. \nii) In geology, the metal detectors are used to detect the presence and quantity of metals such iron in the minerals found in the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48235,803,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is electromagnet? \n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A magnet is prepared by passing an electric current through a copper wire wound around a piece of soft iron. \nii) The magnetism is produced in a piece of iron till the current flows through the copper wire. \niii) The magnet produced in this way, is called an electromagnet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48236,803,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain with the help of diagram how the intensity and direction of the magnetic filed of a bar magnet can be determined.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','i) The region around a magnet where the magnetic force acts on an object is called a magnetic field.','ii) The intensity of a magnetic field at a place can be gauged by the number of lines of force that passes through a unit area at that place perpendicular to that area.\niii) The intensity of the magnetic field can be obtained from the number of lines of force.\niv) The intensity of a magnetic field is low where the lines of force are concentrated.\nv) The direction of the magnetic field is determined by the direction of magnetic lines of force. As magnetic lines. Lines of force always run from the north pole to the south pole.\nvi) Hence, the magnetic field of bar magnet is always directed from the north pole to the south pole.',0,0,1,NULL),(48237,803,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','',' Give detailed information about how the merchant of olden times. Use a magnet while travelling.\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) While travelling the merchant in olden times use the magnet to find the direction of its way.\nii) The freely suspended magnet always settles in the north-south direction.\niii) This helps them in determining the direction of their way.\niv) They were suspended such magnet by a thread, they used to settle in north-south direction.\nv) In this way magnets helped the merchant in their transportation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48238,803,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the properties of magnetic lines of force?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The properties of magnetic lines of force are:\ni) Magnetic lines of force always run from the north pole to the south pole.\nii) Magnetic lines of force are in a state of tension like a stretched spring.\niii) Magnetic lines of force repel each other.\niv) Magnetic lines of force do not intersect each other.\nv) The number of magnetic lines force at a particular point determines the strength of the magnetic field there.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48239,803,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Where and how are magnet used in our houses and our surroundings?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Magnet is used in maglev train on the basis of repulsion proportion and electromagnetisms.\nii) Permanent magnets are used in pin holder and in door of the cupboard.\niii) Electromagnets are used in door bells.\niv) Magnetic materials are in many electrical devices.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48240,803,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the characteristics of magnets?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The characteristics of magnet are:\ni) A magnet always settles in north-south direction.\nii) Opposite poles of magnet attract each other.\niii) The strength of magnet is in its both poles. \niv) Magnet attracts only magnetic substances.\nv) Two poles of magnet cannot be separated.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48241,803,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How does a magnetic force act without direct contact?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The magnetic lines of force spreads around magnet near a certain distance.\nii) The magnetic force is developed around the magnet,\niii) Thus, a magnetic force act without direct contact.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48242,803,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Why is repulsion the real test for identifying a magnet?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) There is attraction between two unlike poles of a magnet.\nii) However, the magnet attracts the magnetic substances.\niii) The magnet attracts the other magnet with unlike poles.\niv) There is repulsion only between two like poles of a magnet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48243,803,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','How will you find a magnet from among the various articles given to you?\n\nAns. : --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Magnet has repulsion and attraction properties.\nii) We will take a bar magnet first and it brings near the various articles that we have taken.\niii) If there is no attraction between both the end then we comes to know it is a non- magnetic substance.\niv) If there is attraction at one of the end and at one is repulsion then the other object is a magnet.\nv) Because, repulsion is a real test of a magnet.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48244,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','When seen from a great distance, the sky seems to be touching the ground along a circle. this circle is called the -----------------------','','','horizon ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48245,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The ------------------- is used while defining the Zodiac Signs.','','','ecliptic ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48246,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Classified according to seasons, one season will have ----------------------- Nakshatras.','','','nine ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48247,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The rising of the sun in the east and its setting in the west is the -------------------- motion of the sun.','','','apparent ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48248,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nebulae are clouds made up mainly of --------------------- gas.','','','hydrogen ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48249,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The portion transversed by the moon in one day is called ---------------------------','','','nakshtra ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48250,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Stars are born out of --------------------------','','','nebulae ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48251,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The circular horizon divides celestial sphere into --------------------- equal parts.','','','two ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48252,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A straight line passing through the three middle stars of Mruga nakashtra (Orion constellation) reaches a bright star called -----------------------\n','','','Vyodh or Sirius ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48253,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Pole star has ------------------ constellation on one side and --------------------- constellation on the other side.','','','The Great Bear (Saptarshi) and Cassiopeia (Sharmishta)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48254,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nebulae are made up of ----- gas and ------.\na) hydrogen, dust particles 	b) nitrogen, water droplets 	c) carbon dioxide, air molecules   	d) helium, ice\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------','','','a) hydrogen, dust particles ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48255,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Stars are born out of -----\na) Supernova 		b) Galaxy 	c) Nebulae 		d) Black hole\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------','','','c) Nebulae ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48256,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- or telescope are used for sky watching.\na) microscope 	b) binoculars      	c) speedometer 		d) stethoscope\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------','','','b) binoculars   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48257,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The moon completes one revolution around the earth in approximately ---- days.\na) 30 		b) 26.3 		c) 27.3 		d) 27.5\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------','','','c) 27.3 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48258,804,'fillinblanks','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The star, brighter and larger than the red giant stars are ------\na) Pole stars 			b) Twin star 		c) Supernova 		d) Sunlike star  \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------','','','c) Supernova ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48259,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Nebulae are clouds made up of only dust particles.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48260,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The ecliptic are divided into 12 parts.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48261,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : The constellation can be seen in bright sunlight.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Wrong ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48262,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Different stars have the different characteristics.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Right ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48263,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : On a sky map, south is shown at bottom and north is shown at the top of the map.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. On a sky map, south is shown at top and north is shown at the bottom of the map.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48264,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : In December, ‘The great Bear’ constellation rises on the horizon at 8 pm in northeast direction.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. In February Great bear constellation rises on the horizon at 8 pm in northwest direction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48265,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : In February, Orion rises at 8 pm on the eastern horizon.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. In December, Orion rises at 8 pm on the eastern horizon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48266,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : As Cassiopeia constellation sets, Great Bear constellation rises.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. As Cassiopeia constellation sets, \'The Great Bear\' constellation rises.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48267,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Say Right or Wrong : Orion is easily seen on summer nights.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False. Orion is easily seen on winter nights.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48268,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Constellation seen on the meridian in February.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Orian or Mrug nakshtra ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48269,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Brightest star in the Scorpio constellation.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Jyeshtha or Antares ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48270,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Apparent motion of sun\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Apparent path ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48271,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Institute located at Pune that carries out fundamental research in astronomy.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','IUCAA – Inter University Centre for Astronomy and Astrophysics.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48272,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Clouds made up of hydrogen gas and dust particles.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Nebula ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48273,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Continuous emptiness between stars and planes.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Space ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48274,804,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Brightest star that helps in identifying a constellation.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Yogtara ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48275,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – b)','',1,0,0,NULL),(48276,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :','','','(1 – e), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a), (5 – c)','',1,0,0,NULL),(48277,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Zenith\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','While standing on a ground the point on the celestial sphere exactly above our head is called zenith.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48278,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Nadir\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','As we stand under the sky, the point on the celestial sphere exactly below our feet is called the Nadir.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48279,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Celestial poles\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','If the axis of rotation of the earth is extended in the north and south direction it penetrates celestial sphere at points called north pole and south poles respectively.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48280,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Meridian\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The great astronomical circle which passes through both the celestial poles and the observes zenith and nadir called a meridian. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48281,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Celestial equator\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When the earth’s equator is extended identify in all direction it will penetrates the celestial sphere along a circle. This circle is known as the celestial equator.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48282,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Ecliptic\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The earth moves around the sun, but seen from the earth, the sun appears to move along a circle on the celestial sphere. The circle which shows the apparent motion of the sun around the earth is called the ecliptic.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48283,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Zodiac sign\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','When ecliptic is divided into 12 equal parts of 30 degrees from the centre of the celestial sphere each is called zodiac sign.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48284,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Nakshtra\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The portion of 27.3 days that revolution around the earth transversed by moon in one day is called nakshatra.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48285,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name any four stars.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Massive Star, Red Super Giant Star, Red Giant Star and Neutron Star.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48286,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the zodiac signs?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Aries, Taurus, Gemini, Cancer, Leo, Virgo, Libra, Scorpio, Sagittarius, Capricorn, Aquarias and Pisces are the zodiac sign.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48287,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','For what purposes the IUCAA have been established in India?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','IUCAA have been established for the astronomy research and to present a virtual projection of various stars and constellations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48288,804,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the constituents of the solar system.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Planets, satellites and comets are some constituents of the solar system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48289,804,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is a constellation?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) A group of the stars occupying a small portion of the celestial sphere is called a constellation.\nii) These stars appear to form a certain figures animals, humans or object.\niii) These figures have been named after a certain events or beliefs of the times when the constellation were identified.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48290,804,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Is it wrong to say that the planets, stars and nakshatras affect human life? Why?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Yes, because any planets, stars and nakshatras do not affect on the human life.\nii) Due to the technological progress man has stepped over the moon.\niii) Therefore, we can not say that planets, stars and any celestial body impact on human life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48291,804,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A star rises at 8 pm tonight. At what time will it rise after a month? Why?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) In the sky a stars rises 4 minutes early every next day.\nii) Hence after a month it will be 120 minutes or 2 hours earlier than tonight. In this way the star will rise at 6pm after a month.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48292,804,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why pole star is important in sky observation?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Since Pole star occupies a position on the earth\'s axis passing through the north pole. \nii) Therefore, it always points the north in a sky map. \niii) The position of Pole Star can be also indicated by constellation of Saptarshi i.e. The Great Bear on one side and Sharmishtha i.e. Cassiopeia on the other side.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48293,804,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a relation between The Great Bear, Cassiopeia and Pole star?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) The constellations of The Great Bear (Saptarshi) and Cassiopeia (Sharmishtha) are useful in locating the Pole Star. \nii) Cassiopeia is made up of five bright stars which are distributed along the figure of the letter M. \niii) The perpendicular bisector of the line joining the third and fourth stars in Cassiopeia goes towards the Pole Star. \niv) The Great Bear is a group of seven bright stars. It is in the shape of a quadrangle with a tail made up of three stars. \nv) It resembles a kite and can be easily recognized. If we extend one side of the quadrangle, it reaches the Pole Star.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48294,804,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What points should be considered before a sky watch?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The following points should be considered before sky watching. \ni) It should be a new moon night\nii) The telescope should be used for sky watching.\niii) The place which is selected should be away from the city.\niv) The pole star should be used as a preference point for sky watching.\nv) The device which is taken for sky watching should be adjusted properly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48295,804,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by ‘The Sun Enters a Nakshtra’? It is said that in the rainy season the sun enters the Mrug Nakshatra. What does it mean?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) When the observer looks from the earth, then observer sees many nakshatras behind the sun.\nii) But clear that a particular Raashi is entered there.\niii) In the rainy season, the sun enters the Mrug nakshatra means behind the sun.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48296,804,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a paragraph on the birth and lifecycle of an ordinary stars.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Life cycle of an ordinary stars :\ni) Ordinary Stars : These types of stars born out of nebulae, which are very big and balls of hot gases.\nii) Red giant stars : If the hydrogen gas at the centre of a star get exhausted, star begins to expand and cool and it becomes bigger 400 times than its original shape.\niii) Planetary Nebulae : In the lifecycle of the star ordinary stars turns planetary nebula.\niv) White Drawff : It has a brightly shining core, and it is very hot. Hence it called a white dwarf. It has low mass and very dense.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48297,804,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a paragraph on the birth and lifecycle of a massive stars.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Life Cycle of Massive Stars :\ni) Massive Star : It is bigger than ordinary stars.\nii) Massive Star : It is many time massive than red giant star.\niii) Supernova :\na) It is explosion of massive stars. \nb) It is very bright.\niv) Neutron Star : These are very compact star which remains after the end of super nova phase.\nv) Black Hole : \na) These black holes formed when a massive star ends after supernova phase. \nb) Here is gravity is very strong. 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48298,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y are in direct proportion and x = 10 when y = 2. Then find the value of x, when y = 4.\na) 16 		b) 20 		c) 24 			d) -20\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','b) 20 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48299,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y are in inverse proportion such that x = 7 when y = 6 then find y when x = 21\na) 2 		          b) 3 		c) 9 		          d) -2\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','a) 2 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48300,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is an example of direct proportion?\na) No. of workers and their wages 		b) No. of workers and no. of days to complete a work	\nc) No. of students and no. of laddus distributed them 	d) None of these\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------','','','a) No. of workers and their wages 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48301,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is an example of inverse proportion?\na) No. of workers and their wages	         b) No. of students and no. of notebooks required 		\nc) Speed of vehicle and time taken to cover a distance 		d) none of these\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','c) Speed of vehicle and time taken to cover a distance ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48302,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','8 men can do a piece of work in 10 days. How long will 10 men take?\na) 12 days 		b) 8 days 		c) 7 days 		d) 6 days\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','b) 8 days ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48303,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rohit bought 12 registers for Rs. 156. Find the cost of 7 registers?\na) Rs. 110		b) Rs. 84 		c) Rs. 91 		d) Rs. 267\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','c) Rs. 91 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48304,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 14 kg of pulses cost Rs. 441, what is the cost of 22 kg of pulses?\na) Rs. 627 	b) Rs. 649 	c) Rs. 671 	d) Rs. 693\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','d) Rs. 693','',0,0,0,NULL),(48305,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 36 men can do a piece of work in 25 days, in how many days will 15 men do it?\na) 50 		b) 56 		c) 60 		d) 72\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','c) 60 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48306,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','6 men can do a piece of work in 8 days. How long will 8 men take?\na) 6 days 		b) 8 days 		c) 7 days 		d) 5 days\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','a) 6 days 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48307,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Raju types 270 words in 15 minutes. The number of words he will type in 3 minutes is -----\na) 44 		b) 64 		c) 74 		d) 54\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','d) 54','',0,0,0,NULL),(48308,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y vary directly, which of the following is not true?\na) If x increases y also increases 		b) If x decreases y also decreases 	\nc) If x decreases y increases 		        d) none of these\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','c) If x decreases y increases ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48309,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','“The water consumed and water bill” is a case of -----\na) direct proportion 	b) inverse proportion 	c) not in proportion 	d) none of these\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','a) direct proportion ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48310,805,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','x and y are said to vary directly with each other if for some positive number k, then ------ = k.\na) y/x²		b) x/y 		c) xy 		d) none of these\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------','','','b) x/y ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48311,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 10 pens cost 60 rupees, what is the cost of 13 such pens?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, cost of 10 pen is ₹ 60\n∴ cost of 1 pen = 60/10 = ₹ 6\n∴ cost of 13 pens = 13 × 6 = ₹ 78 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48312,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the cost of 6 toys is 240, then find the cost of 21 toys.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, cost of 6 toys is ₹ 240\n∴ cost of 1 toy = 240/6 = ₹ 40\n∴ Cost of 21 toys = 21 × 40 = ₹ 840 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48313,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the cost of 8 kg sugar is Rs. 280, find the cost of 15 kg of sugar.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, cost of 8 kg sugar is ₹ 280\n∴ cost of 1 kg sugar = 280/8 = ₹ 35\n∴ cost of 15 kg sugar = 15 × 35 = ₹ 525','',0,0,0,NULL),(48314,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y varies directly, complete the following tables:','\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','x and y varies directly\n∴ 2.5/5 = x/8 \n∴ x × 5 = 2.5 × 8 \n∴ x = 20/5 = 4 ','',1,0,0,NULL),(48315,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y varies directly, complete the following tables:','\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','x and y varies directly\n∴ 9/12 = x/48 \n∴ x × 12 = 9 × 48 \n∴ x = 432/12 = 36 ','',1,0,0,NULL),(48316,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y varies inversely, complete the following tables:','\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','x and y varies inversely\n∴ 16 × 4 = 32 × y\n∴ y = (16 × 4)/32 = 2','',1,0,0,NULL),(48317,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y varies inversely, complete the following tables:','\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','x and y varies inversely\n∴ 9 × 27 = x × 9\n∴ x = (9 × 27)/9 = 27','',1,0,0,NULL),(48318,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 100 students take 20 days to complete the project, then how much time will be taken by 125 students?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, \nx             100             125\ny              20               -----\nGiven example is in inverse variation.\nx and y varies inversely\n∴ 100 × 20 = 125 × y\n∴ y = (100 × 20)/125 = 16','',0,0,0,NULL),(48319,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x varies inversely to y and x = 4, when y = 6. The value of y, when x = 3.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, \nx            4           3\ny            6           -----\nx and y varies inversely\n∴ 4 × 6 = 3 × y\n∴ y = (6 × 4)/3 = 8','',0,0,0,NULL),(48320,805,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For a group of 32 students food lasts for 45 days. For how many days will the same food last for 72 students?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, \nx            32            72\ny            45            -----\nx and y varies inversely\n∴ 45 × 32 = 72 × y','',0,0,0,NULL),(48321,805,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If 7 kg onions cost 140 rupees, how much must we pay for 12 kg onions?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, \nx            7           12\ny           140       ------\nGiven example is in direct variation\n ∴ 7/140  = 12/y \n∴ y × 7 = 12 × 140 \n∴ y = (12 × 140)/7 = 240\n∴ 12 kg onion price is ₹ 240.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48322,805,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If 600 rupees buys 15 bunches of feed, how many will 1280 rupees buy?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, \nx            600           1280\ny            15               ------\nGiven example is in direct variation \n∴ 600/15 = 1280/y \n∴ y × 600 = 1280 × 15\n∴ y = (1280 × 15)/600 = 32\nHence, 32 bunches of feed will be bought by ₹ 1280.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48323,805,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','For 9 cows, 13 kg 500 gm of food supplement are required every day. In the same proportion, how much will be needed for 12 cows?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, \nx        9           12\ny       13.50     -----\nGiven example is in direct variation\n∴ 9/13.5  = 12/y \n∴ y × 9 = 12 × 13.5 \n∴ y = (12 × 13.5)/9 = 18\nHence, 12 cows 18 kg of feed is required.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48324,805,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Fifteen workers take 8 hours to build a wall. How many hours will 12 workers need to build the same wall?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, \nx   15       12\ny    8        -----\nGiven example is in inverse proportion.\n∴ 15 × 8 = 12 × y \n∴ y = 120/12 = 10\n∴ 12 workers will take 10 hours to build the wall.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48325,805,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A 40 page class magazine is to be written. If one student would require 80 days to write it, how many would 4 student require? \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that,\nx     1          4 \ny      80      -----\nGiven example is in inverse proportion.\n∴ 1 × 80 = 4 × y \n∴ y = 80/4 = 20\n∴ 4 students require 20 days.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48326,805,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Five workers take 12 days to weed a field. How many days would 6 workers take? How many would 15 take?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let 6 workers take x days to weed the field.\nSince number of workers and number of days required to weed the field are in inverse proportion.\n∴ x       5          6          15\n    y      12        ----        -----\ni) 5 × 12 = 6 × y \n    ∴ y = (5 × 12)/6 = 10\n    Hence, 6 workers take 10 days to weed a field\nii) 5 × 12 = 15 × y\n    ∴ y = 60/15 = 4\n     Hence, 15 workers take 4 days to weed a filed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48327,805,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','60 cows can graze in a field for 12 days then how many cows will graze in the same field in 5 days.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, \nx    60      ----\ny    12      5 \ngiven example is in inverse proportion.\n∴ 60 × 12 = 5 × x\n∴ x = (60 × 12)/5 = 12 × 12 = 144 \nHence, 144 cows are required to graze the same field in 5 days.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48328,805,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x and y are in direct variation. Complete the following table:','\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','Given that, x and y varies directly proportion.\ni)  x         4               5\n     y       28             ----\n   ∴ 4/28 = 5/y \n   ∴ 4 × y = 5 × 28 \n    ∴ y = (5 × 28)/4 = 5 × 7 = 35\nii)  x      4          ----\n     y      28         56\n    ∴  4/28 = x/56\n    ∴ x × 28 = 4 × 56\n    ∴ x = (4 × 56)/28 = 8 ','',1,0,0,NULL),(48329,805,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table of inverse proportion :','\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','Given that, a and b varies inversely proportion.\ni) a     2      ----\n   b     10    10\n    ∴ 2 × 10 = a × 10\n    ∴  a = 2\nii) a      2           20\n    b      10          -----\n   ∴ 2 × 10 = 20 × b\n   ∴ b = 20/20 = 1','',1,0,0,NULL),(48330,805,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The cost of 12 quintals of Soyabeen is 3600 rupees. How much will 8 quintals cost?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let the cost of 8 quintals of soyabean  is ₹ x\nSince the cost of soyabean and its weight is in direct proportion, we get\n3600/12  = x/8 \n ∴ 3000 = x/8\n  ∴ x = 24000\n  ∴ 8 quintals of soyabean costs ₹ 24000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48331,805,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Two mobiles cost 16000 rupees. How much money will be required to buy 13 such mobiles?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let the cost of 13 mobiles is ₹ x\nSince the cost of mobiles and number of mobiles are in direct proportion.\n∴  16000/2  = x/13\n∴ 8000 = x/13\n∴ x = 8000 × 13\n∴ x = 104000\nHence, the cost of 13 mobiles is ₹ 1,04,000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48332,805,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Mohanrao took 10 days to finish a book, reading 40 pages every day. How many pages must he read in a day to finish it in 8 days?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let the Mohanrao reads x pages every day to finish the book in 8 days.\nSince number of pages read per day and number of days required to finish the book are in inverse variation, we get,\n40 × 10 = x × 8\n∴ x = 400/8 \n∴ x = 50\nHence, Mohanrao must read 50 pages per day to finish the book is 8 days.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48333,805,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Mary cycles at 6 km per hour. How long will she take to reach her Aunt’s house which is 12 km away? If she cycles at a speed of 4km/hr, how long would she take?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Here speed and times are in inverse proportion.\nHence, Speed × time = distance (constant)\nNow, ','We have, speed × time = 12\ni) 6 × x = 12\n    ∴ x = 2\nii) 4 × y = 12\n    ∴ y = 3\n    ∴ Mary takes 2 hours to reach Aunt’s house at the speed 6 km/hr. and Mary takes 3 hours to reach her Aunts house at the speed 4 km/hr.',0,0,1,NULL),(48334,805,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The stock of grain in a government warehouse lasts 30 days for 4000 people. How many days will it last for 6000 people?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let the stock of grain lasts for x days for 6000 people.\nSince number of people and number of days are inverse proportion, we get\n4000 × 30 = 6000 × x\n∴ x =120000/6000 = 20\nHence, the stock of grain will last in 20 days for 6000 people.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48335,805,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Jhelum and Athrava invested 2100 and 2800 rupees respectively and started a business. They made a profit of 3500 rupees. How should it be shared?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let us find out the proportion of investments.\n2100 : 2800 = 2100/2800 = 3/4 = 3 : 4\n∴ Proportion of investments is 3 : 4\nThe profit must also be shared in the same proportion.\nLet Jhelum’s profit be 3x and that of Atharva 4x then,\n3x + 4x = 3500\n∴ 7x = 3500\n∴ x = 500\nJhelum’s share = 3x = 3 × 500 = 1500\nAthrava’s share = 4x = 4 × 500 = 2000 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48336,805,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Suresh and Ramesh together invested 144000 rupees in the ratio 4 : 5 and bought a plot of land. After some years they sold it at a profit of 20%. What is the profit each of them got?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, investment = ₹ 144000 and profit = 20%\n∴ Total profit = 144000 × 20/100 = ₹ 28800\nSince the investment of Suresh and Ramesh is in the ratio 4 : 5. The profit will also distributed in the ratio  4 : 5.\nIf Suresh gets 4x and Ramesh gets 5x as profit, then\n4x + 5x = 28800\n∴ 9x = 28800\n∴ x = 3200\nHence, Suresh gets a profit of 4x = 4 × 3200 = ₹ 12800\nAnd Ramesh gets a profit of 5x = 5 × 3200 = ₹ 16000','',0,0,0,NULL),(48337,805,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Virat and Samrat together invested 50000 and 120000 rupees to start a business. They suffered a loss of 20%. How much loss did each of them incur?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The ratio of investment of Virat and Samrat is \n      50000/120000 = 5/12 = 5 : 12\nAlso, Total investment = 50,000 + 1,20,000 = 1,70,000\nSince their loss of 20%\nTotal loss = 170000 × 20/100 = 34000\nLet Virat incurred loss of 5x and Samrat incurred loss of 12x then\n5x + 12x = 34000\n∴ 17x = 34000\n∴ x = 2000\n∴ 5x = 5 × 2000 = 10,000 and 12x = 12 × 2000 = 24,000\nHence, Virat incurred loss of ₹ 10,000 and Samrat incurred loss of ₹ 24000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48338,805,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A car covers a distance of 125 km in 24 liter of petrol then how far can it travel in 10 liter petrol? \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, \n      x         125     ------\n      y           24        10\n    Here, x and y varies directly \n∴  125/24 = x/10\n∴ x × 24 = 125 × 10\n∴ x = (125 × 10)/24 = 52 km','',0,0,0,NULL),(48339,805,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Chinmaya and Sam invested a total of 130000 rupees in a business in the proportion 3 : 2 respectively. what amount did each of them invest? If their total profit was 36000 rupees, what is the share of each?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The proportion of Chinmaya’s and Sam’s investment is 3 : 2.\nThe profit is shared in the same proportion as the investment, hence, proportion of profit is 3 : 2\nLet Chinmaya’s investment be 3y and Sam’s 2y.\n3y + 2y = 130000\n∴ 5y = 130000\n∴ y = 26000\n∴ Chinmaya’s investment = 3y = 3 × 26000 = 78,000\nSam’s investment = 2y = 2 × 26000 = 52000\nLet Chinmaya’s profit be 3x and Sam’s be 2x\n∴ 3x + 2x = 36000\n∴ 5x = 36000\n∴ x = 7200\n∴ Chinmaya’s profit = 3x = 3 × 7200 = 21600\nSam’s profit = 2x = 2 × 7200 = 14400 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48340,805,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Abdul, Sejal and Soham each gave Sayali 30 rupees, 70 rupees and 50 rupees respectively. Sayali put in 150 rupees and bought a paper, colors, etc. together they made greeting cards and sold them all. If they made a total profit of 420 rupees. What was each one’s share in the profit?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The capital invested by all four was ₹ 300. Of this Sayali had invested 150 rupees, that is half of the capital. \nThe total profit was 420 rupees. So, Sayali’s profit was half of the capital. i.e. 210 rupees.\nThe remaining 210 was shared by Abdul, Sejal and Soham\nAbdul, Sejal and Soham’s investment is 30, 70 and 50 rupees.\nThe proportion is 30 : 70 : 50. i.e. 3 : 7 : 5\nTheir share of the profit is altogether 210 rupees.\nLet their individual profit be 3k, 7k, 5k.\nThen, 3k + 7k + 5k = 210\n∴ 15k = 210\n∴ k = 14\nAbdul’s profit = 3k = 3 × 14 = 42 rupees\nSejal’s profit = 7k = 7 × 14 = 98 rupees\nSoham’s profit = 5k = 5 × 14 = 70 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48341,805,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Saritaben, Ayesha and Meenakshi started a business by investing 2400, 5200 and 3400 rupees. They made a profit of 50%. How should they share it? If they reinvest all their profit by adding it to the capital, what will each one’s share be in the following year?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total capital = 2400 + 5200 + 3400 = 11000 rupees\nProfit 50%\n∴ Total profit = (11000 × 50)/100 = 5500\nProfit will be shared in the same proportion as the investment.\nWe simplify the ratio of two numbers by dividing by a common factor.\nThe same can be done for 3 or more numbers.\nProportion of shares = 2400 : 5200 : 3400 = 24 : 52 : 34 = 12 : 26 : 17\nAssume that, Saritaben’s profit = 12p, Ayesha’s profit = 26p and Meenakshi’s profit = 17p\n∴ 12p + 26p + 17p = 5500\n∴ 55p = 5500\n∴ p = 100\n∴ Saritaben’s profit = 12 × 100 = 1200\nAyesha’s profit = 26 × 100 = 2600 and \nMeenakshi’s profit = 17 × 100 = 1700\nIf they add their profit to the capital, their further investments will be : \nSaritaben’s capital = 2400 + 1200 = ₹ 3600\nAyesha’s capital = 5200 + 2600 = ₹ 7800\nMeenakshi’s capital = 3400 + 1700 = ₹ 5100','',0,0,0,NULL),(48342,805,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Shweta, Piyush and Nachiket together invested 80000 rupees and started a business of selling sheets and towels from Solapur. Shweta’s share of the capital was 30000 rupees and Piyush’s 12000. At the end of the year they had made a profit of 24%. What was Nachiket’s investment and what was his share of the profit?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let Nachiket investment is ₹ x\n∴ Total investment = 30000 + 12000 + x\n∴ 80,000 = 42000 + x\n∴ x = 38000\nNow, profit is of 24%\n∴ Nachiket profit = 24% of his investment = 38000 × 24/100 = 9120\nHence, Nachiket invested ₹ 38000 and got a profit of ₹ 9120 in the business.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48343,805,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A and B shared a profit of 24500 rupees in the proportion 3 : 7. Each of them gave 2% of his share of the profit to the Soldiers Welfare Fund. What was the actual amount given to the Fund by each of them?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let A got a profit of ₹ 3x and B got a profit ₹ 7x\n∴ Total profit = A + B \n∴ 3x + 7x = 24500\n∴ 10x = 24500\n∴ x = 2450\n∴ 3x = 3 × 2450 = 7350 and 7x = 7 × 2450 = 17150\nThus, profit of A = ₹ 73350 and B = ₹ 17150\nNow each of them gave 2% of amount to the soldier’s welfare fund\n∴ A gave 2% of 73350 = 73350 × 2/100 = ₹ 147\nB gave 2% of ₹ 17150 = 17150 × 2/100 = ₹ 343','',0,0,0,NULL),(48344,805,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Jaya, Seema, Nikhil and Neelesh put in altogether 360000 rupees to form a partnership, with their investments being in the proportion 3 : 4 : 7 : 6. What was Jaya’s actual share in the capital? They made a profit of 12%. How much profit did Nikhil make?\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Since the investment is in proportion of 3 : 4 : 7 : 6\nLet the investments of Jaya, Seema, Nikhil and Neelesh are 3x, 4x, 7x and 6x respectively.\nTotal capital = 360000\n∴ 3x + 4x + 7x + 6x = 360000\n∴ 20x = 360000\n∴ x = 18000\n∴ Jaya’s investment = 3x = 3 × 18000 = 54000\nNikhil’s investment = 7x = 7 × 18000 = 126000\nNow, profit percent is 12%\n∴ Nikhil’s profit = 126000 × 12/100 = ₹ 15120\nHence, Jaya’s investment is ₹ 54000 and Nikhil’s profit is ₹ 15120.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48345,805,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ram and Shyam together invested Rs. 50000 and 120000 rupees to start a business. They suffered a loss of 20%. Find their individual loss.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Loss of Ram be 20% of 50,000\n∴ Loss of Ram = 50,000 × 20/100 = ₹ 10,000\nLoss of Shyam be 20% of 120,000\n∴ Loss of Shyam = 120000 × 20/100 = ₹ 24000','',0,0,0,NULL),(48346,805,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Suresh and Ramesh together invested Rs. 144000 in the ratio 4 : 5 for a business. They have 20% profit. Then find their individual profit.\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, ratio of investment of Suresh and Ramesh be 4 : 5\n∴ Suresh invested 4x and Ramesh invested 5x respectively.\n∴ 4x + 5x = 144000\n∴ 9x = 144000\n∴ x = 16000\n∴ Suresh invested = 4x = 4 × 16000 = ₹ 64,000\nRamesh invested = 5x = 5 × 16000 = ₹ 80,000\nThey have 20% profit.\n∴ Suresh profit = 64000 × 20/100 = ₹ 12,800\nRamesh profit = 80000 × 20/100 = ₹ 16000. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48347,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','If the interest on 1700 rupees is 340 rupees for 2 years. The rate of interest must be -----\na) 12% 		b) 15% 		c) 4% 		d) 10%\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','d) 10%','',0,0,0,NULL),(48348,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','If the interest on 3000 rupees is 600 rupees at a certain rate for a certain number of years, what would the interest be on 1500 rupees under the same conditions?\na) Rs. 300		b) Rs. 1000		c) Rs. 700		d) Rs. 500\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','a) Rs. 300	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48349,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of account holder can deposit or withdraw money at any number of times in a day.\na) saving 		b) current 	c) fixed deposit	d) recuring deposit\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','b) current ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48350,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fill in the blanks : A = ----- + I\na) P 		b) R 		c) T 		d) 100\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','a) P 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48351,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fill in the blanks : Interest (I) = (P × ---- × T)/100\na) P 		b) T 		c) R 		d) A\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','c) R ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48352,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The full form of ATM is -----\na) Automatic tailor money 		b) Any time money 	c) Automatic tailor machine		d) None of these \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','c) Automatic tailor machine','',0,0,0,NULL),(48353,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If principal is Rs. 250 and rate of interest is 4% per annum, find the simple interest for 6 years.\na) Rs. 60              b) Rs. 65              c) Rs. 70               d) Rs. 50\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','a) Rs. 60       ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48354,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Calculate the simple interest on Rs. 1000 for 2 years at 10% per annum.\na) Rs. 400          b) Rs. 200            c) Rs. 250             d) Rs. 100\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','b) Rs. 200     ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48355,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A man borrowed Rs. 8000 from a bank per annum. Find the amount he has to pay after 4 ½ years?\na) Rs. 10980                b) Rs. 11000            c) Rs. 10880            d) Rs. 10980\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','c) Rs. 10880 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48356,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Neena borrowed Rs. 900 from her friend at 10% per annum. She returned the amount after six months. How much amount did she pay?\na) Rs. 950             b) Rs. 1000               c) Rs. 935            d) Rs. 945\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','d) Rs. 945','',0,0,0,NULL),(48357,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A person deposits Rs. 25000 in a firm who pays an interest at the rate of 20% per annum. Calculate the income he gets, from it annually.\na) Rs. 5000               b) Rs. 5500              c) Rs. 4000             d) Rs. 4500\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','a) Rs. 5000    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48358,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the interest on Rs. 2400 at 6% per annum for 146 days.\na) Rs. 57                b) Rs. 57.60               c) Rs. 58                d) Rs. 58.60\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','b) Rs. 57.60    ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48359,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','At what rate percent per annum will a sum of money double in 8 years?\na) 12.5%                b) 12%                   c) 14%                  d) 13.5%\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','a) 12.5%   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48360,806,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The simple interest on a sum of money is 25% of the principal and the rate per annum is equal to number of years. Find the rate per cent?\na) 6%                  b) 10%              c) 5%                   d) 8%\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','c) 5%      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48361,806,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If Rs. 120000 are borrowed for 4 years at 10 p. c. p. a. find its interest.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 1,20,000, T = 4 years, R = 10%\n∴ S. I. = (P × R × T)/100 = (1,20,000 × 4 × 10)/100 = ₹ 48,000 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48362,806,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If P = 10000, R = 8%, T = 2 years. Find A.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 10,000, T = 2 years, R = 8 %\n∴ S. I. = (P × R × T)/100 = (10,000 × 2 × 8)/100 = ₹ 1600\n ∴ A = P + I = 10,000 + 1600 = ₹ 11600','',0,0,0,NULL),(48363,806,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any four names of nationalized banks.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','State Bank of India, Bank of India, Bank of Maharashtra, Bank of Baroda, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48364,806,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If I = 1200, T = 5 years, R = 10%, find P.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, I = 1200, T = 5, R = 10\n∴ S. I. = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 1200 = (P × 5 × 10)/100\n∴ 1200 = P/2\n∴ P = 2400','',0,0,0,NULL),(48365,806,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If A = 5082, P = 4200, R = 7%, find T.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, A = 5082, P = 4200, R = 7 %\n∴ A = P + I \n∴ 5082 = 4200 + I \n∴ I = 5082 – 4200 = 882\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 882 = (4200 × 7 × T)/100\n∴ T = 882/294\n∴ T = 3 years','',0,0,0,NULL),(48366,806,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If P = Rs. 5000, T = 4 years, I = Rs. 1200, find R.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 5000, T = 4, I = 1200\n∴  I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 1200 = (5000 × 4 × R)/100\n∴ R = 1200/200 = 6 %','',0,0,0,NULL),(48367,806,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Neha took a loan of 50000 rupees at 12 p. c. p. a. to a buy a two-wheeler. What amount will she return to the bank at the end of the one year?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 50,000, R = 12%, T = 1 year\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ I = (50,000 × 12 × 1)/100\n∴ I = 6000\n∴ A = P + I = 50,000 + 6000 = ₹ 56,000\n∴ Amount to be returned to the bank = ₹ 56,000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48368,806,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Aakash deposited 25000 rupees in a bank at a rate of 8 p. c. p. a. for 3 years. How much interest does he get every year? How much, altogether?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 25,000, R = 8%, T = 3 year\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ I = (25,000 × 8 × 3)/100\n∴ I = 6000','',0,0,0,NULL),(48369,806,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If Rihanna deposits 1500 rupees in the school fund at 9 p. c. p. a. for 2 years, what is the total amount she will get?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 1500, R = 9%, T = 2 year\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ I = (1500 × 9 × 2)/100\n∴ I = 270\n∴ A = P + I = 1500 + 270 = ₹ 1770\n∴ Rihana will get total amount of ₹ 1770.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48370,806,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If Pankaj deposits 150000 rupees in a bank at 10 p. c. p. a. for two years, what is the total amount he will get from the bank?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 150000, R = 10 %, T = 2 year\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ I = (150000 × 10 × 2)/100\n∴ I = 30,000\n∴ A = P + I = 150000 + 30000 = ₹ 180000\n Hence, Pankaj get ₹ 180000 from the bank.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48371,806,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If P = 25000 rupees, Amount = 31000 rupees, Time = 4 years, what is the rate of interest? \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 25000, A = 31000, T = 4 years, R = ?\nA = P + I \n31000 = 25000 + I\nI = 31000 – 25000 = 6000\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 6000 = (25000 × 4 × R)/100\n∴ R = 6000/1000 = 6%','',0,0,0,NULL),(48372,806,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Sandeepbhau borrowed 120000 rupees from a bank for 4 years at the rate of 8.5 p. c. p. a. for his son’s education. What is the total amount he returned to the bank at the end of that period?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 120000, R = 8.5, T = 4\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100 \n     = (120000 × 8.5 × 4)/100 = 40800\nThe total amount returned to the bank = 120000 + 40800\n         = ₹ 160800.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48373,806,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Shrikant deposited 85000 rupees for 2.5 years at 7 p. c. p. a. in a saving bank account. What is the total interest he received at the end of the period?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 85000, R = 7 %, T = 2.5 years\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100 \n      = (85000 × 7 × 2.5)/100 = 14875\nHence, Shrikant received total interest of ₹ 14875 at the end of the period.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48374,806,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Unmesh borrowed some money for 5 years at simple interest. The rate of interest is 9 p. c. p. a. If he returned 17400 rupees altogether at the end of 5 years, how much had he borrowed?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','I = (P × R × T)/100\nThis formula cannot be used directly to solve the problem because we do not know either interest or principle. \nHowever, the interest on principal of 100 rupees for 5 years is 45 rupees. \nHence, the amount is 100 + 45 = 145 rupees.\nNow we can express the ratio of principal and amount in two ways and obtain an equation.\nIf Unmesh’s principal is P then P/17400 = 100/145\n∴ P = (100 × 17400)/145 = 12000\n∴ The principal that Unmesh borrowed was 12000 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48375,806,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If Principal (P) = 4200, R = 7%, T = 3 years, then find Interest (I) and Amount (A).\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','I = (P × R × T)/100\n       = (4200 × 7 × 3)/100 = ₹ 882\nA = P + I = 4200 + 882 = ₹ 5082','',0,0,0,NULL),(48376,806,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If R = 6%, T = 4 years, Interest (I) = 1200, then find P and A?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','I = (P × R × T)/100\n     1200  = (P × 6 × 4)/100 \n∴ P = (1200 × 100)/24\n      = 5000\nA = P + I = 5000 + 1200 = ₹ 6200','',0,0,0,NULL),(48377,806,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If Sharayu deposited Rs. 150000 in a bank for 2 years at 10 p. c. p. a. What amount will she get after 2 years.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 150000, R = 10%, T = 2 years\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n      = (150000 × 10 × 2)/100\n      = 30,000\n∴ A = P + I = 150000 + 30000 = ₹ 1,80,000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48378,806,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Asha borrowed Rs. 21000 for 4 years at certain rate of interest. If she returned Rs. 27300 at the end of 4 years, find the rate of interest she paid. \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','P = 21000, T = 4 years, A = 27300, R = ?\n∴ I = A – P = 27300 – 21000 = 6300\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 6300 = (21000 × 4 × R)/100\n∴ R = 6300/840 = 7.5 %','',0,0,0,NULL),(48379,806,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Jethalal took a housing loan of 250000 rupees from a bank at 10 p. c. p. a. for 5 years. What is the yearly interest he must pay and the total amount he returns to the bank?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 2,50,000, R = 10%, T = 5 years\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n     = (250000 × 10 × 1)/100 = 25000\n∴ Jethalal has to pay an interest of ₹ 25000 yearly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48380,806,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','At a certain rate of interest, the interest after 4 years on 5000 rupees principal is 1200 rupees. What would be the interest on 15000 rupees at the same rate of interest for the same period?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 5000, T = 4 years, I = 1200\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 1200 = (5000 × 4 × R)/100\n∴ R = 1200/200 = 6\nNow, to find interest on 15000 rupees at same time period and same rate.\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n      = (15000 × 6 × 4)/100 = 3600\n∴ The interest on ₹ 15000 for 4 years at 6 % is ₹ 3600.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48381,806,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Javed deposited 12000 rupees at 9 p. c. p. a. in a bank for some years and withdrew his interest every year. At the end of the period, he had received altogether 17400 rupees. For how many years had he deposited his money?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, P = 12000, R = 9 %, A = 17400\nA = P + I \n∴ I = 17400 – 12000 = 5400\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 5400 = (12000 × 9 × T)/100\n∴ T = 5400/1080 = 5\n∴ Javed deposited his money in the bank for 5 years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48382,806,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Lataben borrowed some money form a bank at a rate of 10 p. c. p. a. interest for 2.5 years to start a cottage industry. If she paid 10250 rupees as total interest, how much money had she borrowed?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, R = 10%, T = 2.5 years, I = 10250\n∴ I = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 10250 = (P × 10 × 2.5)/100\n∴ P = 10250 × 4 = 41000\n∴ Lataben borrowed ₹ 41000 from the bank.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48383,806,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If Interest (I) = 6000, Amount (A) = 18000, R = 5%, then find P and T?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, R = 5, I = 6000, A = 18000\nA = P + I \n∴ P = 18000 – 6000 = 12000\nI = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 6000 = (12000 × 5 × T)/100\n∴ T = 6000/600 = 10 years','',0,0,0,NULL),(48384,806,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','If Interest (I) = 2400, R = 2.5%, T = 5 years, then find P and A?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, R = 2.5 %, T = 5 years, I = 2400\nI = (P × R × T)/100\n∴ 2400 = (P × 2.5 × 5)/100\n∴ P = (2400 × 100)/12.5 = 19200\nA = P + I = 19200 + 2400 = 21600\n∴ A = ₹ 21600. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48385,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If arc AXB and arc AYB are the corresponding arcs and m(arc AXB) = 120⁰, then m(arc AYB) = ?\na) 140⁰		b) 60⁰		c) 240⁰		d) 160⁰\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','c) 240⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48386,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The value of π in fractional form is taken as -----\na) 22/7 		b) 23/7 		c) 20/7 		d) 40/7\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','a) 22/7 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48387,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ratio of circumference of a circle to its radius is -----\na) 2π 		b) 4π/3 		c) π			d) π/2\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','c) π		','',0,0,0,NULL),(48388,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If radius of a circle is 7 cm, then its circumference is -----\na) 22 cm 		b) 44 cm 		c) 21 cm 		d) 88 cm\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','b) 44 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48389,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The measure of a semicircular arc is ------\na) 0⁰			b) 90⁰		c) 360⁰		d) 180⁰\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','d) 180⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(48390,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the measure of minor arc is 135⁰ then find the measure of its corresponding major arc.\na) 135⁰		b) 235⁰		c) 45⁰		d) 225⁰\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','d) 225⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(48391,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A chord that passes through the center of a circle is -----\na) diameter 		b) segment 		c) radius 		d) center\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','a) diameter 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48392,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The number of arcs made by a chord on a circle is ------\na) 3 			b) 2 			c) 1 			d) 4\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','b) 2 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48393,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diameter of a circle passes through -----\na) The center 		b) The circumference 		c) Any where in the interior 		d) Always two points\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','a) The center 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48394,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The shortest path between any two points is called -----\na) line segment 		b) angle 		c) curve 		d) chord\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','d) chord','',0,0,0,NULL),(48395,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The relation between radius (r) and diameter (d) of a circle is -----\na) d = 2 × r		b) d = r/2 		c) d = r/3 		d) d = 3r\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','a) d = 2 × r	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48396,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The chord of a circle which passes through the center is ----- the radius.\na) thrice		b) double		c) half 		d) equal to\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','c) half 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48397,807,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The distance around the circle is known as ------\na) diameter 		b) radius 		c) circumference		d) chord\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------','','','c) circumference','',0,0,0,NULL),(48398,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the circumference of a circle with diameter 7 cm?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference = πd = 22/7 × 7 = 22 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48399,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of the longest chord of a circle is 10 cm. What is the length of its radius?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Longest chord of circle is the diameter of circle.\n∴ d = 10 cm\n∴ r = d/2 = 10/2 = 5 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48400,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the measure of a semicircle?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Measure of semicircle is 180⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(48401,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the ratio of the circumference of a circle to its diameter?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference/diameter = πd/d = π','',0,0,0,NULL),(48402,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The measure of a major arc is 270⁰. What is the measure of the corresponding minor arc?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Minor arc = 360 – 270 = 90⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(48403,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If r = 7 cm then find diameter and circumference of circle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference of circle = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 7 = 44 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48404,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a circle the measure of major arc is 230⁰, find the measure of its corresponding minor arc.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Minor arc = 360 – major arc = 360 – 230 = 130⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(48405,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If measure of central angle of a circle is 100⁰, then find the measure of corresponding minor arc.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Measure of minor arc = central angle = 100⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(48406,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If measure of central angle of circle is 70⁰, then find the measure of corresponding major arc.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Measure of major arc = 360 – minor arc \n           = 360 – 70 = 290⁰        [central angle = minor arc]\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48407,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the relation between radius and diameter of circle?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','radius of circle = diameter/2 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48408,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the measure of one complete circle?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Measure of complete circle = 360⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(48409,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If OA = 30 cm, then find the radius OB (in cm), where O is the center of circle.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------','','Radius OA = OB = 30 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(48410,807,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If OA = 12 cm, then find the length of PQ (in cm), where O is the center of circle.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','OA = 12 cm ; radius of circle\n∴ PQ is a diameter of circle\n∴ PQ = 2 × 12 = 24 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(48411,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If d = 28 cm then find radius and circumference of circle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference of circle = πd = 22/7 × 28 = 88 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48412,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If circumference of circle is 616 cm, then find radius and diameter.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference of circle = 616 cm\n∴ 2πr = 616\n∴  r = (616 × 7)/22 × 2 = 98 cm\n∴ d = 2r = 2 × 98 = 196 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48413,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If circumference of a circle is 176 cm. Find its radius.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference of circle = 616 cm\n∴ 2πr = 176\n∴  r = (176 × 7)/22 × 2 = 28 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48414,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The diameter of a circle is 14 cm. Find its circumference.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, d = 14 cm\nCircumference of circle = πd = 22/7 × 14 = 44 cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48415,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The radius of a circle is 35 cm. Find its circumference.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, r = 35 cm\n∴ circumference = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 35 = 220 cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48416,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The circumference of a circle is 198 cm. Find its radius and diameter.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference = 198\n∴ 2πr = 198\n∴ r = (198 × 7)/22 × 2 = 31.5 cm\n∴ d = 2r = 2 × 31.5 = 63 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48417,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If m(arc PQR) = 140⁰, find the measure of its corresponding arc PYR.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','m(arc PYR) = 360 – m(arc PQR)\n                           = 360 – 140 = 220⁰\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48418,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the circumference of a circle is 308 cm, find its diameter.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference = 308 cm\n∴ πd = 308\n∴ d = (308 × 7)/22 = 98 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48419,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diameter of circle is 5.6 cm. Find its circumference.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference = πd = 22/7 × 5.6 = 17.6 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48420,807,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, O is the center of the circle. i) Name the all chords of the circle	ii) Name all radii of the circle	iii) Name a chord, which is not the diameter of the circle','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) chord CP, chord AB\nii) OA, OB, OC, OP\niii) chord CP','',1,0,0,NULL),(48421,807,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If circumference of circle is 72.6 cm, then find radius and diameter of circle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference = 72.6 cm\n∴ 2πr = 72.6 \n∴ r = (72.6 × 7)/2 × 22 = 11.55 cm\n∴ d = 2r = 2 × 11.55 = 23.10 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48422,807,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The radius of a circular garden is 56 m. What is the cost of four layered fence around this garden at the rate of Rs. 40 per meter?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, r = 56 m\nWe know that, length of wire required to put 1 round of fence = circumference\n∴ c = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 56 = 352 m\nThus, length of wire required to put 4 round of fence = 4 × 352 = 1408 m\nHence, cost to put 4 round fence = length of wire × rate \n          = 1408 × 40 = ₹ 56320','',0,0,0,NULL),(48423,807,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The circumference of a circle is 62.80 cm. Taking π = 3.14, find its diameter.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference = 62.80 cm\n∴ πd = 62.80 \n∴ d = (62.80)/3.14 = 20\n∴ diameter = 20 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48424,807,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The radius of circular plot is 7.7 meters. How much will it cost to fence the plot with 3 rounds of wire at the rate of 50 rupees per meter?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference of circular plot = 2πr = 2 × 22/7 × 7.7 = 48.4\nLength of wire required for one round of fencing = 48.4 m.\nCost of one round of fence = length of wire × cost per meter\n            = 48.4 × 50 = 2420 rupees.\nCost of 3 rounds of fencing = 3 × 2420 = 7260 rupees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48425,807,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In a circle with center O, the measure of a minor arc is 110⁰, what is the measure of the major arc PYQ? \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Major arc PYQ = 360 – measure of minor arc = 360 – 110 = 250⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(48426,807,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If diameter of circle is 28 cm, then find its radius and circumference.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Here, d = 28 cm\n∴ r = d/2 = 28/2 = 14 cm\nCircumference of circle = πd = 22/7 × 28 = 22 × 4 = 88 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48427,807,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','If the circumference of circle is 176 cm, find its radius.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference of circle = 176 cm\n∴ 2πr = 176\n∴  r = (176 × 7)/(2 × 22) = 28 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48428,807,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, name the following : i) center of the circle 	ii) radii of the circle 		iii) diameters of the circle 	iv) chords of the circle	v) Two minor and major arcs','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) O be the center of circle\nii) Radii of circle AO, OC, OB, OE\niii) diameters of circle : AC, BE\niv) Chords of circle : AB, BD\nv) Minor arcs : CD, ED, AE, AB, BC\nmajor arcs : ABD, CEB','',1,0,0,NULL),(48429,807,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The diameter of the wheel of a bullock cart is 1.4 m. How many rotations will the wheel complete as the cart travels 1.1 km?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, d = 1.4 m\nWe have, c = πd\n∴ c = 22/7 × 1.4 = 22 × 0.2 = 4.4 m\nNow, the distance covered in one rotation of bullock cart = Circumference of its wheel = 4.4 m\nHence, No. of rotations = (Distance covered)/circumference\n         = (1.1 km)/4.4m = (1.1 × 1000)/4.4 = 250\nHence, the wheel of bullock cart completes 250 rotations to travel a distance of 1.1 km.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48430,807,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The radius of the wheel of a bus is 0.7 m. How many rotations will a wheel complete while travelling a distance of 22 km?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference = πd\n                                = 22/7 × 0.7 = 2.2 m\nWhen the wheel completes one rotation it crosses a distance of 2.2 m\nTotal no. of rotations = distance/circumference\n       = 22000/2.2 = 220000/22 = 10000\nA wheel completes 10000 rotations to cover the distance of 22 km.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48431,807,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Some arcs are shown in the circle with center ‘O’. Write the names of the minor arcs, major arcs and semicircular arcs from among them.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) Minor arcs : arc PXQ, arc PR, arc RY, arc QY, arc PX, arc QX\nii) Major arcs : are PQR, arc XQR, arc XRQ, arc QRP, arc PQX\niii) Semicircular arcs : arc QPR, arc QYR','',1,0,0,NULL),(48432,807,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diameter of a circular garden is 175 m, find the cost of 5 round of fence around it at the rate of Rs. 12 per meter?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, d= 175 m, \n∴ c = πd = 22/7 × 175 = 550 m\n∴ Cost of 5 rounds = 5 × 12 × 550 = 60 × 550 = ₹ 33000','',0,0,0,NULL),(48433,807,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A wheel of bus has diameter 42 cm. Find the distance covered by the bus in 2500 rotations of the wheel.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, d = 42 cm\nCircumference = πd = 22/7 × 42 = 132 cm\n∴ Distance covered = No. of rotations × diameter = 2500 × 132 = 330,000 cm\n∴ Distance = 330,000/1000 × 100 = 3.3 km','',0,0,0,NULL),(48434,807,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the circumference of a circle is 251.2, find the diameter of the circle. (π = 3.14)\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Circumference of circle = 251.2\n∴ c = πd\n∴ d = 251.2/3.14 = 80','',0,0,0,NULL),(48435,807,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The diameter of the wheel of a cart is 1.05 m. How much distance will the cart cover in 1000 rotations of the wheel?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, d = 1.05 m\nCircumference = πd = 22/7 × 1.05 = 3.3 m\n∴ Distance covered = No. of rotations × diameter = 1000 × 3.3 = 3300 m\n∴ Distance = 3300/1000  = 3.3 km','',0,0,0,NULL),(48436,807,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given circle, find the following : i) three radii 		ii) three chords	iii) a diameter 	iv) a triangle that has the center of the circle as vertex.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n           -----------------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) Radii of circle : OA, OB, OC\nii) Chords : AC, CB, AB\niii) diameter : AB\niv) In ΔAOC and ΔOCB; O is the center of circle.','',1,0,0,NULL),(48437,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the length and breadth of a rectangle both are doubled, its perimeter ----\na) remains the same 	b) become double 		c) becomes triple 		d) becomes 4 times\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','b) become double 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48438,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The sum of all sides of a closed figure is its -----\na) perimeter 		b) Area 		c) surface area 		d) volume\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','a) perimeter 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48439,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The perimeter of a square whose side is of length 8 cm is -----\na) 16 cm 		b) 64 cm 		c) 32 cm 		d) 24 cm\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','c) 32 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48440,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The total surface area of a cube whose side is 6 cm is ------\na) 216 cm²		b) 36 cm²		c) 256 cm²		d) 244 cm²\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','a) 216 cm²	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48441,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The total surface area of cuboid is given by ------\na) (lb + bh + lh) 		b) 3(lb + bh + lh) 		c) 2(lb + bh) 		d) 2(lb + bh + lh)\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','d) 2(lb + bh + lh)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48442,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the altitude of triangle whose base is 40 cm and area is 240 cm²?\na) 8 cm 		b) 12 cm 		c) 14 cm 		d) 10 cm\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','b) 12 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48443,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the cost of painting a wall with dimension 25 m by 9 m at the rate of Rs. 120 per square meter.\na) Rs. 25000		b) Rs. 24500			c) Rs. 29450 		d) Rs. 27000\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','d) Rs. 27000','',0,0,0,NULL),(48444,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the length and the width of a rectangle are each doubled, by what percent is the area increased?\na) 75% 		b) 100% 		c) 300% 		d) 400%\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','b) 100% 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48445,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of square of side 12 cm is -----\na) 144 cm 		b) 144 sq cm		c) 48 cm 		d) 48 sq cm\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','b) 144 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48446,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Each side of a square measures 5.6 cm. The perimeter of the square is -----\na) 23.5 cm 		b) 22 cm 		c) 22.4 cm 		d) 22.4 cm²\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','c) 22.4 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48447,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many cm² are there in 1 m²?\na) 100		b) 1000		c) 10		d) 10000\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','d) 10000','',0,0,0,NULL),(48448,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The distance around the closed figure is called as -----\na) volume 		b) area 		c) Perimeter 		d) none of these\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','c) Perimeter ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48449,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the perimeter of an isosceles triangle with equal sides 9 cm each and the third side 5 cm?\na) 40 cm 		b) 23 cm 		c) 22 cm 		d) 69 cm\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','b) 23 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48450,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area of square with perimeter 40 cm is -----\na) 1600 cm²		b) 100 cm²		c) 10 cm²		d) 250 cm²\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','b) 100 cm²	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48451,808,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The cost of fencing a 15 m × 7 m rectangular park at Rs. 45 per meter is -----\na) Rs. 4275 		b) Rs. 4725 		c) Rs. 1980 		d) Rs. 5427 \n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------------','','','b) Rs. 4725 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48452,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for perimeter of square.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Perimeter of square = 4 × side','',0,0,0,NULL),(48453,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for perimeter of rectangle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Perimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]','',0,0,0,NULL),(48454,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The perimeter of rectangle is 64 cm. If its length is 17 cm, what is its breadth?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Perimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]\n∴  64 = 2[17 + breadth]\n∴  breadth + 17 = 32\n∴ breadth = 32 – 17 = 15 cm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48455,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of square.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of square = (side)²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48456,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of rectangle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of rectangle = length × breadth','',0,0,0,NULL),(48457,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If the side of a square is 12 cm, find its area.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of square = (side)² = (12)² = 144 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48458,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If the length of a rectangle is 15 cm and breadth is 5 cm, find its area.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of rectangle = length × breadth = 15 × 5 = 75 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48459,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of right-angled triangle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of right angled triangle = 1/2 × Product of perpendicular sides','',0,0,0,NULL),(48460,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for area of triangle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height','',0,0,0,NULL),(48461,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for surface area of cuboid.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48462,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for surface area of cube.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Surface area of cube = 6l² = 6 × (side)²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48463,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 3 cm.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × 3² = 54 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48464,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 5 cm.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × 5² = 150 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48465,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 7.2 m.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × (7.2)² = 311.04 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48466,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 6.8 m.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × (6.8)² = 277.44 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48467,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cubes having sides is 5.5 m.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cube = 6 × (side)² = 6 × (5.5)² = 181.5 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48468,808,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the base of a triangle is 28 cm and height is 14 cm, find its area.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 28 × 14 = 196 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48469,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The perimeter of a rectangle of length 28 cm and breadth 20 cm is equal to the perimeter of a square. What is the length of the side of that square?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Perimeter of rectangle = 2[length + breadth]\n              = 2(28 + 20) = 96\nIf the side of that square is a then 4a = 96\n∴ 4a = 96\n ∴ a = 96/4 = 24\n∴ Side of square is 24 cm.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48470,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the length and breadth of a rectangle are doubled, how many times the perimeter of the old rectangle will that of the new rectangle be?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let the original length and breadth of the rectangle are x and y respectively\n∴ Perimeter of original rectangle = 2x + 2y\nNow when length and breadth are doubled\ni.e. 2x and 2y then\nPerimeter of new rectangle = 2(2x + 2y) = 2 × 2 (x + y)\n        = 2 × perimeter of original rectangle','',0,0,0,NULL),(48471,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the side of a square is tripled, how many times the perimeter of the first square will that of the new square be?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let the side of old square is x\n∴ Perimeter of old square = 4x\nNow the side of new square is 3x i.e. triple of old square\n∴ Perimeter of new square = 4 × 3x = 3 × 4x\n           = 3 × Perimeter of old square\nHence, the perimeter of new square is 3 times the perimeter of old square.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48472,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The area of a rectangle is 102 sq cm. If its length is 17 cm, what is its perimeter?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, area of rectangle = 102 sq cm, l = 17 cm\nWe have, Area of rectangle = l × b\n∴ 102 = 17 × b\n∴ b = 102/17 = 6 cm\nHence, perimeter of rectangle = 2l + 2b = 2(17) + 2(6) = 34 + 12 = 46 cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48473,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the side of a square is tripled, how many times will its area be as compared to the area of the original square?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let side of original square is x\n∴ Area of original square = (side)² = x²\nNow, when side is tripled\nSide of new square = 3x\n∴ Area of new square = (side)² = (3x)² = 9x² = 9 × area of original square\nThus, when side of a square is tripled its area will be 9 times the area of original square.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48474,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the sides that form the right angle of a triangle are 3.5 cm and 4.2 cm long, find the area of the triangle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of right angles triangle = 1/2 × product of perpendicular sides\n       = 1/2 × 3.5 × 4.2 = 7.35 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48475,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','If the base of the triangle is 5.6 cm and height is 4.5 cm, what is its area?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height = 1/2 × 5.6 × 4.5 = 12.6 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48476,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','A page of calendar is 45 cm long and 26 cm wide. What is its area?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The shape of page of a calendar is rectangular.\n∴ l = 45 cm, b = 26 cm\n∴ Area of page = Area of rectangle = l × b = 45 × 26 = 1170 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48477,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What is the area of a triangle with base 4.8 cm and height 3.6 cm?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of triangle = 1/2 × base × height \n                     = 1/2 × 4.8 × 3.6 = 8.64 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48478,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much sheet metal is required to make a closed rectangular box of length 1.5 m, breadth 1.2 m and height 1.3 m?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','A closed rectangular box is a cuboid shape.\n∴ Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(1.5 × 1.2 + 1.2 × 1.3 + 1.5 × 1.3) = 2(1.8 + 1.56 + 1.95)\n        = 2(5.31) = 10.62 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(48479,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cuboids of length, breadth and height as given : 12 cm, 10 cm, 5 cm\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(12 × 10 + 10 × 5 + 12 × 5) = 2(120 + 50 + 60)\n        = 2(230) = 460 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48480,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cuboids of length, breadth and height as given : 5 cm, 3.5 cm, 1.4 cm\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(5 × 3.5 + 3.5 × 1.4 + 1.4 × 5) = 2(17.5 + 4.9 + 7)\n        = 2(29.4) = 58.8 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48481,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cuboids of length, breadth and height as given : 2.5 m, 2 m, 2.4 m\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(2.5 × 2 + 2 × 2.4 + 2.4 × 2.5) = 2(5 + 4.8 + 6)\n        = 2(15.8) = 31.6 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(48482,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cuboids of length, breadth and height as given : 8 m, 5 m, 3.5 m\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n        = 2(8 × 5 + 3.5 × 5 + 8 × 3.5) = 2(40 + 17.5 + 28)\n        = 2(85.5) = 171 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(48483,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of right-angled triangle whose sides making right angles are 8 cm and 10 cm.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of right angled triangle = 1/2 × product of perpendicular sides\n               = 1/2 × 10 × 8 = 40 sq cm\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48484,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the length of cube is doubled, how many times its surface area becomes?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','If the length of side be x\nSurface area of cube = 6(side)² = 6x²\nNow, length of cube is double then new length = 2x\n∴ Area of new cube = 6 × (2x)² = 6 × 4 × x² \n               = 4 × 6x² = 4 × surface area of old cube','',0,0,0,NULL),(48485,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the area of rectangle is 102 sq cm and its breadth is 6 cm, find its perimeter.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of rectangle = 102\n∴ Area of rectangle = length × breadth \n∴ 102 = 6 × length \n∴ length = 17 cm\n∴ Perimeter of rectangle = 2[l + b]\n                 = 2[17 + 6] = 2 × 23 = 46 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48486,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the total surface area of cube whose side is 5.5 cm.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Total surface area of cube = 6l² = 6 × (5.5)² = 6 × 30.25 = 181.5 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48487,808,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of rectangular sheet 50 cm long and 25 cm wide.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of sheet = area of rectangle = length × breadth\n           = 50 ×  25 = 1250 cm²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48488,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Given alongside is the diagram of a playground. It shows the length of its sides. Find the perimeter of the playground.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','From figure, AF = GE = GD + DE \n∴ AF = BC + DE ---------- (BC = GD)\n∴ AF = 15 + 15 = 30 m and EF = AG = AB + BG \n∴ EF = 10 + 5 = 15 m\nNow, perimeter of playground = AB + BC + CD + DE + EF + AF \n           = 10 + 15 + 5 + 15 + 15 + 30 = 90 m\n∴ Perimeter of playground is 90 m.','',1,0,1,NULL),(48489,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The length and the width of a mobile phone are 13 cm and 7 cm respectively. It has a screen PQRS as shown in the figure. What is the area of screen?','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','ABCD is the rectangular formed by the edges of the mobile. PQRS is the rectangle formed by leaving a 1.5 cm wide edge alongside AB, BC and DC and a 2 cm edge alongside DA.\nLength of rectangle PQRS = 9.5 cm and breadth = 4 cm\nArea of screen = Area of rectangle PQRS = 9.5 × 4 = 38 sq m','',1,0,0,NULL),(48490,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is the value of rectangular plot of land 75.5 m long and 30.5 m broad at the rate of 1000 rupees per square meter?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, l = 75.5 m, b = 30.5 m\n∴ Area of plot = l × b = 75.5 × 30.5 = 2302.75 sq m\nAs the rate of plot is ₹ 1000 per sq m\nCost of the plot = Area × rate = 2302.75 × 1000 = ₹ 2302750\nThus, the value of plot is ₹ 2302750','',0,0,0,NULL),(48491,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Find the perimeter and area of garden with measures as shown in the figure alongside.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','Name the figure of garden as shown','Now, Perimeter of garden = sum of lengths of all sides\n        = AB + BC + CD + DE + EF + FG + GH + HI + IJ + JK + KL + AL \n        = 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13 + 13\n         = 156 m\nAnd from figure, \nArea of the garden = 5 × area of square with side 13 \n          = 5 × (13)² = 5 × 169 = 845 sq m\nThus, perimeter of garden is 156 m and its area is 845 sq m ',1,0,1,NULL),(48492,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','One side of a cubic box is 0.4 m. How much will it cost to paint the outer surface of the box at the rate of 50 rupees per sqm?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','side = l = 0.4 m\nTotal surface area of a cube = 6 l² = 6 × (0.4)² = 6 × 0.16 = 0.96 sq m\nCost of painting 1 sq m is 50 rupees.\n∴ Cost of painting 0.96 sq m will be = 0.96 × 50 = 48 rupees\nIt will cost 48 rupees to paint the outer surface of the box. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48493,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A matchbox is 4 cm long, 2.5 cm broad and 1.5 cm is height. Its outer sides are to be covered exactly with craft paper. How much paper will be required to do so?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, l = 4 cm, b = 2.5 cm, h = 1.5 cm\nTotal surface area of cuboid = 2(lb + bh + lh)\n         = 2(4 × 2.5 + 2.5 × 1.5 + 4 × 1.5) \n          2 [10 + 3.75 + 6] = 2 × 19.75 = 39.50 sq cm\nThus, the area of paper needed to cover the match box is 39.5 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48494,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The measurement of bricks is 10 cm long, 5 cm wide and 4 cm thick, find its total surface area.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, l = 10 cm, b = 5 cm, h = 4 cm\nTotal surface area of brick = total surface area of cuboid \n        = 2[lb + bh + lh] = 2[10 × 5 + 5 × 4 + 10 × 4]\n        = 2[50 + 20 + 40] = 2 × 110 = 220 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48495,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A rectangular plot is 40 m long and 30 m wide. Find its cost at the rate of Rs. 150 per sq m.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Area of rectangular plot = length × breadth = 40 × 30 = 1200 sq m\nRate of plot ₹ 150 per sq cm\n∴ Cost of plot = 150 × 1200 = ₹ 180000.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48496,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the area of shaded region.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) Area of inner rectangular = length × breadth \n             = 18 × 9 = 162 sq m \nii) Here, length of outer rectangle = 18 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 18 + 3 = 21 m\nbreadth = 9 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 9 + 3 = 12 m\n∴ Area of outer rectangle = 21 × 12 = 252 sq m\n∴ Area of shaded region = Area of outer rectangle – Area of inner rectangle \n          = 252 – 162 = 90 sq m','',1,0,0,NULL),(48497,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A rectangular field, 90 m long and 70 m wide has a 3 m wide path all around its inside. Find the area of the path and the cost of its construction at the rate of Rs. 20 per 10 m²?','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) Area of inner rectangle = length × breadth\n               = HG × FG             [HG = 90 – 3 – 3 = 84 and FG = 70 – 3 – 3 = 64]\n               = 84 × 64 = 5376 sq m\nii) Area of outer rectangle = length × breadth = DC × AD = 90 × 70 = 6300 sq m\niii) Area of path = Area of outer rectangle – Area of inner rectangle\n                = 6300 – 5376 = 924 sq m\niv) Cost of construction of path ₹ 20 per 10 sq m \n∴ Cost of construction of path = 20 × 924/10 = 20 × 92.4 = ₹ 1848','',1,0,0,NULL),(48498,808,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the edge of cube is 8 cm long, find its total surface area.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Total surface area of cube = 6l² = 6 × 8² = 64 × 6 = 384 sq cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48499,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','As shown is the figure, four napkins all of the same size were made from a square piece of cloth of length 1 m. What length of lace will be required to trim all four sides of all the napkins?','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','The napkins are made as shown in figure from a cloth.\nThus, length of each square shaped napkin = 1/2 m\n∴ Perimeter of each napkin = 4 × 1/2 = 2 m\nHence, perimeter of all 4 napkins = 4 × 2 = 8 m\nSince the lace required to trim all four sides of each napkin is equal to its perimeter, the length of lace required is 8 m.','',1,0,0,NULL),(48500,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A rectangular garden is 40 m long and 30 m wide. A two-meter wide path is to be paved inside the garden along its boundary, using tiles 25 cm × 20 cm in size. How many such tiles will be required?','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','Let us find the area to be paved\nArea of garden = 40 × 30 = 1200 sq m\nArea not to be paved = 36 × 26 = 936 sq m\n∴ Area to be paved = 1200 – 936 = 264 sqm\nArea of each tile = (25/100) × (20/100) × 1/20\n             = 1/20 sqm\nHence, let us find the number of tiles required to tile 264 sqm\n∴ No. of tiles = Total area/Area of one paver\n             = 264 ÷ 1/20 = 264 × 20 = 5280\n∴ 5280 tiles will be required.','',1,0,0,NULL),(48501,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A rectangular playground is 65 m long and 30 m wide. A pathway of 1.5 m width goes all around the ground, outside it. Find the area of the pathway.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','The playground is rectangular','▭ABCD is the playground. Around it is a pathway 1.5 m wide.\nAround ▭ABCD we get the rectangle ▭PQRS\nLength of new rectangle PQRS = 65 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 68 m\nBreadth = 30 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 33 m\nArea of path = Area of rectangle PQRS – Area of rectangle ABCD \n        = 68 × 33 – 65 × 30 = 2244 – 1950 = 294 sqm',0,0,1,NULL),(48502,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A rectangular hall is 12 m long and 6 m broad. Its flooring is to be made of square tiles of side 30 cm. How many tiles will fit in the entire hall? How many would be required if tiles of side 15 cm were used?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Length of hall, l = 12 m = 1200 cm, b= 6 m = 600 cm\n∴ Area of the flooring of hall = l × b = 1200 × 600 = 720000 sq cm\ni) If tiles of square shape with side 30 cm are used then\nArea of tile = (side)² = 30² = 900 sq cm\n∴ No. of tiles required = Area of flooring/Area of a tile\n        = 720000/900 = 800 tiles.\nii) If tile of side 15 cm is used then Area of a tile = (side)² = (15)² = 225 sq cm\n∴ No. of tiles used = Area of flooring/Area of tile = 720000/225\n            = 3200 \nHence, if tile of 30 cm side is used, 800 tiles are required and if tile of 15 cm side are used 3200 tiles are required for the flooring of the hall.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48503,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','An open box of length 1.5 m, breadth 1 m and height 1 m is to be made for use a trolley for carrying garden waste. How much sheet metal will be required to make the box? The inside and outside surface of the box is to be painted with rust proof paint. At a rate of 150 rupees per sq m, how much will it cost to paint the box?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, l = 1.5 m, b = 1 m, h = 1 m\nSheet metal used to make the open box = (lb + 2bh + 2lh)\n     = (1.5 × 1) + 2(1 × 1) + 2(1.5 × 1) = (1.5 + 2 + 3) = 6.5 sq m\nThe cost of painting the box from both sides is = 2(Area of cuboid) × Rate\n      = 2 × (6.5) × 150 = 13 × 150 = ₹ 1950\nHence, 6.5 sq m metal sheet is required to make the box and it costs ₹ 1950 to paint it.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48504,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The outer sides of the box which is 12 cm long, 8 cm broad and 6 cm high to be covered by a colour paper, find the area of paper needed.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, l = 12 cm, b = 8 cm, h = 6 cm\nTotal surface area of box = Total surface area of cuboid\n     = 1/2[lb + bh + lh] = 1/2[12 × 8 + 8 × 6 + 12 × 60]\n     = 1/2[96 + 48 + 720] = 1/2 [864] = 432 cm²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48505,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A 3 m wide path runs outside and around a rectangular park of length 125 m and breadth 65 m. Find the area if the path.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Area of outer side of rectangle = length × breadth \n           [length = 125 + 3 + 3 = 131 ; breadth = 65 + 3 + 3 = 71]\n∴ Area of outer side of rectangle = 131 × 71 = 9301 sq m\nii) Area of inner side rectangle = length × breadth \n          = 125 × 65 = 8125 sq m\niii) Area of path = Area of outer side rectangle – Area of inner side rectangle\n          = 9301 – 8125 = 1176 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(48506,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the length and breadth of a rectangle are in the ratio 13 : 8 and its perimeter is 420 m, then find the area of the rectangle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given that, ratio of length and breadth of rectangle is 13 : 8.\n∴ length = 13x and breadth = 8x\n∴ Perimeter of rectangle = 2[l + b]\n∴ 420 = 13x + 8x \n∴ 21x = 210\n∴ x = 210/21 = 10  \n∴ length = 13x = 13 × 10 = 130 m and breadth = 8x = 8 × 10 = 80 m\n∴ Area of rectangle = length × breadth = 130 × 80 = 10400 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(48507,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the ratio between the length and the perimeter of a rectangular plot is 1 : 3, then find the ratio between the length and breadth of the plot.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Ratio of length and the perimeter of rectangular plot is 1 : 3.\n∴ length = 1x and perimeter = 3x \n∴ Perimeter = 2[l + b]\n∴ 3x = 2[x + breadth]\n∴ 3x – 2x = 2 × breadth\n∴ breadth = x/2\n∴ Ratio of length and breadth = x/(x/2) = 2/1 = 2 : 1','',0,0,0,NULL),(48508,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The length of a rectangular plot of land is twice its breadth. If the perimeter of the plot be 420 m, find its area. \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let breadth of rectangle = x then length of rectangle = 2x\n∴ Perimeter of rectangle = 2[l + b]\n∴ 420 = 2[2x + x]\n∴ 210 = 3x\n∴ x = 70\n∴ length = 2x = 2 × 70 = 140 m and breadth = x = 70 m\n∴ Area of rectangle = length × breadth = 140 × 70 = 9800 sq m','',0,0,0,NULL),(48509,808,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The area of the rectangular garden of length 40 m is 1000 m². Find i) The breadth of the garden 		ii) The perimeter of the garden. The garden is to be enclosed by 3 rounds of fencing, leaving an entrance of 4 m. Find the cost of fencing the garden at a rate of Rs. 250 per meter. \n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Area of rectangle = l × b\n∴ 1000 = 40 × b\n∴ b = 25 m\nii) The perimeter of the rectangular garden = 2l + 2b \n        = 2 × 40 + 2 × 25 = 80 + 50 = 130 m\nThe perimeter for fencing the garden = (130 – 4) = 126 m\n     ------- (The entrance is 4 m wide)\nThree rounds of fencing = 3 × 126 = 378 m\nCost of fencing = rate × length of fencing = 250 × 378\n        = ₹ 94500','',0,0,0,NULL),(48510,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find a Pythagoras triplet among the following\na) 20, 22, 30 		b) 12, 13, 25 		c) 6, 8, 10 		d) 25, 50, 75\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------\n','','','c) 6, 8, 10 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48511,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the missing number in the Pythagorean triplet (28, ---, 53)\na) 45 			b) 45			c) 43 			d) 41\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','a) 45 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48512,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If sides making right angles of a right- angled triangle are 8 cm and 15 cm. Find its hypotenuse.\na) 16 cm 		b) 17 cm 		c) 19 cm 		d) 21 cm\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','b) 17 cm 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48513,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is -----\na) side opposite to shortest angle		b) side opposite to right angle 		\nc) any side of the triangle		        d) one of the sides making right angle\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','b) side opposite to right angle 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48514,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the Pythagorean triplet (30, 40, ----)\na) 45 		b) 55 		c) 52 		d) 50\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','d) 50','',0,0,0,NULL),(48515,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a ΔABC, if ∠A = ∠B + ∠C, then find ∠A.\na) 60⁰		b) 45⁰		c) 90⁰		c) 120⁰\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','c) 90⁰','',0,0,0,NULL),(48516,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A man goes 24 m due east and then 10 m due north. How far is he away from his starting position?\na) 18 m		b) 26 m		c) 24 m		d) 28 m\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','b) 26 m	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48517,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a right isosceles triangle, the measure of one of the angles other than the right angle is ----\na) 90⁰		b) 60⁰  		c) 45⁰		d) can’t be determined\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','c) 45⁰	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48518,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If 6, x, 10 is a Pythagorean triplet, then value of x is ----\na) 2 		b) 4		c) 8 		d) 12\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','c) 8 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48519,809,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a triangle, one angle is of 90⁰. Then in the given statements which are true?\ni) The other two angles are of 45⁰ each 		ii) In remaining two angles, one angle is 90⁰ and other is 45⁰		iii) Reaming two angles are complementary\na) (i) only 		b) (ii) only 		c) (iii) only 		d) (i) and (ii)\n\nAns. : ----------------------------------------------','','','c) (iii) only 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48520,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','In the figure given below, find the value of x.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','In ΔLMN, ∠LMN = 90⁰\nLN² = LM² + MN²  ----------- (by Pythagoras theorem)\n∴ x² = 7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625\n∴ x = 25','',1,0,0,NULL),(48521,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','In the figure given below, find the value of x.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','In ΔPQR, ∠PQR = 90⁰\n∴ By Pythagoras theorem, PR² = PQ² + QR²\n∴ QR² = PR² - PQ² \n∴ x² = 41² - 9² = 1681 – 81 = 1600\n∴ x = 40   ','',1,0,0,NULL),(48522,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 7, 24, 25\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','7² = 49, 24² = 576, 25² = 625\n∴ 49 + 576 = 625\n∴ 7² + 24² = 25²\n∴ 7, 24 and 25 is Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48523,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 3, 4, 5\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','3² = 9, 4² = 16, 5² = 25\n∴ 9 + 16 = 25\n∴ 3² + 4² = 5²\n∴ 3, 4 and 5 is Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48524,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 2, 4, 5\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','2² = 4, 4² = 16, 5² = 25\n∴ 4 + 16 ≠ 25\n∴ 2² + 4² ≠ 5²\n∴ 2, 4 and 5 is not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48525,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 4, 5, 6\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','4² = 16, 5² = 25, 6² = 36\n∴ 16 + 24 ≠ 36\n∴ 4² + 5² ≠ 6²\n∴ 4, 5 and 6 is not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48526,809,'descriptive','1b',2,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 2, 6, 7\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','2² = 4, 6² = 36, 7² = 49\n∴ 4 + 36 ≠ 49\n∴ 2² + 6² ≠ 7²\n∴ 2, 6 and 7 is not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48527,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 9, 40, 41\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','9² = 81, 40² = 1600, 41² = 1681\n∴ 81 + 1600 = 1681\n∴ 9² + 40² = 41²\n∴ 9, 40 and 41 is Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48528,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Do the following numbers form a Pythagorean triplet : 4, 7, 8\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','4² = 16, 7² = 49, 8² = 64\n∴ 16 + 49 ≠ 64\n∴ 4² + 7² ≠ 8²\n∴ 4, 7 and 8 is not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48529,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle whose base is 12 cm and height is 5 cm?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','We know that, Pythagoras theorem,\n(Hypo)² = (base)² + (height)² = 12² + 5² = 144 + 25 = 169\n∴ Hypotenuse = 13 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48530,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ΔPQR, seg PR is the hypotenuse. Which angle of ΔPQR is a right angle?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In ΔPQR, seg PR is the Hypotenuse.\n∴ ∠Q = 90⁰\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48531,809,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ΔABC, ∠A = 90⁰. Name the sides forming the right angle.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In ΔABC, ∠A = 90⁰\n∴ side AC and AB are the sides forming the right angle. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48532,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In ΔABC, ∠C = 90⁰, l(AC) = 5 cm and l(BC) = 12 cm. What is the length of seg AB?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In ΔABC, ∠C = 90⁰\n∴ seg AB is the Hypotenuse\n∴ (AB)² = AC² + BC² = 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169\n∴ AB = 13 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48533,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','In the figure given below, find the value of x.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','In ΔEDF, ∠EDF = 90⁰\nBy using Pythagoras theorem, EF² = ED² + DF²\n∴ ED² = EF² - DF²\n∴ x² = 17² - 8² = 289 – 64 = 225\n∴ x = 15 unit','',1,0,0,NULL),(48534,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 8, 15, 17\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, 17² = 289\n8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289 \n∴ 17² = 8² + 15² \n∴ sides of the triangle are in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48535,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 11, 12, 15\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, 15² = 225\n11² + 12² = 121 + 144 = 265 \n∴ 15² ≠ 11² + 12² \n∴ sides of the triangle are not in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48536,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 11, 60, 61\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, 61² = 3721\n11² + 60² = 121 + 3600 = 3721 \n∴ 61² = 11² + 60² \n∴ sides of the triangle are in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48537,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 1.5, 1.6, 1.7\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, 1.7² = 2.89\n1.6² + 1.5² = 2.56 + 2.25 = 4.81 \n∴ 1.7² ≠ 1.6² + 1.5² \n∴ sides of the triangle are not in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48538,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The sides of triangles are given. Find out which are right-angled triangle or not : 40, 20, 30\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, 40² = 1600\n20² + 30² = 400 + 900 = 1300 \n∴ 40² ≠ 20² + 30² \n∴ sides of the triangle are not in Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48539,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the remaining side of right-angled ΔLMN.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','In ΔLMN, ∠LMN = 90⁰\nBy using Pythagoras theorem,\nLN² = LM² + MN²\n∴ LM² = LN² - MN² = 37² - 35² = 1369 – 1225 = 144\n∴ LM = 12 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(48540,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the length of wire tied to a window on a wall at height 8 m and which is tied to the ground at 15 m from the wall.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Window is perpendicular to ground.\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(Lenth of wire)² = 8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289\n∴ Length of wire = 17 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(48541,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','PQR is a triangle, right-angled at P. If PQ = 10 cm and PR = 24 cm, find QR.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In ΔPQR, ∠P = 90⁰\n∴ seg QR is hypotenuse\n∴ QR² = PQ² + PR² = 10² + 24² = 100 + 576 = 676 \n∴ QR = 26 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48542,809,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','ABC is a triangle, right-angled at C. If AB = 25 cm and AC = 7 cm, find BC.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In ΔABC, ∠C = 90⁰\n∴ seg AB is a hypotenuse.\n∴ AB² = AC² + BC²\n∴ BC² = AB² - AC² = 25² - 7² = 625 – 49 = 576 \n∴ BC = 24 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48543,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In the right-angled ΔPQR, ∠P = 90⁰, if l(PQ) = 24 cm and l(PR) = 10 cm, find the length of seg QR.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In ΔPQR, ∠P = 90⁰\n∴ Seg QR is a hypotenuse\n∴ QR² = PQ² + PR² = 24² + 10² = 576 + 100 = 676\n∴ QR = 26 cm ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48544,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','In the right-angled ΔLMN, ∠M = 90⁰. If l(LM) = 12 cm and l(LN) = 20 cm, find the length of seg MN.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In ΔLMN, ∠M = 90⁰\n∴ Seg LN is hypotenuse\n∴ By Pythagoras theorem, LN² = LM² + MN²\n∴ MN² = LN² - LM² = 20² - 12² = 400 – 144 = 256\n∴ MN = 16 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48545,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the sides of triangle are 14, 48 and 50. Check whether it is a right-angled triangle or not.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Highest length = 50\n∴ 50² = 2500\n14² + 48² = 196 + 2304 = 2500\n∴ 50² = 14² + 48²\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, given sides formed right angled triangle.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48546,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Verify whether the numbers 35, 65, 100 are in Pythagorean triplet.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Highest length = 100\n∴ 100² = 10000\n35² + 65² = 1225 + 3025 = 4250\n∴ 100² ≠ 35² + 65²\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, given sides 35, 65 and 100 are not Pythagoras triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48547,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the value of y.\n','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','In ΔXYZ, ∠Y = 90⁰\nBy using Pythagoras theorem, XZ² = XY² + YZ²\n ∴ 45² = 27² + y²\n∴ y² = 45² - 27² = 2025 – 729 = 1296\n∴ y = 36','',1,0,0,NULL),(48548,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ΔLMN, ∠M = 90⁰, l(LM) = 9 cm and l(MN) = 12 cm, find the length of LN.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In ΔLMN, ∠M = 90⁰\n∴ Seg LN is a hypotenuse.\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, LN² = LM² + MN²\n∴ LN² = 9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225\n∴ LN = 15 cm','',0,0,0,NULL),(48549,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A 15 m long ladder reached a window 12 m high from the ground on placing it against a wall at a distance ‘a’. Find the distance of the foot of the ladder from the wall.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Let, length of ladder = Hypotenuse = 15 m\nHeight of window = 12 m and distance between wall and ladder = a m\n∴ Wall is perpendicular to ground\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n∴ 15² = 12² + a²\n∴ a² = 225 – 144 = 81\n∴ a = 9 m','',0,0,0,NULL),(48550,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A tree is broken at a height of 8 m from the ground and its top touches the ground at a distance of 15 m from the base of the tree. Find the original height of the tree.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In figure, let AB be the height of tree, BC be the distance between tree top and tree. Tree is broken at point A’ ','Here,  A’B = 8 m, BC = 15 m and AA’ = A’C \nAB ꓕ BC \n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(A’C)² = (A’B)² + (BC)² = 8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289\n∴ A’C = 17 m\n∴ AA’ = A’C = 17 m\n∴ Original height of tree = AA’ + A’B = 8 + 17 = 23 m',0,0,1,NULL),(48551,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','A ladder is placed in such a way that its foot is 5 m away from the wall and its top reaches a window 12 m above the ground. Find the length of the ladder.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Distance between ladder and wall = 5 m and height of window from ground is 12 m\nWall of window is perpendicular to ground.\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem,\nLength of ladder = Hypotenuse\n∴ (Hypo.)² = 12² + 5² = 144 + 25 = 169\n∴ Hypo. = 13 m\n∴ Length of ladder is 13 m. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48552,809,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the value of x.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','In ΔADB, ∠ADB = 90⁰\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \nAB² = AD² + BD²\n∴ BD² = AB² - AD² = (6.5)² - 6² = 42.25 – 36 = 6.25\n∴ BD = 2.5 cm\nIn ΔADC, ∠ADC = 90⁰\n∴ AC² = AD² + DC²\n∴ DC² = AC² - AD² = 10² - 6² = 100 – 36 = 64 \n∴ DC = 8 cm\n∴ BC = x = BD + DC = 2.5 + 8 = 10.5 cm ','',1,0,0,NULL),(48553,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The top of a ladder of length of 15 m reaches a window 9 m above the ground. What is the distance between the base of the wall and that of the ladder?\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In figure, Let AC is the ladder','AB is the wall, the window is at point A, such that AC = 15 m, AB = 9 m\n∠B = 90⁰        ∴ AC is the hypotenuse\n∴ By Pythagoras theorem,\nAC² = AB² + BC² \n∴ BC² = AC² - AB² = 15² - 9² = 225 – 81 = 144\n∴ BC = 12 m\nThus, the distance between the base of the wall and foot of ladder is 12 m.',0,0,1,NULL),(48554,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','From the given figure, find the length of AC and the perimeter of ΔABC.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','In ΔABC, ∠ABC = 90⁰\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, AC² = AB² + BC²\n∴ AC² = 20² + 21² = 400 + 441 = 841\n∴ AC = 29 m\n∴ Perimeter of ΔABC = AB + BC + AC = 20 + 21 + 29 = 70 m','',1,0,0,NULL),(48555,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the numbers (13, 84, x) are in Pythagorean triplet, where x is largest number among them, find x.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','From given information, x is the longest side\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n∴ x² = 13² + 84² = 169 + 7056 = 7225\n∴ x = 85 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48556,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Check whether (3.9, 1.5, 3.6) is a Pythagorean triplet or not.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, (3.9)² = 15.21 \n(1.5)² + (3.6)² = 2.25 + 12.96 = 15.21 \n∴ (3.9)² = (1.5)² + (3.6)²\n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, given numbers are 3.9, 1.5, 3.6 are Pythagorean triplet.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48557,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any four Pythagorean triplet.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Pythagorean triplets are : (3, 4, 5), (13, 84, 85), (20, 21, 29), (15, 9, 12) etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(48558,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A tree is broken at a height of 5 m from the ground and its top touches the ground at a distance of 12 m from the base of the tree. Find the original height of the tree.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In figure, let AB be the height of tree, BC be the distance between tree top and tree. Tree is broken at point A’ ','Here,  A’B = 5 m, BC = 12 m and AA’ = A’C \nAB ꓕ BC \n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(A’C)² = (A’B)² + (BC)² = 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169\n∴ A’C = 13 m\n∴ AA’ = A’C = 13 m\n∴ Original height of tree = AA’ + A’B = 5 + 13 = 18 m',0,0,1,NULL),(48559,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the given figure, find x.','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','In ΔACD, ∠ACD = 90⁰\n∴ By using Pythagoras triplet, AD² = AC² + CD²\n∴ AC² = AD² - CD² = 25² - 7² = 625 – 49 = 576\n∴ AC = 24 cm\nIn ΔACB, ∠ACB = 90⁰\nAB² = BC² + AC²\n∴ BC² = AB² - AC²\n∴ x² = 30² - 24² = 900 – 576 = 324\n∴ x = 18 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(48560,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A tree is broken at a height of 6 m from the ground and its top touches the ground at a distance of 8 m from the base of the tree. Find the original height of the tree.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In figure, let AB be the height of tree, BC be the distance between tree top and tree. Tree is broken at point A’ ','Here,  A’B = 6 m, BC = 8 m and AA’ = A’C \nAB ꓕ BC \n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(A’C)² = (A’B)² + (BC)² = 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100\n∴ A’C = 10 m\n∴ AA’ = A’C = 10 m\n∴ Original height of tree = AA’ + A’B = 6 + 10 = 16 m',0,0,1,NULL),(48561,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','A tree is broken at a height of 15 m from the ground and its top touches the ground at a distance of 20 m from the base of tree. If the original height of tree is k m, then find k.\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','','In figure, let AB be the height of tree, BC be the distance between tree top and tree. Tree is broken at point A’ ','Here,  A’B = 15 m, BC = 20 m and AA’ = A’C \nAB ꓕ BC \n∴ By using Pythagoras theorem, \n(A’C)² = (A’B)² + (BC)² = 15² + 20² = 225 + 400 = 625\n∴ A’C = 25 m\n∴ AA’ = A’C = 25 m\n∴ Original height of tree = AA’ + A’B = 15 + 25 = 40 m',0,0,1,NULL),(48562,809,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the perimeter of rectangle?','\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -----------------------------------------------------------------------','','▭ABCD is a rectangle\n∴ ∠B = 90⁰\nIn ΔABC,\nBy using Pythagoras theorem, AC² = AB² + BC²\n∴ AB² = AC² - BC² = 26² - 10² = 676 – 100 = 576\n∴ AB = 24 cm\n∴ Length of rectangle = 24 cm and breadth = 10 cm\n∴ Perimeter of rectangle = 2(length + breadth) \n             = 2(24 + 10) = 2 × 34 = 68 cm','',1,0,0,NULL),(48563,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Of which of the binomials given below is m²n² + 14mnpq + 49p²q² the expansion?\na) (m + n) (p + q) 		b) (mn – pq)    	c) (7mn + pq)	             d) (mn + 7pq)\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------\n','','','d) (mn + 7pq)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48564,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Which of the options given below is the square of the binomial ','\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','c) 64 – (16/x) + 1/x²','',1,0,0,NULL),(48565,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(a + b)² = -------\na) a² + b²		b) a² + 2ab + b²		c) a² - 2ab + b²		d) a² - b²\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','','b) a² + 2ab + b²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48566,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a wrong sum.\na) (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²		b) (a – b)² = a² - 2ab + b²		\nc) (a + b) (a – b) = a² + b²		d) none of these\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','','c) (a + b) (a – b) = a² + b²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48567,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The factors of 2x² - 128y²\na) (x – 8y) (x + 8y) 		b) 2(x – 8y) (x + 8y) 	\nc) (2x – 8y) (2x + 8y) 	d) (2x – 4y) (2x + 4y)\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','','b) 2(x – 8y) (x + 8y) 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48568,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If ab = 6 and a + b = 5 then a² + b² = -----\na) 11		b) 12 		c) 13 		d) 16\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','','c) 13 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48569,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a + b = 11 and ab = 30 then a² + b² = -----\na) 61 		b) 21 		c) 24 		d) 81\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','','a) 61 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48570,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the value of (10.2 × 9.8) = -----\na) 98.96 		b) 99.6 		c) 99.92 		d) 99.96\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','','d) 99.96','',0,0,0,NULL),(48571,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Evaluate :','\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','c) 12','',1,0,0,NULL),(48572,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the number of terms in product of (2x + 3y) and (4x + 5y)\na) 5 			b) 2 			c) 3 			d) 4\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','','c) 3 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48573,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x – y = 5, xy = 24 then the value of x² + y² will be -----\na) 23 			b) 73 			c) 65 			d) 74\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','','b) 73 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48574,810,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If x + y = 8, xy = 24 then the value of x² + y² will be ------\na) 32 			b) 16 			c) 36 			d) 38\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------','','','b) 16 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48575,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(a + b)² = -------\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48576,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','(a – b)² = ------\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(a - b)² = a² - 2ab + b²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48577,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','a² - b² = -------\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a² - b² = (a – b) (a + b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48578,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If a = 5, b = 3 then verify the formula, (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²	\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, a = 5, b = 3\nLHS = (a + b)² = (5 + 3)² = 8² = 64\nRHS = a² + 2ab + b² = 5² + 2 × 5 × 3 + 3² = 25 + 30 + 9 = 64\n∴ LHS = RHS ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48579,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If a = 11, b = 3, then verify the formula, (a – b)² = a² - 2ab + b²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, a = 11, b = 3\nLHS = (a - b)² = (11 - 3)² = 8² = 64\nRHS = a² - 2ab + b² = 11² - 2 × 11 × 3 + 3² = 121 - 66 + 9 = 64\n∴ LHS = RHS 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48580,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (2x + 3y)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(2x + 3y)² = (2x)² + 2(2x)(3y) + (3y)² = 4x² + 12xy + 9y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48581,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (5x – 4)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(5x – 4)² = (5x)² - 2 × 5x × 4 + 4² = 25x² - 40x + 16','',0,0,0,NULL),(48582,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (51)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(51)² = (50 + 1)² = (50)² + 2 × 50 × 1 + 1² = 2500 + 100 + 1 = 2601','',0,0,0,NULL),(48583,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (98)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(98)² = (100 – 2)² = (100)² - 2 × 100 × 2 + 2² = 10000 – 400 + 4 = 9604','',0,0,0,NULL),(48584,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : (3x + 4y) (3x – 4y)\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(3x + 4y) (3x – 4y) = (3x)² - (4y)² = 9x² - 16y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48585,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : (x + y) (x – y)\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(x + y)(x – y) = x² - y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48586,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : (3x – 5) (3x + 5)\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(3x – 5)(3x + 5) = (3x)² - 5² = 9x² - 25 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48587,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Solve : (a + 6) (a – 6)\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(a + 6)(a - 6) = a² - 6² = a² - 36 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48588,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 201a³b²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','201a³b² = 3 × 67 × a × a × a × b × b','',0,0,0,NULL),(48589,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 91xyt²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','91xyt² = 7 × 13 × x × y × t × t','',0,0,0,NULL),(48590,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 24a²b²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','24a²b² = 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × a × a × b × b','',0,0,0,NULL),(48591,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : tr²s³\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','tr²s³ = t × r × r × s × s × s','',0,0,0,NULL),(48592,810,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Factories : p² - q²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','p² - q² = (p – q)(p + q)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48593,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (5a + 6b)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(5a + 6b)² = (5a)² + 2 × 5a × 6b + (6b)²\n                 = 25a² + 60ab + 36b²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48594,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand : ','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','(a/2  + b/3)² = (a/2)² + 2 × a/2 × b/3 + (b/3)²\n                   = a²/4 + ab/3 + b²/9\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(48595,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (2p – 3q)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(2p – 3q)² = (2p)² - 2 × 2p × 3q + (3q)² = 4p² - 12pq + 9q²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48596,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand :','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','(x – 2/x)² = x² - 2 × x × 2/x + (2/x)² = x² - 4 + 4/x²','',1,0,0,NULL),(48597,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (ax + by)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(ax + by)² = (ax)² + 2 × ax × by + (by)² = a²x² + 2abxy + b²y²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48598,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand : (7m – 4)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(7m – 4)² = (7m)² - 2 × 7m × 4 + (4)² = 49m² - 56m + 16','',0,0,0,NULL),(48599,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand :   ','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','(x + 1/2)² = x² + 2 × x × 1/2 + (1/2)² = x² + x + 1/4','',1,0,0,NULL),(48600,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Expand :','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','(a – 1/a)² = a² - 2 × a × 1/a + (1/a)² = a² - 2 + 1/a²','',1,0,0,NULL),(48601,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Use an expansion formula to find the values : (997)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(997)² = (1000 – 3)² = (1000)² - 2 × 1000 × 3 + 3² \n          = 1000000 – 6000 + 9 = 994,009 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48602,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 4xy + 8xy²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','4xy + 8xy² = 4xy (1 + 2y)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48603,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 9a²bc + 12abc²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','9a²bc + 12abc² = 3abc (3a + 4c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48604,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories : a² - 4b²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','a² - 4b² = a² - (2b)² = (a – 2b)(a + 2b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(48605,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories : 3a² - 27b²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','3a² - 27b² = 3(a² - 9b²) = 3[a² - (3b)²]\n          = 3[(a – 3b)(a + 3b)]\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48606,810,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Factories : ','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','x² - (1/x)² = (x – 1/x)(x + 1/x)','',1,0,0,NULL),(48607,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Use an expansion formula to find the values : (102)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(102)² = (100 + 2)² = (100)² + 2 × 100 × 2 + 2²\n                = 10000 + 400 + 4 = 10,404\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48608,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Use an expansion formula to find the values : (97)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(97)² = (100 – 3)² = (100)² - 2 × 100 × 3 + 3²\n              = 10000 – 600 + 9 = 9409\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48609,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Use the formula to find the values : 502 × 498\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','502 × 498 = (500 + 2)(500 – 2) = (500)² - 2²\n             = 250000 – 4 = 249996\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48610,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Use the formula to find the values : 97 × 103\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','97 × 103 = (100 – 3) (100 + 3) = (100)² - 3²\n         = 10000 – 9 = 9991\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48611,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Factories : i) 4x² - 25y²		ii) y² - 4 \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) 4x² - 25y² = (2x)² - (5y)² = (2x – 5y)(2x + 5y)\nii) y² - 4 = y² - 2² = (y – 2)(y + 2)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48612,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Factories :  ','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) p² - 1/25 = p² - (1/5)² = (p – 1/5)(p + 1/5)\nii) 9x² - y²/16 = (3x)² - (y/4)² = (3x – y/4) (3x + y/4)\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(48613,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factories : 365y⁴z³ - 146y²z⁴\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','365y⁴z³ - 146y²z⁴ = 5 × 73 × y⁴ × z³ - 2 × 73 × y² × z⁴\n          = 73y²z² (5y²z – 2z²)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48614,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use formula to multiply : i) (x – 5) (x + 5) 		ii) (2a – 13) (2a + 13)\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) (x – 5)(x + 5) = x² - (5)² = x² - 25 \nii) (2a – 13)(2a + 13) = (2a)² - (13)² = 4a² - 169 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48615,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use formula to multiply : i) (4z – 5y) (4z + 5y)	ii) (2t – 5) (2t + 5)\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) (4z – 5y)(4z + 5y) = (4z)² - (5y)² = 16z² - 25y²\nii) (2t – 5)(2t + 5) = (2t)² - (5)² = 4t² - 25 \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48616,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Expand : i) (2a – 3b)²		ii) (10 + y)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) (2a – 3b)² = (2a)² - 2 × 2a × 3b + (3b)² = 4a² - 12ab + 9b²\nii) (10 + y)² = 10² + 2 × 10 × y + y² = 100 + 20y + y²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48617,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand : i) (b – 7)²		ii) (xy + 3z)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) (b – 7)² = b² - 2 × b × 7 + 7² = b² - 14b + 49\nii) (xy + 3z)² = (xy)² + 2 × xy × 3z + (3z)² = x²y² + 6xyz + 9z²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48618,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (a² - b²)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(a² - b²)² = (a²)² - 2 × a² × b² + (b²)² = a⁴ - 2a²b² + b⁴','',0,0,0,NULL),(48619,810,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (2x + 5)² - (2x – 5)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(2x + 5)² - (2x – 5)² = (2x)² + 2 × 2x × 5 + 5² - [(2x)² - 2 × 2x × 5 + 5²]\n         = (2x)² + 20x + 25 – (2x)² + 20x – 25 = 40x \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48620,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Use an expansion formula to find the values : (1005)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(1005)² = (1000 + 5)² = (1000)² + 2 × 1000 × 5 + 5²\n                 = 1000000 + 10000 + 25 = 1010025\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48621,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Use the formula to find the values : 54 × 46\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','54 × 46 = (50 + 4)(50 – 4) = (50)² - 4² = 2500 – 16 = 2484','',0,0,0,NULL),(48622,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Use the formula to find the values : 98 × 102\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','98 × 102 = (100 – 2)(100 + 2) = (100)² - 2² = 10000 – 4 = 996','',0,0,0,NULL),(48623,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factories : i) a²b – ab 		ii) 4x²y – 6x²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) a²b – ab = ab(a – 1)\nii) 4x²y – 6x² = 2x²(2y – 3)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48624,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Factories : i) 1/2 y² - 8z²		ii) 2x² - 8y²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) 1/2 (y² - 16z²) = 1/2 [y² - (4z)²] = 1/2 [(y – 4z)(y + 4z)]\nii) 2x² - 8y² = 2(x² - 4y²) = 2[x² - (2y)²] = 2[(x – 2y)(x + 2y)]\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48625,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Expand :    ','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------','','i) (p/3 + q/4)² = (p/3)² + 2 × p/3 × q/4 + (q/4)²\n= p²/9 + pq/6 + q²/16\nii) (y – 3/y)² = y² - 2 × y × 3/y + (3/y)²\n= y² - 6 + 9/y² ','',1,0,0,NULL),(48626,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Solve :     ','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','4x²/3  + 3)² = (4x²/3)² + 2 × 4x²/3 × 3 + 3²\n              = 16x⁴/9 + 8x² + 9\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(48627,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------','','Given that, x – 1/x = 9 \nWe have, (x – 1/x)² = x² - 2 × x × 1/x + 1/x²\n∴ 9² = x² - 2 + 1/x²\n∴ 81 + 2 = x² + 1/x²\n∴ x² + 1/x² = 83','',1,0,0,NULL),(48628,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand :    ','\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','(2m/3 + 3n/2)² = (2m/3)² + 2 × 2m/3  × 3n/2  + (3n/2)²\n            = 4m²/9 + 2mn + 9n²/4\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(48629,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand : (6x² - 5y)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(6x² - 5y)²\n= (6x²)² - 2 × 6x² × 5y + (5y)² = 36x⁴ - 60x²y + 25y²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48630,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Expand : (0.4p – 0.5q)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(0.4p – 0.5q)² = (0.4p)² - 2 × 0.4p × 0.5q + (0.5q)²\n= 0.16p² - 0.40pq + 0.25q²\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48631,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (7m – 8n)² + (7m + 8n)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(7m – 8n)² + (7m + 8n)²\n= (7m)² - 2 × 7m × 8n + (8n)²+ (7m)² + 2 × 7m × 8n + (8n)²\n= 2(7m)² + 2(8n)² = 98m² + 128n²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48632,810,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Simplify : (4m + 5n)² + (5m + 4n)²\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------','','','(4m + 5n)² + (5m + 4n)²\n= (4m)² + 2 × 4m × 5n + (5n)² + (5m)² + 2 × 5m × 4n + (4n)²\n= 16m² + 40mn + 25n² + 25m² + 40mn + 16n²\n= 41m² + 80mn + 41n²','',0,0,0,NULL),(48633,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The average of 33, 34, 35, x, 37, 38, 39 is x. The value of x is ----\na) 40 		b) 32 		c) 42 		d) 36\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------\n','','','d) 36','',0,0,0,NULL),(48634,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The mean of the first ten natural numbers is -----\na) 5 			b) 5.5 		c) 6 			d) 6.5\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------','','','b) 5.5 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48635,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The mean of 12, 10, x, 20 and 18 is 17. Find x.\na) 12 		b) 25 		c) 18 			d) 15\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------','','','b) 25','',0,0,0,NULL),(48636,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of integers lying between -5 and 5.\na) 0.6 		b) 0 		c) 0.57 		d) 0.5\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------','','','b) 0','',0,0,0,NULL),(48637,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mean of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7?\na) 3 		b) 4 		c) 5 			d) 6\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------','','','b) 4 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(48638,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mean of 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13?\na) 3 		b) 5 		c) 7 			d) 9\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------','','','c) 7 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48639,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the mean of the first four prime numbers?\na) 3.5 		b) 7 		c) 5 			d) 4.25\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------','','','d) 4.25','',0,0,0,NULL),(48640,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 10 observations is 12, what is their sum?\na) 120			b) 1.2 			c) 12 		d) 60\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------','','','a) 120','',0,0,0,NULL),(48641,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 3, 7, 11 and x is 9, what is the value of x?\na) 9 			b) 11 		c) 13 		d) 15\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------','','','d) 15','',0,0,0,NULL),(48642,811,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mean is also referred as -----\na) average 		b) difference 		c) total 		d) none of these\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------','','','a) average ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48643,811,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of 4, 6, 7, 9 and 4.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Mean = (Sum of observation)/Total number of observations\n                = (4 + 6 + 7 + 9 + 4)/5 = 30/5 = 6\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48644,811,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the mean of first five odd numbers.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','First five odd numbers are 1, 3, 5, 7, 9\nMean = (1 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 9)/5 = 25/5 = 5\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48645,811,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 4, x and y is 6, then find the mean of x, y and 10.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Given : mean of 4, x and y is 6\n∴ 6 = (4 + x + y)/3\n∴ 4 + x + y = 18\n∴ x + y = 14  ----------- (i)\nMean of x, y and 10 is\nMean = (x + y + 10)/3 = (14 + 10)/3 = 24/3 = 8 -------- [from (i)]','',0,0,0,NULL),(48646,811,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of 2, 3, x, 7, 8 is x, then find the value of x.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total number of observations\n∴  x = (2 + 3 + x + 7 + 8)/5\n∴ 5x = 20 + x \n∴ 5x – x = 20\n∴ 4x = 20 \n∴ x = 20/4 = 5 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48647,811,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the mean of ‘n’ observations is 5 and sum of observations is 25, then find n.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Mean = (Sum of observations)/Total no. of observations\n∴ 5 = 25/n \n∴ 5n = 25\n∴ n = 5  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(48648,811,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the average of the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5)/5 = 15/5 = 3\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48650,811,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the average of the numbers 110, 114, 118, 122, 126, 130?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (110 + 114 + 118 + 122 + 126 + 130)/6 = 720/6 = 120\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48651,811,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the formula for mean (average).\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation','',0,0,0,NULL),(48652,811,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Find the rainfall for each day of a week in a certain city is given in millimeters. Find the average rainfall during the week, 9, 11, 8, 20, 10, 16, 12.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (9 + 11 + 8 + 20 + 10 + 16 + 12)/7 = 86/7 = 12.285\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48653,811,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The annual rainfall in Vidarbha in five years is given below. What is the average rainfall for those 5 years? 900 mm, 650 mm, 733 mm, 400 mm.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average rainfall = Total rainfall in 5 years/5\n         = (900 + 650 + 450 + 733 + 400)/2 = 3133/5 = 626.6\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(48654,811,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','A school conducted a survey to find out how far their students live from the school. Given below is the distance of the houses of six of the students from the school. 950 m, 800 m, 700 m, 1.5 km, 1 km, 750 m. Let us find their average distance from the school.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Here, 1 km = 1000 m\nAverage = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (950 + 800 + 700 + 1500 + 1000 + 750)/6 \n                     = 5700/6 = 950','',0,0,0,NULL),(48655,811,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Rutuja practiced skipping with a rope all seven days of a week. The number of times she jumped the rope in one minute every day is given below. 60, 62, 61, 60, 59, 63, 58. Find average.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (60 + 62 + 61 + 60 + 59 + 63 + 58)/7 \n                     = 423/7 = 60.42','',0,0,0,NULL),(48656,811,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','The distance at which some children live from their school is given below in kilometers. To make frequency table of this data. 1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 4, 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 4, 6, 4, 6.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48657,811,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the average of the numbers 98, 99, 97, 92, 91, 93, 95.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (98 + 99 + 97 + 92 + 91 + 93 + 95)/7 \n                     = 665/7 = 95','',0,0,0,NULL),(48658,811,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the average of 42, 43, x, 47, 44 is 45. Find the value of x.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n∴ 45 = (42 + 43 + x + 47 + 44)/5\n∴ 45 × 5 = 176 + x\n∴ x = 225 – 176 = 49','',0,0,0,NULL),(48659,811,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sachin scored runs in 6 innings as follows, find the average score. 49, 50, 51, 53, 55, 56.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (49 + 50 + 51 + 53 + 55 + 56)/6 \n                     = 314/6 = 52.33','',0,0,0,NULL),(48660,811,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the average of 97, 99, 98, 97, 96, 95, 94, 92, 98, 99.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (97 + 99 + 98 + 97 + 96 + 95 + 94 + 92 + 98 + 99)/10 \n                     = 965/10 = 96.5','',0,0,0,NULL),(48661,811,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','During the annual function of a school, a Women’s Self-Help Group had set up a snacks stall. Their sales every hour were worth Rs. 960, Rs. 830, Rs. 945, Rs. 800, Rs. 847, Rs. 970 respectively. What was the average of the hourly sales?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (960 + 830 + 945 + 800 + 847 + 970)/6 \n                     = 5352/6 = 892','',0,0,0,NULL),(48662,811,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','A farmer bought some sacks of animal feed. The weights of the sacks are given in kilograms. What is the average weight of the sacks?\n49.8, 49.7, 49.5, 49.3. 50, 48.9, 49.2, 48.8.\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         -------------------------------------------------------------------','','','Average = (Sum of observation)/Total no. of observation\n                    = (49.8 + 49.7 + 49.5 + 49.3 + 50 + 48.9 + 49.2 + 48.8)/8 \n                     = 395.2/8 = 49.4','',0,0,0,NULL),(48663,811,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','The height (in cm) of 30 children in a class is given in centimeters. Draw up a frequency table of this data. 131, 135, 140, 138, 132, 133, 135, 133, 134, 135, 132, 133, 140, 139, 132, 131, 134, 133, 140, 140, 139, 136, 137, 136, 139, 137, 133, 134, 131, 140.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48664,811,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Draw frequency table for this given data : 4, 3, 2, 4, 3, 4, 3, 3, 2, 8, 2, 3, 3, 4, 3, 4, 4, 5, 2, 8, 8, 2, 5, 3, 4, 4, 3, 6, 2, 3, 4, 4, 3, 3, 2, 6, 4, 4, 7, 2, 3, 6, 3, 6, 6, 6, 7, 6, 7, 3.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48665,811,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a class test of maths, each of the 30 students scored marks as follows. Prepare a frequency distribution table. 25, 20, 19, 17, 11, 20, 21, 27, 28, 30, 25, 30, 29, 24, 24, 27, 21, 19, 18, 25, 24, 22, 23, 25, 27, 28, 27, 25, 24, 23.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48666,811,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','In a certain colony, there are 50 families. The number of people in every family is given below. draw up the frequency table. 5, 4, 5, 4, 5, 3, 3, 3, 4, 3, 4, 2, 3, 4, 2, 2, 2, 2, 4, 5, 1, 3, 2, 4, 5, 3, 3, 2, 4, 4, 2, 3, 4, 3, 4, 2, 3, 4, 5, 3, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 5, 3, 2, 3, 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48667,811,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','A dice was thrown 40 times and the number on the upper face noted is given below. draw up a frequency table for this data. 3, 2, 5, 6, 4, 2, 3, 1, 6, 6, 2, 3, 5, 3, 5, 3, 4, 2, 4, 5, 4, 2, 6, 3, 3, 2, 4, 3, 3, 4, 1, 4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 5, 3, 3, 4.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48668,811,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The number of chapatis that 30 children in a hostel need at every meal is given below. make a frequency table for these scores. 3, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4, 3, 4, 5, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2, 5, 4, 4, 4, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2, 3, 4, 3, 2, 3, 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n','','','Answer : ','',0,0,1,NULL),(48669,811,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The number of peas in each pod are recorded as follows prepare a frequency distribution table. 4, 3, 5, 3, 4, 2, 1