ves.\nviii. The President can declare an Emergency if there is a situation of crisis in the country.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(62797,1954,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart : ','','','i. National Emergency	ii. State Emergency	iii. Financial Emergency','',1,0,0,NULL),(62843,1938,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Famous prison in Andaman ','','','Cellular Jail','',0,0,0,NULL),(62844,1938,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Mahatma Gandhi\'s Memorial at Pune ','','','Aga Khan Palace','',0,0,0,NULL),(62845,1938,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Ashram of Mahatma Gandhi at Vardha ','','','Sevagram Ashram','',0,0,0,NULL),(62846,1938,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Mahatma Gandhi\'s memorial in Mumbai ','','','Manibhavan','',0,0,0,NULL),(62847,1938,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following sources of history into Material sources, Written sources, Oral sources and Audio-Visual sources:\nNewspapers, memorials, inspirational songs, forts, coins, correspondence, powadas, jail, palaces, films, statues, dailies, inscriptions, maps, folk tales, documentaries, autobiography, fountain, books, travelogue, owees, roads, Doordarshan, factory records, folk songs, bridge.\n','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Material Sources</th>\n    <th>Written sources</th>\n<th>Oral sources</th>\n<th>Audio-Visual sources</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Memorials, forts, coins, jail, palaces, statues, fountain, roads, bridge.</td>\n    <td>Newspapers, correspondence, dailies, inscriptions, maps, autobiography, books, travelogue, factory records.</td>\n    <td>Inspirational songs, powadas, folk tales, owees, folk songs.</td>\n    <td>Films, documentaries, Doordarshan.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62848,1938,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which period is considered to be the period of modern Indian History?','','','The period of Europeans, especially British rulers and period of governance of princely states in India is considered as period of modern Indian history.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62849,1938,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar start the newspaper \'Bahishkrut Bharat\'?','','','Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar started the newspaper \'Bahishkrut Bharat\' for the reformation of common people and to bring unity among them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62850,1938,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the reason behind establishment of \'The Survey of India\' during the British rule\'?','','','The Survey of India was established during the British rule to survey various provinces and cities of India using scientific method to prepare maps.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62851,1938,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which department prepared the original maps of Mumbai port?','','','The original maps of Mumbai port were prepared by the Department of Mumbai Port Trust.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62852,1938,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is included in the material sources of history?','','','The material sources of history includes various objects, monuments, places, coins, sculptures, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62853,1938,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the concept : Oral Sources','','','1) The compositions which are passed on from one generation to the other by way of information or rote leaming and which are not in written form are called the \'oral sources of history\'.\n2) Folk songs, powadas, ovees, inspirational songs, folk tales, etc. are included in oral sources.\n3) The creators of the oral sources mostly remain unknown.\n4) We come to know about prevalent social life, traditions, customs, language, etc. through oral sources.\n5) The aim of oral sources was to entertain, inspire and create vitality in the society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62854,1938,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Photographs','','','1) Before the invention of photography, paintings were an important source of history. But the authenticity of such paintings is doubtful.\n2) Photographs are considered more reliable, as the lens capture the persons, events and objects exactly as they appear.\n3) The photograph of a person gives information about the physical features, dressing style, etc.\n4) Photographs enable us to recreate visual image of an event. The photograph of object or an structure helps in understanding its nature.\nHence, photographs are important visual source of modern Indian history.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62855,1938,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Museums and History ','','','1) A place where ancient artefacts, paintings, photographs, tools, coins, documents, clothes, etc. are exhibited is known as Museum.\n2) Ancient artefacts give us information about the economic, social, political and religious conditions of the respective periods.\n3) The metal and the inscriptions on the coin tell us about the economic conditions of that period and the metals used.\n4) We come to know progress in art of making tools, paintings, sculpture and metallurgy.\n5) History and museums are related. They are important source of writing history.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62856,1938,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Audio Sources','','','1) Recording is a form of audio source in history.\n2) The discovery of technique of recording was very important.\n3) Akashvani, audio tapes are considered important audio sources.\n4) Jana Gana Mana sung by Rabindranath Tagore, speeches of Subash Chandra Bose, Mahatma Gandhi, speeches of leaders and personalities are broadcast from Akashvani.\n5) We get to know about prevalent social, cultural, intellectual and educational history of a particular period from audio source. It is helpful for students conducting research in history.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62857,1938,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','During the British period, newspapers also acted as source of social reformation.','','','1) Newspapers like Amrit Bazaar Patrika, Deenbandhu, Dnyanoday, Kesari, etc. acted as important source to awaken the minds of the people.\n2) Newspapers in the British period not only opposed imperialism but also educated the masses and created awareness among the people.\n3) The Nibandhmala by Vishnushastri Chiplunkar and Shatapatre written by Lokahitawadi alias Gopal Hari Deshmukh published in the weekly Prabhakar commented on various social and cultural issues.\n4) Newspapers threw light on ill customs like sati, child marriage, ban on widow remarriage, etc. and awakened minds of the people.\n5) We came to know about various policies of the British towards India and their effects on India.\n6) Thus newspapers were not only source of political events but also acted as a source of social reformation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62858,1938,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Audio-visual considered recordings are as the most trustworthy source for the study of Modern Indian History.','','','Audio-visual recordings are considered as trustworthy source for study of modern history. Because\n1) Important events during the freedom struggle could be seen in reality.\n2) Audio-visual recordings of Dandi March, Quit India movement, Salt Satyagraha and other historical events are available.\n3) Films also dealt with subjects like social problems, superstitions, practices. They reflect contemporary social, religious. political issues.\nTherefore, audio-visual recordings are considered as a unique discovery of 20th century.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62859,1938,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','We should preserve historical monuments.','','','1) Monuments are witness to our glorious past. Many monuments are connected to freedom fighters and their work.\n2) Sevagram Ashram at Wardha and Mani Bhavan at Mumbai give information about the Gandhian era. us\n3) The cellular jail at Andaman is a witness to the sufferings of V. D. Savarkar and his revolutionary work.\n4) We get information about contemporary history, architecture, nature of monument and the economic condition of that particular period.\n5) We get to know our history because of monuments and they invoke nationalistic feeling among us.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62860,1938,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Statues are considered important for the study of modern history.','','','1) Memorials in the form of statues were erected of many personalities who influenced a particular period.\n2) We come to know about those great people because of statues.\n3) The display plaque on the statues gives information about the name of the concerned person, his work, date of birth and his death.\n4) We get information about important events in his life, brief information about his contribution and a biographical sketch.\n5) We come to know about contemporary rulers and eminent personalities.\n6) The memorials are erected in the memory of various events also.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62861,1938,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maps and drawings are important source of history.','','','1) We come to know about changing nature of a city or a location from the map.\n2) They are important from the view of study of architecture of a building as well as phases of development of a particular area.\n3) The development of Bombay city can be understood through the plans drafted by architects and engineers who made the original plan of Bombay Port Trust.\nTherefore, we can say that maps and drawings are important source of history.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62862,1938,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','It is comparatively easy to study sources of modern history.','','','1) History writing is based on evidences and proofs. It is difficult to obtain ancient and medieval sources of history but sources to study modern history are available in abundance and in variety.\n2) There are many material sources available in museums and documents in archives.\n3) Through the audio-visual source we can see the historical event in reality.\nThus, to study sources of modern history is comparatively easy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62863,1938,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','It is required to verify the written sources before they are used.','','','1) We have to be careful while making use of written sources. The authenticity of the documents needs to be verified.\n2) We need to see the ideas suggested through them.\n3) Before making use of the document it is necessary to know the perspective of the writer, his ideologies and opinions about a particular event.\n4) If all the angles are not considered the writings become one-sided.\nHence it is necessary to verify written sources.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62864,1938,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which monuments are considered as historical monuments?','','','The following monuments considered as historical monuments: are\n1) Palaces of rulers of princely state, residences of officers, leaders and revolutionaries, forts, bridges and government offices.\n2) Buildings, water supply system, fountains, roads.\n3) Prisons, national monuments, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62865,1938,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it important to preserve sources?','','','1) Museums, Archives preserve the sources of history.\n2) This sources give evidence of our rich cultural heritage.\n3) There is a great need to preserve this sources, as it is through them, that the rich tradition of history can be passed on to the next generation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62866,1938,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are films considered effective audio-visual source of modern Indian History?','','','1) Due to technological development there was development in photography, recording and film making.\n2) Dada Saheb Phalke laid the foundation of Indian Film Industry. Many movies were made on historical events and personalities.\n3) Audio-visual recordings of Dandi Yatra and on Salt Satyagraha movement are available.\n4) We get information of social, political condition, dress, traditions, language of particular period from the films and audio tapes.\n5) The events happened in the past could be seen in reality.\nTherefore, films are considered as an effective audio-visual source of modern Indian History.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62867,1938,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the work of newspapers in pre-independence period.','','','1) Newspapers like Amrit Bazaar Patrika, Deenbandhu, Dnyanoday, Kesari, etc. acted as important source to awaken the minds of the people.\n2) They criticized the anti-India policies of Britishers.\n3) Western education and knowledge reached people through them.\n4) Newspapers and weeklies were the source of various political, social and cultural events.\n5) They commented on ill customs and practices in our society and thus were the source of social reformation.\n6) They discussed various policies of British rulers and their effects on India.\n7) The work and ideas of leaders and reformers reached people through them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62868,1938,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the importance of the following as sources of history :\na) Inspirational songs	b) Powadas\n','','','<b>a) Inspirational songs</b>\n1) Many inspirational songs were written during the period of freedom movement.\n2) Many unpublished inspirational songs were memorised by freedom fighters.\n3) We get information about the prevalent conditions during pre-independence period.\n4) We come to know about our heritage, work of great men, injustice done by the Britishers.\n5) They gave inspiration to the society and infused spirit in the freedom movement.\n6) As they convey information about the place, person and his deed they are useful in writing history.\n<b>b) Powadas</b>\n1) Powadas were a dramatic narration by alternatingly reciting poetry and prose extract. It is a source to get information about a historical work of a person or an event.\n2) Powadas were composed on the freedom struggle of 1857 and valour of revolutionaries during the British rule.\n3) Powadas were composed on work of Satyashodhak Samaj to awaken the oppressed classes.\n4) During the Samyukta Maharashtra movement, Powadas were composed to inspire people. They used to create motivation, spirit among the people and infused enthusiasm and vitality.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62869,1938,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : An independent department established during the British period to create maps.','','','The Survey of India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62870,1938,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The person who laid the foundation of Indian film industry in the year 1913.','','','Dadasaheb Phalke.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62871,1938,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are the buildings and monuments built during the modern period an important source of modern Indian history?','','','i. The period of Modern Indian History covers the period of Europeans, especially, the British rulers and the governance of princely states.\nii. Various bridges, roads, buildings, etc. constructed during this period are intact even today. While some of them have been declared as national monuments, some others have been converted into museum.\niii. These buildings and monuments are an important source of Modern Indian history. Information about contemporary history, architecture, nature of the monument and also the economic condition of that particular period can be obtained by visiting these sites.\niv. Moreover, one can also get information about the lives of freedom fighters and other renowned personalities belonging to that period. For e.g:\na. The Cellular Jail in Andaman which is now converted into a museum, gives information about the revolutionary work of V. D. Savarkar.\nb. Similarly, information about the Gandhian era can be obtained from Manibhavan in Mumbai or the Sevagram Ashram in Wardha.\nc. Also, the Aga Khan Palace in Pune gives information about the life of Mahatma Gandhi.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62872,1938,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are memorials an important source for the study of Modern Indian History?','','','i. Memorials of many personalities were erected in the form of statues in the Pre-Independence and the Post-Independence period.\nii. These statues provide information about the contemporary rulers and eminent personalities.\niii. Also, information like the full name of the concerned person, details of birth and death, brief information about their contribution and a biographical sketch of the person can be obtained from the display plaque on the statue.\nFor e.g. Statues of Mahatma Jyotirao Phule, Lokmanya Tilak, Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar, etc.\niv. Similarly, memorials erected in the memory of various events give information about the concerned event, period, people related to the event, etc.\nFor e.g. Hutatma Smarak at various places\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62873,1938,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the various newspapers started by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar.','','','The various newspapers started by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar are as follows:\ni. He started the fortnightly \'Mooknayak\' in January 1920. He handed over the control of this newspaper to his colleagues when he had to leave for higher studies to England.\nii. He also started the newspaper \'Bahishkrut Bharat\' in April 1927, in which he wrote about the reformation of common people and about bringing unity among them.\niii. He also started two other newspapers, viz. \'Janata\' and \'Prabuddha Bharat\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62874,1938,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Powada','','','i. Powada is a dramatic narration of the deeds of heroic men and women in a forceful and inspiring manner. It is done by alternatively reciting poetry and prosaic extracts.\nii. It is an important oral source of history as it gives information about a historical event or the work of a person.\niii. Various Powadas were composed on the freedom struggle of 1857, valour of revolutionaries during British rule, etc. They created motivation and spirit among people.\niv. Powadas based on the work of Satyashodhak Samaj were composed to create awakening among oppressed people.\nv. Apart from this, Powadas were composed on other events and movements like the Samyukta Maharashtra movement, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62875,1938,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Films','','','i. Films, which are considered as a unique discovery of modern technology, are an important video and audio-visual source of history.\nii. A great development took place in the development of filmmaking technique in the 20th century.\niii. Dada Saheb Phalke laid the foundation of Indian film industry in 1913.\niv. Audio-visual recordings of several historical events like Dandi Yatra, Salt Satyagraha, Quit India Movement, etc., are available.\nv. These recordings enable one to see and understand the events of the past.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62879,1938,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Inspirational songs are an important source of history.','','','i. Various types of inspirational songs were composed during the freedom movement.\nii. Some of these songs are available in written form, while many others which were memorised by the freedom fighters remain unpublished.\niii. These songs provide information about the conditions during the pre-independence period.\niv. They also convey the spirit behind the freedom struggle.\nThus, inspirational songs are an importance source of understanding history.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62881,1938,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compared to ancient and medieval period, the sources of study of modern Indian history are found in abundance.','','','Compared to ancient and medieval period, the sources of study of modern Indian history are found in abundance due to the following reasons:\ni. The archaeological sources of the modern period are in a better condition as compared to the sources belonging to ancient and medieval period.\nii. Most of these sources are in written form and are preserved in the archives. However, while using the written sources, the ideology and opinions of the author regarding the particular event should be examined properly.\niii. Also, some sources that belong to the modern period are available in audio, video and audio-visual form. Even these sources are preserved in archives.\niv. As these sources are very important for knowing the various political, social and economic conditions of a particular period, they should be preserved through which the rich and glorious past can be handed over to future generation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(62926,2036,'descriptive','4b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','माझ्या बालपणीच्या जगात पुस्तके म्हणजे माझ्यासाठी एक दुर्मीळ वस्तू होती. मला पुस्तके वाचण्याची खूप आवड होतीत्यामुळे मिळेल ते मी वाचत गेलो.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','माझ्या बालपणीच्या जगात पुस्तके म्हणजे माझ्यासाठी एक दुर्मीळ वस्तू होती. मला पुस्तके वाचण्याची खूप आवड होती. त्यामुळे मिळेल ते मी वाचत गेलो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62931,2036,'descriptive','4b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','वारा घेऊन रूप सानुले, खेळ खेळतो झोपाळा, रात्रही इवली होऊन म्हणते, अंगाईचे गीत तुला, मलाही वाटे लहान होऊन, तुझ्याहूनही लहान रे तुझ्यासंगती सदा राहावे, विसरूनी शाळा, घर सारे !','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','वारा घेऊन रूप सानुले, खेळ खेळतो झोपाळा, रात्रही इवली होऊन म्हणते, अंगाईचे गीत तुला, मलाही वाटे लहान होऊन, तुझ्याहूनही लहान रे तुझ्यासंगती सदा राहावे, विसरूनी शाळा, घर सारे !','',0,0,0,NULL),(62934,2036,'descriptive','4b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','\"उरलेले अन्न फेकू नका, मला दया. मी ते उपाशी लोकांना खायला देतो.\" राजूने पुठ्ठ्याची पाटी तयार केली आणि गळ्यात अडकवली. लोक त्याला अन्न देऊ लागले.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','\"उरलेले अन्न फेकू नका, मला दया. मी ते उपाशी लोकांना खायला देतो.\" राजूने पुठ्ठ्याची पाटी तयार केली आणि गळ्यात अडकवली. लोक त्याला अन्न देऊ लागले.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62937,2036,'descriptive','4b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','कारगील येथे पावसाळा आणि हिवाळा जेवढा जीवघेणा, तेवढाच उन्हाळा सुखावह आणि आल्हाददायी. या काळात हिरवागार निसर्ग रमणीय दिसतो.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','कारगील येथे पावसाळा आणि हिवाळा जेवढा जीवघेणा, तेवढाच उन्हाळा सुखावह आणि आल्हाददायी. या काळात हिरवागार निसर्ग रमणीय दिसतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62943,2036,'descriptive','4b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','अनेक खेडी, गावे आणि शहरे या सर्वांचा मिळून देश बनतो. देशाला निश्चित सीमारेषा असते. या सीमारेषेच्या आत राहणारे लोक त्या देशाचे रहिवासी असतात. भारताच्या सीमारेषेत राहणारे आपण सारे भारतीय म्हणून ओळखले जातो.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','अनेक खेडी, गावे आणि शहरे या सर्वांचा मिळून देश बनतो. देशाला निश्चित सीमारेषा असते. या सीमारेषेच्या आत राहणारे लोक त्या देशाचे रहिवासी असतात. भारताच्या सीमारेषेत राहणारे आपण सारे भारतीय म्हणून ओळखले जातो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62946,2036,'descriptive','4b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','अंघोळीला जाताना अगोदर गीझरचे बटण बंद करावे आणि नंतर गीझरचे गरम पाणी सोडावे. वीज प्रवाहित होताना काही अडथळा आला, तर वीजप्रवाह पाण्यात प्रवाहित होऊ शकतो आणि विजेचा झटका बसू शकतो.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','अंघोळीला जाताना अगोदर गीझरचे बटण बंद करावे आणि नंतर गीझरचे गरम पाणी सोडावे. वीज प्रवाहित होताना काही अडथळा आला, तर वीजप्रवाह पाण्यात प्रवाहित होऊ शकतो आणि विजेचा झटका बसू शकतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62951,2036,'descriptive','4b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','दिवसभर मुलांनी सहलीत धमाल केली. मजा केली. कविता गायल्या. गाण्यांच्या भेंड्या खेळल्या. खूप गप्पा मारल्या. निसर्गाचे निरीक्षण केले. खूप गमतीजमती केल्या.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','दिवसभर मुलांनी सहलीत धमाल केली. मजा केली. कविता गायल्या. गाण्यांच्या भेंड्या खेळल्या. खूप गप्पा मारल्या. निसर्गाचे निरीक्षण केले. खूप गमतीजमती केल्या.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62960,2025,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','बाईनी उर्मिलाचे पत्र वाचून काय उत्तर पाठवले असेल याची कल्पना करा व तसे पत्र लिहा. ','','','दिनांक: १० सप्टेंबर २०२२.\nचि. उर्मिला,\nअनेक आशीर्वाद.\nतुझे पत्र मिळाले. वाचून खूप आनंद झाला. तुला अजूनही माझी आठवण येते हे वाचून मन भरून आले. तू छान पत्र लिहिलं आहेस. तुझ्या अभ्यासाविषयी आणि खेळण्याविषयी वाचून समाधान वाटलं. माझ्या घराबाहेरील गुलमोहर तुम्हां मुलांसारखाच खूप मोठा झालाय. त्याला आता छान फुले येतात. तुझे पत्र मी स्वाती, जय, आणि सलमाला दाखवले. त्यांनी तुझे पत्र वाचून तुला पत्र लिहिण्याचे ठरवले आहे.\nअसेच अधूनमधून पत्र लिहीत जा. खूप अभ्यास कर. खेळांच्या स्पर्धेमध्ये सहभागी हो.\nतुझ्याच,\nपाटील बाई\nश्री. नयना पाटील\nमु. पो. ता. महाड\nजि. रायगड.\nपिन-xxxxxx\nइ-मेल: abc@xyz.com','',0,0,0,NULL),(62966,2025,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','तिसरी शिकलेल्या शाळेतील कोणकोणत्या गोष्टी खूप आवडल्या हे उर्मिलाने पत्रातून कळवले. तसे तुमच्या शाळेतील कोणकोणत्या गोष्टी तुम्हांला खूप आवडतात, हे तुमच्या मामाला पत्र लिहून कळवा. ','','','दिनांक : १० जुलै २०२२.\nतीर्थरूप मामा,\nसाष्टांग नमस्कार.\nकालच तुझे पत्र मिळाले, तू मला माझ्या शाळेतील गमती जमती विचारल्या होत्यास माझ्या शाळेत खूपच मजा येते. आमच्या शाळेचे मैदान मला खूप आवडते. तेथे वेगवेगळे खेळ आम्हांला खेळायला मिळतात. आमचे खेळाचे शिक्षक खूप छान पद्धतीने आम्हांला मार्गदर्शन करतात. आमच्या शाळेचा संगणक कक्षही मला खूप आवडतो. संगणकाच्या तासाला मला वेगवेगळ्या गमतीजमती शिकायला मिळतात. आमच्या चित्रकलेच्या वाईसुद्धा खूप सुंदर चित्रे काढतात. मला चित्रकलेचा तास खूप आवडतो. मलासुद्धा छान छान चित्रे काढायला शिकायचे आहे.\nतीर्थरूप मामीला साष्टांग नमस्कार. दादा व सोनी कसे आहेत? त्यांची खूप आठवण येते. सु‌ट्टीमध्ये भेट होईलच.\nशेवटचा मायना -\nतुझा/तुझी\nतुझाच भाचा.\nअक्षय\nकु. अक्षय वाघमारे\nमु. पो. वडगाव\nता. खेड जि. पुणे\nपिन-xxxxxx\nइ-मेल: abc@xyz.com','',0,0,0,NULL),(62972,2025,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','तुमच्या मित्राला वाढदिवसाच्या शुभेच्छा देणारे पत्र लिहा.','','','दिनांक: ०२ सप्टेंबर २०२२.\nप्रिय अजय,\nसप्रेम नमस्कार\nकालच तुझे वाढदिवसाचे आमंत्रण देणारे पत्र मिळाले. तुझा वाढदिवस ७ सप्टेंबरला असतो हे मला माहिती आहे; पण यावर्षी ७ सप्टेंबरला शाळेचे विज्ञानप्रदर्शन असल्यामुळे मी तुझ्या वाढदिवसाला येऊ शकणार नाही. तुझ्यासाठी छानशी भेट मी अगोदरच घेतली आहे. आपण भेटल्यानंतर मी तुला ती देईन तुला\nवाढदिवसाच्या हार्दिक शुभेच्छा. तुला निरोगी आरोग्य लाभावे आणि तुझ्या साऱ्या इच्छा पूर्ण व्हाव्यात हीच या वाढदिवसाच्या निमित्ताने ईश्वरचरणी प्रार्थना खूप धमाल कर. आपण भेटल्यानंतर गमतीजमती सांग.\nतीर्थरूप काका-काकूंना साष्टांग नमस्कार.\nतुझा मित्र, विजय\nकु. विजय पाटील\nगणेश सदन,\nपाच रस्ता, मुलुंड (प.)\nपिन-xxxxxx\nइ-मेल: abc@xyz.com','',0,0,0,NULL),(62973,2025,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','तुमच्या दादाला चित्रकला स्पर्वेत बक्षीस मिळाल्याबददल त्याचे अभिनंदन करणारे पत्र लिहा.','','','दिनांक: १० नोव्हेंबर २०२२.\nप्रिय दादा, सप्रेम नमस्कार\nकालच आईने सांगितले, की तुला जिल्लास्तरीय चित्रकला स्पर्धेत पाहिल्या क्रमांकाचे बक्षीस मिळाले आहे आणि तुझी निवड राज्यस्तरीय स्पर्धेसाठी झाली आहे. मी तर आनंदाने उड्याच मारल्या. आज मी माझ्या शाळेतील मित्रांनाही तुझ्याब‌द्दल सांगितले. वादा, तुझ्या या यशाबददल खूप खूप अभिनंदन तू असेच यश मिळवावे हीच आमची सवाँची देवाच्या चरणी प्रार्थना. तू सुट्टीत घरी आलास, की मलाही चित्रकलेचे मार्गदर्शन कर, म्हणजे मीही तुझ्यासारखी छान छान चित्रे काढू शकेन.\nतीर्थरूप आई-बाबा मजेत आहेत. तुला भेटण्यासाठी आम्ही सारेच उत्सुक आहोत.\nतुझाच छोटा भाऊ.\nश्रेयस\nकु. श्रेयश राणे.\nमु.पो. बिरवाडी\nजि. रायगड\nपिन-xxxxxx\nइ-मेल: abc@xyz.com','',0,0,0,NULL),(62974,2025,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','तुमच्या आजीला पत्र लिहून तिच्या तब्येतीची चौकशी करा.','','','दिनांक : १२ ऑगस्ट २०२२.\nतीर्थरूप आजी,\nसाष्टांग नमस्कार.\nतू कशी आहेस ? आई म्हणत होती, की गेल्या आठवड्यात तुझी तब्येत बिघडली होती. तू डॉक्टरकडे जाऊन आलीस का? तुझी तब्येत कशी आहे. पावसाचे दिवस आहेत त्यामुळे काळजी घे. औषधे व गोळ्या वेळेवर घेतेस ना ? पाण्यात फार काम करू नकोस. बाबा लवकरच गावी येणार आहेत. पत्र मिळताच तुझी खुशाली नक्की कळव तीर्थरूप आजोबांना साष्टांग नमस्कार. त्यांनाही तब्येतीची काळजी घ्यायला सांग.\nतुझाच नातू,\nयश\nकु. यश पाटील.\nश्री गणेश सोयायटी,\nपाच रस्ता, भांडुप (प)\nपिन-xxxxxx\nइ-मेल: abc@xyz.com','',0,0,0,NULL),(62979,2025,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या विषयांनुसार पत्रलेखन करा.\nटाळेबंदीच्या काळात कराओकेवर गाणी शिकल्याबद्दल मैत्रिणीचे अभिनंदन करणारे पत्र तयार करा.','','','दिनांक : १२ जुलै २०\nप्रिय राधिका,\nसप्रेम नमस्कार.\nलिहितार\nमी काल आईच्या व्हॉटस्अॅपवर तुझे गाणे ऐकले. खूप छान वाटले. शाळेत तू नेहमी गाणे म्हणतेस; पण आज तू कराओकेसोबत गाणे गायलीस तुझे मनापासून अभिनंदन । टाळेबंदी (लॉकडाउन) मुळे सगळेच घरात कंटाळले आहेत. पण तू मात्र कंटाळली नाहीस. कराओकेच्या साथीने गाणे म्हणायला शिकलीस. तुझे \'दिल है छोटासा\' हे गाणे मला खूप आवडले. तुझे पुन्हा एकदा अभिनंदन !\nसार पत्र ई-मेल\nतुझी मैत्रीण,\nराही\nई-मेल : rxxx@gmail.com','',0,0,0,NULL),(62982,2025,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','तुमच्या आईचा वाढदिवस आहे. तिला शुभेच्छा देणारे ई-पत्र तयार करा.','','','दिनांक : २२ ऑक्टोबर २०........\nप्रिय आई,\nशिरसाष्टांग नमस्कार.\nआई आठ दिवसांनी, म्हणजे ३० ऑक्टोबरला तुझा वाढदिवस आहे. तुझ्या वाढदिवसासाठी मी खास भेटकार्ड बनवले आहे. ते खास कार्ड मी तुला मेल करीनच. मी प्रथमच तुझ्यापासून लांब राहत आहे. त्यामुळे ई-मेलद्वारे तुला शुभेच्छा देत आहे.\nतुला वाढदिवसाच्या खूप खूप शुभेच्छा! मला माहीत आहे, तुला वाढदिवस साजरा करायला आवडत नाही. त्या दिवशी तू कुंडीत रोप लावतेस. मला ते खूप आवडते.\nबाबांना व छोट्या साईशाला त्यांची आठवण काढतेय म्हणून सांग.\nतुझा लाडका,\nसागर\nई-मेल : svxxx@gmail.com','',0,0,0,NULL),(62985,2025,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','तुमच्या मित्राला / मैत्रिणीला तुमच्या शाळेला भेट देण्यासाठी निमंत्रण करणारे पत्र लिहा.','','','दिनांक : १२ डिसेंबर २०\nप्रिय श्रेया,\nसप्रेम नमस्कार.\nश्रेया, माझ्या शाळेला भेट देण्याची तुझी इच्छा आहे असे मागच्या मेलमध्ये तू कळवले होते. लवकरच नाताळची सुट्टी सुरू होईल, तेव्हा तू जरूर ये. माझ्या शाळेला फक्त एकच दिवस सुट्टी आहे. त्यामुळे तुला माझी शाळा पाहता येईल.\nमाझ्या शाळेतील सर्व भिंती बोलक्या आहेत. त्यावर विविध चित्रे चितारली आहेत. शाळेचे ग्रंथालय, कलाकेंद्र, संगणक (AV) खोली, खेळाचे मैदान हे सर्वच पाहण्यासारखे आहे. त्यामुळे मला शाळेत जायला खूप आवडते.\nतुलाही माझी शाळा जरूर आवडेल. घरी आईबाबाही तुझ्या येण्याची वाट पाहत आहेत, तेव्हा लवकरच भेटूया.\nतुझा मित्र,\nश्रेयस\nई-मेल : ktcxxx@gmail.com','',0,0,0,NULL),(62990,2026,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','\'आपल्याकडच्या शेतीत काही दम नाही, परदेशातील शेती किती थोर आणि आपल्याकडे ती कशी शक्य नाही,\' असा सूर बऱ्याचदा आळवला जात असतो. हे खरं आहे, की खडकाळ जमीन, क्षारपड झालेली माती, बेभरवशाचा पाऊस, चढतं तापमान, दळणवळणाची गैरसोय असे एक ना अनेक प्रश्न आपल्यापुढे असतात. त्यावर तोडगा काढत शेती करणं जिकिरीचं बनत असतं; पण पावसाच्या भरवशावर आणि नशिबाच्या हवाल्यावर नाही शेती करायची, असं जर कुणी ठरवलं तर? प्रयोगशील आणि आधुनिक पद्धतीने शेती करण्यासाठी कंबर कसली तर? परदेशासारखी शेती आपल्याकडच्या मातीतही फुलताना दिसू शकते. निर्यातयोग्य उत्पादन मिळण्याची शंभर टक्के खात्री बाळगून आपल्याकडचा शेतकरीही रात्रीची निवांत झोप अनुभवू शकतो. आपल्या शेतात गुलाब, ऑर्किड, कार्नेशन, अॅन्थेरिअम अशा रंगीबेरंगी फुलांचे ताटवे फुलले आहेत, असं स्वप्न आपणही प्रत्यक्षात उतरवू शकतो.','पुढील उतारे काळजीपूर्वक वाचा आणि त्याखाली विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','प्रश्न. पुढील प्रश्नांसाठी उत्तराचा अचूक पर्याय निवडा :\n(१) शेतकरी रात्रीची निवांत झोप केव्हा अनुभवेल ?\n(अ) प्रयोगशील आधुनिक पद्धतीने शेती केल्यावर\n(ब) दळणवळणाची गैरसोय दूर झाल्यावर\n(क) पावसावर अवलंबून राहिल्यावर   \n(ड) नशिबावर हवाला ठेवल्यावर\n(२) उताऱ्यावरून पुढीलपैकी असत्य विधान कोणते ?\n(अ) भारतातील शेतीत काही दम नाही.\n(ब) भारतात पाऊस अनियमित पडतो.\n(क) भारतातील बरीचशी जमीन खडकाळ व क्षारपड झालेली आहे.\n(ड) परदेशासारखी शेती भारतातही होऊ शकते.\n(३) कंबर कसणे म्हणजे -\n(अ) खूप व्यायाम करणे     (ब) कसरत करणे\n(क) तयार होणे                (ड) खूप कष्ट करणे','','(१) प्रयोगशील आधुनिक पद्धतीने शेती केल्यावर.\n(२) भारतातील शेतीत काही दम नाही.\n(३) तयार होणे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62993,2026,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','\"बाबा, परवा कामगारदिनी आमच्या कॉलेजमध्ये रक्तदान शिबिर आहे आणि मी रक्तदान करणार आहे. \"\n\" अरे बंडू, रक्तदान करू नकोस. ते चांगलं नसतं. तुझी तब्येत बिघडेल.\"\n\"असं काही होत नाही बाबा उलट रक्तदान केल्याने रक्ताभिसरण संस्था अधिक कार्यक्षम होते. \"\n\"अरे, पण दुसऱ्यांना का रक्त दयायचे?\"\n\"अहो बाबा, त्यामुळे दुसऱ्यांचा जीव वाचतो, यापेक्षा मोठं पुण्य ते काय!\"\n\"हो, पण मला तुझी काळजी वाटतेय.\"\n\"बाबा, काही काळजी करू नका. काही होत नाही.\"\n\"बरं बंडू, कर रक्तदान. अगं संगीता, तुझ्या शाळेत काही कार्यक्रम आहे का त्या दिवशी ?\"\n\"हो, आम्ही त्या दिवशी ग्रामस्वच्छता करणार आहोत.\"\n\"मग आम्ही ग्रामस्थही सहभागी होऊ त्यात.\"\n\"तर मग सर्वच गाव स्वच्छ होणार!\" आई म्हणाली.','पुढील उतारे काळजीपूर्वक वाचा आणि त्याखाली विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) बंडू कोणत्या दिवशी रक्तदान करणार आहे ?\n(२) रक्तदान केल्याने कोणती गोष्ट कार्यक्षम होते ?\n(३) \"सर्वच गाव स्वच्छ होणार!\" असे आई का म्हणाली ?','','(१)  बंडू कामगारदिनी रक्तदान करणार आहे.\n(२) रक्तदान केल्याने रक्ताभिसरण संस्था कार्यक्षम होते.\n(३) ग्रामस्थही सहभागी होणार असल्यामुळे आई \"सर्वच गाव स्वच्छ होणार !\" असे म्हणाली.','',0,0,0,NULL),(62999,2026,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','\"किती भयंकर उकाडा आहे हो!\"\n\"भयंकर, भयंकर। आणि अजून तर एप्रिल-मे महिना यायचा आहे.\"\n\"काल टीव्हीवर सांगितले की, अजून काही दिवस उकाडा राहणार.\"\n\"बाप रे! निसर्गाचे चक्रच उलटेसुलटे झाले आहे।\"\n\"मी सांगू का? याला निसर्ग जबाबदार नाही. आपणच जबाबदार आहोत.\"\n\"ते मला माहीत आहे. आपण वृक्षतोड मोठ्या प्रमाणात करतो.\"\n\"बरोबर. पण तेवढे एकच कारण नाही. आपण सिमेंट काँक्रिटच्या इमारती बांधतो, त्यांच्या भोवतालच्या जागेत लाया बसवतो. रस्ता डांबराने किंवा सिमेंट काँक्रिटने झाकतो.\"\n\"हे पटलं मला. अशा परिस्थितीत गवतसुद्धा उगवणार नाही; तर झाडे कुठून उगवणार? मग उष्णता वाढणारच. शिवाय, घराघरात एसी बसवले आहेत. गाड्यांमध्ये एसी अन् गाड्या तर लाखोंच्या संख्येने. एसीमुळे तापमानात वाढ होतेच, तशीच वाढ गाड्यांनी उत्सर्जित केलेल्या वायूमुळेही होते.\" णार आहे.\"\n\"हे निसर्गावरील अत्याचार आहेत. माणसाने हे सर्व थांबवले पाहिजे. नाहीतर निसर्गाने दिलेले चटके सहन करावेच लागतील, नाही का !\"\n\"अगदी बरोबर बोललात बघा.\"','पुढील उतारे काळजीपूर्वक वाचा आणि त्याखाली विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) कसले चक्र उलटेसुलटे झाले आहे?\n(२) तापमानवाढ कशामुळे होते ?\n(३) निसर्गावरील अत्याचार थांबवले नाहीत तर काय होईल ?','','(१) निसर्गाचे चक्र उलटेसुलटे झाले आहे.\n(२) एसीमुळे तापमानवाढ होते.\n(३) निसर्गावरील अत्याचार थांबवले नाहीत, तर विविध प्रकारचे निसर्गाचे चटके सहन करावे लागतील.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63001,2026,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','गुगल नसेल, तर आज जगण्याची कल्पनाही करता येत नाही. कल्पवृक्षाच्या झाडाखाली बसावे आणि कुठलीही इच्छा व्यक्त करावी की, काही क्षणांत इच्छापूर्ती होते म्हणतात. तसेच गुगलवरही काही सेकंदांत घडते. कुठल्याही अनोळखी शहरात गेल्यावर तिथे ठरलेल्या पत्त्यावर जाताना मोबाइलमध्ये गुगल मॅपला जाण्याचे ठिकाण सांगितले की, गुगल मॅप आपल्याला अगदी त्या माणसाच्या घराची बेल वाजवण्यापर्यंत न चुकता घेऊन जातो. कोणाला पत्र लिहायचेय, महत्त्वाची कागदपत्रे पाठवायची आहेत तर गुगलचे जी-मेल आहेच सेवेला !\nबरे तर बरे, गुगलला हिंदी, मराठी, उर्दू, पंजाबी यांसारख्या बऱ्याच भाषा कळतात. या भाषांमधून गुगल आपल्याला सेवाही उपलब्ध करून देते. किती रुपयांचे किती डॉलर्स किंवा किती पौंडांचे किती डॉलर्स अशी विनिमयाची सगळी किचकट गणिते गुगल आपल्याला चटकन सोडवून देते.\nअशा या गुगलचे निर्माते आहेत लॅरी पेज आणि सर्गी ब्रिन हे दोघे युवक !','पुढील उतारे काळजीपूर्वक वाचा आणि त्याखाली विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) सध्याच्या युगात आपल्या सर्व इच्छा कोण पूर्ण करते ?\n(२) गुगल मॅप आपल्याला कोणत्या गोष्टीचे मार्गदर्शन करतो ?\n(३) गुगलच्या निर्मात्यांची नावे लिहा.','','(१) सध्याच्या युगात गुगल आपल्या सर्व इच्छा पूर्ण करते.\n(२) गुगल मॅप ठरलेल्या पत्त्यावर जाण्याच्या ठिकाणाबद्दल मार्गदर्शन करतो.\n(३) लॅरी पेज व सर्गी ब्रिन हे गुगलचे निर्माते आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63002,2026,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','जयश्री नावाच्या हत्तिणीबरोबर मी जंगलात जात असे, तेव्हा ती चारही बाजूंना गच्च झाडी असलेल्या भागात जाई. मला वाटायचं आता जंगल संपलं, पण समोर असलेल्या वेली, झाडं बाजूला करून ती आणखी आत, शांत ठिकाणी जात असे. ते ठिकाण म्हणजे छान गारवा आणि मस्त हिरवाई यांचा सुंदर मिलाफ होता.\nतुमच्या घरात जशा तुम्हांला हव्या असलेल्या गोष्टी असतात ना, तशाच हत्तीच्या घरातदेखील हत्तीला हव्या असणाऱ्या गोष्टी असतात. जसं तुम्ही मित्रांबरोबर बॉलने खेळता, तसेच हत्तीदेखील मस्त खेळतात; पण बॉलच्या जागी असते एक सुसरबाई. होय, हत्ती खेळतात चक्क सुसरीशी ! ते सुसरीला पाण्यातून उचलतात आणि एकमेकांकडे फेकतात... झेलायचा प्रयत्नही करतात.\nहत्ती नदीवर पोहायलादेखील जातात. आपण पाणी पायांवर घेऊन त्याचं तापमान किती आहे, याचा अंदाज घेतो ना, तसंच हत्तीही करतो. हत्ती पाण्यात ३० ते ३५ तास सहज पोहतात.','पुढील उतारे काळजीपूर्वक वाचा आणि त्याखाली विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) जयश्री हत्तीण जंगलाच्या कोणत्या भागात जाई?\n(२) हत्तिणीने निवडलेल्या जंगलातल्या ठिकाणी सुंदर मिलाफ कसला होता?\n(३) हत्ती कोणाबरोबर खेळतात ?','','(१) जयश्री हत्तीण जंगलात चारही बाजूंना गच्च झाडी असलेल्या भागात जाई.\n(२) हत्तिणीने निवडलेल्या जंगलातल्या ठिकाणी छान गारवा आणि मस्त हिरवाई यांचा सुंदर मिलाफ होता.\n(३) हत्ती सुसरीबरोबर खेळतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63003,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nमी पाहिलेला अपघात','','','                                             मी पाहिलेला अपघात\nपावसाळ्याचे दिवस होते. रिमझिम पाऊस पडून गेला होता. आमची शाळा सुटली आणि आम्ही शाळेच्या फाटकाबाहेर पडलो. एवढ्यात एक मोटरसायकल वेगाने आमच्या समोरून गेली आणि पुढे जोरदार आवाज झाला. गाड्यांच्या ब्रेकचा आवाज आणि एकमेकांवर गाड्या धडकल्याचा आवाज आला. पुढच्या वळणावर त्या मोटरसायकलवाल्याचा अपघात झाला होता.\nवेगाने जाणारा मोटरसायकल स्वार नुकत्याच पडलेल्या पावसामुळे निसरड्या झालेल्या रस्त्यावरून घसरला होता. त्याला वाचवण्याच्या प्रयत्नात मागच्या गाड्यांनी एकदम ब्रेक मारल्यामुळे त्या गाड्या एकमेकींवर आदळल्या होत्या. त्यांचे खूपच नुकसान झालेले दिसत होते. मोटरसायकलवाल्याची अवस्था खूप वाईट दिसत होती. त्याच्या हातापायांना मार लागलेला दिसत होता; मात्र हेल्मेटमुळे त्याला मोठी दुखापत झालेली नव्हती. वेदनेने तो विव्हळत असताना काही लोक त्याच्या मदतीला धावले, त्यांनी त्याला उचलून रस्त्याच्या बाजूला बसवले. त्याची मोटरसायकल एकाने उचलून उभी केली. एवढचात वाहतूक पोलीस आले. त्यांनी रस्ता मोकळा करून अपघाताचा पंचनामा सुरू केला. जमलेली गदीं हटवली. जखमी मोटरसायकलस्वार धक्क्यातून थोडा सावरला होता. एका गाडीवाल्याने त्याला हॉस्पिटलमध्ये भरती केले.\nआजही माझ्या कानात या अपघाताचा कर्कश आवाज घुमतो आणि भीतीने अंगावर शहारे येतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63022,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nमाझा दादा/ताई \nआठवण – नाव – जिल्हा – शिकणे – रपेट - दादा / ताई – खोड्या – भांडण.','','','                                   माझा दादा/ताई \nमला एक मोठा भाऊ आहे. त्याचे नाव आहे धवल. तो इयत्ता नववीमध्ये शिकतो. हा माझा दादा बॅडमिंटनपटू आहे. त्याने आंतरशालेय स्पर्धेत खूप बक्षिसे मिळवली आहेत. कधी कधी तो माझ्या खोड्या काढतो. माझ्यासोबत भांडतोही. काही वेळाने मला सायकलवरून रपेट मारून आणतो. दादा जेव्हा शहरात खेळायला जातो, तेव्हा मला त्याची खूप आठवण येते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63023,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nपरिसरातील पाणी\nफळे – कोकण – घराभोवती – झाडे – गरुड - प्राणी-पक्षी - कुत्रा, मांजर - इत्यादी प्राणी – खार – वानर.','','','                                      परिसरातील पाणी\nमी कोकणात राहतो. माझ्या घराभोवती आंबे, फणस, सुपारीची झाडे आहेत. त्यामुळे झाडावर राहणारे आणि आसपास वावरणारे खूप प्राणी-पक्षी आहेत. कुत्रा, मांजर, गाय, म्हैस, बैल, शेळ्या हे प्राणी आम्ही पाळले आहेत. परंतु झाडांवरून कौलांवर उड्या मारणारे वानर खूप असतात. झाडावर सरसर चढणारी खारूताई मला खूप आवडते. कधी तरी गरुड पक्षी पाहायला मिळतो. कोंबड्या आणि बदके घराभोवती फिरतात. कावळे, चिमण्या, पोपट, साळुंख्या, मैनाही झाडावर फळे खायला येतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63024,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nआपला निसर्ग -  सोबती\nसमाधान - लाकूड, फळे, - फुले – जंगल – नदी – माणूस – निसर्ग – सूर्य – समुद्र – पाणी – ढग.\n','','','                                   आपला निसर्ग -  सोबती\nमाणूस विचार करतो. नवे नवे शोध लावतो. परंतु तो एकटा हे सारे करू शकत नाही. त्याला निसर्गाची साथ लागते. माणसाला सूर्यापासून उष्णता मिळते. उजेड मिळतो. समुद्राच्या पाण्यापासून वाफ तयार होते. त्यापासून पावसाचे ढग तयार होतात. ढगांतून पाऊस पडतो. सर्व सृष्टीला पाणी मिळते. पाणी माणसाची, सर्व सजीवांची तहान भागवते. पाणी नद्यांमध्ये जाते. सगळीकडे बरीच झाडे आहेत. जंगले आहेत. त्या जंगलांमुळे लाकूड मिळते. फळे, फुले, औषधे मिळतात. अशा त-हेने माणसाला निसर्गाची साथ मिळाल्यामुळे माणूस आपले जीवन समाधानाने जगू शकतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63025,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nसंगणक आपला मित्र \nजवळचा मित्र - शाळेचा अभ्यास -  हिशोब करणे  -  रंग भरणे  -  चित्र काढणे - विविध आकारातील  अक्षरे – वर्गमित्र -   संगणक मित्र  - टंकलेखन – दुरुस्त्या.','','','                                 संगणक - आपला मित्र \nवर्गातील विद्यार्थी एकमेकांच्या उपयोगी पडतात. ते एकमेकांचे मित्र बनतात. तसाच शालेय जीवनात, अभ्यासासाठी, माहिती देण्यासाठी संगणक सगळ्यांचा मित्र झाला आहे. त्याचा उपयोग करणाऱ्या सर्वांचा हा मित्र आहे. ज्याला चांगल्या प्रकारे टंकलेखन करता येते, त्याला तो चांगल्या प्रकारे मदत करतो. हव्या त्या दुरुस्त्या करून देतो.\nसंगणकावर वेगवेगळ्या आकारांत अक्षरे काढता येतात. छानशी चित्रे काढता येतात. रंग भरता येतात. हिशेब तर भराभर करता येतात. सध्या तर शाळेचा सगळा अभ्यास या मित्रासोबत होतो. योग्य वेळी आणि योग्य तन्हेने हा वापरला, तर आपला जवळचा मित्र होऊ शकतो. हे आता सगळ्यांनाच पटत असेलच.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63026,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nइंदिरा गांधी\nवंदन - स्त्री-प्रधानमंत्री – भारतरत्न – अणुचाचणी – बांग्लादेश – अभिमानास्पद – पराभव - वैज्ञानिक प्रगती - आर्यभट्ट उपग्रह.','','','                                            इंदिरा गांधी\nइंदिरा गांधी या भारताच्या पहिल्या स्त्री-प्रधानमंत्री होत्या. त्या प्रधानमंत्री असताना भारताने पहिली अणुचाचणी केली. भारतासाठी ही अभिमानास्पद बाब आहे.\n१९५६ भारताची वैज्ञानिक प्रगती त्यांच्याच काळात सुरू झाली. भारताचा आर्यभट्ट हा उपग्रह आकाशात त्यांच्या प्रेरणेने सोडण्यात आला. इंदिरा गांधींनी १९७१ सालच्या भारत-पाकिस्तान युद्धात पाकिस्तानचा पराभव केला आणि बांग्लादेशची निर्मिती केली. १९७१ साली त्यांना भारत सरकारने \'भारतरत्न\' या पुरस्काराने सन्मानित केले. त्यांच्या स्मृतींना शतशः वंदन !','',0,0,0,NULL),(63027,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nसचिन तेंडुलकर\nमहान खेळाडू – भारतरत्न – तंत्रशुद्ध – राज्यसभा – सदस्य – क्रिकेट – विक्रम -  क्रिकेटवीर – खेलरत्न निवृत्ती - शंभर शतके.','','','                                       सचिन तेंडुलकर\nसचिन तेंडुलकर हा भारतीय क्रिकेट संघातील सर्वश्रेष्ठ फलंदाज होता. त्याच्या नावावर क्रिकेटमधील अनेक विक्रमांची नोंद झालेली आहे. त्यामुळेच संपूर्ण जग त्याला क्रिकेटवीर म्हणून ओळखते. त्याच्या कारकिर्दीचा सन्मान म्हणून शासनातर्फे त्याला \'खेलरत्न\' पुरस्कार देण्यात आला. आंतरराष्ट्रीय क्रिकेटमध्ये शंभर शतके करण्याचा विक्रम त्याच्या नावावर आहे. २०१३ साली त्याने क्रिकेटमधून निवृत्ती घेतली.\nराष्ट्रपतींकडून राज्यसभा सदस्य म्हणून त्याची नियुक्ती करण्यात आली आहे. तंत्रशुद्ध खेळ खेळणारा मेहनती खेळाडू म्हणून त्याला \'भारतरत्न\' पुरस्काराने (२०१४) सन्मानित करण्यात आले. देशाचा महान क्रिकेट खेळाडू म्हणून सर्वांनाच त्याचा अभिमान आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63028,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nहोळीचा सण','','','                                              होळीचा सण\nकोकणात इतर सणांप्रमाणे होळीचा सण मोठ्या उत्साहात साजरा केला जातो. यावर्षी होळीच्या सणासाठी मी माझ्या आजोळी गेलो होतो. गावची होळी पाहण्याचा आणि अनुभवण्याचा हा माझा पहिलाच प्रसंग होता. होळीच्या दिवशी अगदी सकाळपासूनच सारे गाव जय्यत तयारीला लागले.\nपहाटेपासूनच ग्रामदेवतेची पालखी गावात फिरत होती. याबरोबरच इतर खेळी किंवा सोंगे घरोघरी फिरत होती. संकासुर हा एक त्यातला प्रकार आहे. काळ्या रंगाचे कपडे, शंकूच्या आकाराची टोपी, लांब लांब सफेद दाढी-मिशा असे या संकासुराचे रूप घाबरवणारे होते. कमरेला बांधलेल्या घुंगरांचा आवाज करत तो घरोघरी नाचत होता आणि काही मुले त्याला चिडवण्यासाठी त्याचे घुंगरू ओढून धावत होते. त्यांना पकडण्यासाठी संकासूर त्यांच्या मागे हातात चाबूक घेऊन धावत जात होता. हा सारा खेळ खूपच मनोरंजक होता.\nपालखी नाचवण्याचा प्रकारही खूप सुंदर होता. दोघेजण पालखी खांदयावर घेऊन ढोल आणि सनईच्या तालावर वर्तुळाकार नाचताना पाहणे खूपच आनंददायी होते. गावातील होळीचा आणखी एक कार्यक्रम म्हणजे गावाच्या आजूबाजूची सुकलेली झाडे गोळा करून आणणे व होळी रचणे यातदेखील आम्ही मुलांनी खूप मजा केली. रात्री रचलेल्या होळीचे पूजन केले गेले. गावातील सर्वमंडळी पूजन सुरू असताना होळीभोवती नारळ घेऊन प्रदक्षिणा मारत होते. पूजा पूर्ण होताच मानकांच्या हस्ते होळी प्रज्वलित केली गेली. मी माझ्या हातातील नारळ होळीमध्ये अर्पण केला. वेगवेगळ्या प्रकारचे नृत्य यावेळी सादर केले गेले. मी पाहिलेला हा होळीचा सण खरोखरच अविस्मरणीय होता.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63029,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nआमचा क्रीडामहोत्सव','','','                                            आमचा क्रीडामहोत्सव\nआमच्या शाळेत यावर्षी साजरा झालेला क्रीडामहोत्सव खरोखरच अविस्मरणीय असा होता. शाळेच्या क्रीडांगणावर सारे विदयार्थी आपापल्या संघाचे रंगीत कपडे घालून रांगेत उभे होते. बँड पथकाच्या विद्यार्थ्यांनी सुंदर संगीताच्या साथीने आलेल्या मान्यवरांचे स्वागत केले. शाळेच्या मैदानावर जणू इंद्रधनुष्य अवतरले होते. मान्यवरांनी आणि प्रमुख पाहुण्यांनी दीपप्रज्वलन करून क्रीडामहोत्सवाचा शुभारंभ केला.\nमी धावण्याच्या स्पर्धेत सहभागी झालो होतो. वेगवेगळ्या ठिकाणी वेगवेगळ्या स्पर्धा सुरू होत्या. पालक टाळ्या वाजवून विद्यार्थीवर्गाला प्रोत्साहन देत होते. धावण्याच्या स्पर्धेत मी दुसऱ्या क्रमाकांवर आलो. महोत्सवाच्या शेवटी आपल्याला बक्षीस मिळणार या विचारानेच मला अभिमान वाटत होता. मैदानी खेळांमध्ये कबड्डीचा सामना खूपच रंगतदार झाला. इयत्ता नववी व दहावीच्या खेळाडूंना कबड्डी खेळताना पाहून आमच्यातही जोश निर्माण झाला होता. आम्ही जोरजोराने ओरडून टाळ्या वाजवून आमच्या संघाला प्रोत्साहन देत होतो. उंच उडी, लांब उडी, थाळी फेक हे सारे क्रीडाप्रकार पाहून पुढील वर्षी आपणही यामध्ये सहभागी व्हायचे मी मनोमन ठरवून टाकले. क्रीडामहोत्सवाच्या समाप्तीपूर्वी मानवी मनोरे व कवायत यांची स्पर्धा घेण्यात आली. त्यामध्ये संगीताच्या साथीने सर्वच संघांनी उत्तम प्रात्यक्षिके सादर केली.\nसर्व स्पर्धा संपल्यानंतर अल्पोपाहार आणि बक्षीस वितरण सोहळा पार पडला. साऱ्या स्पर्धकांच्या चेहऱ्यावर आनंद ओसंडून वाहत होता. क्रीडामहोत्सवाचा दिवस म्हणजे फक्त खेळ आणि खेळ ! पुढच्या वर्षीचा क्रीडामहोत्सव येण्याची वाट बघणे आमच्या हातात होते; मात्र झालेला क्रीडामहोत्सव खूप आनंददायी आठवणी देऊन गेला.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63030,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nमी पाहिलेले प्रदर्शन','','','                                                 मी पाहिलेले प्रदर्शन\nआमच्या सोसायटीमध्ये या वर्षी २७ फेब्रुवारीला मराठी भाषा दिनानिमित्त पुस्तक प्रदर्शन आयोजित करण्यात आले होते. लहान थोरांना मराठी भाषेतील उत्तम साहित्य माहित व्हावं हा यामागील मूळ हेतू होता. सोसायटीच्या आवारात विविध प्रकारच्या पुस्तकांचे स्टॉल लावण्यात आले होते. विविध लेखक, लेखिका, कवी, कवयित्री यांची पुस्तके विक्री व प्रदर्शनासाठी मांडण्यात आली होती. हजारो पुस्तकांचा तो खजिना पाहून ज्ञानाच्या गुहेत आल्यासारखे वाटत होते. पुस्तकांचे प्रकार तरी किती ? छोटी, मोठी, लांब, रुंद, जाडी पुस्तके । रंगीबेरंगी चित्रांची, गुळगुळीत कागदाची पुस्तके. छोट्यांसाठी गोष्टींची पुस्तके, चित्रांची पुस्तके होती. शब्दकोडी, गणिताची मौजमजा, कविताची पुस्तकेही वेगवेगळ्या स्टॉलवर मांडलेली होती. मराठी भाषेतील नाटके, कादंबऱ्या, प्रवासवर्णने, कथासंग्रह, कवितासंग्रह अशी अनेक प्रकारची पुस्तके प्रदर्शनात ठेवलेली होती. या पुस्तक प्रदर्शनाचे वैशिष्ट्य म्हणजे अनेक वर्षांपूर्वीची मासिके, छोट्यांसाठीच्या चित्रकथा असलेली पुस्तके, चंपक, ठकठक, चांदोबा यांच्या जुन्या आवृत्त्या खास प्रदर्शनात ठेवण्यात आल्या होत्या. आम्ही प्रदर्शनात फिरताना, पुस्तके चाळताना वेळ कसा निघून गेला ते समजले नाही.\nया पुस्तक प्रदर्शनाचे खास वैशिष्ट्य म्हणजे पुस्तके वाचनासाठी केलेली खास व्यवस्था. पुस्तके केवळ पाहण्यासाठी नाही, तर वाचण्यासाठी उपलब्ध करून दिल्यामुळे आम्हांला अनेक छोट्या पुस्तकांच्या वाचनाचा आनंद घेता आला.\nया प्रदर्शनातून मी काही पुस्तके खरेदी केली. हे एक दिवसाचे पुस्तक प्रदर्शन खूपच आनंददायी होते आणि त्यामुळेच अजून काही दिवस ते असेच सुरू राहावे अशी इच्छा आम्हां बालगोपाळांच्या मनात निर्माण झाली.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63031,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nमाझा आवडता खेळ','','','                                          माझा आवडता खेळ\nमला सारेच खेळ खेळायला आवडतात; पण माझा सगळ्यात आवडता खेळ क्रिकेट आहे. क्रिकेट हा असा खेळ आहे जो गल्लीपासून अगदी आंतरराष्ट्रीय स्तरापर्यंत तेवढ्याच उत्साहाने आणि स्पर्धात्मक वृत्तीने खेळला जातो. साधारणपणे मोकळ्या मैदानात खेळल्या जाणाऱ्या या खेळाची अनेक रूपे आपण अनुभवू शकतो. कसोटी क्रिकेट, एकदिवसीय क्रिकेट, वीस षटकांचे क्रिकेट हे आंतरराष्ट्रीय स्तरावर मान्यताप्राप्त प्रकार आहेत; मात्र गल्लीत किंवा छोट्या मैदानावर खेळताना क्रिकेटचे स्वरूप अगदी एक टप्पा आऊट पासून षटकार मारला तर बाद अशाप्रकारे बदलते. एक चेंडू आणि बॅट असेल, तर आपल्या मित्राबरोबर हा खेळ कुठेही खेळता येतो. फलंदाजी, गोलंदाजी, क्षेत्ररक्षण ही क्रिकेटच्या खेळातील मूलभूत कौशल्ये आहेत.\nक्रिकेट खेळताना मला फलंदाजी करायला खूप आवडते. सचिन तेंडुलकर, रोहित शर्मा हे माझे आवडते फलंदाज आहेत. त्यांच्याप्रमाणे षटकार, चौकार मारण्याचा सराव मी खेळताना करतो. क्रिकेट खेळताना छान व्यायाम होतो. धावा घेण्यासाठी तसेच क्षेत्ररक्षण करताना पळावे लागते. तसेच गोलंदाजी करताना चांगला व्यायाम होतो. चेंडू मारण्यासाठी हात, पाय, डोळे, यांचा योग्य समन्वय साधावा लागतो. त्यामुळे, फलंदाजी करताना एकाग्रता साधण्याची सवय लागते.\nमला क्रिकेट खेळण्याबरोबरच क्रिकेटचे सामने दूरदर्शनवर पहायलासुद्धा आवडतात. आनंद देणारा आणि शारीरिक व्यायाम देणारा हा खेळ मला खूप आवडतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63032,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nमाझा आवडता प्राणी','','                                                   माझा आवडता प्राणी\nआपल्या आजूबाजूला अनेक प्राणी आपल्याला दिसतात. काही पाळीव प्राणी आहेत, तर काही जंगलात राहणारे प्राणी आहेत. यामध्ये मला घोडा हा प्राणी खूप आवडतो. घोडा हा अगदी पुरातन काळापासून माणसाचा मित्र आहे. आपली ओझी वाहण्यापासून ते युद्धात मदत करण्यापर्यंत घोड्याचा उपयोग होत होता. घोडा हा एक शक्तिशाली प्राणी आहे. त्याच्यावर बसून फेरफटका मारण्यात एक वेगळाच आनंद निळतो. घोडागाडी किंवा घोड्याच्या रथातून सवारीची मजा केवळ अवर्णनीय असते. घोडा हा एक देखणा प्राणी आहे. पांढरेशुभ्र, काळेकुट्ट किंवा तांबड्या रंगाचे घोडे खूपच सुंदर दिसतात. त्यांच्या शेपटीचा झुबका त्यांच्या सौंदर्यात भर घालतो.\nआजही डोंगराळ भागात प्रवासाचे साधन म्हणून तसेच ओझे वाहण्यासाठी घोड्यांचा उपयोग केला जातो. गवत, चणे इत्यादी घाोड्याचे खाणे आहे. वेगवेगळ्या पर्यटनस्थळांवर आपल्याला घोड्याची सवारी करण्याची संधी मिळू शकते. काही ठिकाणी घोडे हे शेतीच्या कामासाठीदेखील वापरले जातात. उत्तम जातीच्या घोड्यांची किंमत खूप जास्त असते. घोडा पाळणे ही प्रतिष्ठेची गोष्ट समजली जाते. घोड्यांना पाहणे हा आनंददायी अनुभव असतो. जुन्या काळात युद्धात घोड्यांचा उपयोग होत असे. आजही पोलीस आणि सैन्यदलात घोड्यांचा वापर केला जातो. लग्नाच्या वरातीमध्ये सजवलेला घोडा खूप सुंदर दिसतो. घोड्यांच्या शर्यतीही लावल्या जातात. त्या पाहणे उत्कंठावर्धक असते.\nअतिशय ताकदवान, देखणा, ऐटदार आणि शांत असा हा प्राणी मला खूप आवडतो.','','',0,0,0,NULL),(63033,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nमाझी आई','','','                                                   माझी आई\nआपल्या कुटुंबाला एकत्र ठेवणारी, आपल्या बाळाची आणि कुटुंबातील प्रत्येकाची काळजी घेणारी असते ती म्हणजे आपली आई माझी आईदेखील अशीच आहे. माझ्यावर मनापासून प्रेम करणारी, घरातील सर्वांना हवं नको ते बघणारी, आलेल्या पाहुण्याचे हसत स्वागत करणारी आणि प्रत्येकाची काळजी घेणारी माझी आई नोकरी करते. मात्र नोकरी आणि कुटुंब या दोन्ही गोष्टी ती व्यवस्थित सांभाळते. मला शाळेसाठी तयार करणे, माझा जेवणाचा डबा हो तर तिची नियमित कामे आहेत. त्याचबरोबर स्वतः ची तयारी आणि बाबांना काय हवे नको तेही आई व्यवस्थिपणे सांभाळत असते. अष्टपैलू म्हणजे काय हे मला आईकडे पाहून लक्षात येते. तिचा कामे उरकण्याचा वेग प्रचंड आहे. कोणतीही कामे योग्य वेळेत पूर्ण करण्यावर तिचा भर असतो. नोकरीवरून आल्यानंतरही कधी कंटाळलेली, थकलेली आई मी पाहिलेली नाही\nआई जेवढी प्रेमळ आहे, तेवढीच हळवी आहे. घरामधे कोणी आजारी असेल, तर तिला चैन पडत नाही. अगदी खाण्यापिण्याच्या गोष्टींपासून वेळेवर औषधे देणे याकडे तिचा कटाक्ष असतो गरजूंना मदत करण्यासाठी ती नेहमीच तत्पर असते. माझ्या आईचा स्वभाव मनमोकळा आहे, मात्र कोणी खोटे बोललेले तिला अजिबात सहन होत नाही. चुकीच्या गोष्टीला प्रोत्साहन देणे तिच्या स्वभावात नाही. माझा अभ्यास, खेळ, छंद मला हव्या असलेल्या गोष्टी या साऱ्यांकडे तिचे बारकाईने लक्ष असते. एखाद्या मित्राने ज्या सहजपणे गप्पा माराव्यात तशा गप्पा आम्ही मारतो ती नेहमीच मला नवनवीन गोष्टी करण्यासाठी प्रोत्साहन देते. तिच्याकडून मला ऊर्जा मिळते.\nअशी माझी लाडकी आई मला खूप खूप आवडते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63034,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nमाझे आजोबा','','','                                           माझे आजोबा\nगावी गेल्यानंतर माझी सगळ्यात जास्त गट्टी कोणाशी जमते. तर ते माझे आजोबा. माझे आजोबा साठ वर्षांचे झाले आहेत. त्यांचे केस पूर्णपणे सफेद झाले आहेत; मात्र त्यांचा उत्साह आणि खेळकर स्वभाव तसाच आहे. आजही ते माझ्यासोबत क्रिकेट खेळतात. आम्हा भावंडांना घेऊन जंगलात बोरं, करवदं गोळा करायला जातात. आजोबांना काम करण्याची प्रचंड आवड आहे. गावाकडच्या शेतात ते काही ना काही काम करत असतात. मळा करण्याचा त्यांचा छंद त्यांना स्वस्थ बसू देत नाही. आमच्या शेतात वेगवेगळ्या \'भाज्या, फुले, फळे यांचा मळा नेहमीच फुललेला असतो. माझ्या आजोबांना गाण्याची खूप आवड आहे. गणेशोत्सवात रात्री वेगवेगळी भजने ते अत्यंत मधुर आवाजात गात असतात. आम्ही सु‌ट्टीमध्ये गावाला गेलो, की आजोबांची धावपळ जवळून पाहतो. आम्हांला गावाकडील वेगवेगळे पदार्थ, रानमेवा खाऊ घालण्याची त्यांची धडपड असते. शहरात राहून शक्य नसलेल्या गोष्टी करण्यासाठी ते आम्हांला नेहमीच प्रोत्साहन देतात. झाडावर चढणे, पोहायला जाणे, डोंगरदऱ्या भटकणे, शेतातील कामे सारे काही आजोबा आमच्याकडून करून घेतात.\nमाझे आजोबा खूप प्रेमळ आहेत. मी त्यांना रागावलेले पाहिलेच नाही. सतत हसतखेळत असणारे आजोबा आपल्या विनोदी स्वभावामुळे आजूबाजूचे वातावरण नेहमीच आनंदी ठेवतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63035,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nआमची शाळा','','','                                            आमची शाळा\nआमच्या शाळेचे नाव ज्ञानविकास विद्यालय आहे. इयत्ता पहिली ते दहावीपर्यंतचे वर्ग माझ्या शाळेत भरतात. माझी शाळा ही मराठी माध्यमाची शाळा आहे. शाळेची इमारत भव्यदिव्य आहे. ती चहूबाजूंनी हिरवळीने नटलेली आहे. शाळेसमोर मोठे पटांगण आहे. या पटांगणात आमची रोजची सामुदायिक प्रार्थना व परिपाठ होतो. शारीरिक शिक्षणाच्या तासाला या पटांगणात आम्ही वेगवेगळे खेळ खेळतो.\nमाझ्या शाळेतील शिक्षक अतिशय प्रेमळ आहेत. ते आमची खूप चांगल्या प्रकारे काळजी घेतात. त्यांची प्रत्येक विषय शिकविण्याची पद्धत खूपच चांगली आहे. माझ्या शाळेत दरवर्षी स्नेहसंमेलन आणि क्रीडामहोत्सव साजरा केला जातो. यावेळी सर्व विदयार्थी मोठ्या उत्साहाने यामध्ये सहभागी होतात. शाळेची शैक्षणिक सहल ही आम्हां मुलांसाठी मोठी पर्वणीच असते. शिक्षक आणि विद्यार्थी एकत्र येऊन मोठी मौजमजा करतात. माझ्या शाळेतील कर्मचारी खूपच मेहनती आहेत, शाळेची स्वच्छता अतिशय काटेकोरपणे ठेवण्यात त्यांचा मोठा वाटा असतो. शाळेतील शिस्तप्रिय शिक्षकवर्गामुळे विदयार्थी प्रत्येक गोष्ट वेळच्या वेळी व नियमाप्रमाणे करतात. शाळेचा इयत्ता दहावीचा निकालही उत्तम लागतो. आमच्या शाळेतील अनेक माजी विद्यार्थी अनेक क्षेत्रांमध्ये मोठमोठ्या पदावर विराजमान आहेत. अशा शाळेत मी शिक्षण घेतो याचा मला मनापासून अभिमान वाटतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63036,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nप्रदूषण','','','                                                  प्रदूषण\nमाणसाने प्रत्येक क्षेत्रात प्रगती केली आहे; मात्र त्याचबरोबर निसर्गाला त्रासदायक अशा काही गोष्टी त्याने निर्माण केल्या आहेत. या गोष्टींमुळे आपल्याला प्रदूषण झालेले दिसून येते. साधारणपणे जलप्रदूषण, वायूप्रदूषण, मृदाप्रदूषण, ध्वनिप्रदूषण असे प्रदूषणाचे विविध प्रकार आहेत. निसर्गाला घातक असे उत्पादन केल्यामुळे निसर्गाचा समतोल बिघडतो व पर्यावरणाचे नुकसान होते यालाच आपण प्रदूषण म्हणतो.\nकचरा ही माणसापुढे असलेली सर्वांत मोठी समस्या आहे. अनेक वस्तूंचे विघटन होत नाही किंवा त्याला खूप काळ लागतो. अशा वस्तू जमिनीत पडून राहिल्यास ती जमीन नापीक बनते. अशा वस्तू जलप्रवाहात गेल्यास ते पाणी प्रदूषित होते. यामध्ये प्रामुख्याने प्लॅस्टिक व इलेक्ट्रॉनिक वस्तूंच्या कचऱ्याचा समावेश होतो. प्लॅस्टिकमुळे पृथ्वीवर झालेले प्रदूषण अतिशय गंभीर स्वरूपाचे आहे. कारखान्यात निर्माण होणाऱ्या उत्पादनानंतर तेथील रसायने जलप्रवाहात सोडण्यात येतात. त्यामुळे, नदी, नाले, ओढे, प्रदूषित होतात. अनेक ठिकाणी जलचर मरण पावतात. माणसाला व इतर सजीवांना आवश्यक असलेले शुद्ध पाणी नष्ट होते. वाहनांचे भोगे, ध्वनिक्षेपक, फटाके इत्यादी घटकांमुळे ध्वनिप्रदूषण होते. यांचा परिणाम मानवाच्या आरोग्यावर होतो.\nप्रदूषणाचा अजून एक प्रकार म्हणजे वायू प्रदूषण. माणसाने कारखान्यातून सोडलेले विषारी वायू वातावरणात पसरतात व त्याचा परिणाम माणसाच्या आरोग्यावर होतो. वाहनांची वाढती संख्या व त्यातून बाहेर पडणाऱ्या विषारी वायूंमुळे वायू प्रदूषणाची समस्या गंभीर बनली आहे. वातावरणातील विषारी वायू शोषून घेणारी झाडे माणसाने तोडल्यामुळे वायूप्रदूषण वाढते. याचबरोबर मोठमोठे मोबाइल टॉवर बनवल्यामुळे छोट्या छोट्या पक्ष्यांना आपले प्राण गमवावे लागले. प्रदूषण ही माणसासाठी अतिशय घातक गोष्ट आहेच, त्याचबरोबर पृथ्वीवरच्या सजीवसृष्टीसाठीदेखील हे भयानक आहे. त्यामुळे, माणसाने विचारपूर्वक वागून प्रदूषण रोखले तरच पर्यावरणाचा हास थांबू शकेल आणि पुढची पिढी पृथ्वीवर मोकळा श्वास घेऊ शकेल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63037,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nस्वच्छतेचे महत्त्व','','','                                      स्वच्छतेचे महत्त्व\nस्वच्छता ही माणसाच्या आरोग्याच्या दृष्टीने अतिशय महत्त्वाची गोष्ट आहे. स्वच्छतेची सुरुवात नेहमी स्वतः पासून करावी असे म्हटले जाते. स्वतःपासून सुरुवात करायची म्हणजे आपल्या शरीराची आपण स्वच्छता राखणे आवश्यक आहे. आपण वेळच्यावेळी अंघोळ करणे आवश्यक आहे. बाहेरून आल्यावर हात, पाय, तोंड व्यवस्थित धुणे निरोगी आयुष्यासाठी गरजेचे आहे. आपली नखे बाढू देऊ नयेत. दात स्वच्छ करावेत. योग्यवेळी केस कापावेत. जेवल्यानंतर तोड, हात स्वच्छ धुवावेत.\nजशी शरीराची स्वच्छता महत्त्वाची आहे त्याचप्रमाणे आपल्या आजूबाजूची स्वच्छता राखली पाहिजे. आपली अभ्यासाची जागा, झोपण्याची जागा स्वच्छ असावी. जेवल्यानंतर आपल्या ताटाबाहेर सांडलेले अन्न आपण स्वतः उचलावे. घरात केरकचरा करू नये. शाळेत, वर्गात स्वच्छता राखली पाहिजे रस्त्यावर कचरा करू नये. कुठेही धुंकू नये. कचराकुंडीचा वापर करावा. परिसर स्वच्छ असेल, तर दुर्गंधी पसरणार नाही. रोगराई पसरणार नाही.\nस्वच्छता असेल, तर आपला परिसर सुंदर दिसतो. काम करण्यासाठी सकारात्मक ऊर्जा मिळते. रोगराई दूर राहते. स्वच्छता असेल, तर अनेक साथीचे रोग आटोक्यात येतात. प्रत्येकाने स्वच्छता पाळलीच पाहिजे. आपले शरीर, घर, आवार, गाव, विहीर, नदी, रस्ते, स्वच्छ ठेवणे हे आपले कर्तव्य आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63038,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nदुष्काळ','','','                                                 दुष्काळ\nहल्ली दुष्काळ हा शब्द साऱ्यांच्या परिचयाचा झाला आहे. वातावरणातील बदलांमुळे हे संकट वारंवार ओढावताना दिसते. या दुष्काळाचे दोन प्रकार आहेत ते म्हणजे ओला दुष्काळ आणि सुका दुष्काळ.\nदुष्काळ पडतो म्हणजे काय तर पर्यावरणाचा समतोल बिघडतो. अन्नधान्याची कमतरता निर्माण होणे म्हणजे दुष्काळाची परिस्थिती होय. दुष्काळाची परिस्थिती निर्माण होण्यासाठी माणूस जबाबदार आहे. आपल्या आजूबाजूला असलेल्या नैसर्गिक साधनसंपत्तीचा आपण विनाश केला, की आपोआपच दुष्काळाची परिस्थिती ओढवते.\nओला दुष्काळ म्हणजे अतिपावसामुळे महापूर येऊन शेती, धान्य नष्ट होते. शेतजमीन नापीक बनते. सुका दुष्काळ फार भयावह असतो. पाऊस न पडल्यामुळे शेतजमीन पूर्णपणे सुकून जाते. जमिनीला तडे पडतात. पिण्यासाठी, इतर वापरासाठी पाणी मिळत नाही. माणसांबरोबर गुरेढोरे, पशुपक्षी पाण्याविना तडफडू लागतात. दुष्काळग्रस्त भागातील लोक आपले घरदार सोडून शहराकडे रवाना होतात.\nमाणसाला जगण्यासाठी सर्वात जास्त आवश्यक घटक म्हणजे पाणी तेच न मिळाल्यामुळे माणसे, पशुपक्षी कासावीस होतान अनेकजण यामध्ये मरण पावतात. पाऊस न पडण्याची अनेक कारणे आहेत. ज्यामध्ये माणसाने केलेली अमर्याद जंगलतोडही सामील आहे. पाण्याचा भरमसाट वापर, सिमेंटचे निर्माण झालेले जंगल, दिवसेदिवस वाढत चाललेले प्रदूषण, यांमुळे दुष्काळाची परिस्थिती निर्माण होते. आपण निसर्गाचे संवर्धन केले नाही, तर हे वारंवार होत राहील आणि सजीव सृष्टी धोक्यात येईल; म्हणून पर्यावरणाचे रक्षण केले पाहिजे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63039,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nआपली मातृभाषा मराठी','','','                                आपली मातृभाषा मराठी\nआपण व्यवहारासाठी वेगवेगळ्या भाषा बोलतो मात्र आपल्या घरी जी भाषा बोलली जाते ती आपली मातृभाषा असते. आपण महाराष्ट्रात राहतो आणि मराठी ही आपली मातृभाषा आहे. मराठी भाषेला खूप मोठा इतिहास आहे. आपण जी मराठी भाषा नेहमीच्या व्यवहारात वापरतो ती प्रमाणभाषा असली तरी मराठी भाषेच्या अनेक बोली आहेत.\nमहाराष्ट्रात प्रत्येक कोसावर मराठी भाषेच्या लहेजा बदलत जातो. कोकणात बोलली जाणारी कोकणी, मालवणी, आगरी, बाणकोटी अशा मराठी भाषेच्या बोली आहेत. तर पुण्याकडची, विदर्भातील, वहाडी, कोल्हापुरी, अहिराणी, कारवारी, नागपुरी. खानदेशी, झाडीवोली अशा अनेक बोली आढळतात.\nभारतातील प्राचीन भाषांपैकी एक असलेल्या मराठी भाषेवर संस्कृत, प्राकृत भाषेचा पगडा आहे. आधुनिक मराठी भाषेमध्ये अनेक भाषांमधील शब्द समाविष्ट झालेले दिसून येतात. आपल्या मराठी भाषेत विपुल प्रमाणात साहित्य लेखन झालेले आहे. ज्ञानेश्वरांनी ज्ञानेश्वरीमध्ये म्हटल्याप्रमाणे अमृतातेही पैजा जिंकणारी आपली मराठी जगभरात पोहोचली आहे. आपणही आपल्या मातृभाषेचा आदर ठेवला पाहिजे. मातृभाषेत लिहिणे, वाचणे, बोलणे आवश्यक आहे. ती टिकवून ठेवणे आणि तिला योग्य तो सन्मान मिळवून देण्यासाठी आपण तिचा वापर आपल्या दैनंदिन जीवनात करणे आवश्यक आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63040,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Invented printing press','','','Johannes Gutenberg','',0,0,0,NULL),(63041,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Empire of Ottoman Turks','','','Byzantine','',0,0,0,NULL),(63042,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The Bill which laid limitations on powers of the King in England.','','','Bill of Rights','',0,0,0,NULL),(63043,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Mughal Emperor who gave permission to Britishers to establish factory at Surat.','','','Emperor Jahangir','',0,0,0,NULL),(63044,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Came to be known as \'World Factory\'.','','','England','',0,0,0,NULL),(63045,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which period in the history of Europe is known as Period of Renaissance?','','','In the history of Europe, the last phase of medieval period i.e. 13th to 16th century in Europe is known as the Period of Renaissance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63046,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the developments that laid the foundation of modern era.','','','The Renaissance, the Reformation and the geographical discoveries laid the foundation of modern era.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63047,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the significance of the invention of printing press.','','','It became possible to take new ideas, new concepts and knowledge to all the sections of society due to the invention of the printing press.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63048,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the effect of the Reformation movement in the area of religion?','','','Individual freedom and rationalism gained importance in the area of religion due to the Reformation movement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63049,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the principles that the French Revolution gave to the world.','','','The French Revolution gave the principles of liberty, equality and fraternity to the world.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63050,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the British establish East India Company?','','','The British established the East India Company to carry out trade in India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63051,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the outcome of the American War of Independence?','','','Due to American War of Independence, a new nation known as the United States of America with a federal government, written constitution and based on the principles of democracy was formed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63052,1939,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Complete the table : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Sailor</th>\n    <th>Contribution</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. ---------</td>\n    <td>Reached the tip of the African continent, now known as the \'Cape of Good Hope\'.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Christopher Columbus</td>\n<td>---------</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>----------</td>\n<td>Sailed passed the Cape of Good Hope and reached the West coast of India at Calicut port.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Sailor</th>\n    <th>Contribution</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Bartolomeu Dias</td>\n    <td>Reached the tip of the African continent, now known as the \'Cape of Good Hope\'.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Christopher Columbus</td>\n<td>Set on his journey to reach India but reached on the eastern coast of American continent.</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Vasco-da-Gama</td>\n<td>Sailed passed the Cape of Good Hope and reached the West coast of India at Calicut port.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63053,1939,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Colonialism','','','1) Exploitation of one country by another and making it into a colony is called Colonialism.\n2) On the basis of economic and military strength one country occupies region of another country and establishes its political supremacy.\n3) Many European nations established their colonies in various parts of the continents of America, Asia, Africa and Australia.\n4) They grabbed the power by using force to exploit the colonies systematically.\nThis gave rise to Imperialism.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63054,1939,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Imperialism','','','1) Imperialism means a powerful country controls other country by establishing its overall domination.\n2) The objective of imperialism is to establish more and more colonies.\n3) European countries with strong military power and imperialist aspirations enslaved the countries in Asia and Africa.\n4) Imperialism gained momentum after industrial revolution and came to an end in twentieth century.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63055,1939,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Age of Renaissance','','','1) Renaissance literally means rebirth or revival. In the latter phase of medieval Europe, reformation, religious reform movement and geographical discoveries gained momentum.\n2) It was a comprehensive movement which touched all aspects of human life. The Greek and Roman traditions in art, architecture and philosophy were revived in the Age of Renaissance.\n3) Humanitarianism got great impetus.\n4) Renaissance inspired all round progress and ushered a new era in the history of world.\n5) This developments took place from 13th century to 16th century in Europe. This period is known as the Age of Renaissance.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63056,1939,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Capitalism','','','1) Many traders participated in the competition for trade with Asia after the discovery of the new sea routes to the east.\n2) As it was not possible to carry out trade single-handedly, it was decided by the traders to pool their resources to raise capital for trade.\n3) Many trading companies like the East India Company were formed.\n4) Trade with Oriental countries was very profitable and responsible for economic prosperity.\n5) The ruling powers gave protection and trade concessions to these companies.\n6) This led to the accumulation of wealth in Europe. This wealth was used as capital for trade and commerce which gave rise to capitalism in Europe.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63057,1939,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Leonardo-da-Vinci','','','1) Leonardo-da-Vinci is regarded as an all round personality of the Renaissance period.\n2) He was well versed with the different branches of science and art.\n3) He had mastery over varied subjects such as mathematics, astronomy, etc. sculpture, architecture, engineering, music, astronomy etc.\n4) But he became world famous as a painter.\n5) His paintings \'Monalisa\' and \'The Last Supper\' became immortal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63058,1939,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Carnatic Wars','','','1) There was competition among the European nationals to secure monopoly of trading rights in India.\n2) Due to this three Carnatic wars were fought between England and France.\n3) England defeated France in the third Carnatic War.\n4) After this, there was no strong European competitor for British East India Company in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63059,1939,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Battle of Buxar ','','','1) Mir Kasim was replaced by Mir Jafar when he tried to put restrictions on illegal practices of the British.\n2) Mir Kasim, the deposed Nawab of Bengal, Shuja-ud-Daulah, the Nawab of Ayodhya and Mughal Emperor Shah Alam formed an alliance.\n3) They undertook a campaign to restrain the activities of the British in Bengal.\n4) They were defeated by the British in the Battle of Buxar in 1764 in Bihar.\n5) The Treaty of Allahabad was signed after the Battle of Buxar. The British secured the right to collect revenue from Bengal province by this treaty.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63060,1939,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Siraj-ud-Daulah was defeated in the Battle of Plassey.','','','1) The officers of the British East India Company misused the trade concessions and built fortification around the factory in Kolkata.\n2) Siraj-ud-Daulah captured the factory at Kolkata.\n3) This created discontent in England.\n4) Robert Clive diplomatically bribed Mir Jafar, Commander in Chief of Nawab\'s army and promised to make him Nawab of Bengal.\n5) So, the Army of Siraj-ud-Daulah under the command of Mir Kasim did not join the battle against the British at Plassey in 1757 AD.\n6) Thus, the British won the battle of Plassey by treachery and deceit.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63061,1939,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The European countries felt the necessity of finding new sea routes to Asia.','','','1) The Ottoman Turks captured Constantinople, the capital of Byzantine Empire, in 1453 AD.\n2) All land trade routes joining Asia and Europe passed through this city.\n3) It stopped the flow of valuable goods from the countries in the east.\nTherefore, the European countries felt the necessity of finding new sea routes to Asia.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63062,1939,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','European rulers started giving military protection and trade concessions to the trading companies.','','','1) With the discovery of new sea routes to the east, trade with oriental untries became very profitable. Thus, the European trade prospered.\n2) Competition for trade began among many European nations.\n3) The traders pooled their resources and formed trading companies like the East India Company.\n4) As this trade brought economic prosperity, the European rulers started giving military protection and trade concessions to these trading companies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63063,1939,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The period between 18th and 19th century came to be known as \'Age of Revolutions\'.','','','1) The British Parliament passed The Bill of Rights in 1689. It established sovereignty of Parliament and curtailed powers of the King.\n2) This gave momentum to the development of Parliamentary Democracy in England.\n3) The thirteen British colonies in America won the War of Independence in 1783.\n4) These thirteen colonies established the first federal republic, known as the United States of America.\n5) The people of France revolted against the unjust and uncontrolled monarchy and feudalism. They established a Republic.\n6) The industrial revolution in Europe in the latter part of the 18th century brought revolutionary changes in Europe.\n7) These changes had long lasting effect on the entire world.\n8) All these revolutionary events took place during 18th and 19th century period.\nHence it is known as the \'Age of Revolutions\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63064,1939,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','England was described as \'World Factory.\'','','','1) There were many revolutionary changes in the latter part of the 18th century in Europe.\n2) Many technological innovations in Europe gave enormous outputs.\n3) Goods were produced with the help of machines running on steam and later on electricity. It was the dawn of the age of machines.\n4) Though the industrial revolution started in England it gradually spread to other parts of the western world.\n5) During this period, England enjoyed industrial prosperity and was described as World Factory.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63065,1939,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British captured Sindh in 1843.','','','1) The British were afraid of Russian aggression Afghanistan. on India via Afghanistan.\n2) They decided to bring Afghanistan under their control to ensure safety of their Indian Empire.\n3) The province of Sindh was located at North west frontier.\n4) All routes to Afghanistan passed through Sindh.\n5) Realising its strategic importance, the British captured Sindh in 1843. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63066,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nखेळांचे महत्त्व','','','                                                खेळांचे महत्त्व\nप्रत्येकाला कोणता ना कोणता खेळ आवडतोच. काही जणांना खेळ खेळायला आवडतात, तर काहींना केवळ पहायला आवडतात. खेळ बैठे असू दे किंवा मैदानी, एकट्याने खेळण्याचे असू देत किंवा सांघिक, प्रत्येक खेळाचे काही ना काही महत्त्व है असतेच. बैठे खेळ म्हटले तर शारीरिक हालचाली होत नाहीत; मात्र आपल्या बुद्धीचा व्यायाम नक्की होतो. एकाग्रता वाढते. डावपेच आखणे, ते अंमलात आणणे याची सवय लागते. मैदानी खेळ तर आरोग्याच्या दृष्टीने महत्त्वाचे आहेत. आपले शरीर सुदृढ राहण्यासाठी, लवचिक बनण्यासाठी, शरीराचा व्यायाम आवश्यक असतो. विविध मैदानी खेळातून हा व्यायाम साधला जातो.\nखेळामुळे आपला आळस दूर होतो, आनंद मिळतो आणि ऊर्जा निर्माण होते. सांघिक खेळाचे वैशिष्ट्य म्हणजे आपल्यात संघ भावना निर्माण होते. निर्णयक्षमता वाढते आणि महत्त्वाचे म्हणजे विजय व पराभव स्वीकारण्याची मानसिकता निर्माण होते. मनाची एकाग्रता, सतर्कता, योग्य वेळी योग्य हालचाली, समयसूचकता, खंबीरता, कधीही हार न मानण्याची वृत्ती, शरीराच्या अवयवांचा समन्वय या साऱ्या गोष्टी आपण खेळातून आपोआप शिकत असतो. लहान मुलांपासून मोठ्या माणसांपर्यंत प्रत्येकाला खेळ आनंद देत असतो म्हणून आपण कोणता ना कोणता खेळ खेळणे आवश्यक आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63067,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nपुस्तकाचे आत्मकयन','','                                              पुस्तकाचे आत्मकथन\nमला ओळखता, की विसरून गेलात? तुमच्या हातात मोबाइल आला आणि माझ्याकडे तुमचं पूर्ण दुर्लक्ष झालं. तुमच्या लहानपणीचा सोबती मी ! तुमचं गोष्टींचं पुस्तक बोलतोय. आपली मैत्री तशी जुनीच आहे. अगदी तुम्हांला लिहता-वाचता येत नव्हतं तेव्हापासूनची. तुम्ही माझ्यातल्या गोष्टी ऐकत मोठे झालात. त्यावेळी माझे रंगीत रूप आणि आकर्षक चित्रे पाहून सतत तुम्ही मला आपल्या हातात घेत होतात. तुम्ही जसे मोठे झालात तसा मी तुम्हांला अधिक आवडू लागलो, कारण तुम्ही आता माझ्यातील गोष्टी वाचू शकत होतात. तुम्हांला वाचताना पाहून मलादेखील खूप आनंद होत असे. माझ्यामुळे तुम्हांला नवीन काहीतरी शिकायला मिळते याचे मला समाधान वाटत असे.\nहळूहळू मी तुमच्या कपाटात बसलो तो कायमचा माझ्यावर धूळ चढली. माझी पाने जीर्ण झाली आहेत. कुठे शिलाई उसवली आहे, तर चित्रांचे रंग उडाले आहेत. कित्येक वर्षांत तुम्ही मला पाहिलेले नाही. पाहणार तरी कसे म्हणा! आता तुम्हांला प्रत्येक गोष्ट मोबाइलवर मिळते. वाचता येते. पाहता येते आणि ऐकताही येते. त्यामुळे, आम्हां पुस्तकांचे महत्त्व तुमच्या दृष्टीने खूप कमी झाले आहे आम्ही एकदा अडगळीत पडलो, की परत काही बाहेर पडत नाही. काळ बदलला आहे हे खरे आहे पण आम्हांला असं दूर सारू नका. आजही आम्ही तुम्हाला तेवढाच आनंद देतो माझी धूळ झटका. मला मोकळा श्वास घेऊ क्या येणाऱ्या पिढीला वाचनाचा आनंद देऊन त्यांच्या चेहऱ्यावरील समाधान पाहण्याची माझी खूप इच्छा आहे. कराल ना माझ्यासाठी एवढं तरी ?                                         ','','',0,0,0,NULL),(63068,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nशाळेचे आत्मकथन','','','                                          शाळेचे आत्मकथन\nखूप छान वाटत रे, मुलांनो, तुम्ही येता आणि माझ्यात एक जिवंतपणा येतो. एवढा आनंद होतो मला! अरे, ओळखलं नाही का? मी तुमची शाळा बोलतेय तुम्हांला आठवतो का तुमचा शाळेतील पहिला दिवस? तुम्ही रडवेल्या चेहऱ्याने माझ्या पायऱ्या चढला होतात. आईला सोडून इकडे यायला तुम्ही अजिबात तयार नव्हतात; पण आज तुम्हांला येथे हसताना, खेळताना पाहते तेव्हा मला समाधान वाटतं. तुम्ही माझ्या सहवासात मोठे होताना पाहण्यात मला खूप आनंद होतो. कोरोना काळात खऱ्या अर्थाने मी एकटी पडले होते. तुम्ही मुलं नाहीत, की शिक्षक नाहीत. शिपाई दादाही नाहीत. सारे कसं शांत मला त्या दिवसांत भीती वाटायची. आजही आठवण आली, की अंगावर काटा येतो.\nतुम्ही येता, खेळता, बागडता, शिक्षण घेता. तुमच्या आवाजाने माझ्यातही ऊर्जा निर्माण होते. तुमच्या गप्पा खेळ, जिंकल्यानंतरचा जल्लोष, हरल्यानंतरची नाराजी सारं मी पाहते. तुमच्या खोड्या आणि दंगामस्तीदेखील माझ्या नजरेतून सुटत नाही बरे का! तुम्ही येथे यावे, शिकावे, या देशाचे उत्तम नागरिक बनावे हीच माझी इच्छा असते. केवळ अभ्यासातच नाही, तर जीवनाच्या प्रत्येक क्षेत्रात तुम्ही नाव कमवावे आणि सोबतच माझेही नाव मोठं व्हावं हीच अपेक्षा आहे माझी.\nजशी मी तुमच्यासाठी महत्त्वाची आहे, तशीच तुम्ही मुलं आहात, म्हणून माझ्या असण्याला अर्थ आहे. तुम्ही प्रगती करा, मोठे व्हा; पण मला कधीही विसरू नका. कधीतरी मला भेटायला आलेले माझे माजी विदयार्थी पाहून आनंदाश्रू येतात माझ्या डोळ्यांतून. खूप अभ्यास करा बाळांनो. खूप मौजमजा करा. माझ्या शुभेच्छा आणि आशीर्वाद सतत तुमच्यासोबत आहेतच.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63069,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nमोवाइलचे आत्मकयन','','','                                          मोवाइलचे आत्मकयन\nनमस्कार, मी एक मोबाइल बोलतोय तुमच्या साऱ्यांच्या आवडीचा मोबाइल मी तंत्रज्ञानाचा एक भाग असून तुम्हा सर्वांच्या जीवनाचा अविभाज्य घटक झालो आहे माझा जन्म काही कुशल कारागिरांच्या कल्पनेतून आणि प्रयत्नाने झाला. मी तयार झाल्यानंतर मला आकर्षक खोक्यात ठेवले गेले आणि एका दुकानात मी विक्रीसाठी मांडला गेलो एका तरुणाने मला विकत घेतले. मला पाहून तो खूपच आनंदी दिसत होता माझा वापर करून गप्पा मारणे, समाजमाध्यमांवर भेट देणे, फोटो काढणे या गोष्टीमध्ये तो सतत व्यस्त असायचा.\nपुढे त्याने माझा अतिवापर सुरू केला माझ्यावर तो एवढे खेळ खेळायचा की मी खूपच गरम होत असे थोड्याच दिवसात तो मला कंटाळला. त्याने मला त्याच्या लहान बहिणीच्या स्वाधीन केले, कारण त्याला नव्या पद्धतीचा दुसरा मोवाइल मिळाला होता त्याची बहीण मला कसेही वापरत असे कित्येक वेळा तिच्या हातातून पडल्यामुळे मला जखमा झाल्या. मग मी बंदच पडलो आज माझी अशी अवस्था आहे, की जुना मोबाइल म्हणून माझ्याकडे कोणी पाहतसुद्धा नाही. माझी कोणाला गरजच उरलेली नाही भविष्यात माझ्यासाठी काय लिहून ठेवले आहे ते माहीत नाही; मात्र झपाट्याने बदलणाऱ्या या तंत्रज्ञानाच्या युगात आज आणलेली वस्तू दुसऱ्या दिवशी कालबाह्य झाल्यासारखी वाटते हे मी स्वत अनुभवले आहे मी आजही मला शक्य त्या पद्धतीने आपली सेवा करण्यास तयार आहे; मात्र कोणी माझ्याकडे लक्ष देत नाही. मित्रांनो, कोणत्याही गोष्टीचा अतिरेक वाईटच ! माझा आवश्यक तेथे वापर करायला हवाय. पण त्यांच्या अतिवापराने तुमच्या आरोग्यावर विपरित परिणाम होतो हे लक्षात घ्या आणि माझा, माझ्या भावंडांचा प्रमाणात वापर करा.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63071,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nबैलाचे आत्मकयन','','','                                                 बैलाचे आत्मकचन\nनमस्कार मित्रांनो ! मी शेतकऱ्यांचा मित्र बोलतोय. ओळखले ना मला? हो, मी बैल बोलतोय. शेतकऱ्याच्या सुखदु खात त्याची सोबत करणारा, त्याचा सखा ऊन, वारा, पाऊस, थंडी काही असू दे. वर्षभर शेतात राबणारा मी बैल आहे या शेतकऱ्याच्या घरीच माझा जन्म झाला. लहानपणी माझे खूप लाड झाले; पण जसा मी तरुण झालो मला कामाला जुंपले गेले. मी गाडी ओढतो. कितीही वजन असू दे मी ते घेऊन जातो. काम करताना माझी कोणतीही चिडचिड नसते तरीही कधी कधी पाठीवर आसूड बसतोच.\nशेताच्या कामात तर वर्षभर माझी साथ असते. नांगरणी असो वा पेरणी किंवा कापणी आणि मळणी मी नेहमीच कामासाठी तत्परतेने उभा असतो.\nवर्षभर काम केल्यानंतर एक दिवस मात्र आम्हांला आराम मिळतो. हो. बैलपोळ्याला आमचे मालक आम्हांला सजवतात. कपाळावर वाशिंग बांधतात. शिंगांना वेगड लावतात. पाठीवर झुली घातल्या जातात. पुरणपोळीचा खास वेत आमच्यासाठी केला जातो. संपूर्ण गावातून वाजतगाजत आमची मिरवणूक निघते, तेव्हा मात्र जीवन सार्थकी लागल्यासारखे वाटते.\nतुम्ही बैलगाडा शर्यत आयोजित करता आणि जिंकण्यासाठी आमच्या पाठीवर आसूड ओढता. आमच्यासाठी हे खूपच वेदनादायी असते. तुम्ही तुमच्या मनोरंजनासाठी आमचा जीव पणाला लावू नका. आम्ही तुमच्या मदतीसाठी नेहमीच तयार आहोत आणि भविष्यातही राहणार आहोत. जरी यंत्रांनी आमची जागा घेतली असली तरी जेथे यंत्र पोहोचू शकत नाहीत अशा अवघड जागी आम्ही शेतकऱ्यांच्या बरोबर असतो. चला माझी शेतावर जायची वेळ झाली.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63072,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the Religious Reform Movement.','','','1) The independent intellectuals attacked the old religious ideologies and beliefs of Roman Catholic Church.\n2) The Christian priests used to give undue importance to religious rites and practices by taking advantage of the ignorance of the people.\n3) They robbed people in the name of religion.\n4) The movement which started against this is known Movement. as Religious Reform Movement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63074,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is Intellectual Revolution?','','','1) Due to Renaissance, the European society stepped out of past ignorance and blind faith.\n2) The pre-determined customs and traditions came to be seen from a critical point of view.\n3) These changes are addressed as Intellectual Revolution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63076,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the significance of French Revolution.','','','1) The French Revolution was the first event in world history in which the might of the people\'s power was witnessed.\n2) It had put an end to uncontrolled and unjust monarchy and the feudalism.\n3) France became a Republican state. It gave the world principles of liberty, equality and fraternity.\n4) The spread of democratic form of government began with the French Revolution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63077,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the changes brought about by the Industrial Revolution in the system of production?','','','1) The latter part of 18th century saw many technological innovations in Europe.\n2) The production started with the help of machines running on steam.\n3) The big factories replaced cottage industries.\n4) Handlooms machines. were replaced by machines.\n5) New modes of transport like railways and steamer were available.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63079,2027,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील विषयांवर निबंध लिहा.\nझाडाच आत्मकयन','','','                                               झाडाचे आत्मकथन\nछान वाटतंय का माझ्या सावलीत ? दचकता कशाला? मी उन्हामध्ये सावली देणारा वटवृक्ष बोलतोय. माझ्या जन्माची कहाणी मोठी मजेशीर आहे. काही वर्षांपूर्वी म्हणजे जवळपास ८०-९० वर्षापूर्वीचा माझा जन्म आहे. मला कोणीही इथे आणून लावलं नाही, की माझी काळजी घेतली नाही. बीज म्हणून जमिनीत पडलेलो मी. पावसाचे पाणी मिळताच अंकुरलो आणि माझा जीवनप्रवास सुरू झाला.\nमाझ्या जीवनातील सुरुवातीचा काळ खूपच खडतर होता. मला पाणी मिळवण्यासाठी खूप कष्ट करावे लागले. माझी मुळे खूप खोलवर आणि दूरपर्यंत जमिनीत पसरली आहेत. मी स्वतः उन्हामध्ये उभा राहून तुम्हांला सावली देतो याचे मला समाधान आहे. माझ्या अंगाखांद्यांवर पक्षी त्यांची घरटी बनवतात. माकडे माझ्या फांट्यांवरून उड्या मारत माझी फळे खातात. माणसांप्रमाणेच अनेक प्राणी माझ्या आश्रयाला येतात. अनेक किडे, मुंग्या, कीटक यांचा मी आश्रयदाता आहे. याचा मला खूप आनंद होतो. वटपौणिमेच्या सणाला नटलेल्या सजलेल्या स्त्रिया माझ्या भोवताली प्रदक्षिणा मारत दोरा गुंडाळतात तेव्हा मला फार गंमत वाटते. तुम्ही या देशाचे उक्याचे नागरिक आहात म्हणून मुद्दाम तुम्हांला एक गोष्ट सांगतो. \'झाडे लावा, झाडे जगवा\', त्यांची काळजी घ्या, कारण आज लावलेली झाडे येणाऱ्या पिढीची काळजी घेतील.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63080,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the conflict between Siraj-ud-Daulah and the British start?','','','1) Siraj-ud-Daulah became the Nawab of Bengal in 1756.\n2) The British traders misused trade concessions and fortified their factories in Kolkata without seeking permission from the Nawab.\n3) So, Siraj-ud-Daulah captured the factory. This was the beginning of conflict between Siraj-ud-Daulah and the British.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63082,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why were the Sikhs defeated in the First Anglo-Sikh War?','','','1) Ranjitsingh, the ruler of Punjab was succeeded by his minor son Duleep Singh.\n2) Queen Jindan assumed the reins of administration on behalf of her minor son.\n3) Taking advantage of the inability of Queen Jindan to control the officers, the British lured some of them to their side.\n4) The Sikhs attacked the British under the impression that the British would invade Punjab.\n5) Thus, the Sikhs were defeated in the first Anglo-Sikh war.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63083,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What brought an end to the Sikh rule in Punjab in 1849?','','','1) After the First Anglo-Sikh War Duleep Singh remained on throne, but the power rested in the hands of the British.\n2) The Sikhs could not accept the growing impact of the British over Punjab.\n3) The growing influence of the British led to the revolt by Mulraj, the chieftain of Multan.\n4) Thousands of participated in this war. Sikh soldiers\n5) The British defeated the Sikhs in the Second Anglo-Sikh war and annexed the whole of Punjab in 1849.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63092,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील मुद्द्यांच्या आधारे कथा लिहा.\nमुद्दे : एक वृद्ध - आंब्याची झाडे लावणे - वाटसरू - आश्चर्य वाटणे - वृद्याला फळांबद्दल विचारणे – वृद्धाचे उत्तर – तात्पर्य.','','','                                           समजदार वृद्ध\nएक वृद्ध आपल्या आवारात आंब्याची लहान लहान रोपे लावत होता. जवळून जाणाऱ्या वाटसरूने त्या थकलेल्या वृद्धाला आंब्याची रोपे लावताना पाहिले. त्याला आश्चर्य वाटले. त्या वाटसरूने थांबून त्या वृद्धाला हसत म्हटले, \"बाबा, तुमचे तर वय झाले. ही आंब्याची रोपे मोठी कधी होणार आणि तुम्हांला त्याचे आंबे कसे मिळणार ?\"\nवृद्धाने वाटसरूचे बोलणे ऐकले आणि तो हसून म्हणाला, \"मुला, तुझे म्हणणे बरोबर आहे. मला याची फळे खाता येणार नाहीत. हे जरी खरे असले तरी आज मी ज्या आंब्याची फळे खातो ते झाड मी लावलेले नाही. माझ्या आजोबा, पणजोबांनी ते लावले म्हणून मी फळे खाऊ शकलो. आपल्या प्रत्येक कामाचा फायदा आपल्यालाच मिळेल असे नाही. काही वेळा तो येणाऱ्या पिढीला मिळणार असतो. निसर्गाचे संतुलन राखायचे असेल, तर प्रत्येकाने झाडे लावलीच पाहिजेत. वाटसरूला त्या वृद्धाचे बोलणे पटले व आपणही झाडे लावण्याचा मनोमन निश्चय करून तो निघून गेला.\nतात्पर्य : काही गोष्टींचा फायदा त्वरित मिळत नाही, तर त्यासाठी वेळ जावा लागतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63093,1939,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Text Book','','Give detailed account of Renaissance.','','','The foundation of Renaissance period was laid between 13th century and 16th century in Europe.\n1) It revived the Greek and Roman traditions.\n2) The changes were seen in the field of art, architecture and philosophy in Europe.\n3) Humanitarianism gained great momentum.\n4) There was a change in the outlook of treating each other as a human being.\n5) Man became the centre of all ideologies instead of religion.\n6) The sentiments and emotions of human being found expressions in the art and literature.\n7) The reform movement could be found in science and different art forms.\n8) Literature was produced in local languages for the better understanding of the people.\n9) Renaissance was a comprehensive movement which pervaded all sectors of human life.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63094,1939,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about American War of Independence.','','','1) After the discovery of American continent, the imperialist European powers took control of different regions of America and established their colonies.\n2) England established 13 colonies on the East coast of America.\n3) Initially, England kept nominal domination but later British Parliament laid oppressive restrictions and taxes on the colonies.\n4) This created discontent among the people and the colonies revolted.\n5) George Washington led the American army in the war against England.\n6) The colonies won the war and established the first federal democratic republic, known as the United States of America.\n7) USA became the first country in the world to have a written constitution and was based on the principles of Democracy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63095,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील मुद्द्यांच्या आधारे कथा लिहा.\nमुद्देः गाढव – जंगलात सिंहाचे कातडे सापडणे - इतर प्राणी सिंह समजून घाबरणे - गाढवाला गर्व - स्वतःला सिंह समजणे – प्राण्यांना घाबरवण्यासाठी जवळ जाणे - गर्जना करताना स्वतःचा आवाज - सर्वांसमोर फजिती - तात्पर्य.','','','                                       गाढवाची फजिती\nएकदा जंगलात फिरता फिरता एका गाढवाला सिंहाचे कातडे सापडले. त्याने ते अंगावर पांघरले. त्याला तसे येताना पाहून प्राणी त्याला सिंह समजून पळू लागले. आपल्याला पाहून प्राणी पळत आहेत हे पाहून गाढवाला खूप मजा वाटली. तो स्वतःला सिंह असल्याचे समजू लागला. आपल्याला सर्व प्राणी घाबरत आहेत हे पाहून गाढवाला गर्व झाला. त्याला दुरून पाहून प्राणी घाबरून पळत होते; पण गर्व झालेले गाढव प्राण्यांना घाबरवण्यासाठी त्यांच्या जवळजवळ जाऊ लागले.\nसिंहाचे कातडे पांघरलेल्या त्या गाढवाला आपणच सिंह असल्यासारखे वाटू लागले होते. जोरदार गर्जना करून प्राण्यांना सळो की पळो करून सोडावे या विचाराने त्याने गर्जना करण्यासाठी तोंड उघडले. घाबरून पळणाऱ्या प्राण्यांनी गाढवाचा आवाज ऐकला आणि सारे प्राणी त्याला हसू लागले. गाढवाची चांगलीच फजिती उडाली. ते खजील होऊन मान खाली घालून निघून गेले.\nतात्पर्य : आपण जे नाही ते असण्याचे उसने अवसान आणणाऱ्याची नेहमीच फजिती होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63098,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील मुद्द्यांच्या आधारे कथा लिहा.\nमुद्दे : गणोबा - कंजूष - गावातल्या बाजारात दूध विकत घेण्यासाठी जाणे - दुधाच्या किमतीत घासाघीस - गवळ्याची युक्ती - गाईचे दूध स्वतः काढून घेण्यास सांगणे - मोफत दूध मिळवण्यासाठी गणोबाचे गाईजवळ जाणे – गाईने जोरदार लाय मारणे - गणोबाच्या हाताला दुखापत - डॉक्टरचा खर्च - दूध महाग – तात्पर्य.','','','                                                   कजूष गणोवा\nएकदा गणोबा नावाचा एक कंजूष इसम सोमनगरमध्ये राहत होता. प्रत्येक गोष्टीत पैसे कमीत कमी कसे खर्च होतील याकडे त्याचे लक्ष असायचे. एके दिवशी दूध आणण्यासाठी तो गावातल्या बाजारात गेला. गावाच्या बाजारात गवळी दूध विकण्यासाठी बसला होता. गणोबाने गवळ्याला दुधाचा दर विचारला. गवळ्याने दर सांगताच गणोबा घासाघीस करू लागला.\nगवळ्याला गणोबाच्या स्वभावाची कल्पना होती. त्याने गणोबाला म्हटले, की तुम्ही माझ्या गाईचे दूध स्वतः काढून घ्या आणि त्याचे पैसेही देऊ नका. गणोबाला धडा शिकवण्यासाठी गवळ्याने ही युक्ती केली होती. मोफत दूध मिळणार हे ऐकून गणोबाचा आनंद गगनात मावेना. तो थेट गाईचे दूध काढण्यासाठी गेला. ज्या गाईजवळ गणोबा गेला ती खूप चिडखोर होती. आपल्या मालकाव्यतिरिक्त ती कोणालाही जवळ उभी करत नसे. गणोबा गाईजवळ बसताच गाईने जोरदार लाथ मारली. ती थेट गणोबाच्या हातावर वसली आणि त्याचे हाताचे हाड तुटले. गणोबाला डॉक्टरकडे जाऊन उपचार घेण्याचा खूप खर्च आला. दुधाचे थोडे पैसे वाचवायला गेला आणि गणोबाचे खूप जास्त नुकसान झाले. तात्पर्य : अतिकंजूषपणा करणाऱ्याला त्यापेक्षा मोठी किंमत चुकवावी लागते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63102,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील मुद्द्यांच्या आधारे कथा लिहा.\nमुद्दे : कावळ्याला मासा सापडणे - खाण्यासाठी झाडावर बसणे- भुकेला कोल्हा येणे- मासा पाहून कावळ्याची स्तुती  - आवाज गोड - गाणं म्हणा कावळ्याने मासा पायात धरणे  - काव काव करणे कोल्हा धूर्त- नाचण्यास सांगणे कावळ्याची हुशारी - मासा चोचीत पकडून नृत्य कोल्हा शरमेने निघून जाणे- तात्पर्य.','','','                                          हुशार कावळा\nएकदा जंगलात फिरत असताना एका कावळ्याला मासा सापडला. त्याने तो चोचीत उचलला आणि एका झाडाच्या फांदीवर जाऊन बसला. तेवढ्यात तेथे एक भुकेला कोल्हा आला. कावळ्याच्या चोचीत मासा पाहून कोल्हयाच्या तोंडाला पाणी सुटले. त्याने तो मासा मिळवण्याचे ठरवले.\nकोल्ह्याने कावळ्याची स्तुती करायला सुरुवात केली. \'कावळ्याचा आवाज किती छान आहे\', हे कोल्हा सांगू लागला. त्याने कावळ्याला एक गाणं म्हणण्याची विनंती केली. कावळा हुशार होता. त्याने कोल्हयाची लबाडी ओळखली. त्याने मासा पायात पकडला आणि \'कावकाव\' केले. कोल्हा धूर्त होता. त्याने कावळ्याला म्हटले, की तुम्ही नाचतादेखील खूप छान. एकदा नाचून दाखवा. कावळ्याने पायातील मासा चोचीत पकडला व दोनचार उड्या मारल्या. कावळा फसला नाही हे पाहून कोल्ह्याला लाज वाटली आणि तो जंगलात निघून गेला.\nतात्पर्य : खोट्या स्तुतीला न भुलता कोणतेही काम केले, तर आपल्याला कोणीही फसवू शकत नाही.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63105,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील मुद्द्यांच्या आधारे कथा लिहा.\nमुद्दे : एक शेतकरी - भाजीचा मळा काटेरी कुंपण खूप उत्पन्न - शेतकरी आजारी- मुलाचे मळ्यात लक्ष देणे - काटे टोचतील म्हणून कुंपण काढणे - गुरेढोरे येऊन मळा उद्ध्वस्त – तात्पर्य.','','','                                                  अविचारी मुलगा\nएका शेतकऱ्याने आपल्या शेतात भाजीचा मळा केला होता. त्या मळ्याची तो खूप चांगल्याप्रकारे काळजी घेत असे. आपत्त्या भाजीपाल्याचे संरक्षण व्हावे म्हणून त्याने मळ्याच्या भोवती काटेरी निवडुंगाचे कुंपण घातले होते. त्यामुळे, त्याला त्या मळ्यातून खूप उत्पन्न मिळत असे.\nएके दिवशी शेतकरी आजारी पडत्त्ता त्याला मळ्यात जाता येईना. त्याने आपल्या मुलाला मळ्यात जाण्यास सांगितले. शेतकऱ्याच्या मुलाला तेथे जाण्यास कंटाळा येत असे; पण वैतागून का होईना, तो भाजीच्या मळ्यात गेला. तेथे काम करताना त्याच्या पायात निवडुंगाचा काटा टोचला. शेतकऱ्याच्या मुलाला याचा राग आला. भाजीच्या मळ्यात या काटेरी निवडुंगाचा त्रास नको म्हणून त्याने सारे कुंपण तोडून टाकले व तो घरी आला. कुंपण नसल्यामुळे गुरेढोरे मळ्यात शिरली व त्यांनी भाजीपाला खाऊन मळा उद्ध्वस्त केला. शेतकऱ्याला डोक्याला हात मारण्याची वेळ आली.\nतात्पर्य : भविष्याचा विचार न करता अविचाराने केलेली कृती नुकसानदायक ठरते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63106,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील मुद्द्यांच्या आधारे कथा लिहा.\nमुद्देः सिंह अशक्त - एकेक प्राणी भेटीला - धूर्त कोल्हा - गेलेली पावले दिसतात – येणारी नाही - गुहेच्या बाहेरून पळ काढतो - जीव वाचतो – तात्पर्य.','','','                                                धूर्त कोल्हा\nएका जंगलात एक सिंह अशक्त झाला. जंगलचा राजा असल्याने त्याला सारेजण घाबरून असत. आपले पोट कसे भरावे या चिंतेत असताना त्याला एक युक्ती सुचली. त्याने जंगलातील प्राण्यांमध्ये दवंडी दिली, की माझे शेवटचे काही दिवस उरलेले असल्यामुळे मी आनंदात राहू इच्छित आहे. म्हणून रोज एका प्राण्याने गुहेत येऊन माझे मनोरंजन करावे. रोज एक प्राणी येऊ लागला आणि सिंह आयती त्याची शिकार करून पोट भरू लागला.\nएके दिवशी कोल्ह्याचा नंबर आला. त्याला सिंहाकडे जायचे होते. तो गुहेच्या दारापर्यंत पोहोचला आणि एकदम चमकला. त्याने पाहिले, की गुहेत जाणाऱ्या प्राण्यांच्या पावलांचे ठसे दिसत होते; मात्र येणारे दिसत नव्हते. यावरून कोल्हयाने ओळखले, की आलेल्या प्राण्यांची शिकार करून सिंह आपले पोट भरत आहे. कोल्हयाने सिंहाला आवाज दिला. सिंहाने प्रेमाने कोल्ह्याला आत बोलावले. कोल्हा म्हणाला, \"महाराज, आज मला पडसे झाले आहे. तुम्हांलाही त्याचा त्रास होऊ नये म्हणून मी बरं वाटलं की येतो.\" असे म्हणून कोल्ह्याने तेथून पळ काढला व त्याचा जीव वाचला.\nतात्पर्य : क्रूर माणूस कितीही गोड बोलला तरी त्याच्यावर विश्वास ठेवू नये.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63107,1939,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Rulers</th>\n    <th>Province</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Siraj-ud-Daulah</td>\n    <td>----------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. ---------------</td>\n<td>Mysore</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Ranjit Singh</td>\n<td>-----------</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Rulers</th>\n    <th>Province</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Siraj-ud-Daulah</td>\n    <td>Bengal</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Haider Ali or Tipu Sultan</td>\n<td>Mysore</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Ranjit Singh</td>\n<td>Punjab</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63111,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The painter of \'Mona Lisa\' and \'Last Supper\'.','','','Leonardo da Vinci','',0,0,0,NULL),(63112,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Number of colonies established by England on the east coast of America.','','','Thirteen','',0,0,0,NULL),(63114,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The Mughal Emperor who gave permission to the British to establish their factory in Surat.','','','Jahangir','',0,0,0,NULL),(63115,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The three wars fought between England and France in India over the monopoly of trade.','','','Camatic Wars','',0,0,0,NULL),(63116,1939,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Officer of Multan who revolted against the British.','','','Mulraj','',0,0,0,NULL),(63117,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि त्यांच्या आधारे कथालेखन करा.','','','                                               उंदराचे मिठाईचे दुकान\nएक होता उंदीर. त्याला काही कामधाम नव्हते. तो नुसताच इकडून तिकडे भटकायचा. एकदा त्याला मिठाईची खोकी मिळाली. त्याने मिठाईचे दुकान थाटले.\nसकाळी सकाळी गाय दुकानात आली. तिने आपल्या वासरासाठी पाव किलो जिलेबी विकत घेतली. काही वेळाने ससुल्या आला. तो उंदराला म्हणाला, \"मला थोडीशी बर्फी चाखायला दया.\" उंदीर म्हणाला, \"बर्फी विकत घ्या. चाखायला मिळणार नाही!\"\nरात्री दुकान बंद करायच्या वेळी मनीमाऊ आली. उंदराने विचारले, \"काय देऊ तुला?\"\nमनीमाऊ म्हणाली, \"मिठाई-बिठाई काही नको मला! रात्रीच्या जेवणासाठी मी तुला न्यायला आले आहे.\"\nहे ऐकून उंदराला घाम फुटला. त्याने दुकान उघडे टाकले आणि बिळात धूम ठोकली !','',1,0,0,NULL),(63123,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि त्यांच्या आधारे कथालेखन करा.','','','                                                   व्याकरणाचा तास\nएकदा आमच्या वर्गात व्याकरणाचा तास सुरू होता. बाई आम्हांला सर्वनाम म्हणजे काय ते समजावत होत्या. काही वेळाने बाईंनी आम्हांला सर्वनामे सांगायला सांगितली.\nआमच्या वर्गात सुदेश नावाचा मुलगा होता. तो खूप खोडकर होता. बाई शिकवत असताना तो खिडकीबाहेर पाहत होता. खिडकीत एक कबुतर बसले होते. सुदेशचे कबुतराकडे लक्ष होते.\nबाईंनी त्यालाच विचारले, \"दोन सर्वनामे सांग!\"\nसुदेश गडबडला. म्हणाला, \"कोण? मी?\"\nभीतीने त्याच्या अंगाला घाम फुटला.\nबाई हसल्या! म्हणाल्या, \"बरोबर उत्तर दिलेस ! कोण आणि मी ही दोन सर्वनामे आहेत.\"\nसुदेशला काही कळलेच नाही; पण सगळा वर्ग खो-खो हसत होता.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63124,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि त्यांच्या आधारे कथालेखन करा.','','','                                         सिंहाची फजिती\nएका जंगलात एक सिंह होता. तो जंगलातल्या प्राण्यांची शिकार करीत असे. त्यामुळे सगळे प्राणे त्याला घाबरत असत. एकदा त्याने गाढ झोपलेल्या एका सशाला पकडले; तेवढ्यात त्याला एक बाळसेदार हरीण दिसले.\nत्याने सशाला सोडले आणि तो हरणाच्या मागे लागला. हरीण खूप वेगाने धावत सुटले. सिंह दमला. तो परत सशाकडे आला. मनात म्हणाला, \'आज सशाला खाऊया.\' तोपर्यंत ससा पळून गेला होता. हातचे सोडून पळत्याच्या मागे धावल्यामुळे सिंहाची फजिती झाली होती.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63125,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि त्यांच्या आधारे कथालेखन करा.','','','                                    अति रागाचा परिणाम\nएक कोल्हा होता. तो एका शेतातून दुसऱ्या शेतात फिरायचा. शेतांची नासधूस करायचा. शेतकऱ्याला त्या कोल्ह्याचा राग यायचा.\nएकदा शेतकऱ्याने त्या कोल्ह्याला पकडले आणि पिंजऱ्यात डांबून ठेवले. एवढे करून शेतकरी थांबला नाही. त्याला कोल्ह्याचा खूप राग आला होता. कोल्ह्याची शेपटी पिंजऱ्यातून बाहेर काढून त्याने त्याच्या शेपटीला चिंधी बांधली; चिंधीला आग लावली आणि पिंजऱ्याचे दार उघडले.\nकोल्हा आगीच्या भीतीने सैरावैरा पळू लागला. जळती शेपटी घेऊन तो शेतात शिरला. शेत पिकून तयार झाले होते. कोल्होबाच्या शेपटीची आग पिकाला लागली. सगळे पीक जळून खाक झाले. हे पाहून शेतकरी धाय मोकलून रडला. त्याचा राग त्यालाच नडला होता.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63129,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the 18th and the 19th century known as the \'Age of Revolutions\' in Europe?','','','Due to the various revolutionary events which took place during the 18th and the 19th century, this period is known as the \'Age of Revolutions\' in Europe.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63130,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the British establish the East India Company?','','','The British established the East India Company in 1600 AD in order to carry out trade in India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63131,2028,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि त्यांच्या आधारे कथालेखन करा.','','','                                                      भित्रे ससे\nएकदा सर्व सशांची सभा भरली होती. त्यांची आपापसात चर्चा सुरू झाली. वयाने ज्येष्ठ असलेला ससा म्हणाला, \"सगळेच आपल्याला भित्रे म्हणतात. मग आपण जगून काय उपयोग? आपण आपल्या जीवनाचा शेवट करूया. या भित्रेपणाचा कंटाळा आला आहे.\"\nसर्व सशांनी या ठरावाला मान्यता दिली. त्यानंतर सगळे ससे तलावाच्या काठावर गोळा झाले. त्यांच्या बोलण्याचा आवाज तळ्याकाठी असलेल्या बेडकांनी ऐकला. सगळे बेडूक सशांना पाहून घाबरले होते. सगळ्या बेडकांनी पाण्यात धडाधड उड्या मारल्या.\nहे सशांनी पाहिले. त्यांना हसू आले. त्यांना आपल्यापेक्षा भित्रा प्राणी दिसला. सर्व सशांनी जीव देण्याचा ठराव रद्द केला आणि आनंदाने जगण्याचे ठरवले.                        ','',1,0,0,NULL),(63132,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was Mir Jafar once again made the Nawab of Bengal?','','','When Mir Kasim tried to put restrictions on the illegal trade of British, the British removed Mir Kasim and once again made Mir Jafar the Nawab of Bengal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63133,1939,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the effect of the American War of Independence in America?','','','After winning over England in the American War of Independence, a new nation known as the United States of America, with a federal government, written constitution and which was based on the principles of Democracy, came into existence.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63140,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','शिक्षक व विदयार्थी यांतील संवाद लिहा.','','','शिक्षक : तू काल शाळेत का आला नव्हतास ?\nविदयार्थी : मला बरं वाटत नव्हतं; त्यामुळे मी काल शाळेत येऊ शकलो नाही.\nशिक्षक : तुला काय त्रास होत होता ?\nविदयार्थी : मला सर्दी-खोकल्याचा त्रास होत होता.\nशिक्षक : आता तुझी तब्येत ठीक आहे का ?\nविदयार्थी : होय, मी कालच आईसोबत डॉक्टरकडे जाऊन आलो, त्यामुळे आता तब्येत ठीक आहे.\nशिक्षक : डॉक्टर काय म्हणाले? काही पथ्य सांगितले आहे का ?\nविदयार्थी : होय, डॉक्टरांनी मला आंबट आणि तेलकट, थंड पदार्थ खाण्यास मनाई केली आहे. काही औषधे लिहून दिली आहेत.\nशिक्षक : ठीक आहे. तू तुझ्या जागेवर बस आणि तब्येतीची काळजी घे.\nविदयार्थी : धन्यवाद सर.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63141,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What changes could be seen during the Renaissance period?','','','i. The Renaissance period saw a lot of development in the field of art, architecture, philosophy, etc.\nii. It saw the revival of Greek and Roman tradition which gave impetus to an overall development.\niii. Humanitarianism was encouraged.\niv. The outlook of the people regarding treating each other as a human being also changed.\nv. Man became the centre point of all ideologies instead of religion.\nvi. Moreover, the Reform movement spread throughout all sectors of human life which could be observed through science, knowledge and different sections of art.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63143,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did knowledge and new ideas become accessible to all sections of society during the Renaissance period?','','','i. The art and literature which was produced during the Renaissance period depicted human sentiments and emotions.\nii. For the better understanding of the people, literature was written in the local language.\niii. Moreover, the discovery of the printing press by Johannes Gutenberg helped in the spread of knowledge, new ideas and concepts to all sections of society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63144,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Enlist the changes that were brought about in the administrative set up of England during the 18th- 19th century.\'','','','The changes that were brought about in the administrative set up of England during the 18th-19th century were as follows:\ni. In England, there was Parliamentary Democracy. development of\nii. Changes were brought about in the structure of Cabinet System.\niii. The power of the King was limited due to the Bill of Rights, 1689.\niv. The sovereignty of Parliament was established.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63145,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','आई व मुलगी यांतील संवाद लिहा.','','','आई : राणू, तू हे काय करतेस ?\nमुलगी : आई मी माझ्या शाळेतील प्रकल्प करतेय.\nआई : कोणत्या विषयाचा प्रकल्प करतेस ?\nमुलगी : मी विज्ञान विषयाचा प्रकल्प करतेय.\nआई : तुझा प्रकल्प झाल्यावर साऱ्या वस्तू जागेवर उचलून ठेव बरं का !\nमुलगी : होय गं आई.\nआई : बरं, तुझा आज दिलेला गृहपाठ करून झाला का ?\nमुलगी : नाही आई, प्रकल्पाचे काम पूर्ण झाल्यावर मी गृहपाठ पूर्ण करणार आहे.\nआई : आज तू मैदानावर खेळायला नाही का गेलीस ?\nमुलगी : नाही. मला आजच हा प्रकल्प पूर्ण करायचा आहे. शिवाय गृहपाठही पूर्ण करायचा आहे.\nआई : ठीक आहे. तरीही प्रकल्प पूर्ण झाल्यावर थोडा वेळ तू बाहेर खेळून ये. तुला बरं वाटेल. मग गृहपाठ पूर्ण कर. \nमुलगी : चालेल आई.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63146,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a note on the French Revolution.','','','i. The people of France revolted against the cruel & oppressive monarchy and feudalism in 1789AD.\nii. This revolt was known as the French Revolution and it established a Republic in France.\niii. In World history, among the political revolutions, it occupies a very important place. The world got the values of \'Liberty, Equality and Fraternity\' due to the French Revolution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63148,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is Industrial Revolution?','','','i. The age of machines that began in the latter period of the 18th century in Europe, is known as the \'Industrial revolution\'.\nii. This revolution first began in England and as England enjoyed industrial prosperity, it came to be known as the \'World Factory\'.\niii. Industrial Revolution gradually spread to other parts of Europe.\niv. During this period, production of goods was done by machines which worked on steam.\nv. Big factories replaced the cottage industries. Instead of handlooms, machines were used.\nvi. Railway and steamer were used as mode of transport.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63150,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was Mir Kasim made the Nawab of Bengal by the British?','','','i. After the Battle of Plassey, 1757; Mir Jafar, the commander-in-chief of Siraj-ud-Daulah\'s army was appointed as the Nawab of Bengal by the British.\nii. However, when he started protesting against the British, his son-in-law, Mir Kasim was made the Nawab of Bengal and Mir Jafar was overthrown from the throne.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63152,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','कंडक्टर व प्रवासी यांच्यातील संवाद लिहा.','','','कंडक्टर : तुम्हांला कोठे जायचे आहे ?\nप्रवासी : मला नेहरुनगरचे एक तिकीट द्या.\nकंडक्टर : ही बस नेहरुनगरला जात नाही. तुम्हांला राणी लक्ष्मीबाई चौकात उतरावे लागेल.\nप्रवासी : मला तिथून नेहरुनगरसाठी बस मिळू शकेल का ?\nकंडक्टर : हो, तिथून ११२, १२० आणि ४०२ या क्रमांकांच्या बस नेहरुनगरसाठी जातात.\nप्रवासी : राणी लक्ष्मीबाई चौक या थांब्याचे तिकीट किती रुपये आहे ?\nकंडक्टर : तुमच्या तिकिटाचे पंधरा रुपये होतात. हे घ्या, तुमच्याकडे पाच रुपये सुट्टे आहेत का ?\nप्रवासी : होय. हे घ्या पाच रुपये आणि तुम्ही दिलेल्या माहितीबद्दल मनापासून धन्यवाद.\nकंडक्टर : प्रवाशांना योग्य माहिती पुरवणे हे आमचे कर्तव्यच आहे. तुमचा प्रवास सुखाचा होवो.\nप्रवासी : धन्यवाद.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63153,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the British take control of the Mysore territory?','','','i. Haider Ali took control over Mysore after a revolt.\nii. After his death, his son, Tipu Sultan, became the ruler of Mysore and he put up a tough fight against the British.\niii. Finally, in the Battle of Srirangapatna, in 1799, Tipu Sultan died.\nIn this way, British took control of the Mysore territory.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63155,1939,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the British capture the entire region of Punjab?','','','i. On behalf of Duleep Singh, his mother Queen Jindan looked after the state affairs, however, she could not check the activities of the officers.\nii. The British took this as an opportunity to bribe some of the Sikh Sardars.\niii. As the Sikh Community thought that the British will attack Punjab, they attacked the British. However, in this first Anglo-Sikh War, the Sikhs were defeated.\niv. In the second Anglo-Sikh War, thousands of Sikh soldiers participated in the war but they were once again defeated.\nIn this way, the British, finally, captured the entire region of Punjab in 1849.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63157,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','बिबट्या आणि चिमणी यांतील संवाद लिहा.','','','चिमणी : अरे, बिबट्या दादा ! कुठे पळतोस एवढ्या वेगाने ?\nबिबट्या : अगं चिमणीताई, शेजारच्या शहरातले लोक मागे लागलेत मला मारण्यासाठी, म्हणून जीव मुठीत घेऊन पळतोय. चिमणी : का रे? काय झालं ?\nबिबट्या : काय सांगू गं तुला. हे शहर जिथं वसलंय, तिथं पूर्वी माझं घर होतं. माझं बालपण तिथलंच; पण या माणसांनी माझं जंगल, माझं घर हिसकावून घेतलं. माझ्या घरात घुसून मला बाहेर काढले.\nचिमणी : खरंच की! आपणा सर्वांना त्यांनी बेघर केले.\nबिबट्या : हो, ना! आज घरची आठवण आली म्हणून तिकडे फिरकले. तर सारे काठ्या घेऊन माझ्या मागे पळू लागले.\nचिमणी : तुला लागले नाही ना ?\nबिबट्या : नाही गं, मी पटकन पळालो, म्हणून वाचलो. मी कुणाला इजा पोहोचवायला नव्हतो गं गेलो.\nचिमणी : आपल्याच घरात आपल्याला आता जागा उरली नाही. हा माणूस सिंमेटची जंगले उभी करताना या पर्यावरणाचा नाश करतोय. त्याची शिक्षा त्याला मिळेलच.\nबिबट्या : अगदी खरं बोललीस बघ ! चल, पळून पळून दमलोय मी. जाऊन जरा आराम करतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63158,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','दोन मित्रांमधील सहलीबद्दल संवाद लिहा.','','','मित्र १ : तू आजची सूचना वाचलीस का ?\nमित्र २ : नाही, मी सूचनाफलक पाहिला नाही रे. कशासंदर्भात सूचना आहे ?\nमित्र १ : आपल्या यावर्षीच्या सहलीसंदर्भात सूचना आहे. तू सहलीला येणार आहेस ना ?\nमित्र २ : हो तर ! पण आपली सहल कुठे जाणार आहे ?\nमित्र १ : यावर्षी आपली सहल रायगड किल्ला पाहण्यासाठी जाणार आहे.\nमित्र २ : खूप छान. छत्रपती शिवाजी महाराजांच्या स्वराज्याची राजधानी पाहण्याची माझी खूप दिवसांपासूनची इच्छा होती.\nमित्र १ : आपण आपली नावे वर्गाशिक्षकांकडे देऊया का ?\nमित्र २ : हो आपण देऊ शकतो; पण अगोदर मला बाबांची परवानगी घ्यावी लागेल.\nमित्र १ : मी पण माझ्या आईची परवानगी घेतो मग आपण आपली नावे देऊ.\nमित्र २ : होय. आपण सहलीला खूप मज्जा करू.\nमित्र १ : चल, आता मधली सुट्टी संपली वर्गात जाऊया.\nमित्र २ : चल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63164,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे संवादातील घटकांच्या जोड्या दिलेल्या आहेत. दिलेले चित्र पाहा, मुद्दे वाचा आणि या घटकांत काय संवाद होऊ शकतो, याचा विचार करून संवादलेखन करा :\nवाघ आणि कावळा\n[मुद्दे : कोकलणे - साद घालणे - शिकार निसटणे – डरकाळी फोडणे  -– कावकाव करणे -  जंगल डोक्यावर घेणे - जंगल सगळ्यांसाठी असणे.]','','','वाघ : ए कावळ्या, किती जोरात कोकलतोस ?\nकावळा : मी माझ्या पिल्लांना साद घालतो आहे.\nवाघ : हो. पण, जरा हळू ओरड, त्यामुळे माझी शिकार निसटते.\nकावळा : तू पण किती जोरजोरात डरकाळी फोडतोस सगळे जंगल हादरवून सोडतोस !\nवाघ : मला खूप राग आला की, मी ओरडतो. तू तर दिवसभर कावकाव करीत असतो.\nकावळा : तुझे बछडे तुझ्या आसपास असतात. त्यामुळे तुला त्यांना बोलवावे लागत नाही.\nवाघ : मान्य! पण, तुम्ही सारेच आवाज करता. जंगल डोक्यावर घेता. त्यामुळे माझी शिकार पळून जाते.\nकावळा : असे कदाचित घडत असेल. पण तुझी शिकार पळून जावी या हेतूने मी कावकाव करीत नाही. हे जंगल आपल्या सगळ्यांसाठी आहे, तेव्हा आपल्या सगळ्यांना आपापल्या परीने राहण्याची मुभा आहे.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63170,1939,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Religious Reform Movement in Europe','','','i. The people of Europe were ignorant and the Christian priests were taking advantage of this.\nii. The priests exploited the people in the name of religion and indulged in meaningless rituals.\niii. Hence, a movement began against it which is known as the \'Religious Reform Movement\'.\niv. This movement gave more importance to human freedom and rationalism than religion.\nv. During this movement, the old religious ideologies of the Roman Catholic Church were attacked by the independent intellectuals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63171,1939,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','American War of Independence','','','i. England had established thirteen colonies on the East Coast of America.\nii. When the English Parliament laid oppressive restrictions and taxes on these American colonies, the people revolted against it.\niii. In order to suppress these colonies, England immediately declared war.\niv. The American colonies organised the army under the leadership of George Washington and defeated England.\nv. This event is known as the American War of Independence. In World history, among the political revolutions, it occupies a important place. very\nvi. After winning over England, a new nation known as the United States of America, with a federal government, written constitution and which was based on the, principles of Democracy, came into existence.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63172,1939,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The people of America revolted against England.','','','i. Once America was discovered the European countries turned their attention towards it.\nii. They took possession of different parts of America. England established 13 colonies on its east coast.\niii. First England had only nominal control over these colonies but gradually the English Parliament imposed oppressive restrictions and taxes on these American colonies.\nThis triggered the people of America to revolt against England.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63173,1939,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British captured Sindh in 1843.','','','i. The British turned their attention towards the north-west frontier in order to strengthen their position in India.\nii. They were cautious about Russia and were worried that it might attack India through Afghanistan. Hence, the British felt the need to establish their influence in Afghanistan.\niii. Since the route towards Afghanistan went through Sindh, British understood the importance of Sindh.\nTherefore, the British captured Sindh in 1843.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63183,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे संवादातील घटकांच्या जोड्या दिलेल्या आहेत. दिलेले चित्र पाहा, मुद्दे वाचा आणि या घटकांत काय संवाद होऊ शकतो, याचा विचार करून संवादलेखन करा.\nट्यूबलाइट आणि पणती\n[ मुद्दे : कोपऱ्यात बसणे - सगळी उजेड पडणे - देव्हाऱ्याजवळ असणे - पणतीची गरज नसणे - पणतीची वात मोठी असणे - देवासमोर उजेड -  अंधार दूर करणे  - वीज जाणे – पणतीचा प्रकाश.]','','','ट्यूबलाइट : ए पणत्ये, त्या कोपऱ्यात गुपचूप बसून राहा.\nपणती : का ग?\nट्यूबलाइट : मी केवढी मोठी आहे? माझा उजेड सगळीकडे पडतो. त्यामुळे तू मध्ये येऊ नकोस !\nपणती : ते मला माहीत आहे. म्हणून मी देव्हाऱ्याजवळ बसले आहे.\nट्यूबलाइट : खरे तर तू या खोलीत असण्याची काही गरज नाही. तू एवढी छोटीशी ! तुझा काय उपयोग? मी एकटी पुरेशी आहे.\nपणती : अग, असू दे मी छोटीशी ! माझ्यामध्ये वात आहे ना; ती मोठी आहे.\nट्यूबलाइट : हो पण, त्या वातीचा उजेड फक्त देव्हाऱ्यापुरताच असतो.\nपणती : माझ्या एका वातीने मी अनेक वाती उजळवते. अंधार दूर करते.\nट्यूबलाइट : ते काम फक्त दिवाळीतच करतेस.\n(तेवढ्यात वीज जाते.)\nपणती : आता हिचा आवाज बंद झाला. आता प्रकाश देण्याची जबाबदारी माझी आहे.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63186,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे संवादातील घटकांच्या जोड्या दिलेल्या आहेत. दिलेले चित्र पाहा, मुद्दे वाचा आणि या घटकांत काय संवाद होऊ शकतो, याचा विचार करून संवादलेखन करा.\nरिक्षा आणि बस\n[मुद्दे : रस्त्याच्या मधून जाणे - मोठा देह -  खूप प्रवासी- गल्ली बोळातून फिरणे - बस निश्चित मार्गावर चालणे – प्रवाशांची सोय महत्त्वाची - भांडण थांबणे.]','','','रिक्षा : ए बसताई, रस्त्याच्या मधून दिमाखात चालतेस ! जरा मला पण जायला जागा दे ना!\nबस : मला बाजूला जाता येणे शक्य नाही. कारण माझा देह एवढा मोठा आहे! माझ्यात बरीच माणसेही बसली आहेत.\nरिक्षा : माणसे तर माझ्यातही बसली आहेत. त्यांनाही घरी जायचे असेल, कामावर जायचे असेल.\nबस : मान्य आहे मला. पण लोकांनी माझ्यात बसावे.\nरिक्षा : तू सगळीकडे कुठे जातेस? मी गल्लीबोळात जाते. लोकांना त्यांच्या पत्त्याप्रमाणे त्या ठिकाणावर घेऊन जाते.\nबस : माझे मार्ग व थांबे निश्चित केलेले आहेत आणि माझे तिकीट स्वस्त आहे.\nरिक्षा हो, अगदी बरोबर आहे. आपण दोघीही प्रवाशांच्या सोयीसाठी आहोत. तेव्हा आपले भांडण\nथांबवूया ! बस : अगदी बरोबर ! मी माझ्या थांब्याजवळ उभी राहते. तू माझ्या बाजूने पुढे जा!','',1,0,0,NULL),(63191,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे संवादातील घटकांच्या जोड्या दिलेल्या आहेत. दिलेले चित्र पाहा, मुद्दे वाचा आणि या घटकांत काय संवाद होऊ शकतो, याचा विचार करून संवादलेखन करा.\nसुका कचरा आणि ओला कचरा\n[मुद्दे : ओला कचरा सुक्या कचऱ्यावर पडणे - काकांनी फेकणे - घाणेरडा वास - जीवजंतु असणे – कुजणे - खत बनणे - पुनर्वापर करणे - खत बनण्याची तयारी.] ','','','सुका कचरा : अरे काय करतोस? माझ्या अंगावर का पडलास ? मला ओलं करून टाकलंस !\nओला कचरा : मी कुठे काय केले? त्या काकांनी मला तुझ्यावर टाकले.\nसुका कचरा : शीः ! किती घाणेरडा वास ! मला तर सहन होत नाही.\nओला कचरा : अरे, आमच्यात भाज्यांच्या साली, उरलेले अन्न, अंड्यांची टरफले असे सारे असते. काही वेळातच माझ्यावर जीवजंतू बसतात. मी कुजतो.\nसुका कचरा : अच्छा ! म्हणून तुझा वास घाणेरडा येतो.\nओला कचरा : हो ना! पण माझ्यात थोडी माती, पालापाचोळा, शेण घातले की माझे खतात रूपांतर होते.\nसुका कचरा : माझा बऱ्यापैकी पुनर्वापर होतो.\nओला कचरा : मी खत झाल्यावर, माझाही पुनर्वापर होतो.\nसुका कचरा : पण, तुझ्यामुळे मी ओला झालो आहे. आता काय करू?\nओला कचरा : काही काळजी करू नकोस! तूही माझ्यासारखा खत बन.\nसुका कचरा : मी फुकट जाण्यापेक्षा खत बनलो तर बरे होईल.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63194,2029,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे संवादातील घटकांच्या जोड्या दिलेल्या आहेत. दिलेले चित्र पाहा, मुद्दे वाचा आणि या घटकांत काय संवाद होऊ शकतो, याचा विचार करून संवादलेखन करा.\nमोकाट कुत्रा आणि मुलगा\n[ मुद्दे : दगड मारणे - मोकाट फिरणे - घाणेरडा वास येणे - हाल होणे - अन्न-पाणी न मिळणे – सगळ्यांना चावणे - खोडी काढणाऱ्यांचा राग येणे - घरी नेणे.]','','','कुत्रा : ए मुला, कशाला दगड मारतोस मला ?\nमुलगा : दगड मारू नको तर काय करू? रस्त्यावर मोकाट का फिरतोस ?\nकुत्रा : मोकाट फिरू नको तर काय करू? अरे, मला कोणी घरात घेत नाही.\nमुलगा : तू इतका घाणेरडा आहेस की, तुला घरी कसे नेणार ?\nकुत्रा : मला पण घाणेरडे राहायला आवडत नाही. माझे रस्त्यावर खूप हाल होतात.\nमुलगा : काय हाल होतात तुझे ?\nकुत्रा : मला नीट झोपायला जागा नाही. खायला अन्न नाही. प्यायला पाणी नाही.\nमुलगा : म्हणून तू चावतोस लोकांना ?\nकुत्रा : मला कोणाला चावायचे नसते. पण, माझी खोडी काढली की मला राग येतो. मग मी चावतो !\nमुलगा : चल तू, माझ्या घरी! मी तुला अंघोळ घालतो आणि खायलाही देतो.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63204,2030,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','                                         कोरोना संकटातील माणुसकीचा झरा !\n                                                आमच्या प्रतिनिधीकडून\nवाशीम, दि. ८ मे : या महिन्यात वाशीम जिल्ह्यात कोरोना साथीने थैमान घातले आहे. प्रत्येक तालुक्यात, गावात कोरोनाचे रुग्ण आहेत. बरीच गावे प्रतिबंधित क्षेत्रात आहेत. त्यामुळे नाश्ता, जेवण यांची व्यवस्था करणे कठीण होत आहे.\nगावागावांतील युवा संघटना रुग्णांची भोजन समस्या सोडवण्यासाठी संघटित झाल्या. त्यांनी समाजसेवी संस्थांकडून निधी मिळवला. स्वतःच्या खिशातून काही रुपयांची भर घातली. जमलेल्या निधीतून स्वयंपाकघर सुरू केले. भाजी, भाकरी व कोशिंबीर अशा अन्नपाकिटांचा पीडित कुटुंबामध्ये वाटप करीत आहेत. या खादयसेवेमुळे अनेकांच्या मनावरील ताण कमी झाला.\nसाथ ओसरेपर्यंत हा उपक्रम चालू राहील, असे युवा संघटनेच्या वतीने सांगण्यात आले.\nवाशीम जिल्ह्याच्या जिल्हाधिकाऱ्यांनी युवा संघटनेचे या उपक्रमाबद्दल खास कौतुक केले.','पुढील बातम्या वाचा आणि त्याखाली दिलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) या बातमीचे शीर्षक काय आहे?\n(२) ही बातमी कोणत्या तारखेची आहे ?\n(३) रुग्णांसाठी भोजनव्यवस्था कोणी केली होती ?','','(१)कोरोना संकटातील माणुसकीचा झरा!\' हे या बातमीचे शीर्षक आहे.\n(२)ही बातमी ८ मे या तारखेची आहे.\n(३) रुग्णांसाठी युवा संघटनेने भोजनव्यवस्था केली होती.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63208,2030,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','                                            वाहतूक कोंडी\n                                     आमच्या प्रतिनिधीकडून\nमुंबई, दि. १६ जुलै : मुंबईसारख्या शहरात वाहतूक कोंडीची समस्या नेहमीच असते. परंतु वाहतूक कोंडीमुळे जेव्हा अपघात होतात, तेव्हा लोकांना खूप त्रास सहन करावा लागतो.\nमुंबई-अहमदाबाद रोडवर मोठ्या प्रमाणात रहदारी असते. कालही मोठ्या प्रमाणात वाहनांची ये-जा चालू होती. भाईंदरच्या खाडीजवळ म्हणजेच मुंबईत शिरण्याच्या मार्गावर मोठी वाहतूक कोंडी झाली. सगळी वाहने ठप्प झाली. त्याचवेळी विरारहून येणाऱ्या एक भरधाव ट्रकने समोरच्या ट्रकला जोरदार धडक दिली. अपघात झालेल्या ट्रकच्या मागच्या भागाचा चेंदामेंदा झाला. त्या ट्रकमध्ये पाच मजुरांचा जागीच मृत्यू झाला. रस्त्यावर भीषण परिस्थिती निर्माण झाली.\nया अपघातामुळे मुंबईकडे जाणारी वाहतूक दोन तासांसाठी पूर्णपणे ठप्प झाली. या घटनेमुळे मनुष्यहानी झाली आणि ट्रकचे नुकसान झाले. तसेच कारमधून प्रवास करणाऱ्या प्रवाशांना प्रचंड मनस्ताप सोसावा लागला.','पुढील बातम्या वाचा आणि त्याखाली दिलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) या बातमीचे शीर्षक काय आहे ?\n(२) ही बातमी कोणत्या तारखेची आहे ?\n(३) या बातमीतील घटना कोठे घडली आहे ?','','(१) \'वाहतूक कोंडी\' हे या बातमीचे शीर्षक आहे.\n(२) ही बातमी १६ जुलै या तारखेची आहे.\n(३) या बातमीतील घटना मुंबई-अहमदाबाद रोडवर घडली आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63214,2030,'descriptive','5a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','                                   शाळेचे अंगण झाले हिरवेगार !\n                                     आमच्या प्रतिनिधीकडून\nशिरूर, दि. ३० जुलै : मराठवाडा हा लहरी व अल्प पावसाचा प्रदेश आहे. त्यामुळे बरेच तालुके रखरखीत आहेत. परंतु बीड जिल्ह्यातील शिरूर तालुक्यात मात्र हिरवागार परिसर असलेली शाळा सध्या पाहायला मिळते आहे.\nशिरूर येथील नगरपरिषद शाळेचा परिसर गेली अनेक वर्षे दगड-धोंड्यांनी भरलेला होता. शाळेच्या विदयार्थ्यांनी महिनाभर खपून पाणी साठवण्यासाठी खड्डे खोदले. उतारावर चर खणले. छोटे बांध तयार केले. छोटी रोपं लावली. बियांची पेरणी केली. पाऊस पडेपर्यंत रोपांना व बियांना पाणी दिले. जून महिन्यात जो पाऊस पडला त्यामुळे बिया रुजल्या, रोपे वाढली. खड्ड्यात पाणी साठत गेले. यामुळे शाळेचा परिसर अल्पावधीत हिरवागार दिसू लागला.\nशाळेच्या परिसरातील हा बदल विद्यार्थी, शिक्षक, पालक सर्वांनाच आवडला. सध्या गावातील लोक, शासकीय अधिकारी ही सारी मंडळी शाळेच्या हिरव्यागार अंगणाला भेट देत आहेत. विद्यार्थी, शिक्षक, क दिली. मुख्याध्यापक यांचे कौतुक करीत आहेत.','पुढील बातम्या वाचा आणि त्याखाली दिलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) या बातमीचे शीर्षक काय आहे ?\n(२) ही बातमी कोणत्या तारखेची आहे ?\n(३) या बातमीतील घटना कोणत्या ठिकाणी घडली आहे ?\n','','(१) \'शाळेचे अंगण झाले हिरवेगार !\' हे या बातमीचे शीर्षक आहे.\n(२) ही बातमी ३० जुलै या तारखेची आहे.\n(३) या बातमीतील घटना शिरूर तालुक्यातील नगरपरिषद शाळेत घडली आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63237,2031,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि चित्रवर्णन लिहा.','','','डोंगरउतारावर झाडांची लागवड केलेली दिसत आहे. काही विद्यार्थी रोपांची लागवड करीत आहेत. एक विद्यार्थी रोपाला झारीने पाणी घालीत आहे. विद्यार्थी पाईपने झाडांना पाणी घालत आहेत. शिक्षक विद्यार्थ्यांना मार्गदर्शन करीत आहेत.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63239,2031,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि चित्रवर्णन लिहा.','','','आकाशात पावसाचे ढग दिसत आहेत. पाऊस पडत आहे. मोर आनंदाने नाचत आहे. गाय गोठ्यात दावणीला बांधलेली आहे. मुले पावसात मजेत नाचत आहेत. जमिनीवर पावसाचे पाणी साठले आहे. मुले त्या पाण्यात उड्या मारत आहेत.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63242,2031,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि चित्रवर्णन लिहा.','','','हे पोळा सणाचे चित्र आहे. शेतकरी बैलांच्या जोडीजवळ उभा आहे. त्याने बैलांना सजवले आहे. त्यांच्या अंगांवर झुली घातल्या आहेत. गळ्यांत घुंगूरमाळा घातल्या आहेत. शेतकरीण बैलांना पुरणपोळी भरवत आहे. शेतकऱ्याची मुले कुतूहलाने हे दृश्य पाहत आहेत.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63245,2031,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि चित्रवर्णन लिहा.','','','हे श्रमदान शिबिर आहे. विद्यार्थी या शिबिरात काम करीत आहेत. शिबिरात राहण्यासाठी तंबू बांधण्यात आले आहेत. काही मुले स्वयंपाकासाठी मदत करीत आहेत. काही मुले माती खोदण्याचे काम करीत आहेत. काही मुले दगडमातीने भरलेल्या टोपल्या नेत आहेत.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63247,2031,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील चित्रांचे निरीक्षण करा आणि चित्रवर्णन लिहा.','','','विदयार्थी स्नेहसंमेलनात गाण्याचा कार्यक्रम सादर करीत आहेत. काहीवानगी आहेत. विदद्यार्थिनी तबला वाजवीत आहे. एक विद्यार्थिनी पेटीवादन करीत आहे. एक विद्यार्थि टाक वाजवीत आहे.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63248,2032,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','स्वच्छतेविषयीच्या खालील पाट्या वाचा व अशा प्रकारची घोषवाक्ये तयार करा.\n• सांडपाणी नको रस्त्यावर, डासांच्या पैदाशीला घाला आवर.\n• स्वच्छता पाळा, रोगराई टाळा.\n• परिसराची करू सफाई, आरोग्याची होई कमाई.\n• जिथे तिथे दिसते घाण. आपल्या मनावर येतो ताण.\n• जिथे स्वच्छता असे, तिथे आरोग्य बसे.\n• केरकचरामुक्त गाव, सर्वत्र होई त्याचे नाव.\n• कचरा टाकणार नाही रस्त्यावर, हा नियम बिंबवू मनावर.\n• कचराकुंडीतच कचरा टाकूया, प्रदूषणाला आळा घालूया.','','','• गाडगेबाबांचा एकच मंत्र, स्वच्छतेचे जाणा तंत्र,\n• उघड्यावर शौचास बसू नका. रोगांना आमंत्रण देऊ नका.\n• लावा सांडपाण्याची योग्य विल्हेवाट, मग उजाडेल निरामय आरोग्याची पहाट\n• स्वच्छ सुंदर परिसर, लाभेल आरोग्यसंपदा निरंतर !\n• ध्यानात ठेवा स्वच्छतेचा मूलमंत्र, निरोगी आरोग्याचा हा खरा गुरुमंत्र !','',0,0,0,NULL),(63249,2032,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','\'पाण्याची बचत\' या विषयावर घोषवाक्ये तयार करा.','','',' i. पाणी वाचवा, जीवन जगवा !\nii. प्रत्येकाचा एकच नारा, पाण्याची काटकसर करा.\niii. पाणी निसर्गाचे अनमोल रत्न, पाणी वाचवण्याचा करा प्रयत्न !\nIV. स्वच्छ, शुद्ध पाणी निरोगी आरोग्याची हमी !\nV. आरोग्यसंपन्नतेचा एकच मंत्र पाणी शुद्धीकरणाचे वापरा तंत्र !','',0,0,0,NULL),(63250,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','कविताच्या यशामुळे हरसूल, सावरपाडा या भागास नवी ओळख मिळाली. आज केवळ राऊत कुटुंबालाच कविताचा अभिमान वाटतो असे नाही, तर संपूर्ण हरसूल परिसराची हीच भावना आहे.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','कविताच्या यशामुळे हरसूल, सावरपाडा या भागास नवी ओळख मिळाली. आज केवळ राऊत कुटुंबालाच कविताचा अभिमान वाटतो असे नाही, तर संपूर्ण हरसूल परिसराची हीच भावना आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63251,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','कीटकवर्गात सर्वांत जास्त उद्योगी, कष्टाळू, शिस्तप्रिय व हुशार म्हणून मुंग्यांचे नाव सर्वप्रथम घेतले जाते. मुंगी हा समाजप्रिय कीटक वसाहत करून राहतो.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','कीटकवर्गात सर्वांत जास्त उद्योगी, कष्टाळू, शिस्तप्रिय व हुशार म्हणून मुंग्यांचे नाव सर्वप्रथम घेतले जाते. मुंगी हा समाजप्रिय कीटक वसाहत करून राहतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63252,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','वन्य प्राण्यांच्या पायांच्या ठशांवरून आपल्याला त्या प्राण्यांची बरीच माहिती मिळते. त्यावरून प्राणी कोणत्या दिशेला गेला ते कळते. काही प्राण्यांच्या पायांच्या ठशांवरून तो नर होता की मादी होती, ते समजते.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','वन्य प्राण्यांच्या पायांच्या ठशांवरून आपल्याला त्या प्राण्यांची बरीच माहिती मिळते. त्यावरून प्राणी कोणत्या दिशेला गेला ते कळते. काही प्राण्यांच्या पायांच्या ठशांवरून तो नर होता की मादी होती, ते समजते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63253,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','ज्या दोन तरुणांनी मदत केली, ते पत्रकार होते. त्यांनी अपघाताची सविस्तर बातमी फोटोसह वृत्तपत्रातून प्रसिद्ध केली. विदयार्थ्यांनी केलेल्या आपत्ती व्यवस्थापनाबद्दल सगळ्यांनी त्यांचे कौतुक केले.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','ज्या दोन तरुणांनी मदत केली, ते पत्रकार होते. त्यांनी अपघाताची सविस्तर बातमी फोटोसह वृत्तपत्रातून प्रसिद्ध केली. विदयार्थ्यांनी केलेल्या आपत्ती व्यवस्थापनाबद्दल सगळ्यांनी त्यांचे कौतुक केले.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63254,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','\"सफाईच्या कामात वाईट काय आहे?\" तात्यांनी विचारले. \"पण, असलं हलकं काम?\" गावकऱ्यांनी प्रश्न विचारला. \"जगामध्ये कोणतंही काम हलकं नाही. तुम्हीही या स्वच्छता मोहिमेत सामील व्हा.\"','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','\"सफाईच्या कामात वाईट काय आहे?\" तात्यांनी विचारले. \"पण, असलं हलकं काम?\" गावकऱ्यांनी प्रश्न विचारला. \"जगामध्ये कोणतंही काम हलकं नाही. तुम्हीही या स्वच्छता मोहिमेत सामील व्हा.\"','',0,0,0,NULL),(63255,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','आमच्या थोरल्या काकूला रांगोळी काढायचा दांडगा नाद. ठिपक्यांच्या तऱ्हेतऱ्हेच्या रांगोळ्या अंगणात ती अशी काढायची, की त्या भुईफुलांना बघून उभ्या गावानं हेवा करावा !','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','आमच्या थोरल्या काकूला रांगोळी काढायचा दांडगा नाद. ठिपक्यांच्या तऱ्हेतऱ्हेच्या रांगोळ्या अंगणात ती अशी काढायची, की त्या भुईफुलांना बघून उभ्या गावानं हेवा करावा !','',0,0,0,NULL),(63256,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','सातपुडा डोंगराच्या परिसरातील होळी महाराष्ट्रातच नव्हे, तर भारतभर प्रसिद्ध आहे. होलिकोत्सवाची सुरुवात माघ पौर्णिमेला दांडापूजनाने होते. या पूजनाला आदिवासींच्या संस्कृतीत विशेष महत्त्व आहे.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','सातपुडा डोंगराच्या परिसरातील होळी महाराष्ट्रातच नव्हे, तर भारतभर प्रसिद्ध आहे. होलिकोत्सवाची सुरुवात माघ पौर्णिमेला दांडापूजनाने होते. या पूजनाला आदिवासींच्या संस्कृतीत विशेष महत्त्व आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63257,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','जमीन ही पाण्याची बँक आहे. पाणी ही संपत्ती या बँकेत भरून बचत केली, तरच भविष्यात पाण्याचा पुरेसा पुरवठा होईल.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','जमीन ही पाण्याची बँक आहे. पाणी ही संपत्ती या बँकेत भरून बचत केली, तरच भविष्यात पाण्याचा पुरेसा पुरवठा होईल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63258,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','पुस्तके म्हणजे माहितीचा खजिना. पुस्तके नवनवीन गोष्टींची माहिती देतात. आपले ज्ञान वाढवतात, नवीन शब्दांची ओळख करून देतात. पुस्तके आपणांस विचार करायला लावतात.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','पुस्तके म्हणजे माहितीचा खजिना. पुस्तके नवनवीन गोष्टींची माहिती देतात. आपले ज्ञान वाढवतात, नवीन शब्दांची ओळख करून देतात. पुस्तके आपणांस विचार करायला लावतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63259,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','पुढच्या वर्षी तुम्ही पाचवीतून सहावीमध्ये जाणार. नवा गणवेश, नवे दप्तर, नवी पुस्तके, नव्या वह्या, सगळेच कसे नवे नवे ! नवे ते हवे, असे तुम्हांला वाटते. तरीही जुन्या वहीतून कोरी पाने काढा आणि पुन्हा वापरा.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','पुढच्या वर्षी तुम्ही पाचवीतून सहावीमध्ये जाणार. नवा गणवेश, नवे दप्तर, नवी पुस्तके, नव्या वह्या, सगळेच कसे नवे नवे ! नवे ते हवे, असे तुम्हांला वाटते. तरीही जुन्या वहीतून कोरी पाने काढा आणि पुन्हा वापरा.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63260,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','\"मुलांनो, आपल्या शाळेच्या बाहेर अपघात झाला आहे. तिकडे लवकर चला. शाळेच्या बाहेर पडा.\" तुफान आल्याप्रमाणे वर्गातले सारे विद्यार्थी भराभर वर्गाबाहेर आले.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','\"मुलांनो, आपल्या शाळेच्या बाहेर अपघात झाला आहे. तिकडे लवकर चला. शाळेच्या बाहेर पडा.\" तुफान आल्याप्रमाणे वर्गातले सारे विद्यार्थी भराभर वर्गाबाहेर आले.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63261,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','सेनापती बापट हे थोर समाजसेवक होते. त्यांनी देशसेवेची प्रतिज्ञा घेतली होती. त्यांनी सफाईचे व्रत अबोलपणे आयुष्यभर राबवले.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','सेनापती बापट हे थोर समाजसेवक होते. त्यांनी देशसेवेची प्रतिज्ञा घेतली होती. त्यांनी सफाईचे व्रत अबोलपणे आयुष्यभर राबवले.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63262,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','सुतारानं यावं अन् लाकडी खेळणी देऊन जावं. पालख-पाळणा आणून दयावा. डाव, रवी घेऊन यावं. घरातले खांब त्याच्याच हातचे. दरवाजे तोच करायचा.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','सुतारानं यावं अन् लाकडी खेळणी देऊन जावं. पालख-पाळणा आणून दयावा. डाव, रवी घेऊन यावं. घरातले खांब त्याच्याच हातचे. दरवाजे तोच करायचा.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63263,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','कोणतीही गोष्ट मिळवण्यासाठी दोन गोष्टींची आवश्यकता असते. एक म्हणजे ध्यास आणि दुसरी म्हणजे अभ्यास. शक्ती आणि युक्ती यांचा योग्य वापर केल्यास कोणत्याही कामातील अडचणी दूर होतात.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','कोणतीही गोष्ट मिळवण्यासाठी दोन गोष्टींची आवश्यकता असते. एक म्हणजे ध्यास आणि दुसरी म्हणजे अभ्यास. शक्ती आणि युक्ती यांचा योग्य वापर केल्यास कोणत्याही कामातील अडचणी दूर होतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63264,2035,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','तुझे झरे अन् तुझी पाखरे, वास तुझा, जनलोक तुझा,\n हवाहवासा मला वाटतो राग तुझा, संतोष तुझा. \nमाय मराठी! तुझिया अंकी लोळण घेते, बागडते, \nतसेच अलगद तव आभाळी भरारणे मज आवडते.','दिलेला उतारा वाचवा स्वच्छ अक्षरात लिहा.','','','तुझे झरे अन् तुझी पाखरे, वास तुझा, जनलोक तुझा,\n हवाहवासा मला वाटतो राग तुझा, संतोष तुझा. \nमाय मराठी! तुझिया अंकी लोळण घेते, बागडते, \nतसेच अलगद तव आभाळी भरारणे मज आवडते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63265,2034,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','                                         <b> * सूचनाफलक</b>\n                                         मालेगाव परिसर\n                                                                            दिनांक १ ऑगस्ट २०.......\nमालेगाव परिसरातील समस्त नागरिकांना विनंती करण्यात येत आहे की, या वर्षी गणेशोत्सव साध्या पद्धतीने साजरा करावा. यासाठी पुढील काही सूचना नागरिकांनी पाळाव्यात ही अपेक्षा आहे.\n(१) गणेशमूर्ती चिकणमातीपासून बनवावी अथवा विकत घ्यावी.\n(२) मूर्तिकारांनी पर्यावरणस्नेही मूर्ती बनवाव्यात.\n(३) मूर्तीचा आकार लहान असावा.\n(४) नियोजित विसर्जनस्थळी मूर्तीचे विसर्जन करावे.\n(५) सजावटीसाठी कागद, कापड, पाने, फुले, फळे, भाज्या इत्यादी गोष्टींचा वापर करावा.\n                                                                                      मालेगाव ग्रामस्थ मंडळ','पुढे दिलेल्या सूचना वाचा आणि त्याखालील प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) गणेशोत्सवाबाबत सूचना कोणी दिल्या आहेत ?\n(२) सूचना कोणी पाळाव्यात ही अपेक्षा आहे?\n(३) मूर्तिकारांनी मूर्ती कशा बनवाव्यात ?','','(१) गणेशोत्सवाबाबत मालेगाव ग्रामस्थ मंडळाने सूचना दिल्या आहेत.\n(२) मालेगाव परिसरातील नागरिकांनी सूचना पाळाव्यात ही अपेक्षा आहे.\n(३) मूर्तिकारांनी पर्यावरणस्नेही मूर्ती बनवाव्यात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63266,2034,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','                                         <b>सूचनाफलक</b>\n                                     विद्या प्रसारक विद्यालय, धुळे\n                                                                                    दिनांक : १२ मार्च २०......\nशाळेतील सर्व विद्यार्थी, शिक्षक व इतर कर्मचारीवर्ग यांना कळवण्यात येत आहे की, आपल्या शहरात आठवडाभरासाठी २० टक्के पाणीकपात होणार आहे. पाणीपुरवठा विभागाकडून तशी सूचना प्राप्त झाली आहे. त्यामुळे सर्वांनी पाणी काटकसरीने वापरावे. यासाठी पुढील सूचना पाळाव्यात :\n(१) तोंड धुताना, चुळा भरताना बेसीनचा नळ बंद करावा. तो चालू ठेवू नये.\n(२) मोठ्या ग्लासाने पाणी पिऊ नका. छोट्या ग्लासात आवश्यक तेवढे पाणी पिण्यासाठी घ्यावे.\n(३) ग्लासातील, बाटलीतील पाणी फेकू नका. ते पाणी झाडाच्या बुंध्यात ओता.\n                                                                                        शालेय समिती\n                                                                                 विद्याप्रसारक विद्यालय, धुळे','पुढे दिलेल्या सूचना वाचा आणि त्याखालील प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) पाणीकपातीबाबत कोणी सूचना दिली आहे ?\n(२) शहरात किती टक्के पाणीकपात होणार आहे ?\n(३) तोंड धुताना कोणती काळजी घ्यावी ?','','(१)  पाणीकपातीबाबत पाणीपुरवठा विभागाकडून सूचना देण्यात आली आहे.\n(२)शहरात २० टक्के पाणीकपात होणार आहे.\n(३) तोंड धुताना बेसीनचा नळ बंद करावा.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63267,2034,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','                                             <b>सूचनाफलक</b>\n                                             आटपाडी, सांगली\n                                                                                                 दिनांक : ५ जुलै २०\nगेले काही दिवस आटपाडी परिसरात साथीचे आजार सुरू झाले आहेत. सर्दी, खोकला, ताप ही साथीच्या आजाराची लक्षणे आढळून येत आहेत.\nपरिसरातील नागरिकांनी घाबरून न जाता पुढील खबरदारी घ्यावी\n(१) वैयक्तिक स्वच्छतेचे पालन करावे.\n(२) हात वारंवार साबणाने स्वच्छ धुवावेत.\n(३) खोकताना व शिंकताना तोंडावर रुमाल धरावा.\n(४) बाहेर जाताना मास्कचा वापर करावा.\n(५) ताप, खोकला, सर्दी ही लक्षणे आढळल्यास डॉक्टरांकडून तपासणी करून घ्यावी.\n(६) गर्दीच्या ठिकाणी जाणे टाळावे.\n                                                                                       ग्रामपंचायत, आटपाडी','पुढे दिलेल्या सूचना वाचा आणि त्याखालील प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) ताप, सर्दी, खोकला ही लक्षणे आढळल्यास काय करावे?\n(२) हातांची स्वच्छता कशी राखावी ?\n(३) नागरिकांनी कोठे जाणे टाळावे ?','','(१)  ताप, सर्दी, खोकला ही लक्षणे आढळल्यास डॉक्टरांकडून तपासणी करून घ्यावी.\n(२) हात वारंवार साबणाने स्वच्छ धुवावेत.\n(३) नागरिकांनी गर्दीच्या ठिकाणी जाणे टाळावे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63281,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' The two layers of the Earth\'s crust are – ________\n(A) Outer and inner crust  (B) Continental and oceanic crust  (C) Earth\'s surface and oceanic crust  (D) Mantle and core','','','(B) Continental and oceanic crust','',0,0,0,NULL),(63282,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' Which of the following elements is common in both mantle and crust? – ________\n(A) Silica  (B) Magnesium  (C) Aluminium  (D) Iron','','','(A) Silica','',0,0,0,NULL),(63283,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following mineral substances are found in the inner core of the Earth? – ________\n(A) Iron-Magnesium  (B) Magnesium-Nickel  (C) Aluminium-Iron  (D) Iron-Nickel','','','(D) Iron-Nickel\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63284,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' What is the state of the inner core? – ________\n(A) Gaseous  (B) Solid  (C) Liquid  (D) Semi-solid','','','(B) Solid','',0,0,0,NULL),(63285,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' The outer core is made up of which of the following? – ________\n(A) Iron  (B) Gold  (C) Hydrogen  (D) Oxygen','','',' (A) Iron','',0,0,0,NULL),(63286,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The layer of the Earth we live on is called – ________\n(A) Mantle  (B) Core  (C) Crust  (D) Continental crust','','','(D) Continental crust','',0,0,0,NULL),(63287,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which seismic waves can travel through liquid medium? – ________\n(A) Primary waves  (B) Secondary waves  (C) Surface waves  (D) Oceanic waves','','','(A) Primary waves','',0,0,0,NULL),(63288,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','This part of the Earth has the highest density of matter – ________\n(A) Crust  (B) Mantle  (C) Outer core  (D) Inner core','','','(D) Inner core','',0,0,0,NULL),(63289,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' These seismic waves can travel through all layers of the Earth – ________\n(A) Primary  (B) Secondary  (C) Tertiary  (D) Surface','','','(A) Primary','',0,0,0,NULL),(63290,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या जाहिराती वाचा आणि त्याखालील प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) ही जाहिरात कसली आहे ?\n(२) हे भाजीपाला विक्री केंद्र कोठे आहे ?\n(३) सण-उत्सवासाठी कोणत्या प्रकारची ऑर्डर स्वीकारली जाईल ?\n','','(१) ही जाहिरात भाजीपाला विक्री केंद्राची आहे.\n(२) हे भाजीपाला विक्री केंद्र आगर बाजार, चिंचणी येथे आहे.\n(३) सण-उत्सवासाठी घाऊक ऑर्डर स्वीकारली जाईल.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63291,1989,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','This part of the Earth is cool and solid – ________\n(A) Crust  (B) Mantle  (C) Outer core  (D) Inner core','','','(A) Crust','',0,0,0,NULL),(63292,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या जाहिराती वाचा आणि त्याखालील प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) संगीत वर्गाची जाहिरात कोणत्या कालावधीसाठी आहे?\n(२) कोणत्या ठिकाणी संगीत वर्ग भरणार आहेत ?\n(३) संगीत वर्गाची फी किती आहे ?','','(१) संगीत वर्गाची जाहिरात २१ एप्रिल ते १५ मे या सुट्टीच्या कालावधीसाठी आहे.\n(२) माहीम येथील मातृमंदिर हॉलमध्ये संगीत वर्ग भरणार आहेत.\n(३) संगीत वर्गाची फी रु २००/- आहे.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63293,1990,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ________ of air depends on its temperature.\n(A) Relative humidity  (B) Vapour-holding capacity  (C) Pressure  (D) Temperature','','','(B) Vapour-holding capacity','',0,0,0,NULL),(63294,1990,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ________ is determined by the number of grams of vapour in one cubic meter of air.\n(A) Relative humidity  (B) Vapour-holding capacity  (C) Absolute humidity  (D) Snowfall','','','(C) Absolute humidity','',0,0,0,NULL),(63295,1990,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In desert regions, the air is dry due to low ________.\n(A) Pressure  (B) Temperature  (C) Relative humidity  (D) Sunlight','','',' (C) Relative humidity','',0,0,0,NULL),(63296,1990,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','_______ type of clouds indicate storms.\n(A) Cirrus  (B) Cumulus  (C) Cumulonimbus  (D) Nimbostratus','','','C) Cumulonimbus','',0,0,0,NULL),(63297,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या जाहिराती वाचा आणि त्याखालील प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','(१) या नाटिकेचे आयोजक कोण आहेत ?\n(२) या नाटिकेचे नाव काय आहे ?\n(३) या नाटिकेची तिकिटे कोणत्या दरांत मिळणार आहेत ?\n','','(१)  बालरंगभूमी हे या नाटिकेचे आयोजक आहेत.\n(२) \'धमाल बाबल्या-गणप्याची\' हे या नाटिकेचे नाव आहे.\n(३) नाटिकेची तिकिटे सवलतीच्या दरांत मिळणार आहेत.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63298,1990,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' In open atmosphere, the vapour in the air condenses around dust particles through ________.\n(A) Emission  (B) Evaporation  (C) Condensation  (D) Saturation','','','D) Saturation','',0,0,0,NULL),(63299,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ocean floor topography is studied based on –\nA. Height and shape of land  B. Amount of marine sediments on the ocean floor  C. Depth from sea level and the shape of the land there  D. Ocean water','','','C. Depth from sea level and the shape of the land there','',0,0,0,NULL),(63300,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The average depth of oceans is approximately ____ meters.\nA. 1200  B. 2000  C. 4800  D. 3700\n','','','D. 3700','',0,0,0,NULL),(63301,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' The continental shelf is also known as –\nA. Ocean plain  B. Marine deposit  C. Submerged land  D. Mid-oceanic submerged mountain','','',' C. Submerged land','',0,0,0,NULL),(63302,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' The continental shelf is very important from the human perspective because –\nA. Many active volcanoes are found here  B. It is rich in fishing zones and sources of mineral oil, natural gas, and various minerals  C. Heights and depths of locations are measured from its base  D. It has submerged landforms like mountains and plateaus','','','B. It is rich in fishing zones and sources of mineral oil, natural gas, and various minerals','',0,0,0,NULL),(63303,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' This is a region on the continental shelf in the Arabian Sea known for mineral oil and natural gas.\nA. Mariana Trench  B. Chagos Plateau  C. Mumbai High  D. Andaman and Nicobar Islands','','','C. Mumbai High','',0,0,0,NULL),(63304,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Small to large submerged uplands, mountains, etc., are found here.\nA. Continental shelf  B. Continental slope  C. Ocean plains  D. Ocean trenches','','','B. Continental slope','',0,0,0,NULL),(63305,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An example of an ocean island is –\nA. Iceland in the Atlantic Ocean  B. Lakshadweep Islands in the Arabian Sea  C. Greenland in the Arctic Ocean  D. Maldives in the Indian Ocean','','',' A. Iceland in the Atlantic Ocean','',0,0,0,NULL),(63306,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Narrow and steep sloping underwater valleys on the ocean floor are called –\nA. Ocean trenches  B. Ocean plains  C. Mid-oceanic submerged mountains  D. Ocean islands','','','A. Ocean trenches','',0,0,0,NULL),(63307,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Deposits of small to large rocks, gravel, sand, and silt transported by rivers and glaciers mostly accumulate on the –\nA. Continental shelf  B. Ocean plains  C. Ocean trenches  D. Continental slope','','','A. Continental shel','',0,0,0,NULL),(63308,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Just like landforms on land, submerged landforms are also found under the sea because –\nA. There is land under water  B. There are volcanoes under water  C. Land is continuous and water is in the depressions  D. Though land is continuous, its level is not the same everywhere like water','','','D. Though land is continuous, its level is not the same everywhere like water','',0,0,0,NULL),(63309,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which part of the ocean floor is mainly used by humans?\nA. Continental shelf  B. Continental slope  C. Ocean plains  D. Ocean trenches','','','A. Continental shelf','',0,0,0,NULL),(63310,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' Which of the following is related to marine deposits?\nA. Rivers, glaciers, plant and animal remains  B. Volcanic ash, continental shelf, plant and animal remains  C. Volcanic ash, lava flows, silt  D. Volcanic ash, marine plant and animal remains, ocean plains','','',' D. Volcanic ash, marine plant and animal remains, ocean plains','',0,0,0,NULL),(63311,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The continental slope is –\nA. Gentle  B. Mild  C. Steep  D. Cold','','','C. Steep','',0,0,0,NULL),(63312,1991,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The average depth of oceans is approximately ____ meters.\nA. 7300  B. 3700  C. 3900  D. 3300','','',' B. 3700','',0,0,0,NULL),(63313,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Labrador Current is in which ocean?\nA. Pacific  B. North Atlantic  C. South Atlantic  D. Indian','','','B. North Atlantic','',0,0,0,NULL),(63314,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' Which of the following currents is in the Indian Ocean?\nA. East Australian Current  B. Peru Current  C. Antarctic Current  D. Somali Current','','','D. Somali Current','',0,0,0,NULL),(63315,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is not affected by coastal ocean currents?\nA. Rainfall  B. Land winds  C. Temperature  D. Salinity','','','B. Land winds','',0,0,0,NULL),(63316,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या जाहिराती वाचा आणि त्याखालील प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','१. ही जाहिरात कशाची आहे ?\n२. या दुकानाचे मालक कोण आहेत ?\n३. या दुकानाची कोणती वैशिष्ट्ये आहेत ?\n४. दुकानातील वस्तूंवर किती सवलत आहे ?','','','१. ही जाहिरात खेळाच्या साहित्याच्या दुकानाची आहे.\n२. श्री. अनिल शेठ हे या दुकानाचे मालक आहेत.\n३. या दुकानातील साहित्य टिकाऊ व मजबूत असून स्वस्त दरात उपलब्ध आहे व ते वि दुकानाची वैशिष्ट्ये आहेत.\n४. दुकानातील प्रत्येक वस्तूवर २०% सूट आहे.',1,0,0,NULL),(63317,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When warm and cold ocean currents meet, what forms in those regions?\nA. Dew  B. Hail  C. Ice  D. Dense fog','','','D. Dense fog','',0,0,0,NULL),(63318,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which currents flow from the Arctic region to Antarctica?\nA. Warm currents  B. Cold currents  C. Surface currents  D. Deep ocean currents','','','D. Deep ocean currents','',0,0,0,NULL),(63319,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Surface ocean currents are pushed over long distances by which winds?\nA. Planetary winds  B. Jet streams  C. Trade winds  D. Air currents','','','A. Planetary winds','',0,0,0,NULL),(63320,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The discharge from ocean currents is measured in which unit?\nA. Richter scale  B. Sverdrup  C. Mohs scale  D. Equilibrium','','','B. Sverdrup','',0,0,0,NULL),(63321,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील जाहिरात वाचा व विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','१. जाहिरात कशाबद्दल आहे ?\n२. जाहिरात कोणासाठी आहे ?\n३. कार्यक्रम कोणी आयोजित केला आहे ?\n४. वह्यांचे वाटप कोठे होणार आहे ?\n','','१.जाहिरात \'वहह्यांचे मोफत वाटप\' या कार्यक्रमाबद्दल आहे.\n२. परिसरातील गरजू विद्यार्थ्यांसाठी ही जाहिरात आहे.\n३. ज्ञानोदय मित्रमंडळाने कार्यक्रम आयोजित केला आहे.\n४. जवाहर मैदान, ठाणे (प.) येथे वह्यांचे वाटप होणार आहे.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63322,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','in which areas are large fishing grounds formed?\nA. Heat-salinity convergence zones  B. Tropical climate areas  C. Areas influenced by deep ocean currents  D. Regions where warm and cold currents meet','','','D. Regions where warm and cold currents meet','',0,0,0,NULL),(63323,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' Ocean currents generally flow close to –\nA. The continental slope  B. Ocean islands  C. A few kilometers from mid-ocean ridges  D. Coastlines','','','A. The continental slope','',0,0,0,NULL),(63324,1992,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The equatorial currents flow –\nA. From east to west  B. From west to east  C. From sea surface towards sea floor  D. From sea floor towards sea surface','','','A. From east to west','',0,0,0,NULL),(63325,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील जाहिरात वाचून विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','9. ही जाहिरात कशासंदर्भात आहे ?\n२. प्रशिक्षण शिबिराचा कालावधी काय आहे ?\n३. प्रशिक्षण शिबीर कोणत्या वयोगटातील मुलांसाठी आहे ?\n४. प्रशिक्षण शिबीर कोणी आयोजित केले आहे ?','','9.ही जाहिरात उन्हाळी क्रिकेट प्रशिक्षण शिबिरासंदर्भात आहे.\n२. प्रशिक्षण शिबिराचा कालावधी दि. १ मे ते १५ मे असा १५ दिवसांचा आहे.\n३. प्रशिक्षण शिबीर १० वर्षे ते १५ वर्षे वयोगटातील मुलांसाठी आहे.\n४. प्रशिक्षण शिबीर श्री. नवीन पाटील यांनी आयोजित केले आहे.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63329,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील जाहिरात वाचून विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','9. ही जाहिरात कशासंदर्भात आहे ?\n२. प्रशिक्षण शिबिराचा कालावधी काय आहे ?\n३. प्रशिक्षण शिबीर कोणत्या वयोगटातील मुलांसाठी आहे ?\n४. प्रशिक्षण शिबीर कोणी आयोजित केले आहे ?','','9.ही जाहिरात उन्हाळी क्रिकेट प्रशिक्षण शिबिरासंदर्भात आहे.\n२. प्रशिक्षण शिबिराचा कालावधी दि. १ मे ते १५ मे असा १५ दिवसांचा आहे.\n३. प्रशिक्षण शिबीर १० वर्षे ते १५ वर्षे वयोगटातील मुलांसाठी आहे.\n४. प्रशिक्षण शिबीर श्री. नवीन पाटील यांनी आयोजित केले आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63330,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील जाहिरात वाचून विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','१. ही जाहिरात कशाची आहे ?\n२. बालनाट्याचा प्रयोग कोठे होणार आहे ?\n३. या बालनाट्याचे लेखक कोण आहेत ?\n४. बालनाट्याचे दिग्दर्शक कोण आहेत ?\n५. बालनाट्य पाहण्यासाठी तिकीट दर काय आहेत ?','','१. ही जाहिरात छोटा भीम आणि राक्षस या बालनाट्याची आहे.\n२. महाकवी कालिदास नाट्यगृह, मुलुंड येथे बालनाट्याचा प्रयोग होणार आहे.\n३. या बालनाट्याचे लेखक श्री. यागेश माने आहेत.\n४. या बालनाट्याचे दिग्दर्शक श्री. विजय पाटील आहेत.\n५. बालनाट्य पाहण्यासाठी रुपये १००, २०० व ५०० हे तिकीट दर आहेत.','',1,0,0,NULL),(63331,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील जाहिरात वाचून विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','१. जाहिरात कोणत्या उत्पादनाची आहे ?\n२. एका पिशवीची किंमत काय आहे.\n३. जाहिरातीत कोणती सूट देण्यात आली आहे ?\n४. जाहिरातीतील पिशव्यांची काय वैशिष्ट्ये आहेत ?','','१.जाहिरात कापडी पिशव्या या उत्पादनाची आहे.\n२. एका पिशवीची किंमत पंचवीस रुपये आहे.\n३. दोन पिशव्यांच्या खरेदीवर १ पिशवी मोफत ही सूट जाहिरातीत देण्यात आली आहे.\n४. जाहिरातीतील पिशव्यांची वैशिष्ट्ये पुढीलप्रमाणे आहेत :\ni. पर्यावरणपूरक उत्पादन      ii. वेगवेगळ्या रंगांत व आकारात उपलब्ध\niii. स्वस्त दर                       IV. मजबूत व टिकाऊ','',1,0,0,NULL),(63334,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','गाताची वाडी, ता. वाशी इवले शेतकरी श्री. उस्मान मुलाणी त्यांच्या स्वतःच्या शेतातली दगडी आणि मालदांडी ज्वारी बाजारभावापेक्षा कमी किमतीत विकणार आहेत. त्यांच्यासाठी सोबतच्या नमुन्याप्रमाणे जाहिरात तयार करा.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(63335,1891,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','चौकटीतील प्रत्येक विषयाशी व चित्राशी संबंधित दिलेले शब्द / मुद्दे लक्षात घ्या. त्या शब्दांचा /मुद्द्यांचा उपयोग करून विषयाचे वर्णन लिहा.\nमाझी मावशी\nमावशी कशी आहे - मावशीचे कामकाज - ताण कमी करणे - मावशीसोबत गट्टी - मावशीचे गुण -  मावशीचे विचार - नवीन व्यवसायाला सुरुवात','','','                                              माझी मावशी       \nमला माझ्या मावशीकडे जायला खूप आवडते; कारण खूप गप्पा मारणारी, मजा करणारी अशी माझी ती मावशी आहे. ती सर्वांपेक्षा वेगळी आहे. तिचे नाव आहे सई. सई मावशी ही माझ्या आईची धाकटी बहीण. ती आमच्या घरी आली की, घर उत्साहाने भरून जाते. तिची स्वतःची पर्यटन कंपनी आहे. अनेक लोकांना ती भारताच्या विविध भागांची सफर घडवून आणते. कधी कधी ती विदेशातल्या सफरींवर जाते. त्यामुळे तिच्याकडे माहितीचा खूप मोठा खजिना आहे. ती घरी आली की, घरातले सारेच तिच्यासोबत गप्पांमध्ये रमतात.\nमधल्या काळात तिची पर्यटन कंपनी बंद होती. त्या काळात तिला थोडा ताण आला; पण ती खचली नाही. त्या कालावधीत तिने भोजनालय सुरू केले. अनेकांना रुचकर भोजन दिले. पर्यटन कंपनीतील कामगारांच्या आर्थिक अडचणी सोडवल्या.\nपुढे तिची पर्यटन कंपनी पुन्हा सुरू झाली; पण आता ती फक्त पर्यटनावर अवलंबून नाही. तिने गावच्या घराशेजारी असलेल्या दोन एकर जमिनीत फळझाडे आणि फुलझाडे यांची लागवड केली आहे. परिसरातल्या लोकांना तिने काम दिले आहे. तिने लावलेल्या फुलांची बाजारात विक्रीही होऊ लागली आहे. तिच्या घराशेजारच्या सांडपाण्याचा पुनर्वापर करून ती ही शेती फुलवत आहे. आम्ही सगळ्यांनी तिच्या या शेतीला भेट दिली आणि तिचे अभिनंदन केले.\nनवे प्रयोग करणारी, धाडसी, स्वावलंबी आणि छान छान पोशाख घालणारी सई मावशी मला खूप खूप आवडते आणि तिची व माझी खूप ग‌ट्टीही आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63336,1891,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','चौकटीतील प्रत्येक विषयाशी व चित्राशी संबंधित दिलेले शब्द / मुद्दे लक्षात घ्या. त्या शब्दांचा / मुद्द्यांचा उपयोग करून विषयाचे वर्णन लिहा.\nमाझे आवडते फूल\nफुले पाहताना काय वाटते - फुलांशी मैत्री होणे - नाजूक पांढरी फुले उमलणे - आईने समजूत काढणे - फुले कोमजणे - पारिजातक आवडणे - फुले न खुडणे','','','                                                 माझे आवडते फूल\nबागेत किंवा एखादया फुलाच्या शेतात मी गेलो की, तेथील सगळी फुले पाहत राहावेसे वाटते. तेथील फुलांचा भरभरून सुगंध घ्यावासा वाटतो. अशा विविध रंगांतील फुलांना किती पाहू आणि काय पाहू असे होते.\nफुलांच्या या मोहामुळे मी माझ्या घराच्या परिसरात अनेक फुलांची रोपे लावली आहेत. मोगरा, रातराणी, पारिजातक, जाई-जुई, शेवंती अशा अनेक त-हेच्या रोपांची लागवड केली आहे. त्या रोपांची नित्यनेमाने काळजी घेतो. त्यांना पाणी घालतो, त्यांना खत घालतो. आता रोपांना कळ्या आलेल्या आहेत. एके दिवशी संध्याकाळी रातराणीच्या रोपांवर कळ्यांचे गुच्छ दिसले, मला खूप आनंद झाला. पण त्याहीपेक्षा जास्त आनंद झाला, तो पहाटेला पारिजातकाच्या डहाळीवर केशरी दांडे असलेली पांढरी फुले उमललेली दिसली तेव्हा !\nसुंदर नाजूक फुले म्हणजेच पारिजातकाची फुले ! सकाळी पारिजातकाची दहा-बारा फुले उमललेली होती. मी त्या फुलांचा भरभरून सुगंध घेतला. त्या फुलांचा नाजूकपणा पाहून हात लावायचा झालेला मोह मी आवरला. आई, आजी यांना बोलावले. त्यांनाही ही फुले पाहून आनंद झाला. आजी म्हणाली, \"ही फुले देवाला वाह्या!\" मला काही ते पटेना! ही फुलांनी डवरलेली डहाळी अशीच राहावी असे मला वाटत होते. त्या पांढऱ्याशुभ्र नाजूक पाकळ्यांच्या फुलांना खुडायला मन धजावत नव्हते.\nदुपारनंतर डहाळीवरची फुले कोमेजू लागली. मी हिरमुसलो. आई म्हणाली, \"अरे वेड्या, डहाळीवरची फुले जरी कोमेजली, तरी डहाळीवर कळ्यांचे गुच्छच गुच्छ दिसत आहेत. त्यामुळे डहाळीवर रोजच तुला फुले पाहायला मिळतील!\" आपल्या अनुभवाने तिने मला समजावले. सध्या माझी आणि पारिजातकाची मैत्री जमली आहे. रोज सकाळी माझी आवडती फुले पाहण्यासाठी मी बागेत जातो. मला असे वाटते की, मला आनंद देण्यासाठीच ही फुले रोज उमलत असावीत !','',0,0,0,NULL),(63338,1891,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','चौकटीतील प्रत्येक विषयाशी व चित्राशी संबंधित दिलेले शब्द / मुद्दे लक्षात घ्या. त्या शब्दांचा /मुद्द्यांचा उपयोग करून विषयाचे वर्णन लिहा.\nमाझा परोपकारी मित्र\nशुभेच्छा - तेजसची हाक ऐकणे - आजारपणात मदत - जिवलग मित्र असणे - वृक्षवेलींना पाणी घालणे - परोपकार हा छंद असणे - आईबाबांचा ओरडा खाणे - सर्वांना मदत करणे  - अभ्यास सांभाळणे.','','','                                         माझा परोपकारी मित्र\n\"सावनी येतेस ना ?\" तेजसची हाक ऐकली आणि मी लगेच बाहेर पडले. आम्हांला अनाथाश्रमातल्या मुलांना गोष्ट सांगायला जायचे होते.\nतेजस हा माझा जिवलग मित्र! आम्ही पहिलीपासून एकाच वर्गात शिकत आहोत. या माझ्या मित्राचा एकच छंद आहे, तो म्हणजे इतरांच्या उपयोगी पडण्याचा! आमच्या शाळेतले मित्र-मैत्रिणी असोत की लहान मुले असोत, तेजस नेहमी सगळ्यांना मदत करतो.\nकोणाच्या वह्या-पुस्तकांना कव्हरे घालून देतो. कोणाला ग्रंथालयातून पुस्तके आणून देतो. शेजाऱ्यांना, वयोवृद्धांना दुकानातून सामान आणून देतो. पेपर वाचून दाखवतो. हे सारे तो आपला अभ्यास साभाळून करतो.\nया सर्वांमुळे त्याला आईबाबांचा ओरडा ऐकावा लागतो. मग दोन-तीन दिवस तो गप्प बसतो. पुन्हा त्याचे मदतकार्य सुरू होते. शाळेच्या परसातील वृक्षवेलींना मे महिन्यात आठवणीने पाणी घालणारा तेजसच आहे. एकदा मी आजारी होते. तेजस न चुकता मला भेटायला यायचा. शाळेतल्या गमती-जमती सांगायचा. अभ्यासाबद्दल माझ्याशी बोलायचा. मला खूप छान वाटायचे.\nएकदा त्याचाच हात दुखापतीमुळे प्लास्टरमध्ये अडकला. त्या वेळी त्याने बरे होण्यासाठी स्वतःलाच छान मदत केली. त्याच्या या वृत्तीचे मी मनापासून अभिनंदन केले. असा संवेदनशील आणि परोपकारी वृत्तीचा तेजस माझा मित्र आहे, याचा मला अभिमान वाटतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63339,1891,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','चौकटीतील प्रत्येक विषयाशी व चित्राशी संबंधित दिलेले शब्द / मुद्दे लक्षात घ्या. त्या शब्दांचा /मुद्द्यांचा उपयोग करून विषयाचे वर्णन लिहा.\nमाझ्या गावची नदी\nनदीजवळ राहणे - नदीचे नाव - सर्वांच्या इच्छा पूर्ण - करणारी नदी -  तीरांवर घाट बांधणे - नदीचे देणे - नदीची स्वच्छता - मासे, कासवे पाहायला मिळणे.','','','                                             माझ्या गावची नदी                                       \nकोणाचे गाव समुद्रकिनाऱ्याजवळ असते; तर कोणाचे गाव तलावाजवळ असते. मात्र माझे गाव आहे नदीकिनारी! या नदीचे नाव आहे पूर्णा !\nसर्व गावकऱ्यांच्या, पर्यटकांच्या इच्छा पूर्ण करणारी नदी म्हणून तिचे नाव पूर्णा ! या नदीमुळे आम्हांला पिण्यासाठी तसेच घरगुती आणि शेतीच्या वापरासाठी मुबलक पाणी मिळते. गावातील शेतकऱ्यांची शेती फुलते. फळबागा फुलतात. फुले वाढतात. यामुळे आज गावात समृद्धी आहे. या नदीतून पर्यटन करता येते. गावातील मुले पाण्यात पोहतात.\nमी आणि माझ्या मित्रांना पोहण्याचे धडे इथेच मिळाले. नदीमध्ये खूप मासे आहेत. कासवे आहेत. त्यांच्याकडे तासन्तास पाहत राहणे सगळ्यांना खूप आवडते. नदीच्या दोन्ही तीरांवर मोठ्या प्रमाणात लोकवस्ती आहे. त्यामुळे तेथे कचराही गोळा होतो. सारे लोक पावसाळ्याच्या आधी नदीतील गाळ बाहेर काढतात. दोन्ही तीरांवर असलेला कचरा खतासाठी वापरतात. आम्ही शाळकरी मुलेही या कामात नाळून सहभागी होतो.\nया उपक्रमामुळे नदीत वर्षभर भरपूर पाणी साठलेले राहते. पाणी स्वच्छही राहते. गेल्या वर्षी नदीच्या तीरांवर मोठे घाट बांधण्यात आले आहेत. त्यामुळे पूर आला तरी गावात पाणी शिरत नाही. पूर्णा नदी आमची काळजी घेणारी आमची माय आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63340,2066,'descriptive','4',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Frederick, King of Prussia, one day rang the call bell but nobody answered. He opened the door of his room and found his servant fast asleep in a chair outside. He went to him and was about to wake him up when he saw a letter hanging out of the servant\'s pocket.\nThe king was curious and took out the letter and read it. It was a letter from the young man\'s mother. In it she thanked her son for sending her a part of his wages to help her in her misery and hardship. The king\'s eyes were wet with tears. He softly went back to his chamber, took a bag of coins and slipped it with the letter into the servant\'s pocket.\n','Read the following passage and do the activities:','A1] What did the King see hanging out of the servant\'s pocket?\nA2] What had the servant\'s mother written in the letter?\nA3] What would the King have done if he had not seen the letter?\nA4] He found his servant fast asleep in a chair outside. (Underline the adverbs.)\nA5] Write from the passage words that refer to unpleasant conditions of life.\n','','A1] The King saw a letter hanging out of the servant\'s pocket.\nA2] In the letter the mother had thanked her son for sending her a part of his wages to help her in her misery and hardship.\nA3] The King would have punished the servant and might have even sentenced him to death.\nA4] He found his servant <u>fast</u> asleep in a chair <u>outside.</u>\nA5] misery, hardship\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63341,2066,'descriptive','4',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The body is like a chariot driven by five horses. These horses are our five senses-sight, hearing, smell, touch and taste. Thanks to our senses, we are able to derive immense pleasure from the world and all God\'s creations.\nWith the eyes we can see all the beautiful things in the world. We smell all the lovely fragrances with the nose. We listen to fine music with our ears. We enjoy tasty meals with our tongue. We feel smoothness and softness with the sense of touch.\nYes, the senses are our friends. The mind is like a bridle that controls the senses. But only when the mind is in control is there restraint and moderation in your life.\n','Read the following passage and do the activities:','A1] Which are the five senses?\nA2] What are the mind and the body compared to ?\nA3] Why are the senses important?\nA4] With the eyes we can see all the beautiful things in the world.\n(Underline the prepositional phrases.)\nA5] Write from the passage words that refer to limits and controls.\n','','A1] The five senses are: sight, hearing, smell, touch and taste.\nA2] The mind is compared to a bridle while the body is compared to a chariot.\nA3] The senses are important because they help us to derive pleasure from the world and all God\'s creations.\nA4] <u>With the eyes</u> we can see all the beautiful things <u>in the world.</u>\nA5] restraint, moderation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63342,2066,'descriptive','4',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Hagfish are also known as \"slime eels\" because they have an eel-like appearance with long, slender bodies and smooth skin. Hagfish use their slime as a defense mechanism. When threatened, they produce large amounts of thick, sticky slime. This slimy substance acts as a protective shield by clogging predators\' gills and mouths, making it difficult for them to attack. Interestingly, the slime is primarily composed of proteins and becomes even more gelatinous when it comes into contact with water. Hagfish are ancient creatures, having lived for hundreds of millions of years. Their unique ability lies in their slime glands, which can rapidly produce large volumes of slime, enabling them to respond quickly to danger.','Read the following passage and answer the questions:','A1] Why are Hagfish also known as \"slime eels\"?\nA2] What do Hagfish use as their defense mechanism?\nA3] How does the slimy substance provide protection against predator\'s attack?\nA4] Write down any 5 prepositions from the passage.\nA5] Their unique ability lies in their slime glands, which can rapidly produce large volumes of slime. (State the modal auxiliary in the sentence.)\n','','A1] Hagfish are also known as \"slime eels,\" because they have an eel-like appearance with long, slender bodies and smooth skin.\nA2] Hagfish use their slime as a defense mechanism.\nA3] The slimy substance acts as a protective shield by clogging predators gills and mouths, making it difficult for them to attack.\nA4] as, with, of, to, in\nA5] can\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63343,2066,'descriptive','4',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Feeling hungry is like our body\'s way of telling us it\'s time to eat. We feel hungry because our bodies need energy to work properly, and food gives us that energy. After we eat, the food breaks down into sugar called glucose, which gives energy to our cells. Our stomach produces a hormone called ghrelin that makes us feel hungry. When our stomach is empty, ghrelin levels go up, and our brain tells us it\'s time to eat. Having an empty stomach is another reason that makes us feel hungry. When our stomach is empty, it sends signals to our brain, saying it\'s time to eat and fill up again. Remember, feeling hungry is completely normal and important. It\'s our body\'s way of asking for the fuel it needs to stay healthy and active.','Read the following passage and answer the questions:','A1] What provides energy to our cells?\nA2] Which hormone does our stomach produce that makes us feel hungry?\nA3] What are the reasons mentioned in the passage that make a person feel hungry?\nA4] We feel hungry because our bodies need energy to work properly, and food gives us that energy. (Identify and write down the Conjunction\'s from the sentence.)\nA5] State the word from the passage which means \'lively.\"\n','','A1] Glucose, a type of sugar, provides energy to our cells.\nA2] Ghrelin, a hormone produced by our stomach, makes us feel hungry.\nA3] A person feels hungry when he lacks energy or has an empty stomach.\nA4] Because, and\nA5] Active\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63345,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a Formal letter to your class teacher requesting him/her to grant you leave of absence.','','','Sacred Heart High School \nBandra,\nMumbai-40050\n25th October, 2020\n\nTo,\nThe Class Teacher,\nSacred Heart High School, \nBandra,\nMumbai-400050\nSubject: Application for leave\nRespected Madam,\nI, Seema Samant, of your class, (VI A) am suffering from viral fever for the past two days. The doctor has asked me to take bed rest for the rest of the week. I would, therefore, request you to kindly grant me leave of absence upto Friday, 28th October.\nI assure you that I will make up for the work I miss during my absence and would submit it to the respective subject teachers as soon as I return.\nI would, therefore, be highly obliged if you grant me this leave of absence.\nThanking you in anticipation,\nYours obediently,\nSeema Samant\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63346,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to the Manager of Cadbury India Ltd. Thane, asking permission to visit the factory as a part of school excursion, to get to know how chocolates are made.','','','Sacred Heart High School \nBandra,\nMumbai-40050\n10th September, 2020\n\nTo,\nThe Manager,\nCadbury India Ltd.,\nPokhran Road,\nThane-401050\nSubject: Permission to visit the factory premises\nDear Sir,\nI am a student of Standard VI, Sacred Heart High School, and the representative of our class. have a school project this term, based on various factories and their products. Therefore, in relation to this project, we would like to visit your factory premises and learn more about how chocolates are made.\nWe request you to kindly grant permission for 30 students and our teacher to visit your factory premises on Saturday, 20th August, 2020 at around 10:30a.m., so we can get a first hand experience in the art of chocolate making.\nThanking you in anticipation, \nYours sincerely, \nXYZ\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63347,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','As the Principal of a school, write a formal letter to Shahid Hussain, a sitar artist, inviting him to be the chief guest for the Cultural Day of your school.','','','Mahima Pratap\nParijat Society,\nPune - 411 051.\n12 August 2022\nSubject: Invitation as Chief Guest\nDear Sir,\nRespectful greetings!\nThe Principal, staff, and student fraternity of Bulbul Vidya Mandir cordially invite you to be the Chief Guest of our school\'s Cultural Day on 30 August 2022. We will be highly obliged if you accept this humble invitation. Our students would love to receive their prizes for the Arts and Music Competition from your hands.\nWe do understand that you must be having a very busy schedule, but we request you to kindly grace the occasion.\nThanking you and awaiting a favourable reply,\nYours sincerely,\nMahima Pratap\nPrincipal\nPrime Public School\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63348,2033,'descriptive','5b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ्यपुस्तक पृष्ठ क्र. ६१ वरील जाहिरातीचा नमुना वाचा. त्यासारखे आणखी नमुने तयार करा.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(63349,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to the Principal of your school asking for permission to hold a Tuck Shop on Children\'s Day in the school.','','','Anita Mathias\nStd VI \nWadia High School, \nAndheri (W), \nMumbai-400 058.\n1 November 2022\n\nTo,\nThe Principal \nWadia High School, \nAndheri (W), \nMumbai-400 058.\nSubject: Permission for a Tuck Shop on Children\'s Day 2022\nRespected Madam,\nAs you know, Children\'s Day is only a couple of weeks away. Our class would love to hold a tuck shop of home-made foods - sweets and savouries - prepared with the help of our parents. We would like to gift the profits to the school peons and mavshis.\nWe would be really grateful if you could grant us permission for the same.\nThanking you in anticipation,\nYours sincerely,\nAnita Mathias \n(Monitor - Class VI-A)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63350,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to your class teacher seeking guidance and advice for a subject in which you want to score good marks.','','','Vijay Kapur\nMadhuvan Park Society, \nAnand Nagar, \nBorivali (West), \nMumbai-401202\n18th July, 2023\n\nTo\nMs Edwina Mendez, \nGloria High School, \nBorivali (West), \nMumbai-401202\nSubject: Seeking guidance and advice for exam preparation.\nRespected Sir/Madam,\nI hope this letter finds you well. I am writing to ask for your help and advice on a subject that I want to do well in, for my upcoming exams. The exams are getting closer and I really want to improve my performance in Mathematics.\nMathematics is challenging for me and I want to improve my math skills. I would be grateful if you could kindly share some study techniques to help me do better. Any recommended resources or textbooks, time management tips would be great. Also, I know I can depend on you to help me with any doubts I may have while studying.\nYour support means a lot to me. Thank you so much for considering my request. I am eagerly waiting for your advice. Your knowledge and encouragement will make a big difference in my exam preparations.\nThanking you,\nYours sincerely, \nVijay Kapur\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63351,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to your favourite author expressing your admiration for their working questions to seek inspiration.','','','Risa Carvalho\n401, Cross Garden Apartment, \nMG Road, \nSantacruz West, \nMumbai 400054\n6th August, 2023\n\nTo\nMs Lily Meadows, \n102, Grace Avenue, \nSV Road, \nBandra West, \nMumbai-400054\nSubject: Expressing Admiration and Seeking Inspiration.\nRespected Ms Lily,\nI hope you\'re doing well. Your stories have truly touched my heart and sparked my imagination. I recently finished reading your latest novel, The Fall\' and I was completely hooked. The way you tell a story makes your characters feel so real. I found myself thinking about the twist in the story long after I finished the book\nI would be honoured if you could answer a few questions that have been on my mind. How do you create such interesting characters? Are there any particular books or authors that have influenced your writing style? As an aspiring writer, do you have any advice for someone like me?\nI greatly appreciate the impact your books have had on me. I eagerly await your response and look forward to reading more of your wonderful stories.\nThanking you,\nYours faithfully, \nRisa Carvalho\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63352,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','You have got a brilliant charity event idea and would like to organize the event in the school. Write a formal letter to your principal, requesting and asking permission to organize this event.','','','Radha Kumar\n601, Raut Housing Society,\nSantacruz West,\nMumbai-400054 \n8th August 2022\n\nTo \nThe Principal, \nSt. Teresa\'s School, \nSV Road, \nSantacruz West, \nMumbai-400055\nSubject: Request for permission to organize a Charity Event.\nRespected Sir/Madam,\nI am a student in your school from class VI, Division A. I along-with my classmates suggest organizing a Carnival for a Cause\' to raise funds for underprivileged children.\nWe plan to have games, food stalls, live performances to actively engage students, teachers as well as the parents. Our goal is to promote team-work and social responsibility among students by actively planning and executing this event. The funds collected from this event will be donated to \'Children\'s Hope Foundation registered charity organization which provides basic education to underprivileged children across the city We have chosen the school grounds for the event\'s venue. I kindly request your permission and support for this charity event. We believe it will reinforce our school\'s commitment to community service.\nThank you for considering our proposal.\nYours sincerely, \nRadha Kumar\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63353,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Your class is going for a picnic. As a class monitor, write a formal letter to your class teacher, discussing the safety guidelines that need to be followed throughout the picnic for the student\'s safety.','','','Ayushi Mehra \n202, Great Heights,\nSarla Marg, \nAndheri East,\nMumbai-400056\n19th November, 20122\n\nTo\nThe Class Teacher,\nSt. Benedict School, \nKarjar Road,\nVile Parle West,\nMumbai-400056\nSubject: Safety Guidelines for School Picnic.\nRespected Sir Madam,\nAs the class monitor of VI A, I wanted to discuss the safety guidelines for our upcoming class picnic. These rules and safety guidelines will ensure a secure and enjoyable experience for everyone.\nEach student will be paired with another classmate to stay together and look out for each other during the picnic. All students must attentively listen to the teachers instructions and follow their guidance throughout the picnic. Students must avoid wandering off to unfamiliar places during the picnic. All students must respect the environment by avoiding littering and maintaining cleanliness. Everyone will return to the meeting point together after the activities. A fully stocked first-aid kit will be available in case of any medical needs.\nAs responsible students, I along-with my classmates promise to follow these rules and make the picnic a great experience for everyone. We kindly request your approval and support in implementing these safety measures\nThanking you,\nYours sincerely,\nAyushi Mehra\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63354,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to your class teacher asking permission to leave early.','','','N.E. Trost High School, \nLal Road, \nAhmednagar 414 003\n12 August 2023\n\nTo\nThe Class Teacher,\nStandard VI C, \nN.E. Trust High School, \nAhmednagar.\nSubject: Request for permission to leave early\nDear Madam,\nI have not been feeling well since morning. Mummy will be taking me to the doctor this evening. We have an appointment at 6 p.m. Could I please leave before the last two periods?\nThanking you,\nYour obedient student, \nXYZ\nStd. VI (C)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63355,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','You have lost an expensive library book. Write a formal letter to your principal apologizing for this.','','','Loyola High School, \nMarket Road, \nWardha-442 003.\n5 January 2023\n\nTo\nThe Principal,\nLoyola High School, \nMarket Road, \nWardha-442 003.\nSubject: Apology for loss of library book\nRespected Sir,\nI am really sorry that I lost the library book \'World War II\' that was issued to me two days back. I had left my bag on a bench in the park when I was playing with my friends. When I returned, the book was missing. I searched for it for a long time but I could not find it. Rita Ma\'am, the librarian, says that I must inform you about the loss.\nI know that it is an expensive and popular book. I have explained to my parents and we will replace the book with a new one. Please forgive me for what happened. I shall be more careful in future.\nYour obedient student, \nXYZ\n(VI C, Roll No. 3)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63356,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your friend who has met with a minor accident and is presently staying at his grandmother\'s house in the village.','','','501, Trimurty House,\nSadashiv Road, \nGoregaon (West), \nMumbai. - 400062 \n29th April, 2020\nDear Sumeet,\nI am so sorry to hear that you have met with an accident at your native village while riding your bicycle. Your father told me that it was nothing serious when I called up to speak to you yesterday. You were sleeping, and thus, I requested him not to wake you up. However, accidents are accidents and you are indeed lucky to have escaped with only a fractured leg!\nUnfortunately, this accident has left you with little option but to stay in bed. Since you cannot travel back by train till your leg is better, I guess you will be missing the first few lessons at school when we reopen. But do not worry, I shall take down all the notes for you.\nI do hope to see you back in Mumbai soon. Convey my regards to all the elders at home and my love to Avinash.\nYour best friend, \nRohit\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63357,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Your school has arranged for an educational tour to some historical place. Write a informal letter to your father from the school hostel requesting him to allow you to go on that trip.','','','La Martinere for Girls,\n14, Rawdon Street, \nKolkata.-600017\n15thSeptember, 2020\nDear Daddy,\nI hope mum and you are doing well. I really miss being at home with you all even though! enjoy myself thoroughly here at the school hostel. You might be wondering why I am writing you for the second time this week...well, it is to seek permission for a school trip.\nOur school is organizing an educational trip for students of standards IX and X to a few places in Rajasthan. The plan is to visit Jaipur - the Pink City, Udaipur and its fine lakes and the Chittor Fort of Rana Pratap. Our Principal and five of the faculty members including our History teacher are going to accompany the students. The idea is to leave on the 25th of October and return by the 31st. The total charges for the tour is INR 5000 per student.\nThis trip will be of great educational value to me and I would really like to join the group. I am, therefore, writing this letter to you to allow me to be a part of this trip. I shall eagerly wait for your response.\nWith love and regards,\nYours affectionately, \nSheila\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63359,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your grandparents living in a different town telling them you would like to spend your forthcoming vacation with them.','','','525, Dale Housing Society, \nMarol Marosh Road, \nAndheri(East), \nMumbai.-400059\n29th November, 2020\nDear Grandma and Grandpa,\nI hope this letter finds you both in good health. I was a little disappointed to learn that you will not be able to visit us this year. I always look forward to your annual visits and needless to say, time simply flies when you are here. All of us were eagerly looking forward to your visit.\nHowever, since the circumstances cannot be changed and the reason you need to stay back is important, so after a brief discussion with parents, Minu and I have decided to spend our holidays with you this year. So yes, we will be coming to Shimla as soon as our vacations begin and we would spend the entire month with you both there... something you have been asking us to do for a long time.\nWe are extremely excited about the entire plan and cannot wait to be there with you both. We have already begun to sort out the warm clothes that we will carry along and other essentials. Please let us know if there is anything you need from here so we can get it for you. We will inform you about the exact date of our travel, once we have our tickets in hand. In the meantime, please take your care.\nWith love and warmest regards, \nYours affectionately, \nTina\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63360,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your friend about the Great Indian Bustard. You may use following points: (i) Why we need to protect the bird. (ii) What you plan to do to help the bird','','','303, Silver Heights, \nNarayan Dabholkar Road, \nMumbai-400 026.\n15th October 2023\nDear Mallika,\nHow are you? How are your college activities and social work? I have not heard from you for a long time.\nMallika, you are an environmentalist, and I remembered you when I was reading about the Great Indian Bustard. It is indeed shameful that this bird is soon going to become extinct. Every animal and bird is a part of the food chain, and the loss of even one species can cause untold damage to the environment. We have to protect the GIB from hunters and from any type of harmful development.\nI would like to help in this. I can prepare posters, poems and drawings and put them up in schools and other places so that people are aware of the threat. I can organize and participate in speech competitions on the subject. I would also like to go to sanctuaries to see the GIB for myself, and to help the authorities there in whatever way I can. I have made my parents aware of what is happening, and they are also ready to help out. Please let me know if I can join any association or NGO that works in this field.\nHope to hear from you soon. Bye.\nYour loving friend, \nShobha\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63361,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','You have won a prize to visit a wild life sanctuary or National Park in Africa or Australia. Choose a location, read about it. Imagine that you are visiting it and write a informal letter to your friend about it. ','','','A-101, Golden Paradise,\nBudhwar Peth,\nSolapur-413 001.\n10th November 2023\nDear Ashutosh,\nGuess what? No, you will never be able to guess, so let me tell you.\nI have won a grand prize! I had written a speech about how to save the Great Indian Bustard, and I won the first prize for it in a competition! And do you know what the prize is? It is a visit to the Serengeti National Park in Africa! All expenses will be paid for a four day trip, for me and my parents! Isn\'t that wonderful?\nYou must have certainly heard of the Serengeti National Park in Africa. It has the greatest concentration of wildlife. There are vast grasslands, inhabited by animals like lions. leopards, rhinos, wildebeest, zebras, buffaloes, elephants, giraffes, etc. The climate of the place is pleasant. It is a well-known tourist spot-and I will be going there soon! I am feeling terribly excited!\nI will let you know how the trip was. Now I am off to pack! Bye!\nYour loving friend,\nBhushan\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63362,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your father telling him about your new teacher.','','','6, Prashant Colony,\nJanpath.\nRavivarpeth,\nNashik-422001\n27 June 2023\nDear Daddy,\nHow are you? All of us are missing you.\nWe have a new teacher for mathematics. Her name is Mrs D\'Sa. She teaches very well. Everyone in the class has started taking interest in mathematics. She makes the sums seem very easy. I find it easy to solve even the most difficult sums now.\nMrs D\'Sa also tells us stories. This is the part we like best. She does not give us too much homework. Mummy is very happy that I have improved in mathematics.\nDo try to return as soon as you can. Everyone sends their love.\nYour loving son,\nPQR\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63363,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your friend, discussing about your favourite animal and share your fascination for the same.','','','Vikas Gupta\n501, Park Avenue,\nSG Road,\nWorli East, \nMumbai-400054\n10th November, 2022\nDear Rowan,\nI hope you\'re doing well. It has been quite some time since we last spoke and I thought of sharing something fascinating with you - White Tigers! I\'m absolutely captivated by these beautiful creatures.\nWhite tigers are a rare variant of Bengal tigers. Their stunning white fur with dark stripes is truly mesmerizing. They hold a special place in mythology and symbolize power and grace. It\'s sad that they are endangered due to habitat loss and illegal trade. I think it\'s important for us to help protect them and their homes. I\'m passionate about raising awareness and supporting their protection.\nI would love to hear your thoughts on white tigers or any other animal that captivates your imagination\nLooking forward to our conversation.\nTake care and talk to you soon!\nYours lovingly,\nVikas\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63364,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','He started Subsidiary Alliance.','','','Lord Wellesley	','',0,0,0,NULL),(63365,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Your elder brother has topped his class in his school. Write a informal letter to him, congratulating him on his success.','','','Liya Mendez\n201, Grand Central.\nBlake Colony,\nBorivali West,\nMumbai-400054\n9thOctober, 2022\nDear Neil,\nHeartiest congratulations on your remarkable achievement! I am overjoyed and immensely proud of you for topping your class with flying colours. Your dedication and hard work have truly paid off!\nI still laugh when I think about how you used to sit at the study table with all those textbooks spread out, trying to figure out difficult math formulas and problems. But you showed incredible determination and patience. Your consistent efforts, late nights of studying and hard-work have led you to this remarkable success.\nI\'m so proud to call you my brother. You continue to inspire me with your courage and hard work. Once again, congratulations on this well-deserved success! I have no doubt that you\'ll achieve even greater things in the future.\nYours truly,\nLiya\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63366,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','First Governor General according to the Regulating Act.','','','Lord Warren Hastings','',0,0,0,NULL),(63367,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','He started Dual Government system in Bengal.','','','Robert Clive	','',0,0,0,NULL),(63368,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Recommended English education in India.','','','Lord Macaulay ','',0,0,0,NULL),(63369,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter inviting one of your classmates to participate in the talent show organized by your school.','','','Jasmine Singh\n702, Rose View Apartments, \nMira Nagar, \nCharni Road East, \nMumbai-400055\n10th December, 2022\nDear Sana,\nOur school is organizing a Talent show and I couldn\'t help but think of you. You have incredible vocal talent and I wanted to personally invite you to participate. The Talent Show promises to be filled with excitement, and I am certain that your participation would make it even more spectacular.\nThe Talent Show is scheduled for 26th January, 2023 in the School Auditorium. One can sing, dance, act or show any talent they have. Just remember to keep your performance within three minutes. I believe you\'ll impress everyone with your singing skills and it\'ll be an unforgettable experience for all of us.\nPlease let me know if you\'d like to participate by 30th December. I would love to see you shine on that stage and have a great time together. Looking forward to your positive response!\nYours lovingly,\nJasmine Singh\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63370,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Your class won the Kabaddi competition held in your school. Write a informal letter to your mother sharing this news with her.','','','701, Coral Apartments, \nGandhi Road, \nGoregaon West, \nMumbai-400054 \n3rd October, 2021\nDear Mom,\nI hope you\'re doing well. I have some exciting news to share! Our class, 9A, won the Kabaddi competition at school. We had some new students who turned out to be outstanding players, and they helped us beat the favorites, 9B. It was an incredible victory, and we are so happy!\nDuring the final match, our team played with great spirit and determination. I was amazed to see how our teammate, Mangesh, overcame the strong player from 9B and scored three points for us. It was a proud moment for all of us.\nI just wanted to share this happy moment with you. Your support and encouragement mean the world to me Can\'t wait to tell you more when I see you.\nLove you lots!\nYours lovingly, \nIshant\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63371,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Loyal officer of Chhatrapati Pratapsingh.','','','Rango Bapuji Gupte.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63372,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your friend to express condolences on his grandmother\'s demise.','','','C-32, Blossom Apartments, \n4, Marine Cross Lane, \nVile Parle,\nMumbai-400 032.\n20 August 2022\nDearest Raj,\nAt the outset, I would like to express my heartfelt and deepest condolences on the demise of your Grandma. She truly lived her life spreading joy to everyone around her. And I am grateful to the Lord that she passed away peacefully in her sleep.\nI remember the times when she would visit your home during summer vacations and prepare delicacies every day. Everyone cherished her warmth and love.\nHer loss is something we will have to come to terms with, but she has left us with some wonderful memories to live by.\nKind regards, \nReema\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63373,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your friend about the Great Indian Bustard.','','','6, Damodar Mansion, \nKarve Road, \nPune - 411004.\n16 February 2022\nMy dear Sanjay\nHope alt is well with you. I just read an article on the Great Indian Bustard and thought of wring to you. You are such an animal lover that I was sure you would enjoy the information.\nAccording to the article, the Great Indian Bustard is India\'s heaviest flying bird! It is also one of the heaviest flying bids in the world. It inhabits arid and semi-arid grasslands. However, only about 200 binds of this species are left in the world. It is going extinct because of hunting and the habitat loss due to deforestation and growing human population.\nI think that we must do something to protect this critically endangered species. We should create awareness in our locality about this issue. We may write to the Animal Welfare Board and concerned authorities to help save this species. Public awareness should be raised through соmреtitions, programmes, and posters, especially in schools, playgrounds and public places like cinema halls, markets, railway stations and bus stops.\nThe give this issue a thought. And I will be happy to help you spread the message of saving the Great Indian Bustard before it is too late.\nYour best friend, \nRohit\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63375,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Witte a informal letter to your father to Increase your pocket money.','','','Lake View,\n79, Ridge Road,\nMumbai - 400 102\n15 July 2022\nDearest Daddy,\nHow are you and how is everyone at home? I have settled in well at the hostel. Life away from home is very different and not so easy! But it is also very exciting since you make new friends and enjoy new experiences too\nDaddy, I have to go to the library every day and since there is no canteen facility, I have to carry some additional snacks from my hostel canteen. If you could please increase my pocket money by Rs.1000/-, it would greatly help me manage my expenses. I promise to keep my expenses to the minimum\nThank you,\nYour loving son,\nNeelay\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63376,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How were the officers administrative services appointed? ','','','The officers in the administrative services were appointed through competitive examination known as Indian Civil Services (ICS).','',0,0,0,NULL),(63377,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your coach, telling him/her how much you appreciate him/her.','','','Vimla Apartments,\n33, Dr P. R. Mody Street, \nMumbai-400 003.\n5 September 2022\nDear Arun Sir,\nThank you for being my coach and teaching me the basics of swimming. You have been very patient and supportive throughout my training.\nI truly appreciate the clarity of your instructions. Your insights too have been beneficial to us. You exemplify a perfect balance of strictness and leniency. We are all truly privileged to learn from someone so learned yet so humble.\nI really thank you for all your encouragement and guidance. Also, it is very kind of you to be genuinely interested in the welfare of students. I earnestly pray that may God bless you and always keep you under the mantle of His loving care.\nYours obediently, \nRoy\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63378,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which principle was introduced by the British in the administration of Justice?','','','The British introduced the principle of \'equality before law\' in the administration of justice all over British India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63379,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which factors hindered the growth of new industries in India?','','','The growth of new industries in India was hindered by the lack of British support, capital and experience of management.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63380,2067,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','You have won a prize to visit a wildlife sanctuary or national park in Africa or Australia. Choose a location and read about it. Imagine that you are visiting it and write a informal letter to your friend about it. ','','','Radhika Mansion, \n45, Azad Hind Street, \nMumbai-400028.\n10 December 2022\nMy dear Aditi,\nHow are you and everyone at home? I am sure you must be surprised to receive this letter from me. Do you remember that the last time you were here I had mentioned a talent contest for wildlife enthusiasts? Well, I had submitted an entry on the Pench National Park new Madhya Pradesh poster photographs. And to my utter delight, I won the second prize a trip to the Hwange National Park in Zimbabwe. Naturally, I was thrilled to bits upon hearing this piece of news.\nHwange is the most beautiful place on this planet a lovely home to elephants, lions, cheetah, and leopards. I was lucky to be there in August since it is during this period that the animals gather around man-made water holes in the park for a few sips. I got to see so many that I lost count!!\nHaving seen such beautiful natural surroundings for the first time made me feel so happy and yet so sad. We do not take pride in maintaining our own parks and wildlife sanctuaries. We must realize that these places must also be well-maintained since they too are a part of our environment.\nGive my regards to all at home.\nAffectionately,\nRamona\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63381,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which cash crops were encouraged by the British Government?','','','The British Government gave more encouragement to cash crops like cotton, indigo, tobacco, tea, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63382,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where were the universities established in 1857? ','','','The universities were established in Mumbai, Kolkata and Madras (Chennai) in 1857.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63383,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the effect of development of modern means of transport and communication?','','','The development of modern means of transport and communication helped to improve communication between the people and strengthened their sense of unity between Indians.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63384,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which values shaped the new era in the 19th century Europe?','','','The new era in the 19th century Europe was shaped on the values humanitarianism, democracy, nationalism and liberalism.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63386,1940,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Person</th>\n    <th>Work</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Lord Cornwallis</td>\n    <td>----------</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n<td>----------</td>\n<td>Passed Sati Prohibition Act.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lord Dalhousie</td>\n<td>----------</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>----------</td>\n<td>Established \'Asiatic Society of Bengal\'.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Person</th>\n    <th>Work</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Lord Cornwallis</td>\n    <td>Introduced the Civil Services in India.</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lord William Bentinck</td>\n<td>Passed Sati Prohibition Act.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lord Dalhousie</td>\n<td>Rejected adoption policy and annexed Indian states.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>William Jones</td>\n<td>Established \'Asiatic Society of Bengal\'.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63389,1940,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Civil Services ','','','1) There was a need of bureaucrats to strengthen the British rule in India.\n2) Lord Cornwallis introduced Civil Services which became an important part of the British government.\n3) The territories occupied by the British were divided into districts for administrative convenience. The district administration was headed by Collector.\n4) The officers appointed through the Civil Services (ICS) were taken into administrative services.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63390,1940,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Commercialisation of Agriculture ','','','1) In the pre-British period, farmers used to cultivate food grains to fulfill domestic need as well as need of the village.\n2) The British government gave encouragements to the cultivation of cash crops like indigo, cotton, tobacco, tea, etc.\n3) The shift from cultivation of foodgrains to profit-yielding cash crops is known as Commercialisation of Agriculture.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63391,1940,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Economic Policy of British ','','','1) Capitalist economy prevailed in England due to the Industrial Revolution.\n2) This system was brought to India to nurture the British economy.\n3) It resulted in the economic gains for England but exploitation and impoverishment of India. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63392,1940,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dual Government ','','','1) The British East India Company in order to consolidate rule introduced new system of governance in India known as Dual Government.\n2) It was introduced by Robert Clive in Bengal in 1765 wherein the East India Company took over the revenue collection. The maintenance of law and order was the responsibility of the Nawab of Bengal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63393,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the conditions laid in Subsidiary Alliance?','','','Lord Wellesley signed Subsidiary Alliance with many Indian rulers in 1798. Its conditions were :\n1) Indian rulers should keep the British army in their Court.\n2) They have to pay the company towards the maintenance of these forces in cash or a part of their territory of equivalent amount of revenue.\n3) Without the intervention of the British, they would not have any alliance or declare war with any power.\n4) They should keep the British resident in their court.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63394,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the provisions in Regulating Act of 1773?','','','According to Regulating Act of 1773:\n1) The Governor of Bengal was designated as the Governor General. Lord Warren Hastings became the first Governor General of India.\n2) It gave the Governor General controlling powers over the Bombay and Madras Presidencies.\n3) It provided a committee of four members to assist the Governor General.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63395,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the Pitt\'s India Act of 1784.','','','1) The Pitt\'s India Act of 1784 established a permanent Board of Control to regulate and manage administration of Company in India.\n2) The Board was authorized to issue directives to the Company regarding the governance of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63396,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the Judicial system introduced by the British in India.','','','1) The new Judicial system was introduced in India on the basis of the Judicial system in England.\n2) Accordingly, each district had a Civil and Criminal Court for the respective cases.\n3) High Courts were established to reconsider the judgements delivered by the District courts.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63397,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the functions of the military?','','','1) The functions of the military were to defend the Indian territories under the control of the British.\n2) It was expected to acquire new territories and quell any uprising/revolt against the British India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63398,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the defects in the British Judicial System?','','','The defects in the British Judicial System were as follows :\n1) There were different laws and separate courts for the Europeans.\n2) It was difficult for the Common people to understand the new laws.\n3) It was very expensive to fight a legal case.\n4) The cases got delayed and remained pending for years.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63399,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What reforms were introduced in the Judicial System by the British in India?','','','1) In the pre-British period, laws were different from place to place.\n2) There was difference in judgement on the basis of casteism.\n3) A committee was set up to create a uniform code of law under the leadership of Lord Macaulay.\n4) The Indian Penal Code was enforced all over British India with British principle of Equality before Law.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63400,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about introduction of the English education system?','','','1) The British were in need of Indians who had received English education in order to run the administration.\n2) English education was imparted in India according to the recommendations of Lord Macaulay in 1835.\n3) Indians were introduced to western thoughts, modern reíorms, science and technology.\n4) They realised the need to study their history, culture, religions and also realised the drawbacks.\n5) The western educated Indian middle class was responsible for initiating social reforms in later period.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63402,1940,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Farmers in India became bankrupt.','','','The British made number of changes in the existing system to increase the revenue.\n1) The payment of revenue was made compulsory in cash and within the prescribed time limit.\n2) Land was confiscated if the revenue was not paid in time.\n3) The land revenue collection differed from place to place which resulted in the exploitation of the farmers.\n4) Farmers were forced to sell their produce to the merchants and middlemen at a low price in order to pay revenue.\n5) Farmers mortgaged land to pay tax and became indebted to moneylenders.\nIn this way, the farmers became bankrupt in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63403,1940,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','There was decline of traditional industries in India.','','','1) The British government levied heavy duty on the goods exported from India to England.\n2) The goods imported from England were produced in factories on a large scale and at minimum cost.\n3) The duty levied on them by British was far too less.\n4) So, these goods were cheap as compared to traditional goods.\n5) The Indian artisans found it difficult to compete with low priced British goods.\n6) Eventually, this led to closing down of traditional industries in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63404,1940,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Britishers entered into Maratha politics.','','','1) Marathas had a strong hold on the areas in and around Mumbai which was prime centre of British in western India.\n2) British tried to acquire nearby territories but the Marathas checked their expansion.\n3) It was only after the death of Peshwa Madhavrao, that they got entry in Maratha politics. Raghunathrao in his greed for Peshwaship sought their help.\nThis led to the entry of the British in Maratha politics.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63406,1940,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British Parliament introduced some laws to keep control over the affairs of the company.','','','1) The \'Dual Government\' system was introduced by Robert Clive in Bengal in 1765.\n2) But, sometimes, the officers of the company pocketed money.\n3) As monopoly of trade was given to East India Company, many trading companies in England envied them.\n4) The working system of the company was criticised in the British Parliament.\nSo, to keep control on the affairs of the company, the British Parliament introduced some laws.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63407,1940,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Salary of company officers were increased.','','','1) Lord Cornwallis introduced bureaucracy in India to strengthen the British rule in India.\n2) He restricted the private trade carried out by the company officers.\nFor this reason, he increased the salary of the company officers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63408,1940,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give an account of the Anglo-Maratha war.','','','1) Raghunathrao in his greed for the seat of Peshwa sought help of the British which facilitated their entry into Maratha politics.\n2) Three wars were fought between Marathas and the British between 1774 to 1818.\n3) As the Marathas fought unitedly, the Britishers were defeated in the first Anglo-Maratha War in 1782.\n4) In 1802, Bajirao II entered into Subsidiary Alliance with the British known as the Treaty of Vasai.\n5) The second Anglo-Maratha War took place as some Maratha Sardars opposed this treaty.\n6) Marathas were defeated in the second Anglo-Maratha War.\n7) The defeat of the Marathas led to the increase in interference of the British in the Maratha state.\n8) Bajirao II declared war against the British as he could not tolerate their interference.\n9) He lost the war and surrendered to the British in 1818.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63409,1940,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the public work done by Chhatrapati Pratapsingh.','','','Chhatrapati Pratapsingh did the following public work :\n1) Water tank was built by him on the back side of Yevteshwar temple and Mahadara which supplied water to Satara city.\n2) He built roads and planted trees on both the sides.\n3) Sanskrit, Marathi and English was taught to girls and boys in schools built by him.\n4) Printing press was set up and many books were published.\n5) A book titled \'Sabhaniti\' was printed on polity in 1827.\n6) A road connecting Satara Mahabaleshwar - Pratapgad was built by him. This road was further extended to Mahad.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63410,1940,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the British administrative system benefit India?','','','There were many benefits of British administrative system in India.\n1) The British introduced railways in India.\n2) Telegraph system connected major cities and military stations got connected.\n3) Postal system was also introduced.\n4) These improved communication generated a sense of unity among the people of India.\n5) Coal, metal, sugar, cement and chemical industries also developed gradually.\n6) The western educated Indians learnt the values of humanism, rationalism, democracy, nationalism and liberalism.\n7) Indians felt the need to study Indian history, religion and traditions.\n8) Universities were established at Kolkata, Mumbai and Madras (Chennai).\n9) The newly educated Indian led the social reform movement in later period.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63411,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A book on polity printed during the reign of Chhatrapati Pratapsingh','','','Sabhaniti','',0,0,0,NULL),(63412,1940,'descriptive','1b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Chief of District Administration','','','District Collector','',0,0,0,NULL),(63413,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The kind of economy that prevailed in England','','','Capitalist','',0,0,0,NULL),(63414,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The kinds of courts in every district had','','','Civil and Criminal','',0,0,0,NULL),(63415,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An example of cash crop','','','Indigo/Tobacco/Tea	','',0,0,0,NULL),(63416,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British Officer who recommended English education for India','','','Lord Macaulay','',0,0,0,NULL),(63417,1940,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The cities of India in 1857 where universities were established','','','Kolkata, Mumbai, Chennai','',0,0,0,NULL),(63418,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What led to the entrance of the British in the Maratha politics?','','','The British entered the Maratha politics when Raghunathrao approached them for help to gain the throne, after the death of Peshwa Madhavrao.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63419,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the reason for Marathas proving superior in the first war against the British?','','','The Marathas proved superior in the first war against the British because they faced the British unitedly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63420,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When did the Anglo-Maratha war come to an end?','','','The Anglo-Maratha war came to an in 1782 with the Treaty of Salbai.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63422,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Whom did General Lake defeat to capture the Mughal emperor?','','','General Lake defeated the troops of Daulatrao Shinde, who had the control of the Mughal capital, and thus captured the Mughal emperor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63423,1940,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the British react to the great Indian famine of 1860 to 1900?','','','The British rulers did not take much effort to overcome the great Indian famine of 1860 to 1900 and also did not allot much expenditure for provision of water supply.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63425,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the British establish themselves in India?','','','i. The European powers including the Portuguese, Dutch, French and British reached the Indian coast due to the geographical discoveries and entered the race for capturing Indian market.\nii. When the British reached India for trading, the Portuguese were already settled in India and gave a bitter resistance to the Britishers.\niii. British developed friendly relations with the Portuguese, but still had to face opposition from French, Dutch and local rulers.\nIn order to establish themselves in India, the Britishers had to overcome all these oppositions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63426,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the terms of the Subsidiary Alliance introduced by Lord Wellesley?','','','i. The Subsidiary Alliance was introduced by Lord Wellesley, after he arrived in India in the year 1798.\nii. He wanted to establish British Dominion all over India through this alliance.\niii. The alliance assured the Indian rulers of British military help, but also laid certain terms on them. They were as follows:\na. British Army was to be maintained by the Indian rulers in their court and cash or part of their territory to be paid to the company for the maintenance of this army.\nb. The Indian ruler could not have any alliance or declare war on any other kingdom without the permission of the British.\nc. A British resident would be stationed in the Indian ruler\'s state.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63427,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the events which led to the annexation of the state of Satara by Lord Dalhousie.','','','i. Chhatrapati Pratasingh Satara was in power, even after the Peshwaship had come to an end.\nii. When Britishers signed a treaty with him, Grant Duff got appointed as an officer to assist him in the state affairs.\niii. However, soon the Chhatrapati was dethroned and sent in exile to Kashi, where he died in 1847.\niv. Later, a loyal officer of the Chhatrapati, Rango Bapuji Gupte, fought a legal battle and even went upto England. However, his efforts were unsuccessful.\nv. After this, Lord Dalhousie rejected the adoption policy and annexed the state of Satara.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63428,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the welfare work of Chhatrapati Pratapsingh.','','','Some of the features of the welfare work done by Chhatrapati Pratapsingh are as follows:\ni. Chhatrapati Pratapsingh made provision of water to the city by building a water tank on the back side of Yevteshwar temple and Mahadara in Satara.\nii. He built roads in the city and planted trees on both sides of the road. The road built during his tenure from Satara to Mahabaleshwar to Pratapgad, further extends till Mahad.\niii. He got schools built for both boys and girls and taught Sanskrit, Marathi as well as English.\niv. He established printing press and published several useful books including Sabhaniti, a book on polity printed in 1827.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63429,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the British Parliament introduce laws to keep control over the East India Company?','','','i. In 1765, a Dual Government was introduced by Robert Clive in Bengal, wherein the revenue collection was undertaken by the East India Company and the Nawab of Bengal maintained law and order.\nii. However, money in form of taxes went into the pockets of the company officers and also the Monopoly of trade in India went to the company.\niii. Not only did other trading companies in England envy the East India Company, but the company also received strong criticism in England.\niv. Thereafter, the British Parliament introduced some important laws to keep control over the East India Company.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63430,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the military, police force and the judicial system of the British in India.','','','i. The British military in India had the task of safeguarding the British occupied regions, acquiring new territories and also break down any revolt which may occur against the British in India.\nii. The police force on the other hand maintained law and order in the country.\niii. Apart from this, the British also established a judicial system in India based on the judicial system in England.\niv. There was a Civil as well as a Criminal court in every district to solve the local cases. In case of reconsiderations of the judgements of these courts, a High Court was established.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63431,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was equality established before the law by the British?','','','i. In India, the law differed from place to place in the early times, and different judgements were given based on castes.\nii. However, a committee was setup under Lord Macaulay to create the code of law.\niii. The India Penal Code was enforced all over the British India, in accordance with the British principle of equality before the law.\nThus, the British tried to establish equality before the law.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63432,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the defects in the new British system of law?','','','i. A new code of law was introduced by the committee setup under Lord Macaulay.\nii. However, there were certain defects in this law, as there were separate courts and different laws for Europeans.\niii. Also, the new laws were difficult to understand to the common man.\niv. Apart from this, the legal cases were expensive for the community and cases remained pending for years together.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63433,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the development of new industries in India during the British rule. ','','','Indian businessmen could not come forward in large numbers to start a business due to the lack of the British support. However, there were few who setup their businesses by overcoming these problems.\ni. The first textile mill was started by Kawasjee Nanabhoy Davar in 1854.\nii. Similarly, the first jute mill was started at Rishra in Bengal in 1855.\niii. Jamshedji Tata started the Tata Iron and Steel company at Jamshedpur in 1907.\niv. Also, coal, metal, sugar, cement and chemical industries were started in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63434,1940,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the British encourage English education in India?','','','i. The British required English educated Indians for carrying out administration in India.\nii. Hence, English education started being imparted in India from 1835 on the recommendation of Lord Macaulay.\niii. Through this education, Indians were introduced to the western thoughts, modern reforms, science and technology, etc.\niv. Also, universities were established at Kolkata, Mumbai and Madras (Chennai) in 1857.\nv. The western educated middle class Indian led the social reform movement in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63435,1940,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Parliamentary Laws','','','i. The Regulating Act of 1773 changed the title of the Bengal Governor as the Governor General and made Lord Warren Hastings, the Governor General of India.\nii. This gave him the rights to control the policies of Mumbai and Madras province and also got a committee of four members to assist him.\niii. The Pitt\'s India Act passed in 1784, provided for the appointment of a permanent Board of Control for better regulation and management of affairs of the East India Company in India.\niv. Also, the Parliament passed several new laws in the years 1813, 1833 and 1853, to make several changes in the working of the East India Company.\nv. Thus the British Government gained an indirect control of the administration of East India Company. Also, this emergence of the British rule led to the development of the new administrative system in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63436,1940,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Land Revenue Policy in India during the pre-British era','','','i. The village economic system in India was self sufficient before the British, wherein agriculture and related services, fulfilled the needs of the villages.\nii. In the pre-British era, land revenue was not the main source of income and revenue was based on the type of crop.\niii. The farmer used to be exempted from the taxation in case of loss of crops and the revenue was collected in form of grains.\niv. Also, the land of the farmer\'s was not confiscated even in case of late payment of tax.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63437,1940,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Land Revenue Policy in India during the British era','','','i. The British made several changes in the land revenue system in India.\nii. The area of the land was measured and the tax was fixed according to this area.\nThis tax had to be paid compulsorily in form of cash and in case of failure of payment, the land of the farmer would be confiscated.\niv. Also, the British system of land revenue differed from place to place. This led to the exploitation of the farmers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63438,1940,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Development of transport and communication by British in India','','','i. The British developed modern facilities like transport and communication to facilitate trade as well as for the convenience of administration.\nii. They introduced railway transport in India and the first train ran from Mumbai to Thane in 1853.\niii. The British started the Telegraph system in India in the same year. This led to a good connectivity between all cities and military stations.\niv. They also started the Postal system.\nv. This development in transport and communication impacted the social life of Indian on large scale and brought a sense of unity among them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63439,1940,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bajirao II surrendered to the British.','','','i. Bajirao II signed the Subsidiary Alliance treaty or the Treaty of Vasai with the British in 1802.\nii. However some Maratha sardars did not accept this treaty and this led to the second Anglo Maratha war.\niii. As the British emerged victorious in this war, their interference into the Maratha state increased.\niv. When this became intolerable, Bajirao II waged a war against the British in which he was defeated by the British.\nHence, in 1818, Bajirao II surrendered to the British.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63440,1940,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British started studying the tradition, history, literature, music and flora and fauna of India.','','','i. In the 19th century Europe, values like humanitarianism, democracy, liberalism, etc. were realised. nationalism,\nii. These changes in the western part of the world also impacted India.\niii. In order to carry out administration effectively, the British had to familiarize themselves with different aspects of Indian society.\nThus, the British started studying the tradition, history, literature, music and flora and fauna of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63506,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the wrong pair, correct it and rewrite :\nRegion → Revolting Tribe\n1) Chhota Nagpur -  Kolams\n2) Odisha - Gonds\n3) Bihar - Kolis\n4) Maharashtra - Bhills\n','','','Wrong pair: Bihar - Kolis\nCorrected pair: Bihar - Santhals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63507,1941,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Led the revolt of Paikas','','','Bakshi Jaganbandhu Bidyadhar','',0,0,0,NULL),(63508,1941,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Last Mughal Emperor of Delhi ','','','Bahadur Shah','',0,0,0,NULL),(63509,1941,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Led the revolt of Bhills in Khandesh ','','','Kajarsingh','',0,0,0,NULL),(63510,1941,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Led the revolt in Satpura ','','','Shankarshah','',0,0,0,NULL),(63511,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the Indian sepoys at Meerut rebel against the British?','','','When the news that Mangal Pandey was arrested and hanged reached Meerut cantonment, the entire regiment of the Indian sepoys rebelled against the British.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63512,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which parts of North India did the revolt spread?','','','After Delhi, the revolt spread in Lucknow, Allahabad, Kanpur, Banaras, Bareli and Jhansi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63513,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who fought the Crimean War?','','','The Crimean war was fought  between Britain and Russia.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63514,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which policy did the British adopt to pollute the minds of Indians?','','','The British adopted the policy of \'Divide and Rule\' to pollute the minds of the Indians.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63517,1941,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Paika Rebellion','','','1) There were many independent kingdoms in Odisha in the medieval period.\n2) The kings used to have a standing army known as Paikas.\n3) The kings gave them rent free land for their cultivation and livelihood.\n4) In return, they were supposed to fight along with the king in case of any war.\n5) The English conquered Odisha in 1803 and took over the hereditary rent free lands granted to the Paikas.\n6) This resulted in armed rebellion of the Paikas against the British under the leadership Bidyadhar. of Bakshi Jaganbandhu\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63518,1941,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rebellion of Umaji Naik ','','','1) Exploitation was on a large scale during the Company\'s rule in India. There was rising discontent among the Indians. Sanyasis-Fakirs rebelled in the Bengal province.\n2) Umaji Naik organised the Ramoshis and the local youth and gave a strong fight to the British in Maharashtra.\n3) They drafted a declaration and appealed to the people to support them in their fight against to overthrow the British government.\n4) People in Ahmednagar, Satara, Pune, Nasik, Solapur and Bhor were inspired by them.\n5) Umaji Naik was arrested and hanged to death in 1832 at Pune.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63526,1941,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Paikas made armed rebellion against the British.','','','1) The Odisha in 1803. Britishers conquered\n2) They confiscated the hereditary rent free land granted to Paikas by the kings.\n3) The life of the common man became miserable due to tax imposed on salt by the British.\nThis resulted in armed rebellion of the Paikas against the British in 1817.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63527,1941,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','There was discontent among the Hindu and Muslim sepoys.','','','1) In 1856, the British introduced long Enfield rifles.\n2) The soldiers were required to bite the greased covering of the cartridges of the rifle to open them.\n3) The news spread among the soldiers that the covering of the cartridges contained the fat of cows and pigs.\n4) The religious sentiments of the Hindu and Muslim soldiers were hurt and there was discontent among them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63528,1941,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Indian sepoys could not keep stand in front of the British army.','','','The Indian sepoys could not keep stand in front of the British army because :\n1) The Indian soldiers were brave but they had no military strategies.\n2) They did not have economic strength.\n3) Indians did not have latest and enough stock of arms and experienced army generals like the British.\n4) On the other hand, the British possessed the modern means of transport and communication and were therefore swift in their movements.\n5) This proved that the wars are not fought only on bravery but also through military strategies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63529,1941,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','After the struggle, Indian army was divided on the basis of caste.','','','There was division of military on the basis of caste because :\n1) The British felt that if the Indians came together they would again unite and rebel against the British rule.\n2) Hence, proper care was taken by dividing them on the basis of caste to avoid future conflict.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63530,1941,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The British imposed heavy taxes on Indian industries.','','','1) The British introduced new revenue system with the sole purpose to increase their income.\n2) The taxes were forcibly collected from the farmers.\n3) British goods were sold in India to gain profits.\n4) They imposed heavy taxes on the goods made by local industries.\n5) This trade policy ruined Indian handicraft and textile industries.\n6) Many artisans became unemployed.\nThe British imposed heavy taxes on Indian industries with the sole purpose to ruin the Indian economy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63531,1941,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','There was discontent among the Indian soldiers.','','','1) Indian soldiers were treated with contempt.\n2) They were denied position higher than Subhedar in the army.\n3) They were given low status by the British officers.\n4) They were paid less salary than the British soldiers.\n5) Their allowances were reduced gradually.\nDue to all these factors there was discontent among the Indian soldiers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63532,1941,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mangal Pandey was arrested and hanged.','','','1) The British introduced long Enfield rifles. The soldiers were required to bite the greased covering of the cartridges of the rifle to open them.\n2) The news spread among the Indian soldiers that the covering of the cartridges contained the fat of cows and pigs.\n3) The religious sentiments of the Muslim and Hindu soldiers were hurt.\n4) The angry soldiers who refused to use the cartridges were severely punished.\n5) In retaliation to this unjust practice, Mangal Pandey from Barrackpore cantonment fired at the British officer. So, he was arrested and hanged.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63533,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','What were the social causes behind the struggle of 1857?','','','The social causes behind the struggle of 1857 were:\n1) The Indians felt that Britishers interfered in their culture, traditions and .customs.\n2) The British enacted Sati Prohibition Act and the Widow-Remarriage Act.\n3) These were seen as an interference in the lifestyle of the Indians.\n4) Indians resented it as it was regarded as the destruction of the way of life by the British government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63534,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Why did the Indians fail in the struggle of 1857?','','','There are several reasons for the failure of Indians in the struggle of 1857.\nThey were :\n1) It did not have central leadership so lacked uniformity.\n2) The Indian soldiers who participated in the struggle did not have latest and sufficient weapons.\n3) They were brave but did not have tactics.\n4) On the other hand, the British had a unified leadership, disciplined army, latest weapons and experienced army generals.\n5) The British controlled transport and communication which made their movement swift. \n6) The majority of the rulers of the Princely States kept themselves away from the freedom struggle.\n7) The struggle was severe in Northern India. It did not take place all over India simultaneously.\n8) Rajputana, Punjab, some parts of Bengal and North west India remained aloof.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63535,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','What were the consequences of struggle of 1857?','','','1) The British government realised that there was growing dissatisfaction among the Indians due to Company\'s rule.\n2) The British Parliament passed the Government of India Act in 1858 and took over the responsibility of ruling over people of India thus bringing an end to Company\'s rule.\n3) The Queen issued a Declaration addressing the people of India.\n4) The Governor General designated as the Viceroy of India. was\n5) A new post of Secretary of State was created to look after the affairs of India.\n6) The British changed the composition of army and its internal policies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63536,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','What were the changes in British policy after the struggle of 1857?','','','1) The British accepted the policy of non-interference in social and religious aspects of Indians.\n2) They took care that Indian society did not unite on social grounds.\n3) They took care to see that conflicts on grounds of caste, religion, race, territory kept raging.\n4) They poisoned and polluted the minds of Indians by following the policy of \'Divide and Rule\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63537,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What changes were made in administration of India after the struggle of 1857?','','','After the struggle of 1857, the British Parliament made the following changes in the administration of India :\n1) The rule of the East India Company came to an end.\n2) The Governor General was designated as Viceroy of India. He was entrusted with powers to rule over people of India.\n3) He carried administration under the control of British Parliament.\n4) A new post called Secretary of State was created in the British Government to look after the affairs of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63538,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about Queen\'s Proclamation.','','','1) Queen Victoria of England issued a proclamation addressed to the people of India in 1858.\n2) She declared that the people in India were her subjects and that discrimination would not be made on grounds of race, religion, caste and place of birth.\n3) Government jobs will be given strictly on the basis of merit.\n4) She promised that government will not interfere in the religious matter of the people.\n5) She promised all the treaties made with the Indian rulers will be honoured and that no state would be annexed in future.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63539,1941,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','The Indians whole heartedly fought against the British, including soldiers, zamindars, kings, commanders and common people. The Indian sepoys started the freedom struggle before the fixed time. Initially they succeeded but military strength and administration of British rule was vast. The British crushed down the struggle by all means. The British made quick recovery and within next six months regained the lost territories. Rani Laxmibai, Kunwar Singh, Ahmedullah laid their lives.\nBahadur Shah was imprisoned at Rangoon. Nanasaheb and Begum Hazrat Mahal took shelter in Nepal, Tatya Tope fought for next ten months against the British but due to betrayal he was caught and hanged to death. In this way, by the end of 1858, the British ruthlessly crushed down the struggle.\n','Read the given passage and answer the questions given below :','1) Name the leaders of the struggle who lost their lives on the battlefield.\n2) Who was hanged to death?\n3) Which leaders took shelter in Nepal?\n','','1) Rani Laxmibai, Kunwar Singh, Ahmedullah laid their lives on the battle field.\n2) Tatya Tope fought for ten months but was caught by betrayal and hanged to death.\n3) Nanasaheb Peshwa and Begum Hazrat Mahal took shelter in Nepal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63540,1941,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the reasons of the struggle of 1857 with following points:\n1) Economic		2) Political\n','','','<b>The reasons of 1857 freedom struggle :  Economic</b>\n1) New revenue system introduced by the British to increase their income and impoverish the peasants.\n2) The farmers had to either sell or mortgage their land to pay tax in cash.\n3) In order to sell British goods in India and earn profit they imposed heavy taxes on local industries.\n4) Many artisans lost their livelihood as handicraft and textile industries ruined.\n<b>Political</b>\n1) The British captured many states after winning the Battle of Plassey in 1757.\n2) Lord Dalhousie annexed many princely states on various reasons.\n3) He dethroned the Nawab of Ayodhya and annexed Ayodhya under the pretext of maladministration.\n4) He annexed princely states of Jhansi, Nagpur and Satara through Doctrine of Lapse.\n5) Due to these policies of Lord Dalhousie the Indians had no trust in the British.\nAll these factors created discontent among artisans, princes and other classes which ultimately led to the freedom struggle of 1857.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63541,1941,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the struggles before 1857.','','','There was rising discontent among the Indians which led to many struggles before 1857:\n1) The peasants in Bengal fought against the British from 1763 to 1857 first under the leadership of the Sanyasis and later the Fakirs.\n2) Umaji Naik in Maharashtra organised Ramoshis and the local youths who fought against the British. The Kolis, the Bhills also fought against the British.\n3) They inspired people in Pune, Satara, Ahmednagar, Solapur, Nasik, Bhor, etc.\n4) Santhals in Bihar, Kolams in Chhota Nagpur and Gonds as well as Paikas in Odisha rebelled against the British.\n5) The Gadkaris in Kolhapur and Fond-Sawant in Konkan fought against the British.\n6) Zamindars, Princes and other classes in Gujarat, Rajasthan and South India gave a strong fight to the British.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63542,1941,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the scope of the struggle increase?','','','1) Mangal Pandey shot a British officer at Barrackpore cantonment who forced him to use cartridges greased with the fat of cows and pigs.\n2) Mangal Pandey was arrested and hanged. News spread like fire to other cantonments.\n3) The entire regiment of Indian soldiers at Meerut battled and marched towards Delhi.\n4) On reaching Delhi, the rebels proclaimed Bahadur Shah as the Emperor of India.\n(5) It spread to the whole of north India. The soldiers in the cantonments from Bihar to Rajputana participated in the revolt.\n6) The revolt spread to Lucknow, Allahabad, Kanpur, Banaras, Bareli and Jhansi.\n7) It spread to the whole of north India and in Nagpur, Satara, Kolhapur and Nargund, Khandesh in western India.\n8) Many princely states, peasants and about 400 female Bhills participated in revolt at Khandesh to free themselves from the oppressive rule of the British.\n9) The struggle was led by Bahadur Shah the Mughal Emperor, Nanasaheb Peshwa, Rani Laxmibai, Tatya Tope, Kunwar Singh of Bihar and Begum Hazrat Mahal of Ayodhya.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63543,1941,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the struggle fail?','','','The reasons for the failure are as follows:\n1) The struggle remained limited to the Northern part and it did not spread all over India at the same time.\n2) Its intensity was severe in North India but Rajputana, Punjab but parts of Bengal and North-west India remained aloof.\n3) Many princely states remained loyal to the British which reduced its intensity.\n4) The majority of the rulers of the Indian states and the educated Indians kept away from the struggle.\n5) Though the soldiers were brave they did not use military tactics.\n6) As there was no commonly accepted leader to fight the British, there was no uniformity of the struggle.\n7) On the other hand, the British had economic strength, experienced generals, disciplined army, latest weapons and modern means of transport and communication which made movements of the British swift.\n8) The naval strength of the British was vast, while rebels were isolated.\n','',0,0,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ep_questions` VALUES (63544,1941,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the places from where these leaders led the revolt-\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Revolutionary Leaders</th>\n    <th>Places</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) Rani Lakshmi Bai</td>\n    <td>-------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii) Bahadur Shah</td>\n    <td>-------</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii) Tatya Tope</td>\n    <td>--------</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv) Shankar Shah</td>\n    <td>--------</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Revolutionary Leaders</th>\n    <th>Places</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) Rani Lakshmi Bai</td>\n    <td>Jhansi</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii) Bahadur Shah</td>\n    <td>Delhi</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii) Tatya Tope</td>\n    <td>Kanpur</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv) Shankar Shah</td>\n    <td>Satpura</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63545,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the British interfere in the daily life of Indians?','','','The British interfered in culture, tradition and customs of India social customs by introducing reforms like sati prohibition and widow remarriage, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63546,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On which grounds was the Nawab of Ayodhya dethroned?','','','The Nawab of Ayodhya was dethroned on grounds of maladministration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63547,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which weapon was provided to the Indian sepoys in 1856?','','','The Indian sepoys were provided with the long enfield rifles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63548,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Whom did the revolutionaries hand the leadership of the freedom struggle to?','','','The revolutionaries handed the leadership of the freedom struggle to the Mughal Emperor Bahadur Shah.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63549,1941,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which regions was the nature of the revolt fierce?','','','The revolt was fierce in Delhi, Kanpur, Lucknow, Jhansi and West Bihar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63550,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What role did Umaji Naik play in the protest against the British before 1857?','','','i. Umaji Naik gave a strong fight to the British by organising the Ramoshis as well as the local youth for a rebellion.\nii. They were determined to overthrow the government for which they drew a declaration and appealed to the people.\niii. People of Ahmednagar, Satara, Bhor, Nasik, Pune and Solapur were inspired by the actions of Umaji Naik.\niv. Umaji Naik was arrested and hanged in Pune in 1832.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63551,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the Indian tribes rebel against the British?','','','i. The livelihood of the Indian tribes and forest communities was dependent on forest resources.\nii. However, the British laws put restrictions on it, which angered the Indian tribes and forest communities.\niii. Thereafter, Bihar, Kolam area of Chota Nagpur, Gonds of Orissa, Santhals of Bihar fought against the British.\niv. The British were also challenged by the Gadkaris from Kolhapur and Fond-Sawant from Konkan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63552,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the various small rebellions before 1857 turned into an armed rebellion?','','','i. Various small rebellions took place in different parts of the country before 1857.\nii. As they were local in nature, the British were able to forcibly break them down.\niii. This made the people unhappy and the discontent among diverse classes grew.\nThis discontent grew and blew out in form of armed rebellion of 1857.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63553,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the economic causes which led to the revolt of 1857?','','','i. In order to increase their income, the British introduced new revenue system.\nii. They forcibly extracted taxes from the farmers, which led to the collapse of the agricultural system.\niii. In accordance to their policy of selling British goods in India and enjoying the profit, the British imposed heavy taxes on the local industries.\niv. This ruined the handicraft and textile industry of India and many Indian artisans became unemployed.\nThese were some of the economic causes which led to a growing discontent in the minds of the Indians against the British.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63554,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which policies of Lord Dalhousie made Indians lose trust in the British?','','','i. Lord Dalhousie annexed several princely states through various different methods.\nii. He dethroned the Nawab of Ayodhya on the grounds of maladministration.\niii. Thereafter, the princely states of Satara, Nagpur and Jhansi were annexed by him on the doctrine of lapse.\nDue to these policies of Lord Dalhousie, the Indians lost trust in the British.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63555,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What led to the discontent among the Indian soldiers in the British Army?','','','i. The British officers gave low status to the Indian soldiers.\nii. They were not allowed to rise above the post of a Subhedar.\niii. Also, the Indian soldiers were paid less as compared to the British soldiers.\niv. Further, their allowances were also reduced gradually.\nThis led to a growing discontent among the Indian soldiers in the British Army.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63556,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the British crush the struggle of 1857?','','','i. The Indian sepoys started the freedom struggle and fought it whole heartedly against the British with the help of soldiers, zamindars, kings, commanders and common people.\nii. They succeeded initially but were not able to withstand the vast military strength and the administration of British rule.\niii. The British not only crushed down the struggle by all means but also made quick recovery by regaining the lost territories within the next six months.\niv. Rani Laxmibai and Kunwar Singh and Ahmedullah lost their lives, while Emperor Bahadur Shah was imprisoned at Rangoon. Also, Nanasaheb and Begum Hazrat Mahal took shelter in Nepal. Tatya Tope who fought. for the next ten months against the British, was finally caught due to betrayal and was hanged to death.\nThus, by the end of 1858, the British ruthlessly crushed down the revolt.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63557,1941,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the Indian army reorganized after the War of Independence?','','','After the War of Independence of 1857, the Indian army was reorganised as follows:\ni. The number of British soldiers in the Indian army was increased.\nii. English officers were located at strategic locations.\niii. Important units like the artillery units were exclusively under the control of British. \niv. In order to avoid unity of Indians to rebel against the British, the Indian Army was divided on the basis of caste.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63558,1941,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The first outburst of the war of independence of 1857','','','i. The British provided long enfield rifles to the Indian sepoys which used cartridges smeared with the fats of cows and pigs.\nii. Since, the end of these cartridges had to be bitten off, it hurt the religious sentiments of the sepoys and they refused to use the cartridges. Due to this, they had to face disciplinary action.\niii. As a reaction to this unjust nature of British officers, Mangal Pandey from Barrackpore cantonment shot an English officer, for which he was arrested and hanged to death.\niv. Thereafter, the entire regiment of Indian sepoys at Meerut took part in the freedom struggle and marched towards Delhi.\nv. This march was voluntarily joined by thousands of Indians on the way towards Delhi.\nvi. The protestors handed over the leadership to the Mughal Emperor Bahadur Shah and declared him as the Emperor of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63559,1941,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Leadership of the struggle of 1857','','','i. The Mughal Empire weakened during the 18th century. At this time, invaders like Nadirshah Abdali attacked India.\nii. Since, the Mughal Empire could not react to these foreign invasions; the Marathas took up the responsibility of safeguarding India and fought against Abdali at Panipat.\niii. Further, the descendant of Mughals in the freedom struggle of 1857, Bahadur Shah, was not capable of fighting against the British.\niv. Hence, Nanasaheb Peshwa, Rani Laxmibai of Jhansi and Tatya Tope took up the charge of the freedom struggle thus giving rise to Hindu-Muslim unity.\nv. The revolt was led in various parts by people like Maulavi Ahmed Allah, Kunwar Singh, the Mughal commander Bakht Khan, Begum Hazrat Mahal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63560,1941,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Queen\'s Proclamation post freedom struggle of 1857','','','i. After the struggle of 1857, Queen Victoria of England issued a Declaration which was addressed to the people of India.\nii. It mentioned that, all Indians were England\'s progeny.\niii. It also assured that there would be no discrimination on the basis of race, religion, caste or birth place and that Government services will be given on merit.\niv. It also gave an assurance that there won\'t be any interference in religious matters.\nv. The declaration made clear that the British crown will honour all treaties and agreements made with the rulers of the princely states by the East India Company and that no princely state would be annexed thereafter.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63602,1942,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','He founded Hindu College at Kolkata.','','','Raja Rammohan Roy','',0,0,0,NULL),(63603,1942,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','First president of Prarthana Samaj.','','','Dr. Atmaram Pandurang Tarkhadkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(63604,1942,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','\'Go Back to the Vedas\' was the slogan of this Institution.','','','Arya Samaj	','',0,0,0,NULL),(63605,1942,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','He represented Hinduism at the Parliament of Religions at Chicago in 1893.','','','Swami Vivekananda.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63606,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the wrong pair:\nReformer    →  Work\n1) Dadoba Pandurang Tarkhadkar - Established Paramhansa Sabha\n2) Abdul Latif - The Mohammedan Literary Society\n3) Dr. Keshav Baliram Hedgewar - Founded Hindu Mahasabha\n4) V. D. Savarkar - Built Patit Pavan temple at Ratnagiri\n','','','Wrong pair: Dr. Keshav Baliram Hedgewar - Founded Hindu Mahasabha \nCorrected pair: Dr. Keshav Baliram Hedgewar - Founded Rashtriya Swayam-sevak Sangh.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63607,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What message was given by Swami Vivekanand to the Indian youth?','','','\'Arise, Awake and stop not till the goal is achieved\' was the message given by Swami Vivekanand to the Indian youth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63608,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the work of Singh Sabha.','','','The Singh Sabha worked to achieve reforms, to spread education among the Sikh community and bring in modernisation among them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63609,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the principles of Prarthana Samaj?','','','The opposition to idol worship, monotheism and opposition to rituals were the principles of Prarthana Samaj.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63610,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which social reformers worked for the cause of widow remarriage?','','','Pandit Ishwarchandra Vidyasagar, Vishnushastri Pandit and Vireshlingam Pantalu worked for the cause of widow remarriage.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63611,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who started \'Anath Balikashram\'?','','','Maharshi Dhondo Keshav Karve started Anath Balikashram, an orphanage for girls, to give education to all women so that they become independent.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63612,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who received the Nobel Prize and in which field?','','','Rabindranath Tagore received Nobel in the field of literature and C. V. Raman for Science.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63613,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was The Mohammedan Anglo Oriental College later known as?','','','The Mohammedan Anglo Oriental College was later known as the Aligarh Muslim University.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63614,1942,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Ramkrishna Mission','','','1) Swami Vivekananda, a close disciple of Ramkrishna Paramhansa, founded the Ramkrishna Mission in 1897.\n2) The mission carried out social work like providing help to famine stricken people, patients and gave medical help to the poor and worked for female education.\n3) It taught people service to humanity is true religion and worked towards spiritual progress of the people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63615,1942,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Reforms for women by Savitribai Phule','','','1) Savitribai Phule, wife Mahatma Phule, advocated education along with him. of women\n2) She supported her husband in his efforts to start first school for girls at Bhide Wada in Pune.\n3) She continued her work in the field of education though she faced severe criticism from the society.\n4) She put great efforts in women reform movement which resulted in putting an end to many unjust practices.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63616,1942,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prarthana Samaj','','','1) Paramhansa Sabha was dissolved and some of its members formed Prarthana Samaj.\n2) Dr. Atmaram Pandurang was its first President.\n3) They opposed idol worship, monotheism and advocated prayers and devotional songs instead of rituals in place of worship of God.\n4) The important contribution of Prarthana Samaj in reforming the society was that it started orphanage, women\'s education institutes, night schools for workers and society for Dalits.\n5) The prestige of Prarthana Samaj rose immensely due to the enrollment of young graduates from Mumbai University.\n6) Justice Ranade, Dr. R. G. Bhandarkar carried the work of Prarthana Samaj forward.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63617,1942,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sir Sayyad Ahmed Khan ','','','1) Sir Sayyad Ahmad Khan worked for the cause of Muslims.\n2) He believed that the Muslims would not make progress without acquiring western education and science.\n3) He founded \'The Mohammedan Anglo Oriental College\' which later became Aligarh Muslim University.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63618,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the contribution of Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde.','','','1) Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde was member of Prarthana contributed in reforming society. Samaj,\n2) He started the \'Depressed Class Mission\'.\n3) He tried to solve problems in society through this mission.\n4) He organised conference against the practice of Devdasi in Mumbai.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63619,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the efforts taken to unite Hindu Society.','','','1) Hindu Mahasabha was formed in 1915 to achieve respectful position of Hindu community and protect it.\n2) Pandit Madan Mohan Malviya founded the \'Banaras Hindu University\'.\n3) Dr. Keshav Baliram Hedgewar established Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh in 1925 at Nagpur to set up a disciplinary and virtuous organisation of Hindu youth.\n4) Patit Pawan Temple built by V. D. Savarkar at Ratnagiri was open to all castes of Hindu religion. He also organized common dining programmes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63620,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give a brief account of the work of women social reformers for the emancipation of women.','','','The women reformers contributed in the following way to improve the condition of women:\n1) Savitribai Phule faced severe criticism of society but continued her work in the field of education.\n2) Tarabai Shinde wrote the book \'Stri Purush Tulana\' in which she fiercely put her views about the rights of women.\n3) Pandita Ramabai founded the Sharada Sadan and took care of disabled women and children.\n4) Ramabai Ranade founded the Seva Sadan Institute. She started the Nursing course for women as well as demanded the right to vote for them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63621,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the outcome of women reform movement.','','','1) The women\'s reforms movement resulted in putting an end to many unjust practices in the society.\n2) They voiced their problems and made efforts to find solution to them.\n3) The women got opportunities to prove their capabilities in different fields.\n4) Women started expressing their ideas, thoughts through writing.\n5) Their performance flourished in every sphere of life due to education.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63622,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What changes came about in the field of Science, Art and Literature during Indian Renaissance?','','','The following changes were seen in the field of Science, Art and Literature during Indian Renaissance :\n<b>(A) Science</b>\n1) C. V. Raman received the Nobel Prize in Science.\n2) Many books were written on science which emphasized scientific outlook.\n3) People realised the importance of experimentation and scientific outlook for progress of the country.\n<b>(B) Art</b>\n1) Music became more popular and people-oriented.\n2) A new school of painting combining traditional Indian style of painting with the western techniques emerged.\n<b>(C) Literature</b>\n1) Rabindranath Tagore received the Nobel Prize in literature.\n2) Stories and novels gave inspiration in gaining independence and expressed thoughts on social reforms.\n3) Women took to writing.\n4) New magazines and newspapers became sources of inspiration and political awakening.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63623,1942,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The social and religious reform movement began in India.','','','1) With the spread of English education in India, there was spread of new ideas, new thoughts, new philosophy.\n2) Indians got introduced to western thoughts and culture.\n3) They wanted to create a society based on principles of Humanity, Equality and Fraternity.\n4) They realised that the flaws like superstitions, casteism, old customs, class system and lack of critical outlook is responsible for the backwardness of India.\n5) This association was responsible for social and religious reform in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63624,1942,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Mahatma Phule conducted a strike of Barbers.','','','1) There was α custom of Keshavapan, i.e. shaving head of widows in India.\n2) In order to oppose this unjust custom, Mahatma Phule conducted a strike of Barbers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63625,1942,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The condition of women was miserable in the beginning of nineteenth century.','','','The condition of women during the British period was very miserable in India, because:\n1) They had no right to education.\n2) There was no equality between men and women.\n3) Women were victims of child marriage, dowry system, sati, Keshavapan, opposition to widow remarriage.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63626,1942,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write briefly about Indian Renaissance. ','','','1) The modern educated Indians realised that the unhealthy social conditions and customs like casteism, superstitions, old customs, class system and lack of critical outlook had arrested the progress of India.\n2) Rise in the spread of new ideas, new thoughts, new philosophy marked the beginning of modern age.\n3) It was necessary to eradicate the flaws and undesirable tendencies in order to create a new society based on principles of Humanity, Equality and Fraternity.\n4) They started finding new ways for development of society and country. Educated thinkers started social awareness through writings.\n5) This intellectual awakening in the contemporary society in India is called the Indian Renaissance.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63627,1942,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give a brief account of the work of social reformers for the betterment of women.','','','1) Raja Rammohan Roy launched agitations against practice of Sati.\n2) It led to the enactment of the Sati Prohibition Act in 1829.\n3) He advocated widow remarriage and female education and opposed Purdah system.\n4) Gopal Hari Deshmukh (Lokhitwadi) criticized the unjust social customs related to women and advocated equality of men and women through his writings in \'Shatapatre\'.\n5) Mahatma Phule gave importance to girl\'s education. He started first school for girls at Bhide Wada in Pune.\n6) Through his writings Babasaheb Ambedkar exposed injustice inflicted on women.\n7) Mahatma Gandhi advocated education for women.\n8) Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar, Vishnushastri Pandit and Vireshlingam Pantalu strove for the recognition of the right to remarriage for the widows.\n9) Gopal Ganesh Agarkar gave his staunch opinion about child marriage and law of consent in his newspaper \'Sudharak\'.\n10) Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde organised a conference to oppose practice of Devdasi.\n11) Maharshi Dhondo Keshav Karve founded the Anath Balikashram for orphan girls and later the first Women\'s University.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63628,1942,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Name of social Reformer</th>\n    <th>Organisation</th>\n<th>Newspaper/Book</th>\n    <th>Work of the Organisation </th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) Raja Rammohan Roy</td>\n    <td>--------</td>\n<td>Sambad Kaumudi</td>\n    <td>--------------</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n    <td>ii) ----------</td>\n    <td>Arya Samaj</td>\n<td>------------</td>\n    <td>------------</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n    <td>iii) Mahatma Phule</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n<td>Gulamgiri</td>\n    <td>-------------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Name of social Reformer</th>\n    <th>Organisation</th>\n<th>Newspaper/Book</th>\n    <th>Work of the Organisation </th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) Raja Rammohan Roy</td>\n    <td>Brahmo Samaj</td>\n<td>Sambad Kaumudi</td>\n    <td>Promotion of widow remarriage and female education.</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n    <td>ii) Swami Dayanand Saraswati</td>\n    <td>Arya Samaj</td>\n<td>Satyarth Prakash</td>\n    <td>Established many education institutions.</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n    <td>iii) Mahatma Phule</td>\n    <td>Satyashodhak Samaj</td>\n<td>Gulamgiri</td>\n    <td>Formation of society on the basis of equality</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63629,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What message did Swami Vivekanand give to the Indian youth?','','','Swami Vivekanand gave the Indian youth the message: \'Arise, Awake and stop not till the goal is achieved\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63630,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who advocated gender equality through his writings in Shatapatre?','','','Gopal Hari Deshmukh, popularly known as Lokhitwadi, advocated gender equality through his writings in Shatapatre.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63631,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who took up special efforts for gaining permission to widow remarriage?','','','The people who took up special efforts for gaining permission to widow remarriage included Pandit Ishwarchandra Vidyasagar, Vishnushastri Pandit and Vireshlingam Pantulu.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63632,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What opinion did Gopal Ganesh Agarkar present in his newspaper Sudharak?','','','Gopal Ganesh Agarkar gave his staunch opinion about child marriage and law of consent in his newspaper Sudharak.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63633,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the intention of Pandita Ramabai in founding the Sharada Sadan?','','','The intention of Pandita Ramabai behind founding the Sharada Sadan was to take up the responsibility of taking care of disabled children and women.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63634,1942,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which course for women was started by Ramabai Ranade?','','','Ramabai Ranade started the Nursing Course for women through the medium of Seva Sadan institute.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63635,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in detail about the Brahmo Samaj?','','','i. Brahmo Samaj was established in Bengal by Raja Rammohan Roy in 1828.\nii. He promoted the ideology of Advait philosophy after studying several languages and religions.\niii. Some of the principles of the Brahmo Samaj included monotheism, caste and class equality, protest against rituals and following the way of prayers.\niv. While it promoted widow remarriage and female education, practices like sati, child marriage, purdah system, etc. were opposed by it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63636,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about role of Mahatma Phule in bringing about the social reform in India.','','','i. Mahatma Jyotirao started Phule the Satyashodhak Samaj in 1873, which worked on the principle of formation of society based on equality.\nii. The members of this samaj not only protested against untouchability but also advocated education among the depressed class and women.\niii. Mahatma Phule severely criticised the customs and traditions which discriminated between men and women.\niv. He also started the first school for girls at Bhide Wada in Pune, established the Bal Hatya Pratibandhak Gruha in his own house and also tried to eradicate the practice of keshavapan by conducting the strike of barbers.\nv. He brought about the reformation of the society - through the medium of books like Brahmananche Kasab, Gulamgiri, Sarvajanik Satya Dharma and Shetkaryacha Asud.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63637,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the social reforms among the Sikhs?','','','i. In order to achieve reforms in Sikh religion, the Singh Sabha was established at Amritsar.\nii. It worked for spread of education among the Sikh community.\niii. It also worked towards bringing about modernisation in the Sikh community.\niv. The tradition of reformation work among the Sikhs was further carried ahead by the Akali movement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63638,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the condition of the women in India during the expansion of the British rule?','','','i. The condition of women in India during the expansion of the British rule was miserable.\nii. They were not treated equal to men and were not given right to education.\niii. Also, various malpractices like child marriages, dowry system, sati, keshavapan, opposition to widow remarriage, etc. prevailed in the Indian society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63639,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the work Maharshi Dhondo Karve for upliftment of women.','','','i. Maharshi Dhondo Karve started an orphanage for girls named Anath Balikashram.\nii. He intended to give education to all women and make them stand on their own feet.\niii. His efforts led to the setup of the first women\'s university in the 20th century.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63640,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What changes were seen in women due to the women\'s reform movement?','','','i. Women\'s reform movement closed down many unjust practices in the society and broke out the voice for problems against the women.\nii. Due to this, women started putting up their own thoughts through their writings.\niii. Education also improved their performance in every sector of life.\niv. Further they also contributed a lot during the freedom struggle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63641,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give a brief outline of the movement in Hindu society.','','','i. The Hindu Mahasabha was established in 1915, in order to give a respectable position to the Hindu society.\nii. Also, the Banaras Hindu University was established by Pandit Madan Mohan Malviya.\niii. Further, Keshav Baliram Hedgewar established the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangha at Nagpur in the year 1925, with an aim to set up a disciplinary and virtuous organisation of Hindu youth.\niv. The \'Patit Pawan Temple\' was established in Ratnagiri for all castes of Hindu religion, by V. D Savarkar. It arranged common dinning programmes for all.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63642,1942,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the development in the field of literature, art and science during the renaissance period in India.','','','A lot of development took place in the fields of literature, art and science along with the social and political reform movement in India.\ni. Rabindranath Tagore and C. V. Raman received the Nobel Prize in the field of literature and science respectively during this period, which led to the building of a Modern India.\nii. Also, several stories, novels and other form of writing published during this period, inspired the Indians to gain independence and also expressed thoughts on social reforms. Women also took up writing in this period.\niii. Many newspapers and magazines were also published which brought about social reform and political awakening.\niv. Art and music too developed by combining Indian styles and western techniques. A new school of paintings also came forward.\nv. Several books based on science were written and people realized that for the progress of the country experimentation and scientific attitude are important.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63643,1942,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prarthana Samaj','','','i. The Paramhansa Sabha formed by Dadoba Pandurang Tarkhadkar in 1848, got dissolved and some of its members founded the Prarthana Samaj with Dadoba\'s brother Dr. Atmaram Pandurang as its first president.\nii. While enrolment of young graduates from Mumbai University increased the prestige of the institution, its work was carried forward by Justice Ranade and Dr. R. G. Bhandarkar.\niii. The principles of the Prarthana Samaj included opposition to idol worship and rituals and acceptance of monotheism. The institution also stressed on devotion and prayers.\niv. In order to reform the society, the institution started orphanage, women\'s education institutes, night schools for workers and society for dalits.\nv. As an attempt to solve the social problems, a member of the institution, Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde established the \'Depressed Classes Mission\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63644,1942,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Contribution of Swami Dayanand Saraswati','','','i. Swami Dayanand Saraswati founded the Arya Samaj in 1875, whose slogan was \'Go Back to Vedas\'.\nii. He wrote the book \'Satyarth Prakash\' which was a commentary on Vedas. In this book he stated that ancient vedic religion was true religion and had no place for casteism.\niii. He also promoted gender equality.\niv. Arya Samaj had branches all over India and it helped to establish many educational institutions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63645,1942,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Religious reforms among the Muslims','','','i. The social reform among the Muslim community was started by Abdul Latif by establishing \'The Mohammedan Literary Society\' in Bengal.\nii. Thereafter, Sir Syed Ahmad Khan established the \'The Mohammedan Anglo Oriental College\', which later developed into the \'Aligarh Muslim University\'.\niii. He not only advocated western science and technology, but also had a firm belief that Muslims could make progress only by studying modern education and science.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63646,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Monsoon','','','                                                         <b>Monsoon</b>\nThe monsoon season begins in the month of June. It is the time of the year when we take out our raincoats and umbrellas, and keep them ready for use. The first showers of rain bring freshness in the air. The trees look green and there are puddles of water everywhere. The rivers and ponds are filled to the brim with water. Young children often like to splash water in the rain and sail their paper boats in the water collected on the streets. The onset of monsoon gives everyone a relief from the summer heat and dust.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63647,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Arunachal Pradesh','','','                                                 <b>Arunachal Pradesh</b>\nArunachal Pradesh situated in the north-eastern part of India, is also known as the Land of the Rising Sun or the Land of the Dawnlit Sun! The people living in this region are simple, hospitable and belong to several tribes who speak their own languages. Their diet is largely made up of rice and vegetables, accompanied with fish and meat. Arunachal Pradesh is a nature lovers\' heaven. The land here is spotted with monasteries, lush green forests, lakes, waterfalls and deep valleys. There is a great variety of wildlife present here.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63648,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Television','','','                                                          <b>Television</b>\nWith the advancement of science and technology, television has replaced the radio. Today we can not only hear what is going on around us, but also see for ourselves the exact state of affains a over the world. Television plays a large role in educating the masses. It gives information, impans knowledge as well as entertains us. It is a source of employment as individuals get the scopes display their acting, singing, dancing and cooking skills. Advertisements on television help funding the programmes that are aired and business houses are able to advertise their produts to the masses very easily in the process. However, television also has an adverse effect. It makes people lazy. Outdoor games and sports have also taken a back seat, as children would rather be cooped up indoors, watching their favourite entertainment programme, rather than going out and enjoying the fresh air these days. It kills the habit of reading in a person and books sit on the shelves gathering dust.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63649,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Kabaddi','','','                                                                    <b>Kabaddi</b>\nKabaddi is an ancient sport which originated in India. It has now become popular in many countries and is a part of many international events these days. While in these competitions international rules are followed, various Kabaddi styles are followed in different parts of India In Kabaddi, two teams of seven players each face each other on a flat, rectangular court, which is divided by a mid-line. The game is usually played in two halves with a halftime, break in between, after which the teams exchange their sides in the court. To play this game, each team sends \'raiders across the mid-line to the other team. The \'raider\' tries to \'tag\' the opponents and run back all in one breath. To show that he hasn\'t inhaled again, he has to chant \'kabaddi-kabaddi\' continuously In case, the player inhales again, he is \'out\'. The opponents try to catch the \"raider\' and stop him from going back to his half till he loses his breath and has to inhale again. If the player manages to get back to his half successfully, all the players he has tagged and all those who have touched him are declared \'out\'. For each player who is declared out, the opposite team scores a point. In case, all seven players in a team are \'out\', the opposite team scores bonus points. The team with the maximum points wins the match. The game of Kabaddi requires good health, muscular strength strategic skills and a lot of practice. Kabaddi is the National Sport of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63650,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Assam','','','                                                           <b>Assam</b>\nThe land lying between the beautiful valleys of Brahmaputra and Barak rivers is called Assam or Ahom. It is popularly known as the Gateway to the Seven Sisters. The people here are hospitable and the cuisine is unique and full of flavour. Assam is known for its artistic heritage, exotic dance forms and elegant temples. The rivers ensure that its territory is lush and fertile. The land boasts of wetlands, orchards, tea estates and scenic forests and parks. Kaziranga National Park and Manas National Parks are World Heritage Sites. The land is also rich in petroleum, natural gas, coal, limestone and other minor minerals. The hand-woven silks of Assam are exquisite and the most sought after in the world of fashion. Assam is known as the land of fairs and festivals and Bihu is the most important festival of this region. The region is also famous for its cane and bamboo work.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63651,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Cricket','','','                                                             <b>Cricket</b>\nCricket is a widely popular and cherished sport worldwide. Two teams with eleven players each play witha bat and hall. The batting team scores runs by hitting and running between points, while the bowling van aims to get them out. Cricket has different formats like Test matches, One-Day Internationals (ODis), and Twenty20 (T20) matches, suited for different excitement levels and time. Cricket started in England in the 16th century and has changed over the years. Now-a-days, cricket has many fans, and matches draw lang crowds who come to watch and enjoy the game.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63652,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Himalayas','','','                                                                  <b>Himalayas</b>\nThe Himalayas are a magnificent and tall mountain range located in Asia. They stretch across sevenl countries, including India, Nepal, Bhutan and China. These mountains are truly awe-inspiring with ther snow-capped peaks and lush green valleys. The Himalayas are home to some of the world\'s highest peaks such as Mount Everest, which is the tallest mountain on Earth. The Himalayas also hold cultural and spiritual significance for the people living in the surrounding regions. Many ancient temples, monasteries and traditions are connected to these majestic mountains.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63653,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Diwali','','','                                                                  <b>Diwali</b>\nDiwali is also known as the Festival of Lights. It is a joyous and popular festival celebrated by millions of people in India and around the world. It is a time of happiness and togetherness, filled with the sparkle of lights, colourful decorations and delicious treats. During Diwali, families and friends come together to light oil lamps, brightening up their homes and surroundings. People also exchange gifts and sweets as a way to express love and good wishes. Diwali is about good winning over evil and starting anew. It\'s a cultural and spiritual festival celebrated in different ways. People light fireworks and feel happy and united. Diwali brings joy and positivity to everyone, making it a truly cherished festival for people all around the world.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63654,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Parent\'s Day','','','                                                          <b>Parent\'s Day</b>\nParent\'s Day is a special day to honor and appreciate our parents for their love, care and guidance. It\'s a time for our families to come together and show gratitude to our parents for everything they do for us. On this day, we take a moment to think about the sacrifices our parents make to raise and support us. Many of us use this opportunity to spend quality time with our parents, showing them how much we love and care for them. Some families go out for a meal or engage in activities that make our parents happy. Parent\'s Day reminds us bow important our family is and how our parents play a significant role in shaping our lives. It\'s a chance for us to say \"thank you\" and make their day extra special.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63655,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Global Warming','','','                                                            <b>Global Warming</b>\nGlobal warming is a serious problem that affects our planet\'s climate. It happens when the Earth\'s temperature rises because of the build-up of certain gases in the atmosphere like carbon dioxide. These gases come from activities like burning fossil fuels and cutting down trees. As the Earth gets warmer, it leads to changes in weather patterns, rising sea levels and melting ice caps. It can also cause extreme weather events like hurricanes and heat waves, harming humans, plants and animals. Global warming is a big problem that needs all countries to work together to cut down on greenhouse gases and protect the planet for the future.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63656,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Disadvantages of children not going to school','','','                                        <b>Disadvantages of children not going to school</b>\nSchool is an institution that provides a platform for children to grow into responsible adults. If children didn\'t go to school, they would not grow up to be learned and knowledgeable citizens. They would miss the fundamentals of education and fail to be productive in life. They would also miss out on the fun of growing up with children of their own age. A school is a place where one learns not only various subjects, but even the lessons of life. Thus, attending school becomes a very important part of one\'s life that one cannot afford to miss.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63657,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Characteristics of a good Kabaddi player','','','                                                 <b>Characteristics of a good Kabaddi player</b>\nKabaddi is an ancient Indian game that is now played all across the world. It is a unique sport that encourages the players to work together as a team. In order to become a good Kabaddi player, one needs to be swift, strong and active. One should develop muscular strength and also master strategic moves through practice. To become a quick player, one should learn to run fast, which will also build one\'s stamina. Another skill that a Kabaddi player needs is the ability to hold his/her breath when in the opponent\'s space. This skill can be developed with a steady practice of yoga. Thus, with passion and practice, one can become an excellent Kabaddi player.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63658,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Measures to Endangered species','','','                                               <b>Measures to Endangered species</b>\nThe endangered species of the Great Indian Bustard can be saved by banning the act of hunting. Hunters should be punished if they are caught hunting these birds. Strict laws should be made to prevent the crime of killing just for fun. Finding a new way to set the electric lines may also contribute towards the safety of these birds. Spreading awareness about the situation of the Great Indian Bustard would also help in making people understand the danger that this species faces. Appeals and letters can be addressed to the leaders regarding their conservation. Sanctuaries, made to preserve these birds, should be visited offen. Also, initiatives like the \'Project Tiger\' would go a long way to save these beautiful birds from disappearing from the face of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63659,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: Autumn','','','                                                                        <b>Autumn</b>\nThe season of autumn is a beautiful sight. The bright colours of gold and red leaves are signs of the season that is observed in the months of September and October. It is one of the six seasons that we get to enjoy in India. The leaves fall down from the branches of the trees and go to a deep sleep because of the approaching winter season. The autumn breeze is known for its sweet music. The sun shines brightly in the day time and at nights, we see the moon in the clear sky. The atmosphere remains pleasant until it\'s time for winters.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63660,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: An Ideal Student','','','                                                             <b>An Ideal Student</b>\nDoes an ideal student actually exist? Or is it a poet\'s thought or vivid imagination? Each of us is ideal in our own way, isn\'t it? Nevertheless, some qualities set an ideal student apart from the rest. An ideal student knows and fulfils his/her responsibilities well. He/she is disciplined in daily routine. He/she observes hygiene and follows healthy eating habits. He she is respectful and compassionate to others. He/she is well-informed and keeps abreast of current affairs. He/she lives by good values. He/she is not arrogant of success and learns from mistakes/failures. He/she follows the rules of the school and obeys the instructions of teachers An ideal student of today is the leader of tomorrow.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63661,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: A Stall at a Fair','','','                                                                     <b>A Stall at a Fair</b>\nGoing to a fair is one of the simple pleasures of life. And luckily, I got a chance to enjoy it this summer when I visited my grandparents. The fair was held in a huge tent which brused several stalls that sold hundreds of things. The magicians and clowns were fun too. I particularly liked a stall which sold pencils that had a seed at one end. After the pencil is used up, you can plant the seed to grow into a sapling. What a lovely way to promote environmental awareness!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63662,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: If I Were a Millionaire...','','','                                                          <b>If I Were a Millionaire...</b>\nIt is a misconception that money can buy happiness; it can\'t. But without money, one can\'t live a decent life. If I were a millionaire, I would enjoy the pleasures of travel and luxury, since the world is definitely worth a round trip! On a serious note, I would also extend my hand wards important causes like environmental awareness, animal welfare, education and gender equality. Supporting such causes will definitely improve the quality of life of one and all in the world we live in.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63663,2068,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the paragraph on the following topic: If Doctors Went on an Indefinite Strike...','','','                                       <b>If Doctors Went on an Indefinite Strike...</b>\nDoctors relieve us of disease and pain and help us regain good health. So it is very difficult to imagine what would happen if doctors went on an indefinite strike. People would be totally helpless: They would not get timely and proper treatment. Many of the serious patients would die. People would self-medicate and may consequently worsen their state of health. The lives of infants, nursing mothers and pregnant women would also be at great risk. We must, therefore, simply hope and pray that such a situation may never arise.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63664,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: A Journey by Train','','','                                                  <b>A Journey by Train</b>\nMy father\'s friend had come to Mumbai on some business trip and was going back to Pune, where he lived. Since he was an old family friend, he came to stay with us for a day before his departure. His son was my friend and exactly of my age, so while going back, he invited me to spend a week at his house in Pune and since it was my summer vacation, my parents readily agreed. I was extremely excited. My parents were not worried at all since they knew I would be in safe hands.\nWe were to travel by train. The train left Chhatrapati Shivaji Terminus (C.S.T) early in the morning. This was my first journey by a long distance train without my parents. I took a window seat and eagerly looked around at the trees, birds, houses, fields, distant roads and hills that rushed just us. Uncle sat next to me and showed me some important buildings and interesting things. I was busy counting the electric poles when it became pitch dark and all the train lights were switched on. Before I could react we were in broad daylight again! Uncle explained to me that we had just gone through a tunnel and emerged at the other side of the mountain. As he was talking to me, there was another small tunnel. This time I observed how we made our way through the mountain and saved the distance of going around it. This was a unique experience for me, Boon, they served breakfast in the trans. I was hungry after all the excitement and quickly gobbled up my share!\nfly now the countryside scene was slowly changing into a concrete jungle and I realised we were close to our destination. We were approaching the Pune Railway Station and my train journey was over Uncle had called for his car to receive us at the station, Boon, we were home and met aunty and Daksh who were both happy and excited to see me.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63665,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: My First Day at a New School','','','                                               <b>My First Day at a New School</b>\nAt the end of my fifth standard, our family shifted to a new house in a different area. Therefore, I became necessary for me to change my school, as the distance was becoming too much, and the school bus services were not available in the new area. My fifth standard results were good, so I had no difficulty in getting admission to the new school\nAs it was my very first day at a new school, I had no books. Therefore, I carried only a notebook and penal with me I felt completely lost in the midst of new faces and unfamiliar surroundings. I did not know where to go, where my class was and where to stand in the assembly hall. I missed my old school and friends terribly: Frankly, I was in tears, but put up a brave front as I was too old to cry! But the teachers were nice and guided me, and tried to make me feel as comfortable as possible.\n\nOnce in my new class, I found an insignificant comer and sat down. Soon, the class teacher came in and after we wished her, she began to take attendance. My name was the last one to be called and immediately all heads turned towards me. It made me terribly self conscious. She asked me something, but I was too nervous and fumbled with the answer making the class erupt in laughter But my class teacher quickly checked them and encouraged me to go on.\nThe next class was Mathematics, my favourite subject. A different teacher came in and engaged us with some Mental Maths. I answered a couple of questions and she was quite pleased with me which helped in improving my image in front of the class. The third period was free and we were allowed to keep ourselves busy and even talk in whispers. I began to make a few friends and even went down to the canteen with some of them during break later\nWe had some language classes after the break where the teachers made some friendly conversation and gave us instructions and details about the future classes. Before I knew, the day was over and I trooped out with others after exchanging some pleasantries with my new friends. I thought to myself that the first day at the new school was not that bad after all.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63666,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: Diwali Shopping','','','                                                          <b> Diwali Shopping</b>\nDiwali, the Festival of Lights is celebrated all over our country. It is celebrated during the month of either October or November. People usually begin to make preparations for the festival, a couple of months in advance.\nIn India, it is a custom to wear new clothes during Diwali. My mother gets a few dresses stitched for me every year and I also get some as gifts from my grandparents and other relatives. Every year as a ritual, we go out for Diwali shopping, when we buy clothes for ourselves and, other relatives along with some gifts for friends and a few new household items.\nDiwali shopping is always a fun, a family affair in our house. My parents, brother and I, all go out together, shop, eat out and have a lot of fun. This time the shopping happened at the last moment. With only one week to go for Diwali, the shops were quite crowded. We still managed to buy three suits for myself, a couple of sets of clothing for my brother, a sari for my mother and some traditional clothes that my father wanted to pick up for himself. We also bought clothes for my grandparents and the rest of the youngsters in our family. The footwear shops took more time than the clothes. We had to visit half a dozen shops before all of us could get something to our liking!\nWe also bought some beautiful home décor items for gifting, candles and diyas and some small decorative lamps. No Diwali is complete without rangoli and Diwali lanterns. So, we bought some colours for the rangoli and a couple of large lanterns as well. Although crackers are an integral part of Diwali, we do not prefer it in our house. So, we just bought a small box of sparklers to keep up the ritualistic practice.\nFinally, we were done with almost all we needed. It was a long, strenuous day, but all of us were happy at the end of it and we had enjoyed ourselves thoroughly. We headed home after ordering some dry fruits for my father\'s colleagues and close friends. As I went to bed after dinner. I was excited at the thought of all the other activities that would follow in our house for the next few days. This was definitely my favourite time of the year.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63667,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: My Mother','','','                                                                       <b>My Mother</b>\nMy mother is the most important person in our family and definitely in my life. She is friendly and approachable, and always concerned with anybody who needs her help. She is always buty doing things for everyone in the family. Needless to say, she is the first one to wake up in the morning and the last person to go to bed every night. All of us depend on her for everything and therefore obviously, she is in charge of the whole house.\nBut my mother is not just a homemaker. She works outside the house too. She holds a senior position in a multinational organization, and plays an equally important role there as well. In spite of the fact that she works both outside the house as well as at home, she never grumbles. She makes us tasty treats whenever she finds the time over the weekends and spends all her free time with us. She reads books to my little sister every night and tells me interesting stories. She is patient, hardworking and a wonderful person full of love and laughter. I wish I can become a person like her when I grow up.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63668,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: My House','','','                                                                          <b>My House</b>\nOur house is far away from the humdrum of the city, amidst natural surroundings. While it takes a while to reach home from anywhere we go, we are fairly well connected with roadways and the transportation network in town. However being away, we are not disturbed by the traffic and other hustle bustle of a busy town in spite of being well connected to everyone and everything.\nSince ours is a bungalow, we have a nice large garden area connected to it. My mother is very fond of gardening and she has grown a lovely flower garden in the compound. We also have a number of fruit trees like banana, papaya, guava and mango which give us ample fruits in their respective seasons. In fact, we have so much of it in excess that we send a portion of them regularly to our relatives. We have a gardener who helps my mother with the gardening and stays in the small outhouse attached to our garden area with his family.\nThe house itself is quite spacious. We have a large hall, a dining area attached to an open kitchen and two bedrooms on the lower floor, and three bedrooms with an open terrace garden on the upper floor. My family comprises of my parents, grandparents and myself. We occupy the three bedrooms upstairs while the two on the ground floor are kept decorated as guest rooms for our relatives and friends who sometimes visit us. Alongside the kitchen, we also have a large storeroom area. My parents have taken a lot of efforts to furnish and decorate the house tastefully, with all the amenities required for modern and comfortable living. We also have a two year old Labrador for company.\nI really love our house. We have enough space to play, walk around and hold family functions and we are really lucky to be amidst such natural surroundings which is a rarity these days.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63669,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: The Camel','','','                                                                    <b>The Camel</b>\nThere is an amazing variety of animals in this world. When we visit a zoo or a National Park, we are able to see quite a few of them. Some of these animals are grand in stature, some beautiful, some more active than the others and some others which are dull and lazy. Some are even shy and timid. Of all these animals, camel is one such which is neither very active nor very interesting to look at.\nThe camel has a queer appearance. His eyes are always sleepy and his lips are hanging! He has a short body, a big hump on his back and long legs with big, padded feet. Its tail is short and unattractive. It looks dull and miserable! However, a camel is such an animal which can live anywhere and eat anything without a complaint. Also, it is so patient that it can put up with any kind of treatment.\nThis animal belongs to the desert region. It is the only animal that can walk on sand without its feet sinking. Camels carry loads for people travelling through the desert region, as well as people on their backs. Most importantly, these animals can go for long periods of time without water. In the desert region, one cannot do without a camel. Hence the camel, though not smart or attractive is an essential and indispensable part of the desert life. The camel is, thus, popularly called the \'Ship of the Desert\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63670,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: The City I would Like to Live in','','','                                                     <b>The City I would Like to Live in</b>\nIt is said, that there is no place like home. Among the varying places in India where I would like to spend at least a couple of years, I think Mumbai is the place I would like to live in the most.\nThis is not surprising, considering a lot of my friends and family members are already settled in the city. I have heard plenty of tales about this city that never sleeps and the various opportunities it offers to anybody who visits. Coming from a small town, I would definitely like to experience life in this city.\nAnother significant reason (at least for me) is safety. Strangely enough, for a city that is on the move 24 x 7, it is said to be safe for women here to travel at all odd hours, and by any mode of public transportation, people tell stories about how women and children can freely roam about in the streets at night without any threat to their safety. I cannot say the same about my hometown.\nI am sure the city has its shortcomings as well, and knowing the local language can always be an added advantage. But a cosmopolitan city like Mumbai cannot possibly shun people who lack the knowledge or a command over Marathi. Safety and comfort of living, as well as a combination of multi-cultural tradition are to be experienced.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63671,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: If I were a Doctor..............','','','                                                               <b>If I were a Doctor..............</b>\nWhenever anyone falls ill, he or she is taken to the doctor. As a child, I was taken to the doctor too, not only when I was sick, but also for regular check-ups. I would always be impressed by the way my doctor would make me feel comfortable before he checked me, and how quickly he cured me when I was unwell. I still remember when I was about nine and had broken my hand, my doctor told me stories while he plastered my hand and I did not even realise when it was all done! Therefore, from a very young age I had made up my mind to become a doctor.\nOver the years, I have seen many doctors. Some are specialists and charge very high consultation fees. Unfortunately, the not so well-off cannot afford to pay such high fees and often have to go without specialised treatment. Therefore, I have decided that if I become a doctor, I will spend most of my time treating the poor and needy. My main aim would be to help the not so privileged who need medical care, but cannot afford it.\nI would love to start a dispensary in a village or small town which I would attend to at least thrice a week and I would take personal care to keep everyone healthy. Along with that, I would like to teach them the importance of keeping their surroundings clean and update them on the facts about personal hygiene.\nI would also like to keep myself updated with what is happening in the medical field on a regular basis, so I could make use of the latest procedures and equipments to improve my services towards mankind in general.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63672,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: The Accident','','','                                                                <b> The Accident</b>\nNobody knows what will happen a moment later... The truth of this statement is reflected in the tale I am about to narrate. This incident took place a couple of years ago but I still find it difficult to get it out of my mind. It all began with a harmless plan for a short holiday with family and friends.\nOur parents readily agreed to go to Lonavala. It was decided that my friend Sanjay\'s family chauffeur, Mohan Chacha would drive us there and back. With all the plans in place, a day was decided and soon we set off early in the morning, so that Mohan Chacha could drive slowly and still reach at a decent hour. Our parents kept calling almost every half hour to make sure we were safe.\nSoon we reached and spent two lovely days at a holiday resort. We left after breakfast and should have been home in a few hours but that was where things began to go wrong.\nWhile on the highway, a truck came charging at us out of nowhere, at breakneck speed. Mohan Chacha tried to veer the car, but we had the divider beside us. Everything happened so fast, that there was no time to breathe or even blink. All I remember was hearing a loud crash and nothing else. I woke up to the antiseptic smell of the hospital. When my vision cleared, I realised that my family was there too. I tried to look around for my friends but my entire body was injured and 1 could not move.\nI was told that though my friends were hurt, they were alright. However, Mohan Chacha was critically wounded and so was the driver of the truck that hit us. Apparently, his brakes had failed causing him to lose control. We were discharged from the hospital in a couple of days and left nursing minor injuries. Unfortunately neither Mohan Chacha nor the driver of the truck made it....\nWhat started as a fun filled holiday for us ended in a critical disaster that none of us would forget. There were lives lost and people wounded, and there was really nobody to blame What if we had not made this plan? What if we had not gone to Lonavala? But there was no point in thinking about that anymore... none of these questions had any answers. Our lives had changed in that or moment, and forever. I have since then, never been very comfortable with the idea of a long drive anymore.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63673,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: A Stitch in Time Saves Nine','','','                                                  <b>A Stitch in Time Saves Nine</b>\nBefore facing failure or heavy loss, if we are able to act and rectify our actions, the loss might seem less or can even be totally avoided. For instance, if our garment is torn in one place, and we stitch it immediately, we can avoid it from getting torn any further. Otherwise, we may have to stitch it in several places, or even throw it away if it becomes completely torn and unusable. Therefore, the lesson here is that, whatever remedial action we take for any matter must be done quickly, and at an appropriate time, before it is too late.\nIf students study regularly, they would neither need to burn the midnight oil before their exams, nor repent for their results. Therefore, in order to avoid any major catastrophe, we must set things right immediately when it comes to our notice rather than postpone the matter to the eleventh hour. Neglect of little things leads to larger issues, finally leading to a large disaster. Therefore, we should do things promptly. So, what can be done tomorrow, do it today and what can be done today, do it now. A timely action can prevent all your future regrets.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63674,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: Every Cloud has a Silver Lining','','','                                                  <b>Every Cloud has a Silver Lining</b>\nPeople tend to feel depressed or unhappy when they go through difficult times. However. when we look at things logically, we find that every negative situation has its own positive side. A butterfly may struggle hard to come out of its cocoon, but its struggle only makes its wings stronger and helps it fly easily later. In the same way, every cloud has a silver lining. Every difficulty has its own positive outcome. This little story will illustrate this proverbial saying.\nSunita was a five-year old child living in an orphanage. One day, the tsunami wiped out the only home she knew, the orphanage. Once again, she found herself completely lost and alone in the world. At a tender age, she did not know what to do and where to go. However, very soon some social workers found and took her with them. Through these social workers, she met a couple who had just lost their only child in the same tsunami. When this couple saw Sunita, they instantly welcomed her into their home and lives. The child finally found a loving home and parents, none of which she had experienced before. Therefore, the tsunami, which was a dreadful, dark cloud in her life, came as a silver lining which found her the most precious thing in her life...loving parents One should always go through life believing that when bad things happen, the good things are not too far behind. The final outcome is often more positive than you can imagine.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63675,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: Perseverance is the Key to Success','','','                                                    <b>Perseverance is the Key to Success</b>\nIn the life of every living creature, it is seen that perseverance leads to prosperity. Tiny little ants go in search of food from morning till night and store it for future use so that they have enough to eat when the times are rough. In the same way, hard work can help us get prosperity. A hardworking farmer often reaps a rich harvest and makes a lot of money. Similarly, a trader who works hard becomes a rich and successful businessman. An industrialist, becomes a millionaire. In other words, people from every walk of life can become prosperous by resorting to hard work.\nProsperity does not always mean a lot of wealth. It is wealth that makes you happy. What is true in this case about individuals is also true about nations. Industrious nations that work hard and produce a lot of goods can sell them in the world market and earn plenty of foreign exchange. In this way, they too become prosperous.\nHard work and the will to work hard, will always remain the main source of prosperity. Perseverance will therefore, always be the key to success.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63676,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: My Favorite Season','','','                                                          <b> My Favorite Season</b>\nI love the rainy season, which starts in June and lasts until September in India. The rain falling effortlessly soothes my soul. I find it to be one of the most delightful sounds to my cars. Eiven the trees, binds and animals seem to rejoice when it rains.\nWearing colorful rainboots and raincoats is one of the many fun things about this season. I enjoy playing in puddles and splashing water. However, heavy rainfall can lead to floods and water-logging. We should take care of our surroundings and be prepared for the rainy season to avoid such problems.\nBy taking care of our environment, we can make sure the rainy season remains a happy time for all. Like Henry Wadsworth beautifully stated, \"For after all, the best thing one can do when it is raining is let it rain So, let\'s embrace the beauty of the rainy season and make the most of this wonderful gift from nature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63677,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: A Visit to an Aquarium','','','                                                     <b>A Visit to an Aquarium</b>\nDuring my visit to the aquarium, I was amazed by the captivating underwater world. The sight of vibrant fishes gliding gracefully through crystal-clear waters filled me with excitement.\nEach aquarium tank held a diverse array of marine life, from dazzling tropical fish to majestic sea turtles and graceful stingrays. The colorful corals and swaying seaweed added to the charming ambience. As I walked through the dim tunnels, I felt like I was underwater, surrounded by fascinating marine creatures all around me. The gentle hum of water and the occasional giggles of delighted children filled the air.\nMy visit to the aquarium made me appreciate the beauty and diversity of ocean life. It deepened my connection with the fascinating underwater world.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63678,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: My School Picnic','','','                                                              <b>My School Picnic</b>\nMy school picnic was a fantastic and joyous day filled with laughter and fun. We started early in the morning, excited for the trip. When we reached the picnic spot, we saw a beautiful lake surrounded by lush greenery.\n\nTeachers had planned games and activities for us, like relay races and tug-of-war, which made us feel united and happy. We enjoyed a delicious picnic lunch together, sharing our favorite snacks. The best part was the boat ride on the calm lake. We explored nature trails and admired the plants and animals.\nAs the day ended, we gathered around a bonfire, singing and sharing stories. It was a day of friendship and memories that we will treasure forever. The school picnic brought us all closer, and it was one of the happiest days of my school life.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63679,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: If I Could Fly','','','                                                                  <b>If I Could Fly</b>\nRichard Bach once said, \"To fly is to be free.\" If I could fly, it would be a thrilling adventure of a lifetime. Imagining myself with wings, I\'d take flight like a bird, soaring high above the world. The feeling of the gentle breeze on my face would fill me with joy and freedom.\nFrom the skies, I\'d witness the natural wonders below, admiring mountains, rivers and forests from a different viewpoint. The most exciting part would be flying with the birds, joining them on their journeys and exploring their world up close. Just like Melanie Griffith said, \"The birds are free. And so am I.”\nI\'d grab the opportunity to visit far-off places and hidden gems, making every moment of flight unforgettable. Though it remains a cherished dream, the thought of flying fills me with a sense of boundless imagination and appreciation for the beauty of our world.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63680,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: Independence Day Celebrations','','','                                              <b>Independence Day Celebrations</b>\nEvery year, India celebrates its Independence Day on 15 August. It is India\'s favourite national festival. Schools, housing societies, sports clubs and other organizations across the country celebrate it in their own unique way.\nThis year\'s Independence Day the 15th of August 2022-marked the 75th anniversary of our independence. And it was rendered momentous by the campaign \'Har Ghar Tiranga\' (which means \'a tricolour in every home\'). Almost every home hoisted the national flag. In general, the celebrations across the country included the flag-hoisting ceremony, march-past, several cultural programmes, art exhibitions and competitions.\nLike every year, our school also celebrated Independence Day with great gusto and enthusiasm. The school organized some entertainment programs and sports events. Some parents donated food grains, clothes, etc, to needy people.\nAt home, we bought the tricolour from the local post office. I was so excited to hoist the majestic flag of our great country, especially in my own home. Jai Hind!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63681,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: A Visit to an Animal Sanctuary','','','                                                    <b>A Visit to an Animal Sanctuary</b>\nLast year I visited one of the best wildlife sanctuaries in the world, the Kaziranga. This beautiful national park is one of India\'s finest wildlife sanctuaries most known for the famous five- rhinoceros, tigers, elephants, asiatic wild buffaloes and the eastern swamp deer.\nA wildlife sanctuary is a place reserved for wildlife and plays an important role in conservation of wildlife, especially the endangered species. We went on a tour in the sanctuary. I was thrilled to see so many species of birds and animals, most of which I had until then seen only in pictures or on television. We were squealing in excitement, and the tour guide kept getting annoyed at us. He was very concerned about the animals getting disturbed since they all have different nap times during the day. The sanctuary was so green and rich in the diversity of flora and fauna! We spotted some one-horned rhinoceros and migratory birds.\nWe all were really sad when our trip came to a wrap and we had to leave. But this one will always live in my memory as a beautiful dream!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63682,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: Picnic Fun','','','                                                                     <b>Picnic Fun</b>\nThis year, we went to the Hanging Gardens in Mumbai for our picnic. We all assembled at the school at around 6 am and left after a short assembly. All along, we had great fun singing songs and telling jokes. We arrived at the Hanging Gardens around 8.30 am.\nSomeone may think that these gardens are really \'hanging\' from somewhere! Well, jokes apart, the Hanging Gardens are terraced gardens perched at the top of the Malabar Hill. The Kamala Nehru Park, famous for its \'Boot House\' or \'Juta Ghar\', stands right opposite the garden.\nThe soft sea breeze was a delight in the warm sunny weather. Snacks and juice made the rounds right away. Our teachers took us around for a nice tour of the nursery to see flowering plants and herbs. We played a lot all day long.\nSoon, it was time to leave. All of us dragged our feet into the bus and dozed off. What a beautiful picnic it was!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63683,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: THE SOLDIER','','','                                                                      <b>THE SOLDIER</b>\nWhenever I see any pictures or films of soldiers, I feel thrilled. I admire soldiers very much, and I have decided that I shall become one some day. My father has told me a lot of things about the life of a soldier.\nIt is the duty of a soldier to keep fit at all times and be in readiness to defend his country.During a war, the soldier has to be at the border fighting the enemy. He is trained to use weapons such as guns, rifles, grenades and tanks. The soldier has to be clever, brave and loyal He has to be fearless, and be prepared to give up his life for his country.\nThe soldier is also expected to help during disasters like earthquakes, tsunamis, etc. His special training helps him to deal with such situations. I am sure I shall be a good soldier when I grow up.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63684,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: A FOOTBALL MATCH','','','                                                                      <b>A FOOTBALL MATCH</b>\nFootball is my favourite game. I play in the Junior team. I also watch as many matches as I can on TV as well as at the local stadium.\nLast Sunday I went with my father to see a match. The stadium was full. The players looked strong and well-built. The Air India team was facing the Mohun Bagan team.\nAir-India scored a goal in the very first few minutes. The crowd got very excited. But then the match lost the tempo and it looked as if neither of the teams was going to score. Whenever a free kick was awarded the excitement rose. But then a missed kick made everyone grean with disappointment. Minutes before half time Mohun Bagan scored a goal\nIn the second half Mohun Bagan were at their best. Their teamwork was excellent. The players made some brilliant passes. Air India played a good game too, but they were mostly on the defensive. Almost at the end of the match Mohun Bagan scored another goal Aur-India tried hard but could not score any goal. Mohun Bagan won the match\nI like football very much and this match thrilled me a great deal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63685,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: MY PET ANIMAL','','','                                                                            <b>MY PET ANIMAL</b>\nMy pet animal is a cute little puppy named Spots. He is a Dalmatian. He is very active although he is hardly two months old. He looks quite big for his age. He is light brown in colour and has black spots all over his body. So we have called him Spots.\nHe loves to play with an old piece of cloth and a ring. Whenever a visitor comes over, he runs to the bedroom and brings them to the visitor and lays them at the visitor\'s feet. He then pants excitedly and looks up at the visitor, expecting him or her to throw the ring or cloth for him to fetch it.\nWe enjoy playing with Spots. He is a very lovely dog. We take good care of him. We took him to a veterinary doctor for a check-up last week.\nAll of us at home love Spots very much.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63686,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: IF I WERE RICH','','','If I were rich, the first thing I would do would be to buy a bigger house. Mummy would have a washing machine. Both Mini and I would have our own study rooms. We would have a separate place in the house for our friends to come and play. I would have my own cricket kit. Mini would have a cabinet to keep her toys.\nWe would have a beautiful garden. There would also be a lovely cricket ground. Mini and I would have our own bicycles. Daddy would have a car, so that he could go to work comfortably.\nIt is not that I am unhappy with the life I live now. Daddy tries his best to give us a comfortable life. But at times I feel that things would be slightly better with a little more money. But as soon as such a thought comes to my mind I remember my Grandpa\'s advice: Be content with what you have.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63687,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: CRUELTY TO ANIMALS','','','                                                     <b>CRUELTY TO ANIMALS</b>\nI love animals and I feel quite upset whenever I see any animal being treated cruelly. I come across many people abusing animals. Once I saw a boy throwing stones at a street dog. I found a man whipping his bullocks to make them go faster. I even saw a boy hitting a squirrel with stones from a catapult.\nMy next-door neighbour has a small pet dog. When my neighbour is tired, he often beats the dog. He says it is to discipline it. My heart aches when I hear its pitiful cries.\nWhy do some people treat animals cruelly? Is it because they are dumb and cannot complain? Or because we are stronger than they are? Why do people not realize that animals feel pain just like we do? That they too have emotions and feelings like we do? Anyone who treats animals cruelly should be punished. There should be strict laws for this. We ought to treat them with love and care.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63688,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: GANDHIJI\'S FAVOURITE FABRIC-KHADI','','','                                      <b>GANDHIJI\'S FAVOURITE FABRIC-KHADI</b>\nKhadi is a fabric that is closely associated with Mahatma Gandhi.\nBefore Independence, the British would buy cotton from India at cheap prices and export it to Britain, where it was woven into cloth. This cloth was then brought back to India and sold to Indians at a high price. Gandhiji wanted to stop this and make Indians self-reliant. The khadi movement aimed at boycotting foreign-made cloth and promoting Indian made goods. Therefore he wanted all Indians to spin and use khadi.\nAnother reason was to provide employment to poor farmers. Farmers may not have enough work to earn their living throughout the year. About four months during the dry season they may be idle. Spinning and then making khadi cloth would provide them enough clothes for their own use. It would also teach Indians dignity of labour.\nKhadi is a fabric that is well-suited to the climate of our country, and today, with new Innovations, it has become a very dignified and elegant fabric.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63689,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: A VISIT TO A STALL AT A SCIENCE FAIR','','','                                         <b>A VISIT TO A STALL AT A SCIENCE FAIR</b>\nOur school had organized a Science Fair. It was a very good fair, and I visited the various stalls. I was most interested in the one that dealt with the adulteration of food.\nThe stall had an attractive poster at the entrance. It said, \'Do you know what you are eating? Then it told us about the presence of adulterants in the food we eat, how widespread adulteration is, and how it affects our systems. There were a variety of other charts put up around the stall. They told us of the adulterants that were present in the common food items that we used every day like milk, oil, spices, etc. and how to detect them.\nThe four pairs of students in the stall had different foodstuff in front of them. The first pair had chilli powder adulterated chilli powder in one bowl and pure chilli powder in another bowl.\nThey showed the audience a simple way to test for artificial colouring. You merely added the chilli powder to water. If it was pure, the water did not change colour. If it was adulterated, the artificial colorants descended as colour streaks.\nThe other three pairs dealt with the testing of other food items, which could easily be done at home. If the testing was more complicated, the details were given in the charts. Everyone was impressed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63690,2069,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: MY FAVOURITE TIME OF THE DAY','','','                                                <b>MY FAVOURITE TIME OF THE DAY</b>\nMy favourite time is early morning. I get up very early and go for a round on my cycle There is no traffic on the road at this time. There are many trees near my house, and I can see birds and flowers. I can even hear the calls of the various birds. The air is cool and pure and there is always a slight breeze. There are dewdrops on the blades of grass in the garden near my house.\nEven on holidays and during vacations, I get up early in the morning for my ride. If It is raining. I sit on the swing on my balcony and look at the trees outside. On cold winter mornings I bundle myself in a warm sweater before I set off. Sometimes I am awake even before Mummy gets up! Mummy jokes that I must have been a milkman in my previous birth!\nTruly, getting up early in the morning makes me feel fresh and gives me plenty of time to do various things. Sometimes I even help Mummy in the kitchen. I would ask all of you to get up early and see the beauty of the dawn you will never wish to sleep late again!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63691,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','Fox walking in jungle...hot day...fox thirsty...long tiring walk...pond with little water...tries to reach...slips and falls... drinks water...bank too high...can\'t climb up... goat passes...fox tricks goat into believing water is sweet... foolish goat jumps in... fox uses goat as step to get out of pond leaving the goat in the lit up jungle.','Frame complete story from the given outline:','','','                                                                   <b>The Clever Fox</b>\nA fox was walking leisurely in a jungle. It was a hot day. It felt very thirsty and was desperately searching for water. After some time, it came to a pond with little water at the bottom. The fox was happy. Standing at the edge of the pond, he tried to reach the water. But the water level was way down and he slipped and fell into the pond! The fox drank till his thirst was quenched. But once that was done, he realized that the bank was too high for him to climb up. So, he began to get worried.\nAfter quite some time, the fox saw a goat coming his way. Immediately he got a clever idea. He called out to the goat saying, \"My dear friend, are you thirsty? You must come and taste this sweet water then.\" The innocent goat did not suspect a trick and responded saying that he was indeed tired and thirsty. Thanking the fox for the invitation, he jumped into the pond to drink water. The fox waited till the goat had drunk all the water he wanted. He then asked the goat to stand near the wall of the embankment and the moment the goat did that, the fox used him as a step ladder to climb out of the pond. Before the goat could understand what was happening, the fox ran away from there, leaving the poor goat to his plight.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63692,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','A cap seller selling caps - passes through a jungle - feels tired - places his bag of caps near him and falls asleep under tree - monkeys on the tree - cap seller wakes up - bag of caps missing - looks everywhere - looks up - he sees monkeys wearing his caps - he takes off his cap-throws it down - monkeys do the same - cap seller quickly collects his caps - goes away.','Frame complete story from the given outline:','','','                                            <b>The Capseller and the Monkeys</b>\nOnce there was a cap seller. He went from place to place selling his caps. One day, the cap seller was passing through a jungle. It was a hot day and the cap seller was tired. He spread a cloth under a tree and decided to take a quick nap. He placed his bag of caps near him. He placed his own cap over his face to keep out the strong rays of the sun. Soon the man fell fast asleep.\nAfter an hour or so, the cap seller woke up with a start. He stood up, composed himself, and was about to pick up his bag of caps and move on. But to his surprise and alarm the bag was missing! The cap seller began to look for his bag of caps. He looked everywhere, but could not spot a single cap. he found the bag lying He sound, he looked up. He saw several monkeys sitting on the branches of a tree. Each of them had a cap on its head. Evidently, they had seen the cap seller wearing a cap and were trying to imitate him! The cap seller picked up a stone and threw it at the monkeys. At once, the monkeys plucked fruits from the tree and threw them at the cap seller. The cap seller was quite annoyed, but realized this method would not work. He suddenly had an idea. Monkeys were great imitators. With in mind, he took off the cap from his head and flung it on the ground. At once the monkeys also this took off their own caps and flung them to the ground. The cap seller quickly picked up his caps and went away happily.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63693,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','Shepherd boy told to cry \"wolf!\" if he saw any near his flock...watches sheep for several days... gets tired of the monotonous work...one day shouts \"wolf!\" as a joke...all villagers hasten to help...find no wolf...boy laughs at them...villagers angry...boy plays same joke on villagers after a few days again....some villagers ignore, some come running...find nothing again...get angry with the boy...one day wolf really comes...boy shouts for help...villagers ignore...wolf kills several sheep.','Frame complete story from the given outline:','','','                                                   <b>The Boy who cried \"Wolf!\"</b>\nOnce there was a young shepherd boy who was sent out to the fields to look after the sheep. He was warned that he should not let the sheep stray as there were wolves around that often attacked and killed the sheep in the area. He was also told that in case something like that happened, he should immediately shout for help and the villagers would come to his rescue.\nDays passed and the boy began to grow bored with the monotonous work. The sheep grazed on these own and there was no sign of any wolf anywhere. One day the boy decided to have some fun to entertain himself. He stood up and shouted at the top of his lungs, \"Wolf Wolf repeatedly till he noticed several villagers rushing to his rescue. But when the villagers reached the spot they found no wolf there. Instead there was this shepherd boy standing there and laughing at their plight! The villagers realized that they had been tricked and were angry. However, since the boy was young, they let him off with a warning.\nA few days passed and the villagers forgot about the incident. At this time the boy decided to play the same joke again. Once again, several villagers rushed to the spot only to realize that they bast been tricked again. The villagers were angry and upset with the young boy and warned him that the consequences would be dine if this was repeated.\nSoon after this incident, a wolf really attacked the flock. The boy in desperation shouted for help repeatedly, at the top of his lungs, but this time no one came. It was impossible for a young lad to fight off a wolf alone and all he could do was stand apart and watch his sheep being massacred. As a result, the wolf killed quite a few sheep and finally left dragging off one in its mouth. It was a huge loss for the boy and his family but they had nobody but the boy himself to blame. The boy began to realize the results of his earlier joke, but it was too late.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63694,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Develop a story of your own around the idea that you should never judge anyone by their appearances.','','','                                       <b>Never judge a Book by its Cover</b>\nOnce there lived a deer in a forest. The deer was extremely proud of his good looks and considered himself to be one of the most beautiful animals in the forest. He belittled the other animals and looked down upon the bear for being too ugly and the elephant for being too large and bulky and so on.\nOne day, this proud deer wandered ahead of its herd in search of food. For a long time, be did not realize that he had been separated from all the others. When he did he started searching for the route to return to his herd but the more he tried, the more lost he got. Soon, he was completely exhausted.\nSuddenly he heard a loud roar from behind the bushes! He looked up and saw a yellowish flash behind the leaves. A leopard had been watching him for a long time and now lunged at him. The 115 deer started running as fast as he could to save his life but the leopard was literally at his heels Just when the leopard was about to pounce on the deer, a huge elephant crossed their path. Shielding the deer, it raised its trunk and flicked the leopard hard to one side. The stunned leopard picked himself up and ran away fearing for his life. The elephant then turned to the deer and asked him to compose himself as he was now out of danger. Having said this, the elephant went about its way. The deer felt ashamed of himself and his earlier attitude. He learnt never to judge anyone by their appearance again.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63695,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a story beginning with: \'It was a stormy evening and I was all alone at home...!','','','                                                                      <b>Home Alone</b>\nIt was a stormy evening and I was all alone at home. This was not the usual practice, but today was an exception. My father had not returned from the office and my mother had to rush off to help a neighbour who had fallen ill suddenly and did not have anyone with her at home. I was not a kid who got scared with things easily, but I had to admit that I was not very fond of thunder and lightning. I told myself that there was nothing to worry about as my mother was in the same building on a different floor and would be back soon.\nThe shadows danced up and down on the window and walls making the atmosphere eerie. Isat in one corner of the sofa in our living room, almost as if, in case I moved, something would happen and someone would catch me! I thought it was a safe place to be as I could look around the house from where I was seated.\nSuddenly, there was a click on the front door... someone was turning the keys in the lock! I froze. I knew mother had not taken the house keys with her as she rushed out in a hurry. Oh my God! Someone was trying to enter the house and I was all alone... I was too scared to turn around. In fact I ducked a little lower on the sofa, wondering if my head was still showing above the back rest and closed my eyes tight; in my silly scared mind I must have thought, if I could not see the intruder, he would not be able to see me there. Suddenly I felt a large hand touch my shoulder. I screamed as loudly as I could. \"Minu, its me, papa!\" said a familiar voice. I turned around quickly and stared at him before hugging him hard. I did not realize that it was already past 7 p.m. and my father came home by this time. Finally, I breathed a sigh of relief!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63696,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','(Outline: girl dreams of bringing to life everything she touches - dream becomes true - accidentally brings a toy dragon to life - causes chaos in town - wise person guides the girl to use her power - peace is restored in town)','Write a story using the outline given below. Use your own ideas and give it a suitable title. ','','','                                                     <b>Dream Come True</b>\nIn the town of Lumina, lived a girl named Lily who loved the starry night. Each night, she dreamt of hands that glowed with sparkling dust, bringing everything she touched to life. One morning, she woke to find sparkling stardust on her bed, just as she had dreamt.\nLily\'s dreams started to come true. Her touch worked magic, making things come alive One fateful day, she accidentally brought a playful toy dragon to life. Chaos ensued, shattering the sky and making the stars disappear. News of Lily\'s magical power reached wise Aurelia, who knew she could help restore peace. With Aurelia\'s help, Lily learned to use her powers. Eventually, Lily soothed the dragon, bringing back peace to Lumina. She then repaired the sky, and the stars returned to their places.\nSince that day, Lumina shined with a magical glow. Lily became known as the protector of the skin and dreams. Her extraordinary gift brought joy and hope to everyone in the town.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63697,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a story around the idea that one must face their biggest fear and find the courage to overcome it in your own words. Give it a suitable title.','','','                                                                 <b>Facing Fears</b>\nIn the peaceful village of Jyotipura, lived Arjun, a boy filled with curiosity but scared of heights Climbing trees or being on tall structures made him anxious. One day, his family visited the lively fun-far the nearby town. Encouraged by them, Arjun decided to confront his fear and join the towering Ferris wheel ride.\nAs the Ferris wheel went up, Arjun\'s heart started racing. But upon reaching the top, he admired the beautiful view. Fear turned into wonder. Determined to overcome his fear, Arjun made a brave choice. \nArjun\'s newfound courage inspired the village of Jyotipura. He fearlessly climbed trees, explored high places and embraced exciting rides. His bravery motivated others to face their fears and embrace adventures Arjun conquered his fear of heights at the Jyotipura carnival. Now, he faces challenges with confidence and courage.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63698,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','(Outline: deer-beautiful antlers ugly legs-tiger attacks-antlers get stuck-feet help escape)','Write a story using the outline given below. Use your own ideas and give it a suitable title','','','                                                     <b>The Proud Deer and his Antlers</b>\nIn a lush forest, there lived a deer named Rusty. He loved his beautiful antlers but didn\'t like his plan feet. One day, while grazing near a sparkling pond, he wished his legs were as pretty as his antlers.\nSuddenly, a hungry tiger appeared, and Rusty ran as fast as he could, surprised by his swift legs. But his antlers got stuck between trees. As the tiger came closer, Rusty realized the value of his \"ugly\' feet. With a burst of strength, he escaped the attacking tiger. Though his antlers were stuck, his quick legs had saved him.\nFrom then on, Rusty appreciated his legs\' strength and beauty. He learned that true worth is not just about looks but embracing our unique abilities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63699,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a story with the moral - A friend in need is a friend indeed. Give it a suitable title.','','','                                                     <b>The Loyal Companion</b>\nIn a small village, there were two best friends, Maya and Raj. They were inseparable and always stood by each other. One day, a heavy rainstorm struck the village, causing floods that damaged Maya\'s house. She was devastated and didn\'t know how to rebuild.\nRaj didn\'t hesitate to lend a helping hand. He invited Maya and her family to stay at his home until their house was fixed. Together, they faced the challenges of rebuilding, supporting each other with kindness and care, As time passed, Maya\'s house was repaired and the floodwaters receded. Maya was grateful for Raj\'s unwavering support and realized that a friend in need is indeed a friend indeed. Their friendship became even stronger, and the entire village learned the value of true friendship and helping each other in times of need.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63700,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','(Outline: A lion king of the jungle - an elephant - an anthill - discussion of power - wise judge - an owl-judgement)','Write a bed time story using the outline given below. You can also use your own ideas to give it a suitable end.','','','Once upon a time, there was a lion who was the king of a jungle. He was walking around his territory when he saw an elephant breaking down a tree. He roared at him and asked him about uprooting the tree. The elephant was in no mood to reply. He was angry and wanted to show his strength, so he pulled down another tree.\nThe lion roared again and the whole forest shook under the sound of his roar. The elephant stood still for a while and the lion smiled to himself. He made it clear to the elephant that he was indeed the \"king of the jungle\".\nThe lion continued walking around the jungle when he saw little ants scurrying all over the place near an anthill. He was curious to see them work so furiously. He asked one of the ants, \"Do you need any help?\" The little ant thanked him politely and said, \"No, sir! Thank you! But we are powerful enough to take care of ourselves.\" The lion laughed mockingly. The little ant felt insulted and thus, called out his friends for support.\nAll of them formed a group and faced the lion. They told him that even though they were tiny creatures, their strength was unmatched when they were united. The lion roared in laughter. The ants felt offended. They decided to go to the wise old judge the owl, who had a solution for everything. The elephant joined them on their way to the wise owl; he also wanted to know the outcome of their discussion on power. So, the lion, the elephant and the ants walked down towards the tree where the old owl lived.\nThe owl saw them and smiled. He asked them the reason for their sudden arrival and the ants narrated the whole story to him. The wise owl thought for a while and then answered, \"Each one of us is powerful in our own way. The elephant is intelligent and possesses physical strength whereas the strength of the ants lies in their speed and unity. And last but not the least, the lion is one of the most agile of all the animals and a fierce hunter. All of us are great in our own way and we should value that.\" All the animals cheered at his wise words and returned back with a new lesson learnt well.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63701,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Form groups and discuss what must have happened to the handkerchief. Write the story in your own words.','','','                                                   <b>Fluffy\'s Journey to Freedom</b>\nOne March day, the handkerchief seeking freedom, escaped from the clothesline, dancing like a snowflake. It struggled hard until it broke free, leaving the clothesline behind. No one knew where it went maybe in a ditch or the sea.\nAs days passed, the handkerchief had a fun adventure. It met a rabbit named Flopsy, and they explored meadows and woods, making animals happy. At a shiny lake, they met Nutty, a playful squirrel The three friends laughed and danced, creating magic. They called the handkerchief Fluffy, and it sparkled like a sail on the water. When it was time to go back, Fluffy got stuck in a lily pad It tried hard and finally broke free, flying high.\nInstead of going back home. Fluffy chose to stay in the charming lake, spreading joy to all. The handkerchief found a new purpose, dancing with friends, forever in the magic of its escape.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63702,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Visit a library. Read the biographies of other Indian Saints. Share at least one story from their life with your friends. What message does it contain?','','','I read a story about Guru Nanak Dev ji. He was the founder of Sikhism. A Sikh means a disciple or student of the Guru. Guru Nanak dev ji\'s father wanted him to get married. He also attempted to set him up in a business as a merchant so that he could support his family. He gave him some money and sent him to make purchases. Guru Nanak Dev ji spent all the money feeding the homeless, hungry and the holy men whom he met on the way. When he returned empty handed, his father became furious and scolded him severely Guru Nanak Dev ji, however, insisted that doing good deeds for others had earned him an excellence in life which made him feel happy and satisfied. This story contains the message that true joy and satisfaction lies in helping the needy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63703,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','India is famous for its handloom fabrics. Write a story of a handloom kurta in your own words.','','','It was my mother\'s birthday and we planned to gift her something different than the usual. My grandmother offered to take us to a handloom factory. We were very excited. On reaching the factory, we saw people working intricately. A lady was weaving cloth and our grandmother informed us that the weave was known as a Paithani.\nThe materials of this weave, whose name is based on the town that the art comes from (Paithan), are usually available as finished products in a variety of rainbow colours, making them one of the best weaves. However, since the fabric is made from extremely fine silk and woven with zari, it is also one of the most luxurious and expensive fabrics. The process used to make this fabric is called silk weaving Though there are cheaper varieties available, it is usually a pretty expensive piece to buy, often woven with colours going length wise and width wise with a little variation causing a rainbow effect. It takes more time to weave this material than the others. We were delighted to buy a Paithani handloom kurta for my mother. It turned out to be more than what we had saved for, but our grandmother offered to contribute. My mother was very happy on seeing her gift. She loved it. Our efforts to make her birthday special paid off.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63704,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','(theft in Akbar\'s court-----------valuable ornaments stolen Emperor tries to find thief unsuccessful------------asks Birbal for help-----------Birbal asks for the chest from which valuables were stolen-----------puts his ear to the lid as if listening says trunk says thief has bright red spot on beard-----------one servant quickly puts hand to his beard------------thief caught)','Write a story using the following outline. Give the story a suitable title:','','','                                                                <b>BIRBAL\'S TRICK</b>\nOne day there was a theft in Akbar\'s court. Some very valuable ornaments were stolen.\nThe Emperor\'s guards tried their best to catch the thief, but they were unsuccessful. Finally, Akbar asked Birbal for help. Birbal asked for the trunk from which the valuables were stolen. In the presence of all the courtiers and servants, Birbal put his ear to the trunk as if he were listening to something. Puzzled, Akbar asked him what he was doing. Birbal said, \"The trunk just spoke to me. It said that the thief has a bright red spot on his beard.\"\nOne of the servants present quickly put his hand to his beard. Birbal pointed to him and said, \"There is your thief, Your Majesty.\"\nThe thief confessed to his crime and was punished. Smart Birbal was praised by everyone.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63705,2070,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Have you ever given up on something good? Share your story. ','','','                                                               <b>THE DAY I GAVE UP</b>\nI remember the day clearly. I was not even eight years old. I had been going for karate classes since two months. Unfortunately, the coach was extremely strict and grumpy. If we forgot some move or were absent even once, he made us do the frog jump. This was not only exhausting but also humiliating.\nI began to develop a dislike for the coach, who never praised any of us or showed any appreciation. He was always impatient and in a hurry to go to his next coaching session. I began to cry every Tuesday and Friday, just before the time when I had to leave for the class. In fact, I even began to fall sick. My mother realized that something was wrong, and she questioned me. When I explained to her, she said that I must persevere and not give up. However, I had developed such a dislike for karate and for the coach that I refused to continue. I turned stubborn, and my mother finally gave up. I stopped going for the classes.\nNow, when I see my friends doing karate moves, I regret that I gave up and did not continue with the coaching. Maybe I will get a chance sometime in the future.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63706,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Your school has always performed well in the inter-school athletic and sports meets, and this year too the students have excelled in most of the events they have taken part in. One of the matches won was cricket. As the sports editor of your school magazine, you decide to print an interview with Sachin Tendulkar in your school magazine, as an inspiration for further upcoming tournaments and for a few handy and beneficial tips that he might share.\nWrite down 10 questions that you would like to ask him:\n','','','i) When did you decide to take up cricket as a career?\nii) You started playing for India at an age when most people are still in school. How did you manage to balance education with cricket?\niii) Who has been your greatest inspiration?\niv) According to you, what are the qualities needed to excel in this sport?\nv) What has been the most memorable moment in your career?\nvi) How do you re-motivate yourself when you lose a match?\nvii) What are your pastimes or hobbies when you are off the field?\nviii) According to you, what are the challenges a sportsperson in India has to face?\nix) What is your idea of success?\nx) Your children have grown up. Would you encourage either of them to take up sports as a career?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63707,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine that you are given the opportunity to interview a veterinary doctor. Frame 10 questions that you would like to ask him.','','','i) When did you decide to become a veterinary doctor?\nii) Were animals and their treatment a natural choice?\niii) What made you decide to treat animals rather than human beings?\niv) Do you have any pets at home?\nv) Animals cannot express how they feel or what is wrong with them. Does this make treating them more difficult than human beings?\nvi) Which case or incident has been the most challenging?\nvii) Do the owners react in the same way as they do for their near and dear ones?\nviii) Please advise us as what steps we should take if we see any stray bird or animal badly injured.\nix) Tell us something about the book you have written on \'Animals - Our Friends\'.\nx) What would you like to advise the future generations who want to excel in the field of veterinary medicine?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63708,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine you are a journalist and have been assigned the task of interviewing a famous actor. Frame 10 questions that you would like to ask him.','','','i) Sir, it is a pleasure to be granted this interview. Please tell me what motivated you to choose acting as a career?\nii) How difficult or easy was it to achieve the popularity that you now experience?\niii) How supportive was your family towards your career choice?\niv) What influenced your decision to act? Was it a movie or was it a particular star?\nv) What are your hobbies?\nvi) Do you get time to pursue your hobbies?\nvii) How does the adulation of the public feel?\nviii) How does your family cope with it?\nix) With fame comes gossip, how do you handle it?\nx) Please give a message for all your fans out there.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63709,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine you are going to meet a Great Indian Bustard very soon. Prepare at least 8-10 questions to interview him.','','','i) You are known as the \'Great Indian Bustard\' and you feel proud about it. Can you attributive the reason for it?\nii) What are your favourite food items?\niii) You are known as \'Hoom\' in Marathi. What is the significance?\niv) How do you protect yourselves from predators?\nv) Do you feel dishonoured for not getting the status of a \'National Bird\'?\nvi) How far is your population spread?\nvii) We are sorry that your population is slowly disappearing. What is the reason?\nviii) How can we help you to survive?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63710,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine you are getting a chance to interview your favourite sports player. Frame 8-10 Interview questions that you would like to ask them.','','','Interview Questions to be asked to a Sports (Football) Player:\n*What\'s the key to your consistent success?\n*How do you handle the pressure of inspiring millions of fans?\n*Who is your role model and why?\n*Can you share your training and fitness secrets?\n*Do you have a favorite goal? What made it special?\n*What challenges did you face in your journey?\n*How does giving back to the community impact you?\n*What keeps you motivated to reach new heights?\n*How do you balance your professional and personal life?\n*Any advice for aspiring footballers?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63711,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine your school has won a first prize for being the most diselplined school in the city. You are appointed to interview your principal for your school magazine. Frame 10 questions that you would like to ask them.','','','Interview Questions to be asked to the Principal:\n*How does it feel to win the most disciplined school award?\n*What do you believe contributed to this success?\n*What strategies were used for maintaining discipline and a positive learning environment?\n*How did teachers and staff help in promoting discipline among students?\n*How do you balance extra-curricular activities with discipline?\n*How do you encourage creativity while enforcing discipline? Any plans to further improve discipline in the future?\n*What long-term benefits does discipline bring to students\' success?\n*What advice would you give to other schools to improve their culture\"\n*How will this award impact the school\'s reputation?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63712,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','You\'ve got a chance to interview your favourite Disney Character. Create 8-10 questions you would like to ask them. ','','','Interview Questions to be asked to the Disney Character Simba from \"The Lion King\':\n*What lessons did you learn on your journey to becoming king?\n*How did your father\'s teachings continue to influence you?\n*How did the mantra \'Hakuna Matata\' shape your leadership style as king?\n*How did Nala\'s affection change you?\n*What gave you the strength to confront Scar?\n*How did Rafiki\'s wisdom impact your understanding of the Circle of Life?\n*How did you motivate the animals to work together in rebuilding their home during Scar\'s reign?\n*How do you balance duty and fun in the Pride Lands?\nWhat valuable life lessons did you learn from Timon and Pumbaa?\n*What plans do you have for a prosperous and harmonious future of the Pride Lands?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63713,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine you were a part of a social service group and your group won the first prize for maintaining cleanliness in public places. Frame 10 questions to be asked to the team leader in an interview for a newspaper article.','','',' Interview Questions to be asked to the Team Leader:\nCongratulations on winning the first prize for maintaining cleanliness in public places!\n*How does it feel to win for maintaining cleanliness in public places?\n*What strategies did you use to ensure cleanliness throughout the year?\n*How did your team overcome major obstacles?\n*What were each team member\'s roles in this task?\n*How did you collaborate with the local community to achieve your goals?\n*What kept your team motivated despite difficulties?\n*Did your team face any criticism while implementing your plan?\n*How did you raise awareness about cleanliness?\n*What was the most rewarding part of this achievement for your group?\n*What are your future plans to improve public space cleanliness?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63714,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Indian cricket team has won the World Cup, and its captain has been invited to your school. Frame about eight to ten questions to interview him.','','','The captain of the Indian cricket team would be happy to answer the following questions during an interview.\n1) How do you feel on your victory?\n2) What or whom do you attribute this success to?\n3) Tell us the kind of practice that is required.\n4) What difficulties did you face?\n5) How did you solve those difficulties?\n6) Which was the most challenging match so far?\n7) Who were your strongest supporters?\n8) What role did your teammates play in your success story?\n9) Please tell us your future plans.\n10) Please give the young budding sports people a message from you and your team.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63715,2071,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','If Birbal were alive and you had to interview him, prepare a set of eight to ten questions that you would like to ask him.','','','The following questions could be put to Birbal in an interview.\n1) For how long have you been in the court of Emperor Akbar?\n2) What is his strongest quality?\n3) Who is your role-model?\n4) Which has been your most memorable moment?\n5) Who is your best supporter?\n6) How many difficult cases have you solved for your Emperor?\n7) What is the best compliment that you have received so far?\n8) What do you say or do if the Emperor disagrees with you?\n9) How pleased are you with the Emperor\'s rule?\n10) What is the toughest problem that you have faced till now?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63716,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a General Diary Entry of a Young School Girl.','','','Monday, 5th August\nA new girl has joined our class today. It must be so awkward for her to be in a new school in the middle of the term. I saw her sitting quietly in one corner of the classroom when I entered. She said her name is Shruti and her father has just been transferred to our city, resulting in her mid-term entry.\nI immediately remembered my first day at this school two years back and how lost I had felt. I sat beside her today and tried to make her a bit comfortable. I even introduced her to Meena and Shilpa. We all got along well with her. She seems nice...maybe we can be friends.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63717,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an Entry about two Friends who have had a Fight.','','','Wednesday, 2ndOctober\nSmita and I had a fight this evening. Now we are not talking with each other. I realized how silly the incident was when I came back home, and that too for over T.V. programme! But I don\'t like the fact that Smita is so dominating all the time and insists that she is always right. Why can\'t someone else like or dislike something different from her? I think it\'s stupid to behave this way. This time, I shall definitely not be the one to go and neutralize things. Let it be the other way round for a change.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63718,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an Entry about a Happy Personal Experience.','','','Saturday, 9th December\nI got three prizes today! I am so excited! Today, was our Annual Prize Day at school. I knew that I was going to be awarded the prize for General Proficiency when my results were declared last year, but the real surprise came when my name was called out to go on stage and receive the award and instead of one, there were announcements made for three prizes! I had also bagged the second and third prizes for Language proficiency. My parents felt they were on the moon.\nWe went out for dinner to celebrate the happy occasion. I am the happiest person in this world tonight.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63719,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an Entry about a Sad Personal Experience.','','','Tuesday, 3 May\nHost my grandmother to cancer today. It was inevitable, but somehow it is still difficult to accept the idea that I cannot run to her or talk to her anymore. I suppose it is never easy to accept the loss of a loved one and yet we have to come to terms with it and move on. All that is left are memories...many cherished one. They are mine to nurture whenever I want to be with her from now on... I will miss you, Nani...more than you know... but I think you know that... I really love you.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63720,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an Entry about a School Event.','','','Saturday, 5th October\nOur Annual Day Function was held this evening at Kala Mandir and it turned out to be more grand than we had anticipated. Each and every programme went off perfectly and the dinner that followed was sumptuous. I think the parents and teachers enjoyed themselves as much as we did performing! Our dance drama was highly appreciated by all. It was truly an enjoyable and fun filled day. I wish we could have Annual Day celebrations every month!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63744,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a diary entry about an unforgettable event in your life.','','','Date: August 1, 2022\nDear Diary,\nToday was unforgettable! My old friend Emily surprised me with a trip to the mountains. We laughed a s and remembered the good times. We hiked challenging trails, reaching a breath-taking summit at sunset. The view was awe-inspiring, and I felt connected to nature\'s beauty Surrounded by twinkling fireflies, we soaked in the peacefulness of the place. This trip showed me how important it is to enjoy the moment, good friends close and find comfort in nature. I\'m grateful for this day, and it\'ll stay with me forever Lite about these precious moments, and I promise to embrace them more.\nSuvarna\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63745,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a timetable for a daily routine for day in school.','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n     <th colspan=\"2\"> Daily Routine </th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Wake up<br>\nBreakfast<br>\nLeave for school<br>\nReturn from school<br>\nLunch<br>\nSleep<br>\nHave milk/juice<br>\nGo out to play<br>\nStudy/Homework<br>\nDinner<br>\nTelevision<br>\nBedtime</td>\n    <td>6:00 a.m. <br>\n6:30 a.m. <br>\n7:15 a.m. <br>\n2:30 p.m. <br>\n2:45 p.m. <br>\n3:00 p.m. <br>\n4:30 p.m. <br>\n5:00 p.m. <br>\n6:30 p.m. <br>\n8:30 p.m. <br>\n9:00 p.m. <br>\n9:45 p.m.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63751,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a timetable during examinations.','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th colspan=\"2\"> Daily Routine </th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td> Wake up<br>\nBreakfast<br>\nStudy<br>\nMid-morning snack<br>\nStudy<br>\nLunch<br>\nStudy<br>\nSleep<br>\nStudy<br>\nGo out to play<br>\nStudy<br>\nDinner<br>\nStudy<br>\nBedtime </td>\n    <td>6:00 a.m. <br>\n6:30 a.m. <br>\n7:00 a.m. to 11 a.m. <br>\n11:00 a.m. <br>\n11:30 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. <br>\n1:30 p.m. <br>\n2:00 p.m. to 3:00 p.m. <br>\n3:00 p.m. to 4:30 p.m. <br>\n4:30 p.m. to 6:00 p.m. <br>\n6:00 p.m. <br>\n7:00 p.m. to 8:30 p.m. <br>\n8.30 p.m. to 9.00 p.m. <br>\n9.00 p.m. to 10 p.m. <br>\n10.00 p.m.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63752,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a Schedule for a conference.','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n   <th colspan=\"2\"> Schedule for Global Conference - Dated October 5, 2020</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Opening<br>\nWelcome Speech<br>\nConference - Part 1<br>\nConference - Part 2<br>\nConference - Part 3<br>\nConference - Part 4<br>\nOpen Forum<br>\nClosing Speech</td>\n    <td>9:00 a.m. <br>\n9:30 a.m. <br>\n9:30 a.m. to 10:30 a.m. <br>\n11:00 a.m. to 1:30 p.m. <br>\n2:30 p.m. to 4:00 p.m. <br>\n4:30 p.m. to 6:00 p.m. <br>\n6:00 p.m. to 6:30 p.m. <br>\n6:30 p.m.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63753,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a diary entry about your dream vacation experience.','','','Date: November 15, 2022\nDear Diary,\nBali, a dream come true! The warm air and pretty smell of flowers made me feel so welcome. My days were filled with playing in crystal-clear waters, exploring ancient temples and trekking up a volcano for a stunning sunrise. The local people were really nice to me. At night, I ate yummy Balinese food under the stars. The place was so nice that I felt calm and got to know myself better. Bali\'s beauty and culture left an unforgettable mark on my heart. I\'m really thankful for this trip, and I know I\'ll never forget these happy memories.\nRohan\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63754,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a diary entry about a debate you had with your class teacher regarding video games.','','','Date: July 5, 2022\nDear Diary,\nToday\'s class was interesting as we debated the impact of video games with Ms lyer. I talked about the good things about video games, like how they help us solve problems and be creative Ms lyer had concerns about their harmful effects. The debate became lively with classmates joining in, expressing different opinions. It was a fantastic learning experience, understanding diverse perspectives. Ms lyer acknowledged the benefits of video games when played in moderation. I felt grateful for the opportunity to share my thoughts and learn from others in a respectful manner. Debates like this make our classroom discussions engaging and thought-provoking. I\'m looking forward to having more debates like this. \nYashada\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63755,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a diary entry about a festival your school celebrated.','','','Date: December 25, 2022\nDear Diary,\nToday was a magical day as we celebrated Christmas in school. The halls were adorned with lights and decorations. The morning assembly featured joyous Christmas carols. In the auditorium, each class presented heart-warming skits and dances. We exchanged thoughtful gifts with our friends. Then the canteen treated us to delicious Christmas treats. It was heart-warming to see the joy on everyone\'s faces. Christmas in our school is always a special time. It was a day filled with warmth and happiness, making it an unforgettable celebration.\nNisha','',0,0,0,NULL),(63758,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a diary about a competition you participated in your locality.','','','Date: August 1, 2023\nDear Diary,\nToday was an unforgettable day as I took part in a singing competition in our locality. The excitement was high as I performed my favourite song on stage. Waiting for the results was really stressful. When the winners were announced, I was thrilled to hear my name among them! This competition has given me the courage to pursue my passion for singing. I am grateful for the support from my family, friends and the organizers. Singing has brought immense joy to my life, and I can\'t wait to see where this journey takes me next. \nAkash\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63760,2073,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write 3 diary entries for the interesting or important events that take place in your school.','','','1) 11th Nov.: Today we had our Elocution Competition. I was one of the three chosen from our class, and I knew that I had to do my best to win the prize. I know that Miss Neena as well as my friends expected this.\nI was so nervous when it was my turn to go up on the stage and say my poem! I was sure I would forget or go wrong. Then I took a deep breath, looked straight ahead, and recited the poem. I knew I had done well when I heard the claps. And the best part is our class won the prize! This was really a super day for me!\n2) 13th Dec.: Today was our Annual Day, and I had been given an important duty-reading the School Report. Of course, I shared it with Alka, and she was as nervous as I was. I knew Mummy and Daddy would be in the auditorium, and I wanted to do my best for them.\nWhen the time came Miss Neena, our class teacher, pushed us onto the stage. \"All the best,\" she said, and left us to stare at nearly five hundred people. I began with a wobble in my voice, but soon both Alka and I gained confidence. After we finished there was a big applause, and Miss Neena patted us on the back. What a wonderful experience it was!\n3) 18th Dec.: Dear Diary,\nToday something very funny happened. A crow entered our classroom during the Maths period. Miss Amita was solving a sum on the board and the crow sat behind her on the table and cawed loudly. She was so startled that she dropped the chalk onto the floor! Then she turned round and saw the big crow and she nearly jumped with shock. She shooed him away, and he flew off. He came back again twice and did the same thing. We all had a hearty laugh. We told Miss that the crow wanted to learn Maths. too!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63762,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draft a speech to be delivered on Prize Distribution Day.','','','A very good morning and hearty welcome to our Chief Guest Mrs. M. Bhabha, mother of our S.S.C. topper for this year, Master Azhar Bhabha. A warm welcome to our Principal, Fr. Ferrao, parents and guests who have made this felicitation possible for our students.\nLike every year, we have gathered here today to award the students who have worked hard in the past year. This ceremony is hosted to felicitate them and encourage others to work harder. Each and every student has the power to reach for the sky. So, they must try and hitch their wagon to the stars, and thus touch immeasurable heights and hence, attain their ultimate goals.\nOur school motto is \'Work is Worship\', for we are all born for greater things. This motto has been followed religiously by our hardworking students who have put their heart and soul into the endless efforts to reach unattainable heights. Today, each and every one of these meritorious students needs to be applauded and rewarded.\nKudos to all these students, who have successfully fought the twin demons of stress and strain, and have achieved success. These students are a source of inspiration to others to attain those pinnacles with one thought \"Where there is a will, there is a way\".\nA big thank you to our Principal, Mr. Doshi, staff, parents and these prize winning students.\nThank You.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63763,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draft a speech on Environmental Hazards being one of the biggest evils of today.','','','Good morning ladies and gentlemen!\nWe are gathered here to discuss about one of the biggest evils destroying our surroundings today Environmental hazard is and always has been one of the greatest hazards known to the health and the life of man. If this trend continues, our future is at stake. Today, the air around us has changed its normal composition as it is choked with all kinds of chemicals which are harmful for our health.\nThe main causes of air pollution are combustion, manufacturing processes, agricultural activities with the help of chemicals, nuclear energy programmes, explosions in the deep sea, etc. These causes have become the agents of death due to this wide spread environmental pollution. In big cities like Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai and especially Delhi, we breathe in this polluted air with every breath we take in. If we want our future generations to survive and live a healthy life, man needs to find some effective ways to control the three major kinds of pollution - air, water and noise\nThere are laws in the cities against excessive noise near schools and hospitals. Pollution of rivers, streams and lakes should be reduced as quickly as possible. To eliminate the existing pollution, there must be separate industrial zones and even changes in the industrial processes, Cities should try and support as well as implement rules like the odd even vehicular rule set in the country\'s capital 100 We have to find some effective ways to put an end to these threats once for all if everyone puts in a little individual effort, the process will soon turn into a revolution for a greener, fresher world It is our duty to gift our future generations, a healthier tomorrow. Remember, drop by drop the ocean grows. So, let us all put in our individual efforts and hope for the best results!\nThank you!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63764,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draft a speech on the Value of Money.','','','My young friends,\nWe are gathered here today, to discuss an important issue, something that affects each and every one of us, no matter, whether we are young or old. Money, as you know is essential to our fives. We need it to buy our daily requirements and we also need to save some of it for our future Some of us have plenty of money, some have a little less and yet others have barely anything at all and these people find it difficult to survive for they are all fighting a harsh battle in life.\nThe question is how do you value money? Do you spend it lavishly and waste it, or are you careful with it? We need to realise that while we work hard to amass wealth, but at the same time, we must not become slaves to money. Money earned through hard work and laborious processes must be treasured and wisely used.\nWhile it is something we cannot do without, money, in fact, can be the root of all evils. Iris, thus up to each one of us individually to learn the value of money and stop improper usage of it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63765,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','An outstanding sportsperson among your classmates has met with a fatal car accident. Prepare a condolence speech to be delivered at the memorial service held in school.','','','Respected Principal, teachers and my dear friends,\nWe have gathered here this evening to pay our tribute to our dear friend Santosh Jairaman, who is no longer with us. His untimely and unfortunate demise has left us all shocked and heartbroken.\nSantosh was a vibrant young boy, a bright scholar and a keen sportsman. He was the captain of our junior school cricket team and much loved by his teammates, and each and every one of us. He was a great leader on the field and extremely helpful towards others. While cricket was his first love, I know he also enjoyed playing basketball.\nHe was a boy who put his heart into whatever he did - be it sports, studies or his numerous extra-curricular activities. We will miss him very much and on behalf of all present here, I offer our deepest condolences and sympathies to his parents and his brother. May his soul rest in peace.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63766,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech on the Taj Mahal','','','Good Morning to one and all.\nI am honoured to stand here and talk about the Taj Mahal an iconic symbol of love and human creativity. Emperor Shah Jahan built this iconic monument in memory of his beloved wife, Mumtaz Mahal\nThe Taj\'s architecture, beautiful marble exterior, and intricate carvings leave us in awe. But you know what\'s even more beautiful? It represents an incredible love story that knows no bounds. It\'s a stunning symbol of their eternal love. We really need to cherish and protect this cultural treasure for future generations. A visit to the Taj Mahal gives us a chance to reflect on history and human achievements.\nLet\'s all come together to protect and celebrate this wonder of the world, and honor the power of love and human creativity.\nThank you!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63767,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Imagine yourself as a sea. Write a speech on what would you say to the rain if you were a sea.','','','Dear Rain,\nAs the vast sea, 1 welcome you with open waves and a heart filled with joy. Your arrival breathes new life into my waters. I embrace your gentle touch like a long-lost friend.\nYour generosity knows no bounds. You quench the thirst of the lands, help flowers bloom and crops flourish. The creatures of the sea and land rejoice in your presence. You make every part of the world come alive. The sea and the rain dance in harmony. The whole world flourishes because of your precious gift.\nThank you, dear Rain, for gracing me with your presence. Let\'s continue to work together to ensure that nature\'s wonders never cease to amaze us.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63768,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech celebrating the gifts of parents on the occasion of Parent\'s Day.','','','Hello, everyone!\nToday, we gather to celebrate a wonderful occasion Parent\'s Day! It\'s a day all about honouring and thanking our amazing parents for everything they do for us.\nParents are like superheroes, always there to support and protect us. They teach us valuable lessons help us grow, and show us unconditional love. Don\'t you think that we should be thanking our parents every day and not just on Parent\'s Day?\nOn this Parent\'s Day, let\'s promise to show our love and respect to our parents every day, not just today. A simple \"thank you\" or a warm hug can mean the world to them. Happy Parent\'s Day to all the incredible moms and dads out there!\nThank you, and have a fantastic Parent\'s Day celebration!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63769,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Your uncle got a job in the Navy. Write a speech, congratulating him on behalf of your family.','','','Hello, everyone!\nToday is a very special day for our family. I\'m here to congratulate my dear uncle on his fantastic achievement - getting a job in the Navy!\nUncle, you have made us all so proud. Your dedication, hard work and perseverance have paid off We couldn\'t be happier for you. Joining the Navy is a significant milestone. It reflects your commitment to serving our country and keeping us safe. As you step into this new chapter of your life, we want you to know that our love and support are always with you.\nCongratulations, dear uncle! We salute you and wish you all the best on this incredible journey!\nThank you!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63770,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech for a social service campaign held in your school, raising awareness about treating animals with kindness and considering pet adoptions.','','','Respected Principal, teachers and my dear friends,\nWe all love pets, don\'t we? Today, I\'d like to address an important matter that touches the hearts of many treating animals with kindness and the significance of pet adoptions.\nAnimals, just like us, feel emotions and deserve love and care. They bring joy, companionship and unconditional love into our lives. Sadly, not all animals are treated with the love and respect they deserve. One way we can make a difference is through pet adoptions. Instead of buying pets from breeders or pet shops, let\'s consider adopting from animal shelters. Many lovely animals are waiting for a loving home. By adopting them, we can give them a new chance at a happy life.\nLet us be the voice for animals who cannot speak for themselves. I encourage you all to spread awareness, support animal shelters and consider pet adoption. Let\'s make a difference in the lives of our furry little friends and show them the love they truly deserve.\nThank you!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63771,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Discuss what a friendly and good-natured peacock would say to a crane. Write his speech. (5-8 lines)','','','A friendly peacock would say:\nHello, dear crane! You are lucky that you can fly so high with the help of your feathers. I have shiny and colourful feathers which make me look beautiful. However, I wish I could fly high into the clouds like you. Please describe the scene that you see from the sky above. Though I am dressed like a king, I am stuck here on earth; while you can fly and go anywhere you please.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63772,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short speech for Ans the State of Maharashtra.','','','Hello, I am the state of Maharashtra. I am the third-largest state of India and located in the western region of the country. Mumbai is my capital city and my state language is Marathi I am the second-most urbanised and one of the wealthiest states of India. Pandharpur, Dehu and Alandi are some of the popular Hindu places of pilgrimage that are located on my land. I maintain a leading position in the industrial sector and I am also the prime location for the Indian entertainment industry. My cultural diversity and liveliness make me an attractive tourist spot worldwide.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63773,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Present a speech about yourself as if you were one of the Seven Sisters.','','','Good day friends.\nI am better known as Ahom, the gateway to the Seven Sisters. Have you guessed who 1 am? Yes, truly I am Assam lying between the valleys of the Brahmaputra and Barak rivers in north-east India. My rivers keep the land lush and fertile, while the picturesque landscapes with the tea estates are world-famous. My people celebrate a festival called Bihu, and the women weave fairy tales on their silk looms. Cane and bamboo are inseparable parts of my life. I am also known as the land of fairs and festivals and my scenic forests and parks are a sight to behold. Come and visit me and enjoy the hospitality and cuisine of my land.\nIt was lovely chatting up with you!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63774,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech to be given on Teachers\' Day.','','','Good morning, respected teacher and my dear classmates.\nAt the very outset, I wish to thank you, teacher, for all you have taught us - from school subjects to grooming ourselves. We shall ever be grateful to you as like a mother, you have gone that extra mile to give us your very best. Teacher, you are the sunshine of our lives for you bring in rays of hope even through the darkest night. We admire you for your knowledge and method of teaching. May God grant you success in all that you do and may He reward you with true joy and eternal bliss.\nThank you!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63775,2074,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short speech on \'My Hobby\'.','','','Hello, friends. Today I would like to tell you something about my hobby.\nMy hobby is reading. I love to read. I read many things-magazines, comic books, story books, newspapers, etc. My favourite storybooks are the Harry Potter series. Once I begin a Harry Potter book, I cannot put it down. I also like books written by Enid Blyton.\nReading is a great and delightful pastime. I am never bored like other boys and girls.\nOn holidays, hot summer afternoons and rainy days, when there is nothing else to do, there is always a book to turn to. Books give me a lot of information and new ideas. They also entertain me. They are my best friends.\nI would like to end by asking all of you to cultivate the habit of reading. Believe me, you will never be bored again. \nThank you.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63778,2075,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a dialogue between a teacher and a student who has not done his homework.','','','Teacher: Alfred, why have you not submitted your homework for correction?\nStudent: Miss, I am sorry, but I have not been able to complete it.\nTeacher: Why Alfred? You had three whole days to write an essay... surely the time was enough.\nStudent: Yes, it was. But there was a medical emergency in my house last Monday due to which all our schedules are a little haywire.\nTeacher: Oh! Is that so? What happened?\nStudent: Miss, my grandmother suddenly became serious and had to be admitted to the hospital. She will be there this entire week, and we leave for the hospital the moment we return home. My mother says my younger sister and I are too young to stay at home for several hours together and that is why we have to accompany her to the hospital when we reach home from school.\nTeacher: Oh I see………….\nStudent: By the time we return it is pretty late, and I first try to study for the tests we have This is the reason I could not finish the essay.\nTeacher: Do you think you will be able to complete it along with your homework during the weekend?\nStudent: Yes. Miss, I will surely do that, Granny will be home on Saturday morning and that will give me enough time to complete for all the pending work. Miss, thank you for understanding my difficulty.\nTeacher: Its alright, sit down.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63782,2075,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a dialogue between a fruit vendor and a buyer.','','','Buyer: How much are these mangoes for?\nFruit Vendor: Madam, they are ninety rupees a kilo.\nBuyer: Ninety rupees a kilo! Aren\'t you asking for too much?\nFruit Vendor: Madam, look at the size and quality of the mangoes. Besides, this year the production is less and therefore, the prices have remained a little high\nBuyer: Please tell me how much you can give them to me for. I will definitely net pay you ninety rupees a kilo!\nFruit Vendor: Madam, how much would you like to buy?\nBuyer: I need at least three kilos of mangoes. I would also like to buy some apples and bananas.\nFruit Vendor: Madam, you can pay me seventy-five rupees for a kilo. Please select the ones that you would like to buy.\nBuyer: Please make sure that you give me a good rate for the apples and bananas also. I hope the fruits are sweet.\nFruit Vendor: I will give them to you at the best possible price. Don\'t worry about the quality of my fruits, they are good. I am sure you will come back to me for more after you have purchased from me once.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63785,2075,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a dialogue between a mother and her daughter. The daughter is seeking permission from the mother to go out with her friends.','','','Daughter: Ma, I need to speak to you about something important.\nMother: Yes Nidhi, tell me.\nDaughter: Ma, can I please go out with my friends on Saturday evening?\nMother: Out with your friends? Why can\'t you get together at someone\'s house? Why not call all your friends home instead?\nDaughter: No ma, actually it is Anusha\'s birthday that day and she wants to give us a treat at the new fast food joint at the mall. Please ma, can I go?\nMother: I see, how many of you are supposed to be going and what is the exact plan?\nDaughter: She has invited ten of us though all of us have not confirmed yet. The plan is to meet in front of the main entrance at 3 p.m. and then go window shopping, go to the fast food joint after that, and wrap things up by 7 p.m. So that we can be home by 7:30 p.m.\nMother: Are any elders going to be there with you?\nDaughter: No, but Anusha lives in the building next to the mall and I can leave her mother\'s number with you. Besides, all of us will be carrying our cellphones, so I can leave a couple of other numbers for you including Anusha\'s. Please ma, can I go? Its going to be a lot of fun.\nMother: Alright, you may go. But make sure you leave all the contact details for me and call me when you reach. Please maintain the time limit you have mentioned or else I will be very much worried.\nDaughter: I will.,,,,,,,,,,, Thank you ma, you are the best!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63787,2075,'descriptive','5',4,3,'Out of Text Book','','Write a dialogue between two friends who have met after a long time.','','','Anil: Shivshankar Patil! Is that you?\nShiv: Yes... I\'m sorry, how do you know me?\nAnil: Well... we used to be in school together. Anil...\nShiv: Anil Vaswani? My God! I did not recognize you! How have you been and where were you all these years? I can see that you have put on a little weight. It suits you.\nAnil: Well, I went off to the U.S. for my higher studies and stayed on. Now, I work and live there.\nShiv: That\'s great! So, now you are settled there with your family.\nAnil: Yes. My wife and I are both practicing surgeons there and we have a little son. What about you?\nShiv: Well, I have been here all along. Currently, I am the Vice-president of a multinational organization. My wife is in H.R. department and we have a little daughter.\nAnil: That\'s wonderful! We should get together with our families before I return. My parents still live where they used to when we were in school and that\'s where we stay when we visit them annually.\nShiv: Definitely! Now that we have met again, we must keep in touch. Please give me your contact number. I shall call you this evening and we\'ll set up a little get-together.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63788,2075,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a dialogue between a doctor and a patient who has been suffering from fever repeatedly.','','','Patient: Good morning, doctor!\nDoctor: Good morning! Please come in and have a seat. Tell me what is the problem?\nPatient: Doctor, for the past week I have been suffering from fever off and on. It gras away for a day or two with medication but comes back again. I have been feeling exceptionally unwell for the past three days. Now I have a stomachache too.\nDoctor: What medicines have you been taking? Is your appetite normal?\nPatient: Basic paracetamol, doctor. And no, I don\'t feel like eating anything at all.\nDoctor: All right. Please lie down here. Let me examine you first.\nPatient: Doctor, I hope it\'s nothing serious. I have already been missing a lot of work and the business is beginning to suffer.\nDoctor: No, I think it is a case of a viral infection. The stomach problem is leading to a recurring fever. I will write the names of a couple of medicines for you, please take them for three days and you should be alright. However, if the problem persists, please come back to see me.\nPatient: Alright doctor, I will. Thank you so much. What about your fees sir?\nDoctor: Please speak to the receptionist outside. She will give you the details.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63790,2075,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a conversation between a father and son discussing about their favourite sport.','','','Father: Hey, did you see that goal in yesterday\'s match? What a brilliant strike!\nSon: Yes, it was awesome! I wish I could kick the ball like that someday.\nFather: You definitely can! You\'ve got some good skills on the field.\nSon: I guess you\'re right. I should practise more often, just like the players I admire.\nFather: Absolutely! Practice makes perfect. By the way, which player do you look up to the most? Son: (thinking) Hmm, I really like Cristiano Ronaldo. He\'s so talented and hardworking.\nFather: Ronaldo is a true legend. But remember to be yourself on the field. Find your style and strengths.\nSon: I\'ll remember that. I can\' wait for the next school match to show my team I\'m strong.\nFather: That\'s the way to go! And don\'t forget that teamwork, team-support and having fun are all equally important. \nSon: I will. I love playing football. It keeps me active and healthy too!\nFather: (smiling) That\'s great! Sports are good for your fitness. \nSon: Do you think one day I could play football professionally?\nFather: (thoughtfully) It\'s tough, but with dedication, you can achieve anything. I believe in you.\nSon: Thanks. I\'ll give it my all!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63791,2075,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a conversation between a Cheetah and an Eagle. ','','','Cheetah: Hey, Eagle! I noticed you\'re a bird. Do you like being one?\nEagle: Hi, Cheetah! Yes, I do. I enjoy being a bird and having wings.\nCheetah: Wings must be fun. But I\'m a land animal, and I love running fast!\nEagle:: Running fast sounds exciting! I wish I could do that too.\nCheetah: Well, you can fly, and that\'s pretty cool too. \nEagle: You\'re right. I can spot my prey from above and dive down to catch them. \nCheetah: I use my speed to catch my food and survive in the jungle.\nEagle: That\'s impressive. We have different ways of living and surviving. \nCheetah: Yes, we do. I\'m glad we both have unique abilities.\nEagle: Me too! It\'s fun being different and special in our own ways. \nCheetah: Take care, and enjoy your high flights in the sky!\nEagle: You too, Cheetah! Stay fast and strong. Goodbye!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63792,2075,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a conversation between two friends planning about the actitivites and games for their upcoming school picnic.','','','Sarah: (excitedly) Hey, guess what? Our school picnic is coming soon! Any ideas for games or activities?\nRiya: How about a treasure hunt? We can split into teams and find clues around the picnic spot.\nSarah: Awesome idea! We can also play football and frisbee for some outdoor fun. \nRiya: Yes! And let\'s have a talent show too. We can showcase our skills and have a good time.\nSarah: Perfect! And we must definitely take some group photos to remember the day.\nRiya: Definitely! Let\'s capture all the fun moments.\nSarah: I can\'t wait! It\'s going to be a super fun day with everyone.\nRiya: I know, right? Unforgettable memories!\nSarah: Our picnic will be the best of the year!\nRiya: (grinning) Absolutely! Let\'s get everything ready for the best picnic ever!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63793,2075,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a conversation between a fitness teacher and a student about following a healthy diet.','','','Fitness Teacher: Hi there! You\'ve been doing great in class. How are you feeling?\nStudent: Hi! Thanks, I feel more energetic and active now.\nFitness Teacher: That\'s great! Let\'s also talk about the importance of a healthy diet.\nStudent: Sure! What foods should I eat to stay healthy?\nFitness Teacher: For kids like you, a balanced diet is important. Eat lots of fruits and vegetables.\nStudent: I love fruits! What about sweets and junk food?\nFitness Teacher: It\'s okay to have treats sometimes, but limit them. Instead, go for healthier snacks yogurt or nuts.\nStudent: Okay. What about drinks? Some of my friends drink sugary sodas.\nFitness Teacher: Choose healthier drinks like water or milk. They keep you hydrated and have es nutrients.\nStudent: I\'ll drink more water instead of soda.\nFitness Teacher: Great choice! Water is good for your body and helps during exercise.\nStudent: I\'ll try my best to be healthy and do well in class.\nFitness Teacher: I believe in you! I\'m here to support you on your fitness journey.\nStudent: Thanks! See you in the next class!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63803,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the wrong pair and correct it:\n1) The first President of Indian National Congress - Wyomesh Chandra Banerjee\n2) Assassinated Rand - The Chapekhar Brothers\n3) He pronounced the word Swaraj for the first time - Lokmanya Tilak\n4) The British officer who took initiative in forming the Indian National Congress. - Allen Octavian Hume\n','','','Wrong Pair: He pronounced the word Swaraj for the first time - Lokmanya Tilak\nCorrected pair: He pronounced the word Swaraj for the first time - Dadabhai Nowrojee.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63804,1943,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Moderate leaders : i) ------------ 	ii) --------------','','','i) Gopal Krishna Gokhale	ii) Ferozshah Mehta','',0,0,0,NULL),(63805,1943,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Extremist leaders : i) -----------	ii) --------------','','','i) Lokmanya Tilak		ii) Lala Lajpat Rai','',0,0,0,NULL),(63806,1943,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','They studied ancient Indian culture : \ni) ------------- 	ii) ---------------\n','','','i) Dr. Bhau Daji Lad		ii) Dr. R.G. Bhandarkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(63807,1943,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Leaders of Home Rule Movement : \ni) -------------- 	ii) -------------\n','','','i) Dr. Annie Besant		ii) Lokmanya Tilak','',0,0,0,NULL),(63808,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which institute was established to study ancient Indian culture?','','','The Asiatic Society was established in Bengal to study ancient Indian culture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63809,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which were the mediums initially used by the extremist leaders to create political awakening?','','','The extremist leaders initially used the mediums of newspapers, national education and national festivals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63810,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the objective of the extremist leaders in founding the educational institutions?','','','The extremist leaders established educational institutions for creating a generation which will have concern for their own language and tradition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63811,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who led the Anti Partition Movement?','','','Surendranath Banerjee, Anand Mohan Bose, Rabindranath Tagore and such other leaders led the Anti-Partition Movement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63812,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Tilak criticise the Montague-Chelmsford Act?','','','Lokmanya Tilak criticised the Montague-Chelmsford Act in the following words, \"This is neither Swaraj nor its foundation.\"','',0,0,0,NULL),(63813,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What did Lokmanya Tilak firmly state?','','','Lokmanya firmly stated that \"Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it.\"','',0,0,0,NULL),(63814,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Moderates ','','','1) The Moderates were the leaders who wanted to carry out nationalistic movement within the constitutional methods and peaceful means.\n2) The leaders were realistic and highly educated and had faith in the justice of the British.\n3) They had hope that if they place their demands in constitutional manner the British will give justice to their demands.\n4) They were aware that a strong foundation needs to be built through organised work.\n5) The philosophy and principles of western thinkers like liberalism, freedom, equality, fraternity influenced them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63815,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Extremists ','','','1) The leaders of the Indian National Congress who advocated intensification of the struggle to attain freedom were known as Extremists.\n2) Moderates and Extremists were unanimous about the objectives of Congress. The Extremists had no faith in the constitutional methods of the Moderates.\n3) According to Extremists, freedom will be attained only if lakhs of people participated in the freedom movement and challenge the British government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63816,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Objectives of the Indian National Congress','','','The Indian National Congress was established in 1885 with the following objectives:\n1) To bring together the people of India on a common platform.\n2) To create a feeling of unity among them, irrespective of religion, race, language, geographical territories. \n3) To provide opportunities to understand one another\'s problems and views.\n4) To increase the feeling of unity among the people.\n5) To take measures for the upliftment of the country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63817,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Anti Partition Movement ','','','1) With the motive to create a divide between the Hindus and the Muslims, Lord Curzon divided the province of Bengal in the Muslim-majority East Bengal and the Hindu-majority West Bengal in 1905.\n2) The day of partition, 16th October was observed as the \'National Mourning Day\'.\n3) All over India, protest meetings were organised to condemn the decision of the government.\n4) Singing of Vande Mataram and Raksha-bandhan programmes were arranged to mark the protest.\n5) Government run schools and colleges were boycotted in large number by the students.\n6) Sensing the intensity and severity of dissatisfaction the British annulled the Partition of Bengal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63818,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Four Point Programme of the Indian National Congress ','','','In the session of 1906 of the Indian National Congress, the four point programme was unanimously accepted.\n1) Swadeshi: To make use of capital, resources, manpower in our country so that it becomes self-sufficient.\n2) Boycott: It was decided to boycott foreign goods as a first step, and boycott of foreign rule be the next step. It would be an attack on the roots of British imperialism.\n3) Swaraj: The final objective is to attain freedom.\n4) National Education: To impart education which will create pride for the nation among people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63819,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','First session of Indian National Congress','','','1) The founding session of the Indian National Congress took place on 28th December, 1885 at Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit school in Mumbai.\n2) It was presided by Wyomesh Chandra Banerjee, a renowned lawyer from Kolkata.\n3) It was attended by seventy-two delegates from different provinces of India.\n4) Allen Octavian Hume took the initiative in establishing of the Indian National Congress. \n5) Increase in the number of Indians in the administration, reduction in military expenditure were the demands placed before the British government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63820,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Servants of India Society ','','','1) Gopal Krishna Gokhale founded the Servants of India Society in 1905.\n2) Its main objectives were: \ni) to create love for the county, \nii) teach them sacrifice of self-interest, \niii) no differentiation on the basis of caste and religion, \niv) to create social harmony, \nv) to spread education.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63821,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Lucknow Pact ','','','1) An attempt was made under the leadership of Lokmanya Tilak to resolve differences in the Indian National Congress at its Lucknow session in 1916.\n2) In the same year, there was an agreement between Indian National Congress and the Muslim League, known as the Lucknow Pact.\n3) According to this Pact, Indian National Congress agreed to separate electorate for Muslims.\n4) In return, the Muslim League agreed to support the Indian National Congress in Bits work for getting political rights to India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63822,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Home Rule Movement ','','','1) Home rule means self-rule or self-government.\n2) It was modelled after the Home Rule Movement in Ireland.\n3) It was led by Dr. Annie Besant and Lokmanya Tilak.\n4) They made extensive tours in different parts of the country so that the demand of self-government could reach the common people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63823,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','In the struggle for Independence, a sense of identity was awakened among the Indians.','','','1) Western education familiarised the educated Indians with modern values such as liberty, equality, democracy and nationalism.\n2) The Asiatic Society at Bengal edited and published hundreds of manuscripts in Sanskrit, languages. Persian and other Indian\n3) The realization that India had a rich ancient heritage aroused the feeling of national pride. This gave a sense of identity to Indians.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63824,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Two groups were formed in the Congress.','','','1) Though at a slow pace, the contribution of Indian National Congress was consistent in the initial stage. But the extremists felt to intensify the freedom struggle.\n2) Moderates and Extremists were unanimous about the objectives of Congress. But they had differences regarding the methods/ways to achieve it.\n3) The moderates insisted on constitutional measures whereas extremists wanted to adopt severe methods.\n4) The tension between these groups increased during the Surat session in 1907.\nThis led to the formation of two groups within the Congress.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63825,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Lord Curzon decided to partition Bengal.','','','1) Bengal was a large province.. So under the pretext of administrative convenience, the province of Bengal was partitioned by Lord Curzon.\n2) Accordingly, the Muslim-majority East Bengal and the Hindu-Majority West Bengal were created in 1905.\n3) The real motive was to create a divide between the Hindus and the Muslims and thereby weaken the nationalist movement. The British used the Policy of \'Divide and Rule\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63826,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A feeling of nationalism developed among the Indians.','','','1) The British established α centralised administration in India and applied uniform policies all over the country.\n2) They also laid down the principle of equality before law.\n3) They introduced the modern means of transport and communication.\n4) It benefited Indians as well. This made possible for the people living in different parts of India to establish contact with one another.\nThis factors developed a feeling of nationalism among the Indians.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63827,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Lokmanya was imprisoned in 1897.','','','1) In 1897, hundreds of people died in Pune due to the epidemic of Plague.\n2) An officer name Rand was appointed to bring the epidemic under control.\n3) He started a search campaign to find Plague patients and adopted oppressive measures.\n4) To avenge this cruel and oppressive treatment meted out to people, the Chapekar brothers assassinated him.\n5) An unsuccessful attempt was made by the government to connect Lokmanya Tilak with this conspiracy.\n6) When they failed in their attempt, they imprisoned him with revenge.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63828,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The National Congress split at its Surat session in 1907.','','','1) The differences between the moderates and the extremists reached a climax in 1907.\n2) The moderates wanted to keep aside the resolution of Swadeshi and Boycott which was thwarted by the extremists.\n3) Some moderate leaders blamed the extremist leaders for trying to capture the Indian National Congress.\n4) It became impossible to arrive at a compromise between both the groups.\nAs a result the Indian National Congress split at its Surat session in 1907.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63829,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The charge of sedition was put on Lokmanya Tilak.','','','1) There was wide protest against the partition of Bengal throughout the country.\n2) With a view to restrain the anti-partition movements government resorted to many suppressive measures.\n3) Strict action was taken against extremists leaders which caused severe reactions in Bengal.\n4) The revolutionaries adopted means of firing and bomb blasts which was advocated by Lokmanya Tilak through his newspaper Kesari.\nTherefore, the charge of sedition was put on Lokmanya Tilak by the British government for which he was sent to Mandalay jail for 6 years.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63830,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The government passed the Morley-Minto Act.','','','1) The economic policies of the British increased poverty in India.\n2) It created dissatisfaction in the minds of the people against British rule.\n3) The oppressive measures adopted by Lord Curzon to suppress the protest of partition of Bengal, exclusion of educated Indians in government services and the unjust treatment towards Indians in Africa all this added to the dissatisfaction among the Indians.\n4) Morley-Minto reforms were passed by the British government as a temporary remedy to calm discontent among the Indians.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63831,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Montague-Chelmsford Act disappointed the people of India. ','','','The Montague-Chelmsford Act disappointed the people of India, because \n1) The British Government had declared its intention to gradually grant the right to self-rule and responsible government in 1917.\n2) In 1919, the British Parliament passed an act to bring constitutional reforms in India.\n3) According to the Act, less important departments were transferred to Indian ministers and important departments like Finance, Home affairs and Revenue was kept with the Governor.\n4) Thus, it belied the hope that the Act would lay foundation of responsible government in India and disappointed the people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63832,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What realisation led to the emergence of a political organisation on all India level?','','','1) English educated Indians were instrumental to bring about Renaissance.\n2) They launched reform movements in social, political, religious, economic and cultural field in different parts of the country.\n3) The political organisations in different parts of the country formed during various movements felt the need to create a political organisation on an all India level having common goal.\n4) It was necessary to bring together groups and people who had political awareness.\n5) It was necessary to draw attention of the people towards questions of the nation.\nThis led to the emergence of a political organization on all India level.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63833,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the impact of imperialistic policy of the British on India.','','','1) The economic exploitation of India through the imperialistic policies led to the drain of the Indian wealth to England.\n2) The compulsion to grow cash crops, burden of land taxes and recurring famines made conditions of farmers miserable. All these broke the backbone of the Indian agriculture.\n3) With the decline of traditional industries, there was increase in unemployment.\n4) The workers were exploited by the capitalists.\n5) The middle class suffered due to imposition of various new taxes.\nThis was the impact of the British imperialist policy which led to discontent among the Indians.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63834,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the impact of western education?','','','1) Due to spread of western education, new ideas like Justice, Liberty, Equality, Democracy, etc. were introduced to the Indians.\n2) The young Indians imbibed the values like rationalism, humanity, nationalism and scientific attitude.\n3) They developed a feeling that they could carry out the work of the country.\n4) English became the new medium of communication.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63835,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What message did Dadabhai Nowrojee give at the session of Congress in 1906?','','','At the session of the Indian National Congress in 1906, Dadabhai Nowrojee pronounced the word \'Swaraj\' for the first time. He gave the message \n1) to remain united, \n2) try sincerely and fulfil the aim of Swaraj so that those who are poor, hungry and having diseases could be saved, \n3) India to get respectable position among the developed countries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63836,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the process of formation of Muslim League.','','','1) The British got very disturbed by the overwhelming public response to the Indian National Congress in the anti partition movement.\n2) They once again resorted to the policy of \'Divide and rule\'.\n3) Many British officers suggested that a separate political organisation was necessary to safeguard the interests of the Muslims.\n4) Due to motivation of British government, under the leadership of Aga Khan a committee of upper class Muslims met Governor General Lord Minto.\n5) Thus, encouraged by Lord Minto and other British officers, the Muslim League was formed in 1906.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63837,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the provisions of Morley-Minto Act?','','','1) The Morley-Minto Act of 1909 provided for increase of the number of Indian members in the legislatures and the inclusion of some elected Indian members.\n2) It created separate electorates for the Indian Muslims.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63838,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is Responsive Cooperation?','','','1) India had to face the brunt of First World War which led to growing dissatisfaction among people.\n2) To curb their dissatisfaction and get cooperation, Montague, the Secretary of State for India, declared in 1917 that Britain would gradually grant the right to self-rule and a responsible government to India.\n3) Lokmanya Tilak declared that if the British Government showed sympathy and a considerate attitude to the demands of the people then the people of India would cooperate with the government. This is called \'Responsive Cooperation\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63839,1943,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the background behind the establishment of Indian National Congress with the help of following points:\n1) Centralisation of administration 	2) Economic exploitation\n3) Western education			        4) Study of Ancient Indian History\n5) Role of newspapers\n','','','The background behind the establishment of the Indian National Congress in 1885 :\n<b>1) Centralisation of administration</b>\n1) The uniform policies, identical reforms and equality before law brought the nation under one roof.\n2) The network of roads and railways brought the people of India together.\n3) It developed the feeling of unity among Indians.\n<b>(2) Economic exploitation</b>\n1) The economic exploitation of India through the imperialistic policies led to the drain of the Indian wealth to England.\n2) Industries declined and the farmers became bankrupt.\n3) Imposition of taxes on middle class and the exploitation of worker class by the capitalist led to growth of discontent.\n<b>(3) Western education</b>\n1) Western education familiarised the educated Indians with modern values and principles such as liberty, equality and democracy.\n2) They accepted principles like rationalism, humanity and nationalism.\n3) They realised that they can carry out work of the country by following them.\n<b>(4) Study of Ancient Indian History </b>\n1) The manuscripts in Sanskrit, Persian and many other languages were examined and research was published.\n2) Many western Scholars started to study Indian culture.\n3) The realisation that India had a rich ancient heritage aroused the feeling of national pride among Indians.\n<b>(5) Role of newspapers</b>\n1) Newspapers in English and vernacular languages carried articles criticising the policies of the government.\n2) It led to social and political awakening.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63840,1943,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give a brief account of the work of Extremist leaders in India\'s freedom struggle.','','','1) Extremist leaders Lokmanya Tilak, Bipin Chandra Pal, Lala Lajpat Rai advocated intensification of the struggle since the British did not respond to the petitions and appeals.\n2) Initially, they used the mediums of newspapers, national festivals and national education to bring about political awakening.\n3) They formed educational institutions to sow the seeds of nationalism in society and to create a generation which will show concern about their language and tradition.\n4) Lokmanya Tilak severely criticised the suppressive policies or the British through the newspapers like \'Kesari\' and \'Maratha\'.\n5) Lokmanya Tilak started Ganesh Utsav and Shiv Jayanti to bring people together.\n6) The leaders did not adopt the means of armed revolution but insisted on extensive agitations.\n7) Swaraj, Swadeshi, National Education and Boycott was the four-point programme accepted.\n8) The Moderates laid the foundation of freedom struggle and the Extremists carried it forward.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63841,1943,'descriptive','5',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which measures were taken by the British government to suppress the Anti-Partition Movement?','','','The British government adopted following measures to suppress the anti-partition movement:\n1) The public meetings were banned.\n2) Strict punishment was given to those who broke the law.\n3) Even school children were beaten up.\n4) Many restrictions were imposed on the newspapers.\n5) The British confiscated many printing presses on false ground of criticising the government.\n6) Writers and editors were imprisoned.\n7) Strict actions were taken against many extremist leaders.\n8) Lokmanya Tilak was sent to Mandalay jail at Myanmar for 6 years on the charge of sedition.\n9) Bipin Chandra Pal was sent to jail and Lala Lajpat Rai was deported out of Punjab.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63843,1943,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the picture and write about his contribution to the freedom struggle.','','','1) The above picture is of Lokmanya Tilak.\n2) He started the newspapers Kesari and Maratha.\n3) He started festivals like Ganesh Utsav and Shiv Jayanti for people to come together and get inspiration from contribution of national personalities.\n4) The bomb attacks by the revolutionary was advocated through newspaper Kesari and Maratha.\n5) Due to this, he was charged with sedition and put in Mandalay jail for 6 years.\n6) When he was in Mandalay prison he wrote Geetarahasya which advocated philosophy of Karmayoga and stressed upon the people to always remain in action.\n7) An attempt was made under his leadership to resolve differences between two groups in the Indian National Congress in its session of 1916.\n8) He started the Home Rule Movement along with Dr. Annie Besant.\n9) He travelled in different parts of India so that the demand for self government reach the common man.\n10) He firmly declared, \'Swaraj is my birth right and I shall have it.\'\n11) He criticised the Montague-Chelmsford Reform Act when it failed to fulfill the demand government. of responsible\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(63844,1943,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Column ‘A’</th>\n    <th>Column ‘B’</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) Asiatic Society</td>\n    <td>a) Pune</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii) Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute</td>\n    <td>b) Bengal</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii) Gopal Krishna Gokhale</td>\n    <td>c) deported from Punjab</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv) Dadabhai Naoroji</td>\n    <td>d) President of the Indian National Congress session (1906)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td>e) President of the Indian National Congress session (1905)</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','','','(i - b), (ii – a), (iii - e), (iv – d)','',0,0,0,NULL),(63845,1943,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Venue of the first session of the Indian National Congress.','','','Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit School, Mumbai.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63846,1943,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British officer who took a lead in the establishment of Indian National Congress.','','','Allan Octavian Hume','',0,0,0,NULL),(63847,1943,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The national leader who firmly stated that \'Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it\'.','','','Lokmanya Tilak','',0,0,0,NULL),(63848,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of newspapers that criticised the government.','','','Newspapers that criticised the government were Darpan, Hindu, Amrit Bazar Patrika, Prabhakar, Kesari and Mahratta.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63849,1943,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which points were discussed during the first session of the Indian National Congress?','','','Increased proportion of Indians in the administration and reduction of military expenses by the British government, such points were discussed during the first session of the Indian National Congress.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63850,1943,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which policy was implemented by the British to cause a split in the Indian national movement?','','','The policy of \'Divide and Rule\' was implemented by the British to cause a split in the Indian national movement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63851,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the early period, which methods were used by the Extremists to bring about political awakening among the Indians?','','','In order to bring about political awakening among the Indians, the extremists during the early period started various newspapers, organised national festivals and established educational institutions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63852,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the extremist leaders establish educational institutions?','','','The extremist leaders established educational institutions for creating a generation who would be concerned and feel proud about their own language & tradition.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63853,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which methods were adopted by the revolutionaries when the British Government used suppressive measures after the anti-partition movement?','','','When the suppressive measures were used by the British Government, the revolutionaries adopted the means of firing, bomb blasts, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63854,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When did the dispute between the Moderates and the extremists resolve?','','','The dispute between the Moderates and the extremists was resolved in the Lucknow session of the Indian National Congress in 1916 under the leadership of Lokmanya Tilak.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63855,1943,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which other country did a similar movement like Home Rule began?','','','A similar movement like Home Rule began in Ireland which was against colonialism.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63856,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which situations in India favoured the establishment of a political organisation at an all-India level?','','','The situations which favoured the establishment of a political organisation at an all-India level were as follows:\ni. Firstly, the newly English educated society sowed the seeds of nationalism among the people by bringing about reforms.\nii. The various movements like the Women\'s reform movement, the Akali Movement, etc., which took place in different parts of India created a favourable situation for the establishment of a political organisation at an all-India level.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63857,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the reason behind the establishment of an all India level political organisation?','','','The reason behind the establishment of an all-India level political organisation was as follows:\ni. To bring together the various provincial political organisations.\nii. To bring together the different groups of politically aware people.\niii. To express the aspiration of the nation by diverting the attention of the people towards the questions of the nation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63858,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Enlist the demands of the Moderate leaders.','','','In the session of the Indian National congress, following demands were put forth by the Moderate leaders-\ni. To give representation in provincial legislature.\nii. To get jobs for educated Indians.\niii. To reduce the increasing expenses of the military.\niv. To separate legislature and judiciary so that the legal rights of Indians can be protected.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63859,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was Lokmanya Tilak\'s contribution to the national movement?','','','Lokmanya Tilak was an Extremist leader. His contribution to the national movement is as follows \ni. Lokmanya Tilak severely criticised the suppressive policy of the British through his newspapers \'Kesari\' and \'Mahratta\'.\nii. The organisation of Shiv Jayanti and Ganesh Utsav was started by Tilak in order to unite the people.\niii. He stressed upon karmayog and wanted people to be always in action and for this purpose he wrote \'Gita Rahasya\'.\niv. During the Lucknow session of the Indian National Congress held in 1916, Lokmanya Tilak made an attempt to resolve the dispute between the Moderates and the Extremists.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63860,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did Lokmanya Tilak organise festivals like Shiv Jayanti and Ganesh Utsav?','','','Lokmanya Tilak organised festivals like Shiv Jayanti and Ganesh Utsav due to the following reasons\ni. Lokmanya Tilak felt that in order to strengthen the national movement, the people should forget the internal differences and come together to exchange their ideas.\nii. He also felt that common people should get inspiration from the contribution of national personalities.\niii. He was of the opinion that if people come together for religious reasons then it is not banned by the government but if people come together for political reasons then it is banned.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63861,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the beliefs of the Extremist leaders?','','','i. The Extremists believed in the participation of the masses in the national movement and wanted lakhs of people to challenge the British government.\nii. They wanted the people to achieve success by struggling against the British government.\niii. They wanted to make the national movement more severe.\niv. They did not adopt the means of armed revolution but insisted for an extensive public agitation.\nv. The Extremists carried forward the movement started by the Moderates.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63862,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What message did Dadabhai Naoroji give during the 1906 session of the Indian National Congress?','','','i. Dadabhai Naoroji was the President of 1906 session of the Indian National Congress.\nii. In his Presidential speech, he gave the message to the people to remain united try sincerely and fulfill the aim of swaraj.\niii. According to him, this would save large number of people who were suffering from poverty, hunger, diseases and India will get a respectable position among the developed countries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63863,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the main objectives of the \'Servants of India Society\'?','','','The main objectives of the \'Servants of India Society\' were as follows -\ni. To create love for the country among the people.\nii. To teach people sacrifice of self - interest.\niii. To destroy the differences between religion and caste.\niv. To create social harmony.\nv. To spread education among the people of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63864,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which suppressive measures did the government adopt to control the anti-partition movement?','','','The British was very disturbed looking at the influential public protest after the anti-partition movement. Hence, in order to control the anti-partition movement, the government adopted various suppressive measures. They were as follows:\ni. Holding public meetings was banned and people who did not follow this were strictly punished.\nii. School children were beaten.\niii. Many restrictions were imposed on newspapers.\niv. Printing press were confiscated for criticising the government and writers & editors were imprisoned.\nv. Strict action was taken against the Extremist leaders.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63865,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is \'Responsive Co-operation\'? ','','','. Several factors like the war atmosphere in Europe, dissatisfaction of the Indian people and the popularity of the Home Rule Movement made it necessary for the British to seek co-operation from the Indians.\nii. Hence, the British government decided to provide some political rights to Indians and in 1917, Montague, the Secretary of State for India declared that Indians would get the right to self-government and responsible political system in phases.\niii. Tilak upon hearing this declared that if the British government shows sympathy and has a considerable attitude towards the demands of the Indians, only then the Indians would also co-operate with the British.\nThis policy of Tilak is known as \'Responsive Co-operation\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63866,1943,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the Montague-Chelmsford Reform Act and how did the Indians react to it?','','','i. The Montague-Chelmsford Reform Act was passed in 1919 to bring constitutional reforms in India.\nii. This Act gave the less important departments to the Indian ministers and all the important departments like Finance, Home Affairs & Revenue were under the control of the Governor.\niii. The Indians obviously were not pleased with this Act as it did not give much exposure to the demand for responsible government in India. Tilak reacted to this Act by saying that, \"This is neither Swaraj nor its foundation.\"\niv. The Indians realised that in order to subdue the British government to meet their demands, a more rigorous agitation was needed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63867,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Moderate leaders','','','i. During the early ten years, after the establishment of Indian National Congress, the Moderate leaders contributed slowly but consistently to the freedom struggle.\nii. These leaders were realistic and highly educated.\niii. To build a strong foundation, they did organised work.\niv. The values of liberalism, liberty, fraternity and equality greatly impacted them.\nv. They believed in constitutional methods in getting their demands fulfilled.\nvi. Gopal Krishna Gokhale, Pherozeshah Mehta, and Surendranath Banerjee were some important moderate leaders.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63868,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Differences between Moderates and Extremists','','','i. There were ideological differences between the Extremists & the Moderates and this difference increased in the Surat session in 1907.\nii. The resolution of Swadeshi and Boycott was being kept aside by the Moderates.\niii. The extremists tried that this opposition should not be successful.\niv. The Moderate leaders like Gopal Krishna Gokhale, Surendranath Banerjee, Pherozeshah Mehta and others blamed the Extremist leaders that they tried to capture the Indian National Congress.\nv. Lala Lajpat Rai acted as a mediator.\nvi. Tilak considered the Indian National Congress as a national platform and did not want it to be divided.\nvii. However, during the Surat Session, tension increased a lot and since no compromise was possible, there was a split in the Indian National Congress, thus, dividing the Moderates and the Extremists.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63869,1943,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Morley-Minto Reform Act (1909)','','','i. The Indians were not satisfied with the work of the British government.\nii. According to the Indians, India was poor due to the economic policies of the British.\niii. The oppressive policy of Lord Curzon, exclusion of the educated Indians in government services and Britain\'s unjust behaviour towards Indians in Africa created dissatisfaction among the people.\niv. So, to satisfy the Indian people temporarily, the British passed the Morley-Minto Reform Act in 1909.\nv. According to this Act, the number of Indian members in the Legislative Assembly was increased, provision was made for the inclusion of some elected Indian representatives in the Legislative Assembly and separate electorates were provided to the Indian Muslims.\nvi. However, this Act sowed the seeds of division among the people of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63870,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British government imprisoned Tilak with revenge in 1897.','','','i. The epidemic of plague had spread in Pune in 1897 on a large scale. This led to the death of a large number of people.\nii. Officer Rand who was appointed to look into this matter searched the plague patients and adopted oppressive measures against them.\niii. So, the Chapekar brothers assassinated Rand. The British thought that Tilak was involved in this plan.\niv. However, when the government was unsuccessful to connect relation of Lokmanya Tilak with this conspiracy, the British government imprisoned Tilak with revenge.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63871,1943,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Muslim League was established in 1906.','','','The Muslim League was established in 1906 due to the following reasons:\ni. The British were worried after seeing the unity of the people and their great response to the Indian National Congress during the Anti-Partition Movement. Hence, they adopted the \'Divide and Rule\' policy once again.\nii. They spread the idea that the interests of the Muslims should be safeguarded through a separate political organisation.\niii. To achieve this, a committee of upper class Muslims led by Aga Khan met Governor General Lord Minto.\niv. The Governor General and other British officers motivated the Muslims which led to the formation of Muslim League in 1906.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63941,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the wrong pair and correct it :\nWork   →  Leader\n1) President of the Lahore session of Congress - Motilal Nehru\n2) Demonstrated against Simon commission at Lahore - Lala Lajpat Rai\n3) Held satyagraha in South Africa -Mahatma Gandhi\n4) Led the Mulshi - Satyagraha Senapati Bapat\n','','','Wrong pair: President of the Lahore session of Congress - Motilal Nehru.\nCorrected pair: President of the Lahore session of Congress - Jawaharlal Nehru.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63942,1944,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Leaders in other countries who were influenced by Satyagraha technique of Mahatma Gandhi.\ni) ------------- 	ii) ----------------\n','','','i) Martin Luther King in USA		ii) Nelson Mandela in South Africa','',0,0,0,NULL),(63943,1944,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Young Congress leaders who demanded Poorna Swaraj.\ni) -------------  	ii) -----------\n','','','i) Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru		ii) Subash Chandra Bose','',0,0,0,NULL),(63944,1944,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Eminent lawyers who boycotted court by giving up their practice\ni) ---------------- 	ii) -----------------\n','','','i) Pandit Motilal Nehru		ii) Chittaranjan Das.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63945,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Which restriction were put up on the Blacks in South Africa according to the declaration of 1906?','','','In 1906, the government declared that the Blacks in South Africa should compulsorily carry an identity card. This was the restriction imposed on their freedom.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63946,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Where did Gandhiji launch the first Satyagraha in India?','','','The first Satyagraha in India was launched by Gandhiji at Champaran at Bihar in 1917.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63947,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Which officer gave orders of firing in the Jallianwala Baug?','','','General Dyer gave orders of firing in the Jallianwala Baug.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63948,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which principles gave α new direction to the freedom movement?','','','The principles of truth and non-violence gave new direction to the freedom movement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63949,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the constructive programme beneficial? ','','','Due to the constructive programme, the national movement became more comprehensive in rural areas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63950,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Lala Lajpat Rai react after he was attacked by the British officer?','','','Lala Lajpat Rai said that Every blow on my body will prove a nail in the coffin of the British Empire, when he was attacked by the British officer.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63951,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji\'s work in South Africa','','','1) In 1893, Gandhiji went to South Africa for some legal work where he began his political work.\n2) Many Indians had settled in South Africa for the purpose of trade and business.\n3) They were treated as criminals and humiliating treatment was given to them.\n4) Many restrictions were imposed on their freedom like it was compulsory for them to carry an identity card.\n5) Gandhiji adopted the path of Satyagraha and gained justice for them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63952,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Champaran Satyagraha ','','','1) The farmers in Champaran region in Bihar were forced to cultivate indigo.\n2) The cultivators suffered as they received a fixed amount as price from the plantation owners.\n3) In 1917, Gandhiji went to Champaran and organized the farmers.\n4) He launched agitation by adopting the technique of Satyagraha.\n5) The farmers were given justice and thus Gandhiji was successful in his first satyagraha in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63953,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Work of Swaraj Party ','','','1) The leaders of the Swaraj Party put up an idea of contesting elections and obstructing the work of the government.\n2) They severely opposed unjust policies of the government.\n3) They demanded that a Responsible Government should be given to India.\n4) They demanded for Round Table Conference.\n5) They made a resolution in Assembly which demanded the release of political leaders taken as prisoners.\n6) Many resolutions were passed in the Central Legislative Assembly but were rejected by the government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63954,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nehru Report ','','','1) Berkenhead, the Secretary of State of India, criticised that Indian leaders were incapable of drafting a unanimously accepted constitution for India.\n2) This challenge was taken up by all party committee under the chairmanship of Pandit Motilal Nehru.\n3) The proposals of the Nehru Report were: \ni) implement Adult Franchise system in India, \nii) establish self government colonies of India, and (iii) division of provinces on linguistic division.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63955,1944,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Rowlatt Act was opposed by the people of India.','','','1) The Rowlatt Act was enacted to suppress the growing discontent among the Indians and suggest measures about it.\n2) This Act authorised the government to arrest any Indian and imprison him without trial or warrant.\n3) There was no provision for appeal against the punishment given under the Act.\n4) Due to such unjust provisions in the Rowlatt Act it was opposed by the people of India and called \'Black Act\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63956,1944,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Gandhiji suspended the Non-co-operation Movement.','','','1) In February, 1922 the police opened fire on a peaceful procession at Chauri-Chaura in Gorakhpur district of Uttar Pradesh.\n2) In retaliation to this, the enraged mob set fire the police station in Chauri-Chaura.\n3) Twenty-two policemen including one officer were killed in this incident.\n4) Gandhiji was hurt by this incident. So he decided to suspend the Non-co-operation Movement on 12 February, 1922.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63957,1944,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Indians boycotted the Simon Commission.','','','1) The reforms introduced by Montague Chelmsford Act of 1919 was unsatisfactory.\n2) It created discontent among Indians. The British Government appointed the Simon Commission under the chairmanship of Sir John Simon in 1927.\n3) There was not a single Indian member on the commission. In protest of this, the Congress boycotted the Simon Commission.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63958,1944,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Khilafat movement was started in India.','','','1) The ruler of Turkey was regarded as the Khalifa or religious head of the Muslims all over the world.\n2) To get the support of Indian Muslims, in the First World War, the British gave false assurance of not harming the Khalifa\'s empire after the war.\n3) But they did not keep their word. It caused resentment among the Indian Muslims.\nSo, they started the Khilafat Movement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63959,1944,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','No-tax campaign in Kheda district was successful.','','','1) Due to continuous famines the crops had failed in Kheda district of Gujarat.\n2) But the farmers were forced to pay land tax by the government.\n3) Gandhiji suggested to the farmers that they should refuse to pay tax.\n4) The farmers started the campaign to scrap the tax in 1918.\n5) The tax was suspended by the British Government which made the movement successful\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63960,1944,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The movement started by Mill workers at Ahmedabad was successful.','','','1) During the First World War the inflation was very high.\n2) The mill workers demanded for rise in salary which was refused by the mill owners.\n3) Gandhiji advised the workers to go on hunger strike.\n4) In the end, the mill owners had to back off and the salary of workers was increased.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63961,1944,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British government passed the Rowlatt Act in 1919.','','','1) Indians extended help to the British government in the First World War.\n2) The Indians expected that after the war is over there will be a system of governance for taking decision for the well-being of the Indians.\n3) Moreover, the rising prices, increased taxes led to growing unrest among the Indians.\n4) To suppress this discontent and suggest measures about it Rowlatt Act was passed in 1919.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63966,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain the philosophy of Satyagraha. ','','','1) Satyagraha, a novel technique, means insistence of truth.\n2) The aim of Satyagraha was to make the unjust person aware of truth and justice and also to transform his views.\n3) A Satyagrahi should never use violence and untruth means. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63967,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Why was the Swaraj Party formed?','','','1) The members of the Indian National Congress like Motilal Nehru and Chittaranjan Das put an idea to contest elections and enter the Legislative Assembly and Provincial Legislative Council to obstruct the working of the government and oppose the unjust policies.\n2) Therefore, they formed the Swaraj Party in 1922.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63968,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was the Hunter Commission appointed?','','','1) General Dyer ordered an unrestrained firing on the unarmed people who gathered for a meeting on 13th April 1919, at Jallianwala Baug.\n2) About 400 people were killed and injured. The injured did not receive any medical help.\n3) There was nationwide protest against this act.\n4) Rabindranath Tagore gave up the title of Sir given by the British government.\n5) The Indians demanded an enquiry against this massacre.\nSo the British government appointed Hunter commission.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63970,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did Gandhiji support the Khilafat movement?','','','1) Indian Muslims started α movement to support Khalifa known as the Khilafat Movement.\n2) Gandhiji felt that if Hindus and Muslims unitedly start a national movement then the government can be brought to its senses.\n3) Therefore, Gandhiji supported the Khilafat movement.\n4) The Khilafat committee accepted Gandhiji\'s proposal and extended support to the Non-co-operation movement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63971,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was Simon Commission opposed in India?','','','1) The Indian National Congress boycotted the Simon Commission because there was not a single Indian in it.\n2) In 1928, when the commission arrived in India, people strongly protested against it shouting \'Simon go back\'.\n3) The police lathi charged the demonstrators. Lala Lajpat Rai himself led the demonstrators in Lahore.\n4) He was injured in lathi charge and succumbed to his injuries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63972,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was Gandhiji sent to six years of imprisonment?','','','1) Gandhiji was arrested in March 1922 for writing three anti-national articles in \'Young India\'.\n2) His trial was conducted in a special court set up in Ahmedabad.\n3) The charges were levied against him. He was imprisoned for six years.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63975,1944,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','The objective of Indian National Congress of Dominion status was not acceptable to many young members. Young leaders like Pandit Nehru and Subhash Chandra Bose were demanding complete independence. i.e. poorna swaraj. Due to the influence of this young group, in the Lahore session of Indian National Congress, resolution of attainment of complete independence was passed. Through this resolution Indian national Congress gave up the objective of dominion status. From here onwards, the objective of National movement was complete independence of India. On 31st December 1929, Pandit Nehru unfurled the tri colour flag and a resolution was passed in which 26 January was declared to be celebrated as Independence Day. On 26 January 1930, people all over the country took pledge for carrying out the freedom movement with non violent means for attainment of independence of India. Everywhere in the country, the atmosphere was charged with a new spirit.','Read the passage and answer the questions given below:','1) What was the pledge taken by Indians on 26th January 1930?\n2) Why was the demand of Poorna Swaraj passed in Lahore session?\n3) Why was the Lahore session historic?\n','','1) On 26th January 1930, people all over the country took the pledge for carrying out of freedom movement with non-violent means for attainment of independence of India.\n2) i) The objective of Dominion Status was not acceptable to Pandit Nehru, Subash Chandra Bose and other young leaders.\nii) Due to their influence, the resolution of demanding complete independence i.e. \'Poorna Swaraj\' was passed in the Lahore session.\n3) i) The Indian National Congress gave up the objective of Dominion Status.\nii) A resolution of complete independence was passed and it became the objective of the national movement.\niii) A resolution was passed to observe 26th January as the Independence Day.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63978,1944,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give an account of the Jallianwala Baug Massacre.\nPoints: a) Preceding events	b) Massacre	c) Reactions\n','','','<b>(a) Preceding events</b>\n 1) The British government resorted to suppressive measures as the protest was more intense in Punjab province.\n2) Amritsar became the centre of this movement.\n3) Gandhiji was prohibited from entering the province of Punjab.\n4) General Dyer had banned public meetings in Amritsar.\n5) Dr. Satyapal and Dr. Kitchelu were arrested for their involvement in Amritsar Hartal case.\n<b>(b) Massacre</b>\n1) Despite the ban, a public meeting was held at Jallianwala Baug in Amritsar on 13th April 1919 on the occasion of Baisakhi.\n2) General Dyer deployed troops around the single gate that led to the ground.\n3) Without giving any warning, he ordered unrestrained firing on the unarmed people who had assembled for the meeting.\n4) 1660 rounds were fired. The firing continued until the ammunition was over.\n5) About 400 men and women were killed.\n6) Curfew was announced after the firing which made it impossible for medical help to reach.\n<b>(c) Reactions</b>\n1) Military law was imposed in entire Punjab. Many were imprisoned.\n2) This led to discontent among the Indians.\n3) Rabindranath Tagore gave away the title of \'Sir\' given by the British.\n4) As Indians demanded the enquiry, the British government appointed the Hunter Commission.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63979,1944,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give an account of the Non-co-operation.','','','1) According to Gandhiji, the British rule lasted in India only because of the co-operation extended by the Indians.\n2) If the Indians withdraw this co-operation and adopt complete non co-operation then the British government will collapse.\n3) With this intention, Gandhiji devised and executed Non-co-operation Movement.\n4) The Indian National Congress passed the resolution of Non-co-operation Movement at its Nagpur session in 1920.\n5) The reins of the movement was given in the hands of Gandhiji.\n6) According to this resolution there would be: (i) Boycott of government offices and courts. (ii) Boycott of government schools and colleges. (iii) Boycott of foreign goods and clothes.\n7) Boycott and bonfire of foreign clothes was seen at several places and demonstration in front of shops selling such goods.\n8) Motilal Nehru, Chittaranjan Das, etc. boycotted court by giving up their practice.\n9) Schools and colleges imparting national education were started.\n10) Elections were boycotted.\n11) The movement spread from the planters in Assam to rail workers in Bengal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63982,1944,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Following.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Column ‘A’</th>\n    <th>Column ‘B’</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) Indigo cultivation</td>\n    <td>a) Motilal Nehru</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii) Swaraj Party</td>\n    <td>b) Champaran, Bihar</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii) Workers\' Movement</td>\n    <td>c) Jawaharlal Nehru</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv) Unfurled the tricolor flag in 1929</td>\n    <td>d) Ahmedabad</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','','',' (i - b), (ii - a), (iii - d), (iv - c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(63983,1944,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The path followed by Gandhiji to gain justice for the people in South Africa.','','','Satyagraha.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63984,1944,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The commission appointed to enquire about the Jallianwala Bagh Massacre.','','','Hunter Commission.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63985,1944,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The person who led the demonstration against the Simon Commission at Lahore.','','','Lala Lajpat Rai.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63987,1944,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The police officer who attacked Lalaji with lathi.','','','Saunders.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63989,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the Indian national movement, what is the period from 1920 to 1947 known as?','','','The period from 1920 to 1947 is known as the \'Gandhian Era\' in the Indian  national movement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(63990,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the Indians Settle in South Africa?','','','The Indians settled in South Africa for business, trade and such other work.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63991,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How were the Indians treated in South Africa?','','','In South Africa, the Indians were treated as criminals and were frequently humiliated.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63992,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which new technique was followed by Mahatma Gandhi to gain justice for the common people?','','','The new technique of Satyagraha was followed by Mahatma Gandhi to gain justice for the common people.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63993,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who was regarded as the religious head of the Muslims all over the world?','','','The Sultan of Turkey was regarded as the religious head of the Muslims all over the world.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63994,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did Gandhiji support the Khilafat Movement?','','','Gandhiji supported the Khilafat Movement because he wanted Hindu-Muslim unity and start the national movement which would help in winning over the government.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63995,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the Simon Commission welcomed in India?','','','In 1928, when the Simon Commission arrived in India, people made demonstration and slogan like \'Simon Go Back\' was raised by the people against them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(63996,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What did Lala Lajpat Rai say during the protest meeting after he was attacked by Saunders with lathi?','','','After Lala Lajpat Rai was attacked by Saunders with lathi, in the protest meeting, he said, \"Every blow on my body will prove a nail in the coffin of the British Empire\".','',0,0,0,NULL),(63997,1944,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is \'Poorna Swaraj\'?','','','Poorna Swaraj means \'Complete Independence\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64000,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the logic behind starting the non-co-operation movement?','','','i. The logic behind starting the Non-co-operation movement was that the British government is completely dependent on the co-operation of Indians.\nii. The British government would completely collapse if the Indians adopted the policy of Non-co-operation.\niii. With this intention, Gandhiji urged the people to take part in the Non-co-operation movement and it was launched in 1920 with a resolution in the Nagpur session put forth by Chittaranjan Das.\niv. It was decided that people would boycott government offices, courts, foreign goods, government schools and colleges.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64001,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Enlist the activities undertaken during the Non-co-operation movement.','','','i. Eminent lawyers like Motilal Nehru, Chittaranjan Das, etc. boycotted the courts by giving up their practice.\nii. British run schools & colleges were boycotted and many national schools, colleges & universities were opened which was based on scheme of national education.\niii. The forthcoming elections were boycotted.\niv. There was boycott and bonfire of foreign clothes. This resulted in the decline in the import of foreign clothes.\nv. Prince of Wales was welcomed by hartals and closed shops in 1921.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64002,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which constructive programmes were initiated by Mahatma Gandhi along with the non-co-operation movement?','','','The constructive programmes initiated by Mahatma Gandhi made the national movement more comprehensive in rural areas. These programmes were as follows-\ni. Spread of Swadeshi.\nii. Propagation of Hindu- Muslim unity.\niii. Prohibition of alcohol.\niv. Removal of untouchability.\nv. Popularising the use of khadi.\nvi. Promotion of national education.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64003,1944,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the work of the Swaraj Party.','','','i. The Swaraj Party was established in 1922 and in the following year, many members like Motilal Nehru, Madan Mohan Malviya, Lala Lajpat Rai & N. C. Kelkar were elected in the Central Legislative Assembly & Provincial Legislative council.\nii. Whenever the political movement in India became passive, the Swaraj Party strengthened the fight in the Legislative Assembly.\niii. They severely criticised the unjust policies of the British and demanded a Responsible Government for India in future.\niv. They made resolutions which had to be passed in the Legislative Assembly like releasing political leaders from prison, convening the Round Table Conference, etc. However, the government accepted some resolutions while rejected most of them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64004,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Kheda Satyagraha','','','i. The crops in the Kheda district of Gujarat had failed due to constant famines.\nii. However, the government forcibly collected the land tax from the farmers.\niii. On Gandhiji\'s suggestion, the farmers began a movement for the scraping of tax at Kheda in 1918.\niv. Later, Gandhiji undertook the leadership of this movement and within a short period of time, the British government suspended the tax in the Kheda district.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64005,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Worker\'s Movement at Ahmedabad','','','i. The First World War had led to an increase in prices of goods.\nii. Hence, the mill workers demanded for a hike in salary. But the mill owners did not accept this demand.\niii. Therefore, as per Gandhiji\'s advice, the mill workers went on hunger strike.\niv. Finally, the mill owners surrendered to the demands of the workers and their salary was increased.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64006,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Jallianwala Bagh Incident','','','i. The anti Rowlatt Act movement gained. momentum in several towns of the Punjab and Amritsar became the centre of this movement.\nii. General Dyer had issued orders which prohibited public meetings in the towns of Amritsar.\niii. Gandhiji was not allowed to enter the province of Punjab. Important leaders like Dr. Satyapal and Dr. Kitchlew were arrested for their involvement in the Amritsar Hartal Case.\niv. On 13th April, 1919, at Amritsar in the Jallianwala Bagh, a large but unarmed crowd had gathered to protest against the arrest of their popular leaders.\nv. In order to terrorise the people, General Dyer arrived with the armed force at the Bagh. The Bagh was enclosed from all sides and had only one small gate.\nvi. General Dyer surrounded the Bagh with his army and closed down the only gate. Without giving any prior notification, Dyer ordered his soldiers to open fire on the unarmed crowd. They fired till their ammunition was exhausted.\nvii. In this massacre, large number of men and women were killed. After firing, as curfew was imposed, immediate treatment could not be given to the wounded. Later, military law was enforced in Punjab and many were sent to jail by the government.\nviii. As a protest against this incident, Rabindranath Tagore renounced the title of \'Sir\' awarded to him by the British government.\nix. Later, the \'Hunter Commission\' was appointed by the British government to enquire about the incident.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64007,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mulshi Satyagraha','','','i. The farmers of Mulshi Taluka in Pune district launched a Satyagraha against the government during the Non-co-operation movement.\nii. It was done under the leadership of Senapati Pandurang Mahadev Bapat.\nFor doing so, he was punished by being imprisoned for six years.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64008,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nehru Report','','','i. When the Simon Commission arrived in India in 1928, it was boycotted by the Indians because not a single Indian was included in the Commission.\nii. So, the Secretary of State for India, Lord Birkenhead challenged the Indian leaders to draft a unanimously accepted Constitution for India.\niii. The political leaders accepted this challenge and an All-Party Committee was formed to draft the Constitution. Pandit Motilal Nehru was the chairman of this Committee.\niv. This Committee prepared the draft Constitution which was called as the \'Nehru Report\'.\nv. Proposals like establishing self-government in the colonies of India, implementing Adult Franchise system, dividing the provinces on linguistic basis, etc. were put forth in this report.\nvi. Moreover, by the end of 1929, it was indicated that if the Nehru Report was not accepted by the government then the civil disobedience movement will be started.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64009,1944,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Poorna Swaraj','','','i. Young leaders like Pandit Nehru and Subhash Chandra Bose were not willing to accept the objective of Indian National Congress of Dominion Status. Instead, they demanded \'Complete Independence\' i.e., Poorna Swaraj.\nii. Therefore, in the Lahore session of the Indian National Congress, the resolution of Poorna Swaraj was passed by the Congress.\niii. Through this resolution, the Indian National Congress gave up the objective of dominion status and declared that the objective of the National Movement was complete independence of India.\niv. On 31st December 1929, the tricolour flag was unfurled by Pandit Nehru and a resolution was passed in which 26th January was to be celebrated as Independence Day.\nv. Accordingly, on 26th January, 1930, people all over India, took the pledge of carrying out the national movement in a non - violent way.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64011,1944,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji took up the vow of service to India.','','','i. Gandhiji returned to India from South Africa on 9th January, 1915.\nii. On the suggestion of Gopal Krishna Gokhale, Gandhiji made a tour of the entire nation.\niii. On his tour, he noticed the poverty and misery of the people. This made him upset. Therefore, Gandhiji took up the vow of service to İndia.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64012,1944,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji launched an agitation at Champaram in Bihar. ','','','i. The farmers of the Champaran region in Bihar were forced to cultivate indigo by the British.\nii. They received fixed amount for their crops from the planters. This affected them economically. Therefore, Gandhiji organised the farmers in Champaran and launched an agitation by following the path of Satyagraha.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64083,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statements are True or False. Correct the False statement and rewrite : Parliamentary System of government is considered as a contribution of United States of America.','','','False.\nCorrect: Parliamentary System of government is a contribution of England.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64084,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statements are True or False. Correct the False statement and rewrite : In the Parliamentary System, the Legislature of the Central Government is known as the \'Parliament\'.','','','True.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64085,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statements are True or False. Correct the False statement and rewrite : Members of the opposition party also take part in the discussion of the questions related to public welfare.','','','True.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64086,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statements are True or False. Correct the False statement and rewrite : In the Parliamentary System, the President is directly elected by the people.','','','False.\nCorrect: In the Presidential System, the President is directly elected by the people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64088,1952,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the information in the following table : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Name of the institution</th>\n    <th>Functions</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Legislature</td>\n    <td>--------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Executive</td>\n    <td>----------</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n    <td>3. Judiciary</td>\n    <td>---------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Name of the institution</th>\n    <th>Functions</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Legislature</td>\n    <td>To make laws</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Executive</td>\n    <td>To implement laws, frame policies</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n    <td>3. Judiciary</td>\n    <td>To provide justice</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64090,1952,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Characteristics of Parliamentary System of India ','','','The following are the characteristics of Parliamentary System adopted by India:\n1) The Parliament of India is composed of the President, Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha.\n2) Fixed number of members of the Parliament are directly elected by the people.\n3) All political parties contest elections, which are held at regular intervals.\n4) The party having majority in Lok Sabha or a coalition government comes into power.\n5) The leader of the majority party becomes the Prime Minister and he selects candidates to form the Council of Ministers.\n6) The Executive has to perform the responsibility of its individual minister-ship as well as the responsibility of the Legislature, for all their actions and policies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64091,1952,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Responsibilities of the Executive in India','','','The Executive in India has to fulfill following responsibilities :\n1) Implementing the laws passed by the Parliament.\n2) Framing policies for governing the nation.\n3) Functioning as the members of the Legislature.\n4) Striving to develop the nation through decisions related to the welfare of the people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64092,1952,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Text Book','','India adopted the Parliamentary System of government.','','','1) The Parliamentary System of government. developed primarily in England.\n2) The British ruled over India for about 150 years. During this period, they ruled in accordance with this system.\n3) So, Indians were familiar with this system of government.\n4) The Constituent Assembly also adopted this system after a thorough discussion.\n5) Hence, the framers of the Constitution made a few changes to suit the Indian situation, and adopted the Parliamentary System.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64093,1952,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Text Book','','Discussions and deliberations are important in a Parliamentary System of government.','','','1) In a Parliamentary System of government, decisions are taken after discussions and deliberations.\n2) Members of the opposition party also participate in the discussions.\n3) They may support the government or point out shortcomings in the government policies.\n4) Laws are framed after detailed discussions on questions of public welfare. This does not happen in autocratic rule.\n5) Hence, to protect the welfare and freedom of common people, discussions and deliberations are important.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64094,1952,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','No-confidence motion is an effective tool of keeping a check over the Executive.','','','1) In the Parliamentary System, the Executive is dependent upon the confidence of the Legislature in them.\n2) If the Legislature feels that the Executive does not work in accordance to its wishes, it can pass a no-confidence motion against the Executive.\n3) If the Legislature passes the no-confidence motion with a majority, then the Executive has to resign.\n4) The Constitution has provided this tool to prevent the Executive from misusing its powers.\nHence, no-confidence motion is an effective tool of keeping a check over the Executive.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64095,1952,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Parliament is of great importance in the Parliamentary System of government.','','','1) In the Parliamentary System of government the members of the Legislature are directly elected by the people.\n2) Since the elected members represent the people of the nation in the Parliament, they express and carry the hopes and carry the hopes and wishes of the people.\n3) The Parliament decides the policies for the welfare of the people.\n4) If the Parliament feels that the Executive does not work in accordance to its wishes, it can remove the Executive from power by passing a no-confidence motion against it.\nSince, the Parliament reflects the wishes and opinions of the people of the nation, it is of great importance.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64098,1952,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','What is a Responsible Government?','','','1) The government system in which the Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers is responsible to the Legislature for all their actions and policies, is called a Responsible Government.\n2) In this system, the Council of Ministers has to take the Legislature into confidence during governance.\n3) Every decision taken by any ministry/department is considered as the decision of the government.\n4) The decisions or policies of every ministry is the collective responsibility of the Council of Ministers.\n5) Thus, the principle of collective responsibility adopted by the Council of Ministers makes it α responsible government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64099,1952,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Enumerate the characteristics of the Presidential System of government.','','','The following are the characteristics of the Presidential System of government :\n1) The President is directly elected by the people.\n2) The President has lot of powers including power to implement laws.\n3) The President is not dependent on the support of the Legislature.\n4) The Legislature and the Executive are not dependent on each other, but still they have mutual control over each other.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64100,1952,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','What is a Parliamentary System of government?','','','1) The Parliamentary System of government is the one where representatives are directly elected by the people.\n2) They are the members of the Legislature, which is also known as the Parliament.\n3) In this system, elections are held after fixed period.\n4) The party which gets the majority forms the government.\n5) In this system, the Executive is dependent on the confidence of the Legislature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64101,1952,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','In India we have Prime Minister but why is there no Prime Minister in the United States of America?','','','1) In the Parliamentary System of government, the Prime Minister is the head of the Executive, i.e. the Council of Ministers.\n2) In the Presidential System of government, the President is the head of the Executive.\n3) Since India has adopted the Parliamentary system, we have the post of the Prime Minister.\n4) Since United States of America has adopted Presidential System, they have the post of the President.\n5) The post of the Prime Minister does not exist in Presidential System of government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64102,1952,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','What is the difference between a Parliamentary and a Presidential System of government?','','','Difference between the Parliamentary and Presidential System of government is as follows:\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Parliamentary System</th>\n    <th>Presidential System</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) President is the Constitutional Head.</td>\n    <td>President is the Executive Head.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) Prime Minister and Council of Ministers have more powers.</td>\n<td>President has more powers.</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) Council of Ministers is formed by the elected members of Parliament.</td>\n<td>President is directly elected by the people.</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4) Parliament can remove the Prime Minister by passing a no-confidence motion.</td>\n<td>President can be removed only by impeachment.</td>\n</tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>5) Members of the Executive are also the members of the Legislature.</td>\n<td>Legislature and Executive are not dependent on each other.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64103,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the Constitution control the Legislature, Executive and Judiciary?','','','The Constitution controls the Legislature, Executive and Judiciary by deciding the functions, jurisdiction and the limitations on power of these three organs as well as their interrelationship.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64104,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the two main types of government systems that are generally followed.','','','The two main types of government systems that are generally followed are the Parliamentary system and the Presidential system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64105,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the governance carried out in a country that has an unwritten constitution?','','','In a country that has an unwritten constitution, the governance is carried out in accordance with traditions, customs and conventions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64106,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which party forms the government after the elections?','','','After the elections, the party which wins majority, i.e. more than half of the seats of the legislature, forms the government.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64108,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who selects the candidates for the ministerial positions in a parliamentary system of government?','','','In a parliamentary system of government, the Prime Minister selects the candidates for the ministerial positions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64110,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name some countries where neither the Presidential nor the Parliamentary form of government is followed.','','','France, Switzerland and Germany are some countries where neither the Presidential nor the Parliamentary form of government is followed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64111,1952,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why have several countries come up with other kinds of governments apart from Presidential or Parliamentary form?','','','Several countries have come up with other kinds of governments apart from Presidential or Parliamentary form due to their unique circumstances.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64112,1952,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in short about the British Parliament?','','','i. The Parliamentary system of government developed in England.\nii. The governance of the country is carried out in accordance with the traditions, customs and conventions.\niii. The British Parliament has evolved over a period of time.\niv. Thus, it can be said that, the Parliamentary system of government has its roots in the \'Parliament\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64113,1952,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the key features of the Lok Sabha?','','','The key features of the Lok Sabha are as follows:\ni. The members of the Lok Sabha are directly elected by the people.\nii. Lok Sabha has fixed number of people.\niii. Elections to the Lok Sabha are held at regular intervals.\niv. These elections are contested by all the major political parties.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64114,1952,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the role of the executive in a parliamentary system of government?','','','i. In a parliamentary form of government the executive, comprising of the Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers has responsibility. a dual\nii. Laws are implemented by them since they are the executive.\niii. They also have to fulfill responsibilities of the legislature since they are members of the legislature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64115,1952,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the legislature considered a supreme institution in a parliamentary form of government?','','','i. The demands and aspirations of the common people are represented by the elected representatives in the Parliament.\nii. The issues of public welfare are decided by the Parliament.\niii. Also, the legislature is the House of representatives of people and expresses sovereign authority.\nDue to these reasons, the legislature is considered a supreme institution in a parliamentary form of government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64116,1952,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Characteristics of Parliamentary system the Indian','','','The characteristic of the Indian Parliamentary system are as follows:\ni. In this system, the legislature of the Central Government is known as the Parliament. The Indian Parliament comprises of the President, the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha.\nii. Members of the Lok Sabha are elected by the people through elections held at regular intervals. The total number of members in the House is fixed.\niii. All political parties contest elections and the party which gets more than half of the seats of the House is considered as the majority party. This party then forms the government.\niv. In case no party gets a clear majority, two or more than two parties come together to form a majority and establish a government. Such a government is known as coalition government.\nv. Through this system, the candidates directly elected by the people become the members of the legislature and the party who wins a majority gets a chance to form the government. \nvi. Also, the leader of the majority party becomes the Prime Minister and selects the candidates for ministerial positions from his elected colleagues.\nvii. The Prime Minister and the council of ministers selected by him forms the Executive in the Parliamentary system of government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64117,1952,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Presidential system of government','','','i. Presidential system of government is another important system of government which is different from the Parliamentary system.\nii. The President who is the executive of this system is independent of the legislature.\niii. He is the Head of the State and is directly elected by the people.\niv. The three organs i.e. the Legislature, the Executive and the Judiciary are essentially independent of each other, but there are enough connections between them for harmonious and coordinated work.\nv. The Presidential system of government exists in countries such as the United States of America.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64150,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find and write : Members of Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha are known as','','','Members of Parliament (MPs) ','',0,0,0,NULL),(64151,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find and write :  The responsibility of making laws is with the','','','Parliament 	','',0,0,0,NULL),(64152,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : represent the Lok Sabha','','','The Speaker	','',0,0,0,NULL),(64153,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : The bill passed by the State legislature becomes a law after it is signed by the','','','Governor','',0,0,0,NULL),(64154,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : The House that gives representation to 29 States and 7 Union Territories is','','','Rajya Sabha.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64155,1953,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Parliament','','','1) The Legislature at the national level is called the Parliament.\n2) It consists of the President and two houses, i.e., the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha.\n3) Though the President is an inseparable part of the Parliament, he/she cannot participate in the discussions in either of the Houses of the Parliament.\n4) The Constitution of India has created the Parliament.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64156,1953,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Amendment to the Constitution ','','','1) According to the changing times, certain necessary changes have to be made in some laws. The process of making such changes is called Amendment.\n2) Only the Parliament has the right to make such amendments to the Constitution.\n3) The Constitution amendment bill is considered to be an important bill.\n4) The Parliament discusses why the amendment is required and decides whether to accept it or not.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64157,1953,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Members of Parliament','','','1) The members of Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha are known as Members of Parliament (MPs).\n2) The members of Lok Sabha are directly elected by the people.\n3) The members of Rajya Sabha are elected from the constituent States and Union Territories.\n4) MPs try to resolve the queries and complaints of their constituencies by raising questions in the Parliament.\n5) MPs also participate in the process of formulation of laws.\n6) The Government allocates funds to MPs to carry out development activities in their constituencies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64158,1953,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bill of Law','','','1) At the initial stage of law-making process, a rough draft of the law is prepared.\n2) This rough draft or outline is known as \'draft proposal of the law\' or \'bill of law\'.\n3) The Bill clearly mentions the objectives and the details of the proposed law.\n4) Every bill has to undergo three readings in both the Houses, and then it is put to vote.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64159,1953,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Chairman of Rajya Sabha ','','','1) The Vice-President of India is the ex-officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha.\n2) He exercises a control over the functioning of Rajya Sabha.\n3) Maintaining discipline in the House is the function of the Chairman of Rajya Sabha.\n4) He also has the responsibility of facilitating discussions in the House by giving members a chance to speak, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64160,1953,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Speaker of Lok Sabha ','','','1) The speaker is elected in the very first meeting after the election of Lok Sabha.\n2) The members of Lok Sabha elect one member among them as the Speaker.\n3) Lok Sabha functions under the guidance and control of the Speaker.\n4) It is the responsibility of the speaker to conduct the business of the House in an unbiased manner.\n5) The Speaker has to maintain decorum and dignity of the House.\n6) Moreover, he has to interpret the rules and work accordingly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64161,1953,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Control over Council of Ministers ','','','1) Council of Ministers are drawn from the Parliament and hence Parliament exercises control over them.\n2) The members of Parliament exercise this control through multiple ways.\n3) It is the responsibility of the Parliament to see that the Council of Ministers does not disregard the Parliament.\n4) In case the Lok Sabha passes a no-confidence motion against the Council of Ministers, it has to resign.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64162,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','How are members of the Lok Sabha elected?','','','1) As per the Constitution, there can be a maximum of 552 members in Lok Sabha.\n2) General elections to Lok Sabha are held after every five years and members are directly elected by the people.\n3) To ensure equal representation to all sections of the community, some seats are reserved for members belonging to Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes.\n4) In case there are no representation from the Anglo-Indian community, the President can appoint two members from this community to the Lok Sabha.\n5) Thus, Lok Sabha is the representative body of the citizens of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64163,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','',' Explain the functions of the Speaker of Lok Sabha.','','','The functions of the Speaker of Lok Sabha are:\n1) To conduct the business of the House in an unbiased manner.\n2) To take care of the rights and privileges of the members of Lok Sabha.\n3) To interpret the rules of daily functioning of the House and work accordingly.\n4) To maintain the decorum and dignity of the House.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64164,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about Lok Sabha elections.','','','1) Since Lok Sabha is a House of people\'s representatives, elections are held after every five years.\n2) Elections to Lok Sabha which are held every five years are known as General elections.\n3) When Lok Sabha is dissolved before the completion of five years and elections are held, such elections are called mid-term elections.\n4) When a member resigns or dies suddenly, election held for such a seat is called by-election.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64165,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the composition of Rajya Sabha.','','','The composition of Rajya Sabha is as follows:\n1) The total membership of Rajya Sabha is 250 members.\n2) Amongst them, 238 members are elected from the constituent states and Union Territories.\n3) Remaining twelve members, who are distinguished personalities from the fields of literature, arts, science, sports and social work, are appointed by the President.\n4) The members of Rajya Sabha are elected through the system of proportional representation\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64166,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the functions of the Parliament.','','','In order to achieve welfare of the people and the objectives of the Constitution, the Parliament has to perform following functions:\n1) To formulate new laws and to repeal the outdated laws.\n2) To make necessary changes in the existing laws.\n3) To amend the Constitution wherever necessary.\n4) To keep control over Council of Ministers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64167,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention the ways of amending the Constitution.','','','The Constitution can be amended in following ways:\n1) Few provisions in the Indian Constitution can be amended by simple majority.\n2) Some provisions require special (2/3rd) majority of the Parliament.\n3) Few other provisions can be amended by special majority plus consent from more than half of the constituent states.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64168,1953,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Text Book','','Rajya Sabha is a permanent House.','','','1) The tenure of the members of Rajya Sabha does not get over at the same time.\n2) 1/3rd of the total membership of Rajya Sabha retire after every two years and equal number of new members get elected.\n3) Because the limited members of Rajya Sabha retire step by step, it can function continuously.\nSince Rajya Sabha is never dissolved completely it is called a permanent House.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64169,1953,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Text Book','','Lok Sabha is known as the first House.','','','1) Lok Sabha is the lower and the first House of the Parliament of India.\n2) Lok Sabha is the House of Representatives directly elected by the people.\nHence, Lok Sabha is known as the first House.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64170,1953,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Though Lok Sabha is the lower House, it is more powerful than Rajya Sabha.','','','1) Since members of Lok Sabha are directly elected by the people, it represents the people of the nation in the true sense.\n2) Money bills related to financial matters are presented in Lok Sabha. Rajya Sabha has very limited powers with respect to Money Bills.\n3) Since the total membership of Lok Sabha is more than Rajya Sabha, it always gets an upper hand when decisions are taken in the joint meeting of both the Houses.\n4) Only Lok Sabha can pass a no-confidence motion against the Council of Ministers.\nHence, though Lok Sabha is the lower House, it is more powerful than Rajya Sabha.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64171,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the steps involved in the law making process.','','','The Bill undergoes following process in order to be converted into a Law (Act).\n<b>1) First reading</b>\ni) The minister of the concerned department/ministry or member of the Parliament presents the bill in the House.\nii) The objectives and the structure of the Bill are mentioned.\n<b>(2) Second reading</b>\ni) The positive and negative points of the bill are discussed.\nii) The defects and faults in the bill are stressed.\niii) After removing the defects and the faults, the bill is put to vote.\n<b>(3) Committee Stage</b>\ni) Sometimes, if required, the Bill is sent to the committee of the House.\nii) The Bill is discussed in the Committee in details.\niii) The Committee report consisting of instructions and recommendations is sent to the House.\niv) The Bill is discussed clause by clause and then voting is taken.\n<b>(4) Third reading</b>\ni) The Bill is once again discussed in the House.\nii) Voting is taken for approving the Bill.\n<b>(5) Approval of the other House</b>\ni) The Bill has to undergo the same procedure even in the other House.\nii) The Bill is approved by majority.\n<b>(6) Assent by the President</b>\ni) After getting an approval from both the Houses, the Bill is further sent for assent by the President.\nii) After the final assent and signature of the President, the Bill is converted into Law.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64172,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The elections that are held before the completion of the tenure of Lok Sabha.','','','Mid term elections','',0,0,0,NULL),(64173,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The community from which the President may nominate two members in the Lok Sabha','','','Anglo-Indian	','',0,0,0,NULL),(64174,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The age when a citizen of India can vote','','','18','',0,0,0,NULL),(64175,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Number of members appointed by the President in the Rajya Sabha','','','12','',0,0,0,NULL),(64176,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : A Bill passed by the State Legislature can become a law only after the approval of the','','','Governor','',0,0,0,NULL),(64177,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who has created the Parliament of India?','','','The Parliament of India has been created by the Constitution of India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64178,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On what basis do the constituent states of India get their seats in the Lok Sabha?','','','The constituent states of India get their seats in the Lok Sabha on the basis of their population.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64179,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does the Legislature at the level of Central Government consist of?','','','The Legislature at the level of the Central Government consists of the President and the two Houses of the Parliament viz., the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64180,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do the constituent states get representation in the Rajya Sabha? ','','','In Rajya Sabha, the constituent states get their representation proportionate to the total population of each state.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64181,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the age criterion for contesting elections to the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha?','','','In order to contest elections to the Lok Sabha a person must have completed 25 years of age, while for Rajya Sabha he must have completed 30 years of age.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64182,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention a special right that only the Rajya Sabha has but the Lok Sabha does not have?','','','An example of a special right that only the Rajya Sabha enjoys is that it can pass a resolution to direct the Parliament to make a law on a subject in the State list, if the matter is of national importance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64183,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who elects the Speaker and the Deputy Speaker of the Lok Sabha?','','','The Speaker and the Deputy Speaker are elected by the members of the Lok Sabha in its very first meeting.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64184,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the two types of bills that are primarily introduced in the House of the Parliament?','','','The two types of bills which are primarily introduced in the House of Parliament are the Money Bill and the Ordinary Bill.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64185,1953,'descriptive','1b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the future of a Bill decided if there is a difference in the opinion of Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha?','','','If there is a difference in the opinion of Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha regarding a Bill, a joint meeting of both the Houses is held.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64186,1953,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who presents the National Budget every year in the Lok Sabha?','','','The Finance Minister presents the National Budget every year in the month of February in the Lok Sabha.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64187,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is equal representation to all sections of the community ensured in the Lok Sabha?','','','i. Lok Sabha is the representative body of the citizens of the country.\nii. There can be a maximum of 552 members in a Lok Sabha as per the constitution, which should ensure representation of all sections of the community.\niii. In order to achieve this, some seats in the Lok Sabha are reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes.\niv. Also, President can nominate two members from Anglo-Indian community to the Lok Sabha, if there are no representatives from this community.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64188,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the composition of the Rajya Sabha.','','','i. There are in all 250 members in the Rajya Sabha. These members are elected through the system of proportional representation out of the total population of the electorate.\nii. Out of the total members, 238 members are elected from the States and Union Territories of the country.\niii. Remaining 12 members are appointed by the President.\niv. These members are generally distinguished personalities from the field of arts, literature, science, sport and social work.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64189,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the job of the Members of Parliament?','','','i. The members of the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha are together known as the Members of the Parliament (MPs).\nii. They raise questions in the Parliament and try to resolve the queries and complaints of their constituencies.\niii. They are allocated funds by the government, which they use to carry out development activities in their constituencies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64190,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the formulation of laws by the Parliament.','','','i. New laws are formulated by the Parliament to achieve the welfare of the people and the objectives of the Constitution.\nii. While changes are made in certain laws and some outdated laws are repealed.\niii. The process of making laws by the Parliament has been described in the Constitution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64191,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why does the Parliament have control over the Council of Ministers?','','','i. The Parliament has the responsibility to ensure that the Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers functions under the Parliament and does not disregard it.\nii. In order to achieve this, Parliament exercises control over the Executive, who are drawn from the Parliament itself.\niii. Also, there are several ways in which the Parliament exercises control over the Prime Ministers and the Council of Ministers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64192,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Enlist the various ways in which the Constitution can be amended.','','','Some of the ways in which the Constitution can be amended are as follows:\ni. Certain laws can be amended by simple majority of the Parliament.\nii. Some laws to be amended require of the Parliament. 2rd 3 majority\niii. Apart from these two ways certain laws are amended by special majority plus consent by more than half of the constituent states.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64193,1953,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the role of the Chairman of Rajya Sabha','','','i. The Chairman of Rajya Sabha exercises control over its functioning.\nii. The Vice-President of India is the ex-officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha.\niii. The Chairman of Rajya Sabha maintains discipline in the house, facilitates discussions, ensures that the members get a chance to speak, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64194,1953,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Members of the Rajya Sabha work as representatives of the constituent states.','','','i. The Rajya Sabha is known as the Upper or the Second House.\nii. The members of the Rajya Sabha are indirectly elected.\niii. The 28 states and 9 Union Territories of the country are represented in the Rajya Sabha.\nTherefore, the members of the Rajya Sabha work as representatives of the constituent states.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64240,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find and write : The President, the Prime Minister, the Council of Ministers are called the ------','','','Executive','',0,0,0,NULL),(64241,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Find and write : During the Parliamentary session, the period around 12 noon is known as --------','','','Zero hour','',0,0,0,NULL),(64242,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : The group of Parliamentarians and members of the state legislatures who elect the President ---------','','','Electoral college','',0,0,0,NULL),(64243,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : One who has the right to declare emergency in case of crisis -------','','','President','',0,0,0,NULL),(64244,1954,'descriptive','2a',2,0,'Text Book','','Impeachment procedure ','','','1) The responsibility of protecting the Constitution is shouldered by the President.\n2) But, if any act of the President violates the Constitution, the Parliament has the authority to remove him.\n3) This process known as process of Impeachment.\n4) The procedure for impeachment is as follows:\ni) Any one House can lay the charge of violation of the Constitution.\nii) The investigation of the charge is carried out by the other House.\niii) If the resolution is passed by special (2/3rd) majority of both the Houses, the President can be removed from his post.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64245,1954,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Text Book','','No-confidence motion ','','','1) In the Parliamentary system of government, the Legislature tries to keep control over the Executive.\n2) The Executive stays in power till it enjoys the support of the majority in Lok Sabha.\n3) The members of the Lok Sabha can move a No-confidence motion by simply expressing \"We do not have confidence in the government\".\n4) If the motion is passed with majority support, the Council of Ministers (the Executive) has to resign.\n5) Thus, this is the most effective way to keep a check on the Council of Ministers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64246,1954,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Text Book','','Jumbo Ministry ','','','1) A huge Council of Ministers with more than necessary ministers is referred to as Jumbo Ministry.\n2) There was a trend to keep large Council of Ministers in our country.\n3) Later, a constitutional amendment was made to limit the size of the Council of Ministers.\n4) As per this amendment, the number of ministers in the Council should not be more than 15% of the total number of members in the Lok Sabha.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64247,1954,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vice-President ','','','1) The Vice-President is elected by members of both the Houses.\n2) The person contesting the election for the post of Vice-President should be a citizen of India and should have completed 35 years of age.\n3) He is the ex-officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha and exercises control over the functioning of Rajya Sabha.\n4) In the absence of the President, his functions are carried out by the Vice President.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64248,1954,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','President','','','1) The President Constitutional Head of India. is the\n2) He is elected by the directly elected representatives of the Central and State legislatures.\n3) The person elected to the post of the President has to take an oath while accepting the post.\n4) According to the oath, the President bears the responsibility of protecting the Constitution and ensuring that the government runs as per the Constitution.\n5) The President governs in accordance with the advice given by the Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers.\n6) He has Legislative, Executive, Judicial, Defence and Emergency powers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64249,1954,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The President is the nominal and constitutional Head of India.','','','1) The Constitution has vested all executive powers in the President.\n2) The government carries out its functions in the name of the President.\n3) However, in reality, the Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers run the government.\nHence, the President is the nominal and Constitutional Head of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64250,1954,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Council of Ministers has to take the Parliament into confidence while framing policies.','','','1) The Council of Ministers has to decide specific policies on subject like education, agriculture, industry, health, foreign affairs, etc.\n2) The Ministers of respective departments have to lay their policy plans in the House and discuss them thoroughly.\n3) The policies cannot be implemented without the approval of the Parliament.\n4) Also, the. Council of Ministers can stay in power till it enjoys the support of the Parliament.\n5) Hence, it has to take the Parliament into confidence while framing policies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64251,1954,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Enumerate the functions of the Council of Ministers.','','','The functions of the Council of Ministers are as follows:\n1) The Council of Ministers takes initiative in the process of Law-making by drafting the bills/proposals.\n2) It introduces and discusses the bills/proposals in the House.\n3) It introduces bills on various subjects like education, agriculture, industry, health, foreign relations, etc. in the Parliament, conducts discussions on them and tries to get them approved by the Parliament.\n4) It also takes the responsibility of implementing the policies approved by the Parliament.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64252,1954,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','How does the Parliament keep a check on the Executive?','','','The Parliament keeps a check on the Executive in the following ways:\n1) The bills/proposals presented by the Council of Ministers are discussed in the Parliament.\n2) These discussions and debates help the members to scrutinise the bills/proposals and point out the short comings, and help in creation of healthy laws.\n3) During Parliamentary sessions, the proceedings of the House begins with questions asked by the members of the House. The concerned Ministers are expected to give satisfactory answers to these questions.\n4) During the Parliamentary sessions, the period around 12 noon is called as \'Zero House\'. During this period, any question of public importance can be raised and discussed.\n5) The Parliament can pass a No-confidence motion on the Executive. If the motion is passed with majority support, then it has to resign.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64253,1954,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the President elected?','','','1) The President is not directly elected by the people of India.\n2) The common people do not vote in the election of the President.\n3) He is elected by the Electoral College.\n4) The Electoral College includes all members/elected representatives of Parliament and the members of the State Legislatures.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64254,1954,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the Council of Ministers formed?','','','1) The party which attains majority in the Lok Sabha election, nominates its leader as the Prime Minister.\n2) The Prime Minister is given an oath by the President.\n3) The Prime Minister then, chooses his trustworthy and efficient colleagues from within the party to form the Council of Ministers.\n4) He gives priority to his colleagues considering their administrative experience, governance skills, efficiency and technical expertise.\n5) The President administers oath to all the Council of Ministers.\nIn this way, the Council of Ministers is formed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64255,1954,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the qualifications necessary for contesting the Presidential election.','','','1) The person contesting the Presidential election should be a citizen of India.\n2) He should be 35 years of age.\n3) He should also fulfill other conditions mentioned by the Constitution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64256,1954,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention the functions of the Prime Minister.','','','The functions of the Prime Minister are as follows:\n1) To form the Council of Ministers, selecting trustworthy, experienced and efficient people.\n2) To allocate portfolios and chair all the meetings of the Council of Ministers.\n3) To lead the Council of Ministers, maintain coordination among various departments, facilitate cooperation among departments and supervise to bring about efficiency and efficacy.\n4) To raise the image of the country at international level.\n5) To support the people during disasters.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64257,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following :  The body or group that elects the President.','','','Electoral College	','',0,0,0,NULL),(64258,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The process of removing the President.','','','Impeachment','',0,0,0,NULL),(64259,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : During the parliamentary session when there can be walk outs, protests or slogans can be raised','','','Question Hour','',0,0,0,NULL),(64260,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the three organs of the government.','','','The three organs of the government are-Legislature, Executive and Judiciary.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64261,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who all are part of the Electoral College?','','','Elected representatives of Central and State legislatures are part of the Electoral College.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64262,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What oath does the President take while accepting the Presidential post?','','','In accordance to the oath taken by the President, he has to promise to protect the Constitution and make sure that the government runs according to the Constitution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64263,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When can a President be impeached?','','','The President can be impeached by the Parliament if any act of the President violates the Constitution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64264,1954,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who elects the Vice-President?','','','The Vice- President is elected by members of both the Houses.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64265,1954,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is the President of India elected?','','','i. The President of India is elected indirectly by the people of India.\nii. It means that the common people are not able to vote in the election of the President.\niii. The directly elected representatives that include the members of the Central and State legislatures vote and elect the President.\niv. These members of the parliament and that of the state legislatures are together known as the Electoral College.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64266,1954,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What kind of heads are the President and the Prime Minister and what kind of relationship exists between the both?','','','i. President is a nominal head where as the Prime Minister is the executive head.\nii. The Government is actually run by the Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers, though it is run in the name of the President.\niii. The conduct of the administration is informed to the President regularly by the Prime Minister by meeting him regularly.\niv. The Prime Minister also informs the President about new laws and policies.\nv. The President has a right to seek any information from the Prime Minister.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64267,1954,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Enlist the functions of the Prime Minister.','','','The functions of the Prime Minister are as follows:\ni. He is responsible to form the Council of Ministers and considers the administrative experience, governance skills and technical expertise while selecting the ministers.\nii. Once the Council of Ministers is decided by the Prime Minister, he allocates portfolios or departments to each minister.\niii. The Prime Minister is the leader of the Council of Ministers and presides over all the meetings.\niv. In order to maintain efficiency, the Prime Minister coordinates the working of various departments, facilitates cooperation amongst the departments as well as supervises the working of these departments.\nv. Apart from these functions, the Prime Minister also is responsible for maintaining a good image of the country internationally so that the country receives a favourable opinion. He also ensures development of trust among people and provides strong support to the people who are victims of any disaster.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64268,1954,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The President is the nominal or constitutional head, whereas the Prime Minister is the actual head.','','','i. The President is the Supreme Head of the State. His office is of great respect and represents the Republic of India.\nii. The Constitution has vested all executive powers to the President and all functions of the country are carried out in his name.\niii. But in reality the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers run the government.\nTherefore, the President is the nominal or constitutional head, whereas the Prime Minister is the actual head.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64269,1954,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Council of Ministers have to resign if the No Confidence motion is passed with majority support.','','','i. The Government stays in power till it enjoys the support of majority in the Lok Sabha.\nii. There can be a loss of majority if the members of the Parliament withdraw their support.\niii. In such a case the government cannot remain in power.\niv. A no-confidence motion in the House can be passed by the members simply saying \'we do not have confidence in the government\'.\nv. Thereafter, the Council of Ministers have to resign if the No Confidence motion is passed with majority support.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64302,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Why does the duration of day and night keep changing?','','','The duration of day and night keeps changing as the earth is tilted at an angle of 23.5 degrees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64303,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','How many longitudes can be drawn on a world map keeping an interval of 1º each?','','','360 longitudes can be drawn on a world map keeping an interval of 1º each.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64304,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','The apparent movement of the sun from east to west is a result of what?','','','The apparent movement of the sun from east to west is a result of the rotation of the earth from west to east.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64305,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','What is the direction of the rotation of the earth?','','','The direction of the rotation of the earth is from west to east.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64306,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','While the earth rotates, how many longitudes face the sun daily?','','','While the earth rotates, 360 longitudes face the sun daily.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64307,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','At which longitude does the date change?','','','The date changes at 180° longitude.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64308,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','How was the time measured in olden days?','','','In olden days, the time was measured with the help of the natural events of sunrise and sunset and the instruments like Ghatikapaatra, sand timer, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64309,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','In present times, what are the instruments used for time measurement? ','','','In present times, the instruments like watches, calendars, etc. are used for time measurement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64310,1988,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mumbai is located at 73° E longitude.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64311,1988,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In India, three standard times exist.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: In India, standard time exists. one\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64312,1988,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the difference between two longitudes is of 20 degrees, the difference in their local times will be of four hours.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: If the difference between two longitudes is of 20 degrees, the difference in their local times will be of 1 hour and 20 minutes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64313,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','What is the maximum number of local times that can there be in the world?','','','The maximum number of local times that can be there in the world is 360.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64314,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','How many longitudes pass the sun in one hour?','','','15 longitudes pass the sun in one hour.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64315,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How much time does the earth take to complete one rotation?','','','The earth takes nearly 24 hours to complete one rotation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64316,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by the local time of a place?','','','When the sun is directly overhead at a particular place, it is considered as noon at that place and that time is considered as a local time of that place.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64317,1988,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table :\nIf it is 10 pm on 21st June at Prime Meridian, write the dates and time at A, B and C in the following table :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Place</th>\n    <th>Longitude</th>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>A</td>\n    <td>120° E</td>\n<td>--------</td>\n<td>--------</td>\n  </tr>\n    <tr>\n    <td>B</td>\n    <td>160° W</td>\n<td>---------</td>\n<td>----------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>C</td>\n    <td>60° E</td>\n<td>---------</td>\n<td>---------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Place</th>\n    <th>Longitude</th>\n<th>Date</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>A</td>\n    <td>120° E</td>\n<td>22nd June</td>\n<td>6 a.m.</td>\n  </tr>\n    <tr>\n    <td>B</td>\n    <td>160° W</td>\n<td>21st June</td>\n<td>11.20 a.m.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>C</td>\n    <td>60° E</td>\n<td>22nd June</td>\n<td>2 a.m.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64318,1988,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Mashad, a town in Iran, is located on the 60° E longitude. When it is 12 noon at Greenwich, calculate the local time of Mashad town.','','','1) Statement : As we move towards the east of the Prime Meridian, the local time increases by four minutes for every longitude. (Mashad is located to the east of Greenwich.)\n2) The difference between the longitudes of Greenwich and Mashad = 60°.\n3) Difference in local time = 60 × 4 = 240 minutes.\n                                  = 240 minutes 60 minutes = 4 hours.\n4) Therefore, when it is noon at Greenwich, it would be 4 p.m. at Mashad.\n(Ahead of Greenwich Mean Time by 4 hours.)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64319,1988,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Manaus city in Brazil is located on 60° W longitude. What would be the local time at Manaus when it is 12 noon at Greenwich?','','','1) Statement : As we move towards the west of the Prime Meridian, the local time decrease by four minutes for every longitude. (Manaus is located to the west of Greenwich.)\n2) The difference between the longitudes of Greenwich and Manaus = 60°.\n3) Difference in local time = 60 x 4 = 240 minutes.\n                    = 240 minutes ÷ 60 minutes = 4 hours.\n4) Therefore, when it is noon at Greenwich, it would be 8 a.m. at Manaus.\n(Behind Greenwich Mean Time by 4 hours.)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64320,1988,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','If it is 12 noon at 60° E longitude, then explain what would be the time at 30° W longitude.','','','1) The difference between 60° E longitude and 30° W longitude will be of 90 degrees. \n        (The difference of 60 degrees between 0° and 60° E + the difference of 30 degrees between 0° and 30° W = 90 degrees.)\n2) Difference in local time = 90 × 4 = 360 minutes.\n                = 360 minutes + 60 minutes = 6 hours.\n3) Longitudes lying to the east of any longitude are ahead of the time of that longitude while those lying to the west are behind.\n4) Therefore, if it is 12 noon at 60° E longitude, then it would be 6 a.m. at 30° W longitude. \n         (behind by 6 hours)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64321,1988,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Mumbai is located at 73° E longitude. Kolkata is located at 88° E longitude. Find the difference between the longitudes of these two cities.','','','Mumbai is located at 73° E longitude. Kolkata is located at 88° E longitude. Therefore, the difference between the longitudes of these two cities would be of (88 - 73 = 15) 15°.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64322,1988,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','If the local time at Mumbai is 3 p.m. then what would be the local time at Kolkata?','','','1) Statement: For any longitude lying to the east of particular longitude, the local time increases by 4 minutes for every longitude.\n2) The difference between the longitudes of Mumbai and Kolkata = 15°.\n3) Difference in local time = 15 x 4 = 60 minutes.\n                  = 60 minutes + 60 minutes = 1 hour.\n4) Kolkata is located to the east of Mumbai. Therefore, if it is 3 p.m. at Mumbai, then it would be 4 p.m. at Kolkata. (ahead by 1 hour)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64323,1988,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','A football match being played at Sao Paulo, Brazil started in India at 6 a.m. IST. Explain what would be the local time at Sao Paulo?','','','1) Statement: For any longitude lying to the west of particular longitude, the local time decreases by 4 minutes for every longitude. (Sao Paulo is located to the west of India)\n2) The difference between the longitudes of Sao Paulo and India = 127°30′.\n3) Difference in local time = 127.5 x 4\n           = 510 minutes. = 510 minutes 60 minutes = 8 hours 30 minutes.\n4) Thus, if it is 6 a.m. at India, it would be 9.30 p.m. of the previous day at Sao Paulo. Therefore, if a football match being played at Sao Paulo, Brazil started in India at 6 a.m. IST, the local time at Sao Paulo would be 9.30 p.m. of previous day.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64324,1988,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','How is the standard time of a place determined?','','','1) The local time at the longitude passing through the middle of a country/place is generally considered as a standard time of that country/place. \n2) If the difference between the local time at the extreme east and west longitude passing through a country is less than one or two hours, one standard time is considered for a country. Thus, there exists only one standard time in a country having comparatively less longitudinal (east-west) extent.\n3) If the difference between the local time at the extreme east and west longitude passing through a country is more than one or two hours, more than one standard time zones are considered for a country. Thus, there exists more than one standard time zones in a country having comparatively more longitudinal (east-west) extent.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64325,1988,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give brief information about the Indian Standard Time.','','','1) The Indian Standard Time (IST) is calculated on the basis of 82°30′ E longitude, passing through Mirzapur, near Allahabad in Uttar Pradesh.\n2) With respect to longitudinal extent, 82°30′ E longitude passes through the middle of India.\n3) When the sun is directly overhead on this longitude, it is considered that it is 12 noon at every place in India. Thus, the local time at 82°30′ E longitude is considered as the Standard time of India.\n4) The difference between the local time at 82°30′ E longitude and the local time at the extreme east and west longitude passing through India is not more than one hour.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64326,1988,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give brief information about Universal Standard Time.','','','1) As the standard time is essential for synchronizing routine activities in a country with comparatively more longitudinal extent, the universal standard time is essential for an international coordinating between the countries in the world.\n2) For this purpose, the local time at Greenwich (Greenwich Mean Time) in England is considered to be the International/Universal Standard Time (UST).\n3) With reference to GMT, the differences in standard times of various countries in the world are calculated.\n4) The Indian Standard Time is ahead of GMT by 5 hours and 30 minutes. For example, if it is 12 noon at Greenwich, then it would be 5.30 p.m. in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64327,1988,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The local time is decided by the noon time.','','','1) When at a particular place, the sun reaches the maximum height in the sky it is assumed that almost half of the daytime is over and this time is considered 12 noon.\n2) The time of a particular place as decided by the overhead position of the sun in the sky is considered as the local time of that place.\n3) During the rotation of the earth, when a particular longitude comes exactly in front of the sun, it is considered as noon time (12 noon) on that longitude. This noon time is considered as local time of that longitude. In this way, the local time is decided by the noon time.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64328,1988,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The local time at Greenwich is considered to be the International Standard Time.','','','1) The International Standard Time has been decided according to 0° longitude.\n2) 0°longitude passes through Greenwich, England.\n3) For an international coordination, it is essential to bring compatibility between the standard times of various countries in the world. For this purpose, the local time at Greenwich is considered to be the International Standard Time.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64329,1988,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The standard time of India has been decided by the local time at 82°30′ E longitude.','','','1) With respect to the longitudinal extent, 82°30′ E longitude passes through the middle of India.\n2) The difference between the local time at 82°30′ E longitude and the local time at the extreme east and west longitude passing through India is not more than one hour. Therefore, the standard time of India has been decided by the local time at 82°30′ E longitude.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64330,1988,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Canada has 6 different standard times.','','','1) The longitudinal extent of Canada is between 52°37′ W and 141° W.\n2) Thus, the difference between the extreme east and west longitude passing through Canada is of 88 degrees.\n3) The difference between the local time at extreme east and west longitude passing through Canada is of 352 minutes i.e. 5 hours and 52 minutes. Therefore, it is not practically helpful to consider single standard time in Canada. Therefore, for synchronizing the routine activities in the country, Canada has 6 different standard times.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64331,1988,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the countries with larger area, it is convenient to use one standard time instead of more than one local times.','','','1) In the countries with larger area, much difference between the local times at extreme east and west longitude passing through the countries is found.\n2) In such countries, if more than one local times are followed, it could lead to lots of confusion and chaos.\n3) For example, if more than one local times are followed, it will become difficult to synchronize the timetable of railways, airways, etc. Therefore, in countries with comparatively larger area, it is convenient to use one standard time instead of more than one local times.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64332,1988,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the figure and answer the following questions:','1) Between which longitudes does the region experience daytime ?\n2) Which longitudes experience noon and the midnight respectively?\n3) Edward from New Orleans is on which longitude?\n4) What is the time at Accra city?\n5) At the same time, what is Sharad from Patna and Yakaito from Japan doing? What time is it in these cities?\n6) Select any one longitude. Calculate the local time of the longitudes lying 1° to the west and east of this longitude.\n','','1) The region shown between 90° W and 90° E in the figure experiences daytime.\n2) 0° longitude and 180° longitude experience noon and the midnight respectively.\n3) Edward from New Orleans is on 90° W longitude.\n4) It is 12 noon at Accra city.\n5) At the same time, Sharad is returning home from school and Yakaito from Japan is preparing for the night\'s sleep. At the same time, it is 5.30 p.m. at Patna and 9.20 p.m. at Japan.\n6) i) The selected longitude: 60° Ε. The local time of the selected longitude is 4 p.m. \nii) The local time of the longitude lying 1 deg to the west of the selected longitude (59⁰E) will be 3.56 p.m. \niii) The local time of the longitude lying 1 deg to the east of the selected longitude (61⁰ E) will be 4.04 p.m.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(64333,1988,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the figure and answer the following questions : ','1) Considering the longitudinal extent of India, how many longitudes with a difference of 1 deg can be drawn on a map?\n2) By how many minutes do two consecutive longitudes differ?\n3) What is the value of degrees of longitude at Mirzapur?\n4) If it is 8 a.m. at 82°30′ E, what would be the time in their clocks at the following places: Jammu, Madurai, Jaisalmer, Guwahati.\n5) Though the distance between them is more why doesn\'t the standard time differ in these places?\n','','1) Considering the longitudinal extent of India, 29 longitudes with a difference of 1 deg can be drawn on a map.\n2) Two consecutive longitudes differ by 4 minutes.\n3) The value of longitude at Mirzapur is 82⁰ 30\'\n4) If it is 8 a.m. at 82°30′ E, it would be 8 a.m. in their clocks at Jammu, Madurai, Jaisalmer, Guwahati.\n5) In India, the time at 82°30′ E is considered as standard time of India and therefore the same time will be considered everywhere in India. Therefore, though the distance between them is more, the standard time doesn\'t differ in these places.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(64334,1988,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Write the situations of place A shown in the following diagrams in the boxes below them:\ni) Sunrise	         ii) Midnight     	iii) Noon       	iv) Sunset.\n','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(64335,1988,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','At the poles, sunrise occurs on one equinox and sunsets on the next equinox.\nIf you happen to be at any of the poles during this time, then what would be the route of the sun in the daytime?\n','','','At the poles, sun rises on one equinox and sets on the next equinox. If we happen to be at any of the poles during this time, then the following would be the route of the sun in the daytime:\n<b>(A) North Pole</b> \ni)The sun will rise at the North Pole approximately on March 21.\nii) Approximately, from March 21 to June 21, the sun will rise higher in the sky with each advancing day. It will reach the maximum height approximately on June 21. \niii) Approximately, from June 21 to September 21, the sun will start sinking towards the horizon in the sky with each advancing day. (4) The sun will set at the North Pole approximately on September 21.\n<b>(B) South Pole</b>\ni)  The sun will rise at the South Pole approximately on September 21. \nii) Approximately, from September 21 to December 21, the sun will rise higher in the sky with each advancing day. It will reach the maximum height approximately on December 211.\niii) Approximately, from December 21 to March 21, the sun will start sinking towards the horizon in the sky with each advancing day. \niv) The sun will set at the South Pole approximately on March 21.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64336,1988,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','On which day, would the sun appear at the highest point in the sky?','','','1) On the North Pole, approximately on June 21, the sun would appear at the highest point in the sky. \n2) On the South Pole, approximately on December 21, the sun would appear at the highest point in the sky.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64337,1988,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','1) If it is 8 a.m. in India, what is the time in Greenwich?\n2) When it is 2 p.m. in India, in which countries would it be 2 p.m. exactly?\n3) When it is 9 a.m. in India, what would be the time at 82°30′ W longitude?\n4) What would be the time at Prime Meridian when a new day starts at 180° longitude?\n','','','1) If it is 8 a.m. in India, it would be 2.30 a.m. in Greenwich.\n2) When it is 2 p.m. in India, it would be 2 p.m. exactly in Sri Lanka.\n3) When it is 9 a.m. in India, it would be 10 p.m. of the previous day at 82°30′ W longitude.\n4) It would be 12 noon at Prime Meridian when a new day starts at 180° longitude.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64338,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','In which of the following countries, does only one standard time exist?\n(Mexico, Sri Lanka, New Zealand, China)\n','','','From the given countries, only one time exists in Sri Lanka, New Zealand and China.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64339,1988,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Why does a country having a large latitudinal extent have only one standard time?','','','The places lying on the same longitude have the same local time. Therefore, a country having a large latitudinal extent have only one standard time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64340,1989,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The density of various materials is not the same in the interior of the earth.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64341,1989,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The core of the earth\'s interior is made up of hard rocks.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: The core of the earth\'s interior is made up of elements, viz. iron and nickel.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64342,1989,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Secondary waves cannot pass through outer core. ','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64343,1989,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Continental crust is made up of silica and magnesium.','','','Incorrect\nCorrect statement: Continental crust is made up of silica and aluminium.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64344,1989,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','As we move deeper the surface, temperature keeps decreasing.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: As we move deeper the surface, temperature keeps increasing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64345,1989,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Magma chambers are found in lower mantle.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: Magma chambers are found in upper mantle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64346,1989,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The discontinuity is found between the crust, mantle and core.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(64347,1989,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called lower layer of the crust?','','','The lower layer of the crust is called Sima.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64348,1989,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Out of which layer of the crust are the continents formed?','','','The continents are formed out of Sial.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64349,1989,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Out of which layer of the crust is most of the oceanic base formed?','','','Most of the oceanic base is formed out of Sima.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64350,1989,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What compounds does the mantle consists of?','','','The mantle consists of the compounds of iron and magnesium.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64351,1989,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which of the layers of the interior of the earth is magma formed?','','','Magma is formed in the mantle (upper mantle) of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64352,1989,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the two parts of the core?','','','Outer core and inner core are the two parts of the core.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64353,1989,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns and complete the chain :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th> \'A\' Column <br> (The layer of the interior of the earth) </th>\n    <th> \'B\' Column <br>(Density) </th>\n<th> \'C\' Column <br>(Elements found) </th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Sial</td>\n    <td>i) 3.3 gm/cm³ </td>\n<td>a) Silica and Magnesium </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) Sima</td>\n    <td>ii) 2.65 to 2.90 gm/cm³ </td>\n<td>b) Nickel and Iron </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) Inner Core</td>\n    <td>iii) 2.90 to 3.3 gm/cm³ </td>\n<td>c) Silica and Aluminium </td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','','','1) Sial – 2.65 to 2.90 gm/cm³ - Silica and Aluminium \n2) Sima – 2.90 to 3.3 gm/cm³ - Silica and Magnesium\n3) Inner Core – 13.3 gm/cm³ - Nickel and iron \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64354,1989,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the changes that take place in the temperatures from the surface of the earth to its centre.','','','1) The process of cooling of the earth started from the surface. The temperature goes on increasing from the surface towards the core.\n2) The surface of the earth is cool and solid.\n3) The centre of the earth is extremely hot. The temperature at the centre of the earth is around 5500° to 6000° C.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64355,1989,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the changes that take place in the density of the earth from its surface to the centre.','','','1) The density of the earth goes on increasing from its surface to the centre.\n2) The average density of the crust of the earth is approximately 2.98 gm/cm³.\n3) The average density of the mantle of the earth is approximately 5.7gm/cm³.\n4) The average density of the outer core of the earth is approximately 9.8 gm/cm³.\n5) The density of the inner core at the centre of the earth is approximately 13 gm/cm³.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64356,1989,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about the travelling of various types of earthquake waves through the layers of interior of the earth.','','','1) The primary earthquake waves travel through the mantle as well as the core of the earth.\n2) While travelling through the core of the earth, the direction of the primary earthquake waves gets altered.\n3) While travelling through the core, the speed of the primary earthquake waves decreases.\n4) The secondary earthquake waves can travel only through the mantle of the earth. These waves get absorbed in the core of the earth. Thus, these waves cannot travel through the core of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64357,1989,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the two parts of the crust? What is the basis of classification?','','','1) Continental crust and the Oceanic crust are the two parts of the crust.\n2) The crust is classified on the basis of whether it lies below the land or the ocean.\n3) The part of the crust lying below the continent is known as the continental crust.\n4) The part of the crust lying below the ocean is known as the oceanic crust.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64358,1989,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the upper layer of the mantle known as the asthenosphere?','','','1) The upper layer of the mantle is in liquid state.\n2) The rocks in the upper 100 to 200 km of the mantle melt due to the heat and magma is created. Magma chambers are found in this layer of the earth.\n3) Due to the heat and pressure, the energy waves are created in mantle. These energy waves move in vertical direction and magma comes out on the surface of the earth by volcanic eruptions.\n4) These endogenitic movements continuously occur in the upper layer of the mantle. Therefore, the upper layer of the mantle is known as the asthenosphere.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64359,1989,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain how the magnetosphere is formed due to the rotation of the earth.','','','1) The average temperature of the outer core of the earth is around 5000° С. The average temperature of the inner core of the earth is around 6000° C. This difference between the temperature results in formation of vertical currents.\n2) The rotation of the earth gives eddy (circular) motion to these currents.\n3) Electric currents develop in these spiral eddies of liquid iron leading to generation of the magnetic field of the earth. This magnetic field is called magnetosphere. In this way, the magnetosphere is formed due to the rotation of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64360,1989,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The crust','','','1) The uppermost layer of the interior of the earth is called the crust. Continental crust and the oceanic crust are the two parts of the crust.\n2) The thickness of the continental crust is approximately 16 km to 45 km. On the other hand, the thickness of the oceanic crust is approximately 7 km to 10 km.\n3) The density of the continental crust is approximately 2.65 to 2.90 gm/cm³. On the other hand, the density of the oceanic crust is approximately 2.65 to 2.90 gm/cm³.\n4) Silica and aluminium are the main elements found in the continental crust. Therefore, it is also called Sial. On the other hand, Silica and Magnesium are the main elements found in the oceanic crust, Therefore, it is also called Sima.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64361,1989,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The mantle','','','1) The mantle is located below the crust of the earth. The mantle is divided into the upper mantle and the lower mantle.\n2) The thickness of the mantle is approximately 2870 km. The average density of mantle is approximately 4.5 gm/cm³.\n3) The rocks in the upper 100 to 200 km of the mantle melt due to the heat and magma is created. Magma chambers are found in this layer of the earth. Due to the heat and pressure, the energy waves are created in mantle. These energy waves move in vertical direction and magma comes out on the surface of the earth by volcanic eruptions.\n4) These endogenitic movements continuously occur in the upper layer of the mantle. Therefore, the upper layer of the mantle is known as the asthenosphere.\n5) The average thickness of mantle is approximately 2870 km. Its average density is approximately 4.5 gm/cm³. The temperature at the depth of 2400 to 2900 km is around 2200° to 2500° C. The density of the lower mantle is approximately 5.7 gm/cm³.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64362,1989,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The outer core','','','1) The outer core is located below the lower mantle.\n2) It is located at the depth from 2900 km to 5100 km from the surface of the earth.\n3) The average temperature of the outer core of the earth is around 5000° C. The density of the outer core of the earth is approximately 9.8 gm/cm³.\n4) The secondary earthquake waves cannot travel from the core of the earth. Therefore, it is inferred that the outer core of the earth must be in liquid or semi-liquid form. The primary earthquake waves can travel from the outer core of the earth.\n5) The vertical currents originate in the outer core of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64363,1989,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','The inner core','','','1) The inner core is located below the upper core.\n2) It is located at the depth from 5150 km to 6371 km from the surface of the earth.\n3) The average temperature of the inner core of the earth is around 6000° C. The density of the inner core of the earth is approximately 13.3 gm/cm³.\n4) The elements, viz. Iron (Ferrous) and Nickel (Ni) are mainly found in the inner core of the earth. Therefore, it is also called Nife.\n5) The temperature at the centre of the earth is almost equivalent to the surface temperature of the sun.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64364,1989,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','There are discontinuities in the interior of the earth.','','','1) There is a difference between the elements, temperatures and the pressure found in the three parts of the interior of the earth, viz. crust, mantle and core.\n2) In the interiors of the earth, the transitional areas are found between \na) continental crust and oceanic crust \nb) crust and mantle \nc) upper mantle and lower mantle \nd) mantle and core and \ne) outer core and inner core. \nTherefore, there are discontinuities in the interior of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64365,1989,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','There is correlation between the density of metals and their location in the interior of the earth.','','','1) The rocks found in the crust have predominance of the compounds of silicon, aluminium, magnesium, etc. These compounds are lighter in weight. Therefore, they are found in the upper layer of the interior of the earth.\n2) The elements like iron, nickel are found in the core of the earth. These elements are heavier in weight. Thus, metals with low density are found in upper level in the interior of the earth and the metals with high density are found in the lower level in the interior of the earth. Thus, there is correlation between the density of metals and their location in the interior of the earth. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64366,1989,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Mantle is the centre of earthquake and volcanic eruptions.','','','1) The rocks in the upper 100 to 200 km of the mantle melt due to the heat and the magma is created.\n2) Magma chambers are found in this layer of the earth.\n3) Due to the heat and pressure, the energy waves are created in mantle. These energy waves move in vertical direction and magma comes out on the surface of the earth by volcanic eruptions. Thus, mantle is the centre of earthquake and volcanic eruptions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64367,1989,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The thickness of the crust below the continents is more as compared to oceans.','','','1) The density of the crust below the continents is 2.65 to 2.90 gm/cm³. The density of the crust below the oceans is 2.90 to 3.3 gm/cm³.\n2) As the density of the crust below the continents is comparatively low, it keeps floating on the mantle. It does not subduct into mantle. As its effect, the thickness of the crust below the continents is found to be high.\n3) As the density of the crust below the ocean is comparatively high, it subducts into mantle leading to its low thickness. Thus, the thickness of the crust below the continents is more as compared to oceans.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64368,1989,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Earth is protected because of the magnetosphere.','','','1) The ozone layer protects the earth from ultraviolet radiation.\n2) The earth\'s magnetosphere serves to deflect most of the solar wind, whose charged particles would otherwise strip away the ozone layer. In this way, the earth is protected because of the magnetosphere.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64369,1989,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The secondary waves of earthquake cannot travel through the core of the earth.','','','1) The secondary waves of earthquake can travel only through the solid state of the interior of the earth.\n2) The outer core of the centre of the earth is in liquid state. Therefore, the secondary waves of earthquake cannot travel through the core of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64370,1989,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The inner core is called Nife.','','','1) The inner core predominantly comprises iron and nickel.\n2) The word Nife is derived from the word Nickel (Ni) and Iron (Ferrous) (Fe). Therefore, the inner core is called (Ni + Fe) Nife.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64371,1989,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The upper layer of the crust is called Sial.','','','1) The rocks in the upper layer of the crust have predominance of the compounds of silicon and aluminium.\n2) The word Sial is derived from the word Silica (Si) and Aluminium (Al). Therefore, the upper layer of the crust is called (Si + Al) Sial.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64372,1989,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The lower layer of the crust is called Sima.','','','1) The rocks in the lower layer of the crust have predominance of the compounds of silicon and magnesium.\n2) The word Sima is derived from the word Silica (Si) and Magnesium. (Ma). Therefore, the lower layer of the crust is called (Si + Ma) Sima.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64373,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to your school Principal, requesting leave from school to attend a family function for a few days.','','','From,\nShruti Singh\n15-Ramesh Nagar, \nBandra, \nMumbai 400050.\n25 October, 2020\n\nTo,\nThe Principal, \nSacred Heart High School, \nBandra, \nMumbai 400050.\nSubject: Request for leave of four days.\nRespected Madam,\nI, Shruti Singh, a student of Standard V (Section A) would like to request you for a four-day leave starting from 1 November 2020 till 4 November, 2020.\nMy cousin is getting married at our native place and being her only sister, I would like to attend this function along with my family. I will ensure that all the school work that I miss is completed, before I return for my classes on 5 November, 2020.\nI would, therefore, be highly obliged if you grant me this leave of absence.\nThanking you in anticipation.\nYours obediently,\nShruti Singh\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64374,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to your Principal seeking medical leave.','','','From,\nAnshita Rajput,\nBandra, \nMumbai 400050.\n10 April, 2020\n\nTo,\nThe Principal,\nSacred Heart High School,\nBandra,\nMumbai 400050.\nSubject: Request for leave of ten days due to sickness\nDear Sir,\nI, Anshita Rajput, a student of Standard V (Section B), have developed measles. Since the disease is contagious, the doctor has advised that I take leave from school for about ten days, till I recover completely and do not pose a threat to others around me.\nKindly grant me leave on medical grounds from tomorrow 11 April, 2020 till 20 of April, 2020.\nThanking you,\nYours obediently,\nAnshita Rajput\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64375,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','You found an amazing location for the class picnic. Write a formal letter to your class teacher expressing your suggestion for the class picnic and seeking their guidance on how to plan it.','','','Simrah Kaur \nRoll no: 4, \nStandard: V-A \nVictoria High School \nThane (West) \nMumbai 401202\n18th July, 2023\n\nTo \nThe Class Teacher \nVictoria High School \nThane (West) \nMumbai 401202\nSubject: Suggestion for Class Picnic and Request for Guidance\nI am Simran Kaur, one of your class 5 students. I\'m writing this letter to share an exciting idea with you Recently, I came across a fantastic water park named Blue Horizon Aquapark that I believe would be a perfect destination for our class picnic. It offers a variety of fun water activities that I\'m sure all of us would enjoy. They also offer discounts for schools and colleges.\nI understand that planning a class picnic involves many details, like permissions and coordination. So, I\'m writing to seek your guidance and approval for this idea. Your experience and advice would be very help in making sure our picnic is safe and enjoyable for everyone. I\'m ready to work closely with you and follow all school procedures to make this picnic a success.\nThank you for considering my suggestion. I look forward to discussing the plan further with you. Your supper means a lot to me and our class. I\'m truly excited about the possibility of creating lasting memories together\nYours sincerely, \nSimran Kaur\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64376,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to your class teacher thanking them for being a great teacher and sharing your appreciation for their hard work.','','','Sakshi Dubey \nRoll No: 44, Std. V-A \nVidya English High school \nSV Road \nMulund \nMumbai-400054\n6th August, 2023\n\nTo \nMs Divya Menon \nVidya English High School \nSV Road,\nMulund \nMumbai-400054\nSubject: Thank You for Being an Outstanding English Teacher.\nRespected Ms Menon,\nI want to say a big thank-you for being such an amazing English teacher. Your teaching has really helped me and I want you to know how much I appreciate it.\nYour way of teaching English is awesome. Even tough things are easy to understand when you explain them You\'re patient and kind, which makes me feel comfortable in English class. When I need help with Engi you\'re always there, and I\'m not afraid to ask questions. You stay after school to help your students and mic sure we have what we need. You care a lot about our English learning, and that makes me want to learn more Thanks again for being a great English teacher. I\'m lucky to have you as my English teacher this year. Your English teaching and kindness mean a lot to me and my classmates. I am and will always be excited and grateful to keep learning English from you.\nYours faithfully, \nSakshi Dubey\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64377,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to your class teacher explaining the reason for your fact from end and requesting any missed assignments of notes.','','','Rishi Vedh \nMilan Housing Society \nSV Road \nSantacruz West \nMumbai-400054\n8th August, 2022\n\nTo \nThe Class Teacher \nSt. Teresa\'s School High School \nSV Road \nSantacruz West \nMumbai-400055\nSubject: Request for missed schoolwork due to illness.\nRespected Sir/Madam,\nI, Rishi Vedh, a student of class 5, am writing this letter regarding my absentee from school on and August. I was not feeling well and had a fever, so my parents took me to the doctor. The doctor advised me to rest and stay at home until I felt better. I am sorry for missing school and any inconvenience this may have caused.\nI am writing to request any assignments or notes that I may have missed during my absence. I don\'t want so fall behind in my studies. If you could please provide me with the homework assignments and class nee from the days I missed, I would greatly appreciate it. This will help me catch up on the lessons and stay on track with the class.\nThank you for your understanding, and I promise to make up for the missed work as soon as possible. I value my education, and I don\'t want to miss any important lessons. Your support means a lot to me, and I will ensure that I complete the missed assignments promptly.\nYours sincerely, \nRishi Vedh\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64378,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to the School Principal apologizing for any misbehavior or rule violation in school and promising to do better. ','','','Ayushi Mehra\nRoll No: 4, Std. V-B \nSt. Benedict School \nKarjar Road \nVile Parle West \nMumbai-400056\n19th November, 2022\n\nTo \nThe Principal \nSt. Benedict School \nKarjar Road \nVile Parle West \nMumbai-400056\nSubject: Apology for misbehavior and commitment to improve.\nRespected Sir/Madam,\nI am Ayushi Mehra, a class 5 student. I\'m writing this letter to say sorry for how I behaved in Mr. Sinhal\'s Math class. I was loud, didn\'t listen to the teacher and disturbed my classmates. I\'m really sorry for all of this.\nI know I did something wrong and I understand it\'s not okay. My behavior made it hard for everyone to lea and that\'s not right. I promise to follow all the school rules, especially during class. I\'ll be more polite to my teachers, classmates and all the school staff. I\'ll try my best to be a good student and help make the school a nice place for everyone.\nI\'m really sorry again for not being disciplined in class and I hope you can forgive me. I really like going to this school and I want to do better. Thank-you for understanding and wishing to get chance to make things right. I\'ll work hard to be a better student and follow the school\'s rules.\nThanking you,\nYours sincerely,\nAyushi Mehra\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64379,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to your Principal apologizing for the noise made in class.','','','Rose English School,\nWardha-442 001\n2nd July, 2023.\n\nTO\nThe Principal,\nRose English School,\nWardha-442 001.\nSubject: Apology for misbehaviour in class.\nRespected Sir,\nI am writing this letter to tell you that I am extremely sorry for what I did. I know you are very angry with the class for making a terrible noise yesterday. But really it isn\'t the fault of the others. It was I who was responsible.\nI had brought a snail in a matchbox. Somehow it got out and Johar saw it on the floor. In the next few minutes all the students had left their places to see it. It was some time before we could get it back into the matchbox.\nWhen you came in you were angry. You punished the whole class. Sir, please forgive everyone, and especially me. I shall never do such a thing again.\nYours obediently,\nXYZ\n(Std. V \'B\')\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64380,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to IKEA, asking them to supply certain items to your office.','','','19, Melroy Building, \nLamington Road, \nMumbai-400 099.\n12 January 2023\n\nThe Manager,\nIKEA, \nNavi Mumbai-400 601.\nSubject: Delivery of office furniture\nDear Sir,\nI shall be grateful if you will kindly arrange for the following IKEA items to be delivered to the above-mentioned address:\n1 Office table-Model MYT 6\n5 Office chairs - Model PPT 4\n1 Coffee table - Model UBN 654\n1 Newspaper rack - Oriental Breeze\nYour bill will be paid on delivery.\nYours faithfully,\nRajesh Mehta\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64381,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to the Manager of Cadbury India Ltd., Thane requesting permission to visit their factory so as to know how chocolates are made.','','','Nivedita High School, \nS.V. Road, \nKandivali (W), \nMumbai-400 067.\n25 February 2023\n\nTo \nThe Manager, \nCadbury India Limited, \nPokhran Road, \nThane (West)-400 062.\nSubject: Permission for factory visit\nDear Sir,\nI represent the Nivedita High School in Kandivali. Our school has started a new initiative under which we take selected students to different factories so as to expose them to modern technological processes involved in creating consumer products. These students are ca on the basis of their interest in such subjects.\nI would, therefore, request your kind permission to visit your factory at a date convenient to you in the near future. I assure you that throughout the visit, the students will maintain proper decorum and will not disturb the on-going factory processes.\nWe would be grateful to hear a positive response from you.\nThanking you,\nSameer Patel \nAdministrator \nNivedita High School\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64382,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to the Principal of your school, suggesting ways to improve the school canteen.','','','Balkrishna Nivas, \nGokhale Road, \nDadar (West),\nMumbai - 400 028.\n19 January 2023\n\nTo \nThe Principal, \nVeer Savarkar Vidyalaya, \nL. J. Road, Mahim, \nMumbai-400 016.\nSubject: Improvement of School Canteen\nRespected Sir,\nThis letter is with regard to some changes required in the school canteen. The school canteen should be a place where we students can have our meals in clean and hygienic conditions. This is lacking, at least, presently. Moreover, the staff is not even concerned about this, and as a result, we are compelled to have our tiffin in the classroom or garden.\nTherefore, I would like to suggest a few ways that will improve the canteen:\n(1) Place wire netting on all windows and doors to keep out flies and mosquitoes.\n(2) To ensure maximum use of dustbins, place them near the door.\n(3) Connect the water coolers to water purifiers to prevent waterborne infections among the students.\n(4) Sprinkle a germicide (like phenol or D.D.T.) on the floor both inside and outside the\ncanteen.\n(5) Instruct the canteen staff to be presentable. Instruct them to be well-behaved and patient, especially with the tiny tots. \nI do hope you will consider the suggestions with utmost urgency and implement them as well.\nYours obediently,\nNamita Salvi \nHead Girl, Std 5 C\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64383,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a formal letter to the Postmaster, informing your change of address so that letters/packages addressed to you may be redirected to the new address.','','','22, Queen\'s Peak, \nMayfair Road, \nMalad (West), \nMumbai-400 068.\n22 February 2023\n\nTo \nThe Postmaster, \nHead Post Office, \nMumbai-400 068.\nSubject: Change in postal address\nDear Sir,\nI have shifted my residence from 15, Konark Heights, Andheri (West), Mumbai - 400 053 to 22, Queen\'s Peak, Mayfair Road, Malad, Mumbai - 400 068. I would therefore request you to kindly redirect my letters or packages to the new address.\nI hope you would grant this request and am grateful for the same.\nYours faithfully,\nManisha Mohan\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64384,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your friend who has met with an accident and is laid up in head.','','','6. Ram Kutir, \nSadashiv Road, \nGoregaon (West), \nMumbai-400 062.\n7 January 2023\nDear Sumeet,\nIt was really upsetting to hear about your accident. Thankfully and by the grace of God you are out of danger and recovering well. Uncle told me that it was not a serious accident. I am sure that you will be up and about before long.\nI am sure you must be thinking of school and that you are missing a lot of studies. Do not worry. Our teacher has requested that some extra notes be mailed to you. I too will come over to visit you this weekend. We could look up the missed portion, and I could solve any queries you may have.\nI wish I were with you now. We could visit your favourite places and play carrom Nevertheless, get well soon so that we can enjoy in the coming days.\nHoping to hear from you soon.\nYour best friend,\nRaj\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64385,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter of appreciation to your teacher.','','','12, Malika Manor, \nBeach Street, \nWorli, \nMumbai-400 072.\n5 February 2023\nDearest Melissa Ma\'am,\nWishing you a very Happy Teachers\' Day! You have been so wonderful to us - right since we were primary graders - that we all fondly remember you, especially on this day.\nYou have taught us to be not only attentive in class but also aware of each others\' needs and problems in life. You moulded us into better persons than we could have been without you. Your practical guidance became the foundation of our later years. I remember the days when we would simply wait for your class to begin. We all really worked hard and followed your advice because you had told us that the results would be worth it. You were right, Ma\'am!\nI deeply appreciate all that you have done for us. We will forever be grateful to you for it. May God bless you and keep you under the mantle of His loving care always.\nYour loving student,\nGavin\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64386,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your uncle thanking him for a birthday gift.','','','Triveni Bungalow, \nM. G. Road, \nParbhani-431 401.\n23rd January, 2023\nDear Uncle,\nThank you so much for your wonderful birthday present. Cousin Roma reached here yesterday and gave me the wrist-watch you sent. Uncle, the gift is superb. It is just\nWe had a lovely party last evening. Roma sang for us. My friends were delighted. I received a what I wanted. lot of presents-but none so grand as yours.\nDo take care of yourself, uncle. Roma says you haven\'t been keeping well lately. Give my love to aunty. And thanks once again for the present.\nYour loving niece,\nXYZ\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64387,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your teacher telling her / him how much you appreciate her/him. ','','','4. Aashirwad,\nMain Road,\nShahad-421 103.\n5th September, 2023\nDear Lydia Teacher,\nToday is Teacher\'s Day, and I remembered you this morning just as I got up. Thank you, teacher, for all that you have done for me. You were my English teacher last year, and you made the lessons in our Balbharati so interesting that I grew to love the language. You also encouraged us to read. Today, I love reading books and my knowledge and my language has improved tremendously.\nTeacher, you also taught us to be kind-hearted and loving. You never shouted at us, however naughty we were, but always made us understand things. Thank you so much for all this. L will always remember you.\nYour obedient student,\nSanjay Patil\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64388,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter sharing your school picnic experience to your father who is on a Business Trip.','','','Vedika Gupta \n501. Orchid Valley \nMG Road \nWorli East \nMumbai 400054\n10th September, 2023\nDear Dad,\nI hope you\'re doing well on your business trip. I wanted to tell you all about our school picnic to a water park called Blue Horizon Aquapark. It was super awesome!\nThe water park had really big water slides, a lazy river and even a pool with waves like the ocean Thes was shining so bright, and it was hot, just the right weather for splashing around. I went on the tall win slide, and it felt like I was flying. We also had a water balloon fight, and I got all wet, but it was so fun. I even made a new friend, Ritika. We had ice cream together and it was yummy. It was the best ever!\nI wished you could have been there with us, but I know you\'re working hard I miss you, Dad and U to see you when you\'re back.\nTake care, and I\'ll save more water park stories to tell you when you\'re home\nYours lovingly.\nVedika\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64389,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your cousin thanking them for the birthday gift.','','','Yamini Dev \n201/Sai Apartment \nGokul Colony \nBorivali West \nMumbai-400054\n9th October, 2022\nDear Nilesh,\nI hope this letter finds you well. I wanted to take a moment to express my heartfelt gratitude for the fam birthday gift you sent me. The headphones you gave me were exactly what I needed, especially since my l pair got damaged. I am absolutely thrilled about them!\nThese headphones are not only stylish but also deliver incredible sound quality. I\'ve been using then listen to my favorite music, watch movies and even for online classes. They have added so mu convenience to my daily life, and I can\'t thank you enough for such a thoughtful and useful gift.\nYour kindness and generosity have made my birthday extra special this year. 1 appreciate the effort you put into selecting a gift that suits my interests perfectly. I can\'t wait to catch up with you and personally express my gratitude.\nOnce again, thank you for the wonderful gift, and thank you for being such an amazing cousin.\nYours lovingly, \nYamini\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64390,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','You recently enjoyed reading a fictional book and would like to recommend it to a friend. Write a informal letter explaining why you liked the book and encouraging your friend to read it.','','','Jasmine Singh \n702. Rose View Apartments \nMira Nagar \nCharni Road East \nMumbai - 400055\n10th August, 2023\nDear Sana,\nI recently read a book called \"Gulliver\'s Travels\' by Jonathan Swift, and I wanted to tell you about it. The book is about a guy named Gulliver who goes on some really crazy adventures. In one part, be meets these tiny people called Lilliputians who tie him up with tiny strings. It\'s super funny and interesting. The author did a great job showing the character\'s feelings, like surprise and fear, which made the story interesting and fun to read.\nWhat I liked most about the book is how creative it is. The author, Jonathan Swift, comes up with these wild ideas and makes you think about how silly some things in our world can be. The story mixes adventure with jokes and things that make you think. I think you\'ll really enjoy it.\nSo, give \'Gulliver\'s Travels\' a try when you have a chance. It\'s a fun and thought-provoking book that I\'m sure you\'ll like. Let\'s chat about it when you\'re done!\nYours lovingly,\nJasmine Singh\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64391,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Your friend has participated in an inter-school speech competition. Write a informal letter encouraging him and also provide advice on some dos and don\'ts of being a good speaker.','','','Siddhant Verma\n701, Sundeck Apartments \nKamala Industrial Estate \nGoregaon West \nMumbai-400054\n3rd October, 2023\nDear Vikram,\nI\'m really proud of you for joining the inter-school speech contest. It\'s not easy to speak in front of others. But you\'re doing it, and that\'s fantastic! However, the last time we spoke, you sounded nervous about speaking in front of a crowd. So I though I\'d share some simple tips about being a good speaker.\nFirst, make sure to speak clearly. Don\'t mumble or talk too quietly or too loudly. It\'s essential to look at the people you\'re talking to; it shows you\'re interested in what you\'re saying. Also, speak at a speed that\'s easy for everyone to follow. Be polite and pleasant nobody likes rude speakers. Lastly, try to think about what the audience might like or be interested in.\nWhen it comes to giving your speech, plan ahead. Write down your ideas and arrange them in a way that makes sense. Then, practice your speech in your mind before you go on stage. With some practice and these tips, I\'m sure you\'ll do great in the competition!\nI believe in you, and I know you can do it. Good luck!\nWarm regards,\nYours lovingly, \nSiddhant\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64392,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your cousin asking him/her to come and stay with you during the summer vacation.','','','29, Trimurty House,\nKulu Manali\nHaryana 800081.\n28 April, 2020\nDear Sandhya,\nI hope that this letter finds you in good health and high spirit. How did your exams go?\nAfter the continuous mental and physical strain that you have undergone in trying to improve your academic scores, I feel that you deserve to enjoy your holidays to the fullest. I am sure that your efforts will pay off.\nDo plan and come to Kulu, the queen of hill stations. This place can definitely be called \'the heaven on earth. It is free from the excessive heat of Mumbai. It is surrounded by the natural beauty of charming flora and fauna and babbling brooks and streams wherever you go. I have made lots of plans and thought of many things that we can do together once you are here.\nBoth of us will enjoy our time together and would visit all the scenic spots. Hoping to see you at the earliest.\nConvey my regards to all at home.\nYour loving sister,\nCharulata\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64393,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your brother living abroad asking him to get you a personal computer.','','','201-Ashiana, \nLinking Road, \nKhar (West), \nMumbai 400052\n15 September, 2020\nDear Rahul,\nI had been so busy with my studies that I could not find time to write to you earlier. Thankfully the exams are finally over and now I am feeling more relaxed. My exams went well and am hoping for a better result. How have you been? Do reply. We are all doing fine.\nOf late, my teachers are expecting the whole class to submit a lot of projects especially for the Science subject. It would be ideal if we had a computer at home. This being the age of technology, it would make my work far easier. I\'ll be really grateful to you if you can get me a computer this Diwali once you are home for the festival. I know that you will get me a present, because you always do, so I thought to ask for something that I not only need but would also love to have. Hope you would consider your younger brother\'s words. Take care of your health and be in touch.\nYours affectionately,\nAnkit\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64394,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your cousin requesting him/her to lend his/her camera for your educational trip.','','','10-Apsara Building,\nChakala,\nAndheri (East),\nMumbai 400093,\n4 October, 2020\nMy Dear Ankit,\nHow are you? It has been a while since we last met. We are doing well here and hope the same for you and all in the family.\nI am very excited as our school is going on an educational trip to New Delhi during the Diwali vacation for ten days. Parents have given me permission to join the trip and I\'m really looking forward to it as I have never been out of Mumbai earlier.\nHowever, I have a request to make. I know there will be a lot of exciting places to see in the capital, but I don\'t have a camera to take photographs and keep the memories of this trip alive. Could I please borrow your camera for the trip? I promise to take very good care of it. If you agree, please send it over with Aarav when he visits our place the next week. I hope that you will not disappoint me.\nPlease convey my regards to Uncle and Aunt.\nYour loving cousin,\nAshish\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64395,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a cheerful letter to a friend who is recovering in hospital.','','','15-Sea Shell,\nNawapur Ward,\nMalegaon 423203.\n3 June, 2020\nDear Anne,\nI hope that this letter finds you in much better health and spirit. When I visited you last time, you were looking very weak and sad. Anne, you should count your blessings and thank God for having granted you a new lease of life. Marika told me that you are now completely out of danger, but will have to stay in the hospital for some more time.\nLife is unfortunately not a bed of roses. But you have had your share of thorns and now, it is time for joys. We are planning a grand homecoming for you. All is well at home and at school. Our class teacher has asked me to inform you that you need not worry about your studies and promotion. As you have fared well in the previous exams and tests, you will be promoted on the basis of the same.\nIt is said that \'health is wealth\'. So, take good care of your health. Nothing else is important right now. I will surely come to meet you again next week. Get well soon and do not forget to smile because you look pretty while smiling.\nYour loving friend, \nRitika\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64396,2114,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a informal letter to your father who is away for his training giving him updates about home.','','','101-Lok Darshan, \nDreamland Society,\nNanded-431601.\n20 October, 2020\nDear Papa,\nI hope that you are having an interesting training programme and are doing well. Since, you have been away, we have been leading a more or less routine life. Things have been kind of dull around the house without you. But many unfortunate incidents have taken place over the last week.\nMum\'s purse was stolen (while travelling) in the train. There wasn\'t much money in it, but the greatest misfortune was losing all house and cupboard keys. So, we had called the carpenter to get all the locks in the house and cupboards changed.\nGrandma\'s diabetes has gone up as she is not controlling her diet. She refuses to listen to us.\nWe are eagerly awaiting your return. It will help in sorting out daily issues easily. Please take care of yourself and come home as soon as your training gets over. We all miss you very much.\nYour loving daughter,\nAnu\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64397,1989,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the Figure  and answer the following questions :','1) What are the three parts of the earth\'s interior?\n2) At what distance is the centre of the earth located from its surface?\n3) What is the average thickness of the crust of the earth?\n4) What is lithosphere?\n5) What is the thickness of the mantle?\n6) What is the thickness of the core?\n','','1) The crust, the mantle and the core are the three parts of the earth\'s interior.\n2) The centre of the earth is located at a distance of 6371 km from its surface.\n3) The average thickness of the crust of the earth is 30 km.\n4) The crust and the portion of the mantle adjacent to the crust up to the depth of 100 km are together called lithosphere.\n5) The thickness of the mantle is about 2870 km.\n6) The thickness of the core is about 3471 km.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(64398,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on THE ELEPHANT.','','','                                                         <b>THE ELEPHANT</b>\nThe elephant is mostly found in Africa and Asia. It is the biggest land animal in the world. It breathes through its trunk. It also uses its trunk to put food into its mouth. The elephant has two ivory tusks coming out of its mouth.\nElephants live in herds. They are very protective about their young ones. Elephants can be tamed and made to do hard work like moving and lifting timber. They eat leaves and grass and fruits. An elephant is said to have a very good memory.\nSome people kill the elephants to make money by selling their tusks. So, in order to protect and preserve them, many nations have banned the killing of elephants.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64399,1989,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the figures given  and answer the following questions:','1) At what depth does the curve of the secondary waves seem to have ended in figure?\n2) What is the velocity of the secondary waves around the boundary of the outer core shown in figure?\n3) What conclusion can be drawn on the basis of the curve of the secondary waves stopping around the depth of 2900 km?\n4) What is the velocity of the primary waves at the time of entering the outer core shown in figure?\n','','1) The curve of the secondary waves seems to have ended around the depth of 2900 km in figure (A).\n2) The velocity of the secondary waves is 6 to 8 km/sec around the boundary of the outer core shown in figure (A).\n3) On the basis of the curve of the secondary waves stopping around the depth of 2900 km, it can be concluded that secondary waves do not enter the core.\n4) The velocity of the primary waves at the time of entering the outer core shown in figure (A) is 8 km/sec.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(64400,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on THE HAPPIEST DAY OF MY LIFE.','','','                                            <b>THE HAPPIEST DAY OF MY LIFE</b>\nI had always wanted a pet of my own. My friend Nikhil had a parrot and Irfan had a cat. Subbu had a tortoise. I was the only one who did not have a pet.\nI wanted a small pet dog I could cuddle. I wanted to play with it and take it for walks. I knew I would take good care of it.\nA few days before my tenth birthday, Mummy asked me what I wanted. I said I wanted a dog. But Mummy shook her head and said that she would get me some books.\nOn my birthday, I got up early in the morning. I saw a parcel on my table, wrapped in coloured paper. I knew that they were my books. But before I could open the parcel I saw a box with big holes in it under the table. I opened the box. The most beautiful little puppy licked my hand. My parents said that the puppy was my real birthday present.\nThat was the happiest day of my life.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64401,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on A VISIT TO A ZOO.','','','                                                            <b>A VISIT TO A ZOO</b>\nEvery year, our school takes us for two picnics. The first one is called a \'study trip. Last week our class went for a study trip to the zoo. We went by bus.\nWe entered the zoo and went past the various cages. We saw lions, tigers and bears. We saw birds of various colours and sizes. The huge snakes terrified us. The monkeys and their tricks made us laugh. Animals such as the crocodile and the hippopotamus lay in a pond. The rhinoceroses were quite ugly. We saw cats of various sizes and colours. None of us had seen such cats before.\nWe spent the entire day in the zoo. We had our lunch in the canteen inside. At 5 p.m. we left and got into the bus. We returned home tired, but happy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64402,1989,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw neat diagrams, label them and explain : The interior of the earth ','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(64403,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on MY COUNTRY.','','','                                                                     <b>MY COUNTRY</b>\nIndia is my country. It is also known as Bharat and Hindustan. It is a large country made up of many states and union territories. It is a land of mountains, plains, plateaus, forests, deserts and seacoasts. It is a land of many rivers like the Ganga, the Yamuna, the Krishna and the Narmada.\nIt has many well-developed cities. Chief among them are Mumbai, Delhi, Chennai, Kolkata and Bengaluru. New Delhi is the capital of India.\nIndia is full of beautiful monuments, buildings, historic caves and beautiful temples. People of many religions and cultures live here. India follows the democratic form of government. I love my country very much.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64404,1989,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Draw neat diagrams, label them and explain : Magnetic pole and Equator ','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(64405,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on HOLI.','','','                                                                     <b>HOLI</b>\nHoli is the festival of colours. Holl is generally celebrated for two days in the month of Phalgun.\nOn the first day (full moon day) a bonfire is lit at night and the goddess Holika is worshipped. Special sweet dishes are made for the occasion. The second day is \'Dhulivandan when adults and children play with coloured water and powder. Children enjoy this day a lot.\nHowever, Holl should be celebrated with care. Healthy trees should not be cut for the bonfire. Harmful colour or paint should not be used. Strangers and passers-by should not be troubled. Water should not be wasted. Only then will everyone enjoy Holi.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64406,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on HOW I COME TO SCHOOL EVERY DAY.','','','                                                  <b>HOW I COME TO SCHOOL EVERY DAY</b>\nMy school is quite far from my house. I cannot walk to school, like many of my friends do. I travel every day by bus to school. It is a journey of about twenty minutes. Sometimes I get a seat, sometimes I don\'t. The bus conductor is always Mr. Jagtap. He is a nice, friendly person and knows me very well. I have a student\'s pass and he never asks me if I have a ticket.\nThe bus takes the same route every day and I know all the landmarks along the route Sometimes interesting people board the bus. Once a paan-chewing fisherman got into a long fight with the conductor. He had to be forcibly removed from the bus.\nThe bus is generally on time, and so I am never late to school. Once there was a bus strike and I couldn\'t go to school at all. I look forward to my journey to school by bus every day.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64407,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on TELEVISION.','','','                                                   <b>TELEVISION</b>\nThe television is a very useful invention. Today, nearly everyone has a TV set in their homes.\nThe television can be useful as well as harmful. We get the latest news from the different parts of the world. We get information about science and history. We get to see different exciting matches. We can watch cartoons and films too.\nHowever, sometimes children neglect their studies to watch TV. Instead of playing healthy outdoor games, they sit in front of the TV. Their health and their eyesight suffer due to this.\nOur teacher tells us that we must watch only good programmes, and that too for a short time. Then the television will be useful and not harmful.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64408,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on MY FAVOURITE GAME.','','','                                                   <b>MY FAVOURITE GAME</b>\nI like playing games. Whenever I have some free time, I run outdoors to play. My favourite game is cricket. I like kabaddi and football too. But not as much as I like cricket.\nThe best thing about cricket is that there are many players. And everybody has to know the rules. The most exciting events are the matches. I am an expert batsman. My highest score so far has been 52, not out. I can bowl well, too.\nI love to watch the highlights of key matches shown on the TV. Sachin Tendulkar is my hero. I like cricket because it provides me with exercise and makes me fit and healthy. It also teaches me the values of team spirit and sportsmanship. I shall never give up playing cricket.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64409,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on MY DAYDREAM.','','','                                                               <b>MY DAYDREAM</b>\nA dream is something that one has at night, when one is asleep. A daydream comes to one\'s mind during the day, when one is wide awake.\nMy own daydream is about becoming a big doctor and fighting diseases like cancer. I dream about how I will cure people of this disease and how they are grateful to me. I dream about the research I will do and the awards and medals I will get. In my daydream 1 even see people thinking about me as a God with great powers! Sometimes I feel that some of the TV serials I see influence my daydream!\nHowever, one thing I know is that I will try to make my daydream come true. I know that it is going to be difficult, but I will work hard and I am sure I will be successful.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64412,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on MY JOURNEY BY BOAT.','','','                                                                     <b> MY JOURNEY BY BOAT</b>\nOnce we had gone to a hill-station called Munnar. There was a small river running through the place, and we thought it would be fun to journey down the river by boat.\nSo, one morning we hired a boat. We decided to row it ourselves. We were given a pair of oars. My father and mother used the oars and we reached the middle of the narrow river.\nThinking that rowing is easy, I snatched at the oar that was in my mother\'s hand. To my horror, the oar was very heavy and it slipped from my hand and fell into the river. We were terrified. Fortunately, after a couple of minutes the current in the river brought the oar back to us. But I really thought at that moment that we would drown in that small river! After this incident, both my brother and I sat quietly in our corners and allowed our parents to do the rowing for the entire journey!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64419,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on MY FAVOURITE SPORTS EVENT','','','                                               <b>MY FAVOURITE SPORTS EVENT</b>\nMy favourite sports event is the FIFA World Cup. This is an international men\'s football tournament. The last event was held in Russia in 2018. The next tournament is scheduled to take place between 21 November to 18 December in Qatar. The final is expected to be played in Moscow. This will be last tournament to involve about 32 national teams.\nI love watching the teams play. The matches are always full of action and excitement. It is also fun to watch the spectators; they are all bursting with interest and enthusiasm, waving the flags of their nations. Sometimes, of course, they get a bit rowdy and there are fights.\nMy only wish is to see India qualify and be a part of this tournament. Perhaps, in the distant future, we may also become the champs!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64420,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on WHAT I WOULD LIKE TO DO FOR MY COUNTRY.','','','                                                <b>WHAT I WOULD LIKE TO DO FOR MY COUNTRY</b>\nWhenever I am travelling and I look out of the window of the car or bus, I feel very sorry to see the poverty around me. What I would like to do is find some way to get rid of this poverty. I would like India to be a rich and developed nation like the U.S., and though it will take years for this to happen, I would like to help in some way!\nMy dream is to set up a large company in a backward area, and provide employment to the unemployed and poor people there. These people should be able to live a happy and dignified life, and should not have to beg or to suffer. They should not have to depend on others or the government for their needs. I would also start schools, colleges and hospitals in these areas.\nI do not know whether my dream will come true, but I am at the moment studying hard so that I can try to fulfil it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64423,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on Good Habits.','','','                                                                        <b>Good Habits</b>\nGood habits are something that improve our lifestyle and develop good health. We must aim at having good habits. We must bathe everyday and brush our teeth twice a day. We must not spit on the road and should keep our nails clean.\nWe must use polite and respectful language while communicating with others. We must be honest and true to ourselves as well as to others.\nWe must keep our home, school and surroundings clean. We must help the needy. Good habits will help us to be successful in every field.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64425,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on A House on Fire.','','','                                                                          <b>A House on Fire</b>\nI had never seen a house on fire before. So, one evening, when I heard fire engines with loud alarm bells rushing past my house. I quickly ran out, and just a few streets away, I joined a large crowd of people.\nWe could see the fire only from a distance because the police would not allow any new near the building on fire. What a terrible scene I saw! Huge flames of fire were coming out of each floor and black and thick smoke was spread all around. Every now and then, tongues of fire would shoot up almost sky-high, sending huge sparks of fire all around.\nThree fire engines were busily engaged and the firemen in their dark uniform were using the hose on various parts of the building. The rushing water from several hoses soaked the building, but it seemed to have little effect on the flames. The tall ladders of the fire engine were stretched upwards and I could see some firemen climbing up with hoses in their hands On reaching almost the top of the ladder, they began to literally flood the topmost part of the building with gushing water. This continuous flooding brought the fire under control after sometime, but the building was completely destroyed. There were several people injured but thankfully, due to the quick and efficient action of the fire fighters, none of the lives were lost.\nWhile fire is a blessing in many ways, it can also be a great danger to human life and property.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64428,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on The Taj Mahal.','','','                                                                            <b>The Taj Mahal</b>\nAn architectural marvel which finds a place among the Seven Wonders of the World is the famous Taj Mahal.\nThe Taj Mahal was constructed by the Indian ruler Shah Jahan in the 17th century. It is a mausoleum named after Mumtaz Mahal, the beloved wife of Shah Jahan, made out of marble and materials including lapis lazuli, jade, crystal, amethyst, and several other precious and semi-precious gemstones. The building\'s central dome is slightly more than 70 meters high, and is surrounded by four minarets. According to Islamic tradition, the walls have verses from the Quran written in calligraphy. Inside the mausoleum (burial place), there is a false tomb of Mumtaz Mahal (also called the cenotaph which is a public monument built in her memory), whereas the real sarcophagus which is a large decorative container for her dead remains lies at the garden level.\nThe complex includes a main gateway, a garden with pools of water, a red sandstone mosque, and an identical building called jawab. Shah Jahan wanted to build the second mausoleum across the river, as impressive as the Taj Mahal, to have his own remains buried there. He also wanted the two mausoleums to be connected with bridges. But, unfortunately, these plans were never carried out and remained only on paper.\nAurangzeb, Shah Jahan\'s third son, forcefully removed his father from the throne in 1658, and became the ruler of the country. Shah Jahan ended up under house arrest, locked up in a tower of the Red Fort at Agra. From there, he would spend his time looking at the Taj Mahal. When he finally passed away in 1666, his body was buried next to Mumtaz Mahal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64431,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on Charactericties of a Good Listener.','','','                                           <b> Charactericties of a Good Listener</b>\nBeing a good listener is really important in various aspects of our lives Being certain important qualities. a good listener means having\nGood listeners pay attention and show respect. They look at the person speaking and focus on what\'s being said. This shows they care about the conversation. They are patient and polite. They wait for the speaker to finish before asking questions or disagreeing. This makes talking easier Good listeners are not afraid to ask questions when they don\'t understand. This helps in clear communication. They take notes. Writing down important things helps them remember and understand better. Lastly, they think about helps them understand tricky words or phrases later.\nIn conclusion, being a good listener means being attentive, patient, polite, willing to ask question, ting notes and thinking about what you heard. These qualities help in learning, talking effectively and being kind to others in everyday life.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64432,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on A Memorable Family Picnic.','','','                                                       <b> A Memorable Family Picnic</b>\nLast summer vacation, my family and I embarked on an unforgettable picnic to Lonavala, a lovely hill Ans station near Mumbai. It was a day of joy and adventure that I\'ll forever treasure We started early, packing our bags with delicious snacks and sandwiches. The drive to Lonavala was scenic, with winding roads surrounded by lush greenery, creating a picturesque journey.\nUpon arriving in Lonavala, we were greeted by the cool mountain breeze and the sweet aroma of chikkis, a local delicacy. We found a peaceful spot in a beautiful garden, laid out our picnic mat and enjoyed our tasty meal. After lunch, we explored some of Lonavala\'s famous spots. We splashed in the refreshing waters of Bhushi Dam, marveled at the ancient carvings in Karla Caves and enjoyed Resting from Tiger Point\nAs the day ended, we pocked our belongings and headed home, bred but content Our family picnic to Lonavala created cherished memories that will always hold a special place in our hearts.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64433,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on My Mother.','','','                                                                     <b>My Mother</b>\nMy mother is an extraordinary person in my life and I treasure her for many reasons. My mother is like a guiding star. She lights up my life with her wisdom and love. Whenever I\'m confused or need advice she\'s there to show me the way, just like stars light up the night sky. Her support is consistent and unwavering, just like the constant presence of shells along the shore.\nMy mother\'s words are like the birds songs, full of comfort and joy. Her voice is soothing and her advice is like music to my ears.\nThere may be many beautiful things in the world, but there is only one mother, and mine is truly amazing. She guides me, supports me and fills my life with love and wisdom, I\'m grateful for her every day.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64434,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on The Marathon.','','','                                                                       <b>The Marathon</b>\nA marathon is a special kind of running race that covers a long distance of about 42 kilometers. But the word \"marathon means more than just a race, it also stands for a task or event that takes a lot of Dame and effort.\nThe very first marathon race happened a long time ago in Greece during the first Olympic Games was a way to honor a brave Greek hero named Pheidippides. This hero had a crucial message to deliver from the battle of Marathon to Athens, and his run was a race against time. \nMarathons have a rich history that extends beyond ancient Greece. Today, they are celebrated as global sporting tradition. These races draw participants and spectators from all walks of life, promoting fitness and goodwill. They have become a platform for athletes to test their physical endurance and mental toughness.\nWhen you see or take part in a marathon, it\'s a reminder of the rich history and the enduring human spirit that this race represents. It\'s a testament to how people can accomplish remarkable feats w dedication and perseverance.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64435,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on My Proudest Achievement.','','','                                                      <b>My Proudest Achievement</b>\nOne of my proudest achievements happened when I won the first prize in a drawing competition at school. It was a special moment in my life and I felt incredibly happy and proud.\nThe competition was about drawing our favorite place I chose to draw a beautiful park near my home where I love to play. I worked hard on my drawing, using bright colors and lots of details to make it look just right. \nWhen the winners were announced and I heard my name called for the first prize, I couldn\'t believe at was overjoyed. My parents and teachers were so proud of me too.\nThis achievement encouraged me to keep practicing my drawing skills and to never stop trying to improve. It also taught me that when you do your best, you can achieve great things. \nWinning that drawing competition will always be a special memory for me. It\'s a reminder that wan effort and determination, I can accomplish my goals and make my loved ones proud too.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64436,2115,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an essay on My Favorite Subject in School.','','','                                                       <b>My Favorite Subject in School</b>\nMy favorite subject in school is science. I love science because it helps me understand how the world works and answers many of the questions I have about the things around me. In science class, we learn about fascinating topics like the planets in our solar system, the water cycle and the life cycles of animals. I enjoy conducting experiments and making discoveries. It\'s like being a little scientist.\nOne of the reasons I like science is because it\'s hands-on. We get to do experiments and see how things react, which is a lot of fun. I also like that science encourages us to ask questions and find answers through observation and investigation. My science teacher is also great. She makes learning fun by showing us cool videos and pictures that make the concepts come alive.\nOverall, science is my favorite subject because it\'s exciting, interactive and helps me learn about the amazing world we live in. It makes me curious and eager to explore more about the mysteries of nature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64438,2116,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','Two bulls - tied together by a rope-a pile of hay a little distance away from them-each tries to go ahead to reach the hay - both fail-one bull tells the other to move together with him - the other agrees - they reach the hay-moral','Write story using the following outline and give a suitable title:','','','                                                     <b>UNITY IS STRENGTH</b>\nTwo bulls in a field were tied together by a rope. There was a pile of hay a little distance away from them. Each tried to move ahead in order to reach the hay. Since the other stayed behind, the bull that moved forward could not reach the hay.\nFinally one bull told the other to move together with him. The other agreed. They reached the hay and enjoyed a good meal. We learn from this story that there is strength in unity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64441,2116,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','Courtiers jealous of Birbal-ask Akbar to let them test his wisdom-one courtier puts a question to him - how many crows in Agra-Birbal asks for a week to study the situation - every day seen on the terrace looking at the crows - after a week courtier asks him again-Birbal says as many crows as hairs on the courtier\'s head - Akbar laughs.','Write story using the following outline and give a suitable title:','','','                                                                  <b>CLEVER BIRBAL</b>\nSome courtiers were jealous of Birbal. They asked for Akbar\'s permission to let them test Birbal\'s wisdom. Akbar told them to go ahead.\nOne courtier stepped up to Birbal and said, \"You are indeed very clever. Surely you can tell me how many crows there are in Agra.\"\nBirbal thought for a moment and said, \"Give me a week and I shall give you the exact number\"\nFrom that evening onwards Birbal was to be seen on the terrace staring at the crows that flew by or were perched on various places.\nA week passed and durbar was called once again. The courtier stepped up to Birbal and put the same question to him again.\nBirbal said to him, \"Could you tell me how many hairs are there on your head?\"\n\"Why no,\" said the man.\n\"I am sorry, then,\" said Birbal, \"for that is exactly how many crows there are in Agra.\"\nAkbar and all the courtiers had a hearty laugh at this.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64444,2116,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','(Outline: a hardworking ant-a carefree grasshopper-ant collected food for winter - grasshopper played fiddle - during winter, ant had plenty of food - grasshopper realises mistake - goes to the ant for help - grasshopper becomes responsible-both share their wisdom with the other animals.)','Write a story using the outline given below. Use your own ideas and give it a suitable title.','','','                                                <b> The Singing Ant and the Lazy Grasshopper</b>\nIn a bustling meadow, there lived a hardworking ant named Andy and a carefree grasshopper tamed Gus Andy spent his days collecting food for the winter, while Gus played his fiddle and sang songs.\nAs the warm days turned chilly, Andy had a mountain of food stored safely. Gus, however, had nothing but his fiddle. He felt bad about his choice to play all summer. He realized his mistake and went to Andy, asking for help. Andy, being kind-hearted, shared his food.\nDuring winter, they stayed warm in Andy\'s home and became friends. Gus learned to be responsible and helped Andy. When spring returned, Gus played his fiddle for everyone, and they all danced and enjoyed They had lots of food, and they shared their wisdom with the other animals, so everyone learned the importance of hard work and having fun.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64450,2116,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','(Outline: a kind-hearted farmer - discovers a magical seed - he plants the seed - seed grows into giant beanstalk reaching the cloud- farmer finds magical land on the beanstalk - a wise owl gives a magical watering can to the farmer - can makes any plany grow instantly - farmer shares can with fellow villagers - everyone is happy.)','Write a story using the outline given below. Use your own ideas and give it a suitable title.','','','                             <b>The Magical Seed and the Generous Farmer </b>\nOnce upon a time, in a quiet village, there lived a kind-hearted farmer named Jaydeep. He was known for his generosity and love for the land. One day, while plowing his fields, Jaydeep discovered a small, glowing seed.\nIntrigued, he planted the seed in his best soil. To his astonishment, it grew into a giant beanstalk that reached the clouds. Climbing up, Jaydeep found a land filled with talking animals and colorful flowers.\nAmong them was a wise owl who gave Jaydeep a magical watering can. It could make any plant grow instantly Jaydeep returned home with the watering can and shared it with his fellow villagers. The barren fields turned lush and bountiful, ending their hardship. Tom\'s generosity brought happiness to everyone The magical seed taught the village that kindness and sharing can create wonders. And so, Jaydeep\'s farm and heart flourished, forever showering his village with abundance.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64454,2116,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a story with the moral - Bad Habits are like weeds. The longer you allow them to grow, the harder is to uproot them. It is wise to get rid of them when it is easy to do so. Give it a suitable title.','','','                                                        <b>The Weeds of Lies</b>\nIn a quiet town, there lived a boy named Prashant. Prashant was a kind-hearted child, but he had a bad habit of telling little lies. He thought these lies were harmless and could save him from trouble.\nAt first. Prashant\'s lies were simple, like pretending he finished his homework when he hadn\'t that time passed his lies grow bigger and more complex. He started making up stories about exciting adventure be never had.\n\nOne day, Prashant told a huge lie about discovering a hidden treasure in the forest. His friends good and followed him to find it. But, of course, there was no treasure. Prashant realized the mess he created and he felt terrible. He decided to confess his lie and face the consequences. His friends forge took time to rebuild.\nFrom that day on, Prashant understood that bad habits like lying were like weeds. The longer you let them grow, the harder they are to uproot. It was wise to get rid of them early when it\'s easier to do so.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64456,2116,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a story with the moral-It is worthless to daydream about things that do not exist. Give it a suitable title.','','','                                                      <b>The Cycle of Day-Dreams</b>\nOnce in the peaceful village of Vijaypur, there lived a young boy named Rahul. Rahul was known for his vivid imagination. He spent most of his time daydreaming about amazing adventures.\n\nOne day, he saw a poster about a village cycle race, and his imagination went wild. Rahul began daydreaming about finding a magical cycle that could help him win the race effortlessly In his daydream, he saw mel zooming past all the other racers, leaving them in awe.\nAs the race day approached, Rahul\'s daydreams grew even grander. He imagined the entire village gathering to cheer for him as he crossed the finish line, holding the competition trophy high. He even daydreamed about the cash prize he\'d receive, spending it on fancy clothes and toys.\nHowever, Rahul got so lost in his daydreams that he forgot to practice for the race. On the day of the race when he got on his regular bicycle, he realized that it wasn\'t magical at all. His legs ached, and be couldn’t keep up with the other racers.\nIn the end, Rahul lost the race. He felt embarrassed and disappointed. He had wasted all his time daydreaming about something that didn\'t exist, instead of putting in the effort to prepare for the race.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64459,2116,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','(Outline) Robert Bruce King at Scotland. He wants to free his country from the English rule -defeated in battle - seeks shelter in a cave - sees a spider in the cave - spider climbing up the walls -falls down several times - tries again and again - at last it succeeds - the King learns a lesson.','Write a story using the outline given below. Give suitable title and moral.','','','                                                          <b>King Bruce and the Spider</b>\nRobert Bruce was the King of Scotland. He wanted to free his country from the English cale He fought many battles against the English but was defeated several times. Afterone one such defeat he was pursued by the enemy and in desperation, took shelter in a cave. The King was saddened by the situation and did not know what to do next. As he sat there feeling dejected, he saw a spider fall down from its web. The King observed the movements of the little creature. The spider began to climb up to its web again. The distance was long and the thread was fine. The spider fell repeatedly but that little insect did not give up hope. He tried again and again reaching a little higher with each try. At last, after innumerable efforts, the little spider reached its home. Robert Bruce\'s heart was filled with hope. He stood up and said \"If a little spider can succeed after failing several times, why can\'t I?\" He made up his mind to wage a battle against the English once again. He went back to his country, worked hard and rebuilt his army. Once again, he fought the English with renewed strength. This time, he was successful and won a glorious victory by getting his country free.\n<b>Moral:</b> Try, try till you succeed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64460,2116,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','Outline: A vain stag was thirsty reached a pool - saw his own reflection-admired his beautiful antlers - cursed his thin legs - wild dogs set by a hunter run after him - desperate tag runs for his life-thin but strong legs help him run way ahead of the dogs - beautiful but wayward antlers get stuck in bushes and become the cause of his death','Write a story using the outline given below. Give suitable title and moral.','','','                                                     <b>The Vain Stag</b>\nIt was a hot summer day. An arrogant and boastful stag was extremely thirsty and was desperately looking for water. He searched all around, but in vain. After hours of painstaking search, he finally spotted a pool. Just as he dipped his head into the water for a sip, he noticed his own reflection. \"Marvelous! What beautiful antlers I have. There\'s nothing as gorgeous /have ever seen. But alas, my legs are so thin and scrawny! I wish they were beautiful\" He got so lost in these thoughts that he did not notice the wild dogs set by a hunter. Startled and frightened, he ran for his life, his thin legs helping him run much faster than the dogs, to a place of safety. But just then his beautiful antlers got stuck in a bush. He tried to break free but got more and more entangled in the bushes. The dogs and the hunter caught up quite fast and killed the proud stag. The dying stag realized that the thin legs he had cursed brought him to a safe place but the antlers he had admired got him killed.\n<b>MORAL:</b> All that glitters is not gold.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64461,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement for an excursion.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">‘There will be a one-day excursion to Panvel on 18th January, 2023. The amount per student is Rs.150/-. Further details will be displayed on the noticeboard by the end of the week. Students who are interested should give their names to the class teacher before January 5, along with the letter of consent from their parents’.</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64462,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement for a Children\'s Day function.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">‘A Children\'s Day function has been organised for 14th November. There will be a Rangoli Competition in the morning and a Drawing Competition in the afternoon. Participants will have to bring their own material. Students wishing to participate must give their names to their Blue House Captains\',\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64464,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement for a Story Writing Competition.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">Tay Cub has organised a Story Writing Competition for students of Std. V on 16th February from 4 p.m. to 5 p.m. in the school hall. Topics will be given on the spot. Prizes will be in the form of books. Those wishing to take part must give their names to their classteachers before 13th February\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64467,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement for a science exhibition to be held in your school.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">     <b>Science Exhibition Calling All Young Scientists! </b>\nThe school has organized a Science Exhibition for Classes V, VI and VII from 19 to 21\" of August. The Exhibition will be held in the school hall at 10 in the morning. Students can choose from a wide range of topics to explore and present, including but not limited to:-\n1.Simple Machines\n2. The Solar System\n3. Environmental Conservation\n4. Forces and Motion\n5. Methods of Preserving Food\nStudents are encouraged to work individually or in small groups. Each exhibit should include a brief explanation of the topic, an eye-catching display and if possible, a simple demonstration Three prizes will be given for each category.\nThose who wish to participate may contact Mrs. Damini Verma for other details. Students must register their names with her before the 30 of July</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64468,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement for misplaced book found in the school library.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">                 <b> Lost and Found - Missing Book</b>\nA Science reference book named \'Wonder of Science with a green cover has been found in the school library If any of the students have misplaced a book matching this description please visit the library and check with the librarian, Mrs. Anita Singh. We aim to return this book to its rightful owner promptly.\n\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64469,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement warning students about the maintenance work of the school electrical wiring system.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\"><b>Important Safety Notice - No Access to Electrical Panel</b>\nStarting next week, maintenance work on the school\'s electrical system will begin. Students are strictly advised not to approach or play around with the electrical panel or equipment on the school premises. Interfering with electrical systems can be extremely dangerous and may lead to severe injuries. Your safety is our priority, please report any concerns to school staff immediately\n\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64470,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement notifying the students to attend the Science Quiz Competition held in the school.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">                        <b> Science Quiz Competition</b>\nJoin us for an exciting Science Quiz Competition on Monday, 3rd November. The competition will take place in the school auditorium and is open to all students. The quiz will start promptly at 9 am, so please arrive on time to ensure a smooth and focused event. Let\'s show our support for our budding quiz champions as they compete for victory!\n\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64471,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement concerning a Story-telling Competition.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">The school has organized a Story-telling Competition for Classes V, VI and VII from 21 March to 24 March, 2020. The competition will be held in the school hall at 10a.m.\nStudents may select -\n1. A fable\n2. A true story\n3. A folk tale\n4. A funny story\n5. A fairy tale\n6. A horror story\nThree prizes will be given for each category. The time limit is 3-5 minutes. The ones who wish to participate may contact Mrs. Saloni Nagar for more details. They must register their names with her before 15 March, 2020.\n\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64472,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement regarding new timings of the school.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">Kindly note that with effect from the next Monday, 2nd January 2023, the school will function from 8:30 a.m. to 2:00 p.m. from Monday to Friday, instead of the regular timings of 8:30 a.m. to 12:30 p.m. The additional hours of work are mandatory as per the instructions received from the Department of Education.\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64473,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement regarding postponement of Unit Test.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">Due to circumstances beyond our control, the Second Unit Test which was to begin from 3rd January has been postponed to 7th January. The revised timetable will be displayed on the Notice Board shortly.\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64474,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement regarding payment of Class Excursion money.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">This announcement is for all the students of Classes 7 and 8 only. All those who have brought money for the Class Excursion may please excuse themselves from the class and come down to the School Office and pay the same.\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64475,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement of SSC results.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">Good morning, students! This is your Principal speaking. It gives me great pleasure announce that the School has obtained cent per cent results at the SSC examination of March 2022.\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64477,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement at a railway station regarding the delay in train services.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">Commuters travelling northwards, please pay attention. Due to a technical snag, all northbound trains are running 15 to 20 minutes behind the schedule. The inconvenience caused to you is deeply regretted.\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64478,2117,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare an announcement at a football tournament regarding a lost mobile phone.','','','<div style=\"border:3px solid black; padding: 20px; margin: 20px; border-radius: 10px\">Dear viewers and players, your attention please. A mobile phone has been found. Anyone who has lost it may please claim it from the Reception desk after identification of the same.\n</div>','',0,0,0,NULL),(64502,2121,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write autobiography on I AM AN UMBRELLA.','','','                                                  <b>I AM AN UMBRELLA</b>\nI am an umbrella, I am not one of those new folding umbrellas, but the old-fashioned, non folding type. I am very proud of this, because I am very sturdy and strong. I do not break easily.\nI was made in a factory in Pune. Then I was packed in a large box with some others and sent to a department store in Mumbai. I hardly had settled down on the display rack when I was picked up by an old gentleman. He opened and closed me several times and then looked at my price. This gentleman bought me immediately.\nMy owner took me wherever he went. He used me as protection from the sun as well as the rain. He looked after me carefully, and never forgot me anywhere.\nI am two years old now and he still takes me with him wherever he goes. Of course, he sometimes uses me as a walking stick, but I don\'t mind that. I love him and I will serve him as long as I can.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64503,2121,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write autobiography on I AM A SCHOOL BAG.','','','                                        <b>I AM A SCHOOL BAG</b>\nI am a multi-coloured attractive school bag. Two years ago I was presented to you on your birthday. You looked at my bright red-and-blue colour and exclaimed with delight, \"It\'s a wonderful bag.\" Since then I have been your constant companion.\nI am made of good strong leather. The buckles on my side are still shiny. Of course, I know that you take good care of me. You have never allowed me to get wet in the rain or get dirty. During holidays, you keep me carefully in your cupboard.\nI feel lonely on holidays, but I take it as a time to rest. I look forward to the day when you will go to school again.\nEach evening as I hang on a peg in the wall I look at you while you are doing your homework. I fondly smile at you and think what a good master I have!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64504,2121,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write autobiography on I AM A THOUSAND RUPEE NOTE.','','','                            <b> I AM A THOUSAND RUPEE NOTE</b>\nToday, I am a thousand-rupee note and command quite a lot of respect in society. I am at present sitting comfortably in the drawer of a jeweller. I was given, along with hundreds of others like me, to the jeweller, in return for a diamond necklace, by a very stylish lady.\nI remember my early days. I was originally a part of a tree. I was cut down, processed, converted to paper, and sent to the press. A lot of things were printed on me. I was checked and rechecked and then sent amidst great security to a bank. The stylish lady took me from the bank and gave me to the jeweller; where I now am, whiling away my time. I wonder what Is my future?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64505,2121,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an Autobiography of a Bird.','','','                                        <b>An Autobiography of a Bird</b>\nI am the Peacock, India\'s cherished national bird, and this is my life\'s journey I was born in the forests of Assam, surrounded by greenery. From the start, I had beautiful feathers, shimmering with different d As I grew up, I learned to find food and stay safe. Even though I felt sad when I saw my friends the crow eagle fly high in the sky, what I loved most was dancing. I\'d spread my feathers and dance to find a saw the seasons change hot summers, rainy monsoons and cool winters. My calls announced the arrival of rain and new life. Being India\'s national bind made me proud. I represented India\'s culture and nature.\nNow, as I grow old, my feathers lose their shine, but I still roam lndia\'s lands. I\'m a symbol of India\'s beauty and diversity, a reminder that nature is precious.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64506,2121,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an Autobiography of a School Uniform.','','','                           <b>An Autobiography of a School Uniform</b>\nI am a school uniform and my story is simple yet meaningful. I was born in a busy factory, carefully stitched to fit a student\'s frame perfectly. My purpose was clear to be worn daily.\nEach morning, my master would put me on, their face filled with anticipation for the day ahead. I became a part of their everyday life, joining them through lessons, play and countless memories. 1 eagerly awaited holidays when I could rest in the closet, knowing my owner would return with exciting tales of adventures.\nThe best part of my journey was the bond with my young master. I felt his joy, provided comfort during exams and celebrated their successes. I am a humble uniform, but I am proud to have been a loyal companion on this wonderful journey of learning and exploration, standing with my master every step of the way.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64507,2121,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an Autobiography of School Lunch Box.','','','                     <b>An Autobiography of a School Lunch Box</b>\nI am a simple school lunch box, and this is my life\'s story in easy words. I was made in a factory, shaped, and painted with bright colors. My journey began when a young student carefully selected me from a stationery store. From that moment, I knew my mission was to help this student in his academic journey. \nMy owner decorated me with stickers and drawings, making me unique. My days were spent carrying sandwiches, fruits and snacks to school. I also remember the delicious aromas that filled me each morning. from the peanut butter and jelly sandwiches to the crispy apple slices and chocolate chip cookies. Those scents made every day a little brighter. I saw my owner make friends and enjoy lunch breaks. Sometimes, I got dropped and dented by him, but the parents fixed me with care.\nNow, I\'m getting old, and my edges are frayed. I know my journey is almost over But I\'m happy because t did my job, helping kids have lunch at school and making them smile.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64508,2121,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an autobiography of a hundred-rupee note. (8-10 lines)','','','            <b>Autobiography of a Hundred-Rupee Note</b>\nI am a hundred rupee note the one that is found in every wallet, closet, treasure and chest pocket of a human. Some people think that I am useful and some think I am useless. Some people take care of me and some don\'t. Some people collect me and some people spend me.\nMy commercial journey started from The State Bank of Nasik from where a hand unceremoniously picked me up out of the box and placed me on the counter along with ninety nine of my brothers who were in the bundle with me. Unlike humans, I am not a standing sculpture of stunning height, rippling muscles and distinct looks, but a common currency note that has the same dimensions, shade and aspects.\nWhen I descend from the currency press in a pack of 100 siblings, I measure 66 mm x 142 mm with a unique serial number consisting seven numbers and two letters which alone is my identification.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64509,2121,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write an autobiography of a Coin.','','','                                     <b>An Autobiography of a Coin</b>\nI was born in a mint in Delhi many years ago. I was brand new then and shone like a star, on my face was impressed the effigy of the King and on my other side, I was stamped with my value. I was then, thrown into a big sack with lots of others like me. One day, I was sent off with my friends to the bank. I lay there in an old iron box for many days.\nOne morning, I was paid over the counter along with others to a gentleman. I went off jingling in his pocket but I was not there for long, as he gave me to a shopkeeper the very same day. The shopkeeper looked pleased to get such a shiny, new coin. Then he put me into a drawer, with a lot of other coins. Whenever he opened the drawer, I could see hundreds of things displayed on the shelves.\nI was just getting used to my surroundings when someone picked me up and pushed me into his pocket. To my horror, I discovered that the pocket had a hole, and before long, I slipped, fell and found myself lying on a dirty street covered with dust. People stepped all over me with big, heavy boots without realizing that I was even there. I cried out in pain but nobody took any notice of me at all.\nFinally, a little boy playing on the streets spotted me, and his eyes lit when he held and examined me. I knew I had made him happy. Just that thought wiped away all my earlier pain and discomfort. In a strange way, I realized I was needed, so I stayed on with the little boy who kept me in a tiny little box. He would open the box, pick me up and look at me every day. Then once again, he would put me back into the box and close it.\nOne day, my owner, who had now grown up, realized that the marks and writings on me were too faded for me to be considered authentic. So I was taken back to the bank and exchanged with a brand new coin, just like I used to be many years ago. Now, I am back to where I had started my journey from, waiting to be taken back to the mint.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64514,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech on \'My Favourite Place\'.','','','                                    <b> MY FAVOURITE PLACE</b> \nDear Friends,\nI would like to tell you something about my favourite place. My favourite place is Goa. I go there every year. Of course, the main attraction is that my grandparents live there, and I am pampered by them when I go there in the vacations. My grandmother makes lovely food, and I get to eat a lot of delicacies. My grandfather takes me to the temples that Goa is famous for, as well as on long motorcycle rides through the picturesque, winding roads. My grandparents\' house is near the beach, so I can go and play in the water daily.\nIn addition to this, the people of Goa are always very relaxed and happy. They are also very friendly. There is no hurry and scurry like we see here in Mumbai. I can get up whenever I like and go to bed whenever I like. I also like the beautiful and serene churches of Goa. Sometimes I wish I could always live there.\nThank you.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64517,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech on \'An inspiring incident in the life of a great leader\'.','','','          <b>AN INSPIRING INCIDENT IN THE LIFE OF A GREAT LEADER</b>\nDear Friends,\nWe have all heard the name \'Baba Amte\'. Baba Amte was a great social leader. He worked for the leprosy-afflicted people, and set up many centers and homes for them.\nBut what was it that inspired him to do this? There is a story behind this. Young Murlidhar, which was Baba\'s name, was leading a comfortable life when a chance visit to his father\'s vast estate brought about a dramatic change in his life. One rainy day, as he was going about some work, he noticed a pile of clothes near a gutter. Prompted by curiosity, Murlidhar went near it. A terrible sight met his eyes. He saw a struggling leprosy patient in the final stages of the disease. The sight haunted him for long, and made him determined to do welfare work for leprosy patients. Today, thousands of patients are leading their lives with dignity because of Baba Amte\'s efforts. And the short and sad incident that I narrated was the incident which changed his life.\nThank you.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64519,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech on \'How I accomplished a difficult task.\'','','','                       <b> HOW I ACCOMPLISHED A DIFFICULT TASK</b> \nDear Friends,\nYou may be surprised when I tell you this, but the most difficult task that I accomplished was learning how to swim.\nYes, I know, some will ask me what is difficult about it. Perhaps it may not be difficult for others, but it was difficult for me, as I was afraid of water. Two years ago, before I knew any swimming, a naughty friend had pushed me into a pool, and I had nearly drowned. Since then I was afraid of water. But my parents had a long talk with me a couple of months back. They told me that the best way to overcome any fear was to face it head on. I had to learn swimming. They got a good coach for me; initially 1 swam only in the shallow water, then slowly my coach led me to the deep water. I was frightened for the first few days; then I began to enjoy it. I now realize that when we come across something difficult, we must not run away but face the difficulty bravely. We will then succeed.\nThank you.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64522,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','On the Occasion of National Sports Day, prepare and present a speech on My Favourite Sportsperson\'. Use the following points.\n*Name of the person\n*Their qualities\n*Any special incident about them\n*The reason why you like him her\n','','','Good morning, respected teachers and friends!\nOn the occasion of National Sports Day, I am thrilled in talk about my favorite sports personality. Major Dhyan Chand. He was an exceptional hockey player from India whose outstanding qualities have always made him my sporting hero.\nDhyan Chand\'s mastery of the game was nothing short of magical. His incredible dribbling and passing skills allowed his team to score goals effortlessly. Even when his spiked shoes hampered his mobility on the muddy ground, he fearlessly played barefoot\nOne special incident that stands out is Dhyan Chand\'s refusal to play for Germany, despite a tempting offer from Adolf Hitler himself. This demonstrated his unwavering loyalty to his motherland, India. Today we celebrate his birthday on August 29 as National Sports Day honoring his immense contributions to Indian sports.\nIn conclusion, Dhyan Chand is not just a sports hero but also a symbol of unwavering dedication and national pride. I hope that his story inspires many of us to follow in his footsteps and make on country proud.\nThank you for listening\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64525,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','On the Occasion of Teachers Day, prepare and present a speech expressing gratitude to all the teachers and highlighting their importance.','','','Good morning, respected teachers and friends!\nToday, on Teacher\'s Day, I want to express my heartfelt gratitude to our wonderful teachers and talk about why they are so important.\nTeachers are like guiding stars in our lives. They show us the path to knowledge and help us grow They teach us not only subjects but also valuable life lessons. Every day, they come to school with a smile to make sure we learn and become better individuals.\nTeachers are patient and caring. They answer our questions, even the silly ones, and never give up de us. They believe in us, even when we doubt ourselves. They are like a lighthouse in the sea of life, helping us navigate through challenges and find our way. They inspire us to dream big and work hard to achieve our goals.\nSo, on this special day, let\'s take a moment to thank our teachers for their dedication, love and hard work. They play a vital role in shaping our future, and we are lucky to have them. Thank you, teachers, for all that you do. Happy Teacher\'s Day!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64527,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech on keeping our surroundings and environment clean.\nUse the following points.\n*Need for cleanliness\n*Measures for cleanliness\n*Appeal to support the cause\n','','','Good morning, respected teachers and dear friends,\nToday, I want to talk about something very important keeping our surroundings and environment clean. It\'s something we all can do to make our world a better place.\nFirst, let me tell you why it\'s crucial. A clean environment means clean air to breathe, clean water drink, and a beautiful place to live. It keeps us healthy and happy.\nWe can start by not throwing litter on the ground. Instead, we should use dustbins to dispose of our trash. Also, we should reduce, reuse, and recycle things whenever we can. This means using things again instead of throwing them away. Another essential thing is planting trees. Trees give us fresh air to breathe and shade to stay cool in the hot sun. We should also save water by not wasting it. Turn off the tap while brushing your teeth and fix any leaking pipes.\nIf we all work together, we can keep our surroundings clean and protect our environment for ourselves and future generations. So, let\'s take care of our world, starting today! Thank you for listening.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64528,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech on The Importance of Reading Books.','','','Good morning, respected teachers and dear friends,\nToday, I want to talk about something really important the value of reading books. Books are le magical doors that open up a world of knowledge and imagination.\nBooks take us on fun adventures to different places and introduce us to interesting characters. We visit amazing lands, meet heroes, and learn about various cultures, all while sitting comfortably at home. But books do more than entertain us, they teach us valuable life lessons. They show us what\'s right and wrong the importance of being kind, and how to be brave.\nSo, my friends, let\'s read every day, whether it\'s a storybook, a comic or something else. Reading makes us smarter, more creative and better people. Let\'s open these magical book doors and go on countless adventures.\nRemember, reading is a ticket to endless journeys and knowledge. Happy reading!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64531,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech on Independence Day.','','','Honourable Principal, Respected Teachers and my fellow students, I am Sneha Patil It is a great privilege and honour to stand before you to express myself on the occasion of 15 August, which we celebrate as our Independence Day. Independence is a luxury, though few of us think of it as one. We all know that under the East India Company, freedom was a distant dream. Our steadfast determination turned it into a reality. Today, we are here to celebrate 67 years of our country\'s Independence.\nOn 15 August 1947, Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru made a speech that still echoes in the mind of every patriot. He said that independence was our first step towards a long journey. This goal of his is shouldered by the youth of present times. We have the strength of a glorious past, a progressive present and a promising future. We have to make our new society free, fearless, and self-respecting. World famous Indians like Amartya Sen, Kalpana Chawla. Sunita Williams, Lakshmi Mittal, Ratan Tata and the Ambani brothers are the faces of this transition.\nIt is up to us to raise the banner and preserve our heritage. Through every act of patriotism, may we pay the heartfelt tribute to those faceless millions who died so that we could live in an independent India. To our country\'s past, present, future and its sacrifice, valour, peace and integrity, we Indians salute our Tiranga. Jai Hind, Jai Bharat!\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64532,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Earth\'s rotation takes 24 hours. In one hour, ________ meridians pass in front of the Sun.\n(A) 05   (B) 10   (C) 15   (D) 20','','','(C) 15\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64534,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','To understand the difference in local time between any two places on Earth, one must know the ________.\n(A) Noon time of both places              (B) Angular distance between the meridians                 (C) Difference in standard time                 (D) Change in international date line','','','(A) Noon time of both places\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64535,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a speech on Teachers\' Day.','','','Respected Madam Principal, beloved teachers and my dear friends, I, Shikha Rajput, am honoured to be chosen to speak before you on this special occasion when we fondly remember our former Vice-President, Dr. Sarvepalli Radhakrishnan. He had dedicated his birthday to the noble teachers who are the backbone of any nation. He himself was a teacher by profession. He is the only teacher who was made to sit in a highly decorated cart, which was pulled by his students till the railway station and seen off when he was promoted as the Vice-Chancellor of the Banaras University. Such was the respect that his students held for him.\nYes, our teachers also deserve such kind of respect and treatment. They know every student equally well alike any parent knows his/her child. They take away ignorance, and bring light into our lives. To such great personalities, who render selfless service to the society, I pay my due respect! They enforce discipline and inculcate several life-related values in us\nOn behalf of all my fellow-students, I thank all the teachers for their unconditional love for us and devotion to help us evolve for our development. I salute and thank one and all to hear me out. Thank you.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64536,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' The local time difference between any two adjacent meridians is ________.\n(A) 15 minutes   (B) 04 minutes   (C) 30 minutes   (D) 60 minutes','','','(B) 04 minutes\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64538,2123,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a speech on My Favourite Place\'.','','','Dear friends,\nA favourite place is one that has impressed you in many ways. And for me, it is the Taj Mahal in Agra. The first time I visited the Taj a few years ago, I was speechless\nRightfully among one of the seven wonders, the Taj is the most famous historical monument in India. It stands outside the city of Agra, on the banks of the river Yamuna. Built on a raised platform, its slim minarets are simply beautiful. It was built by Shah Jahan, the famous Mughal emperor, in memory of his beloved wife, Mumtaz Mahal. Their marble tombs are locatedin the middle section of the main building and are inlaid with precious stones. The walls are inlaid with coloured gemstones in detailed designs of flowers and leaves.\nThe magnificence of the Taj mesmerizes you. I remember leaving the Taj with a heavy heart. Such is the beauty of the marvelous Taj!\nThank you.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64540,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The world is divided into ________ time zones.\n(A) 360   (B) 24   (C) 100   (D) 365','','','(B) 24\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64541,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Indian Standard Time is ________ ahead of Greenwich Mean Time.\n(A) 5 hours 30 minutes ahead   (B) 3 hours 50 minutes ahead   (C) 5 hours 30 minutes behind   (D) 3 hours 50 minutes behind','','','(A) 5 hours 30 minutes ahead\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64543,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Earth\'s rotation occurs…\n(A) From east to west  (B) From north to south  (C) From south to north  (D) From west to east','','',' (D) From west to east\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64545,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','As the Sun rises and climbs in the sky, the shadow…\n(A) Becomes shorter  (B) Becomes longer  (C) Remains the same  (D) Extends far','','','A) Becomes shorter\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64547,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which regions can the duration of daylight be more than 24 hours seasonally?\n(A) Between 50° N/S of Equator  (B) Between Tropic of Cancer and Tropic of Capricorn  (C) Between 23°30′ and 66°30′  (D) From Arctic/Antarctic Circle to the Pole','','','(D) From Arctic/Antarctic Circle to the Pole\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64548,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' The time determined by the Sun\'s position in the sky at a specific place is called…\n(A) Standard time  (B) Local time  (C) Local time at Greenwich  (D) Universal time','','','B) Local time\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64549,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The local time at a place is determined with reference to…\n(A) Sunrise  (B) Sunset  (C) Noon  (D) Midnight','','','(C) Noon\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64550,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The time at a meridian to the ________ of any meridian is ahead.\n(A) North  (B) South  (C) East  (D) West','','','(C) East\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64551,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The difference in local time between two countries is measured with reference to…\n(A) Local time of the place  (B) Indian Standard Time  (C) Universal Standard Time  (D) Sun\'s position','','','(C) Universal Standard Time\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64552,1988,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' Indian Standard Time is…\n(A) 5 hours 30 minutes behind GMT  (B) 2 hours 40 minutes behind GMT  (C) 5 hours 30 minutes ahead of GMT  (D) 3 hours ahead of GMT','','','(C) 5 hours 30 minutes ahead of GMT\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64582,1990,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cumulonimbus clouds are the indicators of the pleasant atmosphere.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: Cumulonimbus clouds are the indicators of the thunderstorm.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64583,1990,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The relative humidity is found to be high in desert region.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: The relative humidity is found to be less in desert region.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64584,1990,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The high temperature of the air leads to rapid evaporation.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64585,1990,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When the humidity in the air is 0 gm/cu.m. at any temperature, the air is said to be humid.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: When the humidity in the air is 0 gm/cu.m. at any temperature, the air is said to be dry.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64586,1990,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cirrostratus appears like a bedsheet with wrinkles. ','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(64587,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Why is the air in a region dry?','','','Due to the low amount of the moisture in the air, the air in a region is dry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64588,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','What are the prerequisites for condensation?','','','High relative humidity and dew point temperature of the air are the prerequisites for condensation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64589,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','On what does the percentage of relative humidity depend?','','','The percentage of relative humidity depends on the absolute humidity and vapour-holding capacity of the air at a given temperature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64590,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Which type of clouds give rain?','','','Nimbostratus and cumulonimbus type of clouds give rain.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64591,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is evaporation?','','','The process of converting water into steam or water vapour is called evaporation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64592,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by the moisture-holding capacity of air?','','','The capacity of air to hold moisture at a given temperature, is called the moisture-holding capacity of air.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64593,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by the saturation of the air?','','','The condition of air at a certain temperature, in which the moisture-holding capacity of air becomes equal to the proportion of moisture present in it, is called saturation of the air.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64597,1990,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns and complete the chain :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>\'A\' Column</th>\n    <th>\'B\' Column</th>\n<th>\'C\' Column</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Cirrus</td>\n    <td>i) Vertical extent in the sky</td>\n<td>a) Roaring clouds</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2) Cumulonimbus</td>\n<td>ii) Higher altitude</td>\n<td>b) Floating clouds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3) Nimbostratus</td>\n<td>iii) Medium altitude</td>\n<td>c) Continuous rainfall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4) Alto-cumulus</td>\n<td>iv) Low altitude</td>\n<td>d) Snowflake clouds</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','','','1) Cirrus - Higher altitude - Snowflake clouds.\n2) Cumulonimbus - Vertical extent in the sky - Roaring clouds.\n3) Nimbostratus - Low altitude - Continuous rainfall.\n4) Alto-cumulus - Medium altitude - Floating clouds.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64600,1990,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','When the temperature of the air is 30° C, its vapour-holding capacity is 30.37 gms/cu.m., If absolute humidity is 18 gms/cu.m., then what will be the relative humidity?','','','Relative humidity (%)  = <math xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\'> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> Absolute humidity </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> Vapour-holding capacity  </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 100 </mn> </math>\n        = <math xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\'> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 18.00 </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 30.37 </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 100 </mn> </math> = 59.26%','',0,0,0,NULL),(64601,1990,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What would be the absolute humidity of air if 1 cu.m. air contains 4.08 gms of vapour at 0° C temperature.','','','Absolute humidity = <math xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\'> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> Mass of water vapour </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> Volume of air  </mn></math> \n   = <math xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\'> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> 4.08 </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> 1 </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </math> = 4.08 gm/ m³ ','',0,0,0,NULL),(64604,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','How is humidity measured?','','','1) The amount of water vapour in 1 cu.m. of air is called the absolute humidity.\n2) Absolute humidity is measured with the help of the following formula : \nAbsolute humidity  = <math xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\'> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> Mass of water vapour  </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> Volume of air </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> </math>\n3) The amount of water vapour present in air expressed as a percentage of the amount needed for saturation at the same temperature is called relative humidity.\n4) Relative humidity is measured with the help of the following formula :\nRelative humidity (%) = <math xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\'> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> Absolute humidity  </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> Vapour-holding capacity  </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 100 </mn> </math>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64608,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What is a cloud? Write its types.','','','A) Meaning: A visible mass of fine particles of ice and water accumulated around the dust particles in the air at high altitude is called a cloud.\nB) Types: The following are the types of clouds:\n1) High clouds: Clouds at an altitude of 7000 m to 14000 m are considered as high clouds. Cirrus, Cirro-Stratus and Cirro-Cumulus are high clouds.\n2) Medium clouds: Clouds at an altitude of 2000 m to 7000 m are considered as medium clouds. Alto-Stratus and Alto-Cumulus are medium clouds.\n3) Low clouds: Clouds at an altitude of less than 2000m are considered as low clouds. Strato-Cumulus, Stratus, Nimbostratus, Cumulus and Cumulonimbus are low clouds.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64609,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about the features of high clouds.','','','Clouds at an altitude of 7000 m to 14000 m are considered as high clouds. High clouds are mainly made up of ice particles. Cirrus, Cirro-Stratus and Cirro-Cumulus are high clouds. The following are the features of high clouds :\n1) Cirrus: Cirrus are wispy.\n2) Cirro-Stratus: Cirro-Stratus look like bed sheet with wrinkles. A halo is generally seen around these clouds.\n3) Cirro-Cumulus clouds: Cirro-Cumulus look like groups of small waves.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64610,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about the features of medium clouds.','','','Clouds at an altitude of 2000 m to 7000 m are considered as medium clouds. Alto-Stratus and Alto-Cumulus are medium clouds. The following are the features of medium clouds :1) Alto-Stratus: Alto-Stratus are comparatively thin. The sun is visible through these clouds as if seen through a milky glass.\n2) Alto-Cumulus: Alto-Cumulus are in the form of layers. They have wave-like structure. They are white in colour and have a grey shade\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64611,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about the features of Strato-Cumulus Stratus and , Nimbostratus.','','','The features of Strato-Cumulus, Stratus and Nimbostratus are as follows:\n1) Strato-Cumulus: Strato-Cumulus have layers. These clouds are mostly seen in round clusters. They are white to earthy in colour.\n2) Stratus: Stratus are found in layers. They have uniform base. They are ash coloured.\n3) Nimbostratus: Nimbostratus have thick layers. They are greyish in colour. They cause continuous rainfall. They are also responsible for snowfall.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64612,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about the features of Cumulus and Cumulonimbus.','','','The features of Cumulus and Cumulonimbus are as follows:\n1) Cumulus: Cumulus are found between 500 m to 6000 m altitude. They are mostly formed due to the vertical flow of the air. They have huge size and dome-like shape. They are grey in colour. They indicate pleasant atmosphere. With an increase in the vertical expanse, these clouds turn into cumulonimbus clouds and bring rain.\n2) Cumulonimbus: Cumulonimbus appear like huge mountain. They are dense and dark in colour. They have anvil-like shape at the top. They are the indicators of thunder, lightning and storm. They bring rain with storm. Sometimes they also bring hailstones. The raindrops of these clouds are found to be larger in size.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64615,1990,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Clouds float in the sky.','','','1) The condensed water or fine particles of snow accumulate around dust particles at high altitude leading to formation of clouds.\n2) The condensed water or fine particles in the clouds are almost weightless.\nTherefore, clouds float in the sky.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64617,1990,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The proportion of relative humidity changes according to altitude.','','','1) The temperature is found to be comparatively high in the areas at sea level. Due to high temperature, moisture-holding capacity of air near sea level is found to be high. Therefore, the humidity of the air at sea level is comparatively high.\n2) The temperature is found to be comparatively low in the areas at high altitude. Due to low temperature, moisture-holding capacity of air at high altitude is found to be low. Therefore, the humidity of the air at high altitude is comparatively low. In this way, the proportion of relative humidity changes according to the altitude.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64618,1990,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Air becomes saturated.','','','1) At certain temperature, the moisture-holding capacity of air gets fulfilled and it becomes equal to the proportion of the moisture present in it.\n2) At this state, no more moisture can be absorbed by the air. Thus, air becomes saturated.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64620,1990,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Cumulus clouds into change cumulonimbus clouds. ','','','1) Cumulus clouds are found between 500 m to 6000 m altitude. They are mostly formed due to the vertical flow of the air.\n2) Sometimes, the vertical expanse of the cumulus clouds increases and it leads to formation of huge mountain-like cumulonimbus clouds. Thus, cumulus clouds change into cumulonimbus clouds.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64621,1990,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The absolute humidity of air in coastal region is higher than humidity of air in inland areas.','','','1) The rate of evaporation is found to be high in the coastal region. As its effect, the amount of moisture in air in coastal region is found to be high. This results in high absolute humidity of air in coastal region.\n2) On the other hand, the rate of evaporation is found to be low in the inland areas. As its effect, the amount of moisture in air in inland regions is found to be low. This results in low absolute humidity of air in inland areas. Thus, the absolute humidity of air in coastal region is higher than humidity of air in inland areas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64623,1990,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The absolute humidity of air in equatorial region is higher than humidity of air in polar region.','','','1) Equatorial region receives perpendicular sunrays throughout the year leading to high temperature. High temperature increases the rate of evaporation and this further leads to high absolute humidity of air in equatorial region.\n2) Polar regions receive extremely slanted sunrays throughout the year leading to very low temperature. Very low temperature decreases the rate of evaporation and this further leads to very low absolute humidity of air in polar region. Thus, the absolute humidity of air in equatorial region is higher than humidity of air in polar region.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64624,1990,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Damp air is found in the coastal regions.','','','1) The rate of evaporation is found to be high in the coastal region. As its effect, the amount of moisture in air in coastal region is found to be high.\n2) The absolute humidity of the air near coastal region is found to be high. Therefore, damp air is found in the coastal regions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64625,1990,'descriptive','2b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dry air is found in the inland regions.','','','1) The rate of evaporation is found to be low in inland region. As its effect, the amount of moisture in air in inland region is found to be low.\n2) The absolute humidity of the air in inland region is found to be low. Therefore, dry air is found in inland regions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64629,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Differentiate between the following :  Humidity and clouds','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Humidity</th>\n    <th>Clouds</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) The amount of water vapour present in the air is called humidity.</td>\n    <td>A mass of fine particles of ice and water accumulated around the dust particles in the air at high altitude is called a cloud</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2) Humidity is in the invisible form. </td>\n<td>Clouds are in the visible form.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64630,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Differentiate between the following : Relative humidity and Absolute humidity','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Relative Humidity</th>\n    <th>Absolute humidity</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) The amount of water vapour present in air expressed as a percentage of the amount needed for saturation at the same temperature is called relative humidity. </td>\n    <td>The amount of water vapour in 1 cu.m. of air is called the absolute humidity.</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2) Relative humidity (%) = <math xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\'> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> Absolute humidity  </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> Vapour-holding capacity </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> × </mo> <mn> 100 </mn> </math> </td>\n<td>Absolute humidity = <math xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\'> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn> Mass of water vapour  </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> Volume of air</mn> </mrow> </mfrac> math> </td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64632,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Differentiate between the following : Cumulus clouds and Cumulonimbus clouds','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Cumulus clouds</th>\n    <th>Cumulonimbus clouds</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) The clouds that are found from 500 m to 6000 m altitude having comparatively less vertical expanse are called cumulus clouds.</td>\n    <td>The clouds that are found from 500 m to 6000 m altitude having comparatively more vertical expanse are called cumulonimbus clouds.</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2) Cumulus clouds are the indicators of pleasant weather. </td>\n<td>Cumulonimbus clouds are the indicators of thunderstorm.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64633,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Differentiate between the following : Densification and Sublimation ','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Densification</th>\n    <th>Sublimation</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) The process of changing of water vapour in the air into liquid state, i.e. water is called densification.</td>\n    <td>The process of changing water vapour in the air into solid state, i.e. snow is called sublimation.</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2) Densification leads to precipitation on the surface of the earth </td>\n<td>Sublimation leads to snowfall on the surface surface of the earth.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64638,1990,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Study given figure and answer the following questions :','1) What is the maximum diameter of the raindrop?\n2) Around what does the condensation of the water vapour in the air occur?\n','','1) The maximum diameter of the raindrop is 5 mm.\n2) The condensation of the water vapour in the air occurs around the minute particles of dust or salt in the air.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(64639,1990,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the given figure and answer the following questions:','1) Which type of charge is found at the upper end of the cumulonimbus clouds?\n2) Which type of charge is found at the low end of the cumulonimbus clouds?\n3) Which type of charge is found on the land below the cumulonimbus clouds?\n','','1) Positive charge is found at the upper end of the cumulonimbus clouds.\n2) Negative charge is found at the low end of the cumulonimbus clouds.\n3) Positive charge is found on the land below the cumulonimbus clouds.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(64641,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','In which season is humidity generally more?','','','Humidity is generally more in monsoon.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64642,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','How does the humidity affect the human body?','','','The rate of respiration and sweating increases due to humidity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64643,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Observe how humidity affects the food materials at our home and write about the same.','','','Due to humidity, fungus flourish on food materials at our home and it gets spoiled.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64644,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Is there any relationship between formation of fungus and humidity?','','','There is a direct relationship between formation of fungus and humidity. Humidity supports the growth of fungus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64645,1990,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','How is the early or late drying up of clothes related to humidity?','','','As the moisture-holding capacity is found to be high in low humidity air, it leads to early drying up of clothes. As the moisture-holding capacity is found to be low in high humidity air, it leads to late drying up of clothes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64646,1990,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What will happen if the temperature of saturated air at 20° C drops to 10° C abruptly?','','','1) If the temperature of saturated air at 20° C drops to 10° C abruptly, the relative humidity of the air will increase abruptly.\n2) Rise in the relative humidity will lead to condensation or sublimation of water vapour in the air. This in turn, will lead to precipitation or snowfall.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64702,1991,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Abyssal plain lies beyond the continental shelf.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: Abyssal plain lies beyond the continental slope.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64703,1991,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Around 51 per cent of the surface of the earth is covered by water.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: Around 71 per cent of the surface of the earth is covered by water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64704,1991,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Natural gas and mineral oil can be obtained from the continental shelf.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64705,1991,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For survey purpose in India, the height of sea level at Kanyakumari is considered to be zero.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: For survey purpose in India, the height of sea level at Chennai is considered to be zero.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64706,1991,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Active volcanoes are mostly seen in marine trenches.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64708,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called continental shelf?','','','The land near the coast and submerged under the sea is called continental shelf.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64709,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called continental slope?','','','The steeper slope of the sea bed beyond the extent of continental shelf is called continental slope.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64710,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called abyssal plain?','','','The flat part of the sea bed that lies beyond the continental slope is called abyssal plain.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64711,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called submerged hills?','','','The hundreds of kilometres wide and thousands of kilometres long hills found on the ocean bed are called submerged hills.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64713,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called marine islands?','','','Peaks of some of the submerged hills that come above the sea level are called marine islands.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64714,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called submarine plateau?','','','The flat and extensive summit of marine island is called submarine plateau.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64715,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called marine deep?','','','The landforms on the ocean bed that are comparatively less extending and shallower ones are called marine deep.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64716,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called marine trench?','','','The landforms on the ocean bed that are comparatively more extending and deeper ones are called marine trench.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64721,1991,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns and complete the chain :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>\'A\' Column</th>\n    <th>\'B\' Column</th>\n<th>\'C\' Column</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Andaman Nicobar</td>\n    <td>i) Submarine plateau</td>\n<td>a) Pacific Ocean</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) Chagos</td>\n    <td>Located on continental shelf</td>\n<td>Bay of Bengal</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) Mariana</td>\n    <td>Islands</td>\n<td>Arabian Sea</td>\n  </tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4) Mumbai High</td>\n<td>Marine deep</td>\n<td>Indian Ocean</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','','','1) Andaman -Nicobar - Islands - Bay of Bengal.\n2) Chagos - Submarine plateau - Indian Ocean.\n3) Mariana - Marine deep - Pacific Ocean\n4) Mumbai High - Located on continental shelf - Arabian Sea.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64725,1991,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is marine deposits?','','','1) Marine deposit is a type of marine deposition.\n2) The rivers and glaciers flowing from the continent bring pebbles, clay, soil, etc. with them when they meet sea or ocean.\n3) These materials get deposited on the continental shelf. The deposits are known as marine deposits.\n4) Due to pressure of sea water and deposition of layers over layers of sediments, sedimentary rocks are formed.\n5) Marine life and the availability of minerals in the sea bed can be studied with the help of marine deposits.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64726,1991,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is marine oozes?','','','1) Marine oozes is a type of marine deposit.\n2) Lava and ash erupting out of volcano, fine soil particles, remains of plants and animals, etc. get deposited on marine abyssal plains. Deposits of these materials on the abyssal plains are known as marine oozes.\n3) Marine oozes are in the form of fine clay. The percentage of remains of plants and animals in marine oozes is up to 30 per cent.\n4) Marine oozes are also useful to study marine life and the availability of minerals in the sea bed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64727,1991,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about the continental shelf.','','','1) The land near the seacoast and submerged under the sea is called continental shelf. The depth of continental shelf is about 200 metres below the sea level.\n2) Continental shelf is found to be narrow along the coasts of some continents and broad along the coasts of some other continents.\n3) Being a shallowest part of the ocean bed, sunlight reaches up to continental shelf easily. As its effect, the food for fish like algae, plankton, etc. grows on a large scale on continental shelf. Therefore, continental shelf is useful for fishing occupation.\n4) Various minerals, natural gas and mineral oil, etc. can also be obtained from mining the continental shelf. For example, Mumbai High located on the continental shelf of the Arabian Sea is a source from where the mineral oil and natural gas is obtained.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64729,1991,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about the continental slope.','','','1) The steep slope of the sea bed beyond the extent of continental shelf is called continental slope.\n2) The depth of the continental slope is from 200 metres to 3600 metres below the sea level.\n3) The continental slope is found to be comparatively narrower.\n4) The lower boundary of the continental slope is considered as the boundary of continent.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64730,1991,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about the abyssal plains.','','','1) The flat part of the sea bed that lies beyond the continental slope is called abyssal plains.\n2) Various submerged landforms like submerged hills, submerged mountains, etc. are seen on abyssal plains.\n3) Submarine plateaus are also found on the abyssal plains.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64731,1991,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about mean sea level.','','','1) The average of the highest high tide and the lowest low tide is considered to calculate the mean sea level.\n2) This average is considered to be zero sea level.\n3) The altitude of any place above the sea level is measured and expressed in positive value. (For example, Mount Everest is 8848 metres high.)\n4) The depth of any place below the sea level is measured and expressed in negative value. (For example, Mariana Trench is 11034 metres below sea level.)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64733,1991,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The study of ocean floor is useful to man.','','','1) Various minerals, rocks, fine soil particles are found on the sea floor.\n2) Remains of marine plants and animals are also found on the ocean floor.\n3) To study the marine life, mineral wealth as well as the process of volcanic eruption, the study of ocean floor is useful to man.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64734,1991,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The continental shelf is a paradise for fishing activity.','','','1) Being a shallowest part of the ocean bed, sunlight reaches up to continental shelf easily.\n2) As its effect, algae, plankton, etc. grows on a large scale on continental shelf.\n3) Algae, plankton, etc. is food for fish. Therefore, fish are found on a large scale on continental shelf. Therefore, the continental shelf is paradise for fishing activity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64735,1991,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Some marine islands are actually the peaks of sea mountains.','','','1) The hundreds of kilometres wide and thousands of kilometres long hills found on the ocean bed are called submerged hills.\n2) Peaks of some of the submerged hills come above the sea level.\n3) These peaks are the visible land areas surrounded from all sides by water. These peaks are called islands. Thus, some marine islands are actually the peaks of sea mountains.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64737,1991,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The continental slope is considered to be the boundary of continents.','','','1) Continental slope lies beyond the continental shelf.\n2) The deep abyssal plain begins beyond the continental slope. Therefore, the continental slope is considered to be the boundary of continents.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64738,1991,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The disposal of waste materials in the oceans by man is harmful to the environment.','','','1) The disposal of waste materials in the oceans by man increases the pollution of sea water.\n2) This disposed waste materials harm the life of marine animal life and marine plant. Thus, the disposal of waste materials in the oceans by man is harmful to the environment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64747,1991,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Differentiate between the following : Landforms and submerged landforms','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Landforms</th>\n    <th>Submerged Landforms</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Natural forms of the land surface found on the surface of the earth are called landforms. </td>\n    <td>Natural forms of land surface found on the ocean bed are called submerged landforms.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) Landforms on the surface of the earth are classified on the classified on the basis of altitudes and the shape of the land there.</td>\n    <td>Submerged landforms are classified on the basis of depth from the sea floor and the shape of the land there. </td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64748,1991,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Differentiate between the following : Marine deep and Marine trench','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Marine deep</th>\n    <th>Marine trench</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) The landforms on the ocean bed that are comparatively less extending and shallower ones are called marine deep.</td>\n    <td>The landforms on the ocean bed that are comparatively more extending and deeper ones are called marine trench.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) Active volcanoes are not seen in marine deep. Therefore, earthquake proneness is less in case of marine deep.</td>\n    <td> Active volcanoes are mostly seen in marine trench. Therefore, earthquake proneness is more in case of trench.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64749,1991,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Answer the following questions :\n1) Name the landforms shown in the following figure.\n','2) What parameters were used for classifying the landforms on the earth?\n3) What parameters were used for naming the landforms below water?\n','','1) ','2) Altitude and the shape of the land were the parameters used for classifying the landforms on the earth.\n3) Depth from the sea floor and the shape of the land were the parameters used for naming the landforms below water.\n',1,0,1,NULL),(64750,1991,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Answer the following questions :\n1) Name the submerged landforms shown in the diagram.','2) Identify the ocean shown in the map of the ocean floor.\n3) Which region would be ideal for fishing and why?\n','','1) ','2)The ocean shown in the map of the ocean floor is Indian Ocean.\n3) The region near Madagascar island would be ideal for fishing. Madagascar island has a shallow continental shelf. The warm stream and cold stream also meet near Madagascar island. Therefore, the region near Madagascar island would be ideal for fishing.\n',1,0,1,NULL),(64751,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Madagascar and Sri Lanka are related to which landform of the ocean floor?','','','Madagascar and Sri Lanka are related to island of the ocean floor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64752,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Near which continent are these landforms located?','','','Madagascar is located near the continent of Africa and Sri Lanka is located near the continent of Asia.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64753,1991,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Which islands in our country are examples of peaks of submerged mountains?','','','Andaman and Nicobar islands in our country are examples of peaks of submerged mountains.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64754,1991,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Name the landforms shown in the following figure and answer following questions :','2) Which of these landforms are useful for deep sea research?\n3) Which of these are appropriate to be used for the protection of marine borders and naval base building?\n','','1) ','2) Submarine plateau and marine trench are the landforms useful for deep sea research.\n3) Continental shelf is appropriate to be used for the protection of marine borders and naval base building.\n',1,0,1,NULL),(64786,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Ocean currents give specific direction and velocity to the water.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64787,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The deep ocean currents flow with high velocity.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(64788,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Generally, surface ocean currents are formed in the equatorial regions.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: Generally, surface ocean currents are formed in the equatorial region as well as polar region.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64789,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Ocean currents hold great importance for human life.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(64790,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The movement of icebergs is not dangerous for water transport.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: The movement of icebergs is dangerous for water transport.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64791,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Water becomes warm near Brazil due to ocean currents. On the other hand, it becomes cold near African coast.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: Water becomes warm near Brazil due to ocean currents. Similarly, it also becomes warm near African coast.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64792,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Labrador is a warm ocean current.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: Labrador is a cold ocean current.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64793,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Surface ocean currents flow with high velocity.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: Surface ocean currents flow with slow velocity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64794,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Even though the velocity of ocean currents is less, the water carried by them is immense.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(64795,1992,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ocean currents flow near the lower boundary of the continental shelf.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(64796,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','What is the reason behind the dynamics of the ocean currents?','','','Planetary winds is the reason behind the dynamics of the ocean currents.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64797,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','How do winds give direction to the ocean currents?','','','Winds give clockwise direction to the ocean currents in the northern hemisphere and anti-clockwise direction to the ocean currents in the southern hemisphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64798,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the main types of ocean currents?','','','Cold ocean currents and warm ocean currents are the main types of ocean currents.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64799,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which region are the warm ocean currents formed?','','','The warm ocean currents are formed in the equatorial region.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64800,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which regions are the cold ocean currents formed?','','','The cold ocean currents are formed in the polar regions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64801,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which factors are responsible for the formation of ocean currents?','','','Difference in temperature of sea water, difference in density of sea water and planetary winds are the factors responsible for the formation of ocean currents.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64802,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which factors are responsible for the direction of flow and velocity of ocean currents?','','','Rotation of the earth and continental structure are the factors responsible for the direction of flow and velocity of ocean currents.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64803,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which unit is the discharge of water in the oceans measured?','','','The discharge of water in the oceans is measured in Sverdrup unit.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64804,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by 1 Sverdrup discharge?','','','1 Sverdrup discharge means 1 million cu.m./second discharge of ocean water.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64805,1992,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which two ocean currents meet near the Newfoundland Island?','','','Gulf warm ocean current and Labrador cold ocean current meet near the Newfoundland Island.','',0,0,0,NULL),(64806,1992,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Match the columns and complete the chain :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>\'A\' Column</th>\n    <th>\'B\' Column</th>\n<th>\'C\' Column</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) California Current</td>\n    <td>i) South Atlantic Ocean</td>\n<td>a) Near the eastern coast of the continent of North America</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n      <tr>\n    <td>2) Hamboldt Current</td>\n    <td>ii) North Pacific Ocean</td>\n<td>b) Near the eastern coast of the continent of South America</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) Brazil Current</td>\n    <td>iii) North Atlantic Ocean</td>\n<td>c) Near the western coast of the continent of South America</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4) Gulf Current</td>\n    <td>iv) South Pacific Ocean</td>\n<td>d) Near the western coast of the continent of North America</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','','','1) California Current - North Pacific Ocean - Near the western coast of the continent of North America.\n2) Hamboldt Current - South Pacific Ocean Near the western coast of the continent of South America.\n3) Brazil Current- South Atlantic Ocean - Near the eastern coast of the continent of South America.\n4) Gulf Current - North Atlantic Ocean - Near the eastern coast of the continent of North America.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64807,1992,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the reasons responsible for the formation of deep ocean currents?','','','1) The temperature varies in various parts of ocean.\n2) Similarly, the density of water in various parts of ocean is also found to be different.\n3) The difference in the temperature and density of seawater, leads to its circulation and the deep ocean currents are formed. This circulation is known as thermohaline circulation. Thus, the difference in temperature and density of water in various parts of ocean are the reasons responsible for the formation of deep ocean currents.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64808,1992,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Why do the ports in the eastern coast of Canada freeze in winter?','','','1) Labrador cold current flows along the eastern coast of Canada.\n2) Due to Labrador cold current, the temperature of sea water near the eastern coast of Canada decreases.\n3) Due to fall in temperature, the sea water along the eastern coast of Canada start freezing. As its effect, the ports in the eastern coast of Canada freeze in winter.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64809,1992,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about warm ocean currents.','','','1) Ocean currents that move water away from the Equator to the poles are called warm ocean currents.\n2) Warm ocean currents are formed in the equatorial region and they flow towards the poles.\n3) The warm currents increase the temperature and precipitation in the coastal areas along with which they flow. For example, the warm ocean currents flowing along with the coastal areas of Western Europe, Southern Alaska and Japan increases the temperature in the coastal areas along with which they flow. As its effect, the ports in the coastal areas of Western Europe, Southern Alaska and Japan do not freeze in winter.\n4) Gulf stream, Agulhas, Somali, etc. are some of the warm ocean currents.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64810,1992,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about cold ocean currents.','','','1) Ocean currents that move water away from the poles to the Equator are called cold ocean currents.\n2) Cold ocean currents are formed in the polar region and they flow towards the equatorial region.\n3) The cold currents decrease the temperature and precipitation in the coastal areas along with which they flow. For example, the cold ocean currents flowing along with the coastal areas of Peru, Chile, and southeastern Africa, decreases the amount of precipitation in the coastal areas along with which they flow. As its effect, arid desert areas are formed in Peru, Chile, and southwestern Africa.\n4) Falkland, Labrador, Canary, Oyashio, Benguela, etc. are some of the cold ocean currents.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64811,1992,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about conveyor belt.','','','1) Due to deep ocean currents, the warm water goes down and the cold water comes to the surface of the earth.\n2) Thus, deep ocean currents redistribute the ocean water.\n3) This redistribution of ocean water takes around 500 years to complete.\n4) This redistribution (movement) of sea water is known as conveyor belt.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64812,1992,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about relation between ocean currents and fishing.','','','1) Algae, plankton, etc. fish food grow on a large scale in the areas where warm ocean current and cold ocean current meet.\n2) Fish come in these areas on a large scale and breed. Therefore, extensive fishing grounds are found in the meeting point of warm and cold ocean currents.\n3) For example, Grand Bank in Atlantic Ocean near the coastal region of North America, Dogger Bank near the continent of Europe, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64813,1992,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fog is found near Newfoundland island.','','','1) Fog is found at the meeting point of warm and cold ocean currents.\n2) Warm Gulf Stream ocean current and cold Labrador ocean current meet near Newfoundland island. Therefore, fog is found near Newfoundland island.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64814,1992,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ocean transport is carried out along with ocean currents.','','','1) Ocean transport carried out along with ocean currents increases the speed of ocean transport and so saves the time.\n2) Ocean transport carried out along with ocean currents decreases the cost of fuel. Therefore, ocean transport is carried out along with ocean currents.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64815,1992,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Extensive fishing ground is found near Newfoundland island.','','','1) Warm Gulf Stream ocean current and cold Labrador ocean current meet near Newfoundland island.\n2) Algae, plankton, etc. fish food grow on a large scale in the meeting point of warm Gulf Stream ocean current and cold Labrador ocean current.\n3) Fish come in this area on a large scale and breed. Therefore, extensive fishing ground is found near Newfoundland island.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64816,1992,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','Warm ocean currents on climate.','','','1) The amount of precipitation increases in the coastal region along with which the warm ocean currents flow.\n2) Similarly, the temperature increases in the coastal region along with which the warm ocean currents flow.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64817,1992,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','Cold ocean currents on climate.','','','1) The amount of precipitation decreases in the coastal region along with which the cold ocean currents flow.\n2) Similarly, the temperature decreases in the coastal region along with which the cold ocean currents flow.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64818,1992,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','Cold ocean currents on the movement of icebergs. ','','','1) Due to cold ocean currents, icebergs are moved away from the polar regions.\n2) These icebergs come along the marine routes and prove hazardous to the ships.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64819,1992,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','The shape of the coast line on ocean currents.','','','1) The extended parts of coastline acts as an obstacle for ocean currents.\n2) The extended parts of coastline alters the direction and velocity of ocean currents.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64820,1992,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','Meeting of warm and cold ocean currents.','','','1) Dense fog is found in the meeting point of warm and cold ocean currents. Algae, plankton, etc. fish food grow on a large scale in these areas.\n2) Fish come in these areas on a large scale and breed. Therefore, extensive fishing grounds are found in the areas where warm and cold ocean currents meet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64821,1992,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','The transportation capacity of ocean currents.','','','1) The transportation capacity of ocean currents alters the amount of precipitation and temperature in the coastal regions along with which they flow.\n2) The transportation capacity of ocean currents leads to transfer of warm water to the bottom from the surface and cold water to the surface from the bottom.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64822,1992,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','Deep ocean currents.','','','1) Deep ocean currents leads to transfer of warm water to the bottom from the surface and cold water to the surface from the bottom.\n2) Deep ocean currents thus leads to redistribution of sea water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64823,1992,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Differentiate between the following : Surface ocean current and Deep ocean current ','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th> Surface ocean current</th>\n    <th> Deep ocean current</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) The ocean current found up to 500 metres of depth from the surface of the sea, is called surface ocean current.</td>\n    <td> The ocean current found metres below 500 of depth from the surface of the sea, is called deep ocean current.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n<td>2) The velocity of surface ocean current is comparatively low.</td>\n<td> The velocity of deep ocean current is comparatively high.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64824,1992,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Differentiate between the following : Cold ocean current and Warm ocean current ','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Cold ocean current</th>\n    <th>Warm ocean current</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Ocean current that move water away from polar regions to equatorial region is called cold ocean current.</td>\n    <td>Ocean current that move water away from equatorial region to polar regions is called warm ocean current.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n<td>2) Normally, cold ocean current is formed in the polar regions.</td>\n<td>Normally, warm ocean current is formed in the equatorial regions.</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64825,1992,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the following map and answer the following questions :','1) What are the major types of ocean currents?\n2) What do you call the currents flowing from the equator to the poles?\n3) What do you call the currents flowing from the poles to the equator?\n4) When the currents are moving in a circular manner, what difference is visible in their direction in Northern and Southern Hemisphere respectively?\n5) What might happen at the places where these two currents meet?\n6) When two different types of currents meet along the coast then what type of human settlements  and occupations are seen? \n','','1) Warm ocean currents and cold ocean currents are the major types of ocean currents.\n2) The currents flowing from the equator to the poles are called warm currents.\n3) The currents flowing from the poles to the equator are called cold currents.\n4) When the currents are moving in a circular manner, they will move in clockwise in Northern Hemisphere and anti-clockwise in the Southern Hemisphere.\n5) Dense fog will get formed at the places where these two currents meet.\n6) When two different types of currents meet along the coast then dense human settlements and fishing occupation are seen there.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(64826,1992,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the following map and answer the following questions :\n','1) How does the Humboldt current affect the climate of the South America?\n2) In which oceans are counter equatorial currents not observed and why?\n3) Which currents are absent in the northern part of the Indian Ocean and why?\n4) In which regions do the cold and warm ocean currents meet?\n','','1) Humboldt current is a cold ocean current and so it decreases the amount of precipitation and temperature on the western coastal region of the South America.\n2) Counter equatorial currents are not observed in Arctic Ocean and Southern Ocean. These oceans are located near the polar regions. Therefore, the effects of trade winds is found to be less in these oceans. Therefore, counter equatorial currents are not observed in these oceans.\n3) Cold currents are absent in the northern part of the Indian Ocean. The northern part of Indian Ocean is included in the temperate zone. So cold currents are absent in the northern part of the Indian Ocean.\n4) The cold and warm ocean currents meet in the following regions: (1) North Atlantic Ocean (cold Labrador current and warm Gulf stream current) (2) North Pacific Ocean (cold Oyashio current and warm Kurishio current) (3) South Atlantic Ocean (cold Falkland current and warm Brazil current) (4) South Pacific Ocean (cold South Polar current and warm East Australian current) (5) Indian Ocean (cold South Polar current and Agulhas Current)\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(64827,1992,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the following map and answer the following questions :','1) Where did a container full of toys fall on 10th January, 1992?\n2) Where did some of the toys reach on 16th November, 1992?\n3) Where did some of the toys reach by the year 2000?\n4) Where did some of the toys reach by the year 2003?\n5) Where did some of the toys reach by the year 2007?\n6) Why did the toys travel in this way?\n','','1) A container full of toys fell near the Hawaii Island on 10th January, 1992.\n2) Some of the toys reached the coast of Alaska on 16th November, 1992.\n3) Some of the toys crossed the Bering Strait and reached the Arctic Ocean by the year 2000.\n4) Some of the toys reached the eastern coast of America by the year 2003.\n5) Some of the toys reached the western coast of continent of Europe by the year 2007.\n6) The toys travelled in this way due to surface ocean currents and mostly due to deep ocean currents.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(64871,25,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ are autotrophic organisms. \na) Fungi          	b) Protozoa 		c) Virus               	d) Algae  ','','','d) Algae  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64872,25,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following do not find place in five-kingdom system of the classification?\na) Bacteria 		b) Fungi           	c) Virus                  	d) Protozoa ','','','c) Virus','',0,0,0,NULL),(64873,25,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bacteriophage is a ------\na) bacteria that attacks virus 		b) virus that attacks bacteria         c) protozoan that attacks bacteria           	d) protozoan that attacks virus ','','','b) virus that attacks bacteria   ','',0,0,0,NULL),(64874,25,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Viruses have long molecules of -----\na) only DNA            	  b) Only RNA               c) both DNA or RNA             	d) either DNA or RNA  ','','','d) either DNA or RNA\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(64875,25,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Tex