 आया है।\n','','','उत्तर: गलत\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76506,2286,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','चित्र में बंदर क्या भूमिका निभा रहा है?\n','','','उत्तर: चित्र में बंदर डॉक्टर की भूमिका निभा रहा है। वह जंगल के जानवरों का इलाज करता है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76509,2286,'descriptive','3',2,1,'Out of Text Book','','डॉक्टर बंदर जानवरों का इलाज कैसे करता है?\n','','','उत्तर: डॉक्टर बंदर दवाई की शीशी और स्टेथोस्कोप से जानवरों का इलाज करता है। वह उन्हें घरेलू जड़ी-बूटियाँ भी सुझाता है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76510,2286,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','भालू डॉक्टर के पास क्यों आता है?\n','','','उत्तर: भालू डॉक्टर के पास दवाई लेने आता है। उसे अपनी तबीयत ठीक करवानी थी।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76511,2286,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','लोमड़ी डॉक्टर बंदर से किस तरह बात करती है?\n','','','उत्तर: लोमड़ी डॉक्टर बंदर से चालाकी से बचने की कोशिश करती है। वह दवाई के स्वाद को लेकर बहाना बनाती है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76512,2286,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','जंगल के जानवर डॉक्टर बंदर की सलाह क्यों लेते हैं?\n','','','उत्तर: जंगल के जानवर अपनी बीमारियों के इलाज के लिए डॉक्टर बंदर से सलाह लेते हैं। उन्हें विश्वास है कि वह उनकी तबीयत ठीक कर देगा।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76513,2286,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','चित्र में बिल्ली का क्या कार्य है?\n','','','उत्तर: चित्र में बिल्ली इलाज के लिए आई हुई है। वह डॉक्टर बंदर की सलाह सुन रही है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76514,2286,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','भालू जंगल की जड़ी-बूटियों के बारे में क्या कहता है?\n','','','उत्तर: भालू जंगल की ताजगी और जड़ी-बूटियों की प्रशंसा करता है। वह सबको प्राकृतिक इलाज की सलाह देता है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76515,2286,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','डॉक्टर बंदर सबको दवाई कैसे देता है?\n','','','उत्तर: डॉक्टर बंदर मिक्सचर या जड़ी-बूटियों की दवा देता है। वह यह दवा दिन में तीन बार पीने के लिए बोलता है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76516,2286,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','चित्र में जानवरों का व्यवहार कैसा है?\n','','','उत्तर: जानवर डॉक्टर के पास अपनी बीमारी को लेकर गंभीर हैं। वे उसकी बातों को ध्यान से सुन रहे हैं।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76517,2286,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','डॉक्टर बंदर के पास कौन-कौन से उपकरण हैं?\n','','','उत्तर: डॉक्टर बंदर के पास दवाई की शीशी और स्टेथोस्कोप है। ये उपकरण इलाज के लिए प्रयोग करता है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76529,2287,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','\"तुम्हें खांसी क्यों हो रही है? आओ, मैं दवा देता हूँ।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: डॉ. बंदर\nकिससे कहा: भालू को','',0,0,0,NULL),(76530,2287,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','\"डॉक्टर साहब, मुझे बहुत बुखार है।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: बिल्ली\nकिससे कहा: डॉ. बंदर को','',0,0,0,NULL),(76531,2287,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','\"यह दवा पीओ, सुबह-शाम लू, तुरंत ठीक हो जाओगे।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: डॉ. बंदर\nकिससे कहा: बिल्ली को','',0,0,0,NULL),(76532,2287,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','\"मुझे कड़वी दवा नहीं चाहिए!\"\n','','','किसने कहा: लोमड़ी\nकिससे कहा: डॉ. बंदर को','',0,0,0,NULL),(76533,2287,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','\"अगर ठीक होना है तो दवा तो पीनी पड़ेगी।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: डॉ. बंदर\nकिससे कहा: लोमड़ी को','',0,0,0,NULL),(76534,2287,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','\"जंगल की ताजी घास खाओ, अच्छे हो जाओगे।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: भालू\nकिससे कहा: सभी जानवरों को','',0,0,0,NULL),(76535,2287,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','\"कृपया मेरी जाँच करें, मुझे बहुत कमजोरी है।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: बिल्ली\nकिससे कहा: डॉ. बंदर को','',0,0,0,NULL),(76536,2287,'descriptive','1b',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','\"हर रोज़ जड़ी-बूटी का सेवन करो।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: डॉ. बंदर\nकिससे कहा: सभी जानवरों को','',0,0,0,NULL),(76601,2288,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','गाँव के बच्चों को स्कूल जाने में कौन-सी बड़ी समस्या थी?\n','','','उत्तर: गाँव के बीचों-बीच बहने वाले नाले के कारण बरसात में बच्चों को स्कूल जाना मुश्किल हो जाता था। नाले के तेज बहाव में पार करना खतरनाक था।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76602,2288,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','गाँव का उदाहरण बच्चों को कैसी शिक्षा देता है?\n','','','उत्तर: गाँव का यह उदाहरण बच्चों को सहयोग, हिम्मत और समस्या सुलझाने की शिक्षा देता है। यह सिखाता है कि मिलकर किसी भी बड़ी मुश्किल को पार किया जा सकता है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76603,2288,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','समस्या के समाधान के लिए किसने प्रेरणा दी?\n','','','उत्तर: बच्चों ने स्कूल की कमी और अनुपस्थिति की बात उठाई, जिसे सुनकर ग्रामसभा में निर्णय लिया गया। सभी ने एकजुट होकर काम किया।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76604,2288,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','गाँव की एकता और सहयोग से क्या सीख मिलती है?\n','','','उत्तर: गाँव वालों ने सामूहिक प्रयास से बड़ी समस्या हल कर ली। इससे यह सीख मिलती है कि एकता में बड़ी शक्ति है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76605,2288,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','नाले की समस्या से बच्चों की पढ़ाई पर क्या प्रभाव पड़ा था?\n','','','उत्तर: बरसात में बच्चे स्कूल नहीं जा पाते थे, जिससे उनकी पढ़ाई और वार्षिक परीक्षा पर असर पड़ता था। कई बार बच्चे पिछड़ जाते थे।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76606,2288,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','गाँव के लोगों ने पुल बनने के बाद किस प्रकार खुशी मनाई?\n','','','उत्तर: गाँव के सभी लोग पुल के उद्घाटन पर इकट्ठा हुए और प्रसन्नता व्यक्त की। बच्चे, माता-पिता और बुजुर्गों ने मिलकर इस उपलब्धि का जश्न मनाया।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76615,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि किससे बात कर रहा है?\n','','','उत्तर: कवि दुखी और निराश लोगों से बात कर रहा है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76616,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि का मुख्य संदेश क्या है?\n','','','उत्तर: जीवन छोटी-बड़ी विफलताओं से खत्म नहीं होता।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76617,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','खिलौनों के खोने से क्या नहीं मरता?\n','','','उत्तर: खिलौनों के खोने से बचपन नहीं मरता।','',0,0,0,NULL),(76618,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि निराशा फैलाने वालों से क्या कहता है?\n','','','उत्तर: कवि कहता है कि नकारात्मकता फैलाना ठीक नहीं है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76619,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','आलोचना या नाराज़ी से क्या होता है?\n','','','उत्तर: आलोचना या नाराज़ी से व्यक्ति का सम्मान कम नहीं होता।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76620,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि का जीवन-दर्शन क्या है?\n','','','उत्तर: कठिनाइयों के बाद भी जीवन चलता रहता है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76621,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','दीप बुझने का अर्थ क्या है?\n','','','उत्तर: जीवन में कुछ मुश्किलें आना आम बात है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76622,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','माला के बिखरने से कवि क्या सीख देता है?\n','','','उत्तर: छोटी परेशानियों से हमें हार नहीं माननी चाहिए।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76623,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','सपनों के मर जाने से क्या होता है?\n','','','उत्तर: सपनों के मरने से जीवन नहीं रुकता।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76624,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि किसको \'मोती व्यर्थ लुटाने वाले\' कहते हैं?\n','','','उत्तर: जो अपना दुख सबके सामने आँसू बहाकर जताते हैं।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76625,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि का मुख्य संदेश क्या है?\n','','','उत्तर: जीवन छोटी-बड़ी विफलताओं से खत्म नहीं होता।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76626,2289,'descriptive','2a',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि किससे बात कर रहा है?\n','','','उत्तर: कवि दुखी और निराश लोगों से बात कर रहा है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76629,2289,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','बचपन के संदर्भ में कवि क्या समझाता है?\n','','','उत्तर: कवि कहता है कि बचपन छोटी-छोटी चीज़ों के खोने या टूटने से खत्म नहीं होता। बचपन की निश्छलता और आनंद हमेशा बना रहता है।','',0,0,0,NULL),(76630,2289,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','जीवन के बदलावों को लेकर कवि का क्या दृष्टिकोण है?\n','','','उत्तर: कवि मानता है कि जीवन में बदलाव आते रहते हैं। किसी चीज़ का बदलना या खोना अंत नहीं हैं, हमेशा नया आरंभ होता है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76631,2289,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि तम यानी अंधकार को बढ़ाने वालों के लिए क्या कहता है?\n','','','उत्तर: कवि कहता है कि अंधकार फैलाना या नकारात्मकता बढ़ाना ठीक नहीं है। हमें हमेशा आशा और रोशनी को प्राथमिकता देनी चाहिए।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76632,2289,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','आलोचना या नाराज़ी होने पर हमें क्या करना चाहिए?\n','','','उत्तर: आलोचना या नाराज़ी से निराश नहीं होना चाहिए। हमें आत्म-विश्वास बनाए रखना चाहिए।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76633,2289,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि के अनुसार माला के बिखरने का क्या अर्थ है?\n','','','उत्तर: माला का बिखरना जीवन में छोटी-छोटी परेशानियों का प्रतीक है। इससे हमारा प्रयत्न और तपस्या बेकार नहीं जाती।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76634,2289,'descriptive','3',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','कवि जीवन की कठिनाइयों से घबराने वालों से क्या कहता है?\n','','','उत्तर: कवि कहता है कि जीवन में कठिनाइयाँ आना स्वाभाविक है। उनसे डरकर या हार मानकर बैठना सही नहीं है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76637,2289,'mcq','1',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','छिप-छिप ________ बहाने वालों!\nA) खुशी B) अश्रु','','','उत्तर: B) अश्रु\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76638,2289,'mcq','1',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','कुछ सपनों के ________ जाने से जीवन नहीं मरा करता है।\nA) मर B) टूट','','','उत्तर: A) मर\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76640,2289,'mcq','1',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','मोती व्यर्थ ________ वालों!\nA) लुटाने B) सजाने','','','उत्तर: A) लुटाने\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76641,2289,'mcq','1',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','माला ________ गई तो क्या है?\nA) टूट B) बिखर','','','उत्तर: B) बिखर\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76643,2289,'mcq','1',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','दीप ________ जाने से आँगन नहीं मरा करता है।\nA) जल B) बुझ','','','उत्तर: B) बुझ\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76644,2289,'mcq','1',1,3,'Out of Text Book','','जिल्द बदलती ________।\nA) कहानी B) पोथी','','','उत्तर: B) पोथी\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76645,2289,'descriptive','1b',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','\"छिप-छिप अश्रु बहाने वालों! मोती व्यर्थ लुटाने वालों!\"\n','','','किसने कहा: कवि (नीरज)\nकिससे कहा: दुखी और निराश लोगों से','',0,0,0,NULL),(76646,2289,'descriptive','1b',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','\"कुछ सपनों के मर जाने से जीवन नहीं मरा करता है।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: कवि\nकिससे कहा: सपना टूटने पर दुखी व्यक्ति से','',0,0,0,NULL),(76647,2289,'descriptive','1b',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','\"माला बिखर गई तो क्या है, पुण्य आँसू गर नीलाम हुए।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: कवि\nकिससे कहा: हार मानने वाले लोगों से','',0,0,0,NULL),(76648,2289,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','\"कुछ दीपों के बुझ जाने से आँगन नहीं मरा करता है।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: कवि\nकिससे कहा: आशा छोड़ देने वाले से','',0,0,0,NULL),(76649,2289,'descriptive','1b',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','\"खोता कुछ भी नहीं यहाँ पर, केवल जिल्द बदलती पोथी।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: कवि\nकिससे कहा: जीवन के बदलावों से घबराने वाले लोगों से','',0,0,0,NULL),(76650,2289,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','\"चन्द खिलौनों के खोने से बचपन नहीं मरा करता है।\"\n','','','किसने कहा: कवि\nकिससे कहा: छोटी-छोटी हानियों से दुखी बच्चों से','',0,0,0,NULL),(76651,2289,'descriptive','1b',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','\"तम की उम्र बढ़ाने वालों! लौ की आयु घटाने वालों!\"\n','','','किसने कहा: कवि\nकिससे कहा: निराशा और नकारात्मकता फैलाने वालों से\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76655,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','कवि जीवन की कठिनाइयों से भाग जाने की सलाह देता है।\n','','','उत्तर: गलत\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76657,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','','','','आँसू बहाने से समस्याएँ दूर हो जाती हैं।\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76658,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','कवि कहता है कि सपनों के मरने से जीवन नहीं मरता।\n','','','उत्तर: सही\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76660,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','दीपकों के बुझ जाने से आँगन का अस्तित्व नहीं मिटता।\n','','','उत्तर: सही\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76662,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','माला के बिखरने का अर्थ है जीवन का अंत।\n','','','उत्तर: गलत\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76663,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','कवि नकारात्मक सोच और निराशा से बचने की सलाह देता है।\n','','','उत्तर: सही\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76665,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','किसी की आलोचना या नाराज़ी से दर्पण यानी आत्मसम्मान खत्म नहीं होता।\n','','','उत्तर: सही\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76666,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','किसी की आलोचना या नाराज़ी से दर्पण यानी आत्मसम्मान खत्म नहीं होता।\n','','','उत्तर: सही\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76667,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','किसी की आलोचना या नाराज़ी से दर्पण यानी आत्मसम्मान खत्म नहीं होता।\n','','','उत्तर: सही\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76670,2289,'descriptive','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','रोकर या दुख जताकर जीवन की कठिनाइयाँ हल हो जाती हैं।\n','','','उत्तर: गलत\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76731,1800,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">FASTER than fairies, \nfaster than witches, \nBridges and houses, \nhedges and ditches; \nAnd charging along \nlike troops in a battle, \nAll through the meadows, \nthe horses and cattle; \nAll of the sights \nof the hill and the plain \nFly as thick \nas driving rain; \nAnd ever again, \nin the wink of an eye, \nPainted stations \nwhistle by. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false.\n      i) The train is running faster than witches.\n      ii) The poet saw horses and cattle all through the meadows.\n     iii) The train is charging along like a wild animal.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n         Write the sights seen through a railway carriage mentioned in the first stanza.\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       Find pairs of rhyming words from the extract.\n','','A1] i) True		ii) True			iii) False\nA2] The sights mentioned in the first stanza are that of bridges, houses, hedges, and ditches. The sights of the horses and cattle grazing in the meadows have also been mentioned in the first stanza.\nA3] i) Witches – ditches		ii) Battle – cattle		iii) Plain - rain\n       iv) Rain – again			v) Eye – by\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76732,1800,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">FASTER than fairies, \nfaster than witches, \nBridges and houses, \nhedges and ditches; \nAnd charging along \nlike troops in a battle, \nAll through the meadows, \nthe horses and cattle; \nAll of the sights \nof the hill and the plain \nFly as thick \nas driving rain; \nAnd ever again, \nin the wink of an eye, \nPainted stations \nwhistle by. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        Write the sights mentioned in the second stanza.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Why are the sights said to \'fly\'?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the word \'meadows\' from the extract.\n','','A1] The sights mentioned in the second stanza are that of the hill, plain and painted stations.\nA2] The sights are said to \'fly\' because the train is running at a full speed. As the train moves, it quickly leaves the hill and the plain behind. The poet has used the word \'fly for sights which are seemingly rushing past. But in reality, the sights are fixed while the train is running ahead.\nA3] Flat surface of land covered with grass\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76733,1800,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">Here is a child \nwho clambers and scrambles, \nAll by himself \nand gathering brambles; \nHere is a tramp \nwho stands and gazes; \nAnd there is the green \nfor stringing the daisies!\nHere is a cart \nrun away in the road \nLumping along \nwith man and load; \nAnd here is a mill \nand there is a river. \nEach a glimpse \nand gone for ever! <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       List the lines that begin with \'Here\' or \'And here / there\'.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       The phrases and sentences that begin with \'Here\', \'And there\' or \'And here\' tell us about things that appear one after the other as the carriage moves. Can we tell which ones are closer to the train and which ones are at a distance? How?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       Find pairs of rhyming words from the extract.\n','','A1] \"Here is a child...\"			\"Here is a tramp...\"		\"Here is a cart...\"\n       \"And there is the green...\"	\"And here is a mill...\"		\"And there is a river.\"\nA2] The sentences that begin with \'Here\' and \'And here\' tell us about the things and persons that are closer to the train, like a child, a tramp, a cart and a mill. The sentences that begin with \'And there\' tell us about things that are at a distance, like the green field of daisies and a river. Therefore, on the basis of the words \'here\' and \'there\' we can tell which things and persons are closer to the train and which ones are at a distance.\nA3] i) Scrambles - brambles 		ii) Road – load			iii) River-ever\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76734,1801,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','With the tip of her nose pressed to the window of the space shuttle, Sayali sat gazing at the view outside for so long, still she didn’t seem to be contended. She had read the description of this view several times before and had seen this scene on the video too. But this was different! Reading the description was one thing, watching the pictures and videos captured by the camera was another but getting an opportunity to view that amazing panorama with your own eyes, being present physically right in the midst of it, was indeed an ‘out of the world’ experience! No matter how long she sat gazing, her awe and wonder did not seem to abate.\nThe earth gradually appeared to get smaller and smaller in the sky. The azure blue sky was now looking jet black. The clouds had disappeared, the stars were shining bright.\nThis trip to the moon was exorbitantly expensive, but still Sayali’s mother had sent her on the trip. ‘In our childhood we could not even think of such long distance trips! The longest trip meant going to Europe. If you are getting this opportunity, make the most of it.’ said mummy.\n‘Oh! Mummy, Neha’s grandmother was saying that in their childhood, the longest trip for school children used to be only up to Delhi or Kolkata. It seems so funny, isn’t it?’ said Sayali laughing. Remembering the incident made her laugh even now.\nNow the shuttle was getting closer and closer to the moon. Then it started orbiting the moon. In every 32 minutes, the shuttle completed one revolution round the moon. 24 Light and darkness followed each other in quick succession. The dome shaped settlements on the moon which resembled the igloos of the Eskimos were now visible. After some time, Sayali’s shuttle made a slow, measured and safe landing on the moon. The door of the shuttle was opened and the children were escorted through an airtight tunnel to the settlement at the Neil Armstrong base.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Say whether the following statements are right or wrong.\n      i) Sayali was travelling in space for the first time.\n      ii) Many changes had occurred on the earth in the hundred years before Sayali\'s trip to the moon.\n     iii) There were no old cities left on the earth.\n     iv) Sayali\'s mother did not want her to go on the trip.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      In what time period is the story taking place?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Guess the meaning of the word \'exorbitantly\' from the extract. Write the smaller an related word that you see within the given word.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the noun form of the word \'appeared\'.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Describe the view that Sayali saw outside the window of the space shuttle.\n','','A1] i) Right		ii) Right		iii) Wrong		iv) Wrong\nA2] The story is taking place in future as the writer has imagined a time when the journey between the moon and the earth has become easier than it is in the present.\nA3] Meaning: To an excessive degree \n       Smaller and related word: Exorbitant\nA4] Appearance\nA5] Outside the window of the space shuttle, Sayali saw that the earth was gradually appearing smaller as they headed towards the moon. The azure blue sky was looking jet black. The clouds had disappeared and the stars were shining brightly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76735,1801,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The receptionist at the entrance to the base gave some instructions at the very outset. ‘I welcome you all to the Neil Armstrong base. To make your stay on the moon pleasant and comfortable, I would like you to take certain precautions. Listen carefully to my instructions and please follow them during your stay. You know that the gravitational force of the moon is 1/6 that of the earth. In the human settlements on the moon, a gravitational force equivalent to that of the earth has been artificially maintained; but outside those settlements, the gravitational force of the moon is at work. You will have to be extremely careful when you roam around there. The second point to remember is related to shopping on the moon. You all must be interested in carrying souvenirs back home from the moon. Please buy the articles only from the shops which are inside the dome shaped settlements. There you will be assured of genuine articles. Do not buy anything from the unauthorised shops outside the settlement. I wish you a very happy and pleasant stay on the moon.’\nA jam-packed three day’s programme awaited the children on the moon. According to the earth calculations, on the moon there were fifteen days of daylight and fifteen days of nighttime. The three days of the trip were going to be part of the nighttime on the moon. One of the most important programmes of the first day was Sky Watch. On the earth, it was usually foggy and the stars were seen rarely and only very faintly. There was no atmosphere on the moon. On the dark background of the moon sky, the stars shone dazzling bright.\nThe most attractive view was that of the disc of the earth! In the moon sky the earth looked thirteen times bigger than the moon in the earth sky. It was also seventy times brighter. The earth light fell on the moon. This beautiful enchanting view made Sayali and her friends ecstatic. They fell asleep feeling happy, excited and comfortable at the same time.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      Say whether the following statements are right or wrong.\n     i) There were no human settlements on the moon.\n     ii) A jam-packed four-day\'s programme awaited the children on the moon.\n     iii) There was no atmosphere on the moon.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What were the two important instructions?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n        Guess the meaning of the following words and write the smaller and related words that you see within these words.\n        i) Precautions		ii) Unauthorised\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adjectives used to describe the emotions of Sayali and her friends before sleeping.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n         What was special about sky watch on the moon?\n','','A1] i) Wrong		ii) Wrong		iii) Right\nA2] The two important instructions were:\n        i) In the human settlements on the moon, a gravitational force equivalent to that of the earth had been artificially maintained but, outside those settlements, the original gravitational force of the moon was functional. Hence, one had to be extremely careful while roaming outside.\n        ii) To only buy articles from the shops inside the dome-shaped human settlements and not from the unauthorised shops on the outside.\nA3] i) Meaning: Measures taken in advance to avoid possible dangers\n           Smaller and related word: Caution\n      ii) Meaning: Without official permission \n          Smaller and related word: Authorised\nA4] Happy, excited and comfortable\nA5] On the earth, it was usually foggy and the stars were seen rarely and only faintly. But, on the moon, there was no atmosphere. The stars could be seen clearly and they appeared dazzling bright. The disc of the earth was the most attractive view. Apart from that, the earth looked thirteen times bigger in the moon sky than the moon did in the earth sky. Hence, sky watch on the moon was special.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76736,1801,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The second day’s attraction was the circus on the moon! One unique feature of the circus was the seating arrangement. The spectators were made to sit in the tent and the circus was going on outside. High jumps and long jumps were the special attractions! One gymnast jumped twenty seven metres high!\nOn both the days, besides the main attractions, much time was reserved for visiting the various settlements there. It was expected that the students should spend some time for self-study. The teacher had assigned them the task of writing an essay on how they spent their time on the moon; which places they visited; what they observed etc. Sayali and her friends had travelled far and wide and had done a lot of sight-seeing.\nOn the third day, their teacher took them to a place of historical importance. A hundred years ago Neil Armstrong had landed at this place. Everyone was looking forward to this visit to the historical monument - it was the oldest place in the history of man on the moon. The first footprint of Neil Armstrong was preserved on the moon land. Beside the footprint was a grand statue of Armstrong. At the base of the statue were carved the first words uttered by a human on the moon, ‘One small step for a man, one giant leap of mankind.’\nThe next attraction for the children was that they were going to wear the space suits for the first time. Then they were going to roam outside experiencing the gravitational force of the moon. “Be careful when you walk, dear children ! Don’t run, or sprint, you will fall !” The teacher was warning repeatedly, but who was in the mood to listen ? They hopped, jumped and ran around. “Buy ornaments specially made from the stones on the moon, very cheap, cheaper than those available in the shops in the settlement. Cheaper and better! At least have a look.” The roadside shopkeepers screamed at the top of their voice. “Buy, sister, buy. I am selling these earrings really cheap,” saying this, he approached Sayali and displayed the sets of shining stones earrings. Sayali suddenly remembered her mother. Tomorrow they were to start on their return journey. She had completely forgotten her mother in the last three days. She felt terribly guilty.\n“Buy these, sister, don’t think much, cheaper and better….take it.” Sayali looked around; the other children had proceeded ahead. “Come on, give me those red earrings, quick.” Sayali pointed at them and asked, “What’s the price?” “Only one thousand rupees,” replied the salesman. Sayali immediately paid the amount, took the earrings and ran to catch up with her friends.\nOn returning home, Sayali told her mother very enthusiastically, “Mummy, look what I have brought for you!” Her mother picked up the earrings. She exclaimed, “Hello dear, how am I going to wear such heavy earrings?’’ Sayali immediately realized her mistake. Sayali had purchased the earrings in the field of the moon’s gravity.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Say whether the following statements are right and wrong.\n      i) Sayali made a hasty purchase.\n      ii) Sayali did not understand the mistake she had made.\n      iii) On the third day, the teacher took the students to a historical place.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What instructions had she ignored?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the following words and Write the smaller and related words that you see within these words. i) Repeatedly		ii) Enthusiastically\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the nouns used for the circus on the moon.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Imagine you are landing on the moon. What would be your feelings?\n','','A1] i) Right		ii) Wrong		iii) Right\nA2] Sayali ignored the instruction that students should not buy anything from unauthorised shops outside the human settlements on the moon.\nA3] i) Meaning: Again and again \n          Smaller and related word: Repeat\n       ii) Meaning: With great interest \n           Smaller and related word: Enthusiast and enthusiastic.\nA4] Gymnast, spectators, seating arrangement\nA5] The very idea of setting foot on the surface of the Moon fills me with joy and excitement. Firstly, I would be filled with a sense of pride and achievement. Secondly. after a lifetime of wondering what floating through space feels like, I would feel curious to try walking on the Moon\'s surface. Finally, I would feel tiny as I gazed at the giant blue marble we call home and surprised because I would be seeing the Earth for the first time without borders.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76737,1801,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','According to the earth calculations, on the moon there were fifteen days of daylight and fifteen days of nighttime. The three days of the trip were going to be part of the nighttime on the moon. One of the most important programmes of the first day was Sky Watch. On the earth, it was usually foggy and the stars were seen rarely and only very faintly. There was no atmosphere on the moon. On the dark background of the moon sky, the stars shone dazzling bright.\nThe most attractive view was that of the disc of the earth! In the moon sky the earth looked thirteen times bigger than the moon in the earth sky. It was also seventy times brighter. The earth light fell on the moon. This beautiful enchanting view made Sayali and her friends ecstatic. They fell asleep feeling happy, excited and comfortable at the same time.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       State whether the statements true or false.\n      i) The three days of the trip were going to be part of the daytime on the moon.\n     ii) The stars are seen rarely and very brightly on the earth.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        What did the beautiful enchanting view make Sayali and her friends feel?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Find the odd man out: Moon, stars, earth, bright\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Pick out the adjective.\n      They fell asleep feeling happy, excited and comfortable at the same time.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       How does the moon look to you from the earth?\n','','A1] i) False		ii) False.\nA2] The beautiful enchanting view made Sayali and her friends feel ecstatic.\nA3] Bright - the rest are nouns\nA4] Asleep, happy, excited, comfortable.\nA5] To me the moon looks like a round shiny ball of cotton. Sometimes, I feel that the moon looks like a pizza layered with cheese. I love to look at the full moon and dream of being there someday.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76738,1801,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The second day’s attraction was the circus on the moon! One unique feature of the circus was the seating arrangement. The spectators were made to sit in the tent and the circus was going on outside. High jumps and long jumps were the special attractions! One gymnast jumped twenty seven metres high!\nOn both the days, besides the main attractions, much time was reserved for visiting the various settlements there. It was expected that the students should spend some time for self-study. The teacher had assigned them the task of writing an essay on how they spent their time on the moon; which places they visited; what they observed etc. Sayali and her friends had travelled far and wide and had done a lot of sight-seeing.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        Fill in the blanks:\n        i) The second day\'s attraction was a …………… on the moon!\n       ii) ………….. and her friends had travelled far and wide and had done a lot of sight-seeing.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      Where did the teacher take the students on the second day?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      Identify the plurals of the following words.\n     i) friend		ii) metre		iii) feature		iv) tent\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Do as directed:\n        1) One gymnast jumped twenty seven metres high! (Add a question tag)\n         2) It was expected that the students should <u>spend some time for self-study.</u>\n               (Frame a \'Wh\' question so as to get the underlined words as the answer.)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       What would be the overall effects of having no atmosphere?\n','','A1] i) circus		ii) Sayali\nA2] The teacher took the students to the circus on the moon on the second day.\nA3] i) friends		ii) metres		iii) features		iv) tents\nA4] 1) One gymnast jumped twenty seven metres high, didn\'t he?\n       2) What was expected from they students?\nA5] Atmosphere is a protective layer of gases that envelopes the earth. It consists of a number of gases including nitrogen, oxygen and many more.\n        Life depends on the atmosphere.\n        *If there was no atmosphere the intense light and harmful radiation of the sun would harm life.\n         *Every creature depending on oxygen from the smallest ant to the biggest whales would perish. There would be no oceans because in the absence of atmosphere, oceans would just evaporate due to the scorching heat of the sun.\n          *No water for living because there would be no water-cycle.\n           *The temperature would be so high that everything would get burnt.\n           *Atmosphere protects asteroid strikes on the earth.\n            *To sum it up, without atmosphere there would be no life.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76741,1802,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','(Father who is a schoolmaster is getting ready to go to school.)\nAbdul: Father, to knowledge and erudition I aspire. To study hard and deep, books I require. Will you lend me a little gold? Books I’ll buy and study to rise high in the world. \nFather : Only the first half of the month has passed, But alas, the money doesn’t last! Great books and manuscripts are so expensive. They are treasures rare and exclusive But from my school, I will try to borrow Books that will take you to a brighter tomorrow. \nAbdul : That will be great! How eagerly I wait! (In the evening, when father returns -)\nAbdul : How many books could you get? Oh! Now my life will be all set \nFather : I am so sorry son, Couldn’t lay my hands on even one! \nChorus : Poor Abdul is sad and dejected Ponders; ‘Why my wishes are thus rejected?’ Continues to do his daily chores Reads borrowed books in tens and scores An idea then crosses his mind To serve the rich, and get paid in kind.\n(Abdul approaches a rich merchant.)\nAbdul : I’m a poor student, give me a chance to serve. Observe my work, pay only what I deserve.\nMerchant : You appear to be smart, be my Clerk. I’ll pay you well, if honestly you work. (Some days pass. The merchant calls Abdul.) \nMerchant : I am pleased with your work and loving care! You are indeed a gem, quite rare. I’ll give you a reward, if your demand is fair. \nAbdul : (excited) May I now read the books in your store? Quench my thirst for knowledge, I need nothing more. \nChorus : Days passed by, he learned and learned Gaining the knowledge for which he yearned Philosophy, Astronomy, Arabic and Persian Abdul the scholar now is one in a million! Will he come to the Emperor’s notice? Will he be given an honourable office?\nFriend : News for you, Abdul. The Emperor has answered your prayer. The Royal Murgikhana is now under your care. \nAbdul : What, the Murgikhana for me, a scholar ! Intellectual, sagacious, astute - made a poultry keeper? Never mind, the chance I won’t refuse. It is an opportunity. I’ll put it to good use.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       What did Abdul demand from the merchant as a payback for his services?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        What request must Abdul have sent to the Emperor?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Guess the meaning of the following in the context of the extract.\n       i) A brighter tomorrow		ii) A gem\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       List three adjectives from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       What does Abdul want to do? How does he plan to do it?\n','','A1] As a payback for his services, Abdul demanded that the merchant should let him real the books in his store.\nA2] Abdul must have requested the Emperor to give him a suitable job or a position at the court.\nA3] i) A better future\n       ii) Someone who is unique and special\nA4] i) Expensive		ii) Exclusive			iii) Dejected\nA5] Abdul wanted a little gold from his father to buy and read books which will help him to rise high in the world. When Abdul\'s father was unable to get any books, Abdul planned to serve the rich and get paid in kind to fulfil his ambition.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76742,1802,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Abdul worked with conviction, zeal and passion The hens were given a new kind of ration. The birds seemed to be in the pink of health But nothing was spent from the Emperor’s wealth!\nEmperor : How is that ? Why are we spending so little on our hens ? Have most of them died in the pens ? \nMinister : No, your Majesty, they are alive and well. The secret of their health, only Abdul can tell. (Abdul enters and bows before the Emperor.) \nEmperor : How come you spend so little on our hens? How do you nourish them, at no expense? \nAbdul : Jahapanah, your humble servant feeds them well. Food from the royal kitchen, if I may tell But only scraps and shells and kitchen waste That’s enough for the hens, they relish the taste. It’s good for their health, see how they thrive. Waste not, want not, that’s my drive. \nEmperor : Interesting, young man, you seem to have brains. We put you in charge of the library for all your pains. Chorus : Oh, no! Not again. Only a keeper of books! And he wished to be a courtier! How sad he looks! \nAbdul : Never mind! I love books. I’ll get down to work. I’ll handle this well, my duties I won’t shirk.\n(After one year. The Emperor is looking at the books in the library.)\nEmperor : What ! All these books have such expensive jackets! Surely they’ve cost a fortune silks, brocades and velvets! \nAbdul : No, Your Majesty. The expenses were none. \nEmperor : I can’t believe it, then how was it done? \nAbdul : Your Majesty, I observed that the formal requests Were sent to you in bags made of the fabrics best. When the papers were read, the bags were discarded. Why waste the silk, velvet, brocade, I demanded? The Royal Tailors made the jackets, nothing did they take. Each book was dressed for Your Majesty’s sake. \nEmperor : You’ve great merit, I’d realised much earlier. I’m happy to announce, now you are my courtier! \nChorus : Wits, brains, and perseverance helped Abdul achieve his aim Patience, determination won him great name and fame.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      What was the result of Abdul\'s efforts?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       How did Abdul manage to feed the birds well without spending money?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Find pairs of rhyming words used in the passage.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the noun form of the word \'passionate\' from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       How did Abdul manage to get expensive jackets for the books?\n','','A1] Abdul was finally able to become the Emperor\'s courtier. He was able to achieve his goal and win name and fame.\nA2] Abdul fed the birds well. He used kitchen waste and shells from the royal kitchen which was enough for maintaining the health of the hens. They not only relished the taste but, they also became healthy. In this way, Abdul managed to feed the birds without spending money.\nA3] i) Passion - ration		ii) Health – wealth		iii) Hens - pens		iv) Well - tell\n      v) Hens - expense		vi) Waste- taste		vii) Thrive – drive	\n      viii) Brains – pains		ix) Books – looks		x) Work – shirk\nA4] Passion\nA5] Formal request letters and documents were sent to the Emperor in bags of silk, brocade and velvet. The bags were thrown away immediately after the letters were read. Abdul did not want to waste such expensive fabrics, so he collected them and approached the Royal Tailors, who made book jackets from the fabrics for free. In this way, he managed to get expensive jackets for the books without any extra cost.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76743,1802,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','(Father who is a schoolmaster is getting ready to go to school.)\nAbdul : Father, to knowledge and erudition I aspire. To study hard and deep, books I require. Will you lend me a little gold? Books I’ll buy and study to rise high in the world. \nFather : Only the first half of the month has passed, But alas, the money doesn’t last! Great books and manuscripts are so expensive. They are treasures rare and exclusive But from my school, I will try to borrow Books that will take you to a brighter tomorrow. \nAbdul : That will be great! How eagerly I wait! (In the evening, when father returns -) \nAbdul : How many books could you get? Oh! Now my life will be all set \nFather : I am so sorry son, Couldn’t lay my hands on even one!\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        Complete the sentences.\n       i. Abdul\'s father was a ………………..\n      ii) Abdul wanted his father to lend him ………………..\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        What does Abdul want to do? How does he plan to do it?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Frame a sentence with the phrase \'brighter tomorrow\'.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Do as directed:\n      1) How many books could you get? (Identify the kind of sentence)\n     2) Pick out a word from the extract which means: to hope or to dream.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Do you like to read books? Why?\n','','A1] i) schoolmaster		ii) a little gold\nA2] Abdul wants to acquire knowledge and become a scholar. He plans to buy books and study them to rise high and become famous.\nA3] Rohan worked day and night for a brighter tomorrow.\nA4] 1) Interrogative			2) aspire\nA5] Yes, I like to read books. I like to read books because it helps to increase our knowledge, gives us a lot of information and keeps us occupied when there is nothing to do. For me. books are my best friends.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76744,1802,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','(Abdul approaches a rich merchant.)\nAbdul : I’m a poor student, give me a chance to serve. Observe my work, pay only what I deserve.\nMerchant : You appear to be smart, be my Clerk. I’ll pay you well, if honestly you work. (Some days pass. The merchant calls Abdul.) \nMerchant : I am pleased with your work and loving care! You are indeed a gem, quite rare. I’ll give you a reward, if your demand is fair. \nAbdul : (excited) May I now read the books in your store? Quench my thirst for knowledge, I need nothing more.\nChorus : Days passed by, he learned and learned Gaining the knowledge for which he yearned Philosophy, Astronomy, Arabic and Persian Abdul the scholar now is one in a million ! Will he come to the Emperor’s notice ? Will he be given an honourable office? \nFriend : News for you, Abdul. The Emperor has answered your prayer. The Royal Murgikhana is now under your care. \nAbdul : What, the Murgikhana for me, a scholar! Intellectual, sagacious, astute - made a poultry keeper? Never mind, the chance I won’t refuse. It is an opportunity. I’ll put it to good use.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      Complete the sentences.\n     i) You appear to be smart, ………………\n    ii) Quench my ………………….\n    iii) I am pleased with you ………………….\n    iv) made a poultry people?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What was the merchant\'s reaction to Abdul\'s work?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Find words/phrases meaning \'rare\'.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Write the noun forms of:\n       i) move 		ii) direct		iii) explore		iv) determine\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        Write how you take care of your books.\n','','A1] i) be my clerk 				ii) thirst for knowledge \n       iii) work and loving care! 			iv) Intellectual, sagacious, astute\nA2] The merchant was very pleased with Abdul\'s work and wanted to reward him.\nA3] One in a million; a gem; unique\nA4] i) movement 		ii) direction 		iii) exploration		iv) determination\nA5] I see to it that my books are always covered, with my name written on it. I do not throw my books anywhere. I keep them neatly whether in my bag or on my table at home. I do not eat or drink anything with my books in front of me so as to avoid spilling and getting oil stains on my books.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76745,1803,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower. \nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labors hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n      What does the bee stand for?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Why does the bee work hour after hour?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n        Write the meaning of the following words:\n        i) Doth		ii) Opening		iii) Cell\n','','A1] The bee stands for efforts and hard work.\nA2] The bee works hour after hour to gather honey and to build a honeycomb.\nA3] i) Does		ii) Blossom		iii) Beehive\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76746,1803,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','How doth the little crocodile \nImprove his shining tail; \nAnd pour the waters of the \nNile On every golden scale! \nHow cheerfully he seems to grin, \nHow neatly spreads his claws, \nAnd welcomes little fishes in, \nWith gently smiling jaws!\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        What does the crocodile stand for?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        Is the crocodile gentle?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       Write the pairs of rhyming word from the extract.\n','','A1] The crocodile stands for mischief, laziness and predation.\nA2] No, the crocodile is not gentle. He tricks fish with a gentle smile to eat them. He use this as a trap to prey on his target.\nA3] i) Crocodile – Nile			ii) Tail - scale\n       iii) Grin – in			iv) Claws – jaws\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76747,1803,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower. \nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labors hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Answer the questions in one word.\n      i) What does the bee gather all the day?\n     ii) How does the bee spread her wax?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      Why does the bee work hour after hour?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n        What is the rhyme scheme of the first stanza?\n','','A1] i) honey		ii) neatly\nA2] The bee works hour after hour to store the nectar that it collects from the flowers.\nA3] The rhyme scheme of the first stanza is abcb.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76748,1804,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Since the year 2015, the world has been celebrating June 21st as International Yoga Day. As most of us will know, yoga is a culture of physical, mental and spiritual discipline perfected in ancient India. Yoga as an ancient Indian discipline is a composite practice, involving the complete mind body-soul complex that is a human being. Here, let us focus on yoga as an integrated system of physical exercises that help us to attain concentration, focus and also improve flexibility, balance, and strength.\nFrom an ancient Indian discipline, let us move to a very contemporary area of research. Experts today are doing a wonderful job of drawing us into the world of biomimicry. In simple terms, bio means life and mimicry is to emulate. Basically biomimicry is the science of solving human problems with solutions already present in the natural world. From this point of view, yoga is also a form of biomimicry where in typical asanas or poses, we emulate mountains, trees, fish, cats, and so many more animals! This helps solve our problems brought on by day to day living – such as stiffness and back pain – and allows us to feel physically vibrant and mentally sharp.\nBefore we proceed further, here are a few basic precautions regarding yoga practice: \n1. If you are a beginner, you should start practising yoga either in the presence of a yoga teacher or join yoga class so that you learn the basics and the do’s and don’ts of yoga. \n2. Asanas should be practised in a clean, airy and well-lit room. The floor should be level. It is advisable to use a yoga mat. If it is not possible to practise indoors, you may practise on level ground outdoors. \n3. The best time to practise is in the morning before breakfast or in the evening before dinner. Some of the poses described below must be learnt from an instructor before you attempt to practise them on your own.\nLet us look at a few well known animal poses in yoga: Bhujangasana is a beginner’s pose. Bhujanga in Sanskrit means a cobra. Bhujangasana is a pose that resembles the posture of a cobra with raised head. This asana helps you tone the muscles of the back and strengthens your spine. It also benefits the chest and shoulders.\nSteps : 1. Lie face down. Relax all the muscles completely so that you are completely at ease. 2. Place your palms on the sides just below your shoulders. 3. Breathe in deeply as you slowly raise your head. 4. Then smoothly raise the upper part of your body. 5. Do not come up with a jerk. Let your movement be smooth and flowing. 6. Come up slowly so that you feel the movement of the spine vertebra by vertebra. 7. Curve the spine well. 8. The navel must keep touching the ground. Hold the breath in this position to a count of twenty. Then come down slowly while you breathe out. To start with you can repeat the process six times.\nSimhasana or Lion Pose is a favourite with young people. In this asana, you assume the sitting position and facial expression of a lion. Although there may be a number of steps, this pose really requires very little effort.\nSteps : 1. Sit with your legs folded under you so that your toes are pointing straight behind and you are sitting on your heels. 2. Keep your spine straight but as relaxed as possible. 3. Close your eyes and focus your attention on the centre of the forehead. In yoga, this point is often referred to as the third eye. 4. Closing your mouth, touch your palette ( roof of your mouth) with your tongue. 5. Take a deep breath through your nose while your tongue is still touching your palette. 6. Exhale in one strong move while sticking out your tongue and opening your jaws as much as possible. Also, your arms should be thrown out and stretched with your fingers splayed. 7. Roar at the top of your voice while exhaling. The roar should be with a ‘Haa’ sound and should not be a long drawn out one. Rather, it should be one strong explosion of breath that empties your lungs in the shortest time possible. 8. Hold this pose for between 20 and 30 seconds. Do not inhale immediately after this pose inhale, after a few seconds. Repeat 5 or 6 times. This asana (posture) gets its name from the way you stick your tongue out as far as possible and the way you hold your fingers splayed out, resembling a roaring lion.\n\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n       Where was yoga perfected?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        What does yoga involve?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Choose the synonyms of the following words from the extract.\n       i) Achieve		ii) Backbone		iii) Burst\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        List five adjectives from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n          What are the benefits of yoga?\n','','A1] Yoga was perfected in India.\nA2] Yoga is an integrated system of physical exercises that involves the complete mind-body-soul complex that is a human being.\nA3] i) Attain		ii) Spine		iii) Explosion\nA4] i) Ancient		ii) Contemporary		iii) Wonderful\n      iv) Vibrant		v) Sharp\nA5] Yoga helps a person attain concentration and focus. It improves flexibility, balance. strength and makes one feel physically vibrant and mentally sharp. It helps to solve the problems that arise due to daily activities, like stiffness and back pain.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76749,1804,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Marjaryasana is a very popular cat pose. This too, is a simple asana for beginners that stretches the muscles of the abdomen and the back. Marjaryasana should be part of your morning yoga routine as it will gently stretch your muscles and get rid of any bodyaches.\nSteps : 1. Start on your hands and knees so that your back is raised in a ‘tabletop’ position. 2. Make sure that your knees are positioned directly below your hips. Your wrists, elbows and shoulders should be in line and perpendicular to the floor. 3. Centre your head in a neutral position, with your eyes looking at the floor. 4. As you exhale, smoothly arch your spine upwards towards the ceiling, making sure to keep your shoulders and knees in position. 5. Release your head toward the floor, but don’t force your chin to your chest. Inhale and come back to the neutral ‘tabletop’ position on your hands and knees. To start with, you can repeat the process six times.\nGarudasana or the Eagle Pose enhances your body’s ability to balance itself. It also strengthens the leg muscles and improves the flexibility of the joints by loosening the joint muscles. \nSteps : 1. Stand straight on both feet. Raise the right leg and wrap it around the left leg 2. The right thigh should be over the left thigh and the right feet should touch the calf muscles from behind. 3. Similarly, wrap the right hand around the left hand. 4. Now place the palms together to resemble the beak of an eagle. 5. Try to maintain the balance and slowly bend the left leg and lower the body until right toes touch the ground. 6. Try to remain in this pose for long as you can maintain the balance. After this asana, relax for some time before you repeat the process.\nUstrasana or Camel Pose strengthens the back; opens the shoulders, chest, and quadriceps; it also boosts mood and energy. This yoga posture adds flexibility and strength to the body and also helps in improving digestion.\nSteps : 1. Kneel on the yoga mat and place your hands on the hips. 2.Your knees should be in line with the shoulders and the sole of your feet should be facing the ceiling. 3.As you inhale, draw in your tail-bone towards the pubis as if being pulled from the navel. 4.Simultaneously, arch your back and slide your palms over your feet till the arms are straight. 5.Do not strain or flex your neck but keep it in a neutral position. 6. Stay in this posture for a couple of breaths. 7.Breathe out and slowly come back to the initial pose. Withdraw your hands and bring them back to your hips as you straighten up. Note: You may place a cushion below your knees to ease your way into the pose.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Match the items given in column A with the items in column B:\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Marjaryasana</td>\n    <td>a. Camel Pose</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Garudasana</td>\n    <td>b. Cat Pose</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Ustrasana</td>\n    <td>c. Eagle Pose</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        How does Garudasana benefit the legs?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary:	</b>\n       Choose the antonyms of the following words from the extract.\n      i) Lowered			ii) Exhale		iii) Bent\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        List the adverbs / adverbial phrases used in the passage.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        What are the advantages of practising the Ustrasana?\n','','A1] (i-b), (ii – c), (iii – a)\nA2] Garudasana makes the leg muscles stronger and improves the flexibility of joints by loosening the joint muscles.\nA3] i) Raised		ii) Inhale		iii) Straight\nA4] Very, too, gently, directly below, in line, perpendicular, as, smoothly, upwards similarly, together, after, also, simultaneously, straight, out, slowly, back.\nA5] The advantages of practising Ustrasana are as follows:\n       i) It strengthens the back.\n       ii) It opens the shoulders, chest and quadriceps\n       iii) It boosts mood and energy.\n       iv) It adds flexibility and strength to the body.\n       v) It helps in improving digestion.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76750,1805,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Our frigate wanted to go back, but the unearthly animal came at us with a speed double our own. We gasped. More stunned than afraid, we stood mute and motionless. The animal caught up with us, played with us. It made a full circle around the frigate and wrapped us in sheets of electricity that were like luminous dust. At any instant it could have dashed against our ship. Meanwhile I was surprised to see that our warship was fleeing, not fighting. I commented on this to Commander Farragut. His face, ordinarily so emotionless, showed great astonishment.\n“Professor Aronnax,” he answered me, “I don’t know what kind of fearsome creature I’m up against, and I don’t want my frigate running foolish risks in all this darkness. Besides, how should we attack this unknown creature, how should we defend ourselves against it? Let’s wait for daylight and then we’ll play a different role.” The whole crew stayed on their feet all night long. No one even thought of sleeping. Unable to compete with the monster’s speed, our frigate, the Abraham Lincoln slowed down. For its part, the animal mimicked the frigate, simply rode with the waves, but did not leave the field of battle. However, near midnight it disappeared, or to use a more appropriate expression, ‘it went out,’ like a huge glowworm. Had it fled from us? We didn’t know and were filled with fear and hope at the same time. But at 12:53 a deafening hiss could be heard, resembling the sound made by a water spout expelled with tremendous intensity.\nBy then Commander Farragut, Ned Land, and I were on the afterdeck, peering eagerly into the darkness. “Tell me, Ned Land, isn’t that the noise cetaceans make when they spurt water from their blowholes?” “The very noise, sir, but this one’s way louder. So there can be no mistake. There’s definitely a whale lurking in our waters.”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false.\n       i) Commander Farragut wanted to attack the unknown creature in the darkness.\n       ii) The whole crew stayed on their feet all night long.\n       iii) The frigate was able to compete with the monster\'s speed.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What is the name of the narrator?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       From the extract, find all the words and phrases used to describe the \'monster\'.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Find all the words ending with the suffix \'-less\' from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       At what time is the event of the hissing sound and the reappearance of the monster taking place?\n','','A1] i. False		ii) True			iii) False\nA2] The name of the narrator is Professor Aronnax.\nA3] Unearthly animal, fearsome creature, unknown creature, a huge glow-worm, cetaceans and whale.\nA4] Motionless, emotionless\nA5] The event of the hissing sound and the reappearance of the monster took place at 12:53 a.m.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76751,1805,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Near two o’clock in the morning, the core of light reappeared, five miles away from the Abraham Lincoln. We stayed on the alert until daylight, getting ready for action. Whaling gear was set up along the railings. Our chief officer loaded the blunderbusses, which can launch harpoons as far as a mile, and long duck guns with exploding bullets that can wound and kill even the most powerful animals. Ned Land was content to sharpen his harpoon, a dreadful weapon in his hands.\nAt six o’clock day began to break, and with the dawn’s early light, the animal’s electric glow disappeared. At seven o’clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger. At eight o’clock the mist rolled away, and the horizon grew wider and clearer. Suddenly, Ned Land’s voice could be heard. “There’s the thing in question, astern to port!” the harpooner shouted. Every eye looked toward the point indicated. There, a mile and a half from the frigate, a long blackish body emerged a metre above the waves. Quivering violently, its tail was creating a considerable current.\nThe crew were waiting impatiently for orders from their leader. The latter, after carefully observing the animal, ordered the engineer to sail full steam towards the animal. Three cheers greeted this order. The hour of battle had sounded. A few moments later, the Abraham Lincoln headed straight for the animal. Unconcerned, the latter let us come nearer, it got up a little speed, and kept its distance.\nThis chase dragged on for about three-quarters of an hour without the frigate getting any closer to the sea animal. At this rate, it was obvious that we would never catch up with it. The Abraham Lincoln gathered speed but so did the animal. This went on for the next hour. The Abraham Lincoln was now speeding so much that its masts trembled down to their blocks. What a chase! No, I can’t describe the excitement that shook my very being. Ned Land stayed at his post, harpoon in hand. Several times the animal let us approach. Then, just as the harpooner was about to strike, the cetacean would steal off swiftly.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Give the meaning of \'astern to port.’\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What order did the leader give to the engineer?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the following words:\n        i) Blunderbusses			ii) Duck guns		iii) Harpoon		iv) Harpooner\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adjective form of the word \'consider\' from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n      What attempts did the ship make to defeat the \'monster\'? Find and copy lines from the extract describing at least two of the attempts.\n','','A1] \'Astern to port\' means along the left horizontal side of a ship, stretching from the front to its rear end.\nA2] After carefully observing the animal, the leader ordered the engineer to sail full steam in its direction.\nA3] i. Old-fashioned guns with a large barrel\n       ii) A gun with a long barrel\n       iii) A missile attached to a rope\n       iv) The one who operates the harpoon or the missile attached to a rope\nA4] Considerate\nA5] The ship headed straight for the animal in full speed. The chase continued for a long time without the frigate getting any closer to the sea animal. The ship was speeding so much that its masts trembled down to their blocks. The lines from the extract that describe these attempts are:\n               \'...ordered the engineer to sail full steam towards the animal.\' \n                ‘This chase dragged on for about three-quarters of an hour.\'\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76752,1805,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Commander Farragut then decided to use more direct methods. “Bah!” he said. “So that animal is faster than the Abraham Lincoln. All right, Mate, man the gun in the bow!” Our forecastle cannon was immediately loaded and levelled. The cannoneer fired a shot, but his shell passed some feet above the cetacean, which stayed half a mile off. “Over to somebody with better aim!” the Commander shouted. “And $500.00 to the man who can pierce that infernal beast!”\nCalm of eye, cool of feature, an old gray-bearded gunner - I can see him to this day - approached the cannon, put it in position, and took aim for a good while. There was a mighty explosion, mingled with cheers from the crew. The shell reached its target; it hit the animal, but bounced off its rounded surface and vanished into the sea two miles out. “Oh drat!” said the old gunner in his anger. “That monster must be covered with six-inch armour plate!” The hunt was on again. Hour after hour went by without the animal showing the least sign of weariness. However, it must be said that we, too, struggled on tirelessly.\nAt 10:50 in the evening, that electric light reappeared three miles away from the frigate, just as clear and intense as the night before. The monster seemed motionless. Was it asleep perhaps, weary from its workday, just riding with the waves? This was our chance, and Commander Farragut decided to take full advantage of it. He gave his orders. The frigate approached without making a sound, stopped two cable lengths from the animal. A profound silence reigned over the deck. We were not 100 feet from the blazing core of light, whose glow grew stronger and dazzled the eyes.\nJust then, leaning over the forecastle railing, I saw Ned Land below me, brandishing his dreadful harpoon. Barely twenty feet separated him from the motionless animal. All at once his arm shot forward and the harpoon was launched. I heard the weapon make a ringing sound as if it had hit some hard substance. The electric light suddenly went out, and two enormous waterspouts crashed onto the deck of the frigate, racing like a torrent from one end of the ship to the other, toppling crewmen, breaking spare masts and yardarms from their lashings. A hideous collision occurred, and thrown over the rail with no time to catch hold of it, I was hurled into the sea.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      Fill in the blanks with suitable words from the extract:\n      i) There was a mighty explosion, mingled with cheers from the ……………..\n      ii) The \'monster\' was in fact a …………………….\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Describe the cannoneer, who actually hit the monster.\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n        Give the meaning of the following words: \n        i) Enormous		ii) Hideous		iii) Cannoneer\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Pick out the common nouns from the following sentence:\n      The electric light suddenly went out, and two enormous waterspouts crashed onto t deck of the frigate.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n      How did the battle between the ship and the monster end? Find and copy the lines where you find the answer to this question.\n','','A1] i) crew		ii) submarine\nA2] The cannoneer who actually hit the monster, had calm eyes, cool features and an o grey beard.\nA3] i) Huge		ii) Very ugly\n       iii) A person who fires cannon, which is a large powerful gun that shoots heavy metal balls\nA4] Common nouns - Light, waterspouts, deck, frigate\nA5] When Ned Land launched the harpoon, the weapon made a ringing sound as if it had hit some hard surface. Thereafter, the electric light went out and two huge waterspouts crashed onto the deck of the frigate. As a result, the crew members lost their balance and the narrator was thrown into the sea. It broke the spare masts and yardarms. The following lines explain the ongoing battle between the ship and the monster:\n           \'I heard the weapon make a ringing sound as if it had hit some hard substance.’\n            ‘....two enormous waterspouts crashed onto the deck\'\n            ‘toppling crewman, breaking spare masts and yardarms\' \n            \'I was hurled into the sea.\'\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76753,1805,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','We stayed on the alert until daylight, getting ready for action. Whaling gear was set up along the railings. Our chief officer loaded the blunderbusses, which can launch harpoons as far as a mile, and long duck guns with exploding bullets that can wound and kill even the most powerful animals. Ned Land was content to sharpen his harpoon, a dreadful weapon in his hands.\nAt six o’clock day began to break, and with the dawn’s early light, the animal’s electric glow disappeared. At seven o’clock a very dense morning mist spread around us. Our best spyglasses were unable to pierce it. The outcome: disappointment and anger. \n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Write whether True or False.\n      i) We stayed on the alert until night and were getting ready for action.\n      ii) The mist was very dense.    \n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      What can the blunderbusses do?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      From the passage, find all the words and phrases used to describe the \'monster\'.    \n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Do as directed:\n     1) Ned had a dreadful weapon. (Add a question tag)\n     2) Pick out two abstract nouns from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n      Do you like travelling on sea? Why?\n','','A1] i) False 		ii) True.\nA2] The blunderbusses can launch harpoons as far as a mile.\nA3] Unearthly animal, fearsome creature, unknown creature, huge glowworm, infernal beast, motionless animal.\nA4] 1) Ned had a dreadful weapon, didn\'t he?\n       2) Disappointment, anger.\nA5] No, I do not like travelling on sea. I don\'t know how to swim and I am very scared of drowning.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76754,1806,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Michael Faraday is regarded as one of the most distinguished scientists and inventors of modern times, and his work on electricity is still a subject of study, in the form of Faraday’s Laws. But few know his inspirational life story, which is all about courage and fighting against the odds. Michael Faraday was born into a poverty stricken family in a dirty London suburb. He suffered from a speech defect as a child. He would pronounce ‘rabbit’ as ‘wabbit’. He could not even say his own name and would call himself ‘Fawaday’. Other children laughed at him and teachers did not help him either. When he was twelve, his mother was forced to take him out of school, thus putting an end to his formal education.\nAt thirteen, however, he started working with a bookbinder, binding hundreds of book during the day and staying up all night to read them. Reading thus became his obsession. One day he came across a book on electricity which had been sent to his master for binding. He started reading it and was completely hooked. That was his first introduction to the subject of electricity, which soon became a lifelong fascination.\nFaraday was still poor at twenty-one. Once, a friend gave him a free ticket to a public lecture and demonstration by the renowned chemist Humphry Davy at London’s Royal Institution. Davy’s work on chemicals and electrical lighting was the subject of conversation among the scientists of that age. Seventy years later, across the Atlantic Ocean in the USA, the same work enabled Thomas Edison to produce the first consistent light bulb.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false.\n      i) Faraday\'s Laws are no longer used today.\n     ii) Michael Faraday\'s teachers helped him a lot in becoming a scientist.\n     iii) Electricity became a lifelong fascination for Michael Faraday.\n     iv) Faraday\'s work on chemicals and electrical lighting helped Thomas Edison to produce the first consistent light bulb.\n','What were the odds against Faraday in his childhood?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n        What is the meaning of:\n        i) Reading became his obsession.\n        ii) Electricity became a lifelong fascination.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Give the verb form of the word \'fascination\'.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Why do you think Faraday\'s friend gave him a free ticket to Davy\'s programme?\n','','A1] i) False		ii) False		iii) True		iv) False\nA2] Faraday was born in a poor family and had a speech defect as a child. He pronounced \'r\' as \'w\' and was laughed at by other children in school. His teachers did not help him either. At the age of twelve, he had to leave his education and work with a bookbinder.\nA3] i) Faraday became fascinated with reading and read as many books as he could.\n       ii) The subject of \'electricity\' fascinated Faraday and he became interested in it for hu entire life.\nA4] Fascinate\nA5] Faraday\'s friend gave him a free ticket to Davy\'s programme because it was about chemicals and electrical lighting and the friend probably knew that Faraday was fascinated by the subject of electricity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76756,1806,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','That day in 1812 Faraday was spellbound by Davy’s lecture. He kept taking notes about the ‘mysterious force of electric fluid’. He was so engrossed in the lecture that he forgot to applaud with the rest of the crowd. When he went back, his notes were so comprehensive that he bound them into a book, meaning to gift it to Davy some day. Faraday decided that day that he didn’t just want to sell books, he wanted to be a great scientist - good enough to write his own books. Davy became his role model. But there was a problem. He did not have the social status, money or the education to pursue science. Faraday thought it would be wonderful if Davy became his mentor, but Davy did not agree initially. Faraday was not dejected; he just kept trying.\nDestiny had a strange plan in store for him. A few years later, a chemical explosion happened inside Davy’s lab and he was temporarily blinded. He now needed an assistant with an excellent memory to help him. He was reminded of Faraday and decided to hire him as his secretary. Davy never believed Faraday could do anything in the field of science going by his social status and education. He therefore dismissed Faraday’s aspirations and advised him to stick to bookbinding. But Faraday was relentless. He worked day and night and learnt as much as he could about Davy’s experiments. Soon Faraday became indispensable to Davy, and was promoted to his lab assistant. This was his first step towards a scientific career. Though much of his job now was cleaning labs, at least he got to see some of Davy’s leading experiments. \n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false.\n      i) Faraday became indispensable to Davy.\n     ii) The job as a lab assistant was Faraday\'s first step towards a scientific career.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Was Davy fair in his treatment of Faraday?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      1) Guess the meaning of the word \'mentor\'.\n      2) Give the antonym of the word \'bored\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       1) Give the noun form of the word \'aspire\' from the extract.\n       2) Identify the conjunction in the following sentence:\n           He was reminded of Faraday and decided to hire him as his secretary.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Why did Davy choose Faraday as his secretary?\n','','A1] i) True		ii) True\nA2] No, Davy wasn\'t fair with Faraday as he judged him based on his social status and education and did not even try to know his worth.\nA3] 1) An experienced person who trains others, a teacher\n       2) Spellbound\nA4] 1) Aspiration		2) Conjunction – and\nA5] Davy was temporarily blinded after a chemical explosion in his lab and needed an assistant with an excellent memory. He was reminded of Faraday and decided to hire him as his secretary, even though he never believed that Faraday could do anything in the field of science.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76757,1806,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Even then Davy did not have much hope for Faraday. Then Faraday got another chance to prove himself. One day Davy tried to re-create a famous electromagnetism experiment with fellow chemist William Wollaston, exploring why when an electric current is applied to a wire, it causes that wire to behave like a magnet. Obviously, the forces were connected but nobody had figured out how to make it happen continuously. Davy believed that if he could find out why it happened and controlled it, there could be many practical applications of the force. But he was unable to figure it out and was frustrated. He then teased Faraday, asking him to try his hand at it after he was done cleaning the lab.\nWithin a few days, Faraday solved the problem. In fact, he went further and the result was the first induction motor, which converted electrical current into continuous mechanical motion. The induction motor spurred a revolution. Fans, air conditioning, sewing machines, photographs, power tools, cars and even trains and aeroplane engines grew out of this simple device which was born out of mockery directed at Faraday.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false.\n      i) Davy and William Wollaston tried to re-create a famous electromagnetism experiment.\n     ii) Davy invented the induction motor.\n     iii) Davy asked Faraday to help him with the experiment he was working on.\n    iv) The induction motor spurred a revolution.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       An induction motor is a commonly used electrical machine. What examples of its use are given in the extract?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      1) Mention a phrase from the extract that means \'to discover\'.\n      2) Give a synonym for the word \'stimulated\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        Write the verb form of the word \'mechanical\'.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        Write what is implied in the sentence, \"Even then Davy did not have much hope for Faraday.\" What do the words \'even then\' suggest?\n','','A1] i) True		ii) False		iii) False		iv) True\nA2] According to the information given in the extract, the induction motor is used in fans, air conditioning, sewing machines, photographs, power tools, cars, trains and aeroplane engines.\nA3] 1) To figure out		2) Spurred\nA4] Mechanise\nA5] Faraday had become very important to Davy, but he still did not believe in his potential. The words \'even then\' suggest that even after proving his worth and dedication and showing that he had the potential, Davy thought Faraday would be unable to do anything remarkable in the field of science because of his lack education.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76759,1806,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Faraday became a celebrity scientist overnight. Nobody now cared about his social status or education; this young man had just created a revolution. One would think, as a teacher Davy was happy at his pupil’s achievement. But in reality he was jealous. People started telling Davy that of all his discoveries, the best was Faraday himself; this made him even more jealous. An angry Davy gave Faraday an impossible task to keep him out of his way. He handed him a piece of Bavarian glass, which was used in the lenses in telescopes and microscopes, and asked him to reverse engineer it. Bavarian glass was manufactured by a secret complicated process and Davy knew that with the equipment available in the lab Faraday would never be able to accomplish the task. This piece of glass became a significant thing in his life.\nFaraday had a never - give - up attitude and he respected Davy. So he accepted the assignment, despite knowing that it would be very difficult. He toiled for four years, with no help from Davy, and, as expected, failed. Faraday never learned the secret, and this remained his first failure as a scientist. To remind himself of these difficult times, he kept a single glass brick on his shelf as a souvenir. This would inspire him during difficult times.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        State whether the following statements are true or false.\n        i) Everybody looked down upon Faraday because of his social status, despite his invention.\n       ii) Bavarian glass could be easily made in any lab.\n       iii) Faraday never learnt the secret process of manufacturing Bavarian glass.\n       iv) Faraday kept a single Bavarian glass brick on his shelf as a souvenir.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        Write what is implied in the sentence, \"People started telling Davy that of all his discoveries, the best was Faraday himself.\" What does it suggest about Davy\'s work?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>	\n       1) Guess the meaning of \'reverse engineer it\'.\n       2) Give the synonym of the word \'student\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Write the noun form of the word \'inspire\'.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Why did Davy ask Faraday to reverse engineer a piece of Bavarian glass?\n','','A1] i) False		ii) False		iii) True		iv) True\nA2] This line implies that people praised Faraday\'s invention more than Davy\'s. It also suggests that Davy had made many good discoveries in the past.\nA3] 1) To reproduce someone else\'s product by observing its construction or composition in detail\n        2) Pupil\nA4] Inspiration\nA5] Davy was jealous of the praise that Faraday received after inventing induction motor. He knew that it was impossible to reverse engineer a piece of Bavarian glass using the equipment available in the lab. So he asked Faraday to do it, to keep him busy in a useless job and thus, keeping him out of his way.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76760,1806,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','In 1829, Davy died and Faraday succeeded him as head of the laboratory. He was free to pursue whatever he liked, and he made another revolutionary discovery. He noticed that if he moved a magnet, it could produce electrical current; thus he could now convert motion into electricity. This is how the electrical generator was born, something still used today to generate all kinds of power, like dynamos and other devices.\nFaraday was now a legend. In 1840, he developed memory loss, which continued for the rest of his life. But the disease did not stop him. He persevered, starting a complicated experiment to prove that light was closely related to electricity and magnetism - a novel thought in those times.\nRemember that piece of Bavarian glass Faraday had kept on his shelf ? He was determined to convert the reminder of his first major failure to an instrument of great success. He used the same glass now to show that in the presence of a magnet, light could be isolated into a single wave rather than spreading out randomly in all directions, a concept called polarization.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false. \n       i) Faraday succeeded Davy as the head of the laboratory after Davy\'s death.\n      ii) Faraday developed a memory loss and discontinued his research.\n     iii) The thought that light was closely related to electricity and magnetism was novel in those times.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Write the idea behind an electrical generator.\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       1) Make a sentence using the word \'pursue\'.\n       2) Give a synonym for the word \'celebrity\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Write two countable and two uncountable nouns from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       What complicated experiment did Faraday begin after he developed a memory loss?\n','','A1] i) True		ii) False		iii) True\nA2] Faraday noticed that if he moved a magnet, it could produce electrical current. Th idea of converting motion into electricity led to the invention of an electrical generator.\nA3] 1) I want to pursue a course in medicine\n       2) Legend\nA4] Countable nouns - magnet, glass \n       Uncountable nouns - electricity, success\nA5] After developing a memory loss, Faraday began a complicated experiment to prove the light was closely related to electricity and magnetism.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76761,1806,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','He then took the age - old experiment of sprinkling iron filings on a sheet of paper near a magnet, making circular patterns. He went on to prove that these patterns were not a property of the iron filings; in fact they were due to the invisible magnetic fields that filled the empty space around the magnet and hence disturbed the filings. This is where his lack of formal education went against him. Faraday did not know much about advanced mathematics, so he just copied the iron filing patterns with his hand. He was unable to explain them in the form of mathematical equations. He made hundreds of such drawings but without equations, they were not accepted. \nFortune favoured the brave Faraday once again when he met James Maxwell, a wealthy, educated physicist well versed in mathematics. He was willing to work with Faraday. It was Maxwell who translated Faraday’s idea into a set of equations that are now called Maxwell’s equations. Their combined work has helped us in many ways. Electronics and communication system today are designed around their discoveries. Some day, we might even be able to communicate with aliens across different galaxies using the products of these discoveries.\nFaraday’s life started with difficulties, but as a great he met each difficulty with perseverance and conviction. He was given impossible tasks, which he undertook as challenges and opportunities. He epitomizes what the Walt Disney character Pinocchio said: \n‘When you wish upon a star It does not matter who you are’.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false.\n      i) Faraday knew a lot about advanced Mathematics.\n      ii) Faraday\'s idea was translated by James Maxwell into a set of equations.\n      iii) We have been able to communicate with aliens using Maxwell and Faraday\'s discoveries.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Why were Faraday\'s drawings not accepted? Does it mean they were wrong?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n        1) Give the synonym of \'luck\' from the extract.\n        2) Give the antonym of the word \'doubt\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adjective form of the word \'mathematics\' from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Michael Faraday said, \"In order to succeed, your desire for success should be greater than your fear of failure.\" Do you agree with him? Give reason for your answer.\n','','A1] i) False		ii) True			iii) False\nA2] Faraday lacked formal education and could not explain his mathematical equations, which is why they were not accepted. It does not mean that they were wrong, it just means that they were incomplete.\nA3] 1) Fortune			2) Conviction\nA4] Mathematical\nA5] Yes, I agree with Michael Faraday. If we are afraid of bad consequences and do not try. fearing we might fail, we might never accomplish anything. If we concentrate on success and keep trying even if we fail in the beginning, we will surely succeed later.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76762,1807,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">If I were Lord of Tartary, \nMyself and me alone, \nMy bed should be of ivory, \nOf beaten gold my throne; \nAnd in my court should peacocks flaunt, \nAnd in my forests tigers haunt, \nAnd in my pools great fishes slant \nTheir fins athwart the sun.\nIf I were Lord of Tartary, \nTrumpeters every day \nTo every meal would summon me, \nAnd in my courtyard bray; \nAnd in the evening lamps would shine, \nYellow as honey, red as wine, \nWhile harp, and flute, and mandoline, \nMade music sweet and gay. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n       ……………………. music (complete the phrase with the help of the extract)\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Find and write the names of the exotic musical instruments and animals mentioned in the extract.\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n        Pick out three nouns from the extract.\n','','A1] Sweet and gay\nA2] Musical instruments - trumpet, harp, flute, mandolin \n       Animals - peacock, tiger, fish\nA3] i) Court		ii) Fins		iii) Music\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76763,1807,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">If I were Lord of Tartary, \nI’d wear a robe of beads, \nWhite, and gold, and green they’d be \nAnd clustered thick as seeds; \nAnd ere should wane the morning-star, \nI’d don my robe and scimitar, \nAnd zebras seven should draw my car \nThrough Tartary’s dark glades.\nLord of the fruits of Tartary, \nHer rivers silver-pale! \nLord of the hills of Tartary, \nGlen, thicket, wood, and dale! \nHer flashing stars, her scented breeze, \nHer trembling lake like foamless seas, \nHer bird-delighting citron-trees \nIn every purple vale! <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        Find and write the names of the exotic musical instruments and animals mentioned in the extract.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Complete the following phrases with the help of the extract:\n       i) ……………. Rivers		ii) …………………. Breeze		\n      iii) …………….. lake		iv) ………………. citron-trees\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question:</b>\n      Pick out three verbs from the extract. \n','','A1] Animals – zebra\nA2] i) Silver-pale	ii) Scented		ii) Trembling		iv) Bird-delighting\nA3] i) Wear		ii) Don			iii) Draw\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76764,1807,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">If I were Lord of Tartary, \nTrumpeters every day \nTo every meal would summon me, \nAnd in my courtyard bray; \nAnd in the evening lamps would shine, \nYellow as honey, red as wine, \nWhile harp, and flute, and mandoline, \nMade music sweet and gay. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Complete the sentences.\n      i) My bed should be ……………….\n      ii) And in my forests ………………….\n      iii) Yellow as honey, ………………….\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      How does the poet begin the poem?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n      Find the words that mean the following, from the first stanza.\n     i) Gold hammered into a flat, thin shape\n     ii) Show off proudly\n     iii) Tilt, move at an angle\n    iv) Across, especially in a slanting direction\n','','A1] i) of ivory			ii) tigers haunt			iii) red as wine\nA2] The poet begins the poem on the assumption that if he were the Lord of Tartary and wants all things beautiful for himself like a little child \"myself and me alone.\"\nA3] i) beaten gold 		ii) flaunt		iii) slant		iv) athwart\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76765,1807,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">If I were Lord of Tartary, \nI’d wear a robe of beads, \nWhite, and gold, and green they’d be \nAnd clustered thick as seeds; \nAnd ere should wane the morning-star, \nI’d don my robe and scimitar, \nAnd zebras seven should draw my car \nThrough Tartary’s dark glades.\nLord of the fruits of Tartary, \nHer rivers silver-pale! \nLord of the hills of Tartary, \nGlen, thicket, wood, and dale! \nHer flashing stars, her scented breeze, \nHer trembling lake like foamless seas, \nHer bird-delighting citron-trees \nIn every purple vale! <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Complete the sentences.\n       i) The lake of Tartary trembles like ………………..\n      ii) The purple dale has ………………..\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       How does the poet want his robe to be?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n        Pick out two pairs of rhyming words from the poem.\n','','A1] i) foamless seas			ii) bird-delighting citron trees\nA2] The poet wants his robe to be made of white, golden and green coloured beads placed quite close to one another.\nA3] i) pale-dale		ii) beads-seeds\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76766,1808,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Getting ready for the programme \nGood Morning friends ! It is a pleasure for me to welcome you all to this festive occasion. Please take your seats. May I request you to keep your mobiles switched off or on silent mode ? Let us ring in to the merry mood of youthful creative expressions and forget our preoccupations and ringtones for the time being.\nArrival of the dignitaries I am happy to announce that the dignitaries have arrived. I request you all to be seated. I request our Head Girl Miss Shubhada Murarka to escort the dignitaries to the dais.\nCommencement of the programme \nHonourable Chief Guest of today’s function an artist of great renown Shri. Charudatta Diwan, President of Balanand Education Society, Shri. Avadhoot Pathak, Our Principal Dr Ajinkya Parakhi, Vice Principal Dr Shaila Singh, all our invited guests, parents, teachers and my dear friends, on behalf of Balanand Vidyalaya I extend a hearty welcome to you all. We have gathered here for the inauguration of our School Art Festival.\nLighting the lamp\nLet us begin this auspicious occasion with something symbolic of knowledge and learning. And for this I would like to request all the honourable members on the dais to kindly light the traditional lamp and offer prayers ... Friends, look at the amazing quality of this lamp; when lit, not only does it carry the tremendous potential to break the darkness of the night but also leaves way for a thousand other lamps to be lit from its flames. Likened to the lamp is knowledge. Knowledge, too, spreads light. It neither divides nor subtracts. It only adds and multiplies. Miss Anagha Bhatia of Std VII and her group will now sing the School Song.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n       Fill in the blanks with suitable words from the extract:\n      i) The head girl\'s name is Miss ………………\n      ii) Miss ………….. and her group sang the School Song.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n           Why does the compère request the audience to switch off their mobile phones?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the word \'festive\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        Identify the subject and the predicate in the following sentence: \n         We have gathered here for the inauguration of our School Art Festival.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       At the beginning of the programme, who does the compère address by name?\n','','A1] i) Shubhada Murarka		ii) Anagha Bhatia\nA2] The compère requests the audience to switch off their mobile phones so that they can enjoy the show and not be disturbed or cause disturbance during the programme.\nA3] Joyous\nA4] We - Subject\n       have gathered here for the inauguration of our School Art Festival – Predicate\nA5] The compere addresses the head girl Miss Shubhada Murarka to escort the dignitaries to the dias. At the beginning of the programme, the compère addresses the dignitaries-Honourable Chief Guest, Shri. Charudatta Diwan. President of the Balanand Education Society, Shri Avadhoot Pathak, Principal of the school. Dr. Ajinkya Parakhi and Vice Principal Dr. Shaila Singh by name.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76767,1808,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Welcome and introduction of the guests \nAtithi Devo Bhava ! Our Indian culture regards guests as gods. Let us welcome and honour our distinguished guests. It is indeed our proud privilege to have amidst us a very eminent personality from the field of Art and Culture, renowned Artist, a Painter of International Repute, and proud recipient of many prestigious awards, President of Kala Ranjan Academy Shri. Charudatta Diwan. I would request our Principal to welcome him by offering a token of our love and appreciation - books - Two Volumes of Cherished Lives of Great Artists. We are offering books because flowers might perish but books can stay forever ... Thank you, Sir. I would now request the Convener of this Art Festival, our Art teacher, respected Ms Shilpa Sanghani to welcome the beacon of our institution, the President of Balanand Academy, Mr Avadhoot Pathak ... I would now request our School Captain and Cultural in-charge Ms Shubhada Murarka to welcome our ever inspiring dynamic Principal Dr Ajinkya Parakhi Sir ... Our enthusiastic Vice-Principal will be welcomed by Ms Anagha Bhatia ... Thank you, Dear Anagha.\nIntroductory speech \nTo appraise you about the Art Festival, I request the Principal, Respected Dr Ajinkya Parakhi to deliver his introductory speech ...\nBook release \nFriends, we have prepared here a volume comprising photographs of all the beautiful and unique pieces of art which are displayed today in the auditorium. They are the artistic and skilful contributions from our students. I request the Chief Guest to release this special book on this occasion and formally inaugurate the art festival ... Thank you, Sir. I know this has aroused curiosity in you to see the exhibition. After this programme you can take that pleasure.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Who does the compère thank? \n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      What do we learn about Shri. Charudatta Diwan from the compère\'s speech?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Guess the meaning of \'Atithi Devo Bhava\'.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the noun form of the word \'cultural\' from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Describe the book that is released.\n','','A1] The compère thanks the Principal.\nA2] From the compère\'s speech, we learn that Shri. Charudatta Diwan is a famous personality from the field of Art and Culture and the President of Kala Ranjan Academy. He is a well-known artist, an internationally-known painter and has received many impressive awards.\nA3] \'Guest is equivalent to God\'\nA4] Culture\nA5] The book that is released includes the photographs of all the beautiful and unique pieces of art displayed in the auditorium of the school. These pieces of art are the artistic and skilful contributions of the students.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76768,1808,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Cultural programme \nI know you all are eagerly waiting to listen to the gems of music from our school. We now invite this talented band of musicians to display their talent. We proudly present the Taal Kacheri. Sahil of Std X A will give a vocal rendition and the same piece will be demonstrated on different instruments. Let’s enjoy the Taal kacheri. Varsha on Mridangam, Vivek on Dholak, Zubin on Tabla and Govind on Ghatam ... Let us have a big round of applause for the excellent demonstration. Different States display their unique identity through their respective culture. Std VII C will now give a beautiful presentation of the famous Koli Dance from Maharashtra ... What a wonderful presentation! Now a light comedy play will be presented by std IX C. I would like to request Std IX artists to perform their play - The Boy Comes Home by A.A.Milne ... That was indeed a fantastic performance!\nPrize distribution \nYou are all eagerly waiting for the prize distribution. There are butterflies in your stomachs. You will soon be relieved of your anxiety. Everyone is unique in his or her potential. All are talented, yes, but some do perform better and their talent needs to be appreciated. I would now invite the Chief Guest to declare the prize winners and give away the prizes for the different categories of Art ... Thank you, Sir.\nAddress by the Chief Guest \nI would now request our illustrious Chief Guest to address the audience ... Thank you, Sir, your speech has given us wonderful insights on various aspects of Art and Culture. We are immensely grateful to you for your invaluable words of enlightenment.\nVote of thanks \nBefore we draw the curtain I take the opportunity to thank all those who have lent a helping hand to make this Art Festival meaningful and for the formal vote of thanks I invite the convener of this programme, our Art teacher Ms Shilpa Sanghani.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Guess the meaning of Taal Kacheri.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Who is taking part in the Taal Kacheri?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the phrase \'butterflies in your stomach\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       List three adjectives from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n      Does the compère say the following lines at the beginning of the programme or after it has ended?\n       i) What a wonderful presentation!\n       ii) That was indeed a fantastic performance!\n','','A1] Taal Kacheri is a kind of musical concert where percussion instruments such as Dholak. Mridangam. Tabla and others are used.\nA2] A talented band of musicians, namely Sahil doing the vocal rendition. Varsha on Mridangam, Vivek on Dholak, Zubin on Tabla and Govind on Ghatam are taking part in the Taal Kacheri.\nA3] A feeling of nervous excitement\nA4] i) Talented		ii) Excellent		iii) Respective\nA5] The above lines are said at the end of two different performances. The compère says. \'What a wonderful presentation\' at the end of a koli dance presented by Class VII \"C\" students and That was indeed a fantastic performance!\' at the end of a stage play performed by Class IX \'C\' students.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76769,1808,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','We have gathered here for the inauguration of our School Art Festival.\nLighting the lamp \nLet us begin this auspicious occasion with something symbolic of knowledge and learning. And for this I would like to request all the honourable members on the dais to kindly light the traditional lamp and offer prayers ... Friends, look at the amazing quality of this lamp; when lit, not only does it carry the tremendous potential to break the darkness of the night but also leaves way for a thousand other lamps to be lit from its flames. Likened to the lamp is knowledge. Knowledge, too, spreads light. It neither divides nor subtracts. It only adds and multiplies. Miss Anagha Bhatia of Std VII and her group will now sing the School Song.\nWelcome and introduction of the guests \nAtithi Devo Bhava! Our Indian culture regards guests as gods. Let us welcome and honour our distinguished guests. It is indeed our proud privilege to have amidst us a very eminent personality from the field of Art and Culture, renowned Artist, a Painter of International Repute, and proud recipient of many prestigious awards, President of Kala Ranjan Academy Shri. Charudatta Diwan. I would request our Principal to welcome him by offering a token of our love and appreciation - books - Two Volumes of Cherished Lives of Great Artists. We are offering books because flowers might perish but books can stay forever ... Thank you, Sir.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Complete the following sentences:\n       i) Our culture regard guests as ……………….\n       ii) We have gathered here for the inauguration of our ……………….\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      Why is the lamp said to be having an amazing quality?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give one word for the following:\n      i) the formal beginning of an event		ii) one who receives\n      iii) having high status				iv) noteworthy and important\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Do as directed:\n        1) Knowledge, too, spreads light (Rewrite the sentence removing \'too\')\n        2) It is indeed a proud privilege. (Make exclamatory)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n      Do you have a school song? What does it tell you?\n','','A1] i) Gods 			ii) School Art Festival\nA2] A lamp can dispel or remove darkness and also help to light a thousand other lamps from its flames. Hence it is said to possess an amazing quality.\nA3] i) inauguration 		ii) recipient 		iii) prestigious 		iv) eminent\nA4] 1) Knowledge also spreads light.\n       2) What a proud privilege it is!\nA5] Yes, we have a school song by the name \'The Temple of Learning\'. It tells us about how a school acts as a sheltered cocoon for students preparing them to take a flight spreading their wings in the beautiful world outside.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76770,1809,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The young crow had fallen from its nest and was fluttering about on the road, in danger of being crushed by a cart or a tonga, or seized by a cat, when I picked it up and brought it home. It was in a sorry condition, beak gaping and head dropping, and we did not expect it to live. But Grandfather and I did our best to bring it round. We fed it by prizing its beak gently open with a pencil, pushing in a little bread and milk, and then removing the pencil to allow it to swallow. We varied this diet with occasional doses of Grandmother’s home-made plum wine, and as a result, the young crow was soon on the road to recovery.\nHe was offered his freedom but he did not take it. Instead he made himself at home in the house. Grandmother, Aunt Mabel, and even some of Grandfather’s pets objected; but there was no way of getting rid of the bird. He took over the administration of the house.\nWe were not sure that he was male, but we called him Caesar. Before long, Caesar was joining us at meal times, besides finding his own grubs or beetles in the garden. He danced about on the dining table and gave us no peace until he had been given his small bowl of meat and soup and vegetables. He was always restless, fidgeting z What is implied in ‘He took over the administration of the house? about, investigating things. He would hop across a table to empty a match-box of its content, or rip the daily paper to shreds, or overturn a vase of flowers, or tug at the tail of one of the dogs.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        What dangers were likely to befall the young crow?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n         Who were the other members of the author\'s family living in the same house?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      Find antonyms for the following words from the extract.\n       i) Captivity			ii) Motionless\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adjective form of the word \'occasion\' from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Did the author\'s Grandfather like animals? How do we know that? Give two example from this extract to support your answer.\n','','A1] The dangers of being crushed by a cart or tonga or seized by a cat on the road we likely to befall the young crow.\nA2] The other members of the author\'s family living in the same house were grandmother, grandfather and grandfather\'s pets.\nA3] i) Freedom		ii) Restless\nA4] Occasional\nA5] Yes, the author\'s Grandfather liked animals a lot. The following examples from the extract suggest it:\n       i) Grandfather took care of the crow and helped it recover along with the author.\n       ii) Grandfather also had many other pets.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76771,1809,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','‘That crow will be the ruin of us !’ grumbled Grandmother, picking marigolds off the carpet. ‘Can’t you keep him in a cage ?’ We did try keeping Caesar in cage, but he was so angry, and objected with such fierce cawing and flapping, that it was better for our nerves and peace of mind to give him the run of the house. He did not show any inclination to join the other crows in the banyan tree. Grandfather said this was because he was really a jungle crow - a raven of sorts - and probably felt a little contemptuous of very ordinary carrion crows. But it seemed to me that Caesar, having grown used to living with humans on equal terms, had become snobbish and did not wish to mix with his own kind. He would even squabble with Harold the Hornbill. Perching on top of Harold’s cage, he would peck at the big bird’s feet, whereupon Harold would swear and scold and try to catch Caesar through the bars.\nIn time, Caesar learnt to talk a little - as ravens sometimes do - in a cracked, throaty voice. He would sit for hours outside the window, banging on the glass with his beak and calling, ‘Hello, hello’. He seemed to recognise the click of the gate when I came home from school, and would come to the door with a hop, skip and jump, saying, ‘Hello, hello!’ I had also taught him to sit on my arm and say ‘Kiss, kiss’, while he placed his head gently against my mouth.\nOn one of Aunt Mabel’s visits, Caesar alighted on her arm and cackled, “Kiss, Kiss!” Aunt Mable was delighted - and possibly flattered - and leant forward for a kiss. But Caesar’s attention shifted to my aunt’s gleaming spectacles, and thrusting at them with his beak, he knocked them off. Aunt Mabel never was a success with the pets.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Guess how Caesar learnt to say \'Hello, hello\'.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What is meant by \'Aunt Mabel never was a success with the pets? Can you think of the reasons why it was so?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n        Give the synonyms for the following words from the extract:\n        i) Rebuke			ii) Achievement\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the verb form of the word \'recognition\' from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        What did Caesar do when the author kept him in a cage?\n','','A1] Caesar may have heard the author greeting someone after returning from school. That is how Caesar might have learnt to say \'Hello, hello\'.\nA2] This line means that the relationship between Aunt Mabel and the pets was never good. The reason for this might be that Aunt Mabel did not like animals. Animals tend to have a keen sense of their surroundings and would have felt the uneasiness. So. even they reacted negatively to her.\nA3] i) Scold		ii) Success\nA4] Recognise\nA5] When the author kept Caesar in a cage, he objected and showed his anger by cawing fiercely as well as flapping his wings and thus, disturbing the peace of the house till he was set free.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76772,1809,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Pet or pest ? Grandmother insisted that Caesar was a pest, in spite of his engaging habits. If he had restricted his activities to our own house, it would not have been so bad; but he took to visiting neighbouring houses and stealing pens and pencils, hair-ribbons, combs, keys, shuttlecocks, tooth brushes and false teeth. He was especially fond of toothbrushes, and made a collection of them on top of the cupboard in my room. Most of the neighbours were represented in our house by a toothbrush. Toothbrush sales went up that year. So did Grandmother’s blood-pressure.\nCaesar spied on children going into the bania’s shop, and often managed to snatch sweets from them as they came out. Clothes pegs fascinated him. Neighbours would return from the bazar to find their washing lying in the mud, and no sign of the pegs. These, too, found their way to the top of my cupboard.\nIt was Caesar’s gardening activities that finally led to disaster. He was helping himself to our neighbour’s beans when a stick was flung at him, breaking his leg. I carried the unfortunate bird home, and Grandfather and I washed and bandaged his leg as best we could. But it would not mend. Caesar hung his head and no longer talked. He grew weaker day by day, refusing to eat. An occasional sip of Grandmother’s homemade wine was all that kept him going.\nOne morning I found him dead on the sofa, his legs stiff in the air. Poor Caesar ! His anti-social habits had led to his early end.\nI dug a shallow grave in the garden, and buried him there, along with all the toothbrushes and clothes pegs he had taken so much trouble to collect.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       List all the things that Caesar stole from the neighbouring houses.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      What is implied in the sentence, \"Most of the neighbours were represented in our house by a toothbrush.\"?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      Give the antonym for the word \'deep\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Identify the proper noun and the common noun in the following sentence:\n       Caesar hung his head and no longer talked.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n      What actions of the author show that he loved Caesar?\n','','A1] Pens, pencils, ribbons, combs, keys, shuttlecocks, toothbrushes, clothes pegs\nA2] This line suggests that Caesar had collected at least one toothbrush as a representation of most of the neighbours. He kept them on the cupboard in the author\'s bedroom. Each toothbrush represented a neighbour, to whom it originally belonged.\nA3] Shallow\nA4] Caesar - Proper Noun; Head - Common Noun\nA5] The author did not consider Caesar\'s mischievous activities to be bad, but instead he found them appealing. He took care of Caesar after he was injured and was upset up his death. He buried all the toothbrushes and clothes pegs along with him. All these actions show that the author loved Caesar.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76774,1809,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The young crow had fallen from its nest and was fluttering about on the road, in danger of being crushed by a cart or a tonga, or seized by a cat, when I picked it up and brought it home. It was in a sorry condition, beak gaping and head dropping, and we did not expect it to live. But Grandfather and I did our best to bring it round. We fed it by prizing its beak gently open with a pencil, pushing in a little bread and milk, and then removing the pencil to allow it to swallow. We varied this diet with occasional doses of Grandmother’s home-made plum wine, and as a result, the young crow was soon on the road to recovery.\nHe was offered his freedom but he did not take it. Instead he made himself at home in the house. Grandmother, Aunt Mabel, and even some of Grandfather’s pets objected; but there was no way of getting rid of the bird. He took over the administration of the house. We were not sure that he was male, but we called him Caesar.\nBefore long, Caesar was joining us at meal times, besides finding his own grubs or beetles in the garden. He danced about on the dining table and gave us no peace until he had been given his small bowl of meat and soup and vegetables. He was always restless, fidgeting about, investigating things. He would hop across a table to empty a match-box of its content, or rip the daily paper to shreds, or overturn a vase of flowers, or tug at the tail of one of the dogs.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Match the columns.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>‘A’</th>\n    <th>‘B’</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) A horse drawn vehicle.</td>\n    <td>a) shreds</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii) Make small nervous movement</td>\n    <td>b) prizing</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii) Torn strips of paper.</td>\n    <td>c) tonga</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv) Use force to move apart.</td>\n    <td>d) fidget</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      What dangers were likely to befall the young crow?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      Use appropriate form of the word given in brackets.\n      i) I picked it up and ……………….. it home. (bring)\n      ii) He was …………….. his freedom (offer)\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Complete the table.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Present Tense</th>\n    <th>Past Tense</th>\n    <th>Past Participle</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) -------------------</td>\n    <td>brought</td>\n    <td>brought</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii) fall</td>\n    <td>-------------------</td>\n    <td>fallen</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii) -------------------</td>\n    <td>kept</td>\n    <td>kept</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv) give</td>\n    <td>-------------------</td>\n    <td>given</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       What pranks did Caesar engage in?\n\n','','A1] (i-c), (ii - d) (iii - a) (iv - b).\nA2] The Young crow was in danger of being crushed by a cart or tonga or seized by a cat.\nA3] i) I picked it up and brought it home.\n       ii) He was offered his freedom.\nA4] i) bring 		ii) fell 		iii) keep 		iv) gave.\nA5] During meal times Caesar would dance about on the dining table until he was given a bowl of meat soup and vegetables. He would empty the contents of the match-box, rip the daily paper to shreds, overturn a vase of flowers or tug at the tail of one of the dogs.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76775,1809,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','We did try keeping Caesar in cage, but he was so angry, and objected with such fierce cawing and flapping, that it was better for our nerves and peace of mind to give him the run of the house. He did not show any inclination to join the other crows in the banyan tree. Grandfather said this was because he was really a jungle crow - a raven of sorts - and probably felt a little contemptuous of very ordinary carrion crows. But it seemed to me that Caesar, having grown used to living with humans on equal terms, had become snobbish and did not wish to mix with his own kind. He would even squabble with Harold the Hornbill. Perching on top of Harold’s cage, he would peck at the big bird’s feet, whereupon Harold would swear and scold and try to catch Caesar through the bars.\nIn time, Caesar learnt to talk a little - as ravens sometimes do - in a cracked, throaty voice. He would sit for hours outside the window, banging on the glass with his beak and calling, ‘Hello, hello’. He seemed to recognise the click of the gate when I came home from school, and would come to the door with a hop, skip and jump, saying, ‘Hello, hello!’ I had also taught him to sit on my arm and say ‘Kiss, kiss’, while he placed his head gently against my mouth.\nOn one of Aunt Mabel’s visits, Caesar alighted on her arm and cackled, “Kiss, Kiss!” Aunt Mable was delighted - and possibly flattered - and leant forward for a kiss. But Caesar’s attention shifted to my aunt’s gleaming spectacles, and thrusting at them with his beak, he knocked them off. Aunt Mabel never was a success with the pets.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Complete the statements.\n      i) Caesar did not show any inclination to ……………………\n     ii) Perching on top of Harold\'s cage, Caesar would ……………..\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Why did the writer not cage Caesar?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      Give verb forms.\n      i) little 		ii) inclination\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Do as directed:\n      1) He would sit for hours outside the window. (Rewrite using modal auxiliary of past ability.)\n      2) I had also taught him to sit on my arm. (Pick out the infinitive.)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        Which two contrasting traits of Caesar does the passage highlight? Do all animals and birds have this?\n','','A1] i) join the other crows in the banyan tree\n       ii) peck at the big bird\'s feet\nA2] When the writer put Caesar in a cage, he cawed and flapped his wings so fiercely that they thought that for their own peace of mind, it was best not to cage him.\nA3] i) belittle		ii) incline\nA4] 1) He could sit for hours outside the window.\n       2) to sit - infinitive.\nA5] Caesar on one hand is a very snobbish and mischievous crow troubling the Hornbill and not mingling with other crows. But on the other hand, he comes across as a very loving bird greeting the writer and kissing him. Yes, I think all animals and birds have these contrasting traits just as we humans do. They are good to some people and mischievous with the ones who don\'t like them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76776,1809,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Pet or pest ? Grandmother insisted that Caesar was a pest, in spite of his engaging habits. If he had restricted his activities to our own house, it would not have been so bad; but he took to visiting neighbouring houses and stealing pens and pencils, hair-ribbons, combs, keys, shuttlecocks, tooth brushes and false teeth. He was especially fond of toothbrushes, and made a collection of them on top of the cupboard in my room. Most of the neighbours were represented in our house by a toothbrush. Toothbrush sales went up that year. So did Grandmother’s blood-pressure.\nCaesar spied on children going into the bania’s shop, and often managed to snatch sweets from them as they came out. Clothes pegs fascinated him. Neighbours would return from the bazar to find their washing lying in the mud, and no sign of the pegs. These, too, found their way to the top of my cupboard.\nIt was Caesar’s gardening activities that finally led to disaster. He was helping himself to our neighbour’s beans when a stick was flung at him, breaking his leg. I carried the unfortunate bird home, and Grandfather and I washed and bandaged his leg as best we could. But it would not mend. Caesar hung his head and no longer talked. He grew weaker day by day, refusing to eat. An occasional sip of Grandmother’s homemade wine was all that kept him going.\nOne morning I found him dead on the sofa, his legs stiff in the air. Poor Caesar! His anti-social habits had led to his early end. I dug a shallow grave in the garden, and buried him there, along with all the toothbrushes and clothes pegs he had taken so much trouble to collect.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        State whether the following sentences true or false.\n       i) Caesar often managed to snatch sweets from children.\n       ii) An occasional sip of Grandmother\'s wine finally led to disaster.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Why did Grandmother\'s blood pressure go up?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give noun forms for the following:\n       i) restrict		ii) fascinate		iii) spy		iv) refuse\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        Do as directed:\n         Grandfather and I washed and bandaged his leg and gave a sip of homemade wine.\n           (Rewrite using \'Not only but also\')\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Do you think the author had any feelings when he found Caesar dead?\n','','A1] i) true 		ii) false.\nA2] Watching Caesar steal combs, keys pens, pencils etc. from neighboring houses, Grandmother\'s blood pressure went up.\nA3] i) restriction		ii) fascination		iii) spy		iv) refusal\nA4] Grandfather and I not only washed and bandaged his leg but also gave a sip of homemade wine.\nA5] The expression \"Poor Caesar!\" by the author shows that he had a soft corner for Caesar. They both shared a loving relationship with Caesar welcoming the author back from school saying, \'hello, hello\' and \"Kiss, Kiss\" by sitting on his arm and placing his head against the author\'s mouth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76778,1810,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">I come from haunts of coot and hern,\nI make a sudden sally \nAnd sparkle out among the fern, \nTo bicker down a valley. \nBy thirty hills I hurry down, \nOr slip between the ridges, \nBy twenty thorpes, a little town, \nAn half a hundred bridges. \nTill last by Philip’s farm I flow \nTo join the brimming river, \nFor men may come and men may go, \nBut I go on for ever. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n       Where does the brook join the river?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        Mention the various places that the brook flows past.\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the following words:\n       i) Ridges		ii) Brimming\n','','A1] The brook joins the river near Philip\'s farm.\nA2] The brook flows past the haunts of coot and hern, the ferns, a valley, thirty hills, the ridges, twenty thorpes, a little town, fifty bridges and Philip\'s farm.\nA3] i) Long narrow hills or mountains\n       ii) Be full, almost to a point that it may overflow\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76779,1810,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">I chatter over stony ways, \nIn little sharps and trebles, \nI bubble into eddying bays, \nI babble on the pebbles. \nWith many a curve my banks I fret \nBy many a field and fallow, \nAnd many a fairy foreland set \nWith willow-weed and mallow. \nI chatter, chatter, as I flow \nTo join the brimming river, \nFor men may come and men may go, \nBut I go on for ever. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       The brook speaks of itself as if it is human. Find one example of human activity of the brook.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Mention the various places that the brook flows past.\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the following words:\n        i) Eddying		ii) Babble		iii) Fallow\n','','A1] \'I fret over the many curves of my banks\' is an example of human activity of the brook.\nA2] The brook flows past stony ways, bays, pebbles, fields, fallows, forelands and wilderness.\nA3] i) Moving in circles; spinning\n      ii) To make murmuring sound of water flowing over stones\n     iii) Uncultivated land\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76780,1810,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">I wind about, and in and out, \nWith here a blossom sailing, \nAnd here and there a lusty trout, \nAnd here and there a grayling, \nAnd here and there a foamy flake \nUpon me, as I travel \nWith many a silvery waterbreak \nAbove the golden gravel, \nAnd draw them all along, and flow \nTo join the brimming river \nFor men may come and men may go, \nBut I go on for ever. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       The brook speaks of itself as if it is human. Find one example of human activity of the brook.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       The poet uses words to create pictures or \'images\' in the reader\'s mind. Write down on line from the extract that creates images or pictures in your mind\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n      Spot and write any three alliterative phrases or sentences from the extract.\n','','A1] \'as I travel\' is an example of the brook\'s human-like activity.\nA2] \"With many a silvery waterbreak, Above the golden gravel\" creates an image of the brook flowing over golden gravel and the water shining like silver as it crashes against the pebbles.\nA3] i) \"And here and there a foamy flake\" - here, the sound of \'f is repeated.\n       ii) \"Above the golden gravel\" here, the sound of \'g\' is repeated.\n       iii) \"And draw them all along, and flow\" - here, the sound of \'a\' is repeated.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76781,1810,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">I slip, I slide, I gloom, I glance, \nAmong my skimming swallows; \nI make the netted sunbeam dance \nAgainst my sandy shallows. \nI murmur under moon and stars \nIn brambly wildernesses; \nI linger by my shingly bars; \nI loiter round my cresses; \nAnd out again I curve and flow \nTo join the brimming river, \nFor men may come and men may go, \nBut I go on for ever. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       The brook speaks of itself as if it is human. Find one example of human activity of the brook.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Which lines are repeated in the poem \"The Brook\'? What do they mean?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       Spot and write any three alliterative phrases or sentences from the extract.\n','','A1] \"I linger by my shingly bars\" is an example of human activity of the brook.\nA2] The lines \"To join the brimming river, For men may come and men may go. But I go on for ever,\" are repeated in the poem \"The Brook\'. The first line of the stanza is however different each time and suggests the different ways of the brook\'s movement. These lines suggest that the life of man is short but the brook will live long and will exist forever. It will always keep on flowing to join the brimming river.\nA3] i) \"I slip, I slide, I gloom, I glance\" - here, the sounds of \'s\' and \'g\' are repeated.\n      ii) \"Among my skimming swallows\" here, the sound of \'s\' is repeated.\n     iii) \"For men may come and men may go\" - here, the sound of \'m\' is repeated.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76782,1811,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','CLOSED SHOES NOT ALLOWED IN EXAMINATION HALLS \nBengaluru: Shivam Institute of Innovative Technology, Bengaluru has recently issued a list of DOs and DON’Ts for candidates taking the Entrance Test to the prestigious institute this year. The candidates will not be allowed to enter the examination hall wearing closed shoes, boots, even socks. Other items which will not be allowed inside include mobile phones, microphones, earplugs, calculators and other electronic gadgets, pouches, printed or blank papers and even pens. The pens will be provided in the hall by the invigilators.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n       Who is conducting the test?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Why is closed footwear (shoes, socks) not allowed in the examination hall?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the following words from the extract:\n       i) Issued		ii) Candidates		iii) Invigilators\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Pick out the subject and predicate from the following sentence:\n      The pens will be provided in the hall by the invigilators.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Why has the institute not allowed electronic gadgets in the hall?\n','','A1] Shivam Institute of Innovative Technology, Bengaluru is conducting the test.\nA2] Closed footwear (shoes, socks) is not allowed in the examination hall to stop students from cheating during the exam.\nA3] i) Distributed					\n       ii) People taking an exam\n       iii) Those who keep a check on people taking an exam\nA4] The pens - Subject \n        will be provided in the hall by the invigilators – Predicate\nA5] The institute has not allowed electronic gadgets in the examination hall because they can create a nuisance in the examination hall and students can also misuse it copying answers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76783,1811,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','RITIKA TAKES A BREAK FROM ACTING \nMumbai, Sept.9, : Famous actress Ritika, who is believed to be the most highly paid star in Bollywood, is all set to break the hearts of her fans. In an informal chat with journalists, she revealed that she will not sign any more films, after completing the big - budget movie ‘Ant’ next month. She refused to give any reasons for her decision. However, sources close to her reveal that now she wants to make a career in politics.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       What will be Ritika\'s reaction to this news?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Guess why \'Ant\' is a big-budget movie.\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       What words are used for the following in the extract?\n        i) Film industry		ii) Something on which a lot of money is spent\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Pick out three adjectives from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n          Give the meaning of the following words / phrases from the extract:\n          i) Most highly paid		ii) Revealed		iii) Sign films\n','','A1] Ritika might be completely surprised after listening to this news as it may not be true or trustworthy.\nA2] \'Ant\' is a big-budget movie because the most highly paid star in Bollywood, Ritika, has acted in the movie. Also, it may sound like a big-budget movie because showing such creatures in a movie would require visual effects and animation which cost a lot of money. These could be the reasons why \'Ant\' is a big-budget movie.\nA3] i) Bollywood		ii) Big-budget\nA4] i) Famous			ii) Informal		iii) Big-budget\nA5] i) One who receives the highest amount of salary\n       ii) To show something that was previously hidden\n       iii) Sign a contract to act in a film\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76784,1811,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\n London : The famous physics professor Stephen Hawking has warned that mankind is facing the most dangerous time in history today. He said that the world today is facing huge challenges like climate change, overpopulation, epidemic diseases. ‘We have the technology to destroy the planet on which we live and have not yet developed the ability to escape it. Perhaps in a few hundred years from now, we will have built colonies amid the stars, but right now, we have only one planet and we need to work together to protect it.’ Hawking wrote recently in the Guardian newspaper.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Give the meaning of \'mankind\' from the extract.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Who is Stephen Hawking?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       List the words / phrases in the extract that refer to \'earth\'.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Write the plurals for the following words:\n        i) Technology		ii) Ability\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Prof. Hawking wrote about the problem in the Guardian. Why is it given as \'news\' in other papers?\n','','A1] The entire human race or the entire human population of the planet\nA2] Stephen Hawking is a famous Physics professor.\nA3] Mankind, world, planet\nA4] i) Technologies		ii) Abilities\nA5] Though Prof. Hawking wrote about the problem in the Guardian, it is given in other papers as \'news\' because this news is important and affects every part of the world. The other newspapers report what Prof. Stephen Hawking said in the Guardian, so that effective steps can be taken to fight the challenges that our planet Earth is facing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76785,1811,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS? \nStaff Reporter: Kavathe : Hundreds of people flock every hour to Ralewadi, a tiny settlement near Ambegaon. They wait in long winding queues in the hot sun to get a ‘special’ powder from Miribaba who claims that it cures all sorts of diseases. According to him, he has developed this powder from a special herb in the Himalayas. Unwilling to give the exact name and source of the herb, he only says that it was revealed to him. He offers it at Rs 25 a packet. Each packet is specially ‘blessed’ by him. Some of his followers claim that they have seen him curing thousands of people with his powder. However, Dr Karnik of Ambegaon Civil Hospital says that the number of patients in their hospital has in fact increased in the last few weeks.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Will Miribaba and his followers like the news?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        Why is there a question mark in the heading?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the following words from the extract:\n       i) Flock		ii) Followers\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Identify whether the underlined phrase is a subject complement or an object complement:\n        They wait in <u>long winding queues</u> in the hot sun.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Which part of the news is reliable? Which part may not be reliable? How do we learn that?\n','','A1] No, Miribaba and his followers will not like the news.\nA2] There is a question mark in the heading because it is not confirmed if Miribaba\'s powder is an actual cure for all ailments or not.\nA3] i) To gather		ii) People who believe in someone\'s teachings\nA4] long winding queues - Subject Complement\nA5] The part of the news that is reliable is the increase in number of patients in Ambegaon Civil Hospital in the last few weeks. The part of the news that is not reliable is that the powder of Miribaba cures all sorts of diseases. We come to learn the difference between the two because the doctor\'s statement can be verified with the hospital\'s records whereas the information given by Miribaba and his followers comes from unreliable sources and cannot be verified.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76786,1811,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','RITIKA TAKES A BREAK FROM ACTING \nMumbai, Sept.9, : Famous actress Ritika, who is believed to be the most highly paid star in Bollywood, is all set to break the hearts of her fans. In an informal chat with journalists, she revealed that she will not sign any more films, after completing the big - budget movie ‘Ant’ next month. She refused to give any reasons for her decision. However, sources close to her reveal that now she wants to make a career in politics.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        What words are used for the following in the news item:\n         i) Film industry.			ii) Something on which a lot of money is spent.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      What will be Ritika\'s reaction to this news?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      Guess the meaning of \'most highly paid\', \'revealed\', \'sign films\'.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Make adjectives.\n      i) heart		ii) decision		iii) reason\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Can you think of other reasons for Ritika\'s decision to not sign any more movies after \'Ant\'?\n','','A1] i) Bollywood.		ii) big-budget.\nA2] Ritika might be surprised to read this news as there may be no truth in it.\nA3] i) most highly paid - someone who is paid the highest amount of salary.\n      ii) revealed - to make something new known to others.\n      iii) sign films - to agree to do a film by signing a contract.\nA4] i) hearty		ii) decisive		iii) reasonable\nA5] There could be many reasons for Ritika\'s decision to not sign any more movies after \'Ant\' like disillusion with film world, desire to seek work in some foreign country, the urge to raise a family and settle down, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76787,1811,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','MOST DANGEROUS TIME IN HISTORY\n London : The famous physics professor Stephen Hawking has warned that mankind is facing the most dangerous time in history today. He said that the world today is facing huge challenges like climate change, overpopulation, epidemic diseases. ‘We have the technology to destroy the planet on which we live and have not yet developed the ability to escape it. Perhaps in a few hundred years from now, we will have built colonies amid the stars, but right now, we have only one planet and we need to work together to protect it.’ Hawking wrote recently in the Guardian newspaper.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Which words or phrases in the news refer to the earth?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      Which of the following problems do you believe is the most serious:\n      a) climate change,		b) overpopulation,		c) epidemic diseases?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Guess the meaning of \'mankind\'?\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Make verb.\n      i) dangerous		ii) work		iii) challenge\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        Prof. Hawking wrote about the problem in the Guardian. Why is it given as \'news\' in other papers?\n','','A1] The word \'planet\' and \'world\' in the news refer to the earth.\nA2] Overpopulation is the most serious problem faced by the world today leading to a host of other problems.\nA3] mankind - human race.\nA4] i) endanger		ii) work		iii) challenge\nA5] Although Prof. Hawking wrote about the problem in the Guardian, it is given as \'news\' in other papers to create awareness about the gravity of the situation and the need to take urgent, constructive steps to protect our planet from further degradation by mending our ways. As the problem in discussion concerns the whole world, putting it in other papers will help spread awareness and take some corrective steps.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76788,1811,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','QUICK CURE FOR ALL AILMENTS? \nStaff Reporter: Kavathe : Hundreds of people flock every hour to Ralewadi, a tiny settlement near Ambegaon. They wait in long winding queues in the hot sun to get a ‘special’ powder from Miribaba who claims that it cures all sorts of diseases. According to him, he has developed this powder from a special herb in the Himalayas. Unwilling to give the exact name and source of the herb, he only says that it was revealed to him. He offers it at Rs 25 a packet. Each packet is specially ‘blessed’ by him. Some of his followers claim that they have seen him curing thousands of people with his powder. However, Dr Karnik of Ambegaon Civil Hospital says that the number of patients in their hospital has in fact increased in the last few weeks.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Fill in the blanks.\n       i) He has developed this powder from a special herb in the ………………..\n       ii) Dr. ……………. of Ambegaon Civil Hospital said number of patients increased in last few weeks.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Will Miribaba and his followers like the news?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Guess the meaning of \'flock\' and \'followers.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Do as directed:\n       1) Hundreds of people flock every hour to <u>Ralewadi.</u>\n            (Frame a Wh - question to get the underlined word as an answer)\n        2) Each packet is specially blessed by him. (Separate the subject and predicate)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       What should you do when you fall ill?\n','','A1] i) Himalayas		ii) Karnik\nA2] Miribaba and his followers will definitely not like the news.\nA3] flock - gather or move in large numbers\n        followers - people supporting someone whom they believe in disciples.\nA4] 1) Where do hundreds of people flock every hour?\n        2) Each packet – Subject\n             is specially blessed by him - Predicate.\nA5] When one falls ill one mus identify the cause of the discomfort and how it can be set right with rest and correct food. Safe home remedies could be tried and if no improvement is found. one should consult a doctor immediately.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76789,1812,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Man was meant to listen more and talk less. That is why as the great British statesman Benjamin Disraeli said: “Nature has endowed man with two ears and one mouth. If man was meant to talk more and listen less, he would have two mouths and only one ear.” Imagine how we would have looked, how strange with two mouths on the two sides and one ear at the centre.\nAnd mind you, the ears are like funnels, open all the time. There is no door with which you can close them. Whereas if you have to speak even one single word, that word must pass through two walls - two fences. There is firstly the fence of these two rows of teeth. There is secondly the fence of the two lips. Before a word can be spoken, it has to pass, it has to pierce through these two walls, through these two fences. Therefore we must think at least twice before we utter a word.\nA very wise man once remarked that of the unspoken word you are a master, of the spoken word, you are a slave. Once you have spoken a word you cannot get it back, do what you will. Therefore you must be very careful about the words that you speak. Once the word has left your lips, you will not be able to get it back.\nWhat are unspoken words? They are things you want to say, but remain unsaid, as thoughts in your mind. Once you have put the thoughts into words, once the words have left your lips, you cannot change them or control them.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      Complete the following:\n      i) Our ears are like funnels because ………………\n     ii) The rows of teeth are like a fence because ……………….\n     iii) The two lips are like a fence because ……………………\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Was Disraeli trying to give a scientific reason?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n   Give the antonym of the word \'slave\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adjective form of the word \'wisdom\' from the extract.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Was Disraeli only trying to give a message in a light-hearted but effective way?\n','','A1] i) just like a liquid is poured into a vessel with a funnel, words enter our body through ears, which are always open\n       ii) words have to pass and pierce through them first, before they can be spoken\n       iii) words have to pass and pierce through them, after they have passed through the teeth, before they can be spoken\nA2] No, Disraeli was not trying to give a scientific reason. He was using the fact that we have two ears and one mouth symbolically.\nA3] Master\nA4] Wise\nA5] Yes, Disraeli was only trying to give a message in a light-hearted but effective way.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76790,1812,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','young man, “Write down on this paper all the harsh things you said to him.” The young man did as he was told, and showed the paper to the teacher. “Now tear up this sheet of paper into as many small bits as you can,” the wise teacher said. Soon, the single sheet was torn into a hundred tiny bits of paper. “Throw the bits out of this window,” the teacher told him.\nThat was easily done! It was a windy day and the tiny bits were scattered far and wide even as the young man watched. “Now, go out into the street and collect as many bits of the paper as you can,” the teacher ordered him. The young man was taken aback. “But…but, that will be difficult …” he stammered. “It will be difficult indeed, but do give it a try,” the teacher suggested. The young man went out. He returned half an hour later, exhausted. He had not been able to get hold of a single torn bit from the paper he had torn up just a while earlier! “This is what happens with the spoken word,” the teacher said to him. “Once you have spoken the words aloud, it is very difficult to take them back. Therefore, learn to think before you speak in anger.”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        Complete the following:\n        i) Harsh words are like scattered bits of paper carried away by the wind because …………….\n       ii) The young man wanted to make amends because ……………………\n      iii) The young man could not collect even a single bit of the torn paper because ………………\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Why had the young man gone to his spiritual leader?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n        Choose a phrase that means \'over a large area\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        Identify whether the underlined word is a direct object or an indirect object.\n         Socrates counselled his <u>disciples.</u>\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        What lesson did the wise teacher give the young man?\n','','A1] i) it is very difficult to take words back once they have been spoken.\n       ii) he had hurt his friend by speaking harsh and unkind words and was afraid that he had lost him as a friend\n      iii) the tiny bits of paper had scattered far and wide as it was a windy day\nA2] The young man had hurt his friend by speaking harsh and unkind words and was afraid of losing his friend. Thus, he went to his spiritual leader to find a way to make amends.\nA3] Far and wide\nA4] disciples - Direct Object\nA5] The wise teacher taught the young man the valuable lesson that once words are spoken aloud, it is very difficult to take them back. We should learn to think before we say something harsh in our anger.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76791,1812,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Socrates was one of the wisest men of the ancient world. Many were the youths whose lives he influenced for the better. Socrates counselled his disciples to keep their mouths shut – and speak only when absolutely necessary. “O wise one, how may we know when it is right to speak?” they asked him. “Open your mouths to speak only after you have asked yourself three questions, and received an affirmative answer to each of the three,” replied Socrates. What were the three questions? The first question we must ask ourselves before we speak is - is it true? If we are not sure about the veracity of what we are saying, it is better that we do not utter a word. When we utter words carelessly, we ourselves become transmitters of untruth.\nThe second question to ask is - is it pleasant? Many are the empty remarks and vain statements that people make in idleness to hurt others. It is better that these unpleasant words remain unspoken. The third question according to Socrates is - is it useful? Is our statement going to benefit the listener? Will our words bring comfort to someone? Are we likely to help someone with what we say? Only in that case should we go ahead and speak.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false.\n      i) Socrates influenced the lives of many youths.\n     ii) Socrates counselled his disciples to always remain silent.\n     iii) We should speak even if we are not sure of the truthfulness of our words.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        What did Socrates counsel his disciples about speaking?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n         Give the antonym of the word \'negative\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        Give the noun form of the following words from the extract:\n        i) Young		ii) Transmit		iii) Idle\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       When should we speak something according to Socrates?\n','','A1] i) True		ii) False	iii) False\nA2] Socrates counselled his disciples to speak only when necessary and to keep their mouths shut at other times.\nA3] Affirmative\nA4] i) Youth		ii) Transmitters		iii) Idleness\nA5] We should speak something only if we get an affirmative answer to the three question suggested by Socrates.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76792,1812,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Man was meant to listen more and talk less. That is why as the great British statesman Benjamin Disraeli said: “Nature has endowed man with two ears and one mouth. If man was meant to talk more and listen less, he would have two mouths and only one ear.” Imagine how we would have looked, how strange with two mouths on the two sides and one ear at the centre.\nAnd mind you, the ears are like funnels, open all the time. There is no door with which you can close them. Whereas if you have to speak even one single word, that word must pass through two walls - two fences. There is firstly the fence of these two rows of teeth. There is secondly the fence of the two lips. Before a word can be spoken, it has to pass, it has to pierce through these two walls, through these two fences. Therefore we must think at least twice before we utter a word.\nA very wise man once remarked that of the unspoken word you are a master, of the spoken word, you are a slave. Once you have spoken a word you cannot get it back, do what you will. Therefore you must be very careful about the words that you speak. Once the word has left your lips, you will not be able to get it back.\nWhat are unspoken words? They are things you want to say, but remain unsaid, as thoughts in your mind. Once you have put the thoughts into words, once the words have left your lips, you cannot change them or control them.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Complete the following.\n       i) Benjamin Disraeli was a great …………………..\n       ii) To speak eve one single words, it must ………………..\n       iii) We must think at least ………………..\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       How do we become slaves of words spoken by us?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Use the words \'left\' in separate sentences and show the difference in the meaning.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Do as directed:\n       1) The wise teacher gave him a fresh sheet of blank paper and pen.\n            (Rewrite ending with \'the wise teacher\')\n        2) You cannot change them or control them. (Make affirmative)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Do you remember someone else speaking to you angrily, without thinking? What did you do on that occasion? Did you also speak angrily?\n','','A1] i) Britsh Statesman\n       ii) pass through two walls -two fences.\n       iii) twice before we utter a word.\nA2] Once we have spoken a word we become slaves as we cannot take it back, no matter how hard we try. You cannot retrieve it.\nA3] My sister uses her left hand efficiently. \n       After the function, a lot of food was left behind. \n        She lift without telling anybody.\nA4] 1) He was given a fresh sheet of blank paper and pen by the wise teacher.\n       2) You can hardly change them or control them.\nA5] Yes, I do remember a few occasions. When people have spoken to me angrily without a valid reason. Though I was angry, I did not speak angrily as I did not want an angry exchange in public.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76793,1812,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Socrates was one of the wisest men of the ancient world. Many were the youths whose lives he influenced for the better. Socrates counselled his disciples to keep their mouths shut – and speak only when absolutely necessary. “O wise one, how may we know when it is right to speak?” they asked him. “Open your mouths to speak only after you have asked yourself three questions, and received an affirmative answer to each of the three,” replied Socrates. What were the three questions? The first question we must ask ourselves before we speak is - is it true? If we are not sure about the veracity of what we are saying, it is better that we do not utter a word. When we utter words carelessly, we ourselves become transmitters of untruth.\nThe second question to ask is - is it pleasant? Many are the empty remarks and vain statements that people make in idleness to hurt others. It is better that these unpleasant words remain unspoken. The third question according to Socrates is - is it useful? Is our statement going to benefit the listener? Will our words bring comfort to someone? Are we likely to help someone with what we say? Only in that case should we go ahead and speak.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        State whether the following statements are true or false.\n       i) Socrates had influenced the lives of many youths for the better.\n      ii) According to Socrates, one must never open one\'s mouth to speak.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What are three questions one needs to ask before speaking?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n        Give antonyms.\n        i) ancient		ii) affirmative\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        Do as directed:\n         1) Socrates was one of the wise men of the ancient world. (Rewrite using \'as ... as\')\n         2) \"O wise one, how may we know when it is right to speak?\" they asked him.\n                (Write in indirect speech)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Do you agree that thinking before speaking saves one from many troubles? Explain.\n','','A1] i) True 	ii) False.\nA2] Before speaking one needs to ask three questions such as - \'Is it true?\', \'Is it pleasant?\', \'Is it useful?\'.\nA3] i) recent / contemporary		ii) negative\nA4] 1) Very few men of the ancient world were as wise as Socrates.\n       2) Addressing him as a wise one, they asked him how they might know when it was right time to speak.\nA5] Yes, I definitely agree that thinking before speaking saves one from many troubles as we get time to analyse our thoughts and decide whether we should speak or not. Many a times when we are angry, if we think before speaking we will not have to regret what we have said. This saves many relationships.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76796,1813,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">Under the greenwood tree \nWho loves to lie with me, \nAnd turn his merry note \nUnto the sweet bird’s throat, \nCome hither, come hither, come hither! \nHere shall he see\n No enemy \nBut winter and rough weather.\nWho doth ambition shun, \nAnd loves to live i’the sun, \nSeeking the food he eats, \nAnd pleased with what he gets, \nCome hither, come hither, come hither:\nHere shall he see \nNo enemy \nBut winter and rough weather. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n       From the poem, find the old word used for each of the following:\n       i) Towards		ii) Here		iii) Does\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Who does the poet invite to join him?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n         List five pairs of rhyming words from the extract.\n','','A1] i) Unto		ii) Hither		iii) Doth\nA2] The poet invites a person who loves nature, shares the same sentiments as his and is ready to leave behind an ambitious life to join him under the greenwood tree.\nA3] i) Tree – me	ii) Note – throat	iii) Hither – weather\n       iv) See – enemy	v) Shun – sun	\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76797,1813,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">Under the greenwood tree \nWho loves to lie with me, \nAnd turn his merry note \nUnto the sweet bird’s throat, \nCome hither, come hither, come hither! \nHere shall he see\n No enemy \nBut winter and rough weather.\nWho doth ambition shun, \nAnd loves to live i’the sun, \nSeeking the food he eats, \nAnd pleased with what he gets, \nCome hither, come hither, come hither:\nHere shall he see \nNo enemy \nBut winter and rough weather. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      How should that person sing?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What message does the poem convey?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n      Pick out lines that mean:\n      i) Sing in such a manner\n      ii) One who wishes to join me\n     iii) Who gives up his desire for wealth, power, fame, etc.\n     iv) Life in the forest is free from ill-wishers\n     v) Happy to eat whatever he can get\n','','A1] The person should sing merrily and turn his note to that of the bird\'s sweet song.\nA2] The poem conveys the message that village life is more peaceful and beautiful than the busy city life. It also suggests that we must stay connected to our roots by staying close to nature.\nA3] i) And turn his merry note\n       ii) Who loves to lie with me,\n       iii) Who doth ambition shun,\n       iv) Here shall he see No enemy\n       v) Seeking the food he eats, And pleased with what he gets,\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76798,1813,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Under the greenwood tree \nWho loves to lie with me, \nAnd turn his merry note \nUnto the sweet bird’s throat, \nCome hither, come hither, come hither! \nHere shall he see\n No enemy \nBut winter and rough weather.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n          State whether the following statement true or false.\n         i) The only enemy in the forest would be the winter and rough weather.\n        ii) People who do not persue their ambition would be happy to live in nature.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Who does the poet invite to join him?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n        Give the rhyme scheme for the 1st four lines.\n','','A1] i) True 		ii) True.\nA2] The poet invites all those people who love nature and are ready to shun their ambitions and appreciate life in nature, to join him.\nA3] The rhyme scheme for the 1st four lines is aabb.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76799,1814,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','“It will be a fantastic birthday this year,” I said in a mocking tone. “All I will get is a pair of shoes and a hand-knitted pullover. I had asked for a table tennis set, but my mother said, ‘It is totally out of the question this year’.” My friend, Muk looked at me thoughtfully. He drew a bit closer and said, “Well, Rohan, I don’t say I believe in it, but my cousin said it works. And there is no harm trying, don’t you think?” “Trying what?” I asked. “Well, my cousin said, ‘If you really want something badly, all you have to do is to run around a banyan tree at midnight and chant seventeen times: Unke, Munke, Timpetoo’. ”\nI desperately wanted my wish to come true. Still I asked, “Why seventeen times and why at midnight?” “Don’t ask me,” replied Muk, who was my best friend. “That is how it is done, I suppose. Why not try it ? It would not do any harm.” I thought it was all nonsense. Besides, I couldn’t think of any banyan tree in our neighbourhood. There was one in old Mrs Groover’s garden. But who dared to enter her garden? Not me ! And not at midnight! Mrs Groover had been the principal of our school till last year. One look from her piercing pale green eyes was enough to silence bullies like Rinkoo Lal of Class X. I had seen it happen with my own eyes.\n“Forget it,” I said. However, the thought kept haunting me till the day before my birthday and I just could not think of anything else. “Okay,” I said to Muk, “if you come along with me, I will go to Mrs Groover’s garden tonight and do it. “Why should I come along?” asked Muk, “I have no secret wish.”\n“Come on, let us go and wish for my table tennis set.” So, at ten minutes to twelve Muk and I met at the back of old Mrs Groover’s garden. We climbed and sat on the boundary wall. We looked down into the garden, which lay dark and silent in the dim light of the moon. And there was a banyan tree, its aerial roots swaying in the gentle breeze of the night. We jumped down, reached the tree and looked around carefully. “Imagine, if she is awake!” I whispered and pointed at Mrs Groover’s house. “Nobody is awake at this ghostly hour of the night,” hissed Muk. “Now get going. Run!”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n       Fill in the blanks with the correct alternative.\n       i) Rohan desperately wished for ……………..\n        A) a pair of shoes		B) a warm pullover		\n        C) a good friend		D) a table-tennis set\n        ii) Rohan and Muk entered the garden at midnight …………….\n        A) to frighten Mrs. Groover		B) to chant around the banyan tree\n        C) to steal some fruits			D) to look for a table-tennis set\n        iii) The idea of \'Unke, Munke, Timpetoo\' was suggested by …………………\n        A) Muk			B) Rohan\'s cousin\n       C) Rohan\'s mother	D) Mrs. Groover\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What was Rohan going to get for his birthday?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the synonyms of the following words:\n        i) Scaring		ii) Border		iii) Dull\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adjective form of the following words:\n      i) Question		ii) Harm		iii) Thought		iv) Wish\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        Find the sentences in which the author and his friend claim that they do not believe in magic. What do their actions show?\n','','A1] (i - D), (ii - B), (iii - A)\nA2] Rohan was going to get a pair of shoes and a hand-knitted pullover for his birthday.\nA3] i) Haunting		ii) Boundary		iii) Dim\nA4] i) Questionable		ii) Harmful		iii) Thoughtful		iv) Wishful\nA5] The sentences in which the author and his friend claim that they do not believe in magic are:\n         \'Well, Rohan, I don\'t say I believe in it, but my cousin said it works.\'\n          \'Why seventeen times and why at midnight?\'\n          \'I thought it was all nonsense.\'\nHowever, their actions showed the opposite of what they claimed, as they did go to Mrs. Groover\'s house at midnight to try the chant around the banyan tree, so that the narrator\'s wish for a table-tennis set as his birthday present could come true.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76800,1814,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','I ran. I ran as fast as I could, chanting “Unke, Munke, Timpetoo, I wish my wish is coming true!” over and over again. I felt a bit scared. That was why I tried to get it over quickly and increased my speed. I increased the chanting too! And all the while I went on listening to the sounds of the night, a crackle here, a swish there. Suddenly a ferocious bark tore the stillness of the night! I froze. Inside Mrs Groover’s house, lights flashed on and the backdoor was pushed open.\n“Who is there?” called Mrs Groover. “Answer, or I will send the dog at you.” “Muk, Muk,” I mumbled, “Say something.” But Muk was climbing up the tree. When I saw Mrs Groover bend down to unleash the dog, I pleaded, “Please don’t send the dog at us. I will explain everything, if you let me.” “Speak out soon,” Mrs Groover shouted. I told her everything. I feared, she might not believe me. But she listened to me without interruption. She just looked at me with the hard, light green eyes of hers as if she was trying to see right into my heart. “So?” she asked finally, “and you believe in Unke, Munke, Timpetoo? Do you think it will help?” I dropped my head and said softly, “I hope it will!” “Well,” she returned, “if you believe so, go and finish your rounds.” She turned saying, “and while leaving, would you mind using the front gate?”\nI did finish the remaining six rounds and left her garden using the front gate. I swore that I would never return.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Fill in the blanks with the correct alternative.\n       i) Rohan had finished …………. rounds of the tree before Mrs. Groover discovered him.\n       A) seventeen		B) six		C) eleven		D) Twelve\n       ii) Mrs. Groover warned that she would send ………….. at them if they did not answer.\n       A) the guards		B) the dog	C) the cat		D) the police\n       iii) Rohan feared that Mrs. Groover might ……………….\n       A) not believe him		B) punish him		C) shout at him	','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       How do we know that Muk was frightened?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the antonyms of the following words:\n       i) Decreased		ii) Melted		iii) Start\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Give the adjective form of the following words:\n      i) Fear		ii) Believe		iii) Froze\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n      If you hear about something that fulfils your wishes, would you try it?\n','','A1] (i - C), (ii - B), (iii - A)\nA2] We know that Muk was frightened because when Mrs. Groover threatened to send the dog at him and Rohan, he started climbing up a tree instead of speaking the truth. Also, when Rohan asked to accompany him to Mrs. Groover\'s house at midnight to make his wish come true, Muk tried to get out of it by saying that he did not have any secret wish.\nA3] i) Increased		ii) Froze		iii) Finish\nA4] i) Fearful		ii) Believable			iii) Frozen\nA5] If I hear about something that fulfils my wishes, I will definitely try it. As long as it is about having harmless fun, there\'s no problem in trying it. Also, it\'s more about one\'s gut feeling and will power.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76801,1814,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','And then the great moment came. I opened my gifts, rejoiced over my new pair of shoes and the hand-knitted pullover! And then my eyes fell on a parcel. A big parcel! I had never received such a big parcel earlier. “To Rohan Khanna,” I read, “From Unke, Munke, Timpetoo!” I stared at Muk, and he looked as surprised as I was. My mother asked, “Who is Unke, Munke, Timpetoo? Isn’t that an unusual name?”\n“Yes,” I said, “very unusual indeed.” I bent over the parcel and began to unpack it. I removed the strings and the paper impatiently. Finally I removed the colourful gift wrapping and found a table tennis set! It was not a brand new table tennis set, but it was a fabulous one. “Wow,” I whispered, just “Wow!” Till today Muk swears he has nothing to do with it. And so do my parents. I wondered if old Mrs Groover was responsible for the surprise. I wondered for a very long time. One day I gathered up all the courage I possessed and went to meet Mrs Groover. I used the front gate and rang the bell. I had gone over my lines a hundred times, but when she looked down on me with her pale green eyes, I felt my heart sink, and my courage vanish and all I could do was stutter like an idiot. “Good afternoon, Madam, do you, I mean .... did you, have you ... sorry, but ... do you believe in ... Unke, Munke, Timpetoo?”\nMrs Groover continued to look at me with a strange look in her eyes. She replied softly but seriously, “Yes, I do believe in Unke, Munke, Timpetoo. I had wished to have somebody over for tea today. And here you are! Now isn’t it a perfect example of Unke, Munke, Timpetoo?” She smiled, and she looked so different, almost human! From that day onwards Muk and I went to see old Mrs Groover quite often. Not only because she made the best tea and had the best cookies. No! we went because we liked her. And then we had one thing in common. We all believed in Unke, Munke, Timpetoo!\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        In your opinion, who must have sent the gift?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      Did the author really rejoice over his shoes and pullover?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the antonyms of the following words:\n       i) Sent		ii) Sail		iii) Loudly\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adjective form of the following words:\n       i) Continue		ii) Courage		iii) Removed\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       The table-tennis set was not new, but was a fabulous set, what does it imply?\n','','A1] In my opinion, the gift must have been sent by Mrs. Groover.\nA2] No, the author did not at all rejoice over his shoes and pullover. He mentioned the same in a mocking tone.\nA3] i) Received		ii) Sink			iii) Softly\nA4] i) Continuous		ii) Courageous		iii) Removable\nA5] The table-tennis set was not new, but was a fabulous set. It implies that though the set was not brand new, it was well-maintained and of good quality. It further shows that it did not matter to the author if the set was new or not, he was just glad with the fact that his wish came true and he got a table-tennis set, which he wanted desperately.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76802,1814,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','I desperately wanted my wish to come true. Still I asked, “Why seventeen times and why at midnight?” “Don’t ask me,” replied Muk, who was my best friend. “That is how it is done, I suppose. Why not try it? It would not do any harm.” I thought it was all nonsense. Besides, I couldn’t think of any banyan tree in our neighbourhood. There was one in old Mrs Groover’s garden. But who dared to enter her garden? Not me ! And not at midnight! Mrs Groover had been the principal of our school till last year. One look from her piercing pale green eyes was enough to silence bullies like Rinkoo Lal of Class X. I had seen it happen with my own eyes.\n“Forget it,” I said. However, the thought kept haunting me till the day before my birthday and I just could not think of anything else. “Okay,” I said to Muk, “if you come along with me, I will go to Mrs Groover’s garden tonight and do it.” “Why should I come along?” asked Muk, “I have no secret wish.” \n“Come on, let us go and wish for my table tennis set.” So, at ten minutes to twelve Muk and I met at the back of old Mrs Groover’s garden. We climbed and sat on the boundary wall. We looked down into the garden, which lay dark and silent in the dim light of the moon. And there was a banyan tree, its aerial roots swaying in the gentle breeze of the night. We jumped down, reached the tree and looked around carefully.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      Complete the following sentences.\n      i) Mrs. Groover\'s garden had .................\n      ii) Rinkoo Lal of class X was ……………….\n      iii) \"Come on, let us go and wish for my ……………….\n      iv) There was a banyan tree, its aerial roots swaying in the ………………….\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Where was the banyan tree located?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Pick out four adverbs from the passage.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Do as directed:\n      1) I could not think of anything else. (Add a question tag)\n      2) One look from her piercing, pale, green eyes was enough to silence bullies.\n          (Pick out the adjectives)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n      Do you believe in magic? Why?\n','','A1] i) a banyan tree				ii) a bully\n       iii) table tennis set			iv) gentle breeze of the night.\nA2] The banyan tree was located in Mrs. Groover\'s garden which was in Rohan\'s neighborhood.\nA3] i) thoughtfully 		ii) badly 		iii) along 		iv) carefully\nA4] 1) I could not think of anything else, could I?\n       2) one, piercing, pale, green.\nA5] No, I do not believe in magic. I do not believe in magic because it is not proven by science.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76803,1814,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Suddenly a ferocious bark tore the stillness of the night! I froze. Inside Mrs Groover’s house, lights flashed on and the backdoor was pushed open. “Who is there?” called Mrs Groover. “Answer, or I will send the dog at you.” “Muk, Muk,” I mumbled, “Say something.” But Muk was climbing up the tree. When I saw Mrs Groover bend down to unleash the dog, I pleaded, “Please don’t send the dog at us. I will explain everything, if you let me.” “Speak out soon,” Mrs Groover shouted. I told her everything. I feared, she might not believe me. But she listened to me without interruption. She just looked at me with the hard, light green eyes of hers as if she was trying to see right into my heart.\n“So ?” she asked finally, “and you believe in Unke, Munke, Timpetoo? Do you think it will help?” I dropped my head and said softly, “I hope it will!” “Well,” she returned, “if you believe so, go and finish your rounds.” She turned saying, “and while leaving, would you mind using the front gate?” I did finish the remaining six rounds and left her garden using the front gate. I swore that I would never return. And then the great moment came. I opened my gifts, rejoiced over my new pair of shoes and the hand-knitted pullover! And then my eyes fell on a parcel. A big parcel! I had never received such a big parcel earlier. “To Rohan Khanna,” I read, “From Unke, Munke, Timpetoo!”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Say whether true or false.\n       i) A ferorious dog ran after Rohan and Muk.\n      ii) Mrs. Groover allowed Rohan to finish his rounds.\n      iii) Mrs. Groover bend down to unleash. the dog.\n      iv) Muk bodly faced the situation when the dog barked.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        Why did Mrs. Groover threaten to unleash the dog?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give one word for the following.\n      i) Stop continuous progress\n      ii) Say something indistinctly and quietly\n      iii) feel or show great joy\n      iv) set free\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      State the tense in the following sentences.\n     i) I told her everything\n     ii) I will explain if you let me.\n     iii) I had never received such a big parcel.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       \"and while leaving, would you mind using the front gate?\" why do you think Mrs. Groover said that?\n','','A1] i) False 		ii) True 		iii) True 		iv) False.\nA2] The barking of the dog awakened Mrs. Groover at night and in the darkness. She did not know who was there in the garden. Hence she asked who was there or else threatened to unleash the dog.\nA3] i) interrupt		ii) mumble	iii) rejoice		iv) unleash\nA4] i) simple past tense	ii) Simple future tense		iii) Past perfect.\nA5] Mrs. Groover being compossionate, understood the young boy\'s heart. She knew that they had jumped into her compound. At midnight, as she wanted them to be safe, she was actually suggesting that they use the front gate.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76804,1814,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','I bent over the parcel and began to unpack it. I removed the strings and the paper impatiently. Finally I removed the colourful gift wrapping and found a table tennis set! It was not a brand new table tennis set, but it was a fabulous one. “Wow,” I whispered, just “Wow!” Till today Muk swears he has nothing to do with it. And so do my parents. I wondered if old Mrs Groover was responsible for the surprise. I wondered for a very long time. One day I gathered up all the courage I possessed and went to meet Mrs Groover. I used the front gate and rang the bell. I had gone over my lines a hundred times, but when she looked down on me with her pale green eyes, I felt my heart sink, and my courage vanish and all I could do was stutter like an idiot. “Good afternoon, Madam, do you, I mean .... did you, have you ... sorry, but ... do you believe in ... Unke, Munke, Timpetoo?”\nMrs Groover continued to look at me with a strange look in her eyes. She replied softly but seriously, “Yes, I do believe in Unke, Munke, Timpetoo. I had wished to have somebody over for tea today. And here you are! Now isn’t it a perfect example of Unke, Munke, Timpetoo?” She smiled, and she looked so different, almost human! From that day onwards Muk and I went to see old Mrs Groover quite often. Not only because she made the best tea and had the best cookies. No! we went because we liked her. And then we had one thing in common. We all believed in Unke, Munke, Timpetoo!\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      Complete the followings.\n      i) I removed the colourful gift wrapping and found …………..\n     ii) I wondered if Mrs. Groover was responsible …………….\n     iii) She smiled and she looked so different ……………….\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      Why did Rohan whisper \"wow!\"?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Form Noun:\n      i) remove		ii) wonder		iii) perfect		iv) quickly\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Do as directed:\n      1) Muk and I went to see Mrs. Groover quite often. Not only because she made the best tea and cookies. We went because we liked her. (Rewrite using not only... but also)\n      2) I removed the strings impatiently. (Pick out the adverb from the given sentence)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        Do you think Mrs. Groover was responsible for the surprise? Why?\n','','A1] i) a table tennis set		ii) for the surprise		iii) almost human\nA2] Rohan whispered \'wow!\" because when he opened the colourful gift wrapping, he found a table tennis set which he was greatly longing for. Also he was too thriled or and surprised to say anything else.\nA3] i) removal		ii) wonderment		iii) perfection		iv) quickness\nA4] 1) Muk and I went to see Mrs. Groover quite often not only because she made the best tea and cookies but also because we liked her.\n       2) impatiently\nA5] Little Rohan longed to get a table tennies set as gift for his birthday and decided to run around a banyan tree in Mrs. Groover\'s garden chanting \"Unke Munke Timpetoo\" seventeen times at 12 midnight. Understanding the child\'s mind and seeing him take so much trouble, she decided to make him happy by gifting a fabulous table tennis set, through not brand new.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76805,1815,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Part I : Mr Wilson’s Story\nWhen I called upon my friend, Mr Sherlock Holmes, he was conversing with a visitor, who was an elderly man with fiery red hair. Holmes introduced me to the visitor, Mr Jabez Wilson. He was a pawn-broker. Holmes asked him to repeat kis story for me. He began by showing us an advertisement in a newspaper. It read as follows:-\nTO THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE\nThere is a vacancy for a member of the League, and the salary is four pounds a week for nominal services. Red-headed men may apply in person on Monday, at eleven o\'clock, to Duncan Ross, at the office of the League, 7 Fleet Street.\n\nThe advertisement had appeared in newspaper two months ago. Mr Wilson’s assistant, Vincent Spaulding, had shown it to him. Mr Wilson liked his assistant Spaulding. He was smart, efficient and worked for only half the normal wages ! But, the assistant also had his faults. Every now and then, he left work and went down into the cellar to develop photographs. Photography was his hobby.\nSpaulding showed the advertisement to Mr Wilson and explained to him that an American millionaire, Mr Ezekiah Hopkins, had founded the famous Red-headed League, to help all red-heads like himself. Spaulding urged Mr Wilson to apply for the job. So, the two of them went to the address given in the advertisement. Fleet Street was full of red-headed people. Mr Wilson thought that with so much competition he would not get the job. He wanted to go back, but Spaulding pushed through the crowd and took Mr Wilson to the office.\nThere was nothing in the office but a couple of chairs and a table. A red-headed man sat behind the table. He was Mr Duncan Ross, a representative of the Red-headed League. He was very pleased to see Mr Wilson, and announced immediately that he was well suited for the job. He shook hands with Mr Wilson, congratulated him, and told all other candidates to go back. Mr Duncan Ross explained that Mr Wilson would have to be in the office from ten to two. If he left the office, he would lose the job. Spaulding assured Mr Wilson that he would look after Mr Wilson’s business in his absence. The pay was fixed at four pounds a week.\n“And the work?” said Mr Wilson. “You have to copy out the Encyclopedia Britannica.” The pay was very good, and the work was light. Mr Wilson accepted the job and began his work the very next day. Mr Duncan Ross was there in the office to see that Mr Wilson did his work properly and did not leave the office. He told Mr Wilson to start with the letter ‘A’. Mr Wilson wrote diligently for four hours without leaving his place. Mr Ross would drop in from time to time to see that all was right with Mr Wilson. At two o’clock, he bade Mr Wilson good-day, and locked the door of the office.\n“This went on day after day, Mr Holmes,” said Mr Wilson, “and on Saturday I got my salary. It was the same next week, and the same the week after. After a few days, Mr Duncan Ross came in only once in a while and after a time, he did not come in at all”.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Who was well suited for the job?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Why was Fleet Street full of red-headed people?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the opposites of the following words:\n       i) Young		ii) Disappeared		iii) Dumb\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Identify the parts of speech that the underlined words belong to in the following sentence:\n       <u>The</u> <u>advertisement</u> had <u>appeared</u> <u>in,/u> <u>newspaper</u> two <u>months</u> <u>ago.</u>\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       What do you have to do when you are told to \'apply in person\'?\n','','A1] Mr. Wilson was well suited for the job.\nA2] Fleet Street was full of red-headed people because many people with red hair had seem the ad for a well-paying job at the Red-headed League and had come to apply for it.\nA3] i) Old		ii) Appeared		iii) Smart\nA4] The - Article\n        Advertisement, newspaper, months - Nouns\n        Appeared - Verb\n        In - Preposition\n        Ago – Adverb\nA5] When we are told to apply in person\' we are supposed to personally go to the assigne place and give our application. The application cannot be sent through a mail or post.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76806,1815,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Mr Wilson continued, “Eight weeks had passed like this, and I had written about Abbots, Archery etc. and hoped that I might get on to ‘B’ soon. And then suddenly the whole business came to an end.” “To an end?” Holmes asked. “Yes, Sir. This morning, I went to my work as usual at ten o’clock, but the door was shut and locked with a little note nailed on it. It said – \nThe Red-headed League is dissolved. Oct 9, 1880.\nI was shocked. I did not know what to do”. Mr Wilson went on with his story, “I made enquiries at the nearby offices, but none of them knew anything about the League. The rooms had been rented under a false name. “I went home and asked my assistant Spaulding for advice. But he could not help me in any way. I want to find out about the League, Mr Holmes, who they are and why they played this prank upon me. The whole thing is a mystery. That is why I came to you. I have heard a lot about you”. Holmes found Mr Wilson’s story very unusual. He asked, “Mr Wilson, this assistant of yours who first called your attention to the advertisement – what is he like?”\n“Small, stout, with no hair on his face. He has a white splash of acid on his forehead.” “I thought as much,” said Mr Holmes. “Is he still with you?” “Oh, yes, sir.” “That will do, Mr Wilson. I can give you my opinion on this subject in a day or two. Today is Saturday, and by Monday we may come to a conclusion.”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Fill in the blanks with suitable words from the extract:\n       i) The whole business came to an end after ................... weeks.\n      ii) Mr Wilson\'s ………………… had called his attention to the advertisement.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What topics had Mr. Wilson copied from the Encyclopaedia Britannica when the business came to an end?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n        Give a synonym for \'a trick\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        Add a suffix to the following words to make new words:\n        i) End		ii) Work		iii) Note		iv) Name\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n      What did Mr. Wilson come to know after enquiring at the nearby offices?\n','','A1] i) eight		ii) assistant\nA2] Mr. Wilson had copied the topics of Abbots, Archery, etc. from the Encyclopaedia Britannica when the business came to an end.\nA3] Prank\nA4] i) Endless		ii) Worker		iii) Noted		iv) Namely\nA5] After enquiring at the nearby offices, Mr. Wilson came to know that the room had been rented under a false name and nobody knew about the League.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76807,1815,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Part II : What happened next -\nDr Watson’s Account\n“Well, Watson, what do you think of it all?” asked Mr Holmes, after Mr Wilson had left. “I make nothing of it,” I answered frankly. Holmes sat silently for some time, and then invited me to go out with him. We went to the square where Mr Wilson had his shop. Holmes observed the area carefully. There were many shops and offices in the square, and a bank just behind Mr Wilson’s shop. Holmes spent some time outside the shop and thumped upon the pavement two or three times. Finally, he knocked on Mr Wilson’s door. A young man opened the door. Mr Holmes asked him the way to the Strand. The Assistant answered the question, and quickly closed the door.\nI said, “I am sure that you enquired your way only in order to see him.” “Not him,” Holmes said, “but the knees of his trousers.” “And what did you see?” “What I expected to see. This matter of Wilson’s is serious. A crime is being planned. But I hope that we can stop it. The offices and banks will be closed for the weekend. Now I’ve to go and make some arrangements, but I shall want your help tonight. Come to Baker Street at 10.00 and bring your revolver.”\nI arrived at Holmes’ residence in time. There were two other men with him – Mr Jones of Scotland Yard and Mr Merryweather, a banker. Holmes announced, “Tonight we are going to hunt one of the smartest criminals in London !” We left together in a carriage and reached the road we had visited in the morning. Holmes told us to follow Mr Merryweather who led us through an iron gate. We followed him down a narrow passage. After going down some stone steps, he led us down a dark, earth-smelling passage and into a huge cellar, full of big boxes. We all sat on the boxes.\n“We are in the cellar of the City branch of one of the main banks in London. Mr Merryweather is the chairman of the bank, and he will tell you why a criminal should take an interest in this cellar at present,” said Holmes. Mr Merryweather explained that the bank had borrowed a huge quantity of gold from the Bank of France and the boxes in the cellar were full of gold.\nHolmes expected the criminals to act that very night. We had to wait there in total darkness without making any noise to take the criminals by surprise. “They have but one escape route,” whispered Holmes. “That is back through Mr Wilson’s house. I hope that some men are waiting at Mr Wilson’s door, Mr Jones?” “I have an inspector and two officers waiting at his door.” “Then we have stopped all the holes. Now we must be silent and wait.”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        Tell the name of the young man who opened the door to Mr. Holmes.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What did Holmes want to see?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the antonyms of the following words:\n       i) Lead		ii) Wide		iii) Light\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adjectives used with the following words:\n       i) Passage			ii) Cellar\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Guess where Mr. Merryweather took the others.\n','','A1] The name of the young man who opened the door to Mr. Holmes was Mr. Vincent Spaulding.\nA2] Mr. Holmes wanted to see if the knees of Mr. Spaulding\'s trousers were dirty or not.\nA3] i) Follow		ii) Narrow		iii) Dark\nA4] i) Earth-smelling		ii) Huge\nA5] Mr. Merryweather took the others through an iron gate, down a narrow passage with stone steps leading to a huge cellar of the City branch of one of the main banks in London.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76808,1815,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','We waited silently for more than an hour. It was pitch dark in the cellar. Then suddenly, a point of bright light appeared in the floor of the cellar, then a line, and a gash seemed to open, and a hand appeared. A broad stone turned over upon its side, and left a square hole. A boyish face emerged. The man looked about and came out of the hole. He had a companion with him, a man with very red hair.The pair was none other than Spaulding the assistant alias the criminal Clay and the red-headed Mr Duncan Ross ! As soon as they climbed out of the hole, Sherlock Holmes sprang out and seized Clay by the collar.\nThe other dived down the hole and disappeared. Clay took out a revolver. But Holmes hit him on his wrist, and the revolver fell on the floor. “It’s no use, John Clay,” said Holmes, “we have caught you.” “So I see. But my friend has escaped.” Holmes replied, “He cannot escape. There are three men waiting for him at the other end !” Then Holmes handed over Mr Clay to the policemen. Mr Merryweather said, “Really, Mr Holmes, I do not know how to thank you. You have foiled one of the most cunning attempts at bank robbery. The bank is grateful to you.”\n“You see, Watson,” Holmes said, after we reached his home, “it was obvious from the first that the only possible object of the strange advertisement and the peculiar job was to get Mr Wilson away from his shop for some hours every day. The Red-headed League was a clever idea. In Mr Wilson’s absence, Clay and his red headed companion wanted to dig an underground tunnel from Mr Wilson’s house to the bank. Then, they would be able to enter the bank and steal the gold without breaking open the doors of the bank. Using the tunnel, they entered the cellar. They planned to steal the gold, and go back to Mr Wilson’s house, again through the tunnel, and then get away.\n“But how could you guess what their motive was?” “When I heard that the assistant worked for half the wages, I became suspicious. Using Mr Wilson’s description of his assistant, I made enquiries. I found that he was the criminal Clay. Why was he working in Mr Wilson’s shop ? And his habit of going into the cellar every now and then ! I inferred that he must be digging a tunnel to some other building. When we visited the shop, I beat upon the pavement with my stick to find out whether the cellar stretched out in front or behind. It was not in front. I saw the bank on the other side of the house and guessed what the criminals had in mind. When Clay answered the bell, the knees of his trousers were wrinkled and stained! It confirmed my suspicion that he was digging. You know the rest of the story. “You reasoned it out beautifully!” I exclaimed in admiration.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      How did the criminals enter the cellar?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Who were the two criminals?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the meaning of the word \'tunnel\' from the extract.\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adverbs from the extract that end with \'-ly\'. Find the adjective in each of the adverb.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Who tried to get away? Do you think he was able to escape? Why?\n','','A1] The criminals entered the cellar through the tunnel from Mr. Wilson\'s house to the bank.\nA2] The two criminals were Mr. Vincent Spaulding (Clay) and Mr. Duncan Ross. Mr. Vincent Spaulding or Clay was a criminal, but worked as Mr. Wilson\'s assistant. Mr. Duncan Ross was the representative of the Red-headed League.\nA3] A long passage under the ground\nA4] \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Adverb</th>\n    <th>Adjective</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Really</td>\n    <td>Real</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Silently</td>\n    <td>Silent</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Suddenly</td>\n    <td>Sudden</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Beautifully</td>\n    <td>Beautiful</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\nA5] Mr. Duncan Ross tried to get away. No, he was not able to escape as there were three men waiting for him at the other end.','',0,0,0,NULL),(76809,1815,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The advertisement had appeared in newspaper two months ago. Mr Wilson’s assistant, Vincent Spaulding, had shown it to him. Mr Wilson liked his assistant Spaulding. He was smart, efficient and worked for only half the normal wages ! But, the assistant also had his faults. Every now and then, he left work and went down into the cellar to develop photographs. Photography was his hobby.\nSpaulding showed the advertisement to Mr Wilson and explained to him that an American millionaire, Mr Ezekiah Hopkins, had founded the famous Red-headed League, to help all red-heads like himself. Spaulding urged Mr Wilson to apply for the job. So, the two of them went to the address given in the advertisement. Fleet Street was full of red-headed people. Mr Wilson thought that with so much competition he would not get the job. He wanted to go back, but Spaulding pushed through the crowd and took Mr Wilson to the office.\nThere was nothing in the office but a couple of chairs and a table. A red-headed man sat behind the table. He was Mr Duncan Ross, a representative of the Red-headed League. He was very pleased to see Mr Wilson, and announced immediately that he was well suited for the job. He shook hands with Mr Wilson, congratulated him, and told all other candidates to go back. Mr Duncan Ross explained that Mr Wilson would have to be in the office from ten to two. If he left the office, he would lose the job. Spaulding assured Mr Wilson that he would look after Mr Wilson’s business in his absence. The pay was fixed at four pounds a week. “And the work?” said Mr Wilson. “You have to copy out the Encyclopedia Britannica.”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Match the columns.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Columns \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Columns \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Mr. Hopkins</td>\n    <td>a. Representative of the Red-headed league</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Mr. Wilson</td>\n    <td>b. American Millionaire</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Vincent Spaulding</td>\n    <td>c. Pawn broker</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. Mr. Duncan Ross</td>\n    <td>d. Assistant to Wilson</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       Why did Wilson like his assistant Spaulding?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Make 4 words of minimum 4 letters from the given words.\n       i) advertisement		ii) representative\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      There was nothing in the office but a couple of chairs and a table. (Remove negative)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        Would you like to do such a job?\n','','A1] (i-b), (ii - c), (iii - d), (iv - a)\nA2] Wilson liked his assistant Spaulding as he was smart, efficient and worked for only half the normal wages.\nA3] i) adverse, verse, meant, said		ii) present, pretest, native, resent\nA4] There were only a couple of chairs and a table in the office.\nA5] I would not like to do such a meaningless mundane and uninteresting job. Jobs should be interesting, challenging and provide scope for further learning and application of different skills which is totally absent in the job taken up by Mr. Wilson.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76810,1815,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The pay was very good, and the work was light. Mr Wilson accepted the job and began his work the very next day. Mr Duncan Ross was there in the office to see that Mr Wilson did his work properly and did not leave the office. He told Mr Wilson to start with the letter ‘A’. Mr Wilson wrote diligently for four hours without leaving his place. Mr Ross would drop in from time to time to see that all was right with Mr Wilson. At two o’clock, he bade Mr Wilson good-day, and locked the door of the office.\n“This went on day after day, Mr Holmes,” said Mr Wilson, “and on Saturday I got my salary. It was the same next week, and the same the week after. After a few days, Mr Duncan Ross came in only once in a while and after a time, he did not come in at all”. Mr Wilson continued, “Eight weeks had passed like this, and I had written about Abbots, Archery etc. and hoped that I might get on to ‘B’ soon. And then suddenly the whole business came to an end.” “To an end?” Holmes asked. “Yes, Sir. This morning, I went to my work as usual at ten o’clock, but the door was shut and locked with a little note nailed on it. It said –\nThe Red-headed League is dissolved. Oct 9, 1880.\nI was shocked. I did not know what to do”. Mr Wilson went on with his story, “I made enquiries at the nearby offices, but none of them knew anything about the League. The rooms had been rented under a false name. “I went home and asked my assistant Spaulding for advice. But he could not help me in any way. I want to find out about the League, Mr Holmes, who they are and why they played this prank upon me. The whole thing is a mystery. That is why I came to you. I have heard a lot about you”. Holmes found Mr Wilson’s story very unusual. He asked, “Mr Wilson, this assistant of yours who first called your attention to the advertisement – what is he like?”\n“Small, stout, with no hair on his face. He has a white splash of acid on his forehead.” “I thought as much,” said Mr Holmes. “Is he still with you?” “Oh, yes, sir.” “That will do, Mr Wilson. I can give you my opinion on this subject in a day or two. Today is Saturday, and by Monday we may come to a conclusion.”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        Complete the following.\n        i) And then suddenly the whole …………………..\n        ii) The rooms had been rented …………………\n        iii) Holmes found Mr. Wilson\'s story ………………….\n       iv) Small, stout, with ……………………..\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        Why was Mr. Wilson shocked?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give one word for the following.\n        i) sincerely and tirelessly\n        ii) A playful trick that causes no harm\n       iii) Something which cannot be explained\n      iv) happening quickly and unexpectedly\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        Do as directed:\n        1) On Saturday I got my salary. (Identify the subject and predicate)\n        2) I had written Abbots, Archery etc. (Begin with \'Abbots...\')\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        What would you advice Mr. Wilson to do in the given situation?\n','','A1] i) business came to an end.		ii) under a false name\n       iii) very unusual				iv) no hair on his face\nA2] Mr. Wilson has been working for eight weeks and as usual when he reached the office at ten o\' clock, he found the door shut and locked. He found a little note nailed on, it which said that the Red-headed league was dissolved. This shocked him.\nA3] i) diligently	ii) prank		iii) mystery		iv) suddenly\nA4] 1) Subject -I; Predicate - got my salary on Saturday\n       2) Abbots, Archery etc. had been written by me.\nA5] I feel Mr. Wilson made the right decision to take the help of Mr. Holmes about whom he had heard a lot.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76811,1815,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Finally, he knocked on Mr Wilson’s door. A young man opened the door. Mr Holmes asked him the way to the Strand. The Assistant answered the question, and quickly closed the door. I said, “I am sure that you enquired your way only in order to see him.” “Not him,” Holmes said, “but the knees of his trousers.” “And what did you see?” “What I expected to see. This matter of Wilson’s is serious. A crime is being planned. But I hope that we can stop it. The offices and banks will be closed for the weekend. Now I’ve to go and make some arrangements, but I shall want your help tonight. Come to Baker Street at 10.00 and bring your revolver.” I arrived at Holmes’ residence in time. There were two other men with him – Mr Jones of Scotland Yard and Mr Merryweather, a banker. Holmes announced, “Tonight we are going to hunt one of the smartest criminals in London !”\nWe left together in a carriage and reached the road we had visited in the morning. Holmes told us to follow Mr Merryweather who led us through an iron gate. We followed him down a narrow passage. After going down some stone steps, he led us down a dark, earth-smelling passage and into a huge cellar, full of big boxes. We all sat on the boxes. “We are in the cellar of the City branch of one of the main banks in London. Mr Merryweather is the chairman of the bank, and he will tell you why a criminal should take an interest in this cellar at present,” said Holmes. Mr Merryweather explained that the bank had borrowed a huge quantity of gold from the Bank of France and the boxes in the cellar were full of gold.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Complete the following.\n       i) Mr. Holmes asked …………………\n      ii) The offices and banks will be …………….\n     iii) We followed him down a ………………..\n     iv) We are in the cellar of the city branch of ………………\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        What announcement did Mr. Holmes make?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Provide suitable adjective to the nouns given below.\n        i) weekend		ii) banker		iii) road		iv) passage\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Do as directed:\n       1) We went to the square were Mr. Wilson had his shop. (Change into future tense.)\n        2) We followed him down a narrow passage.\n            (Rewrite the sentence beginning.... He was followed...)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Why did the criminals take interest in the cellar?\n','','A1] i) the way to the Strand			ii) closed for the weekend\n       iii) narrow passage			iv) one of the main banks of London\nA2] Mr. Holmes announced that they were going to hunt one of the smartest criminals in London.\nA3] i) long weekend				ii) successful banker\n         iii) busy road				iv) interesting passage\nA4] 1) We will go to the square where Mr. Wilson has his shop.\n       2) He was followed by us down a narrow passage.\nA5] Mr. Merryweather\'s banks had borrowed a huge quantity of gold from the Bank of France and the boxes in the cellar were full of gold. Hence the criminals were interested in the cellar.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76812,1816,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">’Mid pleasures and palaces though we may roam, \nBe it ever so humble, there’s no place like home; \nA charm from the sky seems to hallow us there, \nWhich, seek through the world, is ne’er met with elsewhere. \nHome, home, sweet, sweet home! \nThere’s no place like home, oh, there’s no place like home! \nAn exile from home, splendour dazzles in vain; \nOh, give me my lowly thatched cottage again! \nThe birds singing gayly, that come at my call – \nGive me them - and the peace of mind, dearer than all! \nHome, home, sweet, sweet home! \nThere’s no place like home, oh, there’s no place like home! <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n        What does the poet miss?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        How does the poet glorify his home in the first stanza? \n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       How does the poet describe his home in the second stanza?\n','','A1] The poet misses his lowly thatched cottage and the cheerful singing of the birds.\nA2] In the first stanza, the poet says that no pleasure or palace can be compared to his humble home. It has an appeal of its own that makes everything holy which cannot be found elsewhere.\nA3] The poet refers to his home as a lowly thatched cottage where the birds sing happily, in the second stanza. His home is more precious to him than any amount of impressive luxury, as it gives him the peace of mind that he requires.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76813,1816,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">I gaze on the moon as I tread the drear wild, \nAnd feel that my mother now thinks of her child, \nAs she looks on that moon from our own cottage door \nThro’ the woodbine, whose fragrance shall cheer me no more. \nHome, home, sweet, sweet home! \nThere’s no place like home, oh, there’s no place like home!\nHow sweet ’tis to sit ’neath a fond father’s smile, \nAnd the caress of a mother to soothe and beguile! \nLet others delight mid new pleasures to roam, \nBut give me, oh, give me, the pleasures of home. \nHome, home, sweet, sweet home! \nThere’s no place like home, oh, there’s no place like home! \nTo thee I’ll return, overburdened with care; \nThe heart’s dearest solace will smile on me there; \nNo more from that cottage again will I roam; \nBe it ever so humble, there’s no place like home.\n Home, home, sweet, sweet, home! \nThere’s no place like home, oh, there’s no place like home! <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      What does the poet hope for?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What does the poet miss?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       Write out a few things that you would really miss about your home if you were to stay away from it for long.\n','','A1] The poet hopes that he will soon be able to return to the comfort of his home.\nA2] The poet misses sitting beneath his father\'s warm smile and the loving touch of his mother.\nA3] A few things that I would really miss if I were to stay away from home for long are:\n        i) My bed			ii) My blanket		iii) My room\n        iv) My parents		v) My siblings		vi) Home cooked food\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76814,1816,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">’Mid pleasures and palaces though we may roam, \nBe it ever so humble, there’s no place like home; \nA charm from the sky seems to hallow us there, \nWhich, seek through the world, is ne’er met with elsewhere. \nHome, home, sweet, sweet home! \nThere’s no place like home, oh, there’s no place like home! \nAn exile from home, splendour dazzles in vain; \nOh, give me my lowly thatched cottage again! \nThe birds singing gayly, that come at my call – \nGive me them - and the peace of mind, dearer than all! \nHome, home, sweet, sweet home! \nThere’s no place like home, oh, there’s no place like home! <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n        State Whether the statements are True or False:\n       i) Though we may roam around palaces, but there is no place like home.\n       ii) The birds sings dolefully and does not respond to the poet\'s call\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What does the poet mean by \"my lowly thatched cottage\"?\n<b>A3] Poetic devices question: </b>\n       Pick out the rhyming words from the extract.\n','','A1] i) True		ii) False\nA2] By \"my lowly thatched cottage\", the poet means that his home has a simple appearance as it is a humble place of residence.\nA3] roam-home, call-all, there-elsewhere, rain-again.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76817,1818,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','It was Christmas Eve and although it was still afternoon, lights had begun to appear in the shops and houses of the little Russian village, for the short winter day was nearly over. Excited children scurried indoors and now only muffled sounds of chatter and laughter escaped from closed shutters. Old Papa Panov, the village shoemaker, stepped outside his shop to take one last look around. The sounds of happiness, the bright lights and the faint but delicious smells of Christmas cooking reminded him of past Christmas times when his wife had still been alive and his own children little. Now they had gone.\nHis usually cheerful face, with the little laughter wrinkles behind the round steel spectacles, looked sad now. But he went back indoors with a firm step, put up the shutters and set a pot of coffee to heat on the charcoal stove. Then, with a sigh, he settled in his big armchair. Papa Panov did not often read, but tonight he pulled down the big old family Bible and, slowly tracing the lines with one forefinger, he read again the Christmas story. He read how Mary and Joseph, tired by their journey to Bethlehem, found no room for them at the inn, so that Mary’s little baby was born in the cowshed.\n“Oh, dear, oh, dear!” exclaimed Papa Panov, “if only they had come here ! I would have given them my bed and I could have covered the baby with my patchwork quilt to keep him warm.” He read on about the wise men who had come to see the baby Jesus, bringing him splendid gifts. \nPapa Panov’s face fell. “I have no gift that I could give him,” he thought sadly. Then his face brightened. He put down the Bible, got up and stretched his long arms to the shelf high up in his little room. He took down a small, dusty box and opened it. Inside was a perfect pair of tiny leather shoes. Papa Panov smiled with satisfaction. Yes, they were as good as he had remembered - the best shoes he had ever made. “I should give him those,” he decided, as he gently put them away and sat down again. He was feeling tired now, and the further he read the sleepier he became. The print began to dance before his eyes so that he closed them, just for a minute. In no time at all Papa Panov was fast asleep. And as he slept he dreamed. He dreamed that someone was in his room and he knew at once, as one does in dreams, who the person was. It was Jesus. “You have been wishing that you could see me, Papa Panov.” He said kindly, “Then look for me tomorrow. It will be Christmas Day and I will visit you. But look carefully, for I shall not tell you who I am.”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question:</b>\n        Did Papa Panov have a shop away from his home?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       In the little village, what shows that it is Christmas time?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Give the homophones (words which sound the same) of the following words from the extract.\n      i) Went		ii) No		iii) Dear		iv) Hear\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Give the noun form of the following:\n      i) Laugh		ii) Satisfy		iii) Wishing\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n        Have you ever noticed how some people have to work even on festivals? Who are they?\n','','A1] No, Papa Panov had his shop inside his home.\nA2] In the little village, the lights in the shops and houses, start appearing from the afternoon, sounds of happiness, faint but delicious smell of cooking show that it is Christmas time.\nA3] i) Vent		ii) Know		iii) Deer		iv) Hear\nA4] i) Laughter		ii) Satisfaction		iii) Wish\nA5] Yes, I have noticed that some people have to work even on festivals. These people are employed in public services, such as police force, army, social and sanitary work. Many policemen, army personnels, city bus drivers and conductors, cleaners, etc. work on festivals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76818,1818,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','When at last Papa Panov awoke, the bells were ringing out and a thin light was filtering through the shutters. “Bless my soul!” said Papa Panov. “It’s Christmas Day!” He stood up and stretched himself for he was rather stiff. Then his face filled with happiness as he remembered his dream. This would be a very special Christmas after all, for Jesus was coming to visit him. How would he look? Would he be a little baby, as at that first Christmas? Would he be a grown man, a carpenter - or the great King that he is, God’s Son? He must watch carefully the whole day through so that he recognised him however he came.\nPapa Panov put on a special pot of coffee for his Christmas breakfast, took down the shutters and looked out of the window. The street was deserted, no one was stirring yet. No one except the road sweeper. He looked as miserable and dirty as ever, and well he might! Whoever wanted to work on Christmas Day - and in the raw cold and bitter freezing mist of such a morning?\nPapa Panov opened the shop door, letting in a thin stream of cold air. “Come in!” he shouted across the street cheerily. “Come in and have some hot coffee to keep out the cold!” The sweeper looked up, scarcely able to believe his ears. He was only too glad to put down his broom and come into the warm room. His old clothes steamed gently in the heat of the stove and he clasped both red hands round the comforting warm mug as he drank.\nPapa Panov watched him with satisfaction, but every now and then his eyes strayed to the window. It would never do to miss his special visitor. “Expecting someone?” the sweeper asked at last. So Papa Panov told him about his dream. “Well, I hope he comes,” the sweeper said, “you’ve given me a bit of Christmas cheer I never expected to have. I’d say you deserve to have your dream come true.” And he actually smiled. When he had gone, Papa Panov put on cabbage soup for his dinner, then went to the door again, scanning the street. He saw no one. But he was mistaken. Someone was coming.\nThe girl walked so slowly and quietly, hugging the walls of shops and houses, that it was a while before he noticed her. She looked very tired and she was carrying something. As she drew nearer he could see that it was a baby, wrapped in a thin shawl. There was such sadness in her face and in the pinched little face of the baby, that Papa Panov’s heart went out to them. “Won’t you come in,” he called, stepping outside to meet them. “You both need a warm by the fire and a rest.” The young mother let him shepherd her indoors and to the comfort of the armchair. She gave a big sigh of relief.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n          Did Papa Panov believe in his dream?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n      In what forms did Papa Panov imagine Jesus to visit him?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      Give the homophones (words which sound the same) of the following words in the extract.\n      i) Through		ii) Soul			iii) Whole\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Identify the subject and predicate in the following sentence: \n       You both need a warm by the fire and a rest.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Why did Papa Panov give hot coffee to the sweeper?\n','','A1] Yes. Papa Panov believed in his dream.\nA2] Papa Panov imagined Jesus to visit him in the form of a little baby, like on the first Christmas, or as a grown up man, a carpenter or in the form of a great king.\nA3] i) Threw		ii) Sole			iii) Hole\nA4] Subject - You both\n      Predicate - need a warm by the fire and a rest\nA5] The weather outside on Christmas day was raw cold with a bitter, freezing mist in the morning air. The sweeper was the only one who was out on the street, working and looking miserable and dirty on the Christmas morning. Papa Panov felt pity for the sweeper and decided to give him a hot cup of coffee inside and a warm welcome into his house.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76819,1818,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','“I’ll warm some milk for the baby,” Papa Panov said, “I’ve had children of my own - I can feed her for you.” He took the milk from the stove and carefully fed the baby from a spoon, warming her tiny feet by the stove at the same time. “She needs shoes,” the cobbler said. But the girl replied, “I can’t afford shoes, I’ve got no husband to bring home money. I’m on my way to the next village to get work.” A sudden thought flashed through Papa Panov’s mind. He remembered the little shoes he had looked at last night. But he had been keeping those for Jesus. He looked again at the cold little feet and made up his mind.\n“Try these on her,” he said, handing the baby and the shoes to the mother. The beautiful little shoes were a perfect fit. The girl smiled happily and the baby gurgled with pleasure. “You have been so kind to us,” the girl said, when she got up with her baby to go. “May all your Christmas wishes come true!” But Papa Panov was beginning to wonder if his very special Christmas wish would come true. Perhaps he had missed his visitor? He looked anxiously up and down the street. There were plenty of people about but they were all faces that he recognised. There were neighbours going to call on their families. They nodded and smiled and wished him Happy Christmas! Or beggars - and Papa Panov hurried indoors to fetch them hot soup and a generous hunk of bread, hurrying out again in case he missed the ‘Important Stranger’.\nAll too soon the winter dusk fell. When Papa Panov next went to the door and strained his eyes, he could no longer make out the passers - by. Most were home and indoors by now anyway. He walked slowly back into his room at last, put up the shutters, and sat down wearily in his armchair. So it had been just a dream after all. Jesus had not come. Then all at once he knew that he was no longer alone in the room. This was not a dream for he was wide awake. At first he seemed to see before his eyes the long stream of people who had come to him that day. He saw again the old road sweeper, the young mother and her baby and the beggars he had fed. As they passed, each whispered, “Didn’t you see me, Papa Panov?”\n“Who are you?” he called out, bewildered. Then another voice answered him. It was the voice from his dream - the voice of Jesus. “I was hungry and you fed me,” he said. “I was naked and you clothed me. I was cold and you warmed me. I came to you today in everyone of those you helped and welcomed.” Then all was quiet and still. Only the sound of the big clock ticking could be heard. A great peace and happiness seemed to fill the room, overflowing Papa Panov’s heart until he wanted to burst out singing and laughing and dancing with joy. “So he did come after all!” was all that he said.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       How does the author show that Papa Panov was disappointed? What actions of Papa Panov suggest it?\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        How does the author describe Papa Panov\'s happiness?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n      Give the synonyms of the following:\n       i) Nervously		ii) Kind			iii) Silent\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Give the adjective form of the following:\n       i) Peace		ii) Happiness\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Why did Papa Panov give the shoes to the baby though he had been keeping them for Jesus?\n','','A1] The author showed that Papa Panov was disappointed through his actions. When dusk fell, he could not see any passers-by on the street, so he went back inside his house. put down the shutters and sat in his armchair feeling tired. Thus, through the detailed explanation of his actions, the author expresses Papa Panov\'s disappointment.\nA2] The author describes Papa Panov\'s happiness through a beautiful description of his room and his heart. He says that the room was filled with happiness, overflowing his heart until he wanted to burst out singing, laughing and dancing with joy. The author used only one dialogue to describe the satisfaction and pleasure that Papa Panov got from meeting Jesus, which was, \"So he did come after all!\"\nA3] i) Anxiously		ii) Generous		iii) Quiet\nA4] i) Peaceful		ii) Happy\nA5] Papa Panov gave the shoes to the baby although, he had been keeping them for Jesus because the baby had become pale from being outside in the harsh, cold weather and he needed it. The mother could not afford to buy shoes. Thus, out of concern, Papa Panov gave the shoes to the baby.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76820,1818,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','It was Christmas Eve and although it was still afternoon, lights had begun to appear in the shops and houses of the little Russian village, for the short winter day was nearly over. Excited children scurried indoors and now only muffled sounds of chatter and laughter escaped from closed shutters. \nOld Papa Panov, the village shoemaker, stepped outside his shop to take one last look around. The sounds of happiness, the bright lights and the faint but delicious smells of Christmas cooking reminded him of past Christmas times when his wife had still been alive and his own children little. Now they had gone.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n      Write whether true or false.\n         i) The setting of the story is in a Russian city.\n         ii) Papa Panov was sad on Christmas Eve.\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        What could be heard from the closed shutters?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Write the homophone for:\n      i) one		ii) step		iii) been		iv) his\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n       Separate the subject and predicate:\n       1) Old Papa Panov, the village shoemaker, stepped outside. \n       2) With a sigh, he settled in his big armchair.\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       What do you do on festivals?\n','','A1] i) False		ii) True\nA2] Muffled sounds of chatter and laughter could be heard from closed shutters.\nA3] i) won		ii) steppe		iii) bean		iv) hiss\nA4] 1) Old Papa Panov, the village shoemaker - Subject; stepped outside – Predicate\n       2) Subject - He; Predicate - settled in his big armchair with a sigh\nA5] I enjoy myself a lot. I celebrate every festival because people from all religions live in my locality. My friends and I visit houses, eat and enjoy. My relatives too come home to celebrate our festivals with us. I love it when my house is full of people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76821,1818,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Papa Panov put on a special pot of coffee for his Christmas breakfast, took down the shutters and looked out of the window. The street was deserted, no one was stirring yet. No one except the road sweeper. He looked as miserable and dirty as ever, and well he might! Whoever wanted to work on Christmas Day - and in the raw cold and bitter freezing mist of such a morning?\nPapa Panov opened the shop door, letting in a thin stream of cold air. “Come in!” he shouted across the street cheerily. “Come in and have some hot coffee to keep out the cold!”\nThe sweeper looked up, scarcely able to believe his ears. He was only too glad to put down his broom and come into the warm room. His old clothes steamed gently in the heat of the stove and he clasped both red hands round the comforting warm mug as he drank.\nPapa Panov watched him with satisfaction, but every now and then his eyes strayed to the window. It would never do to miss his special visitor. “Expecting someone?” the sweeper asked at last. So Papa Panov told him about his dream.\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Complete the following sentences.\n       i) The Street was deserted ………………..\n      ii) Papa Panov opened the shop door, ……………….\n      iii) The sweeper old clothes steamed ……………………\n     iv) Papa Panov watched him with satisfaction but, …………………..\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n        Why did Papa Panov give hot coffee to the sweeper?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n        Give adverb form of:\n        i) gentle		ii) special		iii) actual		iv) comfort\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n      Pick out four adjectives from the passage:\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Have you ever noticed that some people have to work even on festivals? Who are they?\n','','A1] i) I no one was stirring yet.\n       ii) letting in a thin stream of cold air.\n      iii) gently in the heat of the stove\n     iv) every now and then his eyes strayed to the window.\nA2] When Papa Panov took down his shutters and looked out, all he saw was the road sweeper looking miserable and dirty working on Christmas Day, in the raw cold and bitter freezing mist which made him give the sweeper a hot cup of coffee to make him feel better.\nA3] i) gently		ii) specially		iii) actually		iv) comfortably\nA4] might, cold, bitter, freezing\nA5] Yes, I have noticed some people having to work on festivals. They are the newspaper man, the sweeper, our maid at home, the milkman, people employed in the public service such as the postman/postwoman, bus and train drivers and conductors, policemen/women, soldiers protecting our country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76822,1818,'descriptive','2a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The girl walked so slowly and quietly, hugging the walls of shops and houses, that it was a while before he noticed her. She looked very tired and she was carrying something. As she drew nearer he could see that it was a baby, wrapped in a thin shawl. There was such sadness in her face and in the pinched little face of the baby, that Papa Panov’s heart went out to them. “Won’t you come in,” he called, stepping outside to meet them. “You both need a warm by the fire and a rest.”\nThe young mother let him shepherd her indoors and to the comfort of the armchair. She gave a big sigh of relief. “I’ll warm some milk for the baby,” Papa Panov said, “I’ve had children of my own - I can feed her for you.” He took the milk from the stove and carefully fed the baby from a spoon, warming her tiny feet by the stove at the same time. “She needs shoes,” the cobbler said. But the girl replied, “I can’t afford shoes, I’ve got no husband to bring home money. I’m on my way to the next village to get work.” A sudden thought flashed through Papa Panov’s mind. He remembered the little shoes he had looked at last night. But he had been keeping those for Jesus. He looked again at the cold little feet and made up his mind.\n“Try these on her,” he said, handing the baby and the shoes to the mother. The beautiful little shoes were a perfect fit. The girl smiled happily and the baby gurgled with pleasure. “You have been so kind to us,” the girl said, when she got up with her baby to go. “May all your Christmas wishes come true!”\n','<b>A1] Simple factual question: </b>\n       Complete the following sentences.\n         i) The young mother let him ………………..\n         ii) Papa Panov remembered the little ………………\n         iii) The girl smiled happily and …………………….\n        iv) He took the milk from the stove and ……………..\n','<b>A2] Complex factual question: </b>\n       What does the way the girl walked suggest?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary: </b>\n       Guess the meaning of the following:\n          \"You both need a warm by the fire and a rest\".\n<b>A4] Grammar: </b>\n        Do as directed:\n        i) It\'s Christmas Day. (Add a Question Tag)\n        ii) Expecting someone? (Kind of sentence)\n<b>A5] Personal response question: </b>\n       Why did Papa Panov give the shoes to the baby though he had been keeping them for Jesus?\n','','A1] i) Shepherd her indoors and to the comfort of the armchair.\n       ii) Shoes he had looked at last night.\n       iii) The baby gurgled with pleasure.\n       iv) and carefully fed the baby from a spoon, warming her tiny feet.\nA2] The way the girl walked suggests that she is very hungry, tired and very little energy left in her to move.\nA3] They both needed to get the warmth from the fire in the fireplace and also some rest.\nA4] i) It\'s Christmas Day, isn\'t it?\n       ii) Interrogative sentence\nA5] Papa Panov noticed that the young girl with a baby was weary and hungry. He invited her to his home. He noticed that the baby\'s feet were bare and the mother could not afford shoes for her child. He decided to give the little shoes he had kept for Jesus, to the little baby because he was a very kind-hearted person and could not see them in difficulty.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76823,180,'descriptive','3',5,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the poem \"It’s a Small World\" convey the message of global unity and friendship? Explain using examples and lines from the poem, and discuss why this message is important in today\'s world.','','','The poem \"It’s a Small World\" beautifully conveys the message of global unity and friendship by emphasizing the common experiences and emotions shared by people all over the world. At the start, it mentions that our world is full of both laughter and tears, hopes and fears—showing that everyone, no matter where they are from, experiences similar joys and challenges. The repetition of the line \"It’s a small world after all\" throughout the poem acts as a gentle reminder that, despite distance, we are all closely connected.\n\nIn the second stanza, the poem highlights unity by noting, \"There is just one moon / And one golden sun / And a smile means / Friendship to everyone.\" These lines use the imagery of the moon and sun—objects shared by the entire planet—to underline that natural elements belong to everyone equally, and a simple smile can create friendship across borders. Even though \"the mountains divide / And the oceans are wide,\" the poem insists that these physical barriers are not strong enough to divide human hearts and relationships.\n\nThis message is highly relevant in today’s globalized and often divided world, where misunderstandings based on nationality, race, or religion can create conflict. By focusing on shared feelings, common experiences, and universal symbols like the moon and sun, the poem encourages readers to look beyond differences and embrace a spirit of friendship and understanding. It teaches us the importance of empathy and connection, making the world not just physically small due to technology and travel, but emotionally smaller as we relate to each other as one human family. Thus, \"It’s a small world after all\" is not just a refrain, but a call to celebrate our unity and practice kindness across all boundaries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(76824,1908,'descriptive','2a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">सण बैलांचा\nआज पोळा,\nगोठ्यातील बैल\nन्हाऊ घाला.\nमखमली झुली,\nरंगीत शिंगे,\nकपाळी बांधली\nरेशमी बाशिंगे.\nढवळ्या-पवळ्या\nसजलेधजले.\nगावभर बघा\nमिरवू लागले.\nआजच्या दिनी\nनाही कामधाम,\nपुरणपोळी खाऊन\nनुसता आराम. <div style= \"column-count:2;\">','<b>कृती १ आकलन</b>\nचौकट पूर्ण करा.\nबैलाला असे सजवतात i) ………. ii) ………. iii) …………. iv) \n','<b>कृती २: (आकलन</b>\nचौकटी पूर्ण करा\n१) बैलांच्या सणाचे नाव =\n२) बैलांचे घर =\n३) बैलांची नावे =\n४) गोड पदार्थ =\n<b>कृती ३: \nपुढील शब्दांचे अर्थ लिहा :\n1) सण       २) गाव \n३) दिन      ४) काम \n<b>कृती 4: स्वमत/मुक्तोत्तरी</b>\n बैलांना शेतकऱ्याचा मित्र का म्हणतात?','','1) i) मखमली झुली     ii) रंगीत शिंगे       iii) रेशमी बाशिंगे\n2) १) पोळा                २) गोठा\n३) ढवळ्या-पवळ्या     ४) पुरणपोळी\n3) १) सण = उत्सव      २) गाव = खेडे\n३) दिन = दिवस       ४) काम = कार्य\n4) १) बैल शेत नांगरतात.\n२)  बैल शेतकऱ्यांसाठी खूप कष्ट करतात.\n3) बैल शेतकऱ्यांचा मित्र आहे.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(76825,1909,'descriptive','1a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','दिनूची आई आजारी होती. तो स्वयंपाकघरात आईसाठी चहा करत होता.\nदरवाजाची बेल वाजली. त्याने दर उघडले, तर समोर त्याची वर्गमैत्रीण फातिमा उभी होती.   त्याने तिला घरात बोलावले.\nदीनू: फातिमा ये. बस.\nफातिमा: दीनू, तू काय करतोस?\nदीनू: आईसाठी चहा करतोय. तुझ्यासाठीही करू का?\nफातिमा: (हसत-हसत) नको, मी चहा घेत नाही. कशासाठी? एक कप चहा करण्यासाठी एवढं मोठं पातेलं?\nदीनू: काय बिचडलं?\nफातिमा: अरे, छोटं पातेलं घेतलं असतं, तर पाणी लवकर उकळलं असतं.\nदीनू: हे माझ्या लक्षातच आलं नाही.\nफातिमा: अरे, छोट्या पातेल्यामध्ये चहा लवकर झाला असता आणि हो! ईश्वराला म्हणून गॅसची बचत झाली असती. समजलास?\nदीनू: समजलो. समजलो आणि लक्षातही आलं. आता मी छोटं पातेलं घेतलं आणि आईला नेऊन दिला.','<b>कृती १: (आकलन)</b>\nनावे लिहा: \n१) चहा करणारा\n२) दिनूची वर्गमैत्रीण\n३) दिनू चहा जिच्यासाठी करीत होता ती\n४) उताऱ्यातील इंधन','<b>कृती २: (आकलन)</b>\nलिहा:\n१) फातिमाच्या मते, दिनूने छोटे पातेले घेतले असते, तर …………………\n२) छोट्या पातेल्यामुळे ……………………….\n<b>कृती ३: (स्वमत/मुक्तोत्तरी)</b>\nइंधन म्हणजे काय?\nऊर्जा (शक्ती) मिळणारे जळाऊ पदार्थ म्हणजे इंधन.\nइंधनाचे प्रकार : ','','1) १) दिनू     २) फातिमा       ३) आई         ४) गॅस\n2) १) फातिमाच्या मते, दिनूने छोटे पातेले घेतले असते, तर <b>पाणी लवकर उकळले असते.</b>\n२) छोट्या पातेल्यामुळे <b>चहा लवकर होऊन इंधनाची बचत झाली असती.</b>\n3) i) लाकूड       ii) पेट्रोल         iii) डिझेल     iv) कोळसा','',0,0,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ep_questions` VALUES (76826,1911,'descriptive','1a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','मरीया शाळेतून आली. घर बंद होते. आईबाबा देवदर्शनासाठी गेले होते.\nतिने घराची किल्ली बाहेरून घेतली. दरवाजा उघडला. ती कपडे बदलून जेवण शोधू लागली. तेवढ्यात दरवाजाची बेल वाजली. तिने दरवाजा उघडला. तिच्या मैत्रिणी आल्या. त्यांनी खेळायला सुरुवात केली. त्या खेळात मस्त रमून गेल्या.\nतेवढ्यात मरीयाची आई देवदर्शन करून घरी आली. आईने दरवाजा बंद केला. खेळ थांबला. आई आता घराच्या स्वच्छतेविषयी विचार करू लागली. मरीयाला कविता समजू लागली होती. पावसाचा आवाज कमी झाला अन् मारियाने खिडकी उघडली. बाहेर रिमझिम रिमझिम पाऊस पडत होता. वाहणाऱ्या पाण्याचा खळखळाट हळूहळू कमी झाला आणि थोड्याच वेळात पाऊस थांबला.\nझाडांच्या पानांतून टपटप पाणी गळत होते. पानांआड बसलेले पक्षी बाहेर आले.\nपंखांची फडफड करून त्यांनी पानांवरील पाणी झटकले. मारियाने आकाशाकडे पाहिले. बाहेर लख्ख ऊन पडलेले होते. सगळीकडे कसे स्वच्छ, सुंदर वाटत होते.','<b>कृती १ – आकलन</b>\nचौकट पूर्ण करा.\nलख्ख उन्हामुळे सगळीकडे वाटत होते i) …………. ii) …………….\n','<b>कृती 2– आकलन</b>\nदिलेल्या सूचनानुसार कृती करा!\nआवाज लिहा:\n१) पावसाचा आवाज\n२) पाण्याचा आवाज\n३) पानांतून गळणाऱ्या थेंबांचा आवाज\n४) पंखांचा आवाज\n<b>कृती 3– स्वमत</b>\nतुम्ही तुमचा पावसात भिजण्याचा अनुभव सांगा :\n(पुढील वाक्यांचा योग्य क्रम लावा :)\n१) जोराचा पाऊस आला.\n२) मी एकदा गावी डोंगरावर फिरायला गेलो.\n३) सगळी झाडे भिजली.\n४) अचानक काळे ढग भरून आले.\n५) मी एका झाडाखाली मनमुराद भिजलो.','','1) १) स्वच्छ         २) सुंदर\n2) १) रिमझिम           २) खळखळाट\n३) टप् टप्                   ४) फडफड\n3) 1) मी एकदा गावी डोंगरावर फिरायला गेलो.\n2) अचानक काळे ढग भरून आले.\n3) जोराचा पाऊस आला.\n4) सगळी झाडे भिजली.\n5) मी एका झाडाखाली मनमुराद भिजलो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(76828,1913,'descriptive','2a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">दरीतीरून, वनावनातून,\nझुळझुळ मी वाहत घेते,\nमी मंजुळ गाणे गाते,\nमी पुढेच धावत जाते.\nवसली गावे तीरावरती,\nत्यांना भेटीन जाते पुढती,\nलतावक्ष किती काठावरती,\nभूमितुनी जे मला भेटती.\nफुलवेळी मज सुमने देती,\nकुठे लल्हारीं खेळत बसती,\nकुठे आम्रवृक्ष मायझावरती,\nशीतल आपुली छाया धरती.\nपाणी पिऊनी पक्षी जाती,\nचर भरणी कोणी जल नेती,\nगुरे-वासरे जवळी येती,\nमुले खेळती लाटांवरती.\nमी कोणाची - मी सर्वांची,\nबांधुनिही मज नेणाऱ्यांची!\nजिथे जाईन - तिथे फुलवीन\nबाग मनोहर आनंदाची! <div style= \"column-count:2;\">\n','<b>कृती १ आकलन</b>\nएका शब्दात लिहा : \n१) दरी-वनांतून वाहत येणारी\n२) शीतल छाया धरणारा\n३) खेळत बसणारी\n४) नदीच्या जवळ येणारी\n','','<b>कृती 2 आकलन</b>\nचौकट पूर्ण करा.\nनदीच्या काठावर  असलेल्या दोन गोष्टी i) ……………. ii) …………….  \n<b>कृती ३ व्याकरण </b>\nपुढील शब्दांचे अर्थ लिहा : \n१) नदी                     २) वन\n३) मंजूळ                   ४) तीर\n<b>कृती ४ – काव्यसौंदर्य</b>\nपुढील ओळींचा सरळ अर्थ लिहा :\n\'पाणी पिउनी पक्षी जाती\nघट भरुनी कोणी जल नेती\'','1) १) नदी       २) आम्रतरू\n३) लव्हाळी          ४) गुरे-वासरे\n2) i) गावे      ii) लतावृक्ष\n3) १) नदी – सरिता                 २) वन - रान\n३) मंजूळ – मधुर                        ४) तीर - काठ\n4) नदी म्हणते - पाखरे माझे पाणी पितात व आनंदाने जातात. कुणी माझ्या किनारी येऊन मडके भरून पाणी घेऊन जातात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(76831,1914,'descriptive','1a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','पर्वतउताराकडून मी सपाट मैदानि भागात येते. आता माझा वेग कमी झालेला असतो. माझ्या दोन्ही किनार्‍यांवरील परिसर मी हिरवागार करते. माझ्यामुळे शेतकरी शेतात भरपूर धान्य पिकवतात; पण मला एक गोष्टांची खंत आहे. मी सगळ्यांच्या उपयोगी पडते, तरीही माणसे माझ्या प्रवाहात सांडपाणी सोडतात, कचरा टाकतात, मला प्रदूषित करतात.\nमाणसांच्या वस्त्या, छोटी गावे, शहरे यांच्या जवळून वाहत जाताना मी जास्त प्रदूषित होते. तरीही मी पुढे पुढे जातच राहते. मी कुठेही थांबत नाही. ‘थांबलेली ती सापळा’ असेच मी माणसांना सांगत असते. शेवटी मी सागराला जाऊन मिळते आणि नदी म्हणून माझा प्रवास थांबतो.','रिकाम्या जागा भरा.\nनदीमुळे किनाऱ्यावरील परिसरात या गोष्टी घडतात i) ……………. ii)………………','<b>कृती २: (आकलन)</b>\nचौकटी पूर्ण करा: (Fill in the boxes :)\n१) नदीला प्रदूषित करणाऱ्या गोष्टी ……………..व………………….\n२) धान्य पिकवणारा \n३) नदीला प्रदूषित करणारी\n<b>कृती ३: (स्वमत/मुक्तोत्तरी)</b>\n\'थांबला तो संपला\' म्हणजे काय?','','1) i) परिसर हिरवागार करते. ii) शेतात भरपूर धान्य पिकते. \n2) १) सांडपाणी     व      कचरा\n२) शेतकरी      ३) माणसे\n3) १) माणसाने सतत काम करायला हवे.\n२) काम करणे थांबले की आळशीपणा वाढतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(76834,1915,'descriptive','1a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','पोपट, कोकीळ, चिमण्या, कावळे, साळुंख्या असे कितीतरी पक्षी झाडांवर बसले होते. त्यांचा किलबिलाट चालला होता. पक्षी आनंदाने उडत होते. आम्हीही आनंदाने बागेत होतो. आम्ही लपाछपी, शिवणापाणी, उन्मासळी असे खेळ खेळलो. वाचणे पडल्यावर कॅन्या गोळा करणे. समळ्या कॅन्यांचा चटपटीत स्वाद. आम्ही सहभोजन केले.त्यांनंतर बाईनी आम्हाला चित्र काढायला सांगितले. आंधळ्या झाडावर पोपट आम्ही खालो आहे, असे चित्र मी काढले. कोणी कॅन्यांनी लढलेले चित्र काढले. कोणी एक पक्षी आकाशाच्या पार उडत आहे, असे चित्र काढले. कोणी आकाशात उडणारे विमान काढले. खेळण्याचे मुले होते. शेवटी सर्वांनी चित्रे बघितली. बाईं आम्हाला कॅनी व आंधळा यांचा एक सांगितले. कॅन्यापासून, आंधळ्यापासून बनवले जाणारे वेगवेगळे पदार्थांची नावे सांगितली. सायंकाळी आम्ही घरी परतलो. दिवसभर खेळून, बागेत आणि लांबच लांब चालतदिवशी आम्ही थकून गेलो. घरी आम्हाला दूध प्यायला, आनंदाने व आरामात झोप लागली.','<b>कृती 1: (आकलन)</b>\nरिकाम्या जागा भरा.\nझाडावर बसलेले चार पक्षी i) …………… ii) ………… iii) ………iv) ','<b>कृती २: (आकलन)</b>\nएका शब्दात उत्तर लिहा.\n१) मुलांनी खेळलेले खेळ\n२) पक्ष्यांचे ओरडणे\n३) चट्टापट्टा केलेली वस्तू\n४) याच्यामुळे कैऱ्या पडल्या होत्या\n<b>कृती ३: स्वमत</b>\nकाही पक्षांची नावे लिहा.','','1) i) पोपट        ii) कोकीळ    \n iii) चिमण्या      iv) कावळे\n2) १) लपाछपी, शिवणापाणी व ऊनसावली\n२) किलबिलाट    ३) कैऱ्या    ४) वारा\n3) i) मोर, ii) कोंबडा, iii) गरुड, iv) घुबड','',0,0,0,NULL),(76835,1917,'descriptive','1a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','शाळा सुटली. मी घरी निघालो. वाटेत आई दिसली. तिच्या हातात दोन मोठ्या पिशव्या होत्या. त्या खूप जड असाव्यात, असे तिच्याकडे पाहून जाणवले. मी धावतच आईला गाठले. \"अगं आई, काय देऊन चाललीस एवढं?\" मी आईला विचारले. तिच्या हातातील एक पिशवी घेऊ लागलो. \"अरे हक्कू. सामान सांडेल त्यातलं.\" ती म्हणाली.\"अगं आई, किती जड आहे पिशवी! काय आहे एवढं यात?\" मी विचारले.\"हे आपलं महिनाभराचं किराणा सामान आहे.\" आई म्हणाली. एक पिशवी हातात धरल्यावर मला समजले, त्या ओझ्याने आईचे हात लालेलाल झाले होते. \"एखाद्या सामानाला किती रुपये लागले गं आई?\" मी विचारले.\"किती लागले असावेत?\" आईने उलट प्रश्न केला.मी सांगितलेली रक्कम ऐकून आई हसू लागली अन् म्हणाली, \"वा रे, तुझं व्यवहारज्ञान! वस्तूचे भाव गगनाला भिडलेत. आहेस कुठे तू? जरा बाजारात फिरत जा, दुकानात जात जा.\"','<b>कृती १: (आकलन)</b>\nमुलाने आईला विचारलेले दोन प्रश्न i) ………………… ii) ………………………\n','<b>कृती 2: (आकलन)</b>\n१) आईच्या हातात असलेल्या =\n२) मुलाला आई येथे दिसली =\n३) पिशवीत होते = \n<b> कृती ३: (स्वमत/मुक्तोत्तरी) </b>\n तुम्ही आईला कोणकोणत्या कामात मदत करता ? फक्त तीन कामे लिहा.','','1) १) \"अगं आई, काय घेऊन चाललीस एवढं?\"\n२) \"काय आहे एवढं यात?\"\n2) i) दोन पिशव्या    ii) वाटेत      iii) महिनाभराचे किराणा सामान\n3) १) धान्य निवडून देणे.          2) भांडी घासणे.             3) कचरा काढणे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(76837,1921,'descriptive','1a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','शिवानी. पाचवीत शिकते. माझे कुटुंब छोटेसच आहे. आई, बाबा, भाऊ शिवराज आणि मी. आई घरीच सगळी कामे स्वतःच करते. आहेही तिला मदत करते. एकदा आईचे कपडे धुणं चालत होते. कपडे वाळल्यानंतर भाऊने त्याच्या घड्या करून टेबलावर ठेवले. सायंकाळी पाच वाजता बाबा बाहेरून आले. त्यांनी आईजवळ पैसे दिले. ते तिला टेवायला सांगितले. आई कामाला घाईत होती. तिने पैसे टेबलवर घड्या घातलेल्या कपड्यांवर ठेवले आणि ती कामात गुंतून गेली. दोन दिवसांनंतर बाबांनी आईकडे पैसे मागितले. नेहमीच्या सवयीप्रमाणे आई कपाटात पैसे शोधू लागली; पण पैसे काही सापडेनात. आई कावरीबावरीझाली. पैसे गेले कुठे? आई विचारात पडली. घरात सगळीकडे पैशांची शोधाशोध सुरू झाली; पण पैसे काही सापडले नाहीत.','<b>कृती १: (आकलन)</b>\nअर्थ लिहा\n१) कामात गढून जाणे -           २) कावरीबावरी होणे – \n३) घाम फुटणे –                     ४) कष्टाने कमावून खाणे –                   \n५) शोधाशोध करणे - \n','<b>कृती 2: (आकलन)</b>\nचौकटी पूर्ण करा:\n१) शिवानीच्या भावाचे नाव \n२) कपडे वाळत घालणारी\n३) कपड्यांच्या घड्या घालणारा\n४) आईजवळ पैसे देणारे\n५) आईने पैसे येथे ठेवले\n<b>कृती ३: (स्वमत/मुक्तोत्तरी)</b>\nतुमचा स्वतःचा प्रामाणिकपणाचा एक प्रसंग सांगा :\n(पुढील वाक्यांचा योग्य क्रम लावा.)\n१) बाईंनी कौतुक करून मला शाबासकी दिली.\n२) पण माझे मन मला टोचू लागले.\n३) मी वर्गात निबंध वाचला.\n४) मी बाईंना \'हा निबंध आईने लिहिला\' हे खरे सांगितले.\n५) सर्वांनी टाळ्या वाजवल्या.','','1) १) कामात गढून जाणे - कामात गुंग होणे.\n२) कावरीबावरी होणे – गोंधळून जाणे\n३) घाम फुटणे – घाबरणे.\n४) कष्टाने कमावून खाणे – मेहनत करून पोट भरणे.\n५) शोधाशोध करणे - खूप कसून हुडकणे.\n2) १) शिवराज            २) आई\n३) भाऊ                    ४) बाबा              ५) कपड्यांत\n3) योग्य क्रम\n ३) मी वर्गात निबंध वाचला.\n५) सर्वांनी टाळ्या वाजवल्या.\n२) पण माझे मन मला टोचू लागले.\n४) मी बाईंना \'हा निबंध आईने लिहिला\' हे खरे सांगितले.\n१) बाईंनी कौतुक करून मला शाबासकी दिली.','',0,0,0,NULL),(76838,1923,'descriptive','1a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','शेतकरी घरी आला. त्याने आपली दोन बैल गोठ्यात बांधली. त्यांना खायला टाकले. पाणी प्यायला दिले.\nहात धुऊन तो घरात आला. बायकोने भाकरी-भाजी तयार ठेवली होती. दोघे जेवले.शेतात काम करणे फार थकवणारे होते. अंधार पडू लागला होता. शेतकऱ्याला झोप येऊ लागली. तो लवकर उठला. बाहेर आला. गोठ्यात पाहिले तर एक बैल गायब होता! ‘‘अगं गोठ्यात एकच बैल आहे. आपला दुसरा बैल कुठे गेला?’’ मलण्णाने धावून मालतीलाकशी करायची? मलण्णा डोक्याला हात लावून बसला. तीन-चार दिवस गेले. मलण्णा मालतीला म्हणाला, \"आपण तालुक्याला जाऊ, तिथे गुरांचा बाजार भरतो. तिथून एक बैल खरेदी करू.\" मालती म्हणाली, \"खरं आहे तुमचं. एका बैलावर नांगरणी कशी करायची?\"','<b>कृती १: (आकलन)</b>\nउत्तरे लिहा.\ni) मलण्णाने बैलांना दिलेले-\nii) गोठ्यातील स्थिती\n','<b>कृती 2: (आकलन)</b>\nचौकटी पूर्ण करा : \n१) मलण्णा कुठून घरी आला\n२) मलण्णाने बैलांना बांधले\n३) मलण्णाच्या पत्नीचे नाव\n४) मालतीने जेवायला केलेले पदार्थ\n<b>कृती ३: (स्वमत/मुक्तोत्तरी)</b>\nगुरांच्या बाजारात गुरांची विक्री होते. ग्रामीण भागात आठवड्यातून एकदा बाजार भरतो. या बाजारात कोणत्या वस्तू विकल्या जातात. \n','','1) i) १) चारा      २) पाणी\nii)  १) गोठ्यात एकच बैल होता.       २) दुसरा बैल गायब होता.\n2) १) शेतावरून     २) गोठ्यात      ३) मालती      ४) भाजीभाकरी\n3) १) तांदूळ                          2) मूळे\n3) भाजी पाला               4) मसाल्याचे पदार्थ','',0,0,0,NULL),(76839,1924,'descriptive','2a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style= \"column-count:2;\">पतंग उडू चला,\nगड्यांनो, पतंग उडू चला.\nरंग धमाक मावळतीचा,\nलाल पिवळसर किती मजेचा,\nझुळझुळ वारा नदीकाठचा,\nबाजूचं ढांगमळा.\nकरू चला सुबात बरोबर,\nसोळा सोळा रीळ भराभर,\nपतंग चढवा हे वायावर,\nधमास भेटायला.\nममद वाचवा पाय रेघुनी,\nदेक झटका दोरा ओढुनी,\nपतंग जातील वर वर चढुनी,\nपंख नकोय त्याजला.\nजशी पाखरं आभाळात,\nपंख पसरीत तरंगतात,\nतसिलात तैसे पतंग रंगीत,\nखेळ किती चांगला! <div style= \"column-count:2;\">','<b>कृती १ आकलन</b>\nचौकटी पूर्ण करा: \n१) भराभर सोडा\n२) पतंग चढवायचा\n३) वाळू कशी?\n४) पतंगांना नको\n५) रंगीत पतंग कसे दिसतात?\n','<b>कृती 2 आकलन</b>\n१) सूर्य मावळण्याच्या वेळेस ढगांवर कोणता रंग दिसतो?\n२) पतंग कोणासारखे तरंगतात ?\n<b>कृती ३ व्याकरण </b>\nपुढील शब्दांचे अर्थ लिहा :\n१) ढग          २) मजा\n३) डोंगर      ४) पाय\n<b>कृती ४ – काव्यसौंदर्य</b>\nकंसातील योग्य शब्दांवर√ अशी खूण करा :\n१) पतंगाला बांधलेल्या दोऱ्याला काय म्हणतात?\n२) \'पतंग उडवणे\' याला काय म्हणतात?','','1)१) रीळ २) वाऱ्यावर    ३) मऊ मऊ    ४) पंख   ५) आभाळातली पाखरे\n2) i) सूर्य मावळण्याच्या वेळेस ढगांवर लाल पिवळसर रंग दिसतो.\nii) पतंग पाखरांसारखे तरंगतात.\n3) १) ढंग= मेघ     २) मजा[= मौज\n ३) डोंगर पर्वत    ४) पाय=पद/चरण\n4) 1)पतंग बांधलेल्या दोऱ्याला (√मांजा/सांजा) म्हणतात.\n२) \'पतंग उडवणे\' याला पतंग (चढवणे/√बवणे) असे म्हणतात','',0,0,0,NULL),(76848,1925,'descriptive','1a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','लहानपण्या विठ्ठलला खेळाची अत्यंत आवड होती. हुतुतू, खो-खो, सुपारंब्या यांसारखे खेळ खेळण्यात लहानगा विठ्ठल सदैव पुढे असे. लहानपणाच्या या खेळांमध्ये विठ्ठलला धीरणपणा व सहनशीलता दिसून येई. त्याचे वडील धार्मिक वृत्तीचे होते. त्यांच्या धार्मिक वृत्तीचा विठ्ठलवरसुद्धा परिणाम झाला होता. सर्व जाणिवांनी लोकं त्याच्या घरी येत असत. माणसामाणसांमध्ये भेद करू नये योगा नाही, याचा संस्कार घरातील वातावरणामुळे लहानग्या विठ्ठलच्या मनावर झाला.\nछोटा विठ्ठल अवघा चार वर्षांचा होता, त्या वेळी दक्षिण भारतात महाज्ञात कुंकूळ पडला होता; पण विठ्ठलच्या महकाळा मनाच्या चिंतनाने लोकं आलेले होते. आजोबा सबस्करता हे दानग्राह होते. \n','<b>कृती १: (आकलन)</b>\nएका वाक्यात उत्तरे लिहा : \n१) विठ्ठलचा जन्म कोणत्या गावात झाला होता?\n२) विठ्ठल कोणकोणते खेळ खेळत असे?\n३) विठ्ठलच्या आजोबांनी दुष्काळाच्या वेळी गोरगरिबांना कोणती मदत केली?\n४) महर्षी विठ्ठल रामजी शिंदे यांनी आयुष्यभर कोणते कार्य केले?\n','<b>कृती 2: (आकलन)</b>\nचौकटी पूर्ण करा : \n१) वडील या वृत्तीचे होते\n२) हे लोक घरी येत असत\n३) यांमध्ये भेद करणे योग्य नाही\n४) घरच्या वातावरणामुळे विठ्ठलच्या मनावर उमटलेले\n<b>कृती ३ : (स्वमत/मुक्तोत्तरी)</b>\nकाही समाजसेवक किंवा सामाजिक संस्था गरजूंना मदत करतात. तुम्हांला माहीत असलेले पुढीस बाबतीतील एकेक काम लिहा :\n१) गरजूंना आर्थिक मदत\n2) ज्येष्ठ नागरिकांना मदत\n३) गरजू विद्यार्थ्यांना मदत','','1) i) विठ्ठलचा जन्म जमखंडी या गावात झाला होता.\nii) विठ्ठल हुतूतू, खो-खो, सूरपारंब्या हे खेळ खेळत असे.\niii) दुष्काळाच्या वेळी विठ्ठलचे आजोबा दररोज सकाळच्या वेळी कट्ट्यावर बसून गोरगरिबांना मक्याचे दाणे वाटत असत.\niv) महर्षी विठ्ठल रामजी शिंदे यांनी आयुष्यभर समाजसुधारणेचे कार्य केले.\n2) १) धार्मिक        २) सर्व जातिधर्मांचे       ३) माणसामाणसांत      ४)   संस्कार \n3) १) गरजूंना आर्थिक मदत औषधोपचारांसाठी आर्थिक मदत\n२) ज्येष्ठ नागरिकांना मदत चष्मा वाटप\n३) गरजू विद्यार्थ्यांना मदत - दप्तरांचे वाटप','',0,0,0,NULL),(76851,1926,'descriptive','1a',6,2,'Out of Text Book','फुलपाखरू : बागेतली फुलझाडं किती सुंदर दिसताहेत! फुलांचा सुवास सगळीकडे दरवळतो आहे.\nमधू : मंद वारा सुटला आहे. कसं छान वाटतंय; पण मला थांबायला वेळ नाही.\nफुलपाखरू : थांब थोडा वेळ. आपण या गुलाबाच्या फुलांवर बसून जरा गप्पा मारूया.\nमधू : नको रे बाबा! इकडे तिकडे तुझ्यासारखं मला वेळ घालवायला मुळीच सवड नाही. मी सकाळपासून निघालो खरी, पण मध काही गोळा करून झालेला नाही.\nफुलपाखरू : मध गोळा करण्याचं काम कुणी दिलं तुला?\nमधू : आमच्या राणीमाशीन दिल.','<b>कृती 1 : (आकलन)</b>\nचौकटी पूर्ण करा \n१) मधमाशीला काम आहे ते\n२) मध गोळा करण्याचे काम देणारी\n३) फुलपाखराला गप्पा येथे मारायच्या आहेत\n४) कधीपासून मधमाशी बाहेर पडली\n','<b>कृती 2 : (आकलन)</b>\nकोण ते लिहा :\n१) बागेत स्वच्छंदपणे फिरणारे\n२) कामात मग्न असणारी\n३) थुईथुई नाचणारे\n<b>कृती ३: (स्वमत/मुक्तोत्तरी)</b>\nतुम्हांला काय काय पाहिल्यावर आनंद होतो?\n','','1) १) मध गोळा करणे      २) राणीमाशी\n३) गुलाबाच्या फांदीवर    ४) सकाळपासून\n2) 1) फुलपाखरू   2) मधमाशी   3) कारंजे\n3)  सुंदर निसर्ग','',0,0,0,NULL),(76912,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','उजव्या निलयातून पुढे रक्त नेणाऱ्या रक्तवाहिनीस …………………………..म्हणतात.\nअ) महाधमनी        ब) अधोमहाशीर     क) फुफ्फुसधमनी      ड) फुफ्फुस शीर\n','','','क) फुफ्फुसधमनी         ','',0,0,0,NULL),(76913,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रत्येक अवयवामध्ये …………………..जाळे पसरलेले असते.\nअ) धमन्यांचे                  ब) शिरांचे	क) केशवाहिन्यांचे        ड) रक्तवाहिन्यांचे\n','','','क) केशवाहिन्यांचे        ','',0,0,0,NULL),(76914,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्तद्रवातील ………………………… चेता आणि स्नायू कार्याचे नियंत्रण ठेवतात\nअ) असेंद्रिय आयने         ब) अल्ब्युमिन	क) प्रथिने                       ड) प्रोथ्रोम्बीन\n','','','अ) असेंद्रिय आयने         ','',0,0,0,NULL),(76915,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','छातीच्या पिंजऱ्याच्या तळाशी……………………….. असतो/असते.\nअ) उदरपोकळी             ब) उरोपोकळी		क) श्वासपटल                 ड) बरगडी\n','','','क) श्वासपटल','',0,0,0,NULL),(76916,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रत्येक फुफ्फुसावर................ आवरण असते.\nअ) एकपदरी      ब) दुपदरी	क) त्रिपदरी            ड) पातळ\n','','','ब) दुपदरी','',0,0,0,NULL),(76917,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','दिलेले समीकरण कोणती प्रक्रिया सारांश रूपाने मांडते ? \nC₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + ऊर्जा\nA) अंतःश्वसन        B) पेशीश्वसन	C) श्वास घेणे        D) श्वास सोडणे','','','B) पेशीश्वसन','',0,0,0,NULL),(76918,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','हृदयाचे ………………..  कप्पे असतात.\nA) दोन               B) तीन		C) चार              D) पाच\n','','','C) चार','',0,0,0,NULL),(76919,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्त गोठण्याच्या क्रियेत रक्तातील ……………………. हे घटक मदत करतात.\nA) फायब्रिनोजेन         B) रक्तपट्टिका	C) प्रोथ्रोम्बीन               D) वरीलपैकी सर्व.\n','','','D) वरीलपैकी सर्व.','',0,0,0,NULL),(76920,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','………………………. हा दर्शक प्रयोगशाळेत वापरतात.\nअ) मिथिल रेड           ब) मुळा	क) हळद                     ड) गुलाब\n','','','अ) मिथिल रेड           ','',0,0,0,NULL),(76921,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','लिटमस पेपर ………………………. या वनस्पतीपासून बनवतात.\nअ) गुलाब          ब) जास्वंद	क) मुळा          ड) दगडफूल\n','','','ड) दगडफूल','',0,0,0,NULL),(76922,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मिथिल ऑरेंज आम्लामध्ये ……………………… होतो.\nअ) तांबडा         ब) गुलाबी	क) निळा          ड) रंगहीन\n','','','ब) गुलाबी','',0,0,0,NULL),(76923,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्लारी चवीला ……………………………. असते.\nअ) गोड             ब) आंबट	क) कडवट       ड) खारट\n','','','क) कडवट       ','',0,0,0,NULL),(76924,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पाणी हे ……………………………. आहे.\nअ) उदासीन          ब) आम्लधर्मी		क) उभयधर्मी        ड) आम्लारीधर्मी\n','','','अ) उदासीन          ','',0,0,0,NULL),(76925,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालीलपैकी कोणते खनिज आम्ल नाही ?\nA) हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्ल        B) नायट्रिक आम्ल	C) सल्फ्युरिक आम्ल            D) ऑक्सॅलिक आम्ल\n','','','D) ऑक्सॅलिक आम्ल','',0,0,0,NULL),(76926,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','निळा लिटमस कागद शुद्ध पाण्यात बुडवला असता काय होते ?\nA) निळा लिटमस निळाच राहतो.  B) लिटमस कागद तांबडा होतो. \n C) लिटमस कागद हिरवा होतो.   D) लिटमस कागद पिवळा होतो.','','','A) निळा लिटमस निळाच राहतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(76927,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालीलपैकी कोणता प्रयोगशाळेतील दर्शक नाही ?\nA) फिनॉल्फ्लिन         B) मिथिल रेड	C) लिटमस कागद       D) हळददर्शक कागद\n','','','D) हळददर्शक कागद','',0,0,0,NULL),(76928,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','कपड्यांवरील पिवळा हळदीचा डाग कपडे सावणान धुतल्यानंतर ………………….. होतो.\nA) निळा        B) हिरवा	C) लाल         D) रंगहीन\n','','','C) लाल         ','',0,0,0,NULL),(76929,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालीलपैकी कोणता पदार्थ उदासीन आहे?\nA) व्हिनेगार          B) कॉस