ne side of the road, \nFor children are going to school.\nThrough the green-topped branches, \nBreezes sing their songs of blessings.\nFragrant Flowers awaken the sleepy roads \nwith their melodies. \nAt the corner of the lane, the Peepal in his lush deep foliage \nwaves his hands','1) Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The trees have ------------------------------\n     ii. The Peepal tree is at ------------------------------','2) Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Fragrant flowers</td>\n    <td>a. waves his hands</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. The peepal</td>\n    <td>b. waits and smiles</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>c. awakens the sleepy roads</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n3) From the extract find the words that rhyme with\n     i. power = ---------                ii. cart = --------------------','','1) i) green topped branches \n     ii) at the corner of the lane\n2) (1 – c), (2 – a)\n3) i) hour            ii) heart','',0,0,0,NULL),(82713,184,'descriptive','2b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','The Sun, after his morning dip in the river \ndressed up in his golden muslin turban \nwaits and smiles on one side of the road, \nFor children are going to school.\nThrough the green-topped branches, \nBreezes sing their songs of blessings.\nFragrant Flowers awaken the sleepy roads \nwith their melodies. \nAt the corner of the lane, the Peepal in his lush deep foliage \nwaves his hands','1) Complete the following sentences.\n     i. The trees have ------------------------------\n\n     ii. The Peepal tree is at ------------------------------','2) Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 60%;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Fragrant flowers</td>\n    <td>a. waves his hands</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. The peepal</td>\n    <td>b. waits and smiles</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>c. awakens the sleepy roads</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n3) From the extract find the words that rhyme with\n\n     i. power = ---------                ii. cart = --------------------','','1) i) green topped branches \n     ii) at the corner of the lane\n2) (1 – c), (2 – a)\n3) i) hour            ii) heart','',0,0,0,NULL),(82714,568,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','We were used to teachers shouting at us for poor marks, shabby work, incomplete homework, not attending school regularly and all the other things that teachers worry about. But we didn\'t mind it. It made no difference to us. We all liked our class the way it was. We were, in fact, quite proud of our class.\n- And so was our new Class Teacher, Mrs Desai. She said so in the very first period. She said, \"I have heard about you people from your last year\'s teachers, and I am proud of you. Unity is a very important quality and you must always maintain it. I am very happy to be your Class Teacher.\"','Read the following passage and complete the activity \n1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false \ni) Mrs. Desai was proud of her class\nii) All the boys and girls in the class were united\niii) The teachers never shouted at the class\niv) Mrs. Desai was their new class teacher.','2) Complete the following web :','3) Complete the following chart of opposite words :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Words</th>\n    <th>Opposite words</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) incomplete</td>\n    <td>a) Similarity</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii) regularly</td>\n    <td>b) rich</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii) poor</td>\n    <td>c) complete</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv) difference</td>\n    <td>d) irregularly</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n4) Make the following sentences exclamatory \n    i) Unity is a very important quality.\n    ii) I am very happy.\n    iii) When do your parents shout at you?','1) i) True         ii) True          iii) False        iv) True\n2) i) poor marks     ii) shabby work          iii) incomplete homework         iv) not attending school regularly\n3) i – c, ii – d, iii – b, iv – a, \n4) i) What is a very important quality unity is!\nii) How happy I am!\n5) My mother shouts at me when I am late to me. If I don’t do my homework, my father shouts at me.','',0,1,0,NULL),(82715,185,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','We were used to teachers shouting at us for poor marks, shabby work, incomplete homework, not attending school regularly and all the other things that teachers worry about. But we didn\'t mind it. It made no difference to us. We all liked our class the way it was. We were, in fact, quite proud of our class.\n- And so was our new Class Teacher, Mrs Desai. She said so in the very first period. She said, \"I have heard about you people from your last year\'s teachers, and I am proud of you. Unity is a very important quality and you must always maintain it. I am very happy to be your Class Teacher.\"','Read the following passage and complete the activity \n1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false \ni) Mrs. Desai was proud of her class\nii) All the boys and girls in the class were united\niii) The teachers never shouted at the class\niv) Mrs. Desai was their new class teacher.','1) i) -----------------------------                    ii) ----------------------------\n\n    iii) ----------------------------                      iv) -----------------------------------\n\n2) i) ----------------------------                      ii) ------------------------------------\n\n    iii) --------------------------                         iv) ----------------------------------\n\n3) i) --------------------------                          ii) ----------------------------------\n\n   iii) --------------------------                         iv) ----------------------------------\n\n4) i) -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n   ii) ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n5) -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n2) Complete the following web :','3) Complete the following chart of opposite words :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Words</th>\n    <th>Opposite words</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i) incomplete</td>\n    <td>a) Similarity</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii) regularly</td>\n    <td>b) rich</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii) poor</td>\n    <td>c) complete</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv) difference</td>\n    <td>d) irregularly</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n4) Make the following sentences exclamatory \n    i) Unity is a very important quality.\n    ii) I am very happy.\n    iii) When do your parents shout at you?','1) i) True         ii) True          iii) False        iv) True\n2) i) poor marks     ii) shabby work          iii) incomplete homework         iv) not attending school regularly\n3) i – c, ii – d, iii – b, iv – a, \n4) i) What is a very important quality unity is!\nii) How happy I am!\n5) My mother shouts at me when I am late to me. If I don’t do my homework, my father shouts at me.','',0,1,0,NULL),(82716,185,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','We were used to teachers shouting at us for poor marks, shabby work, incomplete homework, not attending school regularly and all the other things that teachers worry about. But we didn\'t mind it. It made no difference to us. We all liked our class the way it was. We were, in fact, quite proud of our class.\n- And so was our new Class Teacher, Mrs Desai. She said so in the very first period. She said, \"I have heard about you people from your last year\'s teachers, and I am proud of you. Unity is a very important quality and you must always maintain it. I am very happy to be your Class Teacher.\"','1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false.\n\n     i. Mrs. Desai was proud of her class. ------------------\n\n    ii. All the boys and girls in the class were united. -------------------\n\n    iii. The teachers never shouted at the class. --------------------\n\n    iv. Mrs. Desai was their new class teacher. ------------------------\n\n','2) Complete the following web :','--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n3) Write the opposite words of the following\n\n     i. incomplete  : ---------            ii. regularly : ------------','1) i) True            ii) True           iii) False          iv) True\n2) i) poor marks                                 ii) shabby work\n    iii) incomplete homework           iv) not attending school regularly.\n3) i) complete              ii) irregularly\n','',0,1,0,NULL),(82717,568,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','We were used to teachers shouting at us for poor marks, shabby work, incomplete homework, not attending school regularly and all the other things that teachers worry about. But we didn\'t mind it. It made no difference to us. We all liked our class the way it was. We were, in fact, quite proud of our class.\n- And so was our new Class Teacher, Mrs Desai. She said so in the very first period. She said, \"I have heard about you people from your last year\'s teachers, and I am proud of you. Unity is a very important quality and you must always maintain it. I am very happy to be your Class Teacher.\"','1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false.\n     i. Mrs. Desai was proud of her class. ------------------\n    ii. All the boys and girls in the class were united. -------------------\n    iii. The teachers never shouted at the class. --------------------\n    iv. Mrs. Desai was their new class teacher. ------------------------','2) Complete the following web :','3) Write the opposite words of the following\n     i. incomplete × -----------             ii. regularly  × -----------------','1) i) True            ii) True           iii) False          iv) True\n2) i) poor marks                                 ii) shabby work\n    iii) incomplete homework           iv) not attending school regularly.\n3) i) complete              ii) irregularly','',0,1,0,NULL),(82718,568,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','However, there is one last thing which I must tell. We had hoped that we would all do well. Many of us did do well, but alas, there were still some who got poor marks, and some had even failed. So then we all began to feel crushed. Naina, who had got a red mark in her card couldn\'t bear it.\n\"I have let the whole class down,\" she sobbed. \"No, Naina, you haven\'t let anyone down. Look at your card. Last time you had got only two out of fifty in science and this time, it\'s sixteen. That\'s quite a leap. You have started off in the right direction and I know you will keep it up. I am proud of you, each one of you.\"\nCan you guess what happened in the next test?','Read the following passage and complete the activity\n1) Give reasons -------\ni) We all began to feel crushed because --------\nii)  Naina started crying because ---------','2) Complete the following web :','3) From the passage, find out the opposite of\ni) Succeeded x             iii) Wrong x\n4) Do as directed.\n  i) Some had even failed (Make negative)\n   ii) Naina could\'nt bear it (Make affirmative)\n   iii) I have let the whole class down she sobbed. (Punctuate the sentences)\n5) Can you guess, what happened in the next test?','1) i) Some of us got poor marks and some had even failed.\nii) She had got a red mark in her card.\n2) i) You haven’t let any one down.\nii) Last time you had got only two marks but this time you got fifteen.\niii) It is quite a progress\niv) I am sure that you will keep it up.\n3) i) failed      ii) last         iii) right       iv) down\n4) i) i) Some had not even down she sobbed.\nii) Naina was unable to bear it.\niii) “I have let the whole class down”, she sobbed.\n5) In the next test number of failures decreased. Naina made progress and she did not get red mark in any subject.','',0,1,0,NULL),(82719,185,'descriptive','3a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','However, there is one last thing which I must tell. We had hoped that we would all do well. Many of us did do well, but alas, there were still some who got poor marks, and some had even failed. So then we all began to feel crushed. Naina, who had got a red mark in her card couldn\'t bear it.\n\"I have let the whole class down,\" she sobbed. \"No, Naina, you haven\'t let anyone down. Look at your card. Last time you had got only two out of fifty in science and this time, it\'s sixteen. That\'s quite a leap. You have started off in the right direction and I know you will keep it up. I am proud of you, each one of you.\"\nCan you guess what happened in the next test?','Read the following passage and complete the activity\n1) Give reasons -------\ni) We all began to feel crushed because --------\nii)  Naina started crying because ---------\n\n\n','Answers : \n1) i) -----------------------------                    ii) ----------------------------\n2) i) ----------------------------                      ii) ------------------------------------\n\n    iii) --------------------------                         iv) ----------------------------------\n\n3) i) --------------------------                          ii) ----------------------------------\n\n   iii) --------------------------                         iv) ----------------------------------\n\n4) i) -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n    ii) ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n  iii) ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n5) -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n     --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2) Complete the following web :','3) From the passage, find out the opposite of\n\ni) Succeeded x             iii) Wrong x\n4) Do as directed.\n  i) Some had even failed (Make negative)\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n   ii) Naina could\'nt bear it (Make affirmative)\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n   iii) I have let the whole class down she sobbed. (Punctuate the sentences)\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n5) Can you guess, what happened in the next test?\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------------','1) i) Some of us got poor marks and some had even failed.\nii) She had got a red mark in her card.\n2) i) You haven’t let any one down.\nii) Last time you had got only two marks but this time you got fifteen.\niii) It is quite a progress\niv) I am sure that you will keep it up.\n3) i) failed      ii) last         iii) right       iv) down\n4) i) i) Some had not even down she sobbed.\nii) Naina was unable to bear it.\niii) “I have let the whole class down”, she sobbed.\n5) In the next test number of failures decreased. Naina made progress and she did not get red mark in any subject.','',0,1,0,NULL),(82720,2486,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which rights do we have?','','','We have a right to get clean potable water, sufficient food, a safe shelter and education.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82721,2486,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What makes a house feel like a home?','','','The factors like people who live in a house, their love and affection for one another makes a house, feel like a home.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82722,2486,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which types of problems arise due to heavy use of natural resources to build houses?','','','The problems like water pollution, air pollution, noise pollution, soil pollution, felling trees and forests, energy crisis, environmental degradation, etc. arise due to heavy use of natural resources to build houses.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82723,2486,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What kind of energy resources should man use?','','','Man should use non exhaustible type of energy resources.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82724,2486,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give brief information on uses of a house.','','','1) House is used as a shelter or resting place.\n2) It protects us from the sun, the wind, the cold and the rains. \n3) It also protects us from wild animals. \n4) It keeps us safe from anti-social elements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82725,2486,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give brief information of a problem of homelessness.','','','1) Homelessness is a social problem. \n2) Poverty, natural disasters, physical and mental disorders, unemployment, inadequate income, lack of affordable housing are some of the causes for homelessness. \n3) Homeless seek shelter at the roadside, on footpaths, under bridges, in tumbledown buildings, in railway or bus stations and even on open grounds. \n4) The government implements many schemes to provide homes to the homeless. In some cities, the government makes \'night shelters\' available to the homeless.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82726,2486,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which methods are followed by a man to obtain natural resources?','','','The following methods are followed by a man to obtain natural resources : 1) Digging up hills for quarrying \n2) Drawing sand from seashores and riverbeds \n3) Extracting stone and earth from the ground \n4) Drawing out excessive amounts of groundwater \n5) Felling trees to clear land \n6) Filling up ponds, lakes, streams, rivers, creeks and low-lying areas to reclaim land.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82727,2486,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief some characteristics of eco-friendly houses.','','','Some characteristics of eco-friendly houses are as follows : \n1) Eco-friendly houses consume natural resources in the minimum quantities. \n2) Eco-friendly houses use the nonexhaustible sources of energy like biogas, wind energy, solar energy. \n3) In such houses, the recycling of water is practised. \n4) In such houses, recycling of garbage is also followed. \n5) In such houses, the artificial materials and artificial colours are avoided. 6) In such houses, there is a provision for natural light and ventilation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ep_questions` VALUES (82728,2486,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes : Exhaustible sources of energy','','','1) The resources that are quickly exhausted by overuse are the \"exhaustible sources of energy\'. \n2) Coal, natural gas and mineral oil are the exhaustible sources of energy. \n3) It takes lakhs of years for these sources of energy to form in nature and they get consumed once they are used. Therefore these resources are to be used carefully. \n4) The burning of these sources lead to air pollution. Therefore these sources are to be used in the minimum quantities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82729,2486,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes : Non-exhaustible sources of energy','','','1) The resources that never get depleted by use are the non-exhaustible sources of energy. \n2) Solar energy, wind energy, biogas are the non-exhaustible sources of energy. \n3) These resources are available in plenty and therefore never come to an end. \n4) The use of these sources do not cause any pollution. Therefore these sources are to be used in the maximum quantities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82730,2486,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes : Felling trees and forests.','','','1) Because of growing urbanization, the land which was previously being used for agriculture and similar other purposes is now being used to build roads and to raise settlements. \n2) This leads to a shortage of agricultural land. Therefore forestland is used for agriculture. \n3) As its effect, trees from forests are felled in great numbers and forests are reduced. \n4) Felling trees and forests increases the pollution and degrades the environment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82731,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','About ----- of the land of our country is in use for agriculture.\na) 40% b) 50% c) 60% d) 70%','','','c) 60%\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82732,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','----- and ---- are two types of modern methods.\na) Drip irrigation, sprinkle irrigation b) Flood irrigation, furrow irrigation c) Manual irrigation, chemical irrigation d) Well irrigation, canal irrigation','','','a) Drip irrigation, sprinkle irrigation\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82733,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Natural Fertilizers are called -----\na) Manures b) Pesticides c) Fertilizers d) Herbicides','','','a) Manures\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82734,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','----- and ------ are sprayed on the crops.\na) Insecticides, pesticides b) Fertilizers, water c) Seeds, manures d) Herbicides, manures','','','a) Insecticides, pesticides\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82735,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The surplus food grain is stored in huge --------\na) Warehouses b) Silos c) Granaries d) Barns','','','a) Warehouses\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82736,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The seasons from June to October is called ---- season.\na) Rabi b) Kharif c) Summer d) Winter','','','b) Kharif\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82737,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','In farming it is necessary to carry out the tasks of ---- of the land.\na) Ploughing b) Tilling c) Sowing d) Harvesting','','','b) Tilling\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82738,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Overuse of fertilizers lowers the ---- of the soil.\na) Fertility b) Moisture c) Texture d) pH','','','a) Fertility\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82739,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','----- leaves are mixed with the grains.\na) Neem b) Mango c) Guava d) Peepal','','','a) Neem\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82740,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Squirrels store -----\na) Nuts b) Seeds c) Fruits d) Leaves','','','b) Seeds\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82741,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','People are displaced due to disasters like -----\na) Earthquake b) Flood c) Fire d) Cyclone','','','a) Earthquake\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82742,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The credit for research that led to Green Revolution goes to ------\na) Dr. M. S. Swaminathan b) Dr. A. P. J. Abdul Kalam c) Dr. Vikram Sarabhai d) Dr. Homi Bhabha','','','a) Dr. M. S. Swaminathan\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82743,2487,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The laws made to ensure that person gets sufficient food are called ----- laws.\na) Security laws b) Agricultural laws c) Environmental laws d) Trade la','','','a) Security laws\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82744,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: There is only one way of farming.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(82745,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: India is an agricultural country.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82746,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Use of improved seeds does not lead to higher yields.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82747,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Storing grains is an important task.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82748,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: The season from October to March is called season of summer crops.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(82749,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Storing of harvested grains is not as important as the farming operations.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82750,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Crops are traditionally watered by canals.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(82751,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false:  By obstructing the flow of river water downstream, the ground water level can decreases.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(82752,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false:  Drip irrigation is a traditional method of agriculture.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(82753,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: Over watering makes the land saline and fallow.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82754,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false: The harvested crop does not store properly. \n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(82755,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false:  Food is basic need.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(82756,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out: Drip irrigation, traditional, irrigation method, sprinkle irrigation\n','','','traditional','',0,0,0,NULL),(82757,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','\nFind odd man out: Garlic, Radishes, sweet potatoes \n','','','Garlic','',0,0,0,NULL),(82758,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns:\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Column A</th>\n    <th>Column B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Overwatering</td>\n    <td>a) Oxen</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) Drip irrigation</td>\n    <td>b) Saline</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) Sprinkle irrigation</td>\n    <td>c) Pipes with holes</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4) \'Mot\'</td>\n    <td>d) Sprays</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – d), (4 – a)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82759,2487,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns:\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Column A</th>\n    <th>Column B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Grain stored in damp<br>place</td>\n    <td>a) Prevents growth of<br>fungus.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) Grain stored in dry air</td>\n    <td>b) Preservers insect<br>infestations</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) Adding proper chemicals<br>to the stored grains</td>\n    <td>c) Fungus attack</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','','','(1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – b)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82760,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the two modern methods of irrigation?\n','','','Drip and sprinkle irrigation are the two types of irrigation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82761,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the kinds of fertilizers.\n','','','Natural and chemical are two kinds of fertilizers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82762,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the plants which stored food in their stems.\n','','','Plants link onions, potatoes, ginger and garlic stored food in their stems.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82763,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','To whom is the credit of Green revolution in India given?\n','','','The credit of Green Revolution is given to Dr. M. S. Swaminathan \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82764,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by organic farming?\n','','','Farming that relies on natural materials is called organic farming.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82765,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','From where does the farmer get information about modern agricultural technology?\n','','','Farmers get information about modern agriculture technology from Agricultural Assistance programmers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82766,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find out the names of two varieties of improved seeds of Jowar.\n','','','CSH4 and SSV-84, Phule Yashoda, Phule Mauli, Swati are the names of improved seed varieties of Jowar.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82767,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find out who helps the farmer to drive the ‘mot’?\n','','','A pair of oxen help the farmer to drive the mot’.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82768,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find out how is groundwater lifted these days?\n','','','The groundwater was taken out with the help of hand pumps and by digging bore wells. Even now at many places hand pumps are used. However, in majority cases, now electric motor is fitted to draw water from the underground level.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82769,2487,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which vegetables do we get around the time of Diwali? Which fruits? Which grains?\n','','','a) vegetables : Spiny gourd (Kantoli), Ridge gourd (Shiroli), Ghosali, Kohala\nb) Fruits : Sweet lime, Oranges, Apples, Musk melons\nc) Grains : Ragi, Rice \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82770,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the advantages of using improved seeds?\n','','','i) Improved seeds are essential for a good harvest.\nii) These seeds are developed through research\niii) These have a higher yield\niv) They can resist pest attacks.\nv) Such improved seeds lead to an increase in production.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82771,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the modern methods of irrigation? What are their advantages? \n','','','Drip irrigation and sprinkler irrigation are two modern methods of the irrigation.\nAdvantages\ni) By these methods we can provide enough water to the plants\nii) These methods helps to save a lot of water\niii) In the drip irrigation the required amount of water. Drips only near the plants. Where as in the sprinkle spray water directly on plants.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82772,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the ‘drip irrigation’ method.\n','','','i) Drip irrigation also known as trickle irrigation.\nii) In this method water drips slowly to the roots of plants. Through a network of valves, pipes are tubing.\niii) By using this method, we can save lot of water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82773,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the solution? The plant in the pot is not growing well.\n','','','i) Because, plants need a plenty of water and sunlight for growing well. But these content does not get in the right proportion to the potted plants.\nii) Roots of the plants do not grow well in the pot.\niii) Plants do not have a support\niv) Use the fertilizers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82774,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the purpose of storing food grain in our house?\n','','','i) We need food grain throughout the year.\nii) For availability of food grains in the house we stored it.\niii) To avoid from mice, rats and insects’ food grains must be stored.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82775,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the advantage of storing grain in wattle and daub baskets?\n','','','i) Wattle and daub baskets have holes on their surface. This keeps air circulating around the stored grains.\nii) The grains do not become moist and thus do not catch fungus.\niii) Moreover, there is less chance of insect attack due to dry and airy conditions.\niv) The grains stored like this can remain in better condition for a longer period of time. Therefore, grains are stored in wattle and daub baskets.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82776,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it necessary to store food grains?\n','','','i) In some years there is a surplus food grains production. Therefore, excess grains can be stored.\nii) In some years there are natural disasters and there is reduced crop yield. People need food grains during such times.\niii) To provide food grains research difficult times, it is necessary to store food grains.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82777,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which law reacted to food is enacted in India? What is the purpose of this law?\n','','','i) Food Security Law is enacted in India from 2013.\nii) Due to this law every person in India, gets sufficient food according to their needs.\niii) This law also fight against the ills like malnutrition, starvation and deaths due to hunger.\nQuestion','',0,0,0,NULL),(82778,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the activities done through Agricultural assistance programme? \n','','','i) Farmers are given proper information and guidance about the latest technology, irrigation facilities, improved seeds, use of fertilizers and pesticides. ii) Farmers also gives information about weather forecast and other agriculture related information.\niii) Agricultural schools have started to teach modern technology to farmers and their family members.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82779,2487,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the seasons for jowar, bajra, rice, mangoes, oranges and jack fruit?\n','','','a) Jowar and Bajra : During winter		b) Mango and Jackfruit : In summer	c) Rice : In monsoon/rainy season	            d) Oranges : Beginning of winter \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82780,2487,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the reasons due to which growing crops are damaged?\n','','','i) The condition of growing crops depends upon a quality of soil and cultivation.\nii) Crops can be damaged due to diseases and pest attacks.\niii) If the fertility of the soil is reduced it can affects on the crops.\niv) To prevent the damaging crops insecticides or pesticides must be sprayed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82781,2487,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is done to avoid the damage to crops?\n','','','i) To avoid the damage of crops as a remedy insecticides and pesticides must be sprayed.\nii) The certain chemicals are rubbed on the seeds before sowing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82782,2487,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','\nWhat causes the fertility of soil to diminish?\n','','','i) The overuse of chemical fertilizers is reduced the fertility of the soil.\nii) If the same crops are grown frequently in the field, the fertility of soil will be decreases.\niii) Over watering also makes the land saline and fallow.\niv) This lowers the fertility of the soil and reduces its productivity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82783,2487,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What changes has modern technology brought about in the methods of farming?\n','','','i) Through the modern technology farmers get all information about agriculture.\nii) The farmers knows about irrigation facilities, improved seeds, use of fertilizer, latest technology and pesticides through Agriculture Assistance Programme \niii) The agriculture department spread of modern method of agriculture through agriculture universities, television, newspaper and various periodicals work.\niv) This helps to increase production.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82784,2487,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What methods are used to preserve foodgrain?\n','','','Preserving food grain safely is as important as increasing methods are used for this purposes.\ni) The grain is dried well in the sun and filled in the sacks.\nii) The food grain is protected in houses or warehouses from mice, rats and insects to avoid spoil.\niii) Some chemical pests are sprayed at the place of storage.\niv) Some protective chemicals are mixed in the food grain.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82785,2487,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How is water made available for farming?\n','','','i) In traditional method of agriculture a ‘mot’ is used to drawn water from a well with the help of oxen.\nii) Water is made available by building dams and bunds on rivers to abstract the flow of water downstream.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82786,2487,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Organic farming\n','','','i) Traditional farming methods using natural and organic materials is known as organic farming.\nii) In this method, organic fertilizers and pesticides are used. They do not cause pollution of the soil.\niii) Manures consisting of animal and plant products increase the nutritive substances in the soil.\niv) The grains grown by this method are nutritious and tasty.\nv) More and more farmers are opting for organic methods of farming.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82787,2487,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on: Green Revolution.\n','','','i) India has become self-reliant as far as food grain production is concerned. ii) This abundant supply of grains in India due to revolution in agriculture is known as Green Revolution.\niii) The agricultural scientist Dr M. S. Swaminathan is responsible for this Green revolution.\niv) Due to research, there was improvement in the seeds of rice and wheat. Such seeds helped us to grow surplus crops.\nv) After green revolution, we are now exporting surplus grains to other countries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82788,2487,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which parts of plants do we use as food?\n','','','i) Roots : Carrot, Beet root, Radish, Sweet potato\nii) Steam : Potato, Elephant’s foot (Suran), Ginger, Onion\niii) Leaves : Arum, Colocasia, Cabbage, all leafy green vegetables\niv) Fruits : Pumpkin, Tamarind, Bitter gourd, Snake gourd\nv) Flower : Cauliflower, Banana flower\nvi) Seeds : Beans, Lentil, Cow pea, Gram.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82793,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence. : Due to various means of transport, the world has come closer.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(82794,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence. : Transport has helped in improving lifestyle of people.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(82795,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence. : Burning of fuel in the vehicles releases minute particles of lead and mercury into the air.','','','Wrong: Burning of fuel in the vehicles releases minute particles of lead and carbon into the air.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82796,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence. : We should cultivate habits such as walking short distances or riding a bicycle for slightly longer distances.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(82797,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd One out : Carbon monoxide, nitrogen, nitrogen dioxdde, sulphur dioxde.','','','Nitrogen.\nReason: Nitrogen gas is present in air as a part of its natural composition, while the remaining gases are released due to burning of fuel in the vehicles and pollute air.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82798,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd One out : Restlessness, irritability, headache, stomach-ache.','','','Stomach-ache.\nReason: Stomach-ache is not an ill effect of noise pollution caused by vehicles, while the remaining are the ill effects of noise pollution caused by vehicles.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82799,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd One out : Petrol, CNG, diesel, coal.','','','CNG.\nReason: Petrol, diesel, coal (used earlier as fuel to run trains) when burn as fuels in vehicles pollute air heavily while burning of CNG is a cleaner fuel i.e., it causes less air pollution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82800,2489,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Compare the use of two modes of transport \'a bus\' and \'a car\'.','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Using a BEST bus</th>\n    <th>Using a car</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(i) It is an example of public transport.</td>\n    <td>It is an example of private transport.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(ii) It carries many passengers at a time.</td>\n    <td>It carries up to five people at a time.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(iii) It is fuel efficient and cheaper.</td>\n    <td>It is comparatively fuel consuming and expensive.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(iv) It runs on set routes.</td>\n    <td>It takes the routes as preferred by users.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(v) It runs according to its time schedule.</td>\n    <td>It offers self reliance.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(vi) A bus ride offers lesser comfort than a car ride.</td>\n    <td>A car ride offers more comfort than a bus ride.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(82801,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are various means of transport harmful to the nature?','','','Various means of transport are harmful to the nature because they cause air and noise pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82802,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is air pollution?','','','Degradation of the quality of air due to excess of undesirable substances such as carbon monoxide, nitrogen dioxide, lead etc. released in air is called as air pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82803,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What ill effects are observed on health due to air pollution caused by burning of fuel in vehicles?','','','Due to air pollution caused by burning of fuel in vehicles, disorders of trachea, lung and eye (burning of eyes) are observed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82804,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How would constant traffic through forest affect the living beings there?','','','Constant traffic through forest would harm the habitat of plants and animals and force the wild animals to migrate elsewhere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82805,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Will you call a sailing ship eco-friendly? Why?','','','A sailing ship is eco-friendly because such a ship does not use engines running on fuel. Hence, it is eco-friendly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82806,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Write five sentences on how you have benefited from transport facilities.','','','Transport facilities have benefited me in the following ways:\n(i) I go to school by school bus.\n(ii) Over weekends, I visit gardens, malls or nearby exhibitions with my parents in our car.\n(iii) I use my bicycle to go to the library or playground in my locality.\n(iv) Whenever my family members plan one day picnic to visit nearby spots, we all go together in a private bus.\n(v) During vacation, I visit my relatives using trains or planes. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(82807,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','List four other facilities that have become available in the local area due to transport facilities. ','','','Due to transport facilities, following facilities have become available in our local area:\n(i) A vegetable and fruit market is developed. Also, some florists have opened shops in the area.\n(ii) A weekly bazaar sets up in an open municipal ground due to easy availability of transportation of goods.\n(iii) The growth in the number of residential complexes is observed due to transport facilities.\n(iv) Commercial offices, banks, hospitals, multiplexes, libraries etc. have developed and benefited by transport facilities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82808,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Suggest four solutions to reduce the burden on the local transport.','','','Following steps can be taken to reduce the burden on the local transport:\n(i) People should walk to nearby places whenever possible.\n(ii) People can use bicycle as a means of transportation.\n(iii) Public transport should be used as much as possible to reduce crowding of streets with vehicles.\n(iv) Private vehicles can be shared with friends, office colleagues to travel together.\n(v) Traffic rules and regulations should be obeyed properly to avoid traffic jam.\n(vi) Building pandals or carrying out religious or wedding processions on the roads should be avoided.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82809,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : We get rainwater throughout the year.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82810,2489,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Find the area in your locality with the least pollution. Why is this the least polluted area? ','','','The area in my locality with the least pollution is big municipal garden.\n(i) This garden is quite at the interiors, away from the main road.\n(ii) As a result, it is not continuously surrounded by vehicles. Only one or two buses per half an hour ply. Also, as it is in the residential area, there is no continuous traffic of vehicles.\n(iii) The garden is well maintained with many banyan, peepul, neem and mango trees. The dense vegetation helps in purifying air effectively and reducing noise pollution. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(82811,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : During a famine, the government moves people and animals temporarily to safe places.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82812,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The sewage water does not have the impurities.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82813,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Write the full forms of CNG and LPG.','','','The full form of CNG is Compressed Natural Gas and that of LPG is Liquefied Petroleum Gas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82814,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Grain and fodder become scare during a famine.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82815,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Large dams can be built in all places.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82816,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Bunds should be built for storing water.\n','','','\nTrue\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82817,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Flowing water of rivers get purified completely by natural processes.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(82818,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Water evaporates continuously.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82819,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Water pollution is not harmful.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82820,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of Sources of water.\n','','','Wells, tube-wells, rivers, lakes, ponds, streams\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82821,2491,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of Methods of purification of water.\n','','','Settling, Oxygenation, Filtration and Chlorination \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82822,2489,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','i) In the picture given on which vehicle is causing pollution?\nii) What remedy will you suggest to reduce the pollution caused by this vehicle?\n','','','i) The bus shown in the picture is causing pollution.\n(ii) Remedies to reduce the pollution caused by the bus are:\n(a) The bus should be checked to find out the reason behind release of excessive smoke and it should be repaired and then maintained properly.\n(b) It should be run on less polluting fuel like LPG or CNG.\n(c) It should be taken to PUC (Pollution Under Control) centres to monitor smoke released by the bus.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(82823,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are different advantages of using a bicycle?','','','i) Riding a bicycle is a good physical exercise.\n(ii) It does not cause pollution.\n(iii) It leads to less crowding of vehicles on the streets.\n(iv) It can be parked in a small space.\n(v) It has provisions to carry small loads.\n(vi) A bicycle ride saves time and offers rider self reliance.\n(vii) As a bicycle does not need expensive fuel or maintenance, it is very economic.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82824,2489,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why it is necessary to prevent pollution?','','','i) Our environment is sensitive. All living and non-living things are components of the environment.\n(ii) Pollution caused by various activities has destructive effect on environment.\n(iii) Once destroyed, we cannot construct or form the environment again.\n(iv) Pollution is also harmful for all living things including human beings.\nHence, it is necessary to prevent pollution for survival of living things on the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82825,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does transportation pollute air?','','','i) Vehicles run on fuels. Burning of fuels results into emission of smoke and poisonous gases such as carbon monoxide, nitrogen dioxide and sulphur dioxide.\n(ii) Burning of fuel also releases minute particles of carbon and lead. When these substances are present in air excessively, they lower the quality of air in the environment. Thus, transportation causes air pollution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82826,2491,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is achieved by stopping the water from flowing away and making it percolate in the soil?\n','','','Water management\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82827,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill-effects caused by transport on human health?','','','i) Transportation causes air pollution which has harmful effects on human health. Air pollution causes disorders of trachea, lung and eye, including burning of eyes.\n(ii) The constant sound of vehicles leads to noise pollution and causes restlessness. irritability, headaches, psychological disorders and lack of concentration.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82828,2491,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Who undertakes several works of making water available even after the rainy season?\n','','','The Government and the local people\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82829,2491,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is done in water-works?\n','','','Purification of water\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82830,2491,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the three states of water?\n','','','Solid, liquid and gaseous are the three states of water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82831,2491,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which method will you use to kills the germs in water?\n','','','Chlorination method will be used to kill germs in water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82832,2491,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by scarcity of water?\n','','','Places at which falls the water level, there is no water foe animals and agriculture. This is called scarcity of water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82833,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill-effects caused by transport on plants and animals?','','','i) Transportation causes air pollution which has harmful effects on plants. The growth and development of plants is stunted. The leaves of plants shrivel  up and fall, sprouts get scorched.\n(ii) Constant traffic through forest areas may harm the habitat of plants and animals living there. This may force wild animals to migrate elsewhere.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82834,2491,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the different methods that used for purification of water.\n','','','Settling, oxygenation, filtration and chlorination are the methods used for purification of water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82835,2491,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when a spoonful sugar, sawdust and soil are added to a glass of water?\n','','','When sugar is added to the water, it dissolves completely and disappears in water. When sawdust is added, it will float on the surface of the water. when soil is added it will settle at the bottom of the water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82836,2491,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is done to make drinking water clean and safe?\n','','','Methods of settling and filtering will make the water clean. For making it safe it is boiled for some time. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82837,2489,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a note about the following : Advantages of transport:\n','','','Following are the advantages of transport:\nDue to transport facilities,\n(a) different parts of the world are connected making it possible to travel to any part of the world.\n(b) supply of goods at global level has become easy and simple.\n(c) time and efforts are saved.\n(d) quick completion of work is possible.\n(e) trade is facilitated.\n(f) the facilities of tourism, health, education have become more effective and speedy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82838,2491,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What conditions prevail during a famine?\n','','','The following conditions prevail during a famine :\ni) The water level falls in river, wells, lakes, bunds as well as in dams.\nii) Humans and animals suffered from scarcity of water.\niii) Grain and fodder became scared during a famine\niv) There is no water for agriculture.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82839,2491,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What works are undertaken to make water available even after the rainy season?\n','','','i) Works such as building smaller, reservoirs, contour bunding, stopping the water of the smaller stream by constructing bunds.\nii) The large dams can be built to make availability of water after the rainy season.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82840,2491,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it necessary to stop rainwater?\n','','','i) We get rain for only four months but we needs of water throughout the year.\nii) To meet the whole year’s need for water, rainwater must be stopped.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82841,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a note about the following : Disadvantages of transport','','','Following are the disadvantages of transport:\n(a) Transportation causes air pollution.\n(1) Burning of fuel results into emission of smoke and poisonous gases such as carbon monoxide, nitrogen dioxide and sulphur dioxide.\n(2) Burning of fuel also releases minute particles of carbon and lead. When these substances are present excessively in the air, they lower the quality of air in the environment.\n(3) Air pollution causes disorders of trachea, lung and eye including burning of eyes.\n(4) The growth and development of plants is stunted. The leaves of plants shrivel up and fall, sprouts get scorched.\n(b) The constant sound of vehicles leads to noise pollution and causes restlessness, irritability, headaches, psychological disorders and lack of concentration.\n(c) Constant traffic through forest areas may harm the habitat of plants and animals living there. This may force wild animals to migrate elsewhere.\n(d) Constant traffic jams increase air and noise pollution in the surrounding area.\n(e) Traffic accidents cause injuries to people, deaths in some cases and damage to the vehicles.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82842,2491,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by water management?\n','','','Water management is the process of stopping or storing water into the ground to make it available even after the rainy season.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82843,2491,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','How does water pollution take place?\n','','','i) the waste water from households is collected and let out in larger water bodies in cities and villages.\nii) This water contains many impurities. The sewage water contains many diseases-causing micro-organisms.\niii) Poisonous substances are present in the water let out by industries and factories.\niv) Some of the impurities are dissolved in water while some are floating in water. All these impurities cause water pollution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82844,2491,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the methods of storing rainwater?\n','','','i) Wells are dug in the river beds and water is allowed to collect in these wells\nii) Rainwater falling of the rooftops of houses is allowed to flow through pipes in the tanks placed beside the buildings\niii) Bunds or bandharas are constructed on the smaller streams so that the water stops and percolates in the soil. Smaller reservoirs are also made to store rainwater.\niv) Stopping the rainwater and allowing it to percolate in the ground, helps to raise the ground water level. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82845,2489,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a note about the following : Remedies to reduce air pollution caused by transport','','','Air pollution caused by means of transport can be minimised by practising following measures:\n(a) Instead of using fuel that causes pollution like petrol, diesel, fuels that cause less pollution such as CNG (Compressed Natural Gas), LPG (Liquefied Petroleum Gas) should be used.\n(b) Timely maintenance and repair of vehicles should be carried out. This ensures vehicles do not release excess emission of gases.\n(c) Public transport should be used as much as possible and private vehicles should be used only when necessary.\n(d) Trees like neem, peepul, banyan, karanj etc. which adapt easily to the local environment and help to enhance biodiversity of place should be planted.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82846,2491,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','How can we reduce the proportion of water pollution?\n','','','i) Sewage and waste water of towns and cities must be purified before letting into a water body.\nii) Water that released from factories should be treat before letting out.\niii) Trench latrines, septic tanks and soak pits should be built to drain away the waste water from toilets and latrines.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82847,2491,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','While it is raining, collect some of the rainwater directly in a clean container placed in an open. Also collect some of the rainwater that is flowing over the ground. Observe and compare the two. What difference do you see? What is the reason for this?','','','Water collected directly from the falling rain will appear to be clean. The water that flows over the ground will appear turbid. Since water flowing over the ground carries a lot of contamination, it will look turbid and dirty.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82848,2491,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution : Soil in a garden gets washed away with the water due to the slope of the land.\n','','','i) The lateral trenches should be dug at regular intervals across the slope of the land\nii) These trenches prevents flowing soil with the water.\niii) Bunds should be built at slope of the land.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82849,2491,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How should roads and footpaths be built to make rainwater seep into the ground?\n','','','i) Trenches should be built at both sides to allow the flowing rainwater\nii) Bunds should be built at the sides of the footpaths\niii) Roads should be built with the slopes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82850,2491,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution : Your class will be visiting a lake which is far away from the city. Arrangements have to be made for the day’s drinking water.\n','','','i) When will go for a trip we always carry water bottles with us.\nii) We will be visiting a lake so potable water is also available there. However, it may not be clean and safe.\niii) So, we need to carry a clean cloth for filtering the water, a piece of alum for settling the impurities, a purifying chemical for making water safe.\niv) If we go prepared like this, then there will not be a problem of a day’s safe drinking water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82851,2491,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : Water management is being used in your surroundings. \n','','','i) The flowing rainwater is collected into the tanks and reused. This is called water management.\nii) In this method rainwater is stored by the digging a well. \niii) Rainwater falling on roofs of houses is drainage into large tanks.\niv) Flowing rainwater makes seep into the ground.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82852,2491,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','List the substances that are mixed in the waste water drained out of the kitchen and bathroom in your house.\n','','','i) Various substances are mixed with waste water that drains out of the kitchen and bathroom of our house.\nii) This drained water contains excreta, urine, bathing water, liquid soap, detergents, water containing leftover food particles, dirty water formed due to washing utensils and clothes and various other contaminants.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82853,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Study the picture given on the textbook page no. 69 and note the answers to the following.','(a) Where have the children stopped?\n(b) Why have they stopped there?\n(c) What are the children doing? What is troubling them?\n','','(a) The children have stopped at the zebra crossing divider.\n(b) According to traffic rules, pedestrians are supposed to cross the roads by using zebra crossing. Vehicles are supposed to be driven with reduced speed near zebra crossing. Thus, it is safer to cross road at zebra crossings. Hence, children have stopped there to cross the road.\n(c) The girl in the picture is irritated by blaring noise of traffic and is covering her ears. The boy seems to be shocked by rash car driving near zebra crossing, while the little boy is rubbing his eyes as smoke coming out of vehicles; dust lifted due to high speed of vehicles has entered his eyes causing irritation.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(82854,2491,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','If, at some place, it did not rain for a very long period of time, what would be its effect on the life of the people there?\n','','','i) If a particular place does not get enough rains for a long period of time then there would be scarcity of water.\nii) If water is not available, farming cannot be done.\niii) Crops will not grow. People might have to migrate in search of food and water.\niv) Cattle would also die due to lack of food and water. This complete area will be under drought. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82855,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Suggest ways to reduce noise pollution caused by vehicles. ','','','Noise pollution caused by vehicles can be minimized by practising following measures:\n(i) Timely maintenance and repair of vehicles should be carried out. This ensures vehicles do not make excess noise.\n(ii) Honking should be avoided as much as possible.\n(iii) By planting trees or shrubs of suitable varieties noise pollution can be reduced.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82856,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do you mean by public transport?','','','Public transport includes buses, trains and other forms of transport that are available for the general public which charge set fares and run on fixed routes and are owned by governmental body.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82857,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The population of the world is now close to ----- crore.\na) Four hundred b) Six hundred c) Eight hundred d) One thousand','','','b) Six hundred\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82858,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do you understand by private transport?','','','Private transport is transportation service not available for general public but is owned by an individual or a group. Examples: Cars, scooters and cab services.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82859,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Marshy or low-lying areas are filled to ---- the land.\na) Reclaim b) Pollute c) Destroy d) Drain\n','','','a) Reclaim\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82860,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give any two advantages of using a car running on CNG.','','','Using a car running on CNG causes less air pollution and CNG being comparatively cheaper than diesel and petrol, is economical.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82861,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Due to pollution, number of living things reduce and some become ------\na) Common b) Extinct c) Abundant d) Endangered\n','','','b) Extinct\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82862,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','In industries and factories, ------ is used in great quantities.\na) Water b) Fuel c) Electricity d) Sand\n','','','b) Fuel\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82863,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the trees which are planted to reduce pollution.','','','Banyan, peepul, neem and karanj are some of the trees planted to reduce pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82864,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Tremendous quantities of ---- enter the air due to burning of fuels.\na) Oxygen b) Carbon dioxide c) Nitrogen d) Helium\n','','','b) Carbon dioxide\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82865,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why were ships in olden days termed as sailing ships?','','','Sailing ships used to have sails which helped the ships to use the force of wind. Hence, they were termed as sailing ships.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82866,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Due to human ----- in nature, large scale changes are taking place in the air, water and land.\na) Intervention b) Observation c) Care d) Protection\n','','','a) Intervention\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82867,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','We should make all possible efforts for ----- of the living world.\na) Conservation b) Destruction c) Neglect d) Pollution\n','','','a) Conservation\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82868,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Forest areas reserved for the protection and conservation of a particular animal or a plant is called a ------\na) Zoo b) Sanctuary c) Garden d) Farm\n','','','b) Sanctuary\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82869,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Flamingo from ----- region in Northern Asia migrate to Mayani.\na) Siberia b) Himalaya c) Punjab d) Kashmir\n','','','a) Siberia\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82870,2493,'mcq','1a',11,0,'Out of Text Book','','----- or ---- can be called the sanctuaries of ancient times.\na) Devrais or Sharanavanas b) Forests or Jungles c) Parks or Gardens d) Rivers or Lakes\n','','','a) Devrais or Sharanavanas\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82871,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The environment provides ----- to different kinds of living things.\na) Food b) Shelter c) Pollution d) Water\n','','','b) Shelter\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82872,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The proportion of ----- in the air leads to rise the temperature.\na) Oxygen b) Carbon dioxide c) Nitrogen d) Argon\n','','','b) Carbon dioxide\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82873,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Food, clothes and ------- are our basic needs.\na) Shelter b) Education c) Money d) Electricity\n','','','a) Shelter\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82874,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','If the plants on the earth are destroyed the number of ---- also fall.\na) Animals b) Humans c) Insects d) Birds\n','','','a) Animals\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82875,2493,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Indian culture gives importance to the protection of -------\na) Rivers b) Mountains c) Forest d) Animals\n','','','c) Forest\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82876,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following flowchart.','','','i) Private           ii) Ship              iii) Bus/Train               iv) Aeroplane\nv) Boat              vi) Car               vii)  Helicopter.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(82877,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Air pollution may cause disorders of ____ and lung.\n(A) kidney  (B) stomach  (C) trachea  (D) brain','','','C) trachea','',0,0,0,NULL),(82878,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','We should avoid the use of ____ vehicles as much as possible.\n(A) public  (B) fuel-burning  (C) electric  (D) solar','','','B) fuel-burning','',0,0,0,NULL),(82879,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Timely maintenance and repair of vehicles\n(A) saves money  (B) saves time  (C) is unnecessary  (D) reduces pollution','','','D) reduces pollution','',0,0,0,NULL),(82880,2493,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by migration?\n','','','The movement of animals from one area to other is known as migration.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82881,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The sailing ships used to sail using the force of\n(A) wind  (B) ocean waves  (C) burning fuels  (D) engine','','','A) wind','',0,0,0,NULL),(82882,2493,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the lifecycle of birds.\n','','','i) Birds are the important biotic factor	ii) They insects which have on the plants		iii) Birds lives in the forest, lay eggs, give birth to young one	iv) If the number of trees are decreased they are also extinct.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82883,2493,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two causes of air pollution.\n','','','i) Unlimited growth in the number of factories and vehicles		ii) Cutting down the trees		iii) Burning of plastic or rubber		iv) An increasing the proportion of carbon dioxide in the air these are all the causes of air pollution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82884,2493,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','For what purpose do we use land obtained by clearing forests?\n','','','i) We used the land for agriculture		ii) Land is used for building houses	iii) Land is used to making roads as well as factories 	iv) For the railway land is used\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82885,2493,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Many aquatic animals are becoming extinct.\n','','','i) Soil and water is polluted to a great extent due to human intervention.\nii) The chemical fertilizers and insecticides used in farming practices also find their way into water.\niii) Waste water from industries, factories and household also find their way into water.\niv) All these may have poisonous and harmful substances in them. Therefore, many aquatic animals living in such water are becoming extinct.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82886,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Due to the burning of fuel in the vehicles, they constantly emit ____ and some poisonous gases.\n(A) water (B) smoke (C) dust (D) steam\n','','','B) smoke','',0,0,0,NULL),(82887,2493,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What could the reasons of air pollution?\n','','','i) Some natural events may also cause air pollution. E.g. Cyclones, volcanic eruptions, strong winds.\nii) But air pollution is caused to a greater extent due to human activities. E.g.  burning of fossil fuel, the toxic gases and particles, etc. emitted from industries and during transport.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82888,2493,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different purposes for which fuels such as petrol, diesel, kerosene, natural gas, coal and wood are used?\n','','','i) Petrol and diesel are used as a fuel in vehicles used for transport.\nii) Two wheelers, cars, trucks and aeroplanes run on these fuels.\niii) Kerosene, cooking gas (LPG) and wood are used as fuels for cooking and other domestic use.\niv) Coal is used for electricity generation and also in industrial process.\nv) Diesel is also used to run engines and machines in factories.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82889,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If there are traffic jams, air and ____ pollution in that area increases.\n(A) light (B) noise (C) water (D) land\n','','','B) noise','',0,0,0,NULL),(82890,2493,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What makes the loss of biodiversity?\n','','','i) Growth in human population : The human population of the world is continuously increasing. Now it is close to six hundred crore.\nii) Deforestation : Due to increasing human population, the needs of man are ever rising. For agriculture, housing, industry, building roads and railways, forest are cut down.\niii) All such activities are resulting into reduction of forests. Due to this Shelters of animals are destroyed resulting in loss of biodiversity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82891,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','As a remedy to reduce pollution, one should use ____ transport as far as possible.\n(A) private (B) public (C) animal (D) water\n','','','B) public','',0,0,0,NULL),(82892,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the modern times, we use cars, ships and aeroplanes that run on ____.\n(A) electricity (B) coal (C) fuel (D) wood\n','','','C) fuel','',0,0,0,NULL),(82893,2493,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following as natural or man made : Soil, horse, stone, water hyacinth, book, sunlight, dolphin, pen, chair, water, cotton wool, table, trees, brick.\n','','','a) Natural : Soil, horse, stone, water hyacinth, sunlight, dolphin, water, cotton wool, trees\nb) Man-made : Book, pen, chair, table, brick\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82894,2493,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution : Rivers and lakes are filled with water hyacinth.\n','','','i) If the water hyacinth grow excessively it causes problems for other aquatic organism.\nii) In order to save environment of such a water body, water hyacinth should be removed.\niii) Care should be taken not to use organic pollutants.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82895,2493,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What would happen if no kites (birds) are left in a particular region. Which living things would increase in number? Which would decrease?\n','','','i) If the birds are left some kinds of insects will be increased in that region. \nii) The cycles are very important because all animals are dependent on one another for their food.\niii) If no birds are left in the region large number of insects are increased which can be affects on the plants also.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82896,2493,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What should all of us resolve about environment?\n','','','i) We should resolve that no action should be done that will cause pollution or cause harm to living things.\nii) All the possible efforts should be done to conserve nature and living world.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82897,2493,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of question below, explain what would happen if forests are cleared on a large scale. Will the space occupies by the plants and animals increase or decrease? Why?','','','Due to deforestation, the plants and Shelters of animals will be destroyed completely. The barren space would increase but the space occupied by living things would decrease.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82898,2493,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of question below, explain what would happen if forests are cleared on a large scale. Will the number of living things increase or decrease? Why?','','','After deforestation, the number of living things would definitely decrease. Due to loss of forests, there is a loss of habitat. There would be loss of food and shelter and thus the number of living things will dwindle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82899,2493,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : It is important to conserve the living components of the environment.\n','','','i) Living components are very important factors of the environment.\nii) Living components includes plans and animals. They are dependent on each other.\niii) We get foods from this living component.\niv) If the living component do not exist then the food chain and food web will not function.\nv) Some species may become exist\nvi) Therefore, it is important to conserve living component of environment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82900,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(i) Bicycle</td>\n    <td>(c) Private transport</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(ii) Traffic accidents</td>\n    <td>(a) Damage to the vehicles</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(iii) Facilitated trade</td>\n    <td>(d) Transport facilities</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(iv) Sulphur dioxide</td>\n    <td>(b) Air pollution</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i) - (c), (ii) - (a), (iii) - (d), (iv) - (b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(82901,2493,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Explain : The numbers of wild animals are falling day-by-day.\n','','','i) Man has cutting down the trees for building houses, roads, factories and for agriculture.\nii) Due to loss of forest, the animals are displaced.\niii) Hunting of animals is increased day by day. Therefore, the number of wild animals are falling day by day.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82902,2493,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write some information about the Great Indian Bustard or Maldhok.\n','','','i) The Great Indian Bustards are huge weighty birds which are famous for their graceful gait.\nii) These birds live in the open grasslands. \niii) They feed on insects from the grasses and farms. Thus, they provide protection to the crops. Therefore, they are called friends of farmers.\niv) These birds were hunted for their meet and eggs.\nv) Their number was therefore dwindling.\nvi) But now Maharashtra State Government has declared Nannaj Solapur district as Maldhok Sanctuary.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82903,2489,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why it is essential to invent transport that would run on electricity and solar energy?','','','(a) At present, maximum means of transport use fuels. These fuels are formed over millions of years and are limited.\n(b) Burning of these fuels pollute air releasing gases in air which are harmful for all the living beings.\n(c) Electricity and solar energy are comparatively abundant sources of energy.\n(d) Also, they are clean sources of energy which means they do not pollute the environment.\nHence, it is essential to invent transport that would run on electricity and solar energy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82904,2493,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it said that village “Morachi Chincholi” is the best example of conservation of environment?\n','','','i) Morachi chincholi is the village present on the Pune- Ahmednagar Highway.\nii) This village is famous for peafowl population. The local people of this village give protection to the peacocks and peahens. \niii) There are many tamarind trees. These old and well looked after trees give favourable environment to the peafowl.\niv) Nobody disturbs the peafowl population and therefore village Morachi Chicholi is said to be the best example of conservation of the environment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82905,2493,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','If a dam is built at a particular place, what changes will be seen in the environment?\n','','','i) When a large dam is constructed, we get ample water.\nii) In some parts there will be growth of vegetation. But the natural environment which was there previously will be completely lost.\niii) The plant cover, trees, etc. which were there will be totally lost.\niv) Some animals would be completely displaced. Some will be extinct.\nv) Human being’s residents of those areas will be displaced and they will have to migrate to some other place.\nvi) The local environment will be changed forever.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82906,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the disadvantages of using a bicycle?','','','The disadvantages of using a bicycle are:\n(a) It cannot carry many people at a time.\n(b) Riding bicycle takes efforts. Hence, it is not possible for small kids and elderly people to ride a bicycle.\n(c) Also, if a rider rides a bicycle for a long time, he gets exhausted.\n(d) Bicycle ride does not offer comfort. It becomes difficult to ride bicycle in rain or on hot sunny afternoon.\n(e) Being a slow vehicle, it comparatively takes longer time to take the rider to the destination.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82907,2493,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of question below, explain what would happen if forests are cleared on a large scale. Will water and food sources for living things grow or dwindle? Why?','','','i) If deforestation is done on a largest scale, there will be a decrease in the water and food sources for resident living organisms.\nii) Food chains will be disturbed. Animals which are dependent on each other for food will die due to want of food. \niii) When trees are cut down the sources of water will also deplete. The roots of trees hold the soil particles and water.\niv) When forest are cut down there will be soil erosion. There will be decrease in the rainfall resulting in loses of sources of water.\nv) Ground water level will also decrease.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82908,2493,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','With the help of question below, explain what would happen if forests are cleared on a large scale. Will the living things look foe shelters elsewhere or stay on? Why?','','','i) Animals staying in forests with migrate elsewhere in search of food and water.\nii) Because their Shelters are lost, they might also encroach in the surrounding areas in search of shelter.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82909,2478,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The ------ extends under the water also.\na) Lithosphere b) Atmosphere c) Hydrosphere d) Biosphere','','','c) Hydrosphere\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82910,2478,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','----- are the smallest and ----- are the biggest fresh water sources.\na) Streams, Lakes b) Rivers, Lakes c) Rills, Rivers d) Ponds, Rivers','','','c) Rills, Rivers\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82911,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What can be done at an individual level to reduce effects of pollution caused by vehicles?','','','(a) Everyone should use public transport as much as possible.\n(b) Carpooling or sharing of cabs, auto-rickshaws should be followed.\n(c) Owned vehicles should be repaired and maintained properly.\n(d) Cleaner fuels like CNG, LPG should be used in private vehicles.\n(e) Traffic rules should be followed strictly and unnecessary honking must be avoided.\n(f) Trees should be planted in one\'s locality and looked after.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82912,2478,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Huge blocks of ice floating in the sea are called -----\na) Icebergs b) Glaciers c) Icecaps d) Snowfields\n','','','a) Icebergs\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82913,2478,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Almost all the water vapour in the atmosphere is contained in the -----\na) Stratosphere b) Troposphere c) Mesosphere d) Thermosphere\n','','','b) Troposphere\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82914,2478,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The layer of atmosphere which is next to troposphere is called -----\na) Mesosphere b) Thermosphere c) Stratosphere d) Ionosphere\n','','','c) Stratosphere\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82915,2478,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Many lakes and wells get water from -----\na) Springs b) Rivers c) Rainfall d) Oceans\n','','','a) Springs\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82916,2478,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Kangaroos are found only in ------\na) Africa b) Australia c) America d) Antarctica','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(82917,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' We should go to nearby places ____\n(A) by walking  (B) by car  (C) by rickshaw','','','A) by walking','',0,0,0,NULL),(82918,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Traffic jam leads to ____ pollution.\n(A) water  (B) air  (C) soil','','','B) air','',0,0,0,NULL),(82919,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The sun is not the cause of many natural processes on the earth.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(82920,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : We can see rocks split by the tree roots.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(82921,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','We can save money by using a ____\n(A) car  (B) rickshaw  (C) bicycle','','','C) bicycle','',0,0,0,NULL),(82922,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Rills, brooks and streams join each other to form oceans.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82923,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The huge mass of ice slipping down the slope forms a glacier.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(82924,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','___ is a fastest means of transport.\n(A) Aeroplane  (B) Ship  (C) Car','','','A) Aeroplane','',0,0,0,NULL),(82925,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : One can see elephants and lions only in polar region.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(82926,2489,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ is a slow means of transport.\n(A) Aeroplane  (B) Car  (C) Ship','','','C) Ship','',0,0,0,NULL),(82927,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : A water body formed by water collecting naturally is called a lake.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(82928,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Anil lives at the foot of a hill.\n','','','hill','',0,0,0,NULL),(82929,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Ria lives in a plateau region.\n','','','plateau ','',0,0,0,NULL),(82930,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Means of transport ','','','Bicycle, scooter, rickshaw, bullock-cart, horse-cart, car, tempo, truck, bus, boat, ship, train, goods-train, aeroplane, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82931,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : We can see many valleys near Lonavala.\n','','','valleys ','',0,0,0,NULL),(82932,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Mount Everest is situated in the Himalaya mountain ranges.\n','','','mountain \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82933,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Ratan can easily climb the hill of Saptashrungi.\n','','','hill\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82934,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of : Weather related events\n','','','i) Formation of clouds                 ii) Rainfall            iii) Fog\niv) Winds                                      v) Storms\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82935,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Poisonous gases emitted from vehicles','','','Carbon monoxide, nitrogen dioxide, sulphur dioxide.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82936,2478,'mcq','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of : Sources of water.\n','','','i) Rills and brooks                 ii) Streams            iii) Wells and rivers\niv) Lakes and ponds              v) Glaciers ','',0,0,0,NULL),(82937,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Disorders created by pollution harming human beings ','','','Trachea, lung diseases, eye disorders, restlessness, irritability, headaches, psychological disorders, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82938,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The sun is not the cause of many natural processes on the earth.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82939,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : We can see rocks split by the tree roots.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(82940,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Rills, brooks and streams join each other to form oceans.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82941,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The huge mass of ice slipping down the slope forms a glacier.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82942,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Groups :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Bus</td>\n    <td>(b) Public vehicle</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Car</td>\n    <td>(a) Private vehicle</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Poisonous Gas</td>\n    <td>(d) Sulphur dioxide</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1) Bus - Public vehicle\n2) Car - Private vehicle\n3) Poisonous gas - Sulphur dioxide','',0,0,0,NULL),(82943,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : One can see elephants and lions only in polar region.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(82944,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : A water body formed by water collecting naturally is called a lake.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82945,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Anil lives at the foot of a hill.\n','','','hill','',0,0,0,NULL),(82946,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Ria lives in a plateau region.\n','','','plateau \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82947,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : We can see many valleys near Lonavala.\n','','','valleys ','',0,0,0,NULL),(82948,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Mount Everest is situated in the Himalaya mountain ranges.\n','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(82949,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the words that refer to landforms : Ratan can easily climb the hill of Saptashrungi.\n','','','hill','',0,0,0,NULL),(82950,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of : Weather related events\n','','','i) Formation of clouds                 ii) Rainfall            iii) Fog\niv) Winds                                      v) Storms\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82951,2478,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of : Sources of water.\n','','','i) Rills and brooks                 ii) Streams            iii) Wells and rivers\niv) Lakes and ponds              v) Glaciers \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82952,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is transport?','','','Movement of people and goods from one place to another is called \'transport\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82953,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Five oceans\n','','','Atlantic, Pacific, Arctic, Indian Ocean, Southern Ocean.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82954,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the means of transport?','','','The means which are used for the movement of people and goods from one place to another are called the \'means of transport\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82955,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do we need to use in automobiles?','','','We need to use fuels in automobiles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82956,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Seven continents\n','','','Africa, South America, North America, Antarctica, Asia, Australia, Europe.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82957,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is saved by using fast means of transport?','','','We save on our time and efforts by using the fast means of transport.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82958,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Coastal water bodies\n','','','Sea, bay, gulf, strait, creek\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82959,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which facilities have become speedier due to transport?','','','Facilities like tourism, health, education, etc. have become speedier due to transport.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82960,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Animal seen in the snowbound polar regions.\n','','','Polar bear \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82961,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is air pollution?','','','An excess of poisonous gases and lead in the atmosphere that lowers the quality of air is called \'air pollution\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82962,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write the names of the envelopes of the earth.\n','','','Lithosphere, hydrosphere, atmosphere and biosphere are the envelopes of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82963,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is a continents? How many continents are there on earth?\n','','','A vast continuous stretch of land on the earth is called a continent and there are seven continents on the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82964,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Whose lives are endangered due to pollution?','','','The lives of human beings, animals and plants are endangered due to pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82965,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are landforms?\n','','','The unevenness of the land that gives different shapes to the land in the different places are called landforms.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82966,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','How is waterfall formed?\n','','','When in some places, a river cascades down a sudden drop, it forms a water- fall.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82967,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What type of pollution does a traffic jam lead to?','','','A traffic jam leads to air pollution and noise pollution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82968,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is a glacier?\n','','','The huge mass of ice that slips gradually over the slope at a very slow speed is called a glacier.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82969,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What makes the hydrosphere of the earth?\n','','','The water or ice that occupies the earth\'s surface, ground water and the water vapour present in the atmosphere together form the hydrosphere of the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82970,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the main constituents of the atmosphere?\n','','','Nitrogen, oxygen, carbon dioxide and other gases present in small quantity. water vapour and dust particles are the main constituents of the atmosphere.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82971,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which trees are to be planted to reduce pollution?','','','Trees like banyan, peepul, neem, karanj, etc. are to be planted to reduce pollution. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(82972,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','How does ozone gas protect living beings?\n','','','The ozone gas present in the stratosphere absorbs the harmful ultraviolet ray present in the sunlight, due to which living beings are protected from those radiations.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82973,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the source of water in the well?\n','','','Water is stored in the underground layers of rock. Bore wells and dug wells are used to extract this water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82974,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the full form of CNG.','','','The full form of CNG is Compressed Natural Gas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82975,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Where do you lay the foundation of buildings?\n','','','The foundation of buildings is laid on land.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82976,2489,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the full form of LPG.','','','The full form of LPG is Liquefied Petroleum Gas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(82977,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What need do we meet through breathing? How is this need met?\n','','','The need of oxygen to survive is met through breathing. For this purpose we breathe in air through our nose.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82978,2478,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','From where does the earth get light and heat?\n','','','The earth gets light and heat from the sun.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82979,2478,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are clouds made up of?\n','','','i) Clouds are made up of fine droplets of water. \nii) The sun\'s heat evaporates the water to form vapours from the earth\'s surface. \niii) This water vapour rises high up in the atmosphere. At cool heights, it condenses forming fine droplets of water which in turn make the clouds.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82980,2478,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by ‘biosphere’?\n','','','i) Living things are seen in all the envelopes of the earth such as lithosphere, hydrosphere and atmosphere. \nii) All the living things together in these three envelopes form a biosphere. \niii) Thus, the plants, animals and microorganisms together make a biosphere.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82981,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill-effects of transport on plants?','','','The ill-effects of transport on plants are as follows : \n1) Due to smoke and some poisonous gases emitted from vehicles, the leaves of the plants shrivel up and fall. \n2) Sprouts get scorched. The growth and development of plants gets adversely affected. \n3) Constant traffic through forest areas can harm the habitat of plants.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82982,2478,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Where do all the weather-related phenomena take place?\n','','','i) All the weather-related phenomena take place in the troposphere. \nii) Almost all the water vapour found in the atmosphere is in the troposphere. \niii) Therefore, formation of clouds, rains, storms and winds and all other weather-related phenomena take place here.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82983,2478,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why have special arrangements to be made to ensure that passengers in airplane get enough air for breathing?\n','','','i) Aeroplanes fly at the higher levels of the troposphere. \nii) Here the air is rare. There can be less oxygen present which is necessary for breathing. \niii) Therefore, passengers who are in the aeroplanes need some arrangements to ensure that they get enough air for breathing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82984,2489,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill-effects of transport on animals?','','','The ill-effects of transport on animals are as follows : \n1) Animals may suffer from disorders of lung and eye due to pollution created by transport. \n2) Constant traffic through forest areas can harm the habitat of animals. \n3) Due to traffic, the wild animals in the forests begin to migrate elsewhere.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82985,2478,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','In which layer of the atmosphere do we see the rainbow?\n','','','We see rainbow in the layer of troposphere of the atmosphere.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82986,2489,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill-effects of transport on human beings?','','','The ill-effects of transport on human beings are as follows : \n1) Human beings suffer from trachea, lung and eye disorders due to pollution created by transport. They may also suffer from headache and psychological disorders. \n2) An irritable noise created by vehicles makes human beings restless. It increases irritability and decreases the ability to concentrate. \n3) Traffic accidents cause injuries, deaths, damage to vehicles.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82987,2478,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Mountaineers carry oxygen in cylinders when they climb mountains that are more than 5000 m high. What could be the reason for that?\n','','','i) As we climb up the heights, the air at the top becomes rare. \nii) At the height of 5000 m or more the air is very rare. \niii) The oxygen may be limited here. Therefore the mountaineers carry oxygen in cylinders when they climb mountains that are more than 5000 m high.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82988,2478,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why are microorganisms important?\n','','','i) In every envelope of the earth there are microorganisms. \nii) These microorganisms perform important function of degradation. They also occupy important place in the food chain. \niii) The dead and decaying plant and animal matter is converted into soil and minerals by these microorganisms. \niv) If there were no microorganisms, huge piles of debris could have piled up everywhere on the earth. \nv) Therefore, microorganisms are very important organisms on the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82989,2489,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it necessary to prevent pollution?','','','1) Our environment is sensitive. \n2) Pollution created by transport has destructive effects on environment. \n3) These destructive effects are harmful for animals, plants and human beings. Their lives are endangered. Therefore, to save the lives of all living things and to save our environment, it is necessary to prevent pollution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82990,2478,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Make a list of landforms you see in your surroundings and give description of any two of them.\n','','','In my surroundings there is small hillock and a playground. I can see mountain ranges at a far distance. Between these mountain ranges there are valleys and passes. Therefore the list of landforms: Hillock, plain, mountain, valleys, passes.\n1) Hillock : Hillock is a small sized mountain. The land on the hillock is sloping. It is easy to climb a hillock. We can plant trees on the slope of a hill to stop soil erosion.\n2) Plain : Playground is a plain. Plain is a flat part of the land. There is no slope or elevations on the plain. Since the entire surface is flat, we can use it for playing and other activities. We can plant trees around the plain. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82991,2478,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Where do you get water from?\n','','','Water is obtained from different sources at different places. In cities we get it from taps and pipes. In villages we get it from wells or rivers, lakes, ponds, rains, dams, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82992,2478,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Think about all the food stuffs obtained from the sea. Find more information and write ten lines about them.\n','','','i) Many marine organisms inhabit seas. Some of these form our food. \nii) Various edible varieties of fish are caught from the seas. \niii) There are good quality proteins and fatty acids in the fish. Therefore, it is a nutritive food. \niv) Shell fish varieties such as crabs, prawns, lobsters are favourite food items worldwide. Prawns are abundantly available in the Indian waters, therefore we get lots of foreign exchange through export of prawns. \nv) Bivalves, squids, oysters are also eaten as delicacies. \nvi) Algae from the seas are also eaten in the Asian countries, though not in India. \nvii) Agar is the substance made from marine algae which is used in jellies and puddings. \nviii) Salt is made from the sea water. \nix) In some countries potable water is made from the sea water by special processes. \nx) Young ones of prawns and fishes are used for the aquaculture.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82993,2478,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Evaporation \n','','','i) The heat changes water into vapour. This action is called evaporation. \nii) The sun\'s heat converts the water present on the earth\'s surface into water vapour. \niii) The water that has percolated into the soil also evaporates due to heat. All such vapour enters the atmosphere. \niv) This vapour condenses and comes in the form of rain back to the surface of the earth. \nv) Thus, evaporation is necessary for rainfall. The water cycle keeps on function due to evaporation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82994,2478,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Condensation\n','','','i) Condensation is the process in which vapour is changed into water. \nii) In nature, due to condensation, there is rainfall. \niii) The water vapour that reaches the upper layers of atmosphere, gets condensed and forms fine droplets of water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82995,2489,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the advantages of using a bicycle?','','','The advantages of using a bicycle are as follows : \n1) Bicycle can be kept in a small space. \n2) Small loads can easily be carried on a bicycle. \n3) We can save time if we ride on a bicycle instead of going walking. \n4) We can save money, as bicycle does not require any fuel. \n5) Bicycle produces no air pollution, as it does not require fuel. \n6) Use of bicycle results in less crowding of vehicles on the streets. \n7) Riding on a bicycle helps to improve physical health. \n8) Bicycle makes the person self-reliant and helps in getting the work done sooner.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82996,2478,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write short note on : Water cycle\n','','','i) The movement of water is continuously taking place from earth\'s surface to earth\'s atmosphere. \nii) The process of evaporation, condensation and rainfall is a continuous process. \niii) Due to the sun\'s heat, the water on the earth\'s surface is continuously evaporated. Since the water vapour is lighter than air, it rises high in the atmosphere. \niv) Due to cool temperatures at the heights, the water vapour condenses forming fine droplets of water. \nv) These droplets later form clouds. When small droplets of water become heavier and bigger, they descend down on the earth in the form of rain. Rainwater mixes with different water bodies. \nvi) The snowbound regions also show melting of ice and glaciers. This water also eventually mixes with ocean waters. \nvii) In this way water moves in the cyclic manner, which is known as water cycle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(82997,2478,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a labelled diagram showing water cycle.\n','','','\nDiagram : \n','',0,0,1,NULL),(82998,2478,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the solution? Dark patches appear on the skin after exposure to the sun.\n','','','i) The harmful radiation of the sun can cause burning sensation to our skin. In order to avoid this we should not roam in the harsh sunlight. \nii) If it is necessary to leave the house, we should use an umbrella or cap. Wear cotton clothes that would cover the skin of hands and legs. \niii) Use of sun-screen cream is advisable. Covering the face with handkerchief can protect the delicate skin. \niv) After returning from outside. splash cold water on the face. \nv) This will help to reduce the temperature of the skin.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(82999,2478,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the solution? Dark patches appear on the skin after exposure to the sun.\n','','','i) The harmful radiation of the sun can cause burning sensation to our skin. In order to avoid this we should not roam in the harsh sunlight. \nii) If it is necessary to leave the house, we should use an umbrella or cap. Wear cotton clothes that would cover the skin of hands and legs. \niii) Use of sun-screen cream is advisable. Covering the face with handkerchief can protect the delicate skin. \niv) After returning from outside. splash cold water on the face. \nv) This will help to reduce the temperature of the skin.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(83000,2478,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the solution? Glaciers are melting on the entire earth.\n','','','i) Due to the increasing proportion of carbon dioxide, there is a danger of cli mate change. \nii) The entire earth is getting heated up. This phenomena is called global warming. \niii) Due to increased temperature, the glaciers are melting. This results in the discharge of water which flows down to seas and oceans. \niv) This is resulting in increase of sea level. Therefore, all the low-lying areas and the coastal regions are under the threat of submergence. \nv) Therefore, all of us should plant more and more trees and try to reduce the dangers of global warming. \nvi) We should also consume less energy in order to save the earth.\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(83001,2490,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The exchange of information is called ____.\n(A) communication (B) transportation (C) computation (D) navigation\n','','','A) communication','',0,0,0,NULL),(83002,2490,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' ____ are used in modern methods of communication.\n(A) Artificial satellites (B) Railways (C) Roads (D) Vehicles\n','','','A) Artificial satellites','',0,0,0,NULL),(83003,2490,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' ____ help in the expansion of trade and industry.\n(A) Mass media (B) Agriculture (C) Fishing (D) Mining\n','','','A) Mass media','',0,0,0,NULL),(83004,2490,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Space launch technology is used for ____.\n(A) telecommunication (B) navigation (C) construction (D) farming\n','','','A) telecommunication','',0,0,0,NULL),(83005,2490,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Technology of seeing a person when we are talking to on the mobile phone is ____.\nA) audio communication                      B) visual communication \nC) audio-visual communication           D) written communication\n','','','C) audio-visual communication','',0,0,0,NULL),(83006,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence. : India has launched \'INSAT\' series of satellites for communication.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(83007,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence. : Telecommunication devices can be used continuously as one likes.','','','Wrong: Telecommunication devices should be used sensibly. They should not be used in excess.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83008,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence. : The facial expressions and gestures are a type of communication.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(83009,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd One out : Telephone, mobile phone, newspaper, telegraph.','','','Newspaper.\nReason: Newspaper is a mass-media source while the remaining are telecommunication devices.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83010,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd One out : Time saving, economic, improving people\'s way of life, isolation.','','','Isolation.\nReason: Isolation is a disadvantage of telecommunication devices while remaining are advantages.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83011,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Odd One out : Watching Olympics live on TV, sending an e-mail to cousin in Delhi, talking to copassenger in the bus, hearing weather forecast on radio.','','','Talking to co-passenger in the bus.\nReason: Talking to co-passenger is face-to-face communication, while remaining are achieved through broadcasting using artificial satellites.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83012,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the possible disorders caused by excessive use of telecommunication devices?','','','Excessive use of telecommunication devices may cause disorders of the eyes, ears and back, also leads to psychological disorders and isolation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83013,2490,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Write down the educational uses of mass media.','','','Educational uses of mass media:\n(i) Mass media provide easy and quick access to educational information.\n(ii) Information is obtained in various forms such as audio, video, images, articles, power-point presentations, documentaries etc.\n(iii) Various educational books are available in print as well as on internet in the electronic format.\n(iv) Educational programmes telecasted on television and radio about literature, science, commerce, agriculture, environment, current affairs etc. keep viewers well informed.\n(v) Activity based and interactive education is possible with the help of mass media.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83014,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','How were messages communicated before we began to use the telephone?','','','Messages were communicated by sending messengers, writing letters and dispatching  telegrams before we began to use the telephone','',0,0,0,NULL),(83015,2490,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What difference has the computer made to your life? (','','','Computer is a very useful device and serves various purposes for me.\n(i) I am able to complete my school projects using word and power point.\n(ii) I can download educational videos, clippings, images etc. about various subjects for learning.\n(iii) To refresh my mind I play various activity based games, maths puzzle games, chess etc. on computer.\n(iv) I am able to download books and magazines from internet and read them in my leisure time.\n(v) It offers me enjoyments such as listening to different songs, instrumental music and watching some of my favourite movies.\n(vi) I am able to keep in touch with my cousins, friends and relatives by sending emails from my computer.\nComputer has made a lot of tasks easy and has helped me gain knowledge.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83016,2490,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write advantages of telecommunication and mass media.','','','Advantages of telecommunication and mass media:\n(i) Telecommunication devices make contacting easier with people who are far away from us.\n(ii) They have been proven to be time-saving and economic while exchanging information.\n(iii) They help to increase awareness about pollution, environmental balance, gender equality, child labour, illiteracy, etc.\n(iv) Alert can be created about natural calamities such as storms, floods, cyclones etc.\n(v) They create awareness about health, current affairs, educational facilities etc.\n(vi) They help in implementing various government schemes.\n(vii) They help in spreading awareness about a healthy life-style.\n(viii) Mass media helps in the expansion of trade and industry.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83017,2490,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','List various disadvantages of telecommunication devices.','','','Disadvantages of telecommunication devices:\n(i) Excessive use of TV, mobile phones, computers, laptops causes disorders related to back, eyes and ears. It may lead to psychological disorders and isolation.\n(ii) Information from these devices can be misused to disturb the peace and order in society.\n(iii) Time spent with these gadgets leads to negligence  of outdoor sports and physical fitness. Thus, it affects physical health adversely.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83018,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reasons :  Watching TV for long hours has an adverse effect on health of the viewer.','','','i) Watching TV excessively may lead to disorders of eyes, ears and back.\n(ii) It may also cause psychological disorders and isolation.\n(iii) Time spent on watching TV leads to negligence towards outdoor activities and physical fitness.\nHence, watching TV for long hours has an adverse effect on health of the viewer.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83019,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reasons : Telecommunication devices should be used properly and sensibly.','','','i) Telecommunication devices make contacting easier with people who are far away from us. They have been proven to be time-saving and economic while exchanging information.\n(ii) They can be used for the benefit of people by raising awareness about various government schemes, social and environmental issues etc.\n(iii) But information so easily available due to telecommunication devices can be misused to disturb peace and order.\n(iv) Excessive use of these devices causes disorders of eyes, ears and back. It sometimes leads to psychological disorders and isolation.\n(v) Also, time spent using such devices results into negligence of outdoor activities and physical fitness, causing adverse effect on one\'s physical health.\nThus, telecommunication devices have their advantages and disadvantages. Hence, telecommunication devices should be used properly and sensibly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83020,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','We watch TV programmes on many different channels. Where do they come from?','','','TV programmes are telecasted using signals which are sent and received with the help of artificial satellites.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83021,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','We talk to others on our mobile phones. What makes communication on a mobile phone possible?','','','A mobile phone uses a cellular network consisting of many transmitters  and receivers  of radio waves, which makes communication on mobile phone possible.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83022,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Note down the different kinds of information one can obtain from a newspaper.','','','The different kinds of information one can obtain from a newspaper are: educational, social, cultural, historical, medical and information related to sports, current affairs, weather, finance, entertainment etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83023,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How are the artificial satellites launched into space?','','','The artificial satellites are launched into space by means of rockets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83024,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the satellites used for communication in India.','','','In India, INSAT (Indian National Satellites) are used for communication.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83025,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the primary use of obtained information?','','','Information obtained is useful for constructing knowledge.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83026,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can mass media improve people\'s way of life?','','','Mass media can improve people\'s way of life by creating awareness about food, health, clothes and education related matters.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83027,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following. : Any four means of communication.','','','Mobile phones, television, internet, radio, newspaper etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83028,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following. : Any four kinds of information one can obtain from the internet.','','','Educational, political, geographical, social, cultural etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83029,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the words in Group \'A\' with the words given in Group \'B\'.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(i) Telephones</td>\n    <td>(b) Telecommunication</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(ii) Magazines</td>\n    <td>(a) Mass media</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(iii) Artificial satellites</td>\n    <td>(d) Rocket launching</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i) - (b), (ii) - (a), (iii) - (d)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83030,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give two advantages of telecommunication and mass media.','','','Two Advantages of telecommunication and mass media:\n(a) They make contacting far away people possible.\n(b) They have proven to be time-saving and economic while exchanging the information.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83031,2490,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why should one not play games on mobile phones everyday for a long time?','','','(a) Excessive gaming on mobile phone may lead to disorders of eyes, ears and back.\n(b) Time spent on playing mobile games leads to negligence towards outdoor activities and physical fitness.\n(c) It may cause psychological disorders and isolation.\n(d) Thus, playing games on mobile phone everyday for long time has an adverse effect on the health of the user.\nHence, one should not play games on mobile phone everyday for a long time.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83032,2490,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a note on \'newspapers\'.','','','(a) Newspaper is a widely circulating, cheap and easily accessible mean of mass media. Newspapers are available in many languages including regional languages.\n(b) They deliver different kinds of information such as political, social, economical, agricultural etc.\n(c) They update reader about local, national and global happenings. They address local issues and grievances.\n(d) They also help in raising awareness about social, environmental issues and also contribute towards improving the lifestyle of people.\n(e) They help in spreading information about various government schemes and facilities.\n(f) They give common people a platform to freely express their views and opinions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83033,2490,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ uses artificial satellites to broadcast information.\n(A) Newspapers (B) Magazines (C) Radio (D) Television\n','','','C) Radio','',0,0,0,NULL),(83034,2490,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ games increase physical fitness.\n(A) Outdoor (B) Computer (C) Mobile (D) Board\n','','','A) Outdoor','',0,0,0,NULL),(83035,2490,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Overuse of computers leads to disorder of the ____.\n(A) Chest (B) Stomach (C) Eye (D) Ear\n','','','C) Eye','',0,0,0,NULL),(83036,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : Overuse of mobile phones leads to diseases of the ears.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(83037,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : By new technology, we can see each other while talking on the telephone.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(83038,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : Newspaper is the audio-visual means of communication.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(83039,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : We can expand trade with the help of means of communication.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(83040,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : By media, people can be forewarned about natural disasters.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(83041,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','------ in our food give us the ability to resist diseases.\na) Proteins b) Vitamins c) Fats d) Minerals','','','b) Vitamins\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83042,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Calcium makes our bones ------\na) Soft b) Strong c) Brittle d) Thin\n','','','b) Strong\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83043,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Foodstuffs that taste sweet contain various kinds of ------\na) Salts b) Sugars c) Acids d) Proteins\n','','','b) Sugars\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83044,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Sugar cane juice contains sugar called -------\na) Glucose b) Fructose c) Sucrose d) Lactose\n','','','c) Sucrose\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83045,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The condition is called ----- when there is a deficiency of iron in the body.\na) Anemia b) Diabetes c) Obesity d) Anemia\n','','','a) Anemia\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83046,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','If food does not contain enough fibre, it can lead to ------\na) Diarrhea b) Constipation c) Weight loss d) Fatigue\n','','','b) Constipation\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83047,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Substances like starch, sugar and fibre are together called ------\na) Proteins b) Fats c) Carbohydrates d) Vitamins\n','','','c) Carbohydrates\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83048,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','----- makes our bones strong.\na) Iron b) Calcium c) Vitamin D d) Zinc\n','','','b) Calcium\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83049,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Cereals like jowar, bajra, wheat, rice contain a lot of ------\na) Protein b) Starch c) Fat d) Fiber\n','','','b) Starch\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83050,2494,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','We get ----- and ----- from leafy vegetables.\na) Proteins and fats b) Vitamins and minerals c) Carbohydrates and proteins d) Sugars and acids\n','','','b) Vitamins and minerals\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83051,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : In sago and potato there is starch/protein.\n','','','Starch \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83052,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : When a piece of bread is slowly chewed, it tastes sour/sweet.\n','','','sweet','',0,0,0,NULL),(83053,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Fibre is also called roughage/stool.\n','','','roughage','',0,0,0,NULL),(83054,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : The white part/egg yolk of the egg is a fatty substance.\n','','','egg yolk\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83055,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word : Oil makes the paper opaque/translucent. \n','','','translucent \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83056,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Carbohydrates\n','','','Sugar, starch, fibres\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83057,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Vitamins\n','','','A, B, C, D\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83058,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Sugars \n','','','Sucrose, glucose\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83059,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two examples of each : Minerals \n','','','calcium, iron \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83060,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name any four cereals\n','','','maize, rice, wheat, barley, sorghum, millet, oat, rye and triticale etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83061,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which food items have sugars in them?\n','','','Jowar roti, bread, chickoo, butter, Jaggery etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83062,2494,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which food constituents keeps the body warm?\n','','','eggs, fish, ghee, butter etc. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83063,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two sources of each of the following food constituents : Minerals\n','','','Leafy green vegetables, fruits, sprouted pulses.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83064,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two sources of each of the following food constituents : Proteins\n','','','Paneer, eggs, fish\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83065,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give two sources of each of the following food constituents : Starch\n','','','Potato, sago, rice, bread\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83066,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following : \n','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – c), (4 – e), (5 – a)\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(83067,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are carbohydrates?\n','','','The energy providing food constituents which consists of starch, sugars and fibres are called carbohydrates.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83068,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by malnutrition?\n','','','If a person\'s diet lacks proper constituents of food for a long time, then this person does not get proper nourishment. Such condition is called malnutrition.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83069,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is balanced diet?\n','','','The diet that constitutes the proteins, fats, carbohydrates, vitamins and minerals in right quantities is called a balanced diet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83070,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of fatty substances.\n','','','Cream, butter, ghee, oil, nuts, meat, egg yolk, are the examples of fatty sub- stances. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83071,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by diet?\n','','','The food containing different constituents that we eat for our survival is called diet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83072,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','For what purpose do we need food?\n','','','We need food for growth, development of the body and also for proper functioning of the body. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83073,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What substances do we use to give our food a sweet taste?\n','','','For giving sweet taste to our food, we can use sugar, jaggery, honey, fruit juice, dates, ripe bananas, sugarcane juice, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83074,2494,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Of the food that we eat raw, which ones are sweet?\n','','','Ripe fruits, honey, milk, sugarcane juice, jaggery are the food items which are eaten raw and are sweet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83075,2494,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Of what use are the sugars that we get from digestion of starch?\n','','','The sugars formed from digestion of starch are burned slowly. While burning they release energy. The sugars thus act as fuels. This energy is used for different kinds of work. It also keeps the body warm.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83076,2494,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the sources of fibre in our diet.\n','','','Bran in the flour of grains, whole grains and pulses, fruits, vegetables and especially their skin, sprouted pulses with their skins, green leafy vegetables are all sources of fibres in our diet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83077,2494,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','How is fat stored in the body? When is it reused?\n','','','i) The excess of fats in the diet are stored in the body under the skin. \nii) When the fats are more in diet than required for the body they are stored at different places in the body.\niii) If food is not available for some time, then the body can get energy from the stored fats.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83078,2494,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Sugar cane is sweet to taste.\n','','','Sugar cane has sugar called sucrose. Therefore, sugar cane is sweet to taste.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83079,2494,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Boiled egg is one of the best nourishing foods.\n','','','i) In a boiled egg there are proteins and fats. It also has vitamins A, B and D. \nii) There are many minerals such as calcium, phosphorus and potassium in it. \niii) Therefore, an egg provides good nourishment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83080,2494,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Thread gets stuck between the teeth when we eat certain types of mangoes. Which kind of carbohydrates are they?\n','','','The threads that get stuck between the teeth while eating certain types of mangoes are fibres.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83081,2494,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why are children told to drink milk every day?\n','','','i) Milk has almost all the food constituents. \nii) It has carbohydrates, sugars, fats and proteins. It is rich in vitamins A, B and D. \niii) The calcium content of milk is also quite high. Milk is easy to digest. It gives energy. \niv) The growth and development of the body is faster when milk is taken in our every day diet. \nv) Therefore, children are told to drink milk every day.  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83082,2494,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different tastes that foodstuffs have? How do we experience these tastes?\n','','','Sweet, sour, salty and bitter are the main tastes. \'Hot\' is not a taste, it is just the burning sensation of the tongue. We experience these different tastes due to taste buds present on the tongue.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83083,2494,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : The flour of any grains should not be sifted.\n','','','i) The flour contains particles of bran. When the flour is sifted, this bran is left behind on the sieve. \nii) The bran particles are larger and hence they are removed from flour when the flour is sifted. \niii) This bran contains vitamins and minerals and it also gives roughage. This bran is fibre which helps in movement of the food in the alimentary canal. \niv) Fibres in the bran helps to form stools from the undigested food. Because of such benefits, bran should not be removed, thus, the flour should not be sifted.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83084,2494,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : An injury from the outside of the body does not at once cause damage to internal organs.\n','','','There is layer of fat on the internal organs. This layer protects the delicate in- ternal organs. Therefore, even if there is an injury from the outside the body, it does not at once cause damage to the internal organs.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83085,2494,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why do we feel hungrier in winter than we do in summer?\n','','','i) In winter, the weather is cool. We need more calories to keep our body warm. \nii) Therefore, we need more energy. To get more energy, there should be enough nutrients in the body. \niii) When we feel hungrier, we take in more food. More starch, sugars and fats can give us more energy to keep our body warm. \niv) In summer season, such need is not there. Thus we feel hungrier in winter than we do in summer.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83086,2494,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why do we use a padding of cloth under a mortar when we place it on the floor and pound something in it?\n','','','i) While pounding the substance in a mortar, there could be jerks to the floor. \nii) The tiles of the floor can break. \niii) Therefore, a padding of cloth has to be placed between the mortar and the floor to absorb these shocks or jerks. \niv) This offers protection to the floor.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83087,2494,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Do we get all the different constituents of food from a meal that consists of a green veg thalipeeth eaten with yoghurt?\n','','','i) In the flour of thalipeeth there is mixed flour of rice, daals and pulses. \nii) This flour gives carbohydrates and proteins. While roasting the thalipeeth, oil or butter is applied to the fry pan. \niii) Thus, even fats are present in it. If greens are added to the flour, there would be vitamins, minerals like iron and roughage added to the flour. \niv) Curds also provide proteins, carbohydrates, fats and vitamins. \nv) Hence meals consisting of green veg thalipeeth eaten with yoghurt is a complete meal with all the food constituents.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83088,2494,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which food constituents do we get from the ingredients used to make bhel?\n','','','i) The kurmura in the bhel is a carbohydrate. The grams and peanuts are rich in proteins. \nii) The shev is made of gram flour and hence it also contains proteins. Since it is fried, there is good content of fats too in it. \niii) Chutney made from dates have sugars and minerals like iron. Pudina used in bhel is rich in vitamins, minerals and high in fibre content. \niv) If lemon or raw mango is used, the vitamin C content is also high. \nv) However, if bhel is purchased from a roadside shop, it may have microorganisms and contaminants and it can cause illness.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83089,2494,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution? The body requires an adequate quantity of proteins.\n','','','i) Body requires lots of proteins for body building. There is constant wear and tear of the body. \nii) That is also taken care of by proteins. Therefore, large amount of proteins should be in diet.\niii) For vegetarian people, different daals, pulses, groundnuts, grams, paneer, tofu, soyabean have to be included in diet. \niv) For non-vegetarian people, fish, meat, eggs are excellent sources of proteins.\nv) Everyday diet should include sprouted pulses. The milk and milk products are also rich in protein, caesin.\nvi) Such diet would give ample proteins along with vitamins.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83090,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','When we inhale the air from outside it goes into -----\na) Bronchi b) Trachea c) Lungs d) Alveoli','','','b) Trachea\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83091,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The small air sacs in the lungs are called ----\na) Bronchioles b) Alveoli c) Capillaries d) Trachea\n','','','b) Alveoli\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83092,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','----- gas is transported to all parts of the body.\na) Carbon dioxide b) Nitrogen c) Oxygen d) Hydrogen\n','','','c) Oxygen\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83093,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Smoking can cause life-threatening disease like ------\na) Asthma b) Lung cancer c) Diabetes d) Tuberculosis\n','','','b) Lung cancer\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83094,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The lower end of the alimentary canal is -----\na) Mouth b) Anus c) Stomach d) Oesophagus\n','','','b) Anus\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83095,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','In the stomach food is ------\na) Churned b) Digested c) Absorbed d) Excreted\n','','','a) Churned\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83096,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The feces are collected in the ----- for some time.\na) Colon b) Rectum c) Small intestine d) Anus\n','','','b) Rectum\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83097,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Some substances act as ---- and yield ---- to carry out all the tasks.\na) Hormones and enzymes b) Fuels and energy c) Vitamins and minerals d) Carbohydrates and proteins\n','','','b) Fuels and energy\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83098,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Maintaining the ----- is the function of the brain.\na) Coordination b) Digestion c) Circulation d) Respiration\n','','','a) Coordination\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83099,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The stomach is like a ----\na) Tube b) Bag c) Balloon d) Pouch\n','','','b) Bag\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83100,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','--- is necessary for the breathing.\na) Nitrogen b) Oxygen c) Carbon dioxide d) Water vapor\n','','','b) Oxygen\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83101,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','An exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide gases takes place in the -----\na) Bronchi b) Alveoli c) Trachea d) Capillaries\n','','','\nb) Alveoli\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83102,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The bolus passes through the -----\na) Oesophagus b) Stomach c) Intestine d) Rectum\n','','','a) Oesophagus\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83103,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The ----- and the network of blood vessels together form the circulatory system.\na) Brain b) Heart c) Liver d) Kidney\n','','','b) Heart\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83104,2496,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The ---- system expels the waste substances that are formed in the body.\na) Respiratory b) Digestive c) Excretory d) Circulatory\n','','','c) Excretory\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83105,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : We get oxygen from the air.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83106,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Saliva helps in the digestion of food.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83107,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Drinking alcohol does not effects on the body.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83108,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : The blood is continuously forward through the blood vessels.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83109,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Choose appropriate word in the bracket : Respiration takes place because of its up and down movement. (circulation, Trachea, Diaphragm)\n','','','Diaphragm \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83110,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Choose appropriate word in the bracket : The process of keeping the blood flowing continuously throughout the body. (circulation, Trachea, Diaphragm)\n','','','circulation','',0,0,0,NULL),(83111,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Choose appropriate word in the bracket : Air that enters through the nose passes into this tube. (circulation, Trachea, Diaphragm)\n','','','Trachea \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83112,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Nose, Trachea, Stomach, diaphragm\n','','','Stomach','',0,0,0,NULL),(83113,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Mouth, Lungs, Stomach, Small intestine.\n','','','Lungs ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83114,2496,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by breathing?\n','','','The inhalation and exhalation together called breathing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83115,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following :\n','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(83116,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the parts of respiratory system.\n','','','Nose, trachea, lungs, bronchi and diaphragm are the parts of respiratory system.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83117,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Where does the process of digestion begins?\n','','','The process of digestion begins in the mouth. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83118,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which system co-ordinates all the functions of the body?\n','','','The nervous system co-ordinates all the functions of the body.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83119,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Respiration \n','','','The process of taking oxygen from air and give out carbon dioxide through the nose is together called respiration.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83120,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Define : Circulation of blood\n','','','The process of keeping the blood flowing through all parts of the body is called blood circulation. The blood vessels carry blood away from the heart.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83121,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is digestive juice?\n','','','Digestive juice is the secretion that brings about digestive processes after eating food.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83122,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is a gland? Which are the digestive glands?\n','','','An organ that secretes certain substances is called a gland. Salivary glands, liver and pancreas are the digestive glands.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83123,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by a system in a body?\n','','','A group of organs that work together to carry out a particular function of the body is called a system in a body.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83124,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by circulation of blood?\n','','','The process of keeping blood flowing through all the parts of the body is known as circulation of blood.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83125,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the function of the excretory system?\n','','','The excretory system expels the waste substances that are formed in the body.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83126,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the nervous system made up of?\n','','','Nervous system is made up of brain and the network of nerves that run through- out the body.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83127,2496,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the function of the skeletal system?\n','','','The skeletal system gives shape and support to the body and also gives protection to the vital organs of the body.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83128,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the systems that carry out the different functions of the body.\n','','','Respiratory system, digestive system, circulatory system, nervous system, skeletal system and excretory system are some of the systems that carry out different functions of the body.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83129,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','How do the actions of inhalation and exhalation take place?\n','','','There is diaphragm in between the thoracic cavity and abdominal cavity. When the diaphragm moves downward, we inhale automatically. When the diaphragm moves upwards, air in the lungs is pushed out.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83130,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill-effects of smoking on health?\n','','','i) Smoking leads to different diseases of respiratory organs. Lung cancer can also be caused due to smoking. \nii) The solid particles of tobacco smoke from cigarette or bidi forms a sticky layer inside the alveoli. \niii) This reduces the amount of oxygen supplied to the body which causes ill-effects on health.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83131,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the functions of stomach?\n','','','\ni) Stomach is like a bag in which the food is churned. \nii) Some digestive actions take place in the stomach due to the digestive juices secreted here. \niii) Some disease producing germs are also destroyed here. \niv) Food is changed into slurry and then it is passed on to the small intestine.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83132,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which internal organs are vital and work ceaselessly?\n','','','The brain, the heart, the lungs, the digestive system, the diaphragm are some of the organs which are vital and they work ceaselessly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83133,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which organ maintains coordination of the body? How is it maintained?\n','','','The brain maintains the coordination between all the body parts. There is a network of nerves that connects the brain with all the different body parts. The brain helps in maintenance of the body functions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83134,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why do we call saliva a digestive juice?\n','','','Saliva is the juice secreted by the salivary glands. Saliva helps to make bolus from the food that we eat. Saliva also contains a starch digesting enzyme. This enzyme turns starch into sugar. Therefore, saliva is called a digestive juice.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83135,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the organs that about respiration.\n','','','Organs that carry out respiration are the nose, the pharynx (throat), the diaphragm, the trachea, the branches and sub-branches of the trachea, the lungs and the alveoli.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83136,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the organs of the digestive system.\n','','','i) The alimentary canal and associated digestive glands together form the digestive system. \nii) The alimentary canal consists of the oesophagus, the stomach, the small intestine and the large intestine. \niii) The digestive glands are salivary glands present in the mouth, the liver and the pancreas. \niv) The teeth and tongue in the mouth help in food intake.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83137,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which systems work together to provide the body with energy?\n','','','The lungs, the heart, the stomach, the intestines, the brain all of these internal organs work together to provide energy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83138,2496,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a well labelled diagram of the respiratory system. \n','','','Diagram : \n','',0,0,1,NULL),(83140,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Describe how the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide gases take place in the lungs.\n','','','i) At the time of respiration, the external air reaches up to the alveoli in the lungs.\nii) The oxygen in the air enters the blood vessels which surround the alveoli. \niii) Through the network of blood vessels oxygen is supplied to the entire body. At the same time, carbon dioxide is brought by the circulating blood into the alveoli. \niv) From here carbon dioxide is given out of the body through exhalation. In this way exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide gases take place in the alveoli of the lungs.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83141,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which digestive organs are included in the digestive system? Which digestive glands are associated with the alimentary canal?\n','','','i) Alimentary canal is a long and flexible tube present in the body. \nii) Alimentary canal begins with the mouth and ends in anus. In mouth, the teeth, saliva and tongue help in forming a bolus which is easier for swallowing. The anus helps in throwing faces out of the body. \niii) Alimentary canal is of varying diameter in different parts. Oesophagus, stomach, small intestine and large intestine are the main parts of the alimentary canal. All the above are called digestive organs. \niv) Salivary glands, liver and pancreas are the digestive glands associated with the alimentary canal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83142,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write the difference between small intestine and large intestine.\n','','','i) The small intestine is 7 metres long whereas the large intestine is one and half metre long. \nii) In the small intestine, the food is completely digested and absorbed in the blood. \niii) In the large intestine, the water from the digested food is reabsorbed. The remaining undigested matter is called faces and is stored for some time. \niv) In the small intestine, many digestive juices are mixed with the food. While in the large intestine, no secretions are mixed with the food. \nv) From the small intestine, the undigested food enters the large intestine. From the large intestine, the undigested faucal matter is thrown out of the body through the anus.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83143,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why is circulation of blood necessary?\n','','','i) Through blood circulation, the oxygen is sent to the entire body. Similarly, the absorbed food nutrients are also sent to all the body parts. \nii) Carbon dioxide produced in the body is carried by the blood to the lungs for expelling it out. \niii) For producing energy by burning nutrient fuels, circulation of blood is continuously needed. \niv) Thus. for survival of life, blood circulation is necessary.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83144,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why do we sometimes choke suddenly while eating?\n','','','i) The upper ends of both the oesophagus and the trachea open in the throat next to each other, there is a flap that covers the trachea. \nii) When the food is swallowed, the trachea remains closed. But if we eat in a hurry, the food enters the trachea. \niii) This causes choking. But due to coordination of the brain, we immediately start coughing and the food particles are expelled out of the trachea.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83145,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How is the air that we inhale purified in our body?\n','','','i) There are tiny, fine hair-like outgrowths called cilia inside the nose from where the air passes inside. \nii) Larger dust particles get caught in these hairs. The air going inside the trachea thus gets filtered. \niii) There is also a layer of a sticky substance called mucous. \niv) The dust and smoke particles stick to the mucous. \nv) Thus, the air that we inhale gets purified before it reaches the lungs.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83146,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How do we know that we are now hungry and that we should now eat?\n','','','i) When the level of energy giving substances present in the blood falls down, our brain can understand it. \nii) The brain tells us that \'we are hungry and we should eat something to restore this level. \niii) Thus, the coordinating brain gives us order to eat.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83147,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How do the salivary glands come to know that there is food in the mouth and it is time to secrete saliva?\n','','','i) Through the nerves present in the mouth, the brain comes to know that there is food in the mouth. \nii) The brain then gives orders to the salivary glands to secret the saliva. \niii) Thus, when there is food in the mouth, automatic secretion of saliva takes place.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83148,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What makes respiration and blood circulation go on all the time, and the processes of digestion happen at the right time?\n','','','All the systems and their functioning is under the control and coordination of the brain. The brain keeps all the processes working in normally functioning body.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83149,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution? A person fainted and a crowd of people has surrounded him.\n','','','When a person faints, he should be immediately given clean and fresh air. Therefore, ask the crowd to move away. Then splash cold water on the face of this man. If he is not reviving, take him immediately to the doctor with the help of others. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83150,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Means of communication ','','','Television, radio, newspaper, Internet, mobile, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83151,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Label the diagram of the digestive system.\n','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(83152,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Natural calamities ','','','Storms, tsunamis, floods, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83153,2496,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Draw a well labelled diagram for digestive system \n','','','Diagram : \n','',0,0,1,NULL),(83154,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Emotions expressed by face ','','','Joy, sad, anger, fear, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83155,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','something new is called learning a\na) lesson b) skill c) knowledge d) habit','','','b) skill\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83156,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Doing tasks more easily and without making mistakes is called\na) knowledge b) efficiency c) effort d) practice\n','','','b) efficiency\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83157,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','With age our\na) financial strength b) physical strength c) mental strength d) emotional strength\n','','','b) physical strength\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83158,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Young ones of animals learn their skills from their\na) father b) mother c) teacher d) friend\n','','','b) mother\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83159,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Our height grows till we are\na) 25 years old b) 18 years old c) 20 years old d) 30 years old\n','','','b) 18 years old\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83160,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The\na) old age b) growing age c) middle age d) childhood\nis the time to recognize one’s abilities and develop skills.\n','','','b) growing age\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83161,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Groups :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Television</td>\n    <td>(b) Audio-visual</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Cricket</td>\n    <td>(a) Indoor sport</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Fear</td>\n    <td>(c) Outdoor sport</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>(d) Emotion</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1) Television - Audio-visual communication\n2) Cricket - Outdoor sport\n3) Fear - Emotion','',0,0,0,NULL),(83162,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','',' Our\na) personality b) look c) height d) strength\ndevelops through our efforts and interests.\n','','','a) personality\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83163,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The ability to do something well is called -----\na) skill b) knowledge c) talent d) habit\n','','','a) skill\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83164,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is communication?','','','The exchange of information is called communication.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83165,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','During our growth height and ----- increases.\na) weight b) strength c) age d) height\n','','','a) weight\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83166,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','By which means are the artificial satellites launched into space?','','','The artificial satellites are launched into space by means of a rocket.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83167,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Lack of food constituents in the diet leads to ------\na) malnutrition b) fatigue c) dehydration d) illness\n','','','a) malnutrition\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83168,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which satellites are used in India for communication?','','','Indian National Satellites (INSAT) are used in India for communication.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83169,2497,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','We should avoid the ----- and their ill effects.\na) addictions b) exercises c) vitamins d) habits\n','','','a) addictions\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83170,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which types of disorders are caused due to the excessive use of mass media?','','','Disorders of the eyes, ears and back and psychological disorders like depression, irritability, monotony, are caused due to the excessive use of mass media.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83171,2497,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : A baby gradually learns to perform newly learnt tasks without making mistake.\n','','','True\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83172,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is saved by using the modern means of communication?','','','We save on our time and efforts by using the modern means of communication.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83173,2497,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : At birth, we already have all skills.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(83174,2497,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : We cannot manage all our tasks entirely by ourselves. \n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(83175,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Awareness of which basic needs can be created through mass media?','','','The awareness about the basic needs like food, clothing, shelter, education can be created through mass media.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83176,2497,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Our height increases from birth till we grow.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(83177,2497,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Ravi likes geometry he takes an extra effort to learn new things in geometry.\n','','','Good ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83178,2490,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For what purpose is information used?','','','Information is used for constructing knowledge.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83179,2497,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Ganu has started consuming tobacco.\n','','','Bad','',0,0,0,NULL),(83180,2497,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Ahmed helps his father in repairing the bicycle.\n','','','Good','',0,0,0,NULL),(83181,2497,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Manisha shouts at everybody.\n','','','Bad','',0,0,0,NULL),(83182,2497,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Mention whether the following actions are good or bad : Kailas has developed an expertise in drawing\n','','','Good','',0,0,0,NULL),(83183,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What changes does take place during the growth?\n','','','During the growth height and weight increases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83184,2490,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the advantages of mass media?','','','The advantages of mass media are as follows : \n1) By mass media, we can easily contact people far away from us. \n2) By using mass media, the time and efforts spent in exchanging information can be saved. \n3) Mass media helps to raise awareness about issues such as the environmental balance, gender equality and cleanliness. \n4) By using mass media, people can be forewarned about natural disasters like storms, tsunamis, flood, etc. \n5) Mass media creates awareness about health, educational facilities, good things happening in society, etc. \n6) They help in successful implementation of government schemes launched for the benefits of people. \n7) By mass media, the awareness can be raised about basic needs like food, clothes, shelter, education and health. This helps in improving people\'s way of life. \n8) Mass media helps in the expansion of trade and industry.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83185,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are skills?\n','','','The ability to do something well is called skills.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83186,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by learning of skills?\n','','','Achieving control of one\'s movements to learn to do somethings new is called learning a skills.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83187,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by a diet?\n','','','All that we eat during a day is together called a diet.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83188,2490,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the disadvantages of mass media?','','','The disadvantages of mass media are as follows : \n1) Excessive use of the television, computers or mobile phones causes disorders of eyes, ears and back. It also increases irritability, loneliness (isolation) and psychological disorders. \n2) We get all sorts of information through television channels and the Internet. Some people misuse this information and disturb the peace and order in the society. \n3) Time spent on watching television leads to the neglect of outdoor sports and physical fitness. This increases drowsiness and weight. This has an adverse effect on physical and psychological health.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83189,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','From where do we learn the skills?\n','','','Most of the skills we learn from parents, teachers and others.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83190,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','After what age is it called adulthood?\n','','','18 years old\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83191,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Do the clothes and shoes you wore as a baby fit you now?\n','','','No, as we grow we cannot fit into clothes or shoes that we used to wear when we were babies. These remain the same but we grow in size, height and weight.   \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83192,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Can an infant feed itself?\n','','','No, an infant cannot feed itself. It has to be fed by someone else.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83193,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Can a two-year old child fold clothes neatly?\n','','','No.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83194,2497,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','When do they learn to do these tasks?\n','','','They can learn to do these tasks when they are 7 to 8 years old.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83195,2490,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes : Newspapers','','','1) Newspapers is one of the cheapest means of communication. \n2) Newspapers are easily available in cities and villages. \n3) We can obtain education, political, geographical, social, cultural, economical, medical, historical, etc. information from a newspaper. \n4) Newspapers make readers aware about the social, educational, political issues, etc. \n5) Newspapers help in spreading information of government schemes and thus helps in their successful implementation. \n6) It helps to know about employment opportunities, too.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83196,2497,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by heredity?\n','','','Heredity means the characteristic traits and features of parents being passed to their children is called heredity. Due to heredity, family members also show similarities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83197,2490,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes : INSAT','','','1) In India, artificial satellites are used for communication. \n2) These artificial satellites are called the Indian National Satellites (INSAT). \n3) These artificial satellites are launched into space by means of a rocket.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83198,2497,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name any three skills you have acquired.\n','','','We have acquired the following skills.\nskills we have acquired such as skipping on a rope, combing our own hair, learning a song, swimming, cycling, giving a speech, playing a field game, climbing a tree etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83199,2497,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by physical growth?\n','','','Growth in height and weight of a child is called physical growth. The efficiency and strength of the body also rises due to physical growth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83200,2490,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes : Telecasting programmes on Television and Radio','','','1) In India, artificial satellites are used for telecasting programmes on television and radio. \n2) Through artificial satellites, the signals of information are delivered very quickly from one point to another. \n3) Through artificial satellites, we can watch the live telecast of any event going on in any part of the world in the form of programmes. For example, game of football or cricket match. \n4) The President or Prime Minister can address the entire nation at the same time over the radio or television. \n5) Telecasting programmes helps in building social awareness.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83201,2497,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are necessary things for good developing  good personality?\n','','','i) Nutritious food \nii) Adequate exercise \niii) To avoid addictions and their ill- effects \niv) Developing good hobbies \nv) To develop new skills. These are all the things which cause development of good personality.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83202,2497,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Describe good diet.\n','','','During growing years, one should always have a good diet so that all the abilities and characteristics develop to fullest extent. Inadequate nutrition during growing years cause malnutrition and stunted growth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83203,2497,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the factors of supporting growth?\n','','','Following are the necessary things for perfect and good development.\ni) Nutritious food                          ii) Proper exercise \niii) Habit of studying well            iv) Avoiding addictions \nv) Developing hobbies \nvi) Participation in sports and other new skills.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83204,2497,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','How many years does it take for the young ones of the tiger/elephant to start living independently?\n','','','Tiger\'s cubs are independent at the age of one and half years old. But they stay with their mother. But by the end of two and half years of age, they separate from the mother and start an independent life.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83205,2497,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What difference do we see between children in pre-primary classes and children in Std. V?\n','','','i) Children studying in the pre-primary classes are 3 to 4 years old. \nii) Their height and physical strength is not yet fully developed. They cannot go to school alone, on their own. \niii) Their understanding and maturity is also less. If they run, they may fall.\niv) Children from Standard V are 9 to 10 years old. They are a little grown-up. \nv) Their physical and mental capacity is increased. Their height has also increased. \nvi) They can commute alone to the school and back home. They play in the playground in the open.\nvii) They can make calculations and can handle money. They have many more skills as compared to the children in the pre-primary class.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83206,2497,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What changes takes place in us from birth till adulthood?\n','','','i) At birth our weight is 2 to 3 kg. By the time we reach adulthood, it becomes 50 to 60 kg for men and 45 to 50 kg for women. \nii) Our height also increases four to five times. \niii) There is no strength in arms and legs during infancy, but by young age arms and legs become strong and can perform many actions. \niv) The brain develops from birth till the age of 6. Later the structure of the brain becomes very complex. \nv) At the age of 12 to 14 many other major changes take place in the body. In order to become a father or a mother in the future, the body undergoes corresponding changes in the structure and function.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83207,2497,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How does our personality grow?\n','','','i) Every person is different and unique. The physical and mental makeup of every person is different than the others. \nii) This largely depends upon heredity. By constant interests and efforts, our personality develops. \niii) During the growing years, we also learn to understand about what is good and what is bad. \niv) When we put all the good thoughts into practice then our personality grows.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83208,2497,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What skill do we already have before we learn to ride a bicycle?\n','','','i) We should have enough strength in legs to paddle the bicycle. \nii) For balancing the cycle, the entire body should be agile, strong and fit. \niii) We should be able to turn the handle of the bicycle whenever we are riding it. We should be cautious of traffic moving in both the directions. \niv) We therefore turn the neck quickly to look around. \nv) Thus, complete control over legs, hands, neck and eyes is an appropriate skill required to ride a bicycle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83209,2497,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Suman wishes to run a restaurant of her own in the future. Which of the skills she is learning today will be of use in her future occupation?\n','','','i) Suman will need many people to run a restaurant. She should have the ability to interact and work together with the staff. \nii) She should also have information about different recipes, market rates of different foodstuffs, liking of customers, responsibility to keep the restaurant clean and hygienic. \niii) Right from now, she should acquire skills of buying groceries and other provisions, cooking different dishes of food, etc. \niv) Even now when she visits the hotels, she should keenly observe the working and organizing of the hotels.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83210,2497,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Kabir wants to be a professor of zoology. What can he do today to prepare for that? \n','','','i) In order to be a professor of zoology, Kabir should have interest in science subjects. \nii) Kabir should gather information from different magazines, books and pictorial encyclopaedia. \niii) He should develop the habit of observing different animals and birds. Zoology is taught in the science stream, so he needs to concentrate on studies in order to get admission in science stream. \niv) For becoming a professor, Kabir will have to acquire degree of M.Sc. and then Ph.D. For this, he should be able to study for long hours and with all the devotion. \nv) Since childhood, Kabir needs to be inclined towards studies in order to become a professor in the future.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83211,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Intestinal diseases spread through air.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','False \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83212,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Some diseases are caused by the anger of the gods.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83213,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : All micro-organisms cause the diseases.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83214,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Diarrhea spread through air. \n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','False \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83215,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Tuberculosis is an airborne disease.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83216,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Primary Health centers have been established at the village level.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83217,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Cholera spread through contaminated water.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83218,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Micro-organisms are present at everywhere.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83219,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False :  Some micro-organisms are useful.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83220,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : You should not cover your mouth while sneezing or coughing, it makes handkerchief dirty.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83221,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Always keep food items covered.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83222,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Lice and fleas do not spread diseases.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','False \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83223,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : The spittle of a person who has tuberculosis is collected in a vessel.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83224,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : During epidemic many people from a particular area get infected at the same time.\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83225,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Ringworm, typhoid, cholera, diarrhea\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Ringworm','',0,0,0,NULL),(83226,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Tuberculosis, diphtheria, jaundice, flu\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','jaundice','',0,0,0,NULL),(83227,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out :  Diarrhea, Malaria, Jaundice, cholera\n\nAns. : -------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Diarrhea','',0,0,0,NULL),(83228,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of infectious diseases\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Infectious diseases : Flu, cold, ringworm, scabies, chickenpox\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83229,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of diseases that spread through air\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Tuberculosis diphtheria, cold and cough, swine flu\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83230,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of diseases that spread a water\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Cholera, diarrheas, typhoid \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83231,2498,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give examples of vaccines\n\nAns. : ------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','polio ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83232,2498,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the medium through which spread the diseases. \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Air, water, food and insects are the medium through which diseases spread.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83233,2498,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','How does scabies and ringworm spread?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Scabies and ringworm are spread through direct contact.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83234,2498,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which programmes has been undertaken at the national level to prevent infectious diseases?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Community welfare programme has taken to prevent infectious diseases and epidemics.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83235,2498,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the functions of micro-organisms?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The micro-organisms converts the milk into yoghurt and the fermentation of the batter for idlies, dosas etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83236,2498,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','How does given the polio vaccine?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The polio vaccine is given orally.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83237,2498,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is an infectious disease?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The disease caused by specific microorganism is an infectious disease. It is transmitted from one person to another through infections.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83238,2498,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What happens when there is an epidemic of a diseases?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','When the epidemic of a disease is spread, many people from a particular area become ill with the same disease at the same time.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83239,2498,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which preventive measures are done against epidemic malaria?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Malaria is spread through mosquito bites. Hence to prevent epidemic of malaria, mosquitos are eradicated. For we ensure that there is no stagnant water. If it is there then this water is sprayed with insecticides.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83240,2498,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution? You are very hungry but the food has been left uncovered.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','If the food is left uncovered, it will be contaminated with dirt, dust and flies. Many microorganisms may be present in them. Such food can cause infections. Therefore, such food should not be eaten. If we are hungry we can eat packed biscuits or fruits like, banana which can be peeled and eaten. If food is boiling hot, we can consume otherwise, even if we are very hungry, we should not eat uncovered food.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83241,2498,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the diseases given below as diseases that spread through food, through water and through air : Malaria, typhoid, cholera, tuberculosis, jaundice, gastro, diarrhea, diphtheria.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','a) Through air – Tuberculosis, diphtheria\nb) Through food – Typhoid, cholera, jaundice, gastro, diarrhoea\nc) Through water – Typhoid, cholera, jaundice, diarrhoea\nd) Malaria spreads through mosquito bite\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83242,2498,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the mediums of the spread of diseases?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Contaminated air, water, food are mediums of the spread of disease. Lice, flea and mosquito also spread certain diseases. In case of skin diseases, direct contact through touch and contact with contaminated clothes or utensils can also cause disease.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83243,2498,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is a vaccination?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Vaccination is the process in which the vaccine is given to the person or child through injection or as an oral dose. \nii) This vaccine builds resistance against a certain disease. When the disease-causing germs enter our body, we already have the ability to fight against these germs. So we do not get that disease. \niii) Due to vaccination, the out- break of an epidemic can be controlled and disease prevention can be done. \niv) Children are given vaccinations for many diseases as their resistance power is weak. \nv) Community programmes for vaccination are also done to control epidemics.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83244,2498,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Make a list of the vaccinations that are given to a new born baby.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) BCG or anti tuberculosis vaccine. \nii) Triple vaccine: Given against whooping cough, diphtheria and tetanus. \niii) Polio oral vaccine is given to build resistance against polio.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83245,2498,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What care is taken in case of tuberculosis patient?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Patient suffering from tuberculosis is kept in the separate ward in the hospital. This is called quarantine. \nii) Clothes and utensils used by these patient are disinfected. \niii) Spittle of a person is collected in a vessel and covered with phenyl which is a germicide. \niv) Patients are given suitable and proper medicines and injections.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83246,2498,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the benefit of covering food?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) When food is kept covered, it remains safe.\nii) Dust and dirt does not contaminate it. The houseflies do not contaminate the food by sitting on it. \niii) The germs of different diseases are not mixed with the food. \niv) By covering the food, we can prevent spreading of diseases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83247,2498,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which method of preventing the breeding of mosquitoes. Will you recommend for your surrounds. Not allowing water to stagnate or spraying insecticides on the water? Why?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) In the stagnant water mosquitoes breed. \nii) Mosquitoes spread diseases like malaria, dengue and elephantiasis. \niii) Therefore, stagnant water should not be allowed to remain in our surroundings. \niv) One can also use insecticides to destroy mosquitoes. But this would also create insecticide pollution. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83248,2498,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How does the epidemic of jaundice spread?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Epidemic of jaundice spreads through contaminated water and food. \nii) The microorganisms causing jaundice are present in the faecal matter of the patient. \niii) If such faeces gets mixed with river or lake water, the water gets contaminated. \niv) If such water is used by other people for drinking, the germs cause infection to all of them. In this way if the water source gets contaminated, many people get jaundice at a time. \nv) Thus, the epidemic of jaundice is spread.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83249,2498,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : When there is an epidemic of cholera, we should drink boiled water.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Germs of cholera spread through faeces of a patient. They pass into water or food. \nii) If such contaminated water is used for drinking, others may get the infection of cholera. \niii) Therefore, during epidemic of cholera, water should be boiled. Boiling destroys the germs in the water and therefore we are protected against the germs. \niv) In order to keep our health safe, we should drink boiled water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83250,2498,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : We should not allow puddles of water to stand in our surroundings.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) Stagnant water of puddles is a breeding ground for mosquitoes. Germs causing malaria are transmitted through mosquitoes bite from a patient to other people. \nii) Epidemic of such diseases will spread if mosquitoes are abundant. \niii) Therefore, to control infections of malaria and dengue we should eradicate mosquitoes. \niv) Hence, we should not allow puddles of water to stand in our surroundings.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83251,2492,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','As man began to hunt, he started using ____ of animals as clothes.\n(A) Wool (B) Skins (C) Cotton (D) Silk\n','','','B) Skins','',0,0,0,NULL),(83252,2492,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ shawls from Aurangabad are famous.\n(A) Pashmina (B) Himroo (C) Banarasi (D) Paithani\n','','','B) Himroo','',0,0,0,NULL),(83253,2492,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In ____ textiles like Paithani and bedspreads are famous.\n(A) Rajasthan  (B) Jammu and Kashmir  (C) Maharashtra  (D) Tamilnadu\n','','','C) Maharashtra','',0,0,0,NULL),(83254,2492,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The reduction in ____ in humans led to the requirement of clothes.\n(A) body hair  (B) height  (C) weight  (D) brain size\n','','','A) body hair','',0,0,0,NULL),(83255,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word. : The textile industry is related to the manufacture of plastic-wares/clothes.','','','The textile industry is related to the manufacture of <u>clothes</u>.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83256,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Underline the correct word. : Body hair used to help man in regulating the body/environmental temperature.','','','Body hair used to help man in regulating the <u>body</u> temperature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83257,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence. : A variety of fabrics and costumes are used by people according to the climatic conditions of that region.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(83258,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence. : In prehistoric times, man wore cotton clothes.','','','Wrong: In prehistoric times, man did not wear clothes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83259,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The famous garment from Paithan and Yeola.','','','Paithani','',0,0,0,NULL),(83260,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The place in Maharashtra which is famous for bedsheets and bedspreads.','','','Solapur','',0,0,0,NULL),(83261,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by desire?','','','When we want things which we do not need, it is known as our desire.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83262,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What has led to the variety of fabrics and costumes in India?','','','The diverse climatic conditions in different parts of the country have led to the variety of fabrics and costumes in India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83263,2492,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a note of the clothes you would choose to wear on a \'traditional day\'.','','','On a traditional day, students wear clothes which suit their customs and traditions.\nThe different types of clothes which the students may wear are as follows.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Boys</th>\n    <th>Girls</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Dhoti, kurta, pheta, uparne</td>\n    <td>Navvari saree or Paithani saree,</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Gujarati angarkha, dhoti, pheta</td>\n    <td>Chaniyo-choli, Odhani or Patola saree</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Punjabi kurta, pyjama, turban</td>\n    <td>Salwar, kameez, odhani</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Angavastram, lungi</td>\n    <td>Mysore silk saree or Kanjeevaram saree or Valkalam saree or Chiffon saree</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83264,2492,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','The names of some states are given in the table below. Write the names of the fabric or garments they are famous for.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>State</th>\n    <th>Fabric/Garment</th>\n    <th>State</th>\n    <th>Fabric/Garment</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Maharashtra</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n    <td>Odisha</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Gujarat</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n    <td>West Bengal</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Punjab</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n    <td>Karnataka</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','The names of the fabric or garments which are famous in the respective states are given below.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>State</th>\n    <th>Fabrics</th>\n    <th>Garments</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Maharashtra</td>\n    <td>Himroo fabric</td>\n    <td>Dhoti, kurta, pheta, uparne, Navvari saree, Paithani saree, Kadiyal saree, etc.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Gujarat</td>\n    <td>Bandhani silk</td>\n    <td>Angarkha, dhoti, pheta, Chaniyo-choli, Odhani, Patola saree, etc.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Punjab</td>\n    <td>Phulkari fabric</td>\n    <td>Kurta, pyjama, turban, salwar, kameez, odhani</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Odisha</td>\n    <td>Sambalpuri fabric</td>\n    <td>Dhoti, kurta, gamucha, Sambalpuri saree, Bomkai or Sonepuri sarees</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>West Bengal</td>\n    <td>Baluchari silk</td>\n    <td>Punjabi form and dhoti, Korial saree, Dhakai Jamdani, Taant, Garad, Baluchari sarees</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Karnataka</td>\n    <td>Mysore silk</td>\n    <td>Angavastram, lungi, Mysore silk saree, Kanjeevaram saree, Valkalam saree, Chiffon saree</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(83265,2492,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','When did man begin to use clothes made from cotton?','','','When man learnt to make yarn from cotton-wool, he began to use clothes made from cotton.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83266,2492,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Variety of fabrics and costumes become available in all places with the help of better ____ facilities.\n(A) Communication (B) Transport (C) Trade (D) Storage\n','','','B) Transport','',0,0,0,NULL),(83267,2492,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','As man began to hunt, he started to use the skins of ____ killed by him as clothes.\n(A) Birds (B) Animals (C) Fish (D) Reptiles\n','','','B) Animals','',0,0,0,NULL),(83268,2492,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the famous textiles mentioned in Group \'A\' with the districts in Maharashtra mentioned in Group \'B\'.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(i) Bedsheets</td>\n    <td>(a) Yeola</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(ii) Himroo shawls</td>\n    <td>(b) Ichalkaranji</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(iii) Paithani</td>\n    <td>(c) Solapur</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(iv) Powerloom cloth</td>\n    <td>(d) Aurangabad</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i) - (c), (ii) - (d), (iii) - (a), (iv) - (b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83269,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do we get attracted towards new clothes?','','','We get attracted towards new clothes when we see the advertisements on TV and hoardings.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83270,2492,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reasons :  Mumbai became a big centre for textile industry.','','','a) Mumbai being an island  city has a humid weather.\n(b) This facilitates the manufacture of long thread for making cloth.\nDue to this, Mumbai became a big centre for textile industry.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83271,2492,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give reasons : Man felt the need of clothes for protecting him from weather conditions.','','','a) Man went through several changes during his evolution.\n(b) Reduction in the body hair was one of such changes. Body hair helped in regulating the body temperature. Thus it protected him from the external weather conditions.\n(c) Due to the reduction in body hair, man felt the need of clothes for protecting him from weather conditions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83272,2492,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ is the most important raw material in the textile industry.\n(A) Cotton  (B) Water  (C) Colour\n','','','A) Cotton','',0,0,0,NULL),(83273,2492,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The output of textile industry is ____.\n(A) sugar  (B) medicines  (C) cloth\n','','','C) cloth','',0,0,0,NULL),(83274,2492,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ is famous for the production of bedsheets.\n(A) Solapur  (B) Chandrapur  (C) Nagpur\n','','','A) Solapur','',0,0,0,NULL),(83275,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : In prehistoric times, man used to wear cotton and silk clothes. ','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(83276,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : We find diversity in clothes in India. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(83277,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : In the olden times, there were many textile industries at Mumbai. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(83278,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : Nature cannot quench the man\'s greed.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(83279,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State True or False : Poor people have plenty of clothes.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(83280,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following :  Famous Indian saree types ','','','Lucknowi Chikan, Kashmir silk, Banarasi silk, Kadiyal, Pitambari, Pochampalli, Narayanpeth, Kanjivaram, Patola, Mysore silk, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83281,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following :  Famous saree types in Maharashtra ','','','Paithani, Yeola saree.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83282,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following :   Famous textile centres in Maharashtra ','','','Paithan, Yeola, Aurangabad, Solapur, Ichalkaranji.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83283,2492,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Groups :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Solapur</td>\n    <td>(a) Saree</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Paithan</td>\n    <td>(b) Bedsheet</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Aurangabad</td>\n    <td>(c) Curtain</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>(d) Shawl</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1) Solapur - Bedsheet\n(2) Paithan - Saree\n(3) Aurangabad - Shawl\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83284,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is desire?','','','Wanting things when we do not need them is called \'desire\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83285,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is greed for clothes?','','','Wanting clothes when we do not really need them is called \'greed for clothes\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83286,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For which textile product is Paithan famous?','','','Paithan is famous for Pathant sarees.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83287,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which textile product is produced on a large scale at Yeola?','','','Sarees are produced on a large scale at Yeola.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83288,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For which textile product is Aurangabad famous?','','','Aurangabad is famous for \'Himroo shawls\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83289,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which types of looms are used to produce clothes at Ichalkaranji?','','','Handlooms and powerlooms are used to produce clothes at Ichalkaranji.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83290,2492,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the disadvantage of advertisements of clothes?','','','Developing desire / greed for clothes is the disadvantage of advertisements of clothes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83291,2492,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which factors are responsible for variety in clothes in India?','','','Diverse climatic conditions and availability of transport facilities are the factors responsible for variety in clothes in India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83292,2492,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','Write the names of those objects in the list below that you would like to own.\n1) Water bottle          2) Ball             3) Marbles              4) Laptop\n5) Flowerpot               6) Mobile phone                            7) Bicycle \n8) Scooter                     9) Photo frame                              10) Tiffin box.\n','','','The objects that I would like to own : (1) Water bottle (2) Ball (3) Bicycle (4) Tiffin box. I am going to use all these objects myself.\n(Write answer to this question as per the objects that you would like to own and use.)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83293,2492,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why should we minimize the greed for clothes?','','','1) The greed for clothes increases the stock of clothes unnecessarily. \n2) Many poor people from our country do not have sufficient number of clothes to wear. \n3) It is our duty to satisfy the need of poor people for clothes. Therefore, we should minimize the greed for clothes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83294,2492,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes : Diversity in clothes in India.','','','1) There are different climatic conditions in different parts of India. \n2) Due to diversity in climate, people from different regions wear different types of clothes in our nation.\n3) Similarly, better transport facilities have made variety of fabrics and costumes available in all places. \n4) Different types of sarees are seen in different parts of country.\nFor example, Lucknowi Chikan, Kashmir silk, Banarasi silk, Kadiyal, Pitambari, Pochampalli, Narayanpeth, Kanjivaram, Patola, Mysore silk, etc. \n5) Diversity in clothes is a part of the diversity in our country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83295,2492,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes : Textile industry in Maharashtra.','','','1) Textile industry has well developed since the olden times in Maharashtra. Initially, Mumbai was famous worldwide for its textile mills. \n2) Paithan, Yeola, Aurangabad, Solapur, Ichalkaranji, etc. are the famous textile industry centres in Maharashtra. \n3) Paithanis of Paithan, Yeola sarees of Yeola, Himroo shawls of Aurangabad, bedsheets and bedspreads of Solapur, handloom and powerloom cloth of Ichalkaranji are very famous.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83296,2492,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following details on a map of Maharashtra by using the symbols given in the index given below :\n1) A place famous for bedsheets.\n2) A place famous for Himroo shawls.\n3) A place famous for Paithani sarees.\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(83297,2492,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','When does our desire for more clothes change into greed for clothes?','','','When we see advertisements of clothes on TV, hoardings, etc. we get attracted to them and our desire turns into greed for clothes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83298,2492,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which textile industry in Ichalkaranji is famous?','','','The handloom and power loom industry in Ichalkaranji is famous.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83299,2492,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the following details on a map of India by using the symbols given in the index given below :\n(1) A state famous for Patola silk sarees.\n(2) A state famous for Narayanpeth sarees.\n(3) A state famous for Kanjeevaram sarees.\n(4) A state famous for Mysore silk sarees.\n','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(83300,2492,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rohan and Sania have many clothes, but they do not wear many of them. Now they are wondering what they should do with all these clothes. Help them to solve their problem.','','','Rohan and Sania can solve their problem as follows: \n1) Rohan and Sania do not wear many of the clothes they have. They can give these clothes to poor people from their locality. \n2) They can give some clothes to the poor children in the orphanages. \n3) Some of the clothes can be given to NGOs helping needy people. \n4) Some of the clothes can be used for dusting and cleaning the house.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83301,2492,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Make a note of clothes you would choose to wear on a traditional day.','','',' I will choose to wear the following clothes on a traditional day: 1) Dhoti          2) Kurta           3) Pheta 4) Uparne.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83302,2498,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Vaccination is done at the time of pilgrimage.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','i) During pilgrimage, a large number of people come together. \nii) Some of them may be infected or are carriers of certain diseases. \niii) Many a times there is uncovered and un- hygienic food at such events.\niv) People take holy bath together in contaminated water. All these facts can cause the spread of germs. \nv) In order to control spread of diseases and to protect pilgrims and local people, vaccination is given to all during such pilgrimage.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83303,2498,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : To spit in a public place is forbidden by law.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) By spitting, the surroundings become dirty. Many germs of diseases also spread through spittle.\nii) To prevent spread of such disease and to protect the health of people, spitting in a public place is forbidden by law.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83304,2498,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Mouth and nose is to be covered while coughing or sneezing.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) When we cough or sneeze millions of germs may find their way into the air. \nii) Different diseases can be spread through such acts. \niii) By covering the mouth and nose while coughing or sneezing, we protect the health of ours as well as that of others.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83305,2498,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Small babies are given vaccination.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n        ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) By vaccination, the resistance against different diseases can be built up. \nii) Babies are protected against hazardous and lethal diseases. \niii) Even if the germs of the disease enter the body, baby can defend itself. \niv) Therefore, small babies are given vaccination.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83306,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : We can see fine particles of the substances with our naked eyes.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83307,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Objects are made from substances.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83308,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sunlight, wind and water are exhaustible sources of energy.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83309,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Sound is a type of energy.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83310,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Say True or False : Different substances have a different property.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','True \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83311,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : petrol, diesel, CNG, kerosene\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','CNG ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83312,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : sunlight, wind, water, coal\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','coal ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83313,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Nitrogen, kerosene, oxygen, carbon dioxide\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','1','',0,0,0,NULL),(83314,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Hamida rides a bicycle to come to school.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Muscular energy\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83315,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Aunty boils the curry on the gas stove.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Heat energy\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83316,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : We watch TV in the evening.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Electricity ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83317,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Saplings of vegetables are in the sunlight.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Solar energy \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83318,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : It takes time for the food to get cooked in the solar cooker.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Solar energy \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83319,2499,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of energy is used in the following activities : Twings of the tree move due to wind.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Wind energy \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83320,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by peelav?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The minute particles of an object is called peelav.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83321,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the states of substances?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','solid, liquid and gases are the states of substances.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83322,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by kinetic energy?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Energy in the form of motion is called kinetic energy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83323,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the forms of energy?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Electricity, sound, heat and light are the forms of energy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83324,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are solar batteries?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Batteries in which the electricity is produced by using sunlight are called solar batteries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83325,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Name the non-exhaustible sources of energy.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Sunlight, wind and water are non- exhaustible sources of energy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83326,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','In which states is water found in nature?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Solid, liquid and gaseous are the three states in which water is found in nature. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83327,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by energy?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The capacity to do the work is called energy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83328,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What uses of heat energy do we see in our daily life?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','We use heat energy for cooking, keeping the place warm.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83329,2499,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the small particles that can be seen in a beam of light that enters a dark room?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','In the beam of light the small particles of dust are seen.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83330,2499,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following substances as per their states, into solids, liquid and gas : Chalk, water, rangoli, smoke, oxygen, nitrogen, petrol, milk, aluminium, wood, coal, kerosene, ice, water vapour.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','a) Solid – Chalk, Rangoli, Aluminium, Wood, Coal, Ice\nb) Liquid – Water, Petrol, Milk, Kerosene \nc) Gas – Smoke, Oxygen, Nitrogen, Water vapour  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83331,2499,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different properties in the substances?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Hardness, transparency, colour, smell, solubility in water, etc. are the different properties seen in the substances.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83332,2499,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which substances give energy in the form of heat? What are these substances known as?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Substances like coal, diesel, CNG, LPG, petrol, kerosene, etc. are the substances that give energy in the form of heat. These substances are known as fuels.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83333,2499,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write some of the activities that can be done due to wind energy.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The windmills turn, the boats sail, clouds move from one place to the other are the activities that take place due to kinetic energy of wind.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83334,2499,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write some of the activities that can be done due to wind energy.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The windmills turn, the boats sail, clouds move from one place to the other are the activities that take place due to kinetic energy of wind.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83335,2499,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution? We need to make a sherbet quickly for some guests. But we only have sugar candy in the house.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','We can pound sugar candy into fine powder. When the powder is fine, it will quickly dissolve in water. Therefore, we can make sherbet quickly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83336,2499,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What’s the solution? We need to rub salt on a corncob but only salt crystals are available. \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','We can pound salt crystals. Either we can use mortar pestle or we can also crush the crystals of salt in a grinder. Such powdered salt can be easily applied on the corncob.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83337,2499,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Naphthalene balls are also placed into toilets. Why do they become smaller after a few days?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Due to the property of naphthalene to turn into a gaseous state, the naphthalene balls kept in the toilet also become smaller and smaller after few days.   \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83338,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','When and why do clothes smell of naphthalene?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) The naphthalene balls are continuously converted into small particles in the gaseous state. \nii) These particles settle down on the clothes in which they are kept. \niii) Therefore, if naphthalene balls are kept for a long time in the clothes, they smell of naphthalene due to these particles.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83339,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the difference between solid, liquid and gaseous states of a substance?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) The arrangements of the particles in different states of a substance are different. \nii) In solid state the particles are closely packed. \niii) In liquid state, the particles are slightly away from each other \niv) While in gaseous state they are not in contact with each other and are freely placed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83340,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Make a list of all the everyday substances that we use in the form of a powder.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Chilli powder, turmeric powder, masalas, pepper powder, different types of flour, face powder, soap powder, rangoli, kumkum, ash, etc. are the substances used in powder form.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83341,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Naphthalene balls are placed in warm clothes. After some days, why do the clothes also smell of naphthalene?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The chemical naphthalene in the naphthalene balls continuously converts into small particles in the gaseous state. These particles settle down on the clothes in which they are kept. So after some days, the clothes start smelling of naphthalene.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83342,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why do tablets of camphor decrease in size day by day?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Tablets of Camphor are in solid state. \nii) Camphor has a property to turn into gaseous state directly from a solid state. \niii) Therefore, camphor decreases in size gradually. \niv) One can also notice the fragrance of camphor as it turns into vapour.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83343,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How do we save fuel by using public transport?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Each automobile vehicle needs fuel either in the form of petrol, diesel or CNG.\nii) If all the people use a separate vehicle, the amount of fuel consumed would be much more. \niii) If many people start using a public transport, the fuel consumption would be reduced. \niv) A single bus or a train can carry many people and thus it can save lots of fuel.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83344,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How do we save fuel by using public transport?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Each automobile vehicle needs fuel either in the form of petrol, diesel or CNG.\nii) If all the people use a separate vehicle, the amount of fuel consumed would be much more. \niii) If many people start using a public transport, the fuel consumption would be reduced. \niv) A single bus or a train can carry many people and thus it can save lots of fuel.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83345,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','How do we save fuel by using public transport?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Each automobile vehicle needs fuel either in the form of petrol, diesel or CNG.\nii) If all the people use a separate vehicle, the amount of fuel consumed would be much more. \niii) If many people start using a public transport, the fuel consumption would be reduced. \niv) A single bus or a train can carry many people and thus it can save lots of fuel.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83346,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','You do not have rangoli powder. What things can you use as an alternative?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','We can use flour, face powder, chalk powder or any other particulate powdered matter to replace rangoli. We can use coloured powders like turmeric, kumkum, etc. for filling colours in the rangoli.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83347,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','You do not have rangoli powder. What things can you use as an alternative?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','We can use flour, face powder, chalk powder or any other particulate powdered matter to replace rangoli. We can use coloured powders like turmeric, kumkum, etc. for filling colours in the rangoli.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83348,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','You do not have rangoli powder. What things can you use as an alternative?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','We can use flour, face powder, chalk powder or any other particulate powdered matter to replace rangoli. We can use coloured powders like turmeric, kumkum, etc. for filling colours in the rangoli.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83349,2499,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','You do not have rangoli powder. What things can you use as an alternative?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n          --------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','We can use flour, face powder, chalk powder or any other particulate powdered matter to replace rangoli. We can use coloured powders like turmeric, kumkum, etc. for filling colours in the rangoli.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83350,2500,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','How can you achieve excellent health?\n\nAns. : -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Nutritious food, personal cleanliness, exercise and pursuit of hobbies leads to achievement of excellent health.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83351,2500,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What factors are a threat to community health?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Pollution, squalor, epidemics of infectious diseases, addiction, insect-borne diseases, malnutrition are the factors that cause a threat to community health.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83352,2500,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why is spitting in a public place banned?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Spitting can spread different diseases and also makes surrounding dirty, there- fore, spitting in a public place is banned.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83353,2500,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which is the tobacco containing products?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Gutka, snuff, masheri, mawa, pan masala, cigarettes, bidis, chillum, hookah, cigar, pipe, etc. are tobacco containing products.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83354,2500,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is done for becoming addiction-free?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','The addict should take medical help of counsellor and doctor, he should develop hobbies and sports in order to become addiction-free.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83355,2500,'descriptive','2',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which addiction can totally devastate one’s life?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','Getting addicted to drugs like cocaine, brown sugar, etc. can devastate one\'s life. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83356,2500,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill-effects of alcoholism? \n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Alcohol acts on the brain of a person. Person becomes drowsy and con- fused. \nii) ) Alcohol makes a person lose control over one\'s actions. \niii) Alcohol causes diseases of liver, intestines and kidney and urinary bladder.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83357,2500,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','How do we nurture community health?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','To nurture community health following things are done: \ni) general public from infectious diseases. \nii) Observing personal and community hygiene.\niii) Controlling pollution, squalor, insect-borne diseases, addiction, etc.\niv) Making people happy and healthy in all respects.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83358,2500,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the benefit of wholesome life?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) By leading wholesome life, we become happy. We can take interest in many things. \nii) Feelings of hatred and envy are ended. People in the society lead healthy and happy life. \niii) Social tensions decrease and feelings of friendship are nurtured.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83359,2500,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','When is it said that a person is addicted to tobacco?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','When a person gets restless when he does not get tobacco and he is unable to pay attention to anything else, he is said to be addicted to tobacco.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83360,2500,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : When we take care of our health, entire community also becomes wholesome.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) When everybody takes care of their health and hygiene, the entire community health is also achieved. \nii) Through the habits of cleanliness the entire surroundings also become clean. In this way all can lead a wholesome life. \niii) The entire community then becomes wholesome.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83361,2500,'descriptive','3',3,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Pollution and epidemics of infectious diseases are threats to community health.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) When there is an outbreak of epidemic, many people from the same area are affected at the same time. \nii) Similarly, pollution also causes harm to the life of the people. \niii) Many people can die too. Therefore, pollution and epidemics of infectious diseases are threats to community health. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83362,2500,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What are the ill – effects of chewing tobacco?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) If tobacco is consumed, there are ulcers in the mouth. Ulcers develop into\nbigger wounds and they are later turn into tumours. \nii) Oral cancer, i.e. cancer of the mouth may be formed. \niii) Tobacco entering into digestive system cause cancer of any of the digestive organs. It also leads to various complaints of digestive system. \niv) A person with cancer has to undergo painful treatment. It can lead to death.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83363,2500,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is the effect of addiction on the family of an addict?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) When the condition of an addict becomes pathetic, his family members also suffer. \nii) Lot of money is required for treatment. Everyone is put into great trouble and lot of time and efforts are wasted. \niii) The family gets deprived of health and happiness.\niv) The family gets ruined due to insecurity of all kind. \nv) Addict destroys the entire family.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83364,2500,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is to be done for good health?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) For good health, nutritious diet, safe and clean drinking water should be used. \nii) Habits of personal and community cleanliness should be followed. Never get hooked on to any addiction. \niii) Always stay away from tobacco, alcohol and drugs.\niv) Feeling of hatred and envy should be avoided. \nv) Proper exercise should be done. Hobbies and sports should be taken up. \nvi) Along with personal health, community health should also be nurtured.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83365,2500,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','What is to be done to stay away from addictions?\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) We should have good habits, remain happy and enthusiastic. \nii) There should be complete control over mind. \niii) We should remain firm in avoiding addictions.\niv) We should not be lured by people who can force someone in addictions. \nv) Sports and hobbies also keep the addictions away. \nvi) We should be completely aware of dangers of addictions. All these measures can help us to stay away from addictions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83366,2500,'descriptive','4',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Give reason : Always stay away addictions.\n\nAns. : ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n         --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n','','','i) Addictions spoils health of a person. Tobacco addiction can cause cancers of different organs. \nii) By keeping tobacco constantly in the mouth, there are chances of oral cancer. \niii) Alcoholism cause severe problems of liver, kidney and intestines. The addict completely destroys the family. \niv) The family is deprived of health and happiness and is ruined. Due to tobacco spitting the surroundings get dirty. \nv) By drug addiction, life is totally devastated. \nvi) Therefore, always stay away from addictions. \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83367,2500,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','A healthy and wholesome life leads to a feeling of  \nA. sadness  B. loneliness  C. anxiety  D. friendship','','','D. friendship','',0,0,0,NULL),(83368,2500,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Constant presence of tobacco in the digestive organs can lead to a cancer of  \nA. lungs  B. skin  C. digestive organs  D. brain','','','C. digestive organs  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83369,2500,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','----------------------- leads to diseases of the liver, intestines and urinary bladder.  \nA. Smoking  B. Junk food  C. Alcoholism  D. Pollution','','','C. Alcoholism  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83370,2500,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The most important factor in the country’s progress and development are its  \nA. roads  B. leaders  C. industries  D. people','','','D. people','',0,0,0,NULL),(83371,2500,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Good community ------------------------- can be achieved through habits of hygiene and good health.  \nA. education  B. health and hygiene  C. entertainment  D. communication','','','B. health and hygiene','',0,0,0,NULL),(83378,2500,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','It is ------------------------ to spit in a public place.  \nA. acceptable  B. legal offence  C. traditional  D. hygienic','','','B. legal offence','',0,0,0,NULL),(83440,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The ---- of particles in each of the substances is different.\na) size b) weight c) color d) arrangement','','','d) arrangement\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83441,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','All substances around us are made up of tiny ------\na) cells b) molecules c) atoms d) particles','','','d) particles\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83442,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','In our body, energy is obtained by the ----- of certain substances.\na) freezing b) mixing c) burning d) melting','','',' c) burning\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83443,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Energy in the form of motion is called ----- energy.\na) chemical b) potential c) thermal d) kinetic\n','','',' d) kinetic\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83444,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Energy from ------ is stored in the food substances which the plants prepare.\na) water b) soil c) sun light d) air\n','','',' c) sun light\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83445,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','In today’s world the main source of energy are ----- and -------.\na) wind and solar b) fuels and electricity c) water and coal d) biomass and gas\n','','','b) fuels and electricity\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83446,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Many power stations use fuels to produce -------.\na) light b) motion c) heat d) electricity\n','','','d) electricity\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83447,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','When we burn fuels the stored energy in them is converted into ---- energy.\na) sound b) electrical c) light d) heat\n','','','d) heat\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83448,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','All substances are made up of -----\na) liquids b) gases c) large particles d) tiny particles\n','','','d) tiny particles\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83449,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','------ is required to do work.\na) speed b) force c) power d) energy\n','','','d) energy\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83450,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','All moving things have ----- energy.\na) nuclear b) chemical c) potential d) kinetic\n','','','d) kinetic\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83451,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Plants used ----- to prepare their own food.\na) water b) sunlight c) carbon dioxide d) soil\n','','','b) sunlight\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83452,2499,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Water is found in nature in ----- states.\na) one b) two c) four d) three\n','','',' d) three\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83453,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Diseases are caused by ------\na) viruses b) microorganisms c) toxins d) allergens','','',' b) microorganisms\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83454,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','The germs of influenza are present in the ------- or saliva of the patient.\na) blood b) spit c) sweat d) tears','','','b) spit\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83455,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Maintaining cleanliness everywhere is -------\na) harmful b) beneficial c) unnecessary d) difficult\n','','',' b) beneficial\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83456,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','------ is spread through the bite of mosquito.\na) Malaria b) Dengue c) Flu d) Tuberculosis\n','','','a) Malaria\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83457,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Clothes and utensils of a patient are washed with --------.\na) detergents b) soaps c) germicides d) disinfectants\n','','','c) germicides\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83458,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','----- is added to community sources of water to kill the germs.\na) Salt b) Bleaching powder c) Chlorine d) Detergent\n','','','b) Bleaching powder\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83459,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','---- are spread on stagnant water to prevent mosquito breeding.\na) Fertilizers b) Herbicides c) Pesticides d) Insecticides\n','','','d) Insecticides\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83460,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Our body develops resistance to a particular disease due to -------\na) rest b) vaccination c) exercise d) diet\n','','','b) vaccination\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83461,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Soon after a baby is born, she is given the ---- vaccine.\na) measles b) hepatitis c) tuberculosis d) polio\n','','',' c) tuberculosis\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83462,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Mass media is used for raising ------- about health and hygiene issues.\na) public awareness b) gossip c) fear d) confusion\n','','',' a) public awareness\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83463,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Diseases of the throat and the chest spread through ------\na) air b) water c) food d) soil\n','','','a) air\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83464,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','----- spread malaria\na) Mosquitoes b) Flies c) Rats d) Birds\n','','','a) Mosquitoes\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83465,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Diseases that spread from one person to another are called ------ diseases.\na) infectious b) hereditary c) chronic d) genetic\n','','',' a) infectious\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83466,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','------ is an excellent method of preventing disease.\na) Quarantine b) Vaccination c) Surgery d) Medication\n','','',' b) Vaccination\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83467,2498,'mcq','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','------ are very-very small in size.\na) Atoms b) Molecules c) Cells d) Micro-organisms\n','','','d) Micro-organisms\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83468,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When we learn to cope with our emotions, our personality becomes _____.\n(A) Weak (B) Balanced (C) Dull (D) Angry\n','','','B) Balanced','',0,0,0,NULL),(83469,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Emotions should be expressed in ____ manner.\n(A) angry  (B) serious  (C) reasonable  (D) jovial\n','','','C) reasonable','',0,0,0,NULL),(83470,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If we get angry very often we become ____ and stubborn.\n(A) rational  (B) short-tempered  (C) foolish  (D) focussed\n','','','B) short-tempered','',0,0,0,NULL),(83471,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Speaking about our flaws openly with teachers or friends can be ____.\n(A) advantageous  (B) time consuming  (C) boring  (D) insulting\n','','','A) advantageous','',0,0,0,NULL),(83472,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence : Emotions should not be expressed.','','','Wrong: Emotions must be expressed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83473,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence : Anger must be controlled otherwise it can have adverse effects on our mind and body.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(83474,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence : Aspects of our nature that bother us and others can be changed.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(83475,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','How does a balanced personality develop?','','','A balanced personality is developed by learning to cope with emotions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83476,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Why does our attitude of co-operation and understanding decrease?','','','When we become short-tempered and stubborn due to our anger, our attitude of cooperation and understanding decreases.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83477,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What must we do after we have found the flaws in our nature?','','','After we have found the flaws in our nature, we must try to talk openly with our friends, teachers and family members about them and correct them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83478,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do you mean by understanding of your own flaws?','','','Understanding of your own flaws means knowing what you are not able to do.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83479,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will happen if we do not try to change disagreeable aspects of our nature?','','','If we do not try to change the disagreeable aspects of our nature, they may take the shape of flaws in our personality.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83480,2495,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by \'coping with emotions\'?','','','Coping with emotions means,\n(i) We can have control over our emotions by thinking properly.\n(ii) Once one controls his/her emotions, these emotions should be expressed in reasonable manner.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83481,2495,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the harmful effects of anger?','','','The harmful effects of anger are:\n(i) One becomes short-tempered, stubborn and uncooperative.\n(ii) One\'s capacity to understand others decreases and one tends to hurt others.\n(iii) One\'s health is compromised and one may get headaches, experience loss of sleep and dullness.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83482,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Why must we know our flaws?','','','By knowing our flaws we can improve on them. These continuous efforts eventually lead to getting rid of the flaws.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83483,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Write how will you react : Your teacher does not listen to you.','','','If my teacher does not listen to me, I will feel disheartened. But I will try to express myself politely by approaching her one more time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83484,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write how will you react : Your parents ask for your opinion while making decisions.','','','I will feel proud that my parents trust me and consider my opinions. I will feel happy that I am treated as a valuable member of family.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83485,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write how will you react : Your friend got a big prize.','','','I will feel delighted. I will be thankful to them as they are encouraging me by their praises. I will get inspired to work harder.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83486,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','Write how will you react : Rohan insulted you in class.','','','I will feel sad but I will not lose my temper. I will speak to him peacefully later once he has calmed down and sort out the misunderstanding between us.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83487,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Write how will you react : You got less marks in mathematics.','','','I will feel sad but I will find out my mistakes and learn from them. I will practice more and will try to score better next time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83488,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','What will you do in this situation? \nRohini won a prize in an essay competition.','','','I will congratulate Rohini. I will feel inspired to succeed in the things that I like to do.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83489,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','What will you do in this situation? \nKavita refused to eat her tiffin because she was angry.','','','I will request Kavita to calm down. I will point out how being angry can be harmful to her. I will tell her not eating food will also not solve her problems. Rather she should relax, eat food and then think about her situation with fresh view points.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83490,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will you do in this situation? \nVeena keeps herself to herself at school.','','','I will try to befriend Veena and understand if she has any problem which is making her choose isolation. I will introduce her to my friends as well.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83491,2495,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What will you do in this situation? \nMakarand says, \"I can\'t help it if I have a stubborn personality\".','','','I will convey politely to Makarand that stubbornness is usually an outcome of being angry often or not being able to control one\'s anger. I will affirm that he can change this aspect of his personality if he wishes. I will encourage him to try and change his behaviour.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83492,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If wrong, write the correct sentence. : When someone hurts us, it is natural to feel sad.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(83493,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If wrong, write the correct sentence. : One can express his/her emotions instantly without thinking as one likes.','','','Wrong.\nOne should think before expressing emotions. Also, emotions should be expressed in a reasonable manner.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83494,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If wrong, write the correct sentence. : Some people are entirely bad.','','','Wrong.\nNo person is entirely good or entirely bad.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83495,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If wrong, write the correct sentence. : If Reena cannot do a particular thing, it does not mean that she cannot excel at what she is good at.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(83496,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the emotions given in Group \'A\' with the situations in which one feels them given in Group \' B \'.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(i) Anger</td>\n    <td>(a) When you win a competition.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(ii) Joy</td>\n    <td>(b) When you read a horror story.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(iii) Sad</td>\n    <td>(c) When someone shouts at you for no fault of yours.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(iv) Fear</td>\n    <td>(d) When India loses final cricket match in the World Cup.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i) - (c), (ii) - (a), (iii) - (d), (iv) - (b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83497,2495,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does emotional adjustment involve?','','','Emotional adjustment involves\n(a) coping with one\'s emotions,\n(b) controlling these emotions rightfully,\n(c) expressing these emotions sensibly or in a reasonable manner.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83498,2495,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the balanced personality benefit an individual?','','','When the personality is balanced:\n(a) an individual\'s capacity to understand others increases,\n(b) he/she gets rid of faults such as blaming others and becomes tolerant, flexible and empathetic,\n(c) he/she can overcome adverse conditions and becomes happy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83499,2495,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can people improve their behaviour?','','','a) People must first be aware of and realise the flaws in their behaviour.\n(b) They should keep a watchful eye on their that part of behaviour where improvement is needed.\n(c) They can take help of their friends, teachers and family members who would monitor their behaviour and tell them to stop if they show adverse behavioural pattern.\n(d) By practising these steps steadily, each person can get rid of particular flaw or specific undesirable  trait in his/her personality and can eventually improve.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83501,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Just as man is able to think, he is also _____.\n(A) Emotional (B) Strong (C) Tall (D) Physical\n','','','A) Emotional','',0,0,0,NULL),(83502,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','We must always think first about the ____ in our friends.\n(A) Mistakes (B) Virtues (C) Weaknesses (D) Flaws\n','','','B) Virtues','',0,0,0,NULL),(83503,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When we learn to cope with our ____ our personality becomes balanced.\n(A) Dreams (B) Emotions (C) Hobbies (D) Problems\n','','','B) Emotions','',0,0,0,NULL),(83504,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','We must know our ____ as well as our talents.\n(A) Flaws (B) Virtues (C) Hobbies (D) Games\n','','','A) Flaws','',0,0,0,NULL),(83505,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If we ____ properly, we can control our emotions.\n(A) Run (B) Work (C) Think (D) Sleep\n','','','C) Think','',0,0,0,NULL),(83506,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If we cannot control our .........., we become short-tempered and stubborn.\n(A) anger  (B) hunger  (C) greed  (D) none of these\n','','','A) anger','',0,0,0,NULL),(83507,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','None of us is completely good or bad by ____.\n(A) nature  (B) mind  (C) understanding  (D) behaviour\n','','','A) nature','',0,0,0,NULL),(83508,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','We must help each other to get rid of our ____.\n(A) flaws  (B) thoughts  (C) emotions  (D) desires\n','','','A) flaws','',0,0,0,NULL),(83509,2495,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','People\'s likes and dislikes and capabilities are ____.\n(A) same  (B) different  (C) opposite  (D) equal\n','','','B) different','',0,0,0,NULL),(83510,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the different emotions that every human being has?','','','Anger, joy, sorrow, jealousy, disappointment, fear are the different emotions which every human has.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83511,2495,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When can there by flaws in our personality?','','','If we do not try to change the aspects that bother us and others, these can be flaws in our personality','',0,0,0,NULL),(83512,2495,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the benefits of a balanced personality?','','','The benefits of a balanced personality are as follows : \n1) Our ability to understand others increases. \n2) We can overcome adverse conditions. \n3) We become free from faults such as blaming others unnecessarily, calling them names and not being able to tolerate others. \n4) Our ability to relate to others improves. \n5) We learn not to be stubborn. \n6) We become happy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83513,2495,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different aspects of human nature?','','','Human nature has many different aspects : \n1) Sometimes, a person gets very angry; sometimes he is loving. \n2) At times, he is jealous; sometimes he comes forward to help others. \n3) Sometimes he acts selfishly; but sometimes he is willing to make a sacrifice for others.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83514,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','When do we get angry?','','','All of us get angry about something at same point of time. \n1) We get angry if something does not happen according to our wishes, or if we get insulted. \n2) Similarly, we also get angry if we see someone being ill-treated.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83515,2495,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can we change our nature?','','','We can change our nature in the following ways : \n1) We must think about the good qualities or virtues in our friends first. \n2) We must help each other to get rid of our flaws. \n3) We can speak about our capabilities and talents with our friends. \n4) We must also discuss our flaws with our friends.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83516,2495,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','Ranjit and Abhay are fighting over who gets to sit on the first bench. They are both very angry. They are close to throwing each other\'s bags.','','','If I would have been in place of either of them, I would have advised him not to fight in a friendship just because of the place of sitting. Some days I would have compromised to sit on the first bench or I would have told the class teacher that I would sit on the first bench.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83517,2495,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Hemant wants to take part in the kabbadi competition, but he is shy. He is nervous about telling it to the teacher.','','','I would accompany Hemant to the teacher and request her to take Hemant in the kabbadi competition. Alternately, I would convince teacher, about how well Hemant plays kabbadi by inviting him to see his game on the ground and then selecting him in the team.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83518,2495,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rekha accidently took Nisha\'s notebook home.','','','If I would be Nisha, I would have accepted Rekha\'s appology without getting angry on her. But, I would have told her that as I didn\'t have the notebook, I could not complete yesterday\'s homework. I would tell her not to feel bad about it and that I would sit extra hours and complete my notebook.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83530,2438,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','ज्या चांदण्या लुकलुकतात त्यांना ----------- म्हणतात.\n(A) तारे  (B) ग्रह  (C) उपग्रह  (D) सूर्य\n','','','A) तारे','',0,0,0,NULL),(83531,2438,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','ग्रहाच्या सूर्याभोवतीच्या परिभ्रमण करण्याच्या ठराविक मार्गाला त्या ग्रहाची ---------- म्हणतात.\n(A) प्रदक्षिणा  (B) परिभ्रमण  (C) कक्षा  (D) परिवलन\n','','','C) कक्षा','',0,0,0,NULL),(83532,2438,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चंद्र हा पृथ्वीचा ---------- आहे.\n(A) ग्रह  (B) तारा  (C) लघुग्रह  (D) उपग्रह\n','','','D) उपग्रह','',0,0,0,NULL),(83533,2438,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्लुटो हा -------------- आहे.\n(A) ग्रह  (B) उपग्रह  (C) लघुग्रह  (D) बटूग्रह\n','','','D) बटूग्रह','',0,0,0,NULL),(83534,2438,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पृथ्वीच्या सर्वात जवळचा तारा ------------ आहे.\n(A) शुक्र  (B) सूर्य  (C) चंद्र  (D) बुध\n','','','B) सूर्य','',0,0,0,NULL),(83535,2438,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','--------- हे अवकाशात जाणारे पहिले भारतीय अंतराळवीर होत.\n(A) कल्पना चावला  (B) निल आर्मस्ट्राँग  (C) राकेश शर्मा  (D) सुनिता विल्यमस्\n','','','C) राकेश शर्मा','',0,0,0,NULL),(83536,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------- मुळे पृथ्वीगोलाचे दोन समान भाग होतात.\n(A) उत्तर ध्रुव  (B) अक्ष  (C) विषुववृत्त  (D) परिवलन\n','','','C) विषुववृत्त ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83537,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पृथ्वीच्या ज्या भागावर उजेड पडतो तेथे ..... आहे, असे म्हणतात.\n(A) रात्र  (B) दिवस  (C) अंधार  (D) दिन\n','','','B) दिवस ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83538,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','---------- म्हणजे पृथ्वीच्या सूर्याभोवतीच्या एका परिभ्रमणाला लागणारा कालावधी होय.\n(A) वर्ष  (B) दिवस  (C) दिन  (D) चांद्रमास\n','','','A) वर्ष ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83539,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','लीप वर्षात .... दिवस असतात.\n(A) 365  (B) 366  (C) 360  (D) 364\n','','','B) 366 ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83540,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','अमावास्येपासून पोर्णिमेपर्यंत चंद्राचा पृथ्वीवरून दिसणारा प्रकाशित भाग ----------\n(A) वाढत जातो  (B) कमी होता जातो  (C) आहे तेवढाच राहतो  (D) नाहिसा होतो\n','','','A) वाढत जातो ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83541,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पृथ्वीच्या स्वतःभोवती फिरण्यास ----- म्हणतात.\n(A) परिभ्रमण (B) परिवलन (C) ऋतू (D) तिथी\n','','','B) परिवलन','',0,0,0,NULL),(83542,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पृथ्वीच्या सूर्याभोवतीच्या फिरण्यास ----- म्हणतात.\n(A) परिवलन (B) परिभ्रमण (C) कला (D) लीप\n','','','B) परिभ्रमण','',0,0,0,NULL),(83543,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पृथ्वीच्या परिवलनामुळे आपल्याला ----- व ----- होते.\n(A) ऋतू, दिवस (B) दिन, रात्र (C) तिथी, कला (D) लीप, ऋतू\n','','','B) दिन, रात्र','',0,0,0,NULL),(83544,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चांद्रमासातील दिवसांना ----- म्हणतात.\n(A) कला (B) तिथी (C) ऋतू (D) दिन\n','','','B) तिथी','',0,0,0,NULL),(83545,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चंद्राच्या पृथ्वीभोवतीच्या परिभ्रमणामुळे आपल्याला चंद्राच्या ----- दिसतात.\n(A) कला (B) तिथी (C) ऋतू (D) दिन\n','','','A) कला','',0,0,0,NULL),(83546,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चांद्रमास सुमारे ----- ते ----- दिवसांचा असतो.\n(A) २४, २८ (B) २८, ३० (C) ३०, ३२ (D) ३१, ३५\n','','','B) २८, ३०','',0,0,0,NULL),(83547,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पृथ्वीचे सूर्याभोवतीचे परिभ्रमण आणि पृथ्वीचा कललेला अक्ष यामुळे पृथ्वीवर ----- होतात.\n(A) ऋतू (B) दिन, रात्र (C) लीप वर्ष (D) तिथी\n','','','A) ऋतू','',0,0,0,NULL),(83548,2439,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','एका वर्षाचे ३६५ दिवसांऐवजी ३६६ दिवस असतात. त्याला ----- वर्ष म्हणतात.\n(A) ऋतू (B) लीप (C) तिथी (D) कला\n','','','B) लीप','',0,0,0,NULL),(83549,2440,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','जमिनीच्या सलग व मोठ्या भागास -------- म्हणतात.\n(A) शिलावरण  (B) भूरूप  (C) खडक  (D) खंड\n','','','D) खंड ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83550,2440,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','जमिनीच्या सखल भागात नैसर्गिकरित्या पाणी साठून तयार झालेल्या मोठ्या जलाशयाला .... म्हणतात.\n(A) महासागर  (B) तळे  (C) सरोवर  (D) विहीर\n','','','C) सरोवर ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83551,2440,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','थंड भागात समुद्रावर तरंगणाऱ्या बर्फाच्या मोठ्या तुकड्यांना --------- म्हणतात.\n(A) हिमकण  (B) हिमनग  (C) हिमवर्षाव  (D) हिम\n','','','B) हिमनग ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83552,2440,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','हवामानाशी संबंधित सर्व घटना --------- होतात.\n(A) तपांबरात  (B) स्थितांबरात  (C) बाह्यांबरात  (D) हिम\n','','','A) तपांबरात ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83553,2440,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','.... हा वायू सूर्यापासून येणारी अतिनील किरणे शोषून घेतो.\n(A) हायड्रोजन  (B) ओझोन  (C) बाष्प  (D) ऑक्सिजन\n','','','B) ओझोन ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83554,2441,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','-------- म्हणजे आसपासची जागा.\n(A) संतुलन  (B) पर्यावरण  (C) परिसर  (D) निकटता\n','','','C) परिसर ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83555,2441,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','जीवनाशी संबंध असलेले परिसरातील सगळे घटक मिळून ---------- बनते.\n(A) सभोवताल  (B) निसर्ग  (C) वातावरण  (D) पर्यावरण\n','','','D) पर्यावरण ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83556,2441,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रत्येक अन्नसाखळीचा आधार.... असतात.\n(A) वनस्पती  (B) झाडे  (C) शाकाहारी प्राणी  (D) कीटक\n','','','A) वनस्पती ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83557,2441,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्राण्यांची मृत शरीरे कुजवण्याचे काम मातीतील .... करतात.\n(A) पाणी  (B) सूक्ष