ेघ मारा : मसाले प्राण्यांच्या/वनस्पतींच्या विविध भागांपासून मिळतात.','','','मसाले <u>वनस्पतींच्या</u> विविध भागांपासून मिळतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83621,2451,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून लिहा : एका हंगामाचे पीक पुढील हंगाम येईपर्यंत वर्षभर पुरवावे लागते.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83622,2451,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून लिहा : फळे व भाज्या यांचा त्यांच्या नैसर्गिक हंगामातला स्वाद चांगला नसतो.','','','चूक. (फळे व भाज्या यांचा त्यांच्या नैसर्गिक हंगामातला स्वाद सर्वोत्तम असतो.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83623,2451,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून लिहा : आमरस व दूध हे टिकाऊ पदार्थ आहेत.','','','चूक. (आमरस व दूध हे लवकर खराब होणारे पदार्थ आहेत.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83624,2451,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून लिहा : पोळीवर वाढणारे कापसासारखे तंतू म्हणजे कापूसच असतो.','','','चूक. (पोळीवर वाढणारे कापसासारखे तंतू म्हणजे एक प्रकारची बुरशी असते.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83625,2451,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','अन्न कोणकोणत्या पद्धतीने टिकवले जाते?','','','वाळवणे, थंड जागी ठेवणे, उकळणे, हवाबंद डब्यात ठेवणे आणि अन्न-परिरक्षक वापरणे अशा विविध पद्धतींनी अन्न टिकवले जाते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83626,2451,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','खराब झालेले.अन्न आपण खाणयाचे का टाळतो?','','','१) सूक्ष्मजीवांमुळे अन्न खराब होते. हे सूक्ष्मजीव शरीराला अपाय करतात. \n२) खराब अन्नाचा वास आणि स्वरूप दोन्ही बिघडलेले असते. \n३) अशा अन्नाच्या सेवनाने पोटदुखी, जुलाब, उलट्या होऊ शकतात. \n४) कधी कधी खराब अन्नाला बुरशी लागते.\n(५) अशा अन्नाचे पोषणमूल्य कमी होते. या सर्व कारणांमुळे आपण खराब झालेले अन्न खाण्याचे टाळतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83627,2451,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','फळांचे मुरांबे का केले जातात?','','','फळे जास्त काळ टिकत नाहीत. त्यांवर सूक्ष्मजीव किंवा बुरशी वाढू शकते. फळे टिकवायची असल्यास त्यांत साखरेसारखे परिरक्षक घालून ती टिकवता येतात. म्हणून हंगामानंतरही तशाच चांगल्या स्वादातली फळे खाण्यासाठी फळांचे मुरांबे केले जातात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83628,2451,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','परिरक्षकांचा वापर कशासाठी करतात ?','','','जे अन्नपदार्थ टिकवता येत नाहीत ते पदार्थ परिरक्षकांमुळे जास्त काळपर्यंत टिकवता येतात. खराब होणारे अन्नपदार्थ हंगामानंतरही टिकवलेल्या स्वरूपात राहावेत यासाठी परिरक्षकांचा वापर करतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83629,2451,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','मसाल्याचे विविध पदार्थ कोणते ? ते वनस्पतींचा कोणता भाग आहेत?','','','१) धणे, दालचिनी, लवंगा, वेलची, जायफळ, जिरे, काळी मिरी, तमालपत्र असे मसाल्याचे विविध पदार्थ आहेत. \n२) धणे, जिरे, काळी मिरी या वनस्पतीच्या बिया आहेत. दालचिनी ही झाडाच्या खोडापासून बनवतात. खोडाची जाड साल आपण दालचिनी म्हणून वापरतो. तमालपत्र हे पान आहे, तर लवंगा या फुलाच्या सुकलेल्या देठापासून तयार होतात. वेलची, जायफळ ही त्या त्या झाडांची फळे आहेत.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83630,2451,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : दुपारचे जेवण झाल्यावर उरलेले जेवण फ्रिजमध्ये ठेवतात.','','','दुपारचे शिल्लक जेवण बाहेरच ठेवले तर कालांतराने ते खराब होईल. फ्रिजमधल्या थंडाव्यामुळे ते टिकते. त्यावर सूक्ष्मजीव प्रक्रिया करणार नाहीत. रात्री ते खाता यावे म्हणून दुपारचे उरलेले जेवण फ्रिजमध्ये ठेवतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83631,2451,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : अंडी व दूध हे पदार्थ टिकवून मग ते लोकांपर्यंत पोहोचवावे लागतात.','','','अंडी कुक्कुटपालन केंद्र आणि दूध दुग्धव्यवसाय केंद्र येथे तयार होते. या ठिकाणांपासून ग्राहक दूरच्या ठिकाणावर असतो. हे पदार्थ त्याच्याकडे पोहोचेपर्यंत खराब होऊ शकतात. म्हणून त्यांच्या वाहतुकीच्या अगोदरच ते टिकवून मगच लोकांपर्यंत पोहोचवतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83632,2451,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : स्वयंपाकघरात बरेच दिवसांसाठीचे सामान आणून ठेवलेले असते.','','','जेवण बनवताना अनेक पदार्थ लागतात. पुन्हा पुन्हा बाजारात जाऊन या वस्तू आणाव्या लागतील. असे करणे गैरसोयीचे आहे; म्हणून स्वयंपाकघरात बरेच दिवसांसाठीचे सामान आणून ठेवलेले असते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83633,2451,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','शेवया अनेक दिवस चांगल्या राहतात; परंतु शेवयांची खीर लवकरच खराब होते, असे का?','','','शेवया उन्हात वाळवून तयार केलेल्या असतात. त्यात पाण्याचा अंश नसल्याने त्या अनेक दिवस चांगल्या राहतात. परंतु शेवयांची खीर दुधात बनवलेली असते. दूध टिकाऊ नसल्याने खीर लवकरच खराब होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83634,2451,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','पापड सादळले (दमट झाले) आहेत.','','','दमट झालेल्या पापडांना उन्हात वाळवण्यासाठी ठेवावे. तीन-चार दिवस कडक ऊन लावल्यावर हवाबंद डब्यात घट्ट बंद करून ठेवावेत. ज्यांच्या घरी मायक्रोवेन्ह ओळ्हन असेल अशांनी दमट पापड एक-दोन सेकंदभर ओळ्हनमध्ये फिरवून त्यांतील आर्द्रता काढून टाकावी.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83635,2451,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','आंबा, कैरी, आवळा, पेरू अशी फळे; तर मटार, मेथी, कांदा, टोमॅटो अशा भाज्या ठरावीक काळात भरपूर प्रमाणात उपलब्ध असतात. ते वर्षभर वापरायचे आहेत.','','','अन्न टिकवण्याच्या विविध आणि योग्य पद्धती वापरून अशा मोसमी फळे व भाज्या टिकवून ठेवता येतील. उदा., आंब्याचा रस काढून वाळवून ठेवता येतो. आंबापोळीही बनवता येते. कैरी, आवळा यांची लोणची आणि छुंदा, मुरांबा, मोरावळा असे प्रकार बनवून ठेवता येतात. कैरी, आवळा, पेरू यांची सरबते व जॅम देखील बनवता येतो. मटार सोलून त्याचे दाणे फ्रिज करता येतात. बाजारात मिळणारे \'फ्रोजन पीज्\' असेच तयार केलेले असतात. मेथीपासून ठेपले, मुठिया असे पदार्थ करून ती टिकवता येते. टोमॅटोंचा सॉस, टोमॅटो प्युरी - दाट रस अशी उत्पादने बनवण्यात येतात. कांदा वाळवून, कोरड्या जागी योग्य प्रकारे साठवून ठेवता येतो. बारीक कापलेला कांदा तळून ठेवल्यास पुष्कळ दिवस टिकतो. एखादे अन्न उत्पादन जास्त प्रमाणात उपलब्ध असले, तर आपण टिकवण्याच्या योग्य पद्धतींनी व परिरक्षके वापरून त्यांचा साठा वर्षभर वापरू शकतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83636,2451,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','',' पदार्थ खराब झाला, असे आपण केव्हा म्हणतो? त्यात कोणते बदल झालेले असतात ?','','','पदार्थांना विचित्र वास येऊ लागला, तो खराब दिसू लागला, त्यावर बुरशी आली की तो पदार्थ खराब झाला असे आपण म्हणतो.\nअशा पदार्थात रासायनिक बदल होतात. त्यांचा रंग व चव बदलते. त्यावर सूक्ष्मजीव व बुरशी वाढल्यास तो पदार्थ आरोग्यास हानिकारक होतो. खराब पदार्थात घातक बदल होतात व त्यामुळे असा पदार्थ खाऊ नये.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83637,2451,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','अन्नपदार्थ खराब होण्यासाठी लागणारा वेळ वेगवेगळ्या ऋतूंमध्ये वेगळा असतो का ? ','','','अन्नपदार्थ खराब होण्याची क्रिया सभोवतालच्या तापमानावर व दमटपणावर अवलंबून असते. गरम आणि दमट हवेत अन्नपदार्थ लवकर खराब होतात. थंड आणि कोरड्या हवेत अन्नपदार्थ टिकतात. म्हणूनच वेगवेगळ्या ऋतूंमध्ये अन्नपदार्थ खराब होण्यासाठी वेगवेगळा वेळ लागतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83638,2451,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','सर्वांत लवकर खराब होणारे पदार्थ कोणते ?','','','दूध व दुधाचे पदार्थ, फळे, भाज्या, मासे, मांस असे पदार्थ सर्वांत लवकर खराब होतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83639,2451,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','घरात आणलेले पदार्थ जास्त दिवस चांगले राहावेत यासाठी काय काय केले जाते?','','','काही पदार्थ रेफ्रिजरेटरमध्ये ठेवले जातात. काही पदार्थ वाळवून किंवा प्रक्रिया करून ठेवले जातात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83640,2454,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पाण्यात मिसळलेले पदार्थ सजीवांसाठी ----- असतात.\n(A) उपयुक्त (B) अपायकारक (C) पोषक (D) जीवनदायी\n','','','B) अपायकारक','',0,0,0,NULL),(83641,2454,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सांडपाण्यात अनेक ----- असतात.\n(A) पोषकतत्त्वे (B) औषधे (C) अशुद्धी (D) पिके\n','','','C) अशुद्धी','',0,0,0,NULL),(83642,2454,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मैलापाण्यात रोग पसरवणारे ----- असू शकतात.\n(A) खनिजे (B) सूक्ष्मजीव (C) धान्ये (D) जीवनसत्त्वे\n','','','B) सूक्ष्मजीव','',0,0,0,NULL),(83643,2454,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','गावाला पाणीपुरवठा करण्यापूर्वी त्याचे ----- करण्यात येते.\n(A) परीक्षण (B) शुद्धीकरण (C) संकलन (D) वापर\n','','','B) शुद्धीकरण','',0,0,0,NULL),(83644,2454,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सांडपाण्यावर प्रक्रिया करूनच ते बाहेर सोडण्याची ----- वर सक्ती आहे.\n(A) नागरिकांवर (B) शेतकऱ्यांवर (C) कारखानदारांवर (D) विद्यार्थ्यांवर\n','','','C) कारखानदारांवर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83645,2454,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पाण्यात विरघळलेल्या ऑक्सिजनचे प्रमाण कमी झाल्यावर ----- धोका होतो.\n(A) मानवांना (B) जलचरांना (C) प्राण्यांना (D) वनस्पतींना\n','','','B) जलचरांना','',0,0,0,NULL),(83646,2454,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','दुष्काळ ही ----- आपत्ती आहे.\n(A) मानवनिर्मित (B) नैसर्गिक (C) सामाजिक (D) राजकीय\n','','','B) नैसर्गिक','',0,0,0,NULL),(83647,2454,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','दुष्काळाच्या वेळी पाळीव जनावरांची काळजी घेण्यासाठी ----- उभारल्या जातात.\n(A) धान्य गोदामे (B) चारा छावण्या (C) कारखाने (D) पाणीपुरवठा केंद्रे\n','','','B) चारा छावण्या','',0,0,0,NULL),(83648,2454,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून पुन्हा लिहा : पावसाचे पाणी आपल्याला वर्षभर मिळते.','','','चूक. (पावसाचे पाणी आपल्याला वर्षातून चार महिनेच मिळते.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83649,2454,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून पुन्हा लिहा : शासनातर्फे दुष्काळग्रस्त भागातील लोकांना आणि प्राण्यांना सुरक्षित जागी तात्पुरते हलवले जाते.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83650,2454,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून पुन्हा लिहा : नदीच्या वाहत्या पाण्याचे नैसर्गिकरीत्या पूर्णपणे शुद्धीकरण केले जाते.','','','चूक. (नदीच्या वाहत्या पाण्याचे नैसर्गिकरीत्या काही प्रमाणातच शुद्धीकरण केले जाते.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83651,2454,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून पुन्हा लिहा : बाष्पीभवनामुळे पाण्याची सतत वांफ होत असते.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83652,2454,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून पुन्हा लिहा : जलस्रोतांचे प्रदूषण हानिकारक नसते.','','','चूक. (जलस्रोतांचे प्रदूषण हानिकारक असते.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83653,2454,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पाणी अडवणे व जिरवणे यांतून काय साध्य करतात?','','','जलव्यवस्थापन','',0,0,0,NULL),(83654,2454,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पावसाळ्यानंतरच्या काळातही पाणी उपलब्ध होईल यासाठी कोण कोण उपाय करतात?','','','शासन व नागरिक.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83655,2454,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','जलशुद्धीकरण केंद्रात काय केले जाते?','','','पाण्याचे निर्जतुकीकरण.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83656,2454,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','दुष्काळात कोणती परिस्थिती निर्माण होते?','','','१) दुष्काळात जलाशयांची पातळी कमी कमी होते. काही जलाशये व जमिनी कोरड्या पडतात. \n२) जनावरांना आणि माणसांना पिण्याच्या पाण्याची टंचाई जाणवते. \n३) शेतीला पाणी न मिळाल्याने पिके करपून जातात. \n४) दुष्काळ पडलेल्या भागातील प्राणी, वनस्पती व माणसे या सान्यांनाच वाईट परिस्थितीचा सामना करावा लागतो. माणसांना स्थलांतर करावे लागते, कारण त्यांना तेथेच जगणे कठीण होते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83657,2454,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','पावसाळ्यानंतरही पाणी उपलब्ध होणयासाठी शासन आणि नागरिक कोणती कामे करतात?','','','१) पावसाळ्यात जमिनीवर पडणारे पावसाचे पाणी जमिनीत जिरवण्यासाठी वेगवेगळे प्रयत्न केले जातात. \n२) लहान तलावांची निर्मिती करणे; उतारावर लहान बंधारे बांधणे; आडवे चर खणे; गावातील ओढे, नाले यांवर बांध घालून पाणी अडवणे अशी कामे शासन व नागरिक एकत्र येऊन करतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83658,2454,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','पावसाचे पाणी कशासाठी अडवावे लागते?','','','१) पावसाचे पाणी अडवून ठेवल्यानंतर, पावसाळ्याच्या काळानंतरही ते उपलब्ध होते.\n२) अडवलेले पाणी जमिनीत जिरून भूजलाचा साठा वाढतो. \n३) त्यामुळे झाडांना व शेतीला पाणी मिळते.\n४) विहिरी पाण्याने भरलेल्या राहतात आणि दुष्काळाची शक्यता कमी होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83659,2454,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','\'जलव्यवस्थापन\' कशाला म्हणतात?','','','पावसाळ्यानंतरही पावसाचे पाणी वापरण्यासाठी उपलब्ध राहील अशी सोय करणे याला \'जलव्यवस्थापन\' असे म्हणतात. यासाठी पाण्याचा काटकसरीने वापर करणे आणि पावसाचे पाणी अडवून जमिनीत जिरवणे या कृती केल्या जातात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83660,2454,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','पाण्याचे प्रदूषण कसे होते?','','','शहराचे किंवा गावाचे सांडपाणी एकत्र करून एखादया मोठ्या जलसाठ्यात सोडले जाते. या सांडपाण्यात अनेक अशुद्धी असतात. मैलापाण्यात रोगकारक सूक्ष्मजीव असतात. कारखान्याच्या सांडपाण्यात विषारी पदार्थ असतात. काही पदार्थ विरघळलेल्या स्वरूपात असतात, तर काही तसेच तरंगत असतात. अशा सर्व प्रदूषितांमुळे पाण्याचे प्रदूषण होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83661,2454,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','कोणकोणत्या पद्धतींनी पावसाचे पाणी साठवले जाते?','','','१) नदीच्या पात्रात विहिरी खोदून तेथे पाणी साठवण्याची व्यवस्था करतात. \n२) घरांच्या छपरांवर पडणारे पावसाचे पाणी पन्हळींच्या साहाय्याने अंगणात ठेवलेल्या टाक्यांमध्ये साठवतात. ३) वाहत्या ओढ्यांना किंवा नाल्यांना छोटे बांध घालून पाणी अडवले जाते. छोटे तलाव निर्माण करून त्यांतही पावसाचे पाणी साठवले जाते. \n४) पावसाचे पाणी जमिनीमध्ये जिरवून पाण्याची पातळी वाढवण्यात येते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83662,2454,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','जलशुद्धीकरण केंद्रातल्या प्रक्रिया कोणत्या? या कशा व कशासाठी केल्या जातात यासंबंधी एक तक्ता तयार करा.','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>क्रमवार क्रिया</th>\n    <th>कशा केल्या जातात?</th>\n    <th>कशासाठी केल्या जातात?</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>१. निवळणे</td>\n    <td>जलस्रोतांतून आणलेले पाणी स्थिर ठेवणे व त्यावर तुरटी फिरवणे</td>\n    <td>तरंगणाऱ्या अशुद्धी तळाला बसवणे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>२. गाळणे</td>\n    <td>गाळणी यंत्राद्वारे पाणी गाळणे</td>\n    <td>न विरघळणाऱ्या अशुद्धी काढून टाकणे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>३. ऑक्सिजनीकरण</td>\n    <td>पंपाच्या मदतीने पाण्यात ऑक्सिजन खेळवणे</td>\n    <td>हवेतील ऑक्सिजन पाण्यात मिसळणे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>४. निर्जतुकीकरण</td>\n    <td>पाण्यात क्लोरीन मिसळणे</td>\n    <td>पाण्यातले जंतू मारणे</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(83663,2454,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','गावापासून दूर असलेला तलाव पाहण्यासाठी तुमच्या वर्गाची सहल जाणार आहे. दिवसभर लागणाऱ्या पिणयाच्या पाणयाची व्यवस्था करायची आहे. (','','','सहलीला जात असताना आपल्याबरोबर पाण्याच्या बाटल्या आपण घेऊन जातोच. सहल तलावाच्या काठी असल्यामुळे तेथेही पाणी उपलब्ध आहे. ते कदाचित पिण्यासाठी निर्धोक नसेल. म्हणून जाताना पाणी स्वच्छ करण्यासाठी तुरटी; गाळण्यासाठी स्वच्छ, सुती कपडा; पाण्यासाठी वापरण्यात येणारे जंतुनाशक द्रव्य अशा गोष्टी बरोबर घेऊन जाव्यात. आपण मग दिवसभर पिण्यासाठी लागणाऱ्या पाण्याची व्यवस्था करू शकतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83664,2454,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','जमिनीच्या उतारामुळे बागेतील माती पाणयाबरोबर वाहून जात आहे. ','','','जमिनीच्या उतारावर छोटया झुडपांचे रोपण करावे. गवताची बियाणेदेखील रुजवता येईल. उताराच्या बाजूला दगड किंवा विटा लावून बांध घातल्यास माती वाहून जाणार नाही. बागेला पाणी घालताना हलकेच शिंपावे. जोराने पाणी सोडल्यास उतारामुळे अधिक धूप होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83665,2454,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','पावसाचे पाणी जमिनीत मुरणयासाठी रस्ते व पादचारी मार्ग कसे बांधावे.','','','संपूर्ण रस्त्याचे आणि पदपथांचे काँक्रीटीकरण केल्यास पावसाचे पाणी जमिनीत न मुरता वाहून जाईल. असे होऊ नये म्हणून रस्त्याच्या कडेला थोडा भाग मातीचा ठेवावा. येथून पाणी आत मुरेल. तसेच पदपथावर आणि पादचारी मार्गावर लावण्यात येणाऱ्या लाद्यांच्या मध्ये थोडी भेग राखल्यास त्यातूनही पावसाचे पाणी आत मुरेल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83666,2454,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','पाण्याने भरलेल्या भांड्यात चमचा भरून साखर, लाकडाचा भुसा, माती हे पदार्थ टाकले तर काय होईल ?','','','पाण्यात साखर टाकल्यावर ती संपूर्णपणे पाण्यात विरघळेल व दिसेनाशी होईल. लाकडाचा भुसा पाण्याच्या पृष्ठभागावर तरंगत राहील. माती पाण्याच्या तळाला बसेल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83667,2454,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','पाण्याच्या अवस्था कोणत्या ?','','','पाण्याच्या तीन अवस्था १) स्थायुरूप (उदा., बर्फ) \n२) द्रवरूप (उदा., साधे पाणी), \n३) वायुरूप (उदा., बाष्प/वाफ).','',0,0,0,NULL),(83668,2454,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पिण्याचे पाणी स्वच्छ व निर्धोक करण्यासाठी काय करतात ?','','','पिण्याचे पाणी निवळणे व गाळणे या क्रियांनी स्वच्छ केले जाते. त्यानंतर ते उकळले की पिण्यासाठी निर्धोक होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83669,2454,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','पाऊस पडत असताना मोकळ्या जागी ठेवलेल्या स्वच्छ भांड्यात पडलेले पावसाचे पाणी, तसेच जमिनीवरून वाहत जाणारे पाणी वेगवेगळ्या भांड्यांत घेऊन त्यांचे निरीक्षण करा. काय फरक दिसतो? त्याचे कारण काय ?','','','स्वच्छ भांड्यात थेट पडलेले पावसाचे पाणी नितळ व शुद्ध दिसेल. जमिनीवरून वाहत जाणाऱ्या पाण्यात अनेक अशुद्ध पदार्थ मिसळतील, त्यामुळे ते अस्वच्छ दिसेल. जमिनीच्या पाण्याची गढूळता त्यात मिसळलेल्या पदार्थांमुळे असेल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83670,2454,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','तुमच्या घरातील स्वयंपाकघर आणि न्हाणीघरातून बाहेर पडणाऱ्या सांडपाण्यात कोणकोणते पदार्थ मिसळलेले असतात?','','','आमच्या घरातील स्वयंपाकघर आणि न्हाणीघरातून बाहेर पडणाऱ्या सांडपाण्यात मल, मूत्र, अंघोळीचे पाणी, साबणाचे द्रावण, डिटर्जंट, खरकटे पाणी, कपडे-भांडी धुतलेले पाणी असे अनेक दूषित घटक मिसळलेले असतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83671,2454,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','एखादया भागात दीर्घकाळपर्यंत पाऊसच पडला नाही, तर तेथील लोकांच्या जीवनावर कोणते परिणाम होतील?','','','एखादया भागात दीर्घकाळपर्यंत पाऊसच पडला नाही तर पाण्याची टंचाई निर्माण होईल. पाणी नसेल तर शेती करता येणार नाही. पिके तयार होणार नाहीत. माणसांना पाण्यासाठी वणवण करावी लागेल. जनावरे अन्न-पाण्यावाचून तडफडून मरतील. अन्नाच्या आणि पाण्याच्या शोधात माणसे तो भाग सोडून दुसऱ्या भागात स्थलांतर करतील. या भागात दुष्काळ पडला असे म्हटले जाईल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83672,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','जगभरातील एकूण लोकसंख्या आता सुमारे ----- कोटींच्या घरात आहे.\n(A) ३०० (B) ४५० (C) ६०० (D) ७५०\n','','','C) ६००','',0,0,0,NULL),(83673,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','दलदलीच्या प्रदेशात किंवा खोलगट भूभागांत भराव घालून त्या जागी जमिनीचे ----- केले जाते.\n(A) सिंचन (B) सपाटीकरण (C) सिंधुकाम (D) लागवड\n','','','B) सपाटीकरण','',0,0,0,NULL),(83674,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रदूषण झाल्यावर सरतेशेवटी पाण्यातील अनेक सजीव ----- होतात.\n(A) वाढतात (B) स्थलांतर करतात (C) नामशेष (D) सुरक्षित\n','','','C) नामशेष','',0,0,0,NULL),(83675,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारखाने चालवण्यासाठी ----- वापरही मोठ्या प्रमाणावर होऊ लागला.\n(A) इंधनाचा (B) पाण्याचा (C) धुराचा (D) प्राणवायूचा\n','','','A) इंधनाचा','',0,0,0,NULL),(83676,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','इंधनाच्या ज्वलनातून मोठ्या प्रमाणात निघणाऱ्या धुरामुळे हवेतील ----- च्या प्रमाणात वाढ होत गेली.\n(A) ऑक्सिजन (B) कार्बन डायऑक्साइड (C) नायट्रोजन (D) हायड्रोजन\n','','','B) कार्बन डायऑक्साइड','',0,0,0,NULL),(83677,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मानवाच्या ----- हवा, पाणी, जमीन अशा सर्व ठिकाणी मोठे बदल घडून येत आहेत.\n(A) विकासामुळे (B) हस्तक्षेपामुळे (C) आनंदामुळे (D) प्रगतीमुळे\n','','','B) हस्तक्षेपामुळे','',0,0,0,NULL),(83678,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','जगातील सर्व लोकांमध्ये पर्यावरणातील धोक्यांसंबंधी ----- करणे अत्यंत महत्त्वाचे आहे.\n(A) लागवड (B) जाणीव-जागृती (C) प्रदूषण (D) निरीक्षण\n','','','B) जाणीव-जागृती','',0,0,0,NULL),(83679,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','राखीव वनक्षेत्राला किंवा त्यासाठी राखून ठेवलेल्या भागाला ----- म्हणतात.\n(A) संरक्षित क्षेत्र (B) अभयारण्य (C) देवस्थान (D) वस्ती\n','','','B) अभयारण्य','',0,0,0,NULL),(83680,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रोहित पक्षी उत्तर आशियातील ----- या प्रदेशातून मायणीला स्थलांतर करून येतात.\n(A) नेपाळ (B) सायबेरिया (C) तिबेट (D) भूतान\n','','','B) सायबेरिया','',0,0,0,NULL),(83681,2456,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- व ----- ही प्राचीन काळातील अभयारण्येच म्हणावी लागतात.\n(A) उद्याने व गावे (B) तलाव व डोंगर (C) देवराया व शरणवने (D) नद्या व दऱ्या\n','','','C) देवराया व शरणवने','',0,0,0,NULL),(83682,2456,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : निर्जीव घटकांमध्ये मृत सजीवांचा समावेश होतो.','','','बरोबर ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83683,2456,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : जैवविविधता जपणे आवश्यक आहे.','','','बरोबर ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83684,2456,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : मानवाने अधिकाधिक जमीन व जलस्रोत वापरणे सोडून दिले आहे.','','','चूक ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83685,2456,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : दूषित पाणी जमिनीत मुरत राहिले की जमीन सुपीक होते.','','','चूक ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83686,2456,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा :  कार्बन डायऑक्साइडचे प्रमाण वाढले की वातावरणाचे तापमानही वाढते.','','','बरोबर ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83687,2456,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : पृथ्वीवरचे जैविक घटक अस्तंगत होण्याची प्रक्रिया सध्या खूप संथ गतीने चालू आहे.','','','चूक ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83688,2456,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : जैवविविधता उद्यानाला भेट देणाऱ्या लोकांना वनराईचा आनंद घेता येतो.','','','बरोबर ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83689,2456,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : नान्नज माळरानात हरणेदेखील आढळतात.','','','बरोबर ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83690,2456,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','जोडया लावा :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'क\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(१) नान्नज</td>\n    <td>(अ) पुणे-अहमदनगर रस्ता</td>\n    <td>(म) मोर</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(२) चिंचोली</td>\n    <td>(ब) सोलापूर जिल्हा</td>\n    <td>(न) रोहित पक्षी</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(३) मायणी</td>\n    <td>(क) सातारा जिल्हा</td>\n    <td>(प) माळढोक</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'क\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(१) नान्नज</td>\n    <td>(ब) सोलापूर जिल्हा</td>\n    <td>(प) माळढोक</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(२) चिंचोली</td>\n    <td>(अ) पुणे-अहमदनगर रस्ता</td>\n    <td>(न) रोहित पक्षी</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(३) मायणी</td>\n    <td>(क) सातारा जिल्हा</td>\n    <td>(म) मोर</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(83691,2456,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या वस्तू/पदार्थ/घटक यांची मानवनिर्मित व निसर्गनिर्मित अशा गटांमध्ये विभागणी करा : माती, घोडा, दगड, जलपर्णी, पुस्तक, सूर्यप्रकाश, डॉल्फीन, पेन, खुर्ची, पाणी, कापूस, टेबल, झाडे, वीट.','','','१) मानवनिर्मित : पुस्तक, पेन, खुर्ची, टेबल, वीट.\n(२) निसर्गनिर्मित : माती, घोडा, दगड, जलपर्णी, सूर्यप्रकाश, डॉल्फीन, पाणी, कापूस, झाडे.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83692,2456,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','\'स्थलांतर\' म्हणजे काय?','','','प्राण्यांनी वास्तव्यासाठी एका ठिकाणाहून दुसय्या ठिकाणी, तात्पुरते किंवा कायम स्वरूपाचे जाणे याला \'स्थलांतर\' असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83693,2456,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','पक्ष्यांचा जीवनक्रम लिहा.','','','पक्षी नैसर्गिक वातावरणात, गर्द झाडीत राहतात. शहरातले पक्षीदेखील झाडांवर घरटी बांधून त्यात राहतात. नर व मादी दोघेही जण किंवा त्यातील एक जण घरटे बांधतो. मादी घरट्यात अंडी घालते. काही काळासाठी ही अंडी मादी उबवते. अंड्यांतून पिल्ले बाहेर आली की त्यांची काळजी घेतली जाते. त्यांना अन्न भरवले जाते. पिल्लू मोठे झाले, त्याच्या पंखांत ताकद आली की ते घरट्यातून उडून जाते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83695,2456,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','हवा प्रदूषणाची दोन कारणे लिहा.','','','१) उदयोगधंदे, कारखाने यातून निघणारा इंधनाचा धूर. \n२) वाहतुकीमुळे होणारे हवा प्रदूषण. वाहनांतही इंधनांचे मंद ज्वलन होत असल्याने कार्बन डायऑक्साइडचे प्रमाण फार वाढते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83696,2456,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','जमिनीवरच्या उपलब्ध वनक्षेत्राचा वापर आपण कशासाठी करतो?','','','वनक्षेत्रातून आपल्याला अनेक आवश्यक व उपयुक्त वस्तू मिळतात. लाकूड, चारा, राळ, डिंक, आयुर्वेदिक औषधांकरिता वनस्पती अशा गोष्टी वनक्षेत्रातूनच मिळवल्या जातात. निरनिराळ्या वृक्षांच्या पानांचा वापरही आपण वेगवेणळ्या कारणांसाठी करतो. आदिवासी लोक जंगलातील लाकूडफाट्याचा इंधन म्हणूनही वापर करतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83697,2456,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपण सर्वांनी पर्यावरणविषयक कोणता निर्धार केला पाहिजे?','','','आपल्या रोजच्या व्यवहारातील कोणत्याही कृतीमुळे प्रदूषण होऊ नये, जैवसृष्टीचे नुकसान होऊ नये आणि जैवसृष्टीच्या संवर्धनासाठी जास्तीत जास्त प्रयत्न केले पाहिजेत, असा आपण सर्वांनी निर्धार केला पाहिजे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83698,2456,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','माळढोक पक्ष्याची माहिती लिहा.','','','माळढोक हा मोठा व वजनदार पक्षी आहे. त्यांची चाल डौलदार असते. त्यांचा अधिवास गवताळ प्रदेशातील माळरान हे असते. माळढोक पक्षी शेतातले कीटक खातात व पिकांचे रक्षण करतात. म्हणून त्यांना शेतक-याचे मित्र म्हणतात. त्यांच्या मांसासाठी व अंड्यांसाठी त्यांची शिकार केली जाते. त्यांची संख्या घटत चालल्यामुळे सोलापूर जिल्हयातील नान्नज हे क्षेत्र महाराष्ट्र सरकारने माळढोक अभयारण्य म्हणून घोषित केले आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83699,2456,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','मोराची चिंचोली या गावाला पर्यावरण संतुलनाचे उत्तम उदाहरण का म्हटले जाते?','','','मोराची चिंचोली हे गाव पुणे-अहमदनगर रस्त्यावर आहे. येथे सुमारे २५०० मोर आहेत. येथल्या मोरांना ग्रामस्थ संरक्षण देतात. पूर्वापार लावलेली व निगा राखलेली चिंचेची झाडे येथे आहेत. असे पर्यावरण पक्ष्यांना अनुकूल आहे. मानवी हस्तक्षेप करून येथे कोणी नैसर्गिक अधिवास खराब करीत नाहीत. म्हणून मोराची चिंचोली या गावाला पर्यावरण संतुलनाचे उत्तम उदाहरण असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83700,2456,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','एखादया ठिकाणी घारी राहिल्या नाहीत तर काय होईल? कोणत्या सजीवांची संख्या वाढेल? कोणत्या सजीवांची संख्या कमी होईल?','','','घारी या छोटया प्राण्यांची शिकार करून खातात. तसेच मृतावशेष देखील खातात. घारींची संख्या कमी झाली तर उंदीर, घुशी, छोटे पक्षी अशांची संख्या वाढेल. सापांच्या संख्येतदेखील वाढ होईल.\nघारीची संख्या कमी होईल. घारीवर अन्नासाठी अवलंबून राहणारा भक्षक नसल्याने इतर कोणत्याही सजीवांच्या भक्षक संख्येवर परिणाम होणार नाही.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83701,2456,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','काय करावे बरे? \nनदी, तलाव यांमध्ये जलपर्णीची चादर पसरली आहे.','','','जलाशयात सेंद्रिय पदार्थ मिसळत राहिले की त्यात जलपर्णीसारख्या वनस्पतींची अतिरेकी वाढ होते. येथल्या इतर जलीय जीवांना त्यामुळे जगणे कठीण होते. दिवसभरात जलपर्णी ऑक्सिजन सोडतात, पण रात्री त्यांनाही ऑक्सिजन लागतो. त्यामुळे पाण्यातील ऑक्सिजनचे प्रमाण घटून तेथील प्राणी मरतात. त्यांच्या मृत शरीराचे खत होते व ते वापरून जलपर्णी आणखी जोमाने वाढतात. यालाच अतिजैवीकरण असेही म्हणतात. अशा जलाशयांचा पूर्णपणे नाश होतो. म्हणून जलपर्णीची चादर काढून टाकावी. त्या जलाशयातील पाण्यात प्रदूषिते जाणार नाहीत याची काळजी घ्यावी. अशा नद्यांना बांध असावा म्हणजे सेंद्रिय प्रदूषिते त्यात मिसळून जलपर्णी अधिक जोमाने वाढणार नाहीत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83702,2456,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : जैविक घटकांचे संवर्धन करणे महत्त्वाचे आहे.','','','जैविक घटक नष्ट झाले तर पर्यावरणातील अन्नसाखळी व अन्नजाळी खंडित होतील. काही प्रजाती नामशेष होतील. जे जैविक घटक पृथ्वीवर तयार व्हायला कित्येक कोटी वर्षे लागली ते संपून गेले तर पुन्हा तयार करता येणार नाहीत. निसर्गाचा समतोल बिघडला तर त्याचा मानवावरही परिणाम होईल. म्हणून जैविक घटकांचे संवर्धन करणे महत्त्वाचे आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83703,2456,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : वन्यप्राण्यांच्या संख्येत दिवसेंदिवस घट होत आहे.','','','मानवाच्या हस्तक्षेपामुळे अनेक अन्नसाखळ्या नष्ट होत आहेत. जमिनी मोकळ्या करण्यासाठी जंगलतोड होत आहे. वन्यप्राण्यांचे निवारे यामुळे नाहीसे झाले. प्रदूषण आणि हवामान बदलामुळे निसर्गाची चक्रे असंतुलित झाली आहेत. वन्यप्राण्यांच्या शिकारी देखील खूप वर्षे होत राहिल्या. या सर्व कारणांनी वन्यप्राण्यांच्या संख्येत दिवसेंदिवस घट होत आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83704,2456,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : पाण्यातील अनेक सजीव नामशेष होत आहेत.','','','पाण्याचे आणि मातीचे मोठ्या प्रमाणावर प्रदूषण होत आहे. शेतीसाठी रासायनिक खते आणि कीटकनाशकांचा वापर होतो. कारखाऱ्यातील, घरामधील व रस्त्यावरील सांडपाणी देखील जलस्रोतात मिसळते. यात बरेच विषारी घटक आणि धोकादायक रसायने असू शकतात. अशा रितीने प्रदूषितांमुळे पाण्यातील अनेक सजीव नामशेष होत आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83705,2456,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','मोठ्या प्रमाणावर जंगलतोड झाल्यास काय होईल, ते पुढील प्रश्नांच्या आधारे सांगा : जंगलातील सजीवांचे अन्न व पाण्याचे स्रोत घटतील का वाढतील ? का ?','','','मोठ्या प्रमाणावर जंगलतोड झाल्यास सजीवांचे उपलब्ध अन्नही घटेल. अन्नसाखळ्या खंडित होतील. अन्नासाठी एकमेकांवर अवलंबून असलेले सजीव उपासमारीने मरतील. जंगलतोड झाल्यामुळे पाण्याचे स्रोतही घटतील. झाडांची मुळे जमिनीतील मातीला व पाण्याला धरून ठेवतात. उजाड झाल्यामुळे मातीची धूप होईल, पावसाचे प्रमाण कमी होईल आणि पाण्याचे स्रोत घटतील. जमिनीत मुरलेले भूजल कमी होईल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83706,2456,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','मोठ्या प्रमाणावर जंगलतोड झाल्यास काय होईल, ते पुढील प्रश्नांच्या आधारे सांगा : हे सजीव त्याच जागी राहतील का? की निवाऱ्यासाठी इतरत्र जातील? का ?','','','जंगलतोडीमुळे या जंगलातील सजीव अन्न-पाण्याच्या शोधार्थ इतरत्र जातील. जंगलातील झाडे कापली गेल्याने त्यांचे निवारे देखील नष्ट होतील. म्हणून निवारे शोधण्यासाठी ते इतरत्र जातील.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83707,2456,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','मोठ्या प्रमाणावर जंगलतोड झाल्यास काय होईल, ते पुढील प्रश्नांच्या आधारे सांगा : जंगलातील वनस्पतींच्या व प्राण्यांच्या वास्तव्याची जागा कमी होईल का वाढेल?','','','जंगलतोडीमुळे सर्वच सजीवांचे निवारे उद्ध्वस्त होतील. उजाड जागा वाढेल पण वास्तव्याची जागा कमी होईल.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83708,2456,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','मोठ्या प्रमाणावर जंगलतोड झाल्यास काय होईल, ते पुढील प्रश्नांच्या आधारे सांगा : तेथील सजीवांची संख्या वाढेल की कमी होईल ? का?','','','जंगलतोडीमुळे सजीवांची संख्या आपोआप कमी होईल. जंगले नष्ट झाल्याने सजीवांचे अन्न व निवारे नष्ट होतात. म्हणून त्यांची संख्या कमी होईल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83709,2456,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','एखाद्या ठिकाणी धरण बांधले गेले, तर त्या पर्यावरणात कोणते बदल होतील ?','','','धरणामुळे त्या ठिकाणी पाणी मिळेल व कोरडेपणा कमी होईल. काही भागात हिरवाई येईल. परंतु या धरणाने तेथे असणारे नैसर्गिक पर्यावरण नष्ट होईल. तेथे आधीपासून असणाऱ्या वनस्पती, झाडे-झुडपे पाण्याखाली जातील. काही प्राणी पूर्णतः नामशेष होतील. तेथे राहणाऱ्या माणसांना स्थलांतर करावे लागेल. तेथल्या स्थानिक पर्यावरणात खूप बदल होईल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83710,2457,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','उसाच्या रसात ----- नावाची शर्करा असते.\na) ग्लुकोज  b) फ्रुक्टोज  c) सुक्रोज  d) लॅक्टोज\n','','','c) सुक्रोज','',0,0,0,NULL),(83711,2457,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्ताची कमतरता या विकाराला ----- म्हणतात.\na) मलेरिया  b) क्षयरोग  c) ऍनिमिया  d) डेंग्यू\n','','','c) ऍनिमिया','',0,0,0,NULL),(83712,2457,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आहारात पुरेसे तंतुमय पदार्थ नसले की ----- त्रास होऊ शकतो.\na) हाडे कमजोर होणे  b) बद्धकोष्ठता  c) दृष्टी कमी होणे  d) रक्तदाब वाढणे\n','','','b) बद्धकोष्ठता','',0,0,0,NULL),(83713,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : साबुदाणा, बटाटा अशा पदार्थांत पिष्ट/प्रथिन असते.','','','साबुदाणा, बटाटा अशा पदार्थांत <u>पिष्ट</u> प्रथिन असते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83714,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : भाकरीचा तुकडा चावून चावून खाल्ला की आंबट/गोड लागतो.','','','भाकरीचा तुकडा चावून चावून खाल्ला की <u>गोड</u> लागतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83715,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : तंतुमय पदार्थांना कोंडा/चोथा म्हणतात.','','','तंतुमय पदार्थांना <u>चोथा</u> म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83716,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : तेलामुळे कागद अपारदर्शक/अर्धपारदर्शक होतो.','','','तेलामुळे कागद <u>अर्धपारदर्शक</u> होतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83717,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : अंड्यातील पांढरा भाग/पिवळा बलक हा स्निग्ध  पदार्थ आहे.','','','अंड्यातील पांढरा <u>पिवळा बलक</u> हा स्निग्ध  पदार्थ आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83718,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पुढील प्रत्येक अन्नघटकाचे दोन स्रोत सांगा : खनिजे ','','','पालेभाज्या, फळे, मोड आलेली कडधान्ये.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83719,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पुढील प्रत्येक अन्नघटकाचे दोन स्रोत सांगा : प्रथिने ','','','पनीर, अंडी, मासे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83720,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पुढील प्रत्येक अन्नघटकाचे दोन स्रोत सांगा : पिष्टमय पदार्थ ','','','बटाटा, साबुदाणा, भात, पोळी.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83721,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रत्येकी दोन नावे लिहा : कर्बोदके ','','','शर्करा, पिष्ट, तंतुमय पदार्थ.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83722,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रत्येकी दोन नावे लिहा : जीवनसत्वे ','','','\'ए\', \'बी\', \'सी\', \'डी\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83723,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रत्येकी दोन नावे लिहा : शर्करा ','','','सुक्रोज, ग्लुकोज.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83724,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रत्येकी दोन नावे लिहा : खनिजे ','','','कॅल्शिअम, लोह.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83725,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','जोड्या जुळवा :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(१) स्निग्ध पदार्थ</td>\n    <td>(अ) ज्वारी</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(२) प्रथिने</td>\n    <td>(ब) तेल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(३) जीवनसत्त्वे</td>\n    <td>(क) धाऱ्याचा कोंडा</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(४) खनिजे</td>\n    <td>(ड) कडधान्ये</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(५) पिष्टमय पदार्थ</td>\n    <td>(ई) लोह</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(१) स्निग्ध पदार्थ	- तेल\n(२) प्रथिने	-कडधान्ये\n(३) जीवनसत्वे	- धाऱ्याचा कोंडा\n(४) खनिजे	-लोह\n(५) पिष्टमय पदार्थ	-ज्वारी\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83726,2457,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','पिष्टमय पदार्थांच्या पचनातून मिळालेल्या शर्करेचा शरीराला काय उपयोग होतो ?','','','पिष्टमय पदार्थांच्या पचनातून मिळालेल्या शर्करेचे शरीरात मंद ज्वलन होते. या ज्वलनातून निर्माण होणाऱ्या ऊर्जेचा उपयोग शरीराच्या विविध कामांसाठी होतो. शरीर गरम ठेवण्यासाठी देखील ही ऊर्जा उपयोगी असते. अशा रितीने ही शर्करा शरीरासाठी इंधन पुरवते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83727,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','तंतुमय पदार्थांचे स्रोत कोणते?','','','पिठामधील कोंडा, फळांच्या साली, पालेभाज्या, भाज्यांच्या शिरा व साली, धान्ये, कडधान्ये हे सर्व तंतुमय पदार्थांचे स्रोत आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83728,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','कर्बोदके कशाला म्हणतात?','','','शरीराला ऊर्जा देणारे पिष्ट, शर्करा व तंतुमय पदार्थ या तिन्हींना मिळून कर्बोदके असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83729,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','कुपोषण कशाला म्हणतात?','','','एखादया व्यक्तीच्या आहारात काही अन्नघटकांची कमतरता राहिली, तर शरीराचे योग्य पोषण होत नाही. याला कुपोषण असे म्हणतात. कुपोषणाचे आरोग्यावर गंभीर परिणाम होतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83730,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','संतुलित आहार म्हणजे काय?','','','सर्व आवश्यक अन्नघटकांचा योग्य तेवढा पुरवठा करणाऱ्या आहाराला संतुलित आहार असे म्हणतात. संतुलित आहारात कर्बोदके, प्रथिने, स्निर्ध पदार्थ, जीवनसत्त्वे आणि खनिजे यांचे योग्य प्रमाण असते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83731,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','स्निग्ध पदार्थांची उदाहरणे लिहा.','','','दुधावरची साय, लोणी, तूप, वनस्पतींपासून मिळणारी तेले, सुकामेवा, मांसातील चरबी, अंड्यातील पिवळा बलक ही स्निग्ध पदार्थांची उदाहरणे आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83732,2457,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','शरीरात चरबी साठते म्हणजे काय होते?','','','सर्वसाधारणपणे, आहारात आवश्यकतेपेक्षा जास्त प्रमाणात स्निभ पदार्थ आले तर पचन होऊन मिळणाऱ्या ऊर्जादायी पदार्थांचा साठा चरबीच्या रूपात होतो. आपल्याला जर योग्य प्रमाणात अन्न मिळाले नाही तर या साठवलेल्या चरबीच्या साठ्यापासून शरीराला ऊर्जा मिळते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83733,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : ऊस चवीला गोड लागतो.','','','उसात सुक्रोज नावाची शर्करा असते. त्यामुळे ऊस गोड लागतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83734,2457,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : पीठ वापरताना चाळून घेऊ नये.','','','पीठ चाळल्यावर त्यातील कोंडा वेगळा होतो. हा कोंडा टाकला जातो. कोंडा तंतुमय पदार्थ असून त्यात मोठ्या प्रमाणात जीवनसत्ते व खनिजे असतात. कोंड्याचा उपयोग निरुपयोगी व न पचलेल्या अन्नासहित विष्ठा तयार करण्यासाठी देखील होतो. कोंडा असा उपयोगी असल्याने पीठ चाळून तो फेकू नये. पीठ तसेच वापरावे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83735,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : शरीराला बाहेरून हलका मार लागला तरी आत मोठी इजा होत नाही.','','','आपल्या शरीरातल्या आंतरेंद्रियांवर चरबीचे थर असतात. या थरांमुळे नाजूक आंतरेंद्रियांना संरक्षण मिळते. म्हणून शरीराला बाहेरून हलका मार लागला तरी आत मोठी इजा होत नाही.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83736,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : उकडलेले अंडे एक उत्तम पोषण करणारे अन्न आहे.','','','उकडलेल्या अंड्यात प्रथिने आणि स्निभ पदार्थ असतात. त्यात \'ए\', \'बी\' व \'डी\' जीवनसत्ते असतात. फॉस्फरस, पोटॅशिअम, कॅल्शिअम अशांसारखी अनेक महत्त्वाची खनिजे असतात. त्यामुळे उकडलेल्या अंड्याचे पोषणमूल्य खूप चांगले आहे. म्हणूनच ते एक उत्तम पोषण करणारे अन्न आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83737,2457,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','आपल्याला उन्हाळ्यापेक्षा हिवाळ्यात जास्त भूक का लागते? ','','','हिवाळ्यात वातावरणात गारवा असतो. अशा वेळी आपले शरीर गरम ठेवण्यासाठी आपल्याला अधिक अंतर्गत ऊर्जेची गरज असते. अधिक ऊर्जा मिळण्यासाठी शरीरात अधिक इंधन तयार झाले पाहिजे. जास्त भूक लागल्यावर आपण अन्नावाटे शर्करा व पिष्टमय पदार्थ ऊर्जानिर्मितीसाठी शरीरात पाठवतो. उन्हाळ्यात तेवढ्या ऊर्जेची गरज नसते. म्हणून आपल्याला उन्हाळ्यापेक्षा हिवाळ्यात जास्त भूक लागते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83738,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','केसर आंबा खाताना दातांत धागे अडकतात, ते कर्बोदकांच्या कोणत्या प्रकारात मोडतात?','','','केसर आंब्यातील धागे हे तंतुमय पदार्थ आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83739,2457,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','फरशीवर लोखंडी खलबत्त्याने कुटताना खलबत्त्याच्या खाली काय ठेवतात? असे का करतात्त? ','','','खलबत्त्यातील पदार्थ कुटताना खालच्या बाजूस धक्के जाणवतात. त्यामुळे फरशी फुटू शकते. खलबत्ता आणि जमीन या दोघіच्यामध्ये टॉवेलची घडी किंवा जाड पुठ्ठा ठेवल्यास बत्त्याच्या आघातापासून फरशीस संरक्षण मिळून हानी होत नाही.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83740,2457,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','पालेभाज्या घालून केलेले थालीपीठ आणि दही असे जेवण केल्यास आपल्याला मुख्य अन्नघटक मिळतात का? ','','','थालीपिठाच्या पिठात तांदूळ, डाळ व इतर पदार्थ असतात. त्यामुळे त्यातून कर्बोदके व प्रथिने मिळतात. तव्यावर थालीपीठ लावताना तेल अगर लोणी वापरले जाते, त्यामुळे त्यातून स्निर्ध पदार्थही मिळतात. पालेभाज्या घातल्यामुळे थालीपिठात जीवनसत्ते, लोहासारखी खनिजे आणि तंतुमय पदार्थ देखील मिसळले जातात. दह्यामधूनही कर्बोदके, प्रथिने व स्निग्ध पदार्थ मिळतात. त्यामुळे पालेभाज्या घातलेले थालीपीठ आणि दही हे परिपूर्ण अन्नघटक असलेले जेवण, असे म्हणता येईल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83741,2457,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','भेळ तयार करणयासाठी वापरलेल्या विविध अन्नपदार्थांपासून कोणते अन्नघटक मिळतात?','','','भेळेतल्या कुरमुम्यांत कर्बोदके असतात. चणे-दाणे यांत प्रथिने असतात. शेवेच्या पिठात बेसन असते; म्हणजे त्यातही प्रथिने असतात. तळलेले पदार्थ असल्याने स्निग्धांशही असतो. खजुराच्या चटणीतून शर्करा व खनिजे मिळतात. पुदिऱ्याच्या चटणीतून जीवनसत्ते व तंतुमय पदार्थ मिळतात. कैरी वापरली असल्यास \'सी\' जीवनसत्त्वाचे प्रमाण अधिक असते. परंतु उघड्यावर असलेल्या व अस्वच्छ हाताने बनवलेल्या भेळेत रोगकारक सूक्ष्मजीव असू शकतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83742,2457,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','रोज दूध प्यायला का सांगतात? (','','','दुधामध्ये जवळजवळ सर्वच अन्नघटक असतात. कर्बोदकांपैकी शर्करा, प्रथिने व स्निग्ध पदार्थ असतात. जीवनसत्ते \'ए\', \'बी\' व \'डी\' असतात. दुधात कॅल्शिअमचे प्रमाण जास्त असते. पचनास दूध हलके असते. दुधामुळे शरीराला पुरेशी ऊर्जा मिळते. दुधाने शरीराची वाढ व विकास जलद होतो. म्हणून रोज दूध प्यायला सांगतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83743,2457,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','काय करावे बरे?\nशरीराला पुरेशी प्रथिने मिळायला हवीत.','','','शरीराच्या बांधणीसाठी, वाढीसाठी आणि झीज भरून निघण्यासाठी भरपूर प्रमाणात प्रथिने आहारात असावी लागतात. यासाठी निरनिराळ्या प्रकारची प्रथिने जसे, अंडी, मांस, मासे हे मांसाहारी लोकांसाठी प्रथिनांचे स्रोत; शाकाहारी लोकांसाठी वेगवेणळ्या डाळी, दाणे, चणे, पनीर, टोफू, सोयाबीन हे पुरेपूर प्रथिनांचे स्रोत आहारात असले पाहिजेत. रोजच्या जेवणात मोड आलेल्या कडधान्यांचा समावेश असावा. त्यामुळे प्रथिने व जीवनसत्ते मोठ्या प्रमाणात शरीराला मिळतात. दुग्धजन्य पदार्थदेखील आहारात घ्यावेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83744,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','आहार म्हणजे काय ?','','','जगण्यासाठी आवश्यक असे अन्नघटक असणारे जेवण खाणे म्हणजेच आहार होय.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83745,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','आपल्याला कोणकोणत्या कारणांसाठी अन्नाची गरज असते ?','','','आपल्या शरीराच्या वाढीसाठी व विकासासाठी आणि शरीराची सर्व कामे सुरळीतपणे चालण्यासाठी आपल्याला अन्नाची गरज असते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83746,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','अन्नपदार्थांना कोणकोणत्या चवी असू शकतात? त्या आपल्याला कशा कळतात ?','','','गोड, कडू, खारट व आंबट या मुख्य चवी आहेत. तिखट ही चव नाही, तो जिभेचा दाह असतो. सर्व चवी आपल्याला जिभेवरील चव-ग्राहींमुळे कळतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83747,2457,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','एखाद्या खाद्यपदार्थाला गोड चव आणण्यासाठी आपण कोणकोणते अन्नपदार्थ वापरू शकतो ?','','','एखादया खाद्यपदार्थाला गोड चव आणण्यासाठी आपण साखर, गूळ, मध, फळांचे रस, खजूर, पिकलेली केळी, उसाचा रस असे विविध अन्नपदार्थ वापरू शकतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83748,2457,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','आपण जे अन्नपदार्थ कच्चे खातो, त्यांपैकी गोड लागणारे पदार्थ कोणते ? ','','','पिकलेली फळे, मध, दूध, उसाचा रस, गूळ असे पदार्थ गोड लागतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83749,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','नाकावाटे घेतलेली हवा आधी ----- जाते व नंतर फुप्फुसांत जाते.\na) श्वासनलिकेत  b) गुदद्वारात  c) मुखगुहेत  d) अन्ननलिकेत\n','','','a) श्वासनलिकेत','',0,0,0,NULL),(83750,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','फुप्फुसांतल्या हवेच्या पिशव्यांना ----- म्हणतात.\na) हृदयकोश  b) वायुकोश  c) रक्तकोश  d) ऊतककोश\n','','','b) वायुकोश','',0,0,0,NULL),(83751,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- वायू शरीराच्या सर्व भागांपर्यंत पोहोचतो.\na) कार्बन डायऑक्साइड  b) नायट्रोजन  c) ऑक्सिजन  d) हायड्रोजन\n','','','c) ऑक्सिजन','',0,0,0,NULL),(83752,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','धूम्रपानाने फुप्फुसांचा ----- हा दुर्धर रोगही होऊ शकतो.\na) मलेरिया  b) कर्करोग  c) दमा  d) क्षयरोग\n','','','b) कर्करोग','',0,0,0,NULL),(83753,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','अन्ननलिकेचे खालचे टोक म्हणजे ----- होय.\na) जठर  b) यकृत  c) गुदद्वार  d) लहान आतडे\n','','','c) गुदद्वार','',0,0,0,NULL),(83754,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','जठरात अन्न ----- जाते.\na) पचवले  b) घुसळले  c) वितळले  d) साठवले\n','','','b) घुसळले','',0,0,0,NULL),(83755,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','विष्ठा ----- जमा होते.\na) मूत्राशयात  b) लहान आतड्यात  c) मोठ्या आतड्यात  d) मलाशयात\n','','','d) मलाशयात','',0,0,0,NULL),(83756,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','काही पदार्थ शरीरात ----- काम करतात व त्यापासून शरीराला ----- मिळते.\na) इंधनाचे, ऊर्जा  b) पाण्याचे, शुद्धता  c) अन्नाचे, तंतू  d) जीवनसत्त्वाचे, बल\n','','','a) इंधनाचे, ऊर्जा','',0,0,0,NULL),(83757,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','शरीरातील निरनिराळ्या क्रियांमधील ----- साधण्याचे काम मेंदू करतो.\na) वाढ  b) हालचाल  c) समन्वय  d) नियंत्रण\n','','','c) समन्वय','',0,0,0,NULL),(83758,2459,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','जठर हे ----- इंद्रिय असते.\na) गोलाकार  b) पिशवीसारखे  c) चपटे  d) नलिकासारखे\n','','','b) पिशवीसारखे','',0,0,0,NULL),(83759,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','कशाला म्हणतात ते कंसातून शोधा व लिहा : (रक्ताभिसरण, श्वासनलिका, श्वासपटल) \nयाच्या वरखाली होणाऱ्या हालचालींमुळे श्वासोच्छ्वास होतो ','','','श्वासपटल','',0,0,0,NULL),(83760,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','कशाला म्हणतात ते कंसातून शोधा व लिहा : (रक्ताभिसरण, श्वासनलिका, श्वासपटल) \nशरीरात सतत रक्त फिरत ठेवण्याची प्रक्रिया ','','','रक्ताभिसरण','',0,0,0,NULL),(83761,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','कशाला म्हणतात ते कंसातून शोधा व लिहा : (रक्ताभिसरण, श्वासनलिका, श्वासपटल) \nनाकातून आलेली हवा या नळीत येते ','','','श्वासनलिका','',0,0,0,NULL),(83762,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','जोड्या जुळवा :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(१) फुफ्फुसे</td>\n    <td>(अ) रक्ताभिसरण</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(२) जठर</td>\n    <td>(ब) श्वसनसंस्था</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(३) हृदय</td>\n    <td>(क) चेतासंस्था</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(४) मेंदू</td>\n    <td>(ड) पचनसंस्था</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(१) फुप्फुसे - श्वसनसंस्था	\n(२) जठर - पचनसंस्था	\n(३) हृदय - रक्ताभिसरण	\n(४) मेंदू	- चेतासंस्था\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83763,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पाचकरस म्हणजे काय?','','','पचनक्रियेत खाल्लेल्या अन्नात बदल घडवून आणणान्या पदार्थांना पाचकरस असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83764,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','ग्रंथी म्हणजे काय? पचनग्रंथी कोणत्या?','','','ग्रंथी म्हणजे विशिष्ट द्रव स्रवणारे इंद्रिय. लाळग्रंथी, यकृतग्रंथी व स्वादुपिंडग्रंथी या पचनग्रंथी आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83765,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','इंद्रियसंस्था म्हणजे काय?','','','शरीराचे एखादे काम एकत्रितरीत्या पूर्ण करणान्या इंद्रियांच्या गटाला इंद्रियसंस्था असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83766,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्ताभिसरण म्हणजे काय?','','','शरीरात रक्त सतत फिरत ठेवण्याच्या क्रियेला रक्ताभिसरण असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83767,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','उत्सर्जनसंस्था कोणते कार्य करते?','','','शरीरात तयार होणारे टाकाऊ पदार्थ शरीराबाहेर टाकण्याचे कार्य उत्सर्जनसंस्था करते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83768,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चेतासंस्था कशापासून बनते?','','','मेंदू आणि चेतातंतूंचे जाळे यांना एकत्रितपणे चेतासंस्था असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83769,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','अस्थिसंस्था कोणती कार्ये करते?','','','शरीराला आधार, आकार देणे व महत्त्वाच्या आंतरंद्रियांचे संरक्षण करणे, ही कार्ये अस्थिसंस्था करते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83770,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','शरीरात कार्य करणाऱ्या संस्थांची नावे लिहा.','','','श्वसनसंस्था, पचनसंस्था, रक्ताभिसरणसंस्था, अस्थिसंस्था, उत्सर्जनसंस्था, चेतासंस्था इत्यादी शरीरात कार्य करणाऱ्या संस्थांची नावे आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83771,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','श्वास घेणे व उच्छ्वास सोडणे या क्रिया कशा होतात?','','','उरोपोकळी व उदरपोकळी यांच्या मध्ये असणारे श्वासपटल खालच्या दिशेने सरकले की श्वासावाटे नाकातून हवा शरीरात येते. येथून श्वासनलिकेमार्फत ती फुफ्फुसांत जाते. श्वासपटल वरच्या दिशेने सरकल्यावर उच्छ्वासावाटे हवा नाकातून बाहेर पडते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83772,2459,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','फुप्फुसांमध्ये ऑक्सिजन व कार्बन डायऑक्साइड वायूंची देवाणघेवाण कशी होते?','','','श्वासोच्छ्वासाच्या वेळी बाहेरील हवा वायुकोशांत पोहोचते. हवेतील ऑक्सिजन वायुकोशांच्या भोवताली असणान्या रक्तवाहिन्यांत शिरतो. येथून रक्तावाटे तो संपूर्ण शरीराला पुरवला जातो. त्याच वेळी शरीराच्या सर्व भागांतून रक्ताबरोबर आलेला कार्बन डायऑक्साइड वायू रक्तातून वायुकोशांत शिरतो. उच्छ्वास टाकला की ही अशुद्ध हवा शरीराबाहेर जाते. अशा प्रकारे फुफ्फुसांमध्ये वायुकोशांच्या आत ऑक्सिजन व कार्बन डायऑक्साइड वायूंची देवाणघेवाण होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83773,2459,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','धूम्रपानाचे आरोग्यावर कोणते वाईट परिणाम होतात?','','','धूम्रपानाने श्वसनेंद्रियांचे विविध विकार होतात. फुफ्फुसांचा कर्करोगही होतो. सिगरेट-विडीतील तंबाखूच्या धुरातील चिकट कण वायुकोशांच्या आत जमून राहतात. यामुळे शरीराला पुरेशी शुद्ध हवा मिळत नाही. त्यामुळे आरोग्यावर वाईट परिणाम होतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83774,2459,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पचनसंस्थेत कोणकोणती पचनेंद्रिये आहेत व कोणत्या ग्रंथी अन्ननलिकेला जोडलेल्या आहेत?','','','१) अन्ननलिका ही लवचीक व लांब नळी पचनसंस्थेचे काम पार पाडते. \n२) अन्ननलिकेच्या सुरुवातीला तोंड आहे व तोंडातील दात व जीभ अन्नग्रहणात मदत करतात. अन्ननलिकेचा शेवट गुदद्वारात होतो. येथून विष्ठा बाहेर टाकली जाते. \n३) या नळीचा आकार निरनिराळ्या भागात वेगवेगळा असतो. ग्रासिका, जठर, लहान आतडे व मोठे आतडे हे अन्ननलिकेचे भाग आहेत. या सर्वांना मिळून पचनेंद्रिये म्हणतात. \n४) लाळग्रंथी, यकृतग्रंथी व स्वादुपिंडग्रंथी या अन्ननलिकेला जोडलेल्या ग्रंथी आहेत.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83775,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','जठराची कार्ये लिहा.','','','जठर या पिशवीसारख्या इंद्रियात अन्न घुसळले जाते. जठरात थोड्याशा पचनक्रिया होतात. अन्नातील काही रोगजंतू इथे मारले जातात. जठरात अन्नाचे पातळ खिरीसारख्या पदार्थात रूपांतर केले जाते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83776,2459,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','लहान आतडे व मोठे आतडे यांतील फरक लिहा.','','','१) लहान आतडे सुमारे सात मीटर लांबीचे, तर मोठे आतडे सुमारे दीड मीटर लांबीचे असते. \n२) लहान आतड्यात अन्नपचनाच्या क्रिया होतात. पचन झालेले पदार्थ इथे रक्तात शोषले जातात. \n३) मोठ्या आतड्यात पचन झालेल्या व शोषले गेलेल्या अन्नातले उरलेले पाणी केवळ शोषले जाते. \n४) लहान आतड्यात पाचक ग्रंथीपासून येणारे पाचक रस अन्नात मिसळतात. मोठ्या आतड्यात असे होत नाही. \n५) लहान आतड्यातून पदार्थ मोठ्या आतड्यात जातात. मोठ्या आतड्यात विष्ठा तयार होऊन ती गुदद्वारामधून बाहेर टाकली जाते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83777,2459,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्ताभिसरण कशासाठी आवश्यक आहे?','','','रक्ताभिसरणामुळे शरीरातील सर्व भागांना ऑक्सिजन पुरवला जातो. तसेच लहान आतड्यातून शोषलेले पोषक पदार्थ रक्तावाटे शरीरभर पोहोचतात. रक्तात साचलेला कार्बन डायऑक्साइड उच्छ्वासाद्वारे बाहेर टाकण्यासाठी फुप्फुसांत नेला जातो. ऊर्जानिर्मिती होण्यासाठी अगोदर रक्ताभिसरण व्हायला लागते. म्हणून जिवंत असेपर्यंत हृदयाच्या मदतीने सतत रक्ताभिसरण होत असते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83778,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कोणत्या आंतरेंद्रियांची कामे आपल्या नकळत सतत चालू असतात? यांच्या कामांचे नियंत्रण कोणती संस्था करते?','','','श्वासपटल, हृदय, पचनसंस्था, फुफ्फुसे, मेंदू अशा अनेक आंतरेंद्रियांची कामे आपल्या नकळत सतत चालू असतात. यांचे नियंत्रण चेतासंस्था करते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83779,2459,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','विविध शारीरिक कार्यांत समन्वय कोणते आंतरेंद्रिय राखते? कसे?','','','मेंदू आपल्या शरीरातल्या निरनिराळ्या कार्यांत समन्वय राखतो. मेंदू व शरीराच्या विविध भागांमध्ये सतत संपर्क असतो. ते एकमेकांना संदेशवहन करणान्या चेतातंतूंनी जोडलेले असतात. या चेतासंस्थेच्या मदतीने मेंदू विविध कार्यांत समन्वय राखतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83780,2459,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','लाळ हा द्राव पाचकरस का आहे?','','','लाळग्रंथींनी निर्माण केलेली लाळ ही तोंडामध्ये अन्नाचा ओलसर मऊ गोळा तयार करायला मदत करते. लाळेमध्ये पिष्टमय पदार्थ पचन करणारा एक द्रव असतो. तोंडामध्ये पिष्टमय पदार्थांचे शर्करेत रूपांतर होते. म्हणून लाळेला पाचकरस म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83781,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','श्लेष्म या पदार्थाला रोजच्या भाषेत काय म्हणतात? ','','','श्लेष्म या पदार्थाला आपण रोजच्या भाषेत शेंबूड असे म्हणतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83782,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','श्वासोच्छ्वास घडवून आणणयामध्ये कोणकोणत्या इंद्रियांचा सहभाग असतो?','','','श्वासोच्छ्वास घडवून आणप्यामागे नाक, घसा, श्वासपटल, श्वासनलिका, या नलिकेच्या शाखा व उपशाखा, फुप्फुसे व त्यांतील वायुकोश या सान्या इंद्रियांचा सहभाग असतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83783,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पचनसंस्थेमध्ये कोणकोणत्या इंद्रियांचा सहभाग असतो? ','','','पचनसंस्थेमध्ये तोंडातील लाळग्रंथी, ग्रासिका, जठर, लहान व मोठे आतडे यांचा सहभाग असतो. यकृतग्रंथी व स्वादुपिंडग्रंथी या त्यांच्या विविध स्रावांनी पचनक्रिया सुलभ करतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83784,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','शरीराला ऊर्जा मिळवून देणयाच्या कामात कोणकोणत्या इंद्रियसंस्थांचा सहभाग असतो?','','','फुप्फुसे, हृदय, जठर, आतडी, मेंदू या सर्वांचाच एकत्रितरीत्या शरीराला ऊर्जा मिळवून देण्याच्या कामात सहभाग असतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83785,2459,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','भरभर जेवताना जोराचा ठसका का लागतो?','','','आपल्या शरीरात अन्ननलिका आणि श्वासनलिका यांची सुरुवातीची टोके एकमेकांजवळ आहेत. त्या दोघіमध्ये एक झडप आहे. जेळ्हा आपण गिळण्याची क्रिया करतो तेळ्हा श्वासनलिकेचे तोंड बंद राहते. पण आपण भरभर जेवत असलो तर हे अन्न श्वासनलिकेत जाते. शरीराच्या समन्वय राखण्याच्या प्रवृत्तीमुळे आपोआपच ठसका लागून हे अन्न बाहेर फेकले जाते. अशा रितीने ठसका ही सुरक्षित राहण्यासाठी होणारी क्रिया भरभर जेवताना होऊ शकते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83786,2459,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','श्वासावाटे शरीरात येणाऱ्या हवेचे शुद्धीकरण कसे होते?','','','श्वसनेंद्रियांच्या आतल्या त्वचेवर अतिशय बारीक व सूक्ष्म लव असते. यात हवेतील धुळीसारखे मोठे कण अडकतात. ही गाळलेली हवा आत जाते. आतल्या स्तरावर चिकट व बुळबुळीत श्लेष्म असते. हवेतील इतर अशुद्धी यावर चिकटून बसतात. अशा रितीने बाहेरून आत येणारी हवा शुद्ध केली जाते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83787,2459,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','आपल्याला भूक लागली आहे, आता आपण जेवले पाहिजे, हे आपल्याला कसे कळते?','','','आपल्या रक्तातील ऊर्जादायी पदार्थांचे प्रमाण कमी झाले की मेंदूला त्याबद्दल समजते. मेंदू मग आपल्याला आज्ञा देतो आणि आपल्याला भूक लागल्याची भावना होते. \'आता आपण जेवले पाहिजे\' हे आपल्याला असे कळते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83788,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','तोंडात अन्न आहे आणि त्याचे पचन होणयासाठी लाळ स्रवायला हवी हे लाळग्रंथींना कसे कळते?','','','तोंडात अन्न आहे, हे चेतासंस्थेद्वारे मेंदूला समजते व मेंदूकडून लाळग्रंथींना लाळ स्रवण्यासंबंधी आदेश येतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83789,2459,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','श्वसनसंस्था व रक्ताभिसरणसंस्थांच्या इंद्रियांची कामे सतत चालू ठेवणे आणि पचनेंद्रियांची कामे योग्य वेळीच होणे असे अचूक नियोजन कसे होते ?','','','सर्व संस्थांची कामे मेंदूच्या मदतीने समन्वय आणि नियंत्रण झाल्यामुळे होतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83790,2459,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','दुकानदार तुमचे सामान बांधत असताना तुम्ही उभे आहात. तुमच्या बाजूला उभा असलेला दादा भसाभस सिगरेट ओढत आहे. तुम्ही त्याला काय सांगाल?','','','धूम्रपान ही खूप घातक सवय आहे. तुम्हांला त्यामुळे विविध प्रकारचे श्वसनसंस्थेचे रोग होतील. इतक्या तरुण वयात तुम्ही तुमच्या फुप्फुसांची तंबाखूच्या कणांपासून हानी करीत आहात. याशिवाय तुमच्या धूम्रपानामुळे मीसुद्धा अप्रत्यक्ष धूम्रपान करीत आहे. तुम्ही इथल्या हवेचे प्रदूषण करीत आहात. सार्वजनिक ठिकाणी धूम्रपानावर निर्बंधही आहेतच. म्हणून मी तुम्हांला ताबडतोब धूम्रपान थांबवायला सांगत आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83791,2459,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','\"मदयपान करणे केंव्हाही वाईटच\" हे तुम्ही कसे पटवून द्याल?','','','मदयपान केल्यामुळे आरोग्यावर गंभीर परिणाम होतात. चेतासंस्थेवर परिणाम होऊन शरीराच्या हालचालींवर ताबा राहत नाही. मेंदूचे समन्वय कमी होते. पचनेंद्रियांच्या अंतस्त्वचेवर व्रण येतात आणि यकृत व किडनीचे (वृक्काचे) गंभीर आजार होऊ शकतात.\nमदय प्राशन करून वाहन चालवल्यास अपघात होतात. म्हणूनच मदयपान करणे हे अत्यंत घातक व्यसन आहे. अशा व्यसनाने पूर्ण कुटुंबाची वाताहत होते. अशा बाबी सांगून दुसन्यांना मदयपानाच्या दुष्परिणामांबाबत पटवून देऊ.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83792,2459,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','तुम्ही पुस्तक वाचत असताना तुमच्या शरीरात सुरू असणाऱ्या कोणकोणत्या क्रियांची तुम्हांला जाणीव होते ?','','','आपला श्वासोच्छ्‌वास आणि हृदयाची धडधड चालू असते याची अधूनमधून जाणीव होते. कधी मध्येच पोटात विचित्र गुडगुड असे आवाज येतात. म्हणजे अन्नाच्या पचनासंबंधित काही क्रिया होत असाव्यात असे वाटते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83793,2459,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','पचनसंस्थेच्या या आकृतीत बाणांनी दाखवलेल्या निरनिराळ्या भागांची नावे लिहा :','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(83794,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','स्वतःच्या हालचालींवर नियंत्रण येऊन एखादी नवीन कृती करायला येऊ लागणे याला ----- असे म्हणतात.\na) विकास  b) वाढ  c) कौशल्य शिकणे  d) कार्यक्षमता\n','','','c) कौशल्य शिकणे','',0,0,0,NULL),(83795,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','नवीन शिकलेली कामे न चुकता व सहजतेने करता येणे याला ----- असे म्हणतात.\na) समन्वय  b) कार्यक्षमता वाढणे  c) वाढ  d) कौशल्य विकास\n','','','b) कार्यक्षमता वाढणे','',0,0,0,NULL),(83796,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','एक कौशल्य आले की ----- इतरही कौशल्ये बाळाला येऊ लागतात.\na) नवीन  b) जुनी  c) मर्यादित  d) त्याच्यावर अवलंबित\n','','','d) त्याच्यावर अवलंबित','',0,0,0,NULL),(83797,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्राण्यांची पिले आपले अन्न मिळवणे व स्वतःचे रक्षण करणे ही कौशल्ये ----- शिकतात.\na) स्वतःहून  b) शिक्षकांकडून  c) आईकडून  d) मित्रांकडून\n','','','c) आईकडून','',0,0,0,NULL),(83798,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आनुवंशिकता म्हणजे -----\na) आई-वडिलांकडून शारीरिक लक्षणे येण्याची प्रक्रिया  b) कौशल्य शिकण्याची पद्धत  \nc) मानसिक विकास                                                d) व्यायाम करण्याची सवय\n','','','a) आई-वडिलांकडून शारीरिक लक्षणे येण्याची प्रक्रिया','',0,0,0,NULL),(83799,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','वाढीच्या वयात स्वतःच्या क्षमता ओळखणे आणि ----- विकसित करणे हे महत्त्वाचे आहे.\na) छंद  b) कौशल्ये  c) आहार  d) सवयी\n','','','b) कौशल्ये','',0,0,0,NULL),(83800,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चांगला आहार घेतल्यास आपल्या आनुवंशिक गुणधर्मांनुसार ----- होते.\na) चांगले आरोग्य  b) जलद विकास  c) उत्तम वाढ  d) कौशल्य विकास\n','','','c) उत्तम वाढ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83801,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','उत्तम विकास होण्यासाठी पुढीलपैकी कोणती गोष्ट आवश्यक नाही?\na) पौष्टिक अन्न  b) पुरेसा व्यायाम  c) व्यसने करणे  d) छंद जोपासणे\n','','','c) व्यसने करणे','',0,0,0,NULL),(83802,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चांगले व वाईट काम यांचा सारासार विचार करणे हे ----- विकसित करण्यासाठी आवश्यक आहे.\na) शारीरिक बल  b) व्यक्तिमत्त्व  c) अभ्यास  d) कौशल्य\n','','','b) व्यक्तिमत्त्व','',0,0,0,NULL),(83803,2460,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपल्या शारीरिक आणि मानसिक जडणघडण लक्षात घेऊन क्षमता व कौशल्ये जोपासल्याने -----\na) विकास थांबतो  b) व्यक्तिमत्त्व विकसित होते  c) वाढ कमी होते  d) आनुवंशिकता बदलते\n','','','b) व्यक्तिमत्त्व विकसित होते','',0,0,0,NULL),(83804,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : हालचालींवर नियंत्रण येऊन एखादी नवीन कृती करायला येऊ लागणे, याला अभ्यास/कौशल्य शिकणे असे म्हणतात.','','','हालचालींवर नियंत्रण येऊन एखादी नवीन कृती करायला येऊ लागणे, याला अभ्यास/<u>कौशल्य</u> शिकणे असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83805,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : शिकलेली कामे न चुकता व सहजतेने करू लागणे म्हणजे कार्यक्षमता/कौशल्य वाढणे.','','','शिकलेली कामे न चुकता व सहजतेने करू लागणे म्हणजे <u>कार्यक्षमता</u>/कौशल्य वाढणे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83806,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : वयाप्रमाणे मुला-मुलींची शारीरिक/आर्थिक ताकद वाढते.','','','वयाप्रमाणे मुला-मुलींची <u>शारीरिक</u>/आर्थिक ताकद वाढते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83807,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : प्राण्यांची पिले आपल्या आईकडून/वडिलांकडून अनेक कौशल्ये शिकतात.','','','प्राण्यांची पिले आपल्या <u>आईकडून</u>/वडिलांकडून अनेक कौशल्ये शिकतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83808,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : वयाच्या १८/२५ वर्षापर्यंत आपली उंची वाढते.','','','वयाच्या <u>१८</u>/२५ वर्षापर्यंत आपली उंची वाढते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83809,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : वाढीचे वय/म्हातारपण म्हणजे स्वतःच्या क्षमता ओळखण्याची आणि कौशल्ये विकसित करण्याची संधी असते.','','','<u>वाढीचे वय</u>/म्हातारपण म्हणजे स्वतःच्या क्षमता ओळखण्याची आणि कौशल्ये विकसित करण्याची संधी असते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83810,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','योग्य शब्दाखाली रेघ काढा : जेंव्हा आपण चांगल्या विचारांग्रमाणे आचरण करतो, तेळ्हा आपले दिसणे/व्यक्तिमत्त्व चांगले म्हणता येते.','','','जेंव्हा आपण चांगल्या विचारांप्रमाणे आचरण करतो, तेळ्हा आपले दिसणे/<u>व्यक्तिमत्त्व</u> चांगले म्हणता येते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83811,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : नव्याने शिकलेली कामे बाळ हळूहळू न चुकता करू लागते.','','','बरोबर ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83812,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : जन्मतःच आपण कौशल्ये आत्मसात केलेली असतात.','','','चूक ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83813,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : स्वतःची सर्वच कामे आपण स्वतः करीत नाही.','','','बरोबर ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83814,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ते लिहा : जन्मापासून वृद्धावस्थेपर्यंत आपली उंची वाढत राहते.','','','चूक ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83815,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने वाचा. यातल्या कोणत्या गोष्टी चांगल्या आहेत व कोणत्या वाईट आहेत, त्या लिहा : रवीला भूमिती आवडते. त्यासाठी तो माहिती मिळवतो.','','','चांगले','',0,0,0,NULL),(83816,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने वाचा. यातल्या कोणत्या गोष्टी चांगल्या आहेत व कोणत्या वाईट आहेत, त्या लिहा : गणू तंबाखू खाऊ लागला आहे.','','','वाईट','',0,0,0,NULL),(83817,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने वाचा. यातल्या कोणत्या गोष्टी चांगल्या आहेत व कोणत्या वाईट आहेत, त्या लिहा : अहमद वडिलांना सायकल दुरुस्तीत मदत करतो.','','','चांगले','',0,0,0,NULL),(83818,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने वाचा. यातल्या कोणत्या गोष्टी चांगल्या आहेत व कोणत्या वाईट आहेत, त्या लिहा : मनीषा सगळ्यांना रागावून बोलते.','','','वाईट','',0,0,0,NULL),(83819,2460,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने वाचा. यातल्या कोणत्या गोष्टी चांगल्या आहेत व कोणत्या वाईट आहेत, त्या लिहा : कैलास चित्रकलेत प्रवीण झाला आहे.','','','चांगले','',0,0,0,NULL),(83820,2460,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Text Book','','आनुवंशिकता म्हणजे काय?','','','आपल्या आईवडिलांसारखी शारीरिक लक्षणे आपल्यात जन्मतःच येणे, याला आनुवंशिकता असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83821,2460,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','बालवर्गातील मुले व पाचवीचा विद्यार्थी यांच्यात दिसणारे फरक सांगा.','','','बालवर्गातील मुले तीन-चार वर्षांची असतात. त्यांची उंची व शारीरिक ताकद कमी असते. त्यांना शाळेत एकटे जाता येत नाही. जोरजोरात धावल्यास ती पडू शकतात. त्यांची समजही कमी असते. पाचवीचा विद्यार्थी नऊ-दहा वर्षांचा असल्याने मोठा झालेला असतो. त्याची उंची, शारीरिक आणि मानसिक क्षमता वाढलेली असते. तो शाळेत एकटा येऊ-जाऊ शकतो. मैदानी खेळ खेळतो. पैशांचे हिशेब करू शकतो. त्याने बालवर्गातल्या मुलापेक्षा अनेक नवीन कौशल्ये आत्मसात केलेली असतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83822,2460,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','जन्मापासून प्रौढावस्थेपर्यंत आपल्यात कोणकोणते बदल होतात?','','','१) जन्मतः आपले वजन २ ते ३ किलोपर्यंत असते. प्रौढ होईपर्यंत ते पुरुषात ५०-६० किलो होते; तर स्त्रियांत ४५ ते ५० किलोंइतके होते. \n२) उंचीतही खूप फरक होतो. \n३) जन्मतः हाता-पायात ताकद नसते. तरुणपणी हे सर्व अवयव सक्षम होतात. निरनिराळी कामे करता येतात. \n४) मेंदूची वाढ जन्मापासून ते सहा वर्षापर्यंत होते. प्रौढावस्थेपर्यंत आपल्या मेंदूत खूप बदल होतात. \n५) वयाच्या १२ ते १४ वर्षांत आपल्यात अजून मोठे बदल होतात. मोठेपणी आई, बाबा बनण्यासाठी आवश्यक असणारे बदल शरीरात होतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83823,2460,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','शारीरिक वाढ कशाला म्हणतात?','','','शरीराच्या उंचीत आणि वजनात वाढ होणे याला शारीरिक वाढ असे म्हणतात. शरीराच्या क्षमतांत व ताकदीत पण शारीरिक वाढीमुळे फरक पडतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83824,2460,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपला उत्तम विकास होणयासाठी कशाकशाची गरज असते?','','','१) पौष्टिक अन्न \n२) पुरेसा व योग्य व्यायाम \n३) व्यसनांपासून दूर राहणे, त्यांचे वाईट परिणाम टाळणयाची दक्षता घेणे \n४) चांगले छंद जोपासणे व इतर कौशल्ये आत्मसात करणे या सर्व गोष्टींमुळे आपला उत्तम विकास होतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83825,2460,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपले व्यक्तिमत्त्व कसे घडते?','','','प्रत्येक व्यक्ती इतर सर्वांपेक्षा वेगळी असते. प्रत्येकाची शारीरिक व मानसिक जडणघडण ठरावीक प्रकारची असते. ब्याच अंशी हे आनुवंशिकतेवर अवलंबून असते. आपल्याला आवडणाया गोष्टींचा चांगला सराव केल्याने व्यक्तिमत्त्वाचा विकास होतो. वाढीच्या वयात चांगले काय व वाईट काय यांचा सारासार विचार करणे आणि नेहमी चांगल्या विचारांचे आचरण करणे यामुळे आपले व्यक्तिमत्त्व घडते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83826,2460,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','सायकल चालवायला शिकण्यापूर्वी आपल्या विविध अवयवांमध्ये कोणकोणती कौशल्ये विकसित झालेली दिसतात?','','','सायकल चालवण्यासाठी सशक्त पायांची गरज आहे. सायकलचा तोल सावरण्यासाठी सर्व शरीराला काम करावे लागते. हातांनी हँडल पकडून जेथे हवे तेथे वळवण्यासाठी चपळपणा हवा. डोळे सतत सावधानता बाळगून सायकल पुढे न्यायला मदत करतात. आजूबाजूच्या वाहनांची व माणसांची नोंद घेण्यासाठी मान त्वरित निरनिराळ्या दिशांत फिरवावी लागते. त्यामुळे हात, पाय, मान व डोळे यांच्यात ही विविध कौशल्ये विकसित व्हावी लागतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83827,2460,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','सुमनला पुढे स्वतः चे हॉटेल चालवायचे आहे. तिच्या पुढील जीवनातील कामात ती आता शिकत असलेली कोणती कौशल्ये उपयोगी पडणार आहेत?','','','हॉटेल चालवण्यासाठी सुमनला अनेक माणसांची गरज पडेल. त्यामुळे सर्वांशी जुळवून घेऊन एकत्रितरीत्या काम करण्याची क्षमता सुमनला आत्मसात करावी लागेल. अन्नपदार्थांची माहिती, स्वयंपाकाच्या विविध कृती, मालसामानाचे बाजारभाव, ग्राहकांची आवडनिवड, हॉटेलमधील वातावरण आरोग्यपूर्ण व स्वच्छ ठेवण्याची जबाबदारी या सर्व गोष्टी सुमनला भविष्यकाळात यायला पाहिजेत. त्यासाठी आता तिला अभ्यास करणे, वाणसामान आणे, जेवण करायची माहिती घेणे ही कौशल्ये उपयोगी पडतील. आतापासूनच जेळ्हा जेव्हा एखादया हॉटेलला सुमन भेट देते तेल्हा तिने हॉटेलच्या निरनिराळ्या बाबींचे निरीक्षण करणे चालू करायला पाहिजे. उद्योगशीलता, क्षमता व चिकाटी ही कौशल्ये सुमनला मोठेपणी उपयोगी पडणार आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83828,2460,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','कबीरला प्राणिशास्त्र विषयाचा प्राध्यापक व्हायचे आहे. त्यासाठी त्याने आतापासून काय तयारी करावी?','','','प्राणिशास्त्र विषयाचा अभ्यास करणयासाठी कबीरला विज्ञान विषयात आवड असली पाहिजे. निरनिराळ्या पुस्तकांतून, मासिकांतून व चित्रमालिकांमधून प्राणिजगतातील माहिती कबीरने नियमितपणे मिळवली पाहिजे. प्राण्यांचे व पक्ष्यांचे निरीक्षण करणयासाठी वेळ दिला पाहिजे. प्राणिशास्त्र विषय महाविद्यालयात विज्ञान शाखेकडे शिकवला जातो. यासाठी आतापासून त्याने गुणवत्तेवर लक्ष देऊन अभ्यास केला पाहिजे. प्राध्यापक होण्यासाठी एम. एस्सी. व पीएच. डी.ची पात्रता लागते. यासाठी दीर्घकाळ अभ्यास करणे व चिकाटीने विषय समजावून घेणे या क्षमता आवश्यक आहेत. लहानपणापासूनच कबीरने अभ्यासाची आवड लावून घ्यायला पाहिजे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83829,2460,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','सायली सहावीत आहे. तिचा वर्ग सहलीला जाणार आहे. आई सर्वांसाठी लाडू करून देणार आहे; पण तिला सायलीची मदत हवी आहे. त्यासाठी सायली आईला कोणकोणती मदत करू शकेल?','','','सायली आईला दुकानातून लाडू करायला लागणारे सामान आणून देईल. आई लाडू करीत असताना ती आईला जमेल तशी मदत करील. तयार झालेले लाडू डब्यात भरायलाही ती मदत करील.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83830,2460,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','सायली सहावीत आहे. तिचा वर्ग सहलीला जाणार आहे. आई सर्वांसाठी लाडू करून देणार आहे; पण तिला सायलीची मदत हवी आहे. त्यासाठी सायली आईला कोणकोणती मदत करू शकेल?एवढे काम करणयासाठी तिला कोणकोणती कौशल्ये उपयोगी पडणार आहेत?','','','सायलीला एवढे काम करण्यासाठी पुढील कौशल्ये उपयोगी पडतील :\n(१) एकटीने दुकानात जाणे. (२) पैशांचा हिशेब करणे. (३) योग्य त्या वस्तू ओळखून त्याच विकत घेणे. (४) आपल्याकडचे पैसे सांभाळून खरेदी करणे. (५) डब्यात लाडू भरणे.\n(३) आपल्याला कोणती कौशल्ये आवश्यक आहेत?\n(४) तुमच्यात आतापर्यंत कोणकोणती कौशल्ये विकसित झाली आहेत?\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83831,2460,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','तुम्ही बाळ असतानाचे तुमचे कपडे आणि बूट-चपला तुम्हांला आता वापरता येतील का ?','','','नाही. आपण जसजसे मोठे होतो तसतसे जुने कपडे किंवा बूट आपल्याला होत नाहीत. ते लहान होऊ लागतात. मापाला व्यवस्थित बसत नाहीत. कारण आपले शरीर वाढत असते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83832,2460,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','वाघाची/हत्तीची पिल्ले किती वर्षांची झाल्यावर स्वतंत्रपणे जगू शकतात ?\n','','','वाघाची पिल्ले दीड वर्षांची झाली की आईवर अवलंबून राहत नाहीत. परंतु अडीच वर्षांची झाली की आईपासून स्वतंत्र जगू लागतात.\nहत्तीची पिल्ले पाच ते दहा वर्षांपर्यंत आईबरोबर असतात. नंतर ती स्वतंत्रपणे जगत असतील तरीही कळपातच राहतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83833,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','शरीरात ----- प्रवेश मिळाला की रोग होतो.\na) पाणी  b) अन्न  c) रोगजंतूंना  d) हवेचा\n','','','c) रोगजंतूंना','',0,0,0,NULL),(83834,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','फ्ल्यूसारख्या रोगाचे जंतू ----- असतात.\na) पाण्यात  b) रोग्याच्या थुंकीत  c) जमिनीत  d) अन्नात\n','','','b) रोग्याच्या थुंकीत','',0,0,0,NULL),(83835,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सर्व ठिकाणी स्वच्छता पाळणे हे आरोग्यासाठी ----- असते.\na) हानिकारक  b) लाभदायक  c) व्यर्थ  d) धोकादायक\n','','','b) लाभदायक','',0,0,0,NULL),(83836,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','विशिष्ट डास चावल्याने ----- लागण होते.\na) हिवतापाची  b) फ्ल्यूची  c) क्षयरोगाची  d) सर्दीची\n','','','a) हिवतापाची','',0,0,0,NULL),(83837,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रोग होऊ नये म्हणून केलेल्या उपायांना ----- म्हणतात.\na) रोगप्रसार  b) रोगउपचार  c) रोगप्रतिबंध  d) औषधोपचार\n','','','c) रोगप्रतिबंध','',0,0,0,NULL),(83838,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','गावातील पाण्याच्या सार्वजनिक स्रोतांचे ----- वापर करून निर्जतुकीकरण केले जाते.\na) मीठ  b) चुना  c) ब्लीचिंग पावडरचा  d) साबणाचा\n','','','c) ब्लीचिंग पावडरचा','',0,0,0,NULL),(83839,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','साचलेल्या पाण्यावर डासांची निर्मिती होऊ नये म्हणून ----- वापर करतात.\na) रंग  b) कीटकनाशकांचा  c) खताचा  d) पावसाच्या पाण्याचा\n','','','b) कीटकनाशकांचा','',0,0,0,NULL),(83840,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- मुळे शरीराची रोगप्रतिकारक क्षमता विकसित होते.\na) औषधामुळे  b) लसीकरणामुळे  c) व्यायामामुळे  d) पाण्यामुळे\n','','','b) लसीकरणामुळे','',0,0,0,NULL),(83841,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मूल जन्माला आल्यावर लगेच त्याला ----- लस टोचण्यात येते.\na) पोलिओ  b) क्षयप्रतिबंधक  c) टिटॅनस  d) हिवताप\n','','','b) क्षयप्रतिबंधक','',0,0,0,NULL),(83842,2461,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रसारमाध्यमांद्वारे ----- करण्यात येते.\na) स्वच्छता  b) जनजागृती  c) उपचार  d) लसीकरण\n','','','b) जनजागृती','',0,0,0,NULL),(83843,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पुढील विधाने बरोबर की चूक ते सांगा : आतड्याच्या रोगांचा प्रसार हवेतून होतो.','','','चूक ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83844,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पुढील विधाने बरोबर की चूक ते सांगा : काही रोग दैवी प्रकोपामुळे होतात.','','','चूक','',0,0,0,NULL),(83845,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने बरोबर की चूक ते सांगा : काही सूक्ष्मजीव उपयोगी असतात.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83846,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने बरोबर की चूक ते सांगा : खोकताना, शिंकताना तोंडावर रुमाल धरू नये. त्यामुळे रुमाल खराब होतो.','','','चूक','',0,0,0,NULL),(83847,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने बरोबर की चूक ते सांगा : कोणतेही अन्नपदार्थ नेहमी झाकून ठेवावेत.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83848,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने बरोबर की चूक ते सांगा : उवा आणि पिसवा रोगप्रसार करीत नाहीत.','','','चूक','',0,0,0,NULL),(83849,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने बरोबर की चूक ते सांगा : क्षयरोग्याची थुंकी एका भांड्यात जमा केली जाते.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83850,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील विधाने बरोबर की चूक ते सांगा : रोगाच्या साथीत एकाच वेळी अनेक लोकांना रोगाचा संसर्ग होतो.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83851,2461,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','पुढे काही रोग दिले आहेत. त्यांचे अन्नातून प्रसार, पाणयातून प्रसार आणि हवेतून प्रसार असे वर्गीकरण करा : (मलेरिया, टायफॉइड, कॉलरा, क्षय, कावीळ, गॅस्ट्रो, हगवण, घटसर्प)','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>हवेतून प्रसार</th>\n    <th>अन्नातून प्रसार</th>\n    <th>पाण्यातून प्रसार</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>क्षय, घटसर्प</td>\n    <td>टायफॉइड, कॉलरा, कावीळ, गॅस्ट्रो, हगवण</td>\n    <td>हगवण, कॉलरा, टायफॉइड, कावीळ</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(83852,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','संसर्गजन्य रोग म्हणजे काय?','','','जे रोग, रोगजंतूंमुळे होतात आणि एका माणसाकडून दुसय्याला ज्या रोगांची लागण होते त्या रोगांना संसर्गजन्य रोग असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83853,2461,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','रोगप्रसाराची माध्यमे कोणती आहेत?','','','दूषित हवा, पाणी, अन्न ही रोगप्रसाराची माध्यमे आहेत. डास, पिसवा व माश्या यांसारखे कीटकही रोगप्रसार करतात. संपर्कनि होणाया त्वचारोगात अस्वच्छ कपडे व वस्तूही रोगप्रसार करू शकतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83854,2461,'descriptive','2',3,2,'Text Book','','रोगाची साथ येते तेल्हा काय होते?','','','रोगाची साथ आल्यावर एकाच ठिकाणच्या अनेक लोकांना एकाच वेळी ठरावीक संसर्गजन्य रोग होतो. रोगाच्या साथीमुळे मोठ्या प्रमाणात रोग पसरतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83855,2461,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','लसीकरण म्हणजे काय?','','','शरीरामध्ये रोगप्रतिकारक्षमता विकसित करण्यासाठी इंजेक्शनद्वारे किंवा तोंडावाटे जेव्हा लसी देण्यात येतात, तेळ्हा त्याला लसीकरण असे म्हणतात. लसीकरण केल्यामुळे रोगजंतू शरीरात शिरल्यावरही आपले शरीर त्यांना प्रतिकार करते व आपल्याला रोग होत नाही. यामुळे रोगप्रतिबंध आणि रोगप्रसारास आळा बसतो. छोटया बाळांची प्रतिकारक्षमता कमी असल्याने त्यांना विविध रोगप्रतिबंधक लसीकरण केले जाते. साथीच्या व संसर्गजन्य रोगास आळा बसण्यासाठी सामुदायिक लसीकरण करतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83856,2461,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','नवजात बालकाला देण्यात येणाऱ्या लसींची यादी करा.','','','१) क्षयप्रतिबंधक लस \n२) त्रिगुणी लस - यात घटसर्प, डांग्या खोकला व धनुर्वात या रोगांच्या प्रतिबंधक लसी असतात. \n३) तोंडावाटे देण्यात येणारी पोलिओ प्रतिबंधक लस.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83857,2461,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','मलेरियाची साथ पसरू नये म्हणून कोणते प्रतिबंधात्मक उपाय योजतात?','','','मलेरियाचे रोगजंतू डासांमार्फत पसरले जातात. म्हणून मलेरियाच्या प्रतिबंधासाठी डासांची पैदास रोखली जाते. यासाठी पाणी साचू देऊ नये किंवा साचलेल्या पाण्यावर कीटकनाशकांची फवारणी करावी.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83858,2461,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','काविळीची साथ कशी पसरते?','','','कावीळ या संसर्गजन्य रोगाचे रोगजंतू रोग्याच्या विष्ठेमध्ये असतात. अशी विष्ठा पाण्यात मिसळली की हे रोगजंतू पाण्यात पसरतात. असे दूषित पाणी दुसऱ्या निरोगी माणसाने प्यायले की त्याच्या आतड्यात रोगजंतू शिरून त्याला कावीळ होते. एकाच पाणवठ्याचे दूषित पाणी अनेकांनी प्यायले की काविळीची साथ पसरते. दूषित अन्नाने देखील काविळीचा संसर्ग होऊ शकतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83859,2461,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','क्षयरोग्याच्या बाबतीत कोणकोणती काळजी घेतली जाते?','','','क्षयरोग संसर्गजन्य आहे म्हणून अशा रोग्याला स्वतंत्र जागी ठेवतात. हॉस्पिटलमध्येही क्षयरोग्यांसाठी खास विभाग असतो. त्यांनी वापरलेली भांडी, कपडे इत्यादी जंतुनाशकाने धुतात. त्यांची थुंकी एका भांड्यात जमा करून त्यावर फिनाईलसारखे जंतुनाशक टाकले जाते. त्यांना योग्य औषधोपचार व इंजेक्शन्स दिली जातात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83860,2461,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','अन्नपदार्थ झाकून ठेवणयाचे फायदे कोणते?','','','अन्नपदार्थ झाकून ठेवल्यास त्यात कचरा-धूळ अशा गोष्टी मिसळत नाहीत. माश्यांच्या पायांनी रोगजंतू पसरवले जातात. उघड्या अन्नावर अशा माश्या बसून रोगप्रसार होतो. परंतु अन्नपदार्थ झाकून ठेवल्याने त्यात जंतू शिरत नाहीत. रोगप्रसार टाळता येतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83861,2461,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','कारणे दया : गावात कॉलऱ्याची साथ पसरली असता पाणी उकळून प्यावे.','','','कॉलरा या रोगाचे जंतू रोग्याच्या विष्ठेमार्फत पाण्यामध्ये पसरले जातात. असे पाणी प्यायल्यामुळे कॉलयाचा प्रसार होतो. गावाच्या सामाईक स्रोताचे पाणी जर कॉलय्याच्या रोगजंतूंनी दूषित झाले तर कॉलय्याची साथ पसरते. अशा वेळी पाणी उकळून प्यायल्याने त्यातील रोगजंतू नाश पावतात व आपले आरोग्य धोक्यात येत नाही, म्हणून गावात कॉलऱ्याची साथ पसरली असता पाणी उकळूनच प्यावे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83862,2461,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','कारणे दया : परिसरात पाणयाची डबकी होऊ देऊ नयेत.','','','पाण्याच्या डबक्यात डासांची पैदास होते. हिवताप या रोगाचे जंतू डास चावल्यामुळे रोगी माणसाच्या शरीरातून निरोगी माणसाच्या शरीरात पसरवले जातात. हिवतापाच्या लागणीसाठी अशा रितीने डास कारणीभूत असतात. डासांची पैदास रोखली की हिवताप व डेंग्यू यांसारख्या आजारांवर नियंत्रण करता येते, म्हणून परिसरात पाण्याची डबकी होऊ देऊ नयेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83863,2461,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे दया : यात्रेला जाताना रोगप्रतिबंधक लस टोचतात.','','','यात्रेच्या ठिकाणी अनेक लोक एकत्र येतात. यातले काही रोगजंतूंचे वाहक असतात, तर काही आजारी असतात. यात्रेत उघड्यावरचे अन्न खाल्ले जाते. सामुदायिकरीत्या एखादया नदीत स्नान केले जाते. उघड्यावर शौच केले जाते. या सर्व बाबींमुळे रोगजंतूंचा प्रसार होऊ शकतो. यात्रेकरूंचे व स्थानिक समाजाचे आरोग्य टिकवण्यासाठी आणि साथीच्या रोगांचा प्रादुर्भाव टाळण्यासाठी यात्रेला जाताना रोगप्रतिबंधक लस टोचतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83864,2461,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे दया : सार्वजनिक ठिकाणी थुंकण्यास कायदयाने मनाई आहे.','','','थुंकण्यामुळे परिसर अस्वच्छ होतो. रोगजंतूंचा प्रसार थुंकीवाटे होतो. अशा रोगांचा प्रसार होऊ नये आणि परिसरातील सर्वांचे आरोग्य चांगले राहावे यासाठी सार्वजनिक ठिकाणी थुंकण्यास कायदयाने मनाई आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83865,2461,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे दया : खोकताना व शिंकताना तोंडावर रुमाल धरावा.','','','खोकताना व शिंकताना लक्षावधी रोगजंतू हवेत पसरू शकतात. हवेमार्फत पसरणाया रोगांचा प्रसार टाळण्यासाठी खोकताना व शिंकताना तोंडावर रुमाल धरावा. आपल्या या कृतीने आपण इतरांच्या आरोग्याची काळजी घेतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83866,2461,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे दया : लहान बाळाचे लसीकरण करतात.','','','लसीकरणामुळे शरीराच्या रोगप्रतिकारक शक्तीत वाढ होते. रोगाचा प्रतिबंध झाल्याने बाळाचे संरक्षण होते. बाळाच्या शरीरात रोगजंतू शिरले तरी शरीर त्या रोगाचा प्रतिकार करते. विविध रोगांपासून अशा रितीने संरक्षण मिळण्यासाठी लहान बाळाचे लसीकरण करतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83867,2461,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','डासांची पैदास रोखण्यासाठी कीटकनाशकांची फवारणी करणयापेक्षा पाणी साचू न देणे हा अधिक चांगला उपाय आहे का?','','','डासांची पैदास झाली तर मलेरिया, डेंग्यू, हत्तीरोग अशा रोगांचा त्वरित प्रसार होऊ शकतो. यामुळेच पाणी साचू न देणे हा सर्वांत चांगला उपाय आहे. साचलेल्या पाण्यावर कीटकनाशकांची फवारणी केल्यास डास मरतीलच, परंतु आजूबाजूच्या परिसरात कीटकनाशकांमुळे प्रदूषण होईल. मुळातच साचलेले पाणी नसेल तर ही समस्या निर्माण होणार नाही. म्हणून डासांची पैदास रोखण्यासाठी कीटकनाशकांची फवारणी करण्यापेक्षा पाणी साचू न देणे हा अधिक चांगला उपाय आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83868,2461,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','काय करावे बरे? \nखूप भूक लागली आहे; परंतु अन्नपदार्थ उघड्यावर ठेवले आहेत.','','','अन्नपदार्थ उघड्यावर असतील तर त्यावर धूळ, माश्या व इतर कचरा बसलेला असेल. अशा पदार्थांत रोगजंतूंचे प्रमाण खूप असेल. असे दूषित अन्न खाल्ल्यास विविध प्रकारचे पोटाचे आजार होतील, म्हणून शक्यतो असे अन्न खाऊ नये.\nखूपच भूक लागली असल्यास वेष्टनात बंद असलेली बिस्किटे, सोलता येण्याजोगी केळ्ठ्यांसारखी फळे खावीत. अन्नपदार्थ उकळत्या तापमानाला तापवून लगेच खाल्ला तर एक वेळ चालेल. परंतु उघड्यावरचे दूषित पदार्थ खाऊ नयेत.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83869,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','तुमचे मित्र/मैत्रिणी खेळताना पडतात आणि त्यांना लागते, तेव्हा \"त्याच्याजवळ जाऊ नको, तुलाही लागेल\" असे कोणी सांगते का?\n','','','नाही. उलट मित्राला किंवा मैत्रिणीला लागले असेल, तर आपण त्यांना मदत करतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83870,2461,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','आईचे डोके दुखत आहे. तिच्याजवळ गेलात तर तुमचेही डोके दुखू लागेल, असे होते का?','','','ही. उलट आईला आराम वाटेल असे आपण काही करू शकतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83871,2461,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','आजारी व्यक्तीच्या जवळ जाऊ नका, त्यांनी वापरलेल्या भांड्यातून जेवू नका किंवा पाणी पिऊ नका, त्यांचे रुमाल, टॉवेल, कपडे वापरू नका असे केव्हा सांगितले जाते ?','','','जर आजारी व्यक्तीला झालेल्या रोगाची लागण आपल्याला होऊ शकत असेल, तर त्या वेळी त्या व्यक्तीपासून दूर राहण्यास व त्या व्यक्तीच्या वस्तू वापरू नये असे सांगण्यात येते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83872,2461,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','तुमचे डोळे आले असता तुम्ही शाळेत का जाऊ नये?','','','डोळ्यांच्या साथी पसरवणारे रोगजंतू आपल्याही डोळ्यांतून दुसऱ्यांच्या डोळ्यांवर हल्ला करू शकतात. इतरांच्या डोळ्यांचे आरोग्य राखण्यासाठी या संसर्गजन्य रोगजंतूंपासून सर्वांनी दूर राहिले पाहिजे. या रोगाच्या साथीला प्रतिबंध व्हावा म्हणून आपले डोळे आलेले असताना आपण शाळेत जाऊ नये. शिवाय आपल्या डोळ्यांनाही आराम मिळायला पाहिजे व ते लवकर बरे झाले पाहिजेत, म्हणून त्यावर ताण येता कामा नये.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83873,2462,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पदार्थांच्या विविध अवस्थांत त्यांची ----- वेगळी असते.\na) उष्णता  b) मांडणी  c) घनता  d) ऊर्जा\n','','','b) मांडणी','',0,0,0,NULL),(83874,2462,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','निसर्गात आढळणारे सर्व पदार्थ ----- असतात.\na) द्रवरूप  b) कणरूप  c) वायुरूप  d) स्थिररूप\n','','','b) कणरूप','',0,0,0,NULL),(83875,2462,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपल्या शरीरात काही पदार्थांच्या ----- ऊर्जा निर्माण होते.\na) दहनातून  b) ज्वलनातून  c) बाष्पीभवनातून  d) संघननातून\n','','','b) ज्वलनातून','',0,0,0,NULL),(83876,2462,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','गतीच्या स्वरूपातील ऊर्जेला ----- म्हणतात.\na) स्थितिज ऊर्जा  b) गतिज ऊर्जा  c) रासायनिक ऊर्जा  d) विद्युत ऊर्जा\n','','','b) गतिज ऊर्जा','',0,0,0,NULL),(83877,2462,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सूर्यप्रकाशातील ऊर्जा ----- साठवली जाते.\na) पाण्यात  b) अन्नपदार्थांत  c) खनिजांमध्ये  d) हवेतील कणांत\n','','','b) अन्नपदार्थांत','',0,0,0,NULL),(83878,2462,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आजच्या जगात ----- आणि ----- हे आपले ऊर्जेचे मुख्य स्रोत आहेत.\na) पाणी, हवा  b) सूर्य, चंद्र  c) इंधने, विद्युत  d) वारा, वाफ\n','','','c) इंधने, विद्युत','',0,0,0,NULL),(83879,2462,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- करण्यासाठी अनेक केंद्रांमध्ये इंधनांचाच वापर होतो.\na) ऊर्जानिर्मिती  b) जलशुद्धीकरण  c) विद्युतनिर्मिती  d) वायूशुद्धीकरण\n','','','c) विद्युतनिर्मिती','',0,0,0,NULL),(83880,2462,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','इंधने जाळल्यावर त्यांतील साठलेल्या ऊर्जेचे रूपांतर ----- ऊर्जेत होते.\na) गतिज  b) उष्णता  c) प्रकाश  d) ध्वनी\n','','','b) उष्णता','',0,0,0,NULL),(83881,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील पदार्थांची विभागणी त्यांच्या अवस्थांनुसार स्थायू, द्रव व वायू अशा गटांत करा : \n(खडू, पाणी, रांगोळी, धूर, ऑक्सिजन, नायट्रोजन, पेट्रोल, दूध, ऑल्युमिनिअम, लाकूड, कोळसा, केरोसीन, बर्फ, बाष्प.)','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>स्थायू</th>\n    <th>द्रव</th>\n    <th>वायू</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>खडू, रांगोळी, अॅल्युमिनियम, लाकूड, कोळसा, बर्फ</td>\n    <td>पाणी, पेट्रोल, दूध, केरोसीन</td>\n    <td>धूर, ऑक्सिजन, नायट्रोजन, बाष्प</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(83882,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील कार्यामध्ये कोणत्या प्रकारच्या ऊर्जेचा वापर होतो?\nहमीदा सायकल चालवत शाळेला येते.','','','स्नायुऊर्जा ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83883,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील कार्यामध्ये कोणत्या प्रकारच्या ऊर्जेचा वापर होतो?\nआत्या गॅसवर आमटी उकळवते.','','','उष्णता ऊर्जा (','',0,0,0,NULL),(83884,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील कार्यामध्ये कोणत्या प्रकारच्या ऊर्जेचा वापर होतो?\nसंध्याकाळी आम्ही टीक्ही पाहतो.','','','विद्युत ऊर्जा ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83885,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील कार्यामध्ये कोणत्या प्रकारच्या ऊर्जेचा वापर होतो?\nझाडांच्या फांद्या वाऱ्यावर डोलत आहेत.','','','पवन ऊर्जा ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83886,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील कार्यामध्ये कोणत्या प्रकारच्या ऊर्जेचा वापर होतो?\nभाजीची रोपे सूर्यप्रकाशात आहेत.','','','सौरऊर्जा ','',0,0,0,NULL),(83887,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढील कार्यामध्ये कोणत्या प्रकारच्या ऊर्जेचा वापर होतो? \nसौरचुलीवर अन्न शिजायला उशीर होतो आहे.','','','सौरऊर्जा','',0,0,0,NULL),(83888,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','निसर्गत पाणी कोणकोणत्या अवस्थांमध्ये आढळते?','','','निसर्गात पाणी स्थायू, द्रव आणि वायू या अवस्थांमध्ये आढळते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83889,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','डांबरगोळ्या ठेवलेल्या कपड्याला डांबरगोळ्यांचा वास कशामुळे येतो?','','','डांबरगोळ्यांचे वायुरूपातील लहान कणांत सतत रूपांतर होत असते. हे लहान कण कपड्याला चिकटतात. त्यामुळे कपड्याला डांबरगोळ्यांचा वास येतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83890,2462,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','पदार्थांची स्थायू, द्रव, वायू ही अवस्था कशावरून ठरते?','','','पदार्थांत असणाऱ्या कणांच्या मांडणीवरून त्यांच्या अवस्था ठरतात. स्थायू अवस्थांत पदार्थांचे कण जवळजवळ असतात. द्रव अवस्थेत त्यांच्यात थोडे अंतर असते. वायू अवस्थांत पदार्थांचे कण एकमेकांपासून खूप सुटे असतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83891,2462,'descriptive','2',1,2,'Text Book','','ऊर्जा कशाला म्हणतात?','','','कार्य करणाऱ्या पदार्थाच्या क्षमतेला ऊर्जा म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83892,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','पदार्थांत कोणकोणते वेगळे गुणधर्म असतात?','','','कठीणपणा, पारदर्शकता, रंग, वास, चव, पाण्यात विरघळणे असे विविध गुणधर्म निरनिराळ्या पदार्थांत असतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83893,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कोणत्या पदार्थांपासून उष्णतेच्या रूपात ऊर्जा प्राप्त होते? या पदार्थांना काय म्हणतात?','','','कोळसा, डिझेल, पेट्रोल, सी. एन. जी. या पदार्थांपासून उष्णतेच्या रूपात ऊर्जा प्राप्त होते. या पदार्थांना इंधन असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83894,2462,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','वाऱ्यातील गतिज ऊर्जेमुळे शक्य होणाऱ्या काही कार्यांची उदाहरणे लिहा.','','','पवनचक्क्यांची पाती फिरणे, शिडाच्या बोटी पाण्यातून सरकणे, आकाशातील ढग इकडून तिकडे जाणे ही वाऱ्यातील गतिज ऊर्जेमुळे शक्य असलेली काही कार्ये आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83895,2462,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','काय करावे बरे? \nपाहुणयांसाठी पटकन सरबत करायचे आहे. घरात केवळ खडीसाखर आहे.','','','खडीसाखर कुटून त्याची बारीक पावडर करावी. ही पावडर चटकन पाण्यात विरघळू शकते. त्यामुळे सरबतात अशी बारीक केलेली साखर घालता येईल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83896,2462,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','काय करावे बरे? \nमक्याच्या कणसावर लावायला मीठ हवे आहे, पण फक्त खडेमीठ उपलब्ध आहे.','','','खडेमिठाची पावडर किंवा चूर्ण करता येईल. हाताने करायचे झाल्यास खलबत्ता किंवा यंत्राने करायचे असल्यास मिक्सरचा वापर करून खडेमिठापासून बारीक कणांच्या स्वरूपातले मीठ सहज तयार करता येते. असे बारीक केलेले मीठ मक्याच्या कणसावर लावता येईल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83897,2462,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','दैनंदिन वापरात कोणकोणते पदार्थ आपण पूड स्वरूपात वापरतो त्यांची यादी करा.','','','मिरची पूड किंवा तिखट, हळद, मीठ, मिरी पूड, पिठीसाखर, निरनिराळी पिठे, चेहऱ्याला लावायची पावडर, साबण पावडर, कुंकू, अंगारा, अबीर, गुलाल, रांगोळी इत्यादी.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83898,2462,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','कपड्यांमध्ये डांबरगोळ्या (नॅप्थेलिन गोळ्या) ठेवतात. काही दिवसांनी कपड्यांना डांबरगोळ्यांचा वास का येतो?','','','डांबरगोळ्यांतील रसायने वायुरूपातील लहान कणांत सतत रूपांतरित होतात. हे कण कपड्यांना चिकटून राहिल्यामुळे कालांतराने कपड्यांना डांबरगोळ्यांचा वास येतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83899,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','स्वच्छतागृहांमध्ये डांबरगोळ्या ठेवलेल्या असतात. काही दिवसांनी त्या गोळ्यांचा आकार कशामुळे लहान होतो?','','','त्या डांबरगोळ्यांतील सूक्ष्म कण वायुरूपात जाऊन वातावरणात नाहीसे होतात. म्हणून गोळ्यांचा आकार लहान होतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83900,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','रोजच्या जीवनात आपण उष्णता ऊर्जा इतर कोणकोणत्या कामांसाठी वापरत असतो?','','','आपण उष्णता ऊर्जेचा वापर करून स्वयंपाक करतो. थंडीच्या दिवसांत शेकोटीतून उष्णता ऊर्जा मिळवतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83901,2462,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','कापराच्या वड्यांचा आकार हळूहळू कमी होताना का दिसतो? ','','','कापराची वडी स्थायू स्वरूपात असते. या स्थायूचे वायू अवस्थेत आपोआप रूपांतर होत असते. कापराची अवस्था बदलत असताना त्याच्या वायू अवस्थेतील निर्माण होणारा वास म्हणूनच हवेत पसरत असतो. अशा रितीने या वड्यांचा आकार हळूहळू कमी होत जातो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83902,2462,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','सार्वजनिक वाहनांचा उपयोग केल्याने इंधनाची बचत कशी होते?','','','प्रत्येक मोटरगाडीसाठी पेट्रोल, डिझेल किंवा सी. एन. जी. इंधनाची गरज असते. अशा अनेक गाड्या वापरल्या की तितक्या जास्त प्रमाणात इंधन संपते. या सर्व गाड्यांतील प्रवासी एकाच मोठ्या सार्वजनिक वाहनात बसले तर केवळ त्याच एका बससाठी इंधन खर्च होईल. आगगाडीचा पर्याय घेतल्यास अजूनच इंधन बचत होईल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83903,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','खिडकीच्या फटीतून उन्हाची तिरीप आली, की त्यात लहान लहान कण दिसतात. ते कशाचे असतात?','','','उन्हाच्या तिरिपीत दिसणारे कण हे वातावरणातल्या धुळीचे कण असतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83904,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','तुमच्याजवळ रांगोळी नाही. अशा वेळी तुम्ही कोणकोणते पदार्थ रंग म्हणून वापराल?','','','रवा, पीठ, खडूची पावडर असे पदार्थ रांगोळीत वापरता येतील. हळद, कुंकू, अबीर, गुलाल हे रंगीत कणदार पदार्थ आहेत. ते रंग भरण्यासाठी वापरता येतील.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83905,2462,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुस्तकातील कोणताही मजकूर फळ्यावर खडूने लिहा. लिहून झाल्यावर खडूचे निरीक्षण करा. खडूमध्ये कोणता बदल झालेला दिसला ?','','','खडू आकाराने तोकडा होतो. लिहिलेल्या बाजूने तो झिजतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83906,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','फळ्यावर लिहिलेले डस्टरने पुसा आणि डस्टर टेबलावर आपटा. तुम्हांला काय दिसले ?','','','डस्टरने फळा पुसताना फळ्यावर चिकटलेले खडूचे कण खाली पडतात. डस्टर आपटल्यावर हे कण डस्टरमधूनही उडून जातात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83907,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','अचानक पाऊस सुरू झाला, की आपण वळचणीला उभे राहतो. अंगावर पाऊस पडत नसतानाही आपण काही प्रमाणात भिजतो. असे का बरे होते ?','','','पावसाच्या पाण्याचे शिंतोडे उडतात. हे देखील पाण्याच्या कणांपासून बनलेले असतात. त्यामुळे आपण वळचणीला असूनही भिजतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83908,2462,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','अस्मिता माठ आणण्यासाठी गेली होती. तिथे तिने विकायला ठेवलेल्या अनेक वस्तू पाहिल्या.\n१) या सर्वांमधून तिला हवा होता तसा माठ तिने कशावरून ओळखला ?\n२) या सर्व वस्तू कुंभाराने कोणत्या पदार्थांपासून बनवलेल्या असतात ?\n३) पदार्थ आणि वस्तू यांतील कोणता फरक आपल्याला समजतो ?\n','','','१) अस्मिताने आकारावरून तिला हवा होता तसा माठ ओळखला.\n२) या सर्व वस्तू कुंभाराने मातीपासून बनवलेल्या असतात.\n३) वस्तू पदार्थांपासून बनवलेल्या असतात. वस्तूंना आकार व आकृती असते. पदार्थांना आकार किंवा आकृती नसते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83909,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','एक गाडी उभी आहे. तिच्यात इंधन भरले आहे. परंतु ती पुढे चालू शकत नाही. असे का ?','','','नुसते इंधन भरून गाडी चालणार नाही. या इंधनाचे ज्वलन होऊन त्यापासून गतिज ऊर्जा निर्माण झाल्यावरच गाडी चालेल.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83910,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपण खूप अंतर धावलो, तर आपल्याला थकवा येतो. आपल्याला थांबावे लागते. असे का ?','','','आपण खूप अंतर धावलो, की आपल्या शरीरातली ऊर्जा कमी होऊन आपल्याला थकवा येतो. थांबल्यावर पुन्हा ऊर्जानिर्मिती होते व आपण पुन्हा धावू शकतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83911,2462,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','औष्णिक विद्युत केंद्रात ऊर्जेचे मूळ स्रोत कोणते असतात ?','','','उष्णतेपासून विद्युतऊर्जा बनवण्यासाठी कोळशाचे ज्वलन करतात. कोळसा हे इंधन हा ऊर्जेचा मूळ स्रोत आहे. कोळसा जीवाश्मांपासून बनतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83912,2463,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','थुंकण्याने ____ होतो.\n(अ) आनंद  (ब) रोगप्रसार  (क) व्यसन  (ड) स्वच्छता\n','','','ब) रोगप्रसार','',0,0,0,NULL),(83913,2463,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मद्यपानामुळे मेंदूवरील ____ ढळते.\n(अ) रक्त  (ब) आरोग्य  (क) नियंत्रण  (ड) विचार\n','','','क) नियंत्रण','',0,0,0,NULL),(83914,2463,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','व्यसनांपासून दूर राहण्यासाठी काही ____ जोपासावे.\n(अ) मित्र  (ब) छंद  (क) खेळ  (ड) पदार्थ\n','','','ब) छंद','',0,0,0,NULL),(83915,2463,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मनाच्या खंबीरपणामुळे व्यसनापासून ____ राहता येते.\n(अ) जवळ  (ब) सोबत  (क) दूर  (ड) संबंधित\n','','','क) दूर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83916,2463,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सामाजिक व ____ आरोग्याविषयी दक्ष असावे.\n(अ) आर्थिक  (ब) वैयक्तिक  (क) सार्वजनिक  (ड) कौटुंबिक\n','','','क) सार्वजनिक','',0,0,0,NULL),(83917,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पुढील वाक्य चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून लिहा : प्रदूषण, अस्वच्छता, साथीचे आजार, व्यसनाधीनता, कीटकदंशापासून होणारे आजार हे सामाजिक आरोग्य चांगले बनवतात.','','','चूक. (प्रदूषण, अस्वच्छता, साथीचे आजार, व्यसनाधीनता, कीटकदंशापासून होणारे आजार हे सामाजिक आरोग्य धोक्यात आणतात.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83918,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पुढील वाक्य चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून लिहा : सार्वजनिक ठिकाणी थुंकण्यास कायदयाने मनाई केली आहे.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83919,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पुढील वाक्य चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून लिहा : पोषक आहार, वैयक्तिक स्वच्छता, व्यायाम आणि छंदाची जोपासना यांतून उत्तम आरोग्य मिळते.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(83920,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','पुढील वाक्य चूक की बरोबर ते सांगा. चुकीचे विधान दुरुस्त करून लिहा : आपले आरोग्य उत्तम प्रकारे सांभाळून निरामय जीवन जगता येत नाही.','','','चूक. (आपले आरोग्य उत्तम प्रकारे सांभाळून निरामय जीवन जगता येते.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(83921,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चुकीच्या विधानातील एक शब्द बदलून वाक्य बरोबर करा : आरोग्याबाबत जागरूक असणे अनावश्यक आहे.','','','आरोग्याबाबत जागरूक असणे <u>आवश्यक</u> आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83922,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चुकीच्या विधानातील एक शब्द बदलून वाक्य बरोबर करा : सर्वांना तणावग्रस्त जगणे आवडते.','','','सर्वांना तणाव<u>रहित</u> जगणे आवडते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83923,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चुकीच्या विधानातील एक शब्द बदलून वाक्य बरोबर करा : तंबाखूयुक्त पदार्थ खाणे आरोग्यास चांगले आहे.','','','तंबाखूयुक्त पदार्थ खाणे आरोग्यास <u>घातक</u> आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83924,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चुकीच्या विधानातील एक शब्द बदलून वाक्य बरोबर करा : मद्यपानामुळे स्फूर्ती येते.','','','मद्यपानामुळे <u>गुंगी</u> येते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83925,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पुढे दिलेल्या चुकीच्या विधानातील एक शब्द बदलून वाक्य बरोबर करा : पोषणामुळे बालमृत्यूंच्या प्रमाणात वाढ होते.','','','<u>कु</u>पोषणामुळे बालमृत्यूंच्या प्रमाणात वाढ होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83926,2463,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','उत्तम आरोग्य कसे मिळवता येते?','','','पोषक आहार, वैयक्तिक स्वच्छता, व्यायाम आणि छंदांची जोपासना यांतून उत्तम आरोग्य मिळवता येते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83927,2463,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','सामाजिक आरोग्य धोक्यात आणणारे घटक कोणते?','','','स्वच्छतेच्या सवयींबाबतचा बेफिकीरपणा, व्यसनाधीनता, प्रदूषण, कुपोषण इत्यादी कारणांमुळे सामाजिक आरोग्य धोक्यात येते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83928,2463,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','तंबाखू खाणयाचे घातक परिणाम कोणते?','','','१) तंबाखूसेवनाने तोंडात व्रण पडतात. त्यांच्या मोठ्या जखमा होऊन नंतर गाठी होतात.\n२) तोंडामध्ये कर्करोगाचा त्रास सुरू होतो. \n३) तंबाखू पोटात गेला तर अन्ननलिकेचाही कर्करोग होतो. पोटाच्या इतर तक्रारीदेखील सुरू होतात. \n४) कर्करोगाच्या औषधोपचाराचाही त्रास होतो. ब्याचदा मृत्यू संभवतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83929,2463,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','मद्यपानाचे घातक परिणाम कोणते?','','','१) मद्यपानाचा पहिला परिणाम मेंदूवर होतो. गुंगी येऊन मेंदूवरील नियंत्रण ढळते. आपल्या इच्छेनुसार हालचाली करता येत नाहीत. \n२) यकृत, आतडी, वृक्क (किडनी) आणि मूत्राशयाचे विकार जडतात. यकृताचा कर्करोग संभवतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83930,2463,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','कुटुंबावर व्यसनाधीनतेचा परिणाम कसा होतो?','','','व्यसनी माणसाच्या घरातील इतरांना हालअपेष्टा सहन कराव्या लागतात. व्यसनी माणसाला औषधोपचार करावा लागतो, त्यात पैसा व वेळ वाया जातो. कुटुंबात आनंद व आरोग्य उरत नाही. व्यसनांमुळे संपूर्ण कुटुंबच उद्ध्वस्त होऊ शकते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83931,2463,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','सामाजिक आरोग्याची जोपासना कशी करावी?','','','१) सामाजिक आरोग्याची जोपासना करण्यासाठी समाजाचे आरोग्य आणि सार्वजनिक स्वच्छता या दोन गोष्टी राखल्या पाहिजेत. \n२) प्रदूषण, अस्वच्छता, साथीचे आजार, उपद्रवी कीटकांचे प्रमाण आणि व्यसनाधीनता या बाबींचे समूळ निर्मूलन झाले पाहिजे. \n३) सर्वसामान्य जनतेचे आजारांपासून संरक्षण करून त्यांना आनंदी व स्वास्थ्यपूर्ण जीवन जगता आले पाहिजे. अशा रितीने सामाजिक आरोग्याची जोपासना करतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83932,2463,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','चांगल्या आरोग्यासाठी आपण काय करावे?','','','चांगल्या आरोग्यासाठी उत्तम पोषक आहार व शुद्ध पाणी यांचाच वापर व्हावा. वैयक्तिक स्वच्छता आणि सामाजिक स्वच्छतेच्या चांगल्या सवयी अंगात बाणवाव्यात. कोणतेही वाईट व्यसन करू नये. मद्यपान किंवा तंबाखूसेवनापासून नेहमी दूर राहावे. स्वभावात पण निरामयता असावी. योग्य तितका व्यायाम करावा. आपले चांगले छंद जोपासण्यासाठी थोडा वेळ द्यावा. आपल्याबरोबर समाजाचेही आरोग्य राखावे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83933,2463,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','सामाजिक निरामयतेमुळे कोणते फायदे होतात?','','','सामाजिक निरामयतेमुळे सर्वांचे आरोग्य चांगले राहते. एकमेकांत द्वेष, मत्सर नसल्याने बंधुभाव व मित्रत्व वाढते. सर्वचजण आनंदी व उत्साही राहतात. निरामय समाजात ताणतणाव कमी असतात. निरामयतेने सर्वच जण पर्यावरणाचे रक्षण करतात. संपूर्ण समाज स्वास्थ्यपूर्ण होतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83934,2463,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','व्यसनांपासून दूर राहण्यासाठी काय करावे?','','','१) अंगात चांगल्या सवयी बाणवणे, आनंदी व उत्साही राहणे या बाबी व्यसनांपासून दूर राहण्यासाठी आवश्यक आहेत. \n२) मनावर संपूर्ण संयम असला आणि व्यसनाधीन व्यक्तींचा आपल्यावर प्रभाव पडू दिला नाही, तर व्यसनांपासून दूर राहता येते. \n३) खेळाने व व्यायामानेदेखील व्यसनांना दूर ठेवता येते. \n४) विविध छंद जोपासले तर मन चांगल्या गोष्टींत गुंतते. \n५) व्यसनांचे गांभीर्य पूर्णपणे माहीत असावे. व्यसनांना ठामपणे नकार द्यावा. अशा सर्व बाबींमुळे व्यसनांपासून दूर राहता येईल.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(83935,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सार्वजनिक जागी थुंकण्यास कायदयाने मनाई का केली आहे?','','','थुंकण्यामुळे रोगजंतू फैलावतात. रोगप्रसार होऊ नये आणि सामाजिक स्वच्छता राखली जावी यांसाठी सार्वजनिक जागी थुंकण्यास कायद्याने मनाई आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83936,2463,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','तंबाखूयुक्त पदार्थ कोणते?','','','तंबाखू, गुटखा, सिगारेट, विडी, मशेरी, मावा, तपकीर व पानमसाला असे अनेक तंबाखूयुक्त पदार्थ आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83937,2463,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','एखादया व्यक्तीला तंबाखूचे व्यसन लागले असे कधी म्हणतात?','','','तंबाखू खाल्ल्याशिवाय चैन न पडणे, कोणत्याही कामात लक्ष न लागणे, तोंडात सततच तंबाखू असणे असे जेव्हा होते तेल्हा एखादया व्यक्तीला तंबाखूचे व्यसन लागले असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83938,2463,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','व्यसने सोडवणयास (व्यसनमुक्तीसाठी) काय करतात?','','','व्यसने सोडवण्यासाठी वैद्यकीय मदत घ्यावी, चांगले छंद जोपासावेत व योग्य असा व्यायाम करावा.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83939,2463,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','तरुण वयातील मुला-मुलींचे आयुष्य उद्ध्वस्त करणारे व्यसन कोणते?','','','कोकेन, गर्द, हेरॉईन अशा अमली पदार्थांचे सेवन करणे, हे तरुण वयातील मुला-मुलींचे आयुष्य उद्ध्वस्त करणारे व्यसन आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83940,2463,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : आपण आपले आरोग्य सांभाळले की सर्वांचे जीवन आनंदी होते.','','','आपण आपले आरोग्य सांभाळतो तेव्हाच समाजाचे आरोग्यही चांगले राहते. व्यक्तिगत आरोग्य आणि स्वच्छतांच्या सवयीतून समाजाचे आरोग्य आणि सार्वजनिक स्वच्छता राखली जाते. सर्वांचेच जीवन आनंदी, उत्साही आणि निरामय होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83941,2463,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : प्रदूषण, साथीचे आजार हे सामाजिक आरोग्य धोक्यात आणतात.','','','प्रदूषणामुळे श्वसनाचे व पोटाचे विविध विकार होऊ शकतात. आरोग्य चांगले राहत नाही. अस्वच्छता आणि कीटकदंशांमुळे निरनिराळ्ठया रोगांच्या साथी पसरू शकतात. एकाच वेळी अनेकांचे आरोग्य धोक्यात येते. मृत्यूही संभवतात. अशा रितीने प्रदूषण व साथीचे आजार पूर्ण समाजाचेच आरोग्य धोक्यात आणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83942,2463,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','कारणे लिहा : व्यसनांपासून दूर राहावे.','','','व्यसने वैयक्तिक आरोग्याचा नाश करतात. कर्करोगासारखे दुर्धर आजार तंबाखूसेवनाने किंवा मदयपानाने होतात. या व्यसनाचे परिणाम कुटुंबीयांना पण सहन करावे लागतात. कुटुंबात आरोग्यपूर्ण आणि आनंददायी वातावरण राहत नाही. तंबाखूच्या व्यसनाने सामाजिक स्वच्छता धोक्यात येते. तसेच मद्यपानाने संपूर्ण कुटुंब उद्ध्वस्त होते. अमली पदार्थांच्या सेवनाने तरुण वयातच आयुष्य उद्ध्वस्त होते. इतके घातक परिणाम होत असल्यामुळेच व्यसनांपासून पूर्णतः दूर राहावे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(83943,2463,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपल्यापैकी अनेकांना पुढे नमूद केलेल्या सवयींपैकी काही सवयी असतात. त्या सवयींचे चांगल्या सवयी, वाईट सवयी, निरुपयोगी सवयी अशा तीन गटांत वर्गीकरण करा :\n१) आपण कोठे जात आहोत व परत केन्हा येणार हे घरी सांगून बाहेर जाणे.\n२) नेहमी खरे बोलणे.\n३) वेणी, रिबीन किंवा मोबाइलशी सतत खेळणे.\n४) नखे कुरतडणे.\n५) वेळापत्रकानुसार शाळेचे दप्तर आदल्या दिवशी रात्रीच भरून ठेवणे.\n६) छत्री, रेनकोट, पेन्सिल, पेन घरी किंवा शाळेत वारंवार विसरणे.\n७) टीक्ही पाहण्याची वेळ ठरवणे आणि तेवढाच वेळ टीक्ही पाहणे.\n','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>चांगल्या सवयी</th>\n    <th>वाईट सवयी</th>\n    <th>निरुपयोगी सवयी</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>\n      (१) आपण कोठे जात आहोत व परत केव्हा येणार हे घरी सांगून बाहेर जाणे<br>\n      (२) नेहमी खरे बोलणे<br>\n      (३) वेळापत्रकानुसार शाळेचे दप्तर आदल्या दिवशी रात्रीच भरून ठेवणे<br>\n      (४) टीव्ही पाहण्याची वेळ ठरवणे आणि तेवढाच वेळ टीव्ही पाहणे\n    </td>\n    <td>\n      (१) नखे कुरतडणे<br>\n      (२) छत्री, रेनकोट, पेन्सिल, पेन घरी किंवा शाळेत वारंवार विसरणे\n    </td>\n    <td>\n      वेणी, रिबीन किंवा मोबाइलशी सतत खेळणे\n    </td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(83944,2463,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','सामाजिक आरोग्य कशामुळे धोक्यात येते ?','','','सामाजिक आरोग्य अनेक कारणांमुळे धोक्यात येते. स्वच्छतेच्या सवयींबाबतचा निष्काळजीपणा हे त्यांपैकी एक महत्त्वाचे कारण होय. ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86029,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj founded an independent and ____ Swaraj.\n(A) sovereign  (B) dominant  (C) dependant\n','','','A) sovereign','',0,0,0,NULL),(86030,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','With his ____ skill, Shivaji Maharaj drew around himself brave people who were also extremely loyal.\n(A) fighting  (B) organisational  (C) orthodox\n','','','B) organisational','',0,0,0,NULL),(86031,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','At the time of the meeting with Afzalkhan, ____ was a trusted servant in Shivaji Maharaj\'s army.\n(A) Khafikhan  (B) Daulatkhan  (C) Siddi Ibrahim\n','','','C) Siddi Ibrahim','',0,0,0,NULL),(86032,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj followed a ____ religious policy.\n(A) intolerant  (B) tolerant  (C) liberal\n','','','B) tolerant','',0,0,0,NULL),(86033,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ has written a book about the greatness of Shivaji Maharaj.\n(A) Lala Lajpatrai  (B) Lokmanya Tilak  (C) Mahatma Jotirao Phule\n','','','A) Lala Lajpatrai','',0,0,0,NULL),(86034,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Lokmanya Tilak brought about national awakening through the medium of ____ celebrations.\n(A) Diwali  (B) Mahashivaratri  (C) Shivajayanti\n','','','C) Shivajayanti','',0,0,0,NULL),(86035,1680,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The one who killed Bada Sayyad at a critical moment during the meeting with Afzalkhan.','','','Jiva Mahala','',0,0,0,NULL),(86036,1680,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : An important officer in the navy of Swaraj.','','','Daulatkhan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86037,1680,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : He created an independent kingdom in Bundelkhand.','','','Chhatrasal','',0,0,0,NULL),(86038,1680,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : He composed a Powada on the greatness of Shivaji Maharaj.','','','Mahatma Jotirao Phule','',0,0,0,NULL),(86039,1680,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : A Tamil poet who composed a poem on Shivaji Maharaj.','','','Subramanyam Bharati','',0,0,0,NULL),(86040,1680,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The one who has written a long poem on Shivaji Maharaj.','','','Vishwakavi Rabindranath Tagore','',0,0,0,NULL),(86041,1680,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the table : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Column \'A\'</th>\n      <th>Column \'B\'</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>____</td>\n      <td>crossed the siege of Panhalagad in the disguise of Shivaji Maharaj</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Bajiprabhu Deshpande</td>\n      <td>........................................</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>........................................</td>\n      <td>defended the fort of Purander</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Tanaji Malusare</td>\n      <td>---------------</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Column \'A\'</th>\n      <th>Column \'B\'</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>1. Shiva Kashid</td>\n      <td>a. crossed the siege of Panhalagad in the disguise of Shivaji Maharaj</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>2. Bajiprabhu Deshpande</td>\n      <td>b. blocked the advances of the enemy on the way to Vishalgad</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>3. Murarbaji Deshpande</td>\n      <td>c. defended the fort of Purander</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>4. Tanaji Malusare</td>\n      <td>d. sacrificed his life while conquering Sinhagad</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(86042,1680,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which powers dominated Maharashtra, before the foundation of Swaraj?','','','Before the foundation of Swaraj, Maharashtra was under the domination of powers like the Adilshahi, Siddi, Portuguese and the Mughals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86043,1680,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','What grave dangers did Shivaji Maharaj face in his life?','','','Events like the meeting with the Afzalkhan, the siege of Panhala, the attack on Shaistakhan, the escape from Agra were grave dangers faced by Shivaji Maharaj in his life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86044,1680,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Who risked their life at the time of Maharaj\'s escape from Agra?','','','Hiroji Farjand and Madari Meheter risked their life at the time of Maharaj\'s escape from Agra.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86045,1680,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What arrangements had Shivaji Maharaj made regarding the payment of the salaries to his army?','','','Shivaji Maharaj was extremely particular about paying the salaries to the army on time so he had made arrangements to pay the salaries of soldiers in cash instead of making payments in the form of jagirs.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86046,1680,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What had Shivaji Maharaj ordered regarding the waste management on the forts?','','','Regarding the waste management on the forts, Shivaji Maharaj ordered that the garbage on the fort should be burnt in an allotted space without dumping it elsewhere, and vegetables should be grown on its ash.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86047,1680,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who has praised Shivaji Maharaj\'s achievements in his book \'Shivaji and His Times\'?','','','Sir Jadunaath Sarkar has praised Shivaji Maharaj\'s achievements in his book \'Shivaji and His Times\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86048,1680,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What had Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru said about Shivaji Maharaj?','','','Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru had said about Shivaji Maharaj that Maharaj did not belong to Maharashtra alone, he belonged to the whole Indian nation. ... he was a symbol of many virtues, more especially of love of country.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86049,1680,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','What inspiration can future generations draw from the life of Shivaji Maharaj?','','','Future generations can draw inspiration from the life of Shivaji Maharaj as to how Shivaji Maharaj founded Swaraj and turned it into a Surajya, a kingdom that sought the welfare of all.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86050,1680,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What warning did Shivaji Maharaj issue to the Deshmukh of Rohida Valley?','','','i. Shivaji Maharaj constantly struggled against his enemies in his quest of establishing the Swaraj. His subjects also suffered a lot because of such enemy invasions.\nii. During these times, Maharaj took the utmost care to protect his ryot (people).\niii. During Shaistakhan\'s campaign, he had strictly warned the Deshmukh of the Rohida valley to visit every village and shift people to a safe place down the ghats, without any delay.\niv. He further warned him that if he failed to do so, the Mughals would take the people prisoner and it would be seen as his wrong doing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86051,1680,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain Shivaji Maharaj\'s policy regarding the army.','','','i. Shivaji Maharaj maintained a strict discipline in his army.\nii. He was extremely particular about timely payment of their salaries. The salaries were paid in the form of cash rather than in the form of jagirs.\niii. He strictly ordered the army that all valuables collected by them during their enemy campaigns had to be deposited with the Government.\niv. He honoured the soldiers for their bravery in any campaign, looked after the maintenance of the families of those who died in battle, took care of his own wounded soldiers and also the enemy soldiers who were captured or had surrendered.\nThus, Shivaji Maharaj followed the principles of discipline, honesty and humanity in his policy regarding the army.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86052,1680,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What warning did Shivaji Maharaj issue to his soldiers to avoid any loss to the ryot?','','','i. Shivaji Maharaj cared about his ryot and took every measure to prevent any loss to them. He issued strict warnings to his soldiers for their safety.\nii. Generally, in case of a war, during the sowing-growing-reaping period of crops, the movements of army interfered with the work of farmers, causing a huge loss to the farmers.\niii. Moreover, soldiers would cut off and take away the standing crops or would destroy them and rob the houses of the farmers. Shivaji Maharaj prohibited such actions ensuring that his soldiers caused no harm to his ryot.\niv. Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj also wrote a letter in 1674 CE to his military officers regarding this. This shows how minutely he thought about the welfare of the ryot under his Swaraj.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86053,1680,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','How do we know that the religious policy of Shivaji Maharaj was that of tolerance?','','','The following instances show that the religious policy of Shivaji Maharaj was that of tolerance:\ni. In spite of Shivaji Maharaj\'s enemies being Islamic powers, he considered the Muslims in the Swaraj as his own subjects and trusted them deeply.\nThe following are a testimony to the same:\na. Siddi Ibrahim was a trusted servant in his army.\nb. Siddi Hilal was a Sardar in his army.\nc. Daulatkhan was an important officer in the navy of Swaraj.\nii. He even continued the facilities given to the Muslim places of worship that belonged to the captured enemy territories.\niii. The written work by historian Khafikhan on the tolerant religious policy of Shivaji Maharaj states that: \"Shivaji Maharaj had framed strict rules for his soldiers that during the campaigns, they should not harm a mosque. In case, if they came in possession of a copy, of the Holy Quran, they should hand over the same, with reverence, to a Muslim.\"\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86054,1680,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Shivaji Maharaj\'s efforts to establish the Swaraj prove to be an inspiration for freedom of many other kingdoms?','','','i. Shivaji Maharaj\'s efforts to establish the Swaraj were aimed at establishing and maintaining an independent and sovereign existence without accepting the dominance of any other foreign power like Adilshahi, Siddi, Portuguese or the Mughals.\nii. In this struggle of his, Maharaj even inspired others for freedom.\niii. When Chhatrasal - a Mughal servant - met Maharaj, he was inspired by Maharaj to create an independent kingdom in Bundelkhand.\niv. Shivaji Maharaj was also a great source of inspiration for our national movement of independence.\nThis displays that Maharaj\'s efforts to establish the Swaraj proved to be an inspiration for freedom of many other kingdoms.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86055,1680,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is Shivaji Maharaj considered as an ideal ruler?','','','Shivaji Maharaj is a true inspiration for generations to come. He is considered to be an ideal ruler because:\ni. Shivaji Maharaj was very intelligent and had mastered many arts, languages and scripts.\nii. His parents\' teachings of ethics and the establishment of Swaraj were deeply instilled in his mind. His personality was a blend of character, strength, nobility and valour.\niii. He displayed innumerable qualities like leadership, management, foresight, political diplomacy and effective policy about regional and military administration, commitment to truth and justice, attitude of equality.\niv. Also, he possessed skills like visualisation of future activities, the skill of seeing his planned ventures through, rising over the calamities without giving up, alertness, vigilance, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86056,1680,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which actions of Shivaji Maharaj show his revolutionary aspects in the field of religion?','','','i. Shivaji Maharaj followed a tolerant religious policy. He equally respected the people of other religions as he respected the people of his own religion.\nii. In spite of an opposition to a reconversion at that time, Shivaji Maharaj brought back the act of swadharma i.e the persons who had converted their original religion to another religion.\niii. At times of religious opposition to crossing the seas, he built sea forts like Singhudurg and created a navy. This was a stratergy of countering foreign invasions through the sea.\niv. Through his coronation, he officially became the ruler of Swaraj, but later, had a second coronation from a different religious perspective.\nThe above actions of Shivaji Maharaj show his revolutionary aspects in the field of religion.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86057,1680,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The organisational skills of Shivaji Maharaj.','','','i. Shivaji Maharaj possessed unparalleled organisational skill which enabled him to draw around himself brave and extremely loyal people.\nii. This was the reason the close associates of Maharaj risked their lives and performed their duty in the founding of the Swaraj.\niii. Some of these associates were Jiva Mahala, Shiva Kashid, Bajiprabhu Deshpande, Murarbaji Deshpande, Tanaji Malusare, Hiroji Farjand and Madari Meheter. Maharaj too, cared deeply for his associates.\niv. This can be seen from an instance when the ageing Kanhoji Jedhe - Maharaj\'s old associate in the work of the Swaraj - fell ill. Maharaj told Kanhoji Jedhe never to neglect his medicines or treatment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86058,1680,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The literature written on Shivaji Maharaj.','','','Shivaji Maharaj was a great source of inspiration for the people and many influencial personalities. This can be seen from their works and the very mention of Chattrapati Shivaji Maharaj in it. Some of these works are:\ni. Mahatma Jotirao Phule, during his struggle for equality, described the greatness of Shivaji Maharaj in his powadas.\nii. Lokmanya Tilak brought about national awakening via Shivajayanti celebrations.\niii. Lala Lajpatrai has written a book on the greatness of Shivaji Maharaj.\niv. Poets like Subramanyam Bharati and Vishwakavi Rabindranath Tagore have written poems about Shivaji Maharaj.\nv. Sir Jadunaath Sarkar has wrote a book - \'Shivaji and His Times\' - about Maharaj\'s achievements.\nAll Indian languages have works that talk about Shivaji Maharaj as an ideal ruler and a source of inspiration.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86059,1680,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj’s Struggle and Foundation of Swaraj','','','Before the foundation of Swaraj, Maharashtra was under the control of the Adilshahi, Siddi, Portuguese, and Mughal powers. Shivaji Maharaj fought bravely against all of them and established an independent and sovereign Swaraj, which he later transformed into Surajya—a kingdom devoted to the welfare of all.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86060,1680,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Organisational Skills of Shivaji Maharaj','','','Shivaji Maharaj had excellent organisational skills. He inspired people to work for Swaraj and gathered around him loyal and brave associates like Jiva Mahala, Shiva Kashid, Bajiprabhu Deshpande, Tanaji Malusare, and others. He also cared deeply for their well-being, even giving personal instructions for their health and safety.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86061,1680,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj’s Care for the Ryot (People)','','','Maharaj always protected the common people (ryot) from enemy invasions. He ordered local officers to move people to safe places during attacks and ensured that his soldiers never harmed civilians. He believed that protecting the ryot was a ruler’s primary duty.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86062,1680,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Religious Tolerance and Policies','','','Shivaji Maharaj followed a policy of religious tolerance. He respected Muslims living in his kingdom, employed them in important positions, and protected mosques and copies of the Holy Quran during campaigns. His fair and respectful treatment of all religions showed his broad-mindedness and humanity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86063,1680,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Greatness and Inspiration of Shivaji Maharaj','','','Shivaji Maharaj was not only a brave warrior but also a visionary leader, administrator, and reformer. His qualities—like justice, courage, foresight, and equality—made him a symbol of national pride. His life inspired great leaders like Mahatma Phule, Lokmanya Tilak, Rabindranath Tagore, and Pandit Nehru, who considered him a hero of the entire Indian nation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86064,1680,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maharaj\'s greatness is comprehensive.','','','i. Shivaji Maharaj had many brave and loyal associates with him. Inspite of that, he independently faced many adversities and threats to Swaraj. Seeing his courage and valour, his associates were inspired to sacrifice their lives for Swaraj.\nii. His greatness transcended the quality of courage. He also aspired for an ethical and a qualitative base for the Swaraj.\niii. He paid attention to even minor issues along with the important ones, and gave orders regarding them. e.g.: The order that prohibited soldiers from taking vegetables from the fields of the peasants by force.\niv. He also imposed restrictions on the cutting of trees.\nv. He severely punished those who would misbehave with women and took care of all sections of the society like farmers, craftsmen, soldiers, traders, merchants, etc.\nvi. He was not just a great warrior, but also a great sculptor who created a new, independent, ethical and cultured society.\nHence, we can say that Maharaj\'s greatness is comprehensive.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86065,1680,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj is called the founder of Swaraj.','','','Because he fought against powerful rulers like the Adilshahi, Siddis, Portuguese, and Mughals, and established an independent and sovereign kingdom that worked for the welfare of the people.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86066,1680,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj’s army was disciplined and loyal.','','','Because Maharaj maintained strict discipline, paid soldiers’ salaries in cash and on time, and honoured brave warriors. He also cared for the families of those who died in battle, which earned him their loyalty.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86067,1680,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj is known for his religious tolerance.','','','Because he respected people of all religions, employed Muslims in high positions, and gave orders to protect mosques and the Holy Quran during military campaigns.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86068,1680,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj is considered a great administrator.','','','Because he established a just, efficient, and welfare-oriented administration, cared for the ryot, enforced law and order, and created a system that ensured equality and prosperity for all.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86069,1680,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj is regarded as an inspiration for freedom.','','','Because his struggle for Swaraj encouraged others, like Chhatrasal of Bundelkhand, to fight for independence. His efforts symbolized patriotism, courage, and self-rule, inspiring future generations and national leaders.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86070,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Before the foundation of Swaraj, Maharashtra was under the domination of -----\n(A) British, French and Dutch  (B) Adilshahi, Siddi, Portuguese and Mughal  (C) Nizam, Marathas and British\n','','','B) Adilshahi, Siddi, Portuguese and Mughal','',0,0,0,NULL),(86071,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj turned Swaraj into a Surajya because -----\n(A) He wanted to conquer more lands  (B) He ruled for the welfare of all people  (C) He wanted to build more forts\n','','','B) He ruled for the welfare of all people','',0,0,0,NULL),(86072,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- killed Bada Sayyad during the meeting with Afzalkhan.\n(A) Tanaji Malusare  (B) Jiva Mahala  (C) Baji Prabhu Deshpande\n','','','B) Jiva Mahala','',0,0,0,NULL),(86073,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- sacrificed his life while conquering Sinhagad.\n(A) Murarbaji Deshpande  (B) Tanaji Malusare  (C) Hiroji Farjand\n','','','B) Tanaji Malusare','',0,0,0,NULL),(86074,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj paid his soldiers -----\n(A) In the form of jagirs  (B) In cash  (C) In land grants\n','','','B) In cash','',0,0,0,NULL),(86075,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj maintained strict ----- in his army.\n(A) Discipline  (B) Secrecy  (C) Training rules\n','','','A) Discipline','',0,0,0,NULL),(86076,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj followed a policy of ----- towards other religions.\n(A) Hatred  (B) Tolerance  (C) Superiority\n','','','B) Tolerance','',0,0,0,NULL),(86077,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','----- was a trusted Muslim officer in Shivaji Maharaj’s army.\n(A) Daulatkhan  (B) Shaistakhan  (C) Afzalkhan\n','','','A) Daulatkhan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86078,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shivaji Maharaj inspired ----- of Bundelkhand to establish an independent kingdom.\n(A) Chhatrasal  (B) Durgadas Rathore  (C) Jai Singh\n','','','A) Chhatrasal','',0,0,0,NULL),(86079,1680,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The historian ----- praised Shivaji Maharaj’s achievements in his book Shivaji and His Times.\n(A) Jadunath Sarkar  (B) Rabindranath Tagore  (C) Mahatma Phule\n','','','A) Jadunath Sarkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86080,1680,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the column : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <tr>\n      <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n      <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(1) Afzalkhan</td>\n      <td>(a) Shivaji Maharaj escaped from imprisonment with great planning</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(2) Shaistakhan</td>\n      <td>(b) Siege where Shiva Kashid disguised as Shivaji Maharaj</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(3) Agra</td>\n      <td>(c) Mughal Sardar attacked Pune; his fingers were cut off in the raid</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(4) Panhala</td>\n      <td>(d) Meeting at Pratapgad where Shivaji Maharaj faced danger bravely</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>','','','1 - d  2 - c  3 - a  4 - b','',0,0,0,NULL),(86081,1680,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the column : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n <tr>\n      <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n      <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(1) Jiva Mahala</td>\n      <td>(a) Blocked the enemy at Ghodkhind on the way to Vishalgad</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(2) Shiva Kashid</td>\n      <td>(b) Defended the fort of Purandar bravely</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(3) Bajiprabhu Deshpande</td>\n      <td>(c) Disguised himself as Shivaji Maharaj to help him escape from Panhala</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>(4) Murarbaji Deshpande</td>\n      <td>(d) Saved Shivaji Maharaj by killing Bada Sayyad during meeting with Afzalkhan</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>','','','1 - d, 2 - c, 3 - a, 4 - b ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86082,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Chhatrapati Sambhaji Maharaj was born on 14ᵗʰ May 1657 on this Fort.\n(A) Purandar  (B) Raigad  (C) Panhalgad\n','','','A) Purandar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86083,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Aurangzeb was frustrated by his bravery.\n(A) Shahajada Akbar  (B) Chhatrapati Sambhaji Maharaj  (C) Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj\n','','','B) Chhatrapati Sambhaji Maharaj','',0,0,0,NULL),(86084,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','He fought bravely in the Goa battle.\n(A) Yesaji Kank  (B) Nemaji Shinde  (C) Pralhad Niraji\n','','','A) Yesaji Kank','',0,0,0,NULL),(86085,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Aurangzeb had placed him in charge of the Kolhapur province.\n(A) Zulfikarkhan  (B) Shahajada Akbar  (C) Mukarrabkhan\n','','','C) Mukarrabkhan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86086,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','They cut the golden pinnacles of the Emperor\'s tent.\n(A) Santaji and Dhanaji  (B) Santaji Ghorpade and Vithoji Chavan  (C) Khando Ballal and Rupaji Bhosale\n','','','B) Santaji Ghorpade and Vithoji Chavan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86087,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Emperor Aurangzeb died in 1707 CE at this place.\n(A) Bijapur  (B) Ahmednagar  (C) Nashik\n','','','B) Ahmednagar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86088,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','This century is known as the century of the Marathas.\n(A) 18ᵗʰ  (B) 16ᵗʰ  (C) 17ᵗʰ\n','','','A) 18ᵗʰ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86089,1681,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The word that means \'the people who speak Marathi\' or \'Maharashtrian people\'.','','','\'Maratha\'','',0,0,0,NULL),(86090,1681,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : He rebelled against his father and came to the Deccan to take shelter with Sambhaji Maharaj.','','','Shahajada Akbar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86091,1681,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Sambhaji Maharaj\'s army laid a siege to this fort of Siddi.','','','Dandarajpuri Fort','',0,0,0,NULL),(86092,1681,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The Portuguese laid a siege to this fort of the Marathas on the Goa border.','','','Fonda fort','',0,0,0,NULL),(86093,1681,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The one who implemented the strategy of \'Safe deposit locker system\'.','','','Maharani Tarabai','',0,0,0,NULL),(86094,1681,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Chhatrapati Sambhaji Maharaj, Hambirrao Mohite, Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj, Maharani Tarabai','','','Hambirrao Mohite\nReason: Hambirrao Mohite was a Maratha army General, while the remaining were from the Maratha royal family.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86095,1681,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Ramchandrapant Amatya, Pralhad Niraji, Khando Ballal, Rupaji Bhosale','','','Ramchandrapant Amatya\nReason: Rajaram Maharaj had assigned the duty to defend Raigad against the Mughals to Ramchandrapant Amatya, while the remaining were the faithful Sardars who accompanied him to Jinji.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86096,1681,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Khandesh, Varhad, Baglan, Bijapur','','','Bijapur\nReason: The Marathas attacked all the Mughal territories except Bijapur.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86097,1681,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Raigad, Jinji, Dandarajpuri, Sinhagad','','','Dandarajpuri\nReason: Dandarajpuri fort was in possession of Siddi of Janjira, while the rest were in possession of the Marathas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86098,1681,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following chart with respect to powers against whom Sambhaji Maharaj had to fight and the incidents related to them.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <tr>\n      <th>Rule</th>\n      <th>Incident</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>i. The Mughals</td>\n      <td>Resistance to the Ramsej fort siege</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>ii. Siddi of Janjira</td>\n      <td>Seige of the Dandarajpuri fort</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>iii. The Portuguese</td>\n      <td>Siege on the Revdanda fort and Goa</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <tr>\n      <th>Rule</th>\n      <th>Incident</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>i. The Mughals</td>\n      <td>Resistance to the Ramsej fort siege</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>ii. Siddi of Janjira</td>\n      <td>Seige of the Dandarajpuri fort</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>iii. The Portuguese</td>\n      <td>Siege on the Revdanda fort and Goa</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(86099,1681,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the timeline : ','','','i. 14th  May 1657\niii. Aurangzeb attacks the south\nv. 1683 CE\nvii. Rajaram Maharaj leaves for Jinji\nix. 2nd  March 1700\nii. Birth of Rajaram Maharaj\niv. Sambhaji Maharaj\'s campaign against Siddhi\nvi. 11th March 1689\nviii. November 1689\nx. Death of Aurangazeb\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(86100,1681,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart : ','','','i. Shahajiraje\niii. Vyankoji\nv. Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj\nvii. Chhatrapati Shivaji II (Son of Tarabai)\nii. Sambhaji\niv. Chhatrapati Sambhaji Maharaj\nvi. Chhatrapati Shahu Maharaj\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(86101,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did Emperor Aurangzeb himself descended on the South in 1682 CE?','','','Emperor Aurangzeb himself descended on the South in 1682 CE to vanquish Akbar who had rebelled against him.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86102,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','From what age did Sambhaji Maharaj had begun to take interest in the administration of the State and the command of the army?','','','Sambhaji Maharaj had begun to take interest in the administration of the State and the command of the army from the age of fourteen years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86103,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What did the then French traveller Abbe Carrey say about Sambhaji Maharaj\'s battle skills?','','','About Sambhaji Maharaj\'s battle skills, the then French traveller Abbe Carrey said \"Even though the prince is young, he is courageous and brave like his father was famous for....\"','',0,0,0,NULL),(86104,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What vow did Emperor Aurangzeb make?','','','Emperor Aurangzeb made a vow that until he had vanquished Sambhaji, he would not wear his turban.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86105,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Why did Sambhaji Maharaj return halfway from the Janjira Campaign?','','','Sambhaji Maharaj\'s army laid a siege to Siddi\'s Dandarajpuri fort but at the same time, the Mughal army invaded Swaraj and hence Sambhaji Maharaj had to abandon the Janjira campaign and return halfway from it to fight the Mughals.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86106,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Why did Sambhaji Maharaj decide to teach a lesson to the Portuguese?','','','As the Portuguese of Goa had joined hands with Emperor Aurangzeb against Sambhaji Maharaj, he decided to teach them a lesson.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86107,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','To whom did Rajaram Maharaj entrust the responsibility of protecting the Swaraj when he went to Jinji?','','','When Rajaram Maharaj went to Jinji, he entrusted the responsibility of protecting the Swaraj to Ramchandrapant Amatya, Shankaraji Narayan Sachiv, Santaji Ghorpade and Dhanaji Jadhav.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86108,1681,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Sambhaji Maharaj bring Aurangzeb to his knees?','','','i. Sambhaji Maharaj became the Chhatrapati and the war against the Mughals intensified.\nii. Aurangzeb\'s dream was to destroy the Maratha State completely using his military and financial strength and occupy regions from Kabul to Kannyakumari to create a grand unified Mughal Empire.\niii. However, Sambhaji Maharaj, with his bravery and military skills, shattered this dream of the Emperor.\niv. Marathas attacked many of the Mughal territories. Though the Emperor\'s generals tried hard for five years to win the Maratha fort of Ramsej, they couldn\'t do so.\nThus, Sambhaji Maharaj\'s valour brought Aurangzeb to his knees.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86109,1681,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Sambhaji Maharaj carry out his administrative responsibilities?','','','i. Sambhaji Maharaj carried out his administrative responsibilities very well, in spite of being busy fighting on various battle fronts.\nii. He carried forward the prompt justice and revenue system, which was a characteristic of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj\'s era.\niii. The landlords who harassed common people and rebelled against the Swaraj were severely punished. He gave Maharani Yesubai, her own stamp, thereby giving her administrative powers.\niv. He also continued the legacy of Shivaji Maharaj\'s welfare policies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86110,1681,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','After the death of Rajaram Maharaj, Maharani Tarabai came forward to lead the Swaraj movement. Explain.','','','i. After the death of Rajaram Maharaj, Maharani Tarabai came forward to lead the Swaraj movement and fought bravely for seven years.\nii. During those times, Marathas lost Satara and Panhala but dashed up to the Mughal areas of Madhya Pradesh and Gujarat under the leadership of Maharani Tarabai, who expanded the war areas even further. She singlehandedly took charge of the administration and encouraged the Maratha Sardars fight against the Mughals. They went up to Sironje, Mandsaur and Malwa.\niii. Sardars like Krishnaji Sawant, Khanderao Dabhade, Dhanaji Jadhav and Nemaji Shinde helped her in this campaign.\niv. Maharani Tarabai even implemented her strategy known as \'Safe deposit locker system\', to counter the strength of the Mughal army. In the words of the contemporary Mughal historian Khafikhan, \"Maharani Tarabai is an intelligent and knowledgeable woman. Her management of the army and administration had won renown even during the rule of her husband\".\nv. Tarabai achieved great things and displayed the qualities like leadership of the army and management of campaigns, adding strength to the Maratha campaigns.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86111,1681,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Devdatta has described Maharani Tarabai\'s valour in which words?','','','Devdatta a great poet and son of Paramanand has described Maharani Tarabai\'s valour in the following words:\nताराबाई रामराणी । भद्रकाली कोपली ।\nदिल्ली झाली दीनवाणी । दिल्लीशाचे गेले पाणी ।\nरामराणी भद्रकाली । रणरंगी क्रुद्ध झाली ।\nप्रयत्नाची वेळ आली । मुगल हो सांभाळा ।\n\"The queen of the king Rajaram is furious like the Kalimata. This has left Delhi and the ruler of Delhi worried. She is fighting in the battlefield with all her might, so Mughals should now take care of themselves.\"\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86112,1681,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','\"The Maratha war for independence is an exciting and glorious period in the history of India\" - Justify.','','','i. After the death of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, the Marathas fought their war of independence for twenty seven long years, under the leadership of Chhatrapati Sambhaji Maharaj, Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj and Maharani Tarabai.\nii. Among these, Sambhaji Maharaj prevented all attempts of Emperor Aurangzeb to conquer the Maratha forts and territories. Sambhaji Maharaj\'s valour, bravery and military skills brought Aurangzeb to his knees and rendered him helpless. Moreover, Sambhaji Maharaj had also made the lives of Siddi and Portuguese extremely difficult.\niii. After the death of Sambhaji Maharaj, Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj and Maharani Yesubai put up a tough fight against the Mughals.\niv. When Zulfikarkhan had put Raigad under siege, Maharani Yesubai bravely tackled the unprecedented calamity. She kept the Maratha Chhatrapati - Rajaram Maharaj, secure without regard for her own life or that of her son. This decision of her\'s is a shining example of her love for Swaraj and her extreme selflessness.\nv. The Maratha Sardars fought with sheer bravery and generated fear among the Mughals.\nvi. After the death of Rajaram Maharaj, Maharani Tarabai came forward to lead the Swaraj movement in the extremely unfavourable circumstances and fought for seven years. Maharani Tarabai effectively implemented her strategy known as \'Safe deposit locker system\', to counter the strength of the Mughal army.\nvii. Due to the forceful campaigns of the Marathas, even an emperor as mighty as Aurangzeb, could not realise his dream to expand his empire to the Maratha territories and hence, was frustrated.\nThus we can say that the Maratha war for independence is an exciting and glorious period in the history of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86113,1681,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The book \'Budhbhushanam\'','','','i. \'Budhbhushanam\' is a book composed by Sambhaji Maharaj, in Sanskrit language.\nii. Sambhaji Maharaj had studied many ancient books on polity. \'Budhbhushanam\' is the gist of the knowledge that he acquired from those books.\niii. The second chapter of this book discusses polity and includes information about of the following:\na. Virtues of a King, the Prime minister\nb. Princes and their education and functions\nc. The advisers to the king\nd. Forts and the goods & materials needed on a fort\ne. The army\nf. The duties of a king\ng. Espionage, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86114,1681,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj','','','i. Rajaram Maharaj was the second son of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj.\nii. He was born on 24th  February 1670 on Raigad fort.\niii. He became the Chhatrapati after the death of Sambhaji Maharaj. He displayed great courage and tenacity by continuing the war against Aurangzeb for 11 years and protected Swaraj during extremely testing times.\niv. He was a very thoughtful and a kind-hearted ruler. He unified all the capable people in the Maratha Empire and renewed their enthusiasm. Noted historian Riyasatkar G. S. Sardesai has described him as \'Sthirbuddhi\' (Level Headed) which is the best possible description of him.\nv. Rajaram Maharaj died on 2nd  March 1700 on Sinhagad after a short period of illness.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86115,1681,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Siege of Jinji','','','i. After the capture of Raigad, Aurangzeb sent Zulfikarkhan on the campaign of Jinji.\nii. He laid a siege but the Marathas bravely defended the fort for almost eight years.\niii. Santaji and Dhanaji of the Marathas fiercely attacked the Mughal forces engaged in the siege from outside.\niv. Though Rajaram Maharaj escaped through the siege and returned to Maharashtra but the fort of Jinji was captured by Zulfikarkhan.\n4. The strategy implemented by Maharani Tarabai to capture Mughal Forts\nAns: i. Aurangzeb used his large army and captured many Maratha forts.\nii. The Marathas were comparatively weaker to combat Aurangzeb\'s huge army and to recapture the forts.\niii. Hence, Maharani Tarabai used an effective strategy against the Mughals. As per this strategy - As the rainy season would approach, the Killedar of the fort used to pretend to side with Aurangzeb, accept a bribe from him and hand over the fort to him. This bribe amount was then deposited in the Maratha treasury.\niv. Once Aurangzeb repleted the wealth, grains and ammunition on the fort, Tarabai used to attack and capture that fort.\nThis strategy of Tarabai is described as \'Safe deposit locker system\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86116,1681,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Aurangzeb realised that fighting with Marathas was difficult.','','','i. Aurangzeb had a misconception that the Maratha rule would come to an end once the Maratha forts were captured.\nii. So he put a siege around Ramsej fort near Nashik. Aurangzeb\'s army was huge.\niii. In spite of being small, the Maratha army fought with perseverance for five years with was unparalleled bravery.\nThis resistance offered by the Marathas made Aurangzeb realise that fighting with Marathas was difficult.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86117,1681,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sambhaji Maharaj had to abandon the winning campaign of Goa and return to fight with the Mughals.','','','i. The Portuguese of Goa had joined hands with Aurangzeb against Sambhaji Maharaj. In order to teach them a lesson, he attacked the Revdanda fort of the Portuguese in 1683 CE.\nii. To counter this, the Portuguese laid attack to the Fonda fort of the Marathas on the Goa border.But, the Marathas broke through the siege and attacked Goa. Yesaji Kank showed great valour in the battle.\niii. The Portuguese Governor got wounded and retreated but Sambhaji Maharaj followed him landing the Portuguese in deep trouble.\niv. Exactly at the same time, the Mughals attacked the South Konkan region of Marathas.\nHearing this, Sambhaji Maharaj had to abandon the winning campaign of Goa and return to fight with the Mughals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86118,1681,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Aurangzeb turned his attention towards Adilshahi and Qutubshahi kingdoms.','','','i. The high resistance offered by the Marathas made Aurangzeb realise that fighting with Marathas was difficult.\nii. The Marathas, with their bravery and valour, had foiled all his attempts at conquering the Maratha territories.\niii. As a result, Aurangzeb did not succeed in his campaign against the Marathas which made him feel helpless and frustrated.\niv. Therefore, he finally suspended the campaign and turned towards the Adilshahi and Qutubshahi kingdoms to conquer them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86119,1681,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The military strength of Sambhaji Maharaj was weakened.','','','i. The wealth and army of the Adilshahi and Qutubshahi kingdoms conquered by the Mughals, made the already mighty Aurangzeb even stronger.\nii. He then concentrated all his might on defeating the Marathas and attacked their territory from all sides.\niii. The Maratha army General Hambirrao Mohite got killed during one of the encounters.\niv. The death of Hambirrao Mohite was a huge blow to the Maratha army and as a result, the military strength of Sambhaji Maharaj was weakened.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86120,1681,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','After the death of Sambhaji Maharaj, the Marathas intensified their war with the Mughals.','','','i. Aurangzeb was trying his best to defeat Sambhaji Maharaj. For this, he placed Mukarrabkhan in charge of the Kolhapur province.\nii. Mukarrabkhan learnt that Sambhaji Maharaj was at Sangameshwar in Konkan. He raided the place and captured Sambhaji Maharaj.\niii. When Sambhaji Maharaj was captured from Sangameshwar by Mukarrabkhan and taken to Aurangzeb, he faced Aurangzeb fearlessly.\niv. By the Emperor\'s orders, Sambhaji Maharaj was brutally put to death on 11^\"th \"  March 1689.\nv. This Maratha Chhatrapati did not compromise his self respect and faced his death nobly and bravely.\nThis sacrifice of the Maratha Chhatrapati inspired the Marathas to intensify their fight against the Mughals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86121,1681,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','It was decided that the Raigad fort should be fought under the leadership of Maharani Yesubai.','','','i. After the death of Sambhaji Maharaj, Aurangzeb thought that his dream of winning the Maratha kingdom would come true and hence, he sent Zulfikarkhan to put Raigad under siege.\nii. During this time, Rajaram Maharaj, his wife Maharani Tarabai, Sambhaji Maharaj\'s wife Maharani Yesubai and his son Shahu were in Raigad. But, all members of the royal family staying at one place was risky.\niii. Maharani Yesubai bravely resolved that she would not surrender to the Mughals under any conditions and took many important political decisions on Raigad.\niv. She decided that Rajaram Maharaj should escape from Raigad and go to a far off place like Jinji.\nIt was thus decided that in the absence of Rajaram Maharaj, the fight to defend Raigad would continue under the leadership of Maharani Yesubai.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86122,1681,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','After capturing Raigad from Maratha, the Mughal forces were at their wits\' end.','','','i. Raigad was captured in November 1689 and Maharani Yesubai and Shahu were arrested. Moreover, Aurangzeb had tempted many Maratha Sardars to join his side by offering them watans and jagirs. The situation became extremely critical for the Marathas.\nii. However, Rajaram Maharaj gave Mughals the taste of their own medicine by using their tactics against them and turned the tables around for Marathas. He promised that if a Maratha Sardar captured any Mughal territory it would be given to him as a jagir. This promise of jagir brought forward many capable Maratha Sardars.\niii. They invaded the Mughal territory with all their vigour, defeated many Mughal Generals and captured their territories.\niv. Santaji and Dhanaji were at the forefront in these actions. On one occasion, they even cut and carried away the golden pinnacle of the Emperor\'s own tent in a surprise attack. The huge artillery and war materials of the Mughals could not sustain against such surprise attacks and guerrilla tactics.\nThis concludes that post capturing Raigad from Maratha, the Mughal forces were at their wits\' end.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86123,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the field in which Shivaji Maharaj had provided education to Sambhaji Maharaj?','','','Shivaji Maharaj had provided excellent education to Sambhaji Maharaj in civil administration and military compaigns.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86124,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where was Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj born?','','','Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj was born on Raigad Fort.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86125,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When and where did Rajaram Maharaj die?','','','Rajaram Maharaj died on Sinhgad on 2nd  March, 1700.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86126,1681,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When did the Maratha War of Independence end?','','','Maratha War of Independence ended with Aurangzeb\'s death in 1707 CE.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86127,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ made a vow that until he had vanquished Sambhaji, he would not wear his turban.\n(A) Shahajada Akbar  (B) Emperor Aurangzeb  (C) Zulfikarkhan\n','','','B) Emperor Aurangzeb ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86128,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ rebelled against his father and then came to the Deccan to take shelter with Sambhaji Maharaj.\n(A) Shahajada Akbar  (B) Santaji Ghorpade  (C) Mukarrabkhan\n','','','A) Shahajada Akbar ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86129,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ is a book composed by Sambhaji Maharaj, in Sanskrit language.\n(A) Budhbhushanam  (B) Rajtarangini  (C) Shivabharat\n','','','A) Budhbhushanam ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86130,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ had joined hands with the Emperor Aurangzeb against Sambhaji Maharaj.\n(A) Portuguese of Goa  (B) British of Bombay  (C) Siddis of Janjira\n','','','A) Portuguese of Goa ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86131,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The war between the Marathas and the Mughals after the death of Shivaji Maharaj is called the -----.\n(a) Maratha-Mughal War  (b) Maratha War of Independence  (c) Great Indian Battle\n','','','b) Maratha War of Independence','',0,0,0,NULL),(86132,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Maratha War of Independence lasted for ----- years.\n(a) Twenty  (b) Thirty  (c) Twenty-seven\n','','','c) Twenty-seven','',0,0,0,NULL),(86133,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Chhatrapati Sambhaji Maharaj was born on ----- Fort.\n(a) Raigad  (b) Purandar  (c) Sinhgad\n','','','b) Purandar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86134,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Emperor Aurangzeb led the invasion of the South in ----- CE.\n(a) 1682  (b) 1670  (c) 1690\n','','','a) 1682','',0,0,0,NULL),(86135,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Siddi of Janjira used to ----- the people in the Maratha territory.\n(a) Help  (b) Harass  (c) Protect\n','','','b) Harass','',0,0,0,NULL),(86136,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Portuguese joined hands with ----- against Sambhaji Maharaj.\n(a) Aurangzeb  (b) Adilshah  (c) Rajaram Maharaj\n','','','a) Aurangzeb','',0,0,0,NULL),(86137,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sambhaji Maharaj wrote a book called ----- which was based on ancient polity.\n(a) Rajniti  (b) Budhbhushanam  (c) Arthashastra\n','','','b) Budhbhushanam','',0,0,0,NULL),(86138,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mukarrabkhan captured Sambhaji Maharaj at ----- in Konkan.\n(a) Raigad  (b) Sangameshwar  (c) Panhala\n','','','b) Sangameshwar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86139,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj was born on -----.\n(a) 14 May 1657  (b) 24 February 1670  (c) 11 March 1689\n','','','b) 24 February 1670','',0,0,0,NULL),(86140,1681,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rajaram Maharaj died on 2 March 1700 at ----- Fort.\n(a) Sinhgad  (b) Raigad  (c) Jinji\n','','','a) Sinhgad','',0,0,0,NULL),(86141,1681,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the contribution of Chhatrapati Sambhaji Maharaj in the Maratha War of Independence.','','','1.	Sambhaji Maharaj was the eldest son of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, born on 14 May 1657 at Purandar Fort.\n2.	He bravely continued the war against the Mughals after his father’s death.\n3.	His leadership and military skill made Aurangzeb helpless and frustrated.\n4.	He protected Swaraj by fighting multiple enemies like the Mughals, Siddis, and Portuguese.\n5.	His fearless death in 1689 inspired the Marathas to continue the struggle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86142,1681,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the campaigns of Sambhaji Maharaj against the Siddi of Janjira and the Portuguese.','','','1.	In 1682 CE, Sambhaji Maharaj attacked the Siddi’s Dandarajpuri fort and damaged Janjira with artillery.\n2.	He had to withdraw the campaign due to a sudden Mughal invasion.\n3.	In 1683 CE, he attacked the Portuguese at Revdanda fort and later advanced towards Goa.\n4.	Yesaji Kank showed great bravery in this battle.\n5.	The campaign was left incomplete due to Mughal attacks in South Konkan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86143,1681,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the role of Chhatrapati Rajaram Maharaj in continuing the Maratha War of Independence.','','','1.	Rajaram Maharaj, the second son of Shivaji Maharaj, became Chhatrapati after Sambhaji’s death.\n2.	He escaped from Raigad and reached Jinji fort safely to continue the war.\n3.	He appointed capable generals like Santaji Ghorpade and Dhanaji Jadhav.\n4.	Under his leadership, Marathas attacked Mughal territories using guerrilla tactics.\n5.	His efforts kept the struggle for Swaraj alive until his death in 1700 CE.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86144,1681,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the bravery and leadership of Maharani Yesubai and Maharani Tarabai.','','','1.	Maharani Yesubai bravely faced the Mughal siege of Raigad after Sambhaji’s death.\n2.	She chose Rajaram Maharaj as Chhatrapati instead of her own son, showing selflessness.\n3.	Maharani Tarabai led the Maratha army after Rajaram’s death.\n4.	She organized the administration and inspired soldiers to continue the fight.\n5.	Both queens played a vital role in protecting the Swaraj during difficult times\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86145,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The armies of Shahu Maharaj and Maharani Tarabai fought a battle at ____ on the bank of the Bheema.\n(A) Panhala  (B) Satara  (C) Khed\n','','','C) Khed ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86146,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After Shahu Maharaj was released by the Mughals, he made ____ a Peshwa.\n(A) Chimajiappa  (B) Balaji Vishwanath  (C) Bajirao I\n','','','B) Balaji Vishwanath ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86147,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ was the chief of Maratha Navy under Maharani Tarabai.\n(A) Kanhoji Angre  (B) Malharrao Holkar  (C) Ranoji Shinde\n','','','A) Kanhoji Angre ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86148,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Mughal Subhedar, ____ of Allahabad attacked Bundelkhand and defeated Chhatrasal.\n(A) Nizam-ul-Mulk  (B) Farukhsear  (C) Mohammad Khan Bangush\n','','','C) Mohammad Khan Bangush ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86149,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ the Emperor of Iran, invaded India during the time of Bajirao.\n(A) Azamshah  (B) Nadirshah  (C) Hussein Ali\n','','','B) Nadirshah ','',0,0,0,NULL),(86150,1682,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Following : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n  <thead style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <tr>\n      <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n      <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>i. Tarabai</td>\n      <td>a. Hyderabad</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>ii. Portuguese</td>\n      <td>b. Bundelkhand</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>iii. Shahu Maharaj</td>\n      <td>c. Vasai</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>iv. King Chhatrasal</td>\n      <td>d. Satara</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>e. Kolhapur</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>','','','( i-e ), (ii - c), (iii - d), (iv - b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86151,1682,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Prince Shahu was in the captivity of this Mughal Prince after the death of Aurangzeb.','','','Prince Azamshah','',0,0,0,NULL),(86152,1682,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : Balaji was from this town in Konkan.','','','Shrivardhan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86153,1682,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Mughal Emperor Farukhsear appointed him as the Subhedar of Deccan.','','','Nizam-ul-Mulk','',0,0,0,NULL),(86154,1682,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : He ruled Bundelkhand.','','','King Chhatrasal','',0,0,0,NULL),(86155,1682,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The Mughal Emperor invited him to protect Delhi when he felt uneasy on account of Bajirao\'s Delhi expedition.','','','Nizam','',0,0,0,NULL),(86156,1682,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : He defeated the Portuguese.','','','Chimajiappa','',0,0,0,NULL),(86157,1682,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : Bajirao died at this place.','','','Raverkhedi','',0,0,0,NULL),(86158,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the meaning of Chauthai','','','Chauthai was one fourth part of the revenue, collected as tax.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86159,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the meaning of Sardeshmukhi','','','Sardeshmukhi was one tenth part of the revenue collected as an additional tax.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86160,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which two Maratha kingdoms came into existence in 1710 CE?','','','In 1710 CE, the two independent Maratha kingdoms which came into existence were, the kingdom with capital at Satara and headed by Shahu Maharaj and the other headed by Shivaji II, the minor son of Maharani Tarabai, with capital at Kolhapur.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86161,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','With whose help did Balaji Vishwanath obtain the grant or sanad to collect chauthai and sardeshmukhi from the Mughal empire in Deccan?','','','Balaji Vishwanath obtained the grant or sanad to collect chauthai and sardeshmukhi from the Mughal empire in Deccan with the help of the Sayyid brothers, Abdulla (Hasan) and Hussein Ali in the Delhi court.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86162,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Whom did Bajirao send to strengthen the posts in Malwa?','','','Bajirao sent Malharrao Holkar, Ranoji Shinde and Udaji Pawar under the leadership of his brother Chimajiappa, to strengthen the posts in Malwa.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86163,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What did the Nizam agree to secure after his defeat against Bajirao at Bhopal?','','','The Nizam agreed to secure the sanad of Malwa subhedari for the Marathas from the Mughal Badshah after his defeat against Bajirao at Bhopal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86164,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which families gained prominence in Bajirao\'s time?','','','The Shinde, Holkar, Pawar and Gaikwad families gained prominence in Bajirao\'s time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86165,1682,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Bajirao help King Chhatrasal?','','','i. King Chhatrasal had established his own kingdom in Bundelkhand.\nii. When the Mughal Subhedar Mohmmad Khan Bangush of Allahabad attacked Bundelkhand and defeated him, Chhatrasal appealed for help to Bajirao.\niii. Bajirao went to Bundelkhand with a large army and defeated Bangush.\niv. Thus, Bajirao helped King Chhatrasal and got honoured by him.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86166,1682,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Chimajiappa conquer the Portuguese territories?','','','i. The Portuguese ruled over the territories of Vasai and Thane on the Konkan coast and oppressed their subjects.\nii. Chimajiappa, who was sent by Bajirao to conquer these territories conquered Thane and the adjacent areas.\niii. He laid siege to the Vasai fort, which was very strong and had powerful Portuguese artillery.\niv. Chimajiappa continued the siege with perseverance and forced the Portuguese to surrender the fort of Vasai and pass large parts of the Portuguese territory into the hands of the Marathas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86167,1682,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shahu Maharaj','','',' i. The Mughals had kept Shahu Maharaj captive after they captured Raigad in 1689. He was under Prince Azamshah\'s captive after the death of Emperor Aurangzeb.\nii. Prince Azamshah released him, as he knew that there would be a conflict between Shahu Maharaj And Maharani Tarabai for the gadi of the Maratha Chhatrapati, which would hence decrease the Maratha strength.\niii. When Tarabai did not accept Shahu Maharaj\'s claim to the throne, the armies of Shahu Maharaj and Maharani Tarabai fought a battle at Khed on the bank of the Bheema river. Shahu Maharaj won the battle, crowned himself and made Satara the capital of the Maratha kingdom.\niv. He knew the politics, strengths and weaknesses of the North and the Mughals well, as he had spent the early part of his life in the Mughal camps. He was acquainted with many influential people in the Mughal court.\nv. Along with Peshwa Balaji and Peshwa Bajirao, he expanded the Maratha kingdom and its power in the North. He gave a new direction to the Maratha politics in the changing circumstances.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86168,1682,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Balaji Vishwanath','','','i. Balaji Vishwanath hailed from Shrivardhan, in Konkan. When the Mughals released Shahu Maharaj, he gave the position of Peshwa to Balaji\nii. He was competent, experienced and convinced many Sardars that Shahu Maharaj was the real heir of the Maratha empire. He thus made these Sardars join hands with him.\niii. He avoided a war with Kanhoji Angre, the chief of Maratha Navy under Maharani Tarabai and convinced him to support Shahu Maharaj.\niv. After strengthening Shahu Maharaj\'s position in Maharashtra, he took interest in the politics in the North. In 1719 CE, with the help of the Sayyid brothers, Abdulla (Hasan) and Hussein Ali, he obtained the grants or sanads from the Mughal Emperor to collect Chauthai and Sardeshmukhi from the Mughal territory in the Deccan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86169,1682,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Kanhoji Angre','','','i. Kanhoji Angre was the chief of the Maratha Navy.\nii. In the opposition between Shahu Maharaj and Maharani Tarabai, he supported Maharani Tarabai and attacked Shahu Maharaj\'s territories.\niii. Balaji Vishwanath was sent by Shahu Maharaj to fight against him.\niv. However, the war was avoided by Balaji, who then convinced Kanhoji to support Shahu Maharaj.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86170,1682,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Bajirao I','','','i. Bajirao I was a courageous and great general who established the Maratha supremacy in the North and also won status for the Martha power as a formidable power in the entire India.\nii. He was the son of Balaji Vishwanath and was appointed as the Peshwa by Shahu Maharaj in 1720 CE after the death of Balaji.\niii. Bajirao expanded the Maratha Empire during his 20 year term.\niv. When the Mughal Subhedar of Deccan, Nizam - ul - Mulk, went against the Maratha\'s rights to recover Chauthai - Sardeshmukhi from the Mughal areas and captured some part of the Pune Pargana, Bajirao decided to counter him. He defeated the Nizam, at Palkhed near Aurangabad and got back the rights of collecting the revenue.\nv. He strengthened the Maratha power in Malwa and Bundelkhand, by helping King Chhatrasal against Bangush.\nvi. While on his march on Delhi in 1737 CE, he defeated the Nizam at Bhopal and secured the sanad of Malwa subhedari for the Marathas.\nvii. He sent Malharrao Holkar, Ranoji Shinde and Udaji Pawar under the leadership of his brother Chimajiappa to conquer the Vasai and Thane territories from the Portuguese.\nviii. Bajirao I died at Raverkhedi on the banks of the Narmada in April 1740, while he was on his way to defend the Mughals from the invasion by Nadirshah, the Emperor of Iran.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86171,1682,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','The Battle of Palkhed','','','i. Nizam - ul - Mulk was appointed as the Subhedar of Deccan by the Mughal Emperor Farukhsear.\nii. Nizam tried to establish his separate existence at Hyderabad in 1713 CE.\niii. He was against the Mughal Emperor\'s decision of giving the Marathas the right to recover Chauthai - Sardeshmukhi from the Mughal areas.\niv. He captured some part of the Pune Pargana, but got defeated by Bajirao I in the battle of Palkhed near Aurangabad.\nv. Thereafter, the Nizam accepted the Maratha right to collect Chauthai - Sardeshmukhi.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86172,1682,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Azamshah released Chhatrapati Shahu Maharaj from his custody','','','i. There was a fight after Emperor Aurangzeb\'s death among his sons for the throne of Delhi.\nii. Prince Azamshah, his son, marched towards Delhi from the South to seize the imperial throne.\niii. Prince Shahu was in his captivity and he believed that there would be a conflict between Maharani Tarabai and Shahu Maharaj for the gadi of the Maratha Chhatrapati.\niv. Such a conflict would make the Maratha strength weak.\nHence, Azamshah released Chhatrapati Shahu Maharaj from his custody.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86173,1682,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Two separate Maratha States were formed.','','','i. Shahu Maharaj marched towards Maharashtra after his release from the Mughal prince Azamshah.\nii. He was joined by a few Maratha Sardars but Maharani Tarabai declined his claim to the throne of the Maratha Chhatrapati.\niii. Shahu Maharaj\'s army fought with the army of Maharani Tarabai at Khed on the banks of the Bheema and won the battle. He captured Satara and made it the capital of his kingdom. He got himself crowned.\niv. Maharani Tarabai proclaimed her minor son Shivaji II as Chhatrapati at Pahnala in 1710 CE. Kolhapur was the capital of this independent kingdom.\nv. This mutual opposition between Maharani Tarabai and Shahu Maharaj continued.\nThus, two separate Maratha States were formed, one at Kolhapur and the other at Satara.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86174,1682,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Delhi needed the help from the Marathas.','','','i. The Mughal power faced the threat of invasion from Iran and Afghan and also of the local Pathan, local Rajput, Jat and Rohilas rulers.\nii. Aurangzeb\'s successors were fighting amongst themselves. The Delhi court was marked by bickering and confusion.\niii. The internal competition and struggle in the court had also weakened the Mughal power.\nHence, Delhi needed the help from the Marathas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86175,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ was the capital of the Maratha kingdom under Shahu Maharaj.\n(a) Pune (b) Satara (c) Kolhapur\n','','','b) Satara','',0,0,0,NULL),(86176,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After the death of Mughal Emperor ____ there ensued a struggle among his sons for the throne of Delhi.\n(a) Akbar (b) Aurangzeb (c) Shah Jahan\n','','','b) Aurangzeb','',0,0,0,NULL),(86177,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In 1739 CE, Chimajiappa laid siege to the ____ fort, which was under Portuguese rule.\n(a) Vasai (b) Raigad (c) Janjira\n','','','a) Vasai','',0,0,0,NULL),(86178,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','By the time Bajirao reached ____ Nadirshah had returned to Iran with an enormous booty looted from Delhi.\n(a) Burhanpur (b) Delhi (c) Surat\n','','','a) Burhanpur','',0,0,0,NULL),(86179,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the position of the Marathas against the Mughals towards the end of the Maratha war of independence?','','','The Marathas were on the offensive and had a strong position against the Mughals towards the end of the Maratha war of independence.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86180,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did Bajirao march on Delhi in 1737 CE?','','','Bajirao marched on Delhi in 1737 CE because the emperor declined his demand for the office of the Subhedar of Malwa.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86181,1682,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which territories did Chimajiappa conquer from the Portuguese?','','','Chimajiappa conquered the territories of Vasai and Thane on the Konkan coast from the Portuguese.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86182,1682,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Chimajiappa','','','i. Chimajiappa was the brother of Peshwa Bajirao I.\nii. Under his leadership, the Marathas strengthened their posts in Malwa.\niii. He conquered the Thane and adjacent areas from the Portuguese.\niv. In 1739 CE, he laid siege to the Vasai fort and continued it with perseverance against the strong Portuguese. He forced them to surrender and got the Vasai fort and the other Portuguese territory under the Marathas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86183,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After the death of Aurangzeb, Prince ----- released Shahu Maharaj from captivity.\n(a) Azamshah  (b) Bahadur Shah  (c) Jahandar Shah\n','','','a) Azamshah','',0,0,0,NULL),(86184,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The battle between Shahu Maharaj and Maharani Tarabai was fought at ----- on the bank of river Bhima.\n(a) Panhala  (b) Khed  (c) Satara\n','','','b) Khed','',0,0,0,NULL),(86185,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After winning the battle of Khed, Shahu Maharaj made ----- the capital of the Maratha kingdom.\n(a) Kolhapur  (b) Satara  (c) Raigad\n','','','b) Satara','',0,0,0,NULL),(86186,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shahu Maharaj appointed ----- as his Peshwa after his release.\n(a) Balaji Vishwanath  (b) Bajirao I  (c) Chimajiappa\n','','','a) Balaji Vishwanath','',0,0,0,NULL),(86187,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Marathas got the right to collect Chauthai and Sardeshmukhi from Mughal territories in ----- CE.\n(a) 1719  (b) 1725  (c) 1707\n','','','a) 1719','',0,0,0,NULL),(86188,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Kanhoji Angre was the chief of the ----- during Shahu Maharaj’s time.\n(a) Maratha Army  (b) Maratha Navy  (c) Maratha Treasury\n','','','b) Maratha Navy','',0,0,0,NULL),(86189,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bajirao I became the Peshwa in ----- CE after the death of Balaji Vishwanath.\n(a) 1715  (b) 1720  (c) 1730\n','','','b) 1720','',0,0,0,NULL),(86190,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bajirao I defeated the Nizam at the battle of ----- near Aurangabad.\n(a) Palkhed  (b) Bhopal  (c) Bundelkhand\n','','','a) Palkhed','',0,0,0,NULL),(86191,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In 1739 CE, Chimajiappa conquered the fort of ----- from the Portuguese.\n(a) Goa  (b) Vasai  (c) Thane\n','','','b) Vasai','',0,0,0,NULL),(86192,1682,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bajirao I died in April 1740 at ----- on the bank of river Narmada.\n(a) Burhanpur  (b) Raverkhedi  (c) Satara\n','','','b) Raverkhedi','',0,0,0,NULL),(86193,1682,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the release of Shahu Maharaj and its political importance.','','','1.	After Aurangzeb’s death, his son Azamshah released Shahu Maharaj from captivity.\n2.	Azamshah believed Shahu’s release would create conflict with Maharani Tarabai.\n3.	Shahu’s release led to a battle between him and Tarabai at Khed.\n4.	Shahu’s victory united many Maratha Sardars under his leadership and stabilized Maratha power.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86194,1682,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the achievements of Balaji Vishwanath as Peshwa.','','','1.	He convinced many Maratha Sardars to support Shahu Maharaj as the rightful heir.\n2.	He resolved conflict with Kanhoji Angre peacefully and strengthened Shahu’s rule.\n3.	He obtained rights (sanads) from the Mughal Emperor to collect Chauthai and Sardeshmukhi.\n4.	He laid the foundation for future Maratha expansion in North India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86195,1682,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the main achievements of Bajirao I.','','','1.	Bajirao defeated Nizam-ul-Mulk at Palkhed and secured Chauthai-Sardeshmukhi rights.\n2.	He extended Maratha rule to Malwa and Bundelkhand by defeating local rulers.\n3.	He defeated Nizam again at Bhopal and marched up to Delhi, showing Maratha strength.\n4.	Under his leadership, the Marathas became the strongest power in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86196,1682,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the victory of Chimajiappa over the Portuguese.','','','1.	Portuguese ruled Vasai, Thane, and nearby areas and oppressed locals.\n2.	Bajirao sent his brother Chimajiappa to defeat them.\n3.	Chimajiappa conquered Thane and besieged the strong Vasai fort in 1739 CE.\n4.	After a long siege, the Portuguese surrendered, and the Marathas gained Vasai and nearby territories.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86197,1682,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the reasons for the decline of Mughal power and the rise of Maratha power.','','','1.	After Aurangzeb, his weak successors and internal conflicts weakened the Mughal Empire.\n2.	Foreign invasions (by Irani and Afghani rulers) threatened the Mughal Empire.\n3.	The Delhi court needed military help, which the Marathas provided.\n4.	Using this opportunity, Marathas expanded their influence across India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86198,1682,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Azamshah released Shahu Maharaj from captivity.','','','1.	After Aurangzeb’s death, his sons fought for the Mughal throne.\n2.	Azamshah wanted to weaken the Maratha power.\n3.	He believed that releasing Shahu Maharaj would cause conflict between Shahu and Maharani Tarabai.\n4.	This internal struggle, he thought, would reduce Maratha strength.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86199,1682,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Shahu Maharaj was able to win the support of many Maratha Sardars.','','','1.	He was the grandson of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, so many Sardars respected his lineage.\n2.	Balaji Vishwanath convinced Sardars that Shahu was the rightful heir.\n3.	Shahu’s victory at the Battle of Khed strengthened his claim to the throne.\n4.	His wise and diplomatic leadership united many Maratha nobles under him.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86200,1682,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Nizam was defeated by Bajirao I at Palkhed.','','','1.	Nizam tried to stop the Marathas from collecting Chauthai and Sardeshmukhi.\n2.	He captured parts of Pune Pargana to challenge Maratha power.\n3.	Bajirao used swift cavalry movements and avoided direct confrontation.\n4.	His clever military tactics forced the Nizam to surrender and accept Maratha rights.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86201,1682,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Marathas helped the Mughal power instead of fighting against it.','','','1.	After Aurangzeb, Mughal rulers had stopped the policy of destroying the Marathas.\n2.	The Mughals were weak and needed military help against internal and foreign enemies.\n3.	Shahu Maharaj decided to protect the Mughal empire to expand Maratha influence peacefully.\n4.	The Marathas believed that saving the old empire was as good as creating a new one.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86202,1682,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the column :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Shahu Maharaj</td>\n    <td>(a)Defeated Nizam at Palkhed</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Balaji Vishwanath</td>\n    <td>(b) Captured Vasai from the Portuguese</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Bajirao I</td>\n    <td>(c) Battle of Khed</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Chimajiappa</td>\n    <td>(d) Obtained Chauthai–Sardeshmukhi rights</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 - c), (2 - d), (3 - a), (4 - b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86203,1682,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the column :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Azamshah</td>\n    <td>(a) Proclaimed Shivaji II as Chhatrapati at Panhala</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Tarabai</td>\n    <td>(b) Helped Balaji Vishwanath to get Sanads </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Sayyid Brothers</td>\n    <td>(c) Defeated by Bajirao at Palkhed</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Nizam–ul–Mulk</td>\n    <td>(d) Released Shahu Maharaj</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 - d), (2 - a), (3 - b), (4 - c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86204,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After Bajirao I, Shahu Maharaj appointed his son Balaji Bajirao alias ____ as the Peshwa.\n(A) Chimajiappa (B) Dattaji (C) Nanasaheb\n','','','C) Nanasaheb','',0,0,0,NULL),(86205,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','At the behest of the Rohilla chief ____ Abdali invaded India again.\n(A) Najibkhan (B) Ibrahimkhan Gardi (C) Hyder Ali\n','','','A) Najibkhan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86206,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On 14 January 1761, the Marathas began the Third Battle of ____ against Abdali\'s army.\n(A) Attock (B) Udgir (C) Panipat\n','','','C) Panipat','',0,0,0,NULL),(86207,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Hyder Ali was the Sultan of ____.\n(A) Panipat (B) Mysore (C) Ajmer\n','','','B) Mysore','',0,0,0,NULL),(86208,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Approximately ____ people were killed in the battle of Panipat.\n(A) 15,000 (B) 1,50,000 (C) 15,00,000\n','','','B) 1,50,000','',0,0,0,NULL),(86209,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : They came from Afghanistan.','','','Pathans','',0,0,0,NULL),(86210,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : They settled at the foothills of the Himalayas.','','','Rohillas','',0,0,0,NULL),(86211,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The ruler of Afghanistan who marched on the Punjab in 1751 CE.','','','Ahmadshah Abdali','',0,0,0,NULL),(86212,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The Maratha who replied to Qutub Shah, \'हाँ, बचेंगे तो और भी लढेंगे।\'','','','Dattaji Shinde','',0,0,0,NULL),(86213,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,1,'Text Book','','Name the following : The brother of Nanasaheb Peshwa','','','Raghunathrao','',0,0,0,NULL),(86214,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : The chief of Jats at Mathura','','','Surajmal Jat','',0,0,0,NULL),(86215,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : Defeated Nizam at Rakshasbhuvan near Paithan.','','','Peshwa Madhavrao','',0,0,0,NULL),(86216,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Two persons who helped the wounded Marathas in the battle of Panipat.','','','Surajmal Jat and Rani Kishori','',0,0,0,NULL),(86217,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : He took shelter with the British due to his greed for power.','','','Raghunathrao','',0,0,0,NULL),(86218,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd man out : Raghunathrao, Jayappa Shinde, Malharrao Holkar, Peshwa Madhavrao','','','Peshwa Madhavrao\nReason: The other three undertook the campaign of North India to combat Abdali whereas Madhavrao was not a part of the campaign.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86219,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find the odd man out : Mahadji Shinde, Bajirao, Balaji, Madhavrao','','','Mahadji Shinde\nReason: Except Mahadji Shinde rest of the three were from the Peshwa family.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86220,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Complete the names of the following : N _n_ _ _ _ _ _','','','Nanasaheb','',0,0,0,NULL),(86221,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Complete the names of the following : D _ _ _ _ _ _ ','','','Dattaji','',0,0,0,NULL),(86222,1683,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Complete the names of the following : N _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _','','','Najibkhan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86223,1683,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Arrange in chronological order : \ni. The battle of Rakshasbhuvan\nii. The death of Tipu Sultan\niii. The death of Madhavrao Peshwa\niv. The battle of Panipat\nv. The battle of Buradi Ghat\n','','','i. The battle of Buradi Ghat\nii. The battle of Panipat\niii. The battle of Rakshasbhuvan\niv. The death of Madhavrao Peshwa\nv. The death of Tipu Sultan\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86224,1683,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart :','','','i. Bajiraol\niv. Sadashivraobhau\nvii. Peshwa Narayanrao\nii. Balaji alias Nanasaheb\nv. Bajirao II\niii. Janardan\nvi. Peshwa Madhavrao\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(86225,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who invited the Marathas to subdue the Rohillas?','','','The Nawab of Ayodhya invited the Marathas to subdue the Rohillas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86226,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For which provinces were the Maratha given Subhedari by the Mughals in the treaty of April 1752?','','','The Marathas were given the Subhedari of Ajmer and Agra provinces by the Mughals in the treaty of April 1752.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86227,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Whom did Peshwa Nanasaheb send to get a firm hold over the Punjab and to crush Najibkhan in the north?','','','Peshwa Nanasaheb sent Dattaji Shinde and Janakoji Shinde to the north to get a firm hold over the Punjab and to crush Najibkhan.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86228,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who was the adviser of Najibkhan Rohilla?','','','Qutub Shah was the adviser of Najibkhan Rohilla.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86229,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the Maratha soldiers loose courage in the battle of Panipat?','','','The Maratha soldiers lost courage in the battle of Panipat when they saw that their leader Sadashivraobhau had disappeared in the battle fray after Vishwasrao\'s death.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86230,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Whom did Peshwa Madhavrao keep at bay during his time?','','','Peshwa Madhavrao kept the Nizam and Hyder Ali at bay during his time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86231,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which battle did the Marathas defeat Hyder Ali?','','','The Marathas defeated Hyder Ali in the battle at Moti Talav near Srirangapattan in Karnataka.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86232,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','With whose help did Tipu Sultan of Mysore shake the foundation of British supremacy?','','','Tipu Sultan of Mysore took the help of French and shook the foundation of British supremacy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86233,1683,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the attitude of the local rulers in the north towards the Marathas?','','','i. The Marathas were seen as competitors from the south by the local rulers of the north. So they did not consider the broad view of the Marathas and remained neutral, offering no help to the Marathas.\nii. They did not like the Maratha supremacy and interference in the Delhi court. Some orthodox sects from the North saw the Marathas as people from other religions.\niii. They made Abdali invade India to defeat the Marathas and drive them back to the South, beyond Narmada.\niv. Only Surajmal Jat and Rani Kishori helped the wounded Marathas in the battle of Panipat.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86234,1683,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the Marathas re-establish their power in the North?','','','i. The Maratha prestige had suffered a severe setback after their defeat at Panipat.\nii. Peshwa Madhavrao sent Mahadji Shinde, Tukoji Holkar, Ramchandra Kanade and Visajipant Biniwale to re-establish the Maratha rule in the North. The Jats, the Rohillas and the Rajputs were defeated by the Maratha army.\niii. The Marathas restored the Emperor Shah Alam to Delhi\'s throne, which revived the Maratha power in the North.\niv. Abdali and his descendants did not gather enough courage to invade India again as they had suffered huge losses in the battle of Panipat. They sent a messenger to Pune expressing their wish that Marathas should protect the Empire and control the anarchy in the North.\nIn this way, Marathas successfully re-established their power in the North.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86235,1683,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Conflict with the Afghans','','','i. The Marathas chastised the Rohillas who had migrated from Afghanistan to Rohilkhand.\nii. The ruler of Afghanistan, Ahmadshah Abdali, who was attracted by the riches of India and wanted to bring the region up to Punjab under Afghanistan, attacked Punjab in 1751 CE.\niii. As there was anarchy in the Mughal territory, the Mughal Emperor sought help from Marathas to protect Delhi from the threat of Abdali\'s invasion. He signed a treaty with the Marathas in April 1752 to protect the Mughal power from enemies like the Rohillas, the Jats, the Rajputs and the Afghans.\niv. In return, the Marathas were given the right to collect Chauthai in the territories of Punjab, Multan, Rajputana, Sindh and Rohilkhand and the Subhedari of Ajmer and Agra provinces.\nv. As per the treaty, the Peshwa sent the Shinde-Holkar armies to protect Delhi on behalf of the Chhatrapati. On hearing that the Marathas were marching to Delhi, Abdali went back to his country.\nvi. Since Abdali\'s threat got averted, the Emperor gave the Marathas, Chauthai rights to the Mughal subhas of Kabul, Kandahar and Peshawar which were a part of Abdali\'s Afghanistan.\nvii. According to the treaty, it was the Marathas\' duty to win back these subhas from Abdali.\nviii. A conflict ensued between Abdali and the Marathas which further led to the Third Battle of Panipat.\nix. Abdali invaded India six times during this period.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86236,1683,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Raising the Maratha standard at Attock','','','i. Najibkhan, a Rohilla chief, did not like the Maratha supremacy in the North.\nii. At Najibkhan\'s behest, Ahmadshah Abdali invaded India for the fifth time. He captured Delhi and returned to Afghanistan with a huge booty.\niii. It is then that, Raghunathrao and Malharrao Holkar marched to the North again and re-captured Delhi. They captured the Punjab and drove out Abdali\'s officers right up to Attock (in present day Pakistan).\niv. From Attock, they continued their campaign up to Peshawar but failed to make proper arrangements to look after the newly conquered territory.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86237,1683,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dattaji Shinde','','','i. Dattaji Shinde along with Janakoji Shinde, were sent by the Peshwa to the North to get a firm hold over the Punjab and to defeat Najibkhan.\nii. Najibkhan kept Dattaji busy in negotiations and meanwhile established contacts with Abdali requesting him for help.\niii. On receiving his message, Abdali invaded India and a fierce fight ensued between Abdali and Dattaji at Buradi Ghat on the banks of river Yamuna. Dattaji fought with tremendous valour but met a heroic death in this battle.\niv. In the last moments, when Dattaji was severely wounded and collapsed, Najibkhan\'s adviser Qutub Shah asked him, if he would still like to fight, to which Dattaji replied with pride that if he would be alive, he would surely fight.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86238,1683,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sadashivraobhau','','','i. Sadashivraobhau was the son of Chimajiappa and the cousin of Peshwa Nanasaheb.\nii. He had explained the broad viewpoint of the Marathas in his correspondence with the rulers in the North, that they were all native to the land. He had also stated that Abdali was an enemy from a foreign country. However, the northern rulers did not respond favourably to him and took a neutral stand.\niii. Later, Peshwa Nanasaheb sent Sadashivraobhau along with his eldest son Vishwasrao to the north to crush Abdali.\niv. Sadashivraobhau had a huge army and powerful artillery with him. Ibrahimkhan Gardi was the chief of his artillery.\nv. He had defeated the Nizam in the battle of Udgir in 1760 CE, on the strength of this artillery.\nvi. In his campaign of the North, he had captured Delhi before the Battle of Panipat.\nvii. In the Battle of Panipat, Sadashivraobhau went into frenzy when he heard that Vishwasrao had been hit by an enemy bullet and attacked the enemy fiercely.\nviii. He disappeared in the battle fray and the Maratha soldiers lost courage on seeing that their leader had disappeared, due to which the Marathas faced defeat in this battle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86239,1683,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','The after effects of the battle of Panipat','','','i. On 14 January 1761, the Marathas opened the third battle of Panipat, by attacking Abdali\'s army.\nii. The Marathas lost in this battle and suffered severe losses.\niii. A whole generation of young men in Maharashtra including Peshwa\'s son Vishwasrao and many brave Sardars were killed on the battlefield. Sadashivraobhau also disappeared in the battle fray. Approximately 150000 people were killed in this battle.\niv. Taking advantage of the defeat of the Marathas, the Nizam invaded the Maratha territory again.\nv. The Sultan of Mysore, Hyder Ali also attacked the Maratha territory in Karnataka.\nvi. While, the Nizam was defeated by the Marathas under leadership of Peshwa Madhavrao at Rakshasbhuvan near Paithan, Hyder Ali was defeated by Marathas in a battle at Moti Talav near Srirangapattan. Marathas overcame this great defeat of Panipat and revived their supremacy in the politics of the North.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86240,1683,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Tipu Sultan','','','i. After Hyder Ali\'s death in 1782 CE, his son Tipu became the Sultan of Mysore.\nii. He was a skilled warrior, a scholar and a poet.\niii. He increased the might of his kingdom using his competence.\niv. He shook the foundation of British supremacy by establishing contact with French.\nv. He died in a battle against the British in 1799 CE.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86241,1683,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Emperor entered into a treaty with the Marathas in April 1752.','','','i. The Mughals faced the threat of an invasion by Ahmadshah Abdali, when he attacked Punjab in 1751 CE.\nii. During this time, there was anarchy in the Mughal territory.\niii. The Emperor was sure that only the Maratha strength and integrity could provide protection to Delhi.\niv. So they sought the help of the Marathas and entered into a treaty with them ensuring protection to the Mughals against the enemies like the Rohillas, the Jats, the Rajputs and the Afghans.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86242,1683,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A conflict between the Marathas and Abdali was inevitable.','','','i. The threat of Abdali was thwarted due to the Marathas and hence the Mughal Emperor gave them the chauthai rights to the Mughal Subhas including Kabul, Kandahar and Peshawar.\nii. These were previously Mughal Sabhas, however currently they were part of Abdali\'s Afganistan.\niii. As per the treaty of 1752 , it was the duty of the Marathas to win these Subhas back from Abdali and annexe them to the Mughal empire.\niv. On the other hand, Abdali wanted to bring the region upto Punjab under Afghan control.\nHence, a conflict between the Marathas and Abdali was inevitable.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86243,1683,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Marathas faced defeat in the Battle of Panipat.','','','i. Vishwasrao was hit by an enemy bullet in the battle of Panipat.\nii. Sadashivraobhau went into frenzy on hearing this and fiercely attacked the enemy.\niii. But Sadashivraobhau disappeared in the battle fray and thereafter the Maratha soldiers lost their courage in absence of their leader.\niv. Taking advantage of this, the reserve contingents of Abdali launched an attack on the Marathas with a fresh vigour.\nThus, the Marathas faced a brutal defeat in the Battle of Panipat.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86244,1683,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A war between the Marathas and the British ensued.','','','i. After Peshwa Madhavrao, Peshwa Narayanrao and Peshwa Sawai Madhavrao were in the grip of family feuds.\nii. Ragunathrao, who had once strengthened the Maratha power at Attock, sought shelter with the British to satisfy his greed for power.\niii. This led to a war between the Marathas and the British.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86245,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Pathans who had migrated from Afghanistan and settled to the north-west of Ayodhya at the Himalayan foothills were called ____\n(A) Rajputs  (B) Rohillas  (C) Mughals\n','','','B) Rohillas','',0,0,0,NULL),(86246,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Marathas continued their campaign up to ____ after winning at Attock.\n(A) Kabul  (B) Peshawar  (C) Kandahar\n','','','B) Peshawar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86247,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ was Chimajiappa\'s son and Nanasaheb\'s cousin.\n(A) Sadashivraobhau  (B) Madhavrao  (C) Dattaji\n','','','A) Sadashivraobhau','',0,0,0,NULL),(86248,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Peshwa Madhavrao died in ____ CE.\n(A) 1772  (B) 1761  (C) 1755\n','','','A) 1772','',0,0,0,NULL),(86249,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who was killed after being hit by an enemy bullet in the Battle of Panipat?','','','Vishwasrao died after being hit by an enemy bullet in the Battle of Panipat.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86252,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where did Dattaji Shinde breathe his last breath?','','','Dattaji Shinde breathed his last breath at the Buradi Ghat on the banks of Yamuna river in a battle against Abdali.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86253,1683,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What did Hyder Ali agree to after the defeat in the battle of Moti Talav?','','','Hyder Ali agreed to give up the region to the north of the river Tungabhadra to the Marathas after facing defeat in the battle of Moti Talav.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86254,1683,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Peshwa Madhavrao','','','i. Peshwa Madhavrao succeeded his father Peshwa Nanasaheb after his death.\nii. During his reign, Peshwa Madhavrao kept the Nizam and Hyder Ali at bay. He defeated the Nizam at Rakshasbhuvan near Paithan.\niii. He re-established the Maratha supremacy in the north after the Battle of Panipat.\niv. He is described as an honest, hard-working and determined administrator who was keen on public welfare.\nv. He dug many wells in Pune and increased the water supply to the city and also ensured the welfare of the farmers.\nvi. Under his rule, administrators like Nana Phadnavis and judges like Ramshashtri Prabhune emerged. He reformed the judiciary for better justice to common people, started the artillery and ammunition factories and even established a mint to make coins.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86255,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After Bajirao I, Shahu Maharaj appointed his son ____ as the Peshwa.\na) Raghunathrao  b) Balaji Bajirao  c) Madhavrao\n','','','b) Balaji Bajirao','',0,0,0,NULL),(86256,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ahmadshah Abdali was the ruler of _____.\na) Afghanistan  b) Iran  c) Mysore\n','','','a) Afghanistan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86257,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Marathas agreed to protect the Mughal Empire under the treaty of ____ CE.\na) 1752  b) 1740  c) 1761\n','','','a) 1752','',0,0,0,NULL),(86258,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','According to the 1752 treaty, the Marathas were granted the right to collect ____ in several Mughal territories.\na) Land tax  b) Chauthai  c) Jizya\n','','','b) Chauthai','',0,0,0,NULL),(86259,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Maratha standard was hoisted at Attock in ____ CE.\na) 1758  b) 1761  c) 1750\n','','','a) 1758','',0,0,0,NULL),(86260,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dattaji Shinde was killed in the battle against Abdali at _____.\na) Panipat  b) Buradi Ghat  c) Palkhed\n','','','b) Buradi Ghat','',0,0,0,NULL),(86261,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Third Battle of Panipat was fought in ____ CE.\na) 1758  b) 1761  c) 1772\n','','','b) 1761','',0,0,0,NULL),(86262,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Peshwa Madhavrao defeated the Nizam at ____ near Paithan.\na) Rakshasbhuvan  b) Talikota  c) Moti Talav\n','','','a) Rakshasbhuvan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86263,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Hyder Ali was the Sultan of ____.\na) Delhi  b) Mysore  c) Hyderabad\n','','','b) Mysore','',0,0,0,NULL),(86264,1683,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Maratha power in North India was revived under the leadership of ____.\na) Mahadji Shinde  b) Dattaji Shinde  c) Sadashivraobhau\n','','','a) Mahadji Shinde','',0,0,0,NULL),(86265,1683,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the causes that led to the conflict between the Marathas and Ahmadshah Abdali.','','','1.	Ahmadshah Abdali, ruler of Afghanistan, invaded India attracted by its riches.\n2.	The Mughal Empire was weak and unable to resist Abdali’s invasions.\n3.	The Mughals sought help from the Marathas to protect Delhi and their empire.\n4.	As per the treaty of 1752, the Marathas were to defend the Mughals, which brought them into direct conflict with Abdali.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86266,1683,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the significance of the Third Battle of Panipat.','','','1.	The battle was fought between the Marathas and Ahmadshah Abdali on 14 January 1761.\n2.	The Marathas fought bravely under Sadashivraobhau and Vishwasrao, but were defeated.\n3.	A large number of brave Maratha Sardars and soldiers were killed in the battle.\n4.	It was a major setback for the Marathas, yet it showed their patriotic vision — “India is for Indians.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86267,1683,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the achievements of Peshwa Madhavrao.','','','1.	Madhavrao succeeded Nanasaheb as Peshwa and revived Maratha power after Panipat.\n2.	He defeated the Nizam at Rakshasbhuvan and kept Hyder Ali under control.\n3.	He re-established Maratha supremacy in the North through efficient leadership.\n4.	Madhavrao was known as an honest, hardworking, and welfare-oriented administrator.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86268,1683,'descriptive','3a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the role of the Marathas in protecting the Mughal Empire.','','','1.	The Mughal Emperor made a treaty with the Marathas in 1752 CE for protection against enemies.\n2.	The Marathas protected Delhi from Rohillas, Jats, Rajputs, and Afghans.\n3.	They received rights to collect Chauthai and Subhedari of Ajmer and Agra.\n4.	The Marathas even raised their flag at Attock, showing their power in North India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86269,1683,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the Marathas re-establish their supremacy after the Battle of Panipat?','','','1.	Madhavrao Peshwa sent Mahadji Shinde, Tukoji Holkar, and others to the North.\n2.	The Marathas defeated the Jats, Rohillas, and Rajputs.\n3.	They restored Emperor Shah Alam to the throne of Delhi under their protection.\n4.	The Marathas once again became powerful in northern Indian politics.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86270,1683,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Ahmadshah Abdali</td>\n    <td>(a) Battle of Panipat (1761 CE)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Sadashivraobhau</td>\n    <td>(b) Chief of artillery</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Ibrahimkhan Gardi</td>\n    <td>(c) Ruler of Afghanistan</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Vishwasrao</td>\n    <td>(d) Son of Nanasaheb Peshwa</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – b), (4 – d)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86271,1683,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Peshwa Madhavrao</td>\n    <td>(b) Honest and capable administrator</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Hyder Ali</td>\n    <td>(a) Battle of Moti Talav near Srirangapattan</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Raghunathrao</td>\n    <td>(c) Restored Shah Alam to the Delhi throne</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Mahadji Shinde</td>\n    <td>(d) Sought shelter with the British</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 – b), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86272,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ was the founder of the Holkar rule at Indore.\n(A) Ahilyabai Holkar  (B) Malharrao Holkar  (C) Khanderao Holkar\n','','','B) Malharrao Holkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86273,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In 1751 CE, the Bhosales of Nagpur won the Odisha territory from ____\n(A) Ali Vardi Khan  (B) Najib Khan  (C) Khanderao\n','','','A) Ali Vardi Khan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86274,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','From Mumbai, the British marched on the Marathas via ____\n(A) Goa  (B) Surat  (C) Borghat\n','','','C) Borghat','',0,0,0,NULL),(86275,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ made Vadodara in Gujarat a seat of their power.\n(A) Gaikwads  (B) Holkars  (C) Shindes\n','','','A) Gaikwads','',0,0,0,NULL),(86276,1684,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : She took the charge of the Indore administration.','','','Ahilyabai Holkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86277,1684,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : The most capable and brave man in Bhosale family of Nagpur.','','','Raghuji Bhosale','',0,0,0,NULL),(86278,1684,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : He reinstated the Emperor on the throne of Delhi.','','','Mahadji Shinde','',0,0,0,NULL),(86279,1684,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : He managed the politics in South.','','','Nana Phadnavis','',0,0,0,NULL),(86280,1684,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Son of Raghunathrao','','','Bajirao II','',0,0,0,NULL),(86281,1684,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The British flag','','','Union Jack','',0,0,0,NULL),(86282,1684,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Arrange in chronological order : \ni. The battle of Ashti\nii. Maratha dominance over Odisha\niii. The British flew the Union Jack in Pune\n','','','i. Maratha dominance over Odisha\nii. The British flew the Union Jack in Pune\niii. The battle of Ashti\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86283,1684,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart : ','','','i. Indore\nii. Bhosale\niii. Gwalior\niv. Gaikwad\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(86284,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For how many years did Ahilyabai Holkar rule the Indore region?','','','Ahilyabai Holkar ruled for twenty-eight years in the Indore region.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86285,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which jagir was given to Parasoji Bhosale during the reign of Shahu Maharaj?','','','During the reign of Shahu Maharaj, Parasoji Bhosale was given the jagir of Varhad and Gondawan.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86286,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does Maratha ditch mean?','','','The British dug a ditch around the city of Kolkata to protect it from a possible Maratha attack, which came to be known as the Maratha ditch.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86287,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Under the guidance of which French military expert did Mahadji Shinde train his army?','','','Mahadji Shinde trained his army under the guidance of a French military expert de Boigne.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86288,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who strengthened the navy raised by Shivaji Maharaj?','','','Kanhoji Angre and his son Tulaji strengthened the navy raised by Shivaji Maharaj.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86289,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What weakened the Maratha power?','','','The internal conflict between the Sardars weakened the Maratha power.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86290,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When and where did the British defeat the Marathas?','','','The British defeated the Marathas in 1818 CE in the battle of Ashti in Solapur district.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86291,1684,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Malharrao Holkar revive the Maratha prestige in the North?','','','i. Malharrao Holkar was the founder of the Holkar rule at Indore.\nii. He served the Maratha state for a long time and was well versed in guerrilla warfare.\niii. He proved his valour in the northern campaigns of the Marathas during the time of Bajirao I and Nanasaheb Peshwa.\niv. He also played an important role in establishing the Maratha supremacy in Malwa and Rajputana.\nThus, Malharrao Holkar revived the Maratha prestige in the North.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86292,1684,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did Mahadji Shinde bring the Delhi Empire under the control of Marathas?','','','i. Knowing that the Marathas have become weak after the battle of Panipat, the British began to take interest in the politics at Delhi.\nii. They got the Diwani rights of Bengal province and wanted to capture the Emperor of Delhi.\niii. In these conditions, Mahadji Shinde defeated the British and reinstated the Emperor on the throne.\niv. The emperor gave him the title of \'Vakil-i-Mutlak i.e. Chief agent of the emperor, thus giving him the authority of civil and military rights.\nThis achievement of Mahadji Shinde brought the Delhi Empire under the control of Marathas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86293,1684,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','After the death of Peshwa Madhavrao, which Maratha Sardars set right the disarray of the Maratha State?','','','i. After the death of Peshwa Madhavrao, Mahadji Shinde restored the Maratha supremacy in the North.\nii. Nana Phadnavis managed the affairs of the South. In this, he received the help from the Patwardhans, Haripant Phadke, the Rastes and other Sardars and was able to establish Maratha supremacy in the South.\niii. The Holkars of Indore, Bhosales of Nagpur, Shindes of Gwalior, and Gaikwads of Vadodara brought glory to the Maratha power based on their valour, leadership qualities, and capability.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86294,1684,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','The work done by Ahilyabai Holkar','','','i. Punyashloka Ahilyabai Holkar was the wife of Khanderao, who was the son of Malharrao Holkar.\nii. After the deaths of Khanderao and Malharrao, Ahilyabai took the control of the Indore administration.\niii. She was a capable, astute and excellent administrator.\niv. She made new laws for agricultural cess, revenue collection, etc. She also put the affairs of the State in proper order.\nv. She strove to bring more land under cultivation, dug wells for farmers, promoted trade & industry and built lakes & tanks.\nvi. She also built temples, Ghats, Maths, Dharamshalas, drinking water facilities at all important places of pilgrimage in the four quarters of India.\nvii. She looked into judicial matters personally and provided rightful justice.\nviii. She ruled for 28 years and enhanced the Maratha prestige in the North. During her reign, there was peace and order in the kingdom and the people were happy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86295,1684,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Bhosales of Nagpur','','','i. During the reign of Shahu Maharaj, Parasoji Bhosale was given the jagir of Varhad and Gondawan.\nii. Raghuji was the most capable and brave man in Bhosale family of Nagpur.\niii. He brought the area surrounding Tirucherapalli and Arcot in the South under Maratha dominance.\niv. Raghuji Bhosale brought the territories of Bengal, Bihar and Odisha under Maratha dominance when he was assigned the chauthai rights of these territories by Shahu Maharaj.\nv. The Bhosales of Nagpur won the Odisha territory from Ali Vardi Khan in 1751 CE and the Marathas dominated this territory till 1803 CE.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86296,1684,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','The valour shown by Mahadji Shinde','','','i. Mahadji Shinde was as brave and astute as his father Ranoji Shinde. Therefore, he was given the family title of Sardar by Peshwa Madhavrao.\nii. He restored the Maratha supremacy and reputation in the North, which was lost after the Maratha defeat at Panipat.\niii. Realising that the guerilla tactics were not enough for the plains of the North, he trained his army and modernised his artillery under the guidance of de Boigne, a French military expert. With the help of this trained army, he subdued the Rohillas, the Jats, the Rajputs and the Bundellas.\niv. Mahadji Shinde defeated the British when they tried to capture the Emperor of Delhi and also when they marched on the Marathas via Borghat.\nv. He also defeated Najib Khan\'s grandson Ghulam Kadir when he captured the Red Fort and tortured the Emperor and his begums for their wealth. Mahadji also reinstated the Emperor on the throne of Delhi.\nvi. Mahadji defeated the difficult situations with strong determination and looked after the Delhi affairs during 1784 CE to 1794 CE.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86297,1684,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','The Maratha rule in Gujarat','','','i. The foundation of Maratha power in Gujarat was laid by Khanderao Dabhade and his son Trimbakrao.\nii. After the death of Khanderao, his wife Umabai defeated the Mughal Sardar of Ahmadabad and conquered the fort there.\niii. Later, the Gaikwads made Vadodara in Gujarat a seat of their pow\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86298,1684,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mahadji Shinde defeated Kadir.','','','i. The heirs of Najib Khan who were responsible for the battle of Panipat, were still plotting against the Marathas in Rohilkhand.\nii. Najib Khan\'s grandson Ghulam Kadir captured the Red Fort.\niii. He tortured the Emperor and his begums for their wealth. He removed the Emperor\'s eyes and took hold of the royal treasure.\niv. In these circumstances, Mahadji defeated Kadir. He recovered the wealth from him and returned it to the Emperor. He reinstated the Emperor on the throne of Delhi and controlled the politics of India by putting the Emperor under Maratha control.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86299,1684,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British were forced to surrender the custody of Raghunathrao to the Marathas.','','','i. Due to the family disputes amongst the Peshwas, Raghunathrao went to the British camp as he wanted to become a Peshwa with the help of the British.\nii. The Maratha Statesmen were against this and this led to an inevitable conflict between the Marathas and the British.\niii. The British marched on the Marathas via Borghat from Mumbai.\niv. The Maratha army under the leadership of Mahadaji Shinde blocked the supply of foodgrains to the British, using guerrilla tactics.\nv. Both the armies met at Wadgaon and the British were defeated in this battle.\nIt-was then that the British were forced to surrender the custody of Raghunathrao to the Marathas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86300,1684,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','The Maratha rule came to an end.','','','i. After the death of Mahadji Shinde and Nana Phadnavis, the Maratha power began to weaken.\nii. Bajirao II, the son of Raghunathrao was the Peshwa at that time. He did not have leadership qualities and had many vices.\niii. He was unsuccessful in uniting the Maratha Sardars and the internal conflict between the Maratha sardars weakened their power.\niv. The British replaced the Marathas in the North and South.\nv. In 1817 CE, the British captured Pune and hoisted their flag \'Union Jack\' there.\nvi. In 1818 CE the British defeated Marathas in the battle of Ashti in Solapur district.\nBy this, the Maratha rule came to an end.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86301,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Khanderao died in the battle of ____\n(A) Kumbheri  (B) Panipat  (C) Udgir\n','','','A) Kumbheri','',0,0,0,NULL),(86302,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ had rightly judged the capabilities of Ranoji Shinde and made him a Sardar in the North.\n(A) Madhavrao  (B) Bajirao I  (C) Nanasaheb\n','','','B) Bajirao I','',0,0,0,NULL),(86303,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Najib Khan\'s grandson ____ captured the Red Fort.\n(A) Ghulam Kadir  (B) Hyder Ali  (C) Nizam Ali\n','','','A) Ghulam Kadir','',0,0,0,NULL),(86304,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Till ____ Delhi was under Maratha control.\n(A) 1757 CE  (B) 1772 CE  (C) 1803 CE\n','','','C) 1803 CE','',0,0,0,NULL),(86305,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Whose effort reflects the cultural integration of the country?','','','Ahilyabai Holkar\'s effort reflects the cultural integration of the country.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86306,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which title did the Emperor of Delhi give to Mahadji Shinde?','','','The Emperor of Delhi gave to Mahadji Shinde the title \'Vakil-i-Mutlak\' i.e. Chief agent of the emperor.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86307,1684,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who laid the foundation of Maratha power in Gujarat?','','','Khanderao Dabhade and his son Trimbakrao laid the foundation of Maratha power in Gujarat.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86308,1684,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Consequences of battle of Ashti','','','i. After the defeat of the Marathas in the battle of Ashti in Solapur district in 1818 CE, the Maratha power came to an end.\nii. The British gained control over a major part of India.\niii. Because of this, India was exposed to the Western world, which led to many changes in India\'s social system. Many old customs and superstitions came to an end and India went into a major transformation.\niv. Thus, the medieval period of Indian history came to an end and the Modern period began.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86309,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The founder of the Holkar rule at Indore was -----\n(a) Yashwantrao Holkar  (b) Malharrao Holkar  (c) Khanderao Holkar\n','','','b) Malharrao Holkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86310,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ahilyabai Holkar was the wife of -----\n(a) Ranoji Shinde  (b) Khanderao Holkar  (c) Raghuji Bhosale\n','','','b) Khanderao Holkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86311,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The capital of the Holkar rule was at -----\n(a) Gwalior  (b) Indore  (c) Nagpur\n','','','b) Indore','',0,0,0,NULL),(86312,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Bhosales of Nagpur were granted the Varhad and Gondwan jagirs by -----\n(a) Bajirao I  (b) Shahu Maharaj  (c) Madhavrao Peshwa\n','','','b) Shahu Maharaj','',0,0,0,NULL),(86313,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Raghuji Bhosale won the Odisha territory from -----\n(a) Hyder Ali  (b) Ali Vardi Khan  (c) Tipu Sultan\n','','','b) Ali Vardi Khan','',0,0,0,NULL),(86314,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The founder of the Shinde family’s rule in the North was -----\n(a) Mahadji Shinde  (b) Ranoji Shinde  (c) Jayappa Shinde\n','','','b) Ranoji Shinde','',0,0,0,NULL),(86315,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mahadji Shinde modernised his army with the help of the French officer -----\n(a) de Boigne  (b) Dupleix  (c) Clive\n','','','a) de Boigne','',0,0,0,NULL),(86316,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The title ‘Vakil-i-Mutlak’ was conferred upon Mahadji Shinde by -----\n(a) Shahu Maharaj  (b) The Emperor of Delhi  (c) Peshwa Madhavrao\n','','','b) The Emperor of Delhi','',0,0,0,NULL),(86317,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British were defeated by the Marathas at the battle of -----\n(a) Panipat  (b) Wadgaon  (c) Ashti\n','','','b) Wadgaon','',0,0,0,NULL),(86318,1684,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The final defeat of the Marathas took place in the battle of -----\n(a) Surat  (b) Panipat  (c) Ashti\n','','','c) Ashti','',0,0,0,NULL),(86319,1684,'descriptive','4',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ahilyabai Holkar is known as an ideal ruler.','','','•	She looked after her people with care and justice.\n•	She improved agriculture by digging wells and building lakes.\n•	She encouraged trade and industry.\n•	She built temples, ghats, and dharamshalas at pilgrimage places.\n•	She ruled efficiently for 28 years and made her people happy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86320,1684,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mahadji Shinde is called the restorer of Maratha prestige in North India.','','','•	After the defeat at Panipat, he revived Maratha power in the North.\n•	He modernised his army with the help of the French officer de Boigne.\n•	He defeated the Rohillas, Jats, and Rajputs.\n•	He reinstated the Emperor on the throne of Delhi and gained the title Vakil-i-Mutlak.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86321,1684,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Marathas became powerful in North and South India.','','','•	The brave Sardar families like Holkars, Shindes, Bhosales, and Gaikwads expanded Maratha power.\n•	They fought bravely and established control over large territories.\n•	They worked unitedly under the leadership of the Peshwas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86322,1684,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The battle of Wadgaon was important in Maratha history.','','','•	The British army was defeated by the Marathas.\n•	The British were forced to surrender Raghunathrao to the Marathas.\n•	It proved the strength and unity of the Marathas under Mahadji Shinde.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86323,1684,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British replaced the Marathas as the main power in India.','','','•	After Mahadji Shinde and Nana Phadanvis died, the Marathas became weak.\n•	Peshwa Bajirao II lacked leadership qualities.\n•	Internal conflicts among the Sardars weakened their unity.\n•	The British took advantage and defeated the Marathas in 1818 CE.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86324,1684,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Holkars</td>\n    <td>(d) Brought peace and prosperity to Indore</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Bhosales</td>\n    <td>(c) Expanded Maratha rule in Bengal, Bihar, and Odisha</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Shindes</td>\n    <td>(a) Re-established Maratha power in the North after Panipat</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Angres</td>\n    <td>(b) Strengthened Maratha navy</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 - d), (2 - c), (3 - a), (4 - b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86325,1684,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Mahadji Shinde</td>\n    <td>(b) Defeated British in the Battle of Wadgaon</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Ahilyabai Holkar</td>\n    <td>(a) Built temples and lakes for people’s welfare</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Nana Phadanvis</td>\n    <td>(c)  Lacked leadership qualities and caused Maratha downfall</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Bajirao II</td>\n    <td>(d) Managed administration of the South</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 - b), (2 - a), (3 - d), (4 - c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86326,1684,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the contribution of Ahilyabai Holkar to the Maratha Empire.','','','1.	Ahilyabai Holkar was the daughter-in-law of Malharrao Holkar and took charge after his death.\n2.	She improved agriculture by digging wells, building tanks, and bringing new land under cultivation.\n3.	She promoted trade, built temples, dharamshalas, ghats, and water facilities across India.\n4.	She ruled for 28 years and brought peace, prosperity, and cultural unity to her kingdom.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86327,1684,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the achievements of Mahadji Shinde in the North.','','','1.	Mahadji Shinde re-established Maratha supremacy in North India after the defeat at Panipat.\n2.	He modernized his army and artillery with the help of the French expert de Boigne.\n3.	He defeated the Rohillas, Jats, and Rajputs and reinstated the Mughal Emperor on the throne of Delhi.\n4.	He received the title Vakil-i-Mutlak and restored Maratha prestige and political control over Delhi.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86328,1684,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the contributions of the Bhosales of Nagpur to the Maratha Empire?','','','1.	The Bhosales of Nagpur expanded Maratha rule in the South, especially in Tirucherapalli and Arcot.\n2.	Shahu Maharaj granted them chauthai rights over Bengal, Bihar, and Odisha.\n3.	Raghuji Bhosale captured Odisha from Ali Vardi Khan in 1751 CE.\n4.	They maintained Maratha influence in Odisha till 1803 CE and strengthened the empire’s southern boundary.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86329,1684,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the role of Nana Phadanvis in strengthening the Maratha Empire.','','','1.	Nana Phadanvis was a wise and efficient administrator during the Peshwa period.\n2.	He managed the southern affairs of the Maratha Empire while Mahadji Shinde handled the North.\n3.	He worked with leaders like the Patwardhans, Haripant Phadke, and the Rastes to stabilize the State.\n4.	Under his guidance, Maratha supremacy was re-established in the South and peace was restored.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86330,1684,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the reasons for the decline of the Maratha power?','','','1.	After the death of capable leaders like Mahadji Shinde and Nana Phadanvis, leadership weakened.\n2.	Bajirao II, the last Peshwa, lacked leadership qualities and unity among the Sardars broke down.\n3.	Internal conflicts among the Sardars made the empire vulnerable to British attacks.\n4.	In 1818 CE, the British defeated the Marathas at Ashti, leading to the end of Maratha rule in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86331,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maratha power lasted for ____ years.\n(A) 100  (B) 150  (C) 200\n','','','B) 150','',0,0,0,NULL),(86332,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the Hindawi Swaraj, the ____ was responsible for the protection of the village, whereas the ____ handled the revenue.\n(A) Patil, Kulkarni  (B) Kulkarni, Patil  (C) None of these\n','','','A) Patil, Kulkarni','',0,0,0,NULL),(86333,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the Hindawi Swaraj, education was imparted through pathshalas and ____\n(A) tamashas  (B) temples  (C) madarasas\n','','','C) madarasas','',0,0,0,NULL),(86334,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ was the famous architect of the Maratha period of Swaraj.\n(A) Anuprao  (B) Hiroji Indulkar  (C) Mallamma Desai\n','','','B) Hiroji Indulkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(86335,1685,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Following :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Ambabai temple</td>\n    <td>c. Kolhapur</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Ghrineshwar temple</td>\n    <td>d. Verul</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Bhavanidevi temple</td>\n    <td>e. Pratapgad</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Saptkoteshwar temple</td>\n    <td>a. Goa</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>5. Kalaram temple</td>\n    <td>b. Nashik</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – e), (4 – a), (5 – b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86336,1685,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : This festival was considered as one of the three and a half most auspicious occasions and so many new things were started on this day.','','','Dasara','',0,0,0,NULL),(86337,1685,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Apart from songs and dances, this was also the popular form of entertainment during the period of Maratheshahi.','','','Tamasha','',0,0,0,NULL),(86338,1685,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : An important talav in Diveghat in Hadapsar near Pune city.','','','Mastani','',0,0,0,NULL),(86339,1685,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The style in which temples were built in Shivaji Maharaja\'s period.','','','Hemadpanti','',0,0,0,NULL),(86340,1685,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The temple on the confluence of the Godavari and Pravara.','','','Shiva Temple','',0,0,0,NULL),(86341,1685,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The paintings here are remarkable feature of the Peshwa period.','','','Shaniwarwada','',0,0,0,NULL),(86342,1685,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Chess, Gangifa, Dice, Atyapatya','','','Atyapatya\nReason: Atyapatya is an outdoor game, whereas other three are indoor games.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86343,1685,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Mahanubhav, Nath, Teeka, Ramdasi','','','Teeka\nReason: Teeka is a form of literature while others are panths.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86344,1685,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find odd man out : Kalaram temple, Mohiniraj temple, Saptkoteshwar temple, Shiva temple','','','Saptkoteshwar temple\nReason: Saptkoteshwar temple was built by Shivaji Maharaj, whereas others are of Peshwa period.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86345,1685,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Complete the chart : ','','','i. Farmer\nii. Vatandar\niii. Kulkarni\niv. Patil\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(86346,1685,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart : ','','','i. Shaniwarwada in Pune,\nii. Vishrambagwada in Pune\niii. Sarkarwada in Nashik\niv. Raghunathrao Peshwa\'s wada at Kopargaon\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(86347,1685,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the chart : ','','','i. Ragho\nii. Mankoji\niii. Anuprao\niv. Shivram\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(86348,1685,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Use the following points to compare life in the era of Shivaji Maharaj and today\'s life.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Sr. No.</th>\n    <th>Points</th>\n    <th>At the time of Shivaji Maharaj</th>\n    <th>Today</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>Transactions</td>\n    <td>---------</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>Houses</td>\n    <td>-------------</td>\n    <td>Permanently built, well-constructed cement-concrete multi-storeyed houses</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>Transportation</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n    <td>Bus, Railway, Aeroplanes</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>Entertainment</td>\n    <td>------------</td>\n    <td>---------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>v.</td>\n    <td>Script</td>\n    <td>----------------</td>\n    <td>---------------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Sr. No.</th>\n    <th>Points</th>\n    <th>At the time of Shivaji Maharaj</th>\n    <th>Today</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>Transactions</td>\n    <td>Barter System. (Balutedars were paid in kind)</td>\n    <td>Money (coins and currencies)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>Houses</td>\n    <td>In villages, houses were built with mud, bricks and wood.<br>\n    In cities, single and double storey houses were built using baked/unbaked bricks, wooden columns, rafts, boards, dressed stones, arches, finely prepared lime, round tiles for the roof, mud and bamboo.</td>\n    <td>Permanent, well-constructed cement-concrete multi-storeyed houses</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>Transportation</td>\n    <td>Horses, Bullocks and Small Boats</td>\n    <td>Bus, Railway, Aeroplanes</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>Entertainment</td>\n    <td>Tamasha, songs and dances</td>\n    <td>Television, theatres, cinema halls, internet, etc.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>v.</td>\n    <td>Script</td>\n    <td>Modi</td>\n    <td>Devnagari and Roman</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(86349,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why were Anushthans performed?','','','Anushthans were performed to avoid the wrath of Gods and planets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86350,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which era is known as Maratheshahi?','','','The period from 1630 CE to 1810 CE is known as Maratheshahi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86351,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which references to restoration in Maratheshahi are related to architecture?','','','The references to restoration in Maratheshahi that are related to architecture are - Kasba Ganapati Mandir, building of the Lal Mahal, construction on Rajgad and Raigad, building of sea forts, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86352,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention the noteworthy symbols of the medieval wada culture?','','','The noteworthy symbols of the medieval wada culture are - Shaniwarwada and Vishrambagwada in Pune, Sarkarwada in Nashik, Raghunathrao Peshwa\'s wada at Kopargaon, the wadas of the Satara Chhatrapati, as also the old wadas at Wai, Menvali, Toke, Shrigonde and Pandharpur.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86353,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who was the famous painter at the time of Sawai Madhavrao Peshwa?','','','The famous painter at the time of Sawai Madhavrao Peshwa was Gangaram Tambat.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86354,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When and where have the Marathi plays emerged?','','','Marathi plays have emerged near the end of the seventeenth century at Tanjavur in the South.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86355,1685,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the administration at the village level during Maratheshahi?','','','The administration that ran at the village level during Maratheshahi is as follows:\ni. Agriculture and various occupations based on agriculture were the main sources of production in villages.\nii. The Patil was responsible for the protection of the village, whereas the Kulkarni handled the revenue.\niii. For the work done, the Patil not only received land as his inam but also received a part of the village revenue.\niv. The balutedars were paid in the form of objects, foodgrains, etc. for the work they did for the village people.\nv. Peasants worked in their fields in the kali i.e. land, earth or soil, and others, in pandhari i.e. within the village precincts.\nvi. The affairs of the village were conducted with mutual understanding among the people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86356,1685,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What customs and traditions existed during Maratheshahi?','','','i. During Maratheshahi, customs like child marriages, having many wives and marriages of widows existed.\nii. Last rites of dead people were performed in the form of burning, burying and immersion as per the respective tradition.\niii. Muhurta or auspicious occasions were sought for everything, that also included battles. People believed in dreams and omens. To avoid wrath of Gods and planets, anushthans were performed. For the same purpose, charity was also done.\niv. Due to lack of scientific attitude, people believed in astrology. Hence, more than medical treatments, people gave importance to offerings to God (navas).\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86357,1685,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the religious life of the people during Maratheshahi?','','','i. During Maratheshahi, the two religions that were prevalent were Hindu and Muslim.\nii. Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj had a liberal religious policy.\niii. In that period, the general thinking was that everyone should follow his/her religion, and thereby not impose it on others. The followers of both the religions equally participated in the celebration of each other\'s festivals.\niv. The government gave grants to pathshalas, temples, madarasas and mosques.\nv. Similarly, Varkari, Mahanubhav, Datta, Nath and Ramdasi panths were prevalent.\nIn this way, religious life of the people during Maratheshahi was liberal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86358,1685,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the village pattern during the Peshwa period.','','','i. During the Peshwa period, the pattern that was followed for setting up a village was:\na) roads cutting one another at right angles,\nb) stone construction along the sides and\nc) extensive ghats (steps) along the river bank.\nii. A drinking water supply system was also built similar to the one in Ahmednagar and Bijapur.\niii. The Peshwas also built underground pipes, small dams, gardens and courts, tanks, fountains, etc.\niv. The Mastani Talav at Diveghat, Hadapsar near Pune city is noteworthy feature of this period.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86359,1685,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the structure of the wadas.','','','i. In order to built wadas, both Baked as well as unbaked bricks were used.\nii. For the purpose of construction, wooden columns, rafts, boards, dressed stones, arches, finely prepared lime, round tiles for the roof, mud and bamboo were used.\niii. The wadas were also decorated with sketches, paintings, wooden sculptures and mirrors.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86360,1685,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Standard of living in Maratheshahi','','','i. In Maratheshahi, majority of people resided in villages that were mostly self-sufficient. Only salt had to be imported from other places.\nii. The needs of farmers were limited as they grew jowar, bajra, wheat, ragi, maize, rice and other grains in their fields. Their daily diet consisted of bhakari, onion, chutney and an accompaniment.\niii. Rich people\'s diet consisted of rice, dal, chapatis, vegetables, salads, milk and dahi products.\niv. The houses in the village were made of mud and bricks. Wadas with one or two storeys were seen in cities.\nv. In Maratheshahi, men wore a dhoti, kurta, angarakha and mundase; whereas women wore nineyard sarees and blouses.\nvi. They used a barter system for everyday transactions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86361,1685,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Transport and communication in Maratheshahi','','','i. Transport in Maratheshai was carried out by roads, through ghats and across bridges on rivers.\nii. Things like the cloth, foodgrains, groceries, etc. were duly transported by loading them on the backs of bullocks.\niii. Moreover, small boats were used in rivers.\nFor the purpose of communication, messengers and camel riders carried letters to and fro.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86362,1685,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ghats in Maratheshahi','','','i. A significant characteristic of Maratheshahi was the stone ghats built on a river or at the confluence of rivers.\nii. In a flight of steps there used to be one wide step at regular intervals, which enhanced the beauty of the entire ghat construction.\niii. Strong bastions were also built at regular intervals to prevent damage due to water currents.\niv. The remarkable specimen of a ghat is the one at Toke, Pravara Sangam on the confluence of the Godavari and Pravara.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86363,1685,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Painting during the Peshwa period','','','i. The Peshwas encouraged painting. Some of the important painters of Peshwa period are Ragho, Tanaji, Anuprao, Shivram and Mankoji. Gangaram Tambat, a painter, was famous during the time of Sawai Madhavrao Peshwa.\nii. The Wadas in the Pune, Satara, Menvali, Nashik, Chandwad and Nipani regions during the Peshwa period had paintings on their walls. In Shaniwarwada the paintings on the walls are a noteworthy feature of the Peshwa period.\niii. The famous temples located at Pandeshwar, Morgaon, Pal, Benwadi, Pashan near Pune have paintings on the walls. The themes of the mentioned paintings were Dashavatara, Ganapati, Shankar, Rampanchayatana, mythological stories, Ramayana, Mahabharata, festivals and so on.\niv. Jin Charitra paintings adorned the Jain temple, at Jamod in Vidarbha. Illustrations in the pothis, minatures, portraits, scenes, etc. were also painted.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86364,1685,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Women\'s life in Maratheshahi was difficult.','','','i. In Maratheshahi, the women\'s life was full of hardships.\nii. Their world was limited to their parent\'s home and then their husband\'s home.\niii. Education of the women was completely neglected. Very few like, Veermata Jijabai, Maharani Yesubai, Maharani Tarabai, Umabai Dabhade, Gopikabai, Punyashlok Ahilyabai had achieved education.\niv. Moreover to that, certain customs like child marriage, unequal marriage, widowhood, keshwapan, sati, polygamy made their lives difficult.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86365,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','People distributed ____ leaves on Dasara.\n(A) mango  (B) apta  (C) banana\n','','','B) apta','',0,0,0,NULL),(86366,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ were raised to celebrate Gudhi Padwa.\n(A) Flags  (B) Gudhis  (C) Torans\n','','','B) Gudhis','',0,0,0,NULL),(86367,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In Maratheshahi, ____ script was used in most transactions.\n(A) Devanagari  (B) Persian  (C) Modi\n','','','C) Modi','',0,0,0,NULL),(86368,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ ghats built on a river or at confluence of rivers are characteristic of Maratheshahi.\n(A) wooden  (B) stone  (C) clay\n','','','B) stone','',0,0,0,NULL),(86369,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What did the villages in Maratheshahi import from other places?','','','The villages in Maratheshahi imported salt from other places.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86370,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who carried letters to and fro in Maratheshahi?','','','Messengers and camel riders carried letters to and fro in Maratheshahi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86371,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the people in Maratheshahi perform charitable deeds?','','','The people in Maratheshahi performed charitable deeds to avoid the wrath of Gods and planets.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86372,1685,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which idols were commissioned by the Peshwas for the Parvati temple?','','','The Peshwas commissioned the metal idols of Ganapati and Parvati for the Parvati temple.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86373,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Hindawi Swaraj formed by Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj was a State founded for the welfare of -----\n(a) soldiers  (b) common people  (c) traders\n','','','b) common people','',0,0,0,NULL),(86374,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Patil of a village was responsible for -----\n(a) revenue collection  (b) protection of the village  (c) trade and business\n','','','b) protection of the village','',0,0,0,NULL),(86375,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Kulkarni’s main duty in the village was to handle -----\n(a) farming  (b) revenue  (c) law and order\n','','','b) revenue','',0,0,0,NULL),(86376,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The balutedars were paid ----- for their work.\n(a) in kind  (b) in money  (c) in gold coins\n','','','a) in kind','',0,0,0,NULL),(86377,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','During the Peshwa period, Ganeshotsava was celebrated from Bhadrapad Chaturthi to -----\n(a) Kojagiri Pournima  (b) Anant Chaturdashi  (c) Holi\n','','','b) Anant Chaturdashi','',0,0,0,NULL),(86378,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The two main religions during the Maratha period were -----\n(a) Hindu and Christian  (b) Hindu and Muslim  (c) Buddhist and Jain\n','','','b) Hindu and Muslim','',0,0,0,NULL),(86379,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Shaniwarwada and Vishrambagwada are examples of -----\n(a) forts  (b) wada architecture  (c) temples\n','','','b) wada architecture','',0,0,0,NULL),(86380,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Bhavanidevi temple on Pratapgad was built by -----\n(a) Sambhaji Maharaj  (b) Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj  (c) Shahu Maharaj\n','','','b) Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj','',0,0,0,NULL),(86381,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The painter Gangaram Tambat was famous during the time of -----\n(a) Balaji Vishwanath  (b) Sawai Madhavrao Peshwa  (c) Baji Rao I\n','','','b) Sawai Madhavrao Peshwa','',0,0,0,NULL),(86382,1685,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The two major indoor games popular in Maratha period were -----\n(a) Kho-kho and Hututu  (b) Chess and Ganjifa  (c) Mallakhamb and Dandpatta\n','','','b) Chess and Ganjifa','',0,0,0,NULL),(86383,1685,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Hindawi Swaraj was founded by Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj.','','','i) Shivaji Maharaj wanted to establish a State that would ensure the welfare of common people.\nii) He aimed to remove oppression and injustice.\niii) He wanted to protect the Maharashtra Dharma and preserve the culture and freedom of the people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86384,1685,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The villages during the Maratha period were self-sufficient.','','','i) Most people in villages were engaged in agriculture and local occupations.\nii) They produced their own food grains and goods needed for daily life.\niii) Only a few items like salt had to be imported from outside.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86385,1685,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Ganeshotsava was celebrated with great importance during the Peshwa period.','','','i) The Peshwas were great devotees of Lord Ganesha.\nii) They personally took part in Ganeshotsav celebrations every year.\niii) Hence, the festival gained social and cultural significance across Maharashtra.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86386,1685,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Women’s life during the Maratha period was full of hardships.','','','i) Women were confined to household duties only.\nii) Customs like child marriage, polygamy, and sati restricted their freedom.\niii) Their education was neglected, and only a few women like Jijabai and Tarabai gained prominence.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86387,1685,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maratha architecture reached great heights during the Peshwa period.','','','i) The Peshwas built beautiful wadas, temples, ghats, and water systems.\nii) Skilled architects and artists like Hiroji Indulkar worked during this period.\niii) Temples like Kalaram and forts like Shaniwarwada show their architectural excellence.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86454,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ has guaranteed equal rights to all Indian citizens in order to create conducive atmosphere.\n(A) Indian Constitution  (B) Indian Armed Forces  (C) Indian Society\n','','','A) Indian Constitution','',0,0,0,NULL),(86455,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The practice of untouchability has been abolished in order to establish ____ in the Indian society.\n(A) freedom  (B) equality  (C) education\n','','','B) equality','',0,0,0,NULL),(86456,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ is the greatest civilian honour or award given in the country of India.\n(A) Bharat Ratna  (B) Padmashree  (C) Padmabhushan\n','','','A) Bharat Ratna','',0,0,0,NULL),(86457,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Liberty now also includes the Right to ____\n(A) Expression  (B) Education  (C) Movement\n','','','B) Education','',0,0,0,NULL),(86458,1689,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Following : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Right to Equality</td>\n    <td>a. Expressing one\'s thoughts</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Right against Exploitation</td>\n    <td>b. Prohibition of untouchability</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Right to Liberty</td>\n    <td>c. A ban on employing a child below 14 years of age</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – a)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86459,1689,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Complete the chart :','','','i. Freedom to assemble peacefully\nii. Freedom to form associations or unions\niii Freedom to reside and settle in any part of the territory of India\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(86460,1689,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Correct and rewrite the following sentences : No one gets rights at birth.','','','Every individual gets right from birth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86461,1689,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Correct and rewrite the following sentences : The same law applies differently to each person.','','','The same law applies equally to all.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86462,1689,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Correct and rewrite the following sentences : Government can deprive you of a job by discriminating on the basis of religion, sex, place of birth.','','','Government cannot deprive us of a job by discriminating on the basis of religion, sex, place of birth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86463,1689,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Correct and rewrite the following sentences : Legal protection is denied to everybody.','','','Legal protection is not denied to anybody rather it has been given equally to all.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86464,1689,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Correct and rewrite the following sentences : Constitution has not made a special provision to prevent the exploitation of children.','','','Constitution has made a special provision to prevent the exploitation of children according to which it is prohibited to employ children under 14 years of age in hazardous places.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86465,1689,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When will an individual be able to develop his/her skills and qualities?','','','When the individual gets protection from injustice, exploitation, discrimination and deprivation, only then he/she will be able to develop his/her skills and qualities.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86466,1689,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by \'Demanding one\'s rights\'?','','','\'Demanding one\'s rights\' means insisting on creating an atmosphere conducive to the development of the self as well as the entire society.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86467,1689,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why does the Constitution prevent the State from conferring titles like Raja, Maharaja, Raobahadur?','','','The Constitution prevents the State from conferring titles like Raja, Maharaja, Raobahadur, etc. because they create an artificial hierarchy among people which in turn nurtures inequality and draws wedges in social unity.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86468,1689,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by right to live?','','','Right to live means getting a guarantee to live, availability of conducive environment for living and that nobody can take away another\'s life.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86469,1689,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who are entitled to get education as a Fundamental Right?','','','All children between 6 and 14 years of age are entitled to get education as a Fundamental Right.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86470,1689,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does the Right against Exploitation enable us to do?','','','Right against Exploitation enables us to stand up against any kind of exploitation or fight any kind of oppression.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86471,1689,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What do you understand by \'fundamental rights\'?','','','i. The Indian Constitution aims to create an atmosphere conducive to the development of the self as well as the entire society for which it guarantees equal rights to all citizens. These rights are fundamental rights.\nii. As they are included in the Constitution, they have the status of a law. Hence, it is equally imperative for everybody to abide by these rights.\niii. These rights make sure that all individuals get protection from injustice, exploitation, discrimination and deprivation.\niv. These rights also ensure that all individuals are able to develop their skills and qualities without any discrimination against any citizen on the grounds of religion, caste, race, sex, descent or place of birth or residence, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86472,1689,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Why has the Constitution given equal rights to all Indian citizens?','','','i. Only when all Indian citizens get equal protection from injustice, exploitation, discrimination and deprivation, will they be able to develop their skills and qualities.\nii. For this to happen, creating an atmosphere conducive for the same is important.\niii. To create this conducive atmosphere, the Indian Constitution has guaranteed equal rights to all Indian citizens.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86473,1689,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the Constitution ensure our security to keep us safe?','','','The Constitution has provided a legal security to everybody under the Right to Liberty. For instance: Right to life, which implies getting a guarantee to live in a conducive environment.\nThe Constitution keeps us safe by ensuring the following:\ni. Nobody takes away anyone\'s life.\nii. No one is arrested and detained without a reason.\niii. Nobody is punished twice for the same offence.\niv. Even after accepting that he/she has committed a crime, the person is not immediately punished. The police first collect evidence against the accused and put up the case in the court. If the allegations are legally proved before the court, only then the person is punished.\nv. This judicial process takes time, but it ensures that an innocent person does not get punished. In this way, the Constitution ensures our security to keep us safe.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86474,1689,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Name the awards that are conferred by the Government upon people for their distinguished contribution in different fields.','','','i. The State confers certain awards to honour the distinguished contributions in different fields. Some of these awards and honours are:\na. Bharat Ratna\nb. Padmashree\nc. Padmabhushan\nd. Padmavibhushan\nBharat Ratna is the greatest civilian honour / award of our country.\nii. Special Medals of honour are awarded for remarkable services in the armed forces. Some of these are mentioned as follows:\na. Paramveer Chakra\nb. Ashok Chakra\nc. Shaurya Chakra\niii. However, it should be noted that, such awards and medals do not bestow any special rights or privileges upon the receivers but are only a recognition of their contribution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86475,1689,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Why is it prohibited to employ children under 14 years of age in hazardous places?','','','i Employing children less than 14 years of age in hazardous places is exploitation.\nii. It puts them under life threatening dangers.\niii. These young children are the future of our country and hence, they should have an all-round progress.\niv. Apart from damaging their health, employing children less than 14 years of age in hazardous places like factories and mines, may also deprive them of their fundamental right of education.\nv. For their and the country\'s development, it is necessary that they get educated. For this Constitution has entitled all children between 6 and 14 years of age, to get education as a Fundamental Right, under the Right to Liberty.\nHence, it is rightly prohibited to employ children under 14 years of age in hazardous places like factories and mines.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86476,1689,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right to Equality','','','i. The Right to Equality prohibits the State from discriminating among citizens on the basis of social status, gender, etc. It aims to establish equality in Indian society and applies equally to all.\nii. The State cannot discriminate while extending protections given by different laws.\nE.g.: Protection from arrest without warrant.\niii. Also, for employment purposes such as government employment, there cannot be any discrimination on the grounds of religion, caste, race, sex, descent or place of birth or residence.\niv. Under the Right to Equality, the inhuman practice of untouchability has been abolished by law and is a cognizable offence.\nv. Titles like Raja, Maharaja, Raobahadur, etc. that create an artificial hierarchy among people have also been abolished.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86477,1689,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Freedoms included in Right to Liberty','','','Right to Liberty gives a guarantee of all the freedoms that an individual requires in order to develop and live. These freedoms are as follows:\ni. The freedom of speech and expression\nii. The freedom to assemble peaceably\niii. The freedom to form associations or unions\niv. The freedom to move freely throughout the territory of India\nv. The freedom to reside and settle in any part of the territory of India\nvi. The freedom to practise any lawful profession, or to carry on any occupation, trade or business of one\'s choice\nThe Right to Liberty gives legal protection to all. Further, the Right to Education is also included in the Right to Liberty.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86478,1689,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right against Exploitation','','','i. Generally women, children, the weaker sections of society and powerless people are exploited. The Right against Exploitation equally enables everyone to stand up against any kind of exploitation.\nii. Exploitation includes bonded labour or forcing somebody to work against his/ her wish, treating somebody like a slave, denying them legitimate compensation, making them do excessive or strenuous work, starving them or ill-treating them.\niii. It bans all types of oppression and exploitation.\niv. There is a special provision to specifically prevent the exploitation of children. Accordingly, children under the age of 14 years cannot be employed in hazardous places like factories and mines.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86479,1689,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Constitution has abolished the practice of untouchability.','','','Because untouchability created inequality and discrimination in society. To establish equality and ensure that every citizen is treated with dignity, the Constitution abolished untouchability by law.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86480,1689,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Titles like Raja, Maharaja, and Raobahadur were abolished by the Constitution.','','','Because such titles created artificial divisions and a sense of superiority among people. To maintain equality and discourage social hierarchy, the Constitution abolished all titles.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86481,1689,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Liberty is important for every individual.','','','Because it guarantees personal freedom such as speech, expression, and movement. It helps individuals to live freely, develop their abilities, and participate in democracy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86482,1689,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Children below 14 years of age cannot be employed in factories or mines.','','','Because child labour leads to exploitation and harms children’s health and education. The Right against Exploitation protects children and ensures they get their Right to Education.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86483,1689,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Equality ensures equal protection of the laws.','','','Because every citizen, regardless of caste, religion, sex, or place of birth, must be treated equally by the law. This right removes discrimination and promotes fairness in society.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86484,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The rights included in the Indian Constitution are known as -----\n(a) Human Rights  (b) Fundamental Rights  (c) Civil Rights\n','','','b) Fundamental Rights','',0,0,0,NULL),(86485,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Equality means -----\n(a) Equal law for all  (b) Different laws for rich and poor  (c) Law only for government officials\n','','','a) Equal law for all','',0,0,0,NULL),(86486,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The practice of untouchability is ----- in India.\n(a) Allowed  (b) Encouraged  (c) Abolished\n','','','c) Abolished','',0,0,0,NULL),(86487,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Titles like Raja, Maharaja, Raobahadur were ----- by the Constitution.\n(a) Continued  (b) Abolished  (c) Encouraged\n','','','b) Abolished','',0,0,0,NULL),(86488,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Liberty includes the freedom -----\n(a) To form associations  (b) To sleep  (c) To avoid education\n','','','a) To form associations','',0,0,0,NULL),(86489,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Education is available for children between the ages of ----- years.\n(a) 3 to 10  (b) 6 to 14  (c) 10 to 18\n','','','b) 6 to 14','',0,0,0,NULL),(86490,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right against Exploitation protects citizens from -----\n(a) Freedom  (b) Oppression  (c) Equality\n','','','b) Oppression','',0,0,0,NULL),(86491,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Children below 14 years of age cannot be employed in -----\n(a) Schools  (b) Factories and mines  (c) Parks\n','','','b) Factories and mines','',0,0,0,NULL),(86492,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Equality prevents discrimination based on -----\n(a) Religion, caste, sex, place of birth  (b) Age  (c) Language\n','','','a) Religion, caste, sex, place of birth','',0,0,0,NULL),(86493,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The main aim of Fundamental Rights is -----\n(a) To exploit citizens  (b) To ensure welfare and equality  (c) To punish people\n','','','b) To ensure welfare and equality','',0,0,0,NULL),(86494,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The practice of untouchability in any form is a ____ offence.\n(A) punishable  (B) cognizable  (C) minor\n','','','B) cognizable','',0,0,0,NULL),(86495,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Apart from the freedom of speech and movement, the right to liberty has also provided us ____ to keep us safe.\n(A) independence  (B) equality  (C) security\n','','','C) security','',0,0,0,NULL),(86496,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','It is prohibited to employ children under ____ years of age in hazardous places.\n(A) 10  (B) 14  (C) 18\n','','','B) 14','',0,0,0,NULL),(86497,1689,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Liberty now also includes the Right to ____\n(A) Education  (B) Movement  (C) Expression\n','','','A) Education','',0,0,0,NULL),(86498,1689,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do we have to exercise our rights?','','','We have to exercise our rights responsibly.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86499,1689,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does the Right to Liberty guarantee?','','','Right to Liberty gives a guarantee of all the freedoms necessary from the point of view of the individual.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86500,1689,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is included in exploitation?','','','Exploitation includes bonded labour or forcing somebody to work against his/ her wish, treating somebody like a slave, denying them legitimate compensation, making them do excessive or strenuous work, starving them or ill-treating them.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86501,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','India is an important ____ country in the world.\n(A) democratic  (B) secular  (C) cultural\n','','','B) secular','',0,0,0,NULL),(86502,1690,'mcq','1a',0,2,'Text Book','','____ education cannot be made compulsory in educational institutions that get aid from the State.\n(A) Moral  (B) Religious  (C) Physical\n','','','B) Religious','',0,0,0,NULL),(86503,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Our Constitution has given the different communities the right to preserve their ____ uniqueness.\n(A) linguistic  (B) cultural  (C) traditional\n','','','B) cultural','',0,0,0,NULL),(86504,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','We can approach the ____ in case of infringement of our rights.\n(A) police  (B) government  (C) courts\n','','','C) courts','',0,0,0,NULL),(86505,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : Protection from illegal arrest and detention.','','','Habeas Corpus','',0,0,0,NULL),(86506,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : The order of the Supreme Court or the High Court commanding the government to perform an action that is its duty to perform.','','','Mandamus','',0,0,0,NULL),(86507,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : The order preventing a subordinate court from exceeding its jurisdiction.','','','Prohibition','',0,0,0,NULL),(86508,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the following : To restrain a person from acting in an office to which he is not entitled.','','','Quo Warranto','',0,0,0,NULL),(86509,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The order to remove a law suit from a subordinate court to a superior court.','','','Certiorari','',0,0,0,NULL),(86510,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Correct and rewrite the following sentences : Our Constitution encourages religious taxes.','','','Our Constitution forbids religious taxes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86511,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Correct and rewrite the following sentences : It is a constitutional right of the courts to infringe upon the rights of the people.','','','It is a constitutional duty of the courts to protect the rights of the people.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86512,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State the reason why we are free to practice the following : All Indian citizens can celebrate all festivals joyfully, because...','','','Right to freedom of religion is included in our Constitution.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86513,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','State the reason why we are free to practice the following : We can make efforts towards the preservation and propagation of our language, its script and literature, because...','','','According to our Cultural and Educational Rights, we are free to preserve, propagate and promote our own language, script and literature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86514,1690,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the reason why we are free to practice the following : One can approach the court if one is arrested or detained without any reason because...','','','The courts issue various kinds of writs to protect the rights of the citizens as Constitutional Remedies. Habeas corpus is one such writ in which no person can be detained without any reason.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86515,1690,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What rights do the Indian citizens have as per the Right to Freedom of Religion?','','','As per the Right to Freedom of Religion, each Indian citizen has the right to practise any religion and to establish institutions for religious reasons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86516,1690,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called as an infringement of our rights?','','','When an encroachment upon our rights which are guaranteed by the Constitution, prevents us from exercising our rights, it is called as an infringement of our rights.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86517,1690,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which rights do the Indian citizens have as per the Cultural and Educational Rights?','','','As per the Cultural and Educational Rights, Indian citizens have the following rights:\ni. Different communities have the right to preserve their cultural uniqueness.\nii. People are free to preserve and promote their own language, script and literature.\niii. They can also establish institutions for the development of their language.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86518,1690,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What is meant by Right to Constitutional Remedies?','','','i. The Indian citizens\' right to get judicial remedies, in case of encroachment upon their fundamental rights, is called as the Right to Constitutional Remedies.\nii. When an encroachment upon our rights which are guaranteed by the Constitution, prevents us from exercising our rights, it is called as an infringement of our rights.\niii. Constitution itself has provided for legal remedies in such cases and it is the duty of the courts to protect the rights of the people.\niv. For this, the court considers our complaint, investigates it and gives an appropriate verdict.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86519,1690,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State few examples of infringment of individual rights','','','Few examples of infringment of individual rights are:\ni. Arresting or detaining an individual without reason\nii. Preventing a person from leaving a village or a town without providing a valid reason\niii. Refusing food, water or medicines to jail inmates, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86520,1690,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','The Constitution prevents the imposition of religious taxes.','','','i. Taxes that might be used to encourage a particular religion are called as religious taxes.\nii. India is a secular country and one of our fundamental rights is the right to freedom of religion.\niii. Accordingly, each Indian citizen has the freedom to practise any religion.\niv. In order to broaden the scope of the right to freedom of religion, the Constitution prevents the imposition of religious taxes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86521,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Things like diversity of festivals, food and ways of life in our country are part of the ____ of the different communities.\n(A) religions  (B) traditions  (C) cultures\n','','','C) cultures','',0,0,0,NULL),(86522,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Since the fundamental rights are protected by the ____ citizens are able to exercise their rights.\n(A) government  (B) courts  (C) police\n','','','B) courts','',0,0,0,NULL),(86523,1690,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by Right to Constitutional Remedies?','','','The Indian citizens\' right to get judicial remedies, in case of encroachment upon their fundamental rights, is called as the Right to Constitutional Remedies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86524,1690,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What powers do courts have in order to protect the rights of the citizens?','','','Courts have powers to issue various kinds of writs in order to protect the rights of the citizens.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86525,1690,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What should we always remember while exercising our fundamental rights?','','','While exercising our fundamental rights, we should always remember our Fundamental Duties.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86526,1690,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Constitutional Remedies','','','Courts have powers to issue various kinds of writs in order to protect the rights of the citizens. These writs are as follows:\ni. Habeas Corpus: It ensures our protection from unlawful arrest and detention.\nii. Mandamus: It is the order of the Supreme Court or the High Court commanding the government to perform an action that is its duty to perform in public interest.\niii. Prohibition: It prevents a subordinate court from exceeding its jurisdiction.\niv. Quo Warranto: It is used to restrain a person from acting in an office to which he is not entitled.\nv. Certiorari: The order to remove a law suit from a subordinate court to a superior court.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86528,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Freedom of Religion is a part of which document?\n(a) Indian Penal Code  (b) Indian Constitution  (c) Indian Citizenship Act\n','','','b) Indian Constitution','',0,0,0,NULL),(86529,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','India is known as a _____ country.\n(a) Religious  (b) Secular  (c) Communist\n','','','b) Secular','',0,0,0,NULL),(86530,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','According to the Constitution, citizens are free to _____ any religion.\n(a) Deny  (b) Practise  (c) Abolish\n','','','b) Practise','',0,0,0,NULL),(86531,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The State cannot impose _____ taxes.\n(a) Property  (b) Religious  (c) Income\n','','','b) Religious','',0,0,0,NULL),(86532,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Religious education cannot be made compulsory in institutions that _____.\n(a) Are private  (b) Receive State aid  (c) Are foreign-funded\n','','','b) Receive State aid','',0,0,0,NULL),(86533,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to preserve one’s language and culture comes under _____.\n(a) Right to Equality  (b) Cultural and Educational Rights  (c) Right to Liberty\n','','','b) Cultural and Educational Rights','',0,0,0,NULL),(86534,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Right to Constitutional Remedies enables citizens to _____.\n(a) Ignore the law  (b) Approach the court for protection  (c) Make their own laws\n','','','b) Approach the court for protection','',0,0,0,NULL),(86535,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which writ protects a person from unlawful arrest and detention?\n(a) Habeas Corpus  (b) Certiorari  (c) Mandamus\n','','','a) Habeas Corpus','',0,0,0,NULL),(86536,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The writ ‘Mandamus’ is issued to _____.\n(a) Stop a person from holding an unlawful office  (b) Command the government to perform its duty  (c) Transfer a case to a higher court\n','','','b) Command the government to perform its duty','',0,0,0,NULL),(86537,1690,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If a citizen’s fundamental rights are violated, they should approach _____.\n(a) Police Station  (b) Panchayat  (c) Court of Law\n','','','c) Court of Law','',0,0,0,NULL),(86538,1690,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Pairs :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group ‘A’</th>\n    <th>Group ‘B’</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Habeas Corpus</td>\n    <td>a) To prevent a lower court from exceeding its powers</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Mandamus</td>\n    <td>b) Protection from unlawful arrest</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Prohibition</td>\n    <td>c) To order the removal of a case to a higher court</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Certiorari</td>\n    <td>d) To command the government to perform its duty</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – a), (4 – c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86539,1690,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Pairs :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group ‘A’</th>\n    <th>Group ‘B’</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Right to Freedom of Religion</td>\n    <td>a) Right to preserve one’s own language and culture</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Cultural and Educational Rights</td>\n    <td>b) Legal protection of Fundamental Rights</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Right to Constitutional Remedies</td>\n    <td>c) Freedom to practise any religion</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Secularism</td>\n    <td>d) Equal treatment to all religions by the State</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – b), (4 – d)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86540,1690,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right to Freedom of Religion','','','i) Every citizen of India has the freedom to follow and practise any religion.\nii) Citizens can establish institutions for religious purposes.\niii) The State cannot collect or impose religious taxes.\niv) Religious education cannot be made compulsory in government-aided schools.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86541,1690,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cultural and Educational Rights','','','i) Every community has the right to preserve and promote its own culture.\nii) Citizens can protect their language, script, and literature.\niii) Institutions can be established for the development of their language and culture.\niv) These rights help maintain India’s cultural diversity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86542,1690,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right to Constitutional Remedies','','','i) If any fundamental right is violated, citizens can appeal to the courts.\nii) The Supreme Court and High Courts can protect these rights.\niii) Courts can issue writs to ensure justice and protection.\niv) This right is called the “Heart and Soul” of the Constitution (as said by Dr. B. R. Ambedkar).\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86543,1690,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Types of Writs','','','i) Habeas Corpus – Protects citizens from unlawful arrest.\nii) Mandamus – Orders the government to perform its duty.\niii) Prohibition – Prevents a lower court from exceeding its powers.\niv) Certiorari – Transfers a case from a lower court to a higher court.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86544,1690,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Importance of Fundamental Rights','','','i) They protect the freedom and equality of all citizens.\nii) They help in maintaining justice and democracy in the nation.\niii) They ensure that citizens are treated fairly and without discrimination.\niv) They help people to become responsible, alert, and active citizens.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86545,1690,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The State cannot impose religious taxes.','','','Because India is a secular country and every religion is treated equally. Imposing religious taxes would mean promoting one religion over another, which is against the right to freedom of religion.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86546,1690,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Religious education cannot be made compulsory in government-aided schools.','','','Because the Constitution ensures freedom of religion and maintains secularism. Compulsory religious education would violate this principle by promoting a particular faith.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86547,1690,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Citizens can approach the court if their fundamental rights are violated.','','','Because the Constitution provides the Right to Constitutional Remedies, which allows citizens to seek justice and protection of their rights through the Supreme Court or High Courts.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86548,1690,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Different communities have the right to preserve their language and culture.','','','Because the Constitution grants Cultural and Educational Rights so that every community can protect and promote its unique identity, traditions, and literature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86549,1690,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fundamental rights are essential for democracy.','','','Because they protect the liberty, equality, and dignity of citizens, prevent misuse of power, and ensure that all citizens can live freely and equally under the law.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86550,1690,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the Right to Freedom of Religion.','','','i) Every Indian citizen has the freedom to follow, practise, and spread any religion of their choice.\nii) The State cannot impose any religious tax to promote or support a particular religion.\niii) Religious education cannot be made compulsory in schools or institutions that receive government aid.\niv) This right ensures India remains a secular country, where all religions are treated equally before the law.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86551,1690,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are Cultural and Educational Rights?','','','i) Every community in India has the right to preserve and promote its language, script, and culture.\nii) People can establish educational institutions for the development of their language and culture.\niii) The government must protect the rights of minorities to run their own educational institutions.\niv) These rights help maintain India’s unity in diversity and protect cultural uniqueness.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86552,1690,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the Right to Constitutional Remedies.','','','i) It gives citizens the right to approach the courts if their fundamental rights are violated.\nii) The Supreme Court and High Courts can issue writs like Habeas Corpus, Mandamus, Prohibition, Quo Warranto, and Certiorari for justice.\niii) It is called the heart and soul of the Constitution because it protects all other rights.\niv) This right ensures that no person or government authority can deny citizens their fundamental rights.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86553,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fundamental rights put ____ on the power of the government.\n(A) freedom  (B) restrictions  (C) weight\n','','','B) restrictions','',0,0,0,NULL),(86554,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Each Directive contains a ____ for the State Policy.\n(A) subject  (B) verdict  (C) trial\n','','','A) subject','',0,0,0,NULL),(86555,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Constitution did not make the ____ binding upon the State like the fundamental rights.\n(A) responsibilities  (B) objectives  (C) directives\n','','','C) directives','',0,0,0,NULL),(86556,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a ____ citizens have a dual responsibility.\n(A) monarchy  (B) dictatorship  (C) democracy\n','','','C) democracy','',0,0,0,NULL),(86557,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In order that the Indian citizens become conscious of their responsibilities, ____ were included in the Constitution.\n(A) Fundamental Rights  (B) Fundamental Duties  (C) Directive Principles of State Policy\n','','','B) Fundamental Duties','',0,0,0,NULL),(86558,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say \'Yes\' or \'No\' : The jobs advertised in the newspaper are for men and women both.','','','Yes','',0,0,0,NULL),(86559,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say \'Yes\' or \'No\' : Men and women doing the same work in a factory get different wages.','','','No','',0,0,0,NULL),(86560,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say \'Yes\' or \'No\' : Government implements various schemes for the improvement of public health.','','','Yes','',0,0,0,NULL),(86561,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Say \'Yes\' or \'No\' : The State shall protect all monuments of historic interest and national importance.','','','Yes','',0,0,0,NULL),(86562,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following into Directive Principles of State Policy and Fundamental Duties : To secure a uniform civil code applicable to the entire country.','','','Directive Principles of State Policy','',0,0,0,NULL),(86563,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following into Directive Principles of State Policy and Fundamental Duties : To cherish and follow the noble ideals which inspired our national struggle for freedom','','','Fundamental Duties','',0,0,0,NULL),(86564,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following into Directive Principles of State Policy and Fundamental Duties : To protect and improve the natural environment, and to have compassion for living creatures.','','','Fundamental Duties','',0,0,0,NULL),(86565,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following into Directive Principles of State Policy and Fundamental Duties : To offer public assistance to citizens in cases of unemployment, old age, sickness, etc.','','','Directive Principles of State Policy','',0,0,0,NULL),(86566,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Classify the following into Directive Principles of State Policy and Fundamental Duties : To defend the country, and to render national service when called upon to do so.','','','Fundamental Duties','',0,0,0,NULL),(86567,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence : To give lesser wages to women than to men for the same work.','','','Wrong.\nThere is a directive to ensure \'equal pay for equal work\' according to which both men and women should get equal pay for the same work.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86568,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence : Not to let the National Flag fall down on the ground.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(86569,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence : To stand at attention while the National Anthem is playing.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(86570,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence : To carve or paint our names on the walls of a historic place like a fort.','','','Wrong.\nIt is our Fundamental Duty to value and preserve the rich heritage including historic places like fort. Hence, we should not spoil or destroy such historical places by carving or painting our names on them.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86571,1691,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Right or Wrong? If Wrong, write the correct sentence : To keep public places clean.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(86572,1691,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','List the restrictions on the powers of the Government.','','','The restrictions on the powers of the Government are as follows:\ni) State cannot discriminate between and among citizens on the basis of caste, religion, race, language or sex.\nii) State cannot deny anybody equality before the law and equal protection of the law.\niii) No person shall be deprived of his life.\niv) State cannot impose any religious taxes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86573,1691,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are known as Directive Principles of State Policy?','','','The directive instructions given by the Constitution to the State on how to achieve the goals, expressed in the Preamble, through various State policies are called as Directive Principles of State Policy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86574,1691,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why were the directives not imposed upon the State by the makers of the Indian Constitution?','','','The directives were not imposed upon the State, because the makers of the Indian Constitution knew that implementing all the directives at the same time would require a lot of resources and expected the States to implement them slowly and steadily.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86575,1691,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What Fundamental Duty has the Constitution specified for the parents?','','','The Constitution has specified the Fundamental Duty that every citizen of India who is a parent or guardian, should provide opportunities of education to his or her child or ward between the age of six and fourteen years.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86576,1691,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why were the Directives included in the Constitution?','','','i. After independence, India faced the following challenges:\na. Establishing order\nb. Carrying out smooth administration\nc. Eradication of social issues like poverty, backwardness and illiteracy\nd. The task of building a nation\ne. Achieving welfare of people\nBasically, the nation had to be transformed into a developed and progressive one.\nii. To achieve all this, creating and implementing new policies was imperative.\niii. These policies had to be such that they urgently addressed certain aspects of public welfare. Hence, these aspects needed priority.\niv. The subjects to which the Union and State Governments should give priority in their public welfare policies were specified through the Directive Principles of State policy.\nDue to the above reasons, the Directives were included in the Constitution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86577,1691,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Why is it said that the Directive Principles and Fundamental Rights are two sides of the same coin.','','','i. Fundamental Rights give citizens the most needed liberty. On the other hand, Directive Principles of State Policy create an atmosphere conducive to the growth of democracy.\nii. Although, we cannot go to the courts if the government does not implement a Directive, we can put pressure on the Government to make a policy in accordance with the Directive Principles.\niii. This implies that the Directive Principles and Fundamental Rights are both specified in the Constitution with a common goal of public welfare.\nHence, we can say that the Directive Principles and Fundamental Rights are two sides of the same coin.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86578,1691,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','The Directive Principles of the Constitution enumerated in the textbook.','','','i. The directive instructions specified by the Constitution to the State on how to achieve the goals, expressed in the Preamble, are called as Directive Principles of State Policy.\nii. The Directive Principles of the Constitution enumerated in the textbook are as follows:\na. The State must ensure sufficient means of livelihood for all citizens without any gender discrimination.\nb. The State must ensure equal pay for equal work for both men and women.\nc. The State must ensure that the public health improves.\nd. The State must ensure the protection and improvement of the environment by safeguarding the forests and wild-life of the country.\ne. The State should protect all monuments of historic interest and national importance.\nf. The State should promote, with special care, the educational and economic interests of the weaker sections of the society, especially the Scheduled Castes and Tribes.\ng. The State should offer public assistance to citizens in cases of unemployment, old age, sickness, etc.\nh. The State should secure a uniform civil code applicable to the entire country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86579,1691,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Indian Constitution did not make the directives binding upon the State like the fundamental rights.','','','i. The need to transform India into a developed and a progressive nation, called for drawing up and implementing new policies.\nii. These policies had to urgently address certain aspects of public welfare and hence, were prioritised through the Directive Principles of Sate Policy.\niii. Implementing all of these policies at the same time would have required a lot of resources. Hence, they had to be implemented slowly and steadily.\nDue to the above reasons, the Indian Constitution did not make the Directives binding upon the State like the fundamental rights.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86580,1691,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','The State shall protect all monuments of historic interest.','','','i. All monuments of historic interest like artefacts, buildings, etc. give a lot of information about the rich cultural heritage of the country.\nii. Some of these monuments tell us about the inspiring story of India\'s freedom struggle, the reigns and rulers of the past etc.\nHence, the State shall protect all monuments of historic interest.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86581,1691,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','A pension scheme is implemented for old people.','','','i. According to a provision in the Constitution, it is imperative on the State to offer public assistance to citizens in cases of unemployment, old age, sickness, etc.\nii. It is mentioned as one of the Directive Principles of State Policy.\niii. A pension scheme ensures financial help to the enrolled citizens above 60 years of age.\nHence, a pension scheme is implemented for old people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86582,1691,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In a democracy, citizens have a dual responsibility.','','','i. Democracy gives the citizens various freedoms and rights.\nii. The citizens have to be vigilant about the unfair restrictions on their rights.\niii. At the same time, they are also expected to fulfil some duties and responsibilities.\nHence, we can say that in a democracy, citizens have a dual responsibility.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86583,1691,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fundamental Duties were included in the Constitution.','','','i. The government has made various schemes for the people\'s welfare.\nii. The benefits of these schemes cannot reach all people if they do not fulfil their Fundamental Duties.\niii. For this, it is required that the Indian citizens become conscious of their responsibilities.\niv. In order to bring about this consciousness, Fundamental Duties were included in the Constitution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86584,1691,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Free and compulsory education has been made available to children in the age group 6 to 14.','','','i. As per our Constitution, all children between 6 and 14 years of age are entitled to get education as a Fundamental Right.\nii. However, some of them have to work to help their parents earn a living for the family.\niii. In spite of these adverse conditions, as per the Right to Education Act, receiving education is extremely necessary for these children.\nHence, free and compulsory education has been made available to children in the age group 6 to 14 .\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86585,1691,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Why is the provision of a uniform civil code for citizens included in the Directive Principles of the Indian Constitution?','','','i. Secularism is one of our goals as per our Preamble. Accordingly, all religions are considered as equal. No one religion is considered to be the State religion and the citizens are free to follow their own religion.\nii. Also, to make India a \'Sovereign, Socialist, Secular, Democratic Republic\', the principles of justice, liberty, equality and fraternity have been included in our Preamble.\niii. Implementing a uniform civil code for citizens will help realise the above goals, support the above principles and prevent any discrimination among individuals on the basis of caste, creed, race, language, region, place of birth or sex.\niv. This will help realise the larger goal of public welfare which in turn will help the nation to progress.\nHence, the provision of a uniform civil code for citizens is included in the Directive Principles of the Indian Constitution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86586,1691,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the ways in which citizens can conserve the environment? Write with examples.','','','i. The living and non-living components of the environment like animals and trees, air, water, etc are natural resources. It is our duty to protect the resources of our country.\nii. It is important to maintain a balance between these natural components to maintain a stable environment. Even if we take into account one component, that is air, citizen can do many things to protect it. As trees maintain the balance between the different components of air like oxygen and carbon dioxide, citizens can run campaigns for conserving trees around them and also for planting new trees in their surroundings.\niii. A moderate and a considerate use of natural resources will also help to conserve the environment.\niv. E.g.: a. Instead of using a four-wheeler to go to nearby places, one may opt for a bicycle and prevent air pollution.\nb. Car-pooling (sharing the ride with friends and co-workers) is another option to reduce the consumption of fuel and reduce air-pollution.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86587,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The task of ____ had to be undertaken after India\'s independence.\n(A) economic growth (B) nation building (C) industrialization\n','','','B) nation building','',0,0,0,NULL),(86588,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There is a ____ to ensure \'equal pay for equal work.\'\n(A) law (B) directive (C) policy\n','','','B) directive','',0,0,0,NULL),(86589,1691,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What privision has been made in the Directive Principles of State Policy for the upliftment of the weaker sections of the society?','','','The Directive Principles of State Policy instructs the State to promote, with special care, the educational and economic interests of the weaker sections of the society, especially the Scheduled Castes and Tribes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86590,1691,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are our Fundamental Duties towards our country?','','','Our fundamental duties towards our country are to abide by the Constitution, respect the National Flag and National Anthem, to cherish and follow the noble ideals that inspired our national freedom struggle, to uphold and protect the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India, to defend the country, and to render national service when called upon to do so.','',0,0,0,NULL),(86591,1691,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Directive Principles of State Policy','','','i. The directive instructions to the State on how to achieve the goals expressed in the Preamble, through State policies are called as Directive Principles of State Policy.\nii. Through the Directive Principles of State Policy, the Constitution has specified the subjects to which the Union and State Governments should give priority in their policies for people\'s welfare.\niii. The makers of the Indian Constitution did not make the directives binding upon the State like the fundamental rights and expected that States should implement them slowly and steadily as implementing all these policies at the same time would require a lot of resources.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86592,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Directive Principles of State Policy are included in the Constitution to ______.\n(a) impose restrictions on citizens  (b) guide the State in making policies  (c) limit fundamental rights\n','','','b) guide the State in making policies','',0,0,0,NULL),(86593,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Directive Principles aim to achieve ______.\n(a) people’s welfare and national development  (b) only political goals  (c) only economic growth\n','','','a) people’s welfare and national development','',0,0,0,NULL),(86594,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Directive Principles are ______ upon the government.\n(a) legally binding  (b) not binding but advisory  (c) punishable if ignored\n','','','b) not binding but advisory','',0,0,0,NULL),(86595,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a Directive Principle of State Policy?\n(a) Freedom of Speech  (b) Equal pay for equal work  (c) Right to Education\n','','','b) Equal pay for equal work','',0,0,0,NULL),(86596,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Directive Principles were included to help the State ______.\n(a) enforce laws strictly  (b) achieve the goals mentioned in the Preamble  (c) restrict citizens’ rights\n','','','b) achieve the goals mentioned in the Preamble','',0,0,0,NULL),(86597,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is NOT a Directive Principle of State Policy?\n(a) Protection of environment  (b) Freedom of religion  (c) Improvement of public health\n','','','b) Freedom of religion','',0,0,0,NULL),(86598,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Directive Principles and Fundamental Rights are ______.\n(a) two sides of the same coin  (b) opposite to each other  (c) not related\n','','','a) two sides of the same coin','',0,0,0,NULL),(86599,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which Article of the Constitution mentions Fundamental Duties?\n(a) Article 32  (b) Article 51A  (c) Article 21\n','','','b) Article 51A','',0,0,0,NULL),(86600,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','One of the Fundamental Duties of citizens is to ______.\n(a) Respect the National Flag and Anthem  (b) Ignore public property  (c) Disobey the Constitution\n','','','a) Respect the National Flag and Anthem','',0,0,0,NULL),(86601,1691,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who must provide education to children between 6 and 14 years of age?\n(a) The government only  (b) Parents or guardians  (c) Teachers\n','','','b) Parents or guardians','',0,0,0,NULL),(86602,1691,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the pairs :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group ‘A’</th>\n    <th>Group ‘B’</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Equal pay for equal work</td>\n    <td>(a) Protection of forests and wildlife</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Protection of monuments</td>\n    <td>(b) Equality between men and women</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Improvement of public health</td>\n    <td>(c) Clean environment</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Protect and improve environment</td>\n    <td>(d) Historical and national importance</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 – b), (2 – d), (3 – c), (4 – a)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86603,1691,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the pairs :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Group ‘A’</th>\n    <th>Group ‘B’</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Respect the National Flag</td>\n    <td>(a) Protect public property</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Defend the country</td>\n    <td>(b) Abide by the Constitution</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Safeguard public property</td>\n    <td>(c) Serve when called upon</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Provide education to children</td>\n    <td>(d) Children between 6 to 14 years</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – a), (4 – d)','',0,0,0,NULL),(86604,1691,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are Directive Principles of State Policy? Explain their importance.','','','i) Directive Principles are guidelines given to the State for achieving the goals stated in the Preamble.\nii) They direct the government to frame policies for public welfare and national development.\niii) These principles help to establish economic and social justice in the country.\niv) They aim to create a welfare state where every citizen enjoys equality, dignity, and opportunities for progress.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86605,1691,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why were the Directive Principles of State Policy included in the Constitution?','','','i) After independence, India faced challenges like poverty, illiteracy, and backwardness.\nii) The makers of the Constitution wanted to guide the State to achieve national progress.\niii) These principles help in planning welfare schemes for the weaker sections of society.\niv) They ensure balanced social, economic, and cultural development in the nation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86606,1691,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State any four important Directive Principles of State Policy.','','','i) The State should ensure adequate means of livelihood for all citizens.\nii) The State should provide equal pay for equal work for both men and women.\niii) The State should protect the environment, forests, and wildlife.\niv) The State should promote the welfare of weaker sections like Scheduled Castes and Tribes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86607,1691,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write any four Fundamental Duties of Indian citizens.','','','i) To abide by the Constitution and respect the National Flag and National Anthem.\nii) To defend the country and render national service when called upon.\niii) To protect and improve the natural environment and have compassion for living creatures.\niv) To safeguard public property and avoid violence.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86608,1691,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the relationship between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles of State Policy.','','','i) Both aim to establish a democratic and welfare state.\nii) Fundamental Rights give citizens personal liberty and protection.\niii) Directive Principles guide the State to create social and economic conditions for equality.\niv) Together, they ensure that democracy in India is both political and social in nature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86609,1691,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Directive Principles of State Policy','','','i) These are guidelines given in the Constitution to the government for people’s welfare.\nii) They help in achieving the goals mentioned in the Preamble, like justice and equality.\niii) They are not enforceable by the courts but are fundamental in governance.\niv) They direct the government to create a welfare state and ensure social and economic justice.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86610,1691,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Importance of Directive Principles','','','i) They guide the State in making laws and policies for the welfare of citizens.\nii) They help to remove inequality, poverty, and illiteracy.\niii) They aim to improve health, protect the environment, and ensure equal rights.\niv) They make democracy meaningful by promoting overall national development.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86611,1691,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fundamental Duties of Citizens','','','i) These duties remind citizens of their responsibilities towards the nation.\nii) They include respecting the Constitution, National Flag, and National Anthem.\niii) Citizens must protect the environment, promote harmony, and safeguard public property.\niv) Every parent must provide education to children between 6 and 14 years of age.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(86612,1691,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Relationship between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles','','','i) Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles are two sides of the same coin.\nii) Rights give citizens freedom, while Directives guide the State to provide welfare.\niii) Both together help in achieving justice, liberty, and equality in society.\niv) They ensure that democracy in India is strong, active, and people-oriented.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87304,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','____ organised the Round Table Conference in London.\nA. Mahatma Gandhi  B. Ramsay Mac Donald  C. Lord Irwin  D. Winston Churchill\n','','','B. Ramsay Mac Donald','',0,0,0,NULL),(87305,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan established the organisation named ____.\nA. Indian National Congress  B. Khuda-i-Khidmatgar  C. Swaraj Party  D. Forward Bloc\n','','','B. Khuda-i-Khidmatgar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87306,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','____ led the Dharasana Satyagraha.\nA. Mahatma Gandhi  B. Sarojini Naidu  C. Annie Besant  D. Kasturba Gandhi\n','','','B. Sarojini Naidu','',0,0,0,NULL),(87307,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','In the Second Round Table Conference ____ participated as a representative of the Indian National Congress.\nA. Jawaharlal Nehru  B. Sardar Patel  C. Mahatma Gandhi  D. Subhas Chandra Bose\n','','','C. Mahatma Gandhi','',0,0,0,NULL),(87308,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After the resolution of Complete Independence was passed in Lahore session, Gandhiji decided to launch ____ Movement.\n(a) Home Rule  (b) Non-co-operation  (c) Quit India  (d) Civil Disobedience\n','','','d) Civil Disobedience','',0,0,0,NULL),(87309,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','During Civil Disobedience Movement, government imposed Martial law in ……..\n(a) Peshawar  (b) Solapur  (c) Pune  (d) Mumbai\n','','','b) Solapur','',0,0,0,NULL),(87310,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji withdrew the Civil Disobedience Movement in ____.\n(a) April 1934  (b) March 1930  (c) November 1932  (d) May 1933\n','','','a) April 1934','',0,0,0,NULL),(87311,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji broke the Salt Act at ………..\n(a) Dandi  (b) Peshawar  (c) Dharasana  (d) Yerwada\n','','','a) Dandi','',0,0,0,NULL),(87312,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British government gave orders to ____ regiment in Peshawar to open fire on the satyagrahis.\n(a) Garhwal  (b) Sikh  (c) Maratha  (d) Rajput\n','','','a) Garhwal','',0,0,0,NULL),(87313,1945,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Follower of Gandhiji known as Frontier Gandhi.','','','Khan Abdul Gafar Khan ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87314,1945,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Volunteer who died in firing at Solapur satyagraha.','','','Shankar Shivdare ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87315,1945,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Laid down his life in front of the truck boycotting foreign goods in Mumbai.','','','Babu Genu Said ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87316,1945,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Attended the Second Round Table Conference as representative of Dalits.','','','Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87317,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the objective of Gandhiji in starting the Civil Disobedience?','','','The objective of Gandhiji was to break the oppressive and unjust laws of the British Government through peace and Satyagraha.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87318,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','From which place and when did the Dandi March start?','','','The Dandi March started from Sabarmati Ashram in Ahmedabad on 12 March, 1930.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87319,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the leaders present at the First Round Table Conference.','','','Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar, Sir Tej Bahadur Sapru, Barrister Jinnah were the leaders present at the First Round Table Conference.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87320,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which issues were discussed in the First Round Table Conference?','','','The issues like Responsible Government at centre and establishment of Federal State in India were discussed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87321,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What decision was made in the Poona Pact?','','','According to the Poona Pact, separate electorates for the Dalits was cancelled and a provision for reserved seats was made.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87322,1945,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87323,1945,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87324,1945,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87325,1945,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the timeline of Civil Disobedience Movement :','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87326,1945,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhi-Irwin Pact ','','','1) On the appeal of the Prime Minister, the Viceroy released Gandhiji and other political leaders from prison.\n(2) In this conducive atmosphere, a pact was signed between Gandhiji and Viceroy Irwin. It is known as Gandhi-Irwin Pact.\n(a) According to the pact, the British Government gave an assurance of providing Responsible Government in the proposed constitution of India.\n(b) Congress decided to withdraw the Civil Disobedience Movement.\n(c) Agreed to participate in the Second Round Table Conference.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87327,1945,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Second Round Table Conference ','','','1) The Second Round Table Conference was held in 1931.\n(2) Gandhiji participated as the sole representative of the Indian National Congress.\n(3) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar represented the Dalits. The representatives of the various castes and communities, political parties and the princely states also participated.\n(4) The issues of minority representation and nature of proposed federal constitution were discussed.\n(5) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar demanded separate electorates for the Dalits.\n(6) Gandhiji tried to bring unanimity but he was unsuccessful.\n(7) So, he was disappointed and returned to India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87328,1945,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Poona Pact ','','','1) After the Second Round Table Conference, the British Prime Minister Ramsay Mac Donald declared Communal Awards.\n(2) Communal Awards granted separate electorates to the Dalits as demanded by Dr. Ambedkar.\n(3) Gandhiji was against the division on the basis of caste. He started fast unto death in Yerwada jail.\n(4) The leaders of Indian National Congress requested Dr. Ambedkar to reconsider his demand in the interest of the nation.\n(5) So, Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar signed the Poona Pact with Gandhiji in 1932.\n(6) It provided reserved seats instead of separate electorates for the Dalits.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87329,1945,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Round Table Conference ','','','1) The British Prime Minister, Ramsay Mac donald wanted to discuss constitutional issues related to India.\n(2) For this purpose, he organised Three Round Table Conferences at London between 1930 and 1932.\n(3) Representatives of various communities, castes, political parties and princely states attended the conferences.\n(4) Gandhiji attended the Second Round Table Conference as the representative of Indian National Congress.\n(5) As no important decision was taken in them, all these conferences proved to be futile.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87330,1945,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Why did Gandhiji decide to break the Salt Act to begin the Satyagraha?','','','1) Before launching the Civil Disobedience Movement Gandhiji demanded cancellation of salt tax and end the monopoly of the British government to manufacture it.\n(2) Being an important ingredient in the food of the common people, it was unjust to impose a tax on salt.\n(3) Gandhiji decided to launch the Salt Satyagraha by violation of the Salt Act, which was symbolic in breaking all unjust and oppressive laws of the British.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87331,1945,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Why did the Indian National Congress withdraw the Civil Disobedience Movement?','','','1) After the failure of the first Round Table Conference, Prime Minister Ramsay Mac Donald hoped that Indian National Congress would participate in the Second Round Table Conference.\n(2) In order to create α conducive atmosphere Gandhiji and other political leaders were released from jail.\n(3) A pact was signed after discussion between Gandhiji and Viceroy Irwin.\n(4) According to the pact, the British Government gave an assurance of providing Responsible Government in the proposed constitution of India.\n(5) So, the congress agreed to suspend the Civil Disobedience Movement and participate in the Second Round Table Conference.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87332,1945,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the sacrifice of Babu Genu.','','','1) There was agitation against foreign goods in Mumbai.\n(2) Babu Genu Said, a mill worker, was at the forefront in this Satyagraha.\n(3) He threw himself in front of α truck carrying foreign goods in order to stop it.\n(4) He did not budge even after the police threatened him.\n(5) In the end, he was crushed under the truck.\n(6) His martyrdom was a source of inspiration for the national movement\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87333,1945,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write features of Civil Disobedience Movement.','','','The Civil Disobedience Movement launched in 1930 had the following features :\n(1) All the movements launched till then were limited to urban areas.\n(2) But this movement became nationwide as the people from rural areas also participated.\n(3) Women participated in large numbers in the movement and even led it at many places.\n(4) The movement was based on nonviolence. People faced the suppressive measures of the government unarmed and fearlessly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87334,1945,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What suppressive measures were adopted by the British government after the Second Round Table Conference?','','','1) Gandhiji resumed the Civil Disobedience Movement after he failed and returned from the Second Round Table Conference.\n(2) The government responded by resorting to inhuman oppressive measures.\n(3) Civic rights were strangled.\n(4) The Indian National Congress and associated institutions were declared illegal.\n(4) Restrictions were imposed on national newspapers and literature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87335,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Chandrasing Thakur was court martialled and severely punished.','','','1) Khan Abdul Gaffar Khan began Satyagraha at Peshawar on 23rd  April, 1930. His organisation Khuda-i-Khidmatgar led the satyagraha.\n(2) They kept Peshawar town in their control for a week.\n(3) The British government ordered Garhwal regiment to fire on the satyagrahis.\n(4) Chandrasing Thakur an officer of the Garhwal Regiment refused to fire on the satyagrahis.\nTherefore, he was court martialled and severely punished.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87336,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Government declared Martial Law at Solapur.','','','1) After the Salt Satyagraha many movements which were integral of Civil Disobedience Movement started.\n(2) The mill workers in Solapur observed hartal and organised a huge procession on 6th  May 1930.\n(3) The District Collector ordered firing on procession. Many volunteers including Shankar Shivdare died in the firing.\n(4) People got enraged and attacked railway stations, police stations, courts, municipal buildings, etc.\nTherefore, Martial law was imposed in Solapur by the government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87337,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The deliberation in the First Round Table Conference proved to be meaningless.','','','1) The Civil Disobedience Movement intensified in India in 1930.\n(2) The British Prime Minister, Ramsay Mac Donald organised Round Table Conference at London to discuss constitutional issues related to India.\n(3) The representatives of many political parties and princely states participated in the conference. Indian National Congress did not participate in it.\n(4) Without the participation of the Congress which was a body that represented the country deliberations were meaningless.\nThus, the deliberation in the First Round Table Conference proved to be meaningless.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87338,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Gandhiji began fast unto death in the Yerwada jail.','','','1) The Communal Award declared by the British Prime Minister Ramsay Mac Donald provided separate electorates for the Dalits.\n(2) This division of the society on the basis of caste was not acceptable to Gandhiji.\n(3) Therefore, to protest against the Award, Gandhiji began fast unto death in the Yerawada jail.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87339,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji was arrested on 4th  May 1930.','','','1) Gandhiji started Salt Satyagraha on 6th  April 1930 at Dandi by breaking the Salt act.\n(2) With this the Civil Disobedience Movement started all over India.\n(3) In spite of suppressive measures undertaken by the British Government, they could not suppress the movement. The British government found itself in difficult situation.\nSo, Gandhiji was arrested on 4th  May 1930.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87340,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji returned disappointed from the Second Round Table Conference.','','','1) The British Government raised the issue of representation of the minorities and the nature of the federal constitution.\n(2) There were differences of opinion among the representatives.\n(3) Moreover, the efforts of Gandhiji to arrive at a consensus did not succeed.\n(4) This disappointed Gandhiji and he returned from the Second Round Table Conference.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87341,1945,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','The satyagraha at Dharasana in Gujarat was led by Sarojini Naidu. The police lathi charged on the satyagrahis who came forward to break the Salt Act. The satyagrahis silently tolerated the blows of the lathi. When they were taken away for medical aid, another batch of satyagrahis came forward to replace them. This continued endlessly. In Maharashtra, salt satyagraha took place at Wadala, Malvan and Shiroda.','','1) Who led the Dharasana Satyagraha?\n2) At which places in Maharashtra did Salt Satyagraha take place?\n3) Give a brief account of the Dharasana Satyagraha.\n','','1) Sarojini Naidu led the Dharasana Satyagraha in Gujarat.\n2) The Salt Satyagraha in Maharashtra took place at Wadala, Malvan and Shiroda.\n3) i) The leadership of salt satyagraha was taken over by Sarojini Naidu at Dharasana in Gujarat after the arrest of Gandhiji.\nii) The police lathi-charged the batches of the satyagrahis who came forward to break the Salt Act.\niii) They silently tolerated the blows of the lathis.\niv) The injured were provided medical aid and another batch of satyagrahis would replace them.\nv) This chain of satyagrahis continued endlessly at Dharasana.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87342,1945,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the extensive nature of Civil Disobedience Movement.','','','1) After the resolution of complete independence was passed, Mahatma Gandhi decided to launch the Civil Disobedience Movement.\n(2) The Salt Satyagraha was launched on 6th  April 1930 at Dandi. It was a symbolic act by Gandhiji.\n(3) The main objective was to break all unjust and oppressive laws of the British through peace and Satyagraha.\n(4) Salt Satyagraha took place at Dharasana in Gujarat and at Wadala, Malvan and Shiroda in Maharashtra.\n(5) The mill workers were on the forefront in the Solapur Satyagraha.\n(6) The forest Satyagraha was undertaken at Bilashi, Sangamner, Kalavan, Chirner and Pusad by tribals in Maharashtra.\n(7) In the North West Frontier Province, the Satyagraha was organised under the leadership of Khan Abdul Gafar Khan.\n(8) The satyagrahis in Mumbai obstructed the trucks loaded with foreign goods.\n(9) Women also participated in large numbers.\n(10) This movement reached urban as well as the rural areas, thus making it a national movement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87343,1945,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write information of Dandi March.','','','1) Gandhiji decided to launch Civil Disobedience Movement to cancel unjust tax on salt and to end the monopoly of the British Government to manufacture of salt.\n(2) Salt, an important ingredient in the food of common people, was chosen by Gandhiji.\n(3) On 12th  March 1930 he set out from Sabarmati ashram with his 78 followers.\n(4) Gandhiji delivered speeches in the villages on his way to Dandi.\n(5) Due to Gandhiji\'s speech the message of Civil Disobedience spread әverywhere and a favourable atmosphere vas created.\n(6) He appealed to the people to join the novement fearlessly.\n(7) Covering a distance of 385 km , żandhiji reached Dandi, a seashore, on i^\"th \"  April 1930.\n(8) On 6th  April, he broke the salt act by ricking up the salt lying on the seashore.\n(9) With this, the Civil Disobedience lovement started all over the country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87344,1945,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Do you feel Gandhiji\'s Civil Disobedience technique can be used in present times?','','','1) Gandhiji opposed the unjust\nand oppressive laws of the British and broke them through peace and Satyagraha.\n(2) I strongly feel India needs such ways in present times. Political parties and organisations call for strike or start a movement against decisions of government.\n(3) They resort to unconstitutional acts such as stopping the vehicles, vandalising public property, setting objects on fire and starting riots.\n(4) They destroy property of the nation by involving in all this anti-social activities. Innocent people get killed.\n(5) It brings development to a halt, creates a divide in society.\n(6) The feeling of unity, co-operation and tolerance remains no more. Society becomes unsafe.\n(7) Therefore, I feel that even today the nation requires technique of Civil Disobedience.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87345,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji chose ____ seashore, in Gujarat to begin the Salt Satyagraha.\n(A) Dandi  (B) Sabarmati  (C) Daman  (D) Dwarka\n','','','A) Dandi','',0,0,0,NULL),(87346,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On ____, Gandhiji reached Dandi.\n(A) 3rd April, 1930  (B) 4th April, 1930  (C) 5th April, 1930  (D) 6th April, 1930\n','','','D) 6th April, 1930','',0,0,0,NULL),(87347,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____, a mill worker from Mumbai, gained martyrdom in Mumbai.\n(A) Mallappa Dhanshetti  (B) Qurban Hussain  (C) Babu Genu Said  (D) Shankar Shivdare\n','','','C) Babu Genu Said','',0,0,0,NULL),(87348,1945,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ represented the Dalits in the Round Table Conference.\n(A) Mahatma Gandhi  (B) Sarojini Naidu  (C) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar  (D) Babu Genu Said\n','','','C) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87349,1945,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The person who is also known as \'Frontier Gandhi\'.','','','Khan Abdul Gaffar Khan.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87350,1945,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Name any two persons who were given death sentence by the British Government for leading the Solapur Satyagraha.','','','Mallappa Dhanshetti, Shrikrişhna Sarda, Qurban Hussain and Jagannath Shinde.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87351,1945,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Name the places in Maharashtra where Salt Satyagraha was carried out.','','','Wadala, Malvan and Shiroda.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87352,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When did Mahatma Gandhi decide to start the Civil Disobedience Movement?','','','Mahatma Gandhi decided to start the Civil Disobedience Movement after the resolution of Complete Independence was passed in the Lahore session of the Indian National Congress.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87353,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did British Prime Minister Ramsay Mac Donald organise the Round Table Conference while the Civil Disobedience Movement was going on?','','','While the Civil Disobedience Movement was going on British Prime Minister Ramsay Mac Donald organised the Round Table Conference to once again reflect upon the constitutional issues regarding India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87354,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which topics were discussed in the First Round Table Conference of 1930 ?','','','In the First Round Table Conference of 1930, various topics like Responsible Government at the central level, establishment of a federal state in India, etc., were discussed.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87355,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the Third Round Table Conference (1932) prove to be futile?','','','The Third Round Table Conference, organised at London, proved to be futile because it was boycotted by the Indian National Congress.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87356,1945,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the features of the Civil Disobedience Movement.','','','The features of the Civil Disobedience Movement were as follows-\ni. Civil Disobedience Movement attracted people from all regions of the country. Not only did the people from towns and cities participate in it but also the people from villages took part in it.\nFor e.g. The tribal people, in large numbers participated in the Civil Disobedience Movement.\nii. There was active participation of women in this movement. Women like Kasturba Gandhi, Kamladevi Chattopadhyay, Avantikabai Gokhale, Lilavati Munshi and Hansaben Mehta led the satyagraha.\niii. Mahatma Gandhiji\'s principle of non-violence was completely followed in this movement. Though the British Government greatly suppressed it, the Indians fearlessly followed non-violence and protested unarmed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87357,1945,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dandi Marčh','','','i. Dandi, a seashore, in Gujarat was chosen by Gandhiji to begin the Salt Satyagraha.\nii. He set out from Sabarmati Ashram on foot with 78 followers on 12th  March, 1930 for launching the Salt Satyagraha.\niii. On the way, Gandhiji gave speeches to the villagers in which he appealed to the people to bravely participate in the Civil Disobedience Movement.\niv. Due to his speeches, people willingly joined the Movement and it spread everywhere.\nv. A distance of approximately 385 kms was covered. On 5th  April, 1930, Gandhiji along with his followers reached Dandi.\nvi. On the following day, i.e., on 6th  April, 1930, he broke the Salt Act by picking up the salt from the seashore of Dandi. This led to the beginning of the Civil Disobedience Movement all over the country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87358,1945,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dharasana Satyagraha','','','i. Sarojini Naidu led the Satyagraha at Dharasana in Gujarat.\nii. When the satyagrahis came forward to break the Salt Act, the police resorted to lathi charge.\niii. The blows of the lathis were silently tolerated by the satyagrahis and when they were taken away for medical aid, another batch of satyagrahis replaced them. This continued endlessly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87359,1945,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Jungle Satyagraha','','','i. Gandhiji launched the Civil Disobedience Movement in 1930 by breaking the Salt Act.\nii. However, where there were no salt-pans, the forest laws were broken.\niii. The tribal people, in large numbers participated in this Satyagraha.\niv. In Maharashtra, the jungle satyagrahas were carried out at Bilashi, Sangamner, Kalwan, Chirner and Pusad.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87360,1945,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Poona Pact','','','i. After the Second Round Table Conference, Ramsay Mac Donald, the British Prime Minister, declared the \'Communal Award\' which granted separate electorates for the Dalits.\nii. Gandhiji protested the granting of separate electorate on the basis of caste and went on fast unto death at the Yerwada jail in Poona.\niii. As Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar represented the Dalits, he was requested by the leaders of the Indian National Congress to rethink about the demand for separate electorates.\niv. Dr. Ambedkar accepted the request for the wellbeing of the nation.\nv. Hence, a pact was signed between Mahatma Gandhi and Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar in 1932 which came to be known as the \'Poona Pact\'.\nvi. By this pact, a provision for reserved seats was made instead of separate electorates for the Dalits.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87361,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji was arrested on 4th  May, 1930.','','','i. Gandhiji launched the Civil Disobedience Movement in 1930 by breaking the Salt Act. People all over India responded to it positively.\nii. Khan Abdul Gaffar Khan, the loyal follower of Gandhiji, started satyagraha at Peshawar. For nearly a week, the whole town was in the hands of the Satyagrahis.\niii. Chandrasingh Thakur, an officer of Garhwal Regiment disobeyed the British Government orders of opening fire on the Satyagrahis.\niv. Due to the satyagraha launched by Mahatma Gandhi, the British Government found itself in difficult situation.\nTherefore, the British Government arrested Gandhiji on 4th  May, 1930.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87362,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The sacrifice of Babu Genu Said gave inspiration to the Indian national movement.','','','i. In Mumbai, the foreign goods were being boycotted. So, the vehicles which transported foreign goods were obstructed.\nii. Babu Genu Said who worked in the mills in Mumbai led this movement.\niii. Accordingly, when a truck carrying foreign goods which was safeguarded by the police, came in front of Babu Genu; he lied down on the road to obstruct the vehicle.\niv. Though the police threatened him, he did not get up and he was crushed under the truck. He became a martyr.\nThus, the sacrifice of Babu Genu Said gave inspiration to the Indian national movement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87363,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji became disappointed in the Second Round Table Conference (1931).','','','i. The Second Round Table Conference was organised in 1931 and Mahatma Gandhi attended it as a representative of the Indian National Congress.\nii. Several other representatives of various communities, castes, political parties as well as princely states were also present.\niii. The Government took up the issue of minorities in the Conference. The representatives differed on the issue of minorities and also on the nature of the future Federal constitution.\niv. Gandhiji tried to bring harmony in the opinion but failed to do so.\nTherefore, Gandhiji became disappointed in the Second Round Conference (1931) and returned back to India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87364,1945,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji withdrew the Civil Disobedience Movement in April 1934.','','','i. Gandhiji returned India disappointed from the Second Round Table Conference held at London in 1931.\nii. He then decided to relaunch the Civil Disobedience Movement in 1932. The British Government immediately arrested Gandhiji which led to discontent among the Indians.\niii. The Government used inhuman oppressive methods in order to suppress the movement.\niv. The civil rights were curtailed everywhere. Restrictions were imposed on newspapers and literature. Moreover, the Government declared the Indian National Congress and its associated institutions as illegal.\nTherefore, Gandhiji withdrew the Civil Disobedience Movement in April 1934 and the second phase of the movement also ended.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87365,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many Round Table Conferences were organised at London?','','','Three Round Table Conferences were organised at London; in 1930, 1931 and 1932 respectively.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87366,1945,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is \'Communal Award?\'','','','After the Second Round Table Conference, the \'Communal Award\' was declared by the British Prime Minister Ramsay Mac Donald which granted separate electorates to the Dalits.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87372,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','____ was the first satyagrahi of individual satyagraha.\n(A) Mahatma Gandhi  (B) Jawaharlal Nehru  (C) Vinoba Bhave  (D) Sardar Patel\n','','','C) Vinoba Bhave','',0,0,0,NULL),(87373,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The nationwide movement of 1942 is also called ____.\n(A) Non-Cooperation  (B) Dandi March  (C) Quit India  (D) August Kranti\n','','','D) August Kranti','',0,0,0,NULL),(87374,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','In November 1943, Japan conquered ____ islands and handed them over to Azad Hind Government.\n(A) Lakshadweep  (B) Andaman and Nicobar  (C) Maldives  (D) Sri Lanka\n','','','B) Andaman and Nicobar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87375,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Provincial Ministers of ____ resigned in November, 1939.\n(A) Indian National Congress  (B) Muslim League  (C) Hindu Mahasabha  (D) Azad Hind Fauj\n','','','A) Indian National Congress','',0,0,0,NULL),(87376,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Songs of ____ gave inspiration during the 1942 movement.\n(A) Subhash Chandra Bose  (B) Vinoba Bhave  (C) Sane Guruji  (D) Sarojini Naidu\n','','','C) Sane Guruji','',0,0,0,NULL),(87377,1946,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the wrong pair :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Organisation</th>\n    <th>Founder</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Azad Dasta</td>\n    <td>Bhai Kotwal</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Lal Sena</td>\n    <td>General Awari</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Azad Radio</td>\n    <td>Aruna Asaf Ali</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Azad Hind Government</td>\n    <td>Subhash Chandra Bose</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','Wrong pair : Azad Radio - Aruna Asaf Ali\nCorrected pair : Azad Radio - Usha Mehta.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87378,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In 1937 elections, the Indian National Congress gained majority in ____ provinces and came to power.\n(a) seven  (b) two  (c) eight  (d) three\n','','','c) eight','',0,0,0,NULL),(87379,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Parallel government was formed by ____ in Satara.\n(a) Krantisinh Nana Patil  (b) N.G. Gore  (c) Rashtrasant Tukdoji Maharaj  (d) S.M. Joshi\n','','','a) Krantisinh Nana Patil','',0,0,0,NULL),(87380,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Netaji founded the ____ in 1943 in Singapore.\n(a) Free India Centre  (b) Azad Hind Sena  (c) Parallel Government  (d) Azad Hind Government\n','','','d) Azad Hind Government','',0,0,0,NULL),(87381,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ tried to destroy the railway along with his campaigners in Sindh.\n(a) Khudiram Bose  (b) Ashfaqulla Khan  (c) Hemu Kalani  (d) Maganlal Bagdi\n','','','c) Hemu Kalani','',0,0,0,NULL),(87382,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ had formed the Azad Hind Sena with the help of Captain Mohan Singh.\n(a) Rash Behari Bose  (b) Subhash Chandra Bose  (c) Anand Mohan Bose  (d) S.M. Gore\n','','','a) Rash Behari Bose','',0,0,0,NULL),(87383,1946,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Viceroy who announced India\'s involvement in Second World War.','','','Lord Linlithgo ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87384,1946,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : President of Mumbai session of Indian National Congress.','','','Maulana Abul Kalam Azad ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87385,1946,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Leader who put forth Quit India Movement resolution at the Mumbai session of the Congress.','','','Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87386,1946,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The outburst of Navy and Air Force on this British warship.','','','Talwar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87387,1946,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : His songs inspired the National Movement.','','','Rashtrasant Tukdoji Maharaj.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87388,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the resolution passed by the Congress Working Committee in the Wardha session.','','','The Congress Working Committee at its Wardha session passed the resolution that\n(i) the British rule in India should be ended immediately and\n(ii) India be given independence.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87389,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the warning given by the Congress Working Committee in the Wardha session?','','','The Congress Working Committee had given the warning that if the demand for complete independence was not met, it would launch a non-violent movement for the freedom of India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87390,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the British Government imprison Subhash Chandra Bose?','','','The British Government imprisoned Subhash Chandra Bose because through his speeches he made appeal to the people of India to revolt against the British rule.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87391,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What did the British Government realise after the Navy and Air Force revolted?','','','After the revolt of Navy and Air Force, the British Government realised that their rule in India was about to end','',0,0,0,NULL),(87392,1946,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87393,1946,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87394,1946,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87395,1946,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87396,1946,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Complete the table :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Organisation</th>\n    <th>Founder</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Forward Bloc</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Indian Independence League</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Toofan Sena</td>\n    <td>-----------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Organisation</th>\n    <th>Founder</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Forward Bloc</td>\n    <td>Subhash Chandra Bose</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Indian Independence League</td>\n    <td>Rash Behari Bose</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Toofan Sena</td>\n    <td>Krantiagrani G.D. alias Bapu Lad</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(87397,1946,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Prepare a Timeline of the events of the struggle of Azad Hind Senct :','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87398,1946,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Provincial Cabinets ','','','1) According to the Act of 1935, working of the British administered provinces were transferred to the elected representatives.\n(2) Although not satisfied with the act, Congress contested elections to the Provincial Legislatures of eleven provinces in 1937.\n(3) Indian National Congress gained majority in eight provinces.\n(4) Congress ministries did α lot of useful work for the benefit of the people.\n(5) As the British government refused their demand of Independence the Congress ministries resigned in November 1939.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87399,1946,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Individual Satyagraha ','','','1) As the British government was constantly neglecting the demands the Indian National Congress decided to start anti-war propaganda.\n(2) Instead of a collective movement they decided every person should individually disobey the law. This is known as Individual Satyagraha.\n(3) Acharya Vinoba Bhave was the first Individual Satyagrahi.\n(4) After him nearly 25,000 satyagrahis participated in individual satyagraha and were imprisoned.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87400,1946,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rash Behari Bose ','','','1) Rash Behari Bose was living in Japan since 1915.\n(2) He had established the \'Indian Independence League\' by organising the Indian patriots living in the South East Asia.\n(3) Japan conquered the territories of South-East Asia controlled by the British.\n(4) Rash Behari Bose, with the help of Captain Mohan Singh, formed the Indian\nNational Army or Azad Hind Sena from the Indian prisoners of war captured by Japan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87401,1946,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Revolt of Indian Navy and Air Force ','','','1) Inspired by the heroics of the Indian National Army, the soldiers on the British warship \'Talwar\' revolted on 8th  February 1946.\n(2) They hoisted the tricolour flag on the ship and raised slogans against the British Government.\n(3) The exchange of fire between the rebels and the British army took place.\n(4) The workers and comman people extended their support.\n(5) To support this uprising, the Air Force from Delhi, Lahore, Karachi, Ambala, Meerut went on strike.\n(6) The naval soldiers laid down their arms after the mediation by Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87402,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Why did the British Prime Minister send Sir Stafford Cripps to India?','','','1) During the Second World War, England sided with America against Japan.\n(2) As England was ruling India, there was possibility of Japan attacking India.\n(3) The Japanese forces came closer to the eastern border of India.\n(4) It was necessary to have co-operation of India to resist Japanese invasion.\nTherefore, the British Prime Minister, Winston Churchill sent Sir Stafford Cripps to India in March 1942.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87403,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','What kind of reaction was seen during the spread of news of arrest of main leaders of the Indian National Congress?','','','1) Quit India Resolution moved by Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru, was passed on 8th  August with an overwhelming majority.\n(2) On the same night, the leaders of the Indian National Congress were arrested.\n(3) The news of these arrest aroused strong resentment.\n(4) The angry people took out processions, tried to take control of government offices and attacked jails, police stations and railway stations.\n(5) School children took out processions and shouted slogan \"Vande Mataram\".\n(6) Even though the police resorted to lathi charge and firing, the people were not afraid. The young and old showed determination and courage.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87404,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','What appeal did Mahatma Gandhi make in the Quit India session of Indian National Congress?','','','Gandhiji made the following appeal to the people of India during the Quit India session of Indian National Congress at Mumbai :\n(1) Everyone of you, should from this moment onwards, consider yourself a free man or woman.\n(2) Everyone should behave like α citizen of free India.\n(3) Either we shall free India or die in the attempt.\n(4) Through the appeal he stated that Indians should be ready to make a sacrifice with the feeling of \'Do or Die\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87405,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What work did the Azad Radio Centre do during the 1942 underground movement?','','','During the 1942 underground movement Vitthal Zaveri, Usha Mehta and her companions established a secret transmission centre, \'Azad Radio\'.\nThe work done :\n(1) News of agitations in the country were broadcast on it.\n(2) Patriotic songs were sung.\n(3) Patriotic speeches were telecast.\nThis gave inspiration to the agitators during the Quit India Movement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87406,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the significance of the Quit India Movement.','','','1) On 8th  August 1942, in the Mumbai session, the Indian National Congress gave the order of \'Quit India\' to the British government. This was the beginning of a nationwide agitation.\n(2) To fulfil the objective of independence, lakhs of Indians sacrificed their lives in this movement.\n(3) The number of people who participated were so large that even all prisons in the country were not enough to contain them.\n(4) The movement shook the foundation of British rule in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87407,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the impact of the events from 1942 to 1946 on the British rule.','','','Many important events took place between 1942 to 1946. The following effects were seen on the British rule :\n(1) It shook the foundation of British rule in India.\n(2) The Quit India agitation manifested the strong anti-British feeling of the Indian people.\n(3) The Army, Navy and Air Force considered to be backbone of the British power revolted against it.\n(4) The British rulers realised that now their rule will not last long in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87408,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','In November 1939, provincial ministers of Indian National Congress gave their resignations.','','','1) Viceroy Lord Linlithgo declared in 1939 that India had joined the Second World War on the side of England, without consulting the Indian leaders.\n(2) England claimed that it was fighting to save democracy in Europe.\n(3) Thereupon, the Congress claimed that if that claim is true then England should grant Independence to India immediately.\n(4) So the provincial ministries of the Indian National Congress resigned in November 1939 as England did not accept this demand.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87409,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Indian National Army had to lay down their arms.','','','1) The Indian National Army fought against the British in adverse circumstances under the leadership of Subhash Chandra Bose.\n(2) The Imphal Campaign of Indian National Army remained incomplete as the Japanese stopped helping the Indian National Army.\n(3) In the same period, Japan accepted defeat in the Second World War.\n(4) On 18 August 1945, Subhash Chandra Bose died in a plane crash.\nUnder these circumstances, the soldiers of the Indian National Army had to lay down their arms.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87410,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Parallel Government became a source of inspiration to the people.','','','1) During the Quit India Movement, parallel governments were established in many parts of India.\n(2) It rendered great service by doing work like :\n(i) Opposition to money lenders\n(ii) Prohibition of liquor\n(iii) Spread of literacy\n(3) Opposition to caste distinctions, etc.\nDue to this, the Parallel Government became a source of inspiration for the public.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87411,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The plan of federation mentioned in 1935 Act did not materialise.','','','1) The Act of 1935 provided for the formation of a federation of the British administered provinces and the Indian princely states.\n(2) The working of the British administered provinces were transferred to the Indian representatives.\n(3) On joining the federation, the princely states would lose their autonomy.\n(4) So, the proposal of federation did not materialise as the rulers of the princely states refused to join the federation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87412,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Cripps proposal was rejected.','','','(1) The Indian National Congress rejected the Cripps\' proposal because there was no clear mention of the grant of complete independence to India.\n(2) The Muslim League rejected the Cripps\' proposal as it had no mention of creation of Pakistan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87413,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subhash Chandra Bose resigned from the office of Congress President.','','','1) Subhash Chandra Bose became the President of Indian National Congress twice.\n(2) He became the President of Congress when Second World War had commenced. He was of the opinion that as England was engaged in war, the Congress should intensify the agitation by taking advantage of this situation.\n(3) If needed, India should seek the help of the enemies of England.\n(4) Other senior leaders of the Congress did not agree with this view.\nAs a result, Subhash Chandra Bose resigned from the office of the Congress President.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87414,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British government suspended the punishment of INA officers.','','','1) After surrender of the soldiers of the Indian National Army, the British government charged them with treason.\n(2) Expert lawyers like Pandit Nehru, Bhulabhai Desai and Tej Bahadur Sapre defended the INA officers.\n(3) But the military tribunal held them guilty and sentenced them to life imprisonment.\n(4) There was severe unrest in the minds of the Indian people against the British Government. So, they had to suspend the punishment given by the military tribunal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87415,1946,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the Session of Indian National Congress held in Mumbai in August 1942.','','','1) On 14th  July, 1942 the Congress Working Committee passed the Quit India Resolution at Wardha.\n(2) It warned the British government of launch of a non-violent struggle for India\'s independence.\n(3) With this background, Quit India Session of the Indian National Congress began at Gowalia Tank (Kranti Maidan) in Mumbai on 7th  August, 1942.\n(4) Maulana Abul Kalam Azad presided over this session.\n(5) The resolution passed at Wardha session was finally approved in the Mumbai session.\n(6) On 8th  August, Quit India Resolution, moved by Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru was passed with an overwhelming majority.\n(7) Congress decided to launch a nationwide non-violent movement under the leadership of Gandhiji.\n(8) Gandhiji gave inspiring message of \'Do or Die\' to the people and appealed them to be ready to sacrifice for the nation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87416,1946,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give detailed information about Underground Movement.','','','1) At the end of 1942, the young socialist leaders started the Underground Movement.\n(2) S. M. Joshi, Dr. Ram Manohar Lohia, Jayprakash Narayan were among the many leaders who led the movement.\n(3) Aruna Asaf Ali, Usha Mehta, Sucheta Kriplani were the women leaders who led the movement.\n(4) The activists disrupted transport and communication by breakdown of railway routes, cutting telephone lines, blowing up bridges, etc.\n(5) Hemu Kalani tried to destroy railway carrying British troops and supply, in Sindh province.\n(6) Groups like Azad Dasta of Bhai Kotwal in Karjat and Lal Sena of General Awari in Nagpur made the government helpless and witless for months.\n(7) Usha Mehta and Vitthal Zaveri started a secret transmission centre of Azad Radio in Mumbai.\n(8) Many such centres operated at Kolkata, Delhi and Pune.\n(9) Krantisinh Nana Patil formed parallel government in Satara.\n(10) Parallel governments were formed in Midnapore in Bengal, Ballia and Azamgrah in Uttar Pradesh and Purnia in Bihar.\n(11) Krantiagrani GD (Bapu Lad) established \'Toofan sena\' at Kundal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87417,1946,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the given picture and write information about the work done by him.','','','The given picture is of Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose.\n(1) Subhash Chandra Bose became the President of Indian National Congress twice.\n(2) He was of the view to intensify the agitation and seek help from the enemies of England during the Second World War.\n(3) He had difference of opinion with other senior leaders on these points.\n(4) Therefore, he resigned from the office of the Congress President and formed a party, \'Forward Bloc\'.\n(5) Subhash Chandra Bose was imprisoned by the British because he made an appeal to the people of India to revolt against the British.\n(6) He was interned in his house by the British but he managed to escape in disguise and reached Germany in April 1941.\n(7) He founded the \'Free India Centre\' in Germany.\n(8) He made an appeal to the people of India to join in the armed struggle for the independence of India from Berlin radio station.\n(9) On the invitation of Rash Behari Bose, he went to Japan and led the Indian National Army.\n(10) He formed the Azad Hind Government in Singapore in 1943.\n(11) Under his leadership, Azad Hind Army captured the Aarakan province in Myanmar and posts on the eastern border of Assam.\n(12) Netaji appealed to the people of India : \'You give me blood and I will give you independence\'.\n(13) On 18 August, 1945 Netaji died in a plane crash.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87418,1946,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Text Book','','How do you find the contribution of Shirishkumar to be inspirational?','','','(1) Under the leadership of Shirishkumar, the school children took out a procession holding the tricolour flag at Nandurbar.\n(2) \'Vande Mataram\' was the slogan given to them.\n(3) The police fired on them. In the firing Shirishkumar and many other children became martyrs.\n(4) His sacrifice gives inspiration to us to do work for the society and country.\n(5) Gaining independence is no more our objective but to keep the environment clean, to maintain unity among people, and collectively solve the problems is our responsibility.\n(6) As all this is equally challenging we get inspiration from courage and determination of Shirishkumar.\n(7) Shirishkumar inspires us to do our work fearlessly without being α victim of injustice and oppression.\n(8) He also inspires us to make sacrifice, have faith in work and determination.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87419,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','During the Second World War, England supported America against ____.\n(A) India  (B) Japan  (C) Canada  (D) Mexico\n','','','B) Japan','',0,0,0,NULL),(87420,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On 7ᵗʰ August 1942, the session of the Indian National Congress was held at Gowalia Tank in ____.\n(A) Mumbai  (B) Surat  (C) Lucknow  (D) Faizpur\n','','','A) Mumbai','',0,0,0,NULL),(87421,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ was the leader of Azad Hind Sena.\n(A) Rash Behari Bose  (B) Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose  (C) Captain Laxmi Swaminathan  (D) Jagannath Bhosale\n','','','B) Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose','',0,0,0,NULL),(87422,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In October 1943, Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose established Azad Hind Government in ____.\n(A) Singapore  (B) Berlin  (C) Japan  (D) London\n','','','A) Singapore','',0,0,0,NULL),(87423,1946,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Following :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Column \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Column \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Azad Dasta</td>\n    <td>a. General Awari</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Lal Sena</td>\n    <td>b. Usha Mehta</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Azad Radio</td>\n    <td>c. Bhai Kotwal</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i – c), (ii – a), (iii – b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(87424,1946,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare / Complete the timeline.','','','i. By the Act of 1935, elections to the Provincial Legislature were held in eleven provinces in India:\nii. Provincial Ministers of Indian National Congress resigned.\niii. Sir Stafford Cripps put up a proposal of Dominion Status in front of the Indians.\niv. The Congress Working Committee passed a resolution at Wardha which demanded the end of British rule in India.\nv. The Indian National Congress session was held at Gowalia Tank (Kranti Maidan) in Mumbai.\nvi. \'Quit India\' Resolution which was put forth by Jawaharlal Nehru got passed with great majority.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87425,1946,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The President of the Indian National Congress session held at Mumbai on 7th August, 1942.','','','Maulana Abul Kalam Azad','',0,0,0,NULL),(87426,1946,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The Commander of the Rani of Jhansi Regiment.','','','Captain Laxmi Swaminathan','',0,0,0,NULL),(87427,1946,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Name the person who gave the slogan, \"You give me blood and I will give you independence\".','','','Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose','',0,0,0,NULL),(87428,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After the failure of the Cripps Mission, what did the Indian National Congress decide?','','','After the failure of the Cripps Mission, the Indian National Congress decided to start an intense movement for gaining independence.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87429,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which resolution had to be finally approved in the Mumbai session of the Indian National Congress, held on 7th  August, 1942?','','','The resolution that British should leave India immediately which was passed by the Congress Working Committee at Wardha had to be finally approved in the Mumbai session of the Indian National Congress, held on 7th  August, 1942.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87430,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which resolution was passed on 8th  August, 1942?','','','The resolution of \'Quit India\' which was put forward by Jawaharlal Nehru was passed with great majority on 8th  August, 1942 and a nationwide non-violent agitation had to be started under the leadership of Mahatma Gandhi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87431,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','At the end of 1942, the leadership of People\'s Movement went into whose hands?','','','At the end of 1942, the leadership of People\'s Movement went into the hands of young socialist activists.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87432,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name any two young socialist activists of the Underground movement of 1942.','','','Two young socialist activists of the Underground movement of 1942 were Jayprakash Narayan, Dr. Ram Manohar Lohia, Aruna Asif Ali, Sucheta Kriplani, S.M.Joshi, Vasantdada Patil, Usha Mehta, Maganlal Bagdi, Yashwantrao Chavan, Shirubhau Limaye, N.G.Gore, Yusuf Meher Ali, Achyutrao Patwardhan and Chhotubhai Puranik.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87433,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which activities did the young socialist activists undertake?','','','The young socialist activists disrupted transport, communication and government machinery by breaking down railway routes, cutting down telephone lines, blowing up bridges, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87434,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was Hemu Kalani hanged to death?','','','Hemu Kalani was hanged to death because he along with his companions tried to destroy the railway in the Sindh province when he received the news that a railway carrying British troops and supplies was passing through the province.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87435,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','From where did Bhai Kotwal\'s group named \'Azad Dasta\' made the British Government helpless and witless for months?','','','Bhai Kotwal\'s group named \'Azad Dasta\' operated from the Karjat taluka of modern day Raigad district which made the British Government helpless and witless for months.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87436,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','From where did General Awari\'s group named \'Lal Sena\' made the British Government helpless and witless for months?','','','General Awari\'s group named \'Lal Sena\' operated from Nagpur which made the British Government helpless and witless for months.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87437,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the Indian Independence League formed in Japan?','','','Rash Behari Bose, during his stay in Japan since 1915, organised the Indian patriots living in countries of South East Asia and established an organisation named the Indian Independence League in Japân.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87438,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','Who were the chief associates of Subhash Chandra Bose who helped him in Singapore?','','','Shah Nawaz Khan, Jagannath Bhosale, Dr. Laxmi Swaminathan, Gurubaksh Singh Dhillon, Prem Kumar Sehgal, etc. were the chief associates of Subhash Chandra Bose who helped him in Singapore.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87439,1946,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the backbone of British power?','','','Army, Navy and Air force were the backbone of British power.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87440,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was a mixed cabinet formed after the elections of 1937 ?','','','i. Though the Act of 1935 did not satisfy the Indian National Congress, it still decided to take part in the elections of Provincial Legislature which was provided by the Act.\nii. In 1937, elections were held in the eleven provinces of India. From among these provinces, in over eight provinces, the Indian National Congress gained majority and came to power.\niii. In the remaining three provinces, none of the parties could gain a clear majority.\nTherefore, a mixed cabinet was formed after the elections of 1937.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87441,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What useful work was done for the people by the Cabinet ministers of Indian National Congress after the 1937 elections?','','','The useful work done for the people by the Cabinet ministers of Indian National Congress after the 1937 elections were as follows:\ni. They worked towards releasing the political prisoners from jail.\nii. They introduced Basic Education.\niii. They prohibited liquor.\niv. They undertook measures to improve the condition of the Dalits.\nv. They passed an Act which gave relief to the farmers from debt, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87442,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was the Cripps Mission rejected by the political parties in India?','','','i. In 1942, the British Prime Minister Winston Churchill had sent Sir Stafford Cripps to India.\nii. In March 1942, Sir Stafford Cripps gave the proposal of Dominion status to the Indian leaders.\niii. As there was no clear mention of complete Independence, the Indian National Congress rejected the Cripps Mission. On the other hand, the Muslim League rejected the Cripps Mission because the creation of Pakistan was not mentioned in the proposal.\nTherefore, as the Cripps proposal failed to satisfy the political parties in India, they rejected the Cripps Mission.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87443,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What resolution was passed by the Congress Working Committee on 14th  July, 1942 at Wardha?','','','i. On 14th  July, 1942, at Wardha, the Congress Working Committee passed a resolution which demanded that the British rule in India should immediately come to an end and India should be granted freedom.\nii. The Indian National Congress also warned the British Government that if this demand was not fulfilled then India would start a non - violent struggle for acquiring freedom of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87444,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What inspirational message was given by Mahatma Gandhi to the people before starting the Quit India Movement?','','','i. The Quit India Movement was to be started under the leadership of Mahatma Gandhi.\nii. He said, \"Every one of you should, from this moment onwards, consider yourself a free man or woman and behave like a citizen of free India.... we shall either free India or die in the attempt.\niii. He also appealed the people to be ready to sacrifice their life with the feeling of \'Do or Die\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87445,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the importance of \'Azad Radio\' in the underground movement of 1942?','','','At the end of 1942, the leadership of \'People\'s Movement\' went into the hands of young socialist activists.\ni. Vitthal Zaveri, Usha Mehta and her companions established a secret transmission centre named \'Azad Radio\' in Mumbai.\nii. Through this radio, patriotic songs and speeches were broadcasted.\niii. Moreover, the news of agitation also spread to various places due. to \'Azad Radio\'. This encouraged the agitators.\niv. Such transmission centres were in operation for some time at Kolkata, Delhi and Pune.\nThus, the Azad Radio played a very important role in the underground movement of 1942.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87446,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe Subhash Chandra Bose\'s escape from India.','','','i. Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose contributed greatly towards attainment of freedom for India.\nii. He gave inspirational speeches to people. Through his speeches, he appealed the Indians to revolt against the British rule.\niii. Due to this, the British government imprisoned him.\niv. In the prison, he started fast unto death. Hence, he was put under house arrest.\nv. He escaped from his house after disguising himself and reached Germany in April 1941 where he founded the \'Free\' \'India Centre\'.\nvi. From Germany, through the Berlin Radio Station, he once again appealed the Indian people to join in an armed struggle.\nvii. While in Germany, he was invited to Japan by Rash Behari Bose.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87447,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the Indian National Army (Azad Hind Sena) formed?','','','i. The territories in Soúth East Asia which were under the control of the British were conquered by Japan in the early part of 1942.\nii. Thousands of Indian soldiers and officers in British army were taken as prisoners by Japan.\niii. A battalion of these Indian Prisoners of war was formed by Rash Behari Bose with the help of Captain Mohan Singh. This battalion was named as the \'Indian National Army\' (Azad Hind Sena) and Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose was its leader.\nIn this way, the Indian National Army (Azad Hind Sena) was formed by Rash Behari Bose with the help of Captain Mohan Singh.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87448,1946,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How were the revolts of the Indian Naval soldiers and Air Force officers an indication that the sentiments of discontent against the rulers had reached a climax?','','','	i. The Indian National Army inspired the Navy and the Air Force soldiers. They were discontented against the British Government.\nii. On 18^\"th \"  February 1946, the naval soldiers unfurled the tricolor flag on the British worship named \'Talwar\' in Mumbai. They even raised slogans against the British Government.\niii. The British Government sent army who fired on the naval soldiers. The soldiers counter attacked them. These soldiers were supported by the workers and the common people.\niv. However, due to Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel\'s mediation, the naval soldiers, finally, surrendered.\nv. Similarly, the officers of the Air Force from Delhi, Lahore, Karachi, Ambala, Meerut, etc., went on strike in order to extend their support to the uprising of the naval soldiers.\nIn this way, the revolts of the naval soldiers and Air Force Officers were an indication that the sentiments of discontent against the rulers had reached a climax.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87449,1946,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Individual Satyagraha','','','i. The Indian National Congress decided to start an anti-war propaganda since the British government constantly neglected its demands.\nii. To carry this out, it was decided that every single person should disobey the laws instead of a collective movement.\niii. This was known as \'Individual Satyagraha\' and Acharya Vinoba Bhave was the first satyagrahi.\niv. Nearly 25,000 satyagrahis followed him who were sent to jail for taking part in Individual Satyagraha.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87450,1946,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Importance of the Quit India Movement','','','i. The Quit India Movement also called as \'August Kranti\' which started in 1942 had later become a nationwide agitation.\nii. In order to achieve independence, lakhs of Indians made endless sacrifices. Many sacrificed their lives. Even school children participated in it and children like Shirishkumar, Laldas, Dhansukhlal, Shashidhar and Ghanashyam became martyrs.\niii. The Navy, Army and the Air Force which formed the backbone of the British power also opposed the British.\niv. Large number of people participated in the movement and the number was so huge that even all the prisons in the country were not enough to contain them.\nv. The agitators received enthusiasm because of the patriotic songs composed by Sane Guruji, Rashtrasant Tukdoji Maharaj, etc.\nvi. Due to the Quit India Movement, the British Government realised that the discontent of the Indian people against them had reached a climax and that it was now not possible for them to hold power in India for longer days.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87451,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The proposal of federal state in the Act of 1935 was not brought into practice.','','','i. One of the provisions of the Act of 1935 was that the British governed Indian provinces and Princely Sates should form a federation.\nii. Moreover, the working of the British governed provinces would be handed over to the Indian representatives as per the Act.\niii. The princely states, however, refused to join the federation since they would lose their autonomy.\nTherefore, the proposal of federal state in the Act of 1935 was not brought into practice.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87452,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subhash Chandra Bose resigned from the position of President of the Indian National Congress and formed his own party the \'Forward Bloc.','','','i. Subhash Chandra Bose was twice elected as the President of the Indian National Congress and was also an important leader of the Indian National Congress.\nii. According to him, while England was busy in the Second World War, Indians should take advantage of it and make the freedom struggle against the British more intense. He was also of the opinion that if needed India should take help of the enemies of England.\niii. But his viewpoint caused differences between him and other senior leaders of the Congress.\nTherefore, Subhash Chandra Bose resigned from the position of President of the Indian National Congress and in order to place his views in front of the people, formed his own party called the \'Forward Bloc\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87453,1946,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British Government, finally, suspended the punishments given by the military tribunal to the officers of the Indian National Army.','','','i. The Indian National Army under the leadership of Subhash Chandra Bose and with the help of Japan had occupied Andaman & Nicobar Island, Arakan province in Myanmar and posts on the eastern border of Assam.\nii. However, with the defeat of Japan in the Second World War and the death of Subhash Chandra Bose in a plane crash on 18th  August, 1945; the soldiers of the Indian National Army surrendered.\niii. Later, the officers of the Indian National Army were charged with treason by the British Government.\niv. Though expert lawyers like Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru, Bhulabhai Desai and Tej Bahadur Sapru defended these officers, the military tribunal held the officers guilty and sentenced them to life imprisonment.\nv. Due to this, there was great discontent among the Indians against the British Government.\nTherefore, the British Government, finally, suspended the punishments given by the military tribunal to the officers of the Indian National Army.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87454,1946,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Prepare a timeline of the events of the struggle of Indian National Army.','','','i. The Indian National Army was founded.\nii. Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose established the Azad Hind Government in Singapore.\niii. Japan conquered Andaman and Nicobar islands and handed them over to Azad Hind Government.\niv. a. Indian National Army captured the Arakan province in Myanmar.\nb. May 1944 - Azad Hind Sena under the leadership of Shah Nawaz Khan captured a place called \'Modak\'.\nv. Subhash Chandra Bose died in a plane accident.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87455,1946,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','Beginning of People’s Movement : The news of arrest of important leaders of Indian National Congress spread out all over the country. The angry mob took out processions at various places. Even though police lathi charged and opened firing on the public but still people were not afraid. The agitators attacked some places jails, police stations, railway stations etc. which symbolised the suppressive policy of the British. They tried to take control of Government offices. In Maharashtra, the young and old struggled with determination and boundless courage at places like Chimur, Ashti, Yavali, Mahad, Gargoti etc. and made the struggle immortal.','','1) The arrest of which leaders led to the beginning of the People’s Movement?\n2) How did the people react to the arrest of their leaders?\n3) Which places in Maharashtra became centers of struggle during the movement?\n4) What did the agitators attack during the People’s Movement?\n','','1) The arrest of important leaders of the Indian National Congress led to the beginning of the People’s Movement.\n2) The people became angry and took out processions at various places.\n3) Chimur, Ashti, Yavali, Mahad, and Gargoti became centers of struggle.\n4) The agitators attacked jails, police stations, and railway stations symbolizing British suppression.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87456,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Act of ---- made a provision for a federation of British provinces and Princely States.\na) 1930 b) 1932 c) 1935 d) 1940\n','','','c) 1935','',0,0,0,NULL),(87457,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Indian National Congress participated in the elections held in ---- under the Act of 1935.\na) 1936 b) 1937 c) 1938 d) 1939\n','','','b) 1937','',0,0,0,NULL),(87458,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the 1937 elections, Congress gained majority in ---- provinces.\na) Six b) Seven c) Eight d) Nine\n','','','b) Seven','',0,0,0,NULL),(87459,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Cripps Mission came to India in ----.\na) January 1942 b) March 1942 c) June 1942 d) August 1942\n','','','b) March 1942','',0,0,0,NULL),(87460,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Cripps Mission was sent to India by ----.\na) Winston Churchill b) Lord Mountbatten c) Clement Attlee d) Lord Linlithgow\n','','','a) Winston Churchill','',0,0,0,NULL),(87461,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Second World War began in the year ----.\na) 1937 b) 1938 c) 1939 d) 1940\n','','','c) 1939','',0,0,0,NULL),(87462,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Viceroy who declared India’s participation in the Second World War was ----.\na) Lord Mountbatten b) Lord Linlithgow c) Lord Wavell d) Lord Irwin\n','','','b) Lord Linlithgow','',0,0,0,NULL),(87463,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Quit India resolution was passed at ---- in Mumbai.\na) Shivaji Park b) Gowalia Tank c) Red Fort d) India Gate\n','','','b) Gowalia Tank','',0,0,0,NULL),(87464,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The President of the Indian National Congress session held in August 1942 was ----.\na) Jawaharlal Nehru b) Maulana Abul Kalam Azad c) Sardar Patel d) Subhash Chandra Bose\n','','','b) Maulana Abul Kalam Azad','',0,0,0,NULL),(87465,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mahatma Gandhi’s famous slogan during the Quit India Movement was ----.\na) Freedom for all b) Swaraj is my birthright c) Do or Die d) Quit the British\n','','','c) Do or Die','',0,0,0,NULL),(87466,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In Maharashtra, the Quit India Movement was especially strong at ----.\na) Pune and Mumbai b) Chimur, Ashti, and Yavali c) Nagpur and Wardha d) Satara and Kolhapur\n','','','b) Chimur, Ashti, and Yavali','',0,0,0,NULL),(87467,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The leader of the Parallel Government in Satara district was ----.\na) G.D. Bapu Lad b) Krantisinh Nana Patil c) S.M. Joshi d) Yashwantrao Chavan\n','','','b) Krantisinh Nana Patil','',0,0,0,NULL),(87468,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Subhash Chandra Bose founded a political party called ----.\na) Indian Independence League b) Forward Bloc c) Azad Hind Sena d) Congress Socialist Party\n','','','b) Forward Bloc','',0,0,0,NULL),(87469,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The slogan “You give me blood and I will give you independence” was given by ----.\na) Mahatma Gandhi b) Sardar Patel c) Subhash Chandra Bose d) Bal Gangadhar Tilak\n','','','c) Subhash Chandra Bose','',0,0,0,NULL),(87470,1946,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The naval revolt broke out in Mumbai on ----.\na) 15 August 1945 b) 18 February 1946 c) 26 January 1947 d) 2 October 1946\n','','','b) 18 February 1946','',0,0,0,NULL),(87471,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Swatantryaveer Savarkar started a secret organization of revolutionaries named ____.\n(A) Abhinav Bharat  (B) Mitramela  (C) India House  (D) Anushilan Samiti\n','','','B) Mitramela','',0,0,0,NULL),(87472,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','In Punjab, ____ organised an uprising against the Government.\n(A) Lala Lajpat Rai  (B) Bhagat Singh  (C) Ramsingh Kuka  (D) Udham Singh\n','','','C) Ramsingh Kuka','',0,0,0,NULL),(87473,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','____ founded the India House.\n(A) Pandit Shyamji Krishna Varma  (B) V.D. Savarkar  (C) Lala Hardayal  (D) Rash Behari Bose\n','','','A) Pandit Shyamji Krishna Varma','',0,0,0,NULL),(87474,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A youth named ____ killed Jackson, the collector of Nashik.\n(A) Anant Laxman Kanhere  (B) Madan Lal Dhingra  (C) Khudiram Bose  (D) Chandrashekhar Azad\n','','','A) Anant Laxman Kanhere','',0,0,0,NULL),(87475,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Munda tribe in Bihar revolted under the leadership of ____.\n(A) Birsa Munda  (B) Tantya Tope  (C) Sidhu Murmu  (D) Alluri Sitarama Raju\n','','','A) Birsa Munda','',0,0,0,NULL),(87476,1947,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Killed Rand : i) -----------------     	ii) -----------------','','','i) Balkrishna Chafekar 		ii) Damodar Chafekar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87477,1947,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Planned to kill Judge Kingsford and threw bomb on his vehicle : i) ------------	ii) ---------','','','i) Khudiram Bose			ii) Prafulla Chaki','',0,0,0,NULL),(87478,1947,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Leaders of Gadar organization : i) ------------- 	ii) --------------','','','i) Bhai Parmanand			ii) Dr. Pandurang Sadashiv Khankhoje','',0,0,0,NULL),(87479,1947,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Threw bomb in Central Legislative Assembly : i) ------------ 	ii) ------------','','','i) Bhagat Singh			ii) Batukeshwar Dutta','',0,0,0,NULL),(87480,1947,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Killed the District Judge : i) ------------- 		ii) ---------------','','','i) Shanti Ghosh			ii) Suniti Choudhary.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87481,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vasudev Balwant Phadke took the training of arms from ____ .\n(a) Damodar Chafekar  (b) Vastad Lahuji Salve  (c) Swatantryaveer Savarkar  (d) Babarao Savarkar\n','','','b) Vastad Lahuji Salve','',0,0,0,NULL),(87482,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','\'Anushilan Samiti\' received counsel and guidance from ____ .\n(a) Aurobindo Ghosh  (b) Barindrakumar Ghosh  (c) Khudiram Bose  (d) Rash Behari Bose\n','','','a) Aurobindo Ghosh','',0,0,0,NULL),(87483,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ raised the issue of India\'s independence at the World Socialist Conference.\n(a) Pritilata Waddedar  (b) Kalpana Dutta  (c) Madam Cama  (d) Shanti Ghosh\n','','','c) Madam Cama','',0,0,0,NULL),(87484,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ a young girl shot dead the Governor during the convocation ceremony of Kolkata University.\n(a) Shanti Ghosh  (b) Pritilata Waddedar  (c) Suniti Choudhary  (d) Bina Das\n','','','d) Bina Das','',0,0,0,NULL),(87485,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ died in an encounter with police at Alfred Park in Allahabad.\n(a) Bhagat Singh  (b) Chandrashekhar Azad  (c) Madanlal Dhingra  (d) Khudiram Bose\n','','','b) Chandrashekhar Azad','',0,0,0,NULL),(87486,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In his autobiography, ____ wrote about his experience of the terrible days in Andaman jail.\n(a) Bhagat Singh  (b) Vasudev Balwant Phadke  (c) Swatantryaveer Savarkar  (d) Baba Savarkar\n','','','c) Swatantryaveer Savarkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87487,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Anushilan Samiti had a bomb manufacturing unit at ____ .\n(a) Kolkata  (b) Dhaka  (c) Maniktala  (d) Chittagong\n','','','c) Maniktala','',0,0,0,NULL),(87488,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The young men of Hindustan Socialist Republic were influenced by ____ ideology.\n(a) capitalistic  (b) imperialistic  (c) religious  (d) socialist\n','','','d) socialist','',0,0,0,NULL),(87489,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After the British traced the plan of revolt by Gadar Organisation, ____ escaped to Japan and continued his revolutionary work.\n(a) Vishnu Shastri Pingle  (b) Khudiram Bose  (c) Rash Behari Bose  (d) Chandrashekhar Azad\n','','','c) Rash Behari Bose','',0,0,0,NULL),(87490,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ was the chief of revolutionary group in Bengal.\n(a) Anant Singh  (b) Ganesh Ghosh  (c) Rash Behari Bose  (d) Surya Sen\n','','','d) Surya Sen','',0,0,0,NULL),(87491,1947,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the wrong pair, correct it and rewrite :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Work</th>\n    <th>Revolutionary</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Threw bomb on Lord Hardinge</td>\n    <td>Khudiram Bose</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Killed British officer Ash</td>\n    <td>Vanchhi Iyyer</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Assassinated Curzon Wylie</td>\n    <td>Bhagat Singh</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) Killed Dyer in London</td>\n    <td>Sardar Udham Singh</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','Wrong pair : Assassinated Curzon Wylie - Bhagat Singh\nCorrected pair : Assassinated Curzon Wylie - Madanlal Dhingra','',0,0,0,NULL),(87492,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who spread the network of revolutionary work outside Bengal?','','','Rash Behari Bose and Sachindranath Sanyal spread the network of revolutionary organisation outside Bengal.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87493,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the objective in establishing India House?','','','The revolutionary work in India received assistance from India House in London and Indian youth were given scholarships for higher education in England.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87494,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who prepared anti-British plans with the help of German Foreign ministry?','','','Veerendra Nath Chattopadhyay, Bhupen Dutta and Hardayal prepared anti-British plans with the help of German foreign ministry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87495,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who established Provincial Government of free India in Kabul?','','','Mahendra Pratap, Barkatullah and Obaidullah Sindhi established Provincial Government of free India in Kabul.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87496,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What work was entrusted to a separate wing of \'Hindustan Socialist Republican Army\'?','','','The work of gathering arms and execution of programmes was entrusted to a separate wing of Hindustan Socialist Republican Army.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87497,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the death of Lala Lajpat Rai avenged?','','','Bhagat Singh and Rajguru fired bullets and killed officer Saunders to avenge death of Lala Lajpat Rai.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87498,1947,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87499,1947,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87500,1947,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87501,1947,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 70%; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Revolutionary</th>\n    <th>Organisation</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. .........................</td>\n    <td>Abhinav Bharat</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Barindrakumar Ghosh</td>\n    <td>..................................</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Chandrashekhar Azad</td>\n    <td>..............................</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. .......................</td>\n    <td>Gadar</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Revolutionary</th>\n    <th>Organisation</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Swatantryaveer Savarkar</td>\n    <td>Abhinav Bharat</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Barindrakumar Ghosh</td>\n    <td>Anushilan Samiti</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Chandrashekhar Azad</td>\n    <td>Hindustan Socialist Republican Army</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Lala Hardayal</td>\n    <td>Gadar</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(87502,1947,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vasudev Balwant Phadke ','','','1) Vasudev Balwant Phadke gave an armed struggle against the British in Maharashtra.\n(2) He was of the opinion that there should be armed struggle to fight against the British.\n(3) Vastad Lahuji Salve gave him training of arms.\n(4) He organized Ramoshis and led armed struggle in Maharashtra, but was unsuccessful.\n(5) He was sent to Eden Jail by the British Government where he died in 1883.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87503,1947,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Literary and Social work of Swctantryaveer Savarkar ','','','1) When Swatantryaveer Savarkar was detained in the Ratnagiri jail, he started many social movements such as removal of caste differences, removal of untouchability, common dining, purification of language, etc.\n(2) In his autobiography, \'Majhi Janmathep\' he wrote about his experiences of the terrible days in Andaman where he spent ten years.\n(3) He wrote the book \"The Indian War of Independence 1857^\' in which he stated 1857 rebellion as the first war of independence.\n(4) He wrote an inspiring biography of Joseph Mazzini.\n(5) Savarkar was a great writer, social worker and revolutionary.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87504,1947,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Kakori Conspiracy ','','','1) After Gandhiji suspended Civil Disobedience, many youngsters like Chandrashekhar Azad, Ram Prasad Bismil, Yogesh Chatterjee, Sachindra Nath Sanyal got diverted to revolutionary ways.\n(2) On 9th  August, 1925 they looted the Government treasury that was on a train near Kakori railway station in Uttar Pradesh.\n(3) However, due to the immediate action of the government the revolutionaries were arrested and put on trial.\n(4) Ashfaqulla Khan, Ram Prasad Bismil, Roshan Singh, Rajendra Lahiri were hanged.\n(5) Chandrashekhar Azad managed to escape.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87505,1947,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Chafekar brothers killed Rand.','','','1) The Plague epidemic broke out in 1897 in Pune.\n(2) Commissioner Rand was appointed to manage the Plague epidemic.\n(3) In course of curtailing the epidemic, he resorted to tyranny and force.\nAs a revenge, the Chafekar brothers Damodar and Balkrishna shot him dead on 22 June, 1897.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87506,1947,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Khudiram Bose was hanged to death.','','','1) Anushilan Samiti worked with the objective to overthrow the British rule with armed rebellion.\n(2) Prafulla Chaki and Khudiram Bose were the members of Anushilan Samiti.\n(3) They planned to kill α judge named Kingsford.\n(4) They threw a bomb at a vehicle, assuming it to be of Kingsford but the vehicle was not the one carrying him.\n(5) However, two English women were killed in this attack. Khudiram Bose was caught by the police and was hanged.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87507,1947,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutta threw bombs in the Central Legislative Assembly.','','','1) The Government had introduced two bills in the Central Legislature.\n(2) The Bills curtailed civil rights of the people.\n(3) To protest against these bills, Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutta threw bombs in the Central Legislative Assembly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87508,1947,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Swatantryaveer Savarkar was sentenced to fifty years of rigorous imprisonment.','','','1) On knowing the activities of Abhinav Bharat, the government arrested Babarao Savarkar and sentenced to life imprisonment.\n(2) Anant Laxman Kanhere killed Jackson, collector of Nashik in retaliation.\n(3) The government linked Abhinav Bharat and Savarkar with his murder.\n(4) He was arrested and put on trial. So, the court sentenced him to fifty years of rigorous imprisonment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87509,1947,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev and Rajguru were hanged.','','','1) Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutta threw a bomb in Central Legislative Assembly.\n(2) The British Government started arresting the revolutionaries. They immediately raided the centres of \'Hindustan Socialist Republican Army\' and got clues related to the killing of Saunders.\n(3) Bhagat Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev were tried under the charge of sedition.\n(4) They were hanged in the Lahore jail on 23 March 1931.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87510,1947,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Madanlal Dhingra was hanged to death.','','','1) India House was an important centre which provided assistance to Indian revolutionaries staying abroad.\n(2) Madanlal Dhingra was a youth associated with India House.\n(3) He killed Curzon Wylie, a British Officer, therefore he was hanged to death.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87511,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Write a detail description of the attack on Chittagong Armoury.','','','1) Surya Sen drew a plan to attack the armoury at Chittagong.\n(2) He and his associates raided two government armouries on the 18^\"th \"  April, 1930.\n(3) They seized arms and ammunition from the two armouries.\n(4) He took revolutionaries like Anant Singh, Ganesh Ghosh, Kalpana Datta and Pritilata Waddedar with him.\n(5) They cut off the telephone and telegraph connections and succeeded in paralysing the communication.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87512,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about the work of India House.','','','1) India House, founded by Pandit Shyamji Krishna Verma in London, was the centre of revolutionary activities outside India.\n(2) It provided assistance to the revolutionaries staying abroad.\n(3) It gave scholarships to the Indian students studying in England.\n(4) Swatantryaveer Savarkar received such scholarship.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87513,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the aims and objectives of the \'Hindustan Socialist Republican Ässociation\'.','','','1) The main objective of the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association was to free India from British exploitation.\n(2) It also aimed at overthrowing the unjust socio-economic order which exploited the farmers and workers.\n(3) It aimed at creation of a society based on social justice and equality.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87514,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the work of Madam Cama.','','','1) Madam Cama was related with the revolutionary work of India House.\n(2) She raised the issue of India\'s independence at the World Socialist Conference held at Stuttgart in Germany.\n(3) She unfurled the flag of India at this conference.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87515,1947,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the contribution of Swatantryaveer Savarkar in the armed revolutionary movement.','','','1) Swatantryaveer Savarkar founded \'Mitramela\' in 1900 at Nashik and renamed it as \'Abhinav Bharat\' in 1904.\n(2) He went to England for higher studies, despatched revolutionary literature, guns to the members of Abhinav Bharat in India.\n(3) The government linked murder of Jackson to Swatantryaveer Savarkar and arrested him.\n(4) He was put under trial. The court ordered rigorous punishment for 50 years in Andaman jail.\n(5) His contribution is great in the armed revolution of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87516,1947,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give a brief account of the \'Änushilan Samiti\'.','','','1) After the partition of Bengal, the outrage against the British became more severe.\n(2) In Bengal, a revolutionary organisation called \'Anushilan Samiti\' was active.\n(3) This organisation received counsel and guidance from Aurobindo Ghosh.\n(4) Barindrakumar Ghosh was chief of this organisation.\n(5) In 1908, members of Anushilan Samiti, Prafulla Chaki and Khudiram Bose made an unsuccessful attempt to kill a judge Kingsford.\n(6) During the investigation, police got information about the work of Anushilan Samiti.\n(7) So, the government started arresting members of Anushilan Samiti.\n(8) The government was unsuccessful in linking Aurobindo Ghosh to the manufacturing of bombs.\n(9) Other members were sentenced to jail for longer period.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87517,1947,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the activities of Gadar Organisation.','','','1) The Indians settled in America and Canada established the Gadar Organisation.\n(2) The word Gadar means revolt. The objective of the organisation was to revolt against the British and free India from British rule.\n(3) Lala Hardayal, Bhai Parmanand, Dr. Pandurang Sadashiv Khankhoje were the important revolutionaries of the organisation.\n(4) \'Gadar\' Journal, the mouthpiece of the organisation voiced the evil effects of the British rule on India and published the news regarding the heroic deeds of the Indian revolutionaries.\n(5) It conveyed the message of patriotism and armed rebellion to the Indian people.\n(6) During the British rule, the Gadar Organisation decided to take advantage of the discontent against the British rule.\n(7) It incited the Indian soldiers to revolt against the government.\n(8) Rash Behari Bose and Vishnu Ganesh Pingle were entrusted the responsibility to lead the revolt.\n(9) However, the plan could not materialise due to treachery.\n(10) Pingle was arrested and hanged.\n(11) Rash Behari Bose managed to escape to Japan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87518,1947,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What difference have you noted between the Moderates, Extremists and Revolutionary.','','','The struggle against the British was fought in three ways. The difference in the way is as follows :\n(1) The moderates advocated peaceful and constitutional means. They believed in appeals and petitions. They tried to disrupt the working of the government from within. After their demands were met, they would put forth new demands. Thus, they believed in constitutional methods.\n(2) The approach of extremists was severe. They were of the opinion that if lakhs of people take part in the freedom movement and challenge the British government only then success could be achieved.\n(3) Revolutionary wanted to fight the British in armed way. According to them, there should be armed struggle against the British. They adopted methods like killing the British officers, raiding the government treasury, disrupting transport and communication, etc.\nAll the three ways are different but had one objective of achieving Independence for India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87519,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In his autobiography Majhi Janmathep, ………… wrote down his experiences of the terrible days in Andaman.\n(A) Swatantryaveer Savarkar  (B) Vasudev Balwant Phadke  (C) Mahadev Ranade  (D) Babarao Savarkar\n','','','A) Swatantryaveer Savarkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87520,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rash Bihari Bose and ____ spread the network of revolutionary organisations outside Bengal.\n(A) Prafulla Chaki  (B) Khudiram Bose  (C) Sachindra Nath Sanyal  (D) Barindrakumar Ghosh\n','','','C) Sachindra Nath Sanyal','',0,0,0,NULL),(87521,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rash Bihari Bose and his associate threw a bomb at Viceroy ____ who got saved from the attack.\n(A) Lord Dalhousie  (B) Lord Warring  (C) Lord Mountbatten  (D) Lord Hardinge\n','','','D) Lord Hardinge','',0,0,0,NULL),(87522,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A revolutionary named ____ killed a British officer named Ash.\n(A) Vanchhi Iyyer  (B) Prafulla Chaki  (C) Mahadev Ranade  (D) Batukeshwar Dutta\n','','','A) Vanchhi Iyyer','',0,0,0,NULL),(87523,1947,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the table.:\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Revolutionary work in other countries</th>\n    <th>Leaders</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Berlin (Germany)</td>\n    <td>.......................................</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Kabul (Afghanistan)</td>\n    <td>......................................</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Revolutionary work in other countries</th>\n    <th>Leaders</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Berlin (Germany)</td>\n    <td>Veerendra Nath Chattopadhyay, Bhupen Dutta, Hardayal</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Kabul (Afghanistan)</td>\n    <td>Mahendra Pratap, Barkatullah, Obaidullah Sindhi</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(87524,1947,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give names of revolutionary leaders who killed British officers :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Indian revolutionary leaders</th>\n    <th>British officer</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. .........................</td>\n    <td>Curzon Wylie (British officer)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. ..........................</td>\n    <td>Saunders (British police officer responsible for killing Lala Lajpat Rai)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. ............................</td>\n    <td>General Dyer (British General responsible for Jallianwala Bagh massacre)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. .............................</td>\n    <td>District Judge</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Indian revolutionary leaders</th>\n    <th>British officer</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Madanlal Dhingra</td>\n    <td>Curzon Wylie (British officer)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Bhagat Singh, Rajguru</td>\n    <td>Saunders (British police officer responsible for killing Lala Lajpat Rai)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Udham Singh</td>\n    <td>General Dyer (British General responsible for Jallianwala Bagh massacre)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. Shanti Ghosh, Suniti Choudhary</td>\n    <td>District Judge</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(87525,1947,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the timeline.','','','i. Revolutionaries including Chandrashekhar Azad, Ram Prasad Bismil, Yogesh Chatterjee, Sachindra Nath Sanyal, etc. looted a Government treasury from a train near Kakori railway station.\nii. Surya Sen and other revolutionaries seized arms from two armouries in Chittagong.\niii.  23^\"rd \"  March 1931\niv. 16 February 1933\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87527,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Under whose leadership did the Munda tribe in Bihar revolt against the British?','','','The Munda tribe in Bihar revolted against the British under the leadership of Birsa Munda.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87528,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who wrote the \'Indian War of Independence-1857\'?','','','Swatantryaveer Vinayak Damodar Savarkar wrote the \'Indian War of Independence-1857.\'','',0,0,0,NULL),(87529,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which social movements were started by Swatantryaveer Savarkar while at Ratnagiri?','','','After Swatantryaveer Savarkar was shifted from imprisonment at Andaman to Ratnagiri, he started many social movements such as removal of untouchability, common dining, purification of language, etc. over there.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87530,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who established the \'Gadar\' organisation?','','','The Indians living in Canada and America established the \'Gadar\' organisation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87531,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the revolutionaries that were influenced by socialist ideas.','','','The revolutionaries that were influenced by socialist ideas were Bhagat Singh, Rajguru, Sukhdev and Chandrashekar Azad.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87532,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What consequences were faced by the Chapekar brothers after the assassination of Commissioner Rand?','','','i. The Chapekar brothers, Damodar and Balkrishna assassinated Commissioner Rand on 22nd  June 1897 for resorting to tyranny and force while managing the epidemic of plague in Pune.\nii. Thereafter, the British caught the three Chapekar brothers Damodar, Balkrishna and Vasudev as well as their associate Mahadev Ranade and hanged them to death.\nThus, the three brothers from the same house accepted martyrdom for the service of the country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87533,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was Swatantryaveer Savarkar sentenced to fifty years of rigorous imprisonment?','','','i. Government got to know about the activities of Abhinav Bharat, arrested Babarao Savarkar in this connection and sentenced him to life imprisonment.\nii. A youth named Anant Laxman Kanhere killed Jackson, the Collector of Nasik, as revenge to the punishment meted out to Babarao Savarkar.\niii. Due to this, the Government started arresting all the people who were connected with Abhinav Bharat organisation.\niv. Swatantryaveer Savarkar was also linked with the murder of Jackson and was arrested and put under trial.\nThereafter, the court ordered rigorous punishment to Savarkar for fifty years.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87534,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the outrage of the people against the British became more severe after the partition of Bengal?','','','i. After the partition of Bengal, more comprehensive national level movements were conducted instead of local rebellions.\nii. Youths inspired by revolutionary thoughts started establishing their secret organisations in various parts of the country.\niii. The main objectives of these revolutionaries were to have control over the British officers, to weaken the government machinery and to remove the fear of British rule from the minds of the Indians.\nThus, the outrage of the people against the British became more severe after the partition of Bengal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87535,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did British suppress the work of Anushilan Samiti?','','','i. During the investigation of the incident where two English women were killed by a bomb thrown on their vehicle, the police got information about the working of the Anushilan Samiti.\nii. They arrested Aurobindo Ghosh and other members of the organisation, but were not able to prove the connection of Aurobindo Ghosh with the bombs and hence he was set free.\niii. However, other members were sentenced to long terms of imprisonment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87536,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the \'Kakori Conspiracy?\'','','','i. After the suspension of the Civil Disobedience Movement, many young nationalists followed the path of revolutionary activities.\nii. Revolutionaries such as Chandrashekhar Azad, Ram Prasad Bismil, Yogesh Chatterjee, Sachindra Nath Sanyal, etc. came together.\niii. They looted the government treasury that was being carried on a train on 9th  August 1925, near Kakori railway station in Uttar Pradesh. This is known as Kakori conspiracy.\niv. The government arrested these revolutionaries immediately, put into prison and were kept under trail.\nv. From among these revolutionaries Ashfaqulla Khan, Ram Prasad Bismil; Roshan Singh, Rajendra Lahiri were hanged while Chandrashekhar Azad managed to escape.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87537,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about Hindustan Socialist Republican Association and its objectives.','','','i. A decision to setup a nationwide revolutionary organisation was taken by young men who were influenced by socialist ideas.\nii. They included revolutionaries like Chandrashekhar Azad, Bhagat Singh, Rajguru, Sukhdev, etc. who were secular in their thoughts.\niii. A meeting was held in 1928 at Feroz Shah Kotla ground in Delhi, where these young men established the organisation called \'Hindustan Socialist Republican Association\'.\niv. The main objective of this organisation was to free India from British exploitation and also to get rid of the unjust socio economic system which exploited farmers and workers.\nv. Bhagat Singh, a member of this organisation, gave importance to create a society based on social justice and equality.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87538,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the British curtail the activities of the Hindustan Socialist Republican Army?','','','i. After the incidence of the throwing of bombs by Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutt in the Central Legislative Assembly, the British Government immediately raided the centres of \'Hindustan Socialist Republican Army\'.\nii. These raids provided the police the clues related to the killing of Saunders and they started arresting the revolutionaries.\niii. These revolutionaries were tried under the charges of sedition and on 23rd  March 1931, Bhagat Singh, Rajguru and Sukhdev were hanged in the Lahore Jail.\'\niv. Although Chandrashekhar Azad did not fall in the hands of the police, he later died in a encounter with police at Alfred Park in Allahabad.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87539,1947,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was the Gadar organisation setup?','','','i. The revolutionary work against the British Government got a momentum during the First World War.\nii. The revolutionaries felt that by taking help from the enemies of the British, they can bring about the transfer of power in India. They also decided to take help of the Indian soldiers.\niii. Thereafter, various organisations were setup to take advantage of this situation, one of which was the Gadar organisation.\niv. It was established by the Indians living in America & Canada and included revolutionaries like Lala Hardayal, Bhai Parmanand, Dr. Pandurang Sadashiv Khankhoje, etc. as important leaders.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87540,1947,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Revolutionary work of Vasudev Balwant Phadke','','','i. Vasudev Balwant Phadke was a revolutionary from Maharashtra who took up armed struggle against the British for independence of India.\nii. In order to achieve this, he took training of arms from Vastad Lahuji Salve.\niii. He also organised the Ramoshis and revolted against the British. However, this rebellion became unsuccessful.\niv. Thereafter, he was sent to the Eden Jail where he died in 1883.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87541,1947,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Anushilan Samiti','','','i. A revolutionary organisation called Anushilan Samiti with more than 500 branches was active in Bengal.\nii. Barindrakumar Ghosh was the chief of this organisation which received counsel and guidance from his brother Aurobindo Ghosh.\niii. Anushilan Samiti had a bomb manufacturing centre at Maniktala near Kolkata.\niv. In 1908, an attempt to kill a judge named Kingsford by Khudiram Bose and Prafulla Chaki, the members of Anushilan Samiti, became unsuccessful when they mistakenly bombed a vehicle carrying two English women.\nv. Thereafter, Prafulla Chaki shot himself with a bullet and Khudiram Bose was caught by the police. Further investigations, helped police to get information about the working of Anushilan Samiti and it arrested more members including Aurobindo Ghosh. While other members were sentenced to long terms of imprisonment, Aurobindo Ghosh was set free as the court could not associate him with manufacturing of bombs.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87542,1947,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','India House','','','i. India House in London was an important centre which provided assistance to the Indian revolutionary movement.\nii. Pandit Shyamji Krishna Varma, had established the India House, through which Indian youths were given scholarships for higher education in England. Swatantryaveer Savarkar was one such person who had received scholarship.\niii. Another revolutionary of this organisation, Madam Cama, raised the issue of India\'s independence at the World Socialist Conference held at Stuggart in. Germany and unfurled the flag of India.\niv. Madanlal Dhingra, another revolutionary associated with the organisation, killed the British Officer Curzon Wylie, for which he was hanged to death.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87543,1947,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Work of the Gadar organisation','','','i. The Gadar organisation was an important organisation setup by the Indians living in America and Canada.\nii. The word Gadar means \'revolt\'. Also Gadar was the name of the journal of this organisation which acted as its mouthpiece.\niii. This journal pointed out the effects of the British rule in India and gave information about the daring deeds of the revolutionaries and thus inculcated love for the nation and gave the message of rebellion to the Indians.\niv. The leaders of the Gadar organisation decided to take advantage of the war situation and planned a revolt in Punjab against the British.\nv. The Indian soldiers in the British army were persuaded to join the revolt led by Rash Bihari Bose and Vishnu Ganesh Pingle.\nvi. However, due to treachery Pingle fell in the hands of the police and was hanged to death.\nvii. Rash Bihari Bose managed to escape to Japan where he continued his revolutionary work.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87544,1947,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Hindustan Socialist Republican Army','','','i. Hindustan Socialist Republican Army was a wing of the revolutionary organisation Hindustan Socialist Republican Association.\nii. The main work of this army was to gather arms and execute the programmes planned by the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association.\niii. Chandrashekhar Azad was the chief of this wing and he along with the other members of the wing carried out many revolutionary activities, as follows:\na. Bhagat Singh and Rajguru filled an officer Saunders by firing bullets at him in order to avenge the death of Lala Lajpat Rai.\nb. Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutt threw bombs in the Central Legislative Assembly to oppose two bills of the Government which curtailed the civil rights.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87545,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which revolutionary sent revolutionary literature, guns etc. to the members of Abhinav Bharat in India?','','','The revolutionary who sent revolutionary literature, guns etc. to the members of Abhinav Bharat in India was Swatantryaveer Vinayak Damodar Savarkar.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87546,1947,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which centre of London provided assistance to revolutionaries of India?','','','The India House in London provided assistance to revolutionaries of India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87547,1947,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','Abhinav Bharat : In 1900, Swatantryaveer Vinayak Damodar Savarkar founded ‘Mitramela’- a secret organisation of revolutionaries in Nasik. The same organisation was renamed as ‘Abhinav Bharat’ in 1904. Savarkar went to England for higher education. From there he began sending revolutionary literature, guns etc. to the members of Abhinav Bharat in India. He wrote an inspiring biography of Joseph Mazzini, the famous Italian revolutionary. He wrote a book entitled ‘The 1857 War of Independence’ wherein he stated that revolt of 1857 was the first war of independence.','','1) Who founded the secret organisation ‘Mitramela’?\n2) Where was ‘Mitramela’ established?\n3) When was ‘Mitramela’ renamed as ‘Abhinav Bharat’?\n4) Which book was written by Savarkar to describe the revolt of 1857?','','1) Swatantryaveer Vinayak Damodar Savarkar founded the secret organisation ‘Mitramela’.\n2) ‘Mitramela’ was established in Nasik.\n3) ‘Mitramela’ was renamed as ‘Abhinav Bharat’ in 1904.\n4) Savarkar wrote the book ‘The 1857 War of Independence’.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87548,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Swatantryaveer Vinayak Damodar Savarkar founded the secret organisation ‘Mitramela’ in ----.\na) 1899 b) 1900 c) 1902 d) 1904\n','','','b) 1900','',0,0,0,NULL),(87549,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The organisation ‘Mitramela’ was later renamed as ----.\na) Gadar Party b) Abhinav Bharat c) Indian National Army d) Arya Samaj\n','','','b) Abhinav Bharat','',0,0,0,NULL),(87550,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Savarkar wrote the book ‘The 1857 War of Independence’, describing the revolt of 1857 as ----.\na) A British victory b) A military revolt c) The first war of independence d) A local rebellion\n','','','c) The first war of independence','',0,0,0,NULL),(87551,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Savarkar wrote the inspiring biography of the Italian revolutionary ----.\na) Giuseppe Garibaldi b) Joseph Mazzini c) Benito Mussolini d) Victor Emmanuel\n','','','b) Joseph Mazzini','',0,0,0,NULL),(87552,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who founded the secret revolutionary organisation ‘Mitramela’?\na) Lala Lajpat Rai b) Vinayak Damodar Savarkar c) Bal Gangadhar Tilak d) Bhagat Singh\n','','','b) Vinayak Damodar Savarkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87553,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which city was the organisation ‘Mitramela’ founded?\na) Pune b) Nasik c) Mumbai d) Nagpur\n','','','b) Nasik','',0,0,0,NULL),(87554,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The organisation ‘Mitramela’ was renamed as ‘Abhinav Bharat’ in ----.\na) 1902 b) 1904 c) 1905 d) 1906\n','','','b) 1904','',0,0,0,NULL),(87555,1947,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Savarkar sent revolutionary literature and guns to India from ----.\na) Germany b) France c) England d) Italy\n','','','c) England','',0,0,0,NULL),(87556,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','____ founded the Red Cross Society at Rajkot.\n(A) Lala Lajpat Rai  (B) Sane Guruji  (C) Dr. Rakhmabai Janardan Save  (D) Babasaheb Bole\n','','','C) Dr. Rakhmabai Janardan Save','',0,0,0,NULL),(87557,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','____ was the president of mill workers’ union at Amalner.\n(A) Lala Lajpat Rai  (B) Sane Guruji  (C) Babasaheb Bole  (D) Deenbandhu Mitra\n','','','B) Sane Guruji','',0,0,0,NULL),(87558,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The president of the first session of AITUC was ____.\n(A) Lala Lajpat Rai  (B) Babasaheb Bole  (C) Dr. Rakhmabai Janardan Save  (D) Deenbandhu Mitra\n','','','A) Lala Lajpat Rai','',0,0,0,NULL),(87559,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ got a bill passed in the Bombay Provincial Assembly for public water reservoirs to be opened to the untouchables.\n(A) Lala Lajpat Rai  (B) Sane Guruji  (C) Babasaheb Bole  (D) Deenbandhu Mitra\n','','','C) Babasaheb Bole','',0,0,0,NULL),(87560,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Neel Darpan written by ____ brought to notice the wretched conditions of the peasants.\n(A) Sane Guruji  (B) Lala Lajpat Rai  (C) Dr. Rakhmabai Janardan Save  (D) Deenbandhu Mitra\n','','','D) Deenbandhu Mitra','',0,0,0,NULL),(87561,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Through the magazine \'Somavanshiya Mitra\' raised voice regarding issues of Muralis and Jogtins.','','','Shivram Janba Kamble ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87562,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Chairman of Mumbai branch of Satyashodhak Samaj.','','','Narayan Meghaji Lokhande ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87563,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Movement of eradication of untouchability in Tamil Nadu','','','Perriyar Ramaswamy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87564,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Active in international Communist Movement.','','','Manavendranath Roy ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87565,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Founder of Kisan Sabha','','','Baba Ramchandra.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87566,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ was the president of ‘Akhil Bharatiya Kisan Sabha’.\n(A) Sane Guruji  (B) Swami Sahajananda Saraswati  (C) Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde  (D) Manavendranath Roy\n','','','B) Swami Sahajananda Saraswati','',0,0,0,NULL),(87567,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The session of the Congress was held in the rural part of ____ in Maharashtra.\n(A) Nagpur  (B) Baramati  (C) Faizpur  (D) Miraj\n','','','C) Faizpur','',0,0,0,NULL),(87568,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ went on fast unto death to open the doors of Vitthal temple at Pandharpur for the Dalits.\n(A) Sane Guruji  (B) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar  (C) Karmaveer Dadasaheb Gaikwad  (D) Babasaheb Bole\n','','','A) Sane Guruji','',0,0,0,NULL),(87569,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Due to efforts of ____ the workers started getting weekly holiday on Sunday from 10 June 1890.\n(A) Shripad Amrut Dange  (B) Shashipad Banerjee  (C) Lala Lajpat Rai  (D) Narayan Meghaji Lokhande\n','','','D) Narayan Meghaji Lokhande','',0,0,0,NULL),(87570,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In 1881, ____ wrote an article on Marx.\n(A) Dr. Rammanohar Lohia  (B) Lokmanya Tilak  (C) Mahatma Gandhi  (D) Shripad Amrut Dange\n','','','B) Lokmanya Tilak','',0,0,0,NULL),(87571,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Condemned untouchability through his book Vitaal Vidhwansan.\n(A) Gopal Baba Walangkar  (B) Shivram Janba Kamble  (C) Thakkar Bappa  (D) Appasaheb Patwardhan\n','','','A) Gopal Baba Walangkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87572,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Karmaveer Dadasaheb Gaikwad led Satyagraha for entry of Dalits in ____ temple.\n(A) Kalaram  (B) Vitthal  (C) Parvati  (D) Bhavani\n','','','A) Kalaram temple','',0,0,0,NULL),(87573,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ passed the Act of inter-caste marriage and gave it legal acceptance in the state of Kolhapur.\n(A) Mahatma Jyotirao Phule  (B) Rajarshi Shahu Maharaj  (C) Rajaram Maharaj  (D) Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde\n','','','B) Rajarshi Shahu Maharaj','',0,0,0,NULL),(87574,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the significance of \'Neel Darpan\'.','','','Deenbandhu Mitra in his play \'Neel Darpan\' brought to the notice of the society the wretched conditions of the peasants who were forced to cultivate indigo.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87575,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the important work done by \'Akhil Bharatiya Kisan Sabha\'?','','','The \'Akhil Bharatiya Kisan Sabha\' presented a declaration of peasants rights to the Indian National Congress.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87576,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What message did Lala Lajpat Rai give to the workers in the first session of AITUC?','','','At the first session of AITUC Lala Lajpat Rai asked the workers to actively participate in the national movement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87577,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did Dr. Ambedkar and his followers embrace Buddhism?','','','Dr. Ambedkar and his followers embraced Buddhism as it advocated humanity and equality.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87578,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which institutions were founded by Pandita Ramabai?','','','Pandita Ramabai had founded the \'Arya Mahila Samaj\' and \'Sharda Sadan\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87579,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What message did Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar give his followers?','','','Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar gave the inspirational message,\'Be Educated, Be Organised and Be Agitated\' to his followers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87580,1948,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87581,1948,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87582,1948,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87583,1948,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the following table :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Founder</th>\n    <th>Organisation / Institution</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde</td>\n    <td>.................</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. .........................</td>\n    <td>Akhil Bharatiya Kisan Sabha</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Ramabai Ranade</td>\n    <td>...........................</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. ........................</td>\n    <td>Independent Labour Party</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Founder</th>\n    <th>Organisation / Institution</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde</td>\n    <td>Depressed Classes Mission</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Prof. N. G. Ranga</td>\n    <td>Akhil Bharatiya Kisan Sabha</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Ramabai Ranade</td>\n    <td>Seva Sadan Institution</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar</td>\n    <td>Independent Labour Party</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(87584,1948,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Social Work of Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde ','','','1) The goal of Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde was to make the Dalits self respectful, well educated and engaged in work.\n(2) He worked to destroy the delusive ideas regarding Dalits in the minds of the upper caste.\n(3) He founded the \'Depressed Classes Mission\' for the progress of Dalits in 1906.\n(4) To achieve this, he started Marathi schools and work schools in parts of Parel, Deonar in Mumbai.\n(5) He actively took part in various movement which were related to the welfare of the Dalits like Satyagraha for entry in Parvati temple at Pune, Shetkari Parishad of Dalits and Federal Electorates.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87585,1948,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Reforms of Rajarshi Shahu Maharaj in the state of Kolhapur ','','','1) Rajarshi Shahu Maharaj worked for abolishing of caste discrimination.\n(2) He made revolutionary declaration for reservation in the state of Kolhapur.\n(3) He made law for free and compulsory primary education.\n(4) He worked to remove restrictions on inter dining, inter marriage and change of occupation in the caste system.\n(5) He passed the Act of inter caste marriage and made it legal in state.\n(6) He abolished \'Balutedari System\', by publishing a declaration in the Government Gazette of the state of Kolhapur on 22 February, 1918.\n(7) This allowed people to practice any occupation and freed them from type of social slavery.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87586,1948,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Meerut Conspiracy Case ','','','1) The young communist leaders built militant organisations of workers and peasants.\n(2) The growing influence of Communism on Indian youth became a matter of concern for the British and they felt it was α danger.\n(3) The communist leaders Shripad Amrut Dange, Muzaffar Ahmed, Keshav Neelkanth Joglekar, etc. were arrested.\n(4) They were falsely charged with planning of α conspiracy to overthrow the British rule.\n(5) The trial took place at Meerut. So it was called as \'Meerut Conspiracy Case\'.\n(6) They were given different punishments.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87587,1948,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Work of Rakhmabai Janardan Save','','','1) Dr. Rakhmabai Janardhan Save was the first practising woman doctor in India.\n(2) She delivered lectures on health related issues of women.\n(3) She opened a branch of Red Cross Society at Rajkot.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87588,1948,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Government decided to crush down the Communist movement.','','','1) The Communist Party was formed in India in 1925.\n(2) The young communist leaders started building militant organisations of workers and peasants.\n(3) The British government started feeling the danger of Communist movement and hence decide to crush it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87589,1948,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar started newspapers like \'Muknayak\', \'Bahishkrut Bharat\', etc.','','','1) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar wanted to establish a society based on principles of Liberty, Equality and Fraternity.\n(2) He intended to carry out movement to establish self-respect and social equality for Dalits.\n(3) It was necessary to create awakening in society in this respect and to voice their grief.\n(4) The newspaper was an integral part of Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar\'s movement.\n(5) Hence, he started many newspapers so that his ideas and thoughts reach the society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87590,1948,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','There aroused a need of nationwide workers union.','','','1) In the later half of the 19th century, textile mills, railway companies and such other industries were started in India.\n(2) The workers group were not aroused on large scale.\n(3) As there were no unions, the workers were not organised.\n(4) After the First World War, there was rise of worker class in India due to industrialisation.\nTherefore, a necessity was felt for nationwide worker union.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87591,1948,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Narayan Meghaji Lokhande is described as \'Father of Indian Workers Movement\'.','','','1) Industrialisation of India had started during the second half of the 19th century.\n(2) Shashipad Banerjee and Narayan Meghaji Lokhande organised workers at local level and made efforts to solve their problems.\n(3) Narayan Meghaji Lokhande formed the mill workers union known as \'Bombay Mill Hands Association\' in 1890.\n(4) Due to his efforts, the workers started getting weekly holiday on Sunday from 10 June, 1890.\n(5) This was the beginning of organised movement in India.\nThus, his contribution to the working class movement was so valuable that he is described as \'Father of Indian Workers Movement\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87592,1948,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Congress Socialist Party was formed in 1934.','','','1) Many young activists of the Indian National Congress believed that the society should be built on the principles of economic and social equality.\n(2) Due to this, there was spread of socialist ideas and thoughts.\n(3) They believed that to protect interests of the people it was necessary to overthrow the British rule.\n(4) So, they decided to set up a Socialist Party within the Congress fold while they were in Nashik jail. Accordingly, the Congress Socialist Party was formed in 1934.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87593,1948,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Why was the struggle for equality important in the making of Modern India?','','','1) India achieved freedom because of freedom struggle.\n(2) The scope of this struggle was not limited to achieve political freedom but emancipation of man from all types of bondages.\n(3) Therefore in the course of this struggle, there was opposition to feudalism, social inequality and economic exploitation.\n(4) The principle of equality was given equal importance.\n(5) Various groups such as farmers, workers, women and dalits came forward and started movements in order to get justice and bring about reforms.\nThis proves that the struggle for equality has great significance in the making of Modern India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87594,1948,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Write about the work of Sane Guruji in eastern Khandesh.','','','1) In 1938, due to heavy rains crops were destroyed in eastern Khandesh.\n(2) He organised the farmers who were in miserable state.\n(3) Sane Guruji organised meetings and processions at many places in eastern Khandesh to have land revenue waived for the farmers.\n(4) He took out marches on the Collector office.\n(5) He tried to create a strong centre of workers union at Dhule-Ammalner.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87595,1948,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','How was the struggle built up by the workers decisive for the national movement?','','','1) The beginning of the 19th  century witnessed the emergence of working class due to industrialisation. They made efforts to solve their problems.\n(2) During the anti-partition movement, workers carried out strikes from time to time to pressurise the British government.\n(3) The tea plantation workers in Assam launched an agitation against their wretched condition.\n(4) During the anti-paritition movement, workers carried out strikes from time to time in support of Swadeshi.\n(5) In 1928, the Mill Workers Union went on strike for six months. Many such strikes were organised by the Railway workers, jute mill workers, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87596,1948,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Discuss the nature of reform movement related to women.','','','1) Women had secondary position in the Indian social system.\n(2) They were subjected to many injustice because of evil practices.\n(3) In the 20 th century, many reforms were initiated for the betterment of women.\n(4) The reform movement was led by women and institutions formed by them.\n(5) They fought for issues such as right to inheritance, right to vote through medium of these institutions.\n(6) The involvement of women went on increasing. They played an active role in the national movement and in the revolutionary movement.\n(7) Due to the reform movement, women were included in the Provincial Ministries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87597,1948,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the work of Rajarshi Shahu Maharaj for abolition of caste distinction.','','','The following work was done by Rajarshi Shahu Maharaj in his state of Kolhapur for abolition of caste distinction :\n(1) He made revolutionary declaration for reservation in state of Kolhapur.\n(2) There were three restrictions in the caste system : inter dining, inter marriage and change of occupation.\n(3) Shahu Maharaj ate food from the hands of Dalit people and overthrew the restriction on inter dining.\n(4) He passed the Act of inter caste marriage and gave it a legal acceptance in his state.\n(5) He abolished \'Balutedari System\' on 22 February, 1918 and granted permission to practice any occupation by anyone.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87598,1948,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the objectives of Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar in launching the Dalit movement.','','','The objectives of Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar in launching the Dalit movement is as follows:\n(1) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar aimed at building a society on the principles of Liberty, Equality and Fraternity.\n(2) He was of the view that unless the caste system is completely uprooted, the injustice to the Dalits and inequality would not end.\n(3) According to him, social equality is the right of Dalits.\n(4) He intended to carry out the movement based on self-respect.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87599,1948,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the work done by Mahatma Gandhi for eradication of untouchability.','','','1) Mahatma Gandhi took up the issue of eradication of untouchability in his hands and put it up on the platform of Indian National Congress.\n(2) When in Yerwada jail, he debated with rigid Hindu Pandits stating that the religious texts of the Hindus do not support untouchability.\n(3) He gave inspiration to Harijan Sevak Sangh.\n(4) Many took inspiration from him and dedicated themselves to the work for eradication of untouchability.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87600,1948,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give a brief account of the peasant\'s movement during the pre-independence movement.','','','1) The economic policies of the British protected the landlords and the moneylenders and adversely affected the farmers. The peasants revolted against the injustice in different parts of India.\n(2) The peasants in Bengal formed union and revolted against the compulsion to cultivate indigo.\n(3) The peasants in Maharashtra revolted in 1875 against the atrocities of the landlords and moneylenders.\n(4) In 1918, \'Kisan Sabha\' was formed by the farmers in Uttar Pradesh.\n(5) The Mopla peasants in Kerala rose in great revolt.\n(6) With the initiative of Prof N.G. Ranga \'Akhil Bharatiya Kisan Sabha\' was established.\n(7) It presented a declaration of peasants rights to the Indian National Congress.\n(8) Thousands of peasants attended the session of Indian National Congress which was held in rural part of Faizpur.\n(9) In 1938, due to heavy rains crops were destroyed in eastern Khandesh. Sane Guruji organised the farmers who were in a miserable state and took out procession.\n(10) The peasants participated in large numbers in the revolutionary period of 1942.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87601,1948,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write information about the workers\' movement in the pre-independence movement.','','','1) The beginning of the 19th century witnessed the emergence of working class due to industrialisation. They made efforts to solve their problems.\n(2) Shashipad Banerjee and Narayan Meghaji Lokhande organised workers at local level and made efforts to solve their problems.\n(3) Narayan Meghaji Lokhande described as \'Father of Indian Workers Movement\' formed \'Bombay Mill Hands Association\' in 1890. This was the beginning of organised movement in India.\n(4) The workers on the tea plantations in Assam launched an agitation against their wretched conditions.\n(5) In 1899, the workers of the Great Indian Peninsular Railway (GIP) called for α strike for their demands.\n(6) During the anti-partition movement, workers carried out strikes from time to time in support of Swadeshi.\n(7) In 1920, \'All India Trade Union Congress\' (AITUC) was established.\n(8) In 1928, the Mumbai Mill workers went on strike for six months.\n(9) Many such strikes were carried out by the railway workers, jute mill workers, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87602,1948,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the development in the Dalit movement.','','','1) The Dalit movement was launched to remove the injustice done to Dalits due to Indian social structure.\n(2) Mahatma Phule and Narayan Guru brought about awakening among the people regarding social inequality.\n(3) Gopal Baba Walangkar condemned untouchability through his book \'Vitaal Vidhwansan\'.\n(4) Periyar Ramaswamy started α movement for the eradication of untouchability in Tamil Nadu.\n(5) Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde founded the \'Depressed Classes Mission\' for the progress of the Dalits.\n(6) Rajarshi Shahu Maharaj worked to remove restrictions on inter dining, inter marriage and change of occupation.\n(7) Mahatma Gandhi presented the issue of untouchability before the Indian National Congress.\n(8) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar dedicated his entire life for the Dalit movement and gave it broader perspective.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87603,1948,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What work was done for development of workers and Dalits by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar?','','','The following work was done by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar for the development of workers and Dalits :\n(1) He founded the \'Bahishkrut Hitkarni Sabha\' to establish social equality.\n(2) He led the Chavdar Lake Satyagraha in Mahad to make the water reservoir accessible to Dalits.\n(3) He burnt \'Manusmriti\' that advocated social inequality.\n(4) In 1930, he started Satyagraha for the entry of Dalits in the Kalaram temple at Nashik.\n(5) He started periodicals such as \'Muknayak\', \'Bahishkrut Bharat\', Janata\', \'Samata\' etc. to voice the grievances of the Dalits.\n(6) He established \'All India Scheduled Caste Federation\' to put forth issues of the Dalits in effective manner.\n(7) He established \'Independent Labour Party\' to oppose the laws that went against the interest of the workers.\n(8) Through the Constitution of India he made a significant contribution ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87604,1948,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the given incident depicted in the picture and write information on it','','','1) This picture is of the Satyagraha carried out by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar and his followers at Chavdar Lake in Mahad.\n(2) Though the bill regarding opening of public water reservoirs to the Dalits was passed they were denied access.\n(3) He believed that social equality was the right of Dalits.\n(4) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar and his followers went to Chavdar lake and started\na Satyagraha to make the lake water accessible to Dalits.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87605,1948,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do you feel the structure of the society should be based on equality?','','','1) I believe that it is the right of every human being to have society based on equality. \n2) Due to inequality, rights of some groups are denied. They do not get opportunities for their development. \n3) They face injustice in several forms. They do not get self-respect. \n4) Farmers, workers, women and Dalits suffer due to inequality. So, it is important to have structure of the society based on equality.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87606,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The peasants of Bengal formed their union and revolted against the compulsion of cultivating ------.\n(A) Indigo (B) Tea (C) Sugarcane (D) Coffee\n','','','A) Indigo','',0,0,0,NULL),(87607,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Mopla peasants rose in a great revolt in ------.\n(A) Kerala (B) Maharashtra (C) Bengal (D) Gujarat\n','','','A) Kerala','',0,0,0,NULL),(87608,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ is known as ‘Father of Indian Workers Movement’.\n(A) Sashipada Banerjee (B) Narayan Meghaji Lokhande (C) Mahatma Gandhi (D) Shripad Amrut Dange\n','','','B) Narayan Meghaji Lokhande','',0,0,0,NULL),(87609,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','From ------, the workers started getting weekly holiday on Sunday.\n(A) 10th June 1890 (B) 11th June 1890 (C) 12th June 1890 (D) 13th June 1890\n','','','A) 10th June 1890','',0,0,0,NULL),(87610,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Justice Party did valuable work for social equality in ------.\n(A) North India (B) West India (C) East India (D) South India\n','','','D) South India','',0,0,0,NULL),(87611,1948,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In 1930, ------ started a Satyagraha for the entry of Dalits in the Kalaram temple at Nashik.\n(A) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar (B) Karmaveer Dadasaheb Gaikwad (C) Appasaheb Patwardhan (D) Shahu Maharaj\n','','','A) Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87612,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the Incorrect Pair and rewrite the Corrected Ones.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Column \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Column \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Neel Darpan</td>\n    <td>Deenbhandu Mitra</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Mopala peasants</td>\n    <td>Kerala</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Socialist Ideology</td>\n    <td>Shripad Amrut Dange</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. Communism</td>\n    <td>Thakkar Bappa</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','The incorrect pair is: Communism - Thakkar Bappa\nThe appropriate pair should be: Communism - Manvendranath Roy\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87613,1948,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','i. Pandita Ramabai\nii. Seva Sadan\niii. Rakhmabai Janardhan Save\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87614,1948,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare / Complete the timeline : Events related to the work done by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar.','','','i. \'Bahishkrut Hitkarini Sabha\' was established by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar.\nii. Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar started a Satyagraha for the entry of Dalits in the Kalaram temple at Nashik.\niii. \'All India Scheduled Caste Federation\' was established by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar.\niv. Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar along with his large number of followers embraced Buddhism which advocated equality and humanity.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87615,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The place where Sane Guruji tried to create a strong centre of workers union.','','','Dhule-Ammalner','',0,0,0,NULL),(87616,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Name two persons who organised the workers at the local level.','','','Sashipada, Banerjee and Narayan Meghaji Lokhande.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87617,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Founder of Satyashodhak Samaj','','','Mahatma Jyotirao Phule.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87618,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The chairman of the Mumbai branch of Satyashodhak Samaj.','','','Narayan Meghaji Lokhande','',0,0,0,NULL),(87619,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The person who wrote an article on Karl Marx in 1881.','','','Lokmanya Tilak','',0,0,0,NULL),(87620,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The person who played active role in the International Communist Movement.','','','Manavendranath Roy','',0,0,0,NULL),(87621,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : First Indian Female Doctor.','','','Dr. Anandibai Joshi','',0,0,0,NULL),(87622,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The people who followed Mahatma Phule\'s teachings and worked for the eradication of untouchability.','','','Gopal Baba Walangkar and Shivram Janba Kamble.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87623,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The book written by Gopal-Baba Walangkar in 1888 which condemned untouchability.','','','Vithal Vidhwansan','',0,0,0,NULL),(87624,1948,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The person who started a movement for eradication of untouchability in Tamil Nadu.','','','Perriyar Ramaswamy','',0,0,0,NULL),(87625,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which problem was brought to the notice of the society by Deenbandhu Mitra in his play \'Neel Dạrpan\'?','','','Deenbandhu Mitra in his play \'Neel Darpan\' brought to the notice of the society the wretched conditions of the peasants cultivating indigo.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87626,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the farmers in Maharashtra rise in revolt in 1875 ?','','','The farmers in Maharashtra rose in revolt in 1875 against the injustice done on them by the landlords and money lenders.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87627,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did Sane Guruji undertake fast unto death?','','','Sane Guruji undertook fast unto death in order to get opened the doors of Vithal temple at Pandharpur for the Dalits.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87628,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was the All India Trade Union Congress established in 1920?','','','The All India Trade Union Congress was established in 1920 because after the First World War, due to industrialisation, there was a rise of workers class in India and the need for a nationwide workers union was felt.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87629,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who brought about awakening of the people against the unjust treatment given to Dalits in the society?','','','Social reformers like Narayan Guru and Mahatma Jyotirao Phule brought about awakening of the people against the unjust treatment given to Dalits in the society.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87630,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who raised voice regarding issues of Muralis, Jogtins and marriages of Devdasis?','','','Shivram Janba Kamble raised his voice regarding issues of Muralis, Jogtins and marriages of Devdasis.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87631,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which three things were restricted in the caste system?','','','Inter-dining, inter-marriage and change of occupation were restricted in the caste system.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87632,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was \'Manusmriti\' burnt by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar?','','','\'Manusmriti\' was burnt by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar because it advocated inequality.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87633,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who led the Satyagraha for the entry of Dalits in the Kalaram temple at Nashik?','','','Karmaveer Dadasaheb Gaikwad led the Satyagraha for the entry of Dalits in the Kalaram temple at Nashik.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87634,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was the \'Independent Labour Party\' established?','','','The \'Independent Labour Party\' was established by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar to oppose the laws that went against the interest of the workers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87635,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was \'All India Scheduled Caste Federation\' established?','','','\'All India Scheduled Caste Federation\' was established to effectively put forward the problems of the Dalits.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87636,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What important contribution was made by Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar through the Constitution of India?','','','Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar created a social structure based on equality in Modern India through the Constitution of India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87637,1948,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the work done by Mahatma Gandhi for establishing social equality in India?','','','i. The issue of eradication of untouchability was taken up by Mahatma Gandhi and was addressed on the platform of the Indian National Congress.\nii. When he was in the Yerwada jail, he debated with the rigid Hindu Pandits and stated that the Hindu religious texts did not support untouchability.\niii. He inspired the Harijan Sevak Sangh.\niv. He also inspired activists like Amrutlal Vitthaldas Thakkar alias Thakkar Bappa, Appasaheb Patwardhan, etc. who Keenly worked towards equality of the common people.\nThus, Mahatma Gandhi did valuable work for establishing social equality in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87638,1948,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Akhil Bhartiya Kisan Sabha','','','i. Prof. N.G. Ranga established the Akhil Bhartiya Kisan Sabha in 1936.\nii. The President of this organisation was Swami Sahajanand Saraswati.\niii. A declaration of peasants’ rights was presented to the Indian National Congress by this organisation.\niv. Thousands of peasants attended the Indian National Congress session which was held in the rural part of Faizpur in Maharashtra.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87639,1948,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Congress Socialist Party (1934)','','','i. Many of the young activists in the Indian National Congress realised that it was important to restructure the society on the principle of economic and social equality.\nii. They also felt that in order to protect the interests of the people, the British rule should be overthrown from India.\niii. This realisation led to the rise and growth of socialist ideology. The socialist youths decided to form the socialist party within the Indian National Congress while they were in prison at Nasik.\niv. Finally in 1934, they formed the Congress Socialist Party.\nv. Acharya Narendra Dev, Jayprakash Narayan, Minu Masani, Dr. Ram Manohar Lohia, etc. were the leaders of this party and they were in the forefront during the Quit India Movement of 1942.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87640,1948,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar started a Satyagraha at Chavdar Lake in Mahad.','','','Babasaheb Ambedkar started a Satyagraha at Chavdar Lake in Mahad because-\ni. Babasaheb Bole got a Bill passed in the Bombay Provincial Assembly which granted the untouchables the use of public water reservoirs.\nii. However, the Dalits were not allowed to use of these water reservoirs.\nTherefore, Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar started a Satyagraha at Chavdar Lake in Mahad.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87641,1948,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar established the ‘Bahishkrut Hitkarni Sabha’ in July, 1924.','','','i. Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar was the leader of the Dalits. He visualised a society based on the principles of \'Liberty, Equality and Fraternity.\'\nii. He wanted to uproot the caste system in order to end the injustice and inequality shown to the Dalits.\niii. He was of the opinion that social equality was the right of the Dalit and wanted to carry out a movement based on self respect.\nTherefore, Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar established the \'Bahishkrut Hitkarni Sabha\' in July, 1924.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87642,1948,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the Act of 1935 beneficial for women?','','','The Act of 1935 was beneficial for women since it allowed women to be included in Provincial Ministries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87643,1948,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What inspirational message did Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar give to his followers?','','','The inspirational message that Dr. Babasaheb Ambedkar gave to his followers was \'Be Educated, Be Organised and Be Agitated\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87644,1948,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','The native place of Narayan Meghaji Lokhande was Kanhesar near Saswad in Pune district. He formed the mill workers union known as ‘Bombay Mill Hands Association’ in 1890. This workers union is believed to be the beginning of organised movement in India. He was also the chairman of Mumbai branch of Satyashodhak Samaj founded by Mahatma Phule. Due to his efforts, from 10 June 1890, the workers started getting weekly holiday on Sunday.','','1) What was the native place of Narayan Meghaji Lokhande?\n2) What was the name of the mill workers’ union founded by Narayan Meghaji Lokhande?\n3) In which year was the Bombay Mill Hands Association founded?\n4) From which date did the workers start getting a weekly holiday on Sunday due to Narayan Meghaji Lokhande’s efforts?\n','','1) Kanhesar near Saswad in Pune district.\n2) Bombay Mill Hands Association.\n3) In 1890.\n4) From 10 June 1890.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87645,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','------ was the head of the Interim Government.\n(A) Vallabhbhai Patel (B) Mahatma Gandhi (C) Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru (D) Barrister Jinnah\n','','','C) Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru','',0,0,0,NULL),(87646,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The plan of creation of two independent nations, India and Pakistan, was made by ------.\n(A) Lord Wavell (B) Stafford Cripps (C) Lord Mountbatten (D) Pethick Lawrence\n','','','C) Lord Mountbatten','',0,0,0,NULL),(87647,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ put forth the Two-Nation Theory and demanded a separate Muslim nation.\n(A) Barrister Muhammad Ali Jinnah (B) Chaudhary Rahmat Ali (C) Dr. Muhammad Iqbal (D) Lord Mountbatten\n','','','A) Barrister Muhammad Ali Jinnah','',0,0,0,NULL),(87648,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------, Prime Minister of England, declared that England would leave its dominion on India before June 1948.\n(A) Winston Churchill (B) Linlithgow (C) Atlee (D) Mountbatten\n','','','C) Atlee','',0,0,0,NULL),(87649,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ------ initially refused to participate in the Interim Government.\n(A) Indian National Congress (B) Muslim League (C) Hindu Mahasabha (D) Kisan Sabha\n','','','B) Muslim League','',0,0,0,NULL),(87650,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Muslim League decided to observe 16th August as ------ Day.\n(A) Non-Violence (B) Pakistan (C) Direct Action (D) Independence\n','','','C) Direct Action','',0,0,0,NULL),(87651,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','As per ------, India got independence.\n(A) Mountbatten Plan (B) Wavell Plan (C) Indian Independence Act (D) Cabinet Mission\n','','','C) Indian Independence Act','',0,0,0,NULL),(87652,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Gandhiji was assassinated on ------.\n(A) 30 December 1947 (B) 30 January 1948 (C) 30 March 1948 (D) 2 October 1948\n','','','B) 30 January 1948','',0,0,0,NULL),(87653,1949,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : First person to present idea of free Muslim nation.','','','Dr. Muhammad Iqbal','',0,0,0,NULL),(87654,1949,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Last Viceroy of India.','','','Lord Mountbatten','',0,0,0,NULL),(87655,1949,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : One who presented idea of Pakistan.','','','Chaudhary Rahmat Ali','',0,0,0,NULL),(87656,1949,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Went to Noakhali to stop violence','','','Mahatma Gandhi.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87657,1949,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the events on the following timeline :','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87658,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Which demand was advocated by Barrister Jinnah?','','','The two nation theory and demand of separate Muslim nation named Pakistan was advocated by Barrister Jinnah.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87659,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Write the names of the ministers participating in Cabinet Mission.','','','Pethick Lawrence, Stafford Cripps and A.V. Alexander were the ministers participating in the Cabinet Mission.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87660,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What did Prime Minister Atlee declare in the British Parliament of England?','','','Prime Minister Atlee declared in the British Parliament that England would transfer all of its government responsibility to Indians not later than June 1948.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87661,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the Interim Government not work smoothly?','','','The Interim Government could not work smoothly because the Muslim League followed the policy of obstruction.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87662,1949,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which were the important provisions of the Wavell Plan?','','','1) The Wavell Plan provided a proper representation to Muslims, Dalits and minorities in the Central and Provincial Legislatures.\n(2) It provided for an equal number of Hindu and Muslim members in Viceroy\'s Executive Council.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87663,1949,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the provisions of the Indian Independence Äct.','','','1) The Indian Independence Act was passed on the basis of Mountbatten Plan on 18th  July, 1947.\nAccording to the plan :\n(1) Two Independent nations of India and Pakistan will come into existence on 15th  August, 1947.\n(2) Thereafter, the British Parliament would not retain any control over them.\n(3) The British supremacy over the princely states would come to an end.\n(4) They would be free to join either India or Pakistan or remain independent.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87664,1949,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the attainment of independence by India.','','','1) As per the Indian Independence Act, India was granted Independence on 15th  August, 1947.\n(2) A meeting of the Constituent Assembly was going on in the hall of Parliament House in Delhi in the midnight of 14th August.\n(3) At the stroke of midnight, the Union Jack of Britain was lowered and in its place the Indian tricolour flag was unfurled.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87665,1949,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Indian National Congress approved the partition.','','','1) Lord Mountbatten prepared α plan of creation of India and Pakistan as two independent nations.\n(2) Indian National Congress opposed the plan of partition as unity of the Nation was its basic stand.\n(3) However, the Muslim League remained adamant on the creation of Pakistan.\n(4) So, the Indian National Congress was left with no option but to accept the partition with complete helplessness.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87666,1949,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The working of Interim Government could not run smoothly.','','','1) Muslim League demanded separate Muslim nation of Pakistan.\n(2) The followers of the Muslim League resorted to violent ways.\n(3) Though the Muslim League declined to participate initially, they later participated in it.\n(4) However, the leaders of the Muslim League adopted the policy of obstruction and therefore the Interim Government could not run smoothly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87667,1949,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Wavell Plan could not succeed.','','','The Wavell Plan could not succeed because\n(1) Barrister Jinnah insisted that only Muslim League should have the right to suggest the names of Muslim representatives to the Viceroy\'s Executive Council.\n(2) The Congress opposed this and as a result, no consensus could be arrived at the meeting called by Lord Wavell.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87668,1949,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Cabinet Mission Plan could not satisfy the political parties in India.','','','The Cabinet Mission Plan could not satisfy the political parties in India because,\n(1) The Indian National Congress was not happy with some of its provisions.\n(2) The Muslim League was also dissatisfied with the plan as it did not provide for the creation of a separate Muslim state of Pakistan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87669,1949,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The joy of attainment of freedom was not untinted.','','','1) India attained Independence from the slavery of 150 years.\n(2) But, the people were grieved due to the partition of the country and the terrible violence during that period.\nSo, the joy of attainment of freedom was not untinted.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87670,1949,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Text Book','','Why did the British took steps towards granting freedom to India?','','','1) The Indian freedom struggle had become very intense during the period of Second World War. The revolt of Navy and Air Force shook the foundation of the British empire.\n(2) The demand for independence of India was gaining its strength.\n(3) The Muslim League had demanded a separate Muslim nation.\n(4) The followers of the Muslim League resorted to violence.\n(5) The British government realised that it was necessary to take a serious note of it. They realised that their rule in India will not last long.\nAccordingly, the British government started preparing plans for granting Independence to India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87671,1949,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Text Book','','Write information about the Mountbatten Plan.','','','1) England\'s Prime Minister Atlee announced the transfer of power would be before June 1948.\n(2) He appointed Lord Mountbatten as the Viceroy of India who was to arrange the transfer of power.\n(3) He held discussions with the leaders of India.\n(4) Mountbatten prepared a plan for partition of India i.e., creation of two independent nations, India and Pakistan.\n(5) The Indian National Congress opposed the plan.\n(6) But, due to the adamant behaviour of the Muslim League, the Indian National Congress accepted the Plan, with complete helplessness.\n(7) On the basis of Mountbatten Plan, Indian Independence Act was passed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87672,1949,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Text Book','','Why did Muslim League declare to observe 16 August as Direct Action Day? What were its effects?','','','1) The Muslim League was adamant on the demand of Pakistan.\n(2) It was not satisfied with Cabinet Mission plan as it had no provision for a separate Muslim state.\n(3) 16th August 1946 was observed as Direct Action Day.\n(4) The followers of Muslim League resorted to violent ways.\n(5) There were Hindu-Muslim riots in various parts of the country.\n(6) There were massacres in the Noakhali region in the province of Bengal.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87673,1949,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the genesis of Pakistan.','','','1) To weaken the national movement, the British adopted the policy of \'divide and rule\'. Its consequence was the establishment of \'Muslim League\'.\n(2) Famous poet, Dr. Muhammad Iqbal put forth the idea of an independent Muslim state.\n(3) Later Chaudhary Rahmat Ali coined the name Pakistan.\n(4) Barrister Muhammad Ali Jinnah put forth the two nation theory and demanded α separate state for the Muslims naming Pakistan.\n(5) Barrister Jinnah and the Muslim League started the propaganda that Indian National Congress was only a Hindu organisation and the Muslims have no benefit of it.\n(6) Since their demand was not fulfilled, they observed \'Direct Action Day\' and resorted to violence.\n(7) The nation witnessed Hindu-Muslim riots in various parts.\n(8) After the consent of the Indian National Congress to the Mountbatten Plan, the Indian Independence Act provided for the partition.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87674,1949,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do you think everyone needs freedom?','','','1) Freedom is liberation from any kind of bondage.\n(2) The rights of people are denied under foreign rule.\n(3) Lot of restrictions are imposed by foreign power.\n(4) Under a foreign rule or dictatorship, power is not used for the welfare of the people but for the benefit of few.\n(5) All round development of the citizens take place in an independent free state.\n(6) Equality, Fraternity, Justice, Humanity are values which are cherished in freedom. Therefore, everyone needs freedom.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87675,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After the end of ------, the British rulers were in favour of granting independence to India.\n(A) Anglo-Sikh War  (B) Second World War  (C) First World War  (D) Carnatic War\n','','','B) Second World War','',0,0,0,NULL),(87676,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Indian National Congress was founded on the principle of ------.\n(A) Secularism  (B) Communalism  (C) Liberty  (D) Casteism\n','','','A) Secularism','',0,0,0,NULL),(87677,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The British policy of ‘divide and rule’ led to the establishment of ------.\n(A) Muslim League  (B) Indian National Congress  (C) Congress Socialist Party  (D) All India Trade Union Congress\n','','','A) Muslim League','',0,0,0,NULL),(87678,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','British Prime Minister ------ clarified the British policy regarding India.\n(A) A.V. Alexander  (B) Attlee  (C) Pethick Lawrence  (D) Winston Churchill\n','','','B) Attlee','',0,0,0,NULL),(87679,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','‘Direct Action Day’ was observed by the ------.\n(A) Interim Government  (B) Indian National Congress  (C) Muslim League  (D) Cabinet Mission\n','','','C) Muslim League','',0,0,0,NULL),(87680,1949,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ established the Interim Government.\n(A) Barrister Jinnah  (B) Mahatma Gandhi  (C) Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru  (D) Viceroy Lord Wavell\n','','','D) Viceroy Lord Wavell','',0,0,0,NULL),(87681,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who put forth the thought of an independent Muslim state in 1930?','','','A well-known poet, Dr. Muhammad Iqbal put forth the thought of an independent Muslim state in 1930.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87682,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After Dr. Muhammad Iqbal, who put up the idea of Pakistan?','','','After Dr. Muhammad Iqbal, Chaudhary Rahmat Ali put up the idea of Pakistan.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87683,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What kind of propaganda was started by Barrister Jinnah and the Muslim League?','','','The propaganda which was started by Barrister Jinnah and the Muslim League was that the Muslims did not benefit from the Indian National Congress since it was a Hindu organisation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87684,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who was appointed as the Viceroy of India on the background of transfer of power?','','','Lord Mountbatten was appointed as the Viceroy of India on the background of transfer of power.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87685,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On what basis was the Indian Independence Act passed?','','','The Indian Independence Act was passed on 18^\"th \"  July, 1947, on the basis of Mountbatten Plan.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87686,1949,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','As per the Indian Independence Act when did India gain independence?','','','India gained independence on 15th  August, 1947 by the Indian Independence Act of 18th  July, 1947.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87687,1949,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the provisions of the Indian Independence Act.','','','On the basis of the Mountbatten Plan, the Indian Independence Act was passed on 18th  July, 1947.\nThe provisions of the Indian Independence Act were as follows:\ni. Two independent nations, viz. India and Pakistan would be born on 15th  August, 1947.\nii. Once these nations become independent, British Parliament would have no control over them.\niii. The Princely States would also be free from the control of the British.\niv. The Princely States were free to join either of the nations, viz. India or Pakistan or they could remain free.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87688,1949,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did India attain independence on the midnight of 14th  August, 1947 ?','','','i. According to the Indian Independence Act, India was to be granted independence from the British rule on 15th  August, 1947.\nii. Accordingly, on the midnight of 14th  August, 1947; a meeting of the Constituent Assembly was going on in the hall of the Parliament House in Delhi.\niii. At the stroke of midnight hour, the Union Jack of Britain was lowered and it was replaced by unfurling the Indian tricolour flag.\niv. Thus, India\'s dependence ended and India became free from the 150 years of slavery.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87689,1949,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Cabinet Mission was not fully accepted by the Indian National Congress and the Muslim League.','','','i. Following the end of the Second World War, the British rulers decided to give freedom to India. Hence, in the Parliament, the British policy regarding India was clarified by the British Prime Minister Attlee.\nii. Accordingly, the Parliament recognised the right of the Indian people to draft their own Constitution. It also declared that the issue of minorities will not become an obstacle while granting freedom to India.\niii. In March 1946, England\'s plan regarding India was explained to the Indian leaders by representative of British ministers, viz., Pethick Lawrence, Stafford Cripps and A. V. Alexander. This was known as the \'Cabinet Mission\'.\niv. Some provisions of this plan were not liked by the congress. Similarly, the Muslim League was not satisfied since the provision for the creation of a separate independent state for the Muslims was not mentioned.\nTherefore, the Cabinet Mission was not fully accepted by the Indian National Congress and the Muslim League.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87690,1949,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The joy of attainment of freedom on 15th  August, 1947 was tainted.','','','i. As per the Indian Independence Act, India gained independence on 15th  August, 1947.\nii. However, at the same time, the dream of Indian unity was shattered. People were sad due to the partition of the country and the terrible violence which took place during that period.\niii. Moreover, instead of celebrating independence, Gandhiji worked day and night to preserve Hindu-Muslim unity. He tried to stop violence and bring peace in Bengal.\niv. On 30th  January, 1948 , within six months of the attainment of freedom of India, Gandhiji was mercilessly assassinated by Nathuram Godse.\nTherefore, the joy of attainment of freedom on 15th  August, 1947 was tainted.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87691,1949,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','Indian Independence Act : On 18 July 1947, on the basis of Mountbatten Plan, the Indian Independence Act was passed in British Parliament. On 15 August 1947, the two independent nations India and Pakistan will come into existence. Thereafter the British Parliament will not have any control over them. The British supremacy over the princely states will also come to an end. The Act provided them the right to either join India or Pakistan or remain independent.','','1) On which date was the Indian Independence Act passed in the British Parliament?\n2) On which date did India and Pakistan come into existence as independent nations?\n3) The Indian Independence Act was based on which plan?\n4) What right was given to the princely states under the Indian Independence Act?','','1) On 18 July 1947.\n2) On 15 August 1947.\n3) The Mountbatten Plan.\n4)The right to join India or Pakistan or remain independent.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87692,1949,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Wavell Plan (1945)','','','1. Lord Wavell, the Viceroy of India, prepared a plan in June 1945.\n2. It aimed to provide equal representation to Hindus and Muslims in the Viceroy’s Executive Council.\n3. It also proposed fair representation for Dalits and minorities in central and provincial legislatures.\n4. The Simla Conference was held to discuss it, but it failed due to differences between Congress and the Muslim League.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87693,1949,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Cabinet Mission (1946)','','','1. The British Prime Minister Attlee sent a Cabinet Mission to India in March 1946.\n2. It consisted of three British ministers — Pethick Lawrence, Stafford Cripps, and A.V. Alexander.\n3. The Mission recognized India’s right to frame its own Constitution.\n4. The plan failed as Congress and the Muslim League did not fully agree with its proposals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87694,1949,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Direct Action Day (1946)','','','1. The Muslim League declared 16 August 1946 as ‘Direct Action Day’.\n2. It was called to demand the creation of a separate Muslim nation, Pakistan.\n3. The day led to violent Hindu-Muslim riots in many parts of India, especially in Noakhali (Bengal).\n4. Mahatma Gandhi visited Noakhali to restore peace and harmony.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87695,1949,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mountbatten Plan (1947)','','','1. Lord Mountbatten was appointed the Viceroy of India to oversee the transfer of power.\n2. He proposed a plan for partition of India into two nations — India and Pakistan.\n3. The Congress opposed partition but accepted it helplessly to avoid civil war.\n4. This plan became the basis for the Indian Independence Act of 1947.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87696,1949,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Indian Independence Act (1947)','','','1. The Act was passed by the British Parliament on 18 July 1947.\n2. It stated that India and Pakistan would become independent on 15 August 1947.\n3. The British Parliament would have no control over these nations after independence.\n4. The princely states were given the right to join India or Pakistan or remain independent.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87697,1949,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the pairs : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) Indian Independence Act</td>\n    <td>(a) 15 August 1947</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Mountbatten Plan</td>\n    <td>(b) 18 July 1947</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) India and Pakistan</td>\n    <td>(c) Passed the Independence Act</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) British Parliament</td>\n    <td>(d) Came into existence as independent nations</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','  (1 – b), (2 – a), (3 – d), (4 – c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(87698,1949,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the pairs : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) British supremacy</td>\n    <td>(a) Could join India or Pakistan or remain independent</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Princely states</td>\n    <td>(b) Ended after 15 August 1947</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Mountbatten</td>\n    <td>(c) Came to an end over princely states</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) British Parliament control</td>\n    <td>(d) Presented the partition plan</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(1) British supremacy</td>\n    <td>(b) Ended after 15 August 1947</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(2) Princely states</td>\n    <td>(a) Could join India or Pakistan or remain independent</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(3) Mountbatten</td>\n    <td>(d) Presented the partition plan</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>(4) British Parliament control</td>\n    <td>(c) Came to an end over princely states</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(87699,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','There were more than six hundred small and big ------ in India.\n(A) states  (B) villages  (C) princely states  (D) cities\n','','','C) princely states','',0,0,0,NULL),(87700,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','All the princely states merged with India except the states of Junagadh, ------ and Kashmir.\n(A) Aundh  (B) Jhansi  (C) Vadodara  (D) Hyderabad\n','','','D) Hyderabad','',0,0,0,NULL),(87701,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Due to the influence of ------, there was beginning of political awakening among the princely states.\n(A) Champaranya Satyagraha  (B) Civil Disobedience Movement  (C) Non-Co-operation Movement  (D) Quit India Movement\n','','','C) Non-Co-operation Movement','',0,0,0,NULL),(87702,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Junagadh was a princely state in ------.\n(A) Kutch  (B) Saurashtra  (C) South Gujarat  (D) East Gujarat\n','','','B) Saurashtra','',0,0,0,NULL),(87703,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Through ‘Vande Mataram’ movement ------ participated in the struggle for liberation of Hyderabad.\n(A) farmers  (B) workers  (C) students  (D) Nizam’s army\n','','','C) students','',0,0,0,NULL),(87704,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ is celebrated as ‘Marathwada Mukti Din’.\n(A) 13 September  (B) 19 December  (C) 2 August  (D) 17 September\n','','','D) 17 September','',0,0,0,NULL),(87705,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There was significant contribution of ------ Samaj in the Hyderabad Mukti Sangram.\n(A) Arya  (B) Satyashodhak  (C) Brahmo  (D) Prarthana\n','','','A) Arya','',0,0,0,NULL),(87706,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The youth of Azad Gomantak Dal made an armed attack and liberated the territories of ------ from Portuguese rule.\n(A) Diu and Daman  (B) Goa  (C) Dadra and Nagar Haveli  (D) Chandranagar and Mahe\n','','','C) Dadra and Nagar Haveli','',0,0,0,NULL),(87707,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','',' In 1949, France held a plebiscite in ------.\n(A) Mahe  (B) Karaikal  (C) Puducheri  (D) Chandranagar\n','','','D) Chandranagar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87708,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ was an aggressive leader in the struggle for liberation of Goa.\n(A) Swami Ramananda Tirth  (B) Govindbhai Shroff  (C) Mohan Ranade  (D) Babasaheb Paranjpe\n','','','C) Mohan Ranade','',0,0,0,NULL),(87709,1950,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Founder of \'Razakar\' in Princely state of Hyderabad.','','','Kasim Rizvi','',0,0,0,NULL),(87710,1950,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Founder of Hyderabad State Congress','','','Swami Ramananda Tirth ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87711,1950,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Founder of Goa Congress Committee.','','','Dr. T.B. Cunha ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87713,1950,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Took a firm stand in solving the issue of Princely States.','','','Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87714,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which European powers had control over some parts of India after Independence?','','','The Portuguese and French powers had control over some parts of India after Independence.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87715,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are Praja Mandals?','','','People formed organisations like Prajamandals which worked for people\'s interests in the princely states and for securing political rights for them\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87716,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which languages were spoken in the princely state of Hyderabad?','','','Telugu, Kannada and Marathi were the languages spoken in the princely state of Hyderabad.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87717,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is \'Operation Polo\'?','','','Indian government started the police action against the Nizam to merge the princely state of Hyderabad with India. It was symbolically called \'Operation Polo\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87718,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which organisations were formed by people to secure civil and political rights in princely state of Hyderabad?','','','To secure civil and political rights in Hyderabad princely state, people formed organisations such as Andhra Parishad in Telangana region, Maharashtra Parishad in the Marathwada region and Karnataka Parishad in the Karnataka region.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87719,1950,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87720,1950,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87721,1950,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Show the events during merger of Hyderabad princely states on timeline :','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87722,1950,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain the contribution of Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel in the integration of the Princely States in India.','','','1) The problem of the merger of the princely states was very tactfully handled by Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, the then Home Minister of India.\n(2) He convinced the Princes that the merger of their states with India was in their interest.\n(3) He prepared the \'Instrument of Accession\', acceptable to all.\n(4) The Princes accepted the proposal and all the states except Junagad, Kashmir and Hyderabad were merged with India.\n(5) These three states, too, were merged with India subsequently.\n(6) Thus, he took a firm stand and solved the issue of merger of princely states.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87723,1950,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain the contribution of Swami Ramananda Tirth in the struggle for liberation of Hyderabad.','','','1) Hyderabad was the largest princely state in India.\n(2) The autocratic ruler, Nizam had denied civil and political rights to the people.\n3) In 1938, Swami Ramananda Tirth formed Hyderabad State Congress.\n(4) As Nizam banned his organisation, a struggle began for getting recognition to Hyderabad State Congress and democratic rights.\n5) In July, 1947 a resolution was passed by the Hyderabad State Congress for its merger with India.\n6) Swami Ramananda Tirth was assisted by Narayan Reddy and Siraj-ulHasan Tirmiji in his efforts.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87724,1950,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about contribution of Marathwada in the struggle for liberation of Hyderabad.','','','The contribution of Marathwada in the liberation of Hyderabad is as follows :\n(1) To secure civil and political rights of the people in the region, Maharashtra Parishad was formed.\n(2) Many eminent leaders like Ved Prakash, Govind Pansare, etc. from Marathwada led the Hyderabad liberation struggle.\n(3) Through \'Vande Mataram\' Movement many students participated in the movement.\n(4) Many sacrificed their lives in this historical struggle.\n(5) The people and leaders of Marathwada had a lion\'s share in Hyderabad liberation struggle.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87725,1950,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was the struggle for independence not over in spite of India gaining independence?','','','1) When India got independence, there were more than six hundred princely states.\n(2) The French and Portuguese powers too controlled some parts of India.\n(3) It was essential to merge these regions with India.\nTherefore, the struggle for independence continued inspite of India gaining independence.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87726,1950,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the contribution of Dr. T. B. Kunha for the liberation of Goa.','','','1) Dr. T. B. Kunha was in the forefront of the struggle to liberate Goa from the Portuguese rule.\n(2) He worked to bring about awakening among the people against the Portuguese Government.\n(3) With an objective of carrying out the struggle, he founded the Goa Congress Committee.\n(4) He founded the Goa Youth League in Mumbai in 1945.\n(5) He entered Goa in 1946 and was sentenced to eight years imprisonment for the violation of the ban on public speeches.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87727,1950,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Junagad merged with India.','','','(1) Junagad was a princely state in Saurashtra.\n(2) The Nawab of Junagad wanted to merge the state with Pakistan.\n(3) It was opposed by the people vigorously as they wanted the merger with India.\n(4) Due to this, the Nawab fled to Pakistan and Junagad was merged with India in February, 1948.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87728,1950,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Indian government started police action against the Nizam.','','','1) Hyderabad State Congress passed a resolution for merger of Hyderabad state with India in July 1947.\n(2) The Nizam took an anti-India stand and tried for merger with Pakistan.\n(3) The Razakar organisation of Kasim Rizvi committed atrocities on Hindus as well as Muslims who supported the democratic movement.\n(4) The Nizam did not respond to the appeal of the Government of India for peaceful negotiations.\n(5) At last, the Government of India started police action on 13 September, 1948 and the state of Hyderabad was merged with India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87729,1950,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Hari Singh signed the Instrument of Accession with India.','','','1) Hari Singh the ruler of Kashmir, had decided to retain the independence of Kashmir.\n(2) Pakistan was keen to secure the merger of Kashmir with itself.\n(3) So, Pakistan began to put pressure on Hari Singh for this purpose.\n(4) At the instigation of Pakistan, the armed intruders attacked Kashmir in October, 1947.\nTherefore, Hari Singh signed the \'Instrument of Accession\' with India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87730,1950,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Merger of the Princely States was essential.','','','1) When India got independence there were more than six hundred princely states in India.\n(2) If the princely states had remained independent, India would have been split into hundreds of territorial fragments.\n(3) The dream of Indian National Congress of undivided India would have remained incomplete.\n(4) Moreover, the organisations in the princely states had started movements to secure rights.\n(5) It was necessary to free the people from the autocratic rule in the princely states.\n(6) Some princely states started carrying out movements for merging with Pakistan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87731,1950,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','All India States People\'s Conference was formed in 1927.','','','1) There was political awakening among the people of princely states due to influence of non-co-operation movement.\n(2) Praja Mandals were formed to secure the political rights for the people in the Princely states and to work for their interest.\n(3) All the Praja Mandals came together and formed an \"All India States People\'s Conference, in 1927 which gave an impetus to the freedom movement.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87732,1950,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Many organisations were formed in the Hyderabad Princely State.','','','1) There were Telugu, Kannada and Marathi speaking regions in princely state of Hyderabad.\n(2) There was absence of civil and political rights under the autocratic rule of the Nizam.\n(3) To secure these rights organsiatons were formed in the princely states of Hyderabad.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87733,1950,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about Goa Liberation Movement.','','','1) Portuguese refused to hand over territories under its possession to India.\n(2) Dr. T. B. Kunha was in the forefront to lead the struggle.\n(3) He formed the organisation like \'Goa Congress Committee\' and \'Goa Youth League\'.\n(4) He worked to bring about an awakening of the people against the Portuguese government.\n(5) He was arrested and imprisoned in 1946 for violating a ban on public speeches.\n(6) Dr. Ram Manohar Lohia started a Satyagraha for liberation of Goa.\n(7) He was deported by the Portuguese as he violated a ban and delivered a public speech at Madgaon in Goa in 1946.\n(8) The youths of the Azad Gomantak Dal liberated Dadra and Nagar Haveli from the Portuguese rule on 2 August, 1954.\n(9) The Portuguese rulers committed unlimited tyranny and atrocities on the Satyagrahis sent by Goa Mukti Samiti from Maharashtra in 1954.\n(10) As the negotiations between the Government and the Portuguese did not succeed, the Indian government unwillingly took the decision of using military force.\n(11) On 19 December, 1961, Goa was finally liberated from the dominion of the Portuguese.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87734,1950,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How was the integration of the French occupied territories brought about?','','','1) Even after the independence of India, the regions of Chandranagar, Puduchcheri, Karaikal, Mahe and Yaman were in the possession of the French.\n(2) People were keen to merge with India.\n(3) The Indian Government demanded that these territories should be handed over to it.\n(4) In 1949, the French-occupied territory of Chandranagar was transferred to India in view of the result of the plebiscite undertaken there.\n(5) Thereafter, the remaining territories in the French possession were also handed over to India without any problem.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87735,1950,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What would have happened if the Princely States would not have merged with India?','','','Had the Princely States remained independent, the following could have been the result :\n(i) The people in the princely states would never have got freedom and they would have faced injustice.\n(ii) The feeling of unity would have never developed among Indians due to the independent princely states.\n(iii) They would have created trouble in the development of India.\n(iv) Even the boundaries of India would not have remained safe.\n(v) Injustice on the population, encroachment by the princely states and many other issues would have risen.\n(vi) Thus, It would have threatened the peace and security of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87736,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The influence of the ------ movement led to the beginning of political awakening among the princely states.\n(A) Civil Disobedience  (B) Non Co-operation  (C) Swadeshi  (D) Quit India\n','','','B) Non Co-operation','',0,0,0,NULL),(87737,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','After India’s independence, the then Home Minister ------ very diplomatically found out the way of merging the princely states in India.\n(A) Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru  (B) Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel  (C) Dr. T. B. Kunha  (D) Swami Ramananda Tirth\n','','','B) Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel','',0,0,0,NULL),(87738,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel prepared an ------ acceptable to all the princely states.\n(A) Instrument of Rejection  (B) Instrument of Succession  (C) Instrument of Accession  (D) Instrument of Acceptance\n','','','C) Instrument of Accession','',0,0,0,NULL),(87739,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ was the largest princely state in India.\n(A) Aundh  (B) Jhansi  (C) Vadodara  (D) Hyderabad\n','','','D) Hyderabad','',0,0,0,NULL),(87740,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------, an associate of the Nizam of Hyderabad, formed an organisation known as ‘Razakar’.\n(A) Kasim Rizvi  (B) Shoaib Ullah Khan  (C) Siraj-ul-Hasan Tirmji  (D) Hari Singh\n','','','A) Kasim Rizvi','',0,0,0,NULL),(87741,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------, the ruler of Kashmir, had decided to remain independent.\n(A) Kasim Rizvi  (B) Shoaib Ullah Khan  (C) Siraj-ul-Hasan Tirmji  (D) Hari Singh\n','','','D) Hari Singh','',0,0,0,NULL),(87742,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ was founded to liberate Portuguese colonies of Dadra and Nagar Haveli in Gujarat.\n(A) Razakar  (B) Goa Congress Committee  (C) Azad Gomantak Dal  (D) Goa Youth League\n','','','C) Azad Gomantak Dal','',0,0,0,NULL),(87743,1950,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','------ was an aggressive leader in the struggle for the liberation of Goa.\n(A) N. G. Gore  (B) Mohan Ranade  (C) Senapati Bapat  (D) Peter Alvares\n','','','B) Mohan Ranade','',0,0,0,NULL),(87744,1950,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare / Complete the timeline.','','','i. Junagad got merged into India\nii. Nizam of Hyderabad surrendered to Indian government\niii. Youths of Azad Gomantak Dal liberated the territories of Dadra and Nagar Haveli\niv. Goa was liberated from Portuguese dominion\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87745,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which foreign powers had control of some parts of India, even after independence?','','','The foreign powers which had control over some parts of India, even after independence included the Portuguese and the French.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87746,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What did the Praja Mandals come together and form in 1927?','','','In 1927, the Praja Mandals came together and formed the \'All India States People\'s Conference\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87747,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which language speaking regions were included in the princely state of Hyderabad?','','','The princely state of Hyderabad included the Telegu, Kannada and Marathi speaking regions.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87748,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which regional organisations were formed in the state of Hyderabad to secure the civil and political rights?','','','The various regional organisations formed in the state of Hyderabad to secure the civil and political rights include Andhra Parishad in the Telangana region, Maharashtra Parishad in the Marathwada region and the Karnataka Parishad in the Karnataka region.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87749,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was Operation Polo?','','','The police action started by the Indian Government against the Nizam of Hyderabad on 13th  September 1948, was symbolically known as Operation Polo.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87750,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the significance of the \'Marathwada Mukti Din\'?','','','The \'Marathwada Mukti Din\', is celebrated on 17th  September in Marathwada to commemorate the struggle for liberation of Hyderabad.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87751,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which regions were under the control of French, even after India\'s independence?','','','Even after the independence of India, the regions of Chandranagar, Puduchcheri, Karaikal, Mahe and Yanam were under the control of French.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87752,1950,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the work of Azad Gomantak Dal.','','','i. The Azad Gomantak Dal was founded to liberate the Portuguese colonies of Dadra and Nagar Haveli in Gujarat.\nii. The youths of this group carried out an armed attack on 2nd  August 1954 and liberated the territories of Dadra and Nagar Haveli from the Portuguese rule.\niii. The important people who took part in this attack included Vishwanath Lavande, Rajabhau Wakankar, Sudhir Phadke, Nanasaheb Kajrekar, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87753,1950,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Merger of French Colonies','','','i. The French were in the possession of the regions of Chandranagar, Puduchcheri, Karaikal, Mahe and Yanam even after India\'s independence.\nii. The Indian natives of these regions wanted to get merged with India.\niii. Also, the Indian government demanded that these territories being a part of India, should be handed over to it.\niv. In a plebiscite held by France in 1949 in Chandranagar, the people voted for India.\nv. Thereafter, France first handed over Chandranagar and then other territories to the Government of India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87754,1950,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Contribution of Dr. T. B. Kunha in the struggle for the liberation of Goa.','','','i. Portugal denied to hand over to India, the territories under its possession including Goa.\nii. Dr. T. B. Kunha, who was in forefront of the struggle to get these territories under India\'s control, worked to bring about the awakening of the people.\niii. He first established the Goa Congress Committee with the objective of building struggle against the Portuguese Government and later in 1945 founded Goa Youth League in Mumbai.\niv. Further in 1946, he violated the ban on public speech for which he was sentenced to eight years of imprisonment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87755,1950,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','All India States People\'s Conference was formed in 1927.','','','i. The influence of the non-co-operation movement led to a political awakening among the princely states.\nii. It further led to the formation of Praja Mandals i.e. people\'s organisations in these princely states.\niii. These Praja Mandals worked for the interest of the people of the princely states as well as for securing political rights for them.\niv. Various such Praja Mandals came together and formed the \'All India States People\'s Conference\' in 1927, which gave an impetus to the movement in the princely states.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87756,1950,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The leaders and the people of Marathwada had a lion\'s share in the struggle for liberation of Hyderabad.','','','i. Valuable contribution was made by Swami Ramananda Tirth, Govindbhai Shroff, Anant Bhalerao, Ashatai Waghmare, etc. in the struggle for the liberation of Hyderabad.\nii. Students participated in this struggle through the \'Vande Mataram\' movement.\niii. Also, Ved Prakash, Govind Pansare, Shyamlal, Shridhar Vartak, Bahirji Shinde, Janardan Mama, Shoaib Ullah Khan, etc. embraced martyrdom and inspired other Indians.\nSince all these were from the Marathwada part of Hyderabad, it is said that the leaders and the people of Marathwada had a lion\'s share in the struggle for liberation of Hyderabad.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87757,1950,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Indian Government took decision of using military force against the Portuguese in Goa.','','','i. When Portugal denied the handover of the territories under its possession to India; the Indians had to struggle for securing these territories.\nii. Dr. T. B. Kunha, was in the forefront of this struggle and worked to bring about awakening of the people.\niii. Later, the Goa Mukti Samiti formed in 1954, sent many batches of satyagrahis from Maharashtra to Goa.\niv. The Portuguese rulers committed unlimited tyranny and atrocities on the satyagrahis, which led the public opinion to become more infuriated and the struggle for the liberation of Goa took up a fierce form.\nv. Also, the Portuguese government did not respond to the peaceful negotiations held by the Indian government.\nThis forced the Indian government to make use of military force and in December 1961, the Indian Army entered Goa and made the Portuguese army surrender in a short time.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87758,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who assisted Swami Ramananda Tirth in getting recognition to the Hyderabad State Congress?','','','Narayan Reddy and Siraj-ul-Hasan assisted Swami Ramananda Tirth in getting recognition to the Hyderabad State Congress.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87759,1950,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was Dr. T. B. Kunha\'s objective behind establishing Goa Congress Committee?','','','Dr. T. B. Kunha established the Goa Congress Committee with an objective of building struggle against the Portuguese.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87760,1950,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','Hyderabad Mukti Sangram : Hyderabad was the largest princely state in India. It included Telegu, Kannada and Marathi speaking regions. There was the autocratic rule of the Nizam over these provinces. There was absence of civil and political rights. To secure these rights, the people formed organisations such as Andhra Parishad in the Telangana region, Maharashtra Parishad in the Marathwada region, Karnataka Parishad in the Karnataka region. In 1938, Swami Ramananda Tirth formed the Hyderabad State Congress. The Nizam banned this organisation. A struggle began for getting recognition to Hyderabad State Congress and democratic rights. This struggle was led by a militant fighter Swami Ramananda Tirth. He was assisted by Narayan Reddy and Siraj-ul-Hasan Tirmiji. P.V.Narsimha Rao and Govindbhai Shroff were the faithful followers of Swamiji.','','1. Which was the largest princely state in India?\n2. Who founded the Hyderabad State Congress?\n3. Name any two regions included in the Hyderabad State.\n4. Name any two followers of Swami Ramananda Tirth.\n','','1) Hyderabad was the largest princely state in India.\n2) Swami Ramananda Tirth founded the Hyderabad State Congress in 1938.\n3) Telegu and Marathi speaking regions were included in the Hyderabad State.\n4) P. V. Narsimha Rao and Govindbhai Shroff were followers of Swami Ramananda Tirth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87761,1950,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel and Integration of Princely States','','','1. After independence, more than 600 princely states existed in India.\n2. Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, the Home Minister, led the integration process.\n3. He prepared the ‘Instrument of Accession’, convincing rulers to join India.\n4. Except Junagad, Hyderabad, and Kashmir — all other states merged peacefully.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87762,1950,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Hyderabad Mukti Sangram','','','1. Hyderabad was the largest princely state ruled by the autocratic Nizam.\n2. Swami Ramananda Tirth led the movement with leaders like Narayan Reddy and Govindbhai Shroff.\n3. The Nizam supported Pakistan; his associate Kasim Rizvi formed the violent ‘Razakar’ organisation.\n4. India launched ‘Operation Polo’ (13–17 September 1948) — the Nizam surrendered and Hyderabad merged with India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87763,1950,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Contribution of Marathwada in Hyderabad Liberation','','','1. Leaders like Swami Ramananda Tirth, Govindbhai Shroff, and Anant Bhalerao played a major role.\n2. The ‘Vande Mataram’ movement involved many students in the struggle.\n3. Many martyrs like Govind Pansare, Ved Prakash, and Bahirji Shinde sacrificed their lives.\n4. 17 September is celebrated as ‘Marathwada Mukti Din’ to mark this victory.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87764,1950,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Problem of Kashmir','','','1. The ruler Hari Singh wanted to keep Kashmir independent after 1947.\n2. Pakistan-backed intruders attacked Kashmir in October 1947.\n3. Hari Singh signed the Instrument of Accession, and Indian troops defended Kashmir.\n4. India regained most of the territory, but some part remained under Pakistan’s control.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87765,1950,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Struggle for Liberation of Goa','','','1. Goa was under Portuguese rule even after India’s independence.\n2. Leaders like Dr. T. B. Kunha and Dr. Ram Manohar Lohia led protests and Satyagrahas.\n3. In 1954, the Azad Gomantak Dal liberated Dadra and Nagar Haveli.\n4. Finally, in December 1961, the Indian Army freed Goa — ending Portuguese rule completely.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87766,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The State of ____ was formed on 1 May, 1960.\n(a) Goa (b) Karnataka (c) Andhra Pradesh (d) Maharashtra\n','','','d) Maharashtra','',0,0,0,NULL),(87767,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','____ put forth the proposal of Samyukta Maharashtra with Mumbai in the Mumbai Municipal Corporation.\n(a) G. T. Madkholkar (b) Acharya Atre (c) D. V. Potdar (d) Shankarrao Dev\n','','','b) Acharya Atre','',0,0,0,NULL),(87768,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','____ accepted the responsibility as first Chief Minister of Maharashtra.\n(a) Yashwantrao Chavan (b) Pruthviraj Chavan (c) Shankarrao Chavan (d) Vilasrao Deshmukh\n','','','a) Yashwantrao Chavan','',0,0,0,NULL),(87769,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ organised rallies to protest against the report of JVP Committee.\n(a) Sane Guruji (b) P. K. Atre (c) Senapati Bapat (d) Prabodhankar Thackeray\n','','','c) Senapati Bapat','',0,0,0,NULL),(87770,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ established Dar Commission for forming linguistic province.\n(a) Morarji Desai (b) Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru (c) Dr. Rajendra Prasad (d) Yashwantrao Chavan\n','','','c) Dr. Rajendra Prasad','',0,0,0,NULL),(87771,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','According to 1953 Pact, Assembly session would be held once in a year at ____.\n(a) Pune (b) Mumbai (c) Nagpur (d) Aurangabad\n','','','c) Nagpur','',0,0,0,NULL),(87772,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Police used lathi charge and tear gas on the March led by ____.\n(a) Bhai Madhavrao Bagal (b) Comrade Shripad Amrut Dange (c) S. M. Joshi (d) Senapati Bapat\n','','','d) Senapati Bapat','',0,0,0,NULL),(87773,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Acharya Atre started the newspaper ____ which played an important role in Samyukta Maharashtra Movement.\n(a) Navyug (b) Prabodhan (c) Maratha (d) Navakal\n','','','c) Maratha','',0,0,0,NULL),(87774,1951,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Demanded reconstruction of a state based on language in 1915.','','','Lokmanya Tilak ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87775,1951,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Commission which advocated bilingual Mumbai.','','','\'Commission for Reconstruction of States\' ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87776,1951,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Samiti established at Tilak Smarak Mandir, Pune.','','','Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti','',0,0,0,NULL),(87777,1951,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The Act passed by the Parliament in April 1960.','','','Mumbai Reorganisation Act.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87778,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the appeal made by Shankarao Dev in the meeting heldon Kamgar Maidan?','','','Shankarrao Dev appealed to the people in the following words, \"We will oppose the separation of Mumbai from Maharashtra up to our last breath.\"','',0,0,0,NULL),(87779,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the suggestion given by the Commission for Reconstruction of States?','','','The Commission for Reconstruction of States suggested creation of bilingual Mumbai State.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87780,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the resolution proposed by S. M. Joshi on 7 November, 1955 at the meeting of labourers?','','','At the meeting of the labourers on 7 November, 1955 S. M. Joshi proposed a resolution that Samyukta Maharashtra should be created with Mumbai and Vidarbha.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87781,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where was the memorial of 106 martyrs erected? What is it called?','','','The memorial of 106 martyrs was erected in Mumbai near Flora Fountain. It is called \'Hutatma Smarak.\'','',0,0,0,NULL),(87782,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who played an important role in establishing Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti?','','','Acharya P. K. Atre, Madhu Dandavate, Prabodhankar Keshav Thackeray, Y. K. Souni played important role in establishing Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87783,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the lokshahirs who aroused awakening among people during Samyukta Maharashtra Movement.','','','Lokshahirs Annabhau Sathe, Shahir Amar Sheikh and Shahir D. N. Gavankar aroused public awakening among the people during Samyukta Maharashtra Movement.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87784,1951,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87785,1951,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87786,1951,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87787,1951,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the table :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Sr. No</th>\n    <th>Foundation year</th>\n    <th>Name of the Commission/Samiti</th>\n    <th>Name of the President</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1</td>\n    <td>28 July</td>\n    <td>____</td>\n    <td>Shankarrao Dev</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2</td>\n    <td>____</td>\n    <td>Dar Commission</td>\n    <td>Justice S. R. Dar</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3</td>\n    <td>29 December, 1943</td>\n    <td>____</td>\n    <td>Justice Fazal Ali</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4</td>\n    <td>6 February, 1956</td>\n    <td>Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti</td>\n    <td>____</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Sr. No</th>\n    <th>Foundation year</th>\n    <th>Name of the Commission/Samiti</th>\n    <th>Name of the President</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1</td>\n    <td>28 July</td>\n    <td>Maharashtra Ekikaran Parishad</td>\n    <td>Shankarrao Dev</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2</td>\n    <td>17 June, 1947</td>\n    <td>Dar Commission</td>\n    <td>Justice S. R. Dar</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3</td>\n    <td>29 December, 1943</td>\n    <td>Commission for Reconstruction of States</td>\n    <td>Justice Fazal Ali</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4</td>\n    <td>6 February, 1956</td>\n    <td>Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti</td>\n    <td>Comrade Shripad Amrut Dange</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(87788,1951,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Samyukta Maharashtra Parishad','','','1) On 12 May 1946, a resolution was passed regarding Samyukta Maharashtra in the Sahitya Sammelan at Belgaon.\n(2) On this background, Maharashtra Ekikaran Parishad was convened under the leadership of Shankarrao Dev at Mumbai on 28 July.\n(3) A resolution was passed that all Marathi speaking regions should be united in one state.\n(4) It should include the regions of Mumbai, Central provinces Marathwada and Gomantak.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87789,1951,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Contribution of Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti ','','','Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti contributed in the following way to form Samyukta Maharashtra.\n(l) Samyukta Maharashtra movement spread throughout the state and reached in rural areas.\n(2) Common people also joined the movement spontaneously.\n(3) When it was clear that Mumbai will not be included in Maharashtra, Samiti held demonstrations to protest and arouse public agitation.\n(4) The grand success of Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti in Lok Sabha, Vidhan Sabha and Mumbai Municipal Corporation in 1957 made it clear that the voters were in favour of Samyukta Maharashtra.\n(5) The agitations led by Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti during the visit of Prime Minister Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru made the approach of Central Government favourable in the formation of Maharashtra state.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87790,1951,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Dar Commission ','','','1) The President of Constituent Assembly, Dr. Rajendra Prasad established the Dar Commission on 17 June, 1947.\n(2) The Commission started the work under the leadership of Justice S. K. Dar to form states on linguistic basis.\n(3) On 10 December, 1948, the report of Dar Commission was published but the issue remained unsolved.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87791,1951,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Commission for Reconstruction of States ','','','1) Indian Government appointed a \'Commission for Reconstruction of States\' on 29 December, 1953.\n(2) It was formed under the Chairmanship of Justice Fazal Ali.\n(3) In the proposal presented by the commission on 10 October, 1955, a creation of bilingual Mumbai State was advocated.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87792,1951,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What started the demand for an independent state of Marathi speaking people?','','','1) The demand for an independent Marathi speaking state started before independence.\n(2) N. C. Kelkar presented the idea that the entire Marathi speaking population should be under one dominion.\n(3) In 1915, Lokmanya Tilak demanded the reconstruction of a state based on language.\n(4) An important resolution regarding Samyukta Maharashtra was passed in the Sahitya Sammelan at Belgaon. It began the movement to form Independent state of Marathi speaking people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87793,1951,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write information on the workers meeting held on 7 November, 1955.','','','1) The struggle of Marathi speaking people for formation of Maharashtra with Mumbai had started.\n(2) On 7 November, 1955 a meeting of labourers was held.\n(3) Labour Organisation or Communists, Praja Socialists, Socialists, Peasants and Workers Party, Janasangh, etc. participated in the meeting.\n(4) Comrade Shripad Amrut Dange was the President of this meeting.\n(5) S. M. Joshi proposed a resolution to create a Samyukta Maharashtra with Mumbai and Vidarbha.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87794,1951,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What were the provisions in the Nagpur Pact?','','','The following provisions were made in the Nagpur Pact :\n(1) Samyukta Maharashtra should be formed including Western Maharashtra and Vidarbha along with Marathwada.\n(2) Assurance was given regarding equitable financial provisions for development.\n(3) Ample finance will be provided for technical and vocational education.\n(4) Government services will be provided in accordance with the population in the region.\n(5) Once in a year an Assembly session will be held at Nagpur.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87795,1951,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What happened on the day a grand march was taken to Vidhan Sabha?','','','1) For the formation of Samyukta Maharashtra, a grand march was taken to the Vidhan Sabha led by Senapati Bapat.\n(2) The government declared α ban.\n(3) The police started lathi charge and used tear gas on the protestors who broke the ban.\n(4) On the same evening, Comrade Shripad Dange guided a mob of fifty thousand on Kamgar Maidan.\n(5) To give impetus to the Samyukta Maharashtra Movement, it was further decided to hold one day strike on 21 November, 1955.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87796,1951,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which events took place in the final stages of establishment of Maharashtra state?','','','The following events took place in the final stages of establishment of Maharashtra state :\n(1) Due to agitation of Samyukta Maharashtra Movement, central government gave consent to the formation of two linguistic states.\n(2) The Congress President, Indira Gandhi also supported the Samyukta Maharashtra Movement.\n(3) In April 1960, the Parliament passed the Mumbai Reorganisation Act.\n(4) According to this act, Maharashtra State was formed on 1st  May, 1960.\n(5) Pandit Nehru on the occasion of Labour Day made a formal announcement of the formation of Maharashtra State at a special ceremony at Raj Bhavan.\n(6) Yashwantrao Chavan accepted the responsibility of first Chief Minister of Maharashtra.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87797,1951,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti came to be established.','','','1) The demand for a state of Marathi speaking people of all regions was put forth in the Nagpur Pact in 1953.\n(2) There was no positive response from the Central Government for formation of Samyukta Maharashtra with Mumbai.\n(3) The agitation was made severe through strikes, demonstrations, rallies, etc. which were organised from time to time.\n(4) As the issue of the demand of Marathi speaking people started becoming complicated, discontent spread throughout the state.\n(5) A meeting was held under the leadership of Keshavrao Jedhe on 6 February, 1956 at Tilak Smarak Mandir in Pune and the Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti was formed.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87798,1951,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Text Book','','The role of newspapers was important in Samyukta Maharashtra Movement.','','','1) The role of newspapers was equally important in Samyukta Maharashtra Movement. They worked to awaken the people.\n(2) Navyug, Prabhat, Navakal, Sakal, Prabodhan, Kesari are newspapers which played important role.\n(3) The \'Maratha\' newspaper by Acharya Atre had a significant role in Samyukta Maharashtra movement.\n(4) Balasaheb Thackeray took pen name \'Mavla\' and drew caricatures in \'Navyug\'.\n(5) Lokshahir Annabhau Sathe, Shahir Amar Sheikh and Shahir D. N. Gavankar\naroused public awakening on a large scale through their writings.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87799,1951,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Severe response was seen throughout Maharashtra against JVP Committee report.','','','1) The Congress appointed a three ministers committee on 29 December, 1948 to study the conditions of creating linguistic provinces.\n(2) The report suggested that Congress accepted the concept of linguistic state in principle but the time was not proper for it.\n(3) Due to this, severe response of the people was seen throughout Maharashtra against JVP Committee report.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87800,1951,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','How did the Central Government favour Maharashtra\'s movement under the leadership of Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti?','','','1) Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru was going to unveil statue of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj mounted on a horse on Pratapgad on 30 November, 1957.\n(2) Under the leadership of Bhai Madhavrao Bagal, Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti held huge demonstrations.\n(3) Leaders like S. M. Joshi, N. G. Gore, Jayantrao Tilak, P. K. Atre participated in the protest at Pasarni Ghat and Poladpur.\n(4) Pandit Nehru became aware of sentiments of the Marathi speaking people.\n(5) The Congress President Indira Gandhi supported the Samyukta Maharashtra Movement.\n(6) Finally, the Central government gave consent for formation of two states-Gujarat and Maharashtra from former Mumbai (Bombay) state.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87801,1951,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the given picture and identify. Write about his contribution in Samyukta Maharashtra.(','','','1) This picture is of Acharya P. K. Atre. He made important contribution to Samyukta Maharashtra Movement.\n(2) He played important role in formation of Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti.\n(3) He presented and supported the prounited Maharashtra Movement through his newspaper \'Maratha\'.\n(4) He was in the forefront of the demonstrations at Pasarni Ghat and Poladpur during the visit of Prime Minister Pandit Nehru at Pratapgad.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87802,1951,'descriptive','5',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the advantages and disadvantages of linguistic reorganisation of states?','','','Advantages :\n(1) The feeling of unity is very strong among the people speaking common language.\n(2) It guarantees social security.\n(3) It facilitates communication between people.\n(4) It helps in achieving linguistic and cultural unity.\nDisadvantages :\n(1) Linguistic reorganisation may narrow perspective of the people.\n(2) Excessive pride in one\'s language creates hatred towards other language.\n(3) Learning other languages and enjoying the literature never takes places.\n(4) It creates hurdles in social and cultural development.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87803,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ wrote that the entire Marathi speaking population should be under one dominion.\n(A) Acharya Atre (B) N. C. Kelkar (C) Shankarrao Dev (D) Comrade Shripad Amrut Dange\n','','','B) N. C. Kelkar','',0,0,0,NULL),(87804,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Government of India appointed a Commission for Reconstruction of States on 29 December 1953, under the chairmanship of ____.\n(A) Acharya Atre (B) Pattabhi Sitaramayya (C) Justice Fazal Ali (D) Shankarrao Dev\n','','','C) Justice Fazal Ali','',0,0,0,NULL),(87805,1951,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On 6 February 1956, a meeting was conducted under the chairmanship of Keshavrao Jedhe at ____ in Pune.\n(A) Flora Fountain (B) Vidhan Sabha (C) Tilak Smarak Mandir (D) Kamgar Maidan\n','','','C) Tilak Smarak Mandir','',0,0,0,NULL),(87806,1951,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the table :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Organisation/ Commission</th>\n    <th>Chairperson/ President</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. ......................</td>\n    <td>Shankarrao Dev</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Dar Commission</td>\n    <td>......................</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Commission for Reconstruction of States</td>\n    <td>____</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. ____</td>\n    <td>Comrade Shripad Amrut Dange</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Organisation / Commission</th>\n    <th>Chairperson / President</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Maharashtra Ekikaran Parishad</td>\n    <td>Shankarrao Dev</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Dar Commission</td>\n    <td>Justice S. K. Dar</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Commission for Reconstruction of States</td>\n    <td>Justice Fazal Ali</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. Meeting of Labours</td>\n    <td>Comrade Shripad Amrut Dange</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(87807,1951,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','i. Ismat Chugtai\nii. Charusheela Gupte\niii. Kamalatai More\niv. Sultana Johari\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87808,1951,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','i. Communists\nii. Praja Socialists\niii. Peasants and Workers Party\niv. Janasangh\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87809,1951,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare / Complete the timeline : \n','','','i. Sahitya Sammelan at Belgaon where resolution of Samyukta Maharashtra was passed.\nii. \'Commission for Reconstruction of States\' was passed by Government of India.\niii. Meeting was conducted at Tilak Smarak Mandir in Pune under chairmanship of Keshavrao Jedhe.\niv. Maharashtra State was formed.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87810,1951,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The person who said, \'We will oppose the separation of Mumbai from Maharashtra upto our last breath\', at meeting held at Kamgar Maidan in Mumbai.','','','Shankarrao Dev','',0,0,0,NULL),(87811,1951,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The person who presided over the meeting of labours on 7th  November 1955.','','','Comrade Shripad Amrut Dange','',0,0,0,NULL),(87812,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was Dar Commission?','','','The commission established by Dr. Rajendra Prasad, the President of Constituent Assembly, on 17th  June 1948, under the chairmanship of Justice S. K. Dar for forming linguistic provinces, is known as Dar Commission.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87813,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What proved the desire of people that Mumbai should be included in Maharashtra?','','','The fact that, the proposal of Samyukta Maharashtra along with Mumbai, passed by Acharya Atre in the Mumbai Municipal Corporation got passed by 50 against 35 votes, proved the desire of people that Mumbai should be included in Maharashtra.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87814,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What resolution was proposed by S. M. Joshi in the meeting of labours held on 7th November 1955?','','','In the meeting of labours held on 7th November 1955, S. M. Joshi proposed a resolution that Samyukta Maharashtra should be created including Mumbai and Vidarbha.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87815,1951,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why did the Central Government favour the making of Maharashtra?','','','i. Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru was supposed to unveil a statue of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj mounted on a horse, on Pratapgad on 30  November 1957.\nii. The Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti made a huge demonstration under the leadership of Bhai Madhavrao Bagal.\niii. Several other leaders like S. M. Joshi, N. G. Gore, Jayantrao Tilak, P. K. Atre, Uddhavrao Patil, etc. participated in this demonstration.\niv. Apart from this, demonstrations were also conducted at Pasarni Ghat and Poladpur.\nv. These demonstrations by the Samiti succeeded in making Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru aware of the conditions and sentiments of the Marathi speaking people.\nvi. Also, the Indira Gandhi, the President of the Congress party, supported the Samyukta Maharashtra movement.\nThus, the Central Government favoured the making of Maharashtra due to the agitations led by the Samyukta Maharashtra Samiti.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87816,1951,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why was Hutatma Smarak erected in Mumbai near Flora Fountain?','','','i. The resolution for starting the Samyukta Maharashtra Movement was passed in the Sahitya Sammelan held at Belgaon on 12th  May 1946.\nii. The various commissions appointed by Government of India such as the Dar Commission, the Commission for the Reconstruction of States as well as the Congress appointed JVP Committee were not able to resolve the issue of formation of a state of Marathi speaking people. On the other hand, it advocated the creation of a bilingual Mumbai State.\niii. Thereafter, several leaders organised rallies and meetings as also farmers and workers along with several political parties came together and passed a resolution to create a Samyukta Maharashtra including Mumbai and Vidarbha.\niv. The discontent spread throughout the state including the rural areas and a huge public agitation aroused when it was clear that Mumbai will not be included in Maharashtra\nv. Several people became martyr in the firing ordered by the State Government.\nThe Hutatma Smarak was erected in Mumbai near Flora Fountain in memory of 106 such martyrs.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87817,1951,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','JVP Committee','','','i. A committee was appointed by the Congress government, to study the conditions of creating linguistic provinces.\nii. This committee included Jawaharlal Nehru, Vallabhbhai Patel and Pattabhi Sitaramayya, whose initials gave this committee the name JVP committee.\niii. After completing its study, the committee submitted its report which suggested that although Congress accepted the concept of linguistic states in principle, the time for the formation of such states was not proper.\niv. This report drew a very strong response against it, from across Maharashtra. At the same time, Senapati Bapat organised rallies for awakening of the people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87818,1951,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nagpur Pact, 1953','','','i. The purpose of signing the Nagpur Pact in 1953, was to make the Marathi people of all regions resolve to make one state.\nii. A Samyukta Maharashtra was formed according to this pact, which included Western Maharashtra, Vidarbha along with Marathwada.\niii. The various assurances given by the Nagpur Pact are;\na. Equitable financial provision of developmental work\nb. Ample finance for technical and vocational education\nc. Provision of state government services in regions according to its population\nd. Assembly Session once every year in Nagpur\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87819,1951,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The decision of one day token strike was taken on 21st  November 1955.','','','i. A grand march was led by Senapati Bapat to Vidhan Bhavan, at a time when the discontent among the Marathi speaking people fighting for Samyukta Maharashtra was growing.\nii. Due to this, the government led by the Chief Minister Morarji Desai declared a ban.\niii. Further, an open meeting was conducted at the Kamgar Maidan, where Comrade Amrut Dange guided a crowd of around 50 thousand people.\niv. The police lathi charged and also used tear gas at the protestors.\nv. Thereafter, to accelerate the struggle for the Samyukta Maharashtra Movement a decision to hold one day token strike was taken on 21st  November 1955.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87820,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the outcome of the Dar Commission?','','','The Dar Commission published its report on 10th  December 1948, but it could not resolve the issue of formation of linguistic states.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87821,1951,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What was the proposal of the Commission for Reconstruction of States?','','','The proposal presented by the Commission for Reconstruction of States on 10th  October 1955, advocated for creation of bilingual Mumbai State.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87822,1951,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','JVP Committee (Three Ministers Committee) : To study the conditions of creating linguistic provinces, the Congress appointed a committee on 29 December 1948. It included Jawaharlal Nehru, Vallabhbhai Patel and Pattabhisitaramayya. It is known by the initials of these members as ‘JVP Committee’. The report suggested that Congress accepts the concept of linguistic state in principle but the time was not proper for it. Severe response was seen throughout Maharashtra against this report. During that time Senapati Bapat organised rallies for awakening of people. Acharya Atre got passed the proposal of Samyukta Maharashtra with Mumbai in the Mumbai Municipal Corporation. It got passed by 50 against 35 votes. Due to it, the desire that Mumbai should be included in Maharashtra was proven. ','','1. When was the JVP Committee appointed?\n2. Who were the members of the JVP Committee?\n3. What was the main suggestion of the JVP Committee report?\n4. Who got the proposal of Samyukta Maharashtra passed in the Mumbai Municipal Corporation?\n','','1) The JVP Committee was appointed on 29 December 1948.\n2) The members were Jawaharlal Nehru, Vallabhbhai Patel, and Pattabhi Sitaramayya.\n3) The report suggested that Congress accepts the concept of linguistic states in principle, but the time was not proper for it.\n4) Acharya Atre got the proposal of Samyukta Maharashtra passed in the Mumbai Municipal Corporation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87823,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Laws are made by ____.\n(a) Legislature (b) Council of Ministers (c) Judiciary (d) Executive\n','','','a) Legislature','',0,0,0,NULL),(87824,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The Chief Justice of Supreme Court is appointed by the ____.\n(a) Prime Minister (b) President (c) Home Minister (d) Law Minister\n','','','b) President','',0,0,0,NULL),(87825,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','When the common man benefits from the values of freedom, equality and justice, it leads to the widening and deepening of ____.\n(a) values (b) democracy (c) Judiciary (d) Law\n','','','b) democracy','',0,0,0,NULL),(87826,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ helps to protect the rights of people.\n(a) Prime Minister (b) President (c) Judiciary (d) Social Organisation\n','','','c) Judiciary','',0,0,0,NULL),(87827,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ is the fundamental law of the nation.\n(a) Parliament (b) Judiciary (c) Constitution (d) President\'s order\n','','','c) Constitution','',0,0,0,NULL),(87828,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If any law passed by the Legislature or any act of the Executive violates any provision of the Constitution, the said law or act can be declared illegal by the ____.\n(a) Prime Minister (b) Speaker (c) President (d) Supreme Court\n','','','d) Supreme Court','',0,0,0,NULL),(87829,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ has the power to establish a High Court in every state of India.\n(a) Supreme Court (b) Parliament (c) President (d) Prime Minister\n','','','b) Parliament','',0,0,0,NULL),(87830,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Currently, there are ____ High Courts in India.\n(a) 20 (b) 29 (c) 24 (d) 22\n','','','c) 24','',0,0,0,NULL),(87831,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : Nature of Judiciary in India : --------------------','','','Integrated System ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87832,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : In the criminal cases, the first step is to file : -------------------','','','First Information Report (FIR) ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87833,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : The District judges are appointed by : -------------------','','','The Governor','',0,0,0,NULL),(87834,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : The High Court judges are appointed by : -----------------','','','The President','',0,0,0,NULL),(87835,1955,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87836,1955,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87837,1955,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Text Book','','Judicial Review ','','','1) The Constitution is the fundamental law of the nation and to protect it is the prime responsibility of the Supreme Court.\n(2) The Parliament cannot pass any law that violates the Constitution.\n(3) Every act or policy made by the Executive should be consistent with the Constitution.\n(4) If any law passed by the Legislature or any act of the Executive violates any provision of the Constitution, the said law or act is declared illegal.\n(5) So, it is struck down by the Supreme Court.\n(6) This power of the Supreme Court is known as Judicial Review.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87838,1955,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Text Book','','Public Interest Litigation (PIL) ','','','1) Public Interest Litigation (PIL) refers to litigations filed on issues of public importance and issues related to the welfare of the people.\n(2) It can be filed by individual citizens, social organisation or Non-Governmental\nOrganisations (NGOs) on behalf of all the people.\n(3) Issues related to rehabilitation of people who have lost their homes/lands, protection of environment, protection of the weaker sections of society, etc. have been effectively handled through PIL.\n(4) PILs are effective tool which require minimum expenditure and get immediate justice.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87839,1955,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Text Book','','Civil Law ','','','1) It is one of the two main branches of law.\n(2) It deals with conflicts which affect or interfere with the rights of an individual.\n(3) Conflicts regarding land and property, rent agreement, divorce, etc. are included under Civil law.\n(4) After filing α petition in the relevant court, the court gives a decision.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87840,1955,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Text Book','','Criminal Law ','','','1) Serious crimes are dealt under Criminal law.\n(2) Crimes like theft, robbery, dowry, murder, etc. are included under Criminal law.\n(3) In these cases, the first step is to file a \'First Information Report\' (FIR) with the police, who investigate the matter and file a petition in the court.\n(4) If the charges are proved, there are provisions for severe punishment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87841,1955,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Text Book','','Judicial Activism ','','','1) The courts settle the disputes whenever they are approached for that purpose.\n(2) But, in the recent times, this image of the courts has undergone a change.\n(3) They have become increasingly active.\n(4) The courts are now trying to fulfill the constitutional goals of justice and equality.\n(5) The courts have tried to provide legal protection to the marginalised sections of society, women, tribal, workers, farmers and children.\n6) Public Interest Litigations related to issues like victimisation of women, malnourishment among children, etc. have played an important role in boosting Judicial Activism\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87842,1955,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','High Court ','','','1) The Indian Constitution confers the Parliament with the power to establish a High Court in each constituent state in the Union.\n(2) Normally, each state has a High Court. But, in certain cases where the population and area of the states is less, one High Court has jurisdiction over more than one state.\n(3) For example, the Bombay High Court\'s jurisdiction covers the states of Maharashtra and Goa, and the Union Territories of Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu.\n(4) Currently, there are 24 High Courts in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87843,1955,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Supreme Court of India ','','','1) Judiciary in India is an integrated system where Supreme Court is at the apex position.\n(2) The Chief Justice of India (CJI) is the head of the Supreme Court of India.\n(3) By convention, the seniormost judge of the Supreme Court is appointed as the Chief Justice.\n(4) The President appoints the Chief Justice of India and other judges of the Supreme Court.\n(5) The Supreme Court of India is located at New Delhi.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87844,1955,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Judiciary in India is an integrated system.','','','(1) India is a Union of States. The Centre and the Constituent States have a separate Legislature and Executive.\n(2) But there is one judicial system for the whole of India.\n(3) The Supreme Court is the apex court under which there are High Courts.\n(4) The High Courts control the district courts and below them are the lower courts.\nHence, this structure makes Judiciary in India an integrated system.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87845,1955,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Constitution has made provision for independence of Judiciary.','','','1) The Constitution wants Judiciary to work freely, without any kind of pressure.\n(2) The independence of the judiciary is maintained so as to enable the judges to fearlessly carry out the function of giving justice.\nFor this purpose, the Constitution has made provisions for independence of Judiciary.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87846,1955,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Indian judiciary has made a significant contribution in the development of the country.','','','1) The Indian judiciary has always given importance to social values while protecting the Constitution.\n(2) It has exposed wrong practices like superstitions, discrimination, injustice to weaker sections, etc. and forced the legislature to pass laws against them.\n(3) It has protected individual freedom, the federal system and the Constitution of India.\n(4) Common people have a lot of respect and trust in the judicial system.\nThus, the Indian judiciary has made a significant contribution in the development of the country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87847,1955,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Why are laws necessary in society?','','','1) Differences in opinions, thoughts, perspectives, different cultures of people give rise to conflicts. These conflicts can be resolved impartially by the Judiciary.\n(2) Social justice and equality in society can be established with the help of law.\n(3) It also helps to bring weaker sections of the society, women, children differentlyabled and transgenders into the mainstream of the society.\n(4) Law helps the common man to get the benefits of values of freedom, equality and democracy.\n(5) Law helps to protect the rights of the people.\n(6) It prevents emergence of repressive and authoritarian groups and individuals.\nHence, laws are necessary in society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87848,1955,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Enumerate the functions of the Supreme Court.','','','The functions of the Supreme Court are as follows :\n(1) As a federal court, it has the responsibility to settle disputes between the centre and the states; and states on one side and states on the other.\n(2) It gives orders to relevant authorities to protect the fundamental rights of the people.\n(3) It has the power to review decrees and orders of the lower courts and also review its own decisions.\n(4) It provides necessary advice to the President if he/she asks for advice to understand the legal aspects in matters of public importance.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87849,1955,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Which are the provisions that preserve the independence of the judiciary?','','','The Constitution has made following provisions to preserve the independence of the judiciary :\n(1) To avoid any political pressure, judges are appointed by the President.\n(2) Judges enjoy the security of tenure. They cannot be removed from the post for trivial reasons or for political motives.\n(3) The salaries of the judges are drawn from the Consolidated Fund of India. No discussion regarding this takes place in the Parliament.\n(4) Personal criticism cannot be made on judges for their acts and decisions. It is considered as contempt of court and is a punishable offence.\n(5) The Parliament cannot discuss the decisions of the judges.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87850,1955,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the eligibility criteria for appointment of Supreme Court judges?','','','The eligibility criteria laid down by the Constitution for appointment of Supreme Court judges are as follows:\n(1) He/She must be a citizen of India.\n(2) He/ She must be a legal expert.\n(3) He/She must have served as a High Court judge or as an experienced advocate in the High Court.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87851,1955,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention the functions of the High Court.','','','The functions of the High Court are as follows:\n(1) To supervise over the District and other lower courts in its jurisdiction.\n(2) To maintain control over the functioning of the lower courts.\n(3) To give orders to protect the fundamental rights of the citizens.\n(4) To give advice to the governor while appointing judges in the district courts.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87852,1955,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Should the Supreme Court have the power of Judicial Review?','','','The Supreme Court must have the power of Judicial Review for following reasons :\n(1) Protecting the Constitution is the most important responsibility of the Supreme Court.\n(2) Many a times, laws violating the Constitution are passed under public pressure or for gaining popularity.\n(3) The Executive may pass laws or frame policies violating the Constitution.\n(4) Laws violating the fundamental rights of the citizens may be passed which may prove to be harmful for democracy.\n(5) The power of Judicial Review helps in curbing all anti-constitutional practices and protects and strengthens democracy.\n(6) It also helps in keeping the Executive under control.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87853,1955,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Why does the President seek the advice of the Supreme Court on any issue of public importance? Can you tell\n','','','1) Decisions taken on issues of public importance have long-lasting effect on the lives of the people.\n(2) Such decisions should also be according to the Rule of Law which treats everyone equally.\n(3) Care has to be taken that such decisions should not violate the Constitution.\n(4) Since the President is not a legal expert, he has to seek advice of the Supreme Court on any issues of public importance.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87854,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Judiciary helps to protect the ____ of the people.\n(A) Rights (B) Duties (C) Jobs (D) Litigations\n','','','A) Rights','',0,0,0,NULL),(87855,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ of the country is at the apex of the judiciary.\n(A) High Court (B) Supreme Court (C) District Court (D) Civil Court\n','','','B) Supreme Court','',0,0,0,NULL),(87856,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ control the District Courts.\n(A) Supreme Court (B) Civil Courts (C) Criminal Courts (D) High Courts\n','','','D) High Courts','',0,0,0,NULL),(87857,1955,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Governor seeks the advice of the ____ while appointing judges of the district courts.\n(A) The Prime Minister (B) The Chief Minister (C) High Court (D) Supreme Court\n','','','C) High Court','',0,0,0,NULL),(87858,1955,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','i. Rent agreement\nii. Conflicts regarding land and property\niii. Divorce\niv. Murder\nv. Robbery\nvi. Theft\nvii. Dowry\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(87859,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Retirement age of judges of Supreme Court.','','','65 years','',0,0,0,NULL),(87860,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The fund from which the salaries of judges is drawn.','','','Consolidated Funds of India','',0,0,0,NULL),(87861,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The process of removing the judges.','','','Impeachment','',0,0,0,NULL),(87862,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Fundamental law of the nation.','','','Constitution','',0,0,0,NULL),(87863,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Number of High Courts in India.','','','24','',0,0,0,NULL),(87864,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The two main branches of law.','','','Civil law and Criminal law','',0,0,0,NULL),(87865,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does Judiciary help in establishing a healthy society?','','','Judiciary works to give justice, removes injustice from the society and thus helps in establishing a healthy society.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87866,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who heads the Supreme Court?','','','The Chief Justice of India heads the Supreme Court.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87867,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why should there be independence of judiciary?','','','There should be independence of judiciary so that the judges can fearlessly carry out the function of giving justice.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87868,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who appoints the judges of the High Courts?','','','The President appoints the judges of the High Courts.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87869,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which courts are there at the district and tehsil level?','','','The District and lower level courts are there at the district and tehsil level.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87870,1955,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','State the importance of an unbiased judiciary with the help of examples.','','','The importance of an unbiased judiciary can be observed through the following examples:\ni. There can be a conflict of interest between government and an individual wherein people can approach the court for justice if a decision or law made by the government may seem unfair to the people.\nii. The objectives of social justice and equality, laid down in the Constitution, have to be implemented by the government. The judiciary supports the government through its decisions or active involvement and also by bringing the women, children, differently-abled and transgender into mainstream society.\niii. In order to strengthen democracy, it is necessary to make the common man benefit from the freedom of equality and justice which leads to widening and deepening of democracy.\niv. The Judiciary and its decisions are equal for everyone, no matter whether one is rich- poor, young- old, men or women. It thus controls the Rule of Law.\nv. The judiciary protects the rights of people and all the conflicts amongst them is sorted out according to the law. This prevents emergence of repressive and authoritarian groups and individuals.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87871,1955,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the functions of the High Court?','','','The functions of High Court are as follows:\ni. The lower courts under the jurisdiction of the High Court are supervised and controlled by the High Court.\nii. The Fundamental Rights of citizens are protected by the High Courts.\niii. It gives advice to the Governor while appointing the judges of the District Court.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87872,1955,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Judiciary of India is an integrated system','','','i. India is a Union of States.\nii. There is a separate legislature and executive for the Centre and the Constituent States.\niii. However, the judicial system is common for the whole of India.\niv. The Centre and the Constituent States have no division of courts between them.\nThus, it can be said that, the Judiciary of India is an integrated system.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87873,1955,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Courts have played an important role in strengthening the democracy in India.','','','i. A significant contribution has been made by the Indian judiciary towards the development of the country.\nii. The judicial system is trusted and respected by the citizens of the country.\niii. Individual freedom, federal system and the Constitution of India is protected by the judiciary.\nThus, courts have played an important role in strengthening the democracy in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87874,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who appoints the judges of the High Court?','','','The President appoints all the judges of the High Court.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87875,1955,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who is appointed as the Chief Justice?','','','The senior most judge is appointed as the Chief Justice.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87876,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The winter session of Maharashtra Legislature takes place at ____\n(a) Mumbai (b) Nagpur (c) Pune (d) Aurangabad\n','','','b) Nagpur','',0,0,0,NULL),(87877,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The ____ appoints the Governor.\n(a) Chief Minister (b) Prime Minister (c) President (d) Chief Justice\n','','','c) President','',0,0,0,NULL),(87878,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The right to summon the state legislature lies with the ____\n(a) Chief Minister (b) Governor (c) President (d) Speaker\n','','','b) Governor','',0,0,0,NULL),(87879,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Indian Union has totally ____ constituent states.\n(a) 22 (b) 25 (c) 29 (d) 32\n','','','c) 29','',0,0,0,NULL),(87880,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In India, only ____ states have Bicameral legislatures.\n(a) three (b) five (c) six (d) seven\n','','','d) seven','',0,0,0,NULL),(87881,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maharashtra’s legislature conducts minimum ____ sessions in a year.\n(a) two (b) three (c) four (d) five\n','','','b) three','',0,0,0,NULL),(87882,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguished personalities from various fields are nominated by the ____ to the Vidhan Parishad.\n(a) Chief Minister (b) Speaker (c) Governor (d) Chairman\n','','','c) Governor','',0,0,0,NULL),(87883,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When the legislature is not in session and need arises to make law, the Governor can issue ____\n(a) a Bill (b) a Proposal (c) an Ordinance (d) an Order\n','','','c) an Ordinance','',0,0,0,NULL),(87884,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statements are True or False. Correct the False statements and rewrite : States in India have been formed on the basis of population.','','','False. States in India have been formed on the basis of language.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87885,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statements are True or False. Correct the False statements and rewrite : In exceptional circumstances, elections to Vidhan Sabha can be held before the completion of 5 years.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(87886,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statements are True or False. Correct the False statements and rewrite : Absence of cooperation and coordination between Departments can affect the working of the government.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(87887,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statements are True or False. Correct the False statements and rewrite : The people look at the Governor as α \'Problem Solver\'.','','','False. The people look at the Chief Minister as a \'Problem Solver\'.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87888,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the following statements are True or False. Correct the False statements and rewrite : Every constituent state has a Vidhan Parishad.','','','False. Every constituent state has a Vidhan Sabha.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87889,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : The State having a government machinery different than other states','','','Jammu and Kashmir ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87890,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : The Vidhan Bhavan of Maharashtra is located in ..............','','','Mumbai ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87891,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : The member of Vidhan Sabha are called .................','','','MLA ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87892,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : Major challenges faced by Maharashtra ','','','Terrorism and Naxalite movements ','',0,0,0,NULL),(87893,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find and write : The place where the winter session of Maharashtra\'s legislature is conducted ','','','Nagpur','',0,0,0,NULL),(87894,1956,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the table :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Sr. No.</th>\n    <th>Houses</th>\n    <th>Tenure</th>\n    <th>No. of Members</th>\n    <th>Type of Election</th>\n    <th>Head</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1.</td>\n    <td>Vidhan Sabha</td>\n    <td>………….</td>\n    <td>………….</td>\n    <td>………………..</td>\n    <td>……..</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2.</td>\n    <td>Vidhan Parishad</td>\n    <td>………….</td>\n    <td>…………</td>\n    <td>…………….</td>\n    <td>……….</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Sr. No.</th>\n    <th>Houses</th>\n    <th>Tenure</th>\n    <th>No. of Members</th>\n    <th>Type of Election</th>\n    <th>Head</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1.</td>\n    <td>Vidhan Sabha (Maharashtra)</td>\n    <td>5 years. Elections can be held before completion of 5 years.</td>\n    <td>288</td>\n    <td>\n      (1) Directly elected by people.<br>\n      (2) Appointment of one Anglo-Indian member if required by governor.<br>\n      (3) Reserved seats for SC and ST.\n    </td>\n    <td>Speaker : The elected members of Vidhan Sabha elect from among themselves.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2.</td>\n    <td>Vidhan Parishad (Maharashtra)</td>\n    <td>Permanent House. Tenure of every member is six years. (Specific number of members retire every 2 years.)</td>\n    <td>78</td>\n    <td>\n      Indirectly elected in following ways :<br>\n      (1) Distinguished personalities from different fields nominated by the Governor.<br>\n      (2) Few elected by MLAs.<br>\n      (3) Few elected from local government institutions.<br>\n      (4) Few elected from teacher constituencies.<br>\n      (5) Few elected from graduate constituencies.\n    </td>\n    <td>Chairman : The members of Vidhan Parishad elect from among themselves.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(87895,1956,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87896,1956,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87897,1956,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Session ','','','1) A session is a meeting where members of both the Houses meet.\n(2) Maharashtra\'s legislature conducts minimum three sessions in a year.\n(3) The budget session and monsoon session of Maharashtra is held in Mumbai while the winter session is held at Nagpur.\n(4) The Governor has the right to summon and conclude the session.\n(5) During the session, new laws are passed, existing laws are amended and out-dated laws are repealed.\n(6) The sessions are conducted under the leadership and guidance of the Speaker and the Chairman.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87898,1956,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Council of Ministers ','','','1) The Council of Ministers consists of the Chief Minister and other ministers.\n(2) The leader of the majority party in Legislative Assembly is appointed as the Chief Minister by the Governor.\n(3) The Governor also appoints the other ministers chosen by the Chief Minister to form Council of Ministers.\n(4) Framing appropriate laws, working for the welfare of the people, governing the state efficiently are some of the functions of the Council of Ministers.\n(5) The Chief Minister along with his Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Legislative Assembly.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87899,1956,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Text Book','','The Governor ','','','1) The Governor is the titular/ nominal head of the state.\n(2) The government of the state is run in the name of the Governor.\n(3) He is appointed by the President and holds the office during the pleasure of the President.\n(4) He appoints the Chief Minister and the Council of Ministers.\n(5) As the head of the state, he enjoys certain important Legislative and Executive powers.\n(6) He has a right to summon the session of the state legislature.\n(7) In case the need arises, he can issue ordinance to make the law.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87900,1956,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Text Book','','Functions of the Chief Minister ','','','The functions of the Chief Minister, as an Executive head of the state, are as follows:\n(1) To form an able Council of Ministers giving maximum representation to various regions and social groups.\n(2) To distribute portfolios considering the political experience, administrative skills, public awareness, leadership, etc. of the ministers.\n(3) To develop proper cooperation and coordination between Departments, resolve their conflicts and make them work effectively.\n(4) To lead the state, frame proper policies and implement them effectively, intervene in issues of the state and solve them for the comfort of the people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87901,1956,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Powers of the Governor ','','','The Governor enjoys certain important Legislative and Executive powers as the head of the state.\n(1) The Bills passed by Vidhan Sabha and Vidhan Parishad are converted into laws only after receiving his assent.\n(2) He has the right to summon the session of the state legislature and to conclude it.\n(3) He can issue an ordinance to make law, on important subject if required, when the Legislature is not in session.\n(4) He appoints the Chief Minister and the other Council of Ministers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87902,1956,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Governor is the titular/nominal head of the state.','','','1) The Constitution has granted Executive powers to the Governor as the head of the state.\n(2) The government of the state is run in the name of the Governor.\n(3) But in reality, the administration is carried out by the Chief Minister.\nHence, the Governor is the titular/ nominal head of the state.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87903,1956,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vidhan Parishad (Legislative Council) is a permanent House.','','','1) All members of the Vidhan Parishad (MLCs) do not retire at the same time.\n(2) A specific number 1/3 rd of the members retire every 2 years.\n(3) These vacant seats are filled again by conducting fresh elections for those seats.\n(4) The tenure of each member is 6 years.\n(5) Since the Vidhan Parishad is never fully dissolved, it is a permanent House.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87904,1956,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','India has 29 constituent states, but 31 Vidhan Sabha.','','','1) According to the Constitution, every constituent state must have a Vidhan Sabha (Legislative Assembly).\n(2) India has 29 constituent states and each state has its Vidhan Sabha.\n(3) Out of the 7 Union Territories in India, Delhi and Puducherry have Vidhan Sabha.\nHence, India has 29 constituent states, but 31 Vidhan Sabha.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87905,1956,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Chief Minister\'s post is most important in the state.','','','1) The Chief Minister can select his Council of Ministers according to his choice.\n(2) He can ask any of his ministers to resign.\n(3) Framing policies of public welfare and developing the state in a progressive manner entirely depends upon the Chief Minister.\n(4) The Chief Minister leads the state and people look at him as a problem solver.\n(5) His intervention in solving the problems of the state comforts the people.\nHence, all these executive powers makes the Chief Minister\'s post most important in the State.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87906,1956,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Enumerate the functions of the Speaker of the Vidhan Sabha (Legislative Assembly).','','','The Speaker is elected by the members of Vidhan Sabha. His functions are as follows :\n(1) To prepare the order of daily proceedings in the House.\n(2) To carry out the proceedings of the House in a disciplined manner.\n(3) To suspend the members for misbehaviour or misconduct.\n(4) To control and guide the proceedings of the House during sessions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87907,1956,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Why did the Constitution adopt a federal system for India?','','','1) India is a country having large geographic expanse.\n(2) It has a multicultural population.\n(3) There is a great diversity in language, religion, ways of life and regional characteristics.\n(4) It would not have been possible to govern such a huge country from a centralised place.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87908,1956,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the considerations of the Chief Minister while allocation of portfolios? \n                               OR\nFormation of Council of Ministers is a challenging task for the Chief Minister.\n','','','After the formation of the Council of Ministers, the Chief Minister has to distribute portfolios among the ministers. He has to consider following aspects -\n(1) Some portfolios are of prime importance while others are of secondary importance. He has to select appropriate people for each portfolio.\n(2) He has to consider the experience and the efficiency of the ministers he has selected.\n(3) The political experience and administrative skills of the concerned ministers is also taken into consideration by him.\n(4) Other factors like the awareness of public issues, effective leadership, etc. are also considered by him.\n(5) If the government is in coalition, he has to distribute portfolios appropriately among the constituent parties in the alliance.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87909,1956,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention the eligibility conditions for contesting Vidhan Sabha elections.','','','Following are the eligibility conditions for candidates contesting for Vidhan Sabha elections :\n(1) He/ She should be a citizen of India.\n(2) He/  She should have completed 25 years of age.\n(3) He/  She should be a resident of Maharashtra.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87910,1956,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Do you consider Maharashtra to be progressive state?','','','1) Industrial development has taken place in Maharashtra on a large scale. It has many industries based on modern technology.\n(2) Maharashtra\'s literacy rate is higher than many other states.\n(3) It has a rich tradition of saints and social reformers who have nurtured progressive thinking among people.\n(4) Maharashtra is leading in many fields like education, health, agriculture, sports, tourism, etc.\n(5) Maharashtra has been a land of Movements. Many important movements like the Labour movement, Farmer\'s movement have been initiated here.\n(6) Similarly, many progressive laws have been passed in Maharashtra.\nMaharashtra is facing many challenges like terrorism, poverty, crime, Naxalite movements, etc. Even then, I feel that Maharashtra is a progressive state.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87911,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Legislative Assembly is also known as ____\n(A) Vidhan Parishad (B) Vidhan Sabha (C) Parliament (D) Lok Sabha\n','','','B) Vidhan Sabha','',0,0,0,NULL),(87912,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Governor nominates one of the members of the ____ community, in case of inadequate representation of its members in the Legislative Assembly/Vidhan Sabha.\n(A) Anglo-Indian (B) Schedule Caste (C) Schedule Tribe (D) Other Backward Class\n','','','A) Anglo-Indian','',0,0,0,NULL),(87913,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In the absence of the Speaker, the responsibility of the Vidhan Sabha is taken over by the ____\n(A) Governor (B) President (C) Chief Minister (D) Deputy Speaker\n','','','D) Deputy Speaker','',0,0,0,NULL),(87914,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ is the nominal head at the state level.\n(A) Governor (B) President (C) Chief Minister (D) Deputy Speaker\n','','','A) Governor','',0,0,0,NULL),(87915,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The leader of the majority party in the Legislative Assembly becomes the ____\n(A) Chief Minister (B) Governor (C) President (D) Speaker\n','','','A) Chief Minister','',0,0,0,NULL),(87916,1956,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The government of the state is run in the name of the ____\n(A) Chief Minister (B) Governor (C) President (D) Prime Minister\n','','','B) Governor','',0,0,0,NULL),(87917,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Number of states in India that have two Legislature Houses ............','','','7','',0,0,0,NULL),(87918,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : States that have two Legislature Houses are known as .............','','','Bicameral Legislature','',0,0,0,NULL),(87919,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : Full form of MLA and MLC','','','Members of Legislative Assembly and Members of Legislative Council','',0,0,0,NULL),(87920,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : First House of Maharashtra ..................','','','Vidhan Sabha','',0,0,0,NULL),(87921,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The age qualification to contest the Vidhan Sabha election','','','25 years','',0,0,0,NULL),(87922,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which state of India follows a different government machinery as compared to the other states?','','','The state of Jammu and Kashmir in India follows a different government machinery as compared to the other states.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87923,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who elects the Speaker and the Deputy Speaker of the Vidhan Sabha?','','','The newly elected members of the Vidhan Sabha elect the Speaker and the Deputy Speaker amongst themselves, when the newly elected House meets for the first time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87924,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many sessions are conducted in a year by the Maharashtra\'s legislature?','','','Minimum three sessions are conducted in a year by the Maharashtra\'s legislature- Summer, Winter and Monsoon session.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87925,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who nominates some members of the Vidhan Parishad?','','','The Governor nominates some members of the Vidhan Parishad who are distinguished personalities from the field of literature, science and social services.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87926,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who are the Executive of Maharashtra?','','','The Executive of Maharashtra Governor, Chief Minister and Council of Ministers','',0,0,0,NULL),(87927,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When can the Governor issue an Ordinance?','','','When the legislature is not in session and the need arises to make a law, the Governor issues an Ordinance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87928,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','Whom does the Chief Minister choose as the Council of Ministers?','','','The Chief Minister chooses his most trustworthy colleagues as the Council of Ministers.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87929,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does Chief Minister ensure efficient administration in the state?','','','Chief Minister ensures efficient administration in the state by keeping up cooperation and coordination between the departments.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87930,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the two major challenges facing the state of Maharashtra?','','','The two major challenges faced by the state of Maharashtra are terror attacks and Naxalite Movements which are active in certain regions of the state.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87931,1956,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Federal system of government','','','i. A federal system of government means when power is divided between two levels.\nii. The two levels are the Central level and the State level.\niii. The Central government works at the national level.\niv. The State government works at the regional or state level.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87932,1956,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vidhan Sabha of Maharashtra','','','i. Vidhan Sabha is the first house of Maharashtra legislature comprising 288 members.\nii. It is also known as the Legislative Assembly. It has some reserved seats on the basis of caste and tribe.\niii. The Governor nominates one of the member of Anglo-Indian community, in case of inadequate representation of its members in Legislative Assembly/Vidhan Sabha.\niv. Its proceedings are carried out under supervision of Speaker.\nv. In the absence of Speaker the responsibility is taken over by Deputy Speaker.\nvi. The state of Maharashtra is divided into Territorial constituencies, and one member is elected from each constituency.\nvii. The age qualification to contest the election of the Vidhan Sabha is 25 years.\nviii. Tenure of Vidhan sabha is 5 years, in rare cases elections to the Vidhan Sabha can be held before the completion of its term.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87933,1956,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vidhan Parishad of Maharashtra','','','i. Vidhan Parishad is the second House of the Maharashtra legislature.\nii. There are 78 members in the Vidhan Parishad of Maharashtra and they are indirectly elected.\niii. Out of the 78 members some are distinguished personalities from the fields of literature, science and social service.\niv. They are nominated by the Governor and the remaining are indirectly elected by the members of Vidhan Sabha, local government institutions and teacher constituencies and graduate constituencies.\nv. Its proceedings are carried out under supervision of Chairman.\nvi. In the absence of Chairman the responsibility is taken over by Deputy Chairman.\nvii. The Vidhan Parishad which is also known as the Legislative Council is a permanent body and never gets dissolved fully.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87934,1956,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Vidhan Parishad is a permanent House.','','','i. The Vidhan Parishad is never fully dissolved.\nii. A fixed number of members retire every two years.\niii. Fresh elections are held for the seats that are vacated by the retiring members.\nThus, the Vidhan Parishad is a permanent House.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87935,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How many constituent states are there in India?','','','There are 29 constituent states in India','',0,0,0,NULL),(87936,1956,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the two Houses of Legislature of Maharashtra.','','','The two Houses of Legislature of Maharashtra are- Vidhan Sabha and Vidhan Parishad.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87937,1957,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ is permanent in nature.\n(A) Election Commission (B) Bureaucracy (C) President (D) Prime Minister\n','','','B) Bureaucracy','',0,0,0,NULL),(87938,1957,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The bureaucracy is politically ____\n(A) Positive (B) Negative (C) Biased (D) Neutral\n','','','D) Neutral','',0,0,0,NULL),(87939,1957,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Parliament holds the ____ responsible for malpractices.\n(A) Bureaucracy (B) Prime Minister (C) Minister (D) President\n','','','C) Minister','',0,0,0,NULL),(87940,1957,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ has established autonomous institutions like the Public Service Commissions for selecting efficient civil servants.\n(A) Constitution (B) Council of Ministers (C) Parliament (D) President\n','','','A) Constitution','',0,0,0,NULL),(87941,1957,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The \'Indian Foreign Service\' is included under ____\n(A) All India Services (B) Union or Central Services (C) State Civil Services (D) Armed Forces\n','','','B) Union or Central Services','',0,0,0,NULL),(87942,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify if the following statements are correct or wrong and rewrite the wrong statements in their correct form : In a Parliamentary democracy, representatives elected by the people and ministers bear the administrative responsibility.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(87943,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify if the following statements are correct or wrong and rewrite the wrong statements in their correct form : Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) recruits candidates for civil services in Maharashtra.','','','Wrong - Maharashtra Public Service Commission recruits candidates for civil services in Maharashtra.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87944,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify if the following statements are correct or wrong and rewrite the wrong statements in their correct form : Bureaucracy is an important instrument of social transformation.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87945,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify if the following statements are correct or wrong and rewrite the wrong statements in their correct form : Civil servants are not aware of the history of successes and failures of policies.','','','Wrong - Civil servants are aware of the history of successes and failures of policies.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87946,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify if the following statements are correct or wrong and rewrite the wrong statements in their correct form : Civil servants are never publicly criticised.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(87947,1957,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87948,1957,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87949,1957,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','','','','','',1,0,1,NULL),(87950,1957,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bureaucracy','','','1) It is the administrative system that works under the Union Executive.\n(2) As this administrative system includes civil servants i.e. bureaucrats, it is called Bureaucracy.\n(3) It has the responsibility of actual implementation of government\'s policies.\n(4) Though the ministers control the bureaucracy, they are dependent on the bureaucracy for information and efficient functioning.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87951,1957,'descriptive','2a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Armed Forces and Civil Services ','','','1) Defending the country from external aggression and internal threats and ensuring security of the country is carried out by the Armed Forces.\n(2) Services provided to the citizens for making their daily lives comfortable and enabling them to develop themselves and the society are called Civil Services.\n(3) The administrative system developed to provide services to citizens are called Civil Services.\n(4) The armed forces help the civil services for internal security.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87952,1957,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Text Book','','Reservation policy is followed even in Civil Services.','','','1) A part of the society comprising of Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes, Women, Other Backward Castes and specially-abled is termed as the weaker section of the society.\n(2) It was essential to empower these weaker sections.\n(3) They were left out of civil services due to social inequality.\n(4) In order to establish social justice and provide opportunities to weaker sections of the society, reservation policy is followed even in civil services.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87953,1957,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Text Book','','It is necessary for civil servants to be politically neutral.','','','1) In Parliamentary democracy, in India, a new government comes in power after every five years. But the bureaucracy is permanent, i.e. they remain same.\n(2) Bureaucracy is expected to implement the policies and decisions of the earlier\ngovernment with same efficiency and commitment.\n(3) Civil servants are expected to keep away from taking a political stand and remain neutral while discharging their duties.\n(4) If civil servants work according to their political views it will lead to chaos.\nHence, they should be politically neutral.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87954,1957,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Coordination between the Civil Servants and Ministers help in efficient functioning of the government.','','','1) In Parliamentary democracy, the Executive is responsible for efficient governance of the country.\n(2) The decisions relating to the concerned department are made by the minister-in-charge, but necessary information to make such decisions is given by the civil servants.\n(3) The ministers are dependent on the civil servants for many reasons.\nHence, coordination and mutual trust between the civil servants and ministers help in efficient functioning of the government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87955,1957,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Bureaucracy is not held directly responsible for any success or failure of policy.','','','1) It is the Minister who is responsible for the efficient and smooth functioning of his department.\n(2) Though providing necessary information and giving advice is the function of the bureaucracy, it is not accountable for any inefficiency.\n(3) The Parliament holds the Minister responsible for inefficiency and malpractices.\n(4) In such case, the Minister bears the responsibility and protects the bureaucracy.\nThus, the anonymity of bureaucracy is preserved, which means they are not held directly responsible for any success or failure of policy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87956,1957,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bureaucracy is considered as an important instrument of social transformation.','','','1) Many laws are enacted by the government for empowerment of women, protection of children and marginalised sections of the society.\n(2) Social change is brought through the implementation of such policies.\n(3) These laws are successfully implemented by the bureaucracy.\nHence, Bureaucracy is considered as an important instrument of social transformation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87957,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain the role of the ministers and civil servants in the efficient administration of the department.','','','1) The efficiency of a particular department depends upon the interrelationship between the Minister and his Secretary and Deputy secretary.\n(2) The decisions taken by the Ministers are based on the necessary information provided by the bureaucracy.\n(3) The bureaucracy, i.e. civil servants have complete information about the financial provisions for a particular scheme or plan and also about the history of successes and failures of policies.\n(4) Hence, if the Ministers develop mutual trust and transparency with the civil servants, it will help in the efficient administration of the department.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87958,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain how the bureaucracy provides stability to the political system.','','','1) Bureaucracy does the work of implementing decisions taken by the ministers.\n(2) Several important reforms introduced during the post-independent period have been effectively implemented by the bureaucracy.\n(3) It consistently provides services like water supply, public cleanliness, transport, electricity, etc. to the people and brings stability in their day to day life.\n(4) It acts as an instrument of social transformation and democratisation.\nIn this way, it gives stability to the political system.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87959,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain the characteristics of Bureaucracy.','','','1) Bureaucracy is the administrative system which actually implements the government\'s policies.\n(2) Though the government changes, bureaucracy remains constant. Thus, it is permanent in nature.\n(3) It has to remain politically neutral and discharge its duties.\n(4) Its anonymity is maintained, i.e. it is not held directly responsible for any success or failure of policies and it is never publicly criticized.\nThus, the structure of Bureaucracy in India is extensive and complex.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87960,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the significance of Indian bureaucracy.','','','1) The Indian bureaucracy implements the policies made by the government for the welfare of the people effectively.\n(2) It provides several essential services to the people consistently and brings stability in their day to day life.\n(3) It provides stability to the political system.\n(4) It acts as an important instrument of social transformation and democratization of society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87961,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the role of bureaucracy in the democratisation of society.','','','1) Equality and social justice are important values of democracy.\n(2) Bureaucracy has helped in bringing marginalised sections of society into the\nmainstream by implementing the reservation policy effectively.\n(3) Their participation has been increased in the process of decisionmaking.\n(4) Democratisation of society not only requires progressive laws and policies but also effective participation of bureaucracy in implementing it.\nThus, bureaucracy plays an important role in the democratisation of society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87962,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why have autonomous institutions like Public Service Commissions been established?','','','The Constitution of India has established autonomous institutions like Public Service Commissions for following reasons :\n(1) To select civil servants.\n(2) To conduct competitive exams for selecting candidates on the criteria of merit and efficiency.\n(3) To decide other aspect to like the nature of the exam, syllabus, eligibility, etc.\n(4) To select efficient and deserving candidates through interviews and recommend them to the government for appointment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87963,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Express your views on \'ideal bureaucracy\'.','','','Bureaucracy includes civil servants and all the employees working in various government offices. In India, bureaucracy is criticised most of the times and is seldom praised.\nAccording to me, an ideal bureaucracy should function as follows :\n(1) The bureaucracy should advice and force the ministers to adopt policies of public welfare.\n(2) It should give full cooperation to the ministers for implementing policies effectively.\n(3) It should adopt the principle of equality and an unbiased approach while implementing the policies so that maximum people are benefitted.\n(4) It should make the people aware of different beneficial policies.\n(5) It should strictly keep away from illpractices like corruption, unnecessary delay, cheating and frauds, etc.\n(6) It should aim at establishing social justice by extending maximum benefits to weaker sections.\n(7) It should function as an effective medium for development of the country, social transformation and democratisation of society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87964,1957,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ are appointed from the civil service.\n(A) President (B) Prime Minister (C) Secretaries (D) Chief Ministers\n','','','C) Secretaries','',0,0,0,NULL),(87965,1957,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ means that a civil servant should not be directly held responsible for the success or failure of any policy.\n(A) Neutrality (B) Anonymity (C) Bureaucracy (D) Responsibility\n','','','B) Anonymity','',0,0,0,NULL),(87966,1957,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In India there are ____ main types of civil services.\n(A) Three (B) Five (C) Four (D) Two\n','','','A) Three','',0,0,0,NULL),(87967,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify if the following statements are correct or wrong and rewrite the wrong sentences in their correct form : District Collector, Municipal Commissioner, Finance Secretary and Divisional Commissioner are civil servants in the government\'s administrative system','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(87968,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify if the following statements are correct or wrong and rewrite the wrong sentences in their correct form : The Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers and the bureaucracy under their control remains constant.','','','Wrong.\nThe Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers change after every election but the bureaucracy under their control remains constant.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87969,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify if the following statements are correct or wrong and rewrite the wrong sentences in their correct form : Democratisation of society only requires progressive laws and policies.','','','Wrong.\nDemocratisation of society not only requires progressive laws and policies, but also requires the effective participation of bureaucracy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87970,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is bureaucracy?','','','The administrative system which works under the Union Executive and which performs the responsibility of actual implementation of government\'s policy is known as bureaucracy.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87971,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On what does the efficiency of a particular government depend upon?','','','The efficiency of a particular government depends upon the relationship between the Ministers and Department Secretaries and Deputy Secretaries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87972,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is \'Anonymity\' in the bureaucracy?','','','\'Anonymity\' in the bureaucracy means that the success or failure of a policy cannot be linked to a civil servant.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87973,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the two fundamental types of functions performed by the government of any country?','','','The two fundamental types of functions performed by the government of any country are:\ni. Defending the country from external aggression and internal threats.\nii. Providing various services to citizens so as to ease their daily lives and enable them to develop themselves as well as the society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87974,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the government carry out the function of providing security for the country?','','','i. The government protects the country from external aggression and internal threats.\nii. The security of the country is carried out by the armed forces.\niii. The internal security is also maintained as aid to civil authority:\nIn this way, the government carries out the function of providing security for the country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87975,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the significance of Indian bureaucracy?','','','i. The Indian bureaucracy has an extensive and complex structure.\nii. It has effectively implemented several important reforms which were introduced in the postindependence period.\niii. Also, many positive changes are seen due to the implementation of the Government policies by the Indian bureaucracy.\niv. It brings stability to the political system as well as to the people by ensuring provision of their daily needs like water supply, cleanliness, health, agricultural reforms, prevention of pollution, etc.\nv. It also ensures implementation of policies and laws enacted by the government for women empowerment, child security, plans for marginalised sections, etc. Thus, Indian bureaucracy becomes an important instrument of social transformation.\nvi. Also, Indian bureaucracy plays an important role in the democratisation of the society by bringing marginalised sections in to the main stream through reservation policy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87976,1957,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is democratisation of society achieved?','','','The democratisation of society is achieved in the following ways:\ni. The reservation policy helps in bringing the marginalised sections of society into the mainstream.\nii. The bureaucracy ensures increased participation of this section in the decision making process.\niii. Thus, democratisation of society not only requires progressive laws but also the effective participation of bureaucracy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87977,1957,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The bureaucracy is permanent in nature.','','','i. The bureaucracy has to undertake various important tasks like tax collection, environment protection, maintenance of law & order and provisions regarding social security.\nii. Also, although the Prime Minister and the Council of Ministers and hence the ruling political may change after every election, the bureaucracy is expected to discharge its duties by remaining neutral.\nSince, this requires stability, the bureaucracy has to be permanent in nature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87978,1957,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The minister bears the responsibility and protects the bureaucracy.','','','i. Bureaucracy, is the administrative system that works under the Union Executive and is expected to carry out the implementation of their policy decisions with the efficiency and commitment. However, the success or failure of a policy cannot be linked to a civil servant.\nii. The efficient and smooth functioning of the department is the responsibility of the Minister and if there is any inefficiency the Minister would be held responsible.\niii. There can be no public criticism of the Civil Servants.\niv. If there is any malpractice, the Parliament will hold the Minister responsible for it.\nThus, the minister bears the responsibility and protects the bureaucracy.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87979,1957,'descriptive','3',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Bureaucracy is an important instrument of social transformation.','','','i. The government enacts laws for the empowerment of women, child security, plans for marginalised sections, etc.\nii. The implementation of these laws is undertaken by the bureaucracy.\niii. The implementation of these laws and policies helps to brings about a social change.\nHence, it is said that the bureaucracy is an important instrument of social transformation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(87980,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which important tasks have to be undertaken by the bureaucracy?','','','The bureaucracy has to undertake the work of tax collection, environment protection, maintenance of law and order, provisions regarding social security.','',0,0,0,NULL),(87981,1957,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do you mean by political neutrality?','','','Political neutrality means that regardless of the political party in power, the bureaucracy is expected to carry out the implementation of their policy decisions with the same efficiency and commitment.','',0,0,0,NULL),(88323,434,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">I know a curious little boy\nWho is always asking, \"why?\"\nWhy this, why that, why then, why now,\nWhy not, why by-and-by?\nHe wants to know why wood should swim, \nWhy lead and marbles sink; \nWhy stars should shine and winds should blow,\nAnd why we eat and drink.\nHe wants to know what makes the clouds,\nAnd why they cross the sky; \nWhy sinks the sun behind the hills, \nAnd why the flowers die.\nHe wants to know why wind should come \nFrom out the bellows\' nose; \nWhy pop-guns should go pop and why \nThe ocean ebbs and flows.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Match the following parts of sentences to make meaningful sentences.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. The ocean</td>\n    <td>a) a curious little boy</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. The wind</td>\n    <td>b) ebbs and flows</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. The sun</td>\n    <td>c) comes out from the bellow’s nose</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. The poet knows</td>\n    <td>d) sinks behind the hills</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question <b>\n       Complete the following web :','<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write two pairs of rhyming words from the extract.','A1] (1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – d), (4 – a)\nA2] i) wood swims           ii) marbles sink\n       iii) stars shine             iv) we eat and drink\nA3] i) boy – why          ii) sink – drink ','',0,1,0,NULL),(88324,434,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">He wants to know why ice should melt;\nWhy spiders eat the flies;\nWhy bees should sting and why the yeast\nShould make the dough to rise.\nSome of his whys are not too hard \nTo answer, if you\'ll try: \nBut other no one ever yet\nHas found the reason why.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following web ','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following sentences:\n     i. ---------- helps the dough to rise.\n     ii. Some of his whys are not too hard to answer if --------------\n     iii. -------------- why some of this whys are not too hard to answer.\n     iv. According to the poet the yeast makes -------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Complete the following with proper action words from the extract:\n        i. spiders         ii. dough          iii. ice         iv. bees ','','A1] i) Why spiders eat the flies?          ii) Why bees should sting?\n       iii) Why the yeast should make the dough to rise?\n      iv) Why ice should melt?\nA2] i) The yeast            ii) you will try.\n       iii) No one has found the reason       iv) the dough to rise.\nA3] i) eat          ii) rise           iii) melt           iv) sting','',1,0,0,NULL),(88325,435,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Slowly, gently, the exhausted musician laid his tanpura on the carpet. The exquisite strains of Raag Darbari still vibrated within the hall. Emperor Akbar looked up, his eyes lit with admiration. \"Wonderful!\" he said. \"Superb! | listen to you every day, but I can\'t seem to have enough!\"\nTansen bowed in silent gratitude.\n\"I think you have the most wonderful voice in the world!\" said Akbar.\n\"But I don\'t, Shahenshah!\" said Tansen with a smile. \"There is someone who sings far better than I do.\"\n\"Really?\" cried the disbelieving Akbar. \"Then I must have him sing in my court. Can you arrange it?\nTansen shook his head. \"I\'m afraid he will not come, Sire.\"\n\"What! Not even if he hears that the emperor himself has summoned him?\"\n\"No, not even then.\"\nThis reply would have enraged any other emperor.\nBut Akbar was different. \"Very well, Ustadji,\" said Akbar, smiling into Tansen\'s half-scared eyes. \"If he doesn\'t come, I shall go to him, myself. Will you take me to him?\"\n\"Yes, Sire, provided you do not come as the Emperor of Hindustan.\"\n\"I shall go as a humble lover of music.\"\nSant Haridas was the man. Tansen had spoken of. He had been Tansen\'s music teacher and he lived the austere life of a hermit. When Tansen and the emperor reached his hut, he was busy with his daily chores. When they asked him to sing, he smiled but said firmly, \"I\'m long past the age for singing.\" Even his favourite pupil could not persuade him to change his mind.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Write T for True, NT for Not True and CNS for Can Not Say:\n      i. Emperor Akbar greatly admired Tansen\'s music, but now he was bored with it.\n      ii. Akbar was arrogant and expected all his subjects to obey his commands.\n      iii. Guruji recognised the king.\n      iv. Sant Haridas\'s favourite pupil could not persuade him to change his mind.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       How does Emperor Akbar praise Tansen?\n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        Frame your own sentences by using the given words/phrases:\n      i. Wonderful         ii. Superb      iii. Very well      iv. Summoned\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       Match the sentences with their types :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Sentences</th>\n    <th>Types</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. I am long past the age for singing</td>\n    <td>a) Exclamatory</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Wonderful</td>\n    <td>b) Statement/Assertive</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. No, not even then!</td>\n    <td>c) Imperative</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. Take me to him</td>\n    <td>d) Negative</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       What would happen if the Emperor Akbar would summon Sant Haridas.','','A1] i) Not true            ii) Not true         iii) Can not say          iv) Not true\nA2] Emperor Akbar looked up, and said it was indeed wonderful and superb. And he further said that he had the wonderful voice in the world. Thus he praises Tansen.\nA3]  i) What a wonderful scene that was!\n        ii) His handwriting is really Superb.\n        iii) My sister has been doing very well in her studies.\n        iv) The teacher summoned the whole\nA4] (i – b), (ii – a), (iii – d), (iv – c)\nA5] If the Emperor Akbar would summon Sant Haridas, he wouldn\'t have come to his court. The Emperor would have been felt insulted. And even Tansen would have been hurt. And if he wouldn\'t have come to his court, the Emperor would have given him punishment by bringing him in front of him by sending his men. He would not be able to listen to the music of Sant Haridas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(88326,434,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">WHY?\nI know a curious little boy\nWho is always asking, \"why?\"\nWhy this, why that, why then, why now,\nWhy not, why by-and-by?\nHe wants to know why wood should swim, \nWhy lead and marbles sink; \nWhy stars should shine and winds should blow,\nAnd why we eat and drink.\nHe wants to know what makes the clouds,\nAnd why they cross the sky; \nWhy sinks the sun behind the hills, \nAnd why the flowers die.\nHe wants to know why wind should come \nFrom out the bellows\' nose; \nWhy pop-guns should go pop and why \nThe ocean ebbs and flows.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Match the following parts of sentences to make meaningful sentences.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. The ocean</td>\n    <td>a) a curious little boy</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. The wind</td>\n    <td>b) ebbs and flows</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. The sun</td>\n    <td>c) comes out from the bellow’s nose</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. The poet knows</td>\n    <td>d) sinks behind the hills</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following web :','<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write two pairs of rhyming words from the extract.\n<b>B]</b> Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','A1] (1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – d), (4 – a)\nA2] i) wood swims           ii) marbles sink\n       iii) stars shine             iv) we eat and drink\nA3] i) boy – why          ii) sink – drink \nB] i) Title of the poem: Why\nii) Name of the poet: Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme: aaba, cded, faga ………\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) why this, why that, why then, why now Repetition.\nv) Theme / central idea: The poem deals with a curious boy who wants to know the reasons behind some of the phenomena in the nature.','',0,1,0,NULL),(88327,434,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">WHY?\nHe wants to know why ice should melt;\nWhy spiders eat the flies;\nWhy bees should sting and why the yeast\nShould make the dough to rise.\nSome of his whys are not too hard \nTo answer, if you\'ll try: \nBut other no one ever yet\nHas found the reason why.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following web ','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following sentences:\n     i. ---------- helps the dough to rise.\n     ii. Some of his whys are not too hard to answer if --------------\n     iii. -------------- why some of this whys are not too hard to answer.\n     iv. According to the poet the yeast makes -------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Complete the following with proper action words from the extract:\n        i. spiders         ii. dough          iii. ice         iv. bees \n<b>B]</b> Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) Why spiders eat the flies?          ii) Why bees should sting?\n       iii) Why the yeast should make the dough to rise?\n      iv) Why ice should melt?\nA2] i) The yeast            ii) you will try.\n       iii) No one has found the reason       iv) the dough to rise.\nA3] i) eat          ii) rise           iii) melt           iv) sting\nB] i) Title of the poem: Why\nii) Name of the poet: Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme: aaba, cded, faga ………\niv) Figure of speech :\n      a) why this, why that, why then, why now Repetition.\nv) Theme / central idea: The poem deals with a curious boy who wants to know the reasons behind some of the phenomena in the nature.','',1,0,0,NULL),(88328,436,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Sticks and stones may break my bones, \nBut words can also hurt me. \nStones and sticks break only skin,\nWhile words are ghosts that haunt me.\nSlant and curved the fall word-swords\nTo pierce and stick inside me. \nBats and bricks may ache through bones. \nBut words can mortify me.\nPain from words has left its scar \nOn mind and heart that\'s tender \nCuts and bruises now have healed;\nIt\'s words that I remember.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following web :','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following chart\n                           Difference in injuries\n        Made by sticks and stones                Made by words\n    i. -----------------------                                  a) -----------------\n    ii. ---------------------                                    b) ----------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write two words that rhyming with : \n       i. bones          ii. skin           iii. scar','','A1] sticks, stones, bats, bricks\nA2] i) Injuries made by sticks : a) break bones       b) break skins         c) ache through bones          d) can be healed \n      ii) Made by words : a) haunt           b) pierce and stick inside       c) mortify       d) leave scar on mind       e) can’t be forgotten\nA3] i) phones, loans          ii) pin, bin, tin       iii) bar, far, war','',1,0,0,NULL),(88329,436,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Truth\nSticks and stones may break my bones, \nBut words can also hurt me. \nStones and sticks break only skin,\nWhile words are ghosts that haunt me.\nSlant and curved the fall word-swords\nTo pierce and stick inside me. \nBats and bricks may ache through bones. \nBut words can mortify me.\nPain from words has left its scar \nOn mind and heart that\'s tender \nCuts and bruises now have healed;\nIt\'s words that I remember. <br>- Barrie Wade<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following web :','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following chart\n                           Difference in injuries\n        Made by sticks and stones                Made by words\n    i. -----------------------                                  a) -----------------\n    ii. ---------------------                                    b) ----------------\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Write two words that rhyming with : \n       i. bones          ii. skin           iii. scar\n<b>B]</b> Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n    i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] sticks, stones, bats, bricks\nA2] i) Injuries made by sticks : a) break bones       b) break skins         c) ache through bones          d) can be healed \n      ii) Made by words : a) haunt           b) pierce and stick inside       c) mortify       d) leave scar on mind       e) can’t be forgotten\nA3] i) phones, loans          ii) pin, bin, tin       iii) bar, far, war\nB] i) Title of the poem : Truth\nii) The poet of the poem: Barrie Wade\niii) Theme of the poem: Stones and sticks hurt us. Simi- unt larly the insulting words also hurt us. But the injuries of stones and sticks can be healed. But the word - injuries and, leave a permanent mark on our minds.\niv) Number of Stanzas : 3\n     Rhyme scheme: abcb, abab, efgf\nv) Figure of speech:\n     a) Alliteration : i) Stones and sticks break only skin.\n           ii) Sticks and stones may break my bones.\n     b) Repetition :  i) Sticks and stones\n                                ii) break my bones / skins\n      c) Metaphor  : Words are ghosts that haunt me. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(88330,437,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','A.J. Cronin has very well carved the idea of selfless help and explained the importance of dedication towards relationships in the story, The two Gentlemen of Verona. The story revolves around the two boys named Nicola and Jacopo who do numerous things to earn money only to pay for their sister\'s treatment who suffers from tuberculosis of the spine.\nThe story begins with the narrator driving down the foothills of the Alps. Here is when he first encounters the two brothers selling wild strawberries. Even after the driver forbids the narrator to buy the wild fruits, he buys the biggest basket and drives towards the town. This scene emphasizes on the first step of growing intimacy between the narrator and the two boys.\nNext day, the boys are found shining shoes in the public square. This amazes the narrator. This is when they reveal the fact that they do numerous kinds of things to earn money. Here, the narrator is shown to develop a soft corner for them in his heart. He finds them to be innocent, serious, pleasant and earnest.\nThe boys are very useful to the narrator. They are very willing to satisfy all that the narrator requires. They are also found selling newspapers, by the narrator, one stormy night. One day, when the narrator asks if he can help them in any, they say they will be very grateful if they get a lift to the nearby village Poleta. Although it doesn\'t come in the narrator\'s way he takes them there. The boys get down in a building and ask the narrator to wait in a nearby cafe, until they return within an hour.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        State whether the following statements are right or wrong. Correct the wrong statements.\n       i. The boy\'s sister is suffering from T.B.\n       ii. The narroter is driving down the foothills of the Himalaya.\n       iii. The boys are selling blackberries.\n        iv. The narrator is not a helpful person.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following web :\n','<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Find the describing words for the following.\n        i. -------things                ii. -------fruits\n        iii.-------shoes                iv. -------corner.\n<b>A4] Grammar </b>\n       i. The driver forbids the narrator to buy the wild fruits. (Add question tag)\n       ii. They do numerous kinds of things to earn money.\n            [Frame Wh- type question to get “numerous kinds of things” answer]\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Do you help others? How?','A1] i) Right\n        ii) Wrong- The narrator is driving down the foothills of the Alps.\n        iii) Wrong strawberries. The boys are selling\n        iv) Wrong - The narrator is a helpful person.\nA2] i) earnest          ii) innocent          iii) serious          iv) Pleasant\nA3] i) numerous             ii) wild      iii) shining          iv) soft\nA4] i) The driver forbids the narrator to buy the wild fruits, doesn’t he?\n       ii) What do they do to earn money?\nA5] Yes, I always help others. I help my friends in their difficult times. I help my neighbour by doing their work. Sometimes I visit an old People\'s Home near our town and spend that day with the old people.','',0,1,0,NULL),(88331,437,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The story begins with the narrator driving down the foothills of the Alps. Here is when he first encounters the two brothers selling wild strawberries. Even after the driver forbids the narrator to buy the wild fruits, he buys the biggest basket and drives towards the town. This scene emphasizes on the first step of growing intimacy between the narrator and the two boys.\nNext day, the boys are found shining shoes in the public square. This amazes the narrator. This is when they reveal the fact that they do numerous kinds of things to earn money. Here, the narrator is shown to develop a soft corner for them in his heart. He finds them to be innocent, serious, pleasant and earnest.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following web :','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following sentences.\n      i. The narrator first met the two brothers at ----------------\n      ii. The two kinds of things that the brothers did:\n            a) -------------------------          b) --------------------------\n      iii. The narrator is amazed to see -------------------\n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n       Match the following opposite words :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Words</th>\n    <th>Opposites</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. wild</td>\n    <td>a) guilty</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. buy</td>\n    <td>b) private</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. public</td>\n    <td>c) sell</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. innocent</td>\n    <td>d) tame</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A4] Grammar </b>\n       i. This amazes the narrator\n            (Begin the sentence with \'The narrator ........)\n       ii. They do numerous kinds of things to earn money.\n            (Frame a \'wh question to get ‘earn money’ part as an answer)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       What made the writer develop a soft corner for the boys in his heart?','','A1] i) innocent          ii) serious            iii) pleasant            iv) earnest\nA2] i) the foothills of the Alps. \n       ii) a) selling wild strawberries b) shining shoes \n       iii) the two brothers shining shoes in the public square. \nA3] (i – d), (ii – c), (iii – b), (iv – a)\nA4] i) The narrator is amazed by this.\n        ii) Why do they do numerous kinds of things?\nA5]  At first the writer saw the boys selling wild strawberries. Next day the boys were found shining shoes in the public square. When asked, they told the writer that they did numerous kinds of things to earn money. This developed a soft corner for the boys in the heart of the writer. ','',1,0,0,NULL),(88332,438,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">To be a successful and competent vet, \nNeeds knowledge exceedingly wide, \nFor each of the patients he\'s likely to get, \nPossesses a different inside.\nHe must know why the cat is refusing her milk,\nWhy the dog is not eating his bone,\nWhy the coat of the horse is not shining like silk,\nWhy the parrot does nothing but groan;\nWhy the ducks and the chickens are failing to lay,\nWhy so faint the canary bird sings, \nAnd if he is called to the zoo he must say \nAn incredible number of things.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following lines using the information from the extract :\n       i. One needs vast knowledge to become a -----------\n       ii. The vet finds many unbelievable things if he is called to -------------\n       iii. The ducks and chickens ------------------- ','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. The cat</td>\n    <td>a. is failing to lay</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. The dog</td>\n    <td>b. simply groans</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. The parrot</td>\n    <td>c. is refusing her milk</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. The ducks</td>\n    <td>d. is not eating his bone.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A3] Poetic Device </b>\n       Write the rhyming pairs from the extract.\n','','A1] i) competent vet              ii) the zoo          iii) have failed to lay eggs\nA2] (i – c), (ii – d), (iii – b), (iv – a)\nA3] vet-get, wide-inside, milk-silk, bone-groan, lay-say, sings-things.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(88333,438,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">“The Vet”\nTo be a successful and competent vet, \nNeeds knowledge exceedingly wide, \nFor each of the patients he\'s likely to get, \nPossesses a different inside.\nHe must know why the cat is refusing her milk,\nWhy the dog is not eating his bone,\nWhy the coat of the horse is not shining like silk,\nWhy the parrot does nothing but groan;\nWhy the ducks and the chickens are failing to lay,\nWhy so faint the canary bird sings, \nAnd if he is called to the zoo he must say \nAn incredible number of things.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following lines using the information from the extract :\n       i. One needs vast knowledge to become a -----------\n       ii. The vet finds many unbelievable things if he is called to -------------\n       iii. The ducks and chickens ------------------- ','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Match the following parts to make meaningful sentences.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. The cat</td>\n    <td>a. is failing to lay</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. The dog</td>\n    <td>b. simply groans</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. The parrot</td>\n    <td>c. is refusing her milk</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. The ducks</td>\n    <td>d. is not eating his bone.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A3] Poetic Device </b>\n       Write the rhyming pairs from the extract.\n<b>B] </b>Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) competent vet              ii) the zoo          iii) have failed to lay eggs\nA2] (i – c), (ii – d), (iii – b), (iv – a)\nA3] vet-get, wide-inside, milk-silk, bone-groan, lay-say, sings-things.\nB] i) Title of the poem - The vet \nii) Name of the poet Unknown\niii) Rhyme scheme abab, cdcd, efef\niv) Figure of speech : If the lion\'s caught a cold Hyperbole.\nv) Theme / central Idea :- This is a humourous poem. Here the poet says that a veterinarian needs exceedingly wide knowledge as his patients possess a different inside.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(88334,440,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Friendship is a Golden chain, \nThe links our friends so dear, \nAnd like a rare and precious jewel \nIt\'s treasured more each year...\nIt\'s clasped together firmly \nWith a love that\'s deep and true, \nAnd it\'s rich with happy memories \nAnd fond recollections, too... \nTime can\'t destroy its beauty \nFor as long as memory lives, \nYears can\'t erase the pleasure \nThat the joy of friendship gives...<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false :\n      i. Time can destroy the beauty of friendship.\n      ii. Friendship cannot be bought or sold.\n      iii. Friendship binds the hands together.\n      iv. Friendship is like a real rare and precious jewel.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Refer to the extract and write the reasons, why \'Friendship\' is the best gift anyone can give you.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Match the lines with figures of speech they contain\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Lines</th>\n    <th>Figures of Speech</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Friendship is a golden chain</td>\n    <td>a) Simile</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) And like a rare and precious jewel</td>\n    <td>b) Alliteration</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) For as long as memory lives</td>\n    <td>c) Metaphor</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','A1] i) False            ii) True             iii) False             iv) True\nA2] Because : i) Friendship links dear friends.\n       ii) It grows more precious as each year passes.\n       iii) It binds you together with deep and true love.\n       iv) It gives us rich and happy memories.\n       v) It can\'t destroy the beauty of friendship.\n       vi) It continues to give pleasure of friendship. \nA3] (1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – b)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(88335,440,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">“Golden Chain”\nFriendship is a Golden chain, \nThe links our friends so dear, \nAnd like a rare and precious jewel \nIt\'s treasured more each year...\nIt\'s clasped together firmly \nWith a love that\'s deep and true, \nAnd it\'s rich with happy memories \nAnd fond recollections, too... \nTime can\'t destroy its beauty \nFor as long as memory lives, \nYears can\'t erase the pleasure \nThat the joy of friendship gives... <br>- Helen Steiner Rice<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false :\n      i. Time can destroy the beauty of friendship.\n      ii. Friendship cannot be bought or sold.\n      iii. Friendship binds the hands together.\n      iv. Friendship is like a real rare and precious jewel.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Refer to the extract and write the reasons, why \'Friendship\' is the best gift anyone can give you.\n<b>A3] Poetic Device</b>\n       Match the lines with figures of speech they contain\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Lines</th>\n    <th>Figures of Speech</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Friendship is a golden chain</td>\n    <td>a) Simile</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) And like a rare and precious jewel</td>\n    <td>b) Alliteration</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) For as long as memory lives</td>\n    <td>c) Metaphor</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>B]</b> Read the poem. Write an appreciation of the poem in about 12 to 15 sentences with the help of the following points :\n     i. Title                    ii. Poet                     iii.  Rhyme scheme                   iv.  Figures of Speech                v. Theme/Central Idea (2 to 3 lines)','','A1] i) False            ii) True             iii) False             iv) True\nA2] Because : i) Friendship links dear friends.\n       ii) It grows more precious as each year passes.\n       iii) It binds you together with deep and true love.\n       iv) It gives us rich and happy memories.\n       v) It can\'t destroy the beauty of friendship.\n       vi) It continues to give pleasure of friendship. \nA3] (1 – c), (2 – a), (3 – b)\nB] i) Title of the poem :- Golden chain\nii) Name of the poetess :- Helen Steiner Rice\niii) Rhyme scheme: abcb, defe, …….\niv) Figure of speech :- Friendship is a golden chain - Metaphor.\nv) Theme / central idea: Here, in this poem, the poet tells us the very distinctive features of Friendship. It binds two kindred hearts together for years.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(88336,441,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','On 10 April 1912, the largest luxury ship in the world set sail from Southampton in England to New York. She was called the Titanic. The word \'titanic\' means \'huge\'. This was her maiden voyage, that is her first voyage. She set out to cross the Atlantic Ocean and she carried more than two thousand passengers. The Titanic was like a floating town.\nEveryone called the Titanic the finest ship in the world. Everyone was proud of her. She was as tall as a building with fifteen storeys. Inside, she was like a large hotel with grand staircases, dining-rooms, shops and swimming pools.\nEveryone thought that the Titanic could never sink. They called her the unsinkable ship. The ship\'s builders were so sure of this, that they put only a few life-boats on the ship.\nThe journey was very comfortable. The passengers were proud because they were travelling in the best ship in the world. They felt safe.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Say whether the following sentences are True of False.\n      i. The Titanic was going to New York.\n      ii. The passengers called her sinkable ship.\n      iii. There were only a few life-boats on the Titanic.\n      iv. The Titanic set out to cross the Atlantic Ocean. ','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n       Complete the following web :\n','<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        Write down four words related to sea-voyage.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. Everyone was proud of her. (Add a question tag)\n        ii. She was as tall as a building with fifteen stories.  (Change the degree)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       \'People called the Titanic the unsinkable ship\' was it true?','A1] i) True            ii) False          iii) True          iv) True\nA2] i) The Titanic was the largest luxury ship in the world. \n       ii) She çarried more than two thousand passengers. \n       iii) She was as tall as a fifteen storeys building.\n       iv) The passengers were proud to travel in the Titanic.\nA3] ship, sail, ocean, floating, passengers.\nA4] i) Everyone was proud of her, wasn\'t he?\n        ii) A building with fifteen storeys is not taller than she. \nA5] The Titanic was the largest luxury boat. It was well- built. So people thought it could never sink. But it proved false. On 14 th April it met a shipwreck and sank down.','',0,1,0,NULL),(88337,441,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The Carpathia arrived at 3.40 a.m. The ship\'s crew found the life-boats and saved seven hundred people in them. They also found chairs and pieces of wood floating on the water. But almost all of the people in the water had drowned or died of cold. The Carpathia was too late to save them.\nThe sinking of the Titanic made people think harder about the safety of passengers on ships. They asked why ships carry such few life-boats and why they turn off their radios. In 1913, it was decided that all ships must have enough room in their life-boats to carry everyone on board. It was also decided that ships must keep their radios working every hour of the day and night.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Write correct word in the blanks :\n      i. The time of the arrival of the Carpathia : ----------- \n      ii. Number of passengers saved ---------------\n      iii. Articles floating on water -----------------\n      iv. The passengers were died of -------------','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the following chart :','<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        From the passage, write down the words related to time.\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       i. The Carpathia was too late to save them. (Remove \'Too\')\n       ii. Ships must keep their radios working. \n            (Find out the modal and state its function.)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n        Why was it decided that all ships must have enough room in their life-boats to carry everyone on board?','A1] i) 3.40 am                                    ii) 700\n       iii) chairs pieces of wood          iv) cold.\nA2] i) All the ships must have enough room in their lifeboats to carry everyone on board \n       ii) The ships must keep their radios working every hour of the day and night\nA3] a.m, late, hour, day, night.\nA4] i) The Carpathia was so late that it could not save them.\n       ii) Modal - must; Function-legal obligation.  \nA5] The Titanic\'s builders thought that the Titanic could never sink. So, they put only a few life-boats on the ship. The Titanic had more than two thousand people on board. But there was only enough place in the life-boats for a few hundred passengers. If the Titanic had more life-boats, more passengers would have saved.','',0,1,0,NULL),(88338,442,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The main feature of festivals in Tripura is that all the people join in the celebrations. Many of the dances performed during some of the festivities represent hunting, fishing, food-gathering and various other activities. Some of the festivals attract people from all over India.\nNagaland consists of different groups of people who are unique in their customs and traditions and the festivities they celebrate. Songs and dances form the soul of these festivals through which their oral history is passed down. A major festival is Moastu celebrated mainly in the villages after the sowing season. Dressed in colourful clothes and headgear decorated with feathers and wild boar tusks, the villagers dance to traditional music.\nHardly a month passes in any of the seven States without a festival or two. Most of them have a religious importance or significance, as the people offer thanks to the gods for a good harvest to prosperity, or ask for protection against calamities. But these celebrations also help to encourage cultural and artistic activities and bring out the natural talent of the people.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        State whether the following statements are true or false :\n       i. In Tripura a few people join in the celebrations of festivals.\n       ii. People in these seven states pray God for their health.\n       iii. These celebrations of festival bring out the natural talents of the people.\n       iv. Songs and dances of Nagaland tell you their history orally.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following sentences using the information from the passages \n       i. The dances in Tripura festivities stand for --------------- .\n        ii. The Nagaland festival Moastu is celebrated after ---------- .\n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Match the words in Column \'A\' with their meanings in Column \'B\' :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>a) calamities</td>\n    <td>i) important</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>b) bring out</td>\n    <td>ii) importance</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>c) major</td>\n    <td>iii) disasters</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>d) significance</td>\n    <td>iv) develop and present to public</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       i. The people offer thanks to the Gods. (Rewrite using Present Continuous Tense.)\n      ii. Hardly a month passes, without a festival or two. (Rewrite using \'No\' in the sentences.)\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       What advantages do the people get by celebrating the festivals?','','A1] i) False          ii) False          iii) True            iv) True  \nA2] i) hunting, fishing, food-gathering and various activities\n      ii) sowing season\nA3] (a – iii), (b – iv),  (c – i), (d – ii)\nA4] i) The people are offering thanks to the Gods.\n       ii) Almost no month passes, without a festival or two.\nA5] The most important advantage by celebrating festivals is unity among people. They also encourage to understand their religion, culture and customs. They get opportunity to show their skills in dance and music and their talent in different arts. They can enjoy new dresses, good food and meeting their friends and relatives.  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(88339,442,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Read about how different festivals are celebrated in the north-eastern States of India.\nThe North east region of India, consisting of seven States, is a place of diverse cultures. The different communities and tribes celebrate their unique festivals with great enthusiasm and joy. Many of their festivals are based on agriculture and no celebration is complete without the traditional music and dance.\nBlessed with lush greenery and the mighty river Brahmaputra, the people of Assam have a lot to celebrate. So, Bihu is the chief festival of this State. It is celebrated by people of all religions, castes or tribes. The three different sets of Bihu mark the beginning of the harvesting season, the completion of sowing and the end of the harvest season.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Name the following :\n     i. country : --------------        ii. State : -----------------\n    iii. River : -------------             iv. Festival : --------------','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following web :','<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Match the words with their meanings : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Words</th>\n    <th>Meanings</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. diverse</td>\n    <td>a) powerful</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. unique</td>\n    <td>b) much</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. a lot</td>\n    <td>c) only one</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. mighty</td>\n    <td>d) of great variety</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. The people of Assam have a lot to celebrate. (Add a question Tag)\n        ii. It is celebrated by people of all religions.  (Begin the sentence with \'people\')\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Which festival is the chief festival in Maharashtra? When and How is it celebrated?','A1] i) India          ii) Assam         iii) Brahmaputra         iv) Bihu\nA2] i) The North-East region of India consists seven states.\n       ii) it is a place of diverse cultures. \n       iii) The different communities and tribes celebrate their festivals with joy. \nA3] (i – d), (ii – c), (iii – b), (iv – a)\nA4] i) The people of Assam have a lot to celebrate, don\'t they?\n       ii) People of all religions celebrate it. \nA5] The festival of Diwali is the chief festival in Maharashtra. It is celebrated in the month of Ashwin and Kartik. It is celebrated by the people of all castes and religions with great enthusiasm and joy.\n','',0,1,0,NULL),(88340,442,'descriptive','2',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Nagaland consists of different groups of people who are unique in their customs and traditions and the festivities they celebrate. Songs and dances form the soul of these festivals through which their oral history is passed down. A major festival is Moastu celebrated mainly in the villages after the sowing season. Dressed in colourful clothes and headgear decorated with feathers and wild boar tusks, the villagers dance to traditional music.\nHardly a month passes in any of the seven States without a festival or two. Most of them have a religious importance or significance, as the people offer thanks to the gods for a good harvest to prosperity, or ask for protection against calamities. But these celebrations also help to encourage cultural and artistic activities and bring out the natural talent of the people.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       State whether the following statements are true or false :\n      i. Every month has a festival in the seven states. \n      ii. Moasty is celebrated after the harvest season.\n      iii. Oral history of people is passed down through songs and dances.\n      iv. People in Nagaland are unique in their customs.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following web :','<b>A3] Vocabulary</b>\n        Match the pairs of opposite words :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>Words</th>\n    <th>Opposites</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. different</td>\n    <td>a) discourage</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. major</td>\n    <td>b) artificial</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. natural</td>\n    <td>c) minor</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. encouragement</td>\n    <td>d) same</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n        i. The villagers dance to traditional music.  (Add a question Tag).\n      ii. The people offer thanks to the Gods.  (Rewrite using present continuous tense)\n<b>A5] Personal Response </b>\n       How do you celebrate your birthday ?','A1] i) True         ii) False         iii) True           iv) True\nA2] i) Festivals have a religious significance. People offer thanks to the gods for good harvest and protection against calamities. \n     ii) Festivals help to encourage cultural and artistic activities. They bring out the natural talent of the people.\nA3] (i – d), (ii – c), (iii – b), (iv – a)\nA4] i) The villagers dance to traditional music, don\'t they?\n       ii) The people are offering thanks to the gods.\nA5]  I celebrate my birthday in a unique manner. Instead of cakes and candles I buy books, notebooks, pens and pencils. On my birthday I invite the children living in slum areas and distribute among them the books and note- books. Sometimes I give them sweets and chocolates also.','',0,1,0,NULL),(88341,711,'descriptive','4a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Anaemia is a major public health problem that affects women and children throughout their life. In young boys and girls, anaemia limits their growth. development, reduces their concentration, affects their learning capabilities and makes them susceptible towards infection, thereby increasing school drop-out rates, reducing their physical fitness and work productivity. \nIn girls, anaemia leads to early delivery in future, birth of low birth-weight babies, higher infant mortality rates and higher maternal mortality rates.\nAnaemia is a preventable disorder. Adding rich food items to the diet and taking iron-folic acid tablet regularly go a long way in maintaining natural haemoglobin levels and reducing prevalence of anaemia. ','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Choose the correct alternatives and complete the given sentences :\n      i. Anaemia reduces work productivity among ------------------\n         a) only women           b) young boys and girls         c) men        d) children\n     ii. Anaemia is a preventable disorder means it is ------------\n        a) avoidable                                               b) incurable      \n        c) a cause to increase mortality rate     d) unavoidable\n    iii. We can prevent anaemia by maintaining ----------- in our blood.\n          a) oxygen                                 b) hemoglobin        \n          c) red and white cells             d) vitamins\n     iv. In girls, anaemia leads to -------------------\n          a) fatness            b) dullness        c) early delivery in future      d) healthy life','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n       Complete the web : ','<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Match the words in Column ‘A’ with their antonyms in column ‘B’\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. public</td>\n    <td>a) decrease</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. increase</td>\n    <td>b) artificial</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. higher</td>\n    <td>c) private</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. natural</td>\n    <td>d) lower</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       Select a proper question tag and rewrite the sentences :\n      i. Anaemia is a preventable disorder?\n         a) does it?             b) is it?           c) isn’t it? \n     ii. Anaemia affects women and children.\n         a) is it?             b) does it?               c) doesn’t it?\n<b>A5] Personal Response </b>\n       What do you do if you are feeling unwell?','A1]  i) b) young boys and girls          ii) a) avoidable        iii) c) hemoglobin\n        iv) c) early delivery in future \nA2] leads to early delivery in future, higher infant mortality rates, low birth-weight babies, higher maternal mortality rates\nA3] 1) Public × private               2) increase × decrease\n       3) higher × lower                 4) natural × artificial \nA4] i) Anaemia is a preventable disorder, isn\'t it?\n       ii) Anaemia affects women and children, doesn\'t it?\nA5] If I am feeling unwell, I will go to doctor and take medicines first. Then I will take doses on time. I will drink boiled water and take proper rest to recover as early as possible.\n','',0,1,0,NULL),(88342,711,'descriptive','4a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj was the great founder of the Maratha Empire. He spent his childhood in the far-off regions of Maval. The young Shivaji Maharaj was moved by the sufferings of his people. As years passed he become a man well- skilled in physical and military exercises. He made up his mind to free his lands from the cruel rulers. He gathered all the \'Mavalas\' to carve a kingdom of their own and unified them under the slogan of \'Hindawi Swaraj\'. He collected some faithful friends around him. They stood by him in sun and in shower, even at the cost of their lives. Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj took possession of the fort \'Torana\' and began his career. He went on capturing all the important forts from his enemies until he had a fairly large Maratha Empire. He struggled for decades and finally achieved his aim. was finally crowned \'Chhatrapati\' in 1674.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following sentences choosing from the correct alternatives given below:\n       i. The young Shivaji was moved by the ----- of his people.\n           a) hunger      b) sufferings      c) poverty       d) deaths\n       ii. Shivaji began his career by taking possession of the fort -------------\n            a) Raigad          b) Sinhagad          c) Pratapgad      d) Torana\n        iii. Shivaji was crowned \'Chhatrapati\' in -------------\n              a) 1647         b) 1674         c) 1646                 d) 1676.\n         iv. Shivaji was the -------------- of Maratha Empire.\n              a) builder      b) creator         c) founder         d) fighter','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question</b>\n        Arrange the following sentences describing the career of Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj, in order of their occurrence in the passage :\n      i. He collected \'Mavalas\', captured the fort Torana and began his career.\n      ii. He was crowned \'Chhatrapati\'\n      iii. Shivaji Maharaj made up his mind to free his land from the cruel rulers.\n      iv. He founded a fairly large Maratha Empire. \n<b>A3] Vocabulary</b> \n       Match the pair \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color: #e6f3ff; text-align: center;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) carve a kingdom</td>\n    <td>a) to feel sorry for</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) stand by</td>\n    <td>b) to decide firmly</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) make up one’s mind</td>\n    <td>c) to support</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4) be moved by</td>\n    <td>d) to establish a nation</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       i. Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj took possession of the fort \'Torana\'. \n           The correct sentence in past perfect is -----------------\n          a) Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj has taken possession of the fort \'Torana\'.\n         b) Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj had taken possession of the fort \'Torana\'.\n          c) Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj had took possession of the fort \"Torana\'.\n     ii. He was finally crowned \'Chhatrapati\' in 1674. This sentence is in ----------\n        a) Active voice.         b) Passive voice.        c) Neither active nor passive voice.\n<b>A5] Personal Response </b>\n       Write four sentences about any freedom fighter you like. ','','A1] i) suffering              ii) Torana              iii) 1674             iv) founder\nA2] iii, i, iv, ii\nA3] (1 – d), (2 – c), (3 – b), (4 – a)\nA4] i) Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj had taken possession of the fort \'Torana\'.\n       ii) Passive voice.\nA5] Bhagat Singh is considered to be one of the most courageous martyrs of the Indian freedom struggle. He grew up in a patriotic atmosphere. At an early age, he started dreaming of uprooting the British empire. He sacrificed his life for our country. So we should be proud of him.','',0,0,0,NULL),(88343,711,'descriptive','4a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Banana is a wholesome food. It is made up of water (75%), sugar (20%), proteins (2%), fats (1.5%), cellulose (1%), and other substances (1%). It also contains vitamins such as A, C, B1 and B2. It is quite a nourishing and easily digestible fruit. Half a kilo bananas contain 460 calories and are more nourishing than any other fruit. A type of flour is also made of dried bananas. In American and Asian kitchens bananas are used uncooked in salads and with ice creams. In Africa people thatch roofs of their huts with the banana leaves. The plant yields a kind of fibre which is used to make ropes.\nThere are over 200 varieties of banana belonging to about 30 species. They are never grown from the seed but are produced by planting of rhizome pieces. The new plants grow up amazingly fast from the buds. Its height varies between 5 to 30 ft. Each plant has about 10 large leaves and bears a single bunch of fruit. The banana leaves are about 6 to 7 ft. long and 1 to 2 ft. wide.\nToday, the United States is the greatest buyer of bananas, consuming more than 60 million bunches every year.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question </b>\n       Choose the correct alternative for the following questions :\n     i. How many calories does half a kilo bananas contain ?\n        a) 560         b) 250         c) 300       d) 460\n    ii. What types of bananas are used in American and Asian kitchens ?\n        a) Roasted        b) Uncooked        c) Boiled         d) Fried\n    iii. How many varieties of banana are there?\n         a) over 200      b) over 500          c) over 270        d) over 325\n     iv. How many leaves does each banana plant have?\n          a) about 15        b) about 12      c) about 16        d) about 10','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following web diagram.\n','<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n       Find out from the passage opposite words of the following:\n       i. slow          ii. uncooked       iii. indigestible            iv. short\n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       i. The United States is the greatest buyer.\n           (Choose the correct positive degree from the following alternatives.)\n          a) No other buyer is as great as the United States.\n         b) The United States is greater buyer than any other buyers.\n          c) Very few buyers are as great as the United States.\n      ii. Choose the correct comparative degree from the following alternatives : \n           a) The United States is greater buyer.\n           b) The United States is greater buyer than any other buyers.\n            c) The United States is more greater than any other buyer.\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n        Write four sentences about your favourite fruit.','A1] i) 460             ii) uncooked             iii) over 200          iv) about 10\nA2] used in salads and ice creams, nourishing fruit, fibre used for making ropes, roofs of huts\nA3] i) slow × fast           ii) uncooked × cooked \n      iii) indigestible × digestible           iv) short × long\nA4] i) a) No other buyer is as great as the United States.\n       ii) b) The United States is greater buyer than any other buyers.\nA5] An apple is my favourite fruit. It contains iron, fibre and vitamin C. It is low in calories and no fat and cholesterol. So, it is one of the healthiest foods a person can eat. It is also very tasty.','',0,1,0,NULL),(88344,711,'descriptive','4a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','The tea we take, comes from a plant of the same name. It is in the form of a leaf to the plant. The plant rises to the height of about 12 feet. But in tea gardens it is not allowed to be higher than four to five feet. The tea leaf is evergreen and is oval in shape with a pointed end. The flower of the plant is white in colour. Seeds of the tea plant are first sown in a nursery, properly ploughed and harrowed. When the plants grow two feet high, they are uprooted from the nursery and transplanted in rows. Hilly soil where rainwater cannot stagnate, is suitable for tea plantation. In India tea plants are grown in the hilly tracts of Assam, Darjeeling district of West Bengal and some parts of Nilgiri hill tracks of South India. Tea is grown in China, Sri Lanka. Japan and some other countries.\nThe tea plant is pruned every year. The new leaves and buds are gathered and taken to the factory where tea is made. These are assorted and packed in wooden boxes and sent to the market for sale. Tea is one of the most favourite drinks in the world.','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       State whether the following statements are True or False:\n     i. Tea is a favourite drink only in India.\n     ii. The tea leaf is oval in shape.\n     iii. The tree rises to the height of about 10 feet.\n     iv. Tea is grown on plains in muddy soil.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n        Complete the following table :\n      <table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n    <th>Leaf of Tea</th>\n    <th>Countries beside India which grow tea</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) ----------------</td>\n    <td>i) ------------------------------------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) ----------------</td>\n    <td>ii) ------------------------------------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n        Find four parts of the tea plant from the following word puzzle and write them. \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <td>W</td><td>F</td><td>L</td><td>O</td><td>W</td><td>E</td><td>R</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>D</td><td>B</td><td>E</td><td>S</td><td>E</td><td>E</td><td>D</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>B</td><td>U</td><td>A</td><td>D</td><td>U</td><td>B</td><td>C</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>L</td><td>D</td><td>F</td><td>C</td><td>B</td><td>U</td><td>W</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>A4] Grammar </b>\n        Tea is one of the most favourite drinks in the world.\n        Complete the following sentences with the help of the above sentence :\n       i. Very few drinks in ----------------\n       ii. Tea is more favourite than ---------------\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n        What do you like to drink when you feel tired? Why?','','A1] i) False            ii) True           iii) False              iv) False\nA2]  Leaf of Tea                    Countries beside India which grow tea\n      1) Evergreen                   i) China\n      2) Oval shaped               ii) Sri Lanka\nA3] 1) BUD      2) FLOWER       3) SEED          4) LEAF\nA4] i) Very few drinks in the world are as favourite as tea.\n      ii) Tea is more favourite than most other drinks in the world.\nA5] I usually take hot milk when I feel tired, because milk is an energetic drink. It gives me energy as it contains important nutrients like calcium, protein, vitamins, etc. Hot milk makes me fresh, too ','',0,0,0,NULL),(88345,711,'descriptive','4a',10,2,'Out of Text Book','Man has always felt a need to express himself. In ancient times, before paper was invented, man used to inscribe his thoughts on stones or walls. Later, he began using the leaves of the palm and the lotus to write. The art of printing was invented in 1454. Soon it became popular all over the world. Knowledge could now reach even the common man. Thousands of editions of any book became available for anyone who wanted them.\nThe latest invention, the E-book, has brought about a great change in the field of printing. The E-book is an electronic book. It is a book printed in digital form. You can read an E-book on a computer, laptop or smartphone. The E-book needs neither paper nor ink. The CDs of the E-books have a life of many years. The same E-book can be made available in different languages. In the future, there will be libraries of E-books. What a wonderful invention the E-book is!','<b>A1] Simple Factual Question</b>\n       Arrange the following sentences in order of their occurrence in the passage      \n     i. Man began to use the leaves of the palm and the lotus to write.\n    ii. The E-book brought about a great change in the field of printing.\n    iii. The art of printing was invented and books were available.\n    iv. Paper was invented and writing on paper began.','<b>A2] Complex Factual Question </b>\n      Complete the following web diagram','<b>A3] Vocabulary </b>\n       Find out opposite words for the following:\n      i. modern       ii. different        iii. never         iv. uncommon \n<b>A4] Grammar</b>\n       i. What a wonderful invention E-book is!\n           The assertive sentence of this sentence is\n       a) E-book is really a wonderful invention.\n       b) E-book was a very wonderful invention.\n        c) E-book has been a wonderful invention.\n    2) E-book has brought a great change.\n        The exclamatory sentence of this sentence is\n        a) How great change E-book has brought!\n       b) What a great change E-book has brought!\n       c) What a great E-book brought change!\n<b>A5] Personal Response</b>\n       Do you like to read books? Why? \n<b>B] </b>Write a summary of the passage given for comprehension :','A1] i, iv, iii, ii\nA2] i) One can read an E – book on a computer, laptop or smartphone\n      ii) The CDs of the E – books have a life of many years.\n      iii) It needs neither paper nor ink.\n      iv) The E – book is made available in different languages.\nA3] i) ancient           ii) same            iii) always            iv) common\nA4] i) E-book is really a wonderful invention.\n       ii) What a great change E-book has brought!\nA5] Yes, I like to read different kinds of books, magazines and newspapers because it gives me information of every corner of the world. It develops my knowledge, vocabulary and language. It is also a good source of entertainment.\nB]          Advantages of E-book\n     A Man has invented different ways of expressions; writing is one of them. Man used to express his thoughts on stones, walls, leaves of palm and lotus. Then he invented the art of printing in 1454, so that knowledge could reach the common man in the form of books. Recently he has invented E-book which is available in A digital form and can be read on a computer, laptop and mobile. It saves paper. Nowadays these books are available on CDs in different languages. E-books will be soon available in library in future.\n','',0,1,0,NULL),(88346,575,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Sleep, baby, sleep ! \nYour father herds his sheep: \nYour mother shakes the little tree \nFrom which fall pretty dreams on thee; \nSleep, baby, sleep !\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nThe heavens are white with sheep : \nFor they are lambs- those stars so bright: \nAnd the moon\'s shepherd of the night<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1) Complete the following sentences.</b>\n     i. Mother asks her baby to ------------\n     ii. Pretty dreams fall from -------------\n     iii. The stars are ----------------\n     iv. The moon is ---------------','<b>2) Complete the following table </b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th colspan=\"2\">The work that baby’s parents do ---------------</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Father :</td>\n    <td>--------------------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Mother :</td>\n    <td>--------------------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>3) Find out four adjectives from the extract.</b>','','1) i) sleep           ii) the little tree         iii) very bright         iv) the shepherd of the night\n2) i) Father : herds his sleep\n     ii) Mother – shakes the little tree\n3) little, pretty, white, bright\n','',0,0,0,NULL);
INSERT INTO `ep_questions` VALUES (88348,575,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Sleep, baby, sleep! \nAnd bleat not like a sheep, \nOr else the shepherd\'s angry dog\nWill come and bite my naughty rogue; \nSleep, baby, sleep!\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nGo out and herd, the sheep,\nGo out, you barking black dog, go\nAnd waken not my baby so;\nSleep, baby, sleep!<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1) A) Complete the sentences. </b>\n          i. If the baby doesn’t sleep ----\n   B] Have you noticed the changed mood of the mother in the stanza?','<b>2) Complete the following table.</b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <td rowspan=\"2\">Mother asks</td>\n    <td>The baby to -----------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>The dog not to -----------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>3) Write down to pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','A] the shepherds angry dog will bite it.\n     B] Yes, the mother’s changed mood is clearly visible. Earlier the mother warns the baby that the dog will bite it. But here she orders the dog to go out and let the baby sleep quietly.\n2) i) Mother asks the baby to go out and herd the sheep.\n    ii) She asks the dog not to waken her baby.\n3) i) sleep – sheep          ii) go – so \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(88349,575,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Sleep, baby, sleep ! \nYour father herds his sheep: \nYour mother shakes the little tree \nFrom which fall pretty dreams on thee; \nSleep, baby, sleep !\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nThe heavens are white with sheep : \nFor they are lambs- those stars so bright: \nAnd the moon\'s shepherd of the night<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1) Complete the following sentences.</b>\n     i. Mother asks her baby to ------------\n     ii. Pretty dreams fall from -------------\n     iii. The stars are ----------------\n     iv. The moon is ---------------','<b>2) Complete the following table </b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th colspan=\"2\">The work that baby’s parents do ---------------</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Father :</td>\n    <td>--------------------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Mother :</td>\n    <td>--------------------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>3) Find out four adjectives from the extract.</b>','','1) i) sleep           ii) the little tree         iii) very bright         iv) the shepherd of the night\n2) i) Father : herds his sleep\n     ii) Mother – shakes the little tree\n3) little, pretty, white, bright','',0,0,0,NULL),(88350,575,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">Sleep, baby, sleep! \nAnd bleat not like a sheep, \nOr else the shepherd\'s angry dog\nWill come and bite my naughty rogue; \nSleep, baby, sleep!\nSleep, baby, sleep ! \nGo out and herd, the sheep,\nGo out, you barking black dog, go\nAnd waken not my baby so;\nSleep, baby, sleep!<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1) A) Complete the sentences. </b>\n          i. If the baby doesn’t sleep ----\n   B] Have you noticed the changed mood of the mother in the stanza?','<b>2) Complete the following table.</b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <td rowspan=\"2\">Mother asks</td>\n    <td>The baby to -----------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>The dog not to -----------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>3) Write down to pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','A] the shepherds angry dog will bite it.\n     B] Yes, the mother’s changed mood is clearly visible. Earlier the mother warns the baby that the dog will bite it. But here she orders the dog to go out and let the baby sleep quietly.\n2) i) Mother asks the baby to go out and herd the sheep.\n    ii) She asks the dog not to waken her baby.\n3) i) sleep – sheep          ii) go – so ','',0,0,0,NULL),(88351,577,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','The famous physics professor Stephen Hawking has warned that mankind is facing the most dangerous time in its history today. He said that the world today is facing huge challenges like climate change, overpopulation, epidemic diseases. \'We have the technology to destroy the planet on which we live and have not yet developed the ability to escape it. Perhaps in a few hundred years from now, we will have built colonies amid the stars, but right now, we have only one planet and we need to work together to protect it.\' Hawking wrote recently in the Guardian newspaper.','<b>1) Complete the following web </b>','<b>2) From the passage find out the words that are related to astronomy.</b>\n<b>3) Find out the adjective forms of :</b>\n     a) danger           b) fame','','1) i) Climate change           ii) overpopulation       iii) epidemic diseases \n2) i) world         ii) climate         iii) planet          iv) stars\n3) a) dangerous          b) famous\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(88352,577,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','The famous physics professor Stephen Hawking has warned that mankind is facing the most dangerous time in its history today. He said that the world today is facing huge challenges like climate change, overpopulation, epidemic diseases. \'We have the technology to destroy the planet on which we live and have not yet developed the ability to escape it. Perhaps in a few hundred years from now, we will have built colonies amid the stars, but right now, we have only one planet and we need to work together to protect it.\' Hawking wrote recently in the Guardian newspaper.','<b>1) Complete the following web.</b>','<b>2) Find out the past tense of:</b>\n      a) write               b) build\n<b>3) What should we do to save our planet?</b>','','1) Stephen Hawking has warned us that we are e facing the most dangerous time today. Our technology will destroy our earth And we have not the ability to escape it.\n2) a) wrote           b) built\n3) To save our planet we should :\n     i) plant more and more trees.\n     ii) avoid excessive use of natural resources.\n     iii) ban plastic','',1,0,0,NULL),(88353,577,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Hundreds of people flock every hour to Ralewadi, a tiny settlement near Ambegaon. They wait in long winding queues in the hot sun to get a \'special\' powder from Miribaba who claims that it cures all sorts of diseases. According to him, he has developed this powder from a special herb in the Himalayas. \nUnwilling to give the exact name and source of the herb, he only says that it was revealed to him. He offers it at Rs 25 a packet. Each packet is specially \'blessed\' by him. Some of his followers claim that they have seen him euring thousands of people with his powder. However, Dr Karnik of Ambegaon Civil Hospital says that the number of patients in their hospital has in fact increased in the last few weeks.','<b>1) Complete the following web </b>','<b>2) Frame \'wh questions\' to get ‘sorts of diseases’ words by answers.</b>\n     It cures all sorts of diseases.\n<b>3) What shows that Miribaba\'s claim is unreliable?</b>','','Special powder: Miribaba claims that his special powder cures all sort of diseases. He has developed it from a special herb in the Himalayas. He offers it at Rs. 25 a packet.\n2) What does it cure?\n3) Miribaba claims that his special powder cures all sorts of diseases. As a result of his claim, the number of patients should have decreased. But Dr. Karnik points out the that number of patients has increased. It shows that Miribaba\'s claim is unreliable.','',1,0,0,NULL),(88354,579,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','When I called upon my friend, Mr Sherlock Holmes, he was conversing with a visitor, who was an elderly man with fiery red hair.\nHolmes introduced me to the visitor, Mr Jabez Wilson. He was a pawn-broker. Holmes asked him to repeat his story for me. He began by showing us an advertisement in a newspaper. It read as follows-\nTO THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE\nThere is a vacancy for a member of the League, and the salary is four pounds a week for nominal services. Red-headed men may apply in person on Monday, at eleven o\'clock. to Duncan Ross, at the office of the League, 7 Fleet Street.\nThe advertisement had appeared in newspaper two months ago. Mr Wilson\'s assistant, Vincent Spaulding, had shown it to him. Mr Wilson liked his assistant Spaulding. He was smart, efficient and worked for only half the normal wages! But, the assistant also had his faults. Every now and then, he left work and went down into the cellar to develop photographs. Photography was his hobby.','<b>1) Say whether the following sentences are true or false.</b>\n     i. Sherlock Holmes is narrating the story.\n     ii. Mr. Jabez Wilson was a pawn-broker.\n     iii. The office of the Red -headed League is on Fleet street.\n     iv. Mr. Wilson liked his assistant.','<b>2) Match the following words with their meanings</b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Words</th>\n    <th>Meanings</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Converse</td>\n    <td>a. wages</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. fiery</td>\n    <td>b. speak</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. repeat</td>\n    <td>c. like fire</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. salary</td>\n    <td>d. says again</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>3) Identify the tense of the following sentences</b>\n     i. He was conversing with a visitor.\n     ii. Vincent Spaulding had shown it to him.','','1)  i) False           ii) True            iii) True            iv) True\n2) (1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – d), (4 – a)\n3) i) Continuous present tense           ii) perfect past tense\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(88355,579,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','When I called upon my friend, Mr Sherlock Holmes, he was conversing with a visitor, who was an elderly man with fiery red hair.\nHolmes introduced me to the visitor, Mr Jabez Wilson. He was a pawn-broker. Holmes asked him to repeat his story for me. He began by showing us an advertisement in a newspaper. It read as follows-\nTO THE RED-HEADED LEAGUE\nThere is a vacancy for a member of the League, and the salary is four pounds a week for nominal services. Red-headed men may apply in person on Monday, at eleven o\'clock. to Duncan Ross, at the office of the League, 7 Fleet Street.\nThe advertisement had appeared in newspaper two months ago. Mr Wilson\'s assistant, Vincent Spaulding, had shown it to him. Mr Wilson liked his assistant Spaulding. He was smart, efficient and worked for only half the normal wages! But, the assistant also had his faults. Every now and then, he left work and went down into the cellar to develop photographs. Photography was his hobby.','<b>1) Complete the following web.</b>','<b>2) Why was Fleet street full of red – headed people?</b>\n<b>3) There is a vacancy for a teacher in your school Make an advertisement for it.</b>','','1) Vincent Spaulding was Mr. Wilson\'s assistant. He was smart, efficient. He worked for only half the normal wages. Photography was his hobby.\n2) Fleet street was full of red-headed people because there was vacancy for a red handed person.\n3) New English school, Pune \n     Wanted Teacher\n      Sub - English\n      Interested candidates may apply to \n       Headmaster, New English School, Pune.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(88356,580,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">When I quarrel with a friend, \nI am wild and naughty. \nWhen you quarrel with a neighbour, \nyou\'re only doing your duty.\nSo, I am always wrong \nand you are always right. \nOh, why do people grow up \nand spoil a child\'s delight?<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Complete the following table.</b>','<b>2] What blame does the child put on the grow-ups?</b>\n<b>3] From the extract, find out a pair of opposite words.</b>','','i) a friend                   ii) a neighbour \n2] The child blames that the grown-ups grow up only to spoil a child\'s delight.\n3] wrong x right','',1,0,0,NULL),(88357,580,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">When I quarrel with a friend, \nI am wild and naughty. \nWhen you quarrel with a neighbour, \nyou\'re only doing your duty.\nSo, I am always wrong \nand you are always right. \nOh, why do people grow up \nand spoil a child\'s delight?<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Complete the following table.</b>','<b>2] What blame does the child put on the grow-ups?</b>\n<b>3] From the extract, find out a pair of opposite words.</b>','','i) a friend                   ii) a neighbour \n2] The child blames that the grown-ups grow up only to spoil a child\'s delight.\n3] wrong x right','',1,0,0,NULL),(88358,580,'descriptive','3b',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">When I quarrel with a friend, \nI am wild and naughty. \nWhen you quarrel with a neighbour, \nyou\'re only doing your duty.\nSo, I am always wrong \nand you are always right. \nOh, why do people grow up \nand spoil a child\'s delight?<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Complete the following table.</b>','<b>2] What blame does the child put on the grow-ups?</b>\n<b>3] From the extract, find out a pair of opposite words.</b>','','i) a friend                   ii) a neighbour \n2] The child blames that the grown-ups grow up only to spoil a child\'s delight.\n3] wrong x right\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(88359,581,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','\"Come, Baby, Wake up! We will go ant- eating today!\" Mama Pangolin gave a gentle loving push to her one-month old baby, with her long tapering mouth. Baby was still drowsy after a day\'s sleep. Mother tried to persuade him, \"You keep asking me to take you out; you say you are tired of drinking milk all the time. Now get up, get ready.\"\n\"No! Won\'t!\" said Baby, stretching his long tail lazily, \"I don\'t want to walk all the way!\"\n\"Don\'t worry,\" said Mama, \"I\'ll carry you on my tail. Good boy, come on, it\'s time!\"\nMama set him on her tail and finally brought him out of the home. Their home was a snug hole in the earth, nine inches wide and more than a metre long. Mama cautioned Baby to sit tight and hold on to her scales with his claws. She began to walk steadily and cautiously. Just then, a porcupine went past them.\n','<b>1] Who said to whom.</b>\n    i. \"I don\'t want to walk all the way\"\n    ii. \"Now get up, get ready\"','<b>2] From the passage find out the words related to time</b>\n<b>3] Complete the following table</b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Words</th>\n    <th>Present</th>\n    <th>Past</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1.</td>\n    <td>give</td>\n    <td>....................</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2.</td>\n    <td>try</td>\n    <td>....................</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3.</td>\n    <td>drink</td>\n    <td>....................</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4.</td>\n    <td>take</td>\n    <td>....................</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','i) Baby Panglin to his mother.\n    ii) Mother Pangolin to her baby.\n2) today, day, month\n3) i) gave       ii) tried           iii) drank            iv) took','',0,0,0,NULL),(88360,582,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Our frigate wanted to go back, but the unearthly animal came at us with a speed double our own. We gasped. More stunned than afraid, we stood mute and motionless. The animal caught up with us, played with us. It made a full circle around the frigate and wrapped us in sheets of electricity that were like luminous dust. At any instant it could have dashed against our ship.\nMeanwhile I was surprised to see that our warship was fleeing, not fighting. I commented on this to Commander Farragut. His face, ordinarily so emotionless, showed great astonishment.\n\"Professor Aronnax,\" he answered me, \"I don\'t know what kind of fearsome creature I\'m up against, and I don\'t want my frigate running foolish risks in all this darkness. Besides, how should we attack this unknown creature, how should we defend ourselves against it? Let\'s wait for daylight and then we\'ll play a different role.\"','<b>1] Complete the following web </b>','<b>2] Why was the warship fleeing and not fighting?</b>\n<b>3] Find out the verb in the following sentence and say whether it is transitive or intransitive. </b>\n             We will play a different role.','','1) The unearthly animal caught up with them\n     ii) Played with them                 iii) It made a full circle round the frigate. \n     iv) It wrapped the frigate in sheets of electricity.\n2] The commander of the frigate did not want to run foolish risks in the darkness. He decided to wait for daylight. So, the frigate was not fighting but fleeing.\n3] Verb phrase = will play; Transitive verb','',1,0,0,NULL),(88361,582,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Our frigate wanted to go back, but the unearthly animal came at us with a speed double our own. We gasped. More stunned than afraid, we stood mute and motionless. The animal caught up with us, played with us. It made a full circle around the frigate and wrapped us in sheets of electricity that were like luminous dust. At any instant it could have dashed against our ship.\nMeanwhile I was surprised to see that our warship was fleeing, not fighting. I commented on this to Commander Farragut. His face, ordinarily so emotionless, showed great astonishment.\n\"Professor Aronnax,\" he answered me, \"I don\'t know what kind of fearsome creature I\'m up against, and I don\'t want my frigate running foolish risks in all this darkness. Besides, how should we attack this unknown creature, how should we defend ourselves against it? Let\'s wait for daylight and then we\'ll play a different role.\"','<b>1] Answer the following </b>\n     i) What is the name of narrator?\n    ii) At what time does this event take place?','<b>2] Match the words with their meanings.</b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Words</th>\n    <th>Meanings</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. mute</td>\n    <td>a. run away</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. motionless</td>\n    <td>b. cover</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. wrap</td>\n    <td>c. silent</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. flee</td>\n    <td>d. not moving</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n<b>3] Why was the narrator surprised to see that their warship was fleeing?</b>','','1] i) Professor Aronnax               ii) At night\n2] (1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\n3] The narrator\'s ship was a warship. It was meant to fight and not to flee. But when the narrator noticed that the warship was not fighting against the unearthly animal, he was surprised.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(88362,583,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Match the following </b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. sweet</td>\n    <td>a. bee</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. shining</td>\n    <td>b. food</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. busy</td>\n    <td>c. hour</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. opening</td>\n    <td>d. flower</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','<b>2] Answer the following questions </b>\n     i. Why is the bee called busy?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n<b>3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – a), (4 – d)\n2] i) The bee is called busy because she gathers honey all the day.\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',0,0,0,NULL),(88363,583,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Match the parts to make meaningful sentences </b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. The busy bee</td>\n    <td>a. with the sweet food</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. She gathers honey</td>\n    <td>b. skillfully</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. She builds her cell</td>\n    <td>c. improves each shining hour</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. She stores the cell</td>\n    <td>d. from every opening flower</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','<b>2] Answer the following questions</b>\n     i. what is the bee’s cell stored with?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n<b>3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\n2] i) The bee’s cell is stored with sweet food (honey)\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',0,0,0,NULL),(88364,583,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Match the following </b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. sweet</td>\n    <td>a. bee</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. shining</td>\n    <td>b. food</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. busy</td>\n    <td>c. hour</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. opening</td>\n    <td>d. flower</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','<b>2] Answer the following questions </b>\n     i. Why is the bee called busy?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n<b>3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','(1 – b), (2 – c), (3 – a), (4 – d)\n2] i) The bee is called busy because she gathers honey all the day.\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',0,0,0,NULL),(88365,583,'descriptive','3a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','<div style=\"column-count:2;\">How doth the little busy bee \nImprove each shining hour, \nAnd gather honey all the day \nFrom every opening flower.\nHow skilfully she builds her cell; \nHow neat she spreads her wax, \nAnd labours hard to store it well \nWith the sweet food she makes.<div style=\"column-count:2;\">','<b>1] Match the parts to make meaningful sentences </b>\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. The busy bee</td>\n    <td>a. with the sweet food</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. She gathers honey</td>\n    <td>b. skillfully</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. She builds her cell</td>\n    <td>c. improves each shining hour</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. She stores the cell</td>\n    <td>d. from every opening flower</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','<b>2] Answer the following questions</b>\n     i. what is the bee’s cell stored with?\n     ii. How does the bee build her cell?\n<b>3] Find out two pairs of rhyming words.</b>','','(1 – c), (2 – d), (3 – b), (4 – a)\n2] i) The bee’s cell is stored with sweet food (honey)\n     ii) The bee builds her cell very skillfully.\n3] i) hour – flower             ii) cell – well ','',0,0,0,NULL),(88366,584,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','Every afternoon, as they were coming from school, the children used to go and play in the giant\'s garden.\nIt was a large lovely garden with soft green grass. Here and there over the grass stood beautiful flowers like stars, and there were twelve peach trees that in the springtime broke out into delicate blossoms of pink and pearl, and in the autumn bore rich fruit. The birds sat on the trees and sang so sweetly that the children used to stop their games in order to listen to them. \"How happy we are!\" they cried to each other.\nOne day the giant came back. He had been to visit his friend the Cornish Ogre, and had stayed with him for seven years. After the seven years he determined to return to his own castle. When he arrived he saw the children playing in the garden.','<b>1] Complete the following web </b>','<b>2] From the extract, find out the opposites of:</b>\n     i. poor             ii. unhappy\n<b>3] Where do you play? Which games?</b>','','1] The giant\'s garden was very large and lovely. There was green grass everywhere. There were peach trees with rich fruit. Birds sing sweet songs.\n2] i) rich             ii) happy\n3] We play on the playground of our school. we play the games like kho – kho, kabbadi and cricket.','',1,0,0,NULL),(88367,584,'descriptive','2a',5,2,'Out of Text Book','So, he crept downstairs and opened the front door quite softly, and went out into the garden. But when the children saw him they were so frightened that they all ran away, and the garden became winter again. Only the little boy did not run for his eyes were so full of tears that he did not see the giant coming. And the giant stole up behind him and took him gently in his hand, and put him up into the tree. And the tree broke at once into blossom, and the birds came and sang on it, and the little boy stretched out his two arms and flung them round the giant\'s neck, and kissed him. And the other children, when they saw that the giant was not wicked any longer, came running back, and with them came the Spring. \"It is your garden now, little children,\" said the giant, and he took a great axe and knocked down the wall. And when the people were going to market at twelve o\'clock they found the giant playing with the children in the most beautiful garden they had ever seen.','<b>1] Complete the following web</b>','<b>2] The giant knocked down the wall. [Find out the subject and object]</b>\n<b>3] What do your prefer summer or winter? Why?</b>','','1] The trees broke into blossom. The birds came and sing on the tree. All the children came running back. And with them, came the spring.\n2] Subject = the giant object - the wall.\n3] I like winter because winter is wonder season. It is a season of enthusiasm and energy. It is a harvest season in Maharashtra.','',1,0,0,NULL),(90213,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Recreational land use is use of land for ……………\n(a) garden    (b) farming    (c) industry    (d) rail route\n','','','a) garden','',0,0,0,NULL),(90214,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a planned city in India?\n(a) Seoul    (b) Zurich    (c) Chandigarh    (d) Brasilia\n','','','c) Chandigarh','',0,0,0,NULL),(90215,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Of which city are Bandra, Bhandup, etc. suburbs?\n(a) Pune    (b) Nagpur    (c) Nashik    (d) Mumbai\n','','','d) Mumbai','',0,0,0,NULL),(90216,1993,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Mining is not type of land use.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement : Mining is a type of land use.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90217,1993,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','There are factories in the Central Business District.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement: There are many shops, banks and offices in the Central Business District.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90218,1993,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','In urban areas, the largest area is used for residential purposes.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(90219,1993,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The village attendant issues the 7/12 extract.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement : The village talathi issues the 7/12 extract.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90220,1993,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','In rural areas, residential areas occupy large tracts.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement : In rural areas, residential areas occupy small tracts.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90221,1993,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Extract 7 indicates Record of Rights.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(90222,1993,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Extract 12 indicates change in ownership.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect statement : Extract 12 indicates tracts under cultivation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90223,1993,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Government policies affect the land use.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90224,1993,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The areas of mixed land use are found in cities.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90225,1993,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by land use?','','','Land use is the way or purpose for which land in a particular region is used.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90226,1993,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called fallow land?','','','The agricultural land which is temporarily not in use is called fallow land.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90227,1993,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called forest land?','','','The land which is reserved for forest area is called forest land.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90228,1993,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which products are obtained from forest areas?','','','The products like firewood, flowers, fruits, gum, grass, honey, etc. are obtained from forest areas.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90229,1993,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called grassland?','','','The land owned by Village Panchayat or the government and which is used for grazing is called grassland.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90230,1993,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by commercial land use?','','','Commercial land use is the use of land in urban areas for commercial purpose.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90231,1993,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by mixed land use?','','','Mixed land use is the use of land in urban areas for more than one purpose at a time.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90232,1993,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Why is agriculture important in rural land use?','','','1) In India, agriculture is the main occupation of most of people in rural areas.\n(2) In India, farming occupation is very important as it satisfies the need of food grain and provides raw material to industries.\n(3) The maximum land in rural areas is used for carrying out agricultural activities. Therefore, agriculture is important in rural land use.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90233,1993,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','State the factors affecting land use.','','','A) The factors affecting rural land use : \n1) The factors like climatic conditions, fertility of soil, altitude and slope of land, irrigational facilities, natural resources, government policies, etc. affect rural land use.\n(2) For example, the land with gentle slope is not used for residential purpose, but is used for step farming.\n(B) The factors affecting urban land use : (1) The factors like location of land, natural resources, housing development policies of government, transportation routes, industrialisation, trade and commerce, entertainment facilities, government policies, etc. affect urban land use.\n(2) For example, the land site near the railway station, market, etc. is mostly used for commercial purposes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90234,1993,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Clarify the differences between rural and urban land use.','','','The differences between rural and urban land use are as follows:\n(1) Land in rural areas is used for limited purposes. On the other hand, land in urban areas is used for many purposes.\n(2) The pattern of rural land use is comparatively simple. On the other hand, the pattern of urban land use is comparatively complex.\n(3) Use of land for cultivation is the major form of rural land use. On the other hand, use of land for commercial activities and residence are the major forms of urban land use.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90235,1993,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Differentiate between 7/12 extract and property card.','','','The differences between 7/12 extract and property card are as follows :\n(1) 7/12 extract is issued by public revenue department of the government. On the other\nhand, property card is issued by city survey office of government.\n(2) 7/12 extract is the land record of ownership and history of ownership of the land in rural areas. Property card is the land record of ownership and history of ownership of the land in urban areas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90236,1993,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the pattern of land use get developed?','','','1) The pattern of land use gets developed through the continuous interactions between man and geographical factors.\n(2) The interaction between the factors like availability of land, quality of land, human needs, etc. leads to the development of specific pattern of land use.\n(3) For example, mineral rich land is used for carrying out mining activity.\n(4) For example, fertile piece of land is used for carrying out farming activity, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90237,1993,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about residential land use in rural areas.','','','1) The density of population is found to be low in rural areas.\n(2) As population in rural areas is comparatively limited, the residential land use in rural areas is found to be limited.\n(3) In rural areas, the land close to the areas of main occupations is used for settlements.\n(4) For example, farmers\' residences are found near farms, fishermen\'s settlements are found near coastal areas, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90238,1993,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about features of transitional areas.','','','The following are the features of transitional areas :\n(1) The intermediate areas between rural settlements and urban settlements are known as transitional areas.\n(2) The blend of rural and urban land use and culture is found in transitional areas.\n(3) Over time, transitional areas are\nconverted into urban areas and they are called suburbs.\n(4) For example, Bandra, Bhandup, etc. are the suburbs of Mumbai city.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90239,1993,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in brief about property card.','','','The brief information of property card is as follows : \n1) Property card is the land record of ownership and history of ownership of the land in urban areas.\n(2) It is a land record of non-agricultural land.\n(3) Property card is issued by city survey office of government.\n(4) Property cards provide details of city survey number, plot number, amount of tax, area of property assessed, right to access, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90240,1993,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about arable land use and fallow land use in rural areas.','','','The information about arable land use and fallow land use in rural areas is as follows :\n(A) Arable land use :\n(1) The use of land for cultivation is called arable land use.\n(2) In India, in 2011, nearly 57 per cent of land was under arable land use.\n(3) Generally, arable land is owned individually.\n(4) The arable land can be classified on the basis of ownership of the land and types of agriculture.\n(B) Fallow land use : (1) The agricultural land which is temporarily not in use is called fallow land.\n(2) Fallow land use is a form of rural land use.\n(3) To increase the fertility of soil, farmers do not use a part of agricultural land for few seasons.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90241,1993,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about grassland use and forest land use in rural areas.','','','The information about grassland use and forest land use in rural areas is as follows: (A) Grassland land use : (1) The land owned by village panchayat or the government and which is used for grazing purpose is called grassland.\n(2) It is also called pastureland.\n(3) Mostly, grassland is owned by entire village. Very limited individual ownership of grassland is found.\n(B) Forest land use : (1) The land kept reserved for forest area is called forest land.\n(2) Large trees, bushes, creepers and grass are found in forest land.\n(3) The forest products like firewood, fruits, flowers, gum, grass, honey, etc. are obtained from forest land.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90242,1993,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about commercial land use and residential land use in urban areas.','','','The information about commercial land use and residential land use in urban areas is as follows : (A) Commercial land use : (1) The use of urban land for commercial use is called commercial land use.\n(2) Many shops, banks and offices are found in commercial land areas.\n(3) The concept of Central Business District (CBD) has emerged from commercial land use.\n(4) For example, Fort area or BKC (Bandra Kurla Complex) in Mumbai are the areas of commercial land use.\n(B) Residential land use : The use of urban land for residential purpose is called residential land use.\n(2) Houses and residential buildings are found in residential land areas.\n(3) The density of population is found to be high in urban areas. As its effect, the percentage of residential land use in urban areas is increasing day by day.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90243,1993,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about transport land use and public utility area in urban areas.','','','The information about transport land use and Public utility area in urban areas is as follows : (A) Transport land use: (1) The use of urban land for providing transportation facilities is called transport land use.\n(2) The land used for roadways, railways, petrol pumps, vehicle repairing centres, etc. is included in transport land use.\n(B) Public utility area : (1) Government provides various services to fulfill the needs of citizens. The land used for fulfilling these needs is known as public utility area.\n(2) Post offices, police stations, public grounds, public schools, public hospitals, etc. are included in public utility area.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90244,1993,'descriptive','4b',4,1,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about recreational land use and mixed land use in urban areas.','','','The information about recreational land use and mixed land use in urban areas is as follows: (A) Recreational land use : (1) Recreational land use is a use of urban land for fulfilling the needs of entertainment of people.\n(2) Public parks, swimming pools, theatres, etc. are included in recreational land use.\n(B) Mixed land use : (1) Mixed land use is a use of urban land for fulfilling more than one wants at a time.\n(2) For example, area used for residential and entertainment purposes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90245,1993,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write about the features of planned cities.','','','The features of planned cities are as follows : (A) Need : (1) After industrial revolution, unplanned urbanisation occurred on a large scale all over the world.\n(2) Due to unplanned urbanisation, cities began to develop in a very haphazard manner.\n(3) People started migrating to cities in search of employment opportunities.\n(B) Nature : City which is developed as per the pre-determined and planned layout of land use is called planned city.\n(C) Singapore, Seoul from South Korea, Zurich from Switzerland, Washington D.C. from U.S.A., Brasilia from Brazil, Chandigarh, Bhubaneshwar from India are the examples of planned cities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90246,1993,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about 7/12 extract.','','','The information about 7/12 extract is as follows : \n1) 7/12 is an extract published and given by public revenue department of the government.\n(2) 7/12 extract gives detailed information of a particular registered land.\n(3) The register of the revenue department keeps a record of details of ownership rights of family, status of debt and loans, transfer of ownership, areas under different crops etc. related to registered land.\n(4) Village form no. 7 and Village form no. 12 are combined and 7/12 extract is prepared.\n(5) These village forms are available with the talathi of every village.\n(6) The accurate reading of 7/12 extract gives exact idea about ownership of land, tax levied on land, status of loan taken, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90248,1993,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Land use for public facility is extremely important in urban areas.','','','1) Government provides various services to fulfill the needs of citizens. The land used for fulfilling these needs is known as public utility/ facility area.\n(2) The lands used for post offices, police stations, public grounds, public schools, public hospitals, etc. are included public facility land use.\n(3) Land use for public facility increases the social welfare. Therefore, land use for public facility is extremely important in urban areas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90249,1993,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The record of ownership of nonagricultural land is same as that of agricultural land.','','','1) The record of agricultural land is maintained in the form of 7/12 extract. Similarly the record of ownership of non. agricultural land is maintained in the form of property card.\n(2) Like 7/12 extract, property card provides details of ownership of land, city survey number, plot number, amount of tax, area of property assessed, right to access, etc. Thus, the record of ownership of non-agricultural land is same as that of agricultural land.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90250,1993,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','A region can be classified as developed or developing on the basis of land use.','','','1) The land used in a particular region for agriculture, grazing, etc. indicates that the region is underdeveloped or developing.\n(2) The land used in a particular region for commercial purposes, transportation, recreation, etc. indicates that the region is developed. In this way, a region can be classified as developed or developing on the basis of land use.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90251,1993,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','Study the Figure given and answer the following questions:\n','1) In which country is the land under forests more?\n2) In which country is the land under agriculture more?\n3) Considering the two questions above, how will you relate the physiography and climate of India and Japan with their respective land uses?\n','','i) The land under forests is more in Japan.\nii) The land under agriculture is more in India.\niii) Due to plains and monsoon climatic conditions, the population in India is more. Therefore, agriculture land use is prominent in India. Due to mountainous region and more proportion of precipitation, the forest land use is prominent in Japan.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90252,1993,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','Study the Figure given and answer the following questions:','1) Correlate their land use and development.\n2) Which land use is found in Japan?\n3) Considering the land use in both the countries, make a list of factors affecting land use.\n','','1) The land use in India shows comparatively low development. On the other hand, land use in Japan shows comparatively high development.\n2) The land use like agricultural land use, forest land use, grassland land use, other land use, etc. are found in Japan.\n3) Considering the land use in both the countries, lists of factors affecting land use are as follows :\n(A) Factors affecting land use in India : (1) Fertility of soil (2) Climatic conditions (3) Natural resources (4) Slope of land, etc.\n(B) Factors affecting land use in Japan: (1) Technical advancement (2) Availability of resources (3) Government policies, etc.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90253,1993,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Study the Figure given and answer the following questions :','1) Which are the land uses of 1990-91 that show decline in 2010-11? What could be reasons for this?\n2) Which type of land use is the maximum? What would be the impact of this on India\'s environment?\n3) Can a decline in the area under agriculture equated with food shortage?\n','','1) Total agricultural land use and other non-agricultural land use of 1990-91 show decline in 2010-11. Increased urbanisation could be reason for this.\n2) Agricultural land use is the maximum. It indicates that India is an agrarian country and its environment is comparatively more natural.\n3) A decline in the area under agriculture cannot be equated with food shortage. With modern technology, the maximum agricultural food production can be undertaken in limited areas.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90254,1993,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the Figure given and answer the following questions :','1) What is the percentage of land suitable for cultivation?\n2) What is the percentage of barren land?\n3) What is the percentage of land under forest in Maharashtra?\n4) What is the percentage of nonagricultural land in Maharashtra?\n','','1) The percentage of land suitable for cultivation is 56.8 .\n2) The percentage of barren land is 8.3.\n3) The percentage of land under forest in Maharashtra is 16.9 .\n4)The percentage of non-agricultural land in Maharashtra is 10.2 .\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90255,1993,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','If land is left fallow, or is not in use, then can it be termed as a kind of land use?','','','1). The agricultural land which is temporarily not in use is called fallow land.\n(2) Fallow land use is a form of rural land use.\n(3) To increase the fertility of soil, farmers do not use a part of agricultural land for few seasons. After one or two seasons fallow land is again utilized for cultivation. Therefore, if land is left fallow, or is not in use, then it can be termed as a kind of land use.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90256,1993,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write the advantages of planned cities in your own words.','','','The advantages of planned cities are as follows : (1) There will not be a problem of traffic jam in planned cities.\n(2) The level of pollution will be the minimum in the planned cities.\n(3) The planned cities will be clean and they will maintain environmental balance.\n(4) The quality of human life will be superior in planned cities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90257,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Land use means ----\nA) Using land for farming                    B) Purpose for which land is used \nC) Using land for building                   D) Forest only\n','','','B) Purpose for which land is used ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90258,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In rural areas, the main occupation is ----\nA) Industry 	B) Tourism	 C) Agriculture		 D) Banking\n','','','C) Agriculture','',0,0,0,NULL),(90259,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The land which is kept unused to improve fertility is called ----\nA) Arable land 	B) Forest land 		C) Fallow land  	D) Pasture land\n','','','C) Fallow land ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90260,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Land used for grazing animals is called ----\nA) Commercial land 		B) Grassland 		C) Arable land 	D) Transport land\n','','','B) Grassland ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90261,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','CBD stands for ----\nA) Central Bus Depot 	B) Central Bank Division  	C) Central Business District      D) City Business Department\n','','','C) Central Business District ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90262,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The area used for government services is ----\nA) Residential 		B) Recreational 	C) Public Utility 	D) Commercial\n','','','C) Public Utility ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90263,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Transitional areas are located ----\nA) Inside rural areas 	B) Inside cities 	C) Between rural and urban areas 	   D) Only on highways\n','','','C) Between rural and urban areas ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90264,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Planned cities are developed with ----\nA) Random growth 	        B) Pre-determined layout 	C) Forest cutting 	D) Mining\n','','','B) Pre-determined layout ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90265,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The land ownership information is recorded in ----\nA) Atlas 	B) Dictionary 		C) 7/12 extract		 D) Map key\n','','','C) 7/12 extract','',0,0,0,NULL),(90266,1993,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The land under agriculture in Japan is ----\nA) Very high 		B) Lowest 	C) Moderate 		D) More than forests\n','','','B) Lowest ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90267,1993,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Rural Land Use','','','i) Agriculture is the main occupation.\nii) Settlements are located near fields, forests, or water bodies.\niii) More land is available, so population density is low.\niv) Includes arable land, fallow land, forest land and pastureland.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90268,1993,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Urban Land Use','','','i) Land is used for residential, commercial, transport and public utilities.\nii) High population → dense settlements.\niii) Industries and services influence land use patterns.\niv) Recreational and mixed land use areas are also present.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90269,1993,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : 7/12 Extract','','','i) Official document showing land ownership.\nii) Issued by the Revenue Department.\niii) Shows land type, tax assessment and other rights.\niv) Useful for verifying loans, disputes and ownership.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90270,1993,'descriptive','4a',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a short note on : Fallow Land','','','i) Land temporarily not used for farming.\nii) Helps restore soil fertility naturally.\niii) Farmers leave the land unused for 1–2 seasons.\niv) Essential for sustainable agriculture.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90271,1993,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Arable land</td>\n    <td>a. For grazing animals</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Forest land</td>\n    <td>b. Temporarily unused land</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Pasture land</td>\n    <td>c. Land under cultivation</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Fallow land</td>\n    <td>d. Trees, bushes, grass</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1–c, 2–d, 3–a, 4–b','',0,0,0,NULL),(90272,1993,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Commercial area</td>\n    <td>a. Roads and railways</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Transport land use</td>\n    <td>b. Houses and buildings</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Residential area</td>\n    <td>c. Shops and offices</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Recreational area</td>\n    <td>d. Gardens and playgrounds</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1–c, 2–a, 3–b, 4–d','',0,0,0,NULL),(90273,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','If the birth rate is greater than the death rate then the population ____ .\na) decreases 		b) increases 		c) remains constant 		d) becomes surplus\n','','','b) increases','',0,0,0,NULL),(90274,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','People of ____ age group are included in the productive population.\na) 0 to 14 	b) 14 to 60 	c) 15 to 60 	d) 16 to 59\n','','','d) 16 to 59','',0,0,0,NULL),(90275,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The spread of modern technology in society is mostly dependent on ____ .\na) sex ratio 	b) birth rate 	c) literacy 	d) migration\n','','','c) literacy','',0,0,0,NULL),(90276,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ state of India has the highest sex ratio.\na) Maharashtra 	b) Kerala  	c) Punjab 	d) Haryana\n','','','b) Kerala','',0,0,0,NULL),(90277,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ district of Maharashtra has low density of population.\na) Mumbai City 	b) Mumbai Suburb  	c) Thane  	d) Osmanabad\n','','','d) Osmanabad','',0,0,0,NULL),(90278,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The population density of a region can be understood from its area.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence: The population density of a region can be understood from its area and the total volume of population living in it.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90279,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The quality of population is determined on the basis of literacy.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90280,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There is an adverse impact on manpower in the regions of out migration.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90281,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Greater economic prosperity indicates the development of region.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : Greater economic prosperity as well as higher standards of living, higher quality of life, availability of opportunities, freedom, etc. indicate the development of region.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90282,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Developing countries have an HDI of 1 .','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : Developing countries have and HDI of 0.50 to 0.60 .\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90283,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The state of Maharashtra has a balanced population.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : The state of Maharashtra has imbalanced population.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90284,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The areas with plenty of natural resources normally have more volume of population.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90285,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The region with more proportion of youth in population has a rapid speed of development.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(90286,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Most of people in rural areas are engaged in tertiary activities.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : Most of people in rural areas are engaged in primary activities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90287,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Human Development Index is published by World Health Organisation.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : Human Development Index is published by United National Development Programme.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90288,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Density of population is found to be more in the state of Goa.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(90289,1994,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The state of Rajasthan is densely populated.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : The state of Rajasthan is sparsely populated.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90290,1994,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is birth rate?','','','Birth rate is the number of live births per one thousand people in a year.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90291,1994,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is death rate?','','','Death rate is the number of deaths per one thousand people in a year.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90292,1994,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is life expectancy?','','','Life expectancy is the expected average life span of an individual at the time of his/her birth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90293,1994,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by in-migration?','','','Migration of people in a particular region from outside regions is known as in-migration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90294,1994,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by out-migration?','','','Migration of people from a particular region to outside regions is known as out-migration.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90295,1994,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by sex ratio?','','','The total number of females per thousand males is known as sex ratio.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90296,1994,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which types of occupations are people in urban areas involved?','','','People in urban areas are involved in secondary and tertiary occupations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90297,1994,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who put forth the concept of Human Development Index?','','','Mahbub Ul Haq and Amartya Sen put forth the concept of Human Development Index.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90298,1994,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','What are the aspects considered in the structure of population?','','','The following are the aspects considered in the structure of population :\n(1) Sex ratio/structure: Sex ratio/ structure is a structure that considers the sub division of population on the basis of gender.\n(2) Age structure : Age structure is a structure that considers the sub division of population as per age groups.\n(3) Occupational structure:Occupational structure is a structure that considers sub division of population on the basis of dependency and workability.\n(4) Rural - urban structure : Rural Urban structure is a structure that considers sub division of population on the basis of area of residence.\n(5) Literacy structure : Literacy structure is a structure that considers sub division of population on the basis of capability of reading and writing.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90299,1994,'descriptive','4b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a list of advantageous factors affecting population distribution.','','','A list of advantageous factors affecting population distribution is as follows:\n(1) Availability of sufficient sunlight, clean air and water. (2) Region with low altitude (3) Moderate rainfall and temperature (4) Fertile soil (5) Deposits of minerals\n(6) Industrialisation\n(7) Urbanisation (8) Availability of transportation facilities (9) Availability of market (10) Political stability (11) International peace (12) Favourable\ngovernment policies (13) Favourable social customs and traditions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90300,1994,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a list of disadvantageous factors affecting population distribution.','','','A list of disadvantageous factors affecting population distribution is as follows :\n(1) Shortage of sufficient sunlight, clean air and water. (2) Region with high altitude (3) Extremely less or more rainfall and temperature (4) Infertile soil (5) Scarcity of minerals (6) Low industrialisation (7) Lack of urbanisation (8) Non-availability of transportation facilities (9) Non-availability of market (10) Political instability (11) International disputes (12) Unfavourable government policies (13) Evil social customs and traditions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90301,1994,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Prepare a list of advantageous and disadvantageous factors affecting population distribution.','','','A] A list of advantageous factors affecting population distribution is as follows:\n(1) Availability of sufficient sunlight, clean air and water. (2) Region with low altitude (3) Moderate rainfall and temperature (4) Fertile soil (5) Deposits of minerals\n(6) Industrialisation\n(7) Urbanisation (8) Availability of transportation facilities (9) Availability of market (10) Political stability (11) International peace (12) Favourable\ngovernment policies (13) Favourable social customs and traditions.\nB] A list of disadvantageous factors affecting population distribution is as follows :\n(1) Shortage of sufficient sunlight, clean air and water. (2) Region with high altitude (3) Extremely less or more rainfall and temperature (4) Infertile soil (5) Scarcity of minerals (6) Low industrialisation (7) Lack of urbanisation (8) Non-availability of transportation facilities (9) Non-availability of market (10) Political instability (11) International disputes (12) Unfavourable government policies (13) Evil social customs and traditions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90302,1994,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the various types of migration.','','','The various types of migration are as follows :\n(1) In-migration : In-migration is migration of people in a particular region from outside regions.\n(2) Out-migration : Out-migration is migration of people from a particular region to outside regions.\n(3) Short term migration : Short term migration is migration of people on a temporary basis. For example, migration due to tourism or natural disaster or education is a short term migration.\n(4) Long term migration : Long term migration is migration of people on a permanent basis. For example, migration due to transfer of service or marriage or war and partition is a long term migration.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90303,1994,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the effects of migration.','','','The effects of migration are as follows:\n(1) Out-migration leads to fall in population of a region from where people have migrated outside. This in turn leads to shortage of manpower in a region from where out-migration has occurred.\n(2) In-migration leads to increase in population of a region in which people from outside regions have migrated. This in turn, puts a pressure on public amenities and infrastructure of a region in which people have migrated.\n(3) Migration leads to redistribution of population.\n(4) Migration also leads to a change in the structure of population in a particular region.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90304,1994,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Population is an important resource.','','','1) The economic and cultural development of any country depends upon the volume and the quality of population.\n(2) If a country has a high volume and less quality of population, it has slow economic growth and development.\n(3) If a country has optimum population with high quality of population, it has fast economic growth and development. Thus, population is an important resource.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90305,1994,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Productive population is an important group.','','','1) People belonging to age group of 16 to 59 age are considered as productive. Therefore this population is called productive population.\n(2) People below 16 years of age and above 60 years of age are considered as dependent or non-working population.\n(3) Productive population/people are involved in services or businesses. Therefore they play very important role in economic\ngrowth and development of a country.\n(4) If country has more proportion of productive population and youth, it develops rapidly. Thus, productive population is an important group.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90306,1994,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','The study of age structure is important.','','','1) The study of age structure helps to know the percentage of children, adolescents, youth, middle aged, and old aged in population.\n(2) The study of age structure helps to know the proportion of productive people (Working people between 16 years to 59 years of age) and unproductive people (Non working people below 16 years of age and above 60 years of age).\n(3) The study of age structure helps government in planning various economic, social, educational, medical, etc. schemes for the overall development of country.\n(4) The study of age structure helps to know the speed of economic growth. Thus, the study of age structure is important.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90307,1994,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Literacy is directly related to development.','','','1) The country with high literacy rate, develops rapidly in economic, social and cultural aspects.\n(2) The country with low literacy rate, faces many obstacles in its economic, social and cultural growth and development.\n(3) Literacy rate is related to quality of life, the opportunities available and freedom. Thus, literacy is directly related to development.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90308,1994,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The real progress of a country is understood with the help of the Human Development Index.','','','1) Human Development Index is calculated by taking into account the values of economic, health and education parameters.\n(2) Thus, Human Development Index is calculated on the basis of Gross National Product, life expectancy and literacy rate.\n(3) Human Development Index indicates the standard of living, economic and social welfare, quality of life, opportunities available and freedom. Therefore, the real progress of a country is understood with the help of the Human Development Index.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90309,1994,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The density of population is found to be high in Mumbai City and Mumbai Suburb district.','','','1) Mumbai is economic capital of India.\n(2) In Mumbai City and Mumbai Suburb district, industrialisation and urbanisation has taken place on a large scale.\n(3) Transportation, educational, medical, etc. facilities are available on a large scale in these districts.\n(4) Many employment opportunities are also available in these districts. Due to all these favourable factors, many people from other districts of Maharashtra as well as from other states of India have migrated to Mumbai on a permanent basis. Therefore, the density of population is found to be high in Mumbai City and Mumbai Suburb district.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90310,1994,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes on : Sex ratio','','','1) The number of females per thousand males is called sex ratio.\n(2) The sex ratio is calculated with the help of the following formula :\nSex ratio = <math xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\'> <mfrac> <mrow> <mn>Total number of females </mn> </mrow> <mrow> <mn> Total number of males </mn> </mrow> </mfrac> <mo> ⨯</mo> <mn> 1000 </mn> </math>\n (3) If the number of females per thousand males is less, the sex ratio is considered to be low. On the other hand, if the number of\nfemales per thousand males is more, the sex ratio is considered to be high.\n(4) According to Census 2011, the sex ratio was highest (1084) in the state of Kerala. According to Census 2011, the sex ratio was 929 in the state of Maharashtra.\n(5) Out-migration of males leads to high sex ratio in a region from where males have out-migrated. Less female birth rate leads to low sex ratio.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90311,1994,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes on : Age structure','','','1) Sub division of population as per age groups is known as age structure.\n(2) Age structure is helpful in knowing the proportion of children, adolescents, young, middle aged and old people in population.\n(3) It is also helpful in knowing proportion of active population (Population between the ages 16 and 59) and dependent population (Population below 16 years of age and above 60 years of age).\n(4) Age structure is helpful to the government in framing various policies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90312,1994,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes on : Literacy','','','1) Population above the age of seven can be sub divided on the basis of capability of reading and writing.\n(2) People above the age of seven and are unable to read and write are considered illiterate. On the other hand, people above the age of seven and are able to read and write are considered literate.\n(3) High literacy rate is an indicator of social and economic development of society. On the other hand, high illiteracy rate leads to slow economic, social and cultural development of society.\n(4) Literacy rate is related to quality of life, the opportunities available and freedom. Thus, it is directly related to development.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90313,1994,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Study the following map/figure /graph and answer the following questions :','1) Which state has the highest sex ratio?\n2) Which state has the lowest sex ratio?\n3) What changes should take place in Maharashtra, to bring about a balanced sex ratio and how much change is necessary?\n','','1) Kerala state has the highest sex ratio.\n2) Haryana state has the lowest sex ratio.\n3) The following changes should take place in Maharashtra to bring about a balanced sex ratio :\n(1) Sex ratio must get increased by having total control over female infanticide and launching various government schemes for women empowerment, etc.\n(2) The sex ratio must get increased by 71 i.e. the number of females per thousand males must get increased from 929 to 1000.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90314,1994,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare bar graphs on the basis of the table given below and write a note. \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Sr. No.</th>\n    <th>Country</th>\n    <th>% of Literacy</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1.</td>\n    <td>Argentina</td>\n    <td>98.1</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2.</td>\n    <td>Brazil</td>\n    <td>92.6</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3.</td>\n    <td>India</td>\n    <td>72.1</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4.</td>\n    <td>China</td>\n    <td>96.4</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>5.</td>\n    <td>Bangladesh</td>\n    <td>61.5</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>6.</td>\n    <td>Pakistan</td>\n    <td>56.4</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>7.</td>\n    <td>Iran</td>\n    <td>86.8</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>8.</td>\n    <td>Afghanistan</td>\n    <td>38.1</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','A) Bar graph :','Note : (1) From the above bar diagram it can be seen that the literacy rate is found to be high in developed countries, moderate in developing countries and low in underdeveloped countries.\n(2) In 2010, the literacy rate was 72.1% in India.\n(3) In 2010, the literacy rate was 98.1% in Argentina and it was 38.1% in Afghanistan. \n4) Literacy rate is directly or indirectly related to quality of life, opportunities available and the freedom.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(90315,1994,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the given figure and answer the following questions :','1) Which are the most densely populated districts?\n2) Name the sparsely populated districts with a density of less than 100 per sq. km.\n','','1) The most densely populated districts are : \ni) Mumbai City 	ii) Mumbai Suburb 	iii) Thane \niv) Pune 	v) Kolhapur, etc.\n2) The sparsely populated districts with a density of less than 100sq.km : \ni) Gadchiroli 	ii) Beed 	iii) Osmanabad, etc.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90316,1994,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the given figure and answer the following questions :','1) Name two districts with moderate population.\n2) What is the density of the dark shaded regions?\n3) Why is the density of population less in Gadchiroli?\n','','1) District with moderate population : i) Nashik	ii) Nagpur.\n2) The density of the dark shaded regions is greater than 1000 person per sq. km.\n3) The density of population is less in Gadchiroli due to dense forests.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90317,1994,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Have a discussion in the class on the effect of physiography, climate, area under forest, industries, etc. on the density of population.','','','1) Physiography : The density of population is found to be high in plain areas. On the other hand, the density of population is found to be low in mountainous regions.\n(2) Climate : The density of population is found to be high in the areas with moderate temperature and moderate rainfall. On the other hand, the density of population is found to be low in the areas of extremely low or high temperature and extremely high or low rainfall.\n(3) Area under forest : The density of population is found to be high in the area where forest cover is limited. On the other hand, the density of population is found to be low in the area under dense forests.\n(4) Industries : The density of population is found to be high in industrially developed region. The density of population is found to be low in industrially backward region.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90318,1994,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the table given and answer the following questions :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Particulars</th>\n    <th>Change</th>\n    <th>A City</th>\n    <th>B City</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Total Population in 2016</td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>1,00,000</td>\n    <td>1,10,000</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Total number of children born</td>\n    <td>+</td>\n    <td>2,000</td>\n    <td>2,750</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Total number of deaths</td>\n    <td>-</td>\n    <td>1,500</td>\n    <td>2,200</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>People who have migrated from outside</td>\n    <td>+</td>\n    <td>23,000</td>\n    <td>15,000</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>People who have migrated out</td>\n    <td>-</td>\n    <td>2,000</td>\n    <td>5,000</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Total Population in 2017</td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>?</td>\n    <td>?</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','1) Which city has greater number of newly born children?\n2) Which city records greater number of deaths?\n3) Looking at the figures of in-migration and out-migration which city has received more migrants?\n4) Calculate the population of both the cities in 2017.\n','','1) \'B\' City has greater number of newly born children.\n2) \'B\' City records greater number of deaths.\n3) Looking at the figures of in-migration and out-migration, \'A\' City has received more migrants.\n4) The population of \'A\' City in 2017 is 121, 500 and of \'B\' City in 2017 is 120, 550\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90319,1994,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the table given and answer the following questions :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Particulars</th>\n    <th>Change</th>\n    <th>A City</th>\n    <th>B City</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Total Population in 2016</td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>1,00,000</td>\n    <td>1,10,000</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Total number of children born</td>\n    <td>+</td>\n    <td>2,000</td>\n    <td>2,750</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Total number of deaths</td>\n    <td>-</td>\n    <td>1,500</td>\n    <td>2,200</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>People who have migrated from outside</td>\n    <td>+</td>\n    <td>23,000</td>\n    <td>15,000</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>People who have migrated out</td>\n    <td>-</td>\n    <td>2,000</td>\n    <td>5,000</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Total Population in 2017</td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>?</td>\n    <td>?</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','1) After considering all the points, which city has recorded more growth of population in one year?\n2) The total number of births are given. What would be this figure per thousand population? What is the term for this?\n3) What would be the number of deaths per thousand population? What is the term used for this?\n','','1) After considering all the points, \'A\' City has recorded more growth of population in one year.\n2) The total number of births are given. This figure would be 20 per thousand. The term for this is \'Birth rate\'.\n3) The number of deaths per thousand would be 25 . The term used for this is \'Death rate\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90320,1994,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','In India, 0.52% of the population are migrants. What is the actual number of people who have migrated to India?','','','According to Census 2011, the population of India was nearly 121 crore. In India, 0.52% of the population are migrants. Thus, according to Census 2011, the actual number of people who have migrated to India is nearly 629,200.\n(2) For domestic use, the per capita requirement per day is 50 litres of water.\n(3) Taking this into account, the total amount of water requirement in India for domestic purpose only, will be nearly 6050 crores litre.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90321,1994,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the problems in areas of high population distribution?','','','The following are the problems in areas of high population distribution:\n1) Increased level of water, air, sound and soil pollution. \n2) Traffic jam\n3) Shortage of residential areas \n4) Increased criminal tendencies, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90322,1994,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the problems in areas of low population densities?','','','The following are the problems in areas of low population densities : \n1) Shortage of labour supply \n2) Low levels of demand for various goods and services \n3) Under utilization of resources, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90323,1994,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do imbalanced sex ratios affect the society?','','','Imbalanced sex ratios affect the society as follows :\n(1) Increased problems in society, workplace, etc.\n(2) Increased crimes against women.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90324,1994,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What measures can be taken to strike a balance in the sex ratio?','','','The following measures can be taken to strike a balance in the sex ratio :\n(1) Increasing literacy rate of females.\n(2) Increasing employment opportunities for women.\n(3) Eradicating evil social customs.\n(4) Launching government schemes of women empowerment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90325,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Population growth depends mainly on ---- of a region.\na) Climate  b) Birth rate & Death rate  c) Soil  d) Minerals\n','','','b) Birth rate & Death rate','',0,0,0,NULL),(90326,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The number of live births per thousand population ---- is called birth rate.\na) In a day  b) In a month  c) In a year  d) In a decade\n','','','c) In a year','',0,0,0,NULL),(90327,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Migration refers to the ---- of people from one place to another.\na) Stationary  b) Movement  c) Settlement  d) Removal\n','','','b) Movement','',0,0,0,NULL),(90328,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Density of population = Population ---- Area.\na) ×  b) +  c) ÷  d) –\n','','','c) ÷  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90329,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','When population is scattered over an area, it is called ---- distribution.\na) Dense  b) Sparse  c) Uniform  d) Thick\n','','','b) Sparse','',0,0,0,NULL),(90330,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sex ratio is calculated as females per ---- males.\na) 100  b) 500  c) 800  d) 1000\n','','','d) 1000','',0,0,0,NULL),(90331,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','People between 15 to 59 years make up the ---- population.\na) Dependent  b) Productive  c) Old  d) Child\n','','','b) Productive','',0,0,0,NULL),(90332,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A region with more working population shows rapid ----.\na) Decline  b) Development  c) Migration  d) Scarcity\n','','','b) Development','',0,0,0,NULL),(90333,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Human Development Index values range from ----.\na) 0 to 10  b) 0 to 5  c) 0 to 1  d) 1 to 100\n','','','c) 0 to 1','',0,0,0,NULL),(90334,1994,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The HDI is based on health, education, and ---- parameters.\na) Political  b) Economic  c) Climatic  d) Cultural\n','','','b) Economic','',0,0,0,NULL),(90335,1994,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by population density?','','','Population density is the number of people living per square kilometre in a region.\nIt is calculated using: Population ÷ Area.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90336,1994,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is migration?','','','Migration is the movement of individuals or groups from one place to another, either temporarily or permanently.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90337,1994,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What does the sex ratio indicate?','','','Sex ratio indicates the number of females per 1000 males in a population.\nIt shows whether male–female numbers are balanced.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90338,1994,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the three components of Human Development Index (HDI).','','','The three components are:\ni) Standard of living (economic)\nii) Life expectancy (health)\niii) Education (years of schooling)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90339,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Which factors among those mentioned below does not directly impact industrial development?\n(a) Water   (b) Electricity   (c) Labour   (d) Air\n','','','d) Air','',0,0,0,NULL),(90340,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a small industry?\n(a) Machine parts industry   (b) Book binding industry   (c) Silk industry   (d) Sugar industry\n','','','b) Book binding industry','',0,0,0,NULL),(90341,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following cities is not an IT centre?\n(a) Old Delhi   (b) New Delhi   (c) Noida   (d) Bangaluru\n','','','a) Old Delhi','',0,0,0,NULL),(90342,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','An amount of 2% of the profits have to be utilized for which purpose by industries?\n(a) Income tax   (b) Corporate social responsibility   (c) Goods and service tax   (d) Sales tax\n','','','b) Corporate social responsibility','',0,0,0,NULL),(90343,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a medium industry?\n(a) Sugar industry   (b) Textile industry   (c) Cement industry   (d) Food processing industry\n','','','b) Textile industry','',0,0,0,NULL),(90344,1995,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Small and medium industries of a country are harmful to heavy industries.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : Small and medium industries of a country are supportive to heavy industries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90345,1995,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The level of industrialization is an indicator of the economic development of a country.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90346,1995,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The aim of the industrial development corporations is to decentralize industrialization.','','','Correct','',0,0,0,NULL),(90347,1995,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Corporate social responsibility is compulsory for every industries.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : Corporate social responsibility is not compulsory for every industries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90348,1995,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Localization of sugar industry is found near Jamshedpur.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : Localization of iron and steel industry is found near Jamshedpur.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90349,1995,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','India is predominantly manufacturing country.','','','Incorrect.\nCorrect sentence : India is predominantly agrarian country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90350,1995,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Poverty decreases the speed of industrial growth.','','','Correct.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90351,1995,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is manufacturing?','','','The process of converting raw materials into finished goods is called manufacturing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90352,1995,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is called factory?','','','A place where raw materials are converted into finished goods is called a factory.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90353,1995,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','What are the facilities provided by the government to industrial estates?','','','The following are the facilities provided by the government to industrial estates :\n(1) Government gives concessional rates to the estates for electricity, water and taxes.\n(2) Estates are also provided piece of land at concessional rates by the government.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90354,1995,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Write in your words how industrial development impacts national development.','','','1) Industrial development generates employment opportunities.\n(2) Due to increased employment level, people get jobs and per capita income increases. This further increases the standard of living of people.\n(3) Industrial development increases the value of the Gross National Product.\n(4) Industrial development increases the production of various goods. By exporting the surplus goods, country can earn foreign exchange. In this way, industrial development positively impacts national development.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90355,1995,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give your opinion in short on the usefulness of corporate social responsibility.','','','1) Corporate social responsibility is responsibility shown by the industrialists by working for the betterment of the society and for conserving environment.\n(2) Industries making annual profit of more than 5 crores are expected to spend 2% of their profits on some of the following acts : (a) Providing educational or health facilities (b) Establishing centres for mentally or physically challenged, widows, other needy people (c) Developing a particular village or region (d) Establishing\nenvironmental development centres, etc.\n(3) Corporate social responsibility increases the welfare of people and so proves beneficial to the society.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90356,1995,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Mention three features of small industries.','','','Three features of small industries are as follows:\n(1) Small industries require comparatively less amount of inputs like capital, machinery and manpower.\n(2) Small industries require comparatively less premise for its day-to-day operations.\n(3) Generally, the goods produced in small industries have local/regional markets.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90357,1995,'descriptive','3a',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','Mention three features of large industries.','','','Three features of large industries are as follows :\n(1) Large industries require huge amount of inputs like capital, machinery and manpower.\n(2) Large industries require huge premise for its day-to-day operations.\n(3) The goods produced in large industries have wide markets.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90358,1995,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the factors affecting industrial development.','','','The factors affecting industrial development are as follows :\n(1) Scientific and technological advancement, quantity and quality of labour, speedy transportation facilities, ready markets, availability of raw materials, government policies, political stability, etc. are factors that influence the industrial development of a nation.\n(2) For example, dense forest areas, mountainous areas, desert regions have extreme physiographical features. Transport facilities are found to be limited in such regions. Therefore, industrial development is found to be limited in such regions.\n(3) For example, agriculture is developed on a large scale in the fertile plains and the regions of moderate rainfall. Agriculture provides raw materials to many industries. Therefore, industrial development is found to be high in such regions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90359,1995,'descriptive','4b',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','State the advantages of the Maharashtra Industrial Development Corporation.','','','The advantages of the Maharashtra Industrial Development Corporation are as follows: (1) The state government of Maharashtra established Maharashtra Industrial Development Corporation (MIDC) on 1st August, 1962.\n(2) Through MIDC, government has set up industries in all districts of Maharashtra. MIDC supports the industrial growth and decentralization of industries.\n(3) Government gives concessional rates to the estates/industries for electricity, water and taxes. Estates/industries are also provided piece of land at concessional rates by the government.\n(4) MIDC has been playing an important role in generating employment opportunities in all the 36 districts of Maharashtra.\n(5) MIDC has been playing an important indirect role in increasing the per capita income and standards of living of people in Maharashtra.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90360,1995,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the importance of I.T. industry.','','','1) Information technology industry includes various components like searching for technical information, obtaining information, analyzing information, compiling information, graphic presentation of information and providing information.\n(2) Information technology industry provides various types of information through internet on computers, mobiles and other devices. This industry provides information at comparatively less cost, time and efforts.\n(3) The obtained information can be used for personal, economic, social, cultural, etc. development. India has made rapid progress in this industry.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90361,1995,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Considering India\'s population, the development of industries is a good solution to the problem of unemployment. Explain.','','','1) According to Census, 2011, the population of India was nearly 121 crores.\n(2) In India, majority of people are engaged in agriculture. In India, agriculture is seasonal in nature. Therefore, the unemployment rate is found to be high in India. Therefore, it is essential to develop industries in India.\n(3) By developing small industries, unemployed people will get employment opportunities. Small industries will help in providing jobs to less skilled, less educated people.\n(4) By developing medium and large industries in rural areas, the process of urbanisation can be boosted in villages. It will also restrict migration of people from villages to towns and cities. The employment level in a nation can be raised by developing industries. Thus, considering India\'s population, the development of industries is a good solution to the problem of unemployment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90362,1995,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about agro-based industries in India.','','','1) India is predominantly agrarian country. Agriculture is the main occupation of majority of people in India.\n(2) In India, food crops, cash crops, fruits, flowers, vegetables, etc. are cultivated on a large scale. The agricultural produce provides raw material to many industries in India. For example, sugar cane is raw material for sugar industry. Thus many industries in India are agro-based industries.\n(3) The industries processing agricultural products like fruit processing industries, food processing industries, dairy industries, etc. have also flourished in India.\n(4) Agro based industries play an important role in rural economy by generating employment opportunities in rural areas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90363,1995,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Prepare a flow chart for the following statements : The journey of clothes we use from the farm to ourselves.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(90364,1995,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Prepare a flow chart for the following statements : Essential factors for the location of any one industry.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(90365,1995,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between : Medium industries and heavy industries.','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Medium Industries</th>\n    <th>Heavy Industries</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>\n      <b>Quantity of inputs</b><br>\n      Compared to heavy industries, medium industries require inputs like capital, manpower, machinery, etc. in lesser quantities.\n    </td>\n    <td>\n      <b>Quantity of inputs</b><br>\n      Compared to medium industries, heavy industries require inputs like capital, manpower, machinery, etc. in greater quantities.\n    </td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>\n      <b>Examples</b><br>\n      Fruit processing industries, jagerry making industries, furniture manufacturing industries, etc. are the examples of medium industries.\n    </td>\n    <td>\n      <b>Examples</b><br>\n      Sugar manufacturing industries, textile industries, automobile manufacturing industries, etc. are the examples of heavy industries.\n    </td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(90366,1995,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Small industries and Medium industries','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <tr>\n        <th>Category</th>\n        <th>Small Industries</th>\n        <th>Medium Industries</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Quantity of inputs</td>\n        <td>\n            Compared to medium industries, small industries require inputs \n            like capital, manpower, machinery, etc. in lesser quantities.\n        </td>\n        <td>\n            Compared to small industries, medium industries require inputs \n            like capital, manpower, machinery, etc. in greater quantities.\n        </td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Examples</td>\n        <td>\n            Papad making industries, pickle making industries, cane industries, etc. \n            are the examples of small industries.\n        </td>\n        <td>\n            Fruit processing industries, jaggery making industries, furniture \n            manufacturing industries, etc. are the examples of medium industries.\n        </td>\n    </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(90367,1995,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Text Book','','Distinguish between : Agro-based industries and Information technology industries','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <tr>\n        <th>Category</th>\n        <th>Agro-based Industries</th>\n        <th>Information Technology Industries</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Location</td>\n        <td>\n            Agro-based industries are generally located in the rural areas.\n        </td>\n        <td>\n            Information technology industries are generally located in urban areas.\n        </td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Nature</td>\n        <td>\n            Agro-based industries are traditional in nature.\n        </td>\n        <td>\n            Information technology industries are modern in nature.\n        </td>\n    </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(90368,1995,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the given figure and answer the following questions :','1) Name the industry at Jamshedpur.\n2) Which raw material is required for this industry?\n3) From which areas is this raw material obtained?\n4) Why is coal used in this industry?\n5) State with reasons if it would be feasible to set up an iron and steel industry in your district.\n','','1) The name of the industry at Jamshedpur is Iron and steel industry.\n2) Raw material required for this industry is iron, manganese, limestone, coal, etc.\n3) This raw material is obtained from the areas of Jamshedpur, Kulaisila, Gurumahisani, Jgharia, etc.\n4) Coal is used in this industry as fuel to heat iron to a certain temperature.\n5) It would not be feasible to set up an iron and steel industry in our district as there are no reserves of iron ore, manganese, limestone, etc. found in our district.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90369,1995,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the picture given and answer the following questions :','1) In what context is this logo?\n2) What will be the benefit of this programme?\n3) What is correlation between this programme and employment?\n4) Which are the Navratna industries in India?\n','','1) This logo is about \'Make in India\' programme.\n2) This programme will boost the production of various industries located all over India.\n3) This programme will generate employment opportunities on a large scale.\n4) The Navratna industries in India are as follows :\ni) Gas Authority of India	ii) Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited 	iii) Coal India\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90370,1995,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the following figure showing the factors affecting the location of an industry. Taking into account the factors shown in the figure, note in different colours the favourable factors required for (1) Iron and steel (2) Textiles and (3) Sugar industry and answer the following questions :','1) For each industry, prepare a list of necessary factors.\n2) For each industry give an explanation for the location suggested by you.\n3) In a similar manner for which other industries can you decide a suitable location?\n','','1) A list of necessary factors for each industry :\ni) Iron and steel industry : Iron ore, manganese, coal, water, etc.\nii) Textile industry : Cotton, transportation services, water, electricity, etc.\niii) Sugar industry : Sugar cane, water, electricity, etc.\n2) i) Iron and steel industry : The raw material of iron and steel industry is bulky in nature. The transportation of this raw material is not feasible. Therefore, the location of iron and steel industry is suggested near mineral field.\nii) Textile industry : The raw material of textile industry is cotton. Cotton is light in weight. Therefore, it can be transported easily. Therefore, the location of textile industry is suggested near highway and at a place where labours are available.\niii) Sugar industry : The raw material of sugar industry is sugar cane. This raw material is comparatively less durable. Therefore, its transport at a distant location is difficult. Therefore, the location of sugar industry is suggested near the cultivation area.\n3) In a similar manner, a suitable location can be decided for the industries like fruit processing industry, cement industry, etc.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90371,1995,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Which industries could be profitably set up in your surrounding area/locality?','','','As fruits like mangoes, jackfruits, cashew nuts are largely grown, the fruit processing industries could be profitably set up in our surrounding area/locality.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90372,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Raw material is converted into ….. products in industries.\na) Waste  b) Finished  c) Natural  d) Agricultural\n','','','b) Finished','',0,0,0,NULL),(90373,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Industries provide ….. occupation to people.\na) Primary  b) Secondary  c) Tertiary  d) Optional\n','','','b) Secondary','',0,0,0,NULL),(90374,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Iron and steel industry mainly depends on ….. availability.\na) Cotton  b) Water  c) Raw material & energy  d) Fruits\n','','','c) Raw material & energy','',0,0,0,NULL),(90375,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Jamshedpur is famous for ….. industry.\na) Textile  b) Sugar  c) Iron and steel  d) Dairy\n','','','c) Iron and steel','',0,0,0,NULL),(90376,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','MIDC was established on ….. 1962.\na) 1st May  b) 1st August  c) 15th August  d) 1st January\n','','','b) 1st August','',0,0,0,NULL),(90377,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The industry which uses agricultural products is called ….. industry.\na) Chemical  b) Heavy  c) Agro-based  d) Engineering\n','','','c) Agro-based','',0,0,0,NULL),(90378,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','CSR policy expects industries to spend …..% of profit on society.\na) 1%  b) 2%  c) 5%  d) 10%\n','','','b) 2%  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90379,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Pollution caused by industries is known as ….. pollution.\na) Domestic  b) Industrial  c) Natural  d) Forest\n','','','b) Industrial','',0,0,0,NULL),(90380,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Information Technology industry mainly works using …..\na) Tractors  b) Computers  c) Iron rods  d) Cotton\n','','','b) Computers','',0,0,0,NULL),(90381,1995,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Central Pollution Control Board works for …..\na) Export promotion  b) Pollution control  c) Water supply  d) Forest survey\n','','','b) Pollution control','',0,0,0,NULL),(90382,1995,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is an industry?','','','A place where raw material is converted into finished products.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90383,1995,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is raw material?','','','The basic natural material used to produce new products.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90384,1995,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is finished product?','','','A usable, value-added product obtained after processing raw material.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90385,1995,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which industry is located at Jamshedpur?','','','Iron and steel industry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90386,1995,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is MIDC?','','','Maharashtra Industrial Development Corporation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90387,1995,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is CSR?','','','Corporate Social Responsibility—industries working for social welfare.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90388,1995,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What causes industrial pollution?','','','Harmful waste and pollutants released during manufacturing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90389,1995,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Short Note on : Iron and Steel Industry','','','i) Heavy industry that uses iron ore, coal, and limestone as raw materials.\nii) Jamshedpur is a major centre due to availability of raw materials and power.\niii) Produces steel used in machines, vehicles, construction, tools etc.\niv) Important for national economic development and industrialization.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90390,1995,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Short Note on : Agro-Based Industries','','','i) Industries that use agricultural products as raw materials (cotton, sugarcane, fruits).\nii) Examples: textile mills, sugar factories, dairy, food processing.\niii) Located in rural areas where crops are grown.\niv) Provide employment and support agricultural income.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90391,1995,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Short Note on : MIDC (Maharashtra Industrial Development Corporation)','','','i) Established on 1st August 1962 by Maharashtra Government.\nii) Develops industrial estates in different districts.\niii) Provides facilities like water, electricity, roads, and land.\niv) Helps decentralize industries and create employment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90392,1995,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Short Note on : Industrial Pollution','','','i) Factories release harmful gases, chemicals, and waste.\nii) This pollutes air, water, and land, causing health problems.\niii) Strict laws like Water Act and Air Act control pollution.\niv) Industries must treat waste before releasing it into the environment.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90399,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following factor\'s map will be a large-scale map?\n(a) Temple  (b) State  (c) Nation  (d) Continent\n','','','a) Temple','',0,0,0,NULL),(90400,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following scale indicates small-scale map?\n(a) 1:100  (b) 1:1000  (c) 1:10000  (d) 1:100000\n','','','d) 1 : 100000','',0,0,0,NULL),(90401,1996,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is verbal scale?','','','A scale in which distances are expressed with the use of words indicating measurement is called verbal scale.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90402,1996,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is numerical scale?','','','A scale in which distances are expressed as ratio is called numerical scale.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90403,1996,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is linear scale?','','','A scale in which distances are expressed by drawing graphical scale is called linear scale.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90405,1996,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is large scale map?','','','A map in which a particular part of ground covers comparatively more area is called large scale map.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90406,1996,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is small scale map?','','','A map in which a particular part of ground covers comparatively less area is called small scale map.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90407,1996,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','Classify maps showing the following areas into small scale or large scale :\ni) Building           ii) School         iii) Country of India            iv) Church                    v) Mall \nvi) World map     vii) Garden      viii) Dispensary                  ix) Maharashtra state    x) The north sky at night.\n','','','The classification of maps showing the given areas is as follows :\n(A) Small scale maps : i) Country of India        ii) World map       iii) Maharashtra state        iv) The north sky at night.\n(B) Large scale maps : i) Building         ii) School         iii) Church          iv) Mall        v) Garden          vi) Dispensary.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90409,1996,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Text Book','','There are two maps with respective scales of 1 cm = 100 m and 1 cm = 100 km. Give well reasoned answer as to which of them would be a large scale map and which a small scale map. Recognize the types of maps.','','','A) Out of the two maps with respective scales of 1 cm = 100 m  and 1 cm = 100 km, a map with respective scale of 1 cm = 100 m would be a large scale map.\n(B) Reasons : (1) 1 metre is equal to 100 centimetres and 100 metre is equal to 10000 centimetres.\n(2) Thus, the value of the given verbal scale  (1 cm = 100 m) is  1 : 10000 in numerical terms (scale).\n(3) A map having a numerical scale of 1 : 10000 or less than it is called large scale map. Therefore, 1 cm = 100 m would be a large scale map.\n(C) Types of maps : (1) Maps of villages, church, agricultural fields, etc. are the large scale maps.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90410,1996,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Convert verbal scale of 1 cm = 53 km  to a numerical scale.','','','1) 1 kilometre is equal to 100000 centimetres. Therefore, 53 kilometres is equal to 5300000 centimetres.\n(2) Therefore, the verbal scale of 1 cm = 53 km can be converted to a numerical scale as - 1 : 5300000 .\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90411,1996,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','Convert numerical scale of 1 : 10000000 to a verbal scale in the metric system.','','','1) 100000 centimetres is equal to 1 kilometre. Thus, 10000000 centimetres is equal to 100 kilometres.\n(2) Therefore, numerical scale of 1 : 10000000 to a verbal scale in the metric system can be converted as 1cm = 100 km.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90412,1996,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes on : Verbal scale','','','1) A scale in which distances are expressed with the use of words indicating measurement is called verbal scale.\n(2) For example, 1cm=100 km.\n(3) In verbal scale, the word indicating measurement on the left hand side indicates the distance between any two points on a map. On the other hand, the word indicating measurement on the right hand side indicates the ground distance between those two points.\n(4) When the map is reduced or enlarged by taking its photo copy, the verbal scale on the original map does not change.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90413,1996,'descriptive','4a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes on : Numerical scale.','','','1) Numerical scale : A scale in which distances are expressed as ratio is called numerical scale.\n(2) For example, 1 : 10000. It is also known as representative fraction.\n(3) In numerical scale, the same measuring unit is used for the figures on the left hand side and right hand side. However, no words are used to indicate this measuring unit.\n(4) In numerical scale, number 1 on the left hand side indicates the distance between any two points on a map. On the other hand, the number 10000 on the right hand side indicates the ground distance between those two points.\n(5) When the map is reduced or enlarged by taking its photo copy, the numerical scale on the original map does not change.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90414,1996,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short notes on : Linear scale','','','1) A scale in which distances are expressed by drawing graphical scale is called linear scale.\n(2) For example,\n','3) Compass or blade of grass is used if the ruler is not available for the measurement.\n(4) A thread is used for measuring the curved distances between two points shown in a map.\n(5) When the map is reduced or enlarged by taking its photo copy, the linear scale drawn on the original map changes as per the changing size of the map.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(90415,1996,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Large scale map and Small scale map','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 70%; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n    <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n        <th>Category</th>\n        <th>Large Scale Map</th>\n        <th>Small Scale Map</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Meaning</td>\n        <td>When a particular part of ground covers comparatively more area on a map, it is called large scale map.</td>\n        <td>When a particular part of ground covers comparatively less area on a map, it is called small scale map.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Examples</td>\n        <td>Maps of villages, church, agricultural fields, etc. are the large scale maps.</td>\n        <td>Maps of state, country, continent, world, etc. are the small scale maps.</td>\n    </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(90416,1996,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Distinguish between : Numerical scale and Linear scale','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n    <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n        <th>Category</th>\n        <th>Numerical scale</th>\n        <th>Linear scale</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Meaning</td>\n        <td>A scale in which distances are expressed as ratio is called numerical scale.</td>\n        <td>A scale in which distances are expressed by drawing graphical scale is called linear scale.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Examples</td>\n        <td>1 : 1000000</td>\n        <td>1 cm = 10000 km</td>\n    </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(90417,1996,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Study the following map and answer the following questions :','1) How much is the ground distance between Mumbai and Gondia?\n2) How much is the distance between Satara and Sangli on a map?\n','','1) The ground distance between Mumbai and Gondia is approximately 810 kilometres.\n2) The distance between Satara and Sangli on a map is approximately 1.5 centimetres.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(90419,1996,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Text Book','','The distance between two points A and B on the ground is 500 m . Show this distance on paper by a line of 2 cm . Express the map scale by any one method and mention it.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(90420,1996,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the need to use map scale? Think about it and write a paragraph.','','','1) If the map scale is not mentioned in a map, it will become difficult to know the ground (actual) distance between any two points shown in a map.\n(2) Map scale is important element of a map. It facilitates map reading.\n(3) If the map scale is mentioned in a map, it will become very easy to understand the ground (actual) distance between any two points shown in a map.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90421,1996,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following scale will you prefer to use : (a) Verbal scale (b) Numerical scale (c) Linear scale?','','','1) Different measuring units are used in different countries of the world. Due to linguistic differences, particular verbal scale or linear scale may not be used with ease in all the countries.\n(2) Numerical scale is a global scale. It can be used universally. Therefore, we will prefer numerical scale.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90422,1996,'mcq','1a',1,1,'Out of Text Book','','Why does the painter hold the pencil in a particular way while estimating the scene ----?\na) For fun  b) To draw to scale  c) To colour better  d) To draw fast\n','','','b) To draw to scale','',0,0,0,NULL),(90423,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The painter makes a small sketch first ---- why?\na) To save time  b) To draw to scale  c) To use more colours  d) To avoid mistakes\n','','','b) To draw to scale','',0,0,0,NULL),(90424,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How can a large landscape be shown on paper ----?\na) By guessing  b) By using a scale  c) By colouring  d) By folding paper\n','','','b) By using a scale','',0,0,0,NULL),(90425,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is it possible to show all countries on one map ----?\na) By reducing scale  b) By using globe  c) By using computer  d) By using thread\n','','','a) By reducing scale','',0,0,0,NULL),(90427,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is common in the painter’s picture and a map ----?\na) Both are colourful  b) Both use scale  c) Both are natural  d) Both are small\n','','','b) Both use scale','',0,0,0,NULL),(90428,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ratio of map distance to ground distance is called ----\na) Symbol  b) Scale  c) Index  d) Direction\n','','','b) Scale','',0,0,0,NULL),(90429,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which of the following is a numerical scale ----?\na) 1 cm = 60 km  b) One cm equals sixty km  c) 1 : 60,00,000  d) 10 km to 0 km\n','','','c) 1 : 60,00,000','',0,0,0,NULL),(90430,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which scale changes automatically when a map is enlarged or reduced ----?\na) Verbal scale  b) Numerical scale  c) Graphical scale  d) Symbolic scale\n','','','c) Graphical scale','',0,0,0,NULL),(90431,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A map with a scale smaller than 1:10,000 is called ----\na) Large scale map  b) Medium scale map  c) Small scale map  d) Local map\n','','','c) Small scale map  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(90432,1996,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maps of villages, fields or cities are examples of ---- maps.\na) Small scale  b) Large scale  c) World  d) Digital\n','','','b) Large scale','',0,0,0,NULL),(90433,1996,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Painter uses a pencil to estimate the scene so that the picture is drawn to scale. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(90434,1996,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Numerical scale changes when the map is photocopied in a bigger size','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(90435,1996,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Graphical scale changes with the size of the map. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(90436,1996,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ratio 1:50,000 is larger than 1 : 10,000. ','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(90437,1996,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Actual ground distances are measured through surveying. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(90438,1996,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A globe provides more detailed information than a map.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(90439,1996,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','All people in the photograph appear in reduced height in the same proportion. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(90440,1996,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is map scale?','','','The ratio of map distance to actual ground distance.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90441,1996,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is graphical scale?','','','A scale shown as a line divided into equal units on the map.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90442,1996,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do maps need a scale?','','','To convert real-world distances onto paper accurately.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90443,1996,'descriptive','2b',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','The painter holds the pencil in a particular way.','','','i) To estimate the height and width of the scene.\nii) To maintain correct proportion.\niii) To ensure the sketch is drawn to scale.\niv) It helps in comparing objects in the landscape.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90444,1996,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A scale is necessary while preparing maps.','','','i) To accurately convert large ground distances onto paper.\nii) To measure real distances using map distance.\niii) To ensure maps are drawn proportionately.\niv) To help readers understand the actual size of places.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90445,1996,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Graphical scale is useful when maps are enlarged or reduced.','','','i) Graphical scale changes automatically with size.\nii) It remains correct even when map dimensions change.\niii) Verbal/numerical scales do not change proportionally.\niv) Therefore, it maintains accuracy in altered map copies.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(90446,1996,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','1 : 10,000 is a large scale while 1 : 50,000 is a small scale.','','','i) A smaller denominator means a larger scale.\nii) Large scale shows more details.\niii) Small scale shows fewer details but larger areas.\niv) Hence, 1 : 10,000 > 1 : 50,000 in value.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90448,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The main purpose of a geographical field trip is to ----\na) Enjoy nature  b) Get first-hand experience  c) Click photos  d) Do shopping\n','','','b) Get first-hand experience','',0,0,0,NULL),(90449,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A field trip helps students understand the relationship between ----\na) Man and animals  b) Man and environment  c) Teachers and students  d) Soil and water\n','','','b) Man and environment','',0,0,0,NULL),(90450,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Before visiting an office, it is important to get ----\na) Signature  b) Permission  c) Money  d) Vehicle\n','','','b) Permission','',0,0,0,NULL),(90451,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which item is NOT part of a field trip kit ----?\na) Notebook  b) Camera  c) Pencil  d) Lunch timetable\n','','','d) Lunch timetable','',0,0,0,NULL),(90452,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A questionnaire is prepared to ----\na) Decorate the report  b) Ask systematic questions  c) Take attendance  d) Play games\n','','','b) Ask systematic questions','',0,0,0,NULL),(90453,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who conducts training programmes for elections ----?\na) Students  b) Election officers  c) Drivers  d) Teachers\n','','','b) Election officers','',0,0,0,NULL),(90454,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The voting machine (EVM) is used since ----\na) Many years  b) 2 years  c) Newly invented  d) Only during school exams\n','','','a) Many years','',0,0,0,NULL),(90455,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the first step after collecting information during a visit ----?\na) Destroy notes  b) Prepare report  c) Go home  d) Do homework\n','','','b) Prepare report','',0,0,0,NULL),(90456,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is included at the end of the report ----?\na) Festivals  b) References  c) Marks  d) Signatures of students\n','','','b) References','',0,0,0,NULL),(90457,1997,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A by-election is held when ----\na) A candidate resigns  b) Holiday comes  c) School closes  d) Books are printed\n','','','a) A candidate resigns','',0,0,0,NULL),(90458,1997,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Field trips help students observe geographical processes directly. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(90459,1997,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','It is not necessary to prepare a questionnaire for field visits.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(90460,1997,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Permission of the office must be taken before visiting.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(90461,1997,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','EVM cannot be used during elections. ','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(90462,1997,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A report must include an introduction and references. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(90463,1997,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Field trips do not help in learning geography. ','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(90464,1997,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Earlier voting was done without machines. ','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(90465,1997,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the main objective of a field trip?','','','To get first-hand knowledge of geographical factors and processes.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90466,1997,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is a questionnaire prepared?','','','To collect information systematically during a field visit.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90467,1997,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What should be included in the field trip kit?','','','Questionnaire copy, notebook, pen, pencil, camera.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90468,1997,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Who updates the electoral list?','','','Election office officials.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90469,1997,'descriptive','3a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is planning important before a field trip?','','','Planning helps decide the place, topic, objectives, duration, transportation, and materials needed. It ensures safety, discipline and systematic data collection.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90470,1997,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the role of the election division office?','','','It conducts elections, updates voter lists, provides manpower, manages EVMs, organizes training, and ensures transparent and smooth voting.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90471,1997,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does a field trip help in geography learning?','','','Students observe physical and human geography directly. They understand processes, collect data, and relate theory with practical situations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90472,1997,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What problems may be faced during a field visit?','','','Lack of permission, crowding, time shortage, incomplete information, communication problems, or damage risk inside the office.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90473,1997,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A questionnaire is necessary for a field trip.','','','Because it helps collect structured information, prevents missing points, maintains focus, and makes reporting easier.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90474,1997,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Permission must be taken before visiting an office.','','','Offices have fixed timings, security rules, and responsibilities. Prior permission ensures smooth entry and availability of officials.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90475,1997,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','A report is prepared after a field trip.','','','To present findings, observations, tables, photographs, and conclusions in an organized manner for class sharing.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90477,1997,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Field trips make geography easier to understand.','','','Because students directly observe landforms, processes, human activities, and environmental relations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90479,1997,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Questionnaire</td>\n    <td>d. Collecting systematic information</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Field trip kit</td>\n    <td>a. Notebook and camera</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Election office</td>\n    <td>b. Conducts voting process</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Report writing</td>\n    <td>c. Introduction, diagrams</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1–d, 2–a, 3–b, 4–c','',0,0,0,NULL),(90480,1997,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width:fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>A</th>\n    <th>B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. EVM</td>\n    <td>d. Electronic voting machine</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. By-election</td>\n    <td>a. When seat becomes vacant</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Permission</td>\n    <td>b. Needed before visit</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Training</td>\n    <td>c. Given to election staff</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1–d, 2–a, 3–b, 4–c','',0,0,0,NULL),(90481,1997,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Prepare a questionnaire for a visit to a NGO working for cleanliness of marine beaches.','','','A questionnaire for a visit to a NGO working for cleanliness of marine beaches is as follows:\n(1) What is the year of establishment of this NGO?\n(2) What are the aims and objectives of this NGO?\n(3) Which activities/programmes are carried out for the cleanliness of marine beaches?\n(4) How are the people from surrounding areas involved in those various activities and programmes?\n(5) What are the future plans of this NGO?','',0,0,0,NULL),(90484,1997,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short Note on : Field Trip','','','A field trip is an outdoor learning activity where students visit a place to observe geographical facts. It helps understand environment–man relationships. Students collect information, take photographs, interact with officials, and prepare reports.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90485,1997,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short Note on : Questionnaire','','','A questionnaire is a set of written questions used to collect data. It ensures systematic information gathering and covers important points. It is essential when visiting offices like election division, talathi office, or industries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90486,1997,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short Note on : Election Process','','','The election process includes updating voter lists, issuing notices, providing EVMs, training staff, appointing polling officers, conducting voting, counting votes, and declaring results. The election division office supervises all activities.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90487,1997,'descriptive','4a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write short Note on : Report Writing','','','A field trip report contains Introduction, Personnel, Nature of Work, Problems, Acknowledgement, and References. It includes photos, tables, graphs, sketches, and conclusions based on collected data.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90488,1997,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the importance and steps of conducting a geographical field trip.','','','Field trips provide first-hand knowledge of geographical features and human activities. Students observe landforms, processes, and administrative work directly. Planning is the first step, including selecting place, objective, date, and permissions. A field kit is prepared containing a questionnaire, notebook, and camera. During the visit, students interact with officials and collect information. Photographs and notes are taken. After returning, a detailed report is written with diagrams, tables, and conclusions. Thus, field trips develop observation, analysis, communication, and reporting skills.','',0,0,0,NULL),(90489,1997,'descriptive','4b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Describe the role of the Election Division Office as observed during the field visit.','','','The Election Division Office manages all election-related activities in a taluka or district. It prepares and updates voter lists, issues election notices, appoints staff, and conducts training programmes. The office distributes and maintains EVM machines. Additional manpower is taken from other departments during elections. The office ensures transparency through monitoring and proper conduct rules. Voting timing, security arrangements, and counting procedures are supervised. In case of equal votes or faulty EVMs, special procedures are followed. The office also keeps records and issues certificates to elected candidates.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91624,2290,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में मरीजों की देखभाल कौन करता है?\nA) दुकानदार B) डॉक्टर C) किसान D) चालक','','','उत्तर: डॉक्टर\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91625,2290,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में ‘शांति बनाए रखें’ का अर्थ क्या है?\nA) जोर-जोर से बोलो     B) भागदौड़ करो        C) धीरे बोलो व शांत रहो      D) हँसी-मजाक करो','','','','उत्तर: धीरे बोलो व शांत रहो\n\n',0,0,0,NULL),(91626,2290,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','आई.सी.यू. किसके लिए होता है?\nA) पढ़ाई के लिए        B) गंभीर मरीजों के लिए     C) खेलने के लिए          D) डॉक्टरों के आराम के लिए','','','उत्तर: गंभीर मरीजों के लिए\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91627,2290,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में सफाई क्यों ज़रूरी है?\nA) खेलने के लिए जगह चाहिए    B) बीमारियाँ न फैलें      C) डॉक्टर को पसंद है    D) बच्चों को मज़ा आता है','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(91628,2290,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में सूचना-पट क्यों लगाए जाते हैं?\nA) दीवार सजाने के लिए    B) मरीजों को सही जानकारी देने के लिए      C) बच्चों को व्यस्त रखने के लिए      D) पढ़ाई करवाने के लिए','','','उत्तर: मरीजों को सही जानकारी देने के लिए\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91635,2290,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','बड़े परिवार को ‘छोटा परिवार – सुखी परिवार’ कहा जाता है।','','','गलत','',0,0,0,NULL),(91636,2290,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में साफ-सफाई बहुत ज़रूरी होती है।','','','सही','',0,0,0,NULL),(91637,2290,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','आई.सी.यू. में सिर्फ़ डॉक्टर बैठते हैं, मरीज नहीं। ','','','गलत','',0,0,0,NULL),(91638,2290,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में शोर मचाना अच्छा माना जाता है।','','','गलत','',0,0,0,NULL),(91639,2290,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','डॉक्टर का काम मरीजों की जाँच करना और इलाज करना है। ','','','सही','',0,0,0,NULL),(91640,2290,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','शांति बनाए रखें।”','','','अस्पताल के कर्मचारी ने मरीजों और आगंतुकों से कहा।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91641,2290,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','',' “मरीजों की जाँच करनी है।”','','','— डॉक्टर ने नर्स/कर्मचारी से कहा।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91642,2290,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','“कृपया लाइन में रहें।”','','','— रिसेप्शन पर मौजूद कर्मचारी ने मरीजों/परिजनों से कहा।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91643,2290,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में डॉक्टर क्या करते हैं?','','','— डॉक्टर मरीजों की जाँच और इलाज करते हैं।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91644,2290,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में शोर क्यों नहीं करना चाहिए?','','','— क्योंकि मरीजों को आराम की आवश्यकता होती है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91645,2290,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','आई.सी.यू. कहाँ होता है?','','','— अस्पताल में गंभीर मरीजों के लिए विशेष कक्ष होता है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91646,2290,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में सफाई क्यों ज़रूरी है?','','','— ताकि बीमारियाँ न फैलें।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91647,2290,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में सूचना-पट क्यों लगाए जाते हैं?','','','— ज़रूरी निर्देश दिखाने के लिए।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91648,2290,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में मरीजों की देखभाल कैसे होती है?','','','— डॉक्टर उनकी जाँच और उपचार करते हैं।\n— नर्सें समय-समय पर दवा और सहायता देती हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91649,2290,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में शांति क्यों रखनी चाहिए?','','','— मरीज आराम करना चाहते हैं।\n— शोर से उनकी तकलीफ़ बढ़ सकती है।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91650,2290,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','स्वच्छ अस्पताल क्यों आवश्यक है?','','','— गंदगी से बीमारी फैल सकती है।\n— साफ-सफाई मरीजों के स्वास्थ्य के लिए ज़रूरी है।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91651,2290,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','आई.सी.यू. का क्या महत्व है?','','','— यह बहुत गंभीर मरीजों के लिए विशेष कक्ष है।\n— यहाँ डॉक्टर खास निगरानी रखते हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91652,2290,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','छोटा परिवार सुखी परिवार क्यों कहा जाता है?','','','— छोटे परिवार में देखभाल अच्छे से हो सकती है।\n— परिवार के खर्च और ज़िम्मेदारियाँ संतुलित रहती हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91653,2290,'descriptive','5',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल का वातावरण कैसा होना चाहिए?','','','— अस्पताल में साफ-सफाई होनी चाहिए।\n— सभी लोगों को शांति रखनी चाहिए।\n— मरीजों की सहायता के लिए डॉक्टर व नर्सें तैयार रहती हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91654,2290,'descriptive','5',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','डॉक्टर और नर्स की क्या भूमिका होती है?','','','— डॉक्टर मरीजों की बीमारी की पहचान करते हैं।\n— नर्स दवाएँ देती है और देखभाल करती है।\n— दोनों मिलकर मरीज को स्वस्थ बनाने में मदद करते हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91655,2290,'descriptive','5',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','अस्पताल में लगे निर्देश कैसे सहायक होते हैं?','','','— वे मरीजों और परिवारजनों को रास्ता दिखाते हैं।\n— ज़रूरी नियम और सावधानियाँ बताते हैं।\n— इससे अस्पताल में व्यवस्था बनी रहती है।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91656,2291,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में किसके पढ़ने से देश आगे बढ़ेगा?\nA) बेटा B) बेटी C) शिक्षक D) माता–पिता','','','उत्तर: B) बेटी\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91657,2291,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में नई उमंग की लहर किससे दौड़ेगी?\nA) खेल B) बेटियों की शिक्षा C) त्योहार D) प्रकृति','','','उत्तर: B) बेटियों की शिक्षा\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91658,2291,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','',' “सबके हाथों पुस्तक होगी” – कविता “बेटी युग” में यह पंक्ति किसकी निशानी है?\nA) ज्ञान की B) डर की C) बीमारी की D) खेल की','','','उत्तर: A) ज्ञान की\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91659,2291,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','',' कविता “बेटी युग” में पुत्र–पुत्री दोनों को क्या मिलना चाहिए?\nA) नया घर B) पूरी शिक्षा C) खिलौने D) भोजन','','','उत्तर: B) पूरी शिक्षा\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91660,2291,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','',' कविता “बेटी युग” में किसका सम्मान पर्व कहा गया है?\nA) बेटा B) बेटी C) माता D) शिक्षक','','','उत्तर: B) बेटी\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91661,2291,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','बेटा–बेटी, सभी पढ़ेंगे — यह कविता “बेटी युग” की पंक्ति है।','','','✔','',0,0,0,NULL),(91662,2291,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','',' कविता “बेटी युग” में केवल बेटों को पढ़ाने की बात कही है। ','','','✘','',0,0,0,NULL),(91663,2291,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में सबके हाथों पुस्तक होने की कल्पना की गई है।','','','✔','',0,0,0,NULL),(91664,2291,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में कहा गया है कि ज्ञान प्राप्त करने से नुकसान होता है।','','','✘','',0,0,0,NULL),(91665,2291,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में बेटियों के सम्मान का पर्व बताया गया है।','','','✔','',0,0,0,NULL),(91666,2291,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','“अभी भी जग में लगे सुनानी।”\n','','','– यह बात कविता की कवयित्री सभी लोगों से कह रही हैं।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91667,2291,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','“बेटी पढ़ेगी, बेटी बढ़ेगी।”','','','– यह संदेश समाज के लिए कविता में दिया गया है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91668,2291,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','“सबके हाथों पुस्तक होगी।”','','','– कवयित्री यह बात समाज को समझाते हुए कहती हैं।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91669,2291,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में सबके हाथों क्या होने की बात कही है?','','','उत्तर: सबके हाथों पुस्तक होने की बात कही है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91670,2291,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','',' कविता “बेटी युग” में किसे नया दौर कहा गया है?','','','उत्तर: बेटी युग को नया दौर कहा गया है।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91671,2291,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में क्या फैल जाएगी?','','','उत्तर: ज्ञान की प्यारी धारा फैल जाएगी।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91672,2291,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','',' बेटी शिक्षित होने से क्या मिलेगा?','','','उत्तर: बेटी शिक्षित होने से उसे सम्मान और शक्ति मिलेगी।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91673,2291,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” किसके लिए प्रेरणादायक है?','','','उत्तर: बेटियों और पूरे समाज के लिए।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91674,2291,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में नया इतिहास कैसे बनेगा?','','','उत्तर: जब बेटियाँ पढ़ेंगी और आगे बढ़ेंगी तब नया इतिहास बनेगा।\nइससे देश की प्रगति भी तेज़ी से होगी।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91675,2291,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में पुस्तक और कलम को क्या बताया गया है?','','','उत्तर: पुस्तक और कलम को ज्ञान का प्रकाश बताया गया है।\nयह बच्चों के भविष्य को उज्ज्वल बनाते हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91676,2291,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में शिक्षा को क्यों महत्वपूर्ण कहा गया है?','','','उत्तर: शिक्षा से सोचने–समझने की क्षमता बढ़ती है।\nइसके कारण बेटियाँ समाज और देश के विकास में भाग ले सकती हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91677,2291,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में दोनों को पूरी शिक्षा देने की बात क्यों कही गई है?','','','उत्तर: क्योंकि शिक्षा से समानता आती है।\nयह लड़का–लड़की दोनों को आगे बढ़ने का अवसर देती है।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91678,2291,'descriptive','5',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में बेटियों की शिक्षा को इतना महत्वपूर्ण क्यों बताया गया है?','','','उत्तर: कविता में कहा गया है कि बेटी पढ़ेगी तो परिवार और समाज आगे बढ़ेगा।\nबेटियाँ शिक्षित होंगी तो वे आत्मनिर्भर बनेंगी।\nइससे देश में नई उमंग और प्रगति की लहर दौड़ेगी।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91679,2291,'descriptive','5',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','कविता “बेटी युग” में नया युग कैसा बताया गया है?','','','उत्तर: नया युग ऐसा बताया गया है जहाँ लड़का–लड़की समान रूप से पढ़ेंगे।\nहर हाथ में पुस्तक होगी और सबको समान अधिकार मिलेंगे।\nइससे समाज ज्ञानवान और मजबूत बनेगा।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91680,2292,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अकबर ने बीरबल को कौन-सा काम सौंपा?\nA) खेत जोतना B) हरा घोड़ा खोजना C) जंगल जाना D) पहाड़ चढ़ना','','','✔ उत्तर: B\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91681,2292,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','बीरबल ने हरा घोड़ा कब मिलने की बात कही?\nA) जब दिन हो B) जब रात हो C) जब रविवार हो D) जब दोस्त आएँ','','','✔ उत्तर: A (जब दिन हो)\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91682,2292,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','सेठ की पत्नी को किस बात की चिंता थी?\nA) घर की सजावट B) सेठ की मेहनत C) लोगों की बुराई D) पैसे की बर्बादी','','','✔ उत्तर: B\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91683,2292,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','सेठ बाज़ार क्यों गया?\nA) कपड़े खरीदने B) अपनी तस्वीर दिखाने C) काम पर जाने D) मंदिर जाने','','','✔ उत्तर: B\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91684,2292,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','बीरबल किस गुण के लिए प्रसिद्ध थे?\nA) धन के लिए B) सुंदरता के लिए C) बुद्धिमत्ता के लिए D) गायन के लिए','','','✔ उत्तर: C\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91685,2292,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','बीरबल ने अकबर के आदेश को बुद्धि से हल किया। ','','','✔ सही','',0,0,0,NULL),(91686,2292,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','सेठ को अपनी तस्वीर बिल्कुल पसंद नहीं आई। ','','','✔ सही','',0,0,0,NULL),(91687,2292,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','सेठ की पत्नी ने हमेशा सच्ची सलाह दी। ','','','✔ सही','',0,0,0,NULL),(91688,2292,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','हरा घोड़ा वास्तव में मिल गया था। ','','','❌ गलत','',0,0,0,NULL),(91689,2292,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','अकबर बीरबल की बुद्धिमानी से प्रभावित नहीं थे।','','',' ❌ गलत','',0,0,0,NULL),(91690,2292,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे “पहले मुझे दिन बताइए।”','','','— यह बीरबल ने अकबर से कहा।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91691,2292,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे “सब लोग तुम्हारी ही बातें मानेंगे।”\n','','','— यह सेठ की पत्नी ने सेठ से कहा।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91692,2292,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे “यह तस्वीर तो बहुत खराब है।”\n','','','— यह सेठ ने चित्रकार से कहा।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91693,2292,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे अकबर ने हरा घोड़ा खोजने का काम किसे दिया?\n','','','— अकबर ने यह काम बीरबल को दिया।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91694,2292,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे सेठ को अपनी तस्वीर क्यों पसंद नहीं आई?\n','','','— क्योंकि उसमें उसकी कमज़ोरियाँ दिखाई दे रही थीं।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91695,2292,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे बीरबल ने समस्या का हल कैसे निकाला?\n','','','— अपनी चतुराई और तर्क से।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91696,2292,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे सेठ की पत्नी क्या चाहती थी?\n','','','— कि लोग सेठ की बात मानें और उसका सम्मान करें।\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91697,2292,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे बीरबल हरा घोड़ा क्यों नहीं खोज पाए?\n','','','— बीरबल ने कहा कि हरा घोड़ा केवल दिन में ही मिल सकता है।\n— यह सुनकर अकबर समझ गए कि ऐसा घोड़ा होता ही नहीं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91698,2292,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे सेठ की तस्वीर देखकर वह क्यों परेशान हुआ?\n','','','— चित्र में उसकी वास्तविक छवि दिखाई गई थी, जो उसे पसंद नहीं आई।\n— उसे लगा कि लोग उसे इसी रूप में देखेंगे और उसका सम्मान नहीं करेंगे।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91699,2292,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे सेठ की पत्नी ने उसे क्या सलाह दी?\n','','','— उसने कहा कि जिस बात को सब लोग मानें, वही सच माना जाएगा।\n— इसलिए सेठ को चाहिए कि लोगों को अपने पक्ष में करे।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91700,2292,'descriptive','5',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे बीरबल ने अकबर की समस्या कैसे हल की?\n','','','— अकबर ने उन्हें हरा घोड़ा लाने का आदेश दिया।\n— बीरबल ने बुद्धिमानी से कहा कि घोड़ा केवल दिन में मिल सकता है।\n— इससे अकबर समझ गए कि ऐसा घोड़ा असंभव है और बीरबल की बुद्धि की प्रशंसा की।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91701,2292,'descriptive','5',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ दो लघुकथाएँ मे सेठ की तस्वीर वाली कथा से क्या शिक्षा मिलती है?','','','— सेठ अपनी असलियत स्वीकार नहीं कर पा रहा था।\n— उसकी पत्नी ने उसे समझाया कि लोगों की राय ही महत्व रखती है।\n— इससे हमें सीख मिलती है कि सच्चाई से भागना नहीं चाहिए।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91702,2293,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में भाषा का मुख्य गुण क्या बताया गया है?\nA) कठिन होना B) मधुर होना C) लंबा होना D) धीमा होना\n','','','✔ उत्तर: B','',0,0,0,NULL),(91703,2293,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” के अनुसार शब्द किसके वाहक होते हैं?\nA) रंग B) स्वाद C) विचार D) खेल\n','','','✔ उत्तर: C','',0,0,0,NULL),(91704,2293,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” के अनुसार अनर्थ पैदा करने वाले शब्द किसे कहते हैं?\nA) मधुर शब्द B) कठोर शब्द C) हास्य शब्द D) विपरीत शब्द\n','','','✔ उत्तर: B','',0,0,0,NULL),(91705,2293,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” के अनुसार भाषा का विकास किससे होता है?\nA) खेल से B) गलतियाँ करने से C) समाज और समय के अनुभव से D) चित्रों से\n','','','✔ उत्तर: C','',0,0,0,NULL),(91706,2293,'mcq','1',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में कौन-सा शब्द महत्वपूर्ण बताया गया है?\nA) मूल्य B) पुस्तक C) ‘भाषा’ D) खिलौना\n','','','✔ उत्तर: C','',0,0,0,NULL),(91707,2293,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में कहा गया है कि शब्द हमारे विचारों का दर्पण हैं। ','','','✔ सही','',0,0,0,NULL),(91708,2293,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” बताता है कि मधुर शब्द हानि पहुँचाते हैं। ','','','❌ गलत','',0,0,0,NULL),(91709,2293,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” के अनुसार भाषा समय व परिस्थिति से बदलती है। ','','','✔ सही','',0,0,0,NULL),(91710,2293,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में कठोर शब्दों की प्रशंसा की गई है। ','','','❌ गलत','',0,0,0,NULL),(91711,2293,'descriptive','1a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में कहा गया है कि सही शब्द मन को उत्साह देते हैं। ','','','✔ सही\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91712,2293,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ “शब्द संपदा” में मनुष्य के विचारों को व्यक्त करते हैं।”','','','— यह लेखक ने पाठकों से कहा।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91713,2293,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ “शब्द संपदा” में “मधुर शब्द लोगों के हृदय को जीत लेते हैं।”','','','— यह लेखक ने बच्चों को समझाया।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91714,2293,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में शब्दों का महत्व क्या बताया गया है?','','','— शब्द मनुष्य के विचारों और भावों को व्यक्त करते हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91715,2293,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” के अनुसार भाषा कैसे बदलती है?','','','— भाषा समय और समाज के अनुसार बदलती है।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91716,2293,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में कठोर शब्दों का प्रभाव कैसा बताया गया है?','','','— वे लोगों के मन को दुख पहुँचाते हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91717,2293,'descriptive','2a',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में मधुर शब्दों की विशेषता क्या है?','','','— वे मन को प्रसन्न करते हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91721,2293,'descriptive','5',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में शब्दों के महत्व को कैसे समझाया गया है?','','','— पाठ में बताया गया है कि शब्द मनुष्य के विचारों और भावनाओं को व्यक्त करते हैं।\n— मधुर और सही शब्द समाज में सम्मान दिलाते हैं।\n— कठोर शब्द मन में कटुता लाते हैं, इसलिए शब्दों का चयन सोच-समझकर करना चाहिए।\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91722,2293,'descriptive','5',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” से हमें क्या शिक्षा मिलती है?','','','— हमें भाषा और शब्दों का उपयोग सावधानी से करना चाहिए।\n— मधुर, सरल और स्पष्ट शब्दों से लोगों के साथ अच्छे संबंध बनते हैं।\n— सही शब्द मन को उत्साह और शक्ति देते हैं।\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91723,2293,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में भाषा के विकास के कारण क्या बताए गए हैं?\n','','','— भाषा परिस्थितियों, समय और समाज के बदलाव से विकसित होती है।\n— मनुष्य के अनुभव और सोच भी भाषा को नया रूप देते हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91724,2293,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” के अनुसार शब्दों का चयन क्यों आवश्यक है?\n','','','— क्योंकि गलत शब्द मन को आहत कर सकते हैं।\n— सही और मधुर शब्द अच्छे संबंध बनाते हैं।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91725,2293,'descriptive','3',2,0,'Out of Text Book','','पाठ शब्द संपदा” में शब्दों को शक्ति क्यों कहा गया है?','','','— क्योंकि शब्द किसी को प्रेरित भी कर सकते हैं और दुखी भी।\n— इसलिए उनका प्रयोग सोच-समझकर करना चाहिए।','',0,0,0,NULL),(91768,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The ____ rock of the Deccan Plateau in Maharashtra turns into black soil after weathering.\n(A) basalt    (B) granite    (C) gneiss    (D) marble\n','','','A) basalt','',0,0,0,NULL),(91769,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','To form a 2.5 cm thick layer of soil, it takes ____ of years.\n(A) tens    (B) hundreds    (C) thousands    (D) lakhs\n','','','C) thousands','',0,0,0,NULL),(91770,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Soil is a complete ____\n(A) bio system    (B) ecosystem    (C) substance    (D) abiotic system\n','','','B) ecosystem','',0,0,0,NULL),(91771,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A ____ uses soil as an ecosystem.\n(A) Microorganism    (B) Farmer    (C) Potter    (D) Carpenter\n','','','B) Farmer','',0,0,0,NULL),(91772,1743,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the columns : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Black soil</td>\n    <td>a. Areas of low rainfall</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Yellow brown soil</td>\n    <td>b. Areas of no rainfall</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Coarse soil</td>\n    <td>c. Areas of extreme rainfall</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>d. Areas of medium rainfall</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i - d), (ii - c), (iii - a)','',0,0,0,NULL),(91774,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The process of weathering of the rock depends on this factor.','','','Regional climate','',0,0,0,NULL),(91775,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The biotic material that is produced due to the decomposition of dead remains of organic materials.','','','Humus','',0,0,0,NULL),(91776,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The proportion of humus is negligible in this soil.','','','Coarse soil','',0,0,0,NULL),(91777,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : This soil is also referred to as \'regur\' soil.','','','Black soil','',0,0,0,NULL),(91778,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : This occurs when the top layer of the soil gets removed due to the force of wind or water.','','','Soil erosion','',0,0,0,NULL),(91779,1743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rocks like granite or gneiss in South India give rise to regur soil.','','','Wrong.\nRocks like granite or gneiss in South India give rise to red soil.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91780,1743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Interactions between the biotic and abiotic components of soil happen sometimes.','','','Wrong.\nInteractions between the biotic and abiotic components of soil go on continuously.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91781,1743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Regur soil is found on the mouth of the rivers in the western coastal strip.','','','Wrong.\nAlluvial soil is found on the mouth of the rivers in the western coastal strip.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91789,1743,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Factor/Process</th>\n    <th>Role in the formation of soils</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Parent Rock</td>\n    <td>a. …………….</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Regional Climate</td>\n    <td>b. …………….</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Organic material</td>\n    <td>c. ………….</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. Microorganisms</td>\n    <td>d. …………….</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Factor/Process</th>\n    <th>Role in the formation of soils</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. Parent Rock</td>\n    <td>a. The rock gets weathered, turns into powdery material which further turns into earthy material.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Regional Climate</td>\n    <td>b. Climate decides the intensity of weathering. Hence, the same type of rock gives rise to different types of soil when it gets exposed to different climates.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Organic material</td>\n    <td>c. Organic material gets mixed into the weathered rock powder to convert it into soil.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. Microorganisms</td>\n    <td>d. Microorganisms decompose the dead remains of organic material which then gets mixed into the soils to form \'humus\'. Humus makes the soil fertile.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(91790,1743,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Text Book','','Complete the following table with reference to soil :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Action</th>\n    <th>Effect</th>\n    <th>Result (w.r.t fertility)</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Construction of embankments</td>\n    <td>…………</td>\n    <td>…………</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>…………….</td>\n    <td>Wind speed decreased</td>\n    <td>…………..</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Farmland is kept fallow for some period</td>\n    <td>………………</td>\n    <td>…………..</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>…………….</td>\n    <td>Humus content increased</td>\n    <td>…………..</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Trenches are dug across a slope</td>\n    <td>……………..</td>\n    <td>…………..</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Litter is burnt in the farm</td>\n    <td>……………..</td>\n    <td>………………</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>……………..</td>\n    <td>Proves favourable for microorganisms</td>\n    <td>……………</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>……………………</td>\n    <td>Salt content in the soil increases</td>\n    <td>……………….</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Chemical fertilizers used excessively</td>\n    <td>………………</td>\n    <td>…………………</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Action</th>\n    <th>Effect</th>\n    <th>Result (w.r.t fertility)</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Construction of embankments</td>\n    <td>Reduces the soil erosion caused due to rain water</td>\n    <td>Soil becomes more fertile</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Planting trees on embankments</td>\n    <td>Wind speed decreases</td>\n    <td>Reduces the soil erosion caused due to wind and soil becomes more fertile</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Farmland is kept fallow for some period</td>\n    <td>Helps retain the fertility of the soil</td>\n    <td>Soil becomes more fertile</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Biotic material mixed in the soil</td>\n    <td>Humus content increased</td>\n    <td>Soil became more fertile</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Trenches are dug across a slope</td>\n    <td>Construction of trenches at different heights checks the velocity of water running along the slopes. Water arrested by them percolates into the ground.</td>\n    <td>Increases the soil fertility. It also increases the ground-water levels.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Litter is burnt in the farm</td>\n    <td>It is a threat to the microorganisms in the soil.</td>\n    <td>Causes soil degradation</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Organic litter is buried in the farm instead of burning it.</td>\n    <td>Proves favourable for microorganisms.</td>\n    <td>Increased the soil fertility</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Excessive irrigation</td>\n    <td>Salt content in the soil increases</td>\n    <td>Makes soil unproductive</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Chemical fertilizers used excessively</td>\n    <td>Chemical residues remains in the soil for many years</td>\n    <td>Becomes a threat to the existence of microorganisms</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(91791,1743,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Complete the flow chart : ','','','i. Black soil           ii. Laterite soil              iii. Yellow brown soil','',1,0,0,NULL),(91792,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is humus?','','','The microorganisms decompose the dead remains of organic material which then gets mixed into the soils to form \'humus\'. Humus makes the soil fertile.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91793,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What elements are needed to produce compost?','','','Organic waste, water, heat, etc. are the elements needed for the production of compost.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91794,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do the regions with non-arable soil fulfil their food requirements?','','','The regions with non-arable soil fulfil their food requirements by importing food.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91795,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What leads to soil degradation?','','','Excessive spraying of chemicals, use of chemical fertilizers, insecticides, weedicides, etc. in order to obtain a higher agricultural yield, leads to soil degradation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91796,1743,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Organic Manures','','','i. Organic manure is necessary to turn the weathered powdery rock into soil.\nii. It comes from the decomposition of organic matter like the remains of plants and animals, vegetal litters, roots of plants, etc.\niii. Organic matter like remains of plants and animals are continuously decomposed by microorganisms and certain other organisms while organic matter like vegetal litters, roots of plants, etc. get decomposed by water.\niv. This organic manure then gets mixed in the soil and produces humus, which makes the soil fertile.\nv. Now-a-days, vermicompost is produced on a large scale. Its production requires sufficient period of time, organic waste, water, heat, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91797,1743,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Importance of soil in plant growth','','','i. Soil is indispensible for the production and growth of plants that are an important component of the living world on the earth.\nii. Soil provides support to the plants.\niii. In regions having fertile soils, vegetation is abundant. e.g.: equatorial regions\niv. In regions where the soil is not fertile, vegetation is scanty. e.g.: desert regions\nv. In regions having shortage of soils, vegetation is not seen. e.g.: polar regions\nvi. Fertile soil is equally important for the growth of plants along with favourable climate, availability of abundant water and sunlight.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91798,1743,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Importance of soil in human civilization','','','i. Man has been using soil as a resource to obtain agricultural produce, since ancient time.\nii. He eventually realized that fertile soils along the river beds give higher yields, which led humans to settle in the valleys and live in groups.\niii. This gave rise to the ancient river valley civilizations like the Indus Valley civilization.\niv. As the population grew, man started producing more crops.\nv. For a higher production, fertile soil and availability of water were required. As a result, there was a competition to discover the fertile lands and settle there.\nvi. In order to further increase the production, man made use of different fertilizers which led to record agricultural productions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91799,1743,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Place where you will get the information about the suitability of a soil for a particular crop.','','','i. Soil acts as a medium of nutrition for the plants and hence it is important to know about the proportions of the various ingredients of soil in order to determine the suitability of a soil for a particular crop.\nii. The proportions of the various ingredients in the soil can be determined through \'soil testing\' done in soil testing laboratories.\niii. Such laboratories are available in every district. Samples of the surface soil from the different areas of the district are collected in these laboratories.\niv. These samples are dried in an open space under a shade for eight to ten days and are then sifted through a sieve.\nv. Different tests are conducted on these soil samples to determine its suitability for a particular crop.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91800,1743,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Geographic Indication (GI)','','','i. Certain soils are best suited for only a certain type of crops.\nii. As a result, the production of that crop becomes the speciality of that soil and consequently, of that area.\niii. Such crops are given the status of Geographic Indication (GI).\ne.g.: Haapus mango from Sindhudurg, custard apple from Beed district and oranges from Nagpur.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91801,1743,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Text Book','','Measures of soil conservation','','','Considering the importance of soils for occupations like agriculture, it is extremely important to conserve soil and protect the fertile soils of the farmlands from getting washed away.\nTo prevent the soil erosion due to rain water and wind, the following measures have been suggested / adopted:\ni. Government of Maharashtra has implemented \'the catchment area development program\'. As per the program, the following measures for conservation of soil were undertaken:\na. Constructing embankments along the slopes in rural areas:\nThis checks the velocity of the water down the slopes and reduces soil erosion.\nMoreover, planting trees can also control the velocity of winds, thereby reducing the erosion of soil by wind. Also, the roots hold the soil in place and reduce the erosion.\nb. Introducing Continuous Contour Trenches (CCTs) along the steep slopes:\nConstructing Continuous Contour Trenches (CCTs) along the steep slopes at different heights helps check the velocity of running water down these slopes and, thereby reduces the soil erosion.\nii. These measures led to the success of the \'Arrest water, let it percolate\' scheme, which resulted in reduced soil erosion and also raised the ground-water level.\niii. Also, under the recently launched scheme, Jalayukt Shivar, the following measures are undertaken:\na. Construction of farm bunds\nb. Arresting waters of small streams\nc. Connecting the streams\niv. A regular use of organic manures and vermicompost helps to maintain the pH level of the soil, increases the proportion of humus in the soil and retains its fertility.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91803,1743,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The local agricultural produce determines the staple diet of the people.','','','i. Different crops are produced according to the different types of soil and its properties.\nii. Different regions have different types of soil depending on the regional climate and hardness of the rocks found there.\niii. For example, jowar and bajra are produced in the regur soils of the Maharashtra plateau, rice is produced in the soils of Konkan, Tamil Nadu, Kerala and Karnataka and potatoes are produced in regions like Madhya Pradesh where soils are well drained.\nThus, the local agricultural produce determines the staple diet of the people.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91804,1743,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Human settlements get concentrated in the areas having fertile soil.','','','i. Higher agricultural yields can be obtained in areas with fertile soils.\nii. Regions where soils are fertile and arable are self-reliant as far as the food production is concerned.\niii. On the other hand, regions where soil is not arable have to import food to fulfil their food requirements.\niv. Agro-based industries also flourish in such areas and show signs of development.\nHence, human settlements get concentrated in the areas with fertile soil.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91805,1743,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Different types of soils found in Maharashtra','','','','',0,0,0,NULL),(91806,1743,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Different types of soils found in Maharashtra','','','There are five major types of soils in Maharashtra:\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <tr>\n        <th>Type of Soil</th>\n        <th>Places it is found</th>\n        <th>Rainfall requirements and Characteristics</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Coarse soil</td>\n        <td>Hilltops in the western part of Maharashtra.<br>e.g.: Ajanta, Balaghat, Mahadeo hills, etc.</td>\n        <td>Formed in areas of low rainfall.<br>Proportion of humus is negligible.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Regur or Black soil</td>\n        <td>\n            There are two types:<br>\n            <b>Dark black soil:</b> Western part of Deccan plateau<br>\n            <b>Medium black soil:</b> Eastern part (Vidarbha)\n        </td>\n        <td>\n            Formed in areas of medium rainfall, in valleys and alluvial plains of river basins.<br>\n            Proportion of organic components is less.\n        </td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Laterite soil</td>\n        <td>Coastal belt of Konkan to the west of Sahyadris and in the east of Vidarbha</td>\n        <td>\n            Formed in areas of heavy rainfall.<br>\n            Soil has reddish-orange colour.\n        </td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Alluvial soil of the coastal strip</td>\n        <td>\n            Mouth of the rivers on the western coastal strip<br>\n            e.g.: Around Panvel, Uran coast, Dharamtar creek, etc.\n        </td>\n        <td>Deposited at the mouth of the rivers in the western coastal strip.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n        <td>Yellow brown soil</td>\n        <td>Chandrapur, eastern parts of Bhandara and parts of Sahyadri mountains</td>\n        <td>\n            Found in areas of extreme rainfall.<br>\n            Not very useful for agriculture.\n        </td>\n    </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(91807,1743,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different constituents of soil?','','','The different constituents of soil are as follows:\ni. Air\nii. Water\niii. Minerals\niv. Organic material\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91808,1743,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','From where do the abiotic factors become available for soil formation?','','','The different abiotic factors become available for soil formation from the varied natural elements like rocks, climate of the region, time, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91809,1743,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','On which factors does the variety in the soil depend?','','','The variety in the soil depends on the following factors interacting over time:\ni. Climatic conditions\nii. Relief of the land surface (elevation, orientation, and slope of terrain)\niii. Organisms in the soil\niv. Parent Materials of the soil\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91810,1743,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why do equatorial region have fertile soils?','','','The process of soil formation including weathering of rocks depends on the climate of a region.\nIn the regions receiving high rainfall and experiencing high temperatures, the process of soil formation is faster.\nAs the above climatic conditions are found in the equatorial, soil formation process is faster in this region.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91811,1743,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the vegetation sparse in deserts?','','','Vegetation needs fertile soil in order to grow.\nAs the soils in desert regions are not fertile, vegetation is sparse in deserts.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91812,1743,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the map and answer the following questions:','1. Which soil occupies most of the area in Maharashtra?\n2. In which areas are laterite soils found?\n3. Which soil is found in the river valleys of Maharashtra?\n4. Which soil is found in parts of the Sahyadri Ranges?\n5. In which region is alluvial soil found?\n','','1) Black soil occupies most of the area in Maharashtra.\n2) Laterite soil is found in Ratnagiri and Sindhudurga districts.\nIt is also found in major parts of Raigad and certain parts of Nashik, Pune, Satara, Sangli, Kolhapur and Gadchiroli districts.\n3) Alluvial soil is found in the river valleys of Maharashtra.\n4) Yellow Brown soil (Hilly regions) is found in parts of Sahyadri Ranges.\n5) Alluvial soil is found in Mumbai Suburban District and Mumbai City District.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(91813,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ soil is found in the Deccan plateau of Maharashtra.\n(A) Black soil  (B) Alluvial soil  (C) Laterite soil  (D) Sandy soil\n','','','A) Black soil','',0,0,0,NULL),(91814,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ are produced in regions like Madhya Pradesh where soils are well drained.\n(A) Wheat  (B) Potatoes  (C) Rice  (D) Sugarcane\n','','','B) Potatoes','',0,0,0,NULL),(91815,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The local agricultural produce determines the ____ of the people.\n(A) Language  (B) Festivals  (C) Staple diet  (D) Occupation\n','','','C) Staple diet','',0,0,0,NULL),(91816,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','If the ____ level of the soil gets disturbed, it is a sign of soil degradation.\n(A) Moisture  (B) pH  (C) Temperature  (D) Colour\n','','','B) pH','',0,0,0,NULL),(91817,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the first step in the process of soil formation?','','','Weathering of rock into powdery material is the first step in the process of soil formation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91818,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are embankments constructed on farms?','','','Embankments are constructed on farms in order to prevent the soil erosion happening due to running water and wind.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91819,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ soil is found in Osmanabad district of Maharashtra.\na) Black      b) Lateritic      c) Yellow-Brown      d) Alluvial\n','','','a) Black      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91820,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Laterite soil is found in ____ district of Maharashtra.\na) Amravati      b) Beed      c) Raigad      d) Nanded\n','','','c) Raigad      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91821,1743,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In Madhya Pradesh, ____ is cultivated in regions where the soils are well drained.\na) Jowar      b) Bajra      c) Potato      d) Rice\n','','','c) Potato      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91822,1743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Black soil is found in Deccan Plateau region.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91823,1743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Jowar is cultivated on a large scale in Konkan region.','','','False ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91824,1743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rice is a staple in the diet of people from Kerala.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91825,1743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Beed district of Maharashtra is famous for the cultivation of custard apple.','','','True ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91826,1743,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Planting trees helps in reducing erosion of soil.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(91827,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which rocks have given rise to red soil in South India?','','','Granite and gneiss rocks have given rise to red soil in South India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91828,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which soil is formed due to leaching of besalt rock in Western Sahyadris?','','','Lateritic soil is formed due to leaching of besalt rock in Western Sahyadris.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91829,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by humus?','','','The biotic material that gets mixed in soils is known as humus.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91830,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','For which product is the district of Sindhudurg famous?','','','The district of Sindhudurg is famous for Happus mango.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91831,1743,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which district of Maharashtra is famous for orange cultivation?','','','Nagpur district of Maharashtra is famous for orange cultivation.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91832,1743,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','To the west of Sahyadris, laterite soils are formed from basalt rock.','','','1) It rains heavily to the west of Sahyadris (i.e. in Konkan region). \n2) The climate to the west of Sahyadris is humid. \n3) Heavy rainfall and humidity leads to leaching of besalt rock on a large scale. This process gives rise to laterite soils. Thus, to the west of Sahyadris, laterite soils are formed from besalt rock.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91833,1743,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Humus content in the soil increases.','','','1) The regular use of organic manures, vermicompost, compost helps in maintaining the pH balance in the soil. \n2) A large number of microorganisms exist in the soil with high pH . \n3) Microorganisms decompose the dead remains of the plants and animals and other organic materials. The biotic material thus produed is call humus. Thus, by regular use of organic manures, humus content in the soil increases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91834,1743,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Soil formation process is faster in the equatorial climate.','','','1) High temperatures and high rainfall are the main features of equatorial climate. \n2) The temperature of Equatorial region is around 27°C to 30°C throughout the year. \n3) The mean annual rainfall in Equatorial region is around 2500 mm to 3000 mm . It rains throughout the year in this region. \n4) In the region of high temperatures and high rainfall, the process of soil formation is faster. Thus, due to high temperatures and high rainfall, soil formation process is faster in the equatorial climate.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91835,1743,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','The salinity of the soil increases.','','','1) Irrigation facilities are utilized for producing greater amount of crops. \n2) But, excessive irrigation draws the slats from the soil upwards and makes the soil saline and then unproductive. Thus, due to excessive irrigation, the salinity of the soil increases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91836,1743,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Rice is the staple diet of the people from Konkan.','','','1) Hot and humid climate, high rainfall and alluvial soil from Konkan are favourable for the cultivation of rice. Therefore, rice is grown on a large scale in Konkan. \n2) The local agricultural produce determines the staple diet of the people. Therefore, rice is the staple diet of the people from Konkan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91837,1743,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Soil erosion.','','','1) A top layer of soil gets removed due to heavy rains or running water of rivers. \n2) The soil gets eroded due to high velocity of winds and steep slopes. Thus, soil erosion occurs due to running water, winds and slopes.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91838,1743,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Text Book','','Soil degradation','','','1) To obtain higher agricultural yield, chemical fertilizers, insecticides, weedicides and irrigation technique is used. \n2) However, the excessive spraying of chemicals fertilizers leads to soil degradation. In such condition, the proportion of humus in soil decreases and the pH value of soil gets disturbed. \n3) Excessive irrigation draws the salt from the soil upwards and makes the soil saline and then unproductive. Thus, soil erosion occurs due to chemicals, chemical fertilizers and excessive irrigation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91839,1743,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the relation between soil and rise of civilization.','','','1) In ancient times, man realized that sowing of seeds in the soil leads to the growth of plants and yields crops. Therefore, man started using soil as a resource.\n2) Gradually, man realized that the fertile soils along the river beds give higher yields.\n3) Therefore, man settled in the river valleys and started living there in groups. \n4) This further led to the rise of ancient river valley civilizations. For example, the Indus civilization.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91840,1743,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the disadvantages of non availability of fertile soil in particular regions/countries?','','','The disadvantages of non availability of fertile soil in particular regions/countryare as follows : \n1) The regions/countries where soils are not fertile/arable need to fulfil their food requirements through imports. \n2) For example, countries like Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Omen, etc. fulfil their food requirements by importing food from China, India and U.S.A. \n3) The non availability of fertile soil adversely affects the development of agriculture and further the development of agro-based industries. \n4) This ultimately leads to slow development of such regions/countries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91841,1743,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the advantages of availability of fertile soil in particular regions/countries?','','','The advantages of availability of fertile soil in particular regions/countries are as follows : \n1) The regions/countries having fertile/arable soils are self relient as far as food\'\nproduction is concerned. \n2) Human settlements get concentrated in such areas. \n3) Agrobased industries flourish in these areas at later dates. \n4) This ultimately leads to fast development of such regions/countries.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91842,1743,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the difference between the soil and the earth.','','','1) The soil contains the powdered form of weathered rock, partially or completely decomposed organic material and innumerable microorganisms. \n2) Interaction between the biotic and abiotic components in soil go on continuously. \n3) The soil is a complete ecosystem. \n4) On the other hand, the earth is one of the substances in a soil. \n5) The difference between the soil and the earth can be explained with the help of the following example : what is used by a farmer, is soil. A farmer uses soil as an ecosystem. On the other hand, what is used by a potter, is earth. Potter uses the earth as a raw material.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91843,1743,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in detail about the factors necessary for soil formation.','','','The parent rock, the climate of the region, the biotic material, the slope of the land and time are the necessary factors for soil formation. These factors together influence the process of soil formation. Their detailed information is as follows :\n1) Parent rock : Parent rock in a region is an important factor in soil formation. Depending on the hardness of the parent rock and the climate of the region, the parent rock gets weathered. The rock turns into powdery material which further turns into earthy material. For example, (i) The besalt rock of the Deccan Plateau in Maharashtra turns into black soil after weathering. Black soil is also referred to as \'regur\' soil. (ii) Rocks like granite or gneiss in South India give rise to red soil.\n2) Regional climate : The intensity of weathering of rock depends on the climate of the region. The same type of rock gives rise to different types of soils when it is exposed to different climates. For example, In dry climate of Deccan Plateau, weathering of basalt leads to the formation of black soil whereas, in the humid climate of the Western Sahyadris, the leaching of the same basalt rock leads to the formation of lateritic soil.\n3) Biotic material : The biotic material comes from the decomposition of the remains of the plants and animals in a particular region. Many microorganisms and certain other organisms help in decomposition process. The decomposed biotic material gets mixed into the soils and is called humus. If the proportion of humus in the soil is greater, the soils become fertile.\n4) Time : Soil formation is a natural and slow process. To form a 2.5 cm thick layer of top soil, it takes thousands of years. In the region of high rainfall and higher temperatures, the process of soil formation is faster. On the other hand, in the region of low rainfall and low temperatures, the process of soil formation takes more time.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91844,1743,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the coarse and regur types of soil in Maharashtra.','','','The details of coarse and regur types of soil in Maharashtra is as follows :\n(1) Coarse soil : Coarse type of soil is a result of weathering and low rainfall. This soil can be found in the hilltops of the western part of Deccan Plateau and in Ajanta, Balaghat and Mahadeo Hills. The proportion of humus is negligible in this soil.\n(2) Regur soil : Regur type of soil is also known as black soil. It is found in areas of medium rainfall in the valleys and alluvial plains of river basins. Dark black soil is found in\nthe western part of Deccan Plateau while medium black soil is found in the eastern part of Maharashtra (Vidarbha). Though it is black in colour, proportion of organic components is less in this soil.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91845,1743,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information about laterite, alluvial soil of the coastal strip and yellow-brown types of soil in Maharashtra.','','','The information about laterite, alluvial soil of the coastal strip and yellow-brown types of soil in Maharashtra are as follows :\n1) Laterite soil : Laterite type of soil is found to the west of the Sahyadris in the coastal belt of Konkan. It is also found in the east of Vidharbha. In areas of very heavy rainfall, the eroded rocks get washed away in a large quantity. As a result, the parent rock lies bare open. The iron in the rock reacts with oxygen in the air and causes chemical reactions. This chemical reaction gives the reddish-orange colour to the soil.\n2) Alluvial soil of the coastal strip : Majority of the rivers flowing in the Konkan region are short. These rivers flow with great speed. The alluvial brought by them gets deposited at the mouth of rivers. Alluvial soil of the coastal strip is mainly found at the mouth of the rivers in western coastal strip. It is found in the areas around Panvel, Uran coast, Dharamtar creek, etc.\n3) Yellow-brown soil : Yellow-Brown type of soil is found in the areas of extreme rainfall. This soil is very fertile in nature. Therefore, it is useful for agriculture. It is mainly found in Chandrapur, western part of Bhandara and parts of Sahyadri mountains.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91846,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The apparent movement of the sun means …………..\na) the sun revolves around the earth in a year     \n b) it appears that the sun moves to the north or south in a year      \nc) the earth keeps on changing its position\n','','','b) it appears that the sun moves to the north or south in a year      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91847,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','If the axis of the earth were not tilted ……………\na) the earth would not have rotated around itself      \nb) the earth would have revolved around the sun with greater velocity      \nc) different parallels on the earth would have experienced the same climate throughout the year\n','','','c) different parallels on the earth would have experienced the same climate throughout the year','',0,0,0,NULL),(91848,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','21st June and 22nd December are solstice days, that is, ……………\na) the sun starts its southward journey from Tropic of Cancer on 21st June and northward journey from Tropic of Capricorn on 22nd December     \nb) The Dakshinayan of the sun takes place between 21st June and 22nd December      \nc) The Uttarayan of the earth takes place between 21st June and 22^nd December\n','','','a) the sun starts its southward journey from Tropic of Cancer on 21^st June and northward journey from Tropic of Capricorn on 22nd December      ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91849,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The revolution of the earth around the sun and the tilt of the axis of the earth together are responsible for the following seasons.\na) Summer, rainy season, Retreat of Monsoon and winter      b) Summer, winter and spring      c) Summer and winter\n','','','c) Summer and winter','',0,0,0,NULL),(91850,1744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The earth\'s velocity varies according to the period of revolution.','','','Wrong.\nThe earth\'s velocity changes due to the gravitational forces of the sun and the earth. It is reduced during the aphelion position and increases during the perihelion position.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91851,1744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','If we observe from the northern hemisphere, we see the apparent movement of the sun.','','','Wrong.\nWe can observe the apparent movement of the sun not only from the northern hemisphere but from any place on the earth.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91852,1744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','The dates of equinox change every year.','','','Wrong.\nThe dates of equinox may change by a day or so every year.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91853,1744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','North Canada experiences summer from September to March.','','','Wrong.\nNorth Canada experiences winter from September to March.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91854,1744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','When it is summer in South Africa, Australia has winter.','','','Wrong.\nBoth South Africa and Australia lie in the same hemisphere and will have same season at a given time of the year. So when it is summer in South Africa, it will be summer in Australia as well.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91855,1744,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','Duration of daytime is less on the vernal and autumnal equinoxes.','','','Wrong.\nOn both, the vernal as well as autumnal equinoxes, the nighttime and daytime are of equal duration.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91856,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The period from 21st  June to 22nd December when the position of rising sun keeps on moving towards the south.','','','Dakshinayan','',0,0,0,NULL),(91857,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The period from 22nd December to 21st  June when the position of rising sun keeps on moving towards the north.','','','Uttarayan','',0,0,0,NULL),(91858,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The position of the earth in the first week of January when it is at its minimum distance from the sun.','','','Perihelion','',0,0,0,NULL),(91859,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The position of the earth in the first week of July when it is at the farthest point from the sun.','','','Aphelion','',0,0,0,NULL),(91860,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The equinox which occurs on 21st March in the northern hemisphere.','','','Spring or vernal equinox','',0,0,0,NULL),(91861,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the following : The equinox which occurs on 23rd September in the northern hemisphere.','','','Autumnal equinox','',0,0,0,NULL),(91862,1744,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Text Book','','Draw a diagram showing the cycle of seasons in the southern hemisphere.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(91863,1744,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Text Book','','Spot the error in the diagram given below.','','','The dates of winter solstice and summer solstice with respect to northern hemisphere are mentioned incorrectly. The dates of winter solstice and summer solstice are 22nd  December and 21st June respectively.\n','',1,0,1,NULL),(91864,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','What leads to the occurrence of seasons?','','','The combined effect of the revolution of the earth and the tilt of its axis of rotation, leads to the occurrence of seasons.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91865,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','What is the duration of the day on equinox?','','','On the day of equinox both, nighttime and daytime are of equal duration. Thus, the duration of day on equinox is 12 hours.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91866,1744,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the difference between diurnal and annual apparent movement of the sun?','','','i. The sun\'s east-west motion from sunrise to sunset is known as its diurnal apparent motion.\nii. The change in the position of the sunrise towards the north or the south in a year is called as the annual apparent movement of the sun.\niii. Thus, the diurnal movement of the sun is related to the rotation of the earth, whereas its annual apparent movement is related to the revolution of the earth and the tilt of its axis.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91867,1744,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the perihelion and aphelion position of the earth take place?','','','i. The sun is at one of the two centres of elliptical path of the earth around the sun.\nii. The distance of earth from the sun does not remain same, though the position of sun is fixed.\niii. When the distance of earth from the sun is minimum, it is known as perihelion position of the earth. This occurs in the first week of January.\niv. Similarly, when the earth is at the farthest from the sun, it is known as aphelion position of the earth. This occurs in the first week of July.\nv. During the perihelion position, the southern end of the earth\'s axis is towards the sun, while during the aphelion position the northern end of the axis is towards the sun.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91868,1744,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What do you mean by equinox?','','','i. During the earth\'s revolution around the sun, the equator receives perpendicular sunrays only on two days.\nii. On these days, both the poles of the earth are at same distance from the sun. This is called as equinox.\niii. The circle of illumination divides all the parallels from the North Pole to the South Pole equally and also coincides with the great circle defined by the 0<sup>0</sup> and 180<sup>0</sup> meridians.\niv. Hence, on these days, the daytime and the nighttime are of equal duration everywhere on the earth.\nv. This condition occurs on 21st March and 23rd  September.\nvi.  21st  March is called as spring or vernal equinox and 23rd September is called as autumnal equinox in the northern hemisphere.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91870,1744,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Text Book','','Why is the effect of the seasons not experienced in the equatorial region?','','','i. The duration of sunlight, equinoxes and the solstices decide the seasons on the earth.\nii. The sunrays are perpendicular on the equatorial region on the day of equinoxes i.e. 21st March and 23rd  September. On this day, the daytime and the nighttime are equal everywhere on the earth and the circle of illumination divides all the parallels from North Pole to South Pole equally.\niii. On 21st  June, the north pole of the earth is tilted towards the sun and the sunrays become perpendicular on the Tropic of Cancer in the northern hemisphere. On this day, the circle of illumination divides the equator in two equal parts, as shown in the figure. It is summer season in the northern hemisphere and winter in the southern hemisphere.\niv. Similarly, on 22nd  December the south pole of the earth is tilted towards the sun and the sunrays become perpendicular on the Tropic of Capricorn in the southern hemisphere. On this day also the circle of illumination divides the equator in two equal parts. It is summer season in the southern hemisphere and winter in the northern hemisphere.\nThus, we can see that the climate in the equatorial region is almost same in all the cases and hence the effect of the seasons is not experienced in the equatorial region.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91876,1744,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Why is the sun visible for more than 24 hours during Dakshinayan in the region between the Antarctic Circle and the South Pole?','','','i. Dakshinayan starts after the equinox of 23rd  September, wherein the southern hemisphere starts moving towards the sun. Due to this, the duration of day in the southern hemisphere starts increasing.\nii. On 22nd  December, the South Pole is tilted towards the sun.\niii. On this day, the entire region from the Antarctic Circle to the South pole faces the sun.\nHence, the sun is visible for more than 24 hours during Dakshinayan in the region between the Antarctic Circle and the South Pole.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91877,1744,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Why are penguins not found at the North Pole?','','',': i. Penguins are found on the Antarctic region near the South Pole.\nii. They require extremely cold climate and longer nights for their survival. These conditions are found at the South Pole.\niii. Similar conditions also prevail at the North Pole, but there is no land area near the North Pole. Due to this, we do no find penguins at the North Pole.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91878,1744,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Apparent movement of sun','','','i. The position of the sun on the horizon at sunrise and sunset appears to change everyday. However, in reality the sun does not change its position.\nii. This happens due to the revolution of the earth around the sun and the inclination of the earth\'s axis.\niii. The period from 21st  June to 22nd  December, when the position of the rising sun appears to shift towards the south, is called as Dakshinayan.\niv. The period from 22nd  December to 21st  June, when the position of the rising sun appears to shift towards the north, is called as Uttarayan.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91879,1744,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Effects of seasons on the living world.','','','i. Different season occur on earth due to the revolution of the earth around the sun and the inclination of the earth\'s axis.\nii. This results in diversity on earth and affects the life on the earth.\niii. The region in the North Pole and the South Pole receives sunlight only for few months of the year. Even in this period, certain flora and fauna develops in this region.\niv. Penguins, seal and walruses are found in the Antarctic region in the southernmost part of earth, while animals like reindeer, polar bears, Artic foxes are found in the Arctic region in the nothernmost part.\nv. People and living things adapt to the climatic conditions in their respective regions.\nvi. However, this adaptation is possible only upto a certain limit and organisms prefer a certain habitat.\nvii. Number of birds temporarily migrate to other regions during extreme cold climatic condition when food is also scarce. Trees also bear fruits only in a particular season.\nviii. Occupations of humans like agriculture are also dependent on seasons.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91880,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which month the duration of the day was around 12 hours?','','','The duration of day was around 12 hours in the month of September.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91882,1744,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the figure and answer the following questions:','1. How is the northern hemisphere in which you live positioned relative to the sun on the dates given in the figure?\n2. Which season will you experience in the northern hemisphere around 22nd  of December?\n3. What would be the season in the northern hemisphere around 21st  of June?\n4. If it is winter in the northern hemisphere, which season will it be in the opposite hemisphere?\n5. At any given point of time, why are the seasons in the northern and southern hemispheres different?\n','','1) The position of the northern hemisphere relative to the sun on the dates given in the figure is mentioned below:\ni. On 21st  March i.e. Spring Equinox, the northern hemisphere and the southern hemisphere are both at the equal distance from the sun.\nii. On 21st  June i.e. Summer Solstice, the northern hemisphere is tilted towards the sun while the southern hemisphere is away from the sun.\niii. On 23rd  September i.e. Autumnal Equinox, the northern hemisphere and the southern hemisphere are both at the equal distance from the sun.\niv. On 22nd  December i.e. Winter Solstice, the northern hemisphere is tilted away from the sun while the southern hemisphere is tilted towards the sun.\n2) Around 22nd  of December in the Northern hemisphere, we will experience winter season.\n3)  Around 21st  of June in the northern hemisphere, it would be summer season.\n4) If it is winter in the northern hemisphere, it will be summer season in the opposite hemisphere i.e. southern hemisphere.\n5) At any given point of time, there are different seasons in the northern and southern hemispheres due to the inclination of earth\'s axis at angle of   23°30’ with respect to the vertical.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(91883,1744,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Figure shows the position of the earth with its tilted axis on 21st  June and 22nd  December. Its also shows its illuminated and dark portions.\nObserve the figure and answer the following questions:\n','1. In fig \' A\', which pole is receiving sunlight?\n2. In fig \' B \', which pole is not receiving sunlight?\n3. Which hemisphere has the longest daytime on 21st  June?\n4. Which hemisphere will have the longest night on 22nd  December?\n5. On which date will the Tropic of Cancer receive perpendicular sunrays?\n','','1) In fig \' A \', the north pole is receiving sunlight.\n2) In fig \' B \', the north pole is not receiving sunlight.\n3) On 21st  June, the northern hemisphere has the longest daytime.\n4) On 22nd  December, the northern hemisphere will have the longest nighttime.\n5) The Tropic of Cancer will receive perpendicular sunrays on 21st  of June.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(91884,1744,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Figure shows the position of the earth with its tilted axis on 21st  June and 22nd  December. Its also shows its illuminated and dark portions.\nObserve the figure and answer the following questions:\n','1. Considering the position of the north pole, which season will prevail in the northern hemisphere from 22 March to 23 September?\n2. The cricket matches in Australia are generally in summer. When does Australia have summers?\n3. In which period is the \'midnight sun\' observable in Norway? Which season will there be at that time?\n4. At what time will the midnight sun be visible at the \'Bharati\' research station of India at Antarctica? What will be the season there at that time?\n','','1) Considering the position of the north pole, it will be summer season in the northern hemisphere from 22 March to 23 September.\n2) Australia will have summer season from 23 September to 21 March.\n3) The \'midnight sun\' is observed in Norway in the period from 22 March to 23 September. It is summer season in Norway at that time.\n4) The midnight sun will be visible at the \'Bharati\' research station of India at Antarctica in the period from 23 September to 21 March. It will be summer season there at that time.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(91885,1744,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What effects do the seasons have on the biodiversity in India? Write briefly about it.','','','The different seasons have various effects on the biodiversity in India.\ni. Effect on human life: The different seasons in India have effect on the clothing, the type of food as well as festivals celebrated in the country.\nii. Effect on birds and animals: Animals and birds also get affected by the changes in season and respond accordingly. For example, Siberian cranes migrate to India when it is extreme cold in the northern side of India. However, as the temperature in India starts rising, they migrate back to their original habitat.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91886,1744,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The summer capital of Jammu and Kashmir is Srinagar and the winter capital is Jammu. What could be the reason?','','','i. In the state of Jammu and Kashmir, the city of Srinagar is to the north of the Jammu city.\nii. Due to this, the effect of winter is more in Srinagar than in Jammu.\niii. Srinagar is accessible in summer season but not in winter season.\nHence, the summer capital of Jammu and Kashmir is Srinagar and the winter capital is Jammu.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91887,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The position of the rising sun keeps moving towards ____ in the period from 21st June to 22nd December.\na) north             	b) south 	              c) east\n','','','b) south ','',0,0,0,NULL),(91888,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','During revolution of the earth, the equator receives perpendicular rays on the days of ____\na) equinox	 b) solstice 	c) eclipse\n','','','equinox','',0,0,0,NULL),(91889,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On the day of equinox, the circle of ____ coincides with the great circle defined by two opposite meridians of 0° and 180°.\na) illumination 	b) equator 	c) horizon\n','','','illumination','',0,0,0,NULL),(91892,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the longest days in the northern and the southern hemispheres?','','','The Solstice days of 21st  June and 22nd  December are the longest days in the northern and the southern hemispheres respectively.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91894,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the four seasons considered in India?','','','The four seasons considered in India are summer, the rainy season, the period of retreating monsoon and winter.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91895,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which four seasons are considered in Europe and North America?','','','The four seasons which are considered in Europe and North America are summer, autumn, winter and spring.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91897,1744,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why are seasons other than summer and winter also considered in different parts of the world?','','','a. Seasons are mainly caused due to the revolution of the earth and the tilt of the earth\'s axis of rotation.\nb. Apart from these, the changes in the atmosphere, vapour on the air, the wind and the precipitation also influence the seasons.\nc. Continuous occurrence of rain in a specific period gives rise to additional seasons besides summer and winter season.\nThus, due to the local climatic conditions, seasons other than summer and winter are also considered in different parts of the world.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91903,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which direction will the location of sunrise and sunset appear to move after 22nd  December?','','','The location of sunrise and sunset will appear to move in north direction after 22nd  December.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91904,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On equinoxes days, the two poles experience either sunrise or sunset. On which pole will be the sunrise on 21st  March?','','','On equinoxes days, the two poles experience either sunrise or sunset. Sunrise will be on the North Pole on 21st  March.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91905,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the period of Dakshinayan?','','','The period of Dakshinayan is from 21st  June to 22nd  December.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91906,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the period of Uttarayan?','','','The period of Uttrayan is from 22nd  December to 21st  June.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91907,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which day is the longest day in the northern hemisphere?','','','21st June is the longest day in the northern hemisphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91908,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which day is the longest day in the southern hemisphere?','','','22nd December is the longest day in the southern hemisphere.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91909,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Why are penguins not found at the North Pole?','','','Penguins are native to the Southern Pole and so are not found at the North Pole.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91910,1744,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the effects of tilted axis of the earth?','','','Occurrence of seasons and change in seasons are the effects of tilted axis of the earth.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91911,1744,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by the cycles of the seasons?','','','1) Due to difference in the heat of the sun received during different periods of the year, we experience summer and winter every year. \n2) Seasons in the southern and the northern hemispheres occur during different periods, one after the other. This is called the cycles of the seasons.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91912,1744,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What would have happened if the earth\'s axis were not tilted?','','','1) If the earth\'s axis were not tilted, the same climatic conditions would have prevailed throughout the year and the seasons would not have occurred. \n2) The same climatic conditions would have prevailed on each of the différent parallels.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91913,1744,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by equinox day?','','','1) When both the poles of the earth are at the same distance from the earth, it is called an equinox position.\n(2) This position occurs on 21 March and 23 September.\n(3) Therefore, these two days are called equinox days.\n(4) On these days, the sun rays fall perpendicular on the equator at noon.\n(5) On equinoxes, everywhere on the earth nighttime and daytime are of equal duration.\n(6) On equinoxes, the circle of illumination coincides with great circle defined by two opposite meridians.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91914,1744,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by solstice day?','','','1) When any of the poles of the earth is inclined towards the sun to its maximum, it is called solstice position. \n2) This position occurs on 21 June and 22 December. \n3) Therefore, these two days are called solstice days. \n4) On 21 June and 22 December, the sunrays become perpendicular on the Tropic of Cancer and the Tropic of Capricorn respectively.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91915,1744,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in detail about the effects of the cycle of seasons on living world.','','','The effects of the cycle of seasons of living world can be explained with the help of the following points :\n(1) Biodiversity : The cycle of seasons leads to biodiversity on the earth. For example, in the region between 66^∘ 30^\' and 90^∘ in both the hemispheres, even the mild sunlight available for a part of the year gives rise to certain flora and fauna.\n(2) Human life: People in various region adapts to the natural conditions according to the cycle of seasons.\n(3) Migration : cycle of seasons lead to migration of some birds and animals. During extreme cold climate conditions when the food supply becomes scare, a number of birds and animals migrate temporarily.\n(4) Occupations : Trees bear fruit in a particular season. Therefore, the agricultural seasons also depend on to the local climatic conditions/seasons.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91917,1744,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Seasons occur on the Earth','','','i) The Earth rotates on its axis and revolves around the Sun.\nii) The Earth\'s axis is tilted at an angle of 23.5°.\niii) Because of this tilt, the Sun’s rays fall at different angles during the year.\niv) This causes variations in temperature, leading to different seasons.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91918,1744,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Day and night are equal on 21st March and 23rd September','','','i) On these dates, the Sun\'s rays fall vertically on the equator.\nii) The Earth\'s axis is neither tilted towards nor away from the Sun.\niii) Both hemispheres receive equal sunlight.\niv) Hence, the duration of day and night becomes equal (12 hours each).\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91919,1744,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Northern Hemisphere experiences summer on 21st June','','','i) The Northern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun during this time.\nii) The Sun\'s rays fall directly on the Tropic of Cancer.\niii) Days become longer and nights shorter.\niv) Higher temperature due to direct sunlight results in summer.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91920,1744,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The Southern Hemisphere has winter on 21st June','','','i) The Southern Hemisphere is tilted away from the Sun at this time.\nii) The Sun’s rays fall slanting, providing less heat.\niii) Days are shorter and nights are longer.\niv) Low temperature leads to winter season.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91921,1744,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Duration of day and night changes throughout the year','','','i) The tilt of the Earth’s axis remains constant.\nii) During revolution, the angle of sunlight changes.\niii) Some periods receive more sunlight causing longer days, others receive less causing longer nights.\niv) Therefore, the length of day and night varies across the year.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91922,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The apparent movement of the sun … north or south in a year.\nA) actually moves  B) appears to move  C) disappears  D) stops\n','','','B) appears to move','',0,0,0,NULL),(91923,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The period from 21st June to 22nd December is called …\nA) Uttarayan  B) Dakshinayan  C) Solstice  D) Equinox\n','','','B) Dakshinayan','',0,0,0,NULL),(91924,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The period from 22nd December to 21st June is called …\nA) Equinox  B) Summer  C) Uttarayan  D) Arctic Circle\n','','','C) Uttarayan','',0,0,0,NULL),(91925,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Seasons occur on the earth mainly due to the revolution of the earth and …\nA) rotation speed  B) tilt of its axis  C) ocean currents  D) monsoon winds\n','','','B) tilt of its axis','',0,0,0,NULL),(91926,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The earth is closest to the sun in the first week of January. This position is called …\nA) aphelion  B) equilibrium  C) perihelion  D) solstice\n','','','C) perihelion','',0,0,0,NULL),(91927,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On 21st March and 23rd September, day and night are equal everywhere. This situation is called …\nA) solstice  B) equinox  C) eclipse  D) monsoon\n','','','B) equinox','',0,0,0,NULL),(91928,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On 21st June, the sun’s rays fall perpendicular on …\nA) Equator  B) Tropic of Capricorn  C) Arctic Circle  D) Tropic of Cancer\n','','','D) Tropic of Cancer','',0,0,0,NULL),(91929,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The southern hemisphere has winter during …\nA) 21st June  B) 22nd December  C) 21st March  D) 23rd September\n','','','A) 21st June','',0,0,0,NULL),(91930,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','On the equator, the duration of day and night remains … throughout the year.\nA) unequal  B) longest  C) same  D) shortest\n','','','C) same','',0,0,0,NULL),(91931,1744,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Penguins and seals are commonly found in the … region.\nA) Arctic  B) Equatorial  C) Antarctic  D) Temperate\n','','','C) Antarctic','',0,0,0,NULL),(91933,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','Agriculture requires the following : ………\nA) Only ploughing            B) Use of animals, implements, machines and manpower   \nC) Use of only manpower  D) Just cultivate the crop\n','','','B) Use of animals, implements, machines and manpower','',0,0,0,NULL),(91935,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','In which of the following type of farming are the crops rotated?\n(A) Intensive   (B) Plantation   (C) Commercial   (D) All of these\n','','','A) Intensive','',0,0,0,NULL),(91936,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Extensive grain farming is carried out with the help of tractors and crushers due to ____\nA) large farm size and sparse population        B) favourable geographical conditions   \nC) unavailability of heavy capital investment   D) plantations on hilly tracts\n','','','A) large farm size and sparse population','',0,0,0,NULL),(91938,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Market gardening is also known as \'truck farming\' because ____\nA) it is a type of cultivation developed in modern cities   \nB) it works on the principle of economics   \nC) it is dependent on good transport network   \nD) it uses organic and chemical fertilizers\n','','','C) it is dependent on good transport network','',0,0,0,NULL),(91939,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','In India, agriculture has developed because ____ .\nA) there are two seasons of agriculture in India  \nB) majority of the people depend on agriculture   \nC) traditional agriculture is practised in India   \nD) climate, soils, water, etc. are conducive\n','','','D) climate, soils, water, etc. are conducive','',0,0,0,NULL),(91940,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','It is necessary that modern methods and technology be used in agriculture in India because ____\nA) there are factories producing improved seeds   \nB) industries producing fertilizers   \nC) population is growing & agro-based industries exist   \nD) modern means and machines are available\n','','','C) population is growing & agro-based industries exist   (','',0,0,0,NULL),(91941,1745,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the types of farming given in group \' A \' with the regions where they are practiced mentioned in group \' B \'.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Shifting cultivation</td>\n    <td>a. Carried out in temperate grassland regions</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Plantation farming</td>\n    <td>b. Countries having Mediterranean climate</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Extensive grain farming</td>\n    <td>c. Practised in the tropics in densely forested areas</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Horticulture</td>\n    <td>d. Practised in hilly tracts</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 - c), (2 - d), (3 - a), (4 - b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(91942,1745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In India, commercial poultry farming is carried out in the areas close to big cities.','','','Right.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91943,1745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Extensive grain farming and shifting plantation are types of subsistence farming.','','','Wrong.\nIntensive farming and shifting cultivation are types of subsistence farming.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91944,1745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Multiple crop cultivation is a characteristic of commercial farming.','','','Wrong.\nMonocrop cultivation is a characteristic of commercial farming.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91945,1745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Chemical fertilizers and pesticides are not used in organic farming.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(91946,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which occupations are included in agriculture besides cultivation of crops?','','','Besides cultivation of crops occupations like rearing cattle, sheep and goats, maintaining poultry farms, keeping bees, sericulture, horticulture, orchardry, pisciculture, pig farming, emu farming, etc. are included in agriculture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91947,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the core of animal husbandry?','','','Rearing different animals and obtaining various products from them for subsistence is the core of animal husbandry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91948,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is considered to be an inseparable part of mixed farming?','','','Rearing milch animals and animals which can be employed in farming is considered to be an inseparable part of mixed farming.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91949,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where is sheep and goat rearing generally carried out?','','','Sheep and goat rearing is generally carried out in hilly tracts and semiarid regions with dry climate.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91950,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which are the two independent occupations derived out of sericulture?','','','Getting silk from cocoons and manufacturing of silk cloth are two independent occupations derived out of sericulture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91951,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is the main aim of Greenhouse farming?','','','The main aim of Greenhouse farming is to control pest attack by controlling water, light and temperature.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91952,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Where is the shifting type of cultivation generally practised?','','','The shifting type of cultivation is generally practised in the tropics in densely forested areas or hilly tracts.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91953,1744,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Column A</th>\n    <th>Column B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Summer Solstice</td>\n    <td>A. Equal day and night</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Winter Solstice</td>\n    <td>B. Causes change in seasons</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Equinox</td>\n    <td>C. Occurs on 21st June</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Revolution of Earth</td>\n    <td>D. Occurs on 22nd December</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1 – C ; 2 – D ; 3 – A ; 4 – B','',0,0,0,NULL),(91954,1744,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Column A</th>\n    <th>Column B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Tropic of Cancer</td>\n    <td>A. Receives direct rays on equinox</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Tropic of Capricorn</td>\n    <td>B. Axis tilted at 23.5°</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. The Equator</td>\n    <td>C. Receives direct rays on 21st June</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Tilt of Earth</td>\n    <td>D. Receives direct rays on 22nd December</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1 – C ; 2 – D ; 3 – A ; 4 – B','',0,0,0,NULL),(91955,1745,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','In intensive farming, the economic condition of farmer is poor.','','','i. Intensive farming is carried out in limited area of land.\nii. Hence, the farm production is low; which can only suffice the farmer\'s family requirement.\niii. Also, the cultivator and his family are totally dependent on farming.\nDue to all this, the economic condition of farmer is poor.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91956,1745,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','People practicing shifting cultivation also undertake other activities in forests.','','','i. In shifting cultivation type of farming, the production obtained is not sufficient to fulfill the food requirement.\nii. The fallow period in this type of farming is longer than the crop period.\niii. Also, the productivity of the land depletes in two or three years and a different piece of land needs to be selected for farming.\nHence, people practicing shifting cultivation also undertake other activities like hunting, fishing and gathering of bulbs and roots from forests.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91957,1745,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The fruits and vegetables that appear fresh and attractive may not always be good.','','','i. Various artificial chemicals and pesticides are used on plants to get earlier produce.\nii. Apart from getting quicker production, the produce also appears fresh and attractive.\niii. However, the fruits and vegetables produced by such methods are harmful to health and do not last long enough.\nThus, the fruits and vegetables which appear fresh and attractive may not always be good.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91958,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How do bees help in increasing the yield of crops?','','','i. Bees hover around flower bearing plants in order to collect honey (nectar). .\nii. This promotes the process of pollination.\niii. Due to this, the trees bear large number of fruits and thus the yield of crops increases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91959,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How is fish farming or pisciculture carried out?','','','i. Fish farming or pisciculture is carried out in farm ponds which are dug specially for this purpose.\nii. Water is stored in such ponds and fish seeds of freshwater species are released in these ponds.\niii. Scientific methods are employed to achieve the best growth of fish.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91960,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the different risk factors of fishing in open seas?','','','The risk factors associated with fishing in open seas are as follows:\ni. Different aquatic organisms may get caught in the fishing nets. Separating them from the fishes becomes a difficult task.\nii. All the organisms which are caught in the net do not fetch the same price on selling.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91961,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','How does the farmer prepares a cultivable land in shifting type of cultivation?','','','i. In shifting type of cultivation, the farmer initially selects a piece of land in the forest.\nii. In order to make it cultivable, he clears the land by cutting down trees, plants, removing the shrubs and grass.\niii. After the cut trees dry out, the farmer burns them and the leftover ash mixes with the soil and acts as manure.\nThus, a cultivable land is prepared by the farmer.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91962,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why does extensive commercial farming need more capital?','','','a. Extensive commercial farming is carried out in farms that are greater than 200 hectares in size.\nb. Due to such large farm size and sparse population, farming is carried out with the help of machines like tractors and crushers.\nc. Also pesticides are sprayed with the help of helicopters or planes.\nDue to the above reasons, extensive commercial farming requires more capital.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91963,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why does plantation farming need skilled and experienced labour?','','','a. Plantation farming is carried out in hilly tracts where the farm size is 40 hectares or above.\nb. It is difficult to use machines in such areas.\nHence, skilled and experienced local manpower is required for plantation farming.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91964,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Text Book','','Mention the major types of farming. ','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(91965,1745,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Text Book','','Mention the major types of farming and give information about intensive farming and extensive grain farming.','','','Based on the purpose and aim of methods of farming, the crops being cultivated, the techniques used, land use, etc., the major types of farming can be classified as follows -','Intensive farming:\ni. It is a type of subsistence farming and is carried out in the same farm for many years.\nii. The main characteristic of this type of farming is to get maximum production from minimum area.\niii. This type of farming is mostly seen in developing regions.\niv. The per head holding of land is less due to its limited availability and large population.\nv. The cultivator and his family are totally dependent on this type of farming.\nvi. Also due to low farm production, the produce is sufficient to fulfil only the family\'s requirement and hence the economic condition of the cultivator is poor.\nExtensive Grain Farming:\ni. It is a type of commercial farming and is carried out in farms greater than 200 hectares in size in temperate grassland regions.\nii. This type of farming requires the use of machines like tractors and crushers due to the large farm size and sparse population.\niii. Heavy capital investment is required for purchase of machinery like tractors and crushers.\niv. Also helicopters or planes are used to spray pesticides over farms where extensive grain farming is undertaken.\nv. Monocrop cultivation is the main characteristic of this type of farming.\nvi. The different types of crops cultivated include wheat, corn, barley, oats, soyabean, etc.\nvii. Droughts, attack by pests, locusts, etc. and market fluctuations are some of the problems faced by this type of farming.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(91966,1745,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Text Book','','Describe the characteristics of Plantation Farming.','','','Plantation farming is a type of commercial farming.\nThe characteristics of plantation farming are stated as follows -\ni. Plantation is carried out in hilly tracts of tropical regions, in farms of above 40 hectares in size.\nii. As use of machines is not possible in these areas, local manpower is utilized.\niii. Plantation farming is a single crop cultivation technique.\niv. Commercial crops like tea, rubber, coffee, coconut cocoa, spices, etc. which can be cultivated in these geographical conditions are planted. Foodgrains are not cultivated in this type of farming.\nv. The long duration of crops, use of scientific methods, exportable production, processing etc. in this type of farming requires large scale capital investment.\nvi. Some of the issues faced by this type of farming include climate, manpower, deterioration of environment, economic and managerial problems, etc.\nvii. This type of farming is practised in India and other South Asian countries as well as in Africa, South and Central America, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91967,1745,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in short about Marketing Systems.','','','i. Marketing systems help to make the goods produced by the farmers available to the consumer at a fair price and in time.\nii. They are important because agriculture in India is scattered over vast areas and all the farmers and not organized.\niii. Agricultural Produce Market Committees help the farmers from economically weak background to market their production. The farmers bring their produce at these places and sell to the traders.\niv. Institutes like farmer\'s organizations, consumer committees, etc. help in making proper arrangement for the sale of perishable goods and also try to protect farmers from the exploitation by agents, mediators and others.\nv. Also, due to globalization some of the farm produce is directly made available to industries as raw material.\nvi. Many farmers use modern technology in their farms and also sell their products with proper packaging.\nvii. Advertising over internet also helps in selling the products in local as well international markets.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91968,1745,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in short about horticulture and floriculture.','','','i. Horticulture and floriculture are subtypes of market gardening and consists of cultivation of fruits and flowers.\nii. Here, the farm sizes are small and both modern as well as traditional methods of farming are used.\niii. In recent times, irrigation, chemical fertilizers, green houses are also used for getting more profit.\niv. Flowers like lily, gerbera, tulip, dahlia, chrysanthemum, marigold, etc. are major products of horticulture.\nv. Countries like France, Italy and those having Mediterranean climate are famous for horticulture.\nvi. The fruits which are cultivated in fruit farming include mangoes, custard apples, grapes, bananas, pomegranates, dragon fruits, cherries, oranges, raspberries, strawberries, mulberries, etc.\nvii. Fruit farming is seen at places like Mahabaleshwar, Panchgani, Pune, Nagpur, Jalgaon, Nashik, etc.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91969,1745,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Greenhouse Farming','','','i. Greenhouse farming is a highly specialized farming of the modern era. It helps to get maximum product from land.\nii. This type of farming can have total control over factors like climate, heat, atmospheric moisture and soil moisture.\niii. Its main aim is to control water, light and temperature to prevent attacks from pest and also assists in getting maximum economic benefits.\niv. A greenhouse is erected using galvanized iron pipes and plastic sheets.\nv. They are used on larger scales for growing flowers like lily and gerbera.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91970,1745,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Organic Farming','','','i. The farming done using all the vestal matter mixed in the soil is known as organic farming.\nii. Chemical fertilizers and pesticides are not used in this type of farming.\niii. When the nutrients of the soil get used up by the crop, it needs to be replenished. Also, additional nutrients are required to increase the yield of the crop.\niv. Organic fertilizers are prepared for replenishing the nutrient level of the soil, by the following methods:\na. Decomposing the litter in the ground\nb. Burying grasses like sesbania or jute in the soil for making manures\nc. Using cowdung or compost manures\nd. Obtaining vermicompost from organic waste\nv. Also, organic pesticides like neem are used for controlling the pests.\nvi. The crop obtained from organic farming is high in quality.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91971,1745,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Agrotourism','','','i. Agrotourism is a new field in tourism, which has got developed in agrarian countries.\nii. It is based on the curiosity of the city dwellers about farmer\'s life and rural environment.\niii. Since various types of agricultural products are cultivated in the tropics, agrotourism has a greater scope in these regions.\niv. Agrotourism is financially beneficial for the farmer and his village.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91972,1745,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Market Gardening','','','i. Market gardening is a type of cultivation developed as a result of urbanization and the ready markets available in urban centres.\nii. This type of cultivation works on the economic principle of supply and demand.\niii. The land holding in this type of cultivation is small.\niv. The main characteristics of this type of farming include the use of irrigation, organic and chemical fertilizers, low investment, use of manpower, demand of markets, use of science and technology, etc.\nv. Since, the quality and price of the product is dependent on rapid transport, this type of farming is also known as \'truck farming\'.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91973,1745,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Text Book','','Which are the crops cultivated in your area? What are the geographical reasons for that?','','','i. The crops grown in the area near my village i.e. Konkan, are rice, coconuts, mangoes, cashew, etc.\nii. Rice plantation requires ample amount of water supply. Since, this region receives heavy rainfall, rice farming is carried out there.\niii. Also, the humid climate over here is suitable for mango, coconut and cashew crops.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91974,1745,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Text Book','','Why is the agriculture in India seasonal in nature? What are the difficulties for perennial agriculture?','','','i. Agriculture in India depends majorly on rainfall.\nii. Also, India receives rain mainly from the south-western monsoon winds which is seasonal in nature.\niii. Modern techniques of irrigation, which are essential for perennial agriculture, are not used everywhere in India.\niv. Also, the water storage reservoirs required for providing continuous supply to farms is limited in India.\nDue to the above reasons, the agriculture in India is seasonal in nature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91975,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Text Book','','Describe the importance of irrigation for agriculture.','','','i. Agriculture is dependent on various natural resources. Water is the most important among them.\nii. It is impossible to cultivate crops without adequate supply of water.\niii. As it rains only for a specific period in a year, it is necessary to provide proper irrigation to the crops in the remaining months.\niv. Agriculture can be carried out properly throughout the year only with the help of proper irrigation.\nHence, irrigation is important for agriculture.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91976,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Text Book','','Give a comparative account of any two methods of irrigation.','','','i. Watering through canals and drip irrigation are both methods of irrigation.\nii. Traditionally, crops were watered through canals.\niii. However, in this process lot of water through canals is lost due to evaporation and seepage into the ground.\niv. Drip irrigation is a modern method which makes use of pipes with holes.\nv. It ensures that only required amount of water drips near the plants.\nvi. Thus, optimum usage of the available water is ensured in drip irrigation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91977,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What changes have taken place in traditional agricultural practices?','','','Traditionally, agriculture has been carried out with the help of animals like bullocks, oxen, etc. for ploughing the fields. However, with the advance in science and technology, machines like tractors and crushers etc. started being used for these activities.\nAlso, traditional agriculture used to be dependent on natural factors like rain. With the development of various irrigation techniques and water storage systems, water can now be made available to crops throughout the year.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91978,1745,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which improper practices arising out of man\'s greed are observed in agriculture?','','','Agriculture and related occupations are needed to fulfill the man\'s requirement of food. Humans have made advances in technology and developed new techniques of getting maximum production. However, excessive use of technology and artificial methods can hamper the quality of the production. For e.g. Excessive use of chemicals for faster ripening of fruits and vegetables can reduce nutritional levels.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91979,1745,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','In what different ways is ground water obtained for agriculture?','','','i. Digging of wells is one of the methods of utilizing the ground water. Pumps can be used to draw the water from these wells which can then be used for irrigation and other purposes.\nii. Bore wells are also dug at several places to get access to the ground water.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91980,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Commercial dairy farming is mainly undertaken for ____ and ____\n(a) milk & meat  (b) wool & milk  (c) eggs & meat\n','','','a) milk & meat','',0,0,0,NULL),(91981,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Sheep and goats mainly survive on ____ and ____ which grow in remote hilly rural areas.\n(a) short grasses & shrubs  (b) leaves & fruits  (c) grains & hay\n','','','a) short grasses & shrubs','',0,0,0,NULL),(91982,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The occupation of ____ is undertaken to obtain honey and wax.\n(a) sericulture  (b) beekeeping  (c) poultry\n','','','b) beekeeping','',0,0,0,NULL),(91983,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Silk thread is obtained from the ____ of the silk moth.\n(a) larva  (b) cocoon  (c) egg\n','','','b) cocoon','',0,0,0,NULL),(91984,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Leaves of ____ trees are the main food for the silk worms.\n(a) mulberry  (b) mango  (c) neem\n','','','a) mulberry','',0,0,0,NULL),(91985,1745,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ cultivation is a primitive type of cultivation.\n(a) shifting  (b) mixed  (c) intensive\n','','','a) shifting','',0,0,0,NULL),(91986,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write a note on poultry farming.','','','a. Keeping hen and other fowl as household or on commercial basis is known as poultry farming.\nb. Lot of care has to be taken for running a poultry on commercial basis.\nc. Scientific methods are employed for this purpose.\nd. In India, commercial poultry are generally found near big cities, due to the ready markets available for this occupation.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(91987,1745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Farm size is huge in intensive farming.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(91988,1745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Extensive grain farming is carried out with the help of manpower.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(91989,1745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In India, commercial farming is practised on a large scale.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(91990,1745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Skilled labours are required for plantation farming.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(91991,1745,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Vermicompost is organic manures.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(91992,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which commercial crops are planted in plantation farming?','','','The commercial crops like tea, rubber, coffee, coconut, cocoa, spices, various fruits and flowers etc. are planted in plantation farming.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91993,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which crops are planted in commercial farming?','','','The main crops like wheat and corn as well as other crops like barley, oats, soyabeans etc. are planted in commercial farming.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91994,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by pisciculture?','','','Obtaining fish artificially from fish fields and rearing them is called pisculture.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91995,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which animals are reared in animal husbandry?','','','Animals like cows, axen, buffaloes, sheep, goat, hens, rabbit, pig, emu etc. are reared in animal husbandry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91996,1745,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which products are obtained from animal husbandry?','','','The products like milk, eggs, meat, wool, etc. are obtained from animal husbandry.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91997,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the fruits that are grown in fruit farming.','','','The names of fruits that are grown in fruit farming : (i) Mangoes (ii) Custard apples (iii) Grapes (iv) Bananas (v) Pomegranates (vi) Dragon fruits (vii) Cherries (viii) Oranges (ix) Raspberries (x) Strawberries (xi) Mulberries.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91998,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the places in Maharashtra that are famous for fruit farming.','','','The names of places in Maharashtra that are famous for fruit farming : (i) Mahabaleshwar (ii) Panchgani (iii) Pune (iv) Nagpur (v) Jalgaon (vi) Nashik, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(91999,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Name the flowers that are grown in floriculture.','','','The names of flowers that are grown in floriculture : (i) Lily (ii) Gerbera (iii) Tulip (iv) Dahlia (v) Chrysanthemum (vi) Marigold, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92000,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are the risks involved in fishing in open sea?','','','The risks involved in fishing in open sea are as follows : \n1) Different types of fish and other aquatic organisms get caught in the fishing nets. Separating them becomes a major task. \n2) Sometimes, it becomes essential to go to deep sea for fishing. \n3) Storms, heavy rains etc. increases the risks and leads to instability in fishing activity.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92001,1745,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why is the scope of agriculture very wide?','','','1) Cultivation of the crops is considered to be the main and the most important occupation in agriculture. \n2) Besides the cultivation of different crops, rearing cattle, sheep and goats, maintaining poultry farms, keeping bees, sericulture, horticulture, orchardry, pisciculture, pig farming, emu farming, etc. are practised by man to satisfy his variety of needs. \n3) All these activities are included in agriculture. Therefore, the scope of agriculture is very wide\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92002,1745,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why should one avoid using artificially ripened products?','','','1) At times, in order to get the produce earlier, artificial chemicals, pesticides are employed profusely. \n2) These facilitate quicker production and the produce appears fresh and attractive. \n3) However, such products are harmful to the health. \n4) Such products do not last longer after purchase. Therefore, one should avoid using artificially ripened products.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92003,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information on : Bee keeping','','','1) Bee keeping is an important occupation with respect to agriculture. \n2) This occupation is undertaken to obtain honey and wax. \n3) Bees, in order to collect honey, hover plants that bear flowers. \n4) This promotes the process of pollination. As a result, the trees bear large number of fruits and the crop yield increase.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92004,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information on : Pisciculture (Fish farming) ','','','1) Farm ponds are dug out for pisciculture. Water is stored in such ponds. 2) Fish seeds are released in these ponds. \n3) For psiciculture, seeds of freshwater species are used. \n4) In order to achieve the best growth of fish, scientific methods are employed. \n5) Wam, Rohu, Rawas (Indian Salmon), Kolambi (Prawns), etc. are reared in fish farms. \n6) Fishing in open seas has a number of risk factors. As its effect, pisiculture has developed in India.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92005,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information on : Sericulture ','','','1) Rearing of silk moth is called sericulture. Sericulture gets included in allied activities in agriculture. \n2) Different organizations supply silk moth eggs to the farmers. \n3) Leaves of mulberry trees are the main food for the silk worms. Once planted, the Mulberry trees survive for a minimum period of 15 years. \n4) Silk threads are obtained from the cocoon of the silk moth. These threads are very fine and strong. These threads are used to weave soft silk cloth. \n5) Getting silk thread from cocoons and manufacturing silk cloth are independent occupations.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92006,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information on : Nursery ','','','1) In the last few years, the area under floriculture, cultivation of medical and aromatic plants and horticulture has increased. \n2) These plantations require a high standard of seedlings, cuttings, bulbs and seeds. \n3) This had led to the development of nurseries. \n4) Nurseries give good returns.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92007,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information on : Greenhouse farming ','','','1) Greenhouse farming is highly specialized type of farming of the modern era. \n2) Greenhouse farming facilitates getting maximum product from the land. \n3) It can have a total control on natural factors like climate, heat, atmospheric moisture as well as soil moisture. It assists in getting maximum economic benefits. \n4) For erecting a green house galvanized iron pipes and plastic sheets are used. \n5) Its main aim is to control the pest attack by controlling water, light and temperature. \n6) Green houses are used on a larger scale for growing flowers like lily and gerbera.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92008,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information on : Horticulture ','','','1) Horticulture is one of the subtype of market gardening. \n2) In this type of farming modern as well as traditional methods are used. 3) The size of the farm is small and every plant is cared for properly. \n4) Different native and exotic fruits like mangoes, custard apples, grapes, bananas, pomegranates, dragon fruits, cherries, oranges, raspberries, strawberries, mulberries, etc. are cultivated in fruit farming. \n5) Mahabaleshwar, Panchgani, Pune, Nagpur, Jalgaon, Nashik, etc. cities of Maharashtra are famous for fruit farming. \n6) Countries having Mediterranean climate, France, Italy, etc. practice horticulture on a large scale.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92009,1745,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information on : Floriculture ','','','1) Floriculture is one of the subtype of market gardening. \n2) In this type of farming modern as well as traditional methods are used. \n3) The size of the farm is small and every plant is cared for properly. A variety of flowers such as lily, gerbera, tulip, dahlia, chrysanthemum, marigold, etc. are grown in this type of farming. \n4) Countries having Mediterranean climate, France, Italy, etc. are famous for floriculture.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92010,1745,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write in detail about animal husbandry.','','','Rearing different animals and obtaining various products from them for subsistence is the core of animal husbandry. The following are the subtypes of animal husbandry in India :\n(1) Dairy farming : Rearing milch animals and animals which can be employed in farming is called dairy farming. Cows, oxen, buffaloes, etc. are reared for agricultural related work. Dairy farming is considered to be an inseparable part of mixed farming. In India, the nature of dairy farming has recently become commercial. Commercial dairy farming is mainly undertaken for meat and milk.\n(2) Sheep and goat raring : Sheep and goat rearing is a traditional occupation. This occupation is generally carried out in hilly tracts and semi-arid regions with dry climate. Sheep and goat survive on short grasses, shrubs and acacia which grow in remote hilly rural areas away from the urban settlements. In India, this occupation is mainly undertaken for meat. It is also carried out for obtaining wool.\n(3) Poultry : Keeping hens and other fowl is a traditional occupation. It is commonly practiced in all parts of the world. This occupation is carried out as a household occupation and also on a commercial basis.\n(4) Rearing of other animals : In India, rabbit, pig and emu rearing is also undertaken in some areas.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92011,1745,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the characteristics of shifting cultivation.','','','The characteristics of shifting cultivation are as follows :\n(1) Nature : Shifting cultivation is one subtype of traditional farming. It is a primitive type of cultivation.\n(2) Methods : In shifting cultivation, every year a new area is choosen for cultivation. After a specific period of time, old areas are again used for cultivation. In this type of farming, the farmer initially selects a piece of land in the forest. In order to make it cultivable, he clears the land by cutting down the trees, plants, removing the shrubs and grass. Once the trees dry out, he burns them. The left over ash gets mixed in soil and acts as manure. Sowing and harvesting is done before the rainy season.\n(3) Crop period and fallow period : In this type of farming, the fallow period is longer than the crop period. After the productivity of land depletes in two to three years, a different piece of land is selected for cultivation.\n(4) Amount of production : The production obtained from this type of production is not sufficient to fulfil the food requirement. Hence people involved in this type of farming also undertakes hunting, fishing and gathering bulbs and roots from forests.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92012,1745,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the importance of marketing systems.','','','Marketing systems are necessary for marketing the goods produced by the farmers available to the consumers at a fair price and in time. The importance of marketing systems in country like India can be explained with the help of the following points :\n(1) Scattered nature of agriculture : Agriculture in India is scattered over vast areas. Therefore, for collecting agricultural produce from different places and its sale the market systems are essentials.\n(2) Lack of organization of farmers : Farmers in India are not organized. Therefore, marketing systems are essential for assuring fare prices to the farmers for their produce.\n(3) Weak economic condition of farmers : Most of the farmers in India are economically weak and cannot market their production on their own. In such case, the system of Agricultural Produce Market Committees is established at the taluka level. At these places, farmers bring their produce and sell to the traders.\n(4) Perishable produce : Farm produce is perishable. Therefore, there has to be proper arrangement for its sale. In such case, the marketing systems proves helpful.\n(5) Produce used as raw material in industries : Some of the farm produce is directly used by industries (sugar industry, paper making industry, textile industry, medical manufacturing industry) as raw material. In such case the marketing systems proves helpful.\n(6) Availability of international market due to globalization : International markets are now easily available for farm produce due to globalization. Nowadays, many progressive farmers use modern technology in their farms and sell their produce with proper packaging. Marketing systems are very helpful to such farmers. For example, Alphanso Mangoes from Konkan are sold on a large scale in international markets.\n(7) Great demand from hotel industry and malls : Hotel industry and malls require agricultural produce on a large scale. By advertising on the internet, farmers sell their produce in local as well as international markets. For this purpose, the marketing systems are essential and beneficial.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92013,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In early settlements, the occupations of people were dependent on available ____\n(a) markets   (b) tools   (c) natural resources   (d) facilities\n','','','c) natural resources','',0,0,0,NULL),(92014,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The urban areas grow into metropolitan cities if, ____\na) occupations associated to agriculture develop   \nb) the population and the necessary amenities increase on a large scale   \nc) importance of secondary and tertiary occupations decreases   \nd) the proportion of people engaged in primary occupations increases\n','','','b) the population and the necessary amenities increase on a large scale  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(92015,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ settlement is the first step towards a stable life in human history.\n(a) Urban   (b) Rural   (c) Tribal   (d) Metropolitan\n','','','b) Rural','',0,0,0,NULL),(92016,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The day-to-day food requirement of the urban population is fulfilled by ____ .\n(a) rural settlements   (b) modernization   (c) urban settlements   (d) science and technology\n','','','a) rural settlements','',0,0,0,NULL),(92017,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Linear settlements are seen along ____\n(a) roads   (b) railways   (c) rivers   (d) all of the above\n','','','d) all of the above','',0,0,0,NULL),(92018,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In urban settlements, the proportion of people engaged in primary occupation is more.','','','Wrong\nIn urban settlements, the proportion of people engaged in primary occupation decline and importance of secondary and tertiary occupations increases.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92019,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Modernization, science and technology lead to transformation in both, rural as well as urban settlements.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(92020,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There are adequate facilities and services available in scattered settlements.','','','Wrong\nThere are inadequate facilities and services available in scattered settlements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92021,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nucleated settlements are generally close to water sources like brooks, rivulets, rivers, lakes, reservoirs, etc.','','','Right','',0,0,0,NULL),(92022,1746,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the following : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Group \'A\'</th>\n    <th>Group \'B\'</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Forests</td>\n    <td>a. Farmer settlements</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Fertile areas</td>\n    <td>b. Fishing hamlets</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Sea coast</td>\n    <td>c. Tribal hamlets</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(1 - c), (2 - a), (3 - b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(92023,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify the types of human settlements from the following statements : Their money and time is saved by living on the farm.','','','Scattered settlements','',0,0,0,NULL),(92024,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify the types of human settlements from the following statements : There is a lot of social life in this settlement.','','','Nucleated settlements','',0,0,0,NULL),(92025,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify the types of human settlements from the following statements : Shops are located on both the sides of the road.','','','Linear settlements','',0,0,0,NULL),(92026,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify the types of human settlements from the following statements : This settlement is found at the foothills of mountains or along the coast.','','','Linear settlements','',0,0,0,NULL),(92027,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify the types of human settlements from the following statements : Each house is located away from the other.','','','Scattered settlements','',0,0,0,NULL),(92028,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify the types of human settlements from the following statements : This settlement is good from a security point of view.','','','Nucleated settlements','',0,0,0,NULL),(92029,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify the types of human settlements from the following statements : Having houses away from each other is good for health. ','','','Scattered settlements','',0,0,0,NULL),(92030,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Identify the types of human settlements from the following statements : The houses are too close to each other.','','','Nucleated settlements\n\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92031,1746,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Text Book','','Differentiate between : A hamlet and a village','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>A Hamlet</th>\n    <th>A Village</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. The settlement which is dependent on locally available natural resources and consists of people engaged in a particular occupation is known as a hamlet.</td>\n    <td>The settlement where the original occupations of the majority of the people are based on natural resources is called as rural settlements or villages.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Each hamlet consists of people engaged in only one occupation.</td>\n    <td>The people of a village can be engaged in different activities based on natural resources like agriculture, fishery, etc.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(92032,1746,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Text Book','','Differentiate between : Nucleated Settlements and Scattered Settlements','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Nucleated Settlements</th>\n    <th>Scattered Settlements</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. The houses in nucleated settlements are close to each other.</td>\n    <td>The houses in scattered settlements are far away from each other.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. These settlements are found closer to water sources like brooks, rivulets, rivers, lakes, reservoirs, etc.</td>\n    <td>Such settlements are generally found in areas of high relief, dense forests, grasslands, hot deserts and extensive agricultural lands.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Social services are available in these settlements.</td>\n    <td>Facilities and services in these settlements are not adequate.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. The population in these settlements is more as people of different castes, religions, races and ideologies live together.</td>\n    <td>The population in these settlements is limited like that in small hamlets.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(92033,1746,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Differentiate between : Scattered Settlements and Linear Settlements','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Scattered Settlements</th>\n    <th>Linear Settlements</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i. The houses in scattered settlements are far away from each other.</td>\n    <td>The houses in linear settlements are spread along straight line.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii. Such settlements are generally found in areas of high relief, dense forests, grasslands, hot deserts and extensive agricultural lands.</td>\n    <td>Such settlements are seen along roads, railways, rivers, seas coasts and in foothill regions, etc.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii. Facilities and services in these settlements are not adequate.</td>\n    <td>Along with the houses, some shops can also be seen in these settlements.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv. The population in these settlements is limited like that in small hamlets.</td>\n    <td>The population in these settlements is more as the settlements grow along the coastal tracts, major rivers, National or State highways.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(92034,1746,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','Explain the natural factors affecting the location of human settlements.','','','Human settlements generally develop at places with favourable geographical conditions. The important natural factors affecting the location of human settlements are as follows:\ni. Water: Humans cannot survive without water. Hence, early civilizations developed near various sources of water like rivers, seas, etc. Water is also essential for occupations like agriculture.\nii. Climate: Conducive climate is essential for life to sustain. Livings beings including humans cannot survive in regions with extreme climatic conditions like extreme cold or heat. The climate also determines the occupations of the people of a region.\niii. Land: The land should be favorable for settlements to become stable. Generally, human settlements are found more on plains, as constructions of houses on mountainous and hilly terrain is difficult.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92035,1746,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Mention the physical, cultural and economic factors influencing the location of human settlements.','','','The factors influencing the location of human settlements are as follows:\ni. Physical factors: Physiography, land or soils, climate, water supply, river banks.\nii. Cultural factors: Defense, health, education, tourism, historical significance.\niii. Economic factors: Irrigation, occupations, transport and communication, industries, trade, government offices.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92036,1746,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain how human settlements have evolved.','','','i. In ancient times, the occupations of people were dependent on the available natural resources.\nii. As a result, human settlements developed according to the occupations of the people.\ne.g. Fishing hamlets near sea coast, tribal hamlets in forest areas, etc.\niii. As agriculture developed in fertile areas, farmers too build their houses in or near the fields. Later, these settlements gradually grew bigger.\niv. The settlements where the original occupations of majority of the people were based on natural resources came to be known as rural settlements.\nv. Thus, human settlements flourished at places with favourable geographical conditions.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92037,1746,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the process of transformation of rural settlements into urban settlements.','','','i. The settlements where the original occupations of the majority of the people are based on natural resources are called as rural settlements.\nii. People in rural settlements practice occupations like agriculture, fishery, etc.\niii. Over a period of time, other occupations started to evolve in these settlements.\niv. Due to this, people from surrounding areas migrated to such settlements, leading to an increase in the rural population.\nv. Houses were built and different facilities developed, to fulfill the requirements of this growing population.\nvi. Secondary and tertiary occupations increased and the proportion of people practising primary occupations declined.\nvii. This led to the transformation of rural settlements into urban settlements.\nviii. Various religious, historical, commercial, educational and administrative reasons as well as tourism, also lead to transformation of original settlements into urban settlements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92038,1746,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Text Book','','Explain the various types of human settlements.','','','Human settlements are of the following three types:\ni. Scattered settlements:\na. The settlements where the houses are few and are far away from each other are called as scattered settlements.\nb. Such settlements are generally found in areas of high relief, dense forests, grasslands, hot deserts and extensive agricultural lands.\nc. The population in these settlements is limited like that in small hamlets.\nd. The facilities and services in these settlements are not adequate. The houses depend on the centrally located villages for their day-to-day requirements.\ne. Due to their close proximity to nature, these settlements are free from pollution.\nii. Nucleated settlements:\na. The settlements where the houses are close to each other are known as nucleated settlements.\nb. These settlements are found close to water sources like brooks, rivulets, rivers, lakes, reservoirs, etc.\nc. Fertile plains, transport hub & mining centres, commercial centres as also defense, health, education and other social & religious factors lead to the development of such type of settlements.\nd. The settlements take different shapes depending upon how they develop or expand.\ne. The roads in old parts of these settlements are narrow. Social services too are available in these settlements.\nf. As people of different castes, religions, races and ideologies live together, the social life in these settlements is better.\niii. Linear settlements:\na. The settlements which are seen along roads, railways, rivers, seas coasts and in foothill regions, etc. and spread along straight line are known as linear settlements.\nb. They are narrow in shape and multiple lines of houses develop as the settlement grows.\nc. The roads in these settlements are parallel to each other.\nd. Shops are also seen in these types of settlements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92039,1746,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find suitable locations for human settlements and show them in the picture.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(92040,1746,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Text Book','','Study the diagram and identify the types of settlements.','1. Settlement \' A \' has 5-6 houses and the place does not have other facilities.\n2. \'B\' has a high school, a big market and small theatre.\n2. \'C\' has houses, farms, many shops and small industries.\n4. \'D\' is a natural harbour. Many industries have been established there.\n\' C \' is a settlement that has developed along the roadside. Give two reasons of its location.\n','','1) \'A\' is a scattered settlement\n2) \'A\' is a nucleated settlement\n3) \'C\' is a linear settlement\n4) \'D\' is a linear settlement\nThe main reasons for settlement \' C \' developing near roadside are as follows:\ni. The area near the road consists of plains, and hence is suitable for human settlements.\nii. Generally linear settlements are found near roads and highways.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(92041,1746,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the figure and answer the following questions:','1. Name some of the settlements shown in the map.\n2. Which of the settlements in the map are in a scattered form?\n3. How are the houses in the settlements along the roads arranged?\n4. In which settlements are the houses close together? What could be the reason behind this density?\n5. Can you now classify the settlements on basis of these patterns?\n','','1) Songarh, Amalipada, Dumda, Achalva, Sadadkua, Pukhran, Kumkua, Rani Amba, Chapawadi and Rampu are some of the settlements shown in the map.\n2) The settlements of Achalva, Pukhran, Sadadkua and Chapawadi shown in the map are in scattered form.\n3) The houses in the settlements along the road are arranged in a linear pattern.\n4) The houses in the settlement of Songarh are close to each other.\nThis could be because -\ni. this region has plain area\nii. this region falls near the river that makes the location suitable for agriculture\niii. the region has facilities like hospital, market and post office\niv. the road is near to the settlement that aids trade and transport\nv. the region has easy access to railway\n5) On the basis of these patterns, we can classify the settlements as follows -\ni. Scattered settlements like those in Dumda, Sadadkua, etc.\nii. Linear settlements like those in Rani Amba.\nii. Nucleated settlements like those in Songarh.\nThese settlements can also be classified as rural or urban settlements based on their development and the facilities available there.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(92042,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','Name the metropolitan cities of India.','','','Mumbai, Delhi, Chennai, Kolkata, Bengaluru, Pune, Hyderabad and Ahmedabad are some of the metropolitan cities of India.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92043,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Human settlements flourish at places with favourable ____ conditions.\n(a) geographical  (b) economic  (c) climatic\n','','','a) geographical','',0,0,0,NULL),(92044,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The tribal people live in forest areas in ____\n(a) cities  (b) tribal hamlets  (c) metros\n','','','b) tribal hamlets  ','',0,0,0,NULL),(92045,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ settlements have evolved through the expansion and growth of rural settlements.\n(a) Urban  (b) Tribal  (c) Nomadic\n','','','a) Urban','',0,0,0,NULL),(92046,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','There are large scale ____ correlations between rural and urban settlements.\n(a) economic  (b) cultural  (c) political\n','','','a) economic','',0,0,0,NULL),(92047,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which economic factors influence the location of human settlements?','','','Economic factors like irrigation, occupations, transport and communication, industries, trade and government offices influence the location of human settlements.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92048,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Increase of which factor in rural settlements marks the beginning of urbanization?','','','Increase in the population of rural settlements, marks the beginning of urbanization.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92049,1746,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Houses are built and different facilities develop in rural settlements.','','','a. The settlements where the original occupations of majority of the people are based on natural resources are known as rural settlements.\nb. Over a period of time, other associated occupations gradually develop in these settlements.\nc. As a result, people from surrounding areas migrate and settle in these areas.\nd. This leads to the building of houses and developments of different facilities in rural settlements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92050,1746,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','The scattered type of settlements is free from pollution.','','','a. The settlements in which the houses are few and are far away from each other are known as scattered settlements.\nb. Such settlements are generally found in areas of high relief, dense forests, grasslands, hot deserts and extensive agricultural lands.\nc. Thus, the scattered settlements are closer to nature and hence are free from pollution.\ncoastal tracts, major rivers, nationai nignways and state hignways in india.\n(2) Characteristics : (i) Settlements are along single line. As a settlement grows, multiple lines emerge. (ii) Roads in settlements are parallel to each other. (iii) Besides houses, some shops are also seen.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92051,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Life in ____ settlements is more dynamic.\n(a) scattered   (b) rural   (c) urban\n','','','c) urban','',0,0,0,NULL),(92052,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ settlements preserve the cultural aspects.\n(a) Rural   (b) Urban   (c) Metropolitan\n','','','a) Rural','',0,0,0,NULL),(92053,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ settlements flourish due to religious reasons.\n(a) Nucleated   (b) Scattered   (c) Linear\n','','','a) Nucleated','',0,0,0,NULL),(92054,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which settlements are called rural settlements?','','','The settlements where the original occupations of the majority of people are based on natural resources are called rural settlements.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92055,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which type of occupation are majority of people of rural settlements involved?','','','The majority of people of rural settlements are involved in primary occupations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92056,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In which type of occupation are majority of people of urban settlements involved?','','','The majority of people of urban settlements are involved in secondary and tertiary occupations.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92057,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which factors are responsible for transformation of rural settlements into urban settlements?','','','The religious, historical, commercial, educational, administrative, tourism, etc. factors are responsible for transformation of rural settlements into urban settlements.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92058,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which factors are responsible for transformation of urban settlements into metropolitan cities?','','','A large scale increase in population and the necessary amenities are the factors responsible for transformation of urban settlements into metropolitan cities.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92059,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','At which places are the scattered settlements found?','','','The scattered settlements are found in the areas of high relief, dense forests, grasslands, hot deserts and extensive agricultural lands.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92060,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','At which places are the linear settlements found?','','','The linear settlements are found along roads, railways, rivers, sea coasts and in foothill regions, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92061,1746,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which factors lead to transformation in rural as well as urban settlements?','','','Modernization, science and technology leads to transformation in rural as well as urban settlements.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92062,1746,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the process of transformation of rural settlement into urban settlement.','','','1) People engaged in farming, fishing, mining, etc. form rural settlements.\n(2) With the passage of time, other associated occupations also develop in these rural settlements.\n(3) As a result, people from surrounding areas migrate and settle there leading to an increase in the rural population.\n(4) Houses are built and different facilities are developed for the growing population.\n(5) Gradually, the importance of secondary and tertiary sector increases and the importance of primary occupations decreases. In this way, rural settlements get transformed into urban settlements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92063,1746,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the characteristics of scattered settlements.','','','The characteristics of scattered settlements are as follows : \n1) Distance between scattered settlements can be clearly seen. \n2) These settlements have limited populations as in small hamlets. (pada, wadi, etc.) \n3) Facilities and services in these settlements are not adequate. \n4) As these settlements are closer to nature, they are free from pollution. \n5) These settlements depend on the centrally located villages for their day-to-day requirements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92064,1746,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the characteristics of linear settlements.','','','The characteristics of linear settlements are as follows:\n1) Houses in linear settlements are along a single line. As the settlement grows in the course of time, multiple lines emerge. \n2) In this settlements, roads are parallel to each other. \n3) Besides houses, some shops can be seen in these settlements. \n4) These settlements grow along with the roads. For example, such settlements are found along the coastal tracts, major rivers, National or State highways in country.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92067,1746,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information : Factors influencing the location of human settlements','','','1) Physical, cultural and economic factors influence the location of human settlements. \n2) Physical factors like physiography, land/soils, climate, water supply, river banks, etc. influence the location of human settlement. \n3) The cultural factors like defense, health, education, tourism, historical significance, etc. also affect the location of human settlements. (4) The economic factors like irrigation, occupations, transport and communication, industries, trade, government offices, etc. have their impact on the location of human settlements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92068,1746,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information : Progress in construction of shelter ','','','1) Initially, using the resources from the surroundings, man constructed houses and started living in them. \n2) In modern era of science and technology, people have made a remarkable progress in the construction of shelter. They have even built sky-scarpers. \n3) Man is thinking of constructing colonies on other planets and their satellites, too, in future.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92069,1746,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information : Rural settlements and cultural stability ','','','1) Rural settlement is the first step towards a stable life in human history. \n2) The process of growth and expansion of settlements begin in rural settlements. Gradually, the rural settlements get transformed into urban settlements. \n3) The life in urban settlements is more dynamic. On the other hand the life in rural settlement is comparatively traditional. Thus, rural settlements preserves the cultural stability.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92070,1746,'descriptive','3b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Give information : Correlations between rural settlements and urban settlements ','','','1) With the large scale growth in rural settlements, they get transformed into urban settlements. \n2) The day-to-day requirements of urban settlements are fulfilled by rural settlements. \n3) For example, the need of food grain, vegetables, milk, etc. of urban people is fulfilled by nearby rural settlements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92071,1746,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain the causes responsible for the development of nucleated settlement. Explain the features of nucleated settlement.','','','A) The causes responsible for the development of nucleated settlement : \n1) Nucleated settlements are developed due to availability of water. Such settlements are generally close to brooks, rivulets, rivers, lakes, reservoirs, etc. For example, In the deserts of Rajasthan, nucleated settlements are located near assured sources of water. \n2) Fertile plains, transport hubs, mining centres, commercial centres, etc. factors also lead to the development of this type of settlement. \n3) Defense, health, education and other social or religious factors can also give rise to nucleated settlements.\n(B) Features of nucleated settlements : \n1) In nucleated settlements, houses are close to each other. \n2) Social services are available in these settlements. \n3) As these settlements develop in certain places over certain period of time, they attain particular shapes. \n4) Roads are narrow in the old parts of these settlements. (5) People from different casts, religions, races and ideologies live together in these settlements and hence they have a better social life.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92072,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Human settlements flourished at places with favourable ……\n(A) Industries  (B) Geographic conditions  (C) Markets  (D) High population\n','','','B) Geographic conditions','',0,0,0,NULL),(92073,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Settlements where majority people depend on natural resources are called ……\n(A) Urban settlements  (B) Rural settlements  (C) Industrial towns  (D) Ports\n','','','B) Rural settlements','',0,0,0,NULL),(92074,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Fishing hamlets along the coast are known as ……\n(A) Adivasi padas  (B) Koliwada  (C) Wadis  (D) Townships\n','','','B) Koliwada','',0,0,0,NULL),(92075,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The increase in rural population leads to ……\n(A) Desertification  (B) Urbanisation  (C) Migration  (D) Industrial shutdown\n','','','B) Urbanisation','',0,0,0,NULL),(92076,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Urban settlements evolve mainly from ……\n(A) Forests  (B) Villages  (C) Schools  (D) Seas\n','','','B) Villages','',0,0,0,NULL),(92077,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Scattered settlements are mostly found in ……\n(A) Fertile plains  (B) Coastal regions  (C) Dense forests and high relief  (D) Industrial zones\n','','','C) Dense forests and high relief','',0,0,0,NULL),(92078,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In nucleated settlements, houses are ……\n(A) Far apart  (B) Very close together  (C) Built underground  (D) Floating\n','','','B) Very close together','',0,0,0,NULL),(92079,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Linear settlements develop mainly along ……\n(A) Airports  (B) Roads, rivers, railways  (C) Plateaus  (D) Deserts\n','','','B) Roads, rivers, railways','',0,0,0,NULL),(92080,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','A main characteristic of scattered settlements is ……\n(A) High population density  (B) Wide distance between houses  (C) Tall buildings  (D) Wide roads\n','','','B) Wide distance between houses','',0,0,0,NULL),(92081,1746,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nucleated settlements mostly develop near ……\n(A) Mountain peaks  (B) Water sources  (C) Dry deserts  (D) Snow regions\n','','','B) Water sources','',0,0,0,NULL),(92082,1746,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rural settlements develop near natural resources.','','','i) Early human life depended directly on water, fertile soil and forests for survival.\nii) Agriculture, fishing and hunting require immediate access to natural resources.\niii) Availability of food and livelihood makes people settle permanently in such areas.\niv) These conditions support stable living, leading to the growth of rural settlements.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92083,1746,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nucleated settlements grow near water sources.','','','i) Water is essential for drinking, irrigation and daily activities.\nii) Fertile soil near rivers supports agriculture and food production.\niii) People gather around assured water supply, forming tightly packed houses.\niv) Water availability also helps in trade, transport and economic activities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92084,1746,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Urban settlements develop from rural settlements.','','','i) Increase in population in villages leads to the need for more facilities.\nii) Secondary and tertiary occupations start growing gradually.\niii) Migration from surrounding areas increases due to economic opportunities.\niv) Improved services and industries transform rural areas into towns and cities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92085,1746,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Linear settlements are seen along roads and rivers.','','','i) Roads and rivers provide easy transport and communication.\nii) People prefer building houses along long routes for accessibility.\niii) Shops and services grow along these routes, extending the settlement.\niv) Economic activities increase as movement of people and goods becomes easier.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92086,1746,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Scattered settlements are found in high relief and forest areas.','','','i) Uneven land and dense forests make it difficult to build clustered houses.\nii) People must stay close to their farmlands, grazing areas or forest produce.\niii) Population remains low due to harsh physical conditions.\niv) Houses are widely spaced due to isolation and limited transport facilities.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92087,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Scattered settlements are commonly found in regions with high relief and dense forests.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(92088,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nucleated settlements have houses built far apart from each other.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(92089,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Urban settlements develop mainly due to the growth of secondary and tertiary occupations.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(92090,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Linear settlements usually develop along rivers, roads or railways.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(92091,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rural settlements always have all modern facilities and services.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(92092,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Availability of fertile land and water encourages the development of agriculture-based settlements.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(92093,1746,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Scattered settlements have high population density and wide social facilities.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(92094,1746,'descriptive','3a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Column A</th>\n    <th>Column B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Scattered Settlements</td>\n    <td>a. Houses close together</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Nucleated Settlements</td>\n    <td>b. Houses far apart</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Linear Settlements</td>\n    <td>c. Along roads / rivers</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1 – b ; 2 – a ; 3 – c','',0,0,0,NULL),(92095,1746,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Column A</th>\n    <th>Column B</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Rural Settlement</td>\n    <td>a. Development of secondary/tertiary occupations</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Urban Settlement</td>\n    <td>b. Based on natural resources</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Metropolitan City</td>\n    <td>c. Very large population & amenities</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1 – b ; 2 – a ; 3 – c','',0,0,0,NULL),(92096,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','If the contour lines are closer to each other, the slope is ……….\n(a) gentle   (b) steep   (c) flat\n','','','b) steep','',0,0,0,NULL),(92097,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The contour lines on the map represent ………….\n(a) a specific height   (b) population   (c) rainfall\n','','','a) a specific height','',0,0,0,NULL),(92098,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','The slope can be understood from the distance between the …………..\n(a) rivers   (b) adjacent contour lines   (c) boundaries\n','','','b) adjacent contour lines','',0,0,0,NULL),(92099,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','If the distance between two contour lines is more, the ____ is gentle.\n(a) slope   (b) height   (c) rainfall\n','','','a) slope','',0,0,0,NULL),(92100,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ maps prove to be of great use in the planning for a region.\n(a) Physical   (b) Contour   (c) Political\n','','','b) Contour','',0,0,0,NULL),(92101,1747,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Text Book','','How can the distribution of the height and landforms in a region be shown?','','','The distribution of the height and landforms in a region can be shown with the help of contour maps using mathematical and survey methods.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92102,1747,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which facets should be taken into consideration while studying different landforms?','','','The various facets which should be taken into consideration while studying different landforms are altitude, relief, slope, direction of slope and the drainage.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92103,1747,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','To whom are the contour maps useful?','','','The contour maps are useful to mountaineers, trekkers, soldiers, defense officers, etc.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92104,1747,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Text Book','','What do you understand by observing contour lines?','','','The contour lines help us understand the slope of that region','',0,0,0,NULL),(92105,1747,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Text Book','','How will a contour map be useful for a farmer?','','','A contour map helps us to understand the direction of slope and the drainage. For a farmer, it is necessary to understand the direction of flow of water, so as to ensure proper water supply to the entire field. Also if the slope is steep the faster flow of water may result in soil erosion which can lead to the drainage of essential nutrients. Thus, with the help of a contour map a farmer can plan the plantations in his field.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92106,1747,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is meant by contour lines?','','','The lines that join places with same altitude on a map are called contour lines.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92107,1747,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which type of slope is represented by two contour lines that are away from each other?','','','The gentle slope is represented by two contour lines that are away from each other.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92108,1747,'descriptive','2a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which methods are used for drawing two dimensional picture of three dimensional object?','','','Mathematical and survey methods are used for drawing two dimensional picture of three dimensional object.','',0,0,0,NULL),(92109,1747,'descriptive','2b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','When one sees a landform on a contour map, what is the observer\'s position with respect to the landform? (For example, a hill is shown with the help of contours on a map. From where do you think you are looking at it?)','','','When one sees a landform on a contour map, the observer\'s position with respect to the landform is such that observer is looking at the landform from the above. (For example, a hill is shown with the help of contours on a map. We are looking at it from the above.)','',0,0,0,NULL),(92110,1747,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the Figure and answer the following questions :','1) What all do you see in the map?\n2) What is the general direction of the ranges shown in the map?\n3) Towards which direction is the flat land located in the map?\n4) What are the maximum and minimum values of the lines in the map?\n5) What do these values indicate?\n','','1) We can see the following things in the map: (i) Altitude of landforms (ii) Slopes of landforms (iii) Direction of slopes of landforms (iv) The spread of a particular landform in a particular region.\n2) The general direction of the ranges shown in the map is east-west.\n3) The flat land is located towards the south in the map.\n4) The maximum and minimum values of the lines in the map are 600 metres and 800 metres respectively.\n5) These values indicate the altitude of different places from the sea level.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(92111,1747,'descriptive','5b',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','Observe the Figure and answer the following questions :','1) In which direction does fort Purandar lie?\n2) What is the direction of flow of the river Karha?\n3) In which parts are the hill ranges not observed?\n4) Which part of the map is not seen in the model? Why?\n5) In which direction does the altitude of Katraj-Diveghat range decreases?\n6) In which direction are higher hill ranges located?\n','','1) Fort Purandar lies in the south direction.\n2) The direction of flow of the river Karha is from the west to the east.\n3) The hill ranges are not observed in the eastern parts.\n4) The altitude of a particular place, the nature and direction of slope of a particular place, etc. part of the map are not seen in the model. These details are not seen in a model as mathematical and survey methods are not used while preparing model.\n5) The altitude of Katraj-Diveghat range decreases from the west to the east.\n6) Higher hill ranges are located in the south direction.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(92112,1747,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the landforms shown in the following map :','','','The following landforms are shown in the above map : (1) Plains (2) Mountainous region (3) Peak.','',1,0,0,NULL),(92113,1747,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Which landforms from your district will you like to show in a contour line map?','','','I will like to show the following landforms from my district in a contour line map :\n(1) Hillranges : Shukracharya, Belagbad, Aadwa, Kalbhairav, Aashta, Honai, Dandoba, Mallikarjun.\n(2) Hills : Ramgad, Muchandi.\n(3) Plains : Plain of Krishna river.\n(4) Ghats : Tiwra.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92114,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The maps that show altitude, slope and relief of landforms are called ......\n(A) Political maps (B) Contour maps (C) Climate maps (D) Transport maps\n','','','B) Contour maps','',0,0,0,NULL),(92115,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Contour maps are most useful to ......\n(A) Shopkeepers (B) Mountaineers (C) Painters (D) Students only\n','','','B) Mountaineers','',0,0,0,NULL),(92116,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In contour maps, the observer views the landform from ......\n(A) Side view (B) Top view (C) Ground level (D) Underground\n','','','B) Top view','',0,0,0,NULL),(92117,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The direction of the Katraj–Diveghat range in the model is mainly ......\n(A) North–South (B) East–West (C) Northwest–Southeast (D) Northeast–Southwest\n','','','B) East–West','',0,0,0,NULL),(92118,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The northern part of the 3D model shows the basin of rivers ......\n(A) Krishna–Koyna (B) Mula–Mutha (C) Godavari–Pravara (D) Tapi–Purna\n','','','B) Mula–Mutha','',0,0,0,NULL),(92119,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Flat land seen in the contour map usually lies towards the area where contour lines are ......\n(A) Closely spaced (B) Very dark (C) Widely spaced (D) Coloured red\n','','','C) Widely spaced','',0,0,0,NULL),(92120,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Maximum and minimum values shown in a contour map represent ......\n(A) Population (B) Temperature (C) Altitude (D) Rainfall\n','','','C) Altitude','',0,0,0,NULL),(92121,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','The river Karha flows generally towards ......\n(A) North (B) South (C) East (D) West\n','','','C) East','',0,0,0,NULL),(92122,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Higher hill ranges in the model are located towards ......\n(A) North (B) South (C) East (D) West\n','','','B) South ','',0,0,0,NULL),(92123,1747,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Contour lines that are very close to each other indicate ......\n(A) Gentle slope (B) Steep slope (C) Flat plateau (D) River basin\n','','','B) Steep slope','',0,0,0,NULL),(92124,1747,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Contour maps show the altitude, slope, and relief of landforms.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(92125,1747,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Contour maps are not useful for mountaineers and trekkers.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(92126,1747,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Closely spaced contour lines indicate a steep slope.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(92127,1747,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Flat areas are represented by widely spaced contour lines.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(92128,1747,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','In contour maps, the observer sees the landform from the side.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(92129,1747,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Higher hill ranges are always located in the southern part of any model.','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(92130,1747,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Contour maps provide useful information for planning roads, construction, and defense.','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(92131,1747,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Reason for using contour maps in hilly regions.','','','i) Show the height and shape of hills clearly.\nii) Help in finding safe routes for mountaineering.\niii) Indicate steep and gentle slopes accurately.\niv) Assist in planning construction and road networks.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92132,1747,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Why scattered settlements are found in high relief areas.','','','i) Few houses are needed due to limited population.\nii) Terrains are difficult to build large villages.\niii) Dependence on nearby villages for daily needs.\niv) Close to natural resources like forests or pastures.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92133,1747,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','3.	Reason for nucleated settlements near rivers and fertile plains.','','','i) Easy access to water for drinking and irrigation.\nii) Fertile soil supports agriculture.\niii) Concentration helps in better social life and trade.\niv) Roads and transport facilities are more accessible.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92134,1747,'descriptive','4',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Reason why contour lines are important for understanding landforms.','','','i) Show altitude differences between areas.\nii) Help in identifying hills, valleys, and slopes.\niii) Aid in planning trekking or construction activities.\niv) Assist in flood and soil erosion management.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92135,1747,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Scattered Settlements','','','i) Houses are few and far apart.\nii) Found in high relief, dense forests, deserts, and grasslands.\niii) Limited population and inadequate facilities.\niv) Free from pollution and close to nature.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92136,1747,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Nucleated Settlements','','','i) Houses are close together near rivers, lakes, fertile plains, or transport hubs.\nii) Social services like schools, hospitals, and markets are available.\niii) People of different castes and religions live together.\niv) Roads in old parts are narrow; settlements develop particular shapes over time.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92137,1747,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Linear Settlements','','','i) Spread along roads, railways, rivers, or coasts in a straight line.\nii) Houses and some shops are arranged along a single line.\niii) Roads are parallel, and settlements grow along transport routes.\niv) Found along highways, coastal areas, and major rivers.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92138,1747,'descriptive','3b',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Contour Maps','','','i) Show altitude, slope, and relief of landforms.\nii) Contour lines indicate steepness; closely spaced lines = steep slope.\niii) Useful for mountaineers, trekkers, soldiers, and planning roads/buildings.\niv) Help in identifying hills, valleys, and drainage patterns.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92139,1747,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Column A (Type)</th>\n    <th>Column B (Characteristic)</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Scattered</td>\n    <td>a. Houses close together near water sources</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Nucleated</td>\n    <td>b. Few houses, far apart, limited population</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Linear</td>\n    <td>c. Spread along roads, rivers, coasts</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Contour Map</td>\n    <td>d. Shows altitude, slope, and relief of landforms</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1 → b ; 2 → a ; 3 → c ; 4 → d','',0,0,0,NULL),(92140,1747,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"text-align: center; background-color: #e6f3ff;\">\n    <th>Column A (Settlement)</th>\n    <th>Column B (Example)</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1. Scattered</td>\n    <td>a. Villages along coastal fishing areas</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2. Nucleated</td>\n    <td>b. Desert settlements near water wells</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3. Linear</td>\n    <td>c. Settlements along highways and rivers</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>4. Contour Map</td>\n    <td>d. Used for trekking and mountain planning</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','1 → b ; 2 → a ; 3 → c ; 4 → d','',0,0,0,NULL),(92141,1747,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','Define Scattered Settlements.','','','i) Houses are few and far apart.\nii) Found in high relief areas, deserts, dense forests, and grasslands.\niii) Population is limited.\niv) Facilities and services are inadequate.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92142,1747,'descriptive','5a',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','What are Nucleated Settlements?','','','i) Houses are close together, usually near water sources.\nii) Social services like schools, hospitals, and markets are available.\niii) People of different castes and religions live together.\niv) Roads in older parts are narrow; settlements develop particular shapes over time.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92143,1747,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Write characteristics of Linear Settlements.','','','i) Spread along roads, rivers, railways, or coasts.\nii) Houses are arranged along a single line.\niii) Roads are parallel; some shops may also be present.\niv) Settlements grow along transport routes over time.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92144,1747,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','What is a Contour Map?','','','i) Shows the altitude, slope, and relief of landforms.\nii) Contour lines indicate steepness; closely spaced lines mean steep slope.\niii) Used by mountaineers, trekkers, soldiers, and planners.\niv) Helps in identifying hills, valleys, and drainage patterns.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(92145,1747,'descriptive','3a',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','Explain how rural settlements transform into urban settlements.','','','i) Other associated occupations develop gradually around rural settlements.\nii) People from surrounding areas migrate, increasing population.\niii) Facilities like schools, hospitals, and markets are established.\niv) Growth leads to urbanization and eventually large cities if population and amenities increase.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93128,2206,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','दिलेल्या चित्राच्या साहाय्याने आपत्तीकाळातील तुमची भूमिका काय असेल ते लिहा.','','','i) दिलेल्या चित्रावरून असे दिसून येते, की त्या व्यक्तीला त्याच्या भागामध्ये येऊ घातलेल्या कुठल्यातरी आपत्तीविषयी पूर्वसूचना मिळालेली आहे. ii) अशावेळी, आपत्ती व्यवस्थापनामध्ये योग्य भूमिका बजावण्यासाठी त्याने आपल्या आसपासच्या भागातील लोकांना योग्य आणि खात्रीशीर माहिती देऊन त्यांना परिस्थितीची जाणीव करून दयावी. तसेच, आपत्तीच्या काळामध्ये सुरक्षित राहण्यासाठी आणि नुकसान कमीतकमी व्हावे यासाठी कोणती दक्षता घेता येईल याची माहिती देखील त्याने इतरांना दयावी. iii) त्याचप्रमाणे, आपत्तीच्या काळामध्ये अवघड परिस्थिती उद्भवल्यास आवश्यक ठरणारी साधने जसे अग्निशमन टाक्या, अन्नाची पाकिटे, प्रथमोपचार पेटी, इत्यादी तात्काळ गोळा करावे.','',1,0,0,NULL),(93129,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','मला ओळखा : ATP तयार करण्याचा कारखाना आहे.','','','तंतुकणिका','',0,0,0,NULL),(93130,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','मला ओळखा :  एकपदरी आहे; पण पेशीचा परासरणीय दाव नियंत्रित ठेवतो.','','','रिक्तिका','',0,0,0,NULL),(93131,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','मला ओळखा : पेशीला आधार देतो ; पण मी पेशीभित्तिका नाही. माझे शरीर तर जाळीसारखे आहे.','','','आंतर्द्रव्यजालिका','',0,0,0,NULL),(93132,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','मला ओळखा : पेशींचा जणू रसायन कारखाना.','','','पेशीद्रव्य','',0,0,0,NULL),(93133,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','मला ओळखा : माइयामुळे तर आहेत पाने हिरवी.','','','हरितलवक','',0,0,0,NULL),(93134,2207,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','कार्ये लिहा :  पेशीद्रव्य','','','i. पेशीद्रव्यामध्ये अनेक पेशीअंगके विखुरलेली असतात.\nii. पेशीद्रव्यामध्ये अनेक रासायनिक अभिक्रिया घडून येतात.\niii. पेशीअंगकांव्यतिरिक्त पेशीमध्ये असलेल्या पेशीद्रव या भागात अमिनो आम्ले, ग्लुकोज, जीवनसत्त्वे इत्यादी पेशींसाठी महत्त्वाचे असणारे पदार्थ असतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93136,2207,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','माझा रंग कोणामुळे ? (अचूक पर्याय निवडा.)\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>लाल टोमॅटो</td>\n    <td>a. क्लोरोफिल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>हिरवे पान</td>\n    <td>b. कॅरोटीन</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>गाजर</td>\n    <td>c. अन्थोसायनिन</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>जांभूळ</td>\n    <td>d. लायकोपीन</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i - d),(ii - a),(iii - b),(iv - c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93137,2207,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','वनस्पतीच्या पानांना हिरवा रंग, तर फुलांना लाल, पिवळा, केशरी, निळा असे अनेक रंग कशामुळे येत असतील ?','','','i. लवक हे वनस्पतीपेशींना रंग देणारे अंगक आहे.\nii. वनस्पतीच्या पानांमध्ये हरितलवके असतात.\niii. पानांमध्ये असलेल्या हरितलवकांमधील क्लोरोफिल किंवा हरितद्रव्य या रंगद्रव्यामुके पानांना हिरवा रंग येतो.\niv. हरितलवकांचे इतर प्रकारच्या वर्णलवकांत रूपांतर होऊ शकते. उदा. हिरवे कच्चे टोमॅटो पिकल्यावर हरितद्रव्य नष्ट होऊन लायकोपीन तयार झाल्याने त्यांना लाल रंग येतो.\nv. वर्णतवकांमधील खालील रंगद्रव्यांमुले फुलांना रंग प्राप्त होतात.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>फुलांचे रंग</th>\n    <th>रंगद्रव्य</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>केशरी</td>\n    <td>कॅरोटीन</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>पिवका</td>\n    <td>झॅन्थोफिल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>लाल, जांभका, निका</td>\n    <td>ऊॅन्थोसायनिन</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93138,2207,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','कांद्याच्या पापुद्रयात भरपूर द्रवाने भरलेल्या आयाताकृती पेशी तुम्ही पाहिल्या आहेत का ?','','','i. कांद्याच्या पापुद्रयातील भरपूर द्रवाने भरलेल्या आयताकृती पेशी खालील आकृतीत दाखवल्याप्रमाणे दिसतात.','ii. सूक्ष्मदर्शकाखाली पाहिल्या असता कांद्याच्या पापुद्रयातील पेशी एकसारख्या दिसतात.\niii. या गच्च बसवलेल्या असतात आणि आयताकृती दिसतात. तसेच, त्यांच्यामध्ये सुस्पष्ट पेशीभित्तिका आणि केंद्रक दिसून येते.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(93139,2207,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','दिलेल्या  आकृत्यांचे निरीक्षण करा, नावे लिहा व तक्ता पूर्ण करा.','','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>घटक</th>\n    <th>प्राणी पेशी</th>\n    <th>वनस्पती पेशी</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>पेशीपटल</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>पेशीभित्तिका</td>\n    <td>नाही</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>तारक़केंद्र</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n    <td>नाही</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>लवके</td>\n    <td>नाही</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>आंतर्द्रव्यजालिका</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>रिक्तिका</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>गॉल्गी संकुल</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>तंतुकणिका</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n    <td>आहे</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>',1,0,1,NULL),(93140,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','जोड्या जुळवा :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>पेशींचा पॅकिंग विभाग</td>\n    <td>a. गॉल्गी संकुल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>पेशींमधील उदूध्वस्त करणारे पथक</td>\n    <td>b. रिक्तिका</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>c. तंतुकणिका</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>d. लयकारिका</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i - a), (ii - d)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93141,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','जोड्या जुळवा : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>रिक्तिका</td>\n    <td>a. ATP हे ऊर्जासमृद्ध संयुग तयार करणे</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>तंतुकणिका</td>\n    <td>b. वनस्पती पेशींना ताठरपणा देते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>c. वनस्पती पेशींना रंग देते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>d. पेशीमधील चयापचय क्रियांचे नियंत्रण</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i - b), (ii -a )','',0,0,0,NULL),(93142,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पेशीभित्तिकेचे कार्य काय आहे ?','','','पेशीला आधार देणे, पेशीत जाणाय्या अतिरिक्त पाण्याला अड्वून पेशीचे रक्षण करणे ही पेशीभित्तिकेची कार्ये आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93143,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पेशीय भक्षण म्हणजे काय ?','','','बाहेरील पर्यावरणातून अन्न आणि इतर पदार्थ गिकंकृत करण्याच्या पेशीच्या प्रक्रियेला पेशीय भक्षण असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93144,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पेशी उत्सर्जन म्हणजे काय ?','','','पेशींमधून टाकाऊ पदार्थ पेशीबाहेर टाकण्याच्या क्रियेला पेशी उत्सर्जन असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93145,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','वनस्पती पेशी आणि प्राणी पेशींमधील पेशीद्रव्यामध्ये काय फरक असतो ?','','','वनस्पती पेशीतील पेशीद्रव्य तुलनेने पातक असते आणि मोट्या केंद्रीय रिक्तिकेमुके ते कडेला सारलेले असते, तर प्राणी पेशीतील पेशीद्रव्य हे अधिक कणयुक्त व दाट असते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93146,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','केंद्रकी छिद्रांचे कार्य लिहा.','','','केंद्रकी छिद्रांमधून केंद्रकाच्या आतबाहेर पदार्थांचे वहन होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93147,2207,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पेशीचे केंद्रक कशाचे बनलेले असते ?','','','केंद्रक गोलाकार केंद्रकी, रंगसूत्रे आणि केंद्रद्रव्यापासून बनलेले असते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93148,2207,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','फरक स्पष्ट करा : वनस्पतीपेशी आणि प्राणिपेशी','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>वनस्पती पेशी</th>\n    <th>प्राणी पेशी</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>पेशीभित्तिका असते.</td>\n    <td>पेशीभित्तिका नसते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>मोठी केंद्रीय रिक्तिका असते.</td>\n    <td>काही लहान आकाराच्या रिक्तिका असू शकतील किंवा रिक्तिका नसतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>लवके असतात.</td>\n    <td>लवके नसतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>तंतुकणिकांची संख्या तुलनेने कमी असते.</td>\n    <td>तंतुकणिकांची संख्या तुलनेने जास्त असते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>v.</td>\n    <td>पेशीद्रव्य पातळ असते आणि मोठ्या केंद्रीय रिक्तिकेमुळे ते कडेला सारलेले असते.</td>\n    <td>पेशीद्रव्य कणयुक्त व दाट असते आणि ते पेशीमधील बरीचशी जागा व्यापते.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93149,2207,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','सुबक व नामनिर्देशित आकृतीसह प्रद्रव्यपटलाची रचना स्पष्ट करा.','','','','i. प्रद्रव्यपटल हे पेशीभोवती असणारे पातक, नाजूक व लवचीक आवरण असून पेशीतील घटकांना बाह्य पर्यावरणापासून वेगळे ठेवते.\nii. यात स्फुरिल मेढाच्या दोन थरांमध्ये मिसकलेले प्रथिनांचे रेणु अशी रचना असते.\niii. प्रद्रव्यपटलाच्या रचनेमुले ते ठरावीक पदार्थांना ये - जा करू देते, तर काही पदार्थांना अटकाव करते. म्हणून, त्याला निवडक्षम पारपटल असे म्हणतात.\niv. प्रद्रव्यपटलाच्या या गुणधर्मामुले पाणी, क्षार, ऑक्सिजन असे उपयुक्त रेणू पेशीमध्ये प्रवेश करतात, तर कार्बन डायऑक्साइडसारखे टाकाऊ पदार्थ बाहेर पडतात.\nv. प्रद्रव्यपटल समस्थिती राखते. यामुळे, पेशीबाहेर काही बद्ल झाले तरी पेशीतील पर्यावरण कायम राखले जाते.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(93150,2207,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','सुबक नामनिर्देशित आकृतीच्या साहाय्याने प्राण्यांच्या लोहितरक्तकणिकेमधील परासरण स्पष्ट करा. ','','','जास्त पाणी असलेल्या भागाकडून कमी पाणी असलेल्या भागाकडे निवडक्षम पारपटलातून होणारा पाण्याचा प्रवास म्हणजे परासरण होय. ही भौतिक क्रिया असून पेशींमध्ये परासरण खालील पद्धतींनी होते :','i. समपरासारी द्रावण : जेक्हा पेशीभोवती असलेले माध्यम आणि पेशी या दोन्हींमधील पाण्याचे प्रमाण सारखे असते, तेक्हा पाणी आत किंवा बाहेर जात नाही.\nii. अवपरासारी द्रावण : जेक्ला पेशीतील पाण्याचे प्रमाण कमी आणि सभोवतालच्या माध्यमातील पाण्याचे प्रमाण जास्त असते तेक्हा पाणी पेशीत शिरते, याला अंतःपरासरण म्हणतात. उदा. बेदाणे पाण्यात ठेवल्यास ते काही वेळानंतर फुंगतात.\niii. अतिपरासारी द्रावण : पेशीतील पाण्याचे प्रमाण जास्त आणि पेशीभोवतालच्या माध्यमातील पाण्याचे प्रमाण कमी असल्यास पेशीतून पाणी बाहेर पडते. याला बहिःपरासरण असे म्हणतात. उदा. अतिपरासारी द्रावणात ठेवल्यास प्राणिपेशी किंवा वनस्पतीपेशींमधील पेशीद्रव्य आकसते.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(93151,2207,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','आंतर्द्रव्यजालिकेची रचना स्पष्ट करा.','','','','i. आंतर्द्रव्यजालिका तरल पढार्थांनी भरलेल्या सूक्ष्म नलिका व पट एकमेकांना जोडले जाऊन बनलेली जाळ्यासारखी रचना असते.\nii. ही आतील बाजूने केंद्रकाला जोडलेली असते.\niii. बाहेरील बाजूने ती प्रद्रव्यपटलाला जोडलेली असते.\niv. पृष्ठभागावर रायबोझोम्सचे कण असतील, तर तिला खडबड़ीत आंतर्द्रव्यजालिका असे म्हणतात.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(93152,2207,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','गॉल्गी संकुलाची आकृती काढून त्याची रचना स्पष्ट करा.','','','','i. गॉल्गी संकुल हे एकमेकांना समांतर असलेल्या 5 ते 8 चपट्या, पोकळ कोशांपासून बनते. या कोशांना कुंडे म्हणतात.\nii. या कुंडांमध्ये विविध प्रकारची विकरे असतात, जी प्रथिनांमध्ये बढ़ल घडवून आणण्यासाठी उपयोगी असतात.\niii. आंतर्द्रव्यजालिकेकडून आलेली प्रथिने गोलीय पिटिकांमध्ये बंदिस्त होतात. पेशीद्रव्यामार्फत या पिटिका संकुलापर्यंत येतात आणि त्याच्या निर्मिताक्षम बाजूशी संयोग पावून त्यांतील द्रव्य कुंडांमध्ये पाठवले जाते.\niv. या प्रथिनांमध्ये रासायनिक बढ़ल घडवले जातात. ही बदल झालेली प्रथिने पुन्हा गोलीय पिटिकांमध्ये बंद होऊन गॉल्गी संकुलाच्या परिपक्व बाजूने बाहेर पडतात.\nv. म्हणजेच, ही कुंडे कारखान्यातील वस्तू बांधून पुढे पाठवणाया पॅकिंग विभागाप्रमाणे काम करतात.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(93153,2207,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','सुबक व नामनिर्देशित आकृतीच्या साहाय्याने तंतुकणिकेची रचना स्पष्ट करा.','','','','i. तंतुकणिका दुहेरी आवरणाची बनलेली असते.\nii. बाह्य आवरण सच्छिद्र असते, तर आतील आवरण घड्यांनी बनलेले असते.\niii. आतील आवरणाच्या घड्यांना शिखा असे म्हणतात. या घड्यांमुले आवरणाचा पृष्ठभाग वाढतो आणि तेथे पेशीतील अनेक रासायनिक क्रिया घडतात.\niv. तंतुकणिकेच्या आतील पोकळी जेलीसटृश्य द्रव्यांनी भरलेली असून यात रायवोझ़ोम्स व डीऑक्सिरायबो न्यूक्लिक आम्लाचे (DNA) रेणू असतात.\nv. वनस्पती पेशींमध्ये प्राणी पेशींपेक्षा तंतुकणिकांची संख्या कमी असते.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(93154,2207,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','हरितलवकाची सुबक व नामनिर्देशित आकृती काढा आणि वनस्पती पेशींमधील त्याची रचना व कार्य स्पष्ट करा.','','','','i. हरितलवके ही वनस्पतींच्या पानांमध्ये आढळणारी हिख्या रंगाची लवके असून यामध्ये क्लोरोफिल किंवा हरितद्रव्य नावाचे रंगद्रव्य असते, जे वनस्पतींमधील प्रकाशसंश्लेषण प्रक्रियेयाठी महत्त्वाचे असते.\nii. हरितलवकांच्या पीटिकांमध्ये चकत्यांसाख्या थायॉलॅकॉइड असलेल्या लवककण्या असतात.\niii. हरितलवकांमध्ये असलेले हरितद्रव्य सौर ऊर्जा शोषून तिचे रासायनिक ऊर्जेत म्हणजे अन्नात रूपांतर करते.\niv. हरितलवकांच्या पिटिकांमध्ये प्रकाशसंश्लेषणासाठी आवश्यक असणारी विकरे, DNA, रायबोझोम्स आणि पिष्टमय पदार्थ असतात.\nv. हरितलवकांमध्ये DNA व रायबोझोम्स असल्याने ती स्वतः ची प्रथिने तयार करू शकतात.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(93155,2207,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील आकृती पाहा आणि दिलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','i. आकृतीत दाखवलेली रचना ओळखा.\nii. आकृतीतील \' X \' हा घटक ओळखा.\niii. पेशीभित्तिका मुख्यतः कोणत्या कर्बोदकांपासून बनलेली असते ?\n','','i) आकृतीत दाखवलेली रचना पेशीभित्तिकेची आहे.\nii) आकृतीतील \' X \' हा घटक म्हणजे सूक्ष्मकाष्ठ तंतू आहे.\niii) पेशीभित्तिका मुख्यतः सेल्युलोज, हेमीसेल्युलोज आणि पेक्टीन या कर्बोदकांची बनलेली असते.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(93156,2207,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील आकृती पाहा आणि दिलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','i. आकृतीमध्ये कोणते उपकरण दाखवले आहे ?\nii. या उपकरणाचे कार्य काय आहे ?\n','','i) आकृतीमध्ये इलेक्ट्रॉन सूक्ष्मदर्शक दाखवला आहे.\nii) a. या उपकरणाचा उपयोग संयुक्त सूक्ष्मदर्शकापेक्षा जास्त चांगल्या पद्धतीने पेशींचे निरिक्षण करण्यासाठी केला जातो.\nb. केंद्रक आणि हरितलवकांव्यतिरिक्ति इतर सर्व पेशी अंगके फक्त इलेक्ट्रॉन सूक्ष्मदर्शकाखालीच पाहता येतात.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(93157,2207,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील केंद्रकाची आकृती अभ्यासा आणि दिलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','i. आकृतीमध्ये \' X \' हे नाव दिलेला भाग ओळखा.\nii. आकृतीमध्ये \' X \' हे नाव दिलेला भागाचे कार्य लिहा.\n','','i. आकृतीमध्ये \' X \' हे नाव दिलेला भाग म्हणजे केंद्रकी छिद्र आहे.\nii. केंद्रकी छिद्रातून केंद्रकाच्या आत - बाहेर पदार्थांचे वहन होते.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(93158,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','माझा जोडीदार शोधा.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>हृद्याचे ठोके</td>\n    <td>a. 350 मिली</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>RBC</td>\n    <td>b. 7.4</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>WBC</td>\n    <td>c. 37&deg;C</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>रक्तदान</td>\n    <td>d. 72</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>v.</td>\n    <td>निरोगी व्यक्तीच्या शरीराचे तापमान</td>\n    <td>e. 50-60 लक्ष प्रति घ. मिली</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>vi.</td>\n    <td>ऑक्सिजनयुक्त रक्ताचा सामू</td>\n    <td>f. 5000-10000 प्रति घ. मिली</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i - d), (ii - e), (iii - f), (iv - a), (v - c), (vi - b)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93159,2208,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','खालील तक्ता पूर्ण करा.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>इंद्रिय संस्था</th>\n    <th>इंद्रिये</th>\n    <th>कार्ये</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>श्वसन संस्था</td>\n    <td>-</td>\n    <td>-</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>रक्ताभिसरण संस्था</td>\n    <td>...............</td>\n    <td>..........</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>इंद्रिय संस्था</th>\n    <th>इंद्रिये</th>\n    <th>कार्ये</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td rowspan=\"5\">श्वसन संस्था</td>\n    <td>नाक</td>\n    <td>केस व चिकट पदार्थाच्या साहाय्याने हवा गाळून घेते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>घसा</td>\n    <td>हवा श्वासनलिकेत पोहोचवते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>श्वासनलिका</td>\n    <td>हवा फुफ्फुसांपर्यंत पोहोचवते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>फुफ्फुसे</td>\n    <td>वायूंची देवघेव करतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>श्वासपटल</td>\n    <td>हवा आत घेण्याच्या व बाहेर सोडण्याच्या प्रक्रियेला साहाय्य करतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td rowspan=\"2\">रक्ताभिसरण संस्था</td>\n    <td>हृदय</td>\n    <td>संपूर्ण शरीरास रक्ताचा पुरवटा करते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>रक्तवाहिन्या</td>\n    <td>संपूर्ण शरीरात रक्ताचे वहन करते.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93160,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','नामनिर्देशित सुबक आकृत्या काढा : श्वसनसंस्था','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(93161,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','नामनिर्देशित सुबक आकृत्या काढा : हृदयाची आंतररचना','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(93162,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','सकारण स्पष्ट करा : मानवाचे रक्त तांबड्या रंगाचे असते.','','','i. मानवी रक्तातील लाल रंगाच्या लोहयुक्त घटक हिमोग्लोबिनमुके रक्त लाल रंगाचे दिसते.\nii. या लोहाच्या रेणूंची ऑक्सिजनबरोबर क्रिया होऊन गडद लाल रंग येतो.\nम्हणूनच, मानवाचे रक्त तांबड्या (लाल) रंगाचे असते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93163,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','सकारण स्पष्ट करा : श्वासपटलाची वर आणि खाली होण्याची क्रिया एकापाटोपाट एक होते.','','','i. बरगङ्यांनी बनलेल्या छातीच्या पिंजयाच्या तळाशी असलेला स्नायूंचा पडदा म्हणजे श्वासपटल होय.\nii. श्वासपटलाच्या आकुंचन आणि शिथिलीकरणामुके श्वासोच्छासाची क्रिया घडते.\niii. बरगङ्या किंचित वर उचलत्या जातात आणि श्वासपटल खाली येते, तेक्ला फुफुसांवरील दाव कमी होतो. त्यामुके, वाहेरील हवा नाकावाटे फुफ्फुसामध्ये जाते.\niv. बरगङ्या मूक जागी परत येतात आणि श्वासपटल वर उचलले जाते, तेक्हा फुफ्फुसांवर दाब पड़तो. त्यामुके, हवा नाकावाटे वाहेर ढकलली जाते.\nv. म्हणून, श्वासोच्छवासाची प्रक्रिया घडवून आण्णयासाठी श्वासपटलाची वर आणि खाली होण्याची क्रिया एकापाठोपाठ एक होते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93164,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','सकारण स्पष्ट करा : रक्तदानास सर्वश्रेष्ट दान संबोधले जाते.','','','i. अपघाताच्या वेळी होणाय्या रक्तम्रावामुके होणारी रक्ताची कमतरता, शस्त्रक्रिया, अनेमिया, थॅलैसेमिया, कॅन्सर, प्रसूती यांसारख्या विविध परिस्थितींत रक्त पराधानाची आवश्यकता भासते.\nii. तसेच, रक्त हे वापरापूर्वी मर्यादित वेळेपुरतेच जमा करून ठेवता येत असल्याने नियमित रक्तपुरवठ्याची गरज सातत्याने भासत असते.\niii. नियमित रक्तदानामुळे जेक्हा आणि जेथे गरज असेल तेक्हा आणि तेथे सुरक्षित रक्त उपलब्ध होऊ शकते.\niv. आजचा रक्तदाता भविष्यात रक्त घेणाराही असू शकतो. त्यामुके, रक्तदान हे निरपेक्षपणे केले गेले पाहिजे.\nv. निरोगी व्यक्तीने केलेल्या रक्तदानामुले एखाद्या गरजू रुग्णाचे प्राण वाचू शकतात. त्यामुके, रक्तनान हे सर्वश्रेष्ठ दान मानले जाते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93165,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','सकारण स्पष्ट करा : \' O \' रक्तगट असलेल्या व्यक्तीला \'सार्वत्रिक दाता\' म्हणतात.','','','i. रक्तदान करणाया व्यक्तीस रक्तदाता म्हणतात.\nii. \' O \' रक्तगट असलेली व्यक्ती कुछलाही रक्तगट असलेल्या (O, A , B आणि AB) व्यक्तास रक्तदान करू शकते.\nम्हणून \' O \' रक्तगट असलेल्या व्यक्ताला \'सार्वत्रिक दाता\' म्हणतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93166,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','सकारण स्पष्ट करा : आहारात मिठाचे प्रमाण कमी असावे.','','','i. बरेच लोक आपल्या अन्नातून आवश्यकतेपेक्षा अधिक मिठाचे सेवन करतात.\nii. अधिक प्रमाणात मिठाचे (सोडिअम) सेवन केल्यास रक्तदाब वाढून रक्तवाहिन्यांवर ताण येतो.\niii. याचा परिणाम म्हणून उच्च रक्तदाबासारखा आजार शरीरास जडतो.\niv. निरोगी राहण्यासाठी व अशा प्रकारचे आजार होणे टाळण्यासाठी आहारात मिठाचे प्रमाण कमी असणे आवश्यक असते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93167,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','रक्ताभिसरण संस्थेचा श्वसन, पचन व उत्सर्जन संस्थेशी असणारा संबंध कार्याच्या स्वरूपात लिहा.','','','i. रक्ताभिसरण संस्था शरीराच्या विविध अवयवांमध्ये पाणी, संप्रेरके, ऑक्सिजन, विद्राव्य अन्नघटक, टाकाऊ पदार्थ अशा अनेक पदार्थांचे वहन करते.\nii. श्वास घेताना आत घेतलेला ऑक्सिजन वायुकोशांमध्ये जातो आणि रक्तापर्यंत पोहोचतो. त्यानंतर तो पेशीश्वसनाच्या क्रियेसाठी शरीराच्या विविध भागांत असलेल्या पेशींपर्यंत पोहोचवला जातो. पेशीश्वसनाच्या क्रियेत ऑक्सिजनमुके ग्लुकोजचे मंद्चलन होऊन ऊर्जा मोकळी होते.\niii. पेशीश्वसनाच्या क्रियेत निर्माण झालेला कार्बन डायऑक्साइड हा निरुपयोगी पदार्थ वायुकोशांत येतो. त्याचे श्वसनिकेतून वहन होऊन तो श्वासनलिकेत येतो. तेथून तो नाकादूवारे शरीरातून वाहेर टाकला जातो.\niv. पचनसंस्थेतील अवयवांना शरीराच्या पेशींपर्यंत पोषणतत्त्वांचे वहन करण्यासाठी रक्ताभिसरण संस्थेची आवश्यकता असते. ग्लुकोज, अमिनो आम्ले, मेढाम्ले ही साधी पोषणतत्त्वे अन्ननलिकेच्या भित्तिकेतून रक्तात घेतली जातात व शरीराच्या सर्व पेशींपर्यंत पोहोचवर्ली जातात.\nv. अमोनिआ, युरिआ, क्रिएटिनीन यांसारखे नायट्रोजनयुक्त टाकाऊ पदार्थसुद्धा ऊर्तींकडून उत्सजर्नासाठी रक्तात जमा केले जातात.\nvi. अशाप्रकारे, शरीराची कार्ये पार पाडण्यासाठी शरीरातल्या विविध संस्था एकत्रितपणे कार्य करतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93168,2208,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','मानवी रक्ताची संरचना लिहा.','','','रक्ताची संरचना :\ni. रक्त हा लाल रंगाचा प्रवाही पदार्थ असून एक द्रायू संयोगी ऊती आहे.\nii. ऑक्सिजनयुक्त रक्ताचा रंग लाल भडक असतो व ते चवीला खारट असते.\niii. रक्ताचा सामू (pH) 7.4  असतो. (काहीसे आम्लारिधर्मी)\niv. रक्त हे रक्तद्रव व रक्तपेशी (रक्तकणिका) या दोन प्रमुख घटकांनी बनलेले असते.\na. रक्तद्रव : हा फिकट पिवळसर रंगाचा, नितक, आणि काहीसा आम्लारिधर्मी द्रव असतो. यात सुमारे 90 – 92%  पाणी, 6 – 8% प्रथिने, 1 -2%   असेंद्रिय क्षार आणि इतर काही घटक असतात.\nरक्तद्रवाचे घटक आणि कार्ये :\n1. अल्ध्युमिन : संपूर्ण शरीरात पाणी विभागण्याचे कार्य करते.\n2. ग्लोब्युलीन्स : संसर्गाशी लढण्याचे (संरक्षणाचे) महत्त्वाचे कार्य करतात.\n3. फायब्रिनोजेन आणि प्रोश्रोम्बीन : रक्त गोठण्याच्या क्रियेत साहाय्यक ठरतात.\n4. कॅल्शिअम, सोडिअम, पोटैशिअम यांसारखी असेंद्रिय आयने : चेता आणि स्नायू कार्याचे नियंत्रण ठेवतात.\nb. रक्तपेशी : रक्तपेशींचे मुख्य प्रकार पुढालप्रमाणे :\n5. लोहित रक्तपेशी / रक्तकणिका (RBC) : या आकाराने वर्तुळाकार, लहान आणि केंद्रक नसलेल्या पेशी असतात. हिमोग्लोबीन या लोहयुक्त प्रथिनामुळे रक्तपेशी लाल रंगाच्या दिसतात. रक्ताच्या प्रत्येक घनमिलिमिटर मध्ये 50 ते 60 लक्ष RBC असतात. त्यांची निर्मिती अस्थिमज्जेत होते. RBC सरासरी 120 दिवस जगतात.\nकार्य : हिमोग्लोबिनमुले ऑक्सिजन रक्तात विरखकतो.\n2. पांढऱ्यापेशी / श्वेत रक्तकणिका (WBC) : WBC या आकाराने मोठ्या, केंद्रक असलेल्या रंगहीन पेशी असतात. रक्ताच्या प्रत्येक घनमिलिमिटरमध्ये 5000 ते 10000 पांढया पेशी असतात. या पेशींचे बेसोफील, इओसिनोफिल, न्यूट्रोफील, मोनोसाईट्स, लिम्फोसाईट्स असे पाच प्रकार आहेत. पांढय्या पेशींची निर्मिती अस्थिमज्जेत होते.\nकार्य : शरीरात रोगजंतूंचा शिरकाव झाल्यास WBC रोगजंतूंवर हल्ला करून, संसर्गापासून शरीराचा बचाव करतात व विविध रोगांपासून शरीराला संरक्षण देतात.\n3. रक्तपट्टिका : रक्तपट्टीका या अतिशय लहान व तबकडीच्या आकारासारख्या दिसतात. रक्ताच्या एका घनमिलिमिटरमध्ये यांचे प्रमाण सुमारे 2.5 लाख ते 4 लाख इतके असते.\nकार्य: जखमेमुले होणारा रक्तम्राव रक्त गोठवून बंद करणयाचे कार्य रक्तपट्टिका करतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93169,2208,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Text Book','','मानवी रक्ताची  कार्ये लिहा.','','','रक्ताची कार्ये :\ni. वायूंचे परिवहन : फुफ्फुसांतील ऑक्सिजन रक्ताच्या मार्फत संपूर्ण शरीरातील पेशींपर्यंत वाहून नेला जातो. तसेच, कार्बन डायऑक्साइड ऊतींकडून फुफ्फुसांमध्ये आणला जातो.\nii. पोषणतत्त्वांचे वहन (पेशींना खाद्य पुरवणे) : ग्लुकोज, अमिनो आम्ले, मेदाम्ले यांसारखी पचन झालेली पोषणतत्त्वे अन्ननलिकेच्या भित्तिकेतून रक्तात घेतली जातात व शरीराच्या सर्व पेशींपर्यंत पोहोचवली जातात.\niii. टाकाऊ पदार्थांचे वहन : युरिआ, अमोनिआ, क्रिएटिनीन है नायट्रोजनयुक्त टाकाऊ पदार्थ ऊतींकडून रक्तामध्ये जमा केले जातात. यानंतर है टाकाऊ पदार्थ रक्ताढ़वारे वृक्काकडे शरीराबाहेर टाकण्यासाठी वाहून नेले जातात.\niv. शरीराचे संरक्षण : रक्तात निर्माण होणाया प्रतिपिंडांमुळे सूक्ष्म जीवाणू व इतर उपद्रवी कण यांपासून शरीराचे रक्षण होते.\nv. विकर व संप्रेरक परिवहन : विकर व संप्रेरके जिथे स्रवतात, तेथून ज्या ठिकाणी त्यांची अभिक्रिया होते तेथे ती रक्तातून वाहून नेली जातात.\nvi. तापमानाचे नियमन : वाहिनी विस्फारण आणि वाहिनी संकोचन या क्रियांमुळे शरीराचे तापमान 37°C इतके कायम राखले जाते.\nvii. सोडिअम, पोटॅशिअम यांसारख्या शरीरातील क्षारांचा समतोल राखणे.\nviii. रक्तातील रक्तपट्टिका (प्लेटलेट) आणि फायब्रिनोजेन हे प्रथिन गुठकी निर्माण करून जखमेमुले होणारा रक्तम्राव बंद करण्याचे कार्य करतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93170,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','रक्तदानाचे महत्त्व व गरज स्पष्ट करा.','','','i. अपघाताच्या वेळी होणाय्या रक्तम्रावामुके होणारी रक्ताची कमतरता, शस्त्रक्रिया, अनेमिया, थॅलैसेमिया, कॅन्सर, प्रसूती यांसारख्या विविध परिस्थितींत रक्त पराधानाची आवश्यकता भासते.\nii. तसेच, रक्त हे वापरापूर्वी मर्यादित वेळेपुरतेच जमा करून ठेवता येत असल्याने नियमित रक्तपुरवठ्याची गरज सातत्याने भासत असते.\niii. नियमित रक्तदानामुळे जेक्हा आणि जेथे गरज असेल तेक्हा आणि तेथे सुरक्षित रक्त उपलब्ध होऊ शकते.\niv. आजचा रक्तदाता भविष्यात रक्त घेणाराही असू शकतो. त्यामुके, रक्तदान हे निरपेक्षपणे केले गेले पाहिजे.\nv. निरोगी व्यक्तीने केलेल्या रक्तदानामुले एखाद्या गरजू रुग्णाचे प्राण वाचू शकतात. त्यामुके, रक्तनान हे सर्वश्रेष्ठ दान मानले जाते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93171,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','फरक स्पष्ट करा : धमन्या व शिरा','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>धमन्या</th>\n    <th>शिरा</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>हृद्यापासून शरीराच्या वेगवेगळ्या भागांकडे रक्त पोहोचवणाऱ्या रक्तवाहिन्या.</td>\n    <td>शरीराच्या विविध भागांकडून हृदयाकडे रक्त वाहून नेणाऱ्या वाहिन्या.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>फुफ्फुस धमनीचा अपवाद वगळता सर्व धमन्या ऑक्सिजनयुक्त रक्त वाहून नेतात.</td>\n    <td>फुफ्फुसशिरांव्यतिरिक्त इतर सर्व शिरा विनाक्षजती रक्त वाहून नेतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>या रक्तवाहिन्यांना झडपा नसतात.</td>\n    <td>या रक्तवाहिन्यांना झडपा असतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>धमन्या शरीरात खोलवर असतात.</td>\n    <td>बहुतेक शिरा या त्वचेलगतच असतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>v.</td>\n    <td>धमन्यांची भित्तिका जाड असते.</td>\n    <td>शिरांची भित्तिका पातळ असते.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93172,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','फरक स्पष्ट करा : बहि :श्वसन व अंत :श्वसन','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>बहि :श्वसन</th>\n    <th>अंत :श्वसन</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>हवा आत घेणे व बाहेर सोडणे या क्रियांचा समावेश होतो.</td>\n    <td>पेशी व रक्त यांदरम्यान वायूंची देवाणघेवाण (O<sub>2</sub> व CO<sub>2</sub>) होते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>हे फुफ्फुसांमध्ये होते व यांत बाहेरील हवेचा समावेश असतो.</td>\n    <td>हे केवळ शरीरातच होते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>फुफ्फुसांतील ऑक्सिजन रक्तात जातो.</td>\n    <td>रक्तातून आलेला ऑक्सिजन पेशी आणि ऊतीपर्यंत पोहोचवला जातो.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>रक्तातील कार्बन डायऑक्साइड फुफ्फुसांद्वारा बाहेर टाकला जातो.</td>\n    <td>पेशींमधील व ऊतीमधील कार्बन डायऑक्साइडचे रक्ताद्वारे फुफ्फुसात वहन होते.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93173,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','रक्तदान करणाऱ्या व्यक्तीसाठी निरोगी असल्याबाबतचे कोणते निकष लक्षात घ्याल ? ','','','रक्तदान करणाया व्यक्तीसाठी खालील निकष्ष लक्षात घेतले पाहिजेतः\ni. हिमोग्लोबिनचे प्रमाण : स्त्रियांमध्ये रक्तदान करण्यासाठी हिमोग्लोबिनची पातकी 12.5 g/dL, इतकी तर पुरुषांमध्ये कमीतकमी 13.0 g/dL, इतकी असावी लागते.\nii. आजार : रक्तदात्यास कोणताही संसर्गजन्य आजार नसावा.\niii. वय : रक्तदात्याचे वय 16 ते 70 यांदरम्यान असावे.\niv. वजन : रक्तात्याचे वजन कमीतकमी 45 किलो इतके असले पाहिजे.\nv. नाडी : नाडीचा दर हा 60-100 ठोके प्रति मिनिट असावा.\nvi. रक्तदाब : व्यक्तीचा रक्तिदाब हा साधारण रक्तनाबाच्या जवळपास असणारा म्हणजेच 120/80 मिमी असावा. इतका रक्तदाब व्यक्ती निरोगी असल्याचे लक्षण असते.\nvii. शरीराचे तापमान : शरीराचे तापमान हे 37.5°C पेक्षा जास्त नसावे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93174,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','रक्तातील तांबड्या पेशीमध्ये ____ हे लोहाचे संयुग असते.\n(a) हिमोग्लोबिन (b) आम्लारिधर्मी (c) श्वासपटल (d) अस्थिमज्जा\n','','','a) हिमोग्लोबिन','',0,0,0,NULL),(93175,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','____ हे उदरपोकळी व उरोपोकळी यांच्या दरम्यान असते.\n(a) हिमोग्लोबिन (b) श्वासपटल (c) ऐच्छिक (d) अनैच्छिक\n','','','b) श्वासपटल','',0,0,0,NULL),(93176,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','हृदय स्नायू ____ असतात.\n(a) ऐच्छिक (b) अनैच्छिक (c) आम्लधर्मी (d) आम्लारिधर्मी\n','','','(b) अनैच्छिक','',0,0,0,NULL),(93177,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','ऑक्सिजनयुक्त रक्ताचा pH ____ असतो.\n(a) आम्लधर्मी (b) आम्लारिधर्मी (c) श्वासपटल (d) हिमोग्लोबिन\n','','','b) आम्लारिधर्मी','',0,0,0,NULL),(93178,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','RBC ची निर्मिती ____ मध्ये होते.\n(a) अस्थिमज्जा (b) श्वासपटल (c) हिमोग्लोबिन (d) अनैच्छिक\n','','','a) अस्थिमज्जा','',0,0,0,NULL),(93179,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','रोग/ विकार ओळखा : आज तिचे बाळ दीड वर्षाचे झाले ; पण ते निरोगी, हसरे नाही. ते सारखे किरकिर करते, दिवसेंदिवस अशक्त दिसत आहे. त्याला धाप लागते. त्याचा श्वास फार जलद आहे. त्याची नखे निळसर दिसू लागली आहेत.','','','धाप लागणे आणि नखे निकसर दिसणे है ऑक्सिजनची कमतरता दर्शवतात. म्हणून, या बाळाला श्वसनाचा विकार असावा.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93180,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','तुमच्या शेजारच्या काकांचे रक्तदाबाच्या विकाराचे निदान डॉक्टरांनी केले आहे. त्यांचा रक्तदाब नियंत्रणात राहण्यासाठी त्यांनी काय करावे बरे ?','','','i. रक्तदाब नियंत्रित ठेवण्यासाठी त्यांनी डॉक्टरांकडे जाऊन उच्च रक्तदाबाचे योग्य कारण समजून व्यावे.\nii. त्यांनी आहारातील मिठाचे प्रमाण व चरबीयुक्त पढार्थ्थ कमी करावेत.\niii. त्यांनी नियमित व्यायाम करायला हवा.\niv. ताण नियंत्रित करण्यासाठी त्यांनी ध्यान करणे, संगीत ऐकणे यांसारख्या गोष्टी करायला हव्यात.\nv. मद्यपान व धूम्रपान टाळायला हवे. यामुके हृंद्यविकाराचा झटका येण्याचा धोका कमी होतो\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93181,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','इंद्रिये व इंद्रिय संस्था कशापासून बनलेली असतात ?','','','ठरावीक कार्य करणाऱ्या ऊतीपासून इंद्रिये बनतात. ठरावीक काम एकत्रितपणे करणाय्या इंद्रिय समूहाला इंद्रिय संस्था असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93182,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','मानवी शरीरामध्ये कोणकोणत्या इंद्रिय संस्था आहेत ?','','','मानवी शरीरात रक्ताभिसरण संस्था, श्वसन संस्था, पचन संस्था, उत्सर्जन संस्था, चेता संस्था, अस्थि संस्था, स्नायू संस्था, प्रजनन संस्था अशा अनेक इंद्रिय संस्था कार्यरत असतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93183,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','प्राण्यांच्या शरीरामध्ये श्वासोच्छ्वासाचे कार्य कोणकोणती इंद्रिये करतात ?','','','i. वेगवेगळ्या श्वसनाकरिता वेगवेगळी इंद्रिये असतात.\nii. प्राण्यांमध्ये उपत्वचा, कल्ले, फुफ्फुसे, वाहिन्यांचे जाके यांदूवारे श्वसन होते.\niii. मानवामध्ये श्वसनप्रक्रियेत नाक, स्वरयंत्र, घसा, श्वासनलिका, फुफुसे ही इंद्रिये कार्य करतात. श्वासपटल हीसुदूधा श्वसनसंस्थेतील एक महत्त्वाची रचना आहे.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93184,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्ताभिसरण म्हणजे काय ? ','','','ऋदयाकडून शरीराच्या विविध अवयवांकडे रक्त पोहोचवण्याच्या आणि तेथून परत ऋदयाकडे आणण्याच्या क्रियेस \'रक्ताभिसरण\' असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93185,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपण गाढ झोपेत असताना शरीरामध्ये सुरू असलेली कार्ये कोणती ?','','','i. जवळजवक सर्व महत्त्वाची शारीरिक कार्ये म्हणजे रक्ताभिसरण, श्वसन, पचन इत्यादी गाढ झोपेतही घडून येत असतात; मात्र झोपेत आपल्या श्वासोच्छासाची पद्धती बदलते.\nii. तसेच, काही शारीरिक क्रिया झोपेदरम्यान मंदावतात. उदा. वृक्काचे कार्य मंदावते. त्यामुळे, मूत्र उत्पादन कमी होते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93186,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपल्या शरीरात कोणकोणत्या जीवनक्रिया सतत सुरू असतात ?','','','खालील जीवनक्रिया आपल्या शरीरात सतत सुरू असतातः श्वसन (श्वास घेणे आणि श्वास सोड्णे), रक्ताभिसरण, विविध रक्तपेशी आणि घटक यांचे उत्पादन इत्यादी.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93187,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','श्वसनसंस्थेमध्ये कोणकोणत्या इंद्रियांचा समावेश होतो ?','','','श्वसनसंस्थेमध्ये नाक, घसा, स्वरयंत्र, श्वासनलिका, श्वासटल आणि फुफ्फुसे या इंद्रियांचा समावेश होतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93188,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','जेवताना बोलू नये. असे का ?','','','i. श्वासनलिकेच्या सुरुवातीच्या भागात वरच्या बाजूस एक झाकण असते.\nii. हे झाकण हवा घशातून श्वासनलिकेत जाऊ देते.\niii. जेक्हा व्यक्ती अन्न गिकते तेक्रा या झाकणामुळे स्वरयंत्राचा भाग झाकला जातो, ज्यामुले श्वासनलिकेत अन्नाचे कण शिरत नाहीत.\niv. मात्र, जेवताना बोलल्यामुके श्वासनलिका मोककी होऊन अन्नकण अन्नलिकेऐवजी श्वासनलिकेत जाण्याची शक्यता असते. यामुके, गुदमरायला होते\nम्हणूनच, जेवताना बोलू नये.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93189,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','कानांच्या मागे किंवा पायांच्या टाचेच्या वरच्या बाजूस सुद्धा ठोके अनुभवले जातात. हे ठोके कशामुळे होतात ?','','','i. रक्ताभिसरण होत असताना हृदयाकडून आलेले रक्त धमन्यांमध्ये ढकलले जाते. या रक्तप्रवाहामुळे धमन्यांचा विस्तार आणि आकुंचन होते.\nii. धमन्यांच्या भित्तिकेवर पडणाया रक्तप्रवाहाच्या दबावामुके नाडीचे ठोके जाणवतात.\nम्हणून, कानाच्या मागे किंवा पायाच्या टाचेच्या वरच्या बाजूस सुदूधा ठोके अनुभवले जातात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93190,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','इंधनाच्या ज्वलनातून उष्णतेबरोबरच ध्वनी व प्रकाश निर्मिती होते. याच प्रकारे पेशीत अन्नघटकांचे ज्वलन होत असताना ध्वनी व प्रकाश निर्मिती होत असेल काय?','','','पेशीतील अन्नघटकांचे होणारे ज्वलन ही एक संथ प्रक्रिया असते आणि यात अन्नघटक कमी प्रमाणात असतात, त्यामुके यामध्ये इंधन ज्वलनाप्रमाणे मोठ्या प्रमाणात ऊर्जा तयार होत नाही. म्हणूनच, पेशीतील अन्नघटांचे ज्वलन होत असताना ध्वनी व प्रकाश यांची निर्मिती होणार नाही.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93191,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','निलांच्या पोकळीमध्ये इडपा कशासाठी असतात ? त्या नसत्या तर काय झाले असते?','','','i. फुफ्फुसशिरांचा अपवाद वगळता इतर सर्व निलांमधून विनाक्षजती रक्त शरीराच्या विविध भागांकडून हुड्याकड्डे वाहून नेले जाते.\nii. या निलांमधील इडपा रक्तप्रवाह एकाच दिशेने हृंयाकडे राखायला मटत करतात आणि गुरुत्वाकर्षण बलामुळे रक्ताचा प्रवाह मागे वाहण्यास प्रतिबंध करतात.\niii. जर या झडपा नसतील, तर रक्ताचा प्रवाह उलटा मागे वाहण्यास प्रतिवंध करता येणार नाही आणि रक्ताभिसरण संस्थेचे कार्य विघडेल.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93192,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','नाडीचे ठोके : हृदयाचे ठोके व हाताच्या नाडीचे ठोके यांचा सहसंबंध शोधा.','','','i. नाडीचा ठोका म्हणजे हुद्याच्या आकुंचनानंतर धमन्यांमधून वाहणारा दाव तरंग असतो.\nii. एका मिनिटात हृदयाचे जे स्पंदन होते त्यास हंटयाचा ठोका असे म्हणतात.\niii. हृढ़याच्या आकुंचनामुळे धमन्यांमधील रक्तटाव वाढतो. त्यामुले, नाड़ीचे ठोके हट्याच्या ठोक्यांएवढेच असतात.\niv. म्हणूनच, हाताच्या नाडीच्या ठोक्यांच्या परीक्षणावस्न हृद्याचे ठोके मोजता येतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93193,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','श्वसनक्रिया होताना छातीच्या पिंजऱ्याच्या खालील भागात होणाया हालचाली दाखवणाऱ्या आकृतीचे निरीक्षण करा व चर्चा करा.','','','i. उद्रपोककी आणि छातीची पोकळी यांमध्ये स्नायूंचा पड़ा म्हणजेच श्वासपटल असते. श्वासपटलाच्या वर आणि खाली अशा होणाया हालचालींमुके फुफ्फुसांचे प्रसरण व शिथिलीकरण घड्नून येत असते.\nii. बरगड्या किंचित वर उचलल्या जाणे आणि श्वासपटल खाली जाणे यांमुके फुफ्फुसांवरील ढाब कमी होतो. त्यामुले, हवा नाकावाटे फुफ्फुसात जाते.\niii. बरगङ्या मूळ जागी परत आल्या आणि श्वासपटल पुन्हा वर उचलले गेले, की फुफ्फुसांवर दाब पड्तो. यातील हवा नाकावाटे बाहेर ढकलली जाते. श्वासपटल सतत वर आणि खाली होण्याची हालचाल श्वासोच्छास घड्डण्यासाठी गरजेची असते.\niv. या हालचाली खालील आकृतीदूवारे दर्शवता येतात :\n','',0,0,1,NULL),(93194,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','एखादे ठरावीक काम एकत्रितपणे करणाऱ्या इंद्रिय समूहाला ____ असे म्हणतात.\n(a) इंद्रिय संस्था (b) इंद्रिय प्रणाली (c) मज्जासंस्था (d) मेंदू\n','','','a) इंद्रिय संस्था','',0,0,0,NULL),(93195,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','बर्हिश्वसनात हवेतील ऑक्सिजन ____ जातो.\n(a) रक्तामध्ये (b) फुफ्फुसांतून बाहेर (c) पेशींमधून बाहेर (d) मेंदूकडे\n','','','a) रक्तामध्ये','',0,0,0,NULL),(93196,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','अन्ननलिका व श्वासनलिका ____ सुरू होतात.\na) घशापासून b) फुफ्फुसांपासून c) तोंडापासून d) नाकापासून\n','','','a) घशापासून','',0,0,0,NULL),(93197,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','फुफ्फुसांत असंख्य ____ असल्याने वायूंच्या देवाणघेवाणीसाठी विस्तृत पृष्ठभाग उपलब्ध होतो.\na) वायुकोश b) पेशी c) नलिका d) रक्तवाहिन्या\n','','','a) वायुकोश','',0,0,0,NULL),(93198,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','____ यांनी शरीरातील रक्ताभिसरण कसे होते याचे वर्णन केले.\na) आइन्स्टाईन b) न्यूटन c) विल्यम हार्वे d) डार्विन\n','','','c) विल्यम हार्वे','',0,0,0,NULL),(93199,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मानवी हृदय थोडेसे ____ बाजूला कललेले असते.\na) उजव्या b) डाव्या c) मागच्या d) पुढच्या\n','','','b) डाव्या','',0,0,0,NULL),(93200,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','केशिका एकमेकींना जोडल्या जाऊन ____ तयार होतात.\na) धमनी b) शिरा c) हृदय d) नलिका\n','','','b) शिरा','',0,0,0,NULL),(93201,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्त ही ____ ऊती आहे.\na) अस्थि b) द्रव संयोगी c) मज्जा d) उपकला\n','','','b) द्रव संयोगी','',0,0,0,NULL),(93202,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पांढऱ्या पेशींची निर्मिती ____ होते.\na) अस्थिमज्जेत b) रक्तात c) फुफ्फुसात d) यकृतात\n','','','a) अस्थिमज्जेत','',0,0,0,NULL),(93203,2208,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','हृदयाच्या आकुंचनाच्या वेळेस जो दाब नोंदवला जातो त्यास ____ असे म्हणतात.\na) डायस्टॉलिक दाब b) सिस्टॉलिक दाब c) रक्तदाब d) मज्जादाब\n','','','b) सिस्टॉलिक दाब','',0,0,0,NULL),(93204,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सहसंबंध पूर्ण करा : श्वास घेणे : श्वासपटलाचे आकुंचन :: ____ : श्वासपटलाचे प्रसरण','','','श्वास सोडणे.\nश्वास घेताना श्वासपटलाचे आकुंचन होते, तर श्वास सोडताना ते प्रसरण पावते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93205,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सहसंबंध पूर्ण करा : हुट्याचा वरचा कप्पा : अलिंद :: हुद्याचा खालचा कप्पा : ____','','','निलय\nहृद्याच्या वरच्या कप्यांना अलिंद, तर खालील कप्यांना निलय असे म्हणतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93206,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सहसंबंध पूर्ण करा : केंद्रक नसलेल्या रक्तपेश़ : लोहित रक्तपेशी :: केंद्रकयुक्त रक्तपेशी : ____','','','पांढऱ्या रक्तपेशी (WBC)\nलोहित रक्तपेशी या केंद्रक नसलेल्या पेशी असतात, तर पांढऱ्या रक्तपेशी या केंद्रकयुक्त असतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93207,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सहसंबंध पूर्ण करा : ABO रक्तगट : .......... : : AB  रक्तगट : डिकास्टेलो आणि स्टर्ली','','','कार्ल लँडस्टेनर\nकार्ल लँडस्टेनर यांनी A,B आणि O या रक्तगटांचा शोध लावला, तर डिकास्टेलो आणि स्टर्ली यांनी AB रक्तगटाचा शोध लावला.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93208,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','नावे सांगा : शरीराच्या विविध भागांकडून हुदयाकडे रक्त वाहून नेणाऱ्या रक्तवाहिन्या.','','','शिरा','',0,0,0,NULL),(93209,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','नावे सांगा : पांढऱ्या पेशींचे WBC प्रकार','','','बेसोफील, इओसिनोफील, न्यूट्रोफील, मोनोसाईट्स, लिम्फोसाईट्स','',0,0,0,NULL),(93210,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','नावे सांगा : रक्तदान करणारा.','','','रक्तदाता','',0,0,0,NULL),(93211,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','नावे सांगा : रक्तदाब मोजण्याचे यंत्र','','','स्पिग्मोमॅनोमीटर','',0,0,0,NULL),(93212,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','नावे सांगा : हृदयाच्या प्रसरणाच्या वेळी नोंदविला जाणारा दाब','','','डायस्टॉलिक दाब','',0,0,0,NULL),(93213,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','नावे सांगा : माणसाच्या शरीरातील सामान्य रक्तदाबापेक्षा जास्त दाबाची स्थिती.','','','उच्च रक्तदाब','',0,0,0,NULL),(93214,2208,'descriptive','1b',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','जोड्या जुळवा :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>साधारण रक्तदाब</td>\n    <td>d. 120-139 मिमी</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>उच्च रक्तदाब अवस्था - 2</td>\n    <td>a. &gt;160 मिमी</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>b. 140-159 मिमी</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td></td>\n    <td></td>\n    <td>c. 90-119 मिमी</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','( i - c ), ( ii - a)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93215,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्ताभिसरण संस्थेची कार्ये लिहा.','','','शरीराच्या वेगवेगळ्या अवयवांमध्ये विद्राव्य अन्नघटक, संप्रेरके, ऑक्सिजन, कार्वन डायऑक्साइड, पाणी आणि इतर टाकाऊ पदार्थ अशा विविध पदार्थांचे वहन रक्ताभिसरण संस्थेद्वारे केले जाते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93216,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्ताभिसरणाची व्याख्या लिहा.','','','हृदयादूवारे शरीराच्या विविध अवयवांकडे रक्त पोहोचविण्याच्या आणि तेथून परत हृदयाकडे आणण्याच्या क्रियेस ‘रक्ताभिसरण’ असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93217,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','हृदयाच्या स्पदनाची व्याख्या स्पष्ट करा.','','','हृदयांच्या स्नायूंचे एका निश्चित तालात होणारे आकुंचन व शिथिलीकरण याला हृदयाचे स्पंदन म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93218,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्त पराधानाची व्याख्या लिहा.','','','शरीरातील रक्ताची कमतरता भरून काढण्यासाठी रक्त दात्याकडून रक्त ग्राहीला रक्त दिले जाते. या प्रक्रियेस ‘रक्त पराधान’ असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93219,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्तशास्त्र (हिमॅटॉलॉजी) म्हणजे काय ?','','','रक्त, रक्त तयार करणारे अवयव आणि रक्ताचे रोग यांचा अभ्यास करणारी वैस्यक विज्ञानाची शाखा म्हणजे रक्तशास्त्र (हिमॅटॉलॉजी) होय.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93220,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','फरक स्पष्ट करा : श्वास घेणे आणि श्वास सोडणे','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>श्वास घेणे</th>\n    <th>श्वास सोडणे</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>या क्रियेत नाकावाटे हवा आत घेतली जाते.</td>\n    <td>या क्रियेत नाकावाटे हवा बाहेर टाकली जाते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>श्वास घेताना श्वासपटल खाली जाते.</td>\n    <td>श्वास सोडताना श्वासपटल वर उचलले जाते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>श्वास घेताना फुफ्फुसांवरील दाब कमी होतो.</td>\n    <td>श्वास सोडताना फुफ्फुसांवरील दाब वाढतो.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>श्वास घेताना छातीच्या पोकळीचे आकारमान वाढते.</td>\n    <td>श्वास सोडताना छातीच्या पोकळीचे आकारमान कमी होते.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93221,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','जीवनप्रक्रियांची व्याख्या लिहा. कोणतीही दोन उदाहरणे द्या.','','','सजीवांतील लक्षणे प्रकर्षाने दर्शवणाया सर्व जीवनावश्यक क्रियांना जीवनप्रक्रिया असे म्हणतात. उदा. पोषण, श्वसन, पचन, उत्सर्जन, रक्ताभिसरण इत्यादी.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93222,2208,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','श्वसनप्रक्रियेबदूदल स्पष्टीकरण लिहा. ','','','श्वसनप्रक्रिया तीन टफ्यांत होते: बहि:श्वसन, अंतःश्वसन, पेशी श्वसन.\ni. बहि : श्वसन : श्वास घेणे आणि श्वास सोडणे या दोन्ही क्रियांना एकत्रितपणे बहि :श्वसन असे म्हणतात.\na. श्वास घेणे : या प्रक्रियेत नाकातून हवा आत घेतली जाते व श्वास नलिकेद्वारे ती दोन्ही फुफ्फुसांपर्यंत पोहोचवली जाते.\nb. उच्छ्वास / श्वास सोडणे : फुफ्फुसातील हवा उच्छवासावाटे बाहेर टाकणे यास श्वास सोडणे असे म्हणतात. या प्रक्रियेत, रक्तादूवारे शरीरातील CO₂ फुफ्फुसांपर्यंत पोहोचवला जातो व ती हवा उच्छ्वासावाटे बाहेर टाकली जाते.\nii. अंत :श्वसन : अंत ःश्वसन म्हणजे शरीरातील पेशी आणि रक्त यांदरम्यान होणारी वायूंची (O₂ आणि CO₂ ) देवाणघेवाण होय. या प्रक्रियेत ऑक्सिजन रक्तातून पेशींमध्ये जातो व कार्बन डायऑक्साइड पेशींतून रक्तामध्ये येतो.\niii. पेशीश्वसन : पेशींतील ग्लुकोजसारख्या विद्राव्य घटकांचे ऑक्सिजनमुळे मंढ़्चलन होऊन ऊर्जा ATP च्या स्वरूपात मोकळी होते. या प्रक्रियेत CO₂ आणि जलबाष्प हे निरुपयोगी पदार्थ तयार होतात.\nपेशीश्वसन प्रक्रिया खालील समीकरणाच्या साहाय्याने सारांश रूपात मांडतात.\nC₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ ⟶ 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + ऊर्जा (38 ATP)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93223,2208,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','मानवी श्वसनसंस्था सुबक व नामनिर्देशित आकृतीसह स्पष्ट करा.','','','मानवी श्वसनसंस्थेत नाक, घसा, श्वासनलिका, फुफ्फुसे, आणि श्वासपटल असते.','i. नाकः श्वसनसंस्थेची सुरुवात नाकापासून होते. नाकामध्ये असलेल्या केस व चिकट पदार्थ यांमुके हवा गाळून आत घेतली जाते.\nii. घसा : अन्ननलिका व श्वासनलिका या घशापासून सुरू होतात. श्वासनलिकेच्या वरच्या बाजूस एक झाकण असते. अन्ननलिकेतून अन्न जाताना या झाकणामुळे श्वासनलिका झाकली जाते व अन्नाचे कण श्वासनलिकेत शिरत नाहीत. इतर वेळी श्वासनलिका मोकळी असते. यामुके, हवा घशातून श्वासनलिकेत जाते.\niii. श्वासनलिका : स्वरयंत्रामुले श्वासनलिकेचा सुरुवानीचा भाग फुगलेला दिसतो. श्वासनलिका अन्ननलिकेच्या पुढे असते. श्वासनलिकेला छातीमध्ये दोन फाटे फुटतात. एक फाटा उजव्या फुफ्फुसाकडे, तर दुसरा डाव्या फुफ्फुसाकडे जातो.\niv. फुफ्फुसे : छातीच्या पोककीत हृदयाच्या डाव्या व उजव्या बाजूस एकेक फुफ्फुस असते. फुफ्फुसे छातीच्या पोककीचा बराचसा भाग व्यापतात. प्रत्येक फुफ्फुसावर दुपढ़री आवरण असते ज्याला फुफ्फुसावरण असे म्हणतात. फुफुरे ही स्पंजाप्रमाणे स्थितिस्थापक असतात. ती अनेक लहान लहान कण्यांनी वनलेली असतात, ज्यांना वायुकोश म्हणतात. या वायुकोशांच्या भोवती केशवाहिन्यांचे अत्यंत दाट जाळे असते. वायुकोश व केशवाहिन्यांवरील आवरण फार पातक असत्याने या पातक आवरणातून वायूंची देवाणघेवाण सहजपणे होते. फुफ्फुसात असंख्य वायुकोश असतात. त्यामुके, वायूंच्या देवाणघेवाणीसाठी विस्तृत पृष्ठभाग उपलब्ध होतो.\nv. श्वासपटल : छातीच्या पिंजयाच्या तकाशी जो स्नायूचा पडदा असतो त्याला श्वासपटल म्हणतात. हे उद्रपोककी व छातीची पोककी (डरोपोककी) यांच्या दरम्यान असते. बरगङ्या किंचित वर उचलल्या जातात व श्वासपटल खाती जाते. यामुके, फुफ्फुसांवरील दाब कमी होऊन बाहेरील हवा नाकावाटे फुफ्फुसांत जाते. बरगङ्या मूक जागी परत येतात व श्वासपटल वर उचलते जाते. यामुले, फुफ्फुसांवरील दाब वाढतो व फुफ्फुसांतील हवा नाकावाटे बाहेर फेकली जाते. श्वासोच्छासाची क्रिया घड्ण्यासाठी श्वासपटल सतत वर आणि खाती होण्याची हालचाल आवश्यक असते.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(93224,2208,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','मानवी हृद्याचे स्थान व रचना स्पष्ट करा.','','','हृदय :\ni. हे छातीच्या पिंजयात जवळजवळ मध्यभागी, बरगङ्यांच्या मागे, दोन्ही फुफ्फुसांच्या मध्ये आणि थोडेसे डाव्या बाजूला कललेले असते.\nii. मानवी हृदयाचा आकार आपल्या मुठीएवढा असून वजन साधारणपणे 360 ग्रॅम असते.\niii. हदयाभोवती दुपदरी हृदयावरण असते. हृदयावरणाच्या दोन थरांमध्ये असलेल्या द्रवपदार्थामुके घर्षण आणि धक्क्यांपासून हृदयाचे संरक्षण होते.\niv. मानवी हृदय हा एक स्नायूमय अवयव असून ते अनैच्छिक हृदस्नायूंपासून बनलेले असते. या स्नायूंचे आकुंचन व शिथिलीकरण एका ठरावीक तालात होत असते. याला हृदयाचे स्पंदन असे म्हणतात.\nv. हृदयाच्या आतील उभ्या पडद्यामुके त्याचे डावे व उजवे असे दोन भाग पडतात. या दोन भागांचे आणखी़ दोन-दोन कपे पडतात. वरच्या कण्यांना अलिंद, तर खालच्या कण्यांना निलय असे म्हणतात. अशाप्रकारे, मानवी हृदयास चार कप्पे असतात.\nvi. उजवे अलिंद् आणि उजवे निलय यांच्यामध्ये त्रिदली झडप असते. डावे अलिंद आणि डावे निलय यांच्यामध्ये द्विदली झडप असते. या झडपांमुले निलय ते संबंधित अलिंदपर्यंत रक्त मागे वाहण्यास प्रतिबंध होतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93225,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','केशवाहिन्या (केशिका) याबद्दल थोडक्यात माहिती लिहा.','','','i. धमन्या शरीरभर पसरताना त्यांना फाटे फुटून त्यांचा व्यास लहान लहान होत त्या केसासारख्या दि़्रू लागतात. त्यांना केशिका किंवा केशवाहिन्या असे म्हणतात.\nii. केशवाहिन्यांच्या भित्तिका अतिशय बारीक असतात. त्या एकसरी व पातक असल्यामुके केशवाहिन्या आणि पेशी यांच्या दरम्यान होणारी ऑक्सिजन, अन्नघटक, संप्रेरके व जीवनसत्त्वे इत्यादी पदार्थांची देवाणघेवाण सोपी होते.\niii. हे पदार्थ पेशींत मिसकतात आणि पेशींतील टाकाऊ पदार्थ रक्तात येतात.\niv. केशिका एकमेकींना जोडल्या जाऊन शिरा तयार होतात. केशवाहिन्यांचे जाळे प्रत्येक अवयवांमध्ये पसरलेले असते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93226,2208,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','मानवी शरीरातील रक्ताभिसरण क्रिया सविस्तर स्पष्ट करा.','','','आपल्या शरीरात रक्ताभिसरण खालील पद्धतीने घडून येते :','i. शरीराच्या सर्व भागांकडून विनाक्षजती रक्त उजव्या अलिंदामध्ये येते.\nii. उजब्या अलिंदाकडून विनाक्षजती रक्त उजव्या निलयामध्ये येते. त्रिदती झडप रक्ताचा प्रवाह मागे वाहण्यास प्रतिबंध करते.\niii. उजव्या निलयाकडून विनाक्षजती रक्त मग फुफ्फुस धमनीमध्ये येते. ही फुफुसधमनी रक्त ऑक्सिजनयुक्त करण्यासाठी ते फुफ्फुसांकडे वाहून नेते.\niv. फुफ्फुस शिरांच्या दोन जोङ्यांद्वारे फुफ्फुसांकडून डाव्या अलिंदामध्ये ऑक्सिजनयुक्त रक्त वाहत येते.\nv. डाव्या अलिंदाकडून ऑक्सिजनयुक्ति रक्ति डाव्या निलयाकडे येते. दूविदली झडप रक्ताचा प्रवाह मागे वाहण्यास प्रतिबंध करते.\nvi. डाव्या निलयाकडून ऑक्सिजनयुक्त रक्त महारोहिणीमध्ये येते, जी शरीराच्या सर्व भागांकडे रक्त वाहून नेते. अर्धचंद्राकृती झडपा रक्ताचा प्रवाह मागे वाहण्यास प्रतिबंध करतात.\nvii. या सर्व क्रिया हृद्याच्या एका स्पंदनात घडून येतात.\n',0,0,1,NULL),(93227,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','टीप लिहा : मानवी रक्तगट','','','i. रक्तगट हे आपल्या शरीरात माता व पित्याकडून येणाय्या जनुकांवर अवलंबून असतात. म्हणजेच, ते आनुवंशिक असतात.\nii. रक्तामधील प्रतिजन आणि प्रतिपिंडे यांच्या उपस्थिती किंवा अनुपस्थितीवर आधारित मानवी रक्ताचे चार प्रमुख गट पडतात, ते म्हणजे, A,B,AB आणि O होय.\niii. तसेच, रक्तपेशीतील \'आर एच\' या प्रतिजनामुळे प्रत्येक रक्तगटाचे \'आर एच\' (हीसस) पॉझिटिक्ह आणि \'आर एच\' निगेटिद्ह असे प्रकार पडतात. उदा. \'A\' पॉझ़िटिक्ह व \'A\' निगेटिक्ह.\niv. \' O \' रक्तगट असलेल्या व्यक्तीचे रक्त कुठल्यांही रक्तगटास चालते (A,B,AB,O) त्यामुले या रक्तगटास सर्वयोग्य दाता असे म्हटले जाते. तर \' AB \' रक्तगट असलेल्या व्यक्ताला कुठल्याही रक्तगटाचे रक्ता चालते (A, B, AB, O) म्हणून या रक्तगटास सर्वयोग्य ग्राही असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93228,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्तदान करण्यापूर्वी व्यक्तीस कोणत्या गोष्टी माहीत असणे गरजेचे असते ?','','','i. आपल्या शरीरात नवीन रक्त तयार होण्याची प्रक्रिया सतत सुरू असते.\nii. रक्तदानाच्या वेळेस व्यक्तीच्या शरीरातून 350 ते 450 मिली इतके रक्त घेतले जाते, जे 24 तासांत शरीराद्वारे भरून काढले जाते.\niii. गरोदर व स्तनपान करणाया स्त्रिया रक्तदान करू शकत नाहीत.\niv. रक्तदान करताना किंवा केल्यानंतर शरीरास कोणताही त्रास होत नाही.\nv. 18 वर्षांवरील निरोगी व्यक्ती वर्षातून 3 ते 4 वेळा रक्तदान करू शकते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93229,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्तदाब म्हणजे काय ? सिस्टॉलिक व डायस्टॉलिक दाब या संज्ञा स्पष्ट करा.','','','i. रक्तप्रवाहामुळे धमन्यांच्या भिंतीवर जो ढाब पडतो त्याला ‘रक्तदाब’ असे म्हणतात.\nii. सिस्टॉलिक दाब: हृदयाच्या आकुंचनाच्या वेकी नोंदवला जाणारा दाब म्हणजे सिस्टॉलिक (आकुंचक) दाब होय. साधारण सिस्टॉलिक दाब 90-119 मिमी इतका असतो.\niii. डायस्टॉलिक दाब: हृदयाच्या प्रसरणाच्या वेकी नोंदवला जाणारा दांब म्हणजे डायस्टॉलिक दाब होय. साधारण डायस्टॉलिक दाबं हा 60 -79 मिमी इतका असतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93230,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','टीप लिहा : उच्च रक्तदाब','','','i. साधारण रक्तदाबापेक्षा अधिक दाब म्हणजे उच्च रक्तदाब होय.\nii. उच्च रक्तदाबाचा त्रास असणाया व्यक्तींच्या धमन्यांमध्ये अधिक दाब निर्माण होतो.\niii. या वाढलेल्या रक्तदाबामुके हृदय आणि रक्तवाहिन्यांवर ताण येतो.\niv. उच्च रक्ता़ाबात सिस्टॉलिक आणि डायस्टॉलिक दाब फार वाढलेला असतो आणि उच्च रक्तिदाबाच्या विविध अवस्थांमध्ये हे दाब बढ़तत असतात.\nv. साधारण सिस्टॉलिक रक्तदाब हा 90-119 मिमी इतका,तर डायस्टॉलिक दाब हा 60-79 मिमी इतका असतो.\nvi. पूर्व उच्च रक्तदाबाच्या स्थितीत सिस्टॉलिक दाब 120 -139 मिमी इतका वाढतो. तर डायस्टॉलिक दाब हा 80- 89 मिमी इतका वाढतो. उच्च रक्तदाबाच्या पहिल्या अवस्थेत सिस्टॉलिक आणि डायस्टॉलिक रक्ता़ाब हे अनुक्रमे 140-159 मिमी आणि 90-99 मिमी असतात. उच्च रक्तदाबाच्या दुस्या अवस्थेत सिस्टॉलिक रक्तदाब >160 मिमी, तर डायस्टॉलिक दाब हा > 100 मिमी इतका असतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93231,2208,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','शास्त्रीय कारणे लिहा : \'AB\' रक्तगट असलेल्या व्यक्तिस \'सर्वयोग्य ग्राही\' असे म्हटले जाते.','','','i. ज्याला रक्त दि़े जाते त्याला ग्राही असे म्हणतात.\nii. \' AB \' रक्तगटाच्या व्यक्तीस कुठल्याही रक्तिगटाचे (O, A, B आणि A B) रक्त चालते.\nम्हणून, ‘ AB ’ रक्तगट असलेल्या व्यक्तिस ‘सर्वयोग्य ग्राही’ असे म्हटले जाते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93232,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','सारणी / तक्ता पूर्ण करा : ','','','A : रक्तद्रव\nB : प्रथिने\nC : असेंद्रिय क्षार\nD : (RBC) लाल रक्तपेशी (लोहित रक्तपेशी)\nE : (WBC) पांढया पेशी (श्वेत रक्तकणिका)\nF : रक्तपट्टिका\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(93233,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','वायुकोश रचनेची सुबक व नामनिर्देशित आकृती काढा.','','','','',0,0,1,NULL),(93234,2208,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील आकृतीचे निरीक्षण करून संक्षिप्त वर्णन करा. ','','','i. दिलेली आकृती ही धमन्या व निला यांची रचना दाखवते.\nii. धमन्या ऑक्सिजनयुक्त रक्त वाहून नेतात, तर निलांमधून विनाक्षजती रक्त वाहून नेले जाते.\niii. धमन्यांना जे फाटे फुटतात त्यांचा व्यास लहान होत जातो व त्या केरसासारख्या दिसू लागतात. त्यांना केशिका म्हणतात. या केशिका एकत्र जोडल्या जाऊन जास्त व्यासाच्या वाहिन्या तयार होतात ज्यांना आपण शिरा असे म्हणतो.\niv. रोहिणी अंतस्तर आवरण हा धमन्या व निला यांच्या अत्यंत आत असलेला थर असतो.\nv. धमन्यांना झडपा नसतात, तर शिरांना झडपा असतात.\n','',1,0,0,NULL),(93235,2208,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','दिलेल्या आकृतीचे निरीक्षण करा आणि विचारलेल्या प्रश्नांची उत्तरे लिहा.','दिलेल्या आकृतीतील \'क्ष\' ओळखा.','','दिलेल्या आकृतीतील \'क्ष\' म्हणजे केशवाहिन्या आहेत','',1,0,0,NULL),(93236,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मानवी शरीरातील कोणता भाग छातीच्या पोकळीस उदरपोकळीपासून वेगळे करतो ?','','','शरीरातील श्वासपटल छातीच्या पोकळीस उदरपोकळीपासून वेगळे करतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93237,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मानवी हृदयाचे किती कणे असतात ?','','','मानवी हुदयास चार कपे असतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93238,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','पांढऱ्या रक्तपेशींचे पाच प्रकार सांगा.','','','बेसोफील, इओसिनोफील, न्यूट्रोफील, मोनोसाईट्स व लिम्फोसाईट्स हे पांढन्या रक्तपेशींचे पाच प्रकार आहेत.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93239,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रक्तदाब मोजण्याच्या यंत्राचे नाव सांगा.','','','स्पिग्मोमॅनोमीटर हे रक्तदाब मोजण्याच्या यंत्राचे नाव आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93240,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','ग्लुकोजच्या एका रेणूच्या मंदज्वलनातून किती ATP रेणू मुक्त होतात?','','','ग्लुकोजच्या एका रेणूच्या मंदज्वलनातून 38 ATP रेणू मुक्त होतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93241,2208,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','फुफ्फुसातील कोणत्या अवयवामुळे वायूंच्या देवघेवीसाटी विस्तृत पृष्ठभाग उपलब्ध होतो ?','','','फुफ्फुसातील असंख्य वायुकोशांमुके वायूंच्या देवघेवीसाठी विस्तृत पृष्ठभाग उपलब्ध होतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93242,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','खाली दिलेली द्रावणे आम्ल की आम्लारी ते ओळखा.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>द्रावण</th>\n    <th colspan=\"3\">दर्शकात झालेला बदल</th>\n    <th>आम्ल / आम्लारी</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th></th>\n    <th>लिटमस</th>\n    <th>फिनॉल्फ्यॅलिन</th>\n    <th>मिथिल ऑरेंज</th>\n    <th></th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1.</td>\n    <td>............... </td>\n    <td>बदल नाही</td>\n    <td>................</td>\n    <td>(i)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2.</td>\n    <td>............... </td>\n    <td>..............</td>\n    <td>नारंगी रंग बदलून लाल झाला.</td>\n    <td>(ii)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3.</td>\n    <td>लाल लिटमस निळा झाला.</td>\n    <td>.............</td>\n    <td>..................</td>\n    <td>(iii)</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','i. आम्ल 	ii. आम्ल		iii. आम्लारी','',0,0,0,NULL),(93243,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','सूत्रांवरून रासायनिक नावे लिहा :\nH₂SO₄, Ca(OH)₂, HCl, NaOH, KOH, NH₄OH','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>सूत्र</th>\n    <th>रासायनिक नाव</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>H<sub>2</sub> SO<sub>4</sub></td>\n    <td>सल्फ्युरिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub></td>\n    <td>केल्शिकम हायड्रॉक्साइड</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>HCl</td>\n    <td>हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>NaOH</td>\n    <td>सोडिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>KOH</td>\n    <td>पोटॉशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>NH<sub>4</sub> OH</td>\n    <td>अमोनिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93244,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','क्लोराइड क्षार मिळवण्यास कोणते आम्ल वापरले पाहिजे ?','','','क्लोराइड क्षार मिळवण्यास हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्ल (HCl) वापरले पाहिजे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93245,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','एका खडकाच्या नमुन्यावर लिंबाचा रस पिळताच तो फसफसतो आणि त्यात निर्माण होणाऱ्या वायूने चुन्याची निवळी पांढरी बनते. खडकात कोणत्या प्रकारचे संयुग आहे ?','','','खडकात असणारे संयुग हे धातूचे कार्बोनेट आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93246,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','प्रयोगशाळेतील एका अभिक्रियाकारकाच्या बाटलीवरची चिठ्टी खराब झाली. त्या बाटलीतील द्रव्य आम्ल आहे, की नाही हे तुम्ही कसे शोधून काढाल ?','','','i. लिटमस कागदासारखे दर्शक वापरून आपण तो पदार्थ आम्लधर्मी आहे, की नाही हे शोधून काढू शकतो.\nii. जर या रसायनामुले निका लिटमस तांबडा झाला, तर ते आम्लधर्मी आहे आणि जर तांबडा लिटमस निका झाला, तर ते आम्लारिधर्मी आहे हे समजते. म्हणजेच, दर्शकाच्या रंगातील बदलावरून आपण ते रसायन आम्लधर्मी आहे, की नाही हे ओळखू शकतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93247,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','आम्ल व आम्लारीतील फरक स्पष्ट करा.','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>आम्ल</th>\n    <th>आम्लारी</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>आम्लाची चव आंबट असते.</td>\n    <td>आम्लारीची चव कडवट असते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>आम्लामध्ये हायड्रोजन (H^+ )आयन हा मुख्य घटक असतो.</td>\n    <td>आम्लारीमध्ये हायड्रॉक्साइड (OH^- ) आयन हा मुख्य घटक असतो.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>आम्लामुळे निळा लिटमस कागट तांबडा होतो.</td>\n    <td>आम्लारीमुळे तांबडा लिटमस कागद निका होतो.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>सामान्यतः अधातूंचे ऑक्साइड आम्लधर्मी असतात.<br>उदा. HCl,H₂SO₄, इत्यादी.</td>\n    <td>सामान्यतः धातूंचे ऑक्साइड आम्लारीधर्मी असतात.<br>उदा. NaOH,Mg(OH)₂, इत्यादी.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93248,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','दर्शकावर मिटाचा परिणाम का होत नाही ? ','','','i. आम्ल किंवा आम्लारीच्या संपर्काने दर्शक त्यांचे रंग बढ़लतात.\nii. आम्ल (हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्ल) आणि आम्लारी (सोडिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड) यांच्या संयोगातून मीठ (साधे मीठ किंवा सोडिअम क्लोराइड) तयार होते. या अभिक्रियेला उदासिनीकरण अभिक्रिया असे म्हणतात.\niii. परिणामी, मीठ उदासीन असते. म्हणजे, ते आम्लथर्मी किंवा आम्लारीधर्मी नसते.\nम्हणून, दर्शकावर मिठाचा परिणाम होत नाही.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93249,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','उदासिनीकरणातून कोणते पदार्थ तयार होतात ?','','','उदासिनीकरण अभिक्रियेमध्ये आम्ल आणि आम्लारी यांच्या संयोगाने क्षार आणि पाणी तयार होते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93250,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','आम्लातील प्रमुख घटक ____ आहे.\na) हायड्रोजन आयन (H⁺) b) हायड्रॉक्साइड आयन (OH⁻) c) सोडियम आयन (Na⁺) d) क्लोराइड आयन (Cl⁻)\n','','','a) हायड्रोजन आयन (H⁺)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93251,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','आम्लारीतील प्रमुख घटक ____ आहे.\na) हायड्रोजन आयन (H⁺) b) हायड्रॉक्साइड आयन (OH⁻) c) अमोनियम आयन d) कार्बोनेट आयन\n','','','b) हायड्रॉक्साइड आयन (OH⁻)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93252,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','टार्टारिक हे ____ आम्ल आहे.\na) कृत्रिम b) खनिज c) नैसर्गिक (घरगुती) d) औद्योगिक\n','','','c) नैसर्गिक (घरगुती)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93253,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','जोड्या लावा : \n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>\'अ\' गट</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>चिंच</td>\n    <td>a. असेटिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>दही</td>\n    <td>b. सायट्रिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>लिंबू</td>\n    <td>c. टार्टारिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>दिनेगार</td>\n    <td>d. लॉक्टिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i - c), (ii - d), (iii - b), (iv - a)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93254,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Text Book','','आपण रोजच्या आहारात अनेक खाद्यपदार्थांचा उपयोग करतो, जसे लिंबू, चिंच, टोमॅटो, साखर, व्हिनेगर, मीट इत्यादी, सगक्या पदार्थांची चव एकसारखी असते का ?','','','नाही, सगक्र्या पदार्थांची चव एकसारखी नसते.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93255,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Text Book','','लिंबू, साखर, दही, चुन्याची निवकी, खाण्याचा सोडा, आवळा, चिंच, कैरी, डाकिंब, पाणी या पदार्थांची चव कशी आहे ते लिहा. (आंबट, तुरट, गोड, कडवट, चवहीन)','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>पदार्थ</th>\n    <th>चव</th>\n    <th>पदार्थ</th>\n    <th>चव</th>\n  </tr>\n\n  <tr>\n    <td>लिंबू</td>\n    <td>आंबट</td>\n    <td>आवळा</td>\n    <td>आंबट</td>\n  </tr>\n\n  <tr>\n    <td>Sाखर</td>\n    <td>गोड</td>\n    <td>चिंच</td>\n    <td>आंवट</td>\n  </tr>\n\n  <tr>\n    <td>दही</td>\n    <td>आंवट</td>\n    <td>कैरी</td>\n    <td>आंबट</td>\n  </tr>\n\n  <tr>\n    <td>चुन्याची निवळी</td>\n    <td>आंबट</td>\n    <td>डाळिंब</td>\n    <td>गोड</td>\n  </tr>\n\n  <tr>\n    <td>खाण्याचा सोडा</td>\n    <td>तुरट / कडवट</td>\n    <td>पाणी</td>\n    <td>चवहीन</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93256,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','घरातील शहावाद फरशीवर, ओट्यावर लिंवाचा रस, चिंचेचे पाणी यांसारखे आंबट पदार्थ पडले, तर काय घडते? का?','','','i. घरातील शहाबाद फरशी किंवा ओटा यामध्ये कॅल्शिअम कार्वोनेट असते.\nii. लिंबाचा रस, चिंचेचे पाणी यांसारख्या पदार्थांमध्ये असणाय्या आम्लाबरोबर कॅल्शिअम कार्बोनेटची अभिक्रिया होते\niii. या अभिक्रियेत कार्वन डायऑक्साइड वायू तयार होऊन पांढन्या रंगाचा थर जमतो.\niv. परिणामी, शहावाढ़ी फरशी किंवा ओट्यावर पांढन्या रंगाचा जमलेला थर पाण्याने स्वच्छ करता येतो.\nम्हणूनच, रासायनिक अभिक्रिया झाल्याने घरातील शहाबाद फरशी किंवा ओटा यांवर पांढन्या रंगाचा थर दिसतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93257,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','हिरवट डाग पडलेली तांब्याची भांडी व काळसर पडलेली चांदीची भांडी चकचकीत करण्यासाठी काय वापरतात ?','','','धातूची पाणी, हवा किंवा इतर वायूंबरोबर अभिक्रिया झाल्यामुळे तांब्याच्या आणि चांदीच्या भांड्यांवर डाग पडतात. हे डाग काढण्यासाठी आणि ही भांडी पुन्हा चकचकीत करण्यासाठी आम्लांची मदत होते. म्हणूनच. क्हिनेगार, लिंबाचा रस इत्यादी आम्ल असणाया पदार्थांचा हिरवट डाग पडलेली तांब्याची भांडी व काळसर पडलेली चांदीची भांडी चकचकीत करणयासाठी वापर केला जातो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93258,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','दात घासताना टूथपेस्ट का वापरतात ?','','','i. आपल्या तोंडामध्ये अनेक जीवाणू असतात. जेवल्यानंतर तोंडामध्ये जे छोटे - छोटे अन्नाचे कण राहतात त्यांचे हे जीवाणू विघटन करतात.\nii. या प्रक्रियेमध्ये आम्ले तयार होतात. यामुके, दातांच्या आवरणाला हानी पोहोचून दात किड्रू शकतात.\niii. हे रोखण्यासाठी आम्लारी पदार्थ असणाया टूथपेस्टचा वापर केला जातो.\niv. टूथपेस्टमध्ये असणारे आम्लारी दातांमधील अतिरिक्त आम्लाचे उदासिनीकरण करतात.\nम्हणूनच, दात घासताना टूथपेस्ट वापरतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93259,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','लिंबू, कैरी यांसारखे आंबट पदार्थ लोखंडी सुरीने कापले असता सुरीचे पाते उजळ बनते. का ?','','','i. लोखंडी सुरीच्या पृष्ठभागावर आयर्न ऑक्साइड किंवा गंज तयार झाल्यामुके या सुरीची चकाकी कमी होते.\nii. लिंबू, कैरी यांसारख्या आंबट फळांमध्ये सेंद्रिय आम्ले असतात.\niii. ही आम्ले आयर्न ऑक्साइडबरोवर अभिक्रिया करतात आणि सुरीच्या पृष्ठभागावस्न आयर्न ऑक्साइड निघून जाते.\nम्हणूनच, लिंबू, कैरी यांसारखे आंबट पढार्थ लोखंडी सुरीने कापले असता सुरीचे पाते उजळ बनते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93260,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','बहुतांश आम्लधर्मी पदार्थ चवीला ____ असतात.\na) गोड b) कडू c) आंबट d) खारट\n','','','c) आंबट','',0,0,0,NULL),(93261,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मिथिल रेड (Methyl Red) या दर्शकाचा आम्लारीतील रंग ____ असतो.\na) निळा b) पिवळा c) लाल d) हिरवा\n','','','b) पिवळा','',0,0,0,NULL),(93262,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','लाल कोबी आणि जास्वंद हे ____ दर्शक आहेत.\na) कृत्रिम b) रासायनिक c) नैसर्गिक (घरगुती) d) प्रयोगशाळेतील\n','','','c) नैसर्गिक (घरगुती)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93263,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्लाची कार्बोनेटशी अभिक्रिया होऊन ____ वायू मुक्त होतो.\na) O₂ b) CO₂ c) H₂ d) N₂\n','','','b) CO₂','',0,0,0,NULL),(93264,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्लारीची चव ____ असते.\na) कडवट b) आंबट c) गोड d) तुरट\n','','','a) कडवट','',0,0,0,NULL),(93265,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्ल आणि आम्लारींच्या संयोगाने क्षार व ____ निर्माण होतात.\na) हायड्रोजन b) कार्बन c) पाणी d) ऑक्सिजन\n','','','c) पाणी','',0,0,0,NULL),(93266,2210,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपल्या जठरातील ____ हे आम्ल अन्नपचन सुलभ करते.\na) सल्फ्युरिक आम्ल b) नायट्रिक आम्ल c) हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्ल d) कार्बोनिक आम्ल\n','','','c) हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्ल','',0,0,0,NULL),(93267,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ओळखा व चुकीची विधाने दुरुस्त करा :  संहत आम्लामध्ये हळूहळू पाणी मिसळून त्याचे विरळ आम्लात रूपांतर केले जाते.','','','चूक.\nसंहत आम्ले हळू हळू पाण्यामध्ये मिसळून विरल आम्ले तयार केली जातात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93268,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ओळखा व चुकीची विधाने दुरुस्त करा : NaHCO₃ हे खाण्याच्या सोङ्याचे रासायनिक सूत्र आहे.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(93269,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ओळखा व चुकीची विधाने दुरुस्त करा : संहत आम्ले आणि आम्लारी यांमुळे त्वचा भाजते.','','','बरोबर','',0,0,0,NULL),(93270,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ओळखा व चुकीची विधाने दुरुस्त करा : आम्लारी द्रावणामध्ये मिथिल ऑरेंज या दर्शकाचा रंग हिरवा होतो.','','','चूक.\nआम्लारी द्रावणामध्ये मिथिल ऑरेंज या दर्शकाचा रंग पिवळा होतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93271,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ओळखा व चुकीची विधाने दुरुस्त करा : शीतपेयांमध्ये असेटिक आम्ल असते.','','','चूक.\nशीतपेयांमध्ये कार्वोनिक आम्ल असते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93272,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','चूक की बरोबर ओळखा व चुकीची विधाने दुरुस्त करा : रासायनिक खतांच्या अतिवापरामुळे शेतजमिनीतील आम्लाचे प्रमाण कमी होते.','','','चूक.\nरासायनिक खतांच्या अतिवापरामुळे शेतजमिनीतील आम्लाचे प्रमाण वाढते.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93273,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सामान्य नाव आणि रासायनिक सूत्र यांच्या जोड्या जुळवा :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>सामान्य नाव</th>\n    <th>\'ब\' गट</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>चुन्याची निवळी</td>\n    <td>a. CaCO<sub>3</sub><br>b. Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub><br>c. KOH</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>पोटॅश</td>\n    <td>a. CaCO<sub>3</sub><br>b. Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub><br>c. KOH</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','(i - b), (ii - c)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93274,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','नैसर्गिक किंवा सेंद्रिय आम्ले म्हणजे काय ?','','','खाद्यपदार्थांमध्ये असणाऱ्या आम्लांना नैसर्गिक किंवा सेंद्रिय आम्ले म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93275,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','खनिज आम्लाची दोन उदाहरणे द्या.','','','i. हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्ल (HCl)		ii. सल्फ्युरिक आम्ल (H₂SO₄)','',0,0,0,NULL),(93276,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','कॉस्टिक सोडयाचे रासायनिक सूत्र सांगा.','','','NaOH हे कॉस्टिक सोड्याचे रासायनिक सूत्र आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93277,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','दर्शकाची व्याख्या लिहा.','','','जे पदार्थ आम्ल किंवा आम्लारी यांच्या संपर्काने स्वत :चा रंग बदलतात त्यांना दर्शक असे म्हणतात.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93278,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','लिटमस कागदावर होणाऱ्या परिणामाच्या आधारे आम्ल आणि आम्लारींमध्ये काय फरक असतो ?','','','आम्लामुळे निळा लिटमस कागद तांबडा होतो, तर आम्लारींमुळे तांबडा लिटमस कागद निळा होतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93279,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','टीप लिहा : तीव्र खनिज आम्ले','','','i. खनिज आम्ले दाहक व तीव्र असतात. त्यांची संहत द्रावणे त्वचेवर पडल्यास त्वचा भाजते.\nii. त्यांचा धूर श्वसनाद्वारे शरीरात गेल्यास हानिकारक ठरतो.\niii. तीव्र संहत आम्ले हकूहलू पाण्यात टाकतात आणि हळूहकू सतत ढवकत त्यांची विरल आम्ले तयार केली जातात.\niv. संहत आम्लांच्या तुलनेने विरत आम्ले कमी हानिकारक असतात.\nउदा. सल्फ्युरिक आम्ल (H₂SO₄), हायड्रोक्तोरिक आम्ल (HCl), नायट्रिक आम्ल (HNO₃), इत्यादी\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93280,2210,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','प्रयोगशाळेत वापरल्या जाणाऱ्या दर्शकांविषयी स्वतःच्या शब्दांत स्पष्टीकरण द्या.','','','i. प्रयोगशाळेत आम्ल व आम्लारी पदार्थांचे परीक्षण करण्यासाठी मुख्यत्वे लिटमस कागदाचा वापर केला जातो.\nii. आम्लामुळे निका लिटमस कागद तांबडा होतो, तर आम्लारीमध्ये तांबडा लिटमस कागद निळा होतो.\niii. त्याचप्रमाणे मिथिल ऑरेंज, मिथिल रेड, फिनॉल्फ्थैलिन हे दर्शक प्रयोगशाळेत द्रावणस्वरुपात वापरतले जातात.\niv. मिथिल ऑरेंज आम्लामध्ये गुलाबी, तर आम्लारीमध्ये पिवळा होतो. मिथिल रेड आम्लामध्ये तांबडाच राहतो, तर आम्लारीमध्ये पिवळा होतो.\nv. फिनॉल्फ्र्थिलिन आम्लामध्ये रंगहीन राहते, तर आम्लारीमध्ये गुलाबी होते.\nvi. द्रावणरूपात असणारे वैश्विक दर्शक आम्ल व आम्लारीच्या संपर्कात आल्यावर रंग बदलतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93281,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','टीप लिहा : नैसर्गिक किंवा घरगुती दर्शक','','','i. प्रयोगशाळेतील दर्शक उपलब्ध नसल्यास अनेक वेळा घरगुती किंवा नैसर्गिक दर्शकांचा वापर केला जातो.\nii. हे दर्शक जास्वंद, गुलाब, हळद, लाल कोबी, लाल टोमॅटो इत्यादी नैसर्गिक पदार्थांपासून बनवले जातात.\nउदा. हळदीचा पिवका डाग साबणाने धुतल्यास लाल होतो. याचे कारण हळद सावणातील आम्लारीधर्मी पदार्थाबरोबर अभिक्रिया करते. यासाठी हळदीचा दर्शक म्हणून उपयोग केला जातो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93282,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्ल म्हणजे काय ? एक उदाहरण द्या.','','','आम्ल हा असा एक पदार्थ असतो, की ज्याचे पाण्यातील द्रावण हायड्रोजन (H⁺)आयन निर्माण करते.\nउदा. पाण्यातील द्रावणात हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्लाचे विघटन होते व H⁺ आयन आणि Cl⁻ आयन तयार होतात. ही अभिक्रिया पुढीलप्रमाणे :\nHCl(aq) ⟶ H⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)\n5.	उदाहरणे देऊन नैसर्गिक किंवा सेंद्रिय आम्ले स्पष्ट \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93283,2210,'descriptive','4',4,2,'Out of Text Book','','उदाहरणे देऊन नैसर्गिक किंवा सेंद्रिय आम्ले स्पष्ट करा.','','','i. खाद्यपदार्थांमध्ये असणाया आम्लांना नैसर्गिक किंवा सेंद्रिय आम्ले असे म्हणतात.\nii. ही आम्ले सौम्य प्रकृतीची असतात आणि त्यामुके ती अपायकारक नसतात.\niii. ही आम्ले पाण्यात विद्राव्य असतात व त्यांची चव आंबट असते.\niv. नैंसर्गिक आम्ले असणारे पदार्थ पुढीलप्रमाणे :\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>पदार्थ (स्रोत)</th>\n    <th>नैसर्गिक (किंवा सेंद्रिय) आम्ले</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>व्हिनेगर</td>\n    <td>असेटिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>संत्रे</td>\n    <td>ऊस्कॉर्बिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>चिंच</td>\n    <td>टार्टारिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>टोमॅटो</td>\n    <td>ऑक्स्लिलिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>दही</td>\n    <td>लॉक्टिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>लिंबू</td>\n    <td>सायट्रिक आम्ल</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93284,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्लाचे गुणधर्म लिहा.','','','i. आम्लाची चव आंबट असते.\nii. आम्लाचे पाण्यातील द्रावण हायड्रोजन (H⁺)आयन निर्माण करते.\niii. आम्लाची धातूशी अभिक्रिया होऊन हायड्रोजन (H₂) वायू मुक्त होतो.\niv. आम्लाची कार्बोनेटशी अभिक्रिया होऊन कार्बन डायऑक्साइड (CO₂) वायू मुक्त होतो.\nv. आम्लामुके निका लिटमस कागद तांबडा होतो .\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93285,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','आपल्या शरीरात असणाऱ्या सेंद्रिय आम्लांची उदाहरणे द्या.','','','आपल्या शरीरात असणारी काही सेंद्रिय आम्ले पुढीलप्रमाणे :\ni. आपल्या शरीरातील DNA (डिऑक्सिरायवो न्यूक्लिइक आम्ल) आनुवंशिक गुण ठरवते.\nii. प्रोटीन अमिनो ऑसिडपासून बनलेली असतात.\niii. शरीरातील मेद मेदाम्लापासून बनलेला असतो.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93286,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्लारी म्हणजे काय ? उदाहरण द्या.','','','आम्लारी हा एक असा पदार्थ असतो ज्याचे पाण्यातील द्रावण हायड्रॉक्साइड OH⁻ आयन उपलब्ध करून देते.\nउदा. NaOH चे पाण्यातील द्रावण Na⁺ आयन आणि OH⁻ आयन निर्माण करते. ही अभिक्रिया पुढीलप्रमाणे :\nNaOH(aq)  ⟶ Na⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)\nसोडिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड  \n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93287,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्लारीची चार उदाहरणे द्या.','','','i. कॅल्शिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड [Ca(OH)₂]\nii. मॅग्नेशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड [Mg(OH)₂]\niii. सोडिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड (NaOH)\niv. पोटॅशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड (KOH)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93288,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील पदार्थांची सामान्य नावे लिहा. \ni. सोडिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड                       ii. पोटॅशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड\niii. कॅल्शिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड                   iv. मॅग्नेशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड','','','सामान्य नावे पुढीलप्रमाणे :\ni. सोडिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड - कॉस्टिक सोडा\nii. पोटॅशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड - पोटॅश\niii. कॅल्शिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड - चुन्याची निवळी\niv. मॅग्नेशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड - मिल्क ऑफ मॅग्नेशिआ\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93289,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्लारीचे गुणधर्म लिहा.','','','i. आम्लारींची चव कडवट असते.\nii. आम्लारींमुके तांबडा लिटमस कागद निका होतो.\niii. त्यांचा स्पर्श बुळबुळीत असतो.\niv. पाण्यामध्ये आम्लारी हायड्रॉक्साइड (OH⁻) आयन निर्माण करतात.\nv. सामान्यत : धातूंचे ऑक्साइड आम्लारीधर्मी असतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93290,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','उदासिनीकरण अभिक्रिया सोदाहरण स्पष्ट करा. ','','','i. आम्लामध्ये हायड्रोजन (H⁺)आयन आणि आम्लारीमध्ये हायड्रॉक्साइड (OH⁻)आयन असतात.\nii. आम्ल व आम्लारींच्या संयोगाने क्षार आणि पाणी निर्माण होतात.\niii. या अभिक्रियेला उदासिनीकरण अभिक्रिया असे म्हणतात.\nउदा.\nआम्ल + आम्लारी → क्षार + पाणी\nHCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93291,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','शास्त्रीय कारणे द्या : H₂SO₄ चे विरलीकरण करण्यासाठी ते हकूहळू पाण्यामध्ये टाकले जाते.','','','i. जेंव्हा संहत आम्ल पाण्यामध्ये टाकले जाते तेंव्हा प्रचंड ऊर्जा निर्माण होते.\nii. या ऊर्जेमुके रफोट होऊ शकतो.\niii. म्हणून, संहत  H₂SO₄ चे विरलीकरण करणयासाठी ते अतिशय हळूहळू पाण्यात टाकतात आणि सतत हळूहळू ढवळत राहतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93292,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','शास्त्रीय कारणे द्या : आम्लारींची संहत द्रावणे त्वचेसाठी अपायकारक असतात.','','','i. सोडिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड आणि पोटॅशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइडसारखे आम्लारी तीव्र आणि दाहक असतात.\nii. त्यांची संहत द्रावणे त्वचेवर पडल्यास त्वचा भाजते ; कारण ते त्वचेमधील प्रथिनांचे विघटन करतात.\nम्हणून, आम्लारींची संहत द्रावणे त्वचेसाठी अपायकारक असतात. .\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93293,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','शास्त्रीय कारणे द्या : जठरातील वाढलेले आम्लाचे प्रमाण कमी करण्यासाठी आम्लविरोधक औषध वापरतात.','','','i. आपल्या जठरात हायड्रोक्तोरिक आम्ल तयार होते.\nii. हें आम्ल अन्नपचनास मदत्त करते.\niii. मात्र, हे आम्ल गरजेपेक्षा जास्त वाढले, तर अपचन होते.\niv. आम्लविरोधक औषधांमध्ये मिल्क ऑफ मॅग्नेशिआसारखे आम्लारी समाविष्ट असतात आणि ते जठरात असलेल्या अतिरिक्ति आम्लाचे उढासिनीकरण घडवतात.\nम्हणून, जठरातील वाढलेले आम्लाचे प्रमाण कमी करण्यासाठी आम्लविरोधक औषध वापरतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93294,2210,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','शास्त्रीय कारणे द्या : जमीन आम्लधर्मी असेल, तर जमिनीत चुनखडी किंवा निवळी मिसळतात.','','','i. रासायनिक खतांच्या अतिवापरामुळे शेतजमिनीतील आम्लाचे प्रमाण वाढते.\nii. चुनखड़ी (कॅल्शिअम कार्बोनेट) आणि चुन्याची निवक्री (कॅल्शिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड) आम्लारीधर्मी असतात.\niii. जमिनीतील अतिरिक्त आम्ल कमी होण्यास मदत होते\nम्हणून, जमीन आम्लधर्मी असेल, तर जमिनीत शेतीतज्ज्ञांच्या मार्गदर्शनाखाली चुनखडी किंवा चुन्याची निवकी मिसकतात.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93295,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','सारणी / तक्ता पूर्ण करा.\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>आम्लारीचे रासायनिक नाव</th>\n    <th>सूत्र</th>\n    <th>उपयोग</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>सोडिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड</td>\n    <td>-------</td>\n    <td>-------</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>----------</td>\n    <td>----------</td>\n    <td>चुन्यामध्ये किंवा रंगसफेदीसाठी</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>मॅग्नेशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड</td>\n    <td>------</td>\n    <td>-------</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>आम्लारीचे रासायनिक नाव</th>\n    <th>सूत्र</th>\n    <th>उपयोग</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>सोडिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड</td>\n    <td>NaOH</td>\n    <td>कपडे धुण्याच्या साबणामध्ये</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>कॅल्शिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड</td>\n    <td>Ca(OH)_2</td>\n    <td>चुन्यामध्ये किंवा रंगसफेदीसाठी</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>मॅग्नेशिअम हायड्रॉक्साइड</td>\n    <td>Mg(OH)_2</td>\n    <td>आम्लविरोधक औषध (जठरातील आम्ल कमी करण्यासाठी)</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93296,2210,'descriptive','3',3,2,'Out of Text Book','','खालील आकृतीमधील प्रत्येक परीक्षानकीमधील द्रावणाचे स्वरूप ओळखा आणि प्रत्येक परीक्षानळीमध्ये दर्शक घातल्यानंतर येणारा रंग सांगा.','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>परीक्षानळी</th>\n    <th>द्रावणाचे स्वरूप</th>\n    <th>दर्शक घातल्यानंतर द्रावणाला येणारा रंग</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>A</td>\n    <td>आम्लधर्मी</td>\n    <td>रंगहीन (बदल नाही.)</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>B</td>\n    <td>आम्लारीधर्मी</td>\n    <td>पिवळा</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>C</td>\n    <td>आम्लधर्मी</td>\n    <td>तांबडा</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',1,0,0,NULL),(93297,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','खाण्याच्या सोड्याचे रासायनिक सूत्र सांगा.','','','खाण्याच्या सोङ्याचे रासायनिक सूत्र NaHCO₃ आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93298,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','आम्लारीधर्मी द्रावणात फिनॉल्फ्थैलिनचा रंग काय असतो ?','','','आम्लारीधर्मी द्रावणात फिनॉल्फ्थैलिनचा रंग गुलाबी असतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93299,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','हळदीचा पिवळा डाग साबणाने धुतल्यास काय होते ?','','','हळदीचा पिवळा डाग साबणाने धुतल्यास तो लाल होतो.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93300,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','व्हिनेगार मध्ये असणाऱ्या आम्लाचे नाव सांगा.','','','असेटिक आम्ल','',0,0,0,NULL),(93301,2210,'descriptive','1b',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','मिल्क ऑफ मॅग्नेशिआचे रासायनिक सूत्र सांगा.','','','Mg(OH)₂','',0,0,0,NULL),(93302,2211,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','अन्न खराब होणे हा रासायनिक बदल आहे हे त्यात विशिष्ट ____ निर्माण होतो त्यावरून ओळखता येते.\na) रंगीत b) वास c) दुधाळ d) जलद\n','','','b) वास','',0,0,0,NULL),(93303,2211,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','लोखंडाचे गंजणे हा ____ होणारा रासायनिक बदल आहे.\na) सावकाश b) जलद c) बाण d) अष्टक\n','','','a) सावकाश','',0,0,0,NULL),(93304,2211,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','लिंबूरसात थोडे खाण्याच्या सोड्याचे चूर्ण टाकल्यास थोङ्या वेळाने पांढरे कण दिसेनासे होतात, म्हणजेच हा ____ बदल आहे.\na) भौतिक b) रासायनिक c) उत्पादित d) आयनिक\n','','','b) रासायनिक','',0,0,0,NULL),(93305,2211,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','रासायनिक अभिक्रियेचे समीकरण लिहिताना अभिक्रियाकारके व उत्पादिते यांच्यामध्ये ____ काढतात.\na) बाण b) रंगीत c) वास d) जलद\n','','','a) बाण','',0,0,0,NULL),(93306,2211,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','श्वसनक्रियेमध्ये ऑक्सिजन हा एक ____ आहे.\na) अभिकारक b) सहसंयुज c) आयनिक d) अष्टक\n','','','a) अभिकारक','',0,0,0,NULL),(93307,2211,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','परीक्षानळीतील कॅल्शिअम हायड्रॉक्साइडच्या रंगहीन द्रावणात फुंकनळीने फुंकत राहिल्यास काही वेळाने द्रावण ____ होते.\na) दुधाळ b) रंगीत c) जलद d) बाण\n','','','a) दुधाळ','',0,0,0,NULL),(93308,2211,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','सोडिअम क्लोराइड हे ____ संयुग आहे, तर हायड्रोजन क्लोराइड हे ____ संयुग आहे.\na) आयनिक b) सहसंयुज c) अभिकारक d) उत्पादित\n','','','a) आयनिक b) सहसंयुज','',0,0,0,NULL),(93309,2211,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Text Book','','हायड्रोजनच्या रेणूमध्ये प्रत्येक हायड्रोजनचे इलेक्ट्रॉन ____ पूर्ण असते.\na) द्विक b) अष्टक c) दूविक d) संदान\n','','','a) द्विक','',0,0,0,NULL),(93310,2211,'mcq','1a',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','क्लोरीनच्या दोन अणूंमध्ये इलेक्ट्रॉन्सचे ____ होऊन Cl₂ हा रेणू तयार होतो.\na) संदान b) सहसंयुज c) आयनिक d) बरोबरचे चिन्ह\n','','','a) संदान','',0,0,0,NULL),(93311,2211,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Text Book','','शाब्दिक समीकरण लिहून स्पष्ट करा : श्वसन हा एक रासायनिक बदल आहे.','','','i. श्वसन ही आपल्या जीवनात सतत चालू असणारी जैविक प्रक्रिया आहे.\nii. या प्रक्रियेमध्ये सजीव श्वासावाटे हवा आत बेतात आणि उच्छ्वासावाटे कार्वन डायऑक्साइड वायू वाहेर टाकतात.\niii. सखोल अभ्यासानंतर समजले आहे, की श्वासावाटे आत घेतलेल्या हवेतील ऑक्सिजनची पेशींमधील ग्लुकोजवरोवर अभिक्रिया होते व कार्वन डायऑक्साइड आणि पाणी तयार होतात.\nम्हणूनच, श्वसन हा रासायनिक बढ़ल आहे.\niv. श्वसनामध्ये होणाया रासायनिक बढ़लाचे शाब्दिक समीकरण पुढालप्रमाणे आहे :\n         ग्लुकोज + ऑक्सिजन → (श्वसन) कार्बन डायऑक्साइड + पाणी','',0,0,0,NULL),(93312,2211,'descriptive','2',2,3,'Out of Text Book','','शास्त्रीय कारणे लिहा : विरल हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्लाने शहाबादी फरशी स्वच्छ करणे हा रासायनिक बदल आहे.','','','i. शहाबादी फरशीचे रासायनिक संघटन मुख्यत्वे कॅल्शिअम कार्बोनेट (चुनखड़ी) असे आहे.\nii. शहाबादी फरशी हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्लाने स्वच्छ करतेवेकी फरशीच्या वरच्या थराची हायड्रोक्लोरिक आम्लाबरोबर रासायनिक अभिक्रिया होते आणि कॅश्शिअम क्लोराइड, कार्वन डायऑक्साइड आणि पाणी अशी तीन उत्पादिते तयार होतात.\nम्हणून, विरत हायड्रोक्तोरिक आम्लाने शहाबादी फरशी स्वच्छ करणे हा रासायनिक बद़ल आहे.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93313,2211,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','शास्त्रीय कारणे लिहा : सामान्य स्थितीमध्ये राजवायू रासायनिक बंध तयार करत नाहीत.','','','i. रासायनिक बंध तयार करताना अणू त्यांच्या संयुजा इलेक्ट्रॉन्सचे आदान-प्रदान किंवा संदान करतात आणि इलेक्ट्रॉन अष्टक किंवा दूविक पूर्ण करतात.\nii. एखाद्या अणूने तयार केलेल्या रासायनिक बंधांची संख्या त्याच्या संयुजेच्या संख्येएवढ़ी असते.\niii. राजवायूंचे इलेक्ट्रॉन अष्टक किंवा दूविक पूर्ण असते. म्हणजेच, त्यांची संयुजा शून्य असते.\nम्हणूनच, सामान्य स्थितीमध्ये राजवायू रासायनिक बंध तयार करत नाहीत.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(93314,2211,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','शास्त्रीय कारणे लिहा : NaCl हे आयनिक संयुग आहे.','','','सोडिअम (Na) याचे इलेक्ट्रॉन संरूपण (2,8,1) आहे. क्लोरीन (CI) याचे इलेक्ट्रॉन संरूपण (2,8,7) आहे. त्यामुळे अष्टक स्थिती पूर्ण करण्यासाठी सोडिअम एक इलेक्ट्रॉन देऊन धनप्रभार आयन तयार करतो तोच इलेक्ट्रॉन क्लोरीन घेऊन ऋण प्रभार तयार करतो त्यामुळे अणुमध्ये विजातीय प्रभार निर्माण होऊन ते परस्परांच्या जवळ येऊन आयनिक बंध तयार होतो. म्हणून NaCl हे आयनिक संयुग आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93315,2211,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','शास्त्रीय कारणे लिहा : HCI हे सहसंयुज संयुग आहे.','','','हायड्रोजनचे (H) इलेक्ट्रॉन संरूपण 1 आहे. क्लोरीन (CI) यांचे इलेक्ट्रॉन संरूपण 2,8,7 आहे. हायड्रोजनला विक स्थिती पूर्ण करण्यासाठी एका इलेक्ट्रॉनची गरज असते. तर क्लोरीनला अष्टक स्थिती पूर्ण करण्यासाठी एका इलेक्ट्रॉनची आवश्यकता असते. त्यामुळे ही दोन्ही मूलद्रव्ये परस्परांमध्ये इलेक्ट्रॉनची भागिदारी करतात. म्हणून HCI हे सहसंयुज संयुग आहे.','',0,0,0,NULL),(93316,2211,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','फरक स्पष्ट करा : भौतिक बदल आणि रासायनिक बदल','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>भौतिक बदल</th>\n    <th>रासायनिक बदल</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>यामध्ये पदार्थाचे रासायनिक संघटन बदलत नाही.</td>\n    <td>यामध्ये पदार्थाचे रासायनिक संघटन बदलते.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>हे बदल रंगातील किंवा चवीमधील बदल किंवा वायू मुक्त होणे यांवरून ओकखता येत नाहीत.</td>\n    <td>रंगामधील किंवा चवीमधील बदल किंवा वायू मुक्त होणे यावसून हे बदल जाणवतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>भौतिक बदलांमध्ये रासायनिक बंध तुटत नाहीत आणि नवीन रासायनिक बंध तयार होत नाहीत.</td>\n    <td>रासायनिक बदलांमध्ये काही रासायनिक बंध तुटतात आणि नवीन रासायनिक बंध तयार होतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>भौतिक बदलांमध्ये नवीन पदार्थ तयार होत नाहीत</td>\n    <td>रासायनिक बदलांमध्ये नवीन पदार्थ तयार होतो.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>उदा.</td>\n    <td>बर्फ वितकणे.</td>\n    <td>केळे पिकणे.</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(93317,2211,'descriptive','2',2,2,'Out of Text Book','','फरक स्पष्ट करा : आयनिक बंध आणि सहसंयुज बंध','','','<table border=\"1\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: fit-content; text-align: left;\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#e6f3ff; text-align:center;\">\n    <th>क्र.</th>\n    <th>आयनिक बंध</th>\n    <th>सहसंयुज बंध</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>i.</td>\n    <td>हे बंध इलेक्ट्रॉन्सच्या आदान-प्रदानातून तयार होतात.</td>\n    <td>है बंध इलेक्ट्रॉन्सच्या संदानाने तयार होतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>ii.</td>\n    <td>हे बंध वेगवेगके गुणधर्म असलेल्या मूलद्रव्यांच्या अणूंच्या संयोगातून तयार होतात.</td>\n    <td>हे बंध सारखे गुणधर्म असलेल्या मूलद्रव्यांच्या अणूंच्या संयोगातून तयार होतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iii.</td>\n    <td>यामध्ये धन आयन आणि ऋण आयन तयार होतात.</td>\n    <td>यामध्ये आयन तयार होत नाहीत.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>iv.</td>\n    <td>आयनिक बंध तयार करणाया अणूंच्या संज्ञांवर प्रभार दाखवून हे बंध दर्शवले जातात.</td>\n    <td>दोन सहसंयुज बंध या अणूंच्या संज्ञांना जोडणाया रेषेने (-) दर्शवतात.</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>उदा.</td>\n    <td>NaCl मधील बंध</td>\n    <td>HCl मधील बंध</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>','',0,0,0,NULL),(94259,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Dead plants and animals are abiotic components.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(94260,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : It is necessary to conserve biodiversity.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(94261,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Man is not using up more and more land and water resources.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(94262,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Soil becomes fertile, if polluted water seeps into it.\n','','','False','',0,0,0,NULL),(94263,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The temperature of the earth rises when the proportion of carbon dioxide rises.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(94264,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : The process of living things becoming extinct is at a very slow rate.\n','','','False\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(94265,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : People visiting biodiversity park enjoy being close to nature.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(94266,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','State whether the given statement is true or false : Deer are found in the grasslands of Nannaj.\n','','','True','',0,0,0,NULL),(94267,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','How was Mayani Lake formed? Which birds come to this lake?\n','','','By building a dam on the Chand river in Satara district, Mayani lake was formed. Flamingo from Siberia migrate to build nests and lay eggs on this lake.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(94268,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Why is it said that Maldhok is a friend of farmers?\n','','','These birds of grasslands feed on insects and thus protect crops. Therefore, they said that friends of farmer.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(94269,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Which village in Maharashtra gives security of peafowls? \n','','','Morachi Chincholi near Pune\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(94270,2493,'descriptive','1b',1,0,'Out of Text Book','','Match the Columns:\n<table border=\"1\" style=\"width:fit-content; border-collapse: collapse\" class=\"table table-bordered table-striped\">\n  <tr>\n    <th>Group A</th>\n    <th>Group B</th>\n    <th>Group C</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>1) Nannaj</td>\n    <td>a) Pune-Ahmednagar<br>highway</td>\n    <td>p) Peafowl</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>2) Chicholi</td>\n    <td>b) District Solapur</td>\n    <td>q) Flamingo</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td>3) Mayani</td>\n    <td>c) District Satara</td>\n    <td>r) Great Indian<br>Bustard</td>\n  </tr>\n</table>\n','','','(1 – b – r), (2 – a – p), (3 – c – n)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(94620,2069,'descriptive','5',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: Christmas','','','Christmas Essay\n\nChristmas is a popular festival celebrated on 25th December every year.\nIt is celebrated to remember the birth of Jesus Christ.\nPeople decorate their houses with Christmas trees, lights, and stars.\nChildren wait eagerly for Santa Claus to bring gifts and sweets.\nPeople wear new clothes and share happiness with others.\nSpecial prayers are held in churches on Christmas day.\nFamilies and friends come together to celebrate this joyful festival.\nPeople prepare delicious cakes, chocolates, and food items.\nChristmas teaches us love, kindness, and helping the poor.\nWe greet each other by saying “Merry Christmas.”\nIt is a festival of joy, peace, and togetherness.','',0,0,0,NULL),(94621,2069,'descriptive','5',4,0,'Out of Text Book','','Write essays on the following topic: Republic Day','','','Republic Day Essay\n\nRepublic Day is celebrated in India on 26th January every year.\nOn this day, the Constitution of India came into effect in 1950.\nIt is an important national festival of our country.\nThe President of India hoists the national flag in New Delhi.\nA grand parade is held at Rajpath to show India’s unity and strength.\nSoldiers, students, and cultural groups take part in the parade.\nSchools and colleges organize flag hoisting and cultural programs.\nWe sing the national anthem with pride.\nRepublic Day reminds us of our rights and duties as citizens.\nIt teaches us to respect our country and its laws.\nWe feel proud to be Indians on this day.','',0,0,0,NULL),(94622,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Pick out two compound words from the lesson The Kite Festival.','','','Two compound words : kite - flying, power - driven','',0,0,0,NULL),(94624,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use the phrase \'in the middle of the night\' in your own sentence.','','','The baby woke up <u>in the middle of the night</u>  and began to play.','',0,0,0,NULL),(94625,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Build a word chain of five adjectives : long →','','','long → green → naughty → young → grateful → lovely.','',0,0,0,NULL),(94626,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Find out four hidden words from the word \'traditional\': (Each word should have at least 3 letters.)','','','traditional : tradition, train, drain, trait','',0,0,0,NULL),(94627,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use the word \'watch\' in two separate sentences, the word having different meanings (homographs):','','','i) Those policemen <u>watch</u> the house the whole day.\n(ii) I was very upset as I had lost my <u>watch</u>.\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(94628,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Pick out the clauses in the sentence : There is music in the air and traditional delicacies are specially prepared for the day.','','','Coordinate clause - <u>There is music in the air</u>\nCoordinate clause - <u>traditional delicacies are specially prepared for the day.</u>\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(94629,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Identify the type of sentence : The little boy would happily pick up his kite and run out into the field.','','','Assertive sentence.','',0,0,0,NULL),(94630,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rewrite sentence : as an affirmative\nIn India, however, the tradition of kite-flying has not changed.\n','','','In India, however, the tradition of kite-flying has remained the same.','',0,0,0,NULL),(94631,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Rewrite in indirect speech :\nThe harassed mother would often say to her restless son, \"Why don\'t you go fly a kite ?\"','','','The harassed mother would often ask her restless son why he did not go fly a kite.','',0,0,0,NULL),(94632,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use the word \'cut\' as a noun and a verb in two separate sentences.','','','i) The <u>cut</u> on my hand healed soon. (noun)\n(ii) I <u>cut</u> my finger when I was slicing a mango. (verb)\n','',0,0,0,NULL),(94633,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Pick out two words from the lesson ‘The Last Leaf’ whose first letter is silent.','','','pneumonia, gnarled','',0,0,0,NULL),(94634,2968,'descriptive','1',1,2,'Out of Text Book','','Use the phrase \'above the ground\' in your own sentence.','','','During monsoon, the clouds are just a few feet <u>above the ground</u> in